window.LESSON_DATA = {"devotions":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: The Arrival of Jesus and John":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1—2:38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meeting Zechariah — an impossible promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:5–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meeting Zechariah — an impossible promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God keeps his promises, even ones that seem impossible.</li><li>We are learning who Zechariah and Elizabeth were, and the promise the angel Gabriel brought them.</li><li>I can explain why believing an impossible promise can be hard — even with proof right in front of you.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for participation in the opening belief question. Note which students can recall Zechariah's name, his situation, and connect it back to the 'would you believe a superpower claim' opener by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; hook</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome the class back warmly — this is the first day of a brand-new term and a brand-new Bible story. “Welcome back, everyone! This term we’re going to meet someone amazing and important. He’s a Super Saviour!” Build a little excitement and mystery — don’t reveal who yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Opening question — would you believe it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This question is the setup for the whole story, so don't rush it. Push gently on &quot;what would it take to convince you&quot; — most students will land on &quot;I'd need to see it for myself.&quot; Hold onto that thought out loud: &quot;Keep that in mind, because in today's story, someone gets exactly that kind of proof — and still has a hard time believing it.&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If someone told you they had a real superpower, would you believe them? What would it take to convince you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses. Push gently: most kids will say they'd need to SEE proof. Hold onto this — it's exactly the position Zechariah is about to be in, except he gets proof from an actual angel and still struggles to believe it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Introducing the comic &amp; Zechariah</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This term we’ll be reading our very own Bible comic. It begins with a man named Zechariah.” Show the first comic panel. Explain who Zechariah and Elizabeth were — an older married couple who were never able to have children, which in their culture and time carried real sadness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 01-zechariah-elizabeth-intro on screen as you introduce them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gabriel’s promise &amp; the silence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One day, as Zechariah was serving God in the temple, and all the people were praying outside, the angel Gabriel appeared to him!” Show panel 02 — the angel’s promise. Then show panel 03 — the crowd’s confusion as Zechariah comes out unable to speak. Callback to the opening question: Zechariah got the most convincing proof possible — an actual angel, face to face — and still doubted. Build suspense; don’t explain yet exactly what he said or did wrong (revealed Day 2 — he doubted the angel).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 02-gabriel-in-temple and 03-crowd-cant-speak in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Zechariah saw an actual angel and heard the promise himself — so why do you think he still struggled to believe it, and what do you think happened to him inside the temple that made him unable to speak?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — we're building anticipation, not confirming the answer yet. The full reveal comes tomorrow (he doubted the angel's promise about John, even after seeing him face to face).</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals why Zechariah lost his voice, and introduces Mary and the angel’s announcement about Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Two surprising babies":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:24–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Two surprising babies</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened to Zechariah's voice, and why.</li><li>We are learning that God announced both John and Jesus before they were even born.</li><li>I can name John and Jesus and begin to wonder who they are and why their births mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can recall why John's birth was extraordinary and can name both babies by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; reveal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, Zechariah had just seen an angel — and now he couldn’t speak! The angel had told him that he and his wife Elizabeth, who were very old and had never been able to have children, would have a son. They were to name him John.” Show panel 04. Note for yourself (not necessarily stated outright unless asked): Zechariah lost his voice because he doubted the angel’s promise — you can confirm this if students ask directly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 04-elizabeth-pregnant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why John's birth mattered</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pause on the wonder of this moment for Elizabeth before moving on to Mary's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why was John's birth so extraordinary?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his parents were very old and had never been able to have children before — this was humanly impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gabriel visits Mary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“About one month later, God sent his angel Gabriel to visit a young woman named Mary.” Show panels 05–07 in sequence, reading each line aloud with feeling — these are the angel’s actual words to Mary.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 05-gabriel-visits-mary, 06-dont-be-afraid-jesus, and 07-jesus-will-be-great in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the babies</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Check the class has caught both names before moving into the open wonder question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the names of these two important babies?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John and Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Open wonder question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close today’s session by opening the big question without answering it — this is meant to stay unresolved until later in the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who do you think John is? Who do you think Jesus is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses — this is genuinely open at this point in the story; we’ll build the answer across the week.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 reveals more of what the angel said about Jesus becoming King, and shows his birth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: A promised King is born":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:30–35; 2:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A promised King is born</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the angel announced Jesus would be an eternal King.</li><li>We are learning that Mary trusted God even though she didn't understand how it would happen.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can repeat the memory verse with reasonable accuracy by the end of the activity, and whether they can articulate what made John's and Jesus's births unusual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; the King promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“An angel told Mary she would have a very special son, named Jesus. Let’s hear what else the angel said about him.” Show panel 08, reading the angel’s words aloud with weight — this is the heart of the promise: an eternal kingdom.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 08-kingdom-never-end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Mary's question &amp; the angel's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show panel 09. Point out that Mary asked a very reasonable question (“How will this happen?”) and that the angel's answer pointed to God's power, not a normal explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel 09-how-will-this-happen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Mary believed the angel and trusted God. Some time later, baby Jesus was born.” Show panels 10 and 11. Land on the line “Nothing is impossible for God!” as the takeaway image/moment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panels 10-nativity and 11-baby-jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce the verse slide (Luke 1:32–33, CEV). Read it together as a class two or three times, then invite the class to try saying it once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing the comparison between an ordinary birth and what made John's and Jesus's so different.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What's normally needed for a baby to be born — and what was different about John and Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: babies normally just come from a mum and dad; these two were announced in advance, by an angel, and given specific important jobs before they were even born.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings it all together — who John and Jesus are, and what each was born to do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was. He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.” — <em>Luke 1:32–33 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Why John and Jesus matter":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why John and Jesus matter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about John and Jesus.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, who John is and what he was born to do.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, who Jesus is and what he was born to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the four review questions as an informal check of understanding before moving on. Listen for whether students connect John to 'getting people ready' and Jesus to 'the promised King.'</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap intro</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This week we’ve met two very special babies — John and Jesus. Let’s see what we’ve figured out about why they’re so important.” Frame this session as pulling together the whole week's story rather than introducing anything new.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who is Jesus? Who is John?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work through these two questions one at a time, letting students draw on everything seen across the week. Don't rush — this is the moment the whole week's build-up pays off.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who is Jesus? · Who is John?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus: guide toward the Son of God, the promised King. John: guide toward he goes ahead of Jesus to get people ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What were they born to do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Push one level deeper from identity into purpose/mission for each of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think John was born to do? · What do you think Jesus was born to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John: he would help turn people back to God and get them ready for the Lord to come. Jesus: he was born to be King forever — and his kingdom would never end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it together fully from memory this time, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was. He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.” — <em>Luke 1:32–33 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the births of John and Jesus. Please help us understand how important their jobs were. And please help us find out more about them as this term goes on. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: Ready ... set ...":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1—4:13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Getting ready for someone important":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Getting ready for someone important</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that John's job was to get people ready for Jesus' arrival.</li><li>We are learning this week's memory verse and what it means to 'turn back to God.'</li><li>I can explain why John's message of repentance and baptism mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, what job John had — note who can connect 'getting ready' to John preparing people for Jesus by the end of the session.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Dinner-guest hook</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the imagined scenario — really let students picture it. &quot;Imagine someone really important was suddenly coming to your house for dinner tonight.&quot; Push for specifics: would they clean their room? Change clothes? The point to land softly (don't over-explain yet) is that getting ready properly takes real action, not just good intentions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine someone really important was suddenly coming to your house for dinner tonight. What would you do to get ready?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — cleaning up, tidying their room, putting on best clothes, etc. The point is that getting ready takes real effort and action, not just words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reintroducing John</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Last term we met John — the baby whose birth was announced by an angel before he was even born. Now John is grown up, and he has a very important job: getting people ready for someone far more important than a dinner guest.&quot; This is a deliberate callback to Week 1 — give students a moment to remember John if they need it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-01-john-in-desert.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next panel and read John's words aloud with some weight — this is the heart of his preaching. Introduce this week's memory verse here directly from the panel's words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-02-john-preaching-river, then introduce the verse slide (Luke 3:3, CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaiah's prophecy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the Isaiah panel. Point out that this prophecy was written 700 years before John was even born — God had a plan for John's job long, long in advance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-03-isaiah-prophecy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing the link explicit between the opening hook and John's message — getting ready for God isn't just saying sorry, it's a real change. Don't fully resolve this; it's the bridge into tomorrow's lesson on 'producing fruit.'</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John's message wasn't just words — people had to actually do something. Why do you think 'getting ready' for God needed more than just saying sorry?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: real change shows up in what we do, not just what we say. This sets up tomorrow's 'fruit' teaching directly.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 unpacks what 'getting ready' actually looks like in action — through John's teaching about fruit trees and his specific instructions to the crowds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: What 'getting ready' looks like":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:7–18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What 'getting ready' looks like</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that John used the picture of fruit trees to teach what real repentance looks like.</li><li>We are learning that John gave specific, practical instructions to different groups of people.</li><li>I can name one specific action that would show real change in my own life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the fruit-tree metaphor in their own words, and whether they can name a concrete (not vague) example of 'producing fruit' during the activity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; fruit trees</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Yesterday, John told everyone to turn back to God. But what does that actually look like? John had an answer.&quot; Show the fruit-tree panel and let the metaphor land before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-04-fruit-trees-teaching.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Unpacking the metaphor</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This question does the real teaching work — don't rush past it. Make sure the class actually arrives at 'you'd judge by what it produces' before moving on.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If a tree never grew any fruit, how would you know just by looking at it whether it was healthy or not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: you'd know by what it actually produces, not by what it looks like on the outside. That's John's point — real change in us shows up in what we actually do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's specific instructions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence. Point out that John didn't give the same instruction to everyone — he gave specific, practical advice depending on each group's actual situation and temptations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk2-05-what-should-we-do and wk2-06-tax-collectors-soldiers in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal application activity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is the activity where the lesson becomes personal. Push for something concrete and specific rather than a vague good intention — &quot;be nicer&quot; doesn't count, &quot;share my lunch with someone who doesn't have one&quot; does.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John gave different people different instructions, but the idea was the same each time. Invite a few students to share their specific 'fruit' idea for the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Someone more powerful is coming</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel of the day. Let the question hang — this is meant to make students do the connecting work themselves rather than telling them outright.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-07-could-john-be-king.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John just said someone more powerful than him is coming. After everything we know from last term, who do you think that is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus — this is a deliberate callback. Let students make the connection themselves before confirming.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows Jesus himself arriving at the river to be baptised by John.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Jesus is baptised":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:21–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is baptised</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus came to the same river where John was baptising people.</li><li>We are learning what happened immediately after Jesus was baptised — the Holy Spirit and the voice from heaven.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what happened at Jesus' baptism (the dove, the voice) and whether they can repeat the memory verse with reasonable accuracy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;John told everyone someone more powerful than him was coming. One day, that someone arrived — at the very same river.&quot; Show the arrival panel and let the moment build a little before revealing what happens.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-08-jesus-arrives-at-river.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is baptised</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the baptism panel, then ask the puzzle question. This is meant to provoke real thinking — Jesus didn't need forgiveness the way everyone else did, so why be baptised? Don't over-resolve it; a short class discussion is enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-09-jesus-baptised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">John had been telling everyone to turn back to God and be baptised because of their sins. So why do you think Jesus — who hadn't done anything wrong — wanted to be baptised too?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is genuinely open and a bit puzzling on purpose. Accept any thoughtful guesses; the main point for today is what happens next, not fully resolving this question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The Holy Spirit and the voice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the dove/voice panel and read the heavenly voice's words aloud with weight — this is one of the most important moments in the whole Gospel of Luke, confirming exactly who Jesus is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-10-holy-spirit-descends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this week's verse slide (Luke 3:3). Read it together two or three times, then invite the class to try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing check</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recall question to close — make sure the key detail (whose voice, what it said) has landed before moving to tomorrow's wrap-up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Whose voice spoke from heaven, and what did the voice call Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God's voice — calling Jesus his Son, whom he loves.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together — what John's job was, and what Jesus' baptism revealed about who he is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Bringing it together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 3:1—4:13 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bringing it together</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about John and Jesus' baptism.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, what John's job was and what it means to 'produce fruit.'</li><li>I can explain what happened at Jesus' baptism and what it revealed about who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the three review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students connect 'fruit' to real action, and whether they can recall the dove and the voice from heaven.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap intro</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched John get everyone ready, and then watched the very person he was preparing the way for finally arrive.&quot; Show the wide baptism scene as a visual anchor for the whole week's story before moving into review questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk2-11-baptism-wide-scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>John's job</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recall — keep this moving, it's the simplest of the three review questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was John's job, in your own words?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: getting people ready for Jesus, by telling them to turn back to God and show real change.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Producing fruit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try to get specific recall here — if students remember their own Tuesday example, that's a good sign the lesson stuck. Affirm whatever they share.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it mean to 'produce fruit' — and can you remember the example you picked on Tuesday?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Showing real change through actions, not just words. Let students recall and share their own specific example from Day 2 if they can.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' baptism</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is the theological high point of the week — make sure it lands clearly before moving to the verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened right after Jesus was baptised, and what did it tell us about who he is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Holy Spirit came down like a dove, and God's voice called Jesus his Son, whom he loves — confirming Jesus is exactly who the angel said he'd be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it fully from memory, with the screen as backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus returning to Nazareth and claiming to be the promised rescuer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for John, who got people ready for Jesus. Thank you for Jesus, your Son, whom you love. Please help us turn back to you this week, not just in what we say, but in what we do. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: The one you've all been waiting for":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14—30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Jesus says who he is":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus says who he is</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus went back to his hometown synagogue and read a promise from Isaiah about a coming rescuer.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus directly claimed this promise was about himself.</li><li>I can explain what kind of people Jesus said he came for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, that Jesus stood up and claimed the Isaiah promise was about himself — note who can name at least two groups of people the passage says he came for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The long wait</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the personal question — let a few students share something they waited a long time for. Don't rush this; the feeling of anticipation finally paying off is the emotional anchor for the whole week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Has there ever been something you waited for ages to happen — and then it actually did? What was that like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer — a birthday, a holiday, a long-awaited event. The point is the feeling of a long wait finally being over, which is exactly the feeling we're building toward today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and set-up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the story so far across the term: John got everyone ready, Jesus was baptised, God called him his Son. Today we find out what Jesus himself has to say about who he is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-01-jesus-walks-to-nazareth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus reads the scroll</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the synagogue panel, then bring up the verse slide and read it together slowly — this is a dense, important passage. Take time over it rather than rushing through.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-02-reads-isaiah-scroll, then the verse slide (Luke 4:18–19, CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus reads a list of things this promised person was supposed to do. What kinds of people does the list say he's come for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The poor, prisoners, the blind, everyone who suffers — people who are struggling or stuck. Jesus is describing someone who rescues and restores.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Today, in front of you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and read Jesus' line aloud with weight: &quot;What you have heard me read has come true today.&quot; Make sure students grasp the size of the claim — Jesus isn't just teaching about an old prophecy, he's saying it's about him, right now, in that room.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-03-fulfilled-today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't just read an old promise — he said it about himself, right there, out loud. What is Jesus actually claiming about who he is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is claiming to be the promised rescuer-King the Scriptures were talking about — God's chosen one, sent to save and restore people. This is a huge, direct claim.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows how the crowd in his own hometown actually responds to this huge claim — and it isn't what you'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“The Lord’s Spirit has come to me, because he has chosen me to tell the good news to the poor. The Lord has sent me to announce freedom for prisoners, to give sight to the blind, to free everyone who suffers, and to say, ‘This is the year the Lord has chosen.’” — <em>Luke 4:18–19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: The hometown crowd turns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:22–24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The hometown crowd turns</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the people of Nazareth responded to Jesus' claim about himself.</li><li>We are learning that familiarity with Jesus led to doubt rather than belief for many in his hometown.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus didn't perform miracles in Nazareth.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the crowd's familiarity with Jesus — knowing him as 'just' Joseph's son — made them doubt rather than believe his claim.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap yesterday's claim</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quickly remind the class of yesterday's big claim — Jesus said the Isaiah promise was about him. Today we see how people actually responded to that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Amazed, then doubtful</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the crowd's question aloud. Let the discomfort of the question sit for a moment before discussing it — this is the turning point of the whole story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-04-josephs-son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;Isn't this Joseph's son?&quot; — why do you think knowing Jesus since he was a kid made it harder for these people to accept his huge claim, rather than easier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: when you've known someone your whole life as 'just' the carpenter's son, it's hard to suddenly see them as God's promised rescuer. Familiarity made them doubt rather than believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>No miracles at home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence — first the healing happening elsewhere, then the contrast of nothing happening in Nazareth. Make the contrast clear and let students draw the conclusion themselves with the question that follows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk3-05-healing-other-towns and wk3-06-no-miracles-nazareth in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus had done amazing things in other towns, but not in his own hometown. What does that tell us about how the people of Nazareth were responding to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">They weren't willing to trust and believe in him the way people in other towns had. Their familiarity with Jesus had turned into doubt and rejection, not faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring the lesson home with a personal connection — invite a few students to share without putting anyone on the spot. The aim is to help them feel, not just know, why familiarity can get in the way of really seeing someone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the reflection activity as a quick share-around or pair discussion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a time it was hard to take someone seriously just because you knew them really well — a sibling, a classmate, a friend. What made it hard to see them differently?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any honest answer — the point is simply naming that familiarity can make it harder, not easier, to see someone clearly.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the crowd's doubt turning into something far more dangerous — and how Jesus responds to it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: From doubt to danger":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:28–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">From doubt to danger</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the crowd in Nazareth moved from doubting Jesus to trying to physically harm him.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus walked away from the danger completely unharmed.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe how the crowd's response escalated, and whether they can offer a thoughtful idea about what Jesus calmly walking away reveals about him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From doubt to anger</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the caption with appropriate seriousness — this is intense content, so keep the tone calm and matter-of-fact rather than dramatic. Acknowledge it's a strong reaction before moving to the discussion question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-07-crowd-drives-to-cliff.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The crowd's reaction goes from doubt all the way to wanting to hurt Jesus. Why do you think people can react so strongly against someone who challenges what they thought they already knew?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful guesses — being told you're wrong about someone, or that the truth is bigger than you assumed, can feel threatening, and people sometimes respond to that feeling with anger rather than reflection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus walks away</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel. Let students notice how calm Jesus looks compared to the chaos behind him — that contrast is the point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-08-jesus-slips-away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus calmly walks away from a crowd that just tried to throw him off a cliff. What does that tell us about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't in danger the way an ordinary person would be — his time hadn't come yet, and nothing could happen to him outside of God's plan. It's another clue about just how powerful he really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide (Luke 3:3b). Read it together two or three times, then invite the class to try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then run a say-it-together activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing thought</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by naming plainly that today we've seen two very different responses to Jesus already forming — rejection in Nazareth. Tomorrow we'll pull together what we've learned about who Jesus actually is.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together — what Jesus claimed about himself, and the very different ways people respond to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Who is Jesus, and how will you respond?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 4:14–30 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who is Jesus, and how will you respond?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' claim and how people responded to it.</li><li>I can explain in my own words what Jesus claimed about himself.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse and explain what it means in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state Jesus' claim clearly and offer a thoughtful idea about why responses to him differed so sharply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched Jesus stand up in his hometown synagogue and say, out loud, that he was the promised rescuer everyone had been waiting for. Let's pull together everything we've seen.&quot; Frame this session as pulling together the whole week rather than introducing anything new.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-03-fulfilled-today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What did Jesus claim?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let students put the claim in their own words before you confirm it — this is a good check of real understanding rather than just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In your own words, what did Jesus claim about himself this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">That he is the promised rescuer-King the Scriptures spoke about — sent by God to bring good news, freedom, and healing to people who are struggling.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two responses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the cliff-edge panel again as a visual reminder of how seriously the rejection escalated, then work through the question. This is the moment the whole week's build-up pays off — don't rush it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk3-07-crowd-drives-to-cliff.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we saw two very different responses to Jesus: amazement, and then anger and rejection. Why do you think the very same claim can make one person amazed and another person furious?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — it often comes down to whether someone is willing to let go of what they thought they already knew and really listen, or whether they hold onto their assumptions instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time. Challenge the class to recite it fully from memory, with the screen as backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-from-memory activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus calling his first disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for sending Jesus, the one we've all been waiting for. Thank you that he came with good news for people who are struggling. Help us to really listen to him, instead of just thinking we already know who he is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: How perfect are you?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:1–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A bad night, and a strange instruction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:1–5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A bad night, and a strange instruction</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Peter and his friends had fished all night and caught nothing.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus told Peter to try again, in the daytime, in the deep water — against all of Peter's fishing experience.</li><li>I can explain why Peter's choice to obey anyway showed he was starting to trust Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Peter chose to obey Jesus' instruction even though it went against his own experience and expertise as a fisherman.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A rough night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene with the opening story, then show the map and washing-nets panels. Make sure students understand that fishing was Peter's actual job — his livelihood depended on nights like this going well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-00-galilee-map and wk4-01-fishermen-washing-nets.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Caught nothing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and let the disappointment of the line land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-02-caught-nothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you'd worked all night at something hard and had absolutely nothing to show for it. How do you think Peter was feeling right at this moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any reasonable answer — tired, frustrated, discouraged, maybe even a bit embarrassed in front of the watching crowd. The point is to notice Peter isn't in a great mood when Jesus arrives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus teaches, then asks again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next three panels in sequence — Jesus borrowing the boat to teach, then his surprising instruction to go back out, and Peter's tired-but-willing reply. Read Peter's line aloud slowly; it's the turning point of the day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-03-row-out-a-little, wk4-04-teaches-from-boat, and wk4-05-let-down-nets in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter's a professional fisherman — he knows this isn't a good time of day to fish, and he's exhausted. But he does it anyway because Jesus told him to. What does that tell us about how Peter was starting to see Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even though it didn't make sense based on his own experience and expertise, Peter chose to trust and obey Jesus' instruction. He's starting to recognise that Jesus is worth listening to, even when it's hard or seems pointless.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows exactly what happens when those tired, doubtful nets actually go down.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: More fish than the boats can hold":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">More fish than the boats can hold</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the catch of fish was so large it nearly sank two boats.</li><li>We are learning that the scale of this miracle points to Jesus having power over creation itself.</li><li>I can explain why the size of the catch matters to the story, not just the fact that there was a catch.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the overwhelming scale of the catch is what reveals Jesus' power, rather than treating it as just a lucky fishing story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and the catch</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quickly recap yesterday's tired, reluctant obedience, then show the first panel of today's miracle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-06-nets-fill-with-fish.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nets breaking, boats sinking</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence and read the dialogue with real energy — this is meant to feel like chaos and astonishment, not a calm fishing trip.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk4-07-help-nets-breaking and wk4-08-two-boats-sinking in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This isn't just 'quite a good catch' — it's so many fish that two boats nearly sink. Why do you think the story makes such a big deal of exactly how many fish there were?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the sheer scale makes it obvious this wasn't luck or skill — it's something only God's power could do. The size of the miracle is the point; it shows us something about who Jesus really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide and read it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Picture the scene</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the activity as a quick acting-out moment or a vivid group description — the goal is to help the chaos and scale of the miracle really land before moving into tomorrow's quieter, more serious response.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run as a brief group activity — acting out or describing the scene.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how Peter himself reacts to standing in the middle of all this — and it's not what you might expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: \"I am a sinner\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:8–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;I am a sinner&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Peter responded to the miracle by recognising his own sinfulness in front of Jesus.</li><li>We are learning that no-one is perfect except God (Romans 3:23).</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' power led Peter to think about his own sin, and how Jesus responded to that.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect Jesus' display of power to Peter's sudden awareness of his own sinfulness, and whether they can state in their own words that no-one is perfect except God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter's surprising reaction</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines of dialogue clearly — Peter's fear and confession, then Jesus' calm, kind response. Let the contrast between the two reactions sit for a moment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-09-peter-im-a-sinner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You'd think Peter would be thrilled — the biggest catch of his life! But instead, he falls down and says, &quot;Don't come near me, I am a sinner.&quot; Why do you think seeing Jesus' power made Peter think about his own sin, of all things?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: seeing this much power up close made Peter realise exactly who he was standing in front of — not just a clever teacher, but someone with power that belongs to God alone. That realisation made Peter suddenly very aware of how imperfect he was by comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where do we really stand?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run this as a quick, honest discussion rather than a game — the comparison isn't between students, it's between any of us and God's perfection. Bring in the verse once the discussion has had a chance to land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the 1–10 scale discussion, then show the Romans 3:23 verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you imagine a scale from 1 to 10, where 1 is perfectly good and 10 is the worst possible, where would most people place themselves? Now where does the Bible say God belongs on that scale?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Most people will place themselves somewhere in the middle, often closer to 'pretty good.' But Romans 3:23 says all of us have sinned and fallen short of God's glory — the real comparison isn't between people, it's between us and God's perfection, and next to that, no-one is actually close.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by drawing attention to what Jesus does next — not rejection, but a calling. This sets up tomorrow's final response from Peter and his friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus' response to Peter wasn't to agree and walk away — it was to give him a job. What does that tell us about how Jesus treats people who recognise their own sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus doesn't reject Peter for admitting he's a sinner — instead, he calls him into something new. Recognising our own imperfection isn't the end of the story with Jesus; it's actually the starting point for being used by him.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close with how Peter and his friends actually respond to that calling.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“All of us have sinned and fallen short of God's glory.” — <em>Romans 3:23 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Leaving it all behind":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:11 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaving it all behind</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' power and Peter's response to it.</li><li>I can explain why Peter and his friends chose to leave everything to follow Jesus.</li><li>I can say what this week's events showed us about who Jesus is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can name both the power and the holiness themes from this week, and whether they can offer a thoughtful idea about why the size of the miracle made following Jesus an easier choice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we've watched Peter go from an exhausting, fruitless night of fishing, to witnessing the biggest catch of his life, to realising exactly who he was standing in front of. Today we see how the story ends.&quot; Show the wide scene as a visual recap of the miracle itself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-11-wide-boat-scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what two things did we learn about Jesus this week from this one event?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus has power over creation itself (the size of the catch), and Jesus is so perfectly holy that being near him makes ordinary people aware of their own sin (Peter's reaction).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving it all behind</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and give the decision its proper weight — this wasn't a small choice. Let students wrestle with the question before offering the guidance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk4-10-leave-everything-follow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter and his friends just had the best catch of their entire careers — and then they walked away from all of it, boats and fish included, to follow Jesus. Why do you think the size of the miracle actually made that decision easier, not harder?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — having just seen exactly how much power and provision Jesus has, it made more sense to trust him with their whole future than to stay focused on the fish in front of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual — Jesus showing his authority to forgive sins and heal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for being more powerful than anything we could imagine, and thank you that you are perfect when we are not. Help us, like Peter, to be honest about the ways we fall short — and to trust you enough to follow you with our whole lives. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: Who do you think Jesus is?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:17–26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Four friends and a problem":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:17–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Four friends and a problem</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that four friends carried a man who couldn't walk to see Jesus, hoping he could heal him.</li><li>We are learning that when the crowd blocked their way, the friends found another way in — lowering their friend through the roof.</li><li>I can explain what the friends' determination shows us about their faith in Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the obstacle the friends faced (the crowded house) and explain that their unusual solution shows real faith that Jesus could help.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Going to extreme lengths for a friend</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to notice the friends' effort once the comic begins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a friend who really needed your help with something. How far would you actually go to get them what they needed — would you climb something, carry something heavy, or push through a big crowd?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses — we're building towards a story about four friends who go to genuinely extreme lengths for someone they care about. There's no wrong answer here; we just want students thinking about effort and persistence before we meet our four friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A big crowd, an important audience</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene with the first panel. Make sure students notice that Pharisees and experts in the Law of Moses are present — this detail matters later in the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-01-jesus-teaches-crowd.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Carried, but blocked</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the next two panels in sequence. Let the frustration of being blocked land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-02-man-carried-to-jesus and wk5-03-too-crowded in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends have already carried their friend all this way — and now the door is literally blocked. Most people would give up here. What does it tell you about these four friends that they didn't?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: they clearly cared enough about their friend to keep looking for another way, rather than turning back. Their persistence is the whole reason the rest of the story is even possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Through the roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the climbing panel, then give the dramatic roof-breaking panel real time and attention — it's the visual highlight of the week. Read the action slowly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-04-friends-go-upstairs and wk5-05-lowered-through-roof.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Breaking a hole in someone else's roof to lower your friend down through it is a pretty wild plan! Why do you think Luke includes this much detail about exactly how hard the friends worked to reach Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — the level of detail makes clear this wasn't a casual visit. It shows real, determined faith that Jesus could do something about this man's condition, and sets up tomorrow's question of what Jesus actually does in response.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals exactly what Jesus says to the man on the mat — and why it causes such a stir.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"Your sins are forgiven\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:20–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Your sins are forgiven&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus' first response to the man was to forgive his sins, not to heal him.</li><li>We are learning that the Pharisees and teachers of the Law reacted strongly because, in their understanding, only God can forgive sins.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' words shocked the religious leaders watching.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the Pharisees' shock came from their correct belief that only God can forgive sins, and that Jesus' words were therefore a claim about his own identity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An unexpected first response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's roof scene in a sentence, then show today's panel and let the line of dialogue sit — it's deliberately surprising.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-06-your-sins-are-forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man came to be healed of not being able to walk — but Jesus' first words are about forgiveness, not walking. Why do you think Jesus chose to deal with that first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — Jesus is showing that the deeper problem every person has isn't physical, it's sin separating us from God. He's treating that as the more important issue, even though it's not the one anyone in the room was expecting him to mention.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The religious leaders react</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the Pharisees' panel and read all three speech bubbles with real shock and outrage in your voice — this is meant to feel like a scandal in the room.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-07-pharisees-only-god-can-forgive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Pharisees and teachers of the Law knew their Scriptures extremely well, and they immediately understood the size of what Jesus just claimed. According to them, who alone has the right to forgive sins?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only God. That's exactly the Pharisees' objection — by saying 'your sins are forgiven' as if it were his own decision to make, Jesus was making a claim about himself that, if untrue, would be deeply blasphemous. They're not wrong that only God can forgive sins — they're wrong about who's standing in front of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this term's verse slide and read it together — point out that it's the same idea of forgiveness we've just seen Jesus claim the authority to give.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how Jesus answers the Pharisees' objection — with a question, and then with undeniable proof.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Proving the harder claim":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:22–24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Proving the harder claim</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus answered the Pharisees' objection with a question about which claim is easier to prove.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus healed the man visibly in order to prove his invisible authority to forgive sins.</li><li>I can explain why the healing miracle works as proof for the forgiveness claim.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, that healing is the visible miracle that proves the invisible claim of forgiveness — not two separate, unrelated acts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' counter-question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read Jesus' question slowly — give students real time to think it through before offering the guidance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-08-jesus-easier-question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about Jesus' question carefully: which of those two things — forgiving sins, or healing a paralysed man — is actually easier to prove happened, just by looking?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Healing is the easier one to prove — if the man gets up and walks, everyone can see it happened. Forgiving sins is invisible; you can't actually check whether it really happened just by looking. Jesus is about to use the visible, provable miracle to back up the invisible claim nobody could otherwise check.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The proof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the authority panel and read Jesus' command with real weight. This is the turning point of the whole passage — give it space.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-09-authority-get-up-walk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus deliberately does the miracle everyone can see, specifically to prove the claim nobody could see. What does that tell us about why Jesus healed this man — was it only about kindness, or was there something bigger going on?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it was an act of kindness, but it was also evidence. Jesus is using a visible miracle to prove an invisible truth — that he really does have the authority of God himself to forgive sins. The healing answers the Pharisees' exact objection.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close with how the man, and the crowd, respond to what they've just witnessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: \"We have seen a great miracle today\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 5:25–26 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;We have seen a great miracle today&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' authority to forgive and to heal.</li><li>I can explain how the man and the crowd responded to what Jesus did.</li><li>I can say what this week's events showed us about who Jesus is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can restate the link between the healing and the forgiveness claim, and offer a thoughtful idea about the man's instinct to thank God first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we've watched four friends go to extraordinary lengths to bring their friend to Jesus, watched Jesus make a claim that shocked the religious experts in the room, and watched him prove it in front of everyone. Today we see how it all ends.&quot; Show the roof scene again as a visual recap of how the story began.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk5-05-lowered-through-roof.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what was the big claim Jesus made this week, and what did he do to prove it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus claimed he has the authority on earth to forgive sins — something the Pharisees said only God could do. He proved it by healing the man instantly and visibly, since that's the part of the claim everyone could actually check.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Thank you God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both final panels in sequence and let the man's reaction and the crowd's amazement land before discussing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk5-10-man-thanks-god and wk5-11-crowd-amazed in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man's very first reaction, the moment he could move again, was to thank God — not to celebrate being able to walk, or to thank his friends, even though they'd worked so hard for him. What does that tell us about what he understood had really just happened to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — he seems to understand that the real gift wasn't just his legs working again, but God's forgiveness and power at work through Jesus. His instinct is gratitude towards God himself, which suggests he grasped the deeper meaning behind the miracle, not just the physical result.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Turn back to God and be baptised! Then your sins will be forgiven.” — <em>Luke 3:3b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus has the authority to forgive sins and to heal. Thank you that, like the four friends, we can bring the people we care about to Jesus — even when it's hard. Help us to respond like the man on the mat did, with real gratitude to you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: More powerful than the raging sea":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A storm swallows the boat":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A storm swallows the boat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus and his disciples set sail across the Sea of Galilee, and Jesus fell asleep during the journey.</li><li>We are learning that a sudden, violent storm struck the lake while Jesus slept.</li><li>I can explain why the disciples' fishing experience makes their terror in this storm meaningful.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the two key details of today's passage — Jesus asleep, and a storm powerful enough to frighten experienced fishermen — and connect them to build anticipation for tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Wildest storms</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about how genuinely uncontrollable storms are, before we meet someone who controls one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of the wildest storm you've ever seen or heard about — howling wind, pouring rain, huge waves. What would it actually take to make a storm like that just stop, right in the middle of it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few quick responses — most students will say 'nothing' or 'you just have to wait it out.' That's exactly the point: no person can just make a storm stop. Hold onto that thought — we're about to meet someone who does exactly that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Setting sail</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence, setting the scene calmly before the storm hits.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk6-01-lets-go-other-side and wk6-02-jesus-fallen-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The storm hits</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the storm panel and let it land before asking the question — emphasise that these were professional fishermen on their own lake.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-03-strong-wind-struck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At least four of Jesus' disciples were professional fishermen who worked on the Sea of Galilee almost every day — they knew its storms better than anyone. If experienced fishermen like these were terrified by this particular storm, what does that tell you about how powerful it really was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't ordinary bad weather that scared some inexperienced travellers — it frightened people who'd faced storms on this exact lake many times before. That's a strong clue this was a genuinely massive, life-threatening storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Asleep in the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the dramatic sleeping panel and give it real time — it's the visual highlight of today. Read the cliffhanger question slowly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-04-jesus-asleep-in-storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The storm is raging, water is pouring into the boat, and the disciples are terrified — but Jesus is still fast asleep. What do you think is going through the disciples' minds as they look over at him right now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — we're building tension, not confirming an answer yet. Most students will guess disbelief, frustration, or panic that he isn't reacting. Tomorrow we find out exactly what they do about it.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals what the terrified disciples do — and what happens the moment they wake Jesus up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"Master, Master! We're going to drown!\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Master, Master! We're going to drown!&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the terrified disciples woke Jesus and begged him to save them.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus commanded the wind and waves to stop, and they obeyed him instantly.</li><li>I can connect the storm obeying Jesus' spoken word with God speaking creation into existence in Genesis.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what Jesus did (commanded the storm, rather than praying about it or waiting) and explain why instant obedience from wind and waves is significant.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Waking Jesus up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger in a sentence, then show today's panel and read the disciples' cry with real urgency.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-05-master-were-going-to-drown.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples wake Jesus up in a total panic. What do you think they actually expected him to do about a storm this size — and why wake him up at all, if there's nothing he can do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — there's no wrong answer here. Many students will say they just wanted company, or hoped he could at least help bail water. None of them are expecting what happens next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One command, instant calm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of Jesus commanding the storm and give it real weight — this is the turning point of the whole passage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-06-jesus-orders-storm-stop.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't pray about it, wait for it to pass, or ask anyone for help — he just spoke, and the wind and waves obeyed him immediately. Where else in the Bible do we hear about something instantly obeying God's spoken word?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Genesis 1 — God speaks, and creation simply obeys ('Let there be light,' and there was light). Jesus commanding the storm the exact same way is a huge clue about who he really is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back this unit's verse slide and read it together. Point out that the same Jesus who gives eternal life, as our memory verse says, is the one we've just watched command a storm with a single word — he has power over creation itself, and over life and death.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 looks at how the disciples react once the danger is gone — and the big question they're left asking.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: \"Who is this?\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Who is this?&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that once the storm stopped, the disciples' fear of drowning turned into awe and fear of Jesus himself.</li><li>We are learning that Psalm 65:5–7 describes silencing the roaring sea as something only God can do.</li><li>I can explain why the disciples' question 'Who is this?' is really a question about whether Jesus is God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the shift in the disciples' fear (from the storm to Jesus' power) and connect the Psalm 65 passage to the disciples' question about Jesus' identity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>A CEV Bible or the built-in verse slide for Psalm 65:5–7</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Completely still</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the calm-sea panel and let the contrast with yesterday's chaos sink in before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-07-sea-completely-still.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A minute ago the disciples were terrified they were about to drown. Now the danger is completely gone — and yet the comic shows them looking just as scared as before. If it isn't the storm frightening them anymore, what do you think it is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — guide toward: they're now afraid of Jesus himself, or more precisely, of how much power he clearly has. Fear of the storm has turned into awe (and a bit of fear) at what Jesus just did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>&quot;Who is this?&quot;</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final comic panel and read both lines of dialogue with real wonder in your voice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-08-who-is-this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Only God silences the sea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Psalm 65:5–7 slowly — either from the built-in verse slide or a CEV Bible. Give students a moment to notice the specific line about silencing the roaring waves before asking the final question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in Psalm 65:5–7 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the Old Testament, the Psalm we just read says that only God himself has the power to silence the roaring waves and put the mountains in place. So when the disciples ask, 'Who is this?' — what do you think they're really starting to wonder about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: they're starting to wonder if Jesus might actually be God. The Psalm describes something only God can do, and the disciples have just watched Jesus do exactly that.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks what it actually looks like to trust Jesus, even when we're still afraid.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Our God, you save us, and your fearsome deeds answer our prayers for justice! You give hope to people everywhere on earth, even those across the sea. You are strong, and your mighty power put the mountains in place. You silence the roaring waves and the noisy shouts of the nations.” — <em>Psalm 65:5–7 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: More powerful than the raging sea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:22–25 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">More powerful than the raging sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is God's powerful Son who fulfils God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus calming the storm.</li><li>I can explain what the disciples' question 'Who is this?' points to, and how this week's events answer it.</li><li>I can describe what it looks like to trust Jesus even when we're still afraid.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can restate that Jesus' power over the storm points to his identity as God's Son, and offer a thoughtful idea about trusting Jesus in the midst of fear rather than instead of it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">&quot;This week we watched a storm so fierce it terrified experienced fishermen, watched Jesus sleep right through it, and then watched him stand up and command it to stop — instantly.&quot; Show the panel of Jesus commanding the storm again as a visual recap.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-06-jesus-orders-storm-stop.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick recap: what was the big question the disciples were left asking by the end of this story, and what have we learned this week points toward the answer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The big question was 'Who is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!' This week has pointed us toward the answer: Jesus has the same power over creation that only God has, according to Psalm 65 — which strongly suggests Jesus is God's own Son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trusting Jesus in the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the wide feature panel and let it land as the visual high point of the whole week before asking the personal application question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk6-09-jesus-calms-storm-wide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples were in the boat right next to Jesus and they were still absolutely terrified during the storm. Do you think trusting Jesus means we'll never feel scared, or is it something else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: trusting Jesus doesn't mean fear disappears — the disciples were genuinely afraid, and that's okay. The real question is who you turn to in the middle of that fear. The disciples turned to Jesus, even before they fully understood who he was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the verse slide one more time and read it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is so powerful that even the wind and the waves obey him. Thank you that we can trust him, even in the middle of our own storms and fears. Help us to remember today what we've learned about who Jesus is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 7: Faith is ...":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:40–56</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A leader begs, and a stranger's story breaks in":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:40–44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A leader begs, and a stranger's story breaks in</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jairus, a respected synagogue leader, publicly begged Jesus to heal his dying daughter.</li><li>We are learning that a sick woman in the crowd believed that simply touching Jesus' clothing would heal her.</li><li>I can explain why both Jairus' status and the woman's 12-year illness make their approach to Jesus significant.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe what each person believed about Jesus and connect it to what it cost them (Jairus' pride, the woman's quiet risk) to act on it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Begging for help</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about what it takes for a respected person to publicly ask for help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone whose job means people usually come to THEM for help — a doctor, a teacher, a leader of some kind. What would it take for that person to publicly drop to their knees and beg someone else for help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — most students will say something like a genuine emergency, or something they truly couldn't fix themselves. Hold onto that thought: today we meet an important leader who does exactly that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A crowd, and a desperate leader</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence. Give the second panel real weight — an important, respected man falling at Jesus' feet in public.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk7-01-huge-crowd-welcomed-him and wk7-02-jairus-daughter-dying.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jairus was an important, respected leader — he had a reputation to protect. Given that, why do you think he was willing to fall at Jesus' feet, right there in front of the whole crowd, and beg?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his love for his daughter and his desperate need mattered more to him than his pride or his position. Whatever people thought of him didn't matter anymore — only Jesus could help, so that's exactly what he did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A second, quieter kind of desperation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the woman's panel and let the contrast with Jairus land — a public leader begging out loud, and a sick woman planning to act in complete secrecy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-03-if-i-touch-jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us this woman had been sick for 12 years, and no doctor could cure her. She didn't ask Jesus to stop, or even to notice her — she just planned to secretly touch his clothing. What does that plan tell you about how much she trusted Jesus' power, even without him knowing she was there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: she had complete confidence in Jesus — so much that she believed just touching the edge of his robe, without him even realising, would be enough to heal her. She trusted that his power was real, whether or not he was paying attention to her specifically.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out what happens the moment the woman reaches out — and the news that interrupts everything right afterward.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: \"You're now well because of your faith\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:45–48</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;You're now well because of your faith&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the woman was healed the instant she touched Jesus' clothing.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus drew her forward publicly to name her faith, rather than letting her leave unnoticed.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus' public response to the woman mattered as much as her physical healing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Jesus asked 'who touched me?' despite already knowing, and can describe what public restoration meant for someone who'd likely been avoided for 12 years.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Healed instantly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger in a sentence, then show today's panel and ask why Jesus stopped to ask a question he already knew the answer to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-04-woman-was-healed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The moment she touched him, before Jesus even said a word, she was healed. Then Jesus stopped and asked, ‘Who touched me?’ — even though he already knew exactly what had happened. Why do you think Jesus wanted to find out, in front of everyone, who it was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't confused — he wanted this quiet, hidden act of faith brought out into the open, not to embarrass her, but so she (and everyone watching) could hear him say out loud what had really healed her: her faith, not magic or coincidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>“Not me”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of the crowd denying it — build a little suspense before revealing that the woman is about to step forward herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-05-who-touched-me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>“You're now well because of your faith”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both speech bubbles slowly — this is the emotional high point of the woman's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-06-you-are-well-because-of-faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus called her and told her she was healed because of her faith, then blessed her with peace — all out loud, in front of the crowd that had probably avoided her for years. What do you think it meant for her to be spoken to like that, publicly, after 12 years of being overlooked?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't just physical healing — it was public restoration. Jesus gave her back her dignity and her place in the community, not just her health, by naming her faith in front of everyone instead of letting her slip away unseen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Interrupted</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the final cliffhanger line slowly and let it sit — don't reveal what the news is yet.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 reveals the news that arrives for Jairus — and what Jesus says in response.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: \"Don't worry! Have faith\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:49–53</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Don't worry! Have faith&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jairus received news that his daughter had died before Jesus could reach the house.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus told Jairus to keep having faith despite the news, and that mourners mocked Jesus when he arrived.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus asking for faith in a hopeless-looking situation reveals what real faith is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can articulate that faith is tested most when circumstances look hopeless, and can describe why public mockery makes that faith harder to hold onto.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Too late?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before revealing the news — it primes students to feel the weight of what Jairus is about to hear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you'd already asked someone for help, and while you were still waiting, you got news that made it seem too late for that help to matter anymore. What would you be tempted to think in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept thoughtful answers — most students will say something like 'there's no point anymore' or 'it's hopeless now.' Hold onto that thought: today Jairus gets exactly that kind of news.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Devastating news, and a strange response</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both speech bubbles with real weight — the servant's blunt news, then Jesus' surprising instruction straight after.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-07-daughter-has-died.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told Jairus, ‘Don't worry! Have faith and your daughter will get well’ — right after Jairus had just been told it was too late. What does Jesus asking for faith at this exact moment, when the situation looked completely hopeless, tell you about what real faith actually is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: faith isn't just trusting Jesus when things look promising or easy — it's trusting him even when circumstances look hopeless. Jesus wasn't asking Jairus to believe something easy; he was asking him to keep trusting when there seemed to be no reason left to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Laughed at</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel of the mourners laughing and let the social pressure of the moment land before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-08-child-just-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The mourners had already made up their minds about what was true, and they laughed at Jesus for saying something different. Why do you think it might be hard to keep trusting Jesus when everyone else around you is completely certain he's wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it takes real courage to keep trusting Jesus when the people around you are confident, certain, and even mocking. That's exactly the kind of faith Jairus is being asked to hold onto right now — faith that doesn't depend on everyone else agreeing.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 finds out what happens next — and brings both of this week's stories together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Faith is ...":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 8:54–56 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is ...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is believing Jesus and acting on it — and it's not about how much faith you have, but who you put it in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jairus' daughter and the sick woman.</li><li>I can explain what Jairus and the woman had in common, despite how different their situations were.</li><li>I can connect Jesus' power over death in this story to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can name that faith is believing AND doing, and that it's about who you trust rather than how much you trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Child, get up!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read Jesus' two words slowly — let the simplicity of the command land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-09-child-get-up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus took the girl's hand and said just two words: ‘Child, get up!’ — and she did, instantly. Given everything we've watched Jesus do this week already, does it surprise you that his words alone could bring someone back from death? Why or why not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: by this point in the week, students should connect the pattern — if Jesus could heal a 12-year illness with just a touch of his robe, it makes sense that his power isn't limited by how serious, how hopeless, or even how dead a situation looks. His voice alone was enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two very different people, one thing in common</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel, then ask students to think back across the whole week before answering — this is the big connecting question for the unit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk7-10-daughter-back-to-life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over the whole week: Jairus, a powerful, respected leader, and the sick woman, someone many people had probably stopped noticing — they were about as different as two people could be. What was the one thing they both did that made the real difference?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the manual's key point: faith is about believing AND doing — it isn't about how much faith you have, but who you put it in. Jairus believed and acted (falling at Jesus' feet, then continuing on despite the worst possible news). The woman believed and acted (reaching out to touch his robe). Both trusted Jesus specifically — not just believed something vague.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together. Point out that the same Jesus who gives eternal life, as the verse says, is the one we've just watched reverse death itself with two words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. Today we watched Jesus give a 12-year-old girl her life back with just two words. What do you think this shows us about the eternal life Jesus promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: if Jesus has the power to reverse death on the spot for one girl, that's exactly what makes his promise of eternal life credible — it isn't just a nice idea, it's backed up by the same power we've watched him use all week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that anyone can put their faith in Jesus — whether we feel important or overlooked, whether things look hopeful or hopeless. Thank you that Jesus has power even over death. Help us to believe and act on that faith this week. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 8: The signs of life":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:18–22, 44b–45, 51</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: \"But who do you say I am?\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:18–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;But who do you say I am?&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus asked his disciples what people were saying about him, then asked them directly who THEY said he was.</li><li>We are learning that Peter answered that Jesus is the Messiah, God's promised King — the one true answer among many guesses.</li><li>I can explain the difference between being impressed by Jesus and actually recognising who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that lots of people had theories about Jesus, but only Peter's answer was actually true — and can connect this term's miracles to that same recognition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What has this term shown us?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to connect this week to everything they've already seen Jesus do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about everything we've watched Jesus do this term so far — calming a raging storm, healing a woman who'd been sick for 12 years, raising Jairus's daughter back to life. What do you think all of that adds up to? What is it starting to show you about who Jesus really is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few responses — there's no single 'right' wording yet, and that's fine. Hold onto their answers: today's story is the moment in Luke's Gospel where someone finally puts it into words out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What do people say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the first two panels in sequence, then ask the question — let students notice that all the guesses, while impressive, were wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk8-01-jesus-praying-with-disciples and wk8-02-what-do-people-say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">People's guesses were John the Baptist, Elijah, or a prophet come back to life — all seriously significant figures in Israel's history. What do you think it shows that people reached for names like that, even though every single guess turned out to be wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: people could tell Jesus was someone extraordinary and powerful — his miracles and teaching clearly pointed to something big happening. But pointing in the right direction isn't the same as arriving at the truth. Impressive guesses can still be wrong guesses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines slowly — give real weight to Peter's answer as the turning point of the passage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-03-you-are-the-messiah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of everyone's guesses that day, Peter's answer was the only one that was actually true. What's the difference between knowing lots of impressive facts about Jesus and being able to say, like Peter did, exactly who Jesus is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the manual's key idea: Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is — but a sign only does its job if you follow it all the way to what it's pointing at. Peter didn't just admire Jesus' power; he named him as the Messiah, God's promised King. That's the difference between being impressed by Jesus and actually recognising him.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out what being the Messiah is actually going to cost Jesus — and it isn't what the disciples were expecting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: A King who chooses to suffer":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:21–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A King who chooses to suffer</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus told his disciples the Messiah must suffer, be rejected, be killed, and rise to life on the third day.</li><li>We are learning that Mark 10:45 explains this suffering as Jesus giving his life to rescue many people.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus choosing to suffer is part of God's rescue plan, not a plan gone wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state that Jesus predicted his own suffering, death, and resurrection in advance, and can connect this to Mark 10:45's picture of Jesus giving his life to rescue people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Mark 10:45 displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A shocking prediction</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Peter's confession from yesterday in a sentence, then show today's panel and read the speech bubble slowly — let the weight of 'suffer... rejected... killed' land before moving to the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-04-messiah-must-suffer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were one of the disciples expecting Jesus to be a powerful King, how do you think it would feel to hear him say he was going to suffer, be rejected, and be killed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this would be shocking and confusing — many people at the time expected the promised King to defeat their enemies and rule as an earthly king, not suffer and die. Jesus is describing a completely different kind of King than they were picturing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>But three days later...</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Point back to the same panel and re-read just the last line about rising to life — make sure students notice Jesus told them the ending in advance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said he would be killed, 'but three days later he will rise to life!' Why do you think it matters that Jesus told them the ending before it even happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't a victim of circumstances he couldn't see coming — he knew exactly what was going to happen, including that it wouldn't be the end. Suffering and death were part of the plan, not a plan that failed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why suffer at all?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the Mark 10:45 verse slide and read it together, then connect it directly back to today's panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says the Son of Man — that's Jesus' own name for himself — came to 'give his life to rescue many people.' How does that help explain WHY Jesus, God's promised King, would choose to suffer and die?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't trapped into suffering — giving his life was the rescue itself. Being a slave master would mean using power to serve himself; being a slave who gives his life is choosing to use his power to save others instead, at the greatest possible cost to himself.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows how the disciples actually reacted to hearing all this — and it isn't with understanding.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“The Son of Man did not come to be a slave master, but a slave who would give his life to rescue many people.” — <em>Mark 10:45 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Too confused, too scared to ask":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:43b–45</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Too confused, too scared to ask</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the disciples did not understand Jesus' prediction about suffering, death, and resurrection, even after hearing it twice.</li><li>We are learning that the disciples were too afraid to ask Jesus what he meant.</li><li>I can explain why true information can still be hard to accept when it doesn't match what you expected.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the disciples' confusion came from Jesus' words not matching their expectations of a Messiah, not from doubting his power, and can relate this to their own experiences of being afraid to ask questions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>&quot;I don't understand&quot;</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read both lines — let the disciples' confusion feel genuine, not silly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-05-i-dont-understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples had just watched Jesus heal the sick, calm a storm, and raise a girl from the dead this term — yet this news about suffering and dying completely stumped them. Why do you think this particular part of what Jesus said was so hard to understand?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it wasn't that they doubted Jesus' power — they'd just watched proof of it all term. It's that a King who chooses to suffer and die didn't fit any picture they already had of what 'Messiah' meant. New, true information can still be hard to accept when it doesn't match what you expected.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Said again, a week later</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and note how deliberately Jesus repeats himself — this wasn't a throwaway comment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-06-pay-close-attention.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Still confused, too scared to ask</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final panel and let the honesty of 'neither do I' land before asking the question — this is a great moment to connect to students' own experience.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-07-still-dont-understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us the disciples didn't understand — and they were too scared to ask Jesus what he meant. Have you ever not understood something important, but felt too nervous to ask a question about it? What do you think held the disciples back?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: maybe they were afraid of what the answer might mean, or afraid of looking foolish, or maybe the news was just too big and sad to want more details right away. Being confused AND afraid to ask is a very human response, even for people who had already put their trust in Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks what all of Jesus' signs this term have really been pointing to.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The signs of life":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 9:51 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The signs of life</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' miracles are signs that point to who he is and what he came to do — die and rise again to save people from their sin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Peter's confession and Jesus' prediction.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus deliberately chose to go to Jerusalem, knowing what awaited him there.</li><li>I can connect this week's events to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the two review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state that Jesus' miracles are signs pointing to him as the Messiah who came to die and rise again, and can connect that to the memory verse's promise of eternal life.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Made up his mind</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read it slowly — emphasise that Jesus chose this path deliberately.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk8-08-heads-to-jerusalem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus knew exactly what was waiting for him in Jerusalem — suffering and death — and he made up his mind to go anyway. What does that tell you about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't forced into this or caught by surprise — he deliberately chose to walk toward it because it was the only way to rescue people from their sin. That's courage and love, not someone resigned to a fate they couldn't avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What have the signs been pointing to?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to think back across the whole term before answering — this is the big connecting question for the unit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over the whole term: a storm calmed, a sick woman healed, a dead girl raised to life, and now Jesus himself explaining he would suffer, die, and rise again. Putting all of that together, what would you say Jesus came to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: every sign this term has been building toward this answer — Jesus is God's promised King who came not to rule by force, but to suffer, die, and rise again so that people's sins could be forgiven. Guide students back to Peter's confession on Monday: recognising this is exactly what it means to say Jesus is the Messiah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together. Point out that the eternal life promised in the verse only became possible because of the events Jesus predicted this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. How does everything we've watched Jesus predict this week — his suffering, death, and rising to life — connect to that gift becoming possible?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the gift of eternal life isn't just a nice idea — it's only possible because Jesus actually went through with what he predicted this week. His death paid for sin, and his resurrection proves death doesn't get the final word. That's exactly what makes the memory verse true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is the Messiah, your promised King. Thank you that he chose to go toward suffering and death because he loves us, and that his rising to life three days later proves your promise of eternal life is real. Help us to be like Peter, and to say, out loud, who we believe Jesus is. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 9: To die or not to die?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:39–53</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: “Not what I want, but what you want”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:39–42</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Not what I want, but what you want”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus prayed in the Garden of Gethsemane, asking his Father if there was another way, but choosing his Father's will over his own.</li><li>We are learning that the 'cup' Jesus prayed about represents the death that sin deserves.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus chose obedience to his Father even though it meant real suffering for him.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Jesus asked God if the suffering could be avoided, but chose to go through with his Father's will anyway — and can connect the 'cup' to the punishment sin deserves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Romans 6:23a displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Facing something hard</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students to think about what it feels like to know pain is coming.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of the last time you knew something hard or painful was coming — a tricky conversation, a scary appointment, something you'd been putting off. Did you want to face it head-on, or avoid it for as long as you could?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few honest responses — most people, understandably, want to avoid pain and put off hard things. Hold onto this: today we're going to watch Jesus face the hardest, most painful night of his life, and choose not to avoid it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Into the garden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene briefly, then show the first panel and read the speech bubble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-01-pray-not-be-tested.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told his disciples to pray that they wouldn't be tested — right before facing the hardest night of his own life. Why do you think he was thinking about their struggle, even in the middle of facing his own?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was compassionate and others-focused even while dreading what was ahead for himself — a preview of the way he'll respond with compassion even to the soldiers who arrest him later this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The cup</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the second panel and read it slowly, then bring up the Romans 6:23a verse slide to explain what the 'cup' represents before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-02-father-if-you-will, then use the built-in verse block for Romans 6:23a.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus asked if there was another way to avoid the cup — the death sin deserves — but what did he decide in the end?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was completely honest about not wanting the suffering ahead — he genuinely didn't want to face it. But he chose to submit to what his Father wanted, not what he wanted. He was about to take, in our place, the death that sin deserves. That choice — obedience even when it costs everything — is the turning point of the whole passage.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows just how much that choice cost Jesus physically and emotionally — and how his own disciples responded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Sin pays off with death.” — <em>Romans 6:23a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Sweat like drops of blood":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:43–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sweat like drops of blood</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus was in such anguish in the garden that Luke describes his sweat as being like drops of blood.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus' disciples fell asleep instead of praying with him, even after he asked them to stay awake.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus was fully human and truly suffered, even while remaining obedient to his Father.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the physical and emotional weight of Jesus' anguish in the garden, and can articulate how the disciples' failure to stay awake added to that loneliness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Real anguish</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday in a sentence, then show the angel panel and describe the sweat-like-blood detail from the Bible passage before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-03-angel-from-heaven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke tells us Jesus was in so much anguish that night that his sweat fell to the ground like drops of blood, and God sent an angel to strengthen him. What does this tell you about how real this moment was for Jesus — even though he is God's own Son?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus was completely human as well as completely God. This wasn't easy for him or something he was just pretending to struggle with — the suffering ahead genuinely terrified him. Yet even in his anguish, he kept praying and stayed obedient.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Asleep when it mattered most</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the second panel and read it, then ask the question — let students sit with how isolating this moment was for Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-04-why-are-you-asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples couldn't manage to stay awake, even for one hour, on the night Jesus needed them most. How do you think it felt for Jesus to face this alone, when the people closest to him couldn't stay awake with him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like lonely, disappointed, sad. Guide toward: even the people who loved Jesus most let him down that night — yet he didn't give up on his mission or turn away from what he'd chosen to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A moment of silence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close today's session with the short silent reflection — keep it brief and genuinely quiet, no talking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in activity block: sit in silence for fifteen seconds.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows who actually arrives in the garden that night — and how Jesus responds even to the person betraying him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Betrayed with a kiss":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:47–51</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Betrayed with a kiss</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Judas betrayed Jesus with a kiss, and that Jesus was arrested by an armed crowd.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus healed the ear of one of the men arresting him, even in the middle of his own betrayal.</li><li>I can explain, using Matthew 26:53–54, that Jesus had the power to escape but chose not to use it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Jesus responded to betrayal and violence with compassion, and can connect Matthew 26:53–54 to the idea that Jesus was in control the whole time, not a helpless victim.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Matthew 26:53–54 displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A kiss that betrays</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read the speech bubble, then ask the question — give students a moment to sit with how personal this betrayal was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-05-judas-betraying-with-kiss.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Judas had been one of Jesus' own twelve disciples — someone who had walked with him for years. He used a kiss, a normal sign of friendship, as the signal to betray Jesus to the soldiers. Why do you think this detail makes the betrayal even harder to hear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a kiss is normally a sign of love and loyalty. Judas twisted something good into the very method of betrayal. It shows just how far Jesus' own friend was willing to go against him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Enough of that</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show both panels in sequence — the sword question, then the healing — and read them together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk9-06-should-we-attack and wk9-07-enough-of-that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus was about to be unfairly arrested by these very men, yet the first thing he did was heal one of them. What does this tell you about the kind of King Jesus really is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus responded to violence and betrayal with compassion, even toward someone actively working against him. This is exactly the kind of King the disciples didn't expect — one who heals his enemies rather than destroying them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>12 armies of angels</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the verse slide and read it together, then connect it back to Monday's prayer before asking the final question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Matthew 26:53–54.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus points out that he could have called down more than 12 armies of angels to rescue himself — he had all the power he needed to walk away completely unharmed. Why do you think he chose not to use that power?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't weak or powerless in this moment — he was in complete control the whole time. He chose not to use his power to save himself because rescuing us required him not to rescue himself. This connects directly to Monday's prayer: 'not what I want, but what you want.'</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week together and asks the biggest question of all: why did Jesus go through with any of this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't you know that I could ask my Father, and right away he would send me more than 12 armies of angels? But then, how could the words of the Scriptures come true, which say that this must happen?” — <em>Matthew 26:53–54 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Why Jesus chose this":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 22:51–53 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus chose this</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible explains that Jesus chose to die on the cross and rise again because he loves us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about Jesus' prayer, betrayal, and arrest.</li><li>I can explain that Jesus chose to go through all of this because he loves us, not because he was forced into it.</li><li>I can connect this week's events to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can state that Jesus chose to suffer, be betrayed, and be arrested out of love, and can connect that choice to the promise of eternal life in the memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and feature image (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slide)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Darkness is in control</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's panel and read Jesus' words slowly before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk9-08-why-treat-me-like-criminal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus points out that the crowd could have arrested him any day, out in the open at the temple — but they came at night, in secret, with weapons. What do you think Jesus meant when he said 'darkness is in control'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this wasn't really about swords and clubs — it was spiritual darkness and opposition to Jesus finally acting against him. Yet even this dark moment was still part of God's plan, not a plan going wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why did Jesus go through with it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the wide garden image and ask students to think back across the whole week before answering — this is the big connecting question for the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show feature image wk9-09-gethsemane-wide-shot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over everything we've watched this week: Jesus praying in agony, being betrayed by a friend, being arrested by an armed crowd — and choosing not to use his power to escape any of it. Why do you think Jesus went through with all of this?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few responses, then guide toward the week's big idea: Jesus didn't have to go through any of this. He chose to, because he loves us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The greatest way to show love</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the John 15:12–14 verse slide and read it together, then connect it directly back to the week's events before moving to the final two verses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse blocks for John 15:12–14, John 3:16–17, and Romans 6:23b in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says the greatest way to show love for friends is to die for them. How does everything we watched this week — the prayer, the betrayal, the arrest — show that this is exactly what Jesus was doing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wasn't a victim of bad luck. Every choice he made this week, starting with 'not what I want but what you want,' was Jesus deliberately loving us enough to go through with the suffering ahead, all the way to the cross.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Challenge the class to recite the memory verse together fully from memory, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 10 moves on to the next chapter of the Connect B1 Upper Primary manual.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Now I tell you to love each other, as I have loved you. The greatest way to show love for friends is to die for them. And you are my friends, if you obey me.” — <em>John 15:12–14 (CEV)</em><br>“God loved the people of this world so much that he gave his only Son, so that everyone who has faith in him will have eternal life and never really die. God did not send his Son into the world to condemn its people. He sent him to save them!” — <em>John 3:16–17 (CEV)</em><br>“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus chose to go through the garden, the betrayal, and the arrest, because he loves us. Thank you that even in his most painful moments he stayed obedient to you and full of compassion toward others. Help us to trust how much he loves us. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 10: Even stronger than death!":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24; 1 Corinthians 15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Death couldn't hold him down":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 23:44–56; 24:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Death couldn't hold him down</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus died on the cross and was buried in a tomb with a stone rolled in front of it.</li><li>We are learning that some women went to the tomb on the third day and found it empty.</li><li>I can explain that the disciples did not expect or understand that Jesus would rise from the dead.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can recall that Jesus' body was placed in a sealed tomb, and can articulate that the women were confused and afraid when they found it empty rather than expecting a resurrection.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>If someone came back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question before introducing today's story — it primes students for the shock the characters in the story are about to feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we left Jesus being arrested in the garden, with darkness ‘in control.’ If somebody you loved died, and then a few days later you heard they were alive again, what would you think when you first heard that news?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a few honest responses — most kids will land on disbelief, thinking it's a trick, or wanting proof for themselves. Hold onto that reaction, because it's exactly how this week's story unfolds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The cross and the tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly explain that after being arrested, Jesus was put on trial and crucified. Show both panels in sequence and read the captions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-01-crucifixion and wk10-02-body-in-tomb in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Did they believe him?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remind students of Lesson 8's comic, where the disciples said ‘I don't understand’ when Jesus told them he would die and rise again. Ask the question and let students reason through it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus had told his disciples again and again that he would die and then rise again on the third day — we even saw them say ‘I don't understand’ to that news a couple of weeks ago. Do you think the disciples believed him at this point, or were they expecting Jesus to actually come back?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — the disciples didn't understand or believe Jesus' own warnings about his death and resurrection, so when it actually happened, they were devastated and grieving, not hopeful or expectant. That's important context for what happens next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An empty tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene — three days later, early on Sunday morning, some women went to the tomb. Show both panels and read the speech bubbles, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-03-women-who-rolled-stone and wk10-04-tomb-is-empty in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The women expected to find Jesus' dead body in the tomb — but it wasn't there. What do you think might have run through their minds in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — confusion, fear, maybe they thought someone had stolen the body. This is genuinely open at this point in the story; we find out what's really going on tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 reveals who the women meet at the tomb, and what happens when they run to tell the disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: He's not here — he's alive!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:4–12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">He's not here — he's alive!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that two men in bright clothes told the women Jesus had risen, just as he said he would.</li><li>We are learning that the women told the disciples, but the disciples didn't believe them at first.</li><li>I can explain that even Peter, after seeing the empty tomb himself, was still confused rather than convinced.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that the two men's message was a fulfilled promise, not a surprise twist, and can describe how the disciples and Peter responded with doubt rather than instant belief.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; two men appear</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday in a sentence, then show the panel and read the speech bubble before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-05-two-men-appear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The two men said Jesus had been ‘raised from death’ — not stolen, not moved, but alive again. How is that different from what the women were probably expecting to hear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the women were likely expecting either a dead body, or an empty tomb meaning someone had stolen it. The idea that a dead man was now truly alive again was something else entirely — not just surprising, but seemingly impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Remember what Jesus said</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it, then connect back to Week 1's Zechariah before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-06-remember-what-jesus-said.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The two men reminded the women of something Jesus had already told them would happen. Why do you think it can still be hard to believe a promise, even when someone told you exactly what would happen beforehand?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses. Guide toward: this is the same pattern we've seen all term — back in Week 1, Zechariah struggled to believe an angel's promise even face to face. Believing a promise before you see it come true is genuinely hard, even with the clearest warning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peter runs to the tomb</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show all three panels in sequence and read them, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic images wk10-07-women-tell-disciples, wk10-08-peter-runs-to-tomb, and wk10-09-strips-of-linen in sequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter saw the empty tomb with his own eyes, exactly as the women had said. Do you think Peter was convinced yet that Jesus was really alive?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Luke tells us Peter went away ‘wondering what had happened’ — even seeing the evidence himself, he was still confused, not yet convinced. It will take actually seeing Jesus in person to change that, which is where tomorrow's story picks up.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows what happens when Jesus himself finally stands in front of his disciples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Touch me and see":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:36–43</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Touch me and see</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus appeared in person to his disciples about a week after the resurrection.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus proved he had a real physical body by inviting the disciples to touch him and by eating in front of them.</li><li>I can explain why physical proof mattered for convincing the disciples — and for us today.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least two ways Jesus proved his resurrection was physical and real (being touched, eating food), and can explain why that evidence matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Peace be with you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Tuesday in a sentence, then show the panel and read the speech bubble before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-10-peace-be-with-you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn't just appear as a memory or an idea — he stood right there in the room with his disciples. How do you think they felt in that moment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like shocked, terrified, confused. Guide toward: Luke tells us they thought he was a ghost — which sets up exactly what Jesus does next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Look at my hands and feet</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it slowly, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-11-look-at-my-hands-and-feet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told his disciples to look at his hands and feet and even touch him. Why do you think he wanted them to physically see and touch him, rather than just telling them ‘it's really me’?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus wanted to prove, beyond any doubt, that this wasn't a ghost or their imagination playing tricks on them. He had a real physical body, truly raised from death — not just a spirit or a vision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Have you got something to eat?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the panel and read it, then ask the closing question and connect it to why this evidence still matters today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-12-have-you-got-something-to-eat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus even asked for food and ate it in front of everyone — ghosts don't eat! Why do you think a detail like this matters for us today, thousands of years later?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it's real, physical, historical evidence that convinced eyewitnesses at the time. It's part of why we can trust the resurrection actually happened, rather than being just a story or a feeling someone had.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the week to a close, showing where Jesus went next and why his resurrection is such good news for us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Jesus is alive — and so can we be":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 24:44–53; Acts 2:23–24; 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is alive — and so can we be</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is more powerful than death — he rose from the dead, proving he is God's Son, and now offers eternal life to everyone who trusts him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together everything we've learned this week about the resurrection, ascension, and what it all means.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus needed to both die and rise again to deal with sin and death.</li><li>I can connect the eyewitness evidence for the resurrection to the promise of eternal life in our memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the week's wrap-up — use the review questions as an informal check of understanding. Listen for whether students can explain why the resurrection matters (not just that it happened), and can connect it to the promise of eternal life in the memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panels (already embedded in the app)</li><li>Memory verse and supporting verses displayed on screen (built into the app's verse slides)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why die AND rise again?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week in a sentence, then show today's first panel and read it slowly before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-13-jesus-explains-scriptures.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said all of this — his death and his resurrection — happened so people could repent and be forgiven. Why do you think Jesus needed to do both — die AND rise again — rather than just one or the other?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: his death paid the punishment for sin, but if he'd stayed dead, that would mean death and sin had won after all. Because he rose again, we know death has been defeated, and his followers can share in that same new life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Taken up to heaven</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the ascension panel, then bring up the John 14:1–3 verse slide and read it together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-14-taken-up-to-heaven, then use the built-in verse block for John 14:1–3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus promised he's preparing a place in heaven and will come back for his followers. How does knowing Jesus is alive right now, doing exactly that, change how his followers can think about their own future?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any responses — hope, confidence, something to look forward to rather than fear. There's no single right answer; encourage students to put it in their own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Death could not hold him</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the final comic panel, then bring up the Acts 2:23–24 verse slide and read it together before asking the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic image wk10-15-return-to-jerusalem-praising, then use the built-in verse block for Acts 2:23–24.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says death ‘could not hold him in its power.’ Why is Jesus being more powerful than death such good news for anyone who trusts in him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: because Jesus conquered death, everyone who trusts him can share in that same victory — death is not the final word for those who belong to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Eyewitnesses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a verse slide and read it together, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for 1 Corinthians 15:3–8a.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse lists specific eyewitnesses — Peter, the twelve, over five hundred people, James, and all the apostles. Why do you think Paul was so careful to list exactly who saw Jesus alive?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Paul is presenting evidence, not just retelling a nice story. Many of those eyewitnesses were still alive when he wrote this and could be asked directly. This is part of why we can be confident today that the resurrection is a real historical event, not a legend.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's gift is eternal life</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring up the Romans 6:23b memory verse slide and read it together, then ask the final connecting question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Romans 6:23b.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says God's gift is eternal life, given through Jesus. How does everything we've seen this week — the empty tomb, Jesus eating and being touched, his promise to prepare a place for us — make that gift feel real, rather than just a nice idea?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: because Jesus really did rise physically and is alive right now, his promise of eternal life for those who trust him isn't wishful thinking — it's backed up by something that actually happened in history.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Challenge the class to recite the memory verse together fully from memory, with the screen as a backup only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">7</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week — and the term's unit on Luke's Gospel — together with the prayer in the app, said as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 11 begins a new term and a new unit for the B1 cycle.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't be worried! Have faith in God and have faith in me. There are many rooms in my Father's house. I wouldn't tell you this, unless it was true. I am going there to prepare a place for each of you. After I have done this, I will come back and take you with me. Then we will be together.” — <em>John 14:1–3 (CEV)</em><br>“God had already planned and decided that Jesus would be handed over to you. So you took him and had evil men put him to death on a cross. But God set him free from death and raised him to life. Death could not hold him in its power.” — <em>Acts 2:23–24 (CEV)</em><br>“Christ died for our sins, as the Scriptures say. He was buried, and three days later he was raised to life, as the Scriptures say. Christ appeared to Peter, then to the twelve. After this, he appeared to more than five hundred other followers — most of them are still alive, but some have died. He also appeared to James, and then to all of the apostles. Finally, he appeared to me.” — <em>1 Corinthians 15:3–8a (CEV)</em><br>“God's gift is eternal life given by Jesus Christ our Lord.” — <em>Romans 6:23b (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus is more powerful than death, and that he rose again just as he promised. Thank you that his resurrection means we can trust every one of his promises, including the promise of eternal life. Help us to live with hope because Jesus is alive. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: Let the exploring begin!":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: In the beginning...":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1–2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God created everything, including people, and that all of it was very good.</li><li>We are learning that Adam and Eve lived in a perfect relationship with God, each other, and creation.</li><li>I can say this term's new memory verse, Genesis 1:1.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe creation as ‘very good’ in their own words, and can explain what made Eden's relationships between God, people, and creation perfect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 1:1 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Exploring, starting today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open by framing the whole term as an exploring theme leading to Abraham next week, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we're going exploring — all the way back to the very start of the Bible, to a story that leads up to a man named Abraham, who we'll meet next week. If you were exploring a brand new place, what's the first thing you'd want to work out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like a map, landmarks, or a starting point. Guide toward: good explorers look for patterns, so they know what's coming next. That's exactly what we're doing today — looking for a pattern right from the first page of the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Everything, and it was very good</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the two creation story blocks together, keeping the tone celebratory — this is the high point before things go wrong tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Your best day ever</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the question and let students share freely — there's no wrong answer here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about one of the best days you've ever had. What made it so good?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answers. Use this to help students imagine just how good ‘very good’ must have felt for Adam and Eve — no sadness, no arguments, nothing broken between them and God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This term's memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Genesis 1:1 as the new memory verse for this term's unit and say it together a few times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows what happens when that perfect relationship breaks down for the first time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: The first cycle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The first cycle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Adam and Eve chose to disobey God even though he had already given them everything they needed.</li><li>We are learning that God's response to their sin included real consequences, but also real grace.</li><li>I can describe the pattern SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Adam and Eve did wrong, name a consequence they faced, and identify the clothing God made them as an act of grace.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 3:21 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: a perfect start</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap yesterday's perfect relationship between Adam, Eve, and God before introducing the turn.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The one thing they wanted</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the one forbidden tree and the decision to disobey, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Adam and Eve already had a perfect world — but they still wanted the one thing they weren't allowed to have. Why do you think it can be so tempting to want the one thing you can't have, even when you're surrounded by good things already?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — there's no single right answer here. It's a very human pattern: focusing on the one limit rather than everything we already have.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Consequences, then grace</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the consequences of the sin, then reveal that God still made them clothes before sending them out, and ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God could have simply given up on Adam and Eve after this. Instead, before sending them out of the garden, he made them clothes with his own hands. What does that tell you about how God felt toward them, even right after they had sinned against him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows God still cared for them practically, and that being sent out was part of a plan to eventually save them — not to abandon them for good.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the pattern</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE pattern out loud together so it sticks before the memory verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together, trying it once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the same pattern play out again — this time for the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: The cycle again":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The cycle again</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that sin spread across the whole world as more people filled the earth.</li><li>We are learning that God judged the world's sin with a flood, but showed grace to Noah's family and made a covenant with a rainbow as its sign.</li><li>I can spot the SIN → CONSEQUENCES → GRACE pattern for a second time in this unit.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can identify the flood as the consequence and the rainbow covenant as the grace in this second cycle, without needing it spelled out for them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Same pattern, bigger scale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Frame today as the same pattern from yesterday, now playing out for the whole world rather than just two people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sin spreads</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how sin spread as the population grew, then ask the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">That's a pretty extreme description — hearts that were only evil, all the time. Why do you think sin tends to spread and grow worse over time, instead of staying small and contained?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: sin doesn't stay isolated — it spreads through choices, relationships, and generations, a bit like the plague described earlier in the manual's Bible background.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The flood and the ark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the flood and ark story briefly, keeping the focus on judgement plus rescue, not on frightening detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A promise with a rainbow</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reveal the rainbow covenant, then ask the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Once again we see SIN, then CONSEQUENCES... and then GRACE — an undeserved promise, with a rainbow as its sign. Why do you think God chose to give people a visible reminder of his promise, rather than just making the promise and leaving it there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: physical reminders help us remember and trust promises we can't always feel or see day to day — it's a pattern God uses more than once in the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, aiming for fully from memory this time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Genesis 1:1, then the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 shows the pattern one more time — but with a piece missing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: A cycle with a piece missing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 11:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A cycle with a piece missing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Right from the very beginning, people keep breaking their relationship with God through sin — but every single time, God responds with grace instead of giving up on them. Genesis 1–11 sets the stage for the biggest act of grace yet: God's promises to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the people at Babel sinned through pride, trying to make a name for themselves instead of trusting God's plan.</li><li>We are learning that this third cycle ends with consequences but no visible grace — an unfinished pattern.</li><li>I can explain why the missing grace at Babel sets up next week's introduction to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can identify that grace is missing from this third cycle, and can hold the tension of ‘has God stopped loving people?’ without needing it resolved today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: two cycles so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the two completed cycles from earlier in the week before introducing Babel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A tower to the sky</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the Tower of Babel story, then ask the pride question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The people wanted to ‘make a name for themselves’ instead of trusting God's plan for them. What's the difference between working hard at something, and trying to make yourself great instead of trusting God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: the problem isn't hard work or ambition itself — it's pride that rejects God's plan and tries to put ourselves at the centre instead of him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scattered — and something's missing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the scattering and confusion of languages, then ask students to spot what's missing from the pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">SIN, then CONSEQUENCES... but think back to the pattern from the last two days. What's missing this time?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: there's no GRACE step this time — the cycle looks broken and unfinished, as if God has stopped being kind to his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Has God stopped loving people?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the harder question and let it sit — don't over-resolve it, since next week's lesson is the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does that mean God has really stopped loving the people he made?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — the Bible tells us God never stops loving the people he made. It's fine to leave this question sitting unresolved for now; the class finds out how God answers it next week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 12 introduces Abraham, and how God's promises to him begin to answer the missing grace from this week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — <em>Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are loving and kind, and that you keep showing us grace even when we don't deserve it. Please help us trust that you haven't stopped loving your people, even when we can't yet see how. Help us learn all about your plan for Abraham this term. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: Promises, promises, promises":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meet the explorer":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meet the explorer</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God called an ordinary man named Abraham to leave his home and go to a land God would show him.</li><li>We are learning that Abraham trusted and obeyed God, even without knowing exactly where he was going.</li><li>I can say this term's new memory verse, Numbers 23:19, and explain what it means for God to ‘keep’ a promise.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell who Abraham was and what God asked him to do, and can describe Abraham's response as trust rather than certainty.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 12:1–7 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Exploring without a map</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about exploring somewhere new with no map, then bridge into meeting Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were about to go exploring somewhere brand new, with no map and no idea exactly where you'd end up, what's one thing that would make it hard to say yes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses like fear of the unknown, leaving family/friends behind, or not knowing what to pack. Use this to set up how big a deal Abraham's obedience is about to be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An ordinary man</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Abraham as an ordinary descendant of Noah, living his everyday life in Haran with Sarah and Lot, before God spoke to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out God's call</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose four volunteers for Abraham, Sarah, Lot, and God, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your four volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Could you trust that much?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the question and let students be honest about how hard that would be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham left his home country without knowing exactly where he'd end up. Do you think you could trust someone enough to do that — pack up and go, just because they told you to?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: it's a huge ask, which is exactly why the Bible holds Abraham up as such a striking example of trust in God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This term's memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Numbers 23:19 as the new memory verse for this unit and say it together for the first time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 unpacks the three specific promises God made to Abraham in that call.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 12 script: God calls Abraham</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Abraham, Sarah, Lot, God</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hi, my name is Abraham. I live in the land of Haran with my wife Sarah and my nephew Lot, and all our extended family.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Sarah and Lot enter.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One day, I was going about my business as usual when God spoke to me. He said...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leave your country, your family, and your relatives, and go to the land that I will show you. I will bless you and make your descendants into a great nation. You will become famous and be a blessing to others. I will bless anyone who blesses you, but I will put a curse on anyone who puts a curse on you. Everyone on earth will be blessed because of you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">It all seemed pretty strange to me. I was 75 years old when the LORD told me to leave Haran. Sarah was only a little younger than me, and we didn't have any children. But I obeyed and left. I took my wife Sarah, Lot, and all the possessions and servants I had collected while in Haran.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham, Sarah, and Lot act out packing their bags and begin walking on the spot.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We walked and walked for ages until finally we came to the land of Canaan. We went as far as the sacred tree of Moreh, in a place called Shechem, and stopped there.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham, Sarah, and Lot stop walking on the spot.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Canaanites were still living in the land at that time, but the LORD appeared and spoke to me again, and promised...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I will give this land to your family forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was a pretty amazing promise! So I built an altar there for the LORD and thanked him for the promises he made to me. I knew that God would keep the promises he made. So I trusted him!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham acts out building a stone mound.)</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Three big promises":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Three big promises</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the three specific promises God made to Abraham: land, a great nation, and blessing to the whole world.</li><li>We are learning that these promises would have seemed almost impossible to Abraham and Sarah at the time.</li><li>I can name all three promises God made to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can list all three promises (land, great nation, blessings to the world) without prompting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap: God calls Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap yesterday's roleplay before unpacking the promises in detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Land, nation, blessing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce each of the three promises one at a time, giving students a moment to picture what each one meant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The hardest promise to believe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which promise would have felt hardest to believe, given Abraham and Sarah's circumstances.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At the time, Abraham and Sarah didn't own any land, and didn't have any children at all. Which of those three promises do you think would have felt hardest for them to believe?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — there's no single right answer. Guide toward: all three would have seemed almost impossible at the time, which is exactly why trusting God's promise mattered so much.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promises for us too?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Plant the idea that these promises reach beyond Abraham to us today, without fully resolving it yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible says these promises weren't just for Abraham back then — they matter for people today too, including us. Why do you think a promise made to one man, thousands of years ago, could still matter for us now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the third promise — blessing the whole world — is the big clue. It's fine if students can't fully answer this yet; it's something we'll keep discovering this term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 looks at how Abraham actually responded to these promises — by trusting and obeying, one step at a time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Obeying without knowing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Obeying without knowing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham obeyed God immediately, packing up his whole household and setting off without knowing exactly where he'd end up.</li><li>We are learning that when God repeated his promise at Shechem, Abraham responded by building an altar and worshipping God publicly.</li><li>I can describe Abraham's obedience as trusting one step at a time, rather than seeing the whole plan in advance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that Abraham obeyed without knowing the destination, and can identify the altar at Shechem as a public act of worship and trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 12:4–7 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From promise to action</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bridge from yesterday's three promises into how Abraham actually responded to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Packing up at 75</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Abraham obeyed immediately and set off not knowing his destination, then ask the trust question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What's the difference between trusting a plan when you can see every single step in advance, and trusting a plan when you can only see the very next step?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: real trust is shown most clearly when you can't see the whole picture — that's exactly the kind of trust Abraham showed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An altar at Shechem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God repeated the land promise at Shechem and Abraham responded by building an altar, then ask the worship question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham didn't just say a quiet 'thank you' in his head — he built an altar and worshipped God out in the open, where anyone could see. Why do you think he responded that publicly, rather than keeping it to himself?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: worship isn't just private gratitude — it's a way of publicly declaring that we trust and honour God, and it can encourage others around us too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, aiming for fully from memory this time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the three promises back together and asks the big question: is God really a promise keeper?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Is God a promise keeper?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Is God a promise keeper?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Last week's pattern seemed to break at Babel, with no grace in sight. This week we find out God never stopped loving his people — he had a plan all along, starting with an ordinary man named Abraham. Through Abraham, and the promises God makes to him, the whole world will be blessed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham's story answers last week's unresolved question — God never stopped loving the people he made.</li><li>We are learning that God's promises to Abraham were his plan to bless the whole world, undoing the scattering at Babel.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, why the Bible calls God a promise keeper.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect Abraham's story back to the missing grace from last week's Babel cycle, and can explain in their own words why God is described as a promise keeper.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Abraham, the three promises, and his obedience before asking the big question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promise keeper or promise breaker?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the central question of the week and guide students firmly toward the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So here's the big question this whole week has been building to: based on what we've seen so far, do you think God is a promise keeper, or a promise breaker?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, but guide firmly toward: God is a promise keeper — that's exactly what this term's memory verse tells us, and it's what we'll keep seeing all term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The missing grace, found</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Connect Abraham's call directly back to last week's unfinished Babel cycle — the grace was on its way all along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What this means for us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what it means personally that God keeps his promises, letting students make it their own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible tells us these promises weren't only for Abraham — they're for people today too, including us. What do you think it means for you personally that God keeps his promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: if God always keeps his word, that means we can trust him with the big things in our own lives too, not just admire Abraham's story from a distance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 13 shows that even though God keeps every promise, Abraham has to learn to wait a long time for this one to come true.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn’t tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the promises you made to Abraham. Thank you that you are a God who keeps every promise you make, even when we can't see how yet. Please help us trust you the way Abraham did, one step at a time. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: Waiting, waiting, waiting":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 15:1–18:15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 13</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Beginning to wonder":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 15:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Beginning to wonder</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that years passed after God's promises to Abraham with no child being born, and that Abraham began to doubt.</li><li>We are learning that God reassured Abraham with the promise of descendants as countless as the stars.</li><li>I can explain that Abraham trusted God even though he couldn't see how the promise would come true.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the passing of time (rather than the original promise itself) tested Abraham's trust, and can describe the stars promise in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 15:4b–5 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A message you can't yet see</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the sealed-message/time-capsule warm-up question to introduce the theme of trusting something you can't yet see come true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you sealed up a message today that wouldn't be opened for years and years. Do you think it would be easy or hard to trust that it was still safely there, waiting, the whole time you couldn't see it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This is the idea behind a time capsule — something sealed away and easy to forget about, even though it's still there. Use it to introduce this week's big theme: waiting for a promise you can't yet see come true.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and the problem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap last week's three promises, then explain that years passed with still no child for Abraham and Sarah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Waiting shakes trust</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why an ongoing wait can be harder to trust through than the original decision, letting students reflect honestly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham had trusted God enough to leave his whole home behind. But now, years later, with nothing to show for it yet, he was starting to doubt. Why do you think waiting can shake our trust more than the original hard decision did?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it's often easier to trust God for one big decision than to keep trusting him through a long, uncertain wait with no answer in sight — that ongoing trust is a different, harder kind of faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Count the stars</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 15:4b–5 together and unpack why God used a promise too big to count.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham couldn't count the stars — there were far too many. Why do you think God chose a promise like that, one that was literally impossible to count, instead of just giving Abraham a number?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it showed the promise was bigger than Abraham could ever measure or fully picture — trusting it meant trusting God's word, not just doing the maths.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close by explaining that Abraham's trust was credited to him as righteousness, then say this term's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 shows that even Abraham and Sarah, while they waited, sometimes struggled to trust God and tried to take matters into their own hands.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You will have a son of your own, and everything you have will be his.” Then the Lord took Abram outside and said, “Look at the sky and see if you can count the stars. That's how many descendants you will have.” — <em>Genesis 15:4b–5 (CEV)</em><br>“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Taking matters into their own hands":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 16</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Taking matters into their own hands</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Sarah and Abraham grew impatient waiting for God's promise and tried to solve it themselves, through Hagar and Ishmael.</li><li>We are learning that this shortcut caused jealousy and trouble in their family rather than solving anything.</li><li>I can explain that even faithful people can struggle to trust God's timing, and that God stayed faithful anyway.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Sarah and Abraham did instead of waiting, and can describe the consequence without treating the story as simple failure — aim for empathy alongside honesty.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Still waiting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that despite the star promise, more time passed with still no child.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The temptation to force it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question so students can relate personally before hearing today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever wanted something so badly, and waited so long for it, that you were tempted to just make it happen yourself instead of waiting any longer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — most people have felt this. Use it to set up today's story with empathy rather than judgement: waiting is genuinely hard, even for people the Bible holds up as examples of great faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sarah's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of Hagar and Ishmael and the trouble it caused, keeping the tone gentle rather than harshly critical.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Even faithful people struggle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what this reveals about how hard waiting on God can be, guiding students away from judging Abraham and Sarah too harshly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah and Abraham loved God and had already shown real faith in him — but they still tried to solve the waiting their own way, and it went badly. What does that tell us about how hard waiting on God can be, even for people who trust him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: even people the Bible admires for their faith still struggled with impatience — this isn't really a story about Abraham and Sarah failing so much as a story about how genuinely hard waiting is, and how easy it is to want to force our own solution instead of trusting God's timing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God stayed faithful anyway</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Emphasise that God didn't abandon Abraham and Sarah despite their shortcut, then say the memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 jumps forward to when Abraham is 99 years old, as God renews his promise and gives Abraham and Sarah brand new names.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Father of many nations":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 17:1–5, 15–17; 18:10a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Father of many nations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that twenty-four years after the first promise, at age 99, Abraham still had no child of his own.</li><li>We are learning that God renewed his covenant with Abraham, changing his and Sarah's names to reflect the promise still to come.</li><li>I can explain that God gave Abraham and Sarah a specific one-year timeline for their son to be born.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state Abraham and Sarah's ages at this point in the story, and can explain why their names were changed before the promise came true.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 and 18:10a aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Twenty-four birthdays later</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Emphasise just how long twenty-four years is, then ask the reflection question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you'd been waiting twenty-four years for something you'd been promised, do you think you'd still be expecting it to happen — or would you have given up hope by then?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Use it to help students feel the weight of just how long this wait really was — this isn't a short story, it's a quarter of a century.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New names, renewed promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 together and explain the covenant renewal and name changes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why change the names now?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God changed their names before the promise was fulfilled.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham means 'father of many', and Sarah means 'mother of nations' — new names that only made sense if the promise came true. Why do you think God chose to change their names before Isaac had even been born?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it gave Abraham and Sarah a daily reminder of God's promise, every single time anyone said their names — a sign of God's certainty, even before the promise was fulfilled.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One year from now</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 18:10a and ask how it would feel to finally have an exact timeline after so long.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After twenty-four years of waiting with no timeline at all, God suddenly gave Abraham and Sarah an exact promise: one year. How do you think it would feel to finally have a date to look forward to, after waiting that long?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — likely a mix of excitement, disbelief, and maybe still some doubt after so many years of nothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together, once without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 acts out that visit to Abraham's tent, hears Sarah's reaction, and finds out whether God really did keep his promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“I promise that you will be the father of many nations. That's why I now change your name from Abram to Abraham … Abraham, your wife's name will now be Sarah instead of Sarai. I will bless her, and you will have a son by her. She will become the mother of nations, and some of her descendants will even be kings.” — <em>Genesis 17:4–5, 15–16 (CEV)</em><br>One of the guests was the Lord, and he said, “I'll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.” — <em>Genesis 18:10a (CEV)</em><br>“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Is God a promise keeper?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Is God a promise keeper?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept every promise he made to Abraham and Sarah — but it took twenty-five long years. Waiting is hard, and Abraham and Sarah sometimes struggled to trust God's timing along the way. But God never forgot, and he never gave up. Christians believe God can always be trusted to keep his promises, even if he takes far longer than we expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when the Lord visited Abraham's tent and told Sarah she would have a son within the year.</li><li>We are learning that despite Sarah's disbelief, God kept his promise exactly on time — Isaac was born when Abraham was 100 and Sarah was 90.</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, why the Bible calls God a promise keeper even when a promise takes a long time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell the visit to Abraham's tent and Sarah's reaction in their own words, and can explain why God is described as a promise keeper despite the twenty-five year wait.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Volunteers to read Abraham, the LORD, Sarah, and Narrator for the roleplay</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out the visit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose four volunteers for Narrator, Abraham, the LORD, and Sarah, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your four volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Would you have believed it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether students think they'd have believed the promise in Sarah's position.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah thought the idea of having a baby at 89 was so unlikely that she laughed. Do you think you would have believed the promise if you were her — after everything that had already happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: it's completely understandable that Sarah struggled to believe it after so many years — the question isn't really whether her doubt makes sense, but whether God kept his word anyway.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaac is born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how, exactly one year later, Isaac was born and explain the meaning of his name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promise keeper or promise breaker?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the central question of the week and guide students firmly toward the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So here's the big question this whole week has been building to: after twenty-five years of waiting, doubting, and even trying to take matters into their own hands — did God turn out to be a promise keeper, or a promise breaker?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, but guide firmly toward: God is a promise keeper — he kept every single promise, exactly as he said he would, even though it took twenty-five years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What are you waiting for?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Revisit Monday's sealed-message idea, then invite students to name something they're waiting on God for personally.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God's timing wasn't the same as Abraham and Sarah's. Is there something in your own life that you're waiting for right now, where it's hard to trust God's timing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest, personal responses — this can be kept private if a student prefers. Guide toward: waiting is genuinely hard, but this week's story is proof that God's promises are worth trusting even when the timing feels slow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 14 moves on to a drama based on the story of Isaac and Rebekah.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 13 script: Three visitors at Mamre</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Abraham, The LORD, Sarah</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One hot afternoon, Abraham was resting at the entrance of his tent near the great trees of Mamre. He looked up and saw three visitors standing nearby.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham jumps up and hurries over, bowing low.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please, don't go on without resting a while. Let me bring water to wash your feet, and some bread to give you strength before you continue your journey.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Very well. Do as you have said.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham hurries to Sarah's tent.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sarah — quickly! Take our best flour and bake some bread for our guests!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Abraham chooses a tender calf to be prepared, then serves the visitors as they eat under a tree.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Where is your wife Sarah?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">She is there, in the tent.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I'll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sarah was listening at the entrance to the tent behind him. Abraham and Sarah were very old, and Sarah was well past the age for having children.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> (Sarah laughs quietly to herself.)</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sarah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Now that I am worn out, and my husband is old, will I really know such happiness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did Sarah laugh and say, 'Can I really have a child now that I am so old?' Is anything too hard for the Lord? I'll come back about this time next year, and Sarah will have a son.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sarah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I didn't laugh.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">The LORD</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes, you did laugh.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the promises you made to Abraham. Thank you that you kept your promise to give Sarah and Abraham a son. Thank you that the Bible shows us you are a promise keeper. Help us to enjoy learning about your promises this term. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: God's family grows":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A wife for Isaac":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A wife for Isaac</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that over sixty years after God's first promise, Isaac was forty and still had no wife.</li><li>We are learning that Abraham sent his trusted servant to find a wife for Isaac from his own family and homeland, not from the local Canaanite people.</li><li>I can explain why Isaac having a wife mattered for God's promise to Abraham to keep moving forward.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, why a wife for Isaac was necessary for God's promise of a great family to continue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 24:1–9 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trusting someone else's choice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about trusting someone else with an important decision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Has anyone ever made an important decision on your behalf — like a parent choosing your school, or a friend picking a gift for you? How did it feel to trust someone else with something that mattered?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up today's story, where Abraham trusts his servant to make one of the most important decisions of Isaac's life for him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sixty years on</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the promise so far and introduce the problem: Isaac is forty and unmarried.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why this moment mattered</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why a wife for Isaac was so important for the promise, guiding toward the idea of the next generation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Without a wife for Isaac, Abraham's family couldn't grow into the great nation God had promised. Why do you think this moment mattered so much for God's promise to keep moving forward?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the promise depended on Isaac having children of his own one day — no wife meant no next generation, and no next generation meant the promise couldn't grow any further.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Abraham sends his servant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Abraham commissioned his servant, insisting Isaac's wife come from his own family and homeland rather than the local Canaanites.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The servant wasn't so sure about this plan. What if the woman he found didn't want to leave her home and travel back with him? Should he just bring Isaac to meet her instead? What do you think Abraham said to that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses, then reveal: Abraham said no — Isaac must stay in the land God had promised him. If the woman wasn't willing to come, the servant was free to come back alone, but Isaac himself was not to leave.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the servant set off trusting God with the outcome, then say this week's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 follows the servant to a well, where he prays for a very specific sign.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A prayer with a sign":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:10–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A prayer with a sign</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the servant prayed for a specific, testable sign at the well to know which young woman God had chosen for Isaac.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah unknowingly matched the sign exactly, offering to water all ten camels without being asked.</li><li>I can explain what this story shows us about God working through ordinary moments and ordinary kindness.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the servant's specific test, and can explain why Rebekah's actions mattering so precisely shows God at work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 24:12–14 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Arriving at the well</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene: the servant arrives at the well in the evening, the place where the town's young women came to draw water.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A risky kind of prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about how confident students would feel asking God for something that specific.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you had to pick out one specific person, out of everyone who might walk by, using only God's help — would you feel confident asking God for something that exact, or would it feel too risky?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Use this to set up how bold the servant's prayer is about to be — he's not just asking for guidance in general, but for one very specific, testable sign.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The servant's test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the servant's specific prayer and sign — water for him and for all his camels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Offering a stranger a drink would be normal politeness. But offering to water ten thirsty camels too — that's a lot of extra work no one would expect. Why do you think the servant chose a sign that was so specific and so costly to fulfil?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a small, ordinary kindness could happen by chance, but going out of her way to water ten camels as well would be a clear, unmistakable answer — proof that God himself was at work, not just good luck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rebekah arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Rebekah appeared before the servant had even finished praying, and matched the sign exactly without knowing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rebekah had no idea she was answering anyone's prayer, or being tested for anything at all — she was simply being kind to a tired traveller. What does it show us about God's promises that he can work through something as ordinary as one person's everyday kindness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God doesn't need dramatic miracles to keep his promises — he can work through completely ordinary moments and ordinary kindness, even when the person involved has no idea they're part of God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 follows the servant to Rebekah's family, and Rebekah's own choice about whether to go.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Rebekah says yes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24:22–58</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah says yes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the servant explained his prayer and how God had answered it to Rebekah's father and brother, Laban.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah's family chose to trust God, even though they didn't want her to leave home for a distant land.</li><li>I can explain why the Bible shows Rebekah being asked, and choosing for herself, to go and be Isaac's wife.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell why Rebekah's family agreed to let her go, and can explain why Rebekah being asked personally matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Meeting the family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the servant gave gifts and explained his mission to Rebekah's father and Laban.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A hard decision</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what helped the family decide to trust God with such a big, sudden request.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This was a huge, sudden request — a stranger asking a family to send their daughter far away to marry a man she'd never met. Rebekah's father and Laban didn't want her to leave home for a distant land. What do you think helped them decide what to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Reveal: they chose to trust that this was God's doing, because of how exactly the servant's prayer had been answered — so, despite not wanting Rebekah to leave, they agreed to let her choose for herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rebekah's own choice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Rebekah was asked directly, and said yes, then discuss why this detail matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible makes a point of showing that Rebekah's family asked HER, and that she made the choice herself, rather than her father or brother deciding for her. Why do you think that detail matters?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Rebekah's trust in God had to be her own, not just something decided for her — real faith means choosing to trust God yourself, even when it's a huge, costly decision, like leaving your whole family and home behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the family blessed Rebekah, and how she confirmed she was ready to leave straight away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try saying this week's memory verse together once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 acts out the whole story as a class script, then looks at how everyone in it trusted God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Acting it out: God's family grows":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 24 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acting it out: God's family grows</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over sixty years after God first promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, Isaac still had no wife — and without a wife, the promise couldn't grow any further. God used the careful obedience of an unnamed servant, and the willing trust of a young woman named Rebekah, to move his promise forward another step. Christians believe God can be trusted to work through ordinary people, and even the small details of an ordinary day, to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 24 by acting it out as a class.</li><li>We are learning to identify how each character in the story — Abraham, the servant, Rebekah, and her family — showed trust in God.</li><li>I can explain how this story shows God moving his promise to Abraham forward, one step at a time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name how each character trusted God, and can explain that this story represents real (if partial) progress toward God's promise, not its full completion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Four (or five) student volunteers to read the Abraham, Servant, Rebekah, and Laban parts on screen, with the teacher or another volunteer reading Narrator</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the week's story before acting it out together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Acting out Genesis 24</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–8 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Abraham, the Servant, Rebekah, and Laban, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the whole class to watch and follow along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who trusted God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to identify how each character in the story showed trust in God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over everyone in this story — Abraham, the servant, Rebekah, and even Rebekah's family. How did each of them show they trusted God, even when it was hard or uncertain?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide through each: Abraham trusted God enough to send his servant on the journey instead of going himself; the servant trusted God enough to pray for a specific sign and act on it; Rebekah trusted God enough to leave her whole family and home; her family trusted God enough to let her go, and to let her make that choice herself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's promise, one step further</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether God was keeping his promise this week, and tie it back to the ongoing growth of Abraham's family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was God keeping his promise to grow Abraham's family into a great nation, through everything that happened this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: yes — not the whole promise all at once, but another real step forward. Isaac and Rebekah's marriage was the next generation of the family God had promised, moving one step closer to becoming as many as the stars.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 15 continues the story of Isaac and Rebekah's family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 14 script: God's family grows</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Abraham, Servant, Rebekah, Laban</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac grew up and became a man. His mother Sarah had died and his father Abraham was very old. God had made Abraham a rich and successful man. Abraham called his servant to him one day for a very important chat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You need to go and find a wife for my son Isaac. Go to the distant land I came from and find a wife for Isaac from there. Do not choose a wife from the land near here, where we live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant wasn't too sure about this. He said:</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What if the woman doesn't want to come with me and be Isaac's wife? Should I come back and get Isaac?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Abraham</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, Isaac must stay here. If she isn't willing to come, you may come back alone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">So the servant loaded ten of Abraham's camels with valuable gifts for Isaac's new wife. He then set off for a city in northern Syria where Abraham's brother lived. When the servant arrived, he stopped at a well, and while the camels rested, he prayed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Dear God, please keep your promise to Abraham and let me find a wife for Isaac today. I will ask a lady for a drink of water for me and the camels. If she says yes and gets me one, I will know she is the one you have chosen to marry Isaac. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">While the servant was still praying, a beautiful young woman came up to get some water. She was the granddaughter of Abraham's brother — though the servant didn't know it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excuse me. May I please have a drink of water?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I will be glad to give you one. And some for your camels too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant was very excited at this. He gave the young woman some gold bracelets and a ring, and asked if he could meet her family right away, as he had something very important to tell them all. Rebekah and the servant ran straight home to her family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I need to tell you why I have come here. I am Abraham's servant. The Lord has been good to my master, Abraham, and made him very rich. He has many sheep, camels, goats, cattle and donkeys, gold, silver, and servants. But he has only one son, Isaac, and Isaac doesn't have a wife. I promised I would come and find a wife from Abraham's family for Isaac, so that Abraham's land will stay in his family. Today at the well I prayed that God would show me the young woman to be Isaac's wife. Rebekah came, and now I am here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah's dad and her brother, Laban, both wondered what she was going to say. This kind of thing didn't happen every day. They didn't want Rebekah to go away to a distant land, but they chose to trust God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Dad and I believe this is what God wants. Let's ask Rebekah what she wants to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">They asked Rebekah if she would go with the servant. And she replied:</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes, I will be Isaac's wife.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah's family gave her their blessing, and said they would pray that she would have many children and grandchildren. They were very sad to see her leave, but they knew that, somehow, this was all part of God's plan. The servant got ready to leave, but Laban, who loved his sister very much, tried to delay him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please can you wait a week or ten days, so we can be with Rebekah longer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Servant</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, don't make me wait any longer. I must get back to my master Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Laban thought he'd try again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Laban</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let's ask Rebekah what she would like to do! Rebekah, are you happy to leave now to go and be Isaac's wife?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rebekah</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes. I am ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The servant and Rebekah loaded up the camels and then set off across the desert. The end.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear Lord, thank you that the servant and Rebekah trusted you and were obedient. Thank you for the way the Bible shows how you keep your promises, and that people today can trust you and your promises too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: Chalk and cheese":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 15</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Fighting before they were even born":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fighting before they were even born</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Isaac and Rebekah, like Abraham and Sarah before them, were unable to have children for many years until God answered prayer.</li><li>We are learning that Rebekah's pregnancy was unusually difficult, and that God told her the older of her twin sons would serve the younger.</li><li>I can explain why God choosing the younger twin was the opposite of what people in that culture would have expected.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the prophecy about the older serving the younger was so unexpected for its time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:19–23 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rivals before they'd met</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the warm-up question about two people becoming rivals before they've even met.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do you think it's possible for two people to become rivals before they've even met properly, or said a single word to each other? What would that even look like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses. Use this to set up today's very unusual detail: two brothers who were already fighting each other before either of them was even born.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Answered prayer, again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Isaac and Rebekah's marriage and their twenty years without children, until God answered prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why God keeps doing this</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God might have let another couple in this family struggle to have children before answering prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is now the second time in this family's story that God's promise has depended on a couple who couldn't have children for years and years. Why do you think God kept letting this happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it makes it completely clear that any child born is a gift from God, answered through prayer — not something Isaac and Rebekah achieved by themselves. It shows the promise depends on God's power, not human effort.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A painful, unusual pregnancy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe Rebekah's difficult pregnancy and show the image of her in distress, then ask the follow-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in image block showing Rebekah expecting the fighting twins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it might have felt like for Rebekah to feel her own unborn babies fighting inside her, even before she'd met them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — confusing, worrying, painful. This is what led her to go and ask God directly what was happening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The LORD's answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reveal God's prophecy to Rebekah, then discuss how it reversed cultural expectations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In those days, everyone expected the oldest son to be the most important one, the one in charge. What does it tell us about God, that here he flips that expectation and chooses to bless the younger twin instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God doesn't work the way people expect him to. He isn't bound by human traditions about who deserves to be first — he's free to choose whoever he wants for his plans.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 follows the twins' births, and how completely different Jacob and Esau turned out to be.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Chalk and cheese":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:24–28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chalk and cheese</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Esau and Jacob got their names, and how completely different they turned out to be as they grew up.</li><li>We are learning that Isaac favoured Esau while Rebekah favoured Jacob, splitting the family's loyalties.</li><li>I can explain what the saying 'chalk and cheese' means and how it applies to these two brothers.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least two ways Jacob and Esau were different from each other, and explain how parental favouritism affected the family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:24–28 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The twins are born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Esau and Jacob got their names at birth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A hint in Jacob's name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Jacob grabbing his brother's heel might foreshadow about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even the way Jacob was born — grabbing at his brother's heel — hints at something about him. What do you think that detail might suggest about how Jacob will act later in this story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses, then reveal: Jacob's name became linked to grabbing and getting ahead by any means — which foreshadows exactly how he behaves later, trying to grab hold of things that aren't his yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Chalk and cheese</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how differently the boys grew up, then ask about the saying 'chalk and cheese.'</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does anyone know what the saying 'chalk and cheese' means? Can you think of two people you know — maybe even in your own family — who are chalk and cheese?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">It means two things, or two people, that are completely different from each other, even though you might expect them to be similar.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A divided family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain how Isaac favoured Esau and Rebekah favoured Jacob, then discuss what that might have felt like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think it might have felt to grow up in a family where each parent openly favoured a different child?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — hurtful, confusing, likely to cause jealousy and rivalry between the brothers. This favouritism is part of what fuels the conflict still to come in this story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 sees the family's rivalry come to a head over a bowl of stew.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A bowl of stew":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:29–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A bowl of stew</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a birthright meant in this culture, and why it mattered especially for Isaac's family.</li><li>We are learning that Esau sold his birthright to Jacob for a single bowl of stew.</li><li>I can explain what this trade shows about how Esau and Jacob each valued God's promises.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the birthright mattered so much for this family, and can describe what Esau's choice reveals about his priorities.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Genesis 25:29–34 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What a birthright meant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what the birthright represented for this particular family, beyond ordinary inheritance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">As the eldest son, Esau's birthright entitled him to a double share of the family inheritance, and to become the family's leader when Isaac died. But for this particular family, it meant something even bigger. What do you think the birthright represented for Isaac's family, beyond just money or land?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: for this family, the birthright carried God's promise to Abraham forward — the land, the great nation, the blessing for the whole world. It wasn't just an inheritance, it was part of God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Esau comes home starving</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Esau came home exhausted and begged Jacob for stew.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the image of Jacob offering Esau the stew.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The trade</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how Jacob demanded the birthright in exchange, and Esau agreed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Esau's choice shows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Esau's willingness to trade away his birthright reveals about his priorities.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esau said, 'What good will those rights do me if I'm about to die?' What does this tell us about how much Esau actually valued the promises God had made to his family?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Esau cared more about the comfort of the moment than about something with lasting, far greater value — he despised his birthright rather than treasuring it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Jacob's choice shows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what Jacob's willingness to exploit his brother reveals about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does Jacob's willingness to take advantage of his own brother's hunger tell us about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jacob was cunning and opportunistic, willing to exploit his brother's weak moment — even though, unlike Esau, he did seem to understand how valuable the birthright really was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try saying this week's memory verse together once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Numbers 23:19, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 reads back through the whole story together and reflects on what it shows us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Reading it together: Chalk and cheese":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 25:19–34 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading it together: Chalk and cheese</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Twenty years after Isaac married Rebekah, God again answered prayer for a childless couple — but this time with unexpected twins who were fighting each other even before birth. God told Rebekah that the older twin would serve the younger, turning everyone's expectations upside down. As Jacob and Esau grew up to be complete opposites, favouritism split the family in two, and Esau carelessly traded away his birthright — and the promises attached to it — for a single bowl of stew. Christians believe God keeps his promises to his people, but not always through the people we'd expect, or in the way we'd expect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 25:19–34 by reading it together as a class.</li><li>We are learning to identify how the story's ending was different from what people at the time would have expected.</li><li>I can explain that God's promise didn't depend on Jacob or Esau being especially good people, but on God's own power to keep his word.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Jacob ended up with the birthright, and can explain why the outcome was unexpected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A confident reader (or the teacher) to lead the class through the built-in dictation reading</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Briefly recap the week's story before reading it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reading Genesis 25:19–34 together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lead the class through the built-in dictation as a fullscreen read-together, or invite a confident reader to lead it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for the whole class to read together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who won the promise?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which brother ended up with the birthright, and how.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of the two brothers, who ended up walking away with the promises God had made to this family — even though he wasn't the one born first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob did — through Esau's own careless choice to sell his birthright away, not through anything unfair Jacob did to take it (yet — there's more to Jacob's story still to come).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An unexpected way of keeping a promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask whether God kept his promise the way people would have expected, tying back to the lesson's big idea.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thinking back over this whole week — was God keeping his promise to Abraham and Isaac in the way we might have expected, or in a surprising way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: a surprising way. Nobody would have expected the younger twin to be chosen over the older, or for the promise to be won through a bowl of stew rather than a formal blessing. God keeps his promises but not always in the way we expect him to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 16 continues the story of Jacob and Esau.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Whole-Class Reading: Lesson 15 dictation: Genesis 25:19–34</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Isaac was the son of Abraham, and he was forty years old when he married Rebekah, the daughter of Bethuel. She was also the sister of Laban, the Aramean from northern Syria.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Almost twenty years later, Rebekah still had no children. So Isaac asked the LORD to let her have a child, and the LORD answered his prayer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Rebekah gave birth, she knew she was going to have twins, because she could feel them inside her, fighting each other. She thought, 'Why is this happening to me?' Finally, she asked the LORD why her twins were fighting, and he told her:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Your two sons will become two separate nations. The younger of the two will be stronger, and the older son will be his servant.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Rebekah gave birth, the first baby was covered with red hair, so he was named Esau. The second baby grabbed on to his brother's heel, so they named him Jacob. Isaac was sixty years old when they were born.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jacob and Esau grew older, Esau liked the outdoors and became a good hunter, while Jacob settled down and became a shepherd. Esau would take the meat of wild animals to his father Isaac, and so Isaac loved him more, but Jacob was his mother's favourite son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jacob was cooking some stew, when Esau came home hungry and said, 'I'm starving to death! Give me some of that red stew right now!' That's how Esau got the name 'Edom.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob replied, 'Sell me your rights as the firstborn son.' 'I'm about to die,' Esau answered. 'What good will those rights do me?'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Jacob said, 'Promise me your birthright, here and now!' And that's what Esau did. Jacob then gave Esau some bread and some of the bean stew, and when Esau had finished eating and drinking, he just got up and left, showing how little he thought of his rights as the firstborn.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are in control. Thank you that you have always had a plan to save your people from sin through Jesus. Thank you that nothing can stand in the way of you keeping your promises. Thank you that you are a powerful promise keeper! Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: Sibling rivalry":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had already chosen Jacob, before he was even born, to be the one through whom he would keep his promises. But everyone in Isaac's family went about it in the worst possible way. Isaac tried to hand Esau the blessing in secret, going around God's own words to Rebekah. Rebekah and Jacob answered secrecy with deceit, tricking a blind old man into giving away what he thought he was choosing freely. Nobody in this story played fair, and nobody comes out looking good. Yet the blessing still ended up exactly where God had already said it would. Christians believe this shows how powerful God is: he doesn't need anyone's good behaviour to keep his promises — not even the sinful scheming of the very family he's working through.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 16</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Two brothers, still fighting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–4 (context)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac's secret plan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though Isaac and Rebekah both already knew God had chosen Jacob to carry his promises, Isaac tried to give his final blessing to his favourite son Esau instead — and to do it secretly. This sets up a whole family who each try to handle God's promise their own way rather than trusting God with it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping Jacob and Esau's rivalry from last week.</li><li>We are learning that Isaac tried to give the blessing to Esau in secret, even though God had already chosen Jacob.</li><li>I can explain that no human secrecy or scheming can stop God from keeping his promises.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain who Isaac tried to bless, and why that was surprising given what God had already said about Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recap image: the Jacob-and-Esau stew scene from last week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: sibling rivalry</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about animals or siblings fighting for dominance to set the scene for today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever seen two animals — maybe dogs, or even kangaroos — squaring up and shoving each other, both trying to come out on top? What does that kind of fight usually look like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer describing pushing, posturing, or wrestling for dominance. Use this to introduce today's theme: Jacob and Esau are about to have their biggest fight yet — not with fists, but with tricks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap last week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Esau sold his birthright, and that God had already chosen Jacob before he was born.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the recap image of the stew scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Isaac's secret plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the story of Isaac's plan to secretly bless Esau, and Rebekah overhearing it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you feel, hearing that Isaac tried to do this important blessing quietly, behind his family's back — even though he already knew God had chosen Jacob?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Isaac's secrecy here is his own way of going around what he knew God had said — not so different from how Rebekah and Jacob are about to go around Isaac.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jacob disguises himself and steals the blessing meant for Esau.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are powerful enough to keep your promises no matter what people around us try to do. Help us to trust you instead of taking matters into our own hands. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Jacob steals the blessing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:5–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob steals the blessing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebekah and Jacob answered Isaac's secrecy with deceit of their own — disguising Jacob as Esau so thoroughly that Isaac was completely fooled, and gave Jacob the blessing that carried all of God's promises. The outcome matched what God had already planned, but the method was dishonest and wrong.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Rebekah and Jacob disguised Jacob to steal Esau's blessing.</li><li>We are learning to identify what was wrong with the way Jacob got the blessing.</li><li>I can explain that God's plan succeeding doesn't make Jacob and Rebekah's deception acceptable.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe the disguise Jacob used and can identify that deceiving Isaac was wrong, even though the result matched God's plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Image: Isaac blessing the disguised Jacob</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap Rebekah's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what students think Rebekah might have done after overhearing Isaac's plan, before revealing the actual story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard that Rebekah overheard Isaac's secret plan. If you were Rebekah, what might you have done next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. Rebekah's actual plan involved deception, not honesty — set up today's story with this question before revealing what she actually did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The disguise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through how Rebekah dressed Jacob in Esau's clothes and goat skin, and how Jacob deceived Isaac to receive the blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the image of Isaac blessing the disguised Jacob.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There's a problem with this plan straight away — something obvious that could give Jacob away the moment he walked in. What do you think it might be?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Esau was a hairy, outdoorsy hunter, while Jacob was smooth-skinned and stayed close to home. If Isaac touched Jacob, he might immediately know it wasn't Esau.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Was this okay?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss whether Jacob's method of getting the blessing was acceptable, even though the outcome matched God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob now has the blessing God always intended him to have. But think carefully — was the way he got it a good thing to copy, even though the outcome matched what God had planned?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no. Getting the right result through lying and deceiving your own father is not something to imitate. God's plan succeeding doesn't make Jacob and Rebekah's methods okay — it shows God is powerful enough to work his plan out even through people behaving badly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Esau returns and discovers what's happened.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that your plans don't depend on people doing the right thing. Help us to be honest with the people around us, even when it feels easier not to be. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Esau's fury":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:30–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau's fury</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When Esau returned from hunting, he discovered that Jacob had already stolen his blessing by deception. Esau's fury reveals that not one person in this story — Isaac, Rebekah, Jacob, or Esau — acted well or deserved God's blessing on their own merit.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Esau discovered Jacob had taken his blessing.</li><li>We are learning to identify that every character in this story acted selfishly or dishonestly.</li><li>I can explain that God's promises don't depend on any person deserving them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Esau was upset, and whether they can identify that no character in the story behaved well.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: losing out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine working hard toward something only to find someone else already took it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine working hard on something — hunting, cooking, getting everything ready — only to find out someone else already took what you were working towards. How do you think that would feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — frustrating, unfair, angry. This sets up how Esau is about to feel when he returns from the hunt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Esau's discovery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Esau's return, Isaac's trembling, and Esau's bitter cry for a blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was really wrong with how Jacob got this blessing? How did he actually get it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jacob got it by disguising himself and lying directly to his father's face — cheating and deceiving him, not by anything fair or honest.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nobody's the good guy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss how every character in the story acted badly in some way.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaac tried to bless the wrong son in secret. Rebekah and Jacob lied and disguised themselves to steal the blessing. Esau cared more about winning than about God's promises. Was there anyone in this story who comes across as clearly the 'good guy'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — everyone in this family acted selfishly, secretly, or dishonestly in this story. Nobody here deserved God's blessing based on their own behaviour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we retell the whole story as a boxing match commentary, and talk about what it shows us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that even when the people around us let us down or treat each other unfairly, your promises don't depend on any of us being good enough. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Reading it as a boxing match":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 27:1–40 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading it as a boxing match: what this shows us about God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Retelling the whole week's story as a boxing-match commentary helps highlight that nobody in this fight played fair — yet God's plan for Jacob to receive the blessing still went ahead exactly as promised. This shows how powerful and trustworthy God is: his promises don't depend on people's good behaviour.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are recapping the whole story of Genesis 27:1–40 through an acted-out boxing commentary.</li><li>We are learning to identify that everyone in the story played dishonestly, yet God's plan still succeeded.</li><li>I can explain that God is powerful enough to keep his promises even through sinful people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what was wrong with how Jacob won the blessing, and what the story shows about God's power to keep his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A few confident volunteers to read the Commentator, Team Esau, and Team Jacob parts, plus the rest of the class to join in cheering</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: the boxing commentary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Assign volunteers to the Commentator, Team Esau, and Team Jacob roles (or split the class into two cheering sides) and run the built-in roleplay script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What was wrong with the win?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss what was actually wrong with how Jacob won the blessing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now that we've replayed the whole story as a boxing match — what was actually wrong with how Jacob won this 'fight'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jacob tricked and cheated his own father and brother to get the blessing, even though God had already promised it to him. Winning the right result the wrong way is still wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What this shows us about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out the big idea: God is powerful enough to keep his promises even through a sinful, scheming family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">It's hard to believe God would put up with a family behaving like this. So what does this story actually show us about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows how amazing and powerful God is — he planned to save people through this exact family, even though every single person in it acted selfishly, secretly, or dishonestly. Nothing they did could stop God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 17 continues the story of Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God is no mere human! He doesn't tell lies or change his mind. God always keeps his promises.” — <em>Numbers 23:19 (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 16 script: Jacob and Esau's title fight</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Commentator, Team Esau, Team Jacob</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Commentator:</strong> Welcome, everyone, to the biggest title fight this family has ever seen — two brothers who've been battling each other since before they were even born!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the red corner, weighing in as the hairy outdoorsman and champion hunter — it's Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">And in the blue corner, the smooth-skinned home-body — it's Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac is old now, and he's lost his sight. Not sure how much time he has left, he calls in his favourite son, Esau, wanting to give him the big blessing before he dies.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yes! That's our man!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">But here's the catch — Isaac wants to do this quietly, without telling Rebekah or Jacob a thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boo! That's not fair play!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau heads out to hunt down some wild game for his father's favourite meal, just like Isaac asked.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">But Rebekah has been listening at the tent the whole time!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Jacob:</strong> Boo!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">She calls in her own favourite — Jacob — and comes up with a sneaky plan of her own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Esau:</strong> Boo! Boo!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob does exactly what his mother tells him. He fetches two young goats from the flock...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">...Rebekah cooks up the meal, dresses Jacob in his brother's clothes, and straps goat skin to his hands and neck so he'll feel just like hairy Esau.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob carries the food in to his father, pretending the whole time to be his brother!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob! Jacob! Jacob!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac isn't totally sure at first — something about that voice — but he's fooled! He gives Jacob the blessing meant for Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">JACOB! JACOB! JACOB!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau comes back from the hunt with a feast fit for a king, ready to finally get his blessing...</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esau! Esau!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">...only to find out there's nothing left. Jacob has already taken it!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Jacob</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob wins!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Commentator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ding, ding, ding! Esau is furious, and he's already plotting revenge on his brother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Team Esau</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boo! Get him back, Esau!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Commentator:</strong> So — Jacob has snuck away with the win. But let's be honest, everyone in this fight has played dirty, right from Isaac's secret plan to Rebekah and Jacob's disguise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Esau:</strong> Boo! Boo! Boo!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Team Jacob:</strong> Boo! Boo! Boo!</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are powerful in keeping your promises. Thank you that nothing can stand in the way of you keeping your promises — not even people's sinfulness. Thank you that you kept your promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, even though they didn't deserve it. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 7: Growing into greatness":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 29:31–30:24; 37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had promised Abraham a family as countless as the stars, and that promise kept growing — through Isaac, through Jacob, and now through Jacob's twelve sons, the beginning of the twelve tribes of Israel. But this family was far from perfect. Jacob loved Rachel's son Joseph best of all, and made that favouritism obvious with a special coat, which only deepened the hatred his brothers already felt. When Joseph made things worse by sharing dreams of his whole family bowing down to him, his brothers' hatred turned into a plan to get rid of him. They didn't kill him, but they did sell him into slavery in Egypt. It looked like the worst thing that could happen to this growing family — but Christians believe God was already at work, even through this terrible act, planning to use Joseph to rescue and preserve his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 17</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Jacob's family keeps growing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 29:31–30:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's family keeps growing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob, who once tricked his own father and brother, was tricked himself by his uncle Laban into marrying Leah before Rachel. Jacob ended up married to both sisters, and amid their rivalry, Jacob's family grew to twelve sons — God's promise to Abraham of a great family continuing to unfold.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jacob was tricked by Laban into marrying Leah before Rachel.</li><li>We are learning that Jacob's family grew to twelve sons through Leah, Rachel, and their servants.</li><li>I can explain that this week's new memory verse teaches that God is always at work for good, even in a rivalry-filled family.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain how Laban tricked Jacob, and whether they can recite or paraphrase this week's new memory verse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: does trickery catch up with you?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about whether tricking people usually catches up with someone eventually.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week, Jacob tricked his own brother to get what he wanted. Do you think someone who tricks people usually gets away with it forever, or does it usually catch up with them somehow?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. Today's story shows the trickster get tricked himself — Jacob is about to find out what it feels like to be on the receiving end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jacob is tricked by Laban</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Jacob falling in love with Rachel, working seven years for her, and being tricked into marrying Leah first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think it felt for Jacob to be deceived like this, after everything he'd done to deceive his own father and brother?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. It's a fitting turn — the deceiver gets deceived. This doesn't erase what Jacob did to Esau, but it shows Jacob is now living with the same kind of hurt he caused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this week's new memory verse and discuss what it might mean given Jacob's messy, rivalry-filled family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we've got a brand new memory verse. Even with all this family rivalry and trickery going on, what do you think this verse means for how God was working in Jacob's messy family?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God was still at work for good, even through a complicated, rivalry-filled family. This verse will keep coming back all week, including tomorrow when God speaks to Jacob directly, and later when we see it stretch all the way to Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God speaks to Jacob again and gives him a new name.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always at work for good, even in families that don't always get along. Help us to trust that you're at work in our own families too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A new name, an old promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 35:9–12; 49:28 (context)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A new name, an old promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God appeared to Jacob again, renamed him Israel, and renewed his promise of land and a great family. Jacob's twelve sons became the beginning of the twelve tribes of Israel, showing God's promise to Abraham continuing to unfold exactly as he'd said.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God renamed Jacob 'Israel' and renewed his promise to him.</li><li>We are learning the names of Jacob's twelve sons and that they became the twelve tribes of Israel.</li><li>I can explain that Jacob's growing family shows God keeping his promise to Abraham.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God changed Jacob's name, and whether they can name several of Jacob's twelve sons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>None — this is a text/discussion-based day (see the file's header notes for why this lesson ships without images)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: the meaning of a new name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about what a new name might be chosen to remind someone of.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If someone gave you a brand new name to remind you of something important, what would you want that name to remind you of?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. Today we see God give Jacob a whole new name — one meant to remind him of God's promises for the rest of his life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God renames Jacob 'Israel'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read God's words to Jacob from Genesis 35:9–12, renaming him Israel and renewing the promise of land and family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the verse block with Genesis 35:9–12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God changed Jacob's name to Israel. Why do you think God wanted Jacob to have a new name, rather than just keep making the same promise using his old one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the name itself became a permanent, everyday reminder of God's promise — every time someone said Jacob's new name, it pointed back to what God had promised him. It wasn't just words spoken once; it became part of who he was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Twelve sons, twelve tribes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Jacob's twelve sons by name and try the ordering activity as a group.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in activity block to try naming all twelve sons in birth order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Near the end of his life, Jacob gathered all twelve of his sons to bless them. What do you think it shows us that the writer of Genesis records the names of every single one of Jacob's sons?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it shows that Jacob's family truly was becoming the great nation God had promised Abraham — each son mattered enough to be named, because each one would become the head of a whole tribe of Israel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the memory verse together and close with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we meet Jacob's favourite son, Joseph, and find out why his brothers grew to hate him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">God told Jacob: “I am God All-Powerful, and from now on your name will be Israel instead of Jacob. You will have many children, and your family will grow into whole nations. I will give you the same land I promised Abraham and Isaac, and it will belong to your family forever.” — <em>Genesis 35:9–12 (CEV, paraphrased)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you kept your promise to Abraham by growing his family into the twelve tribes of Israel. Thank you that you're always at work for good, even when we can't see the whole plan yet. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Jacob's favourite son":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37:2–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's favourite son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jacob's obvious favouritism toward Joseph — shown through the special coat — combined with Joseph's own bad reports and boastful dreams of ruling over his family, caused his ten older brothers' hatred to grow until they began plotting to get rid of him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Joseph's brothers grew to hate him: favouritism, bad reports, and his dreams.</li><li>We are learning to identify why sharing the dreams the way Joseph did wasn't wise.</li><li>I can explain how favouritism and jealousy built up inside Jacob's family.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the three reasons Joseph's brothers hated him, and whether they can evaluate why sharing the dreams wasn't wise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: obvious favourites</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about how it feels when someone has an obvious favourite.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever felt like someone had an obvious favourite — maybe a favourite student, a favourite sibling, a favourite friend? How did that make everyone else feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses — left out, jealous, unfair. This sets up how Joseph's brothers are about to feel toward him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The coat and the dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Jacob's favouritism toward Joseph, the special coat, Joseph's bad reports, and his two dreams.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do you think it was wise for Joseph to share these dreams with his brothers, who already hated him? Why or why not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — even if the dreams were true and came from God, sharing them this way, to brothers who already resented him, only poured fuel on the fire. Joseph wasn't necessarily wrong to have the dreams, but boasting about them wasn't wise.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Building anticipation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the memory verse to build anticipation for tomorrow's story without giving away the ending.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even though Joseph's brothers were about to do something terrible, this week's memory verse says God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him. What do you think that might mean for what happens next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses — this is meant to build anticipation, not be fully answered yet. Guide toward: it hints that even the very worst things Joseph's brothers plan to do won't be outside of God's control — but don't give away tomorrow's ending yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph's brothers finally act on their hatred, and we find out what God was really doing all along.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that even when families struggle with jealousy and favouritism, you are still at work. Help us to treat the people in our own families fairly, and to trust you when things feel unfair. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Sold, but not forgotten":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37:12–36</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sold, but not forgotten</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph's brothers, driven by hatred, sold him into slavery in Egypt and deceived their father into believing he was dead. It looked like the end for Joseph and for God's growing promise — but this sets up God working through Joseph's suffering for the good of his whole family, exactly as this week's memory verse describes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Joseph's brothers sold him into slavery.</li><li>We are learning how the brothers deceived Jacob into believing Joseph was dead.</li><li>I can explain that God was at work for good even through this terrible act, in line with this week's memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph was sold, and whether they can explain what this week's memory verse means in light of it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: imagining the plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine what plan the brothers might have come up with, before revealing the real story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard Joseph's brothers were plotting to get rid of him. If you were one of the brothers, what's the worst plan you can imagine them coming up with?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. The real story is bad enough without needing to imagine worse — use this to set up today's account.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph is sold into slavery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being thrown into the well, sold to the Midianite traders, and taken to Egypt, and the brothers' lie to Jacob.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">From where we're sitting, this looks like one of the worst things that could happen to this family God had been growing all week. So why do you think this story is still part of God keeping his promise to Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: even though Joseph's brothers meant this for evil, God was already at work — planning to use Joseph's time in Egypt to eventually save his whole family from a coming famine. This is exactly what this week's memory verse has been pointing toward.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory, and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 18 continues Joseph's story in Egypt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always at work for good, even when things look their very worst. Thank you that nothing Joseph's brothers did could stop your plan for Joseph, or your promises to Abraham's family. Help us trust you the same way when hard things happen to us. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 8: Working for good":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39–41</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph was sold into slavery in Egypt, but Christians believe God had not abandoned him. Whether Joseph was a slave in Potiphar's house, forgotten in prison, or finally standing in front of Pharaoh himself, the Bible shows God being with him and blessing everything he did. God even gave Joseph the ability to interpret dreams, which eventually put him in charge of all Egypt during a terrible famine — saving not just Egypt, but the whole world, and keeping God's promise that Abraham's family would be a blessing to everyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God was with Joseph, even as a slave":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39:1–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God was with Joseph, even as a slave</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Even after being sold as a slave in Egypt, Joseph found that God was with him and blessed everything he did. When Joseph refused to sin against God and was punished unfairly for it, he still trusted that God was at work for good.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God was with Joseph even though he was a slave in Potiphar's house.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph chose to obey God even when it cost him something.</li><li>I can explain that trusting God doesn't always mean things turn out fair straight away.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain how Joseph was blessed in Potiphar's house, and why he ended up in prison despite doing the right thing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for Potiphar, Joseph, and Potiphar's wife (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: would God still be with you?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question about whether students would expect God to be with them if they were sold as a slave far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard Joseph's brothers sold him as a slave into Egypt. If you were sold as a slave in a country far from home, would you expect God to still be with you there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. This week's story keeps coming back to that exact question — and the Bible's answer is a clear yes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph in Potiphar's house</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being sold to Potiphar and put in charge of his household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph had every reason to feel like God had forgotten him. What do you think the Bible says happened to Joseph in Potiphar's house?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the Bible says the Lord was with Joseph, and he became successful. Potiphar noticed and put Joseph in charge of his entire household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 1</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 1 script together as a class, covering Potiphar's wife's false accusation and Joseph being thrown in prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, Potiphar, Joseph, and Potiphar's wife if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph did nothing wrong, and he still ended up in prison. Why do you think Joseph still trusted God even when doing the right thing led to something so unfair?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph knew that obeying God mattered more than the outcome. He couldn't control what Potiphar's wife did or how Potiphar reacted, but he could control his own faithfulness — and he trusted God with everything else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph is faithful to God even in prison, and interprets two dreams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 1</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Potiphar, Joseph, Potiphar's wife</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar and Joseph.) Joseph worked hard in Potiphar's house and God made him successful. Potiphar was so pleased with Joseph that he put him in charge of his whole household.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Potiphar</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Joseph in charge, I don't need to worry about anything except for the food I eat! Things could not be better. Joseph really is the best!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Potiphar leaves.) Joseph was great. God was with him in everything he did. But Joseph was also attractive.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar's wife.) Potiphar's wife liked Joseph too and wanted him to be like a husband to her whenever Potiphar was away. But Joseph kept saying …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No, I will not sin against my master and my God!</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Turn Joseph side to side as though he is shaking his head to say 'no'.) Potiphar's wife tried one last time, and when Joseph said 'no' she grabbed him by the coat. Joseph wriggled out of his coat and ran out of the house, leaving his coat behind.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Potiphar.) When Potiphar got home from work that day, his wife told him this story …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Potiphar's wife</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph tried to hurt me when no-one else was around, but I screamed so he ran out of the house, leaving his cloak behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Potiphar was so mad he threw Joseph into the prison where the king's prisoners were kept.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph even when he was a slave and even when he was treated unfairly. Help us to trust that you are with us too, even in hard or unfair situations. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Faithful in prison":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 39:21–40:23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faithful in prison</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God was with Joseph even in prison, giving him favour with the jailer and the ability to correctly interpret the dreams of the king's cupbearer and baker. Even though the cupbearer forgot about Joseph for two years, God had not forgotten him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God was with Joseph even in prison.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph correctly interpreted the dreams of the cupbearer and the baker, and gave God the credit for it.</li><li>I can explain what it might mean to trust God while waiting for a hard situation to change.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what happened with the cupbearer and the baker, and whether they can explain why Joseph gave God the credit for interpreting the dreams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, and Baker (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: forgotten but not forgotten</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students how they might feel about God if they were thrown in prison unfairly, to set up today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday Joseph was thrown in prison for something he didn't even do. If that happened to you, how do you think you'd feel about God at that point?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. It would be completely understandable to feel forgotten or angry. Today's story shows that even in prison, Joseph wasn't actually forgotten by God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph faithful in prison</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph being put in charge of the prisoners, and the cupbearer and baker's troubling dreams.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph told the cupbearer and the baker he could help them with their dreams. Where do you think Joseph believed the power to understand dreams actually came from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph made it clear that dream interpretation belongs to God, not to Joseph himself. Even using this special gift, Joseph gave credit to God rather than taking it for himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 2</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 2 script together as a class, covering the cupbearer and baker's dreams and their fulfilment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, and Baker if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph asked the cupbearer to remember him and tell the king about him. But the manual tells us the cupbearer forgot Joseph — for two whole years! What do you think it might feel like to keep trusting God while you're still waiting for things to change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept honest responses. Guide toward: waiting on God, even when it feels like he's forgotten you or when other people forget their promises, is genuinely hard — but Joseph's story isn't over yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: two years later, the King of Egypt has a troubling dream of his own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, Jailer, Joseph, Cupbearer, Baker</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Enter Jailer and Joseph.) Joseph was faithful to God even in prison. The jailer was so pleased with Joseph that he put him in charge of all the prisoners.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Jailer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Joseph in charge I don't need to worry at all!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Jailer leaves.) One day, two of the king's servants were thrown into prison by the king. These were his baker and his cupbearer — the guy who tested the king's drinks for poison before the king himself drank from his cup.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Enter Cupbearer and Baker.) On the same night both these men had a dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Lay both the Cupbearer and Baker down to 'sleep'.) The next morning they were troubled because of their dreams. No-one could tell them what they meant. Joseph knew that God is the one who gives dreams and their meanings, so he asked the men to tell him their dreams.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Cupbearer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In my dream there was a vine. And on the vine there were three branches. As soon as the grapes had grown on the branches, I squeezed them into Pharaoh's cup and gave it to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The three branches are three days. In three days the king of Egypt will give you your job back. But when he does, please don't forget me. Please ask the king to let me out of prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Baker</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In my dream I was carrying three baskets of bread on my head. In the baskets were all different kinds of bread and baked goods for the King of Egypt. But as I carried them, the birds swooped down and pecked at the bread until there was none left!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The three baskets are three days. In three days you will be gone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(Joseph leaves.) Well, three days later the King of Egypt threw himself a huge birthday party! And in front of all his guests, he gave the Cupbearer his job back, and the Baker was no more.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph even in prison, and that you gave him the ability to help others. Help us to trust you while we wait for things to change in our own lives too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: God gives Pharaoh's dream its meaning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:1–41</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives Pharaoh's dream its meaning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Two years after being forgotten in prison, Joseph was brought before Pharaoh to interpret his troubling dreams. Joseph gave God the credit for the interpretation, correctly predicting seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine — and Pharaoh put Joseph in charge of all Egypt as a result.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph interpreted Pharaoh's dreams of seven years of plenty and seven years of famine.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph gave God the credit for understanding the dreams, not himself.</li><li>I can explain how God was at work through even the difficult parts of Joseph's story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what Pharaoh's dreams meant, and whether they can explain how God had been at work through Joseph's whole story so far.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Craft-stick puppets for the King of Egypt, Joseph, and Cupbearer (optional, for acting out the script)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: does Joseph stay stuck?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to guess whether Joseph stays stuck in prison forever, to build anticipation for today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we heard the cupbearer forgot all about Joseph. Do you think Joseph is going to stay stuck in prison forever, or does something change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept guesses. Today we find out — two years later, something happens that finally gets Joseph out of prison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pharaoh's troubling dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Pharaoh's two dreams and how none of his wise men could interpret them, until the cupbearer remembered Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Once again, Joseph is about to interpret someone's dream. Do you think he's changed his answer about where his ability to do this comes from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no — Joseph tells the king exactly the same thing he told the cupbearer and the baker: he can't tell the king what his dreams mean, but God can. Joseph never made this about his own cleverness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act out Scene 3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Act out or read through the Scene 3 script together as a class, covering Pharaoh's dreams, Joseph's interpretation, and his promotion to be in charge of Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the roleplay block to act out the script on screen. Assign readers for Narrator, King, Joseph, and Cupbearer if acting it out live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After everything Joseph had been through — sold as a slave, falsely accused, forgotten in prison — he ended up in charge of the whole of Egypt. What does this tell you about how God had been at work in Joseph's story the whole time, even in the bad parts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: nothing that happened to Joseph — not the slavery, not the false accusation, not the two years in prison — was wasted. God was working through every part of it to eventually put Joseph exactly where he needed to be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the famine hits, and Joseph's story becomes a blessing for the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Roleplay: Lesson 18 script: Joseph in Egypt — Scene 3</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-amber-soft\">Characters: Narrator, King, Joseph, Cupbearer</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Lie the King of Egypt down as though he's asleep.) In his dream, the King of Egypt was standing by the Nile River when seven fat, healthy cows came out and ate at the edge of the river. After them, seven skinny, ugly cows came out and ate up the healthy ones. The king woke up because he was worried about this dream!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>(stage direction)</em> <strong>Narrator:</strong> (Sit the king up. Finally, the king fell asleep again. But he had another dream! Lie the king down again.) This time, the king saw seven good, healthy heads of corn growing on a single stalk. After them seven other ears of corn sprouted — thin and burnt by the wind. The thin, burnt corn swallowed up the good, healthy corn. The king woke up again!</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">King</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These dreams worry me. I wonder if there is anyone who can tell me what they mean.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">None of the wise men and magicians in the land could tell the King of Egypt what his dreams meant. (Enter Cupbearer.) Finally, the cupbearer remembered Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Cupbearer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your highness. There is one man who can tell you what your dreams mean …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">King</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Get me this man!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">(The Cupbearer leaves and comes back with Joseph.) So the king told Joseph his dreams. And Joseph said …</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Joseph</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I cannot tell you what your dreams mean but God can. Both of the dreams mean the same thing. The seven cows and the seven ears of corn are seven years. God has shown you that for seven years there will be lots and lots of food in Egypt — more food than we can eat. But after that, there will be seven years of famine — there will be no food anywhere in Egypt or in the countries around it. And when the famine comes, the good seven years will be forgotten. This will happen soon!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Narrator</th><td class=\"lt-td\">So the King of Egypt put Joseph in charge of all Egypt to collect the food and store it up for the famine.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you gave Joseph the ability to understand Pharaoh's dream, and that you put him in charge of Egypt at exactly the right time. Help us trust that you're working through every part of our lives too, even the hard parts. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Blessed to be a blessing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:46–57</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed to be a blessing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph's careful storing of grain during the seven years of plenty meant that when famine struck the whole world, people from every nation came to Egypt to buy food from him — fulfilling God's promise to Abraham that his family would be a blessing to the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph's storing of grain saved Egypt and the surrounding world from famine.</li><li>We are learning how this connects to God's promise that Abraham's family would bless the whole world.</li><li>I can explain what it might look like to trust God in my own difficult situations, the way Joseph did.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph saved the world from famine, and whether they can connect this to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recap of the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recap what's stood out to them from the week's story so far.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we've followed Joseph from slavery, to prison, to being second-in-command of all Egypt. Out of everything we've heard so far, what part of Joseph's story has stood out to you the most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer — this is a recap question to get students thinking back over the whole week's story before we finish it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The famine and God's promise to Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the seven years of plenty and famine, and connect Joseph saving the world to God's promise to Abraham in Genesis 12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Lesson 12, God promised Abraham that his family would be blessed, and that the whole world would be blessed through his family. How do you think Joseph selling grain to the whole world connects to that promise God made to Abraham, generations earlier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this is exactly God keeping his promise. Through Joseph, one member of Abraham's family, people from every nation were saved from starving during the famine. God's promise to bless the whole world through Abraham's family was coming true in a way nobody could have predicted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory and personal application</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse fully from memory, then ask students to think personally about trusting God in their own hard situations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes we can find ourselves in difficult situations and it can feel like God doesn't care. While you're sitting there, have a think about a difficult situation you've found yourself in — you may be in one right now. What might it look like to trust God the way Joseph did, even before you can see how things will turn out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This is a personal application question — there's no need for students to share out loud if they don't want to. The point is to connect Joseph's trust in God to something happening in their own lives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 19 continues the story as Joseph's brothers come to Egypt during the famine, not knowing who he is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you were with Joseph. Thank you that you were working everything out for his good, and that you had a plan for his life. Thank you that you promise to work all things for the good of those who love you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 9: Mighty to save":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41–50</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God revealed to Pharaoh that seven years of plenty would be followed by seven years of famine, and gave Joseph the wisdom to store up grain so that Egypt — and the whole world — could be saved. When Joseph's own brothers came to Egypt for food, not recognising the brother they had sold into slavery, God used the famine to bring Jacob's whole family back together in Egypt, keeping his promise that Abraham's family would become a great nation and would bless the whole world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 19</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Joseph saves the world from famine":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 41:46–42:28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph saves the world from famine</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gave Joseph wisdom to prepare Egypt for a terrible famine, saving people from every nation — including Joseph's own family, who came to Egypt for food without recognising him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph's storing of grain saved Egypt and the surrounding world from famine.</li><li>We are learning how this connects to God's promise that Abraham's family would bless the whole world.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph's brothers came to Egypt for food, not recognising the brother they had sold into slavery.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell how Joseph saved the world from famine, and can explain why his brothers didn't recognise him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: a map showing Egypt and Canaan, to point out how far the brothers travelled</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping Joseph's story</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recap what they remember about how God was with Joseph through slavery, prison, and being put in charge of Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we followed Joseph from slavery, to prison, to being second-in-command of all Egypt. What do you remember about how God was with Joseph through all of that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God always works for the good of those who love him — even when Joseph's circumstances looked terrible, God was preparing him for something bigger.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The famine and God's promise to Abraham</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the seven years of plenty and famine, and connect Joseph saving the world to God's promise to Abraham in Genesis 12.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right back in Lesson 12, God promised Abraham that his family would be a blessing, and that the whole world would be blessed through his family. How do you think Joseph selling grain to the whole world connects to that promise God made to Abraham, generations earlier?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this is God keeping his promise. Through Joseph, one member of Abraham's family, people from every nation were saved from starving — exactly what God had promised long ago.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The brothers come to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the brothers' first trip to Egypt, being accused of spying, and Simeon being kept behind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph recognised his brothers straight away, but they had no idea who he was. Why do you think Joseph's brothers might not have recognised him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Joseph had grown up, he was dressed like an Egyptian official, and he spoke to them through an interpreter — Joseph was the last person the brothers expected to see sitting on Egypt's throne.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the brothers return to Egypt with Benjamin, and Joseph tests them one final time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan for Joseph all along. Thank you that you used him to save so many people from starving, and that you always keep your promises. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: One final test":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 43:1–45:15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One final test</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joseph tested his brothers one last time with his silver cup, and when Judah offered to take Benjamin's punishment himself, Joseph saw that his brothers had truly changed and revealed who he really was.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph tested his brothers with his silver cup.</li><li>We are learning that Judah's offer to protect Benjamin showed the brothers had changed.</li><li>We are learning how Joseph revealed himself to his brothers and forgave them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain what changed Joseph's mind about revealing himself, and how he responded to his brothers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: sending Benjamin to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to imagine how Jacob felt sending Benjamin to Egypt after already losing Joseph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday Jacob didn't want to let Benjamin go to Egypt. Eventually the food ran out again and he had no choice. If you were Jacob, how do you think you'd feel sending your last remaining son from Rachel into a country ruled by a stranger?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jacob had already lost Joseph and was terrified of losing Benjamin too — this shows how much courage it took for him to let Benjamin go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The silver cup test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the feast, the silver cup being planted in Benjamin's sack, and Judah's offer to take his place.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Judah begged Joseph to take him as a slave instead of Benjamin, saying his father would die of grief if Benjamin didn't come home. Why do you think this moment finally changed everything for Joseph?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Joseph could see that his brothers had truly changed. Years earlier they had shown no mercy selling their own brother into slavery — now Judah was offering his own freedom to protect the youngest brother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph reveals himself</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Joseph revealing his identity and forgiving his brothers, sending them home to bring Jacob and the whole family to Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Joseph's brothers might have felt in that moment, discovering that the powerful Governor of Egypt was actually the brother they had wronged?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They may have felt relieved and amazed, but also frightened and deeply guilty for what they had done to him all those years before.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jacob's whole family moves to Egypt, and God repeats his promise to make them into a great nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Joseph forgave his brothers instead of taking revenge. Help us to forgive people who have hurt us, the way Joseph did. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: God's promise travels to Egypt":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 46:1–7; 46:28–47:12; 49:33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promise travels to Egypt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As Jacob's family moved to Egypt, God repeated his promise that they would become a great nation, showing that God had never broken any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God repeated his nation promise to Jacob on the way to Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God did not break any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, or Jacob.</li><li>We are learning that Jacob's family settled in Egypt and grew, and that Jacob eventually died there.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state what God promised Jacob in Egypt, and whether they can say that God kept every one of his promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping the three promises</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall the three promises God made to Abraham (land, nation, blessing), and which one connects to today's story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can anyone remember the three promises God made to Abraham, right back near the start of this term? (Land, nation, and blessing.) Which of those promises do you think is about to come true in today's story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward the NATION promise — God promised Abraham's family would become a great nation, and today we see God confirm that this will happen in Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God speaks to Jacob on the way to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through God's promise to Jacob in Genesis 46:3–4, and discuss whether God has broken any promises so far this term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did God break any of his promises to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob along the way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: No he didn't! God is faithful and gracious — when he makes a promise, he keeps it, even when it takes a very long time or looks impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Settling in Egypt, and Jacob's death</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the family settling in Goshen and Jacob's eventual death, and the brothers' renewed fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph reassures his frightened brothers one final time, and we look back at everything God has done this term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">God told Jacob not to be afraid to go to Egypt, because he would make Jacob's family into a great nation there. God promised to go with Jacob to Egypt, and one day to bring his family back out again. — <em>Genesis 46:3–4 (CEV, paraphrased)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you kept your promise to make Abraham's family into a great nation. Thank you that you are always faithful, even when we have to wait a long time to see your promises come true. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: God had a plan all along":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God had a plan all along</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When Joseph's brothers feared revenge after Jacob's death, Joseph reassured them that God had planned everything that happened to him for good — to save many people and to keep God's promises to Abraham's family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Joseph forgave his brothers completely, even after their father died.</li><li>We are learning that God had a plan for Joseph's whole life, even through the hardest parts.</li><li>I can explain how this term's story shows that God is a promise keeper.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Joseph forgave his brothers, and whether they can describe God's plan for Joseph's life in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: why were the brothers afraid?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students why the brothers might have been afraid of Joseph once their father had died.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why might Joseph's brothers have been afraid of Joseph after their father Jacob died?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They thought he might finally take revenge on them for selling him into slavery, now that Jacob wasn't there to keep the peace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Joseph's forgiveness and God's plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Genesis 50:19–21 together and discuss what God's plan for Joseph's life actually was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God had a plan for Joseph the whole time — through slavery, prison, and finally being in charge of Egypt. What do you think that plan was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God chose to use Joseph to save his people from the famine and to keep his promise to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob that their family would become a great nation and a blessing to the whole world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse from memory and term reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this term's memory verse fully from memory, then ask students to reflect on what's stood out to them about how God keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we've followed God's promises all the way from Abraham to Joseph's family settling in Egypt. What's one thing that's stood out to you about the way God keeps his promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer. This is a personal reflection question to finish the term's story of Abraham's family — there's no single right answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 20 moves into a contemporary lesson about Holly and Compassion Australia, a change of pace from the Genesis narrative.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Joseph told his brothers, “Don't be afraid! I have no right to change what God has decided. You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing. I will take care of you and your children.” — <em>Genesis 50:19–21 (CEV)</em><br>“We know that God is always at work for the good of everyone who loves him.” — <em>Romans 8:28a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan for Joseph. Thank you that you used him to save your people and to keep your promises to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. Thank you that you are always at work for the good of everyone who loves you. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 10: Holly and Compassion Australia":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Various (Matthew 14:14; Mark 6:34; Colossians 3:12; James 1:27; Luke 12:13-21)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly, a Compassion Australia volunteer, saw first-hand how Compassion Australia shows the love and compassion of Jesus to children and families living in poverty. Just as Jesus had compassion on the crowds and healed and taught them, God wants his people to have compassion on the poor and needy, and to look for ways to help make a difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B1 Upper Primary — Lesson 20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Meet Holly and Compassion Australia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Introduction to Holly and Compassion Australia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meet Holly and Compassion Australia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly is a young Australian Christian who volunteers for Compassion Australia, an organisation committed to holistically caring for children living in poverty around the world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Holly is and how she came to be involved with Compassion Australia.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia is committed to holistic development for children in poverty.</li><li>We are learning that God calls his people to be fair to the poor and to defend the helpless.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain who Holly is and what holistic development means for Compassion Australia.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au to show students Holly's profile and the Compassion Australia home page</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: richest and poorest countries</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to guess some of the richest and poorest countries in the world, then share the 2017 lists from the manual.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you had to guess, which countries do you think are some of the richest in the world? And which do you think are some of the poorest?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any guesses. In 2017, some of the richest countries in the world (by average spending per person) were Qatar, Luxembourg and Singapore. Some of the poorest were the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Burundi — in fact, all 10 of the poorest countries that year were in Africa.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Meet Holly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Holly using her profile from the manual — her age, school year, favourite foods and hobbies.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why does Holly care so much?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Discuss why Holly might care so much about children in poverty, then explain how her Indonesia trip led her to become a Christian and Compassion Australia volunteer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Holly also cares a lot about children in our world who live in poverty. What are some reasons you think someone like Holly might care so much about children she's never even met?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: caring for the poor and needy is something God asks his people to do, and once you understand how much difference it makes, it's hard not to care.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Holistic development</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Compassion Australia is committed to holistic development — caring for a child's spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs — and discuss why this matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compassion Australia describes itself as 'an international Christian child development and child advocacy ministry' that is committed to holistic development for children in poverty — caring about a child's spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs, not just their physical wellbeing. Why do you think caring for all of these parts of a child's life matters, not just making sure they have enough food or money?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God cares about the whole person, not just their physical needs. A child needs to be loved, to be taught about Jesus, to be safe, and to be given hope for their future — not only to be fed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we look at the four programs Compassion Australia runs, and how Jesus' own compassion for people is the model for this work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work of Compassion Australia. Thank you that through Holly's passion for the poor, she has come into a saving knowledge of Jesus. Please help us to be fair to the poor and to defend those in need, just like your word asks us to. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Compassion Australia's programs and Jesus' compassion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 14:14; Mark 6:34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compassion Australia's programs and Jesus' compassion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compassion Australia runs four programs to holistically care for children from before birth through to university, reflecting the same compassion Jesus showed when he healed and taught the crowds who came to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Compassion Australia's four programs: Child Survival, Child Sponsorship, Leadership Development, and Complementary Interventions.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia partners with local churches in developing countries.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus' own compassion for the crowds is the model for how Compassion Australia cares for children.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name at least two of Compassion Australia's programs and explain how Jesus' compassion connects to this work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au to show students the 'What we do' page and the list of 14 partner countries</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: recapping holistic development</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall the different kinds of needs Compassion Australia cares about from yesterday's lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we learned that Compassion Australia cares about the whole child, not just their physical needs. Can you remember any of the different kinds of needs they care about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Spiritual, economical, social, physical and emotional needs — this is called holistic development.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The four programs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Compassion Australia's four programs: Child Survival, Child Sponsorship, Leadership Development, and Complementary Interventions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Partnering with local churches</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Compassion Australia partners with local churches in 25 developing countries, and discuss why this matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compassion Australia partners with local churches in 25 developing countries across Africa, Asia, and Central and South America to run these programs. Why do you think partnering with local churches, rather than just sending help from Australia, might be so important?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: local churches understand what their own community really needs, and they're already in a trusted position to care for people long after any outside visitors have gone home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' compassion for the crowds</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Matthew 14:14 and Mark 6:34 together, and connect Jesus' compassion for the crowds to the holistic way Compassion Australia cares for children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In these two verses, Jesus healed people who were sick and taught people who needed guidance. How is this similar to the way Compassion Australia cares for the whole child — not just one need, but many?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Jesus' compassion wasn't only for people who were sick — he cared for the whole person, physically and spiritually. Compassion Australia reflects this same kind of compassion in the way it cares for children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Holly's own trip to Indonesia, and what the Bible says about loving God by loving others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“When Jesus got out of the boat, he saw the large crowd. He felt sorry for them and healed everyone who was sick.” — <em>Matthew 14:14 (CEV)</em><br>“When Jesus got out of the boat, he saw the large crowd that was like sheep without a shepherd. He felt sorry for the people and started teaching them many things.” — <em>Mark 6:34 (CEV)</em><br>“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work of Compassion Australia's programs. Thank you that Jesus' own compassion for the sick and the lost is the example for how your people care for others today. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Holly's trip to Indonesia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Colossians 3:12; James 1:27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly's trip to Indonesia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">On a school trip to Wangurer village in Indonesia, Holly saw first-hand how Compassion Australia reflects the love and compassion of Jesus for people living in poverty, both physically and spiritually.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Holly travelled to Wangurer village in Indonesia and saw Compassion Australia's work first-hand.</li><li>We are learning that Compassion Australia shares the hope of Jesus alongside physical help.</li><li>We are learning that loving God means loving others, especially the poor and needy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell what Holly experienced on her trip, and explain what James 1:27 says about true religion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: access to compassion.com.au or a printed copy of the photo of Holly and her school mates with locals from Wangurer village</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: how did Holly find Compassion Australia?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to recall how Holly first came to know about Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We've learned a lot about Compassion Australia's programs this week. Do you remember how Holly first found out about the work of Compassion Australia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Holly came to know about Compassion Australia through a trip to Indonesia organised by her school.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Wangurer village</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through Holly's trip to Wangurer village, meeting the local staff, children and mothers, and read Colossians 3:12 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This verse says that because God has loved and chosen us, we should be gentle, kind, humble, meek and patient with others. How do you think Holly showed these qualities on her trip to Indonesia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: Holly showed kindness and gentleness by meeting with the local mothers and children, listening to their stories, and caring about their lives rather than just observing from a distance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>True religion — James 1:27</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read James 1:27 together and explain that loving God means loving others, especially the poor and needy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to reflect on how hearing about Holly and Compassion Australia has made them feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How has hearing about Holly's trip and the work of Compassion Australia made you feel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. There's no single right answer here — this is a personal reflection question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: how Holly supports Compassion Australia at her own school, and what the Bible says about generosity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“God loves you and has chosen you as his own special people. So be gentle, kind, humble, meek, and patient.” — <em>Colossians 3:12 (CEV)</em><br>“Religion that pleases God the Father must be pure and spotless. You must help needy orphans and widows and not let this world make you evil.” — <em>James 1:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the opportunity Holly had to go to Indonesia and see the work of Compassion Australia first-hand. Please help us to love you by loving the poor and needy, just as your word asks us to. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: How can I help?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 12:13–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How can I help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Holly finds creative ways to support Compassion Australia at her own school, and Jesus' story of the rich farmer reminds us that God wants us to be generous with what he has given us, rather than storing it up only for ourselves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning some of the ways Holly supports Compassion Australia at her own school.</li><li>We are learning what the rich farmer in Jesus' story did wrong.</li><li>We are learning that God wants us to be generous with what he has given us, especially toward the poor and needy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can describe at least one way Holly supports Compassion Australia, and explain what God wants us to do with what he's given us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank paper or the student Activity book, for students to write down their own ideas for how their school could support Compassion Australia</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Way in: ways to support Compassion Australia</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask students to brainstorm ways someone could support an organisation like Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some ways that someone might be able to support an organisation like Compassion Australia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This connects to what students are about to hear about how Holly herself supports Compassion Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Holly helps</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the ways Holly supports Compassion Australia as an events volunteer and at her own school (mufti days, cake stalls, speeches, videos).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The rich farmer — Luke 12:13–21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the rich farmer from Luke 12:13–21 and discuss what he did wrong and what God wants us to do instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does God want us to do with the things he's generously given us, instead of just storing them up for ourselves?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God wants us to be generous with what we've been given — sharing it to help others, especially the poor and needy, rather than hoarding it only for ourselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Personal generosity activity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give students a moment to talk with a partner about one small way they could be generous with what they have.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-discussion activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close together with the built-in verse and prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse and prayer blocks to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the final lesson of Term 2. Term 3 moves to a different level of the curriculum, Connect B2 Upper Primary, starting back at Lesson 1.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Be fair to the poor and to orphans. Defend the helpless and everyone in need.” — <em>Psalm 82:3 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for the work that Holly has been doing in promoting and raising money for Compassion Australia. Thank you for the opportunity to go over to Indonesia to see these programs first-hand. Please help Compassion Australia and Holly to make an impact in our world for those who are in poverty. Please also help us to think of ways that we can help make a difference too. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: Survivor: Egypt Begins":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:1—2:10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A big family in Egypt":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A big family in Egypt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Abraham's family came to live in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God's promise to Abraham — that his family would become a great nation — was coming true, generation by generation.</li><li>I can explain why Jacob's family moved to Egypt.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell, in their own words, how Jacob's family ended up in Egypt and can connect their growth in numbers to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Introducing a new story</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We're starting a brand new Bible story today, from a book called Exodus, about a man named Moses. But first we need to see how his people ended up in Egypt.” Briefly remind students of God's promise to Abraham (a great nation, descendants like the stars) if they covered this in earlier terms — otherwise just introduce it fresh.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jacob's family moves to Egypt</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of Jacob moving his family of seventy — eleven sons and their households — to Egypt to be near Joseph, who had become a powerful leader there. Mention that they settled in Goshen, good land for their flocks, and that the family was finally reunited after everything that had happened between Joseph and his brothers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jacob's family needed to move all the way to Egypt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: there was a severe famine in their own land, and Joseph — through God's plan — was in a position in Egypt to save his family from starving.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A family that keeps growing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Years passed. Jacob, Joseph, and all of that generation eventually died. But their families kept having children, and grandchildren, and great-grandchildren — until the whole region of Goshen was full of them.” Explain that Jacob's twelve sons each became the father of a whole tribe — that's where the twelve tribes of Israel come from.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remember God's promise to Abraham, that his family would become a great nation? What do you notice happening to that family here, generations later, in Egypt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's promise was quietly coming true, even in a foreign country, far from home — the Israelites were multiplying exactly as God had said they would.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Retelling relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pair students up and have them retell today's story in three sentences each — God's promise to Abraham, Jacob's move to Egypt, and the growth into twelve tribes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in retelling relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV). Read it together two or three times, then try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 introduces a new king of Egypt who turns on the Israelites and forces them into slavery — even though it doesn't stop their numbers from growing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are always faithful to your promises, even when we can't see it happening. Thank you for growing Abraham's family, just like you said you would. Help us trust that you keep your word. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: A king who forgot":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:8–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A king who forgot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that a new king in Egypt turned against the Israelites out of fear.</li><li>We are learning that the Israelites were forced into hard slave labour, but kept growing in number anyway.</li><li>I can explain why the growth of Abraham's family both showed God's promise and provoked the king's fear.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can articulate why the king felt threatened, and whether they can connect the Israelites' continued growth under oppression back to God's faithfulness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap and a new king</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, we saw Jacob's family grow into a huge nation while living in Egypt. But years later, everything changed.” Introduce the new king who never knew Joseph and feared the Israelites.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think this new king felt threatened by how big the Israelite family had grown?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: he worried they were too powerful, that they might join Egypt's enemies in a war, or even take over. Notice the irony — the very growth that showed God's promise is what frightened the king.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forced into slavery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the comic panel and explain that the king made the Israelites into slaves, forcing them into hard labour building his cities, Pithom and Rameses, under harsh taskmasters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel wk21-01-slave-labour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Feel the labour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the whole class stand and mime stacking heavy bricks as fast as they can for five seconds, then sit. Point out that the Israelites did that all day, every day, for years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in brick-miming activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Growing anyway</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The harder the Egyptians worked the Israelites, the more the Israelite families kept growing! Nothing the king did could stop it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Israelites were suffering as slaves, yet they kept growing in number. What does that tell us about God's promises, even during hard and unfair times?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's promise didn't stop just because life got hard — he was still faithfully keeping his word to Abraham, even in the middle of real suffering.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A king versus God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the second question about why the king couldn't stop what God had promised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The harder the king tried to crush the Israelites, the more God's promise kept coming true anyway. Why do you think the king couldn't stop something God had promised?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: no earthly king, no matter how powerful, can overpower God's plans. This sets up the whole rest of the Exodus story — a human king going head-to-head with God, and losing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows the king's cruelty getting worse still, and a mother's brave plan to save her baby son.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you don't stop keeping your promises, even when life feels hard or unfair. Help us to trust you when things around us don't make sense. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A basket of hope":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:15–22; 2:1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A basket of hope</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Pharaoh's order to kill Israelite baby boys.</li><li>We are learning how one mother bravely protected her son by placing him in a basket on the Nile.</li><li>I can describe the courage and trust this mother showed in a frightening situation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell the basket scene accurately and can articulate what trust in God might have looked like for this mother.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the comic panel and week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Shiphrah and Puah's courage</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Pharaoh's first, sneakier plan: telling the Hebrew midwives Shiphrah and Puah to secretly kill baby boys at birth. They feared God more than Pharaoh, and refused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the midwives were willing to disobey Pharaoh's direct order, even though it put them at risk?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: their respect and fear of God went deeper than their fear of Pharaoh — they trusted that obeying God, even at personal cost, mattered more than obeying an unjust human command.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The king's cruel order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So the king came up with an even crueller plan: he ordered that every baby boy born to the Israelites should be thrown into the Nile River.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Israelite parents felt when they heard this terrible order?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer that captures fear, grief, or desperation — it must have felt like their hope for the future was under attack.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A mother's brave plan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell the story of the Levite mother hiding her son for three months, then placing him in a tar-covered basket among the reeds of the Nile, with his sister watching nearby. Show the comic panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show comic panel wk21-02-basket-in-reeds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where did her courage come from?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pause on the enormity of what this mother did before opening the question to the class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This mother trusted God with her baby's life in an incredibly scary situation. What do you think gave her the courage to do this?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: faith that the same God who had kept his promise to Abraham could be trusted with her son's life too, even when she couldn't control what happened next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Naming the courage</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have partners name and compare the two acts of courage from today's story: the midwives and the mother.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-discussion activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 reveals who finds the basket — and how this baby's rescue connects all the way back to God's promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, please give us courage to trust you in scary situations, like the midwives and this mother did. Help us remember that you are watching over us even when we feel afraid. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Pulled out of the water":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 2:5–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pulled out of the water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises, even when things look hopeless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Pharaoh's daughter found and adopted baby Moses.</li><li>We are learning that God was working behind the scenes to protect Moses and provide for him through his own mother.</li><li>I can connect Moses's rescue back to God's promise to Abraham about rescuing his people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell how Moses was found and named, and can explain in their own words how his rescue connects to God's bigger promise to Abraham.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The princess finds the basket</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One of Pharaoh's own daughters came down to bathe in the river, noticed the basket, and found the crying baby inside. She felt sorry for him and knew he must be one of the Hebrew babies.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A surprising turn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The baby's sister bravely asked the princess if she wanted a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby — and without realising it, brought back the baby's own mother!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Out of everyone in Egypt, this baby's own mother ended up being the one who got to raise him. What do you think this shows us about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God was working behind the scenes the whole time, even in what looked like pure chance, to protect and provide for this baby.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses is named</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the boy was old enough, his mother brought him to Pharaoh's daughter, who adopted him as her own son and named him Moses, because she said, ‘I pulled him out of the water.’”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Connecting back to the promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remind students of God's promise to Abraham that his family would be rescued from slavery in a foreign land, then open the closing question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does baby Moses's rescue connect to God's promise to Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: this baby, saved in such an unlikely way and raised right inside Pharaoh's own palace, was being prepared by God to one day become exactly the person he would use to rescue his people and keep his promise to Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order, one sentence each: the family growing in Egypt, the cruel king and slavery, the basket in the reeds, and Moses's rescue and naming.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2 (Lesson 2) picks up with Moses as a grown man, his flight from Egypt, and God calling him at the burning bush.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you had a plan all along for Moses, even when things looked hopeless. Thank you that you have a plan for us too. Help us trust you, even when we can't see how things will work out. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 2: The Leader":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:1—4:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A blazing bush":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A blazing bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Moses ended up as a shepherd in Midian, far from Egypt.</li><li>We are learning how God called out to Moses from a bush that burned but was never consumed.</li><li>I can explain what God's instruction about holy ground tells us about who God is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can retell how Moses came to be in Midian and can describe what happened at the burning bush, including why God told him to remove his sandals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>From the palace to the desert</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Last time, baby Moses was rescued from the Nile and raised in Pharaoh's palace. Today we jump ahead many years.” Explain that Moses killed an Egyptian who was mistreating an Israelite, then fled to Midian out of fear, where he became a shepherd and started a family. Pause on the huge change from palace prince to ordinary shepherd.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A strange sight</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One ordinary day, while Moses was out with the sheep near Mount Horeb, he noticed a bush blazing with fire — but it never burned up.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Moses stopped to look closer at the bush, instead of just walking past it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any answer that notices Moses's curiosity at something so unusual. This small choice to pay attention is what led him to encounter God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Holy ground</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“As Moses got closer, God called out to him from the bush and told him to take off his sandals, because the ground he stood on was holy.” Mention that God identified himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and that Moses hid his face, afraid to look at God directly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Taking off our shoes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the class mime removing their shoes like Moses, then discuss with a partner why that gesture mattered.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in shoes-off activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What holy ground tells us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the question about what this moment tells us about God's character.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does God's instruction about holy ground tell us about who God is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God is holy and set apart from everything else, worthy of reverence and respect. The ordinary ground became special simply because God was present there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV) — the same verse used across this unit. Read it together two or three times, then try it once without looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 hears God's rescue plan for Israel and the mission he gives Moses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are holy, and that you still choose to speak to ordinary people like Moses — and like us. Help us to notice when you are trying to get our attention. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: God's rescue plan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:7–15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's rescue plan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God saw and heard the suffering of the Israelites in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning that God sent Moses with a mission to bring his people out of Egypt.</li><li>I can explain what God's name ‘I AM' tells us about who he is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can explain God's rescue plan and mission for Moses, and can say in their own words what the name ‘I AM' reveals about God's character.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God has seen and heard</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God told Moses, ‘I have seen how my people are suffering as slaves in Egypt. I have heard their cries, and I have come down to rescue them.'”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The mission</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God said, ‘So now, go. I am sending you to Pharaoh, to bring my people out of Egypt.'”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Moses felt in that moment, when God told him rescuing his whole people was going to be his job?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Moses likely felt shocked, unqualified, and afraid — he was a shepherd hiding out in the desert, not a powerful leader, and Pharaoh was the most powerful man in Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>‘Who am I?'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses's first response was, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh?' God's answer was simple: ‘I will be with you.' The mission never depended on Moses feeling qualified.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Making it personal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have partners share what they'd say back if God asked them to do something that felt too big.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-sharing activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>I AM WHO I AM</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses asked what he should say God's name was. God replied, ‘I AM WHO I AM — tell them I AM has sent me to you,' explaining he was the same God who had promised Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it means that God calls himself ‘I AM'?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God has always existed and never changes — he is the same faithful God who made promises long ago, still showing up for his people exactly when they need him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 shows Moses making excuses — and the signs God gives him to prove he really was sent by God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you see and hear when your people are suffering, and that you always keep your promises. Thank you that you are the same faithful ‘I AM' today, and that you are with us even when we feel unqualified. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Moses makes excuses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 3:16—4:9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moses makes excuses</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Moses doubted whether the Israelites would believe or listen to him.</li><li>We are learning about the miraculous signs God gave Moses as proof of his calling.</li><li>I can connect Moses's self-doubt to times I've felt unsure about something I was asked to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe at least one of the signs God gave Moses and can explain why God provided this kind of proof.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The plan so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that God told Moses to gather the elders of Israel, explain the plan, and then go together to ask Pharaoh's permission to worship God in the wilderness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses worries — and the first sign</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses worried, ‘What if they don't believe me?' So God gave him a sign: his staff turning into a snake and back again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have the class mime holding a staff that suddenly becomes a snake, acting out Moses's fear, then bravely picking it back up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in staff-to-snake activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two more signs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God made Moses's hand become diseased, then healed it. And a third sign: water from the Nile poured on the ground would turn to blood.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God gave Moses these miraculous signs, instead of just telling him to trust that people would believe him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the signs gave Moses — and later the Israelites and Pharaoh — real evidence that God's power was truly behind him. Seeing helped people believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Making it personal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect honestly on times they've felt unqualified or unready for something.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you ever felt like Moses — like you weren't good enough or ready enough for something you were asked to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept personal responses. Connect it back to the big idea: God equips the people he calls: he doesn't wait for us to feel confident before he can use us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Repeat this unit's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV), together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 shows Moses's final excuse — and how God provides his brother Aaron to help him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you don't need us to feel brave or ready before you can use us. Help us to trust you, even when we feel unsure of ourselves. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Moses and his helper":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 4:10–17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moses and his helper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chooses ordinary, reluctant people and equips them for his extraordinary plans.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Moses's final excuse — that he wasn't a good speaker.</li><li>We are learning how God provided Aaron to help Moses speak to Pharaoh.</li><li>I can explain what God providing Aaron shows us about how God equips the people he calls.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can explain Moses's final objection and describe how God's provision of Aaron addressed it, and can summarise how Moses changed across the week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One last excuse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Moses said, ‘Pardon me, Lord, I have never been a good speaker. I am slow of speech and tongue.' He begged God to send someone else instead.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's anger — and God's provision</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God's anger burned against Moses for still trying to get out of the job — but even angry, God didn't give up on him.” Explain that God reminded Moses his brother Aaron was already on his way to meet him, and that Aaron could speak on Moses's behalf while Moses told him what to say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it show us about God that he had already arranged for Aaron to help Moses, even before Moses asked, and even while God was frustrated with him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God knows our weaknesses ahead of time and provides exactly what we need to obey him. His patience and his provision go together — he doesn't demand that we do everything completely alone, even when he's disappointed in our reluctance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order: the burning bush and holy ground, God's rescue plan and the name ‘I AM', Moses's doubts and the three signs, and God providing Aaron.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Looking back over the week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect on how Moses changed across the whole week's story, from hiding by the bush to finally being ready to go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses started out full of excuses and doubts. By the end, was he ready to go? What changed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Moses wasn't naturally brave or qualified, but God's patient reassurance, the signs, and Aaron's help gave him what he needed to obey — even while he was probably still nervous.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3 (Lesson 3) picks up with Moses and Aaron confronting Pharaoh, and the start of the plagues on Egypt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you knew exactly what Moses needed, even before he asked, and that you didn't give up on him even when he kept making excuses. Thank you that you know what we need too. Help us to trust you and obey, even when we feel unsure. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 3: The Challenge":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 7:1—10:29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Let my people go!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 7:1–8:15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let my people go!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Moses and Aaron first confronted Pharaoh, and how his heart stayed hard even after Aaron's staff-snake swallowed the magicians' snakes.</li><li>We are learning about the first two plagues — blood and frogs — and how Pharaoh's promises during the frogs plague didn't last.</li><li>I can describe the pattern of Pharaoh refusing, God sending a plague, and Pharaoh's heart hardening again.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the confrontation with the staff-snake and retell the first two plagues in order, and can explain in their own words why Pharaoh's change of heart during the frogs plague didn't last.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Face to face with Pharaoh</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Moses and Aaron are now ready to confront Pharaoh. Tell how Aaron's staff became a snake, how the magicians copied the trick, and how Aaron's snake swallowed all of theirs — yet Pharaoh's heart stayed hard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Aaron's snake swallowed all of the magicians' snakes — why do you think Pharaoh still refused to listen, even after seeing that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the sign should have convinced anyone watching honestly, but Pharaoh's pride and stubbornness weren't really about evidence — he simply didn't want to give up his slaves or admit another king had power over him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 1: Blood</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses and Aaron warned Pharaoh at the Nile, how Aaron struck the water and it turned to blood, killing the fish and making the water undrinkable — but the magicians copied it, so Pharaoh's heart stayed hard for seven days.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine living in Egypt for seven days with no clean water to drink, while Pharaoh just walked back into his palace. What does that tell you about the cost of one man's stubborn pride?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: ordinary Egyptian families suffered real hardship because of Pharaoh's refusal to listen — his stubbornness wasn't a private matter, it affected everyone around him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 2: Frogs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how frogs swarmed every corner of Egypt, how Pharaoh finally begged Moses and Aaron to pray for relief and promised to let the people go — but broke his promise the moment the frogs were gone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in freeze-frame frogs activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce (or continue) this week's memory verse and practise it together as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 continues the plagues with gnats, flies, and diseased livestock, and shows God protecting his own people in Goshen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are more powerful than every false god and every stubborn heart. Thank you that you kept your promise to rescue your people, even when Pharaoh refused again and again. Help us to listen to you the first time, instead of only turning to you when things get hard. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Bugs, flies, and sick animals":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 8:16–9:7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bugs, flies, and sick animals</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the plagues of gnats, flies, and diseased livestock, and how each one Pharaoh's own advisors or actions confirmed came from God.</li><li>We are learning how God specifically protected the Israelites in Goshen during these plagues.</li><li>I can explain why God made the difference between Goshen and the rest of Egypt so obvious.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe all three plagues in this section and explain what the protection of Goshen showed about God's power and control.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 3: Gnats</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Aaron struck the dust and it became gnats across Egypt, and how this time the magicians couldn't copy it — they had to admit to Pharaoh that this had to be God's own doing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh's own magicians told him this plague was from God. Why do you think that mattered, and why do you think Pharaoh still refused to listen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it removed any excuse Pharaoh might have had — his continued refusal became entirely his own stubborn choice, not a lack of evidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 4: Flies (and Goshen protected)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God promised Goshen would have no flies, how Pharaoh bargained and offered partial deals, and how his heart hardened again the moment relief came.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 5: Livestock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how every Egyptian animal died from disease, but not one Israelite animal was touched — Pharaoh even checked and confirmed it himself, yet still refused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Three plagues in a row now, God has drawn a clear line between Goshen and the rest of Egypt. Why do you think he kept making that difference so obvious?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it proved these plagues weren't random bad luck, but entirely under God's control — leaving Pharaoh with no excuse to pretend it was just misfortune.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Partner talk and memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the partner discussion activity, then practise the memory verse together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in partner-talk activity block, then the built-in verse block and say-it-together activity.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 continues with boils, the worst hailstorm in Egypt's history, and a devastating swarm of locusts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that nothing that happens is outside of your control — not even swarms of insects or sick animals. Thank you for how carefully you looked after your people in Goshen. Help us trust that you're looking after us too, even when things around us feel chaotic. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Boils, hail, and locusts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 9:8–10:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boils, hail, and locusts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the plagues of boils, hail, and locusts, and how each one grew more serious than the last.</li><li>We are noticing the shift in the story from Pharaoh hardening his own heart to the Lord hardening Pharaoh's heart.</li><li>I can explain the difference between Pharaoh's fear-driven promises and genuine repentance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the three plagues in order and can explain, in their own words, why Pharaoh's repeated promises during the hail and locusts didn't reflect real change.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 6: Boils</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses threw ashes into the air and boils broke out on people and animals, even affecting the magicians themselves. Note the shift in the text — now it says the Lord hardened Pharaoh's heart.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 7: Hail</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God warned of the worst hailstorm in Egypt's history, how some officials heeded the warning and some didn't, how Goshen was untouched, and how Pharaoh's promise collapsed the moment the storm stopped.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh kept promising to let Israel go the moment a plague hit hard, then broke his promise the instant the trouble stopped. What does that tell us about the difference between real regret and only being sorry you got caught?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Pharaoh's ‘sorry' was about escaping pain, not truly caring for the Israelites or turning back to God — real repentance leads to lasting change, not relief-driven bargaining.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: fear vs. real change</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the partner act-it-out activity dramatising Pharaoh's pattern of promising then reneging.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in act-it-out activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 8: Locusts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh's own officials begged him to relent, how his final offer excluded the flocks and herds, and how the locust swarm stripped Egypt of everything green before being swept into the Red Sea — yet Pharaoh's heart was hardened again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Practise the memory verse together, encouraging students to try it from memory.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block and say-it-together activity.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 brings the ninth and final plague of this lesson — three days of total darkness — and looks back over the whole week's story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are patient, even with people who keep breaking their promises to you. Help us to be honest with you when we're sorry — not just sorry the trouble is happening, but truly ready to change. Thank you that your plan to rescue your people never stopped moving forward, no matter how many times Pharaoh said no. Amen.</blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Three days of darkness":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 10:21–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Three days of darkness</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is more powerful than every false god Egypt worshipped — and no amount of stubbornness could stop his plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the ninth plague — three days of darkness — and Pharaoh's final, angry refusal in this lesson's passage.</li><li>We are looking back over the whole week to retell all nine plagues in order.</li><li>I can explain what the nine plagues, taken together, show us about God's power over Egypt's false gods.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Check that students can describe the plague of darkness and Pharaoh's final offer and refusal, can retell the whole week's sequence of plagues in order, and can explain what the plagues showed about God's power over Egypt's false gods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Plague 9: Darkness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses stretched out his hand and three days of total darkness covered Egypt, while the Israelites had light. Describe Pharaoh's final offer — letting everyone go but not their animals — and Moses's refusal, ending in Pharaoh's furious threat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After nine plagues, Pharaoh still refused to fully let God's people go. What does his refusal, even now, tell us about how far pride and stubbornness can carry a person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: Pharaoh's heart had become so hardened that even overwhelming evidence didn't change him — a sober reminder of how serious it is to keep refusing God, and how powerless his stubbornness really was against God's plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whole-week recap relay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In table groups, have students retell the whole week's story in order: the staff-turned-snake, then all nine plagues — blood, frogs, gnats, flies, sick animals, boils, hail, locusts, and darkness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in whole-week recap relay activity block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What was God showing Egypt?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Invite students to reflect on the purpose behind all nine plagues together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Looking back over all nine plagues this week, what do you think God was trying to show Pharaoh and the whole of Egypt through them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: many of these plagues struck directly at things the Egyptians treated as gods — the Nile, frogs, livestock, the sun. Through every plague, the Lord kept proving that he alone is the true and living God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse, from memory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the week by inviting the class to say the memory verse together entirely from memory, without looking at the screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block, then the say-it-together activity that follows it in the app.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4 (Lesson 4) picks up with the tenth and final plague, and the story of the first Passover.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are the only true God, in the sky above and on the earth below, more powerful than every false god Egypt trusted in. Thank you that your plan to rescue your people never failed, even after nine plagues and a king who refused you every time. Help us to trust you fully, the first time you ask, rather than only turning to you when things get hard. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 4: The Rescue":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 11–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The last plague":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 11:1–10; 12:1–28</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The last plague</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the tenth plague was God's final and most serious warning to Pharaoh.</li><li>We are learning that God protected his people through the blood of the Passover lamb, on the condition they trusted and obeyed him.</li><li>I can begin learning this term's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the blood on the doorframes mattered, and can connect obeying God's instructions with being kept safe.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading the Passover instructions aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nine warnings so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that God has already sent nine plagues and Pharaoh still refuses, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So far, God has sent nine plagues on Egypt, but Pharaoh still won't let God's people go. If you were God, and you'd already given someone nine warnings, what would you do next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up how serious the tenth and final plague was — this wasn't God acting rashly, it was the last of ten clear warnings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The tenth plague announced</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God warned Pharaoh of the death of the firstborn as his final, most serious plague.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Blood on the doorframes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the Passover instructions, and how the blood on the doorframes protected Israelite households.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why did God ask for obedience?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God required an action from his people rather than protecting them automatically.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God asked his people to do something — painting blood on their doorframes — rather than just automatically protecting them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God still asked for trust and obedience even in the middle of rescuing them. Their protection came from trusting the Lord and doing what he said, not from anything special about themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this term's new memory verse and try saying it together a few times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 picks up right after the Passover, as Pharaoh finally lets Israel go — and then changes his mind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Pharaoh's army":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 12:31–36; 13:17–22; 14:1–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pharaoh's army</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pharaoh let Israel go, and that God led them with a pillar of cloud and fire.</li><li>We are learning that God deliberately led Israel to a place that looked like a trap, so he could show his power when Pharaoh's army pursued them.</li><li>I can explain what Moses meant when he told the frightened Israelites, 'The Lord will fight for you.'</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God led Israel into a seemingly trapped position on purpose, and can describe Moses' response to the people's fear.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Leaving Egypt at last</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh finally let Israel go, and how the Egyptians sent them off with gold, silver and clothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Led into a trap — on purpose</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the pillar of cloud and fire, and God's deliberate choice to camp near the Red Sea, then ask why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God deliberately led his people into a spot that looked like a trap, instead of a safer route?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward Exodus 14:1–4: God wanted to use this moment to show his power over Pharaoh and the Egyptian army, so that everyone would know he is the Lord — not because he'd made a mistake.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pharaoh's army arrives</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Pharaoh changed his mind and pursued Israel, and how the people responded in fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Israelites had just watched God defeat Egypt through ten plagues and the Passover — yet now they were terrified again. Why do you think it's so easy to become afraid again, even after we've seen God come through before?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: fear can make us forget what we already know about God, which is exactly why Moses' next words were so important.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'The Lord will fight for you'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Moses' reassurance to the people, then close with the memory verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out exactly how God rescues Israel from the Egyptian army at the Red Sea.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: A road through the water":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 14:15–31</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A road through the water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning exactly how God parted, and then closed, the Red Sea to rescue Israel and defeat the Egyptian army.</li><li>We are learning that God's angel and the pillar of cloud protected Israel by holding the Egyptians back overnight.</li><li>I can retell, in my own words, how God saved the Israelites from the Egyptian army.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can retell the sequence of the Red Sea crossing accurately (parting, crossing, closing) using their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An impossible situation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap yesterday's cliffhanger and ask the warm-up question before revealing what happens.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Yesterday we left the Israelites trapped between Pharaoh's army and the Red Sea. If you were one of the Israelites at that moment, what do you think would be going through your mind?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This sets up just how impossible the situation looked, right before God acts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The sea parts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses stretched out his staff and the sea split, and how the cloud/angel protected Israel overnight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Through on dry ground</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the crossing and the Egyptian army's pursuit and panic.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The sea closes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the sea rushed back and swept the Egyptian army away, then ask students to retell the whole rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did God save the Israelites from the Egyptian army?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses that mention the parting of the sea, the pillar of cloud/angel holding back the Egyptians overnight, and the sea closing over the army the next morning. All three parts worked together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together without looking, if the class is ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks what this rescue still means for people today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: Rescued like Israel":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 2:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rescued like Israel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is willing and able to rescue his people from what looks like an impossible situation — from the plagues in Egypt, through the Passover, and all the way through the Red Sea. This same rescuing God still saves people from slavery to sin today, through Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Exodus rescue points forward to people's need for rescue from sin.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is the one who rescues people and brings them to God, as 1 Timothy 2:5 says.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in simple terms, how Jesus' rescue relates to the rescue God gave Israel at the Red Sea.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading 1 Timothy 2:5 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One more rescue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap this week's rescue, then ask students to think of other rescues in the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who else can you think of, from anything you already know about the Bible, that God has rescued?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses, e.g. Noah, or Jesus rescuing people from sin. This connects the Exodus rescue pattern to the wider Bible story students may already know.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rescued from sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that all people need rescuing from sin today, and that God sent Jesus to do this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Jesus rescues us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read 1 Timothy 2:5, then ask how Jesus rescues his people today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does Jesus rescue his people today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: Jesus died on the cross and rose again so that everyone who trusts him can be forgiven and brought back into a right relationship with God — his death and resurrection do for us today what nothing else could.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 moves on to Mount Sinai, where God gives his rescued people his Law to show them how to live as his special people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“There is only one God, and Christ Jesus is the only one who can bring us to God. Jesus was truly human, and he gave himself to rescue all of us.” — <em>1 Timothy 2:5 (CEV)</em><br>“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you rescued your people from Egypt, and thank you that you sent Jesus to rescue us from our sin. Help us to remember and trust that you are the one true God, in heaven and on earth. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 5: The Law":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God's promise to his people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 19:1–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promise to his people</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God led Israel to Mount Sinai and offered them a special covenant relationship.</li><li>We are learning that Israel agreed to obey everything the Lord had said.</li><li>I can explain what it means that Israel would be God's 'treasured possession'.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, what God offered Israel at Sinai and how the people responded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What comes after a rescue?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap last week's rescue at the Red Sea, then ask the warm-up question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we saw God rescue Israel out of Egypt and through the Red Sea. If God has just done something amazing to save you, what do you think he might want from you next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: not payment or earning the rescue back, but a relationship — as we'll see today, God's answer was to invite Israel into a special relationship with him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Arriving at Sinai</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Israel travelled to Mount Sinai and Moses went up to meet with God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's treasured possession</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read God's covenant offer to the people, then ask what being God's 'treasured possession' would mean.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it would mean to be God's 'treasured possession' out of every nation on earth?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: being specially chosen, loved, and set apart by God — not because Israel had earned it, but because God graciously chose them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'We will do everything the Lord has said'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the people responded together, then introduce the memory verse (carried over from last week).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Deuteronomy 4:39, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 finds out exactly what God's Law said, starting with the Ten Commandments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Loving God first":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 20:1–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Loving God first</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the first four of the Ten Commandments, which are about our relationship with God.</li><li>We are learning that these commandments teach Israel to worship only the Lord, and to keep the Sabbath as belonging to him.</li><li>I can explain why commandments about God come before commandments about people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name at least one of the first four commandments and explain, broadly, what it teaches about our relationship with God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What comes first?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question before revealing how the Ten Commandments actually begin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were making a list of rules for how to live well, what do you think should come first on the list?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up how the Ten Commandments begin — not with rules about people, but about our relationship with God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The first four commandments</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the first four commandments together, about worship, idols, God's name, and the Sabbath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why God first?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why commandments about God come before commandments about people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God put commandments about himself first, before commandments about how to treat other people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: our relationship with God comes first and shapes everything else — how we treat others flows out of how we treat God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 covers the rest of the Ten Commandments — how to treat other people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Loving our neighbour":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 20:12–17; 19:3–8 (recap)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Loving our neighbour</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the last six of the Ten Commandments, which are about how to treat other people.</li><li>We are learning that Israel agreed to keep God's Law only after God had already rescued them.</li><li>I can explain why the rescue came before the Law, not after it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain that obedience was Israel's grateful response to being rescued, not a way of earning God's rescue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The last six commandments</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read through the last six commandments together, about how to treat other people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The hardest one</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask which commandment students think would be hardest to keep, and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which of these six commandments do you think would have been the hardest for people to keep, and why?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept any reasonable answer with a real reason. There's no wrong answer here — the goal is for students to notice these commandments are still genuinely hard to keep today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rescue before Law</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Israel agreed to the Law only after being rescued, then ask why the order matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it mattered that God rescued Israel before he gave them the Law, rather than the other way around?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: obedience wasn't how Israel earned their rescue — the rescue came first. Keeping the Law was meant to be a grateful response to what God had already done for them, not a way of earning it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try the memory verse together without looking, if the class is ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 looks at how Jesus summed up the whole Law in just two commandments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: The Law summed up":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 22:36–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Law summed up</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God rescued Israel out of slavery in Egypt, and now, at Mount Sinai, he gives them his Law to show his people how to live as his special, treasured people — not to earn his rescue, but because he had already saved them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus summed up the whole Law in two commandments: love God, and love others.</li><li>We are learning how the Ten Commandments fit under these two headings.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Deuteronomy 4:39, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can sort the Ten Commandments correctly under 'loving God' and 'loving others', and explain Jesus' summary in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Matthew 22:36–40 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus is asked the question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Jesus' answer about the most important commandments.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Connecting it back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how Jesus' two commandments connect to the Ten Commandments learned this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do Jesus' two commandments — love God, and love others — connect to the Ten Commandments we've learned about this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: the first four commandments are all about loving God, and the last six are all about loving others — Jesus wasn't giving a new rule, he was summing up the whole Law we've already learned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How to live today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what God has shown us about how to live today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What has God shown us about how to live today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: the Bible — God's word — still shows people today how to live as his people, out of love and gratitude for what he's done, not to earn his love.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish this week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 6 moves on to the tabernacle — the special tent where God chose to live among his people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“So remember that the Lord is the only true God, whether in the sky above or on the earth below.” — <em>Deuteronomy 4:39 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you rescued us first, before ever asking anything of us. Thank you for your Law that shows us how to live — to love you and love others. Help us to obey you out of love and gratitude, not because we have to earn your rescue. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 6: The Tabernacle":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 25–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God wants to live with his people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 25:1–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God wants to live with his people</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God wanted to live among his people, and asked them to build him a sacred tent.</li><li>We are learning that God gave Moses an exact pattern to follow for the tabernacle.</li><li>I can begin learning this week's new memory verse, Psalm 117:2.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why God wanted a tabernacle, and why building it exactly to the pattern mattered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A home for God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the warm-up question before describing the tabernacle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If God wanted to actually live among his people, right in the middle of their camp, what do you think that tent or building would need to be like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use this to set up just how detailed and careful God's instructions for the tabernacle were.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>'Make me a sacred tent'</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how God asked for gifts to build the tabernacle, so he could live among his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>An exact pattern</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how detailed God's instructions were, then ask why the exact pattern mattered.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it mattered so much that Moses build the tabernacle exactly to God's pattern, instead of using his own ideas?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward Hebrews 8:1–5: the tabernacle was a copy of something real in heaven, so getting the earthly copy exactly right mattered — it wasn't just decoration, it showed something true about God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this week's new memory verse, Psalm 117:2, and try saying it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in verse block for Psalm 117:2, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 looks at why priests and sacrifices were needed before anyone could enter God's presence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Tuesday: Priests and sacrifices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 40 (overview)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Priests and sacrifices</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the tabernacle had separate areas, with the most holy place reserved for God's presence.</li><li>We are learning that priests and sacrifices were needed because of the seriousness of sin.</li><li>I can explain why not just anyone could enter the most holy place.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, simply, why priests and sacrifices were necessary before anyone could approach God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Areas of the tabernacle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the courtyard, holy place, and most holy place, and who could go where.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Priests and sacrifices</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain the role of priests and daily sacrifices in dealing with the people's sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God set up such careful rules about who could come near him, and how?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: God's holiness and people's sinfulness don't mix — the sacrifices and priesthood were God's own gracious way of dealing with sin so his people could still be near him safely, not humans just making it hard for themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How seriously God takes sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what the sacrifice system tells us about how seriously God takes sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does this tell us about how seriously God takes sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: sin is a genuinely serious problem that needed real dealing with — through the blood of an animal sacrifice, standing in for the person's sin, every single day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out how Jesus' death changed all of this, once and for all.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Wednesday: Jesus, our once-for-all sacrifice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 15:37–39; Hebrews 10:11–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus, our once-for-all sacrifice</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the temple curtain tore in two when Jesus died, from top to bottom.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus offered himself once for all as our great high priest, so the old sacrifice system was no longer needed.</li><li>I can explain how Jesus' death is different from the priests' sacrifices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect the torn curtain to what Jesus' death achieved, and explain why his sacrifice was 'once for all'.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The curtain tears</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how the temple curtain tore when Jesus died, then ask why the direction of the tear matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think it's significant that the curtain tore from top to bottom, rather than being ripped from the bottom by a person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: it showed this was God's own doing, not a human tearing it — signalling that the barrier between God and his people had been dealt with by God himself, through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Our great high priest</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Jesus offered himself once for all as the perfect sacrifice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Once for all</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how Jesus' sacrifice is different from what the priests did about sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is what Jesus did different from what the priests did about sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guide toward: priests could never take away sin, only cover it temporarily — but Jesus' one sacrifice deals with sin completely and forever, so that people who trust him are set free from sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this week's memory verse together once more.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 looks forward to when God's people will finally live with him face to face, forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>"},"Thursday: No more tabernacle needed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Revelation 21:1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">No more tabernacle needed</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God chose to live among his people in the tabernacle — but because God is holy and his people are sinful, he also gave detailed instructions for priests and sacrifices, a way to be made right with him. This whole system points forward to Jesus, whose death lets us come into God's presence forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Revelation promises a future where God will live with his people forever, face to face.</li><li>We are learning that this future is what the whole tabernacle story, and Jesus' sacrifice, were pointing toward all along.</li><li>I can say this week's memory verse, Psalm 117:2, fully from memory.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain, in their own words, how the tabernacle story ends with the promise in Revelation 21.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>CEV Bible (optional, for reading Revelation 21:1–4 aloud)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The whole week, and beyond</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the week's journey from the tabernacle to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God's home with his people</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the promise from Revelation 21, then ask students to imagine what it will be like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it will be like to live with God face to face, with no more sin, sadness, or separation between us and him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses — encourage genuine imagination here. Guide toward: this is what the whole tabernacle system was pointing forward to all along — perfect, permanent closeness with God, made possible through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Real hope</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how this future promise changes the way we think about following Jesus today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does knowing this future change the way we think about following Jesus today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: it gives us real hope — everything broken about our relationship with God because of sin will one day be completely fixed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse and closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish this week's memory verse fully from memory, then close together with the prayer in the app.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the say-it-from-memory activity block, then the built-in prayer block to close.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This closes out the B2 Upper Primary Exodus material built so far (Lessons 4–6). Lessons 1–3 for this track, and any lessons beyond 6, are not yet built.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you have always wanted to live among your people — through the tabernacle, and now through Jesus. Thank you that one day we will live with you forever, with nothing broken between us. Help us trust you and love you until that day comes. Amen.</blockquote></div>"}}},"Week 7: The exploration":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Numbers 13–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promises are good promises and he is faithful to them — but when God's people refused to trust and obey him, they missed out on what he had promised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Back on the move":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Numbers 10:11–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaving Sinai — remembering how God provided</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promises are good promises and he is faithful to them — but when God's people refused to trust and obey him, they missed out on what he had promised.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people left Mount Sinai after two years and camped at the edge of the Promised Land.</li><li>We are learning that God provided food, water and protection for his people the whole time they were in the desert.</li><li>I can explain why God's people had good reason to trust him.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name specific things God had already done for his people that gave them reason to trust him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic panel: God's provision (manna in the desert)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two years at Sinai</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the tabernacle from last week, then explain that it is now time for God's people to move on.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The journey so far</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Trace the route out loud: Egypt, Red Sea, Mount Sinai, and now Kadesh-Barnea in the Desert of Paran, at the edge of the land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have the Journey to the Promised Land map, trace the path as you speak.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How God provided</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe manna, quail, water from the rock, and protection from the Amalekites, then show the comic panel and ask the key question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the manna image block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God had fed them, given them water and protected them every single day for two years. What should that have taught them about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God can be trusted. He does what he says. He had never once let them go hungry or thirsty — so there was no good reason to think he would let them down now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trust challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the paired trust challenge, then link it straight back: this week God's people are challenged to trust God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Supervise carefully. Anyone silly or unsafe sits down and misses out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say this term's memory verse, Psalm 117:2, together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 sends twelve explorers into the land — and they come back with two very different reports.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Twelve explorers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Numbers 13:1–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The twelve explorers and their two reports</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promises are good promises and he is faithful to them — but when God's people refused to trust and obey him, they missed out on what he had promised.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God sent twelve explorers into the land he was giving his people.</li><li>We are learning that ten explorers spread a bad report and only Caleb and Joshua trusted God.</li><li>I can explain that the twelve disagreed about God, not about the land.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state what all twelve agreed on, and exactly where the ten and the two differed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: Two reports</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Explorer, Caleb, Joshua (and a Narrator), and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The mission</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain who was sent and what their instructions were — the land, the people, the cities, the soil, the trees, and fruit to bring back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forty days later</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Report what all twelve agreed on — the land is good, flowing with milk and honey — then ask the key question before revealing the split.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The twelve explorers all walked the same land and saw exactly the same things. So why do you think they came back with two completely different reports?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: they didn't disagree about the land — they disagreed about God. Ten measured the job against themselves; two measured it against God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Ten against two</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give the ten explorers' bad report, then Caleb and Joshua's report.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the hands-up vote — who will the Israelites listen to? Keep it as a cliffhanger for tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How quickly they forgot</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the closing question about how quickly the explorers had forgotten what God had already done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How quickly had the explorers forgotten the God who brought them out of Egypt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God had just crushed the strongest empire on earth to get them out, and promised to go ahead of them. The walled cities were never the real problem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out who the people listened to — and what it cost them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: The night they said no":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Numbers 14:1–25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Israel refuses to trust God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promises are good promises and he is faithful to them — but when God's people refused to trust and obey him, they missed out on what he had promised.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people believed the ten explorers and refused to go into the land.</li><li>We are learning that Moses pleaded for the people and God forgave them, but the grumbling generation still did not enter the land.</li><li>I can explain that God was faithful to his promise even when his people were not.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can say what the people had promised at Sinai and why refusing to enter the land was a rejection of God, not just of Moses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: Caleb and Joshua's plea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for People, Caleb, Joshua, God (and a Narrator), and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who did they listen to?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reveal the answer to yesterday's cliffhanger and describe the night of crying and grumbling.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Caleb and Joshua plead</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Caleb and Joshua begged the people, and how the people picked up stones — until God's glory appeared.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who remembers what God's people had promised God at Mount Sinai?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They promised to do everything God commanded — Exodus 19:7–8. This is the promise they had just broken.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Good reasons to trust</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the class to list the reasons God's people already had to trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did God's people have any good reasons to trust God? What were they?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Yes — the plagues, the Red Sea, Mount Sinai, the tabernacle, and two years of food, water and protection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forgiven — and forty years</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Moses interceded, God forgave, and the grumbling generation still would not enter. Then ask the key question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God forgave them. So why do you think they still missed out on the land?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: forgiveness is real but doesn't always erase consequences. God kept his promise about the land — with the next generation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks what a trustworthy God has promised his people today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: God is still trustworthy":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 3:16</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A trustworthy God, then and now</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's promises are good promises and he is faithful to them — but when God's people refused to trust and obey him, they missed out on what he had promised.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God is trustworthy and always keeps his promises.</li><li>We are learning that God has promised eternal life to everyone who trusts in Jesus.</li><li>I can explain that trusting God shows itself in obeying him.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name a promise God has made to people today and describe one way someone shows they trust him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A trustworthy God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw the conclusion out of the story: God's promises are good and he keeps them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How could God's people have shown that they trusted God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. By obeying his command and going in to take the land. Trust shows up in what you do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Caleb and Joshua</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Contrast the people who missed out with the two who trusted God and did enter the land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The same God today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read John 3:16 and connect it to God's promise-keeping.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the John 3:16 verse block alongside the term's memory verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God promised Israel a land. What has God promised Christians today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. That everyone who trusts in Jesus will be forgiven and will live with God forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Showing trust</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how someone shows they trust God, and how they'd know what he wants.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How could someone show God that they trust him — and how would they know what God wants them to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. By obeying him; by reading the Bible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: forty years later, a new generation stands at the edge of the land and Moses gives them God's final instructions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that we learned today that you are trustworthy and always keep your promises. Thank you that you loved the world so much that you sent your Son to bring forgiveness of sins, so that your people can live forever with you. Please help your people to trust and obey you. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 8: The people's response":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Deuteronomy 4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is faithful to his promises and will give his people what he promised — but he wants his people to trust and obey him, and he never gives up on them even when they fail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 8</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Forty years later":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Deuteronomy 4 (introduction); Numbers 20:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A new generation at the edge of the land</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is faithful to his promises and will give his people what he promised — but he wants his people to trust and obey him, and he never gives up on them even when they fail.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that after forty years God brought a new generation to the edge of the Promised Land.</li><li>We are learning that Moses was not allowed to enter the land, and gave three final speeches instead.</li><li>I can explain that trusting God and obeying God go together.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why the people needed reminding of what God had done.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A blindfold (or scarf) for the Way in challenge</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forty years of wandering</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap last week, then describe the forty years and God's continued provision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses' last speeches</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Moses could not enter the land, and that Deuteronomy is his three final speeches.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have the Journey to the Promised Land map, point out the wandering that finished east of the Jordan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Blindfold challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the blindfold challenge with two volunteers, then debrief with the key question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Supervise carefully; clear the route first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">To finish that challenge, what two things did the blindfolded person have to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Trust their partner, and obey them. Trusting without obeying gets you nowhere.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why remind them?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why the people needed reminding of everything God had done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses spent his last speeches reminding the people of everything God had already done. Why do you think they needed reminding?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Their parents forgot, and forgetting led to the disaster last week. Remembering is how God's people stay trusting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 sets out the choice Moses put in front of Israel: blessing or curse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: The choice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Deuteronomy 4; 6:4–9; 11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessing or curse — Israel's choice</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is faithful to his promises and will give his people what he promised — but he wants his people to trust and obey him, and he never gives up on them even when they fail.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Moses called Israel to love God with all their heart, soul and strength.</li><li>We are learning that God set blessings and curses before Israel depending on their obedience.</li><li>I can explain why God asked to be worshipped alone.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state both sides of the choice, and why worshipping God alone matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read together: The choice — read together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the built-in dictation as a fullscreen reader and read it together as a whole class, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block to open the fullscreen reader.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Moses' reminder</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Summarise Moses' reminder of the rescue, the disobedience, and the call to love God completely.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Blessing or curse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set out both sides of the choice clearly — rain, crops and peace, or curse and exile.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the one thing God most wanted his people not to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He did not want them making idols and worshipping them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Worship him alone</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out what God did want, then push on the difference between alone and most.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God said worship me alone, rather than worship me most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God is the only true God and had rescued them himself. Sharing him with idols treats a rescuer like an option.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 asks what God would do if his people broke the agreement — again.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: God never gives up":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Deuteronomy 4:25–31</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's faithfulness when his people fail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is faithful to his promises and will give his people what he promised — but he wants his people to trust and obey him, and he never gives up on them even when they fail.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Israel agreed to at Mount Sinai.</li><li>We are learning that God promised to forgive his people and take them back if they turned to him.</li><li>I can explain that God's faithfulness does not depend on his people's performance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can connect God's promise to take them back with the memory verse about his faithfulness never ending.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read together: God's mercy — read together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the built-in dictation as a fullscreen reader and read it together as a whole class, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block to open the fullscreen reader.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The agreement at Sinai</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap the rescue, the Law and the agreement, then ask what Israel agreed to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Israel agree to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. To worship God alone and obey everything he commanded.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>They will break it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the class to predict what happens to the agreement, then confirm that Moses said the same thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Knowing what you know about them by now — what do you think would happen to that agreement?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They would break it. Moses told them they would — God was not caught by surprise.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>He will never give up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give God's promise to forgive and take them back, then ask what that shows about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it tell us about God that he promised to take them back, even after they had broken their promise to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Guide toward: God's love doesn't depend on performance. He is faithful even when they are not.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together and point out how directly it fits today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 hears Jesus make a promise about a place, just like God promised Israel a land.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: A place prepared":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 14:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus' promise of a place</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is faithful to his promises and will give his people what he promised — but he wants his people to trust and obey him, and he never gives up on them even when they fail.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God saves people today through Jesus' death in their place.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus promised to prepare a place for his people and to come back for them.</li><li>I can name something people might love more than God, and say how someone shows they love God most.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state Jesus' promise in their own words and connect it to God's promise of land to Israel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Saved to belong</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Connect God choosing Israel with God saving people today into his family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How does God save people today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. By sending Jesus to die on the cross in their place so they can be forgiven and have eternal life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jesus' promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read John 14:1–3, set the scene, then draw out the promise.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the John 14 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Jesus tell his disciples to do, and what promise were they to trust?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. To have faith in God and in him; that he was preparing a place and would come back for them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Loving God most</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask both questions about competing loves and how love for God shows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What things might someone be tempted to love more than God today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Note that most are good things — the problem is putting them ahead of God, the same idol problem Israel faced.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week Joshua takes over, and God finally brings his people into the land.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for what we learned today about how Israel had a choice to obey you or to ignore you. Thank you that the Bible teaches us that you invite people today to make a choice, and that you want your people to trust and obey you. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 9: The land":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God brought his people into the land, exactly as he had promised — and his promises to Abraham were always pointing to something bigger than a piece of land: Jesus, and living with God forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Be strong and brave":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:6–9; Joshua 2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua takes over; Rahab and the spies</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God brought his people into the land, exactly as he had promised — and his promises to Abraham were always pointing to something bigger than a piece of land: Jesus, and living with God forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God chose Joshua to lead his people into the land, and told him to be strong, brave and obedient.</li><li>We are learning that Rahab protected the spies because she had heard what God had done.</li><li>I can say which of God's promises to Abraham Israel was still waiting for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why Rahab feared and trusted Israel's God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen script: Rahab and the spies (5 speaking parts)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A new leader</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that Moses died before entering the land and God chose Joshua. Give God's charge from Joshua 1:6–9.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What exactly was Joshua told to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Be strong and brave; obey everything Moses taught; keep reading God's Law; don't be afraid, because God would be with him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Promises still outstanding</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Review God's three promises to Abraham and which ones remained.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which of those had God already kept, and which was Israel still waiting for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Great nation and blessing already evident; the land and blessing to the nations still outstanding.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rahab and the spies</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Assign parts and run the on-screen script.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen; readers follow the fullscreen presenter one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why Rahab believed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why an outsider in Jericho was so sure about Israel's God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab wasn't an Israelite. So why was she so certain that God had given Israel the land?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. She had heard about the Red Sea, the desert and the defeated kings. Jericho was terrified.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 crosses the Jordan River on dry ground.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Crossing the Jordan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God stops the Jordan River</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God brought his people into the land, exactly as he had promised — and his promises to Abraham were always pointing to something bigger than a piece of land: Jesus, and living with God forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God stopped the Jordan River so his people could cross on dry ground.</li><li>We are learning that God did this so the people would know he was with Joshua as he had been with Moses.</li><li>I can compare the Jordan crossing with the Red Sea rescue.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain God's stated reason for the miracle, not just describe it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen script: Crossing the Jordan (4 speaking parts)</li><li>Comic panel: Joshua crossing the Jordan</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The river in the way</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Report the spies' news and set up the problem of the Jordan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Crossing the Jordan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Assign parts and run the on-screen script, then display the comic panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen; then show the crossing image block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where have we seen this?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the class to connect this to the Red Sea.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where had God's people seen water stop like this before?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. At the Red Sea — though that was their parents' and grandparents' generation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why this way?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out God's own stated reason for the miracle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God did it this way, with the priests stepping in first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. So the people would know God was with Joshua as with Moses, and would obey him. Stepping into a flowing river first took trust.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 marches around Jericho.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: The walls fall":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The battle of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God brought his people into the land, exactly as he had promised — and his promises to Abraham were always pointing to something bigger than a piece of land: Jesus, and living with God forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God gave Israel Jericho after they obeyed his unusual instructions.</li><li>We are learning that Rahab and her family were rescued when the city fell.</li><li>I can explain why God's plan made the victory obviously his.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can say why the marching plan mattered, and connect obedience here with the refusal in Week 7.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen script: Battle of Jericho (3 speaking parts)</li><li>Comic panel: the walls of Jericho fall</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A locked city</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the scene — Jericho locked up tight and terrified.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Battle of Jericho</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Assign parts and run the on-screen script, then display the comic panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen; then show the walls image block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why march?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask why God chose such a strange plan, and link back to Week 7.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Marching quietly around a city for a week is a very strange way to win a battle. Why do you think God told them to do it that way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Nobody could claim the army did it — the victory was obviously God's. Obeying an odd-sounding instruction was itself trust, exactly what their parents refused.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Rahab rescued</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Note that Rahab and her family were protected, and ask what that shows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab helped the spies and was rescued when the city fell. What does it tell us that God rescued someone from Jericho?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God's rescue was never only for people born Israelite. Rahab heard, believed and acted — and was saved.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks what God's promise to Abraham was really about all along.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: What the promise was always about":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:8–10; Joshua 24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The promise was always about Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God brought his people into the land, exactly as he had promised — and his promises to Abraham were always pointing to something bigger than a piece of land: Jesus, and living with God forever.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Israel did not keep their promise to worship God alone and eventually lost the land.</li><li>We are learning that Abraham was waiting for an eternal city built by God.</li><li>I can explain that God's promises to Abraham were always pointing to Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can answer the did-God-break-his-promise question using Hebrews 11.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The promise they couldn't keep</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe Israel's renewed promise at the end of Joshua and what actually happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do you think the Israelites managed to keep that promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. No — they worshipped idols and eventually lost the land, exactly as God had warned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Did God break his promise?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pose the harder question and let it sit before answering.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So if God's people ended up not living in the land — does that mean God didn't keep his promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Don't resolve it yet; Hebrews 11 gives the answer next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Abraham was waiting for</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Hebrews 11:9–10 and draw out that Abraham was in the land and still waiting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Hebrews 11 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was Abraham waiting for, and where is it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. An eternal city planned and built by God — heaven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Always about Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Land the point: the promise of Canaan was pointing to heaven, and to Jesus, and it is for people today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Psalm 117:2 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week is the last of the term: the old agreement and the new one God made in Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“His love for us is wonderful, his faithfulness never ends. Shout praises to the Lord!” — <em>Psalm 117:2 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you kept your promise to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and brought your people into the land of Canaan. Thank you that your promises to Abraham are for people today too. Thank you that you sent Jesus to die for people's sins so that they can have eternal life in heaven. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 10: The new covenant":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jeremiah 31:31–34; Hebrews 10:1; Luke 24:27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement God made through Moses was only ever a shadow pointing forward. In Jesus, God made a new agreement — his people are forgiven, and his Spirit lives in them so they can obey him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: They made it — and there was still a problem":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jeremiah 31:31–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The problem with the old agreement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement God made through Moses was only ever a shadow pointing forward. In Jesus, God made a new agreement — his people are forgiven, and his Spirit lives in them so they can obey him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God kept his promise and brought his people into the land.</li><li>We are learning that the old agreement failed because God's people broke it, not because God did.</li><li>I can begin learning this week's new memory verse, Luke 24:27.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state clearly whose side of the agreement failed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic panel: the Happy Promised Land</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>They made it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap last week's arrival in the land and display the comic panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show the Promised Land image block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Still a problem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce the problem and ask which side of the agreement kept failing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The agreement God made at Mount Sinai had two sides — God's side and the people's side. Which side kept failing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The people's side, every time. God kept his promises; his people broke theirs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>God promises something new</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give Jeremiah 31:31–32 — God will make a new agreement, not like the old one they broke.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this term's final memory verse, Luke 24:27, and say it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 lines the old agreement up beside the new one, side by side.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Old agreement, new agreement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:5b–7; Exodus 3:7–10; 19:1–6; 25:8–9; John 1:29; Hebrews 12:18–25; 1 Corinthians 3:16; 1 Peter 2:9; Revelation 21:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old and new agreements side by side</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement God made through Moses was only ever a shadow pointing forward. In Jesus, God made a new agreement — his people are forgiven, and his Spirit lives in them so they can obey him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how each part of the old agreement matches a part of the new agreement.</li><li>We are learning that in the new agreement God's people come from every nation.</li><li>I can name one thing that is the same and one thing that is different between the two agreements.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can pair at least two old-agreement items with their new-agreement counterparts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen whole-class reading: the old agreement and the new</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where we've been this term</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Trace the term quickly: Egypt, Red Sea, Sinai, tabernacle, Promised Land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read it together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5–6 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the whole-class reading on screen, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen. The class reads each line together off the display.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Same and different</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief the comparison with both questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you notice, comparing the two? What is the same, and what is different?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Same: God rescues, chooses, makes an agreement, comes to live with his people. Different: a bigger rescue, a wider people, and God living inside rather than beside them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Luke 24:27 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 asks why God gave the old agreement at all, if it was never going to work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: A shadow of the good things to come":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 10:1; John 1:29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement as a shadow</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement God made through Moses was only ever a shadow pointing forward. In Jesus, God made a new agreement — his people are forgiven, and his Spirit lives in them so they can obey him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Law of Moses is described as a shadow of the good things to come.</li><li>We are learning that the Law could not free people from sin.</li><li>I can explain what the shadow was pointing to.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can use the shadow picture to explain what the old agreement could and could not do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why give it at all?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pose the question, then introduce Hebrews 10:1 and the shadow picture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Picture a shadow stretched out on the ground in front of you. What does a shadow tell you — and what can't it do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. It tells you someone is really there, but it isn't the person. It only points to the real thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What the Law couldn't do</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Apply the picture to the old agreement and the daily sacrifices.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What can't the Law of Moses do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. It can't free people from sin. It showed what sin was but never took it away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pointing to Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name what the shadow pointed to — Jesus, the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God gave his people a shadow first, instead of just sending Jesus straight away?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The shadow taught them how holy God is, how serious sin is, and how badly they needed a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Luke 24:27 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 finishes the term with what the new agreement actually changes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Written on hearts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jeremiah 31:33–34; Luke 24:27; 2 Corinthians 5:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What the new agreement changes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The old agreement God made through Moses was only ever a shadow pointing forward. In Jesus, God made a new agreement — his people are forgiven, and his Spirit lives in them so they can obey him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that in the new agreement God writes his law on people's hearts and minds.</li><li>We are learning that God forgives his people because Jesus died in their place, and his Spirit then lives in them.</li><li>I can explain that everything we studied this term points to Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name the two things the new agreement gives — forgiveness, and God's Spirit living in his people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A law written on hearts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give Jeremiah 31:33–34 and ask how it fixes the old problem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How would that fix the problem the old agreement had?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The old agreement was written outside people, on stone; the new one is written inside, so God's people can actually obey.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Forgiveness, and the Spirit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work through both questions — what God did so he could forgive, and where God lives now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did God do so that he could forgive his people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He sent Jesus to die on the cross in the place of sinners.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A new person</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give 2 Corinthians 5:17 — anyone who belongs to Christ is a new person.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The whole term in one verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the Luke 24 scene and ask the closing review question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Luke 24:27 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think back over this whole term — Egypt, the Red Sea, the Law at Sinai, the tabernacle, the Promised Land. What was all of it pointing to?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. All of it points to Jesus — every part is a shadow of something Jesus brings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the term in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">That's the end of Term 3. Next term the students become journalists in training, starting a new book of the curriculum.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you are faithful to your promises. Thank you that your old agreement with your people points to your new and better agreement. Thank you that Jesus' death on the cross means that people today can be forgiven and your Spirit can live in them. Please help your people to put their trust in Jesus and worship and obey you only. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: The gospel: then and now":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:1–17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept his promises to Abraham in Jesus. That is the good news — the gospel — and Paul was so certain of it that nothing could stop him talking about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Part one and the sequel":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–3 (review); Romans 1:1–2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Part one was always pointing to the sequel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept his promises to Abraham in Jesus. That is the good news — the gospel — and Paul was so certain of it that nothing could stop him talking about it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall God's three promises to Abraham and how we saw him keep them.</li><li>We are learning that God's promises to Abraham were always pointing forward to Jesus.</li><li>I can begin learning this term's memory verse, Ephesians 2:8a.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name the promises to Abraham and say who they were pointing to.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Guess that sequel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the sequel game quickly, then explain what a sequel is and make the link: two terms of part one, and this term is the sequel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Add any current movies your class will know.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What we already know</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Review the promises to Abraham and how God kept them across Terms 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Over the last two terms, did we discover that God was a promise-keeper or a promise-breaker? What did we actually see him do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. A promise-keeper — Isaac's birth, a great nation, the rescue from Egypt, entering the land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The problem that remained</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how God's people struggled to obey, and recap the sin / consequence / grace cycle from Term 2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Always about Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Land the point: God's plan to fix sin started with Abraham but was always about Jesus. That is the gospel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce this term's memory verse, Ephesians 2:8a, and say it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 meets Paul and opens his letter to the Christians in Rome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: A letter to Rome":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Paul, his letter, and what the gospel is</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept his promises to Abraham in Jesus. That is the good news — the gospel — and Paul was so certain of it that nothing could stop him talking about it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Paul was and why he wrote letters.</li><li>We are learning that the word gospel means good news, and that the good news is about Jesus.</li><li>I can say that the gospel is for people of every nation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can define gospel and say who it is about and who it is for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: a real newspaper, to hold up while reading out headlines</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Trainee journalists</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set up the term's job: write a newspaper article outlining the good news about Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have newspapers, hold them up and read out a couple of headlines.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you had some really important news, how would you tell people? And what if it was news you wanted the whole world to know?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Face to face, a message, a post; for the whole world, TV, internet or a newspaper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Paul spread the news</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Paul and the problem of spreading news without modern technology.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">But back then there was no internet, no phones, no television and no newspapers. So how do you think Paul got his message to so many people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He travelled around preaching, and he wrote lots of letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Opening the letter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read or summarise Romans 1:1–7. Draw out who Paul is, what an apostle is, and what the gospel is about.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Bibles, read Romans 1:1–7 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The word gospel simply means good news. From the very start of Paul's letter — what is the good news actually about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jesus Christ, God and man, descended from David, shown to be God's Son by his resurrection — and promised long ago in the Scriptures.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who it's for</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask who the good news is for and note that it was always meant for every nation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And who is the good news for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. People of all nations — everyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 finds out what it actually cost Paul to keep telling people this news.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: Not ashamed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">2 Corinthians 11:25b–28; Romans 1:16–17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Paul would not stop telling people</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept his promises to Abraham in Jesus. That is the good news — the gospel — and Paul was so certain of it that nothing could stop him talking about it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Paul suffered while telling people about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning that Paul was not ashamed of the gospel because it is God's power to save.</li><li>I can explain why Paul thought the good news was worth suffering for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can give Paul's own reason from Romans 1:16, not just a general answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at 2 Corinthians 11:25b–28 and Romans 1:16–17</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read together: Paul's résumé of suffering — read together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the built-in dictation as a fullscreen reader and read it together as a whole class, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block to open the fullscreen reader.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What happened to Paul</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read or retell 2 Corinthians 11:25b–28. Take it slowly — the list is the point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read straight from a CEV Bible if you can.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was life like for Paul as he spoke about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Enormous challenges and a great deal of suffering.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why keep going?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the class to guess before revealing Paul's own answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So why do you think Paul kept talking about Jesus, when he suffered so much as a result?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Let them think, then give Romans 1:16 — he was not ashamed, because the gospel is God's power to save.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 1:16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 1:16–17 and unpack why this made Paul unstoppable.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Romans 1:16 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did Paul go through all those really hard things?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because the gospel is God's powerful way of saving people, and he wanted everyone to know Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks each student to put the good news into their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: The gospel is ...":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:16–17 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Putting the gospel into your own words</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God kept his promises to Abraham in Jesus. That is the good news — the gospel — and Paul was so certain of it that nothing could stop him talking about it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain the gospel in our own words.</li><li>We are learning that the gospel is God's power to save everyone who trusts in Jesus.</li><li>I can connect the memory verse to the idea that salvation cannot be earned.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Collect or listen to students' written definitions. Keep them — they are a useful before-and-after for the end of term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper or Activity books and pencils for the written definition</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read together: The gospel in one paragraph — read together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the built-in dictation as a fullscreen reader and read it together as a whole class, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block to open the fullscreen reader.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why Paul is proud of it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Restate Romans 1:16 in plain terms and connect it to the journalist job.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The gospel is ...</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give two silent minutes for students to write their own definition, then invite a few to read out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Don't correct answers — keep them to compare with the end-of-term article.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who would like to read out what they wrote?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses warmly, without correcting. This is a starting point to return to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Paul's short version</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give the compact summary: the promise to Abraham, kept in Jesus, open to every nation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Much better than we deserve</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Point at the memory verse's wording and use it to set up the next two weeks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our memory verse says we are saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. What does much better than we deserve suggest about how much we could earn?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. That we couldn't earn it at all — which is where Paul goes next.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week Paul explains why anyone needs this good news in the first place.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you always had a plan to deal with people's sin. Thank you for the promises you made to Abraham and for keeping them in Jesus. Thank you that Paul wasn't discouraged when telling others about Jesus, even when he suffered because of it. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 2: The need for the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:18—3:26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every single person has sinned and ignored God, and God's standard is perfection — so nobody measures up. But because of God's great love, he sent Jesus to take the consequences we deserve.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Consequences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:18—2:1 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wrong actions and consequences</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every single person has sinned and ignored God, and God's standard is perfection — so nobody measures up. But because of God's great love, he sent Jesus to take the consequences we deserve.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that wrong actions have consequences, and that justice is a good thing.</li><li>We are learning that people tend to sort wrongs into serious and not-so-serious.</li><li>I can explain why it would feel wrong for people to get away with doing wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can articulate why justice matters to them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A whiteboard or butcher's paper, divided into Consequences and No consequences</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Set up the board</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw a line down the middle of the board — Consequences on one side, No consequences on the other. Use the cheating-on-a-test example.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Sort the actions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the list one item at a time and add each to the side the class chooses. Encourage disagreement and discussion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep this board up — you'll add to it on Day 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Justice is a good thing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how it would feel if nobody faced consequences, then name the desire for justice as a good instinct.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How would you feel if the people who did the things on the Consequences side didn't get in trouble at all?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Angry or outraged; a strong desire for justice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Serious and not-so-serious</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Point back at the borderline items and flag where Paul is heading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look back at the list. Was it always obvious which side something belonged on?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The small ones are the interesting ones — we grade wrongs, and Paul argues God doesn't.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 is our first media briefing of the term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Media briefing: guilty":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 1:29–30; Romans 2:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Guilt, hypocrisy and one standard for everyone</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every single person has sinned and ignored God, and God's standard is perfection — so nobody measures up. But because of God's great love, he sent Jesus to take the consequences we deserve.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a hypocrite is and why hypocrisy provokes such a strong reaction.</li><li>We are learning that God has the same standard for everyone.</li><li>I can explain Romans 2:1 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can say what a hypocrite is and why God having one standard matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 12 Media releases 1 and 2</li><li>The Consequences board from Day 1</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain what a media release is, then run the on-screen briefing. Remind the class these stories are fictional.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen. The class reads each line together off the display.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The first case</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief release 1 quickly — this one is meant to feel obvious.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Alfred Woodcock attacked his neighbour and was found guilty. Why do you think he deserves consequences for what he did?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He deliberately and seriously hurt someone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Paul's list</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read out Paul's list from Romans 1:29–30 and add a few of those words to the Consequences side of the board.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Add points from the passage to the board.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hypocrites</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief release 2, define hypocrite and double standards, then give Romans 2:1 and God's single standard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Add the word Hypocrites to the Consequences side of the board.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now the second release. How do you feel about Jean-Pierre Moreau — and what do you think should happen to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Draw out that he broke the very rule he told others to keep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 asks what God's one standard actually is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: God's standard":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 3:23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Everyone falls short of God's standard</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every single person has sinned and ignored God, and God's standard is perfection — so nobody measures up. But because of God's great love, he sent Jesus to take the consequences we deserve.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's standard is perfection.</li><li>We are learning that all have sinned and fallen short, so everyone belongs on the Consequences side.</li><li>I can explain Romans 3:23 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students include themselves when they describe who has fallen short.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Consequences board from Day 1</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What is the standard?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take guesses before revealing that the standard is perfection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think God's standard is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Take guesses first — students often aim at being good, or better than average.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 3:23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give Romans 3:23 and run the hands-up moment.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept hands only — there almost certainly won't be any.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So how do people measure up against God's standard?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. All have sinned and fallen short; nobody reaches it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Eighty-seven thousand, six hundred</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Walk through the three-wrongs-a-day calculation out loud. Let the number land.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">3 × 365 × 80 = 87,600. Ignore leap years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Everyone</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Add everyone to the Consequences side and ask the closing question honestly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Don't resolve the discomfort today — tomorrow finishes the sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On Monday we said justice is a good thing and people who do wrong should face consequences. How does that feel now that we're on the list too?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This is meant to be uncomfortable. Reassure them Paul's sentence does not stop here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together — and note that it is about to make a lot more sense.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 finishes Paul's sentence — and it changes everything.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: But the verse doesn't finish there":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 3:24–25a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God's grace in Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every single person has sinned and ignored God, and God's standard is perfection — so nobody measures up. But because of God's great love, he sent Jesus to take the consequences we deserve.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God freely accepts his people because of Jesus.</li><li>We are learning that no-one can be good enough for heaven on their own.</li><li>I can explain what grace means using the memory verse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state both halves of Romans 3:23–24 — the problem and God's answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 3:24–25a</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Finishing the sentence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap where you stopped yesterday, then read Romans 3:24–25a, finishing at so that by faith in him we could come to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what does God do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He treats his people much better than they deserve; he shows mercy and grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How God dealt with sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that God dealt with sin himself — Jesus lived a perfect life and took the consequences on the cross.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And how did God deal with sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He sent Jesus to die on the cross in the place of sinners.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nobody deserves it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work through both Connections questions about who deserves heaven and whether anyone can be good enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can anyone ever be good enough to get to heaven?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. No — they would need to be perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How does this feel?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the honest reaction question and receive the answers without correcting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you feel about being offered forgiveness you couldn't earn?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Make sure students understand there is no right or wrong answer here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: if it can't be earned, what is it? Paul explains the difference between wages and a gift.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for teaching us that people who are in your family are sorry for ignoring you and for living their own way. Thank you for sending Jesus who brings forgiveness; he died on the cross and rose again. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 3: The good news of the gospel!":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 3:21—5:21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody deserves eternal life — it is God's gift, given because Jesus took the consequence in our place. And like any gift, it has to be accepted, which is exactly what Abraham did when he simply trusted God's promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 13</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Wages and gifts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 4:4–5 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The difference between wages and a gift</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody deserves eternal life — it is God's gift, given because Jesus took the consequence in our place. And like any gift, it has to be accepted, which is exactly what Abraham did when he simply trusted God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the difference between wages and a gift.</li><li>We are learning that a gift is undeserved and free, while a wage is earned and owed.</li><li>I can explain why anything we get from God has to be a gift.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students use the words earned and deserved accurately when describing the two prizes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two small identical prizes for the Way in demonstration</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two prizes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the two-volunteer demonstration. Make the first student work for it and hand the second theirs for nothing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick two students who will take it in good humour, and make sure both actually receive a prize.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who knows what wages are?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Wages are the payment we get for working — we earn them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Earned or given?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief the demonstration with the earned / given / deserved questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And which one deserved their prize?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The first one. The second didn't deserve it — that's what makes it a gift.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gifts and wages in Romans</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain what Paul is doing with this distinction, and connect it back to last week's conclusion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together and point out the words much better than we deserve.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 is a media briefing about a trial with a very strange verdict.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Media briefing: the pardon":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 3:23–25a</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Guilty, yet declared innocent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody deserves eternal life — it is God's gift, given because Jesus took the consequence in our place. And like any gift, it has to be accepted, which is exactly what Abraham did when he simply trusted God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people are guilty but are declared innocent.</li><li>We are learning that this is possible because Jesus took the consequence in their place.</li><li>I can explain the Stanford story and say what it pictures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can map the story onto the gospel — who is guilty, who takes the consequence, who is declared innocent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 13 Media releases 1 and 2</li><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 3:23–25a</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the on-screen briefing. Remind the class the stories are fictional. Let release 1 sit for a beat before release 2 explains it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A shocking verdict</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief: he was guilty, he should have been punished, he was declared not guilty instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what should have happened to him, and what happened instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He should have faced a consequence for treason; instead he was declared not guilty.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The Prime Minister's choice</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw attention to the fact that Stanford was the Prime Minister's own enemy, and ask for honest reactions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think of that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Some will call it unjust, others generous — both are the reactions people have to the cross.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What God has done</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 3:23–25a and map the story onto it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is it possible for God to freely accept people as his friends when they are guilty sinners?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because Jesus sets his people free from sin — he took the consequence they deserved.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 has a third media release — and forty million dollars.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: The forty million dollar gift":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6:23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Eternal life is a gift that has to be accepted</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody deserves eternal life — it is God's gift, given because Jesus took the consequence in our place. And like any gift, it has to be accepted, which is exactly what Abraham did when he simply trusted God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that eternal life is a free gift, not wages.</li><li>We are learning that a gift still has to be accepted by the person receiving it.</li><li>I can explain Romans 6:23 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain why a gift needs accepting without turning acceptance into something earned.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 13 Media release 3</li><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 6:23</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the on-screen briefing. Remind the class the story is fictional.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Earned or given</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief both characters and press for a clear definition of the difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Link back to Monday's two prizes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There's a huge difference between a gift and a wage. In your own words — what is it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. A wage is earned, deserved and owed; a gift is given, undeserved and free.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 6:23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 6:23 and draw out both halves of the verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Romans 6:23 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does Paul say sin leads to — and what is God's gift to his people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Sin leads to death; God's gift is eternal life in Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Saying yes to a gift</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the no-thanks-I-don't-want-it scenario, then ask how someone accepts God's gift.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Don't fully resolve the last question — tomorrow answers it with Abraham.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So how do you think someone accepts God's gift of eternal life?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The way to accept it is to have faith in Jesus — to trust him, be sorry for living your own way, and want to live God's way.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 goes back to Abraham to see what accepting God's gift actually looks like.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Just like Abraham":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 4:3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Abraham was declared innocent because he trusted God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody deserves eternal life — it is God's gift, given because Jesus took the consequence in our place. And like any gift, it has to be accepted, which is exactly what Abraham did when he simply trusted God's promises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham was declared innocent because he trusted God's promises.</li><li>We are learning that people today are accepted the same way Abraham was.</li><li>I can say what it looks like to accept God's gift of eternal life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students describe accepting the gift as trusting, rather than as doing something to earn it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A whiteboard for the Abraham mind map</li><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 4:3</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Abraham mind map</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write Abraham in a bubble in the middle of the board and collect everything the class remembers around it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Two terms of material — let them fill the board.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you remember about Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Land, a great nation, blessing to the world; leaving home; old age; Isaac's birth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How Abraham responded</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Focus in on the promise of a son and how Abraham responded to something that looked impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Abraham respond to God's promise of a son, remembering that he and Sarah were both very, very old?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He trusted and obeyed God, and chose to live God's way.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 4:3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 4:3 and make the point that Abraham simply believed — he did not earn it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who did God's promises to Abraham point to?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The same way today</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Apply it: nobody can earn heaven, but anyone can trust God's promises fulfilled in Jesus and be declared innocent.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what does the Bible say about how to accept God's gift of eternal life?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. By following Abraham's example — trusting God's promises, fulfilled in Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8a together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is as far as Term 4 is built. Lesson 14 continues Romans, but its manual pages have not been scanned yet — see the file header.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8a (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that we learned about guilt and innocence today. Thank you that Abraham is a good example of what it looks like to trust you. Help your people to always trust and follow Jesus. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 4: The fruit of the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People are declared innocent by trusting in Jesus — and once that happens, their old life and their new life simply don't go together. Christians don't do good things in order to be saved; they do good things because they already are.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Coke and Pepsi":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6:1–2 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Old life and new life don't mix</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People are declared innocent by trusting in Jesus — and once that happens, their old life and their new life simply don't go together. Christians don't do good things in order to be saved; they do good things because they already are.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that a Christian's old life and new life do not go together.</li><li>We are learning that being declared innocent by God changes how a person lives.</li><li>I can learn the extended memory verse, Ephesians 2:8–10.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can explain the rival-companies picture and say what it stands for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: a can of Coke and a can of Pepsi to display and swap over</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Marketing managers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Split the room, set the task, and let both sides share a few ideas.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display the two cans if you have them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">As the marketing manager for Coke or Pepsi, what is something you would never do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They would never promote their opposition's product.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Swap companies</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Swap the sides over and ask what they'd be doing now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Swap the cans over if they're displayed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you be doing now — and would you still be promoting the product from your first job?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Promoting the other product — and no way would they still promote the first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Paul is saying</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make the link: if God has declared you innocent, your old life and new life don't go together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The verse grows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that the memory verse now runs to Ephesians 2:10, and say the whole thing together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 checks back in on Robert Stanford, a month after he walked free.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: One month later":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6:6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A changed life after being declared innocent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People are declared innocent by trusting in Jesus — and once that happens, their old life and their new life simply don't go together. Christians don't do good things in order to be saved; they do good things because they already are.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that we would expect a pardoned person's life to change.</li><li>We are learning that Paul says a Christian's old life was nailed to the cross with Christ.</li><li>I can explain why going back to the old life would be unthinkable.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students connect the Stanford story to Romans 6:6–7 rather than treating it as just a story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper and pencils for the paired interview</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: Interview with Robert Stanford, one month on</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Journalist, Robert Stanford, and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap the case</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring back the Stanford story with the two recall questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What should have happened to Robert Stanford — and what happened instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Prison; instead, declared innocent because the Prime Minister took his consequence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Interview Robert</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the paired interview task, roam to check students are on task, then hear a few.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Allow about five minutes, then lead a short discussion on what came up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you expect Robert Stanford's life to be different once he was declared innocent and released? How?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Yes — he would stop inciting rebellion, and might even support the Prime Minister.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Back to the old life?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask how it would feel if he reoffended, and name why it would be unthinkable.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How would you feel if, once Robert was released, he went straight back to his old way of life, causing riots and committing crimes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Unthinkable — he'd been given a second chance. His old life doesn't fit his new freedom.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 6:6–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 6:6–7 and draw out what Paul says about the old life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does Paul say about their old lives?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Completely transformed by Jesus' death — from controlled by sin to declared innocent.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 asks what it means to be a slave to sin, and what Paul tells God's people to do instead.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: Slaves to sin, free in Christ":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6:8–10; 6:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Free from sin, given to God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People are declared innocent by trusting in Jesus — and once that happens, their old life and their new life simply don't go together. Christians don't do good things in order to be saved; they do good things because they already are.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what it means to be a slave to sin.</li><li>We are learning that death no longer has power over Jesus, and that his people are alive for God.</li><li>I can say what Paul tells God's people to do and not to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can define slavery to sin in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 6:8–10 and 6:12–14</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read together: Old life, new life — read together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open the built-in dictation as a fullscreen reader and read it together as a whole class, one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in dictation block to open the fullscreen reader.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Slaves to sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap Romans 6:6–7 briefly, then unpack the slavery picture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it means to be a slave to sin?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Being controlled by someone else; not free. Sin controls them so they can't help sinning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 6:8–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 6:8–10, noting that Christ refers to Jesus, and draw out both questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What no longer has power over Christ?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Death no longer has power over Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 6:12–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 6:12–14 and work through the not-to-do and to-do questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does Paul tell God's people to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Give themselves to pleasing God — living the way God wants his people to live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 gets the order right: good things come after being saved, not before.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Because, not in order to":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 6:23; Ephesians 2:8–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Good works follow salvation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People are declared innocent by trusting in Jesus — and once that happens, their old life and their new life simply don't go together. Christians don't do good things in order to be saved; they do good things because they already are.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people are not saved by the good things they do.</li><li>We are learning that Christians do good things because they have already been saved.</li><li>I can name things that belong to a Christian's old life and new life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen carefully for the order in students' answers — because saved, not in order to be saved.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How they live matters</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Recap that Jesus took the penalty, and that forgiven people's lives now matter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does the Bible say someone has to do to be declared innocent and have eternal life?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Accept God's gift by trusting in Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Getting the order right</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Press on the key distinction — because saved, not in order to be saved.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do God's people do good things in order to get saved, or because they have already been saved?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because they are already saved. Getting it backwards turns the gift into wages.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Old life, new life</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect the two lists out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this matches the two people outlines.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The verse says it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the last part of Ephesians 2:10 and point out where the good things sit in the sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close in prayer and flag the Spirit for next week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: how the goal of heaven changes the way Christians live now.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that Jesus died on the cross so that people who trust in him can be declared innocent. Please help your people to stop living their own way and to please you with how they live. Thank you that you have given your Spirit to help your followers. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 5: The goal of the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Knowing you have been declared innocent and are heading for heaven changes the way you live right now — and God gives his people his Holy Spirit so they can say no to sin and yes to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 15</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Goals change today":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:1–13 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A future goal shapes the present</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Knowing you have been declared innocent and are heading for heaven changes the way you live right now — and God gives his people his Holy Spirit so they can say no to sin and yes to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that a future goal changes the way a person lives now.</li><li>We are learning that Paul says a Christian's future should shape their present.</li><li>I can describe a goal I have had and how it changed what I did.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name a concrete change they made because of a goal.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Corey's carnival</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell Corey's story — the training, the goal, the trophy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Your goals</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect students' own goals and, importantly, what they changed to reach them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Push past the goal itself to what they actually did differently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did you achieve your goal? And how did you change the way you did things in order to reach it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Look for the pattern: the goal changed what they did in the meantime.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Paul's point</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make the link: Paul says a Christian's future goal should shape how they live now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 is a media briefing from the Olympic marathon.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Media briefing: the marathon":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:11; Romans 8:2–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Christian's goal is secure</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Knowing you have been declared innocent and are heading for heaven changes the way you live right now — and God gives his people his Holy Spirit so they can say no to sin and yes to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that those who belong to Jesus will be raised to life.</li><li>We are learning that a Christian's confidence rests on Jesus' death, not their own effort.</li><li>I can explain where the Stavros picture fits and where it breaks down.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students say the confidence comes from Jesus rather than from good behaviour.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 15 Media release</li><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 8:11 and 8:2–4</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the on-screen briefing. Remind the class the story is fictional.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ten minutes ahead of the silver medallist. What does that tell you about Stavros Massoud?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. An unheard-of margin — he was the best in the field and was always going to win.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 8:11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 8:11 and draw out the future and the goal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what is the goal of those who belong to Jesus? Where are they heading?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Heaven — raised bodies, living with God forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>A secure goal</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Stavros comparison for certainty, then read Romans 8:2–4 for the reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why can those who follow Jesus be confident?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because Jesus, the perfect man, died in the place of sinners.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Where the picture breaks</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be explicit that unlike Stavros, heaven doesn't come from hard work.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is it because of the good things people do that they can be confident of their place in heaven?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. No — only because Jesus died and invites people to receive forgiveness.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 looks at how what people think about reveals what their goal really is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: What you think about shows your goal":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:5–6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking reveals the goal</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Knowing you have been declared innocent and are heading for heaven changes the way you live right now — and God gives his people his Holy Spirit so they can say no to sin and yes to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that what people think about reveals what their goal is.</li><li>We are learning that being ruled by the Spirit leads to life and peace.</li><li>I can contrast a person ruled by desires with a person ruled by the Spirit.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can give concrete examples for both kinds of thinking.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 8:5–6</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 8:5–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 8:5–6 and set up the two contrasting people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this matches the two-halves timeline with thought bubbles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two thought bubbles</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4–5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect examples for both people. Concrete answers matter more than tidy ones.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does the person ruled by the Spirit think about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Spiritual things — loving others, kindness, generosity, putting others first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Life and peace</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name the end result from verse 6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is the end result for someone whose goal is to please God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Life and peace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>If Stavros stopped training</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the sleep-in-and-pizza scenario and land the point about goals shaping behaviour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think might have happened if Stavros Massoud had thought like this: I'm going to win for sure, so I won't train today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He may have been the favourite, but without training and focus he would have lost.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 is about saying no — and who helps you do it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Saying no, saying yes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:13b</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Saying no to sin with the Spirit's help</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Knowing you have been declared innocent and are heading for heaven changes the way you live right now — and God gives his people his Holy Spirit so they can say no to sin and yes to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people say no to sinful desires with the Spirit's help.</li><li>We are learning that obeying God does not earn a place in heaven.</li><li>I can name a sinful choice and the alternative that would please God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students include the Spirit's help rather than describing this as pure willpower.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 8:13b</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 8:13b</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read from But you will live... and draw out the instruction.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does Paul tell the Romans to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. With the Spirit's help they must say no to sinful desires.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Not to earn it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Guard the order again — Christians please God because he has already saved them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>No and yes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect paired answers — the sinful choice, then the alternative that pleases God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this matches the two-column table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what would be the choice in those same situations that would please God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Telling the truth, speaking kindly, obeying, being generous, being thankful.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Not on their own</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Acknowledge how hard this is, then make sure the Spirit's help is clearly stated.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did the Romans have to say no to their sinful desires using their own efforts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. No — God's Spirit lived in them and helped them make the right choices.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: what happens when living as a Christian gets genuinely hard.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for teaching us about what the Bible says about saying no to sinful things. You want your people to instead say yes to you. Help your people to do this. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 6: The hope of the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:14–39</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Living as a Christian can be genuinely hard — but God's people don't suffer alone, they have a hope that is certain rather than a wish, and nothing at all can separate them from God's love.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 16</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Worth suffering for":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:18 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What makes suffering worth it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Living as a Christian can be genuinely hard — but God's people don't suffer alone, they have a hope that is certain rather than a wish, and nothing at all can separate them from God's love.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that some goals are worth suffering for.</li><li>We are learning that Stavros kept going because of his coach and his goal.</li><li>I can say what Paul claims about the sufferings of this life compared with heaven.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students identify both the coach and the goal — those two threads carry the rest of the week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen script: Interview with Stavros Massoud (2 speaking parts)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Worth suffering for?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with both Way in questions before introducing the interview.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there anything you could imagine that would be worth suffering pain for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Students may recall the marathon runner from last week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The interview</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6–7 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose a Host and a Stavros and run the on-screen script. Teacher can take Stavros if the reading load is heavy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen; readers follow one line at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two things kept him going</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Debrief and pin the two threads: the coach, and the goal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write both on the board — you'll come back to them on Days 3 and 4.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Two things kept Stavros going when he wanted to give up. What were they?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. His coach by his side, and keeping his eyes fixed on the goal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 asks whose name a Christian bears, and whose glory they share.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Children of God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:14–17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bearing God's name and sharing Christ's glory</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Living as a Christian can be genuinely hard — but God's people don't suffer alone, they have a hope that is certain rather than a wish, and nothing at all can separate them from God's love.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that those led by God's Spirit are children of God.</li><li>We are learning what the benefits of being God's child are.</li><li>I can say what Christ's glory is and what it means to share in it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can define Christ's glory as his victory over sin and death, not just fame.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 8:14–17</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Whose name?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 8:14–17, then run the two name questions side by side.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this matches the Stavros / Jesus / Christians table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And whose name do Christians bear? What family have they become part of?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God's name, because they have become his children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Benefits of being God's child</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect the benefits — Father, confidence, inheritance, sharing Jesus' glory.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Those who have the Spirit living in them are children of God. What are the benefits of being a child of God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Calling God Father, confidence, the promised inheritance of heaven, and sharing Jesus' glory.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What is glory?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work down the column: Stavros' glory, then Christ's glory, then what Christians share.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what do you think Christ's glory is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. His victory over sin and death, seen in his resurrection; he reigns as Lord.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 is honest about how hard following Jesus can be.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: The path to glory":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 12:2; Romans 8:17b–18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Following Jesus can be hard</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Living as a Christian can be genuinely hard — but God's people don't suffer alone, they have a hope that is certain rather than a wish, and nothing at all can separate them from God's love.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus' path to glory was the cross.</li><li>We are learning that following Jesus can make life harder in real, specific ways.</li><li>I can explain Romans 8:18 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name specific ways following Jesus costs something, rather than staying general.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Hebrews 12:2 and Romans 8:18</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Three paths to glory</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Work across Stavros, Jesus and Christians — training and sacrifice, the cross, sharing Jesus' suffering.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Hebrews 12:2 for Jesus' path.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does Paul say is a Christian's path to glory?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Sharing in Jesus' suffering — Romans 8:17b.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What made it harder</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Start with Stavros' sacrifices, then move to what makes life harder for a Christian.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Don't rush this — specific answers are the useful ones.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some things that might make life harder for someone who follows Jesus than for those who don't?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Caring how they live, generosity, putting others first, not joining in, sometimes being teased.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 8:18</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be honest that it's hard, then give Romans 8:18 and compare with how Stavros felt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Romans 8:18 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Stavros feel about the pain and suffering he went through?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. He didn't complain — the glory of winning was better than anything else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4: what Christian hope actually means, and who helps while they wait.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: A hope you can be certain of":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 8:24–27; 8:26a; 8:38–39</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christian hope is certain, and nobody waits alone</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Living as a Christian can be genuinely hard — but God's people don't suffer alone, they have a hope that is certain rather than a wish, and nothing at all can separate them from God's love.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that biblical hope is certainty, not a wish.</li><li>We are learning that God gives his Spirit to help his people while they wait.</li><li>I can say that nothing in all creation can separate God's people from his love.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students distinguish hope-as-wish from hope-as-certainty.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 8:26a and 8:38–39</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What is hope?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take answers first, then draw the distinction between a wish and a certainty.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is something you hope for that you know will definitely happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Christmas, birthdays, school holidays — sure things, which is Paul's kind of hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Is the Christian hope sure?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare Stavros' certainty with the Christian's, and give the reasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is Christian hope sure? Can God's people be certain that they will go to heaven?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Yes — Jesus defeated sin and death and is alive now, and God always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Waiting, with a helper</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name the waiting, then read Romans 8:26a and make the coach comparison explicit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read just the first sentence, up to help us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stavros had his coach beside him while he waited. Who has God given his people as a helper?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God's Spirit, who is always with his people and will never leave them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Nothing can separate</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish with Romans 8:38–39. Read it slowly; let the list do the work.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the Romans 8:38–39 verse block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: what it means to be part of Christ's body, the Church.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that we could learn about heaven today. The Bible says that one day your people will be perfect and live with you forever. Thank you that you give Christians your Spirit to help them when life gets hard. Help your people today to live your way. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 7: The body of the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 12:1–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When people trust Jesus they become part of his body, the Church. God has made every person different and given them different gifts on purpose — and the body only works properly when every member uses their gifts to love and serve others.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 17</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Pass the ball":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 12:4–5 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A body works best when every part does its job</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When people trust Jesus they become part of his body, the Church. God has made every person different and given them different gifts on purpose — and the body only works properly when every member uses their gifts to love and serve others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that a body works best when every part does its job.</li><li>We are learning that God made our bodies with many different parts, each with a different job.</li><li>I can explain what Paul compares the Church to.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state the comparison — the Church is like a body.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A soft ball for the Way in game</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Pass the ball</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run all three rounds of the ball game, then bring everyone back to their seats.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give strict rules up front. Anyone disruptive sits down and misses out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was it easier to pass the ball when you could use your whole body?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Yes — the whole body working together is far more effective.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Name the parts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect body parts by category — bones, senses, visible, hidden.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>When a part doesn't work</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask what it's like when parts of the body aren't working properly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be sensitive here — some students may have a disability or a family member who does.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would it be like if some of our body parts weren't working properly?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Needing help with some tasks; other tasks taking longer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2's media briefing is about exactly this.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Media briefing: Ricky Hong":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 12:4–5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Many parts, one body in Christ</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When people trust Jesus they become part of his body, the Church. God has made every person different and given them different gifts on purpose — and the body only works properly when every member uses their gifts to love and serve others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that there are real consequences when a part of the body can't do its job.</li><li>We are learning that Christians are many parts of one body in Christ.</li><li>I can explain why a body needs different parts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can state Romans 12:4–5 in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 17 Media release</li><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 12:4–5</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the on-screen briefing. Remind the class the story is fictional.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What would change</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give both debrief questions room. Don't rush past the human weight of it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be sensitive — treat this seriously rather than as a puzzle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What kinds of things do you think would have to change for Ricky?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Getting around, working, reading, recognising people, needing others' help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 12:4–5</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Romans 12:4–5 and draw out the comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Paul say are similar about the Church and a body?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Many members, one body — so it is with the Church.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>All the same?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the giant-ball-of-eyes question — it makes the point stick.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think it would be like if all the parts of the body were the same?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. There would be no body — just a giant ball of eyes or arms.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3: God made every person different on purpose, and gave them different gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: Different on purpose":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 12:6–8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Different people, different gifts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When people trust Jesus they become part of his body, the Church. God has made every person different and given them different gifts on purpose — and the body only works properly when every member uses their gifts to love and serve others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God has made each person unique, by him and for him.</li><li>We are learning that God gives different gifts so the body can work together.</li><li>I can name a gift God has given me.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether every student can name at least one gift of their own without comparing it unfavourably to someone else's.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 12:6–8</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Made different</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe how God has made people different, then run the God has made me like this reflection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this matches the God has made me like this section.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Given different gifts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Move to skills and gifts, then run the God has given me these gifts reflection.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Watch for students who can't name anything — have a suggestion ready for each of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Romans 12:6–8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read the gift list and Paul's instruction to use them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So what does Paul tell us to do with the gifts God has given us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Use them to love and serve others — not to show off or to compare.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>If parts don't do their job</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run both consequence questions, linking back to Monday's game and Tuesday's briefing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what would it be like if some Christians didn't use their gifts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The body of Christ wouldn't work to its full capacity.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 asks what the Church actually is — and where you can use your gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: What the Church actually is":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 12:1–8 (review)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Church, and using your gifts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When people trust Jesus they become part of his body, the Church. God has made every person different and given them different gifts on purpose — and the body only works properly when every member uses their gifts to love and serve others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Church is people, not a building.</li><li>We are learning that every member of Christ's body needs to use their gifts to serve others.</li><li>I can name one way I could use my gifts to serve someone this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Collect or listen to students' one practical idea. Specific and small is better than grand and vague.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper or Activity books and pencils</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What is the Church?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take the building answer first, then give the Bible's definition — people meeting to read the Bible, encourage each other and pray.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So apart from a church building, where else can people meet to learn more about God from the Bible?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. This class is one; also lunchtime groups and kids clubs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Every member matters</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Restate Paul's point that the Church works well when every member uses their gifts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In what ways can a Christian use their gifts to serve others?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Encouraging, helping practically, being generous, including someone, praying.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>One thing this week</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give quiet time to write one practical idea, then hear a few.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Push for specific and doable rather than impressive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And where else could someone use their gifts to serve others?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Help them see they can serve right here at school and in this class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is as far as Term 4 is built. Lessons 18-20 are scanned and available — see the file header before starting Weeks 8-10.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that you have brought people into your family. These people trust and follow Jesus. Please help your people to use the gifts you have given them to love and serve other people. In Jesus' name we pray, Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 8: The love of the gospel":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 16:25–27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The good news about Jesus has God's power to establish and strengthen his people — like a seed planted in rich soil that grows, bears fruit, and drops new seeds — and God means it to be told to people of every nation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The growing plant":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 16:25–27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The plant picture, and the end of Paul's letter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The good news about Jesus has God's power to establish and strengthen his people — like a seed planted in rich soil that grows, bears fruit, and drops new seeds — and God means it to be told to people of every nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the stages a plant goes through as it grows.</li><li>We are learning that Paul praises God because God strengthens his people through the gospel.</li><li>I can say what Paul's good news is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name the message about Jesus as Paul's good news.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Romans 16:25–27</li><li>Optional: the YouTube time-lapse video of a growing plant</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How a plant grows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the question first, then walk through the four stages: seed in rich soil, roots and sprouts, flowers or fruit, then new life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have the time-lapse video of a growing plant, play it here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Picture a plant growing, right from the very start. What happens to a plant as it grows?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Let them describe it before you give the stages.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The end of the letter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that this is the last part of Romans, then read Romans 16:25–27.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why does Paul praise God at the end of his letter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because God can make his people strong by means of his good news.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What is the good news?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out that the good news is the message about Jesus Christ.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what is Paul's good news?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The message about Jesus Christ.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 plants people in the gospel and watches the roots grow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Planted in the gospel":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 16:25–26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Being planted in the gospel and growing strong</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The good news about Jesus has God's power to establish and strengthen his people — like a seed planted in rich soil that grows, bears fruit, and drops new seeds — and God means it to be told to people of every nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the good news was kept secret and has now been made known.</li><li>We are learning that Christians are planted in the gospel and strengthened by God's word.</li><li>I can explain what feeds a Christian's growth.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students identify God's word as what strengthens a Christian.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Kept secret, now told</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Explain that the message was hidden and has now been made known, and that it was written down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God commanded that the good news about Jesus be written down?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. So that people from all nations would obey and have faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Planted in the gospel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Return to the plant picture — seeds planted, roots growing, feeding on nutrients.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Refer back to the seed and sprouting stages from Day 1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In our picture, what were the nutrients that made the plant grow strong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Soil, water and sunshine — for a Christian, God's word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Established and strengthened</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make the distinction: the gospel both establishes trust in Jesus and strengthens it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Paul chose a plant rather than a building to describe how Christians grow?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. A building gets finished; a plant keeps growing and produces more life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Ephesians 2:8–10 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 asks what fruit actually looks like on a Christian.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: Bearing fruit":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 16:25–27 (continued)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The fruit of a Christian's life</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The good news about Jesus has God's power to establish and strengthen his people — like a seed planted in rich soil that grows, bears fruit, and drops new seeds — and God means it to be told to people of every nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what fruit looks like in a Christian's life.</li><li>We are learning that fruit shows the soil and the plant are healthy.</li><li>I can explain that the healthy plant produces fruit, not the reverse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen carefully on the healthy-plant question — it's the same order-of-things point as Week 4.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What fruit looks like</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Collect concrete examples of Christian fruit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Refer back to the flower and fruit stages of the plant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is some of the fruit that Christians will bear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Obeying God's word, meeting with and serving other Christians, loving enemies, kindness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What fruit tells you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out that fruit is evidence of good soil and a healthy plant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does the fruit or the flowers on a plant tell you about the soil and the plant?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The soil is rich and good for growing; the plant is healthy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Which way round?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Press the order question — this is Week 4's point in a new picture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does the fruit make the plant healthy, or does the healthy plant produce the fruit?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The healthy plant produces the fruit — good works come because of the gospel, not to earn it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>And then?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">End on the seeds question and leave it hanging for tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">But what happens after the plant has grown strong and produced flowers or fruit?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. It drops seeds that produce new life.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 is the final media briefing of the term — and the assignment the whole term has been building toward.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: The seeds drop":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Romans 16:26–27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Telling others, and the newspaper article</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The good news about Jesus has God's power to establish and strengthen his people — like a seed planted in rich soil that grows, bears fruit, and drops new seeds — and God means it to be told to people of every nation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God's people tell others about Jesus so they too can be planted in the gospel.</li><li>We are learning that the gospel is for people of every nation.</li><li>I can write a newspaper article explaining the gospel in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The article itself is the assessment. Compare it with the definition each student wrote in Week 1 Day 4.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On-screen media briefing: Lesson 18 Media release</li><li>Paper or Activity books and pencils</li><li>The students' Week 1 gospel definitions, if you kept them</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The seeds drop</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Complete the plant cycle and apply it — Christians tell others, and new people are planted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God wants his people to do this?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. So that people from every nation can come to know Jesus and follow him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Final media briefing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Run the last on-screen briefing. Remind the class the story is fictional. Make a moment of it — training is over.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tap Start the script on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Write the article</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5+ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Set the article task and read the helper questions aloud for anyone who's stuck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This will likely spill past today — that's fine, students can keep working on it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Then and now</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare with the Week 1 definitions and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Cast your mind right back to Week 1. How would your answer be different now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Hand back their Week 1 definitions if you kept them.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week is the Christmas lesson — and it ties every promise in the last three terms back to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“You were saved by faith in God, who treats us much better than we deserve. This is God's gift to you, and not anything you have done on your own. It isn't something you have earned, so there is nothing you can boast about. God planned for us to do good things and to live as he has always wanted us to live. That's why he sent Christ to make us what we are.” — <em>Ephesians 2:8–10 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for Paul's letter to the Romans. Thank you that through this letter so many people can now know the good news about Jesus. Thank you that your plan right from the beginning has been to send Jesus to save people. Please help your people to grow up strong in their trust in you. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 9: The Christmas promise":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas is not really about presents — it is about promises, and about God keeping them. Every promise God made to Abraham, to David and through Isaiah was always pointing to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 19</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Christmas is about promises":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–3; 2 Samuel 7:12–16; Isaiah 53 (overview)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Three promises, made over eleven hundred years</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas is not really about presents — it is about promises, and about God keeping them. Every promise God made to Abraham, to David and through Isaiah was always pointing to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Christmas is about God keeping his promises.</li><li>We are learning the three promises God made — to Abraham, to David, and through Isaiah.</li><li>I can learn this fortnight's memory verse, Luke 24:27.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can name all three promises without prompting by the end.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Three wrapped 'Promise boxes', each with a Bible passage inside</li><li>A CEV Bible</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What's Christmas about?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with both Way in questions, then offer the answer: promises, and God keeping them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what do you think is the most important thing at Christmas?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Don't correct — you're about to offer a different answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Open the promise boxes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask three volunteers to open the boxes and read each Bible passage out, one at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have prepared physical Promise boxes, use them here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This year we've spent a lot of time on promises. What have we learned about them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. The promises to Abraham, and that God is faithful in keeping them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Eleven hundred years apart</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give the timings — David about 900 years after Abraham, Isaiah about 250 years after David.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you have the Bible timeline poster, place arrows at Abraham, David and Isaiah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Over eleven hundred years, three different promises. What would you expect — three separate answers, or one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. All three are pointing to the same thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>New memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce Luke 24:27 for the last two weeks and say it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 opens Matthew 1 and checks the first promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Promise one: blessing for the whole world":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 1:1–17; Revelation 7:9–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The promise to Abraham, kept in Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas is not really about presents — it is about promises, and about God keeping them. Every promise God made to Abraham, to David and through Isaiah was always pointing to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Matthew begins with Jesus' family history.</li><li>We are learning that God's people now come from every nation.</li><li>I can explain how the promise to Abraham was kept in Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students connect Revelation's crowd from every nation back to Genesis 12.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Matthew 1:1–17 and Revelation 7:9–10</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why this baby?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open with the question, then read or summarise Matthew 1:1–17.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You don't need to read every name — the shape of the list is the point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think is so important about this particular baby?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Let them offer their own reasons first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Why the family list?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out that Matthew is proving Jesus came from Abraham's and David's families.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Matthew thought it was so important to tell us about Jesus' family history?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. To show Jesus came from the family of Abraham and of David.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Revelation 7:9–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Revelation 7:9–10, then compare who God's people were then and are now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, fill in the last column of the promises table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So how do you think this promise to Abraham came true through Jesus coming to earth?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Through Jesus' death, people from every nation can join God's family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Luke 24:27 together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 checks the second promise — the king whose kingdom never ends.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: Promise two: a king forever":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:30–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The promise to David, kept in Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas is not really about presents — it is about promises, and about God keeping them. Every promise God made to Abraham, to David and through Isaiah was always pointing to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what God promised King David.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is the king from David's family whose kingdom never ends.</li><li>I can explain how a kingdom could last forever.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students grasp that the promise needs a king who doesn't die.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Luke 1:30–33</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: The angel visits Mary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Gabriel, Mary (and a Narrator), and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The second promise box</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Restate the promise to David and let the class chew on how it could ever be kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Indicate the second Promise box if you're using them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every kingdom in history has eventually ended. How could that promise possibly be kept?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Let them wrestle — the answer needs a king who never dies.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Gabriel's announcement</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Luke 1:30–33 and draw out the four claims — Son of God, made king, rules forever, kingdom never ends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">So how was God's promise to King David kept in Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Jesus is the king in David's family who reigns over God's people forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"2\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>King forever</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">State it plainly: Jesus is king of all the world now and will be forever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How God keeps promises</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask the closing reflection about the way God chose to announce it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it tell you that God announced this to a young woman in a small town?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. God keeps his promises in his own way and time, often not as people expect.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 checks the third promise — and it's the one that explains Jesus' name.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Promise three: a Saviour":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 1:20–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The promise through Isaiah, kept in Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas is not really about presents — it is about promises, and about God keeping them. Every promise God made to Abraham, to David and through Isaiah was always pointing to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Jesus was given his name.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is the servant Isaiah promised.</li><li>I can say what Christmas should remind us of.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students can link Jesus' name to his job — saving his people from their sins.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at Matthew 1:20–21</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: The angel visits Joseph</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Angel, Joseph (and a Narrator), and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The third promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Restate Isaiah's promise — a servant who takes the consequences and makes God's people well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Indicate the third Promise box if you're using them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Matthew 1:20–21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read Matthew 1:20–21 and work through the name questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why was Joseph to give this baby the name Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Because he would save his people from their sin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>How, and what it means</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out the cross and resurrection, and what forgiveness means for God's people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what does that mean for God's people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. They have been forgiven and can become part of God's family.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What Christmas reminds us of</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pull all three promises together, then ask about the things that pull focus at Christmas.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If your students have Activity books, this is the T-shirt design activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some of them — and how could someone keep Jesus in view anyway?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. None of these are bad; the question is what sits at the centre.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Verse and prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say Luke 24:27 together and close in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse and prayer blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week is the last of the term — meeting someone who is living all of this out today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Jesus then explained everything written about himself in the Scriptures, beginning with the Law of Moses and the Books of the Prophets.” — <em>Luke 24:27 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you that the Bible says that you are a faithful God who keeps your promises. We've seen that you've kept your promises to Abraham, David and the rest of your people. Thank you that you sent Jesus to die on the cross to bring forgiveness. As Jesus' birth is celebrated this Christmas we pray that you would help us to remember all that we've learned about him this year. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}},"Week 10: Miriam":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 4:12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam is a real person following Jesus today. She trusted him when she was eight and now sets an example for the students she works with — because Paul told Timothy that being young is no reason for anyone to look down on you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect B2 Upper Primary — Lesson 20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Which country am I?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 4:12 (introduction)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Meeting Miriam and her family</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam is a real person following Jesus today. She trusted him when she was eight and now sets an example for the students she works with — because Paul told Timothy that being young is no reason for anyone to look down on you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about Miriam's cultural background.</li><li>We are learning how Miriam's family came to be a bi-cultural family in Australia.</li><li>I can learn this week's key verse, 1 Timothy 4:12.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students engage with the timeline dates rather than just the story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Optional: a map of China and a timeline for students to fill in</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Which country am I?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read all the clues even if someone guesses early — the detail is the point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Display a map of China afterwards if you have one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What things do you already know about China?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Anything goes — food, language, places, family connections.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Miriam's dad</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give the 1850s gold rush and the 1963 move, then work through the hospital questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If students are keeping a timeline, label 1850 and 1963 here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Roughly how long was that after the first significant number of Chinese people moved to Australia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. About 110 years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Meeting Miriam's mum</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how her parents met and married, then list the four children.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Label 1971 on the timeline, then the four births.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who do you think that nurse turned out to be?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Miriam's mum. They married three years later, in 1971.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Key verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce 1 Timothy 4:12 as the key verse for the final week and say it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the verse block, then the say-it-together activity block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 2 is about growing up in two cultures — and the camp where Miriam became a Christian.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't let anyone make fun of you, just because you are young. Set an example for other followers by what you say and do, as well as by your love, faith, and purity.” — <em>1 Timothy 4:12 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Tuesday: Growing up in two cultures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 4:12 (continued)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam's childhood and becoming a Christian</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam is a real person following Jesus today. She trusted him when she was eight and now sets an example for the students she works with — because Paul told Timothy that being young is no reason for anyone to look down on you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Miriam's bi-cultural childhood was like.</li><li>We are learning that Miriam decided to trust Jesus for herself at eight years old.</li><li>I can say that people can become a Christian at any age.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students understand that faith isn't inherited from parents.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Two cultures at home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe Miriam's childhood — Chinese food and language school, plus sport and choir.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be sensitive with the cultural background question; only invite volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Does anyone here have a mum from one cultural background and a dad from a different one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept hands. Only invite students who are comfortable. Be aware of varied family situations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What your family gave you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Widen it out so every student has something to contribute.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let students pass if they'd rather not answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is something you've been given by your family — a food, a language, a skill, a tradition?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses warmly. There's no wrong answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Church and camp</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell about her family's church, and the camp where she decided to trust Jesus at eight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Label 'Miriam becomes a Christian' on the timeline, about eight years after her birth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Deciding for yourself</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw out that faith isn't inherited, and that people come to trust Jesus at any age.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Miriam's parents were Christians — but she still had to decide for herself. Why do you think that matters?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Nobody is a Christian just because their family is.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 3 follows Miriam from art teacher to school chaplain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't let anyone make fun of you, just because you are young. Set an example for other followers by what you say and do, as well as by your love, faith, and purity.” — <em>1 Timothy 4:12 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Wednesday: From art teacher to chaplain":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 4:12 (continued)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam's work as an assistant chaplain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam is a real person following Jesus today. She trusted him when she was eight and now sets an example for the students she works with — because Paul told Timothy that being young is no reason for anyone to look down on you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a Christian chaplain does.</li><li>We are learning what Miriam does in her job each day.</li><li>I can name someone I could ask if I had questions about God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether every student can name at least one person they could ask about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen/device to display the week's content (already embedded in the app)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Questions with nobody to ask</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Miriam left school with questions, then ask who students could go to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make sure students know they can ask you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who might you be able to talk to if you had questions about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. A Scripture teacher, parents, leaders at church.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Jobs and art</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask about jobs a Christian might have, then Miriam's training as a visual arts teacher.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What kind of jobs do you think someone who loves and follows Jesus might have?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. No right or wrong answer — Christians work in lots of different vocations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"5\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>What a chaplain is</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Define chaplain, then let students guess what Miriam does before telling them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Label 'Assistant chaplain at a Christian school' on the timeline.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who can tell us what a Christian chaplain is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Works in schools, hospitals, nursing homes and prisons to help people understand more about Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Miriam's day</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Describe the daily Bible studies, the leadership training and the visual arts teaching.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What kinds of things do you think Miriam might do as an assistant chaplain?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses first. Then: daily Bible studies for Years 7-12, training senior leaders, teaching visual arts.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Day 4 is the last day of the term — Miriam's favourite verse, and Timothy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't let anyone make fun of you, just because you are young. Set an example for other followers by what you say and do, as well as by your love, faith, and purity.” — <em>1 Timothy 4:12 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n"},"Thursday: Don't let anyone look down on you":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10–15 minutes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Timothy 4:12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Setting an example, however young you are</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Miriam is a real person following Jesus today. She trusted him when she was eight and now sets an example for the students she works with — because Paul told Timothy that being young is no reason for anyone to look down on you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Timothy was and why Paul wrote to him.</li><li>We are learning that Christians are not to be ashamed of following Jesus.</li><li>I can describe what setting an example looks like in practice.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for whether students give concrete, everyday examples rather than abstract ones.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A CEV Bible marked at 1 Timothy 4:12</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">1</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Act it out: Scene: Paul commissions Timothy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose volunteers for Paul, Timothy (and a Narrator), and run the built-in script as a fullscreen presenter for the class to watch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the built-in roleplay block — tap 'Start the script on screen' to open the fullscreen presenter for your volunteers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">2</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Who was Timothy?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3–4 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell how Paul met Timothy in Lystra, trained him, and left him leading the church in Ephesus while still young.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Some students may recall Timothy from an earlier Connect book.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">3</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Not to, and instead</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read 1 Timothy 4:12 and work both questions — what not to do, and what to do instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">And what did Paul tell Timothy to do instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Set an example by what he said and did, and in love, faith and purity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">4</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>The same for Miriam</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Apply it to Miriam and the senior students she trains.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What kinds of things do you think Miriam and those senior students would do to show that they follow Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. Kindness, including others, no gossip, listening to teachers, reading the Bible, praying, going to church.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"4\">5</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>And for you</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2–3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Land the last point of the year: you don't have to be older to follow Jesus or set an example.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul told Timothy that being young was no reason for anyone to look down on him. What does that mean for people your age?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Accept responses. You don't have to wait until you're older. Timothy was young; Miriam was eight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\" rowspan=\"3\">6</th><th colspan=\"1\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Closing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1–2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close the term, and the year, in prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Use the prayer block.</td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">That's the end of Term 4 and the end of the Connect B2 Upper Primary book. A future Term 5 would start a new book — see the handoff doc before beginning one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verse</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">“Don't let anyone make fun of you, just because you are young. Set an example for other followers by what you say and do, as well as by your love, faith, and purity.” — <em>1 Timothy 4:12 (CEV)</em></blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Closing Prayer</div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Dear God, thank you for what we learned about Miriam's life in our lesson today. Thank you for the good example Miriam's family was to her at an early age. Please help Miriam as she continues to live your way and not her own way. Thank you for showing us what a Christian life looks like today. Amen.</blockquote></div>\n"}}}}}}},"writing":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tier 2 vocabulary: descriptive adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): words and phrases are chosen deliberately for effect, including Tier 2 vocabulary that is precise and cross-curricular. Students select words that shape meaning and impact for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Watch Me Choose Better Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'm going to show you how to swap a plain word for a much more interesting one. Watch how I upgrade the word &quot;big&quot; in this sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The big dog ran across the field.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of &quot;big&quot; I could say: enormous, muscular, towering, hulking. I pick &quot;muscular&quot; because it tells us something extra — not just size, but strength. Watch: &quot;The muscular dog ran across the field.&quot; Much more vivid!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself: Does this word paint a picture? If not, upgrade it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's upgrade the plain adjectives in these sentences together. Shout out your ideas!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The nice sunset filled the sky with colour.</li><li>She wore a pretty dress to the concert.</li><li>It was a bad storm that hit the town.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What word could replace the underlined one? Why is your choice better?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by replacing the plain adjective with a stronger, more descriptive one. Try to use a different word each time!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old bridge creaked in the wind.</li><li>We climbed a high mountain on our trip.</li><li>The puppy had a soft coat.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own sentence using two vivid adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Word choice for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by their context… vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to select Tier 2 words (precise, cross-curricular vocabulary such as vivid adjectives) that sharpen meaning and create impact for the reader.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting precise words: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including subject-specific vocabulary, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of upgrading vague adjectives to precise, purposeful ones — a core Phase 2 composing practice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nouns & proper nouns – identification & use":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure &amp; Punctuation (Knowledge): sentences are made up of words that belong to different classes; proper nouns name specific people, places, and things and always begin with a capital letter.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spot the Noun</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A noun is a naming word — it names a person, place, thing, or idea. A proper noun is the specific name of something and always starts with a capital letter. Watch me find and sort them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The girl visited Wellington with her family.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;girl&quot; = common noun (a person). &quot;Wellington&quot; = proper noun (specific place — capital W). &quot;family&quot; = common noun (a group). Proper nouns need a capital because they are the actual name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: Is this the specific name of someone or somewhere? → proper noun → capital letter!</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort &amp; Capitalise</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's sort these words into common nouns and proper nouns together, and fix any missing capitals.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my friend aisha lives in christchurch near the avon river.</li><li>the teacher read a book called the lion, the witch and the wardrobe.</li><li>on tuesday we visited the auckland museum with mrs chen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words need a capital letter? Which are just common nouns?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence correctly, adding capital letters for all proper nouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last summer, my cousin jake visited queenstown with uncle dan.</li><li>our school, parkside primary, is near the hutt river.</li><li>i am reading a book by roald dahl called matilda.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences — one with 2 common nouns, one with 2 proper nouns.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes (e.g. nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, pronouns, prepositions, conjunctions) and understanding word classes helps writers make deliberate choices.&quot; Students learn to identify common and proper nouns as a foundation for accurate and purposeful writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitalisation of proper nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Punctuation signals meaning to readers, including… capital letters at the start of sentences and for proper nouns.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to apply capitalisation conventions accurately and consistently in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Simple sentence structure: subject + verb + object":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a simple sentence contains a subject and a verb; students construct grammatically complete sentences and use a variety of sentence structures for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Build a Simple Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence needs at least a subject (who/what) and a verb (action/state). We can also add an object (what receives the action). Watch me build one piece at a time.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The cat | chased | the mouse.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Subject = &quot;The cat&quot; (who is doing it). Verb = &quot;chased&quot; (the action). Object = &quot;the mouse&quot; (what it acted on). Put them together: complete thought, complete sentence!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check: Who is doing it? What are they doing? What are they doing it to?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Label &amp; Build</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's label each part (S/V/O) in these sentences, then try building our own on the board.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The goalkeeper saved the penalty.</li><li>A huge wave swallowed the sandcastle.</li><li>My little brother ate the last biscuit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Point to the subject, verb, and object. What would happen if we removed the verb?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence with S (subject), V (verb), O (object). Then write three simple sentences of your own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The chef prepared a delicious meal.</li><li>The explorer discovered a hidden cave.</li><li>Lightning struck the tall tree.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an S+V+O sentence about something that happened at school this week.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Simple sentences: S + V + O: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A simple sentence contains one main clause with a subject and a verb… writers use a variety of sentence structures, including simple, compound, and complex sentences, for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson establishes the simple sentence as the core building block students then extend and combine across the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Constructing grammatically correct sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of grammatically correct sentences&quot; and &quot;use knowledge of sentence structure to clarify meaning.&quot; Identifying and labelling subject, verb, and object builds the grammatical awareness needed to write with precision and variety.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Proofreading: capitals & full stops":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge &amp; Practice): sentences begin with a capital letter and end with a full stop, exclamation mark, or question mark; students plan and revise writing so that it is accurate and clear.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Be the Editor</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with a full stop (or ! or ?). Watch me read slowly and fix the mistakes in this passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: the sun rose slowly over the mountains. birds began to sing in the trees it was going to be a perfect day the children raced outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read aloud and pause at every sentence end. &quot;the sun&quot; → capital T. After &quot;mountains.&quot; ✓ already has one. After &quot;trees&quot; — no full stop! I add one. &quot;it&quot; → capital I. And so on. I'll mark every fix in red.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read out loud — you naturally pause at sentence ends. That pause = full stop.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix It Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's fix this passage together. One student comes up and marks each correction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>maya loved visiting her grandma on fridays they would bake together grandma always made chocolate biscuits maya's favourite was the ones with icing on top they would eat them while watching the birds in the garden</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do sentences end? Which words need capitals? How many sentences are in this passage?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage correctly, adding all missing capitals and full stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last weekend dad and i went fishing at the lake we caught three fish but we threw them back the water was cold and clear i could see stones on the bottom dad said it was the best fishing spot he knew</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many sentences you created. Write your own 3-sentence paragraph with perfect punctuation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitals &amp; full stops: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Punctuation signals meaning to readers, including capital letters at the start of sentences and for proper nouns, and end-of-sentence punctuation (full stop, exclamation mark, question mark).&quot; These are the foundational conventions students must control accurately before building to more complex punctuation.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation and capitalisation.&quot; This editing lesson directly builds that revision habit — reading aloud to catch missing punctuation and incorrect capitalisation.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Commas in lists – rules & practice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): commas are used to separate items in a list; students use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Commas Keep Lists Tidy</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we list three or more things in a sentence, we separate them with commas. The last item uses &quot;and&quot; (not a comma). Watch me build a list sentence step by step.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: In my bag I have a pencil, a ruler, a water bottle and my lunchbox.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I list the items: pencil → comma → ruler → comma → water bottle → &quot;and&quot; → lunchbox. No comma before &quot;and&quot; in NZ/UK style. The commas stop the sentence becoming a jumbled mess!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your list aloud. Every natural pause (except before &quot;and&quot;) = a comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Commas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These list sentences are missing commas. Let's fix them together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market sold fresh tomatoes plump strawberries creamy cheese and warm bread.</li><li>Liam packed his boots his shin pads his water bottle his jersey and his mouthguard.</li><li>The painting used shades of crimson burnt orange golden yellow and deep purple.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the pauses fall? Does &quot;and&quot; need a comma before it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add commas to the list sentences, then write two of your own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At the beach we collected shells pebbles driftwood and sea glass.</li><li>The recipe called for flour sugar butter eggs and a pinch of salt.</li><li>Her favourite animals were dolphins elephants red pandas and otters.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence listing 5 things you would bring on a survival adventure.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Commas in lists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Commas are used to separate items in a list and to clarify meaning within sentences.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to know and apply comma conventions accurately, including the NZ/UK convention of no serial comma before &quot;and&quot; in a list.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect, including commas.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of reading aloud to identify list pauses — a practical revision strategy that reinforces accurate punctuation in students' own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Verbs: action vs. state verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): words belong to different classes; verbs express actions, events, or states and are a core building block of every sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Kinds of Verbs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Action verbs show physical or mental movement (run, think, build). State verbs describe a condition or state of being (is, seems, belongs, loves). Watch how I spot the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The hawk soared above the valley. | The valley is very wide.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;soared&quot; — can I picture the hawk doing this? Yes! Action verb. &quot;is&quot; — is the valley doing anything? Not really — it describes its state. State verb. State verbs often can't be continuous: you wouldn't say &quot;the valley is being wide.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Can you picture someone physically doing it? → Probably action. Does it describe a condition? → State.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort Them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's sort these verbs and sentences into action or state, then discuss why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students sprinted to the finish line.</li><li>This soup smells incredible.</li><li>She believes in always trying her best.</li><li>The puppy chewed through the shoe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Action or state? Could you say &quot;is ___-ing&quot;? Would that sound strange?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each underlined verb as ACTION or STATE. Then write one sentence for each type.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The river rushes over the boulders in winter.</li><li>My grandmother owns a beautiful garden.</li><li>He remembers every goal he ever scored.</li><li>The children splashed through the puddles.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence that has BOTH an action verb and a state verb.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes (e.g. nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs…) and understanding word classes helps writers make deliberate choices.&quot; Distinguishing action verbs from state verbs deepens students' understanding of how verbs function, enabling more precise and purposeful verb selection in their own writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting precise verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; including choosing verbs that convey exactly the right meaning. Knowing the difference between action and state verbs helps students avoid weak verb constructions and select words with greater precision.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding simple sentences with adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): adjectives describe or modify nouns; students expand simple sentences by adding detail and description to communicate more precisely.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Dress Up Your Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A simple sentence is fine, but adjectives add colour and detail. Watch how I take a bare sentence and expand it by adding adjectives before nouns.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Before: A dog barked at the stranger. → After: A scruffy, brown dog barked at the hooded stranger.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask: What kind of dog? → scruffy, brown. What kind of stranger? → hooded. I add them before the nouns. Notice I used a comma between two adjectives describing the same noun (scruffy, brown). But &quot;hooded&quot; is alone so no comma needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask &quot;what kind?&quot; or &quot;which one?&quot; before each noun to find adjective spots.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's expand these simple sentences by adding at least one adjective to each noun. Call out your ideas!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The cat sat on the wall.</li><li>A boat moved through the water.</li><li>The girl opened the door.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What adjectives could we add? Where exactly in the sentence do they go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two adjectives. You can also add adjectives to the verb (adverbs) as a bonus challenge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The horse galloped across the field.</li><li>A bird landed on the fence.</li><li>The student opened the book.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own expanded sentence about a place you know, using at least 3 adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Adjectives &amp; noun modification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives describe or modify nouns and can be used before or after the noun to add detail and precision.&quot; Students also learn that a comma is needed between two or more adjectives describing the same noun, connecting adjective use to punctuation knowledge.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding sentences for detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; This lesson builds that core skill of moving from a bare S+V+O sentence to a richer, more detailed construction — a key step in developing writing craft at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: fixing run-on sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students reread and revise writing to ensure sentences are correctly demarcated and ideas are clearly separated.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Taming the Run-On</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A run-on sentence is when two or more complete sentences crash together without proper punctuation. Watch me find and fix one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The rain hammered the roof the windows rattled and the lights flickered on and off everyone ran inside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read slowly. &quot;The rain hammered the roof&quot; — full thought. Full stop! &quot;the windows rattled and the lights flickered on and off&quot; — I could keep this as one sentence since &quot;and&quot; joins related ideas. Full stop! &quot;everyone ran inside.&quot; — done. Result: Three clear sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Say each sentence aloud. If you run out of breath before a pause, it's probably too long.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Run-Ons</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's work through these run-on sentences together. Decide where to split them or what joining word to use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sophia loved drawing she spent every afternoon filling sketchbooks with dragons and stars her mum often had to call her in for dinner.</li><li>The match was tied with five minutes left the striker broke free she shot the ball it curved into the top corner the crowd erupted.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the natural pauses fall? Should we split with a full stop, or join with &quot;and/but/so&quot;?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each run-on as two or three clear sentences. Choose the best punctuation or joining word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My dog loves the park he chases every stick he also tries to swim in the fountain the ranger always laughs.</li><li>The volcano had been quiet for years then one morning smoke rose from the crater by afternoon it was glowing red scientists rushed to the site.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 3 sentences on any topic — no run-ons allowed!</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Sentence demarcation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Sentences are demarcated by capital letters and end punctuation; run-on sentences occur when two or more complete clauses are joined without correct punctuation or an appropriate conjunction.&quot; Understanding sentence boundaries is foundational to all writing accuracy at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising run-on sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation.&quot; This lesson trains students to slow down, read aloud, and fix the most common structural error in Years 5–6 writing — the run-on sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Synonyms: choosing stronger words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): the meaning of words can be extended or changed by context; students select synonyms deliberately to match the intensity and precision needed for their purpose and audience.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Level Up Your Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Synonyms are words with similar meanings — but they're not identical. Choosing the right synonym can completely change the feel of your writing. Watch me use a synonym scale.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: cold → chilly → freezing → glacial → arctic</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All mean &quot;cold&quot; — but they get more intense. &quot;The water was cold&quot; is fine. &quot;The water was glacial&quot; paints a sharper picture. I match the word to the intensity of what I'm describing. A slightly cool breeze is &quot;chilly.&quot; A swimming hole in winter is &quot;glacial.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Think: Is this word strong enough for what I'm describing, or do I need to turn up the dial?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Synonym Scales</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build synonym scales for these words together, then choose the best one for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>HAPPY → (let's find 4 synonyms, from mild to intense)</li><li>MOVE → (find synonyms that show different kinds of movement)</li><li>&quot;She was happy when she won the prize.&quot; → Which synonym fits best?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why would you choose &quot;elated&quot; over &quot;pleased&quot; in some sentences but not others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the plain word in each sentence with a stronger synonym. Then create your own synonym scale for the word &quot;said.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The athlete was tired after the marathon.</li><li>It was a good book — I finished it in one day.</li><li>The crowd was loud when the team scored.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Build a synonym scale for &quot;said&quot; with at least 6 words. Which would you use in a mystery story? A speech? An argument?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Synonyms &amp; word intensity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by their context… vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Understanding synonym scales — that words with similar meanings carry different intensities and connotations — is a key component of building Tier 2 vocabulary at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson directly builds that skill by teaching students to match word intensity to the meaning they want to convey, rather than defaulting to the first word they think of.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Subject-verb agreement (singular/plural)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a verb must agree in number with the subject of the clause; students write grammatically correct sentences and identify and correct errors in their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Subjects &amp; Verbs Must Match</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The subject and verb in a sentence must agree in number. Singular subject = singular verb. Plural subject = plural verb. Watch how I check the match.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The dog barks. ✓ | The dogs bark. ✓ | The dogs barks. ✗</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I find the subject first: &quot;the dogs&quot; — plural. Now the verb: &quot;barks&quot; — singular (the -s gives it away). They don't match! Fix: &quot;bark.&quot; Rule: when the subject has an -s (plural), the verb usually doesn't, and vice versa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Cross out any words between subject and verb — they can trick you. &quot;The group of students [are/is]...&quot; → subject is &quot;group&quot; (singular) → &quot;is.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct verb form for each sentence and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bunch of grapes (was / were) left on the bench.</li><li>Her three brothers (play / plays) for the same rugby team.</li><li>Everyone in the class (know / knows) the answer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the true subject? Is it singular or plural?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Circle the correct verb form and rewrite the sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The flock of birds (move / moves) south in winter.</li><li>My best friend and I (love / loves) going to the movies.</li><li>Each of the sandwiches (need / needs) a label.</li><li>The team (was / were) celebrating their win on the field.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two tricky subject-verb sentences for a partner to solve.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subject-verb agreement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A verb must agree in number and person with the subject of the clause. Errors in subject-verb agreement disrupt the grammatical accuracy of a sentence.&quot; Students learn to identify the true subject (ignoring intervening phrases) and select the correct verb form — including tricky cases such as collective nouns and indefinite pronouns.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Identifying and correcting errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Recognising and fixing subject-verb agreement errors is a core component of that accuracy check, and a skill students are expected to apply independently in their own writing at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences using 'and', 'but', 'so'":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): compound sentences join two main clauses using a coordinating conjunction; students use a variety of sentence structures, including compound sentences, for effect and clarity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Join Two Ideas Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A compound sentence joins two simple sentences using a coordinating conjunction: &quot;and&quot; (adds), &quot;but&quot; (contrasts), &quot;so&quot; (shows result). Watch me join two short sentences into one smooth compound sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The bell rang. The students rushed outside. → The bell rang, and the students rushed outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I put a comma before the conjunction when joining two full sentences. Let me try &quot;but&quot;: &quot;I wanted to go swimming, but the pool was closed.&quot; And &quot;so&quot;: &quot;It was raining heavily, so we moved the game inside.&quot; Each conjunction gives a different meaning!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Remember the comma before the conjunction when joining two complete sentences.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Join the Pairs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join these pairs of sentences using &quot;and,&quot; &quot;but,&quot; or &quot;so.&quot; Discuss which conjunction fits best and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She studied all weekend. She still felt nervous about the test.</li><li>The campfire burned brightly. Everyone gathered around it.</li><li>Jake forgot his lunch. His friend shared her sandwich with him.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why does &quot;but&quot; work better than &quot;and&quot; in the first one? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair of sentences with the best conjunction (and/but/so). Remember your comma!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The explorers were exhausted. They pushed on through the jungle.</li><li>The dog dug a hole under the fence. It escaped into the neighbour's garden.</li><li>She practised every day. She became the best swimmer in her school.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three compound sentences about your last school holiday — use &quot;and&quot; once, &quot;but&quot; once, and &quot;so&quot; once.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Compound sentences &amp; conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A compound sentence consists of two or more main clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or, so, yet). A comma is placed before the conjunction when joining two complete clauses.&quot; Students learn that the choice of conjunction shapes the relationship between ideas — addition, contrast, or result.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using sentence variety for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson introduces compound sentences as the first step beyond the simple sentence, giving students a tool for connecting related ideas smoothly and varying the rhythm of their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying vague words & upgrading them":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students select precise words for effect and reread their writing to identify and replace vague or overused language.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Hunt the Vague Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Vague words are words so overused they've lost their meaning: nice, good, bad, big, small, went, said, things, got. Watch me find them in this passage and replace them with precise language.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: It was a nice day and we went to a nice beach. The water was nice and cold and we had a good time.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I underline every vague word: nice (×3), went, good. Now I replace: &quot;It was a golden, windless day and we drove to a sparkling beach. The water was refreshingly cold and we had a brilliant time.&quot; Far more vivid!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing, circle every &quot;nice,&quot; &quot;good,&quot; &quot;went,&quot; &quot;said,&quot; &quot;got&quot; and challenge yourself to upgrade at least half of them.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's find and replace all the vague words in this passage together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The big dog got up and went to the door. It made a loud noise and looked at us. We thought it was a good dog but it was a bit scary. Then things got better when its owner came.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words can we upgrade? What more precise/vivid option works in each spot?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by replacing every underlined vague word with a more precise one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The nice teacher said that our project was good. We had worked on it for a long time and got everything ready the night before. The big display looked great and all our stuff was neatly arranged.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Pull out your last piece of writing and hunt for three vague words to upgrade.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing vague language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most common vocabulary weakness at Years 5–6 — over-reliance on vague, all-purpose words — and builds the habit of deliberate, precise word selection.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for word precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This editing lesson directly practises that revision process: identifying weak language and replacing it with words that create a clearer, more vivid picture for the reader.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Apostrophes for possession":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): apostrophes are used to show possession; the placement of the apostrophe changes depending on whether the owner is singular or plural.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Who Does It Belong To?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An apostrophe + s ('s) shows that something belongs to someone. If the owner is singular, add 's. If the owner is plural and already ends in s, just add the apostrophe after: s'.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Singular: the cat's collar | Plural: the cats' collars</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One cat owns the collar → cat's (apostrophe before s). Multiple cats own collars → cats' (apostrophe after s). The trick: find the owner first. 'The collar belonging to the cat' = cat → cat's. 'The collars belonging to the cats' = cats → cats'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Find the owner. If singular → add 's. If plural ending in s → add just '.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find the Owner</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's rewrite these 'belonging' phrases using apostrophes, and sort them into singular vs. plural owner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jersey belonging to the player → ___</li><li>The homework belonging to the students → ___</li><li>The beak belonging to the parrot → ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Who is the owner? How many owners are there? Where does the apostrophe go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each phrase using an apostrophe to show possession.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The den belonging to the fox → ___</li><li>The shoes belonging to the dancers → ___</li><li>The medal belonging to the swimmer → ___</li><li>The nests belonging to the birds → ___</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences about things you can see in the classroom using possessive apostrophes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Apostrophes for possession: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Apostrophes are used to show possession — singular owners take 's (e.g. the cat's collar) and plural owners already ending in s take an apostrophe after the s (e.g. the cats' collars).&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to use possessive apostrophes correctly and consistently in their own writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying apostrophes accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including apostrophes for possession. This lesson builds a reliable two-step strategy — identify the owner, then count them — that students can apply independently when editing their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Pronouns: personal & possessive":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): pronouns are a word class that replace nouns to avoid repetition; students use personal and possessive pronouns accurately to create cohesion and clarity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Standing In for Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pronouns replace nouns so we don't repeat the same word over and over. Personal pronouns (I, you, he, she, it, we, they) and possessive pronouns (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) are the most common. Watch me substitute them in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Maya left Maya's bag on Maya's seat when Maya went outside.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This sounds clunky! Replace: &quot;Maya left her bag on her seat when she went outside.&quot; The pronoun &quot;her&quot; replaces &quot;Maya's&quot; (possessive). &quot;she&quot; replaces &quot;Maya&quot; (personal). Much smoother!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Make sure your pronoun clearly refers back to the right noun — if there are two possible people, the reader gets confused.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Swap the Repeated Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the repeated nouns with the correct pronouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team trained hard. The team wanted the team's season to be the team's best ever.</li><li>Tom and Sarah arrived early. Tom and Sarah set up Tom and Sarah's stall at the market.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What pronoun replaces &quot;the team&quot;? What about &quot;Tom and Sarah&quot;? Which is possessive?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence replacing the repeated nouns with correct personal or possessive pronouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The explorer found the explorer's compass in the explorer's coat pocket.</li><li>The dolphins leaped out of the water. The dolphins seemed to enjoy the dolphins' freedom.</li><li>Lily and Ben packed Lily and Ben's bags. Lily and Ben left early for Lily and Ben's trip.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph where you use at least 4 different pronouns correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: pronouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes… pronouns are words that replace nouns and must agree with the nouns they refer to in number and person.&quot; Students learn to distinguish personal pronouns (replacing subjects and objects) from possessive pronouns (showing ownership) — two distinct functions that are frequently confused at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion through pronoun use: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; Using pronouns correctly is a primary cohesion strategy: it removes repetition and helps the writing flow smoothly from one sentence to the next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Writing topic sentences for paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): paragraphs are organised around a main idea expressed in a topic sentence; students structure their writing with clear paragraph organisation.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Anchor Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A topic sentence is the first sentence of a paragraph. It tells the reader the main idea. All the other sentences in the paragraph support or explain it. Watch me write one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: Why exercise is important → Topic sentence: &quot;Exercise is one of the most important habits a person can build for a healthy life.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>My topic sentence: (1) introduces the topic, (2) gives the writer's angle on it (not just &quot;exercise is good&quot; — too vague). The sentences that follow will each give a reason or example to support &quot;one of the most important habits.&quot; I won't switch to a new topic mid-paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test your topic sentence: could every other sentence in your paragraph be &quot;evidence&quot; for it? If yes — it works!</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match &amp; Write</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Match each topic sentence to the paragraph that fits it best. Then write a topic sentence for an unpaired paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic sentence A: &quot;Dogs make loyal and loving companions for all ages.&quot;</li><li>Topic sentence B: &quot;The rainforest is home to an astonishing variety of wildlife.&quot;</li><li>Paragraph without a topic sentence: &quot;...Thousands of species of insects live under its canopy. Brightly coloured frogs cling to leaves, and jaguars prowl silently below. Even the trees themselves host miniature ecosystems...&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which topic sentence fits the mystery paragraph? What clues told you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong topic sentence for each group of supporting sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Supporting sentences: &quot;First, it keeps your heart strong. It also helps you sleep better and boosts your mood. Even a short walk each day makes a difference.&quot;</li><li>Supporting sentences: &quot;Rubbish can entangle sea turtles and birds. Plastic bags are mistaken for jellyfish by whales. Tiny particles enter the food chain through fish.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph of 4 sentences: topic sentence + 3 supports, on a topic of your choice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Paragraph organisation &amp; topic sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Paragraphs group related ideas together, usually organised around a main idea introduced in a topic sentence. Supporting sentences develop, explain, or provide evidence for the main idea.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to understand that each paragraph has a clear focus and that the topic sentence signals this focus to the reader.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring paragraphs effectively: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of writing a controlling topic sentence before adding supporting detail — a key structural skill for all text types at Years 5–6, from narrative to argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking pronoun-antecedent agreement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): pronouns must agree with their antecedents in number; students identify and correct pronoun errors when revising their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Does the Pronoun Match?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A pronoun must agree with its antecedent (the noun it replaces) in number and gender. Singular noun → singular pronoun. Plural noun → plural pronoun. Watch me find and fix the mismatches.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The team picked up their trophy. ✗ → The team picked up its trophy. ✓</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The team&quot; is singular (one team) so the pronoun should be &quot;its&quot; not &quot;their.&quot; Now: &quot;Each student brought their lunch.&quot; Here &quot;each student&quot; is singular, so technically &quot;his or her lunch&quot; — but in modern usage &quot;their&quot; is widely accepted for singular subjects. Context matters!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Find the noun the pronoun replaces. Count it: one → singular pronoun, many → plural.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot &amp; Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix the pronoun agreement errors in these sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Every player gave their best, but the team lost their match in the final minute.</li><li>The flock of geese spread their wings and took off across the lake.</li><li>Anyone who finishes early can bring their book to reading corner.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the antecedent? Is it singular or plural? Does the pronoun match?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix any pronoun agreement errors. Write &quot;correct&quot; if there is no error.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The committee made their decision before the deadline.</li><li>Each of the birds had lost their feathers in the storm.</li><li>Neither of the girls wanted to give up their spot in the team.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a sports team. Use at least 3 different pronouns correctly — and then swap with a partner to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Pronoun-antecedent agreement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A pronoun must agree with its antecedent — the noun it replaces — in number (singular or plural) and person. Errors in pronoun agreement can confuse the reader about who or what is being referred to.&quot; Students also encounter the modern acceptance of singular 'they' and learn to navigate these nuances in context.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for pronoun accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; This editing lesson trains students to check every pronoun against its antecedent — a targeted revision strategy that improves grammatical accuracy and reader clarity in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Exclamation marks & question marks – when & why":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): sentences end with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark; the choice of end punctuation signals the purpose and tone of the sentence to the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Right Ending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence ends with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark. The wrong choice changes the reader's experience completely. Watch me demonstrate why the ending matters.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Come back.&quot; vs &quot;Come back!&quot; vs &quot;Will you come back?&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Come back.&quot; — a calm instruction. &quot;Come back!&quot; — urgent, emotional, even desperate. &quot;Will you come back?&quot; — a genuine question, uncertain. The same two words — completely different meanings from punctuation alone. Exclamation marks are powerful, so use them sparingly or they lose impact.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself: Am I expressing strong emotion or urgency? → ! | Am I asking a genuine question? → ? | Everything else → .</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct end punctuation and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How did the magician make the coin disappear</li><li>Watch out for the wave behind you</li><li>The library opens at nine o'clock every morning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotion or purpose is in each sentence? Would a different mark change the meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct end punctuation to each sentence and write a short explanation of your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did you hear that noise in the middle of the night</li><li>Never open that door again</li><li>The museum was closed on the day we planned to visit</li><li>I can't believe we actually won</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence where changing the end mark completely changes the meaning. Share with a partner.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — End punctuation: . ? !: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Sentences end with a full stop, question mark, or exclamation mark. The choice of end punctuation signals the sentence's purpose to the reader: statements use full stops, questions use question marks, and exclamations express strong emotion or urgency.&quot; Students learn that punctuation is a meaning-making tool, not just a convention to follow.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Punctuation for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This includes using exclamation marks sparingly for genuine impact, and understanding that overuse weakens their effect. Deliberate punctuation choices contribute to the tone, pace, and mood of a piece of writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adjectives: degrees of comparison (er/est/more/most)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison using comparative (-er/more) and superlative (-est/most) forms, including irregular forms.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Comparing with Adjectives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We use comparative adjectives to compare two things, and superlatives to compare three or more. Short adjectives add -er/-est. Longer adjectives use more/most. Watch me form them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: fast → faster (comparative) → fastest (superlative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>beautiful → more beautiful → most beautiful</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rule: 1–2 syllable adjectives → -er/-est: tall/taller/tallest. 3+ syllable adjectives → more/most: exciting/more exciting/most exciting. Exceptions: good/better/best, bad/worse/worst, far/further/furthest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Try adding -er: if it sounds natural, use it. If it sounds awkward (intelligenter?), use &quot;more.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form the Comparatives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Form the comparative and superlative of each adjective and use each in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cold → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>dangerous → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>good → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which rule applies? Are there any exceptions here? Can you use each in an example sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the comparison chart and then write sentences using the correct form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;happiest&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>mysterious → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;more mysterious&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>bad → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;worst&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph comparing two animals you know, using at least four comparative or superlative adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Comparative &amp; superlative adjectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison. Comparative forms (using -er or 'more') compare two things; superlative forms (using -est or 'most') compare three or more. Some adjectives have irregular comparative and superlative forms (good/better/best).&quot; Students learn and apply the spelling and usage rules for both regular and irregular forms.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise comparative language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using comparative and superlative adjectives precisely — rather than defaulting to vague modifiers like 'very good' — is a key marker of more sophisticated vocabulary use at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence variety: short vs. long sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students use a variety of sentence lengths deliberately for effect, understanding that mixing short and long sentences creates rhythm and controls the pace of writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Playing with Rhythm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writers vary their sentence length on purpose. Short sentences create tension and punch. Long sentences slow the pace and build atmosphere. Watch how I mix them for effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Long only: &quot;As the fog rolled silently in off the sea and crept through the narrow streets, wrapping itself around the lampposts and filling every doorway with a pale grey shadow, she stood completely still and listened to the sound of the city slowly disappearing.&quot; → Too slow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now watch: &quot;The fog rolled in. It crept through the streets, wrapping itself around lampposts, filling every doorway. She stood still. Listened. The city was disappearing.&quot; The short sentences (She stood still. Listened.) hit hard. The long one builds atmosphere. Mixing them creates rhythm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your writing aloud. Does it have a beat — fast/slow/fast? If everything is the same length, vary it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Mix It Up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This passage has all long sentences. Let's break some up to create rhythm together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ancient lighthouse had stood on the cliff for over two hundred years and had guided thousands of ships safely past the rocks and through the treacherous harbour entrance in every kind of weather imaginable. It had survived storms and earthquakes and the battering of winter waves and still it stood, its light still turning, still warning, still saving lives that would otherwise have been lost to the sea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where could we cut for impact? Which short sentence would create the most drama?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this all-short paragraph by combining some sentences into longer ones, while keeping 1–2 short ones for effect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire started. It spread fast. Smoke filled the hall. People ran. They called for help. The engines arrived. The firefighters moved quickly. They brought it under control.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 6 sentences about a storm — 2 short, 2 medium, 2 long. Read them aloud to check the rhythm.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence length for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including deliberately mixing sentence lengths. Short sentences create urgency and emphasis; long sentences build atmosphere and detail. This lesson develops students' awareness that sentence length is a craft choice, not an accident.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace &amp; rhythm: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling pace and rhythm. Varying sentence length is one of the most accessible ways to create a sense of drama, tension, or calm — and this lesson gives students a practical tool to apply immediately in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: peer-checking capitals & punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students use an editing checklist to review writing for punctuation accuracy; peer editing builds the habit of reading writing critically and giving specific feedback.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Using an Editing Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional editors use checklists so they don't miss anything. Watch me use our class punctuation checklist on a short passage — I'll mark each issue as I find it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: checklist: ☐ Capital at start of every sentence ☐ Full stop/!/? at end ☐ Commas in lists ☐ Proper nouns capitalised ☐ Apostrophes correct</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once just to understand it. Then I go through with the checklist — one check at a time. I mark issues with a light pencil. I explain why each fix is needed, not just what to change.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Never try to check everything at once — work through one rule at a time on separate read-throughs.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Peer Edit in Pairs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With your partner, use the checklist to edit this passage. One person checks capitals; the other checks full stops and commas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last thursday, mr. tane's class visited te papa museum in wellington. the students saw a giant squid a huge whale skeleton and many taonga from around new zealand. aisha's favourite exhibit was the earthquake house she said &quot;it felt so real.&quot; everyone agreed it was an amazing trip</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What did you each find? Did you agree on every correction? Compare notes.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage alone using the full checklist. Mark and rewrite all corrections.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on saturday morning, emma and her brother luca decided to bake a chocolate cake. they found flour eggs butter sugar and cocoa in the cupboard. the recipe was easy but luca forgot to add the baking powder. the cake came out flat and dense but mum said it still tasted delicious. emma said &quot;next time i'm doing it on my own&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and compare corrections. Discuss any differences.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing with a checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation and capitalisation.&quot; This lesson builds the systematic editing habit — one pass per rule — that prevents students from trying to fix everything at once and missing errors as a result.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing &amp; feedback: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work.&quot; Peer editing is an explicit Phase 2 expectation: students practise giving specific, rule-based feedback (not just &quot;it's good&quot;) and comparing their corrections to develop shared understanding of conventions.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Word families – roots, prefixes, suffixes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): many English words are built from roots, prefixes, and suffixes; understanding word parts unlocks the meaning of unfamiliar words and expands vocabulary.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words Are Built from Parts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most English words are built from parts: a root (the core meaning), a prefix (added to the front), and/or a suffix (added to the end). Knowing the parts unlocks the meaning of unknown words. Watch me break one apart.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: unhappiness → un- (not) + happy (root) + -ness (state of being) = &quot;the state of not being happy&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let me try &quot;prehistoric&quot;: pre- (before) + historic (relating to history) = &quot;before recorded history.&quot; Now &quot;transportation&quot;: trans- (across) + port (carry) + -ation (process) = &quot;the process of carrying things across.&quot; Once you know common roots, you can work out hundreds of words!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Common roots to remember: port (carry), rupt (break), vis (see), struct (build), dict (say), graph (write).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Break Them Down</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's pull apart these words using our knowledge of prefixes, roots, and suffixes. What does each part tell us?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>impossible → <em><strong> + </strong></em> = meaning?</li><li>prediction → <em><strong> + </strong></em> + ___ = meaning?</li><li>exportable → <em><strong> + </strong></em> + ___ = meaning?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How does knowing the prefix &quot;im-&quot; help you with impossible, impolite, impractical?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Break apart each word into its prefix/root/suffix. Write the meaning of each part, then the whole word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>invisible → </li><li>reconstruct → </li><li>disagreement → </li><li>photography → </li><li>✦ Bonus: Use your knowledge of &quot;port&quot; (carry) to work out: import, export, portable, transport, reporter.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Word roots, prefixes &amp; suffixes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Many English words are built from Latin and Greek roots, prefixes, and suffixes. Understanding the meaning of word parts enables students to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words and to build their vocabulary systematically.&quot; This lesson develops morphological awareness — a key component of Tier 2 and Tier 3 vocabulary development at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using word knowledge in writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use knowledge of word origins, roots, and word families to extend vocabulary.&quot; Understanding that &quot;port&quot; means carry (import, export, transport) gives students a transferable tool for both reading unfamiliar words and selecting more precise vocabulary in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adverbs: how, when, where":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs and express manner, time, and place; students use adverbs deliberately to add precision and detail.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Adverbs Tell Us More</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adverbs modify (add information to) verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. They answer How? When? Where? and To what extent? Watch me add adverbs to upgrade a simple sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The explorer walked. → The explorer walked cautiously (how) forward (where) yesterday (when).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I add them one at a time and notice how each one changes the meaning. How? → cautiously (tells us the manner). Where? → forward (tells us direction). When? → yesterday (tells us time). I can layer adverbs, but I should be selective — too many and the sentence becomes clunky.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Adverbs of manner often end in -ly (quickly, silently) — but not always (fast, hard, late).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Right Adverb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an adverb (how/when/where) to each sentence and label what type it is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The hawk circled ___.</li><li>___ the team gathered for their final practice.</li><li>She whispered ___ so no one would hear.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What question does your adverb answer? Could you add a second adverb of a different type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add one adverb of each type (how/when/where) to the sentence. Then write your own sentence using all three types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist worked <em><strong> (how) </strong></em> (when) ___ (where) to find an answer.</li><li>Add how, when, where: &quot;The wolves hunted.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find three adverbs in a book you're reading. Write which verb each modifies and what question it answers.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: adverbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs. They can express manner (how), time (when), place (where), and degree (to what extent). Adverbs of manner often end in -ly but not always.&quot; Students learn to identify adverb types and understand their grammatical role as modifiers within the sentence.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Adding precision with adverbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbs and adverbial phrases to express manner, time, and place.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of asking 'how? when? where?' when editing bare sentences — adding only the adverbial detail that genuinely improves precision, not padding.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Adding detail with 'who', 'where', 'when' phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students expand sentences by adding noun phrases, prepositional phrases, and relative clauses that answer who, where, and when to create a fuller picture for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Dress the Sentence Up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A bare sentence tells us the basics. Adding WHO/WHERE/WHEN phrases gives the reader a full picture. Watch me build one sentence three ways.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: The girl read a book. → + WHERE: The girl read a book in the shade of an old oak tree. → + WHO: The girl, who had borrowed it from the library that morning, read a book in the shade. → + WHEN: Just as the sun peaked at noon, the girl, who had borrowed it that morning, read a book in the shade.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each phrase layers in more context. The reader can now picture the scene, the person, and the moment. I don't have to add all three — I choose the ones that matter most for the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: does my reader know enough to picture this? If not, add one specific WHERE, WHO, or WHEN phrase.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Layer the Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's expand these sentences together by adding a who, where, and when phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog barked.</li><li>A boat drifted.</li><li>The children cheered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which detail matters most for each sentence? Does every detail need to be there, or can we cut one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two detail phrases (who/where/when).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The door slammed.</li><li>A light flickered.</li><li>The crowd fell silent.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an expanded sentence about a character arriving somewhere unexpected. Include who they are, where exactly they arrived, and when.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding sentences with detail phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; Adding who, where, and when phrases is a concrete, scaffolded way to develop this skill — students practise choosing which type of detail best serves each sentence rather than adding detail randomly.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating vivid, specific writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail. This lesson directly addresses the vagueness of bare sentences by giving students a systematic question framework (who? where? when?) to identify and fill gaps in their descriptions.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: tightening wordy sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise writing to remove redundant words and replace wordy constructions with precise, concise language.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Cut the Fluff</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writing is precise. Wordy sentences say in ten words what could be said in five. Watch me find and cut redundant words without losing meaning.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Due to the fact that it was raining, we decided to stay inside the house.&quot; → &quot;Because it was raining, we stayed inside.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Due to the fact that&quot; = just &quot;because.&quot; &quot;decided to stay&quot; = &quot;stayed.&quot; &quot;inside the house&quot; = &quot;inside&quot; (where else would you stay?). I cut 13 words to 7 — same meaning, cleaner punch. Phrases to watch: &quot;in order to&quot; (→ &quot;to&quot;), &quot;at this point in time&quot; (→ &quot;now&quot;), &quot;on a daily basis&quot; (→ &quot;daily&quot;).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a sentence, ask: can I cut any word without losing meaning? If yes — cut it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Tighten Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite these wordy sentences concisely. Count the words before and after.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The reason why the match was cancelled was due to the fact that the field was too wet.</li><li>At this present moment in time, the students are in the process of completing their assignment.</li><li>She made the decision to walk in the direction of the library in order to return the book.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words are doing real work? Which are filler? What is the minimum word count that keeps full meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence as concisely as possible. Record the word count before and after.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the event that the weather is bad, we will need to make the decision to move the event inside. (_<strong>→</strong>_words)</li><li>He was a person who always showed a great deal of enthusiasm for all the different things that he was interested in. (_<strong>→</strong>_words)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph you've written recently and cut it by 20% — same meaning, fewer words.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for conciseness: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson targets a specific and common revision skill: identifying and cutting wordy constructions that obscure meaning, teaching students that fewer, well-chosen words are almost always stronger than more.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise word choice over circumlocution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Replacing wordy phrases (&quot;made the decision to&quot;) with single precise verbs (&quot;decided&quot;) is a key vocabulary skill at Years 5–6 — it shows students understand that one exact word is more powerful than a cluster of vague ones.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Colons & semicolons – introduction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces a list or explanation; a semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses; both are used deliberately for clarity and effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Powerful Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A colon (:) introduces something — a list, an explanation, or a dramatic reveal. A semicolon (;) joins two closely related complete sentences without a conjunction. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Colon: &quot;She needed three things: courage, patience, and luck.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Semicolon: &quot;The sun set behind the hills; the valley grew cold.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Colon: I use it after a complete sentence when what follows explains or lists. &quot;She had one goal: to finish the race.&quot; Semicolon: Both sides must be complete sentences that are closely linked in meaning. &quot;I love summer; the long evenings are my favourite part.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test the colon: could you put &quot;namely&quot; or &quot;that is&quot; there? Test the semicolon: are both halves complete sentences that relate to each other?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Colon or Semicolon?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each gap needs a colon or semicolon and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm brought everything with it ___ rain, hail, thunder, and a biting wind.</li><li>She loved the city ___ she had lived there all her life.</li><li>He only had one request ___ please don't wake him before 8 o'clock.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does what follows LIST or EXPLAIN? Or are you joining two complete ideas? That tells you which to use.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon or semicolon to each sentence and write a brief reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The menu offered four choices pizza, pasta, salad, and soup.</li><li>The hike was long and exhausting the view from the summit was worth every step.</li><li>She packed everything she would need a torch, a map, a compass, and enough food for two days.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two of your own sentences — one using a colon correctly, one using a semicolon correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons &amp; semicolons: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list, explanation, or quotation. A semicolon joins two independent clauses that are closely related in meaning, without using a conjunction.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are introduced to these higher-level punctuation marks as tools that add clarity and sophistication to their writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Using colons &amp; semicolons for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; progressing beyond basic sentence-end punctuation to more complex marks. This lesson teaches the decision-making process behind each mark: 'Am I introducing something, or joining two related ideas?' — building usage that is intentional rather than guessed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Tense consistency: simple past & present":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): verbs must remain consistent in tense throughout a piece of writing; students identify and correct tense inconsistencies when revising.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Pick a Tense and Stay There</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When you write a story or recount, you choose a tense — usually past or present — and stay in it the whole way through. Switching tenses mid-passage confuses the reader. Watch me spot and fix a tense switch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Past: &quot;The knight rode into the village. He looks around nervously. The villagers gathered in the square.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;rode&quot; — past. &quot;looks&quot; — suddenly present! &quot;gathered&quot; — back to past. The switch makes the reader stumble. Fix: &quot;The knight rode into the village. He looked around nervously. The villagers gathered in the square.&quot; — all past. Or I could rewrite all in present: &quot;The knight rides... He looks... The villagers gather...&quot; — both are valid, just choose one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Circle all your verbs. Are they all the same tense? If not, decide which tense your piece should be in and adjust.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot the Switches</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix all tense inconsistencies in this passage. Rewrite it in consistent simple past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The young inventor enters her workshop at dawn. She had spent months designing the machine. She checks each part carefully and placed the final gear. The machine starts to hum. It worked.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are in the wrong tense? Rewrite in consistent past tense.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite the passage in consistent simple present tense. Then identify and fix the errors.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rewrite in present tense: &quot;The geologist climbed the rocky slope. She examined the layers of sediment. Each layer told a story about the earth's past. She makes careful notes and photographs each formation.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an 8-sentence recount (any topic) in consistent simple past tense. Swap with a partner to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs must remain consistent in tense throughout a piece of writing. Shifts between past and present tense disrupt the reader's experience and signal a lack of control over the writing. Students choose a tense appropriate to text type and purpose and maintain it throughout.&quot; Simple past is standard for recounts and narratives; simple present for procedures and some descriptions.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Auditing tense in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Doing a tense audit — underlining every verb and labelling it P (past) or Pr (present) — is a targeted revision strategy that catches the most common grammar error in Year 5–6 narrative and recount writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences: main + subordinate clause":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a complex sentence contains a main clause and a subordinate clause joined by a subordinating conjunction; students use complex sentences to add detail, show relationships, and vary sentence structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Main Idea + Extra Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A complex sentence has a main clause (complete thought) and a subordinate clause (depends on the main clause for meaning). They are joined by a subordinating conjunction: because, although, when, if, since, while, unless, after, before. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Main clause: &quot;The crowd cheered.&quot; + Subordinating conjunction: &quot;when&quot; + Subordinate clause: &quot;the final whistle blew.&quot; → &quot;The crowd cheered when the final whistle blew.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I can also flip it — subordinate clause first: &quot;When the final whistle blew, the crowd cheered.&quot; Notice: when the subordinate clause comes first, I add a comma after it. When it comes second, no comma needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you start with the subordinate clause, use a comma after it. Starter at the end? No comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Complex Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join these pairs into complex sentences using an appropriate subordinating conjunction. Try putting the subordinate clause at the start in one version and at the end in another.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She missed the bus. She left the house late.</li><li>The crops failed. The summer was unusually dry.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which conjunction fits? Does the meaning change if we swap the clause order? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write complex sentences using the given conjunction. Try one with the subordinate clause first, one with it second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use &quot;although&quot;: ___ (subordinate clause first, with comma)</li><li>Use &quot;because&quot;: ___ (subordinate clause second, no comma)</li><li>Use &quot;unless&quot;: ___ (your choice of order)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find three complex sentences in a book you're reading. Identify the main clause and subordinate clause in each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Complex sentences &amp; subordinate clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A complex sentence contains a main clause and one or more subordinate clauses joined by a subordinating conjunction (e.g. because, although, when, if, since, while, unless). When the subordinate clause is fronted, a comma separates it from the main clause.&quot; Understanding the structure of complex sentences is a key Phase 2 grammar expectation.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using complex sentences for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — including complex sentences — for effect and clarity,&quot; using subordinating conjunctions to express relationships of cause, contrast, condition, and time. This lesson gives students explicit control over clause order and the punctuation that comes with it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking tense consistency in a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a systematic verb audit to identify and correct tense inconsistencies in their own and modelled writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tense Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you a systematic way to check tense in a piece of writing. I underline every verb, then check that they're all in the same tense.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Step 1: Underline all verbs. Step 2: Label each P (past) or Pr (present). Step 3: Decide which tense the piece should be. Step 4: Fix mismatches.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The explorer sets out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reaches the ridge.&quot; — sets (Pr), walked (P), reaches (Pr). Two present, one past. I decide: past tense works better for a recount. Fix: &quot;The explorer set out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reached the ridge.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Be especially careful with &quot;was/is&quot; and &quot;said/says&quot; — these are the most common accidental switches.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Tense Audit Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's audit this passage together. Underline every verb, label its tense, then rewrite it in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market is busy on Saturday mornings. Stalls lined both sides of the street. Vendors called out their prices. The smell of fresh bread drifted through the air. Families stopped to chat and children ran between the stalls. It felt like the whole town was there.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs switch tense? What is the most common tense here? Rewrite consistently.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline all verbs in this passage, label each one P or Pr, then rewrite it in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The diver slips below the surface. The water was cold and very clear. She searches for the wreck that locals told her about. After ten minutes, she spots a shadow on the seabed. She swam closer and felt her heart race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of your own writing and do a full tense audit on it. Note how many switches you find.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic tense audit: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; This lesson teaches the tense audit as a specific, repeatable revision strategy — underline every verb, label the tense, decide on consistency, fix mismatches. It is a direct application of the Phase 2 expectation that students revise systematically, not just casually re-read.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Identifying tense in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs carry tense and must remain consistent throughout a piece of writing.&quot; This editing lesson deepens grammatical knowledge by requiring students to identify verb tense in context — distinguishing simple past, simple present, and past continuous forms — before applying that knowledge to make accurate corrections.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Homophones & commonly confused words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings; students use meaning and grammatical context to select the correct form.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Sound, Different Meaning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings. Spellcheck won't catch these — you have to know them! Watch me use context to choose the right one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: there / their / they're | to / too / two | your / you're</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Put it over <em><strong>.&quot; — &quot;there&quot; (place). &quot;It was </strong></em> idea.&quot; — &quot;their&quot; (belonging to them). &quot;___ late.&quot; — &quot;they're&quot; (they are). I always check: Does a pronoun meaning &quot;they are&quot; fit? → they're. Does &quot;belonging to them&quot; fit? → their. A place or pointing? → there.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Expand contractions to check: &quot;they're&quot; = &quot;they are.&quot; &quot;you're&quot; = &quot;you are.&quot; If the expansion doesn't make sense — use the other spelling.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose Correctly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct homophone for each gap and explain the rule that helped you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em><strong> going to need </strong></em> bring ___ own gear. (they're/their/there | to/too/two)</li><li>I don't know <em><strong> </strong></em> ready yet — ___ bags are still in the hall. (whether/weather | you're/your | they're/their/there)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you expand any of them? What meaning clue helped you choose?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill in the correct homophone. Write the rule you used in brackets.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We packed <em><strong> bags the night before. (their/there/they're) [</strong></em>]</li><li>The <em><strong> yesterday was cold and grey. (weather/whether) [</strong></em>]</li><li>I've read this book <em><strong> many times. (to/too/two) [</strong></em>]</li><li>I can't decide <em><strong> to go or stay. (weather/whether) [</strong></em>]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences of your own, each using a different homophone pair correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Conventions (Knowledge)</em> — Homophones &amp; spelling in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): &quot;Homophones are words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings (e.g. there/their/they're, to/too/two, your/you're). Selecting the correct form requires understanding meaning and grammatical function, as spellcheck cannot distinguish between them.&quot; Students develop the habit of checking homophone choices against context and meaning.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for homophone errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking spelling.&quot; Homophone errors are among the most common and persistent spelling issues at Years 5–6, and this lesson directly addresses them by giving students a meaning-checking strategy (expand the contraction; ask what the word means here) they can apply independently.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Prepositional phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): prepositions show relationships of location, time, and direction; prepositional phrases add precision and detail to sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Prepositions Set the Scene</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A preposition shows a relationship between a noun and another part of the sentence — often location (in, on, under, beside, behind) or time (before, after, during, since). A prepositional phrase is a preposition + its noun phrase. Watch how it adds precision.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The cat slept. → The cat slept on the warm windowsill. → The cat slept on the warm windowsill throughout the afternoon.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;on the warm windowsill&quot; = preposition (on) + noun phrase (the warm windowsill). It tells us WHERE. &quot;throughout the afternoon&quot; = preposition (throughout) + noun phrase (the afternoon). Tells us WHEN. I can stack them for a rich, detailed sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Prepositions are almost always followed by a noun phrase, never a verb.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Phrase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a prepositional phrase (location, time, or both) to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist worked. → ___</li><li>A small fire burned. → ___</li><li>The children played. → ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where is the action happening? When? Can you add both a time and a place phrase?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding at least two prepositional phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man sat.</li><li>A bird sang.</li><li>The storm raged.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 4 sentences about a place you know well, using at least one prepositional phrase in every sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Prepositional phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A preposition shows a relationship between a noun or pronoun and another element of the sentence, typically expressing location (in, on, under, beside), time (before, after, during, since), or direction (towards, through, across). A prepositional phrase consists of a preposition and its noun phrase.&quot; Students learn to identify and use prepositional phrases as a key tool for adding specific detail.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using prepositional phrases for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbial and prepositional phrases to express location, time, and manner.&quot; This lesson teaches students to think of prepositional phrases as scene-setting tools — they answer the reader's question 'but where exactly? but when exactly?' — and to stack them purposefully without overloading the sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Fronted adverbials to vary sentence starts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a fronted adverbial is an adverbial phrase placed at the start of a sentence; it requires a comma after it and provides an effective way to vary sentence openings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Don't Always Start with the Subject</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most sentences start with the subject (The dog / She / The team). But you can start with an adverbial phrase instead — this adds variety and can emphasise time, place, or manner. Watch me move the adverbial to the front.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: &quot;She packed her bag quickly before sunrise.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fronted: &quot;Before sunrise, she packed her bag quickly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Before sunrise&quot; is the fronted adverbial — I moved it to the front and added a comma after it. This is the rule: fronted adverbials need a comma after them. The time/place/manner detail now hits the reader first, setting the scene before the action.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a sentence, ask: could I move the time/place/manner phrase to the front? Try it — see if it sounds stronger.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Move It to the Front</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence with a fronted adverbial. Remember the comma!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team celebrated their victory loudly after the final whistle.</li><li>A vast flock of starlings moved through the valley at dusk.</li><li>She slipped out of the house quietly at midnight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the adverbial phrase? Where does the comma go? Does it change the emphasis?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence with a fronted adverbial, then write two original sentences that begin with a fronted adverbial.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sailors battled the storm through the night.</li><li>He suddenly stopped walking at the edge of the cliff.</li><li>They arrived in the village on a cold November morning.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences. Make at least 3 of them start with a fronted adverbial.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Fronted adverbials: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A fronted adverbial is an adverbial word, phrase, or clause placed before the subject at the start of a sentence. It is followed by a comma. Fronted adverbials can express time, place, manner, or condition and are an effective way to vary sentence openings and create emphasis.&quot; This is an explicitly named feature of Phase 2 sentence structure knowledge.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;vary sentence openings to avoid repetition and create effect.&quot; Fronted adverbials are the most accessible and impactful tool for this — they immediately change the rhythm of a piece and signal a more controlled, deliberate writing style. The associated comma rule gives students a clear, reliable convention to apply.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: adding missing detail to bare sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students revise bare sentences by identifying what detail is missing and adding only what the reader needs to picture the scene clearly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bare Bones vs. Fully Dressed</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A bare sentence gives the fact. A dressed sentence gives the experience. When editing, I look for sentences that tell me very little and add just enough detail to make them come alive. Watch me.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The man walked to the door.&quot; → &quot;The elderly man shuffled to the creaking front door, pausing with his hand on the handle.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I added: WHO more specifically (elderly man), HOW (shuffled), WHICH door (creaking front door), and a bonus action (pausing with his hand). I didn't overwrite — I added what matters. The reader can now see it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask bare sentences: WHO exactly? WHAT kind? HOW exactly? WHICH one? Add only what the reader needs to picture it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Dress the Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, let's add meaningful detail to these bare sentences. Ask WHO? WHAT kind? HOW?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bird flew away.</li><li>She opened the box.</li><li>The car stopped.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What detail would create the most vivid picture? What detail is unnecessary?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare sentence into a fully detailed one. Add detail that serves the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog barked.</li><li>Rain fell.</li><li>He laughed.</li><li>She read the letter.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a scene in 4 bare sentences. Then swap with a partner to dress each other's sentences up.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising bare sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson builds a specific revision instinct: recognising a bare sentence, asking the reader's questions (who exactly? what kind? how?), and adding only the detail that genuinely improves the picture — not padding.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating vivid, detailed writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail that allows the reader to visualise the scene. This lesson directly addresses one of the most common weaknesses in Years 5–6 writing — the bare, unelaborated sentence — and gives students a question framework to fix it independently.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Onomatopoeia & sound words in writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): language devices such as onomatopoeia create specific effects; students select and use sound words deliberately to make writing vivid and engaging.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Let the Words Make Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Onomatopoeia is when a word sounds like the thing it describes: crash, sizzle, whisper, gurgle. Using sound words makes writing come alive — readers don't just see the scene, they hear it. Watch me layer them in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Without: &quot;The kitchen was busy.&quot; → With: &quot;Pots clattered, oil sizzled and somewhere above the noise a smoke alarm began to shriek.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I chose sounds that belong to the kitchen — clattered (pots), sizzled (oil), shriek (alarm). Each sound word tells us something is happening AND what it sounds like. I don't need to say &quot;there was a loud noise&quot; — the word IS the noise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Think: what does this place/event actually sound like? Find the word that IS the sound.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Listen &amp; Write</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's brainstorm sound words for each setting, then build a sentence that uses at least two.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Setting: a beach at high tide</li><li>Setting: a forest just after rain</li><li>Setting: a crowded school cafeteria</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What sounds would you hear? Which onomatopoeia words capture them best?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3 sentences, each using at least one onomatopoeia word. Set each in a different place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 — setting: a thunderstorm.</li><li>Sentence 2 — setting: a building site.</li><li>Sentence 3 — setting: a night in the bush.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence paragraph about any noisy event. Use at least 4 onomatopoeia words, but don't make them feel forced.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Language devices: onomatopoeia: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Language devices such as onomatopoeia, simile, metaphor, and personification can be used deliberately to create specific effects for the reader. Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sounds they describe, making writing more vivid and immediate.&quot; Students learn to recognise onomatopoeia as a deliberate vocabulary choice, not just a novelty.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Using language devices for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use language devices, including onomatopoeia, to create specific effects and engage the reader.&quot; This lesson develops the skill of selecting sound words that are genuinely apt (not forced) — understanding that an effective onomatopoeia word removes the need to describe the sound separately.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Conjunctions: coordinating vs. subordinating":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): coordinating conjunctions join two independent clauses as equals; subordinating conjunctions create a dependent clause; understanding the difference helps students build compound and complex sentences with precision.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Kinds of Joiners</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS: For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So) join two equal, independent clauses. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since, while, unless…) create a dependent clause — one that can't stand alone. Watch the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Coordinating: &quot;She ran fast, but she missed the bus.&quot; | Subordinating: &quot;She missed the bus because she ran too late.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the coordinating example, both halves are complete sentences joined as equals with a comma + &quot;but.&quot; In the subordinating example, &quot;because she ran too late&quot; can't stand alone — it depends on the main clause. The meaning shifts: one shows contrast, one shows cause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: FANBOYS = coordinating (join equals). Because/although/when/if = subordinating (create dependence).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort &amp; Identify</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each conjunction as CO (coordinating) or SUB (subordinating) and explain how it affects meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He trained every day, yet he failed to make the team.</li><li>The game was cancelled because the field was flooded.</li><li>Although the climb was steep, they reached the summit by noon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Could the underlined part stand alone? Does it show a relationship (cause/contrast/condition)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill in the best conjunction and label it CO or SUB.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She wanted to stay home, ___ the trip sounded too exciting to miss. (CO/SUB)</li><li>They could not leave ___ the storm passed. (CO/SUB)</li><li>The museum was old, ___ the exhibits were modern and interactive. (CO/SUB)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences about a challenge you overcame — use two coordinating and two subordinating conjunctions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since, while, unless) introduce a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. Understanding the distinction enables students to build both compound and complex sentences accurately.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Building compound &amp; complex sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson consolidates the distinction between compound sentences (coordinating conjunctions, comma before) and complex sentences (subordinating conjunctions, comma only when fronted) — a key structural knowledge milestone at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Paragraph cohesion – linking sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): cohesive paragraphs connect sentences using pronouns, repeated key ideas, and connectives; students revise disjointed writing to improve flow and coherence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making Paragraphs Flow</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A cohesive paragraph doesn't just put sentences next to each other — it connects them. I'll show three linking techniques: (1) repeating a key word/idea, (2) using a pronoun to refer back, (3) using a connective (however, also, in addition, as a result).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Disconnected: &quot;Honey bees are important. They pollinate flowers. Crops need pollinating. Bees are threatened.&quot; → Cohesive: &quot;Honey bees are vital to our food system. They pollinate the flowers of fruit and vegetable crops, making growth possible. However, bee populations worldwide are in decline, threatening the very harvests they support.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;They&quot; links back to &quot;honey bees.&quot; &quot;However&quot; signals a contrast. Repeating &quot;pollinate/pollinating&quot; echoes the key idea. The paragraph now reads as connected thought, not a list of facts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a paragraph, draw an arrow from each sentence to the one before it. If you can't find a link, add one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Connect the Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph so sentences flow into each other using pronouns, connectives, and repeated ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coral reefs are important ecosystems. Coral reefs have high biodiversity. Many species live in coral reefs. Coral reefs are threatened by warming oceans. Warming oceans cause coral bleaching. Coral bleaching is very damaging.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences can be linked with a pronoun? Where would &quot;however&quot; or &quot;as a result&quot; work?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this disjointed paragraph, using at least three different linking techniques.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ancient library was discovered last year. The library was buried underground. Archaeologists excavated the library. The library contained thousands of scrolls. The scrolls were written in three languages. The scrolls are being studied by experts.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a cohesive 5-sentence paragraph about a natural phenomenon, using at least one of each linking technique.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Cohesion devices within paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Cohesion is created within and across paragraphs through the use of pronouns to refer back to nouns, repeated key words and ideas to maintain focus, and connectives and linking adverbs (however, therefore, as a result) to signal relationships between ideas.&quot; Students at Years 5–6 are expected to understand these devices and apply them deliberately.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Creating cohesive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson builds the skill of reading a paragraph as a connected whole rather than a list of sentences — and teaches students to add the linking tissue that makes their writing flow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying and removing repetition":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise writing to eliminate unnecessary word repetition, using synonyms, pronouns, and sentence restructuring to create variety and impact.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Word, Again and Again</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Repetition drains the energy from writing. When a writer repeats the same word in close proximity, it usually means there's a more precise or interesting word available. Watch me identify and fix it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The game was exciting. It was an exciting match with exciting moments right up to the exciting end.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;exciting&quot; appears four times in two sentences. I fix: &quot;The game was thrilling. It was an intense match with breathtaking moments right up to the dramatic finish.&quot; Four different words, all meaning roughly the same — but now each adds its own shade of meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Use Ctrl+F (find) to search for your most-used words. If any appear more than twice in a paragraph — you have a repetition problem.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spot &amp; Replace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find the repeated words in this passage and replace them with synonyms or restructure the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team walked to the field. The team warmed up on the field. The team captain looked at the other team. The team captain knew the other team was strong but the team was ready.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many times does &quot;team&quot; appear? What are the best replacements? Can you also vary the sentence structure?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by eliminating unnecessary repetition. Use synonyms, pronouns, or restructuring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The mountain was tall. The mountain was covered in snow. The snow on the mountain was deep. The mountain had a steep path. The path up the mountain was dangerous. Climbers who climbed the mountain had to be careful.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Pull out a paragraph from your writing. Highlight every word that appears more than twice. Revise.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for repetition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; Eliminating word repetition is a core revision skill that requires students to read their writing through the reader's eyes — noticing patterns they've become blind to as the writer.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Synonyms &amp; pronoun substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose,&quot; and to &quot;use cohesive devices including pronouns and synonyms.&quot; This lesson directly applies those two strategies in the context of revision — replacing repeated nouns with pronouns and synonyms to create variety, precision, and flow in their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Review: punctuation marks covered in Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): review and consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 1 — full stops, commas, apostrophes, question marks, exclamation marks, colons, and semicolons.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Term 1 Punctuation Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's do a full review of every punctuation mark we've learned this term. I'll show each one in action and remind you of the key rule.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Full stop . | Comma , | Apostrophe ' | Question mark ? | Exclamation mark ! | Colon : | Semicolon ;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each mark has a job: full stop ends sentences. Comma separates list items and follows fronted adverbials. Apostrophe shows possession or contraction. ? asks a question. ! shows strong emotion. Colon introduces. Semicolon joins two related sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For each mark, ask: what job is this doing in this sentence? If it's not doing a job — remove it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct punctuation to each sentence and name the rule.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the game began the captain gathered the team for a final talk</li><li>She packed the following items a torch a sleeping bag warm clothes and extra food</li><li>My brothers bike was left in the rain it was completely rusted by morning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which marks are needed and why? Could any sentence use more than one type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add all missing punctuation to this passage. Name every rule you applied.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>at the end of last term our class held a debate the topic was should schools have longer lunch breaks three teams argued for three argued against some students made excellent points however others needed more evidence after the debate mrs kahu said that everyone had improved</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that uses every Term 1 punctuation mark at least once. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Term 1 punctuation review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): Students consolidate their knowledge of all punctuation covered in Term 1: sentence-end punctuation (. ? !), commas in lists and after fronted adverbials, apostrophes for possession and contraction, and introductory colons and semicolons. Review ensures students can identify the function of each mark, not just recognise its shape.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This consolidation lesson asks students to apply all Term 1 punctuation conventions in context — naming the rule behind each choice — building the metacognitive awareness needed to make punctuation decisions independently in their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: grammar concepts from Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): review and consolidate all word classes and grammar concepts from Term 1 — nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, pronouns, conjunctions, and subject-verb agreement.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Grammar Gallery Tour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every grammar concept from Term 1. I'll use a &quot;grammar gallery&quot; — one concept per station with a mini-example.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Stations: Nouns &amp; proper nouns → Verbs (action/state) → Subject-verb agreement → Adjectives &amp; comparison → Adverbs (how/when/where) → Pronouns → Conjunctions (CO &amp; SUB)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Station 1: Nouns — &quot;The explorer found a map.&quot; (nouns: explorer, map). Station 2: Proper noun — &quot;She found it near Lake Taupo.&quot; Station 3: SVA — &quot;Each explorer has their role.&quot; Station 4: Adjectives — &quot;a tattered, ancient map.&quot; ...and so on through each concept.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Grammar is about understanding why a sentence works, not just labelling. Always connect the rule to the effect on meaning.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Grammar Challenge Quiz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll call out a sentence. You identify the grammar feature I'm asking about. We'll go through all Term 1 concepts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;The exhausted climbers finally reached Base Camp.&quot; → Find: noun, adjective, adverb.</li><li>&quot;Neither of the answers is correct.&quot; → Identify: subject, verb. Is SVA correct?</li><li>&quot;Because the trail was icy, the rescue team moved slowly.&quot; → Label: conjunction type, main clause, subordinate clause.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Hands up for each one — can anyone give a different example using the same grammar feature?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar review tasks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence containing: a proper noun, an action verb, and a fronted adverbial.</li><li>Write a compound sentence using &quot;but&quot; and a complex sentence using &quot;although.&quot;</li><li>Write a sentence with a comparative and one with a superlative adjective.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that demonstrates at least 5 different Term 1 grammar concepts. Label them in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Term 1 grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Students consolidate their understanding of all word classes taught in Term 1: nouns (common, proper, collective, abstract), verbs (action, state, tense), adjectives (comparative, superlative), adverbs (manner, time, place), pronouns (personal, possessive), and conjunctions (coordinating, subordinating). They also review subject-verb agreement across a range of sentence types.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying grammar knowledge in sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of word classes and structures.&quot; This review lesson asks students to demonstrate that knowledge by combining multiple features in single sentences — moving from isolated recognition to integrated, purposeful use.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Review: sentence types practised in Term 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): review and apply the full range of sentence structures from Term 1 — simple, expanded, compound, complex, and fronted adverbial sentences — for effect and variety.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Our Sentence Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we built quite a sentence toolkit. Let me show you every type we practised and how to combine them for maximum effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Simple | Simple + adjectives | Compound (FANBOYS) | Complex (subordinate clause) | Fronted adverbial | Who/where/when expanded</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Simple: &quot;The storm broke.&quot; | + adjectives: &quot;The fierce, sudden storm broke.&quot; | Compound: &quot;The storm broke, and the river rose rapidly.&quot; | Complex: &quot;Although residents had been warned, few expected the storm to be this severe.&quot; | Fronted: &quot;Just before dawn, the storm broke over the city.&quot; | Expanded: &quot;The storm, which had been building for three days, broke over the city just before dawn.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The best writing mixes ALL these sentence types. No single type should dominate.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sentence Type Auction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll read out sentences. You identify the type and explain how you know. Then we'll vote on which version of the same idea works best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Version A: &quot;She won.&quot; vs Version B: &quot;After months of training, she finally won the regional championship.&quot; vs Version C: &quot;She won, but the victory felt hollow.&quot;</li><li>&quot;Despite the rain, the festival continued.&quot; — what type? What's the effect?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why might a writer choose Version A over B? When is shorter more powerful?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence of each type listed below, all on the same topic (the ocean).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Simple sentence.</li><li>2. Simple sentence with 2 adjectives.</li><li>3. Compound sentence (use &quot;yet&quot;).</li><li>4. Complex sentence (start with &quot;Although&quot;).</li><li>5. Fronted adverbial sentence.</li><li>6. Expanded sentence with who/where/when.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Arrange your 6 sentences into a mini-paragraph that flows naturally.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence variety: full toolkit: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity,&quot; and to &quot;vary sentence openings&quot; to avoid repetition. This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate control over the full range of sentence types covered in Term 1, and to make deliberate choices about which type serves each moment in their writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Combining sentence types for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling rhythm, pace, and emphasis through sentence variety. This lesson reinforces that effective writing is not about using any one sentence type — it is about choosing the right structure for the right moment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: full-passage edit using Term 1 checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply all Term 1 editing skills in a systematic multi-pass edit, using the full Term 1 checklist to revise a passage for accuracy, vocabulary, and structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'm going to do a complete multi-pass edit on this passage using our full Term 1 checklist. Watch how I tackle one aspect at a time rather than trying to fix everything at once.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Pass 1: Capitals &amp; full stops | Pass 2: Comma usage | Pass 3: Apostrophes | Pass 4: Word choice (vague words) | Pass 5: Sentence variety | Pass 6: Repetition</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once to understand it. Then Pass 1: I check every sentence begins with a capital and ends correctly. Pass 2: I look for lists and compound sentences — are commas right? Pass 3: Any apostrophes? Are they possession or contraction? Pass 4: I highlight &quot;nice,&quot; &quot;good,&quot; &quot;went,&quot; &quot;got&quot; — can I upgrade any? Pass 5: Are all sentences the same length? Pass 6: Any repeated words?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Multi-pass editing catches more than single-pass. Each pass has a specific focus — you're not trying to see everything at once.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Working through each pass together, let's edit this full passage as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last week my class went on a trip to the botanical gardens in the park. it was a nice day and everyone was excited. we walked around and saw lots of nice plants and flowers. our teacher mrs patel said that the gardens had been there for over one hundred years and that they had lots of rare plants from all over the world. we saw carnivorous plants cacti roses sunflowers and many types of ferns. the carnivorous plants were good and everyone wanted to feed them. after the trip we all wrote about our favourite plants in our science books</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass by pass — what are we fixing? Count the improvements at the end.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage using all six passes of the Term 1 checklist. Rewrite the improved version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on friday our school had a sports day the weather was nice so everyone went outside there were lots of different events like running jumping throwing and relay races my friend noah ran really fast and he won his race we all cheered for him. i came second in the long jump which was good. at the end mrs kahu gave out certificates and everyone got a good certificate for trying hard we went home tired but happy</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the same 6-pass edit to a paragraph from your writing journal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Multi-pass editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson makes the revision process systematic and visible — a structured, multi-pass approach that builds the habits of a careful, independent editor. Students apply every skill from Term 1 in a single, integrated editing task.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Term 1 skills: integrated application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate mastery of all Term 1 writing skills in one editing task: punctuation conventions, vocabulary precision, sentence variety, and paragraph cohesion. It directly mirrors the Phase 2 expectation that students &quot;apply writing knowledge and skills across a range of text types and purposes&quot; with increasing independence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tier 2 vocabulary: words for movement & action":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): precise verbs eliminate vague movement words; students select verbs that convey manner and quality of movement, reducing reliance on weak constructions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Swap the Vague Movement Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch me swap plain movement verbs for precise ones that carry more information.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The man went down the street.&quot; → &quot;The man strode down the street.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Went → strode, shuffled, sprinted, lurched. Each verb tells us HOW as well as that movement happened. &quot;Strode&quot; tells us purposeful, confident movement. &quot;Shuffled&quot; tells us tired or reluctant movement. One word replaces a verb + adverb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Precise verbs eliminate the need for adverbs: &quot;ran quickly&quot; → &quot;sprinted.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upgrade the underlined verbs with more precise movement words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bird went through the air.</li><li>She moved across the dance floor.</li><li>He went into the room.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does the precise verb tell us that &quot;went&quot; does not?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace each plain verb with the most precise one you can think of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The snake went through the grass.</li><li>The toddler moved towards the door.</li><li>She went across the rooftops.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences each using a different precise movement verb. No two the same.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Precise verbs for movement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the precision and connotations of words.&quot; Selecting a precise movement verb (strode, shuffled, lurched) rather than a generic one (went, moved) demonstrates Tier 2 vocabulary development — a core expectation at Years 5–6. Each verb carries manner, intent, and character without requiring additional adverbs.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting verbs fit for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of interrogating verb choices — asking not just 'what is happening?' but 'exactly how is it happening?' — and finding the single word that answers both questions simultaneously.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nouns: collective & abstract nouns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): nouns include common, proper, collective, and abstract types; understanding the full range of noun classes develops students' grammatical knowledge and expands their writing vocabulary.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Special Noun Types</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Collective nouns name a group. Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings, or concepts you cannot touch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Collective: a flock of birds | Abstract: courage, freedom, justice</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Flock, swarm, pride, pod — these are collective. Justice, hope, anger — these are abstract. You can't hold justice in your hand, but it's a noun because it names a concept.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you can't touch it or see it, but it's a thing — it's probably abstract.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort the Nouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort these nouns into collective, abstract, or concrete.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a herd of cattle</li><li>curiosity</li><li>a bouquet of flowers</li><li>loyalty</li><li>a school of fish</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How do you know? Could you take a photo of it? Touch it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each noun: collective (C), abstract (A), or concrete (Co).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a murder of crows —</li><li>envy —</li><li>a packet of biscuits —</li><li>a pride of lions —</li><li>determination —</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a sentence for each type using a noun from today's lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Word classes: collective &amp; abstract nouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Words belong to different classes… nouns include common, proper, collective (naming a group), and abstract (naming a concept, idea, or emotion) types.&quot; Understanding the full range of noun classes deepens grammatical awareness and expands the vocabulary available to students when composing at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using abstract nouns for depth: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using abstract nouns deliberately — courage, justice, determination — is a hallmark of sophisticated writing at Phase 2, allowing students to write about ideas and values, not just concrete events.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Simple sentences: revisit with action-verb focus":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students revisit simple sentence structure with a focus on replacing weak verb constructions (do/make/give + noun) with single powerful action verbs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Strong Verb Beats Three Weak Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Revisiting simple sentences — but this time the focus is on choosing a powerful action verb over a weak &quot;do + noun&quot; construction.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: &quot;The volcano did an explosion.&quot; → Strong: &quot;The volcano erupted.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Do + noun&quot; is often the weak version. Replace with a direct action verb: made a decision → decided. Gave a performance → performed. Did an investigation → investigated. Each replacement tightens the sentence and strengthens the verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Did&quot;, &quot;made&quot;, &quot;gave&quot;, &quot;had&quot; + noun = usually replaceable with one strong verb.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find the Strong Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the weak &quot;do + noun&quot; construction with one strong verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She made a complaint about the noise.</li><li>They did a celebration when they won.</li><li>He gave a performance that amazed the crowd.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What single verb can replace each phrase?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using one strong action verb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She made a decision to leave early.</li><li>The team gave a demonstration of the new skill.</li><li>They did an investigation into the disappearance.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 simple sentences using 5 different powerful action verbs.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Strong action verbs in simple sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences&quot; and to select verbs that convey meaning precisely. Replacing weak &quot;do/make/give + noun&quot; constructions with single powerful action verbs is a key revision skill — it tightens sentence structure and removes redundant words simultaneously.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise verb selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson reinforces that one exact verb (erupted, decided, investigated) is more powerful and precise than a vague verb + noun combination — a foundational principle of effective word choice at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Proofreading: capitals for proper nouns in context":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students apply capitalisation rules for proper nouns in complex, multi-sentence text, developing accuracy and consistency in editing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Proper Nouns in Longer Text</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this term's editing focus, we look specifically at proper noun capitals in longer, more complex text.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;last tuesday, ms brown took class 5b to the otago museum in dunedin.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fixed: &quot;Last Tuesday, Ms Brown took Class 5B to the Otago Museum in Dunedin.&quot; Every specific name, title, day, place, and class identifier needs a capital.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Scan specifically for: days, months, place names, people's names/titles, specific event names.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Capital Letter Hunt</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and fix all the proper noun capitalisation errors.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on anzac day, uncle mike and aunt sarah drove to hamilton for the dawn parade at memorial park.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: List every word that needs a capital. How many did you find?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage with all proper noun capitals correctly placed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my cousin riya is visiting from tauranga next friday. she goes to otumoetai college and is in year 10. on saturday we're going to visit the national aquarium of new zealand in napier. riya wants to see the tuatara display and the kiwi house afterwards we might have lunch at the hawke's bay farmers market.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a real or imaginary trip. Include at least 6 proper nouns — all correctly capitalised.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Capitalisation: proper nouns in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Capital letters are used at the start of sentences and for all proper nouns — names of specific people, places, days, months, titles, organisations, and events.&quot; At Phase 2, students are expected to apply this convention consistently across longer, more complex text where proper nouns appear frequently and in varied contexts.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for capitalisation accuracy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct capitalisation.&quot; This lesson trains the targeted scanning strategy — reading specifically for proper nouns — that helps students catch capitalisation errors they overlook in a general read-through.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Commas after fronted adverbials":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a fronted adverbial is followed by a comma; students apply this convention accurately and understand why the comma signals a pause before the main clause.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Opener Comma</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When a sentence starts with an adverbial phrase or clause (a fronted adverbial), we put a comma after it before the main clause begins.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;After the long match, the players collapsed on the grass.&quot; | &quot;During the school holidays, we visited Rotorua.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;After the long match&quot; is the fronted adverbial — it sets the scene. The comma after &quot;match&quot; separates it from the main clause. Without the comma, the reader rushes past the opener without pausing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read aloud. If you naturally pause after the opening phrase before the subject arrives — that pause is your comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Comma</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These sentences have fronted adverbials but are missing the comma. Find the opener and add the comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the sun had risen the fishing boats left the harbour.</li><li>On the last day of term the students performed their assembly item.</li><li>Despite the heavy rain the match went ahead as planned.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does the fronted adverbial end and the main clause begin? That's where the comma goes.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the missing comma to each sentence and write the type of fronted adverbial (time/place/contrast).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Throughout the long journey south the waka stayed on course.</li><li>Although the trail was steep the tamariki kept climbing.</li><li>By the time the tide turned the divers were already back on shore.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences that each begin with a different type of fronted adverbial. Use at least one time, one place, and one contrast opener.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Comma after fronted adverbials: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;When an adverbial word, phrase, or clause is placed before the subject at the start of a sentence (a fronted adverbial), a comma is used to separate it from the main clause.&quot; This is a specific and frequently tested punctuation convention at Years 5–6, appearing across narrative, recount, and persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying fronted adverbial comma: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; The comma after a fronted adverbial is both a convention (accuracy) and a craft tool (effect) — it signals a deliberate pause that gives the opener its weight. This lesson builds the read-aloud checking strategy: the natural pause reveals where the comma belongs.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Future tense: will & going to":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): the future tense is expressed using &quot;will&quot; or &quot;going to&quot;; the choice between them carries different shades of meaning — spontaneous decisions vs planned intentions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Ways to Talk About the Future</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>English expresses future time in two main ways: &quot;will&quot; + verb, and &quot;going to&quot; + verb. They are not always interchangeable. Watch me use each one and explain the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;I will help you with that.&quot; (spontaneous decision) | &quot;We are going to plant native trees next month.&quot; (planned intention)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Will&quot; is used for spontaneous decisions, offers, predictions, and promises made at the moment of speaking. &quot;Going to&quot; is used for plans already decided and for predictions based on current evidence. &quot;Look at those clouds — it's going to rain.&quot; (evidence right there).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Planned ahead → going to. Decided right now → will. Evidence in front of you → going to.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Will or Going To?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose &quot;will&quot; or &quot;going to&quot; and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The marae committee ___ (will/is going to) hold a hui next Saturday — it's been in the calendar for months.</li><li>&quot;I can't carry that box.&quot; &quot;Don't worry, I ___ (will/am going to) help you.&quot;</li><li>Look at those storm clouds — the weather ___ (will/is going to) turn soon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a plan, a spontaneous offer, or a prediction based on evidence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the correct future form. Write a brief reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The school ___ (will/is going to) hold a matariki celebration — we've been practising for weeks.</li><li>&quot;The printer is broken.&quot; &quot;I ___ (will/am going to) fix it.&quot; (spontaneous offer)</li><li>That kūmara plant ___ (will/is going to) need water soon — the soil is bone dry.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a future event you're genuinely planning. Use &quot;going to&quot; for plans and &quot;will&quot; for predictions or promises within it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Future tense: will &amp; going to: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;The future tense in English is expressed using 'will' + verb (for spontaneous decisions, offers, and promises) or 'going to' + verb (for pre-planned intentions and predictions based on present evidence). Understanding the distinction allows writers to express future time with precision rather than using the two forms interchangeably.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using future tense accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences&quot; using a range of tenses accurately. Choosing between &quot;will&quot; and &quot;going to&quot; is a nuanced grammar decision that requires students to consider the context and intended meaning — a marker of grammatical sophistication at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences: new contexts & conjunctions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students use FANBOYS conjunctions in new writing contexts — recount, report, and argument — understanding that the same conjunction carries different weight depending on purpose and audience.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — FANBOYS in Fresh Contexts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We know FANBOYS conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so). This week we use them in new writing contexts to see how the same conjunction does different jobs.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Recount: &quot;We arrived at Tāmaki Makaurau, and the whole whānau was waiting.&quot; | Argument: &quot;The policy is popular, yet the evidence does not support it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;And&quot; adds. &quot;But/yet&quot; contrasts. &quot;So&quot; shows result. &quot;Or&quot; gives alternatives. &quot;For&quot; gives reason (formal). &quot;Nor&quot; adds a negative. The conjunction you choose shapes the relationship between the two ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before picking a conjunction, decide: am I adding, contrasting, showing a result, or giving a reason? That decides which FANBOYS fits.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right FANBOYS</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair of sentences with the best FANBOYS conjunction. Add a comma before the conjunction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The hīkoi had been going for three days. The marchers showed no sign of stopping.</li><li>You can present your findings as a poster. You can create a short video.</li><li>The experiment failed the first time. The team learnt something valuable from it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the relationship between the two ideas? That decides the conjunction.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a compound sentence for each context using the conjunction given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write about a kaitiakitanga project using &quot;so.&quot;</li><li>Write about a time something went wrong using &quot;yet.&quot;</li><li>Write a sentence giving someone two choices using &quot;or.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about an environmental issue. Use at least three different FANBOYS conjunctions. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — FANBOYS in varied contexts: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight. Each conjunction signals a different relationship: addition (and), contrast (but/yet), result (so), alternative (or), reason (for), negative addition (nor).&quot; Students learn that the same conjunction carries different register and weight depending on text type and context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Compound sentences across text types: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity&quot; across different text types. This lesson extends compound sentence use beyond narrative into recount, report, and argument — building students' ability to select the right conjunction for the relationship and context, not just the first one that fits grammatically.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: fixing comma splices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): a comma splice joins two independent clauses with only a comma; students identify comma splices and correct them using a full stop, semicolon, or conjunction.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Comma That Tries to Do Too Much</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A comma splice is when two complete sentences are joined with just a comma. A comma is not strong enough to hold two independent clauses together on its own. Watch me find and fix one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✗ &quot;The waka was ready, the crew climbed aboard.&quot; → ✓ &quot;The waka was ready. The crew climbed aboard.&quot; OR ✓ &quot;The waka was ready, and the crew climbed aboard.&quot; OR ✓ &quot;The waka was ready; the crew climbed aboard.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three fixes: (1) full stop — two separate sentences. (2) comma + conjunction — compound sentence. (3) semicolon — joins two closely related sentences. All three are correct. Choose based on the relationship between the ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: read each side of the comma. If both could stand alone as sentences — the comma is a splice. Fix it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Splice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find the comma splice in each sentence and fix it three ways.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tide was coming in, we had to leave quickly.</li><li>She had been training for months, the race was finally here.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which fix sounds best for each sentence? Does the relationship between the ideas affect your choice?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix each comma splice using the method shown in brackets.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kākāpō is critically endangered, conservation efforts have helped increase numbers. [semicolon]</li><li>The hail hammered the roof, everyone moved away from the windows. [conjunction]</li><li>She had practised the speech a hundred times, she still felt her hands shake. [full stop]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 3 pairs of sentences — then deliberately create a comma splice, and fix it in two different ways each time.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A comma splice occurs when two independent clauses are joined with only a comma, which is insufficient punctuation. It can be corrected by: (1) replacing the comma with a full stop; (2) adding a coordinating conjunction after the comma; or (3) replacing the comma with a semicolon.&quot; Understanding comma splices deepens students' knowledge of sentence boundaries and punctuation function.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Identifying and correcting comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Recognising and fixing comma splices is a targeted editing skill that requires students to identify sentence boundaries and select the punctuation or conjunction that best expresses the relationship between the two clauses.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Antonyms & shades of meaning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): antonyms are words with opposite meanings; understanding the spectrum between antonyms develops nuanced vocabulary and helps students select words with the right shade of meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Meaning Spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Antonyms are opposites — but between most opposites there's a whole spectrum of words. Understanding that spectrum lets you choose exactly the right word for your meaning.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: hot ←——————————————→ cold</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>scalding / burning / warm / tepid / cool / chilly / freezing / arctic</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Hot&quot; and &quot;cold&quot; are antonyms, but the spectrum between them has eight gradations. &quot;The water was cold&quot; is vague. &quot;The water was arctic&quot; is precise. Choosing from the spectrum — rather than defaulting to the antonym — gives writing its precision and texture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before writing a describing word, ask: where on the spectrum between the two extremes does this fall? Then find the word that sits exactly there.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build meaning spectrums for each pair of antonyms. Aim for at least five words between the two extremes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy ←—————————→ sad</li><li>loud ←—————————→ quiet</li><li>fast ←—————————→ slow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which word on the spectrum best describes: a library, a sports crowd, a funeral? Why?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the word from the spectrum that best fits each context. Explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man's voice was ___ (choose from: booming / loud / clear / soft / faint / inaudible) as he told the story.</li><li>After the marathon, the runner felt ___ (choose from: exhausted / tired / weary / drained / spent / destroyed).</li><li>The stream moved ___ (choose from: rushing / flowing / trickling / seeping / still) through the valley.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one antonym pair. Build a spectrum of 8 words. Then use three of them in three separate sentences about the same setting.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Antonyms &amp; meaning spectrums: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;The meaning of words can be extended or changed by context; vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect, so students consider the connotations and precision of words.&quot; Understanding antonyms and the spectrum of meaning between them is a key component of Tier 2 vocabulary development — it moves students beyond binary word choice (hot/cold) to nuanced selection along a continuum.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including subject-specific vocabulary, that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson develops the habit of asking 'exactly where on the spectrum does this fall?' before committing to a word — the key discipline of precise vocabulary selection.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Subject-verb agreement: tricky cases (everyone, each)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): indefinite pronouns such as everyone, each, neither, and someone are grammatically singular and require singular verb forms, even when they refer to multiple people.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tricky Singular Words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most subject-verb agreement is straightforward. But some words look plural and feel plural yet are grammatically singular.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Everyone in the two classes was invited.&quot; (not &quot;were&quot;) | &quot;Each of the players has a number.&quot; (not &quot;have&quot;)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Everyone,&quot; &quot;everybody,&quot; &quot;someone,&quot; &quot;nobody,&quot; &quot;each,&quot; &quot;either,&quot; &quot;neither&quot; — all singular, even when they refer to many people. The trick: mentally replace with &quot;every single person&quot; — then the singular verb feels natural.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Everyone/each/either/neither/nobody = singular. Replace with &quot;every single one&quot; in your head — the right verb follows naturally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the correct verb form for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everyone in the kapa haka group ___ (know/knows) the words.</li><li>Each of the entries ___ (was/were) judged on three criteria.</li><li>Neither of the referees ___ (was/were) happy with the decision.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Replace the subject with &quot;every single one&quot; — which verb sounds right now?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Correct the subject-verb agreement error in each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everyone in the syndicate have submitted their work.</li><li>Each of the kūmara plants were carefully watered.</li><li>Neither of the answers are correct.</li><li>Somebody have left their bag on the mat.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences, each using one of these subjects: everyone, each, neither, somebody, anyone. Make sure agreement is correct in every one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — SVA: indefinite pronouns: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Indefinite pronouns (everyone, everybody, someone, nobody, each, either, neither) are grammatically singular and require singular verb forms, even when they refer to multiple people or things. Errors in agreement with these pronouns are among the most common grammar mistakes at Years 5–6.&quot; Students learn to override the &quot;feels plural&quot; instinct by testing with &quot;every single one.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for tricky SVA: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; Identifying and correcting tricky subject-verb agreement errors — particularly with indefinite pronouns — requires students to understand the grammatical subject of the clause, not just the nearest noun, and is a key editing skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding sentences using \"when\" and \"because\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students expand simple sentences by adding subordinate clauses using &quot;when&quot; and &quot;because&quot; to show time relationships and causation.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Add the Why and the When</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Two of the most useful subordinating conjunctions for expanding sentences are &quot;when&quot; (time) and &quot;because&quot; (cause/reason). Watch me use them to transform bare sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: &quot;The crowd cheered.&quot; → + when: &quot;The crowd cheered when the final whistle blew.&quot; → + because: &quot;The crowd cheered because the home team had just equalised.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;When&quot; tells us the time — the moment the action happened. &quot;Because&quot; tells us the cause — why it happened. Each adds a whole new layer of meaning to the same simple sentence. I can also put the &quot;when/because&quot; clause first: &quot;When the final whistle blew, the crowd cheered.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;When&quot; answers: at what moment? &quot;Because&quot; answers: for what reason? Add the one your sentence is missing.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence by adding a &quot;when&quot; clause AND a &quot;because&quot; clause. Try both.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pohutukawa bloomed.</li><li>The students fell silent.</li><li>The explorer turned back.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which expansion is more interesting for each sentence — the when or the because? Why?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each sentence with the conjunction shown. Try putting the clause at the start AND at the end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The river flooded. [because]</li><li>The kiwi came out. [when]</li><li>She changed her mind. [because]</li><li>The lights went out. [when]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences. Use &quot;when&quot; in at least two and &quot;because&quot; in at least two. Vary whether the clause comes first or last.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Expanding with &quot;when&quot; &amp; &quot;because&quot;: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand simple sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description,&quot; including using subordinating conjunctions to express time (when) and cause (because). This lesson revisits and deepens Term 1 complex sentence work in a fresh context, reinforcing that clause order is flexible and that the fronted version requires a comma.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subordinating conjunctions: time &amp; cause: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;'When' is a subordinating conjunction that introduces a clause expressing time; 'because' introduces a clause expressing cause or reason. Both create complex sentences where one clause depends on the other for full meaning.&quot; Understanding the semantic difference between these two conjunctions helps students choose the right one for the relationship they want to express.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: upgrading bland verbs in a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students identify weak, overused verbs in a passage and replace them with precise verbs that carry meaning, character, and energy.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bland Verbs Drain Energy</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some verbs are so vague they tell us almost nothing: went, got, said, did, made, was. Watch me identify them in a passage and replace them with verbs that do real work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The children went to the beach. They got into the water. Dad said they should come out.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;went&quot; → &quot;raced&quot; | &quot;got&quot; → &quot;plunged&quot; | &quot;said&quot; → &quot;bellowed&quot;. New version: &quot;The children raced to the beach. They plunged into the water. Dad bellowed at them to come out.&quot; The scene comes alive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight every &quot;went,&quot; &quot;got,&quot; &quot;said,&quot; &quot;did,&quot; &quot;made,&quot; &quot;was&quot; in a passage. Each one is an upgrade opportunity.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace every underlined bland verb with a more precise one. The new verb must match the context.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kererū went from branch to branch, getting the ripest berries. It made a loud noise with its wings as it went away.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is actually happening physically? What does a precise verb tell the reader that &quot;went&quot; and &quot;got&quot; don't?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage, upgrading every bland verb you can find.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On field trip day, the class got on the bus early. They went to the wetland reserve near Ōtaki. The guide said something about the different birds. Some students went into the hide and got photos of a bittern. Everyone said it was great.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many bland verbs you replaced. Write the before and after side by side. Which passage is more engaging and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Upgrading bland verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most common verb weakness in Years 5–6 writing — overuse of generic verbs (went, got, said) — and builds the habit of asking 'what is ACTUALLY happening?' before selecting a more precise alternative.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Verb audit in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; The verb audit — highlighting every weak verb and asking if it can be upgraded — is a targeted revision strategy that directly improves the energy and precision of students' writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Apostrophes for contraction":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a contraction apostrophe replaces omitted letters when two words are joined; students apply contraction apostrophes accurately and distinguish them from possessive apostrophes.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Apostrophe That Replaces Letters</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A contraction apostrophe shows where one or more letters have been removed when two words are joined together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;do not&quot; → &quot;don't&quot; (the apostrophe replaces the 'o') | &quot;they are&quot; → &quot;they're&quot; | &quot;I would&quot; → &quot;I'd&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;cannot&quot; → &quot;can't&quot; — the apostrophe replaces &quot;no&quot;. &quot;would not&quot; → &quot;wouldn't&quot; — replaces &quot;o&quot;. &quot;she is&quot; → &quot;she's&quot; — replaces &quot;i&quot;. The apostrophe sits exactly where the missing letters were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The apostrophe goes WHERE THE LETTERS WERE REMOVED — not just anywhere between the words.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Contract and Expand</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the contraction for each pair of words, and expand each contraction back to the full form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>we will → <em><strong>  |  it's → </strong></em></li><li>have not → <em><strong>  |  they'd → </strong></em></li><li>I am → <em><strong>  |  couldn't → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Exactly which letters did the apostrophe replace? Could you be confused between &quot;it's&quot; (it is) and &quot;its&quot; (belonging to it)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix the apostrophe errors in these sentences — some are missing, some are in the wrong place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sh'es not sure if theyre coming to the hangi tonight.</li><li>I wo'nt be ready until the bus ha's already left.</li><li>Weve been waiting here for ages — it isnt fair.</li><li>They're dog isnt allowed inside but its a cold night.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a conversation (6 lines of dialogue) between two friends making plans. Use at least 6 different contractions correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Contraction apostrophes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A contraction apostrophe replaces the letter or letters omitted when two words are combined (e.g. do not → don't, they are → they're, I would → I'd). The apostrophe is placed exactly where the letters were removed.&quot; Students also practise distinguishing contraction apostrophes from possessive apostrophes — a critical and frequently confused distinction at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying contraction apostrophes accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including apostrophes for contraction. The key test — expanding the contraction to check whether the apostrophe placement makes sense — gives students a reliable self-checking strategy they can apply independently when editing their own writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Relative pronouns: who, which, that":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): relative pronouns (who, which, that) introduce relative clauses that give additional information about a noun; the choice of pronoun and use of commas depends on whether the clause defines or adds extra information.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Clauses That Give Extra Information</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A relative clause is a type of subordinate clause that gives more information about a noun. It's introduced by a relative pronoun: &quot;who&quot; (for people), &quot;which&quot; (for things), &quot;that&quot; (for people or things in defining clauses).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The woman who won the award is our neighbour.&quot; | &quot;The bridge, which was built in 1902, is being restored.&quot; | &quot;The book that she recommended was brilliant.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;who&quot; → refers back to a person. &quot;which&quot; → refers back to a thing, adds non-essential info (use commas): &quot;The river, which floods every winter, is rising again.&quot; &quot;that&quot; → defines which one (no commas): &quot;The path that leads to the pā is steep.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Who&quot; = people. &quot;Which&quot; = things + commas (extra info). &quot;That&quot; = defines which one, no commas.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Relative Pronoun</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with who, which, or that.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rangatira ___ led the iwi was known for her wisdom.</li><li>The old pōhutukawa, ___ had stood for 300 years, was finally protected.</li><li>This is the exact track ___ leads to the summit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the clause define which one, or add extra information? Does it refer to a person or a thing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a relative clause to each sentence using the pronoun given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist ___ (who) discovered the new species published her findings last month.</li><li>Rotorua, ___ (which) is famous for its geothermal activity, attracts tourists year-round.</li><li>She finally found the notebook ___ (that) she had been searching for all morning.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about a local place or person. Include at least three relative clauses — one with who, one with which (with commas), and one with that.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Relative pronouns &amp; relative clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Relative pronouns (who, which, that) introduce relative clauses that modify a noun. 'Who' refers to people; 'which' refers to things and introduces non-defining (parenthetical) clauses set off with commas; 'that' introduces defining clauses with no commas.&quot; Mastering this distinction is a key grammar milestone at Phase 2, with direct implications for comma use.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using relative clauses for detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding clauses to add detail and description.&quot; Relative clauses are a sophisticated tool for embedding additional information about a noun within a sentence — avoiding the need for a separate sentence and creating more fluent, complex writing at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences for persuasive paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): persuasive topic sentences state a clear position or claim; students write topic sentences that introduce the paragraph argument and signal the writer's stance.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Topic Sentences That Argue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 we wrote topic sentences for informational paragraphs. This week, topic sentences carry an opinion or argument — they tell the reader not just what the paragraph is about, but what position you're taking.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informational: &quot;Recycling reduces waste sent to landfill.&quot; | Persuasive: &quot;Recycling is not enough — we must stop producing unnecessary plastic altogether.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The persuasive topic sentence does three jobs: (1) introduces the paragraph's main idea, (2) makes the writer's position clear, (3) makes the reader want to know the evidence. &quot;Everyone deserves access to clean water&quot; is both a topic sentence AND an argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: A strong persuasive topic sentence takes a clear position. If someone could disagree with it — it's persuasive. If it's just a fact — it's informational.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade to Persuasive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Turn each informational topic sentence into a persuasive one by adding a position or claim.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Smartphones are used by many young people.</li><li>Schools have canteens that sell food to students.</li><li>Exercise has effects on health.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What position could a writer take on each topic? Add the opinion to make it genuinely persuasive.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a persuasive topic sentence for each argument prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: whether students should have homework.</li><li>Topic: whether animals should be kept in zoos.</li><li>Topic: whether Aotearoa should have a four-day school week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one topic and write the full paragraph — topic sentence + at least two pieces of evidence or reasoning.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Persuasive topic sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;In persuasive writing, each paragraph is organised around a claim or position introduced in the topic sentence. A persuasive topic sentence makes the writer's stance clear and invites the reader to consider the supporting evidence that follows.&quot; Students learn that a topic sentence in argument writing must be contestable — if no one could disagree with it, it is not yet persuasive.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring persuasive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail&quot; across a range of text types including persuasion. This lesson builds the skill of leading with a clear position before providing evidence — a structural discipline that is foundational to effective persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: removing unnecessary apostrophes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): apostrophes are not used to form plurals; students identify and remove apostrophes used incorrectly in plural nouns.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Apostrophe That Shouldn't Be There</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One of the most common apostrophe errors is using one to make a word plural. Apostrophes are NEVER used for plurals — only for possession and contraction. Watch me find and fix the errors.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✗ &quot;We have three cat's and two dog's.&quot; → ✓ &quot;We have three cats and two dogs.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Cat's&quot; — is the cat possessing something? No. Is it a contraction? No. So no apostrophe! &quot;Dog's&quot; — same test, same result. The simple rule: to make a word plural, just add -s or -es. Never add an apostrophe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before adding an apostrophe, ask: is this possession or contraction? If neither — no apostrophe.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find and Remove</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remove every incorrect apostrophe from this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At the market we bought apple's, pear's, and two kind's of cheese. The stall's were busy all morning and the seller's were friendly. By noon, all the basket's were empty.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which apostrophes are wrong? How do you know? What test did you apply?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, correcting all apostrophe errors. Some sentences may have no error.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team's jerseys were hanging on hook's in the changing room.</li><li>Both runner's crossed the line within second's of each other.</li><li>The children's laughter echoed across the playground.</li><li>Three dog's barked at the cat's on the fence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 5 sentences that correctly use apostrophes for possession, contraction, and plural — making sure plurals never have apostrophes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Apostrophes: not for plurals: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Apostrophes are used to show possession and to mark omitted letters in contractions. They are never used to form plurals. The 'greengrocer's apostrophe' (apple's, pear's) is one of the most common punctuation errors in English.&quot; Students develop the two-question test: Is this possession? Is this a contraction? If neither — no apostrophe.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for apostrophe errors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking punctuation.&quot; This lesson builds a targeted apostrophe audit: applying the possession/contraction test to every apostrophe in a passage and removing those that fail both tests — a practical and reliable editing strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Brackets & dashes for parenthetical information":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): brackets and dashes are used to add parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence; students choose between them based on the degree of emphasis and formality required.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Aside in a Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sometimes we want to add extra information inside a sentence without breaking its flow. Brackets and dashes both do this job — but with different emphasis. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Brackets: &quot;The tūī (a native honeyeater) feeds on kōwhai nectar.&quot; | Dashes: &quot;The tūī — our boldest garden visitor — feeds on kōwhai nectar.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Brackets add a quiet aside — the reader can almost skip it. Dashes shout for attention — they make the parenthetical feel dramatic or important. Use brackets for factual clarifications. Use dashes when you want the reader to pause and notice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Remove the parenthetical — does the sentence still make sense? Good — it's truly parenthetical. Now choose: quiet (brackets) or dramatic (dashes)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each sentence, decide whether brackets or dashes are more appropriate. Add them correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pōhutukawa also called the New Zealand Christmas tree blooms in December.</li><li>The final score 14 to 12 in our favour was the best result of the season.</li><li>Captain Cook who arrived in 1769 was the first European to chart New Zealand's coastline.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this extra information quiet and factual, or dramatic and emphatic? That decides the mark.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add brackets or dashes to each sentence. Write a reason for your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Whanganui River legally recognised as a person in 2017 is sacred to the Māori people.</li><li>My brother the one who never reads finished the whole novel in a weekend.</li><li>Mount Taranaki also known as Egmont is one of New Zealand's most symmetrical volcanoes.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 sentences of your own — two using brackets and two using dashes. Make sure the parenthetical is genuinely non-essential in each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Brackets &amp; dashes for parentheticals: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Brackets and dashes are used to add parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence. Brackets indicate quiet, factual asides; dashes create emphasis and draw attention to the additional information. Both marks come in pairs, and the sentence must make grammatical sense if the parenthetical is removed.&quot; This is an explicitly expected Phase 2 punctuation skill.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Using parenthetical punctuation for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; Choosing between brackets and dashes is a purposeful punctuation decision — not a random one. This lesson teaches students that punctuation shapes the reader's experience of additional information: brackets whisper, dashes shout. That awareness is the foundation of punctuation as a craft tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Comparative & superlative adjectives":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): comparative adjectives compare two things; superlative adjectives compare three or more; students apply the rules for regular and irregular forms accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — More, Most, -er, -est</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Comparative adjectives compare two things. Superlative adjectives compare three or more. Short adjectives add -er/-est; longer adjectives use more/most; some are irregular.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: fast → faster → fastest | beautiful → more beautiful → most beautiful | good → better → best</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rule: 1–2 syllable adjectives → -er/-est: tall/taller/tallest. 3+ syllable adjectives → more/most: exciting/more exciting/most exciting. Exceptions: good/better/best, bad/worse/worst, far/further/furthest. Test: if &quot;-er&quot; sounds natural — use it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Try adding -er: if it sounds natural, use it. If it sounds awkward (&quot;intelligenter?&quot;), use &quot;more.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form the Comparatives</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Form the comparative and superlative of each adjective and use each in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cold → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>dangerous → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li><li>good → <em><strong> → </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which rule applies? Are there any exceptions here? Can you use each in an example sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the comparison chart and write sentences using the correct form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>happy → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;happiest&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>mysterious → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;more mysterious&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>bad → <em><strong> → </strong></em>  |  Use &quot;worst&quot; in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph comparing two animals, using at least four comparative or superlative adjectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Comparative &amp; superlative adjectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Adjectives can be modified to express degrees of comparison. Comparative forms (using -er or 'more') compare two things; superlative forms (using -est or 'most') compare three or more. Some adjectives have irregular comparative and superlative forms (good/better/best, bad/worse/worst).&quot; Students learn and apply both the rules and the exceptions.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise comparative language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Using precise comparative and superlative forms — rather than vague modifiers (&quot;more good,&quot; &quot;more bad&quot;) — is a key marker of vocabulary accuracy at Years 5–6. This lesson also reinforces that form choice follows a rule, not guesswork.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Combining short sentences for variety":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students combine multiple short sentences into smoother compound and complex sentences, eliminating choppiness and creating better flow.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Stitch the Choppy Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Too many short sentences in a row creates choppy, monotonous writing. Watch me combine short sentences into longer, smoother ones using conjunctions, relative clauses, and participial phrases.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Choppy: &quot;The kererū landed. It ate the berries. It flew away. It was very fast.&quot; → Smooth: &quot;The kererū landed, ate the berries, and flew away with surprising speed.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I combined four sentences into one by removing repeated subjects and joining the actions with commas and &quot;and.&quot; I also replaced &quot;very fast&quot; with &quot;surprising speed&quot; — more precise and vivid. The rhythm is now smooth rather than clipped.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If three or more sentences in a row are short and start with the same subject — they can probably be combined.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each group of short sentences into one or two smooth sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm arrived quickly. The wind was strong. Trees bent. Branches snapped.</li><li>She practised every day. She was nervous. The performance was coming soon.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences share a subject and can be combined? Which conjunction or relative clause best links the ideas?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each group into one well-constructed sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaiako walked in. She smiled. She placed a pile of books on the desk. She waited.</li><li>The volcano was silent. It had been silent for years. Scientists were watching it. They were worried.</li><li>The dog sat by the door. It was whining. It wanted to go outside. It had been waiting for an hour.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a paragraph in your own writing that has more than three consecutive short sentences. Combine at least two pairs.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Combining sentences for flow: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including combining short sentences to improve rhythm and flow. Eliminating choppy repetition by merging sentences with conjunctions, relative clauses, and shared verb phrases is a key revision skill at Years 5–6 — it shows control over sentence construction, not just sentence production.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for sentence variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson practises a specific revision strategy: identifying runs of short sentences, diagnosing why they feel choppy, and selecting the best combining method — conjunction, relative clause, or shared verb chain.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing: punctuation variety":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a punctuation variety checklist to their own and a partner's writing, giving and receiving specific feedback on comma, colon, semicolon, and dash use.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Punctuation Variety</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'm going to show you how to read a piece of writing specifically to assess its punctuation variety — not just accuracy.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist: ☐ At least one comma after a fronted adverbial ☐ At least one colon ☐ At least one semicolon ☐ At least one pair of brackets or dashes ☐ No comma splices</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once for meaning. Then I read again specifically for punctuation. I mark each type I find. I notice what's missing. Then I give specific feedback: &quot;You've used commas well in lists, but there are no semicolons — try joining two of these related sentences with one.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Specific feedback is helpful. &quot;Your punctuation is good&quot; is not. &quot;You need a comma after your fronted adverbial on line 2&quot; is.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Variety Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's audit this passage together for punctuation variety.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The science fair was held on Thursday. Every class had an entry. Our class made a working volcano. It erupted three times. The judges were impressed. We won first place. It was a good day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where could a semicolon replace a full stop? Where could a dash add drama? Is there a list that needs a colon?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of your writing with a partner. Edit each other's work for punctuation variety using the checklist.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Checklist: At least one semicolon used correctly.</li><li>✦ Checklist: At least one colon introducing a list or explanation.</li><li>✦ Checklist: At least one dash or pair of brackets for parenthetical information.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite your partner's weakest paragraph — the one with the least punctuation variety — as a model of what it could look like.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for punctuation variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work.&quot; Peer editing for punctuation variety is a specific application of this expectation — students give targeted, rule-based feedback (not just &quot;it's good&quot;) and practise reading writing through an editor's eye rather than a writer's eye.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying a punctuation checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This lesson consolidates all Term 2 punctuation learning — commas after fronted adverbials, colons, semicolons, brackets, and dashes — in a peer editing context where students must both recognise correct use and explain the rule behind each mark.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Greek & Latin roots in everyday words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): many English words are built from Greek and Latin roots; understanding common roots enables students to decode unfamiliar words and expand their vocabulary systematically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Crack the Code</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many English words are built from Greek and Latin roots. Once you know a root, you can work out the meaning of dozens of words you've never seen before.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;tele&quot; (far) → telescope, television, telepathy | &quot;aqua&quot; (water) → aquatic, aquarium, aqueduct | &quot;bio&quot; (life) → biology, biography, biodiversity</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If you see &quot;submarine,&quot; you might not know it — but &quot;sub&quot; means under and &quot;marine&quot; means sea. So submarine = under the sea. Same with &quot;microphone&quot; — &quot;micro&quot; (small) + &quot;phone&quot; (sound) = small sound amplifier. Roots unlock words like a key.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you hit an unknown word, look for a root you recognise. Even a partial match gives you a clue.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Decode Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the roots provided to work out what each word means. Then check by looking at the context sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;geo&quot; (earth): geology, geography, geothermal — what does &quot;geothermal&quot; likely mean?</li><li>&quot;script/scribe&quot; (write): manuscript, inscription, transcribe — what does &quot;transcribe&quot; likely mean?</li><li>&quot;spec/spect&quot; (look/see): inspect, spectator, spectacular — what does &quot;spectator&quot; likely mean?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you find other words that share these roots? How does knowing the root help you read unfamiliar texts?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use your knowledge of roots to answer each question.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The root &quot;port&quot; means carry. What do these words mean: export, import, transport, portfolio?</li><li>The root &quot;vis/vid&quot; means see. Use it to work out: visible, invisible, vision, evidence.</li><li>The root &quot;terra&quot; means land/earth. What do: terrain, territory, terrarium, extraterrestrial mean?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find 5 words in a book, magazine, or website that share a Greek or Latin root. List the root, its meaning, and the words that use it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Greek &amp; Latin roots: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Many English words are built from Latin and Greek roots, prefixes, and suffixes. Understanding the meaning of word parts enables students to work out the meaning of unfamiliar words and to build their vocabulary systematically.&quot; This lesson develops morphological awareness — knowing that &quot;geo&quot; means earth unlocks geology, geography, geothermal, and geocentric simultaneously.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Using root knowledge in writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use knowledge of word origins, roots, and word families to extend vocabulary.&quot; Understanding that &quot;port&quot; means carry (import, export, transport) gives students a transferable tool — they can both decode unfamiliar words when reading and select more precise vocabulary when writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Adverbial phrases for time & manner":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): adverbial phrases extend single adverbs by adding precision and texture; students replace weak single adverbs with richer adverbial phrases that tell the reader more.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Beyond Single Adverbs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A single adverb tells us how or when. An adverbial PHRASE does the same job but with more precision and colour.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;She spoke quietly.&quot; → &quot;She spoke in a hushed, urgent whisper.&quot; | &quot;He left quickly.&quot; → &quot;He left before anyone could ask questions.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Quietly&quot; tells us the manner. &quot;In a hushed, urgent whisper&quot; tells us manner AND gives texture — we can picture it. &quot;Before anyone could ask questions&quot; tells us time AND hints at motivation. Adverbial phrases carry more weight than single adverbs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Spot a lone adverb. Ask: can I replace it with a phrase that tells the reader more? If yes — upgrade it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace each underlined adverb with an adverbial phrase that adds more information.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tūī sang loudly in the pōhutukawa.</li><li>The tamariki waited nervously for the results.</li><li>The boat left early.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What kind of adverbial phrase fits — one of manner, time, or place? Can you add detail and stay true to the context?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by replacing the adverb in brackets with a precise adverbial phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaitiaki worked [hard] to restore the wetland.</li><li>The team celebrated [happily] after the final whistle.</li><li>She finished her speech [suddenly].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about an event or journey. Use at least three different adverbial phrases — one of time, one of manner, one of place.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Adverbial phrases for precision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding adverbial and prepositional phrases to express location, time, and manner.&quot; This lesson advances from single adverbs to adverbial phrases — teaching students that a phrase (&quot;in a hushed, urgent whisper&quot;) carries more information and creates a richer picture than a single word (&quot;quietly&quot;) ever can.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing weak adverbs with phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Replacing a generic adverb with a specific adverbial phrase is a key vocabulary refinement skill at Phase 2 — it demonstrates that the student is thinking about the reader's experience and choosing language that creates a precise and vivid picture.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Who/where/when phrases in persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): specific who, where, and when details make persuasive arguments more credible and harder to dismiss; students embed precise detail in persuasive sentences and paragraphs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Detail That Persuades</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 we used who/where/when phrases to add descriptive detail. This week we use the same technique in persuasive writing — where specific detail makes arguments more convincing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Vague: &quot;Schools should have gardens.&quot; → Specific: &quot;Schools across Aotearoa, from urban Auckland to rural Northland, should have gardens where students can grow and harvest their own kai.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The added phrases — &quot;across Aotearoa, from urban Auckland to rural Northland&quot; (where) and &quot;where students can grow and harvest their own kai&quot; (who/what) — make the claim feel specific and grounded. Vague arguments are easy to dismiss. Specific ones are harder to ignore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: In persuasion, specific detail builds credibility. WHO would benefit? WHERE would this happen? WHEN should it change? Answer those questions inside your sentences.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Make It Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add who, where, or when detail to each persuasive sentence to make it more convincing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should have access to nature.</li><li>Communities need to protect waterways.</li><li>Young people deserve to have their voices heard.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which type of detail makes each argument stronger — who, where, or when? Can you add more than one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare persuasive claim using who/where/when phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Claim: &quot;Aotearoa needs more native tree planting.&quot; Add a where and a when phrase.</li><li>Claim: &quot;Students learn better outdoors.&quot; Add a who and a where phrase.</li><li>Claim: &quot;Schools should start later.&quot; Add a who and a when phrase.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a persuasive paragraph (5–6 sentences) on a topic you care about. Every sentence must include at least one who, where, or when detail.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Specific detail in persuasion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader&quot; and to &quot;provide specific, concrete detail that supports the purpose.&quot; In persuasive writing, specificity is a core argument strategy — claims supported by precise who, where, and when details are more credible and more resistant to dismissal than vague generalisations.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Building convincing persuasive paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail&quot; in persuasive writing. This lesson directly builds the ability to support a persuasive topic sentence with specific, embedded detail — the who, where, and when that transforms a claim into a convincing argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: replacing overused words ('nice', 'good')":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students identify and replace the most overused and vague words in their writing with precise, purposeful alternatives.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Vague Word Hit List</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some words appear in almost every piece of student writing: nice, good, bad, big, little, went, got, said, things, stuff. They're not wrong — they're just empty. Watch me hunt them down and replace them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;It was a nice day and we had a good time doing lots of fun things.&quot; → &quot;The sun blazed over the harbour and we spent the afternoon kayaking, laughing until our arms gave out.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Nice day&quot; → specific weather detail. &quot;Good time&quot; → specific activity. &quot;Lots of fun things&quot; → actual examples. The second version creates a picture. The first creates nothing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The vague word hit list: nice, good, bad, big, little, went, got, said, things, stuff, very, really. Highlight every one. Each is an upgrade opportunity.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find every vague word in this passage and replace it with something precise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We went to the nice beach and it was really good. We did lots of fun things and got really tired. The food was nice and everyone said it was a good day.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many vague words can you find? What specific, precise word or phrase could replace each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, replacing every vague word with a precise alternative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The movie was really good and the things that happened were very exciting.</li><li>She is a nice person who always says good things to people.</li><li>It was a bad situation and lots of bad things went wrong.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own writing. Highlight every word from the vague word hit list. Rewrite the paragraph with every vague word upgraded.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Replacing vague words: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience&quot; and to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; This lesson targets the most pervasive vocabulary weakness at Years 5–6 — over-reliance on generic, high-frequency words — and builds the habit of interrogating each word choice against a concrete alternative.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Vocabulary audit in revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; The vague word audit — highlighting every word from the hit list and challenging each one — is a practical revision strategy that directly improves the precision and vividness of students' writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Colons to introduce lists":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces a list only when a complete sentence precedes it; students apply this rule accurately and correct common misuses of the colon.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Colon That Sets Up a List</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We met colons in Term 1. This week we focus specifically on using them to introduce lists — and on the rule about what must come BEFORE the colon.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: ✓ &quot;The kete contained three items: a tī kōuka leaf, a piece of bone, and a carved pounamu.&quot; | ✗ &quot;The kete contained: a tī kōuka leaf, a piece of bone, and a carved pounamu.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The colon introduces the list — but only when a complete sentence comes before it. &quot;The kete contained three items&quot; is a complete sentence ✓. &quot;The kete contained&quot; is NOT a complete sentence ✗ — so no colon. The rule: a complete sentence must precede the colon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before the colon = a complete sentence. After the colon = the list. Test: can the part before the colon stand alone? If not — remove the colon.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix or Confirm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether the colon is used correctly in each sentence. Fix those that aren't.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientist needed: a microscope, slides, and a sample jar.</li><li>The rescue team brought the following equipment: ropes, a first-aid kit, and torches.</li><li>To make rewena bread you will need: flour, potato water, sugar, and salt.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the part before the colon a complete sentence? If not, what needs to change?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon in the correct place, or rewrite so the colon is used correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pā required three things to defend effectively high ground, a clear view, and strong palisades.</li><li>There are many native birds in the Waikato region including: tūī, kererū, and pīwakawaka.</li><li>She packed everything she would need for the tramp: food, a sleeping bag, a compass, and wet-weather gear.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences, each using a colon to introduce a list. Make sure every sentence before the colon is grammatically complete.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons before lists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;A colon is used after a complete clause to introduce a list, explanation, or quotation. A common error is placing a colon after an incomplete clause (e.g. 'The bag contained:') — the part before the colon must be a grammatically complete sentence.&quot; Students learn to test for completeness before adding a colon, developing both accuracy and understanding of clause structure.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying colons accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including colons. This lesson develops the completeness test as a reliable self-checking strategy — students identify whether the part before the colon could stand alone as a sentence before applying the mark, preventing the most common colon error at Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Progressive tenses: past & present continuous":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): past and present progressive tenses express ongoing actions; students form and use these tenses accurately, understanding how past progressive sets a background scene interrupted by simple past events.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Actions in Progress</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The progressive (or continuous) tense shows an action that was happening over a period of time — not just a single completed event.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Present progressive: &quot;The kākāpō is climbing the tree right now.&quot; | Past progressive: &quot;The kākāpō was climbing when we first spotted it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Present progressive = is/are + verb-ing: &quot;She is writing.&quot; Past progressive = was/were + verb-ing: &quot;She was writing when the bell rang.&quot; Key use: past progressive sets a scene that another action interrupts. &quot;He was reading when the power cut out.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Past progressive sets the background scene. Simple past is the event that interrupts it. Together: &quot;She was sleeping when the earthquake struck.&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Form and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the correct progressive form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students ___ (prepare) their speeches when the fire alarm went off.</li><li>Look out the window — the kererū ___ (eat) all the berries!</li><li>While the waka ___ (cross) the harbour, the wind suddenly changed direction.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this happening right now (present) or was it in progress at a past moment (past)? That decides was/were vs is/are.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the progressive tense as directed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using past progressive to set a scene, then use simple past for an interrupting event.</li><li>Describe what three different people are doing right now using present progressive.</li><li>Rewrite in past progressive: &quot;The rangatira spoke to the crowd. The sun set behind the hills.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short recount (5 sentences) of a real or imagined event. Use past progressive at least twice to set the scene before key moments.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Progressive tenses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;The present progressive tense (is/are + verb-ing) describes an action happening at the moment of speaking. The past progressive tense (was/were + verb-ing) describes an action that was in progress at a past moment, often interrupted by another event in simple past. Both forms require the auxiliary verb 'be' and the present participle (-ing form).&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using progressive tense for narrative effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses.&quot; In narrative writing, past progressive is particularly powerful for scene-setting — it creates a sustained background action (&quot;The village was sleeping&quot;) that simple past then disrupts (&quot;when the alarm sounded&quot;). This lesson develops students' control of tense as a narrative craft tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences using 'although', 'unless', 'while'":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): 'although', 'unless', and 'while' are subordinating conjunctions that express contrast, condition, and simultaneity; students use them accurately to create nuanced complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Conjunctions That Add Nuance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although,&quot; &quot;unless,&quot; and &quot;while&quot; are subordinating conjunctions that add nuance — they create contrast, condition, and simultaneous action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;Although it was raining, the match continued.&quot; | &quot;The match will continue unless there is lightning.&quot; | &quot;While the rain fell, the players kept their positions.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although&quot; = contrast (it happened despite something). &quot;Unless&quot; = condition (this won't happen IF something else does). &quot;While&quot; = at the same time. Each creates a different relationship — choosing the right conjunction is part of a writer's craft.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: &quot;Although&quot; = despite this. &quot;Unless&quot; = except if. &quot;While&quot; = at the same time as. Choose based on the relationship between your two ideas.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Conjunction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with although, unless, or while.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>___ the journey was exhausting, Mere never once complained.</li><li>The kaiako won't begin ___ everyone is seated and listening.</li><li>___ the kettle boiled, she set out the cups and the tea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What relationship exists between the two clauses — contrast, condition, or simultaneity?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write two sentences for each conjunction — one with the subordinate clause first, one with it second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>although (contrast)</li><li>unless (condition)</li><li>while (simultaneous)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences about a challenge or difficult situation. Use although, unless, and while at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Subordinating conjunctions: although, unless, while: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;'Although' expresses contrast (despite the subordinate clause, the main clause is still true); 'unless' expresses condition (the main clause won't happen except under the stated condition); 'while' expresses simultaneity (both actions happen at the same time). Each conjunction creates a different logical relationship between the clauses — understanding these distinctions is a key Phase 2 grammar expectation.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Complex sentences for nuance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — including complex sentences — for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson extends complex sentence use to three conjunctions that require genuine understanding of logical relationships: contrast, condition, and simultaneity — moving students beyond the familiar &quot;because&quot; and &quot;when&quot; into more nuanced subordination.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: checking tense consistency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students apply a systematic verb audit to identify and correct tense inconsistencies in modelled and their own writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Verb Audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A tense audit means underlining every verb in a passage, labelling it as past or present, and then correcting any that break the pattern.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The explorer sets out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reaches the ridge.&quot; — sets (Pr), walked (P), reaches (Pr).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Two present, one past — inconsistent. I decide: past tense works better for a recount. Fix: &quot;The explorer set out at dawn. She walked for three hours before she reached the ridge.&quot; — all past, all consistent.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Be especially careful with &quot;was/is&quot; and &quot;said/says&quot; — these are the most common accidental tense switches.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Audit Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline every verb, label each P (past) or Pr (present), then rewrite in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The market is busy on Saturday mornings. Stalls lined both sides of the street. Vendors called out their prices. The smell of fresh bread drifts through the air. Families stopped to chat and children run between the stalls.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs switch tense? What is the dominant tense here? Rewrite consistently.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Underline all verbs, label each one P or Pr, then rewrite in consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The diver slips below the surface. The water was cold and very clear. She searches for the wreck that locals told her about. After ten minutes, she spots a shadow on the seabed. She swam closer and felt her heart race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of your own writing and do a full tense audit on it. Note how many switches you find.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Tense audit as revision strategy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate.&quot; The tense audit — underline every verb, label the tense, decide on consistency, fix mismatches — is a specific, repeatable revision strategy that goes beyond casual re-reading. It is a direct application of the Phase 2 expectation that students revise systematically.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Identifying tense in context: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Verbs carry tense and must remain consistent throughout a piece of writing. Tense shifts disrupt the reader's experience and signal a lack of control over the writing.&quot; This editing lesson deepens grammatical knowledge by requiring students to identify and label verb tense in context before applying corrections — connecting knowledge to practice.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Spelling: tricky high-frequency words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): certain high-frequency words have irregular or non-phonetic spellings that must be learned; students apply strategies to memorise and recall these words accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words That Break the Rules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some high-frequency words don't follow phonetic patterns — they must be memorised. Watch me demonstrate three strategies for locking these tricky spellings in.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: receive, separate, necessary, occurrence, privilege, conscience, rhythm, liaison</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Strategy 1 — Look, Cover, Write, Check: study the word, cover it, write it from memory, check. Strategy 2 — Find the word within the word: &quot;sep-a-rate&quot; (not sep-er-ate) — there's &quot;a rat&quot; in separate! Strategy 3 — Mnemonic: &quot;Rhythm Helps Your Two Hips Move&quot; — first letters spell RHYTHM.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you spell a word wrong twice, look for a mnemonic or pattern that makes the correct spelling stick.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Spelling Strategies</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use at least one strategy to help you learn each of these tricky spellings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>necessary (one c, two s) — find a mnemonic.</li><li>occasion (two c, one s) — find the pattern.</li><li>definitely (not &quot;definately&quot;) — break it into parts.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which strategy worked best for each word? Can you teach your strategy to a partner?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Look, Cover, Write, Check for each word. Then write a sentence using each one correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>accommodate (two c, two m)</li><li>embarrass (two r, two s)</li><li>government (silent n before m)</li><li>privilege (not &quot;priviledge&quot;)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Create a personal spelling list of 5 words you consistently misspell. Apply one strategy to each and test yourself tomorrow.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Conventions (Knowledge)</em> — Tricky high-frequency spellings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Conventions (Knowledge): &quot;Some high-frequency words have irregular or non-phonetic spellings that must be memorised through repeated practice and strategic learning. Students develop spelling knowledge through a range of strategies including visual memory (Look, Cover, Write, Check), morphological analysis (breaking into meaningful parts), and mnemonics.&quot; Accurate spelling of high-frequency words is a core expectation at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Checking and correcting spelling: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking spelling.&quot; This lesson builds students' metacognitive awareness of their own spelling weaknesses and gives them a toolkit of strategies — rather than relying on spellcheck — for identifying and fixing errors in their writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Prepositions of direction & movement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): directional prepositions express movement through, across, towards, and along specific paths; students use them precisely to create clear, vivid spatial descriptions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Which Way? Prepositions of Direction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some prepositions express static location (in, on, under). Others express direction and movement — they tell us where something is going. Watch me use directional prepositions to make movement vivid.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The waka moved across the harbour, through the channel, and towards the shore.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Across&quot; — movement over a surface from one side to another. &quot;Through&quot; — movement from one side of something to the other by passing inside it. &quot;Towards&quot; — movement in the direction of something. &quot;Along&quot; — movement following the length of something. Each is precise about the geometry of the movement.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Visualise the movement and ask: is it over a surface (across), inside something (through), in a direction (towards), or following a line (along)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Preposition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the most accurate directional preposition for each gap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The children ran ___ (across/through/along) the beach to the water's edge.</li><li>The tuna moved ___ (across/through/towards) the dark water of the river.</li><li>She walked ___ (along/towards/through) the ridge, keeping the sea in sight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Picture the movement precisely. Which preposition describes the path most accurately?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct directional preposition and then rewrite the sentence with more specific detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kererū flew <em><strong> the canopy and </strong></em> the clearing.</li><li>The river flows <em><strong> the gorge and </strong></em> the sea.</li><li>She moved <em><strong> the crowd and </strong></em> the stage.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences describing a journey — by foot, waka, or bike. Use at least four different directional prepositions precisely.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Directional prepositions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Prepositions of direction (across, through, towards, along, past, into, out of) express movement in relation to a surface, an interior, or a destination. Choosing the correct directional preposition requires visualising the geometry of the movement — is it over a surface, inside something, towards a target, or along a path?&quot; Students learn to distinguish between similar prepositions by thinking spatially.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Precise spatial writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding prepositional phrases to express location&quot; and direction. Using directional prepositions precisely — rather than vague alternatives (&quot;went near,&quot; &quot;moved by&quot;) — is a key skill in descriptive and narrative writing at Phase 2, enabling students to place the reader inside the movement they are describing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Making vague sentences specific":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students transform vague, general sentences into specific, detailed ones by adding precise nouns, verbs, and modifiers that answer the reader's implicit questions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Vague vs. Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Vague sentences tell the reader almost nothing. Specific sentences give them something to picture. Watch me transform a vague sentence into a specific one by answering the reader's questions.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Vague: &quot;A bird made a sound in the tree.&quot; → Specific: &quot;A tūī burst into a cascading trill from the topmost branch of the pōhutukawa.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reader's questions: WHAT bird? → tūī. WHAT sound? → cascading trill. WHERE in the tree? → topmost branch. WHAT tree? → pōhutukawa. Answering each question turns a generic sentence into one that only this moment could have produced.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For every noun, ask: what exactly? For every verb, ask: how exactly? For every place, ask: where exactly?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Make It Specific</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each vague sentence by answering every implied reader question.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A person walked past.</li><li>The weather changed.</li><li>Something fell.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What are the reader's questions for each sentence? What specific detail answers each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transform each vague sentence into a specific one. Aim for a sentence only THIS moment could produce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A vehicle went by.</li><li>The animal made a noise.</li><li>She did something with the object.</li><li>The food smelled good.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write 4 intentionally vague sentences, then rewrite each one with maximum specificity. Compare the two versions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating specific, vivid writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including providing specific, concrete detail that allows the reader to visualise the scene. This lesson teaches the &quot;reader's question&quot; technique — systematically asking 'what exactly? how exactly? where exactly?' to identify and fill gaps that leave writing vague and generic.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise nouns and verbs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; choosing Tier 2 vocabulary that is precise and cross-curricular. Replacing generic nouns (&quot;bird&quot;) with specific ones (&quot;tūī&quot;) and generic verbs (&quot;made a sound&quot;) with precise ones (&quot;burst into a cascading trill&quot;) is the most direct application of this principle — specificity IS vocabulary.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: strengthening narrative openings":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students evaluate narrative openings against criteria for engagement and revise weak openings to draw the reader immediately into the story.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — First Sentences That Work</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A weak opening loses the reader before the story begins. Watch me evaluate three narrative openings against criteria, then revise the weakest one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: &quot;My name is Jake and this is a story about something that happened to me last summer.&quot; | Strong: &quot;The day I found the box, I almost walked straight past it.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The weak opening: tells rather than shows, introduces the narrator self-consciously, contains no tension or mystery. The strong opening: drops us into a specific moment, creates immediate curiosity (&quot;what was in the box?&quot;), and doesn't explain itself. Criteria for a strong opening: specific moment, no explaining, creates a question in the reader's mind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: does the opening make the reader ask a question? If not — rewrite until it does.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose and Revise</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Evaluate each opening against our three criteria. Then revise the weakest one together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Once upon a time there was a girl who wanted to be an explorer.&quot;</li><li>&quot;The engine coughed twice, then fell silent. In the distance, the lights of the mainland blinked out.&quot;</li><li>&quot;I am going to write about the best day I ever had which was when we went to the beach.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which opening drops you into a specific moment? Which creates a question? Which explains too much?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each weak opening so it meets all three criteria.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;This is the story of how I found out my neighbour was not who she said she was.&quot;</li><li>&quot;One day something strange happened and I will tell you about it in this story.&quot;</li><li>&quot;It was a morning like any other until it wasn't.&quot;</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three alternative openings for a story of your choice. Choose the best one and explain why.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Evaluating and revising narrative openings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson applies that revision process to the highest-stakes part of any narrative — the opening sentence. Students evaluate against explicit criteria (specific moment, no explaining, reader question) and revise until the opening works.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Engaging the reader from the first sentence: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Narrative opening strategy is a key composing skill at Phase 2 — students learn that the most engaging openings drop the reader directly into a specific moment, create immediate curiosity, and resist the urge to explain or introduce the narrator self-consciously.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Alliteration & assonance":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): alliteration (repeated consonant sounds) and assonance (repeated vowel sounds) are sound devices that create rhythm, mood, and texture in writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making Writing Sound as Good as It Reads</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alliteration repeats the same consonant sound at the start of neighbouring words. Assonance repeats the same vowel sound in the middle of words. Both create rhythm and texture that the reader can hear.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Alliteration: &quot;The silent snake slipped through the sedge.&quot; | Assonance: &quot;The rain-slain plain.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alliteration: the &quot;s&quot; sound creates a hissing, slithering effect — perfect for a snake. Assonance: the &quot;ai&quot; sound gives a slow, mournful quality — good for a grey, flat landscape. Both devices work best when they reinforce the meaning or mood, not when they're forced.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Sound devices should serve the meaning. If the reader notices the device more than the content — it's too obvious.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Hear the Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Brainstorm sound words for each setting, then build a sentence using alliteration or assonance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Setting: a beach at high tide</li><li>Setting: a forest just after rain</li><li>Setting: a crowded school cafeteria</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What sounds would you hear? What sounds could you create with alliteration or assonance?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the device specified.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write an alliterative sentence about a NZ animal using the &quot;k&quot; sound (kiwi, kererū, kākāpō, kārearea).</li><li>Write a sentence about the sea using assonance with the &quot;ō&quot; or &quot;oo&quot; sound.</li><li>Write a description of a storm using both alliteration and assonance in the same sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line descriptive poem about a NZ landscape. Use alliteration in at least two lines and assonance in at least one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Sound devices: alliteration &amp; assonance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Language devices such as alliteration (repeated consonant sounds) and assonance (repeated vowel sounds) create specific effects for the reader — rhythm, mood, and texture. They are most effective when they reinforce the meaning of the writing rather than being applied for their own sake.&quot; Students learn to use sound devices deliberately and discriminatingly, not mechanically.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Using sound devices for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use language devices to create specific effects and engage the reader.&quot; Alliteration and assonance are sound-based composing tools — they work on the reader's ear as well as their mind. This lesson develops the discipline of asking 'what mood am I creating?' before selecting which sound to repeat and how often.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students consolidate their understanding of coordinating (FANBOYS) and subordinating conjunctions, applying the distinction to build and identify compound and complex sentences accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Types, Two Jobs</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've studied both types of conjunction across Terms 1 and 2. Today we consolidate: coordinating conjunctions join equal clauses; subordinating conjunctions create a main clause and a dependent clause.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Coordinating: &quot;The hīkoi was long, but the marchers kept going.&quot; | Subordinating: &quot;The marchers kept going although the hīkoi was exhausting.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) = coordinating — both sides could stand alone. AAAWWUBBIS (after, although, as, when, while, until, because, before, if, since) = subordinating — the dependent clause leans on the main clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: remove the conjunction and split the sentence in two. If both halves make sense alone — coordinating. If one half sounds incomplete — subordinating.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Classify and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify whether the underlined conjunction is coordinating or subordinating, then explain how you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kaiako waited until the class was completely silent.</li><li>The experiment failed, yet the students learnt something important.</li><li>Because the tide was out, we could walk across to the island.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Could both sides stand alone? What type of relationship does each conjunction create?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using the conjunction type shown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a compound sentence using a coordinating conjunction that shows contrast.</li><li>Write a complex sentence using a subordinating conjunction that shows cause.</li><li>Write a complex sentence where the subordinate clause comes FIRST (with a comma after it).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a place you know well. Use at least two coordinating and two subordinating conjunctions. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs. subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS: for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight — both sides can stand alone. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, unless, while, until, since, as, before, after, if) introduce a dependent clause that cannot stand alone. Understanding this distinction is essential for building compound and complex sentences accurately.&quot;</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Compound &amp; complex sentence consolidation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity.&quot; This review lesson consolidates the distinction between compound sentences (coordinating conjunctions, comma before) and complex sentences (subordinating conjunctions, comma only when fronted) — integrating Term 1 and Term 2 sentence structure learning at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Paragraph cohesion: pronouns as linking devices":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): students use personal pronouns, demonstrative pronouns (&quot;this,&quot; &quot;these&quot;), and pronoun reference to create cohesion within paragraphs, linking sentences into a connected whole.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Pronouns That Hold a Paragraph Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We used connective words in Term 1. This week, we focus specifically on pronouns as cohesive devices — how &quot;he,&quot; &quot;she,&quot; &quot;they,&quot; &quot;it,&quot; &quot;this,&quot; and &quot;these&quot; reach back to a previous sentence and pull ideas together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The tuatara is one of New Zealand's most ancient reptiles. It has barely changed in 200 million years. This makes it a living link to the age of the dinosaurs.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;It&quot; in sentence 2 refers back to &quot;tuatara&quot; — no need to repeat the noun. &quot;This&quot; in sentence 3 refers back to the whole idea of sentence 2. Each pronoun acts like a thread, stitching sentences into a paragraph rather than a list.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: After writing a paragraph, draw an arrow from each pronoun to what it refers to. Every pronoun should have a clear, unambiguous referent.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Stitch It Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the repeated nouns with appropriate pronouns to improve paragraph cohesion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The pūkeko is a bold and territorial bird. The pūkeko will often chase away much larger animals. The pūkeko's bright red beak makes the pūkeko easy to spot in wetland areas. Despite the pūkeko's aggression, the pūkeko is a beloved part of the New Zealand landscape.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which nouns can be replaced with pronouns? Are there any where replacing would cause confusion?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph so pronouns link the sentences without causing ambiguity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kākāpō is a large, flightless parrot. The kākāpō is critically endangered. Conservation teams monitor every single kākāpō. Each kākāpō has a name and a tracking pack. The tracking pack helps conservation teams follow each kākāpō's movements across the island.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 5 sentences about a NZ animal or place. Use a different pronoun in at least three sentences to link back to the subject. Make sure every pronoun is unambiguous.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Pronoun cohesion within paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including pronouns, synonyms, and connectives — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson focuses specifically on pronoun cohesion: using personal pronouns (it, they), possessive pronouns (its, their), and demonstrative pronouns (this, these) to refer back to nouns and ideas — creating flow rather than repetition.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Pronoun reference &amp; ambiguity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Pronouns must refer unambiguously to their antecedent. When two or more nouns are present, pronouns can create confusion if the referent is unclear.&quot; This lesson develops the habit of checking pronoun reference — drawing arrows from each pronoun to its antecedent — and revising when the reference is ambiguous.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: identifying weak sentence openings":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Practice): students diagnose monotonous sentence opening patterns and revise them using fronted adverbials, adjective openers, and rearranged clause order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Weak Opening Pattern</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When every sentence starts the same way — especially with &quot;The&quot; or &quot;I&quot; — writing becomes monotonous. Watch me diagnose a passage with weak openings and revise it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The sky was dark. The clouds gathered quickly. The wind picked up. The rain began to fall. The thunder rolled in from the south.&quot;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Five sentences. Every one starts with &quot;The.&quot; Revision: &quot;Dark clouds gathered quickly. As the wind picked up, the first drops of rain began to fall. From the south, thunder rolled in — low and threatening.&quot; Varied openers: adjective, adverbial, fronted phrase. Immediately more dynamic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: List the first word of every sentence in your paragraph. If more than two start the same way — vary them.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>List the first word of each sentence. Identify the pattern. Then rewrite three sentences with different openers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I arrived at school early. I put my bag in my locker. I went to find my friends. I sat down and waited for the bell. I felt nervous about the day ahead.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What pattern do you see? What three different ways could you begin these sentences instead?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph, varying the sentence openings so no two consecutive sentences start the same way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The match started well. The team scored early. The crowd cheered loudly. The other team fought back. The score was level at half time. The players looked tired. The coach spoke to them firmly. The second half began.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences about any topic. Make a rule: no two sentences may start with the same word. List the opening word of each sentence underneath.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson teaches a specific diagnostic strategy — listing the first word of every sentence — that makes repetitive opener patterns immediately visible, enabling targeted revision.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;vary sentence openings to avoid repetition and create effect,&quot; using fronted adverbials, adjective openers, and rearranged clause order. This lesson consolidates all sentence variety strategies from Terms 1 and 2, applying them in a revision context where students must diagnose the problem before selecting the solution.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Review: Term 2 punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 2 — commas after fronted adverbials, colons, semicolons, brackets, dashes, apostrophes for contraction, and comma splices.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Term 2 Punctuation Gallery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every punctuation mark from Term 2 and its rule.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Comma after fronted adverbial | Colon before list | Semicolon joining related sentences | Brackets for quiet aside | Dashes for emphatic aside | Contraction apostrophe | No apostrophe for plurals | Comma splice fix</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Before the match began, the referee called the captains together. Each team needed the following things: a captain, a strategy, and confidence. The home team was ready; the visitors looked nervous. One player — the one who had trained hardest — stepped forward.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For each mark, ask: what job is it doing here? If it's not doing a job, remove it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuation Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the correct punctuation to each sentence and name the rule.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the strong wind the waka race continued as planned.</li><li>The pā required three things a strong location high walls and clear sightlines.</li><li>The tī kōuka also known as the cabbage tree is found throughout Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which marks are needed and why? Could any sentence use more than one type?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add all missing punctuation to this passage. Name every rule you applied.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>although the weather was overcast the school cross-country went ahead as planned. the course covered three different terrains the playing field a bush track and the school road. hazel our fastest runner had trained every morning before school. she wasnt nervous she was ready. after two kilometres hazel was still in front the other runners couldnt keep up with her pace.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences that uses every Term 2 punctuation mark at least once. Label each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Term 2 punctuation review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): Students consolidate all punctuation covered in Term 2: commas after fronted adverbials, colons to introduce lists or explanations, semicolons to join related independent clauses, brackets and dashes for parenthetical information, apostrophes for contraction (not for plurals), and the identification and correction of comma splices. Review ensures students can name the function of each mark and apply the relevant rule.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying full range of punctuation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This consolidation lesson asks students to apply all Term 2 punctuation conventions in an extended passage — naming the rule behind each choice — building the metacognitive awareness needed to make punctuation decisions independently across all writing contexts at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: Term 2 grammar":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): students consolidate all grammar covered in Term 2 — collective and abstract nouns, future tense, SVA tricky cases, relative pronouns, progressive tenses, and conjunction types.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Term 2 Grammar Gallery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's revisit every grammar concept from Term 2 — one mini-example each.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Collective &amp; abstract nouns | Future tense: will/going to | SVA: everyone/each | Relative pronouns: who/which/that | Progressive tenses: was-ing/is-ing | Conjunction types: CO vs SUB</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;A pride of lions (collective) shows remarkable loyalty (abstract). Each lion (SVA: singular) has its own role. The pride is going to move (future: planned) at dusk. The leader, who guides (relative: person) the hunt, was circling (past progressive) the herd when the weakest animal stumbled (simple past interrupts).&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Grammar is about understanding why a sentence works, not just labelling. Always connect the rule to the meaning it creates.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Grammar Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each sentence, identify the grammar feature underlined and explain what rule it follows.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A flock of starlings was swooping across the estuary.</li><li>Neither of the answers is correct.</li><li>The taonga, which had been in the whānau for generations, was carefully restored.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What type of noun/pronoun/verb is underlined? What rule governs it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar review tasks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using a collective noun and an abstract noun.</li><li>Write two future tense sentences — one with &quot;will&quot; (spontaneous) and one with &quot;going to&quot; (planned).</li><li>Write a sentence using a relative clause with &quot;which&quot; (remember the commas).</li><li>Write a sentence using past progressive interrupted by simple past.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of 6 sentences demonstrating at least 5 different Term 2 grammar concepts. Label them in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Term 2 grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Students consolidate their understanding of all grammar covered in Term 2: collective and abstract nouns, future tense using will/going to, subject-verb agreement with indefinite pronouns (everyone, each, neither), relative pronouns (who, which, that), past and present progressive tenses, and the distinction between coordinating and subordinating conjunctions. Review connects knowledge to meaning and use.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Integrated grammar application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of word classes and structures.&quot; This review lesson asks students to demonstrate all Term 2 grammar knowledge in context — combining multiple features in single sentences — moving from isolated recognition to integrated, purposeful use at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Review: Term 2 sentence structures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students consolidate all sentence structures from Term 2 — compound sentences in new contexts, complex sentences using although/unless/while, sentence combining, and fronted adverbial sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Term 2 Sentence Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we added powerful new tools to our sentence toolkit. Let me show each one in action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: FANBOYS in context | Complex: although/unless/while | Combined sentences | Fronted adverbials | Who/where/when expanded</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Although the hīkoi had been going for three days, the marchers showed no sign of stopping (although). They would not rest unless they reached the marae (unless). While the leaders held talks, the rest of the group set up camp (while). Before dawn the next morning, they set out again (fronted adverbial).&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Variety is the goal. Read your writing aloud — if it has a rhythm that changes and shifts, you're using your toolkit well.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sentence Type Identification</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the sentence type and explain the structural feature that defines it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the forecast, the outdoor event went ahead without a hitch.</li><li>She wanted to give up, yet something kept her moving forward.</li><li>The waka, which had been carved by hand over two years, was finally blessed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What type is each sentence? What structural feature tells you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence of each type, all on the topic of the sea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compound sentence using &quot;yet.&quot;</li><li>Complex sentence starting with &quot;Although.&quot;</li><li>Complex sentence using &quot;unless&quot; — subordinate clause second.</li><li>Complex sentence using &quot;while.&quot;</li><li>Fronted adverbial sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Combine all five sentences into a cohesive paragraph about the sea. Edit for flow and variety.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Term 2 sentence structure review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students consolidate all sentence structures from Term 2: FANBOYS compound sentences in varied contexts, complex sentences using although/unless/while, combining choppy sentences for flow, and fronted adverbial sentences with correct comma placement. Students demonstrate the ability to identify structural features by name and produce each type on demand.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Sentence variety across a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling rhythm and variety through sentence structure. This consolidation task — writing five different sentence types on the same topic and combining them into a paragraph — directly tests whether students can select the right structure for each moment rather than defaulting to a favourite pattern.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Full-passage edit: Term 2 checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply all Term 2 editing skills in a systematic multi-pass edit, using the full Term 2 checklist to revise a passage for punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, and structure.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Term 2 Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I do a complete multi-pass edit using our full Term 2 checklist. One pass per focus — never try to fix everything at once.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Pass 1: Comma after fronted adverbials | Pass 2: Colons, semicolons, brackets, dashes | Pass 3: Apostrophes (possession AND contraction — no plurals) | Pass 4: Comma splices | Pass 5: Vague verb/word upgrades | Pass 6: Sentence opener variety</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the passage once for meaning. Then Pass 1: check every sentence that starts with an adverbial phrase — is the comma there? Pass 2: look for lists — do they use colons correctly? Pass 3: every apostrophe — possession, contraction, or error? And so on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Multi-pass editing catches more than single-pass. Each pass has a specific focus — you're not trying to see everything at once.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Work through each pass together on this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>despite the rain our school fair went ahead last saturday. the organisers needed three things good volunteers lots of stalls and the right attitude. the fair included many activities a cake stall a bouncy castle a talent show and a silent auction. it wasnt perfect but everyones effort paid off. by the end of the afternoon the school had raised over two thousand dollars for the librarys new books.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass by pass — what are we fixing? Count the total improvements.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage using all six passes of the Term 2 checklist. Rewrite the improved version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>on thursday afternoon the year 5 and 6 students went to the wetland reserve near school. the reserve contained many things native plants birds insects and a small stream. although the track was muddy the students werent put off. the guide who had worked there for twenty years showed them a bittern its one of new zealands rarest birds. everyone said it was the best trip theyd ever been on.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the full 6-pass edit to a paragraph from your own writing journal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Multi-pass editing: Term 2 checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson makes the revision process systematic and visible — a structured, six-pass approach that integrates every editing skill from Term 2. Students apply punctuation, grammar, and vocabulary knowledge simultaneously in a single extended editing task.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Term 2 skills: integrated application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): This consolidation lesson asks students to demonstrate mastery of all Term 2 writing skills in one editing task: fronted adverbial commas, colons and semicolons, brackets and dashes, apostrophes, comma splice correction, vocabulary precision, and sentence opener variety. It directly mirrors the Phase 2 expectation that students &quot;apply writing knowledge and skills across a range of text types and purposes&quot; with increasing independence.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Similes for vivid comparison":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): figurative language such as similes compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as' to create a vivid image; students select figurative language deliberately to enhance meaning for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Comparing Two Unlike Things</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A simile compares one thing to something else using 'like' or 'as', to help the reader picture it more vividly. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The waves crashed. → The waves crashed like a thousand drums.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask: what does this remind me of? Crashing waves — loud, rhythmic, powerful. 'A thousand drums' captures the sound and force. A weak simile (waves like water) tells us nothing new — a strong one adds a fresh image.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Avoid overused similes (cold as ice, brave as a lion). Push for an original comparison instead.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Comparison</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build a simile for each sentence together. Reject the first cliché answer and push for something fresher.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The thunder was loud.</li><li>Her smile was bright.</li><li>The old house was quiet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does this remind you of? Is your first idea a cliché — can you find something more original?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an original simile to each sentence using 'like' or 'as'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The wind howled through the trees.</li><li>The river moved slowly.</li><li>His hands were rough.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences describing the school playground, each using a different, original simile.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: similes: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language, including similes, compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as' to create a vivid image for the reader. Students consider the connotations and freshness of a comparison rather than relying on clichés.&quot; This lesson establishes simile as a deliberate, controllable craft tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting original comparisons: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson builds the habit of rejecting an overused first idea in favour of an original comparison.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Punctuating direct speech":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): direct speech is enclosed in speech marks, with a comma separating the reporting clause, end punctuation inside the speech marks, and a capital letter starting the spoken words; a new speaker begins a new line.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Rules of Speech Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we write exactly what someone says, we use speech marks (&quot; &quot;). There are four rules: 1) speech marks go around the spoken words only, 2) a capital letter starts the spoken words, 3) end punctuation goes inside the speech marks, 4) a comma separates the speech from the reporting clause. Watch me apply all four.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: the boy said i am not going home yet</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix: &quot;I am not going home yet,&quot; the boy said. — Capital 'I' starts the speech. Comma after 'yet' (inside the marks) before closing them. Lower-case 'the boy said' since it continues the sentence. If the reporting clause comes first: The boy said, &quot;I am not going home yet.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read the spoken words only — would a capital letter naturally start them? Then check the comma and end mark sit inside the speech marks.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Punctuate the Dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add speech marks and correct punctuation to these lines together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the teacher said sit down please</li><li>wait for me called Aroha</li><li>I dont understand he whispered</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where do the speech marks go? Where does the comma sit — inside or outside?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each line with correct speech-mark punctuation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>are we there yet asked the little girl</li><li>the captain shouted all hands on deck</li><li>I will never give up she said quietly</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line dialogue between two characters, starting a new line for each new speaker.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Punctuating direct speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Direct speech is enclosed in speech marks. A capital letter begins the spoken words, end punctuation sits inside the speech marks, and a comma separates the speech from the reporting clause. A new line begins each time the speaker changes.&quot; This is an explicit Phase 2 punctuation expectation.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying dialogue conventions accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including the conventions of direct speech, which are tested through both editing and their own narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence openers: -ing clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a sentence can open with a present participle (-ing) clause that describes an action happening at the same time as the main clause, adding variety to sentence beginnings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Starting with an Action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of always starting with the subject, we can open a sentence with an -ing clause describing an action happening at the same time as the main action. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: The dog burst through the door. It was barking loudly. → Combined: Barking loudly, the dog burst through the door.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both actions (barking, bursting through) happen at the same time, so I can combine them. The -ing clause goes first, followed by a comma, then the main clause. This creates a more dynamic opening than 'The dog burst through the door, barking loudly.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check: are both actions truly happening at the same moment? If one happens before the other, this structure won't make sense.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine with an -ing Opener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair of sentences using an -ing clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The climber gripped the rock face. She was gasping for breath.</li><li>The crowd surged forward. They were cheering wildly.</li><li>The cat crept along the fence. It was watching the bird.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Are both actions happening at the same time? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ing opener, remembering the comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The waves pounded the shore. They were roaring furiously.</li><li>The toddler reached for the toy. She was giggling with excitement.</li><li>The soldiers marched into camp. They were singing a marching song.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences that begin with an -ing clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — -ing clause sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A sentence can open with a present participle (-ing) clause describing an action simultaneous with the main clause, followed by a comma. This is one of several techniques for varying sentence openings beyond the subject.&quot; Students learn the simultaneity condition that makes this structure work.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Varying sentence openings for effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including varied sentence openers. This builds directly on Term 1's fronted adverbial work, adding a new opener type to the student's toolkit.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: show, don't tell":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students revise writing by replacing 'telling' statements about emotion or quality with specific sensory and behavioural detail that allows the reader to infer it themselves.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Let the Reader Work It Out</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Telling' states an emotion directly (she was sad). 'Showing' gives details that let the reader infer the emotion themselves. Watch me convert one to the other.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Telling: She was nervous before the race.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What does nervousness look like physically? Showing: Her hands trembled as she rubbed them on her shorts, and she couldn't stop glancing at the starting line. I haven't used the word 'nervous' once — but the reader feels it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: what would a camera see? Convert the emotion word into an action, gesture, or physical sensation.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each 'telling' sentence into 'showing' detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was angry.</li><li>She was excited about the trip.</li><li>The dog was scared of the storm.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What physical signs would show this emotion? What would the character do or say?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each telling sentence using show-don't-tell.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man was tired.</li><li>The children were bored in class.</li><li>She was proud of her painting.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a sentence from your own writing that 'tells' an emotion, and rewrite it to 'show' instead.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Show, don't tell: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including conveying emotion through specific, concrete, sensory detail rather than direct statement. This is a key narrative craft technique at Years 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for engagement: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson gives students a specific, repeatable revision question — 'what would a camera see?' — to apply to their own narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Personification":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): personification gives human qualities or actions to non-human things, creating atmosphere and making descriptive writing more vivid and engaging.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Giving Objects Human Behaviour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Personification gives a non-human thing a human action or feeling. Watch me transform a flat description using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The wind blew through the trees. → The wind whispered secrets through the trees.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wind can't literally whisper — but giving it that human action creates atmosphere (something mysterious or gentle). I match the personification to the mood I want: a storm might 'scream' or 'tear', a calm wind might 'sigh' or 'tiptoe'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Choose a human verb that matches the mood you're creating — gentle, threatening, playful.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Bring It to Life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add personification to each sentence to create the mood shown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire (mood: angry/threatening) ___</li><li>The moon (mood: calm/watchful) ___</li><li>The old gate (mood: tired/creaky) ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What human action or feeling matches this mood? Does it fit the object?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add personification to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain fell on the roof.</li><li>The shadows stretched across the lawn.</li><li>The kettle began to whistle.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences describing a storm, using personification in each to build a frightening mood.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: personification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language, including personification, gives human qualities to non-human things to create atmosphere and engage the reader. Word choice should be deliberate and matched to the intended mood.&quot; This extends Term 3 Week 1's simile work into a second figurative device.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating mood and atmosphere: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies, including figurative language, to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; This lesson connects vocabulary choice directly to the deliberate creation of mood and atmosphere in descriptive and narrative writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Active and passive voice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): in the active voice the subject performs the action; in the passive voice the subject receives the action. Students identify and form both, understanding when each is appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Who Is Doing What?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the active voice, the subject does the action (The dog chased the cat). In the passive voice, the subject receives the action (The cat was chased by the dog). Watch me convert one to the other.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Active: The storm destroyed the bridge. → Passive: The bridge was destroyed by the storm.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I move the object ('the bridge') to the front as the new subject, use a form of 'to be' + past participle ('was destroyed'), and the original subject ('the storm') becomes optional, introduced by 'by'. Active voice is usually punchier; passive voice is useful when the action matters more than who did it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test for passive voice: can you add '...by zombies' after the verb and it still makes sense? (was destroyed by zombies ✓) — that's a sign of passive voice.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each active sentence to passive, and discuss why a writer might choose one over the other.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The council planted the trees.</li><li>A thief stole the painting.</li><li>The scientists discovered the fossil.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the new subject? What form of 'to be' do we need? Does this sentence sound better active or passive?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each sentence as instructed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert to passive: 'The chef prepared the meal.'</li><li>Convert to active: 'The window was broken by the storm.'</li><li>Convert to passive: 'The librarian organised the books.'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one active and one passive sentence about the same event — which version do you prefer, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Active &amp; passive voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;In the active voice, the subject performs the action of the verb; in the passive voice, the subject receives the action, formed using 'to be' plus the past participle. Writers choose between active and passive voice deliberately, depending on what they want to emphasise.&quot; This is a key Phase 2 grammar concept underpinning formal and explanation writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing voice for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity&quot; and to make deliberate grammatical choices appropriate to text type — active voice typically suits narrative, while passive voice often suits formal explanation and report writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Joining ideas with semicolons":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction, creating a more sophisticated link between balanced ideas than a full stop or comma splice.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — A Sharper Link Than a Full Stop</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We met the semicolon in Term 1. Today we focus on using it to join two closely related, balanced ideas for sophisticated effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The crowd fell silent. The referee blew the whistle. → The crowd fell silent; the referee blew the whistle.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both sides are complete sentences, closely linked in time and meaning — a full stop works, but the semicolon shows the reader these two moments are connected. A comma alone here would be a comma splice (an error) — only a semicolon, full stop, or conjunction can join two complete sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: could both halves stand alone as separate sentences? If yes, and they're closely related — a semicolon is a good choice.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Join with a Semicolon</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair using a semicolon. Discuss why these two ideas are closely related enough to deserve one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sky darkened. The first drops of rain began to fall.</li><li>She trained for months. Her hard work finally paid off.</li><li>The engine spluttered. Then it died completely.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Are both halves complete sentences? Are they closely related in meaning?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair with a semicolon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team celebrated. They had won the championship.</li><li>The fire alarm rang. Everyone filed outside calmly.</li><li>He apologised sincerely. She decided to forgive him.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two of your own semicolon sentences about a sporting event.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Semicolons for sophisticated links: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including semicolons to join closely related independent clauses. This Term 3 lesson revisits and deepens Term 1's introduction, focusing on the judgement of when two ideas are 'closely related enough' to deserve one.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Avoiding comma splices: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Two independent clauses cannot be joined by a comma alone (a comma splice); they must be joined by a full stop, a semicolon, or a coordinating conjunction.&quot; This lesson reinforces that boundary while extending students' control of the semicolon as a deliberate stylistic choice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing dialogue punctuation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply the rules of direct speech punctuation systematically when revising a passage of dialogue, checking speech marks, capitalisation, end punctuation, and new lines for new speakers.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Dialogue Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you how to check a whole passage of dialogue against our four rules: speech marks around spoken words only, capital letter starting speech, end punctuation inside the marks, comma before the reporting clause, and a new line for each new speaker.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: hello said Sam how are you today im fine thanks replied Mia</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read slowly and apply one rule at a time. Fix: &quot;Hello,&quot; said Sam. &quot;How are you today?&quot; — new line — &quot;I'm fine, thanks,&quot; replied Mia. Notice Sam's two sentences both need their own speech marks since 'said Sam' interrupts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If the reporting clause interrupts mid-speech, close the speech marks before it and reopen them after, with their own punctuation each time.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix the punctuation in this short dialogue together, one rule at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>where are you going asked dad im going to the library said Leo can I come too he asked sure replied Leo</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does each speaker change? Where do the speech marks, capitals, and commas go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this dialogue with full, correct punctuation and new lines for each speaker.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>did you finish your homework asked mum not yet I was about to start said Tom you'd better hurry she said the bus leaves soon</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your own 4-line dialogue exchange between two characters arguing about something small, with perfect punctuation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic dialogue editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking for correct punctuation.&quot; This lesson applies that revision process specifically to direct speech — one of the most error-prone punctuation areas for Years 5–6 writers.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying speech punctuation accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect,&quot; including the conventions of direct speech. Editing a multi-line dialogue passage consolidates the rule set introduced in Week 1 into an independent, transferable checking strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Metaphors":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): a metaphor describes one thing directly as another (without 'like' or 'as') to create a powerful, often surprising image; students use metaphor deliberately and avoid mixing metaphors.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Saying One Thing Is Another</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unlike a simile, a metaphor says something IS something else — no 'like' or 'as'. It's bolder and can be more powerful. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Simile: Her eyes were like diamonds. → Metaphor: Her eyes were diamonds, glittering in the firelight.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Removing 'like' makes the comparison feel more absolute and dramatic. I extend it slightly ('glittering in the firelight') to make the image fuller. Careful: I shouldn't then say her eyes 'sparkled like stars' in the same sentence — that mixes two different metaphors and confuses the reader.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Stick to ONE metaphor per image. Mixing metaphors (her eyes were diamonds, twinkling like a lighthouse) confuses rather than clarifies.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Metaphor</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Turn each description into a metaphor.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The classroom (mood: chaotic) was ___</li><li>The mountain (mood: ancient, unmoving) was ___</li><li>Her voice (mood: soft, calming) was ___</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What single bold image captures this mood? Are you keeping it to one consistent image?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a metaphor for each prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe a busy motorway using a metaphor.</li><li>Describe a quiet library using a metaphor.</li><li>Describe an angry sky before a storm using a metaphor.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two sentences about the ocean — one using a simile, one using a metaphor. Which is more powerful, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: metaphor: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;A metaphor describes one thing directly as another, without using 'like' or 'as', to create a powerful and often surprising image. Effective metaphors are sustained and not mixed with unrelated comparisons.&quot; This builds on Week 1's simile work to complete the simile/metaphor distinction.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Avoiding mixed metaphors: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; and to consider the coherence of the images they create — recognising and avoiding mixed metaphors is part of controlling figurative language deliberately.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Modal verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) express degrees of possibility, certainty, permission, or obligation; students select modal verbs deliberately to convey precise meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Shades of Possibility</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) change how certain or possible something sounds. Watch how the same sentence shifts meaning with different modals.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: It will rain tomorrow. (certain) | It might rain tomorrow. (possible) | It must have rained last night. (strong inference)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Will' = confident prediction. 'Might'/'may' = uncertain possibility. 'Must' = strong logical conclusion based on evidence. 'Should' = expectation or recommendation. Choosing the right modal tells the reader exactly how sure you are.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before choosing a modal, ask: how certain am I, really? Match the word to your actual confidence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Modal</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the modal verb that best fits the level of certainty described.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>(Strong recommendation) You ___ wear a helmet when cycling.</li><li>(Pure possibility, 50/50) The bus ___ be late today.</li><li>(Near-certain conclusion from evidence) She ___ have left already — her bag is gone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How certain is the speaker? Which modal verb matches that level of certainty exactly?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence with the most precise modal verb, and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Based on the dark clouds, a storm ___ be coming.</li><li>Students ___ complete their homework by Friday (obligation).</li><li>I ___ be free this weekend, but I'm not certain yet.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about a mystery, using a different modal verb in each to show increasing certainty.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Modal verbs and certainty: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, must, should, will, would) express degrees of possibility, permission, obligation, or certainty. Precise modal choice allows a writer to convey exactly how confident or tentative a claim is.&quot; This is an important grammar tool for persuasive and explanatory writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precision in modal choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Choosing the correct modal verb is a precision skill directly relevant to persuasive argument and explanation writing, where overstating or understating certainty weakens credibility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence openers: -ed clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): a sentence can open with a past participle (-ed) clause describing a completed state or action affecting the subject of the main clause, adding further variety to sentence beginnings.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Starting with a Completed State</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An -ed clause opener describes a state or completed action affecting the subject, before the main action. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Normal: The hikers were exhausted. They collapsed at the summit. → Combined: Exhausted, the hikers collapsed at the summit.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Exhausted' describes their state before/as they collapsed. I drop the subject and verb from the first sentence, keep just the -ed word (or phrase), add a comma, then the main clause. Try a longer one: 'Battered by the storm, the old fishing boat limped into harbour.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: -ed openers often describe a state (tired, battered, convinced) rather than an ongoing action — that's the key difference from -ing openers.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Combine with an -ed Opener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ed clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team was defeated. They trudged off the field in silence.</li><li>The cake was burnt. It was thrown straight in the bin.</li><li>The students were convinced by the argument. They voted unanimously in favour.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What -ed word or phrase captures the state? Where does the comma go?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using an -ed opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog was soaked from the rain. It shook itself violently in the hallway.</li><li>The old bridge was weakened by years of neglect. It finally collapsed in the flood.</li><li>The crowd was thrilled by the performance. They leapt to their feet.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences that begin with an -ed clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — -ed clause sentence openers: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A sentence can open with a past participle (-ed) clause describing a completed state affecting the subject of the main clause, followed by a comma. This complements -ing clause openers as a further technique for varying sentence beginnings.&quot; Students learn to distinguish state (-ed) from ongoing action (-ing) openers.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity.&quot; This lesson completes a two-part sequence (Week 1: -ing, Week 3: -ed) giving students two further reliable, controllable sentence-opening techniques beyond fronted adverbials.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for active voice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Sentence Structure (Practice): students identify overused passive voice constructions in a passage and convert them to active voice where this strengthens clarity and energy.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Hunting the Passive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Passive voice has its uses, but overusing it makes writing feel vague and distant. Watch me find and convert unnecessary passive sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The ball was kicked by the striker. It was caught by the goalkeeper.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test: can I add '...by zombies' and it still works? Yes — passive. Who is doing the action? The striker, the goalkeeper. Convert: The striker kicked the ball. The goalkeeper caught it. Much more direct and energetic — better for action narrative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight every 'was/were + past participle' in a passage. Ask: does the reader need to know WHO did it? If yes, switch to active.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find and convert the unnecessary passive sentences in this passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ball was thrown by Mia. It was caught by Sam. A point was scored by the team. The crowd was excited by the win.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences would be stronger as active voice? Who is doing each action?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage, converting passive sentences to active where it strengthens the writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The trophy was lifted by the captain. Photos were taken by the parents. Cheers were given by everyone. The team was congratulated by the coach.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a piece of your own writing with passive voice. Decide if it should stay passive (formal/unknown doer) or switch to active (energy, clarity).</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing overused passive voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson teaches the 'by zombies' test as a quick, memorable way to spot unnecessary passive constructions during self-editing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing voice deliberately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to make deliberate choices between active and passive voice depending on purpose. This lesson reinforces Week 2's grammar knowledge by applying it as a genuine editing decision rather than a grammar exercise alone.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Connectives for persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): cohesive connectives (firstly, furthermore, however, therefore, in addition, on the other hand) signal the relationship between ideas and structure an argument logically for the reader.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Signposting Your Argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Persuasive connectives tell the reader how one idea relates to the next: adding (furthermore, in addition), contrasting (however, on the other hand), or concluding (therefore, as a result). Watch me use them to structure an argument.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Schools should start later. Students get more sleep. Some people disagree. Later starts may clash with parents' work hours. We should still consider it.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With connectives: Schools should start later. Firstly, students would get more sleep. However, later starts may clash with parents' work hours. Nevertheless, the benefits to student wellbeing make this worth considering. The connectives turn a list of ideas into a structured argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Match the connective to the job: adding evidence? furthermore/moreover. Contrasting a counter-point? however/on the other hand. Drawing a conclusion? therefore/as a result.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Connective</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the connective that best links each pair of ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recycling reduces landfill waste. ___, it conserves natural resources. (adding)</li><li>Exercise has many benefits. ___, not everyone has time for it. (contrasting)</li><li>Plastic pollutes the ocean. ___, we should reduce single-use plastic. (concluding)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this adding, contrasting, or concluding? Which connective fits that job?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an appropriate connective to link each pair of sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Homework helps students practise skills. ___, too much homework can cause stress.</li><li>Cities need more cycle lanes. ___, this would reduce traffic congestion.</li><li>Some students prefer working alone. ___, group work builds important teamwork skills.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence persuasive paragraph on a topic of your choice, using at least three different connectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Persuasive connectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Cohesive connectives signal the logical relationship between ideas — addition, contrast, cause, or conclusion — and are essential to structuring a persuasive argument clearly for the reader.&quot; Students learn to categorise connectives by function rather than memorising them as a random list.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring an argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs&quot; in persuasive writing. This lesson gives students a functional connective bank organised by the job each one does within an argument.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so) join equal ideas to form compound sentences, while subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if) join a dependent clause to a main clause to form complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Equal Partners vs. Unequal Partners</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so, yet) join two equal, independent ideas — a compound sentence. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since) join a dependent clause to a main clause — a complex sentence. Watch the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Compound: She trained hard, and she won the race. | Complex: She won the race because she trained hard.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the compound sentence, both halves could stand alone as full sentences — they're equal partners joined by 'and'. In the complex sentence, 'because she trained hard' cannot stand alone — it depends on the main clause for its full meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: read the clause after the conjunction alone. If it makes complete sense by itself — coordinating. If it sounds incomplete — subordinating.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sort and Join</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each conjunction is coordinating or subordinating, then use it to join the sentence pair correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>because — The match was cancelled. The field was flooded.</li><li>but — She wanted to go outside. It was raining heavily.</li><li>although — He was tired. He kept practising.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this conjunction joining two equal ideas, or making one idea depend on the other?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each pair using the conjunction given, and label the sentence as compound or complex.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use 'so': The bus was late. We missed the start of the film.</li><li>Use 'when': The bell rang. The students rushed to lunch.</li><li>Use 'or': You can hand in your work today. You can hand it in tomorrow.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one compound and one complex sentence about the same event — point out which conjunction made each one which type.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Coordinating conjunctions (and, but, or, so, yet) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight to form a compound sentence. Subordinating conjunctions (because, although, when, if, since) join a dependent clause to an independent main clause to form a complex sentence.&quot; This lesson consolidates Term 1–2 sentence work into one explicit comparison.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Choosing sentence type for purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures — simple, compound, and complex — for effect and clarity,&quot; and to understand the grammatical reasoning behind each type, not just produce them by imitation.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: PEEL paragraphs for persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): the PEEL structure (Point, Evidence, Explain, Link) organises a persuasive paragraph so that each claim is supported by evidence, explained for the reader, and linked back to the overall argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building an Argument Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PEEL stands for Point (the claim), Evidence (proof or example), Explain (why this evidence supports the point), and Link (connect back to the argument or next paragraph). Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Point: Schools should have longer lunch breaks. Evidence: Studies show students concentrate better after proper rest. Explain: This means more break time would directly improve learning in afternoon classes. Link: For this reason, schools should reconsider their lunch schedules.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each sentence does one specific job. Point states the claim clearly. Evidence backs it with something concrete. Explain bridges the evidence to the claim — this is the step many writers skip! Link closes the loop back to the argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If your 'explain' sentence just repeats the evidence, it's not doing its job — it must say WHY the evidence proves the point.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build a PEEL Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a PEEL paragraph together for this point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Students should be allowed to choose their own reading books.</li><li>Evidence: ___ (what proof or example supports this?)</li><li>Explain: ___ (why does this evidence support the point?)</li><li>Link: ___ (connect back to the argument)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What evidence is convincing? Does your 'explain' sentence actually explain, or just repeat?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full PEEL paragraph for the given point, labelling each sentence P/E/E/L.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Recycling should be compulsory in all schools.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a second PEEL paragraph arguing the opposite side of any topic you choose — practise constructing a balanced argument.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — PEEL paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Persuasive paragraphs are commonly structured using Point, Evidence, Explain, Link — each sentence performing a distinct function: stating a claim, providing supporting evidence, explaining the connection between evidence and claim, and linking back to the overall argument.&quot; This is the central organisational structure expected for persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Constructing reasoned argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning.&quot; PEEL gives students an explicit, repeatable framework for building this structure independently in persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing PEEL paragraphs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students evaluate a persuasive paragraph against the PEEL structure, identifying missing or weak elements (point, evidence, explain, link) and revising accordingly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Diagnosing a Weak Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I'll show you how to check a paragraph against the PEEL structure and find what's missing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Recycling is good for the environment. Lots of countries recycle a lot. It really helps.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Point ✓ (recycling is good). Evidence — weak ('lots of countries recycle a lot' is vague, not real evidence). Explain — missing entirely! 'It really helps' doesn't say HOW or WHY. Link — missing. Fix: add specific evidence (e.g. a statistic or example), explain the connection, and link back to the argument.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your paragraph and label each sentence P, E, E, or L in the margin. Any letter missing or weak is your revision target.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence P/E/E/L in this paragraph. What's missing or weak?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should have more PE time at school. Exercise is good for you. Schools should change their timetables.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part is missing? Is the evidence specific enough? Where's the explain sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph is missing its Explain and Link sentences. Add them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point: Libraries should stay open later. Evidence: Many students don't have a quiet place to study at home. [Add an Explain sentence] [Add a Link sentence]</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a PEEL paragraph you wrote on Wednesday and check it against the four labels — strengthen any weak part.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Diagnosing paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; Labelling each sentence against the PEEL framework gives students a precise diagnostic tool for identifying structural weaknesses in persuasive paragraphs.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Strengthening argument paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning.&quot; This editing lesson consolidates Wednesday's PEEL construction work by practising the reverse skill: recognising and repairing a paragraph that doesn't yet meet the structure.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Connotation: positive vs negative word choice":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): words with similar denotations can carry different connotations (positive, negative, or neutral associations); writers select words deliberately to shape the reader's attitude towards a subject.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Same Fact, Different Feelings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Connotation is the feeling or association a word carries, beyond its dictionary meaning. Two words can describe the same thing but make the reader feel differently about it. Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: He was stubborn. vs. He was determined. | She was nosy. vs. She was curious.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Stubborn' and 'determined' both mean someone won't change their mind — but 'stubborn' sounds negative, 'determined' sounds admirable. Same fact, opposite feeling. Writers choose connotation deliberately depending on whether they want the reader to sympathise or disapprove.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: do I want the reader to feel positively, negatively, or neutrally about this? Choose your word to match.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Connotation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each neutral idea, choose a positive word AND a negative word with the same basic meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A person who saves money carefully → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li><li>A person who speaks their mind → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li><li>A child who asks lots of questions → positive: <em><strong> negative: </strong></em></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What word makes the reader admire this trait? What word makes them disapprove of it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each neutral sentence twice — once with positive connotation, once with negative — describing the same event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The politician changed her opinion on the policy. (positive / negative)</li><li>The team took a long time to finish the project. (positive / negative)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph describing the same character twice — once so the reader likes them, once so the reader dislikes them — using only connotation, not different facts.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Connotation and word choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Words with similar meanings can carry different connotations — positive, negative, or neutral associations — and writers select vocabulary deliberately to shape the reader's attitude.&quot; This is an advanced application of the precision vocabulary work begun in Term 1 and Term 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Shaping reader attitude: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;consider the effect of word choice on the reader.&quot; Understanding connotation is essential for persuasive and characterisation writing, where the writer deliberately steers how the reader judges a person, action, or idea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Nominalisation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): nominalisation turns a verb or adjective into a noun (decide → decision, careless → carelessness), producing the more formal, condensed sentence style typical of explanation and report writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Turning Actions into Things</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nominalisation changes a verb or adjective into a noun. It creates a more formal, compact sentence — common in reports, explanations, and academic writing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informal/verb-based: The council decided to build a new park, which many people were happy about. → Nominalised: The council's decision to build a new park led to widespread happiness.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Decided' (verb) → 'decision' (noun). 'Happy' (adjective) → 'happiness' (noun). The nominalised version packs the idea into a noun phrase, sounding more formal and report-like — useful for explanation writing, but can feel stiff if overused in narrative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Common pattern: verb + -ion/-ment/-ation, adjective + -ness/-ity. Try it, then ask: does this sentence still sound natural?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Nominalise Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using a nominalisation of the underlined word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The scientists discovered a new species.</li><li>The students were curious about the experiment.</li><li>The government announced new road safety rules.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What noun form of this word exists? Does the new sentence still make sense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence using nominalisation to create a more formal tone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team improved their performance over the season.</li><li>Many people are concerned about climate change.</li><li>The company expanded into new markets.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two versions of a sentence about a school event — one informal/verb-based, one nominalised and formal. Which suits a newsletter? Which suits a casual conversation?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Nominalisation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Nominalisation converts a verb or adjective into a noun (decide → decision, careless → carelessness), producing a more condensed, formal sentence style typical of explanation and report text types.&quot; This is an advanced Phase 2 grammar concept connecting word formation to register.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Register-appropriate word forms: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; Recognising when nominalisation suits formal writing (and when it makes narrative writing feel stiff) builds students' awareness of register as a deliberate choice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Cause and effect sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): cause-and-effect connectives (because, as a result, consequently, due to, this means that) construct sentences that clearly link an action or event to its outcome, essential for explanation writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Linking Cause to Effect</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing relies on clear cause-and-effect links. Watch me build sentences using a range of cause-and-effect connectives, not just 'because'.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Cause: Deforestation removes trees that absorb carbon dioxide. Effect: Greenhouse gases increase. → Deforestation removes trees that absorb carbon dioxide; as a result, greenhouse gases increase.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I could also write: 'Because deforestation removes trees... greenhouse gases increase' or 'Deforestation removes trees... Consequently, greenhouse gases increase.' Varying the connective (because, as a result, consequently, due to, this means that) avoids repetition across a whole explanation text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you've used 'because' three times already in a paragraph, reach for 'as a result', 'consequently', or 'this means that' instead.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Link Cause and Effect</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Join each cause and effect using a different connective each time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cause: Ice caps are melting. Effect: Sea levels are rising.</li><li>Cause: Plastic takes hundreds of years to break down. Effect: It pollutes oceans for generations.</li><li>Cause: Bees pollinate crops. Effect: Many food sources depend on them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which connective haven't you used yet? Does the sentence still make logical sense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a cause-and-effect sentence for each pair, using a different connective in each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cause: Native forests are cleared for farmland. Effect: habitats are lost.</li><li>Cause: Regular exercise strengthens the heart. Effect: overall health improves.</li><li>Cause: Overfishing reduces fish populations. Effect: fishing communities suffer economically.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence explanation paragraph about why recycling matters, using at least three different cause-and-effect connectives.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Cause-and-effect connectives: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of connectives to link cause and effect within explanation writing,&quot; varying beyond 'because' to include 'as a result', 'consequently', 'due to', and 'this means that'. This is a core grammatical requirement of explanation text type.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Explanation text structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Explanation texts are structured around clear cause-and-effect relationships, typically organised sequentially or logically.&quot; This lesson builds the sentence-level tool that underpins the larger explanation text-type structure students are expected to control by Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for formal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students identify informal language (contractions, slang, casual connectives) inappropriate for formal writing and revise it to match a formal register.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Matching Language to Audience</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Formal writing (reports, persuasive essays, explanations) avoids contractions, slang, and casual phrasing. Watch me formalise an informal passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Informal: Kids shouldn't have to do tons of homework, it's just not fair and it's really stressful.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fix: Students should not be required to complete excessive amounts of homework, as it places unnecessary stress upon them. 'Shouldn't' → 'should not'. 'Kids' → 'students' (more formal noun). 'tons of' → 'excessive amounts of'. 'it's just not fair' → cut, replaced with a more formal justification.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Hunt for contractions (don't, it's, can't), slang (kids, tons, stuff), and casual fillers (just, really, like) — these are the first things to formalise.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Formalise Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage in a formal register, removing contractions and casual language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I don't think we should have so much plastic packaging, it's bad for the environment and it's just wasteful.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which words are too casual for formal writing? What more formal word or phrase could replace each one?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in a formal register.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Schools shouldn't ban phones, it's not really fair on kids who need them.</li><li>I reckon the council should fix the playground, it's been broken for ages.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take an informal sentence you might say to a friend and rewrite it as if it were appearing in a formal letter to your school principal.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Formal vs informal register: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience,&quot; including matching register (formal/informal) to text type and audience. This editing lesson targets the most common register slip in Years 5–6 persuasive and explanation writing — contractions and casual phrasing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for audience and purpose: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to vocabulary… to improve clarity and impact,&quot; considering audience and purpose. This lesson builds a specific register-checking strategy students can apply to any formal writing task.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Formal vs informal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): register refers to the level of formality in language, signalled through vocabulary, sentence length, and contractions; writers match register to audience and purpose.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Two Ways to Say the Same Thing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Register is how formal or informal language is. The same message can be said very differently depending on audience and purpose. Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: To a friend: Hey, can you grab me a drink? | To a stranger/formal: Excuse me, would you mind passing me a drink, please?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both ask the same thing! But word choice ('hey'/'excuse me'), contractions, and politeness markers ('would you mind', 'please') signal formality. I choose register based on WHO I'm writing to and WHY.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: would I say this to my principal? If not, it's probably too informal for formal writing — and vice versa.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify the Register</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide if each sentence is formal or informal, and explain the clues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yo, that movie was awesome!</li><li>The committee respectfully requests your attendance at the meeting.</li><li>I reckon we should just go for it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What words or phrases gave away the register? Could you rewrite it in the opposite register?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each informal sentence in formal register, and each formal sentence in informal register.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Informal → Formal: 'Can you tell the boss I'm gonna be late?'</li><li>Formal → Informal: 'We would be grateful if you could submit your assignment by Friday.'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the same short message (e.g. asking for help with a project) twice — once to a teacher (formal), once to a friend (informal).</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Register: formal and informal language: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Register refers to the level of formality in language, signalled through word choice, contractions, sentence length, and politeness markers. Writers match register deliberately to audience and purpose.&quot; This is a key Phase 2 expectation underpinning all text types.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Matching language to audience: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies appropriate to audience and purpose.&quot; This lesson makes register an explicit, comparable choice rather than an instinctive one, preparing students for the formal-register editing work later in the term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Reported (indirect) speech":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): reported speech conveys what someone said without quotation marks, requiring a backshift in tense and changes to pronouns and time/place words; students convert between direct and reported speech accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Speech Without the Speech Marks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reported speech tells us what someone said without quoting their exact words — no speech marks needed. But several things change: the verb tense usually shifts back, and pronouns/time words adjust. Watch me convert.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Direct: Maya said, &quot;I am going to the shop now.&quot; → Reported: Maya said that she was going to the shop then.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'am going' (present) → 'was going' (past) — tense shifts back. 'I' → 'she' — pronoun changes to match the new speaker perspective. 'now' → 'then' — time word shifts since we're reporting it later. No speech marks needed at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Three changes to check: 1) does the tense shift back? 2) do pronouns need to change? 3) do time/place words need to change (now→then, here→there, today→that day)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Convert to Reported Speech</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each direct speech sentence to reported speech.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tom said, &quot;I am tired.&quot;</li><li>She said, &quot;I will finish my homework tomorrow.&quot;</li><li>&quot;We are leaving now,&quot; they said.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the tense shift back? Do the pronouns change? Do any time words need to change?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each sentence as instructed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert to reported speech: Aroha said, &quot;I love this book.&quot;</li><li>Convert to reported speech: &quot;I can't come today,&quot; he said.</li><li>Convert to direct speech: She said that she was going home then.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short conversation in direct speech, then rewrite the whole thing as a reported-speech summary.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Direct and reported (indirect) speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Reported speech conveys what someone said without quotation marks, typically requiring a backshift in verb tense and corresponding changes to pronouns and time or place adverbs.&quot; This consolidates Week 1's direct speech punctuation knowledge by introducing its grammatical counterpart.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Converting between direct and reported speech: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses&quot; and structures. Reported speech is a common feature of recount and news-style writing, requiring students to apply tense and pronoun agreement knowledge simultaneously.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Balancing sentence rhythm":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): writers deliberately balance the length and structure of sentences across a paragraph to control pace, building on short-vs-long sentence variety with attention to rhythm across a whole passage.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Listening to a Paragraph's Rhythm</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've practised varying single sentences. Today, listen to the rhythm of a WHOLE paragraph and learn to balance it across multiple sentences.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: All medium-length paragraph: 'The waves crashed against the rocks. The wind howled through the cliffs. Seabirds circled overhead. The storm grew worse by the minute.' → all the same length — monotonous.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rebalanced: 'The waves crashed against the rocks, and the wind howled through the cliffs. Seabirds circled. The storm grew worse and worse, the sky darkening with every passing minute.' Now there's a long-short-long pattern — a rhythm, not a drumbeat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your paragraph aloud. If every sentence takes the same breath to say, vary at least one to break the pattern.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Rebalance Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph has a flat, repetitive rhythm. Let's rebalance it by combining some sentences and keeping others short.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire spread quickly. The smoke filled the room. The alarm rang loudly. Everyone ran outside. The firefighters arrived fast.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences could combine? Which single short sentence should stay for impact?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rebalance this paragraph's rhythm by combining at least two sentences while keeping one short for impact.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The race began. The runners sprinted forward. The crowd cheered loudly. One runner pulled ahead. She crossed the line first.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 5-sentence paragraph about an exciting moment, deliberately mixing short and long sentences for a clear rhythm. Read it aloud to check.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Balancing sentence rhythm across a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; extended here to consider rhythm across a whole paragraph rather than a single sentence. This builds on Term 1's short-vs-long sentence work with a wider lens.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace across a passage: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including controlling pace and rhythm across an extended piece of writing, not just within a single sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for cohesion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Practice): students revise a multi-paragraph passage to add cohesive devices (connectives, pronouns, repeated key words) that link ideas between paragraphs.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Linking Paragraphs Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Cohesion means ideas flow smoothly between sentences AND paragraphs. Watch me fix a passage where paragraphs feel disconnected.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Para 1 ends: ...recycling reduces landfill waste. Para 2 starts: Pollution is also a big problem.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The jump from 'recycling' to 'pollution' feels abrupt — no link. Fix: 'In addition to reducing landfill waste, recycling also helps tackle a second major issue: pollution.' I added a connective ('in addition to') and referenced the previous idea before introducing the new one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: At the start of a new paragraph, briefly nod back to the previous idea before introducing the new one — this is what creates flow.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Links</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These two paragraph openings feel disconnected. Add a cohesive link between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Para 1 ends: ...native birds are losing their habitats. Para 2 starts: ___ Predators are another major threat to their survival.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How can you connect the idea of habitat loss to the new idea of predators?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a cohesive link sentence or phrase between each pair of paragraph endings/openings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Para 1 ends: ...exercise improves physical health. Para 2 starts: ___ Mental health benefits are just as important.</li><li>Para 1 ends: ...plastic pollution harms marine life. Para 2 starts: ___ Solutions are within our reach.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take two paragraphs from a piece of your own writing and check whether they connect smoothly — add a link if they don't.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion across paragraphs: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use cohesive devices — including connectives, pronouns, and repeated key words — to link ideas within and across sentences and paragraphs.&quot; This lesson extends sentence-level cohesion (Term 1) to the more demanding skill of linking entire paragraphs.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Revising for flow: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson gives students a specific revision technique — briefly referencing the previous idea before introducing a new one — to apply when their writing feels disjointed between paragraphs.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Onomatopoeia":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sound they describe, adding sensory immediacy to descriptive and narrative writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Words That Sound Like What They Mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Onomatopoeia words imitate sounds (crash, sizzle, hiss). They make writing more sensory and immediate. Watch me weave one into a sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The pan was hot. The bacon went in. → The bacon sizzled as it hit the scorching pan.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Sizzled' lets the reader almost hear the sound, instead of just being told something happened. I choose the precise onomatopoeic word to match the exact sound — not just 'made a noise', but the specific noise (sizzle, not crackle, not hiss).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't overuse onomatopoeia — one well-placed sound word is more powerful than five crammed into one sentence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add the Sound</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an onomatopoeic word to bring each sentence to life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The branch ___ as it broke underfoot.</li><li>The balloon ___ when the pin touched it.</li><li>Rain ___ against the tin roof.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What exact sound would this make? Is there a more precise onomatopoeic word?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an onomatopoeic word to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old door ___ open slowly.</li><li>Bees ___ around the flowers.</li><li>The fireworks ___ overhead.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence description of a thunderstorm using at least three different onomatopoeic words.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Onomatopoeia: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate the sound they describe, adding sensory immediacy to writing. Like other figurative and sound devices, it is most effective when used sparingly and precisely.&quot; This continues the term's exploration of figurative and sensory language devices.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Sensory detail in description: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies, including sensory and figurative language, to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Onomatopoeia adds an auditory dimension to the sensory toolkit students are building across the term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Expanded noun phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a noun phrase can be expanded with pre-modifiers (adjectives before the noun) and post-modifiers (prepositional or relative clauses after the noun) to add precise, layered detail.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building a Noun Phrase from Both Sides</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A noun phrase can be expanded BEFORE the noun (pre-modifiers: adjectives) and AFTER the noun (post-modifiers: prepositional phrases or relative clauses). Watch me build one from a bare noun.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Bare: the dog → Pre-modified: the scruffy, three-legged dog → Fully expanded: the scruffy, three-legged dog that lived under the bridge.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Scruffy, three-legged' goes before the noun (pre-modification). 'That lived under the bridge' goes after (post-modification, a relative clause). Together they build a rich, specific noun phrase — much more detailed than 'the dog' alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Pre-modifiers answer 'what kind?'. Post-modifiers answer 'which one, more specifically?' — use both for maximum detail.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Expand the Noun Phrase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each bare noun phrase with both a pre-modifier and a post-modifier.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the house</li><li>the teacher</li><li>the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What adjectives go before? What phrase or clause could go after to add more specific detail?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Expand each noun phrase using both pre- and post-modification.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the car</li><li>the mountain</li><li>the book</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence using a fully expanded noun phrase (pre- and post-modified) as the subject of the sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Expanded noun phrases: pre- and post-modification: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): &quot;A noun phrase can be expanded through pre-modification (adjectives placed before the noun) and post-modification (prepositional phrases or relative clauses placed after the noun), allowing writers to layer precise detail around a single noun.&quot; This is an advanced Phase 2 grammar skill combining several earlier concepts (adjectives, prepositional phrases, relative clauses).</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Building detailed noun phrases: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description.&quot; This lesson draws together adjective, prepositional phrase, and relative clause knowledge from across the year into a single, powerful descriptive tool.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Rhetorical questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a rhetorical question is asked for effect rather than to elicit an answer, used in persuasive writing to provoke thought and draw the reader into agreeing with the writer's position.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Asking Without Expecting an Answer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A rhetorical question isn't asked to get an answer — it's asked to make the reader think, and often the answer is obvious. Watch me use one to open a persuasive paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: Plastic pollution is a serious problem. → Rhetorical: Do we really want future generations to inherit oceans choked with plastic?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The rhetorical question forces the reader to imagine the consequence themselves — more persuasive than simply stating a fact. The implied answer ('No, of course not') pulls the reader towards agreeing with the writer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Use rhetorical questions sparingly — one per paragraph at most, usually at the start or end, or they lose their power.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Turn Statements into Rhetorical Questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Convert each plain statement into a rhetorical question that makes the same point more persuasively.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Animals should not be kept in tiny cages.</li><li>Wasting food is unacceptable when so many people go hungry.</li><li>Every student deserves access to a good education.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What question could you ask whose obvious answer makes your point for you?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a rhetorical question for each persuasive topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: protecting native wildlife.</li><li>Topic: reducing screen time for children.</li><li>Topic: keeping local libraries open.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short persuasive paragraph that opens AND closes with a rhetorical question.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Rhetorical questions for persuasive effect: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a variety of sentence structures and types — including questions — for effect,&quot; specifically rhetorical questions in persuasive writing, where they are used to provoke thought rather than seek information.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Engaging the reader in argument: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; particularly in persuasive writing where drawing the reader into actively considering the argument (rather than passively receiving it) strengthens persuasive impact.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing repetitive sentence openers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students identify repeated sentence-opening patterns (especially repeated subjects) across a passage and revise using the variety of openers learned this term.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spotting the Repetition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If every sentence in a paragraph starts the same way, it feels monotonous. Watch me find and fix repeated openers using this term's toolkit (fronted adverbials, -ing/-ed clauses, varied subjects).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The dog ran across the field. The dog jumped over the fence. The dog barked at the cat.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence starts with 'The dog' — repetitive. Fix using variety: 'Barking excitedly, the dog ran across the field and jumped over the fence to chase the cat.' I combined ideas AND used an -ing opener instead of repeating the subject three times.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Underline the first two or three words of every sentence in a paragraph. If you see the same pattern twice in a row — vary it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Fix the Repetition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This paragraph repeats its sentence openers. Let's vary them together using techniques from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain started to fall. The rain grew heavier. The rain flooded the streets. The rain finally stopped at midnight.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences could combine? Which opener type (fronted adverbial, -ing, -ed) could replace a repeated subject?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this paragraph, fixing the repetitive sentence openers using at least two different techniques from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The girl walked into the room. The girl looked around nervously. The girl saw her friends waiting. The girl smiled with relief.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Check a paragraph of your own writing for repeated sentence openers and fix at least two.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing… making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact.&quot; This lesson trains students to scan specifically for repeated sentence openings — a common Years 5–6 weakness — and apply the term's full toolkit (fronted adverbials, -ing/-ed clauses) to fix it.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying sentence opener variety: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity.&quot; This editing lesson consolidates every sentence-opener technique taught this term into one applied skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Idioms and figurative expressions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): idioms are fixed figurative expressions whose meaning cannot be worked out literally from the individual words; students use idioms appropriately and understand their effect on tone.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Expressions That Don't Mean What They Say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning isn't literal — you can't guess it just from the individual words. Watch me use one correctly and explain its effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: 'It's raining cats and dogs' doesn't mean animals are falling from the sky — it means it's raining very heavily.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Idioms add personality and a conversational tone, which suits informal narrative or dialogue — but they can sound out of place in formal writing. 'She was over the moon about her results' fits a casual recount, but not a formal report.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Idioms suit informal/narrative writing and dialogue. Avoid them in formal persuasive or explanation writing, where literal precision matters more.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Use Idioms Appropriately</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each idiom fits the writing context given, and explain why or why not.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'It cost an arm and a leg' — in a casual recount about a holiday.</li><li>'It cost an arm and a leg' — in a formal report on government spending.</li><li>'Break the ice' — in a story where two characters meet for the first time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does this idiom suit the tone and formality of the writing? What might be a more precise alternative for formal writing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use each idiom correctly in a sentence, matching it to an appropriately informal context.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use the idiom 'once in a blue moon' in a sentence.</li><li>Use the idiom 'hit the nail on the head' in a sentence.</li><li>Use the idiom 'under the weather' in a sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find an idiom you've heard before. Explain its literal words, its real (figurative) meaning, and write a sentence using it correctly.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Idioms and figurative expressions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Idioms are fixed figurative expressions whose meaning cannot be derived literally from their individual words. Their use is generally suited to informal or conversational registers.&quot; This completes the term's run of figurative vocabulary devices (simile, personification, metaphor, onomatopoeia, idiom).</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Matching figurative language to register: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases… that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This lesson connects directly to the term's register work (Week 5–6), reinforcing that even figurative language must be matched to the formality of the text.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Parenthesis review: commas, brackets, dashes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): commas, brackets, and dashes can all mark parenthetical information; students choose between the three based on the level of formality and emphasis required, consolidating a full term's worth of punctuation knowledge.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Three Tools, One Job</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Commas, brackets, and dashes can all set off extra (parenthetical) information — but each creates a different feel. Watch me use all three for the same information.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Commas (neutral): The kiwi, our national bird, is flightless. | Brackets (quiet aside): The kiwi (our national bird) is flightless. | Dashes (emphatic): The kiwi — our national bird — is flightless.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same information, three different effects. Commas are the most neutral and common choice. Brackets feel like a quiet, almost whispered aside. Dashes draw attention and create drama. The choice depends on how much emphasis the information deserves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Default to commas unless you have a reason to choose otherwise: brackets for a quiet factual aside, dashes for emphasis or drama.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Mark</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add parenthetical punctuation to each sentence, and justify whether commas, brackets, or dashes suit it best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mount Cook the highest peak in New Zealand attracts climbers worldwide.</li><li>My brother who is usually quite shy gave a brilliant speech.</li><li>The final score a stunning 3-0 surprised everyone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a neutral aside, a quiet fact, or something worth emphasising? Which punctuation matches that?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add the most appropriate parenthetical punctuation to each sentence and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Treaty of Waitangi signed in 1840 is central to New Zealand's history.</li><li>My dog who never listens finally came when called.</li><li>The exam worth 50% of the final grade is next week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about the same fact, using commas in one, brackets in the second, and dashes in the third — explain how the feeling changes each time.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Parenthesis: commas, brackets, and dashes compared: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): &quot;Commas, brackets, and dashes can all mark parenthetical (non-essential) information within a sentence. The choice between them signals different levels of formality and emphasis: commas are neutral, brackets are understated, and dashes are emphatic.&quot; This consolidates parenthesis punctuation taught separately across Term 1 and Term 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Choosing punctuation deliberately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect.&quot; This review lesson requires students to make an explicit, justified choice between three previously-separate punctuation tools, deepening their control over punctuation as a craft decision.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Embedding clauses for sophistication":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): writers combine multiple clause types (relative, subordinate, and participial) within a single sentence to create sophisticated, information-dense sentences appropriate for Years 5–6 writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Stacking Clauses Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A sophisticated sentence can combine several clause types — relative, subordinate, and participial — within one sentence. Watch me build one, layer by layer.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Base: The scientist made a discovery. → + relative clause: The scientist, who had studied the reef for ten years, made a discovery. → + subordinate clause: The scientist, who had studied the reef for ten years, made a discovery that would change marine biology forever, because the species had never been recorded before.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I started simple, then added a relative clause (who had studied...), then a subordinate clause (because the species...). Each addition adds genuine information, not padding. The sentence stays grammatically correct throughout — I check it still makes sense after each addition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Add one clause at a time and re-read the whole sentence. If it becomes confusing or ungrammatical, simplify — don't just keep piling clauses on.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build a Layered Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a sentence together, adding one clause type at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: The bridge collapsed. (add a relative clause about its age, then a subordinate clause explaining why)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does each addition add real information? Is the sentence still grammatically correct and clear?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a layered sentence for each base, adding at least two different clause types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: The expedition succeeded.</li><li>Base: The painting sold for a record price.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one fully layered sentence (at least three clauses) about a topic of your choice. Read it aloud to check it's still clear, not just complicated.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Embedding multiple clause types: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; including sentences that combine relative, subordinate, and participial clauses. This is one of the most demanding Phase 2 sentence-construction skills, drawing together a full year of grammar learning.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Checking complexity against clarity: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense.&quot; This lesson explicitly teaches that sentence sophistication must not come at the cost of clarity — students learn to test each addition against comprehensibility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Self-editing with a full checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a comprehensive, multi-pass editing checklist (punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, sentence variety, paragraph cohesion) to independently revise a piece of writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Pass Per Rule</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional writers don't try to fix everything at once. I'll model a full self-edit using one pass per category: punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, sentence variety, cohesion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist: ☐ Capitals &amp; end punctuation ☐ Subject-verb agreement ☐ No vague words (nice, good, went) ☐ Varied sentence openers ☐ Paragraphs linked with connectives</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pass 1: I read only for punctuation, marking fixes. Pass 2: I read only for grammar errors. Pass 3: I hunt vague words and upgrade them. Pass 4: I check sentence openers for repetition. Pass 5: I check paragraph links. Five short, focused passes catch far more than one rushed read-through.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Trying to fix everything in one read is why errors get missed. Slow down — one category, one pass.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Edit Together, One Pass at a Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's edit this paragraph together, doing one checklist pass at a time as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the dog ran fast it was a nice dog it went to the park the park was nice too everyone said it was a good day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What did punctuation pass find? What did the vague-word pass find? What about sentence variety?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this paragraph using the full five-pass checklist. Mark and rewrite all your corrections.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>my family went to the beach it was a good day we did lots of fun things the weather was nice we had a good time and went home tired</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the full five-pass checklist to a piece of your own writing from earlier this term.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Comprehensive self-editing checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking punctuation, grammar, vocabulary, and structure.&quot; This lesson teaches the multi-pass strategy — one category per read-through — as the most reliable method for thorough self-editing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Systematic revision strategies: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): &quot;Effective revision involves applying a systematic checklist across multiple read-throughs, rather than attempting to address all aspects of writing quality simultaneously.&quot; This consolidates every editing strategy taught across the year into one transferable habit.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Expanding noun phrases for detail":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students apply pre- and post-modification of noun phrases within full sentences and paragraphs, choosing which nouns deserve expansion for maximum descriptive impact.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Choosing Which Nouns Deserve Detail</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not every noun needs expanding — that would be exhausting to read. Today we practise choosing the most important nouns in a passage to expand. Watch me decide.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: A man walked into a room and saw a painting. → Selectively expanded: A tall, weathered man, his coat dripping from the storm outside, walked into the dim room and saw a painting that seemed to watch him back.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I didn't expand every noun — 'the room' got a little detail (dim), but I focused most of my effort on 'the man' (important character) and 'the painting' (the focus of the scene). Expanding everything would slow the pace too much.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: which 1–2 nouns in this sentence matter most to the reader? Save your detail for those.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose and Expand</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this sentence, decide which noun is most important and expand only that one fully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A dog ran towards a boy near a tree.</li><li>A ship sailed into a harbour past a lighthouse.</li><li>A girl opened a door into a room.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which noun is the focus of this sentence? How much detail does it deserve compared to the others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence, selectively expanding only the most important 1–2 nouns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bird landed on a branch above a river.</li><li>A car stopped outside a shop on a street.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence description of an arrival scene (someone or something arriving somewhere), selectively expanding only the most important nouns in each sentence.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Selective noun phrase expansion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;expand sentences by adding words, phrases, and clauses to add detail and description,&quot; while also exercising judgement about which elements of a sentence most need expansion. This extends Week 7's expanded noun phrase work into a genuine compositional choice.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling pace through selective detail: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader.&quot; Over-expanding every noun slows pace and overwhelms the reader — this lesson teaches students to use detail strategically, a hallmark of more mature writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Passive voice in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): passive voice is the conventional choice in explanation and procedural writing when the process or outcome matters more than who performed the action.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — When Passive Voice Is the Right Choice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We learned passive voice can weaken narrative writing — but in explanation and procedural writing, it's often the BEST choice, because the process matters more than who did it. Watch me apply it correctly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Active (narrative feel): Workers mix the concrete and pour it into moulds. → Passive (explanation feel): The concrete is mixed and poured into moulds.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In an explanation of how concrete is made, we don't care WHO mixes it — we care about the process. Passive voice removes the unnecessary 'workers' and keeps focus on the steps. This is the opposite lesson from our earlier 'cut the passive voice' editing work — context decides which is correct.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: does this text type focus on a process/outcome (passive often works) or on characters taking action (active usually works better)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Voice for the Text Type</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Decide whether each sentence works better as active or passive, based on its text type, and convert if needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explanation: A chef adds the yeast to the flour. (how bread is made)</li><li>Narrative: The flour was added by the chef as the storm raged outside.</li><li>Explanation: People recycle the bottles at the depot.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this explanation (focus on process) or narrative (focus on character)? Which voice suits it better?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in the voice that best suits its text type, and explain your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explanation: People filter the water before it reaches our taps.</li><li>Explanation: Engineers test the bridge for safety every year.</li><li>Narrative: The cake was baked by Grandma with so much love.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence explanation of how something works (e.g. how rain forms), using passive voice deliberately and correctly throughout.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Passive voice in explanation text type: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to make &quot;deliberate grammatical choices appropriate to text type,&quot; recognising that passive voice is the conventional and often preferred construction in explanation and procedural writing, where process matters more than agent.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Voice and text-type convention: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Different text types favour different grammatical conventions; explanation writing typically favours passive constructions that foreground process over agent.&quot; This lesson deliberately revisits and complicates Term 3 Week 2's active-voice editing lesson, teaching students that 'rules' depend on text-type purpose.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sequencing sentences in recount and procedure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): time connectives (firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, finally) sequence events clearly in recount and procedural writing, signalling the order in which actions occur.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Guiding the Reader Through Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Recount and procedural writing need clear time connectives to guide the reader through the sequence of events. Watch me sequence a set of jumbled steps.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Jumbled: We arrived at the beach. We packed our togs. We went swimming. We had lunch.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sequenced: Firstly, we packed our togs. Then, we arrived at the beach. After that, we went swimming. Finally, we had lunch. The connectives make the order crystal clear and stop the reader from having to guess what happened when.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Vary your time connectives — don't just use 'then' for every step. Try firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, eventually, finally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Sequence Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Put these jumbled events in a logical order, adding a time connective to each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The seeds were watered every day. The seeds were planted in the soil. The seedlings were transplanted outside. The plant finally flowered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the correct order? Which connective best introduces each step?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sequenced procedure or recount using these jumbled events, with a different time connective for each step.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jumbled: The bread rose. The dough was kneaded. The bread was baked. The ingredients were mixed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 5-step recount of your morning routine, using a different time connective to introduce each step.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Time connectives in recount and procedure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): &quot;Recount and procedural texts are organised sequentially, signalled to the reader through a range of time connectives (firstly, next, after that, meanwhile, finally). Sequencing connectives should be varied rather than repetitive.&quot; This is a core text-type structural feature at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Sequencing events clearly: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas… using a range of connectives&quot; appropriate to text type. This lesson gives students a varied bank of time connectives to replace the over-reliance on 'then' common in Years 5–6 recount writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing for text-type structure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply text-type-specific structural checklists when peer editing, checking that a partner's writing matches the expected structure for narrative, persuasive, or explanation text types.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Editing for Structure, Not Just Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today's edit isn't about commas or spelling — it's about whether the WHOLE piece follows its text type's expected structure. Watch me check a persuasive paragraph against a structural checklist.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Checklist for persuasive paragraph: ☐ Clear position stated early ☐ PEEL structure followed ☐ At least one persuasive connective ☐ Formal register maintained</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I read the whole paragraph once for structure only — ignoring small grammar issues for now. Does it open with a clear position? Yes. Does it follow Point-Evidence-Explain-Link? Partially — there's no clear Explain sentence. I note this as the priority fix, more important right now than a missing comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Structural problems matter more than small errors — fix the shape of the piece first, then polish sentence-level details.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Structural Check Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Check this persuasive paragraph against the structural checklist together. What's missing?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plastic bags should be banned. They take years to break down. Many shops have already stopped using them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is there a clear position? Does it follow PEEL? Is anything structurally missing?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of persuasive or explanation writing with a partner. Check it against the relevant structural checklist and give one specific piece of feedback.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Checklist (persuasive): clear position, PEEL structure, persuasive connectives, formal register.</li><li>✦ Checklist (explanation): clear introduction, logical cause-and-effect sequence, time/cause connectives.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Identify the single most important structural fix your partner's piece needs, and explain why it matters more than smaller errors.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work,&quot; including feedback on text-type structure specifically, not only sentence-level accuracy. This lesson trains students to prioritise structural feedback over minor error-spotting.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying text-type checklists: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea&quot; matching the conventions of the text type being written. This consolidates the term's persuasive (PEEL) and explanation (cause-effect) structural work into an applied peer-review skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Term review: choosing the best figurative device":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review and select the most effective figurative device (simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, idiom) for a given descriptive purpose, consolidating the term's vocabulary work.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Sentence, Many Possible Devices</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we learned five figurative devices: simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, and idiom. Today, watch me choose the BEST one for a specific purpose, not just any one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Describing a fierce wind: Simile — the wind howled like a wolf. Metaphor — the wind was a wolf, snarling at the windows. Personification — the wind tore at the rooftops in fury.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All three work, but personification feels most powerful here because it makes the wind an active, threatening force throughout a whole passage — not just one comparison. I choose based on which device best sustains the mood across multiple sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't just pick the first device that comes to mind — try two or three, then choose the one that creates the strongest, clearest image.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Try Multiple Devices</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each description, try at least two different figurative devices, then decide which works best and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A calm lake at sunrise</li><li>A crowded, chaotic market</li><li>An old, creaking ship</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which device created the clearest, most original image? Why does it suit this scene better than the others?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose the best figurative device for each scene and write one strong sentence using it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A roaring waterfall.</li><li>A sleepy, quiet village at dusk.</li><li>A blazing, uncontrolled bushfire.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4–5 sentences) describing a thunderstorm, deliberately combining at least three different figurative devices from this term.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Figurative language: term review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): &quot;Figurative language devices — including simile, metaphor, personification, onomatopoeia, and idiom — each create different effects for the reader. Skilled writers select the device best suited to their purpose rather than defaulting to a single familiar technique.&quot; This consolidates the full range of figurative language taught across Term 3.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Deliberate device selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience.&quot; This review lesson moves students from being able to use each device individually to making a genuine craft choice between them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Term review: mixed grammar":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students apply and distinguish between this term's grammar concepts — active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct/reported speech, and conjunction types — in a mixed review.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Spotting Which Rule Applies</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term covered active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct/reported speech, and conjunction types. Today's challenge is recognising which rule applies to a given sentence. Watch me work through a mixed set.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: &quot;The trophy must have been won by the strongest team,&quot; she said.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I check each feature: 'must have been' = a modal verb (must) combined with passive voice (been won). 'she said' with speech marks = direct speech. This single sentence uses three of our term's grammar concepts at once — recognising them is the skill we're practising today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Work through one grammar category at a time: first check voice, then modals, then speech type, then conjunctions — don't try to spot everything in one glance.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify the Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify which grammar features (voice, modal verbs, speech type, conjunction type) appear in each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She said that she might be late.</li><li>The cake was baked by my grandmother, although she rarely cooks.</li><li>&quot;We could win this,&quot; the coach shouted, &quot;if we work together.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this active or passive? Is there a modal verb? Direct or reported speech? Coordinating or subordinating conjunction?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the grammar features in each sentence, then write one original sentence combining at least two features from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify the features: &quot;The match was cancelled because of the storm, and everyone went home disappointed.&quot;</li><li>Identify the features: &quot;You should always check your work,&quot; the teacher reminded them.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write one sentence that combines passive voice, a modal verb, AND reported speech.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Mixed grammar review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): consolidates Term 3's grammar strand — voice (active/passive), modal verbs, direct and reported speech, and conjunction types — requiring students to recognise multiple grammatical features operating within a single sentence, a key indicator of secure grammatical understanding at the end of Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Applying grammar knowledge flexibly: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and structures.&quot; This review lesson tests flexible application rather than isolated practice, mirroring how grammar features combine in real, independent writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Term review: constructing a flexible paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): students construct a single paragraph that deliberately applies a range of this term's sentence-building techniques — varied openers, embedded clauses, semicolons, and rhetorical questions.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Bringing It All Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we build one paragraph using as many of this term's sentence techniques as possible, while keeping it clear and purposeful. Watch me plan and write one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: arriving at a new school.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I plan first: opener type? (-ing clause). A semicolon link? A rhetorical question to close? Draft: &quot;Clutching her new backpack tightly, Mere stepped through the gates for the first time; everything felt unfamiliar, from the unfamiliar faces to the strange smell of fresh paint. Would anyone even notice her standing there?&quot; I checked off three techniques deliberately, not by accident.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Plan your techniques BEFORE drafting — decide which 2–3 you'll use, then write with that plan in mind, rather than hoping they appear naturally.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan and Build Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, plan which techniques to use, then build a paragraph together about this topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: a sudden change in weather during a sports match. Plan: opener type? semicolon or colon? a figurative device? a rhetorical question?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which techniques work together naturally? Does the paragraph still read smoothly, or does it feel forced?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan and write a paragraph (5–6 sentences) on the topic given, deliberately including at least four different techniques from this term (e.g. varied opener, semicolon, figurative device, rhetorical question, modal verb).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: discovering something unexpected.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and identify which techniques they used — did you find all of them?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Deliberate combination of sentence techniques: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity,&quot; demonstrated here through planned, deliberate combination of multiple techniques within a single coherent paragraph — the culmination of the term's sentence-building strand.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Planning before drafting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader,&quot; including planning specific techniques before drafting rather than relying on them to occur incidentally. This is a key independent-writing habit for Years 5–6.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Whole-piece edit: applying the term's checklist":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply the full Term 3 editing checklist (figurative language, voice, register, cohesion, sentence variety, punctuation) to a complete piece of their own writing as the term's culminating revision task.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Complete Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is the term's biggest edit — applying everything we've learned to a whole piece of writing. Watch me model the full checklist on a sample paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Full checklist: ☐ Figurative language used purposefully ☐ Voice (active/passive) suits text type ☐ Register matches audience ☐ Paragraphs cohesively linked ☐ Sentence openers varied ☐ Punctuation accurate (including speech, semicolons, parenthesis)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I work through each item on a sample paragraph, one at a time, making at least one specific improvement per category where needed — not just ticking boxes, but genuinely revising. This is exactly the process a published writer goes through before a piece is finished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't rush the final categories because you're tired — punctuation and cohesion are just as important as the first checks.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Edit a Sample Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the full checklist to this short paragraph as a class, fixing at least three issues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fire was started by accident. It was bad. Everyone got out ok. Firefighters came and put it out it was a relief for everyone.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does each checklist category reveal? Which fix matters most?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the full Term 3 editing checklist to a piece of your own writing from this term (or the paragraph you wrote on Wednesday). Mark every change you make.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✦ Work through each checklist category in turn: figurative language, voice, register, cohesion, sentence variety, punctuation.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short reflection — which checklist category improved your writing the most this term, and why?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Whole-piece systematic editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to &quot;reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking vocabulary, punctuation, and structure.&quot; This culminating lesson requires students to apply a comprehensive checklist independently to a complete piece, demonstrating the full range of Phase 2 writing process skills.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Reflecting on growth as a writer: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): &quot;Students reflect on their development as writers, identifying which strategies have most improved their writing.&quot; This metacognitive reflection closes the term, connecting explicit skill instruction to students' growing self-awareness as writers.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Precise verb choice: replacing weak verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): precise, specific verbs carry more meaning than vague general ones; replacing weak verbs (went, said, did, got, moved) with precise alternatives makes writing more vivid and economical.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Strong Verb Does the Work of Three Weak Ones</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weak verbs like 'went', 'said', 'did', 'moved', and 'got' are placeholder verbs — they tell us almost nothing about HOW something happened. Watch me replace them with precise alternatives that carry the scene.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: She went quickly across the room and said in a loud voice that she was angry. → She stormed across the room and snapped that she was furious.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Went quickly' = two words doing a weak job. 'Stormed' = one word that gives direction, speed, AND mood. 'Said in a loud voice that she was angry' = seven words. 'Snapped' = one word that gives volume, emotion, AND manner. Precise verbs make writing leaner and more powerful simultaneously.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: When you find a weak verb, ask: HOW did they do it? The answer is usually your precise verb (crept, bolted, muttered, slammed).</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade the Verb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace the weak verb in each sentence with a more precise one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The cat moved along the fence in the dark.</li><li>He said something under his breath and went out.</li><li>She did the maths quickly and got the right answer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does HOW it happened tell us — is it fast, slow, fearful, angry? What single verb captures that?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace every weak verb (went, said, got, did, moved, looked) in each sentence with a precise alternative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dog went to the door and looked at us.</li><li>She said she was going and got her bag.</li><li>He moved through the crowd and did his best to get past.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take three consecutive sentences from a piece of your own writing. Find every weak verb and upgrade each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise verb choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically choosing precise, specific verbs over vague placeholders. Precise verb choice is one of the highest-leverage vocabulary skills for Years 5–6 writing quality.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Economy and precision in word choice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting vocabulary that is economical — doing more work with fewer words. This lesson establishes precise verb choice as the cornerstone vocabulary skill of Term 4.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Colons — introducing lists and elaboration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): a colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the idea in the main clause; the clause before the colon must be a complete sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Colon Announces What's Coming</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A colon says: here comes the detail, the list, or the explanation. The clause before the colon must be a complete sentence. Watch me use it two ways.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: List: She packed everything she needed: a torch, a sleeping bag, and three days' worth of food. | Elaboration: There was only one problem: nobody had thought to bring a map.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before each colon I have a complete sentence. After the colon comes either a list (items separated by commas) or an elaboration that explains or expands the main clause idea. I don't put a capital after a colon unless it's a proper noun.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test: can the words before the colon stand alone as a complete sentence? If not, you need more before the colon — or replace it with a comma.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Place the Colon</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon to each sentence in the right place, then check the words before it form a complete sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>There are three rules in this classroom respect others, work hard, and stay curious.</li><li>The forecast was grim heavy rain, strong winds, and possible flooding.</li><li>She had one ambition to represent New Zealand at the Olympics.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where does the list or elaboration begin? Is everything before that point a complete sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add a colon to each sentence and check it is correctly placed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The museum contained many fascinating things ancient fossils, hand-painted maps, and a real Viking helmet.</li><li>His decision surprised everyone he chose to walk away from the prize.</li><li>The recipe requires four key ingredients flour, eggs, butter, and sugar.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two original sentences using colons — one introducing a list, one introducing an elaboration.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge)</em> — Colons for lists and elaboration: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Knowledge): A colon introduces what follows — a list, an explanation, or an elaboration of the main clause idea. The clause preceding the colon must be grammatically complete. The colon is a key Phase 2 punctuation mark expected in more sophisticated explanation and persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Punctuation (Practice)</em> — Applying colons accurately: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Punctuation (Practice): Students are expected to use a range of punctuation accurately and for effect, including the colon for introducing lists and elaboration in explanation and persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Using a colon to introduce and expand an idea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): students build sentences that use a colon to set up a main idea and then expand or clarify it in the second part, creating a sophisticated two-part sentence structure common in formal writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Building the Two-Part Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most powerful use of a colon is in a two-part sentence where part one makes a claim and part two proves, explains, or dramatically reveals it. Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Claim then proof: The ancient kauri was extraordinary: it had stood for over 2,000 years, surviving storms, drought, and the arrival of humans. | Build-up then reveal: She opened the door and stopped: the room was completely empty.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the first example, the colon promises an explanation of 'extraordinary' and delivers one. In the second, the colon creates a pause that makes the reveal more dramatic. Both structures set up reader expectation with part one, then satisfy it with part two.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read part one aloud: does it feel like it's promising something? If yes, the colon is probably right. If part one already feels complete and closed, a full stop may suit better.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Complete the Two-Part Sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each sentence starter with a strong part-two after the colon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team had one weakness:</li><li>The explorer made a startling discovery:</li><li>There was only one thing left to do:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What does part one promise? Does part two deliver a strong explanation, detail, or reveal?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the full two-part sentence for each prompt, using a colon to link them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with: 'The storm had one final surprise'</li><li>Start with: 'Every great piece of writing shares one quality'</li><li>Start with: 'She had been preparing for this moment for ten years'</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph opening (3–4 sentences) where the first sentence uses a colon for dramatic elaboration.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Two-part colon sentence structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including the two-part colon sentence where the first clause makes a claim or builds anticipation and the second clause provides elaboration or revelation.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Building anticipation and revelation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including structural choices that create anticipation. The two-part colon sentence is one of the most reliable tools for creating a moment of revelation or emphasis in both narrative and persuasive writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: upgrading weak verbs throughout a passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students systematically identify and replace weak, vague verbs throughout a whole passage, applying the precise verb skill from Monday in a sustained editing context.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Weak Verb Hunt</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'll show you how to edit a whole passage for weak verbs — methodically, not randomly. I highlight every weak verb first, then replace each one before moving on.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Jake went to school. He looked at his friends and said hello. They all went into class and did their work quietly. The teacher looked up and said they could go.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Highlight: went (x2), looked (x2), said (x2), did. Replace: Jake trudged / He glanced at his friends and nodded. They filed into class and worked quietly. The teacher glanced up and murmured they were free to go. Now every verb earns its place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Highlight ALL weak verbs before changing any of them — this stops you from improving the first sentence and forgetting the rest.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Highlight Then Replace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Highlight every weak verb in this passage first, then replace them one at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The soldiers went across the field. They moved carefully because they didn't want to be seen. The leader said they should stop. Everyone stopped and looked around nervously.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many weak verbs did we find? Which replacement verb best captures the mood — tense, cautious, military?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage by replacing every weak verb with a more precise one. Aim to cut word count at the same time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The girl went into the kitchen and looked in the fridge. She got out some food and did some cooking. She said to herself that it smelled good. Her mum came in and said she was impressed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count the total words before and after your edit. How many words did you save while making the writing stronger?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Systematic weak-verb editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches a systematic, two-step editing strategy — highlight all targets first, then replace — applied specifically to weak verbs across a whole passage.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Verb precision across a sustained passage: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Sustaining precise verb choice across an entire passage — not just a single sentence — is the more demanding and authentic application of this skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Pathetic fallacy: weather and setting reflecting mood":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge) &amp; Composing (Practice): pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather reflects or reinforces the emotional mood of a scene or character.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — When the World Matches the Feeling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pathetic fallacy is when the weather or setting mirrors the mood of the scene — the storm that breaks during a crisis, the sunshine that appears at a moment of hope. Watch me use it deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Neutral: It was raining as she read the letter. → Pathetic fallacy: The rain hammered against the windows as she read the letter, each drop driving harder as if sharing her despair.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the neutral version, rain is just a fact. In the pathetic fallacy version, the rain actively reflects and amplifies her emotional state. I chose 'hammered' and 'driving harder' to match 'despair' — the word choice in the weather description mirrors the emotional register of the scene.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The weather shouldn't just be present — it should seem to participate in the mood. Match your weather verbs and adjectives to the emotion of the scene.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Match Weather to Mood</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a pathetic fallacy description of the weather that reinforces the mood given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mood: dread and foreboding (a character approaches an abandoned house)</li><li>Mood: joy and relief (a team wins an important match)</li><li>Mood: loneliness and isolation (someone waits at an empty bus stop)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What weather suits this mood? What verbs and adjectives make the weather seem to share the feeling?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 1–2 sentences of pathetic fallacy for each scene.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character receives unexpected good news on a grey morning.</li><li>A villain makes their entrance during a hot, still afternoon.</li><li>Two old friends meet again after many years apart.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4–5 sentences) opening a story, where pathetic fallacy in the first two sentences sets the emotional tone for everything that follows.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Pathetic fallacy: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Pathetic fallacy is a literary technique in which the natural environment or weather is described as reflecting or participating in the emotional atmosphere of a scene. Effective use requires matching weather vocabulary — verbs and adjectives — to the emotional register being conveyed. This is a key narrative craft technique at Year 5–6.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Creating mood through setting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies, including figurative language and setting description, to create texts that engage the reader. Pathetic fallacy is the deliberate integration of setting and mood — a hallmark of more accomplished narrative writing at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Verb tense consistency in narrative":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): narrative writing must maintain a consistent past or present tense throughout; tense shifts within a passage are errors unless used deliberately for effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Staying in One Tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most narrative writing is written consistently in either the past tense or the present tense. Accidentally switching between them mid-passage is one of the most common errors at Years 5–6. Watch me find and fix tense shifts.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: She walked into the room and sees a shadow by the window. She stops and her heart races. She had looked around but nothing moves.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Walked' = past. 'Sees', 'stops', 'races' = present — tense shift. 'Had looked' = past perfect (unnecessary here). Fix: She walked into the room and saw a shadow by the window. She stopped and her heart raced. She looked around but nothing moved. All consistent past tense now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Before editing for tense, decide: is this piece past tense or present tense? Then read through looking ONLY for verbs, checking every one matches your decision.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Find and Fix the Tense Shifts</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Find every tense error in this passage and fix it to maintain consistent past tense.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The door creaks open and Hemi steps inside. He looks around the dark room. A noise comes from upstairs. He grabbed his torch and moves to the stairs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are in the wrong tense? What is the consistent tense this passage should use?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage to make it consistently past tense throughout.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The team arrives at the stadium early. They warm up on the field and the coach gives them a talk. Everyone had felt nervous, but when the whistle blew they play their best game of the season.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph of your own in present tense (as if the reader is watching a scene unfold right now) and check it is fully consistent.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Verb tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Narrative writing must maintain a consistent verb tense — past or present — throughout a passage. Unintentional tense shifts are grammatical errors that disrupt the reader's experience. Deliberate tense shifts such as past perfect for flashback must be clearly controlled and purposeful.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for tense consistency: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking verb tense. This lesson makes tense consistency an explicit editing skill — deciding the tense first, then reading specifically for verb forms.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Past perfect for flashback":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): the past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the main narrative past tense; it is the grammatical tool for writing flashback sequences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Going Further Back in Time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When we're writing in past tense and want to refer to something that happened even earlier — a flashback — we shift into the past perfect (had + past participle). Watch me build a flashback using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Main narrative (past): She stared at the old photograph. Flashback (past perfect): She had found it tucked inside her grandmother's journal, on the morning everything had changed. Return to narrative: Now, ten years later, she finally understood what it meant.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The flashback uses 'had found' and 'had changed' — past perfect — to signal these events happened BEFORE 'stared'. When returning to the main narrative, I switch back to simple past. The tense change IS the signal to the reader that we've moved in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: You only need a few past perfect verbs to establish a flashback. Once the reader is clearly in the past, you can drop back to simple past for the body of the flashback.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Flashback</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use past perfect to add a short flashback to each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She looked at the trophy on the shelf. (Add a flashback to the day she won it.)</li><li>He paused at the gate of the old house. (Add a flashback to his childhood there.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What past perfect verbs open the flashback? How do you signal the return to the main narrative?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph (3–5 sentences) for each prompt, using past perfect to signal a flashback and simple past to return.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character finds a faded letter and remembers the day it was written.</li><li>An athlete stands at the start line and recalls her first ever race.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a piece of narrative writing you've done this year and add a flashback sentence using past perfect to deepen the backstory.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Past perfect for flashback: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The past perfect tense (had + past participle) signals that an action was completed before the point established by the main narrative past tense. It is the grammatical mechanism for writing flashback sequences, allowing writers to move back in time within a past-tense narrative.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Controlling narrative time: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and to use tense deliberately as a structural tool. The past perfect flashback is a significant expansion of students' narrative toolkit — allowing them to manage non-linear storytelling at the sentence-grammar level.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing for tense consistency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a tense-consistency editing pass to a multi-paragraph narrative, identifying unintentional tense shifts, distinguishing them from deliberate past perfect flashbacks, and correcting errors systematically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Tense Edit Pass</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today I'll model the tense editing pass on a full paragraph — circling every verb, labelling it (simple past / past perfect / present), deciding if shifts are errors or intentional, and fixing the errors.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The explorers push through the undergrowth. It had been three days since they had lost the path. Now the forest grows darker around them. Last year, Mia had studied this very region — she was their only hope.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Verbs: 'push' (present — error), 'had been' (past perfect — intentional), 'had lost' (past perfect — intentional), 'grows' (present — error), 'had studied' (past perfect — intentional). Fix: 'pushed', 'grew'. The past perfect verbs stay because they correctly reach back in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Circle verbs in pencil first, label each one, THEN erase and fix — never edit while circling or you'll lose track of the pattern.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Circle, Label, Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, circle every verb in this passage, label it (simple past / past perfect / present), and fix any unintentional tense shifts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Aroha runs toward the finish line. She had trained for six months for this moment. Her legs ache but she keeps going. Back in January, she had nearly quit. Now she crossed the line first.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which verbs are errors? Which past perfect verbs are intentional? How do you tell the difference?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage for tense consistency. Fix unintentional shifts — keep intentional past perfect verbs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old lighthouse stood at the edge of the cliffs. For years, it had guided ships through the dangerous strait. Now, the light was broken and the keeper has long since gone. He had left one winter when a storm had damaged the lens beyond repair. Today the building sits empty, its windows dark.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap a piece of narrative writing with a partner. Each of you do a tense edit pass and report back what you found.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Tense consistency editing pass: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check accuracy, including verb tense. This lesson makes tense editing a deliberate, methodical pass — circle all verbs, label, then fix — distinguishing between unintentional errors and purposeful past perfect flashbacks.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Distinguishing error from intention in tense: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use tense deliberately for effect, which requires the meta-skill of distinguishing an intentional past perfect construction from an accidental tense shift. This editing lesson develops exactly that judgement within the context of whole-passage revision.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Emotive language in persuasive writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): emotive language deliberately targets the reader's feelings — using words with strong emotional connotations — to make a persuasive argument feel more urgent, personal, and compelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Making the Reader Feel the Argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Emotive language doesn't just state facts — it makes the reader feel something about them. Watch me compare a neutral and an emotive version of the same argument.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Neutral: Some animals are kept in small spaces in zoos. This is not always good for them. → Emotive: Magnificent wild creatures are condemned to pace the same few square metres for their entire lives — robbed of everything nature intended for them.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Animals' becomes 'magnificent wild creatures'. 'Small spaces' becomes 'the same few square metres'. 'Not always good' becomes 'condemned', 'robbed' — strong verbs with very negative connotations. The facts are the same — the emotional charge is completely different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Choose emotive words that match your audience's values — what do THEY care about? Tap into those feelings specifically.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Upgrade to Emotive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each neutral statement using emotive language that targets the reader's feelings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some children don't have enough food.</li><li>Old people sometimes feel alone.</li><li>The native forest is being cut down.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotions should the reader feel? Which words or phrases most powerfully trigger those feelings?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each statement using emotive language to make it more persuasive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plastic bags are found in the ocean.</li><li>Animals are tested on in laboratories.</li><li>Children spend a lot of time on screens.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two versions of a persuasive opening sentence about a cause you care about — one neutral, one emotive. Explain what each version does differently to the reader.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Emotive language in persuasion: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Emotive language deliberately selects words with strong emotional connotations to make persuasive writing feel urgent and personal, targeting the reader's feelings rather than only their reason. Emotive vocabulary choices are matched to the values and concerns of the intended audience.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Selecting words for emotional impact: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, specifically selecting vocabulary for deliberate emotional impact in persuasive writing. This extends Term 3's connotation work into the persuasive writing context.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Conditional sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): conditional sentences express situations and their consequences using if/unless/provided that; students distinguish real (first) from unreal (second) conditionals and use them in persuasive and explanation writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Depends on What</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (real/possible): If + present simple, will + infinitive. Second conditional (unreal/hypothetical): If + past simple, would + infinitive. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: First (real): If we reduce plastic use, we will see cleaner oceans within a decade. | Second (unreal): If every child had access to books, the world would be a very different place.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First conditional = a realistic possibility and its likely result. Second conditional = a hypothetical scenario and its imagined result. In persuasive writing, first conditionals make arguments feel achievable; second conditionals paint a vivid picture of what could be.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Check the tense pair: If + present then will (first). If + past then would (second). Mixing them creates an error.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Conditional</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete each conditional sentence with the correct form, then label it first or second.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If schools banned single-use plastics, they ___ (reduce) their environmental footprint.</li><li>If every student reads for 20 minutes a day, their vocabulary ___ (improve).</li><li>If there were more cycle paths, more people ___ (choose) to cycle to work.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this a real/likely scenario (first) or a hypothetical one (second)? What verb form does each need?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a conditional sentence for each persuasive topic, choosing first or second conditional and explaining your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: reducing food waste.</li><li>Topic: planting more native trees.</li><li>Topic: giving students more choice in what they study.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write two persuasive sentences about the same topic — one using a first conditional and one using a second conditional. Explain how each creates a different effect on the reader.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Conditional sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Conditional sentences link a condition to a consequence. First conditional (if + present simple, will + infinitive) expresses a real or likely possibility; second conditional (if + past simple, would + infinitive) expresses a hypothetical or unreal scenario. Both are important tools in persuasive writing at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Using conditionals in persuasive writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of structures, including conditional sentences used deliberately for persuasive effect — first conditional for achievable arguments, second conditional for vivid hypothetical scenarios.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Counter-argument and rebuttal sentence structure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a sophisticated persuasive text acknowledges the opposing view (counter-argument) and then dismisses or qualifies it (rebuttal); students practise the structural pattern for doing this within a single paragraph.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Showing Both Sides, Winning One</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A strong persuasive writer doesn't ignore the other side — they raise it themselves and then knock it down. This shows intellectual honesty and makes the argument more credible. Watch me build a counter-argument and rebuttal.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Counter: Some people argue that banning plastic bags is inconvenient and costly for businesses. Rebuttal: However, the long-term environmental cost of plastic pollution — estimated at billions of dollars in ocean clean-up — far outweighs any short-term inconvenience.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I raised the opposing view fairly. Then I used 'however' to pivot and answered it with evidence that outweighs the objection. The rebuttal must be STRONGER than the counter-argument — if it isn't, I haven't won the point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Signal words for counter-argument: 'Some argue that', 'Critics claim'. Signal words for rebuttal: 'However', 'Nevertheless', 'Yet', 'Despite this'.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Counter-Rebuttal Pair</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each position, build a counter-argument sentence and then a rebuttal sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Position: School uniforms should be compulsory.</li><li>Position: Students should have phones allowed in class.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the strongest objection someone would make? Is the rebuttal stronger than the objection?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full counter-argument and rebuttal paragraph (3–4 sentences) for the given position.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Position: All schools should have a vegetarian-only canteen.</li><li>Position: Homework should be banned for students under 12.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a PEEL paragraph you wrote earlier this term and add a counter-argument and rebuttal to it, making it into a fuller, more sophisticated persuasive paragraph.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Counter-argument and rebuttal: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning, including in persuasive writing the acknowledgement and dismissal of opposing views. Counter-argument and rebuttal is an advanced structural expectation for Year 5–6 persuasive writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Building credibility through balance: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including in persuasive writing the strategy of conceding then rebutting, which builds the writer's credibility and authority.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing: strengthening a weak persuasive argument":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students diagnose the specific weaknesses in a persuasive paragraph (vague evidence, missing rebuttal, informal register, weak connectives) and make targeted revisions to strengthen it.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Diagnosing What's Weak</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not all persuasive problems are the same. Watch me read a weak persuasive paragraph and name exactly what's wrong before fixing anything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: I think everyone should recycle because it's really important. Lots of people already do it. Some people don't bother but they should. Recycling is just a good thing to do.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diagnosis: (1) Informal register — 'I think', 'really', 'just'. (2) No specific evidence — 'lots of people' is vague. (3) Counter-rebuttal is present but undeveloped. (4) No persuasive connectives. (5) No PEEL structure. Now I know exactly what to fix, in order of importance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Always diagnose BEFORE revising — if you just start rewriting, you might fix one problem and miss four others.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read this paragraph and produce a diagnosis list before making any changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kids should be able to choose what they eat at school. The food at school isn't very nice. Some people say healthy food is important but lots of kids don't like it. So schools should let kids choose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: How many problems can we name? Which is the most serious? What is the priority order for fixing them?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diagnose then rewrite this paragraph to fix all its weaknesses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zoos are bad for animals. Animals don't like being in cages. People argue that zoos help conservation but animals are still unhappy. We should close zoos.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Diagnose a piece of your own persuasive writing from earlier this term using the same checklist. Write a brief diagnosis, then make the most important fixes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Diagnosing persuasive weaknesses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches diagnosis as a deliberate pre-revision step — naming specific weaknesses before beginning revision.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Targeted persuasive revision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning. This editing lesson consolidates all of Term 4's persuasive skills — register, PEEL, counter-rebuttal, connectives, and emotive language — into a single diagnosis-and-repair process.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Technical vocabulary in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to the topic being explained; students learn to introduce technical terms with definitions and use them consistently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Using the Right Words for the Job</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing uses technical terms — the exact, agreed vocabulary of a subject. Watch me introduce a technical term correctly and then use it consistently.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak: Water turns into gas when it gets hot enough. → With technical vocab: When water reaches 100 degrees Celsius, it undergoes evaporation — the process by which liquid water converts into water vapour. This vapour rises and forms part of the water cycle.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I introduced the technical terms 'evaporation' and 'water cycle' with built-in definitions, then used them consistently instead of replacing them with vaguer words. Technical writing is precise because its audience expects accuracy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Introduce each technical term with a definition the first time, then use it consistently. Don't replace it with a vague synonym — that reduces precision.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Introduce and Use</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upgrade each sentence by introducing a technical term with a definition, then use it in a follow-up sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The moon's pull makes the ocean water move up and down. (technical term: tidal force / tides)</li><li>Plants use sunlight to make food. (technical terms: photosynthesis, chlorophyll)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the correct technical term? How do we define it for the reader the first time?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence by introducing the appropriate technical term and defining it, then write a second sentence using the term correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rocks are broken into smaller pieces by weather and water. (key term: erosion/weathering)</li><li>The heart pumps blood around the body. (key terms: circulation, artery/vein)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-sentence explanation paragraph about a process you know well, using at least two technical terms correctly introduced with definitions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Technical vocabulary in explanation writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Explanation writing uses precise technical vocabulary specific to its subject. Technical terms are introduced with clear definitions when first used and then applied consistently — not replaced with vague synonyms — because precision is central to the explanation text type at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Consistent use of technical terms: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including accurate technical vocabulary appropriate to explanation writing. This lesson makes technical term introduction and consistent usage an explicit writing strategy.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Present tense conventions in explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense because it describes how things generally work (timeless truth), not what happened on one specific occasion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Timeless Truth vs. One-Time Event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing describes how things GENERALLY work — so it uses the simple present tense (timeless truth), not the past tense (which suggests it only happened once). Watch me compare.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Past tense (narrative): The volcano erupted and lava flowed down the hillside. → Present tense (explanation): When pressure builds beneath the earth's surface, magma forces its way through cracks and erupts as lava.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The past tense version sounds like a specific historical event. The present tense version describes the general, repeatable process — which is what explanation writing does. 'Forces', 'erupts' = timeless present = this is how it always works.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If your explanation says what happened, switch it to say what happens. That one word change shifts a narrative sentence into an explanation sentence.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Switch to Explanation Tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each past-tense narrative sentence as a present-tense explanation sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The roots of the plant absorbed water from the soil and carried it to the leaves.</li><li>The caterpillar made a cocoon and changed into a butterfly inside.</li><li>The snow melted when the temperature rose above zero.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the rewritten sentence describe a general, repeatable process? Does it still make sense in the present tense?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each sentence in the timeless present tense appropriate for explanation writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The earthquake shook the buildings and caused cracks in the roads.</li><li>The bee flew from flower to flower and collected pollen on its legs.</li><li>The light passed through the prism and split into seven colours.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 3-sentence explanation of how something natural works, consistently in the timeless present tense.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Present tense in explanation writing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Explanation writing typically uses the simple present tense to convey timeless truth — describing how processes generally work, not what happened on one specific occasion. This distinguishes explanation from narrative and recount text types, which use past tense for specific events.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Text type and tense convention: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): Different text types use different tense conventions: narrative and recount use past tense for specific events; explanation uses simple present for general processes; procedure uses imperative or present tense for instructions. Understanding tense as a text-type signal is a key Phase 2 metalinguistic understanding.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Structuring a full explanation paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): students build a complete, well-structured explanation paragraph with a topic sentence, logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using technical vocabulary, and a concluding link sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Explanation Paragraph Blueprint</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A well-structured explanation paragraph follows a clear pattern: topic sentence (what you're explaining) → cause-and-effect body sentences (the how/why) → concluding link (the outcome or significance). Watch me build one.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: How does rain form? Topic sentence: Rain forms through a continuous cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation. Body: When the sun heats surface water, evaporation occurs — liquid water converts to water vapour and rises into the atmosphere. As the vapour cools at higher altitudes, it condenses around tiny dust particles, forming clouds. When cloud droplets combine and grow heavy enough, they fall as precipitation. Link: This cycle repeats continuously, driven by solar energy, and is essential to all life on Earth.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence does a specific job. Topic sentence names the process and its stages. Body sentences use cause-and-effect connectives and technical terms. Concluding link gives the broader significance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read your topic sentence and your concluding link together. They should form a logical bracket around the paragraph.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, build a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: How does a rainbow form? (Key terms: refraction, spectrum, wavelength)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What goes in the topic sentence? What are the key cause-and-effect steps? What does the concluding link say?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a complete explanation paragraph for the topic given, using the full blueprint: topic sentence → cause-and-effect body → concluding link.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: How do volcanoes form? (Key terms: magma, tectonic plates, eruption, lava)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an explanation paragraph on a topic from another subject you're studying at the moment, applying the same blueprint and using correct technical vocabulary.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Structuring an explanation paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea, supported by relevant detail and reasoning specific to the explanation text type. This lesson gives students an explicit, repeatable blueprint — topic sentence, cause-and-effect body, concluding link — as a transferable cross-curricular writing skill.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Explanation paragraph structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Knowledge): A well-structured explanation paragraph opens with a topic sentence naming the process to be explained, develops through logically sequenced cause-and-effect sentences using appropriate technical vocabulary, and closes with a linking sentence that states the outcome or broader significance.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing explanation writing for clarity and accuracy":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply an explanation-specific editing checklist — checking tense, technical vocabulary, cause-and-effect connectives, paragraph structure, and factual accuracy — to revise a weak explanation paragraph.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Explanation Edit Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation writing has its own specific editing checklist. Watch me apply it to a weak paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The water cycle happens when water goes up into the sky. It then came back down as rain. The clouds made from water make rain fall. This is called the water cycle and it happened all the time.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Checklist: Present tense? — 'came' = past tense error. Technical vocabulary? — 'goes up' should be 'evaporates'. Cause-and-effect connectives? — none used. Topic sentence? — present but vague. Concluding link? — 'happened' = tense error; 'all the time' = informal. Fix each in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Accuracy matters in explanation writing — if you're not sure a fact or term is correct, flag it for checking rather than leaving an error in.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Apply the Checklist Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the explanation editing checklist to this paragraph together — check each item in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Photosynthesis is when plants made food. They use the sunlight which hits the leaves. The green stuff in leaves (chlorophyll) catches the sun. Carbon dioxide went in and oxygen comes out. Plants need this for living.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which checklist item has the most errors? What is the priority order for fixing this paragraph?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this explanation paragraph using the full checklist — tense, technical vocabulary, connectives, structure, and accuracy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Erosion happens when rocks and soil got moved by wind and water. Rivers carried rocks downstream and they break into smaller pieces over time. This is why valleys formed and the coastline changes. Erosion happened over millions of years.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Apply the explanation editing checklist to the paragraph you wrote on Wednesday. What did you find?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Explanation-specific editing checklist: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary, punctuation, and structure to improve clarity and impact, specifically applying text-type criteria to explanation writing. This lesson gives students a five-point explanation checklist as a transferable cross-curricular editing tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Revising explanation structure: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise explanation writing with clear cause-and-effect structure and appropriate language features. The editing checklist consolidates all four days of this week's explanation writing work into one applied revision skill.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Synonyms for said — precise dialogue tags":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): 'said' is a neutral dialogue tag; replacing it with a precise verb (muttered, snapped, insisted, whispered) tells the reader HOW something was said and adds characterisation without additional description.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Said Tells Us Nothing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Said' is invisible — readers skip over it. A precise dialogue tag tells the reader the manner, tone, and emotion of speech in one word. Watch me upgrade dialogue by choosing precise tags.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: 'I don't know where it is,' she said. → 'I don't know where it is,' she muttered, not meeting his eyes.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Muttered' immediately tells us she's evasive, uncertain, perhaps embarrassed. I also added a small beat ('not meeting his eyes') to show rather than tell the emotion further. The combination of precise tag + action beat is the most powerful dialogue technique at this level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Match the tag to the emotion, not just the volume: 'shouted' only tells us it was loud. 'Snarled', 'barked', 'demanded' tell us it was loud AND angry/aggressive.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Precise Tag</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace 'said' with the most precise dialogue tag for the context described.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'Leave me alone,' she said. (emotion: angry, defensive)</li><li>'I think... maybe... we could try it,' he said. (emotion: uncertain, nervous)</li><li>'I know exactly where it is,' she said. (emotion: confident, perhaps smug)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What emotion AND manner is the character expressing? Which verb captures both?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace 'said' with a precise dialogue tag and add a short action beat to show the emotion further.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'That's not fair,' she said.</li><li>'Come on, we'll be fine,' he said.</li><li>'I didn't do it,' the boy said.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 4-line dialogue between two characters in conflict, using a different precise dialogue tag for each line — no 'said' allowed.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Precise dialogue tags: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including precise dialogue tags that convey manner and emotion rather than simply signalling speech. This is a specific application of the term's precise-verb-choice work to dialogue writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Characterisation through dialogue: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including characterisation through dialogue. Precise dialogue tags and action beats together create character voice and emotional subtext without relying on explicit description.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Concessive clauses":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a concessive clause (introduced by although, even though, despite, while, whereas) acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more important one, adding sophistication to both persuasive and analytical writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Acknowledging Before Asserting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A concessive clause admits something is true, then asserts a more important contrasting truth. It's the grammar of nuance. Watch me build sentences using it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Plain: Some students find homework hard. Many still benefit from it. → Concessive: Although some students find homework challenging, the majority benefit significantly from the practice it provides.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Although' introduces the concession (the admitted truth), then the main clause asserts the stronger claim. Other concessive words: 'even though', 'despite', 'while', 'whereas'. Notice that 'despite' takes a noun phrase, not a clause: 'Despite its challenges, homework...' — not 'Despite it is challenging'.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: 'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing form, not a full clause. 'Despite its being difficult' is correct. 'Despite it is difficult' is not.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Concessive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair using a concessive clause opener.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It was raining heavily. The match continued. (use: despite)</li><li>The new policy has its critics. It has already reduced pollution by 20%. (use: although)</li><li>The climb was gruelling. The view from the summit made it worthwhile. (use: even though)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part is the concession and which is the main assertion? Does 'despite' need a noun or -ing form?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a concessive sentence for each pair using the conjunction given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use 'although': Electric cars are currently expensive / they produce far lower emissions over their lifetime.</li><li>Use 'despite': The project took three years / the results exceeded all expectations.</li><li>Use 'whereas': Some students prefer group work / others find individual tasks more productive.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences about the same controversial topic, each using a different concessive conjunction. Do they create subtly different effects?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Concessive clauses: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A concessive clause, introduced by although, even though, despite, while, or whereas, acknowledges one idea while asserting a contrasting or more significant one. 'Despite' takes a noun phrase or gerund, not a full clause. Concessive clauses are a hallmark of sophisticated Phase 2 persuasive and analytical writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Nuanced sentence construction: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including concessive constructions that create nuance by balancing two contrasting ideas within a single sentence. This is closely related to the counter-argument work of Week 3.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Building tension with sentence length variation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice) &amp; Composing (Practice): writers deliberately manipulate sentence length to control pace and tension — short sentences slow the reader down and create impact, long sentences sustain flow or build complexity.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Controlling the Reader's Heartbeat</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Short sentences make the reader stop, breathe, and feel the weight of a moment. Long sentences carry them forward in a rush of action or detail. The writer's job is to control this deliberately. Watch me use both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Long then short: The crowd surged forward, voices rising to a single deafening roar as the scoreboard flickered, counted down, and finally froze on zero. Then: silence.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The long sentence mimics the building rush of noise and action. 'Then: silence.' — two words, the colon, the full stop — brings everything to a dead halt. The contrast makes both sentences more powerful than if the whole passage were the same length.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: For a sudden shock or revelation, make it the shortest sentence in the paragraph. The contrast with the sentences around it does the work.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Design the Tension</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by varying the sentence lengths to build tension toward the last sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She walked to the door. She opened it. There was a figure standing outside. She screamed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which sentences should be long (building dread)? Which single sentence should be the shortest (impact point)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tense paragraph (5–7 sentences) for each scenario, deliberately varying sentence length to build toward a single short impact sentence at the end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario: a player takes the last penalty in a shootout.</li><li>Scenario: a character reaches the top of a mountain and sees what's on the other side.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a tense moment in a book you're reading. Count the sentence lengths. Does the author use this technique? Copy the pattern into your own writing about a different scenario.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Sentence length variation for tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, specifically using deliberate sentence-length contrast to control pace and tension in narrative writing. This formalises the intuitive understanding of rhythm developed in Term 3 into a conscious narrative tool.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Controlling narrative pace and tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including deliberate sentence-length manipulation for pacing and tension. This is one of the highest-level narrative craft skills at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Editing narrative pacing and tension":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a pacing-and-tension editing lens to a narrative passage — identifying where pace is too flat, adding sentence-length variation, short impact sentences, and precise verbs to heighten tension.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Pace</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When editing for pacing, I read the passage aloud at its current rhythm and ask: where does it drag? Where does it rush past something important? Watch me diagnose and fix a flat passage.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: He walked to the edge. He looked down. It was very high. He felt scared. He stepped back.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every sentence is the same length — the rhythm is completely flat. Fix: He crept to the edge, his breath shallow and quick. He looked down. Far below, the river glittered, cold and indifferent. He was so high. His foot found the edge — and he stepped back. Now there's a build, a reveal, a moment of dread, an impact, and a release.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Read the passage aloud. If every sentence takes the same breath to say — vary at least one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Diagnose and Fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read this passage aloud. Identify where the pacing is flat, then rewrite it with deliberate sentence-length variation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The storm was getting worse. The boat rocked from side to side. A wave hit the deck. Everyone held on. Another wave came. The boat tilted dangerously.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where should pace slow down to build dread? Where should a single short sentence hit like a punch?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Edit this passage for pacing and tension using sentence-length variation, precise verbs, and one short impact sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The lion moved through the grass. It was looking at the zebra. The zebra didn't see it. The lion got closer. Then it ran at the zebra.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take the most tense moment from a piece of your own narrative writing. Read it aloud, identify the flattest part, and revise specifically for sentence-length variation and precise verb choice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Editing for pacing and tension: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to vocabulary and structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson teaches reading aloud as a pacing diagnostic tool and then applies sentence-length variation and precise verbs as the specific revision tools.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Pacing as a craft decision: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including managing narrative pacing as a deliberate craft choice. This editing lesson applies Wednesday's sentence-length technique to a real revision context.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Cohesive substitution — avoiding repetition":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): cohesive substitution replaces repeated nouns or noun phrases with synonyms, pronouns, or general-reference nouns to create flow without monotony, while maintaining clarity about who or what is being referred to.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Saying the Same Thing a Different Way</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Repeating the same noun in every sentence is monotonous. But replacing it carelessly can confuse the reader. Watch me substitute carefully.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Repetitive: The kiwi is a flightless bird. The kiwi is nocturnal. The kiwi is endangered. The kiwi can only be found in New Zealand. → Substituted: The kiwi is a flightless, nocturnal bird. This remarkable creature is endangered and can only be found in New Zealand — our unique national symbol.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'The kiwi' becomes 'this remarkable creature' (adds description) and then 'our unique national symbol' (adds perspective). Both substitutions keep it clear we're still talking about the kiwi, while adding new information.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Never substitute in a way that could confuse the reader about who or what you mean. If the substitution could refer to something else, keep the noun.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Substitute Without Confusing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite this passage by substituting the repeated noun at least twice — without confusing the reader.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kauri tree is one of New Zealand's most ancient trees. The kauri tree can live for thousands of years. The kauri tree was heavily logged in the 19th century. The kauri tree is now a protected species.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What synonyms or pronoun references could replace 'the kauri tree'? Does each substitution still clearly refer to the kauri?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each passage using cohesive substitution to reduce repetition without losing clarity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth. Elephants live in family groups. Elephants have excellent memories. Elephants are threatened by habitat loss and poaching.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph of your own writing and highlight every repeated noun. Try at least two substitutions — then check that a reader could still follow clearly who or what you're talking about.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Cohesive substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including using synonyms, pronouns, and reference nouns as cohesive substitution devices to reduce repetition while maintaining textual clarity. This is an advanced vocabulary and cohesion skill at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Cohesion through substitution: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to use cohesive devices — including pronouns and reference words — to link ideas within and across sentences. This lesson extends Term 3's cohesion work beyond connectives into the more subtle substitution devices that distinguish mature Phase 2 writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Relative clauses for embedded definitions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): a relative clause (introduced by which, who, where, whose) can embed a definition or explanation directly inside a sentence, avoiding a clunky separate definition sentence — a key technique in explanation and formal writing.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Defining Without Interrupting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Instead of writing a separate definition sentence, I can embed the definition inside the main sentence using a relative clause. Watch me do it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Separate: Photosynthesis is a process. It converts sunlight into energy. Plants use it. → Embedded: Photosynthesis, which converts sunlight into chemical energy, is the process that sustains almost all plant life on Earth.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The relative clause 'which converts sunlight into chemical energy' is embedded between commas, defining the term right where it appears. The reader gets the definition without the flow being interrupted by a separate sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If the relative clause is essential to identifying WHICH thing you mean, don't use commas. If it's adding extra information about something already identified, use commas around it.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Embed the Definition</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine each pair into one sentence using a relative clause to embed the definition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Erosion is a process. It gradually wears away rock and soil.</li><li>Te Tiriti o Waitangi is a document. It was signed in 1840 between Maori chiefs and the Crown.</li><li>The ozone layer is a region of Earth's atmosphere. It absorbs most of the sun's ultraviolet radiation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the relative clause add extra information (commas) or identify which one (no commas)?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite each pair as a single sentence, embedding the definition with a relative clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Metamorphosis is a process. It transforms a caterpillar into a butterfly.</li><li>New Zealand is a country. It was the first in the world to grant women the right to vote.</li><li>A neuron is a cell. It transmits electrical signals through the nervous system.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Find a technical term in your reading or class notes. Write a sentence that introduces and defines it using an embedded relative clause.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Relative clauses for embedded definitions: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity, including using relative clauses to embed definitions or elaborations directly within a sentence — a key tool for explanation and formal writing. This consolidates relative clause knowledge from earlier terms into a specific, functional application.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Technical vocabulary integration: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including integrating technical terms with their definitions smoothly and economically within explanation sentences. The embedded relative clause definition is the most sophisticated tool for this at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences and paragraph-closing sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Text Structure (Practice): a well-constructed paragraph opens with a topic sentence that states the main idea and closes with a sentence that clinches or links that idea; students distinguish strong from weak topic sentences and write effective closers.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Opening and Closing a Paragraph Cleanly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The topic sentence is a promise to the reader about what this paragraph will deliver. The closing sentence clinches that promise or links to the next paragraph. Watch me write both.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Weak topic sentence: 'This paragraph is about recycling.' → Strong: 'Recycling reduces the pressure on landfill sites while conserving the natural resources needed to produce new materials.' Weak closer: 'So that is why recycling is good.' → Strong: 'For these reasons, recycling is not merely a personal choice — it is a shared environmental responsibility.'</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The strong topic sentence makes a specific claim that the paragraph can prove. The strong closer doesn't just repeat the topic — it elevates it, leaving the reader with a stronger impression.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Test your topic sentence: could the reader predict what your paragraph will contain? Test your closer: does it add something, or just repeat the topic sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Improve the Topic and Closer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Improve the weak topic sentence and closer for this paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weak topic: 'New Zealand has a lot of native birds.' [body: kiwi, kakapo, takahe details] Weak closer: 'So New Zealand native birds are quite unique.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What specific claim could the topic sentence make? What does the closer need to add — elevation, significance, or a link?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong topic sentence and closing sentence for each paragraph body given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Body: sentences about how exercise releases endorphins, reduces stress hormones, and improves sleep quality.</li><li>Body: sentences about how deforestation reduces biodiversity, increases carbon emissions, and leads to soil erosion.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take any paragraph from a piece of your own writing. Rewrite its topic sentence to make a more specific claim, and rewrite its closing sentence to elevate or link rather than just repeat.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Topic and closing sentences: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with a clear main idea established by a strong topic sentence, and to close paragraphs with a sentence that clinches or links — not merely repeats — the main idea. Strong topic and closing sentences are hallmarks of well-controlled Phase 2 writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Paragraph-level composition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including at the paragraph level — opening with a specific, testable claim and closing with an elevated or linking sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Adapting a piece for a different audience":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Vocabulary (Practice): students revise a piece of writing for a different audience, changing vocabulary, register, examples, and level of detail to suit the new reader without changing the core information or argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Same Message, Different Reader</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The same information can be written very differently depending on the audience. Watch me adapt a paragraph about recycling from a formal audience to a young audience.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Formal: Recycling reduces landfill pressure and conserves finite natural resources, contributing to a circular economy. | Young audience: When we recycle, we help keep rubbish out of enormous rubbish dumps called landfills, and we save natural materials so they can be used again.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same facts, completely different vocabulary, sentence structure, and examples. Neither version is wrong — they're both right for their audience.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask three questions before adapting: (1) How much does this audience already know? (2) What vocabulary will they understand? (3) What examples will feel relevant to their lives?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Adapt Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adapt this paragraph — written for adults — for an audience of Year 4 students.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>New Zealand's native biodiversity is under significant threat from introduced predators, particularly rats, stoats, and possums, which compete with or prey directly upon endemic species.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What vocabulary needs changing? What background knowledge can we assume? What everyday examples could replace the technical ones?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Adapt each passage for the new audience given.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Original (for Year 6): 'Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert solar energy into glucose using chlorophyll.' → Adapt for Year 2.</li><li>Original (for Year 2): 'Leaves are green because of a special ingredient called chlorophyll.' → Adapt for an adult science magazine.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own persuasive or explanation writing and adapt it for two different audiences — your school principal, and a Year 3 student. What changes between the two versions?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Audience adaptation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies appropriate to audience and purpose, including adapting existing writing for a new reader by changing vocabulary, register, sentence structure, and examples while preserving the core content. Audience adaptation is a key Phase 2 composing skill with direct cross-curricular application.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Register and vocabulary for audience: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience. This lesson applies the full year's vocabulary and register work to a genuine revision task — rewriting for a different audience — making register not just a label but an active writing decision.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Hyperbole for effect":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Art of Enormous Exaggeration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hyperbole is deliberate over-the-top exaggeration for effect. Unlike lying, the reader KNOWS it's exaggerated — that's the point. Watch me use it for comic and dramatic effect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Comic: I've told you a million times to clean your room. | Dramatic: The silence in the hall was so complete you could have heard a heartbeat three streets away.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'A million times' is obviously untrue — its comic effect comes from the scale of the exaggeration. 'Heard a heartbeat three streets away' is physically impossible — its dramatic effect comes from the image of extreme, unnatural stillness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Hyperbole works best when it's CLEARLY impossible — the more obviously exaggerated, the better. Mild exaggeration just sounds like an inaccuracy.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Build the Hyperbole</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a hyperbolic sentence for each situation, making the exaggeration as vivid and obviously impossible as possible.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bag was very heavy.</li><li>The test was very long.</li><li>He was extremely hungry.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the exaggeration obvious enough to be clearly hyperbole? Does it create a vivid image?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a hyperbolic sentence for each prompt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She ran very fast.</li><li>The crowd was enormous.</li><li>It was a very boring speech.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short comic paragraph (3–4 sentences) about a small everyday annoyance using at least two hyperboles. Aim to make the reader laugh.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Hyperbole: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Hyperbole is deliberate and obvious exaggeration used for humorous, dramatic, or emphatic effect — not intended to be taken literally by the reader. Its effectiveness depends on the scale of the exaggeration being recognisably impossible. This completes the year's range of figurative language devices.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Comic and dramatic voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including humour as a deliberate compositional choice. Hyperbole is one of the primary tools for creating comic voice in writing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Sentence fragments for deliberate effect":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): a sentence fragment lacks a subject, verb, or complete thought; while fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing for pace, emphasis, and dramatic effect.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Breaking the Rules on Purpose</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A fragment is technically incomplete — but skilled writers use them deliberately in narrative for impact. The key is: it must be clearly intentional, not accidental. Watch me use a fragment deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Complete sentences: He reached the top of the hill and looked out at the view. He had never seen anything like it. → With deliberate fragment: He reached the top of the hill and looked out. Nothing could have prepared him. Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Endless. Wild. Breathtaking.' — three fragments in a row. They create a staccato, breathless effect, mimicking the gasp of someone overwhelmed by a view. The incompleteness IS the effect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Only use deliberate fragments in creative/narrative writing — NEVER in formal, persuasive, or explanation writing. And always be able to explain why the fragment is there.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Add a Deliberate Fragment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add one or two deliberate fragments to heighten the impact of each passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>She opened the box. Inside was a letter she had been waiting for.</li><li>The final whistle blew. The scoreboard showed the result.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where would a single-word or short fragment create the most impact? Is the fragment placement clearly intentional?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add deliberate fragments to each narrative moment to heighten the effect. Mark each fragment with [F] so it's clear it's intentional.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He stepped off the plane into the cold air of a new country.</li><li>She looked at the test results for a long time.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph describing an overwhelming moment that uses at least three deliberate fragments for effect.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Deliberate sentence fragments: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): A sentence fragment is a grammatically incomplete sentence lacking a subject, verb, or complete thought. While fragments are errors in formal writing, skilled authors use them deliberately in narrative writing to create pace, emphasis, or a staccato dramatic effect. Deliberate fragments must be clearly intentional and contextually justified.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Style and craft in narrative voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader. Understanding when and why to break a grammar rule — and being able to justify the choice — is a hallmark of the most sophisticated writing at Phase 2 and into Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Opening hooks for different text types":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): an effective opening hook immediately engages the reader; different text types use different hook strategies — narrative (in-scene, sensory), persuasive (bold claim, statistic, rhetorical question), explanation (surprising fact, question).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The First Sentence Does the Heavy Lifting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The opening sentence determines whether the reader continues. Watch me write strong hooks for three different text types.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Narrative hook (in-scene): The smell of smoke reached her before the alarm did. | Persuasive hook (bold claim): Every day, the equivalent of 2,000 trees' worth of paper is wasted in New Zealand's schools. | Explanation hook (surprising fact): Despite its name, a starfish is not actually a fish — and cannot survive outside saltwater for more than a few minutes.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each hook uses a different strategy: narrative puts the reader immediately into a sensory moment. Persuasive opens with a specific, credible statistic. Explanation opens with a counterintuitive fact that creates curiosity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The hook's job is to make the reader ask a question they want answered. What question does each of your hooks make the reader want to answer?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Write the Hook</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a strong opening hook for each text type and topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Narrative: a character who wakes up in an unfamiliar place.</li><li>Persuasive: why zoos should be redesigned as conservation sanctuaries.</li><li>Explanation: how the human eye works.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which strategy suits each text type? Does your hook make the reader want to read the next sentence?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write two different opening hooks for each topic — one using a narrative strategy, one using a persuasive or explanation strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: the ocean.</li><li>Topic: learning a new skill.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Go back to a piece of writing you completed earlier this term. Rewrite its opening sentence using a different hook strategy — does the new hook work better?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Opening hooks by text type: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including selecting an appropriate opening hook strategy matched to the text type and purpose. Narrative hooks use sensory/in-scene openings; persuasive hooks use bold claims, statistics, or rhetorical questions; explanation hooks use surprising or counterintuitive facts.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Text-type appropriate openings: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas with openings appropriate to the text type, including hooks that immediately signal purpose and engage the reader.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Structural edit — whole-text level":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): a structural edit evaluates the organisation of a complete piece of writing — checking that the opening hooks, paragraphs are in the right order, transitions are clear, and the ending resolves what the beginning promised.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Editing the Shape, Not the Sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A structural edit ignores spelling and grammar — it only asks: does the whole piece have the right shape? Watch me apply a structural checklist to a piece plan.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Structural checklist: Does the opening hook immediately engage? Are paragraphs in the most logical/effective order? Is there a clear climax or argument progression? Do transitions between paragraphs feel inevitable? Does the ending resolve or reflect back on what the opening promised?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I apply this top-down, not bottom-up. A structural problem (paragraph 3 should be paragraph 1) is more important than a word choice issue — fix structure first, always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Print or copy your writing and cut it into paragraphs. Can you rearrange them into a better order? If one paragraph could be removed without the reader noticing — it probably should be.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Structural Diagnosis</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the structural checklist to this piece outline together. What needs moving or improving?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Conclusion: recycling is important. 2. Evidence: statistics about landfill. 3. Counter-argument. 4. Hook: what if every piece of rubbish you ever threw away was still here? 5. Main argument. 6. Rebuttal.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is this in the right order? Where does the hook belong? What should the conclusion say rather than just 'recycling is important'?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the structural checklist to a full piece of your own writing. List at least two structural improvements, then make the most important one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Does the opening hook immediately engage?</li><li>Are paragraphs in the most effective order?</li><li>Is there a clear progression/climax?</li><li>Do transitions feel inevitable?</li><li>Does the ending deliver on the opening's promise?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Swap with a partner and do a structural edit of their piece. Tell them: what the shape is, what is working, one specific structural change you'd recommend.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Structural editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing, making changes to structure to improve clarity and impact. This lesson distinguishes structural editing (whole-text organisation) from sentence-level editing — establishing that structure is always revised first, before sentence-level polish.</li><li><em>Writing — Text Structure (Practice)</em> — Whole-text organisation: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Text Structure (Practice): Students are expected to organise ideas into paragraphs with clear whole-text shape — appropriate opening, logical progression, and satisfying ending. The structural edit is the culminating text-structure skill of the year, applied independently to a complete piece.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Vocabulary of concession":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge a valid opposing point while maintaining their own position, adding nuance and credibility to argument.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Conceding Without Surrendering</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Concessive vocabulary lets you admit something is true (concede a point) while keeping your main argument firmly in place. Watch me use several different concessive words to do this.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Admittedly, electric cars are currently more expensive to purchase. Nevertheless, their lower running costs and environmental benefits make them the more sensible long-term investment.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>'Admittedly' signals: I acknowledge this objection. 'Nevertheless' signals: but here's why my argument still holds. Other pairs: 'Granted... even so'; 'It is true that... yet'; 'That said...'. The concession makes the writer seem balanced and credible.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The concessive word and the rebuttal word are a pair — don't use one without the other, or your argument sounds unfinished.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Choose the Right Concessive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add an appropriate concessive phrase and a rebuttal to each situation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some people argue that homework causes too much stress. [Concede + rebut]</li><li>Visiting zoos may be educational for some children. [Concede + rebut]</li><li>Online learning is undeniably more flexible than traditional schooling. [Rebut this concession]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which concessive word fits best — admittedly, granted, that said, even so? Does the rebuttal actually outweigh the concession?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a concede-and-rebut sentence pair for each topic, using a different concessive word each time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: whether competitive sport is good for children.</li><li>Topic: whether social media should be available in schools.</li><li>Topic: whether animals should be kept as pets.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph on a topic of your choice that uses two different concessive phrases — one near the beginning and one near the end — to build a nuanced, credible argument.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Vocabulary of concession: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Concessive adverbs and adverbial phrases (admittedly, granted, nevertheless, even so, that said) allow writers to acknowledge valid opposing points while maintaining their own argument. They are always paired with a rebuttal signal word that reasserts the writer's position. This vocabulary extends Term 3's connective work and Term 4 Week 3's counter-argument structure into a more sophisticated one-sentence technique.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Nuance in persuasive vocabulary: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases that are fit for purpose and audience, including the vocabulary of concession in persuasive writing — words that signal intellectual balance and credibility rather than one-sided assertion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Review: subordination vs coordination across a paragraph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review and apply the distinction between coordinating and subordinating conjunctions across a full paragraph, ensuring controlled variety of simple, compound, and complex sentences.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Mix in One Paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review the year's conjunction work and check we can mix simple, compound, and complex sentences fluently in one paragraph. Watch me read a paragraph and label each sentence type.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: The hawk circled silently. [simple] It rode the thermal higher and higher, and the valley fell away beneath it. [compound] Although the air was still warm, the bird sensed the approaching evening. [complex] It folded its wings and dropped. [compound-simple]</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reading through: every sentence is grammatically correct. No two consecutive sentences are the same type. Short simple sentences contrast with longer compound and complex ones. The variety creates rhythm and sustains the reader's interest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Write a paragraph, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. If you have three in a row of the same type — vary the next one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Label and Improve</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Label each sentence in this paragraph S / Co / Cx and identify where the variety breaks down.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boat left the harbour. The sea was calm and the wind was light. They sailed north. The islands came into view. They anchored and they went ashore. They found the beach deserted.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Where is the variety lacking? Which sentences could be combined or restructured to create better rhythm?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a paragraph of 5–7 sentences on the topic given, then label each sentence S / Co / Cx. Ensure no three consecutive sentences share the same type.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: arriving at a new place for the first time.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a paragraph from your own writing. Label every sentence S / Co / Cx. If any type is missing — add or convert a sentence to include it.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Sentence variety: S/Co/Cx review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the year's sentence-structure strand — simple, compound, and complex sentences created through coordinating and subordinating conjunctions — requiring students to apply all three types within a single paragraph with controlled variety. This is a comprehensive review of the year's grammar in context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Controlled sentence variety in a paragraph: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write a variety of sentence structures for effect and clarity. This review lesson requires students to demonstrate that sentence variety is not accidental but planned — labelling sentence types and deliberately ensuring variety across a paragraph, as they will be expected to do independently in Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Combining techniques — planning before drafting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge): students plan a short piece of writing by deliberately selecting which techniques to deploy (vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation) BEFORE drafting, then write to their plan.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Technique Plan</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most controlled, effective writing comes from planning which techniques to use BEFORE you start, not hoping they appear. Watch me make a technique plan for a short piece.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: the moment before a performance. Plan: Hook — in-scene sensory opening. Vocabulary — precise verbs, pathetic fallacy. Sentences — -ing opener, long-then-short contrast for tension. Punctuation — colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for effect. Register — narrative, first person.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having this plan means every choice I make while drafting is purposeful. I'm not searching for a technique — I'm executing a plan. This is how published writers work.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Aim for 4–5 techniques in your plan. More than that and the piece feels forced — every sentence feels like it's showing off rather than telling a story.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, build a technique plan for this topic before writing a single sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: the last five minutes of a championship match.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What hook strategy? Which vocabulary techniques? Sentence structures for tension? Punctuation for effect? What register and person?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a technique plan, then write a paragraph (5–7 sentences) to your plan. Label each technique you used.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: discovering something unexpected in an old building.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the same topic twice — once with a plan, once without. Which version is more controlled and effective? What does the planning add?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Planning technique use before drafting: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including planning specific techniques before drafting — selecting vocabulary devices, sentence structures, and punctuation choices in advance rather than improvising. Planning-before-drafting is a key indicator of writing maturity at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Pre-drafting planning strategies: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan their use of techniques — vocabulary devices, sentence structures, punctuation choices, and register — before beginning to draft, rather than hoping these features will appear incidentally. This lesson formalises the planning stage of the writing process as an explicit, teachable skill.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Peer editing for voice and style":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a voice-and-style checklist when peer editing, evaluating whether a partner's writing has a consistent, distinctive voice and whether stylistic choices are deliberate and effective.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Reading for Voice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Voice is the sense that a real person is behind the words — consistent in register, distinctive in word choice, deliberate in style. Watch me read two versions of the same passage and identify which has voice and why.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Version A: The old man sat in the chair by the window. He looked at the street. It was busy. Version B: Every morning, he claimed his chair by the window like a king ascending a throne — watching the street below with the patient authority of someone who had earned the right to simply observe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Version A is accurate but voiceless. Version B has consistent register (slightly elevated, respectful), metaphor (king/throne), precise verb ('claimed'), and rhythm. The style feels like a choice, not a default.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Ask: if I removed the name from this piece, would I know who wrote it? If yes, there's voice. If it could have been written by anyone — it probably needs more.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Voice Checklist</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the voice checklist to this passage together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The forest was dark and quiet. We walked through it. There were lots of trees and bushes. We heard some birds. It was a nice experience.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What voice checklist items are missing? Register? Distinctive word choice? Figurative language? Sentence variety?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Swap a piece of writing with a partner. Apply the voice-and-style checklist and write specific feedback — one strength and one specific suggestion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Is register consistent throughout?</li><li>Are word choices distinctive — or could anyone have written this?</li><li>Is figurative language present and purposeful — or absent/cliched?</li><li>Is sentence variety clearly deliberate — or flat?</li><li>Does the piece sound like a real, specific writer?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Using your partner's feedback, revise the weakest section of your own piece for voice — aim to make it clearly, identifiably yours.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Peer editing for voice and style: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to share writing with others and respond to feedback to improve their work, including receiving and giving feedback specifically on voice and style — the most subjective but also most important dimension of writing quality at Phase 2.</li><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Developing a distinctive writing voice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, including developing a consistent, distinctive writing voice through deliberate choices of register, vocabulary, figurative language, and sentence structure. This is the culminating compositional skill of the year.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Year vocabulary review — choosing your best tool":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review the full year's vocabulary toolkit and practise selecting the best tool for a given writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This year we built a vocabulary toolkit. Today, the challenge is not 'can you use this device' but 'which device should you use here, and why?' Watch me make that choice explicitly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Prompt: describe a wildfire approaching a town. Options: simile? metaphor? personification? precise verbs? pathetic fallacy?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Personification — the fire devoured the hillside — works because fire as a predator creates dread. Pathetic fallacy — if the sky turns blood-red — sets mood before the fire arrives. Precise verbs — the fire crept, then surged, then consumed — gives pace and progression. Best combination: start with pathetic fallacy for mood, then precise verbs for pace, then one strong personification at the peak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: The question is never 'which device do I know?' It's always 'which device does this moment need?'</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Toolkit Decision</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each writing prompt, decide which vocabulary technique(s) would be most effective and explain why — before writing any sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Persuasive: argue for protecting New Zealand's native forests.</li><li>Explanation: describe how a glacier forms.</li><li>Narrative: the moment a character realises they are lost.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which vocabulary device(s) suit each text type and purpose? Why are some devices better fits than others here?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each prompt, choose your two best vocabulary tools, explain your choice, and write two sentences using them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A character watches their home disappear as a ship leaves port.</li><li>An explanation of why coral reefs are important ecosystems.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph for one of the prompts above, using at least four different vocabulary techniques deliberately. Label each one in the margin.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Year vocabulary toolkit review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's vocabulary strand — precise verbs, figurative language (simile, metaphor, personification, pathetic fallacy, hyperbole, onomatopoeia, idiom), connotation, emotive language, technical vocabulary, and register — into a meta-level skill: choosing the best vocabulary tool for a specific writing purpose rather than applying devices mechanically.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Strategic vocabulary selection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students are expected to select and use words and phrases, including figurative language, that are fit for purpose and audience. This review lesson assesses whether students have moved beyond technique knowledge to genuine technique selection — the highest-level vocabulary skill at Phase 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Year grammar review — mixed features from all four terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students review the full year's grammar strand — sentence types, conjunctions, tenses, voice, modal verbs, reported speech, conditional sentences, and clauses — applying them in a mixed, integrated context.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — One Sentence, Many Grammar Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>By now, a sophisticated sentence can contain multiple grammar features at once. Watch me build one deliberately, naming each feature as I add it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Build-up: Base: The scientists made a discovery. Add passive: A discovery was made. Add modal: A significant discovery may have been made. Add relative clause: ... by the scientists, who had been working on the project for over a decade. Add concessive: Although the research had taken over a decade, a significant discovery may finally have been made — one that could change everything.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each step adds a grammatical feature: passive voice, modal verb, relative clause, concessive conjunction. The sentence remains grammatically correct throughout. This is the level of control expected heading into Year 7.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: If you're unsure whether a multi-feature sentence is correct, simplify it. A clear, correct simpler sentence is better than a tangled complex one.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Identify All Features</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify every grammar feature from the year's learning in this sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Despite the fact that the match had been postponed three times, the players, who had trained for months, insisted that they would be ready — if the ground was deemed safe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Concessive clause? Passive voice? Past perfect? Relative clause? Conditional? Modal verb? How many features can we find?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identify the grammar features in each sentence, then write one original sentence that combines at least three features from the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>'I should have been told earlier,' she muttered, even though she suspected she wouldn't have listened.</li><li>The ancient bridge, which had stood for 400 years, was finally demolished — a decision that the council had debated for over a decade.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write three sentences — one from each main text type (narrative, persuasive, explanation) — each containing at least three grammar features from the year. Label each feature.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Year grammar toolkit review: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): Consolidates the full year's grammar strand — sentence types (S/Co/Cx), conjunction types, tense system (simple past, present, past perfect), active/passive voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, and clause types (relative, subordinate, concessive, participial) — in an integrated, multi-feature context.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Integrated grammar application: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students are expected to write grammatically correct sentences using a range of tenses and structures. This review lesson assesses the highest-level Phase 2 grammatical skill: combining multiple grammatical features within a single, still-correct sentence — the standard expected at Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Planning and drafting a complete chosen-text-type piece":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice) &amp; Writing Process (Knowledge): students independently plan and draft a complete piece in their chosen text type, applying the year's full toolkit — deliberately planned before drafting.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Full Pre-Draft Plan</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write a complete piece. But first — the full plan. Watch me build one for a persuasive piece in 5 minutes, covering all the dimensions we've learned.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Topic: why every school should have a dedicated nature area. Text type: persuasive. Hook: rhetorical question and statistic. Structure: intro, 3 PEEL paragraphs, counter and rebuttal, conclusion. Vocabulary: emotive language, concessive phrases, precise verbs. Grammar: conditional sentences, relative clauses for definitions. Sentence variety: -ing opener, colon for revelation, deliberate fragment for closing. Register: formal, third person.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Everything is decided before I write word one. The drafting is now execution, not exploration. This means the draft will be more controlled and better structured from the start.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Your plan should be specific enough that someone else could write the piece from it. If it's vague, it won't help you when you're actually drafting.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Plan Together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, build the full plan for a narrative piece about an unexpected discovery.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: a character finds something buried in their garden that changes everything.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: What goes in the plan? Is it specific enough that the draft will write itself from it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose your text type. Build your full plan. Then draft your complete piece (minimum 3–4 paragraphs). Label at least 5 techniques in the draft.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one: Narrative (a moment of unexpected change) / Persuasive (a cause you believe in) / Explanation (a natural process you understand well).</li><li>✦ Bonus: After drafting, circle every deliberate technique you used. Were there any in your draft that weren't in your plan? Were there any in your plan that you forgot to include?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Independent complete-piece composition: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students are expected to use composing strategies to create texts that engage the reader, demonstrating the full integration of the year's vocabulary, grammar, sentence structure, and text organisation strategies in an independently composed, complete piece of writing.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Planning as part of the writing process: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective writers plan systematically before drafting, making deliberate decisions about text type, structure, vocabulary, grammar, and sentence variety that guide and control the drafting process. This lesson demonstrates that planning-before-drafting is the foundation of quality independent writing at Phase 2 and beyond.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Full publication-ready edit of own writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students apply a comprehensive, sequential editing process (structural, paragraph-level, sentence-level, word-level, final proofread) to the piece they drafted on Wednesday, producing a publication-ready version.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Five-Pass Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Professional writers edit in passes — top down, not randomly. Watch me apply all five passes to a paragraph.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Five passes: 1. STRUCTURAL — is the whole piece in the right shape? 2. PARAGRAPH — do topic sentences and closers work? 3. SENTENCE — variety, sentence types, clause work. 4. WORD — precise verbs, figurative language, connotation, register. 5. PROOFREAD — punctuation, spelling, grammar errors.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pass 1: structure check — nothing to fix. Pass 2: first topic sentence is weak — fix it. Pass 3: two consecutive simple sentences — combine one. Pass 4: 'went' appears twice — replace both. Pass 5: missing comma after fronted adverbial. Five focused passes catch what one frantic reading misses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Do NOT proofread first. Structural problems waste your proofreading effort on sentences you might delete.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Pass by Pass</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, take a sample paragraph through all five passes in order, making one fix per pass.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The forest was big and old. Many animals lived there. People were cutting it down and this was bad for the animals. Something should be done about this. It needs to stop.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pass 1: Does this have the right structure for its type? Pass 2: Topic sentence? Pass 3: Sentence variety? Pass 4: Precise verbs and word choice? Pass 5: Punctuation errors?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply all five passes — in order — to the piece you drafted on Wednesday. Mark every change and note which pass caught it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pass 1: Structure check</li><li>Pass 2: Paragraph-level check</li><li>Pass 3: Sentence-level check</li><li>Pass 4: Word-level check</li><li>Pass 5: Final proofread</li><li>✦ Bonus: Count how many changes each pass found. Which pass was most productive for your piece? What does that tell you about where you most need to improve as a writer?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Five-pass sequential editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students are expected to reread and revise their writing to check that it makes sense and is accurate, including checking vocabulary, punctuation, and structure. The five-pass sequential edit — structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread — is the publication-standard editing process that consolidates every editing skill developed across the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Sequential editing as professional practice: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Effective editing is sequential — structural problems are addressed before paragraph-level issues, which are addressed before sentence-level issues, which are addressed before word choice, before proofreading. This is the capstone editing lesson of the year.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Year celebration — vocabulary Hall of Fame":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students reflect on and celebrate the most powerful vocabulary tools they have learned across the year, selecting their personal Hall of Fame and articulating why each device is effective.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Made the Biggest Difference?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We've built a huge vocabulary toolkit this year. Today, I'll share my Hall of Fame — the vocabulary techniques that make the biggest difference to writing quality — and explain exactly why.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: My Hall of Fame nominations: 1. Precise verbs — because one strong verb does what ten weak words can't. 2. Pathetic fallacy — because it makes the whole world part of the story. 3. Connotation — because the difference between 'determined' and 'stubborn' is the difference between a hero and a villain.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each, I give a reason that goes beyond 'it sounds better'. The reflection IS the learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Your Hall of Fame should be personal — the techniques that changed YOUR writing, not just the ones that sound impressive to mention.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Class Hall of Fame</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, nominate vocabulary techniques for the class Hall of Fame and make the case for each one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nominations open: which vocabulary technique made the biggest difference to your writing this year? Make the case in one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why THIS technique specifically? What does it let a writer do that they couldn't do without it?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your personal vocabulary Hall of Fame — three techniques, with one paragraph of justification for each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choice 1 and why it matters.</li><li>Choice 2 and why it matters.</li><li>Choice 3 and why it matters.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph that deliberately uses all three of your Hall of Fame techniques. Annotate it so a reader could see exactly where each one appears and what it does.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge)</em> — Metacognitive vocabulary reflection: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Knowledge): Synthesises the year's vocabulary strand through metacognitive reflection — students evaluate which vocabulary tools have been most effective for their own writing development and articulate the reason, demonstrating the highest-level understanding: not just knowing a technique, but understanding why it works.</li><li><em>Writing — Vocabulary (Practice)</em> — Personal vocabulary Hall of Fame: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary (Practice): Students demonstrate ownership of vocabulary skills by selecting, justifying, and applying their most personally significant vocabulary techniques. This culminating activity confirms that vocabulary knowledge has become internalised and transferable — the standard expected at the transition to Year 7.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Year celebration — grammar mastery challenge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge &amp; Practice): students complete a mixed grammar mastery challenge drawing on the full year's grammar strand, demonstrating secure knowledge of all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — The Grammar Full Circuit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we go back to the beginning and run the full grammar circuit — every major concept from all four terms. Watch me work through a mixed challenge, naming each feature as I go.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: Challenge: rewrite this passage to include a complex sentence, past perfect flashback, reported speech, passive voice, modal verb, and concessive clause.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Original: The team won. The captain was happy. She said she was proud. → Rewrite: Although the season had been difficult — they had nearly given up after a losing streak early on — the team finally won the championship. The captain was reported to have said that she was overwhelmingly proud. It was a result that should have come sooner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Work feature by feature through the challenge list, not all at once. Tick each one off before moving to the next.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Mixed Challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete this grammar challenge together — one feature at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base sentence: The school held a special event. It was a surprise. Everyone enjoyed it. Challenge: add a relative clause, a past perfect, a modal verb, and a concessive clause.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which feature do we add first? Does the sentence remain grammatically correct after each addition?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Your Turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete the grammar mastery challenge. Rewrite the passage using ALL the grammar features listed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base: Scientists found a new species. They had worked for years. The discovery was important. Required features: passive voice, past perfect, modal verb, relative clause, concessive clause, reported speech.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a fully original paragraph — topic of your choice — that contains every grammar feature from the list above, correctly used. Annotate each one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge)</em> — Year grammar mastery challenge: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Knowledge): The year's grammar mastery challenge synthesises all major Phase 2 sentence structure concepts — sentence types, conjunctions, tense system, voice, modal verbs, direct and reported speech, conditional sentences, concessive clauses, and clause types — in a single integrated task that confirms secure grammatical understanding heading into Year 7.</li><li><em>Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice)</em> — Grammar integration at Year 7 readiness level: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Sentence Structure (Practice): Students demonstrate that they can write grammatically correct sentences using the full range of Phase 2 structures simultaneously — the standard expected for Year 7 transition.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Year celebration — flexible writing showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Composing (Practice): students write a short, polished piece in their chosen text type on a topic of their choice, showcasing the full range of the year's techniques as a celebration of writing growth.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — Writing with Everything You Know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is a free write — but 'free' doesn't mean unplanned. It means you choose the topic, the text type, and the techniques. You decide what this piece is and what it does. Watch me make those decisions confidently and fast.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: My choice: narrative. Topic: the last day of summer. Techniques: pathetic fallacy for the opening, precise verbs throughout, one deliberate fragment, and a colon for the emotional revelation in the final paragraph. Register: reflective, first person. Length: 4 paragraphs.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I commit to a plan in 2 minutes, then draft with confidence. The decisions are fast because I trust my toolkit. That's what a year of writing practice is supposed to produce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Don't spend more than 3 minutes planning. Make confident choices and write. The editing pass comes afterwards.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Share Plans</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each student shares their plan (text type, topic, 3 technique choices) with a partner before writing. Partners give a 30-second response: does the plan sound like it will produce a strong piece?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Share: text type, topic, three technique choices, and your intended opening hook.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Does the plan hang together? Does the hook suit the text type and topic?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Write Your Showcase Piece</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan (3 minutes), draft (15 minutes), quick self-edit (5 minutes). Aim for 3–5 paragraphs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your text type and topic freely — any text type, any subject that genuinely interests you.</li><li>Aim to use at least 5 techniques from across the year's learning.</li><li>After drafting, do a quick 5-minute self-edit — one pass for the most important fix.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Annotate your showcase piece — circle and label every technique you used deliberately. This is your record of the year's learning in action.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Composing (Practice)</em> — Independent flexible writing showcase: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Composing (Practice): Students independently compose a complete, polished piece in their chosen text type, demonstrating the full range of Phase 2 composing strategies — including planning, technique selection, deliberate vocabulary and grammar choices, and self-editing — without teacher scaffolding. This is the summative composing task for the year.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Planning to publication independently: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students independently manage the full writing process — planning, drafting, and self-editing — with confident, fast decision-making that reflects internalised Phase 2 writing knowledge. This independent control of the writing process is the central goal of the year's programme.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Portfolio piece — the whole-year best edit":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">NZ Curriculum Link</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing — Writing Process (Practice): students select their best piece of writing from across the year, apply the five-pass edit to bring it to portfolio quality, and write a brief reflection on their growth as a writer.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I Do — What Makes a Portfolio Piece?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A portfolio piece is the best version of your best work. Today I'll show you how to choose it and how to bring it to the highest standard it can reach.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Example: What to look for when choosing: the piece that has the most interesting ideas. The piece where you took the most risks. The piece that is closest to being great — not already perfect, but closest to having genuine power.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The best portfolio piece is often not the longest or the most technically polished — it's the one with the strongest ideas and the most personal voice. Apply the five-pass edit to bring the execution up to the level of the ideas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tip: Edit the ideas first (structural pass), then everything else. A beautifully proofread piece with weak ideas is still a weak piece.</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>We Do — Select and Plan the Edit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a class, share: which piece are you choosing for your portfolio and why? What does the structural pass show needs fixing first?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Share: title of your chosen piece, reason for choosing it, and one structural improvement you know it needs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion prompt: Is the reason for choosing it about ideas and voice, not just length or neatness? What is the most important fix?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>You Do — Final Five-Pass Edit and Reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Apply the five-pass edit to your chosen portfolio piece. Then write a 100-150 word reflection.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Five-pass edit: structural, paragraph, sentence, word, proofread.</li><li>Reflection: What was your writing like at the beginning of the year? What can you do now that you couldn't do then? What technique made the biggest difference? What do you want to get better at in Year 7?</li><li>✦ This is your final writing task for the year. Make it the best version of your best work.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Objectives (AOs)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Practice)</em> — Portfolio-quality editing: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Practice): Students select, revise, and edit a piece to portfolio standard, applying the year's full editing sequence and reflecting metacognitively on their growth. This is the capstone task of the Year 5–6 writing programme.</li><li><em>Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge)</em> — Metacognitive reflection on writing growth: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Writing Process (Knowledge): Students reflect on their development as writers, identifying which strategies have most improved their writing and setting goals for future development. This final lesson closes the year's writing programme by consolidating not just skills but self-awareness — the foundation for continued growth as a writer into Year 7 and beyond.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}},"spelling":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>accident</code>, <code>actually</code>, <code>adventure</code>, <code>afraid</code>, <code>against</code>, <code>August</code>, <code>beautiful</code>, <code>bicycle</code>, <code>bought</code>, <code>breath</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>breathe</code>, <code>business</code>, <code>chocolate</code>, <code>colour</code>, <code>complete</code>, <code>consider</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>country</code>, <code>cousin</code>, <code>describe</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>difficult</code>, <code>disappeared</code>, <code>divide</code>, <code>easy</code>, <code>equal</code>, <code>everybody</code>, <code>everywhere</code>, <code>excited</code>, <code>exercise</code>, <code>experience</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>experiment</code>, <code>extreme</code>, <code>February</code>, <code>finally</code>, <code>follow</code>, <code>forty</code>, <code>frightened</code>, <code>guard</code>, <code>happened</code>, <code>height</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>history</code>, <code>holidays</code>, <code>hospital</code>, <code>imagine</code>, <code>increase</code>, <code>instead</code>, <code>interest</code>, <code>interesting</code>, <code>island</code>, <code>January</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>knowledge</code>, <code>laugh</code>, <code>library</code>, <code>lightning</code>, <code>material</code>, <code>measure</code>, <code>medicine</code>, <code>mention</code>, <code>money</code>, <code>natural</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>nearly</code>, <code>ninth</code>, <code>occasionally</code>, <code>opposite</code>, <code>ordinary</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>peculiar</code>, <code>popular</code>, <code>position</code>, <code>possible</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>pressure</code>, <code>probably</code>, <code>problem</code>, <code>programme</code>, <code>promise</code>, <code>purpose</code>, <code>quarter</code>, <code>received</code>, <code>regular</code>, <code>remember</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 10 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>separate</code>, <code>since</code>, <code>special</code>, <code>strength</code>, <code>suppose</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>temperature</code>, <code>they're</code>, <code>though</code>, <code>tomorrow</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-1-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-green\"><th class=\"lt-th\">This week's word list (sample, 5 words)</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><code>twelfth</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>various</code>, <code>voice</code>, <code>weight</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-2-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-3-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-2&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding what a word means helps us remember how to spell it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can guess a sensible definition for each word, then check it against the real meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕵️ Definition Detective</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before checking a dictionary, guess what each spelling word means — then check how close you were.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Notebook · Dictionary or teacher's help</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Pick the first word and think about where you've seen or heard it before.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write your own guessed definition for each word, then check the real meaning.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Share the definition that surprised you most.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to match a spelling word to its meaning or use in context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can identify the correct word on my card when a clue or definition is read aloud.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎱 Word Bingo Blast</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fill a bingo card with this week's words — teacher reads a definition or sentence, you mark the matching word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Bingo card with words written in a grid · Counters or pencil to mark squares</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write your spelling words into a 3x3 or 4x4 grid in any order.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher reads a clue for a word — if it's on your card, mark it. First to a full line calls &quot;Bingo!&quot;</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Play again with a fresh grid if there's time.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce spelling through large-scale kinesthetic movement rather than only pen and paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can air-write my word clearly enough for my partner to guess it correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💫 Air Writing Wiggle</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Write&quot; a spelling word in the air with your finger, elbow, or nose — your partner has to guess it!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your body — no equipment needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Practise writing one letter big and slow in the air.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Take turns air-writing each word while your partner guesses, then check the spelling together.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Try writing your favourite word with your elbow for a giggle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write spelling words correctly and legibly while moving at pace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can chalk my word correctly before tagging the next teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🖍️ Chalk &amp; Talk Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An outdoor (or hallway) relay where teams race to chalk out each spelling word correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Chalk (or paper strips indoors) · Open path or hallway</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams and line up at the start line.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — One at a time, race up and chalk the next word correctly before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Walk along and check every team's words together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct the correct letter order of a word as quickly as possible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can flip and arrange my letter cards into the correct spelling before my partner does.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔄 Flip &amp; Spell Showdown</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each player writes a spelling word's letters onto flip cards, shuffles them face-down, then races to flip them over in the correct order — whoever spells it correctly first wins the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Blank cards or small paper squares — one per letter · A pen each</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Both players write the letters of word 1 onto their own set of cards, then shuffle and lay them face-down.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, both players flip and rearrange their cards into the correct spelling order. First to spell it correctly wins the point — then reshuffle for the next word.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the round's champion speller.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning that understanding the individual letters inside a word can help us see spelling patterns within other, smaller words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can find smaller real words hidden inside a spelling word, using only its letters.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 💰 Letter Loot Challenge</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Race a 30-second clock to spell as many smaller words as you can using only the letters from one spelling word. Score points for every letter you use — and grab a +2 bonus if you use every single letter!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper &amp; pencil (or call out words aloud) · 30-second timer — built in below!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain the scoring: each word you find scores points equal to how many letters it uses. Use up every letter from the original word across your words to earn a +2 bonus.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Run a 30-second round for each spelling word — hunt for smaller words hiding inside it, then use the score checker below to total your points.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Add up every round's score to crown the class Letter Loot Champion.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recognise the correct letter sequence of our spelling words within a field of distractor letters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spot my whole spelling word, in order, hidden across, down, or diagonally.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔎 Word Search Hunt</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hunt for this week's spelling words hidden in a letter grid — words can run across, down, or diagonally.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Printed or on-screen word search · Pencil or highlighter</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the word list and read each word aloud before you start hunting.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Circle or highlight every word as you find it in the grid.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Tell a partner which word was trickiest to spot and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reconstruct a spelling word when its letters are presented out of order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can unscramble a mixed-up word back into its correct spelling, quickly and confidently.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌀 Speedy Scramble Swap</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners take turns unscrambling each other's mixed-up spelling words as fast as they can.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard or paper · Timer</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Partner A reads out the scrambled letters for word 1 — Partner B unscrambles and spells it aloud.</li><li><strong>12 min</strong> — Take turns through the whole list, swapping roles each word.</li><li><strong>5 min</strong> — Whoever unscrambled the most words correctly picks the next warm-up game!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build muscle memory for tricky spelling patterns through repeated, multisensory writing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each word correctly three times, saying each letter as I go.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌈 Rainbow Write</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write each spelling word three times, using a different colour pencil each time, tracing over the same letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> 3+ coloured pencils or markers · Plain paper</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Choose your 3 colours for today.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Write each word once in each colour, saying each letter as you write it.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Pick your favourite rainbow word and show a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to build up a word sound-by-sound and letter-by-letter, strengthening our sense of letter order.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can build a correct letter pyramid for every word on my list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🔺 Pyramid Words</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build each spelling word one letter at a time, like a growing staircase, until the whole word is spelled out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Teacher shows an example pyramid on the board (e.g. b / be / bec / beca...).</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Build a pyramid for every word on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Trade books with a neighbour and check each other's pyramids.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell words aloud fluently and automatically, one letter at a time, under light time pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can say my share of the letters correctly without hesitating, and say the whole word at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎾 Word Tennis</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Partners &quot;volley&quot; a word back and forth, each saying the next letter — miss a letter and you lose the point!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your voices!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who serves first for word 1.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Rally through the whole list — one letter each, back and forth, then say the whole word together at the end.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Total up the points and celebrate the winner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory, under a small amount of performance pressure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud, letter by letter, without looking at the list.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎤 Spelling Bee Countdown</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teams take turns spelling words aloud — get it right and stay in, get it wrong and sit down. Last team standing wins!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Word list (teacher calls words) · Nothing else needed</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into teams of 3–4 and line up.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Teacher calls a word, first player in line spells it aloud. Correct = go to the back of the line. Wrong = sit down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Last team with players standing wins the round!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to write a spelling word correctly and legibly at speed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can run up, write my word correctly first try, and tag my teammate.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🌍 Around the World Relay</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A running relay — race to the board, write the next word correctly, then tag your teammate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Whiteboard/chalkboard or paper on a wall · Markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Split into even teams and line up single-file.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — On &quot;go&quot;, the first runner races up, writes word 1 correctly, runs back and tags the next player who writes word 2, and so on.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Check every team's words together — first fully-correct list wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use knowledge of spelling patterns to predict letters that are missing from a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can fill every gap in a word with the correct missing letter(s).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🕳️ Mystery Missing Letters</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some letters have vanished from each spelling word — figure out which ones and fill in the gaps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at the first mystery word and predict the missing letters.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Fill in every missing letter for all the words on your list.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Swap with a partner and check each other's answers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell a word correctly when we hear it inside a full sentence, rather than in isolation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can listen for my word inside a sentence and write it with the correct spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: ✍️ Partner Dictation Challenge</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One partner reads a word inside a sentence, the other writes it down — then swap and check together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper · Pencil</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Decide who dictates first.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Dictator says a sentence containing the spelling word; writer writes just the word. Swap roles halfway through the list.</li><li><strong>4 min</strong> — Compare your writing to the real spelling and fix any mistakes together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to break spelling words into syllables to help us spell longer or trickier words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can clap and count the syllables in each word and agree on the total with my partner.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 👏 Syllable Clap Count</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clap out the syllables in each spelling word, then work out how many &quot;chunks&quot; it breaks into.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Just your hands!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Warm up by clapping your own name.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns saying and clapping each spelling word, then agree on the syllable count and write it down.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Find the word on your list with the most syllables — can you beat it next week?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to notice patterns across our spelling words (e.g. syllables, tricky letter strings, silent letters) and use them to group words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can sort every word into a group using a spelling pattern I chose myself.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🗂️ Word Sort Categories</strong> <em>(Pairs · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sort this week's words into your own categories — by syllables, tricky letter patterns, or silent letters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Small cards or sticky notes with words written on them · Table or floor space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Write each word onto its own card.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Choose a sorting rule together (e.g. number of syllables) and sort every word into groups.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Explain your sorting rule to another pair.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to reinforce the correct letter sequence of a word through a creative, visual activity.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can draw and colour each word so that every letter is in the correct order.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎨 Bubble Letter Word Art</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw each spelling word in big bubble or graffiti letters and decorate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Plain paper · Pencil, then colouring pencils/markers</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Look at an example bubble-letter word on the board.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Draw and colour bubble letters for as many words as you can.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Show off your favourite word-art creation to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&lt;a id=&quot;term-4-week-10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sample student has already mastered the entire Year 5 word bank (completed Week 10, Term 1) — no new sample word list to show. The day structure below still applies for any student still progressing through their own list.</div></div>"},"Monday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall spelling words correctly on demand, in a low-pressure game format.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell the word correctly before I take my turn to move.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🐍 Spelling Snakes &amp; Ladders</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Play snakes and ladders — but you can only move if you spell a word correctly first!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Snakes &amp; ladders board (drawn or printed) · Dice · Counters</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Set up the board and counters in groups of 3–4.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Roll the dice — before moving, spell the next word on your list aloud. Get it right to move, wrong to stay put.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — First to reach the end (or whoever's furthest when time is up) wins.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to use our spelling words correctly and meaningfully within a sentence of our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can add a sentence to our class story that uses my spelling word correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📖 Silly Sentence Story Chain</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build one giant silly class story together, where each sentence must use the next spelling word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Paper or shared doc to record the story</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Sit in a circle with your team and decide on a story character.</li><li><strong>14 min</strong> — Take turns adding one sentence to the story, each one using a spelling word correctly.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Read your finished silly story aloud to another team.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to recall and say a spelling word correctly from memory in a fun, active game.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can spell my word aloud correctly when I catch the beanbag.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🎯 Beanbag Toss Spelling</strong> <em>(Team · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Toss a beanbag or soft ball — whoever catches it has to spell the next word out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Beanbag or soft ball · Open space</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>2 min</strong> — Stand in a circle with your team.</li><li><strong>15 min</strong> — Toss the beanbag — catcher spells the next word aloud. Correct = toss again for the next word; incorrect = get a hint and try once more.</li><li><strong>3 min</strong> — Celebrate every word your team got through together.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to deepen our memory of a spelling word by connecting its spelling, its meaning, and its use in a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write my word correctly three times, draw a picture that shows its meaning, and write a sentence that matches my picture.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 🛑🔤👃 Stop, Spell, Smell</strong> <em>(Individual · ~20 min)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose 5 of this week's words. For each one: write it correctly 3 times, draw a picture showing what it means, then write a sentence using the word that matches the picture.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>You'll need:</em> Worksheet or notebook · Pencil · Colouring pencils/markers</p></div></div>"},"Friday":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Intention</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We are learning to spell this week's words correctly and independently, without support.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Success Criteria</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I can write each dictated word with the correct spelling, unaided.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Activity: 📝 Friday Spelling Test</strong> <em>(Individual · teacher-administered)</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher reads each word aloud (in a sentence if helpful), student writes it, then the teacher marks each word ✓ correct or ✗ incorrect. Any word marked incorrect automatically reappears on next week's list for review; mastered words are replaced with new words from the Year 5 bank.</p></div></div>"}}}}}}},"tereo":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā mihi me ngā kupu hou":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greet, farewell and acknowledge people · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings &amp; farewell; AO 1.6 — Politeness conventions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Listen &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we are going to learn our very first words in te reo Māori. I am going to say some words and phrases, and I want you to just listen and notice the sounds. There is nothing to write down — just watch and listen.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kia%20ora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kia ora</a></strong> — Hello / Thank you<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93n%C4%81%20koe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tēnā koe</a></strong> — Hello (formal, to one person)<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%93hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pēhea</a> koe?</strong> — How are you?<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pai</a> au</strong> — I am good<br><strong>E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=noho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noho</a> rā</strong> — Goodbye (to someone staying)<br><strong>Hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kon%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">konā</a> rā</strong> — Goodbye (casual)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the vowels. In te reo Māori, every vowel makes only ONE sound — always:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>A</strong> = &quot;ah&quot;  ·  <strong>E</strong> = &quot;eh&quot;  ·  <strong>I</strong> = &quot;ee&quot;  ·  <strong>O</strong> = &quot;oh&quot;  ·  <strong>U</strong> = &quot;oo&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Say &quot;Kia ora&quot; — KIA = &quot;kee-ah&quot;, ORA = &quot;oh-rah&quot;. Kia ora. Simple, consistent, beautiful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The vowel sounds never change. Once you know them, you can pronounce any Māori word. That's a superpower.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's practise these greetings together. I will say the Māori — you say it back. Then we will figure out when you would use each one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Kia ora</strong> — When would you say this? (casual hello or thank you)</li><li><strong>Tēnā koe</strong> — This is more formal. When might a student use it? (to a teacher, elder, or visitor)</li><li><strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — Break it apart: Kei te = right now, pēhea = how, koe = you. Put it together!</li><li><strong>Kei te pai au</strong> — kei te = right now, pai = good, au = I/me. Say it: &quot;Right now good I.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which greeting would you use for your best friend? Which for the school principal? Why does te reo have different words for this?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise saying these out loud — alone or with a partner. Then try the written challenges.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Say <strong>Kia ora</strong> to three different people in the room right now.</li><li>Your teacher walks in. Which greeting do you use? Say it aloud: <strong>Tēnā koe</strong>.</li><li>Someone asks <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — how do you reply? Write and say your answer.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Teach the five vowel sounds to a partner. Can they say <strong>Tēnā koe</strong> perfectly?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ko wai koe? Ko wai au.":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 1.4: Personal information (name)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we learned how to greet someone. Today we learn how to tell people who we are. In te reo Māori, there is a special word we use when we identify or name something. Watch how it works.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko</strong> Aroha <strong>tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ingoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ingoa</a></strong>.<br>= My name is Aroha.<br><br><strong>Ko</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wai</a> <strong>tōu ingoa?</strong><br>= What is your name?<br><br><strong>Ko</strong> Wiremu <strong>tōna ingoa</strong>.<br>= His/her name is Wiremu.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The word <strong>Ko</strong> is used whenever we are saying what something <strong>IS</strong> — its identity or name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern is simple:<br><strong>Ko</strong> + [name] + [whose] + <strong>ingoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ingoa&quot; means &quot;name&quot;. &quot;Tōku&quot; = my. &quot;Tōu&quot; = your. &quot;Tōna&quot; = his or her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: te reo Māori does NOT have separate words for &quot;he&quot; and &quot;she&quot;. Tōna covers both.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko is like a pointer — it points at what something IS. Ko = &quot;it is&quot; or &quot;my name is.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build Ko sentences together. I will give you the pieces — you put them in the right order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How would you say &quot;My name is [your name]&quot;? What are the pieces? Ko + [your name] + tōku + ingoa</li><li>How would you ask someone their name? Remember: &quot;wai&quot; means &quot;who/what&quot;. Ko wai tōu ingoa?</li><li>Your friend's name is Hana. How would you tell someone her name? Ko Hana tōna ingoa.</li><li>Now put it all together — greet someone AND introduce yourself: <strong>Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think te reo Māori uses Ko rather than just saying &quot;my name is&quot;? What does it feel like to say your name this way?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the Ko structure to introduce yourself — written or spoken.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write: <strong>Ko [your name] tōku ingoa.</strong> Say it aloud.</li><li>Ask the person next to you their name using <strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong> Then introduce them to someone else.</li><li>Write three Ko sentences: your name, a friend's name, your teacher's name.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add a greeting. Write a two-sentence introduction: hello + your name. Practise saying it smoothly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Kei te haere au — I am going":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; movement; AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; states</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So far we have learned how to greet people and say our names. Now we are going to learn how to talk about what is happening RIGHT NOW. Watch how te reo Māori builds a present-tense sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=haere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">haere</a> <strong>au</strong>.<br>= I am going.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kai</a> <strong>koe</strong>.<br>= You are eating.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moe</a> <strong>ia</strong>.<br>= He/she is sleeping.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for right-now actions is always:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> + [action word] + [person]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Kei te&quot; is like &quot;-ing&quot; in English — it shows the action is happening NOW.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Action words: <strong>haere</strong> = go/going · <strong>kai</strong> = eat/eating · <strong>moe</strong> = sleep/sleeping</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>People: <strong>au</strong> = I/me · <strong>koe</strong> = you · <strong>ia</strong> = he/she</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: In te reo Māori, the action comes FIRST, then the person. &quot;Is-going I&quot; not &quot;I am going.&quot; The structure is always Time/Action → Action word → Person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the Kei te structure to build these sentences together. I will give you the pieces — you put them in order.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;I am eating&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · kai · au → <strong>Kei te kai au.</strong></li><li>How do you say &quot;She is sleeping&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · moe · ia → <strong>Kei te moe ia.</strong></li><li>How do you say &quot;You are going&quot;? Pieces: Kei te · haere · koe → <strong>Kei te haere koe.</strong></li><li>Now say all three out loud one after another — notice how the structure never changes, only the action word and person swap.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The structure Kei te + action + person never changes. How is that different from English, where we change &quot;I go / she goes&quot;? Which feels easier?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your own Kei te sentences using the words you have learned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and say: <strong>Kei te kai au.</strong> Now swap to &quot;you&quot; — Kei te kai koe.</li><li>Use <strong>haere</strong> (going) in three sentences — one for au, one for koe, one for ia.</li><li>Look around the room. What are people doing? Write one Kei te sentence about what a classmate is doing.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Can you invent a sentence using a new action? Try <strong>kōrero</strong> (speaking) or <strong>noho</strong> (sitting).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: He aha tēnā? Kōrero!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.2: Introductions · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Watch &amp; discover</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put everything together into a real conversation. Watch me roleplay a full exchange — from greeting to introducing to asking what someone is doing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Kia ora! Ko wai tōu ingoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Mere tōku ingoa. Ko wai tōu?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko Tama tōku ingoa. Kei te pēhea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te pai au, kia ora. Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aha</a> koe?<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei te haere au ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a>.<br><strong>B:</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Āe</a>! Hei konā rā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Hei konā rā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase: <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong> — What are you doing?<br><strong>Aha</strong> = what. So &quot;Kei te aha koe?&quot; literally means &quot;Right now what you?&quot; → &quot;What are you doing?&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase: <strong>ki te kura</strong> — to school (<strong>ki</strong> = to, <strong>kura</strong> = school)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>Āe</strong> — Yes!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have everything you need for a genuine short conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A real conversation has a rhythm: greet → introduce → ask → answer → farewell. Once you know the pattern, you just swap in the pieces.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Practise together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build the conversation step by step as a class. I will be Person A — you are Person B. We go slowly and swap roles.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I say: <strong>Kia ora!</strong> — You reply with a greeting and ask my name.</li><li>I tell you my name is Rangi. You introduce yourself. Then ask: <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong></li><li>I say: <strong>Kei te pai au.</strong> Now ask me what I am doing: <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong></li><li>I say I am going to school. How do you say goodbye? Use <strong>Hei konā rā</strong> or <strong>E noho rā</strong>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which part of the conversation felt most natural? Which part do you want more practice with? What other things might you want to say that we haven't learned yet?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full conversation using only te reo Māori. Use every structure from this week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>With a partner: greet each other using <strong>Kia ora</strong> or <strong>Tēnā koe</strong>.</li><li>Introduce yourselves using <strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa</strong>. Ask each other's names.</li><li>Ask <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> and answer <strong>Kei te pai au</strong>.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add <strong>Kei te aha koe?</strong> and answer with a Kei te sentence. Try to have a 6-line conversation entirely in te reo!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kei te pēhea koe? — What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you going? </li><li>How are you? ✅</li><li>What is your name? </li><li>Are you good? </li></ul></li><li>Which vowel rule is correct for te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Vowels change depending on surrounding letters </li><li>Each vowel has only one fixed sound, always ✅</li><li>Vowels are usually silent at the end of a word </li><li>Only &quot;a&quot; and &quot;e&quot; have consistent sounds </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko Hana tōna ingoa.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko is her greeting word </li><li>Her name is Hana ✅</li><li>Hana's name is different </li><li>Hana says hello </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;What is your name?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko wai tōku ingoa? </li><li>Ko wai tōna ingoa? </li><li>Ko wai tōu ingoa? ✅</li><li>Ko tōu wai ingoa? </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te moe ia.&quot; The word &quot;ia&quot; means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I / me </li><li>you </li><li>he or she ✅</li><li>going </li></ul></li><li>What is the correct order for a present-tense sentence in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Person → Kei te → action </li><li>Action → Kei te → person </li><li>Kei te → person → action </li><li>Kei te → action → person ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te aha koe?&quot; — What does this question ask?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>How are you? </li><li>Where are you going? </li><li>What are you doing? ✅</li><li>Who are you? </li></ul></li><li>Which phrase means &quot;Goodbye&quot; to someone who is leaving?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kia ora </li><li>E noho rā </li><li>Hei konā rā ✅</li><li>Tēnā koe </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu hou — Kīnaki me ngā kai":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Communicate about number · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings (food/taste)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Number &amp; quantity; AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; taste</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Commas in lists and new food vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week we are building our vocabulary for food and drink — kupu pai mō te kai me te inu. We are also going to learn how to say lists of things in te reo Māori. Listen carefully to how we join words together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81poro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āporo</a></strong> — an apple<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=panana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">panana</a></strong> — a banana<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%8Dmate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tōmate</a></strong> — a tomato<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=par%C4%81oa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parāoa</a></strong> — bread<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=miraka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">miraka</a></strong> — milk<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wai</a></strong> — water<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tī</a></strong> — tea<br><strong>He inu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dhua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōhua</a></strong> — hot drink / coffee</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, when we list things, we simply say them one after another — no word for &quot;and&quot; needed in a basic list:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He āporo, he panana, he tōmate.</strong><br>An apple, a banana, a tomato.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But when we want to say &quot;and also&quot; to add something extra, we use <strong>me</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He āporo me he panana.</strong><br>An apple and a banana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And to say &quot;I want&quot; something, we combine what you already know:<br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te āporo.</strong><br>I want to eat the apple.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me = and (joining two things). In a list of three or more, you can just list them — no joining word needed until the last item.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build food lists together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's practise the new food words and build some lists. I will show you the items — you say them in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look at these three things: an apple, bread, water. How do you say them as a list? <strong>He āporo, he parāoa, he wai.</strong></li><li>Now say: &quot;I want to drink the water.&quot; Use Kei te hiahia + ki te inu + i te wai → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i te wai.</strong></li><li>Add me to join two things: &quot;milk and tea&quot; → <strong>He miraka me he tī.</strong></li><li>Now put it together: &quot;I want bread and milk.&quot; → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te parāoa me te miraka.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which of these food words sound similar to the English word? Why do you think that is? What does this tell us about how languages borrow from each other?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your turn with food words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the new vocabulary to build your own sentences about food and drink.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a list of three foods you like using <strong>He… me he… me he…</strong></li><li>Say what you want to eat right now: <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te…</strong></li><li>Ask a partner what they want to drink: <strong>Kei te hiahia koe ki te inu i te aha?</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Can you say five food words from memory without looking? Write them with their English meanings.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha tēnei? He aha tērā? — This and that":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 2.5: Physical descriptions — He structure with demonstratives</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions &amp; identity; AO 2.5 — Descriptions — He structure</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Demonstratives: tēnei, tēnā, tērā</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know He — &quot;it is a…&quot;. Today we add pointer words so we can say THIS thing or THAT thing. Watch how they work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēnei</a></strong> — this (near me)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93n%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēnā</a></strong> — that (near you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tērā</a></strong> — that (over there, away from both of us)<br><br><strong>He aha tēnei?</strong> — What is this?<br><strong>He āporo tēnei.</strong> — This is an apple.<br><strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> — What is that (near you)?<br><strong>He parāoa tēnā.</strong> — That is bread.<br><strong>He aha tērā?</strong> — What is that over there?<br><strong>He kura tērā.</strong> — That is a school.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The three pointer words work just like &quot;this&quot; and &quot;that&quot; in English — they tell us HOW FAR AWAY the thing is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tēnei</strong> — near ME (the speaker)<br><strong>tēnā</strong> — near YOU (the listener)<br><strong>tērā</strong> — away from BOTH of us</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>They slot into your He sentence at the end, after the noun:<br><strong>He [noun] tēnei/tēnā/tērā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And the question is always: <strong>He aha</strong> + the pointer word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Think of tēnei/tēnā/tērā as three distances: HERE · THERE · WAY OVER THERE. They work the same way every time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Point and describe together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will point at things in the room. You tell me what they are in te reo using tēnei, tēnā, or tērā.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I hold up a pencil near me. You ask: <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> I answer: <strong>He pene tēnā.</strong> (pene = pencil)</li><li>Something is on the far wall. How do you ask? → <strong>He aha tērā?</strong> How might you answer if it is a window (matapihi)? → <strong>He matapihi tērā.</strong></li><li>You hold something. I ask <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> You answer with a food word from Monday. Try it!</li><li>Now add an adjective: &quot;This is a big apple.&quot; → <strong>He āporo nui tēnei.</strong> Remember — adjective comes AFTER the noun.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the difference between tēnā and tērā? If you had to explain it to a younger student, how would you describe it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Point and say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Walk around the room (or look around) and describe what you see using tēnei, tēnā, tērā.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences about things near you using <strong>He [noun] tēnei.</strong></li><li>Write two sentences about things near your partner using <strong>He [noun] tēnā.</strong></li><li>Ask a partner <strong>He aha tēnā?</strong> about something they are holding. They answer in te reo.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add an adjective to each of your sentences. E.g. <strong>He pene hou tēnei.</strong> (This is a new pencil.)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding with adjectives — He [noun] [adjective] tēnei":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Communicate about physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: He + noun + adjective</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Physical descriptions; AO 1.6 — Politeness &amp; classroom language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Building richer descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have He, demonstratives, and adjectives. Today we combine them all into full, rich descriptions. Watch how a simple sentence grows.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He āporo.</strong><br>An apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo tēnei.</strong><br>This is an apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whero</a> tēnei.</strong><br>This is a red apple.<br><br><strong>He āporo whero <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nui</a> tēnei.</strong><br>This is a big red apple.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i tēnei āporo whero nui.</strong><br>I want to eat this big red apple.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice how we can stack adjectives after the noun:<br><strong>He āporo [whero] [nui] tēnei.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Colour words you now know:<br><strong>whero</strong> = red · <strong>kōwhai</strong> = yellow · <strong>kākāriki</strong> = green<br><strong>kikorangi</strong> = blue · <strong>mā</strong> = white · <strong>mangu</strong> = black</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And that final sentence shows something powerful — you can plug your whole He description into a Kei te hiahia sentence as the object. The building blocks connect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Noun first, then adjectives — always. You can have two or three adjectives, just keep stacking them after the noun.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grow the sentence together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Start with a bare noun and keep adding — I will prompt you for each piece.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: <strong>He whare.</strong> Add &quot;big&quot;: <strong>He whare nui.</strong> Add &quot;that over there&quot;: <strong>He whare nui tērā.</strong></li><li>Start: <strong>He panana.</strong> Add &quot;yellow&quot;: <strong>He panana kōwhai.</strong> Add &quot;this&quot;: <strong>He panana kōwhai tēnei.</strong></li><li>Now use Kei te with your full description: &quot;I am eating this yellow banana.&quot; → <strong>Kei te kai au i tēnei panana kōwhai.</strong></li><li>Try a new combo: He + [noun from Monday] + [colour] + [size] + tēnā. Build it piece by piece.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You now have three layers: He + noun + adjectives + demonstrative. How is building a sentence like stacking building blocks? What stays fixed and what changes?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Stack the building blocks</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the most complete description sentences you can using everything you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a two-layer sentence: <strong>He [noun] [adjective] tēnei.</strong></li><li>Write a three-layer sentence: <strong>He [noun] [colour] [size] tēnā.</strong></li><li>Write a full Kei te sentence using your description as the object: <strong>Kei te kai/inu au i tēnei…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe a classmate's belongings in three sentences using tēnā (things near them). Read them aloud.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He aha ōu hiahia? What do you want?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.3: Communicate about likes and dislikes</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Likes, dislikes &amp; wants; AO 1.6 — Politeness conventions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A café conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put all of this week's learning into a real conversation. Imagine you are in a café in Tāmaki Makaurau. Listen to how the conversation builds using everything from Monday to Wednesday.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Tēnā koe! He aha ōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hiahia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hiahia</a>?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> Tēnā koe. Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i tētahi tī <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhana</a>.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> He aha tēnā? He tī māmā, he tī <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaha</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rānei</a>?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> He tī māmā, tēnā koe.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> He āporo, he panana rānei hei kai māu?<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> He āporo tēnā? Āe, tēnā koa.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ka pai! E hia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=utu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">utu</a>? E rima <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāra</a>.<br><strong>Manuhiri:</strong> Tēnā koe. Hei konā rā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong>Kaimahi</strong> = worker / staff<br><strong>Manuhiri</strong> = visitor / customer<br><strong>ōu hiahia</strong> = your wants / what you would like<br><strong>tētahi</strong> = a (certain) one<br><strong>māhana</strong> = warm<br><strong>māmā</strong> = light / mild<br><strong>kaha</strong> = strong<br><strong>rānei</strong> = or<br><strong>hei kai māu</strong> = for you to eat<br><strong>tēnā koa</strong> = yes please (polite)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>rānei</strong> is used for &quot;or&quot; in questions — just like FANBOYS in English but for choices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: tēnā koa = yes please. This is the polite, warm way to accept something offered to you. Use it generously!</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the café conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's build the conversation together, one line at a time. I will be the kaimahi — you are the manuhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I greet you: Tēnā koe! You greet me back. Then I ask: <strong>He aha ōu hiahia?</strong> What do you say you want to drink?</li><li>I offer you a choice: <strong>He miraka, he wai rānei?</strong> — milk or water? You answer using <strong>He… tēnā koa.</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ōu hiahia hei kai?</strong> — What would you like to eat? You tell me using <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i…</strong></li><li>I tell you the price: <strong>E whā tāra.</strong> You say thank you and goodbye. Which goodbye word do YOU use — and which do I use? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tēnā koa vs Āe — when might you use each one? What does the &quot;koa&quot; add? Think about how politeness works differently in different situations.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your café roleplay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, perform a full café conversation using this week's vocabulary and structures. Swap roles.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Partner A is the kaimahi — greet, ask what they want, offer a choice using rānei.</li><li>Partner B is the manuhiri — respond politely, say what you want, use tēnā koa.</li><li>Include at least one He description with an adjective: <strong>He [food] [colour/size].</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Extend the conversation to 10 lines. The kaimahi can describe the food: <strong>He āporo whero nui tēnei — he pai!</strong></li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He āporo me he panana.&quot; What does &quot;me&quot; mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>but </li><li>or </li><li>and ✅</li><li>with </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;I want to drink the water&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te kai au i te wai </li><li>Kei te hiahia au ki te inu i te wai ✅</li><li>Kei te inu au ki te wai </li><li>He wai hiahia au </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha tērā?&quot; — Which object is being asked about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Something next to the speaker </li><li>Something next to the listener </li><li>Something far from both speaker and listener ✅</li><li>Something the speaker is holding </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;This is a big house&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He nui whare tēnei </li><li>He whare tēnei nui </li><li>He whare nui tēnei ✅</li><li>Tēnei he whare nui </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He āporo whero nui tēnei.&quot; What is the correct order here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Demonstrative → adjectives → noun </li><li>Noun → demonstrative → adjectives </li><li>Noun → adjectives → demonstrative ✅</li><li>Adjectives → noun → demonstrative </li></ul></li><li>What is the te reo Māori word for &quot;yellow&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>whero </li><li>kikorangi </li><li>kōwhai ✅</li><li>kākāriki </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A customer wants to say &quot;yes please&quot; politely when offered something. What do they say?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Āe </li><li>Tēnā koa ✅</li><li>Kia ora </li><li>Kāo </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He miraka, he wai rānei?&quot; — What does rānei mean in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>and </li><li>but </li><li>or ✅</li><li>also </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupumahi hou — More action words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location and movement · AO 2.5: Physical characteristics and feelings — expanding verb vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; movement; AO 2.5 — Actions &amp; feelings</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Expanding your verb toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know haere (go), kai (eat), inu (drink), moe (sleep), kōrero (speak), and ako (learn). Today we are going to add a whole new set of action words — kupumahi — so you can describe much more of what happens around you. Listen carefully and notice the sounds.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>haere</strong> — go / travel<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoki</a></strong> — return / come back<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tū</a></strong> — stand<br><strong>noho</strong> — sit / stay / live<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tākaro</a></strong> — play<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahi</a></strong> — work / do<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pānui</a></strong> — read<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuhituhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuhituhi</a></strong> — write<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakarongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakarongo</a></strong> — listen<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=titiro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">titiro</a></strong> — look / watch<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=omaoma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">omaoma</a></strong> — run<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaukau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaukau</a></strong> — swim</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have enough verbs to describe almost everything that happens in a school day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All of these slot straight into your <strong>Kei te</strong> structure:<br><strong>Kei te</strong> + [verb] + [person]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong><br>The children are playing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te pānui ia.</strong><br>She is reading.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te mahi au.</strong><br>I am working.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>ngā tamariki</strong> = the children (ngā = the, plural)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every new verb you learn multiplies your sentences. One new verb = dozens of new sentences using structures you already know.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build action sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will call out a verb and a person — you build the Kei te sentence. Fast and fluent.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She is swimming&quot; → <strong>Kei te kaukau ia.</strong> Now swap to &quot;I am swimming&quot; → <strong>Kei te kaukau au.</strong></li><li>&quot;The children are playing&quot; — new word: ngā tamariki = the children → <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong></li><li>&quot;I am writing&quot; → <strong>Kei te tuhituhi au.</strong> &quot;You are listening&quot; → <strong>Kei te whakarongo koe.</strong></li><li>Now try two in a row: &quot;She is reading and I am writing.&quot; Say both sentences back to back — notice the structure never changes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now learned 12 action words. Which three do you think you will use most often in te reo? Why? What actions are missing that you would want to be able to say?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Action sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the new verbs to describe what is happening around you right now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Kei te sentences about what three different people in the room are doing right now.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ngā tamariki</strong> (the children) as the person doing the action.</li><li>Say five new verbs from memory — can you do it without looking at the list?</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence sequence describing what YOU do each morning from waking up to arriving at school. Use Kei te for each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ināianei — Kei te haere ngā tamariki ki hea?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Communicate about location · AO 1.3: Number and reference · Grammar Level 1–2: ki hea (where to), i hea (where from)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location — ki hea &amp; i hea; AO 1.3 — Reference &amp; direction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Asking and answering where</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know ki already — it means &quot;to&quot;. Today we build on that to ask and answer &quot;where?&quot; questions. Watch how two small words unlock a whole new layer of conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — Where (to)?<br><strong>I hea?</strong> — Where (from)? / Where (was it)?<br><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — Where (is it right now)?<br><br><strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong><br>Where are you going?<br><br><strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong><br>I am going to school.<br><br><strong>Kei hea a Hana?</strong><br>Where is Hana?<br><br><strong>Kei te kura ia.</strong><br>She is at school.<br><br><strong>I hea koe inanahi?</strong><br>Where were you yesterday?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three location question words, three different times:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — WHERE IS IT right now? (present location)<br><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — WHERE TO? (direction of movement)<br><strong>I hea?</strong> — WHERE WAS IT? (past location)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Place words to use in answers:<br><strong>te kura</strong> = school · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81inga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kāinga</a></strong> = home<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a></strong> = shop · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māra</a></strong> = garden<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awa</a></strong> = river · <strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=roto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">roto</a></strong> = lake<br><strong>te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dpua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōpua</a></strong> = swimming pool</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=inanahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">inanahi</a></strong> = yesterday</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei hea = where IS it (now). Ki hea = where is it GOING. I hea = where WAS it. The first word tells you the time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Where questions and answers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I ask the question — you build the answer. Then we swap and you ask me.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> You answer that you are going home → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kāinga.</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>Kei hea a Tama?</strong> (Where is Tama?) You say he is at the shop → <strong>Kei te toa ia.</strong></li><li>Now you ask ME where I am going. Use <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> I answer with a place — you decide if it makes sense.</li><li>Past tense: <strong>I hea koe inanahi?</strong> — Where were you yesterday? Try to answer using a place word: <strong>I te kura au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What is the difference between Kei hea and Ki hea? Can you make up a rule that helps you remember which one to use and when?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Location challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use ki hea, kei hea, and i hea in your own sentences and questions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and ask a partner: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> They answer with a real or imagined destination.</li><li>Write two sentences about where two different people are right now: <strong>Kei [place] ia.</strong></li><li>Write one past-tense location sentence: <strong>I [place] au inanahi.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-line dialogue — one person asks where the other is going, why, and when they will come back (hoki mai). Use <strong>Āpōpō</strong> (tomorrow) in the answer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Compound sentences — joining ideas with ā, engari, nō reira":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Communicate about likes, dislikes and reasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Conjunctions and compound sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Reasons &amp; conjunctions; AO 1.5 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Joining sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So far every sentence has stood alone. Today we learn how to join two ideas together — making compound sentences just like you do in English with &quot;and&quot;, &quot;but&quot;, and &quot;so&quot;. Watch how these three words work in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>ā</strong> — and then / and (joining actions in sequence)<br><strong>engari</strong> — but / however<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — so / therefore<br><br><strong>Kei te haere au ki te toa ā Kei te hoko au i te āporo.</strong><br>I am going to the shop and (then) I am buying an apple.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te tākaro engari kei te mahi au.</strong><br>I want to play but I am working.<br><br><strong>Kei te hiakai au nō reira kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>I am hungry so I am going to the shop.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English you might say &quot;and&quot;, &quot;but&quot;, &quot;so&quot;. In te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>ā</strong> — use this to chain two actions that happen in sequence or together<br><strong>engari</strong> — use this for contrast — something unexpected follows<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — use this for result or reason — &quot;therefore / so&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New verb: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoko</a></strong> = buy</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The trick: each joining word tells you the RELATIONSHIP between the two ideas.<br>Adding? → ā<br>Contrasting? → engari<br>Showing result? → nō reira</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Before you choose the joining word, ask yourself: am I ADDING, CONTRASTING, or showing a RESULT? That decides which word fits.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Join the sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two separate sentences — you decide which joining word fits and combine them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I am hungry.&quot; + &quot;I am going to the shop.&quot; — What is the relationship? → <strong>Kei te hiakai au nō reira kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong></li><li>&quot;She wants to swim.&quot; + &quot;She is working.&quot; — Contrast! → <strong>Kei te hiahia ia ki te kaukau engari kei te mahi ia.</strong></li><li>&quot;I am going to school.&quot; + &quot;I am learning te reo.&quot; — Sequence → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura ā kei te ako au i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Now make your own: give me a sentence, I will give you a second idea, you join them with the right word.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which of the three joining words — ā, engari, nō reira — is most like the English word &quot;but&quot;? Which is most like &quot;therefore&quot;? Can you think of a sentence where you could use any of the three but the meaning would change?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Join your ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write compound sentences in te reo using ā, engari, and nō reira.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ā</strong> — two actions in sequence.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>engari</strong> — something unexpected.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>nō reira</strong> — a cause and result.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph about your morning — use at least two joining words. Read it aloud to a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te kura":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Simple classroom language · AO 2.3: Likes and dislikes with reasons · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — Classroom language; AO 2.3 — Likes, dislikes &amp; reasons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about school</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we bring together all three days of this week — new verbs, location words, and joining sentences — in a conversation about school life. This is a conversation two students might have on the way to class.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! Kei te haere koe ki hea?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te haere au ki te kura. Kei te ako au i te pāngarau ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pai ki a koe te pāngarau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he pai ki a au te pāngarau engari he uaua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu akomanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko te akomanga toru tōku. Kei hea tōu akomanga?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko te akomanga whā tōku. Kei te haere tāua ki reira ā ka tīmata te akoranga!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! Kia tere!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81ngarau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pāngarau</a></strong> = maths<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i%CC%84n%C4%81ianei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ināianei</a></strong> = right now / at this moment<br><strong>He pai ki a koe?</strong> = Do you like it? (literally: Is it good to you?)<br><strong>he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uaua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uaua</a></strong> = it is difficult<br><strong>tōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akomanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akomanga</a></strong> = your classroom<br><strong>akomanga toru/whā</strong> = classroom 3/4 (number after noun)<br><strong>tāua</strong> = we two (including you) — from Week 2!<br><strong>ki reira</strong> = there / to there<br><strong>ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmata</a></strong> = it begins / is starting / is starting<br><strong>Kia <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tere</a>!</strong> = Hurry up! / Be quick! / Be quick!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pai ki a koe? = Do you like it? He pai ki a au = I like it. This is one of the most useful patterns in te reo — learn it cold.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the school conversation step by step. I will start each exchange — you complete it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> You say you are going to your classroom (use your actual room number if you know it in Māori).</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pai ki a koe te [subject]?</strong> Choose a school subject. You answer with <strong>Āe/Kāo, he pai/uaua ki a au.</strong></li><li>Add a joining word: &quot;I like reading but it is difficult.&quot; → <strong>He pai ki a au te pānui engari he uaua.</strong></li><li>Use tāua: &quot;Let's go to class together.&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere tāua ki te akomanga.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He pai ki a au = I like it. How does this differ from how you say &quot;I like it&quot; in English? What does &quot;ki a au&quot; literally mean — and what does that tell you about how te reo Māori thinks about liking things?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your school kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full conversation about your school day using everything from this week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>Kei te haere koe ki hea?</strong> Use a real location from your school.</li><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pai ki a koe te [subject]?</strong> Give a reason using <strong>nō reira</strong> or <strong>engari</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>tāua</strong> to suggest doing something together — go to class, eat lunch, play outside.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 6-line dialogue between two students that uses: a location question, a likes/dislikes exchange with a reason, and at least one joining word.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the te reo Māori word for &quot;write&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>pānui </li><li>tuhituhi ✅</li><li>whakarongo </li><li>titiro </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.&quot; What does &quot;ngā tamariki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the teacher </li><li>the children ✅</li><li>the school </li><li>the classroom </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere koe ki hea?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you right now? </li><li>Where were you yesterday? </li><li>Where are you going? ✅</li><li>Where is she? </li></ul></li><li>Which question word asks about where something IS right now (not where it is going)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ki hea </li><li>I hea </li><li>Kei hea ✅</li><li>Nō hea </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You want to say &quot;I am hungry SO I am going to the shop.&quot; Which joining word do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā </li><li>engari </li><li>nō reira ✅</li><li>me </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te hiahia au ki te kaukau engari kei te mahi au.&quot; What does engari signal here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A result — swimming caused the work </li><li>A sequence — swimming then working </li><li>A contrast — wanting to swim but working instead ✅</li><li>An addition — swimming and working </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai ki a koe te pāngarau?&quot; — What is pāngarau?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>reading </li><li>writing </li><li>maths ✅</li><li>science </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Let's go to class&quot; using the inclusive we-two pronoun?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te haere māua ki te akomanga </li><li>Kei te haere tāua ki te akomanga ✅</li><li>Kei te haere mātou ki te akomanga </li><li>Kei te haere kōrua ki te akomanga </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Nōku, nāku — mine! Possessive pronouns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.2: Communicate about possessions · Grammar Level 1–2: Possessive pronouns, ō/ā categories introduced</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.2 — Possessions; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Whose is it? Nō wai? Nā wai?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we learn how to say who things belong to. In te reo Māori there are two ways to say &quot;mine&quot; or &quot;yours&quot; — and which one you use depends on what KIND of thing you are talking about. Listen carefully and you will start to feel the difference.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Nō wai tēnā?</strong> — Whose is that? (things you don't control)<br><strong>Nōku tēnā.</strong> — That is mine.<br><strong>Nōu tēnā.</strong> — That is yours.<br><strong>Nōna tēnā.</strong> — That is his/hers.<br><br><strong>Nā wai tēnā?</strong> — Whose is that? (things you do control)<br><strong>Nāku tēnā.</strong> — That is mine.<br><strong>Nāu tēnā.</strong> — That is yours.<br><strong>Nāna tēnā.</strong> — That is his/hers.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is one of the most interesting things about te reo Māori — things you POSSESS are divided into two groups:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ō-category (nō-)</strong> — things you do NOT control or things that are senior to you:<br>your name, your parents, your feelings, your land, your canoe, your school</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā-category (nā-)</strong> — things you DO control or things junior/subordinate to you:<br>your food, your tools, your book, your pet, your children (in a nurturing sense)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A quick feel for it:<br><strong>Nōku tōku ingoa</strong> — My name belongs to me (ō — you didn't choose it)<br><strong>Nāku tāku pukapuka</strong> — My book is mine (ā — you picked it up)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Don't stress about getting it perfect yet — just start to notice the pattern.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ō = things that own YOU or came to you. Ā = things YOU own or control. Your name, land, canoe → ō. Your food, book, pen → ā.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sort and say</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will name an item. You decide: ō-category or ā-category? Then say who it belongs to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A pencil (pene) — do you control it? Yes → ā-category. &quot;It is mine&quot; → <strong>Nāku tēnā pene.</strong></li><li>Your school (kura) — is it senior to you? Yes → ō-category. &quot;This school is mine/ours&quot; → <strong>Nōku tēnei kura.</strong></li><li>Your lunch (tina) — do you control it? Yes → ā. Someone asks Nā wai tēnā? You answer: <strong>Nāku tēnā.</strong></li><li>Your feelings (kei te pai au) — can you control your feelings? Mostly not → ō. &quot;My feeling is good&quot; — we use tōku: <strong>He pai tōku ngākau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think te reo Māori makes this distinction? What does it tell us about how Māori culture thinks about ownership and relationship? Can you think of something that might be tricky to categorise?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Mine, yours, his/hers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise using nōku/nāku and nōu/nāu in real situations.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Point to three things on your desk. For each one, say whether it is ō or ā category and say <strong>Nāku tēnei</strong> or <strong>Nōku tēnei.</strong></li><li>Someone picks up your book. What do you say? Write and say it: <strong>Nāku tēnā!</strong></li><li>Ask a partner whose pencil case is: <strong>Nā wai tēnā kete pene?</strong> They answer using <strong>Nāku/Nāu/Nāna.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write four sentences — two using nō- words and two using nā- words. Make sure the category fits each item.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Tōku, tāku — my (as part of a sentence)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.2: Communicate about possessions · AO 1.4: Personal information · Grammar Level 1–2: tōku/tāku, tōu/tāu, tōna/tāna</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.2 — Possessives in sentences; AO 1.4 — Personal information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Tōku vs tāku inside a sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we used nōku and nāku to say &quot;it is mine.&quot; Today we learn how to say &quot;my [thing]&quot; INSIDE a sentence — like &quot;my book&quot; or &quot;my name&quot;. These are the words tōku and tāku. Watch how they work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tōku</strong> — my (ō-category)<br><strong>tāku</strong> — my (ā-category)<br><strong>tōu</strong> — your (ō-category)<br><strong>tāu</strong> — your (ā-category)<br><strong>tōna</strong> — his/her (ō-category)<br><strong>tāna</strong> — his/her (ā-category)<br><br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong><br>My name is [name]. (ingoa = ō — you didn't choose your name)<br><br><strong>Kei te pānui au i tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukapuka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukapuka</a>.</strong><br>I am reading my book. (pukapuka = ā — you control it)<br><br><strong>He pai tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākau</a>.</strong><br>His/her heart/feeling is good.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern is the same as nō/nā — just a different form used INSIDE a sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tō-</strong> for ō-category things: tōku (my), tōu (your), tōna (his/her)<br><strong>tā-</strong> for ā-category things: tāku (my), tāu (your), tāna (his/her)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already used tōku in Week 1 without knowing it:<br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now you know WHY it is tōku — ingoa (name) is ō-category.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Useful pairs to learn cold:<br><strong>tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ingoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ingoa</a></strong> — my name · <strong>tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukapuka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukapuka</a></strong> — my food · <strong>tāku kai</strong> — my food<br><strong>tōku kura</strong> — my school · <strong>tāku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pene\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pene</a></strong> — my pen</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tōku before ō-things (name, feelings, land). Tāku before ā-things (food, book, pen). If you already know nō/nā, just swap the n for t.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — My this, your that</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences using tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu. I give you the noun — you choose the right possessive and build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;My name is…&quot; — ingoa is ō → <strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong> Everyone say their own name using this.</li><li>&quot;I am reading my book&quot; — pukapuka is ā → <strong>Kei te pānui au i tāku pukapuka.</strong></li><li>&quot;Your school is big&quot; — kura is ō → <strong>He nui tōu kura.</strong></li><li>&quot;She is eating her food&quot; — kai is ā → <strong>Kei te kai ia i tāna kai.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You now know tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna — six possessive words. How do they compare to English? In English we only have &quot;my&quot;, &quot;your&quot;, &quot;his/her&quot; — three words. Why does te reo Māori need six? What extra information do they carry?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Possessives in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna in full sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Introduce yourself fully: Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] au. Then add one more sentence using tāku.</li><li>Write two sentences about a classmate using tōna/tāna — one ō and one ā item.</li><li>Complete these: <strong>Kei te kai ia i <em><strong> kai.</strong> / <strong>Ko </strong></em> tōna ingoa.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph introducing yourself AND a friend. Use tōku, tāku, tōna, and tāna at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Topic sentences — He kōrero matua":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Communicate about likes and dislikes · AO 1.7: Classroom language · Grammar Level 1–2: He and Ko for topic sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Expressing opinions &amp; topics; AO 1.7 — Structured classroom language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Starting a paragraph in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English, every paragraph starts with a topic sentence that tells the reader what the paragraph is about. Te reo Māori writing does the same thing. Today we learn how to write a strong opening sentence — a kōrero matua — using the structures you already know.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">A strong topic sentence in te reo Māori often uses <strong>He</strong> or <strong>Ko</strong>:<br><br><strong>He nui ngā āhuatanga pai o te kura.</strong><br>There are many good things about school.<br><br><strong>Ko te kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>School is my absolute favourite place.<br><br><strong>He tino pai tāku kura nō te mea he nui ngā hoa ōku.</strong><br>My school is very good because I have many friends.<br><br><strong>Ko te pānui tāku mahi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>Reading is my absolute favourite activity.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A topic sentence in te reo Māori does three things:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>States the TOPIC clearly (what the paragraph is about)</li><li>Makes a CLAIM about it (a point of view, not just a fact)</li><li>Is strong enough to stand alone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhuatanga</a></strong> = the things / aspects / aspects<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81hi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wāhi</a></strong> = place<br><strong>pai rawa atu</strong> = the very best / absolute favourite / absolute favourite / absolute favourite<br><strong>ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoa</a></strong> = friends<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=n%C5%8D+te+mea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nō te mea</a></strong> = because (more formal than nā te mea) (more formal than nā te mea)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>nō te mea</strong> and <strong>nā te mea</strong> both mean &quot;because&quot; — nō te mea is slightly more formal and used in writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A great topic sentence = TOPIC + CLAIM + (optional reason). He or Ko at the start signals &quot;here is what this is about.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build topic sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you build a topic sentence using He or Ko. Then we improve it together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: your favourite food. Start with He: <strong>He [food] tāku kai pai rawa atu.</strong> Add a reason with nō te mea.</li><li>Topic: your school. Start with Ko: <strong>Ko tōku kura…</strong> What claim can you make? He nui? He pai? He tino ātaahua?</li><li>Topic: playing. Start with He: <strong>He pai ki a au te tākaro nō te mea…</strong> You finish it with your own reason.</li><li>Now make it stronger — take your first attempt and add either an adjective, a reason, or a demonstrative to make it more specific.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: What makes a topic sentence strong? Compare these two: &quot;Ko tōku kura he nui&quot; vs &quot;Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu nō te mea he nui ōku hoa.&quot; Which is better and why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Write your own topic sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write topic sentences in te reo for each of these topics.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a He topic sentence about your favourite sport or activity.</li><li>Write a Ko topic sentence about your school.</li><li>Write a topic sentence with a reason using <strong>nō te mea</strong>.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose one of your topic sentences and write two more sentences to follow it — make a mini paragraph in te reo Māori.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Whakaatu i a koe anō — Introducing yourself fully":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.2: Introduce self and others · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.2: Possessions · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.2 — Possessions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full self-introduction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We have now learned Ko, nō, possessives, descriptions, and location. Today we bring them all together into a complete mihi — a self-introduction. This is one of the most important things you can do in te reo Māori. Listen to this full mihi and notice how each piece connects.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings to you all.<br><br><strong>Ko [mountain] tōku maunga.</strong><br>… is my mountain.<br><br><strong>Ko [river] tōku awa.</strong><br>… is my river.<br><br><strong>Ko [iwi] tōku iwi.</strong><br>… is my tribe.<br><br><strong>Ko [school] tōku kura.</strong><br>… is my school.<br><br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</strong><br>My name is …<br><br><strong>Nō [place] ahau.</strong><br>I am from …<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings, greetings, greetings to you all.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the structure of a traditional pepeha — a way of introducing yourself by connecting to the land and people you belong to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key new words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=maunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">maunga</a></strong> = mountain<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awa</a></strong> = river<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iwi</a></strong> = tribe / people<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=katoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">katoa</a></strong> = all / everyone<br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa</strong> = greetings to you all (koutou = you all, 3+)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahau</a></strong> = I / me (more formal form of au) (more formal form of au)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every Ko sentence here uses tōku — maunga, awa, iwi, kura, ingoa are all ō-category. They are senior to us. They connect us to something bigger than ourselves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You don't need to know your iwi or maunga perfectly yet — you can use your town or a nearby landmark for now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koutou katoa — said three times at the end of a mihi. Three times is a tradition of completeness and respect in te ao Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build your mihi together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build a mihi line by line as a class. I will prompt each piece — you supply the content.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with the greeting — which one do we use for the whole class? <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Everyone say it together.</li><li>Now add your school: <strong>Ko ___ tōku kura.</strong> Why is kura ō-category? (It is senior to you — it was here before you.)</li><li>Add your name: <strong>Ko <em><strong> tōku ingoa.</strong> Add where you are from: <strong>Nō </strong></em> ahau.</strong></li><li>Close with the three-part farewell: <strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Say it all the way through.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think Māori introductions start with the land — mountains and rivers — before the person's name? What does this tell us about the relationship between people and place in te ao Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your own mihi</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and practise your own mihi using the full structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your mihi in full — all six Ko/Nō lines plus the opening and closing greetings.</li><li>Practise saying it aloud until it feels smooth. Aim to say it without looking at the page.</li><li>Say your mihi to a partner. They listen and then say theirs back to you.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Add two extra lines to your mihi — one about your favourite place (wāhi pai) and one about what you love doing (mahi pai). Use tōku/tāku correctly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nāku tēnā pene.&quot; What does this sentence tell us about the pencil?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It belongs to someone else </li><li>It is yours (ā-category — you control it) ✅</li><li>It is yours (ō-category — it is senior to you) </li><li>It is lost </li></ul></li><li>Your school (kura) is ō-category. How do you say &quot;That school is mine&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nāku tērā kura </li><li>Nōku tērā kura ✅</li><li>Tāku tērā kura </li><li>Tōku tērā kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko [name] tōku ingoa.&quot; Why is tōku used here instead of tāku?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because ingoa is an ā-category word — you control your name </li><li>Because ingoa is an ō-category word — your name is senior to you and you did not choose it ✅</li><li>Because tōku is always used with Ko sentences </li><li>Because tāku is only used with food </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;She is eating her food&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te kai ia i tōna kai </li><li>Kei te kai ia i tāna kai ✅</li><li>Kei te kai ia i nāna kai </li><li>Kei te kai tāna ia </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pai rawa atu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>quite good </li><li>not good </li><li>the very best / absolute favourite ✅</li><li>good enough </li></ul></li><li>Which sentence is the strongest topic sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He kura tōku kura </li><li>Ko tōku kura he pai </li><li>Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai rawa atu nō te mea he nui ōku hoa ✅</li><li>He nui tōku kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In a pepeha, why are maunga and awa mentioned before the person's name?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because mountains and rivers are more important than people </li><li>Because Māori introductions follow alphabetical order </li><li>Because connecting to land shows who you are and where you belong — land is senior to people ✅</li><li>Because it is easier to say </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Greetings to you all&quot; to a group of more than two people?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tēnā kōrua </li><li>Tēnā koe </li><li>Tēnā koutou katoa ✅</li><li>Tēnā tātou </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā pātai katoa — The full set of question words":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 1.5: Location · Grammar Level 1–2: He aha, Ko wai, Kei hea, E hia, Pēhea</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location questions; AO 1.3 — Number &amp; quantity questions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every question word you need</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been picking up question words gradually — Ko wai, He aha, Ki hea. Today we gather every key question word into one place and make sure you can use them all fluently. This is a big unlock — once you know all the question words, you can ask about anything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko wai?</strong> — Who?<br><strong>He aha?</strong> — What?<br><strong>Kei hea?</strong> — Where (is it now)?<br><strong>Ki hea?</strong> — Where (is it going)?<br><strong>Nō hea?</strong> — Where (is it from)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Āhea</a></strong> — When (future)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=N%C5%8Dn%C4%81hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nōnāhea</a></strong> — When (past)?<br><strong>E hia?</strong> — How many (things)?<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Tokohia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tokohia</a></strong> — How many (people)?<br><strong>Pēhea?</strong> — How? / What is it like?<br><strong>He aha te take?</strong> — Why? (What is the reason?)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each question word slips into a familiar sentence frame:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong> — Who are you? (What is your name?)<br><strong>He aha tēnei?</strong> — What is this?<br><strong>Kei hea tō kura?</strong> — Where is your school?<br><strong>Āhea tāua haere ai?</strong> — When are we going?<br><strong>E hia ngā ākonga?</strong> — How many students are there?<br><strong>Tokohia ngā kaiako?</strong> — How many teachers?<br><strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong> — How are you?<br><strong>He aha te take i haere ai koe?</strong> — Why did you go?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key distinction: <strong>E hia</strong> = how many THINGS. <strong>Tokohia</strong> = how many PEOPLE. Always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E hia for things, Tokohia for people — never mix them. And Pēhea always asks about quality or condition: &quot;How is it? What is it like?&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Question word rapid fire</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you an answer — you give me the right question. Then we flip it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer: &quot;Ko Mere tōku ingoa.&quot; → What was the question? → <strong>Ko wai tōu ingoa?</strong></li><li>Answer: &quot;E rua ngā pukapuka.&quot; (There are two books.) → Question about how many things → <strong>E hia ngā pukapuka?</strong></li><li>Answer: &quot;Tokorima ngā ākonga.&quot; (There are five students.) → <strong>Tokohia ngā ākonga?</strong> — Why tokohia not e hia?</li><li>Answer: &quot;Kei te pai au.&quot; → What question prompted this? → <strong>Kei te pēhea koe?</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E hia vs Tokohia — this distinction treats people differently from things. What does this tell you about how te reo Māori values people? Can you think of other languages that make a similar distinction?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Questions only</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your challenge today: only write questions. No statements allowed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one question using each of: <strong>Ko wai, He aha, Kei hea, E hia, Tokohia.</strong></li><li>Write a question using <strong>Pēhea</strong> that you would genuinely want to ask someone.</li><li>Write a question using <strong>Āhea</strong> about something happening this week.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a 6-question interview in te reo Māori that you could use to find out about a new student. Cover name, origin, likes, and plans.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā āhua — Degrees of adjectives: nui, nui ake, nui rawa atu":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Comparative and superlative adjectives — ake, rawa atu, tino</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Descriptions — degree adjectives; AO 2.3 — Preferences &amp; comparisons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Good, better, best in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know adjectives like nui, pai, iti, roa. Today we learn how to compare things — to say something is BIGGER, or the BIGGEST, or SO big. Te reo Māori has a clean, consistent system for this. Watch how three words do all the work.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>nui</strong> — big<br><strong>nui ake</strong> — bigger (comparative — more than one other)<br><strong>nui rawa atu</strong> — biggest / too big (superlative — most of all)<br><strong>tino nui</strong> — very big (intensifier)<br><br><strong>He nui tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whare</a>.</strong> — This house is big.<br><strong>He nui ake tērā whare.</strong> — That house is bigger.<br><strong>He nui rawa atu tērā whare.</strong> — That house is the biggest / too big.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tino\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tino</a> nui tērā whare.</strong> — That house is very big.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The system works the same way for every adjective:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[adjective] = base<br>[adjective] + <strong>ake</strong> = more / -er (comparative)<br>[adjective] + <strong>rawa atu</strong> = most / -est / too (superlative)<br><strong>tino</strong> + [adjective] = very (intensifier)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's try with pai (good):<br><strong>He pai</strong> — good<br><strong>He pai ake</strong> — better<br><strong>He pai rawa atu</strong> — best / the very best<br><strong>He tino pai</strong> — very good</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And with iti (small):<br><strong>He iti ake</strong> — smaller<br><strong>He iti rawa atu</strong> — smallest<br><strong>He tino iti</strong> — very small</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ake = more. Rawa atu = most/too much. Tino = very. They work with ANY adjective — learn the system, not each word separately.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Compare and contrast</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you two things to compare. You build the comparative sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This apple is big. That apple is bigger. → <strong>He nui tēnei āporo. He nui ake tērā āporo.</strong></li><li>Te reo Māori is good. Maths is better (for me). → <strong>He pai te reo Māori. He pai ake te pāngarau ki a au.</strong></li><li>My bag is small. Your bag is very small. → <strong>He iti tāku kete. He tino iti tāu kete.</strong></li><li>Now use rawa atu: &quot;This is the best food I have ever eaten.&quot; → <strong>He kai pai rawa atu tēnei.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we say &quot;good / better / best&quot; — three different words. Te reo Māori uses &quot;pai / pai ake / pai rawa atu&quot; — the same root every time. Which system do you think is more logical? Which is easier to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Bigger, better, best</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the comparison system with adjectives you already know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences comparing two objects in the room using <strong>[adjective] / [adjective] ake.</strong></li><li>Write a superlative sentence about something you love: <strong>He [adjective] rawa atu tāku…</strong></li><li>Use tino with three different adjectives: <strong>He tino [adj]…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short &quot;top three&quot; list in te reo — e.g. your three favourite foods ranked from good to best, using pai, pai ake, pai rawa atu.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Sentence variety — poto me te roa":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Descriptive language · Grammar Level 1–2: Varying sentence length for effect</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended communication; AO 2.5 — Descriptive variety</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Short sentences punch. Long sentences build a picture.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good writers — in any language — vary the length of their sentences. A short sentence lands hard. A long sentence with detail and joining words draws the reader in. Today we practise doing both deliberately in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">SHORT — punchy, clear, immediate:<br><strong>Kei te ua.</strong> It is raining.<br><strong>Kei te hiakai au.</strong> I am hungry.<br><strong>Ka tū ia.</strong> She stood.<br><br>LONG — descriptive, connected, flowing:<br><strong>Kei te ua tino nui ā kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a> hoki au nō reira kei te hoki au ki tōku kāinga.</strong><br>It is raining very hard and I am cold too so I am going home.<br><br>MIXED — the most effective:<br><strong>Kei te ua. He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a>, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kino\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kino</a> hoki. Nō reira kei te hoki au ki tōku kāinga ā ka kai au i tāku tina.</strong><br>It is raining. It is cold and unpleasant. So I am going home and I will eat my lunch.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Short sentences work best for:<br>— Drama and surprise<br>— Starting a new idea<br>— Describing sudden action</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long sentences work best for:<br>— Building a scene or explaining a reason<br>— Connecting several related ideas<br>— Showing cause and effect</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already have all the tools:<br>Short: <strong>Kei te</strong> + verb + person<br>Long: add <strong>ā / engari / nō reira</strong> to join<br>Longer still: add <strong>tino / ake / rawa atu</strong> for degree<br>Longest: combine everything with a description and a location</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoki</a></strong> = also / too (placed after the word it modifies)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read your te reo aloud. If every sentence sounds the same length, mix it up — drop in a short one for impact, or join two ideas for flow.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Mix the lengths</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a long sentence — you shorten it. I give you a short one — you extend it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shorten this: &quot;Kei te haere au ki te toa ā kei te hoko au i tāku kai.&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te toa.</strong> When would each version be better?</li><li>Extend this: &quot;Kei te ua.&quot; Add a feeling, a reason, and a result. → <strong>Kei te ua tino nui. Kei te makariri au nō reira kei te noho au i roto.</strong></li><li>Write one short dramatic sentence followed by one long explanatory sentence about something happening right now in the classroom.</li><li>Read your two sentences aloud. Does the contrast work? Does the short one feel punchy and the long one feel full?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Listen to these two versions read aloud: &quot;Kei te ua. Kei te makariri.&quot; vs &quot;Kei te ua tino nui ā kei te makariri hoki au.&quot; Which version would you use in a story? Which in a quick text message? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Control your sentence length</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write deliberately — choose your lengths with purpose.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three short sentences (under 5 words each) about what is happening right now.</li><li>Now write one long sentence that combines all three ideas using ā and nō reira.</li><li>Write a &quot;mixed&quot; paragraph: short sentence, long sentence, short sentence. Read it aloud — notice the rhythm.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe your morning using exactly five sentences — make the first and last short and punchy, and the middle three progressively longer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Whakaaro ōrite, whakaaro rerekē — Agree and disagree":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Likes and dislikes with reasons · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.5: Expressing opinions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Opinions &amp; reasons; AO 1.6 — Polite disagreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Agreeing and disagreeing politely</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can now describe, compare, question, and explain. Today we learn one of the most important conversation skills — how to agree, disagree, and give your opinion politely. Listen to how this conversation flows between two students debating which subject is better.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pai ake te pāngarau i te tuhituhi ki a au.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he tika tāu — he pai te pāngarau engari he pai ake ki a au te tuhituhi.<br><strong>A:</strong> Nā te aha?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō te mea ka taea e au te whakaaro nui ake i te wā e tuhituhi ana au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro. Engari ki a au, he uaua rawa atu te tuhituhi.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe pea. He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro!<br><strong>A:</strong> He rerekē — ā he pai tērā!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases for agreeing and disagreeing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tika</a> tāu.</strong> — You are right. / That is correct.<br><strong>Āe, engari…</strong> — Yes, but…<br><strong>Kāo, ki a au…</strong> — No, in my opinion…<br><strong>Ki a au nei…</strong> — In my opinion… / I think…<br><strong>Ka pai tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a>.</strong> — That is a good point.<br><strong>Nā te aha?</strong> — Why? / What for? (asking for a reason)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… / I can…<br><strong>Āe <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pea</a>.</strong> — Maybe. / Perhaps.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rerek%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rerekē</a> ā tātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a>.</strong> — Our opinions are different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>i te wā e… ana</strong> = when I am [doing something] — a new time clause</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tika tāu = &quot;you are right&quot; — a gracious way to acknowledge someone before offering your own view. Use it even when you are about to disagree.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Agree, disagree, explain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will make a claim. You agree or disagree, then give a reason. We keep the conversation going for at least four exchanges.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I say: <strong>He pai ake te tākaro i te ako.</strong> (Playing is better than learning.) Do you agree? Respond with Āe/Kāo + a reason.</li><li>You make a claim using <strong>He [adjective] ake te X i te Y ki a au.</strong> I respond with <strong>Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari…</strong></li><li>Practice: <strong>Nā te aha?</strong> — ask me why I said what I said. I must give a reason using <strong>nō te mea.</strong></li><li>Close the exchange: one person says <strong>He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro</strong> — the other responds with <strong>Āe — ā he pai tērā!</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why is &quot;He tika tāu, engari…&quot; a more powerful response than just &quot;Kāo&quot;? What does acknowledging the other person's point do for the conversation? Can you think of a time when this approach would be really useful?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Te taupatupatu — The debate</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, hold a mini debate in te reo Māori. Use all the new phrases.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a topic to debate — which is better: te kura (school) or te kāinga (home)? Sport or reading? Summer or winter?</li><li>Each person makes their opening claim using <strong>He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Respond to your partner using <strong>He tika tāu, engari…</strong> or <strong>Āe pea, engari ki a au…</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your debate as a short dialogue — at least 6 lines, using he tika tāu, nā te aha, nō te mea, and he rerekē ā tātou whakaaro.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Tokohia ngā kaiako?&quot; vs &quot;E hia ngā pukapuka?&quot; — Why are different question words used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tokohia is formal, e hia is casual </li><li>Tokohia asks about people, e hia asks about things — te reo Māori distinguishes between them ✅</li><li>They mean the same thing and can be swapped freely </li><li>E hia is only used for numbers over ten </li></ul></li><li>How do you ask &quot;When are we going?&quot; (future) in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nōnāhea tāua haere ai? </li><li>Āhea tāua haere ai? ✅</li><li>He aha te wā? </li><li>Ki hea tāua haere ai? </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai rawa atu tēnei kai.&quot; What does rawa atu tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The food is quite good </li><li>The food is better than other food </li><li>The food is the best / extremely good ✅</li><li>The food is too good to eat </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;That house is bigger&quot; using the comparative form?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He nui tino tērā whare </li><li>He nui rawa atu tērā whare </li><li>He nui ake tērā whare ✅</li><li>He tino nui ake tērā whare </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The word &quot;hoki&quot; in &quot;Kei te makariri hoki au&quot; means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>but </li><li>because </li><li>also / too ✅</li><li>however </li></ul></li><li>A writer uses one short sentence followed by a long one. What is the main effect?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It fills more space on the page </li><li>The short sentence loses impact next to the long one </li><li>The short sentence creates impact and the long one adds explanation and flow ✅</li><li>Long sentences should always come first </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He tika tāu, engari…&quot; — What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Completely agreeing with the other person </li><li>Changing the subject </li><li>Acknowledging the other point before offering a different view ✅</li><li>Asking a question </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;In my opinion, school is better than home&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ki a au nei, he pai ake te kura i te kāinga ✅</li><li>He pai ake te kura i te kāinga ki a koe </li><li>Nō te mea he pai te kura </li><li>He tika tāu he pai te kura </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tūāhua — Adverbs: how, when, where":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.4: Time and weather · Grammar Level 1–2: Adverbs of manner, time, and place</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Time expressions; AO 1.5 — Location &amp; manner</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Adding when, where, and how to your sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can already build solid Kei te sentences. Today we learn how to add extra information — when something happens, where it happens, and how it happens. These are adverbs — and in te reo Māori they are beautifully flexible. Watch where they sit in a sentence.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>HOW (manner):</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81ta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āta</a></strong> — slowly / carefully / gently / gently<br><strong>tere</strong> — quickly / fast<br><strong>kaha</strong> — strongly / hard<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārie</a></strong> — calmly / peacefully / peacefully<br><br><strong>WHEN (time):</strong><br><strong>ināianei</strong> — right now<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81p%C5%8Dp%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āpōpō</a></strong> — tomorrow<br><strong>inanahi</strong> — yesterday<br><strong>ā tōnā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a></strong> — soon / in due course / in due course<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=muri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">muri</a> mai</strong> — afterwards / later / later<br><br><strong>WHERE (place):</strong><br><strong>i konei</strong> — here<br><strong>i reira</strong> — there (at that place)<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=runga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">runga</a></strong> — above / on top / on top<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raro</a></strong> — below / underneath / underneath<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waho</a></strong> — outside<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=roto\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">roto</a></strong> — inside</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, adverbs usually come RIGHT AFTER the verb they describe:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te haere tere ia.</strong><br>She is going quickly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te kōrero āta ia.</strong><br>He is speaking carefully/slowly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te mahi kaha ngā ākonga.</strong><br>The students are working hard.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time words often come at the START of a sentence (like in English):</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Āpōpō ka haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>Tomorrow I will go to the shop.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Inanahi i haere ia ki te kura.</strong><br>Yesterday she went to school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Place words usually come AFTER the verb phrase:<br><strong>Kei te noho ia i konei.</strong><br>She is sitting here.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Manner adverbs (how) go right after the verb. Time words can go at the start. Place words go at the end. When in doubt — say it aloud and feel what sounds right.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Add the adverb</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a bare sentence — you add the adverb I specify. Then we check where it landed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te haere ia.&quot; Add &quot;quickly&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere tere ia.</strong> Now add &quot;tomorrow&quot; at the start → <strong>Āpōpō ka haere tere ia.</strong></li><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te kōrero ngā tamariki.&quot; Add &quot;loudly/hard&quot; → <strong>Kei te kōrero kaha ngā tamariki.</strong></li><li>Bare: &quot;Kei te noho au.&quot; Add &quot;here&quot; → <strong>Kei te noho au i konei.</strong> Now add &quot;calmly&quot; → <strong>Kei te noho mārie au i konei.</strong></li><li>Build a full sentence with ALL THREE — time + verb + manner + place: &quot;Yesterday she worked hard outside.&quot; → <strong>Inanahi i mahi kaha ia i waho.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English, adverbs can move around a lot — &quot;She quickly ran&quot; or &quot;She ran quickly&quot; or &quot;Quickly, she ran.&quot; Does te reo Māori feel more fixed or more flexible? Try moving the adverb in one of your sentences and see if it still works.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Adverb challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Add at least one adverb to every sentence you write today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences — one with a manner adverb, one with a time adverb, one with a place adverb.</li><li>Write one sentence using ALL THREE types: time + manner + place.</li><li>Take this bare sentence and add two adverbs: <strong>Kei te ako au.</strong> (I am learning.)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences describing your ideal day — every sentence must include at least one adverb. Read them aloud and listen to how much richer they sound than bare sentences.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu pakiaka — Word families and word building":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · Grammar Level 1–2: kai-, whaka-, -nga, tāngata compound words</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — Vocabulary &amp; word building; AO 2.5 — Extended vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — How te reo Māori builds new words</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One of the most powerful things about te reo Māori is that it builds new words from existing ones using prefixes and suffixes. Once you know a handful of roots, you can decode and create dozens of new words. Watch how this works.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>kai-</strong> prefix = person who does something (agent noun):<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaimahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaimahi</a></strong> — worker (mahi = work) (mahi = work)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaiako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaiako</a></strong> — teacher (ako = learn/teach) (ako = learn/teach)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaip%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaipānui</a></strong> — reader (pānui = read) (pānui = read)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaituhituhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaituhituhi</a></strong> — writer (tuhituhi = write) (tuhituhi = write)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaik%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaikōrero</a></strong> — speaker (kōrero = speak) (kōrero = speak)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kait%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitākaro</a></strong> — player (tākaro = play) (tākaro = play)<br><br><strong>whaka-</strong> prefix = to cause or make something happen:<br><strong>whakarongo</strong> — to listen (cause yourself to hear)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81ori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamāori</a></strong> — to translate into Māori into Māori<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaaro</a></strong> — thought / to think<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakapai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakapai</a></strong> — to improve / make better / make better<br><br><strong>-nga</strong> suffix = the act of / the result of:<br><strong>kōrero</strong> → <strong>kōrerorero</strong>/<strong>kōrerotanga</strong> — conversation<br><strong>ako</strong> → <strong>akoranga</strong> — lesson / learning<br><strong>mahi</strong> → <strong>mahinga</strong> — the work / the doing</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is called word building — pakiaka (root) + prefix/suffix = new word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can BUILD words you have never seen before:<br><strong>kai-</strong> + <strong>omaoma</strong> (run) = <strong>kaiomaoma</strong> — a runner<br><strong>kai-</strong> + <strong>kaukau</strong> (swim) = <strong>kaikaukau</strong> — a swimmer<br><strong>whaka-</strong> + <strong>nui</strong> (big) = <strong>whakanui</strong> — to celebrate / make bigger<br><strong>whaka-</strong> + <strong>iti</strong> (small) = <strong>whakaiti</strong> — to be humble / make smaller</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And you can DECODE words you have never learned:<br>If you see <strong>kaitiaki</strong> — you know kai- means &quot;person who does&quot; and tiaki means &quot;to guard/protect&quot; → kaitiaki = guardian/protector</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been a kaitiaki of your own learning all term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: See kai- at the start of a word → it is a person who does that action. See whaka- → something is being caused or made to happen. These two prefixes unlock hundreds of words.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build and decode</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a root word — you build a new word using kai- or whaka-. Then I give you an unknown word — you decode it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Root: <strong>haere</strong> (go). Build: kai- + haere = ? <strong>Kaihaere</strong> could mean &quot;traveller / one who goes.&quot; Does that feel right?</li><li>Root: <strong>pai</strong> (good). Build: whaka- + pai = <strong>whakapai</strong> — to make better / improve. Use it: <strong>Kei te whakapai ia i tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>Decode: <strong>kaitiaki</strong> — kai- = person who does + tiaki = guard/protect. What does kaitiaki mean? Why is this an important word in Aotearoa?</li><li>Decode: <strong>whakaaro</strong> — whaka- = cause/make + aro = face toward / attend to. So whakaaro = thought (turning your mind toward something). Use it: <strong>He pai tāu whakaaro.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English builds words too — &quot;teacher&quot; comes from &quot;teach&quot;, &quot;runner&quot; from &quot;run&quot;. But te reo Māori is much more systematic — kai- ALWAYS means person who does. Why might a systematic system be easier to learn than the English system of -er, -or, -ist, -ian?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Word builder</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise building and using new words from roots you already know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Build three new kai- words from verbs you know. Write each with its meaning.</li><li>Build two whaka- words. Use each in a sentence.</li><li>Decode this word you have never seen: <strong>kaiwhakaako</strong>. What does it mean? (whaka- + ako = to cause learning, kai- = person who does it)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph about your class using at least four kai- words and one whaka- word. E.g. &quot;In our class there is a kaiako, many kaipānui, and some kaituhituhi...&quot;</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Expanding sentences — who, where, when phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Expanding noun phrases and adding circumstantial information</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Location &amp; circumstance; AO 2.4 — Time phrases in sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Packing more into every sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you learned adverbs (how/when/where) and word building. Today we combine them — learning how to pack time, place, and participant information into a single rich sentence. Think of it like adding layers to a painting.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Start bare:<br><strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki.</strong><br>The children are learning.<br><br>Add WHERE:<br><strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>The children are learning in the classroom.<br><br>Add WHEN:<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning in the classroom.<br><br>Add HOW:<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning hard in the classroom.<br><br>Add WHO WITH (using me — with):<br><strong>Ināianei kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga.</strong><br>Right now the children are learning hard with their teacher in the classroom.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The layering order in a te reo Māori sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[TIME] + Kei te + [verb] + [manner] + [person/subject] + [with whom: me] + [where: i/ki]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>me</strong> (when meaning &quot;with&quot; — different from &quot;me&quot; meaning &quot;and&quot;!) <br>→ <strong>Kei te haere au me ōku hoa.</strong> I am going with my friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New possessive: <strong>tō rātou</strong> = their (ō-category, three or more)<br><strong>tā rātou</strong> = their (ā-category, three or more)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can always strip back to the bare sentence — the extra layers are optional. But adding even one layer makes your language significantly richer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Build sentences in layers — start bare, then add one piece at a time. You can always stop when it feels complete. More layers = more precise, but even two layers is a great sentence.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Layer by layer</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build one sentence together, adding a new layer with each step. Everyone contributes one layer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Layer 1 — bare: <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki.</strong> The children are playing.</li><li>Layer 2 — add where: <strong>Kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> ...outside.</li><li>Layer 3 — add how: <strong>Kei te tākaro kaha ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> ...hard outside.</li><li>Layer 4 — add time at the start: <strong>Ināianei kei te tākaro kaha ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> Right now...</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: At which layer did the sentence start to feel really alive? Was there a point where adding more felt like too much? What is the &quot;just right&quot; amount of information for a te reo sentence?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The layering challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take a bare sentence and build it up — then strip it back to find the best version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with <strong>Kei te kai ia.</strong> Add three layers one at a time — write all four versions.</li><li>Start with <strong>Kei te kōrero ngā ākonga.</strong> Add time, manner, and place.</li><li>Now take your fullest sentence and remove layers until you have the most PRECISE version — not longest, best.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Describe what is happening outside your window right now (real or imagined) in three sentences — bare, medium, and fully layered. Read all three aloud and decide which you prefer.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hou mō tēnei wiki — This week in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time and events · AO 2.3: Extended communication with reasons · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Talking about events &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Extended reasons &amp; opinions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about your week</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use everything from this week — adverbs, word-building words, layered sentences — in a real conversation about what has happened and what is coming up. This is the kind of conversation you might have with a friend on a Friday afternoon.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea tōu wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I mahi kaha au i tēnei wiki. I ako au i ngā kupu hou e tino pai ana ki a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ako au i ngā tūāhua — pēnei i te &quot;tere&quot; me te &quot;āta&quot;. Ka taea e au te kōrero ināianei me ērā kupu.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Āpōpō he aha āu mahi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āpōpō ka haere tere au ki te hākinakina me ōku hoa. Ka tākaro kaha mātou i waho.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! He tika tāu — engari he pai hoki te okioki!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words and phrases from this conversation:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> — How was your week?<br><strong>e tino pai ana ki a au</strong> — that I really liked / that are very good for me<br><strong>ngā mea hou</strong> — the new things<br><strong>i ako ai koe</strong> — that you learned (relative clause — more on this in Term 2!)<br><strong>pēnei i te</strong> — like / such as<br><strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero</strong> — I am able to speak<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%81kinakina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hākinakina</a></strong> — sport / physical activity / physical activity<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=okioki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">okioki</a></strong> — rest / to rest / to rest<br><strong>ērā kupu</strong> — those words (ērā = those, plural of tērā)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice how the conversation uses adverbs naturally — tere, kaha, tino pai, ināianei, āpōpō, i waho. This is everything from this week embedded in real speech.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea tōu wiki? — How was your week? Learn this cold. It is a conversation starter you will use every single Friday for the rest of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Friday kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the &quot;how was your week&quot; conversation step by step. I start — you respond, then lead the next exchange.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> You answer with at least two sentences — what you did and how (use an adverb).</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe?</strong> — What new things did you learn? Name something from this week using a kai- word if you can.</li><li>I say something I liked: <strong>I pai ki a au te ako i ngā tūāhua.</strong> You agree or add your own view using <strong>He tika tāu / Āe, engari…</strong></li><li>Close: I ask <strong>Āpōpō he aha āu mahi?</strong> You tell me your plans — use āpōpō, a verb, and a place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He pēhea tōu wiki? is a question you could ask and answer every Friday in te reo. If you did that for the whole year, what do you think would happen to your fluency? Why does regular, short conversation practice matter more than occasional long study sessions?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your own Friday kōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full &quot;end of week&quot; conversation using everything from Weeks 1–6.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pēhea tōu wiki?</strong> Both partners answer in at least three sentences.</li><li>Each person shares one new thing they learned using a kai- or whaka- word.</li><li>Make a plan for tomorrow using <strong>Āpōpō ka…</strong> — include a place and a manner adverb.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your full conversation as a dialogue — at least 8 lines. Use adverbs, word-building words, layered sentences, and the agree/disagree phrases from Week 5.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere tere ia.&quot; Where does the adverb &quot;tere&quot; sit in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Before the verb </li><li>After the verb and before the subject ✅</li><li>At the start of the sentence </li><li>After the subject </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Inanahi i haere ia ki te kura.&quot; What type of adverb is &quot;inanahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Manner — how she went </li><li>Place — where she went </li><li>Time — when she went ✅</li><li>Degree — how much she went </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The prefix &quot;kai-&quot; in te reo Māori makes a word that means:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to cause something to happen </li><li>a place where something happens </li><li>a person who does that action ✅</li><li>the result of an action </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakapai&quot; most likely mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a person who is good </li><li>to make something better / improve ✅</li><li>the goodness of something </li><li>a very good thing </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere au me ōku hoa.&quot; What does &quot;me&quot; mean in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>and (joining two sentences) </li><li>but </li><li>with ✅</li><li>also </li></ul></li><li>In a layered te reo sentence, where does a TIME word usually go?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>After the verb </li><li>After the subject </li><li>At the start of the sentence ✅</li><li>At the very end </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you ask &quot;How was your week?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He aha tōu wiki? </li><li>Kei te pēhea tōu wiki? </li><li>He pēhea tōu wiki? ✅</li><li>Ko wai tōu wiki? </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak </li><li>I used to be able to speak </li><li>I am able to speak ✅</li><li>I am speaking quickly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā rārangi — Lists, colons, and ko ēnei/ko ērā":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number and reference · AO 2.5: Vocabulary and description · Grammar Level 1–2: Ko ēnei/ko ērā, listing structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Reference &amp; listing; AO 2.5 — Descriptive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making lists and introducing them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have been building individual sentences well. Today we learn how to introduce a list of items — giving your listener a heads-up that several things are coming. In English you use a colon for this. Te reo Māori has its own elegant way of doing the same thing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko ēnei</strong> — these are (introducing a list of things near you)<br><strong>Ko ērā</strong> — those are (introducing a list of things away from you)<br><strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea:</strong> — These are my things:<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea: he pene, he pukapuka, he kete.</strong><br>These are my things: a pen, a book, a bag.<br><br><strong>Ko ērā ngā āhuatanga pai o tōku kura:</strong><br>Those are the good things about my school:<br><strong>he nui ngā hoa, he pai ngā kaiako, he ātaahua ngā papa tākaro.</strong><br>there are many friends, the teachers are good, the playgrounds are beautiful.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know tēnei/tēnā/tērā (this/that — singular).<br>Now meet their PLURAL cousins:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ēnei</a></strong> — these (plural of tēnei, near me) (plural of tēnei, near me)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93n%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ēnā</a></strong> — those (plural of tēnā, near you) (plural of tēnā, near you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ērā</a></strong> — those (plural of tērā, away from both) (plural of tērā, away from both)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When used with Ko to introduce a list:<br><strong>Ko ēnei…</strong> = These are… (here is the list)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New descriptive words for the list:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81taahua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ātaahua</a></strong> — beautiful / wonderful / wonderful<br><strong>papa tākaro</strong> — playground<br><strong>ngā kaiako</strong> — the teachers (plural — you built kaiako from kai- in Week 6!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The list items after the colon each start with <strong>he</strong> — just like individual He sentences, strung together with commas.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ēnei/ko ērā = &quot;these are / those are&quot; — use them whenever you want to introduce a list. Think of them as te reo's colon.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build lists together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you introduce it with Ko ēnei or Ko ērā and then list three items.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: things on your desk. → <strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea i runga i tōku tēpu: he pene, he pukapuka, he aho.</strong> (aho = ruler)</li><li>Topic: the good things about te reo Māori. → <strong>Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o te reo Māori: he ātaahua, he ngāwari, he tino pai hoki.</strong></li><li>Now try Ko ērā — things on the far side of the room: <strong>Ko ērā ngā mea i reira: he…, he…, he…</strong></li><li>Challenge: use Ko ēnei to introduce a list of three things you did yesterday — use the past tense I for each item in the list.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ēnei works like a colon in English writing — it signals &quot;here comes the list&quot;. Can you think of other situations in daily life where introducing a list first would be useful? When would Ko ērā be better than Ko ēnei?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — List maker</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Ko ēnei or Ko ērā to introduce lists in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko ēnei sentence introducing three things you like: <strong>Ko ēnei ōku mea pai: he…, he…, he…</strong></li><li>Write a Ko ērā sentence about three things you can see across the room.</li><li>Write a list about your school using ātaahua, pai, and nui as describing words for each item.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o…&quot; paragraph about your town, your class, or your family. At least five items in your list.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā wā — Mixing past and present tense fluently":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: I (past) and Kei te (present) used in the same passage</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Switching between past and present</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te for now and I for the past. Real language constantly moves between tenses — you say what happened, then describe what is happening now, then say what you thought about it. Today we practise switching fluently without losing track of when things are happening.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Watch how tenses shift in this short account:<br><br><strong>Inanahi i haere au ki te hākinakina me ōku hoa.</strong><br>Yesterday I went to sport with my friends.<br><br><strong>I tākaro kaha mātou — he tino pai!</strong><br>We played hard — it was very good!<br><br><strong>Ināianei kei te ngenge au engari kei te hari hoki au.</strong><br>Right now I am tired but I am also happy.<br><br><strong>He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au.</strong><br>Sport is better than staying home in my opinion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The time word at the START of a sentence tells your listener which tense is coming:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Inanahi</strong> (yesterday) → use <strong>I</strong><br><strong>Ināianei</strong> (right now) → use <strong>Kei te</strong><br><strong>Āpōpō</strong> (tomorrow) → use <strong>Ka</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngenge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngenge</a></strong> — tired<br>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hari</a></strong> — happy / joyful / joyful</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of time words as SIGNALS — they prime your listener for the tense before the verb even arrives. This is why putting time words at the START is so powerful in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When there is NO time word, the tense marker (Kei te / I / Ka) does all the work on its own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Time word at the start = instant tense signal. Inanahi → I. Ināianei → Kei te. Āpōpō → Ka. Your listener knows what is coming before the verb.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tense switching</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you a sequence of events across two time periods. You build each sentence with the right tense marker.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yesterday: you went to the shop. Now: you are eating what you bought. → <strong>Inanahi i haere au ki te toa. Ināianei kei te kai au i tāku kai hou.</strong></li><li>Yesterday: she was tired. Now: she is happy. → <strong>Inanahi i ngenge ia. Ināianei kei te hari ia.</strong></li><li>Yesterday: the children played outside. Now: they are sitting inside. → <strong>Inanahi i tākaro ngā tamariki i waho. Ināianei kei te noho rātou i roto.</strong></li><li>Now try WITHOUT time words — just the tense markers. Tell me two things that happened yesterday and one thing happening right now.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we rely on verb endings to show tense — &quot;played&quot; vs &quot;play&quot;. In te reo Māori the verb never changes — only the tense marker changes. Which system do you think is easier for a learner? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Past and present</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short account that moves between past and present — at least four sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two past-tense sentences about something you did inanahi (yesterday).</li><li>Write two present-tense sentences about what you are doing ināianei (right now).</li><li>Combine them into a flowing four-sentence account — use time words to signal the switches.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence account of your day so far — start in the past (this morning), arrive in the present (right now), and end with a Ka sentence about what you will do next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Complex sentences — i te wā, ahakoa, mehemea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication with reasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Subordinate clauses — when, although, if</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex reasoning; AO 2.4 — Time clauses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — When, although, if — subordinate clauses</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last week you learned to join sentences with ā, engari, and nō reira. Today we go one step further — subordinate clauses. These are mini sentences that add a condition, a time, or a concession to your main sentence. Three small words unlock a huge range of complex ideas.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i te wā</a></strong> — when (referring to a time in the past or ongoing) (referring to a time in the past or ongoing)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahakoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahakoa</a></strong> — although / even though / even though<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mehemea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mehemea</a></strong> — if / supposing that / supposing that<br><br><strong>I te wā i haere au ki te kura, i kite au i tōku hoa.</strong><br>When I went to school, I saw my friend.<br><br><strong>Ahakoa kei te ua, kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong><br>Although it is raining, the children are playing outside.<br><br><strong>Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he miraka māku.</strong><br>If you go to the shop, buy some milk for me.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for each:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>i te wā</strong> + [past clause] + , + [main clause]<br>→ When [this happened], [that happened].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>ahakoa</strong> + [clause] + , + [main clause]<br>→ Although [this is true], [this still happens].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>mehemea</strong> + [Ka clause] + , + [instruction or result]<br>→ If [this happens], [then do/say this].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kite\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kite</a></strong> — saw / noticed (past of kite = see/find) / noticed (past of kite = see/find)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoko</a> mai</strong> — buy and bring back (hoko = buy, mai = towards speaker) (hoko = buy, mai = towards speaker)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māku</a></strong> — for me (mā = for + ku = me) (mā = for + ku = me)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These three words turn simple sentences into sophisticated ones — and they all work the same way: subordinate clause first, comma, main clause.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Subordinate clause FIRST, then comma, then main clause. The smaller idea sets up the bigger one. Same rule for i te wā, ahakoa, and mehemea.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build complex sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two simple sentences. You join them with i te wā, ahakoa, or mehemea — whichever fits.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She was tired.&quot; + &quot;She finished her work.&quot; — when? → <strong>I te wā i ngenge ia, i mutu tonu tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>&quot;It is cold.&quot; + &quot;We are swimming.&quot; — although? → <strong>Ahakoa he makariri, kei te kaukau mātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;You go to the shop.&quot; + &quot;Please buy bread.&quot; — if? → <strong>Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he parāoa māku.</strong></li><li>Now write your own: use ahakoa to say something surprising — something that happens DESPITE an obstacle.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ahakoa is particularly powerful — it signals that something unexpected is happening despite an obstacle. Think of a situation in your life right now where ahakoa would be the perfect word. Can you say it in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Subordinate clause challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write three complex sentences — one with each new subordinating word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using <strong>i te wā</strong> about something that happened this week.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ahakoa</strong> about something surprising or unexpected.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mehemea</strong> — give someone a condition and an instruction.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence paragraph that uses all three: i te wā, ahakoa, and mehemea. Tell a mini-story that has a condition, a time clause, and a surprising twist.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō tērā rā — Telling a story about the past":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time and sequencing · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Storytelling &amp; sequencing; AO 2.4 — Past events &amp; time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Telling what happened</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put this whole week together — lists, tense switching, and complex clauses — into a story about something that happened. Storytelling is one of the most important things you can do in any language. Listen to how these sentences flow.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Inanahi he rā tino pai.</strong><br>Yesterday was a very good day.<br><br><strong>I tīmata i te ata — i omaoma au i waho me ōku hoa.</strong><br>It started in the morning — I ran outside with my friends.<br><br><strong>I te wā i ngenge mātou, i hoki mātou ki roto ā i kai mātou.</strong><br>When we were tired, we went back inside and ate.<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā mea i kainga e mātou: he parāoa, he āporo, he miraka.</strong><br>These are the things we ate: bread, an apple, milk.<br><br><strong>Ahakoa i makariri ā i ua hoki, he tino pai tō mātou rā.</strong><br>Although it was cold and raining too, our day was very good.<br><br><strong>Mehemea ka pērā anō āpōpō, ka haere anō mātou!</strong><br>If it is the same again tomorrow, we will go again!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice what this story does:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opens with a scene-setting sentence (He rā tino pai)</li><li>Uses I te wā for the turning point</li><li>Uses Ko ēnei to introduce a food list</li><li>Uses ahakoa for the surprising twist</li><li>Closes with mehemea — looking forward</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning<br><strong>i tīmata</strong> — it started / began<br><strong>i kainga</strong> — was eaten (passive — the eating happened TO the food)<br><strong>e mātou</strong> — by us (the agent after a passive verb)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%93r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pērā</a> anō</strong> — the same again / like that again<br><strong>ka haere anō</strong> — will go again</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every good story has a beginning (scene-set), a middle (what happened, including a surprise or challenge), and an end (result or look forward). Use your subordinate clauses for the middle.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the story together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We will build a shared class story about an imaginary school trip. I provide the skeleton — you fill in the details.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with a scene-setter: <strong>Inanahi i haere mātou ki te…</strong> (somewhere exciting — you choose the place).</li><li>What happened first? Use I + verb + a time word: <strong>I te ata, i… mātou.</strong></li><li>Add a surprise using ahakoa: <strong>Ahakoa…, i… tonu mātou.</strong> Something went wrong but you carried on.</li><li>Close with a Ko ēnei list of the best things about the trip, then a mehemea sentence about doing it again.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Good storytellers control time — they slow down for the important parts and speed up through the routine ones. Which sentence in our story felt most important? How could you make it slower and more detailed using the tools you have?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tāu kōrero — Your story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tell a story about something that actually happened to you recently — entirely in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write an opening scene-setter: one He or I sentence that sets the time and mood.</li><li>Write the middle: at least two sentences using I, plus one i te wā or ahakoa sentence.</li><li>Include a Ko ēnei or Ko ērā list somewhere in your story.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full story — at least six sentences. Use past tense throughout, include a complex clause, a list, and close with a mehemea or Ka sentence looking forward.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ēnei ōku mea: he pene, he pukapuka, he kete.&quot; What is the role of &quot;Ko ēnei&quot; here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It means &quot;these things are bad&quot; </li><li>It introduces a list — like a colon in English ✅</li><li>It is a question asking what these things are </li><li>It means &quot;I have a pen, book and bag&quot; </li></ul></li><li>What is the plural of tēnei?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tēnā </li><li>tērā </li><li>ēnei ✅</li><li>ēnā </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which tense marker do you use after the time word &quot;Inanahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I ✅</li><li>E…ana </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ināianei kei te hari ia.&quot; What does hari mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>hungry </li><li>happy / joyful ✅</li><li>cold </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa kei te ua, kei te tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.&quot; What does ahakoa signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A time — when the rain started </li><li>A cause — the rain made them play </li><li>A contrast — they play DESPITE the rain ✅</li><li>A condition — if it rains they will play </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Mehemea ka haere koe ki te toa, hoko mai he miraka māku.&quot; What does māku mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for you </li><li>for them </li><li>for me ✅</li><li>for us </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I te wā i ngenge mātou, i hoki mātou ki roto.&quot; What is the function of &quot;i te wā&quot; here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It means although we were tired </li><li>It means if we are tired </li><li>It sets a time — WHEN we were tired, something happened ✅</li><li>It means we were very tired </li></ul></li><li>In a te reo story, which sentence type works best to open with a scene-setting mood?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A mehemea (if) sentence </li><li>A He or I sentence that sets the time and feeling ✅</li><li>A Ko ēnei list </li><li>A question using Ko wai </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu ōrite — Words that sound alike":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · Grammar Level 1–2: Distinguishing similar-sounding words, macrons</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Vocabulary precision; AO 1.6 — Clear communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Words that trip people up</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As your vocabulary grows, you will notice some words look or sound very similar but mean completely different things. Today we look at the most common confusable pairs — and the key things that make them different. Pay close attention to the macrons (the long vowel marks) — they change everything.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a></strong> (long ā) — time / period / space / period / space<br><strong>wa</strong> (short a) — not a standard word on its own — macron matters!<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93mu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēmu</a></strong> — game<br><strong>kemu</strong> — would not mean game without the macron<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mō</a></strong> — for / about / concerning / concerning<br><strong>mo</strong> — a type of lizard (unlikely but real!)<br><br><strong>nō</strong> — belonging to (ō-category possession)<br><strong>nō</strong> vs <strong>nā</strong> — ō vs ā category — these two trip everyone up<br><br><strong>ō</strong> — of (ō-category connector)<br><strong>ā</strong> — of (ā-category connector)<br><br><strong>tōku</strong> — my (ō-category)<br><strong>tāku</strong> — my (ā-category)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most important thing about macrons:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A macron (the line over a vowel: ā ē ī ō ū) makes that vowel LONG — held twice as long as a short vowel. It is not just a spelling rule — it changes the MEANING of the word completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Most confusable pairs in te reo come from:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Macron vs no macron</strong>: <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=keke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">keke</a> (cake) vs <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēkē</a> (armpit!)</li><li><strong>Ō vs ā category</strong>: tōku vs tāku, nō vs nā</li><li><strong>Similar-looking prepositions</strong>: ki (to), i (at/past), o (of-ō), a (of-ā)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A useful pair to know:<br><strong>He aha tō ingoa?</strong> — What is your name? (tō = your, short form of tōu)<br><strong>He aha tā mahi?</strong> — What is the work/task? (tā = the, ā-category)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=keke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">keke</a></strong> — cake · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kēkē</a></strong> — armpit (classic example!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When in doubt about ō or ā — ask yourself: is this thing senior to me and connected to identity (ō), or is it something I control and do things with (ā)? That feeling will guide you right more often than not.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spot the difference</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I will give you two similar-looking words or phrases. You tell me the difference and use each correctly in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>tōku</strong> vs <strong>tāku</strong> — which one for &quot;my book&quot;? Which for &quot;my name&quot;? → tāku pukapuka · tōku ingoa</li><li><strong>nō</strong> vs <strong>nā</strong> — &quot;Whose is this house?&quot; (house = senior, ō) → <strong>Nō wai tēnei whare?</strong> &quot;Whose is this food?&quot; (food = ā) → <strong>Nā wai tēnei kai?</strong></li><li><strong>ki</strong> vs <strong>i</strong> — &quot;I am going TO school&quot; vs &quot;I went FROM/AT school&quot; → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura</strong> · <strong>I te kura au inanahi</strong></li><li><strong>keke</strong> vs <strong>kēkē</strong> — &quot;I want to eat the cake&quot; → <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te kai i te keke.</strong> (NOT kēkē — very different!)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why do you think macrons matter so much in te reo Māori? If you left the macron off &quot;nō&quot;, what confusion might happen? Can you think of English words where a single letter changes the meaning completely — similar to how macrons work?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Precision practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that deliberately use confusable words correctly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences — one using <strong>tōku</strong> correctly and one using <strong>tāku</strong> correctly.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mō</strong> (for/about): <strong>He kōrero mō te kura tēnei.</strong> (This is a talk about school.)</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>ki</strong> (to) and one using <strong>i</strong> (at/past location) — make them clearly different.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (4 sentences) that includes tōku, tāku, nō, and mō — all used correctly. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu tūāhua — Prepositions: i, ki, o, a, nō, mō, hei":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.2: Possessions · Grammar Level 1–2: Prepositions of location, direction, possession, purpose</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.5 — Prepositions of location &amp; direction; AO 2.2 — Prepositions of possession &amp; purpose</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The small words that hold everything together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepositions are the small words that show relationships between things — where something is, where it is going, who it belongs to, what it is for. In te reo Māori, a small set of prepositions does enormous work. Today we look at them all together so you can see the full picture.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>i</strong> — at (location in past) / from / by<br><strong>ki</strong> — to / towards / into<br><strong>kei</strong> — at (location right now)<br><strong>o / ō</strong> — of (ō-category possession/connection)<br><strong>a / ā</strong> — of (ā-category possession/connection)<br><strong>nō</strong> — belonging to (ō-category) / from (origin)<br><strong>nā</strong> — belonging to (ā-category) / by/through (agent)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mō</a></strong> — for / about / concerning<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mā</a></strong> — for (ā-category recipient)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hei</a></strong> — at (future location) / as / for (purpose) / as / for (purpose)<br><strong>ko</strong> — identity marker (not a preposition but works like one)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See them in action:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te noho ia i Ōtautahi.</strong> — She lives in Christchurch. (i = at, general location)<br><strong>Kei te haere au ki Ōtautahi.</strong> — I am going to Christchurch. (ki = to)<br><strong>Kei Ōtautahi ia.</strong> — She is in Christchurch right now. (kei = at, present)<br><strong>He kōrero mō Aotearoa tēnei.</strong> — This is a talk about New Zealand. (mō = about)<br><strong>Hoko mai he miraka māku.</strong> — Buy some milk for me. (mā = for, ā-recipient)<br><strong>He taonga hei homai māu.</strong> — A gift to give to you. (hei = for the purpose of)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New place name: <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C5%8Ctautahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ōtautahi</a></strong> = Christchurch</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ki = to (movement toward). Kei = at (present location, no movement). I = at (past) or from. Mō = about/for (ō things). Mā = for (ā recipient). These five do 90% of the work.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Fill the gap</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a sentence with a gap — you choose the right preposition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I am going ___ school.&quot; Moving toward → <strong>ki</strong> → <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong></li><li>&quot;She is ___ the shop right now.&quot; Present location → <strong>kei</strong> → <strong>Kei te toa ia.</strong></li><li>&quot;This book is ___ you.&quot; For a recipient (ā) → <strong>māu</strong> → <strong>He pukapuka māu tēnei.</strong></li><li>&quot;He is speaking ___ te reo Māori.&quot; About/concerning → <strong>mō</strong> → <strong>Kei te kōrero ia mō te reo Māori.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English has dozens of prepositions — at, in, on, to, for, about, by, with, from, through… Te reo Māori does the same work with about seven. How does a smaller set of prepositions make a language easier or harder to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Preposition challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use six different prepositions from today in six different sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences using <strong>ki</strong>, <strong>kei</strong>, and <strong>i</strong> — make the difference between them clear.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>mō</strong> about something you care about.</li><li>Write a sentence using <strong>māku</strong> or <strong>māu</strong> — something for you or me.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short paragraph (5 sentences) about your day that uses at least five different prepositions. Underline each preposition and name it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Ngā kupu matua i mua — Fronted time and place phrases":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 1.5: Location · Grammar Level 1–2: Fronted adverbials — time and place phrases at the start of sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Fronted time phrases; AO 1.5 — Fronted place phrases</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Put time and place first for impact</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Week 6 you learned that time words can go at the start of a sentence. Today we go deeper — putting whole TIME PHRASES and PLACE PHRASES at the front, not just single words. This is called fronting, and it is one of the most powerful tools for creating rhythm and emphasis in your te reo.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Single time word at front (Week 6):<br><strong>Āpōpō ka haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>Tomorrow I will go to the shop.<br><br>Full time PHRASE at front:<br><strong>I te ahiahi o tērā rā, i haere au ki te toa.</strong><br>In the afternoon of that day, I went to the shop.<br><br>Place PHRASE at front:<br><strong>I Ōtautahi, i noho mātou i tētahi whare nui.</strong><br>In Christchurch, we stayed in a big house.<br><br>Both together:<br><strong>I te ata o te Mane, i roto i te kura, i ako ngā tamariki i te reo Māori.</strong><br>On Monday morning, inside the school, the children learned te reo Māori.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for a fronted time phrase:<br><strong>I te [time word/period] o [day/event],</strong> + [main sentence]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Useful time phrase builders:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahiahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahiahi</a></strong> — in the afternoon/evening/evening<br><strong>i te pō</strong> — at night<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmatanga</a></strong> — at the beginning<br><strong>i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutunga</a></strong> — at the end<br><strong>i tērā wiki</strong> — last week<br><strong>i tēnei rā</strong> — today / on this day</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern for a fronted place phrase:<br><strong>I [place],</strong> + [main sentence]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The comma after the fronted phrase signals to the listener: &quot;scene set — now here comes the main action.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Front-loading gives your listener the CONTEXT before the action. It is like setting the stage before the actors enter. Always add a comma after a fronted phrase.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Front it!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence. You move the time or place phrase to the front and add a comma.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plain: &quot;I learned te reo in the morning.&quot; → Front the time: <strong>I te ata, i ako au i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Plain: &quot;The children played outside yesterday.&quot; → Front the time: <strong>Inanahi, i tākaro ngā tamariki i waho.</strong></li><li>Plain: &quot;We ate in the classroom at lunchtime.&quot; → Front the place: <strong>I roto i te akomanga, i kai mātou i te tina.</strong></li><li>Now try both — front the time AND place: &quot;At the end of school, in the hall, we sang a waiata.&quot; → <strong>I te mutunga o te kura, i roto i te whare hui, i waiata mātou.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Read these two versions aloud: &quot;I went to school in the morning yesterday&quot; vs &quot;Inanahi, i te ata, i haere au ki te kura.&quot; Which feels more dramatic? Why does putting time and place first change the feel of a sentence?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Front-loaded sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that open with a time or place phrase.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences with fronted TIME phrases — use i te ata and i te ahiahi.</li><li>Write two sentences with fronted PLACE phrases — use i roto i te kura and i waho.</li><li>Write one sentence with BOTH a fronted time phrase AND a fronted place phrase.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite this bare sentence with as much fronted detail as you can: &quot;We played.&quot; Aim for a sentence that paints a complete picture of when, where, and how — all before the main verb.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō tōku kāinga — Talking about home":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.2: Possessions · AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 1.5: Location</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Personal information &amp; home; AO 2.5 — Describing your home</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing where you live</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you learned confusable words, prepositions, and fronted phrases. Today we use all three in a conversation about home — where you live, what it is like, and what happens there. This is deeply personal language — the kind that helps you really connect with someone.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Nō hea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō Ōtautahi ahau. He tāone nui, he ātaahua hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei hea tō kāinga i ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te taha o te awa tōku kāinga. He whare hou, he whare māmā hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea pai o tō wāhi noho?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei ngā mea pai: he nui ngā rākau, he mārie te noho, ā he tata ki te kura hoki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I te ahiahi, he aha āu mahi i tō kāinga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I te ahiahi, i te māra, ka mahi au. Ka tiaki hoki au i ngā kararehe o mātou.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words from this conversation:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81one\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāone</a></strong> — town / city<br><strong>te taha o</strong> — the side of / beside<br><strong>awa</strong> — river<br><strong>māmā</strong> — light / easy (also: mild earlier) — context tells you which<br><strong>wāhi noho</strong> — place of living / neighbourhood<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a></strong> — tree / trees<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tata</a></strong> — close / nearby<br><strong>māra</strong> — garden<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a></strong> — to look after / guard / guard<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kararehe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kararehe</a></strong> — animal / pet</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the fronted phrase at the end: <strong>I te ahiahi</strong> — in the afternoon. It sets the time before the action. And <strong>ko ēnei ngā mea pai</strong> from Week 7 — introducing a list of good things.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nō hea koe? = Where are you from? Kei hea tō kāinga? = Where is your home right now? Two different questions — know which one you are answering.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe your home</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the home conversation piece by piece. I ask — you answer using this week's structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Nō hea koe?</strong> You answer with your town/city and one He description of it.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Kei hea tō kāinga?</strong> Use a preposition and a location: <strong>Kei te taha o te…</strong> or <strong>Kei roto i te…</strong></li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea pai o tō wāhi noho?</strong> Use Ko ēnei to introduce a list of three good things.</li><li>I ask: <strong>I te ahiahi, he aha āu mahi?</strong> Answer with a fronted time phrase and two activities.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Your home and where you are from is deeply connected to your identity in te ao Māori. The pepeha from Week 4 connected you to mountains, rivers, and iwi. How does today's conversation connect to that same idea — that place and identity are inseparable?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He kōrero mō tōku kāinga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full description of your home and neighbourhood in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write where you are from and one thing that makes your town special: <strong>Nō… ahau. He…</strong></li><li>Write where your home is using a preposition: <strong>Kei te taha o / Kei roto i / Kei tata ki…</strong></li><li>Use Ko ēnei to list three good things about your neighbourhood.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence paragraph about your home using: a fronted time phrase, a Ko ēnei list, at least three prepositions, and one confusable word pair used correctly (tōku/tāku or nō/nā).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the difference between &quot;keke&quot; and &quot;kēkē&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They are spelling variations of the same word </li><li>keke = cake, kēkē = armpit — the macron completely changes the meaning ✅</li><li>keke is formal, kēkē is informal </li><li>kēkē is the plural of keke </li></ul></li><li>Which possessive would you use for &quot;my school&quot;? (kura is ō-category)<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tāku kura </li><li>nāku kura </li><li>tōku kura ✅</li><li>māku kura </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot; Which preposition shows movement TOWARD a place?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>kei </li><li>i </li><li>ki ✅</li><li>o </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pukapuka māu tēnei.&quot; What does māu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for me </li><li>for him/her </li><li>for you ✅</li><li>for us </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I te ata, i ako au i te reo Māori.&quot; What is the purpose of putting &quot;i te ata&quot; at the front?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It makes the sentence shorter </li><li>It sets the time context before the main action — fronting for impact ✅</li><li>It is a grammar rule that verbs must come last </li><li>It replaces the need for a tense marker </li></ul></li><li>What punctuation always follows a fronted time or place phrase in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A full stop </li><li>A semicolon </li><li>A comma ✅</li><li>A colon </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nō hea koe?&quot; vs &quot;Kei hea tō kāinga?&quot; — what is the difference?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Nō hea asks where you are from (origin), Kei hea asks where your home is right now (location) ✅</li><li>Nō hea is formal, Kei hea is casual </li><li>Nō hea asks about the future, Kei hea asks about the past </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kararehe&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>garden </li><li>tree </li><li>animal / pet ✅</li><li>neighbour </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu oro — Sound words and vivid language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptive and figurative language · AO 2.3: Expressive communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Onomatopoeia, sound imagery, expressive vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Vivid &amp; expressive vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Expressive communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Words that sound like what they mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some of the most powerful words in any language are ones that imitate sounds or carry a strong physical feeling. Te reo Māori is rich with these — words that bring a scene alive the moment you hear them. Today we collect the best ones and learn to use them deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngunguru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngunguru</a></strong> — to growl / rumble (deep sound)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABh%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūhū</a></strong> — to hum / buzz (insect or engine)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pak%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pakū</a></strong> — to bang / explode / crack<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngāoro</a></strong> — to roar / rushing sound of water or wind<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a></strong> — to cry / sound of weeping (also: funeral / to ring)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kō</a></strong> — the sound of a bird call<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oro</a></strong> — sound / noise in general<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pap%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papā</a></strong> — to slap / crack loudly<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABp%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūpē</a></strong> — runny, dripping (wet sound)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hau</a></strong> — wind / breath / spirit</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These words do double work — they describe AND create the sound at the same time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a sentence:<br><strong>I ngunguru te whatitoka i te hau kino.</strong><br>The door growled/rumbled in the fierce wind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka pakū te whatitoka i tōna tūkitanga.</strong><br>The door cracked/banged at its closing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I tangi ngā tamariki ā te pō.</strong><br>The children cried through the night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>whatitoka</strong> — door<br><strong>hau kino</strong> — fierce wind / bad weather<br><strong>tūkitanga</strong> — a striking / closing / impact</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can also pair these sound words with tino for intensity:<br><strong>He tino ngāoro te awa i te ua nui.</strong><br>The river roared greatly in the heavy rain.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Sound words wake a listener up. Drop one into a sentence and the whole thing comes alive. Use them sparingly — one per paragraph is enough.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Bring the scene alive</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a scene — you choose a sound word that fits and build a sentence around it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scene: a storm outside at night. Which sound word fits? → ngāoro, hau, pakū → <strong>I ngāoro te ua i te pō — he pakū hoki te uira.</strong></li><li>Scene: a dog in the yard early morning. → <strong>I ngunguru te kurī i te ata.</strong> (kurī = dog)</li><li>Scene: a child who has hurt themselves. → <strong>I tangi kaha te tamaiti — i rongo ia i te mamae.</strong> (rongo = feel, mamae = pain)</li><li>Now you make one: pick a sound word and describe something you heard today using it in a Kei te or I sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English has onomatopoeia like &quot;buzz&quot;, &quot;crash&quot;, &quot;splash&quot;. Which of today's te reo sound words do you think sounds most like what it means? Why do you think languages around the world independently develop words that imitate sounds?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sound sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use sound words deliberately to make your writing vivid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences — each one using a different sound word from today.</li><li>Take this bare sentence and add a sound word: &quot;The wind came.&quot; → Make it vivid.</li><li>Describe your school at lunchtime using at least two sound words.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence scene — morning at your home, or a storm, or a sports match — using at least three sound words. Read it aloud. Does it sound alive?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hono katoa — All conjunctions: ā, engari, nō reira, ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Full range of coordinating and subordinating conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Conjunctions &amp; complex sentences; AO 2.5 — Connecting ideas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your full conjunction toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Across this term you have learned conjunctions one by one. Today we gather them all in one place, compare them side by side, and practise choosing the right one deliberately. By the end of this lesson you should be able to join any two ideas in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Coordinating (joining equal ideas):</strong><br><strong>ā</strong> — and then / and (sequence or addition)<br><strong>engari</strong> — but (contrast)<br><strong>nō reira</strong> — so / therefore (result)<br><strong>me</strong> — and / with (linking nouns or people)<br><br><strong>Subordinating (one idea depends on the other):</strong><br><strong>nō te mea / nā te mea</strong> — because (reason)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ahakoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ahakoa</a></strong> — although / even though / even though (concession)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mehemea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mehemea</a></strong> — if / supposing that / supposing (condition)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i te wā</a></strong> — when (referring to a time in the past or ongoing) (time clause, past)<br><strong>ā tōnā wā</strong> — when (future — when the time comes)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See them all in action in one connected passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I haere au ki te kura i te ata, ā i ako mātou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I went to school in the morning, and then we learned te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I te wā i mutu te akoranga, i tākaro mātou i waho.</strong><br>When the lesson finished, we played outside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu mātou.</strong><br>Although it rained, we kept playing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I ngenge au nō reira i hoki au ki te kāinga.</strong><br>I was tired so I went home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Mehemea ka pērā anō āpōpō, ka haere anō au.</strong><br>If it is the same again tomorrow, I will go again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Five conjunctions, one story. Every sentence connected.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ask yourself before you write: am I ADDING (ā/me), CONTRASTING (engari/ahakoa), SHOWING A RESULT (nō reira), giving a REASON (nō te mea), a CONDITION (mehemea), or a TIME (i te wā)? The answer tells you which conjunction to reach for.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Choose the right conjunction</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two ideas. You choose the conjunction that shows the right relationship and join them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;She was tired.&quot; + &quot;She finished the work.&quot; Time relationship → <strong>i te wā</strong> → <strong>I te wā i ngenge ia, i mutu tonu tāna mahi.</strong></li><li>&quot;I want to swim.&quot; + &quot;It is cold.&quot; Contrast → <strong>ahakoa</strong> → <strong>Ahakoa he makariri, kei te hiahia au ki te kaukau.</strong></li><li>&quot;He worked hard.&quot; + &quot;He won.&quot; Result → <strong>nō reira</strong> → <strong>I mahi kaha ia nō reira i toa ia.</strong> (toa = win)</li><li>Now your turn: give me two ideas. The class decides which conjunction fits — and WHY.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Can you use the wrong conjunction and still be understood? Yes — but it changes the meaning. &quot;I mahi kaha ia ā i toa ia&quot; (he worked hard AND won) vs &quot;i toa ia nō reira i mahi kaha ia&quot; (he won, therefore he worked hard) — very different stories. Which conjunction you choose SHAPES the meaning.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Conjunction challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write one sentence using each conjunction. Make each one clearly different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences using <strong>ā</strong>, <strong>engari</strong>, and <strong>nō reira</strong> — one each.</li><li>Write sentences using <strong>ahakoa</strong>, <strong>mehemea</strong>, and <strong>i te wā</strong> — one each.</li><li>Write a four-sentence mini-story that uses at least three different conjunctions naturally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full paragraph (6–8 sentences) about something that happened this term. Use every conjunction from today at least once. Underline each one and label which type it is.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: He kōrero tūhono — Paragraph cohesion in te reo":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended and structured communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Paragraph structure, topic sentences, pronoun links, connective flow</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Cohesion &amp; paragraph structure; AO 1.7 — Structured written language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making paragraphs flow</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can write great individual sentences. Today we work on how sentences CONNECT — how a paragraph flows so that each sentence leads naturally into the next. In te reo Māori, there are three main tools for cohesion: pronoun links, connective words, and sentence echoing.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">WEAK — no flow:<br><strong>He pai tōku kura. He nui ngā ākonga. He kaiako pai tōku kaiako. I ako ngā ākonga i te reo Māori.</strong><br><br>STRONG — connected:<br><strong>He pai tōku kura nō te mea he nui ngā ākonga. Ko rātou ngā tamariki māhoe o tō mātou hapori. I ako rātou i te reo Māori i tēnei wiki, ā i tino pai tā rātou mahi. Nō reira, he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three tools for paragraph cohesion:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>1. Pronoun links</strong> — replace a noun with a pronoun so you don't repeat the noun:<br>&quot;ngā ākonga… Ko rātou… I ako rātou…&quot;<br>(the students… They are… They learned…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>2. Connective words</strong> — signal the relationship between sentences:<br>nō reira (therefore), ā (and then), engari (but), nō te mea (because)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>3. Sentence echoing</strong> — repeat a key word or idea to tie sentences together:<br>&quot;He pai tōku kura… he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.&quot;<br>(My school is good… my school is truly special.)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hoe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhoe</a></strong> — talented / bright<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hapori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hapori</a></strong> — community<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=motuhake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">motuhake</a></strong> — special / unique / independent / independent</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: After writing each sentence, ask: does this connect to what came before? If you had to jump to a new idea, use a connective. If you mentioned a noun, replace it with a pronoun next time. If you want to land a final point, echo your opening.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Fix the paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a weak, disconnected paragraph. We improve it together — one sentence at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 is fine: <strong>He pai tōku kura.</strong> Sentence 2 repeats the noun: &quot;He nui ngā ākonga o tōku kura.&quot; → Replace with a pronoun: <strong>He nui ō rātou kaha.</strong> (There is much ability among them.)</li><li>Sentence 3 has no connective: &quot;I ako ngā ākonga.&quot; → Add one: <strong>I tēnei wiki, i ako rātou i ngā kupu hou.</strong></li><li>Sentence 4 needs to land with an echo: &quot;He kura pai.&quot; → Echo the opening: <strong>Nō reira, he kura tino motuhake tōku kura.</strong></li><li>Now read the full improved paragraph aloud together. Can you feel the difference in how it flows?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: A paragraph should feel like a conversation where each sentence responds to the last. Which of the three cohesion tools — pronoun links, connectives, or echoing — do you think is the most powerful? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Write a cohesive paragraph</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full paragraph in te reo Māori that flows from beginning to end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a topic sentence using <strong>He</strong> or <strong>Ko</strong> about your class, school, or something you care about.</li><li>Write two or three supporting sentences — use pronoun links to avoid repeating nouns.</li><li>Add a connective (nō reira / ā / engari) before one of your sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full five-sentence paragraph and deliberately use all three cohesion tools — pronoun link, connective, and sentence echo. Highlight each tool in a different colour.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He arotake — Looking back, moving forward":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 1.4: Personal information</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Reflection &amp; extended kōrero; AO 2.5 — Expressive vocabulary review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Reflecting on what you have learned</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last week of Term 1. Today we use everything you have built — vivid words, conjunctions, cohesion — in a reflective conversation about your learning journey this term. This kind of reflective language is important in te ao Māori and in life.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu whakaaro mō tēnei wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai ki a au tēnei wiki. I ako au i ngā kupu oro — pēnei i te &quot;ngunguru&quot; me te &quot;pakū&quot;. Ka taea e au te whakamahi i ērā kupu ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i uaua ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā kupu tūhono — engari ināianei, ka mārama ana au ki a rātou.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu whainga mō te kāhui o tērā wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako au i ngā kupu arataki katoa o Wīki 10 — ā ka kōrero māmā au i te reo Māori!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro! He ātaahua tō ara ako.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words and phrases for reflection:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>whakaaro</strong> — thought / opinion / idea<br><strong>He aha tāu whakaaro mō…?</strong> — What do you think about…?<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… / I can now…<br><strong>Ka mārama ana au</strong> — I understand / I am understanding<br><strong>i uaua ki a au</strong> — it was difficult for me<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whainga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whainga</a></strong> — goal / aim / target / target<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=arataki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">arataki</a></strong> — to guide / lead<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara+ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara ako</a></strong> — learning pathway / journey / journey<br><strong>He ātaahua tō ara ako.</strong> — Your learning journey is beautiful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice: <strong>ka… ana</strong> is a continuous present form — &quot;I am (currently) understanding.&quot; It implies the understanding is ongoing and growing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori — I am able to speak te reo Māori. Learn this sentence cold. Say it at the start of every te reo session for the rest of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Reflect together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We have a structured reflection conversation. I ask — you answer honestly, then ask me back.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i pai rawa atu ki a koe i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> (What did you like most this term?) Answer with at least two sentences.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i uaua ki a koe?</strong> (What was difficult for you?) Use <strong>i uaua ki a au… engari…</strong> to show you overcame it.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ka taea e koe te kōrero i te reo Māori ināianei?</strong> (Can you speak te reo Māori now?) Answer using <strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> + three things you can do.</li><li>Close: state your goal for Week 10 — <strong>Ko tāku whainga mō tērā wiki:</strong> + your aim.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He ātaahua tō ara ako. What does it mean to say your learning journey is beautiful? In te ao Māori, learning is not just about knowledge — it connects you to people, to land, and to identity. How has learning te reo Māori this term connected you to something bigger than just words?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu ara ako — Your learning journey</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a reflective piece about your learning this term — entirely in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences about what you can do now that you couldn't do in Week 1. Use <strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong></li><li>Write one sentence about something that was difficult using <strong>i uaua ki a au… engari…</strong></li><li>Write your goal for Week 10 using <strong>Ko tāku whainga:</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflective paragraph (6 sentences) about your Term 1 journey. Use a topic sentence, pronoun links, a conjunction, and close with an echo of your opening. Share it with a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which te reo Māori word best describes a deep rumbling or growling sound?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tangi </li><li>pakū </li><li>ngunguru ✅</li><li>hūhū </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I tangi ngā tamariki ā te pō.&quot; What does tangi mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the children laughed through the night </li><li>the children cried through the night ✅</li><li>the children played through the night </li><li>the children slept through the night </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which conjunction would you use to show a RESULT or CONCLUSION?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā </li><li>ahakoa </li><li>nō reira ✅</li><li>i te wā </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu mātou.&quot; What relationship does ahakoa show?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a time — when it rained we played </li><li>a result — because it rained we played </li><li>a concession — DESPITE the rain, we still played ✅</li><li>a condition — if it rains we will play </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is &quot;sentence echoing&quot; in a paragraph?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Saying the same sentence twice in a row </li><li>Repeating a key word or idea at the end to connect back to the opening ✅</li><li>Using the same verb in every sentence </li><li>Writing sentences that are all the same length </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;motuhake&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>difficult </li><li>tired </li><li>special / unique / independent ✅</li><li>beautiful </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I used to be able to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I am able to speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>I am learning to speak te reo Māori </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whainga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>thought / opinion </li><li>learning journey </li><li>goal / aim / target ✅</li><li>community </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 1 vocabulary and pronunciation review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.3: Numbers · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary review · All Level 1–2 AOs consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings review; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything we know: vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the final week of Term 1. Today we celebrate how much vocabulary you have built — from simple greetings in Week 1 to vivid sound words and reflective language in Week 9. We are going to do a full vocabulary review, organised by theme, and find the gaps worth filling before the end of term.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>GREETINGS &amp; FAREWELL</strong> (Week 1)<br>Kia ora · Tēnā koe · Tēnā koutou katoa · E noho rā · Hei konā rā<br><br><strong>PEOPLE &amp; IDENTITY</strong> (Weeks 1–2, 4)<br>au · koe · ia · tāua · mātou · tātou · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br><br><strong>FOOD &amp; DRINK</strong> (Week 2)<br>āporo · panana · parāoa · miraka · wai · tī · keke<br><br><strong>ACTIONS</strong> (Weeks 1, 3, 6)<br>haere · kai · inu · ako · kōrero · tākaro · mahi · pānui · tuhituhi · omaoma · kaukau<br><br><strong>PLACES</strong> (Weeks 3–4, 8)<br>kura · kāinga · toa · awa · roto · māra · akomanga<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTIONS</strong> (Weeks 2, 5, 7)<br>nui · iti · roa · poto · pai · kino · ātaahua · tino · ake · rawa atu<br><br><strong>TIME</strong> (Weeks 6–8)<br>ināianei · āpōpō · inanahi · i te ata · i te ahiahi · i te pō</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's count what you know:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Greetings: 5+ phrases<br>✓ Personal pronouns: 8 (au, koe, ia, māua, tāua, mātou, tātou, koutou…)<br>✓ Verbs/actions: 12+<br>✓ Nouns (places, food, objects): 20+<br>✓ Adjectives: 10+<br>✓ Numbers: 1–100<br>✓ Question words: 11<br>✓ Conjunctions: 7<br>✓ Prepositions: 8<br>✓ Time expressions: 8+<br>✓ Possessives: 6 (tōku, tāku, tōu, tāu, tōna, tāna)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is well over 100 pieces of te reo Māori in 9 weeks.<br><strong>Ka rawe!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The fastest way to review vocabulary is to use it — not read it. Close the list and try to say everything from memory. What comes easily? What do you need to look at again? That gap is your target.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Vocabulary blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed round — I call a category, you give me as many words as you can. Class builds the list together on the board.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ACTIONS: How many verbs can we list in 60 seconds? Aim for 10+. Teacher writes each one on the board.</li><li>DESCRIPTIONS: Call out every adjective you know — including the comparison forms (ake, rawa atu, tino).</li><li>QUESTION WORDS: Can someone recite all 11 question words from memory? Ko wai, He aha, Kei hea…</li><li>WILD CARD: Say a sentence using a word from EACH category — greetings, action, description, time, place — all in one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Discussion prompt: Look at everything on the board. Which category surprised you — you know more than you thought? Which category has the most gaps? Set yourself a personal target: I will learn </em><strong> more words from </strong><em> category before the end of this week.</em></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Personal vocabulary audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Do a personal review of your own vocabulary knowledge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Without looking at the list: write down every te reo Māori word you can remember in 3 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Go back to Week 1 in your mind — what are the five most important things you learned that week? Write them.</li><li>Write the one word or phrase from this term that you find most useful. Write three sentences using it.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Create your own personal &quot;Term 1 kupu list&quot; — organised by category, from memory. Then check it against the lesson summaries. What did you miss? What did you remember perfectly?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Term 1 grammar review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Grammar Level 1–2 structures consolidated: Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, Kāore, possessives, prepositions, conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation; AO 2.5 — Structure review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure you know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put every grammar structure from this term on the table. You have learned more than you realise. Watch how all the pieces fit together — and how one structure leads naturally into the next.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY: Ko</strong><br>Ko Aroha tōku ingoa. Ko tōku kura tōku wāhi pai.<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTION: He</strong><br>He ātaahua tōku kura. He nui ake tēnei whare i tērā.<br><br><strong>PRESENT: Kei te</strong><br>Kei te ako au i te reo Māori. Kei te pēhea koe?<br><br><strong>PAST: I</strong><br>I haere au ki te kura inanahi. I ngenge ia nō reira i hoki ia ki te kāinga.<br><br><strong>NARRATIVE: Ka</strong><br>Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo.<br><br><strong>NEGATIVE: <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81ore\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kāore</a></strong><br>Kāore au e kai ana. Kāore au i haere.<br><br><strong>POSSESSION: tōku/tāku</strong><br>Ko Mere tōku ingoa. Kei te pānui au i tāku pukapuka.<br><br><strong>COMPLEX: ahakoa / mehemea / i te wā</strong><br>Ahakoa i ua, i haere tonu mātou. Mehemea ka haere koe, kōrero mai.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full picture of what you can do:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Identify things and people (Ko)<br>→ Describe things (He + adjective)<br>→ Talk about now (Kei te)<br>→ Talk about the past (I)<br>→ Tell a story (Ka)<br>→ Say something is NOT happening (Kāore)<br>→ Show who things belong to (tōku/tāku and friends)<br>→ Ask any question (Ko wai / He aha / Kei hea / E hia / Tokohia / Pēhea…)<br>→ Join ideas (ā / engari / nō reira / ahakoa / mehemea / i te wā)<br>→ Describe in detail (adjectives, adverbs, fronted phrases)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is a complete communication toolkit. You can express almost any idea with these structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You do not need to remember the grammar names — you need to feel when to use each structure. The best way to do that is to speak and write as much as possible. The names help you think about it; the practice makes it automatic.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Structure challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a concept to express — you choose the right structure and build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Express who you are: → Ko + name/identity. Express what something is like: → He + description.</li><li>Express something happening right now AND something that happened yesterday: → Kei te… / I…</li><li>Say something is NOT happening: → Kāore au e… ana. Say it did NOT happen: → Kāore au i…</li><li>Join these with the right conjunction: &quot;I was tired&quot; + &quot;I kept working&quot; → <strong>Ahakoa i ngenge au, i mahi tonu au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Which structure do you feel most confident with? Which one still feels uncertain? Be honest — the gaps you name now are the ones you can fix in Week 10 and Term 2.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every structure from this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence using each of: Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka, Kāore. (Six sentences.)</li><li>Write one sentence using tōku and one using tāku — make sure the category is right.</li><li>Write a complex sentence using one of: ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a ten-sentence paragraph that uses EVERY structure from this term at least once. Tell a story about a day in your life — past, present, description, and all.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Arotake — Term 1 sentence types review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Descriptive language · All sentence types from Term 1 consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sentence variety review; AO 2.5 — Rich description review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From bare to brilliant: the full sentence toolkit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review every sentence type you have built this term — simple, compound, complex, descriptive, fronted, layered — and practise moving between them fluently. A skilled speaker can shift from a short punchy sentence to a long flowing one in a heartbeat. Let's do that.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SIMPLE — bare and clear:</strong><br>Kei te ua. Ka tū ia. He pai tēnei.<br><br><strong>COMPOUND — joined:</strong><br>I haere au ki te toa ā i hoko au i te āporo.<br><br><strong>COMPLEX — subordinate clause:</strong><br>Ahakoa i ua, i haere tonu mātou ki te kura.<br><br><strong>DESCRIPTIVE — layered:</strong><br>He āporo whero nui tēnei — he tino pai hoki!<br><br><strong>FRONTED — time or place first:</strong><br>I te ata o Mane, i roto i te akomanga, i ako ngā tamariki.<br><br><strong>LISTED — Ko ēnei:</strong><br>Ko ēnei ngā mea i ako ai mātou: he kupu hou, he hanganga hou, he kōrero hou.<br><br><strong>COMPARATIVE:</strong><br>He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau ki a au — he pai rawa atu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The progression from bare to brilliant:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>BARE: <strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki.</strong><br>+WHERE: <strong>Kei te ako ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+HOW: <strong>Kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+TIME: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki i te akomanga.</strong><br>+WITH WHOM: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga.</strong><br>+JOINED: <strong>Ināianei, kei te ako kaha ngā tamariki me tō rātou kaiako i te akomanga — ā he tino pai tā rātou mahi!</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Six layers. One idea. Completely alive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You do not need to use every layer every time. The skill is knowing WHICH layers to add and WHEN. Short when you want impact. Long when you want richness. You now have full control.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build up and strip back</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We take one bare sentence and build it to maximum richness — then strip back to find the &quot;just right&quot; version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: <strong>I tākaro ia.</strong> Add where, how, time, with whom — one layer at a time. Stop when it feels complete.</li><li>Start: <strong>He pai tōku kura.</strong> Add a reason, a comparison, and a Ko ēnei list. How long can it get?</li><li>Now strip back your longest sentence to its most precise version — not shortest, most precise.</li><li>Read both versions aloud — the fully layered one and the stripped-back one. Which works better? When would you use each?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have been building sentences for ten weeks. Look back at a sentence you wrote in Week 1. How would you write it differently now? What does that difference tell you about how much you have grown?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the best sentences you have ever written in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — punchy and precise.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — with a subordinate clause.</li><li>Write your best LAYERED sentence — time, manner, place, with whom.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;best of Term 1&quot; paragraph — eight sentences that show off everything you have learned. Use every sentence type at least once. This is your showcase. Make it sing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End-of-term spoken assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–1.7 and 2.1–2.5 assessed · All six language modes: Whakarongo, Kōrero, Pānui, Tuhituhi, Mātakitaki, Whakaatu</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.3 — Extended communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What a Term 1 conversation looks like</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is your end-of-term spoken assessment. Before we begin, let's look at what a full Term 1 conversation sounds like when everything comes together. This is the standard — and you are closer to it than you think.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai tōu ingoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko Hēmi tōku ingoa. Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Ko wai koe?<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko Aroha tōku ingoa. Nō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=T%C4%81maki+Makaurau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tāmaki Makaurau</a> ahau. Kei te pēhea koe i tēnei rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te pai au, kia ora. Ahakoa he makariri, kei te hari au — he wiki pai tēnei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe i tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ako au i ngā hanganga katoa — Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, me ngā kupu hono. Ka taea e au te kōrero pai ake ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha tāu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako au i ngā tūāhua o Wāhanga Rua — ā ka kōrero māmā ake au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What this conversation demonstrates:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe · greeting and farewell<br>✓ AO 1.2: Ko [name] tōku ingoa · introduction<br>✓ AO 1.4: Nō [place] ahau · personal information<br>✓ AO 1.6: Tēnā koe (×3) · politeness convention<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended kōrero with reasons and reflection<br>✓ AO 2.5: Descriptive and expressive vocabulary<br>✓ Grammar: Kei te, I, Ka, Ko, He, ahakoa, Ka taea e au te</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have everything you need. Now go show it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: If you forget a word mid-conversation, do not stop — keep going. Say what you CAN say. A real conversation in te reo Māori, even with gaps, is far more impressive than silence.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice round</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One full practice conversation with a partner before the assessment. Use the full structure from the model above.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet and introduce: Tēnā koe · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau.</li><li>Ask and answer: Kei te pēhea koe? Answer with a feeling AND a reason using ahakoa or nō te mea.</li><li>Ask and answer: He aha ngā mea hou i ako ai koe? Name three things using Ka taea e au te…</li><li>State your Term 2 goal and close with the three-part farewell: Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You started this term not knowing a single word of te reo Māori. You are finishing it able to have a real conversation. What does that feel like? What would you tell your Week 1 self about what is coming?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — Your assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform your end-of-term conversation assessment with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Have a full conversation — at least 8 lines each. Cover greeting, introduction, how you are feeling, what you learned, and a goal.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess against the checklist below. Be honest.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write three sentences in te reo about how you feel about your learning this term.</li><li>✦ Checklist: ☐ Greeted my partner correctly · ☐ Introduced myself with Ko and Nō · ☐ Used at least one tense (Kei te / I / Ka) · ☐ Used at least one conjunction · ☐ Said something descriptive · ☐ Closed with Tēnā koe (×3)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is the correct plural demonstrative for &quot;those things over there&quot; (plural of tērā)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ēnei </li><li>ēnā </li><li>ērā ✅</li><li>tērā </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori&quot; contains which grammar structure?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore negation </li><li>Ka taea e… te — I am able to… ✅</li><li>Kei te present tense </li><li>Ko identity marker </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses Kāore in the PAST tense negative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore au e haere ana </li><li>Kāore au i haere ✅</li><li>Kāore haere au </li><li>Kāore au ki haere </li></ul></li><li>You want to say &quot;Although it was cold, we kept working.&quot; Which conjunction do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>nō reira </li><li>ā </li><li>ahakoa ✅</li><li>i te wā </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the main purpose of &quot;sentence echoing&quot; in a paragraph?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To fill space when you run out of ideas </li><li>To confuse the reader with repetition </li><li>To tie the paragraph together by returning to a key word or idea from the opening ✅</li><li>To make every sentence the same length </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I te ata o Māne, i roto i te akomanga, i ako ngā tamariki.&quot; What type of sentence is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A simple sentence </li><li>A negative sentence </li><li>A fronted sentence with time and place phrases before the main clause ✅</li><li>A comparative sentence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the end-of-term conversation, which greeting is used when addressing MORE than two people formally?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tēnā koe </li><li>Tēnā kōrua </li><li>Tēnā koutou katoa ✅</li><li>Kia ora </li></ul></li><li>He wāhanga tino pai tēnei. What does &quot;wāhanga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sentence </li><li>question </li><li>term / section / part ✅</li><li>week </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: I haere au — The past tense unlocked":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: I (past tense marker), action verbs in past</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Past tense &amp; time; AO 2.3 — Retelling events</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Welcome back: from now to then</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nau mai, hoki mai — welcome back. Last term you mastered Kei te for right now. This term we begin by going back in time. You already met the past tense briefly in Term 1 — today we make it solid. Watch how simple the switch is.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> haere au. — I am going. (RIGHT NOW)<br><strong>I</strong> haere au. — I went. (PAST)<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> kai ia. — She is eating.<br><strong>I</strong> kai ia. — She ate.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> ako ngā tamariki. — The children are learning.<br><strong>I</strong> ako ngā tamariki. — The children learned.<br><br><strong>Kei te</strong> tākaro rātou i waho. — They are playing outside.<br><strong>I</strong> tākaro rātou i waho inanahi. — They played outside yesterday.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The switch from present to past in te reo Māori is the simplest thing you will ever do:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Replace <strong>Kei te</strong> with <strong>I</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is it. The verb does not change. The person does not change. The word order does not change. Only the tense marker changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> → RIGHT NOW<br><strong>I</strong> → PAST (it happened, it is done)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In English, you must change the verb: &quot;go → went&quot;, &quot;eat → ate&quot;, &quot;run → ran&quot; — and you must memorise dozens of irregular forms. Te reo Māori never does this. The verb is ALWAYS the same. Only the marker changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time words that go with I:<br><strong>inanahi</strong> — yesterday<br><strong>i tērā wiki</strong> — last week<br><strong>i tērā tau</strong> — last year<br><strong>i mua</strong> — before / previously<br><strong>i tēnei ata</strong> — this morning</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te = now. I = then. Swap the marker, keep everything else exactly the same. That is the whole rule.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Switch the tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a present tense sentence — you switch it to past. Then we add a time word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong> → Past: <strong>I haere au ki te kura inanahi.</strong></li><li>Present: <strong>Kei te kai rātou i te tina.</strong> → Past: <strong>I kai rātou i te tina i tērā wiki.</strong></li><li>Present: <strong>Kei te mahi kaha ngā ākonga.</strong> → Past: <strong>I mahi kaha ngā ākonga i tēnei ata.</strong></li><li>Now you give ME a present tense sentence — I will switch it to past. Then swap roles.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English, switching to past tense means changing the verb — &quot;play → played&quot;, &quot;go → went&quot;, &quot;eat → ate&quot;. Te reo Māori never changes the verb. Only the marker changes. Which system feels more logical to you? Which is easier to learn?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell me what happened</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write past tense sentences about real things that happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three past tense sentences about what you did inanahi (yesterday).</li><li>Write two past tense sentences about what happened i tērā wiki (last week).</li><li>Take this sentence and make it past: <strong>Kei te omaoma kaha ia i waho.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence recount of something that happened in the holidays. Use I for every sentence, and include at least two time words.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupumahi o mua — More past tense action verbs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.5: Location · AO 2.3: Retelling · Grammar Level 1–2: Past tense with action verbs, object marker i</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Retelling past events; AO 1.5 — Past location</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Actions in the past with objects</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday we switched tense markers. Today we add objects — the THINGS that actions happen to. In te reo Māori, when a verb has a direct object, you mark it with the small word i (different from the past tense I — notice the capital). Watch how they work together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I hoko au</strong> i te āporo.<br>I bought an apple. (hoko = buy, i te āporo = the apple)<br><br><strong>I pānui ia</strong> i tāna pukapuka.<br>She read her book.<br><br><strong>I tuhituhi ngā tamariki</strong> i ā rātou reta.<br>The children wrote their letters. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=reta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">reta</a> = letter)<br><br><strong>I kite au</strong> i tōku hoa.<br>I saw my friend. (kite = see/find)<br><br><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> ia</strong> i te tangi o te manu.<br>She heard the cry of the bird. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> = hear/feel, <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a> = cry/sound, <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> = bird)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The structure with an object:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I</strong> + [verb] + [person] + <strong>i</strong> + [object]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The small <strong>i</strong> before the object is the OBJECT MARKER — it signals &quot;and here is the thing the action happened to.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Do not confuse:<br>→ <strong>I</strong> (capital, start of sentence) = PAST tense marker<br>→ <strong>i</strong> (lowercase, before object) = OBJECT marker</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both are in this sentence:<br><strong>I</strong> hoko au <strong>i</strong> te āporo.<br>PAST + buy + I + OBJECT + the apple.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New verbs:<br><strong>kite</strong> — see / find / discover<br><strong>rongo</strong> — hear / feel / sense<br><strong>hoatu</strong> — give (away from speaker)<br><strong>homai</strong> — give (toward speaker)<br><strong>tango</strong> — take / pick up<br><strong>reta</strong> — letter</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Capital I at the start = past tense. Lowercase i before an object = object marker. Same sound, completely different job. Context and position always make it clear.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Past tense with objects</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build past tense sentences that include an object. I give you the verb and object — you build the sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Verb: kite (see), Object: tōku hoa (my friend) → <strong>I kite au i tōku hoa.</strong></li><li>Verb: hoko (buy), Object: he panana (a banana) → <strong>I hoko ia i he panana.</strong></li><li>Verb: homai (give to me), Object: tāna pukapuka (her book) → <strong>I homai ia i tāna pukapuka ki a au.</strong></li><li>Now make your own: something you saw, heard, or found yesterday. Use I + verb + au + i + object.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The object marker i does the same job as &quot;the&quot; in &quot;I bought THE apple&quot; — it points at a specific thing. Why do you think te reo Māori needs a separate marker for this? What would happen to the sentence without it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — What did you do?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write past tense sentences that include direct objects.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using different verbs: kite, rongo, hoko. Include an object in each.</li><li>Write a sentence using homai: something someone gave you.</li><li>Write a sentence using tango: something you picked up or took.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (5 sentences) about a trip to a shop or market. Use I for every sentence. Include at least three different objects using the object marker i.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Building past tense stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Sequencing past events with time words and conjunctions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Story sequencing; AO 2.4 — Time &amp; sequence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Chaining past events into a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can write individual past tense sentences. Today we chain them into a flowing story — connecting events in sequence using time words and conjunctions you already know. Watch how a story builds from simple parts.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara</a> au i te ata.</strong><br>I woke up in the morning.<br><br><strong>I kai au i tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parakuihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parakuihi</a>, ā i inu au i tōku tī.</strong><br>I ate my breakfast and I drank my tea.<br><br><strong>I te wā i mutu tōku parakuihi, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> au i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=niho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">niho</a>.</strong><br>When my breakfast was finished, I brushed my teeth.<br><br><strong>I haere au ki te kura — i tere haere au nō te mea i tata ahau ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABreiti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūreiti</a>!</strong><br>I went to school — I went quickly because I was nearly late!<br><br><strong>Ahakoa i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oma</a> au, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tae</a> tonu ahau i muri i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pirangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pirangi</a>.</strong><br>Although I ran, I still arrived after the bell.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words in this story:<br><strong>ara</strong> — wake up / rise<br><strong>parakuihi</strong> — breakfast<br><strong>horoi niho</strong> — brush teeth (horoi = wash/clean, niho = teeth)<br><strong>tūreiti</strong> — late<br><strong>tae</strong> — arrive / reach<br><strong>pirangi</strong> — bell<br><strong>i muri i</strong> — after<br><strong>tata</strong> — nearly / close to</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the conjunctions doing their jobs:<br>→ <strong>ā</strong> joining two actions in sequence<br>→ <strong>i te wā</strong> setting a time clause<br>→ <strong>nō te mea</strong> giving a reason<br>→ <strong>ahakoa</strong> showing a surprise</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>All from Term 1 — now used in a past tense story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: A good past tense story has a beginning (scene-set), a middle (sequence of events), and an end (result or reflection). Use your conjunctions to connect the parts — they are the glue.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the morning story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build a class morning story together. I give the first sentence — you add the next one, and we keep going.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: <strong>I ara au i te ata tino mutunga.</strong> (I woke up very late.) What happened next? Add a sentence with ā.</li><li>Add a when-clause: <strong>I te wā i…, i…</strong> — something that happened when something else did.</li><li>Add a reason using nō te mea — why did something happen the way it did?</li><li>Close with ahakoa — something surprising happened despite the situation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Notice how the conjunctions create tension and surprise in the story. &quot;Ahakoa i oma au, i tae tonu ahau i muri i te pirangi&quot; — the ahakoa sets up an expectation, then the main clause breaks it. How does that feel different from just saying &quot;I ran. I was late.&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell your morning story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a past tense story about your morning today — from waking up to arriving at school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write four I sentences about the sequence of what you did this morning.</li><li>Add at least one i te wā clause and one nō te mea reason.</li><li>Add one ahakoa sentence — something surprising or unexpected.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full story in eight sentences. Include: a fronted time phrase, an object marker, a conjunction, and one vivid sound word from Term 1 Week 9.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā hararei — Talking about the holidays":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Holiday recount; AO 2.4 — Past time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What did you do in the holidays?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most natural first conversation after a holiday is &quot;what did you do?&quot; Today we have that conversation entirely in te reo Māori. Listen to how the past tense flows naturally in real back-and-forth dialogue.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere au ki Ōtautahi me tōku whānau.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā mea i mahia e koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere mātou ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taiao</a>, ā i kite mātou i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> ātaahua o reira.<br><strong>A:</strong> I ngenge koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, i ngenge mātou — engari i hari hoki mātou! Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea te āhua o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangi</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=makariri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">makariri</a> engari i ātaahua hoki. I ua i tētahi rā — ahakoa i tākaro tonu mātou i waho!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words for holiday talk:<br><strong>hararei</strong> — holiday/holidays<br><strong>whānau</strong> — family<br><strong>taiao</strong> — nature / the natural world / outdoors<br><strong>manu</strong> — bird<br><strong>reira</strong> — there / that place (referring back to somewhere mentioned)<br><strong>He aha ngā mea i mahia e koutou?</strong> — What things did you (all) do?<br><strong>i mahia</strong> — was done (passive — the doing happened to the things)<br><strong>e koutou</strong> — by you all (agent marker)<br><strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> — What was the weather like?<br><strong>āhua</strong> — appearance / nature / what something is like<br><strong>Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu.</strong> — That was my best holiday ever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea ōu hararei? — How were your holidays? Learn this cold. It is the first question of every new term for the rest of your school life.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Holiday conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the holiday conversation together. I ask — you answer. Then swap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea ōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hararei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hararei</a>?</strong> You answer with at least two sentences — where you went and one thing you did.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā mea i mahia e koe?</strong> List three things using Ko ēnei or just sequencing with ā.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> Describe the weather using He + adjective.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko ēhea ngā mea pai rawa atu?</strong> (What were the best things?) Use Ko tērā or He pai rawa atu…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tērā tōku hararei pai rawa atu. — That was my best holiday ever. What makes this sentence work so well as a closing line? How does it use Ko, possession, and a superlative all in one? Could you write an equally strong closing line about something else?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu kōrero hararei</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a partner, have a full holiday conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He pēhea ōu hararei?</strong> Both partners respond in at least three sentences.</li><li>Each person shares the best thing about their holidays: <strong>Ko tērā tōku mea pai rawa atu…</strong></li><li>Describe the weather during your holiday using past tense: <strong>I… te rangi.</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up your holiday recount as a paragraph (7 sentences). Use: past tense throughout, at least two conjunctions, a Ko ēnei list, and one fronted time phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you change &quot;Kei te haere au&quot; (I am going) to past tense?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Change &quot;haere&quot; to &quot;haerea&quot; </li><li>Replace &quot;Kei te&quot; with &quot;I&quot; — I haere au ✅</li><li>Add &quot;inanahi&quot; to the end only </li><li>Change &quot;au&quot; to &quot;ahau&quot; </li></ul></li><li>Which time expression means &quot;last week&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>i tērā wiki ✅</li><li>i tērā tau </li><li>inanahi </li><li>i tēnei ata </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I hoko au i te āporo.&quot; What is the role of the lowercase &quot;i&quot; before &quot;te āporo&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is the past tense marker </li><li>It marks the object — the thing the action happened to ✅</li><li>It means &quot;at&quot; </li><li>It is part of the verb </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kite&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hear / feel </li><li>give (toward speaker) </li><li>see / find / discover ✅</li><li>take / pick up </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I ara au i te ata.&quot; What does &quot;ara&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>eat breakfast </li><li>brush teeth </li><li>wake up / rise ✅</li><li>arrive </li></ul></li><li>You want to say &quot;When breakfast was finished, I brushed my teeth.&quot; Which conjunction do you use?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ahakoa </li><li>ā </li><li>i te wā ✅</li><li>nō reira </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you ask &quot;How were your holidays?&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He aha ōu hararei? </li><li>He pēhea ōu hararei? ✅</li><li>Kei te pēhea ōu hararei? </li><li>Ko wai ōu hararei? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whānau&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>holiday </li><li>nature / outdoors </li><li>bird </li><li>family ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ka haere ia — The Ka narrative tense":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Ka (sequential/narrative tense marker)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Narrative &amp; storytelling; AO 2.4 — Sequential time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Ka: the storytelling tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te (now) and I (past). Today we meet the third tense marker — Ka. Ka is the storyteller's tense. It moves a story forward, event by event, like a chain. Listen to how it feels different from I.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Using <strong>I</strong> — reporting what happened:<br><strong>I haere ia ki te toa. I hoko ia i te āporo. I hoki ia ki te kāinga.</strong><br>She went to the shop. She bought an apple. She returned home.<br><br>Using <strong>Ka</strong> — telling it as a story:<br><strong>Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo. Ka hoki ia ki te kāinga.</strong><br>She went to the shop. She bought an apple. She went back home.<br><br>Combined — I for context, Ka for story movement:<br><strong>I te ahiahi o inanahi, ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo whero nui. Ka hoki ia ki te kāinga, ā ka kai ia i tāna āporo i te pō.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>Ka</strong>:<br>→ When telling a story and moving it forward step by step<br>→ When describing what happened in sequence (like &quot;and then… and then…&quot;)<br>→ When the action follows on naturally from what came before<br>→ In instructions: Ka tū koe, ka titiro, ka whakarongo (You stand, you look, you listen)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>I</strong>:<br>→ When reporting a single completed past event<br>→ When giving a specific past time reference (I haere au inanahi)<br>→ When negating: Kāore au i haere</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The feel: <strong>I</strong> = &quot;it happened.&quot; <strong>Ka</strong> = &quot;and then this happened, and then this…&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>i te pō</strong> — at night / in the evening</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka is the &quot;and then&quot; of te reo Māori. Once a story is underway, Ka keeps it moving. Think of it as a chain: Ka… Ka… Ka… each link connected to the last.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tell it as a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a sequence of events — you tell them as a Ka story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Events: wake up → eat breakfast → go to school → learn te reo → come home. Tell it: <strong>Ka ara ia. Ka kai ia i tōna parakuihi. Ka haere ia ki te kura…</strong></li><li>Now combine I and Ka: set the scene with I (past context), then use Ka for the story: <strong>I te Mane ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia…</strong></li><li>Try instructions with Ka: tell a partner how to make a cup of tea step by step. <strong>Ka tango koe i te tī. Ka ringihia e koe te wai…</strong> (ringihia = pour)</li><li>Now swap I and Ka in one of your sentences — how does the meaning or feel change?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka is used in traditional Māori storytelling — kōrero pūrākau — to move the narrative forward. Why do you think storytellers prefer Ka over I? What does Ka add to the feeling of a story that I does not?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell a Ka story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Ka to tell a story with at least five steps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a five-step Ka story about a character going on a journey. Each sentence starts with Ka.</li><li>Combine I and Ka: open with I te [time], then continue with Ka sentences.</li><li>Write a Ka instruction sequence: how to do something (make a snack, pack a bag, play a game).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full eight-sentence story using I for the opening scene-set and Ka for the body. Include an object marker, a conjunction, and a fronted phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Kāore — Negation in present and past":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kāore e…ana (present negative), Kāore i (past negative)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Negation; AO 2.4 — Negative tense forms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying what is NOT happening</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can say what IS happening. Now we learn how to say what is NOT. Negation in te reo Māori is completely regular — one word, Kāore, does all the work. Watch how the structure changes between present and past.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>PRESENT NEGATIVE — Kāore… e… ana:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> au <strong>e</strong> haere <strong>ana</strong>. — I am not going.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>e</strong> kai <strong>ana</strong>. — She is not eating.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ngā tamariki <strong>e</strong> tākaro <strong>ana</strong>. — The children are not playing.<br><br><strong>PAST NEGATIVE — Kāore… i:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> au <strong>i</strong> haere. — I did not go.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>i</strong> kai. — She did not eat.<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ngā tamariki <strong>i</strong> tākaro. — The children did not play.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PRESENT: <strong>Kāore</strong> + [person] + <strong>e</strong> + [verb] + <strong>ana</strong><br>PAST: <strong>Kāore</strong> + [person] + <strong>i</strong> + [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key differences from the positive:<br>→ The person comes AFTER Kāore (not after the verb)<br>→ Present adds e…ana around the verb<br>→ Past uses i (same as positive past, but person comes first)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare:<br>Positive present: Kei te haere <strong>au</strong> → person at end<br>Negative present: Kāore <strong>au</strong> e haere ana → person right after Kāore</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Positive past: I haere <strong>au</strong> → person at end<br>Negative past: Kāore <strong>au</strong> i haere → person right after Kāore</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The person always moves to right after Kāore in negatives.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kāore pushes the person forward — right after itself. Present negative wraps the verb in e…ana. Past negative uses bare i + verb. Two patterns, one rule about person position.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make it negative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a positive sentence — you make it negative. Both present and past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Positive present: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong> → Negative: <strong>Kāore au e haere ana ki te kura.</strong></li><li>Positive past: <strong>I kai ia i te tina.</strong> → Negative: <strong>Kāore ia i kai i te tina.</strong></li><li>Positive: <strong>Kei te mōhio ngā tamariki.</strong> (The children know.) → Negative: <strong>Kāore ngā tamariki e mōhio ana.</strong></li><li>Now make your own — say something true that you did NOT do yesterday using Kāore au i…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kāore is one word that handles all negation in te reo Māori. English uses &quot;not&quot;, &quot;didn't&quot;, &quot;isn't&quot;, &quot;wasn't&quot;, &quot;haven't&quot;, &quot;can't&quot; and more. How does having one negation word make te reo Māori simpler? Can you think of any disadvantages?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The honest negative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise both forms of negation — present and past.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three present negative sentences using Kāore… e… ana.</li><li>Write three past negative sentences using Kāore… i…</li><li>Write a pair: one positive and one negative about the same event (e.g. I went / I did not go).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (5 sentences) where someone explains what they did NOT do and why. Use Kāore for at least three sentences, and include a reason using nō te mea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — I, Ka, Kāore in one passage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Fluent use of multiple tense markers in connected text</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Fluent extended writing; AO 2.4 — Tense control</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three markers, one story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have I, Ka, and Kāore. Today we use all three in a single connected passage — controlling tense and negation deliberately, the way a confident te reo speaker does. Watch how the three markers each do a different job.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>I tēnei ata</strong>, ka ara au i te tino ata. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> au i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kanohi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kanohi</a> ā ka kai au i tōku parakuihi.<br>&lt;i&gt;This morning, I got up very early. I washed my face and I ate my breakfast.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br>Ka haere au ki te kura — <strong>kāore</strong> au i tūreiti i tēnei rā!<br>&lt;i&gt;I went to school — I was not late today!&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>I</strong> kite au i tōku hoa Mere i te ara. <strong>Kāore</strong> ia e haere ana ki te kura i tēnei rā nō te mea <strong>i</strong> māuiui ia.<br>&lt;i&gt;I saw my friend Mere on the path. She is not going to school today because she was sick.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Duri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōuri</a> au mōna — he hoa pai rawa atu ia.<br>&lt;i&gt;I felt sad for her — she is the very best friend.&lt;/i&gt;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>See how the three markers divide the work:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka</strong> — tells the story, moves it forward step by step (ka ara, ka horoi, ka kai, ka haere, ka pōuri)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I</strong> — reports specific completed events (i kite au, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81uiui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māuiui</a> ia)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kāore i</strong> — says something did NOT happen in the past (kāore au i tūreiti)<br><strong>Kāore e…ana</strong> — says something is NOT happening now (kāore ia e haere ana)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kanohi</strong> — face<br><strong>ara</strong> — path / road (also: wake up — same word, different context)<br><strong>māuiui</strong> — sick / unwell<br><strong>pōuri</strong> — sad / dark<br><strong>mōna</strong> — for her/him (mō = for/about, na = him/her)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Let Ka carry the story forward. Use I to anchor specific facts. Use Kāore to correct, deny, or show what did NOT happen. These three together give you full control of any narrative.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Control the tense</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a story prompt — we build it together, choosing the right marker for each sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Story: a student wakes up late, rushes to school, but still makes it on time. Which markers? Ka for the rush, Kāore i for &quot;did not miss the bell&quot;.</li><li>Story: you were going to visit a friend but they were sick. Use I for the plan, Kāore i for what didn't happen, Kāore e…ana for what is currently not happening.</li><li>Add a pōuri (sadness) sentence using Ka: <strong>Ka pōuri au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>End the story with a positive Ka sentence — something that happened instead.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at the model passage. Count how many times each marker appears: Ka, I, Kāore. Which one appears most? Why do you think Ka dominates in storytelling? What would the passage feel like if you replaced every Ka with I?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Three-marker story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses all three markers deliberately.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ka chain of at least four events — a morning sequence.</li><li>Add two I sentences — specific facts or things you saw/heard.</li><li>Add one Kāore i sentence and one Kāore e…ana sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a complete eight-sentence story that uses Ka for movement, I for facts, and Kāore for at least two negatives. Tell the story of a day where something went differently than expected.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero kōrero — A flowing conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 2.4: Past and present</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Fluent conversation; AO 1.1 — Greetings &amp; social language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A real conversation using all three tenses</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put Term 2 Week 2 together in conversation. A natural conversation moves between past and present — what happened, what is happening now, what you think. Watch how Ka, I, and Kāore all appear naturally.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! I haere koe ki hea i tērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere au ki te hākinakina. Ka tākaro mātou ā te ahiahi katoa!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I toa koutou?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore mātou i toa — i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a> ērā atu. Engari i tino pai tā mātou tākaro.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea koe ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore au e ngenge ana — kei te hari tonu au! Ka haere anō mātou ā tērā wiki.<br><strong>A:</strong> Mā wai koutou e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ārahi</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> Mā tō mātou kaiako e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ārahi</a>. He pai rawa atu ia!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases from this conversation:<br><strong>I toa koutou?</strong> — Did you (all) win?<br><strong>i toa</strong> — won (toa = win/victorious)<br><strong>ērā atu</strong> — those others / the others<br><strong>tā mātou tākaro</strong> — our (exclusive) playing / our game<br><strong>Ka haere anō mātou</strong> — We will go again<br><strong>Mā wai koutou e ārahi?</strong> — Who will lead/coach you?<br><strong>Mā… e ārahi</strong> — [Person] will lead (mā = by/through for future agent)<br><strong>ārahi</strong> — to lead / guide / coach</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Mā wai e…? = Who will do [something]? Mā [person] e… = [Person] will do it. This is how you talk about who does future actions — very useful for classroom and sports contexts.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sports or activity conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a sport or activity you enjoy. Use all three tense markers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: greet and ask where they went: <strong>I haere koe ki hea i tērā rā?</strong> Answer with a past event.</li><li>Ask about the result: <strong>I toa koutou?</strong> Answer honestly — win OR use Kāore mātou i toa.</li><li>Ask about now: <strong>He pēhea koe ināianei?</strong> Answer using Kāore e…ana or Kei te.</li><li>Ask who leads/coaches using Mā wai e ārahi? Answer with Mā [name/role] e ārahi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kāore mātou i toa — we did not win. But then: i tino pai tā mātou tākaro — our game was very good. This is gracious losing in te reo Māori. How does te ao Māori think about winning and losing? What values does this kind of language reflect?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your activity conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full conversation about a sport or activity with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask and answer about a past sports or activity event — use I and Ka.</li><li>Ask about the result — use Kāore i toa or i toa.</li><li>Ask how they feel now — use Kei te pēhea koe and Kāore e…ana or Kei te.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up the full conversation (8 lines minimum). Use every tense marker at least once. Include Mā wai e ārahi and end with a Ka haere anō sentence.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere ia ki te toa. Ka hoko ia i te āporo.&quot; What does Ka signal here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>These are future events that have not happened yet </li><li>Ka marks the past tense like I does </li><li>Ka moves the story forward — and then this happened, and then this ✅</li><li>Ka is used only for commands </li></ul></li><li>When would you use I rather than Ka for a past event?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When the event is part of a flowing story </li><li>When reporting a specific completed event or giving a time reference ✅</li><li>Ka and I mean exactly the same thing </li><li>I is only used with time words like inanahi </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kāore au e haere ana.&quot; What is the structure of this present negative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kāore + verb + person + ana </li><li>Kāore + person + e + verb + ana ✅</li><li>Kāore + e + person + verb + ana </li><li>Person + Kāore + verb + e + ana </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore ia i kai i te tina.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is not eating lunch right now </li><li>She did not eat lunch ✅</li><li>She will not eat lunch </li><li>She never eats lunch </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;māuiui&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sad </li><li>tired </li><li>sick / unwell ✅</li><li>angry </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōuri au mōna.&quot; What does mōna mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>for me </li><li>for you </li><li>for him/her ✅</li><li>for them </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Mā wai koutou e ārahi?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where are you all going? </li><li>Who will lead/coach you all? ✅</li><li>What will you all do? </li><li>How will you all get there? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;toa&quot; mean in the context of a game or competition?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>team </li><li>player </li><li>win / victorious ✅</li><li>score </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tae — Colours and detailed description":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Colour words, He structure with multiple adjectives</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Colours &amp; descriptions; AO 1.6 — Descriptive communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Painting pictures with colour</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know a few colours. Today we learn the full colour palette in te reo Māori — and something fascinating about how Māori colour words often come from nature. Listen carefully to the connections.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>whero</strong> — red (from the redness of the tōtara tree heartwood)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81k%C4%81riki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kākāriki</a></strong> — green (from the kākāriki parrot — bright green bird) (from the kākāriki parrot — bright green bird)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dwhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōwhai</a></strong> — yellow (from the kōwhai flower) (from the kōwhai flower)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kikorangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kikorangi</a></strong> — blue (kiko = flesh/colour + rangi = sky) (kiko = flesh/colour + rangi = sky)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81whero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māwhero</a></strong> — pink (mā = white + whero = red) (mā = white + whero = red)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karaka</a></strong> — orange (from the karaka tree berry) (from the karaka tree berry)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parauri</a></strong> — brown (para = residue/sediment + uri = dark) (para = residue/sediment + uri = dark)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mā</a></strong> — white<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mangu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mangu</a></strong> — black<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hina</a></strong> — grey / silver<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōura</a></strong> — gold / golden (from the kōura/crayfish shell) (from the kōura/crayfish shell)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the pattern — almost every colour word connects to something in the natural world:<br>→ kākāriki (green) = the parrot<br>→ kōwhai (yellow) = the flower<br>→ kikorangi (blue) = sky-flesh (sky colour)<br>→ karaka (orange) = the tree</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is te ao Māori — the Māori world view sees language growing from the land and its creatures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a sentence, colours follow the noun as always:<br><strong>He pōtae kākāriki tāku.</strong> — I have a green hat. (pōtae = hat)<br><strong>He pukapuka kikorangi tēnā.</strong> — That is a blue book.<br><strong>He kuri parauri tō rātou.</strong> — They have a brown dog. (kuri = dog)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And you can stack them:<br><strong>He pōtae kākāriki māmā tēnei.</strong> — This is a light green hat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Colour names in te reo come from nature — a parrot, a flower, the sky. Knowing the source helps you remember the colour. kākāriki = the bright green parrot = bright green.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Colour the room</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look around the room. I point to objects — you describe them using He + noun + colour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Your pencil case: <strong>He kete pene [tae] tāku.</strong> What colour is it? Fill in the colour.</li><li>The door: <strong>He whatitoka [tae] tērā.</strong> What colour? Add a size adjective too.</li><li>Something outside: <strong>He [noun] [tae] [tae2] tērā.</strong> Can you stack two colour/description words?</li><li>Ask a partner: <strong>He aha te tae o tōu [object]?</strong> (What colour is your [object]?) They answer with He…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Almost all te reo Māori colour words come from plants, birds, or natural phenomena. English colour words come from many different places — &quot;orange&quot; from the fruit, &quot;purple&quot; from a sea snail dye, &quot;blue&quot; from Old French. What does it tell us about a culture when its colour words come from nature?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Colour descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use colour words to describe things in detail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write five He sentences describing objects around you — each with a different colour word.</li><li>Write a description of your school bag using at least three descriptive words including a colour.</li><li>Write a He sentence using māwhero (pink) and one using kōura (gold).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a description of your favourite place using colours from nature — trees, sky, water, birds. Use at least four colour words and make your description feel like a painting.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He pai ake, he pai rawa atu — Comparatives in new contexts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 2.3: Opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: Comparative ake, superlative rawa atu in persuasion context</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Comparative descriptions; AO 2.3 — Opinions &amp; argument</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Comparing things to argue a point</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last term you learned ake (more) and rawa atu (most/best). Today we use those same tools in a new context — argument and persuasion. When you want to convince someone of something, you compare. Watch how comparison becomes opinion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He pai ake te hākinakina i te karo i.</strong><br>Sport is better than avoiding it.<br><br><strong>He ātaahua ake ngā manu o Aotearoa i ngā manu o ērā atu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whenua</a>.</strong><br>The birds of New Zealand are more beautiful than those of other countries.<br><br><strong>He nui ake ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hua</a> o te ako i te reo Māori i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uauatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uauatanga</a>.</strong><br>The benefits of learning te reo Māori are greater than the difficulties.<br><br><strong>Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is the most beautiful language in the world.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The comparison structure:<br><strong>He [adjective] ake te X i te Y.</strong><br>[X] is more [adjective] than [Y].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The i before Y means &quot;than&quot; in this comparison context — a new use of i you haven't seen before!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hākinakina</strong> — sport / physical activity<br><strong>whenua</strong> — land / country<br><strong>hua</strong> — fruit / benefit / result<br><strong>uauatanga</strong> — difficulty / the state of being hard<br><strong>te ao</strong> — the world / the realm (literally: the daylight world)<br><strong>ērā atu whenua</strong> — other countries (ērā atu = those others)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And for superlatives in comparison:<br><strong>Ko te X te [adjective] rawa atu o [group].</strong><br>X is the most [adjective] of [group].</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He [adj] ake te X i te Y = X is more [adj] than Y. The i here means &quot;than&quot; — a third job for little i (past tense, object marker, and now &quot;than&quot; in comparisons).</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make your argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two things to compare — you build a comparative sentence and defend your position.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compare: te reo Māori (te reo Māori) vs pāngarau (maths) for fun. → <strong>He ngāwari ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau ki a au.</strong></li><li>Compare: ināianei vs inanahi for weather. → Build a He ātaahua ake / He makariri ake sentence.</li><li>Use a superlative: <strong>Ko [something] te [adj] rawa atu o…</strong> Make a bold claim about something you love.</li><li>Now defend your superlative — can you add nō te mea + a reason to justify it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao. Do you agree? How would you argue against this? In what ways is comparison a form of argument? Can comparison ever be unfair?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your comparisons</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use ake and rawa atu to express and defend opinions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three He [adj] ake te X i te Y sentences — compare things you genuinely have opinions about.</li><li>Write one Ko te X te [adj] rawa atu o… sentence — a bold superlative claim.</li><li>Add a nō te mea reason to your superlative sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a five-sentence &quot;argument&quot; paragraph in te reo — make a claim, support it with two comparatives, add a superlative, and close with nō reira. Persuade your reader.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — SVA and tricky agreement":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Accurate communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Subject-verb-object alignment, plural markers ngā and rātou/mātou/tātou</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammatical accuracy; AO 2.5 — Plural agreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making singular and plural agree</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te reo Māori, making sure your nouns and pronouns agree is important for clarity. The key words are the articles — te (the, singular) and ngā (the, plural) — and the pronouns — ia (he/she, one person) vs rātou (they, three or more). Today we make sure these always line up.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SINGULAR — te and ia:</strong><br><strong>Kei te haere te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tamaiti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tamaiti</a>.</strong> — The child is going.<br><strong>Kei te haere ia.</strong> — He/she is going.<br><br><strong>PLURAL — ngā and rātou:</strong><br><strong>Kei te haere ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tamariki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tamariki</a>.</strong> — The children are going.<br><strong>Kei te haere rātou.</strong> — They (3+) are going.<br><br><strong>WATCH:</strong><br><strong>I kai te kaiako i tāna kai.</strong> — The teacher ate his/her food.<br><strong>I kai ngā kaiako i ā rātou kai.</strong> — The teachers ate their food.<br><br>Notice: tāna (his/her, singular ā) → ā rātou (their, plural ā)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The agreement chain:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>SINGULAR noun → te → ia → tāna/tōna<br>PLURAL noun → ngā → rātou/mātou/tātou → ā rātou/ō rātou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Possessives also go plural:<br><strong>tāna</strong> (his/her, ā) → <strong>ā rātou</strong> (their, ā) — the ā stays, rātou replaces na<br><strong>tōna</strong> (his/her, ō) → <strong>ō rātou</strong> (their, ō) — the ō stays, rātou replaces na</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Common mistakes to avoid:<br>✗ Kei te haere <strong>ngā tamariki</strong>. Kei te noho <strong>ia</strong>. (mixing plural noun with singular pronoun)<br>✓ Kei te haere <strong>ngā tamariki</strong>. Kei te noho <strong>rātou</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kaiako</strong> — teacher (you built this with kai- in Term 1 Week 6!)<br><strong>ā rātou</strong> — their (ā-category)<br><strong>ō rātou</strong> — their (ō-category)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ngā needs rātou. Te needs ia. Never mix them. Check every pronoun — does it match the noun it refers back to? Singular or plural?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spot and fix</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you sentences — some correct, some with agreement errors. You find the error and fix it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te ako ngā ākonga. Kei te mōhio ia.&quot; — Error? → ia should be rātou: <strong>Kei te mōhio rātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;I kai te kaiako i ō rātou kai.&quot; — Error? → One teacher (te) should have tōna: <strong>I kai te kaiako i tōna kai.</strong></li><li>&quot;Kei te haere mātou. I hoko tāna kai.&quot; — Error? → mātou is plural, tāna is singular: <strong>I hoko ā mātou kai.</strong></li><li>Now write two correct pairs yourself — one singular, one plural — showing perfect agreement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Why does agreement matter? If you say &quot;ngā tamariki… ia&quot; (the children… he/she), your listener gets confused — who are we talking about now? Agreement is the glue that keeps a listener following the right thread through your sentences.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Agreement practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that demonstrate correct singular and plural agreement.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three pairs: one sentence with te/ia, one with ngā/rātou — same verb, matching articles and pronouns.</li><li>Write a sentence about your teacher (singular) using tōna/tāna correctly.</li><li>Write a sentence about your classmates (plural, 3+) using ō rātou or ā rātou correctly.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about your class — mix singular and plural freely, but make sure every pronoun matches its noun. Check each sentence before moving to the next.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He taupatupatu — A class debate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Formal vocabulary · AO 1.6: Politeness in disagreement</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Debate &amp; persuasion; AO 1.6 — Polite disagreement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Debating in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you have learned colours, comparatives, and agreement. Today we put them all to work in a debate. Using comparatives to argue, politeness phrases to disagree, and accurate grammar to be credible — this is real te reo in action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaiwhakahaere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaiwhakahaere</a>:</strong> Ko tā tātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaupeka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaupeka</a>: He pai ake te hākinakina i te karo i.<br><strong>Taha 1:</strong> Āe, he tika tērā. He nui ake ngā hua o te hākinakina — he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauora</a>, he kaha, he hoa hou hoki.<br><strong>Taha 2:</strong> Kāo, kāore au e whakaae ana. He pai ake te noho ki te kāinga me ōu hoa tata nō te mea he mārie ake tērā.<br><strong>Taha 1:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari ko ngā hua hauora o te hākinakina he nui ake i ngā hua o te noho.<br><strong>Taha 2:</strong> He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — engari he pai tērā! Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New debate vocabulary:<br><strong>Kaiwhakahaere</strong> — chairperson / facilitator (whaka + haere = one who makes things go)<br><strong>kaupeka</strong> — topic / branch / proposition<br><strong>Taha 1 / Taha 2</strong> — Side 1 / Side 2 (taha = side)<br><strong>whakaae</strong> — agree / consent<br><strong>Kāore au e whakaae ana.</strong> — I do not agree.<br><strong>he hauora</strong> — health / wellbeing<br><strong>he hoa hou</strong> — new friends<br><strong>ko ngā hua hauora</strong> — the health benefits<br><strong>Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero.</strong> — This is the lesson of this discussion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Debate structure in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position (Ko tā tātou kaupeka…)</li><li>Support with comparatives (He nui ake ngā hua…)</li><li>Respond politely (Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari…)</li><li>Close graciously (He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — our opinions are different. This is the gracious way to end a debate in te ao Māori. Disagreement is acknowledged — but the relationship is preserved.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Mini debate</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class splits into two sides. Teacher (kaiwhakahaere) introduces the topic. Each side argues in te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaupeka: <strong>He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau.</strong> Side 1 agrees — argue why using He nui ake ngā hua…</li><li>Side 2 disagrees: <strong>Kāore mātou e whakaae ana. He pai ake te pāngarau nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Side 1 responds: <strong>Ka pai tā koutou whakaaro, engari…</strong> Add a comparative to counter.</li><li>Close: everyone together: <strong>He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tēnei te akoranga o tēnei kōrero — this is the lesson of this discussion. What IS the lesson? Not who won — but what did the discussion teach you? In te ao Māori, a debate is not about defeating the other side. What is it about?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your debate argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your side of a debate argument in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a side on: &quot;He pai ake te ako i te kāinga i te ako i te kura.&quot; Write your position statement.</li><li>Write two supporting comparatives using He [adj] ake…</li><li>Write a polite response to the other side: Ka pai tā koutou whakaaro, engari…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full six-sentence debate contribution — position, two supports, a response to opposition, a superlative claim, and a gracious close with He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which te reo Māori colour word comes from a New Zealand bird?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>kikorangi </li><li>kōwhai </li><li>kākāriki ✅</li><li>karaka </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pōtae kikorangi tāku.&quot; What colour is the hat?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>green </li><li>yellow </li><li>blue ✅</li><li>red </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai ake te reo Māori i te pāngarau.&quot; What does the &quot;i&quot; before &quot;te pāngarau&quot; mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>at </li><li>than ✅</li><li>past tense marker </li><li>object marker </li></ul></li><li>How do you say &quot;Te reo Māori is the most beautiful language in the world&quot; using a superlative?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>He ātaahua rawa atu te reo Māori i ērā atu reo </li><li>Ko te reo Māori te reo tino ātaahua rawa atu o te ao ✅</li><li>Te reo Māori he ātaahua ake i ngā reo katoa </li><li>He reo ātaahua rawa atu te reo Māori </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te ako ngā ākonga. Kei te mōhio ia.&quot; What is wrong with this passage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The verb is incorrect </li><li>ia (singular he/she) does not match ngā ākonga (plural) — should be rātou ✅</li><li>The tense marker is wrong </li><li>There is no object marker </li></ul></li><li>What is the plural ā-category possessive for &quot;their&quot; (three or more people)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tā rātou </li><li>ā rātou ✅</li><li>ō rātou </li><li>tō rātou </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;whakaae&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>disagree </li><li>argue </li><li>agree / consent ✅</li><li>debate </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He rerekē ā tātou whakaaro — he pai tērā!&quot; How is this phrase used in a debate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To declare a winner </li><li>To insult the other side </li><li>To graciously acknowledge different views and preserve the relationship ✅</li><li>To ask for more time </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Māua, tāua — Dual pronouns: we two":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships between people · Grammar Level 1–2: Dual pronouns māua/tāua, kōrua, rāua</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Relationships — dual pronouns; AO 1.6 — Inclusive &amp; exclusive we</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — We two (and why it matters which &quot;we&quot;)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>English has one word for &quot;we&quot; — it covers two people, ten people, and everyone in between, and it doesn't tell you if the person you're talking to is included. Te reo Māori has a separate word for every combination. Today we learn the &quot;we two&quot; set — and discover something beautiful about how te reo Māori thinks about relationships.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māua</a></strong> — we two (me and someone else — NOT including you) — NOT including you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāua</a></strong> — we two (me and you — INCLUDING you) — INCLUDING you)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrua</a></strong> — you two<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rāua</a></strong> — they two / those two / those two<br><br><strong>Ka haere māua ki te toa.</strong><br>We two (not you) are going to the shop.<br><br><strong>Ka haere tāua ki te toa.</strong><br>We two (you and I) are going to the shop.<br><br><strong>Kei te kōrero rāua.</strong><br>Those two are talking.<br><br><strong>He aha ā kōrua mahi?</strong><br>What are you two doing?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The inclusive/exclusive distinction:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tāua</strong> = WE TWO INCLUDING YOU → &quot;come with me&quot;<br><strong>māua</strong> = WE TWO EXCLUDING YOU → &quot;just us, not you&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This distinction exists because in te ao Māori, being included or excluded from a group matters deeply — it affects relationships, responsibilities, and mana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of it this way:<br>→ &quot;Ka haere tāua&quot; = an invitation (we're doing this TOGETHER)<br>→ &quot;Ka haere māua&quot; = an announcement (we're going — you're not part of this)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The dual pronouns at a glance:<br><strong>tāua</strong> — we two (inclusive)<br><strong>māua</strong> — we two (exclusive)<br><strong>kōrua</strong> — you two<br><strong>rāua</strong> — they two</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tāua includes the person you're talking to — it's an invitation. Māua excludes them — it's just you and a third person. When in doubt: would you invite them along? Tāua. Would you not? Māua.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Choose the right we</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you choose tāua or māua and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You and your best friend are planning a trip. You're talking to your friend about it. → <strong>tāua</strong> — they ARE included: &quot;Ka haere tāua ki te moana.&quot;</li><li>You and your mum went to the shop. You're telling your friend about it now. → <strong>māua</strong> — friend was NOT there: &quot;I haere māua ko tōku māmā ki te toa.&quot;</li><li>You're looking at two students across the room: → <strong>rāua</strong>: &quot;Kei te kōrero rāua.&quot;</li><li>You're asking two students directly what they're doing: → <strong>kōrua</strong>: &quot;He aha ā kōrua mahi?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: English speakers often find the inclusive/exclusive distinction the hardest thing about te reo Māori pronouns — because English doesn't have it at all. But many languages do — Japanese, Fijian, and many others. What does having this distinction tell us about what te ao Māori values in human relationships?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — We two in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use dual pronouns in real situations.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two sentences using tāua — invite someone to do something with you.</li><li>Write two sentences using māua — report something you and a third person did (excluding the listener).</li><li>Write one sentence about two others using rāua, and one question to two people using kōrua.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short dialogue (6 lines) between two students planning something together. Use tāua at least three times. Make the &quot;inclusive we&quot; feel warm and inviting.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Mātou, tātou — Plural pronouns: three or more":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · Grammar Level 1–2: Plural pronouns mātou/tātou, koutou, rātou</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Group relationships; AO 1.6 — Inclusive &amp; exclusive plurality</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — We many (and the same choice)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday you learned māua/tāua (we two). The same inclusive/exclusive choice applies when there are THREE OR MORE people. Meet the group pronouns.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>mātou</strong> — we (3+ people, NOT including you)<br><strong>tātou</strong> — we (3+ people, INCLUDING you)<br><strong>koutou</strong> — you all (3+ people)<br><strong>rātou</strong> — they (3+ people)<br><br><strong>Ka haere mātou ki te kura.</strong><br>We (our group, not you) are going to school.<br><br><strong>Ka haere tātou ki te kura.</strong><br>We all (including you) are going to school.<br><br><strong>Kei te ako koutou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>You all are learning te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>I tākaro rātou i waho inanahi.</strong><br>They (all those people) played outside yesterday.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pronoun set — now complete:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>SINGULAR:<br>→ au/ahau (I) · koe (you) · ia (he/she)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>DUAL:<br>→ tāua (we 2, incl.) · māua (we 2, excl.) · kōrua (you 2) · rāua (they 2)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PLURAL:<br>→ tātou (we 3+, incl.) · mātou (we 3+, excl.) · koutou (you 3+) · rātou (they 3+)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:<br>→ tā- = inclusive we<br>→ mā- = exclusive we<br>→ kou-/kō- = you (plural)<br>→ rā- = they/those</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every pronoun you will ever need — all sixteen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: tā- always means &quot;including you&quot; (tāua, tātou). mā- always means &quot;not including you&quot; (māua, mātou). The prefix tells you the relationship.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — All the pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rapid fire — I describe who is involved, you give the right pronoun.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The whole class, including the teacher, and you are all in the discussion. → <strong>tātou</strong></li><li>Your team of five played a game. You're telling someone who wasn't there. → <strong>mātou</strong></li><li>You're talking to the whole class (everyone there, 3+). → <strong>koutou</strong></li><li>Three students across the room are working together. → <strong>rātou</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tātou is one of the most beautiful words in te reo Māori — it means &quot;all of us, including you.&quot; It is an act of inclusion. How might saying tātou change the feeling of a meeting, a classroom, or a family gathering? When a rangatira says &quot;tātou katoa&quot; (all of us together) — what is the effect?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — All sixteen pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the full range of pronouns in your writing today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using each of: mātou, tātou, koutou, rātou.</li><li>Write a pair: one with mātou (excluding listener) and one with tātou (including listener) about the same event.</li><li>Write a question to the whole class using koutou.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about a class event or group activity. Use at least four different pronouns from the full set — singular, dual, and plural.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Pronouns in complex sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Pronoun consistency and reference across sentences</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Pronoun reference; AO 2.3 — Cohesive pronoun use</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Keeping track of who is who</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Now that you have all sixteen pronouns, the challenge is keeping track of them in longer passages. When you shift from talking about rāua (two people) to rātou (a group), or from tāua (we two) to tātou (all of us), the reader needs to follow. Today we practise pronoun clarity.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">UNCLEAR — who is &quot;rātou&quot; here?<br><strong>Ka haere māua ko Hana ki te kura. I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro rātou.</strong><br>We two (Hana and I) went to school. We saw our friends. They played.<br>(Who played? Just the friends? Or us too?)<br><br>CLEAR — pronoun signals the group precisely:<br><strong>Ka haere māua ko Hana ki te kura. I kite māua i ō māua hoa tokowhitu. Ka tākaro rātou katoa — ko māua ko rātou.</strong><br>Hana and I went to school. We saw our seven friends. They all played — us two and them.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tools for pronoun clarity:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>State the group first</strong>, then use the pronoun:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro <strong>rātou</strong>.&quot; — ✗ unclear<br>&quot;Ko māua ko rātou — ka tākaro <strong>tātou</strong> katoa.&quot; — ✓ clear (now = all of us)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use katoa (all) to be explicit:</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka tākaro rātou katoa.</strong> — They all played.<br><strong>Ka tākaro tātou katoa.</strong> — We all played.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Name the people when switching:</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko māua ko rātou&quot; = &quot;the two of us and them&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tokowhitu</strong> — seven (people) (toko- + whitu = counting people)<br><strong>katoa</strong> — all / everyone (you've seen this before in Tēnā koutou katoa)<br><strong>Ko māua ko rātou</strong> — us two and them</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you change who you're talking about, signal it clearly — name the group, use katoa, or restate with Ko [person] ko [person]. Never leave your listener guessing who &quot;rātou&quot; is.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Clarify the pronouns</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you an unclear passage — we improve the pronoun clarity together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unclear: &quot;Ka haere rātou. Ka noho ia.&quot; → Who is ia? → Make it clear: state the person or repeat the noun.</li><li>Unclear: &quot;I kite māua i rātou. Ka tākaro mātou.&quot; → Who is in mātou now? → Add &quot;Ko māua ko rātou&quot; to clarify.</li><li>Practice: write two sentences where you clearly shift from tāua (we two) to tātou (all of us).</li><li>Challenge: write a four-sentence story that uses four different pronouns — and make every one crystal clear.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Pronoun clarity is an act of respect for your listener. When you are vague about who you mean, your listener has to guess — and guessing takes energy and creates confusion. How does clear pronoun use connect to the value of manaaki (caring for others)?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Pronoun precision</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write passages with deliberate, clear pronoun use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a four-sentence passage about a group event — use three different pronouns clearly.</li><li>Write a pair of sentences that shift from māua to tātou — make the shift clear.</li><li>Write a sentence using Ko māua ko rātou to clarify a group.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about your class doing an activity together. Use singular, dual, and plural pronouns — make sure every pronoun is crystal clear. Read it aloud and ask a partner if they always knew who you meant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He hui ā-kāinga — A family gathering":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Family &amp; relationships; AO 1.4 — Personal &amp; family information</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about family</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's pronouns are really about relationships — who is in your group and whether you are included. Today we use them to talk about the most important group of all — whānau. Listen to how the pronouns naturally reflect the warmth and structure of a family gathering.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wh%C4%81nau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whānau</a>?<br><strong>B:</strong> He pai rātou katoa — he nui hoki ō mātou whānau!<br><strong>A:</strong> Tokohia koutou i roto i tō whānau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tokowhitu mātou — ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81tua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mātua</a>, ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tu%C4%81kana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuākana</a> tokorua, ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%93ina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tēina</a> tokotoru, ko au.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka haere koutou ki hea i ngā hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka haere tātou katoa ki tō mātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=marae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">marae</a> i ngā hararei. He tino pai — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hui</a> mātou, ka kai tātou, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waiata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waiata</a> tātou!<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua tērā! Ko wai te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kai%C4%81rahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaiārahi</a> o tō koutou whānau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku kuia — ko ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pou</a> o tō mātou whānau.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New family and relationship vocabulary:<br><strong>mātua</strong> — parents (plural of mātua/matua)<br><strong>tuākana</strong> — older sibling of the same gender<br><strong>tēina</strong> — younger sibling of the same gender<br><strong>tokorua / tokotoru / tokowha…</strong> — two / three / four (people) (toko- prefix for counting people)<br><strong>marae</strong> — marae (the communal gathering place)<br><strong>hui</strong> — gather / meeting<br><strong>waiata</strong> — sing / song<br><strong>kaiārahi</strong> — leader / guide (kai- + ārahi = person who leads)<br><strong>kuia</strong> — elder woman / grandmother<br><strong>pou</strong> — pillar / post / foundation<br><strong>Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.</strong> — She is the pillar of our family.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Toko- is the prefix for counting PEOPLE: tokorua (2), tokotoru (3), tokowha (4), torima (5), tokoono (6), tokowhitu (7), tokowaru (8), tokoriwa (9), tekau (10). Use it whenever you count people specifically.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe your whānau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a whānau conversation using this week's pronouns and new family vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Tokohia koutou i roto i tō whānau?</strong> You answer using tokorua/tokotoru etc. + list who is in your family.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ka haere koutou ki hea i ngā hararei?</strong> Answer using Ka haere tātou/mātou and a place.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai te pou o tō koutou whānau?</strong> Answer: Ko [name/role] — ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.</li><li>Ask ME a question about my family using Tokohia or He pēhea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau — she is the pillar of our family. The word pou (pillar/post) comes from the central pillar of a wharenui (meeting house). What does calling someone the pou of the family say about their role? What makes someone a pou in your whānau?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu whānau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your family using this week's pronouns and vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write who is in your family using Tokohia and Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</li><li>Write what your family does together: Ka [verb] tātou/mātou…</li><li>Write who the pou of your family is: Ko [name] te pou o tō mātou whānau nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a six-sentence paragraph about your whānau. Use: tokohia, tātou/mātou (and explain the choice), a Ka sequence of what you do together, and close with a Ko sentence about who your family's pou is.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere tāua ki te moana.&quot; Who is going to the beach?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The speaker and a third person (not the listener) </li><li>The speaker and the listener — both of them together ✅</li><li>The speaker alone </li><li>Three or more people </li></ul></li><li>You went to town with your mum. You're telling your friend (who wasn't there). Which pronoun?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tāua </li><li>tātou </li><li>māua ✅</li><li>mātou </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;tātou&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>we (3+, not including you) </li><li>you all (3+) </li><li>we (3+, including you) ✅</li><li>they (3+) </li></ul></li><li>Which prefix is always associated with an inclusive &quot;we&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>mā- </li><li>kou- </li><li>rā- </li><li>tā- ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I kite māua i ō māua hoa. Ka tākaro rātou.&quot; Why is this unclear?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The tense is wrong </li><li>Rātou is unclear — it is not certain whether māua is included in rātou or not ✅</li><li>Māua is the wrong pronoun </li><li>There is no object marker </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;katoa&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>some </li><li>none </li><li>all / everyone ✅</li><li>a few </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the toko- prefix used for?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Counting things </li><li>Counting places </li><li>Counting people ✅</li><li>Counting time </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.&quot; What does pou mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the youngest member </li><li>the leader who travels most </li><li>the pillar / foundation of the family ✅</li><li>the cook </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tekau mā tahi — Numbers 11–20":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · Grammar Level 1–2: Numbers 11–20, tekau mā pattern</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers 11–20</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Ten and one more: the tekau mā pattern</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 you learned tahi through tekau (1–10). Today we discover how te reo Māori builds numbers above ten — and it is beautifully logical. Once you know the pattern, you never need to memorise another number combination. Watch.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tekau mā tahi</strong> — 11 (ten and one)<br><strong>tekau mā rua</strong> — 12 (ten and two)<br><strong>tekau mā toru</strong> — 13<br><strong>tekau mā whā</strong> — 14<br><strong>tekau mā rima</strong> — 15<br><strong>tekau mā ono</strong> — 16<br><strong>tekau mā whitu</strong> — 17<br><strong>tekau mā waru</strong> — 18<br><strong>tekau mā iwa</strong> — 19<br><strong>rua tekau</strong> — 20 (two tens)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The pattern:<br><strong>tekau mā</strong> + [ones digit] = 11 through 19</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tekau</strong> = 10<br><strong>mā</strong> = and (joining tens and ones)<br><strong>tahi</strong> = 1 → tekau mā tahi = 11<br><strong>rua</strong> = 2 → tekau mā rua = 12<br>…and so on up to 19.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For twenty: <strong>rua tekau</strong> (two tens) — the tens digit comes FIRST.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in real contexts:<br><strong>E tekau mā rima ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga.</strong><br>There are 15 students in this classroom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tokorua tekau mā tahi ō mātou whānau.</strong><br>There are 21 people in our family.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te hoko mātou i te miraka — e tekau mā rua tāra te utu.</strong><br>We are buying milk — it costs $12.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tekau mā = &quot;ten and&quot;. Just add the ones digit after it. For twenty onward: [tens digit] + tekau. Rua tekau = 20. Toru tekau = 30. It could not be more logical.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Count together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Call and response — I say a number in English, you say it in te reo. Then we use numbers in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Numbers 11–19: rapid fire. Teacher says &quot;thirteen&quot; — students say <strong>tekau mā toru.</strong> Keep going to 19.</li><li>In a sentence: &quot;There are 14 students going.&quot; → <strong>Tokorua tekau mā whā ngā ākonga e haere ana.</strong> Wait — how many? Count who is actually going.</li><li>Price: &quot;The book costs $16.&quot; → <strong>E tekau mā ono tāra te pukapuka.</strong></li><li>Age: &quot;I am 11 years old.&quot; → <strong>Tekau mā tahi ōku tau.</strong> (tau = years/age)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The te reo Māori number system is completely regular — no &quot;eleven&quot; or &quot;twelve&quot; (irregular English words from old Germanic). Every number above ten follows the same pattern. How does a regular system help language learners? Are there any disadvantages to regular systems?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Numbers in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 11–20 in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your age in te reo using tekau mā [number] ōku tau.</li><li>Write how many students are in your class: E [number] ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga.</li><li>Write three prices using E [number] tāra te [item].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write five sentences using numbers 11–20 in different contexts — age, price, quantity, distance, time. Use E hia? to ask how many, and answer each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Rua tekau, toru tekau — Numbers 20–100":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · Grammar Level 1–2: Tens and compound numbers to 100</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers 20–100</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All the way to one hundred</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yesterday you learned 11–20. The same logic takes you all the way to 100. Te reo Māori numbers are completely regular — once you know 1–10 and the pattern, you know every number.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>rua tekau</strong> — 20<br><strong>rua tekau mā tahi</strong> — 21<br><strong>rua tekau mā rima</strong> — 25<br><strong>toru tekau</strong> — 30<br><strong>toru tekau mā whā</strong> — 34<br><strong>whā tekau</strong> — 40<br><strong>rima tekau</strong> — 50<br><strong>ono tekau</strong> — 60<br><strong>whitu tekau</strong> — 70<br><strong>waru tekau</strong> — 80<br><strong>iwa tekau</strong> — 90<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kotahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kotahi</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rau</a></strong> — 100 (one hundred) (one hundred)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pattern:<br>[tens digit] + tekau = the tens number<br>[tens digit] + tekau + mā + [ones digit] = the in-between number</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Examples:<br><strong>whā tekau</strong> = 40<br><strong>whā tekau mā rua</strong> = 42<br><strong>iwa tekau mā iwa</strong> = 99<br><strong>kotahi rau</strong> = 100</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in context:<br><strong>E iwa tekau mā rua ngā tau o tōku tīpuna.</strong><br>My ancestor is 92 years old. (tīpuna = ancestor/grandparent)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E rima tekau tāra te utu o tērā kākahu.</strong><br>That piece of clothing costs $50. (kākahu = clothing)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E toru tekau mā whā ngā ākonga i tō mātou kura.</strong><br>There are 34 students in our school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The pattern never breaks: [tens] tekau (mā [ones]). Memorise the tens (rua tekau, toru tekau… iwa tekau) and you can say any number to 99. Add kotahi rau for 100.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Numbers in real life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 20–100 in sentences about real things.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many days in a year? → <strong>E toru rau ono tekau mā rima ngā rā i tētahi tau.</strong> Wait — that's 365. Break it into hundreds: toru rau + ono tekau mā rima.</li><li>Your school: how many students? → <strong>E [number] ngā ākonga i tō mātou kura.</strong></li><li>A price: something that costs between $20 and $99. → <strong>E [number] tāra te [item].</strong></li><li>An age: how old is someone in your whānau? → <strong>E [number] ōna tau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Three hundred and sixty-five in te reo: toru rau ono tekau mā rima. Every single digit stated in order — no shortcuts. Compare to English: &quot;three hundred and sixty-five&quot; — same structure! What does this tell you about how number systems often work across languages?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Number sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use numbers 20–100 across a range of real contexts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write how old three people in your whānau are using E [number] ōna/ōna tau.</li><li>Write three prices for things you know using E [number] tāra te [item].</li><li>Write how many students are in your year group and school.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;number story&quot; — seven sentences, each using a different number between 20 and 100 in a different context (age, price, distance, quantity, score, year, temperature).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Numbers in extended sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Numbers in complex sentence contexts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers in context; AO 2.3 — Extended number sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Using numbers to tell stories</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Numbers become powerful when they appear inside rich sentences. Today we practise embedding numbers into the complex sentence structures you have built this term and last — past tense, comparatives, fronted phrases, and lists.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">With past tense:<br><strong>I haere mātou tokowhitu ki te hākinakina inanahi.</strong><br>Seven of us went to sport yesterday.<br><br>With comparison:<br><strong>He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā — e toru tekau mā rua ki e rua tekau mā whā.</strong><br>There are more students in this class than that one — 32 versus 24.<br><br>With Ko ēnei:<br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā utu: e whā tekau tāra mō te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Dtae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōtae</a>, e rua tekau mā rima tāra mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tū</a>.</strong><br>These are the prices: $40 for the hat, $25 for the shoes. (tū = shoes/footwear)<br><br>With a fronted phrase:<br><strong>I te Hātarei o tērā wiki, e toru tekau mā rua ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ngata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāngata</a> i haere mai ki tō mātou hui.</strong><br>Last Saturday, 32 people came to our meeting.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Numbers in different positions:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As SUBJECT count: <strong>E [number] ngā [noun]</strong> — there are [number] [things]<br>As PEOPLE count: <strong>Toko[number] mātou</strong> — there are [number] of us<br>In COMPARISON: <strong>e [number] ki e [number]</strong> — [number] versus [number]<br>In a LIST: <strong>e [number] tāra mō te [item]</strong> — [number] dollars for the [item]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>Hātarei</strong> — Saturday<br><strong>hui</strong> — meeting / gathering<br><strong>tū</strong> — shoes / footwear<br><strong>haere mai</strong> — come (toward the speaker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Put the number as close to the noun it counts as possible. E rua tekau ngā ākonga — 20 students. The number and the noun stay together like a pair.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Numbers in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences that embed numbers naturally in complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Past + people count: &quot;14 of us played sport yesterday.&quot; → <strong>I tākaro mātou tokorua tekau mā whā i te hākinakina inanahi.</strong></li><li>Comparison with numbers: &quot;This class has more students than that one — 28 vs 21.&quot; → <strong>He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga — e rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi.</strong></li><li>Fronted phrase + number: &quot;Last Friday, 45 people came to the school concert.&quot; → Build it step by step.</li><li>Ko ēnei list with prices: List three items and their prices using Ko ēnei ngā utu…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Numbers make claims precise. &quot;Many people came&quot; is vague. &quot;E whā tekau mā rima ngā tāngata i haere mai&quot; is exact. When does precision matter? Can being too precise ever be inappropriate in te ao Māori contexts?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Rich number sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences that use numbers inside complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a past tense sentence with a people count using toko[number].</li><li>Write a comparison sentence using two numbers and &quot;ki&quot; (versus).</li><li>Write a Ko ēnei list with prices for three items.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) about an event — a sports day, a school fair, or a family gathering. Include at least four numbers in different positions: as a count, as a price, in a comparison, and in a fronted phrase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He toa hākinakina — Sports day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number · AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Numbers in sports context; AO 2.3 — Sports event conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about sports day</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use numbers, pronouns, tenses, and comparatives in a sports day conversation. Sports is one of the richest contexts for te reo — scores, people, comparisons, and feelings all come together.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tā koutou toa hākinakina inanahi?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I toa mātou — e whā tekau mā rima ki e rua tekau mā toru ngā ira!<br><strong>A:</strong> Tokohia koutou i tākaro?<br><strong>B:</strong> Tokorua tekau mā rua mātou. I mahi kaha tātou katoa!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ko wai te kaitākaro pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku hoa ko Rangi — i eke ia i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ira</a> e tekau mā whā!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! He pai ake tēnei hua i tērā tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe — he nui ake ngā ira i tēnei tau! He tino <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=harikoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harikoa</a> mātou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New sports and achievement vocabulary:<br><strong>toa hākinakina</strong> — sports competition / tournament (toa = victorious/champion)<br><strong>ira</strong> — points / score<br><strong>eke</strong> — to score / reach / mount<br><strong>kaitākaro</strong> — player (kai- + tākaro)<br><strong>harikoa</strong> — joyful / elated / very happy<br><strong>i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=eke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eke</a> ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā</strong> — she scored 14 points (eke = scored/reached)<br><strong>hua</strong> — result / outcome (also: benefit, from Term 2 Week 3)<br><strong>i tērā tau</strong> — last year</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the score format:<br><strong>e [score1] ki e [score2] ngā ira</strong> — [score1] to [score2] points</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E [number] ki e [number] ngā ira = [number] to [number] the score. This is how you report any score in te reo Māori. Learn it cold — you will use it constantly in sports contexts.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sports day conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a sports day conversation using numbers, pronouns, and tenses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tā koutou toa hākinakina?</strong> Answer with a result — did you win? Use I toa or Kāore mātou i toa.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Tokohia koutou i tākaro?</strong> Answer using toko[number].</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te ira mutunga?</strong> (What was the final score?) Answer: E [number] ki e [number] ngā ira.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai te kaitākaro pai rawa atu?</strong> Name someone and say what they did: i eke ia i ngā ira e [number].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I eke ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā — she scored 14 points. The word &quot;eke&quot; also means &quot;to mount&quot; or &quot;to ride&quot; — as in riding a wave or climbing a hill. Why might te reo Māori use the same word for scoring points as for riding a wave? What does that connection suggest about achievement?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your sports day report</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sports day report in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the result: I toa/Kāore i toa + E [score] ki e [score] ngā ira.</li><li>Write how many players: Toko[number] mātou i tākaro.</li><li>Write who the best player was: Ko [name] te kaitākaro pai rawa atu — i eke ia i ngā ira e [number].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full sports report (7 sentences) — result, score, player count, best player, atmosphere (harikoa/pōuri), and a comparison with last year. Use at least five different numbers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;17&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tekau mā ono </li><li>tekau mā whitu ✅</li><li>tekau whitu </li><li>whitu tekau </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tekau mā rima ōku tau.&quot; What does this sentence tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>There are 15 things </li><li>I am 15 years old ✅</li><li>The price is $15 </li><li>There are 15 people </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you say &quot;43&quot; in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>toru tekau mā whā </li><li>whā tekau mā toru ✅</li><li>toru whā tekau </li><li>whā tekau toru </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;kotahi rau&quot; in English?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ten </li><li>fifty </li><li>one hundred ✅</li><li>one thousand </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi ngā ira.&quot; What does this describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A comparison of prices </li><li>A score of 28 to 21 ✅</li><li>The number of students in two classes </li><li>Ages of two people </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tokorua tekau mā rua mātou i tākaro.&quot; What does toko- before a number indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The number refers to things </li><li>The number refers to people ✅</li><li>The number is approximate </li><li>The number is a price </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I eke ia i ngā ira e tekau mā whā.&quot; What happened?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She missed 14 points </li><li>She scored 14 points ✅</li><li>She played for 14 minutes </li><li>She won by 14 points </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;harikoa&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired after sport </li><li>sad about losing </li><li>joyful / elated / very happy ✅</li><li>competitive </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Wā, rā, marama, tau — Days, months, and years":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Days, months, dates · AO 2.4: Time · Grammar Level 1–2: Days of the week, months of the year, date expressions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Days, months &amp; dates; AO 2.4 — Time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — When? The days, months, and years</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>AO 1.3 asks us to communicate about number, days, months, and dates. Today we complete that picture — you already have numbers to 100. Now we add the days of the week, months of the year, and how to say a date.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>DAYS OF THE WEEK:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81hina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāhina</a></strong> — Monday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81t%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rātū</a></strong> — Tuesday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81apa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāapa</a></strong> — Wednesday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81pare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāpare</a></strong> — Thursday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81mere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāmere</a></strong> — Friday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rāhoroi</a></strong> — Saturday<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=R%C4%81tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rātapu</a></strong> — Sunday<br><br><strong>MONTHS:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kohit%C4%81tea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kohitātea</a></strong> — January · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Huitanguru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Huitanguru</a></strong> — February · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Pout%C5%ABterangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Poutūterangi</a></strong> — March<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Paengawh%C4%81wh%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paengawhāwhā</a></strong> — April · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Haratua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Haratua</a></strong> — May · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Pipiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pipiri</a></strong> — June<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=H%C5%8Dngongoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hōngongoi</a></strong> — July · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Hereturik%C5%8Dk%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hereturikōkā</a></strong> — August · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Mahuru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mahuru</a></strong> — September<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whiringa-%C4%81-nuku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whiringa-ā-nuku</a></strong> — October · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whiringa-%C4%81-rangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whiringa-ā-rangi</a></strong> — November · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Hakihea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hakihea</a></strong> — December</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>How to say a date:<br><strong>I te [number] o [month], [year]</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>E.g. &quot;On the 14th of March, 2024&quot;:<br><strong>I te tekau mā whā o Poutūterangi, rua mano rua tekau mā whā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Simpler date questions:<br><strong>Ko tēhea rā tēnei?</strong> — What day is today?<br><strong>Ko Rāhina tēnei.</strong> — Today is Monday.<br><strong>Ko tēhea marama tēnei?</strong> — What month is this?<br><strong>Ko Pipiri tēnei.</strong> — This is June.<br><strong>Ko tēhea tau tēnei?</strong> — What year is this?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Days of the week come from:<br>Rā- (sun/day) + the number of the day (Rāhina = Moon-day, Rātū = Tuesday…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tēhea rā/marama/tau tēnei? = What day/month/year is this? Ko [answer] tēnei. = This is [answer]. These two sentences handle almost every date question you will ever need.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Days, months, dates</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Answer date questions and use them in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Ko tēhea rā tēnei?</strong> Answer with today's day.</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko tēhea marama tēnei?</strong> Answer with this month.</li><li>Use a day in a sentence: &quot;Last Monday I went to…&quot; → <strong>I te Rāhina o tērā wiki, i haere au ki…</strong></li><li>Say today's full date: <strong>Ko te [number] o [month] tēnei rā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The Māori month names are not translations of English months — they come from the traditional Māori lunar calendar and describe natural events: Pipiri (June) = the fetal position of animals huddling for warmth; Mahuru (September) = the earth becomes warm. What does this tell you about how Māori understood time and nature?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Dates in context</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use days, months, and dates in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write today's full date: Ko te [number] o [month], [year] tēnei rā.</li><li>Write what day your birthday falls on this year.</li><li>Write three sentences using different days of the week in past tense: I te Rāhina, i te Rātū…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a weekly diary — one sentence for each day of the school week (Rāhina to Rāmere) about what happened or what will happen. Use past tense for days gone, Ka for future days.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha te wā? — Telling the time":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Time · AO 2.4: Time expressions · Grammar Level 1–2: Clock time, He aha te wā?</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Clock time; AO 2.4 — Daily time expressions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What time is it?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know how to say dates. Now let's tell the time. Te reo Māori time-telling uses a simple structure that relies on your numbers — another reason why knowing your numbers to 60 is so useful.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wā</a>?</strong> — What time is it?<br><br><strong>Ko te [hour] <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karaka</a>.</strong> — It is [hour] o'clock.<br><strong>Ko te toru karaka.</strong> — It is 3 o'clock.<br><br><strong>Ko te whā karaka mā rua tekau.</strong> — It is 4:20.<br>(hour + karaka + mā + minutes)<br><br><strong>Ko te iwa karaka māwhā tekau mā rima.</strong> — It is 9:45.<br><br><strong>Ā te [hour] karaka.</strong> — At [hour] o'clock. (for scheduling)<br><strong>Ā te rima karaka ka tīmata te hākinakina.</strong><br>At 5 o'clock sport starts.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The structure:<br><strong>Ko te [hour] karaka</strong> = It is [hour] o'clock<br><strong>Ko te [hour] karaka mā [minutes]</strong> = It is [hour]:[minutes]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>karaka</strong> = clock (from English &quot;clock&quot; → karaka)<br><strong>mā</strong> = and (joining hours and minutes — same mā as in tekau mā tahi)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Time of day expressions:<br><strong>i te ata</strong> — in the morning / AM<br><strong>i te ahiahi</strong> — in the afternoon / evening<br><strong>i te pō</strong> — at night</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full time with AM/PM:<br><strong>Ko te tekau karaka i te ata.</strong> — It is 10 AM.<br><strong>Ko te rua karaka i te ahiahi.</strong> — It is 2 PM.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te [hour] karaka mā [minutes]. Just put the hour, say karaka (clock), add mā (and), then the minutes. Exactly like tekau mā tahi — but for time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — What time is it?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I show you a time — you say it in te reo. Then use it in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>3:00 PM → <strong>Ko te toru karaka i te ahiahi.</strong></li><li>8:30 AM → <strong>Ko te waru karaka mā toru tekau i te ata.</strong></li><li>Scheduling: &quot;School starts at 9 AM.&quot; → <strong>Ā te iwa karaka i te ata ka tīmata te kura.</strong></li><li>Ask and answer: <strong>He aha te wā?</strong> → <strong>Ko te [current time] karaka.</strong> What time is it right now?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Te reo Māori uses &quot;karaka&quot; (from English &quot;clock&quot;) for time-telling. This is a loanword — a word borrowed from another language. Why do languages borrow words? Can you think of other loanwords in te reo Māori? In English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your daily timetable</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your school day timetable in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what time school starts and finishes: Ā te [time] karaka ka tīmata/mutu te kura.</li><li>Write three things you do at specific times today using Ā te [time] karaka ka…</li><li>Ask a partner: He aha te wā i tīmata ai tō kura? (What time did your school start?) They answer.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full school day as a timetable — every lesson with its start time, using Ā te [time] karaka ka [subject/activity]. At least six time entries.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Dates, times, and events in full sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Number/date/time · AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Dates &amp; times in context; AO 2.3 — Scheduling &amp; events</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Invitations, events, and schedules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dates and times become truly useful when you can use them to make plans, describe events, and write schedules. Today we combine everything from this week — days, months, times, numbers — into the kinds of sentences you would use for a real school event.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He hui tēnei ā te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā rima o Hōngongoi, ā te whitu karaka i te ahiahi.</strong><br>There is a meeting this Thursday, the 15th of July, at 7 PM.<br><br><strong>Ka tīmata te hākinakina ā te Rāhoroi i te iwa karaka i te ata.</strong><br>Sport starts on Saturday at 9 AM.<br><br><strong>I haere mātou ki te hui ā te Rāhina. Tokowhitu mātou. Ka tīmata ā te rua tekau mā rua karaka, ā ka mutu ā te toru karaka i te ahiahi.</strong><br>We went to the meeting on Monday. There were seven of us. It started at 12:22 and finished at 3 PM.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Building event sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[WHAT] + [WHEN: day] + [WHEN: time] + [WHEN: date]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key connectors for scheduling:<br><strong>ā te [day]</strong> — on [day] (upcoming)<br><strong>i te [day]</strong> — on [day] (past)<br><strong>ā te [time] karaka</strong> — at [time] o'clock<br><strong>ka tīmata</strong> — starts / will start<br><strong>ka mutu</strong> — finishes / will end<br><strong>mō te [duration]</strong> — for [duration]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hui</strong> — meeting / gathering (you've seen this)<br><strong>duration: mō ngā haora e [number]</strong> — for [number] hours (haora = hour, from English)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Build event sentences in layers: what → what day → what time → how many people → what happened. Each layer adds precision. The listener always knows exactly what, when, and who.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Plan an event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we plan and describe a school event in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name the event and day: <strong>He [event] ā te [day].</strong> What is happening this week at school?</li><li>Add the date and time: <strong>Ko te [number] o [month]. Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li><li>Add how many people: <strong>Toko[number] mātou/tātou.</strong></li><li>Describe what will happen: Use Ka sentences to sequence the events of the evening.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I te iwa karaka i te ata ka tīmata te kura. Does your school really start at 9? What time does it start in te reo? The act of putting your real life into te reo Māori — your actual school time, your actual class size — makes the language genuinely yours. Why does that matter?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Design your event</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan and write about a real or imagined school event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what the event is and what day it is: He [event] ā te [day], ko te [date] o [month].</li><li>Write when it starts and finishes: Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka, ka mutu ā te [time] karaka.</li><li>Write how many people will be there and what will happen: Ka [verb] tātou…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full event invitation (7 sentences) for a real school event — include the event name, day, full date, start and end time, how many people, what will happen, and who will lead (Mā wai e ārahi).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He pānui — Reading and giving announcements":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.3: Date/time · AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.3 — Announcements with dates/times; AO 1.7 — Classroom &amp; school language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making and understanding announcements</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use dates, times, and events in the most practical way possible — making real school announcements. This is AO 1.7 (classroom language) meeting AO 1.3 (dates and numbers) in real action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br>Greetings to you all.<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81nui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pānui</a> āku mōu katoa.</strong><br>I have an announcement for you all. (pānui = announcement/notice/reading)<br><br><strong>Ā te Rāpare, ko te rua tekau o Pipiri, ka tū he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hui</a> mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81tua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mātua</a> i te kura.</strong><br>On Thursday the 20th of June, there will be a parent meeting at school.<br><br><strong>Ka tīmata ā te whitu karaka i te ahiahi, ka mutu ā te iwa karaka.</strong><br>It starts at 7 PM and finishes at 9 PM.<br><br><strong>E tū ana i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whare\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whare</a> hui. Haere mai ngā mātua katoa!</strong><br>It is being held in the meeting room. All parents come!<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Announcement vocabulary:<br><strong>pānui</strong> — announcement / notice (also: to read — same word!)<br><strong>He pānui āku mōu.</strong> — I have an announcement for you.<br><strong>ka tū</strong> — will be held / will stand (tū = stand)<br><strong>E tū ana</strong> — is being held (ongoing location)<br><strong>whare hui</strong> — meeting room / hall<br><strong>Haere mai!</strong> — Come! (invitation toward the speaker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The announcement structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet: Tēnā koutou katoa</li><li>Signal: He pānui āku</li><li>Event + day + date</li><li>Time (start and end)</li><li>Location</li><li>Call to action: Haere mai!</li><li>Close: Tēnā koutou (×3)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pānui āku mōu katoa — I have an announcement for you all. This is your attention-getting phrase. Say it with confidence and the class will listen.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Make an announcement</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write and perform a class announcement for a real upcoming school event.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: Everyone together — <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Signal: <strong>He pānui āku.</strong></li><li>Name the event and when: <strong>Ā te [day], ko te [date] o [month], ka tū he [event].</strong></li><li>Time: <strong>Ka tīmata ā te [time] karaka, ka mutu ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li><li>Location + call: <strong>E tū ana i te [place]. Haere mai [who]!</strong> Then close with Tēnā koutou ×3.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Making an announcement in te reo Māori to your class — even a simple one — is a real act of language use. How does it feel different from just writing sentences in an exercise? What does it add to do something with language that has a real purpose and audience?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your announcement</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and perform your own school announcement in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a real upcoming school event (or invent one).</li><li>Write the full announcement following the seven-step structure.</li><li>Practise delivering it — aim to say it without looking at the page.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Deliver your announcement to the class. The class listens and, without looking at a text, writes down the key details: event, day, date, time, location. Check for accuracy.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Wednesday in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Rāhina </li><li>Rāapa ✅</li><li>Rātū </li><li>Rāmere </li></ul></li><li>Which month is &quot;Pipiri&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>March </li><li>April </li><li>June ✅</li><li>July </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te whā karaka mā rua tekau.&quot; What time is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>4:02 </li><li>4:12 </li><li>4:20 ✅</li><li>24:00 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ā te rima karaka ka tīmata te hākinakina.&quot; What does this sentence tell us?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Sport finished at 5 o'clock </li><li>Sport starts at 5 o'clock ✅</li><li>There are 5 players </li><li>The game lasted 5 hours </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tīmata ā te rua tekau mā rua karaka, ka mutu ā te toru karaka.&quot; When does the event end?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>At 2:22 </li><li>At 3:00 ✅</li><li>At 22:03 </li><li>At 12:22 </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;haora&quot; a loanword for?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>minute </li><li>second </li><li>hour ✅</li><li>day </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pānui āku mōu katoa.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Asking a question </li><li>Saying goodbye </li><li>Giving an announcement ✅</li><li>Introducing themselves </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Haere mai!&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Go away! </li><li>Come here! (invitation toward speaker) ✅</li><li>Be quiet! </li><li>Sit down! </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He pēhea te āhua o te rangi? — Weather and seasons":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time, weather and seasons · Grammar Level 1–2: Weather expressions, Ka for weather narrative</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Weather &amp; seasons; AO 1.5 — Environmental description</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the weather</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather is one of the most universal conversation topics. In te reo Māori, weather expressions are beautifully descriptive and often poetic — reflecting the deep connection between Māori culture and the natural environment. Today we build a full weather vocabulary.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WEATHER CONDITIONS:</strong><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ua</a>.</strong> — It is raining. (ua = rain)<br><strong>He hau.</strong> — It is windy. (hau = wind)<br><strong>He rā pai.</strong> — It is a fine day.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kapua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kapua</a>.</strong> — It is cloudy. (kapua = cloud)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hukarere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hukarere</a>.</strong> — It is snowing. (hukarere = snow)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81niwaniwa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āniwaniwa</a>.</strong> — There is a rainbow. (āniwaniwa = rainbow)<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uira</a>.</strong> — There is lightning.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatitiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatitiri</a>.</strong> — There is thunder.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kohu</a>.</strong> — There is fog/mist.<br><br><strong>TEMPERATURE:</strong><br><strong>He makariri.</strong> — It is cold.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahana</a>.</strong> — It is warm.<br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wera\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wera</a>.</strong> — It is hot.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about weather:<br><strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?</strong> — What is the weather like?<br><strong>He pēhea te rangi?</strong> — How is the weather? (shorter form)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather in the past:<br><strong>I pēhea te āhua o te rangi inanahi?</strong><br>What was the weather like yesterday?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather with Ka (forecasting or narrative):<br><strong>Ka ua āpōpō.</strong> — It will rain tomorrow.<br><strong>Ka mahana te rangi ā tērā wiki.</strong> — The weather will be warm next week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seasons:<br><strong>kōanga</strong> — spring<br><strong>raumati</strong> — summer<br><strong>hōtoke</strong> — winter<br><strong>kārearea</strong> — NOT a season — it is a falcon! The season is...<br><strong>kōanga, raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke</strong> — spring, summer, autumn, winter</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He [weather word] for right now. I [weather word] te rangi for past. Ka [weather word] te rangi for future. Same pattern as any other tense — just weather vocabulary.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Weather report</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we create a week-long weather report for an imaginary or real location.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Today's weather: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi ināianei?</strong> → <strong>He [weather] te rangi.</strong></li><li>Yesterday's weather: <strong>I pēhea te āhua o te rangi inanahi?</strong> → <strong>I [weather] te rangi.</strong></li><li>Tomorrow's forecast: <strong>Ka pēhea te āhua o te rangi āpōpō?</strong> → <strong>Ka [weather] te rangi āpōpō.</strong></li><li>Compare two days: <strong>He [adj] ake te rangi o tēnei rā i tērā rā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The word āniwaniwa (rainbow) contains the word rangi (sky). Hukarere (snow) contains huka (foam/froth) + rere (to flow/fall). Many Māori weather words describe what is happening visually and physically. How does this way of naming things differ from English weather words like &quot;fog&quot; or &quot;snow&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Weather sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use weather vocabulary across past, present, and future.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write today's weather report: He pēhea te āhua o te rangi ināianei? Answer with three details.</li><li>Write yesterday's weather using past tense: I pēhea…? Use at least two weather words.</li><li>Write a three-day forecast using Ka: Ka [weather] te rangi ā te [day].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a weather comparison paragraph — compare this week's weather to last week's using He [adj] ake and fronted time phrases. Include at least five different weather words.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā āhuatanga o te taiao — Seasons and the natural world":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Seasons · AO 2.5: Natural world vocabulary · AO 1.4: Connection to land</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Seasons &amp; nature; AO 2.5 — Natural world vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The seasons and what they bring</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te ao Māori, the seasons are not just weather patterns — they mark the rhythms of planting, harvest, fishing, and ceremony. Today we learn how to describe each season and what happens in it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Danga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōanga</a> te wā o te tipu.</strong><br>Spring is the time of growth. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> = grow/growth)<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raumati\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raumati</a> te wā o te wera me te tākaro.</strong><br>Summer is the time of heat and play.<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngahuru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngahuru</a> te wā o te hauhake.</strong><br>Autumn is the time of harvest. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauhake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauhake</a> = harvest)<br><br><strong>Ko <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dtoke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōtoke</a> te wā o te makariri me te okioki.</strong><br>Winter is the time of cold and rest.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season vocabulary:<br><strong>kōanga</strong> — spring<br><strong>raumati</strong> — summer<br><strong>ngahuru</strong> — autumn<br><strong>hōtoke</strong> — winter</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko [season] te wā o te [noun/activity].</strong><br>[Season] is the time of [noun/activity].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nature vocabulary:<br><strong>tipu</strong> — grow / plant / growth<br><strong>hauhake</strong> — harvest<br><strong>okioki</strong> — rest<br><strong>hua rākau</strong> — fruit (hua = fruit/benefit, rākau = tree)<br><strong>puāwai</strong> — blossom / flower / bloom<br><strong>rau</strong> — leaf / leaves<br><strong>repo</strong> — mud / swamp<br><strong>huka</strong> — frost / foam<br><strong>ngahere</strong> — forest / bush<br><strong>moana</strong> — sea / ocean / large lake</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko [season] te wā o te [activity]. This sentence works for ANY season and ANY activity — learn the frame, swap in the vocabulary.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Seasons and activities</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build descriptions of each season and what people do in them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe kōanga: <strong>Ko kōanga te wā o te tipu. Ka puāwai ngā gete, ka hāpai te ngahere i ōna rau.</strong></li><li>Describe raumati: <strong>Ko raumati te wā o te wera. Ka kaukau ngā tamariki i te moana.</strong></li><li>Describe hōtoke: <strong>Ko hōtoke te wā o te makariri. Ka huka te whenua i te ata.</strong></li><li>Which season is your favourite? <strong>Ko tōku wā pai rawa atu ko [season] nō te mea…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko hōtoke te wā o te okioki — winter is the time of rest. In te ao Māori, rest and conservation are values, not just necessities. How does framing winter as &quot;the time of rest&quot; change how you think about that season? What value does it give to rest?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Season descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the seasons using Ko [season] te wā o te… and nature vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko [season] te wā o te… sentence for all four seasons.</li><li>Write what you do in each season: Ko raumati tōku wā pai rawa atu. Ka [activity] au.</li><li>Write a sentence about nature in two seasons using rau (leaves), puāwai (blossom), huka (frost).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a poem or descriptive paragraph about your favourite season. Use at least six nature words, Ko [season] te wā o te… as your opening line, and at least one comparative (He [adj] ake te [season] i…).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Weather in stories and recounts":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Weather/seasons · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Weather as narrative context-setter</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Weather as scene-setting; AO 2.3 — Weather in recounts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Weather as the opening of a story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Great writers use weather to set the scene — the opening line of a story often describes the weather because it tells us the mood before anything happens. Today we learn how to use weather as a powerful scene-setter in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">WEATHER OPENING — sets the mood immediately:<br><strong>He pō makariri, he pō <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dpeka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōpeka</a>.</strong><br>It was a cold night, a dark night.<br><br><strong>I te ata o Rāhina, ka ua kaha — ka tangi hoki te hau i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a>.</strong><br>On Monday morning, it rained hard — and the wind cried through the trees.<br><br><strong>He rā pai, he rā ātaahua — ko te raumati tonu.</strong><br>It was a fine day, a beautiful day — it was truly summer.<br><br><strong>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngaro</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rā</a> i roto i ngā kapua makariri.</strong><br>The sun disappeared into the cold clouds.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weather as scene-setting techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Double description</strong> (two weather words for emphasis):</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;He pō makariri, he pō kōpeka.&quot; (cold night, dark night)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Weather + effect on nature</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka ua kaha — ka tangi hoki te hau i ngā rākau.&quot;<br>(It rained hard — and the wind cried through the trees)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Weather + contrast</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ahakoa he ua, he ātaahua tonu te rangi.&quot;<br>(Although it rained, the sky was still beautiful)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>kōpeka</strong> — dark / gloomy<br><strong>i ngaro</strong> — disappeared / was lost<br><strong>ka tangi te hau</strong> — the wind cried/wailed (personification!)<br><strong>ngahere</strong> — forest / bush</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Open your story with weather to set the mood before a single character appears. Warm weather = possibility. Cold dark weather = difficulty ahead. Your reader will feel it before they know it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Weather openings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a story mood — you write a weather opening that creates that mood.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mood: excitement and adventure → <strong>He rā pai, he rā ātaahua — ko te raumati tonu. Ka whiti te rā i ngā maunga.</strong></li><li>Mood: sadness or difficulty → <strong>He pō makariri, he pō kōpeka. I ngaro te marama i roto i ngā kapua.</strong></li><li>Mood: warmth and safety → Use mahana, ātaahua, and whiti (shine) to create the feeling.</li><li>Now write your own weather opening for a story about something that happened to you.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau — the wind cried in the trees. This is personification — giving the wind a human emotion. Te reo Māori naturally personifies nature. How does this connect to the Māori understanding of the natural world as a living, feeling entity rather than a backdrop?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Story openings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write three different story openings — each one starting with weather.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a weather opening for a story about a storm using pakū, hau, ua, and kapua.</li><li>Write a weather opening for a summer adventure using mahana, whiti, ātaahua, and moana.</li><li>Write a weather opening for a sad or difficult story using makariri, kōpeka, and i ngaro.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take your best weather opening and write the next two sentences — what happens after you set the scene? Include a character, a Ka action sentence, and at least one conjunction.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te taiao — Nature conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Weather/seasons · AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.5: Natural world vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Nature &amp; seasons; AO 1.4 — Connection to land &amp; environment</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the natural world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week has been about weather, seasons, and nature. Today we bring it all together in a conversation about the natural world around us — connecting to the land in the way te ao Māori teaches us to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i tēnei wiki?<br><strong>B:</strong> He makariri, he ua hoki — he hōtoke tonu tēnei!<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> He pai ki a au te hōtoke nō te mea ka okioki au, ka noho au i roto me tōku whānau.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kei hea koe i noho ai i ngā hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> I noho mātou i te taha o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moana</a> — i ātaahua te rangi, ahakoa i makariri.<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua! He pēhea te āhua o te moana i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I nui, i parauri, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81oro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngāoro</a> hoki — ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversational phrases about nature:<br><strong>He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?</strong><br>What do you like about this time of year?<br><strong>i pai ai</strong> — that you like (pai + ai = relative clause &quot;that is good/liked&quot;)<br><strong>wā o te tau</strong> — time of year<br><strong>te taha o te moana</strong> — the side of the ocean / by the sea<br><strong>i ērā rā</strong> — in those days / during that time<br><strong>parauri</strong> — brown (you learned this in Week 3!) — used for ocean colour<br><strong>i ngāoro</strong> — roared (sound word from Term 1 Week 9!)<br><strong>Ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga.</strong><br>I felt sad about going home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau? — What do you like about this time of year? This question is a beautiful invitation to talk about nature and seasons. Learn it and ask it often.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Nature conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about the natural world and seasons.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i tēnei wiki?</strong> Answer with this week's actual weather.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu i pai ai mō tēnei wā o te tau?</strong> Answer with your favourite season and why.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te wāhi āhua rawa atu i Aotearoa ki a koe?</strong> (What is the most beautiful place in NZ for you?) Answer using Ko and description.</li><li>Close: <strong>He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa.</strong> (The natural environment of NZ is a treasure.) Do you agree? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa — the natural environment of New Zealand is a treasure. What responsibilities come with living in a taonga? In te ao Māori, kaitiakitanga (guardianship) means caring for the natural world as a duty. What does kaitiaki mean for you personally?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu wāhi āhua</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your favourite place in nature.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite natural place and say why: Ko tōku wāhi āhua rawa atu ko [place] nō te mea…</li><li>Describe the weather there using at least three weather words.</li><li>Write what you do there using Ka sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full description of your favourite natural place (7 sentences) — name it, describe the weather and seasons there, what you do there, why you love it. Include a Ko ēnei list of its best features and close with He taonga tēnei nō te mea…</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pēhea te āhua o te rangi?&quot; — What is this question asking?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>What time is it? </li><li>What is the weather like? ✅</li><li>What season is it? </li><li>How cold is it? </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;hukarere&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>fog / mist </li><li>lightning </li><li>snow ✅</li><li>cloud </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ngahuru te wā o te hauhake.&quot; What does hauhake mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>growth </li><li>rest </li><li>harvest ✅</li><li>rain </li></ul></li><li>What are the four seasons in te reo Māori in order from spring?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hōtoke, kōanga, raumati, ngahuru </li><li>kōanga, raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke ✅</li><li>raumati, ngahuru, hōtoke, kōanga </li><li>ngahuru, hōtoke, kōanga, raumati </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau.&quot; What literary device is being used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>simile — comparing wind to something </li><li>metaphor — replacing one word with another </li><li>personification — giving the wind a human emotion ✅</li><li>alliteration — repeated sounds </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;kōpeka&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>cloudy </li><li>windy </li><li>rainy </li><li>dark / gloomy ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He taonga te taiao o Aotearoa.&quot; What does taonga mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>problem </li><li>weather </li><li>treasure / something precious ✅</li><li>danger </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōuri au i te hoki ki te kāinga.&quot; What does pōuri mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>happy </li><li>excited </li><li>tired </li><li>sad / heavy-hearted ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā tūāhua o te hinengaro — Feelings and emotions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics, personality and feelings · Grammar Level 1–2: Kei te/He for emotions, feeling vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Feelings &amp; emotions; AO 2.3 — Expressing inner states</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying how you feel inside</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You already know a few feeling words — pai, ngenge, hari, pōuri. Today we build a full emotional vocabulary. Being able to express how you feel in te reo Māori is one of the most intimate and important things you can do in a language.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te hari au.</strong> — I am happy / joyful.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Duri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōuri</a> au.</strong> — I am sad.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mataku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mataku</a> au.</strong> — I am scared / frightened.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> au.</strong> — I am brave / bold.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamā</a> au.</strong> — I am embarrassed / shy.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%ABh%C4%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hīhī</a> au.</strong> — I am excited / eager.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=riri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">riri</a> au.</strong> — I am angry.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81harahara\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māharahara</a> au.</strong> — I am worried / anxious.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> au.</strong> — I am surprised / startled.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dh%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōhā</a> au.</strong> — I am bored / fed up.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a> au.</strong> — I am feeling love / compassion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Emotion structure:<br><strong>Kei te [emotion] au.</strong> — I am feeling [emotion].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a reason:<br><strong>Kei te pōuri au nō te mea…</strong> — I am sad because…<br><strong>Kei te hari au nā te mea…</strong> — I am happy because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about feelings:<br><strong>Kei te pēhea tōu ngākau?</strong> — How is your heart/feeling?<br><strong>He aha tōu rongo?</strong> — What do you feel? (rongo = feeling/sense)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New concept: <strong>ngākau</strong> = heart (as the seat of emotion, not the physical heart which is manawa)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deep emotion phrase:<br><strong>Ka aroha au ki a koe.</strong> — I feel love/compassion toward you.<br><strong>Aroha</strong> — love, compassion, empathy (one of the most important words in te reo Māori)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te [emotion] au = I am feeling [emotion]. Add nō te mea + reason to explain why. The emotion word goes right after Kei te — just like any other verb.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Express the feeling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you say how you would feel using Kei te [emotion] au.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You just found out you won a prize. → <strong>Kei te hari au! Kei te ohorere hoki au!</strong></li><li>You have a big test tomorrow. → <strong>Kei te māharahara au mō āpōpō.</strong></li><li>Someone said something unkind to you. → <strong>Kei te pōuri au. Kei te riri hoki au.</strong></li><li>You are speaking te reo Māori in front of the class for the first time. → How do you feel? Be honest — use two emotion words.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kei te aroha au ki a koe — I feel love/compassion toward you. The word aroha appears everywhere in te ao Māori — in greetings, farewells, and daily life. How is aroha different from just &quot;love&quot; in English? What does it include that &quot;love&quot; sometimes doesn't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Feelings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express real emotions in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write how you feel right now using Kei te [emotion] au and give a reason.</li><li>Write how you felt inanahi — use past tense: I [emotion] au nō te mea…</li><li>Write three different emotions you have felt this week using three different emotion words.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;feelings journal entry&quot; for today (6 sentences). Describe two emotions you have felt, why, how they changed during the day, and how you feel now. Use Kei te, I, and nō te mea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ahua tangata — Personality and character":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Personality · Grammar Level 1–2: He for character description, ko ia te tangata…</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Character &amp; personality; AO 2.1 — Describing relationships</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing who someone is</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last term you learned He + noun + adjective for describing things. Today we use that same structure to describe people's character — who they are as people, not just what they look like.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He tangata āpōpō ia.</strong> — She is a generous person. (āpōpō here = generous? No...)<br><br>Let's build the vocabulary first:<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atawhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atawhai</a> ia.</strong> — He is a kind/caring person./caring person. (atawhai = kind)<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> ia.</strong> — She is a brave person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dhio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōhio</a> ia.</strong> — He is a knowledgeable/clever person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%ABm%C4%81rie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hūmārie</a> ia.</strong> — She is a gentle/humble person.<br><strong>He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ngere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māngere</a> ia.</strong> — He is a lazy person.<br><strong>He tangata urupā ia.</strong> — She is a hardworking person. (urupā? No — that's a cemetery!)<br><br>Let me give you the right words:<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=urup%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">urupā</a></strong> = cemetery · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kaunui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākaunui</a></strong> = enthusiastic/passionate · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pukumahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pukumahi</a></strong> = hardworking</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Character vocabulary:<br><strong>atawhai</strong> — kind / caring / generous<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave / bold<br><strong>mōhio</strong> — knowledgeable / clever / aware<br><strong>hūmārie</strong> — gentle / humble / peaceful<br><strong>māngere</strong> — lazy<br><strong>pukumahi</strong> — hardworking<br><strong>ngākaunui</strong> — enthusiastic / passionate (ngākau = heart, nui = big)<br><strong>aroha nui</strong> — loving / full of aroha<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave<br><strong>tūmataiti</strong> — shy / reserved</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Used in a sentence:<br><strong>He tangata pukumahi, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.</strong><br>She is a hardworking and kind person.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tō mātou akomanga.</strong><br>She is the most enthusiastic person in our classroom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tangata [character word] ia — He/She is a [character] person. Stack two character words with &quot;he tangata X, he tangata Y hoki ia&quot; — or use the superlative: Ko ia te tangata [X] rawa atu o…</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Describe someone's character</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Think of someone you admire. We will describe their character together using this week's vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who is your person? State: <strong>Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</strong> Then: <strong>He tangata [character] ia.</strong></li><li>Add a second character trait: <strong>He tangata [X] hoki ia.</strong> Stack two traits.</li><li>Give evidence using nō te mea: <strong>He tangata atawhai ia nō te mea ka [action] ia i ngā wā katoa.</strong></li><li>Use a superlative: <strong>Ko ia te tangata [character] rawa atu o tōku ao.</strong> (ao = world/life)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tōku ao — she is the most enthusiastic person in my world. What makes this sentence beautiful? What does &quot;tōku ao&quot; (my world) add that &quot;tōku akomanga&quot; (my classroom) doesn't? When would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Character descriptions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write character descriptions of real people in your life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a He tangata [character] ia sentence about someone you admire.</li><li>Write two or three stacked character traits for that person.</li><li>Write a Ko ia te tangata [X] rawa atu o… superlative about them.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;tribute paragraph&quot; (6 sentences) about someone important to you — Ko [name] tōku [relationship]. Describe their character (3 traits), give evidence for one trait, and close with a superlative.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Feelings and character in stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Feelings/personality · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Emotion and character in narrative context</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Emotion in narrative; AO 2.3 — Character in stories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Show, don't tell — emotions in stories</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There is a saying in writing: &quot;show, don't tell.&quot; Instead of writing &quot;she was happy&quot;, you show the happiness — what she did, how she moved, what she said. Today we practise this in te reo Māori — describing feelings and character through action rather than just naming them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">TELLING (weak):<br><strong>Kei te hari ia.</strong> She is happy.<br><br>SHOWING (strong):<br><strong>Ka omaoma ia ki te kāinga. Ka karanga ia i tōna māmā. Ka oma hoki tōna māmā ki a ia, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=awhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">awhi</a> rāua.</strong><br>She ran home. She called out to her mum. Her mum ran to her too, and they hugged.<br><br>TELLING (weak):<br><strong>He tangata atawhai ia.</strong> He is a kind person.<br><br>SHOWING (strong):<br><strong>I kite ia i te kurī i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mauiui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mauiui</a> ana. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tango\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tango</a> ia i tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=koti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">koti</a> ā ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hipoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hipoki</a> ia i te kurī. Ka noho ia ki reira ā te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taenga</a> mai o tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>.</strong><br>He saw the sick dog. He took off his coat and covered the dog. He stayed there until its owner arrived.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show-don't-tell techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Replace the emotion word with an action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Telling: Kei te hari au.<br>Showing: Ka oma au, ka waiata au, ka kata au! (run, sing, laugh)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Replace the character word with evidence</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Telling: He tangata atawhai ia.<br>Showing: Ka tūāhu ia ki te tangata māuiui. Ka homai ia i āna kai.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>karanga</strong> — to call out / summon<br><strong>awhi</strong> — to embrace / hug<br><strong>hipoki</strong> — to cover<br><strong>taenga mai</strong> — the arrival (tae = arrive, -nga = nominaliser)<br><strong>rangatira</strong> — chief / leader / owner (of an animal in this context)<br><strong>kata</strong> — to laugh<br><strong>tūāhu</strong> — to tend to / look after (person in need)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: To show an emotion — ask yourself: what does someone DO when they feel this? Then write the action, not the feeling word. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kata</a>, ka waiata, ka omaoma — these show happiness better than &quot;Kei te hari&quot;.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Show, don't tell</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a telling sentence — you turn it into a showing sentence using actions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Telling: <strong>I pōuri ia.</strong> → Showing: What did she DO when she was sad? <strong>Ka noho ia i tōna rūma. Kāore ia i kōrero. Ka tangi ia i te pō.</strong></li><li>Telling: <strong>He tangata māia ia.</strong> → Showing: What did he DO to show bravery? <strong>Ka tū ia i mua i te whakamā. Ka kōrero ia ahakoa i ohorere ia.</strong></li><li>Telling: <strong>I harikoa ngā tamariki.</strong> → Showing: Three actions that show their joy.</li><li>Now you write one: tell me an emotion — then show it through three actions in te reo.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka kata, ka waiata, ka omaoma — a laughing, singing, running person. You have not said &quot;hari&quot; (happy) once. But the reader FEELS it. Why is showing more powerful than telling? When might telling be the right choice?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Show it</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rewrite telling sentences as showing sentences in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Telling: &quot;Kei te mataku ia.&quot; — Show the fear through three actions.</li><li>Telling: &quot;He tangata pukumahi ia.&quot; — Show the hard work through evidence.</li><li>Telling: &quot;I hari mātou katoa.&quot; — Show the group's joy through action.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a scene (8 sentences) where a character experiences a strong emotion — but NEVER NAME the emotion. Only use actions, dialogue, and description. Read it to a partner — can they identify the emotion?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā tāngata — Talking about people":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Character/feelings · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 1.2: Introducing others</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Character &amp; feelings; AO 1.2 — Introducing others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Introducing and describing people</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week you have learned feelings and character. Today we use them to introduce and describe real people — friends, whānau, teachers — in a natural, warm conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Hēni tōku hoa pai rawa atu. He tangata atawhai, he tangata ngākaunui hoki ia.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōna āhua?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tangata <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81horahora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhorahora</a> ia — ka kata ia i ngā wā katoa. Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka aroha! He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka pai ki a au tōna māia — ka tū tonu ia ahakoa he uaua te āhua o ngā mea. He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a> tino nui tērā.<br><strong>A:</strong> E ai ki tōu kōrero, ko ia te hoa tino pai rawa atu o tō tātou ao!<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe! Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversational phrases about people:<br><strong>He pēhea tōna āhua?</strong> — What is he/she like? (personality/appearance)<br><strong>He tangata māhorahora ia.</strong> — He/she is a joyful/light-hearted person.<br><strong>Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.</strong> — My heart comes alive at her presence.<br><strong>He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?</strong> — What do you like about their personality?<br><strong>Ka tū tonu ia ahakoa…</strong> — He/she stands firm even though…<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — quality / characteristic (tūāhua + tanga)<br><strong>E ai ki tōu kōrero</strong> — According to what you say<br><strong>Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</strong> — She is the pillar of my world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He pēhea tōna āhua? = What is their personality/character like? This is different from He pēhea ia? (How is he/she?). Āhua focuses on their nature — who they are as a person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Introduce your favourite person</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation introducing someone you admire.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu?</strong> Introduce them: Ko [name] tōku [relationship].</li><li>I ask: <strong>He pēhea tōna āhua?</strong> Give two character traits using He tangata [X] ia.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu e pai ai mō tōna āhua?</strong> Show one quality through action (show don't tell).</li><li>Close: <strong>Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</strong> Why is she/he your pou? Add nō te mea.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ia te pou o tōku ao — she is the pillar of my world. Last week you heard &quot;Ko ia te pou o tō mātou whānau.&quot; Now it's &quot;tōku ao&quot; (my world) rather than &quot;tō mātou whānau&quot; (our family). What is the difference in feeling between these two? When would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He mihi ki tōu hoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tribute to someone important in your life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Introduce them: Ko [name] tōku [relationship]. He tangata [character] ia.</li><li>Show their best quality through an action (show don't tell — no emotion words).</li><li>Write what they mean to you: Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full tribute (8 sentences) about your most important person. Introduce them, describe two character traits, show one quality through evidence, say what they mean to you, and close with Ko ia te pou o tōku ao.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te māharahara au.&quot; How is this person feeling?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>excited and eager </li><li>angry </li><li>worried / anxious ✅</li><li>surprised </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te pēhea tōu ngākau?&quot; What does ngākau mean in this context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>your body </li><li>your stomach </li><li>your heart (as the seat of emotions) ✅</li><li>your head </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pukumahi&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>lazy </li><li>shy </li><li>passionate </li><li>hardworking ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia te tangata ngākaunui rawa atu o tō mātou akomanga.&quot; What does ngākaunui mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the most intelligent </li><li>the most hard-working </li><li>the most enthusiastic / passionate (big-hearted) ✅</li><li>the most kind </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is an example of &quot;showing&quot; rather than &quot;telling&quot; an emotion?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te hari ia. </li><li>I pōuri ia nō te mea he uaua tāna mahi. </li><li>Ka omaoma ia, ka kata ia, ka waiata ia! ✅</li><li>He tangata māia ia. </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;awhi&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to call out </li><li>to cover </li><li>to embrace / hug ✅</li><li>to laugh </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Her face is beautiful </li><li>My heart comes alive at her presence / personality ✅</li><li>She is the most important person </li><li>I feel sad when I see her </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pēhea tōna āhua?&quot; — What is this question asking about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Their physical height </li><li>Their age </li><li>Their health today </li><li>Their character / personality / nature ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō te aho — Linking ideas: cohesion review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Connective vocabulary, paragraph cohesion across all text types</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Cohesion &amp; linking; AO 2.5 — Connective vocabulary review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All the linking tools, together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term you have learned many ways to link ideas — conjunctions, pronouns, connective words. Today we gather all the linking tools in one place and practise choosing between them deliberately. A well-connected passage is what separates good te reo from great te reo.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>COORDINATING CONJUNCTIONS (equal ideas):</strong><br>ā · engari · nō reira · me<br><br><strong>SUBORDINATING CONJUNCTIONS (one idea depends on the other):</strong><br>nō te mea · nā te mea · ahakoa · mehemea · i te wā<br><br><strong>CONNECTIVE ADVERBS (linking sentences):</strong><br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=heoi+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heoi anō</a> — however / but anyway<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81+muri+ake+nei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ā muri ake nei</a> — furthermore / going forward<br>hei tā tēnei — according to this / as a result<br>nō reira — therefore (can work as both conjunction and connective)<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihoki</a> — moreover / and also<br>engari rā — however (more formal than engari alone)<br><br><strong>TIME CONNECTIVES (for sequence):</strong><br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=i+mua+ake\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">i mua ake</a> — beforehand / previously<br><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81+muri+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ā muri iho</a> — afterwards<br>i te wā hōhonu — in the long run</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using connective adverbs (sentence starters):</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I mahi kaha ngā ākonga. Heoi anō, i ngenge rātou ā te mutunga o te rā.</strong><br>The students worked hard. However, they were tired by the end of the day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te reo Māori he reo rangatira. Waihoki, he reo o te ngahere, o te moana, o ngā maunga.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is a chiefly language. Moreover, it is a language of the forest, the sea, and the mountains.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I mahi kaha mātou. Ā muri iho, ka ora ai tā mātou mahi.</strong><br>We worked hard. Afterwards, our work flourished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>rangatira</strong> — chiefly / of high status<br><strong>ora</strong> — alive / well / flourishing<br><strong>heoi anō</strong> — however / but anyway<br><strong>waihoki</strong> — moreover / and also</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Connective adverbs (heoi anō, waihoki, ā muri iho) go at the START of a sentence and signal the relationship to what came before. They are stronger than conjunctions because they create a full pause — a new sentence — before the connection.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Connect the ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two sentences — you connect them using the most appropriate linking word from today's list.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;The team won.&quot; &quot;They were still humble.&quot; → The contrast needs: <strong>heoi anō</strong> → <strong>I toa rātou. Heoi anō, i hūmārie tonu rātou.</strong></li><li>&quot;She studied hard.&quot; &quot;She passed the test.&quot; → Result: <strong>nō reira / ā muri iho</strong> → <strong>I ako kaha ia. Nō reira, i paahi ia i te whakamātautau.</strong></li><li>&quot;Te reo Māori is beautiful.&quot; &quot;It connects us to our ancestors.&quot; → Adding more: <strong>waihoki</strong>.</li><li>Write your own: two sentences about your school, connected with heoi anō OR waihoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Heoi anō and engari both mean &quot;however/but&quot;. Heoi anō starts a NEW sentence. Engari stays INSIDE a sentence. When would you choose heoi anō over engari? What does starting a new sentence add to the contrast?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Connected writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage where every sentence connects to the next.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write four sentences — connect each to the next with a different linking word.</li><li>Use heoi anō at the start of one sentence to create a contrast.</li><li>Use waihoki to add more information to a point.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a persuasive paragraph (7 sentences) about something you care about — your favourite sport, your school, te reo Māori. Use at least four different linking words from today's list. Underline each one.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Term 2 vocabulary and structures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Full Term 2 review · Grammar Levels 1–2 all structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Term 2 review; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything Term 2 has added</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1 gave you the foundations. Term 2 has been building on them. Today we celebrate everything new that you have added — and see how much bigger your te reo Māori world has become.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TENSES you now control:</strong><br>Kei te (now) · I (past event) · Ka (story/sequence) · Kāore e…ana (not now) · Kāore i (didn't happen)<br><br><strong>PRONOUNS you now have:</strong><br>All 16: au, koe, ia / tāua, māua, kōrua, rāua / tātou, mātou, koutou, rātou<br><br><strong>NUMBERS you now know:</strong><br>1–100, dates, times, people counts (toko-)<br><br><strong>NEW VOCABULARY DOMAINS:</strong><br>Colours (12+) · Feelings (12+) · Character (8+) · Weather (10+) · Seasons (4) · Days/months (19) · Family vocabulary · Sports · Announcements</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The growth from Term 1 to Term 2:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1: <strong>Kei te haere au ki te kura.</strong><br>Term 2: <strong>Ā te Rāhina, i te iwa karaka i te ata, ka haere mātou tokowhitu ki te kura — ahakoa he ua tino nui, i tino pai tā mātou rā.</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Same basic idea — going to school. But now:<br>→ day of week (Rāhina)<br>→ time (iwa karaka i te ata)<br>→ Ka narrative<br>→ people count (tokowhitu)<br>→ ahakoa (despite)<br>→ past tense (i tino pai)<br>→ possessive (tā mātou rā)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is what a term of daily practice gives you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that Term 2 sentence aloud. You could not have written it in Week 1 of this term. That is your progress. Say it again and own it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Term 2 blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed review — category by category. Teacher calls a category, students generate examples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>FEELINGS: How many emotion words can we list? Go — hari, pōuri, mataku… aim for 10+.</li><li>PRONOUNS: Say all 16 — starting with singular, then dual, then plural. Together.</li><li>NUMBERS: Count from 11 to 20 together. Then say these: 34, 57, 82, 99.</li><li>WEATHER: Give a full three-day forecast in te reo — today, tomorrow, next week.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look back at Week 1 of this term — the holiday conversation. Could you have had that conversation at the start of Term 1? No. Could you have it now? Easily. What does it feel like to look back and see how far you have come?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Term 2 self-audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test your own Term 2 knowledge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write all 16 pronouns from memory — organised by singular, dual, plural.</li><li>Write one sentence using Ka, one using I, one using Kāore i, one using Kāore e…ana.</li><li>Write today's date and time fully in te reo Māori.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Term 2 showcase sentence&quot; — one sentence that uses as many Term 2 structures as possible: past tense, a time/date, a number, a colour, a feeling, and a conjunction. Make it a real sentence, not just a list.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Term 2 sentence type showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All sentence types from Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sentence type review; AO 2.5 — Rich vocabulary in sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full sentence toolkit, Terms 1 and 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Across two terms you have built an enormous range of sentence types. Today we display them all — see the full range you now control, and choose which ones to use deliberately.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY:</strong> Ko Tāmara tōku ingoa. Nō Tāmaki Makaurau ahau.<br><strong>DESCRIPTION:</strong> He tangata pukumahi, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.<br><strong>PRESENT:</strong> Kei te ako kaha rātou i te reo Māori i tēnei wā.<br><strong>PAST:</strong> I te Rāhina o tērā wiki, i haere mātou tokowhitu ki te hākinakina.<br><strong>NARRATIVE:</strong> Ka ara ia i te ata, ka kai ia i tōna parakuihi, ka haere ia ki te kura.<br><strong>NEGATIVE:</strong> Kāore ia i tae i tēnei rā nō te mea i māuiui ia.<br><strong>COMPLEX:</strong> Ahakoa i ngenge mātou, i mahi tonu mātou nō te mea he mahi nui tō mātou.<br><strong>COMPARATIVE:</strong> He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā — e rua tekau mā waru ki e rua tekau mā tahi.<br><strong>FRONTED:</strong> I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako mō ngā whainga o tērā tau.<br><strong>LISTED:</strong> Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai mātou i tēnei wāhanga: ngā tae, ngā tau, ngā tūāhua, me ngā kupu hono.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ten sentence types. Two terms. You can write all of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The key to fluency is not knowing all the types — it is choosing the RIGHT type for what you want to say:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Want to identify? Ko<br>→ Want to describe? He<br>→ Want to say now? Kei te<br>→ Want to tell a story? Ka<br>→ Want to report a fact? I<br>→ Want to deny? Kāore<br>→ Want to connect? Conjunction<br>→ Want to compare? He [adj] ake<br>→ Want to set the scene? Fronted phrase<br>→ Want to list? Ko ēnei</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have all ten. Use them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best writers don't use all ten types in every paragraph — they choose deliberately. Read your writing aloud and ask: does every sentence sound different from the last? If not, vary the type.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — One prompt, ten types</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you one topic — together we write one sentence of EACH type about it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: your school. Write a Ko identity sentence and a He description sentence.</li><li>Write a Kei te sentence (now), an I sentence (past), and a Ka sentence (narrative).</li><li>Write a Kāore sentence and a complex ahakoa sentence.</li><li>Write a comparative, a fronted phrase, and a Ko ēnei list. Ten sentence types, one topic.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at the ten sentences about your school. Pick the three that feel most alive — most like real te reo, not just an exercise. Why do those three feel stronger? What do they have that the others don't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sentence type showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a topic you care about and write all ten sentence types.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your topic: sport, whānau, nature, music, food, or another.</li><li>Write sentences in types 1–5: Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka.</li><li>Write sentences in types 6–10: Kāore, ahakoa/complex, comparative, fronted, Ko ēnei.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take your ten sentences and weave them into a genuine paragraph — reorder them, add connectives, cut anything that doesn't flow. Aim for a paragraph that reads like real te reo, not a list of exercise sentences.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hou — A spiralled conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 1 and 2 AOs · Full conversation practice</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — Expressive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full Term 2 conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The best test of language learning is conversation. Today we have the most complex conversation of the year so far — drawing on everything from both terms. Listen to how naturally the structures flow when you stop thinking about them individually.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere mātou ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngahere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngahere</a> ko tōku whānau. Ko ēnei ngā mea tino pai: he ātaahua ngā rākau, i ngāoro te awa, ā i kaukau mātou tokowhitu i te roto māhana!<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! He pēhea te āhua o te rangi i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I makariri i te ata engari ka mahana i te ahiahi. Ahakoa he ua i tētahi rā, i haere tonu mātou ki waho.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō āhua i ērā rā?<br><strong>B:</strong> I hari tino nui au! Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao. Ko tōku māmā te tangata i ārahi i a mātou — he tangata mōhio, he tangata atawhai hoki ia.<br><strong>A:</strong> He ātaahua tērā. He aha tāu whainga mō tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whainga: ka ako māmā ake au i te reo Māori — ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation uses:<br>✓ Past tense (I haere, i ngāoro, i makariri, i hari)<br>✓ Ka narrative (ka mahana, ka kaukau, ka ora)<br>✓ Plural pronouns (mātou, tokowhitu)<br>✓ Weather (makariri, mahana, ua)<br>✓ Feelings (i hari, Ka ora tōku ngākau)<br>✓ Character description (He tangata mōhio, he tangata atawhai)<br>✓ Ko ēnei list (Ko ēnei ngā mea tino pai…)<br>✓ Comparative (ako māmā ake)<br>✓ Complex (ahakoa he ua, i haere tonu mātou)<br>✓ Goal statement (Ko tāku whainga…)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is the whole of Terms 1 and 2 in one natural conversation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga. This is the goal — not just to speak te reo in class, but to take it home. What would you need to practise to have this conversation with your own whānau?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full conversation practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. Use the model conversation as a guide — but make it your own. Your holidays, your family, your feelings.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with greetings and ask about holidays: Tēnā koe! He pēhea ōu hararei?</li><li>Report what happened: past tense + people count + Ko ēnei list.</li><li>Describe the weather: past + weather + ahakoa + kept going anyway.</li><li>Share a feeling + describe a person + state your goal for this term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga. Is this a realistic goal for you? What would your first te reo conversation with your whānau look like? What three sentences would you teach them to make it possible?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu kōrero hou</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have your own full Term 2 conversation and write it up.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have a full conversation with a partner — at least 10 lines each — about your holidays.</li><li>Use: past tense, Ka narrative, a plural pronoun, a weather sentence, a feeling, and a goal.</li><li>Write up the conversation as a dialogue.</li><li>✦ Bonus: After writing your dialogue, annotate it — label every structure you used. How many different structures can you find? Aim for at least eight.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I mahi kaha ngā ākonga. Heoi anō, i ngenge rātou.&quot; What does heoi anō signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Therefore — they worked hard as a result of being tired </li><li>Moreover — they were also hard workers </li><li>However — despite working hard, they were tired ✅</li><li>Because — tiredness caused them to work hard </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;waihoki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>however / but </li><li>therefore </li><li>moreover / and also ✅</li><li>because </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these correctly uses Ka for a narrative sequence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka ara ia. Ka kai ia. Ka haere ia ki te kura. ✅</li><li>I ara ia. Ka kai ia. Kei te haere ia ki te kura. </li><li>Ka ara ia. I kai ia inanahi. Ka haere ia. </li><li>Ka ara, kai, haere ia ki te kura. </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I toa rātou. Heoi anō, i hūmārie tonu rātou.&quot; What does hūmārie mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>exhausted </li><li>angry </li><li>humble / gentle / peaceful ✅</li><li>excited </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence type uses &quot;Ko ēnei&quot; to introduce a list?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Identity sentence </li><li>Narrative sentence </li><li>Listed sentence ✅</li><li>Comparative sentence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He nui ake ngā ākonga i tēnei akomanga i tērā.&quot; What sentence type is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko identity </li><li>Kāore negative </li><li>He comparative ✅</li><li>Ka narrative </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tāku whainga: ka kōrero au me tōku whānau i te kāinga.&quot; What does whainga mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>problem </li><li>memory </li><li>goal / aim ✅</li><li>holiday </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao.&quot; What does this express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The weather made me tired </li><li>My heart comes alive in the presence of nature ✅</li><li>The natural world is dangerous </li><li>I am going outside to rest </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 2 full vocabulary and structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 consolidated · All grammar structures Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings review; AO 2.3 — All structures; AO 2.5 — Vocabulary mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full two-term vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last week of Term 2. Today we count everything you now know and celebrate it. By the end of today's lesson you will have a complete picture of your two-term te reo Māori journey.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Two terms of learning. Here is what you can now do:<br><br><strong>COMMUNICATE ABOUT:</strong><br>Yourself, your family, your school, your home, your feelings, your character, the weather, the seasons, numbers 1–100, dates, times, sporting events, holiday recounts, announcements<br><br><strong>STRUCTURES YOU CONTROL:</strong><br>Ko · He · Kei te · I · Ka · Kāore e…ana · Kāore i · E hia / Tokohia · tōku/tāku/tōu/tāu · All 16 pronouns · Ahakoa · Mehemea · I te wā · Comparatives · Fronted phrases · Ko ēnei lists · Conjunctions (7+) · Connective adverbs (heoi anō, waihoki)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Term 2 additions to your Term 1 base:</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">TERM 1 base sentence</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">TERM 2 full sentence</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā ono o Pipiri, i haere mātou tokotoru ki te kura i te iwa karaka i te ata — ahakoa i ua tino nui, i tino hari mātou nō te mea he kaupeka hākinakina ā te ahiahi!&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Translation: On Thursday the 16th of June, three of us went to school at 9 in the morning — although it rained heavily, we were very happy because there was a sports competition in the afternoon!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures: date, day, people count, time, ahakoa, feeling, nō te mea, Ka… You can do all of this now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Say that last sentence aloud. Slowly. &quot;I te Rāpare, ko te tekau mā ono o Pipiri, i haere mātou tokotoru ki te kura…&quot; You are speaking te reo Māori. Real, complex, beautiful te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Two-term vocabulary blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Category blitz — fastest hands/voices win.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ALL EMOTIONS: How many can we list in 60 seconds? hari, pōuri, mataku, māia… aim for 12.</li><li>ALL WEATHER: Every weather word. ua, hau, hukarere, āniwaniwa… aim for 10.</li><li>ALL 16 PRONOUNS: Together, in order — singular, dual, plural.</li><li>WILD CARD: Make one sentence that includes a colour, a feeling, a number, and a conjunction. Go.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now been learning te reo Māori for two terms — roughly 20 weeks, 400 minutes of class time. That is less than seven hours of instruction. In seven hours, you have built a communication toolkit of over 200 words and 15+ grammar structures. What does this tell you about what is possible with daily practice?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Two-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A personal vocabulary and structure audit.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write down every te reo Māori word you can remember in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write the six tense/negative structures: Kei te, I, Ka, Kāore e…ana, Kāore i, E…ana.</li><li>Write your favourite sentence from all of Term 2 — and say why it is your favourite.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your &quot;two-term milestone&quot; — a paragraph describing what you can now do in te reo that you could not do 20 weeks ago. Use as many structures as possible. This is your record of growth.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar and structure deep review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–2 consolidated and tested</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation; AO 2.4 — Tense mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Grammar in the wild</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review grammar not through rules but through real sentences — seeing structures at work in natural te reo. You will identify the structure in each sentence, and then build your own.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Identify the structure in each sentence:<br><strong>1. Ko Mere tōku tuakana.</strong> → ___<br><strong>2. Kei te mahi kaha rātou i waho i tēnei wā.</strong> → ___<br><strong>3. I haere māua ki te tāone i te Hātarei.</strong> → ___<br><strong>4. Ka ara ia, ka horoi ia i tōna kanohi, ka haere ia ki te kura.</strong> → ___<br><strong>5. Kāore mātou i toa i tērā kēmu — he kino tō mātou raru.</strong> → ___<br><strong>6. Ahakoa i ua kaha, i tākaro tonu mātou i te hākinakina.</strong> → ___<br><strong>7. He nui ake ngā ira o tō mātou kapa i ā rātou ira — e rua tekau mā whā ki e tekau mā toru.</strong> → ___<br><strong>8. I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako.</strong> → ___</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Answers:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko → Identity</li><li>Kei te + plural pronoun + adverb → Present</li><li>I + dual pronoun + past location → Past with people</li><li>Ka… Ka… Ka → Narrative sequence</li><li>Kāore i → Past negative + He description</li><li>Ahakoa → Complex sentence (concession)</li><li>He [adj] ake + numbers → Comparative with score</li><li>Fronted time + fronted place + Ka narrative</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every one of these you can write. Every one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you see an unfamiliar te reo sentence, break it into parts: What is the tense marker? Who is the person? What is the action? What else is there? This analysis skill is what makes you independent — you can work out new sentences for yourself.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grammar challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences matching the structure I specify.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Build a Ka narrative — five steps. Something that happened yesterday.</li><li>Build a Kāore i sentence AND a Kāore e…ana sentence — both about the same topic.</li><li>Build a He [adj] ake sentence with two numbers (a score or comparison).</li><li>Build a fronted time + fronted place + I sentence — three layers, one sentence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at sentence 8 from the I Do: &quot;I te ahiahi o Rāpare, i roto i te whare hui, ka kōrero tō mātou kaiako.&quot; Three fronted phrases before the main action. In English this would be unusual. In te reo Māori it is natural and elegant. Why does front-loading information feel different in te reo than in English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-test</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence using each of the eight structures from I Do.</li><li>Write a passage (5 sentences) that uses all of: Ko, I, Ka, Kāore i, ahakoa.</li><li>Write the most complex sentence you can — use at least five different structures in one sentence.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Take a sentence you wrote in Week 1 of this term. Rewrite it now with everything you have learned. How different is it? What does the difference tell you?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Showcase sentences: Terms 1 and 2":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All sentence types Terms 1 and 2</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Showcase &amp; celebration; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your best te reo sentences of the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is a celebration. Today you write the best te reo sentences of your life so far — drawing on everything from two terms. There is no new content today. Just you, your full toolkit, and the task of producing something beautiful.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Here are examples of student showcase sentences:<br><br><strong>&quot;I te ahiahi o Rāhoroi, ko tōku whānau me ōku hoa pukumahi i haere mātou tokotoru tekau ki te tākaro hākinakina — i toa mātou e rua tekau mā ono ki e tekau mā whā!&quot;</strong><br>(On Saturday afternoon, my family and my hardworking friends, thirty of us went to play sport — we won 26 to 14!)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko te taiao o Aotearoa tōku wāhi okioki — ā, ko ia hoki tōku ara ora.&quot;</strong><br>(The natural environment of New Zealand is my place of rest — and it is also my pathway to life.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka ora tōku ngākau i ngā kupu o tēnei reo, nō te mea ko te reo Māori te reo o ōku tīpuna — he taonga tuku iho tēnei.&quot;</strong><br>(My heart comes alive at the words of this language, because te reo Māori is the language of my ancestors — it is a treasure passed down.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences great:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They use REAL information (not just exercises)<br>→ They COMBINE multiple structures naturally<br>→ They say something the writer MEANS<br>→ They have a voice — you can hear a person behind the words</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice:<br>&quot;ko ia hoki tōku ara ora&quot; — and it is also my pathway to life (ara ora — a beautiful new compound: ara + ora = path + life)<br>&quot;he taonga tuku iho&quot; — a treasure passed down (tuku iho = handed down through generations)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are not just grammar exercises. They are statements about identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best sentence you can write today is not the longest or the most complex — it is the most true. Say something you mean. In te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one showcase sentence together on the board. Everyone contributes one element.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: who and when? Ko + person + time/date.</li><li>What happened? I or Ka + action + place.</li><li>Add detail: number + weather or feeling.</li><li>Add meaning: ahakoa or nō te mea + result or reflection.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He taonga tuku iho — a treasure passed down through generations. The phrase tuku iho means &quot;handed down&quot; — from ancestors to descendants. Te reo Māori is a taonga tuku iho. What does it mean to receive something that was passed down across generations? What responsibility does that bring?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the best te reo Māori sentences you have ever written.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — punchy and true.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — with a subordinate clause and real meaning.</li><li>Write your best DESCRIPTIVE sentence — rich with vocabulary.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your SHOWCASE SENTENCE — the single best te reo Māori sentence you can produce. Make it say something true about you, your world, or te reo Māori itself. Read it to the class.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End of Term 2 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes: Whakarongo, Kōrero, Pānui, Tuhituhi, Mātakitaki, Whakaatu</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 2 standard</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is your end-of-term assessment. Before we begin, let's see what a full Term 2 level conversation looks like. This is the standard — it uses structures from both terms in a natural, warm exchange.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe! Ko wai tōu ingoa? Nō hea koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Rangi tōku ingoa. Nō Ōtautahi ahau — he tāone ātaahua, he tāone māhana hoki i te raumati.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea ōu hararei?<br><strong>B:</strong> He tino pai! I haere mātou tokowhā ki te ngahere. I ātaahua te rangi — ahakoa i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hau</a> kaha i tētahi rā.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu āhua ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki — e pai ana ki a au te kura hou!<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha āu whainga mō tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ōku whainga: ka ako ake au i ngā tae, ka mōhio ake au ki ngā tau, ā ka kōrero māmā ake au i te reo Māori.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka rawe! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assessment criteria — what this conversation shows:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe · correct greeting and closing<br>✓ AO 1.2: Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br>✓ AO 1.4: Origin and personal information<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended kōrero with feelings, reasons, goals<br>✓ AO 2.4: Past events · Weather · Seasonal reference<br>✓ AO 2.5: Adjectives · Emotions · Character · Numbers<br>✓ Grammar: I, Ka, Kei te, ahakoa, Ko ēnei (implied), comparative (ake)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki — I am happy, I am also excited. Two feelings in one sentence. That is confident te reo. When you feel something strongly — name it twice.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice round</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One full practice conversation with a partner before the assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet, introduce, say where you're from with one description of your town.</li><li>Describe your holidays: past tense + people count + weather + ahakoa.</li><li>Say how you feel now: two emotions + a reason.</li><li>State three goals for Term 3 using Ko ōku whainga: ka… ka… ā ka…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko ōku whainga — my goals. What are your real goals for Term 3? Not just te reo goals — learning goals, relationship goals, personal goals. Can you say any of them in te reo Māori right now? What one goal would you most like to be able to say in te reo by the end of Term 3?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — End of Term 2</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your end-of-term spoken assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full conversation — at least 10 lines each. Cover greeting, introduction, holidays, feelings, goals.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess honestly against the checklist.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write your Term 3 goal in te reo: Ko tāku whainga mō Wāhanga Toru:</li><li>✦ Checklist: ☐ Greeted correctly (Tēnā koe) · ☐ Introduced self (Ko/Nō) · ☐ Used past tense (I) · ☐ Used Ka narrative · ☐ Expressed a feeling (Kei te [emotion]) · ☐ Used a number or date · ☐ Used a conjunction · ☐ Closed with Tēnā koe ×3</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses Ka for a narrative sequence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka ara ia, ka kai, ka haere ia ki te kura. ✅</li><li>I ara ia, ka kai ia, kei te haere ia. </li><li>Ka ara ia inanahi, ka kai ia āpōpō. </li><li>Ka ara. Kai. Haere ki te kura. </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo Māori he taonga tuku iho.&quot; What does tuku iho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>passed down through generations ✅</li><li>difficult to learn </li><li>belonging to one person only </li><li>recently created </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa i ua kaha, i tākaro tonu mātou.&quot; What structure is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past negative </li><li>Ka narrative </li><li>Complex concession with ahakoa ✅</li><li>Ko identity sentence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He nui ake ngā ira o tō mātou kapa i ā rātou ira.&quot; What does this compare?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The size of two teams </li><li>The scores of two teams ✅</li><li>The number of players </li><li>The colours of two teams </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ia hoki tōku ara ora.&quot; What does ara ora mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>learning journey </li><li>favourite song </li><li>pathway to life ✅</li><li>daily routine </li></ul></li><li>What makes a &quot;showcase sentence&quot; different from an exercise sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is longer </li><li>It uses more vocabulary </li><li>It says something the writer truly means and has a real voice ✅</li><li>It always uses Ka </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te hari au, kei te hīhī hoki.&quot; What does hīhī mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>sad </li><li>excited / eager ✅</li><li>surprised </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ōku whainga: ka ako ake au…&quot; — What does ako ake mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to stop learning </li><li>to learn less </li><li>to learn more / further ✅</li><li>to learn backwards </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā wāhanga o te tinana — Parts of the body":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical characteristics · Grammar Level 1–2: Body vocabulary, E…ana progressive aspect</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Body parts &amp; physical description; AO 1.5 — Location on body</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The body in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This term we go deeper — more complex sentence structures, richer vocabulary, and longer conversations. We begin with the body, because body vocabulary connects everything: feelings (your heart, your stomach), action (your hands, your feet), and description (your face, your eyes). Listen and connect each word to what you feel.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81hunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māhunga</a></strong> — head<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rae</a></strong> — forehead<br><strong>kanohi</strong> — face<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatu</a></strong> / <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=karu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karu</a></strong> — eye(s)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ihu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ihu</a></strong> — nose<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waha</a></strong> — mouth<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taringa</a></strong> — ear<br><strong>niho</strong> — teeth<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaki</a></strong> — neck / throat<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pokohiwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pokohiwi</a></strong> — shoulder<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringa</a></strong> / <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringaringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringaringa</a></strong> — hand / arm<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C5%ABk%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngūkū</a></strong> — elbow<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puku</a></strong> — stomach / belly<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuar%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuarā</a></strong> — back<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waewae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waewae</a></strong> — leg / foot<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=turi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">turi</a></strong> — knee<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waewae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waewae</a> matua</strong> — right foot<br><strong>waewae <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%8Dng%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tōngā</a></strong> — left foot</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Body parts in a sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> tōku māhunga.</strong><br>My head is sore. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> = sore/pain)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He whero ōna karu.</strong><br>His/her eyes are red.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I horoi ia i ōna niho.</strong><br>She brushed her teeth. (horoi = clean/wash)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te ngongo tōku kaki.</strong><br>My throat is sore/tight. (ngongo = tight/constricted)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Possessives with body parts:<br>→ Body parts are ō-category (they are part of you, senior to your control)<br>→ Use tōku, tōu, tōna, ō mātou, ō rātou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tōku māhunga</strong> — my head<br><strong>tōna kanohi</strong> — his/her face<br><strong>ō mātou waewae</strong> — our legs</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Body parts are ō-category — they are part of you, not things you control. So always tōku (not tāku) for body parts. Tōku māhunga, tōku ringa, tōku waewae.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Name and describe the body</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Point and name — I point to a body part, you name it in te reo. Then use it in a sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I point to your ear: <strong>taringa</strong>. Use it: <strong>Kei te aha tōu taringa?</strong> — What is wrong with your ear?</li><li>Describe using He: <strong>He nui ōna karu.</strong> She has big eyes. <strong>He iti tōna ihu.</strong> His nose is small.</li><li>Use past tense: <strong>I mamae tōku puku inanahi.</strong> My stomach was sore yesterday.</li><li>Connect body + feeling: <strong>Ka hōhipū tōku ngākau.</strong> My heart leaps/jumps. (hōhipū = leap/jump with excitement)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: In English we say &quot;I have a sore head&quot; — but in te reo: &quot;Kei te mamae tōku māhunga&quot; — literally &quot;right now sore my head.&quot; The pain is the subject, not you. What does this tell us about how te reo Māori thinks about physical experience?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Body vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use body vocabulary in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write six body part sentences — three with Kei te and three with He.</li><li>Write a past tense sentence about a time something hurt: I mamae tōku… nō te mea…</li><li>Write a body sentence that connects to an emotion: Ka [emotion] tōku ngākau / puku / kanohi.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a seven-sentence &quot;body check&quot; — from head to toe, describe the state of each body part right now. Are you tired? Sore? Alert? Express it through the body.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: E haere ana, e kai ana — The progressive aspect E…ana":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: E…ana (present progressive — ongoing action)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Progressive aspect; AO 2.4 — Ongoing action in time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The action is still happening</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know Kei te for what is happening right now. Today we learn a related but different structure — E…ana. While Kei te states a present action, E…ana emphasises that the action is ONGOING — still in progress, continuing, not yet finished. It is a subtle but powerful distinction.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te kai ia.</strong> — She is eating. (present state)<br><strong>E kai ana ia.</strong> — She is eating (and still going — the eating is ongoing)<br><br><strong>E mahi ana rātou i tēnei wā.</strong><br>They are working right now (the work is ongoing).<br><br><strong>E ako ana tātou i te reo Māori.</strong><br>We are learning te reo Māori (an ongoing process — it continues).<br><br><strong>I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tae mai tōna hoa.</strong><br>While she was working (ongoing), her friend arrived.<br><br><strong>Kāore ia e haere ana ki te kura āpōpō.</strong><br>She is not going to school tomorrow (ongoing plan that is not happening).</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>E…ana</strong> instead of <strong>Kei te</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ When the action is clearly ONGOING and UNFINISHED:<br>&quot;E hanga ana rātou i te whare.&quot; — They are building the house. (still under construction)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ In relative clauses — describing something while it is happening:<br>&quot;I te wā e ako ana mātou&quot; — While we were learning</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ In negative future:<br>&quot;Kāore ia e haere ana&quot; — She is not going (as an ongoing fact)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ For slower, ongoing processes:<br>&quot;E tipu ana ngā rākau.&quot; — The trees are growing. (gradual ongoing process)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kei te tends to be more immediate: Kei te kai au RIGHT NOW.<br>E…ana tends to be more continuous: E tipu ana te whenua — the land is ever growing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E…ana = still happening, ongoing. Think of it as the -ing that is still in progress. Kei te = happening now. E…ana = happening now AND continuing. For processes, use E…ana.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Still going</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a Kei te sentence — you decide if E…ana would work better and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kei te horoi ia i tōna kanohi.&quot; → Could this be E horoi ana ia? Yes — if the face-washing is ongoing, not just a moment.</li><li>&quot;E tipu ana te ngahere o Aotearoa.&quot; — The forests of NZ are growing. Why E…ana not Kei te? Because this is a long-term ongoing process.</li><li>&quot;I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tangi tōna pēpi.&quot; — While she was working, her baby cried. Build a similar sentence with i te wā e…ana.</li><li>&quot;Kāore mātou e haere ana ki te marae āpōpō.&quot; — We are not going to the marae tomorrow. Why Kāore e…ana for a future negative plan?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E ako ana tātou i te reo Māori — we are learning te reo Māori (and the learning is ongoing). This sentence describes what you are doing this whole year. How does adding the ongoing aspect change the feeling of the sentence compared to &quot;Kei te ako tātou&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The ongoing action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences using E…ana for ongoing processes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three E…ana sentences about ongoing things happening in your world right now.</li><li>Write an i te wā e…ana clause: &quot;While [something was ongoing], [something happened].&quot;</li><li>Write a Kāore e…ana negative about something you are not going to do.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) using E…ana for ongoing background actions, Kei te for immediate actions, and Ka for events that punctuate the ongoing scene. E.g. describe a busy classroom.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Body and progressive in stories":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical description · AO 2.3: Extended narrative · Grammar Level 1–2: Body + E…ana in descriptive narrative</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Physical narrative; AO 2.3 — Descriptive story writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Describing action through the body</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Great storytelling uses the body — characters feel things in their stomach, see with wide eyes, and run with aching legs. Today we combine body vocabulary and E…ana to write vivid physical descriptions in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>E omaoma ana ia, e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> ana ōna waewae, engari kāore ia e tū ana.</strong><br>She is running, her legs aching, but she is not stopping.<br><br><strong>Ka tū ia. E hūhū ana tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ng%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngākau</a>. Ka titiro ia ki runga ki ngā maunga.</strong><br>She stood. Her heart was pounding. She looked up at the mountains.<br><br><strong>I te wā e haere ana mātou ki te ngahere, ka kite mātou i tētahi manu nui — e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rere</a> ana ia i runga i ō mātou māhunga!</strong><br>While we were walking to the forest, we saw a huge bird — it was flying above our heads!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Techniques for physical storytelling:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Body + E…ana for ongoing physical state</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;E mamae ana ōna waewae&quot; — her legs aching (still ongoing)<br>&quot;E hūhū ana tōna ngākau&quot; — her heart pounding</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Short Ka sentence to break the ongoing action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tū ia.&quot; — She stopped. (Ka = sudden completed action breaking ongoing scene)</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>i te wā e…ana + Ka for simultaneous action</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I te wā e haere ana mātou… ka kite mātou…&quot; — While we were walking… we saw…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>hūhū</strong> — to buzz / pound / vibrate (sound word from Term 1!)<br><strong>rere</strong> — to fly / flow / move swiftly<br><strong>i runga i</strong> — above / over</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E…ana sets the ongoing background. Ka punches through it with a sudden event. &quot;E omaoma ana ia… Ka tū ia.&quot; She was running… She stopped. The contrast creates drama.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Physical storytelling</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a scene using body vocabulary and E…ana together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Set the ongoing scene: <strong>E omaoma ana ngā tamariki i waho.</strong> Now add a body detail: E mamae ana ō rātou…</li><li>Break the scene with Ka: <strong>Ka tū rātou katoa.</strong> Why did they stop? Add a reason.</li><li>Use i te wā e…ana: &quot;While they were running, they saw…&quot; → <strong>I te wā e omaoma ana rātou, ka kite rātou i…</strong></li><li>Close with a body reaction: heart, eyes, or legs responding to what happened.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E hūhū ana tōna ngākau — her heart was pounding. The word hūhū is a sound word — it describes a buzzing, pulsing sound. Why might a language use a sound word to describe an emotional physical sensation like a pounding heart? What does it make you feel when you hear it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Physical scene</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short physical scene using body vocabulary and E…ana.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a three-sentence ongoing scene using E…ana for two different body states.</li><li>Add a Ka sentence that breaks the scene suddenly.</li><li>Add an i te wā e…ana sentence — something seen or heard while the scene was ongoing.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an eight-sentence physical scene — a sports moment, a scary experience, or an exciting discovery. Use at least four body part words, E…ana for ongoing states, Ka for breaking events, and at least one sound word.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō te hauora — Health conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Physical feelings · AO 1.6: Politeness · AO 2.3: Extended communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Health &amp; wellbeing; AO 1.6 — Politeness in health contexts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — At the sick bay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use body vocabulary and feelings in a real scenario — visiting the school sick bay. This is practical, important language that combines body parts, E…ana, feelings, and politeness.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaimahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaimahi</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hauora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hauora</a>:</strong> Tēnā koe. He aha tāu raruraru?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kei te mamae tōku māhunga, kei te ngongo hoki tōku kaki.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ā, he tino kino tērā. E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mamae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mamae</a> ana tōu māhunga i ēhea wā?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> I tīmata i tēnei ata. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a> noa tōku ao i ētahi wā.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a>. Kei te māuiui koe — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=okioki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">okioki</a> koe. He aha āu i kai ai mō tō parakuihi?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kāore au i kai i tēnei ata — i ngenge noa au.<br><strong>Kaimahi:</strong> Āe, ko tērā pea te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">take</a>. Me kai koe ināianei. Ka taea koe te hoki ki tōu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akomanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akomanga</a> ā te rua karaka.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Health vocabulary:<br><strong>kaimahi hauora</strong> — health worker / school nurse (kai- + hauora)<br><strong>raruraru</strong> — problem / trouble<br><strong>ngongo</strong> — tight / sore (throat)<br><strong>Ka huri noa tōku ao.</strong> — My world just spins. (dizzy)<br><strong>huri</strong> — to turn / spin<br><strong>ao</strong> — world / cloud (here: the world around me)<br><strong>i ēhea wā</strong> — at what times<br><strong>Ka mārama.</strong> — I understand / I see. (literally: it is clear)<br><strong>me okioki koe</strong> — you should rest (me = should/must — a gentle instruction)<br><strong>ko tērā pea te take</strong> — that is perhaps the reason<br><strong>Ka taea koe te hoki</strong> — You are able to go back</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me + verb = should / must do something. Me okioki koe = you should rest. Me kai koe = you should eat. Me is a gentle way to give advice or instruction — softer than a command.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sick bay roleplay</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Roleplay the sick bay scene. One person is the kaimahi hauora, one is the ākonga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaimahi asks: <strong>He aha tāu raruraru?</strong> Ākonga answers with two body complaints using Kei te mamae…</li><li>Kaimahi asks: <strong>E mamae ana tōu [body part] i ēhea wā?</strong> Ākonga answers with a time: I tīmata i…</li><li>Kaimahi uses me: <strong>Me okioki koe / me kai koe / me inu wai koe.</strong> (you should drink water)</li><li>Kaimahi closes: <strong>Ka taea koe te hoki ki tōu akomanga ā te [time] karaka.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Me okioki koe — you should rest. The word me here is much gentler than a command like &quot;Okioki!&quot; (Rest!). How does the choice of me versus a direct command change the relationship between the speaker and listener? When would you use me versus a direct instruction?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sick bay dialogue</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full sick bay dialogue with a partner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the ākonga's part: three complaint sentences using Kei te mamae, E…ana, and a time.</li><li>Write the kaimahi's part: two me sentences giving advice.</li><li>Include Ka huri noa tōku ao or another vivid body expression.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full dialogue (10 lines). Include: He aha tāu raruraru, body vocabulary, E…ana, a time expression, me sentences, Ka mārama, and Ka taea koe te hoki ā te [time].</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What possessive category are body parts in te reo Māori?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ā-category — you control them </li><li>ō-category — they are part of you and senior to your control ✅</li><li>Neither — they use a special category </li><li>It depends on the body part </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te mamae tōku māhunga.&quot; What does mamae mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>tired </li><li>sore / in pain ✅</li><li>spinning </li><li>swollen </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the main difference between &quot;Kei te kai ia&quot; and &quot;E kai ana ia&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>E kai ana emphasises the eating is ongoing and continuing; Kei te is more immediate ✅</li><li>Kei te is past tense; E…ana is present </li><li>E…ana is only used for future plans </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I te wā e mahi ana ia, ka tae mai tōna hoa.&quot; What happened while she was working?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She finished the work </li><li>Her friend arrived ✅</li><li>She fell asleep </li><li>She started eating </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E omaoma ana ia… Ka tū ia.&quot; What effect does the Ka sentence create?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It shows the running is continuing </li><li>It creates drama by breaking the ongoing action with a sudden stop ✅</li><li>It shows the running happened in the past </li><li>It describes where she ran </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;rere&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to fall </li><li>to fly / flow / move swiftly ✅</li><li>to cry </li><li>to laugh </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Me okioki koe.&quot; What does me indicate here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A question </li><li>A past event </li><li>A gentle instruction / you should do this ✅</li><li>A command as strong as E tū! </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Ka huri noa tōku ao&quot; describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The world is changing </li><li>A spinning / dizzy feeling ✅</li><li>The clouds are moving </li><li>Time passing </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Kua mutu — The perfect aspect: Kua":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kua (perfect aspect — action completed with present relevance)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Perfect aspect — Kua; AO 2.3 — Completed action with relevance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — It is done: Kua</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have I for past events, Ka for narrative, and E…ana for ongoing actions. Today we meet the final tense marker — Kua. Kua describes an action that has been completed and whose result is still relevant right now. It is the difference between &quot;I went&quot; and &quot;I have gone (and I am still gone).&quot;</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutu</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=akoranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">akoranga</a>.</strong><br>The lesson has finished. (and we are now in the after-lesson state)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tae</a> mai ia.</strong><br>She has arrived. (she is now here as a result)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a> te ao.</strong><br>The world has changed. (and remains changed)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a> au.</strong><br>I have understood / I understand now. (the understanding has arrived)<br><br><strong>Kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oti</a> i a au.</strong><br>I have finished it. (<a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=oti\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oti</a> = be completed, i a au = by me)<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=K%C4%81ore+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kāore anō</a> kia mutu.</strong><br>It has not finished yet. (kāore anō = not yet, kia = subjunctive with anō)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When to use <strong>Kua</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Something has just happened and the result matters NOW:<br>&quot;Kua tae mai ia.&quot; — She has arrived. (so she is here now — that is why it matters)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ A state that has come about:<br>&quot;Kua mārama au.&quot; — I now understand. (the understanding has arrived)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Official announcements of completion:<br>&quot;Kua mutu te hui.&quot; — The meeting has ended.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare:<br><strong>I mutu te akoranga.</strong> — The lesson ended. (past fact, no connection to now)<br><strong>Kua mutu te akoranga.</strong> — The lesson has ended. (so what comes next? — the result matters)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Negative: <strong>Kāore anō kia mutu</strong> — not yet finished<br>Question: <strong>Kua mutu?</strong> — Is it done yet?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kua = &quot;has [done] and the result is relevant now.&quot; Think of it as an announcement — something has changed state, and that new state is what matters. Kua tae mai = has arrived (and is now present).</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Completed with relevance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I describe a situation — you use Kua to express the completed state.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The class has just ended. → <strong>Kua mutu te akoranga.</strong> What happens next as a result?</li><li>You have just understood something difficult. → <strong>Kua mārama au!</strong> Use it with an exclamation.</li><li>The food has all been eaten. → <strong>Kua kainga te kai katoa.</strong> (kainga = been eaten — passive)</li><li>Ask if someone has finished: <strong>Kua oti i a koe?</strong> Answer yes: <strong>Āe, kua oti i a au.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Kua huri te ao&quot; — the world has changed. This phrase appears often in te reo Māori writing and speech, especially when reflecting on change over time. Why is Kua the right marker here rather than I or Ka? What does the choice of Kua add to the meaning of &quot;the world has changed&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Kua sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Kua for completed actions whose results matter now.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Kua sentences about things that have just happened in your classroom or school.</li><li>Write a Kāore anō kia mutu sentence about something not finished yet.</li><li>Write a Kua oti i a au sentence about something you have completed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) using Kua, I, Ka, and Kei te all in the same passage — a moment-by-moment account of arriving at school today. Use each marker at least once and make sure each one is used correctly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha ōu hiahia? — Wants, needs, and future intention":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: hiahia ki te, māku e, ka taea e au te</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Expressing wants &amp; intentions; AO 2.4 — Future intention</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What do you want? What will you do?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You can describe what is happening, what happened, and what has happened. Today we look forward — expressing what you want, what you intend to do, and what you are able to do. These are the structures of plans, goals, and dreams.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WANT TO:</strong><br><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I want to learn te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>WILL DO (first person):</strong><br><strong>Māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=horoi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">horoi</a> i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rihi</a>.</strong><br>I will wash the dishes. (māku = for me to do, e + verb = the action)<br><br><strong>WILL DO (third person):</strong><br><strong>Māna e hoko i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=miraka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">miraka</a>.</strong><br>She will buy the milk. (māna = for her to do)<br><br><strong>ABLE TO:</strong><br><strong>Ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a> i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I am able to speak te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>CANNOT:</strong><br><strong>E kore e taea e au te haere āpōpō.</strong><br>I will not be able to go tomorrow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three structures for future intention:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te [verb]</strong> = I want to [verb]</li><li><strong>Māku / Māna / Mā rātou e [verb]</strong> = I/she/they will [verb]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>(mā + person + e + verb = that person is the one who will do it)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Mā- takes the person:<br><strong>māku</strong> = for me to do<br><strong>māu</strong> = for you to do<br><strong>māna</strong> = for him/her to do<br><strong>mā mātou</strong> = for us (excl.) to do</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ka taea e au te [verb]</strong> = I am able to [verb]</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E kore e taea e au te [verb]</strong> = I am not able to [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word: <strong>rihi</strong> — dishes / plates</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Māku e [verb] = I will [verb] (I am the one who will do it). Māna e [verb] = she will [verb]. The mā- word tells you WHO is responsible for the action.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Plans and intentions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences about what you want, will do, and can do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What do you want to do after school? <strong>Kei te hiahia au ki te [verb] i muri i te kura.</strong></li><li>Assign a task: <strong>Māu e horoi i ngā rihi.</strong> You will wash the dishes. <strong>Māku e tahu i te ahi.</strong> I will light the fire.</li><li>What can you do in te reo? <strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero / pānui / tuhituhi i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>What can you NOT do yet? <strong>E kore e taea e au te [verb] ināianei — engari āpōpō…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Māku e [verb] — I am the one who will do this. This structure makes the person responsible very clear. In te ao Māori, naming who will do something is an act of commitment. How does saying &quot;māku e&quot; feel different from just saying &quot;I will&quot;? What does making yourself the named agent add?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Wants, plans, abilities</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write sentences expressing what you want, plan, and can do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three hiahia sentences about things you want to do or learn this term.</li><li>Write two māku e sentences — commit to two things you will do.</li><li>Write two Ka taea e au te sentences about things you can now do in te reo.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Term 3 goals&quot; paragraph (7 sentences) — what you want to learn, what you commit to doing, what you can already do, and what you could not do yet but will be able to by end of term. Use hiahia, māku e, Ka taea, and E kore e taea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — All tense markers together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time mastery · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana in one passage</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Full tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Extended tense-controlled writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All six tense markers in one passage</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You now have the full tense toolkit: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, and the negatives Kāore e…ana and Kāore i. Today we use all of them in a single passage — showing how a skilled te reo speaker moves through time fluidly.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kua tae mai au ki te kura i tēnei ata.</strong> (Kua — arrival complete, I am now here)<br><strong>I haere au i te Rāhina</strong> — he roa tōku huarahi. (I — specific past fact)<br><strong>E haere ana tēnā kura ki te hākinakina ā tērā wiki</strong> — ka tū tō mātou ope hei whakamau. (E…ana — ongoing plan; Ka — narrative result)<br><strong>Kei te whakarite mātou mō tērā kaupeka ināianei.</strong> (Kei te — what is happening right now)<br><strong>Kāore anō kua oti ā mātou whakarite</strong> — engari ka oti āpōpō. (Kua negative — not yet; Ka — future narrative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each marker's job in this passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kua</strong> — arrival complete (I am here NOW as a result)<br><strong>I</strong> — specific past fact with detail<br><strong>E…ana</strong> — ongoing future plan (still being planned)<br><strong>Ka</strong> — narrative move (what will happen as a result)<br><strong>Kei te</strong> — what is happening right now<br><strong>Kāore anō kua</strong> — not yet completed</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>huarahi</strong> — journey / path / road<br><strong>ope</strong> — team / group / squad<br><strong>hei whakamau</strong> — to compete against / to challenge (hei = for the purpose of, whakamau = focus on/oppose)<br><strong>whakarite</strong> — to prepare / arrange</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read any te reo passage and identify the tense marker of each sentence. Once you can identify them easily, you can use them accurately. The markers are the skeleton — everything else hangs on them.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — All markers, one story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write a story that uses all six markers deliberately. I specify which marker each sentence needs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sentence 1 — Kua: Something has just been completed with present relevance.</li><li>Sentence 2 — I: A specific past event with a time word.</li><li>Sentence 3 — E…ana + Ka: An ongoing background action interrupted by something.</li><li>Sentence 4 — Kei te: What is happening right now as a result.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have now learned six tense/aspect markers: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, and Kāore. English has twelve tenses. Te reo Māori has six markers that between them cover the same ground — but with a different underlying logic. What is the logic? What question does each marker answer?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — All six markers</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage using all six markers at least once each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Kua sentence and a Kei te sentence — make sure the difference is clear.</li><li>Write an E…ana sentence interrupted by a Ka sentence.</li><li>Write an I sentence (specific past) and a Kāore anō kua sentence (not yet complete).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a complete eight-sentence passage about a project or event at school — use every marker at least once. Label each sentence with the marker you used and why.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā whāinga — Goals and plans conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Future intention · AO 1.7: School language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Goals &amp; planning conversation; AO 2.4 — Future intention</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Planning and goal-setting</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use Kua, hiahia, māku e, and Ka taea in a conversation about goals and plans — the kind of conversation you might have with a teacher, a coach, or a friend about where you are headed.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaiwhakaako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaiwhakaako</a>:</strong> Tēnā koe. Kua mutu tō akoranga mō tēnei wāhanga — he pēhea tōu whakaaro?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kua mārama ake au ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hanganga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hanganga</a> katoa. Ka taea e au te tuhituhi i ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rerenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rerenga</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pakiaka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pakiaka</a> ināianei.<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> Ka pai! He aha āu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i ngā kupu hou o te taiao. Māku hoki e tuhituhi i ōku whakaaro i ngā rā katoa.<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> He whainga pai ērā. Ka taea e koe te kōrero māmā ake āpōpō?<br><strong>Ākonga:</strong> Āe — e kore e taea e au te kōrero māmā ake ināianei, engari ā tērā wāhanga, ka taea!<br><strong>Kaiwhakaako:</strong> Ko tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakapono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakapono</a> tōu ara. Haere tonu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases for goals and planning:<br><strong>Kua mārama ake au ki…</strong> — I now understand more about…<br><strong>rerenga pakiaka</strong> — simple sentences (rerenga = sentence, pakiaka = root/basic)<br><strong>He whainga pai ērā.</strong> — Those are good goals.<br><strong>Ka taea e koe te…</strong> — Are you able to… / You can…<br><strong>Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara.</strong> — Your belief is your path. (whakapono = belief/faith)<br><strong>Haere tonu!</strong> — Keep going! / Press on!<br><strong>māmā ake</strong> — more easily / more fluently (māmā = light/easy, ake = more)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Haere tonu! — Keep going! Tonu means &quot;still&quot; or &quot;continuously&quot; — haere tonu = keep travelling. It is one of the most encouraging things you can say in te reo. Use it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Goal-setting conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a goals conversation with a partner — one plays kaiwhakaako, one plays ākonga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>He pēhea tōu whakaaro mō tōu ako i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> Ākonga answers with Kua mārama ake au ki…</li><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>He aha āu whainga mō tērā wāhanga?</strong> Ākonga answers with hiahia and māku e.</li><li>Kaiwhakaako: <strong>Ka taea e koe te [skill] ināianei?</strong> Ākonga answers honestly — Ka taea OR E kore e taea engari…</li><li>Close: Kaiwhakaako says Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara. Haere tonu! Ākonga responds.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara — your belief is your path. This connects to the Māori concept of whakapono (faith/belief) as something that shapes your direction. How does believing you can do something create the path toward doing it? Can you think of a time your belief opened a path for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whāinga — Your goals</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and share your Term 3 goals.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three hiahia sentences about things you want to be able to do by end of Term 3.</li><li>Write two māku e sentences — things you commit to doing regularly.</li><li>Write what you can already do: Ka taea e au te… (three abilities).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full goals paragraph (8 sentences) — what you have achieved (Kua), what you can do (Ka taea), what you want (hiahia), what you commit to (māku e), what you cannot yet do (E kore e taea), and your path forward (Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kua tae mai ia.&quot; What does this sentence tell us about her arrival?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She arrived a long time ago </li><li>She arrived and as a result she is now here ✅</li><li>She is going to arrive soon </li><li>She was arriving when something interrupted her </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore anō kia mutu.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It has just finished </li><li>It will never finish </li><li>It has not finished yet ✅</li><li>It finished a long time ago </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Māna e hoko i te miraka.&quot; Who will buy the milk?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I will </li><li>You will </li><li>He/she will ✅</li><li>They will </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E kore e taea e au te haere āpōpō.&quot; What does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I do not want to go tomorrow </li><li>I went yesterday instead </li><li>I will not be able to go tomorrow ✅</li><li>I am going tomorrow reluctantly </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which marker signals a completed action whose result is relevant right now?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I </li><li>Kua ✅</li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakarite&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to compete </li><li>to arrive </li><li>to prepare / arrange ✅</li><li>to finish </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tāu whakapono tōu ara.&quot; What does whakapono mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>hard work </li><li>belief / faith ✅</li><li>goal </li><li>journey </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Haere tonu!&quot; — What does this expression encourage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Go home! </li><li>Stop what you are doing! </li><li>Keep going! / Press on! ✅</li><li>Go slowly! </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Nō hea koe? — Origin, iwi, hapū, and identity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information — iwi, hapū, maunga, awa · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity — iwi &amp; hapū; AO 2.1 — Community relationships</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Deeper identity: where you truly come from</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Week 4 of Term 1 you learned the basic pepeha — Ko [mountain] tōku maunga, Ko [river] tōku awa, Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Today we go deeper. We explore what iwi and hapū really mean, why they matter, and how to talk about community identity in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko Tūhoe tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iwi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iwi</a>.</strong><br>Tūhoe is my tribe/people.<br><br><strong>Ko Ngāti Awa tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hap%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hapū</a>.</strong><br>Ngāti Awa is my sub-tribe.<br><br><strong>Ko te Kāhui Ariki tōku whānau pani.</strong><br>The royal family is my extended family of guardians.<br><br><strong>Nō reira ko ahau ko [name], nō [place], nō [iwi].</strong><br>Therefore I am [name], from [place], of [iwi].<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> ahau nō [ancestor].</strong><br>I am a descendant of [ancestor].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identity vocabulary:<br><strong>iwi</strong> — tribe / people (literally: bones — the framework that holds a people together)<br><strong>hapū</strong> — sub-tribe / pregnant (the sub-group that gives birth to new community)<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendant / offspring<br><strong>tīpuna / tupuna</strong> — ancestor / grandparent<br><strong>whakapapa</strong> — genealogy / to layer (whaka = to make, papa = layer/foundation)<br><strong>tangata whenua</strong> — the people of the land (the indigenous people)<br><strong>manuhiri</strong> — visitor / guest<br><strong>Nō reira</strong> — therefore / and so (as a conclusion)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The full pepeha structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko [maunga] tōku maunga.</li><li>Ko [awa] tōku awa.</li><li>Ko [waka] tōku waka.</li><li>Ko [iwi] tōku iwi.</li><li>Ko [hapū] tōku hapū.</li><li>Ko [marae] tōku marae.</li><li>Ko [school] tōku kura.</li><li>Ko [name] tōku ingoa.</li><li>Nō [place] ahau.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Whakapapa literally means &quot;to layer things&quot; — it is the layering of generations, one on top of another, connecting you to your beginnings. When you say your whakapapa, you are saying where you come from — not just geographically, but in every sense.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — The full pepeha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your full pepeha together. Each student adds one line.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start: Ko [your nearest mountain or landmark] tōku maunga. Ko [your nearest river] tōku awa.</li><li>Add iwi/hapū if known: Ko [iwi] tōku iwi. Ko [hapū] tōku hapū. (If not known: Ko [school region] tōku whenua.)</li><li>Add marae and school: Ko [marae] tōku marae. Ko [school name] tōku kura.</li><li>Close: Ko [your name] tōku ingoa. Nō [your town] ahau. Nō reira ko ahau.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Iwi means &quot;bones&quot; — the framework of a people. Hapū means &quot;pregnant&quot; — the sub-group that creates new life. Why do you think Māori used these body words for social structures? What does it tell us about how Māori thought about community?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu pepeha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and practise your full pepeha.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write all nine lines of your pepeha. Research any lines you do not know.</li><li>Practise saying it aloud until it flows without looking at the page.</li><li>Say your pepeha to a partner. They listen and then say theirs.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (6 sentences) explaining what each line of your pepeha means to you personally. Why does it matter that your maunga is named? What does connecting to land in an introduction say about Māori values?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Te marae — The marae and its protocol":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Community · AO 1.6: Politeness and protocol · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Community &amp; marae; AO 1.6 — Protocol &amp; tikanga</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The marae: home of the people</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The marae is the heart of Māori community life. It is where people gather for pōhiri (formal welcomes), tangi (funerals), hui (meetings), and celebration. Today we learn the language of the marae — what the parts are called, what happens there, and how to talk about it respectfully.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Parts of the marae:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=marae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">marae</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81tea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ātea</a></strong> — the open space in front of the wharenui (sacred space for formal encounter)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharenui\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharenui</a></strong> — the meeting house (the large house where people sleep and gather)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharekai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharekai</a></strong> — dining room / food house<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wharepaku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wharepaku</a></strong> — toilet facilities (paku = small, whare paku = small house)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waharoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waharoa</a></strong> — gateway / entrance<br><br><strong>What happens at the marae:</strong><br><strong>Ka haere mātou ki te marae.</strong> We are going to the marae.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%8Dhiritia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pōhiritia</a> mātou e ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangata+whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangata whenua</a>.</strong> We were welcomed by the hosts.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whaikōrero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whaikōrero</a> ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>.</strong> The leaders gave speeches.<br><strong>Ka waiata tātou.</strong> We all sang.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Marae protocol vocabulary:<br><strong>pōhiri</strong> — formal welcome ceremony<br><strong>karanga</strong> — the calling (women's ceremonial call of welcome)<br><strong>whaikōrero</strong> — formal speech (whai = to pursue, kōrero = speech)<br><strong>haka</strong> — dance / challenge<br><strong>hongi</strong> — pressing of noses (sharing the breath of life)<br><strong>tangata whenua</strong> — hosts (the people of the land)<br><strong>manuhiri</strong> — guests / visitors<br><strong>Ka pōhiritia mātou.</strong> — We were welcomed. (passive)<br><strong>Ka whaikōrero ngā rangatira.</strong> — The leaders gave speeches.<br><strong>me noho</strong> — we should sit / must sit (protocol instruction)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: On the marae, women lead through karanga (calling) and men lead through whaikōrero (speech). Both are essential. Neither is less important. The marae is a place of equal and complementary roles.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Marae language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Practise marae vocabulary in sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Parts of marae: Point and name — wharenui, marae ātea, wharekai, waharoa.</li><li>Describe an action: <strong>Ka karanga ngā wāhine.</strong> The women called. <strong>Ka whaikōrero ngā tāne.</strong> The men spoke.</li><li>Passive: <strong>Ka pōhiritia mātou e ngā tangata whenua.</strong> We were welcomed by the hosts.</li><li>Protocol instruction: <strong>Me noho tātou ināianei.</strong> We should all sit now.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka pōhiritia mātou — we were welcomed. The passive voice here makes the welcome something that HAPPENED TO US — we received it. In te ao Māori, welcoming is a gift given by hosts to guests. How does the passive voice capture that relationship better than &quot;the hosts welcomed us&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Marae recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a marae visit — real or imagined.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write what happened using Ka sentences: Ka haere mātou… Ka pōhiritia mātou… Ka waiata tātou…</li><li>Name three parts of the marae using Ko ēnei: Ko ēnei ngā wāhanga o te marae: he wharenui, he wharekai, he marae ātea.</li><li>Write a me sentence about marae protocol: Me [action] tātou i te marae.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full marae visit recount (8 sentences) — where you went, how you were welcomed (passive), what happened (Ka sequence), what you saw (Ko ēnei list), and how you felt (emotion vocabulary).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Community and identity in extended writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 2.1: Community · AO 2.3: Extended writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity writing; AO 2.1 — Community in writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing about who you are and where you belong</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we combine the pepeha, the marae, and community vocabulary into extended writing. This is the kind of te reo writing you might produce for a school presentation, a wall display, or an end-of-year portfolio.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko Aoraki tōku maunga, ko Waitaki tōku awa.</strong><br><strong>Ko Ngāi Tahu tōku iwi, ko Ngāti Māmoe tōku hapū.</strong><br><strong>Nō Ōtautahi ahau.</strong><br><strong>Ko au ko [name].</strong><br><br>I tōku ao, he nui ngā āhuatanga pai — ko tōku whānau, ko ōku hoa, ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a>.<br>Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au: he aroha o tōku whānau, he kaha o ōku hoa, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> o tōku kura.<br>He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna nō reira he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kawenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kawenga</a> tōku — me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a>.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary for identity writing:<br><strong>e mau ana ki a au</strong> — that hold fast to me / that I hold dear<br><strong>māramatanga</strong> — understanding / enlightenment / clarity<br><strong>kawenga</strong> — responsibility / obligation / burden<br><strong>tikanga</strong> — correct practice / custom / culture<br><strong>He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna.</strong> — I am a descendant of the ancestors.<br><strong>nō reira he kawenga tōku</strong> — therefore I have a responsibility<br><strong>me tiaki au i tēnei reo</strong> — I should/must care for this language</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Structure for identity writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha (mountain, river, iwi, hapū, place, name)</li><li>What matters to you (Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au)</li><li>Your identity statement (He uri ahau nō…)</li><li>Your responsibility (Nō reira he kawenga tōku — me [action])</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo — I must care for this language. This is not just a sentence — it is a commitment. When you write it in your own pepeha, you are making te reo Māori part of your identity.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Extended identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We write an extended identity piece together, sentence by sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha: Ko [maunga] tōku maunga. Ko [awa] tōku awa. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau.</li><li>What matters: Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au: he [noun], he [noun], he [noun].</li><li>Identity statement: He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna nō reira…</li><li>Responsibility close: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao — I must care for this language, this culture, this world. Three parallel i phrases. What is the effect of that repetition? Why does saying three things in parallel make a stronger statement than just saying one?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own extended identity piece in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your full pepeha (all nine lines).</li><li>Write Ko ēnei ngā mea e mau ana ki a au — three things you hold dear.</li><li>Write your identity statement: He uri ahau nō…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full identity piece (10 sentences) — pepeha, what matters, identity statement, and responsibility. Use: Ko, He, Kua (what you have become), hiahia, and me tiaki.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He pōhiri — A welcome ceremony":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.4: Community · AO 1.6: Protocol · AO 2.1: Relationships</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal greetings &amp; welcome; AO 1.6 — Marae protocol</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Experiencing a pōhiri</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we experience the language of a pōhiri — a formal welcome ceremony. You will learn the key phrases, the structure, and what each part means. This is among the most important cultural language in te ao Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>THE &lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=KARANGA&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;KARANGA&lt;/A&gt; (call of welcome — women):</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Haere+mai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Haere mai</a>, haere mai, haere mai!</strong><br>Come, come, come! (Repeated three times — three is completion)<br><br><strong>THE &lt;B&gt;&lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=WHAIKŌRERO&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;WHAIKŌRERO&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/B&gt; (formal speech — opens and closes with):</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br><strong>Ko [mountain] tōku maunga…</strong> (pepeha lines)<br><strong>Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong><br><br><strong>THE WAIATA (song following each speech):</strong><br>After each whaikōrero, a waiata is sung by that person's group.<br><br><strong>THE &lt;B&gt;&lt;A CLASS=&quot;TE-AKA-LINK&quot; HREF=&quot;HTTPS://MAORIDICTIONARY.CO.NZ/SEARCH?KEYWORDS=HONGI&quot; TARGET=&quot;_BLANK&quot; REL=&quot;NOOPENER&quot;&gt;HONGI&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/B&gt; (greeting):</strong><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hongi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hongi</a> tātou.</strong> — We will press noses.<br><strong>Ko te hongi he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohu</a> o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a>.</strong> — The hongi is a sign of love/compassion.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pōhiri structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Karanga</strong> — women call the guests onto the marae ātea</li><li><strong>Whaikōrero</strong> — formal speeches by hosts then guests (alternating)</li><li><strong>Waiata</strong> — song after each speech</li><li><strong>Hongi</strong> — pressing of noses to seal the welcome</li><li><strong>Kai</strong> — sharing of food (the division between sacred and everyday is lifted)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the hongi, manuhiri (guests) become tangata whenua (people of the place) — the distinction ends. This is profound: welcoming someone makes them part of you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Key phrase:<br><strong>Nau mai, haere mai, tū mai, noho mai.</strong><br>Come, come, stand here, sit here. (full welcome phrase)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Haere mai × 3 — three repetitions. In te ao Māori, saying something three times is a sign of completion and sincerity. Tēnā koutou × 3 at the end of a speech. The hongi shared three times. Three is the number of fullness.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Pōhiri practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class performs a mini pōhiri — one group as tangata whenua, one as manuhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tangata whenua: Karanga — Haere mai, haere mai, haere mai! (call three times)</li><li>Tangata whenua whaikōrero: Tēnā koutou katoa. Ko [student pepeha lines]. Nō reira, tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>Manuhiri respond: Their whaikōrero — Ko [their pepeha]. Tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>Close: Ka hongi tātou. Ko te hongi he tohu o te aroha. Students share the hongi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: After the hongi, manuhiri become tangata whenua — guests become people of the place. The distinction between &quot;us&quot; and &quot;them&quot; ends. What does this say about the Māori philosophy of welcome? How is this different from how welcome is understood in other contexts you know?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whaikōrero</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own short whaikōrero to deliver in a pōhiri.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your opening: Tēnā koutou katoa.</li><li>Write your pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>Write two sentences about why you are here and what you bring: I haere mai ahau ki [reason]. Ka kawe mai ahau i [something you bring].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write and practise your full whaikōrero (10–12 sentences). Open with Tēnā koutou katoa, give your pepeha, say why you are here, what you bring, what you hope for, and close with Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;uri&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ancestor </li><li>descendant / offspring ✅</li><li>cousin </li><li>elder </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He uri ahau nō ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does tīpuna mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>children </li><li>cousins </li><li>ancestors / grandparents ✅</li><li>tribe </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the marae ātea?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The dining room </li><li>The gateway </li><li>The open space in front of the wharenui — sacred space for formal encounter ✅</li><li>The sleeping area in the meeting house </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka pōhiritia mātou e ngā tangata whenua.&quot; What grammatical feature makes this sentence notable?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It uses Ka for future tense </li><li>It uses the passive — we received the welcome from the hosts ✅</li><li>It uses an object marker i </li><li>It uses Kua for completed action </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;kawenga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ancestor </li><li>mountain </li><li>responsibility / obligation ✅</li><li>genealogy </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Me tiaki au i tēnei reo, i tēnei tikanga, i tēnei ao.&quot; What literary technique uses the three parallel i phrases?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Alliteration </li><li>Simile </li><li>Parallel structure / anaphora for emphasis ✅</li><li>Personification </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What happens after the hongi in a pōhiri?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The guests leave the marae </li><li>The manuhiri become tangata whenua — the distinction between guests and hosts ends ✅</li><li>The hosts perform a haka </li><li>The speeches begin </li></ul></li><li>Why is something said or done three times in te ao Māori protocol?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Because three people must always agree </li><li>Three repetitions signal completion and sincerity ✅</li><li>It is just a tradition with no meaning </li><li>To allow everyone to hear clearly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu hou o te kura — School vocabulary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.7: Classroom language · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: School context vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.7 — School &amp; classroom language; AO 2.3 — School communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — School life in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>AO 1.7 asks us to use and respond to classroom language. Today we build a rich school vocabulary so that more and more of your school day can happen in te reo Māori. The goal is for te reo to move from the classroom into the corridor, the playground, and the staffroom.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SCHOOL PLACES:</strong><br><strong>akomanga</strong> — classroom<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tari</a></strong> — office<br><strong>whare <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81karo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tākaro</a></strong> — gymnasium / sports room<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=papa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papa</a> tākaro</strong> — playground<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81taka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pātaka</a></strong> — library / resource room (literally: storehouse)<br><strong>kaukau</strong> — swimming pool (also: to swim — same word)<br><br><strong>SCHOOL ACTIVITIES:</strong><br><strong>Kei te ako mātou.</strong> — We are learning.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihanga</a> mātou.</strong> — We are creating/building.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangahau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangahau</a> mātou.</strong> — We are researching.<br><strong>Kei te whakaaro mātou.</strong> — We are thinking.<br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taunakitia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taunakitia</a> mātou.</strong> — We are being assessed.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>School subject names:<br><strong>pāngarau</strong> — maths<br><strong>pānui</strong> — reading<br><strong>tuhituhi</strong> — writing<br><strong>akoranga reo</strong> — language lesson<br><strong>akoranga pūtaiao</strong> — science (pūtaiao = science/technology)<br><strong>akoranga tikanga</strong> — social studies / cultural studies<br><strong>akoranga hākinakina</strong> — PE / sports lesson<br><strong>akoranga toi</strong> — art (toi = art/creativity)<br><strong>akoranga puoro</strong> — music (puoro = music)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Classroom commands (AO 1.7):<br><strong>Tū mai!</strong> — Stand up!<br><strong>Noho mai!</strong> — Sit down!<br><strong>Titiro mai!</strong> — Look this way!<br><strong>Whakarongo mai!</strong> — Listen!<br><strong>Āta whakaaro!</strong> — Think carefully!<br><strong>Mahi tahi!</strong> — Work together!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Akoranga = lesson. Put any subject after it: akoranga pāngarau (maths lesson), akoranga toi (art lesson). You can create any school subject name with this word.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School day in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your school day in te reo Māori — places, activities, subjects.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where are you? <strong>Kei te akomanga au.</strong> Kei te pātaka ngā ākonga. Kei te papa tākaro rātou.</li><li>What subject? <strong>Kei te ako mātou i te pāngarau.</strong> I te akoranga toi tātou.</li><li>Command and response: Teacher calls <strong>Whakarongo mai!</strong> — class responds by listening. Teacher says <strong>Mahi tahi!</strong> — class works in pairs.</li><li>Report: <strong>I te Rāapa, i ako mātou i te akoranga pūtaiao — i ātaahua ngā hua o ā mātou rangahau.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: If your school moved to using te reo Māori for all classroom commands — Tū mai, Noho mai, Whakarongo mai — how do you think that would change the feel of the classroom? What would students need to make that work? What would teachers need?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — School day recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your school day in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your timetable for today — each subject using akoranga [subject].</li><li>Write where you are in each lesson: Kei te [place] mātou i te [subject].</li><li>Write three things you did using past tense: I te [time], i [activity] mātou i te [subject].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full school day recount (8 sentences) — every lesson, where you were, what you did, and how you felt about each one. Use Kei te, I, Ka, and E…ana at least once each.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He akoranga pai — What makes a good lesson":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended opinions · AO 1.7: School language · AO 2.5: Evaluative language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Opinion &amp; evaluation; AO 1.7 — School context</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Evaluating and giving opinions about learning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we use evaluative language to talk about what makes learning good — what works, what doesn't, and what you prefer. This connects AO 2.3 (extended opinions) with AO 1.7 (school language).</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He akoranga pai te akoranga Rāhina nō te mea…</strong><br>Monday's lesson was a good lesson because…<br><br><strong>He pai ake te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mahi+tahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mahi tahi</a> i te mahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kotahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kotahi</a> ki a au.</strong><br>Working together is better than working alone in my opinion.<br><br><strong>Ko tāku whakaaro, he nui ake ngā hua o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a> i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuhi</a>.</strong><br>My opinion is that speaking has more benefits than writing.<br><br><strong>I uaua ki a au te [subject] nō te mea…engari kua mārama ake au.</strong><br>I found [subject] difficult because… but I now understand better.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Evaluative language in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He akoranga pai/kino tērā.</strong> — That was a good/bad lesson.<br><strong>Ko tāku whakaaro…</strong> — In my opinion / My thought is…<br><strong>I uaua ki a au…</strong> — I found it difficult… (personal evaluation)<br><strong>I ngāwari ki a au…</strong> — I found it easy… (ngāwari = easy)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te…</strong> — I am able to… (showing learning)<br><strong>He nui ake ngā hua o te X i te Y.</strong> — X has more benefits than Y.<br><strong>He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au.</strong> — X is better than Y for me.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngāwari</strong> — easy / simple<br><strong>hua</strong> — benefit / result (you've seen this before)<br><strong>mahi tahi</strong> — working together (collaborative work)<br><strong>mahi kotahi</strong> — working alone (kotahi = one/alone)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tāku whakaaro = my thought/opinion. Use this whenever you want to signal that what follows is your personal view. It is the te reo equivalent of &quot;I think&quot; or &quot;in my opinion.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Evaluate your learning</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use evaluative language to assess your own learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pick a lesson from this week: <strong>He akoranga pai tērā nō te mea…</strong> Why was it good?</li><li>Compare two ways of learning: <strong>He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi ki a au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Self-evaluate: <strong>I uaua ki a au te [skill/topic] engari kua mārama ake au nō te mea…</strong></li><li>State what you can do now: <strong>Ka taea e au te [skill] ināianei — kāore i taea e au i tērā wiki.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāku whakaaro, he nui ake ngā hua o te kōrero i te tuhi. Do you agree? Is speaking more valuable than writing for language learning? Or is it the opposite? Defend your view in te reo using He pai ake… nō te mea…</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Learning evaluation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an honest evaluation of your learning this term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write He akoranga pai/uaua tērā nō te mea about one lesson from this week.</li><li>Write He pai ake te X i te Y ki a au about two ways of learning.</li><li>Write I uaua ki a au te [topic] engari kua mārama ake au.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full learning evaluation paragraph (7 sentences) — what you have learned this term, what was hard, what was easy, what you prefer, what you can do now that you couldn't before, and what your next step is. Use evaluative language throughout.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — School, community, and identity together":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Identity · AO 1.7: School language · AO 2.1: Community · AO 2.3: Extended writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Identity in school context; AO 2.1 — School community</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — School as community: writing about your place</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we combine the school vocabulary, identity language, and community structures into extended writing about what your school means to you and your community. This is the kind of writing that goes on the wall, into a portfolio, or is shared with whānau.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko tōku kura ko [school name].</strong><br>My school is [name].<br><br><strong>He kura ātaahua tōku kura — kei reira ōku hoa, ōku kaiako, ōku mōhiotanga katoa.</strong><br>My school is a beautiful school — my friends, my teachers, my entire knowledge are there.<br><br><strong>I tōku kura, kei te ako mātou i ngā mea katoa — te reo Māori, te pāngarau, te toi, te hākinakina.</strong><br>At my school, we learn everything — te reo Māori, maths, art, sport.<br><br><strong>Ko tōku kura he wāhi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> mōku — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau.</strong><br>My school is a place of nurturing for me — my thoughts grow, my heart grows too.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New community and school vocabulary:<br><strong>mōhiotanga</strong> — knowledge / understanding (mōhio + tanga)<br><strong>wāhi tiaki</strong> — place of nurturing / safe place (wāhi = place, tiaki = guard/care)<br><strong>ka tipu</strong> — grows (Ka for narrative/ongoing)<br><strong>i tōku kura</strong> — at my school<br><strong>kei reira</strong> — there / there it is (referring back)<br><strong>ko tōku kura he…</strong> — my school is a… (He sentence about identity)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Structure for extended school writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity: Ko tōku kura ko [name].</li><li>Description: He [adjective] tōku kura nō te mea…</li><li>What happens there: I tōku kura, kei te ako mātou i…</li><li>What it means: Ko tōku kura he wāhi… mōku.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku kura he wāhi tiaki mōku — my school is a place of nurturing for me. Wāhi tiaki is a beautiful phrase — a place that guards and cares. What words would you use to describe your school as a place?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School identity writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a school identity piece together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity line: Ko tōku kura ko [school name]. Add a He description: He [adjective] tōku kura.</li><li>Community line: Ko ēnei ngā tāngata o tōku kura: he kaiako [X], he ākonga [X]…</li><li>What happens: I tōku kura kei te ako mātou i [list of subjects].</li><li>Meaning line: Ko tōku kura he wāhi [word] mōku nō te mea…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau — my thoughts grow, my heart grows too. What does it mean for a school to grow both whakaaro (thoughts/intellect) and ngākau (heart/emotion)? What makes a school do both well?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — School identity piece</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own extended school identity piece.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your school identity opening: Ko tōku kura ko [name]. He [adjective] tōku kura.</li><li>Write what happens there: Ko ēnei ngā mea pai o tōku kura… (Ko ēnei list).</li><li>Write what your school means: Ko tōku kura he wāhi [word] mōku nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full school identity piece (10 sentences) — identity, description, community, what happens, what you learn, what it means to you, and close with Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau i tōku kura.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He taumahatanga — Challenges and resilience":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Evaluative vocabulary · AO 1.6: Discussing challenges</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Resilience &amp; challenge conversation; AO 2.5 — Evaluative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about challenges and getting through them</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every learner faces challenges. Today we learn how to talk about challenges, struggles, and resilience in te reo Māori — an important set of language skills for real conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea tōu ako i tēnei wāhanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga — kāore au e mārama ana ki ngā hanganga katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko te Kua — e kore au e mōhio ana ko ēhea wā me whakamahi ai i tēnā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Āe, he uaua tēnā. He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I pātai au ki tōku kaiako — i āwhina ia i a au. Kua mārama ake au ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tōu māia! Ko tērā te ara o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81konga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ākonga</a> pai — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C4%81tai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pātai</a>, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe. Haere tonu!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Resilience vocabulary:<br><strong>taumahatanga</strong> — challenge / burden (taumaha = heavy)<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave / bold / resilient<br><strong>āwhina</strong> — help / support<br><strong>pātai</strong> — to ask a question (also: question)<br><strong>He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?</strong><br>What did you do to help yourself?<br><strong>hei āwhina i a koe</strong> — to help yourself (hei = for the purpose of)<br><strong>Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai.</strong><br>That is the path of a good student.<br><strong>me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro</strong><br>must ask, must work, must think carefully<br><strong>e kore au e mōhio ana ko ēhea wā me whakamahi ai</strong><br>I don't know which times I should use it</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro — three me instructions for a good learner. Ask, work, think carefully. These three together describe almost everything it takes to learn well in any language.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Resilience conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a challenge you have faced and how you got through it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe i tēnei wāhanga?</strong> Answer honestly.</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha āu i mahi ai hei āwhina i a koe?</strong> Answer with what you did: I pātai au ki… / I mahi au… / I āta whakaaro au.</li><li>I confirm your progress: <strong>Kua mārama ake koe ināianei.</strong> You agree or add more.</li><li>Close with the ākonga pai formula: <strong>Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai — me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tērā te ara o te ākonga pai — me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro. Three things: ask, work, think carefully. Is there anything missing from this list? What would YOU add as a fourth quality of a good learner? Can you say it in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu taumahatanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a challenge you faced and how you got through it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name the challenge: He [noun] tōku taumahatanga — I uaua ki a au te [topic/skill].</li><li>Describe what you did: I pātai au ki… / I mahi au… / I āta whakaaro au.</li><li>Show your progress: Kua mārama ake au ināianei nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full resilience account (8 sentences) — name the challenge, when it started, how it felt (emotion vocabulary), what you did about it (three me instructions), who helped, what changed, and how you feel now. Close with Haere tonu!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;pātaka&quot; mean in a school context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>gymnasium </li><li>playground </li><li>library / resource room (storehouse) ✅</li><li>office </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kei te rangahau mātou.&quot; What activity are they doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>creating / building </li><li>thinking </li><li>researching ✅</li><li>being assessed </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;ngāwari&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>difficult </li><li>easy / simple ✅</li><li>interesting </li><li>boring </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi ki a au.&quot; What is being compared?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Two different school subjects </li><li>Working together versus working alone ✅</li><li>Two different teachers </li><li>Morning lessons versus afternoon lessons </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;wāhi tiaki&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a place to play </li><li>a place of learning </li><li>a place of nurturing / safe place ✅</li><li>a place to eat </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka tipu ōku whakaaro, ka tipu hoki tōku ngākau.&quot; What two things are growing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The student's height and their strength </li><li>Knowledge/thoughts and heart/emotion ✅</li><li>The school and the community </li><li>Friends and teachers </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;āwhina&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>burden </li><li>ask a question </li><li>help / support ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Me pātai, me mahi, me āta whakaaro.&quot; What are the three qualities of a good learner?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Listen, write, remember </li><li>Ask, work, think carefully ✅</li><li>Read, speak, practise </li><li>Study, sleep, repeat </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā kupu āhua — Adverbs of degree and intensity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptive language · Grammar Level 1–2: Degree adverbs tino, āhua, noa, rawa atu, atu, mai</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Degree adverbs &amp; intensity; AO 2.3 — Expressive intensity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — How much, how intense, how degree</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You know tino (very) and ake/rawa atu (more/most). Today we look at the full range of degree and intensity words in te reo Māori — the words that control how strong a statement feels.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>tino</strong> — very / truly<br><strong>He tino pai tēnei.</strong> — This is very good.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhua</a></strong> — somewhat / quite / rather / quite / rather<br><strong>He āhua uaua tēnei.</strong> — This is quite difficult.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=noa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noa</a></strong> — just / merely / freely (reduces intensity or shows unconstrained action) / freely (reduces intensity or shows unconstrained action)<br><strong>Ka haere noa ia.</strong> — She just went / she wandered freely.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tonu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tonu</a></strong> — still / continuously / right (emphasises continuity) / right (emphasises continuity)<br><strong>Kei te mahi tonu ia.</strong> — She is still working.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">anō</a></strong> — again / still / also (adds repetition or equivalence) / also (adds repetition or equivalence)<br><strong>Ka haere anō mātou.</strong> — We will go again.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%93\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kē</a></strong> — indeed / actually / on the contrary (emphasises or corrects) / on the contrary (emphasises or corrects)<br><strong>He pai kē tēnei!</strong> — This is actually really good! / This is even better!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The degree adverb toolkit:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>tino</strong> [adjective/verb] = very [adj]<br><strong>āhua</strong> [adjective] = somewhat/quite [adj]<br><strong>[verb] noa</strong> = just [verb] / freely [verb]<br><strong>[verb] tonu</strong> = still [verb] / continuously [verb]<br><strong>[verb] anō</strong> = [verb] again<br><strong>[adjective/verb] kē</strong> = actually [adj] / on the contrary<br><strong>[verb] atu / mai</strong> = [verb] away from me / toward me</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Examples:<br><strong>Ka kai noa ia.</strong> — She just eats (freely, casually).<br><strong>He pai tonu tēnei.</strong> — This is still good / continuously good.<br><strong>Ka hoki anō ia.</strong> — She came back again.<br><strong>He pai kē atu tēnei i tērā!</strong> — This is even better than that!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wani\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wani</a> kē!</strong> — That is amazing! (wani = excellent, kē = indeed)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kē adds a &quot;contrary to expectations&quot; feel — &quot;He pai kē!&quot; means &quot;it is actually good!&quot; (you might not have expected it). Ka wani kē! = That is amazing! (even more than expected). Use kē when something surprises you positively.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Degree challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence — you add a degree adverb and say how the meaning changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai tēnei.&quot; Add tino → <strong>He tino pai tēnei.</strong> Add āhua → <strong>He āhua pai tēnei.</strong> What changed?</li><li>&quot;Kei te mahi ia.&quot; Add tonu → <strong>Kei te mahi tonu ia.</strong> What does tonu add?</li><li>&quot;Ka haere ia.&quot; Add noa → <strong>Ka haere noa ia.</strong> Add anō → <strong>Ka haere anō ia.</strong> Different meanings?</li><li>Express surprise: use kē — &quot;This is actually really delicious!&quot; → <strong>He reka kē tēnei!</strong> (reka = delicious)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka haere noa ia (she just wanders freely) vs Ka haere tonu ia (she keeps going continuously). Both are about ongoing movement but with completely different feelings. What is the emotional difference? In what situation would you use each?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Degree sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use degree adverbs to add intensity and nuance to your sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using tino, āhua, and noa — one each.</li><li>Write two sentences using tonu and anō — show the difference.</li><li>Write a kē sentence that expresses pleasant surprise: <strong>He [adjective] kē tēnei!</strong></li><li>✦ Bonus: Rewrite five plain sentences from your previous work by adding degree adverbs. Read the original and the new version aloud — notice how much more expressive the degree version sounds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He aha te tikanga? — Explaining meaning and custom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended explanation · AO 1.4: Cultural connection · Grammar Level 1–2: Explaining meaning structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Explanation &amp; meaning; AO 1.4 — Cultural meaning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Explaining what things mean</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Being able to EXPLAIN things in te reo — what a word means, why a custom exists, what a phrase does — is a sign of true fluency. Today we learn the structures for giving explanations and definitions in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a> o tēnei kupu?</strong><br>What is the meaning of this word?<br><br><strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;aroha&quot; ko te…</strong><br>The meaning of &quot;aroha&quot; is…<br><br><strong>Ko te tikanga o te hongi ko te tiaki i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manawa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manawa</a> ora.</strong><br>The meaning of the hongi is the keeping of the breath of life.<br><br><strong>Nā reira, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%ABia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kīia</a> ko &quot;aroha&quot; tērā.</strong><br>Therefore, that is called &quot;aroha&quot;.<br><br><strong>He mea nui te [custom] nō te mea…</strong><br>The [custom] is an important thing because…</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation structures:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ko te tikanga o [word/thing] ko te [explanation].</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The meaning of [word] is [explanation].</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>He mea nui/pai/tapu [custom] nō te mea…</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>[Custom] is important/good/sacred because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Nā reira, ka kīia ko [name] tērā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Therefore, that thing is called [name].</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ko tā [word] mahi ko te [function].</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The function of [word] is [function].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tikanga</strong> — meaning / custom / correct practice<br><strong>manawa ora</strong> — breath of life (manawa = breath, ora = life/wellbeing)<br><strong>tapu</strong> — sacred / restricted / forbidden<br><strong>noa</strong> — free from tapu / ordinary / unrestricted (also: just/freely)<br><strong>ka kīia</strong> — is called / is named (passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te tikanga o [X] ko te [explanation]. This is the te reo pattern for defining something. Master this and you can explain any word or concept in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Define and explain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the explanation structures to define te reo words and cultural concepts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define a word: <strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;whakapapa&quot; ko te raupapa o ngā tīpuna.</strong> (raupapa = sequence/ordering)</li><li>Explain a custom: <strong>He mea nui te karanga nō te mea ko ia te reo o ngā wāhine ki ngā manuhiri.</strong></li><li>Use the kīia structure: <strong>Ko te wāhi e noho ai ngā tāngata i ngā hui, ka kīia ko &quot;wharenui&quot; tērā.</strong></li><li>Define a term from this lesson: <strong>Ko te tikanga o &quot;tapu&quot; ko te mea kua whakanoa rānei — he mea whakamōhio e ai ki te tikanga Māori.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te tikanga o &quot;manawa ora&quot; ko te tiaki i te mauri o te tangata — the meaning of &quot;breath of life&quot; is the preservation of a person's life force. The hongi shares this breath. What does this tell us about how Māori understood human connection and greeting? Is there anything like this in other cultures you know?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Explain and define</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write explanations of te reo words and cultural practices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko te tikanga o [word] ko te [explanation] for three te reo words you know.</li><li>Write He mea nui [custom] nō te mea for one cultural practice.</li><li>Write a ka kīia sentence: define something by what it is called.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;te reo glossary entry&quot; for five terms from this term — each entry with Ko te tikanga o… ko te…, He mea nui/pai/tapu… nō te mea…, and one example sentence. This could go on a classroom wall.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Formal and informal register":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 2.3: Register · Grammar Level 1–2: Formal vs informal language choices</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.6 — Formal &amp; informal register; AO 2.3 — Register-appropriate language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Knowing when to be formal and when to be casual</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Good language users know that how you speak changes depending on who you are with and where you are. The same idea can be said formally (at a pōhiri) or casually (with your friends). Today we practise both registers in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CASUAL (with friends, in class):</strong><br><strong>Kia ora! He aha tōu ingoa?</strong> — Hey! What's your name?<br><strong>Ka pai tāu!</strong> — Nice one! / Good on you!<br><strong>E haere ana koe ki hea?</strong> — Where are you going?<br><strong>Āe noa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iho</a>!</strong> — Sure, no worries!<br><br><strong>FORMAL (pōhiri, meeting, addressing elders):</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koe / Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> — Greetings (formal).<br><strong>Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau.</strong> (full pepeha)<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rawe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rawe</a> tāu i mahi ai.</strong> — What you have done is excellent.<br><strong>He nui tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koutou.</strong> — My gratitude to you all is great.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Register markers in te reo Māori:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>FORMAL signals:<br>→ Use full pepeha rather than just your name<br>→ Tēnā koe/kōrua/koutou rather than Kia ora<br>→ He nui tōku mihi ki a koe (formal gratitude)<br>→ Full sentence structures, no shortcuts<br>→ Ka rawe, Ka tino pai (formal praise)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>CASUAL signals:<br>→ Kia ora (friendly, warm)<br>→ Ka pai! (casual praise)<br>→ Shorter sentences<br>→ Direct questions without elaborate framing<br>→ Āe noa iho (sure, no problem)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New formal phrases:<br><strong>He nui tōku mihi ki a koe.</strong> — My gratitude to you is great.<br><strong>Ka rawe tāu i mahi ai.</strong> — What you have done is excellent.<br><strong>Ka ārahina tātou e koe.</strong> — You have led us. (passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Register is about relationship and context, not about one being &quot;better&quot; than the other. Casual with friends shows warmth. Formal with elders shows respect. Knowing which to use is wisdom.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Switch the register</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a casual sentence — you make it formal. Then I give you a formal one — you make it casual.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Casual: &quot;Kia ora! He aha tōu ingoa?&quot; → Formal: <strong>Tēnā koe. Ko wai tō ingoa?</strong></li><li>Formal: &quot;He nui tōku mihi ki a koutou.&quot; → Casual: <strong>Kia ora koutou — ka nui ōku whakaaro ki a koutou!</strong></li><li>Casual: &quot;Ka pai tāu!&quot; → Formal: <strong>Ka rawe tāu i mahi ai. He mea nui tērā.</strong></li><li>Now write your own pair — one casual, one formal — about the same situation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Āe noa iho — sure, no worries. Noa here makes it casual and easy. He nui tōku mihi ki a koe — my gratitude is great. The formal version uses nui (big) to show depth. The same idea — I'm thankful — expressed at completely different levels. What would happen if you used the formal phrase with a close friend? The casual one with an elder?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Register practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the same content in two different registers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a casual greeting to a friend and a formal greeting to a visitor — different words, same warmth.</li><li>Write a casual &quot;well done&quot; and a formal &quot;well done&quot; — different register, same meaning.</li><li>Write one sentence from your life in casual te reo, then rewrite it formally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short speech (6 sentences) as if you were welcoming a visitor to your school — formal register throughout. Then rewrite it as something you would say to a friend visiting — casual register. Read both aloud and notice how different they feel.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero ā-reo — A language conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended conversation · AO 1.4: Cultural connection · AO 2.5: Language vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Language &amp; learning conversation; AO 1.4 — Cultural identity through language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about language itself</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a meta-conversation — we use te reo Māori to talk about te reo Māori. This is one of the highest-level language activities: using a language to reflect on itself, its beauty, its importance, and what it means to learn it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakaaro mō te ako i te reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tāku whakaaro, he taonga nui rawa atu te reo Māori — he reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rangatira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rangatira</a>, he reo ātaahua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā hanganga o te tense — Ko Kua, Ko Ka — engari kua mārama ake au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tēnei akoranga — ko tā mātou mahi tahi i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81whina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āwhina</a>. Ka taea e au te kōrero māmā ake ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu e hiahia ana ki te ako āpōpō?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te kōrero māmā ake me tōku whānau — ko tērā tōku moemoeā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary for language conversation:<br><strong>ngā uauatanga</strong> — the difficulties (plural of uauatanga)<br><strong>i pā mai ki a koe</strong> — that came to you / that you encountered (pā = touch/affect)<br><strong>tā mātou mahi tahi</strong> — our working together (tā mātou = our ā-category thing)<br><strong>māmā ake</strong> — more fluently / more easily<br><strong>moemoeā</strong> — dream / aspiration<br><strong>He reo rangatira.</strong> — It is a chiefly language.<br><strong>ko tērā tōku moemoeā</strong> — that is my dream/aspiration</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Asking about language learning:<br><strong>He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?</strong><br>What difficulties did you encounter?<br><strong>He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?</strong><br>What helped you?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tērā tōku moemoeā — that is my dream. Moemoeā means both &quot;dream&quot; (sleeping) and &quot;aspiration&quot; (waking dream). Using it for a learning goal connects your aspiration to something deep and important.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Language conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about te reo Māori and what it means to learn it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: <strong>He aha tō whakaaro mō te ako i te reo Māori?</strong> Answer with Ko tāku whakaaro…</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?</strong> Answer honestly with I uaua ki a au…</li><li>I ask: <strong>He aha te mea i āwhina i a koe?</strong> Answer with what helped: Ko tēnei akoranga / ko tōku hoa / ko…</li><li>I ask: <strong>Ko ēhea āu moemoeā mō tō reo Māori?</strong> Answer with Kei te hiahia au ki te… Ko tērā tōku moemoeā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā ko te kōrero māmā me tōku whānau — my dream is to speak fluently with my family. Why might speaking te reo with your family feel like the ultimate goal? What does it mean when a language lives at home, not just at school?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu moemoeā</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your te reo Māori journey and dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko tāku whakaaro mō te reo Māori: (your opinion about te reo).</li><li>Write the difficulties you encountered: I uaua ki a au… engari…</li><li>Write what helped: Ko [person/thing] i āwhina i a au.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full language reflection (8 sentences) — your opinion of te reo Māori, what you found hard, what helped, what you can do now (Ka taea e au te…), and your dream for your te reo (Ko tērā tōku moemoeā). Read it to a partner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka haere noa ia.&quot; What does &quot;noa&quot; add to this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is going urgently </li><li>She just goes / wanders freely, casually ✅</li><li>She is going again </li><li>She is still going </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He pai kē tēnei!&quot; What does &quot;kē&quot; signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>This is somewhat good </li><li>This is not good </li><li>This is actually really good / even better than expected ✅</li><li>This is the same as before </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te tikanga o te hongi ko te tiaki i te manawa ora.&quot; What does manawa ora mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the meeting house </li><li>the breath of life ✅</li><li>the welcome ceremony </li><li>the gathering of people </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;tapu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>ordinary / unrestricted </li><li>wrong / incorrect </li><li>sacred / restricted / forbidden ✅</li><li>beautiful </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which phrase signals a FORMAL register for expressing gratitude?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka pai! </li><li>Āe noa iho! </li><li>He nui tōku mihi ki a koe. ✅</li><li>Kia ora! </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;Āe noa iho!&quot; express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I strongly agree </li><li>I disagree completely </li><li>Sure, no worries! (casual agreement) ✅</li><li>That is very difficult </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;moemoeā&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge / difficulty </li><li>the past </li><li>dream / aspiration ✅</li><li>hard work </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He aha ngā uauatanga i pā mai ki a koe?&quot; What is this question asking about?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Where you went </li><li>What you like most </li><li>What difficulties you encountered ✅</li><li>Who helped you </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Ngā mahi toi — Art, music, and creativity":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 2.3: Creative expression · AO 1.4: Cultural arts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Arts &amp; creative vocabulary; AO 1.4 — Cultural arts connection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The arts in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Art, music, dance, and craft are central to te ao Māori — they are not extras but essentials. Today we build vocabulary for the arts and creative expression so you can talk about what you make, perform, and love in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toi</a></strong> — art / creativity / expertise<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakairo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakairo</a></strong> — carving (whakairo = to carve / engrave)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raranga</a></strong> — weaving (raranga = to weave)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81+moko\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tā moko</a></strong> — tā moko / traditional tattoo<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puoro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puoro</a></strong> — music (puoro = music / musical instrument)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kori\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kori</a></strong> — dance / movement arts (kori = movement)<br><strong>toi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ataata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ataata</a></strong> — film / visual arts (ataata = shadow / film)<br><strong>haka</strong> — haka (posture dance / ceremonial challenge)<br><strong>waiata</strong> — song (also: to sing)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=poi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">poi</a></strong> — poi (ball on string, used in performance)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Arts vocabulary in sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka whakairo ia i ngā mahi toi.</strong><br>She carves artworks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He tohunga raranga tōna māmā.</strong><br>Her mother is an expert weaver. (tohunga = expert)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te ako au i te haka.</strong><br>I am learning haka.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te waiata tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu.</strong><br>Singing is my absolute favourite art form.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka kaoa e ia ngā poi.</strong><br>She swings the poi. (kaoa = swing / wave)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tohunga</strong> — expert / skilled person / specialist<br><strong>mahi toi</strong> — artwork / creative work<br><strong>ahua</strong> — image / appearance / likeness<br><strong>kaoa</strong> — swing / wave / rotate</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Toi means both &quot;art&quot; and &quot;expertise&quot; in te reo Māori. Tohunga (expert) comes from the same root. In te ao Māori, art and skill are the same thing — to make something beautiful is to demonstrate mastery.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts vocabulary</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use arts vocabulary in sentences about real creative activities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite art form: Ko tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu ko [art form] nō te mea…</li><li>Describe a skill: He tohunga [art form] tōku [family member/friend].</li><li>Past tense: what did you make or perform? I [verb] au i [art form/object].</li><li>Future intention: Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te [art form]. Māku e [action].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Toi means both art AND expertise. In English, we separate these — &quot;art&quot; and &quot;skill&quot; feel like different things. In te ao Māori, they are the same word. What does this tell us about how Māori understood creative practice? Can you think of examples where art and skill really ARE inseparable?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your creative life using arts vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences about arts activities using three different toi words.</li><li>Write a He tohunga sentence about someone you know who is skilled in an art form.</li><li>Write a past tense sentence about something you made or performed.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (7 sentences) about a creative project you have done or would like to do. Include: the art form, who it involved (pronouns), what you did (Ka sequence), how it felt (emotion vocabulary), and what it meant (He mea nui/taonga tēnei nō te mea…).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He kōrero mō te haka — The language of haka":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.5: Expressive vocabulary · AO 2.3: Extended cultural explanation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Haka &amp; cultural identity; AO 2.5 — Haka vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Understanding and talking about haka</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Haka is one of the most recognised Māori art forms in the world — but it is often misunderstood. Today we learn how to TALK about haka in te reo Māori, what it means, and when it is used.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=haka\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">haka</a> he mahi toi nō te reo Māori.</strong><br>Haka is an art form of te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>He haka mō ngā wā katoa — mō te hari, mō te pōuri, mō te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakataetae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataetae</a>.</strong><br>There is a haka for every occasion — for joy, for sadness, for competition.<br><br><strong>Ko &quot;Ka Mate&quot; tētahi haka rongonui o Aotearoa.</strong><br>&quot;Ka Mate&quot; is a famous haka of New Zealand.<br><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohu</a> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aroha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aroha</a> te haka — he tohu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaha</a> hoki.</strong><br>Haka is a sign of love — it is also a sign of strength.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Haka vocabulary:<br><strong>rongonui</strong> — famous / well-known<br><strong>whakataetae</strong> — competition<br><strong>tohu</strong> — sign / symbol / marker<br><strong>kaha</strong> — strength / powerful (also: hard/strongly as adverb)<br><strong>ngā wā katoa</strong> — every occasion / all times<br><strong>He haka mō te tangi.</strong> — A haka for a funeral/mourning.<br><strong>He haka mō te pōhiri.</strong> — A haka for a welcome.<br><strong>He haka mō te whakataetae.</strong> — A haka for competition.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Parts of haka:<br><strong>kūaha</strong> — opening<br><strong>whakakapi</strong> — conclusion<br><strong>reo</strong> — voice / words<br><strong>ātea</strong> — space / arena<br><strong>ngākau</strong> — heart / spirit (must be in the performance)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He tohu aroha te haka — haka is a sign of love. This surprises many people who see only the fierce exterior. But the fierce face and the deep love are not opposites in te ao Māori — they are two expressions of the same passion.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Haka conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Discuss haka using today's vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define haka: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te [explanation]. Use Ko te tikanga structure from last week.</li><li>Give occasions: He haka mō te [occasion] — list three occasions when haka is performed.</li><li>Express what haka means: He tohu [word] te haka. He tohu [word] hoki.</li><li>Your experience: Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te haka / I ako au i te haka i [time].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tohu aroha te haka — haka is a sign of love — he tohu kaha hoki — and a sign of strength. How can love and strength be expressed in the same action? Can you think of other examples in life where love and strength are not opposites but one and the same?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Haka explanation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an explanation of haka in te reo Māori — for someone who does not know what it is.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Define haka: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te…</li><li>Give three occasions: He haka mō te [X], he haka mō te [Y], he haka mō te [Z].</li><li>Explain the meaning: He tohu [noun] te haka nō te mea…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full explanation of haka (8 sentences) that could go in a school brochure or wall display. Cover: what it is, when it is done, what it means, why it matters to Māori culture, and what it means to you personally.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Creative and cultural writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Creative expression · AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.3: Extended creative writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Creative writing; AO 1.4 — Cultural expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing that is alive with culture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most powerful te reo writing does two things at once — it says something in te reo AND it says something through te reo, using the language itself as a window into te ao Māori. Today we write with that double purpose.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">An example of culturally rich writing:<br><br><strong>Ko te haka ko tōku reo — he reo ō-roto, he reo ō-waho.</strong><br>Haka is my language — an inward language, an outward language.<br><br><strong>Ka ara ōku ringa ki runga — ko tērā he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>.</strong><br>My arms rise — that is a greeting to the ancestors.<br><br><strong>Ka kōrero ōku waewae — ka tangi ōku karu.</strong><br>My feet speak — my eyes cry.<br><br><strong>Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou — ki ngā tīpuna, ki te ao <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81rama\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mārama</a>.</strong><br>Haka is my path to them — to the ancestors, to the world of light.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Techniques for culturally rich writing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use body + action to carry cultural meaning</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka ara ōku ringa ki runga&quot; — arms rising as a greeting</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Personify natural/cultural elements</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka kōrero ōku waewae&quot; — feet speak</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Connect personal action to ancestral meaning</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ko tērā he mihi ki ngā tīpuna — that is a greeting to the ancestors</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Use Ko to make identity statements about art</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrases:<br><strong>he reo ō-roto</strong> — an inner language<br><strong>he reo ō-waho</strong> — an outer language<br><strong>ao mārama</strong> — world of light (the realm of the living or of enlightenment)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te haka tōku ara ki a rātou — ki ngā tīpuna. The art form is a pathway. What art form is YOUR pathway — to understanding, to connection, to something beyond words? Can you say that in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Culturally rich writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses an art form as a window into identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with a Ko statement about the art: Ko te [art form] ko tōku [what it is to you].</li><li>Describe a physical action: Ka [body action] ōku [body part] — ko tērā he [meaning].</li><li>Connect to the ancestors or community: Ko tērā tōku ara ki [who/where].</li><li>Close with a He identity statement: He [quality] te [art form] — he [quality] hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te haka ko tōku reo — haka is my language. What does it mean to say an art form IS your language? What can haka say that words cannot? Is there an art form that speaks for you when words fail?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Creative cultural writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage about an art form that matters to you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ko identity statement: Ko te [art form] ko tōku [meaning to you].</li><li>Write two body-action sentences that carry cultural meaning.</li><li>Connect to something beyond yourself — ancestors, community, nature.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full piece (8 sentences) about an art form that matters to you. Use: Ko for identity, body vocabulary for action, emotional vocabulary, cultural connection, personification, and close with Ko tērā tōku ara ki…</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō ngā mahi toi — Arts conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Arts vocabulary · AO 1.4: Cultural arts · AO 2.3: Extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Arts conversation; AO 1.4 — Arts &amp; identity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A conversation about creative life</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a full conversation about art, music, and creativity — drawing on everything from this week and the whole term.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi e hiahia ana koe ki te ako?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te hiahia au ki te ako i te haka me te waiata — he mahi toi ātaahua ērā ki a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu mahi toi ināianei?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te ako au i te raranga. He mea uaua engari he tino ātaahua ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hua</a>!<br><strong>A:</strong> He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tohunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tohunga</a> koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kāore anō — he ākonga noa iho au ināianei! Engari āpōpō, ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=raranga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">raranga</a> i ngā āhua rerekē.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu māia! Ko ēhea ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu mōu mō tēnei mahi toi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he mārie te noho me te raranga, ka mārama hoki tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puta</a> mai.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New conversation vocabulary:<br><strong>Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi…?</strong> — Which art forms…?<br><strong>he mahi toi ātaahua ērā ki a au</strong> — those are beautiful art forms to me<br><strong>ngā hua</strong> — the results / the outcomes<br><strong>he ākonga noa iho au</strong> — I am just a learner (noa = just/merely, iho = downward particle, emphasising humility)<br><strong>āhua rerekē</strong> — different forms / various designs<br><strong>āhuatanga pai rawa atu</strong> — absolute best aspects / features<br><strong>ka puta mai</strong> — that emerge / that come forth (puta = emerge)<br><strong>ka mārama tōku ngākau</strong> — my heart becomes clear/enlightened</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He ākonga noa iho au — I am just a learner. The noa iho shows humility — making yourself small in a gracious way. In te ao Māori, calling yourself just a learner is not weakness — it is the correct relationship to knowledge.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full conversation about your creative life in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: Ko ēhea ngā mahi toi e hiahia ana koe ki te ako? Answer with your arts interests.</li><li>I ask: He aha tōu mahi toi ināianei? Answer with what you do now.</li><li>I ask: He tohunga koe? Answer humbly — He ākonga noa iho au / Kāore anō.</li><li>I ask: Ko ēhea ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu mōu? Answer with Ko ēnei: a list of what you love about it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He ākonga noa iho au — I am just a learner. And ka mārama tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka puta mai — my heart becomes clear at the forms that emerge. What does it mean for your heart to become clear through making something? Have you ever felt that? What were you doing?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts identity</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your creative life and what it means to you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write which art forms you love: Ko ēnei ōku mahi toi: he [X], he [Y], he [Z].</li><li>Write your current skill level: He ākonga noa iho au / He āhua tohunga au i te [art form].</li><li>Write what you love about your favourite art form: Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu: he…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full arts identity paragraph (8 sentences). Include: which arts you love, your current level, what you love about it, one Ko statement about what the art means to you, a Ka taea sentence about what you can do, and a moemoeā about where you want to go.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;tohunga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>student / learner </li><li>expert / skilled person ✅</li><li>teacher </li><li>artist </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku mahi toi pai rawa atu ko te waiata.&quot; What does waiata mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>carving </li><li>weaving </li><li>song / singing ✅</li><li>poi </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;rongonui&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>sacred </li><li>famous / well-known ✅</li><li>powerful </li><li>ancient </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He tohu aroha te haka — he tohu kaha hoki.&quot; What two things is haka a sign of?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>anger and power </li><li>sadness and strength </li><li>love and strength ✅</li><li>tradition and sport </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te haka ko tōku reo.&quot; What does this Ko statement express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Haka and language are different things </li><li>Haka is the speaker's language — their way of expressing themselves ✅</li><li>Te reo Māori is a form of haka </li><li>The speaker cannot speak te reo </li></ul></li><li>What is &quot;ao mārama&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a dark night </li><li>the world of light / realm of enlightenment ✅</li><li>the ocean </li><li>a rainy day </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He ākonga noa iho au.&quot; What does this expression convey?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I am an expert </li><li>I am refusing to learn </li><li>I am just a learner — humble acknowledgement of learning status ✅</li><li>I do not like learning </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka mārama tōku ngākau i ngā āhua ka puta mai.&quot; What does &quot;ka puta mai&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that disappear </li><li>that are ancient </li><li>that emerge / that come forth ✅</li><li>that are difficult </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō te taiao — Environment and kaitiakitanga":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.4: Natural world · AO 2.3: Extended environmental language</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Kaitiakitanga &amp; environment; AO 2.4 — Environmental vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Guardianship of the natural world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kaitiakitanga — guardianship of the natural environment — is one of the most important concepts in te ao Māori and in modern New Zealand. Today we build the vocabulary to talk about the environment, what threatens it, and what guardianship means in practice.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiakitanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiakitanga</a></strong> — environmental guardianship / stewardship / stewardship<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiaki</a></strong> — guardian / protector (kai- + tiaki) / protector (kai- + tiaki)<br><strong>taiao</strong> — natural world / environment<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=papat%C5%AB%C4%81nuku\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">papatūānuku</a></strong> — the earth / ground (Papa = earth mother) / ground (Papa = earth mother)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangaroa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangaroa</a></strong> — the sea / Tangaroa (atua of the sea) / Tangaroa (atua of the sea)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%81ne\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tāne</a></strong> — the forest / Tāne (atua of the forest and birds) / Tāne (atua of the forest and birds)<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toit%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toitū</a></strong> — sustainable / enduring / intact / enduring / intact<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=paru\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">paru</a></strong> — polluted / dirty / muddy / dirty / muddy<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mauri</a></strong> — life force / vitality / vitality<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ora</a></strong> — well / alive / thriving / thriving</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Environmental language in sentences:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao.</strong><br>We are the guardians of this natural world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kāore e toitū ana te ao ināianei — he paru ngā awa, he ngaro ngā manu.</strong><br>The world is not sustainable right now — the rivers are polluted, the birds are disappearing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o ngā awa, o ngā ngahere, o ngā maunga.</strong><br>We must protect the life force of the rivers, forests, and mountains.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka ora te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taiao</a>, ka ora tātou.</strong><br>When the environment thrives, we thrive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ngaro</strong> — lost / disappeared / extinct<br><strong>tiakina</strong> — be protected (passive of tiaki)<br><strong>toitū te taiao</strong> — a sustainable/enduring environment</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — when the environment is well, we are well. This sentence sums up kaitiakitanga perfectly. The earth's wellbeing and ours are not separate. Learn this sentence cold.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Environmental language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use environmental vocabulary in sentences about the real world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State our responsibility: <strong>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao.</strong> Add a reason: nō te mea…</li><li>Name a problem: <strong>Kāore e toitū ana te [environmental feature] ināianei nō te mea…</strong></li><li>Use me for responsibility: <strong>Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o [feature].</strong></li><li>Close with the core principle: <strong>Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</strong> Discuss what this means.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — the wellbeing of the environment is the wellbeing of people. In English we often talk about &quot;protecting the environment&quot; as if it is separate from us. How does the te reo sentence change that framing? What does it say about the relationship between people and the natural world?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Kaitiakitanga writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about environmental guardianship in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your responsibility statement: Ko au tētahi kaitiaki o te taiao nō te mea…</li><li>Name one environmental issue using Kāore e toitū ana… nō te mea…</li><li>Write what you will do: Māku e [action] hei tiaki i [environmental feature].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a kaitiakitanga statement (8 sentences) — what kaitiakitanga means, what the problems are, what must be done (me sentences), what you personally commit to (māku e), and close with Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu o te ture — Rights, rules, and responsibilities":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 1.6: Social conventions · Grammar Level 1–2: Me, kaua, tikanga in social contexts</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Rights &amp; responsibilities; AO 1.6 — Social conventions &amp; rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Rights, rules, and what we must do</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Language for rights and responsibilities is important for full participation in society. Today we build the vocabulary for rules, what is allowed, what must be done, and what we have the right to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a></strong> — a rule / a correct practice / a right<br><strong>He tikanga tōku ki te kōrero.</strong><br>It is my right to speak.<br><br><strong>Me mahi tātou i tēnei.</strong> — We must do this.<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Kaua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kaua</a> e mahi i tēnā.</strong> — Do not do that. (kaua = do not — negative command)<br><strong>E <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81hei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āhei</a> ana koe ki te…</strong> — You are permitted/able to…<br><strong>Kāore e āhei ana koe ki te…</strong> — You are not permitted to…<br><br><strong>He aha ngā tikanga o tō mātou kura?</strong><br>What are the rules of our school?</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rights and rules vocabulary:<br><strong>āhei</strong> — permitted / allowed / able to<br><strong>kaua e [verb]</strong> — do not [verb] (negative command)<br><strong>kaua e tū ake</strong> — do not stand up<br><strong>kaua e kōrero kino</strong> — do not speak badly<br><strong>he tikanga</strong> — a rule / custom / right<br><strong>He tikanga tōku ki te [verb].</strong> — It is my right to [verb].<br><strong>He kawenga ōku ki te [verb].</strong> — It is my responsibility to [verb].<br><strong>tōpū</strong> — group / together (tōpū mahi = working group)<br><strong>whakaaro nui</strong> — consideration / deep thought</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For rules:<br><strong>Me [verb] tātou</strong> — We must [verb] (rule/obligation)<br><strong>Kaua e [verb]</strong> — Do not [verb] (prohibition)<br><strong>E āhei ana koe ki te [verb]</strong> — You are allowed to [verb]</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Kaua e [verb] = do not [verb]. This is the negative command form. More polite than a bare command but still clear. Kaua e waiho i tō para ki reira! — Don't leave your rubbish there!</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — School rules in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express your school rules in te reo Māori using me, kaua, and āhei.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A school rule: <strong>Me whakarongo mai ngā ākonga ki tō rātou kaiako.</strong> (Students must listen to their teacher.)</li><li>A prohibition: <strong>Kaua e omaoma i roto i ngā whare katoa.</strong> (Do not run inside the buildings.)</li><li>A right: <strong>He tikanga ō ngā ākonga katoa ki te pātai.</strong> (All students have the right to ask questions.)</li><li>A responsibility: <strong>He kawenga ō ngā ākonga ki te tiaki i tō rātou kura.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tikanga tōku ki te kōrero — it is my right to speak. This sentence connects to Article 13 of the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child. Te reo Māori has the vocabulary to talk about rights and justice. Why does a language need this vocabulary? What happens when a language cannot talk about rights?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Rights and rules</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own school or community rules in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three school rules using me [verb] structure.</li><li>Write two prohibitions using kaua e [verb].</li><li>Write one right and one responsibility using He tikanga/kawenga…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;Student Bill of Rights&quot; in te reo (8 sentences) — three rights (He tikanga ōku ki te…), three responsibilities (He kawenga ōku ki te…), and two prohibitions (Kaua e…). This could go on a classroom wall.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Argument and persuasion extended":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended persuasive writing · Grammar Level 1–2: Full argument toolkit in te reo</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Persuasive writing; AO 2.5 — Formal argument vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Making a case in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned to argue, compare, and use linking words. Today we assemble the full argument toolkit into a persuasive piece — the kind of writing that could appear in a school speech, a debate, or a submission.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a>:</strong> He nui ake ngā hua o te kaitiakitanga i ngā uauatanga.<br>&lt;i&gt;My position: The benefits of environmental guardianship are greater than the difficulties.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi:</strong> Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — ko tēnā he tauira o te pono.<br>&lt;i&gt;My first evidence: When the environment thrives, we thrive — that is an example of truth.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Waihoki:</strong> He tikanga ōu mō āpōpō ō ngā uri — me <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> tātou ināianei.<br>&lt;i&gt;Moreover: It is your descendants' right for tomorrow — we must protect now.&lt;/i&gt;<br><br><strong>Nō reira:</strong> me mahi tātou katoa hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaitiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaitiaki</a> o tēnei taiao.<br>&lt;i&gt;Therefore: we must all work as guardians of this environment.&lt;/i&gt;</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full argument structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Tōku tūāhuatanga:</strong> [position] — My position is…</li><li><strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua:</strong> [evidence] — My first/second evidence</li><li><strong>Waihoki:</strong> [additional point] — Moreover</li><li><strong>Heoi anō:</strong> [concession to other side]</li><li><strong>Nō reira:</strong> [conclusion] — Therefore</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — position / characteristic / quality<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taunakitanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taunakitanga</a></strong> — evidence (from last week!) / support / argument<br><strong>tuatahi / tuarua / tuatoru</strong> — first / second / third<br><strong>pono</strong> — true / honest / truth<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendants (you know this word now!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua — my first/second evidence. Taunakitanga is the key word for evidence or supporting argument. Learn it — it makes your argument sound like real persuasive writing.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build the argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we build a persuasive argument about kaitiakitanga using the full structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position: <strong>Tōku tūāhuatanga: me tiaki tātou i te taiao o Aotearoa nō te mea…</strong></li><li>First evidence: <strong>Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</strong></li><li>Second evidence with waihoki: <strong>Waihoki, he tikanga ōu mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong></li><li>Conclusion with nō reira: <strong>Nō reira, me mahi tātou katoa hei kaitiaki.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Tāku taunakitanga — my evidence. Good arguments don't just state opinions — they provide evidence. In te ao Māori, evidence often comes from proverbs (whakataukī), from the natural world, and from the actions of ancestors. What makes evidence convincing in te ao Māori? Is it different from what convinces in a Western argument?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your argument</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a persuasive argument in te reo Māori about a topic you care about.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State your position: Tōku tūāhuatanga: [position].</li><li>Give two pieces of evidence: Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi / tuarua.</li><li>Add waihoki (moreover) and make a third point.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full persuasive piece (8 sentences) — position, three evidence points (tuatahi/tuarua/tuatoru), a waihoki point, a concession with heoi anō, and a nō reira conclusion. Choose a topic you care about.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero mō Aotearoa — Talking about New Zealand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.4: Natural world · AO 2.3: Extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Aotearoa identity; AO 2.4 — NZ environment conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What is Aotearoa?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have a conversation about Aotearoa — what it is, what it means, and what our relationship to it is. This is identity conversation at the national level — connecting language, land, and belonging.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakaaro mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o Aotearoa ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he ātaahua te taiao, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māia</a> ngā tāngata, he rangatira hoki te reo Māori o tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whenua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whenua</a>.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ōu mōrearea mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Drearea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōrearea</a>: ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ngaro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ngaro</a> ngā manu o ēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=motu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">motu</a>, ka ngaro hoki te reo — me tiaki tātou i ērā taonga.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kawenga ki tēnei whenua?<br><strong>B:</strong> He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kawenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kawenga</a> ōku ki te ako i te reo Māori, ki te tiaki i te taiao, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a>.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New Aotearoa vocabulary:<br><strong>wāhi tupu</strong> — place of growth / where you grew up<br><strong>mōrearea</strong> — concern / worry / risk<br><strong>motu</strong> — island / islands (Aotearoa is a motu)<br><strong>taonga</strong> — treasure (you know this well now)<br><strong>He aha ōu mōrearea?</strong> — What are your concerns?<br><strong>he rangatira hoki te reo Māori</strong> — te reo Māori is also a chiefly language<br><strong>kawenga ki tēnei whenua</strong> — responsibility to this land<br><strong>uri</strong> — descendants (used here for future generations)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki. Three Ko statements in a row, connected by commas. This creates a feeling of layering — adding depth to what home means. Use layered Ko statements when you want to say something has many dimensions.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Aotearoa conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about Aotearoa, its beauty, and your responsibilities to it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tō whakaaro mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia…</li><li>I ask: He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o Aotearoa ki a koe? Answer with Ko ēnei list.</li><li>I ask: He aha ōu mōrearea mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ko tōku mōrearea: ka ngaro…</li><li>I ask: He aha tōu kawenga ki tēnei whenua? Answer with He kawenga ōku ki te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He kawenga ōku ki te ako i te reo Māori, ki te tiaki i te taiao, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā uri. Three responsibilities in one sentence. Of these three — learning the language, protecting the environment, thinking deeply about future generations — which feels most urgent to you? Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tōu whanaungatanga ki Aotearoa</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your relationship with Aotearoa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three Ko statements about what Aotearoa means to you.</li><li>Write Ko ēnei list of the best things about Aotearoa: Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu.</li><li>Write your mōrearea: Ko tōku mōrearea mō Aotearoa ko…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full statement about your relationship with Aotearoa (8 sentences) — what it is to you, its best features, your concerns, your responsibilities, and what you will do. Close with Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The environment belongs to all of us </li><li>We must protect the environment or we will suffer </li><li>When the environment thrives, we thrive — our wellbeing is linked ✅</li><li>The natural world is beautiful </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;toitū&quot; mean in an environmental context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>polluted / dirty </li><li>lost / extinct </li><li>sustainable / enduring / intact ✅</li><li>sacred </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kaua e omaoma i roto i ngā whare.&quot; What type of sentence is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A right </li><li>A past tense sentence </li><li>A negative command — do not do this ✅</li><li>A question </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E āhei ana koe ki te pātai.&quot; What does this sentence tell you?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>You must ask a question </li><li>You are not allowed to ask </li><li>You are permitted / able to ask ✅</li><li>You asked a question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;taunakitanga&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>position / viewpoint </li><li>evidence / supporting argument ✅</li><li>conclusion </li><li>topic </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tāku taunakitanga tuarua.&quot; What does tuarua indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>first </li><li>third ✅</li><li>second </li><li>fourth </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;mōrearea&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>responsibility </li><li>treasure </li><li>concern / worry / risk ✅</li><li>beautiful feature </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko Aotearoa tōku kāinga — ko ia tōku wāhi tupu, tōku wāhi tiaki.&quot; What does &quot;wāhi tupu&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>place of rest </li><li>place of learning </li><li>place of growth / where you grew up ✅</li><li>place of danger </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Whakataukī — Proverbs and their wisdom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Figurative language · AO 1.4: Cultural wisdom · AO 2.3: Extended explanation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Proverbs &amp; figurative language; AO 1.4 — Cultural wisdom</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The wisdom of whakataukī</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Whakataukī — proverbs — are one of the richest forms of te reo Māori. They carry generations of wisdom in a few words. Today we explore the most important whakataukī for your learning journey, understand their meaning, and learn to use them.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha te mea nui o te ao?</strong><br><strong>He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.</strong><br>What is the most important thing in the world? It is people, it is people, it is people.<br><br><strong>Hutia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rito\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rito</a> o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=harakeke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">harakeke</a>,</strong><br><strong>kei hea te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dmako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōmako</a> e kō?</strong><br>If you pull the heart of the flax bush, where will the bellbird sing?<br>(meaning: if you destroy the centre of the family/community, what happens to its members?)<br><br><strong>Ehara tāku toa i te toa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=takitahi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">takitahi</a>,</strong><br><strong>he toa <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=takitini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">takitini</a>.</strong><br>My strength is not the strength of one, it is the strength of many.<br><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Wh%C4%81ia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whāia</a> te iti <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kahurangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kahurangi</a>;</strong><br><strong>ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABōhu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūohu</a> koe, me he maunga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=teitei\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">teitei</a>.</strong><br>Pursue excellence; if you must bow your head, let it be to a high mountain.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Understanding whakataukī:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Whakataukī work on two levels:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The LITERAL meaning — what the words say</li><li>The DEEPER meaning — what they teach</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Hutia te rito o te harakeke&quot; — literally about a flax bush and a bellbird. Deeper: the flax bush is the family; the rito (heart shoot) is the child or the core; if you destroy the centre, the community falls apart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi&quot; — pursue the small precious thing (excellence). If you must bow, bow only to something truly great (a high mountain). Meaning: only humble yourself before things truly worthy of respect.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>harakeke</strong> — flax plant (native NZ flax)<br><strong>rito</strong> — the heart shoot of the flax / the central child<br><strong>kōmako</strong> — bellbird<br><strong>toa takitahi</strong> — the strength of one alone<br><strong>toa takitini</strong> — the strength of many together</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: To explain a whakataukī: first state the literal meaning, then say Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te [deeper meaning]. Always explain BOTH levels.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakataukī meanings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For each whakataukī, we unpack the literal meaning and then the deeper meaning together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha te mea nui o te ao? He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.&quot; → Literal: what is the most important thing? People, people, people. → Deeper: people are at the centre of everything.</li><li>&quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; → Literal: my strength is not from one, it is from many. → Deeper: collective strength is greater than individual strength.</li><li>&quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi.&quot; → Pursue excellence. When would you use this whakataukī? Who would you say it to?</li><li>Choose one whakataukī and explain it: Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Hutia te rito o te harakeke, kei hea te kōmako e kō?&quot; The flax family: the outer leaves protect the inner leaves which protect the rito (heart shoot/child). The rito must be protected. This is the architecture of the family in te ao Māori. How does a plant become a model for how families should work?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakataukī</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explore and explain whakataukī in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the literal meaning of one whakataukī: Ko te tikanga ōpaki o tēnei whakataukī ko te…</li><li>Write the deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te…</li><li>Write when you would use this whakataukī and who to.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Choose your favourite whakataukī from today. Write a full explanation (8 sentences) — the words, the literal meaning, the deeper meaning, the context for using it, and why it speaks to you personally. End with how this whakataukī connects to your own life.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hou o te pūtaiao — Science and discovery vocabulary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Vocabulary development · AO 2.3: Extended explanation · Grammar Level 1–2: Science and inquiry vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Science vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Inquiry &amp; explanation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Exploring and explaining the world</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori has its own words for scientific inquiry — observation, hypothesis, experiment, and discovery. Today we learn this vocabulary so that science learning can happen in te reo as naturally as in English.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=p%C5%ABtaiao\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pūtaiao</a></strong> — science / nature study / nature study<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=matai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">matai</a></strong> — to observe / examine / study / examine / study<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakam%C4%81tau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakamātau</a></strong> — experiment / to test / to test<br><strong>rangahau</strong> — research / to research<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kitenga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kitenga</a></strong> — discovery / finding (kite + nga) / finding (kite + nga)<br><strong>pātai</strong> — question / to ask (also: hypothesis)<br><strong>whakaaro</strong> — thought / theory / idea<br><strong>taunakitanga</strong> — evidence (from last week!)<br><strong>hua</strong> — result / outcome<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C4%81ng%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hāngū</a></strong> — accurate / precise</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Science process in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Ka pātai</strong> mātou i tētahi pātai. — We asked a question.</li><li><strong>Ka whakaaro</strong> mātou mō tā mātou whakamātau. — We thought about our experiment.</li><li><strong>Ka whakamātau</strong> mātou. — We experimented.</li><li><strong>Ka matai</strong> mātou i ngā hua. — We observed the results.</li><li><strong>Ka kitea</strong> e mātou tētahi mea hou. — We discovered something new. (kitea = passive of kite)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explaining findings:<br><strong>I kitea e mātou ko te [finding].</strong><br>We discovered that [finding].</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He pono tō mātou whakaaro nō te mea…</strong><br>Our theory is correct because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He rereke ngā hua i ō mātou tūmanako.</strong><br>The results were different from our expectations. (tūmanako = expectations/hopes)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka kitea e mātou — we discovered. Kitea is the passive of kite (see/find). The passive is used here because the discovery &quot;came to us&quot; rather than us actively finding it. This reflects a Māori way of seeing knowledge — it reveals itself to those who are ready.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Science inquiry sequence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a science inquiry description using the Te Reo Māori process vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Start with a question: Ka pātai mātou: <strong>He aha te mea ka hanga ai te kapua?</strong> (What makes a cloud?)</li><li>State the experiment: Ka whakamātau mātou — i mahi mātou i tētahi whakamātau mō te wai.</li><li>State findings: I kitea e mātou ko te [finding]. He [adjective] ngā hua.</li><li>Evaluate: He pono/hē tō mātou whakaaro? (pono = true, hē = wrong/incorrect)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka kitea e mātou — we discovered. The passive voice makes the discovery something that came to you, not something you grabbed. In te ao Māori, knowledge is often described as something that reveals itself to a prepared mind. How is this different from the Western idea of &quot;finding&quot; knowledge? Which feels more accurate to how discovery actually happens?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Science recount</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a science inquiry recount in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your question: Ka pātai mātou:…</li><li>Write your experiment: Ka whakamātau mātou — Ka matai mātou i…</li><li>Write your findings: I kitea e mātou ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full science inquiry recount (8 sentences) — the question, the experiment, the observation, the findings, whether your theory was correct, and what you will explore next. Use Ka for the sequence, I for specific facts, and Kua for completed states.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Research and explanation writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended informational writing · AO 2.5: Technical vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: Explanation text structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Informational &amp; explanatory writing; AO 2.5 — Technical writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing to explain and inform</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write in the explanation/information genre — the kind of writing that explains how something works, why something happens, or what something means. This is a different genre from narrative or argument, and it has its own vocabulary and structure.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Topic: He aha te āniwaniwa?</strong><br><br><strong>Ko te āniwaniwa he tohu ātaahua o te āhua o te rangi.</strong><br>The rainbow is a beautiful sign of the weather.<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=puta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">puta</a> mai te āniwaniwa ina <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tutaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tutaki</a> ana te rā me te ua.</strong><br>The rainbow appears when the sun and rain meet.<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wehewehea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wehewehea</a> e te rā ngā tae o te ua — ka puta mai ngā tae katoa.</strong><br>The sun separates the colours of the rain — all the colours emerge.<br><br><strong>He tohu aroha tēnei i te tikanga Māori — he tohu nō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atua</a>.</strong><br>In Māori tradition, this is a sign of love — a sign from the gods.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explanation text structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Topic sentence</strong> — Ko te [X] he [definition/description].</li><li><strong>Process</strong> — Ka [verb] ana i [X], ka [result].</li><li><strong>Evidence/example</strong> — He tauira: [example].</li><li><strong>Cultural/additional meaning</strong> — I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New explanatory vocabulary:<br><strong>ina… ana</strong> — when / whenever (conditional time clause)<br><strong>tutaki</strong> — meet / encounter<br><strong>wehewehe</strong> — separate / divide<br><strong>ka puta mai</strong> — emerge / come forth<br><strong>he tohu nō ngā atua</strong> — a sign from the gods/spiritual realm<br><strong>atua</strong> — god / spiritual being / ancestor deity</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ina… ana = whenever / when (that happens). This is your explanation connector — Ka X ina Y ana = X happens when Y happens. It is the te reo equivalent of &quot;when&quot; or &quot;whenever&quot; in an explanation.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write an explanation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we write an explanation of a natural phenomenon in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic sentence: Ko te [phenomenon] he [definition]. What natural phenomenon are we explaining?</li><li>Process: Ka puta mai te [X] ina [Y] ana.</li><li>Evidence: He tauira: [specific example].</li><li>Cultural meaning: I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko te…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He tohu nō ngā atua — a sign from the gods. In te ao Māori, natural phenomena are not just physical events — they carry meaning. A rainbow is not just refracted light; it is a sign from the spiritual realm. How does science and spiritual meaning exist together in te ao Māori? Can both be true at the same time?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Explanation writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an explanation of a natural phenomenon or concept in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a topic sentence: Ko te [phenomenon] he [definition].</li><li>Write the process using ina… ana: Ka [result] ina [cause] ana.</li><li>Write a cultural meaning: I te tikanga Māori, ko te tikanga o tēnei ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full explanation (8 sentences) about a natural phenomenon, cultural practice, or concept from this term. Include: definition, process, example, cultural meaning, and your own reflection on what this means to you.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero hūnuku — A deep conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 3 AOs · AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended sophisticated conversation; AO 1.4 — Identity &amp; culture; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A conversation about what matters</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate week of Term 3. Today we have the deepest conversation of the year — using whakataukī, environmental language, arts, identity, and sophisticated vocabulary to talk about what truly matters.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha te mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pou</a> o tōku ora.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō moemoeā mō Aotearoa?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku moemoeā: ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toit%C5%AB\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toitū</a> te reo Māori, ka toitū hoki te taiao — ka ora tātou katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mana\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mana</a> o ngā tāngata katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri?<br><strong>B:</strong> Kei te tūmanako au ka kōrero pai rātou i te reo Māori, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> hoki rātou i tēnei whenua ātaahua.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New sophisticated vocabulary:<br><strong>ngā pou o tōku ora</strong> — the pillars of my life (pou = pillar)<br><strong>ka toitū</strong> — will endure / remain sustainable<br><strong>Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te taiao.</strong> — When the language endures, the environment endures.<br><strong>mana o ngā tāngata katoa</strong> — the dignity/authority of all people<br><strong>tūmanako</strong> — hope / expectation<br><strong>He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri?</strong><br>What do you hope for your descendants?<br><strong>Ka kōrero pai rātou</strong> — they will speak well<br><strong>tēnei whenua ātaahua</strong> — this beautiful land</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ērā ngā pou o tōku ora — those are the pillars of my life. Pou appears again — the pillar that supports everything. Whānau, reo, taiao — three pillars. What are the three pillars of YOUR life? Can you say them in te reo?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Deep conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the deepest te reo conversation of the year — about what truly matters.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha te mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao? Answer with Ko [X], ko [Y], ko [Z] — ngā pou o tōku ora.</li><li>I ask: He aha tō moemoeā mō Aotearoa? Answer with Ka toitū + what you hope will endure.</li><li>I ask: He aha tō whakataukī pai rawa atu? Say the whakataukī and explain its meaning.</li><li>I ask: He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri? Answer with Kei te tūmanako au ka [hope].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tāu e tūmanako ana mō ō uri? What do you hope for your descendants? You are Year 5–6 students — your descendants are decades away. But you are also somebody's descendant right now. What do you think the people who came before you hoped for you? Have those hopes been realised?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Ngā pou o tōku ora</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the pillars of your life and your hopes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko [X], ko [Y], ko [Z] — ngā pou o tōku ora.</li><li>Write your moemoeā for Aotearoa: Ko tōku moemoeā: ka toitū…</li><li>Write your hope for your uri: Kei te tūmanako au ka [hope] ō mātou uri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflection (8 sentences) on what matters most — your pillars, your dreams, a whakataukī that guides you, your hope for the future. This is your Term 3 statement of identity. Make it true.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; What does this whakataukī mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Strength comes from working alone </li><li>My strength is not from one — it is the strength of many together ✅</li><li>Competition makes us strong </li><li>The strongest person wins </li></ul></li><li>What is the &quot;rito&quot; in the harakeke whakataukī?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The outer leaves of the flax </li><li>The ground where the flax grows </li><li>The heart shoot of the flax — representing the child/core of the family ✅</li><li>The bellbird </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;whakamātau&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to observe </li><li>to research </li><li>to discover </li><li>to experiment / to test ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka kitea e mātou ko te [finding].&quot; What grammatical feature is notable here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka for future tense </li><li>Passive voice — the discovery came to us ✅</li><li>E…ana for ongoing action </li><li>Ko for identity </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka puta mai te āniwaniwa ina tutaki ana te rā me te ua.&quot; What does ina…ana mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>because of </li><li>although </li><li>when / whenever (conditional) ✅</li><li>if </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;wehewehe&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to meet / encounter </li><li>to emerge </li><li>to separate / divide ✅</li><li>to observe </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ērā ngā pou o tōku ora.&quot; What does this sentence mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Those are the problems in my life </li><li>Those are the pillars of my life ✅</li><li>Those are my children </li><li>Those are my dreams </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;tūmanako&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>fear / worry </li><li>challenge </li><li>hope / expectation ✅</li><li>memory </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Term 3 vocabulary and structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Term 3 AOs consolidated · Grammar Levels 1–2 complete</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Term 3 review; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three terms of te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last week of Term 3 and of your formal learning year. Today we celebrate everything Term 3 has added to your te reo Māori world — and look at how far three terms of daily practice has taken you.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Term 3 has added these domains to your toolkit:<br><br><strong>BODY AND HEALTH:</strong> māhunga, kanohi, ringa, waewae, mamae, kaimahi hauora, me okioki<br><strong>TENSE MASTERY:</strong> Kua (completed), E…ana (progressive), hiahia ki te, māku e, Ka taea e au te<br><strong>IDENTITY AND COMMUNITY:</strong> pepeha (full), marae, pōhiri, hongi, whaikōrero, uri, tīpuna<br><strong>SCHOOL AND LEARNING:</strong> akoranga subjects, evaluative language, taunakitanga<br><strong>ARTS AND CULTURE:</strong> toi whakairo, raranga, haka, waiata, tohunga<br><strong>ENVIRONMENT:</strong> kaitiakitanga, toitū, mauri, Ka ora te taiao ka ora tātou<br><strong>WISDOM:</strong> whakataukī, tikanga, tapu, noa, mana<br><strong>SCIENCE:</strong> whakamātau, rangahau, kitenga</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The growth across three terms:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 1: &quot;Kei te haere au ki te kura.&quot;<br>Term 2: &quot;I te Rāhina, i haere mātou tokowhā ki te kura…&quot;<br>Term 3: &quot;Ko au tētahi kaitiaki o tēnei taiao — e ako ana au i te reo Māori hei tiaki i tōku ao, hei mōhio ki ngā mōrearea, hei tupu tōku ngākau i ngā whakataukī o ōku tīpuna.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Translation: &quot;I am a guardian of this natural world — I am learning te reo Māori to protect my world, to understand the challenges, to grow my heart through the proverbs of my ancestors.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That sentence uses: Ko identity, E…ana progressive, hei (purpose), ngā tīpuna (ancestors), whakataukī, ngākau (heart), and multiple subordinate connections. You can write this. You are writing this.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that Term 3 sentence aloud. Slowly. Own every word of it. You earned it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Term 3 blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Category rapid fire — cover the whole term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BODY: From māhunga to waewae — name ten body parts in order, head to toe.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: Who can say all four whakataukī from Week 8 from memory?</li><li>ARTS: Name all the toi forms — toi whakairo, toi raranga, toi puoro… aim for 8.</li><li>ENVIRONMENT: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — what does it mean and when would you use it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: You have been learning te reo Māori for three terms — about 600 minutes of class time. In that time you have built a vocabulary of over 400 words and mastered 15+ grammar structures. What would you have thought at the start of Term 1 if someone told you this? What does it tell you about what daily practice can achieve?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Three-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A personal vocabulary and structure audit across all three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write down every te reo word you can remember in 5 minutes — all three terms.</li><li>Write one sentence using each of the six tense markers: Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, Kāore i.</li><li>Write your favourite whakataukī and explain what it means to you.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the most complex te reo sentence you have ever written. Draw on everything from all three terms. Make it say something true about who you are and what you value.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar deep review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–3 consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Grammar consolidation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The complete grammar picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we put the entire three-term grammar picture on the table. This is the complete toolkit — every structure you have learned, organised clearly so you can see how they connect.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IDENTITY: Ko</strong> — Ko [name/thing] tōku [relationship].<br><strong>DESCRIPTION: He</strong> — He [adjective/noun] tēnei. He [adj] ake tērā.<br><strong>PRESENT: Kei te</strong> — Kei te [verb] [person] i [place].<br><strong>ONGOING: E…ana</strong> — E [verb] ana [person] i tēnei wā.<br><strong>PAST: I</strong> — I [verb] [person] i [time/place].<br><strong>NARRATIVE: Ka</strong> — Ka [verb] ia. Ka [verb] anō ia.<br><strong>PERFECT: Kua</strong> — Kua [verb] ia. Kua mutu.<br><strong>NEGATIVES: Kāore</strong> — Kāore [person] e [verb] ana. / Kāore [person] i [verb].<br><strong>WANT: Hiahia</strong> — Kei te hiahia [person] ki te [verb].<br><strong>WILL: Māku e</strong> — Māku/Māna e [verb].<br><strong>ABLE: Ka taea</strong> — Ka taea e [person] te [verb].<br><strong>RULES: Me/Kaua</strong> — Me [verb] tātou. Kaua e [verb].<br><strong>COMPLEX: Ahakoa/Mehemea/I te wā/Nō te mea</strong></blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The test of grammar mastery is not reciting rules — it is fluent use.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A truly fluent sentence from a Term 3 student:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga, kua mārama ake au ināianei — ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori, ka taea e au te tuhituhi hoki, ā ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka kōrero tonu au i ēnei kupu tapu mō ōku uri.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Although it was difficult for me at the beginning, I now understand better — I am able to speak te reo Māori, I am able to write too, and my dream is: I will continue to speak these sacred words for my descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ahakoa (complex), Kua (perfect), Ka taea (ability), Ko + moemoeā (dream), Ka tonu (future ongoing). Every structure in one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Write the test sentence above in your own words. Change the details to make it true for YOU. That is grammar mastery — not knowing the names, but being able to SAY it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Grammar challenge</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences matching each structure one by one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko + He + Kei te in one passage — three sentences about yourself right now.</li><li>I + Ka + Kua in sequence — three moments from this morning.</li><li>Kāore e…ana + Kāore i + mehemea — three negative/conditional sentences.</li><li>Ahakoa + nō te mea + nō reira — one complex sentence with all three.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Look at that test sentence: ahakoa, kua, Ka taea, ko, moemoeā, ka tonu, uri. Seven different structures in one sentence. At the start of Term 1, how many of those words did you know? Now? What is the relationship between vocabulary and grammar — can you have one without the other?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar self-test</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test yourself on every major structure from three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence using each of: Ko, He, Kei te, E…ana, I, Ka, Kua.</li><li>Write a sentence using: Kāore e…ana, Kāore i, hiahia ki te, māku e, Ka taea.</li><li>Write a sentence using: ahakoa, nō te mea, mehemea, i te wā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a ten-sentence passage that uses at least fifteen different grammar structures correctly. This is your three-term grammar showcase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Showcase sentences: three terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–3 · Full sentence type showcase</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Showcase; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary &amp; expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your best te reo ever</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today you write the best sentences of your three-term te reo journey. There is no new content. Just you, your toolkit, and the task of producing your finest te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Student showcase examples from across the year:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko te reo Māori ko tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna — he ara māmā, he ara aroha.&quot;</strong><br>(Te reo Māori is my path to the ancestors — a light path, a loving path.)<br><br><strong>&quot;I te wā e ako ana au i tēnei reo tapu, ka mārama aku karu ki tētahi ao hou — ko te ao Māori, ko te ao ātaahua.&quot;</strong><br>(While I was learning this sacred language, my eyes opened to a new world — the Māori world, the beautiful world.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao — ko tērā tōku whakapono, ko tērā hoki tōku moemoeā.&quot;</strong><br>(When language endures, people endure, and the natural world endures — that is my belief, and that is also my dream.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences exceptional:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They are PERSONAL — these could not have been written by anyone else<br>→ They use MULTIPLE STRUCTURES seamlessly<br>→ They say something the writer DEEPLY BELIEVES<br>→ They use FIGURATIVE LANGUAGE (ara = path, literal AND metaphorical)<br>→ They CONNECT three terms of learning (tīpuna, tapu, mauri, moemoeā, whakapono)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your showcase sentence should:<br>→ Use at least three different structures<br>→ Say something genuinely true about you<br>→ Include at least one word or phrase from each term<br>→ Feel like te reo Māori, not like an English sentence translated</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The best te reo sentence you can write today is the one that is most true. Not the most complex, not the longest — the most true. Write it in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Best sentences together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one final group showcase sentence — everyone contributes one element.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: a Ko statement about what te reo Māori is to us.</li><li>Process: what happens when we learn it — E…ana, Ka, Kua.</li><li>Result: what it gives us — He [noun/quality] tēnei.</li><li>Closing: our hope for the future — Ko tērā tōku/ō mātou moemoeā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. Three things endure together. If language endures, people endure. If people endure, the natural world is protected. Three things, one survival. Is this true? Is it possible for a language to disappear without its people also losing something essential?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your three finest te reo Māori sentences of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best SIMPLE sentence — true and precise.</li><li>Write your best COMPLEX sentence — multiple structures, deep meaning.</li><li>Write your SHOWCASE SENTENCE — the single finest te reo Māori sentence you have ever produced.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your &quot;three-term testament&quot; — three sentences, one from each term's perspective: who you were (Term 1), who you are becoming (Term 2), and who you intend to be (Term 3). Each sentence should use at least three structures from that term.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Preparing for the Term 3 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 3 standard</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next week is your end-of-term assessment. Today we see what the Term 3 standard looks like — a conversation that draws on all three terms, uses sophisticated vocabulary and structures, and sounds like genuine te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai tōu ingoa? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Ko [maunga] tōku maunga, ko [awa] tōku awa, nō [place] ahau.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao. E ako ana au i te reo Māori hei tiaki i ēnei taonga.<br><strong>A:</strong> Kua tae ki a koe tēnei mōhiotanga?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, kua mārama ake au i ia wiki, i ia wiki. Ka taea e au te kōrero i ngā hanganga katoa ināianei.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te mahi ngātahi.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What this conversation demonstrates:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + three-part close<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full introduction with Ko + pepeha<br>✓ AO 1.4: Pepeha (maunga, awa, origin)<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated kōrero<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, taonga, mōhiotanga, whakataukī<br>✓ Grammar: Ko, E…ana, Kua, Ka taea, whakataukī explanation</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?</strong><br>What proverb characterises / represents you?<br><strong>e tūāhuatia ana</strong> — that characterises / represents (passive E…ana)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe? — What proverb represents you? This is a beautiful question. Before the assessment, choose YOUR whakataukī. The one that speaks to who you are. Be ready to say it and explain it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Assessment preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full practice conversation — using the Term 3 standard as your guide.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greet formally + full pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>He aha ngā mea nui rawa atu ki a koe i tō ao? Answer with Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko…</li><li>State your learning progress: Kua mārama ake au. Ka taea e au te…</li><li>Share your whakataukī + explanation + three-part close.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe? Which whakataukī represents you? &quot;He toa takitini&quot; (strength of many) — &quot;Whāia te iti kahurangi&quot; (pursue excellence) — &quot;He tangata, he tangata, he tangata&quot; (it is people). Before you answer, sit with it. The right whakataukī should feel like it has always been yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Assessment preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare for the Term 3 end-of-term assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write and memorise your full pepeha — all nine lines.</li><li>Write Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko… (three pillars of your life).</li><li>Choose your whakataukī — write it and its explanation: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation you plan to have in the assessment (12 lines minimum). Practise it until you can say it without looking. Then say it to a family member tonight.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which of these is a Term 3 structure that was NOT taught in Terms 1 or 2?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ko </li><li>Kua (perfect aspect) ✅</li><li>Kāore </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; In what context would you most naturally use this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When describing the weather </li><li>When talking about kaitiakitanga and environmental responsibility ✅</li><li>When explaining why you are late </li><li>When introducing yourself </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua ki a au i te tīmatanga, kua mārama ake au ināianei.&quot; What two structures are combined here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ka and Kei te </li><li>Ahakoa (complex) and Kua (perfect aspect) ✅</li><li>I and E…ana </li><li>Ko and He </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I want to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I used to be able to speak te reo Māori </li><li>I am able to speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>I am learning to speak te reo Māori </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What makes a showcase sentence different from a grammar exercise sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is always longer </li><li>It uses more vocabulary words </li><li>It says something the writer truly means and has a genuine voice ✅</li><li>It always uses Kua </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo Māori ko tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does ara mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>language </li><li>path / way ✅</li><li>ancestor </li><li>light </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He aha tōu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?&quot; What does tūāhuatia mean here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that you have memorised </li><li>that characterises / represents you (passive) ✅</li><li>that you wrote yourself </li><li>that is the most famous </li></ul></li><li>What are the &quot;ngā pou o tōku ora&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the rules of my school </li><li>the challenges of my life </li><li>the pillars of my life ✅</li><li>the goals for next term </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — End-of-term vocabulary celebration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–3 · Complete vocabulary review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 2.5 — All vocabulary; AO 2.3 — All structures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Three terms: a vocabulary celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we celebrate. In three terms of daily warm-up practice, you have built something remarkable. Let us count it, celebrate it, and prepare to show it.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Three terms of te reo Māori. Here is your vocabulary toolkit:<br><br>TERM 1: Greetings · Ko · He · Kei te · Numbers 1–10 · food · places · wants · feelings · adjectives · conjunctions · adverbs · prepositions<br><br>TERM 2: I (past) · Ka (narrative) · Kua (perfect) · Kāore (negative) · All 16 pronouns · Numbers 1–100 · Dates/times/months · Colours · Comparatives · Weather · Seasons · Emotions · Character · Arts<br><br>TERM 3: Body · E…ana · Kua · hiahia · māku e · Ka taea · Pepeha (full) · Marae · Haka · Whakataukī · Kaitiakitanga · Science vocabulary · Formal/informal register · Argument and persuasion</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What you can now DO:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Have a sophisticated 10-minute conversation entirely in te reo Māori<br>✓ Write a full pepeha and deliver it from memory<br>✓ Explain a whakataukī and its deeper meaning<br>✓ Write a persuasive argument using taunakitanga<br>✓ Describe past, present, and future events fluently<br>✓ Express emotions, opinions, goals, and dreams<br>✓ Talk about the environment, the arts, the body, and the community<br>✓ Switch between formal and casual registers<br>✓ Use all 16 pronouns correctly<br>✓ Count to 100, say the time, and give the date<br>✓ Understand and produce complex sentences with subordinate clauses</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is real. You did this. Ka tino pai!</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka tino pai! — genuinely, truly excellent. Not just &quot;good&quot; — tino good. You have earned tino.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Three-term blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The fastest, most comprehensive vocabulary review of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA: One student delivers their full pepeha from memory. Class listens and checks.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: Who can say all four from Term 3 Week 8? Class helps where needed.</li><li>NUMBERS: Count from 1 to 100 as a class in te reo — everyone joins in.</li><li>SHOWCASE: Each student says their best single te reo sentence of the year. No repeats.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Your best single te reo sentence of the year. Not the longest. Not the most complicated. The most TRUE. What do you want to say, in te reo Māori, about your year of learning? Say it. Own it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final vocabulary audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your last vocabulary audit of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write what you know today that you did not know at the start of Term 1.</li><li>Write your three best te reo sentences of the year.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a letter to your next-year self in te reo Māori — what you have learned, what you struggled with, what you are proud of, and what you hope they will continue. At least ten sentences. This letter is evidence of your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake — Grammar final review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All grammar structures Terms 1–3</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery; AO 2.3 — Grammar review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure, one final time</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we review every grammar structure from three terms — one final time. This is not about memorising rules. It is about confirming what you already know.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Every structure in one passage:<br><br><strong>Ko ahau ko [name]. He ākonga au i tēnei kura.</strong> (Ko · He)<br><strong>Kei te ako tonu au i te reo Māori — e tipu ana tōku māramatanga i ia rā, i ia rā.</strong> (Kei te · E…ana)<br><strong>I tīmata au i tērā tau — kāore au i mōhio ki ētahi kupu.</strong> (I · Kāore i)<br><strong>Ka haere au ki te kura. Ka ako au. Ka hari tōku ngākau.</strong> (Ka sequence)<br><strong>Kua mārama ake au — kua tipu tōku reo.</strong> (Kua)<br><strong>Ka taea e au te kōrero i te reo Māori, ka taea e au te tuhituhi hoki.</strong> (Ka taea)<br><strong>Ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori.</strong> (Ahakoa · Nō te mea · Ko)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures in that passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko (identity)</li><li>He (description)</li><li>Kei te (present)</li><li>E…ana (progressive)</li><li>I (past)</li><li>Kāore i (past negative)</li><li>Ka (narrative × 3)</li><li>Kua (perfect × 2)</li><li>Ka taea (ability × 2)</li><li>Ahakoa (complex concession)</li><li>Nō te mea (reason)</li><li>Ko + moemoeā (dream statement)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Twelve structures in seven sentences. You know all of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: You know all of these. You have used them all. Today's review is not about learning — it is about confidence. Read the passage aloud. Feel how natural it sounds. That is YOUR voice in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Structure identification</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the I Do passage aloud together. Identify every structure as a class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify: Ko, He, Kei te, E…ana — point to each one as you read.</li><li>Identify: I, Kāore i, Ka (three times), Kua (twice).</li><li>Identify: Ka taea (twice), Ahakoa, Nō te mea, Ko + moemoeā.</li><li>Now write your own passage — same structures, your own words and story.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Read your own three-term passage aloud. This is the grammar of your story — your Ko, your He, your Kua, your Ka taea. Every structure you know is in service of what YOU want to say. Grammar is not the goal — it is the tool. What story do you want to tell with these tools?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Grammar showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a passage that uses every major structure from three terms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using Ko, He, and Kei te.</li><li>Write three sentences using I, Ka, and Kua.</li><li>Write three sentences using Ka taea, ahakoa, and nō te mea.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Combine all nine sentences into one flowing passage. Add connective words to make it flow. This is your three-term grammar showcase.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Three-term portfolio sentence":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types Terms 1–3</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Portfolio showcase; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your portfolio sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today you write THE sentence — your single finest piece of te reo Māori from three terms of learning. This sentence will go in your portfolio, on the wall, and in your memory. It should say who you are, what matters to you, and what you carry into the future.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Portfolio sentences from imagined students across the year:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo o ōku tīpuna i roto i āku kupu, i roto i āku mahi, i roto i āku moemoeā.&quot;</strong><br>(The language of my ancestors endures within my words, within my actions, within my dreams.)<br><br><strong>&quot;He ākonga noa iho ahau — engari, ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana ki te tiaki i tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taonga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taonga</a> ātaahua, ko te reo Māori.&quot;</strong><br>(I am just a learner — but my heart desires to protect this beautiful treasure, te reo Māori.)<br><br><strong>&quot;I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — ināianei, kua tipu ōku rerenga ki tētahi ao hou, ko te ao Māori.&quot;</strong><br>(I started with one word — now, my sentences have grown into a new world, the Māori world.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes a portfolio sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ It is PERSONAL — only you could have written it<br>→ It is TRUE — you believe every word<br>→ It CONNECTS to something bigger than yourself (reo, taiao, tīpuna, uri)<br>→ It uses MULTIPLE STRUCTURES seamlessly<br>→ It SOUNDS BEAUTIFUL — read it aloud and feel it</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The three examples above each use:<br>&quot;Ka toitū&quot; — enduring / the perfect aspect<br>&quot;Ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana&quot; — heart progressive want<br>&quot;Kua tipu&quot; — has grown (perfect) into a new world</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your portfolio sentence is not a homework task. It is a declaration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He ākonga noa iho ahau — I am just a learner. And yet — ko tōku ngākau e hiahia ana ki te tiaki i tēnei taonga. The heart wants to protect it. Humility + passion = the perfect learner of te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Draft together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one group portfolio sentence — and each student writes their own.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Class sentence: Ko mātou ngā ākonga o tēnei akomanga — ka tipu tō mātou reo i ia rā, i ia rā.</li><li>Now each student writes their own first draft — alone, in silence, in te reo.</li><li>Share with a partner — read aloud, give feedback, improve.</li><li>Final version: write it clearly, read it to the class.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — I started with one word. What was your first word in te reo Māori? Kia ora? Āe? Your name in Ko? How many words do you know now? What does that journey — from one word to hundreds — feel like to look back on?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Portfolio sentence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your portfolio sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draft: write three possible portfolio sentences — different approaches, different feelings.</li><li>Choose: which one is most true? Which one sounds most like you in te reo?</li><li>Final: write your chosen sentence carefully. This is your keepsake.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a portfolio paragraph (5 sentences) — your portfolio sentence as the opening, followed by four sentences that expand on it. What does this sentence say about who you are, what you have learned, and where you are going?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — End of Term 3 assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Personal information; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The Term 3 standard conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is your final spoken assessment for Term 3. It is also the most sophisticated conversation you will have had in te reo Māori. Here is what the full Term 3 standard looks like:</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full nine-line pepeha from memory]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tō pepeha! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā mea e tiaki ana i a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te mahi ngātahi — ko tērā tōku whakapono.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tōu moemoeā mō tō reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori, ka toitū hoki tēnei reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tapu</a> mō ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē tāu kōrero! Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assessment checklist:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + Tēnā koe ×3 close<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full pepeha with Ko sentences<br>✓ AO 1.4: Maunga, awa, iwi, hapū, marae, kura, ingoa, origin<br>✓ AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, moemoeā, whakataukī, whakapono, uri, toitū<br>✓ Grammar: Ko ×9 (pepeha), E…ana, Ka, Kua, ahakoa (embedded)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Deliver your pepeha with pride. It carries your mountain, your river, your people, and your name. There is no more important thing you can say in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One complete practice run with a partner before the assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha — partner listens and checks all nine lines.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora ko… — three pillars, stated with confidence.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation — say it, explain both levels of meaning.</li><li>Moemoeā + three-part close — Tēnā koe, tēnā koe, tēnā koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori. Is this your dream too? Or is it something else? Before your assessment, sit quietly for one minute and answer: what do YOU hope for your te reo Māori — not for the assessment, not for the teacher, but for yourself, for your life?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — Term 3 assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your end-of-term spoken assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full pepeha from memory — all nine lines.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōu ora + whakataukī + moemoeā — at least 6 sentences.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Full conversation with a partner — at least 12 lines each.</li><li>✦ Assessment checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines) · ☐ Ko ngā pou o tōu ora · ☐ Whakataukī + explanation · ☐ Moemoeā statement · ☐ Ka taea sentence · ☐ Tēnā koe ×3 close</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which Term 3 structure expresses a completed action whose result is relevant now?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>Ka </li><li>I </li><li>Kua ✅</li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.&quot; What are the three pou?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>school, friends, sport </li><li>family, language, natural world ✅</li><li>food, shelter, water </li><li>work, rest, play </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify the structure: &quot;E tipu ana tōku māramatanga i ia rā, i ia rā.&quot;<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te present </li><li>Ka narrative </li><li>E…ana progressive — ongoing growth ✅</li><li>I past </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au.&quot; What does ahakoa signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a result — because it was hard I worked </li><li>a time — when it was hard I worked </li><li>a concession — DESPITE it being hard, I kept working ✅</li><li>a condition — if it is hard I will work </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a &quot;portfolio sentence&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The longest sentence you can write </li><li>A sentence for grammar practice only </li><li>Your finest, most personal, most true te reo sentence — a declaration of who you are ✅</li><li>A sentence that uses every vocabulary word </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — ināianei, kua tipu ōku rerenga ki tētahi ao hou.&quot; What does &quot;ao hou&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a new day </li><li>a new world / new realm ✅</li><li>a new language </li><li>a new friend </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the correct order for a full pepeha?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Name → mountain → river → iwi → school → origin </li><li>Mountain → river → waka → iwi → hapū → marae → school → name → origin ✅</li><li>Origin → name → iwi → mountain → river </li><li>School → name → iwi → hapū → origin </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau i te reo Māori.&quot; What does this dream express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>That the speaker will learn English </li><li>That the speaker's family will speak te reo Māori ✅</li><li>That te reo Māori will be taught in all schools </li><li>That the speaker will win a competition </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Tēnā koutou katoa — Formal greetings and register mastery":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.6: Politeness conventions · AO 1.2: Formal introductions</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal greetings; AO 1.6 — Register mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full greeting system</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to Term 4 — Kia Mārama, the term of fluency. This term we bring everything together and reach for genuine spontaneous conversation. We begin where te reo Māori always begins — with greetings. But this term, we master the FULL system — every register, every occasion, every nuance.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CASUAL (hoa, tamariki):</strong><br><strong>Kia ora!</strong> — Hello / Thank you<br><strong>Kia ora kōrua/koutou!</strong> — Hello you two / Hello everyone<br><br><strong>FORMAL SINGULAR:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koe.</strong> — Greetings to you (one person)<br><br><strong>FORMAL DUAL:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā kōrua.</strong> — Greetings to you two<br><br><strong>FORMAL PLURAL:</strong><br><strong>Tēnā koutou.</strong> — Greetings to you all (3+)<br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> — Greetings to each and every one of you<br><br><strong>FAREWELL SYSTEM:</strong><br><strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> — Goodbye (speaker is leaving)<br><strong>E noho rā.</strong> — Goodbye (listener is staying)<br><strong>Ā, ka kite anō.</strong> — Until we meet again<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=M%C4%81+te+w%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mā te wā</a>.</strong> — Until another time / Farewell (for a long parting)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The greeting system is built on the pronoun system you know:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>koe (you, 1) → Tēnā koe<br>kōrua (you 2) → Tēnā kōrua<br>koutou (you 3+) → Tēnā koutou</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For acknowledgement and gratitude:<br><strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.</strong><br>Thank you for your help.<br>(Tēnā koe + mō + reason for thanks)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi.</strong><br>Thank you all for your work.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For welcoming:<br><strong>Nau mai, haere mai!</strong> — Welcome! Come in!<br><strong>Nau mai ki tō mātou kura.</strong><br>Welcome to our school.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>mō</strong> — for / about / on account of (used for giving thanks)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koe mō [reason] = thank you for [reason]. This is the complete thank-you formula in formal te reo. Tēnā koe alone = greeting. Tēnā koe mō tāu mahi = thank you for your work.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full greeting system practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rapid fire — I describe the situation, you give the right greeting.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Greeting the whole class formally: <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa.</strong> Now greet two students: <strong>Tēnā kōrua.</strong></li><li>Thanking a teacher for help: <strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.</strong> Thanking the whole class: <strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi.</strong></li><li>Saying goodbye as you leave: <strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> Staying while someone else leaves: <strong>E noho rā.</strong></li><li>Welcoming a visitor: <strong>Nau mai, haere mai! Nau mai ki tō mātou kura.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E noho rā (you stay) vs Hei konā rā (farewell where I am). These two farewells tell you who is moving. Why does te reo Māori distinguish who is leaving and who is staying? What does this say about how Māori thinks about presence and movement?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Greeting mastery</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the full greeting system in real sentences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the correct greeting for: your teacher, two classmates, the whole school assembly.</li><li>Write a formal thank-you: Tēnā koe/koutou mō… (three different reasons for thanks).</li><li>Write the correct farewell for: you are leaving, your friend is leaving, it is the end of term.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full formal welcome speech (8 sentences) for a visitor to your school — Nau mai, greeting by role, thank them for coming, introduce the school, close with Tēnā koutou ×3.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hono hou — Advanced linking and cohesion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Advanced cohesion, discourse markers</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Advanced cohesion; AO 2.5 — Discourse markers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Linking ideas like a fluent speaker</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Fluent speakers do not just string sentences together — they signal the relationship between ideas with precision. Today we learn the advanced linking toolkit — the words and phrases that mark transitions, summaries, contrasts, and conclusions in extended te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>To add / expand:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=waihoki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waihoki</a></strong> — moreover / and also<br><strong>ā, ko tērā anō</strong> — and there is also this<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C4%81ore+noa+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kāore noa iho</a></strong> — not only that<br><br><strong>To contrast:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=heoi+an%C5%8D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heoi anō</a></strong> — however<br><strong>engari rā</strong> — but actually / however (stronger) / however (stronger)<br><strong>ahakoa ōna āhuatanga</strong> — despite its qualities<br><br><strong>To conclude:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=n%C5%8D+reira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nō reira</a></strong> — therefore / so<br><strong>ko te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutunga</a></strong> — in conclusion<br><strong>hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakakapi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakakapi</a></strong> — to conclude / to summarise / to summarise<br><br><strong>To illustrate:</strong><br><strong>hei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tauira\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tauira</a></strong> — for example<br><strong>pēnei i te</strong> — such as / like<br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ar%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">arā</a></strong> — that is / namely</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In use:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He taonga te reo Māori. Kāore noa iho — he taonga ā-ahurea hoki.</strong><br>Te reo Māori is a treasure. Not only that — it is also a cultural treasure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He nui ngā hua o te ako i te reo Māori. Heoi anō, he uaua hoki.</strong><br>There are many benefits of learning te reo. However, it is also difficult.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei reo mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong><br>In conclusion, we must protect this language for tomorrow's descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Hei tauira, ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui — arā, he tohu aroha me he tohu kaha.</strong><br>For example, haka is a famous art form — namely, a sign of love and a sign of strength.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>ā-ahurea</strong> — cultural (ā + ahurea = cultural/relating to culture)<br><strong>hei whakakapi</strong> — to summarise / in summary</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Hei tauira = for example. Arā = namely / that is. Use these when you want to illustrate a point with a specific case. They signal: &quot;I am about to give you evidence for what I just said.&quot;</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Advanced linking</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you two sentences — you choose the best advanced linking word and explain why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He pai te reo Māori.&quot; + &quot;He tino rangatira hoki.&quot; → Expanding: <strong>Kāore noa iho — he tino rangatira hoki te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>&quot;He nui ngā ākonga i tēnei kura.&quot; + &quot;Ko tētahi āhuatanga pai tērā.&quot; → Concluding: <strong>Ko te mutunga, he kura nui tēnei.</strong></li><li>&quot;I mahi kaha mātou.&quot; + &quot;I toa mātou.&quot; → Giving an example: <strong>Hei tauira — i toa mātou i te kaupeka hākinakina o tērā wiki.</strong></li><li>Write your own: an argument about kaitiakitanga using waihoki, heoi anō, and nō reira.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga — in conclusion. Hei whakakapi — to summarise. These are the phrases that close an argument. In te ao Māori, how you close a kōrero is as important as how you open it. Why? What does a strong conclusion do that a weak one doesn't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Advanced linking sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use advanced discourse markers in extended writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write three sentences using waihoki, heoi anō, and hei tauira — one each.</li><li>Write a Ko te mutunga or hei whakakapi conclusion sentence about something you believe.</li><li>Use arā to clarify a point: state something general, then use arā to specify.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write an argument paragraph (8 sentences) about te reo Māori. Use at least five different linking markers: kāore noa iho, heoi anō, hei tauira, arā, and Ko te mutunga.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — All structures, full fluency":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · All grammar structures Terms 1–4</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full fluency writing; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing with everything you have</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is Term 4, Week 1. You have three terms of te reo Māori behind you. Today we write without guardrails — using everything you know, in whatever order serves what you want to say. This is what fluency looks like.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A Term 4 student's passage:</strong><br><br>Ko au ko Hēmi. He ākonga au i tēnei kura, he kaitiaki hoki o tēnei taiao.<br>I tīmata au i tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora&quot; — ināianei, kua <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br>E ako tonu ana au — kāore anō kua oti — engari, ka taea e au te kōrero i ngā āhuatanga nui o tōku ao ināianei.<br>Hei tauira: ka taea e au te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a>, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i te reo Māori.<br>Ko te mutunga, ko tēnei reo tōku ara ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>, ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a>, ā ki tōku ake ngākau.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice every structure in that passage:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ko (×2 — identity)<br>He (×2 — description)<br>I tīmata au — past<br>Kua tipu — perfect aspect<br>E ako tonu ana — E…ana progressive<br>Kāore anō kua oti — Kua negative<br>Ka taea e au te — ability (×3)<br>Hei tauira — for example<br>Ko te mutunga — in conclusion<br>Ko + Ko — identity statement chain</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>tōku ake ngākau</strong> — my very own heart (ake = own/particular when after a possessive)<br><strong>Tōku ake</strong> = my very own (more emphatic than tōku alone)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tōku ake = my very own. Tāu ake = your very own. Ake after a possessive adds the sense of &quot;own&quot; — particular to you, belonging to no one else. Use it when you want to say something is truly, personally yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Free writing with everything</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a topic — you write freely, using any structures that serve what you want to say.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic: what te reo Māori means to you. Write four sentences — no structure requirements, just truth.</li><li>Topic: your biggest achievement this year. Write four sentences — past, present, future all welcome.</li><li>Topic: someone who has influenced you. Write four sentences — any structures, show don't tell.</li><li>Read your sentences aloud. Which one sounds most like YOUR voice in te reo? Why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tēnei reo tōku ara ki ngā tīpuna, ki ngā uri, ā ki tōku ake ngākau. The language is a path to the ancestors, to the descendants, and to my very own heart. Three destinations. Which of the three feels most real to you right now?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Free writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write freely in te reo Māori for ten minutes. No constraints. Just truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write for 5 minutes without stopping — anything on your mind in te reo.</li><li>Read back what you wrote. Underline the structures you used without thinking.</li><li>Choose your best sentence from your free writing. Improve it once.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full free-writing piece (10 sentences) on any topic you choose. After writing, annotate every structure you used. Count them. This is evidence of your fluency.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He mihi whakamutunga — End of year farewell language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings/farewells · AO 1.6: Politeness · AO 2.3: Extended formal communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Farewell language; AO 1.6 — Formal occasion language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Language for endings and new beginnings</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Term 4 is a time of endings and transitions — the end of the year, farewells to classmates, transitions to new classes. Today we learn the language of endings and new beginnings in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Mā te wā.</strong> — Until another time / Farewell (for a long parting)<br><strong>Ā, ka kite anō.</strong> — Until we meet again<br><strong>Hei konā rā.</strong> — Farewell (I am leaving)<br><strong>E noho rā.</strong> — Farewell (you stay, I go)<br><br><strong>For a year's end:</strong><br><strong>I tēnei tau, kua tipu tātou katoa.</strong><br>This year, we have all grown.<br><br><strong>He tau tino pai tēnei mōku.</strong><br>This has been a very good year for me.<br><br><strong>Tēnā koutou katoa mō ā koutou mahi i tēnei tau.</strong><br>Thank you all for your work this year.<br><br><strong>Ka nui ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koutou.</strong><br>My greetings/gratitude to you all is great.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition language:<br><strong>Ā tērā tau</strong> — next year<br><strong>Ā tērā kura</strong> — at the next school<br><strong>Ka tipu tonu</strong> — will keep growing<br><strong>Ka huri ake</strong> — will turn / transition<br><strong>Me mōhio tātou</strong> — we should know / let us remember<br><strong>Ko tōku inoi</strong> — my wish / prayer<br><strong>Ka nui ōku mihi</strong> — my greetings/thanks are great</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Farewell speech structure:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tēnā koutou katoa.</li><li>I tēnei tau, kua…</li><li>He tau [adjective] tēnei nō te mea…</li><li>Tēnā koutou mō…</li><li>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka…</li><li>Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou</strong> — my wish/prayer for you all</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou — my greetings and thanks to you all are great. This phrase does double work — it both greets and thanks. In te ao Māori, mihi (greeting/tribute) and gratitude are the same action.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Farewell conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a year-end farewell exchange.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Opening: <strong>Tēnā koutou katoa. I tēnei tau, kua tipu tātou katoa.</strong> What has grown? Add specifics.</li><li>Thank: <strong>Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi i tēnei tau.</strong> What specific things are you thanking them for?</li><li>Wish: <strong>Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka [hope for them next year].</strong></li><li>Close: <strong>Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou. Tēnā koutou ×3. Mā te wā.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku inoi mō koutou — my wish/prayer for you. What IS your wish for your classmates for next year? In te reo Māori? Try to say it. Ka [verb] koutou — may you all [verb]. This is one of the most generous things you can say to someone.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — End of year farewell speech</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a farewell speech for the end of the school year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: Tēnā koutou katoa. I tēnei tau, kua… (what has happened/changed).</li><li>Thank: Tēnā koutou mō ā koutou mahi — be specific about three things.</li><li>Wish: Ko tōku inoi mō koutou: ka [three wishes].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full farewell speech (10 sentences) following the six-step structure. Deliver it to the class — without reading if possible.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You are leaving a room and someone is staying. Which farewell do YOU say?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>E noho rā </li><li>Hei konā rā ✅</li><li>Mā te wā </li><li>Ā ka kite anō </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Greeting someone formally </li><li>Asking for help </li><li>Thanking someone for their help ✅</li><li>Saying goodbye </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which linking word means &quot;not only that&quot; — used to expand on a point?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>heoi anō </li><li>nō reira </li><li>kāore noa iho ✅</li><li>hei tauira </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Hei tauira, ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui.&quot; What is the function of hei tauira?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>To conclude an argument </li><li>To introduce an example / illustration ✅</li><li>To contrast two ideas </li><li>To ask a question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Tōku ake ngākau.&quot; What does ake add after the possessive here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It changes the meaning to &quot;my friends' heart&quot; </li><li>It adds emphasis — my VERY OWN heart, particularly mine ✅</li><li>It makes it past tense </li><li>It is a filler word with no meaning </li></ul></li><li>Which statement best describes Term 4 fluency?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Knowing all the grammar rules by name </li><li>Using structures automatically in service of what you want to say ✅</li><li>Writing the longest sentences possible </li><li>Translating directly from English </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko tōku inoi mō koutou.&quot; What does inoi mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>farewell </li><li>wish / prayer ✅</li><li>work </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I have many problems with you all </li><li>My greetings and gratitude to you all are great ✅</li><li>We have much work to do together </li><li>I wish you would come back </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero ā-waha — Oral storytelling techniques":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended oral communication · AO 2.5: Narrative vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: Oral storytelling structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Oral storytelling; AO 2.5 — Narrative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Telling a story that grips the listener</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Oral storytelling — <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero+p%C5%ABr%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero pūrākau</a> — is one of the oldest and most important skills in te ao Māori. Knowledge was kept in memory and transmitted through story. Today we learn the techniques that make a story gripping when spoken aloud.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>OPENING the story:</strong><br><strong>I tērā wā, i tērā wā…</strong> — In that time, in that time… (traditional opening), in that time… (traditional opening)<br><strong>Nō ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rā</a> o mua…</strong> — In the days of old…<br><strong>He pō, he pō…</strong> — One night, one night… (repetition for effect)<br><br><strong>BUILDING tension:</strong><br><strong>Ā, ka huri noa te ao.</strong> — And the world just spun (something changed)<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> rātou katoa.</strong> — They were all startled / taken by surprise<br><strong>Kāore he tangata i kite.</strong> — No one saw.<br><br><strong>CLOSING the story:</strong><br><strong>Nō reira, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mutu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mutu</a>.</strong> — And so it ended.<br><strong>Tērā ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Drero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōrero</a>.</strong> — That is the story.<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a>.</strong> — The telling turns. (traditional close). (traditional close)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Storytelling techniques:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Repetition for emphasis</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;I tērā wā, i tērā wā…&quot; — repeating sets a rhythm and signals story time<br>&quot;He pō, he pō…&quot; — doubling creates atmosphere</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Short sentences for drama</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tū ia. Ka titiro. Kāore he mea.&quot; — She stood. She looked. Nothing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Personifying nature</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka tangi te hau.&quot; — The wind cried. (from Term 1!)<br>&quot;Ka ngunguru ngā whatitiri.&quot; — The thunder growled.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Direct speech</strong>:</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka mea ia: 'Āe, ka haere au.'&quot; — She said: &quot;Yes, I will go.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>pūrākau</strong> — traditional story / myth / legend<br><strong>huri noa</strong> — just spun/turned (unexpected change)<br><strong>Ka huri.</strong> — The telling turns. (traditional story ending)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: I tērā wā, i tērā wā… — say it twice. Repetition at the opening tells the listener: we are leaving ordinary time and entering story time. This is ancient technology.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Story techniques in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We build a short story together using each technique.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: <strong>I tērā wā, i tērā wā, i noho tētahi kōtiro i te taha o te awa.</strong> Set the scene — who, where.</li><li>Build tension with short Ka sentences: <strong>Ka rongo ia i tētahi oro. Ka tū ia. Ka titiro ia.</strong></li><li>Use personification: <strong>Ka ngunguru te ngahere. Ka tangi te hau i ngā rākau.</strong></li><li>Close: <strong>Nō reira, ka hoki ia ki tōna kāinga. Ka huri.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka huri — the telling turns. This traditional ending signals that the story is complete and the teller is returning control to the listeners. Why do oral cultures develop formal story openings and closings? What do they do for the listener that a written story opening and closing can't?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tell your story</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short pūrākau using the oral storytelling techniques.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with I tērā wā, i tērā wā or Nō ngā rā o mua — establish time, place, character.</li><li>Build tension with three short Ka sentences — action, pause, action.</li><li>Use at least one personification of nature: Ka [sound word] te [natural element].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full pūrākau (10 sentences) — traditional opening, scene-setting, rising action with tension, a moment of surprise (Ka ohorere…), resolution, and Ka huri close. Read it aloud to a partner in your best storytelling voice.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā rerenga pakiaka — Complex subordinate sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Complex subordination, relative clauses, purpose clauses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex sentence mastery; AO 2.5 — Advanced structures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The most complex sentence structures in te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have ahakoa, mehemea, i te wā, nō te mea. Today we add the final complex structures — relative clauses (describing noun phrases) and purpose clauses (saying why you do something). These are the structures that make te reo truly sophisticated.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>RELATIVE CLAUSE (ai):</strong><br><strong>Ko ia te tangata i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%81whina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">āwhina</a> ai i a mātou.</strong><br>She is the person who helped us. (ai marks the relative clause)<br><br><strong>Ko tēnei te pukapuka e ako ana ngā ākonga.</strong><br>This is the book the students are learning from.<br><br><strong>PURPOSE CLAUSE (hei… / mō te…):</strong><br><strong>I haere au ki te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kura</a> hei ako i te reo Māori.</strong><br>I went to school in order to learn te reo Māori.<br><br><strong>I mahi kaha ia mō te toa i te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakataetae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataetae</a>.</strong><br>She worked hard in order to win the competition.<br><br><strong>REASON + PURPOSE together:</strong><br><strong>I ako au nō te mea kei te hiahia au ki te mōhio — hei āwhina i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> āpōpō.</strong><br>I learned because I want to know — in order to help my descendants tomorrow.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The relative clause marker <strong>ai</strong>:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ai appears at the END of a relative clause — it turns a sentence back to describe a noun:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko ia te tangata…&quot; + &quot;i āwhina ia i a mātou&quot; → &quot;Ko ia te tangata i āwhina <strong>ai</strong> i a mātou.&quot;<br>She is the person who helped us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ko tēnei te wā…&quot; + &quot;ka kōrero tātou i tēnei wā&quot; → &quot;Ko tēnei te wā ka kōrero <strong>ai</strong> tātou.&quot;<br>This is the time we speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Purpose clause: <strong>hei [verb]</strong> = in order to [verb]<br>&quot;I haere au ki te kura <strong>hei</strong> ako i te reo Māori.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>Ko tēnei te wā ka kōrero ai tātou.</strong><br>This is the time for us to speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ai at the end of a clause says: &quot;look back — this clause is describing the noun I mentioned earlier.&quot; Think of it as an arrow pointing backward to the noun.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Complex structures</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build relative clauses and purpose clauses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Relative clause: &quot;She is the person who taught us.&quot; → <strong>Ko ia te tangata i ako ai i a mātou.</strong></li><li>Purpose clause: &quot;I am learning te reo in order to speak with my whānau.&quot; → <strong>Kei te ako au i te reo Māori hei kōrero me tōku whānau.</strong></li><li>Combine: &quot;I worked hard because I want to succeed — in order to help my community.&quot; → <strong>I mahi kaha au nō te mea kei te hiahia au ki te angitu — hei āwhina i tōku hapori.</strong></li><li>Write your own: a relative clause about your teacher, and a purpose clause about why you are here.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Hei āwhina i ōku uri āpōpō&quot; — in order to help my descendants tomorrow. You are a Year 5-6 student. Your descendants are decades away. But you are already thinking about them through this language. What does it mean to learn something NOW for people who do not yet exist?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Complex sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write relative clauses and purpose clauses.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two relative clauses using ai: Ko ia te tangata/wāhi/wā… [ai clause].</li><li>Write two purpose clauses using hei: I/Kei te [action] au hei [purpose].</li><li>Combine reason and purpose: I [action] nō te mea… hei…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (8 sentences) about your te reo learning journey using: a relative clause (ai), a purpose clause (hei), ahakoa, nō te mea, Kua, and Ko te mutunga. Make every structure serve something true.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Extended narrative writing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended narrative · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary · Grammar Level 1–2: All narrative structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Extended narrative; AO 2.5 — Narrative vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — A full narrative in te reo Māori</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we write a complete narrative — beginning, middle, and end — using everything from four terms. This is the summit of te reo Māori writing for Year 5-6.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>BEGINNING — set the scene:</strong><br>I tērā wā, i tērā wā, i noho tētahi kōtiro, ko Hine, i roto i te ngahere nui o Aotearoa. He ngahere ātaahua, engari he ngahere mōhio hoki — i rongo ia i ngā mea katoa.<br><br><strong>MIDDLE — build, tension, crisis:</strong><br>I tētahi rā, ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=rongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rongo</a> a Hine i tētahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tangi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tangi</a> kino i roto i ngā rākau. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ohorere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ohorere</a> ia. Ka haere ia ki reira — ka kite ia i tētahi <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=manu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">manu</a> tino iti e mamae ana i tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=parirau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">parirau</a>.<br><br><strong>END — resolution, reflection:</strong><br>Ka tango a Hine i te manu. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a> ia i a ia ā te wā i ora ai. I te wā i rere anō te manu, ka tangi a Hine — ehara i te pōuri, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hari\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hari</a> kē. Ka huri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Narrative structure in te reo:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>BEGINNING: I tērā wā + scene + character introduction<br>MIDDLE: Ka sequence + ohorere moment + crisis<br>END: Resolution + reflection + Ka huri</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words in this narrative:<br><strong>parirau</strong> — wing<br><strong>i ora ai</strong> — until it recovered (ora = well, ai = relative marker)<br><strong>ehara i te pōuri</strong> — it was not sadness<br><strong>he hari kē</strong> — it was actually joy (kē = on the contrary)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the final line:<br>&quot;Ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē.&quot;<br>Not sadness — actually joy. Kē creates the surprise twist. The tears at the end are tears of happiness, not grief.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ehara i te [expected thing] — he [actual thing] kē. This is the te reo pattern for a plot twist or surprise revelation. &quot;It was not what you expected — it was actually this.&quot; Master this and your stories will always have a satisfying ending.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Build a narrative</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together we build a full narrative using the three-part structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEGINNING: I tērā wā… + character + setting. Who? Where? What is the feeling?</li><li>MIDDLE TENSION: Ka rongo ia i… + Ka ohorere ia. + Ka haere ia ki reira.</li><li>CRISIS: Ka kite ia i… — describe what they found. Use body vocabulary and E…ana.</li><li>END: Ka [resolution]. Ka [reflection]. Ehara i te [expected] — he [actual] kē. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tangi a Hine — ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē. The same action (crying) can mean opposite things. The kē creates the twist that reveals the true meaning. What does this teach us about the relationship between language and emotion? Can one word change everything?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Your pūrākau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a complete original narrative in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your BEGINNING (3 sentences): I tērā wā + character + setting.</li><li>Write your MIDDLE (4 sentences): rising action + Ka ohorere moment + crisis.</li><li>Write your END (3 sentences): resolution + reflection + Ka huri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full pūrākau (12 sentences minimum). Use: oral storytelling techniques (repetition, short Ka sentences, personification), all tense markers appropriately, body vocabulary, at least one relative clause (ai), and a kē twist ending.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero pūrākau — Storytelling conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Oral narrative · AO 2.5: Storytelling vocabulary · AO 1.1: Audience and formal register</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Oral narrative; AO 1.1 — Formal audience language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Stories as conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In te ao Māori, stories are not just for entertainment — they teach, they connect, they heal. Today we tell stories to each other and talk ABOUT stories — discussing what they mean, what they teach, and what they connect us to.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kōrero pūrākau pai rawa atu?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko te kōrero mō Māui — ko ia tētahi rangatira o ngā pūrākau Māori.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata — ahakoa he pakiaka noa ia, i toa ia i ngā mea tino nui.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tāu whakaaro. He aha tō pūrākau ake?<br><strong>B:</strong> I ngā rā o mua, ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kuia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kuia</a> te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=kaip%C5%ABr%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kaipūrākau</a> o tō mātou whānau. Ka noho mātou ā te pō, ka kōrero ia i ngā kōrero o ō mātou <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a>.<br><strong>A:</strong> He taonga tino nui tērā. Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New storytelling conversation vocabulary:<br><strong>kaipūrākau</strong> — storyteller (kai- + pūrākau)<br><strong>rangatira o ngā pūrākau</strong> — a chief of stories / a hero of legend<br><strong>pakiaka noa</strong> — just an ordinary person / of humble origin<br><strong>tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</strong> — what that story teaches is…<br><strong>ko tāu kōrero</strong> — your words / your telling<br><strong>He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe?</strong><br>What does that story teach you?<br><strong>Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te [lesson].</strong><br>What that story teaches is [lesson].</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te [lesson] — what that story teaches is [lesson]. This is the te reo pattern for discussing the moral or meaning of a story. Learn it and you can discuss any pūrākau.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Storytelling conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a conversation about a story you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tō kōrero pūrākau pai rawa atu? Name a story and say why: Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko… nō te mea…</li><li>I ask: He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana i a koe? Answer: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>I ask: He aha tō pūrākau ake? Do you have your own family story? Share a detail.</li><li>I say: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero. You respond with Tēnā koe hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata — what that story teaches is the bravery of a person. Every great story teaches something through action, not through statement. What story from your own life — something that actually happened to you — would make a good pūrākau? What would it teach?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He kōrero pūrākau</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tell and discuss stories in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite story (from any culture) and say why: Ko tōku pūrākau pai rawa atu ko… nō te mea…</li><li>Write what it teaches: Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>Describe a kaipūrākau from your own life — someone who told you stories.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a storytelling conversation (10 lines) with a partner — introduce a story, discuss what it teaches, share a memory of being told a story, and close with Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I tērā wā, i tērā wā…&quot; What is the purpose of this opening phrase?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It gives the exact date of the story </li><li>Repetition signals entry into story time — leaving ordinary time ✅</li><li>It means &quot;once upon a time&quot; and nothing more </li><li>It is a formal greeting before a story </li></ul></li><li>What is the traditional te reo Māori story ending?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nō reira ka mutu. </li><li>Tērā ia te kōrero. </li><li>Ka huri. ✅</li><li>Ko te mutunga. </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ia te tangata i āwhina ai i a mātou.&quot; What does ai indicate?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past tense </li><li>The end of a relative clause — pointing back to describe &quot;te tangata&quot; ✅</li><li>Future intention </li><li>A question marker </li></ul></li><li>&quot;I haere au ki te kura hei ako i te reo Māori.&quot; What does hei indicate here?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Past tense of going </li><li>A purpose clause — in order to learn ✅</li><li>A negative </li><li>A question </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ehara i te pōuri, he hari kē.&quot; What does kē signal in this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The feeling is past </li><li>The feeling is very strong </li><li>Contrary to expectation — it was actually joy, not sadness ✅</li><li>The speaker is confused </li></ul></li><li>What are the three parts of a narrative structure in te reo?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Introduction, body, conclusion </li><li>Beginning (scene), middle (tension/crisis), end (resolution/reflection) ✅</li><li>Problem, solution, evaluation </li><li>Past, present, future </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a &quot;kaipūrākau&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a type of story </li><li>a storyteller (kai- + pūrākau) ✅</li><li>a traditional opening phrase </li><li>a formal greeting </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te māia o te tangata.&quot; What does this sentence discuss?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The title of the story </li><li>The author of the story </li><li>What the story teaches — the bravery of a person ✅</li><li>How long the story is </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero māpuna — Figurative language and imagery":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Figurative language · AO 2.3: Extended expressive communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Figurative language; AO 2.3 — Imagery &amp; metaphor</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Language that paints pictures</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori is extraordinarily rich in figurative language — metaphor, simile, and imagery are woven into everyday speech. Today we explore the most beautiful and most useful figures of speech in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>SIMILE (āhua… ana):</strong><br><strong>E oma ana ia āhua te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whatitiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whatitiri</a>.</strong><br>She runs like thunder.<br><br><strong>METAPHOR (Ko [X] ko [Y]):</strong><br><strong>Ko te reo he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i ngā tāngata.</strong><br>Language is a cord that weaves people together.<br><br><strong>PERSONIFICATION:</strong><br><strong>Ka tangi ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=maunga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">maunga</a> i ngā ua.</strong><br>The mountains weep in the rain.<br><br><strong>IMAGERY:</strong><br><strong>He rau <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Dpura\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">kōpura</a> tōna kanohi i tōna kata.</strong><br>Her face was a golden leaf when she laughed. (kōpura = gold/copper)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori figures of speech:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Simile</strong> uses āhua (like/as) or me he (like a):<br>&quot;E oma ana ia āhua te whatitiri.&quot; — runs like thunder<br>&quot;Ka māia ia me he maunga.&quot; — brave like a mountain</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Metaphor</strong> uses Ko:<br>&quot;Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata.&quot;<br>Language is a cord that weaves people together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Personification</strong> — giving nature human actions:<br>&quot;Ka tangi ngā maunga.&quot; — The mountains weep.<br>&quot;Ka karanga te moana.&quot; — The sea calls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New vocabulary:<br><strong>aho</strong> — cord / string / thread<br><strong>whiri</strong> — to weave / plait / braid<br><strong>kōpura</strong> — gold / copper (colour)<br><strong>māia</strong> — brave (you know this)<br><strong>rau kōpura</strong> — golden leaf</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — language is a cord that weaves people together. This is one of the most beautiful metaphors in te reo Māori. Learn it cold. Use it when you want to explain why language matters.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Figurative sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build similes, metaphors, and personification in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simile: &quot;She works like the wind.&quot; → <strong>E mahi ana ia āhua te hau.</strong> Build your own simile about a classmate.</li><li>Metaphor: &quot;He is a pillar of the community.&quot; → <strong>Ko ia he pou o te hapori.</strong> Build a Ko metaphor.</li><li>Personification: give a natural element a human action. <strong>Ka waiata te awa i ngā kōrero o tōna ara.</strong></li><li>Write a simile + a metaphor about te reo Māori itself.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — language is a cord that weaves people together. What does it mean for language to WEAVE people? What happens to a community when the cord is cut? When has language created a connection for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Figurative language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use similes, metaphors, and personification in your writing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write two similes using āhua or me he.</li><li>Write two Ko metaphors about people, places, or language.</li><li>Write two personification sentences using natural elements.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a paragraph (8 sentences) about your year of te reo Māori learning — using at least one simile, one metaphor, and one personification. Make it beautiful.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He kōrero whakaaro — Expressing opinions with sophistication":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: Sophisticated opinion structures</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Sophisticated opinions; AO 2.5 — Evaluative language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Saying what you think, elegantly</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have Ko tāku whakaaro (my thought), He pai ake (better), and debate language. Today we add the final layer of opinion expression — how to say what you think with nuance, qualification, and elegance.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>STATING a strong opinion:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=E+ai+ki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">E ai ki</a> āku whakaaro…</strong> — According to my thinking…<br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%AB%C4%81huatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūāhuatanga</a> ko te…</strong> — My position is that…<br><br><strong>QUALIFYING an opinion:</strong><br><strong>I ētahi wā…</strong> — Sometimes…<br><strong>Ka mutu, ko tōku whakaaro…</strong> — All things considered, my view is…<br><strong>Ahakoa ōna āhuatanga…</strong> — Despite its qualities…<br><br><strong>CONCEDING a point:</strong><br><strong>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tika</a> tāu ki tērā āhuatanga.</strong> — You are right about that point.<br><strong>Āe, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pono</a> tērā — engari rā…</strong> — Yes, that is true — however…<br><br><strong>CLOSING a view:</strong><br><strong>Nō reira, ko tōku whakaaro <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dtika\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōtika</a> ko te…</strong><br>Therefore, my considered view is…</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sophisticated opinion structures:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>E ai ki āku whakaaro</strong> — more formal than Ko tāku whakaaro<br><strong>Ka mutu</strong> — all things considered / when it is done<br><strong>mōtika</strong> — correct / right / genuine<br><strong>Ko tōku whakaaro mōtika</strong> — my considered/genuine view<br><strong>He pono tērā</strong> — that is true<br><strong>Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…</strong><br>Yes, that is true — however…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>mōtika</strong> — correct / genuine / right<br><strong>Ka mutu</strong> — all things considered / when all is said<br><strong>E ai ki</strong> — according to<br><strong>tūāhuatanga</strong> — position / characteristic</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā. &quot;Yes, that is true — however.&quot; This is the most graceful way to concede a point before making your counter-argument. It shows you have listened. In te ao Māori, showing you have heard someone before you respond is a mark of respect.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Sophisticated opinions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express opinions with nuance and elegance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Strong position: E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo Māori te taonga nui rawa atu o Aotearoa.</li><li>Qualify: Ka mutu, ko tōku whakaaro… + your opinion on a topic with some nuance.</li><li>Concede + counter: Āe, he pono tērā… engari rā… + your counter-argument.</li><li>Close: Nō reira, ko tōku whakaaro mōtika ko te… + your final position.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā. When you say &quot;yes, that is true&quot; before arguing your point, you show you have genuinely listened. This is the opposite of winning an argument — it is trying to reach understanding. What would happen if all debates worked this way?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Sophisticated opinion writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a sophisticated opinion piece in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>State position: E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko [topic] te [opinion].</li><li>Qualify: Ka mutu, i ētahi wā… (acknowledge complexity).</li><li>Concede and counter: Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full opinion piece (8 sentences) about te reo Māori, kaitiakitanga, or education. Use: E ai ki āku whakaaro, Ka mutu, Āe he pono tērā engari rā, Ko tōku whakaaro mōtika, and Ko te mutunga.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Year-long structure review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs Terms 1–4 · All grammar structures consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full year review; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The complete four-term picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is Term 4 Week 3. Today we put the entire year on the table — every structure, every domain, every AO — and see the full picture of what you have built.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>EVERYTHING you can now do:</strong><br><br>GREET &amp; FAREWELL at every level of formality<br>INTRODUCE yourself with a full pepeha<br>DESCRIBE people, places, feelings, weather, nature, arts<br>RECOUNT past events with I and Ka<br>NARRATE stories with Ka and oral techniques<br>EXPRESS ongoing actions with E…ana and Kua<br>COMMIT to future actions with māku e and Ka taea<br>NEGATE in present and past with Kāore<br>ARGUE and persuade with taunakitanga and discourse markers<br>EXPLAIN meanings with Ko te tikanga o…<br>EVALUATE learning with ngāwari/uaua and Ka taea<br>DISCUSS culture, art, environment, science, rights<br>PERFORM a pepeha, a pōhiri, a whaikōrero</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The year in one sentence:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>&quot;Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga māia, he kaitiaki o tēnei reo rangatira; e ako tonu ana au, kua tipu tōku reo, ā ka kōrero māmā ake au i ia rā, i ia rā — nō reira, ko tōku moemoeā: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.&quot;</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Ko identity<br>→ He description ×2<br>→ E…ana progressive<br>→ Kua perfect<br>→ Ka narrative<br>→ Nō reira conclusion<br>→ Ko moemoeā dream<br>→ Ka toitū endurance<br>→ Relative: mō ōku uri ā āpōpō (for my future descendants)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is one year of daily practice in one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Read that sentence aloud. Slowly. Every word of it is yours. You built this. Ka tino pai tāu mahi.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Year-long blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The fastest comprehensive review of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA: One student — full nine lines from memory. Class supports.</li><li>STRUCTURES: Call out a structure — class gives an example sentence. Ko, He, Kei te, I, Ka, Kua, E…ana, Kāore, ahakoa, ai, hei…</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ: All four from Term 3 Week 8 — who can say them all?</li><li>SHOWCASE: Each student says the single best te reo sentence they have ever produced.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka tino pai tāu mahi — your work is genuinely, truly excellent. Not just good — tino good. You have earned this. What would your Week 1 Term 1 self think if they heard the sentence you just said?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Four-term audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final comprehensive vocabulary and structure audit.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them. Compare to Week 1.</li><li>Write one sentence using each of the 14 structures from the I Do.</li><li>Write your best sentence of the year — and why it is your best.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the year-summary sentence above in your own words — make every part true for you. Then write a second version that is even better.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero māmā — Spontaneous conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · AO 2.3: Spontaneous extended conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Spontaneous conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary in conversation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The spontaneous conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The highest goal of language learning is spontaneous conversation — talking freely, without a script, responding to what the other person actually says. Today we practise this. No models, no sentence frames — just conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>CONVERSATION STARTERS you know:</strong><br>He pēhea tōu wiki? · He aha ngā mea hou? · Ko ēhea āu hiahia?<br>He aha tō whakaaro mō…? · He aha tāu e tūmanako ana?<br>Ko wai tōu hoa pai rawa atu? · He aha tā tērā pūrākau e ako ana?<br><br><strong>CONVERSATION EXTENDERS:</strong><br><strong>Ā nā?</strong> — And then? / What happened next?<br><strong>Pērā?</strong> — Like that? / Really?<br><strong>He aha tāu e kī ana?</strong> — What do you mean?<br><strong>Ka pai tāu kōrero — heoi anō…</strong> — Good point — however…<br><strong>Nā wai rā…</strong> — Eventually… / As time went on…<br><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — Exactly! / That's it!</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tools for keeping a conversation going:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā nā?</strong> — the simplest conversation extender. &quot;And then?&quot; invites the other person to continue.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Pērā?</strong> — &quot;Like that?&quot; shows you are surprised and engaged.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — &quot;Exactly!&quot; shows enthusiastic agreement. Use it to confirm and encourage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Nā wai rā…</strong> — &quot;Eventually / as time went on…&quot; moves a story forward in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He aha tāu e kī ana?</strong> — &quot;What do you mean?&quot; — asking for clarification. This is one of the most important phrases in any language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>Ā koia!</strong> — Exactly! That's it! (an emphatic agreement)<br><strong>Nā wai rā</strong> — Eventually / Over time / As things progressed</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ā nā? is the single most useful conversation extender in any language. &quot;And then?&quot; makes the other person feel listened to and invites them to continue. Use it constantly.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Spontaneous conversation practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. Teacher gives a starting topic — students talk freely. No scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Topic 1: He pēhea tōu wiki? Start — extend with Ā nā? — agree with Ā koia! — disagree with Āe, he pono tērā engari rā…</li><li>Topic 2: He aha tōu moemoeā mō te reo Māori? Start — ask He aha tāu e kī ana? for clarification.</li><li>Topic 3: He aha te pūrākau pai rawa atu ki a koe? Start — extend with Nā wai rā…</li><li>Topic 4: He aha tō whakaaro mō tō kura? Start — use Pērā? for surprise.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: He aha tāu e kī ana? — What do you mean? This question shows that you are genuinely trying to understand, not just waiting for your turn to speak. In te ao Māori, listening and understanding before responding is a value. How would conversations change if everyone used this question more?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Free conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a fully spontaneous conversation with a partner in te reo Māori — 5 minutes, no script, any topic.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your topic together — something you both genuinely care about.</li><li>Use at least three conversation extenders: Ā nā, Pērā, Ā koia, He aha tāu e kī ana.</li><li>Try to include at least one whakataukī or figurative expression naturally.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Record your conversation (written transcript or voice note). After, count how many different structures and vocabulary domains you used naturally. This is evidence of your fluency.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E oma ana ia āhua te whatitiri.&quot; What figure of speech is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>metaphor </li><li>personification </li><li>simile ✅</li><li>alliteration </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata.&quot; What does aho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>river </li><li>cord / string / thread ✅</li><li>mountain </li><li>ancestor </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E ai ki āku whakaaro…&quot; What does this phrase signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A question is coming </li><li>A formal greeting </li><li>The speaker is about to state their opinion ✅</li><li>A past event </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Āe, he pono tērā — engari rā…&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Agreeing completely </li><li>Conceding a point graciously before making a counter-argument ✅</li><li>Ending the discussion </li><li>Asking for clarification </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which sentence correctly uses the relative clause marker ai?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ko ia te tangata ai āwhina i a mātou </li><li>Ko ia te tangata i āwhina ai i a mātou ✅</li><li>Ko ia te tangata āwhina ai </li><li>I āwhina ai ia ko ia te tangata </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka tino pai tāu mahi.&quot; What does tino add to this sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It makes the sentence past tense </li><li>It makes the praise genuinely / truly excellent — more emphatic ✅</li><li>It makes it a question </li><li>It negates the sentence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ā koia!&quot; What does this expression mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>And then? </li><li>What do you mean? </li><li>Exactly! / That's it! ✅</li><li>Eventually </li></ul></li><li>&quot;He aha tāu e kī ana?&quot; — What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Agreeing </li><li>Asking for clarification — what do you mean? ✅</li><li>Ending the conversation </li><li>Giving an opinion </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō āpōpō — Future, plans, and aspirations":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.4: Future time · Grammar Level 1–2: Future tense mastery</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Future &amp; aspirations; AO 2.3 — Extended planning language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about the future with confidence</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have hiahia, māku e, Ka taea, and Ka for future narrative. Today we consolidate the full future toolkit and add the most aspirational language — talking about what you hope for, dream of, and intend to make happen.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>IMMEDIATE FUTURE (Ka):</strong><br><strong>Ka haere au āpōpō.</strong> — I will go tomorrow.<br><br><strong>INTENTION (māku/māna e):</strong><br><strong>Māku e tiaki i tēnei reo.</strong> — I will protect this language.<br><br><strong>HOPE (tūmanako):</strong><br><strong>Kei te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C5%ABmanako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tūmanako</a> au ka [future hope].</strong><br>I hope that [future hope] will happen.<br><br><strong>DREAM (moemoeā):</strong><br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=moemoe%C4%81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moemoeā</a>: ka [dream].</strong><br>My dream is: that [dream] will happen.<br><br><strong>ASPIRATION (wawata):</strong><br><strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>: ka [aspiration].</strong><br>My aspiration is: [aspiration].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The future language spectrum:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>IMMEDIATE → Ka [verb] āpōpō (happening soon)<br>INTENTION → Māku e [verb] (I commit to this)<br>HOPE → Kei te tūmanako au ka… (I hope this happens)<br>DREAM → Ko tōku moemoeā: ka… (deep personal dream)<br>ASPIRATION → Ko tōku wawata: ka… (highest aspiration)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>wawata</strong> — aspiration / deepest longing / what you reach for<br><strong>wawata</strong> is stronger than moemoeā — it is not just a dream but a deep reaching-toward</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New word:<br><strong>wawata</strong> — deep aspiration / yearning<br><strong>angitu</strong> — success / achievement<br><strong>Ka angitu koe.</strong> — You will succeed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Moemoeā = dream (what you hope for in sleep and waking). Wawata = what you reach toward with your whole being — the deepest aspiration. Use moemoeā for personal dreams, wawata for the things that define your life direction.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Future language spectrum</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Express the same idea at different levels of future commitment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Immediate Ka: <strong>Ka ako au i te reo Māori āpōpō.</strong> Now make it an intention: <strong>Māku e ako i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Hope: <strong>Kei te tūmanako au ka [something you hope will happen to your class next year].</strong></li><li>Dream: <strong>Ko tōku moemoeā: ka kōrero tōku whānau katoa i te reo Māori.</strong></li><li>Aspiration: <strong>Ko tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku wawata — my deepest aspiration. The word wawata contains the idea of reaching outward and upward. In te ao Māori, a wawata is not just a personal desire — it often connects to the wellbeing of others. What is YOUR wawata? Does it only affect you — or does it reach further?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Future and aspiration language</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about your future using all five levels of the future language spectrum.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a Ka sentence about something happening soon.</li><li>Write a māku e commitment about something you will do.</li><li>Write a tūmanako hope about something you hope will happen.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full future statement (8 sentences) — immediate plans, intentions, hopes, a moemoeā, and a wawata. Make the wawata the most ambitious and true thing you say. Close with Ka angitu koe — You will succeed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Ngā kupu hou whakamutunga — Final new vocabulary for the year":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Advanced vocabulary · AO 2.3: Extended communication · All AOs</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Advanced vocabulary; AO 2.3 — Full communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The final vocabulary additions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the last new vocabulary words of the year — carefully chosen to round out your te reo Māori and give you a few more powerful tools for the end-of-year assessment and beyond.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>For describing growth and change:</strong><br><strong>tipu</strong> — grow (you know this) · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=huri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">huri</a></strong> — change / turn · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hou\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hou</a></strong> — new<br><strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro.</strong> — My thinking has become new / I think differently.<br><br><strong>For describing connection:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a></strong> — to connect / link<br><strong>Ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata.</strong><br>This language connects people.<br><br><strong>For describing achievement:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=angitu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">angitu</a></strong> — success · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa</a></strong> — win/champion · <strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=eke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eke</a></strong> — rise/climb/score<br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=angitu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">angitu</a> koe i āu mahi.</strong> — You will succeed in your work.<br><br><strong>For describing legacy:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a></strong> — passed down / handed down (generations) / handed down (generations)<br><strong>taonga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a></strong> — a treasure handed down<br><strong>He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo.</strong> — This language is a treasure handed down.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final vocabulary — how each word connects to the year:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>honohono</strong> — connects back to aho (cord) and whiri (weave). Language connects.<br><strong>angitu</strong> — connects back to Ka taea (able) and toa (champion). You have succeeded.<br><strong>tuku iho</strong> — connects back to uri (descendants) and tīpuna (ancestors). The chain of generations.<br><strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro</strong> — connects back to whakaaro (thought) and tipu (grow). Your thinking has grown new.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are not random new words — each one closes a circle from earlier in the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New compound:<br><strong>honohono</strong> = hono (join) + hono (repeated for intensity) = to deeply connect</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo — this language is a treasure passed down. You have heard taonga throughout the year. You have heard tuku iho in Term 3. Now they come together in your final week. The phrase was always waiting for you at the end of the year.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final vocabulary in action</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use each new word in a sentence that connects it back to the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honohono: <strong>Ka honohono tēnei reo i ōku hoa, i ōku tīpuna, i ōku uri katoa.</strong> Who does it connect?</li><li>Angitu: <strong>Ka angitu koe — kua tino pai tāu mahi i tēnei tau.</strong> What has been your angitu?</li><li>Tuku iho: <strong>He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo — nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.</strong></li><li>Hou: <strong>Ka hou tōku whakaaro mō te reo Māori i tēnei tau.</strong> How has your thinking changed?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri — from my ancestors to me, and from me to my descendants. You are in the middle of this chain. What did your ancestors give you through language? What will you give your descendants?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final vocabulary sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the final year vocabulary in sentences that are true and meaningful.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a honohono sentence about what te reo Māori connects you to.</li><li>Write an angitu sentence about something you have achieved this year.</li><li>Write a taonga tuku iho sentence about the language.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a four-sentence statement about your year of te reo Māori — using honohono, angitu, tuku iho, and Ka hou tōku whakaaro. Make every word count.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The year in sentences":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types · Full year synthesis</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year synthesis; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we do something special — we write about the whole year. Not as a summary, but as a piece of genuine te reo expression that captures what this year of language learning has been and meant.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A year in nine sentences — one structure per term/topic:</strong><br><br>Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga, he kaitiaki, he tangata o tēnei ao. (Ko · He)<br>I <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABmata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīmata</a> au i tētahi kupu inanahi — ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea hou. (I · Kua)<br>E ako tonu ana au — e huri ana tōku whakaaro i ia rā, i ia rā. (E…ana ×2)<br>Ka taea e au te mihi, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i te reo Māori. (Ka taea ×3)<br>Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i a au ki ngā tīpuna, ki ngā uri, ki ngā hoa o tōku ao. (Ko metaphor · ai)<br>Ahakoa he uauatanga, i mahi tonu au — nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku ara. (Ahakoa · Nō te mea · Ko)<br>He taonga <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tuku+iho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tuku iho</a> tēnei reo — māku e tiaki, māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaahua\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaahua</a> ake. (He · māku e ×2)<br>Nō reira, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo rangatira mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. (Nō reira · Ko wawata · Ka)<br>Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri. (Ka ×3 · Ka huri)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the structures in those nine sentences:<br>Ko (×5), He (×4), I (past), Kua (perfect), E…ana (×2), Ka taea (×3), ai (relative), Ahakoa, Nō te mea, māku e (×2), Ko wawata, Ka (×4), Ka huri</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That is the whole year in nine sentences. You can write every one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Notice the final line:<br>&quot;Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri.&quot;<br>You will succeed. We will all succeed. The telling turns.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even the ending is a pūrākau technique — Ka huri closes the year the way it closes a story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka huri — the telling turns. This traditional story ending can also close a year. The year was a story. It had a beginning, a middle, and an end. Ka huri.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Write the year together</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Class writes one shared &quot;year in sentences&quot; — each student contributes one sentence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Student 1: A Ko identity sentence about who we are as a class.</li><li>Student 2: A Kua sentence about what has been completed this year.</li><li>Student 3: An E…ana sentence about what is still ongoing in our learning.</li><li>Continue until every student has contributed. Close together: Ka angitu tātou katoa. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka angitu koe. Ka angitu mātou katoa. Ka huri. Success is named for you individually, then for all of us together, then the year turns. Why does the order matter — you first, then all of us? What does it say about how achievement in te ao Māori is understood?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The year in your sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own &quot;year in sentences&quot; — nine sentences, one per major structure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write sentences 1–3: Ko identity, Kua perfect, E…ana progressive.</li><li>Write sentences 4–6: Ka taea ability, Ko metaphor with ai, Ahakoa + nō te mea.</li><li>Write sentences 7–9: māku e commitment, Ko wawata, Ka huri.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Refine all nine until each one is truly beautiful and true. Read the whole passage aloud. This is your year. Own it.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero whakaaro — A philosophical conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended sophisticated conversation · All AOs</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Philosophical conversation; AO 2.5 — Sophisticated vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Talking about big ideas</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The most advanced thing you can do in a language is discuss abstract ideas — concepts like time, identity, belonging, language, and legacy. Today we have that conversation in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe ināianei, i tērā tau ake?<br><strong>B:</strong> I tērā tau, he mea ngaro noa iho te reo ki a au. Ināianei, ko ia tōku ara, tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hono</a> ki ngā tīpuna.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai tērā. He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki te ao hou — arā, ki ngā kupu o ōku tīpuna, ki ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C5%8Dhiotanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mōhiotanga</a> hou, ki ōku hoa hou.<br><strong>A:</strong> E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata katoa. He tika tāu whakaaro?<br><strong>B:</strong> Āe, he tika — waihoki, ko te reo he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> ki te ao mārama. Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> ia i te ao o nāianei ki te ao o ōku tīpuna.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=h%C5%8Dhonu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hōhonu</a>.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe hoki.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New philosophical vocabulary:<br><strong>he mea ngaro noa iho</strong> — just an invisible/lost thing (ngaro = invisible/lost, noa iho = just/merely)<br><strong>ara ki te ao hou</strong> — a path to the new world<br><strong>māramatanga ki te ao mārama</strong> — enlightenment to the world of light<br><strong>ao o nāianei</strong> — the world of today (nāianei = nowadays)<br><strong>tino hōhonu</strong> — very deep / profound (hōhonu = deep)<br><strong>Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.</strong><br>Thank you for your very profound words.<br><strong>he aho e whiri ana</strong> — a cord that weaves (from Week 3!)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — the language is an enlightenment to the world of light. This brings together māramatanga (understanding/enlightenment) and ao mārama (world of light — both the realm of the living and the realm of clarity). Language as light.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Philosophical conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a conversation about language, identity, and meaning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe ināianei? Answer with a metaphor or deep statement.</li><li>I ask: He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe? Answer: I homai mai e ia ki a au he…</li><li>I make a claim: Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata. You agree or add: Āe, waihoki…</li><li>I close: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu. You respond: Tēnā koe hoki.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama. Language as light, as enlightenment, as access to a brighter world. What does language actually give you access to that you would not have without it? Think specifically — what can you understand, feel, or know in te reo Māori that you cannot in English?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Philosophical writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own philosophical reflection on language and identity.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer: He aha te tikanga o te reo ki a koe? (Not &quot;te reo Māori is important&quot; — something deeper.)</li><li>Write a metaphor: Ko te reo he [metaphor] — with an e…ana relative clause.</li><li>Write what language has given you: I homai mai e ia ki a au he…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full philosophical reflection (8 sentences) — what language means to you, what it has given you, a whakataukī, a metaphor, a wawata, and close with Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — ko tērā tōku whakapono.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the difference between moemoeā and wawata?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Moemoeā is a dream; wawata is a deeper aspiration/yearning — what you reach toward with your whole being ✅</li><li>Moemoeā is formal; wawata is casual </li><li>Wawata is about the past; moemoeā is about the future </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka angitu koe.&quot; What does angitu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>you will leave </li><li>you will succeed / achieve ✅</li><li>you will speak </li><li>you will learn </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;He taonga tuku iho tēnei reo.&quot; What does tuku iho mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>recently created </li><li>belonging to one person </li><li>passed down through generations ✅</li><li>difficult to learn </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;honohono&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to weave clothing </li><li>to separate / divide </li><li>to deeply connect / link ✅</li><li>to speak formally </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The passage ends with &quot;Ka huri.&quot; In what other context is this phrase used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>As a formal greeting </li><li>As a weather expression </li><li>As the traditional close of an oral story (pūrākau) ✅</li><li>As a way to say goodbye </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ia tōku ara, tōku hono ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What two things is the language being called?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a path and a cord of connection ✅</li><li>a treasure and a dream </li><li>a challenge and a reward </li><li>a light and a gift </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te reo he māramatanga ki te ao mārama.&quot; What does māramatanga mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>darkness / confusion </li><li>difficulty / challenge </li><li>enlightenment / understanding / clarity ✅</li><li>silence </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.&quot; What does hōhonu mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>short </li><li>confusing </li><li>loud </li><li>deep / profound ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He kōrero mō ōku ara ako — My learning pathways":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended reflection · All AOs consolidated · Whakaaro ako (metacognition)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Learning reflection; AO 2.5 — Metacognitive vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Thinking about your thinking</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this second-to-last week, we turn inward — thinking about HOW we have learned, what strategies worked, and what kind of learner we have become. This is metacognition — thinking about thinking — expressed in te reo Māori.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>He aha tāu <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ara+ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ara ako</a>?</strong> — What is your learning strategy/pathway?<br><br><strong>Ko tōku ara ako ko te [strategy].</strong><br>My learning strategy is [strategy].<br><br><strong>Ko ngā ara ako pai ki a au ko ēnei:</strong><br>he kōrero, he <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakarongo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakarongo</a>, he tuhituhi, he mahi tahi.<br>The learning strategies that work for me are: speaking, listening, writing, working together.<br><br><strong>I tipu ōku ara ako i tēnei tau.</strong><br>My learning strategies grew this year.<br><br><strong>Ka pai ake tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ako\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ako</a> ina [condition].</strong><br>I learn better when [condition].</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Metacognitive vocabulary:<br><strong>ara ako</strong> — learning pathway / learning strategy<br><strong>whakaaro hōhonu</strong> — deep thinking / reflection<br><strong>Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au.</strong><br>I learn better when I speak.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He mōhiotanga ōku e pā ana ki te reo Māori.</strong><br>I have knowledge relating to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i…</strong><br>My understanding of [X] has become much better.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I tipu ōku ara ako i tēnei tau nō te mea…</strong><br>My learning strategies grew this year because…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New words:<br><strong>metacognition (whakaaro mō te whakaaro)</strong><br><strong>mōhiotanga</strong> — knowledge / understanding<br><strong>e pā ana ki</strong> — relating to / concerning</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina [condition] ana au. &quot;I learn better when I [do this].&quot; This is one of the most useful self-knowledge sentences in any language. Knowing HOW you learn best is as important as what you learn.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Learning reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build sentences about how you learn best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ask: He aha tāu ara ako pai rawa atu? Answer: Ko tōku ara ako pai rawa atu ko te [strategy].</li><li>I ask: Ka pai ake tōu ako ina aha? Answer: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina [condition].</li><li>State growth: <strong>Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i ngā hanganga o te reo Māori i tēnei tau.</strong></li><li>State future strategy: <strong>Māku e [strategy] i muri ake nei hei tiaki i tōku reo.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au — I learn better when I speak. Does this describe you? What IS your best learning strategy? If you could tell next year's te reo students one thing about how to learn it, what would you tell them?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Learning reflection writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a reflection on your learning strategies and growth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your best learning strategy: Ko tōku ara ako pai rawa atu ko te…</li><li>Write when you learn best: Ka pai ake tōku ako ina…</li><li>Write your growth: Kua tino pai ake tōku mōhio i… i tēnei tau.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full learning reflection (8 sentences) — your strategies, your growth, what was hard, what worked, what you wish you had done differently, and your advice to a future learner.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He hononga ki te ao — Connections to the world":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land and people · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Connections to world; AO 2.1 — Relationships &amp; community</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What te reo Māori has connected you to</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te reo Māori is not just a communication tool — it is a connector. Today we reflect on everything this language has connected you to across the year: people, places, ancestors, community, nature, and the future.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo:</strong><br>These are the connections this language has made:<br><br><strong>Ko ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=hoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hoa</a> hou</strong> — my new friends<br><strong>Ko ōku kaiako</strong> — my teachers<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=t%C4%ABpuna\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tīpuna</a> o Aotearoa</strong> — the ancestors of New Zealand<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=uri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uri</a> ō āpōpō</strong> — the descendants of tomorrow<br><strong>Ko te taiao o Aotearoa</strong> — the natural world of New Zealand<br><strong>Ko ngā <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tikanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tikanga</a> o tōku ao</strong> — the customs of my world<br><br><strong>Ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> tēnei reo i ōku whakaaro, i ōku ngākau, i ōku ara katoa.</strong><br>This language connects my thoughts, my heart, and all my pathways.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflecting on connections:</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the connections built by this language:<br>(hanga = build/make, ai = relative marker, e = by — passive)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki…</strong><br>This language connected me to…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Ka honohono tonu tēnei reo i a au ki ngā tāngata o tēnei ao.</strong><br>This language will continue to connect me to the people of this world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>i hanga ai</strong> — that was/has been built (ai = relative past)<br><strong>Ko ērā ngā [things] i hanga ai e tēnei reo.</strong><br>Those are the [things] built by this language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo — those are the connections built by this language. The passive (i hanga ai e) puts the language as the agent — it is the one that builds the connections. Language does something to you; you do not just use it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Connection reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on what te reo Māori has connected you to.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo: + Ko ēnei list of three connections.</li><li>I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki [specific connection] nō te mea…</li><li>Ka honohono tonu tēnei reo i a au ki [who/what] ā tōna wā.</li><li>Close: Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka honohono tēnei reo i ōku whakaaro, i ōku ngākau, i ōku ara katoa — this language connects my thoughts, my heart, and all my pathways. Thoughts, heart, and pathways — three dimensions of a person. What would it mean for a language to connect all three? Has te reo Māori done that for you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Connection writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about the connections te reo Māori has made for you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo + three specific connections.</li><li>Write I honohono tēnei reo i a au ki [specific] nō te mea…</li><li>Write what you hope it will connect you to in the future.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full connection reflection (8 sentences) — what te reo has connected you to (people, ancestors, nature, culture), how, why it matters, and your hope for those connections to grow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The end-of-year showcase":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · All sentence types · Full year showcase</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year showcase; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your finest te reo Māori of the year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate Wednesday lesson of the year. Today is the showcase — not a review, not an audit. A celebration. You write your finest te reo Māori. Not the most complex, not the longest — the most true, the most beautiful, the most you.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">The finest te reo sentences of the year — from any of you:<br><br><strong>&quot;Ko tōku reo he ara māmā ki ngā tīpuna — ka huri noa tōku ao i ngā kupu o ōku mōrearea, ā ka mārama ake i ngā kupu o tōku reo.&quot;</strong><br>(My language is a light path to the ancestors — my world just shifts in the words of my worries, and grows clearer in the words of my language.)<br><br><strong>&quot;He ākonga noa iho ahau — engari ko tōku ngākau e tipu ana i ōku kupu i ia rā, i ia rā.&quot;</strong><br>(I am just a learner — but my heart grows in my words day by day.)<br><br><strong>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot;</strong><br>(When the language endures, my belief endures, and I endure too.)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What makes these sentences the finest:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ They use simple words in unexpected combinations<br>→ They reveal something true about the person<br>→ They use structures naturally, not as demonstrations<br>→ They would lose something if translated to English</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three Ka toitū. The language endures. My belief endures. And then — ka toitū hoki AHAU. I endure too. The speaker is not just an observer of endurance — they are part of it. The final line makes the abstract personal.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is what fluency looks like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: The finest sentence you can write is not the one that uses the most structures — it is the one that uses exactly the right words to say exactly what you mean. Less is often more. Ka toitū ahau. That is enough.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Showcase sentences</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and share your finest sentences. Class listens — no feedback, just appreciation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write in silence for 5 minutes — your finest sentence. No structure requirements.</li><li>Write a second sentence that continues the first.</li><li>Read both aloud to a partner. They listen. No comments — just presence.</li><li>Class share: each person reads their best sentence. Everyone listens.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau. When the language endures, I endure. There is something radical about this claim — that YOUR survival is connected to the survival of te reo Māori. Do you believe it? In what sense might it be true?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The year showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your finest te reo Māori of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your finest SENTENCE — not the most complex, the most true.</li><li>Write your finest PARAGRAPH — three to five sentences, beautifully connected.</li><li>Write your YEAR CLOSER — one sentence that closes your year of te reo Māori.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your complete &quot;end of year te reo portfolio piece&quot; — ten sentences that capture who you were, what you learned, what connected you, what you believe, and where you are going. Read it aloud to someone you trust.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He kōrero whakamutunga — The final conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Full year assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full-year conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second-to-last lesson of the year. Today we see what a fully fluent Year 5-6 student can do in te reo Māori — the result of four terms of daily practice.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? He aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full pepeha — all nine lines]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao — ko ērā ngā mea e mau ana i a au.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe i tēnei tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki ngā tīpuna, he hononga ki ngā uri, ā he māramatanga ki tōku ake ao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata mō tō reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri — ā, ka honohono ia i ngā tāngata katoa o tēnei ao ātaahua.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe hoki. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation demonstrates every AO:<br>✓ AO 1.1: Tēnā koe + Ka nui ōku mihi<br>✓ AO 1.2: Full pepeha<br>✓ AO 1.4: Identity through maunga, awa, iwi, kura<br>✓ AO 2.1: Relationships (whānau, taiao)<br>✓ AO 2.2: Connection to land<br>✓ AO 2.3: Sophisticated extended kōrero<br>✓ AO 2.5: Pou, hononga, māramatanga, wawata, uri<br>✓ Grammar: Ko, He, E mau ana, I…ai (relative), māramatanga, Ka toitū, Ā koia</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the standard. And you can do it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe — my greetings and thanks to you are great. This closes the conversation the way it began — with acknowledgement of the other person. In te ao Māori, beginning and ending with the other person is the mark of true manaaki.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final full practice</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs — the complete full-year conversation. No script after the first line.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open formally + full pepeha from memory.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora ko… + He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe?</li><li>He aha tō wawata mō tō reo Māori? + state your wawata.</li><li>Close: Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe — my greetings and thanks to you are great. You are saying this to your conversation partner after they have spoken. But this year, who else do your mihi go to? Who has been part of your te reo journey? How would you say your mihi to them?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — The full-year conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the full-year conversation with a partner — your finest te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha — all nine lines from memory.</li><li>Ko ngā pou o tōu ora + He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe.</li><li>He aha tō wawata + close with Ka nui ōku mihi.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (14 lines minimum). Then annotate every structure and vocabulary domain you used. How many AOs can you find? How many grammar structures? This is your four-term record.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka pai ake tōku ako ina kōrero ana au.&quot; What does this sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>I speak better than I learn </li><li>I learn better when I speak ✅</li><li>Speaking is the only way to learn </li><li>I prefer writing to speaking </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;ara ako&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>learning result </li><li>learning pathway / strategy ✅</li><li>learning language </li><li>learning achievement </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko ērā ngā hononga i hanga ai e tēnei reo.&quot; What does &quot;i hanga ai e tēnei reo&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that I built through hard work </li><li>that this language built / made (passive) ✅</li><li>that were destroyed by time </li><li>that are yet to be made </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;honohono&quot; express in the context of language?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to separate people by language </li><li>to deeply connect people through language ✅</li><li>to translate between languages </li><li>to forget a language </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū tōku whakapono, ka toitū hoki ahau.&quot; What makes the final clause powerful?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It uses the most complex grammar </li><li>It makes the abstract personal — the speaker themselves endures ✅</li><li>It is the longest clause </li><li>It uses a whakataukī </li></ul></li><li>What is the key difference between the finest sentence and the most complex sentence?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The finest sentence is always the longest </li><li>The finest sentence uses the most vocabulary </li><li>The finest sentence says exactly what you mean — truth over complexity ✅</li><li>The finest sentence always uses Kua </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe.&quot; When is this phrase used?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>As an opening greeting only </li><li>To express great gratitude and acknowledgement — often at the close of a conversation ✅</li><li>As a farewell when you are angry </li><li>Only in formal whaikōrero </li></ul></li><li>A full-year te reo conversation at Term 4 Week 5 level should include which of these?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Only greetings and farewells </li><li>Only past tense sentences </li><li>Full pepeha, sophisticated vocabulary, wawata, and at least six different grammar structures ✅</li><li>Only simple present tense sentences </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake — Full year vocabulary review and celebration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Complete year review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Full year greetings; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything you know: the full year celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the final teaching week. Today we celebrate the full year — everything you have learned, everything you can do, and everything you carry forward. This is not a review drill. It is a celebration of mastery.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">Four terms. Here is the full picture:<br><br><strong>TERM 1 (Tūāhuatanga):</strong> Ko · He · Kei te · Numbers 1–10 · greetings · food · places · wants · basic conversation<br><br><strong>TERM 2 (Kia Tipu):</strong> I · Ka · Kāore · All 16 pronouns · Numbers 1–100 · dates/times · colours · comparatives · weather · seasons · emotions · character<br><br><strong>TERM 3 (Kia Rea):</strong> Body · E…ana · Kua · māku e · Ka taea · pepeha · marae · haka · whakataukī · kaitiakitanga · science · formal/informal · argument<br><br><strong>TERM 4 (Kia Mārama):</strong> Full greeting system · advanced cohesion · oral storytelling · relative clauses (ai) · purpose clauses (hei) · narrative structure · figurative language · philosophical conversation · wawata · Ka huri</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What you can do that most adults in New Zealand cannot:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>→ Deliver a full pepeha from memory<br>→ Explain a whakataukī and its deeper meaning<br>→ Have a philosophical conversation about language and identity in te reo<br>→ Write a narrative with traditional storytelling techniques<br>→ Argue a position with taunakitanga and discourse markers<br>→ Move fluently between six tense markers<br>→ Use all 16 personal pronouns correctly<br>→ Discuss kaitiakitanga, arts, community, and the natural world</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You did this in four terms of daily 20-minute warm-ups. That is approximately 800 minutes — just over 13 hours of instruction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka tino pai tāu mahi.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka tino pai tāu mahi — your work is genuinely, truly excellent. This is the right closing phrase for the year. Not just &quot;ka pai&quot; — tino pai. You have earned every word of it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full year celebration blitz</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One final blitz — the most joyful, celebratory version of everything you know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>PEPEHA challenge: Three students deliver their full pepeha from memory. Class gives Tēnā koe after each.</li><li>WHAKATAUKĪ round: Class says all four whakataukī together. Then explain Ko tā tērā pūrākau mōhio ko te…</li><li>FAVOURITE SENTENCE: Every student says their single favourite te reo sentence of the year. No repeats.</li><li>CLOSE together: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou katoa. Ka toitū tātou i tēnei reo. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou katoa — this language endures through all of us. And: Ka toitū tātou i tēnei reo — we endure through this language. The relationship goes both ways. The language needs you as much as you need it. Does that feel true? What does it mean to say you are part of the survival of a language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final year audit</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your last vocabulary and reflection exercise of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write every te reo word you know in 5 minutes. Count them.</li><li>Write your five favourite sentences from the whole year.</li><li>Write: Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo: [what you give to the language — your commitment going forward].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;letter to the language&quot; in te reo Māori — address te reo itself. Tell it what it has meant to you, what you will do to care for it, and what you hope for it. This is your final gift to your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He whakaaro whakamutunga — Final reflections":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Metacognitive reflection · Full year</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Final reflection; AO 2.5 — Full expression</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Looking back, standing forward</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The last Tuesday of the year. Today we reflect on the journey — not to summarise, but to stand in this moment and feel the weight of what we have built. Then we turn to face forward.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Looking back:</strong><br><strong>I tēnei tau, i <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tipu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tipu</a> tōku reo, i tipu hoki tōku ngākau.</strong><br>This year, my language grew, and my heart grew too.<br><br><strong>Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai au i tēnei tau:</strong><br>he reo, he tikanga, he ao hou — ko tērā tōku taonga.<br>These are the new things I learned this year: a language, a culture, a new world — that is my treasure.<br><br><strong>Standing forward:</strong><br><strong>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me tēnei <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=taonga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">taonga</a> i ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=ringaringa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ringaringa</a>.</strong><br>I go to next year with this treasure in my hands.<br><br><strong>Māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tiaki\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tiaki</a>, māku e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whakaora\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakaora</a>, māku e whakaatu i tēnei reo <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=tapu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">tapu</a>.</strong><br>I will protect it, I will nurture it, I will demonstrate this sacred language.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final reflection vocabulary:<br><strong>i ōku ringaringa</strong> — in my hands (ringaringa = hands)<br><strong>whakaora</strong> — to heal / revitalise / give life to<br><strong>whakaatu</strong> — to demonstrate / show / present<br><strong>Ko tērā tōku taonga.</strong> — That is my treasure.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Ko ēnei ngā mea hou i ako ai au</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>These are the new things I learned: (ai = relative clause marker)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three commitments:<br><strong>Māku e tiaki</strong> — I will protect it<br><strong>Māku e whakaora</strong> — I will nurture/revitalise it<br><strong>Māku e whakaatu</strong> — I will demonstrate it</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three māku e sentences in a row — a commitment in three parts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu — three commitments. In te ao Māori, a commitment said three times is a commitment made truly. Saying māku e three times signals that you are serious.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Three-part commitment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build your three-part commitment to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look back: <strong>I tēnei tau, i tipu tōku reo, i tipu hoki tōku [heart/mind/something specific].</strong></li><li>Name what you carry: <strong>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me [what you are taking with you].</strong></li><li>Three commitments: <strong>Māku e tiaki… Māku e whakaora… Māku e whakaatu…</strong></li><li>Close together: <strong>Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa. Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</strong></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu. Three commitments to the language. Which of these three feels most natural for you? Which feels most challenging? And which — if you only kept one — would matter most?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final reflection</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your final reflection on the year in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I tēnei tau, i tipu tōku reo… describe three things that grew.</li><li>Ka haere au ki tērā tau me [three things you are taking forward].</li><li>Write your three māku e commitments.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your final full reflection (10 sentences) — the year in three sentences, what you carry, your three commitments, a whakataukī that guides you, and close with Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — The year-end showcase and certificate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Whakaatu (presenting) · Year celebration</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year-end showcase; AO 1.1 — Formal presentation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — He whakaaturanga — Presenting your year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The final Wednesday lesson. Today each student prepares a short whakaaturanga — a presentation — of their year in te reo Māori. This is the Whakaatu (presenting) language mode in full action.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Structure for a whakaaturanga:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Open:</strong> Tēnā koutou katoa.<br><strong>2. Identity:</strong> Ko [name] tōku ingoa. [Two pepeha lines].<br><strong>3. Journey:</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>4. Best sentence:</strong> Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your best sentence].<br><strong>5. <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whakataukī\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Whakataukī</a>:</strong> Ko tōku whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a au ko tēnei: [whakataukī + explanation].<br><strong>6. <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wawata</a>:</strong> Ko tōku wawata mō tōku reo: [your aspiration].<br><strong>7. Close:</strong> Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The seven elements of the whakaaturanga:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Formal greeting</strong> — Tēnā koutou katoa</li><li><strong>Identity</strong> — Ko sentences, pepeha (abbreviated)</li><li><strong>Journey</strong> — where you started, where you are now (I, Kua)</li><li><strong>Best sentence</strong> — your finest te reo of the year</li><li><strong>Whakataukī</strong> — the proverb that represents you + Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te…</li><li><strong>Wawata</strong> — your deepest aspiration for your reo</li><li><strong>Formal close</strong> — Tēnā koutou ×3</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Duration: approximately 60–90 seconds per student. That is enough for a full life in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu — my best sentence. Own it. Stand behind it. You chose it because it is true. When you say your best sentence in front of the class, say it like you mean it — because you do.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakaaturanga preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students prepare their whakaaturanga — practise with a partner first.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the seven elements of your whakaaturanga — notes only, not a script.</li><li>Practise with a partner: deliver it fully, they listen without interrupting.</li><li>Partner gives one piece of feedback: &quot;He pai tō [element]. Me [one suggestion].&quot;</li><li>Second run: incorporate the feedback. Aim for 60–90 seconds, from the heart.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei. Your best sentence of the year. What IS it? If you had to choose one sentence that captures something true about you in te reo Māori — what would it be? Choose it now. Write it down. It is yours.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakaaturanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deliver your whakaaturanga to the class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Deliver your full whakaaturanga — all seven elements — to the class.</li><li>Listen to your classmates' whakaaturanga — give a Tēnā koe after each one.</li><li>Write your whakaaturanga as a final written record.</li><li>✦ Bonus: After the class whakaaturanga, write a response: &quot;Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei…&quot; (The sentences that moved me were these…) — choose three sentences from classmates and write why each one moved you.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — He whakamātautau — Final end-of-year assessment":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Full year assessed</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Identity; AO 2.3 — Full sophisticated conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The final assessment</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last lesson of the year. The end-of-year assessment is a full spontaneous conversation — no script, no model to follow. Just you and your reo. This is what you have been building toward since Week 1 of Term 1.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">What a full-year assessment conversation might sound like:<br><br><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full pepeha from memory]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! He aha ngā pou o tōu ora?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe i tēnei tau?<br><strong>B:</strong> I homai mai e ia ki a au he ara ki ngā tīpuna, he hononga ki ōku hoa, ā he māramatanga ki tōku ake ao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu — ka <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=honohono\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">honohono</a> ia i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Tēnā koe mō tāu kōrero tino hōhonu.<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe. Mā te wā.<br><strong>A:</strong> Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Final assessment criteria — what you are showing:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Full pepeha (AO 1.2, 1.4)<br>✓ Sophisticated vocabulary: pou, hononga, māramatanga, wawata (AO 2.5)<br>✓ Complex structures: Ko ngā pou…, He aha…ai…, Ka toitū, Ka honohono (AO 2.3)<br>✓ Correct greeting and farewell system (AO 1.1, 1.6)<br>✓ Spontaneous extension beyond the question (AO 2.3)<br>✓ Natural closing: Ka nui ōku mihi. Mā te wā. (AO 1.1)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You do not need to be perfect. You need to be present, genuine, and in te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka angitu koe. You will succeed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Mā te wā — until another time. This is the farewell for a long parting. At the end of the school year, Mā te wā is right. You are not saying goodbye forever — you are saying: I will carry this language with me, and we will meet again in it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One last practice — quietly, with a partner, in your own words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sit with your partner. Deliver your full assessment — no notes.</li><li>They listen fully. Then they deliver theirs.</li><li>After: Ko tāu rerenga pai rawa atu ko tēhea? Tell your partner their finest moment.</li><li>Class together, final time: Ka toitū tēnei reo i a tātou. Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Mā te wā. Until another time. But in te reo, every time there is an &quot;until another time&quot; rather than a goodbye. The language is waiting for you. What do you say to it before you go?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau — End of year</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final end-of-year assessment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Full spontaneous conversation — at least 14 lines each. No script.</li><li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Self-assess against the criteria above. Be generous and honest.</li><li><strong>Task 3:</strong> Write your final sentence of the year: Ko tōku rerenga whakamutunga o tēnei tau ko tēnei: [sentence]. This is your seal on the year.</li><li>✦ Final checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines) · ☐ Ko ngā pou o tōu ora · ☐ He aha tā tēnei reo i homai ai ki a koe · ☐ Wawata statement · ☐ Ka nui ōku mihi · ☐ Mā te wā</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka tino pai tāu mahi.&quot; What makes this different from &quot;Ka pai tāu mahi&quot;?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>They mean exactly the same thing </li><li>Tino adds genuine emphasis — truly/genuinely excellent, not just good ✅</li><li>Ka tino is a past tense marker </li><li>Tino makes it a question </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo.&quot; What does koha mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>challenge </li><li>question </li><li>gift / contribution / offering ✅</li><li>complaint </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Māku e tiaki, māku e whakaora, māku e whakaatu.&quot; What is the significance of three māku e sentences?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is a grammar requirement for lists </li><li>Three statements signal a deep, complete commitment in te ao Māori ✅</li><li>It makes the sentence easier to remember </li><li>The three verbs all start with wha- </li></ul></li><li>What does &quot;whakaora&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to demonstrate </li><li>to protect </li><li>to heal / revitalise / give life to ✅</li><li>to forget </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What are the seven elements of a whakaaturanga?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>greeting, weather, topic, questions, answers, debate, farewell </li><li>greeting, identity, journey, best sentence, whakataukī, wawata, close ✅</li><li>pepeha, whakataukī, debate, story, song, dance, farewell </li><li>greeting, questions, answers, conclusions, thanks </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei.&quot; What does &quot;i pā mai ki a au&quot; mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>that I wrote myself </li><li>that were too difficult for me </li><li>that moved/touched me ✅</li><li>that I memorised </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Mā te wā.&quot; When is this farewell used rather than Hei konā rā?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>For a short parting like the end of a lesson </li><li>For a long parting — until another time (like the end of the year) ✅</li><li>Only at funerals </li><li>Only between very formal people </li></ul></li><li>What is the final criterion for the end-of-year assessment?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Perfect grammar with no errors </li><li>The longest possible sentences </li><li>Being present, genuine, and in te reo Māori — with spontaneous extension ✅</li><li>Reading from a prepared script </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Greetings, pepeha, and identity (Terms 1–4)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.1: Greetings · AO 1.2: Introductions · AO 1.4: Personal information · AO 1.6: Politeness — full spiral review</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings — full spiral; AO 1.2 — Introductions — full spiral; AO 1.4 — Identity — full spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From Kia ora to Ko tōku wawata: the identity spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to Week 7. These final four weeks are your spiral review — we revisit every major domain from the year, going deeper each time. Today we spiral back to where we began: greetings, introductions, and identity. But look how far these have grown since Term 1 Week 1.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 WEEK 1 level:</strong><br>Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 WEEK 7 level:</strong><br>Tēnā koutou katoa.<br>Ko [maunga] tōku maunga, ko [awa] tōku awa.<br>Ko [waka] tōku waka, ko [iwi] tōku iwi.<br>Ko [hapū] tōku hapū, ko [marae] tōku marae.<br>Ko [kura] tōku kura, ko [name] tōku ingoa.<br>Nō [place] ahau. He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna.<br>Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br>Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu mō ōku uri ā āpōpō.<br>Nō reira, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What has grown from Term 1 to now:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>TERM 1: Kia ora · Ko [name] tōku ingoa · Nō [place] ahau<br>TERM 2: Tēnā koe/kōrua/koutou · Thank-you formulas<br>TERM 3: Full 9-line pepeha · He uri ahau nō ōku tīpuna · Whaikōrero structure<br>TERM 4: Ko ngā pou o tōku ora · Ko tōku wawata · Ka nui ōku mihi</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The identity statement has grown from one line to a full whakaaturanga.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].</strong><br>Therefore, I am [name]. (Nō reira closes the pepeha and plants the speaker firmly in their identity)</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name] — therefore, I am [name]. This closing line of a pepeha is the most powerful. After all the mountain, river, iwi, and hapū — you arrive at yourself. The land and people make the person.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Identity spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Speed round through the full identity progression — each level building on the last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Level 1 (T1): Kia ora. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Nō [place] ahau. Say it fast and smooth.</li><li>Level 2 (T2): Tēnā koe. Ko [name] tōku ingoa. Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina. Add a thank-you.</li><li>Level 3 (T3): Full pepeha — all 9 lines from memory. Ko [maunga]… through Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].</li><li>Level 4 (T4): Add Ko ngā pou o tōku ora + Ko tōku wawata. The complete identity statement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name]. After the mountain, the river, the iwi — you arrive at yourself. The land and the people define you before you define yourself. In your culture or family, how is identity shaped by place and people? Is there a Western equivalent of the pepeha?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full identity spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and say your complete identity statement — all four levels combined.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your full pepeha — all 9 lines — from memory. No looking.</li><li>Add Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko… (three pillars).</li><li>Add Ko tōku wawata: ka… (your deepest aspiration).</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full whakaaturanga incorporating all four levels — pepeha, pillars, wawata, best sentence of the year, whakataukī, and close with Ka nui ōku mihi. Practise delivering it in under 90 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Tense mastery (Terms 1–4)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.4: Time · AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: All tense markers consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.4 — Tense mastery — full spiral; AO 2.3 — Extended tense-controlled writing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — All six tense markers: the complete picture</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned six tense and aspect markers across four terms. Today we spiral back through all of them — not as a list to memorise, but as a living system to feel and use. Each marker answers a different question about time.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Kei te</strong> haere ia. — She is going. (RIGHT NOW — present state)<br><strong>E haere ana</strong> ia. — She is going (and still going — ONGOING)<br><strong>I</strong> haere ia inanahi. — She went yesterday. (COMPLETED PAST — specific event)<br><strong>Ka</strong> haere ia. — She goes / went / and then she went. (NARRATIVE — story movement)<br><strong>Kua</strong> haere ia. — She has gone. (PERFECT — completed, result matters now)<br><strong>Ka haere</strong> ia āpōpō. — She will go tomorrow. (FUTURE — Ka also signals future events)<br><br><strong>NEGATIVES:</strong><br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>e haere ana.</strong> — She is not going. (present negative)<br><strong>Kāore</strong> ia <strong>i haere.</strong> — She did not go. (past negative)<br><strong>Kāore anō kua</strong> haere ia. — She has not yet gone. (perfect negative)</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The question each marker answers:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kei te</strong> — What is happening RIGHT NOW?<br><strong>E…ana</strong> — What is STILL HAPPENING, ongoing?<br><strong>I</strong> — What HAPPENED at a specific time in the past?<br><strong>Ka</strong> — What happened NEXT in the story / what WILL happen next?<br><strong>Kua</strong> — What has BEEN COMPLETED whose result matters NOW?<br><strong>Ka [verb] āpōpō</strong> — What WILL happen?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remember: in te reo Māori, the VERB never changes. Only the MARKER changes. This is the fundamental beauty of the language — one verb, six markers, six relationships to time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: One verb, six markers. Haere is always haere — going. What changes is your relationship to that going: is it now, ongoing, past, narrative, perfect, or future? The marker answers that question.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tense spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you one verb — you use it with all six markers in sequence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Verb: kai (eat). Six sentences: Kei te kai ia · E kai ana ia · I kai ia · Ka kai ia · Kua kai ia · Ka kai ia āpōpō.</li><li>Verb: ako (learn). Your turn — six sentences with all six markers. Which feels most natural?</li><li>Now add negatives: Kāore ia e kai ana · Kāore ia i kai · Kāore anō kua kai ia.</li><li>Challenge: write one sentence that uses THREE different markers — different clauses, same paragraph.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: The verb never changes in te reo Māori — only the marker does. English changes the verb: &quot;eat / ate / eaten / eating / will eat&quot;. If you were teaching a new learner of te reo Māori, how would you explain why the te reo system is actually EASIER than English? What is the hardest part to explain?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Tense spiral writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use all six markers in a connected passage about your year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Kei te and E…ana sentences about right now in class.</li><li>Write I and Ka sentences about what you did this term.</li><li>Write Kua and Ka [future] sentences about what has been achieved and what comes next.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a &quot;year in tenses&quot; passage (10 sentences) — each sentence uses a different marker, tells a different moment of your year, and they connect to form one flowing account. Label each marker.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: Complex sentences from all terms":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · Grammar Level 1–2: All complex structures consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Complex sentence spiral; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary in complex sentences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every complex structure you know</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have learned a full toolkit of complex sentence structures. Today we spiral back through all of them — the joining words, the subordinate clauses, the discourse markers — and practise moving between them fluently.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>COORDINATING:</strong> ā · engari · nō reira · me<br><strong>SUBORDINATING:</strong> nō te mea · ahakoa · mehemea · i te wā · ina…ana<br><strong>RELATIVE CLAUSE:</strong> [noun] + [clause] + ai<br><strong>PURPOSE:</strong> hei [verb]<br><strong>DISCOURSE MARKERS:</strong> waihoki · heoi anō · hei tauira · arā · Ko te mutunga · kāore noa iho<br><strong>DEGREE:</strong> tino · āhua · noa · tonu · anō · kē<br><br><strong>ALL IN ONE PASSAGE:</strong><br>I ako au i te reo Māori i tēnei tau — he mea uaua, <strong>engari</strong> he mea pai <strong>hoki</strong>. <strong>Ahakoa</strong> he uauatanga, i mahi tonu au <strong>nō te mea</strong> ko tōku moemoeā tōku ara. <strong>Kāore noa iho</strong> — ko tēnei reo tōku hononga ki ngā tīpuna, <strong>arā</strong>, ki ngā uri hoki. <strong>Ko te mutunga</strong>, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo <strong>hei</strong> oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Count the complex structures in that passage:<br>engari (contrast)<br>hoki (also)<br>ahakoa (concession)<br>nō te mea (reason)<br>kāore noa iho (not only that)<br>arā (namely)<br>Ko te mutunga (in conclusion)<br>hei (purpose clause)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eight complex structures in five sentences. Every one of them natural, every one of them in service of something true.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral word:<br><strong>oranga</strong> — wellbeing / health / vitality<br><strong>hei oranga</strong> — for the wellbeing of<br><strong>Ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō ngā uri.</strong><br>This language will endure for the wellbeing of descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Every complex structure you know is a tool for precision. Without them, you can say what happened. With them, you can say why, when, although, because, in order to, and despite. They are the difference between reporting and understanding.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Complex sentence spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>I give you a plain sentence — you expand it using three different complex structures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plain: &quot;I ako au i te reo Māori.&quot; → Add ahakoa, nō te mea, and hei. Three expansions.</li><li>Plain: &quot;He pai tēnei reo.&quot; → Add kāore noa iho, arā, and Ko te mutunga.</li><li>Plain: &quot;Ka haere mātou ki te kura.&quot; → Add i te wā, engari, and ā.</li><li>Now write your own: one plain sentence about te reo Māori — expand it with five complex structures.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. Count the structures: Ko te mutunga (discourse marker), Ko (identity), Ka toitū (future), hei oranga (purpose), mō āpōpō ō ngā uri (time + possession). Five structures in one sentence. And yet it sounds natural. Why?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Complex sentence showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a paragraph using every complex structure type at least once.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a sentence with ahakoa + nō te mea in the same clause.</li><li>Write a sentence with a relative clause (ai) + purpose clause (hei).</li><li>Write a sentence using waihoki + arā + Ko te mutunga across three sentences.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your finest complex te reo paragraph (8 sentences) — use all five categories: coordinating, subordinating, relative, purpose, and discourse markers. Make it a genuine statement about your year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: The full-year conversation challenge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Full spontaneous conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full conversation spiral; AO 1.1 — Greetings spiral; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The full-year conversation: every domain</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we have the most comprehensive conversation of the year — touching every major domain from Terms 1–4. This is the spiral in conversation form: identity, past events, feelings, weather, community, arts, environment, whakataukī, and wawata — all in one exchange.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe? [Full pepeha from B]<br><strong>B:</strong> [9-line pepeha] Nō reira, tēnā koe.<br><strong>A:</strong> He pēhea ōu hararei? He aha ngā mea hou?<br><strong>B:</strong> I haere māua ko tōku māmā ki te ngahere. I ātaahua te rangi — ahakoa i ua i tētahi rā, i tākaro tonu mātou i waho. Ka ora tōku ngākau i te āhua o te taiao.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe i tēnei wā?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao.&quot; Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te hononga o ngā mea katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō wawata mō āpōpō?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono tēnei reo i ngā tāngata katoa o Aotearoa — ka toitū hoki ia hei oranga mō ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=%C4%80+koia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ā koia</a>! Ka nui ōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=mihi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mihi</a> ki a koe. Mā te wā.<br><strong>B:</strong> Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every domain in that conversation:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Identity (T1/T3): full pepeha<br>Past events (T2): I haere māua · I ua i tētahi rā<br>Feelings (T2): Ka ora tōku ngākau<br>Weather (T2): i ātaahua te rangi · i ua<br>Nature (T2/T3): i te āhua o te taiao · i te ngahere<br>Concession (T1): ahakoa i ua, i tākaro tonu<br>Whakataukī (T3): stated and explained<br>Wawata (T4): Ko tōku wawata: ka honohono…<br>Purpose (T4): hei oranga mō ōku uri<br>Farewell (T4): Mā te wā</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. This whakataukī connects language, people, and the natural world. It is the perfect whakataukī for a conversation that has touched all three. Have it ready — it fits almost any deep te reo conversation.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Full-year conversation spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Pairs. This conversation must touch every domain from the year. No scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: formal greeting + full pepeha. (T1/T3 identity)</li><li>Exchange: holidays/past events + weather + feeling. (T2 domains)</li><li>Deepen: whakataukī + explanation + wawata. (T3/T4 domains)</li><li>Close: Ka nui ōku mihi + Mā te wā. (T4 farewell)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao. Language, people, natural world — all endure together. This year you have learned a language. In doing so, what else have you learned? What does learning te reo teach you about Aotearoa, about people, about yourself?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full-year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the most complete te reo conversation of your life — every domain, fully spontaneous.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha from memory — no hesitation.</li><li>Past event + weather + feeling — at least four sentences.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation + wawata — your genuine answers.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write up the full conversation (16+ lines). Annotate every domain and structure you used. How many of the 12 AOs appear in your conversation? Aim for all of them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nō reira, ko ahau ko [name].&quot; Where does this line appear in a pepeha and what does it do?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It opens the pepeha as a greeting </li><li>It closes the pepeha — planting the speaker firmly in their identity after naming land and people ✅</li><li>It is a farewell at the end of a speech </li><li>It introduces the mountain and river </li></ul></li><li>What is the correct order of elements in a full pepeha?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Name → mountain → river → iwi → school → origin </li><li>Mountain → river → waka → iwi → hapū → marae → school → name → origin (Nō reira ko ahau) ✅</li><li>Origin → name → iwi → mountain → river </li><li>School → name → iwi → hapū → origin </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Kua haere ia.&quot; What does Kua signal about this action?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>She is going right now </li><li>She was going in the past at a specific time </li><li>She has gone and the result is relevant now ✅</li><li>She will go tomorrow </li></ul></li><li>Which tense marker signals an ONGOING action that is still continuing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Kei te </li><li>I </li><li>Ka </li><li>E…ana ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū tēnei reo hei oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.&quot; What does &quot;hei oranga&quot; express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>a past event </li><li>a purpose clause — for the wellbeing of ✅</li><li>a negative </li><li>a comparison </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kāore noa iho — ko tēnei reo tōku hononga ki ngā tīpuna.&quot; What does kāore noa iho signal?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is not at all </li><li>Not only that — there is more ✅</li><li>It is just nothing </li><li>It is almost nothing </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka toitū te reo, ka toitū te tangata, ka toitū hoki te taiao.&quot; What three things endure together?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>language, school, community </li><li>language, people, and the natural world ✅</li><li>language, ancestors, descendants </li><li>language, culture, money </li></ul></li><li>In the full-year conversation, which farewell is used for a long parting like the end of term?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Hei konā rā </li><li>E noho rā </li><li>Ā ka kite anō </li><li>Mā te wā ✅</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Descriptions, comparisons, and figurative language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.5: Descriptions · AO 2.3: Opinions · Grammar Level 1–2: He, comparatives, simile, metaphor — full spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.5 — Description spiral; AO 2.3 — Comparison &amp; figurative language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From He nui to Ko te reo he aho: the description spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 1 you learned He nui (it is big). By Term 4 you are writing Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata (language is a cord that weaves people). Today we spiral through every layer of description — adjectives, comparatives, superlatives, degree words, simile, and metaphor.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>He nui tēnei whare. He pai tōku kura.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>He nui ake tērā whare i tēnei. He tino pai tōku kura — he pai rawa atu i ērā atu kura.<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>He tino ātaahua tōku kura, he wāhi tiaki mōku — ka tipu ōku whakaaro i konei.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (simile):</strong><br>E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=r%C4%81kau\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">rākau</a> i te tōanga o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=k%C5%8Danga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">koanga</a>.<br>My heart grows like a tree in the opening of spring.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (metaphor):</strong><br>Ko tōku kura he māramatanga ki te ao mārama — ko ia tōku <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> ki ngā tīpuna.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The description spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>He [adjective] → basic description<br>He [adj] ake → comparative<br>He [adj] rawa atu → superlative<br>He tino [adj] → intensified<br>[verb] āhua/me he [comparison] → simile<br>Ko [X] he [metaphor] → metaphor</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>i te tōanga o te kōanga</strong> — in the opening of spring<br><strong>tō</strong> — the opening / unfolding (of something)<br><strong>E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga.</strong><br>My heart grows like a tree in the opening of spring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: From He nui to Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata — that journey took you one year. The first sentence describes a size. The second describes a relationship between people and language. That is what learning a language does to your thinking.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Description spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take one noun through every level of description.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noun: te reo Māori. Level 1: He [adjective] te reo Māori.</li><li>Level 2: He [adj] ake te reo Māori i [comparison].</li><li>Level 3: He tino [adj] te reo Māori — he [adj] rawa atu.</li><li>Level 4: Ko te reo Māori he [metaphor] — E [verb] ana ia āhua te [simile].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga. Heart. Tree. Spring. Three things connected through the word āhua (like). What is it about a tree opening in spring that captures how a heart grows? Why is this a better description than &quot;my heart is growing&quot;?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full description spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write descriptions of your year at every level from basic to figurative.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write He [adj] and He [adj] ake sentences about te reo Māori.</li><li>Write a tino/rawa atu superlative about your best learning moment.</li><li>Write a simile (āhua/me he) about something that grew in you this year.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a Ko [X] he [metaphor] sentence about te reo Māori — something as beautiful as Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata. Then write a short paragraph continuing the metaphor.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Community, environment, and kaitiakitanga":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Connection to land · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.4: Natural world — full spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Land &amp; community spiral; AO 2.4 — Environment &amp; kaitiakitanga spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From Nō hea koe to Ka ora te taiao ka ora tātou: the community spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From Term 1 Week 4 (places and ki) to Term 3 Week 7 (kaitiakitanga and toitū) — the community and environment strand runs through the whole year. Today we spiral it all together: where you come from, who your community is, and your responsibility to the natural world.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Kei te haere au ki te toa.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>Nō Aotearoa ahau — he whenua ātaahua tēnei. Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai o tōku tāone.<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao. Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o ngā awa, o ngā ngahere, o ngā maunga.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou — he pono tēnei. Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū te taiao o Aotearoa hei oranga mō ōku uri. E ai ki āku whakaaro, he kawenga ōu mō tēnei whenua ātaahua.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The community/environment spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T1: Location (nō hea, ki hea, kei hea)<br>T2: Description of place (He ātaahua, Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai)<br>T3: Kaitiakitanga (me tiaki, mauri, toitū)<br>T4: Philosophical connection (Ka ora te taiao, wawata, kawenga)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Full statement:<br><strong>Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — he kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i tōna oranga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</strong><br>I am a descendant of this land — I have a responsibility to protect its wellbeing for tomorrow's descendants.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua.</strong><br>I am a descendant of this land.<br><strong>tōna oranga</strong> — its wellbeing / its vitality</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — I am a descendant of this land. This sentence connects pepeha (belonging to land) with kaitiakitanga (responsibility to land). You are not separate from the land — you come FROM it, and you are responsible TO it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Community and environment spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Spiral through every level of community and environment language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1/T2: Nō [place] ahau. He [adjective] tōku whenua/tāone. Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai…</li><li>T3: Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao. Me tiaki tātou i te mauri o… nō te mea…</li><li>T4: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou. Ko tōku wawata: ka toitū…</li><li>Full: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua — he kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i tōna oranga mō ōku uri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua. You are a descendant of the land. Not just of people — of the land itself. What would it mean to genuinely believe this? How would it change the way you live, eat, travel, and consume if you truly felt you were descended from the land and responsible to it?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Community and environment spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your full community and environment statement — all four levels.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Nō [place] ahau + He [adj] tōku whenua + Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai.</li><li>Write Ko tātou ngā kaitiaki o tēnei taiao + Me tiaki tātou i… + reason.</li><li>Write Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou + Ko tōku wawata mō te taiao.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full kaitiakitanga statement (10 sentences) — your origin, your community, what you love about your place, what threatens it, your responsibility, what you commit to, and your wawata for the natural world of Aotearoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: Argument, opinion, and persuasion":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended persuasive communication · Grammar Level 1–2: Full argument toolkit consolidated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Argument &amp; persuasion spiral; AO 2.5 — Formal argument vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From He pai ake to Tōku tūāhuatanga: the argument spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You began arguing in Term 1 with He pai ake (better than). By Term 4 you are writing full structured arguments with taunakitanga, waihoki, and Ko te mutunga. Today we spiral the full argument toolkit — from simple comparison to philosophical persuasion.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 1 level:</strong><br>He pai ake te hākinakina i te noho ki te kāinga ki a au nō te mea he hauora ake.<br><br><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>He pai ake te mahi tahi i te mahi kotahi — he nui ake ngā hua. Ka pai tāu whakaaro, engari rā…<br><br><strong>TERM 3 level:</strong><br>Tōku tūāhuatanga: he nui ake ngā hua o te kaitiakitanga i ngā uauatanga. Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>E ai ki āku whakaaro, ko te reo Māori te taonga rangatira rawa atu o Aotearoa. Kāore noa iho — ko ia hoki te ara ki te ao o ōku tīpuna. Waihoki, ka honohono ia i ngā tāngata katoa. Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The argument spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T1: He pai ake + nō te mea (simple comparison + reason)<br>T2: Whakaae/whakaaro exchanges, He tika tāu engari rā<br>T3: Tūāhuatanga + taunakitanga tuatahi/tuarua<br>T4: E ai ki āku whakaaro + kāore noa iho + waihoki + Ko te mutunga</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The four-term argument:<br>Simple → Dialogic → Structured → Philosophical</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>taonga rangatira rawa atu</strong> — the most precious / chiefly treasure<br><strong>ko ia hoki te ara</strong> — it is also the path</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko te mutunga — in conclusion. This is your power phrase. After all the evidence, all the concessions, all the comparisons — Ko te mutunga lands your final point with authority. Use it when you mean it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Argument spiral drill</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build the same argument at four different levels of sophistication.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1 level: He pai ake te [X] i te [Y] ki a au nō te mea…</li><li>T2 level: He tika tāu whakaaro — engari rā, ko tāku whakaaro he [counter-view].</li><li>T3 level: Tōku tūāhuatanga: [position]. Tāku taunakitanga tuatahi: [evidence].</li><li>T4 level: E ai ki āku whakaaro… Kāore noa iho… Waihoki… Ko te mutunga…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri. A conclusion that points to future generations. The best arguments in te ao Māori often end by considering uri (descendants). Why? What does including future generations in an argument do to its moral weight?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Full argument spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a four-level argument about te reo Māori — from simple to philosophical.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1 level: He pai ake te ako i te reo Māori i [alternative] nō te mea…</li><li>T3 level: Tōku tūāhuatanga + two taunakitanga (tuatahi/tuarua).</li><li>T4 level: E ai ki āku whakaaro + kāore noa iho + waihoki + Ko te mutunga.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your finest persuasive piece (10 sentences) about why te reo Māori matters — using the full argument toolkit from all four terms. This is your year-end statement of belief.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: He kōrero ā-iwi — A community conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Community · AO 2.1: Relationships · AO 2.3: Extended conversation — all community domains</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.1 — Community relationships spiral; AO 1.4 — Identity &amp; community spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Everything about community in one conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we spiral all the community language from the year — whānau, hapū, iwi, marae, school, environment, kaitiakitanga, relationships — into one rich conversation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Nō hea koe ā ko wai ōu iwi?<br><strong>B:</strong> Nō Ōtautahi ahau. Ko Ngāi Tahu tōku iwi, ko Ngāti Māmoe tōku hapū.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o tō rohe ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> Ko ēnei: he ātaahua te taiao, he māia ngā tāngata, ā he rangatira hoki te reo o tēnei whenua.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tō kawenga ki tōu hapori?<br><strong>B:</strong> He kawenga ōku ki te tiaki i te taiao, ki te kōrero i te reo Māori, ā ki te whakaaro nui mō ngā uri — nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka wani kē! Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.<br><strong>B:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe mō tāu aro mai ki ōku kōrero.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrases:<br><strong>tō rohe</strong> — your region / area / territory<br><strong>Ko ēnei: [list]</strong> — These are: (using Ko ēnei for listing community assets)<br><strong>nā ōku tīpuna ki a au</strong> — from my ancestors to me<br><strong>ā māku ki ōku uri</strong> — and from me to my descendants<br><strong>Ka wani kē!</strong> — That is amazing! (from Term 3 Week 5!)<br><strong>Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.</strong><br>Your words are an enlightenment to me.<br><strong>mō tāu aro mai ki ōku kōrero</strong> — for turning toward my words / for listening to me<br><strong>aro mai</strong> — turn toward / face toward / attend to</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri — from my ancestors to me, and from me to my descendants. This sentence places you in the middle of a chain that stretches from the past to the future. You are not the beginning or the end — you are the bridge.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Community spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a community conversation touching every domain from the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open with formal greeting + Nō hea koe + ko wai ōu iwi/hapū.</li><li>Share Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai rawa atu o tōku rohe: a Ko ēnei list.</li><li>State your kawenga: He kawenga ōku ki te [three responsibilities].</li><li>Close with nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri + Ka nui ōku mihi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au — your words are an enlightenment to me. This is the highest compliment in a te reo conversation — you have not just been understood, you have brought light. When has someone's words brought light to YOU this year? In te reo or in any language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Community spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have a full community conversation — every domain from Terms 1–4.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Exchange origins, iwi/hapū, and region descriptions.</li><li>Share Ko ēnei ngā āhuatanga pai lists about your communities.</li><li>State your kawenga and the nā ōku tīpuna ki a au chain.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (14+ lines). Annotate which term each piece of language comes from — T1, T2, T3, or T4. How evenly distributed is it? Which term contributed the most to this conversation?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E tipu ana tōku ngākau āhua te rākau i te tōanga o te kōanga.&quot; What figure of speech is this?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>metaphor </li><li>personification </li><li>simile — the heart grows LIKE a tree in spring ✅</li><li>alliteration </li></ul></li><li>What is the difference between He nui (T1) and Ko te reo he aho e whiri ana i ngā tāngata (T4)?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>One is a description of size; the other is a metaphor about language connecting people ✅</li><li>They mean the same thing </li><li>One is formal, one is casual </li><li>One uses past tense, one uses present </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko au he uri o tēnei whenua.&quot; What does this sentence claim about the speaker's relationship to the land?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The speaker owns the land </li><li>The speaker is a guardian employed to protect the land </li><li>The speaker is a descendant of the land — belonging to it ✅</li><li>The speaker is a visitor from another land </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka ora te taiao, ka ora tātou.&quot; What principle does this whakataukī-style sentence express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The environment is more important than people </li><li>Our wellbeing and the environment's wellbeing are inseparable ✅</li><li>We must protect the environment because we are told to </li><li>The environment survives without people </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is &quot;tūāhuatanga&quot; in an argument context?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>the conclusion </li><li>the evidence </li><li>your position / stance ✅</li><li>the introduction </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko te mutunga, me tiaki tātou i tēnei taonga mō āpōpō ō ngā uri.&quot; What does including &quot;ngā uri&quot; in a conclusion do?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It weakens the argument by looking too far ahead </li><li>It adds moral weight by considering future generations ✅</li><li>It is required by te reo grammar </li><li>It replaces the need for evidence </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Nā ōku tīpuna ki a au, ā māku ki ōku uri.&quot; What chain does this sentence describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The chain of school learning </li><li>The chain of money and resources </li><li>The chain of responsibility from ancestors through the speaker to descendants ✅</li><li>The chain of greetings in a pōhiri </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au.&quot; What is the speaker expressing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The other person is talking too much </li><li>Your words have brought enlightenment / light to me ✅</li><li>I do not understand what you said </li><li>Please speak more clearly </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Arts, culture, and whakataukī":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 1.4: Cultural identity · AO 2.5: Arts and figurative language · AO 2.3: Extended cultural communication</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.4 — Arts &amp; culture spiral; AO 2.5 — Whakataukī &amp; figurative spiral</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — From toi whakairo to Ko te reo he aho: the culture spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today we spiral the arts, culture, and wisdom strand — haka, waiata, whakairo, raranga, whakataukī, tikanga. These are the domains that connect language to living culture. By Term 4, you can not just name them — you can explain, analyse, and connect them to identity.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 3 level (naming):</strong><br>Ko te haka tētahi mahi toi rongonui o Aotearoa.<br>He tohu aroha te haka — he tohu kaha hoki.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level (explaining):</strong><br>Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata me tōna hapori.<br>Ko tā tēnei mahi toi mōhio ko te mana o te kaha me te aroha — ko ērā ngā mea e kore e wehewehea.<br><br><strong>WHAKATAUKĪ spiral:</strong><br>&quot;He aha te mea nui o te ao? He tangata, he tangata, he tangata.&quot;<br>Ko tā tēnei whakataukī mōhio ko te pūtake o ngā mea katoa — ko ngā tāngata.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The culture spiral levels:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Naming: Ko te haka tētahi mahi toi.<br>Describing: He tohu aroha, he tohu kaha.<br>Explaining: Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te…<br>Connecting to identity: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te mana o te tangata.<br>Using as evidence: Hei tauira, ko te haka — arā, ko ia he tohu o ngā mea e kore e wehewehea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New spiral phrase:<br><strong>whakamana</strong> — to empower / to honour / to give authority to<br><strong>Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata.</strong><br>The meaning of haka is to honour/empower the person.<br><strong>e kore e wehewehea</strong> — that cannot be separated</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: E kore e wehewehea — cannot be separated. Love and strength in haka. Language and identity in te reo. Land and people in kaitiakitanga. The most important things in te ao Māori e kore e wehewehea — they cannot be separated from each other.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Arts and culture spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Spiral through every level of arts and culture language.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name three mahi toi: Ko ēnei ngā mahi toi ōku: he [X], he [Y], he [Z].</li><li>Explain one: Ko te tikanga o te [art form] ko te [explanation].</li><li>Give deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei mahi toi mōhio ko te [deeper lesson].</li><li>Use a whakataukī as evidence: Hei tauira, [whakataukī] — arā, [explanation].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: E kore e wehewehea — cannot be separated. What two things in YOUR life cannot be separated? Not just love and strength — something specific to you. And can you say it in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Arts and culture spiral writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about art, culture, and wisdom at Term 4 level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Name your favourite art form and explain its tikanga: Ko te tikanga o te [art] ko te…</li><li>Give its deeper meaning: Ko tā tēnei mōhio ko te…</li><li>Use a whakataukī as evidence with hei tauira and arā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full cultural explanation (8 sentences) of one art form or cultural practice — naming, describing, explaining tikanga, deeper meaning, whakataukī evidence, connection to identity, and a personal wawata about that art form.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: Arotake ā-reo — Spiral: Full grammar — every structure in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All Grammar Level 1–2 · All AOs — final spiral</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Full grammar spiral; AO 2.4 — Tense &amp; structure mastery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Every structure, natural and fluent</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate grammar lesson of the year. Today we use every major structure from the full year in a single natural passage — not to show off, but to feel how effortlessly they can work together when you are not thinking about them individually.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A four-term grammar showcase passage:</strong><br><br>Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga i tīmatahia noatia e tētahi kupu inanahi, ināianei, kua tipu ōku kupu ki tētahi ao hou.<br><br>E ako tonu ana au, kāore anō kua oti — engari, ahakoa he uauatanga i ōku ara, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.<br><br>Ka taea e au te mihi, ka taea e au te tuhituhi, ka taea e au te whakaaro i tēnei reo rangatira — ko ia te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i a au ki ngā tīpuna.<br><br>Māku e tiaki. Māku e whakaora. Māku e whakaatu.<br><br>Ko te mutunga, ko tōku wawata: ka toitū tēnei reo tapu hei oranga mō ōku uri ā āpōpō. Ka huri.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every structure in that passage:<br>Ko (identity ×2)<br>He (description)<br>I (past: i tīmatahia)<br>Kua (perfect: kua tipu, kua oti)<br>E…ana (progressive: E ako tonu ana)<br>Kāore anō kua (perfect negative)<br>Ahakoa (concession)<br>Nō te mea (reason)<br>Ko + moemoeā (dream)<br>Ka taea (ability ×3)<br>Ko [metaphor] (Ko ia te aho…)<br>ai (relative: e whiri ana i a au)<br>Māku e (commitment ×3)<br>Ko te mutunga (conclusion)<br>Ko + wawata (aspiration)<br>Hei oranga (purpose)<br>Ka huri (story close)</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>17 different structures. One passage. And it sounds like real te reo.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: When you stop thinking about structures and start thinking about what you want to say, the structures take care of themselves. That is fluency. That is what this year has been building toward.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final grammar spiral</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the showcase passage together aloud — then each student writes their own version.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Open: Ko ahau ko [name] — he ākonga i tīmatahia noatia e tētahi kupu inanahi, ināianei…</li><li>Middle: E ako tonu ana au, kāore anō kua oti — ahakoa… nō te mea…</li><li>Achievement: Ka taea e au te… ka taea e au te… ka taea e au te…</li><li>Commitment + close: Māku e tiaki. Māku e whakaora. Ko tōku wawata: ka… Ka huri.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga — my dream is my understanding/clarity. Not wealth, not fame, not even success — māramatanga. Clarity of thought and understanding. Is this your dream too? What does māramatanga mean to you — in te reo and in life?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Final grammar showcase</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write YOUR version of the four-term grammar showcase passage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write Ko + He sentences about your identity and your character.</li><li>Write I + Kua + E…ana about your learning journey.</li><li>Write Ka taea sentences about what you can do + Ko metaphor.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write your full four-term grammar showcase (12 sentences) — every structure from the year, in service of something true about you and your te reo journey. End with Ka huri.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: Hanga rerenga — Spiral: He mihi ki a tātou — A tribute to each other":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — AO 2.3: Extended communication · AO 2.5: Full vocabulary · AO 1.1: Formal acknowledgement</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Tribute writing; AO 1.1 — Formal acknowledgement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Writing tributes to people who matter</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Term 2 Week 8 you wrote tributes to people you admire using He tangata atawhai, He tangata pukumahi, and Ko ia te pou o tōku ao. Today we spiral back to that — but at Term 4 level, writing tributes that use the full year's vocabulary and express deep appreciation.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>TERM 2 level:</strong><br>Ko Hēmi tōku hoa pai rawa atu. He tangata atawhai, he tangata ngākaunui hoki ia.<br>Ka ora tōku ngākau i tōna āhua.<br><br><strong>TERM 4 level:</strong><br>Ko [name] tētahi o ngā tāngata nui rawa atu i tōku ao — ko ia he pou o tōku oranga.<br>I āwhina ia i a au i ngā wā katoa e uaua ana ōku ara — e kore e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wareware\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wareware</a> ana i a au tōna <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=atawhai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">atawhai</a>.<br>Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te tikanga o te <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=toa+takitini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">toa takitini</a> — ka toa tātou i a ia.<br>Nō reira, tēnā koe [name] mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The tribute spiral:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>T2 level: He tangata [character] · Ko ia te pou · Ka ora tōku ngākau<br>T4 level: Ko ia he pou o tōku oranga · e kore e wareware ana · ko tā tōna āhua mōhio · tēnā koe mō tāu [X, Y, Z]</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New tribute vocabulary:<br><strong>e kore e wareware ana i a au</strong> — I will never forget (wareware = forget)<br><strong>ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au</strong> — what their character teaches me<br><strong>ka toa tātou i a ia</strong> — we are victorious/strong through them<br><strong>mō tāu aroha</strong> — for your love/compassion<br><strong>mō tāu oranga ki a au</strong> — for your life force toward me</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au. Three mō phrases — three things you are grateful for. When you say someone's contributions in three, you are honouring them in the way te ao Māori honours everything important — three times, three ways.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Tribute writing</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a tribute to someone in this class — your teacher, a classmate, or someone who helped you learn te reo this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity + character: Ko [name] tētahi o ngā tāngata nui rawa atu i tōku ao. He tangata [X], he tangata [Y] hoki ia.</li><li>What they did: I āwhina ia i a au i [specific moment/way] — e kore e wareware ana i a au.</li><li>What their character teaches: Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te [lesson].</li><li>Close: Nō reira, tēnā koe [name] mō tāu [X], mō tāu [Y], mō tāu [Z].</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Mō tāu oranga ki a au — for your life force toward me. Oranga (wellbeing/vitality) is something you can give to another person — through your presence, your manaakitanga, your care. Who has given their oranga to you this year in this classroom? How would you say it in te reo Māori?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He mihi ki tētahi</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a full tribute in te reo Māori to someone important to your learning journey this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identity + Ko ia he pou o tōku [oranga/ako/ngākau].</li><li>What they did: I āwhina ia i a au i [specific moment] + e kore e wareware.</li><li>What they taught you: Ko tā tōna āhua mōhio ki a au ko te [lesson].</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full tribute (8 sentences) — identity, character (2 traits shown not told), what they did, what they taught you, how they have changed you (Kua hou tōku whakaaro nō tōna āwhina), and close with Tēnā koe mō tāu [X, Y, Z]. Read it aloud to them if you can.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: Kōrero — Spiral: He kōrero ā-tau — A year in conversation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Final comprehensive spiral conversation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Year spiral conversation; AO 2.5 — Full year vocabulary; AO 1.1 — Formal greetings</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The year in one conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the penultimate Thursday lesson. Today we have a conversation that spirals through every term — a conversation that could only happen at the end of Year 5-6 te reo Māori, because it requires everything you have learned.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A:</strong> Tēnā koe. Ko wai koe, ā he aha tō pepeha?<br><strong>B:</strong> [Full 9-line pepeha + Nō reira ko ahau ko B]<br><strong>A:</strong> Ka pai! I tīmata koe i hea i tō ako i te reo Māori?<br><strong>B:</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, e ako tonu ana au, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha ngā mea i uaua rawa atu ki a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> I uaua ki a au ngā hanganga o te tense — Ko Kua, Ko Ka. Engari, ahakoa i uaua, i mahi tonu au nō te mea ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.<br><strong>A:</strong> He aha tāu whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a koe?<br><strong>B:</strong> &quot;Ehara tāku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini.&quot; Ko tā tēnā mōhio ko te mana o tēnei akomanga — ko tā tātou mahi tahi i āwhina i a mātou katoa.<br><strong>A:</strong> Ā koia! Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au. Mā te wā.<br><strong>B:</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koe. Mā te wā.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This conversation touches every term:<br>T1: &quot;Kia ora&quot; as the starting word<br>T2: I tīmata au (past) · kua tipu (perfect) · e ako tonu ana (E…ana)<br>T3: Whakataukī + ko tā tēnā mōhio · moemoeā<br>T4: ko tā tātou mahi tahi (possession) · he māramatanga · Mā te wā</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New phrase:<br><strong>ko tā tātou mahi tahi</strong> — our working together (ā-category possession of the group)<br><strong>i āwhina i a mātou katoa</strong> — helped all of us</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: &quot;Kia ora&quot; was your first word. Mā te wā is how you close the year. Between those two phrases is everything. That is your year in te reo Māori.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The final group conversation practice — every term represented, no scripts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>T1: Tēnā koe + full pepeha from memory.</li><li>T2: I tīmata au i… ināianei kua tipu tōku reo ki…</li><li>T3: Whakataukī + Ko tā tēnā mōhio ko te…</li><li>T4: Ko tāu kōrero he māramatanga ki a au + Mā te wā.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: &quot;Kia ora&quot; was your first word. What will your LAST word in te reo Māori be — not at the end of today, but someday, in your life? What do you hope to be saying in te reo Māori in five years? In twenty?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Year spiral conversation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Have the full year spiral conversation with a partner — touch every term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Full pepeha + Nō reira ko ahau ko [name].</li><li>Learning journey: I tīmata + Kua tipu + E ako tonu ana.</li><li>Whakataukī + explanation + moemoeā.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write the full conversation (16+ lines). After, write a reflection: He aha te mea i mihi ana koe ki a koe ake? (What did you appreciate about yourself?) Ka angitu koe.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko te tikanga o te haka ko te whakamana i te tangata.&quot; What does whakamana mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to punish </li><li>to empower / honour / give authority to ✅</li><li>to teach </li><li>to separate </li></ul></li><li>&quot;E kore e wehewehea&quot; — in the context of haka and aroha/kaha, what does this phrase mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>it should be separated into parts </li><li>it cannot be separated — the two things are inseparable ✅</li><li>it will never be performed again </li><li>it does not exist </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many different grammar structures appear in the four-term showcase passage?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>5 </li><li>9 </li><li>17 ✅</li><li>23 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku moemoeā tōku māramatanga.&quot; What aspiration does this express?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>My dream is wealth and success </li><li>My dream is my understanding/clarity ✅</li><li>My dream is to become a teacher </li><li>My dream is to travel </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;E kore e wareware ana i a au tōna atawhai.&quot; What does wareware mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to forgive </li><li>to remember </li><li>to forget ✅</li><li>to thank </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Tēnā koe mō tāu āwhina, mō tāu aroha, mō tāu oranga ki a au.&quot; What is the significance of three mō phrases?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>It is required by grammar </li><li>Three phrases honour the person in the way te ao Māori honours important things — three times, three ways ✅</li><li>It makes the sentence longer </li><li>Three is the number of people being thanked </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — Kia ora.&quot; What does this sentence reflect on?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The difficulty of te reo Māori </li><li>The start of the learning journey — from a single word ✅</li><li>A complaint about Term 1 </li><li>The importance of greeting properly </li></ul></li><li>In the year spiral conversation, what two phrases mark the beginning and end of the whole year?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>&quot;Tēnā koe&quot; and &quot;E noho rā&quot; </li><li>&quot;Kia ora&quot; as the first word and &quot;Mā te wā&quot; as the year-end farewell ✅</li><li>&quot;Ko wai koe&quot; and &quot;Ka huri&quot; </li><li>&quot;He aha&quot; and &quot;Ka mutu&quot; </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday: He rā whakanui — Celebration and culmination day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · Whakaatu (presenting) · Full year celebrated</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Formal celebration; AO 2.3 — Full year expression; AO 2.5 — Complete vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — He rā whakanui: a day of celebration</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to the final week. This week is a celebration of what you have built. Today is whakanui — honouring and making great. We do not review or test. We celebrate, perform, and share.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>The whakanui structure for today:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Mihi whakatūwhera:</strong> Teacher opens with Tēnā koutou katoa + acknowledgement of the year.<br><strong>2. Pepeha pōhiri:</strong> Each student delivers their pepeha — class responds with Tēnā koe after each.<br><strong>3. Waiata tahi:</strong> Class sings a waiata together — Ka mate, or another known waiata.<br><strong>4. Kōrero pūrākau:</strong> Two or three students share their pūrākau from Term 4 Week 2.<br><strong>5. He whakaaro:</strong> Each student shares their favourite sentence of the year.<br><strong>6. Mihi whakamutunga:</strong> Teacher closes with Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei — Ka nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The language of celebration:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>He rā whakanui tēnei.</strong> — This is a day of celebration.<br><strong>Ka whakanuitia ā tātou mahi.</strong> — Our work is being honoured/celebrated.<br><strong>Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei.</strong><br>This is what our working together has built.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mihi whakatūwhera</th><td class=\"lt-td\">opening tribute</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mihi whakamutunga</th><td class=\"lt-td\">closing tribute</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>whakanui</strong> — to celebrate / to make great / to honour<br><strong>Ka whakanuitia</strong> — is being celebrated/honoured (passive)<br><strong>He tino nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</strong><br>My tributes/thanks to you all are very great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Whakanui — to make great, to celebrate, to honour. When you celebrate someone's achievement, you are not just praising them — you are making them great in the eyes of everyone present. That is what whakanui does.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Whakanui preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare for the full class whakanui celebration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pepeha: Every student practises their full pepeha one final time. No looking — from the heart.</li><li>Favourite sentence: Write your favourite te reo sentence of the year. Say it to yourself. Own it.</li><li>Pūrākau: Two volunteers prepare to share their pūrākau from Term 4 Week 2.</li><li>Waiata: Class practices the opening of Ka Mate or another class waiata together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei — this is what our working together has built. What has the class built together? Not just language skills — but what kind of learning community? What has this class been to you?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — Whakanui contribution</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare your contribution to the class whakanui celebration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Your pepeha: write it from memory, check it, practise until it is completely yours.</li><li>Your favourite sentence: write it, say why it is your favourite.</li><li>A whakaaro for the class: Ko tā tō tātou akomanga hanga ai ko… (What your class has built is…)</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a short mihi to the class (5 sentences) — acknowledge everyone, name three things the class has done together, and close with He tino nui ōku mihi ki a koutou katoa.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday: He whakaaturanga — The end-of-year performance":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Whakaatu (presenting) — final performance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Final performance; AO 1.1 — Formal presentation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — Your whakaaturanga: the full performance</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Today is the end-of-year whakaaturanga — each student performs their full presentation to the class. This is the Whakaatu (presenting) language mode in its fullest expression. You have been preparing for this all year.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>The complete whakaaturanga structure:</strong><br><br><strong>1.</strong> Tēnā koutou katoa.<br><strong>2.</strong> Ko [maunga] tōku maunga… [full pepeha — all 9 lines]<br><strong>3.</strong> Nō reira ko ahau ko [name].<br><strong>4.</strong> I tīmata au i Wāhanga Tahi me tētahi kupu — &quot;Kia ora.&quot; Ināianei, kua tipu tōku reo ki tētahi mea nui ake.<br><strong>5.</strong> Ko ngā pou o tōku ora ko tōku whānau, ko tōku reo, ko tōku taiao.<br><strong>6.</strong> Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your best sentence].<br><strong>7.</strong> Ko tōku whakataukī e tūāhuatia ana i a au ko tēnei: [whakataukī]. Ko tā tērā mōhio ko te [meaning].<br><strong>8.</strong> Ko tōku wawata: [your deepest aspiration for your reo].<br><strong>9.</strong> Tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou, tēnā koutou katoa.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nine elements, approximately 90 seconds:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Formal opening (Tēnā koutou katoa)</li><li>Pepeha (all 9 lines)</li><li>Nō reira ko ahau (arrival at yourself)</li><li>Learning journey (I tīmata + Kua tipu)</li><li>Pillars of life (Ko ngā pou o tōku ora)</li><li>Best sentence (Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu)</li><li>Whakataukī + meaning (Ko tā tērā mōhio)</li><li>Wawata (deepest aspiration)</li><li>Formal close (Tēnā koutou ×3)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is not a test. This is a gift — to yourself, to your class, and to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei — my best sentence of this year is this. When you say it, say it slowly. Let everyone hear it. It is yours. You made it. And it will exist in this classroom forever.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Final preparation</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last preparation round — then the performances begin.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Silent practice: 3 minutes. Eyes closed. Pepeha from memory, then the rest.</li><li>Partner run-through: full whakaaturanga to your partner. They give one word of feedback.</li><li>Class: who goes first? Volunteers? Or a karakia to open?</li><li>Perform: each student delivers their whakaaturanga. Class responds with Tēnā koe after each.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei. Before you perform — what IS your best sentence? Not the most impressive one. The most true. Say it quietly to yourself right now. Feel it. Now you are ready.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakaaturanga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deliver your whakaaturanga to the class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Deliver all nine elements — from Tēnā koutou katoa to Tēnā koutou ×3.</li><li>After everyone has performed, write a mihi: Ko ngā rerenga i pā mai ki a au ko ēnei — [three classmates' sentences that moved you].</li><li>Write your own sentence about the class: Ko tā tō tātou akomanga hanga ai ko…</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a full reflection on delivering your whakaaturanga (6 sentences) — how it felt to say your pepeha in front of everyone, which part felt most true, what you want to remember about this moment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday: He koha ki te reo — A gift to the language":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs · Creative expression · Final creative writing</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 2.3 — Creative gift to the language; AO 2.5 — Full year vocabulary</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — What will you give back?</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You have received so much from te reo Māori this year. Today we think about what we give BACK — a koha (gift/contribution) to the language itself. This might be a poem, a statement, a whakaaro, a pūrākau, or a promise. Whatever form it takes, it is yours and it is real.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Examples of koha ki te reo:</strong><br><br><strong>A promise:</strong><br>Ko tāku koha ki tēnei reo: māku e kōrero, māku e tiaki, māku e homai ki ōku tamariki āpōpō.<br>(My gift to this language: I will speak it, protect it, and give it to my children tomorrow.)<br><br><strong>A poem:</strong><br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=reo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">reo</a> koe<br>He ara koe<br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=aho\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aho</a> koe e <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=whiri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whiri</a> ana i ngā ngākau<br>He <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=m%C4%81ramatanga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">māramatanga</a> koe ki te ao <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pouri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pouri</a><br>Ko koe ko ahau.<br>(You are a language / You are a path / You are a cord that weaves hearts / You are light in the dark world / You are me.)<br><br><strong>A whakaaro:</strong><br>Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu. Ko tāua ko tāua — e kore e wehewehea.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Koha forms:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>PROMISE: Māku e [three commitments]. Ko tērā tāku koha ki a koe.<br>POEM: Use Ko/He/Ka sentences as lines. Let sound and image lead.<br>WHAKAARO: A single profound statement — Ko te reo [metaphor].<br>MIHI: Tēnā koe e te reo — ko tāku mihi ki a koe ko tēnei…</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>New creative phrase:<br><strong>ao pouri</strong> — dark world / world of difficulty<br><strong>Ko koe ko ahau.</strong> — You are me. (profound identification)<br><strong>e kore e wehewehea</strong> — cannot be separated (from Week 8!)<br><strong>Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu.</strong><br>You endure through my words.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ko koe ko ahau — you are me. This is the most intimate thing you can say to a language. The language and the speaker have become one. Can you say this honestly about te reo Māori at the end of this year?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — Create your koha</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your koha to the language — in whatever form feels right.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your form: promise, poem, whakaaro, or mihi to the language.</li><li>Draft: write freely for 5 minutes — no thinking about structures, just what you want to say.</li><li>Refine: read it aloud. Change anything that doesn't sound true. Keep everything that does.</li><li>Share: read your koha aloud to the class. They listen in silence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu. The language endures through your words. You endure through the language's words. A reciprocal endurance. What does it feel like to think that your words — in te reo Māori — will endure? That you will leave something in the language?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He koha ki te reo</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your final creative gift to te reo Māori.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your form and write your koha — at least 5 sentences or lines.</li><li>Write in te reo Māori — let the language carry your meaning.</li><li>Read it aloud once more before you share. Make it yours completely.</li><li>✦ Bonus: Write a bilingual version — your koha in te reo Māori alongside an English version. Then ask: which version feels more true? Which says it better? What does the language you chose reveal about what the words mean?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday: He whakamātautau whakamutunga — The final assessment of the year":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Te Aho Arataki Marau — All AOs 1.1–2.5 · All six language modes · Final year assessment</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Objectives</th><td class=\"lt-td\">AO 1.1 — Greetings; AO 1.2 — Introductions; AO 1.4 — Identity; AO 2.3 — Full spontaneous conversation; AO 2.5 — Full vocabulary; AO 1.6 — Politeness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">I Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tāua Kōrero — The end</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the last lesson of the year. There is no model conversation today. You know what to do. You have been preparing for this since Week 1 of Term 1. Go.</p></div><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\">What the final assessment asks of you:<br><br><strong>Open formally.</strong> Tēnā koe.<br><strong>Give your full <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=pepeha\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pepeha</a>.</strong> All 9 lines. From your heart, not your memory.<br><strong>Talk about your year.</strong> What happened. How you felt. What grew.<br><strong>Share a <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=Whakataukī\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">whakataukī</a>.</strong> The one that represents you. Explain its meaning.<br><strong>State your <a href=\"https://maoridictionary.co.nz/search?keywords=wawata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">wawata</a>.</strong> Your deepest aspiration for your reo and your life.<br><strong>Close.</strong> Ka nui ōku mihi. Mā te wā.<br><br>That is the assessment. Not what structures you use — but whether you mean what you say.</blockquote><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What the assessor is listening for:</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>✓ Does this person MEAN what they are saying?<br>✓ Is the language NATURAL — not performed but inhabited?<br>✓ Do the structures SERVE the meaning, rather than the meaning serving the structures?<br>✓ Is the pepeha delivered with confidence and pride?<br>✓ Is the whakataukī CHOSEN, not recited?<br>✓ Is the wawata GENUINE — something the speaker actually aspires to?<br>✓ Is the farewell warm and complete?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You do not need to be perfect. You need to be present.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ka angitu koe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Tip: Ka angitu koe. You will succeed. Not because you are ready — but because you have been ready since before you knew it. The year built you for this moment. Trust it.</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">We Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Kia Tūhono — He karakia whakamutunga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before the assessments begin, the class says a karakia together — an acknowledgement and closing of the year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Together: <strong>Unuhia, unuhia, unuhia ki uta!</strong> (Lift it, lift it, lift it to the shore!)</li><li>Together: <strong>Kia wātea, kia māmā.</strong> (Let it be free, let it be light.)</li><li>Together: <strong>Āe rā.</strong> (Indeed.)</li><li>Then: assessments begin. Each student takes their turn.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Discussion prompt: Kia wātea, kia māmā — let it be free, let it be light. The karakia releases the class from the constraints of ordinary learning into the freedom of genuine expression. What does it mean to be &quot;free and light&quot; in a language? Have you felt that this year, even once?</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">You Do</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Māu Anō — He whakamātautau whakamutunga</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Your final assessment. Your last act in te reo Māori this year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Full spontaneous conversation</strong> — no script. At least 16 lines. Every domain.</li><li><strong>Self-assessment:</strong> He aha tōku pai? He aha tōku uauatanga? He aha tāku ka ako tonu ai?</li><li><strong>Final written record:</strong> Ko tōku rerenga whakamutunga o tēnei tau ko tēnei: [your final sentence].</li><li>✦ Final checklist: ☐ Pepeha (9 lines, from the heart) · ☐ Learning journey (I/Kua/E…ana) · ☐ Whakataukī + meaning · ☐ Wawata (genuine) · ☐ Ka nui ōku mihi · ☐ Mā te wā · ☐ The whole thing meant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday: Aromatawai — Quiz & Review":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Monday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ka whakanuitia ā tātou mahi.&quot; What does whakanui mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>to test and grade </li><li>to review and correct </li><li>to celebrate / honour / make great ✅</li><li>to repeat and practise </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tā tātou mahi tahi i hanga ai tēnei.&quot; What does this sentence reflect on?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The teacher's individual work </li><li>What their working together has built as a class ✅</li><li>A past event from Term 1 </li><li>A future plan for next year </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Tuesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How many elements does the final whakaaturanga have?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>5 </li><li>7 </li><li>9 ✅</li><li>12 </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ko tōku rerenga pai rawa atu i tēnei tau ko tēnei.&quot; What is the speaker doing?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Asking for the best sentence </li><li>Sharing their finest sentence of the year ✅</li><li>Explaining a whakataukī </li><li>Describing their school </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Wednesday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Ko koe ko ahau.&quot; In the context of language, what does this mean?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>You and I are different </li><li>You are my teacher </li><li>The language and the speaker have become one — a profound identification ✅</li><li>I do not know you </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Ka toitū koe i ōku kupu. Ka toitū au i āu kupu.&quot; What relationship does this describe?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A competitive relationship between speaker and language </li><li>A reciprocal endurance — the language endures through your words, you endure through the language's words ✅</li><li>A one-way gift from language to speaker </li><li>A past memory </li></ul></li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Thursday questions</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The final assessment asks whether you MEAN what you say. What does this reveal about the purpose of language learning?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Grammar accuracy is the only goal </li><li>Language learning is about performing for assessment </li><li>Genuine expression — inhabiting the language rather than just performing it — is the highest achievement ✅</li><li>Vocabulary size determines success </li></ul></li><li>&quot;Kia wātea, kia māmā.&quot; What does this karakia invoke?<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Difficulty and challenge </li><li>Speed and accuracy </li><li>Freedom and lightness — releasing from constraint into genuine expression ✅</li><li>Silence and stillness </li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}},"reading":{"label":"Topic","children":{"Te Haerenga — Term Reading Voyage":{"label":"Week","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Years 5–6 (NZC English, Phase 2) | 1 story per week | 4 lessons/week @ ~50 mins | 10-week term</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the planning skeleton we'll use to build the app. It defines the weekly rhythm, the day-by-day lesson shape, how each day ties to the NZ Curriculum, and the dropdown structure the app needs. Once you're happy with this, we build the real thing (story-by-story content + the app itself).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">1. Story Bank (from your uploaded archive)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>11 texts found — mostly Connected/School Journal texts with a strong <strong>voyaging, migration &amp; discovery</strong> thread (waka, Moriori settlement of Rēkohu, Lapita migration, kūaka/godwit migration). Great built-in theme for the term if you want one, or they can stand alone.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Title</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Type</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Rough level</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Theme</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">1</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Journey (Robin Nathan)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Bird migration, courage, growing up</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">2</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Journey of a Waka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (School Journal)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Voyaging, navigation, wayfinding</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">3</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beyond the Reef</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Moriori oral history)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Migration to Rēkohu</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">4</td><td class=\"lt-td\">A Short History of Rēkohu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Moriori history, place</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">5</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Where No Boat Could Live</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Chatham Islands, weather/seafaring</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe: Aotearoa's First Settlement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wairau Bar archaeology</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">7</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Long Pause</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Lapita migration into the Pacific</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">8</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Kūaka: The Marathon Bird</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Godwit migration</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">9</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Rā: The Last of Its Kind</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Taonga, weaving/sail</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">10</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Identity, taonga</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">11</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Woven Taonga / Over One, Under One</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Connected)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Yr 5/6</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Weaving, craft, culture</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Final term running order:</strong> 10 stories, one per week — <em>Taki Tahi | Over One, Under One</em> was considered for Week 11 but was ultimately dropped, making this a clean 10-week term. The app assigns any story to any week freely, so the order can still be changed.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">2. The Weekly Rhythm (Mon–Fri, same shape every week)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One story = one week. Same 4-day skeleton every time so it becomes routine for students, freeing up their energy for the actual reading rather than &quot;what do we do today.&quot;</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Day</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Focus</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Grouping</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Curriculum emphasis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Monday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead-in + whole-class vocab &amp; comprehension</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole class</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language, vocabulary knowledge, activating prior knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Tuesday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Guided reading</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Level groups</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression — decoding, fluency, comprehension at instructional level</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Wednesday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading activities</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies, collaborative talk, practice &amp; knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Thursday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Understanding project (part 1)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs/small groups</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Deeper comprehension, synthesis, creating a text/response</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Friday</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Understanding project (part 2) + Presentations</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Pairs/small groups → whole class</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language, presenting ideas, key competencies (relating to others, participating &amp; contributing)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Every lesson ≈ 50 minutes.</strong> Suggested internal timing (adjustable per lesson type in the app):</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>5 min: settle + recap/hook</li><li>35–40 min: main activity</li><li>5–10 min: share-back / exit check</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟦 Monday — Whole Class: Lead-in, Vocabulary &amp; Comprehension</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build the shared foundation everyone needs before independent/group work starts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Lead-in question(s)</strong> — 2–3 hook questions per story (predicting from title/cover image, connecting to students' own experience, activating background knowledge — e.g. &quot;Have you ever moved somewhere new? What would you take?&quot;)</li><li><strong>Vocabulary breakdown</strong> — pull 6–10 key/tricky words from the story; explicit teaching (meaning, syllables, example sentence, connect to kupu Māori where relevant)</li><li><strong>First read / read-aloud</strong> — teacher reads or plays audio of the whole text; whole-class basic comprehension check (literal questions — who/what/where/when)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> oral language component of structured literacy; vocabulary knowledge; activating prior knowledge as a comprehension strategy (NZC English Phase 2, Years 4–6)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 Tuesday — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading practice matched to instructional level, teacher rotates or runs one group at a time.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Groups re-read the text (or a levelled excerpt) at their own pace/level</li><li>Teacher-led group gets explicit decoding/fluency/comprehension instruction</li><li>Other groups: independent re-read + simple task (e.g. sequence the events, find evidence for an answer)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> differentiated instruction matched to reading progression; fluency and comprehension strategies at instructional level</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 Wednesday — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Active, hands-on comprehension work — this is where &quot;fun&quot; lives.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paired/hands-on activities such as: sequencing cards, vocabulary matching games, &quot;hot seat&quot; character/interviewee questioning, comprehension scavenger hunt, draw-and-label diagrams, true/false detective (find evidence in text), question each other using question cubes/dice</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> collaborative talk, comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding), practice consolidation</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟧 Thursday — Understanding Project (Part 1)</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students synthesise their understanding into something they create — start the project.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pairs/small groups choose or are assigned a project format (rotate weekly for variety — see menu below)</li><li>Planning + drafting/building time</li><li>Teacher checks in with each group</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟥 Friday — Understanding Project (Part 2) + Presentations</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Purpose</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Finish, polish, and share.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>First ~20–25 min: finish project</li><li>Remaining time: presentations to class (2–3 min per group) + peer feedback (e.g. 2 stars and a wish, thumbs poll)</li><li><strong>Curriculum link:</strong> oral language — presenting ideas clearly; key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Understanding Project menu (rotate through the term so it stays fresh)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic strip / storyboard retelling</li><li>Diorama or model of a key scene/setting</li><li>Podcast-style recorded interview (in role)</li><li>Poster/infographic (non-fiction texts — facts, timeline, diagram)</li><li>Short dramatic re-enactment / freeze-frame scenes</li><li>&quot;Fact file&quot; or travel brochure (great fit for the voyaging/migration texts)</li><li>Book trailer / pitch to sell the story to another class</li><li>Debate or &quot;would you have gone?&quot; persuasive mini-speech</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">4. Curriculum Alignment (NZC English, Phase 2 — Years 4–6, effective from 2025 refresh)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app should tag each lesson against these Phase 2 strands so you can show/print curriculum coverage per week:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Reading progressions</strong> — decoding, vocabulary, fluency, comprehension (literal, inferential, evaluative)</li><li><strong>Oral language</strong> — spoken language development, structured talk, presenting</li><li><strong>Structured literacy components</strong> — explicit, systematic, cumulative teaching of oral language + reading + writing</li><li><strong>Texts reflecting Aotearoa's bicultural heritage</strong> — kupu Māori, Māori and Moriori narratives/perspectives (a strong fit given your story bank)</li><li><strong>Key competencies</strong> — thinking; using language, symbols &amp; text; managing self; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each week's lesson set (Mon–Fri) should show a short tick-list of which of these 5 areas it covers — this becomes your at-a-glance curriculum-coverage report for the term.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian character reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Alongside the NZC strands, every week's full lesson plan includes <strong>one reflection question per school value</strong>, tied to that week's story:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love: God, Others, Self, World)</li><li><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know: God, His story and our place in it)</li><li><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve: faithfully with our gifts and talents)</li><li><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact: bringing God's Kingdom to earth)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Core screens</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Term Overview</strong> — grid of Week 1–11, each cell shows story title + status (planned/in progress/done), with an easy way to drag/reassign a story out to &quot;next term&quot; if the term runs shorter</li><li><strong>Week Planner</strong> — the main workhorse screen</li><li><strong>Story Library</strong> — list of the 11 stories with metadata (title, type, level, theme, uploaded file)</li><li><strong>Curriculum Coverage view</strong> — auto-generated report of which Phase 2 strands have been covered, week by week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Dropdowns (as requested)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the <strong>Week Planner</strong> screen:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Dropdown 1: Week</strong> → Week 1, Week 2 … Week 11</li><li><strong>Dropdown 2: Day</strong> → Monday / Tuesday / Wednesday / Thursday / Friday</li><li>Selecting Week + Day loads that day's lesson: story reference, lead-in questions, vocab list, activity instructions, timing breakdown, curriculum tags — all editable/printable</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Additional dropdown/selector ideas worth building in:</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Story assignment</strong> dropdown per week (pick which of the 11 stories runs that week — fully flexible, not locked to the table order)</li><li><strong>Level group</strong> selector (Tuesday) — set your own group names/levels once, reuse every week</li><li><strong>Understanding project type</strong> dropdown (Thu/Fri) — pick from the project menu above, or mark &quot;custom&quot;</li><li><strong>Print/export</strong> button — generate a one-page lesson summary for the day (handy to have on the whiteboard or printed for relievers)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Data per story (what we'll build out next, story by story)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, file link, type (fiction/non-fiction), reading level</li><li>2–3 lead-in questions</li><li>Key vocabulary list (word + kid-friendly definition)</li><li>Basic comprehension questions (literal, for Monday)</li><li>Level-group discussion/task prompts (Tuesday)</li><li>Paired activity instructions (Wednesday)</li><li>Suggested understanding-project options for that specific story (Thu/Fri)</li><li>Curriculum tags (which Phase 2 strands this story/week covers)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">6. Next Steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Confirm this backbone (rhythm, day purposes, curriculum tags, dropdown structure)</li><li>✅ Running as an <strong>11-week term</strong> — all 11 stories get a week; you decide later if one needs to drop to next term</li><li>✅ <strong>Proof-of-concept content pack built</strong> for Week 1 — <em>The Journey</em> (see <code>story-01-the-journey-content-pack.md</code>) — this is the template every other story will follow</li><li>Decide the <strong>running order</strong> for weeks 2–11 (or let me suggest one — e.g. building from lighter fiction into the denser non-fiction texts like Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe and The Long Pause later in the term)</li><li>Once you're happy with the Week 1 template, we build out the same content pack for the remaining 10 stories</li><li>Build the actual app (dropdowns + content display) wired up to this content</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 1 Content Pack — \"The Journey\" (Robin Nathan)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bird migration, courage, growing up</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>Note:</strong> this was the proof-of-concept template every later week's content pack followed.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wilfred is a young godwit who doesn't want to join his flock's huge migration from Alaska to New Zealand, led by the respected Captain Thunder. Along the way the flock survives a storm, and Wilfred grows from reluctant and scared into brave, gaining a new understanding of duty and courage. It's a gentle, character-driven opener for the term — light on bicultural content by design, since it's a fictional lead-in to the real, non-fiction kūaka migration story that appears later (Week 8).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever had to do something you didn't want to, even though everyone told you it was important? How did you feel?</li><li>Look at the title and first page. Who do you think &quot;Wilfred&quot; is? What might &quot;the Journey&quot; be?</li><li>Do you know any animals that travel huge distances every year? What do you already know about migration?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">godwit</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long-beaked shorebird that flies between Alaska and NZ every year</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">flock</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large group of birds travelling or living together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">solemnly</td><td class=\"lt-td\">in a serious, formal way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">elder</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an older, respected member of a group</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">captain</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the leader in charge of a journey or crew</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">duty</td><td class=\"lt-td\">something you must do because it is your responsibility</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">exhausted</td><td class=\"lt-td\">extremely tired, with no energy left</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">amazement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a feeling of great surprise</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;too chicken&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">idiom — too scared to do something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;birdbrain&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">joking insult meaning silly (funny here — they ARE birds!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where do the godwits live in summer, and where are they flying to?</li><li>About how far is the journey, in kilometres?</li><li>Why doesn't Wilfred want to go at first?</li><li>Who is Captain Thunder, and why does everyone respect him?</li><li>What happened to Flight Officer Wingspan during the storm?</li><li>How did Captain Thunder and the flock survive the storm?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • activating prior knowledge • literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read pages 1–3 together with teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 4 picture/sentence cards in the order events happened.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: how dialogue shows character.</li><li>Task: find 2 pieces of evidence Wilfred is nervous, and 2 that show he's brave by the end.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference &amp; humour — why is calling a bird &quot;chicken&quot; or &quot;birdbrain&quot; funny?</li><li>Task: write one more line of dialogue Wilfred might say once they land in NZ, staying true to character.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression • fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Captain Thunder</strong> — One partner is Captain Thunder, the other a young godwit reporter. Ask about the storm, Wingspan, and why the Journey matters — then swap.</li><li><strong>Storm Sequencing</strong> — Cut up 6 sentences describing the storm scene. Pairs order them and explain how they knew.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>True or False Detective</strong> — Given 8 statements about the story, pairs find evidence in the text to prove or disprove each one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell the storm scene from Captain Thunder's memory, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>&quot;Would you go?&quot; persuasive mini-speech</strong> — argue whether you'd join the Journey if you were Wilfred.</li><li><strong>Fact file / travel brochure</strong> — &quot;Visit New Zealand — Godwit Summer Home!&quot;</li><li><strong>Podcast-style recording</strong> — in role as Wilfred, record a &quot;voice diary&quot; the night before leaving Alaska.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wilfred's family is patient and encouraging even when he grumbles and doesn't want to go. How can we show aroha to someone in our own family or class who is scared or reluctant, the way Wilfred was?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Godwits know exactly when and where to go because that's how God designed them. What does this remind us about trusting God's plan for our lives, even when we can't see the whole journey ahead?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Thunder used his courage and experience to protect and guide the whole flock. What gift or talent has God given you that you could use to serve others this week, even when it feels hard?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The godwits' journey brings new life to two different lands, every single year. How can facing a challenge we don't want to face still end up bringing good to others around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives — light this week (fictional godwit family); flag the real kūaka migration and its significance to Māori as a bridge to the non-fiction companion text later in the term (Week 8)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 2 Content Pack — \"Journey of a Waka\" (School Journal)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (School Journal) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Voyaging, navigation, wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A multi-section, non-fiction explainer on how the ancestors of Māori sailed to Aotearoa aboard waka hourua (double-hulled voyaging canoes) — covering the signs that told them it was time to leave, the essential items they carried, the four key crew roles (rangatira, tohunga, kaihautū, kaiurungi), and the signs that told them land was near. Strong bicultural content — real oral histories and voyaging knowledge, treated with genuine respect throughout.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you were about to sail across the world's biggest ocean with no map and no GPS, what would you pack? What would you need to know?</li><li>What clues in nature might tell you a storm is coming, or that land is near?</li><li>Have you heard of waka hourua, or Polynesian wayfinding/navigation? What do you already know?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">waka hourua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large double-hulled voyaging canoe used for long ocean journeys</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">rangatira</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the captain/leader of the waka</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tohunga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the priest and navigator — reads the sky, stars and sea</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaihautū</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the person who directs the paddlers</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaiurungi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the steerer(s) of the waka</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">adze</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a tool for shaping wood, like a curved axe</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mana</td><td class=\"lt-td\">prestige, authority, and respect</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">oral histories</td><td class=\"lt-td\">stories and knowledge passed down by speaking, generation to generation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">archaeology</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the study of the past through objects and sites people left behind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migrating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">travelling regularly between two places, often with the seasons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Where did the ancestors of Māori sail from, and roughly how long ago did they begin arriving in Aotearoa?</li><li>Name three signs that told the ancestors it was the right time to leave.</li><li>Name three essential items carried on the waka.</li><li>What animals may have helped guide the waka on their journeys?</li><li>Name three signs that told the ancestors land was near.</li><li>What were the four main roles on a waka hourua, and what did each person do?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (incl. kupu Māori) • non-fiction text features • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read one section at a time together (e.g. &quot;Navigation,&quot; then &quot;Essential items&quot;).</li><li>Task: highlight or underline one key fact from each section read.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: locating information across a non-linear, multi-section text.</li><li>Task: answer 3–4 questions requiring info from different sections; match each role name to its job.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: comparing traditional and modern navigation.</li><li>Task: compare wayfinding (stars, currents, wind, birds) with modern GPS/compass. Which would you trust more, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — using text features to locate information • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Text Feature Scavenger Hunt</strong> — Pairs find and list every heading/section title, and explain what each section is about in their own words.</li><li><strong>Role Card Match</strong> — Match cards with role titles (rangatira, tohunga, kaihautū, kaiurungi) to a card describing that job — race another pair.</li><li><strong>Packing List Debate</strong> — Given the &quot;essential items&quot; list, pairs rank the top 5 most important for survival and justify their order.</li><li><strong>Nature-Clue Detectives</strong> — Sort mixed clue cards into: Signs it's time to leave / Signs land is near / Navigation method.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features • comprehension strategies (locating &amp; categorising information) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Design your own infographic</strong> — &quot;How to Survive a Waka Hourua Journey&quot; — in the same labelled-section style as the original.</li><li><strong>Role-play news report</strong> — interview a tohunga about how they read the stars, currents and wildlife to find their way.</li><li><strong>Labelled diagram/model</strong> — a waka hourua showing where key supplies, food, water and people would be positioned.</li><li><strong>Persuasive pitch</strong> — &quot;Why our waka should carry [an animal] on board&quot; using evidence about kurī or kiore.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The waka carried whole families and communities together, all depending on each other to survive the journey. What does this show us about what it means to love and care for others as part of a team?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The ancestors used the stars, currents and signs in creation to find their way across the ocean. How is this similar to how we can look to God's creation and His Word to help guide our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every person on the waka had a role — navigator, steerer, paddler — and used their own skill to serve the whole crew. What's one gift God has given you that you could use to serve your class or family this week?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The voyage of the ancestors led to the beginning of a whole new nation. How can the choices we make now create a positive impact that reaches further than just ourselves?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 3 Content Pack — \"Beyond the Reef\" (Moriori oral history)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (Moriori oral history) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Migration to Rēkohu</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ri'i's village is preparing to secretly leave their home at night, after fighting has broken out and Ri'i's grandfather Horopapa — grieving the loss of his daughter — dreams of a peaceful new island. Ri'i is entrusted with a secret job to help prepare for departure, and has to keep it hidden even from his close friend Moe. The story follows the emotional lead-up to departure, the farewell, and the moment the canoes finally cross beyond the reef. This is a genuine Moriori oral history handed down for generations — treat its grief and hope with the same respect given to any real historical account.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever had to keep a big secret from a close friend — one you knew might hurt them? How did it feel?</li><li>Imagine leaving your home forever, at night, not knowing if you'd ever return. What would be running through your mind?</li><li>What do you already know about the Moriori people and Rēkohu (the Chatham Islands)?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">paihihi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">canoes (as glossed in the text)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakii</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a chant/incantation, said for strength or a safe journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">umere</td><td class=\"lt-td\">chants, part of the departure ritual</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hunau</td><td class=\"lt-td\">family</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">etchu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">spiritual beings</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ominous</td><td class=\"lt-td\">making you feel something bad is about to happen</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ritual</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a set of actions done the same way, often for a special or spiritual reason</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">secrecy</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the state of keeping something hidden</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">navigator</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the person who works out the direction and course of a journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hull</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the main body of a boat or canoe</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who is Ri'i, and why is his village leaving Rēkohu?</li><li>What does Horopapa dream about, and why does it matter to him?</li><li>What was Ri'i's secret job in the weeks before departure?</li><li>Why couldn't Moe's family know about the departure plan?</li><li>What does Horopapa give Ri'i just before the canoes leave (the karakii)?</li><li>What happens as the canoes finally cross beyond the reef?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (incl. Moriori/te reo terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (handled sensitively)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the three &quot;•••&quot; sections together with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 event cards in order, from the fighting breaking out to the canoes crossing the reef.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding mixed emotions.</li><li>Task: find evidence Ri'i feels both fear and hope; discuss why the departure needed to stay secret.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference &amp; cross-text connection.</li><li>Task: what might Horopapa's dream represent? Compare the roles/preparations here with <em>Journey of a Waka</em> (Week 2) — what's the same, what's different?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — inference, understanding time-shifts • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Farewell Hot Seat</strong> — One partner is Ri'i, the other Moe, at their last meeting by the reef. Re-enact or extend their conversation, staying true to how each character feels.</li><li><strong>Event Sequencing</strong> — Pairs order 6–8 cut-up sentences describing key moments and justify using text evidence.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match Moriori/te reo terms (paihihi, karakii, umere, hunau, etchu) and other vocab to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Secret-Keeper Dilemma</strong> — Pairs discuss: was it right to keep the plan secret from Moe? Each argues one side, then write a joint conclusion.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell the farewell scene between Ri'i and Moe, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>Diary entry</strong> — write as Ri'i, the night before leaving, describing his fears and hopes.</li><li><strong>Persuasive mini-speech</strong> — should the village council have listened to &quot;the way of no more fighting&quot; sooner?</li><li><strong>Labelled voyage map</strong> — draw and label the four canoes and their crew roles, linking back to <em>Journey of a Waka</em>.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even while hurting after his daughter's death, Horopapa chooses hope and peace instead of holding onto anger. How can we choose love over anger when we've been badly hurt by someone?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Horopapa holds onto the memory of a peaceful time and dreams of a better future for his people. How does knowing God's story help us hold onto hope even when things around us feel hard or uncertain?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Ri'i is given an important job to help his people, even though he is still young. How can we serve faithfully with what we have, even when we feel too young, small, or unready?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The families' journey is meant to bring a new, peaceful way of life to a new land. What's one way you could help bring more peace into our own school or home this week?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 4 Content Pack — \"A Short History of Rēkohu\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Moriori history, place</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — Aotearoa histories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A timeline-structured non-fiction text tracing Moriori history from the Law of Nunuku (an agreement that ended warfare between Moriori tribes) through the 1835 invasion by Ngāti Mutunga and Ngāti Tama, the false story published about Moriori in a 1916 School Journal, the significance of Tame Horomona Rehe (Tommy Solomon), and up to today's efforts to recognise and support Moriori people and culture. This week is a serious, direct link to Aotearoa's histories — real injustice and real resilience — and should be taught with the same care given to any true account of harm done to real people.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Is it easy or hard to choose peace, even when you're being treated unfairly? Why?</li><li>Have you heard of the Moriori people or the Chatham Islands before today?</li><li>Why do you think it matters to learn the true history of a place and its people?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tchakat henu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;people of the land&quot; — how Moriori describe themselves</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Law of Nunuku</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Nunuku-whenua's agreement that ended warfare between Moriori tribes forever</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">invasion</td><td class=\"lt-td\">when a group of people forcefully takes over land that belongs to others</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">slavery</td><td class=\"lt-td\">forcing people to work for others against their will, with no freedom</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">dendroglyphs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">carvings made into living trees — a form of Moriori art and record</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">marae</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a communal meeting ground, central to cultural life</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Treaty settlement</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an agreement to address past injustice between government and iwi/group</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">resilience</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the ability to keep going and recover, even after very hard times</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">census</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an official count of a country's population</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tribunal</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a type of court that hears and rules on claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does &quot;Rēkohu&quot; mean, and where is it located?</li><li>What is the Law of Nunuku, and who created it?</li><li>What happened when Ngāti Mutunga and Ngāti Tama arrived on Rēkohu in 1835?</li><li>Why was the story published in the 1916 School Journal untrue, and why did it matter?</li><li>Who was Tame Horomona Rehe (Tommy Solomon), and why is he significant?</li><li>Name two things being done today to recognise and support Moriori people and culture.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction timeline text features • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives (handled with care)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the timeline in date order with the teacher, from the Law of Nunuku through to 1900.</li><li>Task: with support, sequence 5–6 major event cards in order using the dates given.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining significance in own words.</li><li>Task: explain what the Law of Nunuku says and why it mattered; find evidence of what happened in 1835 and how Moriori responded.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: critical thinking about historical narratives.</li><li>Task: why does the false 1916 story &quot;have a lasting impact&quot;? Compare that to today's efforts to reclaim the true history.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — chronological/timeline features, cause and effect • differentiated instruction • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Pairs match event cards to their correct dates/order.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (tchakat henu, Law of Nunuku, dendroglyphs, marae, resilience) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Law of Nunuku Discussion</strong> — Discuss respectfully: what does it mean to choose peace even when treated unfairly? Write a joint response.</li><li><strong>Fact-Check Detectives</strong> — Compare the false 1916 School Journal excerpt with the real history told elsewhere in the text.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features • comprehension strategies (evidence-finding, comparing sources) • collaborative talk • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Annotated timeline poster</strong> — choose 8–10 key events from Rēkohu's history and explain each briefly.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why is it important to learn true history, even when it's hard?&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file</strong> — research Kopinga Marae or the Chatham Island black robin conservation story.</li><li><strong>Respectful role-play interview</strong> — in role as a Moriori elder today, discuss the meaning of the Law of Nunuku.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the timeline + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori chose not to fight back because of Nunuku's Law of peace, even when treated with great cruelty. What does this teach us about loving even those who hurt us, the way Jesus calls us to love our enemies?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">For many years, an untrue story was taught about the Moriori people, and it caused harm for generations. Why does it matter that we know the truth — about God's story, and about the true stories of others — rather than believing false ones?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Today, Moriori people are working hard to restore and share their true history and culture. How can we serve others by helping to tell true stories and treating people's histories with honour?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Places like Kopinga Marae show how a community can rebuild and bring good things back after great loss. How can we be part of bringing healing and restoration — a piece of God's Kingdom — into situations of hurt around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 5 Content Pack — \"Where No Boat Could Live\"":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chatham Islands, weather/seafaring</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science/Technology link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An explanation text on how Moriori engineered the wash-through waka — a design adapted for the &quot;roaring forties,&quot; the fierce seas around the Chatham Islands — after their original dugout canoes proved unsuited to the conditions. The text includes a 1791 quote from a European visitor, James Johnstone, who dismissively compared the waka to &quot;wheelbarrows,&quot; and then explains the real engineering genius behind the design. Continues the term's throughline of correcting outsiders' biased first impressions of Moriori ingenuity.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you had to build a boat to survive huge, stormy seas, what would you want it to do — float no matter what? Never sink? Carry a lot?</li><li>Have you ever made something clever out of natural materials (sticks, reeds, plants)? What did you make, and how did you solve a problem with it?</li><li>What do you know about the Chatham Islands and their weather?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">roaring forties</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a stretch of ocean known for very strong winds and storms</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">dugout canoe</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a canoe carved from a single hollowed-out log</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wash-through waka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a boat built to let water flow through it, rather than keep it out</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ballast</td><td class=\"lt-td\">something heavy that makes a boat stable</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">buoyant</td><td class=\"lt-td\">able to float</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">capsize</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to overturn in the water</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">bow</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the front of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">stern</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the back of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hull</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the bottom/body of a boat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">innovators</td><td class=\"lt-td\">people who invent new and clever solutions to problems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why are the waters around the Chatham Islands nicknamed &quot;the roaring forties&quot;?</li><li>Why didn't the Moriori's original dugout canoes work well in Rēkohu's seas?</li><li>What were the two types of wash-through waka, and how were they different in size?</li><li>What materials were used to build a wash-through waka?</li><li>Why didn't the wash-through waka need to be waterproof?</li><li>What did European visitors think of the waka at first, and why were they wrong?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (nautical/technical terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives • cross-curricular Science/Technology link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the sections on waka kōrari and waka pahī with the teacher.</li><li>Task: label a diagram of a wash-through waka with its key parts (bow, stern, hull, runners).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining how and why the design worked.</li><li>Task: explain why letting water &quot;wash through&quot; made the waka safer; find evidence of how Europeans misunderstood the design.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating bias in a primary source.</li><li>Task: compare Johnstone's 1791 &quot;wheelbarrows&quot; quote with what we now know. Why might a visitor misjudge something they don't understand? Connect to Week 4's false 1916 story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — technical vocabulary, evaluating primary sources • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Label the Waka</strong> — Pairs label a diagram of a wash-through waka with its parts and materials (bow, stern, hull, runners, kelp, reeds).</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match nautical/technical terms (ballast, buoyant, capsize, bow, stern, hull) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>First Impressions vs Facts</strong> — Compare Johnstone's dismissive 1791 quote with the true engineering facts. What did he get wrong, and why?</li><li><strong>Design Challenge Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss: if you had to build a boat from natural materials for a stormy sea, what would you use, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, primary sources) • comprehension strategies (comparing sources, evaluating bias) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled diagram or model</strong> of a wash-through waka, showing and naming all key parts and materials.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why the Moriori's boat design was actually genius, not primitive.&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing the old dugout canoe with the new wash-through waka.</li><li><strong>Design challenge (STEM)</strong> — sketch your own natural-material boat for stormy seas, labelling why each part would work.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">European visitors judged the Moriori's waka as &quot;clumsy and primitive&quot; without taking time to understand them. How does this remind us to love others by trying to understand them first, rather than judging by first impressions?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori used careful observation of the natural world — kelp, reeds, wind and sea — to build something that truly worked. How is learning to notice and understand God's creation part of knowing His story and our place in it?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The Moriori used their skills and knowledge to serve their whole community's need for food and safety, inventing a completely new kind of boat. What skill or gift could you develop to serve others facing a real problem?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">For years, outsiders misunderstood and dismissed Moriori ingenuity — but the truth about their skill is now being recognised. How can we help make sure good work and truth are recognised, even when others don't see it straight away?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 6 Content Pack — \"Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe: Aotearoa's First Settlement\" (interview)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wairau Bar archaeology</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and Aotearoa histories links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An interview text exploring the archaeological discoveries at Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe (Wairau Bar), Aotearoa's earliest known settlement site — covering how scientists used DNA, radiocarbon dating, and evidence like a tropical shell chisel to piece together where the first settlers came from and how they lived. The text also covers the disrespectful removal of Rangitāne ancestral remains (kōiwi tangata) in the 1940s–50s, and the years-long fight by kaumātua Hohua Peter MacDonald and others for their return (repatriation). A serious, real account of harm and its resolution — teach with real weight given to both the injustice and the hopeful outcome.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If archaeologists dug up your backyard in 700 years, what might they learn about you just from what you'd thrown away?</li><li>How do you think scientists can figure out how old something is, or even where a person was born, just from bones and teeth?</li><li>Why do you think it matters how we treat ancestral remains and burial sites?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tūpuna</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestors</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kōiwi tangata</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestral (human) remains</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wāhi tapu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a sacred place</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">midden</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an ancient rubbish dump full of discarded bones, shells and tools</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientific method used to estimate the age of things like bone or shell</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">DNA</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the genetic information in a cell that tells us about ancestry</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">argillite (pakohe)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a hard stone, found only at the top of the South Island, used to make tools</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taonga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">treasured cultural objects</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">repatriation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">returning ancestral remains or cultural objects to their community</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hāngī pits</td><td class=\"lt-td\">pits used for traditional cooking, often for large feasts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Te Pokohiwi-o-Kupe also known as, and where is it located?</li><li>About how long ago was Te Pokohiwi first settled, and how do scientists know this?</li><li>What evidence suggests some of the people buried there were born outside Aotearoa?</li><li>Why was Te Pokohiwi a good place to settle?</li><li>What happened to the burial sites and taonga at Te Pokohiwi in the 1940s–50s?</li><li>What is being done today to address this, and who is leading it?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction interview text features • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives • cross-curricular Science link</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the interview Q&amp;A with the teacher, identifying who asks and who answers.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 key events in Te Pokohiwi's history in order.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding scientific evidence.</li><li>Task: explain what DNA, radiocarbon dating, and the tropical shell chisel each reveal; find evidence of the site's spiritual significance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: critical and ethical thinking.</li><li>Task: discuss why removing kōiwi tangata without full community agreement was wrong, and what repatriation means for healing today. Connect to Week 4.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — interview text structure, evaluating evidence • differentiated instruction • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview Feature Hunt</strong> — Notice the Q&amp;A structure. Pairs write one new question they'd ask Dr Meihana, and a likely evidence-based answer.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (tūpuna, kōiwi tangata, wāhi tapu, midden, argillite, taonga, repatriation) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Evidence Detective</strong> — Match each archaeological clue to what it tells scientists about the first settlers.</li><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Order 6–8 events in Te Pokohiwi's history, from first settlement to the return of kōiwi tangata.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (interview structure) • comprehension strategies (evidence-matching, sequencing) • collaborative talk • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Your own interview script</strong> — in role as an archaeologist, write a Q&amp;A about what was discovered at Te Pokohiwi.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why ancestral remains and taonga should be returned to their home communities.&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file/poster</strong> — &quot;What Te Pokohiwi Teaches Us About Aotearoa's First Settlers,&quot; organised by evidence type.</li><li><strong>Timeline poster</strong> of Te Pokohiwi's history, from 1280 AD to today.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking critically and ethically • bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Rangitāne's tūpuna were dug up and displayed without proper respect — even in a fish and chip shop. How does this help us understand why it's so important to treat every person, living or after they've died, with dignity and love, the way God calls us to?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists used bones, teeth, DNA and even a small shell chisel to piece together the true story of where people came from. How is this similar to how we can carefully search for truth about God's story and our own history, rather than guessing or ignoring evidence?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Kaumātua Hohua Peter MacDonald spent years serving his people by fighting for the return of their ancestors, even though he wasn't successful straight away. How can we serve others faithfully, even when results take a long time or don't come easily?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">After many years, Rangitāne successfully negotiated the return of their tūpuna's remains. How can our own actions today help bring healing and put right something that was wronged in the past?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa histories perspectives</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 7 Content Pack — \"The Long Pause\" (Connected, Level 3, by Dr Amber Aranui)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (explanation text, multiple competing scientific theories) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 6 (challenge text)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The 1,000-year gap between the Lapita migration and the settlement of East Polynesia/Aotearoa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science (archaeology, hypothesis-testing) links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>About 3,500 years ago, the Lapita people rapidly explored and settled West Polynesia (Fiji, Sāmoa, Tonga) in just 500 years. Then — mysteriously — exploration stopped for at least another 1,000 years, before people finally voyaged on to Hawai'i, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa. This text presents <strong>three competing scientific theories</strong> for why: (1) canoe technology wasn't yet good enough for the longer, rougher East Polynesian crossings, (2) resource competition and possible droughts in West Polynesia may have pushed people to search for new land, and (3) computer models suggest wind patterns changed at just the right times to make eastward sailing possible. This is the most complex text of the term — a great one for teaching how scientists evaluate multiple, competing explanations rather than settling for a single simple answer.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating (Week 6), and waka technology/the waka hourua (Weeks 2, 3 and 5) all reappear here — worth pointing out explicitly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Imagine sailing into the ocean with no idea what's on the other side, or if there's anything there at all. What would make you brave enough to go?</li><li>Why do you think people might stop exploring somewhere for a very long time, then suddenly start again?</li><li>What do you already know about how the Pacific Islands, including Aotearoa, were first settled?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Lapita</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the early Pacific people who first migrated from the Bismarck Islands out into the Pacific</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migration</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the movement of people from one place to settle in another</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">archaeologist</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientist who studies the past through objects and remains people left behind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hypothesis</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a possible explanation that still needs more evidence to prove it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">outrigger canoe</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a canoe with a float attached to one side for balance, but limited in size and distance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">waka hourua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a double-hulled canoe that could travel further and carry more</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">drought</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long period of very dry weather</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">computer models</td><td class=\"lt-td\">computer programs that use maths and data to predict what might have happened</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hospitable</td><td class=\"lt-td\">pleasant and favourable for living in</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">radiocarbon dating</td><td class=\"lt-td\">measuring radioactive carbon in remains to estimate how long ago something lived</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a denser text with multiple theories — read section by section, pausing after each theory to summarise it in one sentence as a class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who were the Lapita, and where did they first settle?</li><li>About how long was &quot;the long pause&quot; between the two migrations?</li><li>Name two possible reasons scientists suggest for the long pause.</li><li>What is the difference between an outrigger canoe and a waka hourua?</li><li>How do computer models help researchers understand wind patterns from the past?</li><li>Which was the last major area of the Pacific to be settled?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • literal comprehension • bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives • cross-curricular Science link (hypothesis-testing, radiocarbon dating)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the timeline section with the teacher.</li><li>Task: label a simple timeline with the key migration dates (Lapita arrive in Bismarck Islands → West Polynesia settled → the long pause → East Polynesia/Aotearoa settled).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: explaining one theory clearly.</li><li>Task: choose one of the three theories (technology, resources/drought, or wind patterns) and explain it in your own words, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating multiple competing explanations.</li><li>Task: compare all three theories. Which do you find most convincing, and why? Discuss why scientists test several hypotheses rather than settling for one simple answer.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — evaluating multiple explanations, technical vocabulary • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Theory Sorting</strong> — Pairs sort evidence statements under the correct theory heading: Technology / Resources &amp; Drought / Wind Patterns.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (Lapita, hypothesis, outrigger canoe, waka hourua, drought, hospitable) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Canoe Comparison</strong> — Compare the outrigger canoe and the waka hourua using a table or Venn diagram — what could each one do?</li><li><strong>Which Theory Convinces You?</strong> — Pairs discuss and write a short joint justification for which theory they find most convincing, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, timelines) • comprehension strategies (comparing and evaluating explanations) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled timeline poster</strong> showing the Lapita migration → the long pause → the eventual settlement of Aotearoa.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Which theory best explains the long pause?&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing outrigger canoe and waka hourua technology.</li><li><strong>Detective case file</strong> — present the three theories as clues in a mystery investigation into &quot;the case of the long pause.&quot;</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking (evaluating evidence) • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Over generations, people kept improving their boats and knowledge so that one day, exploration could continue — even though they might never see the results themselves. How can loving others sometimes mean patiently working on something now, even if we don't see the outcome ourselves?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists don't just guess about the past — they carefully gather evidence and test different explanations before deciding what's most likely true. How is this similar to how we should search carefully for truth about God and His story, rather than jumping to conclusions?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Over generations, people worked together — developing new tools, boats and knowledge — to serve those who would come after them on this incredible journey. How can we use our own gifts to serve and prepare the way for people who come after us?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The eventual voyages to Aotearoa changed the future of an entire country forever. How can small, patient steps we take now end up having a huge impact later, even if we can't see it yet?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (evaluating multiple explanations, technical vocabulary, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. science/archaeology terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (Polynesian voyaging heritage)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — evaluating competing hypotheses; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (hypothesis-testing, radiocarbon dating, computer modelling)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 8 Content Pack — \"Kūaka: The Marathon Bird\" (School Journal)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (illustrated magazine-style article) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The bar-tailed godwit's incredible non-stop migration, and both scientific and traditional Māori ways of understanding it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kūaka (bar-tailed godwits) make one of the most extraordinary journeys in the animal kingdom — flying non-stop between Alaska and New Zealand, a distance of around 11,000 km, without landing to rest, eat, or drink. Before departure their bodies transform: they fatten up dramatically and some of their internal organs shrink to save weight, then regrow once they arrive. Modern satellite tracking has revealed the details of this journey scientifically, while Māori traditional knowledge (maramataka, the lunar calendar, and whakataukī, traditional proverbs) has understood and marked the kūaka's arrival and departure as a seasonal signal for many generations. This is a great, uplifting text after some heavier weeks — genuine wonder at what creation can do, alongside real science.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this connects naturally to Week 1 (the fictional godwit story, <em>The Journey</em>) and continues the term's Science/bicultural threads from Weeks 6 and 7.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could you run for 8–9 days without stopping to eat, drink, or rest? How do you think a bird could fly that long, non-stop?</li><li>What signs in nature (birds arriving, plants flowering) tell people that a new season is coming?</li><li>What do you already know about the kūaka/godwit and its incredible journey? (A nice link back to Week 1's story, <em>The Journey</em>.)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kūaka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the Māori name for the bar-tailed godwit, a large migratory shorebird</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">migratory</td><td class=\"lt-td\">travelling regularly between two places, usually with the seasons</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">non-stop</td><td class=\"lt-td\">without any pause or rest along the way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">satellite tracking</td><td class=\"lt-td\">using satellites to follow and record an animal's exact movements</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">estuary</td><td class=\"lt-td\">where a river meets the sea, often rich in food for wading birds</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">maramataka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the traditional Māori lunar calendar</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whakataukī</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a Māori proverb or saying that carries traditional wisdom</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">V formation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a flying pattern where birds take turns leading to save energy</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">fuel up / fatten up</td><td class=\"lt-td\">eating heavily to store enough energy for a long journey</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">organs</td><td class=\"lt-td\">internal body parts (like the stomach or liver) that carry out life functions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the article together, pausing at photos/diagrams of the migration route and body changes.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a kūaka, and where does it migrate between?</li><li>About how far do kūaka fly, and how long does the journey take?</li><li>What happens to a kūaka's body before it begins its migration?</li><li>How do scientists track where kūaka go?</li><li>What role does the kūaka play in traditional Māori knowledge of the seasons?</li><li>Why do kūaka fly in a V formation?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (maramataka, whakataukī) • cross-curricular Science link (adaptation, migration, tracking technology)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the &quot;getting ready to fly&quot; section with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence the stages of a kūaka's migration preparation (fatten up → organs shrink → depart → fly non-stop → arrive and refuel → organs regrow).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding scientific tracking.</li><li>Task: explain in your own words how satellite tracking works and what scientists have learned from it; find evidence of the physical toll the journey takes on the bird's body.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: valuing two different ways of knowing.</li><li>Task: discuss why both Māori traditional knowledge (whakataukī, maramataka) and modern satellite science matter for understanding kūaka — two different, valuable ways of knowing the same bird. Connect this to Weeks 6 and 7's science themes.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — sequencing, understanding scientific processes • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Body Changes Sequencing</strong> — Pairs sequence cards showing the stages of a kūaka's body before, during, and after migration.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (kūaka, migratory, satellite tracking, maramataka, whakataukī, V formation) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>V-Formation Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss and sketch why flying in a V formation might save energy for a flock of birds.</li><li><strong>Two Ways of Knowing</strong> — Pairs match pieces of evidence to whether they come from traditional Māori knowledge or modern scientific tracking — and discuss why both matter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (diagrams, photos) • comprehension strategies (sequencing, comparing sources of knowledge) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Labelled migration map/diagram</strong> showing the kūaka's route, with body changes noted at each stage.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why the kūaka's migration is one of the most incredible feats in the animal kingdom.&quot;</li><li><strong>News report/documentary style</strong> comparing traditional Māori knowledge and modern satellite tracking of kūaka.</li><li><strong>Fact file</strong> — &quot;Kūaka: The Marathon Bird&quot; — organised by distance, body changes, tracking science, and cultural significance.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The kūaka take turns leading the flock in a V formation, sharing the hardest work so every bird can complete the journey together. How does this remind us of God's call to love others by sharing burdens and looking out for one another?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The kūaka's body knows exactly how to prepare for this incredible journey, and Māori knew for generations how to read the seasons by its arrival. How does this show us that both creation and traditional wisdom can help us know God's world more fully?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Scientists have spent years carefully tracking and studying kūaka to help protect their journey and habitats for future generations. How can we serve and care for God's creation, even in small ways, in our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every kūaka's journey — though it's just one small bird — has an impact on ecosystems and cultures across half the world. How can something that seems small in our own lives still have a big impact on the world around us?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (sequencing, understanding scientific processes, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. science and te reo terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (maramataka, whakataukī, traditional ecological knowledge)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — valuing multiple ways of knowing; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (adaptation, migration, satellite tracking technology)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 9 Content Pack — \"Te Rā: The Last of Its Kind\" (Connected 2024, an interview by Hamish Ansley)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (interview/Q&amp;A format) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Te Rā, an extraordinary 200+ year old Māori woven sail — mātauranga Māori, science, and coming home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6) — with strong Science and bicultural links</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Te Rā is probably the only Māori woven sail of its kind left in the world — made from harakeke (flax) in Aotearoa in the 1700s, and kept for most of the last 200 years in the British Museum in London, without anyone quite knowing how it got there. In 2019, three researchers — a weaver/scholar, an expert weaver, and a materials scientist — travelled to London to study Te Rā, combining traditional Māori weaving knowledge (mātauranga) with modern science (like polarised light microscopy) to understand how it was made and what it reveals about Māori voyaging technology. The interview also covers the emotional experience of studying and briefly bringing home a taonga that had been away from its people for so long.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to earlier weeks</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this continues the waka/sail technology thread from Weeks 2, 3, 5 and 7, and echoes Week 6's story of research, evidence, and the significance of bringing a taonga home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If your family owned something really old and important, and it ended up far away in another country's museum for 200 years, how would you feel about it coming home?</li><li>What is something in your own family or culture that is treasured and passed down? Why does it matter?</li><li>Have you ever used both traditional knowledge (like a family recipe or skill) and modern science or tools together to understand or check something?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taonga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a treasured object of cultural, spiritual, or historical significance</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mātauranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">traditional Māori knowledge</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whakapapa</td><td class=\"lt-td\">genealogy — the history and lineage of a person, family, or object</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">harakeke</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the Māori word for flax, a plant used for weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">raranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the art of weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">muka</td><td class=\"lt-td\">fibre prepared from flax, used for cord or binding</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">wānanga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a gathering for learning and discussion</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mauri</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the spirit or life-force of a person, place, or object</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">polarised light microscope</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientific tool used to examine fine details of materials like fibres</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">tūpuna</td><td class=\"lt-td\">ancestors</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read as an interview, with different readers taking the parts of Donna, Rānui, and Catherine.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is Te Rā, and how old is it?</li><li>Where was Te Rā kept for most of the last 200 years?</li><li>What did the three researchers want to find out about Te Rā?</li><li>What scientific tool did they use, and what did it prove?</li><li>What might the kererū and kāhu feathers on the sail have been used for?</li><li>How did people feel when Te Rā was brought back to Aotearoa?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge • non-fiction interview text features • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives (mātauranga Māori) • cross-curricular Science link (materials science, microscopy)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the interview Q&amp;A with the teacher.</li><li>Task: identify the three researchers and their roles (weaver/artist/scholar, expert weaver, scientist).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: understanding how science and traditional knowledge worked together.</li><li>Task: explain in your own words what the polarised light microscope revealed, and why proving the harakeke scientifically mattered alongside traditional weaving knowledge.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: emotional and cultural significance.</li><li>Task: discuss why not knowing Te Rā's full whakapapa (how it got to the British Museum) matters, and why bringing it home — even temporarily — was so emotionally significant. Compare this to Week 6's story about Te Pokohiwi.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — interview text structure, understanding scientific evidence • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview Feature Hunt</strong> — Pairs write one new question they'd ask one of the three researchers, and a likely evidence-based answer.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (taonga, mātauranga, harakeke, raranga, muka, wānanga, mauri) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Evidence Detective</strong> — Match each scientific/weaving clue (the microscope images, the zigzag weave pattern, the split feathers) to what it reveals about Te Rā.</li><li><strong>How Would It Feel?</strong> — Pairs discuss and write about how it might feel to see or touch a 200-year-old taonga from your own culture for the first time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">non-fiction text features (interview structure) • comprehension strategies (evidence-matching) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Interview script</strong> — in role as one of the three researchers, write and perform a Q&amp;A about Te Rā.</li><li><strong>Persuasive speech</strong> — &quot;Why it's important for taonga like Te Rā to be studied and shared with their home communities.&quot;</li><li><strong>Fact file/poster</strong> on Te Rā — organised by materials, science, history, and cultural significance.</li><li><strong>Labelled diagram</strong> of Te Rā's sail parts (matairangi, huru manu, eyelets, zigzag weave), with captions.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the text + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others, thinking • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Donna described crying because she felt sorry for Te Rā being alone, away from its people for so long. How does this remind us that people — and even meaningful things — are made to belong and be loved within community, not to be alone?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The researchers combined careful science (the microscope) with traditional weaving knowledge to understand Te Rā fully. How is this like using both careful observation of creation and God's Word together to grow in knowing Him and His world?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Donna, Rānui, and Catherine spent years using their skills and knowledge to serve their community by bringing understanding of Te Rā back home. How can we use our own skills to serve and preserve something important for others?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though Te Rā could only come home for a short time, its return had a huge impact — inspiring a child who saw it to want to make his own sail. How can something we do that seems small or temporary still leave a big, lasting impact on someone else?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (interview/Q&amp;A text features, evidence-based comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo and science terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (mātauranga Māori, taonga, weaving knowledge)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-curricular Science link (materials science, microscopy)</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 10 Content Pack — \"Tahu and the Dancing Feathers\" (Connected 2024, by André Ngāpō)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (narrative) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Patience, the value of small contributions, and understanding a sail's feathers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tahu desperately wants to help his sister Huia and the other weavers make a new sail, Hine-Rangimārie, to replace the old, worn-out Hinetakitaki. Instead, Whaea Pere gives him the job of splitting kererū feathers with a mussel shell — a job Tahu finds boring and unimportant compared to weaving. When he's told he's still too young to weave, he storms off, calling the feathers &quot;useless.&quot; But when the new sail is finally raised and Tahu watches the feathers tremble and dance in the wind, he suddenly understands: the feathers show which way the wind blows, and his &quot;small&quot; job was essential all along.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Nice link back to Week 9</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this fictional story explains the exact real detail from <em>Te Rā</em> — the kererū and kāhu feathers along a sail's edge showing wind direction. Pairing the two texts (fiction and real interview) is a great comprehension/synthesis opportunity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever really wanted to help with something, but been told you were too young or not ready yet? How did that feel?</li><li>Do you think even small, boring-seeming jobs can be important? Can you think of an example?</li><li>What do you already know about how sails were made from harakeke (flax)? (Remember Te Rā from Week 9!)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">raranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">weaving</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakia</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a prayer or ritual chant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">atua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a god or deity — e.g. Tāwhirimātea, atua of the wind</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">e moko</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an affectionate term for a grandchild</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">koro</td><td class=\"lt-td\">grandfather, or a respected male elder</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whaea</td><td class=\"lt-td\">aunty, or a respected female elder/teacher</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ātaahua</td><td class=\"lt-td\">beautiful</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kūtai</td><td class=\"lt-td\">mussel (used here as a cutting tool)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whanaunga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">relatives</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">huru manu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">feathers along the edge of a sail (recap from Week 9's Te Rā!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the story together, noticing how Tahu's feelings shift from frustration to pride and understanding.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What job does Whaea Pere give Tahu while the others weave the new sail?</li><li>Why does Tahu want to help weave the sail instead?</li><li>What is the name of the old sail, and what is happening to it?</li><li>What is the new sail called, and what does Koro Hotu say it will do?</li><li>Why does Tahu get upset and knock over the kete of feathers?</li><li>What does Tahu finally realise about the feathers at the end of the story?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (te reo Māori) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story with the teacher.</li><li>Task: sequence 6 key events in order (the waka returns → Tahu watches the weavers → Tahu splits feathers → Tahu asks to help weave → the new sail is raised → Tahu understands the feathers' purpose).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: tracking a character's changing feelings.</li><li>Task: find evidence of Tahu's feelings changing from frustration to pride and understanding; explain what Whaea Pere means by &quot;each job, big and small, helped make the sail.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: connecting texts.</li><li>Task: discuss the theme of patience and valuing different contributions. Connect this story's feathers to Te Rā's huru manu from Week 9 — this story explains <em>why</em> those feathers matter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — tracking character development, inference • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Feather-Splitting Hot Seat</strong> — One partner is Tahu, the other is Whaea Pere; re-enact the conversation about wanting to help weave.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (raranga, karakia, atua, koro, whaea, kūtai, whanaunga) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>&quot;Every Job Matters&quot; Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss and write about a time a &quot;small&quot; job turned out to be more important than it seemed.</li><li><strong>Comparing Texts</strong> — Pairs compare <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em> (fiction) with <em>Te Rā</em> (non-fiction interview) — what's similar, what's different, about how they present sails and feathers?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">comprehension strategies (inference, comparing texts) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Comic strip</strong> — retell Tahu's feather-splitting and his realisation moment, in 6 panels.</li><li><strong>Persuasive mini-speech</strong> — &quot;Why every job, big or small, matters&quot; — using evidence from the story.</li><li><strong>Diary entry</strong> — write as Tahu, the night after the new sail is raised.</li><li><strong>Fact-meets-fiction poster</strong> — comparing what <em>Te Rā</em> (real, Week 9) and <em>Hine-Rangimārie</em> (fictional, this week) both tell us about a sail's feathers.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the story + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading, comparing texts • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whaea Pere and Koro treat Tahu with patience and kindness even when he's frustrated and knocks over the kete. How can we show love and patience to someone who is struggling with disappointment or impatience, like Tahu?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu didn't understand the purpose of his job until he saw the finished sail in action. How is this like trusting that God has a good purpose for the small or hard tasks He gives us, even when we can't see the full picture yet?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even though splitting feathers felt small and unimportant to Tahu, it was a real act of service that helped the whole sail succeed. How can we serve faithfully in small tasks, trusting that they matter, even when we don't feel important?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Tahu's carefully split feathers ended up being an essential part of guiding the waka safely across the sea. How can small, faithful actions we do now end up making a real difference later, even if we don't realise it at the time?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (tracking character development, inference, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo Māori terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (whānau roles, patience, te reo Māori)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: strong cross-text synthesis link with Week 9's Te Rā</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 11 Content Pack — \"Taki Tahi | Over One, Under One\" (Connected 2024, a poem by Briar Grace-Smith)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Poetry | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Weaving a waka's sail together — the rhythm of shared, skilled work, and the sail's first test at sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>A note on this text:</strong> this is a poem, so in line with copyright practice, this pack describes its content, structure and devices rather than quoting lines directly. Students will read the actual poem from their own copy of the text — the activities below ask them to find and discuss specific lines themselves.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Story Snapshot (for you)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This poem describes a group of weavers making a waka's sail from harakeke, working through the &quot;taki tahi&quot; pattern — over one, under one — a phrase that repeats through the poem like the rhythm of the weaving itself. While their hands work, the weavers chat about everyday things (sport, television) right alongside traditional stories and atua (gods) — showing that this skilled, sacred work is also warm and ordinary, done side by side. The poem follows the sail from its finishing, through a quiet moment of pride, to the moment it's finally raised and tested against the wind — and passes the test, powering the waka out across the sea.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Term capstone</th><td class=\"lt-td\">this is the third and final text from the same journal collection as Weeks 9 and 10 — <em>Te Rā</em> (a real interview about an ancient sail) and <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em> (a fictional story about a sail being made). All three texts explore weaving and waka sails through a different genre: non-fiction, fiction, and poetry. This is a great week to draw the whole collection — and arguably the whole term's voyaging theme — together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever done a repetitive job (like kneading dough, or a chore) while chatting with friends or family about everyday things? What did you talk about?</li><li>Poems often repeat a phrase or line again and again. Why might a poet choose to do that?</li><li>What do you already know about weaving a waka's sail? (Remember Te Rā and Tahu and the Dancing Feathers!)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">taki tahi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the &quot;over one, under one&quot; weaving pattern used in raranga</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">refrain</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a repeated line or phrase in a poem, often for rhythm or emphasis</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">simile</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a comparison using &quot;like&quot; or &quot;as&quot;</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">metaphor</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a comparison that says one thing IS another, without using &quot;like&quot; or &quot;as&quot;</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">imagery</td><td class=\"lt-td\">descriptive language that helps the reader picture, hear, or feel something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ara</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a path, way, or pattern</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Tāwhirimātea</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the atua (god) of wind and storms</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">karakia</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a prayer or ritual chant</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Me inoi tātou&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a phrase meaning &quot;let us pray&quot; — an invitation to karakia</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kaiwhatu / kairaranga</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a weaver — a person who weaves</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Whole-class read</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Read the poem aloud slowly, letting the repeated refrain build a rhythm — perhaps read it once through for the story, then again noticing the repetition.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension questions (literal — whole class)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What are the weavers making in this poem?</li><li>What everyday things do the weavers talk about while they work?</li><li>What traditional stories or figures do the weavers also talk about?</li><li>What repeated phrase runs through the poem, describing the weaving pattern?</li><li>What happens to the sail at the end of the poem?</li><li>Which atua (god) is mentioned in connection with the wind testing the sail?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language • vocabulary knowledge (te reo Māori and poetic terms) • literal comprehension • bicultural perspectives • poetic form and devices</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the poem with the teacher, section by section.</li><li>Task: identify the repeated refrain in your own copy of the poem, and count how many times it appears. Discuss what's happening at each stage (starting to weave → chatting → finishing → raising the sail → sailing).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led guided group)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: identifying poetic devices.</li><li>Task: find one simile and one metaphor in your own copy of the poem. Copy each one down, then explain in your own words what is being compared.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: interpreting meaning and connecting texts.</li><li>Task: discuss why the poet includes everyday chatter (sport, television) alongside sacred, traditional stories (Māui, gods). What does that tell us about weaving as both ordinary and special? Connect this poem's message to <em>Te Rā</em> and <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">reading progression — identifying poetic devices, interpreting meaning • differentiated instruction • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Refrain Hunt</strong> — Pairs find and count the repeated refrain in the poem and discuss the effect it creates (rhythm, a sense of the steady weaving pattern).</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Match key terms (taki tahi, refrain, simile, metaphor, ara, Tāwhirimātea, karakia) to their meanings.</li><li><strong>Simile &amp; Metaphor Detective</strong> — Pairs find and copy down one simile and one metaphor from the poem, then explain what's being compared and why it's effective.</li><li><strong>Three Texts, One Story</strong> — Pairs compare all three texts in the collection (<em>Te Rā</em>, <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>, and this poem) — what does each one contribute to understanding waka sails and weaving?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">poetic devices (simile, metaphor, refrain) • comprehension strategies (comparing texts) • collaborative talk • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Write your own &quot;over one, under one&quot; style poem</strong> about a repetitive activity you do, using your own refrain.</li><li><strong>Illustrated poster</strong> capturing the poem's journey from weaving to sailing.</li><li><strong>Term reflection speech</strong> — &quot;What have I learned about waka, weaving, and Aotearoa's voyaging heritage this term?&quot;</li><li><strong>&quot;Three Texts, One Journey&quot; fact file</strong> comparing <em>Te Rā</em>, <em>Tahu and the Dancing Feathers</em>, and this poem.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min: recap the poem + choose project format</li><li>~35 min: plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min: teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min: finish and polish</li><li>~20–25 min: presentations (2–3 min per pair) + peer feedback (&quot;2 stars and a wish&quot;)</li><li>Consider making this final Friday a small celebration of the whole term's voyage!</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">oral language — presenting ideas clearly • key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others • synthesis — creating a text in response to reading, comparing texts across a term • bicultural perspectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The weavers work side by side, sharing both everyday chatter and treasured stories while making something beautiful together. How does working alongside others in love and friendship, like the weavers do, reflect how God wants us to live in community?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The refrain &quot;over one, under one&quot; repeats throughout the poem, like a steady rhythm the weavers can trust. How is returning again and again to God's Word and story like this steady, trustworthy rhythm in our own lives?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Each weaver serves the group by focusing on their part of the pattern, patiently and carefully, so the whole sail comes together. How can we serve our own family, class, or team with the same patience and care?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The finished sail carries the waka safely across the wild sea, its purpose fulfilled. As we finish this term's reading journey together, what impact do you hope the things you've learned will have on your own life going forward?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions (identifying poetic devices, interpreting meaning, comprehension)</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy components (explicit vocabulary incl. te reo Māori and poetic terms)</li><li>✅ Bicultural/Aotearoa perspectives (traditional weaving, te reo Māori, atua)</li><li>✅ Key competencies (thinking — interpreting meaning; language, symbols &amp; text; relating to others; participating &amp; contributing)</li><li>🌟 Bonus: term capstone — connects across all three texts in this journal collection (Weeks 9–11), and offers a chance to reflect on the whole term's voyaging theme</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Te Haerenga — Term 2 Reading Voyage (Southern Africa)":{"label":"Week","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Term 2 Reading Programme — Southern Africa</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ten weeks of reading exploring South African and Zimbabwean stories, folk tales and history — from a classic cumulative tale through to a Nelson Mandela biography. Each week follows the same structure as Term 1: Monday vocabulary and comprehension, Tuesday levelled guided-reading groups, Wednesday paired activities, Thursday/Friday project build and presentation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Week 1: The Old Woman and Her Pig</li><li>Week 2: South Africa &amp; Zimbabwe: A Journey Through Time</li><li>Week 3: The Small Seed</li><li>Week 4: A Bowl of Phutu</li><li>Week 5: Storm</li><li>Week 6: The Boerewors Man</li><li>Week 7: Sisanda's Gift</li><li>Week 8: Wolf's Supper</li><li>Week 9: Why Crocodile Lives in the River</li><li>Week 10: Nelson Mandela: The Long Walk to Freedom</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 1 Content Pack — “The Old Woman and Her Pig” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — light this week, a classic cumulative folk tale; the historical/cultural depth builds through the term, especially from Week 2's history timeline onward.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a cumulative tale — by working out new vocabulary, tracking a repeating chain of cause and effect, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and performance.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever tried to get someone (or something!) to do what you wanted, and they just would not budge? What did you do?</li><li>Look at the title and cover. What do you think might happen between an old woman and a very stubborn pig?</li><li>This story is a &quot;cumulative tale&quot; — that means it builds a chain, where each new character is added to the ones before. Can you think of any other stories or songs built like a chain or a list that keeps growing?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">cumulative tale</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a story that builds a repeating chain, adding one new part each time</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">stubborn</td><td class=\"lt-td\">refusing to do what you're asked, no matter what</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">pleaded</td><td class=\"lt-td\">asked for something in a begging, desperate way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">fetched</td><td class=\"lt-td\">went and got something, then brought it back</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">butcher</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a person whose job is preparing and selling meat</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">trotting</td><td class=\"lt-td\">moving at a quick, light pace (often used for animals)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">splash</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to make water fly up and land on something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">sigh</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long, deep breath let out slowly, often showing relief or tiredness</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">chew</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to bite and grind something with your teeth, again and again</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">collar</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a band put around an animal's neck, often to attach a lead</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the old woman find while she was sweeping her home?</li><li>What did she decide to buy at the market?</li><li>Why wouldn't the pig get over the fence?</li><li>List at least four other characters or things the old woman asks for help, in order.</li><li>What did the mouse want before it would agree to help?</li><li>What finally happened once the mouse started chewing the rope?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking the growing chain.</li><li>Put picture/word cards for the chain (pig, dog, water, ox, butcher, rope, mouse) in the correct order.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: how the repeating pattern builds tension and humour.</li><li>Find the sentence each time the old woman adds a new complaint to her list — how does it get longer and longer?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: cause and effect chains, and the mouse's clever bargain.</li><li>Explain in your own words why the mouse is the only character who gets something in return. What does this tell us about the mouse?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Chain Reaction Retell</strong> — Using picture or word cards for each link in the chain, pairs take turns retelling the story aloud in order, without looking at the book.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>Persuasion Practice: Hot Seat</strong> — One partner plays the old woman, the other plays the pig, dog, water, ox or butcher. Practise pleading and refusing, using the story's actual words as a starting point, then swap.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (sequencing, cause &amp; effect) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic strip — draw the whole chain reaction in 6–8 panels, from the pig refusing to jump to the final happy ending.</li><li>Podcast-style recording — perform the story as a radio play, using different silly voices for each character in the chain.</li><li>Build your own cumulative chain — invent a brand new chain tale (e.g. &quot;The Girl and Her Kite&quot;) using the same repeating pattern, and write or tell the first 3 links.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The old woman keeps trying, again and again, to get her pig home safely, even when nobody wants to help her. How can we keep showing patience and love to someone even when things get frustrating?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The mouse is small, but it's the one link that finally gets the whole chain moving. What does this remind us about how God can use small, ordinary things — including us — to make a big difference?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Nobody in the chain wants to help at first — until the mouse asks for something in return. What's the difference between serving others freely and only helping when we get something back? Which does God ask us to do?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One small act — the mouse chewing the rope — sets off a chain that finally gets the old woman and her pig home. How can one small kind choice we make this week set off a positive chain reaction for others?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — light this week, a classic cumulative folk tale; the historical/cultural depth builds through the term, especially from Week 2's history timeline onward.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 2 Content Pack — “South Africa & Zimbabwe: A Journey Through Time” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (illustrated history timeline) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This week carries the term's historical and cultural weight directly and honestly, including apartheid and colonisation — introduced here at a timeline/overview level, and returning in more depth in Week 10's Nelson Mandela biography.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to read and understand a non-fiction history timeline — by using text features (headings, Word Watch boxes, dates) to locate information, and by thinking critically about cause and effect across history.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What do you already know about South Africa or Zimbabwe? Turn and share with your partner.</li><li>This text is non-fiction and set out like a timeline with 7 chapters. Skim the chapter list — which time period sounds the most interesting to you, and why?</li><li>The text calls itself a 'time machine.' If you could build a real time machine, which moment in history would you visit first?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hunter-gatherer</td><td class=\"lt-td\">someone who finds food by hunting animals and gathering wild plants</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ochre</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a natural reddish clay used by ancient artists as paint</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">archaeologist</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a scientist who studies the past by digging up old objects and buildings</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">kingdom</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a land ruled by a king or queen</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">upheaval</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a time of great change or disruption</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">colony</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a place ruled and settled by people from another country</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">empire</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a group of countries or territories controlled by one ruler or government</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">apartheid</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a system of laws in South Africa (1948–1994) that separated people by race and gave unfair power to white people</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">liberation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">being set free, especially from unfair rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who were the San people, and what were they known for?</li><li>What was Great Zimbabwe, and how did the country of Zimbabwe get its name?</li><li>Who was Shaka, and what happened to Mzilikazi's people as a result of his rise?</li><li>What happened at the Cape of Good Hope in 1652?</li><li>What is apartheid, and when did it become law in South Africa?</li><li>What happened on 27 April 1994?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Non-fiction text features</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read one chapter together with teacher.</li><li>Retell that chapter's main event in your own words, using the Word Watch box to help.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: cause and effect across two chapters.</li><li>Explain how one chapter's events led to what happened in the next chapter.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating the 'moment to pause on' box.</li><li>Discuss: why do the authors include a box that says wealth for some was built on unfairness for others? Why does that matter to include in a history text?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression — using headings and timelines to locate information • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Cut up the 7 chapter title cards. Pairs put them in the correct chronological order and explain how they knew.</li><li><strong>Word Watch Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each 'Word Watch' term to its definition — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>Which Moment Surprised You?</strong> — Using the text's own 'Talk About It' questions, pairs discuss which moment in the timeline surprised them most and why, then write a joint response.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (sequencing, cause &amp; effect) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Illustrated timeline poster — choose all 7 chapters and summarise each in one sentence with a small drawing.</li><li>&quot;Did You Know?&quot; fact file — research one extra fact about San rock art, Great Zimbabwe, or another chapter, and present it like the text's own &quot;Did You Know?&quot; boxes.</li><li>Persuasive reflection — write a short piece titled &quot;Why history like this matters to remember,&quot; using at least two examples from the text.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, thinking critically</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The text says great wealth for some people was built on great unfairness for others. How can we make sure the way we treat others is fair and loving, even when it would be easier to look away?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">This timeline covers thousands of years in just a few pages. What does knowing this much history help us understand about the people and stories we'll read for the rest of the term?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaders like Nelson Mandela (whom we'll meet properly in Week 10) spent decades serving others by working to end an unjust system. What does it look like to serve others even when it is hard or takes a long time?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One king's rise (Shaka) caused Mzilikazi's people to travel far north and found a whole new kingdom. Big events can have effects that last for centuries. What impact could a choice we make today still have far into the future?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Non-fiction text features</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 This week carries the term's historical and cultural weight directly and honestly, including apartheid and colonisation — introduced here at a timeline/overview level, and returning in more depth in Week 10's Nelson Mandela biography.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 3 Content Pack — “The Small Seed” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a traditional-style village setting, with clear links to the historical kingdoms introduced in Week 2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a narrative about honesty and courage — by working out new vocabulary, reading for meaning, finding evidence of a character's feelings, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and presenting.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever been tempted to hide the truth because you were embarrassed or scared? What happened?</li><li>Look at the title. What do you predict a 'small seed' might have to do with choosing a new chief?</li><li>What do you think makes someone a good leader? Turn and share one idea with your partner.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">chief</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the leader of a village or group of people</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">mielies</td><td class=\"lt-td\">maize/corn plants, grown for food</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">morogo</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a leafy green vegetable, grown and eaten in Southern Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">courage</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the ability to do something difficult or frightening</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">honest / honesty</td><td class=\"lt-td\">telling the truth, even when it is hard to do</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ashamed</td><td class=\"lt-td\">feeling embarrassed or sorry because of something you did or didn't do</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">amazed</td><td class=\"lt-td\">filled with great surprise or wonder</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">heir</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a person who will take over a role, such as chief or king, from someone else</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What task did the old chief give to all the older boys?</li><li>What did Njabulo's mother give him to help plant his seed?</li><li>What happened to Njabulo's seed over the year — did it grow?</li><li>Why was Njabulo scared to go and show the chief his pot?</li><li>What did all the other boys secretly do when their seeds didn't grow?</li><li>Why did the chief choose Njabulo to be the next chief?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking the year passing.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing Njabulo's year, from receiving the seed to the chief's announcement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: how the story shows Njabulo's honesty through his actions.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that Njabulo is honest, and 2 that show the other boys were not honest.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — why the chief boiled the seeds.</li><li>Explain in your own words why the chief gave every boy a seed that could never grow. What was he really testing?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Njabulo</strong> — One partner is Njabulo, the other a village reporter. Ask about the year of waiting, the empty pot, and the moment he told the truth — then swap.</li><li><strong>True or False Detective</strong> — Given 8 statements about the story, pairs find evidence in the text to prove or disprove each one.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Diary entry — write as Njabulo, the night before he must show the chief his empty pot, describing his fears and hopes.</li><li>Persuasive mini-speech — &quot;Why honesty matters more than winning&quot; — using evidence from the story.</li><li>Design a poster — &quot;Wanted: An Honest Leader&quot; — listing the qualities a good chief or leader should have, based on the story.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Njabulo's mother couldn't solve his problem for him, but she still gave him a pot and encouraged him to go and be honest. How can we show love to someone by encouraging them to do the right thing, even when we can't fix it for them?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The chief knew all along that the seeds were boiled and could never grow — he was testing for honesty, not gardening skill. What does this remind us about how God sees our hearts, not just our outward success?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Njabulo says he wants to become chief so he can look after his people well. What does it look like to want a position of leadership because you want to serve others, rather than for the glory of it?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Njabulo's one honest choice — admitting his pot was empty — changed the whole course of his life and his village's future. How could one honest choice we make this week have a bigger impact than we expect?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a traditional-style village setting, with clear links to the historical kingdoms introduced in Week 2.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 4 Content Pack — “A Bowl of Phutu” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a traditional-style tale of kindness and kingship, continuing the village and leadership threads from Weeks 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a narrative about kindness and gratitude — by working out new vocabulary, reading for meaning, finding evidence of character change, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and presenting.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has someone ever been kind to you when you really needed it? How did that feel?</li><li>What does it mean to 'pay it forward' or keep a promise, even many years later?</li><li>Look at the title. What do you think a 'bowl of phutu' might mean to the boy in this story?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">phutu</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a traditional maize porridge, eaten as a staple food in Southern Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Molahlehi</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a name meaning 'the lost one'</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">spear</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a long weapon with a pointed tip, used for hunting</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">promise</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a firm decision to do something, that you commit to keeping</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">gratitude / thankful</td><td class=\"lt-td\">feeling or showing appreciation for something someone has done for you</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">recognised</td><td class=\"lt-td\">realised who someone was, from remembering them before</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">feast</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large, special meal, often to celebrate something</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">king</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the male ruler of a kingdom</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why was the boy in the story given the name Molahlehi?</li><li>What did Molahlehi do every day to try to find food?</li><li>What did the kind woman do for Molahlehi whenever she came to the river?</li><li>What promise did Molahlehi make to the woman before he left?</li><li>How did Molahlehi become king of the village he found?</li><li>What was the first thing King Lebohang did once he became king?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking Molahlehi's journey.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing Molahlehi's journey from boy to king.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evidence of kindness and gratitude.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that the woman was kind to Molahlehi, and 2 that show Molahlehi never forgot her kindness.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — what the name changes mean.</li><li>Explain in your own words why the author changes the boy's name from Molahlehi ('the lost one') to King Lebohang ('be thankful'). What is the author trying to show us?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview King Lebohang</strong> — One partner is King Lebohang, the other a village reporter. Ask about the years of searching, the bowl of phutu, and the day he found the woman again — then swap.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>Journey Sequencing</strong> — Cut up 6 sentences describing Molahlehi's journey from riverbank to kingship. Pairs order them and explain how they knew.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic strip — retell the reunion scene, where King Lebohang gives the woman the bowl of gold, in 6 panels.</li><li>Persuasive mini-speech — &quot;Why we should always remember to be grateful for kindness&quot; — using evidence from the story.</li><li>Letter writing — write a letter from Molahlehi to the kind woman, sent partway through his years of searching for a home, explaining how he still remembers her.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The woman at the river showed love to a hungry stranger, again and again, without expecting anything back. How can we show love to someone this week without expecting a reward?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Molahlehi never forgot the woman's kindness, even after becoming king. What does it mean to really know and remember what someone has done for us, even years later?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">As soon as Molahlehi became king, the very first thing he did was serve and honour the woman who had once served him. Why do you think he made this his first act as king, before anything else?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One small bowl of food, given again and again by a kind stranger, changed the whole course of a lost boy's life. How could a small act of kindness we do this week have an impact we might never fully know?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a traditional-style tale of kindness and kingship, continuing the village and leadership threads from Weeks 2 and 3.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 5 Content Pack — “Storm” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a South African fishing village setting, with a strong disability-awareness and resilience thread.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a narrative about resilience and hidden strengths — by working out new vocabulary, reading for meaning, finding evidence of a character's capability, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and presenting.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does it mean to 'see things differently' to everyone else? Can you think of any strengths that aren't always obvious at first?</li><li>Have you ever found or rescued an animal in need? What happened?</li><li>Look at the title. This story is about both a storm and something else with the same name — what do you predict that might be?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">fishing village</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a small town where most people earn a living by catching fish</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">blind</td><td class=\"lt-td\">unable to see</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;sees things differently&quot;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a phrase used in the story to describe Marie-Lou's blindness in a kind, respectful way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">inspector</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an official whose job includes checking on situations and making decisions, like Inspector Madliwa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">rescue</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to save someone or something from danger</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">bravery</td><td class=\"lt-td\">being willing to do something difficult or frightening</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">whimper</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a soft, weak, crying sound</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">squeal</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a short, high-pitched cry or sound</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What happened in the village on the night Marie-Lou was born?</li><li>Why couldn't Marie-Lou go out fishing with her friends?</li><li>What special skills did Marie-Lou learn instead?</li><li>What did Marie-Lou hear the morning after the second storm?</li><li>What did she find caught among the rocks?</li><li>What did Inspector Madliwa say to Marie-Lou, and what did she decide to name the puppy?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking what happens after the second storm.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing how Marie-Lou finds and keeps Storm.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evidence that Marie-Lou is capable and brave.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that Marie-Lou is skilled despite being blind, and 2 that show her bravery in finding the puppy.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — the double meaning of the title.</li><li>Explain in your own words why the author called this story 'Storm.' What two things does the title refer to, and why is that a clever choice?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Marie-Lou</strong> — One partner is Marie-Lou, the other a village reporter. Ask about growing up blind by the sea, the night of the storm, and finding Storm — then swap.</li><li><strong>Listening Challenge</strong> — With eyes closed, pairs take turns making and identifying quiet sounds (like Marie-Lou does in the story) — then discuss how this connects to her skills in the text.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Diary entry — write as Marie-Lou, the night she finds Storm, describing her fears and hopes.</li><li>Fact poster — research how guide dogs or assistance animals help people, and present 4–5 facts in an illustrated poster.</li><li>Write an extra scene — imagine Storm is a few years older and helps Marie-Lou through a new challenge; write the scene as dialogue.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Marie-Lou's mother always described her blindness kindly, saying she 'sees things differently.' How can the words we choose show love and respect for people who experience the world differently to us?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Marie-Lou knows the wind, the sea and the sounds around her better than almost anyone in the village. What does this remind us about how everyone has different kinds of knowledge and skill, even when others don't expect it?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Once Storm grew close to Marie-Lou, the puppy served and protected her in return for the rescue. What does it look like when love and service go both ways, between two friends?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One quiet, determined choice — Marie-Lou getting up early and following a faint sound — saved a life and changed her own. How could paying close attention to something small this week make a big impact?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a South African fishing village setting, with a strong disability-awareness and resilience thread.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 6 Content Pack — “The Boerewors Man” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a light, funny modern-day South African setting (braai, taxi, nature reserve), giving a change of pace after Weeks 3–5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a humorous chase narrative — by working out new vocabulary, tracking an escalating repeated pattern, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and performance.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Do you know the story of the Gingerbread Man? What usually happens to characters who run away singing?</li><li>Have you ever been to a braai (barbecue)? What foods are usually cooked on one?</li><li>Look at the title and cover. What do you predict will happen to a sausage that can talk and run away?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">braai</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a South African barbecue, often a social gathering around a fire or grill</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">boerewors</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a type of traditional South African sausage, cooked on a braai</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">doek</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a head scarf, often colourfully patterned, worn by some women in Southern Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">vlei</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a South African word for a shallow lake, marsh or wetland</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">nature reserve</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a protected area of land where wild animals and plants are kept safe</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">chuckling</td><td class=\"lt-td\">laughing quietly to oneself</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">predicament</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a difficult, confusing or embarrassing situation</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">fish eagle</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large bird of prey that hunts fish, found near rivers and lakes in Southern Africa</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did Hayley's father make for her at the braai?</li><li>Who chased the Boerewors Man first?</li><li>List at least four different people, animals or vehicles that joined the chase.</li><li>What happened when they reached the Rondevlei Nature Reserve?</li><li>Who offered to help the Boerewors Man cross the vlei?</li><li>What happened to the Boerewors Man in the end?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking who joins the chase.</li><li>Sequence 6 picture/sentence cards showing the order the chase grows, from Hayley to the fish eagle.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: how the chase escalates and repeats.</li><li>List every character who joins the chase, in order, and how each one travels (running, riding, driving).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: comparing to other chase/trickster tales.</li><li>Compare this story to the Gingerbread Man (or a similar tale you know). What's similar? What's different about the ending?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Chase Sequencing</strong> — Cut up 6 sentence strips describing who joins the chase. Pairs order them and explain how they knew.</li><li><strong>Write Your Own Verse</strong> — Using the Boerewors Man's two songs as a model, pairs write one new verse he might sing partway through the chase.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word (braai, boerewors, doek, vlei) to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (sequencing) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comic strip — retell the whole chase in 6–8 panels, from the braai to the fish eagle.</li><li>Radio play — record or perform the chase scene, using different voices for each new character who joins.</li><li>Alternate ending — write a new ending where the Boerewors Man actually gets away, and explain how.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Hayley's family cook and share food together at the braai, right at the start of the story. How does sharing a meal together show love within a family or community?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">So many different people and animals — a fruit seller, a taxi driver, a hippo — all know something helpful about their part of the chase. What does this remind us about how everyone around us has different knowledge worth listening to?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">The cart driver, the taxi driver and the hippo all offer to help total strangers join the chase, without being asked twice. What does it look like to serve others by simply saying yes when we're able to help?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">This whole silly, growing chase started from just one small sausage jumping off a plate! Small, unexpected events can have big, funny (or serious) impacts. What's a small choice that could set off a chain of events this week?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a light, funny modern-day South African setting (braai, taxi, nature reserve), giving a change of pace after Weeks 3–5.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 7 Content Pack — “Sisanda's Gift” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — set on a South African game reserve, with a strong compassion and community thread connecting to earlier weeks' kindness stories.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a narrative about compassion and healing — by working out new vocabulary, reading for meaning, finding evidence of a character's kindness, and showing our understanding through talk, writing and presenting.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Can reading or talking to someone — or even an animal — help them feel better? Have you ever tried comforting someone or something who was sad?</li><li>Have you ever visited a game reserve or seen giraffes up close? What are they like?</li><li>Look at the title. What do you predict Sisanda's 'gift' might be?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">game reserve</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large area of protected land where wild animals live and are cared for</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">calf</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a baby giraffe (or other large animal, like a cow or elephant)</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">swarm</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large group of insects, like bees, moving or attacking together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">grief / comfort</td><td class=\"lt-td\">grief is deep sadness, often after a loss; comfort means helping someone feel better</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">reserve manager</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the person in charge of running a game reserve</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">gift</td><td class=\"lt-td\">something given to someone, often to help or please them</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">rejoice</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to feel or show great joy or happiness</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does Sisanda want to become when she grows up?</li><li>What happened to the mother giraffe?</li><li>What was Sisanda's idea to help the baby giraffe?</li><li>What did Sisanda do every time she visited the baby giraffe?</li><li>What name did Sisanda give the giraffe, and what does it mean?</li><li>What happened when the reserve manager invited Sisanda's whole class to meet the giraffe?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking Sisanda's visits.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing what happens from the bee stings to the class visit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evidence of Sisanda's kindness and the giraffe's recovery.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that Sisanda is kind and caring, and 2 that show the giraffe is getting stronger.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — why reading helped the giraffe.</li><li>Explain in your own words why you think reading to the giraffe helped him feel better, even though he couldn't understand the words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Sisanda</strong> — One partner is Sisanda, the other a school newspaper reporter. Ask about the giraffe, her idea, and the day her whole class visited — then swap.</li><li><strong>Would This Work? Discussion</strong> — Pairs discuss other ways people might comfort a grieving or frightened animal, and whether they think Sisanda's idea was a good one.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write the story Sisanda read to Thokozani — create a short original story a young giraffe might enjoy.</li><li>Design a thank-you card from Thokozani to Sisanda, including a message showing how the giraffe feels.</li><li>Persuasive mini-speech — &quot;Why it's important to care about a cause&quot; — using Sisanda's care for animals as your example, plus one cause you care about.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Sisanda felt the baby giraffe's grief so strongly that she cried for him, then found a way to help. How can noticing when someone (or something) is hurting lead us to show love in a practical way?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Sisanda's parents didn't understand at first why reading to a giraffe would help — but they trusted her idea. What does it mean to know when to trust someone's instincts, even if we don't fully understand yet?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Sisanda gave up her afternoons, again and again, to visit and read to Thokozani. What does it look like to serve someone consistently, over weeks and months, rather than just once?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One little girl's simple idea — reading a story out loud — helped heal a grieving baby giraffe and inspired a whole class, a game reserve, and even a newspaper. How could a simple act of kindness we do have a bigger impact than we expect?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — set on a South African game reserve, with a strong compassion and community thread connecting to earlier weeks' kindness stories.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 8 Content Pack — “Wolf's Supper” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a bushveld animal fable with a built-in moral, setting up the pourquoi-tale pattern that continues into Week 9.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a fable with a moral — by working out new vocabulary, finding evidence of a character's overconfidence, and using the text's own discussion questions to connect the story to our own lives.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Do you know any stories where a trickster ends up getting tricked themselves, or where 'the tables turn'? What happened?</li><li>Have you ever been overconfident about something, and it didn't go the way you expected?</li><li>Look at the title. What do you predict Wolf's supper is going to be — and do you think he'll actually get it?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">bushveld</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a wild area of grassland and scattered trees, found in parts of Southern Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">prowl</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to move around quietly and secretly while hunting</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">prey</td><td class=\"lt-td\">an animal that is hunted by another animal for food</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">springbok</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a type of small, fast antelope found in Southern Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">crafty</td><td class=\"lt-td\">clever in a sly or cunning way</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">pounce</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to jump on something suddenly, in order to catch it</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">predicament</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a difficult, confusing or embarrassing situation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What was Wolf's plan for the day?</li><li>Why didn't Wolf eat the field mouse or the turtle dove?</li><li>What happened at the exact moment Wolf pounced on the springbok?</li><li>What did Wolf actually swallow instead of the springbok?</li><li>How did the animals react when they saw what happened to Wolf?</li><li>What lesson do you think Wolf learned by the end of the story?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking Wolf's day.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing Wolf's plan, from waking up to the moment he pounces.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evidence of Wolf's overconfidence before his mistake.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that Wolf is overconfident, and 1 piece of evidence for what actually goes wrong.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — the story's moral, and connecting it to real life.</li><li>Explain in your own words the lesson Wolf learns. Then describe a time you (or someone you know) learned a similar lesson.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Wolf</strong> — One partner is Wolf after the incident, the other a bushveld news reporter. Ask about his plan, the bee sting, and what he'll do differently next time — then swap.</li><li><strong>Would You Feel Sorry for Wolf? Debate</strong> — Using the story's own discussion questions, pairs discuss whether they feel sorry for Wolf, and whether they'd feel differently if he'd caught the springbok.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evaluating a character) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a &quot;Wolf's diary&quot; entry for that night, describing what went wrong and how he really feels.</li><li>Design a &quot;Wanted&quot; poster for Wolf, listing his crafty tricks and warning other animals to watch out.</li><li>Write your own short trickster fable, with a different animal and a clear lesson at the end.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Even after Wolf's embarrassing mistake, the other animals don't hurt him — they simply laugh and let him go. What does it look like to respond to someone else's mistake with humour and grace, rather than cruelty?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wolf thought he knew exactly what he was doing, but his overconfidence made him careless. What's the difference between true confidence and the kind of overconfidence that leads to mistakes?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Wolf spent the whole story only thinking about serving himself a good meal. What might have been different if Wolf had spent his day helping others instead of hunting only for himself?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One moment of carelessness — closing his eyes at just the wrong time — turned Wolf's whole day upside down. How can staying alert and careful, even in small moments, protect us from bigger mistakes?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — a bushveld animal fable with a built-in moral, setting up the pourquoi-tale pattern that continues into Week 9.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 9 Content Pack — “Why Crocodile Lives in the River” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fiction (traditional/folk tale) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — the second animal fable in a row, closing out the fiction stories before the term's non-fiction biography finale in Week 10.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a pourquoi tale — by working out new vocabulary, tracking a character's changing feelings, and using the text's own discussion questions to think critically about asking for help and managing anger.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Do you know any 'why' stories that explain something about nature, like why the sky is blue, or why a certain animal looks or acts the way it does? What happens in it?</li><li>Have you ever been too embarrassed to ask for help, and tried to solve a problem alone instead? What happened?</li><li>Look at the title. What do you predict this story will explain about crocodiles?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">pourquoi tale</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a traditional story that explains why something in nature is the way it is (from the French word for 'why')</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">forest</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a large area of land covered in trees, home to many animals in this story</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">bathe</td><td class=\"lt-td\">to wash oneself, often in water like a river</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">cautiously</td><td class=\"lt-td\">carefully, in a way that avoids risk or danger</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">miserable</td><td class=\"lt-td\">extremely unhappy</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">meeting place</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a spot where a group gathers to talk and make decisions together</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why did the animals decide to have a party?</li><li>Why did the animals say Crocodile needed to bathe in the river?</li><li>Why was Crocodile too embarrassed to ask the other animals for help?</li><li>Who secretly watched Crocodile learn to swim at night?</li><li>What did the animals find when they finally went looking for Crocodile?</li><li>What does Crocodile threaten at the end of the story, and why does he live in the river to this day?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Literal comprehension</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the story together, tracking Crocodile's feelings.</li><li>Sequence 5 picture/sentence cards showing what happens from the party planning to Crocodile's threat.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evidence of Crocodile's changing feelings — from fear to anger.</li><li>Find 1 piece of evidence that Crocodile is afraid at the start, and 1 that shows he is angry by the end.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: inference — why pourquoi tales exist.</li><li>Explain in your own words why cultures around the world tell 'why' stories like this one. Can you think of another example from a different culture?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression • Fluency &amp; comprehension at instructional level • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Hot Seat: Interview Crocodile or Owl</strong> — One partner plays Crocodile, the other plays Owl. Ask about the secret night swim, and what Owl saw and didn't say — then swap.</li><li><strong>Should He Have Told a Friend? Discussion</strong> — Using the story's own discussion questions, pairs discuss whether it was a good idea for Crocodile to go to the river alone at night without telling anyone.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (inference, evidence-finding) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write your own pourquoi tale — explain, in a short story, why another animal looks or behaves a certain way.</li><li>Comic strip — retell Crocodile's secret night swim and Owl watching silently, in 6 panels.</li><li>Persuasive mini-speech — &quot;Why it's better to ask for help than to hide a problem&quot; — using evidence from the story.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — participating &amp; contributing, relating to others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Crocodile felt so alone with his problem that he tried to solve it in secret, at night. What does it look like to show love to a friend by making it safe for them to ask for help, instead of hiding a struggle?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Owl quietly knew Crocodile's secret the whole time, but chose not to embarrass him by telling everyone. What does it mean to know something about someone and to handle that knowledge with kindness?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">None of the animals stopped to ask Crocodile if he was okay, or offered to help him learn to swim. What could it have looked like for the animals to serve Crocodile well, instead of just giving him an order?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Crocodile's anger at the end has an impact that lasts 'to this day,' the story tells us — he still lives alone in the river. How can staying angry for a long time affect not just us, but the people (or animals!) around us, long into the future?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Structured literacy</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 Global perspectives (Southern Africa) — the second animal fable in a row, closing out the fiction stories before the term's non-fiction biography finale in Week 10.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 10 Content Pack — “Nelson Mandela: The Long Walk to Freedom” (Term 2, Southern Africa unit)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-fiction (biography) | <strong>Reading level:</strong> Year 5/6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Theme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This capstone week returns directly to the apartheid history introduced in Week 2, now told through one life in full depth — the term's most significant true story, handled with honesty and care.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NZC English, Phase 2 (Years 4–6)</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote class=\"lt-quote\"><strong>WALT (week):</strong> We are learning to understand a biography of a real historical figure — by working out new vocabulary, tracking key events in a life in chronological order, and thinking critically about the power of hope and forgiveness.</blockquote></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Lead-in questions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What do you already know about Nelson Mandela? Turn and share with your partner.</li><li>Why might forgiveness be one of the hardest — and most powerful — things a leader can choose to do?</li><li>This is a true story, or biography. What's different about how we read a true story compared to a made-up one?</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key vocabulary</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Word</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Kid-friendly meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">apartheid</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a system of laws in South Africa (1948–1994) that separated people by race and gave unfair power to white people</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">ANC (African National Congress)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a movement and political party that worked, and fought, to end apartheid in South Africa</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">activist</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a person who campaigns strongly for social or political change</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">imprisonment</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the state of being kept in prison</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Robben Island</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a small island off the coast of Cape Town, used as a prison where Mandela was held for 18 years</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">reconciliation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">bringing people back together and repairing relationships after conflict or wrongdoing</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Rainbow Nation</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a name for South Africa after 1994, celebrating its many different cultures and races living together</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">forgiveness</td><td class=\"lt-td\">choosing to let go of anger or blame towards someone who has wronged you</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">legacy</td><td class=\"lt-td\">the lasting impact a person leaves behind after their life or work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Basic comprehension (whole class check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>When and where was Nelson Mandela born, and what did his birth name, Rolihlahla, mean?</li><li>What is apartheid, and how did it affect Black South Africans?</li><li>Why was Mandela arrested and sent to prison, and for how many years was he there?</li><li>What did Mandela do to keep hope alive while in prison?</li><li>What did Mandela do as president to help heal a divided country?</li><li>What is Nelson Mandela International Day, and why is it linked to the number 67?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language • Vocabulary knowledge • Activating prior knowledge • Non-fiction text features (biography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟩 TUESDAY — Level Groups: Guided Reading</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group A — needs more support</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Re-read the biography together with teacher.</li><li>Sequence 6 picture/date cards showing key moments in Mandela's life, from his birth to his death.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group B — at level (teacher-led)</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: finding evidence of Mandela's hope and forgiveness.</li><li>Find 2 pieces of evidence that Mandela stayed hopeful in prison, and 2 that show he chose forgiveness afterwards.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Group C — extending</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Focus: evaluating why forgiveness mattered for South Africa's future.</li><li>Discuss and explain: why might forgiveness have been more powerful for South Africa's future than revenge would have been?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Reading progression — chronological/timeline features, cause and effect • Differentiated instruction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">🟨 WEDNESDAY — Pairs: Reading Activities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Timeline Sequencing</strong> — Cut up 8 key date/event cards from Mandela's life. Pairs order them chronologically and explain how they knew.</li><li><strong>Vocabulary Match-Up</strong> — Card game matching each vocab word to its kid-friendly meaning — fastest correct pair wins a point.</li><li><strong>Talk About It Discussion</strong> — Using the text's own 'Talk About It' questions, pairs discuss what helped Mandela stay hopeful, why forgiveness mattered, and what they could do for 67 minutes to help their community.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Comprehension strategies (evidence-finding, evaluating a real person's choices) • Collaborative talk</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">This week's project options (pairs choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Design a Nelson Mandela Day poster — plan your own 67-minute act of service for your local community, and present it persuasively.</li><li>Write a speech Mandela might give today, encouraging young people to build a fairer world.</li><li>Fact file / poster on Mandela's life — organised by sections: childhood, activism, prison, presidency, and legacy.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Thursday (build/create time)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~5 min — recap the story &amp; choose project format</li><li>~35 min — plan and build/draft</li><li>~5–10 min — quick teacher check-in per pair</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Friday (finish + present)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>~20–25 min — finish and polish the project</li><li>~20–25 min — presentations, 2–3 min per pair</li><li>Peer feedback: &quot;2 stars and a wish&quot; after each presentation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum tags</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Oral language — presenting ideas clearly • Key competencies — relating to others, thinking critically</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Christian Reflection — School Values</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Soli Deo Gloria — To God Alone the Glory</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Value</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Reflection question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Aroha</strong> (Love)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Mandela chose to speak about forgiveness and building a new South Africa together, rather than anger, after 27 years in prison. What does it look like to choose love over bitterness, even after being deeply wronged?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mōhio</strong> (Know)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Mandela read every book he could find in prison and even learned Afrikaans, the language of his guards, so he could understand and speak with them. What does this teach us about the value of seeking to understand people, even those who have treated us unfairly?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Manaaki</strong> (Serve)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Mandela gave 67 years of his life to public service, and Mandela Day asks people to give just 67 minutes to help their own community. What is one way you could serve your own community this week, even in a small way?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Pānga</strong> (Impact)</td><td class=\"lt-td\">One man's choice to keep working for freedom, whatever the cost, helped change an entire country's future. How can staying committed to doing what's right, even when it's hard and takes a long time, have an impact far beyond what we can see right now?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Weekly Curriculum Coverage Tick-List</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>✅ Reading progressions</li><li>✅ Non-fiction text features</li><li>✅ Oral language</li><li>✅ Key competencies</li><li>🌱 This capstone week returns directly to the apartheid history introduced in Week 2, now told through one life in full depth — the term's most significant true story, handled with honesty and care.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Boy: Tales of Childhood — Book Study (Roald Dahl)":{"label":"Week","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Book</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boy: Tales of Childhood (Roald Dahl)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Year level</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Years 5–6 (Ages 9–12) · NZC English Level 3–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10 weeks: Weeks 1–9 have four 45-minute reading lessons (Mon–Thu) and a Friday quiz; Week 10 is the final project and celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Weekly rhythm</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Monday teacher read · Tuesday group read · Wednesday teacher read · Thursday group read · Friday 20-question quiz</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum Alignment</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Term Overview</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Week 1:</b> Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</li><li><b>Week 2:</b> The Great Mouse Plot</li><li><b>Week 3:</b> A mother's courage &amp; Norway</li><li><b>Week 4:</b> Off to boarding school</li><li><b>Week 5:</b> Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</li><li><b>Week 6:</b> Captain Hardcastle</li><li><b>Week 7:</b> Growing older</li><li><b>Week 8:</b> Life at Repton</li><li><b>Week 9:</b> Finding your path</li><li><b>Week 10:</b> Final project &amp; celebration</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Whole-Programme Reading Rubric</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One rubric is used all term; every lesson and the final project point back to these criteria.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Plans, drafts and shares writing or project work with growing independence.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Needs significant prompting to start or complete writing or project tasks.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Completes tasks with growing independence and only occasional prompting.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Works independently, manages their own time, and can explain their choices or process.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 1: Family, home & the sweet-shop":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: Starting-point → Papa and Mama":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Starting-point → Papa and Mama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1: Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can understand how a memoir writer selects vivid, memorable details to stand in for whole years of a life.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why the author remembers almost nothing about his early childhood except a few vivid moments</li><li>I can identify an example of exaggeration (hyperbole) Dahl uses for comic effect.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ask a grandparent or older family member about something surprising or funny that happened to them when they were young. What's one thing you already know about your own family history?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Starting-point → Papa and Mama</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Roald Dahl's father lost his arm in a shocking accident as a teenager — what actually went wrong, and why couldn't doctors fix it properly in 1877?</li><li>What does his father's &quot;glorious walks&quot; theory (about beautiful scenery making unborn babies grow up loving beauty) tell us about the kind of person he was?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3 sentences about an object, skill, or belief that has been passed down in your own family, the way Dahl's father passed down his love of beautiful things.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who can explain why the arm-accident and &quot;glorious walks&quot; details were chosen, meaningful memories (R1) — and who question whether his father's parenting idea was fair or strange (R7).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front window display of Mrs Pratchett's sweet-shop, illustrating at least 5 sweets (Gobstoppers, Bootlaces, Sherbet Suckers, Pear Drops, Tonsil Ticklers) with your own made-up prices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Thwaites explaining how Liquorice Bootlaces are made, ending with the other boys' reactions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out young Roald and his friends peering through the sweet-shop window deciding what to buy with their sixpence — mime and dialogue only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as nine-year-old Roald on the day he first saw the boy backpedalling on his bike.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Kindergarten, 1922–3":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Kindergarten, 1922–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1: Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can understand how a memoir writer selects vivid, memorable details to stand in for whole years of a life.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why the author remembers almost nothing about his early childhood except a few vivid moments</li><li>I can identify an example of exaggeration (hyperbole) Dahl uses for comic effect.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is your very first memory? Why do you think we remember so little from when we were very small?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Kindergarten, 1922–3</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two tragedies hit the Dahl family within weeks of each other — what were they, and how did Roald's mother respond?</li><li>Why do you think the author remembers his tricycle rides so clearly but almost nothing else about his first year of school?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe the bravest thing you know that a member of your own family has ever done, the way Dahl's mother stayed and raised five children alone.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer why the mother's response shows courage, using evidence from the text (R3) — and who contribute their own ideas constructively to the group (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front window display of Mrs Pratchett's sweet-shop, illustrating at least 5 sweets (Gobstoppers, Bootlaces, Sherbet Suckers, Pear Drops, Tonsil Ticklers) with your own made-up prices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Thwaites explaining how Liquorice Bootlaces are made, ending with the other boys' reactions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out young Roald and his friends peering through the sweet-shop window deciding what to buy with their sixpence — mime and dialogue only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as nine-year-old Roald on the day he first saw the boy backpedalling on his bike.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1: Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can understand how a memoir writer selects vivid, memorable details to stand in for whole years of a life.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why the author remembers almost nothing about his early childhood except a few vivid moments</li><li>I can identify an example of exaggeration (hyperbole) Dahl uses for comic effect.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has a much older kid ever done something that seemed impossibly cool to you? What was it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why did the image of the older boy on his bicycle stay in Dahl's memory for sixty years?</li><li>What does this tell us about what nine-year-olds find important?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your own &quot;one glorious day I will…&quot; ambition — something you've watched someone else do that you desperately want to be able to do yourself.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain why the bicycle memory stuck for sixty years (R1) — and who write a clear, well-formed &quot;one glorious day&quot; go-away reflection (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front window display of Mrs Pratchett's sweet-shop, illustrating at least 5 sweets (Gobstoppers, Bootlaces, Sherbet Suckers, Pear Drops, Tonsil Ticklers) with your own made-up prices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Thwaites explaining how Liquorice Bootlaces are made, ending with the other boys' reactions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out young Roald and his friends peering through the sweet-shop window deciding what to buy with their sixpence — mime and dialogue only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as nine-year-old Roald on the day he first saw the boy backpedalling on his bike.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1: Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can understand how a memoir writer selects vivid, memorable details to stand in for whole years of a life.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why the author remembers almost nothing about his early childhood except a few vivid moments</li><li>I can identify an example of exaggeration (hyperbole) Dahl uses for comic effect.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the most disgusting food rumour you've ever heard from a friend at school? Did you believe it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The bicycle and the sweet-shop (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why did Thwaites's story about Liquorice Bootlaces work on the boys even though it was clearly ridiculous?</li><li>Find two details Dahl uses to make Mrs Pratchett seem thoroughly unpleasant.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Invent your own outrageous (and clearly false) explanation for how a sweet or snack is made, in the style of Thwaites.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who identify Dahl's exaggerated, hyperbolic details about Mrs Pratchett (R2) — and who use similarly vivid, exaggerated language in their own invented sweet-story (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front window display of Mrs Pratchett's sweet-shop, illustrating at least 5 sweets (Gobstoppers, Bootlaces, Sherbet Suckers, Pear Drops, Tonsil Ticklers) with your own made-up prices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Thwaites explaining how Liquorice Bootlaces are made, ending with the other boys' reactions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out young Roald and his friends peering through the sweet-shop window deciding what to buy with their sixpence — mime and dialogue only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as nine-year-old Roald on the day he first saw the boy backpedalling on his bike.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 1 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 1: Family, home &amp; the sweet-shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What country was Roald Dahl's father, Harald Dahl, originally from? a) Sweden · b) Denmark · c) Wales · <b>d) Norway ✔</b></li><li>2. How did Harald Dahl lose his left arm? a) He lost it in a factory accident · b) He lost it in a war · c) He was born without it · <b>d) He broke it falling off a roof as a teenager and it was later amputated ✔</b></li><li>3. What business did Harald Dahl set up in Cardiff? a) A bakery · b) A newspaper · c) A sweet-shop · <b>d) A shipbroking firm ✔</b></li><li>4. What was Harald Dahl's theory about &quot;the glorious walks&quot;? a) Walking every day keeps you healthy · b) Walks bring good luck to a family · c) Walks help babies sleep better · <b>d) Beautiful scenery would make an unborn baby grow up loving beauty ✔</b></li><li>5. What two family tragedies happened within weeks of each other when Roald was three? <b>a) His sister died, then his father died ✔</b> · b) Two siblings emigrated to Norway · c) His father lost his job, then his home · d) His mother and father both fell ill</li><li>6. What did Roald's mother decide to do instead of returning to Norway with her children? a) Send the children to relatives · <b>b) Stay and raise them in Wales/England ✔</b> · c) Move back to Norway immediately · d) Remarry quickly</li><li>7. What was the name of the kindergarten Roald attended? a) Sweetshop School · <b>b) Elmtree House ✔</b> · c) Llandaff House · d) Cathedral Kindergarten</li><li>8. What form of transport does Roald remember most vividly from kindergarten? a) His bicycle · <b>b) His tricycle ✔</b> · c) A rowing boat · d) A pony</li><li>9. What school did Roald attend from age 7 to 9? a) Elmtree House · b) St David's College · <b>c) Llandaff Cathedral School ✔</b> · d) Repton School</li><li>10. What amazing thing did the older boy on the bicycle do that impressed young Roald so much? a) He rode backwards down a hill · b) He jumped over a wall · c) He rode standing on the seat · <b>d) He backpedalled at speed with no hands on the handlebars ✔</b></li><li>11. Who was Thwaites, and why did the other boys trust his opinions? <b>a) A friend whose father was a doctor, so he claimed medical authority ✔</b> · b) The oldest boy in the school · c) A teacher's son · d) Mrs Pratchett's nephew</li><li>12. According to Thwaites, what were Liquorice Bootlaces really made from? a) Seaweed · b) Coal · c) Old boots · <b>d) Rats ✔</b></li><li>13. What disease did Thwaites claim you would catch from eating Liquorice Bootlaces? a) &quot;Lacitis&quot; · b) &quot;Bootitis&quot; · c) &quot;Black tongue&quot; · <b>d) &quot;Ratitis&quot; ✔</b></li><li>14. According to Thwaites, why did Gobstoppers change colour as you sucked them? a) They melt in sunlight · b) They are painted in layers · c) It is magic · <b>d) &quot;It's your spit that does it&quot; ✔</b></li><li>15. What did Thwaites claim was inside Tonsil Ticklers, and why? <b>a) Chloroform, supposedly to keep dangerous prisoners quiet ✔</b> · b) Pepper, to make you sneeze · c) Itching powder · d) Glue, to keep your mouth shut</li><li>16. What was the name of the woman who ran the sweet-shop? a) Mrs Dahl · b) Mrs Coombes · c) Mrs Thwaites · <b>d) Mrs Pratchett ✔</b></li><li>17. Name two things Dahl says made Mrs Pratchett so unpleasant. <em>Model answer: Accept any two: her filthy hands, her rudeness, her meanness with the number of sweets in a bag, her sour expression.</em></li><li>18. Inference: Why do you think the boys kept believing Thwaites's outrageous stories even though they didn't stop buying the sweets? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: the stories were entertaining/exciting, or being a doctor's son made him seem believable.</em></li><li>19. Vocabulary: What does it mean that Mrs Pratchett was &quot;loathsome&quot;? a) Very quiet and shy · <b>b) Very unpleasant or hateful ✔</b> · c) Very kind and gentle · d) Very old-fashioned</li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, why do you think Dahl remembers so few things from before he was eight or nine, but remembers this sweet-shop scene in perfect detail? <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for understanding that vivid, exciting or emotional moments stick in memory more than ordinary ones.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: The Great Mouse Plot":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explain how tension and humour build together in a personal narrative, and track cause and effect through a chain of events.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can retell, in order, the chain of events from finding the mouse to the identity parade</li><li>I can explain how Thwaites's scaremongering builds suspense.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you or a friend ever come up with a &quot;brilliant&quot; plan that felt genius at the time? How did it turn out?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The Great Mouse Plot</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Whose idea was the Mouse Plot, and how does Dahl want us to feel about him for owning up to it as an adult?</li><li>Why does Dahl insist &quot;one must strive to be truthful… Truth is more important than modesty&quot; right before describing his own plan?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3 sentences describing a moment when you (or someone you know) felt like &quot;a hero&quot; for pulling off a plan, even a naughty one.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain why Dahl insists on truth before confessing his own plan (R1) — and who notice the humour and exaggeration in how he tells it (R2).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;WANTED&quot; poster for Mrs Pratchett's shop, listing (humorously) the &quot;crimes&quot; the boys accused her of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic showing the Mouse Plot from start (finding the mouse) to the moment the boys realise the shop is closed the next morning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the playground identity parade in groups, with one student as Mr Coombes, one as Mrs Pratchett, and the rest as nervous boys in line — freeze-frame the moment she points.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter of apology from adult Roald Dahl to Mrs Pratchett, looking back on the Mouse Plot years later — is he really sorry?</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Mr Coombes (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mr Coombes (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explain how tension and humour build together in a personal narrative, and track cause and effect through a chain of events.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can retell, in order, the chain of events from finding the mouse to the identity parade</li><li>I can explain how Thwaites's scaremongering builds suspense.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Imagine you did something wrong and then something unexpected and scary happened afterwards that seemed connected. How would you feel?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Mr Coombes (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What do the boys see through the sweet-shop window that alarms them?</li><li>Why does Thwaites's claim about heart attacks terrify young Roald so completely?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a time you worried you'd caused something far worse than you actually had.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer why Thwaites's heart-attack claim terrifies Roald, using evidence from the text (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;WANTED&quot; poster for Mrs Pratchett's shop, listing (humorously) the &quot;crimes&quot; the boys accused her of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic showing the Mouse Plot from start (finding the mouse) to the moment the boys realise the shop is closed the next morning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the playground identity parade in groups, with one student as Mr Coombes, one as Mrs Pratchett, and the rest as nervous boys in line — freeze-frame the moment she points.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter of apology from adult Roald Dahl to Mrs Pratchett, looking back on the Mouse Plot years later — is he really sorry?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: Mr Coombes (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mr Coombes (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explain how tension and humour build together in a personal narrative, and track cause and effect through a chain of events.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can retell, in order, the chain of events from finding the mouse to the identity parade</li><li>I can explain how Thwaites's scaremongering builds suspense.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you think it would feel to be picked out of a line-up of one hundred people?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Mr Coombes (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe how Mr Coombes organises the &quot;identity parade.</li><li>Why is the boys' relief that Mrs Pratchett is alive followed immediately by fresh dread?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the playground scene from Mrs Pratchett's point of view as she walks the lines looking for &quot;them nasty little ones.&quot;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who infer why relief turns straight into fresh dread (R3) — and who use vivid detail and voice in their Mrs Pratchett's-point-of-view scene (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;WANTED&quot; poster for Mrs Pratchett's shop, listing (humorously) the &quot;crimes&quot; the boys accused her of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic showing the Mouse Plot from start (finding the mouse) to the moment the boys realise the shop is closed the next morning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the playground identity parade in groups, with one student as Mr Coombes, one as Mrs Pratchett, and the rest as nervous boys in line — freeze-frame the moment she points.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter of apology from adult Roald Dahl to Mrs Pratchett, looking back on the Mouse Plot years later — is he really sorry?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explain how tension and humour build together in a personal narrative, and track cause and effect through a chain of events.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can retell, in order, the chain of events from finding the mouse to the identity parade</li><li>I can explain how Thwaites's scaremongering builds suspense.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why do you think adults in the past sometimes believed punishing children in front of others taught a stronger lesson?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How does Dahl describe Mr Coombes and the cane to make the scene feel frightening even though he doesn't describe the punishment itself in graphic detail?</li><li>Why is Mrs Pratchett's presence in the room, cheering the Headmaster on, especially shocking to the boys?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without describing violence, write 3–4 sentences about how you imagine Roald felt walking back to class afterwards.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who form a reasoned opinion about whether public punishment was fair (R7) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;WANTED&quot; poster for Mrs Pratchett's shop, listing (humorously) the &quot;crimes&quot; the boys accused her of.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic showing the Mouse Plot from start (finding the mouse) to the moment the boys realise the shop is closed the next morning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the playground identity parade in groups, with one student as Mr Coombes, one as Mrs Pratchett, and the rest as nervous boys in line — freeze-frame the moment she points.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter of apology from adult Roald Dahl to Mrs Pratchett, looking back on the Mouse Plot years later — is he really sorry?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 2 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 2: The Great Mouse Plot</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Where did the boys find the dead mouse? a) Inside the sweet-shop · b) In the school playground · c) In Mr Coombes's study · <b>d) Under a loose floorboard in their hiding place ✔</b></li><li>2. Whose idea was it to put the mouse in a sweet jar? a) Nobody's — it was an accident · b) A younger boy's idea · <b>c) Roald's own idea ✔</b> · d) Thwaites's idea</li><li>3. Which jar did they decide to put the mouse in, and why? <em>Model answer: The Gobstopper jar, because it was out on the counter and used often.</em></li><li>4. What job did Thwaites do while the mouse was being hidden in the jar? <b>a) He distracted Mrs Pratchett by buying sweets ✔</b> · b) He kept watch outside the shop · c) He carried the mouse in his pocket · d) He paid for the whole jar</li><li>5. What did the boys find when they arrived at the shop the next morning? <b>a) The shop was closed, with a notice on the door ✔</b> · b) The shop was open as normal · c) Mrs Pratchett was waiting outside · d) The shop had been boarded up</li><li>6. What could the boys see through the window? a) Mrs Pratchett lying on the floor · b) Nothing — the windows were covered · c) Police searching the shop · <b>d) The smashed jar, gobstoppers everywhere and the dead mouse ✔</b></li><li>7. According to Thwaites, what might happen to an old person who gets &quot;a very big shock&quot;? a) They would go deaf · b) Nothing at all · <b>c) A heart attack, and death within seconds ✔</b> · d) They would catch a cold</li><li>8. Who did Thwaites blame for &quot;killing&quot; Mrs Pratchett? a) Mr Coombes · b) Thwaites himself · <b>c) Roald, because it was his idea ✔</b> · d) The whole class</li><li>9. Who was the Headmaster of Llandaff Cathedral School? a) Captain Hardcastle · b) Mr Thwaites · <b>c) Mr Coombes ✔</b> · d) Mr Pratchett</li><li>10. What order did Mr Coombes give the whole school that morning? a) To go straight to prayers · <b>b) To line up around the playground in silence ✔</b> · c) To stay in their classrooms · d) To empty their pockets</li><li>11. What turned out to be true about Mrs Pratchett, to the boys' great relief? a) The mouse had disappeared · b) Nobody had noticed the mouse · <b>c) She was alive and had come to the school ✔</b> · d) The shop had reopened</li><li>12. What was Mr Coombes and Mrs Pratchett doing as they walked down the lines of boys? <em>Model answer: Inspecting/looking for the boys who played the trick, like an identity parade.</em></li><li>13. Who did Mrs Pratchett identify first? <b>a) Thwaites ✔</b> · b) Mr Coombes · c) A boy who had nothing to do with it · d) Roald</li><li>14. How many boys in total were identified and punished? <b>a) Five ✔</b> · b) Three · c) Ten · d) Seven</li><li>15. What object did Mr Coombes hold when the five boys were called to his study? a) A wooden ruler · b) A rolled-up newspaper · <b>c) A long yellow cane ✔</b> · d) A leather strap</li><li>16. Who watched and cheered during the punishment in the study? <b>a) Mrs Pratchett ✔</b> · b) The whole school · c) Roald's mother · d) Nanny</li><li>17. Who discovered what had happened to Roald later that evening? a) His sister, at dinner · b) Nanny, at bedtime · <b>c) His mother, at bathtime ✔</b> · d) His teacher, the next day</li><li>18. What did Roald's mother do that same evening after finding out? a) She sent Roald to bed early · b) She wrote a polite letter · c) She said nothing · <b>d) She marched out of the house to confront Mr Coombes ✔</b></li><li>19. What did Mr Coombes tell Roald's mother when she complained? <em>Model answer: That she was &quot;a foreigner&quot; and didn't understand how British schools were run.</em></li><li>20. Inference/short answer: In your own words, why do you think Roald's mother's response mattered so much to the story, even though it didn't change what had already happened? <em>Model answer: Open answer, mark for understanding that it shows her courage and her different (Norwegian) values about how children should be treated, and leads to the decision to send him to boarding school in England.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3: A mother's courage & Norway":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3: A mother's courage &amp; Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare our own lives to a time and place very different from our own, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe what the Dahl family's summer holidays in Norway were like</li><li>I can explain why Dahl calls Tjöme &quot;the magic island.&quot;</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has an adult ever stood up for you when you felt you couldn't stand up for yourself? What happened?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Mrs Pratchett's revenge (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How does Roald's mother react when she discovers what happened, compared to how Mr Coombes expects a parent to react?</li><li>What big decision does she make about Roald's education because of this?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a time an adult's actions made you feel protected or proud of them.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who compare the mother's reaction with Mr Coombes's expectations and question who was being fair (R7) — and who explain her decision about schooling (R1).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a travel poster advertising a summer holiday to Tjöme, using details from the text (the fjord, the islands, the breakfast, the fishing).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Illustrate the Dahl family's four-day journey from Wales to Norway as a comic-strip map with 6 key stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out a &quot;skaal&quot; toasting dinner scene at Bestemama and Bestepapa's house, including at least one formal toast.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a postcard home from young Roald describing a day on the magic island.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Going to Norway":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Going to Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3: A mother's courage &amp; Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare our own lives to a time and place very different from our own, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe what the Dahl family's summer holidays in Norway were like</li><li>I can explain why Dahl calls Tjöme &quot;the magic island.&quot;</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the longest journey you've ever taken to get somewhere for a holiday? What made it feel like an adventure?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Going to Norway</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List the different stages of the Dahl family's journey to Norway.</li><li>Why do you think Dahl describes the &quot;skaal&quot; toasting ceremony and the enormous feast in such loving detail?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a family tradition of your own (a food, a game, a saying) that an outsider might find strange but that you love.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who explain why Dahl describes the journey and feast so lovingly (R1) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a travel poster advertising a summer holiday to Tjöme, using details from the text (the fjord, the islands, the breakfast, the fishing).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Illustrate the Dahl family's four-day journey from Wales to Norway as a comic-strip map with 6 key stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out a &quot;skaal&quot; toasting dinner scene at Bestemama and Bestepapa's house, including at least one formal toast.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a postcard home from young Roald describing a day on the magic island.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: The magic island (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The magic island (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3: A mother's courage &amp; Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare our own lives to a time and place very different from our own, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe what the Dahl family's summer holidays in Norway were like</li><li>I can explain why Dahl calls Tjöme &quot;the magic island.&quot;</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What would your idea of the &quot;perfect&quot; holiday spot look like?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The magic island (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What details does Dahl use to make Tjöme sound magical despite the very basic hotel and outdoor toilets?</li><li>Why might very primitive conditions not spoil a holiday for a child?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design (in words) your own &quot;dream hotel breakfast table,&quot; the way Dahl describes the fifty dishes on offer.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who identify the details that make Tjöme magical (R2) — and who use similarly vivid detail in their own dream-breakfast writing (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a travel poster advertising a summer holiday to Tjöme, using details from the text (the fjord, the islands, the breakfast, the fishing).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Illustrate the Dahl family's four-day journey from Wales to Norway as a comic-strip map with 6 key stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out a &quot;skaal&quot; toasting dinner scene at Bestemama and Bestepapa's house, including at least one formal toast.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a postcard home from young Roald describing a day on the magic island.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: The magic island (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The magic island (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3: A mother's courage &amp; Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare our own lives to a time and place very different from our own, using evidence from the text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe what the Dahl family's summer holidays in Norway were like</li><li>I can explain why Dahl calls Tjöme &quot;the magic island.&quot;</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Do you think children today are given as much freedom and responsibility as the Dahl children had in their boats? Why or why not?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The magic island (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compare the row-boat and the motor-boat sections — what changes as the children get older?</li><li>What does Dahl's mother's calm skill in rough seas tell us about her character?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3–4 sentences about the most adventurous or free you have ever felt outdoors.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer the mother's character from her calm skill in rough seas (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a travel poster advertising a summer holiday to Tjöme, using details from the text (the fjord, the islands, the breakfast, the fishing).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Illustrate the Dahl family's four-day journey from Wales to Norway as a comic-strip map with 6 key stops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out a &quot;skaal&quot; toasting dinner scene at Bestemama and Bestepapa's house, including at least one formal toast.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a postcard home from young Roald describing a day on the magic island.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 3 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 3: A mother's courage &amp; Norway</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What decision did Roald's mother make about his schooling by the end of &quot;Mrs Pratchett's revenge&quot;? a) To move the family back to Norway · b) To keep him at Llandaff Cathedral School · c) To teach him at home · <b>d) To send him to an English boarding school ✔</b></li><li>2. What did Mr Coombes accuse Roald's mother of not understanding? a) How to raise a family · <b>b) How British schools are run — he called her &quot;a foreigner&quot; ✔</b> · c) How to pay school fees · d) The rules of cricket</li><li>3. Where did the Dahl family spend every summer holiday from 1920 to 1932? a) Wales · <b>b) Norway ✔</b> · c) Denmark · d) Scotland</li><li>4. Roughly how long did the journey to Norway take? a) One day · <b>b) About four days each way ✔</b> · c) A week · d) Two days</li><li>5. Who in the travelling party got seasick on every crossing? a) Only Nanny · b) Only the children · <b>c) Everyone except the mother ✔</b> · d) Nobody</li><li>6. What were the names of Roald's Norwegian grandparents? <b>a) Bestemama and Bestepapa ✔</b> · b) Bestemor and Bestefar · c) Mormor and Morfar · d) Oma and Opa</li><li>7. What was the centrepiece of the grand welcome feast in Oslo? a) A roast reindeer · b) A whole roast pig · c) A giant pie · <b>d) A huge poached flounder ✔</b></li><li>8. What was special about the ice-cream served at the feast? a) Pieces of gingerbread · b) Chunks of chocolate · c) Fresh strawberries · <b>d) Crunchy bits of burnt toffee ✔</b></li><li>9. What is a &quot;skaal&quot;? <em>Model answer: A formal Norwegian toasting ceremony where you raise your glass and look someone in the eye.</em></li><li>10. What was the name of the island the family stayed on for their Norwegian holiday? <b>a) Tjöme ✔</b> · b) Oslo · c) Bergen · d) Tromsø</li><li>11. What were the toilets like at the hotel on Tjöme? a) Buckets in the bedrooms · b) Modern flush toilets · <b>c) A wooden outhouse with a hole cut in a plank ✔</b> · d) A shared toilet in the lobby</li><li>12. What did Nanny keep saying she was afraid the boat would do? a) Catch fire · b) Hit a rock · <b>c) Sink ✔</b> · d) Drift away</li><li>13. What kind of boat did the family use in the early years, and who rowed it? a) A canoe paddled by Nanny · b) A motor-boat driven by his father · <b>c) A row-boat, rowed by the mother and older half-brother ✔</b> · d) A sailing boat sailed by Roald</li><li>14. What replaced the row-boat in later years? a) A steam ferry · b) A big sailing yacht · <b>c) A small motor-boat with an unreliable engine ✔</b> · d) A fishing trawler</li><li>15. What did the family use as bait for evening fishing? a) Pieces of bread · <b>b) Mussels from the rocks ✔</b> · c) Leftover flounder · d) Worms from the garden</li><li>16. Inference: Why do you think Dahl describes his mother as &quot;enjoying herself most&quot; when the sea got rough? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: she was confident, adventurous, and skilled at handling the boat.</em></li><li>17. Inference: Why might the very basic conditions at the hotel not have bothered the children at all? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: children focus on fun and adventure, not comfort; it was normal to them.</em></li><li>18. Vocabulary: What does Dahl mean when he describes the island as having &quot;absolute peace and beauty&quot;? a) A noisy, busy holiday town · b) A dark and frightening place · c) A place with lots of shops · <b>d) Complete calm and natural loveliness ✔</b></li><li>19. True or False: The Dahl children learned to swim early because there were no sandy beaches, only deep water off the rocks. <b>a) True ✔</b> · b) False</li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, explain why Dahl calls this chapter &quot;The magic island.&quot; <em>Model answer: Open answer, mark for reference to the freedom, natural beauty, and happy memories described.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4: Off to boarding school":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: A visit to the doctor":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A visit to the doctor</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4: Off to boarding school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can infer a character's feelings from their actions and the details a memoir writer chooses to include.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how young Roald felt on his first day at St Peter's, using evidence from the text</li><li>I can explain the purpose of the tuck-box and the letter-writing rule.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has a visit to the doctor or dentist ever turned out to be much more uncomfortable than you expected?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): A visit to the doctor</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What operation does eight-year-old Roald undergo, and how does the author make clear it was done without anaesthetic?</li><li>What does his grandmother's comment at the end suggest about what she has learned in her long life?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a time you had to be braver than you expected to be during a medical or dental visit.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who infer what the grandmother's comment reveals about her long experience of life (R3) — and who write a clear, personal go-away reflection about their own bravery (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the label/contents chart for your own imaginary tuck-box, listing at least 6 items you'd want sent from home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the letter-writing scene, showing the Headmaster catching a &quot;mistake&quot; and the boy's true feelings in a thought bubble he can't say out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment young Roald is left alone on the driveway on his first day, showing his feelings without words (mime), then act out putting on a brave face for the Headmaster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a truthful letter home from Roald that he would never have dared to actually send.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: First day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">First day</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4: Off to boarding school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can infer a character's feelings from their actions and the details a memoir writer chooses to include.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how young Roald felt on his first day at St Peter's, using evidence from the text</li><li>I can explain the purpose of the tuck-box and the letter-writing rule.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Imagine arriving somewhere completely new where you don't know a single person and will be staying for weeks. What would worry you most?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): First day</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is a tuck-box, and why did English boarding schools of the time rely on parents to send food parcels?</li><li>How does Dahl describe the Headmaster to make him seem intimidating from the very first handshake?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry as nine-year-old Roald on the evening of his very first day at St Peter's.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer why the Headmaster's description feels intimidating from the very first handshake (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the label/contents chart for your own imaginary tuck-box, listing at least 6 items you'd want sent from home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the letter-writing scene, showing the Headmaster catching a &quot;mistake&quot; and the boy's true feelings in a thought bubble he can't say out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment young Roald is left alone on the driveway on his first day, showing his feelings without words (mime), then act out putting on a brave face for the Headmaster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a truthful letter home from Roald that he would never have dared to actually send.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: Writing home":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4: Off to boarding school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can infer a character's feelings from their actions and the details a memoir writer chooses to include.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how young Roald felt on his first day at St Peter's, using evidence from the text</li><li>I can explain the purpose of the tuck-box and the letter-writing rule.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you could only write to your family once a week and knew a teacher was reading every word before it was sent, what would you choose to say?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Writing home</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why weren't the boys allowed to complain about the school in their letters home?</li><li>How did the Headmaster cleverly stop the boys from correcting their spelling mistakes, and why did this help hide the fact that letters were censored?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the opening two sentences of a &quot;perfectly cheerful&quot; letter home that secretly hides how you're really feeling — then explain to a partner what you really meant.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain how the Headmaster's letter-editing trick hid the censorship (R2) — and who use a convincingly cheerful 'voice' that hides a different true feeling in their own letter opening (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the label/contents chart for your own imaginary tuck-box, listing at least 6 items you'd want sent from home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the letter-writing scene, showing the Headmaster catching a &quot;mistake&quot; and the boy's true feelings in a thought bubble he can't say out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment young Roald is left alone on the driveway on his first day, showing his feelings without words (mime), then act out putting on a brave face for the Headmaster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a truthful letter home from Roald that he would never have dared to actually send.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: The Matron (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Matron (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4: Off to boarding school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can infer a character's feelings from their actions and the details a memoir writer chooses to include.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how young Roald felt on his first day at St Peter's, using evidence from the text</li><li>I can explain the purpose of the tuck-box and the letter-writing rule.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What's the strictest rule you've ever had to follow, and what happened if someone broke it?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The Matron (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What makes the Matron such a frightening figure to the boys?</li><li>How does the whole school end up being punished for one boy's prank, and how does Arkle's loyalty to his pet frog show his character?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a time the whole class or team was punished for what one person did. Was it fair?</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who form a reasoned opinion about whether whole-school punishment for one boy's prank was fair (R7) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the label/contents chart for your own imaginary tuck-box, listing at least 6 items you'd want sent from home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the letter-writing scene, showing the Headmaster catching a &quot;mistake&quot; and the boy's true feelings in a thought bubble he can't say out loud.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment young Roald is left alone on the driveway on his first day, showing his feelings without words (mime), then act out putting on a brave face for the Headmaster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a truthful letter home from Roald that he would never have dared to actually send.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 4 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 4: Off to boarding school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What was removed from young Roald's mouth by the doctor in Norway? <b>a) His adenoids ✔</b> · b) His tonsils · c) A tooth · d) His wisdom teeth</li><li>2. Was Roald given anaesthetic for this operation? <b>a) No ✔</b> · b) Yes · c) Only a local anaesthetic · d) He refused the operation</li><li>3. What did Roald's grandmother say to him after the operation? <em>Model answer: That it won't be the last time he goes to a doctor in his life, and hopefully they won't do him too much harm.</em></li><li>4. What was the name of Roald's first boarding school? <b>a) St Peter's, in Weston-super-Mare ✔</b> · b) Repton School · c) Llandaff Cathedral School · d) Marlborough College</li><li>5. How did Roald and his mother travel from Cardiff to Weston-super-Mare? <b>a) By paddle-steamer across the Bristol Channel ✔</b> · b) By train · c) By motor-car · d) By aeroplane</li><li>6. What is a tuck-box? <em>Model answer: A small locked wooden trunk for storing food and treasures sent from home; private to its owner.</em></li><li>7. Name two things typically found inside a tuck-box. <em>Model answer: Accept any two: cake, biscuits, fruit, chocolate, sweets, or small treasures like a penknife, magnet, or toy.</em></li><li>8. What unusual pet did a boy called Arkle keep inside his tuck-box? <b>a) A frog, fed on slugs ✔</b> · b) A mouse · c) A lizard · d) A baby bird</li><li>9. What memorable detail does Dahl give about the Headmaster's appearance when they first meet? <b>a) His gold-edged front tooth and glistening, creamed hair ✔</b> · b) His enormous beard · c) His missing eye · d) His very short height</li><li>10. What instruction does the Headmaster give Roald on his very first minute at the school? <b>a) To go and report to the Matron ✔</b> · b) To unpack his tuck-box · c) To write to his mother · d) To find his dormitory</li><li>11. When was letter-writing time at St Peter's? <b>a) Sunday morning, from nine o'clock for one hour ✔</b> · b) Friday evening · c) Every night before bed · d) Saturday afternoon</li><li>12. Why couldn't boys complain about the school in their letters home? <b>a) The Headmaster patrolled and read every letter as it was written ✔</b> · b) There was no paper available · c) Letters were never actually sent · d) Parents wouldn't have believed them</li><li>13. How did the Headmaster stop boys from correcting mistakes in a way parents might notice? <em>Model answer: He made them leave the mistake in the letter and write corrections out separately as a punishment, so the letter looked unedited.</em></li><li>14. Who ruled supreme over the dormitory floor? <b>a) The Matron ✔</b> · b) The Headmaster · c) Captain Hardcastle · d) The Boazers</li><li>15. What did Roald accidentally walk in on the Matron doing in the Sick Room? <b>a) Kissing/embracing the Latin master, Mr Corrado ✔</b> · b) Eating the boys' tuck-box treats · c) Reading the boys' private letters · d) Sleeping on the job</li><li>16. What prank did the boy Wragg play after &quot;lights out&quot;? <b>a) He sprinkled castor sugar all over the corridor floor ✔</b> · b) He hid the Matron's keys · c) He set off a stink bomb · d) He swapped everyone's shoes</li><li>17. How was the whole school punished after the sugar prank? <b>a) Everyone had to hand in their tuck-box keys, and parcels were confiscated ✔</b> · b) Everyone lost a day of holidays · c) Everyone had extra homework · d) Everyone had to write lines</li><li>18. Inference: Why do you think none of the boys &quot;owned up&quot; to the sugar prank even though the whole school was punished? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: loyalty to Wragg, fear of an even worse individual punishment, boy-code of honour.</em></li><li>19. Vocabulary: What does Dahl mean when he calls the Matron a &quot;female ogre&quot; who &quot;ruled with a rod of steel&quot;? <b>a) That she was extremely strict, frightening and had total control ✔</b> · b) That she was kind but firm · c) That she was very old-fashioned · d) That she loved iron and steel objects</li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, explain why Dahl kept writing to his mother every week for over thirty years, even as an adult. <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for reference to their close bond and the letter-writing habit that began at St Peter's.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5: Homesickness & the motor-car":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: The Matron (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Matron (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5: Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explore how people cope with difficult emotions, and evaluate the choices characters make in a text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the trick young Roald used to try to get sent home, and evaluate whether it was a wise choice</li><li>I can describe how travel in the 1920s differed from today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What's the strangest or funniest rule you've heard about from a much older relative's schooldays?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The Matron (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How does Roald cope with his first, most homesick night at St Peter's — what small routine does he create for himself?</li><li>Why do you think Dahl includes such an odd and specific detail (facing the direction of home every night) in his memoir?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a small routine or object that has ever helped you feel less homesick, nervous, or alone.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who infer why Dahl includes the odd, specific detail of facing home every night (R3) — and who write a clear personal reflection about their own comforting routine (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a road-safety poster comparing motor-car travel in 1925 with today, using at least 2 facts from the chapter (no driving test, no seatbelts, glass windscreens that shattered).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the family's disastrous first car ride, from setting off to arriving at Dr Dunbar's.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the roadside argument between Roald's mother and the man with the &quot;thousand fresh-laid heggs,&quot; showing two very different priorities in an emergency.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write Dr Dunbar's private notes after seeing &quot;patient&quot; Roald — what does he really think is going on?</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Homesickness":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Homesickness</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5: Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explore how people cope with difficult emotions, and evaluate the choices characters make in a text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the trick young Roald used to try to get sent home, and evaluate whether it was a wise choice</li><li>I can describe how travel in the 1920s differed from today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever pretended to feel unwell to get out of something? Did it work — and how did you feel about it afterwards?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Homesickness</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What plan does Roald come up with to be sent home, and what earlier event gave him the idea?</li><li>How does Dr Dunbar work out that Roald is faking, and what does he say the real problem is?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about what you would say to a homesick friend instead of pretending to be ill, based on Dr Dunbar's advice to &quot;stick it out.&quot;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who explain how Dr Dunbar worked out the trick and identifies the real problem (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a road-safety poster comparing motor-car travel in 1925 with today, using at least 2 facts from the chapter (no driving test, no seatbelts, glass windscreens that shattered).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the family's disastrous first car ride, from setting off to arriving at Dr Dunbar's.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the roadside argument between Roald's mother and the man with the &quot;thousand fresh-laid heggs,&quot; showing two very different priorities in an emergency.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write Dr Dunbar's private notes after seeing &quot;patient&quot; Roald — what does he really think is going on?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: A drive in the motor-car (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A drive in the motor-car (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5: Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explore how people cope with difficult emotions, and evaluate the choices characters make in a text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the trick young Roald used to try to get sent home, and evaluate whether it was a wise choice</li><li>I can describe how travel in the 1920s differed from today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is the most exciting new gadget or vehicle your family has ever owned?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): A drive in the motor-car (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How does Dahl build up the excitement of the family's first car ride before everything goes wrong?</li><li>What mistakes does the driver (the &quot;ancient half-sister&quot;) make, and why might nobody have stopped her?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3–4 sentences describing the moment just before something exciting suddenly goes wrong — from any experience of your own (a bike, a sports game, a trick gone wrong).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain why nobody stopped the half-sister from driving faster and faster (R1) — and who write a clear, well-paced 'before it went wrong' reflection (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a road-safety poster comparing motor-car travel in 1925 with today, using at least 2 facts from the chapter (no driving test, no seatbelts, glass windscreens that shattered).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the family's disastrous first car ride, from setting off to arriving at Dr Dunbar's.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the roadside argument between Roald's mother and the man with the &quot;thousand fresh-laid heggs,&quot; showing two very different priorities in an emergency.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write Dr Dunbar's private notes after seeing &quot;patient&quot; Roald — what does he really think is going on?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: A drive in the motor-car (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A drive in the motor-car (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5: Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can explore how people cope with difficult emotions, and evaluate the choices characters make in a text.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the trick young Roald used to try to get sent home, and evaluate whether it was a wise choice</li><li>I can describe how travel in the 1920s differed from today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>How do you think you would react if you needed urgent help but someone in your way was more worried about their own problem?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): A drive in the motor-car (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What was strange or frustrating about the man with the horse and cart?</li><li>How does Roald's mother react in this emergency, and what does this reveal about her character?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a time an adult in your life stayed calm and took charge during an emergency or scary moment.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer what the mother's calm reaction reveals about her character (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a road-safety poster comparing motor-car travel in 1925 with today, using at least 2 facts from the chapter (no driving test, no seatbelts, glass windscreens that shattered).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the family's disastrous first car ride, from setting off to arriving at Dr Dunbar's.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the roadside argument between Roald's mother and the man with the &quot;thousand fresh-laid heggs,&quot; showing two very different priorities in an emergency.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write Dr Dunbar's private notes after seeing &quot;patient&quot; Roald — what does he really think is going on?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 5 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 5: Homesickness &amp; the motor-car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. How did Roald work out which direction his home was in from his dormitory bed? <b>a) Using the Bristol Channel as a guide, since Llandaff lay across it ✔</b> · b) By using a compass · c) By listening for church bells · d) He couldn't work it out at all</li><li>2. What punishment did the Matron give to a boy called Tweedie for snoring? <b>a) She dropped shaved soap-flakes into his open mouth ✔</b> · b) She made him sleep in the corridor · c) She took away his tuck-box · d) She made him stand up all night</li><li>3. What illness did Roald pretend to have in order to be sent home? <b>a) Appendicitis ✔</b> · b) Chickenpox · c) A broken leg · d) Measles</li><li>4. What earlier real event gave Roald the idea for his fake illness? <em>Model answer: Watching his half-sister's real appendix operation at home shortly before.</em></li><li>5. According to Nanny, what causes appendicitis? <b>a) Swallowed toothbrush bristles ✔</b> · b) Eating too many sweets · c) Catching a cold · d) Not enough sleep</li><li>6. Who initially believed Roald's fake symptoms and sent him home? <b>a) The Matron and the school doctor ✔</b> · b) Only the Headmaster · c) Nobody believed him · d) His classmates</li><li>7. Who saw through Roald's trick, and how? <em>Model answer: Dr Dunbar; because Roald's stomach was soft, not hard/rigid as it would be with real appendicitis.</em></li><li>8. What did Dr Dunbar say Roald was really suffering from? <b>a) Homesickness ✔</b> · b) Overeating · c) Exhaustion · d) Nothing at all</li><li>9. What &quot;reward&quot; did Dr Dunbar still give Roald despite catching him faking? <b>a) Three extra days at home, with a cover story about a stomach infection ✔</b> · b) A stern lecture and nothing else · c) Extra chores at school · d) A note home to his mother about lying</li><li>10. What was the name of the family's first ever motor-car? <b>a) A De Dion-Bouton ✔</b> · b) A Ford Model T · c) A Rolls-Royce · d) A Morris Minor</li><li>11. Who was allowed to drive the new car despite having had only two half-hour lessons? <b>a) The &quot;ancient half-sister&quot; (Roald's much older half-sister) ✔</b> · b) Roald's mother · c) Roald himself · d) A hired chauffeur</li><li>12. What speed were they doing when the car crashed? <b>a) About thirty-five miles an hour ✔</b> · b) About ten miles an hour · c) About sixty miles an hour · d) It had already stopped</li><li>13. What happened to the car when it hit the bend? <b>a) It skidded and crashed into a hedge ✔</b> · b) It rolled over completely · c) It ran out of petrol · d) Nothing — it stopped safely</li><li>14. What serious injury did Roald suffer in the crash? <em>Model answer: His nose was almost completely cut off, hanging on by a thread of skin.</em></li><li>15. Who did the family meet on the road who was more worried about his own goods than the emergency? <b>a) A man with a horse and cart carrying a thousand eggs (&quot;heggs&quot;) ✔</b> · b) A policeman · c) Another driver · d) A farmer with sheep</li><li>16. Who treated Roald's injury, and where? <b>a) Dr Dunbar, who sewed the nose back on at the family's own house ✔</b> · b) A hospital surgeon, in an operating theatre · c) The Matron, at school · d) Nobody — it healed by itself</li><li>17. What was used to put Roald to sleep for the operation? <b>a) Chloroform/ether on a mask ✔</b> · b) An injection · c) Nothing at all · d) Strong tea and biscuits</li><li>18. What did Roald find under his pillow when he woke up? <b>a) A gold sovereign coin, as a reward for being brave ✔</b> · b) A letter from his mother · c) A new toy · d) Nothing at all</li><li>19. Inference: Why do you think nobody in the family stopped the half-sister from driving faster and faster before the crash? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: excitement/thrill, trust in her, no seatbelt laws or safety culture at the time, or that the story is told with humour and hindsight.</em></li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, compare Roald's fake illness with his real injury in this week's chapters — what is different about how each is treated by the adults around him? <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for insight that the fake illness is seen through and gently corrected, while the real injury is treated seriously and with genuine care and reward for bravery.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6: Captain Hardcastle":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: Captain Hardcastle (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Hardcastle (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6: Captain Hardcastle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can examine how a memoir writer builds a vivid, frightening character through physical description and dialogue, and reflect on how attitudes to school discipline have changed over time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how Dahl builds Captain Hardcastle into a frightening character</li><li>I can discuss, respectfully, how school discipline in the 1920s differed from school today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Think of a strict rule at your own school. What is it there to prevent, and is it fair?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Captain Hardcastle (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List three physical details Dahl uses to describe Captain Hardcastle, and explain how each one makes him seem more alarming.</li><li>What does Dahl mean when he explains that Captain Hardcastle's title was &quot;the bottoms&quot; as army ranks go — why might that detail matter to how the man behaves?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe a teacher, coach, or leader (real or invented) using three vivid physical or behavioural details, the way Dahl describes Captain Hardcastle.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who identify the physical details Dahl uses to build a frightening character (R2) — and who use three similarly vivid details in their own description (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;Wanted&quot; or character profile poster for Captain Hardcastle, using only descriptive details from the text (no images of punishment).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the broken-nib misunderstanding, from Roald whispering to Dobson through to Captain Hardcastle's accusation — stop the strip before any punishment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the Braithwaite/wasp-sting dialogue as a two-person script, focusing on comic timing and frustration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from Highton's point of view, explaining why he decided to write to his own father about what happened to Roald.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Teacher read: Captain Hardcastle (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Hardcastle (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6: Captain Hardcastle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can examine how a memoir writer builds a vivid, frightening character through physical description and dialogue, and reflect on how attitudes to school discipline have changed over time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how Dahl builds Captain Hardcastle into a frightening character</li><li>I can discuss, respectfully, how school discipline in the 1920s differed from school today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever been in trouble for something completely reasonable, just because of bad timing or bad luck?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Captain Hardcastle (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why is poor Braithwaite unable to get help for his wasp sting?</li><li>What small, ordinary accident starts the chain of events that gets Roald into serious trouble?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write your own short &quot;unfair conversation&quot; in script form, where a character tries to explain something reasonable and is misunderstood by an authority figure, like the Braithwaite dialogue.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain why Braithwaite can't get help for his wasp sting (R3) — and who write convincing misunderstood dialogue in script form (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;Wanted&quot; or character profile poster for Captain Hardcastle, using only descriptive details from the text (no images of punishment).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the broken-nib misunderstanding, from Roald whispering to Dobson through to Captain Hardcastle's accusation — stop the strip before any punishment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the Braithwaite/wasp-sting dialogue as a two-person script, focusing on comic timing and frustration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from Highton's point of view, explaining why he decided to write to his own father about what happened to Roald.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Group read: Captain Hardcastle (Part 3)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Hardcastle (Part 3)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6: Captain Hardcastle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can examine how a memoir writer builds a vivid, frightening character through physical description and dialogue, and reflect on how attitudes to school discipline have changed over time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how Dahl builds Captain Hardcastle into a frightening character</li><li>I can discuss, respectfully, how school discipline in the 1920s differed from school today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has an adult ever refused to believe your side of a story, even when you were telling the truth? How did that feel?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Captain Hardcastle (Part 3)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What does Roald actually say to Dobson, and how does Captain Hardcastle misinterpret it?</li><li>Why does the Headmaster automatically believe Captain Hardcastle over Roald — what phrase does he use to explain this?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3–4 sentences about a time you felt you weren't given a fair chance to explain yourself.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who question whether the Headmaster's &quot;officer and a gentleman&quot; reasoning was fair (R7) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;Wanted&quot; or character profile poster for Captain Hardcastle, using only descriptive details from the text (no images of punishment).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the broken-nib misunderstanding, from Roald whispering to Dobson through to Captain Hardcastle's accusation — stop the strip before any punishment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the Braithwaite/wasp-sting dialogue as a two-person script, focusing on comic timing and frustration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from Highton's point of view, explaining why he decided to write to his own father about what happened to Roald.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: Captain Hardcastle (Part 4)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Hardcastle (Part 4)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6: Captain Hardcastle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can examine how a memoir writer builds a vivid, frightening character through physical description and dialogue, and reflect on how attitudes to school discipline have changed over time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how Dahl builds Captain Hardcastle into a frightening character</li><li>I can discuss, respectfully, how school discipline in the 1920s differed from school today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What would you do if you saw a friend treated unfairly and you wanted to help, but you weren't sure you actually could?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Captain Hardcastle (Part 4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Without describing the punishment itself, explain why Dahl notices that the Common Room door was left open — what does he suspect, and why does it upset him?</li><li>Why is Highton's gesture described as &quot;touching and generous&quot; even though &quot;nothing came of it&quot;?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a small act of loyalty or kindness a friend has shown you when you were in trouble, even if it didn't change the outcome.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer why the open Common Room door upset Roald (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a &quot;Wanted&quot; or character profile poster for Captain Hardcastle, using only descriptive details from the text (no images of punishment).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the broken-nib misunderstanding, from Roald whispering to Dobson through to Captain Hardcastle's accusation — stop the strip before any punishment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the Braithwaite/wasp-sting dialogue as a two-person script, focusing on comic timing and frustration.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from Highton's point of view, explaining why he decided to write to his own father about what happened to Roald.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 6 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 6: Captain Hardcastle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What was unusual about the colour of Captain Hardcastle's hair and moustache? <b>a) Bright orange/vermilion ✔</b> · b) Jet black · c) Pure white · d) Bright green</li><li>2. What job/subject did Captain Hardcastle teach? <b>a) Latin ✔</b> · b) Mathematics · c) History · d) Art</li><li>3. What did the boys believe caused Captain Hardcastle's constant twitching and grunting? <b>a) Shell-shock, from serving as a soldier in the First World War ✔</b> · b) A childhood illness · c) A nervous personality · d) Nobody knew</li><li>4. According to Dahl, why was &quot;Captain&quot; not actually a very impressive rank to still be using? <em>Model answer: It was one of the lowest officer ranks, so keeping the title suggested he had little else to boast about.</em></li><li>5. What were the two strict rules of Prep? <b>a) No talking, and no looking up from your work ✔</b> · b) No eating, and no standing up · c) No whispering, and no leaving your seat without asking · d) No pens, and no borrowing</li><li>6. In the Braithwaite dialogue, what had actually happened to Braithwaite? <b>a) A wasp flew in and stung him on the lip, which was swelling up ✔</b> · b) He had a toothache · c) He had bitten his tongue · d) He had a nosebleed</li><li>7. Why couldn't Braithwaite make the master understand his problem? <em>Model answer: His swollen lip made him hard to understand, and the master kept misunderstanding/mocking him instead of listening.</em></li><li>8. What accident started Roald's trouble during Prep? <b>a) He broke the nib of his pen ✔</b> · b) He spilled ink on his desk · c) He fell asleep · d) He lost his exercise book</li><li>9. What was Roald's essay about that night? <b>a) &quot;The Life Story of a Penny&quot; ✔</b> · b) &quot;My Summer Holiday&quot; · c) &quot;A Day at School&quot; · d) &quot;The History of Rome&quot;</li><li>10. Who did Roald whisper to, and what did he actually ask for? <em>Model answer: Dobson; he asked to borrow a spare pen nib.</em></li><li>11. What did Captain Hardcastle accuse Roald of doing? <b>a) Talking, cheating, and lying ✔</b> · b) Falling asleep · c) Breaking a rule about ink · d) Stealing</li><li>12. What is a &quot;Stripe&quot; at St Peter's? <em>Model answer: A punishment slip for very poor work or bad behaviour, which automatically led to a caning from the Headmaster.</em></li><li>13. What phrase did the Headmaster use to explain why he believed Captain Hardcastle over Roald? <b>a) That Captain Hardcastle was &quot;an officer and a gentleman&quot; ✔</b> · b) That Captain Hardcastle had never lied before · c) That Roald had a history of lying · d) That two masters had seen it happen</li><li>14. What did Roald suggest the Headmaster do to check his story, and why did the Headmaster refuse? <em>Model answer: Ask Dobson; the Headmaster refused because he wouldn't go around asking &quot;some silly little boy&quot; to check on a master's word.</em></li><li>15. How many strokes of the cane was Roald given? <b>a) Six ✔</b> · b) Three · c) Ten · d) One</li><li>16. What did Roald notice about the Common Room door as he left the study? <b>a) It had been left open ✔</b> · b) It was locked · c) It was painted a different colour · d) It had a new sign on it</li><li>17. Who did Roald suspect had opened the door on purpose, and why? <em>Model answer: Captain Hardcastle, so the other masters could hear the caning.</em></li><li>18. Which classmate offered to write to his own father in Greece about the unfairness? <b>a) Highton ✔</b> · b) Dobson · c) Braithwaite · d) Thwaites</li><li>19. Inference: Why do you think Dahl chose to describe this whole event in such detail as an adult writing his memoir, decades later? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: it clearly affected him deeply, he still felt it was deeply unjust, or he wanted readers to understand what boarding-school discipline was really like.</em></li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, explain why the Headmaster's reasoning (&quot;Captain Hardcastle is an officer and a gentleman&quot;) was unfair to Roald. <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for understanding that adults were automatically believed over children regardless of the actual truth.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7: Growing older":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: Little Ellis and the boil":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Little Ellis and the boil</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7: Growing older</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can identify how a writer uses humour to deal with topics that could otherwise be upsetting or embarrassing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain what makes the &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; story funny</li><li>I can describe how Dahl felt about the medical treatment he received as a child.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has a doctor, dentist, or nurse ever done something to help you that hurt more than you expected, or surprised you?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Little Ellis and the boil</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What trick does the doctor use on little Ellis, and why does it work?</li><li>How does Dahl, looking back as an adult, feel about how children were treated medically in the 1920s?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a time you had to trust an adult even though what they were about to do seemed scary.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain how Dahl, as an adult narrator, feels about 1920s medical treatment of children (R3) — and who write a clear personal go-away reflection about trusting an adult (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an instruction diagram for getting dressed in Roald's full Repton uniform, labelling each strange item (butterfly collar, studs, tailcoat, straw hat).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the goat's-tobacco prank, from Roald swapping the tobacco to the &quot;manly lover's&quot; dramatic reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment the doctor tricks Little Ellis with the towel — focus on comic timing and Ellis's reaction, no real props needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as the &quot;manly lover,&quot; describing what he thinks happened to him after his pipe (without revealing he ever finds out the truth — or write the moment he does!).</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Goat's tobacco":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Goat's tobacco</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7: Growing older</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can identify how a writer uses humour to deal with topics that could otherwise be upsetting or embarrassing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain what makes the &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; story funny</li><li>I can describe how Dahl felt about the medical treatment he received as a child.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has an older sibling or family member's boyfriend/girlfriend ever annoyed you by taking their attention away from your family? What did you do about it (nothing dangerous, hopefully)?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Goat's tobacco</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why does the whole family resent the &quot;manly lover&quot; before the prank even happens?</li><li>Explain, step by step, how young Roald prepares the prank, and why the whole family becomes part of &quot;a silent, rather dangerous family conspiracy.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe (without anyone getting hurt) the funniest harmless prank you have ever seen, done, or heard about.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who explain how Dahl builds the prank step by step for comic effect (R2) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an instruction diagram for getting dressed in Roald's full Repton uniform, labelling each strange item (butterfly collar, studs, tailcoat, straw hat).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the goat's-tobacco prank, from Roald swapping the tobacco to the &quot;manly lover's&quot; dramatic reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment the doctor tricks Little Ellis with the towel — focus on comic timing and Ellis's reaction, no real props needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as the &quot;manly lover,&quot; describing what he thinks happened to him after his pipe (without revealing he ever finds out the truth — or write the moment he does!).</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: Getting dressed for the big school":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Getting dressed for the big school</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7: Growing older</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can identify how a writer uses humour to deal with topics that could otherwise be upsetting or embarrassing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain what makes the &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; story funny</li><li>I can describe how Dahl felt about the medical treatment he received as a child.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Has getting ready for a big, important day (a wedding, a formal event, a first day somewhere) ever turned into its own comic disaster?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Getting dressed for the big school</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List the different pieces of Roald's Repton uniform, and explain why he found the &quot;butterfly collar&quot; so difficult to put on.</li><li>What does his mother mean when she says &quot;England is a country where men love to wear uniforms and eccentric clothes&quot;?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design and describe (in words) the silliest possible uniform you can imagine for your own school, in the style of Dahl's Repton outfit.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain his mother's comment about England's love of uniforms and eccentric clothes (R2) — and who use vivid, comic detail in their own invented-uniform description (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an instruction diagram for getting dressed in Roald's full Repton uniform, labelling each strange item (butterfly collar, studs, tailcoat, straw hat).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the goat's-tobacco prank, from Roald swapping the tobacco to the &quot;manly lover's&quot; dramatic reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment the doctor tricks Little Ellis with the towel — focus on comic timing and Ellis's reaction, no real props needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as the &quot;manly lover,&quot; describing what he thinks happened to him after his pipe (without revealing he ever finds out the truth — or write the moment he does!).</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: Boazers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boazers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7: Growing older</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can identify how a writer uses humour to deal with topics that could otherwise be upsetting or embarrassing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain what makes the &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; story funny</li><li>I can describe how Dahl felt about the medical treatment he received as a child.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Do older students at your school have any special privileges or responsibilities? Do you think that system is fair?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Boazers</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Who were the &quot;Boazers&quot; at Repton, and what powers did they have over younger boys?</li><li>What does the strange post-beating &quot;inspection ritual&quot; among the boys suggest about how normal this violence had become to them?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without describing any violence, write 3–4 sentences giving your own opinion on whether older students should ever be allowed to discipline younger ones, and why.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who form a reasoned opinion about the fairness of the Boazer system (R7) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an instruction diagram for getting dressed in Roald's full Repton uniform, labelling each strange item (butterfly collar, studs, tailcoat, straw hat).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of the goat's-tobacco prank, from Roald swapping the tobacco to the &quot;manly lover's&quot; dramatic reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform the moment the doctor tricks Little Ellis with the towel — focus on comic timing and Ellis's reaction, no real props needed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry as the &quot;manly lover,&quot; describing what he thinks happened to him after his pipe (without revealing he ever finds out the truth — or write the moment he does!).</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 7 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 7: Growing older</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What medical problem did the boy Ellis have? <b>a) A large boil on his thigh ✔</b> · b) A broken arm · c) A bad cough · d) A sprained ankle</li><li>2. What trick did the doctor use to distract Ellis before treating the boil? <b>a) He threw a towel over Ellis's face so he couldn't see what was coming ✔</b> · b) He told Ellis a funny story · c) He gave Ellis a sweet to suck on · d) He asked Ellis to count backwards</li><li>3. How does Dahl, as an adult narrator, feel about how the doctor treated Ellis? <em>Model answer: He questions/is critical of it, noting readers today wouldn't accept a doctor doing that.</em></li><li>4. Who was the &quot;manly lover&quot; in the &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; chapter? <b>a) The young English doctor engaged to marry Roald's ancient half-sister ✔</b> · b) Roald's older half-brother · c) A family friend visiting from Norway · d) The school Headmaster</li><li>5. Why did the family resent the manly lover at first? <em>Model answer: He kept taking their half-sister away to spend time alone with him, breaking up the family's usual togetherness.</em></li><li>6. What did the manly lover smoke, and what brand of tobacco did he prefer? <b>a) A pipe; Player's Navy Cut ✔</b> · b) A cigar; a Cuban brand · c) Cigarettes; a French brand · d) A pipe; a Norwegian brand</li><li>7. What did young Roald replace the tobacco with? <b>a) Shredded dried goat's droppings ✔</b> · b) Dried grass · c) Tea leaves · d) Crushed leaves from the garden</li><li>8. Who gave away the prank, and how? <b>a) The seven-year-old sister, by blurting out &quot;You've been smoking goat's tobacco!&quot; ✔</b> · b) Roald himself confessed · c) The mother found the evidence · d) Nobody ever found out</li><li>9. How old was Roald when he started at Repton? <b>a) Thirteen ✔</b> · b) Nine · c) Sixteen · d) Eleven</li><li>10. Which two schools did Roald's mother let him choose between? <b>a) Marlborough and Repton ✔</b> · b) Eton and Harrow · c) St Peter's and Repton · d) Marlborough and Eton</li><li>11. Why did Roald choose Repton over Marlborough? <b>a) He thought the word was easier to say ✔</b> · b) It was closer to home · c) His friends were going there · d) It had a better reputation for sport</li><li>12. What was the &quot;butterfly collar,&quot; and why was it so hard to attach? <em>Model answer: A very stiff detachable collar; it was so starchy and rigid that the studs wouldn't push through the slits easily.</em></li><li>13. What trick did Roald use to soften the collar so he could attach it? <b>a) He sucked/chewed on it to soften the starch with spit ✔</b> · b) He soaked it in water overnight · c) He ironed it flat · d) He asked Matron to soften it for him</li><li>14. What piece of clothing did Dahl compare to something an &quot;undertaker's apprentice&quot; would wear? <b>a) The black tail-coat ✔</b> · b) The straw hat · c) The waistcoat · d) The striped trousers</li><li>15. What were Repton's prefects called? <b>a) Boazers ✔</b> · b) Fags · c) Praepostors · d) Monitors</li><li>16. What could a Boazer punish a junior boy (a Fag) for? <em>Model answer: Small, minor mistakes, such as leaving a sock on the floor or burning toast (accept any reasonable example).</em></li><li>17. Which Boazer was famous for delivering his strokes extremely fast, without pausing? <b>a) Williamson ✔</b> · b) Highton · c) Dobson · d) Braithwaite</li><li>18. What ritual took place in the dormitory after a boy was punished? <em>Model answer: Other boys inspected and commented on the bruising like &quot;experts.&quot;</em></li><li>19. Inference: Why do you think the other boys treated the after-punishment &quot;inspection&quot; so casually, almost like a competition? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: it had become completely normal to them; humour/bravado was a way of coping.</em></li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, compare how Dahl uses humour in &quot;Goat's tobacco&quot; with how he writes about &quot;Boazers.&quot; Why might one topic feel funny and the other feel more serious? <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for recognising that the prank is harmless fun while the Boazer system involves real violence Dahl clearly disapproved of.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8: Life at Repton":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: The Headmaster":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Headmaster</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8: Life at Repton</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare school rules and traditions across different times and places, and explain their purpose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe the &quot;fagging&quot; system at Repton</li><li>I can explain how a real memory from this book later inspired one of Dahl's most famous novels.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Can you think of an example of someone saying one thing but doing another (a &quot;do as I say, not as I do&quot; situation)?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): The Headmaster</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What surprising later career did the Repton Headmaster go on to have?</li><li>Why does his behaviour as Headmaster make Dahl begin to have doubts about religion?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3–4 sentences about a time you noticed a gap between what someone said they believed and how they actually behaved.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who infer what the Headmaster's hypocrisy reveals and how it affected Dahl's faith (R3) — and who write a clear personal reflection about noticing a gap between belief and behaviour (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front of a Cadbury &quot;mystery chocolate&quot; testing box like the one described in the chapter, including a rating chart from 0–10.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Corkers's classroom, showing him abandoning maths for the crossword or the folded-paper trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform Carleton's white-glove dust inspection scene, with one Boazer and three trembling Fags.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from adult Roald Dahl explaining how the Cadbury chocolate-testing memory turned into the idea for Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Chocolates":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chocolates</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8: Life at Repton</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare school rules and traditions across different times and places, and explain their purpose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe the &quot;fagging&quot; system at Repton</li><li>I can explain how a real memory from this book later inspired one of Dahl's most famous novels.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If a chocolate company sent you a box of 12 secret new chocolate bars to test and rate, what would you look for in a &quot;perfect&quot; bar?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Chocolates</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What exactly were the boys asked to do with the Cadbury boxes?</li><li>What later, very famous book did this experience directly inspire, according to Dahl?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Invent your own new chocolate bar (name, filling, and one review comment) as if you were one of the Repton chocolate-testers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who explain how a real childhood memory grew into a famous book, connecting ideas beyond the text (R1) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front of a Cadbury &quot;mystery chocolate&quot; testing box like the one described in the chapter, including a rating chart from 0–10.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Corkers's classroom, showing him abandoning maths for the crossword or the folded-paper trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform Carleton's white-glove dust inspection scene, with one Boazer and three trembling Fags.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from adult Roald Dahl explaining how the Cadbury chocolate-testing memory turned into the idea for Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: Corkers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Corkers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8: Life at Repton</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare school rules and traditions across different times and places, and explain their purpose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe the &quot;fagging&quot; system at Repton</li><li>I can explain how a real memory from this book later inspired one of Dahl's most famous novels.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe the most unusual or memorable lesson a teacher has ever given you.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Corkers</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In what ways did Corkers avoid actually teaching mathematics?</li><li>What was the answer to his famous &quot;folding paper fifty times&quot; question, and why did it amaze the class?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design your own &quot;Corkers-style&quot; unusual but fascinating question or activity that a teacher could use instead of a normal lesson.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain how Dahl builds Corkers into a comic character through his odd habits (R2) — and who use vivid, playful detail in their own invented Corkers-style activity (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front of a Cadbury &quot;mystery chocolate&quot; testing box like the one described in the chapter, including a rating chart from 0–10.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Corkers's classroom, showing him abandoning maths for the crossword or the folded-paper trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform Carleton's white-glove dust inspection scene, with one Boazer and three trembling Fags.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from adult Roald Dahl explaining how the Cadbury chocolate-testing memory turned into the idea for Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: Fagging":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fagging</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8: Life at Repton</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can compare school rules and traditions across different times and places, and explain their purpose.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe the &quot;fagging&quot; system at Repton</li><li>I can explain how a real memory from this book later inspired one of Dahl's most famous novels.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever had to do a chore or job for someone older or in charge, that felt unfair or over-the-top?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Fagging</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe Carleton's white-glove inspection ritual — why is it designed to be impossible for the Fags to pass?</li><li>What job did Wilberforce make Roald do, and how does Dahl use humour to describe something that was actually quite unpleasant?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without naming real people, describe (respectfully) an unfair &quot;unwritten rule&quot; you have noticed in a group, team, or school that gives some people power over others.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who form a reasoned opinion about the fairness of the fagging system (R7) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Key Competency — Thinking</b> — Asks questions and forms a reasoned opinion about fairness or authority in the text.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Accepts events in the text without questioning them.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Forms a stated opinion about fairness or authority and gives one reason for it.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Forms a well-reasoned opinion that weighs up more than one side or perspective.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front of a Cadbury &quot;mystery chocolate&quot; testing box like the one described in the chapter, including a rating chart from 0–10.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Corkers's classroom, showing him abandoning maths for the crossword or the folded-paper trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform Carleton's white-glove dust inspection scene, with one Boazer and three trembling Fags.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a diary entry from adult Roald Dahl explaining how the Cadbury chocolate-testing memory turned into the idea for Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 8 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 8: Life at Repton</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What senior religious position did the Repton Headmaster eventually rise to? <b>a) Archbishop of Canterbury ✔</b> · b) Bishop of London · c) Dean of Westminster · d) Cardinal of York</li><li>2. What famous event did this Headmaster later perform on television? <b>a) Crowning the Queen at Westminster Abbey ✔</b> · b) A royal wedding · c) A state funeral · d) The Queen's Christmas broadcast</li><li>3. What does Dahl say this Headmaster's behaviour made him begin to doubt? <b>a) Religion, or God/the Church ✔</b> · b) His own memory · c) The school's academic standards · d) His mother's judgement</li><li>4. How does Dahl describe his friend Michael's headmaster punishment story, in terms of what happened between each stroke of the cane? <em>Model answer: The Headmaster paused to light and fuss with his pipe and lecture about sin between each stroke.</em></li><li>5. What company sent grey cardboard boxes of chocolate bars to the boys? <b>a) Cadbury ✔</b> · b) Rowntree's · c) Fry's · d) Terry's</li><li>6. How many chocolate bars were in each testing box? <b>a) Twelve ✔</b> · b) Six · c) Ten · d) Twenty</li><li>7. What was the &quot;control&quot; bar usually included in each box? <b>a) A Cadbury's Coffee Cream bar ✔</b> · b) A Fruit and Nut bar · c) A Dairy Milk bar · d) A Turkish Delight bar</li><li>8. What were the boys asked to do with each chocolate bar? <em>Model answer: Taste it, give it marks out of ten, and write a comment.</em></li><li>9. What later, very famous children's book did Dahl say this chocolate-testing experience inspired? <b>a) Charlie and the Chocolate Factory ✔</b> · b) James and the Giant Peach · c) Matilda · d) The BFG</li><li>10. What subject was Corkers supposed to teach? <b>a) Mathematics ✔</b> · b) Latin · c) Geography · d) English</li><li>11. What did Corkers actually spend most lessons doing instead? <b>a) Crossword puzzles from the newspaper (and other distractions) ✔</b> · b) Telling war stories · c) Sleeping at his desk · d) Reading the newspaper aloud</li><li>12. In Corkers's famous question, what happens if you fold a piece of tissue-paper 50 times? <b>a) It would become as thick as the distance from the earth to the sun ✔</b> · b) It would fill the whole classroom · c) It would reach the moon · d) It would tear apart after a few folds</li><li>13. What unusual animal did Corkers once bring into class? <b>a) A grass-snake ✔</b> · b) A tortoise · c) A ferret · d) A hedgehog</li><li>14. What was Corkers's running joke that he repeated every few weeks? <em>Model answer: Pretending someone had &quot;farted&quot; and dramatically ordering the windows and doors opened.</em></li><li>15. What is &quot;fagging,&quot; as described at Repton? <em>Model answer: Junior boys (&quot;Fags&quot;) acting as personal servants for senior boys/prefects.</em></li><li>16. What object did Carleton use during his study inspections? <b>a) A white cotton glove ✔</b> · b) A magnifying glass · c) A wooden ruler · d) A torch</li><li>17. What job did the Boazer Wilberforce make Roald do in the freezing outhouse? <b>a) Warm up the toilet seat for him before he used it ✔</b> · b) Polish his shoes every morning · c) Carry his books between lessons · d) Light his fire each evening</li><li>18. What did Roald do to pass the time while performing this job? <em>Model answer: He read books (he says he read the entire works of Dickens this way).</em></li><li>19. Inference: Why do you think Wilberforce described choosing Fags with &quot;hot bottoms&quot; like a &quot;winetaster sampling an old claret&quot;? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: it shows how absurdly seriously/proudly he took a ridiculous, unfair privilege.</em></li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, explain how the fagging and Boazer system at Repton gave power to some students over others, and share your own opinion on whether such a system could ever be fair. <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for clear description plus a reasoned personal opinion.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9: Finding your path":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — Teacher read: Games and photography":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Games and photography</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9: Finding your path</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can trace how a person's childhood experiences shape their later choices, and reflect on our own hopes for the future.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why Dahl was good at some things at school and not others</li><li>I can describe the choices he made as he left school, and compare them with my own hopes after school.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What is something you're surprisingly good at that isn't necessarily the &quot;obvious&quot; thing people expect from you?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Games and photography</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What two games was Dahl unexpectedly excellent at, and what titles did he earn?</li><li>Why did the school authorities refuse to make him a Boazer despite his sporting success, and how does Dahl seem to feel about that, looking back?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write about a hobby, sport, or skill of your own that gives you a similar sense of pride and enjoyment to how Dahl describes fives and photography.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain why Dahl excelled at some things and not others, connecting evidence across the text (R1) — and who write a clear personal reflection about their own hobby or skill (R4).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a recruitment poster for the Shell Company (Eastern Staff) as it might have appeared in 1934, listing the &quot;adventure&quot; of working abroad.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Dahl's secret motorbike rides around Repton in disguise during his last term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the interview scene where Dahl tells the Shell Director he doesn't want to go to Egypt because it's &quot;too dusty,&quot; and the Director's reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a &quot;where are they now&quot; reflection imagining what nine-year-old Roald from Week 1 would think if he could see who he becomes by the end of the book.</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Group read: Goodbye school (Part 1)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Goodbye school (Part 1)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9: Finding your path</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can trace how a person's childhood experiences shape their later choices, and reflect on our own hopes for the future.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why Dahl was good at some things at school and not others</li><li>I can describe the choices he made as he left school, and compare them with my own hopes after school.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you could go straight from school into any job or adventure in the world, what would you choose and why?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Goodbye school (Part 1)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why did Dahl turn down the chance to go to Oxford or Cambridge?</li><li>Out of 107 applicants, how many places were there at Shell, and what does getting one suggest about him?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 3–4 sentences about a bold or unusual choice you would make about your own future, even if people around you expected something different.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer what turning down university and winning a place at Shell reveals about Dahl's character (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a recruitment poster for the Shell Company (Eastern Staff) as it might have appeared in 1934, listing the &quot;adventure&quot; of working abroad.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Dahl's secret motorbike rides around Repton in disguise during his last term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the interview scene where Dahl tells the Shell Director he doesn't want to go to Egypt because it's &quot;too dusty,&quot; and the Director's reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a &quot;where are they now&quot; reflection imagining what nine-year-old Roald from Week 1 would think if he could see who he becomes by the end of the book.</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Teacher read: Goodbye school (Part 2)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Goodbye school (Part 2)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9: Finding your path</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher reads aloud (slideshow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can trace how a person's childhood experiences shape their later choices, and reflect on our own hopes for the future.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why Dahl was good at some things at school and not others</li><li>I can describe the choices he made as he left school, and compare them with my own hopes after school.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe the most daring &quot;last hurrah&quot; you can imagine doing at the end of a big stage of your life.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Goodbye school (Part 2)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher reads the passage aloud from the book; show the chunk slides on screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What risk did Dahl secretly take throughout his last term at Repton, and why does he say he &quot;tremble[s] to think&quot; what would have happened if he'd been caught?</li><li>What was the Newfoundland expedition, and why did Dahl choose to go on it before starting work?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Describe your own idea of an exciting &quot;adventure before real life starts,&quot; even if it's completely imaginary.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tick off students who explain how Dahl builds suspense around the secret motorbike and the risk of being caught (R2) — and who use vivid detail in describing their own imagined adventure (R5).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Identifies how the memoir is shaped for its purpose, incl. hyperbole and comic exaggeration.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Notices that a passage is funny or dramatic, but not yet how the writing creates that effect.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Identifies a specific language feature (e.g. hyperbole, exaggeration) and says what effect it has.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Explains how a feature builds across a whole passage or chapter, using more than one example.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a recruitment poster for the Shell Company (Eastern Staff) as it might have appeared in 1934, listing the &quot;adventure&quot; of working abroad.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Dahl's secret motorbike rides around Repton in disguise during his last term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the interview scene where Dahl tells the Shell Director he doesn't want to go to Egypt because it's &quot;too dusty,&quot; and the Director's reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a &quot;where are they now&quot; reflection imagining what nine-year-old Roald from Week 1 would think if he could see who he becomes by the end of the book.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Group read: Goodbye school (Part 3)":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Goodbye school (Part 3)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9: Finding your path</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reading</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group reading (groups of 4, taking turns), with worksheet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (with Writing responses)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Level 3–4)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reading – Ideas: Show understanding of ideas within, across, and beyond texts.</li><li>Reading – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of how texts are shaped for different purposes and audiences.</li><li>Reading – Processes &amp; Strategies: Integrate sources of information, processes and strategies to identify, form and express ideas.</li><li>Writing – Ideas: Select, form and communicate ideas on a range of topics.</li><li>Writing – Structure &amp; Language: Show understanding of language features, using them appropriately.</li><li>Writing – Processes &amp; Strategies: Select and use processes and strategies to communicate confidently.</li><li>Key Competencies: Thinking, Managing Self, Relating to Others, Participating &amp; Contributing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can trace how a person's childhood experiences shape their later choices, and reflect on our own hopes for the future.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain why Dahl was good at some things at school and not others</li><li>I can describe the choices he made as he left school, and compare them with my own hopes after school.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Sequence (45 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Settle and lead-in questions — 5 min</li><li>Reading — 15–20 min</li><li>Comprehension and inference — 15 min</li><li>Go-away reflection (reading journal) — 5 min</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Lead-in questions (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Have you ever asked for something and been surprised (good or bad) by what you actually got instead?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading (15–20 minutes): Goodbye school (Part 3)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students read in groups of 4, taking turns, then complete the worksheet.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comprehension and inference (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Why did Dahl not want to be posted to Egypt, and what reason did he give?</li><li>How does his mother react when he tells her he'll be gone for three years in Africa, and what does this tell us about her, one final time?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Go-away reflection (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a farewell diary entry as Dahl's mother, watching her son sail away to Africa for three years, knowing she won't see him again for a long time.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric Checkpoint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During group discussion, tick off students who infer what the mother's reaction reveals about her character, one final time (R3) — and who contribute constructively (R8).</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Uses evidence from the text to infer feelings, motives or unstated meaning.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> States an opinion or feeling about a character without pointing to any evidence in the text.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Makes a reasonable inference and backs it up with a specific detail from the text.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Makes a well-supported inference and considers an alternative view or the author’s likely intention.</li></ul></li><li><b>Key Competency — Participating &amp; Contributing</b> — Contributes constructively during group reading discussions.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Rarely offers ideas in group discussion, or dominates it without listening to others.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Offers relevant ideas and listens to others during discussion.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Builds on others’ ideas, invites quieter voices in, and helps keep the group on task.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Weekly Project Menu (choose or assign one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a recruitment poster for the Shell Company (Eastern Staff) as it might have appeared in 1934, listing the &quot;adventure&quot; of working abroad.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Comic strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>6-panel comic of Dahl's secret motorbike rides around Repton in disguise during his last term.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Drama scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Act out the interview scene where Dahl tells the Shell Director he doesn't want to go to Egypt because it's &quot;too dusty,&quot; and the Director's reaction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Journal reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a &quot;where are they now&quot; reflection imagining what nine-year-old Roald from Week 1 would think if he could see who he becomes by the end of the book.</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Quiz: Week 9 Quiz — 20 questions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9 Quiz — 20 questions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 9: Finding your path</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading comprehension quiz</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 minutes: quiz about 35 min, review answers and reflect about 10 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Quiz Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>20 questions covering this week’s four reading chunks; auto-marked in the app, or print the student copy and use the answer key below.</li><li>Review answers together and discuss misconceptions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Quiz — Teacher Answer Key</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. What two games was Roald Dahl unexpectedly excellent at? <b>a) Fives and squash-racquets ✔</b> · b) Cricket and rugby · c) Football and tennis · d) Rowing and boxing</li><li>2. What title did Dahl earn for being the best at fives in the school? <b>a) Captain of Fives ✔</b> · b) Head Boy · c) School Captain · d) Senior Prefect</li><li>3. Why was Dahl never made a Boazer, despite his sporting success? <em>Model answer: The authorities thought he was too unpredictable/didn't like rules and couldn't be trusted with the role.</em></li><li>4. What other hobby did Dahl take very seriously at Repton besides sport? <b>a) Photography ✔</b> · b) Chess · c) Music · d) Debating</li><li>5. What society later awarded Dahl a photography medal for a photo he took while flying solo for the RAF? <em>Model answer: The Egyptian Photographic Society (for a photo of the Arch of Ctesiphon).</em></li><li>6. What two famous universities did Dahl's mother offer to send him to? <b>a) Oxford or Cambridge ✔</b> · b) Cambridge and Durham · c) Oxford and St Andrews · d) London and Edinburgh</li><li>7. Why did Dahl turn down university? <em>Model answer: He wanted to go straight from school to work for a company that would send him to faraway places like Africa or China.</em></li><li>8. Which company did Dahl most want to work for? <b>a) The Shell Company ✔</b> · b) BP · c) Imperial Airways · d) The East India Company</li><li>9. Out of how many applicants did Dahl win one of the places at Shell? <b>a) 107 applicants for 7 places ✔</b> · b) 50 applicants for 5 places · c) 200 applicants for 10 places · d) 20 applicants for 2 places</li><li>10. What did Dahl secretly keep hidden near Repton during his last term? <b>a) A 500cc Ariel motorbike ✔</b> · b) A car · c) A rowing boat · d) A camera collection</li><li>11. How did Dahl disguise himself when riding it? <em>Model answer: With a helmet, goggles, an old raincoat and rubber waders.</em></li><li>12. What expedition did Dahl join for one month before starting work at Shell? <b>a) The Public Schools' Exploring Society's trip to Newfoundland ✔</b> · b) An Antarctic expedition · c) A mountaineering trip to the Alps · d) A sailing trip around Britain</li><li>13. What was daily commuting life like for Dahl as a young Shell trainee in London? <em>Model answer: Wearing a grey suit, bowler or trilby hat, and carrying a furled umbrella every day on the train.</em></li><li>14. What job did Dahl do during his salesmanship training in Somerset? <b>a) Selling kerosene from the back of a tanker to villagers ✔</b> · b) Selling insurance door to door · c) Working in a Shell petrol station · d) Repairing tanker engines</li><li>15. Which country did Dahl's boss originally want to send him to, which he turned down? <b>a) Egypt ✔</b> · b) India · c) South Africa · d) Malaya</li><li>16. What excuse did Dahl give for not wanting to go to Egypt? <b>a) That it was &quot;too dusty&quot; ✔</b> · b) That it was too hot · c) That it was too far from home · d) That it was too dangerous</li><li>17. Which country was Dahl sent to instead? <b>a) East Africa (Kenya/Tanganyika area) ✔</b> · b) West Africa · c) India · d) Malaya</li><li>18. How did Dahl's mother react when he told her he would be away for three years in Africa? <em>Model answer: She hid her true feelings and reacted with joy and support for him.</em></li><li>19. Inference: Why do you think Dahl chose to end his memoir at this point in his life, rather than continuing into his RAF and writing career? <em>Model answer: Accept reasoned answers: the book is specifically about childhood/school, and he says the rest &quot;has nothing to do with childhood or school&quot; — it's &quot;a different tale altogether.&quot;</em></li><li>20. Short answer: In your own words, describe how the boy we met in Week 1 (nervous, homesick, mischievous) has changed by the end of the book, and give one piece of evidence to support your answer. <em>Model answer: Open answer — mark for a clear before/after comparison using specific text evidence.</em></li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10: Final project & celebration":{"label":"Day","children":{"Lesson 1 — Choose & plan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose &amp; plan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 10: Final project &amp; celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading, Writing and Presenting</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One lesson (or a separate celebration afternoon)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can synthesise our understanding of a whole text into an original creative or informative piece of work, and present it clearly to an audience.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can choose and complete a final project that shows deep understanding of Boy: Tales of Childhood</li><li>I can present my work with confidence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Introduce the four final project options and the rubric below. Students choose individually, in pairs, or in small groups, then use the rest of the lesson to plan.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Final Project Choices (choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">&quot;My Own Tales of Childhood&quot; memoir chapter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and illustrate one true, vivid chapter from your own life so far, in Dahl's style — a single memorable event told with humour and detail (not a whole autobiography).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Then-and-now report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research and present (poster, slideshow or short written report) how school discipline, medical treatment, or travel has changed between the 1920s and today, using at least 3 specific examples from the book.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Mini &quot;Boy&quot; museum exhibit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a small museum display (physical or digital) of 5–6 &quot;artefacts&quot; from the book (a tuck-box, a gobstopper jar, a cane — described respectfully as a historical object, a paddle-steamer ticket, a Cadbury testing box) each with an information card explaining its significance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Dramatic scene compilation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a group, choose and rehearse 3 short scenes from across the book (comedy encouraged, no violence performed) to perform for the class or at the celebration, with a short spoken introduction explaining why you chose each scene.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Plans, drafts and shares writing or project work with growing independence.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Needs significant prompting to start or complete writing or project tasks.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Completes tasks with growing independence and only occasional prompting.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Works independently, manages their own time, and can explain their choices or process.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Work time":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Work time</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 10: Final project &amp; celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading, Writing and Presenting</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One lesson (or a separate celebration afternoon)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can synthesise our understanding of a whole text into an original creative or informative piece of work, and present it clearly to an audience.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can choose and complete a final project that shows deep understanding of Boy: Tales of Childhood</li><li>I can present my work with confidence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Independent or group work time, with teacher check-ins. Use the progress checklist to keep every group on track.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Final Project Choices (choose one)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">&quot;My Own Tales of Childhood&quot; memoir chapter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write and illustrate one true, vivid chapter from your own life so far, in Dahl's style — a single memorable event told with humour and detail (not a whole autobiography).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Then-and-now report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research and present (poster, slideshow or short written report) how school discipline, medical treatment, or travel has changed between the 1920s and today, using at least 3 specific examples from the book.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Mini &quot;Boy&quot; museum exhibit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a small museum display (physical or digital) of 5–6 &quot;artefacts&quot; from the book (a tuck-box, a gobstopper jar, a cane — described respectfully as a historical object, a paddle-steamer ticket, a Cadbury testing box) each with an information card explaining its significance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Dramatic scene compilation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a group, choose and rehearse 3 short scenes from across the book (comedy encouraged, no violence performed) to perform for the class or at the celebration, with a short spoken introduction explaining why you chose each scene.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Progress Checklist</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Chosen my/our final project option</li><li>Planned what I need (notes, materials, roles in group)</li><li>Made a first draft or rough version</li><li>Checked my work against the rubric</li><li>Practised my presentation out loud</li><li>Ready to present or display on celebration day</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Plans, drafts and shares writing or project work with growing independence.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Needs significant prompting to start or complete writing or project tasks.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Completes tasks with growing independence and only occasional prompting.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Works independently, manages their own time, and can explain their choices or process.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Presentations & celebration":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Presentations &amp; celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Week</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Week 10: Final project &amp; celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading, Writing and Presenting</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One lesson (or a separate celebration afternoon)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We can synthesise our understanding of a whole text into an original creative or informative piece of work, and present it clearly to an audience.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can choose and complete a final project that shows deep understanding of Boy: Tales of Childhood</li><li>I can present my work with confidence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Presentations or a gallery walk, a short class discussion on resilience, courage and how times have changed, and awarding of certificates.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment — Rubric</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Reading — Ideas</b> — Explains ideas within, across and beyond the text (e.g. why Dahl chooses to remember certain moments).<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Retells what happened, but doesn’t yet explain why it mattered or connect it to other parts of the book.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Explains, with a reason from the text, why a memory or event was included or important.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Connects ideas across several chapters or beyond the text (e.g. to their own life, or to one of Dahl’s later books) with real insight.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Ideas</b> — Selects and communicates own ideas clearly in go-away reflections and journal writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writes a few words or an unclear sentence that doesn’t yet answer the prompt.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Writes a clear, on-topic response with at least one specific detail.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Writes a developed, personal response with more than one idea, clearly organised.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Structure &amp; Language</b> — Uses language features (hyperbole, dialogue, vivid detail) appropriately in own writing.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Writing is plain description with little vivid or deliberate language.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Uses at least one deliberate language feature (vivid detail, dialogue, exaggeration) to good effect.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Uses more than one language feature confidently and consistently to create a clear effect.</li></ul></li><li><b>Writing — Processes &amp; Strategies</b> — Plans, drafts and shares writing or project work with growing independence.<ul class='lt-sublist'><li><em>Not yet:</em> Needs significant prompting to start or complete writing or project tasks.</li><li><em>Achieved:</em> Completes tasks with growing independence and only occasional prompting.</li><li><em>Excelled:</em> Works independently, manages their own time, and can explain their choices or process.</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories":{"label":"Rank","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Year level</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Years 5–6 · NZC English Phase 2 (Years 4–6), Reading: Summarising</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Structure</th><td class=\"lt-td\">8 ranks × 5 stories × 5 lessons = 200 lessons of about 15 minutes. Each story: Lesson 1 Read &amp; Understand, Lessons 2–4 Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (beginning, middle, end), Lesson 5 The Full Scroll.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Differentiation</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every story has three scrolls at three reading levels: I Recruit (simple), II Warrior (intermediate), III Master (advanced). Choose by reading level, not by rank.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">The S.W.O.R.D. Method</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>S — Search:</b> who and what is in this part?</li><li><b>W — Watch:</b> what happens, in order?</li><li><b>O — Omit:</b> cut the detail that is not needed.</li><li><b>R — Rewrite:</b> put it in your own words.</li><li><b>D — Deliver:</b> write one clear sentence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The Eight Ranks</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai</b> (white belt), Stories 1–5: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</li><li><b>Rank 2: Novice Swordsman</b> (yellow belt), Stories 6–10: Finding key events and putting them in order</li><li><b>Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior</b> (orange belt), Stories 11–15: Cutting unnecessary detail</li><li><b>Rank 4: Skilled Ronin</b> (green belt), Stories 16–20: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</li><li><b>Rank 5: Trusted Retainer</b> (blue belt), Stories 21–25: Summarising longer or more complex passages</li><li><b>Rank 6: Honoured Samurai</b> (purple belt), Stories 26–30: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</li><li><b>Rank 7: Elite Samurai</b> (brown belt), Stories 31–35: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</li><li><b>Rank 8: Master Samurai</b> (black belt), Stories 36–40: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">All 40 Stories — Bible Stories</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Creation</li><li>2. Noah’s Ark</li><li>3. Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</li><li>4. Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</li><li>5. Joseph Forgives His Brothers</li><li>6. Baby Moses in the Basket</li><li>7. Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</li><li>8. Crossing the Red Sea</li><li>9. The Ten Commandments</li><li>10. Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</li><li>11. Gideon &amp; the Fleece</li><li>12. Samson's Strength</li><li>13. David &amp; Goliath</li><li>14. David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</li><li>15. Solomon's Wise Judgement</li><li>16. Elijah on Mount Carmel</li><li>17. Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</li><li>18. Daniel in the Lions' Den</li><li>19. Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</li><li>20. Esther Becomes Queen</li><li>21. The Birth of Jesus</li><li>22. The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</li><li>23. John the Baptist</li><li>24. The Baptism of Jesus</li><li>25. Jesus Calls His First Disciples</li><li>26. The Sower &amp; the Seeds</li><li>27. The Good Samaritan</li><li>28. The Lost Sheep</li><li>29. The Prodigal Son</li><li>30. The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</li><li>31. Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</li><li>32. Jesus Calms the Storm</li><li>33. Jesus Walks on Water</li><li>34. Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</li><li>35. Jesus Raises Lazarus</li><li>36. The Last Supper</li><li>37. The Crucifixion</li><li>38. The Resurrection</li><li>39. Doubting Thomas</li><li>40. Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</li></ul></div></div>"},"Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (White belt) — Stories 1–5":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 1: Creation":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Creation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 1’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 1: Creation) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name God and Adam and Eve as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>Adam and Eve</li><li>the mountains</li><li>the ocean</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Adam and Eve are made</li><li>Light is created</li><li>Animals fill the land</li><li>God rests</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God created the world by speaking</li><li>Adam and Eve were made before the animals</li><li>God rested on the seventh day</li><li>The earth was full of light at the very start</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the beginning, the world was empty and dark, and water covered everything. Then God began to create. First, God said, ‘Let there be light,’ and light appeared. God called the light day and the dark night. This was the first day. Next, God made the sky and set it above the water. Then God made the dry land appear and called it earth, and he called the water the seas. Last, God made grass, trees, and every kind of plant grow across the land, and he saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, God made two great lights for the sky: the sun to shine by day and the moon to shine by night, along with the stars. He placed them there to give light on the earth and to mark the days, the seasons, and the years. Then God filled the water with fish and every kind of sea creature, and he filled the sky with birds of every kind, telling them to grow in number and fill the earth. Then he made all kinds of animals for the land, wild and tame, and every small crawling creature. God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last of all, God made people. He made a man and a woman and called them Adam and Eve, shaping them like himself. He gave them care of the fish, the birds, and every living thing, and blessed them, telling them to grow in number and fill the earth. God looked at everything he had made, the light, the sky, the land, the sun, the moon, the stars, the animals, and the people, and he saw that it was very good. On the seventh day, God rested from all his work, because it was finished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At the very beginning, the earth had no shape and was covered in darkness, with deep water stretching in every direction. Then God’s Spirit moved gently over the waters, and God began to speak the world into being. ‘Let there be light,’ he said, and light appeared, pushing back the darkness. God separated the light from the dark, naming the light day and the darkness night, and that was the first day. On the second day, God stretched out a great space above the earth and called it the sky, setting it between the waters below and the waters above. On the third day, God gathered the waters below the sky into one place, so dry ground appeared, and he called it land and the gathered waters seas. Then he filled the land with grass, trees, and every kind of plant, and God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the fourth day, God set two great lights in the sky to govern the earth: a greater light to rule the day and a lesser light to rule the night, scattering the stars among them to mark the days, the seasons, and the years. On the fifth day, God filled the seas with fish and every creature that moves through the water, and filled the sky with birds of every kind, telling them to be fruitful and multiply and fill the waters and the skies. On the sixth day, God turned to the land once more, forming every kind of animal to live there, livestock to be tamed, wild creatures to roam free, and small creatures to move along the ground. Looking over all he had made, God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, on that same sixth day, God said, ‘Let us make mankind in our own image,’ and so he formed a man and a woman, Adam and Eve, giving them a likeness unlike anything else he had made. He blessed them and gave them care over the fish, the birds, the animals, and every living creature, telling them to be fruitful and fill the earth together. When God surveyed everything he had made, the light and the dark, the sky and the seas, the plants and the animals, and finally the man and the woman, he saw that it was very good. On the seventh day, having finished his work, God rested and made the seventh day holy, a day set apart for rest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before anything existed, the earth was formless and empty, wrapped in darkness, with the waters of the deep stretching endlessly in every direction, until God’s Spirit moved gently across that vast, silent water. With nothing but his spoken word, God began to shape the universe out of emptiness. ‘Let there be light,’ he declared, and light broke across the darkness for the very first time. Seeing that the light was good, God separated it from the darkness, naming the light day and the darkness night, and so the first day of creation came to a close. On the second day, God stretched out a vast expanse above the formless earth, calling it the sky, dividing the waters below from the waters above. On the third day, God commanded the waters beneath the sky to gather into one place, and dry ground rose up for the first time; he named the ground land and the gathered waters seas. From that bare earth, God then brought forth grass, trees, and every kind of seed-bearing plant, each able to produce more of its own kind, and he saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the fourth day, God set two great lights to govern the passing of time: a greater light to rule the day, and a lesser light to rule the night, scattering the stars across the heavens between them, so that together they might mark the days, the seasons, and the years, and cast their light upon the earth below. On the fifth day, God turned his attention to the waters and the skies, filling the seas with fish and every kind of swarming, swimming creature, and filling the skies with birds of every feather, commanding them to be fruitful and multiply and fill the waters and the air. On the sixth day, God formed the creatures of the land itself, livestock to be tamed, wild beasts to roam untamed, and countless smaller creatures to move along the ground, and, surveying all he had made, he saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, crowning the whole of his creation, God said, ‘Let us make mankind in our own image, after our likeness,’ and so, on that same sixth day, he formed a man and a woman, Adam and Eve, shaping them unlike anything else he had made, giving them a likeness that reflected his own. God blessed them and entrusted them with the care of the fish of the sea, the birds of the sky, the animals of the land, and every living creature that moved upon the earth, telling them to be fruitful, to multiply, and to fill the earth together as its keepers. When God surveyed the whole of his finished work, the light and the darkness, the sky and the gathered seas, the land with its plants, the sun, moon, and stars, and every living creature, and finally the man and the woman made in his own image, he declared that it was very good, more remarkable than anything that had come before. And so, having finished all his work, God rested on the seventh day and blessed it, setting it apart as holy, a day of rest for all of creation to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Creation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>the light</li><li>the sky</li><li>the land</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Light is created</li><li>The sky appears above the waters</li><li>Land and seas appear</li><li>Plants grow across the land</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>‘Let there be light,’ God said</li><li>the light was called day and the darkness night</li><li>this was the first day</li><li>the sky was set above the waters</li><li>the dry ground was called land, the waters seas</li><li>grass, trees, and every kind of plant grew</li><li>God saw that it was good</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, God made ___ and ___, then ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the beginning, the world was empty and dark, and water covered everything. Then God began to create. First, God said, ‘Let there be light,’ and light appeared. God called the light day and the dark night. This was the first day. Next, God made the sky and set it above the water. Then God made the dry land appear and called it earth, and he called the water the seas. Last, God made grass, trees, and every kind of plant grow across the land, and he saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At the very beginning, the earth had no shape and was covered in darkness, with deep water stretching in every direction. Then God’s Spirit moved gently over the waters, and God began to speak the world into being. ‘Let there be light,’ he said, and light appeared, pushing back the darkness. God separated the light from the dark, naming the light day and the darkness night, and that was the first day. On the second day, God stretched out a great space above the earth and called it the sky, setting it between the waters below and the waters above. On the third day, God gathered the waters below the sky into one place, so dry ground appeared, and he called it land and the gathered waters seas. Then he filled the land with grass, trees, and every kind of plant, and God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before anything existed, the earth was formless and empty, wrapped in darkness, with the waters of the deep stretching endlessly in every direction, until God’s Spirit moved gently across that vast, silent water. With nothing but his spoken word, God began to shape the universe out of emptiness. ‘Let there be light,’ he declared, and light broke across the darkness for the very first time. Seeing that the light was good, God separated it from the darkness, naming the light day and the darkness night, and so the first day of creation came to a close. On the second day, God stretched out a vast expanse above the formless earth, calling it the sky, dividing the waters below from the waters above. On the third day, God commanded the waters beneath the sky to gather into one place, and dry ground rose up for the first time; he named the ground land and the gathered waters seas. From that bare earth, God then brought forth grass, trees, and every kind of seed-bearing plant, each able to produce more of its own kind, and he saw that it was good.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Creation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>the sun and moon</li><li>the fish</li><li>the birds</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sun and moon are placed in the sky</li><li>The stars are scattered among them</li><li>The seas are filled with fish and sea creatures</li><li>The skies and land are filled with birds and animals</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a greater light to rule the day</li><li>a lesser light to rule the night</li><li>to mark the days, the seasons, and the years</li><li>every creature that moves through the water</li><li>birds of every kind</li><li>livestock, wild creatures, and small creatures</li><li>God saw that it was good</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, God filled the sky with ___, and the seas and land with ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, God made two great lights for the sky: the sun to shine by day and the moon to shine by night, along with the stars. He placed them there to give light on the earth and to mark the days, the seasons, and the years. Then God filled the water with fish and every kind of sea creature, and he filled the sky with birds of every kind, telling them to grow in number and fill the earth. Then he made all kinds of animals for the land, wild and tame, and every small crawling creature. God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the fourth day, God set two great lights in the sky to govern the earth: a greater light to rule the day and a lesser light to rule the night, scattering the stars among them to mark the days, the seasons, and the years. On the fifth day, God filled the seas with fish and every creature that moves through the water, and filled the sky with birds of every kind, telling them to be fruitful and multiply and fill the waters and the skies. On the sixth day, God turned to the land once more, forming every kind of animal to live there, livestock to be tamed, wild creatures to roam free, and small creatures to move along the ground. Looking over all he had made, God saw that it was good.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the fourth day, God set two great lights to govern the passing of time: a greater light to rule the day, and a lesser light to rule the night, scattering the stars across the heavens between them, so that together they might mark the days, the seasons, and the years, and cast their light upon the earth below. On the fifth day, God turned his attention to the waters and the skies, filling the seas with fish and every kind of swarming, swimming creature, and filling the skies with birds of every feather, commanding them to be fruitful and multiply and fill the waters and the air. On the sixth day, God formed the creatures of the land itself, livestock to be tamed, wild beasts to roam untamed, and countless smaller creatures to move along the ground, and, surveying all he had made, he saw that it was good.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Creation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>Adam and Eve</li><li>the animals</li><li>the seventh day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God makes a man and a woman</li><li>God blesses them and gives them care of the earth</li><li>God sees that everything is very good</li><li>God rests on the seventh day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>‘Let us make mankind in our own image,’ God said</li><li>giving them a likeness unlike anything else he had made</li><li>telling them to be fruitful and fill the earth together</li><li>more remarkable than anything that had come before</li><li>a day of rest for all of creation to come</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, God made ___, and then ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Last of all, God made people. He made a man and a woman and called them Adam and Eve, shaping them like himself. He gave them care of the fish, the birds, and every living thing, and blessed them, telling them to grow in number and fill the earth. God looked at everything he had made, the light, the sky, the land, the sun, the moon, the stars, the animals, and the people, and he saw that it was very good. On the seventh day, God rested from all his work, because it was finished.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, on that same sixth day, God said, ‘Let us make mankind in our own image,’ and so he formed a man and a woman, Adam and Eve, giving them a likeness unlike anything else he had made. He blessed them and gave them care over the fish, the birds, the animals, and every living creature, telling them to be fruitful and fill the earth together. When God surveyed everything he had made, the light and the dark, the sky and the seas, the plants and the animals, and finally the man and the woman, he saw that it was very good. On the seventh day, having finished his work, God rested and made the seventh day holy, a day set apart for rest.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, crowning the whole of his creation, God said, ‘Let us make mankind in our own image, after our likeness,’ and so, on that same sixth day, he formed a man and a woman, Adam and Eve, shaping them unlike anything else he had made, giving them a likeness that reflected his own. God blessed them and entrusted them with the care of the fish of the sea, the birds of the sky, the animals of the land, and every living creature that moved upon the earth, telling them to be fruitful, to multiply, and to fill the earth together as its keepers. When God surveyed the whole of his finished work, the light and the darkness, the sky and the gathered seas, the land with its plants, the sun, moon, and stars, and every living creature, and finally the man and the woman made in his own image, he declared that it was very good, more remarkable than anything that had come before. And so, having finished all his work, God rested on the seventh day and blessed it, setting it apart as holy, a day of rest for all of creation to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Creation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 1 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 1 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 2 (Noah’s Ark) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, God made ___ and ___, then ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, God filled the sky with ___, and the seas and land with ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, God made ___, and then ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 2: Noah’s Ark":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Noah’s Ark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 2’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 2: Noah’s Ark) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Noah and God as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah</li><li>God</li><li>the ark</li><li>the mountains</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God tells Noah a flood is coming</li><li>God tells Noah to build an ark</li><li>Rain falls for forty days and nights</li><li>God places a rainbow in the sky</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah built the ark because God told him to</li><li>It rained for four days and four nights</li><li>God promised never to flood the whole earth again</li><li>Noah brought three of every animal onto the ark</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The world became full of wrongdoing, and it made God sad. But Noah was a good man who tried to obey God. God told Noah that a great flood was coming to cover the whole earth. God told Noah to build a huge wooden boat called an ark. It had to be big enough for his family and two of every kind of animal.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah did just what God said. He brought his family onto the ark, along with two of every kind of animal. Then God shut the door, and the rain began to fall. It rained for forty days and forty nights. The water rose higher and higher until the whole earth was covered. Only Noah and the others on the ark stayed safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After a long time, the rain stopped. Slowly, the water went down. Noah sent out a dove, and it came back with a leaf. This meant the flood was ending. At last, Noah’s family and all the animals came out onto dry land. God put a rainbow in the sky. It was a promise that he would never flood the whole earth again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As more and more people filled the earth, the world grew full of wrongdoing, and God was troubled by how far people had drifted from him. But there was one man, Noah, who stood apart: he walked closely with God and tried to do what was right, even while everyone around him did not. God told Noah that he planned to send a great flood to cover the whole earth, because of all the wrongdoing he saw. So God gave Noah careful instructions to build a massive wooden boat, called an ark, large enough to hold his family and two of every kind of animal, so that life could begin again after the flood came.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah did everything exactly as God commanded. He gathered his wife, his three sons, and their wives, along with two of every kind of animal, bringing them all safely onto the ark. Once everyone was inside, God shut the door behind them, and the rain began to fall. It did not stop for forty days and forty nights, pouring down until every mountain was covered and the entire world lay beneath the water. Everything that lived on dry land was swept away in the flood, and only Noah and those with him on the ark were left alive, floating safely above it all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many long months, the rain finally stopped, and slowly the water began to go down. Noah sent out a dove to see if it could find dry land, and when it returned carrying a fresh olive leaf, Noah knew the flood was ending. At last the ground grew dry enough for Noah, his family, and every animal to step out of the ark together. God then set a rainbow in the sky and made a promise: he would never again destroy the whole earth with a flood, and the rainbow would always be a sign of that promise, stretched across the clouds for everyone to see.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the number of people on the earth grew, so too did the depth of their wrongdoing, until the world was filled with violence and corruption, and God grieved over how far his creation had strayed from the goodness he had intended for it. Yet amid all this, one man stood apart: Noah, who walked in close relationship with God and lived a life of integrity among people who showed none. Grieved by what he saw, God resolved to send a great flood that would wash over the entire earth, undoing the corruption that had spread across it. But he chose to spare Noah, and gave him detailed, exacting instructions for building an enormous vessel of wood, an ark, sturdy and vast enough to shelter his family and a pair of every kind of living creature, so that life might be preserved and begin anew once the flood had passed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah obeyed every instruction God had given him, without hesitation or complaint. He gathered his wife, his three sons and their wives, and led two of every kind of animal, bird, and creeping creature onto the ark, until all had been safely gathered inside. Once the last of them had entered, God himself shut the door behind them, sealing the ark just as the sky began to break open. Rain fell without pause for forty days and forty nights, and the waters rose relentlessly, first covering the plains, then swallowing the hills, until even the highest mountains vanished beneath the flood. Every living thing that had remained on dry ground was swept away in the deluge, and only those sheltered within the ark, carried safely above the destruction, were left alive to see what would come after.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For many long months the ark drifted upon the endless water, until at last the rain ceased and the flood slowly began to recede. Noah sent out a dove to test whether the earth had dried enough to sustain life, and when it returned to him bearing a freshly plucked olive leaf, he understood that the waters were finally retreating from the land. Not long after, the ground had dried sufficiently for Noah, his family, and every creature that had sheltered with them to step out together onto solid earth once more. In that moment, God set a radiant rainbow across the clouds and made a solemn promise: never again would he destroy the whole earth with a flood, and the rainbow itself would stand forever after as a sign of that covenant, a reminder stretched across the sky for every generation to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Noah’s Ark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah</li><li>God</li><li>the ark</li><li>the flood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The world grows full of wrongdoing</li><li>God tells Noah a flood is coming</li><li>God tells Noah to build an ark</li><li>The ark is built for his family and the animals</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the world grew full of wrongdoing</li><li>God was troubled by how far people had drifted from him</li><li>he walked closely with God</li><li>because of all the wrongdoing he saw</li><li>large enough to hold his family and two of every kind of animal</li><li>so that life could begin again after the flood came</li><li>God gave Noah careful instructions</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told Noah to build ___ because ___ was coming.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The world became full of wrongdoing, and it made God sad. But Noah was a good man who tried to obey God. God told Noah that a great flood was coming to cover the whole earth. God told Noah to build a huge wooden boat called an ark. It had to be big enough for his family and two of every kind of animal.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As more and more people filled the earth, the world grew full of wrongdoing, and God was troubled by how far people had drifted from him. But there was one man, Noah, who stood apart: he walked closely with God and tried to do what was right, even while everyone around him did not. God told Noah that he planned to send a great flood to cover the whole earth, because of all the wrongdoing he saw. So God gave Noah careful instructions to build a massive wooden boat, called an ark, large enough to hold his family and two of every kind of animal, so that life could begin again after the flood came.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the number of people on the earth grew, so too did the depth of their wrongdoing, until the world was filled with violence and corruption, and God grieved over how far his creation had strayed from the goodness he had intended for it. Yet amid all this, one man stood apart: Noah, who walked in close relationship with God and lived a life of integrity among people who showed none. Grieved by what he saw, God resolved to send a great flood that would wash over the entire earth, undoing the corruption that had spread across it. But he chose to spare Noah, and gave him detailed, exacting instructions for building an enormous vessel of wood, an ark, sturdy and vast enough to shelter his family and a pair of every kind of living creature, so that life might be preserved and begin anew once the flood had passed.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Noah’s Ark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah</li><li>God</li><li>the animals</li><li>the mountains</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah’s family and the animals enter the ark</li><li>God shuts the door</li><li>Rain falls for forty days and nights</li><li>The mountains are covered by water</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah did everything exactly as God commanded</li><li>bringing them all safely onto the ark</li><li>God shut the door behind them</li><li>pouring down until every mountain was covered</li><li>the entire world lay beneath the water</li><li>everything that lived on dry land was swept away</li><li>floating safely above it all</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah brought his family and ___ onto the ark, then it rained for ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah did just what God said. He brought his family onto the ark, along with two of every kind of animal. Then God shut the door, and the rain began to fall. It rained for forty days and forty nights. The water rose higher and higher until the whole earth was covered. Only Noah and the others on the ark stayed safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah did everything exactly as God commanded. He gathered his wife, his three sons, and their wives, along with two of every kind of animal, bringing them all safely onto the ark. Once everyone was inside, God shut the door behind them, and the rain began to fall. It did not stop for forty days and forty nights, pouring down until every mountain was covered and the entire world lay beneath the water. Everything that lived on dry land was swept away in the flood, and only Noah and those with him on the ark were left alive, floating safely above it all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noah obeyed every instruction God had given him, without hesitation or complaint. He gathered his wife, his three sons and their wives, and led two of every kind of animal, bird, and creeping creature onto the ark, until all had been safely gathered inside. Once the last of them had entered, God himself shut the door behind them, sealing the ark just as the sky began to break open. Rain fell without pause for forty days and forty nights, and the waters rose relentlessly, first covering the plains, then swallowing the hills, until even the highest mountains vanished beneath the flood. Every living thing that had remained on dry ground was swept away in the deluge, and only those sheltered within the ark, carried safely above the destruction, were left alive to see what would come after.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Noah’s Ark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Noah</li><li>God</li><li>the dove</li><li>the rainbow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain stops and the water goes down</li><li>Noah sends out a dove</li><li>The dove returns with an olive leaf</li><li>Noah’s family and the animals leave the ark</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>after many long months</li><li>slowly the water began to go down</li><li>to see if it could find dry land</li><li>carrying a fresh olive leaf</li><li>the ground grew dry enough</li><li>stretched across the clouds for everyone to see</li><li>he would never again destroy the whole earth with a flood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At last the rain stopped and Noah’s family came out onto ___, and God gave a ___ as a promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After a long time, the rain stopped. Slowly, the water went down. Noah sent out a dove, and it came back with a leaf. This meant the flood was ending. At last, Noah’s family and all the animals came out onto dry land. God put a rainbow in the sky. It was a promise that he would never flood the whole earth again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many long months, the rain finally stopped, and slowly the water began to go down. Noah sent out a dove to see if it could find dry land, and when it returned carrying a fresh olive leaf, Noah knew the flood was ending. At last the ground grew dry enough for Noah, his family, and every animal to step out of the ark together. God then set a rainbow in the sky and made a promise: he would never again destroy the whole earth with a flood, and the rainbow would always be a sign of that promise, stretched across the clouds for everyone to see.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For many long months the ark drifted upon the endless water, until at last the rain ceased and the flood slowly began to recede. Noah sent out a dove to test whether the earth had dried enough to sustain life, and when it returned to him bearing a freshly plucked olive leaf, he understood that the waters were finally retreating from the land. Not long after, the ground had dried sufficiently for Noah, his family, and every creature that had sheltered with them to step out together onto solid earth once more. In that moment, God set a radiant rainbow across the clouds and made a solemn promise: never again would he destroy the whole earth with a flood, and the rainbow itself would stand forever after as a sign of that covenant, a reminder stretched across the sky for every generation to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Noah’s Ark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 2 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 2 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 3 (Abraham’s Call & Journey) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> God told Noah to build ___ because ___ was coming.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Noah brought his family and ___ onto the ark, then it rained for ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> At last the rain stopped and Noah’s family came out onto ___, and God gave a ___ as a promise.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 3: Abraham’s Call & Journey":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 3’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 3: Abraham’s Call & Journey) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Abram and God as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Abram</li><li>God</li><li>the stars</li><li>Canaan</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God tells Abram to leave his home</li><li>Abram travels toward a new land</li><li>Abram and his family arrive in Canaan</li><li>God promises Abram as many descendants as the stars</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God told Abram to leave his home country</li><li>Abram was young when God called him</li><li>God gave Abram a new name, Abraham</li><li>Abram knew exactly where he was going from the start</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to a man named Abram. God told him to leave his home and his family. He was to go to a new land that God would show him. God made him a big promise. He said Abram would become a great nation. God said he would bless Abram, and bless the whole world through him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Abram obeyed, even though he was already old. He took his wife Sarai and his nephew Lot. They packed up everything and left their home. Abram did not know exactly where he was going. Along the way, Abram built altars to worship God. At last, Abram and his family arrived in the land of Canaan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to Abram again in Canaan. He told Abram to look up at the stars. God said Abram would have that many children one day. This seemed impossible, because Abram had no children yet. God gave Abram a new name: Abraham. It meant ‘father of many.’ Abraham believed God’s promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to a man named Abram and told him to leave his home country, his family, and everything familiar, to go to a new land that God would show him. God made him an enormous promise: he would make Abram into a great nation, bless him, and make his name well known, and through him, every family on earth would be blessed. It was an enormous step of trust, because God didn’t tell Abram exactly where this new land was — only that he would show him the way once he began to go.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So Abram obeyed, even though he was already seventy-five years old. He packed up everything he owned, took his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, and all of their servants and animals, and set out for a country he had never seen. Along the way, Abram built altars to worship God at different places he stopped, calling on God’s name and trusting that he was being led in the right direction. Eventually, Abram and his family arrived safely in the land of Canaan, the very place God had been leading them toward all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once Abram had settled in Canaan, God spoke to him again and confirmed his promise. God told Abram to look up at the night sky and count the stars if he could — that was how many descendants Abram would one day have, even though he and Sarai had no children yet. God also gave Abram a new name, Abraham, meaning ‘father of many,’ as a sign of this promise. Abraham believed God, trusting that this promise would somehow come true, even though it seemed impossible at the time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, God spoke directly to a man named Abram, instructing him to leave behind his homeland, his extended family, and everything comfortable and familiar to him, and to set out instead for a land that God had not yet revealed. Along with this instruction came an extraordinary promise: God would transform Abram into a great and mighty nation, bless him abundantly, make his name renowned among the nations, and, most remarkably of all, use him as a source of blessing for every family on the face of the earth. It was a staggering act of trust that God asked of him, since Abram was given no map, no destination, and no timeline — only the assurance that God himself would reveal the way, one step at a time, as Abram began to move forward in obedience.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, Abram obeyed without hesitation, even though he was already seventy-five years old when this call came to him. He gathered his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, and the full household of servants, animals, and possessions they had accumulated, and set out toward a country he had never once laid eyes on. As they travelled, Abram paused at several points along the way to build altars and worship God there, calling on God’s name aloud and trusting, step by step, that he was being guided toward wherever he was meant to go. In time, after a long and uncertain journey, Abram and his household arrived safely in the land of Canaan, the very destination God had been quietly leading them toward the entire time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once Abram and his family had settled into their new surroundings in Canaan, God appeared to him once more and reaffirmed the promise he had made. He led Abram outside on a clear night and told him to gaze up at the vast, star-filled sky, challenging him to try counting them if he could — for that, God said, was the almost unimaginable number of descendants Abram would one day have, despite the fact that he and Sarai remained childless at this point in their lives. As a further sign of this covenant, God changed Abram’s name to Abraham, meaning ‘father of many nations,’ a new identity that reflected the promise itself. Remarkably, Abraham chose to believe God’s word, holding onto this promise with unwavering trust even though, by every earthly measure, it still seemed entirely impossible.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Abram</li><li>God</li><li>the land</li><li>the family</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God tells Abram to leave his home</li><li>God promises to make him a great nation</li><li>God promises to bless every family through him</li><li>Abram does not yet know where the land is</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>his home country, his family, and everything familiar</li><li>God made him an enormous promise</li><li>and make his name well known</li><li>every family on earth would be blessed</li><li>it was an enormous step of trust</li><li>he would show him the way once he began to go</li><li>a new land that God would show him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told ___ to leave his home and go to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to a man named Abram. God told him to leave his home and his family. He was to go to a new land that God would show him. God made him a big promise. He said Abram would become a great nation. God said he would bless Abram, and bless the whole world through him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to a man named Abram and told him to leave his home country, his family, and everything familiar, to go to a new land that God would show him. God made him an enormous promise: he would make Abram into a great nation, bless him, and make his name well known, and through him, every family on earth would be blessed. It was an enormous step of trust, because God didn’t tell Abram exactly where this new land was — only that he would show him the way once he began to go.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, God spoke directly to a man named Abram, instructing him to leave behind his homeland, his extended family, and everything comfortable and familiar to him, and to set out instead for a land that God had not yet revealed. Along with this instruction came an extraordinary promise: God would transform Abram into a great and mighty nation, bless him abundantly, make his name renowned among the nations, and, most remarkably of all, use him as a source of blessing for every family on the face of the earth. It was a staggering act of trust that God asked of him, since Abram was given no map, no destination, and no timeline — only the assurance that God himself would reveal the way, one step at a time, as Abram began to move forward in obedience.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Abram</li><li>Sarai</li><li>the altars</li><li>Canaan</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Abram packs up and leaves home</li><li>Abram builds altars along the way</li><li>Abram calls on God’s name</li><li>Abram and his family arrive in Canaan</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>even though he was already seventy-five years old</li><li>he packed up everything he owned</li><li>a country he had never seen</li><li>to worship God at different places he stopped</li><li>calling on God’s name</li><li>trusting that he was being led in the right direction</li><li>the very place God had been leading them toward all along</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Abram took his family and set out for ___, building ___ along the way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Abram obeyed, even though he was already old. He took his wife Sarai and his nephew Lot. They packed up everything and left their home. Abram did not know exactly where he was going. Along the way, Abram built altars to worship God. At last, Abram and his family arrived in the land of Canaan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So Abram obeyed, even though he was already seventy-five years old. He packed up everything he owned, took his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, and all of their servants and animals, and set out for a country he had never seen. Along the way, Abram built altars to worship God at different places he stopped, calling on God’s name and trusting that he was being led in the right direction. Eventually, Abram and his family arrived safely in the land of Canaan, the very place God had been leading them toward all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, Abram obeyed without hesitation, even though he was already seventy-five years old when this call came to him. He gathered his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, and the full household of servants, animals, and possessions they had accumulated, and set out toward a country he had never once laid eyes on. As they travelled, Abram paused at several points along the way to build altars and worship God there, calling on God’s name aloud and trusting, step by step, that he was being guided toward wherever he was meant to go. In time, after a long and uncertain journey, Abram and his household arrived safely in the land of Canaan, the very destination God had been quietly leading them toward the entire time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Abram</li><li>God</li><li>the stars</li><li>Canaan</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God speaks to Abram again in Canaan</li><li>God tells Abram to count the stars</li><li>God gives Abram a new name, Abraham</li><li>Abraham believes God’s promise</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>once Abram had settled in Canaan</li><li>confirmed his promise</li><li>count the stars if he could</li><li>even though he and Sarai had no children yet</li><li>meaning ‘father of many’</li><li>as a sign of this promise</li><li>even though it seemed impossible at the time</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told Abram to count the ___, and gave him a new name: ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke to Abram again in Canaan. He told Abram to look up at the stars. God said Abram would have that many children one day. This seemed impossible, because Abram had no children yet. God gave Abram a new name: Abraham. It meant ‘father of many.’ Abraham believed God’s promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once Abram had settled in Canaan, God spoke to him again and confirmed his promise. God told Abram to look up at the night sky and count the stars if he could — that was how many descendants Abram would one day have, even though he and Sarai had no children yet. God also gave Abram a new name, Abraham, meaning ‘father of many,’ as a sign of this promise. Abraham believed God, trusting that this promise would somehow come true, even though it seemed impossible at the time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once Abram and his family had settled into their new surroundings in Canaan, God appeared to him once more and reaffirmed the promise he had made. He led Abram outside on a clear night and told him to gaze up at the vast, star-filled sky, challenging him to try counting them if he could — for that, God said, was the almost unimaginable number of descendants Abram would one day have, despite the fact that he and Sarai remained childless at this point in their lives. As a further sign of this covenant, God changed Abram’s name to Abraham, meaning ‘father of many nations,’ a new identity that reflected the promise itself. Remarkably, Abraham chose to believe God’s word, holding onto this promise with unwavering trust even though, by every earthly measure, it still seemed entirely impossible.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Abraham’s Call &amp; Journey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 3 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 3 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 4 (Joseph’s Coat & Dreams) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> God told ___ to leave his home and go to ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Abram took his family and set out for ___, building ___ along the way.</li><li><b>The End:</b> God told Abram to count the ___, and gave him a new name: ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 4: Joseph’s Coat & Dreams":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 4’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 4: Joseph’s Coat & Dreams) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Joseph and Jacob as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>Jacob</li><li>the coat</li><li>the wheat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jacob gives Joseph a colourful coat</li><li>Joseph’s brothers grow jealous</li><li>Joseph dreams of the bundles of wheat bowing</li><li>Joseph dreams of the sun, moon and stars bowing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jacob loved Joseph more than his other sons</li><li>Joseph’s brothers were happy about the coat</li><li>In Joseph’s first dream, the sun and moon bowed to him</li><li>Joseph told his brothers about his dreams</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob had twelve sons. He loved Joseph the most of all of them. Jacob gave Joseph a beautiful, colourful coat. His brothers saw that their father loved Joseph best. This made them jealous. They could not speak kindly to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, Joseph had a dream. He told his brothers about it. In the dream, they were tying up wheat in the fields. Joseph’s bundle stood up straight. His brothers’ bundles bowed down to it. His brothers were very angry. They asked, ‘Do you think you will rule over us?’ They hated him even more.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph had a second dream. This time he told his father too. In the dream, the sun, the moon, and eleven stars bowed down to him. His father asked, ‘Will we really bow down to you?’ Joseph’s brothers grew even more jealous. His father wondered what the dreams meant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob had twelve sons, but he loved Joseph more than any of the others, because Joseph had been born to him in his old age. To show his love, Jacob gave Joseph a beautiful, richly coloured coat, unlike anything his other sons had. When Joseph’s brothers saw how much their father favoured him, they grew jealous and bitter, and they could not bring themselves to speak a kind word to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, Joseph had a dream and eagerly told his brothers about it. He dreamed that they were all out in the fields tying up bundles of wheat, and his bundle stood straight up while all of his brothers’ bundles gathered around and bowed down to it. His brothers were furious. ‘Do you really think you will rule over us?’ they said, and they hated him even more because of his dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then Joseph had a second dream, and this time he told both his brothers and his father. He dreamed that the sun, the moon, and eleven stars all bowed down to him. Even his father was troubled by this and asked, ‘Will your mother and I and your brothers really come and bow down to you?’ Joseph’s brothers grew even more jealous, while his father quietly wondered what these dreams could possibly mean.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Among his twelve sons, Jacob loved Joseph more deeply than any of the others, largely because Joseph had been born to him later in life, in his old age, making him especially precious. To make his favouritism unmistakably clear, Jacob presented Joseph with an elaborate, richly ornamented coat, finer than anything given to his brothers. Watching this unfold, Joseph’s brothers grew increasingly resentful, and their jealousy ran so deep that they found themselves unable to speak even a single kind word to him, no matter the occasion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one night, Joseph experienced a vivid dream, and, with characteristic eagerness, he hurried to share every detail of it with his brothers. In this dream, he and his brothers were out in the fields together binding sheaves of wheat, when suddenly his own sheaf rose upright, while each of his brothers’ sheaves gathered around it and bowed low before it. Outraged by the implication, his brothers snapped back at him, ‘Do you actually believe you are going to reign over us?’ and from that moment on, their hatred toward him grew sharper still, fuelled entirely by this unsettling dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, Joseph had yet another dream, and this time he made sure to recount it not only to his brothers but to his father as well. In this second dream, he saw the sun, the moon, and eleven stars all bowing down before him in a gesture of deep reverence. Even Jacob, upon hearing this, felt a flicker of unease and rebuked him gently, asking, ‘Are your mother and I, along with your brothers, truly going to come and bow down to the ground before you?’ While Joseph’s brothers seethed with even greater jealousy at this second dream, his father, though outwardly doubtful, privately turned the matter over in his mind, wondering quietly what it could possibly signify.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jacob</li><li>Joseph</li><li>the coat</li><li>the brothers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jacob loves Joseph more than his other sons</li><li>Jacob gives Joseph a colourful coat</li><li>The brothers see how much their father favours Joseph</li><li>The brothers grow jealous and bitter</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>because Joseph had been born to him in his old age</li><li>to show his love</li><li>richly coloured coat</li><li>unlike anything his other sons had</li><li>how much their father favoured him</li><li>they grew jealous and bitter</li><li>speak a kind word to him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob gave Joseph a ___, and his brothers grew ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob had twelve sons. He loved Joseph the most of all of them. Jacob gave Joseph a beautiful, colourful coat. His brothers saw that their father loved Joseph best. This made them jealous. They could not speak kindly to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jacob had twelve sons, but he loved Joseph more than any of the others, because Joseph had been born to him in his old age. To show his love, Jacob gave Joseph a beautiful, richly coloured coat, unlike anything his other sons had. When Joseph’s brothers saw how much their father favoured him, they grew jealous and bitter, and they could not bring themselves to speak a kind word to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Among his twelve sons, Jacob loved Joseph more deeply than any of the others, largely because Joseph had been born to him later in life, in his old age, making him especially precious. To make his favouritism unmistakably clear, Jacob presented Joseph with an elaborate, richly ornamented coat, finer than anything given to his brothers. Watching this unfold, Joseph’s brothers grew increasingly resentful, and their jealousy ran so deep that they found themselves unable to speak even a single kind word to him, no matter the occasion.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>the brothers</li><li>the wheat</li><li>the fields</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph has a dream about tying up wheat</li><li>Joseph’s bundle stands up straight</li><li>His brothers’ bundles bow down to it</li><li>The brothers grow angry at Joseph</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>one night</li><li>eagerly told his brothers about it</li><li>tying up bundles of wheat</li><li>stood straight up</li><li>gathered around and bowed down to it</li><li>do you really think you will rule over us</li><li>hated him even more because of his dream</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph dreamed that his brothers’ ___ bowed down to his, and they grew ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, Joseph had a dream. He told his brothers about it. In the dream, they were tying up wheat in the fields. Joseph’s bundle stood up straight. His brothers’ bundles bowed down to it. His brothers were very angry. They asked, ‘Do you think you will rule over us?’ They hated him even more.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, Joseph had a dream and eagerly told his brothers about it. He dreamed that they were all out in the fields tying up bundles of wheat, and his bundle stood straight up while all of his brothers’ bundles gathered around and bowed down to it. His brothers were furious. ‘Do you really think you will rule over us?’ they said, and they hated him even more because of his dream.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one night, Joseph experienced a vivid dream, and, with characteristic eagerness, he hurried to share every detail of it with his brothers. In this dream, he and his brothers were out in the fields together binding sheaves of wheat, when suddenly his own sheaf rose upright, while each of his brothers’ sheaves gathered around it and bowed low before it. Outraged by the implication, his brothers snapped back at him, ‘Do you actually believe you are going to reign over us?’ and from that moment on, their hatred toward him grew sharper still, fuelled entirely by this unsettling dream.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>Jacob</li><li>the sun</li><li>the stars</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph has a second dream</li><li>The sun, moon and stars bow down to him</li><li>Joseph tells his brothers and his father</li><li>Jacob wonders what the dreams mean</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>this time he told both his brothers and his father</li><li>the sun, the moon, and eleven stars</li><li>even his father was troubled by this</li><li>will your mother and I and your brothers really</li><li>grew even more jealous</li><li>quietly wondered</li><li>what these dreams could possibly mean</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Joseph’s second dream, the ___ bowed down to him, and his father ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph had a second dream. This time he told his father too. In the dream, the sun, the moon, and eleven stars bowed down to him. His father asked, ‘Will we really bow down to you?’ Joseph’s brothers grew even more jealous. His father wondered what the dreams meant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then Joseph had a second dream, and this time he told both his brothers and his father. He dreamed that the sun, the moon, and eleven stars all bowed down to him. Even his father was troubled by this and asked, ‘Will your mother and I and your brothers really come and bow down to you?’ Joseph’s brothers grew even more jealous, while his father quietly wondered what these dreams could possibly mean.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, Joseph had yet another dream, and this time he made sure to recount it not only to his brothers but to his father as well. In this second dream, he saw the sun, the moon, and eleven stars all bowing down before him in a gesture of deep reverence. Even Jacob, upon hearing this, felt a flicker of unease and rebuked him gently, asking, ‘Are your mother and I, along with your brothers, truly going to come and bow down to the ground before you?’ While Joseph’s brothers seethed with even greater jealousy at this second dream, his father, though outwardly doubtful, privately turned the matter over in his mind, wondering quietly what it could possibly signify.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Joseph’s Coat &amp; Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 4 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 4 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 5 (Joseph Forgives His Brothers) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> Jacob gave Joseph a ___, and his brothers grew ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Joseph dreamed that his brothers’ ___ bowed down to his, and they grew ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> In Joseph’s second dream, the ___ bowed down to him, and his father ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 5’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Joseph and the brothers as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>the brothers</li><li>Egypt</li><li>the famine</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A famine spreads across the land</li><li>Joseph’s brothers travel to Egypt for grain</li><li>Joseph reveals who he is</li><li>Joseph forgives his brothers and invites them to Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph became a powerful ruler in Egypt</li><li>Joseph’s brothers recognised him right away</li><li>Joseph wanted revenge on his brothers</li><li>Joseph invited his family to live in Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years went by, and Joseph became a powerful ruler in Egypt. A terrible famine came, and food was hard to find. Joseph’s brothers travelled to Egypt to buy grain. They bowed down before Joseph, just like his dreams had said. But they did not know it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph tested his brothers for a while. Then he could not hold back any longer. He sent everyone else away. He told them the truth: ‘I am Joseph, your brother.’ His brothers were afraid. They thought he would want revenge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Joseph was kind to them. He said, ‘Don’t be afraid. You meant to hurt me, but God used it for good.’ Joseph forgave his brothers completely. He invited his whole family to come live in Egypt. The family was together again at last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years passed, and Joseph — who had once been mistreated by his own brothers — rose to become a powerful ruler in Egypt, second only to Pharaoh himself. When a terrible famine spread across the land, Joseph’s brothers travelled all the way to Egypt to buy grain, because they had heard that food could still be found there. They stood before Joseph and bowed down, just as his childhood dreams had once foretold, but they had no idea that the powerful Egyptian official in front of them was their own brother.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After testing them for a while to see if they had truly changed, Joseph could no longer hold back his emotions. He sent everyone else out of the room and revealed the truth to them: ‘I am Joseph, your brother, the one you sold!’ His brothers were terrified, certain that he would now take revenge on them for what they had done to him so many years before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Joseph reassured them, weeping as he spoke. ‘Don’t be afraid,’ he told them. ‘You intended to harm me, but God used it for good, to save many lives.’ He forgave his brothers completely and invited his whole family, including his elderly father Jacob, to come and live safely in Egypt, where there was still plenty of food. Reunited at last, the family that had once been torn apart by jealousy was brought back together again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years had passed since Joseph’s brothers had wronged him, and in that time Joseph had risen from hardship to remarkable power, eventually becoming the second most powerful man in all of Egypt, answering only to Pharaoh himself. When a severe famine spread across the entire region, leaving food scarce almost everywhere, Joseph’s brothers made the long journey to Egypt, having heard that grain could still be found there. Standing before this powerful Egyptian official to request food, they bowed low to the ground before him, unknowingly fulfilling the very dreams Joseph had described to them so many years earlier, all without the faintest idea that the man before them was the brother they had once wronged.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For a time, Joseph tested his brothers, observing them carefully to see whether they had truly changed since the days of their jealousy and cruelty. Eventually, however, he could no longer contain the overwhelming emotion building within him. He ordered everyone else to leave the room, and, alone with his brothers at last, he revealed the truth in a voice thick with feeling: ‘I am Joseph, your brother — the one you sold into slavery.’ His brothers were struck with terror, utterly convinced that he intended to punish them severely for the harm they had inflicted on him so long ago.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yet, rather than seeking revenge, Joseph wept openly and spoke to comfort them. ‘Do not be afraid or distressed,’ he told them gently. ‘Although you intended to harm me, God transformed your actions into something good, using them to preserve the lives of many people during this famine.’ With genuine warmth, Joseph forgave his brothers completely, releasing any bitterness he might have carried, and he invited his entire family, including his elderly father Jacob, to relocate to Egypt, where they would be safe and well provided for during the ongoing famine. In this way, a family once fractured by envy and betrayal was, at long last, joyfully and fully reunited.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>the brothers</li><li>Egypt</li><li>the grain</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph becomes a powerful ruler in Egypt</li><li>A terrible famine spreads across the land</li><li>Joseph’s brothers travel to Egypt for grain</li><li>The brothers bow down without recognising Joseph</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>many years passed</li><li>who had once been mistreated by his own brothers</li><li>second only to Pharaoh himself</li><li>because they had heard that food could still be found there</li><li>just as his childhood dreams had once foretold</li><li>the powerful Egyptian official in front of them</li><li>they had no idea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph became a powerful ruler, and his brothers travelled to Egypt because of the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years went by, and Joseph became a powerful ruler in Egypt. A terrible famine came, and food was hard to find. Joseph’s brothers travelled to Egypt to buy grain. They bowed down before Joseph, just like his dreams had said. But they did not know it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years passed, and Joseph — who had once been mistreated by his own brothers — rose to become a powerful ruler in Egypt, second only to Pharaoh himself. When a terrible famine spread across the land, Joseph’s brothers travelled all the way to Egypt to buy grain, because they had heard that food could still be found there. They stood before Joseph and bowed down, just as his childhood dreams had once foretold, but they had no idea that the powerful Egyptian official in front of them was their own brother.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years had passed since Joseph’s brothers had wronged him, and in that time Joseph had risen from hardship to remarkable power, eventually becoming the second most powerful man in all of Egypt, answering only to Pharaoh himself. When a severe famine spread across the entire region, leaving food scarce almost everywhere, Joseph’s brothers made the long journey to Egypt, having heard that grain could still be found there. Standing before this powerful Egyptian official to request food, they bowed low to the ground before him, unknowingly fulfilling the very dreams Joseph had described to them so many years earlier, all without the faintest idea that the man before them was the brother they had once wronged.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>the brothers</li><li>the room</li><li>Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph tests his brothers</li><li>Joseph sends everyone else out of the room</li><li>Joseph reveals who he is</li><li>The brothers are terrified</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>for a while</li><li>to see if they had truly changed</li><li>could no longer hold back his emotions</li><li>sent everyone else out of the room</li><li>revealed the truth to them</li><li>certain that he would now take revenge</li><li>so many years before</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph told his brothers, ‘I am ___,’ and they felt ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph tested his brothers for a while. Then he could not hold back any longer. He sent everyone else away. He told them the truth: ‘I am Joseph, your brother.’ His brothers were afraid. They thought he would want revenge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After testing them for a while to see if they had truly changed, Joseph could no longer hold back his emotions. He sent everyone else out of the room and revealed the truth to them: ‘I am Joseph, your brother, the one you sold!’ His brothers were terrified, certain that he would now take revenge on them for what they had done to him so many years before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For a time, Joseph tested his brothers, observing them carefully to see whether they had truly changed since the days of their jealousy and cruelty. Eventually, however, he could no longer contain the overwhelming emotion building within him. He ordered everyone else to leave the room, and, alone with his brothers at last, he revealed the truth in a voice thick with feeling: ‘I am Joseph, your brother — the one you sold into slavery.’ His brothers were struck with terror, utterly convinced that he intended to punish them severely for the harm they had inflicted on him so long ago.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>Jacob</li><li>Egypt</li><li>the food</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph reassures his brothers</li><li>Joseph forgives his brothers</li><li>Joseph invites the family to Egypt</li><li>The family is reunited</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>weeping as he spoke</li><li>you intended to harm me</li><li>to save many lives</li><li>forgave his brothers completely</li><li>including his elderly father Jacob</li><li>where there was still plenty of food</li><li>torn apart by jealousy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph ___ his brothers, and invited his family to live in ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Joseph was kind to them. He said, ‘Don’t be afraid. You meant to hurt me, but God used it for good.’ Joseph forgave his brothers completely. He invited his whole family to come live in Egypt. The family was together again at last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But Joseph reassured them, weeping as he spoke. ‘Don’t be afraid,’ he told them. ‘You intended to harm me, but God used it for good, to save many lives.’ He forgave his brothers completely and invited his whole family, including his elderly father Jacob, to come and live safely in Egypt, where there was still plenty of food. Reunited at last, the family that had once been torn apart by jealousy was brought back together again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Yet, rather than seeking revenge, Joseph wept openly and spoke to comfort them. ‘Do not be afraid or distressed,’ he told them gently. ‘Although you intended to harm me, God transformed your actions into something good, using them to preserve the lives of many people during this famine.’ With genuine warmth, Joseph forgave his brothers completely, releasing any bitterness he might have carried, and he invited his entire family, including his elderly father Jacob, to relocate to Egypt, where they would be safe and well provided for during the ongoing famine. In this way, a family once fractured by envy and betrayal was, at long last, joyfully and fully reunited.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Joseph Forgives His Brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 5 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 5 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 6 (Baby Moses in the Basket) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> Joseph became a powerful ruler, and his brothers travelled to Egypt because of the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Joseph told his brothers, ‘I am ___,’ and they felt ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Joseph ___ his brothers, and invited his family to live in ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (Yellow belt) — Stories 6–10":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 6’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Moses and Jochebed as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>Jochebed</li><li>the Nile River</li><li>the basket</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh orders that Hebrew baby boys be thrown into the Nile</li><li>Jochebed hides Moses in a waterproof basket among the reeds</li><li>Pharaoh's daughter finds the basket and decides to keep the baby</li><li>Miriam arranges for Jochebed to nurse her own son, and he is later adopted as Moses</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jochebed placed baby Moses in a basket among the reeds of the Nile</li><li>Pharaoh's daughter found the basket and decided to throw it back into the river</li><li>Miriam suggested finding a nurse for the baby, and secretly brought her own mother</li><li>Moses grew up as a slave, hidden away his whole childhood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel lived in Egypt, and their numbers grew very large. The King of Egypt, called Pharaoh, became afraid of them and gave a cruel order: every baby boy born to the Israelites must be thrown into the Nile River. A woman named Jochebed had a baby boy, and she loved him very much. She hid him at home for three months, but soon he grew too big to hide any longer. Jochebed made a small basket out of reeds and covered it with tar to keep out the water. She placed her baby gently inside and set the basket among the reeds at the edge of the Nile River. His sister Miriam stood a little way off to watch over him and see what would happen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon, Pharaoh's daughter came down to the river to bathe. She noticed the basket among the reeds and sent her servant to bring it to her. When she opened it, she saw the baby crying, and her heart was filled with pity for him. She knew at once that he must be one of the Israelite babies, but she decided to keep him safe anyway. Miriam, who had been watching nearby, bravely stepped forward and asked the princess if she would like a nurse to care for the baby. Pharaoh's daughter agreed, and Miriam ran to fetch her own mother, Jochebed, without the princess ever knowing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jochebed was overjoyed to be given her own son to care for and raise until he was older. When the boy grew, she brought him back to Pharaoh's daughter, who adopted him as her own son. She named him Moses, because she had drawn him out of the water. Moses grew up in the royal palace of Egypt, safe and cared for, even though he was born an Israelite. Years later, Moses would grow up to lead his people out of slavery in Egypt, but it all began with his mother's brave decision to trust God and place him safely in a basket on the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had settled in the land of Egypt, and over many years their numbers grew large and strong. This worried the King of Egypt, known as Pharaoh, who feared the Israelites might one day rise up against him. To keep them weak, Pharaoh gave a cruel and frightening command: every baby boy born to the Israelites was to be thrown into the Nile River. During this dangerous time, a woman named Jochebed gave birth to a healthy baby boy. Determined to protect him, she hid him in her home for three months, but eventually he grew too big and noisy to keep hidden any longer. Thinking quickly, Jochebed wove a small basket out of papyrus reeds and coated it carefully with tar and pitch to make it waterproof. She placed her precious baby inside the basket and gently set it among the tall reeds growing at the edge of the Nile River, trusting that he would somehow be kept safe. His older sister, Miriam, stood a little way off along the riverbank, watching closely to see what would happen to her baby brother.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, Pharaoh's own daughter came down to the riverbank with her attendants to bathe in the Nile. As she walked along the water's edge, she spotted the small basket hidden among the reeds and sent one of her servant girls to fetch it for her. When the princess opened the basket, she found a crying baby inside, and her heart was immediately filled with compassion for him. She realised straight away that this must be one of the Hebrew babies her father had ordered to be destroyed, yet she chose to protect him rather than obey the harsh command. Seeing her chance, Miriam, who had been watching from a distance the whole time, stepped forward bravely and asked the princess whether she would like her to find a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby for her. The princess agreed at once, and Miriam ran quickly to bring her own mother, Jochebed, without the princess ever suspecting the connection between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jochebed was overjoyed beyond words to be reunited with her son and to be paid to care for and nurse her own baby until he grew older. When Moses was old enough, Jochebed brought him back to Pharaoh's daughter, who formally adopted him and raised him as her own son within the royal palace. She named the boy Moses, explaining that she had drawn him out of the water. From that day on, Moses grew up safely inside the palace of Egypt, educated and cared for as royalty, even though he had been born a Hebrew slave. Many years later, this same boy would grow into the man God would call to lead the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt — but the entire story began with one mother's courage and trust in placing her baby safely into a small basket on the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, many generations after Jacob's family had first settled in Egypt during a famine, the people of Israel had grown into a large and increasingly powerful nation living within Egypt's borders. This troubled the reigning Pharaoh greatly, for he feared that in the event of war, the Israelites might join Egypt's enemies and fight against him. Determined to weaken them, Pharaoh issued a merciless decree: every baby boy born to the Hebrews was to be thrown into the waters of the Nile River and drowned. It was during this perilous and frightening time that a Hebrew woman named Jochebed gave birth to a healthy son. Refusing to accept her son's fate, she hid him carefully within her home for three long months, feeding him in secret and shielding him from prying eyes. Eventually, however, the baby grew too large and too loud to remain hidden any longer, and Jochebed knew she needed another plan. Thinking with remarkable courage and ingenuity, she wove together a small, sturdy basket from papyrus reeds and sealed every gap carefully with tar and pitch, making it completely waterproof. Wrapping her baby snugly, she placed him gently inside the little vessel and set it afloat among the tall reeds growing along the edge of the Nile, trusting that somehow, someone would find and protect him. Nearby, hidden among the tall grasses along the riverbank, her young daughter Miriam kept careful watch, determined to see exactly what would happen to her baby brother.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, as fate would have it, Pharaoh's own daughter came walking down to that very stretch of riverbank, accompanied by her attendants, intending to bathe in the cool waters of the Nile. As she strolled along the water's edge, her eye was caught by a small basket bobbing gently among the reeds, and, curious, she sent one of her servant girls wading in to retrieve it. When the princess lifted the lid and peered inside, she discovered a crying baby boy, and immediately her heart softened with deep compassion for the helpless child. Though she quickly understood that this must be one of the very Hebrew infants her own father had ordered destroyed, she made up her mind then and there to protect and save him regardless of the risk. Seizing her opportunity, Miriam, who had been watching every moment from a careful distance, stepped forward with remarkable boldness and respectfully asked the princess whether she might like her to find a Hebrew nursemaid to care for the child on her behalf. The unsuspecting princess agreed immediately, and Miriam raced home as fast as she could to fetch none other than her own mother, Jochebed, all without the princess ever realising the truth of the connection between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To Jochebed's overwhelming joy, she found herself not only reunited with her precious son but even paid by the Egyptian princess herself to nurse and raise her own baby for the earliest years of his life. When Moses had grown old enough, Jochebed brought him, as promised, back to Pharaoh's daughter, who formally welcomed him into the royal household and raised him from that day forward as her very own son, right within the walls of Pharaoh's palace. She gave him the name Moses, explaining that she had drawn him safely out of the water. And so it was that Moses spent his childhood and youth growing up in extraordinary privilege and comfort inside the Egyptian royal court, educated among princes, even though he had been born into slavery as a Hebrew. Little did anyone know that this same rescued child, drawn from the reeds of the Nile by his mother's daring faith, would one day grow into the very man God would call upon to lead his entire enslaved nation to freedom out of the land of Egypt.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jochebed</li><li>Moses</li><li>Pharaoh</li><li>the basket</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh orders that Hebrew baby boys be thrown into the river</li><li>Jochebed hides her baby for three months</li><li>She makes a waterproof basket from reeds</li><li>She places Moses in the basket among the reeds by the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>their numbers grew very large</li><li>afraid of them</li><li>gave a cruel order</li><li>covered it with tar to keep out the water</li><li>stood a little way off to watch over him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Pharaoh ordered that ___ be thrown into the river, so ___ made a waterproof ___ to keep her baby safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel lived in Egypt, and their numbers grew very large. The King of Egypt, called Pharaoh, became afraid of them and gave a cruel order: every baby boy born to the Israelites must be thrown into the Nile River. A woman named Jochebed had a baby boy, and she loved him very much. She hid him at home for three months, but soon he grew too big to hide any longer. Jochebed made a small basket out of reeds and covered it with tar to keep out the water. She placed her baby gently inside and set the basket among the reeds at the edge of the Nile River. His sister Miriam stood a little way off to watch over him and see what would happen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had settled in the land of Egypt, and over many years their numbers grew large and strong. This worried the King of Egypt, known as Pharaoh, who feared the Israelites might one day rise up against him. To keep them weak, Pharaoh gave a cruel and frightening command: every baby boy born to the Israelites was to be thrown into the Nile River. During this dangerous time, a woman named Jochebed gave birth to a healthy baby boy. Determined to protect him, she hid him in her home for three months, but eventually he grew too big and noisy to keep hidden any longer. Thinking quickly, Jochebed wove a small basket out of papyrus reeds and coated it carefully with tar and pitch to make it waterproof. She placed her precious baby inside the basket and gently set it among the tall reeds growing at the edge of the Nile River, trusting that he would somehow be kept safe. His older sister, Miriam, stood a little way off along the riverbank, watching closely to see what would happen to her baby brother.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, many generations after Jacob's family had first settled in Egypt during a famine, the people of Israel had grown into a large and increasingly powerful nation living within Egypt's borders. This troubled the reigning Pharaoh greatly, for he feared that in the event of war, the Israelites might join Egypt's enemies and fight against him. Determined to weaken them, Pharaoh issued a merciless decree: every baby boy born to the Hebrews was to be thrown into the waters of the Nile River and drowned. It was during this perilous and frightening time that a Hebrew woman named Jochebed gave birth to a healthy son. Refusing to accept her son's fate, she hid him carefully within her home for three long months, feeding him in secret and shielding him from prying eyes. Eventually, however, the baby grew too large and too loud to remain hidden any longer, and Jochebed knew she needed another plan. Thinking with remarkable courage and ingenuity, she wove together a small, sturdy basket from papyrus reeds and sealed every gap carefully with tar and pitch, making it completely waterproof. Wrapping her baby snugly, she placed him gently inside the little vessel and set it afloat among the tall reeds growing along the edge of the Nile, trusting that somehow, someone would find and protect him. Nearby, hidden among the tall grasses along the riverbank, her young daughter Miriam kept careful watch, determined to see exactly what would happen to her baby brother.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh's daughter</li><li>Moses</li><li>Miriam</li><li>the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh's daughter comes to the river to bathe</li><li>She finds the basket and feels sorry for the crying baby</li><li>Miriam offers to find a nurse for the baby</li><li>Miriam brings her own mother, Jochebed, without the princess knowing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>sent her servant to bring it to her</li><li>her heart was filled with pity</li><li>she knew at once that he must be one of the Israelite babies</li><li>bravely stepped forward</li><li>without the princess ever knowing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, ___ found the baby and felt sorry for him, so ___ offered to find a nurse, and secretly brought ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon, Pharaoh's daughter came down to the river to bathe. She noticed the basket among the reeds and sent her servant to bring it to her. When she opened it, she saw the baby crying, and her heart was filled with pity for him. She knew at once that he must be one of the Israelite babies, but she decided to keep him safe anyway. Miriam, who had been watching nearby, bravely stepped forward and asked the princess if she would like a nurse to care for the baby. Pharaoh's daughter agreed, and Miriam ran to fetch her own mother, Jochebed, without the princess ever knowing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, Pharaoh's own daughter came down to the riverbank with her attendants to bathe in the Nile. As she walked along the water's edge, she spotted the small basket hidden among the reeds and sent one of her servant girls to fetch it for her. When the princess opened the basket, she found a crying baby inside, and her heart was immediately filled with compassion for him. She realised straight away that this must be one of the Hebrew babies her father had ordered to be destroyed, yet she chose to protect him rather than obey the harsh command. Seeing her chance, Miriam, who had been watching from a distance the whole time, stepped forward bravely and asked the princess whether she would like her to find a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby for her. The princess agreed at once, and Miriam ran quickly to bring her own mother, Jochebed, without the princess ever suspecting the connection between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, as fate would have it, Pharaoh's own daughter came walking down to that very stretch of riverbank, accompanied by her attendants, intending to bathe in the cool waters of the Nile. As she strolled along the water's edge, her eye was caught by a small basket bobbing gently among the reeds, and, curious, she sent one of her servant girls wading in to retrieve it. When the princess lifted the lid and peered inside, she discovered a crying baby boy, and immediately her heart softened with deep compassion for the helpless child. Though she quickly understood that this must be one of the very Hebrew infants her own father had ordered destroyed, she made up her mind then and there to protect and save him regardless of the risk. Seizing her opportunity, Miriam, who had been watching every moment from a careful distance, stepped forward with remarkable boldness and respectfully asked the princess whether she might like her to find a Hebrew nursemaid to care for the child on her behalf. The unsuspecting princess agreed immediately, and Miriam raced home as fast as she could to fetch none other than her own mother, Jochebed, all without the princess ever realising the truth of the connection between them.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jochebed</li><li>Moses</li><li>Pharaoh's daughter</li><li>the palace</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jochebed nurses and raises Moses while he is young</li><li>She brings him back to Pharaoh's daughter when he is older</li><li>Pharaoh's daughter adopts him and names him Moses</li><li>Moses grows up in the royal palace of Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>overjoyed</li><li>adopted him as her own son</li><li>because she had drawn him out of the water</li><li>safe and cared for</li><li>even though he was born an Israelite</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jochebed raised Moses until he was older, then ___ adopted him and named him ___, and he grew up in the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jochebed was overjoyed to be given her own son to care for and raise until he was older. When the boy grew, she brought him back to Pharaoh's daughter, who adopted him as her own son. She named him Moses, because she had drawn him out of the water. Moses grew up in the royal palace of Egypt, safe and cared for, even though he was born an Israelite. Years later, Moses would grow up to lead his people out of slavery in Egypt, but it all began with his mother's brave decision to trust God and place him safely in a basket on the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jochebed was overjoyed beyond words to be reunited with her son and to be paid to care for and nurse her own baby until he grew older. When Moses was old enough, Jochebed brought him back to Pharaoh's daughter, who formally adopted him and raised him as her own son within the royal palace. She named the boy Moses, explaining that she had drawn him out of the water. From that day on, Moses grew up safely inside the palace of Egypt, educated and cared for as royalty, even though he had been born a Hebrew slave. Many years later, this same boy would grow into the man God would call to lead the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt — but the entire story began with one mother's courage and trust in placing her baby safely into a small basket on the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To Jochebed's overwhelming joy, she found herself not only reunited with her precious son but even paid by the Egyptian princess herself to nurse and raise her own baby for the earliest years of his life. When Moses had grown old enough, Jochebed brought him, as promised, back to Pharaoh's daughter, who formally welcomed him into the royal household and raised him from that day forward as her very own son, right within the walls of Pharaoh's palace. She gave him the name Moses, explaining that she had drawn him safely out of the water. And so it was that Moses spent his childhood and youth growing up in extraordinary privilege and comfort inside the Egyptian royal court, educated among princes, even though he had been born into slavery as a Hebrew. Little did anyone know that this same rescued child, drawn from the reeds of the Nile by his mother's daring faith, would one day grow into the very man God would call upon to lead his entire enslaved nation to freedom out of the land of Egypt.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Baby Moses in the Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 6 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 6 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 7 (Moses & the Burning Bush) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Pharaoh ordered that ___ be thrown into the river, so ___ made a waterproof ___ to keep her baby safe.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, ___ found the baby and felt sorry for him, so ___ offered to find a nurse, and secretly brought ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jochebed raised Moses until he was older, then ___ adopted him and named him ___, and he grew up in the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 7: Moses & the Burning Bush":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 7’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 7: Moses & the Burning Bush) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Moses and God as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>God</li><li>the burning bush</li><li>Mount Horeb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses leads his flock near Mount Horeb and sees a bush on fire that does not burn up</li><li>God calls out to Moses from the bush and tells him the ground is holy</li><li>God tells Moses He has heard the cries of the Israelites in Egypt</li><li>God sends Moses back to Egypt to lead the Israelites out of slavery</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses saw a bush that was on fire but was not burning up</li><li>Moses immediately agreed without any fear or doubt</li><li>God told Moses that the ground he stood on was holy</li><li>God sent Moses to Egypt to become the new Pharaoh</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after Moses grew up, he left Egypt and became a shepherd in the land of Midian, taking care of his father-in-law's flock of sheep. One day, Moses led the flock far into the desert, near a mountain called Horeb, the mountain of God. As he was walking, Moses noticed something very strange. A bush nearby was on fire, but even though the flames kept burning, the bush itself was not being burned up. Moses was amazed, and he said to himself, ‘I must go and see this strange sight, to see why the bush does not burn.’ He walked closer to get a better look at the astonishing fire.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When God saw that Moses had come closer to look, He called out to him from inside the bush, saying, ‘Moses, Moses!’ Moses answered, ‘Here I am.’ God told Moses to take off his sandals, because the ground he was standing on was holy ground. Then God told Moses that He had seen how much the people of Israel were suffering as slaves in Egypt, and that He had heard their cries for help. God said He was going to send Moses back to Egypt to speak to Pharaoh and lead the people of Israel out of slavery and into freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Moses was afraid and unsure of himself. He asked God, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh?’ God promised Moses that He would be with him every step of the way. When Moses asked what he should say if the people asked who had sent him, God told him to say, ‘I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ God also gave Moses the power to perform miraculous signs, so the people would believe that God had truly sent him. Moses obeyed God, gathered his family, and set out on the long journey back to Egypt to free his people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after Moses had fled Egypt following a terrible incident, he settled in the land of Midian, where he married and took up work as a shepherd, tending the flocks belonging to his father-in-law, Jethro. One ordinary day, Moses led the sheep far out into the wilderness, eventually arriving near a mountain known as Horeb, often called the mountain of God. As he walked among the rocky hillsides, Moses suddenly noticed something truly extraordinary: a small bush nearby was engulfed in bright flames, yet remarkably, the bush itself remained completely unharmed and was not being consumed by the fire at all. Astonished and deeply curious, Moses said to himself, ‘I must go over and look more closely at this strange and wonderful sight, to understand why the bush does not burn up.’ Filled with wonder, he began walking toward the flickering flames to investigate further.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as God saw that Moses had turned aside to look more closely, He called out to him from the very midst of the burning bush, saying, ‘Moses! Moses!’ Startled but attentive, Moses immediately answered, ‘Here I am.’ God instructed him to remove his sandals at once, explaining that the ground on which he stood had become holy ground because of God's presence there. God then revealed to Moses that He had clearly seen the terrible suffering of the Israelite people enslaved in Egypt, and that He had heard their desperate cries for help rising up to Him. God declared that He intended to send Moses back to Egypt, to stand before Pharaoh himself and demand that he let the enslaved Israelites go free, leading them out of bondage and into the freedom of a new land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Overwhelmed by the enormity of the task, Moses grew afraid and deeply unsure of his own ability to carry it out. He asked God honestly, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh and lead the Israelites out of Egypt?’ In response, God promised Moses that He would be with him constantly, every step along the way. When Moses wondered aloud what he should say if the Israelites asked him who exactly had sent him, God answered, ‘Tell them, I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ To further strengthen Moses's confidence, God also granted him the power to perform several miraculous signs, so that the people would recognise and believe that God had truly sent him to rescue them. Trusting in God's promise despite his fears, Moses gathered his wife and sons and set out on the long, uncertain journey back toward Egypt to free his enslaved people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after fleeing Egypt as a young man following a violent confrontation, Moses had settled quietly into a new life in the land of Midian, marrying Zipporah and working faithfully as a shepherd, tending the flocks that belonged to his father-in-law, a priest named Jethro. On one seemingly ordinary day, Moses led his flock far out beyond the usual pastures, deep into the wilderness, eventually arriving at the base of a rugged mountain known as Horeb, sometimes referred to as the mountain of God. As he made his way among the rocky hillsides, something extraordinary suddenly caught his attention: a modest bush nearby appeared completely engulfed in bright, crackling flames, and yet, astonishingly, the bush itself remained entirely unharmed, never once being consumed or blackened by the fire. Filled with wonder and disbelief, Moses said quietly to himself, ‘I must go over and see this remarkable sight for myself, and understand why this bush refuses to burn away.’ Overcome with curiosity, he began making his way carefully toward the mysterious, flickering flames to investigate more closely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment God saw that Moses had turned aside to look more closely at the strange sight, He called out to him directly from within the very heart of the burning bush, saying, ‘Moses! Moses!’ Startled yet immediately attentive, Moses answered without hesitation, ‘Here I am.’ God commanded him at once to remove the sandals from his feet, explaining solemnly that the very ground upon which he now stood had become holy ground because of God's own presence there. God then revealed to Moses that He had watched closely and seen the terrible, ongoing suffering endured by the enslaved Israelite people in Egypt, and that He had clearly heard their desperate cries rising up to Him for deliverance. With great authority, God declared His firm intention to send Moses back into Egypt, to stand boldly before Pharaoh himself and demand, in God's name, that he release the enslaved Israelites from their bondage, leading them out toward freedom in a promised new land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Overwhelmed by the sheer weight and enormity of such an impossible-seeming task, Moses grew deeply afraid and painfully unsure of his own worthiness or ability to carry it out successfully. Honestly and humbly, he asked God, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh and somehow lead the entire nation of Israel out of Egypt?’ In response, God offered him a powerful and reassuring promise: that He Himself would be with Moses constantly, guiding and protecting him every single step of the way. When Moses wondered aloud what he ought to say if the Israelites questioned him about exactly who had sent him on this mission, God answered simply yet profoundly, ‘Tell them: I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ To further strengthen Moses's wavering confidence, God graciously granted him the power to perform several miraculous, undeniable signs, ensuring that the people would recognise beyond doubt that God Himself had truly sent him to rescue them. Trusting deeply in God's unwavering promise despite his own lingering fears and doubts, Moses gathered together his wife and his sons, and at last set out on the long and uncertain journey back toward Egypt, determined to free his enslaved people at last.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>the sheep</li><li>the bush</li><li>Mount Horeb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses is a shepherd looking after his father-in-law's flock</li><li>He leads the flock near Mount Horeb</li><li>He sees a bush that is on fire but is not burning up</li><li>Moses decides to go closer and look at the strange sight</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>became a shepherd in the land of Midian</li><li>taking care of his father-in-law's flock</li><li>the mountain of God</li><li>Moses was amazed</li><li>to see why the bush does not burn</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Moses was looking after ___ near ___, when he saw a ___ that would not burn up.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after Moses grew up, he left Egypt and became a shepherd in the land of Midian, taking care of his father-in-law's flock of sheep. One day, Moses led the flock far into the desert, near a mountain called Horeb, the mountain of God. As he was walking, Moses noticed something very strange. A bush nearby was on fire, but even though the flames kept burning, the bush itself was not being burned up. Moses was amazed, and he said to himself, ‘I must go and see this strange sight, to see why the bush does not burn.’ He walked closer to get a better look at the astonishing fire.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after Moses had fled Egypt following a terrible incident, he settled in the land of Midian, where he married and took up work as a shepherd, tending the flocks belonging to his father-in-law, Jethro. One ordinary day, Moses led the sheep far out into the wilderness, eventually arriving near a mountain known as Horeb, often called the mountain of God. As he walked among the rocky hillsides, Moses suddenly noticed something truly extraordinary: a small bush nearby was engulfed in bright flames, yet remarkably, the bush itself remained completely unharmed and was not being consumed by the fire at all. Astonished and deeply curious, Moses said to himself, ‘I must go over and look more closely at this strange and wonderful sight, to understand why the bush does not burn up.’ Filled with wonder, he began walking toward the flickering flames to investigate further.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after fleeing Egypt as a young man following a violent confrontation, Moses had settled quietly into a new life in the land of Midian, marrying Zipporah and working faithfully as a shepherd, tending the flocks that belonged to his father-in-law, a priest named Jethro. On one seemingly ordinary day, Moses led his flock far out beyond the usual pastures, deep into the wilderness, eventually arriving at the base of a rugged mountain known as Horeb, sometimes referred to as the mountain of God. As he made his way among the rocky hillsides, something extraordinary suddenly caught his attention: a modest bush nearby appeared completely engulfed in bright, crackling flames, and yet, astonishingly, the bush itself remained entirely unharmed, never once being consumed or blackened by the fire. Filled with wonder and disbelief, Moses said quietly to himself, ‘I must go over and see this remarkable sight for myself, and understand why this bush refuses to burn away.’ Overcome with curiosity, he began making his way carefully toward the mysterious, flickering flames to investigate more closely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>Moses</li><li>the ground</li><li>Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God calls Moses's name from the bush</li><li>God tells Moses to take off his sandals because the ground is holy</li><li>God says He has heard the cries of the Israelites</li><li>God tells Moses He will send him to Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Here I am</li><li>because the ground he was standing on was holy ground</li><li>how much the people of Israel were suffering as slaves</li><li>heard their cries for help</li><li>lead the people of Israel out of slavery and into freedom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, God called to ___ from the bush and said the ___ was holy, then told him He would send him to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When God saw that Moses had come closer to look, He called out to him from inside the bush, saying, ‘Moses, Moses!’ Moses answered, ‘Here I am.’ God told Moses to take off his sandals, because the ground he was standing on was holy ground. Then God told Moses that He had seen how much the people of Israel were suffering as slaves in Egypt, and that He had heard their cries for help. God said He was going to send Moses back to Egypt to speak to Pharaoh and lead the people of Israel out of slavery and into freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as God saw that Moses had turned aside to look more closely, He called out to him from the very midst of the burning bush, saying, ‘Moses! Moses!’ Startled but attentive, Moses immediately answered, ‘Here I am.’ God instructed him to remove his sandals at once, explaining that the ground on which he stood had become holy ground because of God's presence there. God then revealed to Moses that He had clearly seen the terrible suffering of the Israelite people enslaved in Egypt, and that He had heard their desperate cries for help rising up to Him. God declared that He intended to send Moses back to Egypt, to stand before Pharaoh himself and demand that he let the enslaved Israelites go free, leading them out of bondage and into the freedom of a new land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment God saw that Moses had turned aside to look more closely at the strange sight, He called out to him directly from within the very heart of the burning bush, saying, ‘Moses! Moses!’ Startled yet immediately attentive, Moses answered without hesitation, ‘Here I am.’ God commanded him at once to remove the sandals from his feet, explaining solemnly that the very ground upon which he now stood had become holy ground because of God's own presence there. God then revealed to Moses that He had watched closely and seen the terrible, ongoing suffering endured by the enslaved Israelite people in Egypt, and that He had clearly heard their desperate cries rising up to Him for deliverance. With great authority, God declared His firm intention to send Moses back into Egypt, to stand boldly before Pharaoh himself and demand, in God's name, that he release the enslaved Israelites from their bondage, leading them out toward freedom in a promised new land.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>God</li><li>Pharaoh</li><li>Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses asks God who he is to go to Pharaoh</li><li>God promises to be with Moses</li><li>God tells Moses to say 'I AM WHO I AM' sent him</li><li>Moses sets out on the journey back to Egypt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>afraid and unsure of himself</li><li>every step of the way</li><li>if the people asked who had sent him</li><li>the power to perform miraculous signs</li><li>gathered his family</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, God promised to be with ___, told him to say ___ had sent him, and Moses set out for ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Moses was afraid and unsure of himself. He asked God, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh?’ God promised Moses that He would be with him every step of the way. When Moses asked what he should say if the people asked who had sent him, God told him to say, ‘I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ God also gave Moses the power to perform miraculous signs, so the people would believe that God had truly sent him. Moses obeyed God, gathered his family, and set out on the long journey back to Egypt to free his people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Overwhelmed by the enormity of the task, Moses grew afraid and deeply unsure of his own ability to carry it out. He asked God honestly, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh and lead the Israelites out of Egypt?’ In response, God promised Moses that He would be with him constantly, every step along the way. When Moses wondered aloud what he should say if the Israelites asked him who exactly had sent him, God answered, ‘Tell them, I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ To further strengthen Moses's confidence, God also granted him the power to perform several miraculous signs, so that the people would recognise and believe that God had truly sent him to rescue them. Trusting in God's promise despite his fears, Moses gathered his wife and sons and set out on the long, uncertain journey back toward Egypt to free his enslaved people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Overwhelmed by the sheer weight and enormity of such an impossible-seeming task, Moses grew deeply afraid and painfully unsure of his own worthiness or ability to carry it out successfully. Honestly and humbly, he asked God, ‘Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh and somehow lead the entire nation of Israel out of Egypt?’ In response, God offered him a powerful and reassuring promise: that He Himself would be with Moses constantly, guiding and protecting him every single step of the way. When Moses wondered aloud what he ought to say if the Israelites questioned him about exactly who had sent him on this mission, God answered simply yet profoundly, ‘Tell them: I AM WHO I AM has sent me to you.’ To further strengthen Moses's wavering confidence, God graciously granted him the power to perform several miraculous, undeniable signs, ensuring that the people would recognise beyond doubt that God Himself had truly sent him to rescue them. Trusting deeply in God's unwavering promise despite his own lingering fears and doubts, Moses gathered together his wife and his sons, and at last set out on the long and uncertain journey back toward Egypt, determined to free his enslaved people at last.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Moses &amp; the Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 7 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 7 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 8 (Crossing the Red Sea) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Moses was looking after ___ near ___, when he saw a ___ that would not burn up.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, God called to ___ from the bush and said the ___ was holy, then told him He would send him to ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, God promised to be with ___, told him to say ___ had sent him, and Moses set out for ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 8’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Moses and the Israelites as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>Pharaoh's army</li><li>the Red Sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh changes his mind and chases the Israelites with his army</li><li>The Israelites are trapped between the army and the Red Sea</li><li>Moses stretches out his hand and the sea splits into two walls of water</li><li>The Israelites cross safely and the sea closes over Pharaoh's army</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses stretched out his hand and the Red Sea parted</li><li>The Israelites were never afraid because they trusted Moses completely</li><li>The Egyptian army chased the Israelites into the sea</li><li>Some of Pharaoh's soldiers survived by swimming to shore</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many plagues struck Egypt, Pharaoh finally agreed to let the Israelites leave Egypt and go free. Moses led the people out of Egypt, but soon Pharaoh changed his mind. He gathered his army and chariots and chased after the Israelites. The people of Israel found themselves trapped between the Egyptian army behind them and the Red Sea in front of them. The Israelites were terrified and cried out to Moses, wondering if they had only left Egypt to die in the desert. Moses told the people not to be afraid, saying that God would fight for them and save them that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told Moses to raise his staff and stretch out his hand over the sea. When Moses did this, a strong wind blew all night, and the waters of the Red Sea parted, splitting into two walls of water with a dry path in between. The Israelites walked through the middle of the sea on dry ground, with walls of water standing on their left and on their right. Pharaoh's army saw them crossing and chased after them into the sea, riding their chariots between the walls of water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once all the Israelites had safely reached the other side, God told Moses to stretch out his hand again. This time, the walls of water came crashing back down, covering the entire Egyptian army and their chariots completely. Not one of Pharaoh's soldiers survived. The Israelites stood safely on the far shore, watching the sea return to normal. They were amazed by God's great power, and they sang songs of praise and thanks to God for saving them from the Egyptians and leading them safely to freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After a series of devastating plagues had struck the land of Egypt one after another, Pharaoh finally relented and agreed to let the Israelites leave Egypt and travel toward freedom. Moses led the enormous crowd of Israelite people out of Egypt, but it was not long before Pharaoh changed his mind once again, deeply regretting the loss of his slave labour force. Furious, he quickly gathered together his army, complete with hundreds of swift chariots, and set off in pursuit of the fleeing Israelites. Before long, the people of Israel found themselves trapped in a desperate situation, with the powerful Egyptian army closing in rapidly behind them while the wide waters of the Red Sea blocked any escape ahead of them. Overcome with terror, the Israelites cried out anxiously to Moses, questioning whether they had only been led out of Egypt to die helplessly in the desert. Moses, however, calmly reassured the frightened crowd, telling them firmly not to be afraid, because God Himself would fight on their behalf and rescue them that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following God's clear instruction, Moses raised his shepherd's staff high and stretched his hand out directly over the surface of the sea before him. The moment he did this, an extremely powerful wind began blowing steadily throughout the entire night, and miraculously, the waters of the Red Sea split apart completely, forming two enormous walls of water with a wide path of completely dry ground running between them. Without hesitation, the Israelites began walking straight through the middle of the parted sea on dry land, with towering walls of water standing firm on either side of them as they went. Watching this in disbelief, Pharaoh's furious army pressed on in pursuit, driving their chariots directly into the very same path between the walls of water, determined to catch the escaping Israelites before they could reach safety.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once every last Israelite had safely crossed over and reached the opposite shore, God instructed Moses once again to stretch his hand back out over the sea. Immediately, the towering walls of water came crashing down with tremendous force, completely covering the entire pursuing Egyptian army along with their chariots and horses. Not a single one of Pharaoh's soldiers managed to survive the sudden flood. Standing safely on the far shore, the Israelites watched in stunned amazement as the sea returned entirely to its normal, calm state. Overwhelmed with gratitude and awe at God's tremendous power and faithfulness, the people broke out into joyful songs of praise and thanksgiving, celebrating how God had rescued them completely from the Egyptians and led them safely at last into freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After enduring a devastating series of ten terrible plagues that had struck the land of Egypt one after another, each more severe than the last, Pharaoh finally relented completely and agreed to release the enslaved Israelites, allowing Moses to lead them out of Egypt toward long-awaited freedom. Moses gathered the enormous multitude of Israelite men, women, and children and led them out of Egypt at last, but it was not long before Pharaoh, deeply regretting the sudden loss of his entire slave labour force, abruptly changed his mind once again. Consumed with fury and determination, he swiftly assembled his formidable army, including hundreds of swift war chariots, and set off in fierce pursuit of the fleeing Israelites across the desert. Before long, the Israelites found themselves trapped in an utterly desperate and terrifying situation, caught between the rapidly advancing Egyptian army closing in from behind and the vast, impassable waters of the Red Sea blocking any possible escape ahead of them. Overcome with sheer panic and terror, the Israelites cried out bitterly to Moses, questioning in despair whether they had only been led out of slavery in Egypt merely to perish helplessly in the wilderness. Yet Moses remained remarkably calm, firmly reassuring the terrified crowd that they had no reason to fear, for God Himself would fight powerfully on their behalf and deliver them to safety that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Acting exactly as God had instructed him, Moses lifted his shepherd's staff high into the air and stretched his hand directly out over the surface of the sea stretching before them. The instant he did so, an extraordinarily powerful wind began sweeping steadily across the water throughout the entire night, and miraculously, the waters of the Red Sea split completely apart, forming two towering walls of water on either side with a broad, perfectly dry path of solid ground running clearly between them. Without a moment's hesitation, the vast crowd of Israelites began making their way directly through the middle of the parted sea upon dry land, with immense walls of water standing firm and unmoving on their left and right as they walked steadily onward. Watching in stunned disbelief from a distance, Pharaoh's relentless and furious army pressed forward in hot pursuit, boldly driving their chariots directly into that very same narrow pathway between the towering walls of water, utterly determined to catch and capture the escaping Israelites before they could reach safety on the far shore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once every single Israelite man, woman, and child had safely crossed the entire distance and reached the opposite shore unharmed, God instructed Moses once more to stretch his hand back out firmly over the sea before him. In an instant, the enormous, towering walls of water came crashing back down with overwhelming and unstoppable force, completely swallowing and covering the entire pursuing Egyptian army along with every last one of their chariots, horses, and riders. Not a single one of Pharaoh's soldiers managed to survive the sudden and catastrophic flood that engulfed them entirely. Standing safely together on the far shore, the Israelites watched on in stunned, breathless amazement as the mighty sea gradually returned to its calm, ordinary state once again. Overwhelmed with profound gratitude, wonder, and awe at witnessing God's extraordinary power and unwavering faithfulness firsthand, the entire community broke out joyfully into songs of praise and heartfelt thanksgiving, celebrating together how God had rescued them completely and decisively from their Egyptian pursuers and led them safely at last into a future of lasting freedom.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>the army</li><li>the Red Sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pharaoh lets the Israelites leave Egypt</li><li>Pharaoh changes his mind and gathers his army</li><li>The army chases after the Israelites</li><li>The Israelites are trapped between the army and the sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>after many plagues struck Egypt</li><li>finally agreed to let the Israelites leave</li><li>chariots and chased after the Israelites</li><li>terrified and cried out to Moses</li><li>God would fight for them and save them that very day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, ___ chased after the Israelites with his ___, trapping them at the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many plagues struck Egypt, Pharaoh finally agreed to let the Israelites leave Egypt and go free. Moses led the people out of Egypt, but soon Pharaoh changed his mind. He gathered his army and chariots and chased after the Israelites. The people of Israel found themselves trapped between the Egyptian army behind them and the Red Sea in front of them. The Israelites were terrified and cried out to Moses, wondering if they had only left Egypt to die in the desert. Moses told the people not to be afraid, saying that God would fight for them and save them that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After a series of devastating plagues had struck the land of Egypt one after another, Pharaoh finally relented and agreed to let the Israelites leave Egypt and travel toward freedom. Moses led the enormous crowd of Israelite people out of Egypt, but it was not long before Pharaoh changed his mind once again, deeply regretting the loss of his slave labour force. Furious, he quickly gathered together his army, complete with hundreds of swift chariots, and set off in pursuit of the fleeing Israelites. Before long, the people of Israel found themselves trapped in a desperate situation, with the powerful Egyptian army closing in rapidly behind them while the wide waters of the Red Sea blocked any escape ahead of them. Overcome with terror, the Israelites cried out anxiously to Moses, questioning whether they had only been led out of Egypt to die helplessly in the desert. Moses, however, calmly reassured the frightened crowd, telling them firmly not to be afraid, because God Himself would fight on their behalf and rescue them that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After enduring a devastating series of ten terrible plagues that had struck the land of Egypt one after another, each more severe than the last, Pharaoh finally relented completely and agreed to release the enslaved Israelites, allowing Moses to lead them out of Egypt toward long-awaited freedom. Moses gathered the enormous multitude of Israelite men, women, and children and led them out of Egypt at last, but it was not long before Pharaoh, deeply regretting the sudden loss of his entire slave labour force, abruptly changed his mind once again. Consumed with fury and determination, he swiftly assembled his formidable army, including hundreds of swift war chariots, and set off in fierce pursuit of the fleeing Israelites across the desert. Before long, the Israelites found themselves trapped in an utterly desperate and terrifying situation, caught between the rapidly advancing Egyptian army closing in from behind and the vast, impassable waters of the Red Sea blocking any possible escape ahead of them. Overcome with sheer panic and terror, the Israelites cried out bitterly to Moses, questioning in despair whether they had only been led out of slavery in Egypt merely to perish helplessly in the wilderness. Yet Moses remained remarkably calm, firmly reassuring the terrified crowd that they had no reason to fear, for God Himself would fight powerfully on their behalf and deliver them to safety that very day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>the sea</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>Pharaoh's army</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses stretches out his hand over the sea</li><li>A strong wind blows and the sea splits in two</li><li>The Israelites walk through on dry ground</li><li>Pharaoh's army chases them into the sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>raise his staff</li><li>a strong wind blew all night</li><li>walls of water standing on their left and on their right</li><li>chased after them into the sea</li><li>riding their chariots</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, ___ stretched out his hand and the ___ split apart, so the Israelites walked through while ___ chased them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told Moses to raise his staff and stretch out his hand over the sea. When Moses did this, a strong wind blew all night, and the waters of the Red Sea parted, splitting into two walls of water with a dry path in between. The Israelites walked through the middle of the sea on dry ground, with walls of water standing on their left and on their right. Pharaoh's army saw them crossing and chased after them into the sea, riding their chariots between the walls of water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following God's clear instruction, Moses raised his shepherd's staff high and stretched his hand out directly over the surface of the sea before him. The moment he did this, an extremely powerful wind began blowing steadily throughout the entire night, and miraculously, the waters of the Red Sea split apart completely, forming two enormous walls of water with a wide path of completely dry ground running between them. Without hesitation, the Israelites began walking straight through the middle of the parted sea on dry land, with towering walls of water standing firm on either side of them as they went. Watching this in disbelief, Pharaoh's furious army pressed on in pursuit, driving their chariots directly into the very same path between the walls of water, determined to catch the escaping Israelites before they could reach safety.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Acting exactly as God had instructed him, Moses lifted his shepherd's staff high into the air and stretched his hand directly out over the surface of the sea stretching before them. The instant he did so, an extraordinarily powerful wind began sweeping steadily across the water throughout the entire night, and miraculously, the waters of the Red Sea split completely apart, forming two towering walls of water on either side with a broad, perfectly dry path of solid ground running clearly between them. Without a moment's hesitation, the vast crowd of Israelites began making their way directly through the middle of the parted sea upon dry land, with immense walls of water standing firm and unmoving on their left and right as they walked steadily onward. Watching in stunned disbelief from a distance, Pharaoh's relentless and furious army pressed forward in hot pursuit, boldly driving their chariots directly into that very same narrow pathway between the towering walls of water, utterly determined to catch and capture the escaping Israelites before they could reach safety on the far shore.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>the walls of water</li><li>Pharaoh's army</li><li>the Israelites</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>All the Israelites reach the other side safely</li><li>Moses stretches out his hand again</li><li>The walls of water crash down on the Egyptian army</li><li>The Israelites sing songs of praise to God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>once all the Israelites had safely reached the other side</li><li>covering the entire Egyptian army and their chariots completely</li><li>not one of Pharaoh's soldiers survived</li><li>amazed by God's great power</li><li>leading them safely to freedom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the walls of water crashed down on ___, and the ___ sang songs of ___ to God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once all the Israelites had safely reached the other side, God told Moses to stretch out his hand again. This time, the walls of water came crashing back down, covering the entire Egyptian army and their chariots completely. Not one of Pharaoh's soldiers survived. The Israelites stood safely on the far shore, watching the sea return to normal. They were amazed by God's great power, and they sang songs of praise and thanks to God for saving them from the Egyptians and leading them safely to freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once every last Israelite had safely crossed over and reached the opposite shore, God instructed Moses once again to stretch his hand back out over the sea. Immediately, the towering walls of water came crashing down with tremendous force, completely covering the entire pursuing Egyptian army along with their chariots and horses. Not a single one of Pharaoh's soldiers managed to survive the sudden flood. Standing safely on the far shore, the Israelites watched in stunned amazement as the sea returned entirely to its normal, calm state. Overwhelmed with gratitude and awe at God's tremendous power and faithfulness, the people broke out into joyful songs of praise and thanksgiving, celebrating how God had rescued them completely from the Egyptians and led them safely at last into freedom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once every single Israelite man, woman, and child had safely crossed the entire distance and reached the opposite shore unharmed, God instructed Moses once more to stretch his hand back out firmly over the sea before him. In an instant, the enormous, towering walls of water came crashing back down with overwhelming and unstoppable force, completely swallowing and covering the entire pursuing Egyptian army along with every last one of their chariots, horses, and riders. Not a single one of Pharaoh's soldiers managed to survive the sudden and catastrophic flood that engulfed them entirely. Standing safely together on the far shore, the Israelites watched on in stunned, breathless amazement as the mighty sea gradually returned to its calm, ordinary state once again. Overwhelmed with profound gratitude, wonder, and awe at witnessing God's extraordinary power and unwavering faithfulness firsthand, the entire community broke out joyfully into songs of praise and heartfelt thanksgiving, celebrating together how God had rescued them completely and decisively from their Egyptian pursuers and led them safely at last into a future of lasting freedom.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Crossing the Red Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 8 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 8 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 9 (The Ten Commandments) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, ___ chased after the Israelites with his ___, trapping them at the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, ___ stretched out his hand and the ___ split apart, so the Israelites walked through while ___ chased them.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the walls of water crashed down on ___, and the ___ sang songs of ___ to God.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 9: The Ten Commandments":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: The Ten Commandments</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 9’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 9: The Ten Commandments) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Moses and God as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Moses</li><li>God</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>Mount Sinai</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Israelites arrive at Mount Sinai and camp at its base</li><li>Moses climbs the mountain to meet with God</li><li>God gives Moses the Ten Commandments</li><li>Moses comes down and reads the commandments to the people</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God gave Moses ten important commandments for the people to follow</li><li>The Ten Commandments were written on paper scrolls</li><li>The commandments taught the people to love God and treat others fairly</li><li>The people refused to promise to obey the commandments</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After crossing the Red Sea, the Israelites travelled through the desert until they reached Mount Sinai. God told Moses to come up the mountain to meet with Him. The people camped at the bottom of the mountain, while thunder crashed and lightning flashed above the peak, and thick smoke covered the mountain because God had come down onto it in fire. The people trembled with fear at the sight, and they stayed a safe distance away while Moses climbed up the mountain alone to speak with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the mountain, God gave Moses ten important rules for the people to follow, called the Ten Commandments. The commandments taught the people to love and worship only God, to not make idols, to keep the Sabbath day holy, and to honour their parents. The commandments also taught the people not to murder, not to steal, not to lie, and not to be jealous of what belonged to others. God wanted these rules to help His people live good, kind, and honest lives together as a community.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God wrote the Ten Commandments onto two stone tablets and gave them to Moses. When Moses came down from the mountain, he read the commandments to all the people of Israel. The people promised to obey everything that God had commanded them. The Ten Commandments became the foundation of how the Israelites were meant to live, guiding their relationship with God and with one another for many generations to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After their dramatic escape across the parted Red Sea, the Israelites journeyed onward through the harsh desert wilderness for many weeks until they finally arrived at the base of Mount Sinai. There, God called out to Moses, instructing him to climb up the mountain so that they could meet together face to face. The Israelites set up camp at the foot of the mountain, and soon powerful thunder began crashing overhead while brilliant flashes of lightning lit up the sky above the mountain's peak. Thick, heavy smoke completely covered the entire mountain, because God Himself had descended down onto it in a blazing display of fire. Overwhelmed with fear and awe at this terrifying and magnificent sight, the people kept a careful, respectful distance from the mountain, while Moses alone bravely climbed up to meet and speak directly with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While standing on the mountain in God's presence, Moses received ten deeply important rules meant to guide the people's lives, which came to be known as the Ten Commandments. These commandments instructed the people to love and worship God alone, never making or bowing down to any idols in His place, and to faithfully keep the Sabbath day set apart as holy for rest and worship. The commandments also taught the people to honour and respect their fathers and mothers, and firmly established that they must never murder, steal, lie, or covet jealously anything that rightfully belonged to their neighbours. Through these carefully chosen commandments, God intended to help His people live together in a manner that was good, kind, honest, and fair toward one another as a unified community.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God Himself personally wrote out all Ten Commandments onto two flat stone tablets, and then gave them directly into Moses's care. When Moses finally descended from the mountain, he gathered the entire nation of Israel together and read every one of the commandments aloud to them clearly. Moved by what they heard, the people solemnly promised that they would faithfully obey everything that God had commanded of them. From that momentous day onward, the Ten Commandments became the essential foundation for how the Israelites were meant to live their entire lives, carefully guiding both their relationship with God and their relationships with one another for many generations still to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following their dramatic and miraculous escape across the parted waters of the Red Sea, the Israelites continued journeying onward through the harsh and unforgiving desert wilderness for many long weeks, facing hunger, thirst, and uncertainty, until they finally arrived at the base of the towering Mount Sinai. It was there that God called out directly to Moses, instructing him to climb up the mountain alone so that the two of them could meet together face to face in solemn conversation. The enormous camp of Israelites settled at the foot of the mountain, and before long, powerful claps of thunder began crashing dramatically overhead while brilliant, blinding flashes of lightning repeatedly lit up the darkened sky surrounding the mountain's rugged peak. Thick, heavy clouds of smoke completely enveloped the entire mountain, for God Himself had visibly descended down upon it in an awe-inspiring display of consuming fire. Overwhelmed entirely with fear, reverence, and wonder at this terrifying yet magnificent sight unfolding before them, the assembled Israelites carefully kept a safe and respectful distance from the base of the mountain, while Moses alone, showing remarkable courage and faith, climbed steadily upward to meet and converse directly with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Standing there upon the mountain in the direct presence of God, Moses received ten profoundly important laws meant to guide and shape the entire people's way of life, which have since become known throughout history as the Ten Commandments. These sacred commandments carefully instructed the Israelite people to love and worship God alone above all else, strictly forbidding them from ever making or bowing down before any carved idols in His place, and firmly commanding them to faithfully keep the Sabbath day set apart as holy, reserved specifically for rest and worship each week. The commandments likewise taught the people to honour and deeply respect both their father and their mother, and established with absolute clarity that they must never commit murder, steal from one another, tell lies, or enviously covet anything that rightfully belonged to their neighbours. Through these ten carefully chosen and deeply meaningful commandments, God intended to help shape His chosen people into a community capable of living together with genuine goodness, kindness, honesty, and fairness toward one another, generation after generation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With His own hand, God personally inscribed all Ten Commandments onto two solid stone tablets, and then entrusted them directly into Moses's safekeeping to carry back down the mountain. Upon finally descending from the mountain's peak, Moses immediately gathered together the entire assembled nation of Israel and read every single one of the sacred commandments aloud to them with great clarity and solemnity. Deeply moved and humbled by everything they had just heard, the assembled people solemnly and unanimously promised that they would faithfully obey every single commandment that God had given them. From that momentous and unforgettable day forward, the Ten Commandments became the essential and unshakeable foundation upon which the entire nation of Israel was meant to build their lives, carefully guiding both their sacred relationship with God Himself and their everyday relationships with one another for countless generations still to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: The Ten Commandments</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the Israelites</li><li>Mount Sinai</li><li>Moses</li><li>God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Israelites travel through the desert to Mount Sinai</li><li>They camp at the bottom of the mountain</li><li>Thunder, lightning, and smoke cover the mountain</li><li>Moses climbs the mountain alone to meet God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>after crossing the Red Sea</li><li>thunder crashed and lightning flashed above the peak</li><li>thick smoke covered the mountain</li><li>trembled with fear at the sight</li><li>a safe distance away</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the Israelites camped at the bottom of ___ while ___ climbed up to meet ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After crossing the Red Sea, the Israelites travelled through the desert until they reached Mount Sinai. God told Moses to come up the mountain to meet with Him. The people camped at the bottom of the mountain, while thunder crashed and lightning flashed above the peak, and thick smoke covered the mountain because God had come down onto it in fire. The people trembled with fear at the sight, and they stayed a safe distance away while Moses climbed up the mountain alone to speak with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After their dramatic escape across the parted Red Sea, the Israelites journeyed onward through the harsh desert wilderness for many weeks until they finally arrived at the base of Mount Sinai. There, God called out to Moses, instructing him to climb up the mountain so that they could meet together face to face. The Israelites set up camp at the foot of the mountain, and soon powerful thunder began crashing overhead while brilliant flashes of lightning lit up the sky above the mountain's peak. Thick, heavy smoke completely covered the entire mountain, because God Himself had descended down onto it in a blazing display of fire. Overwhelmed with fear and awe at this terrifying and magnificent sight, the people kept a careful, respectful distance from the mountain, while Moses alone bravely climbed up to meet and speak directly with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following their dramatic and miraculous escape across the parted waters of the Red Sea, the Israelites continued journeying onward through the harsh and unforgiving desert wilderness for many long weeks, facing hunger, thirst, and uncertainty, until they finally arrived at the base of the towering Mount Sinai. It was there that God called out directly to Moses, instructing him to climb up the mountain alone so that the two of them could meet together face to face in solemn conversation. The enormous camp of Israelites settled at the foot of the mountain, and before long, powerful claps of thunder began crashing dramatically overhead while brilliant, blinding flashes of lightning repeatedly lit up the darkened sky surrounding the mountain's rugged peak. Thick, heavy clouds of smoke completely enveloped the entire mountain, for God Himself had visibly descended down upon it in an awe-inspiring display of consuming fire. Overwhelmed entirely with fear, reverence, and wonder at this terrifying yet magnificent sight unfolding before them, the assembled Israelites carefully kept a safe and respectful distance from the base of the mountain, while Moses alone, showing remarkable courage and faith, climbed steadily upward to meet and converse directly with God.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: The Ten Commandments</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>Moses</li><li>the Ten Commandments</li><li>the people</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God gives Moses ten important rules</li><li>The rules teach the people to worship only God</li><li>The rules teach honesty and honouring parents</li><li>The rules teach the people not to steal, lie, or be jealous</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>important rules for the people to follow</li><li>to not make idols</li><li>keep the Sabbath day holy</li><li>not to murder, not to steal, not to lie</li><li>good, kind, and honest lives</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, God gave ___ the ___ so that the ___ could live good and honest lives.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the mountain, God gave Moses ten important rules for the people to follow, called the Ten Commandments. The commandments taught the people to love and worship only God, to not make idols, to keep the Sabbath day holy, and to honour their parents. The commandments also taught the people not to murder, not to steal, not to lie, and not to be jealous of what belonged to others. God wanted these rules to help His people live good, kind, and honest lives together as a community.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While standing on the mountain in God's presence, Moses received ten deeply important rules meant to guide the people's lives, which came to be known as the Ten Commandments. These commandments instructed the people to love and worship God alone, never making or bowing down to any idols in His place, and to faithfully keep the Sabbath day set apart as holy for rest and worship. The commandments also taught the people to honour and respect their fathers and mothers, and firmly established that they must never murder, steal, lie, or covet jealously anything that rightfully belonged to their neighbours. Through these carefully chosen commandments, God intended to help His people live together in a manner that was good, kind, honest, and fair toward one another as a unified community.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Standing there upon the mountain in the direct presence of God, Moses received ten profoundly important laws meant to guide and shape the entire people's way of life, which have since become known throughout history as the Ten Commandments. These sacred commandments carefully instructed the Israelite people to love and worship God alone above all else, strictly forbidding them from ever making or bowing down before any carved idols in His place, and firmly commanding them to faithfully keep the Sabbath day set apart as holy, reserved specifically for rest and worship each week. The commandments likewise taught the people to honour and deeply respect both their father and their mother, and established with absolute clarity that they must never commit murder, steal from one another, tell lies, or enviously covet anything that rightfully belonged to their neighbours. Through these ten carefully chosen and deeply meaningful commandments, God intended to help shape His chosen people into a community capable of living together with genuine goodness, kindness, honesty, and fairness toward one another, generation after generation.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: The Ten Commandments</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God</li><li>Moses</li><li>the stone tablets</li><li>the Israelites</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God writes the commandments onto two stone tablets</li><li>Moses comes down the mountain with the tablets</li><li>Moses reads the commandments to the people</li><li>The people promise to obey everything God commanded</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wrote the Ten Commandments onto two stone tablets</li><li>gave them to Moses</li><li>read the commandments to all the people</li><li>promised to obey everything</li><li>guiding their relationship with God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Moses read the commandments from the ___ to the ___, and the people promised to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God wrote the Ten Commandments onto two stone tablets and gave them to Moses. When Moses came down from the mountain, he read the commandments to all the people of Israel. The people promised to obey everything that God had commanded them. The Ten Commandments became the foundation of how the Israelites were meant to live, guiding their relationship with God and with one another for many generations to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God Himself personally wrote out all Ten Commandments onto two flat stone tablets, and then gave them directly into Moses's care. When Moses finally descended from the mountain, he gathered the entire nation of Israel together and read every one of the commandments aloud to them clearly. Moved by what they heard, the people solemnly promised that they would faithfully obey everything that God had commanded of them. From that momentous day onward, the Ten Commandments became the essential foundation for how the Israelites were meant to live their entire lives, carefully guiding both their relationship with God and their relationships with one another for many generations still to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With His own hand, God personally inscribed all Ten Commandments onto two solid stone tablets, and then entrusted them directly into Moses's safekeeping to carry back down the mountain. Upon finally descending from the mountain's peak, Moses immediately gathered together the entire assembled nation of Israel and read every single one of the sacred commandments aloud to them with great clarity and solemnity. Deeply moved and humbled by everything they had just heard, the assembled people solemnly and unanimously promised that they would faithfully obey every single commandment that God had given them. From that momentous and unforgettable day forward, the Ten Commandments became the essential and unshakeable foundation upon which the entire nation of Israel was meant to build their lives, carefully guiding both their sacred relationship with God Himself and their everyday relationships with one another for countless generations still to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: The Ten Commandments</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 9 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 9 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 10 (Joshua & the Fall of Jericho) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the Israelites camped at the bottom of ___ while ___ climbed up to meet ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, God gave ___ the ___ so that the ___ could live good and honest lives.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Moses read the commandments from the ___ to the ___, and the people promised to ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 10: Joshua & the Fall of Jericho":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 10’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 10: Joshua & the Fall of Jericho) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Joshua and the Israelites as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joshua</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>Jericho</li><li>the priests</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Israelites march silently around Jericho once a day for six days</li><li>On the seventh day, they march around the city seven times</li><li>The priests blow their trumpets and the people shout</li><li>The walls of Jericho collapse and the Israelites capture the city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Israelites marched around Jericho seven times on the seventh day</li><li>The Israelite army attacked Jericho with battering rams and ladders</li><li>The walls of Jericho collapsed after the priests blew their trumpets and the people shouted</li><li>Joshua led the Israelites while Moses was still alive and leading them too</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Moses died, God chose Joshua to lead the Israelites into the Promised Land. The people came to the city of Jericho, which had thick walls and its gates were shut tight because the people inside were afraid of the Israelites. God gave Joshua an unusual plan for capturing the city. He told Joshua that the Israelite army should march around the city walls once every day for six days, with seven priests carrying trumpets walking in front of the Ark of the Covenant. No one was allowed to shout or make a sound during the marching.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua obeyed God's instructions exactly. Every day for six days, the army marched silently around the walls of Jericho one time, then returned to their camp. On the seventh day, something different happened. The army marched around the city walls seven times instead of just once. As they completed the seventh time around, the priests blew a long, loud blast on their trumpets, and Joshua commanded all the people to shout as loudly as they could.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment the people shouted, the huge walls of Jericho came crashing down completely, collapsing flat to the ground. The Israelite army walked straight into the city from every direction and captured Jericho completely. The people of Israel knew that it was God's power, not their own weapons, that had brought down the walls and given them victory. This amazing event showed the Israelites that God was with Joshua, just as He had been with Moses before him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the death of Moses, God chose Joshua to take over as leader and guide the Israelites the rest of the way into the Promised Land. Eventually the people arrived outside the city of Jericho, a heavily fortified city surrounded by thick, towering walls, with its gates firmly shut and barred because the terrified inhabitants inside had heard of the Israelites and feared them greatly. Rather than instructing Joshua to attack the city using conventional weapons or siege equipment, God gave him a highly unusual and surprising plan for capturing it. God instructed that the entire Israelite army should march silently around the outer walls of the city exactly once each day for six consecutive days, with seven priests carrying ceremonial trumpets walking directly in front of the sacred Ark of the Covenant as it was carried along. Throughout this entire silent procession, absolutely no one was permitted to shout, cheer, or make any noise at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua carefully and precisely obeyed every detail of God's unusual instructions. For six full days in a row, the army marched silently around the towering walls of Jericho exactly one time each day, before quietly returning to their camp outside the city. However, on the seventh day, something remarkably different took place. Instead of marching around the city just once as before, the entire army circled the walls of Jericho a full seven times that single day. As they completed their seventh and final circuit around the city, the seven priests suddenly sounded a long, loud, sustained blast on their trumpets, and at that exact moment Joshua commanded every single person in the army to shout out as loudly as they possibly could.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very instant the people let out their tremendous shout, the massive, seemingly impenetrable walls surrounding Jericho suddenly came crashing down entirely, collapsing completely flat to the ground in an instant. Without any further obstacle standing in their way, the Israelite army immediately advanced straight into the fallen city from every direction at once and successfully captured Jericho completely. The people of Israel understood clearly and without doubt that it had been God's own miraculous power alone, rather than any weapon or military strategy of their own devising, that had brought down those enormous walls and delivered them such a decisive victory. This astonishing and unforgettable event proved conclusively to the Israelites that God's presence and favour remained firmly with Joshua, exactly as it had once been with Moses before him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following the death of Moses, God chose Joshua, Moses's faithful and trusted assistant, to step into the role of leader and guide the entire nation of Israel the remaining distance into the long-promised land God had prepared for them. In time, the enormous camp of Israelites arrived just outside the walled city of Jericho, a heavily fortified stronghold surrounded on every side by massive, towering stone walls, its heavy gates firmly shut and barred tight, for the terrified inhabitants within had already heard alarming reports about the Israelites and feared them greatly. Rather than commanding Joshua to launch a conventional military assault using siege weapons, battering rams, or ladders, God instead presented him with an extraordinarily unusual and unexpected strategy for capturing the seemingly impenetrable city. God instructed that the entire Israelite army was to march completely silently around the outer walls of Jericho exactly once each day for six consecutive days in a row, with seven priests solemnly carrying ceremonial ram's-horn trumpets marching directly ahead of the sacred Ark of the Covenant as it was reverently carried along behind them. Throughout this strange and solemn silent procession, absolutely no one among the entire army was permitted to shout, cheer, or utter any sound whatsoever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua, demonstrating unwavering faith and obedience, carried out every single detail of God's highly unusual instructions with meticulous precision. For six consecutive days, the enormous Israelite army marched in complete silence around the towering, seemingly impregnable walls of Jericho exactly one single time each day, before quietly withdrawing back to the safety of their encampment outside the city each evening. However, everything changed dramatically on the seventh day. Rather than circling the city just once as they had every previous day, the entire army instead marched steadily around the walls of Jericho a full seven times in succession that single day. The very moment they completed their seventh and final lap around the city's perimeter, the seven priests suddenly sounded one long, thunderous, sustained blast upon their ram's-horn trumpets, and at that precise, dramatic instant, Joshua commanded every single soldier throughout the entire army to raise their voices and shout out as loudly and powerfully as they possibly could.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the very same instant that the tremendous, unified shout rose up from the entire Israelite army, the massive, thick, seemingly indestructible walls surrounding the city of Jericho suddenly and dramatically came crashing down in their entirety, collapsing completely flat to the ground within mere moments. With absolutely no remaining obstacle standing between them and the fallen city, the Israelite army immediately surged forward and advanced straight into Jericho from every direction simultaneously, swiftly and completely capturing the entire city. The assembled people of Israel understood with total clarity and certainty that it had been God's own extraordinary, miraculous power alone — and certainly not any weapon, siege engine, or clever military strategy devised by human hands — that had brought those massive walls crashing down and delivered them such a complete and decisive victory. This truly astonishing and unforgettable demonstration of divine power proved beyond any doubt to the watching Israelites that God's presence, guidance, and favour remained firmly and unmistakably with Joshua, exactly as it had once rested so powerfully upon Moses before him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joshua</li><li>Jericho</li><li>the priests</li><li>the Ark of the Covenant</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God chooses Joshua to lead the Israelites</li><li>The Israelites arrive at the city of Jericho</li><li>God tells Joshua to march around the city for six days</li><li>Seven priests carry trumpets in front of the Ark of the Covenant</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>after Moses died</li><li>thick walls and its gates were shut tight</li><li>afraid of the Israelites</li><li>an unusual plan for capturing the city</li><li>no one was allowed to shout or make a sound</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, God told ___ to march around ___ for six days, with priests carrying ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Moses died, God chose Joshua to lead the Israelites into the Promised Land. The people came to the city of Jericho, which had thick walls and its gates were shut tight because the people inside were afraid of the Israelites. God gave Joshua an unusual plan for capturing the city. He told Joshua that the Israelite army should march around the city walls once every day for six days, with seven priests carrying trumpets walking in front of the Ark of the Covenant. No one was allowed to shout or make a sound during the marching.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the death of Moses, God chose Joshua to take over as leader and guide the Israelites the rest of the way into the Promised Land. Eventually the people arrived outside the city of Jericho, a heavily fortified city surrounded by thick, towering walls, with its gates firmly shut and barred because the terrified inhabitants inside had heard of the Israelites and feared them greatly. Rather than instructing Joshua to attack the city using conventional weapons or siege equipment, God gave him a highly unusual and surprising plan for capturing it. God instructed that the entire Israelite army should march silently around the outer walls of the city exactly once each day for six consecutive days, with seven priests carrying ceremonial trumpets walking directly in front of the sacred Ark of the Covenant as it was carried along. Throughout this entire silent procession, absolutely no one was permitted to shout, cheer, or make any noise at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Following the death of Moses, God chose Joshua, Moses's faithful and trusted assistant, to step into the role of leader and guide the entire nation of Israel the remaining distance into the long-promised land God had prepared for them. In time, the enormous camp of Israelites arrived just outside the walled city of Jericho, a heavily fortified stronghold surrounded on every side by massive, towering stone walls, its heavy gates firmly shut and barred tight, for the terrified inhabitants within had already heard alarming reports about the Israelites and feared them greatly. Rather than commanding Joshua to launch a conventional military assault using siege weapons, battering rams, or ladders, God instead presented him with an extraordinarily unusual and unexpected strategy for capturing the seemingly impenetrable city. God instructed that the entire Israelite army was to march completely silently around the outer walls of Jericho exactly once each day for six consecutive days in a row, with seven priests solemnly carrying ceremonial ram's-horn trumpets marching directly ahead of the sacred Ark of the Covenant as it was reverently carried along behind them. Throughout this strange and solemn silent procession, absolutely no one among the entire army was permitted to shout, cheer, or utter any sound whatsoever.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the army</li><li>the walls</li><li>the priests</li><li>the seventh day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The army marches around the walls once a day for six days</li><li>On the seventh day, they march around seven times</li><li>The priests blow a long blast on their trumpets</li><li>Joshua commands the people to shout</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>obeyed God's instructions exactly</li><li>marched silently around the walls of Jericho one time</li><li>something different happened</li><li>a long, loud blast on their trumpets</li><li>as loudly as they could</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, on ___ the army marched around the walls seven times, then the ___ blew their trumpets and the people ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua obeyed God's instructions exactly. Every day for six days, the army marched silently around the walls of Jericho one time, then returned to their camp. On the seventh day, something different happened. The army marched around the city walls seven times instead of just once. As they completed the seventh time around, the priests blew a long, loud blast on their trumpets, and Joshua commanded all the people to shout as loudly as they could.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua carefully and precisely obeyed every detail of God's unusual instructions. For six full days in a row, the army marched silently around the towering walls of Jericho exactly one time each day, before quietly returning to their camp outside the city. However, on the seventh day, something remarkably different took place. Instead of marching around the city just once as before, the entire army circled the walls of Jericho a full seven times that single day. As they completed their seventh and final circuit around the city, the seven priests suddenly sounded a long, loud, sustained blast on their trumpets, and at that exact moment Joshua commanded every single person in the army to shout out as loudly as they possibly could.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joshua, demonstrating unwavering faith and obedience, carried out every single detail of God's highly unusual instructions with meticulous precision. For six consecutive days, the enormous Israelite army marched in complete silence around the towering, seemingly impregnable walls of Jericho exactly one single time each day, before quietly withdrawing back to the safety of their encampment outside the city each evening. However, everything changed dramatically on the seventh day. Rather than circling the city just once as they had every previous day, the entire army instead marched steadily around the walls of Jericho a full seven times in succession that single day. The very moment they completed their seventh and final lap around the city's perimeter, the seven priests suddenly sounded one long, thunderous, sustained blast upon their ram's-horn trumpets, and at that precise, dramatic instant, Joshua commanded every single soldier throughout the entire army to raise their voices and shout out as loudly and powerfully as they possibly could.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the walls</li><li>the Israelites</li><li>Jericho</li><li>God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The people shout and the walls of Jericho collapse</li><li>The Israelite army enters the city from every direction</li><li>The Israelites capture Jericho completely</li><li>The people know it was God's power that won the victory</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>came crashing down completely</li><li>collapsing flat to the ground</li><li>captured Jericho completely</li><li>not their own weapons</li><li>just as He had been with Moses before him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the ___ came crashing down, the ___ captured the city, and the people knew it was ___ power.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment the people shouted, the huge walls of Jericho came crashing down completely, collapsing flat to the ground. The Israelite army walked straight into the city from every direction and captured Jericho completely. The people of Israel knew that it was God's power, not their own weapons, that had brought down the walls and given them victory. This amazing event showed the Israelites that God was with Joshua, just as He had been with Moses before him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very instant the people let out their tremendous shout, the massive, seemingly impenetrable walls surrounding Jericho suddenly came crashing down entirely, collapsing completely flat to the ground in an instant. Without any further obstacle standing in their way, the Israelite army immediately advanced straight into the fallen city from every direction at once and successfully captured Jericho completely. The people of Israel understood clearly and without doubt that it had been God's own miraculous power alone, rather than any weapon or military strategy of their own devising, that had brought down those enormous walls and delivered them such a decisive victory. This astonishing and unforgettable event proved conclusively to the Israelites that God's presence and favour remained firmly with Joshua, exactly as it had once been with Moses before him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the very same instant that the tremendous, unified shout rose up from the entire Israelite army, the massive, thick, seemingly indestructible walls surrounding the city of Jericho suddenly and dramatically came crashing down in their entirety, collapsing completely flat to the ground within mere moments. With absolutely no remaining obstacle standing between them and the fallen city, the Israelite army immediately surged forward and advanced straight into Jericho from every direction simultaneously, swiftly and completely capturing the entire city. The assembled people of Israel understood with total clarity and certainty that it had been God's own extraordinary, miraculous power alone — and certainly not any weapon, siege engine, or clever military strategy devised by human hands — that had brought those massive walls crashing down and delivered them such a complete and decisive victory. This truly astonishing and unforgettable demonstration of divine power proved beyond any doubt to the watching Israelites that God's presence, guidance, and favour remained firmly and unmistakably with Joshua, exactly as it had once rested so powerfully upon Moses before him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Joshua &amp; the Fall of Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 10 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 10 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 11 (Gideon & the Fleece) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, God told ___ to march around ___ for six days, with priests carrying ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, on ___ the army marched around the walls seven times, then the ___ blew their trumpets and the people ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the ___ came crashing down, the ___ captured the city, and the people knew it was ___ power.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (Orange belt) — Stories 11–15":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 11: Gideon & the Fleece":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Gideon &amp; the Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 11’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 11: Gideon & the Fleece) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Gideon and the angel as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon</li><li>the angel</li><li>the fleece</li><li>the winepress</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Midianites raid Israel's crops, so Gideon hides his wheat in a winepress</li><li>An angel appears and calls Gideon a mighty warrior sent to save Israel</li><li>Gideon asks God for a sign, and lays out a wool fleece overnight</li><li>God answers the fleece test, and Gideon leads Israel's army against Midian</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon was hiding from the Midianites when the angel found him</li><li>Gideon believed at once that he could defeat the Midianites without any sign</li><li>Gideon asked God to make the wool fleece wet while the ground stayed dry</li><li>God refused to answer Gideon's request for a sign a second time</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had turned away from God, and for seven years the Midianites raided their land again and again, stealing their crops and leaving them hungry and afraid. A man named Gideon was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress, hiding from the Midianites so they would not steal his grain. Suddenly, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down nearby, saying to him, ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Gideon was startled and asked why, if God was truly with Israel, all these troubles had come upon them. The angel told Gideon that God was sending him to rescue Israel from the Midianites.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon doubted that he could do such a big job, since his family was poor and he felt like the least important person around. God promised Gideon that He would be with him. That night, Gideon obeyed God and tore down his father's altar to the false god Baal, even though he was afraid and did the work in the dark. Still unsure of himself, Gideon asked God for a sign. He set out a wool fleece on the threshing floor overnight and asked God to make the fleece wet with dew while the ground around it stayed completely dry.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the morning, Gideon found the fleece soaked with water, and when he wrung it out, a whole bowlful of dew poured from it, while the ground stayed perfectly dry. Gideon still wanted to be sure, so he asked God for one more sign, the opposite way around: this time, keep the fleece dry and make the ground wet with dew. God graciously answered again exactly as Gideon asked. Now certain that God was truly with him, Gideon gathered the Israelite men together and prepared to lead them into battle against the Midianites, trusting that God would give them victory.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had turned away from following God, and as a result, for seven long years the Midianites swept in and raided their land again and again, stealing their crops and animals and leaving the Israelites hungry, poor, and terrified. Because of this danger, a man named Gideon was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress instead of out in the open, hoping to hide his grain from any Midianite raiders passing by. Suddenly, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down beneath a nearby tree, greeting him with the surprising words, ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Startled and confused, Gideon asked how that could possibly be true, when so much suffering had fallen upon his people. Calmly, the angel explained that God Himself was now sending Gideon to rescue Israel out of the hand of the Midianites.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon doubted very much that he was the right person for such an enormous task, since his family was among the poorest in his tribe, and he considered himself the least important member of it. Even so, God promised Gideon that He would be with him constantly, no matter how impossible the mission seemed. That very night, trusting God despite his fear, Gideon obeyed God's command and tore down his own father's altar built to the false god Baal, choosing to do the risky work under cover of darkness so the townspeople would not see him and try to stop him. Still feeling unsure of the whole plan, Gideon asked God for a clear sign to strengthen his shaky faith. He laid a wool fleece out on the threshing floor overnight and asked God to make the fleece soaking wet with dew by morning, while leaving the ground around it completely dry.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sure enough, when morning came, Gideon discovered the fleece thoroughly soaked through with water, and when he wrung it tightly, an entire bowlful of dew poured out of it, even though the ground surrounding it remained perfectly dry, just as he had asked. Yet Gideon, still wanting to be absolutely certain before risking his life and the lives of his men, respectfully asked God for one more sign, this time the exact opposite way around: he asked that the fleece stay dry while the ground itself became wet with dew. Patiently and graciously, God answered Gideon's second request exactly as he had asked, without ever growing angry at his doubts. Finally convinced beyond any doubt that God was truly with him, Gideon gathered the Israelite fighting men together, organised them for battle, and prepared to lead them out against the far larger Midianite army, trusting completely that God Himself would grant them the victory.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, after generations of turning away from God, the Israelites found themselves oppressed for seven miserable years by the Midianites and other desert raiders, who swept across the land every harvest season, destroying crops, seizing livestock, and leaving the people so terrified that many fled to live in caves carved into the mountains. In the town of Ophrah, a man named Gideon, son of Joash the Abiezrite, was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress rather than out in the open, trying to hide his small harvest from any Midianite raiders passing nearby. As Gideon worked in secret, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down beneath a large oak tree belonging to his father, greeting him with astonishing words: ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Startled and doubtful, Gideon replied that if the Lord were truly with Israel, he could not understand why such terrible troubles had come upon his people, or where the wonderful miracles their ancestors spoke of had gone. Undeterred, the angel told Gideon plainly that God Himself was sending him, weak and unlikely as he felt, to rescue Israel from the Midianites.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon protested that he could hardly be the right choice for such a task, since his clan was considered the weakest in the whole tribe of Manasseh, and he felt he was the least significant person even within his own father's household. Nevertheless, God promised Gideon that He would be with him without fail through every part of the coming struggle. Strengthened by this promise, yet still trembling with fear, Gideon gathered ten of his father's servants that same night and obeyed God's difficult command to tear down his own father's altar to the false god Baal, choosing the cover of darkness because he was too afraid of the townspeople to do it openly. Even after this bold act, Gideon still felt unsteady about leading Israel into battle, so he asked God for a clear sign. That evening, he laid a single wool fleece on the bare threshing floor and asked God that, by morning, the fleece alone would be drenched with dew while the ground surrounding it remained entirely dry.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just as Gideon had asked, morning revealed the fleece thoroughly soaked with moisture, and when he wrung it, an entire bowlful of dew poured out, even though the ground all around had stayed perfectly dry overnight. Even with such a striking answer already given, Gideon, keenly aware of how much rested on getting this right, respectfully asked for permission to test the sign one final time, but in reverse: this time, he asked that the fleece remain dry while the ground around it became wet with dew. God did not grow frustrated with Gideon's repeated requests, but graciously granted this second sign exactly as asked. At last fully convinced that God was truly with him, Gideon summoned the Israelite fighting men from the surrounding tribes, organised them into companies, and began preparing to lead them out against the vast and previously terrifying Midianite army, trusting that God Himself, and not the size of his army, would win them the victory.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Gideon &amp; the Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon</li><li>the angel</li><li>the winepress</li><li>the Midianites</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Midianites raid Israel's land and Gideon hides his wheat</li><li>Gideon threshes wheat secretly inside a winepress</li><li>An angel appears and calls Gideon a mighty warrior</li><li>The angel tells Gideon that God is sending him to save Israel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>for seven years</li><li>stealing their crops</li><li>startled</li><li>why, if God was truly with Israel</li><li>all these troubles</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, an ___ found Gideon hiding wheat in a ___, and told him God was sending him to save ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had turned away from God, and for seven years the Midianites raided their land again and again, stealing their crops and leaving them hungry and afraid. A man named Gideon was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress, hiding from the Midianites so they would not steal his grain. Suddenly, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down nearby, saying to him, ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Gideon was startled and asked why, if God was truly with Israel, all these troubles had come upon them. The angel told Gideon that God was sending him to rescue Israel from the Midianites.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the people of Israel had turned away from following God, and as a result, for seven long years the Midianites swept in and raided their land again and again, stealing their crops and animals and leaving the Israelites hungry, poor, and terrified. Because of this danger, a man named Gideon was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress instead of out in the open, hoping to hide his grain from any Midianite raiders passing by. Suddenly, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down beneath a nearby tree, greeting him with the surprising words, ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Startled and confused, Gideon asked how that could possibly be true, when so much suffering had fallen upon his people. Calmly, the angel explained that God Himself was now sending Gideon to rescue Israel out of the hand of the Midianites.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, after generations of turning away from God, the Israelites found themselves oppressed for seven miserable years by the Midianites and other desert raiders, who swept across the land every harvest season, destroying crops, seizing livestock, and leaving the people so terrified that many fled to live in caves carved into the mountains. In the town of Ophrah, a man named Gideon, son of Joash the Abiezrite, was threshing wheat secretly inside a winepress rather than out in the open, trying to hide his small harvest from any Midianite raiders passing nearby. As Gideon worked in secret, an angel of the Lord appeared and sat down beneath a large oak tree belonging to his father, greeting him with astonishing words: ‘The Lord is with you, mighty warrior.’ Startled and doubtful, Gideon replied that if the Lord were truly with Israel, he could not understand why such terrible troubles had come upon his people, or where the wonderful miracles their ancestors spoke of had gone. Undeterred, the angel told Gideon plainly that God Himself was sending him, weak and unlikely as he felt, to rescue Israel from the Midianites.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Gideon &amp; the Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon</li><li>God</li><li>the altar</li><li>the fleece</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon doubts he can do such a big job</li><li>God promises to be with Gideon</li><li>Gideon tears down his father's altar to Baal at night</li><li>Gideon asks God for a sign using a wool fleece</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>poor</li><li>least important person</li><li>even though he was afraid</li><li>still unsure of himself</li><li>overnight</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Gideon tore down the altar to ___ at night, then asked God for a sign using a wool ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon doubted that he could do such a big job, since his family was poor and he felt like the least important person around. God promised Gideon that He would be with him. That night, Gideon obeyed God and tore down his father's altar to the false god Baal, even though he was afraid and did the work in the dark. Still unsure of himself, Gideon asked God for a sign. He set out a wool fleece on the threshing floor overnight and asked God to make the fleece wet with dew while the ground around it stayed completely dry.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon doubted very much that he was the right person for such an enormous task, since his family was among the poorest in his tribe, and he considered himself the least important member of it. Even so, God promised Gideon that He would be with him constantly, no matter how impossible the mission seemed. That very night, trusting God despite his fear, Gideon obeyed God's command and tore down his own father's altar built to the false god Baal, choosing to do the risky work under cover of darkness so the townspeople would not see him and try to stop him. Still feeling unsure of the whole plan, Gideon asked God for a clear sign to strengthen his shaky faith. He laid a wool fleece out on the threshing floor overnight and asked God to make the fleece soaking wet with dew by morning, while leaving the ground around it completely dry.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Gideon protested that he could hardly be the right choice for such a task, since his clan was considered the weakest in the whole tribe of Manasseh, and he felt he was the least significant person even within his own father's household. Nevertheless, God promised Gideon that He would be with him without fail through every part of the coming struggle. Strengthened by this promise, yet still trembling with fear, Gideon gathered ten of his father's servants that same night and obeyed God's difficult command to tear down his own father's altar to the false god Baal, choosing the cover of darkness because he was too afraid of the townspeople to do it openly. Even after this bold act, Gideon still felt unsteady about leading Israel into battle, so he asked God for a clear sign. That evening, he laid a single wool fleece on the bare threshing floor and asked God that, by morning, the fleece alone would be drenched with dew while the ground surrounding it remained entirely dry.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Gideon &amp; the Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gideon</li><li>the fleece</li><li>God</li><li>the Midianites</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God makes the fleece wet while the ground stays dry</li><li>Gideon asks for a second sign, the opposite way around</li><li>God answers the second sign exactly as asked</li><li>Gideon gathers the army and prepares to fight the Midianites</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wrung it out</li><li>a whole bowlful</li><li>graciously</li><li>now certain</li><li>trusting that God would give them victory</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, God answered Gideon's fleece test twice, so Gideon gathered the ___ and trusted God for victory over the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the morning, Gideon found the fleece soaked with water, and when he wrung it out, a whole bowlful of dew poured from it, while the ground stayed perfectly dry. Gideon still wanted to be sure, so he asked God for one more sign, the opposite way around: this time, keep the fleece dry and make the ground wet with dew. God graciously answered again exactly as Gideon asked. Now certain that God was truly with him, Gideon gathered the Israelite men together and prepared to lead them into battle against the Midianites, trusting that God would give them victory.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sure enough, when morning came, Gideon discovered the fleece thoroughly soaked through with water, and when he wrung it tightly, an entire bowlful of dew poured out of it, even though the ground surrounding it remained perfectly dry, just as he had asked. Yet Gideon, still wanting to be absolutely certain before risking his life and the lives of his men, respectfully asked God for one more sign, this time the exact opposite way around: he asked that the fleece stay dry while the ground itself became wet with dew. Patiently and graciously, God answered Gideon's second request exactly as he had asked, without ever growing angry at his doubts. Finally convinced beyond any doubt that God was truly with him, Gideon gathered the Israelite fighting men together, organised them for battle, and prepared to lead them out against the far larger Midianite army, trusting completely that God Himself would grant them the victory.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just as Gideon had asked, morning revealed the fleece thoroughly soaked with moisture, and when he wrung it, an entire bowlful of dew poured out, even though the ground all around had stayed perfectly dry overnight. Even with such a striking answer already given, Gideon, keenly aware of how much rested on getting this right, respectfully asked for permission to test the sign one final time, but in reverse: this time, he asked that the fleece remain dry while the ground around it became wet with dew. God did not grow frustrated with Gideon's repeated requests, but graciously granted this second sign exactly as asked. At last fully convinced that God was truly with him, Gideon summoned the Israelite fighting men from the surrounding tribes, organised them into companies, and began preparing to lead them out against the vast and previously terrifying Midianite army, trusting that God Himself, and not the size of his army, would win them the victory.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Gideon &amp; the Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 11 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 11 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 12 (Samson's Strength) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, an ___ found Gideon hiding wheat in a ___, and told him God was sending him to save ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Gideon tore down the altar to ___ at night, then asked God for a sign using a wool ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, God answered Gideon's fleece test twice, so Gideon gathered the ___ and trusted God for victory over the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 12: Samson's Strength":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: Samson's Strength</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 12’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 12: Samson's Strength) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Samson and Delilah as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samson</li><li>Delilah</li><li>his hair</li><li>the Philistines</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>An angel tells Samson's parents their son will be a Nazirite set apart for God</li><li>God gives Samson amazing strength that he uses to protect Israel</li><li>Delilah discovers that Samson's strength is tied to his uncut hair</li><li>Samson's hair is cut, his strength leaves, but God gives it back one last time</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>An angel told Samson's parents he would be a Nazirite set apart for God</li><li>Samson's incredible strength came from the weapons he carried</li><li>Delilah discovered that Samson's strength was connected to his uncut hair</li><li>Samson never asked God for help again after his hair was cut</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an angel told a childless couple, Manoah and his wife, they would have a son who must never cut his hair, for he would be a Nazirite set apart to serve God and would begin to rescue Israel from the Philistines who ruled harshly over them. They named their son Samson. As Samson grew from a boy into a young man, God blessed him with astonishing strength unlike anyone else in Israel, and he served as a judge, leading his people and standing bravely against the Philistines.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Samson often clashed with the Philistines, using his God-given strength in daring and surprising ways to protect his people, once even carrying off the heavy city gates of Gaza on his shoulders. Samson fell in love with a woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers paid her a large sum of silver to discover the secret of his strength. Delilah asked Samson again and again, and finally, worn down by her constant pleading, he told her the truth: his strength was tied to a vow to God, shown by his hair, which had never been cut.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While Samson slept, Delilah had his hair cut off, and his strength left him just as he feared. The Philistines captured Samson, but as time passed his hair slowly began to grow back. Later, at a great feast, the Philistines gathered in a large building to mock God in front of Samson. Samson quietly prayed to God for strength one final time, then pushed apart the two central pillars holding up the building with all his might, and it collapsed, bringing an end to the Philistines' mockery of God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during a time when the Philistines ruled harshly over Israel, an angel appeared to a childless couple named Manoah and his wife, promising them that they would finally have a son. The angel gave them a very unusual instruction: their boy must be raised as a Nazirite, set apart specially for God from birth, which meant, among other things, that his hair must never be cut. This son, the angel said, would begin the work of rescuing Israel from the Philistines. In time, the couple's son was born, and they named him Samson. As Samson grew into a young man, God blessed him again and again with astonishing physical strength unlike anyone else in Israel, and eventually Samson became one of Israel's judges, leading his people and standing boldly against the Philistines who had oppressed them for so long.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his life as a judge, Samson clashed with the Philistines many times, using his remarkable God-given strength in bold, sometimes surprising ways to defend and protect his people from their enemies. Eventually, Samson fell deeply in love with a Philistine woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers, desperate to defeat him, offered Delilah a large sum of silver if she could discover the true secret behind his incredible strength. Delilah pestered Samson again and again, wearing down his resistance bit by bit, until finally, worn out by her constant questioning, Samson told her the whole truth: his extraordinary strength was tied directly to the special vow he had made to God, a vow shown outwardly through his hair, which had never once been cut since the day he was born.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sensing her chance at last, Delilah waited until Samson had fallen fast asleep, and then had someone quietly cut off all of his hair while he slept, and just as he had feared, his remarkable strength left him completely and instantly. The Philistines seized the opportunity and captured Samson, though as the following weeks passed by slowly in captivity, his hair gradually began growing back once again, little by little. Later on, the Philistines held a great feast in a large temple building, gathering together to mock and celebrate over Samson, and through him, to mock the God of Israel Himself. Standing quietly between the two central supporting pillars of the enormous building, Samson prayed earnestly to God one final time, asking for his strength to return just once more, and then, trusting completely in God's power, pushed against both pillars with all his remaining might until the entire building collapsed, bringing a dramatic end to the Philistines' mockery of God that day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during a difficult era when the Philistines ruled harshly over the Israelites, an angel of the Lord appeared to a childless couple named Manoah and his wife, astonishing them with news that they would soon have a son after years of being unable to have children. The angel explained that this boy must be raised as a Nazirite, someone set apart especially for God, which meant he could never drink wine, could never touch anything unclean, and most importantly, could never have his hair cut, for his uncut hair would be the visible sign of his lifelong devotion to God. The angel further explained that this son would begin the work of rescuing Israel out of the hands of the Philistines who oppressed them. In time, exactly as promised, the couple's son was born, and they named him Samson. As Samson grew into a strong young man, the Spirit of the Lord blessed him again and again with extraordinary strength unlike anything Israel had witnessed before, and in time Samson became one of Israel's judges, leading his people and standing fearlessly against their oppressors.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his years as judge, Samson clashed with the Philistines time and again, relying on his God-given strength in bold and sometimes unexpected ways, once even lifting the enormous city gates of Gaza clean off their hinges and carrying them away on his shoulders. Eventually, Samson fell deeply in love with a Philistine woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers, growing desperate to defeat him, offered Delilah a large sum of silver if she could uncover the secret of his strength. Delilah questioned Samson relentlessly, and three times he gave her false answers just to test her, watching each attempt to capture him fail. Eventually, worn down by her constant pleading day after day, Samson gave in and told Delilah the truth at last: his strength was tied to the sacred vow he had made to God since before his birth, a vow made visible through his hair, which had never once been cut.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sensing she finally had the real answer, Delilah waited until Samson had fallen into a deep sleep upon her lap, and quietly had someone cut off every strand of his hair; the moment it was gone, his strength left him completely, just as he had feared. The Philistines captured Samson and set him to work grinding grain in their prison, though as the weeks passed, his hair gradually began growing back, strand by strand. Later, the Philistines gathered thousands of people for a great feast inside an enormous temple building, celebrating loudly and using Samson as entertainment to mock both him and, through him, the God of Israel whom he served. Positioned between the two massive central pillars supporting the crowded building's roof, Samson quietly prayed to God one final time, asking for his strength to return just once more. Trusting completely in God's power, Samson braced himself firmly against both pillars and pushed with every last bit of strength he had, until the pillars gave way and the entire building came crashing down, bringing a dramatic end to the Philistines' mockery of God that day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: Samson's Strength</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samson</li><li>Manoah and his wife</li><li>an angel</li><li>the Philistines</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>An angel tells Manoah and his wife they will have a son</li><li>The son must never cut his hair, as a Nazirite set apart for God</li><li>They name their son Samson</li><li>God blesses Samson with amazing strength as he grows</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>long ago</li><li>childless couple</li><li>astonishing strength</li><li>who ruled over them</li><li>leading Israel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, an angel told ___ and his wife that their son must never cut his ___, because he would serve ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an angel told a childless couple, Manoah and his wife, they would have a son who must never cut his hair, for he would be a Nazirite set apart to serve God and would begin to rescue Israel from the Philistines who ruled harshly over them. They named their son Samson. As Samson grew from a boy into a young man, God blessed him with astonishing strength unlike anyone else in Israel, and he served as a judge, leading his people and standing bravely against the Philistines.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during a time when the Philistines ruled harshly over Israel, an angel appeared to a childless couple named Manoah and his wife, promising them that they would finally have a son. The angel gave them a very unusual instruction: their boy must be raised as a Nazirite, set apart specially for God from birth, which meant, among other things, that his hair must never be cut. This son, the angel said, would begin the work of rescuing Israel from the Philistines. In time, the couple's son was born, and they named him Samson. As Samson grew into a young man, God blessed him again and again with astonishing physical strength unlike anyone else in Israel, and eventually Samson became one of Israel's judges, leading his people and standing boldly against the Philistines who had oppressed them for so long.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during a difficult era when the Philistines ruled harshly over the Israelites, an angel of the Lord appeared to a childless couple named Manoah and his wife, astonishing them with news that they would soon have a son after years of being unable to have children. The angel explained that this boy must be raised as a Nazirite, someone set apart especially for God, which meant he could never drink wine, could never touch anything unclean, and most importantly, could never have his hair cut, for his uncut hair would be the visible sign of his lifelong devotion to God. The angel further explained that this son would begin the work of rescuing Israel out of the hands of the Philistines who oppressed them. In time, exactly as promised, the couple's son was born, and they named him Samson. As Samson grew into a strong young man, the Spirit of the Lord blessed him again and again with extraordinary strength unlike anything Israel had witnessed before, and in time Samson became one of Israel's judges, leading his people and standing fearlessly against their oppressors.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: Samson's Strength</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samson</li><li>Delilah</li><li>the Philistines</li><li>his hair</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samson uses his strength to protect Israel from the Philistines</li><li>Samson falls in love with Delilah</li><li>The Philistine rulers pay Delilah to find his secret</li><li>Samson tells Delilah his strength is tied to his uncut hair</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>often clashed</li><li>daring and surprising ways</li><li>again and again</li><li>paid her</li><li>the truth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the Philistine rulers paid ___ to discover Samson's secret, and he finally told her his strength was tied to his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Samson often clashed with the Philistines, using his God-given strength in daring and surprising ways to protect his people, once even carrying off the heavy city gates of Gaza on his shoulders. Samson fell in love with a woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers paid her a large sum of silver to discover the secret of his strength. Delilah asked Samson again and again, and finally, worn down by her constant pleading, he told her the truth: his strength was tied to a vow to God, shown by his hair, which had never been cut.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his life as a judge, Samson clashed with the Philistines many times, using his remarkable God-given strength in bold, sometimes surprising ways to defend and protect his people from their enemies. Eventually, Samson fell deeply in love with a Philistine woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers, desperate to defeat him, offered Delilah a large sum of silver if she could discover the true secret behind his incredible strength. Delilah pestered Samson again and again, wearing down his resistance bit by bit, until finally, worn out by her constant questioning, Samson told her the whole truth: his extraordinary strength was tied directly to the special vow he had made to God, a vow shown outwardly through his hair, which had never once been cut since the day he was born.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his years as judge, Samson clashed with the Philistines time and again, relying on his God-given strength in bold and sometimes unexpected ways, once even lifting the enormous city gates of Gaza clean off their hinges and carrying them away on his shoulders. Eventually, Samson fell deeply in love with a Philistine woman named Delilah, and the Philistine rulers, growing desperate to defeat him, offered Delilah a large sum of silver if she could uncover the secret of his strength. Delilah questioned Samson relentlessly, and three times he gave her false answers just to test her, watching each attempt to capture him fail. Eventually, worn down by her constant pleading day after day, Samson gave in and told Delilah the truth at last: his strength was tied to the sacred vow he had made to God since before his birth, a vow made visible through his hair, which had never once been cut.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: Samson's Strength</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samson</li><li>Delilah</li><li>the Philistines</li><li>the pillars</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Delilah has Samson's hair cut while he sleeps</li><li>Samson's strength leaves him and the Philistines capture him</li><li>Samson's hair slowly grows back</li><li>Samson prays and pushes apart the pillars to stop the mockery</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>while Samson slept</li><li>just as he feared</li><li>over time</li><li>a great feast</li><li>with all his might</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Samson's hair was cut and his ___ left him, but he later prayed to ___ and pushed apart the two ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While Samson slept, Delilah had his hair cut off, and his strength left him just as he feared. The Philistines captured Samson, but as time passed his hair slowly began to grow back. Later, at a great feast, the Philistines gathered in a large building to mock God in front of Samson. Samson quietly prayed to God for strength one final time, then pushed apart the two central pillars holding up the building with all his might, and it collapsed, bringing an end to the Philistines' mockery of God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sensing her chance at last, Delilah waited until Samson had fallen fast asleep, and then had someone quietly cut off all of his hair while he slept, and just as he had feared, his remarkable strength left him completely and instantly. The Philistines seized the opportunity and captured Samson, though as the following weeks passed by slowly in captivity, his hair gradually began growing back once again, little by little. Later on, the Philistines held a great feast in a large temple building, gathering together to mock and celebrate over Samson, and through him, to mock the God of Israel Himself. Standing quietly between the two central supporting pillars of the enormous building, Samson prayed earnestly to God one final time, asking for his strength to return just once more, and then, trusting completely in God's power, pushed against both pillars with all his remaining might until the entire building collapsed, bringing a dramatic end to the Philistines' mockery of God that day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sensing she finally had the real answer, Delilah waited until Samson had fallen into a deep sleep upon her lap, and quietly had someone cut off every strand of his hair; the moment it was gone, his strength left him completely, just as he had feared. The Philistines captured Samson and set him to work grinding grain in their prison, though as the weeks passed, his hair gradually began growing back, strand by strand. Later, the Philistines gathered thousands of people for a great feast inside an enormous temple building, celebrating loudly and using Samson as entertainment to mock both him and, through him, the God of Israel whom he served. Positioned between the two massive central pillars supporting the crowded building's roof, Samson quietly prayed to God one final time, asking for his strength to return just once more. Trusting completely in God's power, Samson braced himself firmly against both pillars and pushed with every last bit of strength he had, until the pillars gave way and the entire building came crashing down, bringing a dramatic end to the Philistines' mockery of God that day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: Samson's Strength</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 12 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 12 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 13 (David & Goliath) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, an angel told ___ and his wife that their son must never cut his ___, because he would serve ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the Philistine rulers paid ___ to discover Samson's secret, and he finally told her his strength was tied to his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Samson's hair was cut and his ___ left him, but he later prayed to ___ and pushed apart the two ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 13: David & Goliath":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: David &amp; Goliath</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 13’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 13: David & Goliath) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name David and Goliath as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Goliath</li><li>the sling</li><li>the armies</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Goliath challenges Israel's army to send out one man to fight him</li><li>David, a young shepherd boy, arrives and hears Goliath's taunts against God</li><li>David refuses King Saul's armour and chooses five smooth stones and a sling</li><li>David defeats Goliath with a single stone, trusting God's help</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Goliath challenged the Israelite army to send out a champion to fight him</li><li>David was an experienced soldier who had fought many wars before facing Goliath</li><li>David chose to fight Goliath with just a sling and five smooth stones</li><li>King Saul's heavy armour and sword helped David defeat Goliath easily</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The armies of Israel and the Philistines lined up on opposite hills, facing each other across a valley. Each day, a huge Philistine warrior named Goliath, nearly three metres tall, stepped forward and shouted a challenge, daring Israel to send one man to fight him. He wore heavy armour and carried a massive spear, and every soldier in Israel's army was too afraid to face him. A young shepherd boy named David came to the battle lines to bring food to his older brothers and heard Goliath's terrible insults against the armies of the living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was shocked that no one dared to fight for God's honour, so he offered to face Goliath himself. King Saul warned him he was too young and inexperienced, but David explained that God had already helped him fight off lions and bears while guarding his sheep, and God would help him defeat Goliath too. Saul offered David his own armour and sword, but they were too heavy and unfamiliar, so David took them off. Instead, David picked up his shepherd's staff, chose five smooth stones from a stream, and readied his sling.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Goliath laughed when he saw David, a boy with no armour, sword, or shield, and mocked him fiercely. David answered boldly that he came in the name of the Lord, who would give the Philistines into his hands that day. As Goliath moved closer, David ran quickly toward him, swung his sling, and released a single stone, striking Goliath in the forehead. Goliath fell to the ground, and the Philistine army fled in fear, while all Israel praised God for the amazing victory He had given through David.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The armies of Israel and the Philistines gathered on two opposite hillsides, camped facing one another across a valley, each side waiting for the other to move. Every day for forty days, an enormous Philistine warrior named Goliath, standing nearly three metres tall, stepped out in front of his army and bellowed a challenge across the valley, daring the Israelites to send out just one man to fight him in single combat. Covered from head to toe in heavy bronze armour and carrying a massive spear, Goliath terrified every soldier in Saul's army, and not a single Israelite dared step forward. Into this frightening scene walked a young shepherd boy named David, who had come to the battle lines simply to deliver food to his three older brothers, and it was there that he first heard Goliath's repeated insults hurled against the armies of the living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was genuinely shocked that not a single Israelite soldier dared to defend God's honour against such insults, so despite being just a boy, he bravely offered to go out and fight Goliath himself. King Saul warned David that he was far too young and inexperienced to face such a fearsome warrior, but David calmly explained that while guarding his father's sheep, God had already helped him defeat both a lion and a bear, and he trusted that same God would help him defeat Goliath too. Saul reluctantly offered David his own royal armour and sword, but everything was so heavy and unfamiliar that David could barely move, so he removed it all. Instead, trusting completely in God rather than weapons, David picked up his shepherd's staff, selected five smooth stones from a nearby stream, and readied his sling for the fight ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Goliath finally caught sight of David approaching, a boy with no armour, sword, or shield, he laughed scornfully and mocked him with cruel words, insulted that Israel would send a mere child to face him. David answered back boldly and without fear, declaring that he came not with sword or spear, but in the name of the Lord of Heaven's Armies, who would surely hand the Philistines over to Israel that day. As Goliath moved forward, David ran quickly toward him rather than away, reached into his bag, swung his sling with practised skill, and released a single stone that flew straight and true, striking Goliath directly in the forehead. Goliath toppled to the ground, and seeing their champion defeated, the entire Philistine army turned and fled in terror, while all of Israel rejoiced and praised God for the astonishing victory He had given them that day through one brave shepherd boy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For forty long days, the armies of Israel and the Philistines had faced one another from two opposite hillsides, camped on either side of a valley, with neither army willing to make the first move. Twice each day throughout this standoff, an enormous Philistine warrior named Goliath, said to stand nearly three metres tall and dressed in heavy bronze scale armour, marched out and bellowed a thunderous challenge across the valley, daring the Israelites to send out just one man to settle the entire conflict through single combat. Terrified by his size and fearsome weapons, not one soldier in King Saul's army, however experienced, dared step forward. Meanwhile, back home, a young shepherd named David had been sent by his father, Jesse, simply to carry food to his three older brothers serving in Saul's army. It was there, standing among the soldiers, that David first heard Goliath bellow his daily insults, not only against Israel's soldiers, but against the very armies and honour of the living God Himself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was deeply troubled that not a single soldier in Israel's army was willing to defend God's honour against such open mockery, and despite his older brother Eliab scolding him for even asking questions, David bravely volunteered to face Goliath himself. King Saul pointed out that David was merely a boy while Goliath had been a trained warrior since his youth, but David calmly explained that while guarding his father's flock, God had already given him the strength to fight off and defeat both a lion and a bear, and he believed that same God would deliver him from this Philistine too. Saul eventually agreed to let David go, first offering him his own royal armour and sword, but David found he could barely walk wearing such heavy equipment, so he removed it all and set it aside. Instead, trusting entirely in God, David picked up nothing but his shepherd's staff, chose five smooth stones from a stream, and readied his sling for whatever lay ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment Goliath caught sight of David approaching, a boy without armour, sword, or shield, he laughed scornfully and hurled cruel, threatening insults at him, offended that Israel would dare send a mere child to face a trained warrior. Without a trace of fear, David answered back boldly, declaring that he came not relying on sword or spear, but in the powerful name of the Lord of Heaven's Armies, who would surely hand every last Philistine over to Israel that day. As Goliath began moving forward, David ran quickly straight toward his enormous opponent, reached calmly into his bag, swung his sling with practised precision, and released a single stone that struck Goliath squarely in the forehead. Goliath collapsed instantly, and the moment their champion fell, the entire Philistine army turned and fled back across the valley, while the Israelite soldiers rose with a great shout and chased after them, and all of Israel rejoiced together, praising God for the astonishing victory He had given them through nothing more than one faithful shepherd boy and a single small stone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: David &amp; Goliath</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Goliath</li><li>the armies</li><li>the valley</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The armies of Israel and the Philistines line up facing each other</li><li>Goliath steps forward each day and challenges Israel to send one man</li><li>Every Israelite soldier is too afraid to face him</li><li>David arrives with food for his brothers and hears Goliath's insults</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>nearly three metres tall</li><li>heavy armour</li><li>massive spear</li><li>too afraid</li><li>against the armies of the living God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, a huge Philistine warrior named ___ challenged Israel to send one man to fight him, but every soldier was too ___ until ___ arrived with food for his brothers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The armies of Israel and the Philistines lined up on opposite hills, facing each other across a valley. Each day, a huge Philistine warrior named Goliath, nearly three metres tall, stepped forward and shouted a challenge, daring Israel to send one man to fight him. He wore heavy armour and carried a massive spear, and every soldier in Israel's army was too afraid to face him. A young shepherd boy named David came to the battle lines to bring food to his older brothers and heard Goliath's terrible insults against the armies of the living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The armies of Israel and the Philistines gathered on two opposite hillsides, camped facing one another across a valley, each side waiting for the other to move. Every day for forty days, an enormous Philistine warrior named Goliath, standing nearly three metres tall, stepped out in front of his army and bellowed a challenge across the valley, daring the Israelites to send out just one man to fight him in single combat. Covered from head to toe in heavy bronze armour and carrying a massive spear, Goliath terrified every soldier in Saul's army, and not a single Israelite dared step forward. Into this frightening scene walked a young shepherd boy named David, who had come to the battle lines simply to deliver food to his three older brothers, and it was there that he first heard Goliath's repeated insults hurled against the armies of the living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For forty long days, the armies of Israel and the Philistines had faced one another from two opposite hillsides, camped on either side of a valley, with neither army willing to make the first move. Twice each day throughout this standoff, an enormous Philistine warrior named Goliath, said to stand nearly three metres tall and dressed in heavy bronze scale armour, marched out and bellowed a thunderous challenge across the valley, daring the Israelites to send out just one man to settle the entire conflict through single combat. Terrified by his size and fearsome weapons, not one soldier in King Saul's army, however experienced, dared step forward. Meanwhile, back home, a young shepherd named David had been sent by his father, Jesse, simply to carry food to his three older brothers serving in Saul's army. It was there, standing among the soldiers, that David first heard Goliath bellow his daily insults, not only against Israel's soldiers, but against the very armies and honour of the living God Himself.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: David &amp; Goliath</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Saul</li><li>Goliath</li><li>the sling</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David offers to fight Goliath instead of the frightened soldiers</li><li>King Saul warns David he is too young and inexperienced</li><li>David explains that God helped him defeat lions and bears</li><li>David chooses five smooth stones and his sling instead of armour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>too young</li><li>lions and bears</li><li>too heavy and unfamiliar</li><li>shepherd's staff</li><li>readied his sling</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, King ___ warned David he was too young, but David said ___ had helped him before, so he chose five smooth ___ and his sling instead of armour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was shocked that no one dared to fight for God's honour, so he offered to face Goliath himself. King Saul warned him he was too young and inexperienced, but David explained that God had already helped him fight off lions and bears while guarding his sheep, and God would help him defeat Goliath too. Saul offered David his own armour and sword, but they were too heavy and unfamiliar, so David took them off. Instead, David picked up his shepherd's staff, chose five smooth stones from a stream, and readied his sling.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was genuinely shocked that not a single Israelite soldier dared to defend God's honour against such insults, so despite being just a boy, he bravely offered to go out and fight Goliath himself. King Saul warned David that he was far too young and inexperienced to face such a fearsome warrior, but David calmly explained that while guarding his father's sheep, God had already helped him defeat both a lion and a bear, and he trusted that same God would help him defeat Goliath too. Saul reluctantly offered David his own royal armour and sword, but everything was so heavy and unfamiliar that David could barely move, so he removed it all. Instead, trusting completely in God rather than weapons, David picked up his shepherd's staff, selected five smooth stones from a nearby stream, and readied his sling for the fight ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>David was deeply troubled that not a single soldier in Israel's army was willing to defend God's honour against such open mockery, and despite his older brother Eliab scolding him for even asking questions, David bravely volunteered to face Goliath himself. King Saul pointed out that David was merely a boy while Goliath had been a trained warrior since his youth, but David calmly explained that while guarding his father's flock, God had already given him the strength to fight off and defeat both a lion and a bear, and he believed that same God would deliver him from this Philistine too. Saul eventually agreed to let David go, first offering him his own royal armour and sword, but David found he could barely walk wearing such heavy equipment, so he removed it all and set it aside. Instead, trusting entirely in God, David picked up nothing but his shepherd's staff, chose five smooth stones from a stream, and readied his sling for whatever lay ahead.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: David &amp; Goliath</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Goliath</li><li>the stone</li><li>Israel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Goliath mocks David for having no armour or sword</li><li>David says he comes in the name of the Lord</li><li>David slings a stone and strikes Goliath's forehead</li><li>Goliath falls and the Philistine army flees</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>laughed</li><li>mocked him fiercely</li><li>boldly</li><li>single stone</li><li>praised God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, David said he came in the name of the ___, then struck Goliath with a ___, and the Philistine army ___ in fear.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Goliath laughed when he saw David, a boy with no armour, sword, or shield, and mocked him fiercely. David answered boldly that he came in the name of the Lord, who would give the Philistines into his hands that day. As Goliath moved closer, David ran quickly toward him, swung his sling, and released a single stone, striking Goliath in the forehead. Goliath fell to the ground, and the Philistine army fled in fear, while all Israel praised God for the amazing victory He had given through David.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Goliath finally caught sight of David approaching, a boy with no armour, sword, or shield, he laughed scornfully and mocked him with cruel words, insulted that Israel would send a mere child to face him. David answered back boldly and without fear, declaring that he came not with sword or spear, but in the name of the Lord of Heaven's Armies, who would surely hand the Philistines over to Israel that day. As Goliath moved forward, David ran quickly toward him rather than away, reached into his bag, swung his sling with practised skill, and released a single stone that flew straight and true, striking Goliath directly in the forehead. Goliath toppled to the ground, and seeing their champion defeated, the entire Philistine army turned and fled in terror, while all of Israel rejoiced and praised God for the astonishing victory He had given them that day through one brave shepherd boy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The moment Goliath caught sight of David approaching, a boy without armour, sword, or shield, he laughed scornfully and hurled cruel, threatening insults at him, offended that Israel would dare send a mere child to face a trained warrior. Without a trace of fear, David answered back boldly, declaring that he came not relying on sword or spear, but in the powerful name of the Lord of Heaven's Armies, who would surely hand every last Philistine over to Israel that day. As Goliath began moving forward, David ran quickly straight toward his enormous opponent, reached calmly into his bag, swung his sling with practised precision, and released a single stone that struck Goliath squarely in the forehead. Goliath collapsed instantly, and the moment their champion fell, the entire Philistine army turned and fled back across the valley, while the Israelite soldiers rose with a great shout and chased after them, and all of Israel rejoiced together, praising God for the astonishing victory He had given them through nothing more than one faithful shepherd boy and a single small stone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: David &amp; Goliath</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 13 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 13 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 14 (David & Jonathan's Friendship) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, a huge Philistine warrior named ___ challenged Israel to send one man to fight him, but every soldier was too ___ until ___ arrived with food for his brothers.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, King ___ warned David he was too young, but David said ___ had helped him before, so he chose five smooth ___ and his sling instead of armour.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, David said he came in the name of the ___, then struck Goliath with a ___, and the Philistine army ___ in fear.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 14: David & Jonathan's Friendship":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 14’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 14: David & Jonathan's Friendship) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name David and Jonathan as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Jonathan</li><li>King Saul</li><li>the covenant</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David defeats Goliath and becomes a hero, and Jonathan becomes his close friend</li><li>Jonathan and David make a special covenant of loyal friendship before God</li><li>King Saul grows jealous of David's fame and tries to harm him</li><li>Jonathan warns David of danger and helps him escape, keeping their friendship</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonathan and David made a covenant of friendship and loyalty before God</li><li>King Saul was delighted by David's growing popularity and encouraged it</li><li>Jonathan gave David his robe, sword, bow, and belt as a sign of friendship</li><li>Jonathan refused to warn David when King Saul planned to harm him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David defeated Goliath, he became famous throughout the whole of Israel, and King Saul's son, Jonathan, immediately felt a strong bond of friendship with him. Jonathan loved David as much as he loved himself, and the two young men made a special covenant, a solemn promise of loyal friendship made before God. To show how much he valued this new friendship, Jonathan gave David several of his own royal belongings as gifts: his royal robe, his sword, his bow, and his belt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As time passed, David became a successful and popular soldier in Saul's army, winning battle after battle, and the people began praising David even more than they praised King Saul himself. This made Saul extremely jealous and afraid that David would one day take his throne. In his anger, Saul even threw his spear at David one evening while he played the harp, though David quickly dodged it and was not harmed. Saul began plotting secretly to have David killed, but Jonathan, still fiercely loyal to his best friend, refused to let any harm come to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jonathan warned David about his father's dangerous plans and helped him escape safely into hiding. Before David fled, the two friends met secretly in a field and wept together, knowing they might not see each other again for a long time. They renewed their covenant of friendship, asking God to watch over each other's families forever. Even though it meant standing against his own father, Jonathan kept his promise and remained David's loyal and trustworthy friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David's stunning victory over Goliath, news of his courage spread quickly throughout the whole nation of Israel, and King Saul's own son, Jonathan, felt an immediate and powerful bond of friendship form between them the very same day. The Bible describes Jonathan as loving David as much as he loved his own life, and before long the two young men made a special covenant together, a solemn and binding promise of loyal friendship made in the presence of God Himself. To demonstrate just how deeply he valued this new friendship, Jonathan generously gave David several of his own royal possessions as gifts, including his royal robe, his sword, his bow, and even his own belt, symbolically sharing his position and honour with his new friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the months went by, David continued serving successfully as a soldier in King Saul's army, winning battle after battle, until the people of Israel began openly praising David's achievements even more enthusiastically than they praised King Saul's own. This growing popularity filled Saul with intense jealousy and fear, convinced that David was secretly plotting to steal away his throne and kingdom. Overcome by this jealous anger on one particular evening, Saul suddenly hurled his spear directly at David while he played the harp for him, though David managed to dodge the weapon quickly and escaped completely unharmed. From that day forward, Saul began secretly plotting and scheming to have David killed altogether, yet Jonathan, remaining fiercely loyal to his dearest friend despite being the king's own son, refused to allow any harm to come to David.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Learning of his father's dangerous and secret intentions, Jonathan carefully warned David about the growing danger and helped arrange for him to escape safely into hiding before Saul's men could find him. Before parting ways, the two devoted friends met secretly out in an open field, where they embraced and wept together, painfully aware that this parting might keep them separated for a very long time, perhaps even forever. Together, they solemnly renewed their earlier covenant of friendship one final time, specifically asking God to watch over and protect each other's families faithfully in all the years ahead, no matter what happened to either of them. Even though doing so meant standing bravely against his own father the king, Jonathan chose to keep his sacred promise fully, remaining David's loyal, trustworthy, and courageous friend until the very end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David's stunning and unlikely victory over the giant Goliath, news of his remarkable courage spread rapidly throughout the entire nation of Israel, and King Saul's own son and heir, Jonathan, felt an immediate, powerful bond of friendship form between the two of them that very same day, almost as though their hearts were somehow knit together. The Bible describes Jonathan as loving David every bit as much as he loved his own life, and it was not long before the two young men formally made a special covenant together, a solemn, binding promise of complete loyalty and friendship made openly in the presence of God as their witness. To demonstrate just how deeply he valued this remarkable friendship, Jonathan generously gave David a set of his own royal possessions as meaningful gifts, including his robe, his sword, his bow, and even his own belt, symbolically sharing his royal position and honour with his newfound friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the following months passed, David continued to serve with great success and skill as a soldier and commander in King Saul's growing army, winning victory after victory in battle, until eventually the people of Israel began openly praising David's remarkable achievements even more loudly and enthusiastically than they praised King Saul's own. This rapidly growing popularity filled Saul with intense, burning jealousy and a deep, gnawing fear that David was secretly plotting all along to steal away his throne and kingdom for himself. Overcome by this consuming jealous anger on one particular evening, while David was quietly playing his harp to soothe Saul's troubled spirit as he often did, Saul suddenly seized his spear and hurled it directly at David, though David managed to dodge the weapon just in time and escaped completely unharmed. From that unsettling day forward, Saul began secretly plotting and scheming in earnest to have David killed once and for all, yet Jonathan, remaining fiercely and courageously loyal to his dearest friend despite being the reigning king's own son and rightful heir, spoke up boldly on David's behalf and refused to allow any harm to come to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having learned firsthand of his father's dangerous and secret intentions toward David, Jonathan devised a clever, secret plan to warn his friend safely: the two agreed that Jonathan would go out to a nearby field and shoot arrows as though practising archery, calling out different words to his servant depending on whether the coast was clear or whether David needed to flee immediately for his own safety. When the signal confirmed that real danger was near, Jonathan sent his servant boy back to the town alone and then met David secretly out in the open field himself, where the two devoted friends embraced tightly and wept together, painfully aware that this heartbreaking parting might well keep them separated from one another for a very long time, perhaps even forever. Together, standing alone in that quiet field, they solemnly renewed the covenant of friendship they had made so long before, specifically asking God to watch faithfully over and protect each other's families in all the uncertain years that still lay ahead of them. Even though remaining loyal to David meant standing bravely against his own father the king, and risking his own safety and future inheritance in doing so, Jonathan chose without hesitation to keep his sacred promise completely, remaining David's endlessly loyal, trustworthy, and courageous friend right to the very end.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Jonathan</li><li>the covenant</li><li>the robe</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David becomes famous after defeating Goliath</li><li>Jonathan feels an instant bond of friendship with David</li><li>Jonathan and David make a covenant of loyal friendship before God</li><li>Jonathan gives David his robe, sword, bow, and belt</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>immediately</li><li>as much as he loved himself</li><li>a solemn promise</li><li>royal robe</li><li>as gifts</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, after David defeated Goliath, ___ felt an instant bond of friendship with him, and they made a ___ of loyal friendship before ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David defeated Goliath, he became famous throughout the whole of Israel, and King Saul's son, Jonathan, immediately felt a strong bond of friendship with him. Jonathan loved David as much as he loved himself, and the two young men made a special covenant, a solemn promise of loyal friendship made before God. To show how much he valued this new friendship, Jonathan gave David several of his own royal belongings as gifts: his royal robe, his sword, his bow, and his belt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David's stunning victory over Goliath, news of his courage spread quickly throughout the whole nation of Israel, and King Saul's own son, Jonathan, felt an immediate and powerful bond of friendship form between them the very same day. The Bible describes Jonathan as loving David as much as he loved his own life, and before long the two young men made a special covenant together, a solemn and binding promise of loyal friendship made in the presence of God Himself. To demonstrate just how deeply he valued this new friendship, Jonathan generously gave David several of his own royal possessions as gifts, including his royal robe, his sword, his bow, and even his own belt, symbolically sharing his position and honour with his new friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After David's stunning and unlikely victory over the giant Goliath, news of his remarkable courage spread rapidly throughout the entire nation of Israel, and King Saul's own son and heir, Jonathan, felt an immediate, powerful bond of friendship form between the two of them that very same day, almost as though their hearts were somehow knit together. The Bible describes Jonathan as loving David every bit as much as he loved his own life, and it was not long before the two young men formally made a special covenant together, a solemn, binding promise of complete loyalty and friendship made openly in the presence of God as their witness. To demonstrate just how deeply he valued this remarkable friendship, Jonathan generously gave David a set of his own royal possessions as meaningful gifts, including his robe, his sword, his bow, and even his own belt, symbolically sharing his royal position and honour with his newfound friend.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David</li><li>Saul</li><li>Jonathan</li><li>the spear</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>David becomes a popular and successful soldier</li><li>The people praise David even more than King Saul</li><li>Saul grows jealous and throws his spear at David</li><li>Jonathan refuses to let harm come to his friend</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>extremely jealous</li><li>quickly dodged it</li><li>not harmed</li><li>secretly</li><li>loyal to his best friend</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, King Saul grew ___ of David and even threw his ___ at him, but ___ refused to let harm come to his friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As time passed, David became a successful and popular soldier in Saul's army, winning battle after battle, and the people began praising David even more than they praised King Saul himself. This made Saul extremely jealous and afraid that David would one day take his throne. In his anger, Saul even threw his spear at David one evening while he played the harp, though David quickly dodged it and was not harmed. Saul began plotting secretly to have David killed, but Jonathan, still fiercely loyal to his best friend, refused to let any harm come to him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the months went by, David continued serving successfully as a soldier in King Saul's army, winning battle after battle, until the people of Israel began openly praising David's achievements even more enthusiastically than they praised King Saul's own. This growing popularity filled Saul with intense jealousy and fear, convinced that David was secretly plotting to steal away his throne and kingdom. Overcome by this jealous anger on one particular evening, Saul suddenly hurled his spear directly at David while he played the harp for him, though David managed to dodge the weapon quickly and escaped completely unharmed. From that day forward, Saul began secretly plotting and scheming to have David killed altogether, yet Jonathan, remaining fiercely loyal to his dearest friend despite being the king's own son, refused to allow any harm to come to David.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the following months passed, David continued to serve with great success and skill as a soldier and commander in King Saul's growing army, winning victory after victory in battle, until eventually the people of Israel began openly praising David's remarkable achievements even more loudly and enthusiastically than they praised King Saul's own. This rapidly growing popularity filled Saul with intense, burning jealousy and a deep, gnawing fear that David was secretly plotting all along to steal away his throne and kingdom for himself. Overcome by this consuming jealous anger on one particular evening, while David was quietly playing his harp to soothe Saul's troubled spirit as he often did, Saul suddenly seized his spear and hurled it directly at David, though David managed to dodge the weapon just in time and escaped completely unharmed. From that unsettling day forward, Saul began secretly plotting and scheming in earnest to have David killed once and for all, yet Jonathan, remaining fiercely and courageously loyal to his dearest friend despite being the reigning king's own son and rightful heir, spoke up boldly on David's behalf and refused to allow any harm to come to him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonathan</li><li>David</li><li>the covenant</li><li>the field</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonathan warns David of his father's dangerous plans</li><li>David escapes safely into hiding</li><li>The two friends meet in a field and weep together</li><li>They renew their covenant, asking God to watch over their families</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>secretly</li><li>wept together</li><li>for a long time</li><li>renewed</li><li>trustworthy friend</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jonathan warned David and helped him ___, and the two friends met in a ___ to renew their ___ before God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jonathan warned David about his father's dangerous plans and helped him escape safely into hiding. Before David fled, the two friends met secretly in a field and wept together, knowing they might not see each other again for a long time. They renewed their covenant of friendship, asking God to watch over each other's families forever. Even though it meant standing against his own father, Jonathan kept his promise and remained David's loyal and trustworthy friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Learning of his father's dangerous and secret intentions, Jonathan carefully warned David about the growing danger and helped arrange for him to escape safely into hiding before Saul's men could find him. Before parting ways, the two devoted friends met secretly out in an open field, where they embraced and wept together, painfully aware that this parting might keep them separated for a very long time, perhaps even forever. Together, they solemnly renewed their earlier covenant of friendship one final time, specifically asking God to watch over and protect each other's families faithfully in all the years ahead, no matter what happened to either of them. Even though doing so meant standing bravely against his own father the king, Jonathan chose to keep his sacred promise fully, remaining David's loyal, trustworthy, and courageous friend until the very end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having learned firsthand of his father's dangerous and secret intentions toward David, Jonathan devised a clever, secret plan to warn his friend safely: the two agreed that Jonathan would go out to a nearby field and shoot arrows as though practising archery, calling out different words to his servant depending on whether the coast was clear or whether David needed to flee immediately for his own safety. When the signal confirmed that real danger was near, Jonathan sent his servant boy back to the town alone and then met David secretly out in the open field himself, where the two devoted friends embraced tightly and wept together, painfully aware that this heartbreaking parting might well keep them separated from one another for a very long time, perhaps even forever. Together, standing alone in that quiet field, they solemnly renewed the covenant of friendship they had made so long before, specifically asking God to watch faithfully over and protect each other's families in all the uncertain years that still lay ahead of them. Even though remaining loyal to David meant standing bravely against his own father the king, and risking his own safety and future inheritance in doing so, Jonathan chose without hesitation to keep his sacred promise completely, remaining David's endlessly loyal, trustworthy, and courageous friend right to the very end.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: David &amp; Jonathan's Friendship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 14 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 14 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 15 (Solomon's Wise Judgement) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, after David defeated Goliath, ___ felt an instant bond of friendship with him, and they made a ___ of loyal friendship before ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, King Saul grew ___ of David and even threw his ___ at him, but ___ refused to let harm come to his friend.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jonathan warned David and helped him ___, and the two friends met in a ___ to renew their ___ before God.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 15’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Solomon and the two women as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Solomon</li><li>the two women</li><li>the baby</li><li>the sword</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God appears to Solomon in a dream and offers him anything he asks for</li><li>Solomon asks God for wisdom to govern Israel well, rather than riches</li><li>Two women both claim to be the mother of the same baby</li><li>Solomon suggests cutting the baby in two, and this reveals the true mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Solomon asked God for great riches and a long life above everything else</li><li>Two women came before Solomon, each claiming to be the mother of the same baby</li><li>Solomon ordered his guards to cut the baby in half, ending the dispute at once</li><li>The real mother begged Solomon to give the baby to the other woman</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, not long after he became king, God appeared to Solomon in a dream and told him he could ask for anything at all that he wanted. Instead of asking for riches, a long life, or victory over his enemies, Solomon humbly asked God for wisdom to lead the people of Israel well and to always know the difference between right and wrong. God was very pleased with Solomon's unselfish request and promised to give him great wisdom, along with riches and honour besides.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon after, two women came before King Solomon with a difficult problem. Both women lived in the same house and had each recently had a baby, but one baby had died during the night. Each woman claimed that the living baby was her own son, and neither would agree with the other. They argued in front of the king, and there was no one else who had seen what truly happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Solomon thought carefully, then ordered a sword be brought, saying he would cut the living baby in two and give half to each woman. The real mother's heart broke with love for her son, and she begged Solomon to give the baby to the other woman rather than let him be harmed. The other woman coldly agreed to the plan. Solomon knew at once that the woman who showed such love was the true mother, and he gave her the baby. All of Israel heard about this wise decision and respected Solomon greatly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night not long after Solomon became king, God appeared to him in a dream and generously told him that he could ask for absolutely anything he wanted, and it would be given to him. Rather than asking for great riches, a long life, or victory over his many enemies, as many kings might have done, Solomon humbly asked God instead for wisdom and a discerning heart, so that he could govern the people of Israel fairly and always know the difference between what was right and what was wrong. God was deeply pleased that Solomon had asked for something so unselfish, and promised not only to give him extraordinary wisdom beyond anyone before or after him, but also great riches and honour on top of it, since he had not asked selfishly for these things himself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, two women came together before King Solomon, bringing with them an extremely difficult and emotional problem for him to settle. Both women lived together in the very same house, and both had given birth to babies within just a few days of each other, but tragically, one of the babies had died suddenly during the night. Each woman now insisted passionately that the surviving, living baby belonged to her, and neither woman was willing to back down or agree with the other's version of events. They argued bitterly in front of the king and his entire court, and since there had been no other witnesses present that night, there was seemingly no way for anyone to know for certain which woman was telling the truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After listening carefully to both sides of the argument, Solomon thought for a long moment and then ordered that a sword be brought to him, announcing that he would settle the dispute fairly by cutting the living baby cleanly in two and giving one equal half to each grieving woman. Hearing this shocking suggestion, the baby's real mother was overcome with heartbroken love and compassion for her son, and she begged Solomon desperately to give the baby to the other woman instead, rather than see her own child harmed in any way. The other woman, however, coldly agreed to Solomon's proposed plan without any hesitation or protest at all. Recognising immediately that only the baby's true mother would ever sacrifice her own claim out of such selfless love, Solomon confidently gave the living baby to her. Word of this remarkably wise judgement soon spread throughout the whole of Israel, and the people came to deeply respect and admire their new king's God-given wisdom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, not long after Solomon had become king following his father David's death, he travelled to the city of Gibeon, where he offered many sacrifices to God, and it was there that God appeared to him in a dream and generously invited him to ask for absolutely anything he wanted, promising it would be granted. Rather than requesting great personal riches, a long life, or victory over his many enemies, as other kings might have chosen, Solomon instead humbly asked God for wisdom and a discerning heart above everything else, so that he might govern the often divided nation of Israel fairly and always know the difference between right and wrong. God was deeply pleased that Solomon had asked for something so unselfish, and promised not only to give him wisdom greater than anyone before or after him, but also great riches and honour on top of it, precisely because he had not selfishly requested such things himself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long after this dream, two women living together in the same house came before King Solomon's royal court, bringing an extraordinarily difficult and emotional dispute for the young king to settle. Both women had given birth to healthy baby boys within days of one another, but tragically, during the night, one of the two babies had died after his mother accidentally rolled over onto him in her sleep. Grief-stricken, that woman had apparently taken the other woman's living baby and quietly placed her own dead child in its place while the other mother slept, unaware of what had happened. When morning came and the true mother realised the child beside her was not her own son, both women appeared before the king, each now stubbornly insisting the surviving baby belonged to her alone. They argued bitterly in front of the entire court, and since there had been no witnesses that night, there seemed no reliable way to determine which woman was telling the truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having listened carefully to both sides of this heartbreaking argument, Solomon paused thoughtfully before ordering that a sword be brought to him, announcing that he intended to settle the dispute fairly by cutting the surviving baby in two and giving one equal half to each grieving woman. Upon hearing this shocking proposal, the baby's true mother was completely overcome with heartbroken compassion for her son, and she begged Solomon urgently to instead give the living baby entirely to the other woman, rather than allow her precious child to be harmed in any way. The other woman, however, coldly agreed at once to Solomon's proposed plan without showing the slightest hesitation or genuine grief. Recognising immediately that only the baby's true mother would ever willingly sacrifice her own claim purely out of such selfless love, Solomon calmly gave the living baby to her without further delay. Word of this remarkably wise judgement quickly spread throughout the nation of Israel, and the people came to hold their young king in tremendous awe, recognising that the wisdom of God Himself was working powerfully within him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Solomon</li><li>God</li><li>wisdom</li><li>a dream</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God appears to Solomon in a dream</li><li>God tells Solomon he can ask for anything he wants</li><li>Solomon asks for wisdom instead of riches or long life</li><li>God is pleased and promises to give Solomon wisdom, riches, and honour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>one night</li><li>instead of asking for riches</li><li>know the difference between right and wrong</li><li>pleased with</li><li>besides</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, God appeared to Solomon in a ___ and offered him anything he wanted, so Solomon asked for ___ instead of riches or a long ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, not long after he became king, God appeared to Solomon in a dream and told him he could ask for anything at all that he wanted. Instead of asking for riches, a long life, or victory over his enemies, Solomon humbly asked God for wisdom to lead the people of Israel well and to always know the difference between right and wrong. God was very pleased with Solomon's unselfish request and promised to give him great wisdom, along with riches and honour besides.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night not long after Solomon became king, God appeared to him in a dream and generously told him that he could ask for absolutely anything he wanted, and it would be given to him. Rather than asking for great riches, a long life, or victory over his many enemies, as many kings might have done, Solomon humbly asked God instead for wisdom and a discerning heart, so that he could govern the people of Israel fairly and always know the difference between what was right and what was wrong. God was deeply pleased that Solomon had asked for something so unselfish, and promised not only to give him extraordinary wisdom beyond anyone before or after him, but also great riches and honour on top of it, since he had not asked selfishly for these things himself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, not long after Solomon had become king following his father David's death, he travelled to the city of Gibeon, where he offered many sacrifices to God, and it was there that God appeared to him in a dream and generously invited him to ask for absolutely anything he wanted, promising it would be granted. Rather than requesting great personal riches, a long life, or victory over his many enemies, as other kings might have chosen, Solomon instead humbly asked God for wisdom and a discerning heart above everything else, so that he might govern the often divided nation of Israel fairly and always know the difference between right and wrong. God was deeply pleased that Solomon had asked for something so unselfish, and promised not only to give him wisdom greater than anyone before or after him, but also great riches and honour on top of it, precisely because he had not selfishly requested such things himself.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>two women</li><li>Solomon</li><li>the baby</li><li>the house</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two women who live in the same house each had a baby</li><li>One baby dies during the night</li><li>Both women claim the living baby is their own</li><li>They argue in front of the king with no other witnesses</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>soon after</li><li>recently had a baby</li><li>neither would agree</li><li>in front of the king</li><li>truly happened</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, two women who lived in the same house both claimed the same ___ was theirs after one baby had ___ during the night, so they argued before ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon after, two women came before King Solomon with a difficult problem. Both women lived in the same house and had each recently had a baby, but one baby had died during the night. Each woman claimed that the living baby was her own son, and neither would agree with the other. They argued in front of the king, and there was no one else who had seen what truly happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, two women came together before King Solomon, bringing with them an extremely difficult and emotional problem for him to settle. Both women lived together in the very same house, and both had given birth to babies within just a few days of each other, but tragically, one of the babies had died suddenly during the night. Each woman now insisted passionately that the surviving, living baby belonged to her, and neither woman was willing to back down or agree with the other's version of events. They argued bitterly in front of the king and his entire court, and since there had been no other witnesses present that night, there was seemingly no way for anyone to know for certain which woman was telling the truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long after this dream, two women living together in the same house came before King Solomon's royal court, bringing an extraordinarily difficult and emotional dispute for the young king to settle. Both women had given birth to healthy baby boys within days of one another, but tragically, during the night, one of the two babies had died after his mother accidentally rolled over onto him in her sleep. Grief-stricken, that woman had apparently taken the other woman's living baby and quietly placed her own dead child in its place while the other mother slept, unaware of what had happened. When morning came and the true mother realised the child beside her was not her own son, both women appeared before the king, each now stubbornly insisting the surviving baby belonged to her alone. They argued bitterly in front of the entire court, and since there had been no witnesses that night, there seemed no reliable way to determine which woman was telling the truth.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Solomon</li><li>the sword</li><li>the real mother</li><li>the baby</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Solomon orders a sword and says he will cut the baby in two</li><li>The real mother begs Solomon to give the baby to the other woman</li><li>The other woman agrees coldly to the plan</li><li>Solomon gives the baby to the real, loving mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>thought carefully</li><li>heart broke with love</li><li>rather than let him be harmed</li><li>coldly agreed</li><li>respected Solomon greatly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Solomon said he would cut the baby in two, but the real mother begged him to give the baby to the ___ instead, so Solomon knew she was the true ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Solomon thought carefully, then ordered a sword be brought, saying he would cut the living baby in two and give half to each woman. The real mother's heart broke with love for her son, and she begged Solomon to give the baby to the other woman rather than let him be harmed. The other woman coldly agreed to the plan. Solomon knew at once that the woman who showed such love was the true mother, and he gave her the baby. All of Israel heard about this wise decision and respected Solomon greatly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After listening carefully to both sides of the argument, Solomon thought for a long moment and then ordered that a sword be brought to him, announcing that he would settle the dispute fairly by cutting the living baby cleanly in two and giving one equal half to each grieving woman. Hearing this shocking suggestion, the baby's real mother was overcome with heartbroken love and compassion for her son, and she begged Solomon desperately to give the baby to the other woman instead, rather than see her own child harmed in any way. The other woman, however, coldly agreed to Solomon's proposed plan without any hesitation or protest at all. Recognising immediately that only the baby's true mother would ever sacrifice her own claim out of such selfless love, Solomon confidently gave the living baby to her. Word of this remarkably wise judgement soon spread throughout the whole of Israel, and the people came to deeply respect and admire their new king's God-given wisdom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Having listened carefully to both sides of this heartbreaking argument, Solomon paused thoughtfully before ordering that a sword be brought to him, announcing that he intended to settle the dispute fairly by cutting the surviving baby in two and giving one equal half to each grieving woman. Upon hearing this shocking proposal, the baby's true mother was completely overcome with heartbroken compassion for her son, and she begged Solomon urgently to instead give the living baby entirely to the other woman, rather than allow her precious child to be harmed in any way. The other woman, however, coldly agreed at once to Solomon's proposed plan without showing the slightest hesitation or genuine grief. Recognising immediately that only the baby's true mother would ever willingly sacrifice her own claim purely out of such selfless love, Solomon calmly gave the living baby to her without further delay. Word of this remarkably wise judgement quickly spread throughout the nation of Israel, and the people came to hold their young king in tremendous awe, recognising that the wisdom of God Himself was working powerfully within him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Solomon's Wise Judgement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 15 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 15 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 16 (Elijah on Mount Carmel) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, God appeared to Solomon in a ___ and offered him anything he wanted, so Solomon asked for ___ instead of riches or a long ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, two women who lived in the same house both claimed the same ___ was theirs after one baby had ___ during the night, so they argued before ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Solomon said he would cut the baby in two, but the real mother begged him to give the baby to the ___ instead, so Solomon knew she was the true ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (Green belt) — Stories 16–20":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 16’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Elijah and the prophets of Baal as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elijah</li><li>the prophets of Baal</li><li>the altar</li><li>the fire</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Ahab and many Israelites are worshipping the false god Baal</li><li>Elijah challenges 450 prophets of Baal to a contest on Mount Carmel</li><li>Elijah rebuilds God's altar, soaks it with water, and prays a simple prayer</li><li>God sends fire from heaven, and the people declare that the Lord is God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal to see whose god could send fire</li><li>The prophets of Baal cried out to Baal all morning and fire came immediately</li><li>Elijah had his altar soaked with water three times before he prayed</li><li>When the fire fell, the people declared that the Lord alone is God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the reign of wicked King Ahab, many people in Israel had stopped worshipping the one true God and were worshipping a false god named Baal instead. The prophet Elijah bravely challenged 450 prophets of Baal to a contest on Mount Carmel, in front of all the watching people of Israel. Each side would build an altar and place a sacrifice on it, but neither side was allowed to light the fire themselves. Whichever god sent down fire from heaven to burn up the sacrifice would be shown to be the one true and living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The prophets of Baal went first, shouting and dancing around their altar from morning until noon, begging Baal to send fire. Nothing happened, no matter how loudly they called out. Elijah mocked them gently, suggesting that maybe Baal was busy or sleeping. The prophets shouted even louder all afternoon, but still no fire came, no matter how hard they tried.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it was Elijah's turn, he repaired God's broken-down altar and had the people pour water over the sacrifice and the wood three times, until water even filled a trench around the altar. Then Elijah prayed a simple, calm prayer, asking God to show that He alone was God in Israel. Instantly, fire fell from heaven and burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones, and even the water in the trench. When the people saw this, they fell to the ground and cried out, 'The Lord, He is God!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the troubled reign of wicked King Ahab, many people throughout Israel had gradually abandoned worshipping the one true God and had turned instead to worshipping a false god named Baal, largely because of the influence of Ahab's wife, Queen Jezebel. To settle the matter publicly once and for all, the prophet Elijah bravely challenged 450 prophets of Baal to a public contest held on Mount Carmel, gathering all the people of Israel together to witness it. The rules were simple and fair: each side would build their own altar and place a sacrifice upon it, but neither side would be allowed to light the fire themselves. Whichever god responded by sending down fire from heaven to burn up the sacrifice completely would be proven, beyond any doubt, to be the one true and living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The prophets of Baal went first, according to the agreed order, shouting loudly and dancing wildly around their altar from early morning right through until noontime, desperately begging and pleading with Baal to send down fire from the sky. Despite their frantic efforts, absolutely nothing happened at all, no matter how loudly or how long they continued calling out to their silent god. Elijah, watching calmly nearby, began mocking them gently but pointedly, suggesting perhaps that Baal was simply too busy thinking, travelling somewhere else, or even fast asleep and needed waking up. Stung by his teasing, the prophets only shouted even louder and more desperately throughout the entire long afternoon, cutting themselves and working themselves into a frenzy, yet still, after all their frantic effort, absolutely no fire came down from the sky at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it finally came time for Elijah's turn, he first carefully repaired God's old, broken-down altar using twelve stones, one for each of the twelve tribes of Israel, and then astonishingly instructed the watching people to pour large jars of water over both the sacrifice and the surrounding wood not just once, but three separate times, until water even filled and overflowed a trench he had dug all the way around the base of the altar. Then, in stark contrast to the earlier frantic shouting, Elijah simply prayed a short, calm, and quietly confident prayer, humbly asking God to reveal Himself clearly as the one true God in Israel that day. Instantly and dramatically, fire fell suddenly from heaven and completely burned up not only the sacrifice itself, but also the wood, the stones of the altar, the surrounding soil, and even every last drop of water sitting in the trench. When the watching crowd saw this astonishing and undeniable miracle unfold before their very eyes, they immediately fell face down onto the ground in awe and cried out together as one voice, 'The Lord, He is God! The Lord, He is God!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the long, troubled reign of wicked King Ahab, Israel had suffered through more than three years of severe drought and famine, sent by God because so many people throughout the nation had abandoned worshipping the one true God and turned instead to worshipping a false, lifeless god named Baal, largely due to the influence of Ahab's foreign wife, Queen Jezebel. Emerging boldly after hiding from Ahab's anger for years, the prophet Elijah finally confronted the king and arranged a dramatic public contest to settle the matter once and for all: he would face 450 prophets of Baal on the summit of Mount Carmel, with all the people of Israel gathered as witnesses. The rules Elijah proposed were simple and impossible to fake: each side would build its own altar and place a sacrificial bull upon it, but neither side would be permitted to light the fire themselves. Whichever god responded by sending genuine fire down from heaven to consume the sacrifice would be proven beyond doubt to be the one true and living God of Israel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>According to the order they had agreed, the prophets of Baal went first, shouting at the top of their voices and dancing wildly around their altar from the earliest hour of the morning right through until well past noon, desperately begging their silent god to send fire down. Despite all their frantic effort, absolutely nothing happened, no matter how loudly or how long they called out to a god who never answered. Elijah, watching calmly from nearby, eventually began mocking the prophets gently yet pointedly, suggesting with dry humour that perhaps their god was simply too deep in thought, travelling far away, or even fast asleep and needed waking up. Stung by his teasing, the increasingly desperate prophets shouted even louder throughout the remaining afternoon, yet still, despite every effort, absolutely no fire ever came down from the silent sky above.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it finally came time for Elijah's own turn, he carefully repaired God's old, broken-down altar, using twelve large stones, one to represent each of the twelve tribes descended from Jacob, before astonishingly instructing the watching crowd to pour jars of precious water, scarce after years of drought, over the sacrifice and surrounding wood, not once, but three separate times, until the water filled and overflowed a deep trench he had dug around the base of the altar. Then, in striking contrast to the prophets' earlier frantic shouting, Elijah simply offered a short, calm, quietly confident prayer, humbly asking God to reveal Himself clearly that day as the one true God over Israel. Instantly, in response to this simple prayer, fire fell suddenly from the open heavens and completely consumed the sacrifice, every piece of wood, every stone of the altar, and even every drop of water in the trench. When the astonished crowd witnessed this undeniable miracle, they fell face down in reverence and cried out together with one voice, 'The Lord, He is God! The Lord, He is God!' Not long afterward, exactly as Elijah had promised, the terrible three-year drought finally broke, and welcome rain began falling once more across the grateful land.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elijah</li><li>the prophets of Baal</li><li>Mount Carmel</li><li>the altar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Ahab and many Israelites are worshipping the false god Baal</li><li>Elijah challenges 450 prophets of Baal to a contest</li><li>Each side builds an altar and places a sacrifice on it</li><li>Whichever god sends fire from heaven will be shown as the true God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wicked King Ahab</li><li>stopped worshipping God</li><li>in front of all the people</li><li>not light the fire themselves</li><li>shown to be</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Elijah challenged the ___ to a contest on ___, where whichever god sent down ___ would be shown as the true God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the reign of wicked King Ahab, many people in Israel had stopped worshipping the one true God and were worshipping a false god named Baal instead. The prophet Elijah bravely challenged 450 prophets of Baal to a contest on Mount Carmel, in front of all the watching people of Israel. Each side would build an altar and place a sacrifice on it, but neither side was allowed to light the fire themselves. Whichever god sent down fire from heaven to burn up the sacrifice would be shown to be the one true and living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the troubled reign of wicked King Ahab, many people throughout Israel had gradually abandoned worshipping the one true God and had turned instead to worshipping a false god named Baal, largely because of the influence of Ahab's wife, Queen Jezebel. To settle the matter publicly once and for all, the prophet Elijah bravely challenged 450 prophets of Baal to a public contest held on Mount Carmel, gathering all the people of Israel together to witness it. The rules were simple and fair: each side would build their own altar and place a sacrifice upon it, but neither side would be allowed to light the fire themselves. Whichever god responded by sending down fire from heaven to burn up the sacrifice completely would be proven, beyond any doubt, to be the one true and living God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the long, troubled reign of wicked King Ahab, Israel had suffered through more than three years of severe drought and famine, sent by God because so many people throughout the nation had abandoned worshipping the one true God and turned instead to worshipping a false, lifeless god named Baal, largely due to the influence of Ahab's foreign wife, Queen Jezebel. Emerging boldly after hiding from Ahab's anger for years, the prophet Elijah finally confronted the king and arranged a dramatic public contest to settle the matter once and for all: he would face 450 prophets of Baal on the summit of Mount Carmel, with all the people of Israel gathered as witnesses. The rules Elijah proposed were simple and impossible to fake: each side would build its own altar and place a sacrificial bull upon it, but neither side would be permitted to light the fire themselves. Whichever god responded by sending genuine fire down from heaven to consume the sacrifice would be proven beyond doubt to be the one true and living God of Israel.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the prophets of Baal</li><li>Elijah</li><li>Baal</li><li>the altar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The prophets of Baal shout and dance around their altar all morning</li><li>They beg Baal to send fire, but nothing happens</li><li>Elijah mocks them, suggesting Baal is sleeping or busy</li><li>The prophets shout louder all afternoon, but still no fire comes</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>shouting and dancing</li><li>from morning until noon</li><li>mocked them gently</li><li>even louder</li><li>no matter how hard they tried</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the prophets of Baal shouted and danced all morning, but no ___ came, so Elijah mocked them by saying maybe ___ was sleeping or busy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The prophets of Baal went first, shouting and dancing around their altar from morning until noon, begging Baal to send fire. Nothing happened, no matter how loudly they called out. Elijah mocked them gently, suggesting that maybe Baal was busy or sleeping. The prophets shouted even louder all afternoon, but still no fire came, no matter how hard they tried.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The prophets of Baal went first, according to the agreed order, shouting loudly and dancing wildly around their altar from early morning right through until noontime, desperately begging and pleading with Baal to send down fire from the sky. Despite their frantic efforts, absolutely nothing happened at all, no matter how loudly or how long they continued calling out to their silent god. Elijah, watching calmly nearby, began mocking them gently but pointedly, suggesting perhaps that Baal was simply too busy thinking, travelling somewhere else, or even fast asleep and needed waking up. Stung by his teasing, the prophets only shouted even louder and more desperately throughout the entire long afternoon, cutting themselves and working themselves into a frenzy, yet still, after all their frantic effort, absolutely no fire came down from the sky at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>According to the order they had agreed, the prophets of Baal went first, shouting at the top of their voices and dancing wildly around their altar from the earliest hour of the morning right through until well past noon, desperately begging their silent god to send fire down. Despite all their frantic effort, absolutely nothing happened, no matter how loudly or how long they called out to a god who never answered. Elijah, watching calmly from nearby, eventually began mocking the prophets gently yet pointedly, suggesting with dry humour that perhaps their god was simply too deep in thought, travelling far away, or even fast asleep and needed waking up. Stung by his teasing, the increasingly desperate prophets shouted even louder throughout the remaining afternoon, yet still, despite every effort, absolutely no fire ever came down from the silent sky above.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elijah</li><li>the altar</li><li>the fire</li><li>the people</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Elijah repairs God's altar and has it soaked with water three times</li><li>Elijah prays a simple, calm prayer to God</li><li>Fire falls from heaven and burns up everything on the altar</li><li>The people fall down and declare that the Lord is God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>repaired</li><li>three times</li><li>simple, calm prayer</li><li>instantly</li><li>fell to the ground</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Elijah had the altar soaked with ___ three times, prayed to God, and ___ fell from heaven, so the people cried out, 'The ___, He is God!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it was Elijah's turn, he repaired God's broken-down altar and had the people pour water over the sacrifice and the wood three times, until water even filled a trench around the altar. Then Elijah prayed a simple, calm prayer, asking God to show that He alone was God in Israel. Instantly, fire fell from heaven and burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones, and even the water in the trench. When the people saw this, they fell to the ground and cried out, 'The Lord, He is God!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it finally came time for Elijah's turn, he first carefully repaired God's old, broken-down altar using twelve stones, one for each of the twelve tribes of Israel, and then astonishingly instructed the watching people to pour large jars of water over both the sacrifice and the surrounding wood not just once, but three separate times, until water even filled and overflowed a trench he had dug all the way around the base of the altar. Then, in stark contrast to the earlier frantic shouting, Elijah simply prayed a short, calm, and quietly confident prayer, humbly asking God to reveal Himself clearly as the one true God in Israel that day. Instantly and dramatically, fire fell suddenly from heaven and completely burned up not only the sacrifice itself, but also the wood, the stones of the altar, the surrounding soil, and even every last drop of water sitting in the trench. When the watching crowd saw this astonishing and undeniable miracle unfold before their very eyes, they immediately fell face down onto the ground in awe and cried out together as one voice, 'The Lord, He is God! The Lord, He is God!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it finally came time for Elijah's own turn, he carefully repaired God's old, broken-down altar, using twelve large stones, one to represent each of the twelve tribes descended from Jacob, before astonishingly instructing the watching crowd to pour jars of precious water, scarce after years of drought, over the sacrifice and surrounding wood, not once, but three separate times, until the water filled and overflowed a deep trench he had dug around the base of the altar. Then, in striking contrast to the prophets' earlier frantic shouting, Elijah simply offered a short, calm, quietly confident prayer, humbly asking God to reveal Himself clearly that day as the one true God over Israel. Instantly, in response to this simple prayer, fire fell suddenly from the open heavens and completely consumed the sacrifice, every piece of wood, every stone of the altar, and even every drop of water in the trench. When the astonished crowd witnessed this undeniable miracle, they fell face down in reverence and cried out together with one voice, 'The Lord, He is God! The Lord, He is God!' Not long afterward, exactly as Elijah had promised, the terrible three-year drought finally broke, and welcome rain began falling once more across the grateful land.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Elijah on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 16 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 16 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 17 (Shadrach, Meshach & Abednego) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Elijah challenged the ___ to a contest on ___, where whichever god sent down ___ would be shown as the true God.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the prophets of Baal shouted and danced all morning, but no ___ came, so Elijah mocked them by saying maybe ___ was sleeping or busy.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Elijah had the altar soaked with ___ three times, prayed to God, and ___ fell from heaven, so the people cried out, 'The ___, He is God!'</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach & Abednego":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 17’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach & Abednego) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name King Nebuchadnezzar and Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Nebuchadnezzar</li><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego</li><li>the golden statue</li><li>the furnace</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Nebuchadnezzar builds a huge golden statue and commands everyone to bow to it</li><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego refuse to bow because they only worship God</li><li>The furious king has the three men thrown into a blazing furnace</li><li>God protects the men, and a fourth figure appears with them, unharmed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Nebuchadnezzar ordered everyone to bow down to a giant golden statue</li><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego happily bowed down to avoid getting in trouble</li><li>The three men were thrown into a blazing furnace for refusing to worship the statue</li><li>When the king looked into the furnace, he saw a fourth figure walking with the men</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon built an enormous golden statue and commanded everyone in his kingdom to bow down and worship it whenever music played, or else be thrown into a blazing furnace. Three young Hebrew men, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, refused to bow down, because they worshipped only the one true God and would not worship an idol. Some jealous officials reported this to the king, who became furious.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The king gave the three men one more chance, warning them that if they still refused, they would be thrown immediately into the blazing furnace. The three men respectfully but firmly told the king that their God was able to save them from the fire, but even if He chose not to, they would still never bow down to worship the golden statue. Enraged by their answer, the king ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than usual and had the three men tied up and thrown inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The furnace was so hot that the soldiers who threw the men in were killed by the flames, yet Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego walked around unharmed inside the fire. Astonished, King Nebuchadnezzar looked closer and saw a fourth figure walking with them, one that looked like a divine being. The king called the men out of the furnace, and everyone saw that not even their hair was singed or their clothes burned. The king praised God for sending His angel to rescue the three men who trusted Him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon ordered an enormous golden statue to be built, towering many metres high, and then commanded absolutely everyone throughout his vast kingdom to bow down and worship it the very moment they heard a particular combination of musical instruments play, warning that anyone who refused would be thrown immediately into a blazing, fiery furnace. Three young Hebrew men serving as officials in the kingdom, named Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, bravely refused to bow down before the statue along with everyone else, because they worshipped only the one true God of Israel and would never worship a lifeless golden idol, no matter the cost to themselves. Some jealous Babylonian officials, resentful of the three men's high positions, quickly reported their disobedience directly to the king, who became absolutely furious upon hearing the news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Giving the three men one final opportunity to save themselves, the king warned them sternly that if they continued refusing to bow down, they would be thrown without delay into the blazing furnace, and he mockingly asked what god could possibly rescue them from his hands. The three men respectfully yet firmly told the king that their God was more than able to save them from the fire if He so chose, but they wanted the king to know clearly that even if He chose not to rescue them, they would still never bow down and worship his golden statue under any circumstances. Enraged beyond measure by their calm, unwavering defiance, the king ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than its usual temperature, and commanded his strongest soldiers to tie the three men up securely and throw them straight into the roaring flames.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The furnace had been heated so extremely hot that the powerful soldiers who threw the three men inside were themselves instantly killed by the intense heat and flames, and yet, remarkably, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego were seen walking calmly around unharmed inside the blazing fire. Astonished and deeply shaken, King Nebuchadnezzar leaned forward to look more closely into the furnace and was amazed to see a fourth figure walking freely alongside the three men, one whose appearance looked strikingly like a divine, heavenly being. Overwhelmed, the king immediately called the three men to come out of the furnace, and when everyone gathered around to examine them closely, they discovered that not a single hair on their heads had been singed, their clothing was completely unburned, and there was not even the faint smell of smoke on them anywhere. Deeply humbled, King Nebuchadnezzar publicly praised the God of Israel for sending His angel to miraculously rescue the three faithful men who had trusted Him rather than bow down.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon ordered his workers to construct an enormous golden statue, said to stand around twenty-seven metres tall, and set it up on the plain of Dura before commanding every official, governor, and citizen throughout his vast empire to bow down and worship it the instant they heard a specific combination of musical instruments begin to play, warning that anyone who dared disobey would be thrown into a blazing furnace. Three young Hebrew men appointed to high positions within the kingdom's provincial administration, named Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, bravely and openly refused to bow down before the golden statue along with everyone else gathered there, because they were devoted entirely to worshipping only the one true God of Israel and refused to worship any lifeless idol, no matter the price. Some jealous Babylonian astrologers, resentful of the honour given to these three foreign officials, seized their opportunity and quickly reported the men's disobedience directly to King Nebuchadnezzar, who flew into a furious rage upon hearing the news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Giving the three men one final chance to reconsider and save their own lives, the enraged king summoned them before him and warned sternly that if they persisted in refusing to bow down, they would be thrown immediately into the blazing furnace, arrogantly demanding to know what god could possibly be powerful enough to rescue them from his own royal hands. The three men, standing calmly and respectfully yet remaining completely unwavering in their conviction, told the king plainly that their God was more than able to rescue them from the flames if He so chose, but they wanted him to understand clearly that even if their God chose not to save them on this occasion, they would still never bow down or worship his golden statue under any circumstances whatsoever. Enraged beyond all measure by their calm, unwavering defiance in front of his entire royal court, the king furiously ordered that the furnace be heated to seven times its normal temperature, and commanded some of his own strongest soldiers to bind the three men tightly in their cloaks, trousers, and turbans before throwing them directly into the roaring flames below.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So intensely hot had the furnace been made that the soldiers responsible for throwing the three bound men inside were themselves instantly killed by the overwhelming heat the moment they approached its opening, and yet, remarkably, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego could clearly be seen walking calmly around completely unharmed inside the blazing fire itself. Astonished and visibly shaken, King Nebuchadnezzar leapt to his feet and leaned forward to peer more closely into the furnace, and was amazed to discover a fourth figure walking freely alongside the three men, one whose radiant appearance looked strikingly like a divine, heavenly being sent to protect them. Overwhelmed with awe, the king immediately called for the three men to come out at once, and when the officials gathered around to examine them, they discovered that not a single hair on their heads had been singed, their clothing remained completely unburned, and there was not even the faint smell of smoke clinging to them. Deeply humbled, King Nebuchadnezzar publicly praised the God of Israel before his entire royal court for sending His angel to rescue the three faithful men who had trusted Him rather than bow down to any other god.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Nebuchadnezzar</li><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego</li><li>the golden statue</li><li>the furnace</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The king builds a huge golden statue</li><li>He commands everyone to bow down to it or be thrown in the furnace</li><li>The three men refuse to bow because they worship only God</li><li>Jealous officials report them to the king</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>enormous</li><li>whenever music played</li><li>worshipped only the one true God</li><li>jealous officials</li><li>furious</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, King Nebuchadnezzar built a huge golden ___ and ordered everyone to bow, but ___ refused because they worshipped only ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon built an enormous golden statue and commanded everyone in his kingdom to bow down and worship it whenever music played, or else be thrown into a blazing furnace. Three young Hebrew men, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, refused to bow down, because they worshipped only the one true God and would not worship an idol. Some jealous officials reported this to the king, who became furious.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon ordered an enormous golden statue to be built, towering many metres high, and then commanded absolutely everyone throughout his vast kingdom to bow down and worship it the very moment they heard a particular combination of musical instruments play, warning that anyone who refused would be thrown immediately into a blazing, fiery furnace. Three young Hebrew men serving as officials in the kingdom, named Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, bravely refused to bow down before the statue along with everyone else, because they worshipped only the one true God of Israel and would never worship a lifeless golden idol, no matter the cost to themselves. Some jealous Babylonian officials, resentful of the three men's high positions, quickly reported their disobedience directly to the king, who became absolutely furious upon hearing the news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon ordered his workers to construct an enormous golden statue, said to stand around twenty-seven metres tall, and set it up on the plain of Dura before commanding every official, governor, and citizen throughout his vast empire to bow down and worship it the instant they heard a specific combination of musical instruments begin to play, warning that anyone who dared disobey would be thrown into a blazing furnace. Three young Hebrew men appointed to high positions within the kingdom's provincial administration, named Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, bravely and openly refused to bow down before the golden statue along with everyone else gathered there, because they were devoted entirely to worshipping only the one true God of Israel and refused to worship any lifeless idol, no matter the price. Some jealous Babylonian astrologers, resentful of the honour given to these three foreign officials, seized their opportunity and quickly reported the men's disobedience directly to King Nebuchadnezzar, who flew into a furious rage upon hearing the news.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the king</li><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego</li><li>the furnace</li><li>God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The king gives the three men one more chance to bow down</li><li>They say God is able to save them, but they still won't bow</li><li>The king becomes enraged at their answer</li><li>He has the furnace heated seven times hotter and the men thrown in</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>one more chance</li><li>respectfully but firmly</li><li>even if He chose not to</li><li>enraged</li><li>seven times hotter</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the three men told the king that ___ could save them, but even if not, they would never ___, so the furious king had the furnace heated seven times ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The king gave the three men one more chance, warning them that if they still refused, they would be thrown immediately into the blazing furnace. The three men respectfully but firmly told the king that their God was able to save them from the fire, but even if He chose not to, they would still never bow down to worship the golden statue. Enraged by their answer, the king ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than usual and had the three men tied up and thrown inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Giving the three men one final opportunity to save themselves, the king warned them sternly that if they continued refusing to bow down, they would be thrown without delay into the blazing furnace, and he mockingly asked what god could possibly rescue them from his hands. The three men respectfully yet firmly told the king that their God was more than able to save them from the fire if He so chose, but they wanted the king to know clearly that even if He chose not to rescue them, they would still never bow down and worship his golden statue under any circumstances. Enraged beyond measure by their calm, unwavering defiance, the king ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than its usual temperature, and commanded his strongest soldiers to tie the three men up securely and throw them straight into the roaring flames.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Giving the three men one final chance to reconsider and save their own lives, the enraged king summoned them before him and warned sternly that if they persisted in refusing to bow down, they would be thrown immediately into the blazing furnace, arrogantly demanding to know what god could possibly be powerful enough to rescue them from his own royal hands. The three men, standing calmly and respectfully yet remaining completely unwavering in their conviction, told the king plainly that their God was more than able to rescue them from the flames if He so chose, but they wanted him to understand clearly that even if their God chose not to save them on this occasion, they would still never bow down or worship his golden statue under any circumstances whatsoever. Enraged beyond all measure by their calm, unwavering defiance in front of his entire royal court, the king furiously ordered that the furnace be heated to seven times its normal temperature, and commanded some of his own strongest soldiers to bind the three men tightly in their cloaks, trousers, and turbans before throwing them directly into the roaring flames below.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego</li><li>the fourth figure</li><li>the furnace</li><li>King Nebuchadnezzar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The three men walk around unharmed inside the fire</li><li>The king sees a fourth figure walking with them</li><li>The king calls the men out of the furnace unharmed</li><li>The king praises God for rescuing the three men</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>so hot</li><li>astonished</li><li>not even singed</li><li>called the men out</li><li>praised God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the king saw a ___ walking with the three men in the fire, and when they came out unharmed, he ___ the God who had ___ them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The furnace was so hot that the soldiers who threw the men in were killed by the flames, yet Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego walked around unharmed inside the fire. Astonished, King Nebuchadnezzar looked closer and saw a fourth figure walking with them, one that looked like a divine being. The king called the men out of the furnace, and everyone saw that not even their hair was singed or their clothes burned. The king praised God for sending His angel to rescue the three men who trusted Him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The furnace had been heated so extremely hot that the powerful soldiers who threw the three men inside were themselves instantly killed by the intense heat and flames, and yet, remarkably, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego were seen walking calmly around unharmed inside the blazing fire. Astonished and deeply shaken, King Nebuchadnezzar leaned forward to look more closely into the furnace and was amazed to see a fourth figure walking freely alongside the three men, one whose appearance looked strikingly like a divine, heavenly being. Overwhelmed, the king immediately called the three men to come out of the furnace, and when everyone gathered around to examine them closely, they discovered that not a single hair on their heads had been singed, their clothing was completely unburned, and there was not even the faint smell of smoke on them anywhere. Deeply humbled, King Nebuchadnezzar publicly praised the God of Israel for sending His angel to miraculously rescue the three faithful men who had trusted Him rather than bow down.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So intensely hot had the furnace been made that the soldiers responsible for throwing the three bound men inside were themselves instantly killed by the overwhelming heat the moment they approached its opening, and yet, remarkably, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego could clearly be seen walking calmly around completely unharmed inside the blazing fire itself. Astonished and visibly shaken, King Nebuchadnezzar leapt to his feet and leaned forward to peer more closely into the furnace, and was amazed to discover a fourth figure walking freely alongside the three men, one whose radiant appearance looked strikingly like a divine, heavenly being sent to protect them. Overwhelmed with awe, the king immediately called for the three men to come out at once, and when the officials gathered around to examine them, they discovered that not a single hair on their heads had been singed, their clothing remained completely unburned, and there was not even the faint smell of smoke clinging to them. Deeply humbled, King Nebuchadnezzar publicly praised the God of Israel before his entire royal court for sending His angel to rescue the three faithful men who had trusted Him rather than bow down to any other god.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Shadrach, Meshach &amp; Abednego</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 17 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 17 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 18 (Daniel in the Lions' Den) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, King Nebuchadnezzar built a huge golden ___ and ordered everyone to bow, but ___ refused because they worshipped only ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the three men told the king that ___ could save them, but even if not, they would never ___, so the furious king had the furnace heated seven times ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the king saw a ___ walking with the three men in the fire, and when they came out unharmed, he ___ the God who had ___ them.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 18’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Daniel and King Darius as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Daniel</li><li>King Darius</li><li>the lions</li><li>the den</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jealous officials trick King Darius into signing a law about prayer</li><li>Daniel continues praying to God three times a day, just as he always had</li><li>Daniel is thrown into the lions' den as punishment for breaking the law</li><li>God sends an angel to shut the lions' mouths, and Daniel is found safe</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jealous officials tricked King Darius into signing a law about prayer to trap Daniel</li><li>Daniel stopped praying to God once the new law was signed</li><li>Daniel was thrown into a den of lions as punishment for praying to God</li><li>King Darius was pleased when he learned Daniel had been thrown to the lions</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served King Darius faithfully and was so wise and trustworthy that the king planned to put him in charge of the whole kingdom. This made the other officials extremely jealous, so they tricked King Darius into signing a law saying that for thirty days, no one could pray to anyone except the king, or they would be thrown into a den of lions. King Darius did not realise the law was really a trap meant for Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after the law was signed, Daniel continued praying to God three times a day, just as he always had done, with his windows open toward Jerusalem. The jealous officials watched him and reported him to the king. King Darius was very upset, because he liked Daniel and searched for a way to save him, but the law could not be changed once it was made. Sadly, the king had no choice but to have Daniel thrown into the den of lions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Daniel was thrown into the den, the king told him that his God, whom he served so faithfully, would rescue him. That night, King Darius could not sleep at all, worrying about Daniel. Early the next morning, the king rushed to the den and called out anxiously to see if Daniel was still alive. Daniel answered that God had sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths, and he had not been harmed at all. The king was overjoyed and had Daniel lifted out unharmed, and he commanded everyone in his kingdom to respect the God of Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served faithfully under King Darius of Babylon, distinguishing himself so remarkably through his wisdom, honesty, and trustworthy character that the king planned to promote him above all the other officials to be placed in charge of the entire kingdom. This planned promotion filled the other jealous officials with intense resentment, so they secretly plotted together and tricked King Darius into signing a strict new law declaring that for thirty whole days, no one throughout the kingdom was permitted to pray to any god or person except the king himself, on pain of being thrown into a den of hungry lions. King Darius, flattered by what he believed was simple admiration from his officials, signed the law without realising it was actually a trap designed specifically to destroy Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after this dangerous new law had been officially signed and could not legally be changed, Daniel continued praying openly to God exactly three times every single day, just as he had always faithfully done, kneeling by his upstairs window which stood open toward the city of Jerusalem. The jealous officials, watching closely and waiting for exactly this opportunity, caught Daniel in the act and immediately reported him directly to King Darius. The king was deeply troubled upon hearing this news, since he genuinely liked Daniel a great deal, and he spent the rest of that day searching desperately for any possible way to save him, but according to the unchangeable laws of the Medes and Persians, not even the king himself had the power to overturn a law once formally signed. Reluctantly and with great sorrow, the king ultimately had no choice left but to give the order for Daniel to be thrown into the den of hungry lions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just before Daniel was lowered down into the lions' den, King Darius spoke to him directly, expressing his sincere hope that Daniel's God, whom he served so faithfully, would somehow rescue him from what lay ahead. That entire night, King Darius could not sleep at all, refusing food and worrying constantly about Daniel's fate down in the darkness of the den. At the very first light of dawn the next morning, the anxious king rushed to the den and called out with great concern to discover whether Daniel had somehow survived the night. From within, Daniel calmly answered that God had sent His angel to shut the mouths of the lions completely, and that he had not been harmed at all. Overjoyed, the king immediately ordered Daniel lifted safely out of the den unharmed, and commanded that everyone throughout his kingdom must now respect and honour the living God whom Daniel served so faithfully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served with remarkable distinction under King Darius of Babylon, one of three senior administrators placed in charge of overseeing the kingdom's many regional governors, and he proved himself so extraordinarily wise, honest, and trustworthy in every task given to him that King Darius began seriously planning to promote Daniel above all the others to place him in charge of the entire kingdom. This looming promotion filled the other jealous administrators with intense resentment and envy, and after searching carefully through all of Daniel's conduct and finding absolutely no fault they could accuse him of, they cunningly plotted instead to trap him through his devotion to God. They convinced King Darius, through flattery, to sign a strict new law declaring that for a full thirty days, no one throughout the kingdom would be permitted to pray to any god or person except the king himself, under penalty of being thrown into a den of ravenous lions. Unaware of their true motive, King Darius signed the law into unchangeable royal decree, never suspecting it had actually been carefully designed as a trap aimed specifically at destroying Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after this dangerous and unchangeable new law had been formally signed and sealed with the king's own royal ring, Daniel continued praying openly to God exactly three times every single day, precisely as he had always faithfully done, kneeling beside his upstairs window that stood open toward the distant city of Jerusalem. The jealous officials, who had been watching his house closely in anticipation of exactly this moment, caught Daniel praying openly and immediately hurried to report his disobedience directly to King Darius himself. The king was deeply troubled upon hearing this unwelcome news, since he sincerely respected and valued Daniel above all his other officials, and he spent the remainder of that entire day desperately searching for any possible legal way to save him. Sadly, however, according to the strict and unchangeable laws of the Medes and Persians, not even the king himself possessed the authority to overturn a royal decree once it had been formally signed. Reluctantly, and with genuine sorrow, the king ultimately had no remaining choice but to give the difficult order for Daniel to be thrown down into the den of hungry lions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just before Daniel was lowered carefully into the darkness of the lions' den, King Darius spoke to him one final time, sincerely hoping that Daniel's God, whom he had faithfully served, would somehow find a way to rescue him. Throughout that long night, King Darius could not sleep at all, refusing every meal while worrying constantly about Daniel's fate down in the den. At the earliest light of dawn, the anxious king hurried to the den's entrance and called out to discover whether Daniel had survived the night among the lions. From within the darkness, Daniel calmly answered that God had mercifully sent His angel to shut the mouths of every lion in the den, and that he had not been harmed in the slightest. Overjoyed, the king immediately commanded that Daniel be lifted safely out of the den, completely unharmed, and issued a powerful new decree throughout his kingdom that everyone must now respect, fear, and honour the living God of Daniel forever.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Daniel</li><li>King Darius</li><li>the officials</li><li>the law</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Daniel serves King Darius faithfully and wisely</li><li>The king plans to put Daniel in charge of the kingdom</li><li>Jealous officials trick the king into signing a law about prayer</li><li>The law is really a trap meant for Daniel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>extremely jealous</li><li>wise and trustworthy</li><li>thirty days</li><li>den of lions</li><li>did not realise</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, jealous officials tricked King ___ into signing a law that no one could ___ to anyone but the king, as a trap for ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served King Darius faithfully and was so wise and trustworthy that the king planned to put him in charge of the whole kingdom. This made the other officials extremely jealous, so they tricked King Darius into signing a law saying that for thirty days, no one could pray to anyone except the king, or they would be thrown into a den of lions. King Darius did not realise the law was really a trap meant for Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served faithfully under King Darius of Babylon, distinguishing himself so remarkably through his wisdom, honesty, and trustworthy character that the king planned to promote him above all the other officials to be placed in charge of the entire kingdom. This planned promotion filled the other jealous officials with intense resentment, so they secretly plotted together and tricked King Darius into signing a strict new law declaring that for thirty whole days, no one throughout the kingdom was permitted to pray to any god or person except the king himself, on pain of being thrown into a den of hungry lions. King Darius, flattered by what he believed was simple admiration from his officials, signed the law without realising it was actually a trap designed specifically to destroy Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Daniel served with remarkable distinction under King Darius of Babylon, one of three senior administrators placed in charge of overseeing the kingdom's many regional governors, and he proved himself so extraordinarily wise, honest, and trustworthy in every task given to him that King Darius began seriously planning to promote Daniel above all the others to place him in charge of the entire kingdom. This looming promotion filled the other jealous administrators with intense resentment and envy, and after searching carefully through all of Daniel's conduct and finding absolutely no fault they could accuse him of, they cunningly plotted instead to trap him through his devotion to God. They convinced King Darius, through flattery, to sign a strict new law declaring that for a full thirty days, no one throughout the kingdom would be permitted to pray to any god or person except the king himself, under penalty of being thrown into a den of ravenous lions. Unaware of their true motive, King Darius signed the law into unchangeable royal decree, never suspecting it had actually been carefully designed as a trap aimed specifically at destroying Daniel.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Daniel</li><li>the officials</li><li>King Darius</li><li>the law</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Daniel keeps praying to God three times a day</li><li>The officials watch him and report him to the king</li><li>King Darius is upset and searches for a way to save Daniel</li><li>The law cannot be changed, so Daniel is thrown to the lions</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>three times a day</li><li>windows open</li><li>very upset</li><li>could not be changed</li><li>sadly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Daniel kept ___ to God, so the officials reported him, and though King Darius was upset, the ___ could not be changed, so Daniel was thrown to the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after the law was signed, Daniel continued praying to God three times a day, just as he always had done, with his windows open toward Jerusalem. The jealous officials watched him and reported him to the king. King Darius was very upset, because he liked Daniel and searched for a way to save him, but the law could not be changed once it was made. Sadly, the king had no choice but to have Daniel thrown into the den of lions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after this dangerous new law had been officially signed and could not legally be changed, Daniel continued praying openly to God exactly three times every single day, just as he had always faithfully done, kneeling by his upstairs window which stood open toward the city of Jerusalem. The jealous officials, watching closely and waiting for exactly this opportunity, caught Daniel in the act and immediately reported him directly to King Darius. The king was deeply troubled upon hearing this news, since he genuinely liked Daniel a great deal, and he spent the rest of that day searching desperately for any possible way to save him, but according to the unchangeable laws of the Medes and Persians, not even the king himself had the power to overturn a law once formally signed. Reluctantly and with great sorrow, the king ultimately had no choice left but to give the order for Daniel to be thrown into the den of hungry lions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even after this dangerous and unchangeable new law had been formally signed and sealed with the king's own royal ring, Daniel continued praying openly to God exactly three times every single day, precisely as he had always faithfully done, kneeling beside his upstairs window that stood open toward the distant city of Jerusalem. The jealous officials, who had been watching his house closely in anticipation of exactly this moment, caught Daniel praying openly and immediately hurried to report his disobedience directly to King Darius himself. The king was deeply troubled upon hearing this unwelcome news, since he sincerely respected and valued Daniel above all his other officials, and he spent the remainder of that entire day desperately searching for any possible legal way to save him. Sadly, however, according to the strict and unchangeable laws of the Medes and Persians, not even the king himself possessed the authority to overturn a royal decree once it had been formally signed. Reluctantly, and with genuine sorrow, the king ultimately had no remaining choice but to give the difficult order for Daniel to be thrown down into the den of hungry lions.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Darius</li><li>Daniel</li><li>the angel</li><li>the lions</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The king tells Daniel his God will rescue him</li><li>The king cannot sleep, worrying about Daniel all night</li><li>The king finds Daniel alive and unharmed the next morning</li><li>Daniel explains that an angel shut the lions' mouths</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>could not sleep at all</li><li>rushed</li><li>anxiously</li><li>overjoyed</li><li>commanded everyone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the king rushed to the den and found ___ alive, who explained that ___ had shut the lions' ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Daniel was thrown into the den, the king told him that his God, whom he served so faithfully, would rescue him. That night, King Darius could not sleep at all, worrying about Daniel. Early the next morning, the king rushed to the den and called out anxiously to see if Daniel was still alive. Daniel answered that God had sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths, and he had not been harmed at all. The king was overjoyed and had Daniel lifted out unharmed, and he commanded everyone in his kingdom to respect the God of Daniel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just before Daniel was lowered down into the lions' den, King Darius spoke to him directly, expressing his sincere hope that Daniel's God, whom he served so faithfully, would somehow rescue him from what lay ahead. That entire night, King Darius could not sleep at all, refusing food and worrying constantly about Daniel's fate down in the darkness of the den. At the very first light of dawn the next morning, the anxious king rushed to the den and called out with great concern to discover whether Daniel had somehow survived the night. From within, Daniel calmly answered that God had sent His angel to shut the mouths of the lions completely, and that he had not been harmed at all. Overjoyed, the king immediately ordered Daniel lifted safely out of the den unharmed, and commanded that everyone throughout his kingdom must now respect and honour the living God whom Daniel served so faithfully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just before Daniel was lowered carefully into the darkness of the lions' den, King Darius spoke to him one final time, sincerely hoping that Daniel's God, whom he had faithfully served, would somehow find a way to rescue him. Throughout that long night, King Darius could not sleep at all, refusing every meal while worrying constantly about Daniel's fate down in the den. At the earliest light of dawn, the anxious king hurried to the den's entrance and called out to discover whether Daniel had survived the night among the lions. From within the darkness, Daniel calmly answered that God had mercifully sent His angel to shut the mouths of every lion in the den, and that he had not been harmed in the slightest. Overjoyed, the king immediately commanded that Daniel be lifted safely out of the den, completely unharmed, and issued a powerful new decree throughout his kingdom that everyone must now respect, fear, and honour the living God of Daniel forever.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Daniel in the Lions' Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 18 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 18 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 19 (Jonah & the Great Fish) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, jealous officials tricked King ___ into signing a law that no one could ___ to anyone but the king, as a trap for ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Daniel kept ___ to God, so the officials reported him, and though King Darius was upset, the ___ could not be changed, so Daniel was thrown to the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the king rushed to the den and found ___ alive, who explained that ___ had shut the lions' ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 19: Jonah & the Great Fish":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 19’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 19: Jonah & the Great Fish) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jonah and the great fish as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonah</li><li>the great fish</li><li>the ship</li><li>Nineveh</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God tells Jonah to preach to Nineveh, but Jonah runs away on a ship instead</li><li>A storm hits the ship, and the sailors throw Jonah overboard at his request</li><li>A great fish swallows Jonah, who prays to God from inside it for three days</li><li>The fish spits Jonah onto dry land, and he finally goes to Nineveh to obey God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God told Jonah to travel to Nineveh and warn the people there</li><li>Jonah obeyed immediately and sailed straight to Nineveh</li><li>Jonah was swallowed by a great fish and prayed to God from inside it</li><li>After the fish spat him out, Jonah still refused to go to Nineveh</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told a prophet named Jonah to go to the great city of Nineveh and warn its people to turn away from their wicked ways. But Jonah did not want to go, so instead he ran in the opposite direction and got on a ship sailing far away to Tarshish, trying to escape from God. While the ship was out at sea, God sent a powerful storm that threatened to break the ship apart, and the terrified sailors were afraid they would all drown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sailors soon realised that Jonah was running away from God, and Jonah told them that if they threw him into the sea, the storm would stop. Reluctantly, the sailors picked Jonah up and threw him overboard, and the sea instantly became calm. Instead of drowning, Jonah was swallowed by a great fish that God had sent, and he stayed alive inside the fish for three days and three nights.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Inside the fish, Jonah prayed to God, admitting he was sorry for running away and thanking God for saving his life. God then commanded the fish to spit Jonah out onto dry land. This time, when God told Jonah again to go to Nineveh, Jonah obeyed and travelled there to warn the people. The people of Nineveh listened to Jonah's warning, turned away from their wicked ways, and God spared the city from destruction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke clearly to a prophet named Jonah, instructing him to travel to the great and powerful city of Nineveh in order to warn its people about the terrible consequences of their wickedness unless they changed their ways. However, Jonah, who disliked the people of Nineveh and did not want them to be forgiven, decided he simply did not want to obey God's command, so instead he deliberately ran in the completely opposite direction, boarding a ship bound for the distant city of Tarshish in an attempt to escape from God's presence entirely. While the ship was sailing far out at sea, God sent a powerful and violent storm that threatened to tear the entire ship apart, terrifying the experienced sailors on board, who feared that they were all about to drown in the raging waves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the storm continued raging fiercely, the desperate sailors eventually discovered through casting lots that Jonah was the cause of their trouble, running away from his own God, and Jonah honestly admitted to them that if they simply threw him overboard into the churning sea, the terrible storm would immediately calm down. Although understandably reluctant to be responsible for another man's likely death, the sailors eventually had no other choice, so they picked Jonah up together and threw him into the raging sea, and remarkably, the moment he hit the water, the entire sea instantly grew calm and peaceful again. Rather than sinking and drowning beneath the waves as expected, Jonah was miraculously swallowed whole by an enormous great fish that God had specifically prepared and sent for this very purpose, and astonishingly, Jonah survived alive inside the fish's stomach for three full days and three full nights.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During those three long days trapped inside the great fish, Jonah prayed earnestly and sincerely to God from the depths of the sea, honestly admitting how deeply sorry he was for running away from his God-given task, and gratefully thanking God for miraculously saving his life rather than allowing him to drown. Hearing Jonah's heartfelt prayer, God then commanded the great fish to swim toward the shore and spit Jonah safely out onto dry land. This time, when God spoke to Jonah again and repeated His original command to travel to Nineveh, Jonah finally obeyed without further hesitation or argument, and he made the long journey there to deliver God's urgent warning. Remarkably, the people of Nineveh actually listened carefully to Jonah's message, genuinely turned away from their wicked and violent ways, and as a direct result, God graciously chose to spare their entire city from the destruction He had originally planned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke clearly and directly to a prophet named Jonah, instructing him to travel immediately to the great and powerful Assyrian city of Nineveh, in order to warn its many wicked inhabitants about the consequences awaiting them unless they genuinely repented and changed their violent, sinful ways. However, Jonah, who deeply disliked the people of Nineveh, Israel's fierce enemies, and secretly feared that God might actually choose to forgive them if they repented, decided he simply refused to obey God's clear command, and instead deliberately fled in the completely opposite direction, paying his fare and boarding a ship bound for the distant city of Tarshish in a desperate attempt to escape from God's presence entirely. While the ship was sailing far out across the open sea, God sent an unusually powerful and violent storm that threatened to tear the entire vessel apart, terrifying even the ship's experienced sailors, who feared they were all about to drown beneath the raging waves and began throwing valuable cargo overboard to lighten the ship.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the terrifying storm continued raging around them, the desperate sailors first cried out to their own various gods for help, before eventually casting lots to discover who among them had caused this disaster, and the lot fell directly upon Jonah, who honestly confessed that he was running away from his own God, the powerful Creator of the sea and the dry land. Jonah then told the sailors plainly that if they simply threw him overboard into the churning waves, the terrible storm would calm down immediately. Although understandably reluctant to be responsible for another man's apparent death, and trying desperately to row back to shore first, the exhausted sailors eventually had no remaining choice, so they solemnly picked Jonah up together and threw him into the raging sea, and remarkably, the very moment he hit the water, the entire sea instantly grew calm and peaceful again. Rather than sinking and drowning as everyone expected, Jonah was miraculously swallowed whole by an enormous great fish that God had specifically prepared and sent for this exact purpose, and astonishingly, Jonah somehow survived alive inside the great fish's stomach for three full days and three full nights.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During those three long, dark days trapped inside the great fish, Jonah prayed earnestly to God from what felt like the very depths of the sea itself, admitting just how sorry he now felt for running away from his God-given task, and gratefully thanking God for mercifully saving his life rather than allowing him to drown. Hearing Jonah's heartfelt prayer, God then commanded the great fish to swim toward the nearest shore and spit Jonah safely out onto dry land. This time, when God spoke to Jonah once again and repeated His command to travel to Nineveh, Jonah finally obeyed without further hesitation, and made the long journey there to deliver God's urgent warning. Remarkably, the people of Nineveh listened carefully to Jonah's message, and even their own king rose from his throne, removed his royal robes, and put on rough sackcloth as a sign of genuine repentance, commanding the entire city to turn away from their wicked ways at once. As a result of this widespread change of heart, God graciously chose to spare Nineveh entirely from the destruction He had originally planned.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonah</li><li>God</li><li>Nineveh</li><li>the ship</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>God tells Jonah to go warn the people of Nineveh</li><li>Jonah does not want to go, so he runs the other way</li><li>Jonah boards a ship sailing to Tarshish</li><li>God sends a powerful storm that threatens to break the ship apart</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wicked ways</li><li>opposite direction</li><li>trying to escape from God</li><li>terrified sailors</li><li>would all drown</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, God told ___ to warn the people of ___, but Jonah ran away on a ___ instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God told a prophet named Jonah to go to the great city of Nineveh and warn its people to turn away from their wicked ways. But Jonah did not want to go, so instead he ran in the opposite direction and got on a ship sailing far away to Tarshish, trying to escape from God. While the ship was out at sea, God sent a powerful storm that threatened to break the ship apart, and the terrified sailors were afraid they would all drown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke clearly to a prophet named Jonah, instructing him to travel to the great and powerful city of Nineveh in order to warn its people about the terrible consequences of their wickedness unless they changed their ways. However, Jonah, who disliked the people of Nineveh and did not want them to be forgiven, decided he simply did not want to obey God's command, so instead he deliberately ran in the completely opposite direction, boarding a ship bound for the distant city of Tarshish in an attempt to escape from God's presence entirely. While the ship was sailing far out at sea, God sent a powerful and violent storm that threatened to tear the entire ship apart, terrifying the experienced sailors on board, who feared that they were all about to drown in the raging waves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>God spoke clearly and directly to a prophet named Jonah, instructing him to travel immediately to the great and powerful Assyrian city of Nineveh, in order to warn its many wicked inhabitants about the consequences awaiting them unless they genuinely repented and changed their violent, sinful ways. However, Jonah, who deeply disliked the people of Nineveh, Israel's fierce enemies, and secretly feared that God might actually choose to forgive them if they repented, decided he simply refused to obey God's clear command, and instead deliberately fled in the completely opposite direction, paying his fare and boarding a ship bound for the distant city of Tarshish in a desperate attempt to escape from God's presence entirely. While the ship was sailing far out across the open sea, God sent an unusually powerful and violent storm that threatened to tear the entire vessel apart, terrifying even the ship's experienced sailors, who feared they were all about to drown beneath the raging waves and began throwing valuable cargo overboard to lighten the ship.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the sailors</li><li>Jonah</li><li>the storm</li><li>the great fish</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The sailors realise Jonah is running from God</li><li>Jonah tells them to throw him into the sea to stop the storm</li><li>The sailors throw Jonah overboard and the sea calms</li><li>A great fish swallows Jonah and he lives inside it for three days</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>reluctantly</li><li>instantly</li><li>instead of drowning</li><li>three days and three nights</li><li>sent</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the sailors threw ___ into the sea and the storm stopped, and a great ___ swallowed him for three ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sailors soon realised that Jonah was running away from God, and Jonah told them that if they threw him into the sea, the storm would stop. Reluctantly, the sailors picked Jonah up and threw him overboard, and the sea instantly became calm. Instead of drowning, Jonah was swallowed by a great fish that God had sent, and he stayed alive inside the fish for three days and three nights.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the storm continued raging fiercely, the desperate sailors eventually discovered through casting lots that Jonah was the cause of their trouble, running away from his own God, and Jonah honestly admitted to them that if they simply threw him overboard into the churning sea, the terrible storm would immediately calm down. Although understandably reluctant to be responsible for another man's likely death, the sailors eventually had no other choice, so they picked Jonah up together and threw him into the raging sea, and remarkably, the moment he hit the water, the entire sea instantly grew calm and peaceful again. Rather than sinking and drowning beneath the waves as expected, Jonah was miraculously swallowed whole by an enormous great fish that God had specifically prepared and sent for this very purpose, and astonishingly, Jonah survived alive inside the fish's stomach for three full days and three full nights.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the terrifying storm continued raging around them, the desperate sailors first cried out to their own various gods for help, before eventually casting lots to discover who among them had caused this disaster, and the lot fell directly upon Jonah, who honestly confessed that he was running away from his own God, the powerful Creator of the sea and the dry land. Jonah then told the sailors plainly that if they simply threw him overboard into the churning waves, the terrible storm would calm down immediately. Although understandably reluctant to be responsible for another man's apparent death, and trying desperately to row back to shore first, the exhausted sailors eventually had no remaining choice, so they solemnly picked Jonah up together and threw him into the raging sea, and remarkably, the very moment he hit the water, the entire sea instantly grew calm and peaceful again. Rather than sinking and drowning as everyone expected, Jonah was miraculously swallowed whole by an enormous great fish that God had specifically prepared and sent for this exact purpose, and astonishingly, Jonah somehow survived alive inside the great fish's stomach for three full days and three full nights.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonah</li><li>God</li><li>Nineveh</li><li>the fish</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jonah prays to God from inside the fish</li><li>The fish spits Jonah out onto dry land</li><li>Jonah obeys God and travels to Nineveh</li><li>The people of Nineveh repent and God spares the city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>admitting he was sorry</li><li>commanded</li><li>obeyed</li><li>listened to Jonah's warning</li><li>spared the city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the fish spat Jonah onto dry land, and this time he obeyed and went to ___, where the people turned from their wicked ways and God ___ the city.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Inside the fish, Jonah prayed to God, admitting he was sorry for running away and thanking God for saving his life. God then commanded the fish to spit Jonah out onto dry land. This time, when God told Jonah again to go to Nineveh, Jonah obeyed and travelled there to warn the people. The people of Nineveh listened to Jonah's warning, turned away from their wicked ways, and God spared the city from destruction.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During those three long days trapped inside the great fish, Jonah prayed earnestly and sincerely to God from the depths of the sea, honestly admitting how deeply sorry he was for running away from his God-given task, and gratefully thanking God for miraculously saving his life rather than allowing him to drown. Hearing Jonah's heartfelt prayer, God then commanded the great fish to swim toward the shore and spit Jonah safely out onto dry land. This time, when God spoke to Jonah again and repeated His original command to travel to Nineveh, Jonah finally obeyed without further hesitation or argument, and he made the long journey there to deliver God's urgent warning. Remarkably, the people of Nineveh actually listened carefully to Jonah's message, genuinely turned away from their wicked and violent ways, and as a direct result, God graciously chose to spare their entire city from the destruction He had originally planned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During those three long, dark days trapped inside the great fish, Jonah prayed earnestly to God from what felt like the very depths of the sea itself, admitting just how sorry he now felt for running away from his God-given task, and gratefully thanking God for mercifully saving his life rather than allowing him to drown. Hearing Jonah's heartfelt prayer, God then commanded the great fish to swim toward the nearest shore and spit Jonah safely out onto dry land. This time, when God spoke to Jonah once again and repeated His command to travel to Nineveh, Jonah finally obeyed without further hesitation, and made the long journey there to deliver God's urgent warning. Remarkably, the people of Nineveh listened carefully to Jonah's message, and even their own king rose from his throne, removed his royal robes, and put on rough sackcloth as a sign of genuine repentance, commanding the entire city to turn away from their wicked ways at once. As a result of this widespread change of heart, God graciously chose to spare Nineveh entirely from the destruction He had originally planned.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Jonah &amp; the Great Fish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 19 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 19 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 20 (Esther Becomes Queen) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, God told ___ to warn the people of ___, but Jonah ran away on a ___ instead.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the sailors threw ___ into the sea and the storm stopped, and a great ___ swallowed him for three ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the fish spat Jonah onto dry land, and this time he obeyed and went to ___, where the people turned from their wicked ways and God ___ the city.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 20’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Esther and King Xerxes as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Esther</li><li>King Xerxes</li><li>Mordecai</li><li>the crown</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Xerxes searches for a new queen after Queen Vashti is removed</li><li>Esther, a young Jewish woman raised by her cousin Mordecai, is brought to the palace</li><li>Esther keeps her Jewish identity secret, as Mordecai advised</li><li>The king chooses Esther and places the royal crown on her head, making her queen</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Xerxes searched the kingdom for a new queen after Queen Vashti was removed</li><li>Esther was raised by her parents, who lived comfortably in the royal palace</li><li>Mordecai advised Esther to keep her Jewish background a secret at first</li><li>King Xerxes chose Esther and placed the royal crown on her head</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over a huge kingdom, but after Queen Vashti was removed from her position, the king needed a new queen. His officials searched the kingdom and brought many young women to the palace so the king could choose one to be his new queen. Among them was a young Jewish woman named Esther, who had been raised by her older cousin Mordecai after her parents had died.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Mordecai advised Esther not to reveal that she was Jewish, so she kept it a secret from everyone in the palace. Esther was given special care and beauty treatments along with the other young women, and she was well liked by everyone who met her, including the man in charge of the palace women. Mordecai walked near the palace courtyard every day to find out how Esther was doing and to make sure she was safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it was finally Esther's turn to go before the king, King Xerxes was more pleased with her than with any of the other young women, and he loved her more than all of them. The king chose Esther to be his new queen and placed the royal crown on her head. He held a great feast in her honour and announced a holiday throughout the provinces. Esther had become queen, though she still kept her Jewish background a secret, just as Mordecai had wisely advised her to do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over an enormous empire stretching from India to Ethiopia, but after his previous queen, Vashti, was removed for refusing one of the king's commands, the king found himself needing a new queen. Following the advice of his royal officials, messengers were sent across every province, and many young women were gathered and brought to the royal palace in the capital city of Susa, so the king could choose whichever one pleased him most. Among these young women was a young Jewish woman named Esther, also known by her Hebrew name Hadassah, who had been raised since childhood by her older cousin Mordecai after both of her parents had died.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wisely, Mordecai advised his young cousin Esther not to reveal to anyone in the palace that she belonged to the Jewish people, so Esther carefully kept her true identity and background a closely guarded secret from absolutely everyone she encountered there. Once inside the palace, Esther was given twelve full months of special beauty treatments alongside all the other young women being considered, and throughout this entire lengthy process, she quickly became genuinely well liked and respected by everyone who met her, including Hegai, the palace official specifically placed in charge of overseeing all the king's young women. Meanwhile, faithful Mordecai made a habit of walking near the palace courtyard every single day without fail, anxiously hoping to learn how his beloved young cousin was faring and to reassure himself continually that she remained completely safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the long-awaited day finally arrived for Esther to be brought before King Xerxes himself, the king found himself more genuinely pleased and delighted with her than with any of the numerous other young women he had already met and considered, and the Bible records that he came to love Esther more deeply than all the others put together. Without further hesitation, the king made his decision and chose Esther above everyone else to become his new queen, ceremonially placing the magnificent royal crown directly upon her head in a formal coronation. To properly celebrate this joyful occasion, the king held an enormous banquet specifically in Esther's honour, inviting all of his nobles and officials, and even generously announced a special holiday to be celebrated throughout every province of his vast empire. And so it was that Esther had truly become queen of the entire Persian Empire, though remarkably, she still continued keeping her Jewish background a closely guarded secret from everyone around her, exactly as her wise cousin Mordecai had originally and carefully advised her to do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over an enormous empire stretching from India in the east to Ethiopia in the west, comprising 127 separate provinces, but after his previous queen, Vashti, had been formally removed from her royal position for publicly refusing to obey one of the king's commands during a lavish banquet, the king eventually found himself genuinely needing a new queen to take her place. Acting on the advice of his royal officials, messengers were dispatched across every province of the kingdom, and many young women were gathered together from far and wide and brought to the royal palace within the fortified citadel of Susa, so that the king could select whichever young woman pleased him most to become his new queen. Among these young women brought before the king was a lovely young Jewish woman named Esther, also known by her Hebrew name Hadassah, who had been lovingly raised since early childhood by her much older cousin Mordecai after both of her parents had sadly died, leaving her orphaned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Acting wisely, Mordecai firmly advised his beloved young cousin Esther not to reveal to anyone within the palace that she belonged to the Jewish people, so throughout her entire time there, Esther carefully kept her true identity and family background a closely guarded secret from everyone she encountered, exactly as instructed. Once safely inside the royal palace, Esther underwent a full twelve months of specially prescribed beauty treatments alongside all the other young women being considered for the position, and throughout this lengthy and demanding process, she quickly and genuinely became remarkably well liked, admired, and respected by virtually everyone who met her, including Hegai, the senior palace official placed in charge of overseeing the king's young women, who immediately favoured Esther above all the others and generously provided her with the best food, seven chosen attendants, and the most desirable living quarters available. All the while, faithful Mordecai made an unwavering daily habit of walking near the palace courtyard every single morning without fail, anxiously hoping to learn news of how his beloved young cousin was faring, and continually reassuring himself that she remained safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the long-awaited day finally arrived for Esther to be formally brought before King Xerxes himself, the king found himself far more genuinely pleased and captivated by her graceful character than by any of the numerous other young women he had already carefully considered over the preceding months, and the biblical account records that he came to love Esther more deeply than every other woman brought before him. Without any further hesitation, the king promptly made his final royal decision and chose Esther above absolutely everyone else to become his new queen, ceremonially placing the magnificent royal crown directly upon her head during an elaborate, official coronation. To properly celebrate this joyful royal occasion, the king organised an enormous banquet held specifically in Esther's honour, inviting all of his nobles and provincial governors from across the empire, and he even generously granted a special day of tax relief and distributed lavish royal gifts throughout every province of his vast kingdom in celebration. And so it was that Esther, a once-orphaned young Jewish woman, had truly and remarkably become queen over the entire Persian Empire, though astonishingly, she still continued faithfully keeping her Jewish heritage a closely guarded secret from everyone around her, exactly as her wise older cousin Mordecai had originally advised her to do.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>King Xerxes</li><li>Esther</li><li>Mordecai</li><li>Queen Vashti</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Queen Vashti is removed from her position as queen</li><li>King Xerxes searches the kingdom for a new queen</li><li>Many young women are brought to the palace</li><li>Esther, raised by her cousin Mordecai, is among them</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>huge kingdom</li><li>his officials searched</li><li>so the king could choose</li><li>raised by</li><li>had died</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, after Queen ___ was removed, King ___ searched for a new queen, and ___, raised by her cousin Mordecai, was brought to the palace.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over a huge kingdom, but after Queen Vashti was removed from her position, the king needed a new queen. His officials searched the kingdom and brought many young women to the palace so the king could choose one to be his new queen. Among them was a young Jewish woman named Esther, who had been raised by her older cousin Mordecai after her parents had died.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over an enormous empire stretching from India to Ethiopia, but after his previous queen, Vashti, was removed for refusing one of the king's commands, the king found himself needing a new queen. Following the advice of his royal officials, messengers were sent across every province, and many young women were gathered and brought to the royal palace in the capital city of Susa, so the king could choose whichever one pleased him most. Among these young women was a young Jewish woman named Esther, also known by her Hebrew name Hadassah, who had been raised since childhood by her older cousin Mordecai after both of her parents had died.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>King Xerxes of Persia ruled over an enormous empire stretching from India in the east to Ethiopia in the west, comprising 127 separate provinces, but after his previous queen, Vashti, had been formally removed from her royal position for publicly refusing to obey one of the king's commands during a lavish banquet, the king eventually found himself genuinely needing a new queen to take her place. Acting on the advice of his royal officials, messengers were dispatched across every province of the kingdom, and many young women were gathered together from far and wide and brought to the royal palace within the fortified citadel of Susa, so that the king could select whichever young woman pleased him most to become his new queen. Among these young women brought before the king was a lovely young Jewish woman named Esther, also known by her Hebrew name Hadassah, who had been lovingly raised since early childhood by her much older cousin Mordecai after both of her parents had sadly died, leaving her orphaned.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mordecai</li><li>Esther</li><li>the palace</li><li>Jewish</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mordecai advises Esther to keep her Jewish background secret</li><li>Esther keeps it a secret from everyone in the palace</li><li>Esther is well liked by everyone who meets her</li><li>Mordecai checks on her near the palace courtyard every day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>advised</li><li>special care and beauty treatments</li><li>well liked by everyone</li><li>in charge of the palace women</li><li>every day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Mordecai advised Esther to keep her ___ background a secret, and she was well liked while ___ checked on her near the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Mordecai advised Esther not to reveal that she was Jewish, so she kept it a secret from everyone in the palace. Esther was given special care and beauty treatments along with the other young women, and she was well liked by everyone who met her, including the man in charge of the palace women. Mordecai walked near the palace courtyard every day to find out how Esther was doing and to make sure she was safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wisely, Mordecai advised his young cousin Esther not to reveal to anyone in the palace that she belonged to the Jewish people, so Esther carefully kept her true identity and background a closely guarded secret from absolutely everyone she encountered there. Once inside the palace, Esther was given twelve full months of special beauty treatments alongside all the other young women being considered, and throughout this entire lengthy process, she quickly became genuinely well liked and respected by everyone who met her, including Hegai, the palace official specifically placed in charge of overseeing all the king's young women. Meanwhile, faithful Mordecai made a habit of walking near the palace courtyard every single day without fail, anxiously hoping to learn how his beloved young cousin was faring and to reassure himself continually that she remained completely safe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Acting wisely, Mordecai firmly advised his beloved young cousin Esther not to reveal to anyone within the palace that she belonged to the Jewish people, so throughout her entire time there, Esther carefully kept her true identity and family background a closely guarded secret from everyone she encountered, exactly as instructed. Once safely inside the royal palace, Esther underwent a full twelve months of specially prescribed beauty treatments alongside all the other young women being considered for the position, and throughout this lengthy and demanding process, she quickly and genuinely became remarkably well liked, admired, and respected by virtually everyone who met her, including Hegai, the senior palace official placed in charge of overseeing the king's young women, who immediately favoured Esther above all the others and generously provided her with the best food, seven chosen attendants, and the most desirable living quarters available. All the while, faithful Mordecai made an unwavering daily habit of walking near the palace courtyard every single morning without fail, anxiously hoping to learn news of how his beloved young cousin was faring, and continually reassuring himself that she remained safe.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Esther</li><li>King Xerxes</li><li>the crown</li><li>Mordecai</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Esther goes before the king</li><li>The king is more pleased with Esther than anyone else</li><li>The king places the crown on Esther's head, making her queen</li><li>Esther still keeps her Jewish background secret, as Mordecai advised</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>finally</li><li>more pleased</li><li>great feast</li><li>announced a holiday</li><li>wisely advised</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, King Xerxes was more pleased with ___ than anyone else, so he placed the royal ___ on her head, making her queen, though she kept her Jewish background ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When it was finally Esther's turn to go before the king, King Xerxes was more pleased with her than with any of the other young women, and he loved her more than all of them. The king chose Esther to be his new queen and placed the royal crown on her head. He held a great feast in her honour and announced a holiday throughout the provinces. Esther had become queen, though she still kept her Jewish background a secret, just as Mordecai had wisely advised her to do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the long-awaited day finally arrived for Esther to be brought before King Xerxes himself, the king found himself more genuinely pleased and delighted with her than with any of the numerous other young women he had already met and considered, and the Bible records that he came to love Esther more deeply than all the others put together. Without further hesitation, the king made his decision and chose Esther above everyone else to become his new queen, ceremonially placing the magnificent royal crown directly upon her head in a formal coronation. To properly celebrate this joyful occasion, the king held an enormous banquet specifically in Esther's honour, inviting all of his nobles and officials, and even generously announced a special holiday to be celebrated throughout every province of his vast empire. And so it was that Esther had truly become queen of the entire Persian Empire, though remarkably, she still continued keeping her Jewish background a closely guarded secret from everyone around her, exactly as her wise cousin Mordecai had originally and carefully advised her to do.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the long-awaited day finally arrived for Esther to be formally brought before King Xerxes himself, the king found himself far more genuinely pleased and captivated by her graceful character than by any of the numerous other young women he had already carefully considered over the preceding months, and the biblical account records that he came to love Esther more deeply than every other woman brought before him. Without any further hesitation, the king promptly made his final royal decision and chose Esther above absolutely everyone else to become his new queen, ceremonially placing the magnificent royal crown directly upon her head during an elaborate, official coronation. To properly celebrate this joyful royal occasion, the king organised an enormous banquet held specifically in Esther's honour, inviting all of his nobles and provincial governors from across the empire, and he even generously granted a special day of tax relief and distributed lavish royal gifts throughout every province of his vast kingdom in celebration. And so it was that Esther, a once-orphaned young Jewish woman, had truly and remarkably become queen over the entire Persian Empire, though astonishingly, she still continued faithfully keeping her Jewish heritage a closely guarded secret from everyone around her, exactly as her wise older cousin Mordecai had originally advised her to do.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: Esther Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 20 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 20 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 21 (The Birth of Jesus) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, after Queen ___ was removed, King ___ searched for a new queen, and ___, raised by her cousin Mordecai, was brought to the palace.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Mordecai advised Esther to keep her ___ background a secret, and she was well liked while ___ checked on her near the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, King Xerxes was more pleased with ___ than anyone else, so he placed the royal ___ on her head, making her queen, though she kept her Jewish background ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (Blue belt) — Stories 21–25":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 21: The Birth of Jesus":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: The Birth of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 21’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 21: The Birth of Jesus) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Mary and Joseph and baby Jesus as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mary and Joseph</li><li>baby Jesus</li><li>the stable</li><li>the manger</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Roman ruler orders everyone to travel to their home town to be counted</li><li>Joseph and Mary travel to Bethlehem, but every inn is full</li><li>Mary gives birth to Jesus in a stable because there is no room</li><li>She wraps him in cloths and lays him in a manger, and a new star shines</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph and Mary had to travel to Bethlehem to be counted in a census</li><li>Joseph easily found a comfortable room for them at a busy inn</li><li>Mary gave birth to Jesus in a stable and laid him in a manger</li><li>A bright new star appeared in the sky the night Jesus was born</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the Roman ruler sent out an order that everyone had to travel to their home town to be counted. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting a baby very soon, had to make the long journey to Bethlehem, because Joseph belonged to the family of King David. Mary rode most of the way on a donkey while Joseph walked alongside her. It was a long, tiring journey along dusty roads, and by the time they finally arrived, Bethlehem was already crowded with many other travellers who had come for the very same reason.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried from place to place searching for somewhere to stay, but every inn in the busy little town was already full. The only space he could find at last was a plain stable where animals were kept warm. That night, in that humble stable, Mary gave birth to her baby son, Jesus. She wrapped him snugly in cloths to keep him warm and gently laid him down in a manger, the wooden box normally used to feed the animals, because there was no proper bed ready for him anywhere.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though Jesus was born in such a plain and humble place, he was no ordinary baby. He was God's own Son, sent down from heaven to earth to one day save people everywhere from their sins. Mary and Joseph named him Jesus, just as an angel had told them to. Far away, a bright new star appeared in the sky above Bethlehem, shining more brightly than any star before it, as a sign that something wonderful and important had just happened that night in the little stable.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the Roman emperor sent out an order that everyone across the land had to travel to their own family's home town to be counted for a census. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting a baby very soon, had no choice but to make the long and difficult journey to the town of Bethlehem, because Joseph came from the family line of King David, and that was where his family belonged. Mary rode for much of the journey on a donkey, while Joseph walked patiently beside her, guiding the animal carefully along the rough and dusty roads. It was a tiring trip that took several days, and by the time the weary couple finally arrived in Bethlehem, the little town was already bursting with other travellers who had come for the very same reason.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried from place to place searching for somewhere for them to stay the night, but to his dismay every inn in Bethlehem was completely full, with no room left for anyone else. The only place he could find at last was a humble stable, a simple shelter where animals were kept safe and warm through the cold night. It was there, in that plain and lowly stable, surrounded by the gentle smell of hay and animals, that Mary's baby was finally born. She wrapped her newborn son snugly in strips of cloth to keep him warm, and gently laid him down to sleep in a manger, the wooden feeding box normally used for animals, because there was no proper cradle or bed ready for him anywhere in the crowded little town.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though baby Jesus had been born in such a plain, humble, and unexpected place, he was far from an ordinary child. He was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven to earth for a very special purpose, to one day save people everywhere from their sins. Mary and Joseph named the baby Jesus, exactly as an angel had instructed them to, because that name means 'God saves' his people. On that same remarkable night, far off in the sky above Bethlehem, a bright new star appeared, shining more brilliantly than any star before it, as a wonderful sign to the whole world that something truly amazing had just taken place in that quiet little stable.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during the reign of the Roman Emperor Augustus, an order went out across the whole land commanding every single person to travel back to their own family's home town so they could be properly counted in a great census. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting her first baby very soon, had no choice but to set out on the long and difficult journey to the town of Bethlehem, because Joseph belonged to the royal family line of King David, and that ancient town was where his family had always come from. The journey was tiring and slow along the dusty, winding roads, and by the time the weary couple finally reached Bethlehem, the small town was already completely packed with countless other travellers who had come from far and wide for that very same reason.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried anxiously from door to door across the crowded little town, searching desperately for somewhere safe for them to stay the night, but to his growing dismay, every single inn in Bethlehem was already full to bursting, with absolutely no room left anywhere for a young couple in need. The only shelter he could find at last, tucked away from the noise and crowds, was a humble stable, a simple, plain place where animals were kept warm and safe through the night. It was there, in that lowly stable surrounded only by the gentle sounds and smells of the animals, that Mary's long-awaited baby was finally born. Tenderly, she wrapped her tiny newborn son snugly in strips of soft cloth to keep him warm through the cold night, and gently laid him down to rest in a manger, the simple wooden feeding box normally used to hold hay for the animals, because there was no proper cradle or bed prepared anywhere for him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though this remarkable baby had come into the world in such a plain, humble, and completely unexpected place, he was truly no ordinary child at all. This newborn baby was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven to earth for the most important purpose imaginable, to grow up and one day save people everywhere from their sins and bring them back to God. Mary and Joseph named him Jesus, exactly as an angel had instructed them, because that name means 'God saves'. Nobody in busy, crowded Bethlehem that night realised that the most important baby who would ever be born had just arrived quietly among the animals, unnoticed by almost everyone in the town. On that same extraordinary night, far off in the dark sky stretching high above Bethlehem, a bright and beautiful new star suddenly appeared, shining more brilliantly than any star that had ever been seen before, as a wonderful sign to the whole watching world that something truly amazing and history-changing had just quietly taken place inside that humble little stable.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: The Birth of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph and Mary</li><li>the Roman ruler</li><li>Bethlehem</li><li>King David</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Roman ruler orders everyone to be counted</li><li>Joseph and Mary must travel to Bethlehem</li><li>Joseph belongs to the family of King David</li><li>Bethlehem is crowded with other travellers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>expecting a baby</li><li>long, tiring journey</li><li>family of King David</li><li>crowded</li><li>other travellers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the Roman ruler ordered everyone to travel to their ___ to be counted, so ___ travelled to Bethlehem.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the Roman ruler sent out an order that everyone had to travel to their home town to be counted. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting a baby very soon, had to make the long journey to Bethlehem, because Joseph belonged to the family of King David. Mary rode most of the way on a donkey while Joseph walked alongside her. It was a long, tiring journey along dusty roads, and by the time they finally arrived, Bethlehem was already crowded with many other travellers who had come for the very same reason.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, the Roman emperor sent out an order that everyone across the land had to travel to their own family's home town to be counted for a census. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting a baby very soon, had no choice but to make the long and difficult journey to the town of Bethlehem, because Joseph came from the family line of King David, and that was where his family belonged. Mary rode for much of the journey on a donkey, while Joseph walked patiently beside her, guiding the animal carefully along the rough and dusty roads. It was a tiring trip that took several days, and by the time the weary couple finally arrived in Bethlehem, the little town was already bursting with other travellers who had come for the very same reason.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, during the reign of the Roman Emperor Augustus, an order went out across the whole land commanding every single person to travel back to their own family's home town so they could be properly counted in a great census. Joseph and Mary, who was expecting her first baby very soon, had no choice but to set out on the long and difficult journey to the town of Bethlehem, because Joseph belonged to the royal family line of King David, and that ancient town was where his family had always come from. The journey was tiring and slow along the dusty, winding roads, and by the time the weary couple finally reached Bethlehem, the small town was already completely packed with countless other travellers who had come from far and wide for that very same reason.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: The Birth of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph</li><li>the inn</li><li>the stable</li><li>Mary</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Joseph searches for a place to stay</li><li>Every inn in Bethlehem is already full</li><li>The only space he can find is a stable</li><li>Mary gives birth to Jesus that night</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>searched</li><li>already full</li><li>humble stable</li><li>gave birth</li><li>wrapped him snugly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, every ___ in Bethlehem was full, so Joseph found a ___ where Mary gave birth to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried from place to place searching for somewhere to stay, but every inn in the busy little town was already full. The only space he could find at last was a plain stable where animals were kept warm. That night, in that humble stable, Mary gave birth to her baby son, Jesus. She wrapped him snugly in cloths to keep him warm and gently laid him down in a manger, the wooden box normally used to feed the animals, because there was no proper bed ready for him anywhere.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried from place to place searching for somewhere for them to stay the night, but to his dismay every inn in Bethlehem was completely full, with no room left for anyone else. The only place he could find at last was a humble stable, a simple shelter where animals were kept safe and warm through the cold night. It was there, in that plain and lowly stable, surrounded by the gentle smell of hay and animals, that Mary's baby was finally born. She wrapped her newborn son snugly in strips of cloth to keep him warm, and gently laid him down to sleep in a manger, the wooden feeding box normally used for animals, because there was no proper cradle or bed ready for him anywhere in the crowded little town.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Joseph hurried anxiously from door to door across the crowded little town, searching desperately for somewhere safe for them to stay the night, but to his growing dismay, every single inn in Bethlehem was already full to bursting, with absolutely no room left anywhere for a young couple in need. The only shelter he could find at last, tucked away from the noise and crowds, was a humble stable, a simple, plain place where animals were kept warm and safe through the night. It was there, in that lowly stable surrounded only by the gentle sounds and smells of the animals, that Mary's long-awaited baby was finally born. Tenderly, she wrapped her tiny newborn son snugly in strips of soft cloth to keep him warm through the cold night, and gently laid him down to rest in a manger, the simple wooden feeding box normally used to hold hay for the animals, because there was no proper cradle or bed prepared anywhere for him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: The Birth of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mary</li><li>the manger</li><li>baby Jesus</li><li>the star</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mary wraps baby Jesus in cloths</li><li>She lays him gently in a manger</li><li>Jesus is God's own Son, sent to save people</li><li>A bright new star appears in the sky above Bethlehem</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wrapped him snugly</li><li>gently laid him down</li><li>God's own Son</li><li>bright new star</li><li>shining</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Mary wrapped baby Jesus and laid him in a ___, and a bright new ___ appeared as a sign that ___ had happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though Jesus was born in such a plain and humble place, he was no ordinary baby. He was God's own Son, sent down from heaven to earth to one day save people everywhere from their sins. Mary and Joseph named him Jesus, just as an angel had told them to. Far away, a bright new star appeared in the sky above Bethlehem, shining more brightly than any star before it, as a sign that something wonderful and important had just happened that night in the little stable.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though baby Jesus had been born in such a plain, humble, and unexpected place, he was far from an ordinary child. He was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven to earth for a very special purpose, to one day save people everywhere from their sins. Mary and Joseph named the baby Jesus, exactly as an angel had instructed them to, because that name means 'God saves' his people. On that same remarkable night, far off in the sky above Bethlehem, a bright new star appeared, shining more brilliantly than any star before it, as a wonderful sign to the whole world that something truly amazing had just taken place in that quiet little stable.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Even though this remarkable baby had come into the world in such a plain, humble, and completely unexpected place, he was truly no ordinary child at all. This newborn baby was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven to earth for the most important purpose imaginable, to grow up and one day save people everywhere from their sins and bring them back to God. Mary and Joseph named him Jesus, exactly as an angel had instructed them, because that name means 'God saves'. Nobody in busy, crowded Bethlehem that night realised that the most important baby who would ever be born had just arrived quietly among the animals, unnoticed by almost everyone in the town. On that same extraordinary night, far off in the dark sky stretching high above Bethlehem, a bright and beautiful new star suddenly appeared, shining more brilliantly than any star that had ever been seen before, as a wonderful sign to the whole watching world that something truly amazing and history-changing had just quietly taken place inside that humble little stable.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: The Birth of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 21 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 21 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 22 (The Shepherds & the Angels) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the Roman ruler ordered everyone to travel to their ___ to be counted, so ___ travelled to Bethlehem.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, every ___ in Bethlehem was full, so Joseph found a ___ where Mary gave birth to ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Mary wrapped baby Jesus and laid him in a ___, and a bright new ___ appeared as a sign that ___ had happened.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 22: The Shepherds & the Angels":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 22’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 22: The Shepherds & the Angels) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the shepherds and the angel as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherds</li><li>the angel</li><li>baby Jesus</li><li>the fields</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shepherds are watching their sheep in the fields at night near Bethlehem</li><li>An angel appears and tells them a Saviour has been born</li><li>A huge multitude of angels appears, praising God and singing</li><li>The shepherds hurry to Bethlehem, find the baby, and spread the good news</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shepherds were watching their sheep in the fields when the angel appeared</li><li>The shepherds were not surprised at all when the angel appeared</li><li>A multitude of angels appeared, praising God and singing in the sky</li><li>The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem and found the baby just as the angel said</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very night Jesus was born, some shepherds were out in the fields near Bethlehem, watching over their flock of sheep in the darkness, just as they always did. Suddenly, a bright angel appeared in the sky above them, and the shepherds were terrified by the glowing light shining all around. The angel told them not to be afraid, because he had come to bring wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant for everyone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel explained that a Saviour had been born that very night in Bethlehem, a baby who was Christ the Lord, sent to rescue the whole world. He told the shepherds exactly how to find the baby: he would be wrapped snugly in cloths and lying in a manger. Suddenly, the sky filled with a huge multitude of angels, all praising God together and singing, \"Glory to God in the highest, and peace on earth.\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the angels finished singing and went back up to heaven, the shepherds excitedly agreed to hurry straight to Bethlehem to see this wonderful thing for themselves, exactly as the Lord had made known to them. Leaving their sheep behind for a while, they found Mary, Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger, just as the angel had told them. Afterwards, the shepherds could not help spreading the news to everyone they met along the way, and everyone who heard it was amazed by what the shepherds told them, while the shepherds returned to their fields glorifying and praising God for everything they had seen and heard that night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very same night that Jesus was born in Bethlehem, some ordinary shepherds were out in the fields nearby, keeping watch over their flocks of sheep through the darkness, just as they did every single night of the year. Suddenly, without any warning at all, a bright and glorious angel appeared in the sky right above them, and the glow of the Lord's glory shone all around, terrifying the poor shepherds completely, so that some of them nearly fell to the ground in fear. But the angel spoke kindly and calmly to them, telling them not to be afraid, because he had come to bring wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant for every single person, not just for kings and important people in the land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel joyfully explained that a Saviour had been born that very night in the town of Bethlehem, a baby who was none other than Christ the Lord himself, sent from heaven to rescue the whole world. He told the amazed shepherds exactly how they would recognise this special baby: they would find him wrapped snugly in strips of cloth and lying peacefully in a manger, the humble feeding box meant for animals. All at once, the dark sky above them filled completely with an enormous multitude of heavenly angels, all singing and praising God together with one joyful voice, declaring, \"Glory to God in the highest heaven, and peace on earth to everyone he favours.\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as the dazzling angels had finished singing and returned back up to heaven, the excited shepherds turned to one another and immediately agreed that they must hurry straight to Bethlehem without delay to see for themselves this amazing thing that the Lord had told them about. Leaving their flocks behind, they hurried into town and eventually found Mary, Joseph, and the newborn baby lying exactly where the angel had said, safely in the manger. Afterwards, the overjoyed shepherds could not stop themselves from spreading the incredible news to absolutely everyone they met along the way, and everyone who heard their story was utterly amazed by what they were told. At last, the shepherds returned back to their fields and their flocks, glorifying and praising God the whole way for everything they had seen and heard that unforgettable night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very same night that Jesus was born in the little town of Bethlehem, some ordinary shepherds happened to be out in the surrounding fields nearby, keeping careful watch over their flocks of sheep through the darkness, exactly as they did on every other quiet night of the year. Suddenly, without any warning at all, a bright and glorious angel of the Lord appeared in the night sky directly above them, and the dazzling glory of the Lord shone brilliantly all around them, terrifying the poor shepherds so much that they nearly fell to the ground in fear. But the angel spoke gently and reassuringly to them, telling them plainly not to be afraid, because he had come especially to bring them wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant not only for kings and priests but for every single ordinary person, everywhere in the whole world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel joyfully announced that a long-awaited Saviour had been born that very night in the town of David, a tiny baby who was none other than Christ the Lord himself, sent down from heaven to rescue and save the whole world from sin. He carefully told the astonished shepherds exactly how they would be able to recognise this remarkable baby among all the other babies in Bethlehem: they would find him wrapped snugly in simple strips of cloth and lying peacefully in a lowly manger, the humble wooden feeding box normally used only for animals. All at once, before the shepherds could even respond, the entire dark sky above them filled completely with a vast, shining multitude of heavenly angels, a great heavenly army, all singing and praising God together in perfect harmony, joyfully declaring, \"Glory to God in the highest heaven, and peace on earth to everyone he favours.\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as the dazzling angels had finished their beautiful song and returned quietly back up into heaven, the astonished shepherds turned excitedly to one another and immediately agreed without hesitation that they simply had to hurry straight to Bethlehem to see for themselves this amazing thing that the Lord had just told them about through his angel. Leaving their precious flocks behind in the fields, they hurried quickly into the crowded little town and eventually found Mary and Joseph together, with the newborn baby lying exactly where the angel had promised, safely resting in the humble manger. Afterwards, the shepherds could not contain their overwhelming joy, and they eagerly spread the incredible news of what they had seen and heard to absolutely everyone they happened to meet along their way home. Everyone who heard their remarkable story was utterly amazed by what the shepherds told them, while Mary quietly treasured up all these wonderful things in her heart and thought deeply about them. At last, filled with great joy, the shepherds made their way back to their fields and their waiting flocks, glorifying and praising God the entire way for everything they had personally seen and heard on that truly unforgettable night.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherds</li><li>the sheep</li><li>the fields</li><li>an angel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shepherds are watching their sheep at night</li><li>They are in fields near Bethlehem</li><li>An angel suddenly appears in the sky</li><li>The shepherds are terrified by the bright light</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>watching over</li><li>as they always did</li><li>suddenly</li><li>terrified</li><li>glowing light</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, some ___ were watching their sheep at night when an ___ suddenly appeared, and they were ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very night Jesus was born, some shepherds were out in the fields near Bethlehem, watching over their flock of sheep in the darkness, just as they always did. Suddenly, a bright angel appeared in the sky above them, and the shepherds were terrified by the glowing light shining all around. The angel told them not to be afraid, because he had come to bring wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant for everyone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very same night that Jesus was born in Bethlehem, some ordinary shepherds were out in the fields nearby, keeping watch over their flocks of sheep through the darkness, just as they did every single night of the year. Suddenly, without any warning at all, a bright and glorious angel appeared in the sky right above them, and the glow of the Lord's glory shone all around, terrifying the poor shepherds completely, so that some of them nearly fell to the ground in fear. But the angel spoke kindly and calmly to them, telling them not to be afraid, because he had come to bring wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant for every single person, not just for kings and important people in the land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the very same night that Jesus was born in the little town of Bethlehem, some ordinary shepherds happened to be out in the surrounding fields nearby, keeping careful watch over their flocks of sheep through the darkness, exactly as they did on every other quiet night of the year. Suddenly, without any warning at all, a bright and glorious angel of the Lord appeared in the night sky directly above them, and the dazzling glory of the Lord shone brilliantly all around them, terrifying the poor shepherds so much that they nearly fell to the ground in fear. But the angel spoke gently and reassuringly to them, telling them plainly not to be afraid, because he had come especially to bring them wonderful news of great joy, news that was meant not only for kings and priests but for every single ordinary person, everywhere in the whole world.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the angel</li><li>the Saviour</li><li>the manger</li><li>the multitude</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The angel tells them not to be afraid</li><li>He says a Saviour has been born in Bethlehem</li><li>He tells them the baby is lying in a manger</li><li>A huge multitude of angels appears, praising God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wonderful news</li><li>great joy</li><li>wrapped in cloths</li><li>huge multitude</li><li>praising God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the angel said a ___ had been born and was lying in a ___, and a huge ___ of angels appeared.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel explained that a Saviour had been born that very night in Bethlehem, a baby who was Christ the Lord, sent to rescue the whole world. He told the shepherds exactly how to find the baby: he would be wrapped snugly in cloths and lying in a manger. Suddenly, the sky filled with a huge multitude of angels, all praising God together and singing, \"Glory to God in the highest, and peace on earth.\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel joyfully explained that a Saviour had been born that very night in the town of Bethlehem, a baby who was none other than Christ the Lord himself, sent from heaven to rescue the whole world. He told the amazed shepherds exactly how they would recognise this special baby: they would find him wrapped snugly in strips of cloth and lying peacefully in a manger, the humble feeding box meant for animals. All at once, the dark sky above them filled completely with an enormous multitude of heavenly angels, all singing and praising God together with one joyful voice, declaring, \"Glory to God in the highest heaven, and peace on earth to everyone he favours.\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The angel joyfully announced that a long-awaited Saviour had been born that very night in the town of David, a tiny baby who was none other than Christ the Lord himself, sent down from heaven to rescue and save the whole world from sin. He carefully told the astonished shepherds exactly how they would be able to recognise this remarkable baby among all the other babies in Bethlehem: they would find him wrapped snugly in simple strips of cloth and lying peacefully in a lowly manger, the humble wooden feeding box normally used only for animals. All at once, before the shepherds could even respond, the entire dark sky above them filled completely with a vast, shining multitude of heavenly angels, a great heavenly army, all singing and praising God together in perfect harmony, joyfully declaring, \"Glory to God in the highest heaven, and peace on earth to everyone he favours.\"</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherds</li><li>Bethlehem</li><li>the baby</li><li>God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The shepherds decide to hurry to Bethlehem</li><li>They find Mary, Joseph, and the baby in the manger</li><li>They tell everyone they meet about what happened</li><li>They return to their fields glorifying and praising God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>hurry to Bethlehem</li><li>just as the angel had said</li><li>spread the news</li><li>amazed</li><li>glorifying and praising</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the shepherds hurried to ___ and found the baby, then told everyone the news while ___ and praising ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the angels finished singing and went back up to heaven, the shepherds excitedly agreed to hurry straight to Bethlehem to see this wonderful thing for themselves, exactly as the Lord had made known to them. Leaving their sheep behind for a while, they found Mary, Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger, just as the angel had told them. Afterwards, the shepherds could not help spreading the news to everyone they met along the way, and everyone who heard it was amazed by what the shepherds told them, while the shepherds returned to their fields glorifying and praising God for everything they had seen and heard that night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as the dazzling angels had finished singing and returned back up to heaven, the excited shepherds turned to one another and immediately agreed that they must hurry straight to Bethlehem without delay to see for themselves this amazing thing that the Lord had told them about. Leaving their flocks behind, they hurried into town and eventually found Mary, Joseph, and the newborn baby lying exactly where the angel had said, safely in the manger. Afterwards, the overjoyed shepherds could not stop themselves from spreading the incredible news to absolutely everyone they met along the way, and everyone who heard their story was utterly amazed by what they were told. At last, the shepherds returned back to their fields and their flocks, glorifying and praising God the whole way for everything they had seen and heard that unforgettable night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As soon as the dazzling angels had finished their beautiful song and returned quietly back up into heaven, the astonished shepherds turned excitedly to one another and immediately agreed without hesitation that they simply had to hurry straight to Bethlehem to see for themselves this amazing thing that the Lord had just told them about through his angel. Leaving their precious flocks behind in the fields, they hurried quickly into the crowded little town and eventually found Mary and Joseph together, with the newborn baby lying exactly where the angel had promised, safely resting in the humble manger. Afterwards, the shepherds could not contain their overwhelming joy, and they eagerly spread the incredible news of what they had seen and heard to absolutely everyone they happened to meet along their way home. Everyone who heard their remarkable story was utterly amazed by what the shepherds told them, while Mary quietly treasured up all these wonderful things in her heart and thought deeply about them. At last, filled with great joy, the shepherds made their way back to their fields and their waiting flocks, glorifying and praising God the entire way for everything they had personally seen and heard on that truly unforgettable night.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: The Shepherds &amp; the Angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 22 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 22 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 23 (John the Baptist) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, some ___ were watching their sheep at night when an ___ suddenly appeared, and they were ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the angel said a ___ had been born and was lying in a ___, and a huge ___ of angels appeared.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the shepherds hurried to ___ and found the baby, then told everyone the news while ___ and praising ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 23: John the Baptist":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: John the Baptist</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 23’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 23: John the Baptist) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name John the Baptist and Jesus as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John the Baptist</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the Jordan River</li><li>the wilderness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>An angel tells Zechariah that his son John will prepare the way for the Lord</li><li>John grows up living in the wilderness, wearing rough clothes and eating simply</li><li>John preaches by the Jordan River, telling people to turn from their sins</li><li>John baptises those who repent and says someone far greater is coming</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John the Baptist lived in the wilderness and wore clothes made of camel's hair</li><li>John told people that he himself was the promised Saviour</li><li>John baptised people in the Jordan River as a sign they had turned from sin</li><li>John said he was not worthy to untie the sandals of the one coming after him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his work, God sent a man named John to get people ready for him. John lived out in the wilderness, wearing rough clothes made of camel's hair with a leather belt, and eating only locusts and wild honey that he found there. He was a cousin of Jesus, and even before he was born, an angel had told his father, Zechariah, a priest, that John would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew up, he travelled along the banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly to the large crowds of people who came to hear him speak. He told the people plainly that they needed to turn away from their sins and be baptised in the river as a sign that they were sorry and truly wanted to change their lives. Many people travelled from towns all over the region just to listen to John's preaching and to be baptised by him in the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>John always made it clear to everyone that he was not the Saviour himself, but only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way. He told the people that someone far greater than him was coming soon, someone so much greater that John said he was not even worthy to bend down and untie his sandals. John's whole purpose in life was to point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he did this faithfully and humbly for as long as he lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his important work, God sent a special man named John to get the people ready to receive him. John grew up to live out in the rugged wilderness, wearing rough clothes made from camel's hair with a leather belt around his waist, and eating nothing but locusts and wild honey that he found there for himself. John was actually a cousin of Jesus, born to elderly parents named Zechariah and Elizabeth after many years of hoping and praying for a child. Even before John was born, an angel had appeared unexpectedly to his father, Zechariah, a priest, and told him that his son John would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew older, he began travelling along the banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly and fearlessly to the large crowds of people who gathered to hear him speak, no matter who they were or where they came from. He told everyone plainly that they needed to turn away from their sins and be baptised in the river as an outward sign that they were truly sorry and wanted to change their lives for good. Many people, including ordinary farmers, soldiers, and tax collectors, travelled from towns and villages all across the region just to listen to John's powerful preaching and to be baptised by him in the flowing waters of the Jordan River.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his ministry, John always made it very clear to everyone that he himself was not the promised Saviour, but only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way for someone else far greater than himself. He told the crowds again and again that someone far greater than himself was coming soon after him, someone so much greater and more powerful that John said he did not even feel worthy enough to bend down and untie the straps of his sandals, a task usually left for the lowliest servant. John also warned honestly that while he only baptised people with water, the one coming after him would baptise with the Holy Spirit himself. John's entire life's purpose was to point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he carried out this important task faithfully and humbly, without ever seeking honour for himself, until the very end of his life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his important public work, God sent a very special man named John to prepare the people's hearts to receive him properly. John grew up to live out in the rugged, lonely wilderness, wearing rough clothing made from camel's hair with a simple leather belt fastened around his waist, and surviving on nothing but locusts and wild honey that he gathered there for himself. John was actually a cousin of Jesus, born to elderly parents named Zechariah and Elizabeth after many long years of waiting and hoping for a child of their own. Even before John was born, an angel had appeared unexpectedly to his father Zechariah, a priest serving in the Temple, and told him with certainty that his long-awaited son would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord, just as the ancient prophets had promised many hundreds of years before that a messenger would come first.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew into a man, he began travelling along the winding banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly and fearlessly to the large, curious crowds of people who gathered eagerly from every walk of life to hear his powerful message. He told everyone plainly and directly that they urgently needed to turn away from their sins and change their ways, and he baptised those who repented in the river as an outward sign that they were truly sorry and sincerely wanted a fresh start with God. Word of John's remarkable preaching spread quickly across the whole region, and many people travelled from towns and villages far and wide, including ordinary farmers, soldiers, tax collectors, and even religious leaders from Jerusalem, all coming just to listen to him and to be baptised by him in the flowing waters of the Jordan River.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his entire ministry, John always made it unmistakably clear to everyone around him that he himself was not the promised Saviour that the people had been waiting for, but rather only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way for someone far greater. He told the crowds again and again that someone far more powerful than himself was coming soon after him, someone so much greater that John openly said he did not even feel worthy enough to bend down and untie the straps of his sandals, a task usually left to the lowest servant in any household. John also warned the people honestly that while he baptised them only with water, the one coming after him would baptise with the Holy Spirit, offering something far greater than John could ever give. John's entire life's purpose was to faithfully point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he carried out this humble and important calling with great courage and without complaint, right until the very end of his life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: John the Baptist</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John</li><li>Zechariah</li><li>the angel</li><li>the wilderness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>An angel tells Zechariah about his son John</li><li>John is born and is a cousin of Jesus</li><li>John grows up living in the wilderness</li><li>He wears rough clothes and eats locusts and honey</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>prepare the way</li><li>cousin of Jesus</li><li>rough clothes</li><li>camel's hair</li><li>wild honey</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, an angel told ___ that his son John would prepare the way, and John grew up living in the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his work, God sent a man named John to get people ready for him. John lived out in the wilderness, wearing rough clothes made of camel's hair with a leather belt, and eating only locusts and wild honey that he found there. He was a cousin of Jesus, and even before he was born, an angel had told his father, Zechariah, a priest, that John would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his important work, God sent a special man named John to get the people ready to receive him. John grew up to live out in the rugged wilderness, wearing rough clothes made from camel's hair with a leather belt around his waist, and eating nothing but locusts and wild honey that he found there for himself. John was actually a cousin of Jesus, born to elderly parents named Zechariah and Elizabeth after many years of hoping and praying for a child. Even before John was born, an angel had appeared unexpectedly to his father, Zechariah, a priest, and told him that his son John would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus began his important public work, God sent a very special man named John to prepare the people's hearts to receive him properly. John grew up to live out in the rugged, lonely wilderness, wearing rough clothing made from camel's hair with a simple leather belt fastened around his waist, and surviving on nothing but locusts and wild honey that he gathered there for himself. John was actually a cousin of Jesus, born to elderly parents named Zechariah and Elizabeth after many long years of waiting and hoping for a child of their own. Even before John was born, an angel had appeared unexpectedly to his father Zechariah, a priest serving in the Temple, and told him with certainty that his long-awaited son would grow up to prepare the way for the Lord, just as the ancient prophets had promised many hundreds of years before that a messenger would come first.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: John the Baptist</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John</li><li>the crowds</li><li>the Jordan River</li><li>sin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John preaches to crowds by the Jordan River</li><li>He tells people to turn away from their sins</li><li>He baptises people as a sign they are sorry</li><li>Many people come from all over to hear him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>preaching boldly</li><li>turn away from their sins</li><li>baptised</li><li>sign</li><li>came from all over</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, John preached by the ___, telling people to turn from their ___ and be ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew up, he travelled along the banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly to the large crowds of people who came to hear him speak. He told the people plainly that they needed to turn away from their sins and be baptised in the river as a sign that they were sorry and truly wanted to change their lives. Many people travelled from towns all over the region just to listen to John's preaching and to be baptised by him in the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew older, he began travelling along the banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly and fearlessly to the large crowds of people who gathered to hear him speak, no matter who they were or where they came from. He told everyone plainly that they needed to turn away from their sins and be baptised in the river as an outward sign that they were truly sorry and wanted to change their lives for good. Many people, including ordinary farmers, soldiers, and tax collectors, travelled from towns and villages all across the region just to listen to John's powerful preaching and to be baptised by him in the flowing waters of the Jordan River.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As John grew into a man, he began travelling along the winding banks of the Jordan River, preaching boldly and fearlessly to the large, curious crowds of people who gathered eagerly from every walk of life to hear his powerful message. He told everyone plainly and directly that they urgently needed to turn away from their sins and change their ways, and he baptised those who repented in the river as an outward sign that they were truly sorry and sincerely wanted a fresh start with God. Word of John's remarkable preaching spread quickly across the whole region, and many people travelled from towns and villages far and wide, including ordinary farmers, soldiers, tax collectors, and even religious leaders from Jerusalem, all coming just to listen to him and to be baptised by him in the flowing waters of the Jordan River.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: John the Baptist</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the sandals</li><li>the messenger</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>John says he is not the Saviour himself</li><li>He says someone far greater is coming</li><li>He says he is not worthy to untie his sandals</li><li>John's job is to point people towards Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>messenger</li><li>far greater</li><li>not worthy</li><li>untie his sandals</li><li>point people towards</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, John said he was only a ___, and that someone far ___ than him, meaning ___, was coming soon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>John always made it clear to everyone that he was not the Saviour himself, but only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way. He told the people that someone far greater than him was coming soon, someone so much greater that John said he was not even worthy to bend down and untie his sandals. John's whole purpose in life was to point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he did this faithfully and humbly for as long as he lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his ministry, John always made it very clear to everyone that he himself was not the promised Saviour, but only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way for someone else far greater than himself. He told the crowds again and again that someone far greater than himself was coming soon after him, someone so much greater and more powerful that John said he did not even feel worthy enough to bend down and untie the straps of his sandals, a task usually left for the lowliest servant. John also warned honestly that while he only baptised people with water, the one coming after him would baptise with the Holy Spirit himself. John's entire life's purpose was to point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he carried out this important task faithfully and humbly, without ever seeking honour for himself, until the very end of his life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Throughout his entire ministry, John always made it unmistakably clear to everyone around him that he himself was not the promised Saviour that the people had been waiting for, but rather only a humble messenger sent ahead to prepare the way for someone far greater. He told the crowds again and again that someone far more powerful than himself was coming soon after him, someone so much greater that John openly said he did not even feel worthy enough to bend down and untie the straps of his sandals, a task usually left to the lowest servant in any household. John also warned the people honestly that while he baptised them only with water, the one coming after him would baptise with the Holy Spirit, offering something far greater than John could ever give. John's entire life's purpose was to faithfully point people towards Jesus rather than towards himself, and he carried out this humble and important calling with great courage and without complaint, right until the very end of his life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: John the Baptist</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 23 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 23 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 24 (The Baptism of Jesus) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, an angel told ___ that his son John would prepare the way, and John grew up living in the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, John preached by the ___, telling people to turn from their ___ and be ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, John said he was only a ___, and that someone far ___ than him, meaning ___, was coming soon.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 24’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and John the Baptist as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>John the Baptist</li><li>the Jordan River</li><li>the dove</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus travels to the Jordan River and asks John to baptise him</li><li>John is surprised, but Jesus says it is right, so John agrees</li><li>Jesus is baptised, and the Spirit of God comes down like a dove</li><li>A voice from heaven says Jesus is God's beloved Son</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus travelled to the Jordan River to be baptised by John</li><li>John was eager to baptise Jesus and thought nothing of it</li><li>The Spirit of God came down like a dove after Jesus was baptised</li><li>A voice from heaven said Jesus was God's beloved Son</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, he left his home town of Nazareth in Galilee and travelled a long way to the Jordan River to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people there. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, just like everyone else who had come that day, but John was very surprised and did not think he should, saying that Jesus should really be the one baptising him instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently explained that it was right for this to happen, so that everything God required would be properly fulfilled. John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the cool, flowing waters of the river, just as he had done for so many others. As soon as Jesus came up out of the water, the sky suddenly opened above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently, appearing like a dove, and rested peacefully on him for everyone nearby to see.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very moment, a powerful voice spoke clearly from heaven, saying, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father, showing everyone watching by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own Son, sent down from heaven for a special purpose. This special moment, with the Father speaking, the Son being baptised, and the Spirit coming down like a dove, marked the very beginning of Jesus's important work here on earth among the people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, he left the quiet town of Nazareth in Galilee, where he had grown up working as a carpenter, and travelled a long way to the Jordan River to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people who had come to repent of their sins. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, just like everyone else who had gathered there that day, but John was very surprised by the request and tried to stop him, saying honestly that he was not worthy to baptise Jesus, and that it should really be the other way around entirely, with Jesus baptising him instead of the other way round.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently but firmly explained to John that it was right and necessary for this to happen in this way, in order to fulfil everything that God the Father required of them both. John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the flowing waters of the Jordan River, just as he had done for so many others before him. As soon as Jesus came back up out of the water, something remarkable happened: the sky suddenly opened wide above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently from heaven, appearing to those watching like a dove descending softly through the air, and rested peacefully upon Jesus for everyone gathered nearby to see with their own eyes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very same moment, a powerful and unmistakable voice spoke clearly from heaven above, declaring for all to hear, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father himself, publicly showing everyone who was watching by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own Son, sent down from heaven for a special and important purpose. This remarkable moment beside the Jordan River, with the Father speaking from heaven, the Son being baptised in the water below, and the Holy Spirit descending gently like a dove, together marked the very beginning of Jesus's important public work here on earth among the people he had come to save, and soon afterwards he would begin gathering his first followers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, having grown up quietly in the small town of Nazareth in Galilee, working as a carpenter alongside his earthly father Joseph, he set out on the long journey to the Jordan River specifically to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people who had come from all around the region to repent of their sins. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, exactly like everyone else who had come to the river that day, but John was deeply surprised by this unexpected request and immediately tried to stop him, saying honestly that he felt completely unworthy to baptise someone as great as Jesus, and that it should really be the other way around entirely, with Jesus baptising him instead of the other way round.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently but firmly explained to John that it was right and necessary for this to happen in this particular way, in order to fulfil everything that God the Father required of them both and to properly begin the work God had planned for him. So John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the cool, flowing waters of the Jordan River, just as he had done for so many other repentant people before him. As soon as Jesus came back up out of the water, dripping and standing on the riverbank, something truly remarkable and completely unforgettable happened before the eyes of everyone present: the sky suddenly opened wide directly above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently from heaven, appearing to those watching like a dove descending softly and peacefully through the open air, and rested calmly upon Jesus for everyone gathered nearby to witness clearly with their own eyes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very same extraordinary moment, a powerful and unmistakable voice spoke out clearly from the opened heavens above, declaring for absolutely everyone present to hear, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father himself, publicly announcing and showing everyone who stood watching in wonder by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven for a special and world-changing purpose. This remarkable and unforgettable moment beside the Jordan River, with the Father speaking from heaven above, the Son standing freshly baptised in the water below, and the Holy Spirit gently descending upon him like a dove, together marked the very beginning of Jesus's important public work and ministry here on earth among the very people he had come to save. Soon afterwards, Jesus would go out into the wilderness to be tested, and then begin travelling from town to town, teaching, healing the sick, and gathering his first followers to help carry his message of good news to the world.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>John</li><li>the Jordan River</li><li>baptise</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus travels to the Jordan River</li><li>He finds his cousin John baptising people</li><li>Jesus asks John to baptise him too</li><li>John is surprised and does not think he should</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>thirty years old</li><li>baptising people</li><li>surprised</li><li>should be the one</li><li>instead</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus travelled to the ___ and asked ___ to baptise him, but John was ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, he left his home town of Nazareth in Galilee and travelled a long way to the Jordan River to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people there. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, just like everyone else who had come that day, but John was very surprised and did not think he should, saying that Jesus should really be the one baptising him instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, he left the quiet town of Nazareth in Galilee, where he had grown up working as a carpenter, and travelled a long way to the Jordan River to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people who had come to repent of their sins. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, just like everyone else who had gathered there that day, but John was very surprised by the request and tried to stop him, saying honestly that he was not worthy to baptise Jesus, and that it should really be the other way around entirely, with Jesus baptising him instead of the other way round.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Jesus was about thirty years old, having grown up quietly in the small town of Nazareth in Galilee, working as a carpenter alongside his earthly father Joseph, he set out on the long journey to the Jordan River specifically to find his cousin John, who was busy baptising large crowds of people who had come from all around the region to repent of their sins. Jesus asked John to baptise him too, exactly like everyone else who had come to the river that day, but John was deeply surprised by this unexpected request and immediately tried to stop him, saying honestly that he felt completely unworthy to baptise someone as great as Jesus, and that it should really be the other way around entirely, with Jesus baptising him instead of the other way round.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>John</li><li>the river</li><li>the Spirit</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus explains that it is right for this to happen</li><li>John agrees and baptises Jesus in the river</li><li>Jesus comes up out of the water</li><li>The sky opens and the Spirit comes down like a dove</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>right for this to happen</li><li>agreed</li><li>came up out of the water</li><li>suddenly opened</li><li>gentle dove</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, John agreed and baptised ___ in the river, and the sky opened as the ___ came down like a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently explained that it was right for this to happen, so that everything God required would be properly fulfilled. John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the cool, flowing waters of the river, just as he had done for so many others. As soon as Jesus came up out of the water, the sky suddenly opened above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently, appearing like a dove, and rested peacefully on him for everyone nearby to see.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently but firmly explained to John that it was right and necessary for this to happen in this way, in order to fulfil everything that God the Father required of them both. John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the flowing waters of the Jordan River, just as he had done for so many others before him. As soon as Jesus came back up out of the water, something remarkable happened: the sky suddenly opened wide above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently from heaven, appearing to those watching like a dove descending softly through the air, and rested peacefully upon Jesus for everyone gathered nearby to see with their own eyes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently but firmly explained to John that it was right and necessary for this to happen in this particular way, in order to fulfil everything that God the Father required of them both and to properly begin the work God had planned for him. So John finally agreed and baptised Jesus in the cool, flowing waters of the Jordan River, just as he had done for so many other repentant people before him. As soon as Jesus came back up out of the water, dripping and standing on the riverbank, something truly remarkable and completely unforgettable happened before the eyes of everyone present: the sky suddenly opened wide directly above him, and the Spirit of God came down gently from heaven, appearing to those watching like a dove descending softly and peacefully through the open air, and rested calmly upon Jesus for everyone gathered nearby to witness clearly with their own eyes.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a voice</li><li>heaven</li><li>God's Son</li><li>Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A voice speaks from heaven</li><li>It says Jesus is God's beloved Son</li><li>It says God is well pleased with him</li><li>This marks the start of Jesus's work on earth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>voice spoke</li><li>from heaven</li><li>beloved Son</li><li>well pleased</li><li>important work</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a ___ spoke from heaven, saying Jesus was God's beloved ___, and this began his work on ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very moment, a powerful voice spoke clearly from heaven, saying, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father, showing everyone watching by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own Son, sent down from heaven for a special purpose. This special moment, with the Father speaking, the Son being baptised, and the Spirit coming down like a dove, marked the very beginning of Jesus's important work here on earth among the people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very same moment, a powerful and unmistakable voice spoke clearly from heaven above, declaring for all to hear, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father himself, publicly showing everyone who was watching by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own Son, sent down from heaven for a special and important purpose. This remarkable moment beside the Jordan River, with the Father speaking from heaven, the Son being baptised in the water below, and the Holy Spirit descending gently like a dove, together marked the very beginning of Jesus's important public work here on earth among the people he had come to save, and soon afterwards he would begin gathering his first followers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At that very same extraordinary moment, a powerful and unmistakable voice spoke out clearly from the opened heavens above, declaring for absolutely everyone present to hear, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.\" It was the voice of God the Father himself, publicly announcing and showing everyone who stood watching in wonder by the riverbank that Jesus truly was God's own beloved Son, sent down from heaven for a special and world-changing purpose. This remarkable and unforgettable moment beside the Jordan River, with the Father speaking from heaven above, the Son standing freshly baptised in the water below, and the Holy Spirit gently descending upon him like a dove, together marked the very beginning of Jesus's important public work and ministry here on earth among the very people he had come to save. Soon afterwards, Jesus would go out into the wilderness to be tested, and then begin travelling from town to town, teaching, healing the sick, and gathering his first followers to help carry his message of good news to the world.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: The Baptism of Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 24 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 24 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 25 (Jesus Calls His First Disciples) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus travelled to the ___ and asked ___ to baptise him, but John was ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, John agreed and baptised ___ in the river, and the sky opened as the ___ came down like a ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, a ___ spoke from heaven, saying Jesus was God's beloved ___, and this began his work on ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 25’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and Peter, James, and John as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Peter, James, and John</li><li>the fishing boat</li><li>the nets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus teaches the crowd from Peter's boat beside the Sea of Galilee</li><li>Jesus tells Peter to row out and let down his nets to catch fish</li><li>They catch so many fish that the nets begin to tear</li><li>Peter, James, and John leave everything to follow Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus taught the crowd from a fishing boat belonging to Simon Peter</li><li>Peter and his friends had already caught a huge number of fish that night</li><li>When they let down their nets, they caught so many fish the nets began to tear</li><li>Peter, James, and John left everything behind to follow Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large crowd of people beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close to listen that he climbed into a fishing boat belonging to a man named Simon Peter and kindly asked him to push out a little way from the shore. Sitting down in the boat, Jesus continued teaching the crowd, and after he had finished, he told Peter to row out into deeper water and let down his nets there to catch some fish.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter told Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen had already worked hard all through the night and had caught nothing at all, but he agreed to try again simply because Jesus had asked him to. When they let down their nets, they suddenly caught so many fish that the nets began to tear apart, and they had to call urgently to their partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come and help them haul it all in. Before long, both boats were filled so full of fish that they nearly began to sink under the weight.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Amazed and a little afraid by this incredible miracle, Peter fell down on his knees before Jesus, and Jesus told him gently not to be afraid, saying that from now on Peter would be catching people for God's kingdom instead of fish. Peter, James, and John brought their boats back to shore, and right there on the beach, without hesitation, they left everything behind, including their nets and boats, to follow Jesus and become his very first disciples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large and eager crowd of people beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close to listen to him speak that he climbed into an empty fishing boat belonging to a fisherman named Simon Peter and asked him kindly to push out a little way from the crowded shore. Sitting down comfortably in the boat, as teachers often did, Jesus continued teaching the crowds gathered along the shoreline for some time, and after he had finished speaking, he turned to Peter and told him to row the boat out into deeper water and let down his nets there to catch some fish for the day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter respectfully told Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen, tired and a little discouraged, had already worked hard all through the previous night and had caught absolutely nothing at all for their trouble, but he agreed to try again anyway simply because Jesus had asked him to. When they finally let down their nets into the deep water as Jesus instructed, they suddenly caught such an enormous number of fish that their nets actually began to tear apart under the tremendous weight, and Peter had to signal urgently to his fishing partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come quickly and help him haul it all in. Before long, both fishing boats were filled so completely full of fish that they began sinking dangerously low into the water beneath them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly amazed and a little afraid by this incredible miracle he had just personally witnessed, Peter fell down on his knees right there in the boat before Jesus, honestly admitting that he felt far too sinful and unworthy to be near him, but Jesus told him gently not to be afraid at all. Jesus then told Peter that from this day onward, he would no longer simply catch fish for a living, but would instead be catching people for God's kingdom, bringing them to know and follow God. Peter, James, and John brought their boats safely back to the crowded shore, and right there on that ordinary beach, without any hesitation or looking back, they left everything behind, including their boats, their nets, and their entire fishing business, in order to follow Jesus faithfully and become his very first disciples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large and eager crowd of people gathered beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close, all wanting to hear the word of God, that he climbed into an empty fishing boat belonging to a fisherman named Simon Peter and politely asked him to push the boat out just a little way from the crowded shore. Nearby, Peter and his fellow fishermen had just finished a long, exhausting night of work and were washing out their empty nets on the beach. Sitting down comfortably in the boat, as teachers often did, Jesus continued teaching the crowds gathered along the shoreline for some time, and after he had finished speaking to everyone, he turned to Peter and told him to row the boat out into the deeper water and let down his fishing nets there to catch some fish for the day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter respectfully explained to Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen, tired and discouraged, had already worked extremely hard through the entire previous night without any success at all and had caught absolutely nothing for their trouble, but he agreed to try again anyway simply because Jesus had asked him to do it. When they finally let down their nets into the deep water as Jesus had instructed, they suddenly caught such an enormous, overwhelming number of fish that their nets actually began to tear apart completely under the tremendous weight, and Peter had to signal urgently to his fishing partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come quickly and help haul in the massive catch. Before very long, both fishing boats were filled so completely full of fish that they began sinking dangerously low into the water beneath them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly amazed and even a little afraid by this astonishing miracle he had just personally witnessed, Peter fell down on his knees right there in the boat before Jesus, honestly admitting that he felt far too sinful and unworthy to even be near someone so powerful, but Jesus spoke to him gently and told him not to be afraid at all. Jesus then told Peter that from this day onward, he would no longer simply catch fish for a living, but would instead be catching people for God's kingdom, bringing them to know and follow God for the rest of their lives. Peter, together with his fishing partners James and John, brought their boats safely back to the crowded shore, and right there on that ordinary beach, without any hesitation or looking back, all three men left everything behind completely, including their boats, their valuable nets, and their entire established fishing business, in order to follow Jesus faithfully from that day forward and become his very first disciples.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the crowd</li><li>Peter</li><li>the boat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A large crowd gathers to hear Jesus teach</li><li>Jesus climbs into Peter's fishing boat</li><li>He asks Peter to push out from the shore</li><li>Jesus teaches the crowd from the boat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>large crowd</li><li>pressed in to listen</li><li>fishing boat</li><li>push out a little</li><li>finished teaching</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, a large ___ gathered to hear Jesus teach, so he climbed into ___'s fishing ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large crowd of people beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close to listen that he climbed into a fishing boat belonging to a man named Simon Peter and kindly asked him to push out a little way from the shore. Sitting down in the boat, Jesus continued teaching the crowd, and after he had finished, he told Peter to row out into deeper water and let down his nets there to catch some fish.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large and eager crowd of people beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close to listen to him speak that he climbed into an empty fishing boat belonging to a fisherman named Simon Peter and asked him kindly to push out a little way from the crowded shore. Sitting down comfortably in the boat, as teachers often did, Jesus continued teaching the crowds gathered along the shoreline for some time, and after he had finished speaking, he turned to Peter and told him to row the boat out into deeper water and let down his nets there to catch some fish for the day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Jesus was teaching a large and eager crowd of people gathered beside the Sea of Galilee, and so many people pressed in close, all wanting to hear the word of God, that he climbed into an empty fishing boat belonging to a fisherman named Simon Peter and politely asked him to push the boat out just a little way from the crowded shore. Nearby, Peter and his fellow fishermen had just finished a long, exhausting night of work and were washing out their empty nets on the beach. Sitting down comfortably in the boat, as teachers often did, Jesus continued teaching the crowds gathered along the shoreline for some time, and after he had finished speaking to everyone, he turned to Peter and told him to row the boat out into the deeper water and let down his fishing nets there to catch some fish for the day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter</li><li>the nets</li><li>the fish</li><li>James and John</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus tells Peter to let down his nets</li><li>Peter says they caught nothing all night but agrees to try</li><li>They catch so many fish the nets begin to tear</li><li>James and John help haul in the huge catch</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>worked hard all night</li><li>caught nothing</li><li>began to tear</li><li>call their partners</li><li>nearly began to sink</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Peter let down his ___ and caught so many fish they began to ___, so ___ came to help.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter told Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen had already worked hard all through the night and had caught nothing at all, but he agreed to try again simply because Jesus had asked him to. When they let down their nets, they suddenly caught so many fish that the nets began to tear apart, and they had to call urgently to their partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come and help them haul it all in. Before long, both boats were filled so full of fish that they nearly began to sink under the weight.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter respectfully told Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen, tired and a little discouraged, had already worked hard all through the previous night and had caught absolutely nothing at all for their trouble, but he agreed to try again anyway simply because Jesus had asked him to. When they finally let down their nets into the deep water as Jesus instructed, they suddenly caught such an enormous number of fish that their nets actually began to tear apart under the tremendous weight, and Peter had to signal urgently to his fishing partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come quickly and help him haul it all in. Before long, both fishing boats were filled so completely full of fish that they began sinking dangerously low into the water beneath them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter respectfully explained to Jesus that he and his fellow fishermen, tired and discouraged, had already worked extremely hard through the entire previous night without any success at all and had caught absolutely nothing for their trouble, but he agreed to try again anyway simply because Jesus had asked him to do it. When they finally let down their nets into the deep water as Jesus had instructed, they suddenly caught such an enormous, overwhelming number of fish that their nets actually began to tear apart completely under the tremendous weight, and Peter had to signal urgently to his fishing partners, James and John, in another boat nearby to come quickly and help haul in the massive catch. Before very long, both fishing boats were filled so completely full of fish that they began sinking dangerously low into the water beneath them.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the nets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter falls down before Jesus, amazed</li><li>Jesus tells him not to be afraid</li><li>Jesus says Peter will catch people instead of fish</li><li>They leave everything behind to follow Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>amazed</li><li>not to be afraid</li><li>catching people</li><li>left everything behind</li><li>first disciples</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jesus told Peter he would catch ___ instead of fish, and Peter, James, and John left ___ to become his first ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Amazed and a little afraid by this incredible miracle, Peter fell down on his knees before Jesus, and Jesus told him gently not to be afraid, saying that from now on Peter would be catching people for God's kingdom instead of fish. Peter, James, and John brought their boats back to shore, and right there on the beach, without hesitation, they left everything behind, including their nets and boats, to follow Jesus and become his very first disciples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly amazed and a little afraid by this incredible miracle he had just personally witnessed, Peter fell down on his knees right there in the boat before Jesus, honestly admitting that he felt far too sinful and unworthy to be near him, but Jesus told him gently not to be afraid at all. Jesus then told Peter that from this day onward, he would no longer simply catch fish for a living, but would instead be catching people for God's kingdom, bringing them to know and follow God. Peter, James, and John brought their boats safely back to the crowded shore, and right there on that ordinary beach, without any hesitation or looking back, they left everything behind, including their boats, their nets, and their entire fishing business, in order to follow Jesus faithfully and become his very first disciples.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly amazed and even a little afraid by this astonishing miracle he had just personally witnessed, Peter fell down on his knees right there in the boat before Jesus, honestly admitting that he felt far too sinful and unworthy to even be near someone so powerful, but Jesus spoke to him gently and told him not to be afraid at all. Jesus then told Peter that from this day onward, he would no longer simply catch fish for a living, but would instead be catching people for God's kingdom, bringing them to know and follow God for the rest of their lives. Peter, together with his fishing partners James and John, brought their boats safely back to the crowded shore, and right there on that ordinary beach, without any hesitation or looking back, all three men left everything behind completely, including their boats, their valuable nets, and their entire established fishing business, in order to follow Jesus faithfully from that day forward and become his very first disciples.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Jesus Calls His First Disciples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 25 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 25 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 26 (The Sower & the Seeds) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, a large ___ gathered to hear Jesus teach, so he climbed into ___'s fishing ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Peter let down his ___ and caught so many fish they began to ___, so ___ came to help.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jesus told Peter he would catch ___ instead of fish, and Peter, James, and John left ___ to become his first ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (Purple belt) — Stories 26–30":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 26: The Sower & the Seeds":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: The Sower &amp; the Seeds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 26’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 26: The Sower & the Seeds) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the farmer and the seed as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the farmer</li><li>the seed</li><li>the field</li><li>the harvest</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus tells a crowd a story about a farmer who scatters seed</li><li>Some seed falls on the path and is eaten by birds</li><li>Some seed falls on rocky ground and withers, and some falls among thorns</li><li>Some seed falls on good soil and produces a wonderful harvest</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the story, a farmer scatters seed across his field</li><li>All of the seed the farmer scattered grew into a huge harvest</li><li>Seed that fell among thorns was choked and could not grow</li><li>Seed that fell on good soil produced a wonderful harvest</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a large crowd gathered around Jesus beside the lake to hear him teach, so he climbed into a boat, sat down, and told the people this story. A farmer went out into his field to sow his seed by scattering it across the ground as he walked. Some seed fell on the hard path, and hungry birds quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it could ever sprout or grow at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed fell on rocky ground where there was only a thin layer of soil covering the stone below. It sprang up very quickly, but because its roots could not grow deep, it withered away and died as soon as the hot sun came out overhead. Still other seed fell among thorny weeds growing nearby, and as the seed grew, the thorns grew up quickly around it and choked the young plants completely, so they never produced any grain at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But some seed fell on good, rich soil, and there it grew into strong, healthy plants that produced a truly wonderful harvest, some plants producing thirty times as much as was planted, some sixty times as much, and some even a hundred times as much. Afterwards, when his disciples asked what the story meant, Jesus explained patiently that the seed is God's word, and the different types of ground are like the different ways that people's hearts receive it when they hear it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, such a large crowd of people gathered around Jesus beside the lake to hear him teach that he climbed into a boat and sat down a little way from the shore, while the great crowd stood listening attentively on the sand nearby. Jesus loved teaching important truths using simple stories called parables, so he told the people this memorable story about a farmer who went out into his field one morning to sow his seed by scattering generous handfuls of it across the ground as he walked steadily along. As he scattered the seed, some of it fell along the hard, well-worn path running straight through the middle of the field, and hungry birds circling nearby quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it ever had any real chance to sprout or grow at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed that the farmer scattered fell onto rocky ground where there was only a thin, shallow layer of soil covering the stone underneath. This seed sprang up very quickly because the shallow soil warmed up fast in the sun, but because its roots could not grow deep enough down to reach any water, the young plants soon withered away completely and died as soon as the hot sun rose high in the sky above them. Still other seed happened to fall among thorny weeds growing nearby in the same field, and although the seed did sprout and grow for a while, the thorns grew up quickly and thickly all around the young, tender plants and choked them completely, so that they could never manage to produce any grain at all by harvest time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But some of the farmer's seed fell onto good, rich soil, and there it grew up strong and healthy, eventually producing a truly wonderful harvest, with some plants producing thirty times as much grain as was originally planted, some producing sixty times as much, and some plants producing an incredible hundred times as much. Afterwards, when his disciples asked him what this story meant, Jesus explained patiently that the seed represents God's word, and the four very different types of ground represent the different ways that people's hearts can receive that word when they hear it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, such an enormous crowd of people gathered around Jesus beside the lake, all eager to hear him teach, that he climbed into a small boat and sat down a little way from the shore, while the great crowd stood listening attentively on the sand nearby. Jesus often loved teaching important truths using simple stories called parables, so on this occasion he told the people a memorable story about a farmer who went out into his field one morning to sow his seed by scattering generous handfuls of it across the ground as he walked steadily along. As he scattered the seed with a wide sweep of his arm, some of it fell along the hard, well-worn path that ran straight through the middle of the field, and hungry birds circling nearby quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it ever had any real chance to sprout or grow at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed that the farmer scattered that morning happened to fall onto rocky ground, where there was only a thin, shallow layer of soil stretched thinly over solid stone underneath. This particular seed sprang up remarkably quickly because the shallow soil warmed up fast in the sun, but because its roots could never grow deep enough down to reach any water below, the young plants soon withered away completely and died as soon as the hot midday sun rose high overhead. Still other seed happened to fall among a patch of thorny weeds growing nearby in the same field, and although this seed did sprout and begin growing for a while, the thorns quickly grew up thickly all around the young, tender plants and choked them completely, so that they could never manage to produce any grain at all by harvest time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But finally, some of the farmer's remaining seed fell onto good, rich, well-prepared soil, and there it grew up wonderfully strong and healthy all season long, eventually producing a truly remarkable harvest at the end, with some plants producing thirty times as much grain as was originally planted, some plants producing sixty times as much, and some plants producing an incredible hundred times as much as before. Afterwards, when his disciples came to him privately and asked what this story actually meant, Jesus explained patiently and carefully that the seed represents God's own word, and the four very different types of ground each represent a different way that people's hearts can receive that word when they hear it: some hearts are hardened like the path, some are shallow like the rocky ground, some are distracted by worries and riches like the thorny ground, and some are truly ready and open, like the good soil that produces such a wonderful harvest.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: The Sower &amp; the Seeds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the farmer</li><li>the seed</li><li>the path</li><li>the birds</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus tells the crowd a story about a farmer</li><li>The farmer goes out to sow his seed</li><li>Some seed falls on the hard path</li><li>Birds swoop down and eat the seed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>large crowd</li><li>scattering it</li><li>hard path</li><li>swooped down</li><li>before it could grow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus told a story about a ___ who scattered seed, but some fell on the ___ and ___ ate it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a large crowd gathered around Jesus beside the lake to hear him teach, so he climbed into a boat, sat down, and told the people this story. A farmer went out into his field to sow his seed by scattering it across the ground as he walked. Some seed fell on the hard path, and hungry birds quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it could ever sprout or grow at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, such a large crowd of people gathered around Jesus beside the lake to hear him teach that he climbed into a boat and sat down a little way from the shore, while the great crowd stood listening attentively on the sand nearby. Jesus loved teaching important truths using simple stories called parables, so he told the people this memorable story about a farmer who went out into his field one morning to sow his seed by scattering generous handfuls of it across the ground as he walked steadily along. As he scattered the seed, some of it fell along the hard, well-worn path running straight through the middle of the field, and hungry birds circling nearby quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it ever had any real chance to sprout or grow at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, such an enormous crowd of people gathered around Jesus beside the lake, all eager to hear him teach, that he climbed into a small boat and sat down a little way from the shore, while the great crowd stood listening attentively on the sand nearby. Jesus often loved teaching important truths using simple stories called parables, so on this occasion he told the people a memorable story about a farmer who went out into his field one morning to sow his seed by scattering generous handfuls of it across the ground as he walked steadily along. As he scattered the seed with a wide sweep of his arm, some of it fell along the hard, well-worn path that ran straight through the middle of the field, and hungry birds circling nearby quickly swooped down and ate it all up before it ever had any real chance to sprout or grow at all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: The Sower &amp; the Seeds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the rocky ground</li><li>the roots</li><li>the thorns</li><li>the plants</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some seed falls on rocky ground</li><li>It springs up quickly but has shallow roots</li><li>It withers when the hot sun comes out</li><li>Other seed falls among thorns and is choked</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rocky ground</li><li>sprang up quickly</li><li>shallow</li><li>withered away</li><li>choked</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, seed on the ___ sprang up but withered in the sun, and seed among ___ was ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed fell on rocky ground where there was only a thin layer of soil covering the stone below. It sprang up very quickly, but because its roots could not grow deep, it withered away and died as soon as the hot sun came out overhead. Still other seed fell among thorny weeds growing nearby, and as the seed grew, the thorns grew up quickly around it and choked the young plants completely, so they never produced any grain at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed that the farmer scattered fell onto rocky ground where there was only a thin, shallow layer of soil covering the stone underneath. This seed sprang up very quickly because the shallow soil warmed up fast in the sun, but because its roots could not grow deep enough down to reach any water, the young plants soon withered away completely and died as soon as the hot sun rose high in the sky above them. Still other seed happened to fall among thorny weeds growing nearby in the same field, and although the seed did sprout and grow for a while, the thorns grew up quickly and thickly all around the young, tender plants and choked them completely, so that they could never manage to produce any grain at all by harvest time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Other seed that the farmer scattered that morning happened to fall onto rocky ground, where there was only a thin, shallow layer of soil stretched thinly over solid stone underneath. This particular seed sprang up remarkably quickly because the shallow soil warmed up fast in the sun, but because its roots could never grow deep enough down to reach any water below, the young plants soon withered away completely and died as soon as the hot midday sun rose high overhead. Still other seed happened to fall among a patch of thorny weeds growing nearby in the same field, and although this seed did sprout and begin growing for a while, the thorns quickly grew up thickly all around the young, tender plants and choked them completely, so that they could never manage to produce any grain at all by harvest time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: The Sower &amp; the Seeds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the good soil</li><li>the harvest</li><li>God's word</li><li>people's hearts</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some seed falls on good soil</li><li>It grows into a wonderful harvest</li><li>Jesus explains the seed is God's word</li><li>The different ground is like people's hearts</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>good, rich soil</li><li>wonderful harvest</li><li>God's word</li><li>different types</li><li>hearts</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, seed on ___ soil grew a wonderful ___, and Jesus explained the seed is God's ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But some seed fell on good, rich soil, and there it grew into strong, healthy plants that produced a truly wonderful harvest, some plants producing thirty times as much as was planted, some sixty times as much, and some even a hundred times as much. Afterwards, when his disciples asked what the story meant, Jesus explained patiently that the seed is God's word, and the different types of ground are like the different ways that people's hearts receive it when they hear it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But some of the farmer's seed fell onto good, rich soil, and there it grew up strong and healthy, eventually producing a truly wonderful harvest, with some plants producing thirty times as much grain as was originally planted, some producing sixty times as much, and some plants producing an incredible hundred times as much. Afterwards, when his disciples asked him what this story meant, Jesus explained patiently that the seed represents God's word, and the four very different types of ground represent the different ways that people's hearts can receive that word when they hear it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But finally, some of the farmer's remaining seed fell onto good, rich, well-prepared soil, and there it grew up wonderfully strong and healthy all season long, eventually producing a truly remarkable harvest at the end, with some plants producing thirty times as much grain as was originally planted, some plants producing sixty times as much, and some plants producing an incredible hundred times as much as before. Afterwards, when his disciples came to him privately and asked what this story actually meant, Jesus explained patiently and carefully that the seed represents God's own word, and the four very different types of ground each represent a different way that people's hearts can receive that word when they hear it: some hearts are hardened like the path, some are shallow like the rocky ground, some are distracted by worries and riches like the thorny ground, and some are truly ready and open, like the good soil that produces such a wonderful harvest.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: The Sower &amp; the Seeds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 26 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 26 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 27 (The Good Samaritan) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus told a story about a ___ who scattered seed, but some fell on the ___ and ___ ate it.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, seed on the ___ sprang up but withered in the sun, and seed among ___ was ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, seed on ___ soil grew a wonderful ___, and Jesus explained the seed is God's ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 27: The Good Samaritan":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: The Good Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 27’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 27: The Good Samaritan) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the hurt man and the Samaritan as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the hurt man</li><li>the Samaritan</li><li>the priest</li><li>the Levite</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A man is attacked by robbers and left hurt beside the road</li><li>A priest sees him but walks past on the other side</li><li>A Levite also sees him but walks past without helping</li><li>A Samaritan stops, bandages his wounds, and pays for his care at an inn</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Robbers attacked a man travelling on the road and left him hurt</li><li>The priest stopped straight away and helped the hurt man</li><li>The Samaritan stopped to help even though Jews usually looked down on Samaritans</li><li>Jesus said the Samaritan was the one who truly acted like a neighbour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher asked Jesus a tricky question, wanting to know who really counted as his neighbour, and Jesus answered him with a story. A man was travelling alone on the dangerous, rocky road down from Jerusalem to Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly attacked him, took everything he had, beat him, and left him hurt and in trouble beside the road, with nobody nearby to help.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, a priest came along the road, saw the hurt man lying there, but simply walked past on the other side without stopping to help him at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked at the Temple, came along the same road, looked at the hurt man, and he too walked past on the other side, leaving the poor man lying there just as badly hurt as before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone from a group of people the Jews usually looked down on and avoided, came along that same road. When he saw the hurt man lying there, he felt great compassion for him and stopped at once to help. He knelt down, gently bandaged the man's wounds, took him to a nearby inn, and paid the innkeeper to take care of him until he was completely well again. Jesus then asked the religious teacher which of the three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour, and the answer was clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher stood up in front of the crowd and asked Jesus a tricky question, hoping to test him, wanting to know who really counted as his neighbour, and Jesus answered him with a memorable story instead of a simple direct answer. A man was travelling alone along the steep and dangerous road that wound its way down from the city of Jerusalem to the town of Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly attacked him along the way, stripped him of his clothes and belongings, beat him badly, and left him lying half-dead, hurt and in trouble beside the rocky road, with nobody nearby who seemed willing to help him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, a priest happened to be travelling down that very same lonely road on his own journey, and when he noticed the hurt man lying there, he deliberately crossed over to the far side of the road and walked straight past without stopping to help him in any way at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked and served at the Temple in Jerusalem, came travelling along that same road, walked over and looked closely at the hurt man for a moment, and then he too crossed over and walked past on the other side, leaving the poor injured man lying there just as badly hurt as he had found him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone from a group of people whom the Jewish people usually looked down upon and avoided, came along that same road on his journey. But when he saw the man lying there badly hurt, he felt great compassion for him and stopped immediately to help. He knelt down, gently cleaned and bandaged the man's wounds, lifted him onto his own donkey, and carried him carefully to a nearby inn to look after him through the night. The next morning, he even paid the innkeeper generously to continue caring for the man until he was completely well again. Jesus then asked the religious teacher which of the three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour to the hurt man, and the answer was completely clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher stood up in front of the crowd and asked Jesus a tricky question, hoping to test him, wanting to know exactly who really counted as his neighbour according to God's law, and Jesus answered him with a memorable story rather than giving a simple direct answer. A man was travelling alone along the steep, rocky, and genuinely dangerous road that wound its way down from the city of Jerusalem to the town of Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly ambushed him along the way, stripped him of his clothes and everything he owned, beat him badly, and left him lying half-dead, hurt and in trouble beside the road, with nobody nearby who seemed willing to help him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long, a priest happened to be travelling down that very same lonely road on his own journey, and when he noticed the hurt man lying there beside the path, he deliberately crossed over to the far side of the road and walked straight past without stopping to help him in any way at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked and served at the Temple in Jerusalem, came travelling along that same road, walked over and looked closely at the hurt man for a moment, and then he too crossed over and walked past on the other side, leaving the poor injured man lying there just as badly hurt as he had found him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone belonging to a group of people whom the Jewish people usually looked down upon, distrusted, and generally avoided altogether, came travelling along that same road on his own journey. But when he saw the man lying there badly hurt and helpless, he felt deep compassion for him and stopped his journey immediately to help. He knelt down beside the man, gently cleaned and bandaged his wounds with oil and wine, carefully lifted him up onto his own donkey, and led the animal slowly to a nearby inn so he could look after the man properly through the night. The very next morning, before continuing his own journey, he even paid the innkeeper two silver coins and generously promised to repay any further cost, asking him to keep caring for the injured man until he was completely well again. Jesus then turned back to the religious teacher and asked him which of these three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour to the hurt man, and the teacher himself had to admit that the answer was completely clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy, and Jesus told him to go and do exactly the same.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: The Good Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the man</li><li>the robbers</li><li>the road</li><li>Jerusalem to Jericho</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A man travels the road from Jerusalem to Jericho</li><li>Robbers attack him along the road</li><li>They take everything he has</li><li>They leave him hurt and in trouble beside the road</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>dangerous road</li><li>attacked him</li><li>took everything</li><li>hurt and in trouble</li><li>beside the road</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, a man travelling from Jerusalem to ___ was attacked by ___, who left him ___ beside the road.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher asked Jesus a tricky question, wanting to know who really counted as his neighbour, and Jesus answered him with a story. A man was travelling alone on the dangerous, rocky road down from Jerusalem to Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly attacked him, took everything he had, beat him, and left him hurt and in trouble beside the road, with nobody nearby to help.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher stood up in front of the crowd and asked Jesus a tricky question, hoping to test him, wanting to know who really counted as his neighbour, and Jesus answered him with a memorable story instead of a simple direct answer. A man was travelling alone along the steep and dangerous road that wound its way down from the city of Jerusalem to the town of Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly attacked him along the way, stripped him of his clothes and belongings, beat him badly, and left him lying half-dead, hurt and in trouble beside the rocky road, with nobody nearby who seemed willing to help him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a religious teacher stood up in front of the crowd and asked Jesus a tricky question, hoping to test him, wanting to know exactly who really counted as his neighbour according to God's law, and Jesus answered him with a memorable story rather than giving a simple direct answer. A man was travelling alone along the steep, rocky, and genuinely dangerous road that wound its way down from the city of Jerusalem to the town of Jericho when a group of robbers suddenly ambushed him along the way, stripped him of his clothes and everything he owned, beat him badly, and left him lying half-dead, hurt and in trouble beside the road, with nobody nearby who seemed willing to help him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: The Good Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the priest</li><li>the Levite</li><li>the other side</li><li>the hurt man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A priest sees the hurt man on the road</li><li>He walks past on the other side</li><li>A Levite also comes along and sees the man</li><li>He also walks past without helping</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>priest</li><li>walked past</li><li>other side</li><li>Levite</li><li>without stopping</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a ___ saw the hurt man but walked past, and then a ___ also walked past on the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, a priest came along the road, saw the hurt man lying there, but simply walked past on the other side without stopping to help him at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked at the Temple, came along the same road, looked at the hurt man, and he too walked past on the other side, leaving the poor man lying there just as badly hurt as before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, a priest happened to be travelling down that very same lonely road on his own journey, and when he noticed the hurt man lying there, he deliberately crossed over to the far side of the road and walked straight past without stopping to help him in any way at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked and served at the Temple in Jerusalem, came travelling along that same road, walked over and looked closely at the hurt man for a moment, and then he too crossed over and walked past on the other side, leaving the poor injured man lying there just as badly hurt as he had found him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long, a priest happened to be travelling down that very same lonely road on his own journey, and when he noticed the hurt man lying there beside the path, he deliberately crossed over to the far side of the road and walked straight past without stopping to help him in any way at all. A little later, a Levite, a man who also worked and served at the Temple in Jerusalem, came travelling along that same road, walked over and looked closely at the hurt man for a moment, and then he too crossed over and walked past on the other side, leaving the poor injured man lying there just as badly hurt as he had found him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: The Good Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the Samaritan</li><li>the wounds</li><li>the inn</li><li>a neighbour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A Samaritan comes along and sees the hurt man</li><li>He feels sorry for him and bandages his wounds</li><li>He takes him to an inn and pays for his care</li><li>Jesus asks who truly acted like a neighbour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samaritan</li><li>felt sorry</li><li>bandaged</li><li>paid for the innkeeper</li><li>showed mercy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a ___ stopped to help, bandaged his ___, and paid for him to stay at an ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone from a group of people the Jews usually looked down on and avoided, came along that same road. When he saw the hurt man lying there, he felt great compassion for him and stopped at once to help. He knelt down, gently bandaged the man's wounds, took him to a nearby inn, and paid the innkeeper to take care of him until he was completely well again. Jesus then asked the religious teacher which of the three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour, and the answer was clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone from a group of people whom the Jewish people usually looked down upon and avoided, came along that same road on his journey. But when he saw the man lying there badly hurt, he felt great compassion for him and stopped immediately to help. He knelt down, gently cleaned and bandaged the man's wounds, lifted him onto his own donkey, and carried him carefully to a nearby inn to look after him through the night. The next morning, he even paid the innkeeper generously to continue caring for the man until he was completely well again. Jesus then asked the religious teacher which of the three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour to the hurt man, and the answer was completely clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then a Samaritan traveller, someone belonging to a group of people whom the Jewish people usually looked down upon, distrusted, and generally avoided altogether, came travelling along that same road on his own journey. But when he saw the man lying there badly hurt and helpless, he felt deep compassion for him and stopped his journey immediately to help. He knelt down beside the man, gently cleaned and bandaged his wounds with oil and wine, carefully lifted him up onto his own donkey, and led the animal slowly to a nearby inn so he could look after the man properly through the night. The very next morning, before continuing his own journey, he even paid the innkeeper two silver coins and generously promised to repay any further cost, asking him to keep caring for the injured man until he was completely well again. Jesus then turned back to the religious teacher and asked him which of these three travellers had truly acted like a neighbour to the hurt man, and the teacher himself had to admit that the answer was completely clear: it was the one who had shown him mercy, and Jesus told him to go and do exactly the same.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: The Good Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 27 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 27 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 28 (The Lost Sheep) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, a man travelling from Jerusalem to ___ was attacked by ___, who left him ___ beside the road.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a ___ saw the hurt man but walked past, and then a ___ also walked past on the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, a ___ stopped to help, bandaged his ___, and paid for him to stay at an ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 28: The Lost Sheep":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 28’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 28: The Lost Sheep) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the shepherd and the lost sheep as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherd</li><li>the lost sheep</li><li>the ninety-nine</li><li>heaven</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Religious leaders grumble that Jesus spends time with sinners</li><li>Jesus tells of a shepherd whose one sheep wanders off and gets lost</li><li>The shepherd leaves the ninety-nine and searches until he finds it</li><li>He carries it home joyfully and calls friends to rejoice with him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In the story, one sheep wanders away from the flock of a hundred</li><li>The shepherd decides it is not worth searching for just one sheep</li><li>The shepherd carries the lost sheep home on his shoulders when he finds it</li><li>Jesus said there is great rejoicing in heaven when one sinner repents</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders grumbled that Jesus welcomed sinners and even ate meals with them, so Jesus told them this simple story to help them understand. Imagine a shepherd who owns a hundred sheep in total, and one of them wanders away from the flock and becomes completely lost out in the wild countryside. The shepherd leaves the other ninety-nine sheep safely in the open field and goes out determinedly to search for the one sheep that is lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches carefully and patiently, climbing over rocky hills and walking through steep valleys, calling out again and again for his lost sheep, until at last, after a long search, he finds it. He does not scold the frightened sheep or simply leave it to find its own way back to the flock. Instead, filled with joy, he gently picks it up, carries it carefully on his shoulders, and carries it the whole way home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives home carrying his sheep, he happily calls his friends and neighbours together and says joyfully, \"Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then explained that in exactly the same way, there is great rejoicing in heaven among the angels over just one sinner who genuinely repents and turns back to God, even more than over ninety-nine other people who never needed to repent because they never wandered away at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders and teachers of the law grumbled among themselves that Jesus willingly welcomed sinners and tax collectors and even ate meals together with them, so Jesus told them this simple but powerful story to help them truly understand God's own heart. Imagine a shepherd who owns a hundred sheep in total, and one single sheep suddenly wanders away carelessly from the flock and becomes completely and hopelessly lost out in the wild countryside. What does that shepherd do the moment he realises a sheep is missing? He leaves the other ninety-nine sheep safely behind together in the open field and sets out at once, determined to search everywhere for the one sheep that has wandered off and become lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches everywhere with great care and patience, climbing steadily over rocky hills and walking carefully through steep, shadowy valleys, calling out loudly again and again for his lost sheep by name, until at long last, after a long and tiring search, he finally finds it hiding somewhere, frightened, exhausted, and completely alone. He does not scold the frightened sheep even once for wandering away, nor does he simply leave it there to struggle and find its own way back to the flock by itself. Instead, filled with overwhelming joy and relief at having found it safe, he gently lifts the tired sheep up, lays it carefully across his own shoulders, and carries it the whole long way back home, utterly delighted to have found what had been lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives back home carrying his sheep, he happily calls his friends and neighbours together and says joyfully to them all, \"Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then explained to the grumbling religious leaders that in exactly the same way, there is tremendous rejoicing in heaven among the angels of God over just one single sinner who genuinely repents and turns back to God, even more joy than over ninety-nine other people who never needed to repent because they never wandered away in the first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders and teachers of the law grumbled quietly among themselves that Jesus willingly welcomed sinners and tax collectors and even sat down to eat meals together with them, so Jesus told them this simple but deeply meaningful story to help them truly understand God's own heart. Imagine, Jesus said, a shepherd who owns a flock of exactly a hundred sheep in total, and suddenly one single sheep wanders carelessly away from the safety of the flock and becomes completely and hopelessly lost out in the wild, rugged countryside. What does that shepherd do the moment he realises one sheep is missing? He immediately leaves the remaining ninety-nine sheep safely behind together in the open field or with someone to watch them, and sets out at once, determined to search everywhere for the one single sheep that has wandered off and become lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches everywhere with great care and patience, climbing steadily over rocky hills and walking carefully through steep, shadowy valleys, calling out loudly again and again for his lost sheep by name, until at long last, after a long, tiring, and difficult search, he finally finds it hiding somewhere, frightened, exhausted, and completely alone. He does not scold the frightened little sheep even once for wandering away and causing him so much trouble, nor does he simply leave it there to struggle and find its own way back to the flock by itself. Instead, filled with overwhelming joy and relief at having found it safe, he gently lifts the tired sheep up, lays it carefully and securely across his own shoulders, and carries it the whole long way back home, utterly delighted to have found what had been lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives back home carrying his rescued sheep safely on his shoulders, he cannot contain his happiness, and he calls his friends and neighbours together at once and says joyfully to them all, \"Come and rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then turned back to the grumbling religious leaders and explained clearly that in exactly the same way, there is tremendous rejoicing in heaven among all of God's angels over just one single sinner who genuinely and sincerely repents and turns back to God, even more joy than over ninety-nine perfectly good people who never needed to repent at all, because they never wandered away from God in the first place. In this way, Jesus showed that God our Father cares deeply and personally about every single lost person, and actively searches for them, just as a good shepherd searches faithfully for even one single missing sheep.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the religious leaders</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the shepherd</li><li>the sheep</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Religious leaders grumble that Jesus spends time with sinners</li><li>Jesus tells them a story about a shepherd</li><li>The shepherd has a hundred sheep</li><li>One sheep wanders away and gets lost</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>grumbled</li><li>told them this story</li><li>a hundred sheep</li><li>wanders away</li><li>gets lost</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the religious leaders grumbled that Jesus spent time with ___, so Jesus told a story about a shepherd whose one ___ got ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders grumbled that Jesus welcomed sinners and even ate meals with them, so Jesus told them this simple story to help them understand. Imagine a shepherd who owns a hundred sheep in total, and one of them wanders away from the flock and becomes completely lost out in the wild countryside. The shepherd leaves the other ninety-nine sheep safely in the open field and goes out determinedly to search for the one sheep that is lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders and teachers of the law grumbled among themselves that Jesus willingly welcomed sinners and tax collectors and even ate meals together with them, so Jesus told them this simple but powerful story to help them truly understand God's own heart. Imagine a shepherd who owns a hundred sheep in total, and one single sheep suddenly wanders away carelessly from the flock and becomes completely and hopelessly lost out in the wild countryside. What does that shepherd do the moment he realises a sheep is missing? He leaves the other ninety-nine sheep safely behind together in the open field and sets out at once, determined to search everywhere for the one sheep that has wandered off and become lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some religious leaders and teachers of the law grumbled quietly among themselves that Jesus willingly welcomed sinners and tax collectors and even sat down to eat meals together with them, so Jesus told them this simple but deeply meaningful story to help them truly understand God's own heart. Imagine, Jesus said, a shepherd who owns a flock of exactly a hundred sheep in total, and suddenly one single sheep wanders carelessly away from the safety of the flock and becomes completely and hopelessly lost out in the wild, rugged countryside. What does that shepherd do the moment he realises one sheep is missing? He immediately leaves the remaining ninety-nine sheep safely behind together in the open field or with someone to watch them, and sets out at once, determined to search everywhere for the one single sheep that has wandered off and become lost.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherd</li><li>the ninety-nine</li><li>the field</li><li>the lost sheep</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The shepherd leaves the ninety-nine sheep in the field</li><li>He goes out to search for the one lost sheep</li><li>He searches over hills and through valleys</li><li>At last he finds the lost sheep</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ninety-nine</li><li>safely</li><li>searches carefully</li><li>calling out</li><li>at last he finds it</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the shepherd left the ___ sheep safely and searched the hills until he ___ the lost ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches carefully and patiently, climbing over rocky hills and walking through steep valleys, calling out again and again for his lost sheep, until at last, after a long search, he finds it. He does not scold the frightened sheep or simply leave it to find its own way back to the flock. Instead, filled with joy, he gently picks it up, carries it carefully on his shoulders, and carries it the whole way home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches everywhere with great care and patience, climbing steadily over rocky hills and walking carefully through steep, shadowy valleys, calling out loudly again and again for his lost sheep by name, until at long last, after a long and tiring search, he finally finds it hiding somewhere, frightened, exhausted, and completely alone. He does not scold the frightened sheep even once for wandering away, nor does he simply leave it there to struggle and find its own way back to the flock by itself. Instead, filled with overwhelming joy and relief at having found it safe, he gently lifts the tired sheep up, lays it carefully across his own shoulders, and carries it the whole long way back home, utterly delighted to have found what had been lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The shepherd searches everywhere with great care and patience, climbing steadily over rocky hills and walking carefully through steep, shadowy valleys, calling out loudly again and again for his lost sheep by name, until at long last, after a long, tiring, and difficult search, he finally finds it hiding somewhere, frightened, exhausted, and completely alone. He does not scold the frightened little sheep even once for wandering away and causing him so much trouble, nor does he simply leave it there to struggle and find its own way back to the flock by itself. Instead, filled with overwhelming joy and relief at having found it safe, he gently lifts the tired sheep up, lays it carefully and securely across his own shoulders, and carries it the whole long way back home, utterly delighted to have found what had been lost.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the shepherd</li><li>his shoulders</li><li>his friends</li><li>heaven</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The shepherd carries the sheep home on his shoulders</li><li>He calls his friends and neighbours together</li><li>He says to rejoice because his sheep is found</li><li>Jesus says heaven rejoices over one sinner who repents</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>carries it gently</li><li>friends and neighbours</li><li>rejoice</li><li>found my lost sheep</li><li>rejoicing in heaven</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the shepherd carried the sheep home on his ___ and called his friends to ___, just as heaven rejoices over one ___ who repents.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives home carrying his sheep, he happily calls his friends and neighbours together and says joyfully, \"Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then explained that in exactly the same way, there is great rejoicing in heaven among the angels over just one sinner who genuinely repents and turns back to God, even more than over ninety-nine other people who never needed to repent because they never wandered away at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives back home carrying his sheep, he happily calls his friends and neighbours together and says joyfully to them all, \"Rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then explained to the grumbling religious leaders that in exactly the same way, there is tremendous rejoicing in heaven among the angels of God over just one single sinner who genuinely repents and turns back to God, even more joy than over ninety-nine other people who never needed to repent because they never wandered away in the first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the shepherd finally arrives back home carrying his rescued sheep safely on his shoulders, he cannot contain his happiness, and he calls his friends and neighbours together at once and says joyfully to them all, \"Come and rejoice with me, for I have found my sheep that was lost!\" Jesus then turned back to the grumbling religious leaders and explained clearly that in exactly the same way, there is tremendous rejoicing in heaven among all of God's angels over just one single sinner who genuinely and sincerely repents and turns back to God, even more joy than over ninety-nine perfectly good people who never needed to repent at all, because they never wandered away from God in the first place. In this way, Jesus showed that God our Father cares deeply and personally about every single lost person, and actively searches for them, just as a good shepherd searches faithfully for even one single missing sheep.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 28 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 28 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 29 (The Prodigal Son) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the religious leaders grumbled that Jesus spent time with ___, so Jesus told a story about a shepherd whose one ___ got ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the shepherd left the ___ sheep safely and searched the hills until he ___ the lost ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the shepherd carried the sheep home on his ___ and called his friends to ___, just as heaven rejoices over one ___ who repents.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 29: The Prodigal Son":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: The Prodigal Son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 29’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 29: The Prodigal Son) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the younger son and the father as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the younger son</li><li>the father</li><li>the older son</li><li>the feast</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The younger son asks for his inheritance and travels far away</li><li>He wastes his money and ends up feeding pigs, starving</li><li>He decides to return home and ask to be a servant</li><li>His father runs to welcome him home and holds a great feast</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The younger son asked his father for his share of the inheritance early</li><li>The younger son wisely saved all his money in the faraway country</li><li>The younger son ended up feeding pigs because he was so poor</li><li>The father ran to welcome his son home and held a great feast</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable story about a father who had two sons, one older and one younger. The younger son grew restless and boldly asked his father for his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had died, and surprisingly, the kind father agreed and gave it to him. Soon after, the younger son packed up his things and travelled to a faraway country, where he foolishly wasted all his money living wildly and carelessly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After his money completely ran out, a terrible famine struck that country, and the young man became so poor and hungry that he had to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, and he was so hungry himself that he even longed to eat the pigs' food. At last he came to his senses, realised how foolish he had been, and decided to return home, planning to tell his father he was truly sorry and ask to be treated like a hired servant instead of a son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping for his return, saw him coming and ran out to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of joy. The father excitedly called for his best robe, a ring, and sandals, and ordered a great feast to celebrate, joyfully saying, \"My son was lost, and now he is found!\" The father's older son felt jealous and angry when he heard about the celebration, but his father gently reminded him that celebrating was right, because his brother who was lost had now finally come home safely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable story about a father who had two sons, one older and one younger. The younger son grew restless and boldly asked his father to give him his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had actually died, and surprisingly, the kind father agreed and divided his property between his two sons. Soon after receiving his share, the younger son packed up all his belongings and set off on a long journey to a distant, faraway country, where he foolishly wasted every bit of his money living wildly, carelessly, and without any wisdom at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After all his money had completely run out, a severe famine struck that whole faraway country, and the desperate young man became so poor and hungry that he was forced to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, and he became so hungry himself that he even longed to eat the very food meant for the pigs. At last, sitting alone in that pig pen, he came to his senses and realised how foolish he had been, so he decided to return home to his father, planning honestly to admit that he had sinned and to humbly ask to be treated like one of his father's hired servants rather than as a son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off down the road, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping for his return, saw him coming from a distance, and filled with compassion, ran out to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of joy. Before the son could even finish his rehearsed apology, the father excitedly called for his best robe, a ring for his finger, and sandals for his feet, and ordered his servants to prepare a great feast to celebrate, joyfully declaring, \"My son was lost, and now he is found!\" Meanwhile, the father's older son, who had stayed home and worked hard all along, felt jealous and angry when he heard about the celebration, but his father gently reminded him that celebrating was absolutely right, because his younger brother, who had been lost, had now finally come home safely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable and deeply moving story about a father who had two sons, one older and one considerably younger. The younger son grew restless and impatient with life at home, and one day boldly asked his father to give him his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had actually died, which was a shocking and disrespectful request in those days. Surprisingly, the kind and generous father agreed anyway and divided his property fairly between his two sons. Soon after receiving his share of the inheritance, the younger son gathered together all his belongings and set off eagerly on a long journey to a distant, faraway country, where he foolishly wasted absolutely everything he owned living wildly, carelessly, and without any wisdom or self-control at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After every last coin of his money had completely run out, a severe famine struck that whole faraway country, and the desperate young man became so poor and hungry that he was forced to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, which was considered a deeply shameful task for any Jewish son. He became so hungry himself, in fact, that he even longed to fill his stomach with the very food meant for the pigs, yet nobody there gave him anything at all. At last, sitting alone in that muddy pig pen, utterly humbled, he came to his senses and honestly realised just how foolish and selfish he had been, so he decided to return home to his father, carefully planning what he would say, rehearsing his apology, and preparing to humbly ask to be treated like one of his father's hired servants rather than as a son, since he no longer felt worthy of that title.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off down the dusty road, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping faithfully for his return every single day, saw him coming from a great distance, and filled instantly with compassion, ran out eagerly to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of overwhelming joy. Before the son could even finish stumbling through his carefully rehearsed apology, the father excitedly called for his very best robe, a valuable ring for his finger, and new sandals for his feet, and ordered his servants at once to kill the fattened calf and prepare a great feast to celebrate, joyfully declaring to everyone, \"My son was dead and is alive again; he was lost, and now he is found!\" Meanwhile, the father's older son, who had stayed faithfully at home and worked hard all along without complaint, felt jealous, hurt, and angry when he heard about the celebration and refused at first to go inside, but his father came out and gently reminded him that celebrating was absolutely right and proper, because his younger brother, who had been lost, had now finally come home safely, and that was surely something worth celebrating together as a family.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: The Prodigal Son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the father</li><li>the younger son</li><li>the inheritance</li><li>a faraway country</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The younger son asks for his inheritance early</li><li>The father kindly gives it to him</li><li>The son travels to a faraway country</li><li>He wastes his money living wildly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>inheritance</li><li>kindly gave it</li><li>faraway country</li><li>wasted all his money</li><li>wildly and carelessly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the younger son asked for his ___ and travelled to a ___, where he wasted his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable story about a father who had two sons, one older and one younger. The younger son grew restless and boldly asked his father for his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had died, and surprisingly, the kind father agreed and gave it to him. Soon after, the younger son packed up his things and travelled to a faraway country, where he foolishly wasted all his money living wildly and carelessly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable story about a father who had two sons, one older and one younger. The younger son grew restless and boldly asked his father to give him his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had actually died, and surprisingly, the kind father agreed and divided his property between his two sons. Soon after receiving his share, the younger son packed up all his belongings and set off on a long journey to a distant, faraway country, where he foolishly wasted every bit of his money living wildly, carelessly, and without any wisdom at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told a memorable and deeply moving story about a father who had two sons, one older and one considerably younger. The younger son grew restless and impatient with life at home, and one day boldly asked his father to give him his share of the family inheritance right away, long before his father had actually died, which was a shocking and disrespectful request in those days. Surprisingly, the kind and generous father agreed anyway and divided his property fairly between his two sons. Soon after receiving his share of the inheritance, the younger son gathered together all his belongings and set off eagerly on a long journey to a distant, faraway country, where he foolishly wasted absolutely everything he owned living wildly, carelessly, and without any wisdom or self-control at all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: The Prodigal Son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the famine</li><li>the pigs</li><li>the son</li><li>his senses</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A terrible famine strikes the land</li><li>The son takes a job feeding pigs</li><li>He becomes so hungry he wants to eat pig food</li><li>He comes to his senses and decides to return home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>famine</li><li>feeding pigs</li><li>so hungry</li><li>came to his senses</li><li>return home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a ___ struck the land, so the son took a job feeding ___, and decided to return ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After his money completely ran out, a terrible famine struck that country, and the young man became so poor and hungry that he had to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, and he was so hungry himself that he even longed to eat the pigs' food. At last he came to his senses, realised how foolish he had been, and decided to return home, planning to tell his father he was truly sorry and ask to be treated like a hired servant instead of a son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After all his money had completely run out, a severe famine struck that whole faraway country, and the desperate young man became so poor and hungry that he was forced to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, and he became so hungry himself that he even longed to eat the very food meant for the pigs. At last, sitting alone in that pig pen, he came to his senses and realised how foolish he had been, so he decided to return home to his father, planning honestly to admit that he had sinned and to humbly ask to be treated like one of his father's hired servants rather than as a son.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After every last coin of his money had completely run out, a severe famine struck that whole faraway country, and the desperate young man became so poor and hungry that he was forced to take a humiliating job feeding pigs for a local farmer, which was considered a deeply shameful task for any Jewish son. He became so hungry himself, in fact, that he even longed to fill his stomach with the very food meant for the pigs, yet nobody there gave him anything at all. At last, sitting alone in that muddy pig pen, utterly humbled, he came to his senses and honestly realised just how foolish and selfish he had been, so he decided to return home to his father, carefully planning what he would say, rehearsing his apology, and preparing to humbly ask to be treated like one of his father's hired servants rather than as a son, since he no longer felt worthy of that title.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: The Prodigal Son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the father</li><li>the son</li><li>the feast</li><li>the older son</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The father sees his son coming and runs to meet him</li><li>He hugs and kisses his son with joy</li><li>He orders a great feast to celebrate</li><li>The older son feels jealous, but the father says to celebrate</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ran to meet him</li><li>kissing him with joy</li><li>great feast</li><li>lost, and now he is found</li><li>jealous and angry</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the father ran to meet his ___ and ordered a great ___, though the older son felt ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping for his return, saw him coming and ran out to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of joy. The father excitedly called for his best robe, a ring, and sandals, and ordered a great feast to celebrate, joyfully saying, \"My son was lost, and now he is found!\" The father's older son felt jealous and angry when he heard about the celebration, but his father gently reminded him that celebrating was right, because his brother who was lost had now finally come home safely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off down the road, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping for his return, saw him coming from a distance, and filled with compassion, ran out to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of joy. Before the son could even finish his rehearsed apology, the father excitedly called for his best robe, a ring for his finger, and sandals for his feet, and ordered his servants to prepare a great feast to celebrate, joyfully declaring, \"My son was lost, and now he is found!\" Meanwhile, the father's older son, who had stayed home and worked hard all along, felt jealous and angry when he heard about the celebration, but his father gently reminded him that celebrating was absolutely right, because his younger brother, who had been lost, had now finally come home safely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>While the son was still a long way off down the dusty road, his father, who had clearly been watching and hoping faithfully for his return every single day, saw him coming from a great distance, and filled instantly with compassion, ran out eagerly to meet him, throwing his arms around him and kissing him warmly with tears of overwhelming joy. Before the son could even finish stumbling through his carefully rehearsed apology, the father excitedly called for his very best robe, a valuable ring for his finger, and new sandals for his feet, and ordered his servants at once to kill the fattened calf and prepare a great feast to celebrate, joyfully declaring to everyone, \"My son was dead and is alive again; he was lost, and now he is found!\" Meanwhile, the father's older son, who had stayed faithfully at home and worked hard all along without complaint, felt jealous, hurt, and angry when he heard about the celebration and refused at first to go inside, but his father came out and gently reminded him that celebrating was absolutely right and proper, because his younger brother, who had been lost, had now finally come home safely, and that was surely something worth celebrating together as a family.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: The Prodigal Son</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 29 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 29 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 30 (The Wise & Foolish Builders) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the younger son asked for his ___ and travelled to a ___, where he wasted his ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a ___ struck the land, so the son took a job feeding ___, and decided to return ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the father ran to meet his ___ and ordered a great ___, though the older son felt ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 30: The Wise & Foolish Builders":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 30’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 30: The Wise & Foolish Builders) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the wise builder and the foolish builder as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the wise builder</li><li>the foolish builder</li><li>the rock</li><li>the sand</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A wise man builds his house on solid rock</li><li>A foolish man builds his house on soft sand</li><li>The rain, floods, and winds beat against both houses</li><li>The house on the rock stands firm, but the house on the sand falls</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The wise man built his house on solid rock</li><li>The foolish man dug deep to build his house on solid rock too</li><li>When the storm came, the house on the sand collapsed completely</li><li>Jesus said obeying his words is like building a house on rock</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the end of one of his important teachings, Jesus told the gathered crowd this memorable story about two very different builders. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy house, and rather than taking any shortcuts, he chose to dig down deep until he reached solid rock, and laid the whole foundation of his house firmly on that rock. It took hard work, patience, and careful planning, but he knew deep down it was worth doing the job properly from the start.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around the same time, but instead of digging down to solid rock, he simply built his house quickly on soft, loose sand, without bothering to check what lay underneath it first. From the outside, his finished house actually looked just as good as the wise man's house, and because he skipped the difficult digging work, he finished building it much faster, so he foolishly believed he had made the smarter choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before long, the rain came pouring down heavily, the rivers rose and flooded, and strong winds blew fiercely, all beating hard against both houses at once. The house built on the solid rock stood completely firm through the whole storm and did not fall, because it had such a strong foundation. But the house built on the soft sand collapsed completely under the force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed. Jesus then explained that anyone who hears his words and actually obeys them is like that wise builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but does not obey them is like that foolish builder.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the very end of one of his important teachings, Jesus told the gathered crowd this memorable story about two very different builders, to help them understand what it truly means to listen to his words. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy house, and rather than taking any shortcuts, he chose to dig down deep through the soil until he reached solid rock, and laid the entire foundation of his house firmly upon that rock. It took a great deal of hard work, patience, and careful planning to build this way, but the wise man knew deep down that it was worth doing the job properly from the very beginning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around the same time, but instead of taking the time to dig down to solid rock, he simply built his house quickly directly on top of soft, loose sand, without bothering to check what lay underneath it first. From the outside, his finished house actually looked just as impressive and just as good as the wise man's house, and because he had skipped the difficult digging work, he finished building it much more quickly, so he foolishly believed he had made the smarter and more efficient choice all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before very long, the rain began pouring down heavily, the nearby rivers rose and flooded their banks, and strong winds blew fiercely, all beating relentlessly against both of the two houses at once. The house that had been built on the solid rock stood completely firm through the whole storm and did not fall down at all, because it had been built upon such a strong and secure foundation from the very start. But the house that had been built on the soft sand collapsed completely under the force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed, destroying everything inside it. Jesus then explained clearly to everyone listening that anyone who hears his words and actually obeys them is like that wise builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but does not obey them is exactly like that foolish builder.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the very end of one of his most important teachings, delivered to a large crowd gathered on a hillside, Jesus told the people this memorable and vivid story about two very different builders, in order to help everyone understand clearly what it truly means to listen carefully to his words and actually put them into practice. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy, lasting house, and rather than taking any risky shortcuts to save time or effort, he patiently chose to dig down deep through the loose soil until he finally reached solid, unmovable rock underneath, and laid the entire foundation of his house firmly and securely upon that rock. It took a great deal of hard work, patience, and careful planning to build his house this way, but the wise man understood deep down that it was truly worth doing the job properly and thoroughly right from the very beginning, no matter how long it took.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around that same time, but instead of taking the time and effort to dig all the way down to solid rock as the wise man had done, he simply built his house quickly directly on top of soft, loose, shifting sand near the riverbank, without ever bothering to check carefully what lay hidden underneath it first. From the outside, his newly finished house actually looked just as impressive, sturdy, and good as the wise man's house standing nearby, and because he had conveniently skipped all of the difficult digging work, he finished building his house much more quickly and with far less effort, so he foolishly convinced himself that he had made the smarter, more efficient choice all along, without realising his mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before very long, the season of storms arrived: the rain began pouring down heavily day and night, the nearby rivers rose higher and higher until they overflowed their banks completely, and strong winds blew fiercely and relentlessly, all beating hard against both of the two houses at exactly the same time. The house that had been carefully built on the solid rock stood completely firm and steady all through the fierce storm and did not fall down even a little, because it had been built upon such a strong, deep, and secure foundation from the very beginning. But the house that had been carelessly built on the soft, shifting sand collapsed completely and suddenly under the tremendous force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed, destroying absolutely everything inside it in one terrible moment. Jesus then turned to the crowd and explained clearly and directly that anyone who truly hears his words and faithfully obeys them, putting them into practice in their own life, is exactly like that wise and careful builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but never actually obeys them is exactly like that foolish and careless builder, whose house could not stand.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the wise man</li><li>the rock</li><li>the foundation</li><li>hard work</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus tells a story about two builders</li><li>A wise man decides to build his house</li><li>He digs deep and builds on solid rock</li><li>It takes hard work and careful planning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>two builders</li><li>wise man</li><li>solid rock</li><li>hard work</li><li>careful planning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus told a story about two ___, and a wise man dug deep to build his house on solid ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the end of one of his important teachings, Jesus told the gathered crowd this memorable story about two very different builders. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy house, and rather than taking any shortcuts, he chose to dig down deep until he reached solid rock, and laid the whole foundation of his house firmly on that rock. It took hard work, patience, and careful planning, but he knew deep down it was worth doing the job properly from the start.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the very end of one of his important teachings, Jesus told the gathered crowd this memorable story about two very different builders, to help them understand what it truly means to listen to his words. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy house, and rather than taking any shortcuts, he chose to dig down deep through the soil until he reached solid rock, and laid the entire foundation of his house firmly upon that rock. It took a great deal of hard work, patience, and careful planning to build this way, but the wise man knew deep down that it was worth doing the job properly from the very beginning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Near the very end of one of his most important teachings, delivered to a large crowd gathered on a hillside, Jesus told the people this memorable and vivid story about two very different builders, in order to help everyone understand clearly what it truly means to listen carefully to his words and actually put them into practice. A wise man decided to build himself a sturdy, lasting house, and rather than taking any risky shortcuts to save time or effort, he patiently chose to dig down deep through the loose soil until he finally reached solid, unmovable rock underneath, and laid the entire foundation of his house firmly and securely upon that rock. It took a great deal of hard work, patience, and careful planning to build his house this way, but the wise man understood deep down that it was truly worth doing the job properly and thoroughly right from the very beginning, no matter how long it took.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the foolish man</li><li>the sand</li><li>the house</li><li>quickly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A foolish man also builds a house</li><li>He builds it quickly on soft sand</li><li>He does not dig down to solid ground</li><li>His house looks just as good on the outside</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>foolish man</li><li>soft sand</li><li>without bothering</li><li>looked just as good</li><li>finished much faster</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a ___ man built his house quickly on soft ___, without digging down to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around the same time, but instead of digging down to solid rock, he simply built his house quickly on soft, loose sand, without bothering to check what lay underneath it first. From the outside, his finished house actually looked just as good as the wise man's house, and because he skipped the difficult digging work, he finished building it much faster, so he foolishly believed he had made the smarter choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around the same time, but instead of taking the time to dig down to solid rock, he simply built his house quickly directly on top of soft, loose sand, without bothering to check what lay underneath it first. From the outside, his finished house actually looked just as impressive and just as good as the wise man's house, and because he had skipped the difficult digging work, he finished building it much more quickly, so he foolishly believed he had made the smarter and more efficient choice all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A foolish man also decided to build himself a house around that same time, but instead of taking the time and effort to dig all the way down to solid rock as the wise man had done, he simply built his house quickly directly on top of soft, loose, shifting sand near the riverbank, without ever bothering to check carefully what lay hidden underneath it first. From the outside, his newly finished house actually looked just as impressive, sturdy, and good as the wise man's house standing nearby, and because he had conveniently skipped all of the difficult digging work, he finished building his house much more quickly and with far less effort, so he foolishly convinced himself that he had made the smarter, more efficient choice all along, without realising his mistake.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the storm</li><li>the rock</li><li>the sand</li><li>Jesus's words</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rain, floods, and winds beat against both houses</li><li>The house on the rock stands firm</li><li>The house on the sand falls with a great crash</li><li>Jesus explains that obeying his words is like building on rock</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>beating against</li><li>stood firm</li><li>collapsed completely</li><li>very great</li><li>hears his words and obeys</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the storm hit both houses, but the house on the ___ stood firm while the house on the ___ fell, just like ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before long, the rain came pouring down heavily, the rivers rose and flooded, and strong winds blew fiercely, all beating hard against both houses at once. The house built on the solid rock stood completely firm through the whole storm and did not fall, because it had such a strong foundation. But the house built on the soft sand collapsed completely under the force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed. Jesus then explained that anyone who hears his words and actually obeys them is like that wise builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but does not obey them is like that foolish builder.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before very long, the rain began pouring down heavily, the nearby rivers rose and flooded their banks, and strong winds blew fiercely, all beating relentlessly against both of the two houses at once. The house that had been built on the solid rock stood completely firm through the whole storm and did not fall down at all, because it had been built upon such a strong and secure foundation from the very start. But the house that had been built on the soft sand collapsed completely under the force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed, destroying everything inside it. Jesus then explained clearly to everyone listening that anyone who hears his words and actually obeys them is like that wise builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but does not obey them is exactly like that foolish builder.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, before very long, the season of storms arrived: the rain began pouring down heavily day and night, the nearby rivers rose higher and higher until they overflowed their banks completely, and strong winds blew fiercely and relentlessly, all beating hard against both of the two houses at exactly the same time. The house that had been carefully built on the solid rock stood completely firm and steady all through the fierce storm and did not fall down even a little, because it had been built upon such a strong, deep, and secure foundation from the very beginning. But the house that had been carelessly built on the soft, shifting sand collapsed completely and suddenly under the tremendous force of the storm, and its fall was very great indeed, destroying absolutely everything inside it in one terrible moment. Jesus then turned to the crowd and explained clearly and directly that anyone who truly hears his words and faithfully obeys them, putting them into practice in their own life, is exactly like that wise and careful builder, while anyone who merely hears his words but never actually obeys them is exactly like that foolish and careless builder, whose house could not stand.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Wise &amp; Foolish Builders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 30 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 30 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 31 (Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus told a story about two ___, and a wise man dug deep to build his house on solid ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a ___ man built his house quickly on soft ___, without digging down to ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the storm hit both houses, but the house on the ___ stood firm while the house on the ___ fell, just like ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 7: Elite Samurai (Brown belt) — Stories 31–35":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 31’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and the boy as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the boy</li><li>the loaves</li><li>the baskets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A huge crowd follows Jesus to a hillside to hear him teach</li><li>A boy offers his five loaves and two fish</li><li>Jesus gives thanks and breaks the bread and fish to share</li><li>Everyone eats until full and twelve baskets of food are left over</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>More than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet hillside</li><li>A young boy shared his lunch of five loaves and two fish</li><li>Jesus gave thanks to God before breaking the bread and fish to share</li><li>Twelve baskets of leftover food were gathered after everyone had eaten</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet hillside because they wanted to hear him teach and see him heal the sick. Jesus welcomed them kindly and taught them all day long, and as evening came near, his disciples grew worried because there was no food nearby and the people were hungry after such a long day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A young boy in the crowd offered to share his own small lunch, which was just five small loaves of bread and two little fish, but the disciples wondered how such a tiny amount could ever feed so many thousands of hungry people. Jesus told everyone to sit down together in neat groups on the grass, then he took the boy's loaves and fish, looked up to heaven, and thanked God for the food.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus broke the bread and fish into pieces and gave them to his disciples to hand out, and somehow the food never ran out no matter how much they gave away. Everyone in the enormous crowd ate until they were completely full, and afterward the disciples gathered up twelve whole baskets of leftover pieces, showing everyone just how generous and powerful Jesus truly was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet hillside near the Sea of Galilee because they wanted to hear him teach wonderful things about God and watch him heal people who were sick. Jesus welcomed the crowd warmly and felt compassion for them, so he spent the whole day teaching and healing, and as evening began to fall, his disciples came to him and grew worried because they were far from any town and the people had nothing at all to eat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The disciples suggested sending the crowd away to buy their own food, but Jesus told them instead to give the people something to eat themselves, which seemed completely impossible with so many mouths to feed. A young boy in the crowd bravely offered to share his own small lunch, which was just five small barley loaves and two little fish, and although the disciples wondered how such a tiny amount could ever feed so many thousands of hungry people, Jesus told everyone to sit down together in organised groups on the green grass.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then took the boy's five loaves and two fish, looked up to heaven, and gave thanks to God for the food before breaking it into pieces and handing it to his disciples to distribute among the crowd. Remarkably, the food never seemed to run out no matter how many pieces the disciples handed out, and everyone in the enormous crowd ate until they were completely satisfied and full. Afterward, the disciples carefully gathered up the leftover pieces and filled twelve whole baskets, showing everyone in the crowd just how generous, powerful, and caring Jesus truly was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet, grassy hillside near the Sea of Galilee because they desperately wanted to hear him teach wonderful things about God's kingdom and watch him heal the many sick people who had come along with them. Jesus welcomed the enormous crowd warmly and felt deep compassion for them, seeing that they were like sheep without a shepherd, so he spent the entire day teaching them patiently and healing everyone who needed it. As evening began to fall and the sun grew low in the sky, his disciples came to him looking rather worried, pointing out that they were far away from any town or village and the people had absolutely nothing at all to eat for their evening meal.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The disciples suggested that Jesus should send the enormous crowd away so that people could walk to nearby villages and buy their own food before it grew dark, but Jesus surprised them by saying that they themselves should give the crowd something to eat, which seemed like a completely impossible task with so many thousands of hungry mouths to feed. Philip quickly calculated that it would take almost a year's wages just to buy enough bread for everyone to have even a small bite. Just then, a generous young boy in the crowd offered to share his own small packed lunch, which was only five small barley loaves and two little fish, and although the disciples wondered aloud how such a tiny amount could possibly feed so many thousands of hungry people, Jesus calmly told everyone to sit down together in neat, organised groups on the soft green grass.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then took the boy's five loaves and two fish gently in his hands, looked up to heaven, and gave thanks to God for the food before breaking it into pieces and handing the pieces to his disciples to distribute among the seated crowd. Remarkably and miraculously, the food never seemed to run out no matter how many pieces the disciples kept handing out to the long rows of hungry people, and everyone in the enormous crowd ate as much as they wanted until they were completely full and satisfied. Afterward, Jesus told his disciples to carefully gather up every leftover piece so that nothing would be wasted, and they filled twelve whole baskets full of bread and fish that remained. This astonishing miracle showed the huge crowd, and shows us even today, just how generous, powerful, and deeply caring Jesus truly is toward everyone who comes to him, even when a problem looks utterly impossible to solve.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the crowd</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the hillside</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A huge crowd follows Jesus onto a quiet hillside</li><li>Jesus teaches and heals the people all day</li><li>Evening comes and the people have no food</li><li>The disciples grow worried about feeding everyone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>huge crowd</li><li>quiet hillside</li><li>taught them all day</li><li>evening came near</li><li>grew worried</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, a huge ___ followed Jesus onto a quiet ___, and by evening the people had no ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet hillside because they wanted to hear him teach and see him heal the sick. Jesus welcomed them kindly and taught them all day long, and as evening came near, his disciples grew worried because there was no food nearby and the people were hungry after such a long day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet hillside near the Sea of Galilee because they wanted to hear him teach wonderful things about God and watch him heal people who were sick. Jesus welcomed the crowd warmly and felt compassion for them, so he spent the whole day teaching and healing, and as evening began to fall, his disciples came to him and grew worried because they were far from any town and the people had nothing at all to eat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a huge crowd of more than five thousand people followed Jesus out to a quiet, grassy hillside near the Sea of Galilee because they desperately wanted to hear him teach wonderful things about God's kingdom and watch him heal the many sick people who had come along with them. Jesus welcomed the enormous crowd warmly and felt deep compassion for them, seeing that they were like sheep without a shepherd, so he spent the entire day teaching them patiently and healing everyone who needed it. As evening began to fall and the sun grew low in the sky, his disciples came to him looking rather worried, pointing out that they were far away from any town or village and the people had absolutely nothing at all to eat for their evening meal.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the boy</li><li>the loaves</li><li>the fish</li><li>Jesus</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A boy offers his five loaves and two fish</li><li>The disciples wonder how it could feed so many</li><li>Jesus tells everyone to sit down in groups</li><li>Jesus gives thanks to God for the food</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>small lunch</li><li>five loaves</li><li>two fish</li><li>sit down</li><li>gave thanks</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a boy offered his five ___ and two ___, so Jesus told everyone to sit down and gave ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A young boy in the crowd offered to share his own small lunch, which was just five small loaves of bread and two little fish, but the disciples wondered how such a tiny amount could ever feed so many thousands of hungry people. Jesus told everyone to sit down together in neat groups on the grass, then he took the boy's loaves and fish, looked up to heaven, and thanked God for the food.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The disciples suggested sending the crowd away to buy their own food, but Jesus told them instead to give the people something to eat themselves, which seemed completely impossible with so many mouths to feed. A young boy in the crowd bravely offered to share his own small lunch, which was just five small barley loaves and two little fish, and although the disciples wondered how such a tiny amount could ever feed so many thousands of hungry people, Jesus told everyone to sit down together in organised groups on the green grass.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The disciples suggested that Jesus should send the enormous crowd away so that people could walk to nearby villages and buy their own food before it grew dark, but Jesus surprised them by saying that they themselves should give the crowd something to eat, which seemed like a completely impossible task with so many thousands of hungry mouths to feed. Philip quickly calculated that it would take almost a year's wages just to buy enough bread for everyone to have even a small bite. Just then, a generous young boy in the crowd offered to share his own small packed lunch, which was only five small barley loaves and two little fish, and although the disciples wondered aloud how such a tiny amount could possibly feed so many thousands of hungry people, Jesus calmly told everyone to sit down together in neat, organised groups on the soft green grass.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the bread</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the crowd</li><li>the baskets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus breaks the bread and fish into pieces</li><li>The disciples hand the food out to everyone</li><li>The food never runs out and everyone eats until full</li><li>Twelve baskets of leftovers are gathered up</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>broke it into pieces</li><li>handed it out</li><li>never ran out</li><li>ate until full</li><li>twelve baskets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jesus broke the ___ and it never ran out, so everyone ate until full and ___ baskets of ___ were left over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus broke the bread and fish into pieces and gave them to his disciples to hand out, and somehow the food never ran out no matter how much they gave away. Everyone in the enormous crowd ate until they were completely full, and afterward the disciples gathered up twelve whole baskets of leftover pieces, showing everyone just how generous and powerful Jesus truly was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then took the boy's five loaves and two fish, looked up to heaven, and gave thanks to God for the food before breaking it into pieces and handing it to his disciples to distribute among the crowd. Remarkably, the food never seemed to run out no matter how many pieces the disciples handed out, and everyone in the enormous crowd ate until they were completely satisfied and full. Afterward, the disciples carefully gathered up the leftover pieces and filled twelve whole baskets, showing everyone in the crowd just how generous, powerful, and caring Jesus truly was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then took the boy's five loaves and two fish gently in his hands, looked up to heaven, and gave thanks to God for the food before breaking it into pieces and handing the pieces to his disciples to distribute among the seated crowd. Remarkably and miraculously, the food never seemed to run out no matter how many pieces the disciples kept handing out to the long rows of hungry people, and everyone in the enormous crowd ate as much as they wanted until they were completely full and satisfied. Afterward, Jesus told his disciples to carefully gather up every leftover piece so that nothing would be wasted, and they filled twelve whole baskets full of bread and fish that remained. This astonishing miracle showed the huge crowd, and shows us even today, just how generous, powerful, and deeply caring Jesus truly is toward everyone who comes to him, even when a problem looks utterly impossible to solve.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Jesus Feeds the Five Thousand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 31 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 31 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 32 (Jesus Calms the Storm) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, a huge ___ followed Jesus onto a quiet ___, and by evening the people had no ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a boy offered his five ___ and two ___, so Jesus told everyone to sit down and gave ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jesus broke the ___ and it never ran out, so everyone ate until full and ___ baskets of ___ were left over.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 32’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and the disciples as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the boat</li><li>the storm</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus and the disciples sail out and Jesus falls asleep in the boat</li><li>A fierce storm blows up and fills the boat with water</li><li>The frightened disciples wake Jesus up</li><li>Jesus tells the storm to be still and it becomes calm</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus fell asleep in the back of the boat during the crossing</li><li>A fierce storm blew up with strong winds and huge waves</li><li>The disciples woke Jesus up because they were afraid of drowning</li><li>Jesus told the wind and waves to be still and the sea became calm</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, Jesus and his disciples climbed into a small fishing boat to cross over to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Jesus was very tired from a long day of teaching, so he lay down at the back of the boat, rested his head on a cushion, and quickly fell fast asleep as the boat sailed gently across the calm water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, a fierce storm blew up out of nowhere, with strong winds howling and huge waves crashing over the sides of the little boat, filling it up with water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen, were absolutely terrified that their boat was about to sink, so they rushed to wake Jesus up, crying out that they were all going to drown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus stood up calmly in the middle of the storm and spoke firmly to the wind and the waves, saying, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly, the wind stopped blowing and the sea became perfectly calm and smooth again. Jesus gently asked his disciples why they had been so afraid and whether they still had little faith, and the amazed disciples wondered together what kind of man this was, that even the wind and the waves obeyed him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, after a long and busy day of teaching large crowds beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus told his disciples that they should cross over to the other side of the lake in their small fishing boat. Jesus was very tired, so he settled down at the back of the boat, rested his head on a cushion, and quickly fell fast asleep as the boat sailed gently out across the calm evening water, leaving the crowds behind on the shore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, without any warning at all, a fierce storm blew up out of nowhere on the lake, with powerful winds howling and enormous waves crashing hard over the sides of the small boat, filling it up dangerously with water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen who knew this lake well, were absolutely terrified that their little boat was about to be swamped and sink beneath them, so they rushed anxiously to the back of the boat to wake Jesus up, crying out desperately that they were all about to drown in the storm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus woke up calmly, stood up in the middle of the wild, tossing boat, and spoke firmly and directly to the raging wind and the crashing waves, commanding them, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly and completely, the wind stopped blowing and the sea became perfectly calm and smooth once again, as though the storm had never even happened. Jesus then turned to his disciples and gently asked them why they had been so afraid, wondering aloud whether they still had so little faith in him, and the disciples, filled with awe and wonder, asked each other what kind of man this could possibly be, that even the wind and the waves themselves obeyed his every word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, after a long and busy day of teaching enormous crowds of people beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus told his disciples that they should climb into their small fishing boat and cross over together to the quieter side of the lake. Jesus was completely worn out from the day's teaching, so he settled down comfortably at the back of the boat, rested his head upon a cushion, and quickly fell into a deep, peaceful sleep as the boat sailed gently out across the calm evening water, leaving the noisy crowds far behind them on the shore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, without any warning at all, a fierce and violent storm blew up out of nowhere on the open lake, with powerful winds howling loudly and enormous waves crashing hard over the sides of the small wooden boat, filling it up dangerously with cold water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen who had known this unpredictable lake their whole lives, were absolutely terrified that their little boat was about to be swamped completely and sink beneath the churning waves, so they scrambled anxiously to the back of the boat to wake Jesus up, crying out desperately over the roaring wind that they were all about to drown right there in the storm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus woke up calmly despite the chaos all around him, stood up steadily in the middle of the wildly rocking, tossing boat, and spoke firmly and directly to the raging wind and the crashing waves, commanding them simply, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly and completely, exactly as he spoke, the fierce wind stopped blowing altogether and the churning sea became perfectly calm and glassy smooth once again, almost as though the terrifying storm had never even happened at all. Jesus then turned to his shaken disciples and gently asked them why they had been so very afraid, wondering aloud whether they still had so little faith in him even after everything they had already seen him do. The disciples, filled with a mixture of relief, awe, and wonder, turned and asked each other quietly what kind of man this Jesus could possibly be, that even the wind and the waves themselves would listen to him and obey his every word so completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the boat</li><li>the cushion</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus and his disciples set sail across the lake</li><li>Jesus is very tired from teaching all day</li><li>He lies down at the back of the boat</li><li>Jesus falls fast asleep as they sail</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>set sail</li><li>very tired</li><li>back of the boat</li><li>cushion</li><li>fast asleep</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus and his ___ set sail across the lake, and Jesus, feeling very ___, fell fast ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, Jesus and his disciples climbed into a small fishing boat to cross over to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Jesus was very tired from a long day of teaching, so he lay down at the back of the boat, rested his head on a cushion, and quickly fell fast asleep as the boat sailed gently across the calm water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, after a long and busy day of teaching large crowds beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus told his disciples that they should cross over to the other side of the lake in their small fishing boat. Jesus was very tired, so he settled down at the back of the boat, rested his head on a cushion, and quickly fell fast asleep as the boat sailed gently out across the calm evening water, leaving the crowds behind on the shore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, after a long and busy day of teaching enormous crowds of people beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus told his disciples that they should climb into their small fishing boat and cross over together to the quieter side of the lake. Jesus was completely worn out from the day's teaching, so he settled down comfortably at the back of the boat, rested his head upon a cushion, and quickly fell into a deep, peaceful sleep as the boat sailed gently out across the calm evening water, leaving the noisy crowds far behind them on the shore.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the storm</li><li>the wind</li><li>the waves</li><li>the boat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A fierce storm blows up suddenly</li><li>Strong winds howl and waves crash over the boat</li><li>The boat begins to fill up with water</li><li>The disciples become terrified they will sink</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>fierce storm</li><li>howling winds</li><li>crashing waves</li><li>filling with water</li><li>terrified</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a fierce ___ blew up, with strong winds and crashing ___, filling the ___ with water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, a fierce storm blew up out of nowhere, with strong winds howling and huge waves crashing over the sides of the little boat, filling it up with water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen, were absolutely terrified that their boat was about to sink, so they rushed to wake Jesus up, crying out that they were all going to drown.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, without any warning at all, a fierce storm blew up out of nowhere on the lake, with powerful winds howling and enormous waves crashing hard over the sides of the small boat, filling it up dangerously with water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen who knew this lake well, were absolutely terrified that their little boat was about to be swamped and sink beneath them, so they rushed anxiously to the back of the boat to wake Jesus up, crying out desperately that they were all about to drown in the storm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Suddenly, without any warning at all, a fierce and violent storm blew up out of nowhere on the open lake, with powerful winds howling loudly and enormous waves crashing hard over the sides of the small wooden boat, filling it up dangerously with cold water. The disciples, several of whom were experienced fishermen who had known this unpredictable lake their whole lives, were absolutely terrified that their little boat was about to be swamped completely and sink beneath the churning waves, so they scrambled anxiously to the back of the boat to wake Jesus up, crying out desperately over the roaring wind that they were all about to drown right there in the storm.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the wind</li><li>the sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The disciples wake Jesus up, afraid of drowning</li><li>Jesus stands up and speaks to the storm</li><li>The wind stops and the sea becomes calm</li><li>The disciples wonder what kind of man Jesus is</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>woke him up</li><li>spoke to the storm</li><li>be still</li><li>perfectly calm</li><li>wondered</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the disciples woke ___ up, and he told the storm to be ___, so the wind stopped and the ___ became calm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus stood up calmly in the middle of the storm and spoke firmly to the wind and the waves, saying, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly, the wind stopped blowing and the sea became perfectly calm and smooth again. Jesus gently asked his disciples why they had been so afraid and whether they still had little faith, and the amazed disciples wondered together what kind of man this was, that even the wind and the waves obeyed him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus woke up calmly, stood up in the middle of the wild, tossing boat, and spoke firmly and directly to the raging wind and the crashing waves, commanding them, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly and completely, the wind stopped blowing and the sea became perfectly calm and smooth once again, as though the storm had never even happened. Jesus then turned to his disciples and gently asked them why they had been so afraid, wondering aloud whether they still had so little faith in him, and the disciples, filled with awe and wonder, asked each other what kind of man this could possibly be, that even the wind and the waves themselves obeyed his every word.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus woke up calmly despite the chaos all around him, stood up steadily in the middle of the wildly rocking, tossing boat, and spoke firmly and directly to the raging wind and the crashing waves, commanding them simply, 'Quiet! Be still!' Instantly and completely, exactly as he spoke, the fierce wind stopped blowing altogether and the churning sea became perfectly calm and glassy smooth once again, almost as though the terrifying storm had never even happened at all. Jesus then turned to his shaken disciples and gently asked them why they had been so very afraid, wondering aloud whether they still had so little faith in him even after everything they had already seen him do. The disciples, filled with a mixture of relief, awe, and wonder, turned and asked each other quietly what kind of man this Jesus could possibly be, that even the wind and the waves themselves would listen to him and obey his every word so completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Jesus Calms the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 32 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 32 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 33 (Jesus Walks on Water) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus and his ___ set sail across the lake, and Jesus, feeling very ___, fell fast ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a fierce ___ blew up, with strong winds and crashing ___, filling the ___ with water.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the disciples woke ___ up, and he told the storm to be ___, so the wind stopped and the ___ became calm.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 33’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and Peter as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Peter</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the boat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus sends the disciples ahead in the boat and goes to pray alone</li><li>A strong wind tosses the boat during the night</li><li>The disciples see Jesus walking on the water and are afraid</li><li>Peter walks toward Jesus, begins to sink, and Jesus saves him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus stayed behind alone on the hillside to pray</li><li>The disciples' boat was tossed by strong waves during the night</li><li>The disciples thought Jesus was a ghost walking on the water</li><li>Peter began to sink and Jesus reached out and caught him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand people, Jesus told his disciples to get into their boat and sail on ahead of him across the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind on the hillside by himself to spend some quiet time praying to God. Later that night, the disciples were out on the water, and their boat was being tossed about by strong waves because a wind was blowing hard against them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, while it was still dark, the disciples suddenly saw someone walking toward them across the top of the water. They were absolutely terrified and cried out in fear, thinking that they were seeing a ghost, but Jesus called out to them straight away, telling them not to be afraid because it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter bravely called out to Jesus, asking if he could come to him by walking on the water too, and Jesus told him to come. Peter climbed out of the boat and actually walked on the water toward Jesus, but when he noticed the strong wind, he grew afraid and began to sink, crying out for Jesus to save him. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught Peter, and once they climbed into the boat together, the wind died down completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand hungry people, Jesus told his disciples to climb into their boat and sail on ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind alone on the quiet hillside to spend some precious time praying to his Father in heaven. Later that night, the disciples were still out on the open water, quite far from land, and their small boat was being tossed roughly about by strong waves because a fierce wind was blowing hard directly against them, making it difficult for them to row.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, sometime between three and six o'clock, while it was still completely dark, the disciples suddenly noticed someone walking toward them across the very top of the choppy water. They were absolutely terrified by this strange and frightening sight and cried out loudly in fear, convinced that they were seeing a ghost, but Jesus called out to them straight away in a reassuring voice, telling them to take courage and not be afraid, because it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter, always eager and impulsive, bravely called out to Jesus across the water, asking if he could come to him by walking on the water too, and Jesus simply told him, 'Come.' Peter climbed out of the rocking boat and, remarkably, actually began walking on top of the water toward Jesus, but when he took his eyes off Jesus and noticed how strong the wind was blowing around him, he grew afraid and started sinking down into the waves, crying out desperately for Jesus to save him. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught hold of Peter, gently asking him why he had doubted, and once the two of them climbed back into the boat together, the strong wind died down completely and the other disciples worshipped Jesus, declaring that he truly was the Son of God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand hungry people on the hillside, Jesus told his disciples to climb straight into their boat and sail on ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind completely alone on the quiet hillside to spend some precious time praying to his Father in heaven, something he liked to do whenever he could find a quiet moment away from the busy crowds. Later that night, the disciples were still out on the open water, quite far from land by this point, and their small wooden boat was being tossed roughly about by strong, choppy waves, because a fierce wind had picked up and was blowing hard directly against them, making it very difficult for the tired disciples to row any further.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, sometime between three and six o'clock, while it was still completely dark outside and the disciples had been battling the wind for hours, they suddenly noticed a strange figure walking steadily toward them across the very top of the choppy, moonlit water. They were absolutely terrified by this bewildering and frightening sight and cried out loudly in fear, utterly convinced that they must be seeing a ghost out on the lake, but Jesus called out to them straight away in a calm, reassuring voice, telling them to take courage and not be afraid at all, because it was really him and not a ghost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter, who was always eager, bold, and a little impulsive, bravely called out across the water to Jesus, asking if he too could come to him by walking on the water, and Jesus simply replied, 'Come.' Peter climbed out of the rocking, unsteady boat and, remarkably and miraculously, actually began walking on top of the water toward Jesus, one careful step after another. However, when Peter took his eyes off Jesus for a moment and instead noticed how strong and frightening the wind was blowing all around him, he grew suddenly afraid and started sinking down into the dark waves, crying out desperately for Jesus to save him at once. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught hold of Peter before he could sink any further, gently asking him why he had doubted, and once the two of them climbed back safely into the boat together, the strong wind died down completely and the sea grew calm. The other disciples, having witnessed this whole extraordinary event, worshipped Jesus right there in the boat, declaring joyfully that he truly was the Son of God.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the boat</li><li>the hillside</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus sends his disciples ahead in the boat</li><li>Jesus stays behind alone to pray</li><li>Night falls while the disciples are on the water</li><li>A strong wind tosses their boat about</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>sent them ahead</li><li>stayed behind</li><li>praying alone</li><li>tossed about</li><li>strong wind</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus sent his ___ ahead in the boat while he stayed behind to ___, and a strong ___ tossed their boat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand people, Jesus told his disciples to get into their boat and sail on ahead of him across the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind on the hillside by himself to spend some quiet time praying to God. Later that night, the disciples were out on the water, and their boat was being tossed about by strong waves because a wind was blowing hard against them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand hungry people, Jesus told his disciples to climb into their boat and sail on ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind alone on the quiet hillside to spend some precious time praying to his Father in heaven. Later that night, the disciples were still out on the open water, quite far from land, and their small boat was being tossed roughly about by strong waves because a fierce wind was blowing hard directly against them, making it difficult for them to row.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After feeding the huge crowd of five thousand hungry people on the hillside, Jesus told his disciples to climb straight into their boat and sail on ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, while he stayed behind completely alone on the quiet hillside to spend some precious time praying to his Father in heaven, something he liked to do whenever he could find a quiet moment away from the busy crowds. Later that night, the disciples were still out on the open water, quite far from land by this point, and their small wooden boat was being tossed roughly about by strong, choppy waves, because a fierce wind had picked up and was blowing hard directly against them, making it very difficult for the tired disciples to row any further.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the water</li><li>the disciples</li><li>a ghost</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus walks toward the boat on top of the water</li><li>The disciples see him and are terrified</li><li>They think he must be a ghost</li><li>Jesus calls out and tells them not to be afraid</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>walking on the water</li><li>terrified</li><li>a ghost</li><li>called out</li><li>not to be afraid</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Jesus walked toward the boat on top of the ___, and the disciples, thinking he was a ___, cried out in ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, while it was still dark, the disciples suddenly saw someone walking toward them across the top of the water. They were absolutely terrified and cried out in fear, thinking that they were seeing a ghost, but Jesus called out to them straight away, telling them not to be afraid because it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, sometime between three and six o'clock, while it was still completely dark, the disciples suddenly noticed someone walking toward them across the very top of the choppy water. They were absolutely terrified by this strange and frightening sight and cried out loudly in fear, convinced that they were seeing a ghost, but Jesus called out to them straight away in a reassuring voice, telling them to take courage and not be afraid, because it was really him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early in the morning, sometime between three and six o'clock, while it was still completely dark outside and the disciples had been battling the wind for hours, they suddenly noticed a strange figure walking steadily toward them across the very top of the choppy, moonlit water. They were absolutely terrified by this bewildering and frightening sight and cried out loudly in fear, utterly convinced that they must be seeing a ghost out on the lake, but Jesus called out to them straight away in a calm, reassuring voice, telling them to take courage and not be afraid at all, because it was really him and not a ghost.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter</li><li>the water</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the wind</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter asks Jesus if he can walk on the water too</li><li>Peter climbs out and begins walking toward Jesus</li><li>Peter grows afraid of the wind and begins to sink</li><li>Jesus catches Peter's hand and the wind dies down</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>walk on the water</li><li>climbed out</li><li>began to sink</li><li>caught his hand</li><li>died down</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Peter climbed out and walked toward ___, but he grew afraid and began to ___, so Jesus caught his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter bravely called out to Jesus, asking if he could come to him by walking on the water too, and Jesus told him to come. Peter climbed out of the boat and actually walked on the water toward Jesus, but when he noticed the strong wind, he grew afraid and began to sink, crying out for Jesus to save him. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught Peter, and once they climbed into the boat together, the wind died down completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter, always eager and impulsive, bravely called out to Jesus across the water, asking if he could come to him by walking on the water too, and Jesus simply told him, 'Come.' Peter climbed out of the rocking boat and, remarkably, actually began walking on top of the water toward Jesus, but when he took his eyes off Jesus and noticed how strong the wind was blowing around him, he grew afraid and started sinking down into the waves, crying out desperately for Jesus to save him. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught hold of Peter, gently asking him why he had doubted, and once the two of them climbed back into the boat together, the strong wind died down completely and the other disciples worshipped Jesus, declaring that he truly was the Son of God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter, who was always eager, bold, and a little impulsive, bravely called out across the water to Jesus, asking if he too could come to him by walking on the water, and Jesus simply replied, 'Come.' Peter climbed out of the rocking, unsteady boat and, remarkably and miraculously, actually began walking on top of the water toward Jesus, one careful step after another. However, when Peter took his eyes off Jesus for a moment and instead noticed how strong and frightening the wind was blowing all around him, he grew suddenly afraid and started sinking down into the dark waves, crying out desperately for Jesus to save him at once. Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught hold of Peter before he could sink any further, gently asking him why he had doubted, and once the two of them climbed back safely into the boat together, the strong wind died down completely and the sea grew calm. The other disciples, having witnessed this whole extraordinary event, worshipped Jesus right there in the boat, declaring joyfully that he truly was the Son of God.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Jesus Walks on Water</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 33 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 33 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 34 (Zacchaeus the Tax Collector) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus sent his ___ ahead in the boat while he stayed behind to ___, and a strong ___ tossed their boat.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Jesus walked toward the boat on top of the ___, and the disciples, thinking he was a ___, cried out in ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Peter climbed out and walked toward ___, but he grew afraid and began to ___, so Jesus caught his ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 34’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Zacchaeus and Jesus as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the tree</li><li>the crowd</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus, a short tax collector, wants to see Jesus in Jericho</li><li>Zacchaeus climbs a sycamore-fig tree to see over the crowd</li><li>Jesus looks up, calls him by name, and invites himself to his house</li><li>Zacchaeus promises to give to the poor and repay those he cheated</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus was a wealthy chief tax collector in Jericho</li><li>Zacchaeus climbed a sycamore-fig tree because he was too short to see</li><li>Jesus called Zacchaeus by name and asked to stay at his house</li><li>Zacchaeus promised to give half his belongings to the poor and repay those he had cheated</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who was a wealthy chief tax collector, though many people in the town disliked him because he had become rich by collecting more money than he should have from ordinary people. One day, when Zacchaeus heard that Jesus was coming to Jericho, he was very eager to see him, but because Zacchaeus was quite short, he could not see over the tall crowd of people gathered along the street.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So Zacchaeus ran on ahead of the crowd and climbed up into a sycamore-fig tree so that he could get a good view of Jesus as he passed by underneath. When Jesus reached that spot, he looked up into the tree, saw Zacchaeus sitting there, and called him by name, telling him to come down quickly because Jesus wanted to stay at his house that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus climbed down at once, filled with joy, and happily welcomed Jesus into his home, even though some people in the crowd grumbled that Jesus was going to visit the house of such a well-known sinner. Zacchaeus stood up and promised Jesus that he would give half of everything he owned to poor people and pay back four times as much to anyone he had ever cheated. Jesus told him that salvation had come to his house that day, showing everyone how Jesus loved and welcomed people that others had given up on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the busy town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who was a very wealthy chief tax collector, though most people in the town strongly disliked him because he had grown rich by collecting far more money than he should have from ordinary, hard-working people. One day, when word spread through Jericho that Jesus was passing through the town, Zacchaeus was extremely eager to catch a glimpse of him, but because Zacchaeus was quite a short man, he simply could not see over the tall, tightly packed crowd of people that had gathered along the busy street.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Determined not to miss his chance, Zacchaeus ran on ahead of the noisy crowd and climbed up into a sycamore-fig tree so that he would have a clear view of Jesus as he walked past underneath. When Jesus finally reached that very spot in the road, he stopped, looked up into the branches of the tree, and saw Zacchaeus perched there watching, and to everyone's surprise, Jesus called him by name and told him to come down quickly, because Jesus wanted to stay as a guest at his house that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus scrambled down out of the tree at once, absolutely overjoyed, and happily welcomed Jesus into his home, even though many people in the watching crowd began muttering and grumbling that Jesus had chosen to visit the house of such a well-known and dishonest sinner. Standing before Jesus and everyone gathered there, Zacchaeus made a bold promise that he would give away half of everything he owned to help poor people, and that he would pay back four times as much money to anyone he had ever cheated through his unfair tax collecting. Jesus responded warmly, declaring that salvation had come to this house that very day, and this remarkable story shows everyone even today how Jesus loves, seeks out, and welcomes people that the rest of society had already given up on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the busy, bustling town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who held the important position of chief tax collector and had become extremely wealthy through his work, though most ordinary people in the town strongly disliked and resented him, believing that he had grown rich dishonestly by collecting far more money than he should have from hard-working families. One day, exciting word spread quickly through the streets of Jericho that Jesus was passing through the town on his travels, and Zacchaeus was extremely eager and curious to catch even a brief glimpse of this famous teacher, but because Zacchaeus happened to be quite a short man, he simply could not see anything over the tall, tightly packed crowd of people that had gathered eagerly along the busy street to watch Jesus go by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Determined not to miss his one chance to see Jesus, Zacchaeus hurried on ahead of the noisy, jostling crowd and cleverly climbed up into the branches of a sycamore-fig tree so that he would have a perfectly clear view of Jesus as he walked past underneath along the road. When Jesus finally reached that very spot beneath the tree, he stopped walking, looked directly up into the branches, and saw Zacchaeus perched there watching intently, and to the great surprise of absolutely everyone present, Jesus called him by name and told him to come down quickly from the tree, because Jesus said he wanted to stay as an honoured guest at his house that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus scrambled down out of the tree as fast as he possibly could, absolutely overjoyed and thrilled beyond words, and happily welcomed Jesus warmly into his own home, even though many people in the watching crowd began muttering unhappily and grumbling loudly that Jesus had deliberately chosen to visit the house of such a well-known and dishonest sinner instead of someone more respectable. Standing bravely before Jesus and the whole gathered crowd, Zacchaeus made a bold and generous promise that he would immediately give away half of everything he owned to help poor and needy people, and that he would pay back four times as much money to any person he had ever cheated through his unfair tax collecting practices in the past. Jesus responded warmly and joyfully, declaring publicly that salvation had come to this very house that day, since Zacchaeus too was a son of Abraham, and this remarkable, heart-warming story shows everyone, even today, how Jesus deliberately loves, seeks out, and joyfully welcomes people that the rest of society had already judged and given up on completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the crowd</li><li>Jericho</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus, a wealthy tax collector, lives in Jericho</li><li>Jesus is passing through the town</li><li>Zacchaeus wants to see him but is too short</li><li>The tall crowd blocks his view</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tax collector</li><li>passing through</li><li>too short</li><li>blocks his view</li><li>eager to see</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Zacchaeus, a wealthy tax ___, wanted to see Jesus, but he was too ___ to see over the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who was a wealthy chief tax collector, though many people in the town disliked him because he had become rich by collecting more money than he should have from ordinary people. One day, when Zacchaeus heard that Jesus was coming to Jericho, he was very eager to see him, but because Zacchaeus was quite short, he could not see over the tall crowd of people gathered along the street.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the busy town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who was a very wealthy chief tax collector, though most people in the town strongly disliked him because he had grown rich by collecting far more money than he should have from ordinary, hard-working people. One day, when word spread through Jericho that Jesus was passing through the town, Zacchaeus was extremely eager to catch a glimpse of him, but because Zacchaeus was quite a short man, he simply could not see over the tall, tightly packed crowd of people that had gathered along the busy street.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In the busy, bustling town of Jericho lived a man named Zacchaeus, who held the important position of chief tax collector and had become extremely wealthy through his work, though most ordinary people in the town strongly disliked and resented him, believing that he had grown rich dishonestly by collecting far more money than he should have from hard-working families. One day, exciting word spread quickly through the streets of Jericho that Jesus was passing through the town on his travels, and Zacchaeus was extremely eager and curious to catch even a brief glimpse of this famous teacher, but because Zacchaeus happened to be quite a short man, he simply could not see anything over the tall, tightly packed crowd of people that had gathered eagerly along the busy street to watch Jesus go by.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus</li><li>the tree</li><li>Jesus</li><li>his name</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus runs ahead and climbs a sycamore-fig tree</li><li>Jesus reaches that spot in the road</li><li>Jesus looks up and sees Zacchaeus</li><li>Jesus calls him by name and tells him to come down</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>climbed a tree</li><li>sycamore-fig</li><li>looked up</li><li>called his name</li><li>come down quickly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Zacchaeus climbed a sycamore ___, and Jesus looked up, called him by ___, and told him to come ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So Zacchaeus ran on ahead of the crowd and climbed up into a sycamore-fig tree so that he could get a good view of Jesus as he passed by underneath. When Jesus reached that spot, he looked up into the tree, saw Zacchaeus sitting there, and called him by name, telling him to come down quickly because Jesus wanted to stay at his house that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Determined not to miss his chance, Zacchaeus ran on ahead of the noisy crowd and climbed up into a sycamore-fig tree so that he would have a clear view of Jesus as he walked past underneath. When Jesus finally reached that very spot in the road, he stopped, looked up into the branches of the tree, and saw Zacchaeus perched there watching, and to everyone's surprise, Jesus called him by name and told him to come down quickly, because Jesus wanted to stay as a guest at his house that very day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Determined not to miss his one chance to see Jesus, Zacchaeus hurried on ahead of the noisy, jostling crowd and cleverly climbed up into the branches of a sycamore-fig tree so that he would have a perfectly clear view of Jesus as he walked past underneath along the road. When Jesus finally reached that very spot beneath the tree, he stopped walking, looked directly up into the branches, and saw Zacchaeus perched there watching intently, and to the great surprise of absolutely everyone present, Jesus called him by name and told him to come down quickly from the tree, because Jesus said he wanted to stay as an honoured guest at his house that very day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the poor</li><li>the crowd</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Zacchaeus climbs down and welcomes Jesus with joy</li><li>Some in the crowd grumble that Jesus visits a sinner</li><li>Zacchaeus promises to give half his belongings to the poor</li><li>Jesus says salvation has come to his house</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>climbed down</li><li>welcomed him with joy</li><li>grumbled</li><li>give to the poor</li><li>salvation</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Zacchaeus welcomed Jesus with ___ and promised to give half his belongings to the ___, and Jesus said ___ had come to his house.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus climbed down at once, filled with joy, and happily welcomed Jesus into his home, even though some people in the crowd grumbled that Jesus was going to visit the house of such a well-known sinner. Zacchaeus stood up and promised Jesus that he would give half of everything he owned to poor people and pay back four times as much to anyone he had ever cheated. Jesus told him that salvation had come to his house that day, showing everyone how Jesus loved and welcomed people that others had given up on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus scrambled down out of the tree at once, absolutely overjoyed, and happily welcomed Jesus into his home, even though many people in the watching crowd began muttering and grumbling that Jesus had chosen to visit the house of such a well-known and dishonest sinner. Standing before Jesus and everyone gathered there, Zacchaeus made a bold promise that he would give away half of everything he owned to help poor people, and that he would pay back four times as much money to anyone he had ever cheated through his unfair tax collecting. Jesus responded warmly, declaring that salvation had come to this house that very day, and this remarkable story shows everyone even today how Jesus loves, seeks out, and welcomes people that the rest of society had already given up on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Zacchaeus scrambled down out of the tree as fast as he possibly could, absolutely overjoyed and thrilled beyond words, and happily welcomed Jesus warmly into his own home, even though many people in the watching crowd began muttering unhappily and grumbling loudly that Jesus had deliberately chosen to visit the house of such a well-known and dishonest sinner instead of someone more respectable. Standing bravely before Jesus and the whole gathered crowd, Zacchaeus made a bold and generous promise that he would immediately give away half of everything he owned to help poor and needy people, and that he would pay back four times as much money to any person he had ever cheated through his unfair tax collecting practices in the past. Jesus responded warmly and joyfully, declaring publicly that salvation had come to this very house that day, since Zacchaeus too was a son of Abraham, and this remarkable, heart-warming story shows everyone, even today, how Jesus deliberately loves, seeks out, and joyfully welcomes people that the rest of society had already judged and given up on completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Zacchaeus the Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 34 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 34 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 35 (Jesus Raises Lazarus) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Zacchaeus, a wealthy tax ___, wanted to see Jesus, but he was too ___ to see over the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Zacchaeus climbed a sycamore ___, and Jesus looked up, called him by ___, and told him to come ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Zacchaeus welcomed Jesus with ___ and promised to give half his belongings to the ___, and Jesus said ___ had come to his house.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 35’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and Lazarus as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Lazarus</li><li>Mary and Martha</li><li>the tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lazarus becomes sick and his sisters send word to Jesus</li><li>Jesus arrives after Lazarus has already died and been in the tomb four days</li><li>Jesus weeps with Mary and Martha and asks for the stone to be rolled away</li><li>Jesus calls Lazarus, who walks out of the tomb alive</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mary and Martha sent word to Jesus that their brother Lazarus was sick</li><li>By the time Jesus arrived, Lazarus had been in the tomb for four days</li><li>Jesus wept alongside Mary and Martha because he cared for his friends</li><li>Jesus called out and Lazarus walked out of the tomb alive</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close friends named Mary and Martha, who lived with their brother Lazarus in a village called Bethany. One day, Lazarus became very sick, so his sisters sent an urgent message to Jesus, asking him to come quickly and heal their brother, because they knew that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and had the power to help him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>By the time Jesus finally arrived in Bethany, Lazarus had already died and been placed in a tomb for four days. Both Martha and Mary were very upset and told Jesus that if only he had come sooner, their brother would not have died. Jesus, deeply moved and full of compassion for his grieving friends, wept alongside them, showing everyone just how much he cared about the people he loved.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus asked for the stone covering the tomb to be rolled away, then he prayed to God and called out in a loud voice, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the astonishment of everyone watching, Lazarus walked out of the tomb, still wrapped in his grave clothes, completely alive and well again. This wonderful and joyful miracle showed everyone that Jesus truly had power over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many others with hope and belief in Jesus.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close and dear friends named Mary and Martha, who lived together with their brother Lazarus in a small village called Bethany, not far from Jerusalem. One day, Lazarus became extremely sick, so his worried sisters quickly sent an urgent message all the way to Jesus, asking him to come as soon as possible and heal their beloved brother, because they knew very well that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and had the power to help him recover.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than rushing straight there, Jesus waited a couple more days before finally setting off for Bethany, and by the time he arrived, Lazarus had already died and been resting in a tomb for four whole days. Both Martha and Mary were deeply upset and grieving, and each of them told Jesus, one after the other, that if only he had come sooner, their dear brother would surely not have died. Jesus, deeply moved in his spirit and filled with compassion for his grieving friends, wept openly alongside them at the tomb, showing everyone watching just how much he genuinely cared about the people he loved.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then asked for the heavy stone covering the entrance of the tomb to be rolled away, even though Martha gently warned him that there would already be a bad smell after four days. Jesus prayed aloud to God, thanking him for always hearing him, and then called out in a loud, commanding voice, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the utter astonishment of everyone gathered there watching, Lazarus walked calmly out of the tomb, still completely wrapped in his linen grave clothes from head to foot, yet fully alive and well once again. This wonderful and deeply joyful miracle showed everyone present that Jesus truly held power even over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many other witnesses with fresh hope and strong belief in who Jesus really was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close and much-loved friends named Mary and Martha, who lived together with their brother Lazarus in a small village called Bethany, not very far from the city of Jerusalem, and Jesus often enjoyed visiting their home whenever he was travelling through that area. One day, Lazarus became extremely and seriously sick, so his worried sisters quickly sent an urgent message all the way to Jesus, wherever he happened to be at the time, asking him to come as soon as possible and heal their beloved brother, because they knew very well that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and certainly had the power to help him recover from his illness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather surprisingly, instead of rushing straight there when he heard the news, Jesus chose to wait a couple more days before finally setting off on the journey to Bethany, and by the time he and his disciples actually arrived, Lazarus had already died and had been resting quietly inside a tomb for four whole days already. Both Martha and, later, Mary were deeply upset and grieving over the loss of their brother, and each of the sisters, one after the other, gently told Jesus that if only he had arrived sooner, their dear brother would surely not have died at all. Jesus, deeply moved in his spirit and filled with genuine compassion for his two grieving friends, wept openly right there alongside them at the tomb, showing everyone who was watching just how much he truly and personally cared about the people he loved so dearly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then asked for the heavy stone covering the entrance of the tomb to be rolled away completely, even though practical Martha gently warned him that there would surely already be an unpleasant smell after four long days. Jesus looked up and prayed aloud to God, thanking him for always hearing his prayers, and then called out in a loud, commanding voice for everyone to hear, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the utter astonishment and amazement of everyone gathered there watching that day, Lazarus walked calmly and steadily out of the dark tomb, still completely wrapped from head to foot in his white linen grave clothes, yet now fully alive and perfectly well once again. This wonderful and deeply joyful miracle showed everyone present, beyond any doubt, that Jesus truly held power even over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many other amazed witnesses that day with fresh hope, great joy, and strong, lasting belief in exactly who Jesus really was.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lazarus</li><li>Mary and Martha</li><li>Jesus</li><li>Bethany</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lazarus lives in Bethany with his sisters Mary and Martha</li><li>Lazarus becomes very sick</li><li>His sisters send an urgent message to Jesus</li><li>They ask Jesus to come and heal their brother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bethany</li><li>very sick</li><li>urgent message</li><li>come quickly</li><li>heal their brother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Lazarus, who lived in Bethany with his sisters ___, became very ___, so they sent word to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close friends named Mary and Martha, who lived with their brother Lazarus in a village called Bethany. One day, Lazarus became very sick, so his sisters sent an urgent message to Jesus, asking him to come quickly and heal their brother, because they knew that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and had the power to help him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close and dear friends named Mary and Martha, who lived together with their brother Lazarus in a small village called Bethany, not far from Jerusalem. One day, Lazarus became extremely sick, so his worried sisters quickly sent an urgent message all the way to Jesus, asking him to come as soon as possible and heal their beloved brother, because they knew very well that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and had the power to help him recover.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus had two close and much-loved friends named Mary and Martha, who lived together with their brother Lazarus in a small village called Bethany, not very far from the city of Jerusalem, and Jesus often enjoyed visiting their home whenever he was travelling through that area. One day, Lazarus became extremely and seriously sick, so his worried sisters quickly sent an urgent message all the way to Jesus, wherever he happened to be at the time, asking him to come as soon as possible and heal their beloved brother, because they knew very well that Jesus loved Lazarus dearly and certainly had the power to help him recover from his illness.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Lazarus</li><li>the tomb</li><li>Mary and Martha</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus arrives after Lazarus has already died</li><li>Lazarus has been in the tomb for four days</li><li>Martha and Mary tell Jesus he could have saved him</li><li>Jesus weeps alongside his grieving friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>already died</li><li>four days</li><li>could have saved him</li><li>wept</li><li>grieving friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Jesus arrived after Lazarus had already died and been in the ___ for four ___, and Jesus ___ with his friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>By the time Jesus finally arrived in Bethany, Lazarus had already died and been placed in a tomb for four days. Both Martha and Mary were very upset and told Jesus that if only he had come sooner, their brother would not have died. Jesus, deeply moved and full of compassion for his grieving friends, wept alongside them, showing everyone just how much he cared about the people he loved.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than rushing straight there, Jesus waited a couple more days before finally setting off for Bethany, and by the time he arrived, Lazarus had already died and been resting in a tomb for four whole days. Both Martha and Mary were deeply upset and grieving, and each of them told Jesus, one after the other, that if only he had come sooner, their dear brother would surely not have died. Jesus, deeply moved in his spirit and filled with compassion for his grieving friends, wept openly alongside them at the tomb, showing everyone watching just how much he genuinely cared about the people he loved.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather surprisingly, instead of rushing straight there when he heard the news, Jesus chose to wait a couple more days before finally setting off on the journey to Bethany, and by the time he and his disciples actually arrived, Lazarus had already died and had been resting quietly inside a tomb for four whole days already. Both Martha and, later, Mary were deeply upset and grieving over the loss of their brother, and each of the sisters, one after the other, gently told Jesus that if only he had arrived sooner, their dear brother would surely not have died at all. Jesus, deeply moved in his spirit and filled with genuine compassion for his two grieving friends, wept openly right there alongside them at the tomb, showing everyone who was watching just how much he truly and personally cared about the people he loved so dearly.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the stone</li><li>Lazarus</li><li>the tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus asks for the stone to be rolled away</li><li>Jesus prays and calls out in a loud voice</li><li>Lazarus walks out of the tomb alive</li><li>Everyone is amazed and filled with hope</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rolled away</li><li>loud voice</li><li>come out</li><li>walked out alive</li><li>filled with hope</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jesus asked for the ___ to be rolled away and called out, 'Lazarus, come ___!', and Lazarus walked out ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus asked for the stone covering the tomb to be rolled away, then he prayed to God and called out in a loud voice, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the astonishment of everyone watching, Lazarus walked out of the tomb, still wrapped in his grave clothes, completely alive and well again. This wonderful and joyful miracle showed everyone that Jesus truly had power over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many others with hope and belief in Jesus.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then asked for the heavy stone covering the entrance of the tomb to be rolled away, even though Martha gently warned him that there would already be a bad smell after four days. Jesus prayed aloud to God, thanking him for always hearing him, and then called out in a loud, commanding voice, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the utter astonishment of everyone gathered there watching, Lazarus walked calmly out of the tomb, still completely wrapped in his linen grave clothes from head to foot, yet fully alive and well once again. This wonderful and deeply joyful miracle showed everyone present that Jesus truly held power even over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many other witnesses with fresh hope and strong belief in who Jesus really was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus then asked for the heavy stone covering the entrance of the tomb to be rolled away completely, even though practical Martha gently warned him that there would surely already be an unpleasant smell after four long days. Jesus looked up and prayed aloud to God, thanking him for always hearing his prayers, and then called out in a loud, commanding voice for everyone to hear, 'Lazarus, come out!' To the utter astonishment and amazement of everyone gathered there watching that day, Lazarus walked calmly and steadily out of the dark tomb, still completely wrapped from head to foot in his white linen grave clothes, yet now fully alive and perfectly well once again. This wonderful and deeply joyful miracle showed everyone present, beyond any doubt, that Jesus truly held power even over death itself, and it filled Mary, Martha, and many other amazed witnesses that day with fresh hope, great joy, and strong, lasting belief in exactly who Jesus really was.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Jesus Raises Lazarus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 35 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 35 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 36 (The Last Supper) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Lazarus, who lived in Bethany with his sisters ___, became very ___, so they sent word to ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Jesus arrived after Lazarus had already died and been in the ___ for four ___, and Jesus ___ with his friends.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jesus asked for the ___ to be rolled away and called out, 'Lazarus, come ___!', and Lazarus walked out ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 8: Master Samurai (Black belt) — Stories 36–40":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 36: The Last Supper":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: The Last Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 36’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 36: The Last Supper) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and the disciples as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the bread</li><li>the cup</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus and his disciples gather for the Passover meal in Jerusalem</li><li>Jesus washes his disciples' feet to show humble love</li><li>Jesus shares the bread and cup, saying they represent his body and blood</li><li>Jesus tells them one will betray him, and they go to the Mount of Olives</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus washed his disciples' feet before the Passover meal</li><li>Jesus shared bread and told the disciples it represented his body</li><li>Jesus shared a cup of wine and told them it represented his blood</li><li>Jesus told his disciples one of them would betray him that night</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special festival of Passover, Jesus asked his twelve disciples to prepare a room in Jerusalem so they could all share a special meal together. That evening, Jesus and his disciples gathered around the table, and before they began eating, Jesus surprised everyone by getting up, wrapping a towel around his waist, and washing each of his disciples' dusty feet, showing them how important it was to serve one another with humility and love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal, Jesus took a loaf of bread, thanked God for it, broke it into pieces, and gave it to his disciples, telling them that it represented his body, which he was about to give for them. He then took a cup of wine, thanked God again, and gave it to them, telling them that it represented his blood, which would be poured out for the forgiveness of sins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told his disciples to remember him whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future. Sadly, Jesus also told them that one of them sitting right there at the table would betray him that very night, which made the disciples feel confused and sorrowful. After the meal, they sang a hymn together and went out to the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special Jewish festival of Passover, Jesus asked two of his twelve disciples to go ahead into Jerusalem and prepare a large upstairs room where they could all share a special meal together that evening. When evening came, Jesus and his twelve disciples gathered around the table, and before the meal even began, Jesus surprised everyone present by quietly getting up from the table, wrapping a towel around his waist like a humble servant, and washing each of his disciples' tired, dusty feet one by one, showing them all through this simple act just how important it was to serve one another with genuine humility and love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal itself, Jesus took a loaf of ordinary bread in his hands, gave thanks to God for it, broke it into pieces, and gave it around to his disciples, telling them solemnly that this bread represented his own body, which he was about to give up for them very soon. He then took a cup filled with wine, gave thanks to God once again, and passed it around to them, telling them that this wine represented his own blood, which would soon be poured out for the forgiveness of everyone's sins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently told his disciples to remember him like this whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future, as a way of remembering everything he had done for them. Sadly, Jesus also told his disciples that one of them sitting right there at the table with him that very night would soon betray him, which understandably left the disciples feeling deeply confused, troubled, and sorrowful, each of them wondering privately if it might somehow be themselves. After they had finished the meal together, they sang a hymn quietly and then went out together to a quiet garden on the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested by soldiers that same night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special Jewish festival of Passover, an important yearly celebration remembering how God had rescued his people long ago, Jesus asked two of his twelve disciples to go on ahead into the city of Jerusalem and prepare a large upstairs room where they could all share a special evening meal together. When evening finally came, Jesus and his twelve disciples gathered together closely around the low table, and before the meal even began, Jesus quietly surprised everyone present by getting up from his place, wrapping a plain towel around his waist just like a humble household servant would, and washing each of his disciples' tired, dusty feet one by one with his own hands, showing them all through this simple, humble act just how important it truly was to serve one another with real humility and genuine love, even Peter, who at first tried to refuse.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal itself, Jesus took a simple loaf of unleavened bread in his hands, gave heartfelt thanks to God for it, broke it carefully into pieces, and passed it around to each of his disciples, telling them solemnly that this bread represented his own body, which he was about to give up willingly for them very soon. He then took a cup filled with wine, gave thanks to God once again, and passed it carefully around the table to them, telling them that this wine represented his own blood, poured out to seal a new covenant for the forgiveness of everyone's sins, forever changing how his followers would understand their relationship with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently told his disciples to remember him in exactly this way whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future, as a lasting way of remembering everything he had done and was about to do for them. Sadly, Jesus also told his gathered disciples that one of them sitting right there beside him at that very table would betray him before the night was even over, which understandably left the disciples feeling deeply confused, troubled, and sorrowful, each one quietly wondering to himself whether he might somehow be the one. Jesus also warned Peter, who confidently insisted he would never abandon him, that Peter himself would actually deny even knowing him three times before the rooster crowed the next morning. After they had all finished the meal together, they sang a hymn quietly in the dim lamplight and then went out together into the cool night air toward a quiet garden on the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested by soldiers that same difficult night.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: The Last Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the room</li><li>the towel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus and his disciples gather for the Passover meal</li><li>Jesus gets up from the table</li><li>He wraps a towel around his waist</li><li>Jesus washes each disciple's feet</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Passover meal</li><li>got up</li><li>wrapped a towel</li><li>washed their feet</li><li>humility and love</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus and his ___ gathered for the Passover meal, and Jesus wrapped a ___ around his waist and washed their ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special festival of Passover, Jesus asked his twelve disciples to prepare a room in Jerusalem so they could all share a special meal together. That evening, Jesus and his disciples gathered around the table, and before they began eating, Jesus surprised everyone by getting up, wrapping a towel around his waist, and washing each of his disciples' dusty feet, showing them how important it was to serve one another with humility and love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special Jewish festival of Passover, Jesus asked two of his twelve disciples to go ahead into Jerusalem and prepare a large upstairs room where they could all share a special meal together that evening. When evening came, Jesus and his twelve disciples gathered around the table, and before the meal even began, Jesus surprised everyone present by quietly getting up from the table, wrapping a towel around his waist like a humble servant, and washing each of his disciples' tired, dusty feet one by one, showing them all through this simple act just how important it was to serve one another with genuine humility and love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the special Jewish festival of Passover, an important yearly celebration remembering how God had rescued his people long ago, Jesus asked two of his twelve disciples to go on ahead into the city of Jerusalem and prepare a large upstairs room where they could all share a special evening meal together. When evening finally came, Jesus and his twelve disciples gathered together closely around the low table, and before the meal even began, Jesus quietly surprised everyone present by getting up from his place, wrapping a plain towel around his waist just like a humble household servant would, and washing each of his disciples' tired, dusty feet one by one with his own hands, showing them all through this simple, humble act just how important it truly was to serve one another with real humility and genuine love, even Peter, who at first tried to refuse.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: The Last Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the bread</li><li>the cup</li><li>his body</li><li>his blood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus takes bread, gives thanks, and breaks it</li><li>He says the bread represents his body</li><li>Jesus takes a cup of wine and gives thanks</li><li>He says the wine represents his blood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>gave thanks</li><li>broke it</li><li>represents his body</li><li>cup of wine</li><li>represents his blood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Jesus took the ___, gave thanks, and said it was his ___, then shared a cup of ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal, Jesus took a loaf of bread, thanked God for it, broke it into pieces, and gave it to his disciples, telling them that it represented his body, which he was about to give for them. He then took a cup of wine, thanked God again, and gave it to them, telling them that it represented his blood, which would be poured out for the forgiveness of sins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal itself, Jesus took a loaf of ordinary bread in his hands, gave thanks to God for it, broke it into pieces, and gave it around to his disciples, telling them solemnly that this bread represented his own body, which he was about to give up for them very soon. He then took a cup filled with wine, gave thanks to God once again, and passed it around to them, telling them that this wine represented his own blood, which would soon be poured out for the forgiveness of everyone's sins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>During the meal itself, Jesus took a simple loaf of unleavened bread in his hands, gave heartfelt thanks to God for it, broke it carefully into pieces, and passed it around to each of his disciples, telling them solemnly that this bread represented his own body, which he was about to give up willingly for them very soon. He then took a cup filled with wine, gave thanks to God once again, and passed it carefully around the table to them, telling them that this wine represented his own blood, poured out to seal a new covenant for the forgiveness of everyone's sins, forever changing how his followers would understand their relationship with God.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: The Last Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>betray</li><li>the Mount of Olives</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus tells them to remember him this way</li><li>He says one of them will betray him that night</li><li>The disciples feel confused and sorrowful</li><li>They sing a hymn and go to the Mount of Olives</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>remember him</li><li>betray him</li><li>confused and sorrowful</li><li>sang a hymn</li><li>Mount of Olives</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jesus said one of them would ___ him that night, and afterward they sang a ___ and went to the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus told his disciples to remember him whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future. Sadly, Jesus also told them that one of them sitting right there at the table would betray him that very night, which made the disciples feel confused and sorrowful. After the meal, they sang a hymn together and went out to the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently told his disciples to remember him like this whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future, as a way of remembering everything he had done for them. Sadly, Jesus also told his disciples that one of them sitting right there at the table with him that very night would soon betray him, which understandably left the disciples feeling deeply confused, troubled, and sorrowful, each of them wondering privately if it might somehow be themselves. After they had finished the meal together, they sang a hymn quietly and then went out together to a quiet garden on the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested by soldiers that same night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus gently told his disciples to remember him in exactly this way whenever they shared bread and wine together in the future, as a lasting way of remembering everything he had done and was about to do for them. Sadly, Jesus also told his gathered disciples that one of them sitting right there beside him at that very table would betray him before the night was even over, which understandably left the disciples feeling deeply confused, troubled, and sorrowful, each one quietly wondering to himself whether he might somehow be the one. Jesus also warned Peter, who confidently insisted he would never abandon him, that Peter himself would actually deny even knowing him three times before the rooster crowed the next morning. After they had all finished the meal together, they sang a hymn quietly in the dim lamplight and then went out together into the cool night air toward a quiet garden on the Mount of Olives, where Jesus would soon be arrested by soldiers that same difficult night.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: The Last Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 36 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 36 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 37 (The Crucifixion) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus and his ___ gathered for the Passover meal, and Jesus wrapped a ___ around his waist and washed their ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Jesus took the ___, gave thanks, and said it was his ___, then shared a cup of ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jesus said one of them would ___ him that night, and afterward they sang a ___ and went to the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 37: The Crucifixion":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: The Crucifixion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 37’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 37: The Crucifixion) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and Pilate as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Pilate</li><li>the cross</li><li>the Temple curtain</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus is arrested and brought before Pilate, who finds no fault in him</li><li>The crowd calls for Jesus to be crucified and Pilate gives in</li><li>Jesus is placed on the cross and forgives those around him</li><li>Jesus dies, the sky darkens, and the Temple curtain tears in two</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pilate could not find any real reason to punish Jesus</li><li>Jesus carried his own cross out to a hill called Golgotha</li><li>Jesus asked God to forgive the people even while he was on the cross</li><li>The Temple curtain tore in two when Jesus died, showing the way to God was open</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus was arrested in a quiet garden and taken before the leaders and the Roman governor, Pilate, who could not find any real reason to punish him. Even so, a crowd shouted for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, Pilate gave in to the crowd. Jesus was led away, carrying a heavy wooden cross, out to a hill called Golgotha, just outside the city walls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on the hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other men, and even while he was suffering, he spoke words of forgiveness, asking God to forgive the people who were treating him so unfairly because they did not understand what they were doing. Jesus loved people so much that he chose to give up his life for them, taking the punishment for the wrong things people had done so that they could be forgiven and made right with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus breathed his last breath, the sky grew strangely dark in the middle of the day, and the huge curtain in the Temple, which had always separated ordinary people from the holiest part of the building, tore in two from top to bottom. This showed everyone that because of Jesus's great love and sacrifice, the way to God was now open to all people. Jesus's friends took his body down gently and placed it in a stone tomb, feeling very sad, but the story of Jesus's love was far from over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus went to a quiet garden called Gethsemane to pray, and it was there that he was arrested and taken before the Jewish leaders and then before the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, who questioned Jesus carefully but could not find any real reason to punish him at all. Even so, a large crowd, stirred up by some of the religious leaders, shouted loudly for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, wanting to keep the peace, Pilate eventually gave in to the crowd's demands. Jesus was then led away by soldiers, carrying a heavy wooden cross on his shoulders, out through the city gates to a hill called Golgotha, which means 'the place of the skull,' just outside the walls of Jerusalem.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on that quiet hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other condemned men, and even while he was suffering greatly, he spoke words of gentle forgiveness, asking his Father in heaven to forgive the very people who were treating him so unfairly, because he said they truly did not understand what they were doing. Jesus loved every single person so deeply and completely that he willingly chose to give up his own life for them, taking upon himself the punishment for all the wrong things people had ever done, so that anyone who believed in him could be forgiven completely and made right with God forever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus finally breathed his last breath late that afternoon, the sky grew strangely and eerily dark across the whole land in the middle of the day, and at that very same moment, the huge, thick curtain inside the Temple, which had always separated ordinary worshippers from the holiest part of the sacred building, suddenly tore completely in two from top to bottom. This remarkable sign showed everyone watching that, because of Jesus's incredible love and sacrifice, the way to God himself was now finally open to absolutely everyone. Jesus's close friends took his body down very gently from the cross and placed it carefully inside a stone tomb, feeling deeply sad and heartbroken, but the wonderful story of Jesus's love and power was, thankfully, still far from over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus went out to a quiet garden called Gethsemane to pray earnestly, and it was there, later that same night, that he was arrested and taken first before the Jewish leaders and then before the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, who questioned Jesus carefully and thoroughly but honestly could not find any real reason at all to punish him. Even so, a large crowd, stirred up by some of the religious leaders who felt threatened by Jesus's teaching, shouted loudly and insistently for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, wanting above all to avoid a riot and keep the peace, Pilate eventually and reluctantly gave in to the crowd's demands. Jesus was then led away by Roman soldiers, carrying a heavy wooden cross on his shoulders, out through the busy city gates to a hill just outside the walls of Jerusalem called Golgotha, meaning 'the place of the skull.'</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on that quiet, solemn hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other condemned men, and even while he was suffering greatly for the sake of others, he spoke words of gentle, remarkable forgiveness, asking his Father in heaven to forgive the very people who were treating him so unfairly and unjustly, because he said plainly that they truly did not understand what they were doing to him. Jesus loved every single person in the world so deeply and completely that he willingly and courageously chose to give up his own precious life for them all, taking upon himself the punishment for every wrong thing people had ever done, so that anyone who believed in him could be forgiven completely and made right with God forever, restoring the broken friendship between people and their Creator.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus finally breathed his very last breath late that afternoon, the sky over the whole land grew strangely and eerily dark in the middle of the day, as though creation itself were grieving alongside him, and at that very same moment, the huge, thick curtain inside the Temple, which had always separated ordinary worshippers from the holiest part of the sacred building, suddenly and dramatically tore completely in two, from the very top all the way down to the bottom. This remarkable and powerful sign showed everyone watching that, because of Jesus's incredible love and willing sacrifice, the way to God himself was now finally and fully open to absolutely everyone, with nothing left standing in between. Jesus's close and grieving friends took his body down very gently and carefully from the cross that evening and placed it respectfully inside a stone tomb, rolling a large stone across the entrance, feeling deeply sad and heartbroken as they walked away in the fading light. Yet even in that dark and sorrowful moment, the wonderful, unfinished story of Jesus's love, courage, and power for everyone was, thankfully, still far from over.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: The Crucifixion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Pilate</li><li>the crowd</li><li>the cross</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus is arrested after the Last Supper</li><li>He is taken before the governor, Pilate</li><li>Pilate can find no real reason to punish him</li><li>The crowd shouts for Jesus to be crucified</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>arrested</li><li>the governor</li><li>no real reason</li><li>shouted</li><li>crucified</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus was arrested and taken before ___, who could find no reason to punish him, but the ___ shouted for him to be ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus was arrested in a quiet garden and taken before the leaders and the Roman governor, Pilate, who could not find any real reason to punish him. Even so, a crowd shouted for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, Pilate gave in to the crowd. Jesus was led away, carrying a heavy wooden cross, out to a hill called Golgotha, just outside the city walls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus went to a quiet garden called Gethsemane to pray, and it was there that he was arrested and taken before the Jewish leaders and then before the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, who questioned Jesus carefully but could not find any real reason to punish him at all. Even so, a large crowd, stirred up by some of the religious leaders, shouted loudly for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, wanting to keep the peace, Pilate eventually gave in to the crowd's demands. Jesus was then led away by soldiers, carrying a heavy wooden cross on his shoulders, out through the city gates to a hill called Golgotha, which means 'the place of the skull,' just outside the walls of Jerusalem.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After the Last Supper, Jesus went out to a quiet garden called Gethsemane to pray earnestly, and it was there, later that same night, that he was arrested and taken first before the Jewish leaders and then before the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, who questioned Jesus carefully and thoroughly but honestly could not find any real reason at all to punish him. Even so, a large crowd, stirred up by some of the religious leaders who felt threatened by Jesus's teaching, shouted loudly and insistently for Jesus to be crucified, and sadly, wanting above all to avoid a riot and keep the peace, Pilate eventually and reluctantly gave in to the crowd's demands. Jesus was then led away by Roman soldiers, carrying a heavy wooden cross on his shoulders, out through the busy city gates to a hill just outside the walls of Jerusalem called Golgotha, meaning 'the place of the skull.'</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: The Crucifixion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the cross</li><li>Golgotha</li><li>forgiveness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus carries his cross out to Golgotha</li><li>He is placed on the cross between two other men</li><li>Jesus asks God to forgive the people</li><li>He chooses to give his life out of love</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>carried his cross</li><li>Golgotha</li><li>asked God to forgive</li><li>out of love</li><li>give his life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Jesus carried his ___ out to a hill called ___, where he asked God to ___ the people.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on the hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other men, and even while he was suffering, he spoke words of forgiveness, asking God to forgive the people who were treating him so unfairly because they did not understand what they were doing. Jesus loved people so much that he chose to give up his life for them, taking the punishment for the wrong things people had done so that they could be forgiven and made right with God.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on that quiet hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other condemned men, and even while he was suffering greatly, he spoke words of gentle forgiveness, asking his Father in heaven to forgive the very people who were treating him so unfairly, because he said they truly did not understand what they were doing. Jesus loved every single person so deeply and completely that he willingly chose to give up his own life for them, taking upon himself the punishment for all the wrong things people had ever done, so that anyone who believed in him could be forgiven completely and made right with God forever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There on that quiet, solemn hill, Jesus was placed on the cross between two other condemned men, and even while he was suffering greatly for the sake of others, he spoke words of gentle, remarkable forgiveness, asking his Father in heaven to forgive the very people who were treating him so unfairly and unjustly, because he said plainly that they truly did not understand what they were doing to him. Jesus loved every single person in the world so deeply and completely that he willingly and courageously chose to give up his own precious life for them all, taking upon himself the punishment for every wrong thing people had ever done, so that anyone who believed in him could be forgiven completely and made right with God forever, restoring the broken friendship between people and their Creator.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: The Crucifixion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the sky</li><li>the Temple curtain</li><li>the tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus breathes his last breath</li><li>The sky grows dark in the middle of the day</li><li>The Temple curtain tears in two from top to bottom</li><li>Jesus's friends place his body in a stone tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>last breath</li><li>grew dark</li><li>tore in two</li><li>top to bottom</li><li>stone tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, as Jesus died, the ___ grew dark and the Temple ___ tore in two, and his friends placed his body in a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus breathed his last breath, the sky grew strangely dark in the middle of the day, and the huge curtain in the Temple, which had always separated ordinary people from the holiest part of the building, tore in two from top to bottom. This showed everyone that because of Jesus's great love and sacrifice, the way to God was now open to all people. Jesus's friends took his body down gently and placed it in a stone tomb, feeling very sad, but the story of Jesus's love was far from over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus finally breathed his last breath late that afternoon, the sky grew strangely and eerily dark across the whole land in the middle of the day, and at that very same moment, the huge, thick curtain inside the Temple, which had always separated ordinary worshippers from the holiest part of the sacred building, suddenly tore completely in two from top to bottom. This remarkable sign showed everyone watching that, because of Jesus's incredible love and sacrifice, the way to God himself was now finally open to absolutely everyone. Jesus's close friends took his body down very gently from the cross and placed it carefully inside a stone tomb, feeling deeply sad and heartbroken, but the wonderful story of Jesus's love and power was, thankfully, still far from over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Jesus finally breathed his very last breath late that afternoon, the sky over the whole land grew strangely and eerily dark in the middle of the day, as though creation itself were grieving alongside him, and at that very same moment, the huge, thick curtain inside the Temple, which had always separated ordinary worshippers from the holiest part of the sacred building, suddenly and dramatically tore completely in two, from the very top all the way down to the bottom. This remarkable and powerful sign showed everyone watching that, because of Jesus's incredible love and willing sacrifice, the way to God himself was now finally and fully open to absolutely everyone, with nothing left standing in between. Jesus's close and grieving friends took his body down very gently and carefully from the cross that evening and placed it respectfully inside a stone tomb, rolling a large stone across the entrance, feeling deeply sad and heartbroken as they walked away in the fading light. Yet even in that dark and sorrowful moment, the wonderful, unfinished story of Jesus's love, courage, and power for everyone was, thankfully, still far from over.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: The Crucifixion</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 37 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 37 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 38 (The Resurrection) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus was arrested and taken before ___, who could find no reason to punish him, but the ___ shouted for him to be ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Jesus carried his ___ out to a hill called ___, where he asked God to ___ the people.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, as Jesus died, the ___ grew dark and the Temple ___ tore in two, and his friends placed his body in a ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 38: The Resurrection":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Resurrection</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 38’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 38: The Resurrection) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Jesus and Mary Magdalene as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Mary Magdalene</li><li>the angels</li><li>the tomb</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The women go to the tomb early in the morning with spices</li><li>They find the stone rolled away and the tomb empty</li><li>Angels tell them Jesus has risen from the dead</li><li>The women tell the disciples and Jesus appears to them alive</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mary Magdalene and other women went to the tomb early in the morning</li><li>The women found the heavy stone already rolled away</li><li>Two angels told the women that Jesus had risen from the dead</li><li>Jesus later appeared to his disciples alive and well</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still dark outside, a few of Jesus's friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked to the tomb where Jesus's body had been placed. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices to gently prepare his body, but they wondered along the way who would roll away the heavy stone blocking the entrance to the tomb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women arrived, they were amazed to find that the heavy stone had already been rolled away, and when they looked inside, the tomb was completely empty. Suddenly, two angels dressed in shining clothes appeared and told the frightened women wonderful news, that Jesus was not there because he had risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised he would.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The women ran quickly to tell the disciples this incredible news, and although some of the disciples found it hard to believe at first, Jesus soon appeared to them himself, alive and well. Jesus's resurrection filled his followers with enormous joy and hope, showing everyone that death could not defeat him and that his promises could always be trusted.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still quite dark outside, a few of Jesus's faithful friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked together to the tomb where Jesus's body had been carefully placed only a few days earlier. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices with them in order to gently and respectfully prepare his body, but along the way they wondered aloud who would be able to roll away the extremely heavy stone that was blocking the entrance to the tomb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women finally arrived at the tomb, they were completely amazed to discover that the heavy stone had already been rolled away from the entrance, and when they bravely looked inside, they found that the tomb was entirely empty, with no body to be seen anywhere. Suddenly, two angels dressed in dazzling, shining clothes appeared before them, and although the women were understandably frightened, the angels told them the most wonderful news imaginable: that Jesus was not there any longer because he had truly risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised his followers he would.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The excited women ran as quickly as they could back to tell the disciples this incredible, joyful news, but although some of the disciples found it very hard to believe such an amazing story at first, Jesus himself soon appeared to them in person, clearly alive and completely well again. Over the following days, Jesus appeared to many more of his followers, eating with them and even inviting doubtful Thomas to touch his hands. Jesus's astonishing resurrection filled all his followers with enormous joy, hope, and courage, showing everyone clearly that death could never defeat him and that every one of his promises could always, always be trusted completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still quite dark outside and the city was silent, a few of Jesus's faithful friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked together to the tomb where Jesus's body had been carefully and hastily placed only a few days earlier before the Sabbath began. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices with them in order to gently and respectfully prepare his body properly, as was the custom, but along the dim, quiet way they wondered aloud to one another who would possibly be able to roll away the extremely heavy stone that was blocking the entrance to the tomb, since it would normally take several strong men to move it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women finally arrived at the tomb just as the sun was beginning to rise, they were completely amazed and puzzled to discover that the heavy stone had already been rolled away from the entrance, and when they bravely stepped closer and looked inside, they found that the tomb was entirely and unmistakably empty, with no body to be seen anywhere at all. Suddenly, two angels dressed in dazzling, shining white clothes appeared before them, and although the women were understandably terrified and bowed their faces to the ground, the angels gently told them the most wonderful news imaginable: that Jesus was not there any longer because he had truly and genuinely risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised his followers he would, back before all of this had even happened.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The excited, breathless women ran as quickly as their feet could carry them back into the city to tell the disciples this incredible, joyful news, but although some of the disciples found it very hard to believe such an astonishing story at first and dismissed it as nonsense, Jesus himself soon appeared to them in person, clearly alive and completely well again, showing them his hands and feet as proof. Over the following days, Jesus appeared to many more of his amazed followers in different places, walking and talking with two travellers on a road, eating a meal with his disciples, and even later inviting a doubtful Thomas to reach out and touch his hands for himself. Jesus's astonishing resurrection completely filled all of his followers with overwhelming joy, unshakeable hope, and fresh courage, showing everyone clearly, for all time, that death could never truly defeat him and that every single one of his promises could always, always be trusted completely, no matter how impossible things might sometimes seem.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Resurrection</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the women</li><li>Mary Magdalene</li><li>the tomb</li><li>spices</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The women set out for the tomb early in the morning</li><li>It is still dark outside</li><li>They bring sweet-smelling spices with them</li><li>They wonder who will roll away the heavy stone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>early in the morning</li><li>still dark</li><li>sweet-smelling spices</li><li>heavy stone</li><li>wondered</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the women, including Mary ___, set out for the tomb early with ___, wondering who would roll away the heavy ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still dark outside, a few of Jesus's friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked to the tomb where Jesus's body had been placed. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices to gently prepare his body, but they wondered along the way who would roll away the heavy stone blocking the entrance to the tomb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still quite dark outside, a few of Jesus's faithful friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked together to the tomb where Jesus's body had been carefully placed only a few days earlier. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices with them in order to gently and respectfully prepare his body, but along the way they wondered aloud who would be able to roll away the extremely heavy stone that was blocking the entrance to the tomb.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Early on the first day of the week, while it was still quite dark outside and the city was silent, a few of Jesus's faithful friends, including Mary Magdalene, walked together to the tomb where Jesus's body had been carefully and hastily placed only a few days earlier before the Sabbath began. They wanted to bring sweet-smelling spices with them in order to gently and respectfully prepare his body properly, as was the custom, but along the dim, quiet way they wondered aloud to one another who would possibly be able to roll away the extremely heavy stone that was blocking the entrance to the tomb, since it would normally take several strong men to move it.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Resurrection</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the stone</li><li>the tomb</li><li>the angels</li><li>risen</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The women arrive and find the stone rolled away</li><li>They look inside and find the tomb empty</li><li>Two angels in shining clothes appear</li><li>The angels say Jesus has risen from the dead</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rolled away</li><li>tomb empty</li><li>shining clothes</li><li>risen from the dead</li><li>amazed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the women found the ___ already rolled away and the tomb ___, and two ___ told them Jesus had risen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women arrived, they were amazed to find that the heavy stone had already been rolled away, and when they looked inside, the tomb was completely empty. Suddenly, two angels dressed in shining clothes appeared and told the frightened women wonderful news, that Jesus was not there because he had risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised he would.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women finally arrived at the tomb, they were completely amazed to discover that the heavy stone had already been rolled away from the entrance, and when they bravely looked inside, they found that the tomb was entirely empty, with no body to be seen anywhere. Suddenly, two angels dressed in dazzling, shining clothes appeared before them, and although the women were understandably frightened, the angels told them the most wonderful news imaginable: that Jesus was not there any longer because he had truly risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised his followers he would.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the women finally arrived at the tomb just as the sun was beginning to rise, they were completely amazed and puzzled to discover that the heavy stone had already been rolled away from the entrance, and when they bravely stepped closer and looked inside, they found that the tomb was entirely and unmistakably empty, with no body to be seen anywhere at all. Suddenly, two angels dressed in dazzling, shining white clothes appeared before them, and although the women were understandably terrified and bowed their faces to the ground, the angels gently told them the most wonderful news imaginable: that Jesus was not there any longer because he had truly and genuinely risen from the dead, exactly as he had promised his followers he would, back before all of this had even happened.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Resurrection</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the women</li><li>the disciples</li><li>Jesus</li><li>joy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The women run to tell the disciples</li><li>Some disciples find it hard to believe at first</li><li>Jesus appears to them alive and well</li><li>His followers are filled with joy and hope</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ran to tell</li><li>hard to believe</li><li>alive and well</li><li>filled with joy</li><li>hope</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the women ran to tell the ___, and Jesus appeared to them ___, filling everyone with ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The women ran quickly to tell the disciples this incredible news, and although some of the disciples found it hard to believe at first, Jesus soon appeared to them himself, alive and well. Jesus's resurrection filled his followers with enormous joy and hope, showing everyone that death could not defeat him and that his promises could always be trusted.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The excited women ran as quickly as they could back to tell the disciples this incredible, joyful news, but although some of the disciples found it very hard to believe such an amazing story at first, Jesus himself soon appeared to them in person, clearly alive and completely well again. Over the following days, Jesus appeared to many more of his followers, eating with them and even inviting doubtful Thomas to touch his hands. Jesus's astonishing resurrection filled all his followers with enormous joy, hope, and courage, showing everyone clearly that death could never defeat him and that every one of his promises could always, always be trusted completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The excited, breathless women ran as quickly as their feet could carry them back into the city to tell the disciples this incredible, joyful news, but although some of the disciples found it very hard to believe such an astonishing story at first and dismissed it as nonsense, Jesus himself soon appeared to them in person, clearly alive and completely well again, showing them his hands and feet as proof. Over the following days, Jesus appeared to many more of his amazed followers in different places, walking and talking with two travellers on a road, eating a meal with his disciples, and even later inviting a doubtful Thomas to reach out and touch his hands for himself. Jesus's astonishing resurrection completely filled all of his followers with overwhelming joy, unshakeable hope, and fresh courage, showing everyone clearly, for all time, that death could never truly defeat him and that every single one of his promises could always, always be trusted completely, no matter how impossible things might sometimes seem.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Resurrection</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 38 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 38 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 39 (Doubting Thomas) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the women, including Mary ___, set out for the tomb early with ___, wondering who would roll away the heavy ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the women found the ___ already rolled away and the tomb ___, and two ___ told them Jesus had risen.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the women ran to tell the ___, and Jesus appeared to them ___, filling everyone with ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 39: Doubting Thomas":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Doubting Thomas</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 39’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 39: Doubting Thomas) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Thomas and Jesus as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Thomas</li><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>the locked room</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus appears to the disciples but Thomas is not there</li><li>Thomas says he will not believe unless he sees and touches Jesus</li><li>A week later Jesus appears again while Thomas is present</li><li>Thomas touches Jesus and declares him Lord and God</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Thomas was not with the other disciples when Jesus first appeared</li><li>Thomas said he would not believe unless he saw and touched Jesus's hands</li><li>A week later, Jesus appeared again while the doors were locked</li><li>Jesus invited Thomas to touch him, and Thomas believed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose from the dead, he appeared to his disciples in a locked room one evening, showing them his hands and side, and filling them with great joy. However, one disciple named Thomas was not there with the others that evening, and when the other disciples excitedly told him afterward that they had seen the Lord alive again, Thomas found it hard to believe such amazing news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the others firmly that unless he could see the nail marks in Jesus's hands and touch them for himself, he simply would not believe that Jesus had truly risen from the dead. A week later, the disciples were gathered together again in the same room, and this time Thomas was with them, when suddenly Jesus appeared among them once more, even though the doors were locked.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus spoke kindly to Thomas, inviting him to touch his hands and side and to stop doubting and believe. Thomas was overwhelmed with joy and immediately declared that Jesus was his Lord and his God. Jesus gently told him that although Thomas believed because he had seen, there would be a special blessing for everyone in the future who believed in him without seeing him with their own eyes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose from the dead, he appeared to his gathered disciples one evening inside a locked room in Jerusalem, showing them the marks in his hands and side, and filling them all with overwhelming joy at seeing him alive again. However, one particular disciple named Thomas happened not to be there with the others that evening, and when the rest of the disciples excitedly told him afterward that they had truly seen the Lord alive, Thomas found the amazing news very difficult to believe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the other disciples quite firmly that unless he could personally see the nail marks in Jesus's hands and actually touch them with his own fingers, he simply would not believe that Jesus had really and truly risen from the dead. A week later, the disciples were all gathered together again in that very same room, and this time Thomas happened to be with them as well, when suddenly Jesus appeared among them once more, standing right there among them even though the doors had been carefully locked from the inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus spoke kindly and gently to Thomas, inviting him personally to reach out and touch his hands and his side, and encouraging him to stop doubting and instead believe wholeheartedly. Thomas was completely overwhelmed with wonder and joy, and he immediately fell to his knees and declared that Jesus truly was his Lord and his God. Jesus then told him gently that although Thomas had come to believe because he had personally seen him, there would be a very special blessing set aside for everyone in the future who would believe in him faithfully without ever seeing him with their own eyes, just as we today believe through the good news passed down to us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose gloriously from the dead, he appeared to his gathered disciples one quiet evening inside a locked room somewhere in Jerusalem, showing them the marks in his hands and in his side, and filling every one of them with overwhelming joy and wonder at seeing him alive and well again. However, one particular disciple named Thomas, also known as Didymus, happened not to be there with the rest of the group that particular evening, and when the other excited disciples told him afterward, again and again, that they had truly seen the Lord alive with their own eyes, Thomas found this amazing, almost unbelievable news very difficult to accept.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the other disciples quite firmly and stubbornly that unless he could personally see with his own eyes the nail marks in Jesus's hands and actually place his own fingers into them, he simply would not and could not believe that Jesus had really and truly risen from the dead, no matter how many people told him so. A full week later, the disciples were all gathered together once again in that very same room, and this time Thomas happened to be with them as well, when suddenly, without any warning, Jesus appeared right there among them once more, standing calmly among the group even though the doors of the room had been carefully locked from the inside the whole time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus turned and spoke kindly and gently, without any hint of scolding, directly to Thomas, inviting him personally to reach out and touch the marks in his hands and in his side, and warmly encouraging him to stop doubting and instead believe wholeheartedly in what he could now see for himself. Thomas was completely overwhelmed with wonder, relief, and joy all at once, and he immediately responded with the beautiful declaration that Jesus truly was his Lord and his God. Jesus then told him gently and thoughtfully that although Thomas had come to believe simply because he had personally seen him standing there, there would be an especially wonderful blessing set aside for everyone in every generation to come who would believe in him faithfully without ever seeing him with their own eyes, trusting instead the joyful good news passed down faithfully from one generation of believers to the next, all the way to us today.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Doubting Thomas</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the disciples</li><li>Thomas</li><li>the room</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus appears to his disciples in a locked room</li><li>He shows them his hands and side</li><li>The disciples are filled with joy</li><li>Thomas was not there with them that evening</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>locked room</li><li>hands and side</li><li>filled with joy</li><li>not there</li><li>that evening</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus appeared to his disciples in a locked ___ and showed them his ___, but ___ was not there.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose from the dead, he appeared to his disciples in a locked room one evening, showing them his hands and side, and filling them with great joy. However, one disciple named Thomas was not there with the others that evening, and when the other disciples excitedly told him afterward that they had seen the Lord alive again, Thomas found it hard to believe such amazing news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose from the dead, he appeared to his gathered disciples one evening inside a locked room in Jerusalem, showing them the marks in his hands and side, and filling them all with overwhelming joy at seeing him alive again. However, one particular disciple named Thomas happened not to be there with the others that evening, and when the rest of the disciples excitedly told him afterward that they had truly seen the Lord alive, Thomas found the amazing news very difficult to believe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Jesus rose gloriously from the dead, he appeared to his gathered disciples one quiet evening inside a locked room somewhere in Jerusalem, showing them the marks in his hands and in his side, and filling every one of them with overwhelming joy and wonder at seeing him alive and well again. However, one particular disciple named Thomas, also known as Didymus, happened not to be there with the rest of the group that particular evening, and when the other excited disciples told him afterward, again and again, that they had truly seen the Lord alive with their own eyes, Thomas found this amazing, almost unbelievable news very difficult to accept.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Doubting Thomas</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Thomas</li><li>the nail marks</li><li>believe</li><li>a week</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The disciples tell Thomas they saw the Lord</li><li>Thomas says he will not believe without seeing the nail marks</li><li>A week passes and the disciples gather again</li><li>This time Thomas is with them</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>saw the Lord</li><li>nail marks</li><li>would not believe</li><li>a week later</li><li>gathered again</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Thomas said he would not ___ unless he saw the nail ___, so a week later the disciples gathered ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the others firmly that unless he could see the nail marks in Jesus's hands and touch them for himself, he simply would not believe that Jesus had truly risen from the dead. A week later, the disciples were gathered together again in the same room, and this time Thomas was with them, when suddenly Jesus appeared among them once more, even though the doors were locked.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the other disciples quite firmly that unless he could personally see the nail marks in Jesus's hands and actually touch them with his own fingers, he simply would not believe that Jesus had really and truly risen from the dead. A week later, the disciples were all gathered together again in that very same room, and this time Thomas happened to be with them as well, when suddenly Jesus appeared among them once more, standing right there among them even though the doors had been carefully locked from the inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Thomas told the other disciples quite firmly and stubbornly that unless he could personally see with his own eyes the nail marks in Jesus's hands and actually place his own fingers into them, he simply would not and could not believe that Jesus had really and truly risen from the dead, no matter how many people told him so. A full week later, the disciples were all gathered together once again in that very same room, and this time Thomas happened to be with them as well, when suddenly, without any warning, Jesus appeared right there among them once more, standing calmly among the group even though the doors of the room had been carefully locked from the inside the whole time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Doubting Thomas</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>Thomas</li><li>Lord and God</li><li>blessing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus appears again even though the doors are locked</li><li>He invites Thomas to touch his hands and side</li><li>Thomas declares Jesus is his Lord and his God</li><li>Jesus speaks of a blessing for those who believe without seeing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>doors locked</li><li>touch his hands</li><li>Lord and my God</li><li>believe without seeing</li><li>special blessing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Jesus invited ___ to touch his hands, and Thomas declared, 'My ___ and my ___!'</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus spoke kindly to Thomas, inviting him to touch his hands and side and to stop doubting and believe. Thomas was overwhelmed with joy and immediately declared that Jesus was his Lord and his God. Jesus gently told him that although Thomas believed because he had seen, there would be a special blessing for everyone in the future who believed in him without seeing him with their own eyes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus spoke kindly and gently to Thomas, inviting him personally to reach out and touch his hands and his side, and encouraging him to stop doubting and instead believe wholeheartedly. Thomas was completely overwhelmed with wonder and joy, and he immediately fell to his knees and declared that Jesus truly was his Lord and his God. Jesus then told him gently that although Thomas had come to believe because he had personally seen him, there would be a very special blessing set aside for everyone in the future who would believe in him faithfully without ever seeing him with their own eyes, just as we today believe through the good news passed down to us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Jesus turned and spoke kindly and gently, without any hint of scolding, directly to Thomas, inviting him personally to reach out and touch the marks in his hands and in his side, and warmly encouraging him to stop doubting and instead believe wholeheartedly in what he could now see for himself. Thomas was completely overwhelmed with wonder, relief, and joy all at once, and he immediately responded with the beautiful declaration that Jesus truly was his Lord and his God. Jesus then told him gently and thoughtfully that although Thomas had come to believe simply because he had personally seen him standing there, there would be an especially wonderful blessing set aside for everyone in every generation to come who would believe in him faithfully without ever seeing him with their own eyes, trusting instead the joyful good news passed down faithfully from one generation of believers to the next, all the way to us today.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Doubting Thomas</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 39 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 39 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 40 (Pentecost and the Holy Spirit) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus appeared to his disciples in a locked ___ and showed them his ___, but ___ was not there.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Thomas said he would not ___ unless he saw the nail ___, so a week later the disciples gathered ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Jesus invited ___ to touch his hands, and Thomas declared, 'My ___ and my ___!'</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 40’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the Holy Spirit and Peter as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the Holy Spirit</li><li>Peter</li><li>the disciples</li><li>Jerusalem</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus promises to send the Holy Spirit and tells the disciples to wait in Jerusalem</li><li>On the day of Pentecost, a sound like rushing wind fills the house</li><li>Tongues of fire appear and everyone speaks in different languages</li><li>Peter explains the events and about three thousand people believe</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus promised to send the Holy Spirit as a special helper</li><li>A sound like a rushing wind filled the house on the day of Pentecost</li><li>Tongues of fire appeared and everyone spoke in different languages</li><li>About three thousand people believed and were baptised that day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus returned to heaven, he promised his disciples that he would send them a very special helper called the Holy Spirit, and he told them to wait together in Jerusalem until this promise came true. Some time later, on the day of a Jewish festival called Pentecost, all of Jesus's followers were gathered together in one place when suddenly a sound like a rushing, powerful wind filled the whole house where they were sitting.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small tongues of fire appeared and rested on each person there, and everyone present was filled with the Holy Spirit. Amazingly, they all began speaking in different languages that they had never learned before, praising God, and a large crowd of visitors from many different countries gathered outside, astonished to hear their own languages being spoken by ordinary people from Galilee.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter stood up bravely and explained to the crowd that this wonderful event was exactly what God had promised long ago through the prophets. Many people who heard Peter's message that day believed in Jesus and were baptised, and about three thousand people joined the growing group of believers. From that exciting day onward, the Holy Spirit helped Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, and united as they shared the good news with people everywhere.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus finally returned to heaven, he made a wonderful promise to his disciples that he would soon send them a very special helper called the Holy Spirit, and he told them clearly to wait together patiently in Jerusalem until this promise came true. Some time later, on the day of an important Jewish festival called Pentecost, all of Jesus's followers were gathered closely together in one place when suddenly a loud sound like a rushing, powerful wind came from heaven and completely filled the whole house where they were sitting together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small, flickering tongues of fire then appeared and gently rested on each and every person gathered there, and everyone present was completely filled with the Holy Spirit for the very first time. Amazingly and miraculously, they all began speaking fluently in different languages that they had never actually learned before, joyfully praising God, and a large, curious crowd of visitors from many different countries quickly gathered outside, utterly astonished to hear their own native languages being spoken clearly by ordinary, uneducated fishermen and workers from Galilee.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter then stood up bravely in front of the whole crowd and carefully explained that this wonderful, extraordinary event was exactly what God had promised long ago through his prophets, and that Jesus, whom they had crucified, had indeed risen and was now exalted at God's right hand. Many people who heard Peter's powerful message that day were deeply moved, believed in Jesus, and were baptised, and remarkably, about three thousand people joined the small but growing group of believers on that single day alone. From that exciting and unforgettable day onward, the Holy Spirit continued to help Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, generous, and wonderfully united as they shared the good news about Jesus with people everywhere they went.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus finally returned to heaven in glory, he made a wonderful and important promise to his gathered disciples that he would very soon send them a special helper and comforter called the Holy Spirit, and he told them clearly and firmly to wait together patiently in the city of Jerusalem until this promise came completely true, rather than setting off on their own. Some time later, on the day of an important annual Jewish festival called Pentecost, which many visiting pilgrims had travelled to Jerusalem to celebrate, all of Jesus's followers were gathered closely together in one single place when suddenly a loud, startling sound like a rushing, powerful wind came unexpectedly from heaven and completely filled the whole house where they were sitting together in expectant prayer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small, flickering tongues of fire then mysteriously appeared and gently rested on each and every person gathered there in the room, and everyone present was completely and wonderfully filled with the Holy Spirit for the very first time in their lives. Amazingly and quite miraculously, they all immediately began speaking fluently in different languages that they had genuinely never learned or studied before, joyfully praising God at the top of their voices, and a large, curious, and rapidly growing crowd of visiting pilgrims from many different countries quickly gathered outside in the street, utterly astonished and bewildered to hear their own native languages being spoken so clearly and correctly by ordinary, uneducated fishermen and workers who plainly came from Galilee.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter then stood up boldly and confidently in front of the whole bewildered crowd and carefully explained, quoting the ancient prophet Joel, that this wonderful and extraordinary event was exactly what God had promised long ago through his prophets, and that Jesus, whom many of them had rejected and helped crucify, had indeed truly risen from the dead and was now exalted at God's own right hand in glory. Many people who heard Peter's powerful, heartfelt message that day were deeply moved and convicted, cried out asking what they should do, believed wholeheartedly in Jesus, and were baptised there and then, and remarkably, about three thousand people joined the small but rapidly growing group of believers on that single, unforgettable day alone. From that exciting and truly unforgettable day onward, the Holy Spirit continued to help and empower Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, wonderfully generous, and completely united as one family, as they devoted themselves to prayer, to learning together, and to sharing the good news about Jesus joyfully with people absolutely everywhere they travelled.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus</li><li>the Holy Spirit</li><li>the disciples</li><li>Jerusalem</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jesus promises to send the Holy Spirit</li><li>He tells the disciples to wait in Jerusalem</li><li>The disciples gather together in one place</li><li>It is the day of the festival of Pentecost</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Holy Spirit</li><li>wait in Jerusalem</li><li>gathered together</li><li>Pentecost</li><li>special helper</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Jesus promised to send the Holy ___ and told the disciples to wait in ___ until the day of ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus returned to heaven, he promised his disciples that he would send them a very special helper called the Holy Spirit, and he told them to wait together in Jerusalem until this promise came true. Some time later, on the day of a Jewish festival called Pentecost, all of Jesus's followers were gathered together in one place when suddenly a sound like a rushing, powerful wind filled the whole house where they were sitting.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus finally returned to heaven, he made a wonderful promise to his disciples that he would soon send them a very special helper called the Holy Spirit, and he told them clearly to wait together patiently in Jerusalem until this promise came true. Some time later, on the day of an important Jewish festival called Pentecost, all of Jesus's followers were gathered closely together in one place when suddenly a loud sound like a rushing, powerful wind came from heaven and completely filled the whole house where they were sitting together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before Jesus finally returned to heaven in glory, he made a wonderful and important promise to his gathered disciples that he would very soon send them a special helper and comforter called the Holy Spirit, and he told them clearly and firmly to wait together patiently in the city of Jerusalem until this promise came completely true, rather than setting off on their own. Some time later, on the day of an important annual Jewish festival called Pentecost, which many visiting pilgrims had travelled to Jerusalem to celebrate, all of Jesus's followers were gathered closely together in one single place when suddenly a loud, startling sound like a rushing, powerful wind came unexpectedly from heaven and completely filled the whole house where they were sitting together in expectant prayer.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a rushing wind</li><li>tongues of fire</li><li>the disciples</li><li>languages</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A sound like a rushing wind fills the house</li><li>Tongues of fire rest on each person</li><li>Everyone is filled with the Holy Spirit</li><li>They begin speaking in different languages</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rushing wind</li><li>tongues of fire</li><li>filled with the Spirit</li><li>different languages</li><li>amazed crowd</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a sound like a rushing ___ filled the house, and tongues of ___ rested on each person, who then spoke in different ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small tongues of fire appeared and rested on each person there, and everyone present was filled with the Holy Spirit. Amazingly, they all began speaking in different languages that they had never learned before, praising God, and a large crowd of visitors from many different countries gathered outside, astonished to hear their own languages being spoken by ordinary people from Galilee.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small, flickering tongues of fire then appeared and gently rested on each and every person gathered there, and everyone present was completely filled with the Holy Spirit for the very first time. Amazingly and miraculously, they all began speaking fluently in different languages that they had never actually learned before, joyfully praising God, and a large, curious crowd of visitors from many different countries quickly gathered outside, utterly astonished to hear their own native languages being spoken clearly by ordinary, uneducated fishermen and workers from Galilee.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>What looked like small, flickering tongues of fire then mysteriously appeared and gently rested on each and every person gathered there in the room, and everyone present was completely and wonderfully filled with the Holy Spirit for the very first time in their lives. Amazingly and quite miraculously, they all immediately began speaking fluently in different languages that they had genuinely never learned or studied before, joyfully praising God at the top of their voices, and a large, curious, and rapidly growing crowd of visiting pilgrims from many different countries quickly gathered outside in the street, utterly astonished and bewildered to hear their own native languages being spoken so clearly and correctly by ordinary, uneducated fishermen and workers who plainly came from Galilee.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter</li><li>the crowd</li><li>believers</li><li>baptised</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peter stands up and explains the event to the crowd</li><li>He tells them this fulfils God's promise</li><li>Many people believe Peter's message</li><li>About three thousand people are baptised that day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>stood up</li><li>explained</li><li>believed the message</li><li>three thousand</li><li>baptised</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, ___ explained the event to the crowd, and about three ___ people believed and were ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter stood up bravely and explained to the crowd that this wonderful event was exactly what God had promised long ago through the prophets. Many people who heard Peter's message that day believed in Jesus and were baptised, and about three thousand people joined the growing group of believers. From that exciting day onward, the Holy Spirit helped Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, and united as they shared the good news with people everywhere.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter then stood up bravely in front of the whole crowd and carefully explained that this wonderful, extraordinary event was exactly what God had promised long ago through his prophets, and that Jesus, whom they had crucified, had indeed risen and was now exalted at God's right hand. Many people who heard Peter's powerful message that day were deeply moved, believed in Jesus, and were baptised, and remarkably, about three thousand people joined the small but growing group of believers on that single day alone. From that exciting and unforgettable day onward, the Holy Spirit continued to help Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, generous, and wonderfully united as they shared the good news about Jesus with people everywhere they went.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Peter then stood up boldly and confidently in front of the whole bewildered crowd and carefully explained, quoting the ancient prophet Joel, that this wonderful and extraordinary event was exactly what God had promised long ago through his prophets, and that Jesus, whom many of them had rejected and helped crucify, had indeed truly risen from the dead and was now exalted at God's own right hand in glory. Many people who heard Peter's powerful, heartfelt message that day were deeply moved and convicted, cried out asking what they should do, believed wholeheartedly in Jesus, and were baptised there and then, and remarkably, about three thousand people joined the small but rapidly growing group of believers on that single, unforgettable day alone. From that exciting and truly unforgettable day onward, the Holy Spirit continued to help and empower Jesus's followers to be bold, loving, wonderfully generous, and completely united as one family, as they devoted themselves to prayer, to learning together, and to sharing the good news about Jesus joyfully with people absolutely everywhere they travelled.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Pentecost and the Holy Spirit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Bible Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 40 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 40 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 41, coming soon, as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Jesus promised to send the Holy ___ and told the disciples to wait in ___ until the day of ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a sound like a rushing ___ filled the house, and tongues of ___ rested on each person, who then spoke in different ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, ___ explained the event to the crowd, and about three ___ people believed and were ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}},"Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends":{"label":"Rank","children":{"Programme Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Year level</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Years 5–6 · NZC English Phase 2 (Years 4–6), Reading: Summarising</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Structure</th><td class=\"lt-td\">8 ranks × 5 stories × 5 lessons = 200 lessons of about 15 minutes. Each story: Lesson 1 Read &amp; Understand, Lessons 2–4 Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (beginning, middle, end), Lesson 5 The Full Scroll.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Differentiation</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Every story has three scrolls at three reading levels: I Recruit (simple), II Warrior (intermediate), III Master (advanced). Choose by reading level, not by rank.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">The S.W.O.R.D. Method</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>S — Search:</b> who and what is in this part?</li><li><b>W — Watch:</b> what happens, in order?</li><li><b>O — Omit:</b> cut the detail that is not needed.</li><li><b>R — Rewrite:</b> put it in your own words.</li><li><b>D — Deliver:</b> write one clear sentence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The Eight Ranks</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai</b> (white belt), Stories 1–5: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</li><li><b>Rank 2: Novice Swordsman</b> (yellow belt), Stories 6–10: Finding key events and putting them in order</li><li><b>Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior</b> (orange belt), Stories 11–15: Cutting unnecessary detail</li><li><b>Rank 4: Skilled Ronin</b> (green belt), Stories 16–20: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</li><li><b>Rank 5: Trusted Retainer</b> (blue belt), Stories 21–25: Summarising longer or more complex passages</li><li><b>Rank 6: Honoured Samurai</b> (purple belt), Stories 26–30: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</li><li><b>Rank 7: Elite Samurai</b> (brown belt), Stories 31–35: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</li><li><b>Rank 8: Master Samurai</b> (black belt), Stories 36–40: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">All 40 Stories — Japanese Legends</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Momotaro (Peach Boy)</li><li>2. Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</li><li>3. Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</li><li>4. Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</li><li>5. Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</li><li>6. Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</li><li>7. Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</li><li>8. Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</li><li>9. Urashima Taro</li><li>10. Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</li><li>11. Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</li><li>12. The Boy Who Drew Cats</li><li>13. The Stonecutter</li><li>14. Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</li><li>15. Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</li><li>16. Kintaro (Golden Boy)</li><li>17. Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</li><li>18. Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</li><li>19. Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</li><li>20. The Grateful Fox</li><li>21. Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</li><li>22. Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</li><li>23. The Mirror of Matsuyama</li><li>24. Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</li><li>25. Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</li><li>26. Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</li><li>27. Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</li><li>28. Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</li><li>29. Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</li><li>30. The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</li><li>31. Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</li><li>32. Hachiko's Promise</li><li>33. Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</li><li>34. Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</li><li>35. Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</li><li>36. Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</li><li>37. Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</li><li>38. The Legend of Mount Fuji</li><li>39. Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</li><li>40. Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</li></ul></div></div>"},"Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (White belt) — Stories 1–5":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 1’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Momotaro and the old woman as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the old woman</li><li>Onigashima</li><li>the kibi dango</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old woman finds a giant peach in the river</li><li>Momotaro is born from the peach and raised by the couple</li><li>Momotaro shares his dumplings with a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant</li><li>Momotaro and his friends defeat the oni and bring back the treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro was born from a giant peach found in the river</li><li>Momotaro travelled to Onigashima alone</li><li>Momotaro shared his kibi dango with a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant</li><li>The oni kept all of the treasure and Momotaro went home empty-handed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, an old man and an old woman lived happily together. They had no children, and they often wished for a family of their own. One day, the old woman went to the river to wash clothes. While she was working, she saw something amazing floating on the water. It was a giant peach, bigger than anything she had ever seen. She pulled it out of the river and carried it home. That evening, when the old man cut the peach open, a baby boy jumped out! The old couple were amazed and overjoyed. They named him Momotaro, which means “Peach Boy.” They raised him with love, and he grew into a strong and kind young man.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Momotaro heard terrible news. Fierce oni, or ogres, lived on a place called Onigashima, Ogre Island. The oni sailed to nearby villages and stole food, treasure, and anything they wanted. The villagers were frightened and did not know what to do. Momotaro decided he would go to Onigashima and stop the oni himself. His parents were worried, but they saw how determined he was. His mother made him kibi dango, sweet millet dumplings, to take on his journey. Momotaro said goodbye to his parents and set off down the road toward the sea, carrying his dumplings and his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his way, Momotaro met a hungry dog. He shared one of his dumplings, and the dog happily joined him. Soon he met a monkey, and then a pheasant, and he shared his dumplings with them too. All three animals promised to help him. Together, the four friends sailed to Onigashima. When they arrived, the oni were shocked to see such a determined team standing before them. Momotaro and his friends worked together bravely, and the oni chief agreed to give back everything they had stolen and promised never to trouble the villagers again. Momotaro and his friends sailed home with the treasure and shared it with everyone in the village, including his proud parents.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, an old man and an old woman lived together in a small house beside a river. They were kind and hardworking, but they had never had children, and they often wished for a family of their own to share their days with. Every morning, the old man went to the mountains to gather firewood, while the old woman went to the river to wash clothes. One ordinary morning, as the old woman knelt beside the water, something extraordinary came floating downstream: an enormous peach, far bigger than any peach she had ever seen. She waded in, pulled it from the current, and carried it home, amazed at its size. That evening, when the old man raised his knife to cut the peach open, it split apart on its own, and a healthy baby boy tumbled out, laughing. The astonished couple wept with joy and named him Momotaro, “Son of the Peach,” and raised him as their own, watching him grow into a strong, kind-hearted young man.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Momotaro grew older, troubling news began to reach the village. Far across the sea lived fierce oni, ogre-like creatures with sharp horns and terrible tempers, on a place known as Onigashima, or Ogre Island. From time to time, the oni sailed to the mainland, raided villages, and carried off food, treasure, and anything valuable they could carry. The villagers lived in fear, never knowing when the oni might return. When Momotaro heard how much suffering the oni had caused, he made up his mind: he would sail to Onigashima himself and put a stop to it. His parents were afraid for him, but they had never seen him so determined, so they agreed to let him go. His mother spent the whole night preparing kibi dango, sweet dumplings made from millet, to give him strength on his journey. The next morning, Momotaro bowed to his parents, thanked them for raising him, and set off down the winding road toward the sea, his bag of dumplings tied firmly at his side.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, Momotaro came across a dog barking hungrily beside the path. Momotaro offered him one of his precious kibi dango, and in return, the grateful dog promised to travel with him and fight by his side. Further along the road, he met a chattering monkey and, later, a proud pheasant, and shared his dumplings with each of them in turn. One by one, the animals pledged their loyalty, and soon Momotaro had gathered three brave companions. Together, the unlikely team built a small boat and sailed across the rough sea to Onigashima. When they landed on the island, the oni were astonished by the sight of a boy leading a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant, all standing together without fear. Working as one, the four friends outwitted and overpowered the startled oni, who quickly surrendered. The oni chief bowed low, promised never to trouble the villagers again, and returned every treasure his clan had stolen over the years. Momotaro and his friends loaded the treasure onto their boat and sailed proudly home, sharing every last piece with the grateful village and his overjoyed parents.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village tucked among the mountains of Japan, there lived an elderly couple who had never been blessed with a child of their own. Each day, the old man gathered firewood in the hills, while the old woman washed clothes at the riverbank. One morning, as she worked, she noticed something drifting toward her on the current: an enormous peach, far bigger than any that had ever grown in the village orchards. Curious, she waded in, hauled it from the water, and carried it home to share with her husband. That evening, as the old man lifted his knife to slice the peach open, it split apart on its own, and to their astonishment, a healthy, laughing baby boy tumbled out. Overcome with joy, the couple wept, embraced the child, and named him Momotaro, meaning “Son of the Peach.” They raised him with endless love, and he grew into a strong, courageous, and kind-hearted young man, admired throughout the village.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Momotaro grew into adulthood, unsettling rumours began reaching the village from travellers. Far across the churning sea, on a remote place called Onigashima, or Ogre Island, lived a clan of fearsome oni, horned creatures with terrible strength and even worse tempers. From time to time, the oni sailed to the mainland under cover of darkness, raiding quiet villages and carrying off food, gold, and treasured heirlooms, leaving frightened families with nothing. When Momotaro learned how much suffering the oni had caused, year after year, his resolve hardened: he decided that he alone would sail to Onigashima and put an end to their raids for good. His parents were terrified at the thought of losing him, but they had never seen him so calm and certain, and so, reluctantly, they gave their blessing. His mother stayed up through the night preparing a generous batch of kibi dango, sweet dumplings made from millet flour, believed to give travellers strength and good fortune. At dawn, Momotaro thanked his parents for a lifetime of love and set off alone down the long road toward the distant sea, his bag of dumplings tied at his waist.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Momotaro had not travelled far when he came upon a dog, lean and hungry, barking beside the path. Without hesitation, he broke off a piece of his precious kibi dango and offered it freely. Grateful and impressed, the dog pledged to accompany him and fight at his side. A little further along, Momotaro met a clever monkey, and then a proud, sharp-eyed pheasant. To each, in turn, he offered a share of his dumplings, and each, moved by his generosity, joined his growing band of companions. By the time he reached the shore, Momotaro had gathered three loyal, unlikely friends. Together they built a sturdy boat and set out across the wild sea toward Onigashima. When at last they landed on the island's rocky shore, the oni were astonished to see a determined young man, flanked calmly by a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant, standing before their fortress without fear. Working together with skill and courage, the four companions outmanoeuvred and overwhelmed the startled oni, who threw down their weapons and surrendered. Bowing low in shame, the oni chief swore never to trouble the mainland villages again and returned every last piece of treasure his clan had stolen over the years. Momotaro and his three faithful friends loaded the treasure onto their boat and sailed triumphantly home, sharing every coin and jewel with the grateful village and with the old couple who had raised him with such love.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the old man</li><li>the old woman</li><li>the giant peach</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old woman finds a giant peach in the river</li><li>She carries it home to the old man</li><li>The peach splits open and a baby boy appears</li><li>The couple name him Momotaro and raise him with love</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>far bigger than any peach she had ever seen</li><li>she waded in and pulled it from the current</li><li>that evening</li><li>when the old man raised his knife to cut it open</li><li>the astonished couple wept with joy</li><li>which means ‘Peach Boy’</li><li>he grew into a strong and kind young man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old woman found ___ in the river, then a baby boy named ___ was born from it, and the old couple raised him with ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, an old man and an old woman lived happily together. They had no children, and they often wished for a family of their own. One day, the old woman went to the river to wash clothes. While she was working, she saw something amazing floating on the water. It was a giant peach, bigger than anything she had ever seen. She pulled it out of the river and carried it home. That evening, when the old man cut the peach open, a baby boy jumped out! The old couple were amazed and overjoyed. They named him Momotaro, which means “Peach Boy.” They raised him with love, and he grew into a strong and kind young man.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, an old man and an old woman lived together in a small house beside a river. They were kind and hardworking, but they had never had children, and they often wished for a family of their own to share their days with. Every morning, the old man went to the mountains to gather firewood, while the old woman went to the river to wash clothes. One ordinary morning, as the old woman knelt beside the water, something extraordinary came floating downstream: an enormous peach, far bigger than any peach she had ever seen. She waded in, pulled it from the current, and carried it home, amazed at its size. That evening, when the old man raised his knife to cut the peach open, it split apart on its own, and a healthy baby boy tumbled out, laughing. The astonished couple wept with joy and named him Momotaro, “Son of the Peach,” and raised him as their own, watching him grow into a strong, kind-hearted young man.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village tucked among the mountains of Japan, there lived an elderly couple who had never been blessed with a child of their own. Each day, the old man gathered firewood in the hills, while the old woman washed clothes at the riverbank. One morning, as she worked, she noticed something drifting toward her on the current: an enormous peach, far bigger than any that had ever grown in the village orchards. Curious, she waded in, hauled it from the water, and carried it home to share with her husband. That evening, as the old man lifted his knife to slice the peach open, it split apart on its own, and to their astonishment, a healthy, laughing baby boy tumbled out. Overcome with joy, the couple wept, embraced the child, and named him Momotaro, meaning “Son of the Peach.” They raised him with endless love, and he grew into a strong, courageous, and kind-hearted young man, admired throughout the village.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the oni</li><li>Onigashima</li><li>the kibi dango</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro hears that oni are raiding nearby villages</li><li>He decides to travel to Onigashima to stop them</li><li>His mother makes him kibi dango for the journey</li><li>Momotaro says goodbye and sets off toward the sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>fierce oni, or ogres</li><li>sailed to nearby villages and stole food, treasure, and anything they wanted</li><li>the villagers were frightened</li><li>his parents were worried</li><li>sweet millet dumplings</li><li>carrying his dumplings and his courage</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Momotaro learned that ___ were troubling the villagers, so he decided to sail to ___, taking ___ that his mother had made.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, Momotaro heard terrible news. Fierce oni, or ogres, lived on a place called Onigashima, Ogre Island. The oni sailed to nearby villages and stole food, treasure, and anything they wanted. The villagers were frightened and did not know what to do. Momotaro decided he would go to Onigashima and stop the oni himself. His parents were worried, but they saw how determined he was. His mother made him kibi dango, sweet millet dumplings, to take on his journey. Momotaro said goodbye to his parents and set off down the road toward the sea, carrying his dumplings and his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Momotaro grew older, troubling news began to reach the village. Far across the sea lived fierce oni, ogre-like creatures with sharp horns and terrible tempers, on a place known as Onigashima, or Ogre Island. From time to time, the oni sailed to the mainland, raided villages, and carried off food, treasure, and anything valuable they could carry. The villagers lived in fear, never knowing when the oni might return. When Momotaro heard how much suffering the oni had caused, he made up his mind: he would sail to Onigashima himself and put a stop to it. His parents were afraid for him, but they had never seen him so determined, so they agreed to let him go. His mother spent the whole night preparing kibi dango, sweet dumplings made from millet, to give him strength on his journey. The next morning, Momotaro bowed to his parents, thanked them for raising him, and set off down the winding road toward the sea, his bag of dumplings tied firmly at his side.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As Momotaro grew into adulthood, unsettling rumours began reaching the village from travellers. Far across the churning sea, on a remote place called Onigashima, or Ogre Island, lived a clan of fearsome oni, horned creatures with terrible strength and even worse tempers. From time to time, the oni sailed to the mainland under cover of darkness, raiding quiet villages and carrying off food, gold, and treasured heirlooms, leaving frightened families with nothing. When Momotaro learned how much suffering the oni had caused, year after year, his resolve hardened: he decided that he alone would sail to Onigashima and put an end to their raids for good. His parents were terrified at the thought of losing him, but they had never seen him so calm and certain, and so, reluctantly, they gave their blessing. His mother stayed up through the night preparing a generous batch of kibi dango, sweet dumplings made from millet flour, believed to give travellers strength and good fortune. At dawn, Momotaro thanked his parents for a lifetime of love and set off alone down the long road toward the distant sea, his bag of dumplings tied at his waist.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the dog, monkey and pheasant</li><li>the oni</li><li>the treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro shares his dumplings with a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant</li><li>The four friends sail together to Onigashima</li><li>They work together and the oni surrender</li><li>Momotaro and his friends bring the treasure home to share</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a hungry dog</li><li>soon he met a monkey, and then a pheasant</li><li>all three animals promised to help him</li><li>the oni were shocked</li><li>the oni chief agreed to give back everything they had stolen</li><li>sailed home with the treasure</li><li>shared it with everyone in the village</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Momotaro and his new friends sailed to ___, defeated the ___ together, and brought the ___ home to share with the village.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his way, Momotaro met a hungry dog. He shared one of his dumplings, and the dog happily joined him. Soon he met a monkey, and then a pheasant, and he shared his dumplings with them too. All three animals promised to help him. Together, the four friends sailed to Onigashima. When they arrived, the oni were shocked to see such a determined team standing before them. Momotaro and his friends worked together bravely, and the oni chief agreed to give back everything they had stolen and promised never to trouble the villagers again. Momotaro and his friends sailed home with the treasure and shared it with everyone in the village, including his proud parents.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, Momotaro came across a dog barking hungrily beside the path. Momotaro offered him one of his precious kibi dango, and in return, the grateful dog promised to travel with him and fight by his side. Further along the road, he met a chattering monkey and, later, a proud pheasant, and shared his dumplings with each of them in turn. One by one, the animals pledged their loyalty, and soon Momotaro had gathered three brave companions. Together, the unlikely team built a small boat and sailed across the rough sea to Onigashima. When they landed on the island, the oni were astonished by the sight of a boy leading a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant, all standing together without fear. Working as one, the four friends outwitted and overpowered the startled oni, who quickly surrendered. The oni chief bowed low, promised never to trouble the villagers again, and returned every treasure his clan had stolen over the years. Momotaro and his friends loaded the treasure onto their boat and sailed proudly home, sharing every last piece with the grateful village and his overjoyed parents.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Momotaro had not travelled far when he came upon a dog, lean and hungry, barking beside the path. Without hesitation, he broke off a piece of his precious kibi dango and offered it freely. Grateful and impressed, the dog pledged to accompany him and fight at his side. A little further along, Momotaro met a clever monkey, and then a proud, sharp-eyed pheasant. To each, in turn, he offered a share of his dumplings, and each, moved by his generosity, joined his growing band of companions. By the time he reached the shore, Momotaro had gathered three loyal, unlikely friends. Together they built a sturdy boat and set out across the wild sea toward Onigashima. When at last they landed on the island's rocky shore, the oni were astonished to see a determined young man, flanked calmly by a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant, standing before their fortress without fear. Working together with skill and courage, the four companions outmanoeuvred and overwhelmed the startled oni, who threw down their weapons and surrendered. Bowing low in shame, the oni chief swore never to trouble the mainland villages again and returned every last piece of treasure his clan had stolen over the years. Momotaro and his three faithful friends loaded the treasure onto their boat and sailed triumphantly home, sharing every coin and jewel with the grateful village and with the old couple who had raised him with such love.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 1: Momotaro (Peach Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 1 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 1 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 2 (Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old woman found ___ in the river, then a baby boy named ___ was born from it, and the old couple raised him with ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Momotaro learned that ___ were troubling the villagers, so he decided to sail to ___, taking ___ that his mother had made.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Momotaro and his new friends sailed to ___, defeated the ___ together, and brought the ___ home to share with the village.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 2’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Issun-boshi and the princess as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi</li><li>the princess</li><li>the oni</li><li>the uchide no kozuchi (magic mallet)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi is born, only one inch tall, and grows up clever and brave</li><li>He travels to the city in a rice-bowl boat and works for a kind lord</li><li>Issun-boshi bravely fights off an oni while guarding the princess</li><li>The magic mallet grows Issun-boshi to full size and he and the princess become friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi was born only one inch tall</li><li>Issun-boshi travelled to the city using a rice bowl as a boat</li><li>Issun-boshi bravely fought off an oni to protect the princess</li><li>Issun-boshi stayed one inch tall for the rest of his life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an old man and an old woman in Japan wished very much for a child, even a tiny one. One day, their wish came true in a surprising way: a baby boy was born to them who was only one inch tall! They loved him dearly and named him Issun-boshi, which means \"One-Inch Boy.\" Even though he never grew any bigger, Issun-boshi was clever, brave, and full of energy. When he became a young man, he decided it was time to see the world and make his own way. He asked his parents for a rice bowl to use as a boat, a chopstick to use as a paddle, and a needle to use as a sword. His parents were sad to see him go, but they packed his tiny bag and wished him well.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Issun-boshi set off down the river in his little rice-bowl boat, paddling bravely with his chopstick. He floated past reeds and lily pads until he reached a great city, where he found work in the house of a kind lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle princess, was delighted by the clever, tiny boy and enjoyed his company. Issun-boshi worked hard and proved that his small size did not stop him from being helpful and brave. He often walked beside the princess to protect her whenever she travelled outside the house.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, while Issun-boshi was guarding the princess on a journey, a fearsome oni jumped out to frighten them. The oni was much bigger than tiny Issun-boshi, but he did not run away. He drew his needle sword and fought bravely, even leaping into the oni's mouth to attack from inside! The startled oni fled, dropping a magical mallet called an uchide no kozuchi as it ran. The princess picked it up and wished that Issun-boshi could grow to a normal size. She tapped the mallet, and in an instant, Issun-boshi grew into a tall, strong young man. Everyone celebrated his courage, and Issun-boshi and the princess became the best of friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, an old man and an old woman wished more than anything for a child, even one no bigger than a fingertip. Their wish was answered in a most unusual way: the old woman gave birth to a tiny baby boy, only one inch tall. Overjoyed despite his tiny size, they named him Issun-boshi, meaning \"One-Inch Boy,\" and raised him with all the love they could give. Although he never grew any taller, Issun-boshi grew clever, cheerful, and remarkably brave. When he reached the age of a young man, he told his parents he wished to travel to the great city and make a life of his own. His parents were heavy-hearted, but they helped him prepare: a hollowed-out rice bowl for a boat, a chopstick for a paddle, and a sewing needle to carry as a sword. Issun-boshi bowed to his parents and set off down the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Paddling carefully with his chopstick oar, Issun-boshi drifted past reeds and lily pads until his little boat reached a bustling city. There, he found work in the house of a kind lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle young princess, was charmed by the clever, tiny visitor and welcomed him warmly. Issun-boshi worked hard to prove that his small size was no measure of his worth, and before long he had become the princess's trusted companion, walking beside her whenever she travelled to keep her safe from harm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, while escorting the princess, they were suddenly confronted by a fearsome oni, far taller and stronger than tiny Issun-boshi. Rather than flee, Issun-boshi drew his needle sword and stood his ground, even leaping straight into the startled oni's mouth to strike from within. Shocked and in pain, the oni fled into the forest, dropping a magical hammer known as an uchide no kozuchi. The princess picked up the hammer and, remembering old stories about its magic, wished aloud that her brave friend could grow to an ordinary size. The moment she tapped it, Issun-boshi began to grow, taller and taller, until he stood as a strong young man before her astonished eyes. From that day on, Issun-boshi and the princess remained the closest of friends, and he was celebrated throughout the city for his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, there lived an old man and an old woman who wished more than anything for a child to love, even one no bigger than a fingertip. Their wish was finally answered in the most unusual way imaginable: the old woman gave birth to a tiny, healthy baby boy who was only one inch tall from head to toe. Though astonished by his impossibly small size, the couple were overjoyed and named him Issun-boshi, meaning \"One-Inch Boy,\" raising him with every bit of love they could offer. The years passed, and though he never grew a single inch taller, he grew clever, cheerful, and remarkably brave. By the time he reached the age of a young man, Issun-boshi felt a restless longing to see the wider world and make a life of his own in the great capital city. His parents were heavy-hearted at the thought of parting with their only son, but seeing his determination, they helped him prepare: a hollowed-out rice bowl to serve as a boat, a chopstick to use as an oar, and a sewing needle to carry as his sword. With his tiny bundle packed, Issun-boshi bowed low to his parents and set off down the winding river current.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Paddling steadily with his chopstick oar, Issun-boshi drifted for many days past swaying reeds and lily pads, watching curiously as the scenery slowly changed. At last, his little boat came to rest at the edge of a great, bustling city, full of noise and colour unlike anything he had seen in his quiet village. Undaunted by his small size, Issun-boshi found work within the household of a kind and well-respected lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle young princess, was charmed by the clever, tiny visitor, and welcomed him warmly into her household. Issun-boshi worked tirelessly at every task given to him, determined to prove that his small stature was no measure of his worth. Before long, he had earned the princess's complete trust, and walked proudly beside her whenever she travelled beyond the house, standing guard over her as faithfully as any samurai.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One afternoon, while escorting the princess along a quiet forest road, the pair were suddenly confronted by a fearsome oni, towering high above them, with sharp horns and a sharper temper. The creature loomed over tiny Issun-boshi, clearly expecting him to flee in terror. Instead, without hesitation, Issun-boshi drew his gleaming needle sword and stood his ground, refusing to let the princess come to any harm. When the oni lunged to snatch him up, Issun-boshi did something no one expected: he leapt straight into the creature's open mouth and began stabbing bravely from within, until the oni howled in pain. Beaten and bewildered by his tiny, fearless opponent, the oni fled deep into the forest, dropping a strange hammer, an uchide no kozuchi, a magical mallet said to grant wishes, as it stumbled away. The princess picked up the fallen hammer, remembering old stories about its powers, and, moved by gratitude for her friend's courage, she wished aloud that Issun-boshi might grow to an ordinary size. The instant she tapped it, a warm light surrounded him, and Issun-boshi began to grow, taller and taller, until at last he stood before her as a strong young man. From that day onward, Issun-boshi and the princess remained the closest of friends, and his story of courage despite his once-tiny size was told throughout the city for years to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi</li><li>the old man</li><li>the old woman</li><li>the rice bowl</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old couple wish for a child of their own</li><li>A tiny baby boy, only one inch tall, is born to them</li><li>They name him Issun-boshi and raise him with love</li><li>He asks for a rice bowl, chopstick, and needle to travel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>even one no bigger than a fingertip</li><li>only one inch tall</li><li>meaning \\\"One-Inch Boy\\\"</li><li>he grew clever, cheerful, and remarkably brave</li><li>a hollowed-out rice bowl for a boat</li><li>a sharp sewing needle to carry as a sword</li><li>bowed to his parents and set off down the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, an old couple wished for ___, and a baby boy only ___ tall was born, so they named him ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an old man and an old woman in Japan wished very much for a child, even a tiny one. One day, their wish came true in a surprising way: a baby boy was born to them who was only one inch tall! They loved him dearly and named him Issun-boshi, which means \"One-Inch Boy.\" Even though he never grew any bigger, Issun-boshi was clever, brave, and full of energy. When he became a young man, he decided it was time to see the world and make his own way. He asked his parents for a rice bowl to use as a boat, a chopstick to use as a paddle, and a needle to use as a sword. His parents were sad to see him go, but they packed his tiny bag and wished him well.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, an old man and an old woman wished more than anything for a child, even one no bigger than a fingertip. Their wish was answered in a most unusual way: the old woman gave birth to a tiny baby boy, only one inch tall. Overjoyed despite his tiny size, they named him Issun-boshi, meaning \"One-Inch Boy,\" and raised him with all the love they could give. Although he never grew any taller, Issun-boshi grew clever, cheerful, and remarkably brave. When he reached the age of a young man, he told his parents he wished to travel to the great city and make a life of his own. His parents were heavy-hearted, but they helped him prepare: a hollowed-out rice bowl for a boat, a chopstick for a paddle, and a sewing needle to carry as a sword. Issun-boshi bowed to his parents and set off down the river.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, there lived an old man and an old woman who wished more than anything for a child to love, even one no bigger than a fingertip. Their wish was finally answered in the most unusual way imaginable: the old woman gave birth to a tiny, healthy baby boy who was only one inch tall from head to toe. Though astonished by his impossibly small size, the couple were overjoyed and named him Issun-boshi, meaning \"One-Inch Boy,\" raising him with every bit of love they could offer. The years passed, and though he never grew a single inch taller, he grew clever, cheerful, and remarkably brave. By the time he reached the age of a young man, Issun-boshi felt a restless longing to see the wider world and make a life of his own in the great capital city. His parents were heavy-hearted at the thought of parting with their only son, but seeing his determination, they helped him prepare: a hollowed-out rice bowl to serve as a boat, a chopstick to use as an oar, and a sewing needle to carry as his sword. With his tiny bundle packed, Issun-boshi bowed low to his parents and set off down the winding river current.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi</li><li>the lord</li><li>the princess</li><li>the city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi paddles his rice-bowl boat down the river</li><li>He reaches the city and finds work with a kind lord</li><li>The lord's daughter, a princess, welcomes him warmly</li><li>Issun-boshi walks beside the princess to protect her</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>past tall reeds and floating lily pads</li><li>a bustling city</li><li>a kind and generous lord</li><li>charmed by the clever, tiny visitor</li><li>his trusted companion</li><li>to keep her safe from harm</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Issun-boshi paddled to the ___, found work with a kind lord, and became a trusted friend of the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Issun-boshi set off down the river in his little rice-bowl boat, paddling bravely with his chopstick. He floated past reeds and lily pads until he reached a great city, where he found work in the house of a kind lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle princess, was delighted by the clever, tiny boy and enjoyed his company. Issun-boshi worked hard and proved that his small size did not stop him from being helpful and brave. He often walked beside the princess to protect her whenever she travelled outside the house.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Paddling carefully with his chopstick oar, Issun-boshi drifted past reeds and lily pads until his little boat reached a bustling city. There, he found work in the house of a kind lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle young princess, was charmed by the clever, tiny visitor and welcomed him warmly. Issun-boshi worked hard to prove that his small size was no measure of his worth, and before long he had become the princess's trusted companion, walking beside her whenever she travelled to keep her safe from harm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Paddling steadily with his chopstick oar, Issun-boshi drifted for many days past swaying reeds and lily pads, watching curiously as the scenery slowly changed. At last, his little boat came to rest at the edge of a great, bustling city, full of noise and colour unlike anything he had seen in his quiet village. Undaunted by his small size, Issun-boshi found work within the household of a kind and well-respected lord. The lord's daughter, a gentle young princess, was charmed by the clever, tiny visitor, and welcomed him warmly into her household. Issun-boshi worked tirelessly at every task given to him, determined to prove that his small stature was no measure of his worth. Before long, he had earned the princess's complete trust, and walked proudly beside her whenever she travelled beyond the house, standing guard over her as faithfully as any samurai.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Issun-boshi</li><li>the oni</li><li>the princess</li><li>the magic mallet</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A fearsome oni confronts Issun-boshi and the princess</li><li>Issun-boshi bravely fights the oni with his needle sword</li><li>The oni flees, dropping a magical hammer</li><li>The princess uses the mallet to grow Issun-boshi to full size</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a fearsome oni</li><li>drew his needle sword and stood his ground</li><li>leap straight into the startled oni's mouth</li><li>dropping a magical hammer</li><li>an uchide no kozuchi</li><li>wished aloud that her brave friend could grow</li><li>the closest of friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Issun-boshi bravely fought the ___, which dropped a magic ___, and the princess used it to make him ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, while Issun-boshi was guarding the princess on a journey, a fearsome oni jumped out to frighten them. The oni was much bigger than tiny Issun-boshi, but he did not run away. He drew his needle sword and fought bravely, even leaping into the oni's mouth to attack from inside! The startled oni fled, dropping a magical mallet called an uchide no kozuchi as it ran. The princess picked it up and wished that Issun-boshi could grow to a normal size. She tapped the mallet, and in an instant, Issun-boshi grew into a tall, strong young man. Everyone celebrated his courage, and Issun-boshi and the princess became the best of friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, while escorting the princess, they were suddenly confronted by a fearsome oni, far taller and stronger than tiny Issun-boshi. Rather than flee, Issun-boshi drew his needle sword and stood his ground, even leaping straight into the startled oni's mouth to strike from within. Shocked and in pain, the oni fled into the forest, dropping a magical hammer known as an uchide no kozuchi. The princess picked up the hammer and, remembering old stories about its magic, wished aloud that her brave friend could grow to an ordinary size. The moment she tapped it, Issun-boshi began to grow, taller and taller, until he stood as a strong young man before her astonished eyes. From that day on, Issun-boshi and the princess remained the closest of friends, and he was celebrated throughout the city for his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One afternoon, while escorting the princess along a quiet forest road, the pair were suddenly confronted by a fearsome oni, towering high above them, with sharp horns and a sharper temper. The creature loomed over tiny Issun-boshi, clearly expecting him to flee in terror. Instead, without hesitation, Issun-boshi drew his gleaming needle sword and stood his ground, refusing to let the princess come to any harm. When the oni lunged to snatch him up, Issun-boshi did something no one expected: he leapt straight into the creature's open mouth and began stabbing bravely from within, until the oni howled in pain. Beaten and bewildered by his tiny, fearless opponent, the oni fled deep into the forest, dropping a strange hammer, an uchide no kozuchi, a magical mallet said to grant wishes, as it stumbled away. The princess picked up the fallen hammer, remembering old stories about its powers, and, moved by gratitude for her friend's courage, she wished aloud that Issun-boshi might grow to an ordinary size. The instant she tapped it, a warm light surrounded him, and Issun-boshi began to grow, taller and taller, until at last he stood before her as a strong young man. From that day onward, Issun-boshi and the princess remained the closest of friends, and his story of courage despite his once-tiny size was told throughout the city for years to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 2: Issun-boshi (One-Inch Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 2 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 2 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 3 (Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, an old couple wished for ___, and a baby boy only ___ tall was born, so they named him ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Issun-boshi paddled to the ___, found work with a kind lord, and became a trusted friend of the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Issun-boshi bravely fought the ___, which dropped a magic ___, and the princess used it to make him ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 3’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the old man and the sparrow as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the sparrow</li><li>the old woman</li><li>the two baskets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man kindly cares for his pet sparrow</li><li>The old woman cuts the sparrow's tongue in anger and chases her away</li><li>The old man finds the sparrow's family and is offered a choice of baskets</li><li>The kind old man is rewarded with treasure, but the greedy old woman is not</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man loved and cared for his pet sparrow</li><li>The old woman cut the sparrow's tongue because she was angry</li><li>The old man chose the small basket instead of the large one</li><li>The greedy old woman received a basket full of gold and jewels</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind old man lived with his unkind wife in a small village in Japan. The old man kept a little sparrow as a pet and loved it very much. He fed the sparrow every day and enjoyed listening to its cheerful song. One day, while the old man was away, the sparrow accidentally ate some starch paste that the old woman had made for washing clothes. The old woman was furious. In her anger, she grabbed the little bird and cut its tongue before chasing it away into the forest. When the old man came home and learned what had happened, he was heartbroken and set off at once into the woods to search for his beloved sparrow friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man wandered through the forest for a long time, calling out for his sparrow. Eventually, the sparrow heard his gentle voice and flew down to greet him, delighted to see her kind friend again. She led him to her home, a cosy little house deep in the bamboo grove, where her sparrow family welcomed him warmly. They served him a wonderful feast and danced for him to say thank you for all his kindness. When it was time for the old man to leave, the sparrows offered him a choice between two baskets, one large and one small, as a parting gift. The humble old man chose the small basket, not wanting to be greedy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man returned home and opened his small basket, he found it overflowing with gold, jewels, and treasure! He and his wife would never be poor again. When the greedy old woman heard about the treasure, she rushed into the forest herself to find the sparrows, hoping to get an even bigger reward. She found the sparrow family and demanded a basket, choosing the large one without a moment's thought. But when she opened it on her way home, terrifying creatures jumped out instead of treasure, and she fled home in fright, learning a hard lesson about greed. The old man, meanwhile, lived happily and kindly for the rest of his days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, there lived a kind old man whose wife, sadly, was often unkind and quick to anger. The old man kept a small sparrow as a beloved pet, feeding her tenderly every day and delighting in her cheerful song each morning. One day, while the old man was working in the fields, the little sparrow accidentally pecked at a pot of starch paste the old woman had prepared for stiffening freshly washed clothes. Furious, the old woman seized the frightened bird without a shred of mercy, cut her tongue with a pair of scissors, and chased her away into the forest. When the old man came home that evening and discovered what his wife had done to his beloved pet, his heart broke with sorrow, and he set out at once into the darkening woods to search for his dear sparrow friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man wandered through the forest for hours, gently calling his sparrow's name. At last, from deep in a bamboo grove, the sparrow recognised his caring voice and flew down joyfully to greet him. She led him to her family's cosy home among the bamboo stalks, where her sparrow family greeted the kind old man with warm delight. They prepared a wonderful feast in his honour and danced to thank him for the years of gentle care he had shown their daughter. When it was time for him to head home, the grateful sparrows offered him a choice between two woven baskets, one large and heavy, the other small and light. Being a humble man who never wished to seem greedy, the old man chose the smaller basket and thanked the sparrows before setting off for home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man reached his cottage and opened the small basket by lamplight, he could hardly believe his eyes: it was filled to the brim with glittering gold, jewels, and fine silk. He and his wife would never again know poverty. Word of the fortune spread quickly, and when the greedy old woman heard, she hurried into the forest herself, determined to claim an even greater reward. She found the sparrows and demanded a gift, snatching up the large basket without a second thought, certain it must hold twice the treasure. But when she set it down on the road home and lifted the lid, terrifying creatures and snakes came pouring out instead of gold, and the terrified old woman fled screaming all the way home, learning a hard lesson about greed. The old man, meanwhile, shared his good fortune with the village and lived out his days in quiet happiness and kindness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village nestled among the hills of Japan, there lived a kind and gentle old man whose wife, unfortunately, had grown sharp-tongued, bitter, and quick to lose her temper over the smallest things. The old man kept a small, cheerful sparrow as his beloved pet, feeding her tenderly every morning and finding endless comfort in her sweet song. One afternoon, while the old man was away tending the fields, the little sparrow, hopping about curiously in the kitchen, accidentally pecked at a pot of starch paste that the old woman had spent all morning preparing to stiffen the family's freshly washed clothing. Enraged at the sight of her ruined paste, the old woman seized the terrified bird without the slightest shred of mercy, cut her tongue with a pair of sharp scissors, and chased her out of the house and into the forest, shouting cruel words after her as she fled. When the old man returned home that evening and learned exactly what his wife had done to his cherished companion, his heart broke completely, and he set out at once into the darkening woods, calling desperately for his dear little sparrow friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The heartbroken old man wandered deep into the forest for what felt like hours, gently calling out his sparrow's name into the gathering shadows. At long last, from within a peaceful bamboo grove, the sparrow recognised his familiar voice and flew down at once, overjoyed to be reunited with her friend. She led him eagerly to her family's cosy home, nestled among the tall bamboo stalks, where her entire sparrow family welcomed the kind old man with warmth and open wings. They prepared a wonderful feast in his honour, filled with delicious rice cakes and sweet plum wine, and performed a joyful dance together to thank him properly for the years of gentle care he had shown their daughter. As evening fell and the time came for him to make his way home, the grateful sparrow family presented him with a difficult choice between two beautifully woven baskets, one large and heavy, the other small and light, offered as a parting gift. Being a humble man who never wished to appear greedy, the old man chose the smaller basket without hesitation, thanked the sparrow family warmly, and set off contentedly for home beneath the darkening sky.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man arrived back at his cottage and opened the small basket by lamplight, he could hardly believe what lay before his eyes: it was filled right to the brim with glittering gold coins, sparkling jewels, and beautifully woven silk treasures. He and his wife, he realised with quiet joy, would never again have to know poverty. Word of this remarkable fortune spread swiftly through the village, reaching the greedy old woman, who hurried into the forest herself without delay, determined to claim an even greater treasure for herself. She found the sparrow family among the bamboo and demanded a parting gift with rude, impatient words, snatching up the large, heavy basket the very instant it was offered, certain in her greed that it must hold twice the fortune her husband had received. But when she finally set the basket down on the roadside and eagerly lifted its lid, terrifying snakes and monstrous creatures came pouring out in a dreadful rush instead of gold, and the terrified old woman fled screaming the entire way home, learning a hard, unforgettable lesson about the true cost of greed and cruelty. The old man, meanwhile, generously shared his good fortune throughout the grateful village and lived out the remainder of his years in quiet contentment and lasting kindness toward every creature he met.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the sparrow</li><li>the old woman</li><li>the starch paste</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man cares for his pet sparrow</li><li>The sparrow accidentally eats the old woman's starch paste</li><li>The angry old woman cuts the sparrow's tongue</li><li>The old man searches the forest for his sparrow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>often unkind and quick to anger</li><li>feeding her tenderly every single day</li><li>accidentally pecked at a pot of starch paste</li><li>seized the frightened little bird without a shred of mercy</li><li>cut her tongue</li><li>chased her away into the forest</li><li>his heart broke with sorrow</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old man's sparrow accidentally ate the ___, so the angry old woman cut its ___ and chased it away.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind old man lived with his unkind wife in a small village in Japan. The old man kept a little sparrow as a pet and loved it very much. He fed the sparrow every day and enjoyed listening to its cheerful song. One day, while the old man was away, the sparrow accidentally ate some starch paste that the old woman had made for washing clothes. The old woman was furious. In her anger, she grabbed the little bird and cut its tongue before chasing it away into the forest. When the old man came home and learned what had happened, he was heartbroken and set off at once into the woods to search for his beloved sparrow friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, there lived a kind old man whose wife, sadly, was often unkind and quick to anger. The old man kept a small sparrow as a beloved pet, feeding her tenderly every day and delighting in her cheerful song each morning. One day, while the old man was working in the fields, the little sparrow accidentally pecked at a pot of starch paste the old woman had prepared for stiffening freshly washed clothes. Furious, the old woman seized the frightened bird without a shred of mercy, cut her tongue with a pair of scissors, and chased her away into the forest. When the old man came home that evening and discovered what his wife had done to his beloved pet, his heart broke with sorrow, and he set out at once into the darkening woods to search for his dear sparrow friend.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village nestled among the hills of Japan, there lived a kind and gentle old man whose wife, unfortunately, had grown sharp-tongued, bitter, and quick to lose her temper over the smallest things. The old man kept a small, cheerful sparrow as his beloved pet, feeding her tenderly every morning and finding endless comfort in her sweet song. One afternoon, while the old man was away tending the fields, the little sparrow, hopping about curiously in the kitchen, accidentally pecked at a pot of starch paste that the old woman had spent all morning preparing to stiffen the family's freshly washed clothing. Enraged at the sight of her ruined paste, the old woman seized the terrified bird without the slightest shred of mercy, cut her tongue with a pair of sharp scissors, and chased her out of the house and into the forest, shouting cruel words after her as she fled. When the old man returned home that evening and learned exactly what his wife had done to his cherished companion, his heart broke completely, and he set out at once into the darkening woods, calling desperately for his dear little sparrow friend.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the sparrow family</li><li>the bamboo grove</li><li>the two baskets</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man finds the sparrow deep in the forest</li><li>The sparrow family welcomes him with a feast and a dance</li><li>They offer him a choice of a large basket or a small basket</li><li>The humble old man chooses the small basket</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a bamboo grove</li><li>greeted the kind old man with warm delight</li><li>a wonderful feast in his honour</li><li>a graceful dance</li><li>a choice between two woven baskets</li><li>a humble man who never wished to seem greedy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the sparrow family welcomed the old man with a feast, then offered him a choice of ___, and he humbly chose the ___ one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man wandered through the forest for a long time, calling out for his sparrow. Eventually, the sparrow heard his gentle voice and flew down to greet him, delighted to see her kind friend again. She led him to her home, a cosy little house deep in the bamboo grove, where her sparrow family welcomed him warmly. They served him a wonderful feast and danced for him to say thank you for all his kindness. When it was time for the old man to leave, the sparrows offered him a choice between two baskets, one large and one small, as a parting gift. The humble old man chose the small basket, not wanting to be greedy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man wandered through the forest for hours, gently calling his sparrow's name. At last, from deep in a bamboo grove, the sparrow recognised his caring voice and flew down joyfully to greet him. She led him to her family's cosy home among the bamboo stalks, where her sparrow family greeted the kind old man with warm delight. They prepared a wonderful feast in his honour and danced to thank him for the years of gentle care he had shown their daughter. When it was time for him to head home, the grateful sparrows offered him a choice between two woven baskets, one large and heavy, the other small and light. Being a humble man who never wished to seem greedy, the old man chose the smaller basket and thanked the sparrows before setting off for home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The heartbroken old man wandered deep into the forest for what felt like hours, gently calling out his sparrow's name into the gathering shadows. At long last, from within a peaceful bamboo grove, the sparrow recognised his familiar voice and flew down at once, overjoyed to be reunited with her friend. She led him eagerly to her family's cosy home, nestled among the tall bamboo stalks, where her entire sparrow family welcomed the kind old man with warmth and open wings. They prepared a wonderful feast in his honour, filled with delicious rice cakes and sweet plum wine, and performed a joyful dance together to thank him properly for the years of gentle care he had shown their daughter. As evening fell and the time came for him to make his way home, the grateful sparrow family presented him with a difficult choice between two beautifully woven baskets, one large and heavy, the other small and light, offered as a parting gift. Being a humble man who never wished to appear greedy, the old man chose the smaller basket without hesitation, thanked the sparrow family warmly, and set off contentedly for home beneath the darkening sky.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the treasure</li><li>the old woman</li><li>the large basket</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man opens his basket and finds treasure inside</li><li>The greedy old woman hears about the treasure</li><li>She demands the large basket from the sparrows</li><li>Frightening creatures, not treasure, come out of her basket</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>filled to the brim with glittering gold</li><li>would never again know poverty</li><li>the greedy old woman</li><li>snatching up the large, heavy basket</li><li>terrifying creatures and snakes came pouring out</li><li>a hard and lasting lesson about greed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the old man's basket was full of ___, but the greedy old woman chose the ___ basket and got a fright instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man returned home and opened his small basket, he found it overflowing with gold, jewels, and treasure! He and his wife would never be poor again. When the greedy old woman heard about the treasure, she rushed into the forest herself to find the sparrows, hoping to get an even bigger reward. She found the sparrow family and demanded a basket, choosing the large one without a moment's thought. But when she opened it on her way home, terrifying creatures jumped out instead of treasure, and she fled home in fright, learning a hard lesson about greed. The old man, meanwhile, lived happily and kindly for the rest of his days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man reached his cottage and opened the small basket by lamplight, he could hardly believe his eyes: it was filled to the brim with glittering gold, jewels, and fine silk. He and his wife would never again know poverty. Word of the fortune spread quickly, and when the greedy old woman heard, she hurried into the forest herself, determined to claim an even greater reward. She found the sparrows and demanded a gift, snatching up the large basket without a second thought, certain it must hold twice the treasure. But when she set it down on the road home and lifted the lid, terrifying creatures and snakes came pouring out instead of gold, and the terrified old woman fled screaming all the way home, learning a hard lesson about greed. The old man, meanwhile, shared his good fortune with the village and lived out his days in quiet happiness and kindness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When the old man arrived back at his cottage and opened the small basket by lamplight, he could hardly believe what lay before his eyes: it was filled right to the brim with glittering gold coins, sparkling jewels, and beautifully woven silk treasures. He and his wife, he realised with quiet joy, would never again have to know poverty. Word of this remarkable fortune spread swiftly through the village, reaching the greedy old woman, who hurried into the forest herself without delay, determined to claim an even greater treasure for herself. She found the sparrow family among the bamboo and demanded a parting gift with rude, impatient words, snatching up the large, heavy basket the very instant it was offered, certain in her greed that it must hold twice the fortune her husband had received. But when she finally set the basket down on the roadside and eagerly lifted its lid, terrifying snakes and monstrous creatures came pouring out in a dreadful rush instead of gold, and the terrified old woman fled screaming the entire way home, learning a hard, unforgettable lesson about the true cost of greed and cruelty. The old man, meanwhile, generously shared his good fortune throughout the grateful village and lived out the remainder of his years in quiet contentment and lasting kindness toward every creature he met.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 3: Shita-kiri Suzume (The Tongue-Cut Sparrow)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 3 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 3 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 4 (Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old man's sparrow accidentally ate the ___, so the angry old woman cut its ___ and chased it away.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the sparrow family welcomed the old man with a feast, then offered him a choice of ___, and he humbly chose the ___ one.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the old man's basket was full of ___, but the greedy old woman chose the ___ basket and got a fright instead.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 4’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the kind old man and Shiro the dog as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the kind old man</li><li>Shiro the dog</li><li>the unkind neighbour</li><li>the lord</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shiro the dog helps the kind old man find buried treasure</li><li>The unkind neighbour borrows Shiro and later hurts him out of anger</li><li>Shiro's spirit helps the old man make magical rice cakes, then magical ashes</li><li>The old man's ashes make a dead tree bloom, and a lord rewards his kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shiro the dog helped the kind old man find a chest of gold coins</li><li>The unkind neighbour treated Shiro with kindness and patience</li><li>The old man's magic ashes made a dead cherry tree burst into bloom</li><li>The lord rewarded the kind old man with gold and gifts</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind old man and his wife lived next door to an unkind old man and his wife in a small Japanese village. The kind old couple owned a little dog named Shiro, whom they loved very much. One day, Shiro began digging at a spot in the garden and barking excitedly. When the old man dug there, he found a chest full of gold coins! The greedy neighbour saw this and borrowed Shiro, hoping to find treasure too, but when he dug, he found only rubbish. Furious, the unkind neighbour hit Shiro, and sadly, the little dog died. The kind old man was heartbroken and buried Shiro under a tree in the garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, Shiro appeared to the old man in a dream and told him to cut down the tree and make a mortar, a wooden bowl for grinding rice, from its wood. The old man did as he was told, and when he used the mortar, rice cakes appeared inside it as if by magic! The unkind neighbour asked to borrow the mortar, but when he tried to use it, only foul-smelling rubbish appeared. Angry once again, he chopped the mortar into firewood and burned it. The kind old man asked for the ashes so he could keep something to remember his beloved Shiro by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried the ashes home, a strong wind blew some of them onto a nearby dead cherry tree. Instantly, the tree burst into beautiful pink blossoms! Just then, a lord happened to be passing by and was amazed by the sight. The old man explained that he could make dead trees bloom with his magic ashes, and the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold and gifts. The jealous neighbour tried the same trick, but his ashes only blew into the lord's face, and he was punished instead. The kind old man lived happily ever after, remembering his loyal friend Shiro with love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, a kind old man and his wife lived next door to another old couple whose selfish and unkind ways were well known throughout the village. The kind old couple treated their little dog, Shiro, like a true member of the family. One morning, Shiro suddenly began digging furiously at a spot in the garden, barking with great excitement. The old man dug where his dog pointed and, to his astonishment, uncovered a heavy wooden chest overflowing with gold coins. Word of the discovery reached the greedy neighbour, who came at once to borrow Shiro, hoping the clever dog might sniff out treasure for him too. But when the neighbour dug at the spot Shiro chose, he found nothing but rubbish. Consumed with fury, the cruel man struck poor Shiro, and the gentle little dog sadly died. Heartbroken, the kind old man buried his beloved companion beneath a young tree in his garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, Shiro appeared to the old man in a dream, telling him to cut down the tree above his grave and carve its wood into a mortar, a wooden bowl used for pounding rice into cakes. The old man did as he was told, and when he used the finished mortar, rice cakes appeared inside as if by magic, multiplying endlessly. Hearing of this wonder, the greedy neighbour insisted on borrowing the mortar for himself. But when he tried it, only foul-smelling rubbish appeared instead. Enraged, the bitter man chopped the mortar into pieces and burned it as firewood. The kind old man, mourning the loss of yet another piece of Shiro, quietly asked for the leftover ashes to remember his beloved dog by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried the ashes home, a gust of wind swept some of them up and scattered them across the bare branches of a nearby cherry tree that had died years before. Instantly, the withered tree burst into full bloom, covered in soft pink blossoms. Just then, a powerful lord happened to be passing by and stopped in wonder. When the old man explained that his ashes could make dead trees blossom again, the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold and gifts. Hearing of his neighbour's fortune, the jealous man rushed to try the same trick, but his ashes blew straight into the lord's face, and he was punished instead of rewarded. The kind old man lived out his days in comfort and happiness, remembering his loyal Shiro with love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village tucked among the hills of Japan, a kind and gentle old man lived with his kind wife right next door to another old couple, whose selfish and unkind ways were well known throughout the village. The good old couple treated their small white dog, Shiro, as though he were truly a member of their family. One morning, Shiro suddenly began digging furiously at a spot near the vegetable patch, barking with excitement as though he had uncovered something important. Curious, the old man fetched his spade and dug where his loyal dog pointed, and to his astonishment, he uncovered a heavy wooden chest overflowing with gold coins. Word of this discovery travelled quickly, reaching the greedy neighbour next door, who hurried over to borrow Shiro, hoping the dog might sniff out an equally wonderful treasure. But when the neighbour dug at the spot Shiro pointed to, he found nothing but rotting rubbish. Consumed by fury and wounded pride, the cruel man struck poor Shiro, and the loyal little dog died soon afterward. Heartbroken, the kind old man carried his companion home and buried him beneath a young sapling in his garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, as the grieving old man slept, Shiro appeared to him in a dream, instructing him to cut down the young tree above his grave and carve its wood into a mortar, a wooden bowl traditionally used for pounding rice into sticky cakes. Trusting his friend completely, the old man did exactly as instructed, and when he used the finished mortar, warm rice cakes began appearing inside it as if by magic, multiplying endlessly. Hearing rumours of this wonder, the greedy neighbour insisted on borrowing the magical mortar, convinced it would bring him instant riches. But when he tried it, only foul-smelling rubbish came pouring out, spilling across his floor. Enraged, the bitter man chopped the innocent mortar into splinters and burned every piece as firewood. The kind old man, mourning the loss of yet another piece of his beloved Shiro, gently asked his neighbour if he might keep the leftover ashes to remember his dog by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried his bag of ashes homeward, a gust of wind swept a handful into the air and scattered them across the bare branches of a nearby cherry tree that had stood dead for years. Instantly, to everyone's astonishment, the withered tree burst into full bloom, its branches covered in soft pink cherry blossoms. At that moment, a powerful travelling lord happened to be passing with his procession, and he stopped in wonder at the extraordinary sight. When the humble old man explained that his special ashes could bring even long-dead trees back into blossom, the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold, fine silk robes, and precious gifts far beyond anything the modest old man had ever dreamed of. Hearing of his neighbour's astonishing fortune, the jealous man rushed eagerly to try the same trick, scooping up a handful of ordinary ashes from his own fireplace. But when he threw his handful into the air before the lord's procession, the ashes blew straight into the startled lord's face instead of onto any tree, and the foolish man was scolded and sent away in disgrace rather than rewarded as he had so greedily hoped. The kind old man, meanwhile, lived out his remaining years in quiet comfort and happiness, always remembering his loyal friend Shiro with deep love and gratitude.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the kind old man</li><li>Shiro</li><li>the unkind neighbour</li><li>the chest of gold</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shiro digs at a spot in the garden and finds treasure</li><li>The unkind neighbour borrows Shiro hoping for treasure too</li><li>The neighbour finds only rubbish and hurts Shiro in anger</li><li>The kind old man buries Shiro sadly under a tree</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>digging furiously at a particular spot</li><li>a heavy wooden chest overflowing with gold coins</li><li>came at once to borrow Shiro</li><li>nothing but rotting rubbish</li><li>struck poor Shiro</li><li>buried him tenderly beneath a young tree</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Shiro the dog helped the old man find a chest of ___, but the unkind neighbour got angry and hurt ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind old man and his wife lived next door to an unkind old man and his wife in a small Japanese village. The kind old couple owned a little dog named Shiro, whom they loved very much. One day, Shiro began digging at a spot in the garden and barking excitedly. When the old man dug there, he found a chest full of gold coins! The greedy neighbour saw this and borrowed Shiro, hoping to find treasure too, but when he dug, he found only rubbish. Furious, the unkind neighbour hit Shiro, and sadly, the little dog died. The kind old man was heartbroken and buried Shiro under a tree in the garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, a kind old man and his wife lived next door to another old couple whose selfish and unkind ways were well known throughout the village. The kind old couple treated their little dog, Shiro, like a true member of the family. One morning, Shiro suddenly began digging furiously at a spot in the garden, barking with great excitement. The old man dug where his dog pointed and, to his astonishment, uncovered a heavy wooden chest overflowing with gold coins. Word of the discovery reached the greedy neighbour, who came at once to borrow Shiro, hoping the clever dog might sniff out treasure for him too. But when the neighbour dug at the spot Shiro chose, he found nothing but rubbish. Consumed with fury, the cruel man struck poor Shiro, and the gentle little dog sadly died. Heartbroken, the kind old man buried his beloved companion beneath a young tree in his garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet village tucked among the hills of Japan, a kind and gentle old man lived with his kind wife right next door to another old couple, whose selfish and unkind ways were well known throughout the village. The good old couple treated their small white dog, Shiro, as though he were truly a member of their family. One morning, Shiro suddenly began digging furiously at a spot near the vegetable patch, barking with excitement as though he had uncovered something important. Curious, the old man fetched his spade and dug where his loyal dog pointed, and to his astonishment, he uncovered a heavy wooden chest overflowing with gold coins. Word of this discovery travelled quickly, reaching the greedy neighbour next door, who hurried over to borrow Shiro, hoping the dog might sniff out an equally wonderful treasure. But when the neighbour dug at the spot Shiro pointed to, he found nothing but rotting rubbish. Consumed by fury and wounded pride, the cruel man struck poor Shiro, and the loyal little dog died soon afterward. Heartbroken, the kind old man carried his companion home and buried him beneath a young sapling in his garden.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shiro's spirit</li><li>the mortar</li><li>rice cakes</li><li>the unkind neighbour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shiro appears in a dream and tells the old man to make a mortar</li><li>The mortar magically produces rice cakes for the old man</li><li>The unkind neighbour borrows it and gets only rubbish</li><li>The neighbour burns the mortar and the old man keeps the ashes</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>appeared gently to him in a vivid dream</li><li>carve its wood carefully into a mortar</li><li>delicious rice cakes appeared inside as if by pure magic</li><li>foul-smelling, rotten rubbish</li><li>chopped the innocent mortar into pieces and burned</li><li>keep something small to remember his beloved dog by</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Shiro's spirit told the old man to make a magic ___, and when the neighbour burned it, the old man kept the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, Shiro appeared to the old man in a dream and told him to cut down the tree and make a mortar, a wooden bowl for grinding rice, from its wood. The old man did as he was told, and when he used the mortar, rice cakes appeared inside it as if by magic! The unkind neighbour asked to borrow the mortar, but when he tried to use it, only foul-smelling rubbish appeared. Angry once again, he chopped the mortar into firewood and burned it. The kind old man asked for the ashes so he could keep something to remember his beloved Shiro by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, Shiro appeared to the old man in a dream, telling him to cut down the tree above his grave and carve its wood into a mortar, a wooden bowl used for pounding rice into cakes. The old man did as he was told, and when he used the finished mortar, rice cakes appeared inside as if by magic, multiplying endlessly. Hearing of this wonder, the greedy neighbour insisted on borrowing the mortar for himself. But when he tried it, only foul-smelling rubbish appeared instead. Enraged, the bitter man chopped the mortar into pieces and burned it as firewood. The kind old man, mourning the loss of yet another piece of Shiro, quietly asked for the leftover ashes to remember his beloved dog by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, as the grieving old man slept, Shiro appeared to him in a dream, instructing him to cut down the young tree above his grave and carve its wood into a mortar, a wooden bowl traditionally used for pounding rice into sticky cakes. Trusting his friend completely, the old man did exactly as instructed, and when he used the finished mortar, warm rice cakes began appearing inside it as if by magic, multiplying endlessly. Hearing rumours of this wonder, the greedy neighbour insisted on borrowing the magical mortar, convinced it would bring him instant riches. But when he tried it, only foul-smelling rubbish came pouring out, spilling across his floor. Enraged, the bitter man chopped the innocent mortar into splinters and burned every piece as firewood. The kind old man, mourning the loss of yet another piece of his beloved Shiro, gently asked his neighbour if he might keep the leftover ashes to remember his dog by.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the ashes</li><li>the dead cherry tree</li><li>the lord</li><li>the unkind neighbour</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wind blows the ashes onto a dead cherry tree</li><li>The tree instantly bursts into blossom</li><li>A passing lord rewards the old man with gold and gifts</li><li>The jealous neighbour tries the same trick and is punished instead</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>scattered them across the bare, lifeless branches</li><li>burst magnificently into full bloom</li><li>a powerful travelling lord</li><li>rewarded him generously</li><li>blew straight into the lord's face</li><li>scolded and punished</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the magic ashes made the dead ___ bloom, and the lord rewarded the old man, while the unkind neighbour was ___ instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried the ashes home, a strong wind blew some of them onto a nearby dead cherry tree. Instantly, the tree burst into beautiful pink blossoms! Just then, a lord happened to be passing by and was amazed by the sight. The old man explained that he could make dead trees bloom with his magic ashes, and the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold and gifts. The jealous neighbour tried the same trick, but his ashes only blew into the lord's face, and he was punished instead. The kind old man lived happily ever after, remembering his loyal friend Shiro with love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried the ashes home, a gust of wind swept some of them up and scattered them across the bare branches of a nearby cherry tree that had died years before. Instantly, the withered tree burst into full bloom, covered in soft pink blossoms. Just then, a powerful lord happened to be passing by and stopped in wonder. When the old man explained that his ashes could make dead trees blossom again, the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold and gifts. Hearing of his neighbour's fortune, the jealous man rushed to try the same trick, but his ashes blew straight into the lord's face, and he was punished instead of rewarded. The kind old man lived out his days in comfort and happiness, remembering his loyal Shiro with love.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the old man carried his bag of ashes homeward, a gust of wind swept a handful into the air and scattered them across the bare branches of a nearby cherry tree that had stood dead for years. Instantly, to everyone's astonishment, the withered tree burst into full bloom, its branches covered in soft pink cherry blossoms. At that moment, a powerful travelling lord happened to be passing with his procession, and he stopped in wonder at the extraordinary sight. When the humble old man explained that his special ashes could bring even long-dead trees back into blossom, the delighted lord rewarded him generously with gold, fine silk robes, and precious gifts far beyond anything the modest old man had ever dreamed of. Hearing of his neighbour's astonishing fortune, the jealous man rushed eagerly to try the same trick, scooping up a handful of ordinary ashes from his own fireplace. But when he threw his handful into the air before the lord's procession, the ashes blew straight into the startled lord's face instead of onto any tree, and the foolish man was scolded and sent away in disgrace rather than rewarded as he had so greedily hoped. The kind old man, meanwhile, lived out his remaining years in quiet comfort and happiness, always remembering his loyal friend Shiro with deep love and gratitude.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 4: Hanasaka Jiisan (The Old Man Who Made the Trees Bloom)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 4 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 4 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 5 (Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Shiro the dog helped the old man find a chest of ___, but the unkind neighbour got angry and hurt ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Shiro's spirit told the old man to make a magic ___, and when the neighbour burned it, the old man kept the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the magic ashes made the dead ___ bloom, and the lord rewarded the old man, while the unkind neighbour was ___ instead.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 5’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the old man and the oni as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the oni</li><li>the unkind neighbour</li><li>the lump</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man shelters from a storm and discovers oni dancing in the forest</li><li>He joyfully joins their dance and the oni are delighted</li><li>The oni magically remove his lump, believing it is something precious</li><li>The jealous neighbour tries the same trick and ends up with two lumps</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man had a large lump on the side of his face</li><li>The old man danced joyfully with the oni in the forest</li><li>The oni magically removed the old man's lump</li><li>The unkind neighbour's lump was also removed by the oni as a reward</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a village in Japan, there lived a cheerful old man who had a large lump growing on the side of his face. It did not hurt him, but he had lived with it his whole life. One day, while gathering wood, he was caught in a storm and took shelter inside a hollow tree in the forest. As night fell, he heard strange noises and peeked out to see a group of oni gathered in a clearing, dancing, drinking, and having a wonderful party. The old man loved to dance, and before he could stop himself, he jumped out and joined the dance, moving with such joy and skill that the oni were delighted.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief was so impressed by the old man's dancing that he begged him to come back and dance for them again the next night. To make sure the old man would return, the oni decided to take something valuable from him as a promise. Looking him over, they noticed his large lump and, believing it must be something precious since he carried it so carefully, they magically plucked it right off his face! The old man was overjoyed, for the lump was gone and his face was smooth at last. He hurried home to tell his wife the wonderful news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man's unkind neighbour also had a lump on his face and was jealous when he heard the story. He rushed into the forest that very night, hoping the oni would remove his lump too. When the oni appeared, the neighbour danced clumsily and badly, without any joy in his heart. The disappointed oni decided he was not the same excellent dancer from before, so as punishment, they stuck the first old man's lump onto the unkind neighbour's face as well, leaving him with two lumps instead of none! The kind old man, meanwhile, lived happily ever after with a smooth face and a wonderful story to tell.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, there lived a cheerful, good-natured old man who had carried a large, round lump on the side of his face for as long as anyone could remember. It caused him no pain, though the local children sometimes stared, and he had long since learned to laugh about it himself. One afternoon, while out gathering firewood in the forest, he was caught in a sudden storm and took shelter inside a hollow tree to wait it out. As darkness fell, he began to hear strange, lively noises, and peeking out from his hiding place, he spotted a crowd of oni gathered in a torch-lit clearing, laughing, drinking, and dancing with tremendous energy. The old man, who had always loved to dance, found himself unable to resist, and before he could stop himself, he leapt out and joined the astonished oni in their wild dance, moving with such genuine joy and skill that the whole crowd cheered him on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief was so delighted by the old man's dancing that he begged him to return and perform again the very next night. Determined to guarantee their new favourite dancer would come back, the oni decided to take something valuable from him as a promise. Studying him in the torchlight, they noticed the lump on his face and, mistakenly assuming it must be something precious, used their strange magic to pluck it cleanly off his face in an instant. Far from being upset, the old man was overjoyed, for the troublesome lump was finally gone, and his face felt wonderfully smooth. He hurried home through the dark forest as fast as he could, eager to share the astonishing news with his waiting wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's good fortune spread through the village, reaching his neighbour, an unkind and envious man who also carried a similar lump on his face. Burning with jealousy, the neighbour rushed into the forest that very night, expecting the oni to remove his lump just as easily. When the oni appeared once more, the neighbour attempted to dance, but his movements were clumsy, stiff, and joyless, performed only out of greed. Thoroughly unimpressed, the oni decided this rude stranger could not be the same wonderful dancer from before. As punishment, they stuck the first old man's discarded lump onto the neighbour's face as well, sending him home with two lumps instead of none. The kind old man, meanwhile, lived out his days with a smooth face and a wonderful story to tell for years to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village tucked among the mountains of Japan, there lived a cheerful, warm-hearted old man who had carried a large, round lump on the side of his face for as long as anyone in the village could remember. The lump caused him no pain, and although the local children occasionally stared at it, the good-natured old man had long since learned to laugh about it himself. One overcast afternoon, while gathering firewood deep in the forest, he was caught in an unexpected storm, and he hurried to shelter inside a large, hollow tree trunk to wait for the rain to pass. As darkness fell, he began to hear strange, lively noises drifting through the trees, and peeking out from his hiding place, he was astonished to spot a crowd of oni gathered in a torch-lit clearing, laughing, drinking, and dancing with wild energy. The old man, who had always loved dancing more than almost anything else, found himself unable to resist the joyful rhythm, and before he could stop his own feet, he leapt out from his hiding place and joined the astonished oni in their celebration, moving with such genuine joy and skill that the delighted crowd cheered him on with tremendous enthusiasm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief himself, thoroughly delighted by the old man's unexpected dancing skill, begged him to return and perform again the very next night, promising him an even warmer welcome. Determined to guarantee their new favourite dancer would keep his promise and return, the oni decided they ought to take something valuable from him first, as a kind of binding promise. Studying the old man by the flickering torchlight, they noticed the large lump on the side of his face and, mistakenly assuming it must be something precious, they used their strange, ancient magic to pluck it cleanly off his face in a single startling instant. Far from being upset, the old man was overjoyed, for the troublesome lump he had quietly carried his entire life was now finally gone, and his face felt wonderfully smooth for the very first time. He hurried home through the dark, dripping forest as fast as his legs could carry him, bursting with excitement to share the joyful news with his waiting wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's remarkable good fortune spread rapidly through the village, reaching his neighbour, a notably unkind and envious man who also happened, by strange coincidence, to carry a similar lump on his own weathered face. Burning with jealousy, this envious man rushed into the forest that very same night, fully expecting the oni to remove his own lump just as easily as they had done for the other old man. When the oni appeared once more in their familiar clearing, the envious neighbour attempted to dance energetically, but his movements proved clumsy, stiff, and joyless, performed grudgingly out of pure greed rather than any real love of dancing. Thoroughly unimpressed and rather insulted, the assembled oni quickly decided that this rude, greedy stranger could not possibly be the same wonderful dancer who had so genuinely delighted them the night before. As a fitting punishment for his greed and poor manners, the oni promptly stuck the first old man's discarded lump straight onto the neighbour's own face as well, sending him staggering home in the darkness with two ugly lumps instead of none. The kind, cheerful old man, meanwhile, lived out the rest of his contented days with a perfectly smooth face at long last, and he delighted in sharing his wonderful, unforgettable story with his grandchildren and the whole village for years to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the storm</li><li>the hollow tree</li><li>the oni</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man is caught in a storm while gathering firewood</li><li>He shelters inside a hollow tree in the forest</li><li>He hears noises and sees oni dancing in a clearing</li><li>He cannot resist and joins their dance</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a large, round lump on the side of his face</li><li>caught in a fierce, sudden storm</li><li>a large hollow tree</li><li>a whole crowd of oni gathered in a torch-lit clearing</li><li>leapt out of his hiding place</li><li>joined the astonished oni in their wild dance</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old man sheltered from a ___ inside a hollow tree, then saw ___ dancing and joined in.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a village in Japan, there lived a cheerful old man who had a large lump growing on the side of his face. It did not hurt him, but he had lived with it his whole life. One day, while gathering wood, he was caught in a storm and took shelter inside a hollow tree in the forest. As night fell, he heard strange noises and peeked out to see a group of oni gathered in a clearing, dancing, drinking, and having a wonderful party. The old man loved to dance, and before he could stop himself, he jumped out and joined the dance, moving with such joy and skill that the oni were delighted.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, there lived a cheerful, good-natured old man who had carried a large, round lump on the side of his face for as long as anyone could remember. It caused him no pain, though the local children sometimes stared, and he had long since learned to laugh about it himself. One afternoon, while out gathering firewood in the forest, he was caught in a sudden storm and took shelter inside a hollow tree to wait it out. As darkness fell, he began to hear strange, lively noises, and peeking out from his hiding place, he spotted a crowd of oni gathered in a torch-lit clearing, laughing, drinking, and dancing with tremendous energy. The old man, who had always loved to dance, found himself unable to resist, and before he could stop himself, he leapt out and joined the astonished oni in their wild dance, moving with such genuine joy and skill that the whole crowd cheered him on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful village tucked among the mountains of Japan, there lived a cheerful, warm-hearted old man who had carried a large, round lump on the side of his face for as long as anyone in the village could remember. The lump caused him no pain, and although the local children occasionally stared at it, the good-natured old man had long since learned to laugh about it himself. One overcast afternoon, while gathering firewood deep in the forest, he was caught in an unexpected storm, and he hurried to shelter inside a large, hollow tree trunk to wait for the rain to pass. As darkness fell, he began to hear strange, lively noises drifting through the trees, and peeking out from his hiding place, he was astonished to spot a crowd of oni gathered in a torch-lit clearing, laughing, drinking, and dancing with wild energy. The old man, who had always loved dancing more than almost anything else, found himself unable to resist the joyful rhythm, and before he could stop his own feet, he leapt out from his hiding place and joined the astonished oni in their celebration, moving with such genuine joy and skill that the delighted crowd cheered him on with tremendous enthusiasm.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the oni chief</li><li>the old man</li><li>his lump</li><li>his wife</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The oni chief is delighted and asks him to return the next night</li><li>The oni notice his lump and think it must be precious</li><li>They magically pluck the lump off his face</li><li>The overjoyed old man hurries home to tell his wife</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>thoroughly impressed and utterly delighted</li><li>begged him to return and perform for them again</li><li>mistakenly assuming it must be something precious</li><li>pluck it cleanly off his face</li><li>absolutely overjoyed</li><li>eager to share the astonishing news</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the oni chief asked the old man to return, and the oni magically removed his ___, leaving him overjoyed and eager to tell his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief was so impressed by the old man's dancing that he begged him to come back and dance for them again the next night. To make sure the old man would return, the oni decided to take something valuable from him as a promise. Looking him over, they noticed his large lump and, believing it must be something precious since he carried it so carefully, they magically plucked it right off his face! The old man was overjoyed, for the lump was gone and his face was smooth at last. He hurried home to tell his wife the wonderful news.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief was so delighted by the old man's dancing that he begged him to return and perform again the very next night. Determined to guarantee their new favourite dancer would come back, the oni decided to take something valuable from him as a promise. Studying him in the torchlight, they noticed the lump on his face and, mistakenly assuming it must be something precious, used their strange magic to pluck it cleanly off his face in an instant. Far from being upset, the old man was overjoyed, for the troublesome lump was finally gone, and his face felt wonderfully smooth. He hurried home through the dark forest as fast as he could, eager to share the astonishing news with his waiting wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The oni chief himself, thoroughly delighted by the old man's unexpected dancing skill, begged him to return and perform again the very next night, promising him an even warmer welcome. Determined to guarantee their new favourite dancer would keep his promise and return, the oni decided they ought to take something valuable from him first, as a kind of binding promise. Studying the old man by the flickering torchlight, they noticed the large lump on the side of his face and, mistakenly assuming it must be something precious, they used their strange, ancient magic to pluck it cleanly off his face in a single startling instant. Far from being upset, the old man was overjoyed, for the troublesome lump he had quietly carried his entire life was now finally gone, and his face felt wonderfully smooth for the very first time. He hurried home through the dark, dripping forest as fast as his legs could carry him, bursting with excitement to share the joyful news with his waiting wife.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the unkind neighbour</li><li>the oni</li><li>the dance</li><li>two lumps</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jealous neighbour hears the story and rushes to the forest</li><li>He dances clumsily and without any joy</li><li>The disappointed oni decide he is not the same dancer</li><li>They stick the first lump onto his face, leaving him with two</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>burning with jealousy rather than genuine joy</li><li>clumsy, stiff, and utterly joyless</li><li>unimpressed and rather insulted</li><li>could not possibly be the same wonderful dancer</li><li>stuck the first old man's discarded lump onto the unfortunate neighbour's face</li><li>two ugly lumps instead of none</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the jealous neighbour danced badly for the ___, so they stuck the lump onto his face, leaving him with ___ lumps instead of none.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old man's unkind neighbour also had a lump on his face and was jealous when he heard the story. He rushed into the forest that very night, hoping the oni would remove his lump too. When the oni appeared, the neighbour danced clumsily and badly, without any joy in his heart. The disappointed oni decided he was not the same excellent dancer from before, so as punishment, they stuck the first old man's lump onto the unkind neighbour's face as well, leaving him with two lumps instead of none! The kind old man, meanwhile, lived happily ever after with a smooth face and a wonderful story to tell.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's good fortune spread through the village, reaching his neighbour, an unkind and envious man who also carried a similar lump on his face. Burning with jealousy, the neighbour rushed into the forest that very night, expecting the oni to remove his lump just as easily. When the oni appeared once more, the neighbour attempted to dance, but his movements were clumsy, stiff, and joyless, performed only out of greed. Thoroughly unimpressed, the oni decided this rude stranger could not be the same wonderful dancer from before. As punishment, they stuck the first old man's discarded lump onto the neighbour's face as well, sending him home with two lumps instead of none. The kind old man, meanwhile, lived out his days with a smooth face and a wonderful story to tell for years to come.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's remarkable good fortune spread rapidly through the village, reaching his neighbour, a notably unkind and envious man who also happened, by strange coincidence, to carry a similar lump on his own weathered face. Burning with jealousy, this envious man rushed into the forest that very same night, fully expecting the oni to remove his own lump just as easily as they had done for the other old man. When the oni appeared once more in their familiar clearing, the envious neighbour attempted to dance energetically, but his movements proved clumsy, stiff, and joyless, performed grudgingly out of pure greed rather than any real love of dancing. Thoroughly unimpressed and rather insulted, the assembled oni quickly decided that this rude, greedy stranger could not possibly be the same wonderful dancer who had so genuinely delighted them the night before. As a fitting punishment for his greed and poor manners, the oni promptly stuck the first old man's discarded lump straight onto the neighbour's own face as well, sending him staggering home in the darkness with two ugly lumps instead of none. The kind, cheerful old man, meanwhile, lived out the rest of his contented days with a perfectly smooth face at long last, and he delighted in sharing his wonderful, unforgettable story with his grandchildren and the whole village for years to come.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 5: Kobutori Jiisan (The Old Man Who Lost His Lump)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 1: Apprentice Samurai (white belt) — focus: What IS a summary? Finding “who/what”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 5 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 5 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 6 (Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old man sheltered from a ___ inside a hollow tree, then saw ___ dancing and joined in.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the oni chief asked the old man to return, and the oni magically removed his ___, leaving him overjoyed and eager to tell his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the jealous neighbour danced badly for the ___, so they stuck the lump onto his face, leaving him with ___ lumps instead of none.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (Yellow belt) — Stories 6–10":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 6’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the old man and the crane as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the crane</li><li>the cloth</li><li>the forbidden room</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man rescues an injured crane caught in a hunter's trap</li><li>A young woman arrives and marries the old man</li><li>The woman secretly weaves beautiful cloth every night, warning him never to look</li><li>The old man breaks his promise, discovers she is the crane, and she must leave</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man rescued a crane caught in a hunter's trap</li><li>The young woman asked her husband never to look into the weaving room</li><li>The old man kept his promise and never looked into the room</li><li>The old man discovered his wife was the crane he had once rescued</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor but kind old man lived alone in a small house in the countryside of Japan. One winter day, while walking home, he found a beautiful white crane caught in a hunter's trap, injured and struggling. Feeling great pity for the bird, the old man gently freed it from the trap and watched it fly away into the snowy sky. That very night, a young woman knocked on his door, asking for shelter from the cold. The old man welcomed her in kindly, and before long, the two decided to live together and were married.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The couple was very poor, so the young woman offered to weave beautiful cloth to sell at the market. She asked her husband one important thing: he must never look into the room while she was weaving. Every night, she went into the room alone, and every morning, she came out with a stunning piece of cloth, more beautiful than anything the villagers had ever seen. The old man sold the cloth at the market for a good price, and the couple began to live comfortably. But as time passed, the old man grew curious about how his wife made such wonderful cloth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, unable to resist his curiosity any longer, the old man quietly peeked into the forbidden room. To his shock, he saw not his wife, but a crane, plucking out her own feathers to weave into the cloth! The crane noticed him watching and was heartbroken that her secret had been discovered and her promise broken. She explained that she was the very crane he had rescued and had come to repay his kindness. Because their promise had been broken, she could no longer stay. With sorrow in her heart, she transformed back into a crane and flew away, leaving the old man with a lesson about trust and keeping promises.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the quiet countryside of Japan, there lived a poor but exceptionally kind old man who worked hard yet had very little to his name. One bitterly cold winter's day, as he made his way home along a snowy path, he came upon a beautiful white crane caught helplessly in a hunter's cruel trap, struggling weakly and clearly injured. Moved by great pity, the old man carefully freed the crane from the trap, before watching it fly gracefully away into the falling snow. That very same night, as the old man sat alone by his small fire, a young woman appeared at his door, cold and shivering, asking humbly for shelter from the bitter weather. The kind old man welcomed her inside without hesitation, and as the days passed, the two eventually decided to live together as husband and wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Since the couple had very little money, the young woman offered to weave beautiful cloth to sell for a fair price at the village market. Before she began, however, she asked her husband to promise one important thing: he must never look into the room while she was weaving inside it. Every night, she disappeared alone into the small back room, and every morning she emerged with a stunning length of cloth, woven so finely that the villagers had never seen anything to match it. The old man sold each piece at market for an excellent price, and the once-poor couple began to live in real comfort. Yet as the weeks passed, the old man's curiosity about his wife's mysterious weaving grew harder to ignore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, unable to resist his curiosity, the old man crept to the forbidden room and peered through a gap in the door. To his shock, he saw not his wife, but the very same crane he had once rescued, plucking her own feathers and weaving them into the shimmering cloth. Sensing she was watched, the crane turned, and her heart broke, for her secret had been discovered and their promise now broken. Gently, she explained she was the crane he had rescued, and had come disguised as a woman to repay his kindness. But now that the promise was broken, she could no longer remain his wife. With deep sorrow, she transformed once more into a crane and flew slowly away into the grey morning sky, leaving the old man with a lasting lesson about trust and keeping promises.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in the quiet, snow-covered countryside of Japan, there lived a poor but truly kind-hearted old man who worked tirelessly from dawn until dusk yet possessed very little money or comfort. One bitterly cold winter's day, as he trudged home along a narrow, snow-covered path, he came upon a beautiful white crane caught helplessly in a hunter's cruel iron trap, struggling weakly and clearly suffering from a painful injury to its leg. Moved deeply by pity, the old man knelt carefully in the snow and gently freed the crane from the trap, before watching it rise gracefully into the air and disappear into the falling snow toward the distant hills. That very same night, as the old man sat alone beside his small fire, a young woman appeared unexpectedly at his door, cold and shivering, humbly asking whether she might shelter with him from the bitter weather. The kind-hearted old man welcomed her inside at once without hesitation, and as the days slowly passed and the two came to know one another, they eventually decided, quite happily, to live together as husband and wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Since the newly married couple possessed very little money between them, the young woman soon offered to weave beautiful, fine cloth that they might sell for a fair price at the bustling village market. Before she began her secret work, she asked her husband earnestly to promise one important thing: that he must never, under any circumstances, attempt to look into the small back room while she was weaving quietly within it. From that night onward, she disappeared alone into the little room every evening, and every morning she emerged carrying a stunning, shimmering length of cloth, woven with such extraordinary skill that none of the villagers had ever seen its equal. The old man carried each finished piece to market and sold it for an excellent price, and slowly but steadily, the once desperately poor couple began to live in genuine comfort for the very first time. Yet as the weeks stretched into months, the old man's burning curiosity about his wife's mysterious, secretive weaving grew stronger and harder to resist.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, finally unable to resist his overwhelming curiosity, the old man crept quietly toward the forbidden room and peered through a narrow gap in the sliding door. To his shock, he saw not his beloved wife seated calmly at the loom, but instead the very same white crane he had once rescued from the hunter's trap, carefully plucking her own delicate feathers one by one and skilfully weaving them into the shimmering cloth strand by strand. Sensing instinctively that she was being watched, the crane turned her head and noticed him at once, and her gentle heart broke instantly at the painful realisation that her closely guarded secret had finally been discovered and their sacred promise had now been broken forever. Gently and sorrowfully, she explained to her heartbroken husband that she was indeed the very same crane he had rescued from that cruel trap many months before, and that she had disguised herself as a woman purely to repay his extraordinary kindness. But now that their sacred promise had sadly been broken, she told him softly, she could no longer remain by his side as his loving wife, no matter how deeply she cared for him. With profound sorrow etched across her gentle face, she transformed once more into a graceful white crane before his very eyes and rose slowly into the pale morning sky, flying steadily away toward the distant mountains, leaving the heartbroken old man alone with a lasting, valuable lesson about the true importance of trust, patience, gratitude, and always keeping one's most sacred promises.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the crane</li><li>the trap</li><li>the young woman</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man finds a crane caught in a hunter's trap</li><li>He gently frees the injured crane</li><li>A young woman arrives at his door asking for shelter</li><li>The old man welcomes her and they marry</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>caught in a hunter's cruel trap</li><li>moved by great pity for the suffering bird</li><li>gently freed the crane from the trap</li><li>asking humbly for shelter from the bitter winter weather</li><li>welcomed her inside without hesitation</li><li>decided to live together as husband and wife</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old man freed a ___ from a trap, and later a young woman arrived asking for ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor but kind old man lived alone in a small house in the countryside of Japan. One winter day, while walking home, he found a beautiful white crane caught in a hunter's trap, injured and struggling. Feeling great pity for the bird, the old man gently freed it from the trap and watched it fly away into the snowy sky. That very night, a young woman knocked on his door, asking for shelter from the cold. The old man welcomed her in kindly, and before long, the two decided to live together and were married.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the quiet countryside of Japan, there lived a poor but exceptionally kind old man who worked hard yet had very little to his name. One bitterly cold winter's day, as he made his way home along a snowy path, he came upon a beautiful white crane caught helplessly in a hunter's cruel trap, struggling weakly and clearly injured. Moved by great pity, the old man carefully freed the crane from the trap, before watching it fly gracefully away into the falling snow. That very same night, as the old man sat alone by his small fire, a young woman appeared at his door, cold and shivering, asking humbly for shelter from the bitter weather. The kind old man welcomed her inside without hesitation, and as the days passed, the two eventually decided to live together as husband and wife.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in the quiet, snow-covered countryside of Japan, there lived a poor but truly kind-hearted old man who worked tirelessly from dawn until dusk yet possessed very little money or comfort. One bitterly cold winter's day, as he trudged home along a narrow, snow-covered path, he came upon a beautiful white crane caught helplessly in a hunter's cruel iron trap, struggling weakly and clearly suffering from a painful injury to its leg. Moved deeply by pity, the old man knelt carefully in the snow and gently freed the crane from the trap, before watching it rise gracefully into the air and disappear into the falling snow toward the distant hills. That very same night, as the old man sat alone beside his small fire, a young woman appeared unexpectedly at his door, cold and shivering, humbly asking whether she might shelter with him from the bitter weather. The kind-hearted old man welcomed her inside at once without hesitation, and as the days slowly passed and the two came to know one another, they eventually decided, quite happily, to live together as husband and wife.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the young woman</li><li>the cloth</li><li>the market</li><li>the forbidden room</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The woman offers to weave cloth to sell at the market</li><li>She asks her husband never to look into the weaving room</li><li>Every night she weaves and every morning brings beautiful cloth</li><li>The couple grows comfortable, but the old man grows curious</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>offered to weave beautiful cloth</li><li>never, under any circumstances, look into the room</li><li>a stunning length of cloth</li><li>sold each piece at the market for an excellent price</li><li>began to live in real comfort</li><li>curiosity about his wife's mysterious weaving</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the young woman wove beautiful ___ to sell, but made her husband promise never to look into the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The couple was very poor, so the young woman offered to weave beautiful cloth to sell at the market. She asked her husband one important thing: he must never look into the room while she was weaving. Every night, she went into the room alone, and every morning, she came out with a stunning piece of cloth, more beautiful than anything the villagers had ever seen. The old man sold the cloth at the market for a good price, and the couple began to live comfortably. But as time passed, the old man grew curious about how his wife made such wonderful cloth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Since the couple had very little money, the young woman offered to weave beautiful cloth to sell for a fair price at the village market. Before she began, however, she asked her husband to promise one important thing: he must never look into the room while she was weaving inside it. Every night, she disappeared alone into the small back room, and every morning she emerged with a stunning length of cloth, woven so finely that the villagers had never seen anything to match it. The old man sold each piece at market for an excellent price, and the once-poor couple began to live in real comfort. Yet as the weeks passed, the old man's curiosity about his wife's mysterious weaving grew harder to ignore.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Since the newly married couple possessed very little money between them, the young woman soon offered to weave beautiful, fine cloth that they might sell for a fair price at the bustling village market. Before she began her secret work, she asked her husband earnestly to promise one important thing: that he must never, under any circumstances, attempt to look into the small back room while she was weaving quietly within it. From that night onward, she disappeared alone into the little room every evening, and every morning she emerged carrying a stunning, shimmering length of cloth, woven with such extraordinary skill that none of the villagers had ever seen its equal. The old man carried each finished piece to market and sold it for an excellent price, and slowly but steadily, the once desperately poor couple began to live in genuine comfort for the very first time. Yet as the weeks stretched into months, the old man's burning curiosity about his wife's mysterious, secretive weaving grew stronger and harder to resist.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the crane</li><li>the promise</li><li>the sky</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man breaks his promise and peeks into the room</li><li>He sees a crane plucking its own feathers to weave</li><li>The crane explains she is the bird he once rescued</li><li>Because the promise is broken, she must fly away forever</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>unable to resist his growing curiosity</li><li>plucking her own delicate feathers</li><li>her secret had been discovered</li><li>the very crane he had once rescued</li><li>she could no longer remain by his side</li><li>flew slowly away into the grey morning sky</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the old man broke his ___ and saw the crane weaving, so she sadly flew away into the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, unable to resist his curiosity any longer, the old man quietly peeked into the forbidden room. To his shock, he saw not his wife, but a crane, plucking out her own feathers to weave into the cloth! The crane noticed him watching and was heartbroken that her secret had been discovered and her promise broken. She explained that she was the very crane he had rescued and had come to repay his kindness. Because their promise had been broken, she could no longer stay. With sorrow in her heart, she transformed back into a crane and flew away, leaving the old man with a lesson about trust and keeping promises.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, unable to resist his curiosity, the old man crept to the forbidden room and peered through a gap in the door. To his shock, he saw not his wife, but the very same crane he had once rescued, plucking her own feathers and weaving them into the shimmering cloth. Sensing she was watched, the crane turned, and her heart broke, for her secret had been discovered and their promise now broken. Gently, she explained she was the crane he had rescued, and had come disguised as a woman to repay his kindness. But now that the promise was broken, she could no longer remain his wife. With deep sorrow, she transformed once more into a crane and flew slowly away into the grey morning sky, leaving the old man with a lasting lesson about trust and keeping promises.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, finally unable to resist his overwhelming curiosity, the old man crept quietly toward the forbidden room and peered through a narrow gap in the sliding door. To his shock, he saw not his beloved wife seated calmly at the loom, but instead the very same white crane he had once rescued from the hunter's trap, carefully plucking her own delicate feathers one by one and skilfully weaving them into the shimmering cloth strand by strand. Sensing instinctively that she was being watched, the crane turned her head and noticed him at once, and her gentle heart broke instantly at the painful realisation that her closely guarded secret had finally been discovered and their sacred promise had now been broken forever. Gently and sorrowfully, she explained to her heartbroken husband that she was indeed the very same crane he had rescued from that cruel trap many months before, and that she had disguised herself as a woman purely to repay his extraordinary kindness. But now that their sacred promise had sadly been broken, she told him softly, she could no longer remain by his side as his loving wife, no matter how deeply she cared for him. With profound sorrow etched across her gentle face, she transformed once more into a graceful white crane before his very eyes and rose slowly into the pale morning sky, flying steadily away toward the distant mountains, leaving the heartbroken old man alone with a lasting, valuable lesson about the true importance of trust, patience, gratitude, and always keeping one's most sacred promises.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 6: Tsuru no Ongaeshi (The Crane Wife)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 6 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 6 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 7 (Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old man freed a ___ from a trap, and later a young woman arrived asking for ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the young woman wove beautiful ___ to sell, but made her husband promise never to look into the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the old man broke his ___ and saw the crane weaving, so she sadly flew away into the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 7’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the old man and the straw hats as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the straw hats</li><li>the Jizo statues</li><li>the New Year</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man weaves straw hats to sell for New Year money</li><li>Nobody buys his hats and he begins the walk home</li><li>He kindly places hats on six snow-covered Jizo statues, giving away his own too</li><li>The statues repay his kindness by leaving rice and treats at his door</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man wove straw hats to sell for New Year money</li><li>Nobody in town wanted to buy the old man's hats</li><li>The old man placed hats on six snow-covered Jizo statues</li><li>The Jizo statues left rice and treats outside the old man's home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor old man and his wife lived together in a small village in Japan. It was almost New Year, and they had no money for a proper celebration. The old man decided to weave straw hats, called kasa, and travel to the town to sell them so they could buy rice for the holiday. He walked for hours through the snow, but nobody wanted to buy his hats, and he began the long walk home with a heavy heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his way home, the old man passed six stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits, standing beside the road, completely covered in snow. Feeling sorry for the cold statues, the kind old man brushed the snow off each one and placed a straw hat on each of their heads to keep them warm. Since he did not have enough hats for all six statues, he even gave his own hat to the last one. He arrived home with no money and no rice, but he told his wife what he had done, and she agreed he had done the right thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, as the old couple slept, they heard strange sounds outside, like something heavy being dragged through the snow. In the morning, they opened their door to find sacks of rice, vegetables, and other New Year treats piled up outside their house, along with footprints leading away toward the road. The kind old man and his wife realised that the grateful Jizo statues had repaid his kindness. They celebrated a wonderful New Year after all, thankful for their good fortune and for the joy of giving to others.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small, snowy village in Japan, there lived a poor but hardworking old man together with his equally poor and kind wife. With the New Year quickly approaching, the old couple realised they had no money left for even the simplest holiday celebration. Determined to find a solution, the old man spent several days weaving a set of straw hats, known as kasa, planning to carry them into the nearby town and sell them so he might buy rice and treats for the coming holiday. He walked for hours through the deepening snow, offering his hats at every corner, but sadly nobody in the busy town seemed interested in buying even one. Disappointed and growing colder by the hour, the old man eventually gave up and began the long walk home through the falling snow, his heart heavy with worry about the empty New Year ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his weary way home, as the light began to fade, the old man passed six small stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits often found watching over travellers, standing silently beside the path and blanketed in thick, cold snow. Feeling a pang of sympathy, the kind-hearted old man stopped, brushed the snow from each one in turn, and placed one of his unsold hats gently on each statue's head to keep them warm through the night. Realising he had only five hats but six statues, the generous old man did not hesitate: he removed his own hat and placed it on the sixth statue instead. He finally arrived home empty-handed, with no rice or money, but when he told his wife what he had done, she smiled warmly and agreed he had done the right thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, as the couple lay sleeping, they were suddenly woken by strange, heavy sounds outside, almost as though something large were being dragged through the snow. They rose the next morning and opened their door to discover an astonishing sight: several large sacks filled with rice, vegetables, and New Year treats had been piled neatly outside their home overnight, with mysterious footprints leading away toward the road. The kind old man and his wife realised, with tears of joy, that the six Jizo statues had somehow come to life and returned to repay his kindness from the day before. Thanks to this wonderful gift, the old couple celebrated a truly joyful New Year after all, thankful for their good fortune and the joy of giving to others.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, snow-covered village nestled deep in the mountains of Japan, there lived a poor but hardworking old man together with his equally poor, gentle wife. With the New Year rapidly approaching and their small savings gone, the old couple realised with growing worry that they had no money left for even the most modest holiday celebration. Determined not to give up, the old man spent several days carefully weaving a set of sturdy straw hats, known traditionally as kasa, hoping to carry them into the bustling nearby town and sell them for enough money to buy rice, fish, and a few simple treats for the holiday. He trudged for hours through the deepening snow, offering his hats at every street corner, but sadly, despite all his effort, nobody in the busy town seemed interested in buying even a single one. Disappointed and growing colder, the old man eventually gave up and began the long, lonely walk back toward home through the softly falling snow, his heart heavy with worry about the bleak New Year that now seemed to lie ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his weary way home, just as the last light of the winter afternoon began to fade, the old man happened to pass six small stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits traditionally believed to watch over travellers, standing perfectly still beside the frozen path and blanketed under a thick layer of cold snow. Feeling a deep pang of sympathy, the kind-hearted old man stopped, carefully brushed the clinging snow away from each stone figure in turn, and placed one of his unsold hats tenderly on each statue's bare head to shield them from the freezing night ahead. Realising he had woven only five hats but there were six statues before him, the generous old man did not hesitate: he removed his own worn hat and placed it lovingly on the sixth and final statue instead, leaving his own head bare against the falling snow. He finally arrived home completely empty-handed, but when he explained to his wife exactly what he had done and why, she smiled warmly through her own disappointment and agreed wholeheartedly that he had done the right thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, as the tired old couple lay sleeping in their small, humble house, they were both suddenly woken by strange, heavy sounds coming from just outside their front door, almost as though something large and weighty were being dragged through the deep, crunching snow toward their home. Curious and a little nervous, they rose early the next morning and cautiously opened their front door together to discover an astonishing, heart-warming sight: several large sacks brimming with fresh rice, vegetables, dried fish, and other wonderful New Year treats had been carefully piled up just outside their home during the quiet night, with a trail of mysterious, snow-covered footprints leading away through the fresh snow toward the moonlit road beyond. The kind old man and his grateful, tearful wife realised at once, with tears of overwhelming joy welling in their eyes, that the six stone Jizo statues had somehow, impossibly, come to life during the night and returned to repay his simple, selfless act of kindness from the day before. Thanks entirely to this wonderful, unexpected gift, the humble old couple were finally able to celebrate a truly joyful New Year together after all, remaining deeply thankful for both their sudden good fortune and the simple, lasting joy that comes only from giving generously to others in genuine need.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the straw hats</li><li>the town</li><li>the New Year</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old couple have no money for New Year</li><li>The old man weaves straw hats called kasa</li><li>He walks to town hoping to sell them</li><li>Nobody buys his hats and he starts walking home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>no money left at all for even the simplest holiday celebration</li><li>weaving a set of straw hats, known as kasa</li><li>carry them into the nearby town and sell them</li><li>nobody in the busy town seemed interested in buying even a single one</li><li>his heart heavy with worry</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old man wove ___ to sell, but nobody in the ___ wanted to buy them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor old man and his wife lived together in a small village in Japan. It was almost New Year, and they had no money for a proper celebration. The old man decided to weave straw hats, called kasa, and travel to the town to sell them so they could buy rice for the holiday. He walked for hours through the snow, but nobody wanted to buy his hats, and he began the long walk home with a heavy heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small, snowy village in Japan, there lived a poor but hardworking old man together with his equally poor and kind wife. With the New Year quickly approaching, the old couple realised they had no money left for even the simplest holiday celebration. Determined to find a solution, the old man spent several days weaving a set of straw hats, known as kasa, planning to carry them into the nearby town and sell them so he might buy rice and treats for the coming holiday. He walked for hours through the deepening snow, offering his hats at every corner, but sadly nobody in the busy town seemed interested in buying even one. Disappointed and growing colder by the hour, the old man eventually gave up and began the long walk home through the falling snow, his heart heavy with worry about the empty New Year ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, snow-covered village nestled deep in the mountains of Japan, there lived a poor but hardworking old man together with his equally poor, gentle wife. With the New Year rapidly approaching and their small savings gone, the old couple realised with growing worry that they had no money left for even the most modest holiday celebration. Determined not to give up, the old man spent several days carefully weaving a set of sturdy straw hats, known traditionally as kasa, hoping to carry them into the bustling nearby town and sell them for enough money to buy rice, fish, and a few simple treats for the holiday. He trudged for hours through the deepening snow, offering his hats at every street corner, but sadly, despite all his effort, nobody in the busy town seemed interested in buying even a single one. Disappointed and growing colder, the old man eventually gave up and began the long, lonely walk back toward home through the softly falling snow, his heart heavy with worry about the bleak New Year that now seemed to lie ahead.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the Jizo statues</li><li>the snow</li><li>his own hat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man passes six snow-covered Jizo statues</li><li>He brushes the snow off each statue</li><li>He places a hat on each statue's head</li><li>He gives his own hat to the last statue</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>six small stone statues of Jizo</li><li>completely blanketed in thick, cold snow</li><li>brushed the snow from each one in turn</li><li>placed one of his unsold straw hats gently on each statue's head</li><li>removed his own hat from his head</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the old man brushed snow off six ___ statues and placed a hat on each, even giving away his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his way home, the old man passed six stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits, standing beside the road, completely covered in snow. Feeling sorry for the cold statues, the kind old man brushed the snow off each one and placed a straw hat on each of their heads to keep them warm. Since he did not have enough hats for all six statues, he even gave his own hat to the last one. He arrived home with no money and no rice, but he told his wife what he had done, and she agreed he had done the right thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his weary way home, as the light began to fade, the old man passed six small stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits often found watching over travellers, standing silently beside the path and blanketed in thick, cold snow. Feeling a pang of sympathy, the kind-hearted old man stopped, brushed the snow from each one in turn, and placed one of his unsold hats gently on each statue's head to keep them warm through the night. Realising he had only five hats but six statues, the generous old man did not hesitate: he removed his own hat and placed it on the sixth statue instead. He finally arrived home empty-handed, with no rice or money, but when he told his wife what he had done, she smiled warmly and agreed he had done the right thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On his weary way home, just as the last light of the winter afternoon began to fade, the old man happened to pass six small stone statues of Jizo, gentle guardian spirits traditionally believed to watch over travellers, standing perfectly still beside the frozen path and blanketed under a thick layer of cold snow. Feeling a deep pang of sympathy, the kind-hearted old man stopped, carefully brushed the clinging snow away from each stone figure in turn, and placed one of his unsold hats tenderly on each statue's bare head to shield them from the freezing night ahead. Realising he had woven only five hats but there were six statues before him, the generous old man did not hesitate: he removed his own worn hat and placed it lovingly on the sixth and final statue instead, leaving his own head bare against the falling snow. He finally arrived home completely empty-handed, but when he explained to his wife exactly what he had done and why, she smiled warmly through her own disappointment and agreed wholeheartedly that he had done the right thing.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old couple</li><li>the sacks of rice</li><li>the footprints</li><li>the New Year</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>That night, the couple hears strange sounds outside</li><li>In the morning, they find sacks of rice and treats at their door</li><li>Footprints lead away from the gifts toward the road</li><li>The couple realises the grateful statues repaid his kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>strange, heavy sounds coming from just outside</li><li>several large sacks filled with rice, fresh vegetables</li><li>a trail of large, mysterious footprints leading away</li><li>the six Jizo statues had somehow come to life</li><li>a truly joyful New Year after all</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the couple found sacks of ___ outside their door, left by the grateful ___ statues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, as the old couple slept, they heard strange sounds outside, like something heavy being dragged through the snow. In the morning, they opened their door to find sacks of rice, vegetables, and other New Year treats piled up outside their house, along with footprints leading away toward the road. The kind old man and his wife realised that the grateful Jizo statues had repaid his kindness. They celebrated a wonderful New Year after all, thankful for their good fortune and for the joy of giving to others.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, as the couple lay sleeping, they were suddenly woken by strange, heavy sounds outside, almost as though something large were being dragged through the snow. They rose the next morning and opened their door to discover an astonishing sight: several large sacks filled with rice, vegetables, and New Year treats had been piled neatly outside their home overnight, with mysterious footprints leading away toward the road. The kind old man and his wife realised, with tears of joy, that the six Jizo statues had somehow come to life and returned to repay his kindness from the day before. Thanks to this wonderful gift, the old couple celebrated a truly joyful New Year after all, thankful for their good fortune and the joy of giving to others.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, as the tired old couple lay sleeping in their small, humble house, they were both suddenly woken by strange, heavy sounds coming from just outside their front door, almost as though something large and weighty were being dragged through the deep, crunching snow toward their home. Curious and a little nervous, they rose early the next morning and cautiously opened their front door together to discover an astonishing, heart-warming sight: several large sacks brimming with fresh rice, vegetables, dried fish, and other wonderful New Year treats had been carefully piled up just outside their home during the quiet night, with a trail of mysterious, snow-covered footprints leading away through the fresh snow toward the moonlit road beyond. The kind old man and his grateful, tearful wife realised at once, with tears of overwhelming joy welling in their eyes, that the six stone Jizo statues had somehow, impossibly, come to life during the night and returned to repay his simple, selfless act of kindness from the day before. Thanks entirely to this wonderful, unexpected gift, the humble old couple were finally able to celebrate a truly joyful New Year together after all, remaining deeply thankful for both their sudden good fortune and the simple, lasting joy that comes only from giving generously to others in genuine need.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 7: Kasa Jizo (The Grateful Statues)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 7 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 7 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 8 (Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old man wove ___ to sell, but nobody in the ___ wanted to buy them.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the old man brushed snow off six ___ statues and placed a hat on each, even giving away his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the couple found sacks of ___ outside their door, left by the grateful ___ statues.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 8’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the tanuki and the kind man as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tanuki</li><li>the kind man</li><li>the monk</li><li>the street performer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A kind man rescues a tanuki from a hunter's trap</li><li>The grateful tanuki transforms into a teakettle and is sold to a monk</li><li>The heat of the fire makes the tanuki's disguise slip, frightening the monk</li><li>A street performer discovers the truth and the tanuki becomes a beloved performer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A kind man rescued a tanuki from a hunter's trap</li><li>The grateful tanuki transformed itself into a teakettle</li><li>The monk was delighted when the teakettle's disguise slipped in the fire</li><li>The tanuki and the street performer became a successful performing team</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind man rescued a tanuki, a Japanese raccoon dog, from a trap in the forest. The grateful tanuki wanted to repay the man's kindness, so he used his magical shape-shifting powers to transform himself into a beautiful, shiny teakettle. The man, not realising the teakettle was really his animal friend, sold it to a poor monk at the local temple, who was delighted to have such a fine kettle for making tea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, the monk placed the teakettle over the fire to heat water. But as the kettle grew hot, the tanuki could no longer keep up his disguise! Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with fur, paws, and a tail sticking out, he jumped up and scurried around the room in a panic, much to the monk's shock and surprise. The startled monk did not know what to make of such a strange sight and sold the unusual kettle to a travelling street performer instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever street performer soon realised the truth about the tanuki-kettle and, rather than being afraid, came up with a wonderful idea. He and the tanuki became a performing team, putting on a magical show where the tanuki would walk a tightrope and dance while looking like a teakettle with paws. People came from all around to see the amazing act, and the performer and the tanuki earned enough money to live comfortably. In the end, the tanuki returned to the kind man who had first rescued him, and they remained the best of friends for the rest of their days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the countryside of Japan, a kind and gentle man happened to rescue a tanuki, a clever Japanese raccoon dog known in folklore for its magical shape-shifting powers, from a cruel hunter's trap deep in the forest. Deeply grateful for having his life saved, the tanuki resolved to repay the man's great kindness somehow, and using his special magical abilities, he transformed himself entirely into a beautiful, gleaming copper teakettle, complete with a polished lid and a sturdy handle. The kind man, having absolutely no idea that the fine teakettle sitting in his home was actually his grateful animal friend in disguise, later sold it to a poor but hardworking monk at the local temple, who was overjoyed to finally own such a fine, handsome kettle for brewing his daily tea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, the unsuspecting monk carefully placed his new teakettle directly over the crackling fire to heat some water for his evening tea. But as the metal kettle steadily grew hotter and hotter, the poor tanuki inside could no longer bear the heat and simply could not maintain his clever disguise any longer. Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with soft brown fur, four furry paws, and a long bushy tail all sticking out at odd angles, the startled creature suddenly leapt up off the fire and scurried frantically around the temple room in a complete panic, much to the poor monk's utter shock and disbelieving amazement. The bewildered monk, having absolutely no idea what to make of such a bizarre and unsettling sight, quickly decided to sell the strange, unpredictable kettle to a travelling street performer who happened to be passing through the village that same week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever, quick-thinking street performer soon discovered the surprising truth about his unusual new tanuki-kettle, and rather than reacting with fear or alarm as the monk had done, he came up with a truly wonderful idea instead. Working together as an unlikely performing team, the man and the good-natured tanuki began putting on a delightful magical show for curious audiences, in which the shape-shifting tanuki would skilfully walk along a tightrope and dance gracefully while still cleverly disguised as a teakettle with small furry paws poking out. Word of the astonishing performance spread quickly, and people travelled from villages far and wide just to witness the amazing spectacle for themselves, allowing the performer and his tanuki partner to earn more than enough money to live comfortably together. In the end, once they had earned enough to be secure, the loyal tanuki finally returned to visit the kind man who had first rescued him from that terrible trap so long ago, and the two of them remained the very closest and happiest of friends for the rest of their long and eventful lives.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful countryside of Japan, a kind and gentle-hearted man happened one day to rescue a tanuki, a clever and mischievous Japanese raccoon dog long known throughout local folklore for its remarkable magical shape-shifting powers, from a cruel hunter's trap hidden deep within the forest undergrowth. Deeply grateful for having his life saved by this unexpected act of kindness, the resourceful tanuki resolved firmly to repay the man's generosity somehow, and calling upon his own special magical abilities, he transformed himself completely into a beautiful, gleaming copper teakettle, complete with an elegantly polished lid, a sturdy handle, and an altogether flawless, convincing disguise. The kind man, having no idea whatsoever that the fine teakettle now sitting quietly in his humble home was, in fact, his own grateful animal friend in disguise, later carried it to the local temple and sold it there to a poor but industrious monk, who was overjoyed to finally own such a fine kettle for brewing his daily tea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very same night, the unsuspecting monk carried his wonderful new teakettle over to the temple's small fireplace and placed it above the crackling flames to heat water for his evening tea. But as the metal kettle grew hotter and hotter, the poor tanuki hidden inside could no longer bear the heat, and could not maintain his once-flawless disguise for a moment longer. Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with soft brown fur sprouting from his sides, small furry paws, and a bushy tail whipping about at odd angles, the startled creature leapt clean off the fire and went scurrying frantically around the room in a panic, much to the poor monk's utter shock and amazement. The bewildered monk, having no idea what to make of such a bizarre sight, quickly decided to sell the strange, unpredictable kettle onward to a travelling street performer passing through the village that same week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever, quick-thinking street performer soon discovered the delightful truth about his strange new tanuki-kettle purchase, and rather than reacting with fear as the poor monk had done, he instead came up with a truly wonderful idea of his own. Working together thereafter as an unlikely but remarkably successful performing team, the man and his good-natured tanuki companion began putting on a delightful magical show for curious audiences everywhere they travelled, one in which the shape-shifting tanuki would skilfully balance and walk along a stretched tightrope and dance gracefully, all while remaining cleverly disguised for most of the act as an ordinary teakettle, with only small furry paws and the tip of a bushy tail occasionally peeking humorously out. Word of this astonishing performance spread rapidly from village to village, and delighted people soon began travelling from far and wide just to witness the extraordinary spectacle for themselves, allowing both the performer and his tanuki partner to earn more than enough money to live in genuine comfort. In the end, once the two of them had earned enough to feel truly secure, the loyal tanuki finally returned once more to visit the kind man who had first rescued him so long ago from that terrible trap, and from that joyful day forward, the two of them remained the very closest and happiest of lifelong friends for the remainder of their long, contented lives together.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the kind man</li><li>the tanuki</li><li>the trap</li><li>the teakettle</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kind man rescues a tanuki from a trap</li><li>The grateful tanuki wants to repay his kindness</li><li>The tanuki transforms into a shiny teakettle</li><li>The man sells the teakettle to a monk</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a cruel hunter's trap deep in the forest</li><li>resolved to repay the man's great kindness</li><li>transformed himself entirely into a beautiful, gleaming copper teakettle</li><li>having absolutely no idea</li><li>sold it to a poor but hardworking monk</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the kind man rescued a ___ from a trap, and it transformed into a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind man rescued a tanuki, a Japanese raccoon dog, from a trap in the forest. The grateful tanuki wanted to repay the man's kindness, so he used his magical shape-shifting powers to transform himself into a beautiful, shiny teakettle. The man, not realising the teakettle was really his animal friend, sold it to a poor monk at the local temple, who was delighted to have such a fine kettle for making tea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the countryside of Japan, a kind and gentle man happened to rescue a tanuki, a clever Japanese raccoon dog known in folklore for its magical shape-shifting powers, from a cruel hunter's trap deep in the forest. Deeply grateful for having his life saved, the tanuki resolved to repay the man's great kindness somehow, and using his special magical abilities, he transformed himself entirely into a beautiful, gleaming copper teakettle, complete with a polished lid and a sturdy handle. The kind man, having absolutely no idea that the fine teakettle sitting in his home was actually his grateful animal friend in disguise, later sold it to a poor but hardworking monk at the local temple, who was overjoyed to finally own such a fine, handsome kettle for brewing his daily tea.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful countryside of Japan, a kind and gentle-hearted man happened one day to rescue a tanuki, a clever and mischievous Japanese raccoon dog long known throughout local folklore for its remarkable magical shape-shifting powers, from a cruel hunter's trap hidden deep within the forest undergrowth. Deeply grateful for having his life saved by this unexpected act of kindness, the resourceful tanuki resolved firmly to repay the man's generosity somehow, and calling upon his own special magical abilities, he transformed himself completely into a beautiful, gleaming copper teakettle, complete with an elegantly polished lid, a sturdy handle, and an altogether flawless, convincing disguise. The kind man, having no idea whatsoever that the fine teakettle now sitting quietly in his humble home was, in fact, his own grateful animal friend in disguise, later carried it to the local temple and sold it there to a poor but industrious monk, who was overjoyed to finally own such a fine kettle for brewing his daily tea.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the monk</li><li>the fire</li><li>the tanuki</li><li>the street performer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The monk places the teakettle over the fire</li><li>The heat makes the tanuki's disguise slip</li><li>Half-kettle, half-tanuki, he scurries around in a panic</li><li>The startled monk sells him to a street performer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>placed his new teakettle directly over the crackling fire</li><li>could no longer maintain his clever disguise</li><li>scurried frantically around the temple room in a complete panic</li><li>utter shock and disbelieving amazement</li><li>sell the strange, unpredictable kettle to a travelling street performer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the heat from the ___ made the tanuki's disguise slip, so the monk sold him to a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That night, the monk placed the teakettle over the fire to heat water. But as the kettle grew hot, the tanuki could no longer keep up his disguise! Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with fur, paws, and a tail sticking out, he jumped up and scurried around the room in a panic, much to the monk's shock and surprise. The startled monk did not know what to make of such a strange sight and sold the unusual kettle to a travelling street performer instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very night, the unsuspecting monk carefully placed his new teakettle directly over the crackling fire to heat some water for his evening tea. But as the metal kettle steadily grew hotter and hotter, the poor tanuki inside could no longer bear the heat and simply could not maintain his clever disguise any longer. Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with soft brown fur, four furry paws, and a long bushy tail all sticking out at odd angles, the startled creature suddenly leapt up off the fire and scurried frantically around the temple room in a complete panic, much to the poor monk's utter shock and disbelieving amazement. The bewildered monk, having absolutely no idea what to make of such a bizarre and unsettling sight, quickly decided to sell the strange, unpredictable kettle to a travelling street performer who happened to be passing through the village that same week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>That very same night, the unsuspecting monk carried his wonderful new teakettle over to the temple's small fireplace and placed it above the crackling flames to heat water for his evening tea. But as the metal kettle grew hotter and hotter, the poor tanuki hidden inside could no longer bear the heat, and could not maintain his once-flawless disguise for a moment longer. Half-kettle and half-tanuki, with soft brown fur sprouting from his sides, small furry paws, and a bushy tail whipping about at odd angles, the startled creature leapt clean off the fire and went scurrying frantically around the room in a panic, much to the poor monk's utter shock and amazement. The bewildered monk, having no idea what to make of such a bizarre sight, quickly decided to sell the strange, unpredictable kettle onward to a travelling street performer passing through the village that same week.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the street performer</li><li>the tightrope</li><li>the audiences</li><li>the kind man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The street performer discovers the tanuki's secret</li><li>They become a performing team with a tightrope show</li><li>People travel from far away to watch the amazing act</li><li>The tanuki returns to visit the kind man who first rescued him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>discovered the astonishing and rather delightful truth</li><li>walk along a stretched tightrope</li><li>people travelled from villages far and wide</li><li>earn more than enough money to live comfortably</li><li>returned once more to visit the very same kind man</li><li>the closest and happiest of friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the tanuki and the performer put on a ___ show, and afterwards the tanuki returned to visit the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever street performer soon realised the truth about the tanuki-kettle and, rather than being afraid, came up with a wonderful idea. He and the tanuki became a performing team, putting on a magical show where the tanuki would walk a tightrope and dance while looking like a teakettle with paws. People came from all around to see the amazing act, and the performer and the tanuki earned enough money to live comfortably. In the end, the tanuki returned to the kind man who had first rescued him, and they remained the best of friends for the rest of their days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever, quick-thinking street performer soon discovered the surprising truth about his unusual new tanuki-kettle, and rather than reacting with fear or alarm as the monk had done, he came up with a truly wonderful idea instead. Working together as an unlikely performing team, the man and the good-natured tanuki began putting on a delightful magical show for curious audiences, in which the shape-shifting tanuki would skilfully walk along a tightrope and dance gracefully while still cleverly disguised as a teakettle with small furry paws poking out. Word of the astonishing performance spread quickly, and people travelled from villages far and wide just to witness the amazing spectacle for themselves, allowing the performer and his tanuki partner to earn more than enough money to live comfortably together. In the end, once they had earned enough to be secure, the loyal tanuki finally returned to visit the kind man who had first rescued him from that terrible trap so long ago, and the two of them remained the very closest and happiest of friends for the rest of their long and eventful lives.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The clever, quick-thinking street performer soon discovered the delightful truth about his strange new tanuki-kettle purchase, and rather than reacting with fear as the poor monk had done, he instead came up with a truly wonderful idea of his own. Working together thereafter as an unlikely but remarkably successful performing team, the man and his good-natured tanuki companion began putting on a delightful magical show for curious audiences everywhere they travelled, one in which the shape-shifting tanuki would skilfully balance and walk along a stretched tightrope and dance gracefully, all while remaining cleverly disguised for most of the act as an ordinary teakettle, with only small furry paws and the tip of a bushy tail occasionally peeking humorously out. Word of this astonishing performance spread rapidly from village to village, and delighted people soon began travelling from far and wide just to witness the extraordinary spectacle for themselves, allowing both the performer and his tanuki partner to earn more than enough money to live in genuine comfort. In the end, once the two of them had earned enough to feel truly secure, the loyal tanuki finally returned once more to visit the kind man who had first rescued him so long ago from that terrible trap, and from that joyful day forward, the two of them remained the very closest and happiest of lifelong friends for the remainder of their long, contented lives together.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 8: Bunbuku Chagama (The Lucky Teakettle)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 8 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 8 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 9 (Urashima Taro) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the kind man rescued a ___ from a trap, and it transformed into a ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the heat from the ___ made the tanuki's disguise slip, so the monk sold him to a ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the tanuki and the performer put on a ___ show, and afterwards the tanuki returned to visit the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 9: Urashima Taro":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: Urashima Taro</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 9’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 9: Urashima Taro) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Urashima Taro and the turtle as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Urashima Taro</li><li>the turtle</li><li>the Dragon Palace</li><li>the tamabako (box)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Urashima Taro kindly rescues a turtle from teasing children</li><li>The grateful turtle carries him to the undersea Dragon Palace</li><li>Urashima Taro stays for what feels like a few happy days as a guest</li><li>He returns home to find hundreds of years have passed and opens the forbidden box</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Urashima Taro rescued a turtle from children who were teasing it</li><li>The grateful turtle carried Urashima Taro to the undersea Dragon Palace</li><li>Urashima Taro stayed at the Dragon Palace for what felt like only a few days</li><li>Hundreds of years had actually passed by the time Urashima Taro returned home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind young fisherman named Urashima Taro lived in a village by the sea. One day, he saw a group of children teasing a small turtle on the beach. Feeling sorry for the poor creature, Urashima Taro gently stopped the children and set the turtle free, watching it swim happily back into the ocean waves. He thought nothing more of it and went back to his fishing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The next day, the very same turtle came back and spoke to him, thanking him for his kindness. The turtle offered to take Urashima Taro to visit a magical undersea kingdom called the Dragon Palace, far beneath the waves. Curious and excited, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's back, and together they swam down into a beautiful underwater world of coral, glowing lights, and gentle music. There, a lovely princess welcomed him warmly, and he stayed for what felt like just a few wonderful days, enjoying the marvels of the undersea kingdom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Urashima Taro began to miss his home and his mother, so he asked to return to the surface. The princess gave him a beautiful box, called a tamabako, as a farewell gift, warning him never, ever to open it. When Urashima Taro returned to his village, riding home on the turtle's back, everything had changed. His house was gone, his mother had passed away, and nobody recognised him. In fact, hundreds of years had passed while he had spent just a few days undersea! Overcome with sadness and confusion, Urashima Taro forgot the princess's warning and opened the box. A cloud of white smoke poured out, and in an instant, he became a very old man, understanding at last that time in the Dragon Palace moved very differently from time on land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small fishing village beside the sea in Japan, there lived a kind and good-natured young fisherman named Urashima Taro, well liked by everyone for his gentle and generous heart. One afternoon, as he walked along the sandy shore after fishing, he came upon a group of local children teasing and poking cruelly at a small, helpless turtle they had found stranded on the beach. Feeling great sympathy, Urashima Taro persuaded the children to stop, gently scooped the turtle up, and carried it back to the water's edge, setting it free to swim happily away into the ocean waves. Thinking little more of it, he gathered his gear and made his way home as usual.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very next day, as Urashima Taro sat by the shore, the same turtle reappeared and, to his astonishment, spoke to him directly, thanking him warmly for saving its life. As a reward, the turtle offered to carry him deep beneath the waves to visit a magical undersea kingdom known as the Dragon Palace, said to be more beautiful than anywhere on dry land. Curious and thrilled, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's shell, and together they swam down into a breathtaking underwater world of coral gardens, glowing lights, and gentle music. There, a beautiful princess greeted him warmly, and Urashima Taro remained her honoured guest for what felt like only a handful of wonderful, carefree days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Urashima Taro began to feel a quiet longing for his old home and his elderly mother, and so he asked the princess for permission to return to the surface. Before he left, she gave him a beautifully decorated box, known as a tamabako, as a farewell gift, warning him solemnly that he must never open it. Riding home on the turtle's back, Urashima Taro returned to his home village, only to discover, with mounting horror, that everything had changed beyond recognition. His house had vanished, his mother had long since passed away, and nobody recognised his face or knew his name. He came to understand that several hundred years had passed in the world above, though it had felt like only a few days below the waves. Overwhelmed with grief and confusion, he forgot the princess's warning and opened the box. A cloud of white smoke poured out, and in an instant, Urashima Taro became a very old man, finally understanding that time in the Dragon Palace had always moved differently from time on land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, peaceful fishing village beside the wide, restless sea in Japan, there lived a kind, hardworking, and good-natured young fisherman named Urashima Taro, well liked and respected throughout his village for his gentle manner. One afternoon, as he strolled homeward along the sandy shore after a long day spent fishing, he came upon a small group of village children cruelly teasing and poking at a small, terrified turtle they had found stranded on the beach. Feeling immediate sympathy for the frightened creature, Urashima Taro spoke kindly but firmly to the children until they stopped, gently scooped the turtle up, and carried it back to the water's edge, where he set it free to swim away happily into the rolling waves. Thinking little more of this simple act of kindness, he calmly gathered his fishing gear and made his way home for the evening exactly as he always did.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very next morning, as Urashima Taro sat resting by the shoreline, the same turtle he had rescued the day before suddenly reappeared out of the water and, to his complete astonishment, began speaking to him directly, thanking him sincerely for having saved its life. As a heartfelt reward, the grateful turtle offered to carry Urashima Taro on an extraordinary journey deep beneath the waves, to visit a magnificent, magical undersea kingdom known throughout old legends as the Dragon Palace, said to be even more beautiful than anywhere to be found on dry land. Filled with curiosity and excitement, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's broad shell, and together the unlikely pair swam steadily downward through the clear water into a breathtaking underwater realm filled with vivid coral gardens, shimmering lights, and the gentle sound of distant music drifting through the water. Deep within the grand undersea palace itself, a beautiful and gracious princess greeted him warmly and made him feel completely welcome, and Urashima Taro happily remained there as her honoured guest for what felt like only a handful of wonderful, carefree days, marvelling joyfully at the wonders surrounding him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, despite all the wonders surrounding him, Urashima Taro began to feel a quiet, growing longing for his old home, his simple fishing village, and above all for his elderly mother, and so he gently asked the kind princess for permission to return to the surface world above the waves. Before he departed on his long journey home, the princess presented him with a beautifully decorated box, known traditionally as a tamabako, offering it as a heartfelt parting gift, but warning him solemnly and repeatedly that he must never, under any circumstances, dare to open it once he returned home. Riding homeward once more upon the faithful turtle's broad back, Urashima Taro at long last returned to the shores of his home village, only to discover, with mounting horror, that everything around him had changed beyond all recognition. His family's house had vanished without a trace, his mother had long since passed away, and not a single soul in the village recognised his face or knew his once-familiar name. He gradually came to understand, with a dawning sense of dread, that several hundred years had somehow quietly passed in the world above the waves, while he himself had experienced what felt like merely a few short, carefree days visiting the wondrous Dragon Palace far below the surface. Overwhelmed by grief and a desperate longing for the simple life he had once known, Urashima Taro finally forgot the princess's solemn warning, and with trembling hands, he slowly lifted the lid and opened the mysterious box at last. A thick cloud of pale white smoke poured out immediately, and in a single startling instant, Urashima Taro transformed completely into a very old, frail, white-haired man, understanding at last, though far too late to change anything, that time within the wondrous Dragon Palace had always moved very differently from the steady passing of time on the ordinary land above.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: Urashima Taro</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Urashima Taro</li><li>the children</li><li>the turtle</li><li>the beach</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Children are teasing a small turtle on the beach</li><li>Urashima Taro stops the children</li><li>He gently carries the turtle back to the water</li><li>The turtle swims free and Urashima Taro goes home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>teasing and poking cruelly at a small, helpless turtle</li><li>feeling great sympathy for the frightened little creature</li><li>gently scooped the turtle up in his arms</li><li>setting it free to swim happily away</li><li>made his way home for the evening as usual</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Urashima Taro stopped children from teasing a ___ and set it free in the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind young fisherman named Urashima Taro lived in a village by the sea. One day, he saw a group of children teasing a small turtle on the beach. Feeling sorry for the poor creature, Urashima Taro gently stopped the children and set the turtle free, watching it swim happily back into the ocean waves. He thought nothing more of it and went back to his fishing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small fishing village beside the sea in Japan, there lived a kind and good-natured young fisherman named Urashima Taro, well liked by everyone for his gentle and generous heart. One afternoon, as he walked along the sandy shore after fishing, he came upon a group of local children teasing and poking cruelly at a small, helpless turtle they had found stranded on the beach. Feeling great sympathy, Urashima Taro persuaded the children to stop, gently scooped the turtle up, and carried it back to the water's edge, setting it free to swim happily away into the ocean waves. Thinking little more of it, he gathered his gear and made his way home as usual.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, peaceful fishing village beside the wide, restless sea in Japan, there lived a kind, hardworking, and good-natured young fisherman named Urashima Taro, well liked and respected throughout his village for his gentle manner. One afternoon, as he strolled homeward along the sandy shore after a long day spent fishing, he came upon a small group of village children cruelly teasing and poking at a small, terrified turtle they had found stranded on the beach. Feeling immediate sympathy for the frightened creature, Urashima Taro spoke kindly but firmly to the children until they stopped, gently scooped the turtle up, and carried it back to the water's edge, where he set it free to swim away happily into the rolling waves. Thinking little more of this simple act of kindness, he calmly gathered his fishing gear and made his way home for the evening exactly as he always did.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: Urashima Taro</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the turtle</li><li>the Dragon Palace</li><li>the princess</li><li>Urashima Taro</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The turtle returns and thanks Urashima Taro</li><li>It offers to take him to the undersea Dragon Palace</li><li>Urashima Taro rides the turtle down into the sea</li><li>The princess welcomes him and he stays for a few happy days</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>thanking him warmly for having saved its life</li><li>a magnificent, magical undersea kingdom known as the Dragon Palace</li><li>swam steadily downward into a breathtaking underwater world</li><li>a beautiful princess greeted him warmly</li><li>only a handful of wonderful, carefree days</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the grateful turtle carried Urashima Taro to the ___, where a kind ___ welcomed him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The next day, the very same turtle came back and spoke to him, thanking him for his kindness. The turtle offered to take Urashima Taro to visit a magical undersea kingdom called the Dragon Palace, far beneath the waves. Curious and excited, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's back, and together they swam down into a beautiful underwater world of coral, glowing lights, and gentle music. There, a lovely princess welcomed him warmly, and he stayed for what felt like just a few wonderful days, enjoying the marvels of the undersea kingdom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very next day, as Urashima Taro sat by the shore, the same turtle reappeared and, to his astonishment, spoke to him directly, thanking him warmly for saving its life. As a reward, the turtle offered to carry him deep beneath the waves to visit a magical undersea kingdom known as the Dragon Palace, said to be more beautiful than anywhere on dry land. Curious and thrilled, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's shell, and together they swam down into a breathtaking underwater world of coral gardens, glowing lights, and gentle music. There, a beautiful princess greeted him warmly, and Urashima Taro remained her honoured guest for what felt like only a handful of wonderful, carefree days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The very next morning, as Urashima Taro sat resting by the shoreline, the same turtle he had rescued the day before suddenly reappeared out of the water and, to his complete astonishment, began speaking to him directly, thanking him sincerely for having saved its life. As a heartfelt reward, the grateful turtle offered to carry Urashima Taro on an extraordinary journey deep beneath the waves, to visit a magnificent, magical undersea kingdom known throughout old legends as the Dragon Palace, said to be even more beautiful than anywhere to be found on dry land. Filled with curiosity and excitement, Urashima Taro climbed onto the turtle's broad shell, and together the unlikely pair swam steadily downward through the clear water into a breathtaking underwater realm filled with vivid coral gardens, shimmering lights, and the gentle sound of distant music drifting through the water. Deep within the grand undersea palace itself, a beautiful and gracious princess greeted him warmly and made him feel completely welcome, and Urashima Taro happily remained there as her honoured guest for what felt like only a handful of wonderful, carefree days, marvelling joyfully at the wonders surrounding him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: Urashima Taro</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tamabako (box)</li><li>his village</li><li>hundreds of years</li><li>the white smoke</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Urashima Taro asks to go home and is given a mysterious box</li><li>He is warned never to open the box</li><li>He returns home to find hundreds of years have passed</li><li>He opens the box and turns into an old man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a beautifully decorated box, known as a tamabako</li><li>warning him solemnly and repeatedly never to open it</li><li>everything around him had changed beyond all recognition</li><li>several hundred years had somehow passed</li><li>a thick cloud of white smoke poured out</li><li>transformed into a very old man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Urashima Taro returned home to find ___ had passed, and when he opened the forbidden ___, he became an old man.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Urashima Taro began to miss his home and his mother, so he asked to return to the surface. The princess gave him a beautiful box, called a tamabako, as a farewell gift, warning him never, ever to open it. When Urashima Taro returned to his village, riding home on the turtle's back, everything had changed. His house was gone, his mother had passed away, and nobody recognised him. In fact, hundreds of years had passed while he had spent just a few days undersea! Overcome with sadness and confusion, Urashima Taro forgot the princess's warning and opened the box. A cloud of white smoke poured out, and in an instant, he became a very old man, understanding at last that time in the Dragon Palace moved very differently from time on land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Urashima Taro began to feel a quiet longing for his old home and his elderly mother, and so he asked the princess for permission to return to the surface. Before he left, she gave him a beautifully decorated box, known as a tamabako, as a farewell gift, warning him solemnly that he must never open it. Riding home on the turtle's back, Urashima Taro returned to his home village, only to discover, with mounting horror, that everything had changed beyond recognition. His house had vanished, his mother had long since passed away, and nobody recognised his face or knew his name. He came to understand that several hundred years had passed in the world above, though it had felt like only a few days below the waves. Overwhelmed with grief and confusion, he forgot the princess's warning and opened the box. A cloud of white smoke poured out, and in an instant, Urashima Taro became a very old man, finally understanding that time in the Dragon Palace had always moved differently from time on land.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, despite all the wonders surrounding him, Urashima Taro began to feel a quiet, growing longing for his old home, his simple fishing village, and above all for his elderly mother, and so he gently asked the kind princess for permission to return to the surface world above the waves. Before he departed on his long journey home, the princess presented him with a beautifully decorated box, known traditionally as a tamabako, offering it as a heartfelt parting gift, but warning him solemnly and repeatedly that he must never, under any circumstances, dare to open it once he returned home. Riding homeward once more upon the faithful turtle's broad back, Urashima Taro at long last returned to the shores of his home village, only to discover, with mounting horror, that everything around him had changed beyond all recognition. His family's house had vanished without a trace, his mother had long since passed away, and not a single soul in the village recognised his face or knew his once-familiar name. He gradually came to understand, with a dawning sense of dread, that several hundred years had somehow quietly passed in the world above the waves, while he himself had experienced what felt like merely a few short, carefree days visiting the wondrous Dragon Palace far below the surface. Overwhelmed by grief and a desperate longing for the simple life he had once known, Urashima Taro finally forgot the princess's solemn warning, and with trembling hands, he slowly lifted the lid and opened the mysterious box at last. A thick cloud of pale white smoke poured out immediately, and in a single startling instant, Urashima Taro transformed completely into a very old, frail, white-haired man, understanding at last, though far too late to change anything, that time within the wondrous Dragon Palace had always moved very differently from the steady passing of time on the ordinary land above.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 9: Urashima Taro</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 9 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 9 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 10 (Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Urashima Taro stopped children from teasing a ___ and set it free in the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the grateful turtle carried Urashima Taro to the ___, where a kind ___ welcomed him.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Urashima Taro returned home to find ___ had passed, and when he opened the forbidden ___, he became an old man.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 10’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the farmer and the kappa as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the farmer</li><li>the kappa</li><li>the river</li><li>the dish of water</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The farmer finds a kappa struggling on dry land with an empty dish</li><li>He refills the kappa's dish with water, saving its life</li><li>The grateful kappa promises to repay the farmer's kindness one day</li><li>The kappa secretly helps water the farmer's fields every night, keeping his promise</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kappa's dish of water had tipped over and spilled</li><li>The farmer refilled the kappa's dish and saved its life</li><li>The grateful kappa promised to repay the farmer's kindness</li><li>The kappa secretly helped water the farmer's fields every night</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a hardworking farmer lived beside a river in Japan. One hot afternoon, he noticed a kappa, a strange river spirit with a shell on its back and a shallow dish of water balanced on its head, struggling on dry land far from the water. The kappa's dish had tipped, spilling out the water it needed to survive. Feeling sorry for the poor creature, the kind farmer quickly refilled the dish with fresh water from the river, saving the kappa's life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The grateful kappa promised the farmer that he would repay this kindness one day. The farmer thanked him and went back to his work, not expecting anything in return. Time passed, and the farmer often worried about his fields during the hot, dry summer months, when the crops needed plenty of water from the river to grow well.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his word, the kappa began secretly helping the farmer every single night. He guided extra water from the river into the farmer's fields and even helped catch fish for the family to eat. The farmer's crops grew taller and healthier than anyone else's in the village, and his family never went hungry. The farmer and the kappa became true friends, and the kappa kept his promise faithfully for the rest of the farmer's life, proving that honesty and kindness are always repaid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the countryside of Japan, there lived a hardworking farmer whose small house and fields stood beside a wide, slow-moving river. One especially hot summer afternoon, as he walked along the riverbank checking his fields, he noticed a strange creature struggling weakly on the dry ground some distance from the water: a kappa, a mysterious river spirit known in local folklore for the hard shell on its back, webbed hands and feet, and a shallow dish of water balanced carefully atop its head. To his concern, the kappa's dish had tipped over and spilled its precious water, and without it, the poor creature grew weaker by the moment, unable to move. Feeling immediate sympathy for the struggling kappa, the kind-hearted farmer hurried to the riverbank, filled his own cupped hands with fresh water, and carefully refilled the creature's dish, watching with relief as the kappa's strength returned almost at once.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deeply grateful for having his life saved in such a simple act of kindness, the kappa solemnly promised the farmer that he would repay this generous favour one day, whenever the opportunity arose. The farmer, being a modest and humble man, thanked the kappa warmly but returned to his daily work without expecting anything further in return for his small act of kindness. As the weeks passed, the long, hot summer months arrived in earnest, and the farmer began to worry, as he did every year, about whether his thirsty fields would receive enough water from the river to keep his crops growing strong and healthy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his solemn word, the kappa began quietly and secretly helping the farmer every single night while the rest of the village slept soundly. Under cover of darkness, he skilfully guided extra channels of water from the river directly into the farmer's dry fields and even caught fresh fish from the river to leave for the grateful family to eat each morning. Thanks to the kappa's faithful, secret help, the farmer's crops grew taller, greener, and considerably healthier than anyone else's fields in the entire village, and his family never once went hungry, even during the driest years. Over time, the farmer and the loyal kappa became true and lasting friends, and the kappa continued to keep his promise faithfully for the rest of the farmer's long life, proving to everyone who eventually heard the story that honesty, gratitude, and simple kindness are very often repaid many times over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet stretch of countryside in Japan, there lived a hardworking, honest farmer whose modest house and carefully tended fields stood close beside a wide, slow-moving river that wound its way gently through the valley. One particularly hot and airless summer afternoon, as he walked steadily along the familiar riverbank checking on the condition of his fields, the farmer suddenly noticed a strange, unfamiliar creature struggling weakly on the dry, sun-baked ground some considerable distance from the safety of the water: a kappa, a mysterious river spirit well known throughout local folklore for the hard, tortoise-like shell covering its back, its webbed hands and feet, and, most importantly, a shallow dish of water balanced carefully and precariously atop the very centre of its head. To his growing concern, the farmer quickly realised that the kappa's dish had somehow tipped over and spilled out every last drop of its precious water, and without that vital water to sustain it, the poor creature grew visibly weaker by the moment, barely able to move or lift its own head from the parched ground. Feeling an immediate and genuine rush of sympathy for the struggling, helpless kappa lying before him, the kind-hearted farmer hurried at once down to the riverbank, cupped his own two hands together, scooped up fresh, cool water, and carefully, gently refilled the creature's tilted dish, watching with quiet relief and satisfaction as the kappa's strength and colour returned to it almost immediately.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deeply and sincerely grateful for having his very life saved through such a simple, selfless act of kindness, the kappa solemnly promised the farmer, with great seriousness, that he would repay this generous favour one day, whenever a suitable opportunity should eventually arise. The farmer, being a genuinely modest, humble, and unassuming man by nature, simply thanked the kappa warmly for its kind words and returned calmly to his daily farm work without ever truly expecting anything further in return for such a small, ordinary act of everyday kindness. As the following weeks gradually passed by, the long, punishingly hot summer months arrived in earnest across the valley, and the farmer began to worry once again, exactly as he did every single year around that time, about whether his increasingly thirsty fields would manage to receive quite enough water from the slow-moving river to keep his precious crops growing strong, tall, and properly healthy throughout the difficult dry season ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his solemn word and promise, the loyal kappa began quietly, faithfully, and entirely secretly helping the unsuspecting farmer every single night, working tirelessly while the rest of the sleeping village remained completely unaware of his presence. Under the cover of darkness, the resourceful kappa skilfully and carefully guided extra hidden channels of clear water directly from the river into the farmer's parched, thirsty fields, and he even went to the trouble of catching plump, fresh fish from the river's depths to leave quietly for the grateful farming family to discover and eat each following morning. Thanks entirely to the kappa's faithful, tireless, and completely secret assistance, the farmer's crops grew noticeably taller, greener, and considerably healthier than absolutely anyone else's fields anywhere else in the entire village, and his family never once went hungry, even throughout the very driest and most difficult years that followed. Over the many years that followed, the honest farmer and his loyal, hardworking kappa friend gradually became true, deep, and lasting friends in every real sense of the word, and the faithful kappa continued keeping his original promise without fail for the rest of the farmer's long and prosperous life, proving conclusively to everyone in the village who eventually came to hear the remarkable story that simple honesty, heartfelt gratitude, and everyday kindness are, in the end, very often repaid many times over.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the farmer</li><li>the kappa</li><li>the dish</li><li>the river</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The farmer notices a kappa struggling on dry land</li><li>He sees that its dish of water has tipped over</li><li>The farmer refills the dish with fresh water</li><li>The kappa's strength returns and its life is saved</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a strange creature struggling weakly on the dry ground</li><li>a shallow dish of water balanced carefully atop its head</li><li>the kappa's dish had tipped over and spilled</li><li>carefully refilled the creature's dish</li><li>the kappa's strength returned almost at once</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the farmer found a ___ struggling on dry land and refilled its ___ with water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a hardworking farmer lived beside a river in Japan. One hot afternoon, he noticed a kappa, a strange river spirit with a shell on its back and a shallow dish of water balanced on its head, struggling on dry land far from the water. The kappa's dish had tipped, spilling out the water it needed to survive. Feeling sorry for the poor creature, the kind farmer quickly refilled the dish with fresh water from the river, saving the kappa's life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the countryside of Japan, there lived a hardworking farmer whose small house and fields stood beside a wide, slow-moving river. One especially hot summer afternoon, as he walked along the riverbank checking his fields, he noticed a strange creature struggling weakly on the dry ground some distance from the water: a kappa, a mysterious river spirit known in local folklore for the hard shell on its back, webbed hands and feet, and a shallow dish of water balanced carefully atop its head. To his concern, the kappa's dish had tipped over and spilled its precious water, and without it, the poor creature grew weaker by the moment, unable to move. Feeling immediate sympathy for the struggling kappa, the kind-hearted farmer hurried to the riverbank, filled his own cupped hands with fresh water, and carefully refilled the creature's dish, watching with relief as the kappa's strength returned almost at once.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet stretch of countryside in Japan, there lived a hardworking, honest farmer whose modest house and carefully tended fields stood close beside a wide, slow-moving river that wound its way gently through the valley. One particularly hot and airless summer afternoon, as he walked steadily along the familiar riverbank checking on the condition of his fields, the farmer suddenly noticed a strange, unfamiliar creature struggling weakly on the dry, sun-baked ground some considerable distance from the safety of the water: a kappa, a mysterious river spirit well known throughout local folklore for the hard, tortoise-like shell covering its back, its webbed hands and feet, and, most importantly, a shallow dish of water balanced carefully and precariously atop the very centre of its head. To his growing concern, the farmer quickly realised that the kappa's dish had somehow tipped over and spilled out every last drop of its precious water, and without that vital water to sustain it, the poor creature grew visibly weaker by the moment, barely able to move or lift its own head from the parched ground. Feeling an immediate and genuine rush of sympathy for the struggling, helpless kappa lying before him, the kind-hearted farmer hurried at once down to the riverbank, cupped his own two hands together, scooped up fresh, cool water, and carefully, gently refilled the creature's tilted dish, watching with quiet relief and satisfaction as the kappa's strength and colour returned to it almost immediately.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the kappa</li><li>the promise</li><li>the farmer</li><li>the summer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The grateful kappa promises to repay the farmer</li><li>The farmer thanks him and returns to work</li><li>Time passes and the hot summer arrives</li><li>The farmer worries about water for his crops</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>solemnly promised the farmer that he would repay this generous favour</li><li>without ever truly expecting anything further in return</li><li>the long, hot summer months arrived in earnest</li><li>worry once again</li><li>keep his precious crops growing strong</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the grateful kappa ___ to repay the farmer, who later worried about water during the hot ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The grateful kappa promised the farmer that he would repay this kindness one day. The farmer thanked him and went back to his work, not expecting anything in return. Time passed, and the farmer often worried about his fields during the hot, dry summer months, when the crops needed plenty of water from the river to grow well.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deeply grateful for having his life saved in such a simple act of kindness, the kappa solemnly promised the farmer that he would repay this generous favour one day, whenever the opportunity arose. The farmer, being a modest and humble man, thanked the kappa warmly but returned to his daily work without expecting anything further in return for his small act of kindness. As the weeks passed, the long, hot summer months arrived in earnest, and the farmer began to worry, as he did every year, about whether his thirsty fields would receive enough water from the river to keep his crops growing strong and healthy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Deeply and sincerely grateful for having his very life saved through such a simple, selfless act of kindness, the kappa solemnly promised the farmer, with great seriousness, that he would repay this generous favour one day, whenever a suitable opportunity should eventually arise. The farmer, being a genuinely modest, humble, and unassuming man by nature, simply thanked the kappa warmly for its kind words and returned calmly to his daily farm work without ever truly expecting anything further in return for such a small, ordinary act of everyday kindness. As the following weeks gradually passed by, the long, punishingly hot summer months arrived in earnest across the valley, and the farmer began to worry once again, exactly as he did every single year around that time, about whether his increasingly thirsty fields would manage to receive quite enough water from the slow-moving river to keep his precious crops growing strong, tall, and properly healthy throughout the difficult dry season ahead.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the kappa</li><li>the fields</li><li>the fish</li><li>the friendship</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kappa secretly guides extra water into the farmer's fields at night</li><li>He also catches fish for the family to eat</li><li>The farmer's crops grow taller and healthier than anyone else's</li><li>The farmer and the kappa remain true friends for life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>guided extra channels of water from the river directly into the farmer's fields</li><li>caught fresh fish from the river's depths</li><li>grew noticeably taller, greener, and considerably healthier</li><li>never once went hungry</li><li>became true, deep, and lasting friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the kappa secretly watered the farmer's ___ every night, and the two became true ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his word, the kappa began secretly helping the farmer every single night. He guided extra water from the river into the farmer's fields and even helped catch fish for the family to eat. The farmer's crops grew taller and healthier than anyone else's in the village, and his family never went hungry. The farmer and the kappa became true friends, and the kappa kept his promise faithfully for the rest of the farmer's life, proving that honesty and kindness are always repaid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his solemn word, the kappa began quietly and secretly helping the farmer every single night while the rest of the village slept soundly. Under cover of darkness, he skilfully guided extra channels of water from the river directly into the farmer's dry fields and even caught fresh fish from the river to leave for the grateful family to eat each morning. Thanks to the kappa's faithful, secret help, the farmer's crops grew taller, greener, and considerably healthier than anyone else's fields in the entire village, and his family never once went hungry, even during the driest years. Over time, the farmer and the loyal kappa became true and lasting friends, and the kappa continued to keep his promise faithfully for the rest of the farmer's long life, proving to everyone who eventually heard the story that honesty, gratitude, and simple kindness are very often repaid many times over.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>True to his solemn word and promise, the loyal kappa began quietly, faithfully, and entirely secretly helping the unsuspecting farmer every single night, working tirelessly while the rest of the sleeping village remained completely unaware of his presence. Under the cover of darkness, the resourceful kappa skilfully and carefully guided extra hidden channels of clear water directly from the river into the farmer's parched, thirsty fields, and he even went to the trouble of catching plump, fresh fish from the river's depths to leave quietly for the grateful farming family to discover and eat each following morning. Thanks entirely to the kappa's faithful, tireless, and completely secret assistance, the farmer's crops grew noticeably taller, greener, and considerably healthier than absolutely anyone else's fields anywhere else in the entire village, and his family never once went hungry, even throughout the very driest and most difficult years that followed. Over the many years that followed, the honest farmer and his loyal, hardworking kappa friend gradually became true, deep, and lasting friends in every real sense of the word, and the faithful kappa continued keeping his original promise without fail for the rest of the farmer's long and prosperous life, proving conclusively to everyone in the village who eventually came to hear the remarkable story that simple honesty, heartfelt gratitude, and everyday kindness are, in the end, very often repaid many times over.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 10: Kappa no Sara (The Kappa's Promise)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 2: Novice Swordsman (yellow belt) — focus: Finding key events and putting them in order</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 10 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 10 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 11 (Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the farmer found a ___ struggling on dry land and refilled its ___ with water.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the grateful kappa ___ to repay the farmer, who later worried about water during the hot ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the kappa secretly watered the farmer's ___ every night, and the two became true ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (Orange belt) — Stories 11–15":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 11’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the white rabbit and Yoshi as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the white rabbit</li><li>Yoshi</li><li>the crocodiles</li><li>the cattail reeds</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rabbit tricks the crocodiles into forming a bridge across the sea</li><li>The rabbit laughs and admits the trick, and the last crocodile hurts him</li><li>Unkind travellers give the hurt rabbit bad advice that makes things worse</li><li>Kind Yoshi teaches the rabbit how to heal, and they become friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The white rabbit tricked the crocodiles into forming a bridge</li><li>The last crocodile tore off the rabbit's fur when he admitted the trick</li><li>Yoshi told the rabbit to bathe in salty seawater</li><li>The rabbit and Yoshi became lasting friends after the rabbit healed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a clever white rabbit lived on a small island and wished very much to visit the mainland across the sea. Rabbits cannot swim such a long way, so the rabbit came up with a trick. He called out to a line of crocodiles resting in the water and told them he wanted to compare how many crocodiles lived in the sea against how many rabbits lived on the land. He asked them to line up in a row stretching all the way to the shore so he could hop across and count them. The curious crocodiles agreed, and the rabbit hopped from back to back, counting happily as he went.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just as he reached the very last crocodile, almost at the shore, the rabbit could not help laughing and admitted it was only a trick to cross the sea, and there was no real contest at all. The last crocodile was furious and snapped at the rabbit, tearing off his soft white fur and leaving him hurt and crying on the sand. Some unkind travellers passed by and told him to bathe in salty seawater, which only made the pain worse.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came along the path and stopped when he saw the rabbit crying. He listened gently to what had happened and told the rabbit to wash in fresh river water and roll in the soft cattail seeds growing nearby. The rabbit did exactly as he was told, and slowly his fur grew back soft and white again. Grateful for such kindness and wisdom, the rabbit thanked Yoshi warmly, and the two became lasting friends, with the rabbit always remembering that honesty matters more than clever tricks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, on a small island surrounded by sea, there lived a clever white rabbit who wished more than anything to cross over to the mainland and see the wider world. A wide stretch of ocean lay between the island and the shore, and no rabbit could hope to swim so far. One day, watching a long line of crocodiles resting in the water nearby, the rabbit came up with a bold idea. He called out, telling the crocodiles he wanted to settle an argument about which animal had more of its kind, crocodiles in the sea or rabbits on the land. He asked them to line up in a neat row stretching all the way to the shore, so he could hop across their backs and count every one. Delighted by the strange contest, the crocodiles agreed and arranged themselves in a long line across the water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopping eagerly from back to back, the rabbit counted aloud as he went, drawing closer to the mainland shore. Just as he reached the very last crocodile, with the sand almost within reach, the rabbit could not resist laughing and blurted out the truth: there had never been a real contest at all, and he had only used the crocodiles as a living bridge. Furious at being tricked, the last crocodile snapped its jaws at the rabbit and tore away his soft white fur, leaving him crying in pain on the sand. Some rough, unkind travellers came walking along the shore soon after, and when the hurt rabbit asked for help, they told him to bathe in salty seawater, advice that only made his wounds burn worse.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some time later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came walking along the same path, well behind his rougher companions. He noticed the crying rabbit and stopped, asking gently what had happened. When the rabbit explained everything, from his trick to the crocodile's fury to the cruel advice he had already tried, Yoshi did not scold him for the trick he had played. Instead, he told the rabbit kindly to wash in fresh water from the nearby river, then to roll gently in the soft, fluffy seeds of the cattail reeds growing along the bank, which would soothe his sore skin far better than salt water ever could. The rabbit followed his instructions exactly, and slowly, over the following days, his pain eased and his soft white fur grew back thicker than before. Overwhelmed with gratitude, the rabbit thanked Yoshi again and again, and from that day the two remained the closest of friends, the rabbit never forgetting that kindness and honesty are worth far more than any clever trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, on a small green island surrounded by sea, there lived a clever white rabbit who wished more than anything to cross over to the mainland and see the wider world beyond his little shore. A wide, deep stretch of ocean separated the island from the coast, and no rabbit, however determined, could hope to swim such a distance safely. One afternoon, while watching a long line of crocodiles resting lazily in the shallow water nearby, the rabbit came up with a bold and rather sneaky idea. He called out cheerfully, telling the crocodiles he wanted to settle a friendly argument once and for all about which creatures were more numerous, crocodiles living in the sea or rabbits living on the land. He asked them to line up in a long, neat row stretching all the way from the island to the distant shore, so he could hop carefully across their backs and count every single one for the contest. Delighted and curious about this unusual challenge, the crocodiles agreed at once and arranged themselves obediently in a long, unbroken line stretching across the water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopping nimbly from back to back, the rabbit counted aloud with each leap, drawing closer to the mainland shore with every crocodile he passed. Just as he reached the very last crocodile, with the warm sand almost within his reach, the rabbit's pride got the better of him, and he could not resist laughing out loud and admitting the truth: there had never been any real contest at all, and he had simply used the whole line of crocodiles as a clever, living bridge to cross the sea for free. Furious at being so thoroughly tricked, the last crocodile snapped its powerful jaws shut around the rabbit just as he leapt for shore, tearing away nearly all of his soft white fur and leaving him crying in pain on the sand, his skin raw and stinging in the sea air. Not long afterward, a group of rough, unkind travellers came strolling along the coastal path, and when the hurt rabbit called out weakly for help, they laughed and told him to bathe in the salty seawater and lie exposed in the drying wind, cruel advice that only made his painful wounds burn and sting far worse than before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some time later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came wandering along the very same coastal path, trailing quite far behind his rougher, hastier companions. He noticed the small, trembling rabbit crying weakly on the sand and stopped at once, kneeling down beside him and asking gently what had happened to leave him in such a state. When the rabbit tearfully explained the whole story, from his clever trick on the crocodiles to the last crocodile's fury to the cruel, useless advice he had already tried following, Yoshi did not scold him harshly for the trick he had played. Instead, he spoke to the rabbit kindly and patiently, telling him to wash himself gently in the cool, fresh water of the nearby river to rinse away the salt and stinging sand, and then to roll carefully in the soft, fluffy white seeds of the cattail reeds growing thickly along the riverbank, which he explained would soothe and protect his sore, bare skin far better than salt water ever could. The rabbit followed every one of Yoshi's careful instructions exactly, and slowly, over the days that followed, his terrible pain eased away and his soft white fur grew back thicker and cleaner than it had ever been before. Overwhelmed with gratitude for such patient kindness and wisdom, the rabbit thanked Yoshi again and again, and from that day onward the two remained the very closest of friends, with the rabbit never once forgetting the important lesson he had learned: that true kindness and plain honesty are worth far more than any clever trick.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the white rabbit</li><li>the crocodiles</li><li>the island</li><li>the mainland</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rabbit wants to cross the sea to the mainland</li><li>He asks the crocodiles to line up like a bridge</li><li>He tells them he wants to count the two groups</li><li>The crocodiles agree and line up across the water</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>wished more than anything to cross over to the mainland</li><li>no rabbit, however determined, could hope to swim such a distance</li><li>settle a friendly argument once and for all</li><li>line up in a long, neat row stretching all the way</li><li>delighted and curious about this unusual challenge</li><li>arranged themselves obediently in a long, unbroken line</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the clever ___ tricked the ___ into forming a bridge so he could cross to the mainland.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a clever white rabbit lived on a small island and wished very much to visit the mainland across the sea. Rabbits cannot swim such a long way, so the rabbit came up with a trick. He called out to a line of crocodiles resting in the water and told them he wanted to compare how many crocodiles lived in the sea against how many rabbits lived on the land. He asked them to line up in a row stretching all the way to the shore so he could hop across and count them. The curious crocodiles agreed, and the rabbit hopped from back to back, counting happily as he went.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, on a small island surrounded by sea, there lived a clever white rabbit who wished more than anything to cross over to the mainland and see the wider world. A wide stretch of ocean lay between the island and the shore, and no rabbit could hope to swim so far. One day, watching a long line of crocodiles resting in the water nearby, the rabbit came up with a bold idea. He called out, telling the crocodiles he wanted to settle an argument about which animal had more of its kind, crocodiles in the sea or rabbits on the land. He asked them to line up in a neat row stretching all the way to the shore, so he could hop across their backs and count every one. Delighted by the strange contest, the crocodiles agreed and arranged themselves in a long line across the water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, on a small green island surrounded by sea, there lived a clever white rabbit who wished more than anything to cross over to the mainland and see the wider world beyond his little shore. A wide, deep stretch of ocean separated the island from the coast, and no rabbit, however determined, could hope to swim such a distance safely. One afternoon, while watching a long line of crocodiles resting lazily in the shallow water nearby, the rabbit came up with a bold and rather sneaky idea. He called out cheerfully, telling the crocodiles he wanted to settle a friendly argument once and for all about which creatures were more numerous, crocodiles living in the sea or rabbits living on the land. He asked them to line up in a long, neat row stretching all the way from the island to the distant shore, so he could hop carefully across their backs and count every single one for the contest. Delighted and curious about this unusual challenge, the crocodiles agreed at once and arranged themselves obediently in a long, unbroken line stretching across the water.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the rabbit</li><li>the last crocodile</li><li>the fur</li><li>the sand</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rabbit reaches the last crocodile, almost at the shore</li><li>He laughs and admits it was only a trick</li><li>The furious crocodile tears off the rabbit's fur</li><li>Unkind travellers tell him to bathe in salty seawater</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the rabbit's pride got the better of him</li><li>there had never been any real contest at all</li><li>used the whole line of crocodiles as a clever, living bridge</li><li>tearing away nearly all of his soft white fur</li><li>cruel advice that only made his painful wounds burn worse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the rabbit admitted his ___, and the angry crocodile tore off his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Just as he reached the very last crocodile, almost at the shore, the rabbit could not help laughing and admitted it was only a trick to cross the sea, and there was no real contest at all. The last crocodile was furious and snapped at the rabbit, tearing off his soft white fur and leaving him hurt and crying on the sand. Some unkind travellers passed by and told him to bathe in salty seawater, which only made the pain worse.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopping eagerly from back to back, the rabbit counted aloud as he went, drawing closer to the mainland shore. Just as he reached the very last crocodile, with the sand almost within reach, the rabbit could not resist laughing and blurted out the truth: there had never been a real contest at all, and he had only used the crocodiles as a living bridge. Furious at being tricked, the last crocodile snapped its jaws at the rabbit and tore away his soft white fur, leaving him crying in pain on the sand. Some rough, unkind travellers came walking along the shore soon after, and when the hurt rabbit asked for help, they told him to bathe in salty seawater, advice that only made his wounds burn worse.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopping nimbly from back to back, the rabbit counted aloud with each leap, drawing closer to the mainland shore with every crocodile he passed. Just as he reached the very last crocodile, with the warm sand almost within his reach, the rabbit's pride got the better of him, and he could not resist laughing out loud and admitting the truth: there had never been any real contest at all, and he had simply used the whole line of crocodiles as a clever, living bridge to cross the sea for free. Furious at being so thoroughly tricked, the last crocodile snapped its powerful jaws shut around the rabbit just as he leapt for shore, tearing away nearly all of his soft white fur and leaving him crying in pain on the sand, his skin raw and stinging in the sea air. Not long afterward, a group of rough, unkind travellers came strolling along the coastal path, and when the hurt rabbit called out weakly for help, they laughed and told him to bathe in the salty seawater and lie exposed in the drying wind, cruel advice that only made his painful wounds burn and sting far worse than before.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshi</li><li>the river</li><li>the cattail reeds</li><li>the fur</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kind Yoshi finds the crying rabbit and asks what happened</li><li>He tells the rabbit to wash in the fresh river water</li><li>He tells the rabbit to roll in the soft cattail seeds</li><li>The rabbit heals and the two become close friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>trailing quite far behind his rougher, hastier companions</li><li>did not scold him harshly for the trick he had played</li><li>wash himself gently in the cool, fresh water of the nearby river</li><li>roll carefully in the soft, fluffy white seeds of the cattail reeds</li><li>soothe and protect his sore, bare skin far better than salt water</li><li>the two remained the very closest of friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Yoshi told the rabbit to wash in the ___ and roll in soft ___, and the two became lasting friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came along the path and stopped when he saw the rabbit crying. He listened gently to what had happened and told the rabbit to wash in fresh river water and roll in the soft cattail seeds growing nearby. The rabbit did exactly as he was told, and slowly his fur grew back soft and white again. Grateful for such kindness and wisdom, the rabbit thanked Yoshi warmly, and the two became lasting friends, with the rabbit always remembering that honesty matters more than clever tricks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some time later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came walking along the same path, well behind his rougher companions. He noticed the crying rabbit and stopped, asking gently what had happened. When the rabbit explained everything, from his trick to the crocodile's fury to the cruel advice he had already tried, Yoshi did not scold him for the trick he had played. Instead, he told the rabbit kindly to wash in fresh water from the nearby river, then to roll gently in the soft, fluffy seeds of the cattail reeds growing along the bank, which would soothe his sore skin far better than salt water ever could. The rabbit followed his instructions exactly, and slowly, over the following days, his pain eased and his soft white fur grew back thicker than before. Overwhelmed with gratitude, the rabbit thanked Yoshi again and again, and from that day the two remained the closest of friends, the rabbit never forgetting that kindness and honesty are worth far more than any clever trick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some time later, a kind young traveller named Yoshi came wandering along the very same coastal path, trailing quite far behind his rougher, hastier companions. He noticed the small, trembling rabbit crying weakly on the sand and stopped at once, kneeling down beside him and asking gently what had happened to leave him in such a state. When the rabbit tearfully explained the whole story, from his clever trick on the crocodiles to the last crocodile's fury to the cruel, useless advice he had already tried following, Yoshi did not scold him harshly for the trick he had played. Instead, he spoke to the rabbit kindly and patiently, telling him to wash himself gently in the cool, fresh water of the nearby river to rinse away the salt and stinging sand, and then to roll carefully in the soft, fluffy white seeds of the cattail reeds growing thickly along the riverbank, which he explained would soothe and protect his sore, bare skin far better than salt water ever could. The rabbit followed every one of Yoshi's careful instructions exactly, and slowly, over the days that followed, his terrible pain eased away and his soft white fur grew back thicker and cleaner than it had ever been before. Overwhelmed with gratitude for such patient kindness and wisdom, the rabbit thanked Yoshi again and again, and from that day onward the two remained the very closest of friends, with the rabbit never once forgetting the important lesson he had learned: that true kindness and plain honesty are worth far more than any clever trick.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 11: Inaba no Shirousagi (The White Rabbit of Inaba)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 11 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 11 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 12 (The Boy Who Drew Cats) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the clever ___ tricked the ___ into forming a bridge so he could cross to the mainland.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the rabbit admitted his ___, and the angry crocodile tore off his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Yoshi told the rabbit to wash in the ___ and roll in soft ___, and the two became lasting friends.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 12’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the boy and the priest as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the boy</li><li>the priest</li><li>the temple</li><li>the rat-goblin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boy draws cats everywhere and is sent to study at a temple</li><li>The priest sends him away with strange advice about small places</li><li>The boy shelters in an abandoned temple and sleeps in a small closet</li><li>His drawn cats come to life in the night and defeat the goblin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boy loved drawing cats more than anything else</li><li>The priest told the boy to avoid large places at night</li><li>The boy slept in the wide open main hall of the temple</li><li>The boy's drawn cats came to life and fought the goblin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small Japanese village, there lived a boy who loved to draw cats more than anything else in the world. He drew cats on paper, on walls, and even on the family's screens, no matter how often his parents told him to stop. His parents worried he would never learn anything useful, so they sent him to live and study at a temple with a wise old priest. The priest hoped the boy would learn to read and write properly, but the boy still could not stop drawing cats every chance he got, filling scrap paper and even the temple's walls with his sketches.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, the priest gently told the boy that he was not suited to become a priest after all, but gave him some kind advice before sending him on his way: \"Avoid large places at night. Keep to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand what this meant, but he thanked the priest and set off to find somewhere else to stay. Wandering through the dark countryside, he came upon what looked like an empty temple and decided to shelter there for the night, not realising that a dangerous goblin was said to live inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the priest's strange advice, the boy chose to sleep in a small closet rather than the big open hall, and before curling up, he could not help drawing a few cats on the closet's screen doors. In the middle of the night, he woke to terrible shrieking and thumping sounds coming from the main hall. Too frightened to look, he stayed hidden until morning. When daylight came, he crept out and found a huge, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the hall, and strangely, the cats he had drawn on the screen now had blood on their claws. His drawings had come to life in the night and saved him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, there lived a boy who loved drawing cats more than anything else in the whole world. He drew cats on paper, on the walls of his house, and even across his family's wooden screens, no matter how many times his parents scolded him and told him to stop. Worried that their son would never learn anything sensible, his parents decided to send him away to live and study at a nearby temple, hoping the wise old priest there could teach him proper lessons and better habits. The priest tried patiently to teach the boy his letters and his prayers, but the boy simply could not resist drawing cats at every spare moment, filling scraps of paper, and eventually even parts of the temple's own walls, with his endless sketches.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After some time, the kindly old priest realised, with some sadness, that this particular boy was not suited to becoming a priest, and gently explained that he would have to leave the temple. Before sending him off, he offered one last piece of strange advice: \"Avoid large places at night. Keep to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand these odd words, but he thanked the priest and set off alone into the darkening countryside to find somewhere new to live. As night fell, tired and unsure where to go, he spotted what appeared to be an old, abandoned temple and decided gratefully to shelter there until morning, not realising that the villagers avoided the place because a dangerous goblin was said to lurk within its walls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the priest's puzzling advice, the boy chose to sleep inside a small, cramped closet rather than in the big, open main hall, and, true to habit, he could not resist drawing a handful of cats across the closet's screen doors before settling down to rest. In the deepest hours of the night, he was jolted awake by terrifying shrieks, growls, and heavy thumping sounds echoing from the main hall nearby. Far too frightened to open the closet door and look, he lay frozen until the first light of dawn crept through the cracks. When he finally crept out, he discovered an enormous, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the middle of the hall floor, and to his astonishment, the cats he had drawn on the closet screen now had fresh blood upon their claws. Somehow, in the darkness, his drawings had come alive and fought off the goblin to save his life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small and peaceful village in Japan, there lived a boy who loved drawing cats more passionately than anything else in the whole world. From the moment he could hold a brush, he drew cats everywhere he could, on scraps of paper, across the walls of his family's home, and even over the delicate wooden screens his parents treasured, no matter how many times they scolded him and told him firmly to stop his endless doodling. Growing increasingly worried that their son would never learn anything sensible or useful, his parents eventually decided to send him away to live and study at a nearby temple, hoping fervently that the wise old priest there might succeed where they had failed, teaching him proper letters, prayers, and disciplined habits. The elderly priest tried his very best, patiently guiding the boy through his early lessons, but the boy simply could not resist the pull of his beloved cats, filling every scrap of spare paper, and eventually even quiet corners of the temple's own walls, with his endless, careful sketches of cats in every pose imaginable.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many months of gentle but fruitless effort, the kindly old priest came to realise, with a certain sadness, that this particular boy was simply not suited to a life as a priest, however talented he clearly was with a brush, and so he gently explained that the boy would need to leave the temple and find another path in life. Before sending him off, the old priest offered one final, rather mysterious piece of advice, spoken slowly and with unusual seriousness: \"Avoid large places at night, my child. Keep always to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand what these strange words could possibly mean, but he thanked the priest and gathered together his few belongings before setting off alone into the gathering dusk of the countryside, uncertain exactly where he might go next. As true darkness fell and a chill crept into the evening air, tired and increasingly uneasy, he spotted what appeared to be an old, abandoned temple standing silent a short distance off the road, and he decided gratefully to shelter there for the night, unaware that the villagers of that region had long avoided the crumbling building, whispering fearfully that a dangerous goblin was said to lurk somewhere within its dark and empty halls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the old priest's puzzling advice at the very last moment, the boy made the wise choice to curl up and sleep inside a small, cramped storage closet at the side of the temple, rather than stretching out comfortably in the tempting, wide-open main hall, and, true to his lifelong habit, he simply could not resist drawing a small handful of cats across the closet's plain wooden screen doors before finally settling down to try to rest. Deep in the darkest hours of the night, he was suddenly jolted wide awake by a chorus of terrifying shrieks, ferocious growls, and heavy, crashing thumps echoing violently from the main hall close by. Far too frightened to even consider opening the closet door to look, he lay perfectly still in the darkness, his heart pounding, until the very first pale light of dawn began creeping in through the cracks around the door frame. When he finally summoned the courage to push the door open and creep cautiously out into the hall, he discovered an enormous, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the very centre of the temple floor, and to his complete astonishment, the small cats he had drawn upon the closet screen the night before now bore fresh blood upon their painted claws. Somehow, in the depths of that dark night, his own drawings had come quietly to life and fought off the fearsome goblin to save him, and from that day forward, the boy's remarkable gift for drawing cats was never doubted by anyone again.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the boy</li><li>cats</li><li>the priest</li><li>the temple</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boy draws cats on everything, everywhere he goes</li><li>His parents send him to study at a temple</li><li>The priest tries to teach him proper lessons</li><li>The boy still cannot stop drawing cats</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>loved drawing cats more passionately than anything else</li><li>no matter how many times they scolded him sternly</li><li>send him away to live and study at a nearby temple</li><li>teaching him proper letters, prayers, and disciplined habits</li><li>filling every scrap of spare paper</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the boy loved drawing ___ so much that his parents sent him to study at a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small Japanese village, there lived a boy who loved to draw cats more than anything else in the world. He drew cats on paper, on walls, and even on the family's screens, no matter how often his parents told him to stop. His parents worried he would never learn anything useful, so they sent him to live and study at a temple with a wise old priest. The priest hoped the boy would learn to read and write properly, but the boy still could not stop drawing cats every chance he got, filling scrap paper and even the temple's walls with his sketches.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet village in Japan, there lived a boy who loved drawing cats more than anything else in the whole world. He drew cats on paper, on the walls of his house, and even across his family's wooden screens, no matter how many times his parents scolded him and told him to stop. Worried that their son would never learn anything sensible, his parents decided to send him away to live and study at a nearby temple, hoping the wise old priest there could teach him proper lessons and better habits. The priest tried patiently to teach the boy his letters and his prayers, but the boy simply could not resist drawing cats at every spare moment, filling scraps of paper, and eventually even parts of the temple's own walls, with his endless sketches.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small and peaceful village in Japan, there lived a boy who loved drawing cats more passionately than anything else in the whole world. From the moment he could hold a brush, he drew cats everywhere he could, on scraps of paper, across the walls of his family's home, and even over the delicate wooden screens his parents treasured, no matter how many times they scolded him and told him firmly to stop his endless doodling. Growing increasingly worried that their son would never learn anything sensible or useful, his parents eventually decided to send him away to live and study at a nearby temple, hoping fervently that the wise old priest there might succeed where they had failed, teaching him proper letters, prayers, and disciplined habits. The elderly priest tried his very best, patiently guiding the boy through his early lessons, but the boy simply could not resist the pull of his beloved cats, filling every scrap of spare paper, and eventually even quiet corners of the temple's own walls, with his endless, careful sketches of cats in every pose imaginable.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the priest</li><li>small places</li><li>the boy</li><li>the countryside</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The priest realises the boy is not suited to be a priest</li><li>He gives the boy advice: avoid large places, keep to small places</li><li>The boy sets off alone into the countryside</li><li>He shelters for the night in an abandoned temple</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>simply not suited to a life as a priest</li><li>avoid large places at night, my child</li><li>keep always to small places</li><li>setting off alone into the gathering dusk of the countryside</li><li>a dangerous, monstrous goblin was said to lurk</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the priest warned the boy to avoid ___ at night and keep to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One evening, the priest gently told the boy that he was not suited to become a priest after all, but gave him some kind advice before sending him on his way: \"Avoid large places at night. Keep to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand what this meant, but he thanked the priest and set off to find somewhere else to stay. Wandering through the dark countryside, he came upon what looked like an empty temple and decided to shelter there for the night, not realising that a dangerous goblin was said to live inside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After some time, the kindly old priest realised, with some sadness, that this particular boy was not suited to becoming a priest, and gently explained that he would have to leave the temple. Before sending him off, he offered one last piece of strange advice: \"Avoid large places at night. Keep to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand these odd words, but he thanked the priest and set off alone into the darkening countryside to find somewhere new to live. As night fell, tired and unsure where to go, he spotted what appeared to be an old, abandoned temple and decided gratefully to shelter there until morning, not realising that the villagers avoided the place because a dangerous goblin was said to lurk within its walls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After many months of gentle but fruitless effort, the kindly old priest came to realise, with a certain sadness, that this particular boy was simply not suited to a life as a priest, however talented he clearly was with a brush, and so he gently explained that the boy would need to leave the temple and find another path in life. Before sending him off, the old priest offered one final, rather mysterious piece of advice, spoken slowly and with unusual seriousness: \"Avoid large places at night, my child. Keep always to small places.\" The boy did not fully understand what these strange words could possibly mean, but he thanked the priest and gathered together his few belongings before setting off alone into the gathering dusk of the countryside, uncertain exactly where he might go next. As true darkness fell and a chill crept into the evening air, tired and increasingly uneasy, he spotted what appeared to be an old, abandoned temple standing silent a short distance off the road, and he decided gratefully to shelter there for the night, unaware that the villagers of that region had long avoided the crumbling building, whispering fearfully that a dangerous goblin was said to lurk somewhere within its dark and empty halls.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the closet</li><li>the cats</li><li>the goblin</li><li>the boy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The boy sleeps in a small closet, drawing cats on the doors</li><li>He hears terrifying sounds from the main hall at night</li><li>In the morning he finds the goblin lying dead</li><li>His drawn cats now have blood on their claws</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>curl up and sleep inside a small, cramped storage closet</li><li>shrieks, ferocious growls, and heavy, crashing thumps</li><li>lying dead and utterly still in the very centre of the temple floor</li><li>bore fresh, dark blood upon their painted claws</li><li>his drawings had come quietly to life and fought off the fearsome goblin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the boy's drawn ___ came to life in the night and defeated the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the priest's strange advice, the boy chose to sleep in a small closet rather than the big open hall, and before curling up, he could not help drawing a few cats on the closet's screen doors. In the middle of the night, he woke to terrible shrieking and thumping sounds coming from the main hall. Too frightened to look, he stayed hidden until morning. When daylight came, he crept out and found a huge, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the hall, and strangely, the cats he had drawn on the screen now had blood on their claws. His drawings had come to life in the night and saved him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the priest's puzzling advice, the boy chose to sleep inside a small, cramped closet rather than in the big, open main hall, and, true to habit, he could not resist drawing a handful of cats across the closet's screen doors before settling down to rest. In the deepest hours of the night, he was jolted awake by terrifying shrieks, growls, and heavy thumping sounds echoing from the main hall nearby. Far too frightened to open the closet door and look, he lay frozen until the first light of dawn crept through the cracks. When he finally crept out, he discovered an enormous, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the middle of the hall floor, and to his astonishment, the cats he had drawn on the closet screen now had fresh blood upon their claws. Somehow, in the darkness, his drawings had come alive and fought off the goblin to save his life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering the old priest's puzzling advice at the very last moment, the boy made the wise choice to curl up and sleep inside a small, cramped storage closet at the side of the temple, rather than stretching out comfortably in the tempting, wide-open main hall, and, true to his lifelong habit, he simply could not resist drawing a small handful of cats across the closet's plain wooden screen doors before finally settling down to try to rest. Deep in the darkest hours of the night, he was suddenly jolted wide awake by a chorus of terrifying shrieks, ferocious growls, and heavy, crashing thumps echoing violently from the main hall close by. Far too frightened to even consider opening the closet door to look, he lay perfectly still in the darkness, his heart pounding, until the very first pale light of dawn began creeping in through the cracks around the door frame. When he finally summoned the courage to push the door open and creep cautiously out into the hall, he discovered an enormous, monstrous rat-goblin lying dead in the very centre of the temple floor, and to his complete astonishment, the small cats he had drawn upon the closet screen the night before now bore fresh blood upon their painted claws. Somehow, in the depths of that dark night, his own drawings had come quietly to life and fought off the fearsome goblin to save him, and from that day forward, the boy's remarkable gift for drawing cats was never doubted by anyone again.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 12: The Boy Who Drew Cats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 12 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 12 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 13 (The Stonecutter) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the boy loved drawing ___ so much that his parents sent him to study at a ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the priest warned the boy to avoid ___ at night and keep to ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the boy's drawn ___ came to life in the night and defeated the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 13: The Stonecutter":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: The Stonecutter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 13’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 13: The Stonecutter) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Hoshi and the mountain spirit as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi</li><li>the mountain spirit</li><li>the mountain</li><li>the chisel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi wishes to be rich, and the mountain spirit grants it</li><li>Hoshi wishes to be a prince, then the powerful sun</li><li>Hoshi wishes to be a mountain, feeling nothing is more powerful</li><li>A stonecutter's chisel humbles him, and he wishes to be himself again</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi was a humble stonecutter who worked on a mountainside</li><li>A kind magical spirit granted each of Hoshi's wishes</li><li>Hoshi wished to become a mountain after becoming the sun</li><li>Hoshi decided to stay as the sun forever at the end of the story</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi who worked hard every day cutting stone from the side of a mountain. He earned just enough to live simply, and although he was not unhappy, he sometimes wished his life could be a little easier. One day, while delivering stone to a rich man's house, Hoshi saw the beautiful furniture and fine silk inside and thought how wonderful it would be to live like that. That night, a kind magical spirit who lived in the mountain heard his wish and granted it: Hoshi suddenly found himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was delighted with his new life. But one day he saw a prince ride by in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shade him from the sun. Hoshi wished aloud that he could be a powerful prince instead, and again the mountain spirit granted his wish. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by all, but soon he noticed how the blazing sun beat down on everyone, even him, and he wished he could be the sun itself, the most powerful thing of all. The spirit granted this too, and Hoshi became the sun, shining brightly over the whole earth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt very powerful indeed, until one day a great mountain blocked his rays and would not let his light pass. Hoshi wished he could be that mighty mountain instead, and the spirit granted it once more. But then Hoshi felt a stonecutter's chisel striking at his rocky feet, chipping away at him bit by bit, and realised that even a mountain was not the most powerful thing after all. Understanding at last that everyone has their place, Hoshi wished simply to be himself again, a stonecutter. The spirit granted this final wish, and Hoshi returned to his old life, working happily and never wishing to be anyone else again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the hills of Japan, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi, who spent every day cutting blocks of stone from the mountainside to sell in the nearby village. He earned just enough to live a simple life, and though he was not unhappy, he sometimes found himself wishing that life could be a little easier and grander. One day, while delivering stone to the house of a wealthy merchant, Hoshi caught a glimpse of the beautiful furniture, silk cushions, and glittering ornaments inside, and thought how wonderful it would be to live in such comfort. That night, a kind magical spirit who dwelled within the mountain heard Hoshi's quiet wish and, moved by curiosity, decided to grant it. The next morning, Hoshi awoke to find himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury he had never known before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was overjoyed with his new life. But before long, he witnessed a powerful prince passing through the streets in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shield him from the sun. Struck with fresh envy, Hoshi wished aloud that he might become a prince instead, and once again the spirit granted his wish. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by everyone around him, yet it was not long before he noticed how the blazing sun beat down on everyone below, prince and peasant alike. Realising the sun's power reached over every living thing, Hoshi wished he could become the sun itself, surely the most powerful thing of all, and the spirit granted this too, transforming Hoshi into the great sun shining over the whole earth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt magnificently powerful, until one day a great mountain range rose up and simply blocked his rays from passing through, no matter how fiercely he shone against its solid rock. Impressed by its strength, Hoshi wished he could become that mighty mountain instead, and the spirit granted this wish too. But then, as an enormous mountain, Hoshi suddenly felt the sharp, steady blows of a stonecutter's chisel striking at his rocky feet, chipping away tiny pieces of him day after day, and he understood at last that even a mighty mountain was not the most powerful thing after all. Realising that every single thing, however humble, has its own important place and its own kind of quiet strength, Hoshi wished simply to become himself again, an ordinary stonecutter. The spirit granted this last, wisest wish, and Hoshi returned gladly to his old life on the mountainside, working contentedly with his chisel and never wishing to be anyone else again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, high in the hills of Japan, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi, who spent his days cutting stone from the mountainside to sell in the village below. His work was hard, but it earned him just enough to live simply, and though he was not truly unhappy, he sometimes wished that life could be a little easier. One day, delivering stone to a wealthy merchant's house, Hoshi glimpsed the fine furniture and silk cushions inside and thought how wonderful it would be to live in such comfort. That night, a kind magical spirit living deep within the mountain overheard his wistful wish and, moved by curiosity, decided to grant it. The next morning, Hoshi awoke to find himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury he had never known before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was overjoyed with his new life. But it was not long before he saw a powerful prince passing through the streets in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shield him from the sun. Struck with envy, Hoshi wished aloud that he might become a prince instead, and once again the patient spirit granted his wish at once. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by everyone around him, yet before long he noticed how the blazing sun beat down mercilessly on everyone below, prince and peasant alike, its heat reaching even him as he rode in his own fine carriage. Realising the sun's power reached over every living thing, Hoshi wished he could become the sun itself, surely the most powerful thing in the whole world, and the spirit granted this too, transforming Hoshi into the great, glowing sun shining over the whole earth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt magnificently powerful, his light and heat reaching every corner of the land below, until one day a great range of mountains rose up and simply blocked his brilliant rays from passing through at all, no matter how fiercely he shone against their solid rock. Impressed by this immovable strength, Hoshi wished he could become that mighty mountain instead, believing it must be more powerful still than the sun, and the patient spirit granted this final wish too. But then, standing as an enormous mountain, Hoshi suddenly felt the sharp, steady blows of a lone stonecutter's chisel striking patiently at his own rocky feet, chipping away tiny fragments day after day without stopping, and he understood at last, with a humbling flash of clarity, that even a mighty mountain was not truly the most powerful thing in the world after all. Realising that every single thing, however humble it might seem, has its own important place and its own kind of quiet strength, Hoshi wished, for the very last time, simply to become himself again, an ordinary stonecutter with rough hands and an honest trade. The kind spirit granted this final, wisest wish, and Hoshi returned gladly to his old life on the mountainside, working contentedly with his chisel from that day on, never again wishing to be anyone other than exactly who he truly was.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: The Stonecutter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi</li><li>the merchant's house</li><li>the mountain spirit</li><li>wealth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi works hard cutting stone on the mountainside</li><li>He sees a rich merchant's fine furniture and silk</li><li>He wishes he could live in comfort and luxury</li><li>The mountain spirit hears his wish and grants it</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>spent every day of his life patiently cutting great blocks of stone</li><li>earned him just enough to live a simple, honest life</li><li>beautiful carved furniture, fine silk cushions</li><li>moved by curiosity more than anything else</li><li>living as a genuinely rich man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Hoshi saw a rich merchant's ___ and wished for comfort, and the ___ granted it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi who worked hard every day cutting stone from the side of a mountain. He earned just enough to live simply, and although he was not unhappy, he sometimes wished his life could be a little easier. One day, while delivering stone to a rich man's house, Hoshi saw the beautiful furniture and fine silk inside and thought how wonderful it would be to live like that. That night, a kind magical spirit who lived in the mountain heard his wish and granted it: Hoshi suddenly found himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the hills of Japan, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi, who spent every day cutting blocks of stone from the mountainside to sell in the nearby village. He earned just enough to live a simple life, and though he was not unhappy, he sometimes found himself wishing that life could be a little easier and grander. One day, while delivering stone to the house of a wealthy merchant, Hoshi caught a glimpse of the beautiful furniture, silk cushions, and glittering ornaments inside, and thought how wonderful it would be to live in such comfort. That night, a kind magical spirit who dwelled within the mountain heard Hoshi's quiet wish and, moved by curiosity, decided to grant it. The next morning, Hoshi awoke to find himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury he had never known before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, high in the hills of Japan, there lived a humble stonecutter named Hoshi, who spent his days cutting stone from the mountainside to sell in the village below. His work was hard, but it earned him just enough to live simply, and though he was not truly unhappy, he sometimes wished that life could be a little easier. One day, delivering stone to a wealthy merchant's house, Hoshi glimpsed the fine furniture and silk cushions inside and thought how wonderful it would be to live in such comfort. That night, a kind magical spirit living deep within the mountain overheard his wistful wish and, moved by curiosity, decided to grant it. The next morning, Hoshi awoke to find himself living as a rich man, surrounded by comfort and luxury he had never known before.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: The Stonecutter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the prince</li><li>the sun</li><li>Hoshi</li><li>the mountain spirit</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hoshi sees a prince and wishes to be one instead</li><li>The spirit grants it and Hoshi becomes a prince</li><li>The blazing sun makes Hoshi wish to become the sun</li><li>The spirit grants it and Hoshi becomes the sun</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a truly powerful prince passing grandly through the village streets</li><li>admired, deeply respected, and obediently followed</li><li>the blazing summer sun beat down mercilessly upon every living creature</li><li>the single most powerful thing that existed in all the wide world</li><li>shining brilliantly and proudly over the whole wide earth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Hoshi wished to become a ___, then wished to become the ___ itself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was delighted with his new life. But one day he saw a prince ride by in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shade him from the sun. Hoshi wished aloud that he could be a powerful prince instead, and again the mountain spirit granted his wish. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by all, but soon he noticed how the blazing sun beat down on everyone, even him, and he wished he could be the sun itself, the most powerful thing of all. The spirit granted this too, and Hoshi became the sun, shining brightly over the whole earth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was overjoyed with his new life. But before long, he witnessed a powerful prince passing through the streets in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shield him from the sun. Struck with fresh envy, Hoshi wished aloud that he might become a prince instead, and once again the spirit granted his wish. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by everyone around him, yet it was not long before he noticed how the blazing sun beat down on everyone below, prince and peasant alike. Realising the sun's power reached over every living thing, Hoshi wished he could become the sun itself, surely the most powerful thing of all, and the spirit granted this too, transforming Hoshi into the great sun shining over the whole earth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At first, Hoshi was overjoyed with his new life. But it was not long before he saw a powerful prince passing through the streets in a grand procession, with servants holding a golden umbrella to shield him from the sun. Struck with envy, Hoshi wished aloud that he might become a prince instead, and once again the patient spirit granted his wish at once. Hoshi became a prince, admired and obeyed by everyone around him, yet before long he noticed how the blazing sun beat down mercilessly on everyone below, prince and peasant alike, its heat reaching even him as he rode in his own fine carriage. Realising the sun's power reached over every living thing, Hoshi wished he could become the sun itself, surely the most powerful thing in the whole world, and the spirit granted this too, transforming Hoshi into the great, glowing sun shining over the whole earth.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: The Stonecutter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the mountain</li><li>the chisel</li><li>the stonecutter</li><li>himself</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A mountain blocks the sun, so Hoshi wishes to be a mountain</li><li>He becomes the mountain and feels very powerful</li><li>A stonecutter's chisel chips away at his rocky feet</li><li>Hoshi realises everyone has a place and wishes to be himself again</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>simply, stubbornly blocked his brilliant rays from passing through</li><li>more powerful still than even the great sun itself</li><li>the sharp, steady, rhythmic blows of a lone stonecutter's chisel</li><li>even a truly mighty mountain was not, in fact, the single most powerful thing</li><li>never again wishing, even for a moment, to be anyone other than exactly who he truly was</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a ___ chipped away at Hoshi's rocky feet, so he wished to become a ___ again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt very powerful indeed, until one day a great mountain blocked his rays and would not let his light pass. Hoshi wished he could be that mighty mountain instead, and the spirit granted it once more. But then Hoshi felt a stonecutter's chisel striking at his rocky feet, chipping away at him bit by bit, and realised that even a mountain was not the most powerful thing after all. Understanding at last that everyone has their place, Hoshi wished simply to be himself again, a stonecutter. The spirit granted this final wish, and Hoshi returned to his old life, working happily and never wishing to be anyone else again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt magnificently powerful, until one day a great mountain range rose up and simply blocked his rays from passing through, no matter how fiercely he shone against its solid rock. Impressed by its strength, Hoshi wished he could become that mighty mountain instead, and the spirit granted this wish too. But then, as an enormous mountain, Hoshi suddenly felt the sharp, steady blows of a stonecutter's chisel striking at his rocky feet, chipping away tiny pieces of him day after day, and he understood at last that even a mighty mountain was not the most powerful thing after all. Realising that every single thing, however humble, has its own important place and its own kind of quiet strength, Hoshi wished simply to become himself again, an ordinary stonecutter. The spirit granted this last, wisest wish, and Hoshi returned gladly to his old life on the mountainside, working contentedly with his chisel and never wishing to be anyone else again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun, Hoshi felt magnificently powerful, his light and heat reaching every corner of the land below, until one day a great range of mountains rose up and simply blocked his brilliant rays from passing through at all, no matter how fiercely he shone against their solid rock. Impressed by this immovable strength, Hoshi wished he could become that mighty mountain instead, believing it must be more powerful still than the sun, and the patient spirit granted this final wish too. But then, standing as an enormous mountain, Hoshi suddenly felt the sharp, steady blows of a lone stonecutter's chisel striking patiently at his own rocky feet, chipping away tiny fragments day after day without stopping, and he understood at last, with a humbling flash of clarity, that even a mighty mountain was not truly the most powerful thing in the world after all. Realising that every single thing, however humble it might seem, has its own important place and its own kind of quiet strength, Hoshi wished, for the very last time, simply to become himself again, an ordinary stonecutter with rough hands and an honest trade. The kind spirit granted this final, wisest wish, and Hoshi returned gladly to his old life on the mountainside, working contentedly with his chisel from that day on, never again wishing to be anyone other than exactly who he truly was.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 13: The Stonecutter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 13 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 13 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 14 (Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Hoshi saw a rich merchant's ___ and wished for comfort, and the ___ granted it.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Hoshi wished to become a ___, then wished to become the ___ itself.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, a ___ chipped away at Hoshi's rocky feet, so he wished to become a ___ again.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 14’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the father mouse and the daughter mouse as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the father mouse</li><li>the daughter mouse</li><li>the sun</li><li>the stone wall</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The father mouse seeks the most powerful husband for his daughter</li><li>The sun says the cloud is more powerful, and the cloud names the wind</li><li>The wind admits the stone wall can resist it completely</li><li>The wall reveals it fears mice most, and the daughter marries a mouse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The father mouse wanted the most powerful husband for his daughter</li><li>The sun said a cloud could block its light</li><li>The stone wall said it was afraid of nothing at all</li><li>The daughter mouse ended up marrying a fellow mouse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a cosy mouse hole in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse who loved their daughter very much. They believed she was so wonderful that only the most powerful husband in the whole world would be good enough for her. The father mouse decided he would find out who was truly the most powerful and ask them to marry his daughter. First, he thought of the sun, since it shone brightly over the whole earth and warmed everything below. He travelled to the sun and asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his daughter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sun replied kindly that although it was bright, a cloud could easily cover it and block its light, so the cloud must be more powerful. The mouse then went to the cloud, who admitted that the wind could easily blow it away wherever it wished, so the wind must be even stronger still. The mouse travelled next to the wind, who confessed that it could blow and blow with all its might, yet it could never move a sturdy stone wall standing firm in its path. Surely, the wind said, the wall must be the most powerful of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopeful at last, the father mouse hurried to the great stone wall and asked for its daughter's hand. But the wall laughed gently and said that the one thing it truly feared was a little mouse, who could burrow through its foundations and weaken it bit by bit. Astonished, the father mouse realised that his own kind, the humble mouse, was truly the most powerful of all. He returned home and happily arranged for his daughter to marry a fine young mouse from a nearby burrow, and the whole mouse family celebrated a joyful wedding together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a cosy little mouse hole beneath a farmhouse in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse, who loved their only daughter more than anything else in the world. Convinced that she was the most wonderful young mouse who had ever lived, they decided that only the single most powerful husband in the whole world could possibly be good enough to marry her. The determined father mouse set off on a journey to discover exactly who or what was truly the most powerful thing in existence. His first thought was of the great sun itself, which shone down brightly over the whole earth and warmed every living thing beneath it, so he travelled a long way to visit the sun and respectfully asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his beloved daughter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sun, however, replied kindly but honestly that although it certainly shone bright and warm, even a single passing cloud could easily drift across the sky and cover it completely, blocking every ray of its light, so the cloud, the sun suggested, must in fact be even more powerful. Taking this advice seriously, the father mouse travelled onward to visit the cloud, who admitted quite openly that the wind could blow at any moment and scatter it effortlessly across the sky, meaning the wind, surely, must be stronger still than either the sun or the cloud. Following this new lead, the mouse travelled next to visit the mighty wind, who confessed rather sheepishly that although it could blow with all its considerable might, it could never manage to move a sturdy, ancient stone wall standing firm directly in its path, no matter how hard or how long it tried. Surely, the wind reasoned, the wall itself must therefore be the truly most powerful thing of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Filled with fresh hope at this news, the father mouse hurried eagerly onward to the great old stone wall standing at the edge of the village and respectfully asked for its permission to marry his precious daughter. But the wall only laughed gently at the very suggestion, explaining that the one thing it genuinely feared above all others was, in fact, a small, humble mouse, who could patiently burrow tiny tunnels through its foundations, bit by tiny bit, weakening its ancient stones from below until eventually the whole wall might come crumbling down. Utterly astonished by this revelation, the father mouse suddenly realised, with a warm rush of pride, that his own humble kind, the ordinary little mouse, was in fact truly the most powerful of all the things he had visited on his long journey. Delighted and relieved, he hurried straight home to share the wonderful news, and happily arranged for his beloved daughter to marry a fine, hardworking young mouse from a nearby burrow, and the whole extended mouse family gathered together joyfully to celebrate a warm and happy wedding at last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a cosy little mouse hole beneath the floorboards of a farmhouse in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse who loved their only daughter more than anything in the world. Convinced she was the most wonderful young mouse who had ever lived, the doting parents decided that only the single most powerful husband in the whole world could possibly be good enough for her. The determined father mouse set off on a journey to discover, once and for all, exactly who or what was truly the most powerful thing in existence. His first thought turned to the great sun itself, which shone brightly over the whole earth and warmed every living creature beneath its golden light, so he travelled a long way to visit the sun directly and respectfully asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his beloved daughter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The great sun, however, replied kindly and honestly that although it certainly shone bright and warm each day, even a single small cloud could easily glide across the sky and cover it completely within moments, blocking every last ray of its light from reaching the earth below, so the humble cloud, the sun suggested, must in fact be even more powerful than the sun itself. Taking this advice seriously, the father mouse travelled onward to visit the cloud, who admitted quite openly that the mighty wind could rise up at any moment and scatter it effortlessly across the sky in whatever direction it pleased, meaning the wind, surely, must be stronger still. Following this new lead, the mouse travelled next to visit the mighty wind, who confessed rather sheepishly that although it could blow with all its considerable might, it could never once manage to move a single sturdy, ancient stone wall standing firm directly in its path, no matter how hard or how persistently it tried. Surely, the wind reasoned, the old stone wall itself must therefore be the single most powerful thing of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Filled with fresh, renewed hope at this news, the father mouse hurried eagerly onward to the great, old, weathered stone wall standing quietly at the edge of the village, and respectfully asked for its kind permission to marry his precious daughter to whatever powerful being it might recommend. But the old stone wall only laughed gently to itself, before patiently explaining that the one thing it genuinely feared above absolutely everything else in this world was, in fact, nothing grander than a small, humble, ordinary mouse, who could patiently and quietly burrow tiny tunnels through its ancient foundations, bit by tiny bit, day after day, slowly weakening its old stones from underneath until, eventually, given enough time, the whole proud wall might come tumbling down into ruin. Utterly astonished by this completely unexpected revelation, the proud father mouse suddenly realised, with a warm and joyful rush of pride swelling in his small chest, that his own humble kind, the ordinary little mouse, was in fact truly and genuinely the most powerful of all the many things he had visited on his long and winding journey. Delighted and thoroughly relieved in equal measure, he hurried straight home as fast as his little legs could carry him to share the wonderful news with his waiting family, and happily arranged for his beloved daughter to marry a fine, hardworking young mouse from a nearby burrow down the lane, and the whole extended mouse family gathered together warmly and joyfully that very weekend to celebrate a truly happy and memorable wedding at long last.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the father mouse</li><li>the daughter</li><li>the sun</li><li>marriage</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The proud parents believe their daughter deserves the best husband</li><li>The father mouse sets off to find the most powerful being</li><li>He visits the sun and asks for its hand</li><li>The sun says the cloud is more powerful than it is</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>loved their one and only daughter more dearly than anything</li><li>the single most powerful husband in the entire wide world</li><li>shone down brightly and warmly over the whole wide earth</li><li>bowing respectfully and asking politely for its hand</li><li>must in fact be considered even more powerful</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the father mouse asked the ___ to marry his daughter, but it said the ___ was stronger.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a cosy mouse hole in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse who loved their daughter very much. They believed she was so wonderful that only the most powerful husband in the whole world would be good enough for her. The father mouse decided he would find out who was truly the most powerful and ask them to marry his daughter. First, he thought of the sun, since it shone brightly over the whole earth and warmed everything below. He travelled to the sun and asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his daughter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a cosy little mouse hole beneath a farmhouse in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse, who loved their only daughter more than anything else in the world. Convinced that she was the most wonderful young mouse who had ever lived, they decided that only the single most powerful husband in the whole world could possibly be good enough to marry her. The determined father mouse set off on a journey to discover exactly who or what was truly the most powerful thing in existence. His first thought was of the great sun itself, which shone down brightly over the whole earth and warmed every living thing beneath it, so he travelled a long way to visit the sun and respectfully asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his beloved daughter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a cosy little mouse hole beneath the floorboards of a farmhouse in Japan, there lived a proud father mouse and mother mouse who loved their only daughter more than anything in the world. Convinced she was the most wonderful young mouse who had ever lived, the doting parents decided that only the single most powerful husband in the whole world could possibly be good enough for her. The determined father mouse set off on a journey to discover, once and for all, exactly who or what was truly the most powerful thing in existence. His first thought turned to the great sun itself, which shone brightly over the whole earth and warmed every living creature beneath its golden light, so he travelled a long way to visit the sun directly and respectfully asked for its hand in marriage on behalf of his beloved daughter.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the cloud</li><li>the wind</li><li>the stone wall</li><li>the father mouse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The father mouse visits the cloud next</li><li>The cloud says the wind can blow it away</li><li>He visits the wind, who cannot move a stone wall</li><li>The wind says the wall must be most powerful</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>scatter it effortlessly across the entire sky in whatever direction</li><li>could never once, in all its long existence, manage to move</li><li>a single sturdy, ancient stone wall standing firm and utterly unmoving</li><li>must be stronger still than either the great sun or the drifting cloud</li><li>the single truly most powerful thing of all</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the ___ admitted the wind was stronger, and the wind said the ___ was stronger still.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sun replied kindly that although it was bright, a cloud could easily cover it and block its light, so the cloud must be more powerful. The mouse then went to the cloud, who admitted that the wind could easily blow it away wherever it wished, so the wind must be even stronger still. The mouse travelled next to the wind, who confessed that it could blow and blow with all its might, yet it could never move a sturdy stone wall standing firm in its path. Surely, the wind said, the wall must be the most powerful of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The sun, however, replied kindly but honestly that although it certainly shone bright and warm, even a single passing cloud could easily drift across the sky and cover it completely, blocking every ray of its light, so the cloud, the sun suggested, must in fact be even more powerful. Taking this advice seriously, the father mouse travelled onward to visit the cloud, who admitted quite openly that the wind could blow at any moment and scatter it effortlessly across the sky, meaning the wind, surely, must be stronger still than either the sun or the cloud. Following this new lead, the mouse travelled next to visit the mighty wind, who confessed rather sheepishly that although it could blow with all its considerable might, it could never manage to move a sturdy, ancient stone wall standing firm directly in its path, no matter how hard or how long it tried. Surely, the wind reasoned, the wall itself must therefore be the truly most powerful thing of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The great sun, however, replied kindly and honestly that although it certainly shone bright and warm each day, even a single small cloud could easily glide across the sky and cover it completely within moments, blocking every last ray of its light from reaching the earth below, so the humble cloud, the sun suggested, must in fact be even more powerful than the sun itself. Taking this advice seriously, the father mouse travelled onward to visit the cloud, who admitted quite openly that the mighty wind could rise up at any moment and scatter it effortlessly across the sky in whatever direction it pleased, meaning the wind, surely, must be stronger still. Following this new lead, the mouse travelled next to visit the mighty wind, who confessed rather sheepishly that although it could blow with all its considerable might, it could never once manage to move a single sturdy, ancient stone wall standing firm directly in its path, no matter how hard or how persistently it tried. Surely, the wind reasoned, the old stone wall itself must therefore be the single most powerful thing of all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the wall</li><li>the mouse</li><li>the daughter</li><li>the wedding</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The father mouse asks the stone wall for its hand</li><li>The wall says it fears mice, who can burrow through it</li><li>The father mouse realises mice are truly the most powerful</li><li>His daughter marries a fine young mouse, and they celebrate</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the one single thing it genuinely feared above absolutely everything else</li><li>burrow tiny tunnels through its ancient foundations</li><li>the whole proud wall might come tumbling and crumbling down into ruin</li><li>was in fact truly and genuinely the most powerful</li><li>gathered together warmly and joyfully that very weekend to celebrate</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the wall admitted it feared the ___, so the daughter happily married a fellow ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hopeful at last, the father mouse hurried to the great stone wall and asked for its daughter's hand. But the wall laughed gently and said that the one thing it truly feared was a little mouse, who could burrow through its foundations and weaken it bit by bit. Astonished, the father mouse realised that his own kind, the humble mouse, was truly the most powerful of all. He returned home and happily arranged for his daughter to marry a fine young mouse from a nearby burrow, and the whole mouse family celebrated a joyful wedding together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Filled with fresh hope at this news, the father mouse hurried eagerly onward to the great old stone wall standing at the edge of the village and respectfully asked for its permission to marry his precious daughter. But the wall only laughed gently at the very suggestion, explaining that the one thing it genuinely feared above all others was, in fact, a small, humble mouse, who could patiently burrow tiny tunnels through its foundations, bit by tiny bit, weakening its ancient stones from below until eventually the whole wall might come crumbling down. Utterly astonished by this revelation, the father mouse suddenly realised, with a warm rush of pride, that his own humble kind, the ordinary little mouse, was in fact truly the most powerful of all the things he had visited on his long journey. Delighted and relieved, he hurried straight home to share the wonderful news, and happily arranged for his beloved daughter to marry a fine, hardworking young mouse from a nearby burrow, and the whole extended mouse family gathered together joyfully to celebrate a warm and happy wedding at last.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Filled with fresh, renewed hope at this news, the father mouse hurried eagerly onward to the great, old, weathered stone wall standing quietly at the edge of the village, and respectfully asked for its kind permission to marry his precious daughter to whatever powerful being it might recommend. But the old stone wall only laughed gently to itself, before patiently explaining that the one thing it genuinely feared above absolutely everything else in this world was, in fact, nothing grander than a small, humble, ordinary mouse, who could patiently and quietly burrow tiny tunnels through its ancient foundations, bit by tiny bit, day after day, slowly weakening its old stones from underneath until, eventually, given enough time, the whole proud wall might come tumbling down into ruin. Utterly astonished by this completely unexpected revelation, the proud father mouse suddenly realised, with a warm and joyful rush of pride swelling in his small chest, that his own humble kind, the ordinary little mouse, was in fact truly and genuinely the most powerful of all the many things he had visited on his long and winding journey. Delighted and thoroughly relieved in equal measure, he hurried straight home as fast as his little legs could carry him to share the wonderful news with his waiting family, and happily arranged for his beloved daughter to marry a fine, hardworking young mouse from a nearby burrow down the lane, and the whole extended mouse family gathered together warmly and joyfully that very weekend to celebrate a truly happy and memorable wedding at long last.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 14: Nezumi no Yomeiri (The Mouse's Wedding)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 14 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 14 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 15 (Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the father mouse asked the ___ to marry his daughter, but it said the ___ was stronger.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the ___ admitted the wind was stronger, and the wind said the ___ was stronger still.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the wall admitted it feared the ___, so the daughter happily married a fellow ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 15’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Taro and Taro's mother as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Taro</li><li>Taro's mother</li><li>the mountain</li><li>the village lord</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Custom demands Taro carry his elderly mother up the mountain to leave her</li><li>His mother marks the trail with twigs so he can find his way home</li><li>Taro cannot leave her, so he hides her safely beneath the floor at home</li><li>Her wisdom solves the lord's riddles, ending the cruel old custom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Village custom said old parents should be left on the mountain</li><li>Taro's mother marked the path with twigs to help him get home safely</li><li>Taro left his mother on the mountain as custom demanded</li><li>The lord ended the old custom after learning of the mother's wisdom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small mountain village in Japan, there was an old custom that troubled many families: when a parent grew too old to work, custom said they should be carried up the mountain and left there. A kind young man named Taro lived with his elderly mother, whom he loved dearly, but the village elder reminded him it was time to follow the old custom. With a heavy heart, Taro carried his mother up the mountain path on his back, planning to leave her there as expected.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As they climbed higher, Taro's mother quietly broke small twigs from the trees and dropped them along the path. When Taro asked why, she explained gently that she was marking the trail so he would not get lost on his way back down. Moved deeply by her continued love and care for him, even now, Taro could not bring himself to leave her on the mountain. Instead, he secretly carried her back home and hid her safely in a hidden room beneath the floor, bringing her food quietly every day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon after, a powerful lord threatened the village, saying he would destroy it unless someone could solve three impossible riddles. No one in the village could answer them, until Taro nervously asked his hidden mother for help. Drawing on her long years of wisdom, she solved each riddle cleverly, one by one. Taro brought her answers to the lord, who was astonished and pleased. When Taro finally admitted the truth about his hidden mother, the lord was so impressed by her wisdom that he ended the cruel old custom forever, and elders were honoured and cared for from that day on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small, poor mountain village in Japan, there existed an old custom that troubled many families: whenever a parent grew too old or frail to work, custom demanded that they be carried up the tall mountain nearby and left there to face their fate alone. A kind, hardworking young man named Taro lived with his elderly mother, whom he loved dearly and had cared for faithfully all his life, but one day the stern village elder came to remind him it was time to follow the old custom, as everyone else in the village had always done before him. With a heavy heart, Taro lifted his mother onto his back and began the long climb up the steep mountain path, dreading every step of the journey ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the two of them climbed higher and higher up the winding trail, Taro noticed his mother quietly reaching out and breaking small twigs from the branches they passed, dropping each one along the path behind them as they went. Puzzled, Taro finally asked her why, and she explained gently, without any trace of bitterness, that she was simply marking the trail so that her son would not lose his way on his journey back down the mountain alone. Deeply moved almost to tears by this quiet, selfless act of continued love and care for him, even now, Taro found that he simply could not bring himself to leave his mother alone on that cold, windswept mountainside as custom demanded. Instead, he secretly carried her the whole way back down the mountain and home again, hiding her safely in a small room he quickly dug beneath the floorboards, bringing her food and water quietly every day, without anyone in the village ever suspecting a thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, a powerful lord arrived suddenly at the village, threatening to destroy the entire settlement unless someone among its people could answer three seemingly impossible riddles he intended to set before them. To the growing despair of the whole village, not a single person could work out the answers, no matter how hard they thought, until at last a nervous, desperate Taro secretly turned to his hidden mother for help, whispering the terrible situation to her. Drawing calmly on her many years of hard-won wisdom, Taro's mother solved each of the three difficult riddles cleverly and correctly, one after another, without much difficulty at all. Taro carried her wise answers back to the impatient lord, who was utterly astonished and delighted by such unexpected cleverness from a simple village. When Taro finally worked up the courage to confess the whole truth about his hidden mother, the lord was so genuinely moved by her remarkable wisdom that he immediately declared an end to the cruel old mountain custom once and for all, and from that day onward, every elder in the village was honoured and lovingly cared for at home for the rest of their days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, poor mountain village in Japan, there existed an old and troubling custom that weighed heavily upon many families living there: whenever a parent grew too old or frail to work any longer, the harsh old custom demanded that they be carried up the tall, steep mountain nearby by a family member and left there alone to face whatever fate awaited them. A kind, hardworking, and devoted young man named Taro lived with his beloved elderly mother, whom he loved dearly and had cared for faithfully throughout his entire life, but one grim day the stern village elder came knocking at their door to remind Taro firmly that the time had come for him to follow the old custom, exactly as every other family in the village had always reluctantly done before him. With a heavy, aching heart, Taro gently lifted his frail mother onto his strong back and began the long, difficult climb up the steep, winding mountain path, dreading with every step the terrible task waiting at the journey's end.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the two of them climbed higher up the narrow, winding trail together, Taro noticed his mother quietly reaching out from her perch on his back and breaking small twigs from the low branches they passed, dropping each little twig along the path behind them as they climbed. Puzzled by this repeated behaviour, Taro eventually turned and asked her why, and she explained gently, without the faintest trace of bitterness, that she was simply marking the trail so that her dear son would not lose his own way and become lost on his lonely journey back down the mountain alone. Deeply moved almost to tears by this quiet, selfless act of continued love and devoted care for him, even now, at what she genuinely believed to be the very end of her own life, Taro suddenly found that he simply could not bring himself, no matter what custom demanded, to leave his beloved mother behind alone on that cold, windswept mountainside. Instead, acting on a sudden and determined decision, he secretly carried her the whole way back down the mountain and home again under cover of darkness, hiding her away in a small, secret room he swiftly dug out beneath the floorboards, faithfully bringing her food and water in secret every day, without a single soul in the village ever once suspecting the truth.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not very long afterward, a powerful and rather fearsome lord from a neighbouring region arrived suddenly at the village gates, angrily threatening to destroy the entire settlement unless someone among its frightened people could answer three seemingly impossible riddles he now intended to set before them as a cruel test. To the rapidly growing despair of the entire village, not a single person, however clever, could work out even one correct answer, until at last a nervous, desperate Taro secretly slipped down to the hidden room beneath his floorboards to turn to his mother for help, whispering the urgent situation to her as clearly as he could manage. Drawing calmly on her many long years of hard-won wisdom, Taro's remarkable mother solved each of the three fiendishly difficult riddles cleverly and correctly, seemingly without any real difficulty at all. Taro hurried back and carried her wise answers to the impatient lord, who was utterly astonished and quite unable to hide his admiration for such unexpected cleverness emerging from such a simple, humble mountain village. When Taro finally worked up the courage to confess the whole truth about his hidden mother, the powerful lord was so genuinely moved and deeply impressed by her extraordinary wisdom that he immediately and publicly declared an end to the cruel old mountain custom once and for all, before the whole gathered village. And from that joyful day onward, every elder living in that village was honoured and lovingly cared for at home by their families for the rest of their days, exactly as they always should have been.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Taro</li><li>his mother</li><li>the village elder</li><li>the mountain</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The village has an old custom about elderly parents</li><li>The village elder tells Taro it is time to follow it</li><li>Taro carries his mother up the mountain with a heavy heart</li><li>He plans to leave her there as custom demands</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>whenever a parent grew too old, frail, or weak to work</li><li>carried up the tall, steep mountain nearby by a family member</li><li>loved very dearly indeed and had cared for faithfully</li><li>the time had now come for him to follow the old custom</li><li>the deepest possible reluctance weighing on every step</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, village custom said Taro must carry his elderly ___ up the ___ and leave her there.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small mountain village in Japan, there was an old custom that troubled many families: when a parent grew too old to work, custom said they should be carried up the mountain and left there. A kind young man named Taro lived with his elderly mother, whom he loved dearly, but the village elder reminded him it was time to follow the old custom. With a heavy heart, Taro carried his mother up the mountain path on his back, planning to leave her there as expected.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small, poor mountain village in Japan, there existed an old custom that troubled many families: whenever a parent grew too old or frail to work, custom demanded that they be carried up the tall mountain nearby and left there to face their fate alone. A kind, hardworking young man named Taro lived with his elderly mother, whom he loved dearly and had cared for faithfully all his life, but one day the stern village elder came to remind him it was time to follow the old custom, as everyone else in the village had always done before him. With a heavy heart, Taro lifted his mother onto his back and began the long climb up the steep mountain path, dreading every step of the journey ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small, poor mountain village in Japan, there existed an old and troubling custom that weighed heavily upon many families living there: whenever a parent grew too old or frail to work any longer, the harsh old custom demanded that they be carried up the tall, steep mountain nearby by a family member and left there alone to face whatever fate awaited them. A kind, hardworking, and devoted young man named Taro lived with his beloved elderly mother, whom he loved dearly and had cared for faithfully throughout his entire life, but one grim day the stern village elder came knocking at their door to remind Taro firmly that the time had come for him to follow the old custom, exactly as every other family in the village had always reluctantly done before him. With a heavy, aching heart, Taro gently lifted his frail mother onto his strong back and began the long, difficult climb up the steep, winding mountain path, dreading with every step the terrible task waiting at the journey's end.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the twigs</li><li>the trail</li><li>Taro</li><li>his mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Taro's mother quietly breaks twigs along the path</li><li>She explains she is marking the trail for his return</li><li>Taro is moved by her continued love and care</li><li>He secretly carries her back home instead</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>breaking small, thin twigs from the low branches they passed</li><li>marking the trail so that her dear son would not lose his own way</li><li>this quiet, entirely selfless final act of continued love</li><li>simply could not bring himself, no matter what custom</li><li>acting on a sudden, fierce, and entirely determined decision</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Taro's mother marked the trail with ___, and Taro secretly carried her back ___ instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As they climbed higher, Taro's mother quietly broke small twigs from the trees and dropped them along the path. When Taro asked why, she explained gently that she was marking the trail so he would not get lost on his way back down. Moved deeply by her continued love and care for him, even now, Taro could not bring himself to leave her on the mountain. Instead, he secretly carried her back home and hid her safely in a hidden room beneath the floor, bringing her food quietly every day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the two of them climbed higher and higher up the winding trail, Taro noticed his mother quietly reaching out and breaking small twigs from the branches they passed, dropping each one along the path behind them as they went. Puzzled, Taro finally asked her why, and she explained gently, without any trace of bitterness, that she was simply marking the trail so that her son would not lose his way on his journey back down the mountain alone. Deeply moved almost to tears by this quiet, selfless act of continued love and care for him, even now, Taro found that he simply could not bring himself to leave his mother alone on that cold, windswept mountainside as custom demanded. Instead, he secretly carried her the whole way back down the mountain and home again, hiding her safely in a small room he quickly dug beneath the floorboards, bringing her food and water quietly every day, without anyone in the village ever suspecting a thing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the two of them climbed higher up the narrow, winding trail together, Taro noticed his mother quietly reaching out from her perch on his back and breaking small twigs from the low branches they passed, dropping each little twig along the path behind them as they climbed. Puzzled by this repeated behaviour, Taro eventually turned and asked her why, and she explained gently, without the faintest trace of bitterness, that she was simply marking the trail so that her dear son would not lose his own way and become lost on his lonely journey back down the mountain alone. Deeply moved almost to tears by this quiet, selfless act of continued love and devoted care for him, even now, at what she genuinely believed to be the very end of her own life, Taro suddenly found that he simply could not bring himself, no matter what custom demanded, to leave his beloved mother behind alone on that cold, windswept mountainside. Instead, acting on a sudden and determined decision, he secretly carried her the whole way back down the mountain and home again under cover of darkness, hiding her away in a small, secret room he swiftly dug out beneath the floorboards, faithfully bringing her food and water in secret every day, without a single soul in the village ever once suspecting the truth.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the lord</li><li>the riddles</li><li>Taro's mother</li><li>the custom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A powerful lord threatens the village with three riddles</li><li>No one can solve them until Taro asks his hidden mother</li><li>Her wisdom solves all three riddles correctly</li><li>The lord ends the cruel custom after learning the truth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>three seemingly impossible riddles that he now intended to set</li><li>solved each of the three fiendishly difficult riddles cleverly</li><li>confess the whole truth about his secretly hidden mother</li><li>declared an end to the cruel old mountain custom once and for all</li><li>honoured, deeply respected, and lovingly cared for at home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Taro's mother solved the lord's ___, and the lord ended the cruel ___ forever.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Soon after, a powerful lord threatened the village, saying he would destroy it unless someone could solve three impossible riddles. No one in the village could answer them, until Taro nervously asked his hidden mother for help. Drawing on her long years of wisdom, she solved each riddle cleverly, one by one. Taro brought her answers to the lord, who was astonished and pleased. When Taro finally admitted the truth about his hidden mother, the lord was so impressed by her wisdom that he ended the cruel old custom forever, and elders were honoured and cared for from that day on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not long afterward, a powerful lord arrived suddenly at the village, threatening to destroy the entire settlement unless someone among its people could answer three seemingly impossible riddles he intended to set before them. To the growing despair of the whole village, not a single person could work out the answers, no matter how hard they thought, until at last a nervous, desperate Taro secretly turned to his hidden mother for help, whispering the terrible situation to her. Drawing calmly on her many years of hard-won wisdom, Taro's mother solved each of the three difficult riddles cleverly and correctly, one after another, without much difficulty at all. Taro carried her wise answers back to the impatient lord, who was utterly astonished and delighted by such unexpected cleverness from a simple village. When Taro finally worked up the courage to confess the whole truth about his hidden mother, the lord was so genuinely moved by her remarkable wisdom that he immediately declared an end to the cruel old mountain custom once and for all, and from that day onward, every elder in the village was honoured and lovingly cared for at home for the rest of their days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Not very long afterward, a powerful and rather fearsome lord from a neighbouring region arrived suddenly at the village gates, angrily threatening to destroy the entire settlement unless someone among its frightened people could answer three seemingly impossible riddles he now intended to set before them as a cruel test. To the rapidly growing despair of the entire village, not a single person, however clever, could work out even one correct answer, until at last a nervous, desperate Taro secretly slipped down to the hidden room beneath his floorboards to turn to his mother for help, whispering the urgent situation to her as clearly as he could manage. Drawing calmly on her many long years of hard-won wisdom, Taro's remarkable mother solved each of the three fiendishly difficult riddles cleverly and correctly, seemingly without any real difficulty at all. Taro hurried back and carried her wise answers to the impatient lord, who was utterly astonished and quite unable to hide his admiration for such unexpected cleverness emerging from such a simple, humble mountain village. When Taro finally worked up the courage to confess the whole truth about his hidden mother, the powerful lord was so genuinely moved and deeply impressed by her extraordinary wisdom that he immediately and publicly declared an end to the cruel old mountain custom once and for all, before the whole gathered village. And from that joyful day onward, every elder living in that village was honoured and lovingly cared for at home by their families for the rest of their days, exactly as they always should have been.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 15: Obasute Yama (The Wise Old Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 3: Disciplined Warrior (orange belt) — focus: Cutting unnecessary detail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 15 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 15 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 16 (Kintaro (Golden Boy)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, village custom said Taro must carry his elderly ___ up the ___ and leave her there.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Taro's mother marked the trail with ___, and Taro secretly carried her back ___ instead.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Taro's mother solved the lord's ___, and the lord ended the cruel ___ forever.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (Green belt) — Stories 16–20":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 16’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Kintaro and the bear as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro</li><li>the bear</li><li>Yorimitsu</li><li>the mountains</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro grows up in the mountains with amazing strength</li><li>He befriends the forest animals, especially a great bear</li><li>The samurai Yorimitsu hears of his strength and tests him</li><li>Kintaro joins Yorimitsu and becomes a celebrated warrior</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro grew up alone in the mountains with his mother</li><li>Kintaro made friends with the animals instead of fearing them</li><li>Yorimitsu challenged Kintaro to a friendly test of his strength</li><li>Kintaro refused to ever leave the mountains</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, deep in the forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy.\" Raised alone in the wild by his mother, Kintaro grew up stronger than any ordinary child, able to lift huge rocks and wrestle grown animals without any trouble at all. Rather than fearing the forest creatures around him, Kintaro made friends with them, and a great black bear soon became his closest companion, letting Kintaro ride proudly on its back through the trees.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his days playing joyfully with the animals of the forest, wrestling a deer here or racing a monkey there, always careful never to truly hurt his forest friends despite his amazing strength. He also carried a small axe over his shoulder wherever he went, helping to clear fallen branches and chop firewood for his mother. His fame slowly spread beyond the mountains, as travellers who passed through told stories of the astonishingly strong boy who lived among the bears and deer as if he were one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous samurai warrior named Yorimitsu was travelling through the mountains and heard the local stories about Kintaro's incredible strength. Curious, he sought Kintaro out and challenged him to prove his abilities. Impressed beyond measure by what he saw, Yorimitsu invited Kintaro to leave the mountains and become one of his trusted retainers. Kintaro agreed, and in time he grew into one of the most celebrated warriors in the land, always remembering the forest, the bear, and the mother who had raised him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, deep within the forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy.\" Raised alone in the wilderness by his devoted mother, far from any village or town, Kintaro grew up far stronger than any ordinary child could ever hope to be, able to lift enormous boulders with ease and wrestle fully grown forest animals without the slightest trouble. Rather than fearing the wild creatures that shared his mountain home, Kintaro made friends with every one of them, and a great black bear soon became his very closest companion, happily letting the boy ride proudly upon its broad back as they explored the winding forest trails together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his happy days playing joyfully among the animals of the forest, wrestling a sturdy deer here or racing a quick monkey there, always taking great care never to truly hurt any of his beloved forest friends despite his astonishing strength. He also carried a small, sturdy axe over his shoulder wherever he went, using it to help clear fallen branches from the trails and chop firewood for his mother's small fire each evening. Gradually, his growing fame spread far beyond the quiet mountains, as curious travellers who passed through the region carried home astonishing stories of the incredibly strong young boy who lived happily among bears and deer as though he were simply one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous and well-respected samurai warrior named Yorimitsu happened to be travelling through those very mountains and heard the local villagers' astonishing stories about Kintaro's remarkable strength. Deeply curious, he sought the boy out personally and challenged him to a friendly test of his abilities, watching closely as Kintaro effortlessly lifted boulders and outran his animal friends through the trees. Genuinely impressed beyond measure by everything he witnessed that day, Yorimitsu warmly invited Kintaro to leave his quiet mountain home behind and come serve as one of his own trusted retainers. After saying a fond farewell to his mother and his beloved bear, Kintaro agreed, and in time he grew into one of the most celebrated warriors in the entire land, though he never once forgot the forest, the loyal bear, or the devoted mother who had first raised him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, deep within the thickly forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy,\" a name given to him because of his ruddy, golden-tinted skin and his astonishing natural strength from the moment he was born. Raised entirely alone in the wilderness by his devoted mother, far from any village, town, or other children his own age, Kintaro grew up far stronger than any ordinary child could ever hope to become, able to lift enormous boulders as easily as pebbles and wrestle fully grown forest animals without the slightest sign of difficulty or strain. Rather than fearing the many wild creatures that shared his remote mountain home, as almost anyone else surely would, young Kintaro instead made warm and genuine friends with every one of them, and a great, powerful black bear soon became his very closest companion of all, happily letting the boy climb up and ride proudly upon its broad, strong back as the two of them explored the winding, hidden forest trails together for hours at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his happy, carefree days playing joyfully among the many animals of the forest, wrestling a sturdy deer here or racing a quick, darting monkey there, always taking great and careful pains never to truly hurt any of his beloved forest friends despite his genuinely astonishing physical strength. He also carried a small, sturdy axe balanced over his shoulder wherever he wandered, using it cheerfully to help clear fallen branches from the winding forest trails and to chop firewood each evening for his mother's small cooking fire back at their modest mountain home. Gradually, over many months and years, his growing fame began to spread far beyond the quiet, remote mountains where he lived, as curious travellers who occasionally passed through the region carried home increasingly astonishing stories of the incredibly strong young boy who apparently lived quite happily among wild bears and deer as though he were simply one of them, rather than a human child at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous and widely respected samurai warrior named Yorimitsu happened to be travelling through those very same remote mountains on other business entirely, and quite by chance heard the astonished local villagers' stories about young Kintaro's truly remarkable strength and his unusual friendship with the forest animals. Deeply curious and more than a little skeptical, Yorimitsu decided to seek the boy out for himself, and upon finding him, challenged him warmly to a friendly test of his much-talked-about abilities, watching closely and with growing amazement as Kintaro effortlessly lifted heavy boulders overhead and easily outran even his swiftest animal friends through the dense trees. Genuinely impressed beyond all measure by everything he personally witnessed that remarkable day, Yorimitsu warmly and respectfully invited Kintaro to leave his quiet, isolated mountain home behind and come serve honourably as one of his own most trusted retainers down in the wider world. After saying a fond and rather tearful farewell to his devoted mother and his beloved bear companion, both of whom he promised faithfully to visit whenever he could, Kintaro finally agreed to go, and in the years that followed he steadily grew into one of the most celebrated and admired warriors in the entire land, though he never once forgot, for even a single day, the quiet forest, the loyal bear, or the devoted mother who had first lovingly raised him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro</li><li>his mother</li><li>the mountains</li><li>strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro is raised alone in the mountains by his mother</li><li>He grows far stronger than any ordinary child</li><li>He can lift boulders and wrestle forest animals</li><li>He is unafraid of the wild creatures around him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>raised entirely alone in the wilderness by his devoted mother</li><li>grew up far stronger than any ordinary child could ever hope to become</li><li>lift enormous boulders as easily as pebbles</li><li>wrestle fully grown forest animals without the slightest sign of difficulty</li><li>rather than fearing the many wild creatures that shared his remote mountain home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Kintaro grew up in the ___ with his mother, becoming far stronger than any ordinary ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, deep in the forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy.\" Raised alone in the wild by his mother, Kintaro grew up stronger than any ordinary child, able to lift huge rocks and wrestle grown animals without any trouble at all. Rather than fearing the forest creatures around him, Kintaro made friends with them, and a great black bear soon became his closest companion, letting Kintaro ride proudly on its back through the trees.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, deep within the forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy.\" Raised alone in the wilderness by his devoted mother, far from any village or town, Kintaro grew up far stronger than any ordinary child could ever hope to be, able to lift enormous boulders with ease and wrestle fully grown forest animals without the slightest trouble. Rather than fearing the wild creatures that shared his mountain home, Kintaro made friends with every one of them, and a great black bear soon became his very closest companion, happily letting the boy ride proudly upon its broad back as they explored the winding forest trails together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, deep within the thickly forested mountains of Japan, there lived a remarkably strong boy named Kintaro, whose name meant \"Golden Boy,\" a name given to him because of his ruddy, golden-tinted skin and his astonishing natural strength from the moment he was born. Raised entirely alone in the wilderness by his devoted mother, far from any village, town, or other children his own age, Kintaro grew up far stronger than any ordinary child could ever hope to become, able to lift enormous boulders as easily as pebbles and wrestle fully grown forest animals without the slightest sign of difficulty or strain. Rather than fearing the many wild creatures that shared his remote mountain home, as almost anyone else surely would, young Kintaro instead made warm and genuine friends with every one of them, and a great, powerful black bear soon became his very closest companion of all, happily letting the boy climb up and ride proudly upon its broad, strong back as the two of them explored the winding, hidden forest trails together for hours at a time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the bear</li><li>Kintaro</li><li>the axe</li><li>travellers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bear becomes Kintaro's closest companion</li><li>Kintaro rides the bear through the forest trails</li><li>He plays with the animals and chops firewood with his axe</li><li>Travellers begin telling stories of his strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a great, powerful black bear soon became his very closest companion of all</li><li>ride proudly upon its broad, strong back</li><li>wrestling a sturdy deer here or racing a quick, darting monkey there</li><li>carried a small, sturdy axe balanced over his shoulder wherever he wandered</li><li>carried home increasingly astonishing stories of the incredibly strong young boy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Kintaro rode his friend the ___ through the forest, and travellers spread stories of his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his days playing joyfully with the animals of the forest, wrestling a deer here or racing a monkey there, always careful never to truly hurt his forest friends despite his amazing strength. He also carried a small axe over his shoulder wherever he went, helping to clear fallen branches and chop firewood for his mother. His fame slowly spread beyond the mountains, as travellers who passed through told stories of the astonishingly strong boy who lived among the bears and deer as if he were one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his happy days playing joyfully among the animals of the forest, wrestling a sturdy deer here or racing a quick monkey there, always taking great care never to truly hurt any of his beloved forest friends despite his astonishing strength. He also carried a small, sturdy axe over his shoulder wherever he went, using it to help clear fallen branches from the trails and chop firewood for his mother's small fire each evening. Gradually, his growing fame spread far beyond the quiet mountains, as curious travellers who passed through the region carried home astonishing stories of the incredibly strong young boy who lived happily among bears and deer as though he were simply one of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintaro spent his happy, carefree days playing joyfully among the many animals of the forest, wrestling a sturdy deer here or racing a quick, darting monkey there, always taking great and careful pains never to truly hurt any of his beloved forest friends despite his genuinely astonishing physical strength. He also carried a small, sturdy axe balanced over his shoulder wherever he wandered, using it cheerfully to help clear fallen branches from the winding forest trails and to chop firewood each evening for his mother's small cooking fire back at their modest mountain home. Gradually, over many months and years, his growing fame began to spread far beyond the quiet, remote mountains where he lived, as curious travellers who occasionally passed through the region carried home increasingly astonishing stories of the incredibly strong young boy who apparently lived quite happily among wild bears and deer as though he were simply one of them, rather than a human child at all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yorimitsu</li><li>Kintaro</li><li>the test</li><li>a warrior</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yorimitsu hears the stories and seeks Kintaro out</li><li>He challenges Kintaro to a friendly test of strength</li><li>Impressed, he invites Kintaro to become his retainer</li><li>Kintaro agrees and becomes a celebrated warrior</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>quite by chance heard the astonished local villagers' stories</li><li>challenged him warmly to a friendly test of his much-talked-about abilities</li><li>invited Kintaro to leave his quiet, isolated mountain home behind</li><li>come serve honourably as one of his own most trusted retainers</li><li>grew into one of the most celebrated and admired warriors in the entire land</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, ___ invited Kintaro to become his retainer, and Kintaro grew into a celebrated ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous samurai warrior named Yorimitsu was travelling through the mountains and heard the local stories about Kintaro's incredible strength. Curious, he sought Kintaro out and challenged him to prove his abilities. Impressed beyond measure by what he saw, Yorimitsu invited Kintaro to leave the mountains and become one of his trusted retainers. Kintaro agreed, and in time he grew into one of the most celebrated warriors in the land, always remembering the forest, the bear, and the mother who had raised him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous and well-respected samurai warrior named Yorimitsu happened to be travelling through those very mountains and heard the local villagers' astonishing stories about Kintaro's remarkable strength. Deeply curious, he sought the boy out personally and challenged him to a friendly test of his abilities, watching closely as Kintaro effortlessly lifted boulders and outran his animal friends through the trees. Genuinely impressed beyond measure by everything he witnessed that day, Yorimitsu warmly invited Kintaro to leave his quiet mountain home behind and come serve as one of his own trusted retainers. After saying a fond farewell to his mother and his beloved bear, Kintaro agreed, and in time he grew into one of the most celebrated warriors in the entire land, though he never once forgot the forest, the loyal bear, or the devoted mother who had first raised him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a famous and widely respected samurai warrior named Yorimitsu happened to be travelling through those very same remote mountains on other business entirely, and quite by chance heard the astonished local villagers' stories about young Kintaro's truly remarkable strength and his unusual friendship with the forest animals. Deeply curious and more than a little skeptical, Yorimitsu decided to seek the boy out for himself, and upon finding him, challenged him warmly to a friendly test of his much-talked-about abilities, watching closely and with growing amazement as Kintaro effortlessly lifted heavy boulders overhead and easily outran even his swiftest animal friends through the dense trees. Genuinely impressed beyond all measure by everything he personally witnessed that remarkable day, Yorimitsu warmly and respectfully invited Kintaro to leave his quiet, isolated mountain home behind and come serve honourably as one of his own most trusted retainers down in the wider world. After saying a fond and rather tearful farewell to his devoted mother and his beloved bear companion, both of whom he promised faithfully to visit whenever he could, Kintaro finally agreed to go, and in the years that followed he steadily grew into one of the most celebrated and admired warriors in the entire land, though he never once forgot, for even a single day, the quiet forest, the loyal bear, or the devoted mother who had first lovingly raised him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 16: Kintaro (Golden Boy)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 16 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 16 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 17 (Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Kintaro grew up in the ___ with his mother, becoming far stronger than any ordinary ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Kintaro rode his friend the ___ through the forest, and travellers spread stories of his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, ___ invited Kintaro to become his retainer, and Kintaro grew into a celebrated ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 17’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Hidesato and the princess as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato</li><li>the princess</li><li>the giant centipede</li><li>the bridge</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato calmly crosses a bridge blocked by a serpent</li><li>The princess reveals the serpent was her and asks for his help</li><li>Hidesato's arrows cannot pierce the giant centipede's shell</li><li>He defeats the centipede with saliva on his arrow and is rewarded</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato calmly stepped over the serpent on the bridge</li><li>The princess asked Hidesato to defeat the giant centipede</li><li>Hidesato's very first arrow defeated the centipede</li><li>Hidesato was rewarded with a magical bag of rice that never emptied</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a brave warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long wooden bridge over a lake when he noticed a huge serpent stretched right across his path. Most travellers would have turned back in fear, but Hidesato calmly stepped over the serpent and continued on his way without a trace of alarm. That night, a young woman appeared before him and explained that the serpent had actually been herself in disguise, a princess from an underwater palace beneath the lake, testing his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The princess explained that her family's palace was under terrible threat from a giant centipede monster that lived on a nearby mountain and attacked her home every night. She begged Hidesato for his help, and moved by her plight, he bravely agreed. That night, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering toward the palace, its eyes glowing like torches in the darkness. Hidesato took aim carefully with his bow and fired arrow after arrow, but the creature's tough shell seemed impossible to pierce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering an old story that human saliva could harm such creatures, Hidesato wet the tip of his final arrow with a touch of his own saliva before firing. This time, the arrow struck true, and the giant centipede finally fell, defeated at last. The grateful princess welcomed Hidesato warmly into the palace and rewarded him with a magical bag of rice that never emptied, no matter how much was taken from it. From that day on, Hidesato was known throughout the land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in Japan, a brave and calm warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long wooden bridge stretched over a wide lake when he suddenly noticed a huge serpent stretched right across the middle of his path, blocking the way completely. Most ordinary travellers would surely have turned back at once in fear, but Hidesato simply stepped calmly over the enormous serpent and continued on his way without showing the slightest trace of alarm or hesitation. That very night, as he rested at a nearby inn, a young woman quietly appeared before him and explained, to his surprise, that the serpent had actually been herself in disguise all along, a princess from a magnificent underwater palace beneath the lake, testing travellers' courage as they crossed the bridge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The princess went on to explain, with clear worry in her voice, that her family's beautiful undersea palace was under terrible and constant threat from a giant centipede monster that lived upon a nearby mountain and attacked her peaceful home night after night without mercy. She begged Hidesato earnestly for his help in driving the monster away for good, and moved by her genuine distress, he bravely agreed to try. That very night, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering steadily toward the undersea palace, its many eyes glowing eerily like torches in the pitch darkness. Hidesato took careful aim with his trusted bow and fired arrow after arrow at the approaching creature, but its impossibly tough shell seemed to turn every single arrow harmlessly aside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering an old story he had once heard, that ordinary human saliva could somehow harm such fearsome creatures, Hidesato wet the sharp tip of his very last remaining arrow with a touch of his own saliva before taking careful aim and firing one final time. This time, at long last, the arrow struck true and sank deep, and the giant centipede let out a terrible cry before finally collapsing, defeated once and for all. The overjoyed and deeply grateful princess welcomed Hidesato warmly into her magnificent undersea palace as an honoured guest, and rewarded his courage generously with a magical bag of rice that never once emptied, no matter how much rice was taken from it. From that remarkable day onward, Hidesato became known throughout the whole land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior, a name that honoured his bravery and his generous, never-ending gift.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in Japan, a brave and remarkably calm warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long, sturdy wooden bridge stretched high over a wide, still lake when he suddenly noticed a huge, coiled serpent stretched right across the very middle of his path, its great body completely blocking the narrow way forward. Most ordinary travellers, faced with such a terrifying sight, would surely have turned back at once in understandable fear, but Hidesato, known throughout the region for his unshakeable courage, simply stepped calmly and deliberately over the enormous serpent's body and continued steadily on his way without showing the slightest trace of alarm or hesitation in his measured stride. That very night, as he rested comfortably at a quiet nearby inn, a beautiful young woman quietly appeared before him without warning and explained, much to his genuine surprise, that the great serpent on the bridge had actually been herself in clever disguise all along, a princess from a magnificent underwater palace hidden beneath the lake's still surface, who regularly tested the courage of travellers as they crossed her family's bridge above.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gentle princess went on to explain, with clear and growing worry evident in her soft voice, that her family's beautiful undersea palace was under terrible and constant threat from a giant, fearsome centipede monster that made its lair upon a nearby mountain and attacked her once-peaceful home night after night without the slightest mercy or pause. She begged Hidesato earnestly and desperately for his brave help in driving the terrible monster away for good, and deeply moved by her genuine distress and the plight of her undersea family, he agreed at once, without hesitation, to try his very best. That very same night, right on schedule, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering steadily and menacingly toward the undersea palace gates, its countless glowing eyes shining eerily like a hundred distant torches in the pitch darkness of the deep water. Hidesato took careful, steady aim with his trusted bow and fired arrow after arrow at the rapidly approaching creature with practised skill, but its impossibly tough, armoured shell seemed to turn every single arrow harmlessly aside, no matter how carefully or forcefully he aimed each shot.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering, almost as a last resort, an old story he had once heard long ago as a boy, that ordinary human saliva could somehow harm such fearsome, otherwise invulnerable creatures, Hidesato calmly wet the sharp metal tip of his very last remaining arrow with a touch of his own saliva before taking one final, careful aim and firing with all the steady skill he possessed. This time, at long last, after so many failed attempts, the arrow struck true and sank deep into the creature's one vulnerable spot, and the giant centipede let out a terrible, echoing cry of pain before finally collapsing motionless, defeated once and for all after its long reign of terror. The overjoyed and deeply grateful princess welcomed brave Hidesato warmly into her magnificent undersea palace as an honoured and celebrated guest, feasting him generously for several days before rewarding his remarkable courage with a magical bag of rice that never once emptied, no matter how much rice was taken from it by anyone, however hungry. From that truly remarkable day onward, Hidesato became known and celebrated throughout the whole land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior, a fitting name that honoured both his quiet bravery and his wonderfully generous, never-ending magical gift.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato</li><li>the serpent</li><li>the bridge</li><li>the princess</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato crosses a bridge blocked by a huge serpent</li><li>He calmly steps over it and continues on</li><li>That night, a young woman appears before him</li><li>She reveals she was the serpent, testing his courage</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a huge, coiled serpent stretched right across the very middle of his path</li><li>simply stepped calmly and deliberately over the enormous serpent's body</li><li>without showing the slightest trace of alarm or hesitation</li><li>a princess from a magnificent underwater palace hidden beneath the lake's still surface</li><li>who regularly tested the courage of travellers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Hidesato calmly stepped over a huge ___ on the bridge, which was really a ___ in disguise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a brave warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long wooden bridge over a lake when he noticed a huge serpent stretched right across his path. Most travellers would have turned back in fear, but Hidesato calmly stepped over the serpent and continued on his way without a trace of alarm. That night, a young woman appeared before him and explained that the serpent had actually been herself in disguise, a princess from an underwater palace beneath the lake, testing his courage.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in Japan, a brave and calm warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long wooden bridge stretched over a wide lake when he suddenly noticed a huge serpent stretched right across the middle of his path, blocking the way completely. Most ordinary travellers would surely have turned back at once in fear, but Hidesato simply stepped calmly over the enormous serpent and continued on his way without showing the slightest trace of alarm or hesitation. That very night, as he rested at a nearby inn, a young woman quietly appeared before him and explained, to his surprise, that the serpent had actually been herself in disguise all along, a princess from a magnificent underwater palace beneath the lake, testing travellers' courage as they crossed the bridge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in Japan, a brave and remarkably calm warrior named Hidesato was crossing a long, sturdy wooden bridge stretched high over a wide, still lake when he suddenly noticed a huge, coiled serpent stretched right across the very middle of his path, its great body completely blocking the narrow way forward. Most ordinary travellers, faced with such a terrifying sight, would surely have turned back at once in understandable fear, but Hidesato, known throughout the region for his unshakeable courage, simply stepped calmly and deliberately over the enormous serpent's body and continued steadily on his way without showing the slightest trace of alarm or hesitation in his measured stride. That very night, as he rested comfortably at a quiet nearby inn, a beautiful young woman quietly appeared before him without warning and explained, much to his genuine surprise, that the great serpent on the bridge had actually been herself in clever disguise all along, a princess from a magnificent underwater palace hidden beneath the lake's still surface, who regularly tested the courage of travellers as they crossed her family's bridge above.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the palace</li><li>the centipede</li><li>Hidesato</li><li>arrows</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The princess asks Hidesato to defeat the giant centipede</li><li>He agrees and waits for the monster that night</li><li>The centipede attacks the underwater palace</li><li>His arrows cannot pierce its tough shell</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>under terrible and constant threat from a giant, fearsome centipede monster</li><li>attacks her once-peaceful home night after night without the slightest mercy</li><li>countless glowing eyes shining eerily like a hundred distant torches</li><li>its impossibly tough, armoured shell seemed to turn every single arrow harmlessly aside</li><li>no matter how carefully or forcefully he aimed each shot</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Hidesato agreed to defeat the ___, but his ___ could not pierce its shell.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The princess explained that her family's palace was under terrible threat from a giant centipede monster that lived on a nearby mountain and attacked her home every night. She begged Hidesato for his help, and moved by her plight, he bravely agreed. That night, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering toward the palace, its eyes glowing like torches in the darkness. Hidesato took aim carefully with his bow and fired arrow after arrow, but the creature's tough shell seemed impossible to pierce.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The princess went on to explain, with clear worry in her voice, that her family's beautiful undersea palace was under terrible and constant threat from a giant centipede monster that lived upon a nearby mountain and attacked her peaceful home night after night without mercy. She begged Hidesato earnestly for his help in driving the monster away for good, and moved by her genuine distress, he bravely agreed to try. That very night, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering steadily toward the undersea palace, its many eyes glowing eerily like torches in the pitch darkness. Hidesato took careful aim with his trusted bow and fired arrow after arrow at the approaching creature, but its impossibly tough shell seemed to turn every single arrow harmlessly aside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gentle princess went on to explain, with clear and growing worry evident in her soft voice, that her family's beautiful undersea palace was under terrible and constant threat from a giant, fearsome centipede monster that made its lair upon a nearby mountain and attacked her once-peaceful home night after night without the slightest mercy or pause. She begged Hidesato earnestly and desperately for his brave help in driving the terrible monster away for good, and deeply moved by her genuine distress and the plight of her undersea family, he agreed at once, without hesitation, to try his very best. That very same night, right on schedule, the fearsome giant centipede came slithering steadily and menacingly toward the undersea palace gates, its countless glowing eyes shining eerily like a hundred distant torches in the pitch darkness of the deep water. Hidesato took careful, steady aim with his trusted bow and fired arrow after arrow at the rapidly approaching creature with practised skill, but its impossibly tough, armoured shell seemed to turn every single arrow harmlessly aside, no matter how carefully or forcefully he aimed each shot.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>saliva</li><li>the arrow</li><li>the princess</li><li>the rice bag</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hidesato remembers that human saliva can harm the creature</li><li>He wets his final arrow and fires it</li><li>The arrow strikes true and defeats the centipede</li><li>The grateful princess rewards him with a magic rice bag</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>that ordinary human saliva could somehow harm such fearsome, otherwise invulnerable creatures</li><li>wet the sharp metal tip of his very last remaining arrow</li><li>sank deep into the creature's one vulnerable spot</li><li>let out a terrible, echoing cry of pain before finally collapsing motionless</li><li>a magical bag of rice that never once emptied, no matter how much rice was taken</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Hidesato wet his last ___ with saliva and defeated the monster, earning a magic ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering an old story that human saliva could harm such creatures, Hidesato wet the tip of his final arrow with a touch of his own saliva before firing. This time, the arrow struck true, and the giant centipede finally fell, defeated at last. The grateful princess welcomed Hidesato warmly into the palace and rewarded him with a magical bag of rice that never emptied, no matter how much was taken from it. From that day on, Hidesato was known throughout the land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering an old story he had once heard, that ordinary human saliva could somehow harm such fearsome creatures, Hidesato wet the sharp tip of his very last remaining arrow with a touch of his own saliva before taking careful aim and firing one final time. This time, at long last, the arrow struck true and sank deep, and the giant centipede let out a terrible cry before finally collapsing, defeated once and for all. The overjoyed and deeply grateful princess welcomed Hidesato warmly into her magnificent undersea palace as an honoured guest, and rewarded his courage generously with a magical bag of rice that never once emptied, no matter how much rice was taken from it. From that remarkable day onward, Hidesato became known throughout the whole land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior, a name that honoured his bravery and his generous, never-ending gift.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remembering, almost as a last resort, an old story he had once heard long ago as a boy, that ordinary human saliva could somehow harm such fearsome, otherwise invulnerable creatures, Hidesato calmly wet the sharp metal tip of his very last remaining arrow with a touch of his own saliva before taking one final, careful aim and firing with all the steady skill he possessed. This time, at long last, after so many failed attempts, the arrow struck true and sank deep into the creature's one vulnerable spot, and the giant centipede let out a terrible, echoing cry of pain before finally collapsing motionless, defeated once and for all after its long reign of terror. The overjoyed and deeply grateful princess welcomed brave Hidesato warmly into her magnificent undersea palace as an honoured and celebrated guest, feasting him generously for several days before rewarding his remarkable courage with a magical bag of rice that never once emptied, no matter how much rice was taken from it by anyone, however hungry. From that truly remarkable day onward, Hidesato became known and celebrated throughout the whole land as Tawara Toda, the Rice-Bag Warrior, a fitting name that honoured both his quiet bravery and his wonderfully generous, never-ending magical gift.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 17: Tawara Toda (The Rice-Bag Warrior)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 17 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 17 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 18 (Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Hidesato calmly stepped over a huge ___ on the bridge, which was really a ___ in disguise.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Hidesato agreed to defeat the ___, but his ___ could not pierce its shell.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Hidesato wet his last ___ with saliva and defeated the monster, earning a magic ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 18’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Netaro and the villagers as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro</li><li>the villagers</li><li>the river</li><li>the boulders</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro sleeps for three years while neighbours call him lazy</li><li>A terrible drought threatens the village's crops</li><li>Netaro suddenly rises and moves huge boulders at the river</li><li>He redirects the river and saves the crops, revealed as a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro slept for three whole years while others thought him lazy</li><li>A terrible drought threatened to ruin the village's crops</li><li>Netaro moved boulders that ten men together could not budge</li><li>Netaro's years of sleep were actually patient preparation for this moment</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a farming village in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do nothing but sleep, day after day, for three whole years. His neighbours whispered that he was hopelessly lazy, and even his own worried mother struggled to understand why her son would not get up and help with the farm work like everyone else in the village. Little did anyone know, Netaro was secretly using this quiet time to build up incredible strength within himself, resting and preparing his body for something important that lay ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One year, a terrible drought struck the region, drying up the rivers and threatening to ruin every farmer's crops in the whole village. The villagers grew desperate, arguing about how they might possibly redirect what little water remained from a distant river to save their dying fields. Suddenly, to everyone's astonishment, Netaro rose from his sleeping mat for the first time in years, stretching his arms as though waking from a long and restful nap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without a single word of explanation, Netaro walked calmly to the riverbank and began moving enormous boulders that had blocked the water's natural path, lifting stones that no ordinary group of ten men could have budged together. Working steadily through the day, he redirected the entire river toward the village's thirsty fields, saving every single crop from the terrible drought. The astonished villagers realised that Netaro's three long years of sleep had not been laziness at all, but patient preparation for this very moment, and he was celebrated as a hero from that day forward.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet farming village in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do nothing at all but sleep, day after day, for three entire years without ever once getting up to help with any of the usual farm work. His neighbours whispered disapprovingly among themselves that he was hopelessly and shamefully lazy, and even his own worried mother struggled greatly to understand why her only son would not simply get up and pull his weight like every other young person in the village. Little did anyone around him know, however, that Netaro was secretly using this long, quiet time to patiently build up an extraordinary reserve of strength deep within himself, resting his body completely and preparing carefully for something important that he somehow sensed still lay ahead, though he could not yet have said exactly what.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly difficult year, a terrible drought struck the entire region without warning, drying up the rivers to a mere trickle and threatening to ruin every single farmer's crops throughout the whole village before the harvest could even begin. The increasingly desperate villagers gathered again and again, arguing anxiously about how they might possibly redirect what little water still remained from a distant river toward their dying, cracked fields before it was too late. Suddenly, one ordinary morning, to the astonishment of absolutely everyone watching, Netaro finally rose from his sleeping mat for the first time in three long years, stretching his arms slowly and calmly as though simply waking from an unusually long but restful nap, rather than three whole years of apparent idleness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without offering a single word of explanation to anyone, Netaro walked calmly and purposefully down to the riverbank and began moving enormous boulders that had long blocked the water's natural path toward the village, lifting massive stones single-handedly that no ordinary group of even ten strong men working together could ever have hoped to budge. Working steadily and tirelessly throughout the entire day without a moment's rest, he skillfully redirected the whole force of the river toward the village's parched, thirsty fields, saving every single crop from the terrible drought just in the nick of time. The utterly astonished villagers finally realised, with growing wonder and shame at their earlier judgments, that Netaro's three long years of apparent sleep had never been laziness at all, but rather patient, deliberate preparation for this very moment, and from that day forward he was celebrated warmly and proudly as a true hero throughout the entire village.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet, hardworking farming village somewhere in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do absolutely nothing at all but sleep, day after day, month after month, for three entire long years without ever once getting up properly to help with any of the usual, endless farm work expected of every young person in the village. His neighbours whispered disapprovingly and often quite unkindly among themselves that he must surely be hopelessly and shamefully lazy, a burden rather than a help to his family, and even his own worried, increasingly exasperated mother struggled greatly, day after day, to understand why her only son would not simply get up off his sleeping mat and pull his fair share of the weight like every other young person in the village around him. Little did anyone around him know, however, not even his own mother, that Netaro was secretly using this long, quiet, seemingly idle time to patiently build up an extraordinary, almost superhuman reserve of strength deep within his sleeping body, resting completely and preparing himself carefully, day by day, for something important that he somehow sensed still lay ahead of him, though even he could not yet have said exactly what that moment would be or when it might finally arrive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly difficult year, a terrible and unrelenting drought struck the entire surrounding region without any real warning, drying up the once-flowing rivers to little more than a thin, cracked trickle and threatening seriously to ruin every single farmer's precious crops throughout the whole village long before the autumn harvest could ever begin in earnest. The increasingly desperate and frightened villagers gathered together again and again in worried meetings, arguing anxiously late into the night about how they might possibly manage to redirect what little water still remained flowing in a distant river toward their dying, deeply cracked fields before it was simply too late to save anything at all. Suddenly, one perfectly ordinary morning, to the utter astonishment of absolutely everyone who happened to be watching nearby, Netaro finally rose calmly from his sleeping mat for the very first time in three long, uneventful years, stretching his powerful arms slowly and quite calmly, as though he were simply waking from an unusually long but entirely restful afternoon nap, rather than from three whole years of what everyone had firmly assumed was shameful idleness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without offering a single word of explanation to his stunned mother or to any of the gathering, astonished villagers, Netaro walked calmly, confidently, and with clear purpose down toward the parched riverbank, and there began single-handedly moving enormous boulders that had long ago blocked the river's natural path toward the village's fields, effortlessly lifting massive stones that no ordinary group of even ten strong, determined men working together could ever have hoped to budge even an inch. Working steadily, tirelessly, and without complaint throughout the entire long day, never once pausing to rest, he skillfully and carefully redirected the whole considerable force of the river toward the village's parched, desperately thirsty fields, saving every single remaining crop from the terrible, lingering drought just in the very nick of time before true disaster could strike. The utterly astonished villagers, gathering afterward in stunned silence to watch him work, finally realised, with growing wonder and no small measure of shame at their earlier harsh judgments, that Netaro's three long years of apparent, shameful laziness had never truly been laziness at all, but rather patient, deliberate, and remarkably disciplined preparation for this single, crucial moment, and from that unforgettable day forward he was celebrated warmly, proudly, and gratefully as a true hero throughout the entire grateful village for the rest of his long life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro</li><li>the neighbours</li><li>his mother</li><li>strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro sleeps for three whole years without working</li><li>His neighbours whisper that he is hopelessly lazy</li><li>His mother struggles to understand him</li><li>He is secretly building up incredible strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>do absolutely nothing at all but sleep, day after day, month after month</li><li>whispered disapprovingly and often quite unkindly among themselves</li><li>struggled greatly, day after day, to understand why her only son would not simply get up</li><li>secretly using this long, quiet, seemingly idle time</li><li>build up an extraordinary, almost superhuman reserve of strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, ___ slept for three whole years while secretly building up incredible ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a farming village in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do nothing but sleep, day after day, for three whole years. His neighbours whispered that he was hopelessly lazy, and even his own worried mother struggled to understand why her son would not get up and help with the farm work like everyone else in the village. Little did anyone know, Netaro was secretly using this quiet time to build up incredible strength within himself, resting and preparing his body for something important that lay ahead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet farming village in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do nothing at all but sleep, day after day, for three entire years without ever once getting up to help with any of the usual farm work. His neighbours whispered disapprovingly among themselves that he was hopelessly and shamefully lazy, and even his own worried mother struggled greatly to understand why her only son would not simply get up and pull his weight like every other young person in the village. Little did anyone around him know, however, that Netaro was secretly using this long, quiet time to patiently build up an extraordinary reserve of strength deep within himself, resting his body completely and preparing carefully for something important that he somehow sensed still lay ahead, though he could not yet have said exactly what.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a quiet, hardworking farming village somewhere in Japan, there lived a young man named Netaro who seemed to do absolutely nothing at all but sleep, day after day, month after month, for three entire long years without ever once getting up properly to help with any of the usual, endless farm work expected of every young person in the village. His neighbours whispered disapprovingly and often quite unkindly among themselves that he must surely be hopelessly and shamefully lazy, a burden rather than a help to his family, and even his own worried, increasingly exasperated mother struggled greatly, day after day, to understand why her only son would not simply get up off his sleeping mat and pull his fair share of the weight like every other young person in the village around him. Little did anyone around him know, however, not even his own mother, that Netaro was secretly using this long, quiet, seemingly idle time to patiently build up an extraordinary, almost superhuman reserve of strength deep within his sleeping body, resting completely and preparing himself carefully, day by day, for something important that he somehow sensed still lay ahead of him, though even he could not yet have said exactly what that moment would be or when it might finally arrive.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the drought</li><li>the villagers</li><li>the river</li><li>Netaro</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A terrible drought strikes the village</li><li>The crops are threatened and the villagers grow desperate</li><li>They argue about redirecting water from a distant river</li><li>Netaro suddenly rises from his sleeping mat</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>drying up the once-flowing rivers to little more than a thin, cracked trickle</li><li>threatening seriously to ruin every single farmer's precious crops</li><li>gathered together again and again in worried meetings, arguing anxiously</li><li>redirect what little water still remained flowing in a distant river</li><li>rose calmly from his sleeping mat for the very first time in three long</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a terrible ___ threatened the crops, and Netaro suddenly rose from his sleeping ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One year, a terrible drought struck the region, drying up the rivers and threatening to ruin every farmer's crops in the whole village. The villagers grew desperate, arguing about how they might possibly redirect what little water remained from a distant river to save their dying fields. Suddenly, to everyone's astonishment, Netaro rose from his sleeping mat for the first time in years, stretching his arms as though waking from a long and restful nap.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly difficult year, a terrible drought struck the entire region without warning, drying up the rivers to a mere trickle and threatening to ruin every single farmer's crops throughout the whole village before the harvest could even begin. The increasingly desperate villagers gathered again and again, arguing anxiously about how they might possibly redirect what little water still remained from a distant river toward their dying, cracked fields before it was too late. Suddenly, one ordinary morning, to the astonishment of absolutely everyone watching, Netaro finally rose from his sleeping mat for the first time in three long years, stretching his arms slowly and calmly as though simply waking from an unusually long but restful nap, rather than three whole years of apparent idleness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly difficult year, a terrible and unrelenting drought struck the entire surrounding region without any real warning, drying up the once-flowing rivers to little more than a thin, cracked trickle and threatening seriously to ruin every single farmer's precious crops throughout the whole village long before the autumn harvest could ever begin in earnest. The increasingly desperate and frightened villagers gathered together again and again in worried meetings, arguing anxiously late into the night about how they might possibly manage to redirect what little water still remained flowing in a distant river toward their dying, deeply cracked fields before it was simply too late to save anything at all. Suddenly, one perfectly ordinary morning, to the utter astonishment of absolutely everyone who happened to be watching nearby, Netaro finally rose calmly from his sleeping mat for the very first time in three long, uneventful years, stretching his powerful arms slowly and quite calmly, as though he were simply waking from an unusually long but entirely restful afternoon nap, rather than from three whole years of what everyone had firmly assumed was shameful idleness.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the boulders</li><li>the river</li><li>the crops</li><li>a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Netaro walks to the riverbank without explanation</li><li>He moves enormous boulders no group of men could budge</li><li>He redirects the river to save the village's crops</li><li>The villagers realise he was preparing all along and call him a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>single-handedly moving enormous boulders that had long ago blocked the river's natural path</li><li>no ordinary group of even ten strong, determined men working together</li><li>redirected the whole considerable force of the river toward the village's parched, desperately thirsty fields</li><li>patient, deliberate, and remarkably disciplined preparation for this single, crucial moment</li><li>celebrated warmly, proudly, and gratefully as a true hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Netaro moved huge ___ to redirect the river, saving the crops and becoming a celebrated ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without a single word of explanation, Netaro walked calmly to the riverbank and began moving enormous boulders that had blocked the water's natural path, lifting stones that no ordinary group of ten men could have budged together. Working steadily through the day, he redirected the entire river toward the village's thirsty fields, saving every single crop from the terrible drought. The astonished villagers realised that Netaro's three long years of sleep had not been laziness at all, but patient preparation for this very moment, and he was celebrated as a hero from that day forward.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without offering a single word of explanation to anyone, Netaro walked calmly and purposefully down to the riverbank and began moving enormous boulders that had long blocked the water's natural path toward the village, lifting massive stones single-handedly that no ordinary group of even ten strong men working together could ever have hoped to budge. Working steadily and tirelessly throughout the entire day without a moment's rest, he skillfully redirected the whole force of the river toward the village's parched, thirsty fields, saving every single crop from the terrible drought just in the nick of time. The utterly astonished villagers finally realised, with growing wonder and shame at their earlier judgments, that Netaro's three long years of apparent sleep had never been laziness at all, but rather patient, deliberate preparation for this very moment, and from that day forward he was celebrated warmly and proudly as a true hero throughout the entire village.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without offering a single word of explanation to his stunned mother or to any of the gathering, astonished villagers, Netaro walked calmly, confidently, and with clear purpose down toward the parched riverbank, and there began single-handedly moving enormous boulders that had long ago blocked the river's natural path toward the village's fields, effortlessly lifting massive stones that no ordinary group of even ten strong, determined men working together could ever have hoped to budge even an inch. Working steadily, tirelessly, and without complaint throughout the entire long day, never once pausing to rest, he skillfully and carefully redirected the whole considerable force of the river toward the village's parched, desperately thirsty fields, saving every single remaining crop from the terrible, lingering drought just in the very nick of time before true disaster could strike. The utterly astonished villagers, gathering afterward in stunned silence to watch him work, finally realised, with growing wonder and no small measure of shame at their earlier harsh judgments, that Netaro's three long years of apparent, shameful laziness had never truly been laziness at all, but rather patient, deliberate, and remarkably disciplined preparation for this single, crucial moment, and from that unforgettable day forward he was celebrated warmly, proudly, and gratefully as a true hero throughout the entire grateful village for the rest of his long life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 18: Sannen Netaro (Three-Year Sleeping Taro)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 18 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 18 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 19 (Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, ___ slept for three whole years while secretly building up incredible ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a terrible ___ threatened the crops, and Netaro suddenly rose from his sleeping ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Netaro moved huge ___ to redirect the river, saving the crops and becoming a celebrated ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 19’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Yoshitsune and the tengu as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>the tengu</li><li>Mount Kurama</li><li>the wooden sword</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune is sent to a temple and practises swordsmanship alone at night</li><li>A tengu watches him from the shadows of the forest</li><li>The tengu is impressed and offers to secretly train him</li><li>Yoshitsune becomes a legendary warrior thanks to the tengu's training</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune practised his wooden sword alone at night in the forest</li><li>The tengu was a legendary forest guardian with wings and a long nose</li><li>The tengu frightened Yoshitsune away instead of training him</li><li>Yoshitsune became one of Japan's most celebrated legendary generals</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent to live at a quiet temple on Mount Kurama after his family fell on hard times. Every night, while the other students at the temple slept, Yoshitsune would sneak out alone into the forest to practise swinging his wooden sword against the trees, determined to become a skilled warrior one day, no matter how difficult the training became.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, deep in the forest, Yoshitsune noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly from the shadows between the pine trees. This was a tengu, a legendary forest guardian with a long nose and feathered wings, said to be a master of swordsmanship and clever strategy. Rather than frightening the boy away, the tengu was impressed by Yoshitsune's dedication and offered to teach him everything it knew about fighting and strategy in secret.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night, the tengu trained Yoshitsune deep in the forest, teaching him swift sword techniques and clever battle strategies that no ordinary teacher could have shown him. Under the tengu's careful guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably skilled, far beyond anything the temple's regular lessons could have taught him. Years later, Yoshitsune left the mountain as a fully trained warrior and went on to become one of the most celebrated and legendary generals in all of Japan's history, always remembering the mysterious teacher who trained him in secret on Mount Kurama.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent away to live at a quiet, isolated temple on Mount Kurama after his family fell on particularly hard times and could no longer properly care for him at home. Every single night, while all the other students at the temple slept soundly in their beds, young Yoshitsune would quietly sneak out alone into the dark surrounding forest to practise swinging his simple wooden sword against the sturdy trees, determined with unusual intensity to become a truly skilled warrior one day, no matter how exhausting or difficult the lonely training became for him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particular night, deep within the shadowy forest, Yoshitsune suddenly noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly and intently from the deep shadows between the towering pine trees. This mysterious figure was a tengu, a legendary forest guardian spirit with a notably long nose and great feathered wings, widely said among old stories to be a true master of both swordsmanship and clever military strategy. Rather than frightening the determined young boy away, as one might expect, the tengu found itself genuinely impressed by Yoshitsune's remarkable dedication and quiet persistence, and it offered, after watching him practise for some time, to teach him everything it knew about fighting and clever strategy, all in careful secret.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night from that point onward, the patient tengu trained Yoshitsune deep within the forest, carefully teaching him swift, precise sword techniques and clever battle strategies that no ordinary human teacher back at the temple could ever possibly have shown him. Under the tengu's careful and demanding guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably and rapidly skilled, far beyond anything the temple's regular, everyday lessons could ever have taught him on their own. Years later, once he felt truly ready, Yoshitsune finally left the mountain behind as a fully trained and formidable warrior, and went on in time to become one of the most celebrated and genuinely legendary generals in the whole of Japan's long history, though he always remembered, with quiet gratitude, the mysterious winged teacher who had trained him faithfully in secret on Mount Kurama.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent away to live at a quiet, isolated temple high on Mount Kurama after his family fell on particularly hard and difficult times, times so hard that they could no longer properly care for him or keep him safely at home with them. Every single night, without fail, while all the other students living at the temple slept soundly and peacefully in their simple beds, young Yoshitsune would quietly and carefully sneak out entirely alone into the dark, silent surrounding forest to practise swinging his simple wooden training sword against the sturdy old trees for hours at a time, determined with quite unusual and fierce intensity to become a truly skilled warrior one day, no matter how exhausting, painful, or difficult the lonely nightly training might become for him as the months wore on.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particular night, deep within the shadowy, silent forest, Yoshitsune suddenly and unexpectedly noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly and intently from the deep, concealing shadows between the towering ancient pine trees nearby. This mysterious and rather startling figure was, in fact, a tengu, a legendary forest guardian spirit possessing a notably long nose and a pair of great, feathered wings, widely said in old stories passed down for generations to be a true master of both swift swordsmanship and clever, patient military strategy. Rather than frightening the determined young boy away entirely, as one might reasonably expect from such an encounter, the watchful tengu instead found itself genuinely and increasingly impressed by Yoshitsune's remarkable dedication, discipline, and quiet persistence, and it eventually offered, after observing him practise alone for quite some time in silence, to teach him everything it personally knew about fighting skillfully and thinking strategically, all conducted in careful, complete secret between the two of them alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night from that point onward, for what became many long months and eventually years, the patient and demanding tengu trained young Yoshitsune deep within the quiet forest, carefully and painstakingly teaching him swift, precise sword techniques and genuinely clever battle strategies that no ordinary human teacher back at the busy temple could ever possibly have shown him, however skilled they themselves might have been. Under the tengu's famously careful, patient, and often demanding guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably and rapidly skilled with each passing season, developing abilities that reached far beyond anything the temple's regular, everyday lessons could ever hope to have taught him on their own, however diligently he might have studied there instead. Years later, once he finally felt truly and completely ready to face the wider world, Yoshitsune at last left the quiet mountain behind him as a fully trained, formidable, and remarkably skilled warrior, and went on in the years that followed to become one of the most celebrated, admired, and genuinely legendary generals in the whole of Japan's long and storied history, though he always remembered, with quiet and lasting gratitude for the rest of his life, the mysterious winged teacher who had faithfully and patiently trained him in complete secret, night after night, high on Mount Kurama.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>the temple</li><li>Mount Kurama</li><li>the wooden sword</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune is sent to live at a temple on Mount Kurama</li><li>His family could no longer care for him at home</li><li>He sneaks out alone every night into the forest</li><li>He practises swinging his wooden sword against the trees</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>fell on particularly hard and difficult times</li><li>could no longer properly care for him or keep him safely at home</li><li>quietly and carefully sneak out entirely alone into the dark, silent surrounding forest</li><li>practise swinging his simple wooden training sword against the sturdy old trees</li><li>determined with quite unusual and fierce intensity</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Yoshitsune was sent to a temple on ___, and he secretly practised with his wooden ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent to live at a quiet temple on Mount Kurama after his family fell on hard times. Every night, while the other students at the temple slept, Yoshitsune would sneak out alone into the forest to practise swinging his wooden sword against the trees, determined to become a skilled warrior one day, no matter how difficult the training became.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent away to live at a quiet, isolated temple on Mount Kurama after his family fell on particularly hard times and could no longer properly care for him at home. Every single night, while all the other students at the temple slept soundly in their beds, young Yoshitsune would quietly sneak out alone into the dark surrounding forest to practise swinging his simple wooden sword against the sturdy trees, determined with unusual intensity to become a truly skilled warrior one day, no matter how exhausting or difficult the lonely training became for him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, a young boy named Yoshitsune was sent away to live at a quiet, isolated temple high on Mount Kurama after his family fell on particularly hard and difficult times, times so hard that they could no longer properly care for him or keep him safely at home with them. Every single night, without fail, while all the other students living at the temple slept soundly and peacefully in their simple beds, young Yoshitsune would quietly and carefully sneak out entirely alone into the dark, silent surrounding forest to practise swinging his simple wooden training sword against the sturdy old trees for hours at a time, determined with quite unusual and fierce intensity to become a truly skilled warrior one day, no matter how exhausting, painful, or difficult the lonely nightly training might become for him as the months wore on.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tengu</li><li>the shadows</li><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>strategy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A tall, winged tengu watches Yoshitsune from the shadows</li><li>The tengu is a legendary master of swordsmanship and strategy</li><li>It is impressed by his dedication rather than frightened away</li><li>It offers to secretly train him in fighting and strategy</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a legendary forest guardian spirit possessing a notably long nose and a pair of great, feathered wings</li><li>a true master of both swift swordsmanship and clever, patient military strategy</li><li>genuinely and increasingly impressed by Yoshitsune's remarkable dedication, discipline, and quiet persistence</li><li>teach him everything it personally knew about fighting skillfully and thinking strategically</li><li>conducted in careful, complete secret between the two of them alone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the ___ was impressed by Yoshitsune and offered to secretly teach him fighting and ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One night, deep in the forest, Yoshitsune noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly from the shadows between the pine trees. This was a tengu, a legendary forest guardian with a long nose and feathered wings, said to be a master of swordsmanship and clever strategy. Rather than frightening the boy away, the tengu was impressed by Yoshitsune's dedication and offered to teach him everything it knew about fighting and strategy in secret.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particular night, deep within the shadowy forest, Yoshitsune suddenly noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly and intently from the deep shadows between the towering pine trees. This mysterious figure was a tengu, a legendary forest guardian spirit with a notably long nose and great feathered wings, widely said among old stories to be a true master of both swordsmanship and clever military strategy. Rather than frightening the determined young boy away, as one might expect, the tengu found itself genuinely impressed by Yoshitsune's remarkable dedication and quiet persistence, and it offered, after watching him practise for some time, to teach him everything it knew about fighting and clever strategy, all in careful secret.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particular night, deep within the shadowy, silent forest, Yoshitsune suddenly and unexpectedly noticed a tall, winged figure watching him quietly and intently from the deep, concealing shadows between the towering ancient pine trees nearby. This mysterious and rather startling figure was, in fact, a tengu, a legendary forest guardian spirit possessing a notably long nose and a pair of great, feathered wings, widely said in old stories passed down for generations to be a true master of both swift swordsmanship and clever, patient military strategy. Rather than frightening the determined young boy away entirely, as one might reasonably expect from such an encounter, the watchful tengu instead found itself genuinely and increasingly impressed by Yoshitsune's remarkable dedication, discipline, and quiet persistence, and it eventually offered, after observing him practise alone for quite some time in silence, to teach him everything it personally knew about fighting skillfully and thinking strategically, all conducted in careful, complete secret between the two of them alone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tengu</li><li>sword techniques</li><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>a legendary general</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tengu trains Yoshitsune night after night in the forest</li><li>Yoshitsune learns swift sword techniques and clever strategy</li><li>He grows far more skilled than temple lessons could teach</li><li>He becomes one of Japan's most celebrated legendary generals</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>carefully and painstakingly teaching him swift, precise sword techniques</li><li>genuinely clever battle strategies that no ordinary human teacher</li><li>grew remarkably and rapidly skilled with each passing season</li><li>left the quiet mountain behind him as a fully trained, formidable, and remarkably skilled warrior</li><li>one of the most celebrated, admired, and genuinely legendary generals</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the tengu's secret training helped Yoshitsune become one of Japan's most celebrated ___, thanks to his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night, the tengu trained Yoshitsune deep in the forest, teaching him swift sword techniques and clever battle strategies that no ordinary teacher could have shown him. Under the tengu's careful guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably skilled, far beyond anything the temple's regular lessons could have taught him. Years later, Yoshitsune left the mountain as a fully trained warrior and went on to become one of the most celebrated and legendary generals in all of Japan's history, always remembering the mysterious teacher who trained him in secret on Mount Kurama.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night from that point onward, the patient tengu trained Yoshitsune deep within the forest, carefully teaching him swift, precise sword techniques and clever battle strategies that no ordinary human teacher back at the temple could ever possibly have shown him. Under the tengu's careful and demanding guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably and rapidly skilled, far beyond anything the temple's regular, everyday lessons could ever have taught him on their own. Years later, once he felt truly ready, Yoshitsune finally left the mountain behind as a fully trained and formidable warrior, and went on in time to become one of the most celebrated and genuinely legendary generals in the whole of Japan's long history, though he always remembered, with quiet gratitude, the mysterious winged teacher who had trained him faithfully in secret on Mount Kurama.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night after night from that point onward, for what became many long months and eventually years, the patient and demanding tengu trained young Yoshitsune deep within the quiet forest, carefully and painstakingly teaching him swift, precise sword techniques and genuinely clever battle strategies that no ordinary human teacher back at the busy temple could ever possibly have shown him, however skilled they themselves might have been. Under the tengu's famously careful, patient, and often demanding guidance, Yoshitsune grew remarkably and rapidly skilled with each passing season, developing abilities that reached far beyond anything the temple's regular, everyday lessons could ever hope to have taught him on their own, however diligently he might have studied there instead. Years later, once he finally felt truly and completely ready to face the wider world, Yoshitsune at last left the quiet mountain behind him as a fully trained, formidable, and remarkably skilled warrior, and went on in the years that followed to become one of the most celebrated, admired, and genuinely legendary generals in the whole of Japan's long and storied history, though he always remembered, with quiet and lasting gratitude for the rest of his life, the mysterious winged teacher who had faithfully and patiently trained him in complete secret, night after night, high on Mount Kurama.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 19: Yoshitsune and the Tengu of Mt. Kurama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 19 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 19 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 20 (The Grateful Fox) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Yoshitsune was sent to a temple on ___, and he secretly practised with his wooden ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the ___ was impressed by Yoshitsune and offered to secretly teach him fighting and ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the tengu's secret training helped Yoshitsune become one of Japan's most celebrated ___, thanks to his ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 20: The Grateful Fox":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: The Grateful Fox</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 20’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 20: The Grateful Fox) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Kenji and the fox as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kenji</li><li>the fox</li><li>the trap</li><li>the riverbank</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kenji frees a fox caught in a hunter's trap and bandages its leg</li><li>The fox secretly begins mending fences and guarding the crops</li><li>The fox urgently warns Kenji of a collapsing riverbank at night</li><li>Kenji's family escapes safely, and he leaves food for his friend</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kenji freed a fox caught in a hunter's trap and bandaged its leg</li><li>The fox secretly began guarding Kenji's crops from pests</li><li>The fox ignored Kenji completely after it healed and ran away</li><li>The fox warned Kenji about a collapsing riverbank at night</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind farmer named Kenji found a small fox caught tightly in a hunter's trap at the edge of his field. Feeling sorry for the frightened creature, Kenji carefully freed the fox and gently bandaged its injured leg before letting it limp away into the forest. He did not expect anything in return and soon forgot about the small act of kindness as his busy farming days continued.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many months later, strange things began happening around Kenji's farm. Every morning, he found his fences quietly mended and his tools tidied away, as though some helpful spirit had visited overnight. One evening, hiding quietly to watch, Kenji spotted the very same fox, now fully healed, carefully patrolling the edges of his field, chasing away hungry crows and other pests that might have damaged his crops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One stormy night, the fox suddenly appeared at Kenji's door, barking urgently and tugging at his sleeve. Confused but trusting the animal, Kenji followed it outside, only to discover that a nearby riverbank was about to collapse and flood his fields entirely. Thanks to the fox's timely warning, Kenji managed to move his family and animals to safety just in time. From that day forward, Kenji always left a small offering of food for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two shared a quiet, lasting bond of gratitude.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, a kind and gentle farmer named Kenji found a small fox caught tightly and painfully in a hunter's cruel trap at the edge of his field one quiet morning. Feeling immediate sympathy for the frightened, whimpering creature, Kenji carefully and patiently worked the trap open to free the fox, then gently cleaned and bandaged its injured leg before finally letting it limp slowly away into the safety of the nearby forest. He did not expect anything in return for his simple kindness and soon largely forgot about the small act as his busy, demanding farming days continued on much as before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many months later, strange and rather puzzling things gradually began happening around Kenji's farm. Every single morning, without fail, he found his fences quietly mended overnight and his scattered tools tidied neatly away, almost as though some helpful, unseen spirit had visited the farm secretly while everyone slept. One quiet evening, deciding finally to hide and watch closely to solve the mystery once and for all, Kenji spotted the very same fox from months before, now fully healed and moving with ease, carefully patrolling the edges of his field, busily chasing away hungry crows and other troublesome pests that might otherwise have damaged his precious crops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly stormy night, the fox suddenly appeared at Kenji's front door, barking loudly and urgently and tugging insistently at his sleeve with its teeth. Confused but instinctively trusting the clever animal completely, Kenji quickly followed it out into the driving rain, only to discover with horror that a nearby riverbank was dangerously close to collapsing entirely and flooding his family's fields. Thanks entirely to the fox's timely and urgent warning, Kenji managed to move his whole family and all their farm animals to safety just barely in time before the riverbank gave way completely. From that unforgettable day forward, Kenji always made sure to leave a small offering of food out for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two shared a quiet, lasting bond of gratitude and trust for many years afterward.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful Japanese countryside, a kind and gentle farmer named Kenji happened to find a small fox caught tightly and quite painfully in a cruel hunter's trap at the very edge of his field early one quiet morning as he walked out to check on his crops. Feeling immediate and genuine sympathy for the frightened, whimpering little creature struggling helplessly before him, Kenji carefully and patiently worked the stubborn trap open to free the fox completely, then gently cleaned and carefully bandaged its injured leg with a strip of cloth before finally letting it limp slowly and gratefully away into the welcoming safety of the nearby forest. He did not expect anything at all in return for such a simple, unremarkable act of kindness, and quite soon largely forgot about the whole small incident entirely as his busy, demanding daily farming work continued on much as it always had before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long months later, strange and rather puzzling little things gradually began happening around Kenji's quiet farm, one after another. Every single morning, without a single exception, he found his wooden fences quietly mended overnight and his scattered farming tools tidied neatly away in their proper places, almost as though some helpful, entirely unseen spirit had visited the farm secretly while the whole household slept soundly. One particularly quiet evening, deciding finally to hide patiently nearby and watch closely in order to solve the growing mystery once and for all, Kenji spotted, to his genuine surprise, the very same fox from all those months before, now fully healed and moving about with complete ease, carefully and diligently patrolling the edges of his field, busily chasing away hungry crows and various other troublesome pests that might otherwise have seriously damaged his precious, hard-won crops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly stormy and windswept night, the fox suddenly and quite urgently appeared right at Kenji's front door, barking loudly, insistently, and quite unlike its usual quiet self, tugging firmly and repeatedly at his sleeve with its teeth as though trying desperately to communicate something important. Confused at first but instinctively trusting the clever, familiar animal completely, Kenji quickly grabbed his coat and followed it straight out into the driving, howling rain, only to discover with sudden horror that a nearby riverbank, weakened by days of heavy rain, was dangerously close to collapsing entirely and flooding his family's precious fields and even their modest home. Thanks entirely to the fox's timely, urgent, and quite possibly life-saving warning, Kenji managed to wake his whole family and quickly move every one of them, along with all their farm animals, safely uphill just barely in time before the riverbank finally gave way completely with a tremendous, roaring crash. From that unforgettable and deeply grateful day forward, Kenji made absolutely certain to always leave a small, generous offering of food out near the field's edge for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two of them shared a quiet, lasting, and deeply mutual bond of gratitude, trust, and genuine friendship for many long years that followed.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: The Grateful Fox</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kenji</li><li>the fox</li><li>the trap</li><li>the leg</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kenji finds a fox caught in a hunter's trap</li><li>He feels sympathy for the frightened creature</li><li>He carefully frees the fox and bandages its leg</li><li>He lets it limp away, expecting nothing in return</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>caught tightly and quite painfully in a cruel hunter's trap</li><li>feeling immediate and genuine sympathy for the frightened, whimpering little creature</li><li>carefully and patiently worked the stubborn trap open to free the fox</li><li>gently cleaned and carefully bandaged its injured leg</li><li>did not expect anything at all in return</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Kenji freed a ___ from a hunter's ___ and bandaged its leg.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind farmer named Kenji found a small fox caught tightly in a hunter's trap at the edge of his field. Feeling sorry for the frightened creature, Kenji carefully freed the fox and gently bandaged its injured leg before letting it limp away into the forest. He did not expect anything in return and soon forgot about the small act of kindness as his busy farming days continued.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, a kind and gentle farmer named Kenji found a small fox caught tightly and painfully in a hunter's cruel trap at the edge of his field one quiet morning. Feeling immediate sympathy for the frightened, whimpering creature, Kenji carefully and patiently worked the trap open to free the fox, then gently cleaned and bandaged its injured leg before finally letting it limp slowly away into the safety of the nearby forest. He did not expect anything in return for his simple kindness and soon largely forgot about the small act as his busy, demanding farming days continued on much as before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful Japanese countryside, a kind and gentle farmer named Kenji happened to find a small fox caught tightly and quite painfully in a cruel hunter's trap at the very edge of his field early one quiet morning as he walked out to check on his crops. Feeling immediate and genuine sympathy for the frightened, whimpering little creature struggling helplessly before him, Kenji carefully and patiently worked the stubborn trap open to free the fox completely, then gently cleaned and carefully bandaged its injured leg with a strip of cloth before finally letting it limp slowly and gratefully away into the welcoming safety of the nearby forest. He did not expect anything at all in return for such a simple, unremarkable act of kindness, and quite soon largely forgot about the whole small incident entirely as his busy, demanding daily farming work continued on much as it always had before.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: The Grateful Fox</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the fences</li><li>the crops</li><li>the fox</li><li>Kenji</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Strange helpful things start happening on Kenji's farm</li><li>His fences are mended and his tools tidied overnight</li><li>Kenji hides to watch and discovers it is the healed fox</li><li>The fox patrols the field, chasing away pests</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>found his wooden fences quietly mended overnight</li><li>his scattered farming tools tidied neatly away in their proper places</li><li>hide patiently nearby and watch closely in order to solve the growing mystery</li><li>the very same fox from all those months before, now fully healed</li><li>chasing away hungry crows and various other troublesome pests</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Kenji discovered the healed ___ was secretly guarding his ___ from pests.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many months later, strange things began happening around Kenji's farm. Every morning, he found his fences quietly mended and his tools tidied away, as though some helpful spirit had visited overnight. One evening, hiding quietly to watch, Kenji spotted the very same fox, now fully healed, carefully patrolling the edges of his field, chasing away hungry crows and other pests that might have damaged his crops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many months later, strange and rather puzzling things gradually began happening around Kenji's farm. Every single morning, without fail, he found his fences quietly mended overnight and his scattered tools tidied neatly away, almost as though some helpful, unseen spirit had visited the farm secretly while everyone slept. One quiet evening, deciding finally to hide and watch closely to solve the mystery once and for all, Kenji spotted the very same fox from months before, now fully healed and moving with ease, carefully patrolling the edges of his field, busily chasing away hungry crows and other troublesome pests that might otherwise have damaged his precious crops.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long months later, strange and rather puzzling little things gradually began happening around Kenji's quiet farm, one after another. Every single morning, without a single exception, he found his wooden fences quietly mended overnight and his scattered farming tools tidied neatly away in their proper places, almost as though some helpful, entirely unseen spirit had visited the farm secretly while the whole household slept soundly. One particularly quiet evening, deciding finally to hide patiently nearby and watch closely in order to solve the growing mystery once and for all, Kenji spotted, to his genuine surprise, the very same fox from all those months before, now fully healed and moving about with complete ease, carefully and diligently patrolling the edges of his field, busily chasing away hungry crows and various other troublesome pests that might otherwise have seriously damaged his precious, hard-won crops.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: The Grateful Fox</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the storm</li><li>the riverbank</li><li>Kenji's family</li><li>the fox</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The fox urgently appears at Kenji's door during a storm</li><li>It leads him outside toward the riverbank</li><li>Kenji discovers the riverbank is about to collapse</li><li>He moves his family to safety and rewards the fox with food</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>barking loudly, insistently, and quite unlike its usual quiet self</li><li>a nearby riverbank, weakened by days of heavy rain, was dangerously close to collapsing</li><li>managed to wake his whole family and quickly move every one of them</li><li>before the riverbank finally gave way completely with a tremendous, roaring crash</li><li>leave a small, generous offering of food out near the field's edge</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the fox warned Kenji that the ___ was collapsing, and his family escaped to ___ just in time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One stormy night, the fox suddenly appeared at Kenji's door, barking urgently and tugging at his sleeve. Confused but trusting the animal, Kenji followed it outside, only to discover that a nearby riverbank was about to collapse and flood his fields entirely. Thanks to the fox's timely warning, Kenji managed to move his family and animals to safety just in time. From that day forward, Kenji always left a small offering of food for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two shared a quiet, lasting bond of gratitude.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly stormy night, the fox suddenly appeared at Kenji's front door, barking loudly and urgently and tugging insistently at his sleeve with its teeth. Confused but instinctively trusting the clever animal completely, Kenji quickly followed it out into the driving rain, only to discover with horror that a nearby riverbank was dangerously close to collapsing entirely and flooding his family's fields. Thanks entirely to the fox's timely and urgent warning, Kenji managed to move his whole family and all their farm animals to safety just barely in time before the riverbank gave way completely. From that unforgettable day forward, Kenji always made sure to leave a small offering of food out for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two shared a quiet, lasting bond of gratitude and trust for many years afterward.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One particularly stormy and windswept night, the fox suddenly and quite urgently appeared right at Kenji's front door, barking loudly, insistently, and quite unlike its usual quiet self, tugging firmly and repeatedly at his sleeve with its teeth as though trying desperately to communicate something important. Confused at first but instinctively trusting the clever, familiar animal completely, Kenji quickly grabbed his coat and followed it straight out into the driving, howling rain, only to discover with sudden horror that a nearby riverbank, weakened by days of heavy rain, was dangerously close to collapsing entirely and flooding his family's precious fields and even their modest home. Thanks entirely to the fox's timely, urgent, and quite possibly life-saving warning, Kenji managed to wake his whole family and quickly move every one of them, along with all their farm animals, safely uphill just barely in time before the riverbank finally gave way completely with a tremendous, roaring crash. From that unforgettable and deeply grateful day forward, Kenji made absolutely certain to always leave a small, generous offering of food out near the field's edge for his loyal, grateful friend, and the two of them shared a quiet, lasting, and deeply mutual bond of gratitude, trust, and genuine friendship for many long years that followed.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 20: The Grateful Fox</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 4: Skilled Ronin (green belt) — focus: Summarising in your own words (not copying)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 20 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 20 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 21 (Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Kenji freed a ___ from a hunter's ___ and bandaged its leg.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Kenji discovered the healed ___ was secretly guarding his ___ from pests.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the fox warned Kenji that the ___ was collapsing, and his family escaped to ___ just in time.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (Blue belt) — Stories 21–25":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 21’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Hachikazuki and Saisho as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachikazuki</li><li>Saisho</li><li>the bowl</li><li>the village</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachikazuki's dying mother places a wooden bowl on her head</li><li>The bowl will not come off, and villagers mock her for years</li><li>Kind Saisho notices her true, hardworking, gentle character</li><li>The bowl falls away, revealing hidden treasure, and they marry</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachikazuki's mother placed a wooden bowl on her head before she died</li><li>The villagers were always kind to Hachikazuki because of the bowl</li><li>Saisho noticed how hardworking and gentle Hachikazuki truly was</li><li>Hidden treasure was found inside the bowl when it finally fell away</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind girl named Hachikazuki lived happily with her mother, who loved her very much. When her mother grew very ill, she called Hachikazuki close and gently placed a large wooden bowl over her head, saying it would protect her always. Then her mother passed away, and try as she might, Hachikazuki could never lift the bowl off again. It seemed stuck fast, no matter how hard anyone pulled.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>People in the village laughed and pointed whenever they saw her, calling her strange names because of the bowl hiding her face. Hachikazuki felt sad sometimes, but she never let their unkind words change her. She worked hard, stayed gentle and patient, and helped wherever she could, even when others mocked her for how she looked.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a kind nobleman's son named Saisho saw past the bowl entirely. He noticed how hardworking, gentle, and honest Hachikazuki truly was, and he cared for her just as she was. When at last the bowl came loose and fell away, it revealed not only Hachikazuki's own face but also hidden treasure her mother had tucked inside, a final gift of love. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married, and everyone finally saw the kindness that had been there all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet corner of Japan, there lived a kind and gentle girl named Hachikazuki, who was loved dearly by her mother. When her mother grew very ill and knew she would soon pass away, she called Hachikazuki to her side one last time and carefully placed a large wooden bowl over her daughter's head, telling her softly that it would always protect and watch over her. Soon afterward, her mother passed away, and no matter how hard Hachikazuki or anyone else tried, the bowl simply would not come off again. It seemed to have become fixed in place, hiding her face from the world completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From that day on, people in the village would often laugh and whisper whenever Hachikazuki passed by, giving her unkind names because of the strange bowl covering her head and shoulders. Some children even threw small stones or refused to play with her at all. Although this made Hachikazuki feel lonely and sad at times, she never allowed their cruel teasing to change who she truly was inside. She kept working hard at every task given to her, remained patient and gentle with everyone she met, and quietly helped in any way she could, even as the mockery around her continued.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Hachikazuki came to work in the household of a kind nobleman, and it was there that his thoughtful son, a young man named Saisho, first began to notice her. Rather than seeing only the strange bowl that everyone else stared at, Saisho paid attention to how hardworking, honest, and gentle Hachikazuki truly was in everything she did, and slowly he came to care for her deeply, exactly as she was. In time, and after facing some doubt from others around them, the bowl finally came loose and fell away on its own, revealing not only Hachikazuki's lovely face at last but also a hidden store of treasure her mother had secretly placed inside it long ago, one final gift of love from beyond. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married soon after, and everyone in the village finally saw the true kindness that had been there all along, hidden beneath the bowl.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful corner of old Japan, there lived a kind and gentle girl named Hachikazuki, who was loved more dearly by her mother than anything else in the world. When her mother grew gravely ill and understood that she would soon pass away, she called her beloved daughter close to her side one final time, and with trembling but loving hands, she carefully placed a large, plain wooden bowl over Hachikazuki's head, telling her softly through her tears that this strange gift would always protect and watch over her, whatever might come. Not long afterward, her mother quietly passed away, and no matter how desperately Hachikazuki or the household servants tried to lift it free, the wooden bowl simply would not come off again, seeming almost to have grown fast to her head and shoulders, hiding her face completely from the world outside.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From that sorrowful day onward, people throughout the village would often laugh cruelly or whisper behind their hands whenever poor Hachikazuki passed by, calling her unkind names on account of the strange bowl that covered her head. Some of the crueller children would even throw small stones in her direction or refuse outright to play alongside her, treating her as though she were something to be feared or mocked rather than a girl who had simply lost her mother. Although this constant unkindness left Hachikazuki feeling lonely and sad on many quiet nights, she never once allowed their cruel teasing to change the good and gentle person she truly was underneath it all. She continued working hard at every single task given to her, remained patient and kind toward everyone she met regardless of how they treated her in return, and quietly lent a helping hand wherever one was needed, even as the mockery around her carried on for years.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, in time, Hachikazuki came to work within the household of a kind and respected nobleman, and it was there, among the daily chores and quiet routines, that his thoughtful son, a fine young man named Saisho, first truly began to notice her. Rather than staring only at the strange bowl that everyone else in the household whispered about, Saisho paid close and careful attention to how hardworking, honest, and gentle Hachikazuki proved herself to be in absolutely everything she did, and slowly, over many months, he came to care for her deeply and sincerely, loving her exactly as she was, bowl and all. In time, despite a good deal of doubt and disapproval from others in his family who could not see past appearances, the ancient wooden bowl at last came loose on its own and fell gently away, revealing not only Hachikazuki's lovely, long-hidden face for everyone finally to see, but also a hidden store of treasure her mother had secretly tucked inside it many years before, one final, quiet gift of love left for her beyond the grave. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married joyfully soon afterward, and at long last, everyone throughout the village came to see the true and steady kindness that had been living inside her all along, patiently waiting beneath the bowl for someone willing to look.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachikazuki</li><li>her mother</li><li>the bowl</li><li>the village</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachikazuki's mother grows very ill</li><li>Her mother places a wooden bowl over her head</li><li>Her mother says it will always protect her</li><li>The bowl will not come off after her mother dies</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>loved dearly by her mother</li><li>knew she would soon pass away</li><li>always protect and watch over her</li><li>the bowl simply would not come off again</li><li>seemed to have become fixed in place</li><li>hiding her face from the world completely</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Hachikazuki's dying mother placed a wooden ___ over her head, and it would not ___ off again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a kind girl named Hachikazuki lived happily with her mother, who loved her very much. When her mother grew very ill, she called Hachikazuki close and gently placed a large wooden bowl over her head, saying it would protect her always. Then her mother passed away, and try as she might, Hachikazuki could never lift the bowl off again. It seemed stuck fast, no matter how hard anyone pulled.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet corner of Japan, there lived a kind and gentle girl named Hachikazuki, who was loved dearly by her mother. When her mother grew very ill and knew she would soon pass away, she called Hachikazuki to her side one last time and carefully placed a large wooden bowl over her daughter's head, telling her softly that it would always protect and watch over her. Soon afterward, her mother passed away, and no matter how hard Hachikazuki or anyone else tried, the bowl simply would not come off again. It seemed to have become fixed in place, hiding her face from the world completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful corner of old Japan, there lived a kind and gentle girl named Hachikazuki, who was loved more dearly by her mother than anything else in the world. When her mother grew gravely ill and understood that she would soon pass away, she called her beloved daughter close to her side one final time, and with trembling but loving hands, she carefully placed a large, plain wooden bowl over Hachikazuki's head, telling her softly through her tears that this strange gift would always protect and watch over her, whatever might come. Not long afterward, her mother quietly passed away, and no matter how desperately Hachikazuki or the household servants tried to lift it free, the wooden bowl simply would not come off again, seeming almost to have grown fast to her head and shoulders, hiding her face completely from the world outside.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the village</li><li>Hachikazuki</li><li>the bowl</li><li>the children</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Villagers laugh and give her unkind names</li><li>Some children throw stones or refuse to play with her</li><li>Hachikazuki never lets their teasing change her</li><li>She keeps working hard and stays gentle and patient</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>would often laugh and whisper whenever Hachikazuki passed by</li><li>unkind names because of the strange bowl</li><li>made Hachikazuki feel lonely and sad at times</li><li>never allowed their cruel teasing to change who she truly was</li><li>quietly helped in any way she could</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the villagers gave Hachikazuki unkind ___, but she stayed ___ and kept working hard.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>People in the village laughed and pointed whenever they saw her, calling her strange names because of the bowl hiding her face. Hachikazuki felt sad sometimes, but she never let their unkind words change her. She worked hard, stayed gentle and patient, and helped wherever she could, even when others mocked her for how she looked.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From that day on, people in the village would often laugh and whisper whenever Hachikazuki passed by, giving her unkind names because of the strange bowl covering her head and shoulders. Some children even threw small stones or refused to play with her at all. Although this made Hachikazuki feel lonely and sad at times, she never allowed their cruel teasing to change who she truly was inside. She kept working hard at every task given to her, remained patient and gentle with everyone she met, and quietly helped in any way she could, even as the mockery around her continued.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>From that sorrowful day onward, people throughout the village would often laugh cruelly or whisper behind their hands whenever poor Hachikazuki passed by, calling her unkind names on account of the strange bowl that covered her head. Some of the crueller children would even throw small stones in her direction or refuse outright to play alongside her, treating her as though she were something to be feared or mocked rather than a girl who had simply lost her mother. Although this constant unkindness left Hachikazuki feeling lonely and sad on many quiet nights, she never once allowed their cruel teasing to change the good and gentle person she truly was underneath it all. She continued working hard at every single task given to her, remained patient and kind toward everyone she met regardless of how they treated her in return, and quietly lent a helping hand wherever one was needed, even as the mockery around her carried on for years.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Saisho</li><li>Hachikazuki</li><li>the bowl</li><li>treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Saisho notices Hachikazuki's true character, not the bowl</li><li>He comes to care for her deeply</li><li>The bowl finally comes loose and falls away</li><li>Hidden treasure is found, and they marry happily</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>paid attention to how hardworking, honest, and gentle Hachikazuki truly was</li><li>came to care for her deeply, exactly as she was</li><li>the bowl finally came loose and fell away on its own</li><li>a hidden store of treasure her mother had secretly placed inside</li><li>one final gift of love from beyond</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, kind ___ saw Hachikazuki's true worth, and when the bowl fell away it revealed hidden ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, a kind nobleman's son named Saisho saw past the bowl entirely. He noticed how hardworking, gentle, and honest Hachikazuki truly was, and he cared for her just as she was. When at last the bowl came loose and fell away, it revealed not only Hachikazuki's own face but also hidden treasure her mother had tucked inside, a final gift of love. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married, and everyone finally saw the kindness that had been there all along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, Hachikazuki came to work in the household of a kind nobleman, and it was there that his thoughtful son, a young man named Saisho, first began to notice her. Rather than seeing only the strange bowl that everyone else stared at, Saisho paid attention to how hardworking, honest, and gentle Hachikazuki truly was in everything she did, and slowly he came to care for her deeply, exactly as she was. In time, and after facing some doubt from others around them, the bowl finally came loose and fell away on its own, revealing not only Hachikazuki's lovely face at last but also a hidden store of treasure her mother had secretly placed inside it long ago, one final gift of love from beyond. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married soon after, and everyone in the village finally saw the true kindness that had been there all along, hidden beneath the bowl.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Eventually, in time, Hachikazuki came to work within the household of a kind and respected nobleman, and it was there, among the daily chores and quiet routines, that his thoughtful son, a fine young man named Saisho, first truly began to notice her. Rather than staring only at the strange bowl that everyone else in the household whispered about, Saisho paid close and careful attention to how hardworking, honest, and gentle Hachikazuki proved herself to be in absolutely everything she did, and slowly, over many months, he came to care for her deeply and sincerely, loving her exactly as she was, bowl and all. In time, despite a good deal of doubt and disapproval from others in his family who could not see past appearances, the ancient wooden bowl at last came loose on its own and fell gently away, revealing not only Hachikazuki's lovely, long-hidden face for everyone finally to see, but also a hidden store of treasure her mother had secretly tucked inside it many years before, one final, quiet gift of love left for her beyond the grave. Saisho and Hachikazuki were married joyfully soon afterward, and at long last, everyone throughout the village came to see the true and steady kindness that had been living inside her all along, patiently waiting beneath the bowl for someone willing to look.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 21: Hachikazuki (The Princess with the Bowl on Her Head)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 21 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 21 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 22 (Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Hachikazuki's dying mother placed a wooden ___ over her head, and it would not ___ off again.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the villagers gave Hachikazuki unkind ___, but she stayed ___ and kept working hard.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, kind ___ saw Hachikazuki's true worth, and when the bowl fell away it revealed hidden ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 22’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Kaguya-hime and the bamboo cutter as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaguya-hime</li><li>the bamboo cutter</li><li>the suitors</li><li>the moon</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bamboo cutter finds tiny, glowing Kaguya-hime inside a bamboo stalk</li><li>She grows quickly into a beautiful young woman</li><li>Many suitors try and fail her impossible tasks</li><li>She reveals her secret and returns to her faraway home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bamboo cutter found Kaguya-hime glowing inside a bamboo stalk</li><li>Kaguya-hime grew up at the normal, ordinary speed of any child</li><li>Every noble suitor who tried her tasks eventually failed</li><li>Kaguya-hime said a tender farewell before returning to her faraway home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an old bamboo cutter found a tiny, glowing girl inside a shining stalk of bamboo. He and his wife had no children of their own, so they gladly brought the tiny girl home and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her with great love and tenderness. She grew unusually quickly, and within just a few months she had become a beautiful, radiant young woman, admired by everyone who saw her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Word of Kaguya-hime's beauty spread far and wide, and many noble suitors came asking to marry her. She set each of them a nearly impossible task to complete before she would agree, and one by one, every suitor failed. Kaguya-hime remained gentle with the old bamboo cutter and his wife throughout, but she often seemed distant, gazing quietly up at the moon on clear nights, as though remembering somewhere far away.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One autumn evening, Kaguya-hime revealed a gentle secret: she had come from a mysterious, faraway place beyond the sky, and now the time had come for her to return there. Despite everyone's sadness, she said a tender farewell to her beloved parents, thanking them warmly for their years of loving care. Then, wrapped in a soft glow of light, she rose gently into the night sky and was gone, leaving behind only loving memories and a quiet, shining moonlit sky.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, an old bamboo cutter was working quietly among the tall stalks one day when he noticed one particular stalk glowing with a strange, soft light. Curious, he cut it open and found inside a tiny girl, no bigger than his thumb, shining gently in the dim forest light. He and his wife had long wished for a child of their own, so they gladly carried the tiny girl home and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her from that day forward with great tenderness and love. To their astonishment, she grew far more quickly than any ordinary child, and within only a few short months she had blossomed into a strikingly beautiful young woman, admired by absolutely everyone who happened to see her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of Kaguya-hime's remarkable beauty had spread across the whole province, and soon many noble suitors began arriving at the bamboo cutter's humble home, each one hoping to win her hand in marriage. Rather than simply choosing one, Kaguya-hime set each hopeful suitor a nearly impossible task to complete before she would agree to marry him, and one after another, every single suitor eventually failed, no matter how hard they tried or how clever their attempts. Throughout all of this, Kaguya-hime remained warm and gentle toward the elderly bamboo cutter and his wife who had raised her so lovingly, yet those who knew her well noticed she often seemed distant and thoughtful, gazing quietly up at the moon on clear autumn nights as though remembering some faraway place she could not quite name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One quiet autumn evening, with a heavy heart, Kaguya-hime finally revealed a gentle and long-kept secret to her parents: she had originally come from a mysterious, faraway realm beyond the sky itself, and now, much as it grieved her, the time had come at last for her to return there for good. Despite the deep sadness that filled the little household at this news, Kaguya-hime spoke a tender and heartfelt farewell to the old couple who had raised her so devotedly, thanking them warmly again and again for all their years of patient, loving care. Then, gently wrapped in a soft and shimmering glow of light, she rose slowly and peacefully into the night sky above the bamboo grove and was gone, leaving behind only cherished memories and a quiet, silvery moon shining softly over the fields where she had once grown up.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the quiet Japanese countryside, there lived an old bamboo cutter who spent his days working carefully among the tall, swaying stalks of a great bamboo grove near his home. One ordinary morning, as he moved between the stalks with his tools, he suddenly noticed one particular length of bamboo glowing softly with a strange and gentle light unlike anything he had ever seen before. Filled with curiosity, he carefully cut the glowing stalk open and discovered, nestled inside, a tiny girl no larger than his own thumb, shining faintly in the dim green light of the grove. He and his wife had long wished for a child of their own but had never been blessed with one, so overjoyed at this small miracle, they gladly carried the tiny girl home with them and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her from that very day forward with the greatest tenderness, patience, and love a couple could offer. To their continuing astonishment, the little girl grew far more swiftly than any ordinary child ever could, and within only a handful of short months she had blossomed fully into a strikingly beautiful young woman, admired and spoken of warmly by absolutely everyone fortunate enough to see her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, word of Kaguya-hime's extraordinary beauty had spread widely across the whole province and even beyond it, and soon a great many noble suitors began arriving one after another at the bamboo cutter's humble little home, each hopeful young man dreaming of winning her hand in marriage. Rather than simply accepting any one of them outright, Kaguya-hime thoughtfully set each hopeful suitor a task that seemed nearly impossible to complete, asking for rare treasures and wonders from far-off, half-imagined lands, and one after another, every single suitor eventually failed in his quest, no matter how determined his efforts or how clever his attempted tricks and shortcuts turned out to be. All the while, Kaguya-hime remained consistently warm, patient, and gentle toward the elderly bamboo cutter and his devoted wife who had raised her with such care, yet those closest to her could not help but notice that she often seemed strangely distant and thoughtful at unexpected moments, gazing quietly upward at the moon on clear, cool autumn nights as though trying to remember some faraway place whose name she could no longer quite recall.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one hushed and quiet autumn evening, with a heavy and reluctant heart, Kaguya-hime finally chose to reveal a gentle, long-kept secret she had carried silently for years: she explained softly that she had originally come from a mysterious and impossibly faraway realm beyond the sky itself, and now, much as it grieved her deeply to say so, the time had at last come for her to return there for good, called back by forces she herself could not fully resist or explain. Despite the profound sadness that immediately filled the small household upon hearing this news, Kaguya-hime spoke a warm, tender, and heartfelt farewell to the elderly couple who had devotedly raised her as their very own, thanking them again and again, with genuine tears in her eyes, for every one of their many years of patient, unconditional, loving care. Then, gently wrapped in a soft, shimmering glow of silvery light that seemed to rise up from all around her, she lifted slowly and peacefully into the night sky above the quiet bamboo grove and was gone from sight entirely, leaving behind nothing but cherished memories, a few fallen bamboo leaves, and a calm, silvery moon shining softly and steadily over the very same fields where she had once grown up so happily among people who had loved her completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the bamboo cutter</li><li>the bamboo stalk</li><li>Kaguya-hime</li><li>his wife</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bamboo cutter sees a glowing bamboo stalk</li><li>He finds a tiny girl shining inside it</li><li>He and his wife raise her with love</li><li>She grows quickly into a beautiful young woman</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>glowing softly with a strange and gentle light</li><li>a tiny girl no larger than his own thumb</li><li>raising her from that very day forward with the greatest tenderness</li><li>grew far more swiftly than any ordinary child ever could</li><li>admired and spoken of warmly by absolutely everyone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the bamboo cutter found tiny, glowing ___ inside a ___ stalk.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, an old bamboo cutter found a tiny, glowing girl inside a shining stalk of bamboo. He and his wife had no children of their own, so they gladly brought the tiny girl home and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her with great love and tenderness. She grew unusually quickly, and within just a few months she had become a beautiful, radiant young woman, admired by everyone who saw her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, an old bamboo cutter was working quietly among the tall stalks one day when he noticed one particular stalk glowing with a strange, soft light. Curious, he cut it open and found inside a tiny girl, no bigger than his thumb, shining gently in the dim forest light. He and his wife had long wished for a child of their own, so they gladly carried the tiny girl home and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her from that day forward with great tenderness and love. To their astonishment, she grew far more quickly than any ordinary child, and within only a few short months she had blossomed into a strikingly beautiful young woman, admired by absolutely everyone who happened to see her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the quiet Japanese countryside, there lived an old bamboo cutter who spent his days working carefully among the tall, swaying stalks of a great bamboo grove near his home. One ordinary morning, as he moved between the stalks with his tools, he suddenly noticed one particular length of bamboo glowing softly with a strange and gentle light unlike anything he had ever seen before. Filled with curiosity, he carefully cut the glowing stalk open and discovered, nestled inside, a tiny girl no larger than his own thumb, shining faintly in the dim green light of the grove. He and his wife had long wished for a child of their own but had never been blessed with one, so overjoyed at this small miracle, they gladly carried the tiny girl home with them and named her Kaguya-hime, raising her from that very day forward with the greatest tenderness, patience, and love a couple could offer. To their continuing astonishment, the little girl grew far more swiftly than any ordinary child ever could, and within only a handful of short months she had blossomed fully into a strikingly beautiful young woman, admired and spoken of warmly by absolutely everyone fortunate enough to see her.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaguya-hime</li><li>the suitors</li><li>the tasks</li><li>the moon</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Many noble suitors come to ask for her hand</li><li>Kaguya-hime sets each one an impossible task</li><li>Every suitor eventually fails his task</li><li>She often gazes quietly up at the moon</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>word of Kaguya-hime's extraordinary beauty had spread widely</li><li>set each hopeful suitor a task that seemed nearly impossible</li><li>no matter how determined his efforts</li><li>gazing quietly upward at the moon on clear, cool autumn nights</li><li>admired by everyone who saw her</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, many ___ tried Kaguya-hime's impossible ___, and every one of them failed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Word of Kaguya-hime's beauty spread far and wide, and many noble suitors came asking to marry her. She set each of them a nearly impossible task to complete before she would agree, and one by one, every suitor failed. Kaguya-hime remained gentle with the old bamboo cutter and his wife throughout, but she often seemed distant, gazing quietly up at the moon on clear nights, as though remembering somewhere far away.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of Kaguya-hime's remarkable beauty had spread across the whole province, and soon many noble suitors began arriving at the bamboo cutter's humble home, each one hoping to win her hand in marriage. Rather than simply choosing one, Kaguya-hime set each hopeful suitor a nearly impossible task to complete before she would agree to marry him, and one after another, every single suitor eventually failed, no matter how hard they tried or how clever their attempts. Throughout all of this, Kaguya-hime remained warm and gentle toward the elderly bamboo cutter and his wife who had raised her so lovingly, yet those who knew her well noticed she often seemed distant and thoughtful, gazing quietly up at the moon on clear autumn nights as though remembering some faraway place she could not quite name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, word of Kaguya-hime's extraordinary beauty had spread widely across the whole province and even beyond it, and soon a great many noble suitors began arriving one after another at the bamboo cutter's humble little home, each hopeful young man dreaming of winning her hand in marriage. Rather than simply accepting any one of them outright, Kaguya-hime thoughtfully set each hopeful suitor a task that seemed nearly impossible to complete, asking for rare treasures and wonders from far-off, half-imagined lands, and one after another, every single suitor eventually failed in his quest, no matter how determined his efforts or how clever his attempted tricks and shortcuts turned out to be. All the while, Kaguya-hime remained consistently warm, patient, and gentle toward the elderly bamboo cutter and his devoted wife who had raised her with such care, yet those closest to her could not help but notice that she often seemed strangely distant and thoughtful at unexpected moments, gazing quietly upward at the moon on clear, cool autumn nights as though trying to remember some faraway place whose name she could no longer quite recall.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaguya-hime</li><li>her secret</li><li>the bamboo cutter</li><li>the sky</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kaguya-hime reveals she came from a faraway place</li><li>She says the time has come to return there</li><li>She says a tender farewell to her parents</li><li>She rises gently into the night sky and is gone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>she had originally come from a mysterious and impossibly faraway realm</li><li>the time had at last come for her to return there for good</li><li>thanking them again and again, with genuine tears in her eyes</li><li>lifted slowly and peacefully into the night sky</li><li>wrapped in a soft, shimmering glow of light</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Kaguya-hime said a tender ___ and rose gently into the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One autumn evening, Kaguya-hime revealed a gentle secret: she had come from a mysterious, faraway place beyond the sky, and now the time had come for her to return there. Despite everyone's sadness, she said a tender farewell to her beloved parents, thanking them warmly for their years of loving care. Then, wrapped in a soft glow of light, she rose gently into the night sky and was gone, leaving behind only loving memories and a quiet, shining moonlit sky.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One quiet autumn evening, with a heavy heart, Kaguya-hime finally revealed a gentle and long-kept secret to her parents: she had originally come from a mysterious, faraway realm beyond the sky itself, and now, much as it grieved her, the time had come at last for her to return there for good. Despite the deep sadness that filled the little household at this news, Kaguya-hime spoke a tender and heartfelt farewell to the old couple who had raised her so devotedly, thanking them warmly again and again for all their years of patient, loving care. Then, gently wrapped in a soft and shimmering glow of light, she rose slowly and peacefully into the night sky above the bamboo grove and was gone, leaving behind only cherished memories and a quiet, silvery moon shining softly over the fields where she had once grown up.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one hushed and quiet autumn evening, with a heavy and reluctant heart, Kaguya-hime finally chose to reveal a gentle, long-kept secret she had carried silently for years: she explained softly that she had originally come from a mysterious and impossibly faraway realm beyond the sky itself, and now, much as it grieved her deeply to say so, the time had at last come for her to return there for good, called back by forces she herself could not fully resist or explain. Despite the profound sadness that immediately filled the small household upon hearing this news, Kaguya-hime spoke a warm, tender, and heartfelt farewell to the elderly couple who had devotedly raised her as their very own, thanking them again and again, with genuine tears in her eyes, for every one of their many years of patient, unconditional, loving care. Then, gently wrapped in a soft, shimmering glow of silvery light that seemed to rise up from all around her, she lifted slowly and peacefully into the night sky above the quiet bamboo grove and was gone from sight entirely, leaving behind nothing but cherished memories, a few fallen bamboo leaves, and a calm, silvery moon shining softly and steadily over the very same fields where she had once grown up so happily among people who had loved her completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 22: Kaguya-hime (The Bamboo Princess)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 22 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 22 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 23 (The Mirror of Matsuyama) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the bamboo cutter found tiny, glowing ___ inside a ___ stalk.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, many ___ tried Kaguya-hime's impossible ___, and every one of them failed.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Kaguya-hime said a tender ___ and rose gently into the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 23’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the daughter and the mother as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the daughter</li><li>the mother</li><li>the mirror</li><li>Matsuyama</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The dying mother gives her daughter a small mirror</li><li>She promises the girl will always see her mother's face in it</li><li>The girl grows up, working hard and being kind like her mother</li><li>As a woman, she realises the reflection is her own, grown like her mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The mother gave her daughter a small round mirror before she died</li><li>The mother said the mirror would always show her daughter's face</li><li>The daughter grew up treating people with warmth and kindness</li><li>The grown woman realised how like her mother she had become</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a village called Matsuyama, a kind mother lay dying and called her young daughter close. She gave the girl a small round mirror, saying that whenever she looked into it, she would always be able to see her mother's face gazing back at her. The girl treasured the mirror deeply and kept it safe, holding onto her mother's gentle promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years passed, and the girl grew from a child into a young woman, working hard and treating everyone with the same warmth and kindness her mother had shown her. Whenever she felt sad or lonely, she would take out the small mirror and look inside it, remembering her mother's loving words and feeling comforted all over again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, looking closely into the mirror as a grown woman, she suddenly realised the face gazing back was not really her mother's at all, but her own, and how remarkably like her kind mother she had become. She smiled through happy tears, understanding at last that her mother's gentle spirit had lived on inside her all along, growing kindness into kindness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a peaceful village called Matsuyama, there lived a kind and loving mother who fell very seriously ill. Sensing that her time was drawing near, she called her young daughter close to her bedside one final time and gently placed a small, round mirror into the girl's hands. She told her softly that whenever she looked into this mirror in the years ahead, she would always be able to see her mother's face gazing lovingly back at her, no matter how far apart death might keep them. The young girl treasured this small mirror deeply from that day forward, wrapping it carefully and keeping it always close, holding tightly onto her mother's gentle, comforting promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years slipped by, and the little girl gradually grew from a child into a capable, thoughtful young woman, working hard at everything asked of her and treating every single person she met with the same warmth, patience, and kindness that her own mother had always shown to others. Whenever she felt lonely, tired, or simply missed her mother's presence, she would quietly take out the small mirror from its wrapping and gaze into it, remembering her mother's soft, loving words from all those years before and feeling comforted and steadied all over again, as though her mother were somehow still watching over her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One quiet afternoon, now fully grown into womanhood herself, she happened to look more closely into the mirror than she usually did, and with a sudden rush of feeling, she realised that the gentle face gazing back at her was not actually her mother's face at all, but her very own, and yet how remarkably, wonderfully like her kind and gentle mother she had become over the years. She smiled softly through happy, grateful tears, finally understanding that her beloved mother's warm spirit had been living on inside her the whole time, quietly growing kindness into further kindness with every passing year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful little village known as Matsuyama, there lived a kind and gentle mother who, much to her family's sorrow, fell very seriously ill as the years went by. Sensing quietly that her time in this world was drawing near, she called her young daughter close to her bedside one final, tender time, and with careful, trembling hands, she gently placed a small, round mirror into the girl's open palms. Looking steadily into her daughter's eyes, she told her softly that whenever she looked into this very mirror in all the years still ahead of her, she would always be able to see her mother's own face gazing lovingly back at her from within the glass, no matter how far apart death itself might one day keep them from each other. From that sorrowful day forward, the young girl treasured this small mirror more deeply than any other possession she owned, wrapping it with great care in a soft cloth and keeping it always close beside her, holding tightly onto her mother's gentle, quietly comforting promise through every season that followed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years slipped steadily by, and the little girl gradually grew, season after season, from a small child into a capable, thoughtful, and genuinely kind young woman, working hard at absolutely everything that was asked of her and treating every single person she happened to meet along the way with exactly the same warmth, patience, and gentle kindness that her own beloved mother had always shown so freely to others throughout her life. Whenever she felt lonely, weary from a long day's work, or simply found herself missing her mother's warm and familiar presence more than usual, she would quietly unwrap the small mirror from its soft cloth and gaze steadily into it, remembering her mother's soft, loving words from all those distant years before and feeling comforted, steadied, and less alone all over again, almost as though her gentle mother were somehow still watching over her from somewhere close by.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One especially quiet afternoon, now fully grown into a capable and confident woman herself, with a household and daily duties of her very own, she happened by chance to look more closely and carefully into the old mirror than she usually did in her hurried daily glances, and with a sudden, warm rush of feeling washing over her, she slowly realised that the gentle, familiar face gazing calmly back at her from the glass was not actually her mother's face at all, but entirely her own, and yet how remarkably, wonderfully like her kind and patient mother she had quietly become over all those passing years without even fully noticing it happen. She smiled softly and warmly through happy, grateful tears that welled up unexpectedly in her eyes, finally understanding, in that single quiet moment, that her beloved mother's warm and generous spirit had been living on steadily inside her the whole time, quietly and faithfully growing kindness into further kindness with every single passing year of her life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the mother</li><li>the daughter</li><li>the mirror</li><li>Matsuyama</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The mother becomes seriously ill</li><li>She calls her young daughter close</li><li>She gives her daughter a small round mirror</li><li>She promises she will always see her mother's face in it</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>sensing that her time was drawing near</li><li>gently placed a small, round mirror into the girl's hands</li><li>she would always be able to see her mother's face gazing lovingly back</li><li>treasured this small mirror deeply from that day forward</li><li>holding tightly onto her mother's gentle promise</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the dying mother gave her daughter a small round ___, promising she would always see her mother's ___ in it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a village called Matsuyama, a kind mother lay dying and called her young daughter close. She gave the girl a small round mirror, saying that whenever she looked into it, she would always be able to see her mother's face gazing back at her. The girl treasured the mirror deeply and kept it safe, holding onto her mother's gentle promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a peaceful village called Matsuyama, there lived a kind and loving mother who fell very seriously ill. Sensing that her time was drawing near, she called her young daughter close to her bedside one final time and gently placed a small, round mirror into the girl's hands. She told her softly that whenever she looked into this mirror in the years ahead, she would always be able to see her mother's face gazing lovingly back at her, no matter how far apart death might keep them. The young girl treasured this small mirror deeply from that day forward, wrapping it carefully and keeping it always close, holding tightly onto her mother's gentle, comforting promise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful little village known as Matsuyama, there lived a kind and gentle mother who, much to her family's sorrow, fell very seriously ill as the years went by. Sensing quietly that her time in this world was drawing near, she called her young daughter close to her bedside one final, tender time, and with careful, trembling hands, she gently placed a small, round mirror into the girl's open palms. Looking steadily into her daughter's eyes, she told her softly that whenever she looked into this very mirror in all the years still ahead of her, she would always be able to see her mother's own face gazing lovingly back at her from within the glass, no matter how far apart death itself might one day keep them from each other. From that sorrowful day forward, the young girl treasured this small mirror more deeply than any other possession she owned, wrapping it with great care in a soft cloth and keeping it always close beside her, holding tightly onto her mother's gentle, quietly comforting promise through every season that followed.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the girl</li><li>kindness</li><li>the mirror</li><li>her mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Years pass and the girl grows into a young woman</li><li>She works hard and treats people with kindness</li><li>She looks into the mirror whenever she feels sad</li><li>Remembering her mother's words comforts her</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>gradually grew from a child into a capable, thoughtful young woman</li><li>treating every single person she met with the same warmth</li><li>quietly take out the small mirror from its wrapping</li><li>feeling comforted and steadied all over again</li><li>whenever she felt lonely or simply missed her mother</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the girl grew up treating everyone with ___, and looking into the mirror always brought her ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years passed, and the girl grew from a child into a young woman, working hard and treating everyone with the same warmth and kindness her mother had shown her. Whenever she felt sad or lonely, she would take out the small mirror and look inside it, remembering her mother's loving words and feeling comforted all over again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years slipped by, and the little girl gradually grew from a child into a capable, thoughtful young woman, working hard at everything asked of her and treating every single person she met with the same warmth, patience, and kindness that her own mother had always shown to others. Whenever she felt lonely, tired, or simply missed her mother's presence, she would quietly take out the small mirror from its wrapping and gaze into it, remembering her mother's soft, loving words from all those years before and feeling comforted and steadied all over again, as though her mother were somehow still watching over her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years slipped steadily by, and the little girl gradually grew, season after season, from a small child into a capable, thoughtful, and genuinely kind young woman, working hard at absolutely everything that was asked of her and treating every single person she happened to meet along the way with exactly the same warmth, patience, and gentle kindness that her own beloved mother had always shown so freely to others throughout her life. Whenever she felt lonely, weary from a long day's work, or simply found herself missing her mother's warm and familiar presence more than usual, she would quietly unwrap the small mirror from its soft cloth and gaze steadily into it, remembering her mother's soft, loving words from all those distant years before and feeling comforted, steadied, and less alone all over again, almost as though her gentle mother were somehow still watching over her from somewhere close by.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the woman</li><li>the mirror</li><li>her mother</li><li>kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>As a grown woman, she looks closely into the mirror</li><li>She realises the face is her own, not her mother's</li><li>She sees how like her kind mother she has become</li><li>She smiles through happy tears, understanding at last</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the gentle face gazing back at her was not actually her mother's face</li><li>how remarkably, wonderfully like her kind and gentle mother</li><li>smiled softly through happy, grateful tears</li><li>her beloved mother's warm spirit had been living on inside her</li><li>smiled softly through happy, grateful tears</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the grown woman realised her own ___ was in the mirror, and how like her kind ___ she had become.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, looking closely into the mirror as a grown woman, she suddenly realised the face gazing back was not really her mother's at all, but her own, and how remarkably like her kind mother she had become. She smiled through happy tears, understanding at last that her mother's gentle spirit had lived on inside her all along, growing kindness into kindness.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One quiet afternoon, now fully grown into womanhood herself, she happened to look more closely into the mirror than she usually did, and with a sudden rush of feeling, she realised that the gentle face gazing back at her was not actually her mother's face at all, but her very own, and yet how remarkably, wonderfully like her kind and gentle mother she had become over the years. She smiled softly through happy, grateful tears, finally understanding that her beloved mother's warm spirit had been living on inside her the whole time, quietly growing kindness into further kindness with every passing year.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One especially quiet afternoon, now fully grown into a capable and confident woman herself, with a household and daily duties of her very own, she happened by chance to look more closely and carefully into the old mirror than she usually did in her hurried daily glances, and with a sudden, warm rush of feeling washing over her, she slowly realised that the gentle, familiar face gazing calmly back at her from the glass was not actually her mother's face at all, but entirely her own, and yet how remarkably, wonderfully like her kind and patient mother she had quietly become over all those passing years without even fully noticing it happen. She smiled softly and warmly through happy, grateful tears that welled up unexpectedly in her eyes, finally understanding, in that single quiet moment, that her beloved mother's warm and generous spirit had been living on steadily inside her the whole time, quietly and faithfully growing kindness into further kindness with every single passing year of her life.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 23: The Mirror of Matsuyama</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 23 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 23 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 24 (Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the dying mother gave her daughter a small round ___, promising she would always see her mother's ___ in it.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the girl grew up treating everyone with ___, and looking into the mirror always brought her ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the grown woman realised her own ___ was in the mirror, and how like her kind ___ she had become.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 24’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the old man and the mice as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the mice</li><li>the neighbour</li><li>the rice ball</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man's rice ball rolls into a mouse hole</li><li>He hears the mice singing and follows it down himself</li><li>The grateful mice reward his kindness and laughter with treasure</li><li>A greedy neighbour copies him rudely and gets stuck in the dark</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man's rice ball rolled into a hole in the hillside</li><li>The old man was frightened when he tumbled down the hole</li><li>The mice rewarded the old man's kindness with baskets of treasure</li><li>The greedy neighbour was polite and patient with the mice</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a cheerful old man sat down on a hillside to eat his lunch, when he accidentally dropped one of his rice balls. It rolled quickly downhill and disappeared into a small round hole. The old man peered curiously into the hole and heard tiny, delighted mouse voices singing a happy little song below, thanking him for the tasty treat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Charmed by the sound, the old man dropped another rice ball into the hole just to hear the cheerful mouse song again, and then, laughing at his own curiosity, he leaned in a little too far and tumbled right down the hole himself. Instead of being afraid, he found a cosy home full of grateful mice, who happily thanked him and rewarded his kindness and cheerful laughter with baskets of shining treasure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Word of the old man's good fortune spread through the village, and a greedy neighbour decided to copy him exactly, hoping for treasure too. But when he dropped his rice ball down a similar hole and tumbled in himself, he behaved rudely and impatiently toward the mice, demanding treasure at once. Startled and offended, the mice scurried away and blew out their lantern, leaving the greedy neighbour stuck fumbling about in the dark, comically unable to find his way back out for quite some time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, a cheerful old man sat down comfortably on a sunny hillside to eat his simple lunch of rice balls, when suddenly one slipped from his fingers and dropped to the ground. To his surprise, it rolled quickly away downhill and disappeared entirely into a small, round hole tucked among the grass. Curious rather than upset, the old man knelt down and peered carefully into the dark little hole, and to his delight he heard tiny, cheerful mouse voices singing a happy little song from somewhere down below, thanking him warmly for the unexpected and tasty treat that had just rolled in.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly charmed by the sound of their singing, the old man could not resist dropping a second rice ball into the hole, simply to hear the cheerful little mouse song echo up to him once again, and then, laughing heartily at his own foolish curiosity, he leaned in just a little too far and tumbled right down the hole himself, landing with a soft thump. Rather than feeling frightened, he found himself inside a cosy, warmly lit little home filled with a whole community of grateful mice, who gathered round him happily, thanked him again and again for his kindness, and delighted in his easy, cheerful laughter, eventually rewarding him generously with baskets overflowing with shining treasure before helping him safely back up to the surface.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's remarkable good fortune had spread throughout the whole village, and a greedy, impatient neighbour decided he would simply copy the old man's actions exactly, hoping to win himself an equally grand pile of treasure. Following the very same steps, he dropped his own rice ball down a similar hole he found nearby and then deliberately tumbled in after it himself, but once inside, he behaved rudely and impatiently toward the gathered mice, shoving forward and loudly demanding his treasure at once rather than showing any warmth or gratitude. Startled and rather offended by his rudeness, the mice quickly scurried away in every direction and blew out their little lantern altogether, leaving the greedy, grumbling neighbour stuck fumbling about hopelessly in the pitch darkness, comically unable to find his way back out again for quite some considerable time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful Japanese countryside, there lived a cheerful old man who one fine, sunny day sat himself down comfortably on a grassy hillside to enjoy his simple lunch of freshly made rice balls, wrapped neatly in cloth as he always carried them. Quite suddenly, and entirely by accident, one of the rice balls slipped clean out of his fingers and dropped to the ground beside him, and to his considerable surprise it rolled quickly away down the slope of the hill before disappearing entirely into a small, round hole tucked away among the tall grass. Rather than feeling upset at the loss of his lunch, the old man, being naturally curious by nature, knelt down at once and peered carefully into the dark little hole, and to his genuine delight he heard tiny, cheerful mouse voices singing a happy little song from somewhere far down below, thanking him warmly and enthusiastically for the unexpected and thoroughly tasty treat that had just come rolling unexpectedly into their home.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly charmed and thoroughly amused by the delightful sound of their singing, the old man simply could not resist the temptation to drop a second rice ball down into the very same hole, purely so that he might hear the cheerful little mouse song echo pleasantly up to him once more, and then, laughing heartily and rather helplessly at his own foolish curiosity, he leaned in just that little bit too far and tumbled right down the hole himself, landing with a soft, gentle thump somewhere in the cosy darkness below. Rather than feeling the slightest bit frightened by his tumble, he instead found himself inside a warm, cheerfully lit little home filled from wall to wall with an entire lively community of grateful mice, who gathered eagerly all around him, thanked him again and again with great enthusiasm for his unexpected kindness, and delighted openly in his easy, warm-hearted laughter, before eventually rewarding him most generously with several baskets overflowing with shining treasure and then carefully helping him find his way safely back up to the surface above.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, word of the cheerful old man's remarkable stroke of good fortune had spread rapidly throughout the whole surrounding village, and a greedy, impatient neighbour who heard the story decided almost at once that he would simply copy the old man's every action exactly, hoping eagerly to win for himself an equally grand and glittering pile of treasure without any of the genuine warmth that had earned it in the first place. Following what he believed were the very same steps precisely, he deliberately dropped his own rice ball down a similar hole he managed to find nearby and then tumbled in determinedly after it himself, but once safely inside the little home below, he behaved rudely, loudly, and impatiently toward the gathered mice, pushing forward without any manners at all and demanding his promised treasure immediately, rather than showing even the smallest hint of warmth, patience, or genuine gratitude in return. Startled, offended, and thoroughly unimpressed by such graceless rudeness, the mice quickly scattered away from him in every direction and promptly blew out their little lantern altogether, leaving the greedy, grumbling neighbour stuck fumbling about entirely hopelessly in the pitch-black darkness, comically unable to find his way back up out of the hole again for quite some considerable and thoroughly deserved time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>the rice ball</li><li>the hole</li><li>the mice</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man sits down to eat his lunch</li><li>He accidentally drops a rice ball</li><li>It rolls into a small round hole</li><li>He hears mice singing a happy song below</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>sat down comfortably on a sunny hillside to eat his simple lunch</li><li>one slipped from his fingers and dropped to the ground</li><li>disappeared entirely into a small, round hole</li><li>he heard tiny, cheerful mouse voices singing a happy little song</li><li>rolled quickly away downhill</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the old man's ___ rolled into a small round ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a cheerful old man sat down on a hillside to eat his lunch, when he accidentally dropped one of his rice balls. It rolled quickly downhill and disappeared into a small round hole. The old man peered curiously into the hole and heard tiny, delighted mouse voices singing a happy little song below, thanking him for the tasty treat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the Japanese countryside, a cheerful old man sat down comfortably on a sunny hillside to eat his simple lunch of rice balls, when suddenly one slipped from his fingers and dropped to the ground. To his surprise, it rolled quickly away downhill and disappeared entirely into a small, round hole tucked among the grass. Curious rather than upset, the old man knelt down and peered carefully into the dark little hole, and to his delight he heard tiny, cheerful mouse voices singing a happy little song from somewhere down below, thanking him warmly for the unexpected and tasty treat that had just rolled in.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the peaceful Japanese countryside, there lived a cheerful old man who one fine, sunny day sat himself down comfortably on a grassy hillside to enjoy his simple lunch of freshly made rice balls, wrapped neatly in cloth as he always carried them. Quite suddenly, and entirely by accident, one of the rice balls slipped clean out of his fingers and dropped to the ground beside him, and to his considerable surprise it rolled quickly away down the slope of the hill before disappearing entirely into a small, round hole tucked away among the tall grass. Rather than feeling upset at the loss of his lunch, the old man, being naturally curious by nature, knelt down at once and peered carefully into the dark little hole, and to his genuine delight he heard tiny, cheerful mouse voices singing a happy little song from somewhere far down below, thanking him warmly and enthusiastically for the unexpected and thoroughly tasty treat that had just come rolling unexpectedly into their home.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the old man</li><li>another rice ball</li><li>the hole</li><li>the mice</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The old man drops another rice ball to hear the song</li><li>He leans in too far and tumbles down the hole</li><li>He finds a cosy home full of grateful mice</li><li>The mice thank him for his kindness and laughter</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>could not resist dropping a second rice ball into the hole</li><li>leaned in just a little too far and tumbled right down the hole</li><li>a cosy, warmly lit little home filled with a whole community of grateful mice</li><li>thanked him again and again with great enthusiasm</li><li>landing with a soft, gentle thump</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the old man tumbled down the ___, and the grateful mice thanked him for his ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Charmed by the sound, the old man dropped another rice ball into the hole just to hear the cheerful mouse song again, and then, laughing at his own curiosity, he leaned in a little too far and tumbled right down the hole himself. Instead of being afraid, he found a cosy home full of grateful mice, who happily thanked him and rewarded his kindness and cheerful laughter with baskets of shining treasure.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly charmed by the sound of their singing, the old man could not resist dropping a second rice ball into the hole, simply to hear the cheerful little mouse song echo up to him once again, and then, laughing heartily at his own foolish curiosity, he leaned in just a little too far and tumbled right down the hole himself, landing with a soft thump. Rather than feeling frightened, he found himself inside a cosy, warmly lit little home filled with a whole community of grateful mice, who gathered round him happily, thanked him again and again for his kindness, and delighted in his easy, cheerful laughter, eventually rewarding him generously with baskets overflowing with shining treasure before helping him safely back up to the surface.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Utterly charmed and thoroughly amused by the delightful sound of their singing, the old man simply could not resist the temptation to drop a second rice ball down into the very same hole, purely so that he might hear the cheerful little mouse song echo pleasantly up to him once more, and then, laughing heartily and rather helplessly at his own foolish curiosity, he leaned in just that little bit too far and tumbled right down the hole himself, landing with a soft, gentle thump somewhere in the cosy darkness below. Rather than feeling the slightest bit frightened by his tumble, he instead found himself inside a warm, cheerfully lit little home filled from wall to wall with an entire lively community of grateful mice, who gathered eagerly all around him, thanked him again and again with great enthusiasm for his unexpected kindness, and delighted openly in his easy, warm-hearted laughter, before eventually rewarding him most generously with several baskets overflowing with shining treasure and then carefully helping him find his way safely back up to the surface above.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the mice</li><li>treasure</li><li>the neighbour</li><li>the dark</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The mice reward the old man with baskets of treasure</li><li>A greedy neighbour hears about his good fortune</li><li>The neighbour copies him but is rude to the mice</li><li>The mice blow out the lantern, leaving him stuck in the dark</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rewarding him generously with baskets overflowing with shining treasure</li><li>decided he would simply copy the old man's every action exactly</li><li>behaved rudely and impatiently toward the gathered mice</li><li>leaving the greedy, grumbling neighbour stuck fumbling about hopelessly</li><li>helping him safely back up to the surface</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the greedy neighbour was rude to the mice, so they blew out their ___ and left him stuck in the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Word of the old man's good fortune spread through the village, and a greedy neighbour decided to copy him exactly, hoping for treasure too. But when he dropped his rice ball down a similar hole and tumbled in himself, he behaved rudely and impatiently toward the mice, demanding treasure at once. Startled and offended, the mice scurried away and blew out their lantern, leaving the greedy neighbour stuck fumbling about in the dark, comically unable to find his way back out for quite some time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, word of the old man's remarkable good fortune had spread throughout the whole village, and a greedy, impatient neighbour decided he would simply copy the old man's actions exactly, hoping to win himself an equally grand pile of treasure. Following the very same steps, he dropped his own rice ball down a similar hole he found nearby and then deliberately tumbled in after it himself, but once inside, he behaved rudely and impatiently toward the gathered mice, shoving forward and loudly demanding his treasure at once rather than showing any warmth or gratitude. Startled and rather offended by his rudeness, the mice quickly scurried away in every direction and blew out their little lantern altogether, leaving the greedy, grumbling neighbour stuck fumbling about hopelessly in the pitch darkness, comically unable to find his way back out again for quite some considerable time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, word of the cheerful old man's remarkable stroke of good fortune had spread rapidly throughout the whole surrounding village, and a greedy, impatient neighbour who heard the story decided almost at once that he would simply copy the old man's every action exactly, hoping eagerly to win for himself an equally grand and glittering pile of treasure without any of the genuine warmth that had earned it in the first place. Following what he believed were the very same steps precisely, he deliberately dropped his own rice ball down a similar hole he managed to find nearby and then tumbled in determinedly after it himself, but once safely inside the little home below, he behaved rudely, loudly, and impatiently toward the gathered mice, pushing forward without any manners at all and demanding his promised treasure immediately, rather than showing even the smallest hint of warmth, patience, or genuine gratitude in return. Startled, offended, and thoroughly unimpressed by such graceless rudeness, the mice quickly scattered away from him in every direction and promptly blew out their little lantern altogether, leaving the greedy, grumbling neighbour stuck fumbling about entirely hopelessly in the pitch-black darkness, comically unable to find his way back up out of the hole again for quite some considerable and thoroughly deserved time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 24: Omusubi Kororin (The Rolling Rice Ball)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 24 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 24 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 25 (Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the old man's ___ rolled into a small round ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the old man tumbled down the ___, and the grateful mice thanked him for his ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the greedy neighbour was rude to the mice, so they blew out their ___ and left him stuck in the ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 25’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the poor man and the straw as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the poor man</li><li>the straw</li><li>the horse</li><li>the wealthy man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The man is told to keep whatever he is given first, and keeps a piece of straw</li><li>He trades the straw-tied fly for an orange, then the orange for cloth</li><li>He trades the cloth for a tired horse, and nurses it back to health</li><li>A wealthy man trades his house and land for the healthy horse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The man was told to keep whatever he was given first after leaving the temple</li><li>The man refused to trade his straw for anything at all</li><li>The man traded the cloth for a tired horse and nursed it back to health</li><li>A wealthy man offered his house and land for the healthy horse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor but hopeful man prayed for good fortune, and he was told in a vision to keep whatever he was given first when he left the temple. Walking out, he tripped and accidentally grabbed hold of a single piece of straw. He decided to trust the vision and kept it carefully, wondering what could possibly come of something so small.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Along the road, a buzzing fly kept bothering a crying baby, so the man tied the straw to the fly to amuse the child, and the grateful mother gave him a juicy orange in thanks. Further along, he traded the orange for a length of fine cloth from a thirsty traveller, then traded the cloth for a tired horse from a merchant who needed help, and nursed the horse back to health with care.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a wealthy man needed to travel urgently and offered his own grand house and land in exchange for the now-healthy horse, since he had no time to negotiate further. The poor man agreed gladly, and just like that, a single piece of straw had led him, step by step and trade by fair trade, all the way to becoming a rich and grateful landowner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, there lived a poor but hopeful man who prayed earnestly at a temple for a change in his fortune, and that very night he was told in a vivid vision to keep carefully whatever he happened to be given first after he left the temple grounds the following morning. Walking out the next day exactly as the vision had instructed, he stumbled slightly over a loose stone and accidentally grabbed hold of a single piece of straw lying on the path to steady himself. Rather than tossing it aside as worthless, he decided to trust the strange vision completely and kept the straw carefully in his hand, wondering all the while what on earth could possibly come of something so small and seemingly useless.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A little further along the dusty road, he noticed a buzzing fly that kept pestering and upsetting a crying baby being carried by its tired mother, so the kind man tied his single piece of straw to the fly's leg to amuse the little one, and the grateful mother, delighted at how quickly her baby calmed down and smiled, gave him a large, juicy orange in return as thanks for his cleverness. Continuing on his way a little further still, he happily traded the orange for a fine length of beautifully woven cloth offered by a thirsty traveller desperate for something refreshing to eat, and not long after that, he traded the cloth again, this time for a tired, worn-out horse belonging to a merchant who urgently needed help along his own journey and no longer had the means to feed it properly. The man patiently and gently nursed the exhausted horse back to full health and strength over the following weeks, feeding it well and treating it kindly at every turn.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, one day, a wealthy man travelling in a great hurry needed a strong, healthy horse at once to reach an urgent appointment, and having no time whatsoever to haggle or negotiate a lengthy trade, he generously offered his own grand house and surrounding land in exchange for the now fully healthy horse the poor man had so carefully nursed back to strength. The poor man, scarcely able to believe his continued good fortune, agreed gladly and gratefully to the fair and generous exchange, and just like that, a single humble piece of straw had led him, step by careful step and fair trade by fair trade, all the way from having almost nothing at all to becoming a genuinely rich and deeply grateful landowner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in old Japan, there lived a poor but sincerely hopeful man who, weary of his continued bad luck, prayed earnestly and with great devotion at a quiet temple for some meaningful change in his fortune, and that very same night he was told, quite clearly, in a vivid and memorable vision, to keep careful hold of whatever he happened to be given first after he left the temple grounds the following morning, whatever it might turn out to be. Walking out from the temple gates the next day exactly as the vision had carefully instructed him, he stumbled unexpectedly over a loose stone lying across the path and accidentally grabbed hold of nothing more than a single, ordinary piece of straw lying nearby to steady himself as he nearly fell. Rather than simply tossing the straw carelessly aside as something entirely worthless, he decided, somewhat against his own better judgement, to trust the strange vision completely and kept the humble little piece of straw carefully in his hand as he continued on his way, wondering quietly to himself all the while what on earth could possibly ever come of something so small, plain, and seemingly useless as a single strand of straw.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A little further along the long, dusty road, he happened to notice a persistent, buzzing fly that kept pestering and upsetting a crying baby being carried wearily by its tired mother, so the kind and inventive man tied his one small piece of straw gently to the fly's leg to amuse the fretful little one, and the grateful mother, utterly delighted at how quickly and happily her baby settled down and began to smile again, gave him a large, ripe, and thoroughly juicy orange in warm thanks for his simple cleverness. Continuing on his way a good deal further still, he happily and fairly traded the juicy orange for a fine, beautifully woven length of cloth offered eagerly by a thirsty traveller who was desperate for something refreshing to eat along his own difficult journey, and not very long after that particular exchange, he traded the fine cloth once again, this time for a tired, worn-out, and rather neglected horse belonging to a travelling merchant who urgently needed help pressing onward and no longer had either the time or the means to feed and care for the poor animal properly. The kind man then patiently and gently nursed the exhausted, thin horse back to full health and strength over the many days and weeks that followed, feeding it well, grooming it carefully, and treating it with genuine kindness at every single turn along the way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, one particular day, a genuinely wealthy man travelling in a tremendous hurry to reach some urgent and unavoidable appointment found himself in desperate need of a strong, healthy horse at once, and having absolutely no spare time whatsoever left to haggle over a lengthy or complicated trade, he generously and gratefully offered his own grand house together with all its surrounding land in fair exchange for the now fully healthy, strong horse that the poor man had so carefully and patiently nursed back to strength from near ruin. The poor man, scarcely able to believe his own remarkable and continuing good fortune, agreed gladly, gratefully, and without a moment's hesitation to this fair and thoroughly generous exchange, and just like that, in the space of a single remarkable day's journey, one small and entirely humble piece of straw had led him, step by careful step and fair trade by fair honest trade, all the way from having almost nothing at all in the world to becoming a genuinely rich, deeply grateful, and thoroughly contented landowner for the rest of his days.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the man</li><li>the temple</li><li>the vision</li><li>the straw</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The poor man prays at the temple for good fortune</li><li>He is told in a vision to keep whatever he is given first</li><li>He stumbles and grabs a single piece of straw</li><li>He decides to trust the vision and keeps it</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>prayed earnestly at a temple for a change in his fortune</li><li>keep carefully whatever he happened to be given first</li><li>accidentally grabbed hold of a single piece of straw</li><li>decided to trust the strange vision completely</li><li>wondering what could possibly come of something so small</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the poor man was told in a ___ to keep whatever he was given, and he kept a single piece of ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a poor but hopeful man prayed for good fortune, and he was told in a vision to keep whatever he was given first when he left the temple. Walking out, he tripped and accidentally grabbed hold of a single piece of straw. He decided to trust the vision and kept it carefully, wondering what could possibly come of something so small.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, there lived a poor but hopeful man who prayed earnestly at a temple for a change in his fortune, and that very night he was told in a vivid vision to keep carefully whatever he happened to be given first after he left the temple grounds the following morning. Walking out the next day exactly as the vision had instructed, he stumbled slightly over a loose stone and accidentally grabbed hold of a single piece of straw lying on the path to steady himself. Rather than tossing it aside as worthless, he decided to trust the strange vision completely and kept the straw carefully in his hand, wondering all the while what on earth could possibly come of something so small and seemingly useless.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in old Japan, there lived a poor but sincerely hopeful man who, weary of his continued bad luck, prayed earnestly and with great devotion at a quiet temple for some meaningful change in his fortune, and that very same night he was told, quite clearly, in a vivid and memorable vision, to keep careful hold of whatever he happened to be given first after he left the temple grounds the following morning, whatever it might turn out to be. Walking out from the temple gates the next day exactly as the vision had carefully instructed him, he stumbled unexpectedly over a loose stone lying across the path and accidentally grabbed hold of nothing more than a single, ordinary piece of straw lying nearby to steady himself as he nearly fell. Rather than simply tossing the straw carelessly aside as something entirely worthless, he decided, somewhat against his own better judgement, to trust the strange vision completely and kept the humble little piece of straw carefully in his hand as he continued on his way, wondering quietly to himself all the while what on earth could possibly ever come of something so small, plain, and seemingly useless as a single strand of straw.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the fly</li><li>the orange</li><li>the cloth</li><li>the horse</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He ties the straw to a fly to amuse a crying baby</li><li>The grateful mother gives him an orange</li><li>He trades the orange for cloth, then the cloth for a horse</li><li>He nurses the tired horse back to health</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tied his single piece of straw to the fly's leg to amuse the little one</li><li>gave him a large, juicy orange in return as thanks</li><li>traded the cloth again, this time for a tired, worn-out horse</li><li>nursed the exhausted horse back to full health and strength</li><li>gave him a large, juicy orange in return as thanks</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the man traded his way from straw to ___, then to cloth, then to a tired ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Along the road, a buzzing fly kept bothering a crying baby, so the man tied the straw to the fly to amuse the child, and the grateful mother gave him a juicy orange in thanks. Further along, he traded the orange for a length of fine cloth from a thirsty traveller, then traded the cloth for a tired horse from a merchant who needed help, and nursed the horse back to health with care.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A little further along the dusty road, he noticed a buzzing fly that kept pestering and upsetting a crying baby being carried by its tired mother, so the kind man tied his single piece of straw to the fly's leg to amuse the little one, and the grateful mother, delighted at how quickly her baby calmed down and smiled, gave him a large, juicy orange in return as thanks for his cleverness. Continuing on his way a little further still, he happily traded the orange for a fine length of beautifully woven cloth offered by a thirsty traveller desperate for something refreshing to eat, and not long after that, he traded the cloth again, this time for a tired, worn-out horse belonging to a merchant who urgently needed help along his own journey and no longer had the means to feed it properly. The man patiently and gently nursed the exhausted horse back to full health and strength over the following weeks, feeding it well and treating it kindly at every turn.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A little further along the long, dusty road, he happened to notice a persistent, buzzing fly that kept pestering and upsetting a crying baby being carried wearily by its tired mother, so the kind and inventive man tied his one small piece of straw gently to the fly's leg to amuse the fretful little one, and the grateful mother, utterly delighted at how quickly and happily her baby settled down and began to smile again, gave him a large, ripe, and thoroughly juicy orange in warm thanks for his simple cleverness. Continuing on his way a good deal further still, he happily and fairly traded the juicy orange for a fine, beautifully woven length of cloth offered eagerly by a thirsty traveller who was desperate for something refreshing to eat along his own difficult journey, and not very long after that particular exchange, he traded the fine cloth once again, this time for a tired, worn-out, and rather neglected horse belonging to a travelling merchant who urgently needed help pressing onward and no longer had either the time or the means to feed and care for the poor animal properly. The kind man then patiently and gently nursed the exhausted, thin horse back to full health and strength over the many days and weeks that followed, feeding it well, grooming it carefully, and treating it with genuine kindness at every single turn along the way.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the wealthy man</li><li>the horse</li><li>the house</li><li>land</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A wealthy man needs a healthy horse urgently</li><li>He offers his house and land in exchange</li><li>The poor man agrees gladly to the trade</li><li>The straw has led him step by step to great wealth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>needed a strong, healthy horse at once to reach an urgent appointment</li><li>offered his own grand house and surrounding land in exchange</li><li>agreed gladly and gratefully to the fair and generous exchange</li><li>led him, step by careful step and fair trade by fair trade</li><li>having no time whatsoever to haggle or negotiate</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a wealthy man traded his ___ and land for the horse, making the poor man a rich ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, a wealthy man needed to travel urgently and offered his own grand house and land in exchange for the now-healthy horse, since he had no time to negotiate further. The poor man agreed gladly, and just like that, a single piece of straw had led him, step by step and trade by fair trade, all the way to becoming a rich and grateful landowner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, one day, a wealthy man travelling in a great hurry needed a strong, healthy horse at once to reach an urgent appointment, and having no time whatsoever to haggle or negotiate a lengthy trade, he generously offered his own grand house and surrounding land in exchange for the now fully healthy horse the poor man had so carefully nursed back to strength. The poor man, scarcely able to believe his continued good fortune, agreed gladly and gratefully to the fair and generous exchange, and just like that, a single humble piece of straw had led him, step by careful step and fair trade by fair trade, all the way from having almost nothing at all to becoming a genuinely rich and deeply grateful landowner.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, one particular day, a genuinely wealthy man travelling in a tremendous hurry to reach some urgent and unavoidable appointment found himself in desperate need of a strong, healthy horse at once, and having absolutely no spare time whatsoever left to haggle over a lengthy or complicated trade, he generously and gratefully offered his own grand house together with all its surrounding land in fair exchange for the now fully healthy, strong horse that the poor man had so carefully and patiently nursed back to strength from near ruin. The poor man, scarcely able to believe his own remarkable and continuing good fortune, agreed gladly, gratefully, and without a moment's hesitation to this fair and thoroughly generous exchange, and just like that, in the space of a single remarkable day's journey, one small and entirely humble piece of straw had led him, step by careful step and fair trade by fair honest trade, all the way from having almost nothing at all in the world to becoming a genuinely rich, deeply grateful, and thoroughly contented landowner for the rest of his days.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 25: Warashibe Choja (The Straw Millionaire)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 5: Trusted Retainer (blue belt) — focus: Summarising longer or more complex passages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 25 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 25 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 26 (Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the poor man was told in a ___ to keep whatever he was given, and he kept a single piece of ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the man traded his way from straw to ___, then to cloth, then to a tired ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, a wealthy man traded his ___ and land for the horse, making the poor man a rich ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (Purple belt) — Stories 26–30":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 26’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Minokichi and the snow woman as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi</li><li>the snow woman</li><li>Oyuki</li><li>the old man</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi and the old man shelter from a snowstorm in a hut</li><li>The snow woman spares young Minokichi but makes him promise secrecy</li><li>Years later, Minokichi marries gentle Oyuki and they raise a family</li><li>He tells Oyuki the story, and she reveals she was the snow woman</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi and the old man took shelter from a snowstorm in a hut</li><li>The snow woman warned Minokichi never to speak of what he saw</li><li>Minokichi never married after that snowy night</li><li>Oyuki revealed that she herself was the snow woman</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, two woodcutters, an old man and a young man named Minokichi, were caught in a terrible snowstorm and took shelter in a small hut. In the middle of the night, Minokichi woke to see a pale, beautiful woman made of snow leaning gently over the old man, her cold breath drifting through the air. She turned to Minokichi and, seeing his youth, spared him, but warned him sternly never to speak of what he had seen, or terrible consequences would follow.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years later, Minokichi married a gentle, kind woman named Oyuki, and together they lived happily and raised a family. One quiet evening, watching her calm face in the candlelight, Minokichi found himself telling her the strange story from his youth, the snow storm and the pale woman he had once seen and never spoken of since.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished speaking, Oyuki's expression changed, and she quietly revealed that she herself was the snow woman from that very night, and that he had just broken his solemn promise. Yet because of their children and the love they shared, she chose not to leave in anger, but gently reminded him to always care well for their family, and from then on their life together continued on, gentle and unafraid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the snowy mountains of Japan, two woodcutters, an old man and a young man named Minokichi, were caught unexpectedly in a fierce and sudden snowstorm one evening and took shelter together in a small, drafty hut to wait it out. In the deep, quiet hours of the night, Minokichi woke suddenly and saw, through the dim light, a pale and strikingly beautiful woman made entirely of drifting snow leaning gently over the sleeping old man, her cold, misty breath curling softly through the freezing air of the hut. Turning slowly to look at Minokichi himself, the snow woman studied him for a long moment, and seeing how young he still was, she chose to spare his life, though she warned him sternly, with quiet but unmistakable seriousness, that he must never speak a single word of what he had witnessed that night to anyone, ever, or terrible consequences would surely follow him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years passed peacefully after that strange night, and in time Minokichi married a gentle, quietly beautiful woman named Oyuki, and together the two of them built a warm and happy home, raising a small family with great love and contentment through the passing seasons. One quiet winter evening, years into their marriage, as Minokichi sat watching his wife's calm, familiar face glowing softly in the candlelight, he found himself slowly telling her the strange and half-forgotten story from his youth, the sudden snowstorm long ago, the small hut, and the pale, otherworldly woman he had once glimpsed and never spoken a word of to a single living soul since that frightening night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished recounting the tale, Oyuki's calm, familiar expression changed slowly and unmistakably before his eyes, and she quietly, almost sadly, revealed to him that she herself had been the very snow woman from that long-ago night, and that he had now, at long last, broken the solemn promise he had made to her all those years before. And yet, because of the deep love they shared and the children they had raised together so happily, she chose gently not to leave him in anger or vanish as the old warning might have suggested, but instead reminded him softly and tenderly to always care well and faithfully for their family in the years still ahead. From that evening onward, their life together simply continued on much as it always had, gentle, steady, and entirely unafraid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, high in the snowy mountains of old Japan, two woodcutters, an experienced old man and a hardworking young man named Minokichi, found themselves caught unexpectedly one bitter evening in a fierce and sudden snowstorm that swept in without warning across the mountainside, and the two of them hurried to take shelter together inside a small, drafty wooden hut to wait out the worst of the freezing weather until morning. In the deep, silent hours of that long night, Minokichi woke with a start and saw, through the dim, flickering light, a pale and strikingly beautiful woman made entirely of drifting, swirling snow leaning gently and silently over the sleeping old man beside him, her cold, misty breath curling softly and eerily through the freezing air that filled the tiny hut. Turning slowly and deliberately to look directly at Minokichi himself, the mysterious snow woman studied his face carefully for a long, tense moment, and seeing plainly how young and gentle-hearted he still was, she chose quietly to spare his life entirely, though she warned him sternly and with quiet but unmistakable seriousness that he must never, under any circumstances, speak a single word of what he had witnessed that strange night to any living soul, or else terrible and lasting consequences would surely and certainly follow him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many peaceful years passed steadily by after that strange and unforgettable night, and in time Minokichi eventually married a gentle, quietly beautiful young woman named Oyuki, and together the two of them built a warm, contented, and genuinely happy home for themselves, raising a small and loving family together through many passing seasons of quiet joy. One particularly quiet winter evening, years and years into their long and settled marriage, as Minokichi sat contentedly watching his wife's calm, endlessly familiar face glowing softly and warmly in the flickering candlelight of their little home, he found himself, almost without quite meaning to, slowly telling her the strange and half-forgotten story from his distant youth, the sudden snowstorm long ago on the mountainside, the small, drafty hut, and the pale, otherworldly, snow-white woman he had once glimpsed there and had never spoken a single word of to any other living soul since that frightening, unforgettable night so long before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished quietly recounting the whole strange tale from beginning to end, Oyuki's calm and endlessly familiar expression changed slowly, subtly, and quite unmistakably before his startled eyes, and she quietly, almost sadly and with great tenderness, revealed to him at last that she herself had in fact been the very snow woman from that distant, long-ago night, and that he had now, after all these peaceful years together, finally broken the solemn and sacred promise he had once made to her as a frightened young man. And yet, because of the deep and genuine love the two of them had built and shared together over so many years, and because of the children they had raised together so happily and so well, she chose gently and deliberately not to leave him in anger or vanish suddenly into the winter air as the old warning might once have suggested she must, but instead reminded him softly, tenderly, and without a trace of bitterness to always care well and faithfully for their beloved family in all the years still stretching out ahead of them both. From that quiet evening onward, their long and settled life together simply continued on much as it always had before, gentle, steady, deeply loving, and entirely unafraid of whatever secrets the years had once held.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi</li><li>the old man</li><li>the snowstorm</li><li>the hut</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi and the old man are caught in a snowstorm</li><li>They shelter together in a small hut</li><li>Minokichi wakes and sees the pale snow woman</li><li>She spares him but warns him to stay silent</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>caught unexpectedly in a fierce and sudden snowstorm</li><li>took shelter together in a small, drafty hut</li><li>a pale and strikingly beautiful woman made entirely of drifting snow</li><li>warned him sternly that he must never speak a single word</li><li>her cold, misty breath curling softly through the freezing air</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Minokichi saw a pale ___ in the hut, and she warned him never to ___ of it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, two woodcutters, an old man and a young man named Minokichi, were caught in a terrible snowstorm and took shelter in a small hut. In the middle of the night, Minokichi woke to see a pale, beautiful woman made of snow leaning gently over the old man, her cold breath drifting through the air. She turned to Minokichi and, seeing his youth, spared him, but warned him sternly never to speak of what he had seen, or terrible consequences would follow.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the snowy mountains of Japan, two woodcutters, an old man and a young man named Minokichi, were caught unexpectedly in a fierce and sudden snowstorm one evening and took shelter together in a small, drafty hut to wait it out. In the deep, quiet hours of the night, Minokichi woke suddenly and saw, through the dim light, a pale and strikingly beautiful woman made entirely of drifting snow leaning gently over the sleeping old man, her cold, misty breath curling softly through the freezing air of the hut. Turning slowly to look at Minokichi himself, the snow woman studied him for a long moment, and seeing how young he still was, she chose to spare his life, though she warned him sternly, with quiet but unmistakable seriousness, that he must never speak a single word of what he had witnessed that night to anyone, ever, or terrible consequences would surely follow him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, high in the snowy mountains of old Japan, two woodcutters, an experienced old man and a hardworking young man named Minokichi, found themselves caught unexpectedly one bitter evening in a fierce and sudden snowstorm that swept in without warning across the mountainside, and the two of them hurried to take shelter together inside a small, drafty wooden hut to wait out the worst of the freezing weather until morning. In the deep, silent hours of that long night, Minokichi woke with a start and saw, through the dim, flickering light, a pale and strikingly beautiful woman made entirely of drifting, swirling snow leaning gently and silently over the sleeping old man beside him, her cold, misty breath curling softly and eerily through the freezing air that filled the tiny hut. Turning slowly and deliberately to look directly at Minokichi himself, the mysterious snow woman studied his face carefully for a long, tense moment, and seeing plainly how young and gentle-hearted he still was, she chose quietly to spare his life entirely, though she warned him sternly and with quiet but unmistakable seriousness that he must never, under any circumstances, speak a single word of what he had witnessed that strange night to any living soul, or else terrible and lasting consequences would surely and certainly follow him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Minokichi</li><li>Oyuki</li><li>the family</li><li>years</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Years pass and Minokichi marries gentle Oyuki</li><li>Together they build a happy home</li><li>They raise a family through the seasons</li><li>One evening he begins telling her the old story</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>married a gentle, quietly beautiful woman named Oyuki</li><li>built a warm and happy home</li><li>raising a small family with great love and contentment</li><li>found himself slowly telling her the strange and half-forgotten story</li><li>watching his wife's calm, familiar face glowing in the candlelight</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Minokichi married gentle ___, and years later he told her the old ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years later, Minokichi married a gentle, kind woman named Oyuki, and together they lived happily and raised a family. One quiet evening, watching her calm face in the candlelight, Minokichi found himself telling her the strange story from his youth, the snow storm and the pale woman he had once seen and never spoken of since.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years passed peacefully after that strange night, and in time Minokichi married a gentle, quietly beautiful woman named Oyuki, and together the two of them built a warm and happy home, raising a small family with great love and contentment through the passing seasons. One quiet winter evening, years into their marriage, as Minokichi sat watching his wife's calm, familiar face glowing softly in the candlelight, he found himself slowly telling her the strange and half-forgotten story from his youth, the sudden snowstorm long ago, the small hut, and the pale, otherworldly woman he had once glimpsed and never spoken a word of to a single living soul since that frightening night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many peaceful years passed steadily by after that strange and unforgettable night, and in time Minokichi eventually married a gentle, quietly beautiful young woman named Oyuki, and together the two of them built a warm, contented, and genuinely happy home for themselves, raising a small and loving family together through many passing seasons of quiet joy. One particularly quiet winter evening, years and years into their long and settled marriage, as Minokichi sat contentedly watching his wife's calm, endlessly familiar face glowing softly and warmly in the flickering candlelight of their little home, he found himself, almost without quite meaning to, slowly telling her the strange and half-forgotten story from his distant youth, the sudden snowstorm long ago on the mountainside, the small, drafty hut, and the pale, otherworldly, snow-white woman he had once glimpsed there and had never spoken a single word of to any other living soul since that frightening, unforgettable night so long before.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Oyuki</li><li>the promise</li><li>the family</li><li>their life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Oyuki's expression changes as he finishes the story</li><li>She reveals she was the snow woman all along</li><li>She chooses not to leave, because of their family</li><li>Their gentle life together continues on</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>revealed to him that she herself had been the very snow woman</li><li>he had now, at long last, broken the solemn promise</li><li>chose gently not to leave him in anger</li><li>reminded him softly and tenderly to always care well</li><li>reminded him softly to always care well for their family</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Oyuki revealed she was the ___, but she stayed for the sake of their ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished speaking, Oyuki's expression changed, and she quietly revealed that she herself was the snow woman from that very night, and that he had just broken his solemn promise. Yet because of their children and the love they shared, she chose not to leave in anger, but gently reminded him to always care well for their family, and from then on their life together continued on, gentle and unafraid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished recounting the tale, Oyuki's calm, familiar expression changed slowly and unmistakably before his eyes, and she quietly, almost sadly, revealed to him that she herself had been the very snow woman from that long-ago night, and that he had now, at long last, broken the solemn promise he had made to her all those years before. And yet, because of the deep love they shared and the children they had raised together so happily, she chose gently not to leave him in anger or vanish as the old warning might have suggested, but instead reminded him softly and tenderly to always care well and faithfully for their family in the years still ahead. From that evening onward, their life together simply continued on much as it always had, gentle, steady, and entirely unafraid.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As he finished quietly recounting the whole strange tale from beginning to end, Oyuki's calm and endlessly familiar expression changed slowly, subtly, and quite unmistakably before his startled eyes, and she quietly, almost sadly and with great tenderness, revealed to him at last that she herself had in fact been the very snow woman from that distant, long-ago night, and that he had now, after all these peaceful years together, finally broken the solemn and sacred promise he had once made to her as a frightened young man. And yet, because of the deep and genuine love the two of them had built and shared together over so many years, and because of the children they had raised together so happily and so well, she chose gently and deliberately not to leave him in anger or vanish suddenly into the winter air as the old warning might once have suggested she must, but instead reminded him softly, tenderly, and without a trace of bitterness to always care well and faithfully for their beloved family in all the years still stretching out ahead of them both. From that quiet evening onward, their long and settled life together simply continued on much as it always had before, gentle, steady, deeply loving, and entirely unafraid of whatever secrets the years had once held.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 26: Yuki-onna (The Snow Woman)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 26 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 26 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 27 (Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Minokichi saw a pale ___ in the hut, and she warned him never to ___ of it.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Minokichi married gentle ___, and years later he told her the old ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Oyuki revealed she was the ___, but she stayed for the sake of their ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 27’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the rabbit and the tanuki as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the rabbit</li><li>the tanuki</li><li>the farmer</li><li>the boats</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tanuki plays unkind tricks on the hardworking farmer</li><li>The clever rabbit challenges the tanuki to a boat race</li><li>The rabbit builds the tanuki's boat from clay, her own from wood</li><li>The clay boat crumbles, and the soaked tanuki learns his lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tanuki played unkind tricks on a hardworking farmer</li><li>The rabbit built the tanuki's boat out of sturdy wood</li><li>The tanuki's clay boat crumbled apart in the water</li><li>The tanuki left the farmer's crops alone after his lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a mischievous tanuki kept playing unkind tricks on a hardworking farmer, ruining his crops and causing trouble around the farm again and again. A clever rabbit who lived nearby and cared about the farmer decided the tanuki needed to learn a firm but gentle lesson about kindness and consequences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The rabbit challenged the tanuki to a friendly boat race across the pond, offering to build them each a boat. Slyly, the rabbit built the tanuki's boat out of soft clay, while building her own from sturdy wood. As the two boats set off across the water, the tanuki's clay boat slowly began to crack and soften.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, the tanuki's boat crumbled apart completely, dropping him with a splash into the shallow water below. Soaked and spluttered but unharmed, the tanuki paddled to shore feeling rather silly, while the rabbit calmly reminded him that unkind tricks always lead to trouble. From that day on, the tanuki left the farmer's crops alone and thought more carefully before playing tricks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the hills of Japan, a mischievous tanuki kept playing one unkind trick after another on a hardworking farmer who lived nearby, sneaking into his fields at night to ruin his carefully tended crops and causing all manner of trouble around the farm again and again, no matter how many times the farmer tried to stop him. A clever rabbit who lived close by and genuinely cared about the farmer's wellbeing eventually decided that enough was enough, and that the troublesome tanuki badly needed to learn a firm, if gentle and entirely non-violent, lesson about kindness, honesty, and the natural consequences of his own careless behaviour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a plan already forming in her quick mind, the rabbit approached the tanuki and cheerfully challenged him to a friendly boat race across the wide village pond, generously offering to build a sturdy little boat for each of them so the race could be fair. Slyly, without the tanuki noticing at all, the rabbit built his boat out of nothing more than soft, unbaked clay, while carefully building her own boat from good, sturdy wood that she knew would hold together perfectly well on the water. As the two little boats set off eagerly across the pond side by side, with both animals paddling hard toward the far shore, the tanuki's clay boat slowly but surely began to soften in the water, cracking apart bit by bit beneath him as he paddled on, entirely unaware of what was happening.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, the tanuki's crumbling clay boat finally gave way completely, breaking apart beneath him and dropping him with a great, comical splash straight down into the shallow water of the pond below. Soaked from head to toe and spluttering indignantly, but entirely unharmed by his sudden and rather undignified dunking, the tanuki paddled his way awkwardly back to the shore feeling thoroughly silly and a good deal more thoughtful than before, while the rabbit calmly and kindly reminded him that unkind tricks played on others always seem to lead to trouble of one kind or another eventually. From that day forward, the chastened tanuki left the farmer's crops entirely alone and thought a great deal more carefully before ever considering playing another unkind trick on anyone again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the quiet hills of old Japan, there lived a mischievous tanuki who took great and rather selfish delight in playing one unkind trick after another on a hardworking farmer living nearby, sneaking repeatedly into the man's carefully tended fields under cover of darkness to ruin his crops and causing all manner of frustrating trouble around the small farm again and again, no matter how many clever traps or careful precautions the increasingly weary farmer tried to set against him. A genuinely clever rabbit who lived close by and cared a great deal about the honest farmer's wellbeing eventually decided, after watching the trouble continue for far too long, that enough was truly enough, and that the endlessly troublesome tanuki badly needed to learn a firm, though entirely gentle and non-violent, lesson about kindness, honesty, and the natural, inevitable consequences that tend to follow careless, selfish behaviour sooner or later.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a clever plan already forming steadily in her quick and thoughtful mind, the rabbit approached the unsuspecting tanuki one bright morning and cheerfully challenged him to a friendly boat race across the wide village pond, generously offering to build a sturdy little boat for each of them personally so that the whole race might seem perfectly fair and above board to any onlookers. Slyly, and entirely without the boastful tanuki noticing what she was really doing, the rabbit carefully built his boat out of nothing sturdier than soft, unbaked clay scooped straight from the riverbank, while quietly and carefully building her own boat instead from good, sturdy, well-seasoned wood that she knew perfectly well would hold together strongly and safely on the open water. As the two little boats were finally launched and set off eagerly across the pond side by side, with both animals paddling hard and competitively toward the distant far shore, the tanuki's soft clay boat slowly but surely began to soften and dissolve in the cool pond water, cracking apart bit by careless bit beneath him as he paddled determinedly on, entirely unaware of the trouble steadily closing in around him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, the tanuki's slowly crumbling clay boat finally gave way completely and without warning, breaking apart noisily beneath him and dropping him with a great, thoroughly comical splash straight down into the shallow water of the pond below, much to the quiet amusement of a few nearby ducks. Soaked entirely from head to toe and spluttering indignantly as he floundered about, but entirely unharmed and quite unhurt by his sudden and rather undignified dunking in the pond, the thoroughly embarrassed tanuki paddled his way awkwardly back toward the shore feeling remarkably silly indeed and a good deal more thoughtful than he had felt in a very long while, while the clever rabbit calmly and kindly, without a trace of cruelty in her voice, reminded him gently that unkind tricks played carelessly on others always seem to lead to some form of trouble or embarrassment sooner or later, one way or another. From that memorable day forward, the thoroughly chastened tanuki left the hardworking farmer's crops entirely and permanently alone, and he thought a great deal more carefully from then on before ever again considering playing an unkind trick on anyone he happened to meet.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tanuki</li><li>the farmer</li><li>the rabbit</li><li>the crops</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tanuki plays unkind tricks on the farmer</li><li>He ruins crops and causes trouble again and again</li><li>The clever rabbit decides he needs a lesson</li><li>She wants to teach him about kindness and consequences</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>kept playing one unkind trick after another on a hardworking farmer</li><li>sneaking into his fields at night to ruin his carefully tended crops</li><li>genuinely cared about the farmer's wellbeing</li><li>badly needed to learn a firm, if gentle and entirely non-violent, lesson</li><li>causing all manner of trouble around the farm</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the mischievous ___ kept playing tricks on the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a mischievous tanuki kept playing unkind tricks on a hardworking farmer, ruining his crops and causing trouble around the farm again and again. A clever rabbit who lived nearby and cared about the farmer decided the tanuki needed to learn a firm but gentle lesson about kindness and consequences.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the hills of Japan, a mischievous tanuki kept playing one unkind trick after another on a hardworking farmer who lived nearby, sneaking into his fields at night to ruin his carefully tended crops and causing all manner of trouble around the farm again and again, no matter how many times the farmer tried to stop him. A clever rabbit who lived close by and genuinely cared about the farmer's wellbeing eventually decided that enough was enough, and that the troublesome tanuki badly needed to learn a firm, if gentle and entirely non-violent, lesson about kindness, honesty, and the natural consequences of his own careless behaviour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in the quiet hills of old Japan, there lived a mischievous tanuki who took great and rather selfish delight in playing one unkind trick after another on a hardworking farmer living nearby, sneaking repeatedly into the man's carefully tended fields under cover of darkness to ruin his crops and causing all manner of frustrating trouble around the small farm again and again, no matter how many clever traps or careful precautions the increasingly weary farmer tried to set against him. A genuinely clever rabbit who lived close by and cared a great deal about the honest farmer's wellbeing eventually decided, after watching the trouble continue for far too long, that enough was truly enough, and that the endlessly troublesome tanuki badly needed to learn a firm, though entirely gentle and non-violent, lesson about kindness, honesty, and the natural, inevitable consequences that tend to follow careless, selfish behaviour sooner or later.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the rabbit</li><li>the tanuki</li><li>clay</li><li>wood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The rabbit challenges the tanuki to a boat race</li><li>She offers to build a boat for each of them</li><li>She builds the tanuki's boat from soft clay</li><li>She builds her own boat from sturdy wood</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cheerfully challenged him to a friendly boat race across the pond</li><li>generously offering to build a sturdy little boat for each of them</li><li>built his boat out of nothing more than soft, unbaked clay</li><li>carefully building her own boat from good, sturdy wood</li><li>built his boat out of nothing more than soft, unbaked clay</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the rabbit built the tanuki's boat from ___ and her own from sturdy ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The rabbit challenged the tanuki to a friendly boat race across the pond, offering to build them each a boat. Slyly, the rabbit built the tanuki's boat out of soft clay, while building her own from sturdy wood. As the two boats set off across the water, the tanuki's clay boat slowly began to crack and soften.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a plan already forming in her quick mind, the rabbit approached the tanuki and cheerfully challenged him to a friendly boat race across the wide village pond, generously offering to build a sturdy little boat for each of them so the race could be fair. Slyly, without the tanuki noticing at all, the rabbit built his boat out of nothing more than soft, unbaked clay, while carefully building her own boat from good, sturdy wood that she knew would hold together perfectly well on the water. As the two little boats set off eagerly across the pond side by side, with both animals paddling hard toward the far shore, the tanuki's clay boat slowly but surely began to soften in the water, cracking apart bit by bit beneath him as he paddled on, entirely unaware of what was happening.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>With a clever plan already forming steadily in her quick and thoughtful mind, the rabbit approached the unsuspecting tanuki one bright morning and cheerfully challenged him to a friendly boat race across the wide village pond, generously offering to build a sturdy little boat for each of them personally so that the whole race might seem perfectly fair and above board to any onlookers. Slyly, and entirely without the boastful tanuki noticing what she was really doing, the rabbit carefully built his boat out of nothing sturdier than soft, unbaked clay scooped straight from the riverbank, while quietly and carefully building her own boat instead from good, sturdy, well-seasoned wood that she knew perfectly well would hold together strongly and safely on the open water. As the two little boats were finally launched and set off eagerly across the pond side by side, with both animals paddling hard and competitively toward the distant far shore, the tanuki's soft clay boat slowly but surely began to soften and dissolve in the cool pond water, cracking apart bit by careless bit beneath him as he paddled determinedly on, entirely unaware of the trouble steadily closing in around him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the boat</li><li>the water</li><li>the tanuki</li><li>the lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tanuki's clay boat cracks apart in the water</li><li>He falls with a splash into the shallow pond</li><li>Soaked but unharmed, he paddles to shore feeling silly</li><li>He learns his lesson and leaves the crops alone</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tanuki's crumbling clay boat finally gave way completely</li><li>dropping him with a great, comical splash</li><li>entirely unharmed by his sudden and rather undignified dunking</li><li>left the farmer's crops entirely alone</li><li>paddled his way awkwardly back to the shore feeling thoroughly silly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the tanuki's clay boat fell apart in the ___, and he learned that unkind tricks lead to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before long, the tanuki's boat crumbled apart completely, dropping him with a splash into the shallow water below. Soaked and spluttered but unharmed, the tanuki paddled to shore feeling rather silly, while the rabbit calmly reminded him that unkind tricks always lead to trouble. From that day on, the tanuki left the farmer's crops alone and thought more carefully before playing tricks.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, the tanuki's crumbling clay boat finally gave way completely, breaking apart beneath him and dropping him with a great, comical splash straight down into the shallow water of the pond below. Soaked from head to toe and spluttering indignantly, but entirely unharmed by his sudden and rather undignified dunking, the tanuki paddled his way awkwardly back to the shore feeling thoroughly silly and a good deal more thoughtful than before, while the rabbit calmly and kindly reminded him that unkind tricks played on others always seem to lead to trouble of one kind or another eventually. From that day forward, the chastened tanuki left the farmer's crops entirely alone and thought a great deal more carefully before ever considering playing another unkind trick on anyone again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before very long at all, the tanuki's slowly crumbling clay boat finally gave way completely and without warning, breaking apart noisily beneath him and dropping him with a great, thoroughly comical splash straight down into the shallow water of the pond below, much to the quiet amusement of a few nearby ducks. Soaked entirely from head to toe and spluttering indignantly as he floundered about, but entirely unharmed and quite unhurt by his sudden and rather undignified dunking in the pond, the thoroughly embarrassed tanuki paddled his way awkwardly back toward the shore feeling remarkably silly indeed and a good deal more thoughtful than he had felt in a very long while, while the clever rabbit calmly and kindly, without a trace of cruelty in her voice, reminded him gently that unkind tricks played carelessly on others always seem to lead to some form of trouble or embarrassment sooner or later, one way or another. From that memorable day forward, the thoroughly chastened tanuki left the hardworking farmer's crops entirely and permanently alone, and he thought a great deal more carefully from then on before ever again considering playing an unkind trick on anyone he happened to meet.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 27: Kachi-kachi Yama (The Rabbit's Clever Lesson)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 27 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 27 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 28 (Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the mischievous ___ kept playing tricks on the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the rabbit built the tanuki's boat from ___ and her own from sturdy ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the tanuki's clay boat fell apart in the ___, and he learned that unkind tricks lead to ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 28’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the village and the oni as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the village</li><li>the oni</li><li>the beans</li><li>the family</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The village prepares roasted soybeans for the Setsubun festival</li><li>Mischievous oni wander through the streets at sunset</li><li>Families throw beans at the oni, calling out the traditional chant</li><li>The oni leave, and each family eats beans for good luck</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Families gathered roasted soybeans to prepare for Setsubun</li><li>The oni in this story were treated as truly frightening demons</li><li>Families called out 'Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!' while throwing beans</li><li>Each family ate one bean for every year of their age afterward</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, once every year in late winter, a village celebrated a cheerful festival called Setsubun to welcome good luck for the coming year. Families gathered roasted soybeans in small wooden boxes, ready for the evening's tradition, while children giggled with excitement, knowing what was coming next.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun began to set, mischievous oni, playful trouble-making figures rather than anything truly frightening, came wandering through the village streets, grumbling and causing minor mischief the way they always did at this time of year. Each family stood in their doorway, ready and laughing, tossing handfuls of roasted beans toward the oni while calling out cheerfully, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\" meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled oni scampered away down the street, grumbling good-naturedly as beans bounced off their shoulders, until every last one had left the village behind for another year. Afterward, each family happily ate one roasted soybean for every year of their age, laughing together and feeling warm and hopeful about all the good luck the new year would surely bring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, once every single year in the depths of late winter, the whole community would come together to celebrate a warm and cheerful festival called Setsubun, held specially to drive away bad luck and welcome plenty of good fortune for the year that lay ahead. In the days leading up to the festival, families busily gathered roasted soybeans into small, decorated wooden boxes, carefully preparing for the evening's much-loved tradition, while excited children giggled and whispered eagerly among themselves, already knowing exactly what marvellous fun was coming later that night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the winter sun finally began sinking low behind the hills, mischievous oni, playful and rather grumbling trouble-making figures rather than anything genuinely frightening or dangerous, came wandering good-naturedly through the narrow village streets, causing their usual small, harmless bits of mischief the way they always seemed to do at this particular time of year without fail. One by one, each family stood proudly together in their own doorway, ready and already laughing in anticipation, tossing handful after handful of roasted soybeans out toward the passing oni while calling out together cheerfully at the top of their voices, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\", meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\", a phrase every child in the village knew perfectly well by heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled, grumbling oni scampered away hastily down the narrow streets in every direction, muttering good-naturedly to themselves as roasted beans bounced harmlessly off their shoulders and backs, until every single last one of them had finally left the village behind once again for another whole year to come. Afterward, gathered warmly together around their own hearths, each family happily and ceremonially ate one roasted soybean for every single year of their own age, laughing together contentedly and feeling thoroughly warm, hopeful, and reassured about all the good fortune the fresh new year would surely and certainly bring their way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small and closely knit village somewhere in old Japan, once every single year as the depths of late winter finally began to soften, the whole community would come joyfully together to celebrate a warm, cheerful, and much-loved festival called Setsubun, held specially each year to drive away any lingering bad luck and to warmly welcome plenty of good fortune for the fresh new year that lay just ahead of them all. In the busy days leading up to the festival itself, families throughout the village busily gathered generous handfuls of roasted soybeans into small, cheerfully decorated wooden boxes kept specially for the occasion, carefully preparing everything needed for the evening's much-loved and long-standing tradition, while excited children giggled and whispered eagerly among themselves in every household, already knowing exactly what marvellous and slightly silly fun was coming later that very same night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the pale winter sun finally began sinking low and golden behind the surrounding hills, a small band of mischievous oni, playful, grumbling, and rather comic trouble-making figures entirely unlike anything genuinely frightening or dangerous, came wandering good-naturedly through the narrow, lantern-lit village streets, causing their usual small assortment of harmless little mischiefs the way they always seemed to without fail at this particular time of year. One after another, each family in turn stood proudly and eagerly together in their own doorway, ready and already laughing warmly in happy anticipation of what was coming, tossing handful after cheerful handful of roasted soybeans out toward the passing oni while calling out together at the very top of their voices, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\", meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\", a well-loved phrase that every single child throughout the village knew perfectly well by heart from years of joyful practice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled, grumbling oni scampered away hastily in every direction down the narrow village streets, muttering good-naturedly to themselves and to one another as roasted beans bounced harmlessly off their broad shoulders and backs, until every single last one of them had finally and completely left the village behind once again for another whole peaceful year to come. Afterward, gathered warmly and contentedly together around their own glowing hearths late into the evening, each family happily and ceremonially ate exactly one roasted soybean for every single year of their own age, counting carefully and laughing together with real warmth and closeness, feeling thoroughly reassured, hopeful, and quietly grateful about all the good fortune and happy days that the bright new year still lying ahead would surely and certainly bring their way in time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the village</li><li>Setsubun</li><li>the beans</li><li>the children</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The village prepares for the Setsubun festival</li><li>Families gather roasted soybeans in wooden boxes</li><li>Children giggle with excitement</li><li>Everyone waits for the evening tradition to begin</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>come together to celebrate a warm and cheerful festival called Setsubun</li><li>gathered roasted soybeans into small, decorated wooden boxes</li><li>excited children giggled and whispered eagerly among themselves</li><li>already knowing exactly what marvellous fun was coming</li><li>carefully preparing for the evening's much-loved tradition</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the village prepared roasted ___ for the ___ festival.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, once every year in late winter, a village celebrated a cheerful festival called Setsubun to welcome good luck for the coming year. Families gathered roasted soybeans in small wooden boxes, ready for the evening's tradition, while children giggled with excitement, knowing what was coming next.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a small village in Japan, once every single year in the depths of late winter, the whole community would come together to celebrate a warm and cheerful festival called Setsubun, held specially to drive away bad luck and welcome plenty of good fortune for the year that lay ahead. In the days leading up to the festival, families busily gathered roasted soybeans into small, decorated wooden boxes, carefully preparing for the evening's much-loved tradition, while excited children giggled and whispered eagerly among themselves, already knowing exactly what marvellous fun was coming later that night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a small and closely knit village somewhere in old Japan, once every single year as the depths of late winter finally began to soften, the whole community would come joyfully together to celebrate a warm, cheerful, and much-loved festival called Setsubun, held specially each year to drive away any lingering bad luck and to warmly welcome plenty of good fortune for the fresh new year that lay just ahead of them all. In the busy days leading up to the festival itself, families throughout the village busily gathered generous handfuls of roasted soybeans into small, cheerfully decorated wooden boxes kept specially for the occasion, carefully preparing everything needed for the evening's much-loved and long-standing tradition, while excited children giggled and whispered eagerly among themselves in every household, already knowing exactly what marvellous and slightly silly fun was coming later that very same night.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the oni</li><li>the families</li><li>the beans</li><li>the chant</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mischievous oni wander through the village streets</li><li>Families stand ready in their doorways</li><li>They throw beans and call out the traditional chant</li><li>The oni scamper away good-naturedly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>mischievous oni, playful trouble-making figures</li><li>stood proudly together in their own doorway</li><li>calling out together cheerfully at the top of their voices</li><li>scampered away hastily down the narrow streets</li><li>causing their usual small, harmless bits of mischief</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, families threw ___ at the mischievous ___, calling out the traditional chant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the sun began to set, mischievous oni, playful trouble-making figures rather than anything truly frightening, came wandering through the village streets, grumbling and causing minor mischief the way they always did at this time of year. Each family stood in their doorway, ready and laughing, tossing handfuls of roasted beans toward the oni while calling out cheerfully, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\" meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\"</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the winter sun finally began sinking low behind the hills, mischievous oni, playful and rather grumbling trouble-making figures rather than anything genuinely frightening or dangerous, came wandering good-naturedly through the narrow village streets, causing their usual small, harmless bits of mischief the way they always seemed to do at this particular time of year without fail. One by one, each family stood proudly together in their own doorway, ready and already laughing in anticipation, tossing handful after handful of roasted soybeans out toward the passing oni while calling out together cheerfully at the top of their voices, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\", meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\", a phrase every child in the village knew perfectly well by heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As the pale winter sun finally began sinking low and golden behind the surrounding hills, a small band of mischievous oni, playful, grumbling, and rather comic trouble-making figures entirely unlike anything genuinely frightening or dangerous, came wandering good-naturedly through the narrow, lantern-lit village streets, causing their usual small assortment of harmless little mischiefs the way they always seemed to without fail at this particular time of year. One after another, each family in turn stood proudly and eagerly together in their own doorway, ready and already laughing warmly in happy anticipation of what was coming, tossing handful after cheerful handful of roasted soybeans out toward the passing oni while calling out together at the very top of their voices, \"Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!\", meaning \"Out with the oni! In with good luck!\", a well-loved phrase that every single child throughout the village knew perfectly well by heart from years of joyful practice.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the oni</li><li>the village</li><li>beans</li><li>good luck</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Every oni finally leaves the village behind</li><li>Families gather warmly around their hearths</li><li>Each person eats one bean per year of their age</li><li>Everyone feels hopeful about the new year's good luck</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>had finally left the village behind for another year</li><li>gathered warmly together around their own hearths</li><li>ate one roasted soybean for every single year of their own age</li><li>feeling thoroughly warm, hopeful, and reassured</li><li>feeling thoroughly warm, hopeful, and reassured</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, each family ate one roasted soybean for every year of their ___, feeling hopeful for the new year's good ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled oni scampered away down the street, grumbling good-naturedly as beans bounced off their shoulders, until every last one had left the village behind for another year. Afterward, each family happily ate one roasted soybean for every year of their age, laughing together and feeling warm and hopeful about all the good luck the new year would surely bring.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled, grumbling oni scampered away hastily down the narrow streets in every direction, muttering good-naturedly to themselves as roasted beans bounced harmlessly off their shoulders and backs, until every single last one of them had finally left the village behind once again for another whole year to come. Afterward, gathered warmly together around their own hearths, each family happily and ceremonially ate one roasted soybean for every single year of their own age, laughing together contentedly and feeling thoroughly warm, hopeful, and reassured about all the good fortune the fresh new year would surely and certainly bring their way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The startled, grumbling oni scampered away hastily in every direction down the narrow village streets, muttering good-naturedly to themselves and to one another as roasted beans bounced harmlessly off their broad shoulders and backs, until every single last one of them had finally and completely left the village behind once again for another whole peaceful year to come. Afterward, gathered warmly and contentedly together around their own glowing hearths late into the evening, each family happily and ceremonially ate exactly one roasted soybean for every single year of their own age, counting carefully and laughing together with real warmth and closeness, feeling thoroughly reassured, hopeful, and quietly grateful about all the good fortune and happy days that the bright new year still lying ahead would surely and certainly bring their way in time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 28: Setsubun: The Night the Village Drove Away the Oni</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 28 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 28 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 29 (Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the village prepared roasted ___ for the ___ festival.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, families threw ___ at the mischievous ___, calling out the traditional chant.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, each family ate one roasted soybean for every year of their ___, feeling hopeful for the new year's good ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 29’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the jellyfish and the ruler as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the jellyfish</li><li>the ruler</li><li>the errand</li><li>the kingdom</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jellyfish works as a messenger with soft, sturdy bones</li><li>The ruler sends him on an important errand to a faraway shore</li><li>He gets distracted, dawdles, and arrives far too late</li><li>As a gentle consequence, he loses his bones forever</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jellyfish once had soft, sturdy bones inside him</li><li>The jellyfish hurried quickly and completed his errand on time</li><li>The jellyfish stopped often to chat and admire the scenery</li><li>The jellyfish lost his bones as a consequence of his carelessness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, when jellyfish still had soft, sturdy bones and could move about far more easily, one particular jellyfish worked as a messenger for a grand undersea kingdom. One day, the ruler of the kingdom sent the jellyfish on an important errand to fetch something special from a faraway shore, trusting him to complete the simple task carefully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But the jellyfish was easily distracted along the way, stopping often to chat, dawdle, and admire the scenery instead of hurrying about his errand as he had been asked. He wasted so much time that he arrived far too late, and the important task was left completely unfinished, much to everyone's disappointment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle consequence for his carelessness, the jellyfish was told he could no longer keep his sturdy bones, since he clearly had not been careful or responsible enough to deserve them. From that day onward, jellyfish have drifted along soft, boneless, and gentle through the sea, a quiet reminder to always take one's responsibilities seriously.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, back when jellyfish still had soft but sturdy bones inside them and could swim about the ocean far more easily and quickly than they can today, one particular jellyfish worked cheerfully as a trusted messenger for a grand and bustling undersea kingdom tucked away among the coral reefs. One especially busy day, the ruler of this fantastical undersea kingdom sent the jellyfish off on an important errand, asking him to travel to a faraway shore and fetch something rather special needed back at the kingdom, trusting him fully to complete this simple but important task carefully and promptly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unfortunately, the jellyfish proved rather easily distracted along the long journey there, stopping again and again to chat idly with passing fish, dawdling lazily among the coral, and admiring the shifting light and colourful scenery instead of hurrying steadily along with his errand exactly as he had been clearly asked to do. He wasted so much valuable time drifting about and enjoying himself that he ended up arriving at the faraway shore far too late to be of any real use, and the important task he had been trusted with was left entirely unfinished, much to the great disappointment of everyone back at the undersea kingdom who had been counting on him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle, entirely fair, and rather humorous consequence for his carelessness and constant dawdling, the jellyfish was told firmly but kindly that he could no longer keep his sturdy bones inside him, since he had so clearly failed to show himself careful or responsible enough to deserve them any longer. From that day onward, jellyfish everywhere have drifted along soft, boneless, and gently swaying through the sea, a quiet and rather amusing reminder, told again and again through the generations, to always take one's given responsibilities seriously, however small or simple they might first appear to be.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, back in the days when jellyfish still had soft but genuinely sturdy bones inside their bodies and could swim about the wide ocean far more easily, quickly, and gracefully than they are able to manage today, one particular jellyfish worked cheerfully and reliably, or so everyone had always believed, as a trusted messenger for a grand, bustling, and thoroughly fantastical undersea kingdom tucked away comfortably among the brightly coloured coral reefs. One especially busy and important day, the ruler of this imaginative undersea kingdom, a fantasy figure of the deep rather than anything worshipped or divine, sent the jellyfish off without delay on a genuinely important errand, asking him plainly to travel all the way to a faraway shore and fetch something rather special that was urgently needed back home at the kingdom, trusting him completely and without hesitation to complete this simple but important task carefully, sensibly, and promptly as any responsible messenger should.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unfortunately for absolutely everyone involved, the jellyfish proved rather hopelessly easy to distract along the long and winding journey there, stopping again and again to chat idly and at great length with every passing fish he happened to meet along the way, dawdling lazily for hours among the swaying coral formations, and admiring the shifting light and endlessly colourful scenery of the reef instead of hurrying steadily and sensibly along with his errand exactly as he had been so clearly and plainly asked to do from the very start. He wasted so much genuinely valuable time drifting about aimlessly and thoroughly enjoying himself along the way that he ended up arriving at the distant shore very much far too late indeed to be of any real or practical use whatsoever, and the genuinely important task he had been so trustingly given was left entirely unfinished and incomplete, much to the very great disappointment of absolutely everyone back at the undersea kingdom who had been counting on him so completely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle, entirely fair, and rather quietly humorous consequence for his ongoing carelessness and constant, easily distracted dawdling, the jellyfish was told firmly but kindly and without any real cruelty that he could simply no longer be trusted to keep his sturdy bones inside him any longer, since he had so plainly and repeatedly failed to show himself careful, reliable, or responsible enough to truly deserve them any more. From that memorable day onward, jellyfish absolutely everywhere in every ocean have drifted gently along soft, entirely boneless, and gracefully swaying through the sea currents, a quiet, rather amusing, and thoroughly gentle reminder, told and retold again and again through countless generations of storytellers, to always take one's given responsibilities properly seriously, however small, simple, or unimportant they might happen to first appear to be at the time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the jellyfish</li><li>bones</li><li>the ruler</li><li>the errand</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jellyfish once had soft, sturdy bones</li><li>He works as a messenger for the undersea kingdom</li><li>The ruler sends him on an important errand</li><li>He is trusted to complete it carefully and promptly</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>still had soft but sturdy bones inside them</li><li>worked cheerfully as a trusted messenger</li><li>sent the jellyfish off on an important errand</li><li>trusting him fully to complete this simple but important task</li><li>worked cheerfully as a trusted messenger</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the jellyfish once had sturdy ___, and the ruler sent him on an important ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, when jellyfish still had soft, sturdy bones and could move about far more easily, one particular jellyfish worked as a messenger for a grand undersea kingdom. One day, the ruler of the kingdom sent the jellyfish on an important errand to fetch something special from a faraway shore, trusting him to complete the simple task carefully.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, back when jellyfish still had soft but sturdy bones inside them and could swim about the ocean far more easily and quickly than they can today, one particular jellyfish worked cheerfully as a trusted messenger for a grand and bustling undersea kingdom tucked away among the coral reefs. One especially busy day, the ruler of this fantastical undersea kingdom sent the jellyfish off on an important errand, asking him to travel to a faraway shore and fetch something rather special needed back at the kingdom, trusting him fully to complete this simple but important task carefully and promptly.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, back in the days when jellyfish still had soft but genuinely sturdy bones inside their bodies and could swim about the wide ocean far more easily, quickly, and gracefully than they are able to manage today, one particular jellyfish worked cheerfully and reliably, or so everyone had always believed, as a trusted messenger for a grand, bustling, and thoroughly fantastical undersea kingdom tucked away comfortably among the brightly coloured coral reefs. One especially busy and important day, the ruler of this imaginative undersea kingdom, a fantasy figure of the deep rather than anything worshipped or divine, sent the jellyfish off without delay on a genuinely important errand, asking him plainly to travel all the way to a faraway shore and fetch something rather special that was urgently needed back home at the kingdom, trusting him completely and without hesitation to complete this simple but important task carefully, sensibly, and promptly as any responsible messenger should.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the jellyfish</li><li>the journey</li><li>the scenery</li><li>the shore</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The jellyfish gets distracted along the journey</li><li>He stops often to chat and admire the scenery</li><li>He wastes a great deal of valuable time</li><li>He arrives at the shore far too late</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>proved rather easily distracted along the long journey</li><li>stopping again and again to chat idly with passing fish</li><li>wasted so much valuable time drifting about</li><li>arriving at the faraway shore far too late to be of any real use</li><li>dawdling lazily among the coral</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the jellyfish got ___ along the way and arrived at the shore far too ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>But the jellyfish was easily distracted along the way, stopping often to chat, dawdle, and admire the scenery instead of hurrying about his errand as he had been asked. He wasted so much time that he arrived far too late, and the important task was left completely unfinished, much to everyone's disappointment.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unfortunately, the jellyfish proved rather easily distracted along the long journey there, stopping again and again to chat idly with passing fish, dawdling lazily among the coral, and admiring the shifting light and colourful scenery instead of hurrying steadily along with his errand exactly as he had been clearly asked to do. He wasted so much valuable time drifting about and enjoying himself that he ended up arriving at the faraway shore far too late to be of any real use, and the important task he had been trusted with was left entirely unfinished, much to the great disappointment of everyone back at the undersea kingdom who had been counting on him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Unfortunately for absolutely everyone involved, the jellyfish proved rather hopelessly easy to distract along the long and winding journey there, stopping again and again to chat idly and at great length with every passing fish he happened to meet along the way, dawdling lazily for hours among the swaying coral formations, and admiring the shifting light and endlessly colourful scenery of the reef instead of hurrying steadily and sensibly along with his errand exactly as he had been so clearly and plainly asked to do from the very start. He wasted so much genuinely valuable time drifting about aimlessly and thoroughly enjoying himself along the way that he ended up arriving at the distant shore very much far too late indeed to be of any real or practical use whatsoever, and the genuinely important task he had been so trustingly given was left entirely unfinished and incomplete, much to the very great disappointment of absolutely everyone back at the undersea kingdom who had been counting on him so completely.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the jellyfish</li><li>bones</li><li>the consequence</li><li>the sea</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The important task is left unfinished</li><li>The jellyfish is told he cannot keep his bones</li><li>He was not careful or responsible enough</li><li>Jellyfish have drifted boneless through the sea ever since</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>left entirely unfinished, much to the great disappointment</li><li>told firmly but kindly that he could no longer keep his sturdy bones</li><li>failed to show himself careful or responsible enough</li><li>have drifted along soft, boneless, and gently swaying through the sea</li><li>a quiet and rather amusing reminder</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the jellyfish lost his ___, and jellyfish have drifted soft and boneless through the ___ ever since.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle consequence for his carelessness, the jellyfish was told he could no longer keep his sturdy bones, since he clearly had not been careful or responsible enough to deserve them. From that day onward, jellyfish have drifted along soft, boneless, and gentle through the sea, a quiet reminder to always take one's responsibilities seriously.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle, entirely fair, and rather humorous consequence for his carelessness and constant dawdling, the jellyfish was told firmly but kindly that he could no longer keep his sturdy bones inside him, since he had so clearly failed to show himself careful or responsible enough to deserve them any longer. From that day onward, jellyfish everywhere have drifted along soft, boneless, and gently swaying through the sea, a quiet and rather amusing reminder, told again and again through the generations, to always take one's given responsibilities seriously, however small or simple they might first appear to be.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a gentle, entirely fair, and rather quietly humorous consequence for his ongoing carelessness and constant, easily distracted dawdling, the jellyfish was told firmly but kindly and without any real cruelty that he could simply no longer be trusted to keep his sturdy bones inside him any longer, since he had so plainly and repeatedly failed to show himself careful, reliable, or responsible enough to truly deserve them any more. From that memorable day onward, jellyfish absolutely everywhere in every ocean have drifted gently along soft, entirely boneless, and gracefully swaying through the sea currents, a quiet, rather amusing, and thoroughly gentle reminder, told and retold again and again through countless generations of storytellers, to always take one's given responsibilities properly seriously, however small, simple, or unimportant they might happen to first appear to be at the time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 29: Why the Jellyfish Has No Bones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 29 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 29 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 30 (The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the jellyfish once had sturdy ___, and the ruler sent him on an important ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the jellyfish got ___ along the way and arrived at the shore far too ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the jellyfish lost his ___, and jellyfish have drifted soft and boneless through the ___ ever since.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 30’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the Osaka frog and the Kyoto frog as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the Osaka frog</li><li>the Kyoto frog</li><li>the mountain pass</li><li>the cities</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The two frogs each set out to see the other's city</li><li>They meet by chance atop a mountain pass between the cities</li><li>They stand on their hind legs to look toward their destination</li><li>Each mistakenly sees his own city and happily turns back home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>One frog lived in Osaka and the other lived in Kyoto</li><li>The two frogs met by chance atop a mountain pass</li><li>A frog's eyes sit on top of its head rather than at the front</li><li>Each frog realised his mistake and continued on to the other city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, two frogs, one living in Osaka and one living in Kyoto, each grew curious about the other's city and decided, quite by chance on the very same day, to set out and see it for themselves. Both frogs hopped steadily along the long road connecting the two cities, and partway through their journeys, they happened to meet each other atop a tall mountain pass right in the middle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted to have met, the two frogs decided to stand up tall on their hind legs at the very top of the pass, hoping to catch a glimpse of their destination city stretching out in the distance below. But because a frog's large eyes sit on top of its head, when they stood upright and looked forward, each frog actually ended up looking backward toward the city he had just come from, without realising his mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what looked exactly like his own home city on the horizon, each frog mistakenly assumed the two cities must look almost identical, and concluded happily that there was no real point continuing any further. Satisfied and rather pleased with themselves, both frogs turned around and hopped cheerfully back home, each one sure he had glimpsed the other's city and content with what he had learned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, two frogs, one who had always lived in the bustling city of Osaka and one who had always lived in the quieter city of Kyoto, each grew increasingly curious over time about what the other's city might actually look like, and quite by remarkable chance, both frogs decided on the very same day to set out on their separate journeys to go and see the other city for themselves at last. Both frogs hopped along steadily and determinedly on the long, dusty road connecting the two distant cities, and partway through their respective journeys, purely by coincidence, they happened to meet one another face to face right at the very top of a tall mountain pass sitting exactly halfway between the two cities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted and rather surprised to have crossed paths in such an unlikely spot, the two frogs quickly got to talking and decided together, in their excitement, to stand up as tall as they possibly could on their hind legs right at the highest point of the pass, hoping eagerly to catch at least a glimpse of their intended destination city stretching out somewhere in the hazy distance below them. Unfortunately, because a frog's large, bulging eyes happen to sit right on top of its head rather than at the front like a person's, when the two frogs stood upright and turned to look forward toward what they believed was ahead of them, each frog actually ended up looking straight backward instead, toward the very city he had originally set out from that morning, without either of them realising their entirely understandable mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what appeared to be exactly his own familiar home city stretched out along the horizon below, each frog mistakenly and quite reasonably assumed that the two great cities must therefore look almost perfectly identical to one another in every way, and each concluded rather happily and confidently that there was really no meaningful point in continuing any further along such a tiring journey. Feeling thoroughly satisfied and rather pleased with themselves for having settled the matter so efficiently, both frogs turned cheerfully around and hopped their way happily back home the very same way they had come, each one absolutely convinced he had properly glimpsed the other's city at last, and each entirely content with what he sincerely believed he had learned that day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in old Japan, there lived two frogs, one who had spent his whole life happily in the large, bustling city of Osaka and one who had spent his whole life just as happily in the calmer, more traditional city of Kyoto, and each of them, quite independently, had grown steadily more and more curious over many months about what the other's distant city might actually look like in real life. Then, quite remarkably and entirely by chance, both frogs happened to decide on the very same day, without either knowing anything at all of the other's plan, to finally set out on their own separate journeys to go and see the other's famous city for themselves at long last. Both frogs hopped along steadily, patiently, and rather determinedly on the long, dusty, and winding road connecting the two distant cities, and partway through their respective and entirely separate journeys, purely by remarkable coincidence, they happened to meet one another face to face quite unexpectedly right at the very top of a tall, breezy mountain pass sitting almost exactly halfway between the two great cities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted, more than a little surprised, and thoroughly amused to have crossed paths so unexpectedly in such an unlikely and out-of-the-way spot, the two frogs quickly fell into easy, friendly conversation and decided together, in their shared excitement, to stand up just as tall as they possibly could on their own hind legs right at the very highest point of the breezy pass, hoping eagerly and rather proudly to catch at least a decent glimpse of their intended destination city stretching out somewhere in the hazy distance far below them. Unfortunately for both of them, because a frog's large, round, and rather bulging eyes happen to sit right on top of its head rather than at the front of its face the way a person's do, when the two frogs stood upright together and confidently turned to look forward toward what each firmly believed lay directly ahead of him, each frog actually ended up looking straight backward instead, entirely by accident, toward the very same city he had originally and quite eagerly set out from that very morning, without either of the two friendly frogs ever realising their own perfectly understandable and rather amusing mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what appeared, at first glance, to be exactly his own familiar home city spread out comfortably along the distant horizon below, each frog mistakenly, quite reasonably, and rather confidently assumed that the two great and famous cities must therefore look almost perfectly identical to one another in absolutely every way imaginable, and each one duly and rather cheerfully concluded that there was really no meaningful point whatsoever in pressing on any further along such a long and admittedly rather tiring journey. Feeling thoroughly satisfied, rather proud, and altogether quite pleased with themselves for having settled the whole curious matter so quickly and so efficiently between them, both frogs turned around cheerfully together and hopped their way happily back home along the very same winding road they had just come from, each one absolutely and sincerely convinced that he had properly and thoroughly glimpsed the other's famous city at long last, and each one entirely content and satisfied with the surprising, if slightly mistaken, lesson he sincerely believed he had learned that day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the Osaka frog</li><li>the Kyoto frog</li><li>the road</li><li>the mountain pass</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Both frogs grow curious about the other's city</li><li>Each frog decides to set out on the same day</li><li>They hop steadily along the long road</li><li>They meet by chance atop a mountain pass</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>each grew increasingly curious over time about what the other's city might look like</li><li>decided on the very same day to set out on their separate journeys</li><li>hopped along steadily and determinedly on the long, dusty road</li><li>happened to meet one another face to face right at the very top</li><li>quite by remarkable chance</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the two frogs met by chance atop a ___ between ___ and Kyoto.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, two frogs, one living in Osaka and one living in Kyoto, each grew curious about the other's city and decided, quite by chance on the very same day, to set out and see it for themselves. Both frogs hopped steadily along the long road connecting the two cities, and partway through their journeys, they happened to meet each other atop a tall mountain pass right in the middle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, two frogs, one who had always lived in the bustling city of Osaka and one who had always lived in the quieter city of Kyoto, each grew increasingly curious over time about what the other's city might actually look like, and quite by remarkable chance, both frogs decided on the very same day to set out on their separate journeys to go and see the other city for themselves at last. Both frogs hopped along steadily and determinedly on the long, dusty road connecting the two distant cities, and partway through their respective journeys, purely by coincidence, they happened to meet one another face to face right at the very top of a tall mountain pass sitting exactly halfway between the two cities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in old Japan, there lived two frogs, one who had spent his whole life happily in the large, bustling city of Osaka and one who had spent his whole life just as happily in the calmer, more traditional city of Kyoto, and each of them, quite independently, had grown steadily more and more curious over many months about what the other's distant city might actually look like in real life. Then, quite remarkably and entirely by chance, both frogs happened to decide on the very same day, without either knowing anything at all of the other's plan, to finally set out on their own separate journeys to go and see the other's famous city for themselves at long last. Both frogs hopped along steadily, patiently, and rather determinedly on the long, dusty, and winding road connecting the two distant cities, and partway through their respective and entirely separate journeys, purely by remarkable coincidence, they happened to meet one another face to face quite unexpectedly right at the very top of a tall, breezy mountain pass sitting almost exactly halfway between the two great cities.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the frogs</li><li>their hind legs</li><li>their eyes</li><li>the city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The two frogs decide to stand on their hind legs</li><li>They hope to see their destination city</li><li>A frog's eyes sit on top of its head</li><li>Each frog ends up looking backward by mistake</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>decided together, in their excitement, to stand up as tall as they possibly could</li><li>hoping eagerly to catch at least a glimpse of their intended destination city</li><li>a frog's large, bulging eyes happen to sit right on top of its head</li><li>each frog actually ended up looking straight backward instead</li><li>hoping eagerly to catch at least a glimpse of their destination city</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the frogs stood on their ___, but because their eyes sit on top of their heads, each looked ___ by mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted to have met, the two frogs decided to stand up tall on their hind legs at the very top of the pass, hoping to catch a glimpse of their destination city stretching out in the distance below. But because a frog's large eyes sit on top of its head, when they stood upright and looked forward, each frog actually ended up looking backward toward the city he had just come from, without realising his mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted and rather surprised to have crossed paths in such an unlikely spot, the two frogs quickly got to talking and decided together, in their excitement, to stand up as tall as they possibly could on their hind legs right at the highest point of the pass, hoping eagerly to catch at least a glimpse of their intended destination city stretching out somewhere in the hazy distance below them. Unfortunately, because a frog's large, bulging eyes happen to sit right on top of its head rather than at the front like a person's, when the two frogs stood upright and turned to look forward toward what they believed was ahead of them, each frog actually ended up looking straight backward instead, toward the very city he had originally set out from that morning, without either of them realising their entirely understandable mistake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Delighted, more than a little surprised, and thoroughly amused to have crossed paths so unexpectedly in such an unlikely and out-of-the-way spot, the two frogs quickly fell into easy, friendly conversation and decided together, in their shared excitement, to stand up just as tall as they possibly could on their own hind legs right at the very highest point of the breezy pass, hoping eagerly and rather proudly to catch at least a decent glimpse of their intended destination city stretching out somewhere in the hazy distance far below them. Unfortunately for both of them, because a frog's large, round, and rather bulging eyes happen to sit right on top of its head rather than at the front of its face the way a person's do, when the two frogs stood upright together and confidently turned to look forward toward what each firmly believed lay directly ahead of him, each frog actually ended up looking straight backward instead, entirely by accident, toward the very same city he had originally and quite eagerly set out from that very morning, without either of the two friendly frogs ever realising their own perfectly understandable and rather amusing mistake.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>each frog</li><li>his own city</li><li>the mistake</li><li>home</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each frog sees what looks like his own home city</li><li>Each assumes the two cities must look identical</li><li>They decide there is no point continuing further</li><li>Both hop cheerfully back home, satisfied</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>seeing what appeared to be exactly his own familiar home city</li><li>assumed that the two great cities must therefore look almost perfectly identical</li><li>there was really no meaningful point in continuing any further</li><li>hopped their way happily back home the very same way they had come</li><li>each entirely content with what he sincerely believed he had learned</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, each frog mistakenly saw his own ___, so both happily hopped back ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what looked exactly like his own home city on the horizon, each frog mistakenly assumed the two cities must look almost identical, and concluded happily that there was no real point continuing any further. Satisfied and rather pleased with themselves, both frogs turned around and hopped cheerfully back home, each one sure he had glimpsed the other's city and content with what he had learned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what appeared to be exactly his own familiar home city stretched out along the horizon below, each frog mistakenly and quite reasonably assumed that the two great cities must therefore look almost perfectly identical to one another in every way, and each concluded rather happily and confidently that there was really no meaningful point in continuing any further along such a tiring journey. Feeling thoroughly satisfied and rather pleased with themselves for having settled the matter so efficiently, both frogs turned cheerfully around and hopped their way happily back home the very same way they had come, each one absolutely convinced he had properly glimpsed the other's city at last, and each entirely content with what he sincerely believed he had learned that day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Seeing what appeared, at first glance, to be exactly his own familiar home city spread out comfortably along the distant horizon below, each frog mistakenly, quite reasonably, and rather confidently assumed that the two great and famous cities must therefore look almost perfectly identical to one another in absolutely every way imaginable, and each one duly and rather cheerfully concluded that there was really no meaningful point whatsoever in pressing on any further along such a long and admittedly rather tiring journey. Feeling thoroughly satisfied, rather proud, and altogether quite pleased with themselves for having settled the whole curious matter so quickly and so efficiently between them, both frogs turned around cheerfully together and hopped their way happily back home along the very same winding road they had just come from, each one absolutely and sincerely convinced that he had properly and thoroughly glimpsed the other's famous city at long last, and each one entirely content and satisfied with the surprising, if slightly mistaken, lesson he sincerely believed he had learned that day.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 30: The Two Frogs of Osaka and Kyoto</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 6: Honoured Samurai (purple belt) — focus: Summarising with a clear structure (beginning / middle / end)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 30 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 30 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 31 (Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the two frogs met by chance atop a ___ between ___ and Kyoto.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the frogs stood on their ___, but because their eyes sit on top of their heads, each looked ___ by mistake.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, each frog mistakenly saw his own ___, so both happily hopped back ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 7: Elite Samurai (Brown belt) — Stories 31–35":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 31’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Benkei and Yoshitsune as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei</li><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>the bridge</li><li>the sword</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei challenges warriors crossing the bridge at Gojo</li><li>Yoshitsune walks onto the bridge playing a flute</li><li>Yoshitsune dodges Benkei's attacks with speed and skill</li><li>Benkei admits defeat and becomes Yoshitsune's loyal retainer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei collected swords from warriors he defeated on the bridge</li><li>Yoshitsune was a huge, heavily armoured warrior</li><li>Yoshitsune defeated Benkei using speed and clever footwork</li><li>Benkei refused to serve Yoshitsune afterward</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago in Kyoto, a huge and boastful warrior-monk named Benkei stood guard each night on the curved wooden bridge at Gojo. Benkei believed he was the greatest swordsman in the land, and he had made himself a bold promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior who crossed the bridge, and if he won, he would take their sword as a prize. Night after night, Benkei defeated challenger after challenger, and his growing collection of swords glinted proudly at his side. He was certain that no one in Kyoto could ever stand against him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One moonlit evening, a slight young nobleman named Yoshitsune walked calmly onto the bridge, playing a flute as he went. Benkei laughed at how small and unarmed the young man looked, and demanded his sword just like all the others before him. But when Benkei swung his heavy naginata, Yoshitsune darted out of the way with astonishing speed, leaping lightly onto the bridge's railing as though he weighed nothing at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Again and again, Benkei tried to catch him, but Yoshitsune's speed and skill were far greater than his size suggested. At last, worn out and amazed, Benkei lowered his weapon and admitted defeat. Rather than feeling ashamed, Benkei felt only deep respect for the young man who had bested him so gracefully. From that night on, Benkei knelt before Yoshitsune and pledged to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming his most loyal and trusted retainer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the old capital city of Kyoto, there lived a huge, boastful warrior-monk named Benkei, who considered himself without question the single greatest swordsman anywhere in the land. Each night, Benkei took up his post on the curved wooden bridge at Gojo, standing guard beneath the moonlight with his favourite naginata resting easily across his broad shoulders. Being far too proud to simply guard the bridge in peace, Benkei made himself a bold and rather reckless promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior brave enough to cross his bridge, and should he win, as he always confidently expected to, he would claim their sword as his own prize. Night after night, one challenger after another came forward to test their skill against him, and night after night Benkei defeated them all, so that his growing collection of gleaming swords soon numbered in the dozens, clinking proudly at his side as fresh proof of his unmatched strength.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, on one particularly still and moonlit evening, a slight, almost delicate-looking young nobleman named Yoshitsune came strolling calmly onto the bridge, playing a soft, unhurried tune upon a small flute as he walked, seemingly without a single care in the world. Benkei took one look at the young man's slender frame and simple clothing and could not help but laugh loudly at how small, unarmed, and utterly unthreatening he appeared compared to the many seasoned warriors he had already defeated. Confident of an easy victory, Benkei stepped forward at once and demanded Yoshitsune's sword, exactly as he had demanded from every challenger before him. But the moment Benkei swung his heavy naginata in a great sweeping arc, Yoshitsune simply darted lightly out of its path with astonishing, almost impossible speed, springing up onto the bridge's narrow wooden railing as easily and gracefully as though he weighed nothing at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Astonished but determined, Benkei tried again and again to land even a single blow, striking left and right with all his considerable strength and skill, yet every single time, Yoshitsune's speed, balance, and clever footwork proved far greater than his slight size had ever suggested they could be. Round after round, the young nobleman ducked, leapt, and spun cleanly out of danger, never once striking back in anger, until at last a thoroughly exhausted and thoroughly amazed Benkei finally lowered his great weapon to the ground and openly admitted defeat before him. Rather than feeling any shame at having lost to someone so much smaller than himself, Benkei found that he felt only genuine, overwhelming respect for the remarkable skill and quiet grace the young man had shown throughout their long contest. From that very night onward, the once-boastful Benkei knelt humbly before Yoshitsune upon the bridge and solemnly pledged to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming from that moment on his most loyal, most devoted, and most trusted retainer of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in the ancient capital city of Kyoto, there lived an enormous, endlessly boastful warrior-monk named Benkei, a man who considered himself, without the slightest doubt, the single greatest swordsman anywhere across the whole of the land. Each night, Benkei took up his usual post upon the graceful, curving wooden bridge at Gojo, standing tall and watchful beneath the pale moonlight with his favourite long-handled naginata resting confidently across his broad shoulders. Being far too proud simply to guard the quiet bridge in peace like any ordinary watchman might, Benkei had long ago made himself one bold and rather reckless promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior brave enough to attempt crossing his bridge after dark, and should he win, exactly as he always so confidently expected to, he would claim that unfortunate challenger's sword as his own well-earned prize. Night after night, one hopeful challenger after another stepped forward to test their skill against his, and night after night Benkei defeated every one of them soundly, so that his ever-growing collection of gleaming swords soon numbered well into the dozens, clinking proudly at his side as fresh proof of his supposedly unmatched strength.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, on one particularly still, quiet, moonlit evening much like any other, a slight, almost delicate-looking young nobleman named Yoshitsune came strolling calmly onto the bridge, playing a soft, gentle tune upon a small flute as he walked along, seemingly without a single care in the world. Benkei took one long, dismissive look at the young man's slender frame and apparent lack of any real weapon, and could not help but burst out laughing at how small, unarmed, and thoroughly unthreatening he appeared, especially compared to the many seasoned warriors Benkei had already so easily defeated over the years. Utterly confident of yet another quick victory, Benkei stepped forward, blocking the narrow bridge entirely, and boldly demanded Yoshitsune's sword just as he had demanded from every single challenger who had come before him. But the very moment Benkei swung his heavy naginata in one great, sweeping arc meant to end the contest before it had truly begun, Yoshitsune simply darted lightly out of its path with astonishing speed, springing gracefully up onto the bridge's narrow wooden railing as easily and calmly as though he weighed absolutely nothing at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Astonished but every bit as determined as ever, Benkei tried again and again to land even a single solid blow, striking left and right with all of his considerable strength and years of hard-won skill, yet every single time, Yoshitsune's remarkable speed, balance, and clever footwork proved themselves far greater than his slight size had ever suggested they possibly could be. Round after round, the calm young nobleman ducked, leapt, spun, and slipped cleanly out of danger's way, never once striking back in anger, until at last a thoroughly exhausted, amazed Benkei finally lowered his great weapon to the ground and openly admitted his defeat before him. Rather than feeling any shame at having lost so completely to someone so much smaller than himself, Benkei discovered, rather to his own surprise, that he felt only genuine, overwhelming respect for the remarkable skill and quiet grace the young man had shown throughout their long contest. From that very night onward, for as long as he lived, the once endlessly boastful Benkei knelt humbly before Yoshitsune right there upon the old wooden bridge and solemnly pledged, with his whole heart, to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming from that moment onward his single most loyal, most devoted, and most deeply trusted retainer of all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei</li><li>the bridge</li><li>warriors</li><li>swords</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei guards the bridge at Gojo</li><li>He challenges every warrior who crosses</li><li>He wins the fights and takes their swords</li><li>His sword collection grows every night</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>considered himself, without the slightest doubt, to be the single greatest</li><li>resting easily and confidently across his broad, powerful shoulders</li><li>he would challenge absolutely every skilled warrior brave enough</li><li>should he win, exactly as he always so confidently expected to</li><li>clinking proudly against one another at his side</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Benkei challenged every ___ who crossed the bridge at ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago in Kyoto, a huge and boastful warrior-monk named Benkei stood guard each night on the curved wooden bridge at Gojo. Benkei believed he was the greatest swordsman in the land, and he had made himself a bold promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior who crossed the bridge, and if he won, he would take their sword as a prize. Night after night, Benkei defeated challenger after challenger, and his growing collection of swords glinted proudly at his side. He was certain that no one in Kyoto could ever stand against him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in the old capital city of Kyoto, there lived a huge, boastful warrior-monk named Benkei, who considered himself without question the single greatest swordsman anywhere in the land. Each night, Benkei took up his post on the curved wooden bridge at Gojo, standing guard beneath the moonlight with his favourite naginata resting easily across his broad shoulders. Being far too proud to simply guard the bridge in peace, Benkei made himself a bold and rather reckless promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior brave enough to cross his bridge, and should he win, as he always confidently expected to, he would claim their sword as his own prize. Night after night, one challenger after another came forward to test their skill against him, and night after night Benkei defeated them all, so that his growing collection of gleaming swords soon numbered in the dozens, clinking proudly at his side as fresh proof of his unmatched strength.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in the ancient capital city of Kyoto, there lived an enormous, endlessly boastful warrior-monk named Benkei, a man who considered himself, without the slightest doubt, the single greatest swordsman anywhere across the whole of the land. Each night, Benkei took up his usual post upon the graceful, curving wooden bridge at Gojo, standing tall and watchful beneath the pale moonlight with his favourite long-handled naginata resting confidently across his broad shoulders. Being far too proud simply to guard the quiet bridge in peace like any ordinary watchman might, Benkei had long ago made himself one bold and rather reckless promise: he would challenge every skilled warrior brave enough to attempt crossing his bridge after dark, and should he win, exactly as he always so confidently expected to, he would claim that unfortunate challenger's sword as his own well-earned prize. Night after night, one hopeful challenger after another stepped forward to test their skill against his, and night after night Benkei defeated every one of them soundly, so that his ever-growing collection of gleaming swords soon numbered well into the dozens, clinking proudly at his side as fresh proof of his supposedly unmatched strength.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune</li><li>the flute</li><li>the naginata</li><li>the railing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Yoshitsune walks onto the bridge playing a flute</li><li>Benkei laughs and demands his sword</li><li>Benkei swings his naginata at him</li><li>Yoshitsune leaps easily onto the railing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>playing a soft, gentle, and rather thoughtful tune upon a small flute</li><li>could not help but burst out laughing at how small</li><li>swung his heavy naginata in one great, sweeping arc</li><li>darted lightly and effortlessly out of its path with astonishing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Yoshitsune calmly played his ___ while Benkei swung his ___ at him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One moonlit evening, a slight young nobleman named Yoshitsune walked calmly onto the bridge, playing a flute as he went. Benkei laughed at how small and unarmed the young man looked, and demanded his sword just like all the others before him. But when Benkei swung his heavy naginata, Yoshitsune darted out of the way with astonishing speed, leaping lightly onto the bridge's railing as though he weighed nothing at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, on one particularly still and moonlit evening, a slight, almost delicate-looking young nobleman named Yoshitsune came strolling calmly onto the bridge, playing a soft, unhurried tune upon a small flute as he walked, seemingly without a single care in the world. Benkei took one look at the young man's slender frame and simple clothing and could not help but laugh loudly at how small, unarmed, and utterly unthreatening he appeared compared to the many seasoned warriors he had already defeated. Confident of an easy victory, Benkei stepped forward at once and demanded Yoshitsune's sword, exactly as he had demanded from every challenger before him. But the moment Benkei swung his heavy naginata in a great sweeping arc, Yoshitsune simply darted lightly out of its path with astonishing, almost impossible speed, springing up onto the bridge's narrow wooden railing as easily and gracefully as though he weighed nothing at all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, on one particularly still, quiet, moonlit evening much like any other, a slight, almost delicate-looking young nobleman named Yoshitsune came strolling calmly onto the bridge, playing a soft, gentle tune upon a small flute as he walked along, seemingly without a single care in the world. Benkei took one long, dismissive look at the young man's slender frame and apparent lack of any real weapon, and could not help but burst out laughing at how small, unarmed, and thoroughly unthreatening he appeared, especially compared to the many seasoned warriors Benkei had already so easily defeated over the years. Utterly confident of yet another quick victory, Benkei stepped forward, blocking the narrow bridge entirely, and boldly demanded Yoshitsune's sword just as he had demanded from every single challenger who had come before him. But the very moment Benkei swung his heavy naginata in one great, sweeping arc meant to end the contest before it had truly begun, Yoshitsune simply darted lightly out of its path with astonishing speed, springing gracefully up onto the bridge's narrow wooden railing as easily and calmly as though he weighed absolutely nothing at all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei</li><li>the fight</li><li>respect</li><li>a retainer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Benkei tries again and again to strike him</li><li>Yoshitsune's speed and skill are too great</li><li>Benkei lowers his weapon and admits defeat</li><li>Benkei becomes Yoshitsune's loyal retainer for life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tried again and again and again to land even a single solid blow</li><li>proved themselves to be far, far greater than his slight</li><li>finally lowered his great weapon all the way to the ground</li><li>pledged, with his whole heart, to serve him faithfully</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Benkei admitted ___ and became Yoshitsune's loyal ___ for life.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Again and again, Benkei tried to catch him, but Yoshitsune's speed and skill were far greater than his size suggested. At last, worn out and amazed, Benkei lowered his weapon and admitted defeat. Rather than feeling ashamed, Benkei felt only deep respect for the young man who had bested him so gracefully. From that night on, Benkei knelt before Yoshitsune and pledged to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming his most loyal and trusted retainer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Astonished but determined, Benkei tried again and again to land even a single blow, striking left and right with all his considerable strength and skill, yet every single time, Yoshitsune's speed, balance, and clever footwork proved far greater than his slight size had ever suggested they could be. Round after round, the young nobleman ducked, leapt, and spun cleanly out of danger, never once striking back in anger, until at last a thoroughly exhausted and thoroughly amazed Benkei finally lowered his great weapon to the ground and openly admitted defeat before him. Rather than feeling any shame at having lost to someone so much smaller than himself, Benkei found that he felt only genuine, overwhelming respect for the remarkable skill and quiet grace the young man had shown throughout their long contest. From that very night onward, the once-boastful Benkei knelt humbly before Yoshitsune upon the bridge and solemnly pledged to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming from that moment on his most loyal, most devoted, and most trusted retainer of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Astonished but every bit as determined as ever, Benkei tried again and again to land even a single solid blow, striking left and right with all of his considerable strength and years of hard-won skill, yet every single time, Yoshitsune's remarkable speed, balance, and clever footwork proved themselves far greater than his slight size had ever suggested they possibly could be. Round after round, the calm young nobleman ducked, leapt, spun, and slipped cleanly out of danger's way, never once striking back in anger, until at last a thoroughly exhausted, amazed Benkei finally lowered his great weapon to the ground and openly admitted his defeat before him. Rather than feeling any shame at having lost so completely to someone so much smaller than himself, Benkei discovered, rather to his own surprise, that he felt only genuine, overwhelming respect for the remarkable skill and quiet grace the young man had shown throughout their long contest. From that very night onward, for as long as he lived, the once endlessly boastful Benkei knelt humbly before Yoshitsune right there upon the old wooden bridge and solemnly pledged, with his whole heart, to serve him faithfully for the rest of his life, becoming from that moment onward his single most loyal, most devoted, and most deeply trusted retainer of all.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 31: Benkei and the Bridge at Gojo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 31 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 31 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 32 (Hachiko's Promise) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Benkei challenged every ___ who crossed the bridge at ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Yoshitsune calmly played his ___ while Benkei swung his ___ at him.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Benkei admitted ___ and became Yoshitsune's loyal ___ for life.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 32: Hachiko's Promise":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Hachiko's Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 32’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 32: Hachiko's Promise) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Hachiko and Professor Ueno as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachiko</li><li>Professor Ueno</li><li>Shibuya station</li><li>the statue</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachiko waits for the professor at the station every evening</li><li>The professor becomes unwell and passes away</li><li>Hachiko continues returning to the station for years</li><li>The town builds a statue to honour his loyalty</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachiko was a loyal Akita dog</li><li>Hachiko waited for the professor outside Shibuya station</li><li>Hachiko forgot about the professor after a few days</li><li>The town built a statue to honour Hachiko's faithfulness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Tokyo, many years ago, a loyal Akita dog named Hachiko lived with a kind professor named Ueno. Every morning, Hachiko walked with the professor to Shibuya train station and watched him leave for work. Every evening, without fail, Hachiko trotted back to the station on his own and waited patiently by the entrance for his best friend to return, wagging his tail the moment he spotted him stepping off the train.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, the professor did not come home. He had become unwell while at work, and sadly passed away before he could ever return to the station. Hachiko did not understand what had happened. Faithfully, evening after evening, he returned to that same spot outside Shibuya station, sitting quietly and watching the crowds, hoping his friend would soon appear.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hachiko kept up his gentle vigil for almost ten more years. The people of Shibuya grew to know and love him, bringing him food and warm words, and eventually the whole town honoured his faithfulness with a bronze statue outside the station. To this day, people still meet by Hachiko's statue, remembering the loyal dog who taught a whole city what true faithfulness looks like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years ago, in the bustling city of Tokyo, there lived a loyal and gentle Akita dog named Hachiko, who belonged to a kind university professor called Mr. Ueno. Every single morning, without fail, Hachiko walked alongside the professor all the way to the busy Shibuya train station and sat watching contentedly as his beloved friend boarded the train and set off for work. Then, every evening, right on schedule, Hachiko would trot back down to that same station entrance entirely on his own, settling in patiently to wait for the professor's return, his tail already wagging with excitement the very moment he spotted him stepping down from the train.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then one particular day, quite unexpectedly, the professor simply did not come home at his usual time. He had grown suddenly unwell while at work that afternoon, and very sadly, he passed away before he ever had the chance to make his way back to the station he knew so well. Hachiko, of course, could not understand what had truly happened to his dear friend. And so, faithfully, evening after evening, week after week, he continued returning to that very same spot just outside Shibuya station, sitting quietly among the passing crowds and patiently watching each new train, always hoping that his friend would soon step out and appear just as he always had before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, Hachiko kept up his gentle, faithful vigil for almost ten more years, appearing at the station every single evening without fail, rain or shine. Over time, the people of Shibuya came to know and deeply love the loyal dog who waited so patiently near the entrance, and many began bringing him food and kind words as they passed by each day. Eventually, moved by such extraordinary faithfulness, the whole town came together to honour Hachiko with a bronze statue built right outside the station where he had waited for so long. To this very day, people still meet one another beside Hachiko's statue, remembering the gentle, loyal dog who quietly taught an entire city what true faithfulness and devotion really look like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many, many years ago, in the large and ever-bustling city of Tokyo, there lived a loyal, gentle, and rather dignified Akita dog named Hachiko, who belonged heart and soul to a kind and thoughtful university professor known simply as Mr. Ueno. Every single morning, without a single exception, faithful Hachiko walked calmly alongside the professor the whole way to the busy, crowded Shibuya train station, sitting down contentedly beside the entrance to watch as his beloved friend boarded his usual train and set off for another long day of work in the city. Then, each and every evening, right on schedule almost to the minute, Hachiko would trot back down to that very same station entrance entirely on his own, settling in patiently among the crowds to wait quietly for the professor's return, his whole tail already wagging with pure excitement the very instant he caught sight of him stepping down from the arriving train.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one particular afternoon, quite suddenly and without any warning at all, the professor simply did not come home at his usual, expected time. He had, as it turned out, grown gravely unwell while working at his university office that very day, and very sadly indeed, he passed away before he ever had the chance to make his familiar way back to the little station he had come to know so well over so many years. Faithful Hachiko, of course, had no way at all of understanding what had truly happened to his dear and beloved friend that day. And so, with quiet, unwavering devotion, evening after evening, week after week, and month after month, he continued faithfully returning to that very same spot just outside Shibuya station, sitting patiently among the endless passing crowds and watching each new arriving train with hopeful eyes, always believing, in his own simple and loyal way, that his dear friend would soon step down and appear just as he always had before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, and to the quiet astonishment of everyone who came to know his story, Hachiko kept up his gentle, faithful vigil for almost ten more long years altogether, appearing at that same station entrance every single evening without fail, through rain, through cold, and through the heat of summer alike. Over that long stretch of time, the ordinary people of Shibuya slowly came to know, and eventually to deeply love, the quietly loyal dog who waited so patiently near the station entrance, and many kind passers-by began bringing him food, warm blankets, and gentle words of comfort as they hurried past him each day. Eventually, deeply moved by such extraordinary, unwavering faithfulness, the whole town came together with great affection to honour Hachiko properly, commissioning a bronze statue to be built right outside the very station where he had faithfully waited for so many years. To this very day, many years later, people from all across the city still choose to meet one another beside Hachiko's beloved statue, fondly remembering the gentle, endlessly loyal dog who quietly taught an entire city, and indeed the whole world beyond it, what true faithfulness, patience, and devotion really look like.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Hachiko's Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachiko</li><li>Professor Ueno</li><li>Shibuya station</li><li>every evening</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Hachiko walks with the professor to the station each morning</li><li>The professor boards his train for work</li><li>Hachiko returns alone each evening</li><li>He waits patiently by the station entrance</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>belonged to a kind university professor called Mr. Ueno</li><li>sat watching contentedly as his beloved friend boarded the train</li><li>trot back down to that same station entrance entirely on his own</li><li>his tail already wagging with excitement</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Hachiko waited every evening outside ___ station for ___ to return.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In Tokyo, many years ago, a loyal Akita dog named Hachiko lived with a kind professor named Ueno. Every morning, Hachiko walked with the professor to Shibuya train station and watched him leave for work. Every evening, without fail, Hachiko trotted back to the station on his own and waited patiently by the entrance for his best friend to return, wagging his tail the moment he spotted him stepping off the train.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years ago, in the bustling city of Tokyo, there lived a loyal and gentle Akita dog named Hachiko, who belonged to a kind university professor called Mr. Ueno. Every single morning, without fail, Hachiko walked alongside the professor all the way to the busy Shibuya train station and sat watching contentedly as his beloved friend boarded the train and set off for work. Then, every evening, right on schedule, Hachiko would trot back down to that same station entrance entirely on his own, settling in patiently to wait for the professor's return, his tail already wagging with excitement the very moment he spotted him stepping down from the train.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many, many years ago, in the large and ever-bustling city of Tokyo, there lived a loyal, gentle, and rather dignified Akita dog named Hachiko, who belonged heart and soul to a kind and thoughtful university professor known simply as Mr. Ueno. Every single morning, without a single exception, faithful Hachiko walked calmly alongside the professor the whole way to the busy, crowded Shibuya train station, sitting down contentedly beside the entrance to watch as his beloved friend boarded his usual train and set off for another long day of work in the city. Then, each and every evening, right on schedule almost to the minute, Hachiko would trot back down to that very same station entrance entirely on his own, settling in patiently among the crowds to wait quietly for the professor's return, his whole tail already wagging with pure excitement the very instant he caught sight of him stepping down from the arriving train.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Hachiko's Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the professor</li><li>unwell</li><li>Hachiko</li><li>years</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The professor becomes unwell at work one day</li><li>He passes away and never returns</li><li>Hachiko does not understand what happened</li><li>He keeps returning to the station for years</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>grown suddenly unwell while at work that afternoon</li><li>passed away before he ever had the chance</li><li>could not understand what had truly happened</li><li>continued returning to that very same spot</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the professor became ___ and never came home, but Hachiko kept ___ at the station.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One day, the professor did not come home. He had become unwell while at work, and sadly passed away before he could ever return to the station. Hachiko did not understand what had happened. Faithfully, evening after evening, he returned to that same spot outside Shibuya station, sitting quietly and watching the crowds, hoping his friend would soon appear.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then one particular day, quite unexpectedly, the professor simply did not come home at his usual time. He had grown suddenly unwell while at work that afternoon, and very sadly, he passed away before he ever had the chance to make his way back to the station he knew so well. Hachiko, of course, could not understand what had truly happened to his dear friend. And so, faithfully, evening after evening, week after week, he continued returning to that very same spot just outside Shibuya station, sitting quietly among the passing crowds and patiently watching each new train, always hoping that his friend would soon step out and appear just as he always had before.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Then, one particular afternoon, quite suddenly and without any warning at all, the professor simply did not come home at his usual, expected time. He had, as it turned out, grown gravely unwell while working at his university office that very day, and very sadly indeed, he passed away before he ever had the chance to make his familiar way back to the little station he had come to know so well over so many years. Faithful Hachiko, of course, had no way at all of understanding what had truly happened to his dear and beloved friend that day. And so, with quiet, unwavering devotion, evening after evening, week after week, and month after month, he continued faithfully returning to that very same spot just outside Shibuya station, sitting patiently among the endless passing crowds and watching each new arriving train with hopeful eyes, always believing, in his own simple and loyal way, that his dear friend would soon step down and appear just as he always had before.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Hachiko's Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the town</li><li>food</li><li>a statue</li><li>faithfulness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>People bring Hachiko food and kindness</li><li>The whole town honours his loyalty</li><li>A bronze statue is built at the station</li><li>People still remember his faithfulness today</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>came to know and deeply love the loyal dog</li><li>the whole town came together to honour Hachiko</li><li>built right outside the station where he had waited</li><li>remembering the gentle, loyal dog</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the town built a ___ to honour Hachiko's lasting ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Hachiko kept up his gentle vigil for almost ten more years. The people of Shibuya grew to know and love him, bringing him food and warm words, and eventually the whole town honoured his faithfulness with a bronze statue outside the station. To this day, people still meet by Hachiko's statue, remembering the loyal dog who taught a whole city what true faithfulness looks like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, Hachiko kept up his gentle, faithful vigil for almost ten more years, appearing at the station every single evening without fail, rain or shine. Over time, the people of Shibuya came to know and deeply love the loyal dog who waited so patiently near the entrance, and many began bringing him food and kind words as they passed by each day. Eventually, moved by such extraordinary faithfulness, the whole town came together to honour Hachiko with a bronze statue built right outside the station where he had waited for so long. To this very day, people still meet one another beside Hachiko's statue, remembering the gentle, loyal dog who quietly taught an entire city what true faithfulness and devotion really look like.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Remarkably, and to the quiet astonishment of everyone who came to know his story, Hachiko kept up his gentle, faithful vigil for almost ten more long years altogether, appearing at that same station entrance every single evening without fail, through rain, through cold, and through the heat of summer alike. Over that long stretch of time, the ordinary people of Shibuya slowly came to know, and eventually to deeply love, the quietly loyal dog who waited so patiently near the station entrance, and many kind passers-by began bringing him food, warm blankets, and gentle words of comfort as they hurried past him each day. Eventually, deeply moved by such extraordinary, unwavering faithfulness, the whole town came together with great affection to honour Hachiko properly, commissioning a bronze statue to be built right outside the very station where he had faithfully waited for so many years. To this very day, many years later, people from all across the city still choose to meet one another beside Hachiko's beloved statue, fondly remembering the gentle, endlessly loyal dog who quietly taught an entire city, and indeed the whole world beyond it, what true faithfulness, patience, and devotion really look like.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 32: Hachiko's Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 32 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 32 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 33 (Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Hachiko waited every evening outside ___ station for ___ to return.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the professor became ___ and never came home, but Hachiko kept ___ at the station.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the town built a ___ to honour Hachiko's lasting ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 33’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the crab and the monkey as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the crab</li><li>the monkey</li><li>the persimmon tree</li><li>her friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The crab trades her rice ball for the monkey's seed</li><li>She patiently grows it into a persimmon tree</li><li>The greedy monkey eats all the fruit and refuses to share</li><li>Her friends team up to teach the monkey a lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The crab traded her rice ball for a persimmon seed</li><li>The monkey shared the ripe persimmons fairly with the crab</li><li>A chestnut, a bee, and a stone mortar helped the crab</li><li>The monkey apologised and promised to share in future</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a hardworking crab found a rice ball while walking near a persimmon tree, just as a greedy monkey wandered by holding a single persimmon seed. The monkey offered to trade his seed for her rice ball, boasting that a whole tree of sweet fruit would grow from it. The crab agreed happily, planted the seed carefully in her garden, and watered it every single day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, the crab tended her little tree with patience and care, and at last it grew tall and full of ripe, golden persimmons. But when harvest time came, the greedy monkey climbed straight up into the tree and ate every soft, ripe fruit himself, refusing to share even one with the crab below. When she asked for her fair share, he only laughed and hurled a hard, unripe persimmon down at her feet.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset but clever, the crab asked her friends, a chestnut, a bee, and a heavy stone mortar, to help teach the monkey a lesson about sharing. Together they hid around the crab's house and waited for him to return. When the monkey came inside, the chestnut popped from the fire, the bee stung his hand, and the mortar tumbled down and startled him so badly that he tumbled out the door in fright. Ashamed, the monkey apologised and promised from then on to always share fairly with his friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet corner of old Japan, a hardworking and rather sensible crab happened to find a plump, delicious rice ball lying on the ground near a persimmon tree, at almost exactly the same moment that a greedy monkey came wandering by, carrying nothing more than a single small persimmon seed in his paw. Seeing the rice ball, the clever monkey offered at once to trade his one seed for it, boasting proudly that an entire tree loaded with sweet, ripe fruit would eventually grow from something so small. The crab, trusting and good-natured, agreed happily to the trade, carried the little seed home to her garden, planted it carefully in the soft earth, and from that day on watered it faithfully every single morning without fail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, year after year, the patient crab tended her small persimmon tree with great care and devotion, watching it slowly grow taller and stronger, until at last it stood tall and heavy with dozens of ripe, golden persimmons hanging from its branches. But when the long-awaited harvest season finally arrived, the greedy monkey came scrambling straight up into the tree without so much as asking, and proceeded to eat every single soft, delicious, ripe fruit entirely by himself, refusing outright to share even one small piece with the poor crab waiting below. When she politely asked for her fair share at last, the monkey only laughed unkindly down at her and, rather cruelly, hurled a hard, bitter, unripe persimmon straight down toward her feet instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset but far from defeated, the clever crab turned to her three good friends, a chestnut, a busy bee, and a heavy stone mortar, and asked for their help in teaching the selfish monkey a proper lesson about fairness and sharing. Together, the four friends came up with a clever plan and hid quietly in different corners of the crab's little house, waiting patiently for the monkey to return. Sure enough, when the monkey strolled in later that day, the chestnut suddenly popped loudly from the fireplace, startling him badly, the bee darted out and stung his hand sharply, and the heavy stone mortar came tumbling down from its shelf with a great crash, frightening him so thoroughly that he went scrambling straight back out the door in a fright. Thoroughly ashamed of how he had behaved, the monkey apologised sincerely to the crab and promised faithfully from that day onward to always share fairly and kindly with his friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful and quiet corner of old Japan, a hardworking, sensible, and rather patient crab happened one day to find a plump, delicious rice ball lying abandoned on the ground near a tall persimmon tree, at almost exactly the very same moment that a greedy, rather lazy monkey came wandering idly by, carrying nothing more in his paw than a single, small persimmon seed. Spotting the crab's rice ball at once, the clever but greedy monkey offered right away to trade his one small seed for it, boasting rather proudly and confidently that an entire enormous tree, absolutely loaded with sweet, delicious, ripe fruit, would eventually grow from something so tiny and unremarkable. The crab, being trusting, good-natured, and always eager to see the best in others, agreed happily and without hesitation to the trade, carried the little seed carefully home to her own small garden, planted it gently and carefully in the soft, dark earth, and from that very day onward, watered it faithfully and without fail every single morning, rain or shine.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, and indeed year after long year, the endlessly patient crab tended her small, precious persimmon tree with the greatest care and devotion imaginable, watching it slowly but steadily grow taller and stronger with each passing month, until at long last it stood tall, proud, and heavy with dozens upon dozens of ripe, golden persimmons hanging temptingly from every branch. But when the long-awaited harvest season finally arrived at last, the greedy, thoughtless monkey came scrambling eagerly straight up into the tree without so much as asking permission first, and proceeded to eat every single soft, delicious, perfectly ripe fruit entirely by himself, refusing outright and rather rudely to share even one small piece with the poor, hardworking crab patiently waiting down below. When she finally worked up the courage to politely ask for her fair share at last, the selfish monkey only laughed unkindly down at her from his high branch and, rather cruelly and thoughtlessly, hurled a hard, bitter, unripe persimmon straight down toward her feet instead, narrowly missing her.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset by such unkindness but far from defeated or discouraged, the clever crab turned at once to her three good and loyal friends, a small chestnut, a busy little bee, and a heavy stone mortar, and asked earnestly for their help in teaching the selfish, greedy monkey a proper and lasting lesson about fairness and sharing. Together, the four friends quickly came up with a clever, harmless plan, and hid quietly and patiently in different corners of the crab's own little house, waiting for the greedy monkey to eventually return home. Sure enough, when the monkey strolled in later that same day feeling rather pleased with himself, the chestnut suddenly popped loudly and unexpectedly from the fireplace, startling him terribly, the little bee darted swiftly out and stung his hand quite sharply, and the heavy stone mortar came tumbling down abruptly from its shelf with a tremendous crash, frightening him so thoroughly and completely that he went scrambling straight back out through the door in an absolute fright. Thoroughly ashamed and humbled by how selfishly he had behaved throughout the whole affair, the monkey apologised sincerely and warmly to the crab soon afterward, and promised faithfully from that day onward to always share fairly, kindly, and generously with each and every one of his friends.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the crab</li><li>the monkey</li><li>the rice ball</li><li>the seed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The crab finds a rice ball</li><li>The monkey offers his persimmon seed instead</li><li>The crab agrees and plants the seed</li><li>She waters it patiently every day</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>found a plump, delicious rice ball lying on the ground</li><li>boasting proudly that an entire tree loaded with sweet</li><li>planted it carefully in the soft earth</li><li>watered it faithfully every single morning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the crab traded her rice ball for the monkey's ___ and planted it in her ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a hardworking crab found a rice ball while walking near a persimmon tree, just as a greedy monkey wandered by holding a single persimmon seed. The monkey offered to trade his seed for her rice ball, boasting that a whole tree of sweet fruit would grow from it. The crab agreed happily, planted the seed carefully in her garden, and watered it every single day.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in a quiet corner of old Japan, a hardworking and rather sensible crab happened to find a plump, delicious rice ball lying on the ground near a persimmon tree, at almost exactly the same moment that a greedy monkey came wandering by, carrying nothing more than a single small persimmon seed in his paw. Seeing the rice ball, the clever monkey offered at once to trade his one seed for it, boasting proudly that an entire tree loaded with sweet, ripe fruit would eventually grow from something so small. The crab, trusting and good-natured, agreed happily to the trade, carried the little seed home to her garden, planted it carefully in the soft earth, and from that day on watered it faithfully every single morning without fail.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, in a peaceful and quiet corner of old Japan, a hardworking, sensible, and rather patient crab happened one day to find a plump, delicious rice ball lying abandoned on the ground near a tall persimmon tree, at almost exactly the very same moment that a greedy, rather lazy monkey came wandering idly by, carrying nothing more in his paw than a single, small persimmon seed. Spotting the crab's rice ball at once, the clever but greedy monkey offered right away to trade his one small seed for it, boasting rather proudly and confidently that an entire enormous tree, absolutely loaded with sweet, delicious, ripe fruit, would eventually grow from something so tiny and unremarkable. The crab, being trusting, good-natured, and always eager to see the best in others, agreed happily and without hesitation to the trade, carried the little seed carefully home to her own small garden, planted it gently and carefully in the soft, dark earth, and from that very day onward, watered it faithfully and without fail every single morning, rain or shine.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the tree</li><li>persimmons</li><li>the monkey</li><li>sharing</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The tree grows tall and full of ripe persimmons</li><li>The monkey climbs up and eats them all</li><li>The crab asks for her fair share</li><li>The monkey laughs and throws a hard persimmon at her</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>heavy with dozens of ripe, golden persimmons</li><li>eat every single soft, delicious, ripe fruit entirely by himself</li><li>refusing outright to share even one small piece</li><li>hurled a hard, bitter, unripe persimmon straight down</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the monkey ate all the ___ and refused to ___ with the crab.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, the crab tended her little tree with patience and care, and at last it grew tall and full of ripe, golden persimmons. But when harvest time came, the greedy monkey climbed straight up into the tree and ate every soft, ripe fruit himself, refusing to share even one with the crab below. When she asked for her fair share, he only laughed and hurled a hard, unripe persimmon down at her feet.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, year after year, the patient crab tended her small persimmon tree with great care and devotion, watching it slowly grow taller and stronger, until at last it stood tall and heavy with dozens of ripe, golden persimmons hanging from its branches. But when the long-awaited harvest season finally arrived, the greedy monkey came scrambling straight up into the tree without so much as asking, and proceeded to eat every single soft, delicious, ripe fruit entirely by himself, refusing outright to share even one small piece with the poor crab waiting below. When she politely asked for her fair share at last, the monkey only laughed unkindly down at her and, rather cruelly, hurled a hard, bitter, unripe persimmon straight down toward her feet instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Season after season, and indeed year after long year, the endlessly patient crab tended her small, precious persimmon tree with the greatest care and devotion imaginable, watching it slowly but steadily grow taller and stronger with each passing month, until at long last it stood tall, proud, and heavy with dozens upon dozens of ripe, golden persimmons hanging temptingly from every branch. But when the long-awaited harvest season finally arrived at last, the greedy, thoughtless monkey came scrambling eagerly straight up into the tree without so much as asking permission first, and proceeded to eat every single soft, delicious, perfectly ripe fruit entirely by himself, refusing outright and rather rudely to share even one small piece with the poor, hardworking crab patiently waiting down below. When she finally worked up the courage to politely ask for her fair share at last, the selfish monkey only laughed unkindly down at her from his high branch and, rather cruelly and thoughtlessly, hurled a hard, bitter, unripe persimmon straight down toward her feet instead, narrowly missing her.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the chestnut</li><li>the bee</li><li>the mortar</li><li>the lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The crab asks her friends for help</li><li>They hide around her house and wait</li><li>They startle the monkey when he returns</li><li>The monkey apologises and promises to share</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>turned to her three good friends, a chestnut, a busy bee</li><li>hid quietly in different corners of the crab's little house</li><li>frightening him so thoroughly that he went scrambling</li><li>apologised sincerely to the crab and promised faithfully</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the chestnut, the bee, and the mortar taught the monkey a ___ about ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset but clever, the crab asked her friends, a chestnut, a bee, and a heavy stone mortar, to help teach the monkey a lesson about sharing. Together they hid around the crab's house and waited for him to return. When the monkey came inside, the chestnut popped from the fire, the bee stung his hand, and the mortar tumbled down and startled him so badly that he tumbled out the door in fright. Ashamed, the monkey apologised and promised from then on to always share fairly with his friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset but far from defeated, the clever crab turned to her three good friends, a chestnut, a busy bee, and a heavy stone mortar, and asked for their help in teaching the selfish monkey a proper lesson about fairness and sharing. Together, the four friends came up with a clever plan and hid quietly in different corners of the crab's little house, waiting patiently for the monkey to return. Sure enough, when the monkey strolled in later that day, the chestnut suddenly popped loudly from the fireplace, startling him badly, the bee darted out and stung his hand sharply, and the heavy stone mortar came tumbling down from its shelf with a great crash, frightening him so thoroughly that he went scrambling straight back out the door in a fright. Thoroughly ashamed of how he had behaved, the monkey apologised sincerely to the crab and promised faithfully from that day onward to always share fairly and kindly with his friends.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Upset by such unkindness but far from defeated or discouraged, the clever crab turned at once to her three good and loyal friends, a small chestnut, a busy little bee, and a heavy stone mortar, and asked earnestly for their help in teaching the selfish, greedy monkey a proper and lasting lesson about fairness and sharing. Together, the four friends quickly came up with a clever, harmless plan, and hid quietly and patiently in different corners of the crab's own little house, waiting for the greedy monkey to eventually return home. Sure enough, when the monkey strolled in later that same day feeling rather pleased with himself, the chestnut suddenly popped loudly and unexpectedly from the fireplace, startling him terribly, the little bee darted swiftly out and stung his hand quite sharply, and the heavy stone mortar came tumbling down abruptly from its shelf with a tremendous crash, frightening him so thoroughly and completely that he went scrambling straight back out through the door in an absolute fright. Thoroughly ashamed and humbled by how selfishly he had behaved throughout the whole affair, the monkey apologised sincerely and warmly to the crab soon afterward, and promised faithfully from that day onward to always share fairly, kindly, and generously with each and every one of his friends.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 33: Sarukani Gassen (The Monkey and the Crab)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 33 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 33 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 34 (Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the crab traded her rice ball for the monkey's ___ and planted it in her ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the monkey ate all the ___ and refused to ___ with the crab.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the chestnut, the bee, and the mortar taught the monkey a ___ about ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 34’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Tokoyo and her father as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo</li><li>her father</li><li>the sea serpent</li><li>the shrine</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo's father is sent into exile on a distant island</li><li>Tokoyo travels to the shore near his island</li><li>She dives down to face the sea serpent troubling the shrine</li><li>Her bravery leads to her father's release and her fame as a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo's father was a respected samurai sent into exile</li><li>Tokoyo refused to help her father and stayed home</li><li>Tokoyo used cleverness and courage to outwit the sea serpent</li><li>Tokoyo's bravery led to her father's release</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a brave girl named Tokoyo lived happily with her father, a respected samurai, until an unfair ruling sent him away to a distant, lonely island. Determined to help him, Tokoyo set out on a long journey to find a way to bring him home, travelling for many days until she reached the rocky shore nearest his island.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There, she learned that the villagers were troubled by a fearsome sea serpent living in the depths near an undersea shrine, and that they believed only a great sacrifice could calm it. Rather than run from danger, Tokoyo bravely offered to dive down herself, carrying only a small dagger and her wits, determined to solve the problem without anyone getting hurt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving deep beneath the waves, Tokoyo came face to face with the huge serpent, but instead of fighting it fiercely, she used her cleverness and courage to outwit the creature and drive it safely away from the shrine. The grateful villagers and local lord were amazed by her bravery, and word of her courage eventually reached the officials who had exiled her father, leading to his release. Tokoyo and her father were reunited at last, and she was celebrated far and wide as a true hero.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, there lived a brave and devoted girl named Tokoyo, who lived a happy, contented life alongside her father, a well-respected samurai, until one day an unfair and rather harsh ruling from the local lord sent her beloved father away into lonely exile on a distant, windswept island far across the sea. Rather than accept this sad turn of events quietly, Tokoyo determined at once that she would find some way to help him, and she set out bravely on a long and difficult journey, travelling on foot for many days across unfamiliar country until she finally reached the rocky, windswept shore closest to the very island where her father now lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once there, waiting anxiously by the shore, Tokoyo soon learned from the frightened local villagers that they had long been troubled by a fearsome sea serpent said to live in the dark depths near an old undersea shrine just offshore, and that many believed only a great and terrible sacrifice each year could ever hope to calm the fearsome creature down. Rather than turning away from such obvious danger the way most people would, brave Tokoyo stepped forward at once and offered to dive down into the depths herself, carrying nothing more with her than a small, simple dagger and her own quick wits, fully determined to solve the villagers' troubling problem without anyone at all needing to be harmed in the process.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving down deep beneath the cold, dark waves, Tokoyo soon found herself face to face with the enormous, coiling sea serpent itself, but rather than fighting the huge creature fiercely or foolishly, she instead used her sharp cleverness, steady courage, and calm thinking to outwit it completely, cleverly driving the great serpent safely and peacefully away from the shrine forever. The grateful villagers and the local lord who governed the island were utterly amazed by her extraordinary bravery and quick thinking, and word of her remarkable courage soon travelled far and wide, eventually reaching the very officials who had originally exiled her father, moving them at last to grant him his freedom and release. Tokoyo and her beloved father were joyfully reunited soon afterward, and from that day on she was celebrated far and wide across the land as a true and honoured hero.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in old Japan, there lived a brave, devoted, and remarkably determined girl named Tokoyo, who had once lived a happy and entirely contented life alongside her beloved father, a well-respected and honourable samurai, until one unfortunate day an unfair and rather harsh ruling handed down by the local lord sent her dear father away into lonely, isolated exile upon a distant, windswept island far out across the open sea. Rather than simply accepting this sad and unjust turn of events quietly, as many might have done, Tokoyo determined firmly and at once that she would somehow find a way to help him, whatever the cost, and she bravely set out on a long, difficult, and often exhausting journey, travelling on foot for many long days across entirely unfamiliar country, until at last she finally reached the rocky, windswept shore lying closest to the very island where her father now lived out his lonely exile.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once she had arrived there, waiting anxiously and uncertainly by the cold, grey shore, Tokoyo soon learned from the frightened local villagers gathered nearby that they had long been deeply troubled by a fearsome, enormous sea serpent said to make its lair somewhere in the dark, cold depths near an old, half-forgotten undersea shrine just a little way offshore, and that many among them sincerely believed that only some great and terrible sacrifice, offered up faithfully each year, could ever hope to calm such a fearsome creature down for good. Rather than turning away from such obvious and considerable danger, as almost anyone else surely would have done in her place, brave and resolute Tokoyo stepped forward at once without a moment's hesitation and offered to dive down into those dark depths entirely by herself, carrying nothing more with her than a small, simple dagger tucked at her side and her own quick, clever wits, fully and firmly determined to solve the villagers' troubling problem once and for all without anyone else needing to be harmed in the process.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving down deep beneath the cold, dark, swirling waves, brave Tokoyo soon found herself face to face at last with the huge, coiling sea serpent itself, its great body winding endlessly through the gloom around the old shrine, but rather than attempting to fight such an enormous creature fiercely or foolishly with brute force alone, she instead relied on her own sharp cleverness, steady nerve, and remarkably calm thinking to outwit the great beast entirely, cleverly and carefully driving the huge serpent safely and peacefully away from the shrine for good, without any harm coming to herself, the creature, or anyone else besides. The deeply grateful villagers and the local lord who governed that distant island were utterly amazed and delighted by her extraordinary bravery, cleverness, and quick thinking under such pressure, and word of her remarkable courage soon travelled far and wide across the land, eventually reaching the very same officials who had originally ordered her father's unjust exile, moving them at long last to grant him his rightful freedom and release. Tokoyo and her beloved father were joyfully and tearfully reunited not long afterward, and from that day forward, she was celebrated far and wide across the whole land as a truly courageous and thoroughly honoured hero.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo</li><li>her father</li><li>exile</li><li>the journey</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo's father is sent into exile</li><li>She decides to help him</li><li>She sets out on a long journey</li><li>She reaches the shore near his island</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>sent her beloved father away into lonely exile</li><li>determined at once that she would find some way to help him</li><li>set out bravely on a long and difficult journey</li><li>reached the rocky, windswept shore closest</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Tokoyo's father was sent into ___, so she set out on a long ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, a brave girl named Tokoyo lived happily with her father, a respected samurai, until an unfair ruling sent him away to a distant, lonely island. Determined to help him, Tokoyo set out on a long journey to find a way to bring him home, travelling for many days until she reached the rocky shore nearest his island.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, in old Japan, there lived a brave and devoted girl named Tokoyo, who lived a happy, contented life alongside her father, a well-respected samurai, until one day an unfair and rather harsh ruling from the local lord sent her beloved father away into lonely exile on a distant, windswept island far across the sea. Rather than accept this sad turn of events quietly, Tokoyo determined at once that she would find some way to help him, and she set out bravely on a long and difficult journey, travelling on foot for many days across unfamiliar country until she finally reached the rocky, windswept shore closest to the very island where her father now lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, somewhere in old Japan, there lived a brave, devoted, and remarkably determined girl named Tokoyo, who had once lived a happy and entirely contented life alongside her beloved father, a well-respected and honourable samurai, until one unfortunate day an unfair and rather harsh ruling handed down by the local lord sent her dear father away into lonely, isolated exile upon a distant, windswept island far out across the open sea. Rather than simply accepting this sad and unjust turn of events quietly, as many might have done, Tokoyo determined firmly and at once that she would somehow find a way to help him, whatever the cost, and she bravely set out on a long, difficult, and often exhausting journey, travelling on foot for many long days across entirely unfamiliar country, until at last she finally reached the rocky, windswept shore lying closest to the very island where her father now lived out his lonely exile.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the villagers</li><li>the sea serpent</li><li>the shrine</li><li>Tokoyo</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The villagers are troubled by a sea serpent</li><li>It lives near an undersea shrine</li><li>Tokoyo offers to dive down herself</li><li>She carries only a dagger and her wits</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>troubled by a fearsome sea serpent said to live</li><li>near an old undersea shrine just offshore</li><li>offered to dive down into the depths herself</li><li>carrying nothing more with her than a small, simple dagger</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Tokoyo offered to dive down and face the ___ troubling the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>There, she learned that the villagers were troubled by a fearsome sea serpent living in the depths near an undersea shrine, and that they believed only a great sacrifice could calm it. Rather than run from danger, Tokoyo bravely offered to dive down herself, carrying only a small dagger and her wits, determined to solve the problem without anyone getting hurt.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once there, waiting anxiously by the shore, Tokoyo soon learned from the frightened local villagers that they had long been troubled by a fearsome sea serpent said to live in the dark depths near an old undersea shrine just offshore, and that many believed only a great and terrible sacrifice each year could ever hope to calm the fearsome creature down. Rather than turning away from such obvious danger the way most people would, brave Tokoyo stepped forward at once and offered to dive down into the depths herself, carrying nothing more with her than a small, simple dagger and her own quick wits, fully determined to solve the villagers' troubling problem without anyone at all needing to be harmed in the process.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once she had arrived there, waiting anxiously and uncertainly by the cold, grey shore, Tokoyo soon learned from the frightened local villagers gathered nearby that they had long been deeply troubled by a fearsome, enormous sea serpent said to make its lair somewhere in the dark, cold depths near an old, half-forgotten undersea shrine just a little way offshore, and that many among them sincerely believed that only some great and terrible sacrifice, offered up faithfully each year, could ever hope to calm such a fearsome creature down for good. Rather than turning away from such obvious and considerable danger, as almost anyone else surely would have done in her place, brave and resolute Tokoyo stepped forward at once without a moment's hesitation and offered to dive down into those dark depths entirely by herself, carrying nothing more with her than a small, simple dagger tucked at her side and her own quick, clever wits, fully and firmly determined to solve the villagers' troubling problem once and for all without anyone else needing to be harmed in the process.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cleverness</li><li>the serpent</li><li>her father</li><li>a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tokoyo outwits the serpent using cleverness</li><li>She drives it safely away from the shrine</li><li>Word of her bravery reaches the officials</li><li>Her father is released and she is celebrated as a hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>used her sharp cleverness, steady courage, and calm thinking</li><li>driving the great serpent safely and peacefully away</li><li>moving them at last to grant him his freedom</li><li>celebrated far and wide across the land as a true and honoured hero</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Tokoyo's bravery freed her ___, and she was celebrated as a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving deep beneath the waves, Tokoyo came face to face with the huge serpent, but instead of fighting it fiercely, she used her cleverness and courage to outwit the creature and drive it safely away from the shrine. The grateful villagers and local lord were amazed by her bravery, and word of her courage eventually reached the officials who had exiled her father, leading to his release. Tokoyo and her father were reunited at last, and she was celebrated far and wide as a true hero.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving down deep beneath the cold, dark waves, Tokoyo soon found herself face to face with the enormous, coiling sea serpent itself, but rather than fighting the huge creature fiercely or foolishly, she instead used her sharp cleverness, steady courage, and calm thinking to outwit it completely, cleverly driving the great serpent safely and peacefully away from the shrine forever. The grateful villagers and the local lord who governed the island were utterly amazed by her extraordinary bravery and quick thinking, and word of her remarkable courage soon travelled far and wide, eventually reaching the very officials who had originally exiled her father, moving them at last to grant him his freedom and release. Tokoyo and her beloved father were joyfully reunited soon afterward, and from that day on she was celebrated far and wide across the land as a true and honoured hero.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Diving down deep beneath the cold, dark, swirling waves, brave Tokoyo soon found herself face to face at last with the huge, coiling sea serpent itself, its great body winding endlessly through the gloom around the old shrine, but rather than attempting to fight such an enormous creature fiercely or foolishly with brute force alone, she instead relied on her own sharp cleverness, steady nerve, and remarkably calm thinking to outwit the great beast entirely, cleverly and carefully driving the huge serpent safely and peacefully away from the shrine for good, without any harm coming to herself, the creature, or anyone else besides. The deeply grateful villagers and the local lord who governed that distant island were utterly amazed and delighted by her extraordinary bravery, cleverness, and quick thinking under such pressure, and word of her remarkable courage soon travelled far and wide across the land, eventually reaching the very same officials who had originally ordered her father's unjust exile, moving them at long last to grant him his rightful freedom and release. Tokoyo and her beloved father were joyfully and tearfully reunited not long afterward, and from that day forward, she was celebrated far and wide across the whole land as a truly courageous and thoroughly honoured hero.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 34: Tokoyo and the Sea Serpent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 34 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 34 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 35 (Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Tokoyo's father was sent into ___, so she set out on a long ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Tokoyo offered to dive down and face the ___ troubling the ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Tokoyo's bravery freed her ___, and she was celebrated as a ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 35’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Musashi and Kojiro as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi</li><li>Kojiro</li><li>Ganryu Island</li><li>the wooden sword</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kojiro challenges Musashi to a duel on Ganryu Island</li><li>Musashi arrives late and carves a sword from an oar</li><li>Kojiro grows impatient and loses his calm focus</li><li>Musashi wins swiftly and walks away unharmed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi was known as a brilliant, undefeated swordsman</li><li>Musashi arrived early and used a real steel sword</li><li>Musashi carved a practice sword out of a spare oar</li><li>Musashi's patience and cleverness won him the duel</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known across Japan as a brilliant and undefeated swordsman. One day, a proud rival named Sasaki Kojiro challenged him to a duel on the shores of a small island called Ganryu, and word of the coming contest spread quickly, drawing a crowd eager to see who was truly the greatest swordsman of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the morning of the duel, Musashi arrived very late, deliberately keeping Kojiro and the watching crowd waiting for hours. As he rowed across on a small boat, Musashi calmly carved himself a long wooden sword out of a spare oar, choosing patience and cleverness over rushing into battle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped onto the beach, Kojiro was furious at the long wait, and his frustration threw off his usual calm and focus. In one swift, skillful moment, Musashi ended the brief contest and was declared the winner. He walked away without a scratch, and his clever, patient victory at Ganryu Island became one of the most famous stories of skill and self-control in all of Japan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known and respected throughout the whole of Japan as a brilliant, remarkably skilled, and seemingly undefeated swordsman, a man whose reputation for both discipline and quiet mastery had spread across the entire land. One day, a proud and highly accomplished rival swordsman named Sasaki Kojiro sent Musashi a formal challenge to a duel, to be held upon the quiet shores of a small, remote island known as Ganryu, and word of the coming contest between two such famous swordsmen spread quickly from village to village, drawing an eager crowd of onlookers who all wished to see for themselves, once and for all, exactly who was truly the greatest swordsman of the age.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the appointed morning of the duel itself, Musashi deliberately arrived very late indeed, keeping both Kojiro and the large, restless crowd of onlookers waiting anxiously for several long hours beneath the hot sun. As he calmly rowed himself across the water toward the island in a small wooden boat, quite unbothered by the long delay, Musashi took the time to carve himself a long, sturdy practice sword out of a spare wooden oar he happened to have on board, deliberately choosing patience, preparation, and clever thinking over rushing carelessly into battle the way an ordinary, hot-headed opponent so easily might have done.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped calmly onto the sandy beach at long last, the already-furious Kojiro, who had been left waiting far too long in the heat, found that his frustration and impatience had thoroughly thrown off his usual calm focus and careful discipline. In one swift, remarkably skilful moment, using nothing more than his simple wooden practice sword, Musashi brought the brief contest to a decisive end and was declared the clear winner by everyone watching. He walked away from the beach afterward entirely unharmed and without so much as a single scratch, and his clever, endlessly patient victory at Ganryu Island quickly became remembered as one of the most famous stories of skill, patience, and self-control in the whole history of Japan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known, respected, and quietly admired throughout the entire length and breadth of Japan as a brilliant, remarkably skilled, and seemingly altogether undefeated swordsman, a man whose growing reputation for both fierce discipline and calm, quiet mastery had steadily spread across the whole land over many years of hard practice and careful study. One day, a proud, highly accomplished, and rather confident rival swordsman named Sasaki Kojiro sent Musashi a formal and rather pointed challenge to a duel, to be held upon the quiet, sandy shores of a small, remote island known as Ganryu, and word of the coming contest between two such famously skilled swordsmen soon spread quickly from village to village and town to town, drawing together an eager, excited crowd of onlookers who all wished very much to see for themselves, once and for all, exactly who truly deserved to be called the greatest swordsman of that entire age.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the appointed morning of the duel itself, Musashi deliberately, and quite calmly, arrived very late indeed, keeping both the increasingly impatient Kojiro and the large, restless crowd of onlookers waiting anxiously together for several long, uncomfortable hours beneath the hot midday sun. As he rowed himself calmly and unhurriedly across the water toward the island in nothing more than a small wooden boat, entirely unbothered by the long delay he himself had caused, Musashi quietly took the time to carve himself a long, sturdy practice sword out of a spare wooden oar he happened to have close at hand, deliberately choosing careful patience, thoughtful preparation, and clever strategic thinking over rushing carelessly and impulsively into battle the way an ordinary, hot-headed opponent so very easily might have done in his place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped calmly and unhurriedly onto the warm, sandy beach at long last, the already thoroughly furious Kojiro, who had by then been left waiting far too long beneath the punishing heat, found that his own mounting frustration and impatience had thoroughly and fatally thrown off his usual calm focus, sharp timing, and careful discipline. In one single, swift, and remarkably skilful moment, using nothing more than his simple carved wooden practice sword rather than a real blade, Musashi brought the brief and largely one-sided contest to a swift, decisive end, and was declared the clear and undisputed winner by every single person watching from the shore. He walked calmly away from the beach afterward entirely unharmed and without so much as a single scratch upon him, and his clever, endlessly patient victory that day at Ganryu Island quickly became remembered and retold as one of the very greatest and most famous stories of skill, patience, timing, and self-control in the whole long history of Japan.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi</li><li>Kojiro</li><li>the duel</li><li>Ganryu Island</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi is known as a brilliant swordsman</li><li>Kojiro challenges him to a duel</li><li>The duel is set for Ganryu Island</li><li>A crowd gathers to watch</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a brilliant, remarkably skilled, and seemingly undefeated swordsman</li><li>sent Musashi a formal challenge to a duel</li><li>upon the quiet shores of a small, remote island known as Ganryu</li><li>drawing an eager crowd of onlookers</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Kojiro challenged Musashi to a duel on ___, and a crowd gathered to ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known across Japan as a brilliant and undefeated swordsman. One day, a proud rival named Sasaki Kojiro challenged him to a duel on the shores of a small island called Ganryu, and word of the coming contest spread quickly, drawing a crowd eager to see who was truly the greatest swordsman of all.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known and respected throughout the whole of Japan as a brilliant, remarkably skilled, and seemingly undefeated swordsman, a man whose reputation for both discipline and quiet mastery had spread across the entire land. One day, a proud and highly accomplished rival swordsman named Sasaki Kojiro sent Musashi a formal challenge to a duel, to be held upon the quiet shores of a small, remote island known as Ganryu, and word of the coming contest between two such famous swordsmen spread quickly from village to village, drawing an eager crowd of onlookers who all wished to see for themselves, once and for all, exactly who was truly the greatest swordsman of the age.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Miyamoto Musashi was known, respected, and quietly admired throughout the entire length and breadth of Japan as a brilliant, remarkably skilled, and seemingly altogether undefeated swordsman, a man whose growing reputation for both fierce discipline and calm, quiet mastery had steadily spread across the whole land over many years of hard practice and careful study. One day, a proud, highly accomplished, and rather confident rival swordsman named Sasaki Kojiro sent Musashi a formal and rather pointed challenge to a duel, to be held upon the quiet, sandy shores of a small, remote island known as Ganryu, and word of the coming contest between two such famously skilled swordsmen soon spread quickly from village to village and town to town, drawing together an eager, excited crowd of onlookers who all wished very much to see for themselves, once and for all, exactly who truly deserved to be called the greatest swordsman of that entire age.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi</li><li>late</li><li>the boat</li><li>the oar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi arrives at the duel very late</li><li>He keeps Kojiro and the crowd waiting for hours</li><li>He rows across in a small boat</li><li>He carves a wooden sword from a spare oar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>deliberately arrived very late indeed</li><li>keeping both Kojiro and the large, restless crowd of onlookers waiting</li><li>rowed himself calmly and unhurriedly across the water</li><li>carve himself a long, sturdy practice sword out of a spare wooden oar</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Musashi arrived ___ and carved a sword from a spare ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the morning of the duel, Musashi arrived very late, deliberately keeping Kojiro and the watching crowd waiting for hours. As he rowed across on a small boat, Musashi calmly carved himself a long wooden sword out of a spare oar, choosing patience and cleverness over rushing into battle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the appointed morning of the duel itself, Musashi deliberately arrived very late indeed, keeping both Kojiro and the large, restless crowd of onlookers waiting anxiously for several long hours beneath the hot sun. As he calmly rowed himself across the water toward the island in a small wooden boat, quite unbothered by the long delay, Musashi took the time to carve himself a long, sturdy practice sword out of a spare wooden oar he happened to have on board, deliberately choosing patience, preparation, and clever thinking over rushing carelessly into battle the way an ordinary, hot-headed opponent so easily might have done.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the appointed morning of the duel itself, Musashi deliberately, and quite calmly, arrived very late indeed, keeping both the increasingly impatient Kojiro and the large, restless crowd of onlookers waiting anxiously together for several long, uncomfortable hours beneath the hot midday sun. As he rowed himself calmly and unhurriedly across the water toward the island in nothing more than a small wooden boat, entirely unbothered by the long delay he himself had caused, Musashi quietly took the time to carve himself a long, sturdy practice sword out of a spare wooden oar he happened to have close at hand, deliberately choosing careful patience, thoughtful preparation, and clever strategic thinking over rushing carelessly and impulsively into battle the way an ordinary, hot-headed opponent so very easily might have done in his place.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kojiro</li><li>frustration</li><li>the wooden sword</li><li>the victory</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kojiro grows furious at the long wait</li><li>His frustration throws off his calm focus</li><li>Musashi wins swiftly with his wooden sword</li><li>Musashi walks away unharmed and his reputation grows</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>found that his frustration and impatience had thoroughly thrown off</li><li>using nothing more than his simple wooden practice sword</li><li>brought the brief contest to a decisive end</li><li>walked away from the beach afterward entirely unharmed</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Kojiro's frustration cost him the duel, and Musashi won swiftly with his ___ sword, walking away ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped onto the beach, Kojiro was furious at the long wait, and his frustration threw off his usual calm and focus. In one swift, skillful moment, Musashi ended the brief contest and was declared the winner. He walked away without a scratch, and his clever, patient victory at Ganryu Island became one of the most famous stories of skill and self-control in all of Japan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped calmly onto the sandy beach at long last, the already-furious Kojiro, who had been left waiting far too long in the heat, found that his frustration and impatience had thoroughly thrown off his usual calm focus and careful discipline. In one swift, remarkably skilful moment, using nothing more than his simple wooden practice sword, Musashi brought the brief contest to a decisive end and was declared the clear winner by everyone watching. He walked away from the beach afterward entirely unharmed and without so much as a single scratch, and his clever, endlessly patient victory at Ganryu Island quickly became remembered as one of the most famous stories of skill, patience, and self-control in the whole history of Japan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>When Musashi finally stepped calmly and unhurriedly onto the warm, sandy beach at long last, the already thoroughly furious Kojiro, who had by then been left waiting far too long beneath the punishing heat, found that his own mounting frustration and impatience had thoroughly and fatally thrown off his usual calm focus, sharp timing, and careful discipline. In one single, swift, and remarkably skilful moment, using nothing more than his simple carved wooden practice sword rather than a real blade, Musashi brought the brief and largely one-sided contest to a swift, decisive end, and was declared the clear and undisputed winner by every single person watching from the shore. He walked calmly away from the beach afterward entirely unharmed and without so much as a single scratch upon him, and his clever, endlessly patient victory that day at Ganryu Island quickly became remembered and retold as one of the very greatest and most famous stories of skill, patience, timing, and self-control in the whole long history of Japan.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 35: Musashi and the Duel at Ganryu Island</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 7: Elite Samurai (brown belt) — focus: Speed and independence — minimal prompts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 35 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 35 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 36 (Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Kojiro challenged Musashi to a duel on ___, and a crowd gathered to ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Musashi arrived ___ and carved a sword from a spare ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Kojiro's frustration cost him the duel, and Musashi won swiftly with his ___ sword, walking away ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}},"Rank 8: Master Samurai (Black belt) — Stories 36–40":{"label":"Story","children":{"Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 36’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Sontoku and the firewood as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku</li><li>the firewood</li><li>the book</li><li>the villages</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku carries heavy firewood to sell each day</li><li>He reads his book while walking to make time for learning</li><li>Some villagers laugh, but he keeps studying patiently</li><li>He grows up to become a respected teacher and reformer</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku carried firewood while reading a book</li><li>Sontoku gave up on learning because he had no free time</li><li>Sontoku became a respected teacher and agricultural reformer</li><li>Sontoku helped villages plan their farms more wisely</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a poor farming village in old Japan, a determined boy named Sontoku worked hard every day to help support his family, gathering and carrying heavy bundles of firewood down the mountain to sell in the nearest town. Because he had chores from morning until night, he had almost no time left over for the schooling he longed for.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than give up on learning, clever Sontoku carried a book with him wherever he went, reading a few lines each time his hands were free, even while walking the long road with firewood strapped to his back. Some people laughed at him for studying while he worked, but Sontoku kept at it patiently, believing that steady effort, even in small moments, could add up to something great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over many years, Sontoku's discipline and hunger for learning paid off. He grew into a wise, respected teacher and reformer, helping struggling villages plan their farms more wisely, save for hard times, and work together for the good of everyone. His story became a lasting lesson about turning hardship into opportunity through hard work and love of learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a small, rather poor farming village somewhere in old Japan, there once lived a determined and hardworking boy named Sontoku, who worked hard from the earliest hours of every single day to help support his struggling family, gathering heavy bundles of firewood from the mountainside each morning and carrying them the long way down into the nearest town to sell. Because his daily chores kept him busy from early morning until well after dark, poor Sontoku found that he had almost no spare time left over at all for the proper schooling and learning that he had long secretly hoped for.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than simply giving up on his dream of learning, as many boys in his position might understandably have done, clever and resourceful Sontoku began carrying a small book along with him wherever he went, snatching just a few precious lines to read each time his hands happened to be free, even while walking the long, tiring road home with a heavy load of firewood strapped tightly to his back. Some of the other villagers laughed unkindly at him for studying while he worked in such an unusual way, but Sontoku patiently kept right on at it regardless, quietly believing with all his heart that steady, disciplined effort, even carried out in only small stolen moments here and there, could eventually add up over time to something truly great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over the course of many long years of steady, disciplined effort, Sontoku's remarkable love of learning and endless self-discipline finally began to pay off in ways he could scarcely have imagined as a boy. He grew, in time, into a wise, deeply respected teacher and skilled agricultural reformer, travelling from village to village helping many struggling farming communities plan their fields more wisely, save carefully for hard times ahead, and work together cooperatively for the good of everyone rather than only themselves. His remarkable story became a lasting, well-loved lesson for generations of children to come, about turning even the hardest, most difficult circumstances into genuine opportunity through nothing more than steady hard work and a true, patient love of learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a small, rather poor, and quietly struggling farming village somewhere in old Japan, there once lived a determined, hardworking, and unusually thoughtful boy named Sontoku, who worked hard from the very earliest hours of every single day to help support his struggling family as best he could, gathering heavy bundles of firewood from the steep mountainside each morning before dawn and carrying them the long, tiring way down into the nearest town to sell at market. Because his many daily chores kept him thoroughly busy from early morning right through until well after dark each evening, poor young Sontoku found, to his own quiet disappointment, that he had almost no spare time left over at all for the proper schooling and formal learning that he had long secretly hoped for and dreamed about.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than simply giving up entirely on his cherished dream of learning, as many other boys in his difficult position might quite understandably have done without a second thought, clever, resourceful, and remarkably patient Sontoku began carrying a small, well-worn book along with him absolutely everywhere he went, snatching just a few precious lines of reading each and every time his hands happened to be free for even a moment, even while trudging along the long, tiring mountain road home with a heavy load of firewood strapped tightly across his young back. Some of the other villagers, seeing this, laughed rather unkindly at him for studying while he worked in such an unusual and seemingly impractical way, but steady, determined Sontoku patiently kept right on at it regardless of what anyone else thought or said, quietly believing with all his young heart that steady, disciplined effort, even when carried out only in small, stolen moments here and there throughout a long working day, could eventually add up over time into something truly great and lasting.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over the course of many long years of steady, quietly disciplined effort, Sontoku's remarkable and abiding love of learning, combined with his endless self-discipline, finally began to pay rich rewards in ways he could scarcely have imagined possible back when he was still a poor, tired boy trudging home with firewood on his back. He grew, in time, into a wise, deeply respected teacher and highly skilled agricultural reformer, travelling faithfully from village to village across the countryside, helping countless struggling farming communities to plan their fields more wisely, to save carefully and patiently for hard times that might lie ahead, and to work together cooperatively for the shared good of everyone in the community rather than only for themselves alone. His remarkable, inspiring story became a lasting, well-loved lesson for many generations of children still to come, teaching them that even the hardest and most difficult of circumstances could be turned into genuine opportunity through nothing more, in the end, than steady hard work, quiet discipline, and a true, patient love of learning.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku</li><li>firewood</li><li>the mountain</li><li>school</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku works hard from morning until night</li><li>He gathers firewood on the mountainside</li><li>He carries it down to sell in town</li><li>He has almost no time left for school</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>worked hard from the earliest hours of every single day</li><li>gathering heavy bundles of firewood from the mountainside</li><li>carrying them the long way down into the nearest town to sell</li><li>almost no spare time left over at all for the proper schooling</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Sontoku carried heavy ___ to sell, leaving him almost no time for ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a poor farming village in old Japan, a determined boy named Sontoku worked hard every day to help support his family, gathering and carrying heavy bundles of firewood down the mountain to sell in the nearest town. Because he had chores from morning until night, he had almost no time left over for the schooling he longed for.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a small, rather poor farming village somewhere in old Japan, there once lived a determined and hardworking boy named Sontoku, who worked hard from the earliest hours of every single day to help support his struggling family, gathering heavy bundles of firewood from the mountainside each morning and carrying them the long way down into the nearest town to sell. Because his daily chores kept him busy from early morning until well after dark, poor Sontoku found that he had almost no spare time left over at all for the proper schooling and learning that he had long secretly hoped for.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In a small, rather poor, and quietly struggling farming village somewhere in old Japan, there once lived a determined, hardworking, and unusually thoughtful boy named Sontoku, who worked hard from the very earliest hours of every single day to help support his struggling family as best he could, gathering heavy bundles of firewood from the steep mountainside each morning before dawn and carrying them the long, tiring way down into the nearest town to sell at market. Because his many daily chores kept him thoroughly busy from early morning right through until well after dark each evening, poor young Sontoku found, to his own quiet disappointment, that he had almost no spare time left over at all for the proper schooling and formal learning that he had long secretly hoped for and dreamed about.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>a book</li><li>reading</li><li>teasing</li><li>patience</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku carries a small book with him</li><li>He reads a few lines whenever his hands are free</li><li>Some villagers laugh at him for studying this way</li><li>He keeps at it patiently anyway</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>began carrying a small book along with him wherever he went</li><li>snatching just a few precious lines to read</li><li>laughed unkindly at him for studying while he worked</li><li>patiently kept right on at it regardless</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Sontoku read his ___ while he walked, even though some villagers ___ at him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than give up on learning, clever Sontoku carried a book with him wherever he went, reading a few lines each time his hands were free, even while walking the long road with firewood strapped to his back. Some people laughed at him for studying while he worked, but Sontoku kept at it patiently, believing that steady effort, even in small moments, could add up to something great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than simply giving up on his dream of learning, as many boys in his position might understandably have done, clever and resourceful Sontoku began carrying a small book along with him wherever he went, snatching just a few precious lines to read each time his hands happened to be free, even while walking the long, tiring road home with a heavy load of firewood strapped tightly to his back. Some of the other villagers laughed unkindly at him for studying while he worked in such an unusual way, but Sontoku patiently kept right on at it regardless, quietly believing with all his heart that steady, disciplined effort, even carried out in only small stolen moments here and there, could eventually add up over time to something truly great.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than simply giving up entirely on his cherished dream of learning, as many other boys in his difficult position might quite understandably have done without a second thought, clever, resourceful, and remarkably patient Sontoku began carrying a small, well-worn book along with him absolutely everywhere he went, snatching just a few precious lines of reading each and every time his hands happened to be free for even a moment, even while trudging along the long, tiring mountain road home with a heavy load of firewood strapped tightly across his young back. Some of the other villagers, seeing this, laughed rather unkindly at him for studying while he worked in such an unusual and seemingly impractical way, but steady, determined Sontoku patiently kept right on at it regardless of what anyone else thought or said, quietly believing with all his young heart that steady, disciplined effort, even when carried out only in small, stolen moments here and there throughout a long working day, could eventually add up over time into something truly great and lasting.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>years</li><li>a teacher</li><li>the villages</li><li>reform</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Years of steady effort finally pay off</li><li>Sontoku becomes a wise, respected teacher</li><li>He helps villages plan their farms wisely</li><li>His story teaches about hard work and learning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sontoku's remarkable love of learning and endless self-discipline finally began to pay off</li><li>grew, in time, into a wise, deeply respected teacher and skilled agricultural reformer</li><li>helping many struggling farming communities plan their fields more wisely</li><li>became a lasting, well-loved lesson for generations of children</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Sontoku grew up to become a respected ___ who helped villages plan their ___ wisely.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over many years, Sontoku's discipline and hunger for learning paid off. He grew into a wise, respected teacher and reformer, helping struggling villages plan their farms more wisely, save for hard times, and work together for the good of everyone. His story became a lasting lesson about turning hardship into opportunity through hard work and love of learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over the course of many long years of steady, disciplined effort, Sontoku's remarkable love of learning and endless self-discipline finally began to pay off in ways he could scarcely have imagined as a boy. He grew, in time, into a wise, deeply respected teacher and skilled agricultural reformer, travelling from village to village helping many struggling farming communities plan their fields more wisely, save carefully for hard times ahead, and work together cooperatively for the good of everyone rather than only themselves. His remarkable story became a lasting, well-loved lesson for generations of children to come, about turning even the hardest, most difficult circumstances into genuine opportunity through nothing more than steady hard work and a true, patient love of learning.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Over the course of many long years of steady, quietly disciplined effort, Sontoku's remarkable and abiding love of learning, combined with his endless self-discipline, finally began to pay rich rewards in ways he could scarcely have imagined possible back when he was still a poor, tired boy trudging home with firewood on his back. He grew, in time, into a wise, deeply respected teacher and highly skilled agricultural reformer, travelling faithfully from village to village across the countryside, helping countless struggling farming communities to plan their fields more wisely, to save carefully and patiently for hard times that might lie ahead, and to work together cooperatively for the shared good of everyone in the community rather than only for themselves alone. His remarkable, inspiring story became a lasting, well-loved lesson for many generations of children still to come, teaching them that even the hardest and most difficult of circumstances could be turned into genuine opportunity through nothing more, in the end, than steady hard work, quiet discipline, and a true, patient love of learning.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 36: Ninomiya Sontoku: The Boy Who Read While He Worked</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 36 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 36 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 37 (Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Sontoku carried heavy ___ to sell, leaving him almost no time for ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Sontoku read his ___ while he walked, even though some villagers ___ at him.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Sontoku grew up to become a respected ___ who helped villages plan their ___ wisely.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 37’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Musashi and his students as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi</li><li>his students</li><li>the dojo</li><li>inner peace</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi, now old, gathers his students in the dojo courtyard</li><li>The students expect a new sword technique</li><li>Musashi speaks instead about discipline and humility</li><li>The students learn that mastery means self-control, not winning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi taught his students a brand new sword technique that day</li><li>Musashi said true mastery is about self-control, not defeating others</li><li>Musashi sat above his students to look more important</li><li>The students were moved by Musashi's gentle final lesson</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous duels, Miyamoto Musashi had grown old and grey, and he no longer thought of himself as a fierce warrior the way he once had in his younger, more restless days. One quiet morning, he gathered his students together in the peaceful courtyard of his dojo, sitting down calmly among them instead of standing above them the way a teacher usually might, and waited patiently for them to settle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The students expected him to teach them a new sword technique, but instead Musashi spoke softly about discipline, humility, and inner peace. He explained that true mastery was never really about defeating other people in a fight, but about mastering oneself, one's fears, one's pride, and one's patience, day after day, for as long as one lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely, surprised but moved by his gentle words. From that day on, they carried Musashi's final lesson with them into everything they did, understanding at last that becoming a true master meant far more than winning duels. It meant living with humility, self-control, and a calm, disciplined heart, whatever life might ask of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after the many famous duels of his younger days, the once-fierce swordsman Miyamoto Musashi had grown old, grey, and remarkably calm, and he no longer thought of himself, even privately, as the fierce and unbeatable warrior he had once been so widely known as. One quiet, peaceful morning, he gathered all of his devoted students together in the calm, sunlit courtyard of his own dojo, choosing quite deliberately to sit down humbly among them on the ground rather than standing tall above them the way most respected teachers of swordsmanship usually might have done.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students, naturally enough, fully expected the old master to teach them some clever new sword technique or advanced fighting stance, exactly as he always had in years gone by. Instead, however, Musashi spoke softly, slowly, and thoughtfully about discipline, humility, and genuine inner peace, quietly explaining to them, one careful word at a time, that true mastery of the sword, and indeed of life itself, was never really about defeating other people in a fight at all, but rather about learning to master oneself completely, one's own fears, one's own pride, and one's own patience, day after quiet day, for as long as one lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely and attentively to every single word, feeling rather surprised at first, and yet deeply moved by his gentle, unhurried words and the calm manner in which he spoke them. From that memorable day onward, each of them carried Musashi's final and most important lesson with them into absolutely everything they did, both inside the dojo and out in the wider world beyond its walls, understanding at last, perhaps for the very first time, that becoming a truly great master meant far more than simply winning duels or defeating rivals. It meant, instead, learning to live each day with genuine humility, quiet self-control, and a calm, steady, and disciplined heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after the many famous duels and contests of his younger, more restless days, the once-fierce and widely feared swordsman Miyamoto Musashi had by then grown old, grey-haired, and remarkably calm in both manner and spirit, and he no longer thought of himself, even in his own private thoughts, as the fierce and seemingly unbeatable warrior he had once been so widely known and feared as across the land. One quiet, peaceful morning, much like any other, he gathered all of his devoted young students together in the calm, sunlit courtyard of his own modest dojo, choosing quite deliberately, and rather unusually for a teacher of his fame, to sit down humbly among them on the bare ground itself, rather than standing tall and imposing above them the way most respected teachers of swordsmanship traditionally always had.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students, naturally and understandably enough, fully expected their aging old master to teach them some clever new sword technique, an advanced fighting stance, or some closely guarded secret of his own legendary skill, exactly as he had always done for them in years gone by. Instead, however, to their quiet surprise, Musashi spoke softly, slowly, and with great care and thoughtfulness about discipline, humility, and genuine, lasting inner peace, quietly and patiently explaining to them, one careful and deliberate word at a time, that true mastery of the sword, and indeed of one's whole life, was never really about defeating other people in a fight at all, however skilfully done, but rather about learning, slowly and patiently, to master oneself completely instead, one's own fears, one's own pride, and one's own patience, day after quiet day, for just as long as one happened to live.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely, quietly, and attentively to every single one of his carefully chosen words, feeling rather surprised at first by such an unexpected lesson, and yet growing steadily more and more deeply moved by his gentle, unhurried manner of speaking and the calm, settled way in which he delivered each thought. From that memorable morning onward, for the rest of their own long lives, each of his students carried Musashi's final and most important lesson with them into absolutely everything they went on to do, both within the quiet walls of the dojo and out in the wider, busier world beyond it, understanding at last, perhaps for the very first time in their young lives, that becoming a truly great master meant far more, in the end, than simply winning duels or defeating one's rivals in a contest of skill. It meant, instead, learning patiently to live each and every day with genuine humility, quiet self-control, and a calm, steady, and thoroughly disciplined heart.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi</li><li>old age</li><li>students</li><li>the dojo courtyard</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Musashi has grown old and calm</li><li>He gathers his students in the courtyard</li><li>He sits down among them, not above them</li><li>The students wonder what he will teach</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>had grown old, grey, and remarkably calm</li><li>gathered all of his devoted students together in the calm, sunlit courtyard</li><li>choosing quite deliberately to sit down humbly among them</li><li>expected the old master to teach them some clever new sword technique</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, an old and calm Musashi gathered his ___ in the ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous duels, Miyamoto Musashi had grown old and grey, and he no longer thought of himself as a fierce warrior the way he once had in his younger, more restless days. One quiet morning, he gathered his students together in the peaceful courtyard of his dojo, sitting down calmly among them instead of standing above them the way a teacher usually might, and waited patiently for them to settle.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after the many famous duels of his younger days, the once-fierce swordsman Miyamoto Musashi had grown old, grey, and remarkably calm, and he no longer thought of himself, even privately, as the fierce and unbeatable warrior he had once been so widely known as. One quiet, peaceful morning, he gathered all of his devoted students together in the calm, sunlit courtyard of his own dojo, choosing quite deliberately to sit down humbly among them on the ground rather than standing tall above them the way most respected teachers of swordsmanship usually might have done.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after the many famous duels and contests of his younger, more restless days, the once-fierce and widely feared swordsman Miyamoto Musashi had by then grown old, grey-haired, and remarkably calm in both manner and spirit, and he no longer thought of himself, even in his own private thoughts, as the fierce and seemingly unbeatable warrior he had once been so widely known and feared as across the land. One quiet, peaceful morning, much like any other, he gathered all of his devoted young students together in the calm, sunlit courtyard of his own modest dojo, choosing quite deliberately, and rather unusually for a teacher of his fame, to sit down humbly among them on the bare ground itself, rather than standing tall and imposing above them the way most respected teachers of swordsmanship traditionally always had.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>discipline</li><li>humility</li><li>inner peace</li><li>mastery</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students expect a new sword technique</li><li>Musashi speaks about discipline and humility instead</li><li>He explains mastery is about oneself, not others</li><li>He speaks of fears, pride, and patience</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>spoke softly, slowly, and thoughtfully about discipline, humility, and genuine inner peace</li><li>true mastery of the sword, and indeed of life itself, was never really about defeating other people</li><li>learning to master oneself completely</li><li>one's own fears, one's own pride, and one's own patience</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Musashi explained that true mastery means mastering ___, not defeating ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The students expected him to teach them a new sword technique, but instead Musashi spoke softly about discipline, humility, and inner peace. He explained that true mastery was never really about defeating other people in a fight, but about mastering oneself, one's fears, one's pride, and one's patience, day after day, for as long as one lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students, naturally enough, fully expected the old master to teach them some clever new sword technique or advanced fighting stance, exactly as he always had in years gone by. Instead, however, Musashi spoke softly, slowly, and thoughtfully about discipline, humility, and genuine inner peace, quietly explaining to them, one careful word at a time, that true mastery of the sword, and indeed of life itself, was never really about defeating other people in a fight at all, but rather about learning to master oneself completely, one's own fears, one's own pride, and one's own patience, day after quiet day, for as long as one lived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students, naturally and understandably enough, fully expected their aging old master to teach them some clever new sword technique, an advanced fighting stance, or some closely guarded secret of his own legendary skill, exactly as he had always done for them in years gone by. Instead, however, to their quiet surprise, Musashi spoke softly, slowly, and with great care and thoughtfulness about discipline, humility, and genuine, lasting inner peace, quietly and patiently explaining to them, one careful and deliberate word at a time, that true mastery of the sword, and indeed of one's whole life, was never really about defeating other people in a fight at all, however skilfully done, but rather about learning, slowly and patiently, to master oneself completely instead, one's own fears, one's own pride, and one's own patience, day after quiet day, for just as long as one happened to live.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the students</li><li>the lesson</li><li>humility</li><li>self-control</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The students listen closely, surprised and moved</li><li>They carry his lesson into everything they do</li><li>They understand mastery is more than winning duels</li><li>They learn to value humility and self-control</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>listened closely and attentively to every single word</li><li>carried Musashi's final and most important lesson with them</li><li>becoming a truly great master meant far more than simply winning duels</li><li>learning to live each day with genuine humility, quiet self-control</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the students learned that true mastery means living with ___ and a calm, ___ heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely, surprised but moved by his gentle words. From that day on, they carried Musashi's final lesson with them into everything they did, understanding at last that becoming a true master meant far more than winning duels. It meant living with humility, self-control, and a calm, disciplined heart, whatever life might ask of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely and attentively to every single word, feeling rather surprised at first, and yet deeply moved by his gentle, unhurried words and the calm manner in which he spoke them. From that memorable day onward, each of them carried Musashi's final and most important lesson with them into absolutely everything they did, both inside the dojo and out in the wider world beyond its walls, understanding at last, perhaps for the very first time, that becoming a truly great master meant far more than simply winning duels or defeating rivals. It meant, instead, learning to live each day with genuine humility, quiet self-control, and a calm, steady, and disciplined heart.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>His students listened closely, quietly, and attentively to every single one of his carefully chosen words, feeling rather surprised at first by such an unexpected lesson, and yet growing steadily more and more deeply moved by his gentle, unhurried manner of speaking and the calm, settled way in which he delivered each thought. From that memorable morning onward, for the rest of their own long lives, each of his students carried Musashi's final and most important lesson with them into absolutely everything they went on to do, both within the quiet walls of the dojo and out in the wider, busier world beyond it, understanding at last, perhaps for the very first time in their young lives, that becoming a truly great master meant far more, in the end, than simply winning duels or defeating one's rivals in a contest of skill. It meant, instead, learning patiently to live each and every day with genuine humility, quiet self-control, and a calm, steady, and thoroughly disciplined heart.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 37: Miyamoto Musashi's Final Lesson</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 37 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 37 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 38 (The Legend of Mount Fuji) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, an old and calm Musashi gathered his ___ in the ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Musashi explained that true mastery means mastering ___, not defeating ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the students learned that true mastery means living with ___ and a calm, ___ heart.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 38’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name the emperor and the elixir as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the emperor</li><li>the elixir</li><li>the mountain</li><li>the smoke</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A traveller brings the emperor a rare elixir of eternal life</li><li>The emperor considers what living forever alone would mean</li><li>He chooses his loved ones over eternal life</li><li>He has the elixir burned atop the mountain, now called Fuji</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The elixir was said to grant eternal life</li><li>The emperor drank the elixir right away without thinking</li><li>The emperor chose his loved ones over living forever alone</li><li>People say smoke can still be seen rising from Mount Fuji's peak</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, it is said, a great emperor of Japan was given a rare and precious elixir, a potion said to grant whoever drank it eternal life, never growing old or dying. The elixir had been carried to him from a wise traveller who claimed it was the most valuable gift in the whole world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking it right away, the emperor thought carefully about what such a gift would truly mean. If he lived forever, he would eventually have to watch everyone he loved grow old and pass away while he remained unchanged, always alone in the end. He decided that his short, ordinary life, filled with the people he loved, was worth far more to him than living forever without them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So the emperor ordered his servants to carry the elixir to the very top of the tallest, most beautiful mountain in the land and to burn it there instead of drinking it, letting its wisp of smoke rise gently into the sky. From that day on, people called the mountain Fuji, and they say that on quiet days, a thin trail of smoke can still be seen drifting from its peak, a lasting reminder that an ordinary life filled with love is worth more than living forever alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, according to an old and cherished Japanese legend, a great and thoughtful emperor was once given a rare and remarkably precious elixir, a mysterious potion said to grant whoever drank it complete and total eternal life, meaning that person would never again grow old, grow weak, or ever die. This wondrous elixir had been carried to the emperor's palace from far away by a wise and mysterious traveller, who solemnly claimed that it was, without question, the single most valuable and remarkable gift to be found anywhere in the entire world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking the precious elixir right away, as almost anyone else surely would have done without a second thought, the thoughtful emperor instead sat quietly and considered very carefully exactly what such an extraordinary gift would truly mean for the rest of his days. He realised, after much careful thought, that if he lived forever without ever growing old himself, he would eventually be forced to watch every single person he had ever loved slowly grow old and pass away all around him, one after another, while he alone remained forever unchanged, entirely alone in the end no matter how long he lived. Weighing this sad and lonely truth carefully in his mind, he decided at last that his own short, ordinary human life, filled as it was with the people he loved most dearly, was worth far, far more to him than living forever without them by his side.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And so, having made up his mind completely, the emperor ordered his most trusted servants to carry the rare elixir all the way to the very summit of the tallest, most beautiful mountain anywhere in the entire land, and there to burn it completely rather than ever drinking it himself, allowing its thin, curling wisp of smoke to rise gently and peacefully up into the wide sky above. From that day onward, people all across the land began calling the great mountain Fuji, and to this very day, it is often said that on certain calm, quiet mornings, a thin trail of smoke can still sometimes be seen drifting gently from its distant peak, a lasting and gentle reminder that an ordinary human life, filled with genuine love, is worth far more in the end than living forever entirely alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, according to an old and much-cherished Japanese legend passed down carefully through many generations, a great, thoughtful, and rather wise emperor was once given an exceedingly rare and remarkably precious elixir, a mysterious, glowing potion said to grant whoever finally drank it complete and total eternal life, meaning that fortunate person would never again grow old, grow weak, fall ill, or ever truly die at all. This wondrous and closely guarded elixir had been carried all the way to the emperor's own palace from somewhere far, far away by a wise and rather mysterious travelling stranger, who solemnly and confidently claimed that it was, without the slightest question or doubt, the single most valuable and remarkable treasure to be found absolutely anywhere in the entire wide world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking the precious elixir right away without a second thought, as almost anyone else in his position surely would have done, the unusually thoughtful emperor instead chose to sit quietly by himself for many long days, considering very carefully and deeply exactly what such an extraordinary and rather frightening gift would truly mean for the rest of his long, unending days ahead. He came to realise, after much slow and careful thought, that if he were to live forever without ever growing old himself, he would eventually be forced to watch every single person he had ever loved and cherished slowly grow old and quietly pass away all around him, one after another, while he alone remained forever young and entirely unchanged, utterly alone in the end no matter how many centuries he might go on living. Weighing this sad, sobering, and rather lonely truth very carefully in his mind and heart, he decided at last, with quiet resolve, that his own short, ordinary human life, filled as it richly was with the people he loved most dearly in all the world, was worth immeasurably more to him in the end than living forever without any of them ever again by his side.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And so, having finally and firmly made up his mind about the whole matter, the emperor ordered his most trusted and loyal servants to carry the rare and precious elixir all the way up to the very summit of the tallest, grandest, and most beautiful mountain to be found anywhere across the entire land, and there, at last, to burn it away completely rather than ever allowing anyone to drink it, letting its thin, gently curling wisp of pale smoke rise slowly and peacefully up into the wide, open sky far above. From that very day onward, people all across the land began respectfully calling the great and towering mountain Fuji, and to this very day, many, many years later, it is often still said that on certain still, quiet mornings, a thin, delicate trail of smoke can sometimes yet be glimpsed drifting gently from its distant, snow-capped peak, a lasting, gentle, and quietly moving reminder to all who hear the old story that an ordinary human life, richly filled with genuine love, is worth immeasurably more in the end than living forever completely and utterly alone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the emperor</li><li>the elixir</li><li>the traveller</li><li>eternal life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A wise traveller brings the emperor an elixir</li><li>It is said to grant eternal life</li><li>The emperor does not drink it right away</li><li>He thinks carefully about what it would mean</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>given a rare and remarkably precious elixir</li><li>said to grant whoever drank it complete and total eternal life</li><li>carried to the emperor's palace from far away by a wise and mysterious traveller</li><li>sat quietly and considered very carefully</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, a traveller brought the emperor an ___ said to grant ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, it is said, a great emperor of Japan was given a rare and precious elixir, a potion said to grant whoever drank it eternal life, never growing old or dying. The elixir had been carried to him from a wise traveller who claimed it was the most valuable gift in the whole world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long ago, according to an old and cherished Japanese legend, a great and thoughtful emperor was once given a rare and remarkably precious elixir, a mysterious potion said to grant whoever drank it complete and total eternal life, meaning that person would never again grow old, grow weak, or ever die. This wondrous elixir had been carried to the emperor's palace from far away by a wise and mysterious traveller, who solemnly claimed that it was, without question, the single most valuable and remarkable gift to be found anywhere in the entire world.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Long, long ago, according to an old and much-cherished Japanese legend passed down carefully through many generations, a great, thoughtful, and rather wise emperor was once given an exceedingly rare and remarkably precious elixir, a mysterious, glowing potion said to grant whoever finally drank it complete and total eternal life, meaning that fortunate person would never again grow old, grow weak, fall ill, or ever truly die at all. This wondrous and closely guarded elixir had been carried all the way to the emperor's own palace from somewhere far, far away by a wise and rather mysterious travelling stranger, who solemnly and confidently claimed that it was, without the slightest question or doubt, the single most valuable and remarkable treasure to be found absolutely anywhere in the entire wide world.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>loved ones</li><li>growing old</li><li>alone</li><li>his choice</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The emperor imagines living forever without ageing</li><li>He would watch loved ones grow old and pass away</li><li>He would remain unchanged and alone</li><li>He chooses his ordinary life over eternal life</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>would eventually be forced to watch every single person he had ever loved</li><li>slowly grow old and quietly pass away all around him</li><li>remained forever young and entirely unchanged, utterly alone</li><li>his own short, ordinary human life... was worth immeasurably more</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, the emperor realised that living forever would mean losing everyone he ___, so he chose his ordinary ___ instead.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking it right away, the emperor thought carefully about what such a gift would truly mean. If he lived forever, he would eventually have to watch everyone he loved grow old and pass away while he remained unchanged, always alone in the end. He decided that his short, ordinary life, filled with the people he loved, was worth far more to him than living forever without them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking the precious elixir right away, as almost anyone else surely would have done without a second thought, the thoughtful emperor instead sat quietly and considered very carefully exactly what such an extraordinary gift would truly mean for the rest of his days. He realised, after much careful thought, that if he lived forever without ever growing old himself, he would eventually be forced to watch every single person he had ever loved slowly grow old and pass away all around him, one after another, while he alone remained forever unchanged, entirely alone in the end no matter how long he lived. Weighing this sad and lonely truth carefully in his mind, he decided at last that his own short, ordinary human life, filled as it was with the people he loved most dearly, was worth far, far more to him than living forever without them by his side.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Rather than drinking the precious elixir right away without a second thought, as almost anyone else in his position surely would have done, the unusually thoughtful emperor instead chose to sit quietly by himself for many long days, considering very carefully and deeply exactly what such an extraordinary and rather frightening gift would truly mean for the rest of his long, unending days ahead. He came to realise, after much slow and careful thought, that if he were to live forever without ever growing old himself, he would eventually be forced to watch every single person he had ever loved and cherished slowly grow old and quietly pass away all around him, one after another, while he alone remained forever young and entirely unchanged, utterly alone in the end no matter how many centuries he might go on living. Weighing this sad, sobering, and rather lonely truth very carefully in his mind and heart, he decided at last, with quiet resolve, that his own short, ordinary human life, filled as it richly was with the people he loved most dearly in all the world, was worth immeasurably more to him in the end than living forever without any of them ever again by his side.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the mountain</li><li>the summit</li><li>the smoke</li><li>Mount Fuji</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The emperor orders the elixir carried up the mountain</li><li>His servants burn it at the summit instead of drinking it</li><li>The mountain becomes known as Mount Fuji</li><li>Smoke is still said to rise from its peak</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ordered his most trusted and loyal servants to carry the rare and precious elixir</li><li>burn it away completely rather than ever allowing anyone to drink it</li><li>people all across the land began respectfully calling the great and towering mountain Fuji</li><li>a thin, delicate trail of smoke can sometimes yet be glimpsed drifting gently</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the elixir was burned atop the mountain, now called ___, where ___ is still said to rise.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>So the emperor ordered his servants to carry the elixir to the very top of the tallest, most beautiful mountain in the land and to burn it there instead of drinking it, letting its wisp of smoke rise gently into the sky. From that day on, people called the mountain Fuji, and they say that on quiet days, a thin trail of smoke can still be seen drifting from its peak, a lasting reminder that an ordinary life filled with love is worth more than living forever alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And so, having made up his mind completely, the emperor ordered his most trusted servants to carry the rare elixir all the way to the very summit of the tallest, most beautiful mountain anywhere in the entire land, and there to burn it completely rather than ever drinking it himself, allowing its thin, curling wisp of smoke to rise gently and peacefully up into the wide sky above. From that day onward, people all across the land began calling the great mountain Fuji, and to this very day, it is often said that on certain calm, quiet mornings, a thin trail of smoke can still sometimes be seen drifting gently from its distant peak, a lasting and gentle reminder that an ordinary human life, filled with genuine love, is worth far more in the end than living forever entirely alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>And so, having finally and firmly made up his mind about the whole matter, the emperor ordered his most trusted and loyal servants to carry the rare and precious elixir all the way up to the very summit of the tallest, grandest, and most beautiful mountain to be found anywhere across the entire land, and there, at last, to burn it away completely rather than ever allowing anyone to drink it, letting its thin, gently curling wisp of pale smoke rise slowly and peacefully up into the wide, open sky far above. From that very day onward, people all across the land began respectfully calling the great and towering mountain Fuji, and to this very day, many, many years later, it is often still said that on certain still, quiet mornings, a thin, delicate trail of smoke can sometimes yet be glimpsed drifting gently from its distant, snow-capped peak, a lasting, gentle, and quietly moving reminder to all who hear the old story that an ordinary human life, richly filled with genuine love, is worth immeasurably more in the end than living forever completely and utterly alone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 38: The Legend of Mount Fuji</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 38 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 38 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 39 (Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, a traveller brought the emperor an ___ said to grant ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, the emperor realised that living forever would mean losing everyone he ___, so he chose his ordinary ___ instead.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the elixir was burned atop the mountain, now called ___, where ___ is still said to rise.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 39’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Kintoki and the lord as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintoki</li><li>the lord</li><li>strength</li><li>kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro grows up into the strong young man Kintoki</li><li>A famous lord invites him to join his household</li><li>Kintoki uses his strength to protect and help others</li><li>He is remembered as a great man with a kind heart</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintoki is the grown-up version of the boy Kintaro</li><li>Kintoki used his strength to bully and boast</li><li>A famous lord invited Kintoki to become his retainer</li><li>Kintoki was remembered for his strength and kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years after his adventures as a boy in the mountains, the golden-haired boy Kintaro grew into a tall, powerful young man remembered across the land by his adult name, Kintoki. He never forgot the strength and kindness his mother and the forest animals had taught him, and he carried both qualities with him always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's remarkable strength and gentle nature soon caught the attention of a famous lord, who invited him to join his household as a trusted retainer. Rather than using his great strength to boast or bully, Kintoki used it to protect the lord's people, lift heavy burdens others could not manage, and stand firmly for what was right.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Villagers who had once heard tales of the golden boy who wrestled bears now watched the grown warrior Kintoki serve faithfully and kindly wherever he was needed. His childhood strength and kindness had grown up right alongside him, and people said that the boy from the mountains had truly become a great man, remembered for the same big heart he had always carried.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his remarkable adventures as a boy growing up wild among the animals of the mountains, the once golden-haired boy known as Kintaro had grown steadily into a tall, remarkably strong, and powerfully built young man, now known and respected right across the land by his proper adult name, Kintoki. Even as a grown man, he never once forgot the great strength and genuine kindness that his devoted mother and the many forest animals of his childhood had so patiently taught him, and he made sure to carry both of these important qualities with him faithfully into everything he did, wherever his adult life happened to take him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's truly remarkable physical strength, combined with his unusually gentle and thoughtful nature, soon caught the watchful attention of a rather famous and well-respected lord, who invited the young man warmly to join his own household as a trusted and valued retainer. Rather than ever using his considerable strength merely to boast, show off, or bully weaker people around him, as many strong young men so easily might have done, Kintoki instead chose consistently to use his gift to protect the lord's own people, to lift heavy burdens that no one else in the household could manage alone, and to stand firmly and calmly for what was right, whatever the situation demanded of him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ordinary villagers who had once, as children themselves, heard exciting old tales of the golden-haired boy who wrestled bears and rode through the mountains now watched with quiet pride and admiration as the grown warrior Kintoki served faithfully, humbly, and kindly wherever he happened to be needed most. His childhood strength and gentle kindness had, in the end, grown up right alongside him into full adulthood, and people across the land began saying warmly to one another that the little boy from the mountains had truly grown into a great and admirable man, remembered fondly and lastingly for exactly the same big, generous heart he had always carried, even from his very earliest days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after his remarkable and much-loved adventures as a boy growing up wild and free among the animals of the deep mountains, the once golden-haired boy widely known as Kintaro had, over time, grown steadily into a tall, remarkably strong, and powerfully built young man, now known and deeply respected right across the entire land by his proper adult name, Kintoki. Even as a fully grown man with responsibilities of his own, he never once forgot the great physical strength and the genuine, patient kindness that his devoted mother and the many forest animals of his happy childhood had so carefully and patiently taught him over the years, and he made a point of carrying both of these important qualities with him faithfully into absolutely everything he did, wherever his busy adult life happened to take him next.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's truly remarkable and widely admired physical strength, combined with his unusually gentle, thoughtful, and considerate nature, soon caught the watchful attention of a rather famous and highly respected local lord, who invited the young man warmly and personally to join his own household as a trusted, valued, and much-relied-upon retainer. Rather than ever choosing to use his considerable strength merely to boast, to show off in front of others, or to bully weaker people around him, exactly as many other strong young men in his position so very easily might have chosen to do instead, Kintoki instead chose, consistently and deliberately, to use his remarkable gift only to protect the lord's own people from harm, to lift heavy burdens that absolutely no one else in the entire household could ever hope to manage alone, and to stand firmly, calmly, and steadily for what was right, whatever difficult situation happened to demand it of him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ordinary villagers who had once, as small children themselves, delighted in hearing exciting old tales of the cheerful golden-haired boy who wrestled bears and rode joyfully through the mountains now watched with quiet pride, warmth, and genuine admiration as the grown warrior Kintoki went about serving faithfully, humbly, and kindly wherever he happened to be needed most, day after day. His childhood strength and gentle, generous kindness had, in the end, grown up steadily and naturally right alongside him into full and settled adulthood, and people all across the land began saying warmly to one another, again and again, that the cheerful little boy from the mountains had truly and fully grown into a great, admirable, and thoroughly good man, remembered fondly and lastingly by everyone who knew him for exactly the same big, generous heart that he had carried faithfully with him, even from his very earliest and happiest days in the mountains.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro</li><li>Kintoki</li><li>strength</li><li>kindness</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Kintaro grows up into a strong young man</li><li>He is now known as Kintoki</li><li>He remembers his mother's and the animals' teaching</li><li>He carries strength and kindness with him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>grown steadily into a tall, remarkably strong, and powerfully built young man</li><li>known and respected right across the land by his proper adult name, Kintoki</li><li>never once forgot the great strength and genuine kindness</li><li>carry both of these important qualities with him faithfully</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, the boy Kintaro grew up into the strong young man known as ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Years after his adventures as a boy in the mountains, the golden-haired boy Kintaro grew into a tall, powerful young man remembered across the land by his adult name, Kintoki. He never forgot the strength and kindness his mother and the forest animals had taught him, and he carried both qualities with him always.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his remarkable adventures as a boy growing up wild among the animals of the mountains, the once golden-haired boy known as Kintaro had grown steadily into a tall, remarkably strong, and powerfully built young man, now known and respected right across the land by his proper adult name, Kintoki. Even as a grown man, he never once forgot the great strength and genuine kindness that his devoted mother and the many forest animals of his childhood had so patiently taught him, and he made sure to carry both of these important qualities with him faithfully into everything he did, wherever his adult life happened to take him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after his remarkable and much-loved adventures as a boy growing up wild and free among the animals of the deep mountains, the once golden-haired boy widely known as Kintaro had, over time, grown steadily into a tall, remarkably strong, and powerfully built young man, now known and deeply respected right across the entire land by his proper adult name, Kintoki. Even as a fully grown man with responsibilities of his own, he never once forgot the great physical strength and the genuine, patient kindness that his devoted mother and the many forest animals of his happy childhood had so carefully and patiently taught him over the years, and he made a point of carrying both of these important qualities with him faithfully into absolutely everything he did, wherever his busy adult life happened to take him next.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the lord</li><li>the household</li><li>a retainer</li><li>strength</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A famous lord notices Kintoki's strength</li><li>He invites Kintoki to join his household</li><li>Kintoki becomes a trusted retainer</li><li>He chooses to protect rather than boast</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>caught the watchful attention of a rather famous and well-respected lord</li><li>invited the young man warmly to join his own household as a trusted and valued retainer</li><li>never using his considerable strength merely to boast, show off, or bully</li><li>instead to use his gift to protect the lord's own people</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, a famous ___ invited Kintoki to become his trusted ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's remarkable strength and gentle nature soon caught the attention of a famous lord, who invited him to join his household as a trusted retainer. Rather than using his great strength to boast or bully, Kintoki used it to protect the lord's people, lift heavy burdens others could not manage, and stand firmly for what was right.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's truly remarkable physical strength, combined with his unusually gentle and thoughtful nature, soon caught the watchful attention of a rather famous and well-respected lord, who invited the young man warmly to join his own household as a trusted and valued retainer. Rather than ever using his considerable strength merely to boast, show off, or bully weaker people around him, as many strong young men so easily might have done, Kintoki instead chose consistently to use his gift to protect the lord's own people, to lift heavy burdens that no one else in the household could manage alone, and to stand firmly and calmly for what was right, whatever the situation demanded of him.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Kintoki's truly remarkable and widely admired physical strength, combined with his unusually gentle, thoughtful, and considerate nature, soon caught the watchful attention of a rather famous and highly respected local lord, who invited the young man warmly and personally to join his own household as a trusted, valued, and much-relied-upon retainer. Rather than ever choosing to use his considerable strength merely to boast, to show off in front of others, or to bully weaker people around him, exactly as many other strong young men in his position so very easily might have chosen to do instead, Kintoki instead chose, consistently and deliberately, to use his remarkable gift only to protect the lord's own people from harm, to lift heavy burdens that absolutely no one else in the entire household could ever hope to manage alone, and to stand firmly, calmly, and steadily for what was right, whatever difficult situation happened to demand it of him.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>villagers</li><li>the mountains</li><li>a great man</li><li>his heart</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Villagers recall the tales of the boy who wrestled bears</li><li>They now watch Kintoki serve faithfully and kindly</li><li>His childhood character grew up alongside him</li><li>He is remembered as a great man with a kind heart</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>delighted in hearing exciting old tales of the cheerful golden-haired boy who wrestled bears</li><li>watched with quiet pride and admiration as the grown warrior Kintoki served faithfully</li><li>had, in the end, grown up right alongside him into full adulthood</li><li>remembered fondly and lastingly for exactly the same big, generous heart</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, Kintoki was remembered as a great man with the same kind ___ he had as a ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Villagers who had once heard tales of the golden boy who wrestled bears now watched the grown warrior Kintoki serve faithfully and kindly wherever he was needed. His childhood strength and kindness had grown up right alongside him, and people said that the boy from the mountains had truly become a great man, remembered for the same big heart he had always carried.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ordinary villagers who had once, as children themselves, heard exciting old tales of the golden-haired boy who wrestled bears and rode through the mountains now watched with quiet pride and admiration as the grown warrior Kintoki served faithfully, humbly, and kindly wherever he happened to be needed most. His childhood strength and gentle kindness had, in the end, grown up right alongside him into full adulthood, and people across the land began saying warmly to one another that the little boy from the mountains had truly grown into a great and admirable man, remembered fondly and lastingly for exactly the same big, generous heart he had always carried, even from his very earliest days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ordinary villagers who had once, as small children themselves, delighted in hearing exciting old tales of the cheerful golden-haired boy who wrestled bears and rode joyfully through the mountains now watched with quiet pride, warmth, and genuine admiration as the grown warrior Kintoki went about serving faithfully, humbly, and kindly wherever he happened to be needed most, day after day. His childhood strength and gentle, generous kindness had, in the end, grown up steadily and naturally right alongside him into full and settled adulthood, and people all across the land began saying warmly to one another, again and again, that the cheerful little boy from the mountains had truly and fully grown into a great, admirable, and thoroughly good man, remembered fondly and lastingly by everyone who knew him for exactly the same big, generous heart that he had carried faithfully with him, even from his very earliest and happiest days in the mountains.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 39: Kintoki: Kintaro Grown Tall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 39 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 39 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 40 (Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared) as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, the boy Kintaro grew up into the strong young man known as ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, a famous ___ invited Kintoki to become his trusted ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, Kintoki was remembered as a great man with the same kind ___ he had as a ___.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1 — Read & Understand":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 1 of 5: Read &amp; Understand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show how ideas connect and what matters most in a text.</li><li><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas or themes develop across a whole text, and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Comprehension strategies:</strong> making inferences and connections using evidence from the text plus prior knowledge; monitoring understanding while reading.</li><li><strong>Years 5&ndash;6 &mdash; Text form &amp; structure:</strong> identifying how a text is structured (e.g. beginning/middle/end) and how that structure is used to communicate ideas clearly.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6) of the refreshed English Learning Area, in effect from 1 January 2026 &mdash; this replaces the old &ldquo;curriculum level 3&rdquo; framework for these year levels. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Where this lesson sits in the Years 5&ndash;6 teaching sequence:</strong></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This lesson deliberately does <em>not</em> ask students to summarise yet &mdash; it builds the literal comprehension (who/what/when) that the curriculum’s summarising practices depend on. Lessons 2&ndash;5 of this story apply the S.W.O.R.D. method directly against the Year 5/6 summarising practices above, one paragraph at a time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to understand a whole story clearly before I try to summarise it.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app Master’s Seal)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I know who the story is about.</li><li>I know what happened at the beginning, middle and end.</li><li>I could explain the whole story to someone else in my own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first lesson of Story 40’s five-lesson cycle (part of the wider 200-lesson programme). Use it to establish Bushido Code rule one, &ldquo;Honour the truth,&rdquo; &mdash; never change what the story actually says &mdash; as the reason careful reading has to come before summarising.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to Lesson 1 (Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared) &mdash; or printed Scroll handouts (use the &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; button and choose the level for each student or group).</li><li>Optional: mini whiteboard or chart paper to introduce the Bushido Code poster.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation &mdash; the three scrolls</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><strong>Scroll I &middot; Recruit</strong> (short sentences, simple vocabulary): students who need high support, are below year-level decoding, or are new to English.</li><li><strong>Scroll II &middot; Warrior</strong> (moderate length, some descriptive language): most Year 5 students, or on-level Year 4.</li><li><strong>Scroll III &middot; Master</strong> (richer vocabulary, more complex sentences): confident readers and Year 6 extension.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The refreshed curriculum’s guidance for this age group favours keeping students at year-level ideas and adapting the support around a text, rather than only simplifying the text itself &mdash; so for a student reading below their assigned scroll, consider reading it aloud together first, or pairing them with a stronger reader, rather than dropping them straight to Scroll I.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Read the framing text aloud together. Ask: &ldquo;Why do you think a samurai trains in tiny daily sessions instead of one big one?&rdquo; Connect this to today’s rule &mdash; understanding must come before summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: engagement with the idea &mdash; there’s no right/wrong answer here.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; The Scroll (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Assign each student or group their scroll level. Read all three paragraphs once silently, then re-read once more, this time focusing on who is in the story and what happens. Let students briefly switch scrolls to notice the story stays the same &mdash; only the telling changes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Talk moves: &ldquo;What stayed the same across the scrolls? What changed?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: are students reading all three paragraphs (beginning/middle/end), not just skimming one?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Comprehension Check (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work through the three interactive checks in the app: Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event sequence chips), and True/False (fact chips). For a whole-class version, do this orally or on mini-whiteboards first, then let students confirm it on the app.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students correctly name Momotaro and the village as characters (not a place or a thing)? Can they sequence the story’s four key events in the correct order?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Retell It (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students retell the whole story in their own words in the text box. Reassure them this is <em>not</em> a summary yet, so they shouldn’t worry about leaving anything out.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a full, sequenced retelling in the student’s own words, not copied phrases from the scroll &mdash; early evidence of the &ldquo;own voice&rdquo; Bushido rule, ahead of formal summarising in Lesson 2.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students self-check the three success-criteria boxes. Use this as a quick check-in &mdash; ask a few students to justify one box aloud before moving on.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comprehension check answers (character ID, event order, true/false) give quick formative data on literal comprehension.</li><li>The retell text shows depth of understanding and use of the student’s own words, ahead of formal summarising.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask confident readers (Scroll III) to find one sentence in the story that couldn’t be cut without losing meaning, and explain why &mdash; an early seed of the &ldquo;Remove&rdquo; step in S.W.O.R.D., which arrives in Lesson 2.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>For students needing more support, read Scroll I aloud together as a group before independent reading, and offer sentence starters for the retell step: &ldquo;This story is about ___&rdquo; and &ldquo;First ___, then ___, and at the end ___.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Story Check Data (Lesson 1 chips)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Search — characters / key things</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the village</li><li>the treasure</li><li>his friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch + Order — events to put in sequence</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro gathers the whole village together at dusk</li><li>He and his friends bring out the treasure from Onigashima</li><li>They share the treasure fairly among every family</li><li>The village celebrates courage, friendship, and generosity</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">True / False statements</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro kept all the treasure for himself</li><li>Momotaro shared the treasure fairly with the whole village</li><li>The dog, the monkey, and the pheasant helped share the treasure</li><li>The story reminds us that friendship is worth more than treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit (simple)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous journey to Onigashima, Momotaro was remembered throughout the land as a great village hero. One evening, as the sun set warmly over the rooftops, he gathered the whole village together in the square, along with his three loyal friends: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, they brought out the treasure they had carried home from Ogre Island so long ago and shared it out fairly among everyone gathered, family by family, making sure no one went without. Momotaro reminded the villagers that the real treasure of that old adventure had never been the gold or silk alone, but the courage and friendship that had made the journey possible in the first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The villagers cheered and thanked Momotaro and his loyal friends, and that evening became a joyful celebration remembered for years afterward. From that day on, people told the story of Momotaro not only as a tale of bravery, but as a reminder that true wealth is shared freely, and that courage and friendship are worth far more than any treasure kept only for oneself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior (intermediate)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous and much-retold journey all the way to Onigashima, the distant island of the oni, Momotaro had, over time, come to be remembered warmly throughout the whole land as a truly great and much-loved village hero. One particularly beautiful evening, as the warm evening sun set gently over the rooftops of the village he had grown up in, Momotaro gathered the entire village together in the wide village square, standing proudly alongside his three loyal and faithful friends from that long-ago adventure: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, the four old friends brought out the last of the treasure they had carried home so long ago from distant Ogre Island, and carefully, patiently, and fairly shared it out among every single family gathered there in the square that evening, making quite sure that not a single person went without their fair share. As the treasure was passed from hand to hand, Momotaro reminded the gathered villagers warmly that the true treasure of that old adventure had never really been the gold, silver, or fine silk at all, but rather the courage and steady friendship that had made the whole difficult journey possible for him and his companions in the very first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gathered villagers cheered warmly and thanked Momotaro and his three loyal friends again and again for their generosity, and that particular evening quickly became a joyful, memorable celebration that people across the village spoke fondly about for many years afterward. From that day onward, whenever people told the old story of Momotaro to their own children and grandchildren, they told it not merely as an exciting tale of bravery and adventure, but as a gentle and lasting reminder that true wealth is always meant to be shared freely with others, and that courage and friendship, in the end, are worth far, far more than any amount of treasure kept only for oneself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master (advanced)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after his famous, much-loved, and often-retold journey all the way to distant Onigashima, the faraway island where the fearsome oni had once made their home, Momotaro had, over the passing years, come to be remembered warmly and fondly throughout the entire land as a truly great, generous, and much-loved village hero. One particularly beautiful and still evening, as the warm, glowing evening sun set gently and slowly over the familiar rooftops of the very village he had grown up in as a boy, Momotaro gathered the entire village together in the wide, open village square, standing there proudly and happily alongside his three loyal, faithful, and much-loved friends from that long-ago adventure so many years before: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, the four old and devoted friends brought out the very last of the treasure they had carried home together so long ago from distant Ogre Island, and carefully, patiently, and scrupulously fairly, they shared it out among every single family gathered there together in the square that quiet evening, making quite certain, family by family, that not a single person among them went without their own fair and proper share. As the gleaming treasure was passed warmly from hand to hand around the square, Momotaro reminded the gathered villagers, speaking gently but firmly, that the true and lasting treasure of that old adventure had never really been the gold, the silver, or the fine silk at all, however precious those things might have seemed at the time, but rather the steady courage and unwavering friendship that had made the whole difficult and dangerous journey possible for him and his loyal companions in the very first place, so very long ago.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gathered villagers cheered warmly, joyfully, and gratefully, thanking Momotaro and his three loyal friends again and again for their generosity, their goodness, and their unshakeable friendship, and that particular quiet evening quickly became a joyful, heartfelt celebration that people all across the village would go on speaking fondly about for many, many years afterward. From that memorable day onward, whenever people told the old and cherished story of Momotaro to their own children and grandchildren gathered close around the fire, they told it not merely as an exciting tale of bravery, danger, and daring adventure, but as a gentle, lasting, and important reminder to every generation of villagers still to come that true wealth is always meant to be shared freely and generously with others, and that courage and friendship, in the very end, are worth immeasurably more than any amount of treasure ever kept only for oneself alone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 2 — Draw the Sword: The Beginning":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 2 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Beginning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 1.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the first time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 1 only (&ldquo;The Beginning&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 1 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 1 Data (The Beginning)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro</li><li>the village</li><li>dusk</li><li>his friends</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Momotaro is remembered as a great hero</li><li>He gathers the whole village at dusk</li><li>His three loyal friends stand beside him</li><li>The dog, monkey, and pheasant join him</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>come to be remembered warmly throughout the whole land as a truly great and much-loved village hero</li><li>gathered the entire village together in the wide village square</li><li>standing proudly alongside his three loyal and faithful friends</li><li>the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>First, Momotaro gathered the whole ___ at dusk, alongside his three loyal ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous journey to Onigashima, Momotaro was remembered throughout the land as a great village hero. One evening, as the sun set warmly over the rooftops, he gathered the whole village together in the square, along with his three loyal friends: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many years after his famous and much-retold journey all the way to Onigashima, the distant island of the oni, Momotaro had, over time, come to be remembered warmly throughout the whole land as a truly great and much-loved village hero. One particularly beautiful evening, as the warm evening sun set gently over the rooftops of the village he had grown up in, Momotaro gathered the entire village together in the wide village square, standing proudly alongside his three loyal and faithful friends from that long-ago adventure: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Many long years after his famous, much-loved, and often-retold journey all the way to distant Onigashima, the faraway island where the fearsome oni had once made their home, Momotaro had, over the passing years, come to be remembered warmly and fondly throughout the entire land as a truly great, generous, and much-loved village hero. One particularly beautiful and still evening, as the warm, glowing evening sun set gently and slowly over the familiar rooftops of the very village he had grown up in as a boy, Momotaro gathered the entire village together in the wide, open village square, standing there proudly and happily alongside his three loyal, faithful, and much-loved friends from that long-ago adventure so many years before: the dog, the monkey, and the pheasant.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 3 — Draw the Sword: The Middle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 3 of 5: Draw the Sword: The Middle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 2.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the second time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 2 only (&ldquo;The Middle&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 2 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll draw the SWORD again on the next paragraph.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 2 Data (The Middle)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the treasure</li><li>every family</li><li>fairly</li><li>Onigashima</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>They bring out the treasure from Onigashima</li><li>They share it fairly among every family</li><li>No one goes without their share</li><li>Momotaro reminds them of its true meaning</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>brought out the last of the treasure they had carried home so long ago from distant Ogre Island</li><li>shared it out among every single family gathered there in the square</li><li>making quite sure that not a single person went without their fair share</li><li>reminded the gathered villagers warmly that the true treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Next, Momotaro and his friends shared the ___ fairly among every ___.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, they brought out the treasure they had carried home from Ogre Island so long ago and shared it out fairly among everyone gathered, family by family, making sure no one went without. Momotaro reminded the villagers that the real treasure of that old adventure had never been the gold or silk alone, but the courage and friendship that had made the journey possible in the first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, the four old friends brought out the last of the treasure they had carried home so long ago from distant Ogre Island, and carefully, patiently, and fairly shared it out among every single family gathered there in the square that evening, making quite sure that not a single person went without their fair share. As the treasure was passed from hand to hand, Momotaro reminded the gathered villagers warmly that the true treasure of that old adventure had never really been the gold, silver, or fine silk at all, but rather the courage and steady friendship that had made the whole difficult journey possible for him and his companions in the very first place.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Together, the four old and devoted friends brought out the very last of the treasure they had carried home together so long ago from distant Ogre Island, and carefully, patiently, and scrupulously fairly, they shared it out among every single family gathered there together in the square that quiet evening, making quite certain, family by family, that not a single person among them went without their own fair and proper share. As the gleaming treasure was passed warmly from hand to hand around the square, Momotaro reminded the gathered villagers, speaking gently but firmly, that the true and lasting treasure of that old adventure had never really been the gold, the silver, or the fine silk at all, however precious those things might have seemed at the time, but rather the steady courage and unwavering friendship that had made the whole difficult and dangerous journey possible for him and his loyal companions in the very first place, so very long ago.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 4 — Draw the Sword: The End":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 4 of 5: Draw the Sword: The End</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area (in effect from 1 January 2026). <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Comprehension, Summarising.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 5 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> selecting key details and organising them to show what matters most in a text. <strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> organising details to show how they build, shift or connect. This lesson applies both directly, at paragraph level, using the S.W.O.R.D. method as the taught strategy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to summarise one paragraph clearly, using the S.W.O.R.D. method.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (matches the in-app self-check)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I only kept what matters from paragraph 3.</li><li>My summary is in my own words, not copied from the scroll.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the third time students draw the full S.W.O.R.D. on this story. It works on paragraph 3 only (&ldquo;The End&rdquo;) &mdash; students should already have read the whole story in Lesson 1 and drawn the SWORD on the earlier paragraph(s).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A device with the app open to this lesson, or the printed Scroll and worksheet (use &ldquo;Print this reading&rdquo; and &ldquo;Print worksheet&rdquo;).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Differentiation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The app scaffolds this lesson: a sentence starter is given for the Deliver step, and the Remove step is a tap-to-cross-out activity rather than open-ended cutting. For students who finish early, ask them to try writing the summary a second time without the sentence starter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 5 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Katana Warm-Up (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick recall of the S.W.O.R.D. letters so far. Remind students today’s work covers paragraph 3 only.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Re-read the Paragraph (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students re-read just this paragraph, at their assigned scroll level.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Draw the S.W.O.R.D. (5&ndash;7 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Search (character chips), Watch + Order (event chips), Remove (tap-to-cross-out detail chips), then Deliver (sentence starter). Model the first one as a class if this is Lesson 2.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: can students tell a character from a place/thing? Do they sequence the events correctly? Do they choose sensible detail to cut?</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Dojo Challenge (2&ndash;3 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students write their own one-sentence summary of this paragraph. This sentence is saved and will be used again in Lesson 5.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">5 &middot; Quick Self-Check (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Transition line: &ldquo;Next lesson, you’ll bring all three paragraphs together into one finished summary.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The one-sentence Dojo Challenge summary is the key formative artefact &mdash; check it keeps only the essential event(s) from this paragraph and uses the student’s own words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> ask students to write their summary again in fewer words. <strong>Support:</strong> read the paragraph aloud together before independent work, and let students use the sentence starter as their final answer rather than a scaffold to move beyond.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Paragraph 3 Data (The End)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">S — Search: characters</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>the villagers</li><li>a celebration</li><li>friendship</li><li>true wealth</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">W — Watch + Order: events</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The villagers cheer and thank Momotaro's group</li><li>The evening becomes a joyful celebration</li><li>People retell the story for years afterward</li><li>The story reminds everyone that friendship is worth more than treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">O/R — Remove: details to cross out</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>cheered warmly and thanked Momotaro and his three loyal friends</li><li>became a joyful, memorable celebration that people across the village spoke fondly about</li><li>true wealth is always meant to be shared freely with others</li><li>courage and friendship, in the end, are worth far, far more than any amount of treasure</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">D — Deliver: sentence starter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Finally, the village celebrated, and the story reminded everyone that ___ is worth more than ___ kept alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll I · Recruit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The villagers cheered and thanked Momotaro and his loyal friends, and that evening became a joyful celebration remembered for years afterward. From that day on, people told the story of Momotaro not only as a tale of bravery, but as a reminder that true wealth is shared freely, and that courage and friendship are worth far more than any treasure kept only for oneself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll II · Warrior</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gathered villagers cheered warmly and thanked Momotaro and his three loyal friends again and again for their generosity, and that particular evening quickly became a joyful, memorable celebration that people across the village spoke fondly about for many years afterward. From that day onward, whenever people told the old story of Momotaro to their own children and grandchildren, they told it not merely as an exciting tale of bravery and adventure, but as a gentle and lasting reminder that true wealth is always meant to be shared freely with others, and that courage and friendship, in the end, are worth far, far more than any amount of treasure kept only for oneself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-slate-soft\">Scroll III · Master</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The gathered villagers cheered warmly, joyfully, and gratefully, thanking Momotaro and his three loyal friends again and again for their generosity, their goodness, and their unshakeable friendship, and that particular quiet evening quickly became a joyful, heartfelt celebration that people all across the village would go on speaking fondly about for many, many years afterward. From that memorable day onward, whenever people told the old and cherished story of Momotaro to their own children and grandchildren gathered close around the fire, they told it not merely as an exciting tale of bravery, danger, and daring adventure, but as a gentle, lasting, and important reminder to every generation of villagers still to come that true wealth is always meant to be shared freely and generously with others, and that courage and friendship, in the very end, are worth immeasurably more than any amount of treasure ever kept only for oneself alone.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 5 — The Full Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Story 40: Momotaro's Legacy: The Treasure Shared</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Programme</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summarise Like Samurais — Japanese Legends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lesson 5 of 5: The Full Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Rank</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank 8: Master Samurai (black belt) — focus: Full independence — teaching and checking others’ summaries</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">English — Reading (Summarising)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">About 15 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC English Phase 2)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">NZ Curriculum links &middot; Years 5&ndash;6</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Learning area:</strong> English &middot; <strong>Curriculum phase:</strong> Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6), refreshed English Learning Area. <strong>Strand:</strong> Reading &mdash; Summarising a whole text.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Year 6 &mdash; Summarising:</strong> tracking how ideas develop across a whole text and organising details to show how they build, shift or connect &mdash; exactly the skill this lesson practises by joining three paragraph-level summaries into one.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Source: Ministry of Education, <em>NZC &ndash; English Phase 2 (Years 4&ndash;6)</em>, Tāhūrangi &mdash; <a href=\"https://newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz/nzc---english-phase-2/5637238346.p\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">newzealandcurriculum.tahurangi.education.govt.nz</a></p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning intention (student-facing)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&ldquo;I am learning to combine three short summaries into one clear summary of a whole story.&rdquo;</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Success criteria (the Bushido checklist)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honour the truth &mdash; says only what the story says.</li><li>Discipline to cut &mdash; leaves out what doesn’t matter.</li><li>Clarity is strength &mdash; understood in one read.</li><li>Speak with your own voice &mdash; in the student’s own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Context &amp; prior knowledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students arrive with three saved sentences from Lessons 2&ndash;4 (the app recalls these automatically; on paper, students bring their three worksheets). This is the &ldquo;Master’s Seal&rdquo; moment for Story 40 &mdash; the first time this story’s whole cycle closes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Materials</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students’ completed Lesson 2&ndash;4 worksheets or devices (their three saved sentences).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching sequence (mirrors the 4 steps in the app)</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">1 &middot; Your Three S.W.O.R.D.s (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students read their three saved sentences together, in order. Ask: &ldquo;Do any of them repeat something? Where could they join smoothly?&rdquo;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">2 &middot; Combine &amp; Polish (4&ndash;5 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students merge their three sentences into one 2&ndash;4 sentence summary, cutting repetition and smoothing the joins. The app can pre-fill a draft from their three saved sentences as a starting point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><em>Look for: a summary that reads as one connected piece, not three sentences stapled together.</em></p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">3 &middot; Bushido Checklist (1&ndash;2 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students check their finished summary against all four rules of the Code. Encourage them to fix their summary if any box doesn’t tick.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">4 &middot; Master’s Seal (1 min)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Celebrate: Story 40 of 40 is complete. Preview Story 41, coming soon, as the next scroll to unlock.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assessment opportunities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The combined summary is the main summative artefact for the whole story &mdash; use the Bushido checklist as a simple 4-point rubric.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Support</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><strong>Extension:</strong> challenge students to get their combined summary under a set word count (e.g. 30 words) without losing meaning. <strong>Support:</strong> let students read their three sentences aloud to a partner first, before writing the combined version.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">The Three Paragraph Sentences (from Lessons 2–4)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>The Beginning:</b> First, Momotaro gathered the whole ___ at dusk, alongside his three loyal ___.</li><li><b>The Middle:</b> Next, Momotaro and his friends shared the ___ fairly among every ___.</li><li><b>The End:</b> Finally, the village celebrated, and the story reminded everyone that ___ is worth more than ___ kept alone.</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}}}},"topic":{"label":"Category","children":{"All":{"label":"Topic","children":{"Polynesian Migration (Wayfinder)":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 1: The Shattered Shard":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHERE WE BEGAN — The Lapita People and the First Great Voyages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how the perspectives of people in the past differ, and how this is influenced by the limited and varied evidence available.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how exploration and discovery have consequences for people and places.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to understand who the Lapita people were and where the first great Pacific voyages began.</li><li>We are learning how archaeologists use physical evidence — like pottery — to understand history that was never written down.</li><li>I can explain why some history has to be pieced together like a puzzle, rather than read in a book.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of student participation in the evidence jigsaw and class discussion. Review of student exit tickets and quest-log entries for understanding of how unwritten history is recovered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Teacher holds up a torn, fragmented picture — pieces of a &quot;found journal&quot; prop, or simply a jigsaw envelope. &quot;Good morning, everyone. Today we begin a brand-new voyage through history — one that starts not with a date in a textbook, but with a single broken piece of pottery. Before anyone wrote a single word about it, real people set out across the largest ocean on Earth and settled it, island by island. We're going to meet them through the clues they left behind.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Let's start with a wonder question: if a people never wrote anything down, how could we possibly know anything true about their lives thousands of years later?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Genesis reminds us that from the very beginning, God placed people across the earth with purpose. Acts 17:26 tells us God 'marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands.' Today we're going to explore one of the greatest, and least written-about, journeys in human history — and see if we can find the truth in the clues it left behind.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Was the Lapita Culture? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Around 3,000 years ago, archaeologists began finding a very particular kind of pottery scattered across islands of the western Pacific — decorated with fine, comb-like dots pressed into the clay before it was fired, called dentate-stamped pottery.</li><li>This pottery was first properly studied at a site called Lapita in New Caledonia in the 1950s–60s — which is where the name 'Lapita culture' comes from.</li><li>Show a world/Pacific map. Explain: archaeologists believe these people first appeared near the Bismarck Archipelago, close to Papua New Guinea, then spread an extraordinary distance — all the way to Fiji, Tonga and Samoa — in only a few hundred years. For people without metal tools, compasses, or maps, this was one of the fastest and farthest human migrations in the ancient world.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If they didn't write anything down, how do we know any of this actually happened?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Piecing Together the Evidence (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that historians and archaeologists piece together unwritten history using several kinds of evidence: archaeology (broken pottery, old rubbish heaps called middens, tools), linguistics (related words for the same things — like 'canoe' or 'house' — showing up in many different Pacific languages, because they share a common ancestor language), and oral tradition (stories, chants and whakapapa passed down by families for generations).</li><li>Activity: In small groups, students piece together a jigsaw image of a Lapita-style pottery shard laid over a faint Pacific map. As the picture comes together, it reveals the region archaeologists believe is the likely homeland.</li><li>Discuss as each group finishes: what does this physical evidence tell us, and what doesn't it tell us? (E.g. pottery shows us where people lived and roughly when, but not their names, feelings, or exact reasons for moving.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why the Timeline Matters (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Important distinction for students — the Lapita spread (around 3,000 years ago) happened across the western Pacific (Papua New Guinea to Samoa/Tonga). The much later voyages into the 'Polynesian Triangle' — to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and eventually Aotearoa — happened roughly 1,000–1,300 years after that, around 700–800 years ago. They are two different chapters of the same long story, not the same event.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter to get the order of events right, instead of treating it as one single voyage?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Biblical Connection — Every Nation, Known to God (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>&quot;Acts 17:26–27 tells us God 'marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands ... so that they would seek him.' Even though we only have fragments of evidence for the Lapita story, none of these real people and their journeys were lost to God, even when they were lost to written history.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">On a small piece of paper, write down ONE type of evidence historians use to learn about history that was never written down, and explain why it's useful. Be ready to share your answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: God placed in us a longing to know where we come from. When we uncover ancient pottery shards, we glimpse the creativity of people made in His image — adventurers, makers, and storytellers, just like us.</li><li>Verse: &quot;From one man he made all the nations, that they should inhabit the whole earth; and he marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands.&quot; — <em>Acts 17:26 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning where we come from helps us know God's story and our place in it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If God marked out appointed times and places for every nation, what does that tell us about the Lapita people — and about where YOU were born?</li><li>The voyagers left their mark in pottery. What marks are you making that might tell your story to someone a thousand years from now?</li><li>Tama is searching for where he comes from. How does knowing God as our Creator change the way we understand where we come from?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific Ocean map</li><li>Jigsaw sets: Lapita pottery image over a faint Pacific map (one per group)</li><li>Evidence cards (archaeology / linguistics / oral tradition)</li><li>Tama's Journal prop (or a sealed box/journal for the term-long story)</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Lapita pottery and the Pacific migration</em> — search: &quot;Lapita pottery Pacific migration explained&quot;. Gives a visual, age-appropriate overview of the real archaeological evidence behind this lesson.</li><li><em>Animated map of Pacific migration routes</em> — search: &quot;Pacific migration map animation&quot;. Helps students visualise the scale and direction of the spread discussed in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Own Lapita-Style Pottery Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Real Lapita pottery was decorated using a technique called dentate-stamping — pressing a small comb-like tool into wet clay to leave rows of tiny dots and lines.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On paper (or air-dry clay/playdough if available), design your own repeating pattern using only dots and lines, the way a Lapita potter would have.</li><li>Use a real comb, the edge of a fork, or a rolled-up piece of card to press your pattern in, if working with clay/dough — or draw it carefully if working on paper.</li><li>Add a short caption explaining what makes this pottery style historically important.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does a pattern like this tell future archaeologists about the people who made it — and what doesn't it tell them?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: An Archaeologist's Field Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Imagine you are an archaeologist who has just uncovered a piece of genuine Lapita pottery on a dig.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write a field diary entry describing the moment you found it.</li><li>Describe what the pottery looks like, where you found it, and what you think it might tell us.</li><li>Include at least one sentence about why this discovery matters for understanding unwritten history.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Aim for approximately 150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does writing as an archaeologist help you understand how real historical knowledge is built from physical evidence?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Migration Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You are going to create a simple digital timeline (using Canva, Google Slides, or a similar tool) showing the spread of the Lapita culture across the Pacific.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create at least 4 points on your timeline: the likely starting region (near Papua New Guinea), and three island groups it spread to (e.g. Fiji, Tonga, Samoa).</li><li>Add an approximate date or &quot;years ago&quot; label to each point.</li><li>Add one image or icon per point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how quickly this migration happened?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Evidence of the Lapita Voyagers</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Lapita culture or early Pacific migration.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Lapita pottery Pacific migration explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the type of evidence used, the region the people came from, and roughly how long ago this happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Your Review Task</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete a simple table: Evidence Type | What It Shows | Why It Matters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most convincing piece of evidence in the video, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 2: How Far Is Far?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE SCALE OF THE PACIFIC — Understanding the Polynesian Triangle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how places influence people and people influence places.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people view and use places differently.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to understand the true scale of the Pacific Ocean.</li><li>We are learning what the 'Polynesian Triangle' is and why it represents one of the greatest feats of human exploration.</li><li>I can compare real distances between Pacific island groups using a map and scale.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the floor-map measuring activity. Review of star compass design and written distance comparisons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we found our first clue about where the Pacific voyagers began. Today, we need to understand exactly how big the challenge ahead of them really was.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Quick question before we start: how big do you think the Pacific Ocean actually is? Bigger than New Zealand? Bigger than Australia? Let's find out.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show a true-scale world map (not the commonly distorted Mercator-projection versions) to reveal the Pacific's real size relative to landmasses.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Just How Big Is the Pacific? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The Pacific Ocean covers about 165 million square kilometres — roughly one-third of the entire surface of the Earth, and bigger than all the continents on Earth combined.</li><li>Introduce the Polynesian Triangle: a region of the Pacific with three corner points — Hawaii in the north, Aotearoa New Zealand in the southwest, and Rapa Nui (Easter Island) in the southeast. Inside this triangle are over 1,000 islands, nearly all of them settled by voyaging Polynesians over several centuries.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If you placed this triangle over a map of Europe, what do you think would happen?&quot; (It would swallow the entire continent several times over.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Measuring the Triangle (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: On a large floor map or outdoor chalk map of the Pacific, use string/wool cut to a measured scale to physically lay out the distances between Hawaii, Aotearoa, and Rapa Nui, forming a giant triangle the class can stand inside or around.</li><li>Real distances for reference: Tahiti to Hawaii is roughly 4,400 km. Tahiti to Aotearoa is roughly 4,300 km. Tahiti to Rapa Nui is roughly 4,000 km. Hawaii to Rapa Nui — almost corner to corner of the whole triangle — is roughly 7,000 km.</li><li>Ask students to guess how many classrooms or school halls would fit end-to-end along just ONE side of the triangle, before revealing the real number.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building a Tool for the Voyage (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, design a simple paper star compass wheel — a circle divided into key directions, decorated and labelled (e.g. sunrise point, sunset point, key star positions). This becomes a tool used again later in the term.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a voyager need a tool like this, instead of just guessing direction by feel?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence describing the real scale of the Pacific Ocean in your own words, using at least one number from today's lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Polynesian Triangle covers a third of the Earth's surface — and people crossed it in wooden canoes guided by stars alone. God made us with an extraordinary capacity to explore and inhabit the world He created for us.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God blessed them and said to them, &quot;Be fruitful and increase in number; fill the earth and subdue it.&quot;&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:28 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding the full scale of Polynesian migration helps us grasp the wonder of how God spread His people across the earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Pacific covers a third of the earth — what does the fact that people crossed it in wooden canoes tell you about how God designed humans?</li><li>If God called people to 'fill the earth,' do you think the Polynesian voyagers were part of that calling without knowing it?</li><li>What places in the world do you think God is calling people to reach in your generation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large floor/wall map of the Pacific</li><li>A true-scale (not Mercator) world map for comparison</li><li>Wool/string cut to a measured scale</li><li>Paper, compasses (drawing), pencils for star compass craft</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How big is the Pacific Ocean, really?</em> — search: &quot;true size of the Pacific Ocean map comparison&quot;. Helps correct the common misconception (from standard maps) of how small the Pacific looks compared to its real size.</li><li><em>The Polynesian Triangle explained</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian Triangle map explained&quot;. Gives a clear visual explanation of the three-point region central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Polynesian Triangle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw your own decorated map of the Polynesian Triangle, marking Hawaii, Aotearoa, and Rapa Nui, plus at least 3 other island groups inside it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the rough outline of the Pacific.</li><li>Mark and label the three triangle points plus extra islands.</li><li>Add a decorative compass rose in one corner.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest part of getting the scale right?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Describing the Ocean Ahead</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter, in role as a young voyager, to a family member staying behind, describing how big and frightening the ocean ahead seems.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mention at least one real distance fact from today's lesson.</li><li>Describe how it feels to face that much open ocean.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did using a real number change the way you wrote about the ocean's size?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Distance Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a spreadsheet or simple table tool (Google Sheets, Canva chart) to chart the four distances mentioned today (Tahiti–Hawaii, Tahiti–Aotearoa, Tahiti–Rapa Nui, Hawaii–Rapa Nui).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enter each distance.</li><li>Create a simple bar chart comparing them.</li><li>Add one sentence noting which is the longest.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the chart change how you picture these distances compared to just hearing the numbers?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Mapping the Pacific</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the size of the Pacific Ocean or the Polynesian Triangle.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian Triangle map explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the three corner points, how many islands are inside, and any real distance figures mentioned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most surprising fact you noted?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 3: Reading the Sky":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WEATHER vs. CLIMATE — What Every Voyager Needed to Know</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Appreciate that science is a way of explaining the world, and that science knowledge changes over time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the difference between weather and climate.</li><li>We are learning how observing wind and sky helped real voyagers make safe decisions.</li><li>I can use real, observed conditions to make and justify a simple prediction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the card-sorting activity and outdoor wind observation. Review of students' written voyage forecasts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, we measured how big the ocean ahead really is. Today we need to understand something just as important: how do you know it's actually safe to go?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what's the difference between knowing today's weather, and knowing what a whole season is usually like?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Real voyagers couldn't check a weather app. They relied entirely on careful, repeated observation of the sky, wind, and sea — skills built up over a lifetime, and passed down through generations.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Weather vs. Climate (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Weather is what's happening in the sky right now — today's wind, rain, temperature, cloud cover. Climate is the long-term pattern of weather in a place, built up over many years (e.g. &quot;the Pacific has a wet season and a dry season&quot;).</li><li>Real example: in much of the South Pacific, cyclone season runs roughly from November to April. A voyager who understood climate would avoid setting out during this period, even on a calm day — because they understood the bigger seasonal pattern, not just that day's conditions.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to know both the weather AND the climate before setting out on a long voyage?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sorting Weather from Climate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, race to physically sort scattered statement/picture cards into two zones — Weather (right now) and Climate (the long-term pattern). Example cards: &quot;It rained today&quot; (weather), &quot;December to March is generally hot and wet in the tropical Pacific&quot; (climate), &quot;There are strong winds this afternoon&quot; (weather).</li><li>Discuss as a class once sorted: what real decisions would each type of information help a voyager make?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Real Sky (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside (or to a window) for a genuine sky and wind observation using a simple pinwheel or paper streamer. Record wind direction, cloud type, and general conditions.</li><li>Teacher Input (brief, simplified): trade winds in the tropical Pacific blow fairly steadily from the east, because warm air rising near the equator and sinking further north and south creates a repeating pattern of wind. Voyagers used this knowledge to plan routes that worked with the wind, not against it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Based on today's real observation, write one sentence: would a voyager be safe to set sail today? Justify your answer using something you actually observed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The voyagers read the sky like a letter written by God. They had no instruments — only attention, and the sky was enough. The same heavens that guided their canoe have always been declaring God's glory.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The heavens declare the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands.&quot; — <em>Psalm 19:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning to read God's creation helps us know the Creator who designed and sustains it all.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 19 says the sky 'proclaims' God's work without any words. What do you think the sky is saying?</li><li>The voyagers trusted the sky with their lives. What does it take to trust something that completely?</li><li>What part of creation speaks most powerfully to you about God's creativity?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weather/climate sorting cards</li><li>Pinwheels or paper streamers for outdoor wind test</li><li>Simple cloud-type reference chart</li><li>Paper for voyage forecast logs</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Weather vs climate explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;weather vs climate explained for kids&quot;. A clear, age-appropriate explanation of the core science concept for this lesson.</li><li><em>What are trade winds?</em> — search: &quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;. Gives students a simplified but accurate picture of the real wind pattern referenced in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Voyager Reading the Sky</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene of a voyager studying the sky and sea before deciding whether to set sail.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include at least one weather clue (clouds, wind, waves) in your drawing.</li><li>Add a thought bubble showing what the voyager is deciding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What clue in your drawing would matter most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Forecast Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;ship's log&quot; entry in role as a voyager studying today's real sky and wind.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe what you actually observed outside today.</li><li>State your decision: safe to sail, or not — and why.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How confident would you really be making this decision with no weather app?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Simple Weather-Tracking Sheet</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper tracking sheet that records the weather for 5 days (could continue beyond today's lesson).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Record wind, cloud, and temperature each day.</li><li>After 5 days, write one sentence: is this weather, or is it starting to show a climate pattern?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many days of data do you think you'd need before you could call something a &quot;pattern&quot; rather than just &quot;today's weather&quot;?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reading Wind and Sky</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about trade winds or weather basics.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">why winds blow in consistent patterns, and how that might help (or hinder) a sea voyage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was one fact that would have genuinely helped a real voyager?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 4: The Floating Trial":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHY THINGS FLOAT — Buoyancy and the First Voyaging Vessels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how materials are selected for use based on their physical and functional properties.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are products or systems developed to meet a need.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why some objects float and others sink.</li><li>We are learning how shape and material affect a vessel's ability to carry weight safely.</li><li>I can design, test, and improve a simple floating vessel using real engineering reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the build-and-test challenge, including the redesign stage. Review of written design explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We now know how big the ocean is, and how to read the sky. But none of it matters if the vessel itself doesn't survive the water. Today, we become engineers.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why does a heavy ship made of solid metal float, while a small stone sinks straight to the bottom?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Science of Floating (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: An object floats when it pushes aside (displaces) enough water to support its own weight. This is sometimes called Archimedes' Principle, named after the ancient Greek scientist who first explained it. Shape matters hugely: a solid lump of clay sinks, but the same amount of clay shaped into a wide, hollow bowl floats — because the bowl shape displaces far more water relative to its weight.</li><li>Introduce density simply: density compares how much something weighs for its size. Denser materials need a shape that displaces more water in order to float.</li><li>Demonstrate: drop a solid ball of clay/foil into water (sinks), then reshape the same material into a wide, shallow bowl shape and try again (floats).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Build and Test (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, using a limited material kit (foil, a small amount of clay, straws, tape), build a small vessel that must float AND carry a 'cargo' (a marble or small weight) across a tub of water without sinking.</li><li>Test each design live in front of the class.</li><li>Key Question during testing: &quot;What made some designs more stable than others?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Redesign and Improve (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: After the first test, each team gets one chance to redesign and improve their vessel based on what failed the first time — this mirrors real engineering, where the first attempt is rarely the final design.</li><li>Discuss: what specific change did you make, and why did you expect it to help?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, why shape affects whether something floats.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The waka was one of the most sophisticated vessels ever built by human hands — designed using nothing but observation, experience, and inherited wisdom. God gives wisdom to those who seek it, and the waka builders are evidence of that extraordinary gift.</li><li>Verse: &quot;By wisdom a house is built, and through understanding it is established; through knowledge its rooms are filled with rare and beautiful treasures.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 24:3-4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Using our God-given skills to build something that serves and carries others is at the heart of Manaaki — faithful service with our gifts.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The waka builders had no computers or modern tools — how do you think God gave them the wisdom to build something so advanced?</li><li>If 'by wisdom a house is built,' what kind of wisdom do you think you're building right now in school that will serve others later?</li><li>How might God want you to use your particular skills to serve your community?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Foil, clay/playdough, straws, tape (per team)</li><li>Tubs or basins of water</li><li>Marbles or small weights for &quot;cargo&quot;</li><li>Paper for design sketches/explanations</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Archimedes' Principle for kids</em> — search: &quot;Archimedes principle buoyancy explained for kids&quot;. Explains the real scientific principle behind today's lesson in an age-appropriate way.</li><li><em>Why do boats float? Density explained</em> — search: &quot;why do boats float density explained kids&quot;. Reinforces the link between density, shape, and floating ahead of the hands-on build.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Vessel Blueprint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a labelled &quot;blueprint&quot; style drawing of your ideal voyaging vessel.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw your vessel from the side and from above.</li><li>Label at least 3 design features and explain what each one does.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which feature do you think matters most for surviving open ocean, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: An Engineer's Test Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short report, in role as the engineer who built today's vessel, describing the test, what failed, and what you changed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe the first design and what happened when tested.</li><li>Describe your redesign and why you made that choice.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is this similar to how real engineers work?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use Canva or a similar tool to create a digital poster of your vessel design, labelling key features.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add an image or drawing of your vessel.</li><li>Label at least 3 features with short explanations.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would you change if you had to build it twice as big?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Why Boats Float</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining buoyancy or boat design.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why do boats float density explained kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">the real scientific reason objects float, and how shape changes this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video change how you'd design your next vessel? How?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 5: The First Quilt Square":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE FOLD-AND-CUT TECHNIQUE — Beginning a Pacific-Inspired Quilt</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore a variety of materials and tools, and discover elements and techniques.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from the past and recognise that this reflects the technology and aesthetics of cultures and times.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning a fold-and-cut technique that produces symmetric, radiating patterns similar to traditional Pacific design.</li><li>We are learning that Pacific art often carries real cultural meaning, not just decoration.</li><li>I can create one considered, technique-based square as part of a small-group collaborative quilt.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of technique use, care taken with the fold-and-cut process, and group collaboration while assembling the quilt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Every great journey deserves to be remembered — not just in words, but in something we can see. This term, instead of one giant class artwork, each of you will create your own small square every Friday, inspired by a real Pacific design tradition. Square by square, your small group's squares will be glued together into your own quilt, growing week by week.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Before we begin, it's worth knowing: across the real Pacific, art was never just decoration. Patterns and motifs often carried real meaning — telling stories of family, place, and belief. Today's square is inspired by the very first clue we found this term: Lapita pottery.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Pacific Art Traditions (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce tapa cloth (also called ngatu in Tonga, siapo in Samoa) — a real, traditional Pacific art form made by beating the inner bark of the paper mulberry tree into a flat cloth, then decorating it with natural dyes using stencils and repeating patterns.</li><li>Explain that these patterns were not random — they often represented genealogy, status, or stories specific to a family or village, similar in purpose to the carved patterns in whakairo (Māori carving) or ta moko (Māori tattoo design).</li><li>Show 2–3 real examples of tapa/ngatu/siapo patterns, and the dentate-stamped (dotted/lined) Lapita pottery style referenced in Monday's trial, if available.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teaching the Fold-and-Cut Technique (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher demonstrates the technique step by step: 1) Trim an A4 sheet into a square. 2) Fold the square in half diagonally to form a triangle. 3) Fold that triangle in half again to form a smaller triangle. 4) Cut small notches, triangles, or dots into the folded edges — NOT all the way across, leaving the centre fold intact. 5) Carefully unfold to reveal a symmetric, radiating pattern.</li><li>This week's pattern inspiration: dots and short lines, echoing the dentate-stamped Lapita pottery pattern from Monday's trial.</li><li>Safety note: model safe scissor use on folded paper (multiple layers can be thicker to cut) before students begin independently.</li><li>Practise the fold and one or two test cuts together on scrap paper first, so students see the unfolded result before committing to their real square.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Making and Assembling the Quilt Squares (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: split the class into small groups (4–5 students each). Each student creates their own square using today's technique and pattern inspiration.</li><li>Each group glues their finished squares onto a shared backing sheet (poster paper or card), beginning that group's own quilt — kept small and manageable rather than one large class piece.</li><li>Encourage genuine collaboration within each group — comparing techniques, helping each other line up cuts, discussing what to do with hidden secret cuts (see Quest below).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is one real purpose Pacific art traditions like tapa cloth served, beyond just looking decorative?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Lapita pottery makers were artists — every dot and line a deliberate choice. When we create art, we are doing something deeply human: making things that reflect the imagination of the God who made us in His image.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we are God's handiwork, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance for us to do.&quot; — <em>Ephesians 2:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every creative act done with care and intention brings a little of God's Kingdom creativity into the world.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If we are 'God's handiwork,' what does that tell us about how God feels about the things we make?</li><li>The Lapita people put their patterns on pottery — you put yours on paper. Why does it matter HOW we do our creative work?</li><li>How can making art be a way of bringing glory to God?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (plus spares for mistakes)</li><li>Safety scissors</li><li>Glue and a backing sheet (poster paper or card) per small group</li><li>Reference images of tapa/ngatu/siapo cloth and Lapita pottery patterns</li><li>Scrap paper for technique practice</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How tapa cloth is made</em> — search: &quot;tapa cloth ngatu making process&quot;. Shows the real traditional technique referenced in this lesson, grounding the art activity in genuine cultural practice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create Your First Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — create your fold-and-cut square and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your square's pattern represent, and what did you hide as your &quot;secret&quot; cut?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Describe a Pacific Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research (or use today's examples) one real tapa/ngatu/siapo pattern and write a short description of what it might represent.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might it matter that a pattern has real meaning, rather than being purely decorative?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Pattern Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to design your own repeating pattern inspired by tapa cloth or Lapita pottery geometry.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of real tapa cloth design did you borrow, and what part did you make your own?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Making Tapa Cloth</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on how tapa cloth is traditionally made.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;tapa cloth ngatu making process&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the process surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 6: Why Leave at All?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PUSH AND PULL — Why Real Voyagers Risked Everything to Leave</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how people's lives have been shaped by significant events, and how they responded to these events.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people make decisions about resources and the consequences of these decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real reasons historians believe ancient Pacific peoples chose to set out on dangerous ocean voyages.</li><li>We are learning to tell the difference between a &quot;push&quot; factor and a &quot;pull&quot; factor.</li><li>I can weigh up evidence and form my own reasoned opinion about a historical decision.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the village council debate and class vote. Review of student tokens and justification for what they would have carried.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week, we discovered who the first Pacific voyagers were and how big the ocean ahead of them truly was. But here's the real question historians still debate: why would anyone choose to leave everything they knew, to cross an ocean that had already swallowed others who tried?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if your home was safe, would you ever choose to leave it for somewhere you'd never seen?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is not just an old question — it's one people still face today, all over the world. Understanding it helps us understand both the past and the present.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Push Factors (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Historians and anthropologists believe several real pressures pushed people to leave Pacific islands over time. Islands have limited land and limited fresh water — as populations grew over generations, food and resources could become stretched thin.</li><li>Pacific islands were also vulnerable to natural pressures like drought cycles and crop failures, which could make staying far riskier than it first seems.</li><li>Social pressures mattered too: historians believe disputes over land, status, or leadership sometimes led groups to leave rather than stay and fight.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might 'staying' sometimes have been the more dangerous choice, even though leaving meant crossing open ocean?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Pull Factors (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: It wasn't only danger driving people away — there were also real reasons to be drawn toward the unknown. Voyagers gained enormous prestige (sometimes called mana) from successfully finding and settling new land — discovery itself was a great achievement, not just a desperate escape.</li><li>A genuinely interesting real theory: some researchers believe voyagers may have used the seasonal migration of certain birds — like the long-tailed cuckoo, which migrates between the Pacific Islands and areas far to the south — as a clue that more land existed beyond the horizon.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might curiosity and the chance for prestige have been just as powerful a reason to leave as fear or hardship?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Village Council Debate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Split the class into two camps — a 'Stay or Go' village council, in role as elders. Deal each camp evidence cards covering real push factors (overcrowding, drought, conflict) and real pull factors (prestige, curiosity, bird migration clues, rumours of other islands).</li><li>Each camp builds its case using only its dealt cards, then holds a genuine stand-up debate in role.</li><li>After the debate, the whole class votes in role as the village: Go, or Stay? Discuss as a class: was this an easy decision, or a genuinely difficult one?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence identifying ONE real push factor and ONE real pull factor discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's community made one of the hardest decisions a human can make: to leave everything familiar for somewhere unknown. Abraham made the same choice — and both stories show us that God sometimes calls us to trust before we can see.</li><li>Verse: &quot;By faith Abraham, when called to go to a place he would later receive as his inheritance, obeyed and went, even though he did not know where he was going.&quot; — <em>Hebrews 11:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Leaving what is familiar for the sake of those you love — your children's future, your family's flourishing — is one of the deepest expressions of love.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Abraham left without knowing where he was going. Have you ever had to trust someone completely without being able to see the outcome?</li><li>The voyagers left because they loved their families enough to give them a better future. What do you love enough to make a hard sacrifice for?</li><li>What does it feel like to know that God sometimes calls people to go without knowing the destination?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Evidence cards: push factors (overcrowding, drought, conflict) and pull factors (prestige, curiosity, bird migration, rumour of land)</li><li>Two clearly marked debate &quot;camps&quot; / spaces in the room</li><li>Small paper/token-making materials</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why did Polynesians migrate?</em> — search: &quot;why did Polynesians migrate across the Pacific&quot;. Provides a real, age-appropriate overview of the genuine historical theories behind Pacific migration.</li><li><em>How birds may have guided Pacific voyagers</em> — search: &quot;birds guiding Polynesian navigation migration clues&quot;. Introduces the real and genuinely interesting theory about bird migration as a navigation clue.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Token of What Mattered Most</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a small drawn or crafted token representing the one thing a real voyager might have chosen to bring with them, leaving everything else behind.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Decide what your token represents (a tool, a memory, a family object).</li><li>Create it on paper or with simple materials.</li><li>Write a one-sentence caption explaining why it mattered enough to bring.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your choice say about what you value most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Elder's Decision</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short speech, in role as a village elder, either arguing to stay or to go, using at least one real push or pull factor from today's lesson.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose your position: Stay, or Go.</li><li>Use at least one real factor from class to support your argument.</li><li>End with a call to action for your &quot;village.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Was it harder to argue for staying or for going? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Push/Pull Factor Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital table or mind-map tool (Google Slides, Canva) to sort today's push and pull factors into two clear categories.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List at least 3 push factors and 3 pull factors.</li><li>Add a short icon or image for each.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which single factor do you think mattered most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reasons for Migration</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on why Pacific peoples migrated.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why did Polynesians migrate across the Pacific&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">What to Watch For</th><td class=\"lt-td\">any push factors and pull factors mentioned.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video mention any factor we didn't discuss in class?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 7: The Ocean's Roads":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">OCEAN CURRENTS — The Pacific's Hidden Highways</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how natural features influence the ways people interact with an environment.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how places are connected to other places through movement of people, goods, and information.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what ocean currents are and how they move across the Pacific.</li><li>We are learning how real voyagers used currents to travel further with less effort.</li><li>I can explain why understanding a current is different from understanding the wind.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the floor-map current activity and the class discussion on current vs. wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We know voyagers had to cross enormous distances. But here's something many people don't realise: the ocean itself has moving rivers within it, called currents — and a clever voyager could use them like a highway, travelling faster with far less effort.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you dropped a bottle in the ocean today, do you think it would stay in one place, or travel somewhere completely different?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Are Ocean Currents? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Ocean currents are large, fairly consistent streams of moving water within the ocean, caused mainly by wind patterns, the rotation of the Earth, and differences in water temperature and saltiness (density).</li><li>In the Pacific, several major currents move in fairly predictable patterns — including the South Equatorial Current and North Equatorial Current near the equator, which generally flow from east to west, and the Equatorial Countercurrent running between them, flowing the opposite way.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If a current generally flows one direction, how might that help a voyager — and how might it also be a problem on the way home?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Currents vs. Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: It's an easy mix-up — wind moves the air above the water, while currents move the water itself. A skilled voyager needed to understand both separately, because a current could carry a vessel one direction even while the wind pushed the sail another.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to plan around BOTH the current and the wind, not just one or the other?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Mapping the Ocean's Roads (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: On the large floor/wall Pacific map, lay down ribbon or string to represent major current 'roads.' In teams, move a waka token from a starting island to a destination, where landing on a current allows extra movement (simple board-game style mechanic).</li><li>Discuss after playing: which team reached their destination fastest, and why?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining the difference between an ocean current and the wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The ocean currents are invisible roads that have been there since creation — God's hidden architecture written into the sea. The Psalmist says sailors who go out on the deep see God's wonderful deeds. The voyagers saw them every day.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Some went out on the sea in ships, trading on the mighty waters. They saw the works of the LORD, his wonderful deeds in the deep.&quot; — <em>Psalm 107:23-24 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that God designed the currents and created the sea helps us understand His creation with wonder and gratitude.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalm says sailors 'saw the works of the LORD' on the ocean. What do you think they saw?</li><li>The ocean currents are invisible but were always there — what other things has God made that are invisible but completely real?</li><li>How might looking at the natural world more closely change the way you pray or think about God?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Ribbon or string to mark current &quot;roads&quot;</li><li>Waka tokens for the movement game</li><li>Dice or a simple spinner for movement (optional)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How ocean currents work</em> — search: &quot;how ocean currents work explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind ocean currents.</li><li><em>Pacific Ocean currents map</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Ocean currents map explained&quot;. Shows the real major current systems referenced in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Ocean's Roads</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the Pacific Ocean with its major currents shown as flowing, decorated &quot;roads&quot; of water.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a simple Pacific outline.</li><li>Add at least 2 current &quot;roads&quot; with arrows showing direction.</li><li>Decorate each current with a unique colour or pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose the colours/patterns you used for each current?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Message in a Bottle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story or diary entry, in role as a message in a bottle, describing the journey you might take if dropped into a real Pacific current.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did thinking about a current's direction shape your story?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Animate a Current Route</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple slideshow tool (Google Slides, Canva) to create a 3–4 slide &quot;animation&quot; showing a waka travelling along a current from one island to another.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you have to think about to make the journey look realistic?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ocean Currents</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining ocean currents.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how ocean currents work explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world example (e.g. floating debris, temperature) did the video use to show currents are real?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 8: Where Wind Comes From":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TRADE WINDS — Why the Sky Pushes the Same Way, Again and Again</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring observations and ideas, and comparing these with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why wind forms in the first place.</li><li>We are learning what trade winds are and why they blow in consistent patterns in the tropical Pacific.</li><li>I can use a simple model to explain a real weather pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the warm/cold air demonstration and outdoor wind test. Review of students' Wind Wager predictions once checked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how ocean currents move like rivers within the sea. Today, we look up — because the sky has its own version of the same thing: winds that blow in steady, predictable patterns, called trade winds.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why do you think wind happens at all? What's actually moving, and why?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Wind Happens (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Wind is simply air moving from an area of higher pressure to an area of lower pressure. Near the equator, the sun heats the air strongly, causing it to rise. As this warm air rises, cooler air rushes in from further north and south to take its place — and that moving air is what we feel as wind.</li><li>Demonstration: use warm and cold coloured water in a clear container (warm rises, cold sinks) to visually model the same principle that drives this air movement, or have students physically act out 'warm air rising' and 'cool air rushing in.'</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Are Trade Winds? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Because the Earth is constantly spinning, this rising-and-sinking air pattern doesn't move in a straight line — it curves. This bending effect (sometimes called the Coriolis effect) is part of why trade winds blow fairly consistently from the east in much of the tropical Pacific.</li><li>These trade winds were hugely important to real voyagers: understanding which way they reliably blew helped voyagers plan routes that worked with the wind rather than constantly fighting against it.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If trade winds usually blow one direction, what would a voyager need to think about for the journey home?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Real Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside for a genuine wind observation using a pinwheel or paper streamer. Record the wind's direction and strength.</li><li>Activity: In groups, predict tomorrow's wind direction based on today's pattern, and log it visibly on a 'Wind Wager' board to check later.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, why wind happens.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus used the wind to describe the Holy Spirit — mysterious, powerful, impossible to fully control or predict, yet clearly real and present. The voyagers knew this feeling every day: the wind was their engine, their partner, and sometimes their greatest danger.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The wind blows wherever it pleases. You hear its sound, but you cannot tell where it comes from or where it is going. So it is with everyone born of the Spirit.&quot; — <em>John 3:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding the science of wind deepens our knowledge of the creation — and connects us to the Creator whose Spirit moves like the wind.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus compared the Holy Spirit to the wind. What are some things they have in common?</li><li>The voyagers depended on the wind for their survival. What does it mean to depend on something you can't see or fully control?</li><li>How do you recognise the Holy Spirit working in your life?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clear container, warm and cold coloured water</li><li>Pinwheels or paper streamers</li><li>Wind Wager prediction board</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What causes wind?</em> — search: &quot;what causes wind explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the basic science behind wind formation.</li><li><em>What are trade winds?</em> — search: &quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;. Reinforces the specific real-world wind pattern central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Wind Cycle</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled diagram showing warm air rising near the equator and cooler air rushing in to replace it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the equator and the rising warm air.</li><li>Draw cooler air moving in from above and below.</li><li>Label each part simply.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest part of the cycle to draw clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Wind Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry, in role as a voyager, describing how today's real wind would affect your plans to sail.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Would you have set sail today, based on the real wind you observed?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Wind Direction Tracker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper tracker recording wind direction over several days (can continue beyond today).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">After a few days, does the wind seem random, or is there a pattern forming?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Trade Winds</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about trade winds or what causes wind.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;trade winds explained simple&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact would have mattered most to a real voyager planning a long trip?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 9: The Double-Hulled Secret":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE WAKA HOURUA — Real Engineering Behind the Voyaging Canoe</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how the properties of materials and structures affect the performance of a product.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are evaluated for fitness for purpose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a waka hourua (double-hulled canoe) is and why its design was so effective.</li><li>We are learning how stability and balance relate to vessel design.</li><li>I can test and explain a real engineering advantage using evidence from my own model.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the comparative float test between single- and double-hulled designs. Review of written engineering explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week we built a vessel that could float and carry cargo. But real Pacific voyagers needed something far more capable for an ocean crossing — a vessel that could stay upright in rough seas for weeks at a time. Today we explore one of the most important real inventions of Pacific voyaging: the waka hourua, the double-hulled canoe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why might TWO connected hulls be far more stable than just one?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Waka Hourua? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A waka hourua is a voyaging canoe made of two hulls, lashed together with crossbeams (sometimes called kiato), with a deck built across the top. This design gave voyagers a much wider, more stable base than a single-hulled canoe, while still being light enough to paddle and sail efficiently.</li><li>Real-world example: in 1976, a reconstructed double-hulled voyaging canoe named Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional, instrument-free wayfinding — successfully proving that this ancient design and navigation method genuinely worked across a real, modern ocean crossing.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think building TWO smaller hulls, instead of one giant hull, was a smarter engineering choice?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Science of Stability (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A wider base resists tipping more than a narrow one, because the vessel's weight is spread across a larger area, making it much harder for a wave to roll it over. This is the same basic principle that makes a wide-based table sturdier than a narrow one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Build and Compare (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, build a single-hull vessel and a double-hull vessel (two connected hulls) using the same limited materials (straws, foil, card). Test both against a simulated 'wave' (gently rocking the tub, or pouring water past it) to compare stability.</li><li>Discuss: using your own test results, explain in your own words why the double-hulled design resisted tipping better.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, using your own test results, why a double-hulled design is more stable than a single-hulled one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Every lashing on a waka, every curve of the hull, required accumulated wisdom passed down through generations. This is the kind of wisdom Proverbs describes — practical, careful, grounded in experience. God values and gives this kind of knowledge.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Blessed are those who find wisdom, those who gain understanding, for she is more profitable than silver and yields better returns than gold.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 3:13-14 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Developing skill and applying it carefully to build something that serves others faithfully is at the heart of Manaaki.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs says wisdom is worth more than silver or gold — do you believe that? Why or why not?</li><li>How is the wisdom to build something physical similar to the wisdom needed to make good decisions in life?</li><li>What skill are you developing right now that you might one day use to serve others?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Straws, foil, card, tape (per team)</li><li>Tubs or basins of water</li><li>Paper for written explanations</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is a waka hourua?</em> — search: &quot;waka hourua double-hulled voyaging canoe explained&quot;. Provides real visual and factual context for the vessel design studied in this lesson.</li><li><em>Hōkūleʻa voyaging canoe story</em> — search: &quot;Hokulea voyaging canoe Hawaii Tahiti traditional navigation&quot;. Connects the lesson to a genuine, well-documented modern voyage that proved traditional design and navigation worked.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Labelled Waka Hourua</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a detailed, labelled drawing of a waka hourua, showing both hulls, the connecting crossbeams, and the deck.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the vessel from above and from the side.</li><li>Label at least 4 parts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which labelled part do you think mattered most for surviving a long voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Hōkūleʻa Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Research (or use today's video) the real story of the Hōkūleʻa voyage and write a short recount of what happened and why it mattered.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this voyage was considered such a big deal when it happened?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Stability Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital chart or table comparing your single-hull and double-hull test results (e.g. how many &quot;waves&quot; each survived before tipping).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did your results match what you expected before testing?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Waka Hourua</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about waka hourua or the Hōkūleʻa voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;waka hourua double-hulled voyaging canoe explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real detail from the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 10: Weaving the Sail":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PAPER STRIP WEAVING — Pandanus Sail Patterns in a Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore a variety of materials and processes, discovering how they can be used to make art works.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: share the ideas, feelings, and stories communicated by their own and others' art works.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pacific sail-weaving uses the same over-under rhythm as all weaving traditions worldwide.</li><li>We are practising the over-under weaving technique to create a geometric woven grid on a square.</li><li>I can explain how my woven square connects to the voyage story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed woven square and written reflection connecting weaving to the voyage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show a photo of a real pandanus woven sail panel. Ask: &quot;How was this made — not sewn, not glued. What do you notice?&quot; After responses, introduce the over-under weaving technique. Roa, Tama's mother, is a weaver — today students work as she does.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Preparing the Materials (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each student takes one A4 square base sheet and a second sheet to cut into strips approx 1–1.5cm wide.</li><li>Teacher demonstrates: make evenly-spaced slits across the base sheet stopping 2cm from each edge to create the warp.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Weaving the Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students weave their strips over-and-under through the slits in alternating rows.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Keep your strips tight and straight — a sail that sags won't catch the wind.&quot;</li><li>When complete, tuck or tape the ends on the back. The result is a tight grid of horizontal and vertical strips.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Quilt Assembly (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write 2–3 sentences: what does the over-under rhythm of weaving remind you of from this week's voyage? How is patience in weaving similar to patience at sea?</li><li>Glue the woven square to the group's quilt backing, next to Week 1's square.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One sentence: what would break down first on a real woven sail — and what would that mean for the voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Every strand in a woven sail depends on every other strand — pull one out and the whole thing weakens. A sail is a picture of community: each person essential, each contribution holding the rest together. God designed us to work this way.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Two are better than one, because they have a good return for their labour: if either of them falls down, one can help the other up.&quot; — <em>Ecclesiastes 4:9-10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully contributing your part to a shared work — like a strand in a woven sail — is how we serve together in God's way.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>When you wove your paper strips, what happened if one strand went wrong? How is that like a community or team?</li><li>'Two are better than one' — what's something you've achieved with others that you could never have done alone?</li><li>How does working faithfully on something small — like a single woven strip — contribute to something much bigger?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper squares (2 per student)</li><li>Rulers for cutting straight strips</li><li>Tape or glue for securing ends</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Traditional Pacific pandanus weaving</em> — search: &quot;Pacific pandanus weaving traditional technique mat sail&quot;. Shows the real material and technique students are referencing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Weave Your Pandanus Sail Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — weave your paper strip square and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how the weaving held itself together?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Roa's Hands</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short scene (80–120 words) from Roa's perspective as she weaves a sail panel the night before departure.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What details about the weaving process made the scene feel real?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Weave Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a grid-based drawing tool to design a woven pattern digitally, alternating two colours in the over-under sequence.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was different about planning a weave on screen versus by hand?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Pacific Weaving Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Pacific weaving traditions.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific pandanus weaving traditional technique mat sail&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the weaver's hands do that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 11: Leaving Sight of Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COURAGE AND PROVISIONS — What It Really Took to Cross the Ocean</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate the relationship between people and significant historical events, and how people responded.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people's wellbeing is connected to their access to resources.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what real Pacific voyaging canoes carried, and why those choices mattered for survival.</li><li>We are learning to understand the real emotional and physical challenge of losing sight of land.</li><li>I can connect a historical moment to a real human experience of courage.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the 'last sight of land' reflection and the trust walk. Review of students' captain's orders.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For two weeks we've prepared — we know the scale of the ocean, the currents, the winds, and the double-hulled vessel strong enough to survive it. Today we face the moment historians believe was often the hardest of the entire voyage: the last sight of land disappearing behind you.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think would be harder — the physical danger of the ocean, or the feeling of having no land in sight for weeks at a time?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What a Real Voyaging Canoe Carried (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Voyaging canoes weren't just carrying people — historians and archaeologists believe they carried entire futures. Real voyaging canoes are believed to have carried food crops like kūmara (sweet potato) and other plants, intended to be planted at any new land found, alongside dried food and water stored in gourds or coconut shells for the journey itself.</li><li>Some voyages are believed to have also carried animals such as dogs, pigs, or chickens — meaning a successful voyage wasn't just about survival at sea, but about being able to start an entirely new life upon arrival.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If you could only bring a limited number of things to start a brand-new life somewhere unknown, what would actually be worth the space on board?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Challenge of Losing Sight of Land (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Historians and psychologists studying voyaging cultures note that losing sight of land was both a practical and an emotional turning point — practically, because all navigation now depended entirely on sky, sea and memory; emotionally, because there was no longer any visible link back to everything familiar.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a voyage's danger have been as much about the mind as it was about the body?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Stepping Into the Moment (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Quietly narrate the last sight of land disappearing behind the voyage. In pairs, students share (low-stakes) a time they had to be brave doing something unfamiliar.</li><li>Activity: Run a blindfolded 'trust walk' in pairs across a short, safe course, mirroring the trust a voyager had to place in their crew and their preparation once land was gone.</li><li>Activity: In small groups, write a short 'captain's order' — a sentence a voyage leader might say to keep the crew's courage up at this exact moment.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is ONE item historians believe real voyaging canoes carried, and why would it have mattered?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The moment land disappears behind you is one of the most frightening — and most freeing — moments of any voyage. Tama's hand is shaking as he writes. But God's promise in Jeremiah was written for exactly this moment: a future we can't see yet, held by a God who can.</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;For I know the plans I have for you,&quot; declares the LORD, &quot;plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.&quot;&quot; — <em>Jeremiah 29:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — God's love for us is shown in the plans He holds for our future — even when we cannot see the destination from where we stand.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Tama can't see land behind him or land ahead. Have you ever been in a situation where you couldn't see the way forward? What helped?</li><li>God says His plans are 'to give you hope and a future.' How does it change things to know that Someone knows what's ahead, even when you don't?</li><li>How do you hold on to hope when you're in the 'middle of the ocean' — far from what was familiar and not yet at what's next?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blindfolds for the trust walk</li><li>A short, clear, safe course for the activity</li><li>Paper for captain's orders</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What did voyaging canoes carry?</em> — search: &quot;what did Polynesian voyaging canoes carry provisions&quot;. Provides real, age-appropriate detail on what voyaging canoes are believed to have carried.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Loaded Canoe</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a cross-section or &quot;cutaway&quot; view of a voyaging canoe, showing what real historians believe it would have carried.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include people, food crops, water storage, and one animal.</li><li>Label each item.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how much had to be planned for, beyond just the people?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Last Sight of Land</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short personal reflection, in role as a voyager, describing the exact moment land disappears from view.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What emotion did you choose to focus on, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Provisions Packing List</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital checklist or table of everything a real voyaging canoe is believed to have carried, with a one-line reason for each item.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If you had to remove one item to save space, which would you cut, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Voyaging Provisions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on what voyaging canoes carried.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;what did Polynesian voyaging canoes carry provisions&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What item did the video mention that you hadn't thought of?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 12: What the Ocean's Surface Tells You":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WAVE PILOTING — Reading the Real Signs of Land</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people use their understanding of natural features and patterns to make decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning real techniques voyagers used to detect land long before it was visible.</li><li>We are learning how swells, birds, and floating debris each provided different real clues.</li><li>I can match a type of evidence to the conclusion it supports.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the detective-style clue-matching activity and the &quot;report to the captain&quot; presentations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we faced the moment land disappeared. Today, we learn one of the most genuinely impressive real skills in human history: how voyagers could sense land was near long before they could see it, just by reading the ocean's surface.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: how do you think someone could possibly know land was nearby, without seeing it?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Wave Piloting — A Real, Documented Skill (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: In real Pacific navigation traditions — including ones still taught today in places like the Marshall Islands — skilled navigators learned to feel and read subtle changes in ocean swells. When swells hit an island, they bend and reflect off it in patterns that can be felt by an experienced navigator lying in the hull of a canoe, sometimes long before the island is visible.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might FEELING a wave pattern be a skill that takes years to learn, compared to simply looking for land?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Birds and Floating Clues (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Certain real seabirds, like frigatebirds, generally don't land on the ocean's surface — so spotting one far out at sea is a genuine clue that land must be within flying range. Many seabirds also reliably return to land at dusk, meaning their direction of flight at the end of the day could point a voyager toward an island.</li><li>Floating plant matter, particular leaves, or changes in water colour near reefs were also real, used clues that land was close.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Detective Challenge: Match the Clue (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Set up a detective-style station challenge with scattered clue cards showing real wayfinding signs (a bird far from land, drifting plant matter, a swell pattern bending around an unseen island). In teams, match each clue to what it would tell a real voyager.</li><li>Activity: Teams present their best clue-match as a short 'report to the captain' — what does the evidence suggest right now?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real clue voyagers used to detect land before seeing it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus challenged the Pharisees for being able to read the weather but unable to read what God was doing right in front of them. The voyagers were extraordinary readers of the ocean's signs. God invites us to be readers of His signs too — attentive, curious, and awake.</li><li>Verse: &quot;You know how to interpret the appearance of the sky, but you cannot interpret the signs of the times.&quot; — <em>Matthew 16:3 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God means learning to read what He is doing in the world — in nature, in history, and in our own lives.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The voyagers read the ocean's surface for signs of land. What 'signs' in your daily life might God be using to say something to you?</li><li>Jesus said the Pharisees could read the sky but missed what God was doing. What might you be missing that God is doing right now?</li><li>How do you practise being more attentive to what God might be saying?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clue cards: bird behaviour, floating debris, swell patterns</li><li>Simple Pacific map for context</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Wave piloting — reading ocean swells</em> — search: &quot;wave piloting Marshall Islands navigation swells&quot;. Documents the real, still-taught navigation technique referenced in this lesson.</li><li><em>How birds helped Pacific navigators find land</em> — search: &quot;birds signs of land Pacific navigation&quot;. Reinforces the real bird-behaviour clues discussed in class.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Wave Pattern Bending Around an Island</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple diagram showing how ocean swells bend and reflect when they hit an island.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw an island and incoming straight swell lines.</li><li>Show how the lines bend around the island.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why would this pattern be detectable even before the island is visible?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Navigator's Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;report to the captain&quot; in role as a navigator who has just noticed a real clue (bird, debris, or swell pattern) suggesting land is near.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How confident would you really feel acting on just one clue?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Clue-Matching Digital Quiz</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital matching activity (Google Slides, Canva) pairing each real clue (bird, debris, swell) with what it tells a voyager.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which clue do you think would be hardest to learn to read well?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reading the Ocean</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on wave piloting or bird-based navigation.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;wave piloting Marshall Islands navigation swells&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of this skill most impressed you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 13: Cloud Code":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">READING CLOUDS — A Real Trick for Finding Islands Before You See Them</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate weather phenomena and how they affect people and the environment.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring observations and comparing these with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to identify basic cloud types and what each can indicate.</li><li>We are learning a real technique voyagers used: spotting a stationary cloud cap to find an island before seeing land.</li><li>I can make and justify a simple weather prediction using real observation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of outdoor cloud observation and the class's 'voyage forecast' predictions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how to read the sea's surface. Today we look up again — because clouds hold a genuinely clever real secret that helped voyagers find islands long before they could see them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: have you ever noticed a cloud that seems to just sit in one place, while others move past it?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Basic Cloud Types (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce three simple cloud types — cumulus (puffy, often fair weather), cumulonimbus (tall, dark, often storm-bearing), and cirrus (thin, wispy, high up, often signalling a change in weather coming).</li><li>Show real images of each type for students to identify.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Island Cloud Trick (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Here's a genuinely real and clever navigation trick — islands, especially ones with mountains, often cause a single cloud to form and sit almost stationary directly above them, because rising warm air off the land cools and condenses at a consistent point. A trained voyager could sometimes spot this 'island cloud' on the horizon well before the island itself came into view.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a cloud that doesn't move, while others around it drift past, be a genuinely useful clue?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Sky Observation (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Take the class outside to observe real clouds, identifying any of today's three types and noting whether any appear to be staying in one place rather than moving.</li><li>Activity: In groups, use today's observation to make and justify a real 'voyage forecast' prediction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining the real &quot;island cloud&quot; trick discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The cloud code the voyagers read was God's weather system — a complex, interconnected design that brings rain to mountains, signals danger to sailors, and tells stories to those who know how to look. Every cloud type is part of a system God designed and still maintains.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He covers the sky with clouds; he supplies the earth with rain and makes grass grow on the hills.&quot; — <em>Psalm 147:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Understanding how God's creation works — from weather patterns to cloud types — deepens our knowledge of the wise Creator behind it all.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 147 says God 'covers the sky with clouds' — does knowing God made the weather system change how you look at clouds? How?</li><li>Hina taught Tama to read clouds like a letter. What 'language' do you think God uses most often to communicate with you?</li><li>Why do you think God designed a world that rewards careful observation and patient learning?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reference images of cumulus, cumulonimbus, and cirrus clouds</li><li>Paper for voyage forecast logs</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Cloud types explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;cloud types explained for kids cumulus cirrus cumulonimbus&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate overview of the basic cloud types covered in this lesson.</li><li><em>How clouds reveal hidden islands</em> — search: &quot;how clouds form over islands navigation clue&quot;. Explains the real, documented phenomenon central to this lesson's key navigation trick.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate an Island Cloud</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an island with its real &quot;island cloud&quot; forming above it, while other clouds drift past in the background.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the island and its stationary cloud.</li><li>Draw 2–3 other clouds clearly moving in a different direction.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you do in your drawing to show one cloud is &quot;stuck&quot; while others move?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager Spots the Sign</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story moment, in role as a voyager, describing the instant you notice a stationary cloud and realise it might mean land.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you feel in that exact moment, after weeks without land?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Cloud Identification Guide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital reference guide (Canva, Google Slides) with an image and short description of each cloud type from today's lesson.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which cloud type do you think would be easiest to misidentify, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Clouds and Islands</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on cloud types or the island-cloud phenomenon.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how clouds form over islands navigation clue&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video would you tell a friend who didn't believe this trick was real?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 14: Catch the Wind":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE CRAB CLAW SAIL — A Real, Remarkably Efficient Design</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how the shape and properties of materials affect the performance of a product.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are evaluated and improved through testing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how sail shape and angle affect how efficiently a vessel catches wind.</li><li>We are learning about the real 'crab claw' sail design used across the Pacific.</li><li>I can test and improve a sail design using real engineering reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the sail-testing and redesign process. Review of written explanations connecting shape to performance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've built a stable double-hulled vessel and learned to read sea, sky, and clouds for clues. Today we focus on the part that actually moves the whole vessel forward: the sail. And it turns out, Pacific sailors developed one of the most efficient sail shapes in the whole of human sailing history.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: do you think a sail's shape actually matters, or is any large piece of material just as good at catching wind?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Crab Claw Sail (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Many traditional Pacific sailing vessels used a distinctive triangular sail shape, sometimes called a 'crab claw' sail because of its curved, claw-like silhouette. Modern sailing scientists studying traditional designs have found this shape to be genuinely, remarkably efficient at generating forward motion from the wind — among the most aerodynamically effective traditional sail shapes ever studied.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a society without modern science have still arrived at such an efficient design, just through generations of trial and observation?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Shape, Angle, and Wind (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A sail works by being angled so wind flowing across its surface creates forward force, similar to how an aeroplane wing generates lift. The exact angle and curve of a sail changes how efficiently it captures that force.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Test, Redesign, Improve (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, design and test a simple sail (paper or fabric on a small mast) attached to a model vessel, testing in front of a fan or moving air to find the most effective shape and angle.</li><li>Activity: After the first test, redesign and re-test at least once, just as real sail-makers improved their designs over generations.</li><li>Key Question during testing: &quot;What specifically changed between your first and second design, and why did you expect it to help?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining why sail shape and angle affect how well a sail catches wind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: A sail doesn't fight the wind — it trusts it. A sail trimmed correctly uses the wind's power to go places no amount of paddling could reach. Isaiah's image of soaring on wings is about the same kind of trust: not straining against the circumstances, but finding the right angle to let God's strength carry you.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Those who hope in the LORD will renew their strength. They will soar on wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not be faint.&quot; — <em>Isaiah 40:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — When we align our gifts with God's purposes — like a sail catching the wind — we have far greater impact than we could ever achieve alone.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A sail doesn't generate power — it harnesses what's already there. How might that be a picture of how God wants us to work?</li><li>'Soar on wings like eagles' — what does it feel like when everything is working the way it should, and you're moving with God's strength rather than just your own?</li><li>How do you 'trim your sail' — position yourself to catch God's strength rather than relying only on your own effort?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper or light fabric, small masts/sticks, tape</li><li>A fan or open space with moving air for testing</li><li>Model vessels from Week 1/2 (optional, can reuse)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The crab claw sail explained</em> — search: &quot;crab claw sail Pacific Polynesian efficient design&quot;. Documents the real, scientifically-studied sail design referenced in this lesson.</li><li><em>How sails work</em> — search: &quot;how sails work wind science explained&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the basic physics behind sailing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Decorated Crab Claw Sail</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled crab claw sail design, decorated with a pattern that represents your &quot;voyage&quot; so far.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the distinctive curved triangular shape.</li><li>Add a decorative pattern.</li><li>Label the parts that catch the wind.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this curved shape might work better than a flat rectangle?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Sail-Maker's Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short set of &quot;notes,&quot; in role as a real sail-maker, describing what you changed between your first and second sail design and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is this similar to how real inventors improve their designs over time?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Sail Design Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital chart comparing your two sail test results (e.g. speed, stability, how well it caught the wind).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did your redesign perform the way you expected?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Crab Claw Sail</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the crab claw sail or how sails work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;crab claw sail Pacific Polynesian efficient design&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how effective this traditional design really is?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 15: Reading the Patterns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KŌWHAIWHAI DESIGN STUDY — Wave Scrolls, Colour &amp; Meaning</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore the visual qualities of art works from a range of cultures and periods.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify and describe ways in which meanings are communicated through art works.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past and present cultures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to read and interpret kōwhaiwhai patterns as a visual language used by Māori artists.</li><li>We are understanding how colour (black, red/kōkōwhai, white) and repeating motifs create rhythm and meaning.</li><li>I can design my own wave-pattern panel inspired by kōwhaiwhai, and explain the choices I made.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of pattern study and student written reflection explaining motif choices and personal connection to the patterns.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Display the archival kōwhaiwhai reference image (Tikitiki Church, Arawa Rotorua, and Tikitiki panels). Give students 2 minutes to simply look — no talking — before asking: &quot;What do you notice about the colours? The shapes? Do you see any shapes repeating?&quot; Take 3–4 responses, then explain: &quot;These patterns are a language. Every scroll, every chevron tells a story about the ocean, about movement, about who these people were. Today we're going to learn to read that language — and then speak a little of it ourselves.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading the Patterns Together (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce the three key kōwhaiwhai patterns visible in the archival image — (1) the koru/scroll (a curling fern frond representing new life, growth, and the ocean returning on itself); (2) the chevron/arrow pointing forward (direction, courage, the voyage ahead); (3) the repeating band structure (the rhythm of the sea, one wave always following another).</li><li>Discuss: Māori artists traditionally used only three colours — black (charcoal), red/kōkōwhai (red ochre pigment), and white (pipe clay). Why might these specific colours have been chosen? What do they suggest? (Black: night sky, the deep ocean; Red: blood, life, fire, courage; White: the stars, seafoam, light.)</li><li>Point out: in the Arawa Rotorua panel (middle band), the scroll reverses direction as it repeats — ask students: what might that reversal represent? (The wave going out and coming back. The voyage leaving home and returning.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Guided Design Sketch (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: give each student a landscape strip of paper (roughly A5 size, or half an A4). Students choose ONE motif from the reference image to practise — either the scroll/koru or the chevron — and repeat it across the strip as a border pattern, using pencil first.</li><li>Teacher circulates: the key question to prompt is 'What does your pattern look like it's doing?' — is it moving, breathing, surging? Help students feel the rhythm rather than just copy mechanically.</li><li>Encourage students to vary the size of repeats slightly — Māori artists were not rigid; the pattern breathes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Adding Colour and Reflection (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: students colour their pattern using only the three traditional colours — black, red, and white — using felt tips, dye, or paint as available. Remind them: the background matters as much as the motif.</li><li>Activity: in their journal or on the back of the strip, students write 2–3 sentences: what pattern they chose, what it reminds them of from this week's voyage, and what they think the original artist might have felt making it.</li><li>Optional extension: students may add a personal touch — a shape or line that is theirs — while still honouring the pattern's structure.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Share one sentence with the class: &quot;My pattern is saying ____<strong> because </strong>____.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The kōwhaiwhai scroll is a pattern that keeps returning — the wave curls back on itself, the spiral never truly ends. Paul's words about Christ echo this rhythm: all things hold together in Him. Even when the wave breaks, even when the scroll reaches its edge, it begins again.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He is before all things, and in him all things hold together.&quot; — <em>Colossians 1:17 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Art that carries meaning — like the kōwhaiwhai scroll — brings God's Kingdom creativity into the world and points to the One who holds all things together.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The kōwhaiwhai wave reverses direction and returns — 'going out and coming back.' How might that be a picture of something in your own faith journey?</li><li>If 'in him all things hold together,' what does that mean for the parts of your life that feel like they might be falling apart?</li><li>How can the art you make today be an act of worship?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Printed or projected archival kōwhaiwhai reference image (Tikitiki Church / Arawa Rotorua panels)</li><li>A5 strips of white cartridge paper (or half A4), one per student</li><li>Pencils for sketching</li><li>Black, red, and white felt tips, paint, or dye</li><li>Student journals for written reflection</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Introduction to Kōwhaiwhai Patterns</em> — search: &quot;kowhaiwhai patterns Maori art meaning for kids&quot;. Provides accessible context for students on the meaning and tradition behind kōwhaiwhai, supporting the pattern-reading discussion at the start of the lesson.</li><li><em>Māori Rafter Painting Technique</em> — search: &quot;Maori rafter painting kowhaiwhai technique traditional&quot;. Shows how kōwhaiwhai patterns are applied in real wharenui contexts, helping students understand the patterns as living tradition rather than historical artefact.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Kōwhaiwhai Panel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — study the archival reference, choose your motif, and design your own kōwhaiwhai wave-pattern strip using the three traditional colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does your pattern look like it's doing — moving, surging, breathing? How did you make that happen?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Language of Patterns</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece (100–150 words) imagining you are the Māori artist who created the Arawa Rotorua panel. Why did you choose the scroll pattern? What were you trying to say about the ocean or the voyage?</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is designing a pattern similar to writing a sentence?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Kōwhaiwhai</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool to recreate one kōwhaiwhai motif digitally, then repeat it to form a border pattern. Use only black, red, and white.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to control digitally — the shape, the spacing, or the colour? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Kōwhaiwhai Meaning</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on kōwhaiwhai patterns and their meaning in Māori art.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;kowhaiwhai patterns Maori art meaning for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about what these patterns represent?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 16: Lost Without Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WAYFINDING — A Real Skill Trained Over a Lifetime</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how a significant person or group has influenced the course of events.</li><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the perspectives of people in the past were shaped by the time and place in which they lived.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what wayfinding really is, and why it took years to master.</li><li>We are learning about a real master navigator who helped revive this skill in modern times.</li><li>We are learning a genuinely real navigational idea called 'etak.'</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the memory-sequence game and class discussion. Review of students' written reflections.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Over the last three weeks, we've learned to read currents, wind, swells, birds, and clouds. Today we bring it all together under one real word: wayfinding. This wasn't a single trick — it was an entire skill, trained over years, sometimes decades, and the knowledge was almost lost in the 20th century before being deliberately revived.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: do you think you could memorise enough information to navigate across an entire ocean, with no instruments at all?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Master Navigator (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A real and important figure in this story is Mau Piailug, a master navigator from the Micronesian island of Satawal, born in 1932. He had learned traditional wayfinding from his elders using methods passed down for generations — at a time when this knowledge was disappearing across much of the Pacific.</li><li>In 1976, Mau Piailug navigated the double-hulled canoe Hōkūleʻa from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional wayfinding — no instruments at all — successfully proving the ancient method still worked, and helping spark a real revival of Pacific navigation traditions that continues today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think it mattered so much to prove this method still worked, rather than just writing down what was remembered about it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Navigational Idea: Etak (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce a genuinely fascinating real concept used by traditional Micronesian/Carolinian navigators called etak. Rather than thinking of the canoe as moving toward a fixed island, navigators using etak imagined the canoe as staying still, while islands 'moved' past it in relation to reference stars. This mental framework helped navigators track progress without any instruments.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might imagining yourself as 'staying still' actually make it easier to track a long journey, rather than imagining yourself constantly moving?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Training the Memory (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Run a memory-sequence game — show a sequence of symbols (representing a 'star path') briefly, then hide it. Teams must recall and recreate the sequence accurately, just as real wayfinders trained their memory over years through repetition.</li><li>Discuss: why might memorising something be safer at sea than relying on a written chart that could be lost or ruined by water?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: who was Mau Piailug, and why does his 1976 voyage matter to this story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After the storm, Tama doesn't know where he is. The stars are hidden. The ocean gives no clues. This is the feeling Psalm 119 was written for — the moment when you can't see the way forward and you need something beyond yourself to light the path. God's word is that lamp.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.&quot; — <em>Psalm 119:105 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God and His word gives us a light to navigate by, even when everything around us is dark and disorienting.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Have you ever been in a situation where you genuinely didn't know what to do next? What helped you find direction?</li><li>A lamp only lights 'your feet' and 'your path' — not the whole landscape. What does it mean to trust God one step at a time?</li><li>How do you practically use God's word as a 'lamp' in your daily life?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Symbol/sequence cards for the memory game</li><li>A simple timer for the memory challenge</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mau Piailug and the Hōkūleʻa voyage</em> — search: &quot;Mau Piailug master navigator Hokulea voyage&quot;. Documents the real historical figure and voyage central to this lesson.</li><li><em>What is etak navigation?</em> — search: &quot;etak navigation concept explained Micronesia&quot;. Explains the real, genuinely clever navigational concept introduced in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Mau Piailug's Voyage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene depicting the 1976 Hōkūleʻa voyage, showing the double-hulled canoe and the open ocean it crossed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include the canoe and a simple route from Hawaii to Tahiti.</li><li>Add a small label noting the real navigator's name.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this moment in history is worth illustrating and remembering?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Tribute to a Master Navigator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short tribute or biography paragraph about Mau Piailug and why his knowledge mattered.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real skill of his impressed you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build an Etak Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing/slides tool to create a diagram explaining the etak concept — the canoe staying &quot;still&quot; while islands move past it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did making the diagram change your understanding of the idea?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: A Real Master Navigator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Mau Piailug or the Hōkūleʻa voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Mau Piailug master navigator Hokulea voyage&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would you want to share with someone who'd never heard this story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 17: The Star Compass":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NAVIGATING BY STARS — Real Names, Real Sky Positions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people view and use the natural environment differently, including for navigation and orientation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a star compass is and how it works.</li><li>We are learning real Pacific names and meanings for key stars and constellations.</li><li>I can use a star compass to determine direction on a map.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the star compass navigation activity on the floor map.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we met a real navigator who used the stars to cross an entire ocean. Today, we build the actual tool in his mind: the star compass — and we learn some of the real star names that mattered to Pacific voyagers.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Star Compass? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A star compass is a mental (or sometimes physical) wheel of direction points, each linked to where a particular star or constellation reliably rises or sets on the horizon. Because stars rise and set in almost the same spot every night, a trained navigator could use them like a giant, glowing set of compass points.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Stars, Real Names (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce a few real, genuinely used star reference points from Pacific navigation traditions. Matariki (the Pleiades star cluster) is well known across the Pacific and in Māori tradition, often associated with the new year and remembrance. Te Matau a Māui (Maui's fishhook, part of the constellation Scorpius) is a star pattern named in Māori tradition after the legendary fishhook of Māui. The Southern Cross, known in Māori as Te Punga (the anchor), is another key reference point still used for finding south in the southern hemisphere sky today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might giving stars meaningful names — instead of just numbers or letters — have helped voyagers remember them more easily across generations?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building and Using a Star Compass (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Build a more detailed star compass wheel, marking key real stars/constellations (Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, Te Punga/Southern Cross) at points around it.</li><li>Activity: Use the star compass to 'navigate' a paper waka token across a star-marked floor map between islands, choosing direction based on real star positions rather than guessing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real star or constellation used in Pacific navigation, and what it's called.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The voyagers memorised hundreds of stars to navigate thousands of kilometres of open ocean. The same stars that Hina taught Tama to read are the 'work of God's fingers' — set in place deliberately, as a gift to navigators and a declaration of His greatness.</li><li>Verse: &quot;When I consider your heavens, the work of your fingers, the moon and the stars, which you have set in place, what is mankind that you are mindful of them, human beings that you care for them?&quot; — <em>Psalm 8:3-4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Looking at the stars and learning their names is an act of knowing God — who named every star and knows us each by name too.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalmist looks at the stars and asks 'who am I that God cares about me?' — do you ever feel that way? What do you do with that feeling?</li><li>God named every star — Matariki, Te Punga, the Southern Cross — and God knows your name. What does that tell you about how God sees you?</li><li>How does learning about the vastness of creation make you feel about God — and about yourself?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper, compasses (drawing), pencils for star compass craft</li><li>A star-marked floor/wall map for the navigation activity</li><li>Reference images of Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, and the Southern Cross if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is a star compass?</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian star compass navigation explained&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real tool central to this lesson.</li><li><em>Matariki and Māori star navigation</em> — search: &quot;Matariki Te Matau a Maui star navigation Maori&quot;. Introduces real, culturally significant star names relevant to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Real Star Names</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a night sky scene featuring Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, and Te Punga (the Southern Cross), labelled with their real names and meanings.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of these star names did you find most interesting, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Why Names Matter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph explaining why giving stars meaningful names (rather than just numbers) might have helped voyagers remember and use them.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Can you think of a modern example where a meaningful name helps you remember something better than a number would?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Star Compass</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool to build a digital star compass wheel, labelling at least 3 real star reference points.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest part of placing the points accurately?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Star Navigation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Polynesian star compass or Matariki.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian star compass navigation explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real star name or fact from the video stuck with you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 18: Why the Sky Changes":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE REAL REASON FOR SEASONS — It's Not About Distance from the Sun</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate the components of the solar system, including the Earth's place within it.</li><li>Build science knowledge by exploring and comparing observations with current scientific understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real scientific reason seasons happen — and addressing a common misconception.</li><li>We are learning why the night sky changes with the seasons.</li><li>I can use a simple model to explain a real astronomical phenomenon.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the seasons demonstration and group discussion. Review of seasonal sky wheels.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This week we've learned real star names voyagers relied on. But here's an important real science question: why do different stars appear at different times of the year at all? And here's a common misconception worth tackling directly: many people think seasons happen because Earth gets closer to or further from the Sun. That's actually not true — let's find out the real reason.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Addressing the Misconception (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Ask the class directly: &quot;Why do you think we have seasons?&quot; Many will likely suggest Earth moves closer to or further from the Sun. Explain clearly: this is a common misconception — Earth's distance from the Sun barely changes across the year, and it isn't the real cause of seasons.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Cause: Earth's Tilt (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Earth is tilted on its axis by about 23.5 degrees, and this tilt stays pointed in roughly the same direction as Earth orbits the Sun across the year. This means different parts of Earth receive more or less direct sunlight at different times of year, depending on whether they're tilted toward or away from the Sun — and that is the real cause of seasons.</li><li>Demonstration: use a torch as the 'sun' and have a student slowly orbit it holding a tilted globe (or ball with a marked tilt), observing how light hits different latitudes more directly at different points in the orbit.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why the Night Sky Changes Too (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: As Earth orbits the Sun across the year, the part of space we face at night gradually changes too — which is why different stars and constellations (like Matariki) become visible at different times of year.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why would a voyager need to know not just the season, but how the season changes which stars they could rely on at night?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence correcting the common misconception about why seasons happen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The seasons change because God built them into the fabric of creation — the tilt of the earth, the orbit around the sun, the rhythms of warm and cold. God designed a world that rewards the patient learner who pays attention to His patterns.</li><li>Verse: &quot;It was you who set all the boundaries of the earth; you made both summer and winter.&quot; — <em>Psalm 74:17 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning why the sky changes — and that God set those rhythms in place — deepens our knowledge of the Creator who built order into all things.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God 'made both summer and winter.' How do you experience God differently in different seasons — both in nature and in your own life?</li><li>The changing sky confused navigators until they understood God's pattern behind it. What confusing things in your life might make more sense once you understand God's pattern?</li><li>What does it say about God that He built reliable, repeating rhythms into creation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A torch or lamp (&quot;the sun&quot;)</li><li>A globe or ball marked with a visible tilt</li><li>Paper for seasonal sky wheels</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why do we have seasons? (the real reason)</em> — search: &quot;why do we have seasons axial tilt explained for kids&quot;. Directly addresses and corrects the common 'distance from the sun' misconception with the real scientific explanation.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Earth's Tilt Through the Year</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw Earth at four points in its orbit around the Sun, clearly showing the consistent tilt at each point.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the Sun in the centre.</li><li>Draw Earth at four positions around it, tilted the same direction each time.</li><li>Label which hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun at each point.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest part of showing the tilt staying consistent?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Correcting the Myth</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, clear explanation (as if to a younger student) correcting the &quot;distance from the sun&quot; myth and explaining the real cause of seasons.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do you think this myth is so common, even though it isn't correct?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Seasonal Star Wheel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital or paper wheel showing which real stars (e.g. Matariki) are visible in each season.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does this wheel connect back to what voyagers needed to know?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Real Reason for Seasons</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining the real scientific cause of seasons.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why do we have seasons axial tilt explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the video change or confirm what you already believed about seasons?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 19: Old Ways, New Tools":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE MAGNETIC COMPASS — A Real Invention, Compared to Real Wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are products or systems developed to meet a need, and can be evaluated for fitness for purpose.</li><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how a technological device makes use of a natural phenomenon to function.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a magnetic compass is, how it works, and its real history.</li><li>We are learning to compare traditional wayfinding with magnetic compass navigation.</li><li>I can form and justify an opinion using evidence from both navigation methods.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the compass-vs-wayfinding comparison challenge and class debate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've spent four weeks learning real wayfinding skills passed down across generations. Today we meet a different real invention entirely: the magnetic compass — and ask an honest question: does having an instrument make a method 'better'?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Invention with a Long History (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The magnetic compass is believed to have first been developed in ancient China, with early versions used for navigation by around the 11th century. It works because Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, and a free-spinning magnetised needle naturally aligns itself with Earth's magnetic field, consistently pointing toward magnetic north.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might knowing the compass's real history help us appreciate it as just one more clever human invention, rather than something that makes older methods 'wrong'?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comparing the Two Methods (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A magnetic compass is reliable and doesn't require years of training, but it can be affected by magnetic interference and tells you direction only — not your real position. Traditional wayfinding takes years to master, but combines direction, position, and environmental awareness (wind, swells, stars, birds, clouds) into one constantly-updating mental picture.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Test and Debate (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Give groups a real compass to experiment with alongside their star compass wheel from Tuesday. Navigate the same simple course twice — once using only the star compass/memory method, once using the real compass — and compare speed, accuracy, and how each felt to use.</li><li>Activity: Hold a short class debate: which method would you trust more on a real voyage, and why? Is there value in both?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: where and roughly when was the magnetic compass first developed?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: GPS is extraordinary — but it needs a satellite signal, a battery, and a working device. The stars require nothing except eyes that have been taught to read them. Hina's wisdom was about knowing something so deeply that no technology can replace it. This is the trust in God that Proverbs describes: not leaning on tools alone, but on deep, abiding relationship.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Trust in the LORD with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways submit to him, and he will make your paths straight.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 3:5-6 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Developing deep wisdom that serves others — not just relying on shortcuts and tools — is at the heart of faithful service.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>GPS tells you where to go without needing to understand why. How might that be different from the deep knowledge Hina passed to Tama?</li><li>'Lean not on your own understanding' — does this mean we shouldn't use our minds? Or does it mean something else?</li><li>Is there an area of your life where you've been relying too much on external shortcuts rather than developing real wisdom?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Real compasses (one or more per group)</li><li>Star compass wheels from Tuesday</li><li>A simple course for the navigation comparison</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>History of the magnetic compass</em> — search: &quot;history of the magnetic compass invention China&quot;. Provides real historical context for the invention discussed in this lesson.</li><li><em>How does a compass work?</em> — search: &quot;how does a compass work Earth magnetic field explained&quot;. Explains the real science behind the compass's function.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Hybrid&quot; Navigation Tool</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a tool on paper that combines the best of both traditional wayfinding and modern compass technology.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch your hybrid tool.</li><li>Label one traditional feature and one modern feature it combines.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to combine those two specific features?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Compass's Real History</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short factual recount of the magnetic compass's real history and how it works.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of its history surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital table comparing traditional wayfinding and the magnetic compass across categories like: training time, reliability, and information provided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which method &quot;wins&quot; more categories — and does that settle the debate?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Compass</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the magnetic compass's history or science.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;history of the magnetic compass invention China&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would change someone's mind in today's debate?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 20: Painting the Stars":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">MATARIKI STAR PAINTING — Star Map Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore colour, line, and pattern to express ideas.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify how artists have used the environment as inspiration.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past and present cultures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to use the night sky as an artistic reference, placing stars intentionally.</li><li>We are understanding how Matariki and Te Punga held meaning for Māori and Pacific navigators.</li><li>I can create a personal star map square showing at least two named star formations.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed star map square with at least two identifiable star formations and a written sentence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dim the lights if possible. Display the Milky Way image from Monday's trial. Ask: &quot;If this was your only road map, which dots would you memorise first?&quot; Introduce Matariki (nine stars of the Pleiades) and Te Punga (Southern Cross) — the two most important clusters for Pacific navigation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sketching the Star Positions (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use white chalk or light pencil to mark star positions on dark paper — start with Matariki's tight cluster of nine, then Te Punga's four stars of the Southern Cross.</li><li>Encourage students to use actual relative positions from the star compass, not random placement.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Painting the Stars (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using white and gold paint or pastel, paint each star — varying dot size to show brightness.</li><li>Optional: add a faint Milky Way streak using a dry brush or smudged white pastel.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;A star that navigators trusted had to be remembered clearly. Make your important stars unmistakable.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Labelling and Assembly (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lightly label key star clusters in pencil: Matariki, Te Punga, any others known.</li><li>Write one sentence on the back: which star would YOU trust most on a dark night, and why?</li><li>Add the square to the group quilt and step back — compare with Weeks 1–3's squares.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Point to one star on your square and say its name and what it tells a navigator.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Matariki. Te Punga. Māui's fishhook. The voyagers gave stars names because names matter — names make things real, knowable, and part of your story. God does the same with every star in the universe. And He does it with us too: He knows your name.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He determines the number of the stars and calls them each by name.&quot; — <em>Psalm 147:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Painting the stars and naming them reflects back the glory of the God who named them all — bringing wonder and worship into the world through our art.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God calls every star by name — and there are more stars than grains of sand on every beach on earth. What does that tell you about God's capacity to know and love?</li><li>If God names the stars, and names YOU, what does that mean for how God sees you on your hardest days?</li><li>As you painted your star map, how might you make that kind of work an act of worship rather than just an art activity?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dark blue or black paper squares (A4 trimmed to square)</li><li>White and gold paint, pastels, or chalk</li><li>Fine brushes or cotton buds for dot stars</li><li>Star compass reference image from Tuesday</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Matariki — the nine stars explained</em> — search: &quot;Matariki nine stars Maori New Year meaning kids&quot;. Provides context for each of the nine Matariki stars and their meanings.</li><li><em>Polynesian star navigation</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian star navigation Pacific wayfinding stars&quot;. Shows how specific star clusters were used in practice.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Paint Your Star Map Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — paint your Matariki star map on dark paper and add it to the group quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you discover about placing stars intentionally versus randomly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Night Watch</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short journal entry (80–120 words) as Tama on night watch, identifying stars one by one.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which star felt most like a friend by the end of your watch?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Star Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a drawing tool to create a digital star map showing Matariki, Te Punga, and at least two other named stars with labels.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest thing about making stars look like stars on screen?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Matariki — the Stars Explained</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Matariki and its nine stars.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Matariki nine stars Maori New Year meaning kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of the nine stars' meanings surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Sea Trial — Station 1: True Voyager or Myth?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 1 — Testing Real History Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate understanding of historical events, evidence, and the people involved, drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating what we have learned about real Pacific migration history from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working as a team under time pressure to recall accurate historical facts.</li><li>I can tell the difference between a true historical fact and a common misconception.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 History content. Score team performance on the quick-fire round and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting before Week 6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The Council of Wayfinders has summoned you. Before this voyage can continue, you must prove what you have truly learned across four weeks of trials. Today's first station: history itself. Quick wits and accurate memory will serve you far better than guessing.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the format: this is a rapid-fire True/False round. Read each statement aloud; teams confer briefly (10–15 seconds) then signal their answer (show a card, raise a hand, or use a simple buzzer system).</li><li>Award one point per correct answer. Consider a bonus point for a team that can correctly explain WHY a false statement is false.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Quick-Fire Question Bank (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. The Lapita culture is named after a site in New Caledonia. (TRUE)</li><li>2. The Lapita people are believed to have first appeared near Papua New Guinea. (TRUE)</li><li>3. Voyagers reached Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa at the same time as the early Lapita spread. (FALSE — this happened roughly 1,000+ years later)</li><li>4. Voyaging canoes are believed to have carried food crops like kūmara, intended for planting at new lands. (TRUE)</li><li>5. Mau Piailug navigated the Hōkūleʻa using modern GPS technology. (FALSE — he used only traditional, instrument-free wayfinding)</li><li>6. The Hōkūleʻa voyage sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti in 1976. (TRUE)</li><li>7. Overcrowding and drought are real examples of 'pull' factors that drew people toward a new land. (FALSE — these are push factors that drove people away from a place)</li><li>8. Bird migration patterns may have been a real clue voyagers used to suspect that land existed somewhere unseen. (TRUE)</li><li>9. The magnetic compass was first invented in ancient Greece. (FALSE — it is believed to have first been developed in ancient China)</li><li>10. Etak is a real navigational idea where the canoe is imagined to stay still while islands move past it. (TRUE)</li><li>Optional extension: invite teams to write one additional True/False statement of their own from Weeks 1–4 content, to challenge another team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal team scores. Briefly revisit any question more than one team got wrong, with a short explanation, before moving to tomorrow's station.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As a team, write down the ONE question from today you're most confident you'll remember correctly in a year's time — and why it stuck.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire question bank (above) printed or read aloud</li><li>A simple way for teams to signal answers (cards, hands, buzzers)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Sea Trial Medal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a medal or badge that could be awarded to a team that performs well in today's History Station.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the medal design.</li><li>Include one symbol from this term's story (pottery shard, waka, compass, star).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular symbol to represent History knowledge?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of history content from Weeks 1–4 that you understood best, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you come to understand it so well — what helped it stick?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Scoreboard</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital scoreboard (spreadsheet or slide) to track team scores across all five Sea Trial stations this week.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information does a good scoreboard need to show clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Quest Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own Quest Log entries from Weeks 1–4 and note one thing you wrote that you'd improve on now, with what you know today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does this tell you about how much you've learned this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Sea Trial — Station 2: Place the Islands":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 2 — Testing Real Geography Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: demonstrate understanding of geographic features, scale, and patterns drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our memory of real Pacific geography from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working under pressure, using memory rather than guesswork.</li><li>I can recall and place real geographic features correctly without looking.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Geography content. Use results to identify which geographic concepts (scale, currents, navigation signs) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, your knowledge of history was tested. Today, the Council tests your knowledge of place itself. A real voyager could not always see — sometimes they had to know, from memory alone, where things truly were.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out the large Pacific floor/wall map used throughout the term, but cover or remove the usual labels.</li><li>Explain the challenge: one team member is blindfolded (or simply closes their eyes), and must correctly place a token at a named location based purely on memory, guided only by verbal directions (not physical guidance) from their team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Call out locations one at a time for each team to place: Hawaii, Aotearoa, Rapa Nui (the three points of the Polynesian Triangle), Tahiti, and one island group from Week 1's distance work.</li><li>Award points for correct or close placement. Follow with a short verbal recap round: ask teams to recall one real fact about ocean currents (South Equatorial Current, North Equatorial Current, Equatorial Countercurrent) and one real wave-piloting or bird-based navigation clue from Week 3.</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which was harder — remembering WHERE something was, or remembering WHAT a clue meant?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Briefly re-show correct locations on the map for anything placed incorrectly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which real Pacific location or geographic fact from this term do you feel most confident about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map (labels removed or covered)</li><li>Blindfolds (optional) and placement tokens</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Triangle from Memory</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without looking at any reference, draw your best memory of the Polynesian Triangle and its three points.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your drawing to a real map afterward — what did you get right, and what surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of geography content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you remember it so clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Map Labelling Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital map tool or slide to label as many real locations and currents from this term as you can recall, unaided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many could you label without checking back?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Term's Map Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own star compass and distance work from Weeks 1 and 4. What's one detail you'd add now that you wouldn't have known back then?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Sea Trial — Station 3: Read the Conditions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 3 — Testing Real Science Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of weather, climate, and astronomical phenomena drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of real weather, wind, and seasonal science from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are using real observed or given conditions to make a justified decision.</li><li>I can apply scientific reasoning under realistic pressure, not just recall facts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Science content. Use results to identify which scientific concepts (weather vs. climate, wind formation, cloud signs, seasons) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;A real voyager often had only moments to decide: is it safe to sail today, or not? Today, the Council gives you a real or simulated set of conditions. Your knowledge of sky and sea will be tested for real.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If possible, take the class outside for genuine conditions; if not, prepare 3–4 written/illustrated weather scenario cards (e.g. &quot;calm seas, light cumulus clouds, steady wind from the east&quot; or &quot;darkening cumulonimbus clouds building rapidly, wind shifting&quot;).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present each scenario to teams in turn. Teams must decide: safe to sail, or not — and justify their answer using real content from this term (weather vs. climate, cloud types, trade wind patterns).</li><li>Include at least one question testing the real cause of seasons (Earth's axial tilt) and one testing the real 'island cloud' phenomenon from Week 3.</li><li>Award points for correct decisions AND for scientifically sound justifications, even if the final decision is debatable.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Discuss any scenario where teams disagreed — was there a genuinely defensible case for both decisions?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real science concept from this term would you most want a real voyager to understand before setting sail?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Weather scenario cards (or real outdoor conditions)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a &quot;Safe to Sail&quot; Scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a sky/sea scene showing conditions a real voyager would consider genuinely safe to set sail in, labelling the clues that make it safe.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the one clue you'd never ignore, even if everything else looked fine?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science concept from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it to someone who missed this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Decision Flowchart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital flowchart: &quot;Is it safe to sail?&quot; — branching through weather, cloud, and wind checks based on this term's content.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What question did you put first in your flowchart, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Voyage Forecasts</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own voyage forecast entries from Weeks 1–3. Were your past decisions justified by real evidence, or guesswork? Reflect honestly.</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Sea Trial — Station 4: The Final Float Test":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL STATION 4 — Testing Real Technology Knowledge and the Class Vessels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of design, materials, and structures, drawn from prior learning and testing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our real engineering knowledge from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are testing our own vessel designs under genuine, timed conditions.</li><li>I can explain why a real design choice worked, using evidence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Technology content. Use the float-and-sail test and verbal explanations as evidence of understanding buoyancy, stability, and sail design.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Every voyager's fate rests on their vessel. Today, the Council asks for proof: bring forward your waka hourua and sail, built and improved across these four weeks, and show that it can truly survive the open sea.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gather the class's waka hourua models (Week 2) and sail designs (Week 3). If models were not kept, allow a short rebuild using familiar materials.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Final Float-and-Sail Test (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each team tests their vessel in front of the class: float test with cargo (Week 1–2 buoyancy knowledge), stability test against a simulated wave (Week 2 double-hull knowledge), and a sail test in front of a fan or moving air (Week 3 crab claw sail knowledge).</li><li>After each test, the team must verbally explain ONE real engineering reason their design succeeded or failed, using correct vocabulary from this term (displacement, density, stability, sail angle).</li><li>Award points for both performance AND for the quality/accuracy of the verbal explanation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Celebrate the strongest design feature seen across all teams, regardless of final score.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the single most important design feature your vessel needed to survive this final test?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Class waka hourua and sail models from Weeks 1–3 (or rebuild materials)</li><li>Tubs of water, a fan or moving air source</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Sea-Worthy&quot; Certificate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a certificate that could be awarded to a vessel that passes today's final test, listing the real engineering features it demonstrated.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would a certificate need to show to prove a vessel is genuinely sea-worthy?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Technology MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of technology content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did testing your own design help you understand it better than just reading about it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Evolution Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple before/after digital poster showing your vessel's design at Week 1 versus its final, improved version today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the biggest improvement you made, and what taught you to make it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Build Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your design notes and sketches from Weeks 1–3. What's one change you made that you're proudest of?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Bonus Station: Sea Trial — Bonus Station: Star Navigation & The Council's Verdict":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA TRIAL FINALE — Star Navigation Test and the Council Ceremony</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences / The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of navigation knowledge and contribute meaningfully to a shared class outcome, drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our ability to use a star compass for real navigation decisions.</li><li>We are reflecting honestly on a full term of learning and contribution.</li><li>I can recognise growth in my own understanding across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the star navigation challenge. Use the Council Ceremony as a celebratory, low-pressure close to Weeks 1–4's assessment, not as a new graded task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;You have faced History, Geography, Science, and Technology. One final test remains, and then the Council of Wayfinders will render its verdict on this voyage so far.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Bonus Station: Star Navigation (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using the star compass wheels built in Week 4, teams must correctly 'steer' a token from one named island to another on the floor map, using real star reference points (Matariki, Te Matau a Māui, Te Punga/the Southern Cross) rather than guessing direction.</li><li>Award bonus points for correct use of the real star names alongside correct direction.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tallying the Sea Trial (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add up all points from Monday through Friday's bonus station. Announce results — frame this as a genuine achievement regardless of which team scores highest, since every team has demonstrated four weeks of real learning.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Council Ceremony (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If the class has &quot;passed&quot; (a generous bar — this is about participation and growth, not a cutoff score), narrate the compass prop visibly coming back to life: spin it once, then let it settle.</li><li>Read a closing line in Tama's voice: &quot;You have proven yourselves true Wayfinders-in-training. The compass settles, ready for what comes next — for soon, after so long at sea, we will see land.&quot;</li><li>This sets up Week 6's arrival in Aotearoa.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking back over Weeks 1–4, what is the one thing you understand now that you didn't at the start of the term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Star compass wheels from Week 4</li><li>The floor/wall Pacific map</li><li>The class's compass/journal prop for the ceremony</li><li>A running scoreboard from the week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Wayfinder-in-Training&quot; Badge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a small badge or certificate every student could receive for completing the Sea Trial.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What symbol best represents what you've learned this term so far?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter to Myself at the Start of Term</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter to yourself from Week 1, telling your past self what you now understand about this voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would surprise your Week 1 self the most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Highlights Slide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create one digital slide summarising your proudest moment or piece of learning from Weeks 1–4.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular highlight?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Voyage Quilt</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your group's quilt squares built across Weeks 1–4 and choose the one square you think tells the story best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What makes that square so effective at telling the story?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 21: Land!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">ARRIVAL IN AOTEAROA — One of the Last Great Landmasses Settled by Humans</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how the perspectives of people in the past differ, based on evidence available about their lives and choices.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how the arrival of people in a new place changes both the people and the place.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning roughly when and how Aotearoa was first settled, based on real evidence.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa was one of the last major landmasses on Earth to be reached by people.</li><li>I can use real evidence to support a claim about a historical arrival.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the &quot;first 24 hours&quot; priority-ranking discussion. Review of student decisions and justifications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;After five weeks of preparing, sailing, and surviving, the voyage finally reaches land. But here's a fact that often surprises people: historians and archaeologists believe Aotearoa was one of the very last major landmasses on Earth to be settled by humans — likely only around 700 years ago, sometime around 1300 CE.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if humans had already been living on most other continents for tens of thousands of years, why might Aotearoa have been one of the last places reached?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Evidence of Early Arrival (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: one of the most important real archaeological sites in this story is Wairau Bar, in Marlborough at the top of the South Island. Excavations there uncovered evidence of one of New Zealand's earliest known settlements, including tools, ornaments, and the bones of moa — a now-extinct giant flightless bird hunted by early arrivals — helping archaeologists estimate settlement dates of roughly 1280–1300 CE.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might bones and tools left behind tell us more reliable information than a guess based on stories alone — and why might we still need both kinds of evidence together?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why So Late? (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa's extreme distance from other landmasses, combined with the genuinely difficult ocean crossing required to reach it, are real reasons historians believe it remained unsettled for so long compared to most of the rest of the world.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The First 24 Hours (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: close eyes and narrate the moment of stepping onto an unknown shore — the sounds, smells, the ground feeling strange after weeks at sea — then open eyes to a real image of Aotearoa's coastline.</li><li>Activity: in groups, rank the top 3 decisions a small group of new arrivals would genuinely need to make in their first 24 hours (shelter, fresh water, food, safety), and defend the chosen order.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: roughly when do historians believe Aotearoa was first settled, and what is one piece of real evidence that supports this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After weeks at sea, land appears — green mountains, birds, the smell of earth. This is the moment the whole voyage was for. The Bible is full of moments like this: God bringing His people to a new place after all the fear and waiting, and saying — here. This is it.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He brought us to this place and gave us this land, a land flowing with milk and honey.&quot; — <em>Deuteronomy 26:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — God's love is shown in the places He brings us to — after the storms, after the long waiting, after the courage it cost.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Deuteronomy describes God 'bringing' His people to a new place. In what ways has God brought YOU somewhere new — a new school, a new friendship, a new understanding?</li><li>Tama's family carried plants and seeds to grow in the new land. What things do you carry with you that are important to who you are?</li><li>What would it mean for you to treat your home — your town, your school — as a gift from God worth caring for?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Image of Aotearoa's coastline/landscape</li><li>Information or images relating to Wairau Bar (if available)</li><li>Paper for the priority-ranking activity</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>When was Aotearoa first settled?</em> — search: &quot;when was New Zealand first settled by Polynesians Wairau Bar&quot;. Provides real, age-appropriate detail on the archaeological evidence behind this lesson's key dates.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Moment of Arrival</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the moment of first stepping onto Aotearoa's coastline, based on a real description of the landscape.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Include the coastline, the waka, and the first arrivals.</li><li>Add one detail showing how different this land looks from a Pacific island.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to focus on to show this was a genuinely new and different place?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Wairau Bar: A Discovery Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short report, in role as an archaeologist, describing what was found at Wairau Bar and why it matters for understanding Aotearoa's early history.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does physical evidence like this matter so much when no written records exist?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a &quot;First 24 Hours&quot; Decision Tree</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital flowchart or decision tree showing the order of priorities a new arrival would face in their first 24 hours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you put first, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The First Arrivals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the early settlement of Aotearoa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;when was New Zealand first settled by Polynesians Wairau Bar&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real evidence mentioned in the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 22: A Different Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A LAND UNLIKE ANY OTHER — Aotearoa's Real Ecological Differences</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how the natural features of a place affect the people who live there.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning real, significant differences between tropical Pacific islands and Aotearoa.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa had no native land mammals before humans arrived — a genuinely unusual fact.</li><li>I can compare two environments using real evidence and explain the impact of the differences.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation during the comparison-chart activity and class discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, we arrived. Today, we need to understand just how different this new land really was. Wonder question: what do you think it would be like landing somewhere with no familiar animals at all — not even ones you might expect, like deer, foxes, or wild dogs?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Scale and Climate (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa is far larger than most Pacific islands, with genuine mountain ranges — including the Southern Alps — and a temperate climate with four distinct seasons, very different from the tropical wet/dry seasonal pattern of most Pacific islands.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Unusual Real Fact (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa is believed to have had no native land mammals at all before human arrival — no deer, no foxes, not even mice or rats, aside from bats. Instead, it had extraordinary, unique birdlife that had evolved without natural land predators, including the moa, a giant flightless bird that grew up to several metres tall.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might birds evolve so differently on a land with no land predators, compared to most other places on Earth?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comparing the Two Environments (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort image/fact cards comparing a typical Pacific island (small, warm, coral, limited land, familiar tropical animals) versus Aotearoa (large landmass, mountains, varied climate, no native land mammals, unique birdlife) into a physical comparison chart.</li><li>Activity: decide and justify which single difference would have been hardest for new arrivals to adjust to.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is one genuinely unusual real fact about Aotearoa's wildlife before humans arrived?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama stands on a hill looking at mountains that stretch further than he has ever seen land go. This is the feeling of 'new' — overwhelming, wonderful, and a little frightening. Isaiah's God is the God of new things: not nostalgia, not just the past, but a living Creator still at work.</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;See, I am doing a new thing! Now it springs up; do you not perceive it? I am making a way in the wilderness and streams in the wasteland.&quot;&quot; — <em>Isaiah 43:19 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — God is always doing new things and calls us to be part of bringing His Kingdom into new places and new situations.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God asks: 'Do you not perceive it?' — what new thing might God be doing in your life right now that you might be missing?</li><li>Tama's first reaction to Aotearoa is awe — this land is enormous and strange. When have you experienced that kind of awe? What did it make you think about God?</li><li>How might you be someone who helps bring God's 'new things' into your school or community?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comparison cards: Pacific island vs. Aotearoa features</li><li>Images of moa and other unique native birds if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>New Zealand before humans arrived</em> — search: &quot;New Zealand wildlife before human arrival moa no land mammals&quot;. Explains the real, unusual ecological history central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Aotearoa's Unique Birdlife</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a moa or another unique native bird of Aotearoa, labelling at least one real fact about it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What about this bird makes it different from animals new arrivals would have known from the Pacific islands?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: &quot;Welcome Guide&quot; for New Arrivals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;welcome guide&quot; (in role) listing 3 real things to expect that are different about this new land.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which difference do you think would need explaining most urgently?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital table comparing a typical Pacific island and Aotearoa across climate, size, and wildlife.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which category shows the biggest difference?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: A Land Without Land Mammals</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Aotearoa's wildlife before human arrival.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;New Zealand wildlife before human arrival moa no land mammals&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would most surprise someone who'd never heard it?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 23: New Seasons, New Rules":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SURVIVING A COLDER YEAR — Real Adaptations for a New Climate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how living things, including humans, are suited to their particular way of life.</li><li>Build science knowledge connected to real survival and food-growing decisions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a colder, four-season climate genuinely changed how new arrivals had to live.</li><li>We are learning a real food-storage technique developed to survive Aotearoa's winters.</li><li>I can plan a realistic seasonal survival strategy using real evidence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the survival-calendar planning activity and team presentations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we learned how different this land truly was. Today, we focus on a genuinely serious real challenge: Aotearoa's winters are far colder than anything most Pacific voyagers had ever experienced — and food crops they relied on didn't grow the same way here.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Harder Climate for Growing Food (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: kūmara (sweet potato), a crop voyagers are believed to have carried with them, struggled to grow in much of Aotearoa's cooler climate compared to the tropical Pacific. Early growers had to find sheltered, sun-facing slopes and develop new techniques to give it any real chance of growing well.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real, Clever Solution: Storage Pits (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: a genuinely real and clever solution that developed was the rua kūmara — purpose-built storage pits dug into the ground, used to keep harvested kūmara protected from frost and cold through the winter months, ready for the following season.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might keeping food safe through an entire cold season have been just as important as growing it in the first place?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Planning a Survival Calendar (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, plot a basic 'first year survival calendar,' deciding what should happen in each season (planting, storing, hunting, gathering) to survive a full year in this new climate.</li><li>Activity: present your team's survival calendar and defend one key decision in it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what a rua kūmara was and why it mattered.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Roa is already worried about keeping Pipi warm through the coming cold. The seasons of Aotearoa are completely different from what they knew. But God built seasons into creation on purpose — as rhythm, as rest, as reminder that time moves forward and He holds it all.</li><li>Verse: &quot;There is a time for everything, and a season for every activity under the heavens.&quot; — <em>Ecclesiastes 3:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully preparing for each season — whether literal or in life — and caring for those around us through change is at the heart of Manaaki.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ecclesiastes says there is 'a season for every activity.' What season do you think you're in right now — in your life, your faith, your learning?</li><li>Roa prepares for winter by planning ahead for her family. What does faithful preparation for the people around you look like in your own life?</li><li>How does knowing that God holds all the seasons — including the difficult ones — change the way you face change?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple seasonal calendar templates</li><li>Images of kūmara and storage pit reconstructions if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How early Māori grew and stored kūmara</em> — search: &quot;rua kumara storage pits early Maori farming&quot;. Documents the real, clever food-storage solution central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Rua Kūmara</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled cross-section of a rua kūmara (storage pit), showing how it protected food through winter.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What design feature do you think mattered most for keeping food safe?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Grower's Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short set of notes, in role as an early grower, explaining the challenge of growing kūmara in this new climate and the solution you found.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world problem-solving does this remind you of today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Survival Calendar</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital seasonal calendar showing planting, storing, and gathering tasks across a full year in Aotearoa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which season looks the most demanding on your calendar?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Storing Food for Winter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about rua kūmara or early Māori food storage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;rua kumara storage pits early Maori farming&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video impressed you most about this solution?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 24: New Land, New Tools":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">ADZE AND POUNAMU — Real Tool Adaptation in a New Land</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how materials available in an environment shape the tools and technologies a society develops.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how real tools and materials changed once people settled in Aotearoa.</li><li>We are learning about pounamu (greenstone), a genuinely significant material found here.</li><li>I can explain why available materials shape the technology a society develops.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the tool redesign activity and written explanations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;New land. New challenges. Yesterday we focused on food. Today, we focus on tools — because the materials available in Aotearoa were genuinely different from what voyagers had used across the tropical Pacific, and that changed what tools they could make.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuinely Significant New Material: Pounamu (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Aotearoa's South Island contains pounamu (greenstone/NZ jade), a hard, beautiful stone that became hugely significant for both practical tools (like adzes, a type of cutting/shaping tool) and for ornaments carrying real cultural and spiritual value, still highly treasured today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a material that is both useful AND beautiful become especially treasured by a society?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Adapting Old Tools for New Tasks (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: voyaging-era tools designed for fishing and boat-building had to be adapted for entirely new tasks in Aotearoa — including hunting moa, working unfamiliar timbers, and building shelters suited to a much colder climate than most voyagers had known.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Redesign Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, redesign a simple tool (fishing, building, or carrying) using 'new land' materials and constraints set by the teacher (e.g. a fishing tool needs to work in a river instead of open ocean).</li><li>Activity: explain what you changed about the original design and why the new land required it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is pounamu, and why did it become so significant in Aotearoa?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's ocean tools don't work in the river. Everything he knew has to be adapted. James tells us that when we lack wisdom for exactly these moments — when the old ways don't fit the new place — we can ask God, who gives generously. The hīnaki was the answer to that prayer.</li><li>Verse: &quot;If any of you lacks wisdom, you should ask God, who gives generously to all without finding fault, and it will be given to you.&quot; — <em>James 1:5 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Seeking God's wisdom to serve our community well — even when we have to learn completely new ways — is at the heart of faithful service.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Have you ever been in a situation where what you knew before wasn't enough for where you were? What did you do?</li><li>The hīnaki was created by adapting Pacific weaving knowledge to a new challenge. How might God want you to adapt your gifts for a new situation?</li><li>James says to ask God for wisdom 'without doubting.' What would it look like to ask God for wisdom about a real challenge you're facing right now?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of pounamu tools/ornaments if available</li><li>Materials for the tool redesign challenge (card, string, sticks)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is pounamu (greenstone)?</em> — search: &quot;pounamu greenstone New Zealand significance explained&quot;. Introduces the real, culturally significant material central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Pounamu-Inspired Tool or Ornament</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a tool or ornament inspired by pounamu, labelling why both its usefulness and beauty might matter.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to make your design useful, decorative, or both?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Why Materials Matter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of why the materials available in a place shape the tools a society can develop, using pounamu as your example.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Can you think of a modern example of a material shaping what we're able to build?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Tool Redesign Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital &quot;before and after&quot; poster showing your tool's original tropical design and its redesigned Aotearoa version.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the most important change you made?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Pounamu</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about pounamu and its significance.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;pounamu greenstone New Zealand significance explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video would you want to share with someone unfamiliar with pounamu?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 30: Rock Art — Marking the Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC PETROGLYPH SKETCHING — Arrival Marks in a Quilt Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore line, shape, and texture to make art works.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate the purpose of objects and images from past cultures.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: identify and describe ways in which meanings are communicated through art works.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Pacific petroglyphs used bold simple outlines to mark arrival and connection to place.</li><li>We are practising drawing with confident unshaded lines to create a carving-like effect.</li><li>I can sketch at least one petroglyph motif that connects to this week's landfall story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed petroglyph square with at least one clear motif and a written sentence explaining its choice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Show images of real Pacific petroglyphs — Hawaiian lava field carvings, NZ stone carvings. Ask: &quot;What do you notice about how these are drawn? No shading. No colour. Just lines.&quot; Explain: petroglyph artists worked with stone on stone — every mark was deliberate. Today students work the same way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing and Practising a Motif (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Display reference sheet of Pacific petroglyph motifs: sea turtle, fish, human figure, canoe/waka, bird, spiral.</li><li>Students choose 1–2 motifs connecting to Tama's landfall story. Sketch rough draft on scrap paper first.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;These marks were made by people who had just arrived somewhere new. What would YOU carve to say: I was here?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Drawing the Petroglyph Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw chosen motif(s) in pencil — bold simple outlines, no interior shading. Scale to fill most of the square.</li><li>Go over pencil lines with black ink pen or dark crayon, pressing firmly for strong confident marks.</li><li>Add a simple repeated border pattern around the edge (dots, dashes, or small triangles).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Assembly (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence on back: which motif they chose and what it means for Tama's arrival.</li><li>Add square to group quilt. Compare: how does petroglyph square look different from woven, kōwhaiwhai, and star squares?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If you could carve ONE thing into the rocks at your landing place, what would it be and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Pacific peoples carved their arrival into rock — turtles, canoes, spirals — not because they had to, but because they needed to say: I was here, this is who I am, this matters. God invites us to leave marks of His Kingdom in the world. What will yours say?</li><li>Verse: &quot;Let this be written for a future generation, that a people not yet created may praise the LORD.&quot; — <em>Psalm 102:18 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — The marks we leave — in art, in kindness, in truth-telling — can point future generations toward God, long after we are gone.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The Psalm says to write things 'for a future generation.' What are you doing now that might matter to someone who hasn't been born yet?</li><li>Pacific petroglyph artists carved their marks without knowing who would see them centuries later. How does that change the way you think about doing your work faithfully today?</li><li>If you could carve ONE thing into a rock that would be read in 1000 years, what would it say?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (continuing the group quilt from previous weeks)</li><li>Black ink pens or dark wax crayons for confident outlines</li><li>Pencils and erasers for rough sketching</li><li>Reference sheet of Pacific petroglyph motifs (turtle, fish, human figure, canoe/waka, bird, spiral)</li><li>Scrap paper for practising the motif before committing to the final square</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific petroglyphs and rock art</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Hawaiian petroglyphs rock art&quot;. Shows real examples of Pacific petroglyphs so students can see the bold, simple carving style before sketching their own.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch Your Petroglyph Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — sketch and ink your petroglyph motif and add it to your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What motif did you choose, and what does it say about arriving somewhere new?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Explain Your Motif</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of the petroglyph motif you chose and why it connects to Tama's landfall story.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might carving something in stone make a message last longer than writing it on paper?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Petroglyph Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to design a bold, simple-line petroglyph-style motif of your own.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did keeping the design bold and simple, without shading, change about how you drew it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rock Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on real Pacific petroglyphs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific Hawaiian petroglyphs rock art&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about where or how these carvings were made?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 26: Not Just One Voyage":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE LONG PAUSE — A Real Mystery in the Pacific Settlement Story</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how a historical event can be understood differently depending on the evidence available, and recognise genuine ongoing debate among historians.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real, two-stage timeline of Pacific settlement.</li><li>We are learning about a genuine, still-debated mystery in Pacific history: the &quot;long pause.&quot;</li><li>I can engage thoughtfully with a question historians themselves have not fully answered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the match-the-voyage activity and the class discussion on the &quot;long pause&quot; mystery.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've arrived in Aotearoa — but this was not one single voyage. Today we discover that the full settlement of the Pacific happened in two real, distinct stages, separated by a genuine mystery historians still debate today.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a journey can be made successfully once, why might people then wait over a thousand years before continuing it further?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Two Real Stages of Settlement (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: recall from Week 1 that the Lapita spread reached Tonga and Samoa roughly 2,800–2,900 years ago. Here is the genuinely surprising part: archaeological evidence suggests voyagers then did NOT continue further east into the rest of the Pacific for roughly 1,000 to 1,500 years.</li><li>Only around 1,000–1,200 CE did voyaging resume and spread rapidly into what's called Eastern Polynesia — reaching Tahiti and the Society Islands, then Hawaii and Rapa Nui (Easter Island) around 1200 CE, and finally Aotearoa by roughly 1300 CE.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Genuine, Real Historical Mystery (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: historians and archaeologists genuinely do not fully agree on why this 'long pause' happened. Some suggest changes in voyaging technology or sailing conditions made the next stage possible only later; others wonder if it relates to population growth, climate shifts, or simply hasn't yet been fully explained by current evidence.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why is it actually more honest for historians to say 'we don't fully know' rather than guessing confidently? What does this teach us about how real history works?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Match the Voyage (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: a 'match the voyage' card game — place/fact cards (Tonga, Samoa, Hawaii, Rapa Nui, Aotearoa) matched and placed correctly onto the big Pacific floor map, then trace voyage routes between island groups with string, visually building the full spread of settlement across both real stages.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the &quot;long pause,&quot; and why is it still considered a genuine historical mystery?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama discovers that people like him reached Hawaii, Tonga, Samoa, Rapa Nui — the farthest corners of the Pacific. John's vision in Revelation pictures the same beautiful truth: at the end of all things, people from every nation, tribe, and language stand together before God. The voyagers were part of that story.</li><li>Verse: &quot;After this I looked, and there before me was a great multitude that no one could count, from every nation, tribe, people and language, standing before the throne.&quot; — <em>Revelation 7:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that God's story includes every nation and every people helps us understand our place in His much larger plan.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Revelation pictures people from every nation and language together. What does that tell us about what God values?</li><li>Tama didn't know about the other voyages that spread his people across the Pacific. What parts of God's bigger story might you not know about yet?</li><li>How does knowing that people from every culture are part of God's plan change how you think about people who are different from you?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Match-the-voyage cards (Tonga, Samoa, Hawaii, Rapa Nui, Aotearoa)</li><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>String for tracing routes</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The &quot;long pause&quot; in Pacific settlement</em> — search: &quot;long pause Polynesian settlement history mystery&quot;. Explores the real, still-debated historical question central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Two-Stage Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a visual timeline showing the Lapita spread (West Polynesia) and the much later Eastern Polynesian expansion, with the &quot;long pause&quot; clearly marked between them.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you choose to visually represent a gap that historians still don't fully understand?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Historian's Honest Answer</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short paragraph, in role as a historian, honestly explaining what we know and don't know about the &quot;long pause.&quot;</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might admitting uncertainty actually make a historian more trustworthy, not less?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Two-Stage Digital Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital timeline showing both real stages of Pacific settlement, with approximate dates for each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about how long the pause really was?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Long Pause</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about the Pacific settlement timeline.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;long pause Polynesian settlement history mystery&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What theory mentioned in the video did you find most convincing, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 27: How Far, Really?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE GATEWAY DISTANCE — Real Numbers Behind the Long Pause</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: use scale and distance to interpret and compare real geographic challenges.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real distance between West and East Polynesia — the &quot;gateway&quot; voyage of the long pause.</li><li>We are learning to compare multiple real voyage distances to understand relative difficulty.</li><li>I can use real measurement to support reasoning about a historical decision.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the measuring activity and the team rankings/justifications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday we discovered the long pause. Today, we measure the real distance voyagers eventually had to cross to end it — and compare it honestly against other voyages we've already studied this term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real Gateway Distance (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the voyage from the Samoa/Tonga region (West Polynesia) to Tahiti and the Society Islands (the gateway into East Polynesia) covers roughly 2,500 kilometres of open ocean — a genuinely serious crossing in its own right, before any further voyages to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, or Aotearoa could even begin.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might crossing this 'gateway' distance first, successfully, have mattered so much for everything that came after it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Measuring and Comparing (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: using the measuring-string technique from Week 1, measure and compare the Samoa/Tonga-to-Tahiti distance against the previously studied Tahiti–Hawaii (~4,400 km), Tahiti–Aotearoa (~4,300 km), and Tahiti–Rapa Nui (~4,000 km) distances.</li><li>Activity: rank all four voyages from shortest to longest, and discuss which required the most courage — and why distance isn't the only thing that makes a voyage hard.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discussion (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Key Question: &quot;Now that you know the real numbers, does the 'long pause' make more or less sense to you? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: roughly how far is the real &quot;gateway&quot; voyage from Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tahiti to Rapa Nui: 3,700 kilometres of open ocean — the longest migration voyage in human history. And yet Paul writes that nothing in all creation, no height or depth, can separate us from the love of God. No distance is too great for that.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Neither height nor depth, nor anything else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord.&quot; — <em>Romans 8:39 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — The love of God stretches further than any ocean voyage — and reaches every person on every island, no matter how remote.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says nothing can separate us from God's love — not even 3,700 kilometres of open ocean. Does that feel real to you? What would change if it did?</li><li>The people who settled Rapa Nui were the most isolated humans on earth — and yet God's love still reached them. What does that tell you about God?</li><li>Is there a part of your life or your story where you've felt 'too far out' — separated from God or from others? What does Paul's promise mean for that?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Measuring string from Week 1</li><li>A simple ranking chart</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mapping the Polynesian voyaging routes</em> — search: &quot;Polynesian voyaging routes map distances explained&quot;. Reinforces the real distances and routes discussed in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Gateway Voyage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw the route from Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti on a simple map, labelling the real distance.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does drawing the actual distance change how you picture this voyage?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager's Calculation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry, in role as a voyager planning the gateway crossing, weighing up the real distance and the risk involved.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real number from today would have weighed most heavily on your decision?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Distance Ranking Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital bar chart ranking all four voyage distances studied today and this term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which voyage surprised you most once placed on the same chart?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Voyaging Distances</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video mapping Polynesian voyaging routes.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Polynesian voyaging routes map distances explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What route shown in the video did you not know about before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 28: Different Skies, Different Seas":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">LATITUDE AND CLIMATE — Real Numbers Behind Each New Home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how latitude affects climate and conditions experienced in different places.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real latitude of each major island group settled this term.</li><li>We are learning how latitude genuinely changes climate, star visibility, and seasons.</li><li>I can use real latitude figures to predict a location's likely climate.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the latitude-line activity and climate-matching task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Each island group settled this term sits at a genuinely different latitude — and that changes almost everything about daily life there. Wonder question: do you think Tonga and Aotearoa would feel like the same kind of place to live, or completely different?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Latitude Figures (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Tonga and Samoa sit at roughly 13–21° south of the equator (tropical), Tahiti at around 17° south (tropical), Hawaii at around 20° north (subtropical), Rapa Nui at around 27° south (subtropical), and Aotearoa stretches from roughly 35° to 47° south (temperate) — a genuinely huge real range across the voyaging story.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Based on these real numbers, which island group do you think experienced the biggest climate shock moving south?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Latitude Matters (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: recall from Week 4 that Earth's tilt causes seasons — latitude determines how strongly that tilt affects a place. Locations closer to the equator experience milder, more consistent year-round warmth, while locations further from it (like Aotearoa) experience much greater seasonal swings between summer and winter.</li><li>Latitude also changes which stars are visible at night — voyagers travelling to a very different latitude, like Aotearoa, would have noticed real, genuine differences in their night sky compared to their tropical homeland.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Latitude Line Activity (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: stretch a 'latitude line' across the floor/room, with students standing at different points representing each island group's real latitude, predicting (then checking) typical weather/temperature for each point.</li><li>Activity: match each island group to a climate description (tropical, subtropical, temperate) and justify using the real latitude figures from today.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which island group studied this term sits at the most extreme latitude, and what climate does that latitude produce?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hawaii is tropical; Aotearoa is temperate. The same ocean, completely different weather — because of where each island sits in relation to the sun. God built latitude into creation. He built order and variety into the same world, so that every place and every people would be different.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The heavens declare the glory of God; the skies proclaim the work of his hands. Day after day they pour forth speech; night after night they reveal knowledge.&quot; — <em>Psalm 19:1-2 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning how God designed climate and latitude deepens our wonder at a Creator who made variety and order work together across the whole earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The sky 'pours forth speech' day after day. What do you think the sky in your part of the world is saying about God?</li><li>God designed climate zones so that every region of the earth is different. What does that variety tell you about God's creativity?</li><li>How might understanding the science of why our weather is the way it is change the way you feel about the place where you live?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A long rope or marked line for the latitude activity</li><li>Latitude reference cards for each island group</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How latitude affects climate</em> — search: &quot;how latitude affects climate explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Latitude Range</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple diagram showing each island group placed at its real latitude, from Hawaii in the north to Aotearoa in the south.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does the spread of latitudes tell you about how varied this voyaging story really was?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Voyager Notices the Sky Change</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection, in role as a voyager travelling from Tonga to Aotearoa, noticing the real changes in climate and night sky along the way.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What change do you think would have been most noticeable first?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Latitude-Climate Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital chart matching each island group's real latitude to its climate type.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did any result surprise you, based on what you expected?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Latitude and Climate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how latitude affects climate.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how latitude affects climate explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real example from the video helped the idea make the most sense?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 29: Then and Now: Sailing Technology":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FROM WAKA HOURUA TO MODERN CATAMARANS — A Real Engineering Legacy</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand how the same underlying engineering principle can appear in both historical and modern technologies.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the double-hull principle behind the waka hourua is still used in modern sailing technology today.</li><li>We are learning to fairly compare traditional and modern vessel technology.</li><li>I can identify a genuine engineering connection between historical and modern design.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the feature-sorting activity and class discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We met the waka hourua back in Week 2 — a double-hulled design built for stability. Here's a genuinely interesting real fact: that same basic principle is still used in some of the fastest sailing vessels in the world today.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real Modern Connection (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: modern competitive sailing, including vessels used in events like the America's Cup, often uses multi-hulled designs (catamarans) for exactly the same fundamental reason the waka hourua worked centuries ago — a wider base spread across two hulls resists tipping and allows for greater speed and stability than a single hull alone.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a genuinely good engineering idea show up again and again across completely different times and cultures?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Has Actually Changed (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: while the core stability principle has carried through, materials, navigation tools, and speed have changed dramatically — modern vessels use lightweight modern materials and GPS navigation instead of woven sails and star compasses.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Feature Sort and Discuss (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort feature cards (sail material, navigation method, speed, materials, crew size, hull design) into 'Then,' 'Now,' or 'Both,' comparing a traditional waka hourua to a modern sailing vessel.</li><li>Activity: pick one technology that's changed the most and explain why that change mattered; pick one that's stayed essentially the same and explain why it never needed to change.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real engineering principle connects the waka hourua to some modern sailing vessels today?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The waka hourua with kōwhaiwhai sails and solar panels isn't a contradiction — it's a sign. Traditional wisdom and modern technology can serve the same mission. God's Kingdom works the same way: ancient truth, lived out in a new generation.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Jesus said to them, &quot;Therefore every teacher of the law who has become a disciple in the kingdom of heaven is like the owner of a house who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old.&quot;&quot; — <em>Matthew 13:52 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing both what is ancient and true AND what is new and useful helps us serve God's Kingdom wisely in the world we actually live in.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus says a wise disciple brings out 'new treasures as well as old.' What 'old treasures' of your faith do you most want to carry into the future?</li><li>The modern waka uses solar panels alongside traditional carvings. What new tools might God want you to use to carry ancient truths into your generation?</li><li>What traditions or wisdom from your family, church, or culture do you most want to preserve — and how?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Feature sort cards: Then / Now / Both</li><li>Images of modern sailing catamarans if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Modern catamarans and ancient double-hull design</em> — search: &quot;catamaran double hull design history Polynesian influence&quot;. Documents the real engineering connection between traditional and modern vessel design central to this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Bridge&quot; Vessel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a vessel that visually bridges the waka hourua and a modern catamaran, labelling one feature kept from each era.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to keep those specific features?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Between Eras</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short imagined letter from a waka hourua builder to a modern catamaran engineer, comparing their designs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What do you think each builder would be most impressed by in the other's work?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Then/Now/Both Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital three-column chart sorting vessel features into Then, Now, or Both.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which column ended up with the most features, and why might that be?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Double-Hull Legacy</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about modern catamarans and their historical design roots.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;catamaran double hull design history Polynesian influence&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the video most clearly showed the real connection between old and new?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 35: Many Hands, One Ocean":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">REGIONAL PACIFIC PATTERN PAINTING — One Tradition per Square</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore and use art-making conventions from different cultural traditions.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: investigate how cultural identity is expressed through visual art.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: describe how their own art work communicates cultural connection.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that different Pacific cultures developed distinct visual traditions from a shared ancestor.</li><li>We are practising painting in the conventions of ONE chosen Pacific regional style.</li><li>I can explain the artistic choices I made and how they connect to the region I chose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed regional pattern square with written identification of region, colour choices, and motif.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Display three examples side by side: Hawaiian kapa geometric bands, Tongan ngatu tapa diamonds, Māori kōwhaiwhai scrolls. Ask: &quot;These all came from the same ancestral Pacific culture — how did they become so different?&quot; Today each student chooses ONE and works entirely within its conventions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing a Regional Style and Planning (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one: Hawaiian (bold horizontal bands in earthy red/black/brown), Tongan (repeating diamonds and crosses in dark brown/black on white), or Māori (kōwhaiwhai koru scrolls in black/red/white).</li><li>Sketch a rough plan in pencil — what pattern fills the square? What colour palette? How does it repeat?</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Is this pattern speaking in one voice, or mixing styles? Keep it clean and single-region.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Painting the Regional Pattern (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paint chosen pattern using only 2–3 colours from that region.</li><li>Focus on repetition and rhythm. The pattern should feel like it could extend beyond the edges.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Pacific artists worked within rules. The rules are what made the pattern recognisable as theirs.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Assembly and Comparison (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write one sentence identifying the chosen region and why they chose it.</li><li>Groups assemble this week's squares — the quilt shows three different Pacific traditions side by side.</li><li>Ask groups: &quot;Which square looks most different from the others? Why might that region have developed so differently?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">One sentence: what did working within strict colour and pattern rules feel like?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hawaiian kapa, Tongan ngatu, Māori kōwhaiwhai — three different traditions from one shared ancestor. Every Pacific tradition is a different expression of the same human impulse: to make beauty, and to say something true through it. God is glorified in all of it.</li><li>Verse: &quot;There is neither Jew nor Gentile, neither slave nor free, nor is there male and female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus.&quot; — <em>Galatians 3:28 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Every person and culture is equally loved by God — and our different ways of creating beauty reflect the richness of His image across humanity.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says we are 'all one in Christ' while remaining different people from different places. What does unity look like that doesn't erase difference?</li><li>Your regional pattern today is unique to one tradition. What do you think would be lost if all Pacific cultures had looked the same?</li><li>How might celebrating the art traditions of other cultures be an act of love?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reference images of Hawaiian kapa, Tongan ngatu, and Māori kōwhaiwhai</li><li>A4 paper squares</li><li>Paint, dye, or felt tip in regional colour palettes</li><li>Pencils for planning sketches</li><li>Group quilt backing sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific tapa cloth traditions</em> — search: &quot;Pacific tapa cloth ngatu kapa bark cloth tradition&quot;. Shows the range of tapa/kapa traditions and how they are made.</li><li><em>Kōwhaiwhai patterns in context</em> — search: &quot;kowhaiwhai patterns wharenui Maori art rafter&quot;. Shows kōwhaiwhai patterns in real wharenui, reinforcing the convention-based nature of the art form.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Paint Your Regional Pattern Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — paint your chosen regional pattern using only that region's colours and motifs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What rule did you find hardest to stick to?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Same Ancestors</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2–3 sentences: if Hawaiian, Tongan, and Māori art all came from the same original Pacific culture, what does that tell us about how cultures change over time?</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does &quot;the same but different&quot; mean to you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Regional Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a drawing tool to create a digital version of your chosen regional pattern using only that region's colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did working digitally make it easier or harder to stick to the regional conventions?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch: Pacific Tapa Cloth Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on Pacific tapa cloth traditions.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific tapa cloth ngatu kapa bark cloth tradition&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connected the different regional tapa traditions even though they look different?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 31: Passed Down, Not Written Down":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHAKAPAPA AND ORAL TRADITION — A Real, Sophisticated System of Memory</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how stories, traditions, and customs are passed down across generations, and recognise this as a valid form of historical record.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what whakapapa is and how it functions as a genuine system of recorded history.</li><li>We are learning real techniques (rhythm, repetition, pattern) that help oral traditions survive accurately across generations.</li><li>I can explain why oral tradition is a legitimate historical record, not just &quot;stories.&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the memory-chain activity and class discussion. Review of student-created chants for understanding of rhythm/repetition techniques.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For seven weeks we've followed Tama's voyage using a journal that magically records itself. But real history mostly wasn't recorded that way at all. Today we explore something genuinely impressive: how an entire history can be carried accurately across hundreds of years, without a single page of paper.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you had to memorise something important enough that it had to be passed down perfectly for 20 generations, how would you even begin?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Whakapapa? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: whakapapa is a genealogical framework — a way of tracing lines of descent connecting people back through generations, often all the way to significant ancestors, and even to atua (deities) in traditional belief. It is far more than a simple list of names; it functions as a structured, memorised historical record, carrying real information about identity, land connections, and significant events alongside the genealogy itself.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might knowing exactly who you're descended from also mean knowing a great deal of real history at the same time?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Techniques for Accurate Memory (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: oral traditions across many cultures, including in the Pacific, rely on real, deliberate techniques to help information survive accurately across generations — rhythm and repetition (making information easier to recall correctly, the same reason songs are easier to remember than plain speech), waiata (song) and karakia (chant) as structured formats for preserving important information, and the practice of regular, deliberate retelling, often in formal or ceremonial settings, which helped check and correct the story over time.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a story told the SAME way, in a formal setting, every single time, be less likely to change accidentally than a casual story told differently each time?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Memory Chain Activity (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: teach a short, simple chant or rhythmic phrase (summarising part of this term's voyage story) and pass it person-to-person around the room purely by listening and repeating, then compare the start and end versions.</li><li>Discuss: what changed (if anything), and what made the parts that survived intact easier to remember?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is whakapapa, and why is it more than just a list of names?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Hina taught Tama the chants, generation after generation, no paper required. Moses commanded Israel to teach God's words to their children constantly — in the morning, at bedtime, walking the road. Oral tradition was how God's story survived for centuries before a single word was written down.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Impress these commandments on your children. Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road, when you lie down and when you get up.&quot; — <em>Deuteronomy 6:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully passing on what we've received — stories, wisdom, faith — is one of the most important ways we serve the generations who come after us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God commanded parents to talk about His words 'when you walk along the road.' Who in your life has passed something important to you just through ordinary conversation?</li><li>Oral tradition kept the Pacific peoples' history alive for centuries. What story from your family or faith community do YOU need to be a keeper of?</li><li>What would you most want to pass on to the generation after yours — and how would you do it without any technology?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A simple chant/phrase prepared in advance for the memory chain activity</li><li>Paper for student-created chants</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is whakapapa?</em> — search: &quot;what is whakapapa explained Maori genealogy&quot;. Provides a real, respectful, age-appropriate explanation of whakapapa as a genuine historical record system.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Whakapapa-Style Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple, respectful illustrated diagram showing how a genealogical chart connects generations, using a fictional family line as your example (not a real whakapapa, out of respect).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how much information a chart like this can hold?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Story Worth Remembering</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short chant or rhyme (in your own words) summarising one key moment from this term's voyage, using rhythm and repetition to make it memorable.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60–100 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What choices did you make to help your chant be easy to remember accurately?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Record a Spoken Chant</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If equipment allows, record yourself or your group performing a chant created in class, and consider how recording changes (or doesn't change) the oral tradition.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Does recording a chant change what makes oral tradition special?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Whakapapa</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining whakapapa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;what is whakapapa explained Maori genealogy&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail from the video most changed how you think about &quot;history&quot; as a concept?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 32: The Ocean Today":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE GREAT PACIFIC GARBAGE PATCH — A Real, Current Challenge</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how people's actions affect the sustainability of natural environments, both locally and globally.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what is genuinely happening in the Pacific Ocean today, connecting back to currents studied in Week 2.</li><li>We are learning that the same forces that helped real voyagers can also carry real problems.</li><li>I can identify a realistic, age-appropriate action connected to a real environmental issue.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the sorting activity and the realistic action-pledge discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;In Week 2, we learned how ocean currents acted like roads, carrying real voyagers across the Pacific. Today we look honestly at something happening in those same waters right now — gently, but truthfully.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Real, Current Issue (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the same ocean currents studied in Week 2 are real, and they are known to gather floating plastic debris into large, diffuse collection zones — the best known being the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, located between Hawaii and California. It is important to clarify accurately: it isn't a solid 'island' of rubbish, but a vast area where plastic debris, much of it broken into small fragments, becomes more concentrated than surrounding ocean water.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter to describe this accurately, rather than imagining something more dramatic (like a solid floating island) than what's really there?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Connecting It Back (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: revisit Week 2's current map — the very same real current systems that carried voyaging canoes can also carry plastic debris released into the ocean today, gathering in similar predictable patterns.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Realistic Response (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: sort picture cards of ocean debris versus natural materials into two groups.</li><li>Activity: in groups, brainstorm one realistic action your class/school could genuinely take connected to ocean care (e.g. reducing single-use plastic, a litter-free lunch initiative, writing to a local council) — keep it achievable, not symbolic.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence accurately describing what the Great Pacific Garbage Patch actually is (and isn't).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The same currents that carried the voyagers now carry plastic. Genesis 2 says God put humans in the garden to 'tend and keep' it — to work it and care for it. That responsibility didn't end in Eden. It travels with us to every new shore.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The LORD God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and take care of it.&quot; — <em>Genesis 2:15 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Caring for the ocean — and all of creation — is part of bringing God's Kingdom to earth: tending and keeping what He made and called 'very good.'</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God put humans in the garden to 'work it AND take care of it.' What do you think the difference is between those two things?</li><li>Tama asked you to 'show him there is still hope' for the ocean. What hope can you genuinely offer?</li><li>What is one concrete thing you could do this week to be a better 'keeper' of the part of creation you live in?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Picture cards: ocean debris vs. natural materials</li><li>The Week 2 ocean current map for reference</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch?</em> — search: &quot;Great Pacific Garbage Patch explained for kids&quot;. Provides an accurate, age-appropriate explanation, avoiding the common &quot;floating island&quot; misconception.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Real Scale</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a diagram accurately showing the Great Pacific Garbage Patch as a diffuse area of scattered debris (not a solid island), labelled with its real location between Hawaii and California.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does an accurate drawing matter more than a dramatic one here?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Realistic Pledge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, realistic pledge for your class or school connected to ocean care, explaining why you chose that specific action.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What makes your pledge realistic rather than just a nice idea?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Awareness Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple, accurate digital poster about the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, correcting one common misconception.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What misconception did you choose to correct, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ocean Plastic</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Great Pacific Garbage Patch explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What fact from the video surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 33: When the Sky Turns Violent":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC CYCLONES — Real Science, Real Preparedness</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate extreme weather phenomena and how communities prepare for and respond to them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real science of how tropical cyclones form.</li><li>We are learning genuine, real ways Pacific communities prepare for extreme weather today.</li><li>I can combine traditional and modern knowledge into a realistic safety plan.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the checklist-building activity. Review of student understanding connecting cyclone formation to earlier weather/climate content (Week 1).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Back in Week 1, we learned the real difference between weather and climate. Today we look at one of the most powerful real weather events in the Pacific: tropical cyclones — and how real communities prepare for them, both today and in the past.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How Cyclones Really Form (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: tropical cyclones form over warm ocean water (generally above 26.5°C), where warm, moist air rises rapidly, creating an area of low pressure. As more warm, moist air rushes in to replace it, the system begins to rotate (influenced by the Earth's rotation, similar to the trade wind effect studied in Week 2), eventually forming the organised, swirling storm system recognisable as a cyclone.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why does it make sense that the warm tropical Pacific — the very same region voyagers like Tama sailed through — is also a real region where cyclones commonly form?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Modern Preparedness (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Pacific nations today use real, modern early-warning systems — satellite tracking, weather forecasting services, and official cyclone warning categories — to give communities real advance notice, often days ahead, to prepare or evacuate if necessary.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What real advantage does days of advance warning give a community, compared to a voyager reading the sky just hours before a storm hit?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building a Combined Checklist (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, create a 'storm preparedness checklist' combining one traditional sky/sea-reading sign (from earlier weeks) with one modern safety practice (early warning systems, secured shelter, emergency supplies).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, in your own words, how a tropical cyclone actually forms.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama's storm in Week 3 was terrifying. The cyclones in your time are stronger. But Mark 4 records a moment when Jesus stood in a boat being swamped by waves — and spoke three words that stopped the storm. He is Lord over the very things we fear most.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He got up, rebuked the wind and said to the waves, &quot;Quiet! Be still!&quot; Then the wind died down and it was completely calm.&quot; — <em>Mark 4:39 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — The God who calms the storm loves us enough to be with us in the storm — and has power over every force we face.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The disciples were terrified even though Jesus was IN the boat. Have you ever been scared of something even while knowing God was with you? What happened?</li><li>Jesus didn't stop the storm before it happened — He was in it with them first. What does that tell you about how God works with us in hard times?</li><li>What is the storm in your life right now that you most need to hear 'Peace! Be still!' spoken into?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple diagram of cyclone formation if available</li><li>Checklist templates for the combined activity</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How do cyclones form?</em> — search: &quot;how do tropical cyclones form explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, accurate, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Cyclone Formation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled diagram showing warm ocean water, rising air, and the rotating storm system forming above it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the process was trickiest to show clearly in a drawing?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Community Preparedness Notice</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, clear preparedness notice (as if for a real community) combining a traditional sign and a modern safety step.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might combining both kinds of knowledge make a community safer than relying on just one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Cyclone Tracker Mockup</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital mockup of a cyclone tracking map, showing a storm's path and a warning zone.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information would be most important to show clearly on a real tracker?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Cyclone Science</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how cyclones form.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how do tropical cyclones form explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real fact from the video would you want a younger student to know?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 34: A Voice from the Sky":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">GPS — Real Technology, Honestly Compared to Real Wayfinding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technologies have a real impact on society, and that no technology is purely &quot;better&quot; without considering trade-offs.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what GPS actually is and how it genuinely works.</li><li>We are learning to fairly compare GPS with traditional wayfinding, without assuming newer is automatically better.</li><li>I can evaluate two technologies in different realistic scenarios rather than declaring one universally superior.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the scenario-decision activity and class discussion on trade-offs.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;We've spent this whole term learning real, sophisticated traditional wayfinding. Today we meet GPS honestly — not as something that makes the old ways obsolete, but as one more real tool with its own real strengths and real limitations.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How GPS Actually Works (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: GPS (Global Positioning System) relies on a network of satellites orbiting Earth, each constantly broadcasting a precise time signal. A GPS receiver (like one in a phone or ship) calculates its exact position by measuring the tiny time differences between signals received from several satellites at once — a genuinely clever piece of real engineering.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Strengths and Real Limitations (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: GPS is extremely accurate and doesn't require years of training to use — but it depends entirely on functioning satellites, a working receiver, and battery power, and it provides only position and direction, not the rich environmental awareness (wind, swells, wildlife, sky signs) a trained wayfinder builds over years.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;In what realistic situation might GPS actually fail you, when traditional wayfinding skills wouldn't?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Scenario Decisions (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: present realistic scenarios (clear night with good stars vs. GPS device with a dead battery; cloudy night with no stars but working GPS; a sudden equipment failure mid-voyage) and have teams decide which method they'd trust in each case, with reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name one real strength and one real limitation of GPS navigation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: GPS tells you exactly where you are and exactly where to go. But it requires a signal, a device, and a battery. Walking with God is more like the star compass — slower to learn, but internalized, always available, and pointing truly.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we live by faith, not by sight.&quot; — <em>2 Corinthians 5:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing God deeply — not just using spiritual 'tools' — means developing a relationship with Him that guides us from the inside, not just the outside.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>'By faith, not by sight' — what is the difference between trusting something you can see and trusting something you can't? Which is harder?</li><li>Hina's star knowledge was memorised, internalised, always available. What spiritual truths do you have so deeply in you that they guide you automatically?</li><li>In what area of your life right now are you being asked to walk by faith rather than by what you can see?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario cards for the decision activity</li><li>A real GPS device or phone map app for demonstration if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How does GPS work?</em> — search: &quot;how does GPS work satellites explained for kids&quot;. Gives a clear, accurate, age-appropriate explanation of the real technology discussed in this lesson.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate GPS Satellites at Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a diagram showing satellites in orbit sending signals down to a receiver, labelling the basic process.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of how GPS works surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Fair Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, balanced comparison of GPS and traditional wayfinding, including a genuine strength and limitation of each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why is it more honest to compare fairly than to declare one method simply &quot;better&quot;?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Scenario Decision Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital chart listing today's scenarios and which navigation method you'd choose for each, with a one-line reason.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did any scenario genuinely surprise you in your own decision?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: How GPS Works</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining GPS technology.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;how does GPS work satellites explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real-world example from the video helped GPS make sense to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 40: Old Motifs, New Square":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">BLENDED DESIGN — Drawing and Painting Two Traditions Together</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: use drawing and painting to combine and transform visual ideas.</li><li>Communicating and Interpreting: explain how their art work expresses a personal response to cultural traditions.</li><li>Understanding the Arts in Context: explore how contemporary Pacific artists blend traditional and modern influences.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how contemporary Pacific artists combine traditional motifs to create something new.</li><li>We are practising intentional design — making choices about why two motifs belong together.</li><li>I can create a blended design square and explain the relationship between the two motifs I chose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Completed blended square and written explanation of the two motifs chosen and how they connect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Share an example of contemporary Pacific art blending traditions. Ask: &quot;How do you know this is both traditional AND modern? What rules did the artist keep? What did they change?&quot; Today's square is a dialogue between two earlier traditions the student has already practised.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing and Planning the Combination (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Look back at the group's quilt squares from Weeks 1–7. Choose TWO motifs to bring together: e.g. kōwhaiwhai scroll with petroglyph turtle, woven grid background with star cluster dots, regional tapa pattern with kōwhaiwhai border.</li><li>Sketch a rough plan: which motif is the dominant layer? Which is the accent? How will they sit together without one overwhelming the other?</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;This is not a random combination. Why do THESE two belong together on YOUR square?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Drawing and Painting the Blended Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the background motif in pencil first — lightly, as a foundation layer. Paint in its traditional colours.</li><li>Add the second motif on top or interwoven, using its own colours. Allow overlap where it feels intentional.</li><li>Teacher prompt: &quot;Does this feel like a conversation between two ideas, or just two things on one page?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflection and Assembly (7 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Write 2 sentences on the back: which two motifs combined and what the combination is trying to say.</li><li>Add square to group quilt. Groups step back and look at the full sequence of 8 squares.</li><li>Ask: &quot;What story does your group's quilt tell from Week 1 to Week 8?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Describe your blended square in one sentence, starting with: &quot;This square shows...&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Today's square blends two earlier motifs into something new. This is exactly what Paul describes: we are God's masterpiece, His workmanship, crafted with care and purpose. And masterpieces take time, layering, and intention.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For we are God's handiwork, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared in advance for us to do.&quot; — <em>Ephesians 2:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every act of creation done with intention and care reflects the God who made us — and brings His Kingdom creativity into the world.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul calls us God's 'handiwork' — literally His masterpiece. Does that feel true for you? What makes it hard to believe sometimes?</li><li>Your blended square combines two traditions into something new. How might God be combining different parts of YOUR life and experience into something that couldn't exist any other way?</li><li>God 'prepared in advance' the good works He has for you. What does it feel like to know your life's purpose was thought about before you were born?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper, one square per student (the final square for the group quilt)</li><li>Pencils, erasers, and paint or coloured pencils matching earlier weeks' palettes</li><li>Students' completed quilt squares from Weeks 1–7 for reference</li><li>Reference images of contemporary Pacific artists blending traditional and modern styles</li><li>Scrap paper for planning the motif combination before painting</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Contemporary Pacific art blending traditions</em> — search: &quot;contemporary Pacific Island artists traditional motifs&quot;. Shows real contemporary Pacific artists combining traditional motifs in new ways, modelling the blending task students are asked to do.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create Your Blended Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — combine two earlier motifs into one blended square and add it as the final piece in your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why do your two chosen motifs belong together on this square?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Explain Your Blend</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of the two motifs you combined and what their combination says about the whole voyage.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How is combining two traditions different from just copying one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Blended Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing tool (Canva, Google Drawings) to layer two motifs from earlier weeks into one digital design.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder about combining two ideas than using just one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Blending Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on contemporary Pacific artists blending traditional and modern styles.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;contemporary Pacific Island artists traditional motifs&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the artist keep from tradition, and what did they change?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Trial 36: Tama's Descendants":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PACIFIC PEOPLES IN AOTEAROA TODAY — A Living, Continuing Story</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the past is connected to the present, and that historical events have living, ongoing consequences.</li><li>Understanding Diversity: understand how cultural identity is shaped by both heritage and present-day community.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that the Pacific migration story studied this term is not only history — it has living, present-day continuation.</li><li>We are learning that Aotearoa today is home to large, real, and diverse Pacific communities.</li><li>I can recognise and respectfully discuss the connection between a historical event and present-day people and communities.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion and the acknowledgement statement created together. This is a sensitive, relational lesson — assess respectful engagement and thoughtful reflection over factual recall alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;For eight weeks, we've followed a single voyage from thousands of years ago. Today, we close the gap between then and now: the real descendants of voyages like Tama's are alive today, all around us, including right here in Aotearoa.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: have you ever thought about how a story from thousands of years ago could still be genuinely connected to people living in your own town today?&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Living, Continuing Story (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: the Pacific migration story studied this term did not end in the past — Aotearoa today is home to large, vibrant Pacific communities, including people of Samoan, Tongan, Cook Islands, Niuean, Fijian, and many other Pacific heritages, alongside tangata whenua Māori whose own history traces directly back to the voyaging story explored this term.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter, after a whole term studying this as 'history,' to also recognise it as a living, present-day reality for real people today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Handling This Respectfully (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: this is a moment for genuine respect and care. If any students in the class have personal or family connections to this story, this is an opportunity for pride and voice, never an obligation or a spotlight. Make clear that sharing is always entirely optional, and that everyone's connection to today's lesson, personal or purely academic, is equally valid.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Class Acknowledgement (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a respectful class discussion/circle, talk about Pacific communities living in Aotearoa today — their cultures, languages, and continuing connection to the voyaging story learned this term.</li><li>Activity: as a class, create a simple, sincere acknowledgement statement recognising the achievement of the original voyagers, to read aloud together as a meaningful close to the discussion.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: why does it matter that the story we've studied this term is not only history, but also a living, present-day reality?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama says his descendants might be in this very room. Kapa haka is the living proof — the chant survived, the culture survived, the story survived. Psalm 145 describes exactly this: one generation commending God's works to the next, the chain unbroken across centuries.</li><li>Verse: &quot;One generation commends your works to another; they tell of your mighty acts.&quot; — <em>Psalm 145:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Faithfully passing on what we have received — stories, culture, faith — is how we serve the generations who come after us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 145 says each generation commends God's works to the next. What has been commended to you — by a parent, a grandparent, a teacher — that you don't want to lose?</li><li>Kapa haka kept the culture alive across centuries with no written records. What is something in YOUR culture or faith that must be passed on person to person, not just written down?</li><li>Who are you already 'commending God's works' to — even if you don't think of it that way?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>No specific physical resources required — this lesson centres on respectful class discussion</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific communities in Aotearoa today</em> — search: &quot;Pacific peoples in New Zealand today communities culture&quot;. Provides real, respectful, contemporary context connecting this term's history to present-day communities.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate &quot;Then and Now&quot; Side by Side</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple illustration showing a voyager from this term's story on one side, and a contemporary scene of Pacific community life today on the other, connected by a single thread or line.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to connect the two halves of your illustration, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Across Time</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, respectful letter from a present-day perspective to a voyager like Tama, telling them what their courage helped make possible today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you most want a voyager from the past to know about today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Acknowledgement Display</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital slide or poster of the class's acknowledgement statement, suitable for sharing or displaying.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made your class's wording feel genuine and sincere, rather than generic?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Living Connections</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Pacific communities in Aotearoa today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific peoples in New Zealand today communities culture&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connection between past and present stood out to you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Trial 37: Caring for the Ocean":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">REAL PACIFIC OCEAN CONSERVATION — Genuine Hope in Action</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: explore how individuals and communities take real, active responsibility for environmental sustainability.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about real, genuine ocean conservation efforts happening in the Pacific today.</li><li>We are learning to design a realistic, achievable action connected to ocean care.</li><li>I can identify a real example of positive environmental action and explain why it matters.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the research/discussion activity and the realistic pledge created.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Last week, we faced an honest, difficult truth about plastic in the Pacific. Today, we look for real, genuine hope — because real people and real communities across the Pacific are actively working to protect the ocean right now.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Conservation in Action (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: present 1–2 real, simplified examples of Pacific ocean conservation. One genuine example: large-scale Marine Protected Areas have been established by several Pacific nations (such as the Cook Islands' Marae Moana, one of the world's largest protected ocean areas) to limit harmful fishing practices and protect marine ecosystems. Community-led beach and reef clean-up initiatives are also real, ongoing efforts across many Pacific communities, including in Aotearoa.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might protecting a very large area of ocean, rather than just a small part, make a genuinely bigger real difference?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Researching and Sharing (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in small groups, research or discuss (using teacher-provided simplified material) one real example of ocean conservation, then present it back to the class as &quot;good news for Tama.&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Realistic Pledge (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: design a realistic, genuinely doable conservation pledge for your own class or school to commit to — emphasise achievable action over symbolic gestures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence naming ONE real, genuine example of Pacific ocean conservation discussed today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama asks you to show him there is still hope for the ocean. The answer — healthy fish, marine reserves, communities taking care of what they received — is the answer Genesis always pointed to: we were made to tend and keep, and when we do, life returns.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God saw all that he had made, and it was very good.&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Caring for creation — keeping it 'very good' — is one of the most direct ways we bring God's Kingdom purposes to earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God called creation 'very good.' What does it mean for us to be guardians of something God Himself declared good?</li><li>The healthy fish in the marine reserve show what creation looks like when humans protect rather than exploit it. What does that give you hope about?</li><li>What one specific action could you commit to this term that helps 'keep' part of God's good creation?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simplified research material on real conservation examples (teacher-prepared)</li><li>Paper for pledge-writing</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Pacific Ocean conservation success stories</em> — search: &quot;Pacific Ocean conservation marine protected area success&quot;. Provides real, genuine examples of positive environmental action in the Pacific, central to this lesson's hopeful framing.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Protected Ocean Area</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a scene showing a thriving, protected area of ocean, labelling at least one reason it's protected.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to show thriving in your protected ocean scene?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Good News for Tama</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, hopeful letter to Tama sharing the real conservation example you researched today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose to share this particular piece of good news with him?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Conservation Fact File</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital fact file about one real Pacific conservation initiative, including what it protects and why it matters.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real number or fact from your research surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Real Ocean Hope</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about a real Pacific Ocean conservation success.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Pacific Ocean conservation marine protected area success&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the video felt most genuinely hopeful to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Trial 38: A Rising Sea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SEA LEVEL RISE — Real Science, Gently and Honestly Explained</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science (Planet Earth and Beyond)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Investigate how environmental change affects places and communities, using real scientific evidence appropriate to the age group.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the real, basic science behind sea level rise.</li><li>We are learning why this issue genuinely matters more for some Pacific communities than others.</li><li>I can explain a real environmental process using a simple, accurate model.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the demonstration and group discussion. This is a sensitive topic — assess understanding and thoughtful engagement, framed with genuine care rather than alarm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Today's topic deserves real gentleness and real honesty, both at once. We're going to understand, simply and accurately, why sea levels are rising — and why this matters so much to some of the very places connected to our voyage this term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Real, Basic Science (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: sea levels are rising for two main real reasons: thermal expansion (warmer ocean water genuinely takes up slightly more space than cooler water) and melting land ice (glaciers and ice sheets on land, when they melt, add genuinely new water into the ocean that wasn't there before).</li><li>Demonstration: use a clear container with ice and water to gently demonstrate (note: ice already floating in water melting does NOT raise the water level much, since it already displaces its own weight — but ice melting that was sitting on LAND and running into the ocean genuinely does add new water. This is an important, accurate distinction worth making clearly to avoid a common misconception.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why It Matters More for Some Places (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: low-lying Pacific island nations (some with average heights only a few metres above sea level) face genuinely real, serious challenges from even relatively small rises in sea level, in ways that don't affect higher, larger landmasses like Aotearoa in the same way.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might the very same amount of sea level rise affect two different real places so differently?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Holding Hope and Honesty Together (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Discuss as a class: why might it matter to understand a real challenge clearly AND hold onto real hope at the same time, rather than only focusing on one or the other?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining, accurately, ONE real reason sea levels are rising.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tuvalu is disappearing into the ocean. Paul writes that creation 'groans' — and that it waits for God's people to respond. Romans 8 frames this not as hopeless but as a groan of expectation: things are not as they should be, and God's people are called to act.</li><li>Verse: &quot;We know that the whole creation has been groaning as in the pains of childbirth right up to the present time.&quot; — <em>Romans 8:22 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Responding to creation's 'groan' by working for justice and care is one of the clearest ways we can bring God's Kingdom to earth.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says creation 'groans.' When you look at the Tuvalu image, what does that groan sound like to you?</li><li>Romans 8 says this groaning is like 'the pains of childbirth' — painful, but leading somewhere. What might God be bringing forth through this crisis, if His people respond faithfully?</li><li>What would it look like for YOUR school or community to respond to creation's groan in a way that brings God's Kingdom closer?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A clear container, ice, and water for the demonstration</li><li>A simple world map showing low-lying Pacific nations if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Why are sea levels rising?</em> — search: &quot;why are sea levels rising explained for kids gently&quot;. Gives an accurate, gentle, age-appropriate explanation of the real science behind this sensitive topic.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Two Real Causes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw two simple labelled diagrams: one showing thermal expansion (warmer water taking up more space) and one showing melting land ice adding new water to the ocean.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of the two causes was trickier to show clearly, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Message of Hope and Honesty</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, gentle message that holds both the real challenge and real hope together, as if writing to someone living in a low-lying Pacific community.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you balance honesty and hope in your message?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Before/After Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital diagram showing a low-lying coastline before and after a small rise in sea level.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the comparison help you understand that words alone might not?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rising Seas</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short, gentle video explaining sea level rise.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;why are sea levels rising explained for kids gently&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the explanation made the most sense to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Trial 39: The Waka of the Future":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">SUSTAINABLE OCEAN TECHNOLOGY — Real Innovation Inspired by Tradition</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand how technological development can draw on both historical knowledge and new innovation to address real present-day needs.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about real, genuine examples of sustainable ocean technology being developed today.</li><li>We are learning to design a vessel that combines traditional wisdom, modern technology, and sustainability.</li><li>I can synthesise a full term's learning into one original, considered design.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the design process and final presentations. This is a genuine culminating task — assess synthesis of prior learning across the term, not just creativity alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is genuinely the last new content of the term before our final celebration. Today, we look at real sustainable ocean technology being developed right now — and then design something of our own, combining everything we've learned this entire term.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Sustainable Innovation Today (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: real modern sailing and ocean technology increasingly draws on sustainable design principles — including solar-powered vessels, sails made from recyclable or more sustainable materials, and a genuine renewed interest in wind-powered cargo shipping (sometimes called 'sail cargo') as a lower-emission alternative to fully fuel-powered shipping.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might modern engineers be looking back toward wind power, something voyagers like Tama relied on completely, as part of a more sustainable future?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Designing the Waka of the Future (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in teams, design (sketch or build a simple model) a 'future waka' combining traditional wayfinding wisdom (from this term's content), modern technology, and sustainable/eco-friendly materials or features.</li><li>Activity: present your design, explaining one traditional feature kept, one modern feature added, and one sustainable choice made — drawing explicitly on real content from across the whole term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what ONE traditional feature from this term would you never leave out of a &quot;waka of the future,&quot; and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama asks: if you were to build the next great voyaging vessel, what would it look like — knowing the old wisdom, the new tools, AND everything the ocean still needs from us? That question is exactly the one God asks His people in every generation: what are you building, and who are you building it for?</li><li>Verse: &quot;&quot;For I know the plans I have for you,&quot; declares the LORD, &quot;plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.&quot;&quot; — <em>Jeremiah 29:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Building toward God's Kingdom — with the gifts, wisdom, and creativity He's given us — is what it means to bring His future into the present.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God says He has plans for you — 'plans to give you hope and a future.' How does that change what you think you're capable of designing and building?</li><li>Your 'future waka' must carry the old wisdom AND the new tools. What old wisdom from this term do you most want to carry into your future?</li><li>If you were building something for God's Kingdom — not a waka but something in your actual life — what would it be, and who would it serve?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Materials for building simple vessel models (card, straws, foil)</li><li>Paper for design sketches</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Sustainable sailing technology today</em> — search: &quot;sustainable sail cargo ships wind power today&quot;. Introduces real, genuine examples of sustainable ocean technology inspired by traditional sail power.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design and Illustrate Your Future Waka</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a detailed, labelled illustration of your future waka design, clearly showing the traditional, modern, and sustainable features you chose.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which single feature are you proudest of including, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Pitching the Future Waka</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;pitch&quot; explaining your future waka design and why each feature was chosen, as if presenting it to the Council of Wayfinders.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of your pitch do you think would convince the Council most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Design Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital poster of your future waka design, labelling its traditional, modern, and sustainable features.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did combining all three types of feature change your design process?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Sustainable Sailing</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about sustainable sail-powered shipping today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;sustainable sail cargo ships wind power today&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real example from the video most inspired your own design?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Trial 40: The Map Is Complete":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMPLETING THE VOYAGE QUILT — A Term-Long Artwork, Finished</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Art)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Communicating and Interpreting: share the ideas in a completed body of work, and reflect on the development of that work over time.</li><li>Developing Ideas: develop and refine ideas using a range of materials and techniques across an extended project.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are completing a genuine, term-long collaborative artwork.</li><li>We are reflecting on how a shared artwork develops and changes over an extended period.</li><li>I can narrate and take pride in a finished, collaborative piece of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the final quilt square contribution and each group's 'walk the quilt' narration. This is the culminating, celebratory assessment of the whole term's Art strand.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Eight weeks ago, your first quilt square was a simple folded, cut piece of paper. Today, each group finishes its quilt and looks back at the whole journey it tells, square by square.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Final Quilt Square (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: each student creates their final quilt square — inspired by Thursday's 'waka of the future' designs. This is an open, free-choice square: use any technique from across the term (dots, woven lines, wave burst, star burst, silhouette, regional style) or combine elements. The focus is on making this final square feel like a considered ending to the set.</li><li>Glue all final squares onto each group's backing sheet, completing the quilt at eight or nine squares per group.</li><li>Once glued, lay every group's finished quilt out side by side so the whole class can see the full range at once.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Walking the Quilt (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in small groups, do a final 'walk the quilt' moment — narrate your own group's quilt from its very first square (Week 1's Lapita pottery pattern) to its last (today's future waka square), in order, as if telling the full story to someone seeing it for the very first time.</li><li>Optional: if time allows, swap and narrate another group's quilt too — noticing how the same journey looks different through another group's choices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking at your group's finished quilt, what part are you proudest to have contributed, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tama looks at the quilts and says: look how far you've come. This is the last square — your square, your voice, your part of the story. Revelation 21:5 describes God saying the same thing at the end of all things: 'I am making everything new.' Your square is a small echo of that promise.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He who was seated on the throne said, &quot;I am making everything new!&quot;&quot; — <em>Revelation 21:5 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Every act of faithful creativity — done to God's glory — is a small participation in the new creation God is bringing about.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>God says 'I am making everything new.' What would you most like to see made new — in your life, your community, your world?</li><li>This is your final square — free choice, your voice. What did you choose to put in it, and why?</li><li>The whole quilt is made of different squares, different voices, different parts of the story. How is that like the church — the body of Christ?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A4 paper squares for the final square</li><li>Safety scissors</li><li>Each group's quilt backing sheet</li><li>Space to lay all quilts out side by side</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Create and Complete Your Final Quilt Square</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This week's main activity IS the art focus — create your final quilt square using any technique from this term, and complete your group's quilt.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking at all your squares from Week 1 to now, what changed the most about how you use the technique?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Narrate the Complete Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short narration, as if guiding a visitor along your group's finished quilt from start to finish, describing the whole voyage in your own words.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What moment in the whole nine-week journey did you most enjoy retelling?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Quilt Documentary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compile photos of your group's quilt squares across the term (if available) into a simple digital slideshow telling the story from start to finish.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What changed the most between your Week 1 square and your final one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Reflect on the Term's Art Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own quest log entries from across the whole term and choose the one quilt square you're proudest of.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does that square show about how your skills or understanding grew this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 1: Arrival and Legacy":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 1 — Testing Real History Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate understanding of historical events, evidence, and the people involved, drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating what we have learned about arrival, settlement, and legacy from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are working as a team under time pressure to recall accurate historical facts.</li><li>I can connect real historical evidence to real present-day significance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 History content. Score team performance on the quick-fire round and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting before the end of term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The Council has called us back one final time. Today's station tests everything we've learned since arriving in Aotearoa — the landing itself, the long pause, the scattering across the Pacific, and the living legacy that continues into today.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the format: this is a rapid-fire True/False round, same as Week 5. Read each statement aloud; teams confer briefly (10–15 seconds) then signal their answer.</li><li>Award one point per correct answer, with a bonus point for a team that can correctly explain WHY a false statement is false.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Quick-Fire Question Bank (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Aotearoa was one of the very first landmasses on Earth settled by humans. (FALSE — it was one of the LAST major landmasses settled, around 1300 CE)</li><li>2. Wairau Bar is a real archaeological site providing evidence of early settlement in Aotearoa. (TRUE)</li><li>3. Aotearoa had many native land mammals before humans arrived, similar to most other landmasses. (FALSE — it had no native land mammals aside from bats)</li><li>4. The rua kūmara was a real storage pit used to protect kūmara through Aotearoa's colder winters. (TRUE)</li><li>5. Pounamu (greenstone) became significant in Aotearoa for both practical tools and valued ornaments. (TRUE)</li><li>6. Pacific settlement happened all at once, in a single continuous wave of voyaging. (FALSE — there was a real, still-debated 'long pause' of roughly 1,000+ years between the Lapita spread and the later voyages to Hawaii, Rapa Nui, and Aotearoa)</li><li>7. Whakapapa is simply a list of names with no other real information attached. (FALSE — it is a structured historical record carrying real information about identity, land, and events)</li><li>8. The same double-hull principle behind the waka hourua is still used in some modern sailing vessels today. (TRUE)</li><li>9. Aotearoa today has no real, living connection to the Pacific migration story studied this term. (FALSE — Aotearoa is home to large, living Pacific communities with direct, real connection to this story)</li><li>10. Real, contemporary Pacific and Māori artists still actively practice traditions like weaving and carving today. (TRUE)</li><li>Optional extension: invite teams to write one additional True/False statement of their own from Weeks 6–9 content, to challenge another team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal team scores. Briefly revisit any question more than one team got wrong, with a short explanation, before moving to tomorrow's station.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As a team, write down the ONE fact from Weeks 6–9 you're most confident you'll remember in a year's time — and why it stuck.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire question bank (above) printed or read aloud</li><li>A simple way for teams to signal answers (cards, hands, buzzers)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Final Wayfinder Medal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a medal or badge that could be awarded to a team that performs well across the whole Wayfinder's Examination.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the medal design.</li><li>Include a symbol representing the whole term's journey, not just one week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular symbol to represent the entire term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of history content from across the WHOLE term you understood best, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking back over ten weeks, how did your understanding change from Week 1 to now?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Final Digital Scoreboard</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital scoreboard to track team scores across all of this week's stations, building on Week 5's format.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would make a scoreboard feel like a fitting close to a whole term's effort?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Whole Term's Quest Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own Quest Log entries from across the entire term and choose the one you're proudest of.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does that entry show about how much you've grown this term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 2: Distance and Climate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 2 — Testing Real Geography Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: demonstrate understanding of geographic features, scale, climate, and environmental change drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our memory of real Pacific geography from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are working under pressure, using memory and reasoning rather than guesswork.</li><li>I can connect real geographic concepts (latitude, distance, ocean health) into one coherent understanding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Geography content. Use results to identify which geographic concepts (latitude, distance, ocean conservation) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday tested your memory of history. Today, the Council tests your understanding of place itself — distance, climate, and the real state of the ocean that carried this whole story.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out the large Pacific floor/wall map used throughout the term. Prepare a set of challenge cards covering: real distances (gateway voyage, Tahiti–Hawaii, Tahiti–Rapa Nui, Tahiti–Aotearoa), real latitude figures for each island group, and real ocean conservation facts from Week 9.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round 1: call out two locations at a time (e.g. &quot;Samoa/Tonga to Tahiti&quot; or &quot;Tahiti to Rapa Nui&quot;) and have teams estimate and rank the real distance compared to the others, without looking at notes.</li><li>Round 2: call out an island group and have teams correctly identify its real climate type (tropical, subtropical, temperate) based on latitude.</li><li>Round 3: ask teams to recall one real example of genuine Pacific ocean conservation from Week 9, and explain why it matters.</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which was harder to remember accurately — a number, like distance or latitude, or a real-world example, like a conservation project? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Briefly re-show correct answers on the map for anything answered incorrectly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which real Pacific geography fact from this term do you feel most confident about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large Pacific floor/wall map</li><li>Challenge cards: distances, latitude, conservation facts</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Whole Voyage Route from Memory</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without looking at any reference, draw your best memory of the full voyage route studied this term, from the Lapita homeland to Aotearoa and beyond.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your drawing to a real map afterward — what did you remember well, and what surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of geography content from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you come to understand it so clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Term Map Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital map tool to label as many real locations, distances, and climate zones from across the term as you can recall, unaided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many could you label correctly without checking back?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Term's Map Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your star compass, distance, and latitude work from across the whole term. What's the one geography skill you feel most confident in now?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 3: The Real World, Honestly":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 3 — Testing Real Science Knowledge from Weeks 6–9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of seasonal change, extreme weather, and environmental science drawn from a full term of prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of real seasonal, weather, and environmental science from Weeks 6–9.</li><li>We are using real content to reason through honest, sometimes difficult scenarios.</li><li>I can apply scientific understanding to real present-day issues with care and accuracy.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Science content. Use results to identify which scientific concepts (seasonal survival, cyclones, sea level rise) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Today's station asks you to bring together everything we've learned about real science this term — from surviving Aotearoa's first winter, to understanding the real forces shaping the ocean's future.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Prepare 4–5 scenario cards covering: surviving Aotearoa's seasons (Week 6), how cyclones form (Week 8), and the real, gentle science of sea level rise (Week 9).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Present each scenario to teams in turn. Teams must respond using real content from this term — for example: &quot;Explain, using real science, why early Aotearoa settlers needed the rua kūmara,&quot; or &quot;Explain, using real science, why sea levels are rising.&quot;</li><li>Award points for both accuracy AND for genuinely thoughtful, well-reasoned answers, especially on the more sensitive topics (sea level rise, cyclones).</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which real science concept from this term do you think matters most for understanding the world you actually live in today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Use this moment to reinforce that understanding difficult real-world science, calmly and accurately, is itself a genuine achievement.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real science concept from this term do you think will stay with you the longest, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scenario cards covering seasons, cyclones, and sea level rise</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate &quot;Understanding, Not Fear&quot;</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an image representing how real scientific understanding (rather than fear) helps people face a real environmental challenge.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to show as the source of hope in your illustration?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science concept from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it clearly to someone who missed this whole term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Science Concept Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital concept map connecting the science topics studied this term (weather, seasons, cyclones, sea level rise) and how they relate to each other.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connection between topics surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Voyage Forecasts and Science Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your science notes and forecasts from across the term. Has your confidence in explaining real weather and environmental science grown? How can you tell?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Wayfinder's Examination — Station 4: Then, Now, and What's Next":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FINAL EXAMINATION STATION 4 — Testing Real Technology Knowledge and the Future Waka Pitch</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding of design, materials, and technological development drawn from a full term of prior learning and a culminating design task.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our real engineering and technology knowledge from across the whole term.</li><li>We are presenting our &quot;waka of the future&quot; designs as the culminating task of the term.</li><li>I can explain why a design choice draws meaningfully on both tradition and innovation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 6–9 Technology content, alongside the term's culminating design task from Week 9. Use the pitch presentations as evidence of synthesis across the whole term's Technology learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is the final technology test, and it brings together the whole term: from the waka hourua, to GPS, to the 'waka of the future' you designed last week. Today, you pitch it properly to the Council.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Gather the 'waka of the future' designs created in Week 9. If not already presented in full, allow a few minutes for teams to finalise their pitch.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Then, Now, and What's Next Quick Round (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire questions connecting Technology content across the whole term: &quot;What real engineering principle connects the waka hourua to modern catamarans?&quot; &quot;What is one real strength and one real limitation of GPS?&quot; &quot;What is one real example of sustainable ocean technology today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Future Waka Pitch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: each team formally pitches their 'waka of the future' design to the Council (the class/teacher), explaining the traditional feature kept, the modern feature added, and the sustainable choice made.</li><li>Award points for both technical accuracy and the quality of the pitch's reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the single most important lesson about technology and design you're taking away from this whole term?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams' 'waka of the future' designs from Week 9</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Final Pitch Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a polished, presentation-ready poster of your &quot;waka of the future&quot; design for today's pitch.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you refine or improve since first designing it last week?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Term Technology MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of technology content from across the whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did building and testing real models help you understand this better than just reading about it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Term Technology Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a simple digital timeline showing the technology studied across the whole term, from the waka hourua to the waka of the future.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does this timeline show about how technology builds on what came before it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Whole Build Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at every design, sketch, and model you've created this term. What's the single biggest improvement you can see in your own thinking?</p></div></div>"},"Friday — Bonus Station: Wayfinder's Examination — Finale: The Wayfinder Ceremony":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TERM FINALE — Final Star Navigation Challenge and the Wayfinder Ceremony</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences / The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate understanding and contribute meaningfully to a shared class outcome, drawn from a full term of prior learning, and reflect on growth across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our ability to use real navigation knowledge built across the whole term.</li><li>We are formally reflecting on a full term of learning, growth, and contribution.</li><li>I can recognise and celebrate genuine growth in my own understanding across an extended unit of work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the final star navigation challenge. Use the Wayfinder Ceremony as a genuinely celebratory close to the whole term — not a new graded task, but a meaningful culmination of ten weeks of learning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;This is it, crew. The final challenge, and then — if the Council agrees — a celebration ten weeks in the making.&quot;</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Final Star Navigation Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Using everything learned across the term (star compass from Week 4, latitude from Week 7), teams must correctly 'steer' a token across the floor map from the voyage's starting point all the way to Aotearoa, using real star reference points and reasoning rather than guessing.</li><li>Award bonus points for teams who can also correctly explain WHY their chosen route makes sense, using real content from across the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Tallying the Final Examination (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add up all points from Monday through Friday's final challenge. Announce results, framing this as a genuine, shared achievement — every team has demonstrated a full term's worth of real learning, regardless of final score.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Wayfinder Ceremony (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Narrate the compass prop coming fully, calmly to life one final time — steady now, not restless.</li><li>Read Tama's closing words aloud to the class (from the Boss Battle page): his thanks, and the moment he formally calls the class &quot;Wayfinders&quot; for the first time.</li><li>Present each student or team with a simple certificate, badge, or token marking their completion of the term, in role as the Council's formal recognition.</li><li>Lay out every group's completed quilt side by side one final time as a class, looking back over the whole ten-week journey together.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: looking back over the WHOLE term, what is the one thing you're proudest of having learned or done?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Star compass wheels and the floor/wall Pacific map</li><li>The class's compass/journal prop for the ceremony</li><li>Simple certificates or badges for each student/team</li><li>Each group's completed voyage quilt</li><li>A running scoreboard from the week</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design Your Own Wayfinder Certificate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a personal certificate marking your completion of &quot;The Wayfinder's Journal,&quot; including one symbol representing your favourite part of the term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What symbol did you choose, and why does it represent your term best?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter to Next Year's Class</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter to the students who will do this voyage next year, telling them what to expect and what mattered most to you.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the one piece of advice you'd most want them to have?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Term Highlights Reel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create one final digital slide or page summarising your proudest moment, design, or piece of learning from the entire term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular highlight out of everything from this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Voyage, Start to Finish</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over your group's entire completed quilt and your full Quest Log from Week 1 to now.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking at where you started in Week 1 and where you are now, what's the biggest change you can see in yourself?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"Zimbabwe and South Africa":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 1: The Logbook Opens":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE LOGBOOK OPENS — Locating Southern Africa and Meeting the San, its First Peoples</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people's awareness of spatial patterns helps them make sense of geographic space, and locate significant places on a map.</li><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the perspectives of people who lived before written record can still be known today, through evidence and continuing living culture.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to locate Zimbabwe and South Africa on a map of Africa, and get a real sense of how big this region actually is.</li><li>We are learning who the San people are — the first peoples of Southern Africa, whose knowledge of the land stretches back tens of thousands of years.</li><li>I can explain why deep, unwritten knowledge of a landscape is a genuine form of expertise, not a lesser one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the map-location activity and class discussion; review of exit tickets for understanding of Southern Africa's location and the San as Southern Africa's first peoples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>An old leather logbook tumbles out of a battered suitcase prop. The moment it opens, the classroom window seems to fill with cloud, and the room tilts gently, like it's banking in the air. &quot;Sharp, crew!&quot; A boy's voice, grinning, framed by red dust and thorn trees. &quot;I'm Tapiwa. This logbook belonged to my grandfather, Sekuru Josiah — he flew bush planes his whole life, and now my cousin and I use it to bring people to meet our world. You're airborne now, whether you meant to be or not. Before we go anywhere, you should know where 'here' actually is. Zimbabwe, South Africa — big names, but I want you to see them properly, on the map, and meet the very first people who ever called this land home: the San. Ready when you are.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: before any map, any road, any shop existed here — how would you even begin to learn a landscape well enough to survive in it?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Finding Southern Africa (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Using a large map or globe, locate Africa as a continent, then zoom in to find Zimbabwe and South Africa in the southern part of it. Compare their size to a familiar reference (e.g. South Africa is roughly four to five times the size of New Zealand; Zimbabwe is a little larger than New Zealand's North and South Islands combined).</li><li>Introduce &quot;Southern Africa&quot; as a region name, not a single country — point out neighbouring countries (Botswana, Zambia, Mozambique, Namibia) so students see it as part of a connected region, not an isolated place.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might it matter to look at the actual size of a place on a map, instead of just hearing its name?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Meeting the San — Southern Africa's First Peoples (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The San (sometimes called Bushmen, a term some San communities use themselves and others do not — explain that naming matters, and &quot;San&quot; is the most widely respected general term) are widely considered to hold the oldest continuous cultural and genetic lineage known to science, with a presence in Southern Africa stretching back tens of thousands of years.</li><li>Explain that San communities historically lived as skilled hunter-gatherers across the deserts and savannas of the region, and that their languages are famous for using click consonants — sounds no other language family uses in quite the same way.</li><li>Important nuance: San people are not a &quot;vanished&quot; people — San communities live in Southern Africa today, in the Kalahari and elsewhere, some continuing traditional practices and many living modern lives, often facing real challenges around land rights and recognition.</li><li>Discuss: what kind of knowledge would you need — about water, animals, plants, weather — to live well in a place like this without shops, taps or roads? The San built exactly that kind of knowledge, refined and passed down over an extraordinary span of time.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Would You Need to Know? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In pairs, students list three things they would need to know about a landscape before they could survive in it without modern conveniences (e.g. where water hides, which plants are safe to eat, how to read weather signs).</li><li>Share back as a class, then compare the class's list to what is actually known about San survival knowledge — water-finding (including from underground root sources and dew), tracking for food, plant knowledge for medicine and nutrition.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Is this kind of knowledge easier or harder to learn than what's in a textbook? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing the San needed to know to live well in Southern Africa, and explain why that counts as real knowledge, not just &quot;old ways.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Long before any map was drawn, God had already placed the San people here, painting their world onto rock. Every nation's story — including this one — is part of the larger story God has been telling since the beginning.</li><li>Verse: &quot;From one man he made all the nations, that they should inhabit the whole earth; and he marked out their appointed times in history and the boundaries of their lands.&quot; — <em>Acts 17:26 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning where the San people came from helps us know God's story, and see that every people group has a place within it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If God marked out appointed times and places for every nation, what does that tell us about the San people — and about the country you were born in?</li><li>Tapiwa and Nomvula are proud of where they come from. What are you proud of about where YOU come from?</li><li>How does knowing God as the one who placed every nation change the way we look at a country very different from our own?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large map of Africa (physical or digital), plus a globe if available</li><li>Battered suitcase and/or &quot;old leather logbook&quot; prop, for the ongoing story device</li><li>A small set of respectful, contemporary and historical images of San communities (avoid images that only show the past, so students understand San people exist today)</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Who are the San people?</em> — search: &quot;San people Southern Africa first peoples documentary&quot;. Gives an age-appropriate, respectful introduction to San history and identity, grounding the lesson in real people rather than stereotype.</li><li><em>Map overview of Southern Africa</em> — search: &quot;Southern Africa map Zimbabwe South Africa geography overview&quot;. Helps students visually anchor the region before the term's journey begins.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Route So Far</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple route line from where you live to Hwange, Zimbabwe, marking one thing you're curious to find out along the way.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a very simple world/regional outline, or use a printed map.</li><li>Mark your home and Hwange, and draw a dotted line between them.</li><li>Add a small label near Hwange naming one question you have about it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose that particular question to mark?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: First Logbook Entry</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry in role as a student who has just discovered the logbook and realised where it's taking you.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is the one detail from today's lesson you most wanted to include, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Simple Digital Map Marker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple slide, document, or drawing tool, mark Zimbabwe and South Africa on a digital map outline and add one labelled fact about the San people to each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which fact did you choose to include, and why did it stand out to you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The San People</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video introducing the San people of Southern Africa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;San people Southern Africa first peoples documentary&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that changed how you think about &quot;old&quot; knowledge?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 2: Three Kinds of Country":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THREE KINDS OF COUNTRY — Savanna, Highveld and Lowveld Climate Zones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how physical processes shape landscapes and environments, and how people adapt their lives to different climate conditions.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Zimbabwe and South Africa are not one single kind of landscape — different climate zones shape very different ways of life within the same countries.</li><li>We are learning to identify a savanna, a highveld and a lowveld, and describe key differences in rainfall, temperature and vegetation between them.</li><li>I can predict how a place's climate zone shapes the housing, clothing and food of the people who live there.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the climate-zone sorting activity and group predictions; review of postcard tasks for accurate use of climate clues.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Sharp, crew — back already. Good. Here's something that took me a while to get: Zimbabwe and South Africa aren't just one kind of place. There's the savanna where I live, all dry grass and thorn trees. There's the highveld, up high and cooler. There's the lowveld, hot and low near the rivers. My gogo — my grandmother — always says you can't farm the same way in all three, and you definitely can't dress the same way either. Help me show you the difference?&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if two places are in the same country, how could they possibly need completely different houses, clothes and crops?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Makes a Climate Zone? (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that a climate zone is shaped mainly by altitude (how high above sea level), latitude, and distance from the coast — all of which affect rainfall and temperature.</li><li>Introduce the three zones simply: savanna (warm, dry grassland with scattered trees, like around Hwange), highveld (a high inland plateau, cooler, found across much of South Africa's interior), and lowveld (low-lying, hot, near river systems, including part of the Kruger region).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might being higher above sea level make a place cooler, even in a hot country?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sorting Savanna, Highveld and Lowveld (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Using a physical/climate map of Southern Africa, students sort a set of landscape photographs into the correct climate zone (savanna, highveld, lowveld), discussing rainfall, temperature and vegetation clues in each image.</li><li>Groups justify their sorting choices using specific visual evidence — vegetation type, land shape, signs of dryness or rainfall — not just a guess.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What was the biggest clue that gave a zone away?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Predicting Life in Each Zone (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students predict what kind of house, clothing and food would suit each of the three zones, explaining their reasoning using the climate clues from the sorting activity.</li><li>Discuss as a class: farming, building materials and daily life all have to adapt to climate — this is true everywhere, including the students' own region.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of the three zones would be hardest to farm, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE difference between two of the three climate zones, and explain how that difference would change the way people live there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: God filled creation with astonishing variety — not one single landscape repeated everywhere, but savanna, highveld and lowveld each doing something different, each declared &quot;good.&quot; Noticing that variety is one way of noticing God's creativity.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God saw all that he had made, and it was very good.&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Loving the world God made includes noticing its variety closely enough to actually understand it, not just glancing past it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Why do you think God made three such different kinds of country within the same region, instead of just one?</li><li>Tapiwa's gogo farms differently depending on the zone. What is one way people in YOUR region adapt to the climate you live in?</li><li>What is one part of creation near you that you could look at more closely this week, the way today's lesson asked you to look closely at savanna, highveld and lowveld?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Physical/climate map of Southern Africa</li><li>Photograph sets of savanna, highveld and lowveld landscapes (printed or digital), unlabelled for sorting</li><li>Blank postcard templates for the creative task</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Southern Africa's climate zones explained</em> — search: &quot;Southern Africa savanna highveld lowveld climate zones&quot;. Gives students a clear, visual overview of the three zones before or after the sorting activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Postcard From a Zone</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple postcard from one of the three zones (savanna, highveld or lowveld), showing what you'd see, hear and feel there.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one zone.</li><li>Illustrate one scene that clearly shows that zone's climate.</li><li>Write a short &quot;wish you were here&quot; message using at least two climate-specific details.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What one detail in your postcard makes it obviously THAT zone and not one of the other two?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Between Zones</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter from a child living in one climate zone to a cousin living in a different one, describing daily life.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest everyday detail to imagine differently for the other zone?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Climate Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple document, slide, or spreadsheet to build a comparison chart of the three zones across rainfall, temperature and vegetation.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which zone would be hardest for YOU to adapt to, based on your chart?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Southern Africa's Climate Zones</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining Southern Africa's climate zones.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Southern Africa savanna highveld lowveld climate zones&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which zone surprised you the most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 3: The Tree That Stores Water":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE TREE THAT STORES WATER — Savanna Biome and Baobab Adaptation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand that living things have life processes that help them survive, and that they are suited to their particular habitat.</li><li>Living World, Ecology: begin to understand the interdependence between living things and their environment.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe the savanna biome — its climate, seasons and typical plant and animal life.</li><li>We are learning how the baobab tree survives extreme dry seasons through specific physical adaptations.</li><li>I can explain how many other living things depend on a single baobab tree for food, water or shelter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of group discussion and the interdependence-web activity; review of labelled baobab sketches for accurate understanding of adaptation features.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;There's a tree near our home that's older than anyone can properly count — Sekuru Josiah reckons some baobabs are over a thousand years old. It looks upside-down, like someone planted its roots in the sky by mistake. During the dry season, when everything else has given up, it's still standing there, full of water it stored months ago. How does a TREE do that? I've wondered my whole life.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a tree can't move to find water, what could it possibly do instead to survive months of drought?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Savanna Biome (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce the savanna biome — warm year-round, with a clearly split wet and dry season, grassland scattered with drought-tolerant trees, and home to some of the world's most recognisable wildlife.</li><li>Discuss: savanna plants and animals have all had to develop ways of coping with months without rain — today's focus is one of the most dramatic examples, the baobab tree.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How the Baobab Survives (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The baobab's swollen trunk is a genuine water-storage adaptation — it can hold hundreds, even thousands, of litres of water, taken up during the wet season and drawn on through the dry months.</li><li>Explain its other features: a wide, shallow but far-reaching root system to capture rainfall quickly; the ability to drop its leaves entirely in the dry season to reduce water loss; a very long lifespan that lets it survive repeated droughts across centuries.</li><li>Activity: In pairs, students label a simple baobab diagram (trunk, roots, leafless dry-season branches) with what each part does to help the tree survive.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a tree that can't move need to be especially good at storing what it needs?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Everything That Depends on One Tree (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, students list every living thing that might depend on a single baobab tree for food, water or shelter (e.g. elephants eating bark and fruit for moisture, birds and bats nesting or feeding in its canopy, insects living in its bark).</li><li>Discuss as a class: this is interdependence — one tree's survival supporting a whole web of other species around it, especially during drought when other water sources are scarce.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What might happen to that whole web if baobab trees became rare?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE adaptation that helps the baobab survive the dry season, and explain how it works.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The baobab's strange, upside-down shape is exactly the kind of wild creativity the Psalms praise God for — creation isn't just functional, it's astonishing, made with a wisdom far beyond what any one tree needed just to survive.</li><li>Verse: &quot;How many are your works, Lord! In wisdom you made them all; the earth is full of your creatures.&quot; — <em>Psalm 104:24 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Looking closely at how a baobab survives is a way of loving the world God made — noticing its wisdom, not just its usefulness.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The baobab looks strange compared to trees you might know. Why do you think God made creation full of such different, surprising designs?</li><li>So many animals depend on one tree surviving. What does that teach us about how connected God made living things to be?</li><li>What is one &quot;ordinary&quot; plant or tree near you that might be more remarkable than you've ever noticed?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Photos or a short video clip of baobab trees, ideally showing both wet-season and dry-season appearance</li><li>Simple unlabelled baobab diagram, one per pair</li><li>Chart paper for the interdependence-web activity</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How the baobab tree survives drought</em> — search: &quot;baobab tree adaptation water storage explained&quot;. Grounds the lesson's key science content in real, visual footage of the tree students are learning about.</li><li><em>Savanna biome overview</em> — search: &quot;savanna biome plants animals climate overview&quot;. Gives broader context for the biome the baobab belongs to.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch a Baobab</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sketch a baobab and label three features that help it survive the dry season.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw the tree's distinctive swollen trunk and root-like branches.</li><li>Add three labels pointing to specific survival features.</li><li>Add one small animal that depends on the tree.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which feature took the longest to draw accurately, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Baobab's Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry in role as a baobab tree, describing what the dry season feels like from its point of view.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What scientific fact did you build your &quot;diary entry&quot; around?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Interdependence Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple drawing or slide tool to build a digital web showing at least four living things connected to a baobab tree.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which connection in your web did you find most surprising?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Baobab Tree</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how baobab trees survive drought.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;baobab tree adaptation water storage explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is the most impressive fact you learned about the baobab?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 4: Reading the Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">READING THE LAND — From San Trackers to Modern Ranger Technology</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Knowledge: understand that technologies have designed features that suit their purpose, and that a range of technologies exist to help people gather and interpret information.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technology both reflects and changes society, and that technological outcomes are developed to meet a need or opportunity.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the San people read the land — footprints, broken grass, dung — to know what animals had passed and when.</li><li>We are learning that modern ranger technology (radios, GPS, cameras) does a similar job to ancient tracking skill, just with different tools.</li><li>I can read and interpret a set of &quot;spoor&quot; clues to solve a simple tracking challenge.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the spoor-reading challenge and class discussion; review of exit tickets for understanding of tracking as an information-gathering technology, ancient and modern.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook falls open onto a wide, dry stretch of Hwange bush at first light. Tapiwa crouches by a smudge in the red dirt. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; he grins up at the class. &quot;Before anyone had a radio or a camera, my ancestors could read a whole story out of dirt like this. Come and see if you can.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you had no radio, no camera, and no GPS, how could you possibly know an elephant had walked past two hours ago instead of two days ago?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Spoor: The Original Tracking Technology (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The San people of southern Africa are famous for tracking — reading &quot;spoor&quot; (footprints, droppings, broken twigs, disturbed sand) to work out which animal passed, how long ago, how fast it was moving, and even how it was feeling.</li><li>Explain that this is genuinely a technology: a learned system of skills and knowledge, passed down and refined over generations, used to solve a real problem — finding food, or, today, finding and protecting animals.</li><li>Show spoor identification cards (elephant, lion, rhino, antelope) — discuss what shape and depth might tell a tracker.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Try It: A Spoor-Reading Challenge (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In small groups, students receive a set of &quot;spoor clue cards&quot; (footprint size/shape, droppings description, a broken branch, a partial water-hole print) for a mystery animal and must work out what animal it was, roughly how long ago it passed, and which direction it was heading.</li><li>Groups justify their answer using the specific clues, not just a guess — mirroring how a real tracker builds a case from evidence.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What could go wrong if a tracker read the clues too quickly, or missed one?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">From Spoor to Satellite: Why Rangers Still Need Both (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Modern rangers — like Tapiwa's dad, Baba Farai — still learn to read spoor, because it works without batteries, signal or expensive equipment. But they now pair that skill with radios, GPS units and cameras (which the class will meet over the coming weeks) to cover far bigger areas, faster.</li><li>Discuss: old and new technology aren't rivals — the best ranger teams use both, the way a pilot like Sekuru Josiah used both his instruments and his own trained eye.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might relying on only ONE kind of technology be risky for a ranger?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing a tracker can learn from spoor, and explain how that same information helps protect an animal today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Long before satellites, God had already written signs into creation for anyone patient enough to read them. Job reminds his friends that even the animals and the earth itself can teach us something true, if we're willing to look closely.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Ask the animals, and they will teach you, or the birds of the air.&quot; — <em>Job 12:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning to read the land, ancient or modern, is a way of knowing creation more closely — and knowing it is part of knowing the God who made it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The San people could read a whole story out of a footprint. What does that tell you about how closely they paid attention to the world God made?</li><li>Tapiwa's dad uses both old tracking skill and new technology. Why might God want us to value old wisdom AND new tools, instead of choosing one over the other?</li><li>What is one thing in creation you could &quot;read&quot; more closely this week, if you slowed down enough to look?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Spoor identification cards (elephant, lion, rhino, antelope) — printable or hand-drawn</li><li>Mystery animal &quot;clue card&quot; sets, one per group (footprint, droppings, broken branch, partial print)</li><li>Southern Africa map for context</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>San trackers reading animal spoor</em> — search: &quot;San Bushmen tracking skills spoor reading documentary&quot;. Shows real San tracking skill in action, grounding the lesson in an authentic, respectful example.</li><li><em>How modern rangers combine tracking with technology</em> — search: &quot;anti-poaching rangers tracking technology Africa&quot;. Bridges today's ancient-tracking focus with the ranger technology theme running through the rest of the term.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Spoor Field Guide Page</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design one page of a &quot;field guide&quot; showing three animal tracks from today's lesson.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw each footprint to scale, roughly.</li><li>Label one clue for each that would help identify it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which track was hardest to tell apart from another, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Tracker's Notebook Entry</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry in role as a young tracker who has just found and interpreted a fresh set of spoor.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you notice, and what did you decide it meant?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Simple Tracking App Idea</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sketch or describe (on paper or a simple drawing tool) an idea for an app that could help a ranger log spoor sightings in the field.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information would it need to record to actually be useful?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Reading Spoor</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video of real trackers reading spoor.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;San Bushmen tracking skills spoor reading&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about how much information one footprint can hold?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 5: Stories on Stone":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">STORIES ON STONE — San Rock Art as Storytelling</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts, Visual Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Developing Practical Knowledge: explore materials and techniques to create a symbol-based artwork.</li><li>Developing Ideas: use observation and imagination to develop ideas that communicate a story through visual symbols alone.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that San rock art is one of the oldest storytelling traditions in the world, using symbols and images instead of written words.</li><li>We are learning that a single symbol can carry a whole story, if the meaning behind it is clear enough.</li><li>I can create a symbol-based artwork that tells a simple story without using any words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the artwork creation process and the partner &quot;reading&quot; activity; review of finished pieces for evidence of intentional, story-carrying symbol choices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tapiwa's voice softens, more careful than usual. &quot;San rock art is some of the oldest storytelling in the entire world — paintings on cave walls and rock faces that have survived for thousands of years. No pen, no paper, just ochre and rock and something that mattered enough to last. Today I want you to make a symbol of your own, one that tells a story the way theirs did — through shape alone, not words.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you could tell only ONE story using only shapes and symbols, with no words at all, what would you choose to show, and how?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Makes San Rock Art Remarkable (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: San rock art appears across Southern Africa, painted using natural pigments (ochre, charcoal, animal fat as a binder) onto rock shelters and cave walls, some pieces many thousands of years old.</li><li>Explain that the art depicts animals, human figures and symbolic scenes, often connected to spiritual beliefs and important events — a genuine storytelling and record-keeping system, not simple decoration.</li><li>Discuss respectfully: this is a living cultural tradition with deep meaning to San communities, not just an ancient curiosity — treat the images shown with the same respect given to any sacred or meaningful artwork.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Symbol-Based Rock Art: Making Your Own (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: On paper made to resemble stone (torn-edged brown/orange paper), students use ochre-toned paint, pastel or crayon to create a symbol-based artwork inspired by San rock art — figures, animals or symbols telling a simple story without any written words.</li><li>Encourage students to plan their symbols before painting: what is the story, and what is the smallest number of clear shapes that could tell it?</li><li>Circulate with prompting questions: &quot;What is happening in your story?&quot; &quot;How would someone who's never met you understand that from the shapes alone?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading Each Other's Stories (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Students swap artworks with a partner and try to &quot;read&quot; the story in their symbols before the artist explains it — then compare what was guessed with what was actually meant.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What made a symbol easy or hard for your partner to read correctly?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Give your finished piece a title using only symbols, no words at all — then, on paper, write one sentence explaining what your title means.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: God filled Bezalel with skill and creativity to craft beautiful, meaningful things for His people — a reminder that artistic skill itself is a gift from God, given so we can create things that tell the truth and carry meaning forward.</li><li>Verse: &quot;I have filled him with the Spirit of God, with wisdom, with understanding, with knowledge and with all kinds of skills.&quot; — <em>Exodus 35:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Creating symbols that carry real meaning, the way San artists did, is a way of knowing that art can hold truth just as powerfully as words.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Exodus says God filled a craftsman with skill for a purpose. What do you think your own creative skill might be &quot;filled&quot; for?</li><li>San rock art lasted thousands of years without a single word written down. Why do you think some stories are better told through image than through words?</li><li>What is one story or value from your own life that you'd want to last as long as San rock art has?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Torn-edged brown/orange paper, cut to resemble stone</li><li>Ochre-toned paint, pastel or crayon (browns, reds, oranges, black)</li><li>A small set of respectful reference images of San rock art (figures, animals, symbolic scenes)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>San rock art of Southern Africa</em> — search: &quot;San rock art Southern Africa explained&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in the real tradition before they create their own symbol-based artwork.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend Your Rock Art Symbol</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose one symbol from your finished artwork and redesign it three different ways, each slightly simpler than the last.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">At what point did the symbol become too simple to still tell the story?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write the Story Behind the Symbols</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, the story your symbol-based artwork was telling.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How different is the written story from what your partner guessed earlier?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Symbol Set</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, design a set of 4–5 symbols that could tell a short story, in the spirit of San rock art.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder — designing the symbols, or making sure they'd be understood without explanation?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: San Rock Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about San rock art.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;San rock art Southern Africa explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing that surprised you about how old, or how meaningful, this art form is?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 6: The Stone City":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE STONE CITY — Great Zimbabwe and a Trading Kingdom Before European Contact</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the perspectives of people who lived before written record can still be known today, through evidence and continuing living culture.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how a significant place can reveal the scale and sophistication of a past society.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Great Zimbabwe, the Stone City, was a powerful trading kingdom centuries before any European set foot in Southern Africa.</li><li>We are learning that its dry-stone walls were built without mortar, and what that tells us about the skill and organisation of the people who built them.</li><li>I can explain why some impressive civilisations are less globally well-known than others, and what that says about whose stories get told.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion and group reasoning task; review of dry-stone pattern sketches for engagement with the &quot;no mortar&quot; construction idea.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook falls open, but this time it isn't Tapiwa's voice that crackles through first — it's older, warmer, a little crackly with static. &quot;Sharp, crew — Sekuru Josiah's telling this one, not me, so listen carefully.&quot; Tapiwa's grandfather's voice carries on, unhurried. &quot;Long before any European ever set foot here, my people built a city of stone — walls stacked so precisely, without a scrap of mortar, that they've stood for centuries. Great Zimbabwe, they call it now. A whole trading kingdom, right here, before anyone from outside had ever heard of us.&quot; Tapiwa cuts back in, grinning: &quot;He tells this one every time, and I still get a look on my face like it surprises me it's actually true.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: how could a whole city of stone walls stay standing for centuries without any mortar holding the stones together?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Great Zimbabwe? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Great Zimbabwe was the capital of a powerful trading kingdom that flourished roughly between the 11th and 15th centuries, in what is now Zimbabwe — the country itself later took its name from this site (&quot;dzimba dza mabwe&quot; means &quot;houses of stone&quot; in Shona).</li><li>Explain that at its height, Great Zimbabwe may have been home to thousands of people, and was the centre of a trade network reaching as far as the East African coast, the Middle East, India and China — evidenced by imported glass beads, Chinese porcelain and Persian pottery found at the site.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What does trading with places as far away as China and Persia tell us about how connected this kingdom actually was?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Stone Without Mortar (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Show images of Great Zimbabwe's dry-stone walls, some over five metres high, built by carefully shaping and stacking granite blocks with no mortar or cement holding them together — a construction method requiring immense skill and planning.</li><li>Activity: In pairs, students examine close-up images of the walls and discuss what they notice about how the stones fit together, then attempt to stack small blocks, books or blocks of soap without any adhesive to feel, briefly, how much precision dry-stone building demands.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If one stone is cut or placed badly, what happens to the whole wall? What does that tell you about the builders' skill?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Isn't This Better Known? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: For a long time, some colonial-era writers wrongly claimed Great Zimbabwe could not have been built by Africans, despite clear archaeological evidence — a misconception now firmly rejected by historians and archaeologists.</li><li>Discuss as a class: why might a civilisation as impressive as Great Zimbabwe be less globally well-known than other ancient sites of a similar age, and what does that reveal about whose stories tend to get told, and whose get overlooked?</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Whose job is it to make sure a true story like this one gets told properly?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE fact about Great Zimbabwe that shows it was a sophisticated, powerful society, and explain why that fact matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Sekuru Josiah tells this story with pride, wanting the next generation to know it properly. The Psalms describe exactly that responsibility — telling the next generation the praiseworthy deeds of the past, so a true story is never lost or overlooked.</li><li>Verse: &quot;We will tell the next generation the praiseworthy deeds of the Lord, his power, and the wonders he has done.&quot; — <em>Psalm 78:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing a people's real history — not the flattened or dismissed version — is part of honouring what God has done through every nation and generation.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 78 says we should tell the next generation the truth about what came before us. Who has told you a story like that, about your own family or country?</li><li>Sekuru Josiah is proud to pass this story on. What is something from your own history you'd be proud to pass on to someone younger than you?</li><li>Why do you think it matters to God that every people's true story gets told, not just some?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of Great Zimbabwe's dry-stone walls (wide shots and close-up detail)</li><li>Small blocks, books, or offcuts of soap for the no-mortar stacking demonstration</li><li>Map of Southern Africa showing Great Zimbabwe's location and its trade routes to the East African coast</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Great Zimbabwe explained</em> — search: &quot;Great Zimbabwe stone city history explained&quot;. Gives students a clear visual introduction to the site and its scale before the stacking activity.</li><li><em>How dry-stone walling works</em> — search: &quot;dry stone wall building technique explained&quot;. Helps students understand the specific skill involved in mortar-free construction.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch a Dry-Stone Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sketch a simple stone wall pattern inspired by Great Zimbabwe's dry-stone construction, without using any mortar or glue in your drawing's &quot;joins.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw a grid of irregular stone shapes, interlocking like a real dry-stone wall.</li><li>Shade each stone slightly differently to show individual blocks.</li><li>Add one label pointing to where the wall would be weakest if a stone were placed badly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made interlocking the stones on paper harder than you expected?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Trader's Account</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short first-person account from a trader arriving at Great Zimbabwe for the first time, describing what they see and trade.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail did you include to show how impressive the city was?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Map the Trade Network</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing or slide tool, map Great Zimbabwe's trade connections to the East African coast, the Middle East, India and China, with one line per connection.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which trade connection surprised you the most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Great Zimbabwe</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining Great Zimbabwe and its history.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Great Zimbabwe stone city history explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact you learned that you didn't expect?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 7: What the Family Table Needs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHAT THE FAMILY TABLE NEEDS — Subsistence Farming and Food Security</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people's connection to the land shapes how they meet their own needs, and how this differs across communities.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what subsistence farming is, and how it differs from farming to sell a crop.</li><li>We are learning why crops like maize, sorghum and groundnuts matter to a family's food security.</li><li>I can plan a small subsistence plot and explain the reasoning behind my choices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class planning discussion; review of drawn family plots for evidence of reasoned crop choices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;My mum keeps a small plot behind our house — maize, sorghum, groundnuts. Nothing fancy, nothing for selling, just enough to feed us,&quot; Tapiwa says, turning the wooden elephant carving over in his pocket, the way he does when he's thinking something through. &quot;Pipi — my little cousin — thinks food just appears, which, fair enough, she's six. But growing just enough for your own family, that's a whole different job to growing a big field to sell. Help me show you the difference?&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you were only growing food for your own family — not to sell to anyone — would you choose the same crops as a farmer growing food for a whole town?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Subsistence Farming? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Subsistence farming means growing crops and raising animals mainly to feed your own family, with little or nothing left over to sell — this is different from commercial farming, which grows large quantities of a crop specifically to sell for income.</li><li>Introduce maize, sorghum and groundnuts as three staple subsistence crops common across rural Zimbabwe and much of Southern Africa, each chosen for being reliable, nutritious and suited to the local climate.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a family choose to grow three different crops instead of just one?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why It Still Matters (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that subsistence farming remains central to food security for many rural households across Southern Africa — when a family grows enough to feed itself, it is less dependent on buying food, which matters especially when money or transport is limited.</li><li>Discuss the risks too: a poor rainfall year can threaten a subsistence family's food supply directly, in a way that affects them differently to a family that buys all its food from a shop.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What could go wrong for a subsistence-farming family in a year with very little rain?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Plan a Subsistence Plot (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: As a class, plan a small subsistence plot — students choose three crops they would grow if the goal was simply feeding their own family through the year, and must justify each choice (nutrition, reliability, how well it stores).</li><li>Groups compare their choices and discuss trade-offs: a crop that's nutritious but hard to store, versus one that's easy to grow but less filling.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of your three crops would you protect first in a difficult year, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE crop a subsistence farmer might grow, and explain why it would be a good choice for feeding a family.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Tapiwa's mum tends her plot carefully, trusting that steady, patient work will feed her family through the year. The Bible often pictures faithful provision this way — not instant abundance, but daily bread, worked for and trusted for, season after season.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Give us today our daily bread.&quot; — <em>Matthew 6:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Growing enough to feed your own family, faithfully and without waste, is a real and honourable way of bringing God's care for people into daily, practical life.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus taught his followers to pray for &quot;daily bread,&quot; not a huge stockpile all at once. Why might trusting for just what's needed, day by day, matter more than we think?</li><li>Tapiwa's family relies on their own plot for food. Who grows or provides the food your own family relies on?</li><li>What is one way you could show gratitude this week for food that didn't just &quot;appear,&quot; the way Pipi thinks it does?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or real samples of maize, sorghum and groundnuts</li><li>A simple blank plot-planning grid (paper or digital) for the class activity</li><li>Map showing rural Zimbabwe/Southern Africa farming regions</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is subsistence farming?</em> — search: &quot;subsistence farming explained for kids Africa&quot;. Gives students a clear, age-appropriate definition before the plot-planning activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw Your Family Plot</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw your imagined family plot from above, like a map, labelling what's growing where.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw a simple rectangular plot outline.</li><li>Divide it into three sections and label each with a chosen crop.</li><li>Add one small symbol near each crop showing why you chose it (e.g. a raindrop for &quot;grows well with little rain&quot;).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which crop took up the most space on your plot, and why did you give it priority?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter About the Harvest</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter in role as a child describing this year's harvest from your family's subsistence plot to a relative who lives far away.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did you describe a good harvest or a difficult one, and why did you choose that?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Crop Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple document, slide or spreadsheet to compare maize, sorghum and groundnuts across nutrition, drought-resistance and storage life.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Based on your chart, which crop would you rely on most in a dry year?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Subsistence Farming</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining subsistence farming in Southern Africa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;subsistence farming explained for kids Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that changed how you think about where your own food comes from?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 8: Built for the Dry":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">BUILT FOR THE DRY — How Savanna Plants Survive Drought</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand how living things are suited to their particular habitat, and how survival adaptations solve specific environmental problems.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that savanna plants have specific physical adaptations that help them survive months without rain.</li><li>We are learning how thorns, small leaves and deep roots each solve a different survival problem.</li><li>I can explain the specific problem a chosen plant adaptation solves, using evidence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group adaptation-matching task and discussion; review of designed &quot;super-adapted&quot; plants for correct reasoning behind each feature.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Thorns, tiny leaves, roots that go deeper than the plant is tall — the plants out here aren't lucky, they're built,&quot; Tapiwa says, crouched beside a thorny scrub bush at the edge of the logbook page. &quot;Every single feature is solving a problem: how do you survive months without rain? I want to actually understand WHY, not just know that they do.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a plant can't move to find water, what has to change about the plant itself to survive months without rain?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Three Problems, Three Solutions (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce three common savanna plant adaptations — thorns (which deter grazing animals from eating scarce, precious leaves and stored water), small leaves (which reduce surface area and therefore water loss through evaporation), and deep root systems (which reach underground water far below the dry surface).</li><li>Explain that each adaptation exists because it solves a very specific survival problem, shaped by generations of plants that survived and reproduced because they had that feature.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these three problems do you think is the biggest threat to a savanna plant's survival?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Matching Adaptations to Problems (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, students are given cards describing savanna plant features (thorns, small/waxy leaves, deep roots, swollen water-storing stems) and must match each to the specific survival problem it solves, explaining their reasoning.</li><li>Groups present one matched pair to the class, justifying the connection with evidence from the discussion.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Could a plant survive the dry season with only ONE of these adaptations, or does it usually need more than one working together?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Design a Super-Adapted Plant (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In pairs, students sketch an imaginary &quot;super-adapted&quot; plant for extreme drought, labelling at least three features and the specific problem each one solves.</li><li>Share back: a few pairs explain their plant's features and the reasoning behind each choice.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which feature of your plant would matter most in the very driest years?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE savanna plant adaptation and explain the specific survival problem it solves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Every thorn, waxy leaf and deep root in the savanna is a precise answer to a real problem — nothing wasted, nothing accidental. The Psalms describe God's wisdom filling creation exactly this way: intricate, purposeful design in even the toughest, driest corners of the earth.</li><li>Verse: &quot;How many are your works, Lord! In wisdom you made them all; the earth is full of your creatures.&quot; — <em>Psalm 104:24 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Looking closely at how carefully even a thorn bush is designed to survive is one way of loving and honouring the world God made, instead of overlooking it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 104 says God made everything &quot;in wisdom.&quot; How does learning WHY a plant has thorns or deep roots change the way you see a &quot;boring&quot; desert plant?</li><li>Tapiwa says these plants aren't &quot;lucky,&quot; they're &quot;built.&quot; Is there a challenge in your own life where being &quot;built&quot; for it, rather than lucky, matters more?</li><li>What is one part of creation near you that you've never really looked closely at, the way today's lesson asked you to look at savanna plants?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or real specimens of thorny/small-leaved savanna plants, if available</li><li>Adaptation-matching card sets (feature cards and problem cards) for the group activity</li><li>Diagram showing a cross-section of a deep-rooted savanna plant</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How savanna plants survive drought</em> — search: &quot;savanna plant adaptations drought explained&quot;. Gives students a clear visual grounding in the specific adaptations before the matching activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch Your Super-Adapted Plant</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an imaginary &quot;super-adapted&quot; plant for extreme drought, labelling each feature and the problem it solves.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch your plant with at least three visible adaptations.</li><li>Label each feature clearly.</li><li>Next to each label, write one sentence naming the specific problem it solves.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of your three features do you think a real botanist would find least realistic, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Plant's-Eye View</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short first-person account from the point of view of a savanna plant surviving a long dry season, describing how its features help it cope.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which adaptation did you focus on the most in your writing, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build an Adaptation Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing or slide tool, create a labelled diagram of a savanna plant showing at least three adaptations and the problem each solves.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was hardest to show clearly in your diagram — the feature itself, or the problem it solves?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Savanna Plant Adaptations</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how savanna plants survive drought.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;savanna plant adaptations drought explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one adaptation you saw that you hadn't thought of before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 9: Voices Across the Bush":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">VOICES ACROSS THE BUSH — Radio Communication and Ranger Teamwork</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Systems: understand that technological systems have inputs, controlled processes and outputs, and are the means by which people control, monitor or send information.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technology can have both intended and unintended impacts, and that solutions often involve trade-offs.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Relating to Others; Thinking</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why fast, reliable communication is one of the most important technologies for protecting animals across huge areas.</li><li>We are learning how a two-way radio system actually works, in simple terms.</li><li>I can plan and run a short relay of accurate information, and understand what can go wrong along the way.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the radio relay activity, focusing on accuracy of information passed on; review of written reflections on why clear communication protocols matter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook settles over Hwange again, but this time there's a crackle of static first. &quot;Sharp, crew — that's Baba,&quot; Tapiwa says, suddenly serious, turning the wooden elephant over in his pocket. &quot;My dad's on patrol today. When his radio goes quiet too long, that's when Sekuru Josiah taught me to really worry.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: Hwange National Park is bigger than some entire countries. If a ranger spots something urgent on one side of it, how does help get there fast enough to matter?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Radios, Not Just Phones (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that many reserves and parks are far larger than mobile phone coverage can reach, and radios don't rely on phone towers — they send signals directly, ranger to ranger, which is why they're still the backbone of ranger communication today.</li><li>Simple explanation of how a two-way radio works: a microphone turns your voice into an electrical signal, a transmitter turns that into a radio wave, and a receiver on another radio turns the wave back into sound — Sekuru Josiah used exactly this kind of radio to talk to airfields when he was flying.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a system that doesn't need phone signal be especially important in wild, remote places?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Rules of Good Radio Communication (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Real ranger and pilot radio communication uses short, clear phrases, confirms messages were received (&quot;copy&quot;), and avoids talking over each other — because a garbled message in an emergency can be dangerous.</li><li>Model a short example exchange (e.g., reporting a sighting) using clear, simple radio phrasing.</li><li>Discuss: what could go wrong if a message about an animal's location was passed on inaccurately?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Message Relay Challenge (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In a line or circle, one student is given a short, specific &quot;ranger report&quot; (e.g., an animal type, a rough location, a time) and must pass it on by whispering, relay-style, to the end of the chain — no repeating.</li><li>Compare the final message to the original — discuss how and why details changed.</li><li>Reframe: this is why real radio protocol insists on reading back important details, rather than just saying &quot;got it.&quot;</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What is one radio habit that could have stopped our message from changing?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is ONE rule of clear radio communication, and why does it matter when the message is about protecting an animal?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Ecclesiastes reminds us that we weren't made to carry every load alone — we're stronger, and safer, working as a connected team. A ranger's radio is a small, practical picture of that truth: nobody protects a whole reserve by themselves.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Two are better than one, because they have a good return for their labor.&quot; — <em>Ecclesiastes 4:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Serving faithfully with our gifts often means being a reliable link in someone else's chain — passing on what we know, clearly and honestly, so others can act on it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If &quot;two are better than one,&quot; why do you think God designed us to need each other, instead of making each of us able to do everything alone?</li><li>Baba Farai depends on other rangers hearing his radio message correctly. Who depends on YOU passing on information clearly — at school, or at home?</li><li>What does it look like to be a &quot;clear voice&quot; for someone else this week?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Real or toy two-way radios, if available (or simple &quot;walkie-talkie&quot; role-play)</li><li>Pre-written &quot;ranger report&quot; message cards for the relay activity</li><li>Diagram of how a radio signal travels (microphone → transmitter → receiver → speaker), simplified</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How two-way radios work</em> — search: &quot;how does a two way radio work simple explanation&quot;. Gives students a clear, age-appropriate picture of the technology behind today's activity.</li><li><em>A day with anti-poaching rangers</em> — search: &quot;anti-poaching rangers daily patrol Africa&quot;. Shows the real context radios are used in, connecting to Tapiwa's dad's work.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Ranger Radio Call-Sign Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple chart of radio call-signs and short phrases a ranger team might use (e.g., what they'd say for &quot;all clear&quot; or &quot;need help&quot;).</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why is short and clear better than long and detailed, on a radio?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write the Radio Transcript</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, realistic radio exchange between two rangers reporting and responding to an animal sighting.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you include to keep the message clear and short?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Map a Communication Network</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool or paper, map out how a message might travel from a ranger in the field to help arriving — ranger to base, base to nearest team, etc.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Where in your chain could a message get delayed or lost?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ranger Radios in Action</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video showing rangers using radios on patrol.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;anti-poaching rangers daily patrol Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What tone or style of speaking did you notice in how they communicated?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 10: Carving a Story in Stone":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">CARVING A STORY IN STONE — Shona Stone Sculpture as Storytelling</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Developing Practical Knowledge: explore and use materials, techniques and technologies with growing confidence to develop a work with intention.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Shona stone sculpture is an internationally renowned art tradition from Zimbabwe, not just decoration but genuine storytelling.</li><li>We are learning that carvers plan a figure from multiple angles before removing any material, because material removed can't be put back.</li><li>I can shape a simple figure that represents an idea or feeling, and explain the intention behind it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the planning sketches and carving process; review of finished carvings and titles for evidence of clear intention behind the chosen form.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Shona stone sculpture is famous the world over — proper famous, museums-and-galleries famous,&quot; Tapiwa says, holding up a small worn photo tucked in the back of the logbook. &quot;It's not just decoration, it's storytelling, the same way San rock art is, just carved instead of painted. A block of stone becomes a bird, an ancestor, a feeling. I want you to try shaping something today, even if all we've got is soap and not stone.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you could only remove material, never add it back, how carefully would you need to plan before you even started cutting?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Shona Stone Sculpture? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Shona stone sculpture is a modern Zimbabwean art tradition, internationally celebrated since the mid-20th century, in which artists carve figures — often abstract or symbolic representations of ancestors, animals, family bonds, or emotions — from stones such as serpentine.</li><li>Explain that, like San rock art, Shona sculpture is a form of storytelling: the shape itself, not written words, carries the meaning, and viewers are often invited to interpret feeling and idea from form.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;How is telling a story through a carved SHAPE different from telling a story through words?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Planning Before Carving (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that skilled carvers plan every side of a figure before removing any material, because — unlike drawing or painting — material that's carved away cannot be put back. This makes planning from multiple angles essential.</li><li>Model briefly: sketch a simple figure from front, side and top view before demonstrating a first careful cut into soap or clay.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What could go wrong if a carver started cutting without planning all sides first?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Soap or Clay Carving (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Using soft carving soap (or air-dry clay as a substitute), students carve a simple figure inspired by Shona stone sculpture traditions — an animal, ancestor figure, or abstract form representing an idea or feeling — using safe carving tools under supervision.</li><li>Before carving, students sketch their planned figure from three different angles, mirroring how real carvers plan every side before removing material they can't put back.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Did your finished carving end up matching your plan, or did it change along the way? Why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Give your finished carving a one-word title that captures the feeling or idea behind it — then write one sentence explaining why you chose that word.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: God filled Bezalel with skill and creativity to craft beautiful, meaningful things for His people — a reminder that artistic skill itself is a gift from God, given so we can shape material into something that carries real meaning forward.</li><li>Verse: &quot;I have filled him with the Spirit of God, with wisdom, with understanding, with knowledge and with all kinds of skills.&quot; — <em>Exodus 35:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Carving something with care and intention, the way Shona sculptors do, is a way of loving both the material and the idea being shaped — taking the time to plan, rather than rushing.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Exodus says God filled a craftsman with skill for a purpose. Carving means planning carefully because mistakes can't be undone — where else in life do you need to &quot;plan before you cut&quot;?</li><li>Shona sculptors often carve feelings, not just objects. What feeling would YOU want to carve into stone if you could?</li><li>What is one thing you're proud of that took careful planning, the way today's carving did?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Soft carving soap or air-dry clay, one block per student</li><li>Safe carving tools (blunt-tipped, teacher-supervised) appropriate for the material used</li><li>A small set of respectful reference images of Shona stone sculptures</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Shona stone sculpture of Zimbabwe</em> — search: &quot;Shona stone sculpture Zimbabwe art explained&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in the real tradition before they attempt their own carving.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend Your Carving Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose your carving's three-angle plan and add one more angle (from above), showing how the figure would look from directly overhead.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did adding a fourth angle reveal that the first three didn't show?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write the Story Behind the Carving</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, the story or feeling your finished carving was meant to represent.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Do you think someone looking at your carving without this explanation would guess the same story? Why or why not?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Sculpture Concept</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, sketch a digital concept for a Shona-inspired sculpture from three angles, in the spirit of planning before carving.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder — planning the front view, or the side view? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Shona Stone Sculpture</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Shona stone sculpture.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Shona stone sculpture Zimbabwe art explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing that surprised you about how meaningful, or how famous, this art form is?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 11: The Gold and Ivory Kingdom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE GOLD AND IVORY KINGDOM — Mapungubwe and Southern Africa's Ancient Trade Links</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the perspectives of people who lived before written record can still be known today, through evidence and continuing living culture.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how trade and movement connect places across long distances, even in the distant past.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Mapungubwe was a gold and ivory trading kingdom in Southern Africa, active before Great Zimbabwe reached its height.</li><li>We are learning that Mapungubwe traded across the Indian Ocean, as far as Arabia, India and China.</li><li>I can trace a plausible trade route on a map and explain what goods might have travelled along it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the map-tracing activity and class discussion; review of exit tickets for understanding of Mapungubwe's trade connections.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A new voice crackles in over the radio for the first time — steady, a little shy, but sure of itself. &quot;Sharp, crew, this side too.&quot; Tapiwa grins. &quot;That's my cousin Nomvula, finally! She's been waiting to tell you this one herself.&quot; Nomvula continues: &quot;Before Great Zimbabwe even faded, there was Mapungubwe — a gold and ivory trading kingdom, right here, trading all the way to Arabia, India, even China. That's the pattern of it, crew: this land has been connected to the whole world for a very, very long time.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a kingdom here was trading with places as far away as China, centuries ago, what would that journey have actually taken to make happen?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Was Mapungubwe? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Mapungubwe was a powerful kingdom that flourished from roughly the 11th to 13th centuries, near where South Africa, Zimbabwe and Botswana now meet — it is considered a forerunner to Great Zimbabwe, and one of Southern Africa's earliest known class-based societies.</li><li>Explain that Mapungubwe grew wealthy through trading gold and ivory, and that archaeologists have found evidence of this — including a small, famous golden rhino ornament discovered in a royal burial site.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What does finding gold objects buried with a ruler tell us about how the society was organised?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Trading Across the Indian Ocean (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Show a map tracing Mapungubwe's trade connections — goods travelled overland to the East African coast, then by dhow (sailing vessel) across the Indian Ocean to Arabia, India, and as far as China, evidenced by imported glass beads and Chinese ceramics found at the site.</li><li>Activity: In pairs, students trace a possible trade route from Mapungubwe to one of its trading partners (Arabia, India, or China) on a map, discussing what goods might have travelled each way (gold and ivory outward; glass beads, ceramics, cloth inward).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might gold and ivory have been so valuable to traders thousands of kilometres away?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A World Already Connected (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Discuss as a class that long before European contact, Southern Africa was already part of a wide, ancient trade network — a fact often missing from popular versions of world history.</li><li>Connect back to Great Zimbabwe (Week 2): Mapungubwe's decline is thought to link to Great Zimbabwe's later rise, showing how power and trade shifted between kingdoms over centuries.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think most people haven't heard of Mapungubwe, even though it traded with China centuries ago?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE place Mapungubwe traded with, and explain what that connection tells us about this region's history.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Mapungubwe's traders used honest weights and measures to build trust across vast distances — the kind of fair dealing the Bible repeatedly calls for. A kingdom's reputation, then as now, rested on whether its word and its scales could be trusted.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The Lord detests dishonest scales, but accurate weights find favor with him.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 11:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that Southern Africa has always been connected to the wider world helps us see God's story as one big, joined-up story, not a set of separate, unconnected ones.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs says God cares about honest dealing between people — even in trade routes thousands of years old. Why do you think fairness matters to God, even in something as ordinary as buying and selling?</li><li>Nomvula is proud that her home has &quot;always been connected to the whole world.&quot; What is something about your own home or community that connects it to somewhere far away?</li><li>What does it look like to deal honestly and fairly with people this week, the way good traders had to?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Map of the Indian Ocean trade network, showing Mapungubwe, the East African coast, Arabia, India and China</li><li>Images of Mapungubwe artefacts (the golden rhino, glass beads, imported ceramics), if available</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mapungubwe: the golden kingdom</em> — search: &quot;Mapungubwe golden rhino kingdom history explained&quot;. Gives students a clear, visual introduction to Mapungubwe and its famous golden rhino artefact.</li><li><em>Indian Ocean trade routes explained</em> — search: &quot;Indian Ocean trade routes Africa Arabia India China history&quot;. Helps students understand the scale and reach of the trade network Mapungubwe was part of.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Merchant's Trade-Mark</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple trade-mark or seal that a Mapungubwe merchant might have used on their goods.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a symbol that represents trust or quality.</li><li>Sketch it as a small, simple stamp-like design.</li><li>Add one label explaining what your symbol means.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why might a simple, recognisable symbol matter more than a detailed picture for a trade-mark?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Trader's Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short first-person account from a trader travelling from Mapungubwe to the East African coast to meet a foreign ship.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the biggest risk your trader faced on the journey?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Map the Trade Route</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing or slide tool, mark Mapungubwe's trade route to one trading partner (Arabia, India or China), labelling goods travelling each direction.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which good travelling along your route do you think was most valuable, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Mapungubwe</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Mapungubwe and its history.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Mapungubwe golden rhino kingdom history explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact you learned that you didn't expect?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 12: Farming for the World, Not Just the Table":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FARMING FOR THE WORLD, NOT JUST THE TABLE — Commercial Farming and Export Crops</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people's economic activities connect a place to the wider world, and how scale changes both purpose and risk.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what commercial farming is, and how it differs in scale and purpose from the subsistence farming we studied in Week 2.</li><li>We are learning that tobacco, citrus and sugar are grown in Zimbabwe and South Africa mainly for export to markets far beyond the region.</li><li>I can compare subsistence and commercial farming across purpose, scale and risk.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the comparison-chart activity and class discussion; review of export labels for evidence of understanding commercial farming's purpose.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Tobacco, citrus, sugar — some farms around here aren't growing food for the family table, they're growing for export, for markets far beyond Zimbabwe or South Africa entirely,&quot; Tapiwa says, spreading a hand-drawn map of farmland across the logbook page. &quot;That's a completely different kind of farming to my mum's little plot. Different scale, different risks, different everything. Let's compare them properly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a whole farm exists to sell one crop to buyers on the other side of the world, what happens to that farm if the price of that crop suddenly drops?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Commercial Farming? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Commercial farming means growing crops at large scale specifically to sell for profit, often to markets far beyond the country where they're grown — this is different from subsistence farming (Week 2), which grows just enough to feed a family.</li><li>Introduce tobacco, citrus (oranges, grapefruit) and sugar as major export crops grown commercially in Zimbabwe and South Africa, often on much larger farms than a family subsistence plot.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a commercial farm need far more land, workers and equipment than a subsistence plot?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Comparing Purpose, Scale and Risk (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: As a class, build a comparison chart of subsistence farming vs commercial farming across three categories: purpose (feeding a family vs earning income), scale (small plot vs large farm), and what happens if the harvest fails (a subsistence family faces hunger directly; a commercial farm faces lost income and possible worker layoffs).</li><li>Discuss: a commercial farm depends on far-away buyers and prices it cannot control — a risk a subsistence family growing its own food doesn't face in the same way.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which type of farming feels riskier to you, and why — and does 'riskier' always mean 'worse'?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Both Matter (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that both types of farming matter to Southern Africa's economy and food security — commercial farming brings in income and jobs, while subsistence farming keeps rural families fed directly, and many regions rely on a mix of both.</li><li>Discuss: some families do both — a subsistence plot for their own food, alongside working on or near a commercial farm for income.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a family choose to do both kinds of farming instead of just one?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE difference between subsistence and commercial farming, and explain why that difference matters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Whether a farmer grows just enough for their own family's table or a whole field bound for markets overseas, Scripture treats both kinds of labour with the same dignity — honest work, worthy of its reward, wherever the harvest ends up.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The hardworking farmer should be the first to receive a share of the crops.&quot; — <em>2 Timothy 2:6 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Recognising the value of both small, family-feeding farms and large export farms is a way of honouring the dignity of hard work at every scale.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>2 Timothy says a hardworking farmer deserves a fair share of the harvest. Do you think that's equally true for a small family farmer and someone working a huge export farm? Why or why not?</li><li>Tapiwa's family farms differently from a commercial tobacco or citrus farm nearby. What is one kind of hard work in your own family or community that deserves more recognition than it gets?</li><li>What does it look like to value someone's work fairly, whether their &quot;harvest&quot; is small or huge?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or samples of tobacco, citrus and sugar (or packaging showing these as export products)</li><li>Blank two-column comparison chart template (subsistence vs commercial)</li><li>Map showing major commercial farming regions of Zimbabwe and South Africa</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>What is commercial farming?</em> — search: &quot;commercial farming explained for kids export crops&quot;. Gives students a clear, age-appropriate definition before the comparison activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design an Export Label</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple export label for a Southern African farm product of your choice (tobacco, citrus or sugar).</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one export crop.</li><li>Design a label including the product name, country of origin, and one simple image.</li><li>Add one line describing the product for a buyer far away.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to highlight to make your product appealing to a buyer who has never seen it grown?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Two Farms, Two Letters</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write two very short letters — one from a subsistence farmer, one from a commercial farm worker — each describing their day.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60–80 words each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the clearest difference between the two days you imagined?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Farming Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple document, slide or spreadsheet to compare subsistence and commercial farming across purpose, scale and risk.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which category showed the biggest difference between the two types of farming?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Commercial Farming</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining commercial farming and export crops.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;commercial farming explained for kids export crops&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that changed how you think about where farm products in shops come from?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 13: Who Eats Whom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHO EATS WHOM — Savanna Predators and Food Webs</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand how living things are related to each other and to their environment through feeding relationships, and how changes affect the whole system.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a food web is, and how plants, herbivores and predators connect within one.</li><li>We are learning specifically how lions and leopards fit into the savanna food web as predators.</li><li>I can predict what might happen to a food web if one part of it disappeared.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class food web construction and group predictions; review of drawn food web sections for accurate, connected relationships.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;You can't understand a lion without understanding everything it eats, and everything THAT eats, all the way down to the grass,&quot; Tapiwa says, sketching lines in the dirt with a stick. &quot;My dad's team studies this constantly — predators, prey, and everything connecting them. Show me how it all fits together?&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you removed just ONE animal from the savanna — say, every antelope disappeared overnight — how far do you think the effects would actually spread?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Food Web? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A food web shows how energy moves through an ecosystem — from producers (plants, which make their own energy from sunlight) to herbivores (which eat plants) to predators (which eat other animals) — and how many of these feeding relationships connect and overlap, unlike a simple single-line food chain.</li><li>Introduce lion and leopard as key savanna predators, explaining what each typically hunts (antelope, zebra and other herbivores) and how their presence affects herbivore behaviour and numbers.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why is a 'web' a better word than a 'chain' for describing these feeding relationships?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Building a Savanna Food Web (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: As a class, build a simple savanna food web diagram together on the board, using arrows to connect grass → antelope/zebra → lion/leopard, then adding extra connections (e.g. scavengers, other prey species) as students suggest them.</li><li>Discuss how energy and connection flow in more than one direction once several species and predators are added.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What would happen to the lions if there were suddenly far fewer antelope?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Ripple Effect Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students predict what might happen to the whole food web if one link — for example, all the antelope — suddenly disappeared, tracing the ripple effects forward (less food for lions) and sideways (more grass left uneaten, affecting other herbivores).</li><li>Groups share one ripple effect they predicted and explain their reasoning using the food web diagram.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which part of the food web do you think would be hit hardest, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE feeding connection in the savanna food web, and explain what might happen if it were broken.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: A food web is a picture of astonishing, interlocking design — every creature connected to another, nothing standing entirely alone. Genesis describes God's care in exactly this way: a world made whole, each part depending on and supporting the next.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God saw all that he had made, and it was very good.&quot; — <em>Genesis 1:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Understanding how deeply every creature in the savanna depends on another helps us love and care for creation as the connected whole God made it to be, not as isolated pieces.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Genesis says God looked at everything he'd made, connected and complete, and called it &quot;very good.&quot; How does seeing a food web change the way you think about that verse?</li><li>Tapiwa's dad's team studies how everything out here is connected. Who or what are YOU connected to and depend on, in ways you don't always notice?</li><li>What is one small way you could care for a &quot;connection&quot; in your own life or community this week — a friendship, a responsibility, a piece of creation nearby?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Whiteboard or large paper for building the class food web diagram</li><li>Animal/plant cards (grass, antelope, zebra, lion, leopard, scavenger) for the diagram-building activity</li><li>Images of lions and leopards hunting or resting, for context</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How savanna food webs work</em> — search: &quot;savanna food web lion leopard explained kids&quot;. Gives students a clear visual grounding in food web relationships before the ripple-effect activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw Your Food Web Section</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw your own small section of a savanna food web, with at least four connected organisms.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose four organisms (at least one plant, one herbivore, one predator, one other).</li><li>Draw arrows showing &quot;eaten by&quot; connections between them.</li><li>Label each arrow with the direction of energy flow.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which organism in your web has the most connections, and what does that suggest about its importance?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Day in the Food Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story following one meal, from grass to antelope to lion, describing the journey of energy through the food web.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you about tracing energy this way?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Food Web Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing or slide tool, build a labelled food web diagram showing at least five connected organisms and the direction of energy flow.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest connection to represent clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Savanna Food Webs</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about how savanna food webs work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;savanna food web lion leopard explained kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one connection in the food web you hadn't thought about before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 14: The Watching Eye":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE WATCHING EYE — Camera Traps and Studying Animals Without Disturbing Them</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Products: understand that materials, electronics and sensors can be combined to form a technological product that senses and responds to its environment.</li><li>Technological Practice: understand the role of planning and evaluation in the development of a technological outcome.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a camera trap is and how it senses and reacts to a passing animal.</li><li>We are learning why researchers prefer to study predators without disturbing them.</li><li>I can plan where I would place a camera trap and justify my choice using real reasoning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the camera-trap placement activity and group justifications; review of written design explanations for understanding of sensor logic.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook opens over the Zambezi at dusk, and for a moment the class hears Nomvula's voice crackle in over the radio for the first time — &quot;Sharp, crew, this side too!&quot; — before Tapiwa answers, then turns to the class. &quot;Out here, if you want to see a lion properly, you can't just walk up and ask it to wait around. My dad's team uses something smarter.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: how could you find out exactly which animals visit a waterhole at night, without ever being there yourself?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How a Camera Trap Actually Works (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A camera trap is a weatherproof camera with a motion and/or heat sensor (similar to the sensor in some automatic lights) — when something warm moves in front of it, the sensor triggers the camera to take a photo or short video, then goes back to waiting.</li><li>Explain the input → process → output idea simply: input = movement/heat detected, process = sensor triggers the camera, output = a photo or video saved automatically, day or night.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a sensor-triggered camera capture something a human with a camera never could?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why Not Just Watch in Person? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Predators like lion and leopard change their behaviour around people — some avoid humans, some become dangerously bold. A camera trap lets researchers gather real, honest data about what animals actually do when no one is watching.</li><li>Discuss real uses: counting how many individual animals live in an area, tracking health and injuries over time, and — increasingly — spotting people or vehicles where they shouldn't be, which links camera traps directly to anti-poaching work too.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What's the difference between data collected by camera trap and a story someone remembers from a sighting?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Placement Planning Challenge (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students are given a simple map of a waterhole/game trail area and must decide and justify exactly where they'd place 2–3 camera traps to capture the most useful information, considering animal paths, height, and angle.</li><li>Groups present their placement choices to another group, who challenge them with one &quot;what if&quot; question (e.g., &quot;what if the sun is behind your camera every afternoon?&quot;).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What is one thing that could go wrong with a badly placed camera trap?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: why is a camera trap sometimes a better research tool than a person with a camera?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Psalms describe God watching over the whole earth — seeing what happens even when no human eye is there. A camera trap does a small, human-sized version of that: it keeps a faithful, patient watch, even in the dark, even when nobody's looking.</li><li>Verse: &quot;From heaven the LORD looks down and sees all mankind.&quot; — <em>Psalm 33:13 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Watching carefully and patiently — the way a camera trap does — helps us know creation as it truly is, not just the version we happen to see.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>If God sees the whole earth, even the parts no human ever visits, what does that tell you about how much He cares about all of it — not just the parts people notice?</li><li>A camera trap tells the truth about what happens, even in the dark. Why does it matter to be truthful, even when nobody's watching us either?</li><li>What's one part of creation you think God delights in that most people never even see?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple diagram of a camera trap (sensor, camera, weatherproof case)</li><li>Printed waterhole/game trail maps, one per group</li><li>Example real camera-trap photos (lion, leopard, antelope at night) if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How camera traps work</em> — search: &quot;how do camera traps work wildlife research&quot;. Explains the sensor technology behind today's lesson in an age-appropriate way.</li><li><em>Best camera trap footage of African predators</em> — search: &quot;camera trap footage lion leopard Africa night&quot;. Gives students a genuine sense of what this technology reveals that a human observer couldn't.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Camera Trap Housing</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled design for a weatherproof camera trap case that could survive rain, dust and curious animals.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What feature of your design solves a specific problem, and what problem is it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Researcher's Field Notes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short field-notes entry in role as a researcher reviewing a week of camera trap photos from one waterhole.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What pattern did your imagined data reveal?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Sketch a Sensor Flowchart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple flowchart (input → process → output) showing exactly how a camera trap reacts when an animal walks past.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would happen if the sensor was too sensitive, or not sensitive enough?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Camera Trap Footage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch real camera trap footage of African predators.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;camera trap footage lion leopard Africa night&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the footage show that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 15: Painting the Walls":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">PAINTING THE WALLS — Ndebele Geometric House Art</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Developing Practical Knowledge: explore and use materials, techniques and technologies with growing confidence, including pattern and symmetry, to develop a work with intention.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Ndebele house painting is a real, living geometric art tradition, painted directly onto the outside walls of homes.</li><li>We are learning that Ndebele wall art relies on precise, repeated geometry and symmetry, not free-hand guessing.</li><li>I can plan and create a geometric pattern with intentional meaning, in the spirit of Ndebele wall art.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the symmetry-planning and painting process; review of finished patterns and explanations for evidence of intentional design choices.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;In my village, the walls themselves are art,&quot; Nomvula says, and for the first time the class can hear the sound of her village behind her voice — distant voices, someone laughing. &quot;My gogo is one of the best house-painters around — bold geometric patterns, painted straight onto the outside walls, for everyone passing by to see. Every pattern can mean something, if you know how to look. That's the pattern of it. Today, design your own — something that would mean something to YOU, painted big enough for the whole street to see.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if your own house's outside wall was painted with one pattern, visible to your whole street, what would you want it to say about your family?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is Ndebele Wall Art? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Ndebele house painting is a living tradition from Southern Africa, most associated with Ndebele communities, in which women traditionally paint bold, geometric patterns — straight lines, triangles, zigzags and repeating shapes in strong, contrasting colours — directly onto the outside walls of their homes.</li><li>Explain that this art form is not random decoration: patterns are planned with real precision and symmetry, and can carry meaning, mark celebrations, or express identity and pride, visible to the whole community.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might painting a pattern on the OUTSIDE of a house, for everyone to see, feel different from painting something just for yourself?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Planning With Symmetry (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Real Ndebele wall art relies on careful, repeated geometry — straight lines and precise angles, often planned out before any paint is applied, so the finished mural reads as sharp and intentional rather than freehand and uneven.</li><li>Model briefly: sketch a simple symmetrical geometric pattern on grid paper, showing how a shape repeated and mirrored builds a stronger design than one placed randomly.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What might go wrong with a wall pattern that wasn't planned for symmetry first?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Ndebele-Inspired Geometric Wall Art (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Using bold, bright paint or oil pastel on paper, students design a geometric pattern inspired by Ndebele house painting — straight lines, triangles, and repeating shapes in strong contrasting colours, arranged the way a real house-wall mural would be.</li><li>Before painting, students plan their pattern's symmetry on grid paper, mirroring how real Ndebele wall art is planned with precise, repeated geometry rather than free-hand guessing.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which part of your pattern repeats, and why does repetition make a wall mural stronger?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose one shape in your design and explain, in one sentence, what it represents to you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Nomvula's gogo paints patterns bold enough for a whole street to see — a visible, public expression of identity and pride. The Bible often speaks of light that isn't meant to be hidden, but placed where everyone can see it.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Let your light shine before others, that they may see your good deeds.&quot; — <em>Matthew 5:16 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Creating something bold and visible, meant to be seen and shared with a whole community, is one way of serving others with our gifts rather than keeping them hidden.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus talks about a light that shouldn't be hidden. Ndebele wall art is a light of a different kind — visible pride, painted for everyone. What &quot;light&quot; do you have that you sometimes keep too hidden?</li><li>Nomvula's gogo paints for her whole street, not just her own house. Who in your life creates or does something generous, meant for others to enjoy, not just themselves?</li><li>What is one way you could let something good about you &quot;shine&quot; more visibly this week, the way this wall art does?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bold, bright paint or oil pastels in strong contrasting colours (e.g. black, white, red, yellow, blue)</li><li>Grid paper for symmetry planning</li><li>A small set of respectful reference images of real Ndebele house-painted walls</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Ndebele house painting explained</em> — search: &quot;Ndebele house painting geometric wall art South Africa&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in the real tradition before they plan their own pattern.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend Your Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take your finished geometric pattern and design a second version using a completely different colour combination, keeping the same shapes and symmetry.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How much did changing only the colours change the overall feeling of your pattern?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Meaning Behind the Wall</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, what your pattern represents and why you chose those particular shapes and colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Do you think a stranger walking past your &quot;wall&quot; would guess the same meaning without your explanation?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Symmetrical Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, create a digital geometric pattern with clear symmetry, in the spirit of Ndebele wall art.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to achieve digitally — the geometry, or the symmetry?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ndebele Wall Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Ndebele house painting.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Ndebele house painting geometric wall art South Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing that surprised you about how precise or how meaningful this art form is?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 16: A Kingdom on the Move":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A KINGDOM ON THE MOVE — The Mfecane and the Rise of the Ndebele Kingdom</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how conflict, environment and movement have shaped the formation of communities and nations over time.</li><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how people respond to environmental pressures such as drought by moving and rebuilding elsewhere.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Language, Symbols and Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the Mfecane was, and how drought and conflict pushed large movements of people across Southern Africa in the early 1800s.</li><li>We are learning that the Ndebele Kingdom — Nomvula's own people — rose out of this period of upheaval.</li><li>I can explain at least three possible reasons a community might choose to leave everything and rebuild somewhere new.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group discussion and reasoning; review of migration-route sketches for engagement with the scale of the movement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The radio crackles with more static than usual today. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, quieter than before. &quot;Before I tell you today's history, you should know — it's connected to something happening right now, this week. But first, the story: in the early 1800s, drought and conflict pushed huge movements of people across this region — the Mfecane, it's called — and out of it, the Ndebele Kingdom rose. Whole nations, on the move, rebuilding somewhere new. Nomvula's people, actually. Her whole village traces back to it.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if drought and conflict forced your whole community to leave and rebuild somewhere completely new, what do you think would be hardest to leave behind?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Was the Mfecane? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The Mfecane (&quot;the crushing&quot; or &quot;scattering&quot;) was a period of major upheaval across Southern Africa in the early 1800s, driven by a combination of severe drought, competition for land and resources, and conflict between growing kingdoms — it caused large-scale migration and the reshaping of entire communities.</li><li>Explain that out of this period of movement and hardship, new nations formed, including the Ndebele Kingdom, whose people migrated and rebuilt in a new region — the same Ndebele heritage Nomvula's village traces back to today.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might drought AND conflict together push people to move, when either one alone might not?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Leaving and Rebuilding (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students discuss what might make a whole community decide to leave everything they know and rebuild somewhere entirely new, listing at least three possible reasons (e.g. lack of water, safety, land to farm, keeping the community together).</li><li>Groups share their reasons, and the class discusses which reasons feel most urgent versus which feel more like long-term planning.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What skills or knowledge would a community need to carry WITH them, even if they had to leave everything else behind?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">From Upheaval to a New Kingdom (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that the Ndebele Kingdom that formed from this period became a significant and lasting nation in the region, with its own leadership, culture and identity — a reminder that difficult, disruptive periods in history can still lead to communities that endure and thrive.</li><li>Connect to today: Nomvula's village, its geometric wall art (Week 3) and beadwork traditions (this Friday) all trace back to this history of movement and rebuilding.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a community that has had to rebuild once become especially good at holding onto what matters most to them?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE reason a community might have moved during the Mfecane, and explain why that reason makes sense to you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Mfecane was a genuinely hard chapter — drought, conflict, whole communities uprooted. Yet out of it came new nations that endured. The Psalms describe exactly this kind of hope: God as a refuge and strength precisely in seasons of real trouble, not only in easy ones.</li><li>Verse: &quot;God is our refuge and strength, an ever-present help in trouble.&quot; — <em>Psalm 46:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that a community can face real upheaval and still rebuild something lasting helps us understand both history and hope more honestly.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 46 says God is &quot;an ever-present help in trouble&quot; — not only after trouble ends. What does it look like to trust that in the MIDDLE of a hard time, not just once it's over?</li><li>Nomvula's people rebuilt after huge upheaval. Has your own family or community ever had to rebuild after something hard? What helped?</li><li>Tapiwa sounds worried today, even before telling the history. What's one way you could be a steady presence for a friend who's worried about something, this week?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Map of Southern Africa showing the approximate scale and direction of Mfecane-era migrations</li><li>Timeline showing the early 1800s in regional context</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The Mfecane explained</em> — search: &quot;Mfecane Southern Africa history explained migration&quot;. Gives students an age-appropriate overview of this period of upheaval and migration.</li><li><em>The Ndebele Kingdom</em> — search: &quot;Ndebele Kingdom history South Africa Zimbabwe&quot;. Connects the historical period directly to Nomvula's own heritage.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Migration Route</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple migration route on a map, imagining the journey a community might have taken during the Mfecane.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a simple regional map outline.</li><li>Draw a dotted route from a starting point to a new settlement.</li><li>Mark one obstacle (river, mountain, drought area) the community would have had to cross.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the hardest part of the journey to imagine — the distance, or the uncertainty of where it would end?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter Before Leaving</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter in role as a young person in a community about to leave and rebuild somewhere new, describing how you feel.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to focus on — what you were losing, or what you hoped to find?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Migration Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple document or slide tool, build a short timeline showing drought/conflict, migration, and the formation of a new kingdom.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which stage of your timeline do you think took the longest, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Mfecane</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining the Mfecane and its impact.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Mfecane Southern Africa history explained migration&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that helped you understand why so many people were on the move at once?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 17: When the Rain Doesn't Come":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHEN THE RAIN DOESN'T COME — The Kariba Dam, Water and Drought</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how water systems connect places, and how drought affects communities differently depending on their scale and reliance on that water.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the Kariba Dam is and its role in providing water and electricity across the region.</li><li>We are learning that drought affects far more than one field — it ripples out to farms, electricity, wildlife and people all connected to the same water system.</li><li>I can identify at least three things that depend on the Zambezi's water and explain what happens to each in a drought.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the mapping activity and group discussion; review of water-saving posters for clarity of message.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The Kariba Dam is one of the biggest in the world — it holds back the Zambezi River, makes electricity for whole countries,&quot; Tapiwa says, sketching a wide dam wall across the page. &quot;But when the rains fail, even something that huge feels small against the problem. Drought doesn't just dry out a field, crew — it changes everything downstream from it. I want you to see the whole picture, not just my mum's plot.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if one dam provides water and electricity for whole countries, what do you think happens to everyone downstream when the rains simply don't come?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is the Kariba Dam? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: The Kariba Dam sits on the Zambezi River between Zambia and Zimbabwe, and is one of the largest dams in the world by reservoir volume — it generates hydroelectric power for both countries and supports Lake Kariba, a major source of water and fishing for the region.</li><li>Explain simply how a hydroelectric dam works: water held back by the dam flows through turbines to generate electricity, meaning the dam's water level directly affects how much power it can produce.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a single dam being affected by drought cause problems for people who live nowhere near it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Mapping What Depends on the Water (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students map out everything that depends on the Zambezi's water — farms, electricity generation, wildlife, drinking water, fishing, tourism — and discuss what happens to each specifically when a drought hits.</li><li>Groups present one dependency they mapped and explain the ripple effect of drought on it.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these dependencies do you think would be affected FIRST when a drought begins, and which would take longer to feel the effects?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Small Choices, Big Picture (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that while a single family can't control rainfall, water-saving choices at a household level (shorter showers, fixing leaks, watering gardens carefully) add up across a whole community and can meaningfully ease pressure during drought.</li><li>Discuss: this connects &quot;big picture&quot; problems like a regional drought to genuinely useful &quot;small choices&quot; anyone can make.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What is one water-saving habit that would be easy for YOUR household to start this week?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing that depends on the Zambezi's water, and explain what happens to it in a drought.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Water sustains everything downstream of it — farms, power, animals, people — in a way that mirrors how deeply connected creation truly is. Scripture pictures God's provision the same way: not stingy or isolated, but flowing out to sustain a whole, interconnected world.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He waters the mountains from his upper chambers; the land is satisfied by the fruit of his work.&quot; — <em>Psalm 104:13 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Recognising how one resource — water — ripples out to affect an entire region helps us see that even small, careful choices genuinely matter to a much bigger picture.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 104 pictures God watering the whole land, sustaining far more than any one person could see at once. How does thinking about the whole Zambezi system change how you think about &quot;just&quot; turning off a tap?</li><li>Tapiwa says &quot;big picture, small choices — they're connected.&quot; Where else in your own life is a small daily choice actually connected to something much bigger?</li><li>What is one way you could be more careful with a resource this week — water or otherwise — because you know it affects more than just you?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Map of the Zambezi River system showing Kariba Dam and Lake Kariba</li><li>Simple diagram of how a hydroelectric dam generates power</li><li>Blank poster template for the water-saving creative task</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>The Kariba Dam explained</em> — search: &quot;Kariba Dam Zambezi River hydroelectric explained&quot;. Gives students a clear visual introduction to the dam and how it works before the mapping activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Water-Saving Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple poster encouraging water-saving at home, using one clear image and a short slogan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one water-saving habit to focus on.</li><li>Sketch one clear, simple image supporting your message.</li><li>Write a short, memorable slogan (under 8 words).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular habit to highlight over other options?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Diary of Drought</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry in role as a farmer near the Zambezi during a drought year, describing what's changing around you.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to worry about most in your diary entry, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Map the Water Dependency Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing or slide tool, map at least four things that depend on the Zambezi's water, with a note on how drought affects each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which dependency in your map surprised you the most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: The Kariba Dam</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining the Kariba Dam and its role in the region.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Kariba Dam Zambezi River hydroelectric explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact about the dam's scale that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 18: The Rhino Crisis":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE RHINO CRISIS — Understanding Endangered Species and Conservation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand that human activity can threaten a species' survival, and that conservation efforts respond to this with real, evidence-based action.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what makes a species &quot;endangered,&quot; and that rhino poaching is a real and serious threat facing Southern Africa today.</li><li>We are learning that real people and organisations are actively working to protect rhino populations.</li><li>I can explain why protecting one endangered species can matter to an entire ecosystem, not just to that species alone.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion, handled with care given the sensitivity of the topic; review of awareness-symbol designs for evidence of engaged, thoughtful response.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tapiwa goes quiet — properly quiet, not joke-quiet — turning a small wooden elephant over and over in his pocket. &quot;Poachers,&quot; he says eventually. &quot;Near the reserve. Dad's team is out right now.&quot; Nomvula's voice comes through, steady. &quot;That's the pattern of it, crew — this is the hard week. Rhino poaching is real, and it's happening near people we love. But listen: there ARE people, and tools, fighting for them. I don't know if we can fix this one completely. I don't think anyone does. But we're not giving up on it either.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if protecting a rhino population takes real people, real tools and real effort, what do you think that effort actually looks like, day to day?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Does &quot;Endangered&quot; Mean? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain &quot;endangered&quot; simply — a species whose population has dropped so low that it faces a real risk of dying out entirely, usually because of human activity such as habitat loss or, in the rhino's case, poaching for their horns.</li><li>Explain, gently and without graphic detail, that rhino are poached because their horns are illegally sold, despite having no proven medicinal value, and that this illegal trade has caused a serious decline in wild rhino populations across Africa.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think laws alone haven't been enough to stop this problem?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why One Species Matters to a Whole Ecosystem (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that rhino are a &quot;keystone&quot; or ecologically important species — their grazing habits shape grassland and vegetation patterns in ways that affect many other species sharing their habitat, so losing rhino would change the whole ecosystem, not just remove one animal.</li><li>Discuss as a class: why protecting one endangered species can matter to an entire ecosystem, not just to that one animal.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Can you think of another animal we've studied this term whose loss might change more than just its own population?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The People Fighting For Them (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Introduce, at an appropriate level of detail, the real people and organisations working to protect rhino — anti-poaching rangers (like Baba Farai), conservation scientists, and wildlife organisations — emphasising that this is an active, ongoing effort with real progress in many reserves.</li><li>Activity: As a class, discuss what qualities or skills these different roles (ranger, scientist, community educator) would each need to contribute to protecting rhino.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these roles do you think you'd be best suited to, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE reason protecting rhino matters beyond just the rhino itself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: This is a genuinely hard topic, and Tapiwa and Nomvula don't pretend otherwise — but they hold onto hope alongside the honesty. The Bible speaks often of caring for creation as something entrusted to us, and of hope that keeps working even when a problem feels too big to fully solve.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The righteous care for the needs of their animals.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 12:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Choosing to care about a creature's wellbeing, and to keep working for it even without a guaranteed happy ending, is a real and honest way of bringing God's care into the world.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs says the righteous &quot;care for the needs of their animals.&quot; Why do you think caring for creation is treated as a matter of character, not just a nice extra?</li><li>Nomvula says &quot;I don't know if we can fix this one completely... but we're not giving up on it either.&quot; Is there a hard problem in your own life or community where that same attitude might help?</li><li>What is one small way you could show care for an animal or for creation this week, even if it feels like a small gesture against a big problem?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Respectful, non-graphic images of rhino in their habitat, and of rangers on patrol</li><li>Simple diagram showing a rhino's role in shaping grassland vegetation</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Understanding the rhino conservation crisis</em> — search: &quot;rhino conservation crisis explained for kids Africa&quot;. Gives students an age-appropriate, honest introduction to the topic, with a focus on real conservation response.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Rhino Conservation Symbol</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple awareness symbol for rhino conservation that could appear on a poster or badge.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose one clear message about rhino conservation.</li><li>Sketch a simple, memorable symbol representing it.</li><li>Add a short caption (under 6 words).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose that particular image to represent something as serious as this issue?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Ranger's Reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, thoughtful reflection in role as a ranger like Baba Farai, describing why the work matters to them even when it's hard.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to focus on — the difficulty of the work, or the reason it's worth doing?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Research a Conservation Organisation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using approved resources, research one real organisation working to protect rhino, and note three facts about their work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing this organisation does that you hadn't thought of as part of &quot;conservation&quot;?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rhino Conservation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about efforts to protect rhino populations.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;rhino conservation crisis explained for kids Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing that gave you hope after watching this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 19: Marked and Traced":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">MARKED AND TRACED — DNA, Microchips and the Fight to Protect Rhino</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand how technologies can be used ethically and responsibly to respond to a genuine societal need.</li><li>Technological Knowledge: understand how a technological system, such as a database, can be used to identify and trace materials.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a DNA database is, in simple terms, and why it can identify one specific rhino horn out of thousands.</li><li>We are learning about horn notching and microchipping as real, current anti-poaching technologies.</li><li>I can explain why identifying evidence is a crucial part of stopping wildlife crime, not just catching someone in the act.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the matching/identification activity and class discussion; review of exit tickets for understanding of how tracing evidence supports enforcement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The radio crackles mid-lesson, just like Baba Farai warned it might this week. Tapiwa goes quiet — properly quiet, not joke-quiet — turning the wooden elephant over and over in his pocket. &quot;Poachers,&quot; he says eventually. &quot;Near the reserve. Dad's team is out right now.&quot; Nomvula's voice comes through steady: &quot;That's the pattern of it, crew — this is the hard week. But listen: there are tools built exactly for this fight.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if a rhino horn is found somewhere it shouldn't be, how could anyone possibly prove exactly which rhino, and which reserve, it came from?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Why This Week Is Different (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Briefly and gently connect to the week's story — poaching is presented honestly as a real, serious problem, but the focus of this lesson is on the real people and real technology working against it, giving students agency rather than only alarm.</li><li>Set the tone: today is about the tools of protection, not the details of the crime.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">DNA as a Fingerprint (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain DNA simply — a unique genetic &quot;code&quot; inside every living thing's cells, different for every individual. In South Africa, a real system called RhODIS (Rhino DNA Index System) stores DNA samples from individual rhinos and from horns, so that if a horn is ever recovered, it can potentially be matched back to the exact rhino and location it came from — much like matching a fingerprint at a crime scene.</li><li>Activity: give groups a simplified &quot;DNA pattern matching&quot; worksheet — a set of horn samples with matching pattern codes, and a set of &quot;known rhino&quot; records — students match evidence to the correct individual, mirroring (in a very simplified way) how RhODIS works.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why does it matter to prove exactly WHERE a horn came from, not just that it is a rhino horn?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Notching and Microchipping (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain two further real techniques used by wildlife vets and rangers — ear notching (a small, painless pattern cut into a rhino's ear so individuals can be told apart at a distance) and microchipping (a tiny chip inserted into the horn itself, detectable by scanner, similar to a pet microchip).</li><li>Discuss as a class: these technologies don't stop a determined poacher on their own — they work alongside rangers, radios, and law enforcement. Technology is one part of a bigger system of protection, not a magic fix.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might using several different technologies together work better than relying on just one?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE real technology used to help protect rhino, and explain in your own words why it helps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Proverbs calls God's people to speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves. A rhino cannot explain what happened to it, or ask for help — but real people, using real technology, have chosen to be its voice and its defence.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 31:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Pānga is about bringing God's kingdom to earth in real, practical ways — and standing up for a creature that cannot stand up for itself is exactly that kind of impact.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs says to speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves. Who — or what — around you might need someone to do that for them?</li><li>Tapiwa is scared for his dad this week, but he still trusts the fight is worth it. Where do you find courage when something you care about feels under threat?</li><li>Technology alone doesn't save a rhino — people using it well does. What is one gift or skill YOU have that could be used to protect something that matters?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simplified &quot;DNA pattern matching&quot; worksheet sets (horn sample codes vs known rhino records)</li><li>Diagram or photo reference of ear-notching patterns used to identify individual rhino</li><li>Simple diagram of a microchip scanner (for context only — no need for real equipment)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How rhino DNA tracking works</em> — search: &quot;rhino DNA database RhODIS South Africa explained&quot;. Grounds today's lesson in the real system referenced in class, at an appropriate level of detail for the age group.</li><li><em>Rangers protecting rhino in South Africa</em> — search: &quot;rhino anti-poaching rangers South Africa&quot;. Shows the real people and teamwork behind the technology, connecting to Tapiwa's dad's work this week.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design an Ear-Notch Identification Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple chart showing 4–5 different notch patterns and the &quot;name&quot; each would identify.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does a pattern need to be simple enough to recognise from a distance?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Ranger's Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short, calm, factual radio report in role as a ranger who has just found a horn and needs to describe it accurately for identification.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why does accuracy matter more than speed in a report like this?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Explain the DNA-Matching Process</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple step-by-step diagram or flowchart explaining how a horn sample could be matched to a known rhino using a database.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What would happen to the system if the database wasn't kept accurate and up to date?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Rhino Protection Technology</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on real rhino DNA tracking or ranger anti-poaching work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;rhino DNA database RhODIS South Africa explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the system most impressed you, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 20: What a Colour Can Say":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHAT A COLOUR CAN SAY — Beadwork and Southern African Colour Symbolism</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Communicating and Interpreting: understand and use symbolic elements, including colour, to communicate ideas and feelings intentionally.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that traditional Southern African beadwork uses colour as a real, shared language, not just decoration.</li><li>We are learning to design a pattern where each colour choice carries intentional meaning.</li><li>I can explain what my finished beadwork pattern communicates, using only its colours.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the colour-code agreement and design process; review of finished patterns and explanations for evidence of intentional colour meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;My gogo says every bead colour and pattern can communicate something, if you know the language,&quot; Nomvula says, turning her half-finished beadwork bracelet over in her hands — one bead further along than last time. &quot;After this week, I wanted a gentler day — something that reminds us why we protect what we protect. Design your own bead pattern today, and give it real meaning, not just pretty colours.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you could only communicate a feeling using colours, with no words at all, how would you even begin to choose which colour meant what?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Colour as a Real Language (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that in many Southern African beadwork traditions, colours are assigned shared, understood meanings within a community — for example, green often relating to land or growth, blue to water, red to courage or strength, and white to purity or peace — and that patterns combining these colours can communicate real messages.</li><li>Explain that, like Ndebele wall art (Week 3), this is a genuine symbolic language, not random decoration — a viewer who knows the &quot;code&quot; can read real meaning into a pattern.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a community agree on shared colour meanings, instead of everyone choosing their own?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Agreeing on a Class Colour Code (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: As a class, agree on a simple shared &quot;colour code&quot; — three or four colours and what each represents (e.g. green for land, blue for water, red for courage, white for hope) — before students design individually using that shared code.</li><li>Discuss briefly why agreeing on the code together, before designing, makes the finished patterns &quot;readable&quot; by the whole class.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What would happen if everyone used their own private meaning for each colour instead of a shared code?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Beadwork Pattern Design (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Using paper &quot;beads&quot; (coloured circles/squares) or real beads on string/card, students design a beadwork pattern inspired by traditional Southern African colour symbolism, using the class's agreed colour code to give their pattern intentional meaning.</li><li>Students plan their pattern's sequence before assembling it, deciding deliberately which meaning should stand out most.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which colour did you use the most in your pattern, and does that reflect what you most wanted to say?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what your finished pattern says, using only its colours as your &quot;words.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After a genuinely hard week, Nomvula reaches for something small and steady — one more bead, added carefully, meaning something real. The Bible often pictures faithfulness this way too: not one dramatic act, but small, meaningful additions, made steadily over time.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Do not despise these small beginnings, for the Lord rejoices to see the work begin.&quot; — <em>Zechariah 4:10 (NLT)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that a pattern's meaning is built one small, deliberate choice at a time helps us see that faithfulness is often made of small things, added steadily, not one big gesture.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Zechariah says God rejoices even in small beginnings, not just finished, impressive things. Nomvula's bracelet is only one bead further along — why might that still be worth celebrating?</li><li>After a hard week in the story, this lesson was deliberately made gentler. Why might it matter to have a &quot;gentler day&quot; sometimes, even when there's still a bigger problem out there?</li><li>What is one small, steady thing you could add to this week — like Nomvula's one more bead — that would mean something real over time?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Paper &quot;beads&quot; (coloured circles/squares) or real beads on string/card</li><li>A simple reference sheet of traditional Southern African bead colour meanings</li><li>String, card, or paper strips for assembling patterns</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Southern African beadwork and colour meaning</em> — search: &quot;African beadwork colour symbolism meaning explained&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in the real tradition before designing their own pattern.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend Your Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Take your finished beadwork pattern and design a second, shorter version using only your TWO most important colours.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you have to leave out, and did the message get clearer or less clear without it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Meaning Behind the Beads</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, what your finished pattern communicates and why you chose that particular colour sequence.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Do you think someone using the same colour code as you would read your pattern the same way you meant it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Bead Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, design a digital version of a beadwork pattern using the class colour code.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to represent digitally — the colours, or the pattern's sequence?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Beadwork Colour Symbolism</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Southern African beadwork and colour meaning.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;African beadwork colour symbolism meaning explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one colour meaning that surprised you, or that you hadn't thought of before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: The Lost City Trial — Station 1: True Story or Myth?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TRUE STORY OR MYTH? — Testing Real History Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate understanding of historical events, evidence, and the people involved, drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating what we have learned about real Southern African history from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working as a team under time pressure to recall accurate historical facts.</li><li>I can tell the difference between a true historical fact and a common misconception.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 History content. Score team performance on the quick-fire round and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting before Week 6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook flares open onto every leg of the route flown so far — Hwange, the Zambezi, the reserve under threat — stitched together like a map coming alive. &quot;This is it, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, steadier than the class has heard him all month. &quot;Sekuru Josiah always says a good pilot doesn't just have one tool. Today, the logbook wants proof you've been paying attention.&quot; Nomvula's voice crackles in: &quot;That's the pattern of it. Quick wits and accurate memory will serve you far better than guessing.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: after four weeks of real history — the San, Great Zimbabwe, Mapungubwe, the Ndebele migration — which single fact do you think you'll still remember a year from now?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the format: this is a rapid-fire True/False round. Read each statement aloud; teams confer briefly (10–15 seconds) then signal their answer (show a card, raise a hand, or use a simple buzzer system).</li><li>Award one point per correct answer. Consider a bonus point for a team that can correctly explain WHY a false statement is false.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Quick-Fire Question Bank (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. The San people are believed to be among the first peoples to live in Southern Africa, with knowledge of the land stretching back tens of thousands of years. (TRUE)</li><li>2. San rock art was painted purely for decoration, with no storytelling or spiritual meaning behind it. (FALSE — it is understood as a genuine storytelling and spiritual tradition)</li><li>3. Great Zimbabwe's famous stone walls were built without any mortar holding the stones together. (TRUE)</li><li>4. Great Zimbabwe was a small village with no trade links beyond its own region. (FALSE — it was the centre of a powerful trading civilisation)</li><li>5. Mapungubwe was a trading kingdom whose links reached as far as Arabia, India and China. (TRUE)</li><li>6. Mapungubwe is famous for gold and ivory trade goods, including gold artefacts found by archaeologists. (TRUE)</li><li>7. The Mfecane was a period of major upheaval in the early 1800s caused only by conflict, with drought playing no real part. (FALSE — severe drought was a key driving factor alongside conflict)</li><li>8. The Ndebele Kingdom — Nomvula's own heritage — rose out of the migration and rebuilding that followed the Mfecane. (TRUE)</li><li>9. Subsistence farming (Week 2) means growing crops mainly to sell for profit rather than to feed your own family. (FALSE — that describes commercial farming; subsistence farming is mainly for the family's own food)</li><li>10. Maize, sorghum and groundnuts are all real examples of subsistence crops grown by families in the region. (TRUE)</li><li>Optional extension: invite teams to write one additional True/False statement of their own from Weeks 1–4 content, to challenge another team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal team scores. Briefly revisit any question more than one team got wrong, with a short explanation, before moving to tomorrow's station.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">As a team, write down the ONE question from today you're most confident you'll remember correctly in a year's time — and why it stuck.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Joshua told the Israelites to stack up stones as a memorial, so that when their children asked &quot;what do these stones mean?&quot; the story of what God had done would never be forgotten. Great Zimbabwe's stone walls do something similar — they make sure a story is never lost.</li><li>Verse: &quot;In the future, when your children ask you, &quot;What do these stones mean?&quot; tell them…&quot; — <em>Joshua 4:6–7 (NIV, shortened)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Piecing together Great Zimbabwe, Mapungubwe and the Ndebele migration helps us know that every civilisation's story matters to God, even the ones that were never written down.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Why do you think God cares about people remembering their history, not just living in the present?</li><li>What &quot;stones&quot; — objects, stories, photos — does your own family keep to remember its story?</li><li>Tapiwa says the trial only works if the crew works together. How is that true of God's people too?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quick-fire question bank (above) printed or read aloud</li><li>A simple way for teams to signal answers (cards, hands, buzzers)</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Lost City Trial Medal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a medal or badge that could be awarded to a team that performs well in today's History Station.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the medal design.</li><li>Include one symbol from this term's story so far (the logbook, the wooden elephant carving, a stone wall pattern, a bead).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular symbol to represent History knowledge?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of history content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you come to understand it so well — what helped it stick?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Scoreboard</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital scoreboard (spreadsheet or slide) to track team scores across all five Lost City Trial stations this week.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What information does a good scoreboard need to show clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Flight Log Entries</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back through your own Quest Log entries from Weeks 1–4 and note one thing you wrote that you'd improve on now, with what you know today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What does this tell you about how much you've learned this month?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: The Lost City Trial — Station 2: Farming & Water Challenge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FARMING &amp; WATER CHALLENGE — Testing Real Geography Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: demonstrate understanding of climate zones, land use, and human-environment interactions drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our memory of real Southern African geography from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are working under pressure, matching real knowledge rather than guesswork.</li><li>I can correctly match a farming type or water challenge to the correct climate zone or cause.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Geography content. Use results to identify which geographic concepts (climate zones, subsistence vs. commercial farming, water systems) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday, your knowledge of history was tested,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;Today, the logbook wants to know if you really understand this land itself — where things grow, and what happens when the water doesn't come.&quot; Nomvula adds: &quot;A real guide can't always show you. Sometimes you have to know, from memory alone, where things truly stand.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: of everything you've learned about farming and water this term, which fact do you think matters most to a family actually living here?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out the large regional floor/wall map used throughout the term, with climate zone areas (savanna, highveld, lowveld) marked but unlabelled.</li><li>Prepare a set of matching cards: farming types (subsistence, commercial), crops (maize, sorghum, groundnuts, tobacco, citrus, sugar), and water features (Zambezi River, Kariba Dam, Lake Kariba).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams take turns drawing a card and correctly placing/matching it to the right climate zone or water feature on the map, explaining their reasoning aloud (e.g. &quot;tobacco is a commercial crop, grown for export, so it needs reliable irrigation near a major water source&quot;).</li><li>Include at least one question testing why subsistence farming differs from commercial farming, and one testing what the Kariba Dam provides to the region and what happens to it in drought.</li><li>Key Question for discussion: &quot;Which was harder to remember — WHERE something grows, or WHY it grows there?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Briefly re-show correct placements on the map for anything placed incorrectly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which real geography fact from this term do you feel most confident about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Genesis promises that as long as the earth endures, seedtime and harvest will not cease — a rhythm God built into creation itself. Yet this month's trial has also shown how fragile that rhythm can feel when the rain doesn't come, and how much real work goes into farming this land well.</li><li>Verse: &quot;As long as the earth endures, seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, day and night will never cease.&quot; — <em>Genesis 8:22 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Understanding how farming and water genuinely connect a whole region helps us see that caring well for land and water is real Kingdom work, not just a school topic.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Genesis 8:22 promises seedtime and harvest will not cease — yet this term has shown droughts genuinely happen. How do both of those things fit together?</li><li>Which farming type — subsistence or commercial — do you think requires more trust that the rain will come, and why?</li><li>What is one way you could show care for water this week, knowing how much depends on it further downstream?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large regional floor/wall map (climate zones marked, unlabelled)</li><li>Matching cards: farming types, crops, water features</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw the Region from Memory</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Without looking at any reference, draw your best memory of Southern Africa's three climate zones and where each farming type belongs.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your drawing to a real map afterward — what did you get right, and what surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE piece of geography content from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did you remember it so clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Map Labelling Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple digital map tool or slide to label as many real locations, crops and water features from this term as you can recall, unaided.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How many could you label without checking back?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Term's Farming and Water Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own farming and water-saving work from Weeks 2 and 4. What's one detail you'd add now that you wouldn't have known back then?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: The Lost City Trial — Station 3: Build the Food Web":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">BUILD THE FOOD WEB — Testing Real Science Knowledge from Weeks 1–4</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: demonstrate understanding of adaptation, interdependence and food webs, drawn from prior learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of real savanna ecology from Weeks 1–4.</li><li>We are correctly linking predators, herbivores and plants using evidence from this term.</li><li>I can apply real ecological reasoning under pressure, not just recall isolated facts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Science content. Use results to identify which scientific concepts (plant adaptation, food webs, endangered species) may need revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tapiwa turns his wooden elephant carving over once in his pocket, then sets it down deliberately. &quot;A real ranger has to understand how everything out there is connected, crew — not just one animal at a time. Today, the logbook wants to see if you understand the whole web, not just a piece of it.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you removed just ONE part of a food web — one plant, one herbivore, one predator — how far do you think the effects would actually spread?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out connector cards or string and a set of savanna organism cards: baobab tree, grasses/thorn plants, herbivores (e.g. impala, zebra), predators (lion, leopard), and the rhino (endangered species).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams correctly link predators, herbivores, and plants into a real food web using connector cards or string, justifying each link with real content from this term (plant adaptation, predator-prey relationships, why rhino grazing shapes grassland).</li><li>Include at least one question testing why thorns and deep roots help savanna plants survive drought, and one testing why losing an endangered species like the rhino can affect a whole ecosystem, not just that species.</li><li>Award points for correct links AND for the quality of the scientific justification, even where teams reasoned slightly differently.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores. Discuss any link where teams disagreed — was there a genuinely defensible case for more than one connection?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what real science concept from this term would you most want a young ranger to understand before their first patrol?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Psalms describe God's wisdom on display in the sheer number and variety of living things He has made — each one fitted to its place, each one connected to the next. Building today's food web is a small window into just how carefully all of it fits together.</li><li>Verse: &quot;How many are your works, Lord! In wisdom you made them all; the earth is full of your creatures.&quot; — <em>Psalm 104:24 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Seeing how deeply every part of the savanna depends on every other part helps us love God's creation more carefully, not just admire one animal at a time.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 104 marvels at the sheer number of living things God made. Which connection in today's food web surprised you the most?</li><li>If removing one part of a food web affects far more than expected, what does that suggest about the ripple effects of our own choices — even small ones?</li><li>Tapiwa is learning to understand the whole web, not just one animal. Is there an area of your own life where you need to see the bigger picture, not just one piece of it?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Savanna organism cards (plants, herbivores, predators, rhino)</li><li>Connector cards or string for linking the food web</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate a Complete Food Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled savanna food web showing at least five organisms and the real connections between them.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which connection did you find hardest to draw accurately, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science concept from Weeks 1–4 you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it to someone who missed this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Food Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple digital diagram showing at least five real Southern African organisms and the connections between them, using this term's content.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did digital arrows make clearer than a hand-drawn version might?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Rhino Reflections</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own rhino conservation reflection from Week 4. Has anything you thought then changed after today's food web challenge?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: The Lost City Trial — Station 4: The Ranger's Workshop":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE RANGER'S WORKSHOP — Designing a Monitoring Solution (Consolidation Challenge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Practice: undertake planning to guide the development of an outcome, and use feedback to make ongoing decisions.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand how technological outcomes are evaluated, including by considering how well they meet the need they were designed for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to bring together everything we've learned about tracking, radios, camera traps and DNA tracing into one design.</li><li>We are learning to plan, build and defend a simple technology solution to a real conservation problem.</li><li>I can explain and justify my team's design choices using reasoning from the whole month's learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the design-and-pitch challenge, assessing use of prior learning (tracking, radios, camera traps, DNA tracing) and clarity of justification. Peer feedback recorded on pitch evaluation sheets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook flares open onto every leg of the route flown so far — Hwange, the Zambezi, the reserve under threat — stitched together like a map coming alive. &quot;This is it, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, steadier than the class has heard him all month. &quot;Sekuru Josiah always says a good pilot — or a good ranger — doesn't just have one tool. They know how to put them all together.&quot; Nomvula's voice crackles in: &quot;That's the pattern of it. Today, you build the plan.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you had to design ONE monitoring solution for a reserve, and could only use ideas from this month — tracking, radios, camera traps, DNA tracing — how would you combine them to cover the most ground?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Challenge Brief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Present the Boss Battle brief — teams are &quot;ranger technology consultants&quot; asked to design a simple monitoring plan for a fictional reserve, combining at least THREE of the four technologies studied this month (spoor-reading, radios, camera traps, DNA/notching).</li><li>Clarify success criteria: the design must explain what each technology is used for, why it was chosen over the alternatives, and how the technologies work together, not just alongside each other.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Design Time (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, students sketch and annotate their monitoring plan on a simple reserve map (provided), marking where each technology would be used and why.</li><li>Circulate and prompt teams with challenge questions drawn from the month: &quot;What happens at night, when your camera trap can't see colour?&quot; &quot;What if the radio signal drops out here?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Pitch and Peer Challenge (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Each team pitches their design to the class in under 2 minutes, then fields one challenge question from a rival team — mirroring how real conservation technology proposals are questioned and improved before funding.</li><li>Class votes (or teacher scores) on which design best combines the month's technologies with clear reasoning, not just the most technology crammed in.</li><li>Debrief: technology works best as a system, not a single silver-bullet tool — exactly the lesson Baba Farai lives by on patrol.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: which TWO technologies from this month worked best together in your design, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Colossians reminds us to give our whole heart to whatever we do, as if doing it for God Himself — not for the applause of the pitch, but because the work itself matters. Today's challenge is a chance to offer real effort toward something worth protecting.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart.&quot; — <em>Colossians 3:23 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Combining our best thinking to serve a real need — even a pretend reserve, standing in for a real one — is Manaaki in action: using our gifts fully, not half-heartedly.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Why do you think God cares about the EFFORT we put in, not just whether our idea &quot;wins&quot;?</li><li>Your team had to combine different tools to solve one problem. How is that like the way God gives different gifts to different people in a team, a class, or a church?</li><li>Tapiwa says a good ranger &quot;doesn't just have one tool.&quot; What are some different &quot;tools&quot; — skills, gifts, strengths — your own team brought to today's challenge?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank annotated reserve maps, one per team</li><li>Challenge question cards (one per rival team, for the pitch stage)</li><li>Simple pitch evaluation/peer-feedback sheets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How conservation technology works together</em> — search: &quot;wildlife conservation technology ranger tools combined&quot;. Reinforces the consolidation theme — that real conservation success usually comes from combining tools, not relying on one.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Your Monitoring Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a clear, labelled illustration of your team's final reserve design.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If you had to explain your design to someone in 10 seconds, what would you say first?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write the Pitch Script</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out your team's 2-minute pitch as a script, ready to present.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">150–200 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What's the strongest piece of reasoning in your pitch, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digital Reserve Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Recreate your team's monitoring plan digitally (Canva, Google Drawings, or similar), with labels for each technology used.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did turning your sketch digital make you reconsider?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Conservation Technology in Action</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on how real conservation teams combine different technologies.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;wildlife conservation technology ranger tools combined&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What combination of tools did you notice, and did it match your team's design?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: The Lost City Trial — Station 5: One Symbol, Three Traditions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">ONE SYMBOL, THREE TRADITIONS — A Consolidation Symbol-Design Challenge</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Communicating and Interpreting: understand and use symbolic elements from more than one tradition to communicate a single intentional idea.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others; Participating &amp; Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are bringing together San storytelling symbols, Ndebele geometric patterns, and beadwork colour-meaning into one combined design.</li><li>We are working as a team to agree on what our symbol should communicate before designing it.</li><li>I can explain how each part of my finished symbol draws on a real tradition studied this term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for Weeks 1–4 Art content. Review finished symbols and team explanations for genuine, respectful use of San, Ndebele and beadwork traditions studied this term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Nomvula holds up her half-finished beadwork bracelet — one bead further along than last week. &quot;My gogo says every good pattern points back to something bigger than itself. Today's the last station before the logbook decides if we've earned the right to cross the border. One symbol, crew — but it has to carry three traditions inside it, properly, not just mixed together.&quot; Tapiwa grins, steadier than he's sounded all week: &quot;Sharp, crew. Let's finish this properly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you had to combine three real art traditions into ONE symbol, which part of each would you keep, and which would you have to simplify?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Recap: Three Real Traditions (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Briefly recap San rock art as storytelling symbols (Week 1), Ndebele geometric wall painting as a shared visual language (Week 3), and beadwork colour symbolism as an agreed community &quot;code&quot; (Week 4).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What do all three of these traditions have in common, even though they look completely different?&quot; (Guide toward: each uses shared, understood symbols to communicate real meaning, not just decoration.)</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Agreeing on the Message (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, agree on ONE message the combined symbol should communicate (e.g. &quot;we protect this land together,&quot; &quot;the story continues,&quot; &quot;small pieces make something bigger&quot;).</li><li>Teams briefly justify why they chose that message over other options.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Design the Combined Symbol (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: Teams design one symbol that genuinely draws on a storytelling shape or figure inspired by San rock art, a geometric pattern element inspired by Ndebele wall painting, and a colour choice with real meaning from the class's beadwork colour code (Week 4).</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;Which part of your symbol is doing the storytelling, which part is doing the pattern, and which part is doing the colour-meaning?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what your team's combined symbol communicates, and name the three traditions inside it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Bezalel was the craftsman God specifically filled with skill to create beautiful, meaningful work for the tabernacle — a reminder that combining real skill and real meaning into one finished piece has always mattered to God, not just to art class.</li><li>Verse: &quot;I have filled him with the Spirit of God, with skill, ability and knowledge in all kinds of crafts.&quot; — <em>Exodus 35:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (Self/Others) — Working as a team to combine three real traditions into one honest, meaningful symbol is a way of loving both the people who created those traditions and the teammates working alongside us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Exodus says God filled Bezalel with skill for a purpose bigger than himself. What's one skill God has given YOU that could serve something bigger than just yourself?</li><li>Your team had to agree on one message before designing. Was that easier or harder than designing alone — and why?</li><li>Looking back at Weeks 1–4 together, which of the three traditions in today's symbol did you personally find most meaningful, and why?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reference images/examples of San rock art, Ndebele wall painting, and the class's agreed beadwork colour code (from Week 4)</li><li>Paper, pencils and colouring materials for the combined symbol design</li><li>A visible scoreboard (if scoring this station alongside the others)</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend Into a Team Banner</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Combine every team's finished symbol into one shared class banner or display.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Looking at every team's symbol together, what pattern or theme keeps showing up across the whole class?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Art MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE art tradition from Weeks 1–4 you enjoyed learning about most, and why.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made that tradition stick with you more than the others?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Digitise Your Symbol</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Recreate your team's combined symbol using a simple digital drawing tool, keeping all three traditions visible.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to translate digitally — the storytelling shape, the pattern, or the colour meaning?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Trial</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back across all five Lost City Trial stations this week. Which station are you proudest of your team's performance in, and why?</p></div></div>"}}},"Week 6":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 21: New Arrivals, New Boundaries":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">NEW ARRIVALS, NEW BOUNDARIES — Colonisation and Its Consequences</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: understand how the arrival of new groups of people can change land ownership, power, and daily life for those already living there.</li><li>Understanding the Past: investigate how the perspectives of people in the past differ, based on evidence available about their lives and choices.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what colonisation was, and that European settlers and the British South Africa Company changed who owned land in this region and how.</li><li>We are learning to look at this history honestly, focusing on what people DID in response, not only on what happened to them.</li><li>I can name at least two real ways communities responded to land and power changing hands.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion and resilience list; review of &quot;before and after&quot; sketches for engagement with the scale of the change, handled with age-appropriate care.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The route crosses into South Africa for the first time — bigger cities, a different rhythm. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, a little out of his depth for once. &quot;This part's not really mine to tell. Nomvula, over to you.&quot; Nomvula, careful with her words: &quot;European settlers arrived here, and the British South Africa Company after them — and it changed everything about who owned this land, and how. It's a heavy story. I want to tell it honestly, but through what people DID next, not just what happened to them.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: when something as basic as land ownership changes suddenly and unfairly, what do you think would matter most to a community trying to hold onto who they are?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Changed, and How (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain simply that from the late 1800s, European settlers arrived in the region, and organisations such as the British South Africa Company gained control over large areas of land, changing who owned and controlled land that communities had lived on for generations.</li><li>Keep the explanation focused on the real, factual consequence — land and power changing hands — rather than graphic detail, in line with an honest but age-appropriate telling.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might controlling land be one of the very first things a new, powerful group tries to change?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What People Did Next (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a class, discuss what choices remained available to communities affected by this sudden change — for example, continuing to pass on language, stories, farming knowledge and art (many of which the class has already studied this term), organising and resisting where possible, and rebuilding community life under new, harder conditions.</li><li>List real examples of resilience discussed by the class, not only examples of loss, keeping the focus on what people actively did.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of the things we've learned about this term — the San's stories, Great Zimbabwe's walls, Ndebele patterns, beadwork meaning — do you think became even MORE important to hold onto during a hard time like this?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE real way a community can hold onto its identity even when land or power is taken from it unfairly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Micah doesn't ask people to pretend hard history didn't happen — he asks what faithfulness looks like in response to it: justice, mercy, and humility. Today's honest history lesson is a real place to practise exactly that kind of response.</li><li>Verse: &quot;And what does the Lord require of you? To act justly and to love mercy and to walk humbly with your God.&quot; — <em>Micah 6:8 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing this part of history honestly, and asking what justice, mercy and humility look like in response to it, helps us hold hard truths without either ignoring them or losing hope.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Micah 6:8 asks for justice AND mercy AND humility together. Why might it be easy to focus on just one of those three, and miss the others?</li><li>Nomvula chose to tell this story honestly, but through what people DID, not just what happened to them. Why might that choice matter?</li><li>Is there a hard truth in your own life or community that's easier to avoid than to face honestly? What would &quot;walking humbly&quot; through it actually look like?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple regional map showing land use/ownership before and after this period</li><li>A running list (board or poster) for the &quot;what people did&quot; discussion</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Colonisation in Southern Africa, an honest overview</em> — search: &quot;colonisation Southern Africa history explained for kids&quot;. Gives students an age-appropriate, honest overview before the class discussion, focused on consequence rather than graphic detail.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw Before and After</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple &quot;before and after&quot; pair of sketches showing land use changing over time in one imagined community.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the &quot;before&quot; scene, showing a community using their land freely.</li><li>Sketch the &quot;after&quot; scene, showing the same land under new control.</li><li>Add one small detail in the &quot;after&quot; scene showing the community still holding onto something of their own.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why did you choose that particular detail to include as a sign of resilience?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter That Wasn't Sent</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter in role as a young person from this time, describing one thing they refused to give up, even as land and power changed hands.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose as the &quot;one thing,&quot; and why did you choose that over anything else?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Timeline Slide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple document or slide tool, build a short timeline showing land use before, during, and after this period of change.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which point on your timeline do you think mattered most to the people actually living through it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Colonisation, Honestly Told</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining colonisation in this region at an age-appropriate level.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;colonisation Southern Africa history explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that you hadn't known before today?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 22: Whose Land, Whose Food":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHOSE LAND, WHOSE FOOD — Land Use Change and Food Security</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how changes in land ownership and use affect a community's ability to feed itself, both historically and today.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that land use change historically affected who could farm for themselves, and that this still shapes food security today.</li><li>We are learning what a community genuinely needs — land, water, seeds, knowledge — to reliably feed itself.</li><li>I can explain what happens to a community's food security when even one of those needs is taken away.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group discussion; review of food security checklist posters for clarity and genuine understanding of what communities need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;When land use changes hands, food security changes with it,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;Families who once farmed for themselves sometimes lost the ability to. It's not just a history fact — it still shapes who has enough to eat today. Let's look at it properly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you suddenly lost access to the land your family used to grow food on, what do you think would need to happen for your family to feel food-secure again?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">From Land Change to Food Change (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that when land use and ownership changed during this period, many families who had farmed for their own food (subsistence farming, Week 2) lost access to land they had relied on, which directly affected their ability to feed themselves.</li><li>Explain that this history still shapes food security in the region today — where people can farm, what they can grow, and how reliably they can feed their own families.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might losing farmland affect a family more seriously and more immediately than losing almost anything else?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Does a Community Need to Feed Itself? (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students discuss and list what a community genuinely needs to feed itself reliably — land, water, seeds/crops, and knowledge (farming skill, passed down like Tapiwa's family's own maize and tobacco plot) — then discuss what happens if even ONE of those is taken away.</li><li>Groups share their list and their strongest example of what happens when one need is missing.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these four needs — land, water, seeds, knowledge — do you think is hardest to get back once it's lost?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Fairness, Not Just Farming Skill (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: draw the discussion together — food security isn't only about farming skill or effort; fair access to land and water matters just as much, connecting today's geography directly to Monday's history.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;How is today's lesson connected to what we learned on Monday, even though one was history and one is geography?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing, besides farming skill, that a community needs to be truly food-secure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Leviticus reminds Israel that the land itself ultimately belongs to God, and that people are only ever stewards and sojourners on it, not final owners. That truth cuts both ways — it's a caution against ever treating land as something to be seized unfairly, and a comfort to anyone who has had land taken from them.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The land must not be sold permanently, because the land is mine and you reside in my land as foreigners and strangers.&quot; — <em>Leviticus 25:23 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Understanding that land ultimately belongs to God, not to any one person or company, helps us see fair access to land and food as a real matter of justice, not just economics.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Leviticus says the land is God's, and people are &quot;foreigners and strangers&quot; on it, even in their own home. How does that change how you think about who has the &quot;right&quot; to land?</li><li>Nomvula connects today's geography straight back to yesterday's history. Why do land and food security keep showing up together throughout the Bible and throughout this term?</li><li>What is one way you could support fair access to food for others, even in a small way, this week?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank poster template for the food security checklist</li><li>Recap notes/images from Week 2's subsistence farming lesson</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Food security explained simply</em> — search: &quot;what is food security explained for kids&quot;. Gives students a clear, simple grounding in the concept before connecting it to the region's history.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Food Security Checklist Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple poster listing what a community needs to feed itself well.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>List the four key needs discussed in class (land, water, seeds, knowledge).</li><li>Sketch one small icon for each.</li><li>Add a short title summing up the whole poster's message.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which of the four icons was hardest to represent visually, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Family's Food Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story in role as a family whose access to farmland changed suddenly, describing how they adapted.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did your family choose to do first when their situation changed?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Needs Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple slide or drawing tool, build a diagram showing the four things a community needs for food security, and what happens if one is removed.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which connection in your diagram surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Food Security</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining food security.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;what is food security explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that connects to this week's history lesson?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 23: The Animal That Shapes the Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE ANIMAL THAT SHAPES THE LAND — Elephants as a Keystone Species</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand that some species have a disproportionately large effect on their ecosystem relative to their numbers, and that removing them changes the whole system.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a &quot;keystone species&quot; is, and that elephants are a real example in the savanna.</li><li>We are learning specific ways elephant herds physically shape the landscape for countless other species.</li><li>I can predict what would happen to a savanna landscape if elephants disappeared from it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group discussion and prediction task; review of &quot;before and after&quot; landscape sketches for genuine ecological reasoning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Elephants aren't just big, crew — they're what scientists call a keystone species. They knock down trees, dig waterholes, spread seeds for kilometres. Take elephants away, and the whole savanna changes shape. I want you to see just how much weight rests on one kind of animal.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: can you think of one small, everyday action YOU take that quietly shapes the space around you more than you'd expect, the way an elephant shapes a whole savanna?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Keystone Species? (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that a &quot;keystone species&quot; is one whose impact on its ecosystem is far larger than its numbers alone would suggest — remove it, and the whole system changes shape, the way removing a keystone from an arch would collapse the whole structure.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think this idea is called a 'keystone' species, borrowing a word from building an arch?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How Elephants Shape the Land (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: present real examples of how elephant herds shape the savanna — knocking down trees (which opens space for grasses and smaller plants), digging waterholes that other species rely on, and spreading seeds across kilometres through their dung, helping plants grow in new places.</li><li>Activity: in groups, students list three real ways an elephant herd changes its landscape, then predict what would happen to each of those changes if the herd disappeared.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these three effects do you think would be missed FASTEST by other animals if elephants vanished?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Connecting the Whole System (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Discuss briefly as a class: how does today's keystone species idea connect to the food web work from Week 5's Lost City Trial?</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE real way an elephant herd shapes the land around it, beyond just eating plants.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 50 pictures God declaring ownership of every wild animal, &quot;the cattle on a thousand hills&quot; — a reminder that elephants and every other creature already belong to God before they belong to any ecosystem study. Recognising their outsized importance is really recognising the intricate care God built into creation.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For every animal of the forest is mine, and the cattle on a thousand hills.&quot; — <em>Psalm 50:10 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Understanding just how much weight rests on one keystone species helps us love and care for creation with real knowledge, not just general good feeling.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 50 says every animal already belongs to God. How does that change the way you think about protecting a keystone species like the elephant?</li><li>If one animal can shape an entire landscape just by living its ordinary life, what does that suggest about the importance of &quot;ordinary&quot; things in general?</li><li>What is one part of God's creation near you — even something small — that you think might be doing more important work than people usually notice?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or video of elephant herds shaping savanna landscape (waterholes, felled trees, seed dispersal)</li><li>Blank paper for &quot;before and after&quot; landscape sketches</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Elephants as a keystone species</em> — search: &quot;elephants keystone species savanna explained&quot;. Gives students a clear visual introduction to how elephants physically shape their environment before the group discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Before-and-After Savanna</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a &quot;before and after&quot; savanna landscape showing the difference elephants make.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a savanna WITH elephants present, showing waterholes and open spaces.</li><li>Sketch the SAME savanna without elephants, showing how it might change over time.</li><li>Label at least two differences between the two scenes.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which difference between your two scenes do you think would happen fastest?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Keystone Species Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short factual report explaining what a keystone species is and why elephants qualify as one.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is the clearest piece of evidence you used to support your explanation?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Cause-and-Effect Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a diagram showing at least three effects elephants have on their landscape, and what happens if each is removed.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which cause-and-effect chain in your diagram surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Elephants Shaping the Savanna</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on elephants as a keystone species.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;elephants keystone species savanna explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact from the video you hadn't already listed in class?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 24: Following From the Sky":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FOLLOWING FROM THE SKY — GPS Collars and Elephant Movement</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Systems: understand that a technological system's outputs can be used as inputs into another system, such as a collar feeding data into a monitoring map.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technology can help balance the needs of people and the needs of wildlife.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a GPS satellite collar works and what data it actually collects.</li><li>We are learning how tracking elephant movement can help prevent conflict between elephants and nearby farms and villages.</li><li>I can interpret a simple set of GPS movement data and explain what it might mean.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the data-interpretation activity and discussion; review of written explanations linking GPS data to real decision-making.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The route crosses into South Africa for the first time, and for once it's Nomvula who's quiet, taking in a bigger, busier world than her village. Tapiwa fills the silence, predictably: &quot;Sharp, crew — you know what doesn't get lost, even out here? A collared elephant. Not with the tech my dad's team uses.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: elephants can walk 50 kilometres or more in a single day. How could researchers possibly know exactly where a whole herd has been, without following it on foot the entire way?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">How a GPS Collar Works (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A GPS (Global Positioning System) collar contains a receiver that picks up signals from satellites orbiting Earth, calculates its own position from those signals, and stores or transmits that location at set intervals — building up a trail of exactly where the animal has been.</li><li>Explain simply: several satellite signals are needed together to work out one precise position — students don't need the maths, just the idea that multiple signals combine to pinpoint one spot.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might researchers want a location every hour, rather than just once a day?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reading Real Movement Data (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In groups, students are given a simplified table or map of GPS &quot;ping&quot; points for a fictional elephant herd over one week, and must plot the route and describe the pattern (e.g., moving toward water each evening, avoiding a particular area during the day).</li><li>Discuss: what could explain a sudden change in the pattern (finding water, avoiding a threat, moving toward farmland)?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">From Data to Decisions: Preventing Conflict (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain that when GPS data shows a herd heading toward farmland or a village, rangers and conservation teams can sometimes warn communities in advance — giving both people and elephants a chance to avoid a dangerous, costly encounter.</li><li>Discuss: this is technology serving BOTH people and animals at once, not choosing one over the other — a theme the class will meet again next week.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why is preventing a conflict usually better than only reacting after it happens?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is ONE piece of information a GPS collar gives researchers, and how could it help prevent a problem before it happens?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 139 describes a God who already knows every place we could ever go, before we go there. A GPS collar can only track where an elephant has been — but God's knowledge of where we are, and where we're headed, is complete and unending.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Where can I go from your Spirit? Where can I flee from your presence?&quot; — <em>Psalm 139:7 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Tracking technology helps us know an animal's path in part; knowing that God already knows the whole of every path — ours included — is a much bigger comfort.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>A GPS collar only knows where an elephant has already been. Psalm 139 says God knows where we're going before we even get there. How does that make you feel?</li><li>Why do you think it matters to use tracking technology to help BOTH elephants and the people living nearby, rather than choosing a side?</li><li>Is there somewhere you've felt like nobody knew where you were or what you were going through? What does it mean that God always does?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simplified GPS &quot;ping&quot; data tables/maps, one set per group</li><li>Southern Africa outline map for plotting movement</li><li>Coloured pencils/markers for tracing routes</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How GPS satellite collars track wildlife</em> — search: &quot;GPS tracking collar elephants how it works&quot;. Explains the real technology behind today's lesson in an age-appropriate way.</li><li><em>Elephants and human-wildlife conflict solutions</em> — search: &quot;elephant tracking preventing human wildlife conflict Africa&quot;. Connects today's data lesson to the real-world purpose — protecting both people and elephants.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Map an Elephant's Week</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw and label a simple map showing an imagined elephant herd's movement over one week, based on today's data patterns.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What real need (water, food, safety) explains each part of your route?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Researcher's Data Summary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short summary, in role as a researcher, explaining what a week of GPS data revealed about a herd's movement and what action (if any) it suggests.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What decision did your data lead you to recommend?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Plot Data Digitally</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple spreadsheet or drawing tool to plot the week's GPS points and connect them into a route.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was easier to see once the data was visual instead of just numbers?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: GPS Collars in Action</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on GPS tracking collars and elephant movement.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;GPS tracking collar elephants how it works&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the technology did you not expect?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 25: A Wall Big Enough for a Whole Story":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A WALL BIG ENOUGH FOR A WHOLE STORY — Township Art and Mural Storytelling</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Communicating and Interpreting: understand how communities use shared public art to communicate collective stories and messages.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that township communities in South Africa use large public murals to tell their own stories, in public, for everyone to see.</li><li>We are learning to design one section of a shared mural that connects to a single agreed class theme.</li><li>I can explain what message my section of a collaborative mural communicates.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the theme-agreement discussion and collaborative design process; review of finished mural sections and written explanations for genuine connection to the shared theme.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;In townships across South Africa, whole walls become murals — communities telling their own stories in public, for everyone to see, not hidden away,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;Today, imagine a wall wide enough for a whole story, and design what you'd paint on it.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if your whole class had ONE wall to tell the world something true about your school or community, what do you think it should say?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Murals as Public, Collective Storytelling (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that in many townships across South Africa, communities paint large public murals — on walls, buildings, and community spaces — to tell their own stories, celebrate identity, and communicate messages that matter to everyone in that community, rather than keeping art private.</li><li>Explain that, unlike a single artist's individual piece, a mural is often collaborative — many people contribute sections that connect into one larger, shared story.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a community choose a huge public wall to tell a story, instead of something smaller and more private?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Agreeing on One Shared Theme (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a group (or whole class), agree on ONE shared theme for the mural before anyone starts painting their individual section, so that every section connects into a single story rather than several unrelated ones.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What would happen to a mural if everyone painted their own theme instead of agreeing on one first?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Mural Section Design (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: on a long shared sheet of paper (or joined individual sheets), each student or pair designs one section of a community mural inspired by township art and mural storytelling — a scene, symbol, or message the community would want everyone passing by to see, connected to the agreed theme.</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;How does your section connect visually or thematically to the sections next to it?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what story your section of the mural tells, and how it connects to the whole class's theme.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Isaiah pictures God turning ashes into something beautiful — a crown of beauty in place of grief. After a genuinely heavy week of history, a mural is exactly that kind of response: taking a real, sometimes difficult community story and turning it into something public, beautiful, and shared.</li><li>Verse: &quot;To bestow on them a crown of beauty instead of ashes, the oil of joy instead of mourning.&quot; — <em>Isaiah 61:3 (NIV, excerpt)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (Others) — Choosing to turn a community's story — even a hard one — into something beautiful and shared is a real act of love for the people who will see it and recognise themselves in it.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Isaiah pictures beauty coming out of genuinely hard things, not instead of them. Why do you think that's different from just ignoring the hard part?</li><li>This week's history was heavy. Why might creating something beautiful together, like a mural, be an especially fitting way to close a week like this one?</li><li>Whose story would YOU want to help tell on a shared class or community wall, and why?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of real township murals from South Africa (age-appropriate examples)</li><li>A long shared sheet of paper, or individual sheets that can be joined together</li><li>Paint, markers, or colouring materials</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Township mural art in South Africa</em> — search: &quot;South African township mural art storytelling&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in the real tradition before designing their own mural section.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Extend the Mural</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a second, smaller mural section that could be added to the class mural, using the same agreed theme.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you keep the same from the first design, and what did you change?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Mural's Message</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, what your class's whole mural communicates and why the class chose that theme.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Do you think someone walking past your mural, who didn't know the class, would understand its message?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Mural Mockup</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, create a digital mockup of how the whole class mural could look joined together.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was harder to plan digitally — the individual sections, or how they connect?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Township Mural Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about township mural art and storytelling.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;South African township mural art storytelling&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one message or story from a real mural that stood out to you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 26: I Am, Because We Are":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I AM, BECAUSE WE ARE — Apartheid and the Struggle for Freedom</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding How Societies Work: understand that societies can build unjust systems, and that people can respond to injustice with courage, unity and reconciliation.</li><li>Understanding the Past: investigate a significant struggle for freedom, and the values that carried people through it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what apartheid was, and that it was an unjust system that separated and disadvantaged people because of race.</li><li>We are learning about the struggle for freedom, Nelson Mandela, and the idea of Ubuntu — &quot;I am, because we are.&quot;</li><li>I can explain, in my own words, what Ubuntu means and give one example of courage or unity from this history.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the Ubuntu discussion and class contribution; review of symbol designs for genuine engagement with the idea of unity between people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The route lands in Nomvula's own village at last. Her voice is proud, but there's something careful underneath it too. &quot;This is my home, crew,&quot; she says. &quot;And this next part of the story is my family's, properly. Apartheid, the struggle for freedom, Nelson Mandela — it's hard history. But it's also a story about an idea called Ubuntu: 'I am, because we are.' Courage and unity got my grandmother's generation through it. I want you to understand the courage, not just the hardship.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think &quot;I am, because we are&quot; might mean — and can a person really only become fully themselves through other people?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">An Unjust System (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain simply that apartheid was a system in South Africa that legally separated people and gave them very different rights and opportunities based on race, and that it was deeply unjust — most South Africans were denied basic freedoms because of it.</li><li>Keep the explanation focused on fact and consequence, in line with an honest but age-appropriate telling, without graphic detail.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think it matters that we call this system 'unjust,' and not just 'different' or 'unfair'?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Struggle for Freedom, and Ubuntu (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce Nelson Mandela and the long struggle for freedom against apartheid, and the idea of Ubuntu — a philosophy meaning roughly &quot;I am, because we are,&quot; which held that people find their humanity through their connection to others, and shaped how the struggle for freedom and, eventually, reconciliation were approached.</li><li>Activity: as a class, discuss real examples of courage and unity from this history — people organising together, refusing to give up, and eventually working toward reconciliation rather than revenge.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;How might believing 'I am, because we are' change the way a whole movement chooses to fight for freedom?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence, in your own words, explaining what &quot;I am, because we are&quot; means.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Paul pictures the church as one body with many parts, each needing the others to function — a Christian echo of the very idea Ubuntu holds. Nomvula's family's history shows real people living out that truth under real pressure: that we are never fully ourselves alone.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Just as a body, though one, has many parts, but all its many parts form one body, so it is with Christ.&quot; — <em>1 Corinthians 12:12 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (Others) — Understanding Ubuntu helps us love others the way God designed people to need each other — not as an inconvenience, but as part of what it means to be fully human.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says the body needs ALL its parts, not just some. How is that similar to what Ubuntu teaches?</li><li>Nomvula says she wants the class to understand the courage of this history, not just the hardship. Why might focusing on courage matter?</li><li>Who is someone in your own life who helps you &quot;be yourself&quot; more fully, the way Ubuntu describes?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple, age-appropriate timeline or picture book covering the struggle for freedom and Nelson Mandela</li><li>Board or poster space for the Ubuntu discussion</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Ubuntu and Nelson Mandela, explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;Ubuntu Nelson Mandela South Africa explained for kids&quot;. Gives students an age-appropriate, honest overview of Ubuntu and the struggle for freedom before the class discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design an Ubuntu Symbol</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple symbol representing Ubuntu — unity between people — for the class to display.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a symbol showing two or more figures connected or supporting each other.</li><li>Choose colours that feel warm and unified rather than divided.</li><li>Add one word or short phrase capturing the idea.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of your symbol shows connection, not just individual people?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: What Ubuntu Means to Me</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece explaining, in your own words, what &quot;I am, because we are&quot; means and give one real example from your own life.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Was it easy or hard to think of a real example? Why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build an Ubuntu Mind Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a mind map connecting the idea of Ubuntu to at least three people who help make you who you are.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which connection on your mind map surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Ubuntu Explained</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining Ubuntu and Nelson Mandela's role in the struggle for freedom.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Ubuntu Nelson Mandela South Africa explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that you hadn't known before today?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 27: More Than Just Cattle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">MORE THAN JUST CATTLE — Nguni Cattle Culture and Livestock Farming</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand that livestock farming can carry deep cultural significance far beyond its economic or food value.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Nguni cattle are central to livestock farming in this region, and that they carry deep cultural meaning beyond farming.</li><li>We are learning what status, lobola (dowry) and identity mean in relation to cattle in Southern African culture.</li><li>I can compare something valuable in my own culture to what cattle represent here.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the pairs discussion; review of hide pattern designs for genuine engagement with cattle as more than an economic resource.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Nguni cattle aren't just farm animals here,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;They're status, they're dowry, they're identity. My uncle's herd means something far bigger than the beef or milk it provides. I want to show you a kind of farming that's really about culture as much as food.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: can you think of something your own family owns or keeps that means far more than its practical use?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Livestock Farming, Nguni Style (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that Nguni cattle are a hardy, distinctively patterned breed central to livestock farming across parts of Southern Africa, valued for milk, meat and hide as well as their resilience to the local climate.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a hardy, adaptable breed of cattle matter especially in a region with unpredictable rainfall?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Cattle as Status, Dowry and Identity (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that beyond farming, cattle carry deep cultural significance in many Southern African communities — the size of a family's herd can reflect status, cattle are traditionally part of lobola (a dowry exchanged between families at marriage), and individual cattle patterns can even be recognised and named, becoming part of family identity.</li><li>Activity: in pairs, students discuss something valuable in their own family or culture that means more than its practical use, and compare it to what cattle represent here.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What's the difference between owning something because it's USEFUL and owning something because of what it MEANS?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing Nguni cattle represent in this culture, beyond food or farming.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Proverbs urges a wise person to know the condition of their flocks well — a call to attentive, careful stewardship of what's been entrusted to us. Nguni cattle, known individually by pattern and name, are a living example of exactly that kind of careful attention.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Be sure you know the condition of your flocks, give careful attention to your herds.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 27:23 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Caring attentively for what we've been given — whether a herd of cattle or something else entirely — is a real, practical way of serving faithfully with what God has entrusted to us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs calls for &quot;careful attention,&quot; not just ownership. What's the difference between owning something and truly caring for it?</li><li>Nomvula says cattle carry identity, not just economic value. What is something YOU care for that carries meaning beyond its usefulness?</li><li>How might &quot;giving careful attention&quot; to what God has given you look different from just using it up?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of Nguni cattle showing their distinctive hide patterns</li><li>Blank paper or card for hide pattern designs</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Nguni cattle and their cultural significance</em> — search: &quot;Nguni cattle culture South Africa explained&quot;. Gives students a clear, respectful grounding in the real cultural significance of Nguni cattle before the pairs discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Nguni-Style Hide Pattern</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple Nguni-style hide pattern, choosing your own colours and shapes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the outline of a cattle hide shape.</li><li>Fill it with a bold, distinctive pattern of your own design.</li><li>Give your pattern a name, the way real Nguni patterns are often named and recognised.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose that particular pattern and name?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Family Herd Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece in role as a member of a family describing their herd and what it means to them, beyond its economic value.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to emphasise — status, identity, or something else?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a chart comparing what cattle represent here to something valuable in your own culture.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about the comparison?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Nguni Cattle Culture</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Nguni cattle and their cultural significance.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Nguni cattle culture South Africa explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact from the video you hadn't already discussed in class?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 28: The Small Workers of the Fields":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE SMALL WORKERS OF THE FIELDS — Sunbirds, Weaver Birds and Pollination</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand the essential role pollinators play in plant reproduction, and that many pollinators are small and easily overlooked.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what pollinators are, and that sunbirds and weaver birds are important pollinators in this region.</li><li>We are learning that small, easily overlooked animals can play an essential role in an ecosystem.</li><li>I can name at least one plant or crop that depends on pollinators and explain what would happen without them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group discussion; review of labelled sunbird/weaver bird drawings for genuine understanding of the pollination process.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Sunbirds, weaver birds — small, easy to overlook,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;But without them, so many plants around my grandmother's fields wouldn't reproduce at all. I want you to notice the small, quiet workers today, not just the big dramatic animals.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: can you think of something small in your own life that does far more important work than people usually notice?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Pollinator? (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that pollination is the process by which pollen moves from one part of a flower to another (or between flowers), allowing plants to reproduce and produce seeds and fruit, and that animals like birds and insects that carry this pollen are called pollinators.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a plant NEED help from an animal, instead of pollinating itself?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sunbirds, Weaver Birds and Their Job (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce sunbirds and weaver birds as real, important pollinators in Southern African landscapes — sunbirds feed on nectar and carry pollen between flowers on their beaks and heads as they feed, while weaver birds contribute to plant reproduction and seed dispersal as they forage and build their distinctive woven nests.</li><li>Activity: in groups, students list three plants or crops (including some studied earlier this term) that would struggle without pollinators, and discuss what would happen to the wider food web without them.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If pollinators disappeared, what is the very FIRST thing you think would happen, before anything else?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE plant or crop that depends on pollinators, and what would happen to it without them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus points to the birds of the air as proof that God notices and provides for even the smallest, most overlooked creatures. Sunbirds and weaver birds — easy to miss, essential to the whole savanna — are a living picture of exactly that kind of quiet, faithful provision.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Look at the birds of the air; they do not sow or reap or store away in barns, and yet your heavenly Father feeds them.&quot; — <em>Matthew 6:26 (NIV, excerpt)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Noticing the essential work of small, easily overlooked creatures helps us love and pay attention to the whole of God's creation, not just the parts that are big or dramatic.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jesus uses ordinary birds to make a point about God's care. Why do you think he chose something so small and ordinary?</li><li>Nomvula asks you to notice the &quot;small, quiet workers&quot; today. What is something small in creation you've never really noticed before?</li><li>If God pays attention to something as small as a sunbird, what might that tell you about how God pays attention to you?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of sunbirds and weaver birds, including weaver nests</li><li>Blank paper for labelled bird drawings</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Sunbirds and weaver birds as pollinators</em> — search: &quot;sunbirds weaver birds pollination Africa explained&quot;. Gives students a clear visual introduction to how these specific birds pollinate before the group discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Sunbird or Weaver Bird Visiting a Flower</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a sunbird or weaver bird visiting a flower, and label how it helps the plant.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch the bird feeding at or near a flower.</li><li>Add arrows or labels showing where pollen would transfer.</li><li>Add one caption explaining the bird's role in one sentence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was the trickiest part of showing HOW pollination happens, rather than just what the bird looks like?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Day in the Life of a Pollinator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short story from the point of view of a sunbird or weaver bird, describing a day spent pollinating flowers.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail did you include to show the bird doing essential, if unnoticed, work?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Pollination Food Web Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a diagram showing how pollinators connect to at least three other parts of the savanna food web.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which connection in your diagram was the least obvious before today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Pollinators at Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about sunbirds, weaver birds and pollination.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;sunbirds weaver birds pollination Africa explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact from the video that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 29: The Buzz That Builds Peace":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE BUZZ THAT BUILDS PEACE — Beehive Fences and Elephant-Safe Farming</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Nature of Technology: understand that a good technological solution often solves more than one problem at once, and considers the needs of multiple groups.</li><li>Technological Practice: understand the value of a simple, low-cost solution over a complex one, when it meets the need effectively.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a beehive fence works and why elephants naturally avoid bees.</li><li>We are learning that a genuinely clever technology solution doesn't have to be expensive or complicated to be effective.</li><li>I can explain how one invention can benefit two different groups (farmers and elephants) at the same time.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the design-reasoning discussion and diagram activity; review of written explanations linking the invention to real needs on both sides.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The crew lands in Nomvula's village, where the air hums — literally — near the edge of Gogo Thandiwe's fields. &quot;Listen,&quot; Nomvula says, pride creeping into her voice for the first time in a while. &quot;That's not just bees. That's the fence that keeps my grandmother's crops safe, and the elephants too.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: elephants are some of the strongest animals on land — strong enough to push through almost any ordinary fence. So what could possibly stop one, without hurting it?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Problem: Elephants and Farms in the Same Space (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: As farmland and elephant territory increasingly overlap across parts of Africa, elephants sometimes raid crops — a real problem for family food security, and one that can lead to elephants being harmed in response. A genuinely useful solution needs to protect crops WITHOUT harming elephants.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If a fence needs to be strong enough to stop an elephant but not hurt it, what problem does that create for a fence-builder?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Beehive Fence Solution (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Explain the real invention — beehives are hung on wires strung between posts around a field. If an elephant brushes the wire, the hives swing and disturb the bees, which the elephant hears and smells and instinctively avoids, since bees can sting sensitive areas like the trunk and around the eyes.</li><li>Explain why this works so well: it uses the elephant's OWN natural instinct rather than force, is far cheaper than an electric fence, and gives farmers a bonus — honey to sell or use, alongside protecting their crops.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a solution that works WITH an animal's natural behaviour be better than one that tries to overpower it?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Design Reasoning Challenge (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In pairs, students list every group who benefits from a beehive fence (farmers, elephants, bees/pollinators, the wider community) and how each one benefits — building toward the idea of a genuinely &quot;win-win&quot; technology.</li><li>Discuss: this connects directly to this week's pollinators lesson — the bees themselves are doing double duty, protecting the field AND pollinating it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: explain, in your own words, why a beehive fence protects elephants as much as it protects the farm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 8 marvels that God gave people real responsibility over creation — not to dominate it carelessly, but to care for it wisely. A beehive fence is a small, beautiful picture of that kind of care: clever enough to protect a family's crops without harming the very creature it's keeping out.</li><li>Verse: &quot;You made him ruler over the works of your hands.&quot; — <em>Psalm 8:6 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha (World) — Loving the world God made sometimes looks like finding a clever solution that protects people AND protects animals, instead of assuming it always has to be one or the other.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 8 says God gave people responsibility over creation. What kind of responsibility do you think that is — control, or care? What's the difference?</li><li>The beehive fence protects Gogo Thandiwe's crops without harming the elephants outside them. Can you think of another situation where two different needs could both be met, if someone thought creatively?</li><li>What's one clever, low-cost way YOU could solve a problem this week, instead of reaching for the most expensive option first?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Diagram of a beehive fence (posts, wire, hanging hives) around a field</li><li>Simple &quot;who benefits&quot; worksheet for the design reasoning activity</li><li>Reference photos of real beehive fence projects, if available</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How beehive fences protect farms from elephants</em> — search: &quot;beehive fence elephants deterrent Africa&quot;. Shows the real invention referenced in this lesson and how it works in practice.</li><li><em>Elephants and bees: the science of why it works</em> — search: &quot;why elephants afraid of bees&quot;. Explains the natural behaviour the invention relies on, deepening understanding of WHY the solution is clever.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Diagram a Beehive Fence</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw and label a diagram of a beehive fence around a field, showing posts, wire and hives.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of your diagram shows HOW the fence actually works, not just what it looks like?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Farmer's Perspective</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece, in role as a farmer near Gogo Thandiwe's village, explaining why you chose a beehive fence over an electric one.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What trade-off did you consider before deciding?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: List the &quot;Win-Win&quot; Chain</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple diagram or flowchart showing every group that benefits from a beehive fence, and how.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which benefit surprised you most when you mapped it out?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Beehive Fences</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on real beehive fence projects.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;beehive fence elephants deterrent Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the invention did you think was the cleverest, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 30: Threads That Tell a Story":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THREADS THAT TELL A STORY — Weaving and Shweshwe Textiles</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Developing Practical Knowledge: explore weaving as a practical craft technique, planning and executing a repeating pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Shweshwe fabric and basket weaving are important textile traditions in this region.</li><li>We are learning basic weaving technique, and that real weaving requires planning a pattern before starting.</li><li>I can create a simple woven piece using a planned, repeating pattern.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the planning and weaving process; review of finished woven pieces for evidence of a genuinely planned, deliberate pattern.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Shweshwe fabric, basket weaving — my grandmother's other great skill, alongside painting the walls,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;Every thread, woven with intention. Today, I want you to try weaving something yourselves — even something small teaches you how much patience real weaving takes.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why do you think a weaver plans their whole pattern before a single thread crosses another, instead of just starting and seeing what happens?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Weaving Traditions of the Region (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce Shweshwe, a distinctive printed cotton fabric with deep roots in Southern African fashion and identity, and basket weaving, a practical and artistic craft tradition using natural fibres woven into deliberate, often intricate patterns.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What do you think is the difference between a pattern that happens by accident, and one a weaver plans on purpose?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Simple Paper Weaving (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: using strips of coloured paper, students weave a small mat or panel inspired by Shweshwe textile patterns and basket weaving techniques — over-and-under, in a deliberate, repeating pattern, planning their colour sequence on paper first.</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;How does your planned colour pattern compare to how it's actually turning out as you weave?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining why real weavers plan their pattern before they begin weaving.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Proverbs 31 praises a woman who works with willing, capable hands — &quot;she selects wool and flax and works with eager hands&quot; — a picture of skilled, intentional craft as something genuinely worth honouring, not just decoration.</li><li>Verse: &quot;She selects wool and flax and works with eager hands.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 31:13 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Learning a real, skilled craft like weaving helps us know and honour the kind of intentional, patient work the Bible itself holds up as admirable.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs 31 praises &quot;eager hands&quot; doing skilled work. Why might eagerness matter as much as skill itself?</li><li>Nomvula says every thread is &quot;woven with intention.&quot; Where else in your own life could a little more intention change the result?</li><li>What is one skill you'd like to get patient and eager enough about to genuinely master, the way Gogo Thandiwe has mastered weaving?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of real Shweshwe fabric and basket weaving</li><li>Strips of coloured paper for weaving</li><li>Glue or tape to secure finished woven pieces</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Shweshwe fabric and basket weaving traditions</em> — search: &quot;Shweshwe fabric basket weaving South Africa&quot;. Gives students a clear, respectful visual grounding in the real textile traditions before the paper-weaving activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Weave a Second Panel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weave a second, smaller panel using a different planned colour pattern from your first.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you change about your planning process the second time?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Naming Your Weaving</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short explanation of your finished weaving's name and what its pattern reminds you of.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60–100 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Did the name come before or after you finished weaving?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Pattern Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, design a colour pattern plan for a weaving before making it by hand.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Was planning digitally easier or harder than planning on paper?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Weaving Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about Shweshwe fabric and basket weaving.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Shweshwe fabric basket weaving South Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one detail from the video that you tried to include in your own weaving?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 31: The Day the Flag Changed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE DAY THE FLAG CHANGED — Independence in Zimbabwe and South Africa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding How Societies Work: understand what independence and democratic self-determination mean for a country's people.</li><li>Understanding the Past: investigate significant national moments and what changed, and did not immediately change, because of them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Zimbabwe gained independence in 1980 and South Africa held its first democratic election in 1994.</li><li>We are learning what democracy and self-determination mean for a country's people.</li><li>I can name one thing that changes quickly on an independence day, and one thing that takes much longer to change.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion; review of badge/flag designs for genuine engagement with what these days meant to real people.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Music drifts somewhere in the distance. Tapiwa's unusually still, watching a small kite shaped like an aircraft. &quot;Independence Day, crew!&quot; he says. &quot;Zimbabwe in 1980, South Africa's first democratic election in 1994 — both huge moments, both about the same thing at heart: a country's people finally getting to choose for themselves. Sekuru Josiah says he still remembers exactly where he was flying the day it happened. Let's find out what democracy and self-determination actually meant, for real people, on those days.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think would be the very first thing that felt different for a family, on the actual day their country became independent?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Two Independence Days (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that Zimbabwe gained independence in 1980, ending British colonial rule and minority rule, and South Africa held its first fully democratic election in 1994, ending apartheid, with Nelson Mandela elected as its first democratically elected president.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think both of these moments involved an ELECTION or a change in who could vote and lead, rather than just a change of flag?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Changes, and What Takes Longer (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a class, discuss what might change quickly on a day like this (a flag, a national anthem, who holds office) and what might take much longer to change (economic opportunity, land ownership, everyday attitudes) — listing three of each.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think some kinds of change happen instantly, while others take generations?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing that changed quickly, and ONE thing that took much longer, after independence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Paul writes that Christ set us free specifically FOR freedom — not just freedom from something, but freedom for something better. Independence Day, for Zimbabwe and South Africa alike, was exactly that kind of moment: freedom won not as an ending, but as a beginning.</li><li>Verse: &quot;It is for freedom that Christ has set us free. Stand firm, then, and do not let yourselves be burdened again by a yoke of slavery.&quot; — <em>Galatians 5:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Recognising that real freedom is meant to be used for something good, not just celebrated once, helps us see independence as a call to keep building justice, not just a finished achievement.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says Christ set us free &quot;for freedom,&quot; not just to end slavery. What do you think a country's freedom should be FOR, once it's won?</li><li>Sekuru Josiah remembers exactly where he was on Zimbabwe's independence day. Do you know a story like that from your own family's history?</li><li>Why might it matter to &quot;stand firm&quot; in freedom, even long after the actual day it was won?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Simple, age-appropriate timeline of Zimbabwean and South African independence</li><li>Blank card/paper for badge or flag element designs</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Zimbabwe and South African independence, explained for kids</em> — search: &quot;Zimbabwe independence 1980 South Africa 1994 election explained for kids&quot;. Gives students a clear, celebratory but honest overview of both independence moments before the class discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Commemorative Badge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple commemorative badge or flag element celebrating one of these independence days.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose Zimbabwe (1980) or South Africa (1994).</li><li>Sketch a badge using colours and symbols meaningful to that country's independence.</li><li>Add one short word or phrase capturing the feeling of that day.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose those particular colours or symbols?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Letter on Independence Day</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short letter in role as a young person living through one of these independence days, describing what they hope will change.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What hope did you choose to include, and why that one?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Two-Country Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a timeline comparing Zimbabwe's 1980 independence and South Africa's 1994 election.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What similarity between the two timelines stood out most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Two Independence Days</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining both independence moments.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;Zimbabwe independence 1980 South Africa 1994 election explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing you learned that you hadn't known before today?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 32: From Field to Table":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FROM FIELD TO TABLE — Markets and Supply Chains</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how food moves from producer to consumer through markets and supply chains.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the steps food takes from a farm to a family's table, including markets and transport.</li><li>We are learning to connect this journey back to the subsistence and commercial farming studied earlier this term.</li><li>I can sequence the steps a crop takes from planting to reaching a dinner table.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the sequencing activity; review of flow diagrams for accurate, logical steps in the supply chain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Farm to table isn't as simple as it sounds,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;Markets, transport, storage — a lot has to happen between my mum's plot and someone's dinner plate. Let's trace the whole journey, connecting back to everything we've learned about farming this term.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: how many different people do you think touch a single piece of fruit between the farm it grew on and your own dinner table?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Recap: Two Kinds of Farming (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: briefly recap subsistence farming (Week 2) and commercial farming (Week 3), reminding the class that food's journey to a table looks different depending on which kind of farming produced it.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of the two farming types do you think involves a LONGER supply chain, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Sequencing the Journey (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a class, sequence the steps a maize crop takes from planting to reaching a family's dinner table — planting, growing, harvesting, storage, transport, market/sale, and preparation — identifying where things could go wrong along the way (spoilage, transport delays, unfair pricing).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which single step in the chain do you think is the MOST likely to go wrong, and why?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE step in the farm-to-table journey where something could go wrong.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Proverbs 31 describes a capable woman who &quot;provides food for her family&quot; with foresight and care — bringing food from distant places, planning ahead. The whole farm-to-table chain, at its best, is a shared act of exactly that kind of care for others.</li><li>Verse: &quot;She is like the merchant ships, bringing her food from afar.&quot; — <em>Proverbs 31:14 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Understanding the many hands and steps behind a simple meal helps us recognise farm-to-table work as real service — care for others carried out through careful, unglamorous effort.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Proverbs 31 praises someone who works hard so others can eat well. Who are the &quot;hidden&quot; people in a modern farm-to-table chain doing something similar?</li><li>Tapiwa says a lot has to happen between the field and the plate. Why might it be easy to forget all those steps when you're just hungry?</li><li>How could you show more gratitude for the people involved in getting food to YOUR table?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank flow-diagram template for the farm-to-table sequence</li><li>Recap notes/images from Week 2 (subsistence) and Week 3 (commercial) farming lessons</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How food gets from farm to table</em> — search: &quot;farm to table supply chain explained for kids&quot;. Gives students a clear, simple grounding in supply chains before the class sequencing activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Farm-to-Table Flow Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a simple flow diagram showing &quot;farm to table&quot; for a crop of your choice.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a crop studied this term (maize, tobacco, citrus, etc).</li><li>Sketch each step from planting to plate as connected boxes or icons.</li><li>Add one arrow labelled with a risk (e.g. spoilage, delay).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which step took the longest to draw clearly, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Day in the Supply Chain</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece in role as one person in the farm-to-table chain (farmer, transporter, market seller), describing their part of the journey.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made your chosen role's part of the journey challenging?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Supply Chain Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a map or flowchart showing the farm-to-table journey for a chosen crop.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which part of your digital map would be hardest to represent as a real photo?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Farm to Table</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video explaining how food travels from farm to table.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;farm to table supply chain explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one step in the chain you hadn't thought about before?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 33: The Dung Beetle's Big Job":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE DUNG BEETLE'S BIG JOB — Decomposers in the Savanna</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand the role of decomposers in recycling nutrients and maintaining a healthy ecosystem.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a decomposer is, and that dung beetles are an important decomposer in the savanna.</li><li>We are learning specific ways dung beetles help the savanna ecosystem, beyond just being unusual.</li><li>I can explain what would happen to a savanna ecosystem without decomposers like the dung beetle.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the group discussion; review of comic strips for genuine understanding of the dung beetle's ecological role.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Okay, this one's genuinely funny, but stick with me,&quot; Tapiwa grins. &quot;Dung beetles roll up animal dung and bury it — which sounds odd, until you realise they're recycling nutrients back into the soil, controlling parasites, even helping seeds spread. Small, unglamorous, essential. My favourite kind of hero, honestly.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: can you think of a job in real life that sounds unglamorous but is actually essential to how everything else works?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Is a Decomposer? (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that decomposers are organisms that break down dead material and waste, recycling nutrients back into the soil so plants can use them again, making them essential to any healthy ecosystem.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What do you think a savanna would look like after just a few months without ANY decomposers at work?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">The Dung Beetle's Big Job (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain the dung beetle's real, essential role — rolling and burying dung, which recycles nutrients into the soil, helps control fly and parasite populations, and even helps disperse seeds that were inside the dung, connecting back to plant growth across the savanna.</li><li>Activity: in groups, discuss why an ecosystem might struggle without its decomposers, even though they're small and easy to overlook, listing at least two specific consequences.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think it took scientists a while to properly appreciate how important dung beetles are?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE specific way dung beetles help the savanna ecosystem.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 104 celebrates the sheer variety of God's creatures, right down to the smallest and strangest, all sustained and given purpose by God's Spirit. The dung beetle — genuinely funny, genuinely essential — is a perfect small example of a creation full of purpose in unexpected places.</li><li>Verse: &quot;When you send your Spirit, they are created, and you renew the face of the ground.&quot; — <em>Psalm 104:30 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love (World) — Appreciating even the strangest, least glamorous parts of God's creation — like the dung beetle — helps us love the whole of creation, not just the parts that seem impressive at first glance.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 104 celebrates ALL kinds of creatures, not just the impressive ones. Why might God have made something as unglamorous as the dung beetle so essential?</li><li>Tapiwa calls the dung beetle his &quot;favourite kind of hero.&quot; What's your own favourite example of something small doing something essential?</li><li>Is there a job or role in YOUR life or classroom that's unglamorous but actually really important? How could you appreciate it more?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or video of dung beetles at work</li><li>Blank comic-strip template</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Dung beetles and their ecological role</em> — search: &quot;dung beetle decomposer savanna ecosystem explained&quot;. Gives students a clear, genuinely engaging introduction to the dung beetle's role before the group discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw a Dung Beetle &quot;Hero&quot; Comic Strip</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a dung beetle &quot;hero&quot; comic strip showing its unglamorous but essential job.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Draw 3–4 panels showing the dung beetle finding, rolling and burying dung.</li><li>Add a caption in each panel explaining what's happening ecologically.</li><li>Give your comic strip a heroic title.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose that particular title?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Dung Beetle's Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short diary entry from the point of view of a dung beetle describing its day's work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What detail did you include to show why the work matters, not just what it looks like?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Decomposer Cycle Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a diagram showing how dung beetles recycle nutrients back into the soil and plant growth.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which part of the cycle was hardest to represent clearly?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Dung Beetles at Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about dung beetles and decomposers.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;dung beetle decomposer savanna ecosystem explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one fact from the video that made you laugh or surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 34: Eyes That Never Sleep":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">EYES THAT NEVER SLEEP — Drones, Thermal Cameras and Modern Ranger Patrols</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Systems: understand how several different technologies can be combined into one patrol system.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that new technologies raise new questions about how they should be used responsibly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how drones and thermal cameras extend what a ranger team can see, especially at night.</li><li>We are learning what patrol-management software does and why logging data carefully matters.</li><li>I can explain how combining several modern technologies creates a stronger patrol system than any one on its own.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the patrol-planning activity and discussion; review of written explanations for understanding of how combined systems reduce blind spots.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>It's Independence Day, and the whole route feels lighter — music somewhere in the distance, Tapiwa unusually still for once, watching a small aircraft-shaped kite someone's flying nearby. &quot;Sekuru Josiah used to fly the whole night sky just with instruments and instinct,&quot; he says. &quot;These days, my dad's team has a few more eyes than that.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: a rhino poacher is far more likely to move at night. So how do you patrol a reserve the size of a small country, in the dark, when you can't see very far by torchlight?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Seeing in the Dark: Thermal Cameras (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A thermal camera doesn't detect light like a normal camera — it detects heat, showing warm shapes (a person, an animal) as a glowing outline even in total darkness or thick bush, because living bodies are almost always warmer than their surroundings.</li><li>Discuss: why might this be especially useful for a night patrol looking for both wildlife AND people who shouldn't be there?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Eyes in the Sky: Drones (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: A drone (small remote-controlled aircraft) fitted with a thermal or regular camera can cover a huge area quickly from above, spotting things a ground patrol on foot might walk right past — while keeping the ranger team safely at a distance.</li><li>Discuss trade-offs honestly: drones need trained pilots, batteries run out, and they work best alongside ground teams, not instead of them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Bringing It Together: Patrol-Planning Simulation (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: In teams, students plan a night patrol route for a reserve map, deciding where to use ground patrol, where a drone sweep would help most, and where a fixed camera trap (from Week 3) would be the better tool — justifying each choice.</li><li>Teams share one decision and the reasoning behind it.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why is combining several technologies usually stronger than choosing just the newest or most impressive one?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE way a drone or thermal camera helps rangers do something they couldn't do on foot alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Nehemiah's people didn't just build — they kept watch, day and night, while they worked, because they knew the threat was real. Modern rangers do something similar: careful, constant vigilance, using every tool available, because what they're protecting matters enough to stay alert for.</li><li>Verse: &quot;We prayed to our God and posted a guard day and night.&quot; — <em>Nehemiah 4:9 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Manaaki includes the quiet, unglamorous kind of service — staying alert, keeping watch, showing up night after night for something worth protecting.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Nehemiah's people prayed AND posted a guard — they trusted God, but they still did the hard, practical work too. Why do you think both mattered?</li><li>Rangers use drones and thermal cameras to stay watchful even when they're tired. What helps YOU stay faithful to something even when it's hard or tiring?</li><li>What is something worth &quot;keeping watch&quot; over in your own life or community?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank reserve patrol maps, one per team</li><li>Diagram/photo examples of thermal camera imagery (heat-shape outlines), if available</li><li>Diagram of a simple drone with a camera attached</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>How thermal cameras help anti-poaching patrols</em> — search: &quot;thermal camera anti-poaching night patrol Africa&quot;. Shows the real application of thermal imaging technology referenced in this lesson.</li><li><em>Drones used in wildlife conservation</em> — search: &quot;drones wildlife conservation anti-poaching Africa&quot;. Demonstrates real drone use in African conservation, grounding today's design activity.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Patrol Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a labelled patrol map showing where ground teams, drones and camera traps would each be used across one night.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which area of your map relies on more than one technology, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Night Patrol Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short report, in role as a ranger, summarising what a night patrol using drones and thermal cameras found.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did the combined technologies let you notice that a single tool might have missed?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Compare the Technologies</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a simple comparison chart of ground patrol, drones and camera traps — strengths and weaknesses of each.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which technology would you trust least on its own, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Modern Ranger Technology</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on drones or thermal cameras used in anti-poaching work.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;drones wildlife conservation anti-poaching Africa&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of the technology impressed you most?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 35: Marimba and Gumboot":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">MARIMBA AND GUMBOOT — Music and Dance as Art</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Music/Dance, Developing Practical Knowledge: explore rhythm and movement traditions with real cultural and historical roots, and perform a simple rhythm sequence.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Participating and Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that marimba music, drumming and gumboot dance are expressive traditions with real cultural and historical roots.</li><li>We are learning basic rhythm-making using body percussion, inspired by gumboot dance.</li><li>I can perform a simple rhythm pattern as part of a group.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of group participation and rhythm-pattern performance; genuine engagement with the celebratory tone of the day.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Music and dance ARE Independence Day, crew,&quot; Nomvula says, already tapping a rhythm. &quot;Marimba, drumming, gumboot dance — expressive traditions with real history behind them, especially gumboot, born out of hard work in the mines, turned into something joyful. Today, make some noise, properly, in celebration.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: why do you think people so often turn to music and dance specifically to celebrate freedom or independence?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Marimba, Drumming and Gumboot Dance (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce marimba music (a xylophone-like instrument central to Zimbabwean musical tradition), drumming patterns, and gumboot dance — a rhythmic, stamping and boot-slapping dance style that originated among miners in South Africa and became a powerful expression of resilience and joy.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;How does knowing gumboot dance began as something born out of hard work change the way you look at it as a celebration today?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Creating a Rhythm Pattern (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: in groups, students create a simple 8-beat rhythm pattern using body percussion (stamping, clapping, slapping), inspired by gumboot dance, then perform it for the class.</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;How does your group keep everyone in time together?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining why gumboot dance is a good symbol for turning something hard into something joyful.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 150 calls for praising God with dancing and rhythm, tambourine and strings, holding nothing back. A classroom full of stamping, clapping rhythm on a celebration day is, in its own small way, exactly the kind of full-bodied, joyful expression the psalm describes.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Praise him with tambourine and dancing, praise him with the strings and pipe.&quot; — <em>Psalm 150:4 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha  Love — Making music and moving together in celebration is a genuine act of Aroha — joy shared out loud, in community, echoing how the Bible itself describes praising God.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 150 lists lots of different instruments and dancing, not just quiet singing. Why do you think celebration in the Bible is often so physical and loud?</li><li>Gumboot dance turned hard work into something joyful. Is there something hard in your own life that could be turned into something joyful, the way gumboot dance was?</li><li>What's one thing worth celebrating loudly and physically in your own life or classroom right now?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recorded marimba music or simple percussion instruments</li><li>Open floor space for the rhythm-pattern activity</li><li>Video or images of gumboot dance for reference</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Gumboot dance and marimba music</em> — search: &quot;gumboot dance marimba South Africa Zimbabwe explained&quot;. Gives students a clear, energetic visual introduction to both traditions before creating their own rhythm pattern.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Rhythm Notation Card</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple visual notation card showing your group's 8-beat rhythm pattern using symbols or icons.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What symbol did you choose for a stamp versus a clap, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Writing About Gumboot Dance</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece explaining what gumboot dance is and why it began, in your own words.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What part of gumboot dance's history surprised you most?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Record Your Group's Rhythm</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If equipment allows, record a short audio or video of your group's 8-beat rhythm pattern.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you notice about your rhythm when you listened back that you hadn't noticed while performing it?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Gumboot Dance and Marimba</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about gumboot dance and marimba music.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;gumboot dance marimba South Africa Zimbabwe explained&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one movement or sound from the video you tried to copy in your own rhythm pattern?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Flight Log 36: Two Worlds, One Life":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">TWO WORLDS, ONE LIFE — Southern Africa Today</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand that modern Southern Africa includes busy cities, rural life, and communities living between both.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Southern Africa today includes large modern cities as well as rural villages and reserves.</li><li>We are learning about the diaspora — people and families who live and work between two different places.</li><li>I can describe what might be hard and what might be good about living between a city and a home village.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the pairs discussion; review of two-scene drawings for genuine engagement with living &quot;between two worlds.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both Tapiwa and Nomvula sound a little older this week, somehow. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;Southern Africa today isn't just villages and reserves — it's huge, busy cities too, and a lot of young people living between both worlds, like Nomvula's own mum, working in Johannesburg during the week and coming home on weekends. I want you to see the whole modern picture, not just the parts we've shown you so far.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think it would feel like to belong fully to two very different places at once?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">A Modern, Varied Region (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that Southern Africa today includes large, modern cities like Johannesburg and Harare, alongside rural villages, farms and wildlife reserves — a much more varied picture than any single week's story location has shown.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think it's easy to picture only ONE version of a country or region, instead of the whole varied picture?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Living Between Two Worlds (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce the idea of the diaspora — people who live and work away from their home community, like Nomvula's mother working in Johannesburg during the week and returning home on weekends — and explain this is a genuinely common experience for many families in the region.</li><li>Activity: in pairs, students discuss what might be hard, and what might be good, about living between a busy city and a home village or town.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What do you think would be the hardest thing to explain to a friend in the OTHER world about your life in this one?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing that might be hard, and ONE thing that might be good, about living between two worlds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jeremiah's promise wasn't given to a settled, comfortable people — it was given to Israelites living far from home, uncertain about the future. It's a fitting promise for families like Nomvula's, stretched between two worlds: God's plans for hope and a future don't depend on staying in one place.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For I know the plans I have for you,&quot; declares the Lord, &quot;plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future.&quot; — <em>Jeremiah 29:11 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Knowing that God's good plans for us don't depend on staying in one fixed place helps us see families living between two worlds not as divided, but as carried by the same hope wherever they are.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Jeremiah 29:11 was written to people living far from home. Why might that make the promise even MORE meaningful, rather than less?</li><li>Nomvula's mum lives between two worlds for her family's sake. Do you know anyone in your own life who does something similar?</li><li>How might knowing God has a plan for you change how you feel about a big change or move in your own life?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images contrasting modern Southern African cities and rural/village life</li><li>Blank paper for two-scene drawings</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Modern Southern Africa: cities and rural life</em> — search: &quot;modern South Africa Zimbabwe cities rural life today&quot;. Gives students a clear, current visual picture of the region's variety before the pairs discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Draw Two Connected Scenes</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw two small connected scenes — one city, one village — joined by a single line representing a family's journey between them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a small city scene on one side.</li><li>Sketch a small village or rural scene on the other.</li><li>Draw a single connecting line or road between them, representing the journey.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did you choose to include in each scene to show they're both &quot;home&quot; in different ways?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Weekend Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece in role as someone who travels between a city job and a home village each week, describing the journey and what they look forward to.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did your character look forward to most, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a &quot;Two Worlds&quot; Comparison Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a chart comparing city life and village life in modern Southern Africa.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which difference on your chart do you think matters most to a family living between both?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Modern Southern Africa</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about cities and rural life in Southern Africa today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;modern South Africa Zimbabwe cities rural life today&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing about modern Southern Africa that surprised you?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Flight Log 37: Farming for a Changing Climate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FARMING FOR A CHANGING CLIMATE — Sustainable and Climate-Smart Farming</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: understand how farming communities adapt their practices in response to a changing climate.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what sustainable and climate-smart farming means, drawing together the farming knowledge built across this whole term.</li><li>We are learning specific techniques farmers use to adapt to a changing climate.</li><li>I can design a simple farm plan that includes at least two climate-smart features.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the brainstorm discussion; review of climate-smart farm diagrams for genuine understanding of adaptation, not just decoration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;The climate's changing, crew — that's not really up for debate anymore, my mum says,&quot; Tapiwa explains. &quot;Sustainable, climate-smart farming is about adapting: new methods that protect the land while still feeding families. I want you to plan farming for the future, not just describe farming as it's always been.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you were a farmer today, what's one thing about the weather you couldn't count on the way farmers once could?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Recap: Farming Across the Term (8 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: briefly recap subsistence farming (Week 2), commercial farming (Week 3), water and irrigation challenges (Week 4), and land/food security (Week 6), setting up today's forward-looking question: how does farming need to adapt now?</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Looking back across everything we've learned about farming, what's the ONE challenge that's come up again and again?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Climate-Smart Techniques (17 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce real climate-smart farming techniques used across the region — conservation agriculture (minimal soil disturbance), drought-resistant crop varieties, rainwater harvesting, mixed cropping, and better water management.</li><li>Activity: in groups, students brainstorm three changes a farmer might make to protect their crops and soil as rainfall patterns shift, drawing on techniques just introduced.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these techniques do you think would be easiest for a small family farm to adopt, and which would be hardest?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE climate-smart farming technique and explain what problem it solves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Paul writes that creation itself is &quot;groaning&quot; under the weight of a broken world, waiting for renewal — a genuinely honest picture of a changing climate. Farmers adapting wisely, working with the land rather than against it, are a small but real part of caring for that same waiting creation.</li><li>Verse: &quot;For the creation waits in eager expectation for the children of God to be revealed... in hope that the creation itself will be liberated from its bondage to decay.&quot; — <em>Romans 8:19, 21 (NIV, shortened)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Choosing to farm in ways that protect the land for the future is real Kingdom work — small, practical action that cares for a creation the Bible says is still waiting to be fully renewed.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Paul says creation is &quot;waiting,&quot; almost like it's holding its breath for something better. What do you think climate-smart farming has to do with that waiting?</li><li>Tapiwa says his mum talks about the climate changing. Have you noticed anyone in your own life talking about changes like this?</li><li>What's one small, practical thing YOU could do that cares for the land the way a climate-smart farmer does?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images or diagrams of real climate-smart farming techniques</li><li>Blank paper for climate-smart farm diagrams</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Climate-smart farming explained</em> — search: &quot;climate smart farming techniques explained for kids&quot;. Gives students a clear, practical introduction to real adaptation techniques before the group brainstorm.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Climate-Smart Farm Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple &quot;climate-smart farm&quot; diagram showing at least two sustainable features.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sketch a small farm layout.</li><li>Add and label at least two climate-smart features (e.g. rainwater tank, mixed crops).</li><li>Add a short caption explaining what problem each feature solves.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which feature did you think was the cleverest solution, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Farmer's Plan for the Future</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short piece in role as a farmer explaining the changes they're making to farm sustainably as the climate shifts.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What change did your farmer prioritise first, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Climate-Smart Techniques Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a chart listing climate-smart techniques and the specific problem each one solves.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which technique would you want to learn more about, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Climate-Smart Farming</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about climate-smart farming techniques.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;climate smart farming techniques explained for kids&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one technique from the video you didn't discuss in class?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Flight Log 38: Where Things Are Getting Better":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">WHERE THINGS ARE GETTING BETTER — Conservation Success Stories</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: understand that conservation efforts, such as national parks and community conservancies, can lead to measurable recovery of species and habitats.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that national parks and community conservancies are producing real, measurable conservation successes in Southern Africa today.</li><li>We are learning to evaluate what makes a conservation project genuinely successful, not just well-intentioned.</li><li>I can describe one real conservation success story from this region.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the class discussion; review of &quot;conservation win&quot; certificates for genuine understanding of what a real success looks like.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;After the rhino week, I want to show you something hopeful,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;National parks and community conservancies really are protecting wildlife today — real, measurable wins, not just good intentions. It's not all struggle, crew. Some of it's working.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: what do you think is the difference between a conservation project that SOUNDS good and one that's actually, measurably working?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Real Conservation Wins (12 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: introduce real conservation success stories from the region — national parks and community conservancies (locally-run protected areas that often directly benefit nearby communities) that have led to measurable recovery in species populations or habitat protection, connecting back to Week 4's rhino crisis and Week 6's elephants.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why might a conservancy RUN by the local community succeed differently than one run entirely by outsiders?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">What Makes Success Real? (13 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a class, discuss what makes a conservation project actually successful long-term — measurable population recovery, community involvement and benefit, ongoing funding, and effective anti-poaching enforcement — rather than just well-intentioned.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;If you were checking whether a conservation project was REALLY working, what evidence would you look for?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE thing that makes a conservation project genuinely successful, not just well-intentioned.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Revelation pictures God making all things new — real, complete renewal, not just good intentions. Every genuine conservation success story, however small, is a small preview of that larger promise: things that were breaking down being restored.</li><li>Verse: &quot;He who was seated on the throne said, &quot;I am making everything new!&quot;&quot; — <em>Revelation 21:5 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Celebrating real conservation wins helps us see restoration work as genuine Kingdom impact — small, real signs of the renewal God has promised for the whole of creation.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Revelation pictures God making &quot;everything&quot; new, not just some things. How does that compare to a conservation project that succeeds in one small area?</li><li>Tapiwa wanted to show you something hopeful today, after the harder rhino lesson. Why might hope matter just as much as honesty about hard things?</li><li>What's one small &quot;renewal&quot; YOU could be part of, in your own community or family, this week?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Real examples/images of conservation success stories from the region</li><li>Blank certificate template</li><li>Small pieces of paper for exit tickets</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Conservation success stories in Southern Africa</em> — search: &quot;conservation success stories Southern Africa national parks&quot;. Gives students real, hopeful examples of measurable conservation success before the class discussion.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a &quot;Conservation Win&quot; Certificate</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a simple &quot;conservation win&quot; certificate for a species or area of your choice, imagining its recovery.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Instructions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Choose a species or area studied this term.</li><li>Design a certificate celebrating its imagined recovery.</li><li>Add one sentence explaining what made the recovery successful.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What evidence did you imagine would prove your chosen recovery was real?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: A Ranger's Good News Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short &quot;good news&quot; report in role as a ranger describing a real conservation success they've witnessed.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What specific evidence did your ranger include to prove the success was real?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Conservation Success Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a timeline showing the steps from a conservation problem to a measurable success.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which step in your timeline do you think was the turning point?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Conservation Wins</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about real Southern African conservation success stories.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;conservation success stories Southern Africa national parks&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one success story from the video that gave you genuine hope?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: Flight Log 39: Blueprints for Tomorrow":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">BLUEPRINTS FOR TOMORROW — Planning the Conservation Tech Expo</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Practice: undertake planning to guide the development of an outcome, identifying key considerations and stakeholders.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand that technological outcomes are evaluated in terms of how well they meet the need or opportunity they were developed for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self; Participating and Contributing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to choose an endangered Southern African animal and identify a real, specific problem affecting it.</li><li>We are learning to apply the technological design process (plan, create, evaluate) to invent a device or system that could help.</li><li>I can plan a project, including what I'll need and how I'll know if it worked.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the planning process; review of completed planning templates for clarity of problem definition and realistic evaluation criteria.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Both Tapiwa and Nomvula sound different this week — a little older, somehow. &quot;Sekuru Josiah always says the whole point of learning something is being able to pass it on,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;So here's your chance. Next week, it's not us teaching you. It's you, showing everyone what you've learned.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: out of everything you've learned this term — tracking, radios, camera traps, DNA tracing, GPS collars, beehive fences, drones — if you had to invent ONE new idea to help protect a specific animal, what problem would you choose to solve?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choosing an Animal and a Real Problem (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Remind students of the specific animals and challenges met this term (rhino poaching, elephant-human conflict, predator monitoring) and any others they know of. Emphasise choosing ONE specific, real problem rather than a vague one — &quot;help elephants&quot; is too broad; &quot;warn a village before elephants reach the crops&quot; is a real, solvable problem.</li><li>Activity: individually or in pairs, students choose their animal and write a single clear sentence describing the specific problem they will try to solve.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Applying the Design Process (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Walk through the technological design process the class has been using all term without necessarily naming it: Plan (what's the problem, who needs it, what materials/ideas are available), Create (sketch or build a first version), Evaluate (would it actually solve the problem — what are its limits).</li><li>Activity: students complete a simple planning template — problem, chosen technology idea (can combine ideas from the term, e.g. a camera trap plus an alert system), materials needed, and one way they'll know if it worked.</li><li>Circulate and prompt with the same kind of challenge questions used in Week 5's Boss Battle.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Peer Feedback (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: students swap plans with a partner and give one honest piece of feedback: one strength, and one question that would make the plan stronger.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What is the difference between a good idea and a plan that could actually be built and tested?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what specific problem will your Conservation Tech Expo project try to solve, and for which animal?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Ephesians reminds us that we are God's own handiwork, made on purpose, for good work prepared in advance for us to do. Planning something that could genuinely help protect a real animal is a small taste of stepping into that kind of purposeful, good work.</li><li>Verse: &quot;We are God's handiwork, created to do good works.&quot; — <em>Ephesians 2:10 (NIV, shortened)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Careful planning — not just a good idea, but a plan someone could actually act on — is itself a form of faithful service with the gifts God has given us.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Ephesians says we were made for &quot;good works prepared in advance.&quot; Do you think God cares about small, practical plans like this one, or only &quot;big&quot; things? Why?</li><li>Tapiwa and Nomvula are learning to pass on what they know, the way Sekuru Josiah passed it to them. Who has passed something valuable on to YOU, and what will you do with it?</li><li>What is one piece of feedback you received today that will genuinely make your plan better?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Conservation Tech Expo planning templates, one per student/pair</li><li>List/reminder cards of the term's technologies (tracking, radios, camera traps, DNA tracing, GPS collars, beehive fences, drones) for inspiration</li><li>Peer feedback slips</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Young inventors solving conservation problems</em> — search: &quot;young inventors wildlife conservation technology students&quot;. Shows relatable, age-appropriate examples of real people designing solutions, to inspire students' own planning.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Sketch Your First Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a labelled first-draft sketch of your Conservation Tech Expo idea.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is the one feature of your design that solves the actual problem, specifically?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write Your Project Pitch</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short pitch explaining your chosen animal, the problem, and your idea.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is the strongest sentence in your pitch, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Planning Board</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a simple document or slide to organise your plan under headings: Problem, Idea, Materials, How I'll Know It Worked.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which heading was hardest to fill in clearly, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Young Conservation Inventors</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about young people inventing conservation solutions.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;young inventors wildlife conservation technology students&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one thing their process has in common with yours?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Flight Log 40: Faces Carved to Remember":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">FACES CARVED TO REMEMBER — Mask and Carving Traditions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Developing Ideas: explore mask and carving traditions as forms of memory, ceremony and storytelling.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing Self</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that mask and carving traditions run across this whole region, and that they carry memory, ceremony and storytelling, not just decoration.</li><li>We are learning to design a mask or carved-face artwork with real personal meaning behind it.</li><li>I can explain what value, memory or feeling my artwork represents.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the planning and design process; review of finished masks/faces and written explanations for genuine personal meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Masks and carving traditions run right across this whole region, crew — not just decoration, but memory, ceremony, storytelling passed down through generations,&quot; Nomvula says. &quot;Today, before next week's big finish, I want you to carve or draw a face that means something — a memory, a value, a feeling you want to carry forward.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you could carve or draw ONE face to represent something you want to remember from this whole term, what would it be?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Masks and Carving as Memory (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: explain that mask and carving traditions across Southern Africa are rarely made purely for decoration — they carry meaning connected to memory, ceremony, storytelling and identity, often representing values, ancestors, or important community moments.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Why do you think a physical object, like a carved mask, might help a community remember something better than words alone?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Designing a Meaningful Mask or Face (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: using card, clay, or paper, students design a mask or carved-face artwork representing a value, memory, or feeling they want to carry forward from the term, first writing one sentence about what it represents before starting, in keeping with real ceremonial masks never being made without a clear purpose.</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;How does the SHAPE or expression of your face design connect to what it represents?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining what your mask or face design represents and why you chose it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Psalm 139 marvels that each person is &quot;fearfully and wonderfully made&quot; — carefully, intentionally crafted, not accidental. Designing a mask or face with real, chosen meaning behind it is a small echo of that same idea: careful, intentional creation carries purpose.</li><li>Verse: &quot;I praise you because I am fearfully and wonderfully made; your works are wonderful, I know that full well.&quot; — <em>Psalm 139:14 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Making something with real, chosen meaning behind it helps us know that intentional creation — whether a mask or a person — carries real purpose, not accident.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 139 says WE are &quot;fearfully and wonderfully made,&quot; on purpose. How is that similar to how you approached designing your mask today?</li><li>Real ceremonial masks are never made without a clear purpose. Why do you think that matters, compared to art made just to look nice?</li><li>What is one value or memory from this whole term you'd genuinely want to &quot;carve&quot; into how you live going forward?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Images of real mask and carving traditions from the region (age-appropriate examples)</li><li>Card, clay, or paper for mask/face designs</li><li>Paint, markers, or colouring materials</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Mask and carving traditions across Southern Africa</em> — search: &quot;African mask carving traditions ceremony storytelling&quot;. Gives students a respectful, visual grounding in real mask and carving traditions before designing their own.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Second Mask</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a second mask or face representing a DIFFERENT value, memory or feeling from the term.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How does your second design differ in style from your first, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Explaining Your Mask</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, what your mask or face design represents and why you chose it.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Was it easier to design the mask or to explain it in words?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Design a Digital Mask Concept</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple drawing tool, create a digital concept sketch for a mask design before making it by hand.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What changed between your digital concept and your finished physical mask?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Mask and Carving Traditions</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video about mask and carving traditions across the region.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;African mask carving traditions ceremony storytelling&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What is one tradition from the video that influenced your own design?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: The Legacy Trial — Station 1: The Whole Route, In Order":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE WHOLE ROUTE, IN ORDER — Timeline Synthesis from the San to Today</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding the Past: demonstrate a coherent understanding of a sequence of historical events and their relationships to each other, drawn from a full term's learning.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are demonstrating our understanding of the whole term's history, from the San to today, in correct sequence.</li><li>We are working as a team to place major historical moments in the correct order on a shared timeline.</li><li>I can explain how at least one earlier historical event influenced a later one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS the assessment for the term's full History strand. Score team performance on the timeline challenge and use results to identify any concepts needing revisiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook lies open one final time, every leg of the route glowing faintly across its pages — Hwange, the Zambezi, the reserve, the border, Gogo Thandiwe's village, all the way home. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, and for once his voice doesn't crack a joke early. &quot;Sekuru Josiah always said a route only really matters if someone else can fly it after you. Today, the logbook wants to see the WHOLE route, laid out properly, in order.&quot; Nomvula adds: &quot;That's the pattern of it. Everything connects to everything else. Show us you can see it.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: looking back across the entire term, which single historical moment do you think changed the course of everything that came after it the most?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lay out a long floor timeline (string, tape, or paper strip) with a clear &quot;earliest&quot; to &quot;today&quot; direction, plus a shuffled set of event cards covering the whole term: the San as first peoples, Great Zimbabwe, Mapungubwe, the Ndebele migration (Mfecane), colonisation, apartheid and the struggle for freedom, and independence (Zimbabwe 1980, South Africa 1994).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Challenge Round (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams work together to place all event cards in correct chronological order along the timeline, justifying each placement aloud with a real fact from that week's learning.</li><li>Include at least one question testing WHY one event influenced a later one (e.g. how land and power changing hands during colonisation connects to the later struggle against apartheid).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which two events on this timeline are most closely connected, even though they happened years apart?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal the correct order. Briefly discuss any placements teams disagreed about, and why.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name ONE way an earlier event on the timeline directly influenced a later one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Moses told the Israelites to talk about God's story constantly — at home, on the road, lying down, getting up — so that the story would never be lost between generations. Building this timeline together is exactly that kind of remembering: making sure a true story gets carried forward, not forgotten.</li><li>Verse: &quot;These commandments that I give you today are to be on your hearts... Talk about them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road, when you lie down and when you get up.&quot; — <em>Deuteronomy 6:6–7 (NIV, shortened)</em></li><li>Value: Mōhio  Know — Piecing together the whole term's history, in order, helps us know that every nation's story — the hard parts and the hopeful parts — is worth remembering carefully and passing on.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Deuteronomy says to talk about important things constantly, in ordinary moments, not just on special occasions. Why might that matter for remembering history accurately?</li><li>Tapiwa says a route only matters if someone else can fly it after you. What is one part of this term's history YOU could &quot;pass on&quot; to someone else?</li><li>Looking at the whole timeline together, what does it tell you about how God has been present across all of this region's history, not just parts of it?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Long floor timeline (string, tape, or paper strip)</li><li>Shuffled event cards covering the whole term's History content</li><li>A visible scoreboard</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate the Full Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled illustrated timeline showing at least six major events from this term's History strand, in correct order.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which event was hardest to place correctly, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My History MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE history topic from this whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How would you explain it to someone who missed this term?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a timeline of the whole term's History content, from the San to today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What connection did building it digitally make clearer than the floor timeline did?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Term's Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own work from Week 1 (the San) through to Week 9 (Southern Africa today). What's one thing your understanding now includes that it didn't back then?</p></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: The Legacy Trial — Station 2: A Farming Plan for the Future":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A FARMING PLAN FOR THE FUTURE — Pitching Sustainable Farming</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Understanding Place and Environment: apply understanding of farming types and sustainability to design and present a coherent farming plan.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Using Language, Symbols &amp; Text</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are applying everything we've learned about subsistence, commercial, and climate-smart farming to design one coherent plan.</li><li>We are working as a team to present a farming plan clearly and persuasively.</li><li>I can justify at least two choices in my farming plan using real content from this term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS a capstone assessment for the term's Geography/farming content. Assess team plans against a simple rubric: coherence, use of real term content, and clarity of presentation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>&quot;Yesterday you showed us you remember the story,&quot; Tapiwa says. &quot;Today's different. The logbook doesn't just want memory — it wants a real plan. Pitch us a farming plan for the future, using everything you've learned about this land.&quot; Nomvula: &quot;Subsistence, commercial, climate-smart — you have all three now. Show us how they fit together.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you were designing a farm from scratch today, which matters more to plan for first — what the land can already do, or what the climate will demand tomorrow?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the challenge: in teams, students design and pitch a sustainable farming plan for an imagined Southern African community, drawing on subsistence, commercial, and climate-smart farming content from across the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Planning and Pitching (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams plan a farm that balances family food security (subsistence, Week 2), income opportunity (commercial farming, Week 3), and resilience to drought and changing climate (Week 4's water challenges, Week 9's climate-smart techniques).</li><li>Each team pitches their plan in 2–3 minutes, explaining what they chose to grow, how they manage water, and how their plan is climate-smart.</li><li>Key Question for the audience after each pitch: &quot;What is the strongest, most realistic part of this plan?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores based on the rubric. Discuss which pitched plans balanced ALL three goals most convincingly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name the ONE choice in your team's farming plan you're most confident is genuinely sustainable.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus praises the servant who takes what he's been given and puts it to good, faithful use, growing it rather than burying it out of fear. A well-planned farm for the future is exactly that kind of faithful stewardship — taking what the land offers and using it wisely, for the long term.</li><li>Verse: &quot;His master replied, &quot;Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things.&quot;&quot; — <em>Matthew 25:21 (NIV, excerpt)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Planning a farm that's genuinely sustainable, not just profitable in the short term, is real faithful stewardship — using what God has given wisely, for the sake of a future we won't fully see ourselves.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>The servant in the parable was praised for being faithful with what he had, not for having the most. How does that change what &quot;success&quot; looks like in your team's farming plan?</li><li>Which part of your farming plan required you to trust something you couldn't fully control, like rainfall?</li><li>What is one resource in YOUR own life you could be more faithful in using well, for the long term rather than just right now?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recap notes/images from Weeks 2, 3, 4 and 9 farming lessons</li><li>Planning template for the farming plan pitch</li><li>A visible scoreboard/rubric</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Design a Farm Plan Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a labelled poster showing your team's farming plan, including crops, water management, and climate-smart features.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which part of your poster would be most convincing to a real farmer?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Geography MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE farming or geography concept from this whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did that concept help shape your team's plan today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Farm Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a diagram or slide presenting your team's farming plan for the future.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did presenting it digitally add that a hand-drawn poster couldn't?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Farming Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own farming-related work from Weeks 2, 3, 4 and 9. What's one idea from your earlier work that made it into today's plan?</p></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: The Legacy Trial — Station 3: Design a Conservation Plan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">DESIGN A CONSERVATION PLAN — Protecting an Endangered Species of Choice</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Living World, Ecology: apply understanding of adaptation, food webs and endangered species to design a coherent conservation plan.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are applying our understanding of food webs, adaptation and real conservation strategies to design a plan for one endangered species.</li><li>We are working as a team to justify our plan using real science content from this term.</li><li>I can explain how at least two parts of my conservation plan address a real threat to my chosen species.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This IS a capstone assessment for the term's Science/conservation content. Assess team plans against a simple rubric: scientific accuracy, use of real term content, and coherence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tapiwa turns his wooden elephant carving over once in his pocket, then sets it down deliberately, like he's done every big moment this term. &quot;One trial left before Thursday's Expo and the ceremony,&quot; he says. &quot;Today, design a real conservation plan — properly, like you're the ranger in charge. Choose your animal. Protect it properly.&quot; Nomvula: &quot;Everything you've learned this whole term — food webs, adaptation, real threats — it all leads here.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: if you could only choose ONE strategy to protect an endangered animal — protecting its habitat, stopping poaching, or community education — which would you choose first, and why?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Station Setup (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Explain the challenge: in teams, students choose one endangered Southern African species studied this term (e.g. the rhino) or another real species of their choice, and design a conservation plan addressing a real threat it faces.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Designing the Plan (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teams identify their chosen species' real threats (habitat loss, poaching, climate change, human-wildlife conflict) and design a plan combining at least two real strategies drawn from this term's learning (anti-poaching technology, food web/habitat protection, beehive-fence-style win-win solutions, community conservancies).</li><li>Each team presents their plan briefly, explaining the threat, their strategies, and why they'd work, using real reasoning from across the term.</li><li>Key Question for the audience after each pitch: &quot;What is the most scientifically sound part of this plan?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Debrief (5 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Reveal scores based on the rubric. Discuss which plans best combined multiple real strategies rather than relying on just one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: name the real threat your team's chosen species faces, and your plan's main strategy against it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: The Psalm declares that the whole earth, and everything in it, belongs to the Lord — not to us to use however we please. Designing a genuine conservation plan is a small act of taking that ownership seriously: caring for what was never fully ours to begin with.</li><li>Verse: &quot;The earth is the Lord's, and everything in it, the world, and all who live in it.&quot; — <em>Psalm 24:1 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Pānga  Impact — Designing a real conservation plan for a real species is genuine Kingdom impact — small hands taking seriously that the earth, and everything in it, ultimately belongs to God.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Psalm 24 says the earth and everything in it belongs to God, not to us. How does that change how a conservation plan should be designed?</li><li>Which part of your team's plan required the most careful scientific thinking, and why?</li><li>If the whole earth is God's, what responsibility do you think that gives US toward it, right now, at your age?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recap notes/images from Weeks 1, 3, 4, 6 and 9 (food webs, predators, rhino crisis, elephants, conservation wins)</li><li>Planning template for the conservation plan</li><li>A visible scoreboard/rubric</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Illustrate Your Conservation Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled diagram showing your team's chosen species, its main threat, and your plan's key strategies.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which strategy in your diagram do you think would be hardest to actually put into practice?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: My Science MVP Moment</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on the ONE science or conservation concept from this whole term you understood best.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80–120 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How did that concept shape your team's conservation plan today?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Build a Digital Conservation Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Using a simple digital tool, build a slide or diagram presenting your team's conservation plan.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did presenting it digitally make easier to explain than speaking alone?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review Your Own Conservation Work</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own work from Week 4 (rhino crisis) and Week 9 (conservation wins). What's one idea from your earlier work that made it into today's plan?</p></div></div>"},"Thursday — Technology: The Legacy Trial — Station 4: The Route-Keepers' Invention":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE ROUTE-KEEPERS' INVENTION — Conservation Tech Expo Showcase</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technology</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Technological Practice: evaluate an outcome against the need or opportunity it was developed for, using feedback.</li><li>Nature of Technology: understand how technology reflects the values and priorities of the people who create it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to present a finished (or model/prototype) technology idea clearly, explaining the problem it solves and how.</li><li>We are learning to evaluate our own and others' work honestly, using the term's learning as the standard.</li><li>I can reflect on how the whole term's Technology learning has changed the way I think about protecting animals.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation and rubric-based assessment of the Expo showcase presentations, evaluating clarity of problem definition, technical reasoning, and use of the term's learning. Peer feedback recorded.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook lies open one final time, every leg of the route glowing faintly across its pages — Hwange, the Zambezi, the reserve, the border, Gogo Thandiwe's village, all the way home. &quot;Sharp, crew,&quot; Tapiwa says, and for once his voice doesn't crack a joke early. &quot;Sekuru Josiah always said a route only really matters if someone else can fly it after you. So today... you show us what you'd build.&quot; Nomvula, bracelet finished at last, adds quietly: &quot;That's the pattern of it. You're not visitors on this route anymore.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: after everything you've learned this term, what would YOU build, right now, if you knew it could genuinely help protect one real animal?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Final Preparation (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Give students final time to complete their model, prototype, poster or diagram from last week's plan, using the planning template as their guide.</li><li>Remind the class of the success criteria: clearly explain the problem, the technology idea, why it would work, and one honest limitation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Conservation Tech Expo Showcase (20 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: students present their finished (or in-progress) Conservation Tech Expo project in small groups or to the whole class, in the style of a real expo — explaining their chosen animal, the specific problem, and how their idea helps.</li><li>Audience members ask at least one genuine question per presentation, echoing the challenge-and-improve culture built since Week 5's Boss Battle.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Reflecting on the Whole Term (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: Guide a class discussion connecting the dots across the whole term's Technology strand — from San spoor-reading in Week 1 to today's original inventions — asking what stayed the same about GOOD technology the whole way through (it solves a real problem, for a real need, honestly evaluated).</li><li>Key Question: &quot;What is one thing you now understand about technology that you didn't understand in Week 1?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence: what is the single most useful thing you learned this term about how technology helps protect animals?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: Jesus said to let our light shine, not for our own praise, but so that others would see good works and glorify God because of them. The Conservation Tech Expo is exactly that kind of moment — the class's best effort, offered outward, for something and Someone bigger than themselves.</li><li>Verse: &quot;Let your light shine before others, so they may see your good deeds.&quot; — <em>Matthew 5:16 (NIV, shortened)</em></li><li>Value: Manaaki  Serve — Showcasing our best work isn't about being praised for talent — it's Manaaki completed: using our gifts fully, and letting that service point beyond ourselves.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Why do you think Jesus said to let our good works be SEEN, instead of keeping them hidden — when so much of the Bible also talks about humility?</li><li>Tapiwa and Nomvula say the class isn't just &quot;visitors on this route&quot; anymore. What do you think they mean by that?</li><li>Looking back over the whole term's Technology learning — from tracking to today's invention — what is one thing you'd want to &quot;pass on&quot; to someone else, the way Sekuru Josiah passed his knowledge on?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Student Conservation Tech Expo projects/prototypes/posters from Week 9 planning</li><li>Simple presentation/assessment rubric</li><li>Peer question prompt cards, if helpful for shy presenters</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Suggested Videos</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><em>Real conservation technology innovations</em> — search: &quot;new wildlife conservation technology innovations&quot;. A closing, forward-looking video connecting the class's own inventions to real, ongoing innovation in the field.</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Finalise Your Expo Display</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Complete a polished, labelled final version of your project's poster, model or diagram for the showcase.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What one change did you make since last week's first draft, and why?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: Write Your Term Reflection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a short reflection on how your understanding of conservation technology changed across the term, from Week 1's tracking lesson to today.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">120–180 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What surprised you most about the term's Technology learning?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Record a Short Pitch Video/Audio</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If equipment allows, record a short (under 1 minute) pitch of your project, as if presenting to a real conservation organisation.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What did explaining it out loud make you realise you needed to clarify?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Watch and Note: Real Conservation Innovation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Watch a short video on current real-world conservation technology innovations.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Search term</th><td class=\"lt-td\">&quot;new wildlife conservation technology innovations&quot;</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Which real innovation is most similar to your own Expo project?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Friday — Art: Boss Battle 2 Finale: The Baobab Route Artwork":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">THE BAOBAB ROUTE ARTWORK — A Whole-Term Legacy Piece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visual Arts, Communicating and Interpreting: synthesise ideas, techniques and traditions explored across a sustained body of work into one cohesive final piece.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Participating and Contributing; Relating to Others</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are drawing on every art form studied this term to create one final &quot;Baobab Route&quot; artwork.</li><li>We are creating this piece as a class legacy, not for personal praise, echoing the term's motto.</li><li>I can explain how my contribution connects to at least one art tradition studied this term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher observation of the collaborative creation process and final class discussion; review of the finished artwork for genuine synthesis of the term's art traditions, offered as a whole-class legacy piece.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The logbook's pages ripple slightly, as if the whole route is holding its breath. &quot;That's the pattern of it, crew — everything we've learned, everywhere we've been, all leading somewhere,&quot; Tapiwa says. Nomvula holds up her bracelet, finished at last, bead by bead across the whole term. &quot;Today, we make one final piece together — a legacy, not just a picture. Everything you've learned about art this term, all in one place.&quot;</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wonder question: after a whole term of learning, making and discovering, who do you think a final piece like this is really FOR?</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Recalling the Term's Art (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Teacher Input: briefly recap every art tradition explored this term — San rock art, Shona stone sculpture, Ndebele house painting, beadwork and colour symbolism, township murals, weaving and Shweshwe textiles, music and dance, and mask and carving traditions.</li><li>Key Question: &quot;Which of these traditions do you think will be hardest to represent within just ONE shared artwork?&quot;</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Creating the Baobab Route Artwork (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Activity: as a class, create one large final artwork (a class mural, a collaborative canvas, or joined individual sections) shaped like or centred on a baobab tree, with each student contributing one element inspired by an art tradition studied this term, connecting it back to that tradition.</li><li>Circulate and prompt: &quot;Which tradition does your contribution draw from, and how does it connect to the pieces around it?&quot;</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Exit Ticket</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write one sentence explaining which art tradition your contribution to the Baobab Route Artwork draws from, and why you chose it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ponder: After a whole term of learning, making and discovering, this final artwork isn't about being praised for talent — it's about offering the best of what we've learned back to God, the way Nomvula's grandmother says every good pattern points back to its maker.</li><li>Verse: &quot;So whether you eat or drink or whatever you do, do it all for the glory of God.&quot; — <em>1 Corinthians 10:31 (NIV)</em></li><li>Value: Aroha &amp; the whole-term motto, Soli Deo Gloria — Bringing together everything learned this term into one final piece is a chance to practise Manaaki — using our gifts — for God's glory alone, not our own.</li><li>Discussion questions:<ul class='lt-sublist'><li>Why do you think our school motto is &quot;To God Alone the Glory&quot; and not &quot;to me&quot;?</li><li>Tapiwa and Nomvula now call the class &quot;Route-Keepers.&quot; What is God calling YOU to keep and pass on?</li><li>Looking back over the whole term — Aroha, Mōhio, Manaaki, Pānga — which one did you grow in the most, and why?</li></ul></li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A large shared surface (mural paper, canvas, or joined individual sheets) shaped or themed around a baobab tree</li><li>Paint, markers, beads, fabric scraps and other materials reflecting the term's art traditions</li><li>Quick recap images from each art tradition studied this term, for reference</li></ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Art: Add a Personal Detail</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Once the shared artwork is complete, add one small personal detail nearby (a name tag, a tiny symbol) marking your specific contribution.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What made you choose that detail to represent your part?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Read/Write: The Legacy Piece's Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write out, in full sentences, the story of how the whole class's Baobab Route Artwork came together, and what it represents.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Word Count Guide</th><td class=\"lt-td\">100–150 words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What do you hope someone looking at this artwork next year would understand about your class?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Technology: Document the Artwork</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>If equipment allows, photograph the finished class artwork and write a short digital caption explaining its meaning.</p></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Reflection Prompt</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What was hardest to capture in a photo that was easy to see in person?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Watch &amp; Review: Review the Whole Term's Art</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back at your own art pieces from across the whole term, from Week 1's San-inspired symbol to today. Which piece are you proudest of, and why?</p></div></div>"}}}}},"Persia: The Royal Road Race":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: The Gates of the Plateau":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Before the Empire: Elamites, Medes and Persians":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Before the Empire: Elamites, Medes and Persians — Who walked here first — and how can we be sure?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 1: The Gates of the Plateau (up to c. 550 BC) · Zagros Mountains, Ecbatana, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-R — English: Reading. Students read informational and narrative texts and make meaning from them.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe who lived in ancient Persia and how we know about them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name three peoples (Elamites, Medes, Persians).</li><li>I can explain what a source is and give an example.</li><li>I can say why we use more than one source.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A ‘source-use’ sentence (‘I know X because of source Y’), the Path product (photographed) and observation notes from the Council Fire discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names some peoples or sources with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes the three peoples accurately and gives an example of a source in their own words.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why historians use more than one source, using evidence and vocabulary precisely.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates a source’s bias and compares two perspectives; applies the idea to a new context (e.g. NZ).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>source</b> — Anything that tells us about the past (an object, a text, a picture, a story).</li><li><b>archaeologist</b> — A scientist who studies the past by digging up and examining objects.</li><li><b>artefact</b> — An object made by people long ago.</li><li><b>cuneiform</b> — Wedge-shaped writing pressed into wet clay with a reed.</li><li><b>bias</b> — When someone’s own side or opinion shapes how they tell a story.</li><li><b>Elamites</b> — People of the ancient kingdom of Elam in south-west Iran (capital: Susa).</li><li><b>Medes</b> — People of north-western Iran; their capital was Ecbatana.</li><li><b>Persians</b> — People from Persis (Fars) in the southern Zagros; Cyrus rises among them about 559 BC.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Dig Envelopes</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Courier! Shirin here. Last night I crept into a ruined caravan stop and found three sealed Dig Envelopes hidden under a loose stone. Inside each one: a scrap of clay tablet, a painted pottery shard, and a copied map. And look — silver hoofprints in the dust, heading east. Someone else is hunting the Seal. Open your envelope, Courier. Be a detective! Who made these things? How old are they? And how can you be sure?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: prepare one Dig Envelope per team (see Materials). Form teams (‘Caravans’) of 4–5, mixed ability — students keep these teams all term. Tell them to remember their Caravan: Thursday they design its crest.</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud (or use the app’s read-aloud). Pause at ‘Be a detective!’ and let the drama land.</li><li>Teams examine their envelope for 4 minutes and record on a mini-whiteboard: Who made this? How old might it be? How could we check?</li><li>Take two quick shares. Praise careful language: ‘I think… because…’. Do NOT correct guesses yet — the Discovery will.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria (kid-language, on screen). Students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> First lesson of the term — no retrieval. Starting Tuesday, open with 2–3 retrieval questions from the previous day on mini-whiteboards.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dig deeper, Courier. Three peoples, four kinds of evidence — and one big question: whom can we trust?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline strip (5 min): unroll a strip on the floor/wall: Elam &amp; Susa → Medes (Ecbatana) → Persians (Cyrus c. 559 BC). Add date cards as you teach. Use the app’s interactive timeline on the big screen.</li><li>Sources (5 min): introduce the four source types. Run the app’s Source Sorter as a whole-class game (hands up / A–D cards). Spend 1 minute on Herodotus: ‘Why might a Greek writer see Persia differently from a Persian scribe?’</li><li>Persia vs Iran (2 min): use the flip card. Keep the tone respectful. Invite Iranian-NZ students to share only if they wish — never put a child on the spot as a ‘cultural expert’.</li><li>Model one source sentence on the board: ‘I know the Elamites lived at Susa because archaeologists have dug up clay tablets there.’ Students write their own on a mini-whiteboard.</li><li><b>Three peoples, one plateau:</b> <b>Elamites</b> lived in the south-west, around the city of <b>Susa</b> — some of the earliest city life in the world, thousands of years before Cyrus (about 4000–2700 BC). <b>Medes</b> lived in the north-west mountains; their capital was <b>Ecbatana</b> (today the city of Hamadan). <b>Persians</b> came from <b>Persis</b> in the southern Zagros. About <b>559 BC</b> a Persian called <b>Cyrus</b> began to rise… but that is next week’s story.</li><li><b>How do we know? Four kinds of source:</b> <b>Archaeology</b> (objects dug from the ground) · <b>Written records</b> (clay tablets in cuneiform) · <b>Greek historians</b> (like <b>Herodotus</b> — he wrote about Persia, but he was Greek and often writing about his people’s rivals, so he is <i>not neutral</i>) · <b>The Bible</b> (an ancient text that names Elam and Persia).</li><li><b>Why more than one source?:</b> A single source can leave things out or tell only one side. When two or three <i>different</i> kinds of source agree, we can be more confident. When they disagree, that is a clue too — a good historian asks <i>why?</i></li><li><b>Seven coloured walls?:</b> Herodotus said Ecbatana had <b>seven walls</b>, each a different colour — white, black, purple, blue, orange, silver and gold! It is a magnificent picture. But archaeologists have not been able to confirm it. A great example of why we check our sources.</li><li><b>Persia or Iran?:</b> In <b>1935</b> the country asked the world to call it <b>Iran</b>. The name <b>Persia</b> comes from <b>Persis</b> (also called Pars or Fars), the home region of the Persians. Both names matter, and we treat both with respect — Persian history belongs to all the peoples and the people of Iran today.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>dig</li><li>timeline</li><li>sources</li><li>persiairan</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Archaeologist’s Field Report:</b> Choose ONE artefact from your Dig Envelope. Examine it like a real archaeologist and complete a Field Report: what you see, what you think, what you still wonder, and where you could check.</li><li><b>Path B — Timeline Scroll:</b> Build a 2–3 metre class timeline from Elam to Cyrus. Each student creates ONE segment — a date, a caption and a picture — and the whole class joins them into a scroll.</li><li><b>Path C — Voice of the Plateau (radio bulletin):</b> Record a 90-second news bulletin announcing the rise of the Persians among the Medes. Include a ‘source’ credit so listeners know how we know.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 10:22; Acts 17:26–27 — Genesis 10 lists the families of the nations, and Elam is named there as one of Shem’s sons. In Acts 17, Paul says God made every nation from one person and set the times and places where peoples would live, so that they would reach out and look for Him.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it mean that God set the times and places of the nations? What does it tell us about how God sees every people group — including the peoples we are studying?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> How does the Bible fit with archaeology as a source? Where do they agree (Elam is real and in the right region)? Where does each tell only part of the story? (Look out for the Elamites, Medes and Parthians together in Acts 2:9 — we will meet them again!)</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write ONE sentence thanking God for the people groups of the world, and ONE thing you will do to treat other peoples’ stories carefully.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Archaeologist’s Field Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose ONE artefact from your Dig Envelope. Examine it like a real archaeologist and complete a Field Report: what you see, what you think, what you still wonder, and where you could check.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew or described my artefact clearly</li><li>I wrote 3 things I can SEE (facts)</li><li>I wrote what I THINK and gave a ‘because’</li><li>I wrote at least one thing I still WONDER</li><li>I named one place I could CHECK (a second source)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the word bank (clay · tablet · wedge-shaped · pottery · pattern · ancient) and the sentence frames above. Work beside a partner for the ‘Where could I check?’ box.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete all four boxes in full sentences and name one specific kind of source you would use to check.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare TWO artefacts. Which gives better evidence about everyday life? Explain how a single artefact could mislead an archaeologist.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>What I see:</b> I can see… I notice… <b>What I think:</b> I think this was made by… because… <b>What I still wonder:</b> I wonder why… how… <b>Where could I check?</b> I could check by looking at… (a museum website, an atlas, a second source).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Timeline Scroll</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a 2–3 metre class timeline from Elam to Cyrus. Each student creates ONE segment — a date, a caption and a picture — and the whole class joins them into a scroll.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My segment has a date or date range</li><li>My caption is 1–2 clear sentences in my own words</li><li>My picture matches my caption</li><li>I wrote which source tells us this (archaeology, tablet, Herodotus, Bible)</li><li>My segment fits in the right order on the strip</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose a segment from the list. Use the caption frame and pick a picture from the picture bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write the caption yourself, include a date, a drawing and your source credit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add an ‘inference panel’ at the end of your segment: What do we NOT know yet? How might an archaeologist find out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Segments to share out:</b> (1) Susa grows into one of the world’s earliest cities (about 4000–2700 BC) · (2) The kingdom of Elam · (3) The Medes and their capital Ecbatana · (4) The Persians in Persis · (5) About 559 BC: Cyrus begins to rise · (6) Today: archaeologists keep digging. <b>Caption frame:</b> About ___, the ___ … We know this from ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Voice of the Plateau (radio bulletin)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Record a 90-second news bulletin announcing the rise of the Persians among the Medes. Include a ‘source’ credit so listeners know how we know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My bulletin has a headline and a sign-off (‘This is Voice of the Plateau’)</li><li>I named WHO, WHERE and WHEN</li><li>I gave a ‘source’ credit (‘according to the clay tablets…’)</li><li>It lasts about 90 seconds</li><li>I spoke clearly, with expression</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the script frame with a partner; read it aloud twice before recording.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own script with a source credit. Use at least two vocabulary words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second voice: a Greek reporter AND a Persian scribe describing the same event differently — then explain why they differ.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Headline:</b> ‘Breaking news from the plateau!’ <b>Who/Where/When:</b> The Persians of Persis… under the Medes… before 550 BC. <b>Source credit:</b> According to ___, … <b>Sign-off:</b> This is ___, Voice of the Plateau.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 10:22; Acts 17:26–27; Acts 2:9 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Genesis 10 is often called the ‘Table of Nations’: it shows how peoples are connected rather than giving a modern anthropology. Say honestly: ‘Christians read some details differently.’ Keep the focus on what the passage affirms — God’s care for every nation.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How do people in Aotearoa record the past — whakapapa, pūrākau, photographs, archives, museums? Which ‘sources’ would a historian use 2,000 years from now? (Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū for local knowledge; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Greek writers like Herodotus described Persians through their own perspective — name this as bias rather than ‘fact’. Treat the names Persia and Iran respectfully. Some students may be Iranian-NZ or from Muslim backgrounds; never single them out.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dig envelopes (see Materials)</li><li>Timeline strip + date cards</li><li>Source-sorter cards (app) or printed set</li><li>Persia ↔ Iran flip card (app)</li><li>Path A field-report sheet (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Recording device or tablet for Path C</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did students already know about ‘how we know the past’?</li><li>Which students could name a source AND explain its limits?</li><li>What will I change in tomorrow’s map work to build on today’s source skills?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: The Great Plateau: Reading the Land":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Great Plateau: Reading the Land — Why did people build here — and not there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Settlements &amp; Cities</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 1: The Gates of the Plateau (up to c. 550 BC) · Zagros Mountains, Ecbatana, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to locate Persia and explain why people settled where they did.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can find the Zagros, Alborz, Caspian Sea, Persian Gulf and deserts on a map.</li><li>I can use a compass and scale.</li><li>I can give two reasons people settle in a place.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Map conventions checklist; the reasoning in Path C (three reasons); photographs of Path products.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Locates one or two features with support and a prompt.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Locates the main features accurately and uses a compass and scale correctly.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why people settle in a place using water, soil, defence and trade as evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares two settlement choices (Persian &amp; local) and evaluates trade-offs.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>plateau</b> — A large, high, fairly flat area of land.</li><li><b>qanat</b> — A gently sloping underground tunnel that carries water from the mountains to a village.</li><li><b>settlement</b> — A place where people live together.</li><li><b>settlement hierarchy</b> — Settlements ranked by size: farm → hamlet → village → town → city.</li><li><b>compass rose</b> — The map symbol showing north, south, east and west.</li><li><b>scale</b> — How map distance relates to real distance.</li><li><b>Zagros / Alborz</b> — Iran’s western and northern mountain ranges.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Caravan Tokens on the Floor-Map</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The silver hoofprints led us to a vast map spread across the floor of the old map-room! Shirin snaps her fingers: ‘Every caravan needs a navigator. Move your token — and use a compass, Courier, or you’ll end up in the salt desert!’ Remember yesterday’s artefacts? Where on this plateau might they have come from?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): three mini-whiteboard questions from Monday (name two peoples; what is a source; why use more than one?).</li><li>Floor map: each team places a caravan token at the start. Call directions: ‘Move NE to the mountains. Now 3 squares south…’ Teams take turns to be the navigator and call a direction.</li><li>Link: ‘Yesterday’s pottery shard — where might it have been found? Why there?’ Take predictions without comment.</li><li>Share LI/SC (students read aloud).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: three peoples; what a source is; why we use more than one.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The plateau is a high, wild land — ringed by mountains, sprinkled with deserts, edged by two seas. Read the land like a Courier!</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Landforms (6 min): label the Zagros, Alborz, Kavir, Lut, Caspian and Gulf on the big map as you talk. Students point to each on their own map.</li><li>Qanat (4 min): run the animated qanat in the app. Ask: ‘Why shafts? Why a gentle slope? What would happen if the slope were steep?’</li><li>Site selection (3 min): water · soil · defence · trade. Mini-whiteboards: ‘Name the best of these for a village near a river.’</li><li>Settlement ladder (2 min): link to local examples — choose from your own rohe/region (e.g. a high-country farm → a small village → a town → Christchurch).</li><li><b>A high land ringed by mountains:</b> The <b>Iranian Plateau</b> is high land surrounded by mountains. The <b>Zagros</b> run down the <b>west</b>; the <b>Alborz</b> curve across the <b>north</b> (Iran’s highest peak, <b>Damavand</b>, is about 5,600 m). In the middle and east lie two great deserts: the <b>Dasht-e Kavir</b> (Great Salt Desert) and the <b>Dasht-e Lut</b>. The <b>Caspian Sea</b> lies to the north and the <b>Persian Gulf</b> to the south. Iran is about <b>six times</b> the size of Aotearoa New Zealand!</li><li><b>Water is life: rivers and qanats:</b> Rain falls in the mountains but much of the plateau is dry. People learned to bring water to their fields by <b>qanats</b> — gently sloping tunnels dug underground from a ‘mother well’ in the foothills to a village below. The water flows by gravity (no pump!) and, because it is underground, less of it evaporates in the hot sun. Some qanats are thousands of years old and still work today.</li><li><b>Why here? Four questions for a settlement:</b> People choose a place by asking: <b>Water</b> — is there a river, spring or qanat? <b>Soil</b> — can we grow food? <b>Defence</b> — can we protect ourselves? <b>Trade</b> — are there paths to meet other peoples? The best sites answer several of them.</li><li><b>A settlement ladder:</b> Settlements come in sizes: a <b>farm</b> → a <b>hamlet</b> → a <b>village</b> → a <b>town</b> → a <b>city</b>. In the foothills of the Zagros, early farming <b>villages</b> grew. Susa, in the south-west, grew into a <b>city</b>. A city is more than size — it has leaders, markets, specialist workers and often walls.</li><li><b>Plateau vs Southern Alps:</b> Our own Southern Alps rise to <b>Aoraki/Mt Cook</b> (3,724 m). Damavand in Iran’s Alborz is about 5,600 m — and the plateau’s mountains are far wider. Both ranges bring rain and snow to one side and leave a drier land on the other.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>plateauMap</li><li>qanat</li><li>siteGame</li><li>ladder</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Relief Map of the Plateau:</b> Build a salt-dough or papier-mâché relief map of the plateau. Show the mountain ranges, deserts and seas, and add labels.</li><li><b>Path B — Journey Map of the Race:</b> Draw (or build digitally) a map with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Show the route of the race and label 8 places.</li><li><b>Path C — “Why Here?” Site Selection:</b> Look at a map card showing resources and hazards. Choose the best village site and justify your choice with three reasons. Then compare it with how people chose pā or kāinga sites in your rohe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Acts 17:26–27; Psalm 24:1 — Paul says God chose the exact places where peoples would live. The psalmist sings that the earth and everything in it belongs to the Lord — the world, and all who live in it.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> If the earth is the Lord’s, how should we treat the places where we live — our school grounds, our rivers, our maunga?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Compare what you learned about settling (water, soil, defence, trade) with the Acts text — ‘exact places’. Does careful geography make God’s care less real, or help us see it more clearly?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> ‘Place thanks’: name ONE feature of your local landscape you will care for this term, and say it aloud to a partner.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Relief Map of the Plateau</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a salt-dough or papier-mâché relief map of the plateau. Show the mountain ranges, deserts and seas, and add labels.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I shaped the Zagros (west) and Alborz (north)</li><li>I showed the Dasht-e Kavir and Dasht-e Lut</li><li>I showed the Caspian Sea and the Persian Gulf</li><li>I added labels and a small key</li><li>I can explain one way the land shapes where people live</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made base outline and a label bank; the teacher marks mountain positions with chalk first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build and label independently; add a key with three symbols.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a cross-section strip showing height from the Gulf up over the Zagros; explain how height affects settlement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label bank:</b> Zagros · Alborz · Dasht-e Kavir · Dasht-e Lut · Caspian Sea · Persian Gulf · Susa · Ecbatana. <b>Say it:</b> People live near ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Journey Map of the Race</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw (or build digitally) a map with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Show the route of the race and label 8 places.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title</li><li>Compass rose (N, S, E, W)</li><li>Scale bar</li><li>Key with symbols</li><li>Route of the race drawn clearly</li><li>8 labelled places</li><li>Neat, readable labels</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map; trace the route and add compass, scale bar and four labels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with all conventions and 8 labels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a scale conversion panel (1 cm = ? km) and calculate the distance of one stage of the route.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Map conventions checklist:</b> T · A · L · K · E · S (Title · Arrow/compass · Legend · Key · Edge/border · Scale). <b>8 places to choose from:</b> Zagros · Alborz · Susa · Ecbatana · Kavir · Lut · Caspian · Gulf.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: “Why Here?” Site Selection</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look at a map card showing resources and hazards. Choose the best village site and justify your choice with three reasons. Then compare it with how people chose pā or kāinga sites in your rohe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I chose ONE site</li><li>I gave 3 reasons (water, soil, defence, trade…)</li><li>I named one hazard and how people could manage it</li><li>I compared with a pā/kāinga site in my rohe (using local guidance)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pick from the reason bank (water · soil · defence · trade) and complete the frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Give three reasons and one hazard with a way to manage it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank the four sites from best to worst and write a short persuasive paragraph; compare with a local example.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Reason frame:</b> I chose Site __ because (1) …, (2) …, (3) …. <b>Hazard:</b> One risk is …, but people could … <b>Compare:</b> In our rohe, people chose sites near …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Acts 17:26–27; Ps 24:1 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Avoid implying that geography ‘proves’ the Bible. Frame it as noticing: the Creator’s world has patterns we can study. Pair with kaitiakitanga language.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare the Zagros with the Southern Alps (rain on one side, dry on the other). How did the choices of early settlers in your rohe depend on water, food, defence and travel routes? Use local guidance.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep references to modern Iran neutral and respectful; this week is about land, not politics.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Giant floor map + caravan tokens</li><li>Atlases, compasses, rulers</li><li>Salt dough / papier-mâché / card bases</li><li>Site-selection cards (app or printed)</li><li>Qanat diagram (app)</li><li>Local-area map for Path C comparison</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which map conventions did most students forget?</li><li>Could students explain WHY a site is good, or only name features?</li><li>What scaffolds helped the supported group most?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: What Is a Habitat? The Zagros Mountain Community":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What Is a Habitat? The Zagros Mountain Community — Whose footprints are these? (Not Gandom’s.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Animal Communities &amp; Habitats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 1: The Gates of the Plateau (up to c. 550 BC) · Zagros Mountains, Ecbatana, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-LW-LP — Science: Living World — Life Processes. Students understand that all living things have requirements to stay alive.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain what a habitat and a community are and how animals suit mountain habitats.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can define habitat, population, community and ecosystem.</li><li>I can match 3 Zagros animals to their adaptations.</li><li>I can record observations clearly.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Vocabulary use; accuracy of the adaptation–habitat link; clarity of diagrams and tally charts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names an animal and one feature with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes three adaptations correctly and uses ‘habitat’ and ‘community’ accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how each adaptation helps survival in the Zagros and links animals in a community.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Predicts how a change (introduced species, drought) would affect the community.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>habitat</b> — The natural home of a living thing.</li><li><b>population</b> — All the living things of ONE kind in a place (e.g. all the ibex on one mountain).</li><li><b>community</b> — All the different populations living together in one place.</li><li><b>ecosystem</b> — A community PLUS its non-living surroundings (rock, water, air, sunlight, temperature).</li><li><b>adaptation</b> — A feature or behaviour that helps a living thing survive in its habitat.</li><li><b>abiotic</b> — Non-living parts of a habitat (rock, water, temperature).</li><li><b>carnivore / herbivore / omnivore</b> — Eats meat / eats plants / eats both.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Mystery Footprints</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dawn on the Zagros. Cold air, thin and sharp. And there, in a patch of mud by the stream — footprints! Four sets, all different. Shirin crouches: ‘Tracks tell stories, Courier. Whose are they? Be careful — Gandom has already guessed… and she has been wrong every single time.’ Gandom the camel lifts her chin proudly. She chewed the map again.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — name a mountain range; what is a qanat; give two reasons people settle in a place.</li><li>Lay out the four Mystery Footprint cards on the floor/projector (ibex hoof, leopard paw, bear paw with claw marks, eagle foot). Teams discuss: ‘Which animal? How do you know?’</li><li>Use the app’s Footprint game: reveal one at a time. Gandom ‘guesses’ first — and is wrong. (Laugh with the class.)</li><li>Share LI/SC; students tick off the vocabulary as it appears.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: Zagros &amp; Alborz; qanat; why people settle where they do.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Meet the Zagros community — four animals, one mountain home, and a whole ecosystem of rock, water and sunlight.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Vocabulary (5 min): build the five-word ladder together: habitat → population → community → ecosystem → adaptation. Use a concrete example: ‘all the ibex on one mountain = population; ibex + leopard + eagle + oaks = community; add rock + stream + weather = ecosystem’.</li><li>Zagros community (5 min): use the app’s interactive habitat scene — tap each animal and plant to learn its adaptation. Students record on their fact-file.</li><li>Adaptation game (3 min): ‘Hooves for what? Spots for what? Claws for what?’ Quick-fire pairs.</li><li>Introduce Animal Cards (2 min). Remind students to look after them and bring them to Council Fire.</li><li><b>Five big words:</b> <b>Habitat</b> = the natural home of a living thing. <b>Population</b> = all one kind in a place. <b>Community</b> = all the different kinds together. <b>Ecosystem</b> = the community plus rock, water, air and temperature. <b>Adaptation</b> = a feature or behaviour that helps survival.</li><li><b>The Zagros community:</b> Oak woodland and grasses cover the slopes. Living here: the <b>bezoar ibex</b> (a wild goat), the <b>Persian leopard</b> (rare and endangered), the <b>brown bear</b> and the <b>golden eagle</b>. Their food chain: grass → ibex → leopard.</li><li><b>Built for the mountain:</b> <b>Ibex:</b> hard-rimmed, grippy hooves for steep rock; very long horns; a thick coat in winter. <b>Leopard:</b> spotted coat for camouflage; strong body for climbing. <b>Brown bear:</b> thick fur, powerful claws for digging, eats plants AND meat. <b>Golden eagle:</b> far sharper eyesight than ours, hooked beak and strong talons.</li><li><b>Animal Cards:</b> Each week you collect new <b>Animal Cards</b> — tilt them to see the holo shine! This week: ibex, leopard, bear and eagle.</li><li><b>Tahr in Aotearoa:</b> The ibex has a cousin on the other side of the world: the <b>Himalayan tahr</b>, introduced to the Southern Alps in the early 1900s. Why might an <i>introduced</i> species change a habitat? We will explore this again in Weeks 6 and 8.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>footprints</li><li>zagrosHabitat</li><li>vocabMatch</li><li>animalCards</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Animal Fact-File &amp; Adaptation Diagram:</b> Choose one Zagros animal. Make a fact-file with a labelled adaptation diagram — three adaptations, and why each helps it survive.</li><li><b>Path B — Mountain Habitat Diorama:</b> Build a mountain habitat diorama showing the community and the non-living (abiotic) parts: rock, water, temperature, sunlight.</li><li><b>Path C — Habitat Detectives (outdoors):</b> Run a quadrat survey of the school grounds. Sketch and tally what you find, then answer: Which Zagros animals could live here — and why or why not?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Psalm 104:16–18; Genesis 1:24–25 — The psalmist sings that the high mountains belong to the wild goats and the rocky cliffs are a refuge for the hyraxes — God looks after every creature in its own home. In Genesis, God makes the animals of the land, each according to its kind, and sees that it is good.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it say about God that He makes so many kinds of creatures, each in its own place?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What do you see in the adaptations — hooves, spots, eyes — that fits this? How does scientific observation help us notice what the Bible celebrates?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a two-line ‘Creation Wonder’ note (a thank-you or a question you want to ask the Creator).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Animal Fact-File &amp;amp; Adaptation Diagram</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose one Zagros animal. Make a fact-file with a labelled adaptation diagram — three adaptations, and why each helps it survive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Animal name + habitat</li><li>A clear, labelled diagram with 3 adaptations</li><li>I explained WHY each adaptation helps</li><li>I used the words habitat, community and adaptation</li><li>I wrote what it eats and what might eat it</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the pre-drawn outline of the animal and the label bank; complete the ‘has… which helps…’ frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently with three adaptations and explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a food chain and explain what would happen to the community if the animal disappeared.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Frame:</b> The ___ has ___ which helps it ___. <b>Diet:</b> It eats … It is a herbivore/carnivore/omnivore. <b>Community link:</b> It lives with …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Mountain Habitat Diorama</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a mountain habitat diorama showing the community and the non-living (abiotic) parts: rock, water, temperature, sunlight.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I showed at least 3 animals/plants</li><li>I labelled the abiotic parts (rock, water, temperature)</li><li>I labelled one adaptation for each animal</li><li>I can explain one way living and non-living parts depend on each other</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start from a shoebox with the sky and ground already painted; add and label three things.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Independently build and label with an explanation for each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Show a food web with arrows and explain what could change if the stream dried up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label bank:</b> bezoar ibex · Persian leopard · brown bear · golden eagle · oak · grass · stream · rock · snow. <b>Say it:</b> The ___ depends on ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Habitat Detectives (outdoors)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Run a quadrat survey of the school grounds. Sketch and tally what you find, then answer: Which Zagros animals could live here — and why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I placed my quadrat and recorded the location</li><li>I made a tally chart of what I found</li><li>I sketched what I saw</li><li>I compared our grounds to the Zagros with evidence</li><li>I wrote one thing I would investigate next</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made tally chart and pair with a buddy; hand lens provided.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own table, sketch and write a short comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the density per square metre and explain how a second quadrat would improve your data.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Table:</b> What I found | How many | Where. <b>Compare:</b> The Zagros animals could / could not live here because … <b>Next question:</b> I wonder …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 104:16–18; Gen 1:24–25 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Psalm 104 is a poem — celebrate it as praise, not a science textbook. Science explains HOW living things are suited to habitats; the psalm praises WHO made them and cares for them.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare the Zagros ibex with the NZ tahr (introduced). Why do introduced species matter? Link to NZ habitats you know — and to kaitiakitanga (guardianship of the environment).</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Persian leopard is rare and endangered — present this as a reason to care, not to panic. Keep the diet and food-chain talk matter-of-fact.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mystery Footprint cards (app or printed)</li><li>Animal Cards (collect weekly)</li><li>Quadrats / hula hoops, clipboards, hand lenses</li><li>Large paper for dioramas; recycled materials</li><li>Animal fact-file sheet</li><li>Photos of the four Zagros animals</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Which vocabulary words did students still confuse (population vs community)?</li><li>Did the outdoor quadrat produce usable data?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Week 2’s grassland community?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Pattern, Power & Symmetry: Rosettes, Lotus & Team Banners":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pattern, Power &amp; Symmetry: Rosettes, Lotus &amp; Team Banners — Make the pattern of kings — then make it yours.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Persian Traditions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 1: The Gates of the Plateau (up to c. 550 BC) · Zagros Mountains, Ecbatana, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to use symmetry and repeated pattern as Persian artists did.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can create a radial (rosette) pattern.</li><li>I can repeat a motif in a border.</li><li>I can explain why Persian palaces used patterns.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy of symmetry and repeat (mirror lines, even spacing); the artist’s statement (1–2 lines) in the Caravan Log.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a simple pattern with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Creates a symmetrical pattern with accurate repeats and explains one choice.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls radial symmetry/repeat precisely and explains why patterns were used in Persian palaces.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines patterns with intent, compares to other cultures and explains how design communicates identity.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>rosette</b> — A flower-shaped, round pattern with petals around a centre.</li><li><b>radial symmetry</b> — A pattern that repeats evenly around a centre point.</li><li><b>reflection (mirror) symmetry</b> — One half is the mirror image of the other.</li><li><b>motif</b> — A single design unit that is repeated (e.g. a lotus).</li><li><b>frieze / border</b> — A band of repeated pattern.</li><li><b>positive / negative shape</b> — The shape itself / the empty space around it.</li><li><b>palette</b> — The set of colours used (today: lapis, turquoise, gold).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Fold-and-Cut Challenge</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the terrace the stonecutters are working — chip, chip, chip. Patterns bloom across the stone: rosettes like suns, lotus flowers and buds in long, perfect rows. Shirin hands you a square of paper and a pair of scissors: ‘Fold it. Cut it. Open it. How many mirror lines can you find? Persian carvers made patterns like this to honour their king — and to show that order is beautiful.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — define habitat; name two Zagros animals; give one adaptation.</li><li>Show images/describe Persepolis rosettes and lotus-and-bud borders (use the app’s scene or printed pictures).</li><li>Fold-and-cut challenge (5 min): fold square paper into halves/quarters/eighths; cut a shape; open it. Count the lines of symmetry.</li><li>Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: habitat vs community; one adaptation of the ibex/eagle/leopard.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Patterns were power — and beauty. Learn the stonecutters’ secrets: symmetry, repetition and a very limited palette.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Symmetry (5 min): demonstrate reflection and radial symmetry with folds; count the lines of a rosette. Use the app’s Symmetry Lab on the big screen (tap &amp; draw in one wedge — see it mirror).</li><li>Positive/negative (3 min): cut a shape from black paper; show both pieces.</li><li>Palette &amp; purpose (4 min): look at images; discuss the three reasons for pattern (order, beauty, honour).</li><li>Model the first step of each Path (3 min); students choose A, B or C. Remind them: Path C is the Caravan’s identity for all ten weeks.</li><li><b>Two kinds of symmetry:</b> <b>Reflection symmetry:</b> a mirror line splits the pattern into matching halves. <b>Radial symmetry:</b> the pattern repeats evenly around a centre — like a flower or a rosette. A rosette with 8 petals has 8 mirror lines!</li><li><b>The motifs of Persepolis:</b> Carvers at Persepolis (begun about <b>518 BC</b>) repeated <b>rosettes</b>, <b>lotus flowers and buds</b> and rows of marching figures. Repetition creates <b>rhythm</b> — like a drumbeat you can see.</li><li><b>Positive and negative shape:</b> A <b>positive shape</b> is the thing; the <b>negative shape</b> is the space around it. Good pattern makers make BOTH interesting.</li><li><b>A limited palette:</b> Artists often used rich blues (<b>lapis</b>), <b>turquoise</b> and <b>gold</b>. Using just a few colours makes a design feel powerful and calm.</li><li><b>Why patterns?:</b> Patterns showed <b>order</b>, <b>beauty</b> and <b>honour to the king</b>. They also took great skill — the work of many craftspeople.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>symmetryLab</li><li>frieze</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Golden Rosette:</b> Use a compass and protractor to design a radial (rosette) pattern in gold paint on a lapis background.</li><li><b>Path B — Lotus Frieze:</b> Create a foam-print border by repeating a lotus motif six times with careful rhythm.</li><li><b>Path C — Team Banner &amp; Crest:</b> Design your Caravan’s crest using symmetrical motifs and an animal emblem. It becomes your team’s identity for all 10 weeks!</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Exodus 31:1–5 — God says He has chosen Bezalel and filled him with the Spirit — with skill, ability and knowledge — to design beautiful things in gold, silver, bronze, stone and wood, to be used in worship.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Is making beautiful things ‘spiritual’? Can craftsmanship be a gift from God?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persian carvers served kings; Bezalel’s craft served God’s worship. What is the difference? What is the same (skill, patience, beauty)?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Thank God for ONE artistic skill you have or admire in someone else. Write or draw it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Golden Rosette</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use a compass and protractor to design a radial (rosette) pattern in gold paint on a lapis background.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My centre is clear and circles are drawn with a compass</li><li>I divided the circle evenly (protractor)</li><li>My petals repeat evenly all the way round</li><li>I used lapis background and gold + turquoise accents</li><li>I can name the number of mirror lines in my design</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-marked circle with angles drawn; trace and paint petals.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compass-and-protractor design with your own petal shape.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Combine two rosettes of different sizes and explain how the repeated rhythm creates power.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Draw circles with a compass. 2 Mark equal angles (45° gives 8 petals; 30° gives 12). 3 Draw one petal. 4 Repeat. 5 Paint, light to dark. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My rosette has ___ petals. I used ___ to show ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Lotus Frieze</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a foam-print border by repeating a lotus motif six times with careful rhythm.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I designed my motif in foam</li><li>I printed it 6 times in a row</li><li>The spacing is even (rhythm)</li><li>I used the limited palette</li><li>I can explain why repetition creates rhythm</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a simple lotus template; practise printing on scrap first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own motif and print six repeats.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Alternate lotus and bud to make an A-B-A-B rhythm, then add a second border with a different pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch a lotus-and-bud motif. 2 Press lines into foam. 3 Roll paint thinly. 4 Print and repeat — rotate the motif for a mirror effect. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I repeated my motif ___ times because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Team Banner &amp;amp; Crest</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design your Caravan’s crest using symmetrical motifs and an animal emblem. It becomes your team’s identity for all 10 weeks!</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Crest uses reflection OR radial symmetry</li><li>One animal emblem (leopard, simurgh, lion…)</li><li>Limited palette (3–4 colours)</li><li>A short battle cry or creed word (not required to show text)</li><li>Everyone in the team contributed</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with a shield outline and a printed emblem; add motifs and colour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design the crest and banner as a team with a division of roles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write an ‘explainer card’ about your crest’s symbols and how they reflect your Caravan Creed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Choose the emblem. 2 Choose two motifs (rosette? lotus?). 3 Sketch with a mirror line down the centre. 4 Agree on colours. 5 Paint the banner together. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> Our crest shows ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Exod 31:1–5 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Be even-handed: Persian craftsmanship was genuinely excellent. The point is not that Persian art is ‘worse’ but to ask WHO the craft honours and why. Keep it open — ‘Christians think about this in different ways.’</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with kowhaiwhai and tukutuku — pattern as story and identity. Invite a local artist or your Māori Education lead to guide this conversation. Do NOT imitate sacred or tapu designs; focus on the idea of pattern carrying meaning.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian patterns come from living cultures; avoid ‘exotic’ language. Banner designs should not copy any religious or sacred symbols.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compass, protractors, rulers, scissors, paper for fold-and-cut</li><li>Lapis, turquoise and gold paint; foam printing sheets; brayers</li><li>Large paper for banners; fabric offcuts (optional)</li><li>Persepolis rosette and lotus-border images</li><li>The app’s Symmetry Lab (for planning)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students use compass/protractor accurately?</li><li>Who needed more time on printing technique?</li><li>How well did teams collaborate on the crest?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Charter Day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Charter Day — The race begins. Sign the Charter. Win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 1: The Gates of the Plateau (up to c. 550 BC) · Zagros Mountains, Ecbatana, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about Persia and work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the peoples, land, animals and art of Week 1.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Caravan Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the idea.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Charter Day</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Gates of the Plateau stand open. The old cairn on the high pass is waiting. But no caravan may enter until it signs the Caravan Charter. Shirin unrolls a scroll: ‘Write your Creed, Courier. How will you race? With kindness? With courage? With honesty?’ A far-off horn sounds. The Showdown begins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Caravan Creed on a strip and reads it aloud.</li><li>Teachers may display the Creeds on the wall for the rest of the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q2, Q6, Q9) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Caravan Creed: ONE sentence about how you will race with integrity. Starters: We will always… / We promise to… even when… / Our Caravan will never… / We will cheer on…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Acts 17:26–27; Ps 24:1; Exod 31:1–5 — This week we met a God who sets the places of the nations, owns the whole earth, fills creatures with fitting skill and fills people with creative skill.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most wonderful thing you learned about God’s world this week?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s sources and questions made you think hardest?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Caravan Creed together. Pray for each other and for the journey ahead.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Which mountain range runs through western Iran and the heart of ancient Persia? A) Southern Alps · <b>B) Zagros ✔</b> · C) Rockies · D) Himalaya <em>The Zagros Mountains run down the west of Iran — home to the first Persian villages and to ibex and leopards.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>2. The ancient people who lived at Susa were the… A) Vikings · B) Romans · C) Mongols · <b>D) Elamites ✔</b> <em>Susa was the Elamite city — one of the earliest cities in the world, long before Cyrus.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>3. The capital of the Medes was… <b>A) Ecbatana ✔</b> · B) Athens · C) Memphis · D) Rome <em>Ecbatana (today the city of Hamadan) was the Median capital. Herodotus said it had seven coloured walls — a story archaeologists cannot confirm.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>4. A scientist who studies objects dug from the ground is an… A) meteorologist · B) astronomer · C) chemist · <b>D) archaeologist ✔</b> <em>Archaeologists study artefacts — pottery, tablets, tools — to learn about the past.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>5. A habitat is… A) a type of tool · B) a desert only · <b>C) the natural home of a living thing ✔</b> · D) a kind of map <em>A habitat is the natural home of a living thing — it gives food, water, shelter and space.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>6. Which animal lives in the Zagros mountains? A) Penguin · <b>B) Bezoar ibex (wild goat) ✔</b> · C) Walrus · D) Kākāpō <em>The bezoar ibex has grippy hooves and climbs steep rock. Kākāpō are native to Aotearoa; penguins and walruses live in other habitats.</em> [SC-LW-Eco] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>7. In 1935 the country asked the world to call it… A) Persia only · B) Elam · <b>C) Iran ✔</b> · D) Babylon <em>In 1935 the government asked the world to use ‘Iran’. ‘Persia’ comes from Persis, the home region of the Persians. Both names matter.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>8. A rosette is… A) a river · B) a weapon · C) a tent · <b>D) a flower-shaped round pattern ✔</b> <em>A rosette is a round, flower-like pattern with petals around a centre — common at Persepolis.</em> [VA-UC]</li><li>9. Acts 17:26 says God determined the nations’ times and… <b>A) the exact places where they live ✔</b> · B) the number of kings · C) the best food · D) the colour of rivers <em>Acts 17:26: God set the times and the exact places where peoples would live — so that they would look for Him.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Why do historians compare more than one source? <b>A) To check bias and see different perspectives ✔</b> · B) Because one source is never allowed · C) Because maps are boring · D) To make lessons longer <em>Every source has a point of view. Comparing different kinds of source helps us check bias and see the whole picture.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>11. A qanat is… A) a mountain · <b>B) an underground water channel ✔</b> · C) a type of camel · D) a royal robe <em>A qanat is a gently sloping underground tunnel that carries water from the mountains to a village.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Which is the best reason to build a village near a river? A) Kings like boats · B) Rivers are pretty · <b>C) Water for drinking, crops and travel ✔</b> · D) Mountains are loud <em>Rivers give water to drink, water to grow crops and a way to travel and trade — three big reasons to settle.</em> [SS-PE]</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (e.g. geography and science) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Acts 17:26–27; Ps 24:1; Exod 31:1–5 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful; celebrate effort, not only winning.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week (whakapapa and sources; Southern Alps; tahr; kowhaiwhai).</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown — keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching on Monday?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 2: The King Who Opened the Gates":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Cyrus Rises: From Anshan to the Gates of Babylon":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cyrus Rises: From Anshan to the Gates of Babylon — Legend, evidence — and a king remembered as fair.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 2: The King Who Opened the Gates (559–530 BC) · Anshan, Pasargadae, Ecbatana, Babylon</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Fairness; Inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how Cyrus built an empire and why people remembered him as fair.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can put Cyrus’s key events in order (Media about 550 BC, Lydia about 546 BC, Babylon 539 BC).</li><li>I can tell legend from evidence.</li><li>I can give two perspectives on Cyrus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A claim-and-evidence sentence (‘Cyrus was fair because…’), the Path product (photographed) and observation of Council Fire discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names Cyrus and one event with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Orders Cyrus’s main events and separates a legend from evidence with an example.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why Cyrus was remembered as fair, using a named source and a perspective.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Weighs two or more perspectives, notes the Cylinder’s purpose and bias, and forms a reasoned judgement.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>empire</b> — A large group of lands and peoples ruled by one king or government.</li><li><b>conquer</b> — To take control of a place or people by force.</li><li><b>legend</b> — A story told for a very long time that may contain some truth but cannot be fully checked.</li><li><b>evidence</b> — Facts or objects that help show whether something is true.</li><li><b>perspective</b> — A point of view — how a person sees something because of who they are.</li><li><b>cylinder</b> — A round, tube-shaped object. The Cyrus Cylinder is a clay barrel covered in writing.</li><li><b>decree</b> — An official order or announcement from a ruler.</li><li><b>exile</b> — A person forced to live away from their home country.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Rumour Mill</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Courier! The torn map has led us to a bustling market, where everyone has a story about the great king Cyrus. ‘He was a baby left on a mountain!’ ‘A king’s dream warned of him!’ ‘A shepherd’s wife raised him!’ Shirin raises one eyebrow: ‘Stories are fun — but which ones are true? Whisper it along the line, Courier, and watch it change. Then we sort the legend from the evidence.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Friday — name two of the three peoples of the plateau; what is a source; why do historians use more than one?</li><li>Rumour Mill (4 min): whisper a short sentence about baby Cyrus down each caravan line (e.g. ‘A king dreamed his grandson would take his throne, so he ordered the baby to be taken away — but a shepherd’s family raised him’). Compare the first and last version. Why do stories change?</li><li>Tell the legend briefly: the Greek historian Herodotus tells this story. It has the shape of many ancient hero legends, so historians doubt the details.</li><li>Sort (3 min): use the Legend / Possible fact / Fact widget on the big screen — hands up or A–C cards.</li><li>Share LI/SC; students read them together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Friday: three peoples of the plateau; what a source is; why we compare sources.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One king, three conquests, many stories — and one clay Cylinder. Let’s separate what we know from what we are told.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline (4 min): add Cyrus 559, Media about 550, Lydia about 546, Babylon 539 to the Week 1 timeline strip. Stand 4 students as ‘living dates’ and have the class order them.</li><li>Three conquests (4 min): point to Anshan, Ecbatana, Sardis and Babylon on the map; talk about how far apart these are.</li><li>Cylinder (4 min): show the image. Read two or three plain sentences from a teacher-prepared summary. Ask: ‘Who wrote this? Who was it for? Why might a king want it written?’ Be careful: do not call it the ‘first charter of human rights’ — scholars disagree, and it is better to describe what it says.</li><li>Perspectives (3 min): a quick three-column chart: Greek / Babylonian / Biblical. What does each source tell us? What might each leave out?</li><li><b>A small kingdom, a big ambition:</b> About <b>559 BC</b> a Persian called <b>Cyrus II</b> became king of <b>Anshan</b> in the southern Zagros. Not long after, he challenged his overlord, <b>Astyages</b> of the <b>Medes</b> — and about <b>550 BC</b> the Median army went over to Cyrus’s side. Cyrus now ruled the Medes and Persians together, with a capital at <b>Ecbatana</b> to add to his own homeland.</li><li><b>Three big dates:</b> <b>About 550 BC</b> — Cyrus takes over the Median kingdom. <b>About 546 BC</b> — he defeats <b>Croesus</b>, the rich king of <b>Lydia</b> (in what is now western Turkey), and captures his capital <b>Sardis</b>. <b>539 BC</b> — he enters <b>Babylon</b>, the biggest city in the world. Some details are uncertain: ancient writers did not agree on every date.</li><li><b>The Babylon surprise:</b> Babylonian records say the city fell with little fighting. The Babylonian king, <b>Nabonidus</b>, was unpopular with some of his own priests, and Cyrus’s army entered the city. Cyrus said he came as the one chosen by the god Marduk — which tells us how he wanted Babylonians to see him.</li><li><b>The Cyrus Cylinder:</b> A <b>clay barrel</b> about the length of your forearm, written in Babylonian <b>cuneiform</b> and found in Babylon in 1879. It is now in the <b>British Museum</b>. In it, Cyrus says he restored temples, brought home the statues of gods to their sanctuaries and let peoples return to their own towns. It is a real ancient source — but <b>Cyrus’s own team wrote it</b>, so it shows how he wanted to be seen. It is not a modern ‘human rights charter’, and some scholars think it follows an old Babylonian habit of kings praising themselves.</li><li><b>Whose voice? Many perspectives:</b> <b>Greek writers</b> like Herodotus and Xenophon praised Cyrus, though they were not Persian. <b>Babylonian records</b> show a king who respected local gods. The <b>Bible</b> (Isaiah and Ezra) says that Cyrus let the exiles from Judah go home to rebuild the temple in Jerusalem. Each source has its own purpose — and together they give a fuller picture.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>sort: Legend, possible fact or fact?</b> — Sort each statement about Cyrus into the right bin. Think about who tells the story and whether anything else supports it.</li><li><b>order: Cyrus’s rise, in order</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>match: Words of the week</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>reveal: Three sources, three voices</b> — Tap each card to see what that source says — and what it might leave out.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Front Page: Babylon Falls Without a Storm:</b> Write a newspaper front page for the day Cyrus entered Babylon. Include a headline, two columns, a picture and a source box saying how we know.</li><li><b>Path B — Courtroom: Was Cyrus Great?:</b> Prepare a prosecution OR defence statement about whether Cyrus deserves the title ‘Great’, using at least two pieces of evidence. Then hold a mini-hearing.</li><li><b>Path C — Storyboard: Cyrus’s Rise:</b> Draw an 8-frame storyboard of Cyrus’s rise, with a label under each frame saying LEGEND or EVIDENCE, and a short caption.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Isa 44:28–45:4; Ezra 1:1–4; Prov 21:1 — In Isaiah, God says Cyrus will be His shepherd and that He will open doors for him, naming him even though Cyrus did not know God. In Ezra, Cyrus announces that the exiles may return to rebuild the temple in Jerusalem, and the Lord had stirred his heart. The proverb says a king’s heart is like channels of water in the Lord’s hand: He can turn it wherever He wishes.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it say about God that He named a foreign king before he ever came to power and stirred his heart? Does God care about the leaders of every nation?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> The Cylinder shows Cyrus’s own policy and how he wanted to be seen; Ezra says God moved him. Can both be true? What does each source tell us — and what does neither tell us?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Paul tells Christians to pray for kings and all in authority (1 Timothy 2:1–2). Name ONE leader (a teacher, a principal, a mayor, a prime minister) you will pray for this week.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Front Page: Babylon Falls Without a Storm</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a newspaper front page for the day Cyrus entered Babylon. Include a headline, two columns, a picture and a source box saying how we know.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Headline names Cyrus and Babylon (539 BC)</li><li>Two columns of clear facts in my own words</li><li>A picture with a caption</li><li>A source box (e.g. ‘The Cyrus Cylinder says…’)</li><li>I separated what the sources say from what is only a rumour</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the newspaper template with sentence frames and a word bank (Cyrus · Babylon · gates · Cylinder · fair · temple).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write both columns independently and include a source box with two sources.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second, short ‘Babylonian priest’ opinion column that shows a different perspective, and explain why it differs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Headline:</b> BABYLON FALLS WITHOUT A STORM! <b>Column 1:</b> Who? What? Where? When? <b>Column 2:</b> Why do people say Cyrus was fair? <b>Source box:</b> According to the Cyrus Cylinder… Some historians say… but…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Courtroom: Was Cyrus Great?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Prepare a prosecution OR defence statement about whether Cyrus deserves the title ‘Great’, using at least two pieces of evidence. Then hold a mini-hearing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked my side (prosecution or defence)</li><li>I gave 2 pieces of evidence and named the source</li><li>I thought about what the other side would say</li><li>I spoke clearly and listened politely to the other side</li><li>I changed my mind or explained why I did not</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the evidence cards and sentence frames; prepare with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Prepare your own statement with two named sources and one reply to the other side.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Act as the judge: write a fair verdict that weighs power (conquest) and justice (kindness) and says what more evidence you would want.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Opening:</b> Your Honour, Cyrus was / was not great because… <b>Evidence 1:</b> The Cylinder says… <b>Evidence 2:</b> Herodotus / Ezra says… <b>Reply to the other side:</b> They might say… but…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Storyboard: Cyrus’s Rise</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an 8-frame storyboard of Cyrus’s rise, with a label under each frame saying LEGEND or EVIDENCE, and a short caption.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Eight frames in the correct order</li><li>Dates on at least three frames (550, 546, 539 BC)</li><li>Each frame is labelled Legend or Evidence</li><li>Captions are in my own words</li><li>The final frame shows Cyrus at Babylon</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the pre-numbered frames with the captions partly written.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own captions and label each frame as Legend or Evidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘Source Box’ under three frames naming where the information comes from, and say how sure we can be.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Frames:</b> 1 Baby Cyrus (legend) · 2 King of Anshan · 3 Medes join him · 4 Croesus of Lydia · 5 Sardis · 6 March to Babylon · 7 The gates open · 8 The Cylinder. <b>Label:</b> Legend / Evidence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Isa 44:28–45:4; Ezra 1:1–4; Prov 21:1; 1 Tim 2:1–2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Present the Cylinder and Ezra respectfully side by side — they are not the same document and do not mention each other. Christians read Isaiah’s naming of Cyrus as prophecy; some scholars date the passage differently. Either way, Cyrus appears in the Bible as a ruler God used. Avoid claiming the Cylinder ‘proves’ the Bible, or that the Bible ‘explains away’ the Cylinder.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How have leaders in Aotearoa responded to peoples whose land they governed? If your class has already met Te Tiriti o Waitangi, link its promises to the idea of a leader keeping their word. Keep it age-appropriate and invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide the conversation; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Cyrus is a hero in Iranian memory and a figure in the Bible — treat his story with respect. Avoid presenting the Cylinder as a modern ‘human rights charter’ and avoid saying one source is simply ‘true’. Some students may be Iranian-NZ or from Muslim backgrounds; invite, never expect, personal contributions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rumour Mill story cards (app or printed)</li><li>Legend / Possible fact / Fact sort cards</li><li>Cyrus Cylinder image</li><li>Timeline strip from Week 1 (add the new date cards)</li><li>Path A newspaper template; Path B courtroom role cards; Path C 8-frame storyboard sheet</li><li>Map of Anshan, Ecbatana, Sardis and Babylon</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students separate a legend from evidence — and say why?</li><li>Did they notice that the Cylinder was written for Cyrus?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Tuesday’s city work?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Pasargadae, Ecbatana & Babylon: Royal Cities and Gardens":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pasargadae, Ecbatana &amp; Babylon: Royal Cities and Gardens — Why was the capital built right there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Cities &amp; Capitals</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 2: The King Who Opened the Gates (559–530 BC) · Anshan, Pasargadae, Ecbatana, Babylon</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Community &amp; participation; Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why capital cities were built where they were and what makes a city a capital.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe three functions of a city (government, trade, worship).</li><li>I can explain the site of Pasargadae, Ecbatana or Babylon.</li><li>I can plan a capital city with zones.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use of function / site / situation vocabulary in Path products; reasoned site selection; photographs of plans and models.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names a city or function with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes the three functions and locates a capital on a map.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why a capital was sited where it was, using water, routes and defence as evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares two cities’ sites and justifies which is better, weighing trade-offs.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>capital</b> — The city where a country or empire is governed from.</li><li><b>function</b> — What a place is used for (government, trade, worship…).</li><li><b>site</b> — The exact spot where a city is built.</li><li><b>situation</b> — Where a city is in relation to other places (rivers, routes, neighbours).</li><li><b>zone</b> — A part of a city used for one main purpose (e.g. markets, homes).</li><li><b>canal</b> — A human-made waterway for moving water or boats.</li><li><b>paradise</b> — From an old Persian word for a walled garden.</li><li><b>palace</b> — A large home and workplace for a ruler.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Where Would YOU Put the Capital?</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Shirin spreads half a map on the table. ‘Our map is torn, Courier — but a king has to put his capital somewhere! Here is a blank land with a river, a plain, a mountain and a coast. Where would YOU put the capital? Pin your choice — and be ready to defend it.’ Behind her, Gandom stares at a pin. She has already eaten two.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — what year did Cyrus enter Babylon? What is a legend? Name one source about Cyrus.</li><li>Pin it (4 min): each caravan places one pin on a blank map of NZ and gives two reasons (central, port, safe from floods, near farms…).</li><li>Reveal (2 min): Wellington — central, a good harbour, and Cook Strait between the islands. Compare: did any team’s reasons match?</li><li>Share LI/SC; invite students to read them aloud.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: 539 BC; legend vs evidence; one source for Cyrus.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Capitals are chosen — and the choice tells a story. Visit three royal cities and one very special garden.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Functions (4 min): on the board, draw three columns: government / trade / worship. Place a few places in each (palace, market, temple). Ask: could one building have more than one function?</li><li>Three cities (6 min): use the map. Compare the three cities by site (high plain / cool mountain slope / river plain) and situation (routes, neighbours). Why choose each?</li><li>Garden (3 min): show the plan. Trace the water channels. Ask: why is water so special here? How does shade help in the heat?</li><li>Link to NZ (2 min): compare Pasargadae with Wellington and Babylon with a river city such as Hamilton or Christchurch. Keep it factual.</li><li><b>What makes a capital?:</b> A <b>capital</b> is the centre of government. Great cities often have three main <b>functions</b>: <b>government</b> (rulers and officials), <b>trade</b> (markets and workshops) and <b>worship</b> (temples and shrines). <b>Site</b> is the exact spot; <b>situation</b> is where it lies in relation to roads, rivers and neighbours.</li><li><b>Pasargadae: the tomb-city:</b> Cyrus built <b>Pasargadae</b> on a high plain in <b>Fars</b>, his homeland, at about 1,900 m above sea level. Its remains include palaces, a garden and the stone <b>tomb</b> that tradition links to Cyrus. It was a ceremonial and royal centre more than a busy trading city. Its royal spaces were open, with gardens and wide courtyards.</li><li><b>Ecbatana: the cool summer capital:</b> <b>Ecbatana</b> (today’s <b>Hamadan</b>) lies high on the slopes of <b>Mount Alvand</b>, in the Median heartland. Its cool air made it a pleasant summer capital, and it sat on the main route across the Zagros. Later Persian kings kept spending their summers there. Archaeologists have found only a little of the ancient city — the modern city sits on top of it.</li><li><b>Babylon: the river city:</b> <b>Babylon</b> stood on the <b>Euphrates</b> river in a flat, fertile plain. Canals brought water to farms, and ships and caravans brought goods. The <b>Ishtar Gate</b>, with its blue glazed bricks, was built before Cyrus by <b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b>. Babylon was one of the biggest and busiest cities of its day.</li><li><b>The Persian garden — and a famous word:</b> At Pasargadae a garden was laid out with straight <b>stone water channels</b> and pavilions. Later gardens often used a <b>four-part plan</b>, with water dividing the space into quarters — a lovely idea in a dry land. Our word <b>paradise</b> comes from an old Persian word for a <b>walled garden</b>. Historians still debate how the first gardens looked, so this is a clue, not a certainty.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Cities and their clues</b> — Match each place to its description.</li><li><b>sort: A capital’s three functions</b> — Is each of these mostly about government, trade or worship?</li><li><b>reveal: Site or situation?</b> — Tap each card to see whether it describes the SITE (exact spot) or the SITUATION (where it is in relation to others).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Capital City Plan:</b> Draw a bird’s-eye plan of your own capital city, divided into zones, with a key. Mark where the palace, markets, homes, gardens and defences go, and explain your choices.</li><li><b>Path B — Compare Three Cities:</b> Compare Pasargadae, Babylon and a New Zealand city using a table and a Venn diagram. Explain which is the best site and why.</li><li><b>Path C — Persian Garden Model:</b> Make a drawn or 3D model of a four-part Persian garden with water channels, trees and shade. Label the purpose of each part.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 2:8–15; Rev 22:1–2 — In Genesis, God plants a garden in Eden, with a river flowing out of it to water the garden. In Revelation, John sees a river of life and a tree of life in the middle of the city, with leaves for the healing of the nations.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why do people love gardens and cities together? What does it say that the Bible begins in a garden and ends with a garden-city?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persians made walled ‘paradise’ gardens to bring shade and water to dry land; Eden is God’s garden. What do human gardens echo about what God first made? Where do they fall short?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write ONE way you can help your school grounds feel more like a garden — for others, for animals, or for the soil.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Capital City Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a bird’s-eye plan of your own capital city, divided into zones, with a key. Mark where the palace, markets, homes, gardens and defences go, and explain your choices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bird’s-eye view with a title</li><li>At least 5 zones (palace, market, homes, gardens, defence)</li><li>A key with symbols or colours</li><li>Water source and route marked</li><li>I can explain two reasons for my site</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the zone outline with labelled boxes; place and colour the zones, then complete the explanation frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently with a key and two reasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second plan of the same city 100 years later after it has grown, and explain what changed and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Zones:</b> palace · market · homes · gardens · defence · temple. <b>Explain:</b> I put the ___ near ___ because ___. <b>Site:</b> My capital is on ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Compare Three Cities</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare Pasargadae, Babylon and a New Zealand city using a table and a Venn diagram. Explain which is the best site and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Table covers site, situation, functions and water</li><li>Venn diagram shows what is the same and different</li><li>I used geography words (site, situation, function)</li><li>I gave a reasoned opinion</li><li>Facts are accurate</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a partly-filled table and a word bank; compare two cities rather than three.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete the table for three cities and one Venn.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Explain how site and situation changed what each city was used for; predict how each might have to adapt to a flood, drought or invasion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Table headings:</b> Name · Site · Situation · Water · Functions. <b>Venn:</b> Pasargadae only · Both · NZ city only. <b>Judgement:</b> I think ___ is the best site because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Persian Garden Model</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a drawn or 3D model of a four-part Persian garden with water channels, trees and shade. Label the purpose of each part.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Four-part garden plan</li><li>Water channels drawn or modelled</li><li>Trees and shade labelled</li><li>At least three labels with purposes (cooling, food, rest)</li><li>I can explain why gardens mattered in a dry land</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn square with a cross of channels; add and label four items.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan and build independently with labels explaining purpose.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a short plaque explaining how the word ‘paradise’ is linked to gardens, and compare to a garden in your own community.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label bank:</b> water channel · pavilion · fruit tree · shade · path · pool. <b>Say it:</b> The water helps by ___. The shade helps by ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 2:8–15; Rev 22:1–2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Be careful not to claim Persian gardens ‘prove’ Eden or that ‘paradise’ means the same thing everywhere. Say: ‘People across cultures have loved gardens — Christians see that as an echo of how God made us.’</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Why is Wellington our capital — harbour, centre of the country, Cook Strait? Compare with other possible capitals students suggested. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to talk about places that mattered in your own rohe before European towns; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep references to modern Iran, Iraq and the Middle East neutral — today’s cities and sites are living places. Ruins are the remains of real communities. Treat Babylon (modern Iraq) with the same respect as Persian sites.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank NZ outline map and sticky pins</li><li>Maps of Fars, Hamadan and Babylon (app or printed)</li><li>Pasargadae garden plan (app or printed)</li><li>Capital-plan graph paper; coloured pencils</li><li>Venn diagram / comparison table sheet</li><li>Clay, card, string and sand for Path C garden model</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain a site in terms of water, routes and defence?</li><li>Did they use the words site and situation correctly?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Wednesday’s grassland work?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Cyrus’s Camel Secret & the Grassland Community":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cyrus’s Camel Secret &amp; the Grassland Community — Why did the horses refuse to charge?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Grassland Communities &amp; Fair Tests</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 2: The King Who Opened the Gates (559–530 BC) · Anshan, Pasargadae, Ecbatana, Babylon</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how grassland animals suit open habitats and to run a fair test.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name a producer, a consumer and a decomposer.</li><li>I can explain two adaptations (camouflage and speed).</li><li>I can keep one variable the same in a fair test.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fair-test plan (what changed / measured / kept the same); correct use of producer, consumer, decomposer; the graph and conclusion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names an animal or a food-chain link with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Labels producers, consumers and decomposers and names an adaptation.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Plans and carries out a fair test with one variable and explains the result using camouflage or speed.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates the test, suggests improvements, and predicts how a change would affect a grassland community.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>producer</b> — A living thing (like grass) that makes its own food from sunlight.</li><li><b>consumer</b> — A living thing that eats other living things.</li><li><b>decomposer</b> — A living thing (like a fungus) that breaks down dead plants and animals.</li><li><b>predator</b> — An animal that hunts and eats other animals.</li><li><b>prey</b> — An animal that is hunted and eaten.</li><li><b>camouflage</b> — Colours or patterns that help an animal blend in.</li><li><b>variable</b> — Something that can change in an experiment.</li><li><b>fair test</b> — An experiment where only one thing is changed, one thing is measured and everything else stays the same.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Cyrus’s Camel Trick</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The map is torn but the story is old: before the siege of Sardis, the Lydian cavalry thundered out to meet Cyrus’s army. Cyrus had a clever plan. ‘He put his camels at the front,’ says Shirin, ‘and the Lydian horses took one sniff and wanted nothing to do with them!’ Gandom raises her head, offended. ‘What does that tell us about animal senses, Courier?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — name the three functions of a city; what is the difference between site and situation?</li><li>Tell Herodotus’s story (3 min): this is a story from a Greek writer, so we treat it as a ‘possible fact’ — historians think horses really can be unsettled by the smell and sight of camels.</li><li>Sniff-test mystery bags (4 min): pass closed pots with safe smells; groups guess without peeking. Discuss: what senses do animals use? Which senses are strongest for horses (smell, hearing)?</li><li>Share LI/SC; students tick the vocabulary as it appears.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: three functions of a city; site vs situation.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Welcome to the open steppe — wide skies, long grass, sharp eyes and fast legs.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Community (5 min): build a food chain on the board: grass → onager → cheetah → vultures and decomposers. Label producer / consumer / decomposer.</li><li>Adaptations (3 min): quick-fire pairs: ‘long legs for?’, ‘sandy colour for?’, ‘big ears for?’ Link to last week’s adaptation words.</li><li>Fair test (5 min): model with a paper ‘moth’ example. Ask: What do we change? What do we measure? What stays the same? Use mini-whiteboards.</li><li>Animal Cards (2 min): hand out. Remind students to treat the endangered cards as a reason to care, not as a game.</li><li><b>A community on the plains:</b> Wide, grassy plains cover parts of Iran. <b>Grasses</b> are the <b>producers</b> — they make food from sunlight. <b>Insects</b>, the <b>Persian onager</b> and the <b>goitered gazelle</b> eat the plants and are <b>consumers</b>. <b>Predators</b> such as the <b>Asiatic cheetah</b> hunt them. <b>Vultures</b> clean up dead animals, and <b>fungi</b> and bacteria are <b>decomposers</b> that return nutrients to the soil.</li><li><b>The three big animals:</b> <b>Persian onager (a wild ass):</b> long legs, big ears and fast, steady running; endangered. <b>Goitered gazelle:</b> sandy-coloured coat for camouflage and speed; males have a swollen throat in the breeding season. <b>Asiatic cheetah:</b> the fastest land animal, built for short, super-fast chases; critically endangered, with only a very small number left in Iran.</li><li><b>Two adaptations: hide or run:</b> On open ground there is nowhere to hide — so animals either <b>blend in</b> (camouflage: sandy fur, spots) or <b>run</b> (long legs, strong lungs, big eyes and ears). Both help predators catch food or help prey escape.</li><li><b>What is a fair test?:</b> A scientist <b>changes one thing</b> (the variable), <b>measures one thing</b> and <b>keeps everything else the same</b>. If we change colour AND background AND time, we can’t tell which one made the difference.</li><li><b>Animal Cards: this week’s three:</b> Collect this week’s <b>Animal Cards</b>: the <b>onager</b>, the <b>goitered gazelle</b> and the <b>Asiatic cheetah</b>. Tilt them to see the holo shine, and read how each is suited to the open plains.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>animalCards</li><li><b>sort: Producer, consumer or decomposer?</b> — Sort each living thing of the steppe into the right role in the food chain.</li><li><b>match: Adaptations at work</b> — Match each adaptation to the job it does.</li><li><b>order: Plan a fair test</b> — Put the steps of a fair test in the right order.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Camouflage Fair Test:</b> Scatter coloured paper ‘moths’ on grass or a green mat. Classmates act as ‘predators’ and collect for 20 seconds. Record, graph and explain which colours survived best.</li><li><b>Path B — Grassland Food-Chain Mobile:</b> Make a hanging mobile of two grassland food chains (e.g. grass → onager → cheetah) and label each link as producer, consumer or decomposer.</li><li><b>Path C — Predator–Prey Card Game:</b> Design a card game with 8 grassland creatures. Give each strengths (speed, camouflage, senses) and write a rule set for a fair game.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Job 39:5–8; Matt 6:26 — In Job, God asks who set the wild donkey free, whose home is the open steppe and who laughs at the noise of the city. In Matthew, Jesus says to look at the birds: they do not plant or harvest, yet the heavenly Father feeds them — and you are worth much more to Him.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why does God care for animals that people cannot use or tame? What does that say about how He sees the wild world?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Adaptations like speed and camouflage help animals survive. How can careful science help us notice God’s care? What is the difference between ‘how it works’ and ‘who looks after it’?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose ONE wild creature (a bird, an insect, a skink) to observe respectfully this week. Write what you noticed and say thank you.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Camouflage Fair Test</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Scatter coloured paper ‘moths’ on grass or a green mat. Classmates act as ‘predators’ and collect for 20 seconds. Record, graph and explain which colours survived best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote a question and a prediction</li><li>I changed ONE thing (colour) and kept everything else the same</li><li>I recorded results in a table</li><li>I drew a bar graph</li><li>I explained the result using the word camouflage</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the table template and sentence frames; test just three colours.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan the test independently, record in a table and graph the results.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Repeat on a second background (e.g. brown paper) and explain why the best colour changed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> Which colour of moth is hardest to find on grass? <b>Change:</b> colour. <b>Measure:</b> number found in 20 seconds. <b>Same:</b> size, number of each colour, time, background. <b>Conclusion:</b> The ___ moths were found least because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Grassland Food-Chain Mobile</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a hanging mobile of two grassland food chains (e.g. grass → onager → cheetah) and label each link as producer, consumer or decomposer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two food chains of at least 3 links</li><li>Arrows show who eats whom</li><li>Every living thing is labelled (producer / consumer / decomposer)</li><li>A decomposer is included</li><li>I can explain what would change if one link disappeared</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use ready-cut animal cards and a chain strip; add arrows and labels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label two chains independently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Link both chains into a small food web and predict three changes if one species vanished.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Chain:</b> grass → ___ → ___. <b>Labels:</b> producer · consumer · decomposer. <b>Say it:</b> If the ___ disappeared, then ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Predator–Prey Card Game</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a card game with 8 grassland creatures. Give each strengths (speed, camouflage, senses) and write a rule set for a fair game.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>8 cards, each with a name and a picture</li><li>Each card has two realistic adaptations</li><li>A clear rule for who ‘wins’ when two cards meet</li><li>The rules are fair and easy to follow</li><li>I tested the game with a partner</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use blank card templates with the stat boxes; use only 6 cards.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create 8 cards and a written rule set; test once.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Balance the game using data: swap a rule after testing and explain how you kept the test fair.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Card:</b> Name · Habitat · Speed (1–5) · Camouflage (1–5) · Special sense. <b>Rule:</b> When a predator meets prey, compare ___ to decide. <b>Test:</b> What did we change after playing?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Job 39:5–8; Matt 6:26 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Job 39 is poetry in a speech from God, not a field guide. Enjoy it as praise. Avoid saying that science ‘proves design’; instead ask: ‘What does this make us wonder?’ Christians have different views on how the details fit together, but the passage affirms that God cares for the wild.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> NZ tussock grasslands are home to skinks, kea and kārearea (NZ falcon). Compare: which of our animals use camouflage, and which use speed? Link to kaitiakitanga. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū for local knowledge about native species; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Asiatic cheetah and Persian onager are rare and endangered — present this as a reason to care, not to panic. Keep the predator–prey talk matter-of-fact. Take care that ‘predator’ games do not become rough: no touching, no chasing, and keep everyone safe.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sniff-test mystery bags (safe smells in closed pots)</li><li>Grassland photos and animal cards (app)</li><li>Coloured paper ‘moths’ (several colours, same size)</li><li>Green mat or grass patch; timer; clipboards</li><li>Food-chain mobile materials (card, string, coat hangers)</li><li>Card-game template for Path C; graph paper</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students name the variable they changed — and the ones they kept the same?</li><li>Which students used ‘camouflage’ accurately in their conclusion?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Thursday’s art lesson?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Writing in Clay: Cuneiform and Cylinder Seals":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Writing in Clay: Cuneiform and Cylinder Seals — Press, roll, repeat — and make a promise in clay.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Writing in Clay</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 2: The King Who Opened the Gates (559–530 BC) · Anshan, Pasargadae, Ecbatana, Babylon</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to use impressed and relief techniques to make writing-art in clay.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can make a clay tablet with wedge marks.</li><li>I can roll a seal to make a repeated print.</li><li>I can explain what clay writing was used for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Craftsmanship and technique (even wedges, clean seal print, neat calligraphy); explanation of purpose in the artist’s statement.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes wedge marks or a seal print with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Makes a tablet or seal with clear marks and explains one use of clay writing.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls impressing, rolling or calligraphy with care and explains why seals and tablets mattered.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines techniques with intent, compares writing in clay with other forms of record, and evaluates which is best for lasting messages.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>cuneiform</b> — Wedge-shaped writing pressed into wet clay with a reed.</li><li><b>tablet</b> — A flat piece of clay used for writing.</li><li><b>stylus</b> — A pointed tool (a reed in ancient times) used to press marks into clay.</li><li><b>cylinder seal</b> — A small stone cylinder carved with a design, rolled over wet clay to leave a print.</li><li><b>impress</b> — To press a shape into a soft surface.</li><li><b>relief</b> — A design that is raised up from a surface.</li><li><b>scribe</b> — A person trained to write official documents.</li><li><b>calligraphy</b> — Beautiful, careful handwriting.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Wedge-Name Code</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Shirin hands each caravan a smooth clay tablet and a wooden wedge. ‘This is how the clerks of Babylon wrote — and how Cyrus’s decrees were written. A king’s word, pressed into clay! Can you write your name in wedges? And look — the thumbprint on our torn map had a wedge mark. Someone who writes in clay is leaving us clues.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — name a producer, a consumer and a decomposer; what makes a test fair?</li><li>Hold (replica) tablets or look at photos. Ask: What is it made of? How was it made? Why clay?</li><li>Name-code (5 min): use the printed strip; students press their initials in wedge marks. Keep clay damp; wash hands.</li><li>Show the Cyrus Cylinder image again: its writing is cuneiform. Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: producer / consumer / decomposer; fair test = change one thing.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Clay was the paper of the ancient world — cheap, strong and everywhere. And seals were the world’s first signatures.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clay &amp; cuneiform (5 min): demonstrate making a flat tablet, smoothing the edges and pressing wedge marks at an angle. Show how marks are made with the corner of a flat stick.</li><li>Seals (4 min): show how a design cut into soap or foam leaves a raised print. Roll slowly for an even pattern. Practise on scrap first.</li><li>Calligraphy (3 min): hold the pen at a steady angle; keep strokes even; plan the border before the words.</li><li>Model the first step of each Path (3 min); students choose A, B or C. Remind them about careful work and clean-up.</li><li><b>Clay: the paper of the ancient world:</b> People in Mesopotamia and Persia wrote on <b>clay tablets</b>. Clay was easy to find, cheap, and — once dried or baked — lasted for thousands of years. That is why we still have so many tablets today, while writing on cloth or leather has mostly rotted away.</li><li><b>Cuneiform: wedge writing:</b> <b>Cuneiform</b> means ‘wedge-shaped’. A scribe pressed the end of a cut reed into wet clay to make wedge marks. Different groups of wedges stood for words or sounds. Cuneiform was used for languages like Babylonian, Elamite and Old Persian, and was used for about 3,000 years.</li><li><b>Cylinder seals: signatures in stone:</b> A <b>cylinder seal</b> is a small stone tube carved with a design. Rolled over wet clay, it leaves a repeating print — a mark of <b>identity or ownership</b>, like a signature. Seals were used to close jars, doors and letters so people knew who had sent them.</li><li><b>Art techniques: press, smooth, score, roll:</b> <b>Press</b> a shape into clay (impress); <b>smooth</b> to remove cracks; <b>score</b> (scratch) to join pieces; <b>roll</b> a seal for a repeat pattern. Raised designs are called <b>relief</b>, and pressed-in designs are <b>incised</b> or <b>impressed</b>.</li><li><b>The Cylinder: a decree to be remembered:</b> The <b>Cyrus Cylinder</b> was shaped like a barrel and covered in cuneiform. Rulers sometimes buried barrels like this in the walls of buildings they had repaired, so future builders would find their name. It tells us what Cyrus wanted people to remember. Calligraphy is another way to make words beautiful.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Clay-writing words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Making a clay tablet</b> — Put the steps in the right order.</li><li><b>reveal: What was clay writing used for?</b> — Tap each card to find out how people used clay and seals.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Clay Tablet: A Decree of Kindness:</b> Make a clay tablet with your name and a short royal ‘decree of kindness’ in cuneiform-style marks. Add a key on paper showing what your marks mean.</li><li><b>Path B — Cylinder Seal Prints:</b> Carve a design into clay or foam, wrap it around a roller (or roll the clay) and print a repeating sequence on paper. Create a pattern that tells something about you or your caravan.</li><li><b>Path C — Illuminated Decree Scroll:</b> Use calligraphy and a decorative border to write a kind law for your caravan (e.g. ‘Every voice is heard’). Make it look like a royal scroll.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Hab 2:2; Ezra 1:1–4 — In Habakkuk, God tells the prophet to write the vision down and make it plain on tablets so that a runner can read it. In Ezra, the decree of Cyrus is written and sent through the whole kingdom.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why does God use words that are written down? Why do written promises matter?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> The Cylinder and Ezra both record a decree. What does a written promise do that a spoken one might not? How do we keep a promise even when no one can see?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write ONE promise you can keep this term — to a friend, to your family or to God — and sign it with your seal mark.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Clay Tablet: A Decree of Kindness</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a clay tablet with your name and a short royal ‘decree of kindness’ in cuneiform-style marks. Add a key on paper showing what your marks mean.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Smooth, flat tablet</li><li>Wedge marks clear and evenly spaced</li><li>My name appears on the tablet</li><li>A decree of kindness is shown (in marks or in the key)</li><li>I wrote a key and one sentence about why people wrote on clay</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the name-code strip and pre-cut clay; press three words only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your name and decree with a key and a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a seal print to your tablet and explain how scribes made documents hard to forge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Roll clay about 1 cm thick. 2 Cut a rectangle. 3 Smooth edges. 4 Press wedges in rows. 5 Let it dry. <b>Decree idea:</b> ‘In this caravan, everyone shall be welcomed.’ <b>Say it:</b> People wrote on clay because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Cylinder Seal Prints</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Carve a design into clay or foam, wrap it around a roller (or roll the clay) and print a repeating sequence on paper. Create a pattern that tells something about you or your caravan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A clear, simple design</li><li>Design carved or pressed deeply enough to print</li><li>Printed at least 5 times in a row</li><li>Even spacing and pressure</li><li>I can explain what my seal shows and why people used seals</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a ready-made foam strip and simple template; print three times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own seal and print five repeats.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Make a sequence that tells a story from left to right and explain how the repeats create rhythm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch a simple design (animal, plant, pattern). 2 Carve or press into soap/clay/foam. 3 Roll in thin paint. 4 Print along the paper. <b>Statement:</b> My seal shows ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Illuminated Decree Scroll</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use calligraphy and a decorative border to write a kind law for your caravan (e.g. ‘Every voice is heard’). Make it look like a royal scroll.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Border planned and neat</li><li>A short, kind law written in clear lettering</li><li>Spacing and slope are even</li><li>A decorative symbol or seal at the bottom</li><li>I can explain why leaders write decrees down</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a lined guide sheet and trace the first letter; short law of eight words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own law with a border and a seal mark.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two linked laws and explain how they would be fair for people who disagree.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Law frame:</b> In our caravan, we promise to ___. <b>Border idea:</b> rosettes or lotus from Week 1. <b>Say it:</b> Writing a law down helps because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Hab 2:2; Ezra 1:1–4 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Habakkuk 2:2 is about a message made clear; do not claim that it means clay tablets specifically. Keep the focus on the value of clear, written words. Remember that decrees can be used well or badly — it is the character of the person who keeps them that matters.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How are promises and agreements recorded in Aotearoa — signatures, carving, whakapapa, documents like Te Tiriti? Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide this; do not copy sacred or tapu designs, and avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Treat Mesopotamian and Persian writing as the achievement of real, skilled cultures. Use clay and tools safely — wooden skewers are pointed, so supervise carefully. Do not use any sacred scripts or religious texts as ‘decoration’.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Air-dry clay or plasticine; rolling pins; boards</li><li>Wooden skewers, pencils or reeds for wedge marks</li><li>Cuneiform name-code strips</li><li>Soap, foam, clay or dowel rollers for seals</li><li>Paper and ink pads or paint for prints</li><li>Calligraphy pens and parchment paper; border templates</li><li>Image of the Cyrus Cylinder and a cylinder seal</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Were wedge marks and seal prints even and controlled?</li><li>Which students could explain why clay was used?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Friday’s Showdown?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Camel Charge":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Camel Charge — Charge fast. Think faster. Win back the torn map.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 2: The King Who Opened the Gates (559–530 BC) · Anshan, Pasargadae, Ecbatana, Babylon</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about Cyrus, royal cities, grasslands and clay writing, and to work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about Cyrus, the royal cities, grassland animals and cuneiform.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; updated Caravan Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the idea to a new situation.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations that show reasoning about sources.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Camel Charge</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The camels are lined up at the starting gate, snorting. ‘This is the Camel Charge, Courier,’ says Shirin. ‘Speed matters — but so does honesty. Each right answer wins back a piece of the torn map. Add a promise to your Creed, then charge!’ Gandom steps forward. She has not been asked, but she is ready.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each caravan reads its Week 1 Creed, then adds a new line about fairness and reads it aloud.</li><li>Remind teams: speed bonus is for accurate answers; careless clicking loses points.</li><li>Display the updated Creeds on the wall for the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q9, Q10, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, look at your Caravan Creed from Week 1 and add ONE promise about being fair — to rivals, to teammates, and to people who are different from you. Starters: We will treat others fairly even when… / Our Caravan promises to be kind to… / We will keep our word, especially when… / We will not blame…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Isa 45:1–4; Job 39:5–8; Hab 2:2 — This week we met a God who names a foreign king and opens doors, who sets the wild donkey free on the open plains, and who asks for His words to be written down plainly.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most wonderful thing you learned this week about leaders, animals or words?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which source or question made you think hardest — and how did you decide what to trust?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Caravan Creed together, including your new promise about fairness. Pray for the leaders you named on Monday.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Who entered Babylon in 539 BC? A) Xerxes · B) Alexander · C) Darius III · <b>D) Cyrus the Great ✔</b> <em>Cyrus II of Persia entered Babylon in 539 BC, according to Babylonian records, with little fighting.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. Herodotus was a Greek writer who wrote about Persia. What should we remember when we read him? <b>A) He wrote from a Greek point of view, so we should check other sources ✔</b> · B) He was perfectly neutral · C) He was Persian · D) He lived during Cyrus’s reign <em>Herodotus is useful but not neutral — a good historian compares him with other sources, as we learned in Week 1.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>3. Cyrus’s first great royal city, with a garden and a tomb, was… <b>A) Pasargadae ✔</b> · B) Rome · C) Athens · D) Memphis <em>Pasargadae, on the plain of Fars, was Cyrus’s royal centre — with palaces, a garden and the tomb linked to him.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>4. From Week 1: Ecbatana was the capital of which people? A) The Greeks · B) The Elamites · C) The Romans · <b>D) The Medes ✔</b> <em>Ecbatana (modern Hamadan) was the Median capital, and later a cool summer capital of the Persian kings.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>5. In Herodotus’s story, which animals did Cyrus put at the front of his army against the Lydian horses? A) Lions · B) Elephants · C) Eagles · <b>D) Camels ✔</b> <em>Herodotus says the Lydian horses were frightened by the smell and sight of camels. It is a Greek story, but it shows how animals’ senses matter.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>6. In a food chain, grass is a… <b>A) producer ✔</b> · B) decomposer · C) consumer · D) predator <em>Grass makes its own food from sunlight, so it is a producer. Onagers and gazelles are consumers; fungi are decomposers.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>7. Cuneiform writing is made of… A) letters like ours · B) pictures only · <b>C) wedge-shaped marks ✔</b> · D) knots <em>Cuneiform means ‘wedge-shaped’ — scribes pressed a reed into wet clay to make wedge marks.</em> [VA-UC]</li><li>8. The word ‘paradise’ comes from an old Persian word meaning… A) camel · <b>B) walled garden ✔</b> · C) mountain · D) palace <em>‘Paradise’ comes from an old Iranian word for a walled garden, like the gardens of Pasargadae.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>9. In Ezra 1, Cyrus announced that the exiles could… A) stay in Babylon forever · <b>B) return to Jerusalem and rebuild the temple ✔</b> · C) become kings · D) hide in the desert <em>Ezra 1 says Cyrus announced the exiles could return to Jerusalem and rebuild the temple. The Cylinder does not mention this — but it shows a similar policy of returning peoples.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — The Cyrus Cylinder was written for Cyrus. Which is the best way to use it as a source? A) Call it a modern charter of human rights · B) Ignore it because it is biased · <b>C) Use it as real evidence of how Cyrus wanted to be seen, and compare it with other sources ✔</b> · D) Trust everything it says because it is old <em>The Cylinder is a genuine ancient source that shows Cyrus’s policy and image. Because it was written by his own side, a good historian also looks at other sources, such as Babylonian records, Greek writers and the Bible.</em> [SS-DO] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. In a fair test you should change… A) everything · B) nothing · <b>C) one thing at a time ✔</b> · D) three things <em>A fair test changes ONE variable, measures one thing and keeps everything else the same.</em> [SC-NoS-I]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Isaiah 44–45 tells us that God… A) was not interested in foreign kings · B) was surprised by Cyrus · C) lived only in Babylon · <b>D) named Cyrus long before he ruled and used him, even though Cyrus did not know Him ✔</b> <em>Isaiah says God named Cyrus ahead of time and would open doors for him, showing that God is Lord over all nations and rulers.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (e.g. history and science) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Isa 45:1–4; Job 39:5–8; Hab 2:2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful; celebrate effort, not only winning. Be gentle with students who find public rankings stressful.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to this week’s NZ connections: leaders keeping their word (Te Tiriti), Wellington as capital, tussock grassland animals, and ways of recording promises.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown — keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. Take care when discussing Cyrus and the Bible so no student feels their own faith or background is being judged.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception (e.g. calling the Cylinder a ‘human rights charter’) needs re-teaching?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 3: The Royal Road":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Darius the Organiser: Satraps, Coins, Roads & Law":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Darius the Organiser: Satraps, Coins, Roads &amp; Law — How do you hold the biggest empire in the world together?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 3: The Royal Road (522–486 BC) · Behistun, Susa, Persepolis, the Sardis–Susa road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how Darius held a huge empire together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name four tools Darius used (provinces and satraps, coinage, the Royal Road, written records).</li><li>I can explain why each tool helped.</li><li>I can give a perspective on whether Darius was fair or controlling.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A cause-and-effect explanation (‘Darius used ___ so that ___’); the Path product (photographed); notes from the Council Fire discussion.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one or two of Darius’s tools with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Names all four tools and explains simply why each helped.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains cause and effect for each tool using evidence, and says whose perspective a source shows.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates whether Darius was fair or controlling, weighing different sources and perspectives, and applies the idea to another leader.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>empire</b> — Many lands and peoples ruled by one government or king.</li><li><b>satrap</b> — A governor who ran a province (satrapy) for the Persian king.</li><li><b>satrapy</b> — One of the provinces of the Persian Empire.</li><li><b>daric</b> — The Persian gold coin, showing a kneeling archer, introduced under Darius.</li><li><b>inscription</b> — Writing carved into stone or rock.</li><li><b>propaganda</b> — Information shaped to make a ruler look good and persuade people.</li><li><b>archive</b> — A place where official records are kept safe.</li><li><b>decree</b> — An official order or announcement from a ruler.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Relay Race</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Mud, rain and no road: the pass is blocked and the caravan has stopped. Shirin pulls a scroll tube from her pack. ‘A message must reach the king before sundown, Courier. Two ways to send it: whisper it down the line, or run it hand to hand. Which one will arrive correct? Which one will arrive first? Try both. And think about this: a king ruled lands from the sea to the mountains. How did his orders ever get there?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: write a 20-word ‘royal order’ on a slip of paper and put it in the baton tube. Prepare a second, identical-length order to whisper. Form Caravans of 4–5 (same teams as Weeks 1–2).</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud. Pause after ‘Which one will arrive correct?’</li><li>Round 1 (3 min): whisper the order down a Caravan line. The last person says it aloud; compare it with the original. Expect giggles and mistakes.</li><li>Round 2 (3 min): pass the baton in relay; the last student reads the written order aloud. Compare it with the original. Time both rounds.</li><li>Discuss (2 min): Which was faster? Which was more accurate? What did couriers, stations and written messages give a king? Take two shares, without correcting yet.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria. Students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Start of the unit’s new stage. Quick link back: ‘Last week, Cyrus began an empire. Today we ask how the next king kept it together.’ One question: what did a good king need to hold lands together? (Take 2 ideas.)</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One king, an enormous empire, and four big tools. Find out how Darius used them, and decide for yourself whether he was fair.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline (3 min): add date cards to the strip: Cyrus c. 559–530 BC; Darius 522–486 BC; Persepolis begun c. 518 BC.</li><li>Four tools (7 min): present each tool in one minute; use the app’s province map and daric zoom. After each, students show on mini-whiteboards: ‘This helped because…’.</li><li>Behistun (3 min): show the carving. Ask: ‘Who made this? Who is it for? Is it neutral?’ Link to Week 1’s bias lesson. Mention the three languages and how later scholars used them.</li><li>Perspective (2 min): introduce the fair/controlling question. Write two column headings on the board. Do NOT settle it: students decide later in Path C.</li><li>Be careful with numbers: say ‘about’ and name Herodotus as the source for stations and travel times.</li><li><b>Darius takes the throne:</b> <b>Darius I</b> ruled from about <b>522 to 486 BC</b>. He was a Persian nobleman who became king after a time of upheaval following the death of Cyrus’s son Cambyses. The empire he ruled stretched from the Aegean Sea in the west to the Indus region in the east: probably the biggest the world had seen so far. Holding it together was a huge challenge.</li><li><b>Tool 1: provinces and satraps:</b> Darius divided the empire into about <b>twenty or more provinces</b> called <b>satrapies</b> (the exact number depends on which list you read). Each was run by a governor called a <b>satrap</b>, who collected taxes and kept order. To check on them, the king sent inspectors. Greek writers call them ‘the King’s Eyes and Ears’. Persian rulers were not only strict: local laws and customs were often allowed to carry on.</li><li><b>Tool 2: the daric:</b> Darius introduced a <b>gold coin</b>, the <b>daric</b>, showing a kneeling archer. Gold and silver coins gave the empire a <i>standard</i> money that officials and soldiers could be paid with. Taxes, though, were still often paid in goods such as grain, animals and silver. Coins helped trade and organisation, but did not replace everything.</li><li><b>Tool 3: the Royal Road:</b> The <b>Royal Road</b> ran from <b>Sardis</b> in the west to <b>Susa</b> in the south-west of Iran: about <b>2,700 km</b>. The Greek historian <b>Herodotus</b> says it had around <b>111 relay stations</b>, and that ordinary travellers needed about three months, while royal riders passing messages could manage it in about a <b>week</b>. Remember: Herodotus is a Greek, not a neutral reporter, so treat the numbers as ‘about’.</li><li><b>Tool 4: records and the Rock of Behistun:</b> On a cliff at <b>Behistun</b> Darius had a huge carving made, with writing in <b>three languages</b>: Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian. It tells how he became king. It is also <b>propaganda</b>: Darius is telling the story his way. In the 1800s scholars used the three languages (just like a Rosetta stone) to help read cuneiform again. Darius’s officials also kept archives of records, which matters in Ezra’s story today.</li><li><b>Fair or controlling?:</b> Some say Darius was a wise organiser: roads, coins and records helped trade, travel and justice. Others point out that taxes were heavy, that rebellions were crushed, and that the Behistun carving shows rebel kings in chains. <b>Both</b> are parts of the picture. Your job: weigh the evidence, and say <i>whose</i> perspective you are using.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Darius’s Four Tools</b> — Match each tool of the empire to what it did.</li><li><b>order: Darius’s Timeline</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>sort: Fact, Reported, or Opinion?</b> — Sort each statement. Is it well-supported by several sources, reported by one source, or an opinion?</li><li><b>reveal: Who Wrote It? Whose Side?</b> — Tap each card to see whose perspective the source shows.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — The King’s Eye: Inspection Report:</b> You are one of the King’s Eyes and Ears. Visit an imagined province and write an inspection report for Darius: how are the roads, the taxes, the fairness of the satrap? End with a recommendation.</li><li><b>Path B — Design a Daric:</b> Design the front and back of a gold coin for Darius’s empire, then write a museum label explaining its symbols and what it was used for.</li><li><b>Path C — Persuasive Speech: Wise or Controlling?:</b> Choose a side: ‘Darius was a wise ruler’ OR ‘Darius controlled too much’. Write and deliver a 60-second speech using at least three pieces of evidence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Ezra 5:3–6:15; Isa 55:11 — In Ezra, local officials question the Jews rebuilding the temple, so Darius orders a search of the royal records. A scroll is found at Ecbatana with Cyrus’s earlier decree, and Darius commands that the work go on and that it be paid for. The temple is finished. Isaiah says that God’s word does not return empty: it does what He sends it to do.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why did one record in an archive matter so much? What might it mean that a promise made years earlier was found and kept?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persian record-keeping helped Darius run his empire. In Ezra, the same records helped a small people keep a promise made long before. How might we see God at work in the ordinary tools of a ruler, without saying that Darius was God’s servant in the same way as Israel’s kings? (Christians think about this in different ways.)</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write about a promise someone has kept to you, and thank God for His faithfulness. Then write one promise you can keep this week.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: The King’s Eye: Inspection Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>You are one of the King’s Eyes and Ears. Visit an imagined province and write an inspection report for Darius: how are the roads, the taxes, the fairness of the satrap? End with a recommendation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I named my imagined province and its satrap</li><li>I reported on roads and stations</li><li>I reported on taxes and whether they were fair</li><li>I gave one piece of good news and one problem</li><li>I made a recommendation to the king and explained why</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the report frame and the word bank (satrap · tax · road · station · fair). Work with a partner on the recommendation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a full report in your own words, with a reason for each judgement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write TWO reports of the same province: one from the satrap and one from a local farmer. Explain why they differ.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Headline:</b> Report from the province of ___ <b>Roads:</b> The road is … because … <b>Taxes:</b> People pay … and it is / is not fair because … <b>Good news / problem:</b> … <b>Recommendation:</b> I advise the king to … because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Design a Daric</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design the front and back of a gold coin for Darius’s empire, then write a museum label explaining its symbols and what it was used for.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Front and back both designed</li><li>Each symbol has a reason</li><li>My museum label says what the coin was used for</li><li>I explained how a standard coin helps an empire</li><li>My drawing is neat and my label is clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the circular template and the label frame; choose symbols from a bank (archer, crown, spear, bow).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design both sides and write a four-sentence museum label.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare your coin with a New Zealand coin. What do they each say about the people who made them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Front:</b> shows ___ because ___. <b>Back:</b> shows ___ because ___. <b>Museum label:</b> This coin was used to … It helped the empire by … <b>Challenge:</b> Why might a king want his picture on a coin?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Persuasive Speech: Wise or Controlling?</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a side: ‘Darius was a wise ruler’ OR ‘Darius controlled too much’. Write and deliver a 60-second speech using at least three pieces of evidence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I stated my opinion clearly at the start</li><li>I used three pieces of evidence from today</li><li>I named the source of at least one piece of evidence</li><li>I answered one point from the other side</li><li>My ending was strong</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the speech frame with a partner; the evidence cards give you three facts to arrange.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own speech with three evidence points and a counter-argument.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Argue the opposite side to your own belief for 30 seconds, then explain which side you find stronger and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Opening:</b> I believe Darius was … <b>Evidence 1, 2, 3:</b> For example, … This shows … <b>Other side:</b> Some say … but … <b>Closing:</b> So, in the end, …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ezra 5:3–6:15; Isa 55:11 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Ezra 5–6 is the book’s account of the temple’s completion, c. 516–515 BC. Keep the focus on the record found and the promise honoured. Say honestly that historians cannot confirm every detail of the Ezra story from outside sources, and that Christians read it as part of Scripture. Do not suggest that all Persian rulers shared the Jewish faith.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How do leaders, councils and iwi share information with their communities in Aotearoa today? Think of newsletters, hui, notices, radio, websites. Which methods reach everyone, and which might leave people out? (Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to share how information moves in your rohe; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Darius’s own inscription shows him winning against rebels, and Greek writers show him as an enemy. Name both as perspectives rather than ‘the truth’. Some students may be Iranian-NZ or from Muslim backgrounds: treat Iranian history as a heritage to be proud of, not as a villain’s story, and never single students out.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Baton (scroll tube) and a short written message</li><li>Timeline strip with new date cards (522 BC, 518 BC, 516 BC)</li><li>Map of the empire’s provinces (app or printed)</li><li>Daric template and foil</li><li>Path A inspection-report sheet (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Behistun Inscription image</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain how each tool helped, not just name it?</li><li>Who used a source name (Herodotus, Behistun) correctly?</li><li>What do I need to revisit tomorrow before we measure the road?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: The Royal Road: A Highway Through the Landscape":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Royal Road: A Highway Through the Landscape — Why do towns grow along a road?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Routes &amp; Settlements</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 3: The Royal Road (522–486 BC) · Behistun, Susa, Persepolis, the Sardis–Susa road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Community &amp; participation; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Ecological sustainability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how roads shape settlements and to use scale to measure distance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use a scale bar to measure distance on a map.</li><li>I can explain why settlements grow along routes.</li><li>I can describe the levels of settlement along a route.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy of scale calculations; the reasoning in Path B or C (reasons for the settlement or site); photographs of Path products.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Measures a distance with support and names one reason roads matter.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Uses the scale bar accurately and explains why settlements grow on routes.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Calculates distances and times correctly, and explains how a station could grow into a village or town.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates a site or route, weighs advantages and disadvantages, and compares with another place.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>route</b> — A way from one place to another.</li><li><b>relay station</b> — A stopping place on a road where riders swap tired horses for fresh ones.</li><li><b>scale bar</b> — A line on a map showing what a distance on the map equals in real life.</li><li><b>pass</b> — A gap between mountains that people can travel through.</li><li><b>terrace</b> — A flat, raised platform, built up from the ground.</li><li><b>hierarchy</b> — An order from smallest to largest, or least to most important.</li><li><b>capital</b> — The most important city, where the ruler and government are based.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>String and Scale</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The mud is still deep, so Shirin spreads the great map of the Royal Road across the ground. ‘We cannot ride today, so we shall measure instead,’ she says. ‘String along the road, then lay it against the scale bar. How many kilometres is it? And how long would it take if you walked 30 kilometres a day? How long by relay?’ Gandom the camel has already tried to eat the string.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — name two tools of Darius; what is a satrap; what does ‘about 2,700 km’ tell us about the empire?</li><li>Give each Caravan a piece of string, a map and a scale bar. Teams lay the string along the road from Sardis to Susa, mark the length, and measure it against the scale bar.</li><li>Calculate: 2,700 ÷ 30 = about 90 days on foot. Then: if royal riders took about a week, roughly how far per day? (about 385 km). Compare with a modern car at 100 km/h.</li><li>Take two predictions about why the relay was so much faster (fresh horses, no stopping to rest, a fixed route).</li><li>Share LI/SC (students read aloud).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: the four tools of Darius; satrap; why records mattered.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A road is more than a line on a map. Follow the Royal Road and watch how a route can make a town, and a town can make a city.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scale (5 min): demonstrate on the board: 1 cm = 100 km. Students practise two measurements on mini-whiteboards.</li><li>Route features (3 min): show where the road meets rivers, passes and deserts. Ask: ‘What would a rider need to cross each?’</li><li>Settlement growth (4 min): act out the growth. One student sits as a ‘station’; add a stable, a market, a house. Draw the hierarchy: station → village → town → capital.</li><li>Persepolis (3 min): show plan and photos. Teams predict why the kings chose this place; reveal the card after.</li><li>Be careful: numbers like 111 stations come from Herodotus. Say ‘about’ or ‘according to’.</li><li><b>Measuring the road:</b> Maps use a <b>scale</b>. If <b>1 cm</b> on the map equals <b>100 km</b>, a road that measures 27 cm is about <b>2,700 km</b>. That is about seven trips from Christchurch to Dunedin (roughly 360 km each way, depending on the route), and further than the whole length of New Zealand. Use a string for a winding road, then lay it against the scale bar.</li><li><b>The shape of the road:</b> The Royal Road ran from <b>Sardis</b> near the Aegean coast, across the plains and mountains of Anatolia, through Assyria and along the foothills of the Zagros to <b>Susa</b>. Travellers had to deal with <b>rivers</b>, <b>mountain passes</b> and <b>hot, dry land</b>. Roads usually follow the easiest way, but where a pass or a river crossing is the only way, everyone has to go through it.</li><li><b>Stations a day apart:</b> Herodotus says the road had about <b>111 stations</b>, each roughly a day’s journey apart for an ordinary traveller. At each one, a royal rider could hand the message to a fresh rider with a fresh horse. Think of it like a <b>relay race</b>: nobody runs the whole way, and nobody gets tired.</li><li><b>How a road makes a town:</b> A station needs food, water, a stable and beds. Soon traders stop there too, and farmers come to sell food. A <b>station</b> becomes a <b>village</b>, a village becomes a <b>town</b>, and a town on several roads can become a <b>capital</b>. Roads <i>pull</i> settlement towards them.</li><li><b>Persepolis: a site chosen with care:</b> <b>Persepolis</b> was begun about <b>518 BC</b>. It stands on a huge stone <b>terrace</b> at the foot of <b>Mount Rahmat</b> on the open <b>Marvdasht plain</b> in Persis. Why there? The land is flat enough for big halls and ceremonies, the mountain gives a backdrop and some defence, there is good stone nearby, and it is in the Persian homeland. It was a place for great ceremonies, rather than an everyday city.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: From Station to Capital</b> — Put the settlement levels in order from smallest to largest.</li><li><b>match: Map Words</b> — Match each map word to its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Good Site or Poor Site?</b> — Persepolis was built on a terrace beside Mount Rahmat. Sort these statements about the site.</li><li><b>reveal: Calculate the Journey</b> — Tap each card to check your thinking.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Scale Map and Distance Challenge:</b> Draw a map of the Royal Road with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Mark at least six stops, then calculate the distance between two stops and the time it would take at 30 km/day and by relay.</li><li><b>Path B — The Station Master’s Handbook:</b> Design a relay station and write its rules. Show the buildings, the animals, the supplies and the people. Then predict how a village might grow around it.</li><li><b>Path C — Persepolis Site Plan:</b> Annotate a plan of the Persepolis terrace. Label the stairs, halls, gates and treasury, then give three reasons the site was chosen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Isa 40:3–4; Isa 35:8 — Isaiah speaks of a voice calling to prepare a way: valleys lifted up, mountains made low, rough ground made smooth. Elsewhere he describes a highway where the redeemed will walk, a road marked out for God’s people.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What is special about ‘making a way’ for others? Who builds the road, and who walks it?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persian roads carried messages, soldiers, traders and officials. Isaiah’s picture of a road is about God coming to His people and bringing them home. How does a road join people, ideas and faith? Is a road good or bad in itself, or does it depend on how it is used?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Name one ‘road’ (a kindness, a welcome, an invitation) you can build for a new student or a lonely classmate this week.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Scale Map and Distance Challenge</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a map of the Royal Road with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Mark at least six stops, then calculate the distance between two stops and the time it would take at 30 km/day and by relay.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose, scale bar and key</li><li>Sardis and Susa marked clearly</li><li>At least six stops labelled</li><li>One distance measured with string and scale</li><li>Travel time calculated for walking and relay</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map and a ready-made scale bar; complete the measure frame with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with all the map conventions and two calculations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the time for a rider who travels at 200 km/day, and compare with the relay. Explain why the relay is faster than one rider.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Measure:</b> My string measured ___ cm. The scale says 1 cm = ___ km. So the distance is ___ km. <b>Walking:</b> ___ km ÷ 30 = ___ days. <b>Relay:</b> A rider might do about 385 km a day, so ___ km ÷ 385 = ___ days.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: The Station Master’s Handbook</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a relay station and write its rules. Show the buildings, the animals, the supplies and the people. Then predict how a village might grow around it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plan of the station with labelled buildings</li><li>At least five rules for riders and station staff</li><li>A list of supplies the station needs</li><li>A prediction of how a village could grow, with a reason</li><li>Labels and key are clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the station template and choose five buildings from the word bank. Complete the prediction frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own station plan with rules and a prediction.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second station a day’s ride away and explain how the two might compete or cooperate as they grow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Buildings:</b> stable, well, store, rest room … <b>Rules:</b> 1 Fresh horse for every rider. 2 … <b>Supplies:</b> food, water, … <b>Prediction:</b> In ten years, the station might become a village because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Persepolis Site Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Annotate a plan of the Persepolis terrace. Label the stairs, halls, gates and treasury, then give three reasons the site was chosen.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I labelled the terrace, stairs and at least three buildings</li><li>I wrote three reasons the site was chosen</li><li>I included one disadvantage of the site</li><li>I used a key or numbers</li><li>I can explain what the site was used for</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-labelled plan with a bank of reasons; match the reasons to the right feature.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Annotate independently and give three reasons with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare Persepolis with Susa as a capital. Which was better for ruling the empire, and which for great ceremonies? Justify your view.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Labels:</b> terrace · stairway · Gate of All Nations · Apadana · Treasury · Hall of 100 Columns. <b>Reasons:</b> The site was chosen because (1) … (2) … (3) … <b>Disadvantage:</b> A problem might be …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Isa 40:3–4; Isa 35:8 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Isaiah 40 is poetry, with the road imagery used for God’s coming and the return from exile. Do not claim the Persian road was ‘the’ road of Isaiah. Use it as a picture: when we prepare a way for others, we reflect something of God’s heart. Keep it practical and kind.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Think about ara and tracks in Aotearoa: well-known travel routes, including trails used to carry pounamu, and how roads and rail shaped New Zealand towns. How did the route to the West Coast shape Arthur’s Pass? Where does your own town sit on a route? (Invite local iwi/hapū or your Māori Education lead to share the stories of ara in your rohe; avoid generalising across iwi.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep references to modern Iran, Iraq and Turkey neutral and respectful: this week is about an ancient road, not modern borders. The Royal Road was also used by armies and tax collectors, and people had little say. Talk about both the benefits and the costs.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large map of the Royal Road with scale bar</li><li>String, scale rulers, calculators</li><li>Atlases</li><li>Persepolis plan and photos (app or printed)</li><li>Relay station planning sheets</li><li>Local-area map for the NZ comparison</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students use the scale bar correctly, or just guess?</li><li>Could they explain why a town grows on a route, not only that it does?</li><li>Which scaffold helped the supported group most?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Lions, Leopards & the Royal Hunt: Predators in the Food Web":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lions, Leopards &amp; the Royal Hunt: Predators in the Food Web — What happens when the top predator disappears?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Food Webs &amp; Predators</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 3: The Royal Road (522–486 BC) · Behistun, Susa, Persepolis, the Sardis–Susa road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to build food webs and explain what happens when a predator disappears.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can draw a food web with arrows showing the flow of energy.</li><li>I can explain what an apex predator is.</li><li>I can predict what happens when one species is removed.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy of arrows (food → eater); the explanation of what happens when a predator is removed; use of vocabulary.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Draws a food chain with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Draws a food web with correctly directed arrows and defines apex predator.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains what happens when a species is removed, using cause and effect and vocabulary.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Predicts and evaluates multiple effects, noting uncertainty, and links to a real conservation example.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>food chain</b> — A single line showing who eats whom: grass → ibex → leopard.</li><li><b>food web</b> — Many food chains joined together to show all the feeding links in a community.</li><li><b>producer</b> — A living thing that makes its own food, such as a plant.</li><li><b>consumer</b> — A living thing that gets energy by eating others.</li><li><b>predator / prey</b> — The animal that hunts / the animal that is hunted.</li><li><b>apex predator</b> — A top predator with few or no natural enemies.</li><li><b>decomposer</b> — A living thing (such as a fungus or bacterium) that breaks down dead material.</li><li><b>extinct</b> — No longer alive anywhere in the world.</li><li><b>endangered</b> — At risk of becoming extinct.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The String Web Game</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The mudslide has cleared one side of the pass, and Shirin leads you into a hunting park: walls, trees, a stream, and a thousand rustles in the grass. ‘Persian kings kept enclosed gardens full of animals,’ she says. ‘But even a king’s garden is a web. Take a label, Courier. Hold the wool. Pull one thread… and see what moves.’ Gandom the camel is already being very careful not to stand on the string.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: prepare labels: grass, ibex, onager, leopard, lion, golden eagle, decomposer. A class of 25+ can add extra grass, extra ibex and so on.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — what is a scale bar; what is a relay station; name one level of settlement.</li><li>Students stand in a circle holding their label. Start with the sun-fed grass holding the wool. Pass the wool to something that eats grass (ibex/onager), then to what eats them (leopard/lion/eagle), and onwards, always holding a piece of the string. Everything eventually links to the decomposer.</li><li>Remove the lion: that student drops the string. Ask: who feels the slack? Who feels it next? What would the ibex and onager do? What would happen to the grass?</li><li>Share LI/SC; students read aloud. Take care that the ‘removed’ student is not left out. Give them a role as observer or recorder.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: scale bar; relay station; the levels from station to capital.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Every animal is part of a web. Meet the great cats of the old Persian lands, and find out what happens when the top of the web is lost.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Vocabulary (4 min): build the ladder together: producer → consumer → predator → apex predator, with decomposers recycling at the end.</li><li>Food web (5 min): draw a web on the board with the class. Insist that arrows point from food to eater. Check with the whiteboard: ‘Which way does the arrow go from grass to ibex?’</li><li>Persian cats (4 min): use the Animal Cards (Asiatic lion, Caspian tiger). Talk about extinction with care: this is a reason to protect, not to despair.</li><li>Cascade (2 min): return to the string game. Use the cause-and-effect chain: lion gone → more prey → less grass.</li><li>Be careful with dates: ‘early 1940s’ for lions in Iran, ‘about the 1970s’ for the Caspian tiger. Say that exact dates are uncertain.</li><li><b>Chain or web?:</b> A <b>food chain</b> is one line: <b>grass → ibex → leopard</b>. But most animals eat more than one thing, and are eaten by more than one thing, so the lines tangle into a <b>food web</b>. The <b>arrows</b> show the flow of <b>energy</b>: they point <i>from</i> the food <i>to</i> the animal that eats it. Plants (producers) start the flow with energy from the sun.</li><li><b>Apex predators:</b> An <b>apex predator</b> is at the top of a food web, with few or no natural enemies. In the old Persian lands, apex predators included the <b>Asiatic lion</b>, the <b>Caspian tiger</b> and the <b>Persian leopard</b>. They help keep prey populations in balance, which in turn protects plants.</li><li><b>The Asiatic lion:</b> The <b>Asiatic lion</b> once lived across south-west Asia, including parts of Iran and Iraq. Lions disappeared from Iran in the early <b>1940s</b>, after hunting and the loss of their habitat. Today a small population survives in the <b>Gir Forest</b> of India. Persian reliefs and carvings often show lions, as symbols of power.</li><li><b>The Caspian tiger:</b> The <b>Caspian tiger</b> lived in the forests and reed-beds near the Caspian Sea and in Central Asia. It is now <b>extinct</b>, with the last known animals gone by about the <b>1970s</b>. Scientists have found that its DNA is very close to the Siberian (Amur) tiger. The <b>Persian leopard</b> survives, but is <b>endangered</b>: a good reason to protect it.</li><li><b>The royal paradise:</b> Persian kings built walled gardens and hunting parks with trees, water and animals. The Old Persian word <i>pairidaeza</i> meant ‘walled enclosure’, and it is where our word <b>paradise</b> comes from. Reliefs and later writing show kings hunting lions: partly sport, and partly a show of power. Today we think more about <b>protecting</b> animals than hunting them.</li><li><b>What if the top goes missing?:</b> Remove the top predator and the animals it hunted may <b>increase</b>. More ibex and onager eat more plants. Plants can be <b>overgrazed</b>, soil may wash away, and other animals can lose food and shelter. Scientists call this a <b>cascade</b>. It does not always happen the same way, so we say ‘<i>may</i>’ and ‘<i>often</i>’, not ‘always’.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>animalCards</li><li><b>order: The Cascade</b> — Put this cause-and-effect chain in order.</li><li><b>sort: Producer, Consumer or Decomposer?</b> — Sort each living thing by its job in a food web.</li><li><b>reveal: Which Way Does the Arrow Point?</b> — Tap each card to check the direction of the arrow.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — ‘What If?’ Food Web:</b> Draw a food web for the Zagros or Persian grasslands with at least eight living things. Add arrows for the flow of energy. Then write five ‘What if…?’ predictions.</li><li><b>Path B — Design the Perfect Predator:</b> Draw and label an imaginary predator for a Persian habitat, using only real types of adaptation. Explain how each feature helps it catch food and survive.</li><li><b>Path C — Ecosystem Role-Play Report:</b> Re-run the string web with a narrator. Your Caravan performs a ‘predator disappears’ scene, then writes a short report on what the class learned.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Daniel 6:16–23; Psalm 104:21 — In Daniel, the king is sorry that he must send Daniel into the lions’ den, and he spends a sleepless night. In the morning, Daniel says that God sent His angel and shut the lions’ mouths. The psalmist, meanwhile, says that young lions roar for their prey and seek their food from God.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> The lions are in God’s care and still dangerous. What does that teach us about trusting God, and about respecting the power of wild animals?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Daniel’s courage is set beside the lions’ real strength. The story does not say lions are weak or that danger is not real. How does Daniel’s trust compare with the way the king trusted his own power? (In Daniel 6, ‘Darius the Mede’ is the king. Historians and Christians debate how he relates to Darius I, so we will not assume they are the same.)</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write: ‘One place I need courage this week is…’. Then pray for that courage, quietly or aloud.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: ‘What If?’ Food Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a food web for the Zagros or Persian grasslands with at least eight living things. Add arrows for the flow of energy. Then write five ‘What if…?’ predictions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At least eight organisms, including a producer, a decomposer and an apex predator</li><li>Every arrow points from food to eater</li><li>I wrote five ‘What if…?’ predictions</li><li>Each prediction has a ‘because’</li><li>I used the words predator, prey and apex predator</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with a half-drawn web; add arrows and three more organisms. Use the prediction frame for three ‘What if…?’ questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently and write five predictions with reasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose one prediction and trace its effects for three steps (first, then, after that), explaining why a scientist cannot be sure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Organisms:</b> grass, oak, ibex, onager, hare, leopard, lion, eagle, fungus. <b>Prediction frame:</b> If the ___ disappeared, then ___ would … because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Design the Perfect Predator</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw and label an imaginary predator for a Persian habitat, using only real types of adaptation. Explain how each feature helps it catch food and survive.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A clear, annotated sketch</li><li>At least four adaptations, all based on real animals</li><li>Each adaptation explained: ‘has… which helps…’</li><li>I named its habitat and its prey</li><li>I named one weakness or limit</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn outline and the adaptation bank; complete four ‘has… which helps…’ sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with four adaptations and a weakness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second drawing of its prey, with adaptations to avoid it. Explain how the two ‘race’ each other over time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Adaptation bank:</b> forward-facing eyes, camouflage fur, retractable claws, powerful jaws, night vision, speed, patience. <b>Frame:</b> My predator has ___ which helps it ___. <b>Weakness:</b> It might struggle when …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Ecosystem Role-Play Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Re-run the string web with a narrator. Your Caravan performs a ‘predator disappears’ scene, then writes a short report on what the class learned.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each person has a role (producer, prey, predator, decomposer, narrator)</li><li>The narrator explains each link clearly</li><li>We removed one species and showed the effects</li><li>The report names three effects</li><li>The report says what we would do to protect the predator</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the script frame and perform in a small group; the narrator reads from the card.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own narration and a short report with three effects.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Run it twice (removing a different species each time) and compare the results in a table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Narrator frame:</b> The grass gives energy to … <b>Effect frame:</b> When the ___ was removed, the ___ … so the ___ … <b>Report:</b> We learned that … We would protect the ___ by …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Dan 6:16–23; Ps 104:21 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Be honest that the identity of ‘Darius the Mede’ is discussed among scholars; some link him to Cyrus’s general or governor, others to Darius I under a different title. We will not settle that today. Keep the focus on Daniel’s faithfulness and the real power of lions. Psalm 104 is praise poetry, not a science textbook.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> New Zealand’s native birds evolved with few land predators. Introduced stoats, rats and possums, and cats, have had a huge effect. Why are predator-free sanctuaries such as Zealandia, or predator control programmes, so important? What might a ‘cascade’ look like in a New Zealand forest? (Invite DOC or a local conservation group, and your Māori Education lead for the idea of kaitiakitanga; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Extinction can feel sad: present it as a reason to care, not to panic. Keep the hunting talk matter-of-fact: kings hunting lions was a display of power, and today we value protecting predators. Be careful that ‘royal hunt’ does not become a game that glorifies killing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Balls of wool; labels (grass, ibex, onager, leopard, lion, eagle, decomposer)</li><li>Animal Cards (Asiatic lion, Caspian tiger)</li><li>Food web planning sheets</li><li>Images of Persian hunting scenes and ‘paradise’ gardens</li><li>Large paper for Path A and Path C</li><li>Clipboards and pencils</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students point the arrows from food to eater?</li><li>Who could explain a cascade with at least two steps?</li><li>What do I need to revisit about the difference between a chain and a web?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Carving the Kings: Relief Sculpture of the Apadana":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Carving the Kings: Relief Sculpture of the Apadana — Carve a procession of nations — then make your own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Relief Sculpture</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 3: The Royal Road (522–486 BC) · Behistun, Susa, Persepolis, the Sardis–Susa road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Diversity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to create relief sculpture showing a procession.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can make a figure in profile.</li><li>I can repeat figures with rhythm.</li><li>I can describe what the Apadana reliefs show.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technique (profile, raised figure, rhythm) in the carving or embossing; the artist’s statement; the group’s contribution to the mural.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a simple figure with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Creates a figure in profile with some relief and repeats it with rhythm.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls relief and repetition, and explains what the Apadana reliefs show and why they were made.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines technique and meaning with intent, and evaluates whose story the reliefs tell.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>relief</b> — A sculpture where shapes stand out from a flat background.</li><li><b>low relief</b> — A relief that rises only a little from the background.</li><li><b>incised</b> — Carved into the surface as lines or grooves.</li><li><b>profile</b> — A figure seen from the side.</li><li><b>procession</b> — A line of people moving together in a ceremony.</li><li><b>delegation</b> — A group sent to represent their people or country.</li><li><b>emboss</b> — To press a pattern so that it stands out from a surface.</li><li><b>Apadana</b> — The great columned audience hall at Persepolis.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Spot the Procession</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At last the pass is open and the road leads to the great stone terrace. Up the steps, along the walls, a stone parade is marching: delegations from many nations, carrying gifts and leading animals. Shirin taps the carving. ‘Look carefully, Courier. Count the faces. Find the animals. Spot the pots and the cloth. Whose gifts are these? And why did the king want them carved in stone?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — what is an apex predator; which way do arrows point in a food web; one cause-and-effect from a missing predator.</li><li>Show 2–3 images of the Apadana reliefs. Students spot on mini-whiteboards: animals, textiles, vessels, repeated figures, profile views.</li><li>Take one share per spot. Ask: ‘How do you know they come from different places?’ (clothes, hairstyles, gifts).</li><li>Say honestly that we do not know exactly who each delegation was, and some labels are debated. Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: apex predator; arrows from food to eater; the cascade.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Stone that tells a story. Learn how relief sculpture works, and how to make your own procession.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Relief (3 min): show a soft clay slab. Press a shape and show low relief. Show an incised line.</li><li>Apadana (4 min): study one image together: what is repeated, what is different?</li><li>Interpretation (3 min): ask students to decide whose story the reliefs tell, and whose might be missing. Link to the bias lesson from Week 1.</li><li>Safety (3 min): model safe tool use. Set rules: sit down, tool away from body, hands behind the blade, tidy as you go. Use plastic knives only.</li><li>Model the first step of each Path (2 min). Remind students that Path C is a whole-class mural, with each Caravan creating a section.</li><li><b>What is a relief?:</b> A <b>relief</b> is a sculpture that stays attached to a flat background, so the shapes stand out. <b>Low relief</b> is shallow, and <b>high relief</b> stands out far. Some reliefs are <b>incised</b>: lines are cut into the surface. The carvings at Persepolis were originally <b>painted in bright colours</b>, though little paint is left today.</li><li><b>The Apadana stairways:</b> The <b>Apadana</b> was a huge columned hall at Persepolis, begun under Darius. Its stairways are covered in carvings of <b>delegations</b> from many parts of the empire, usually counted as about <b>23</b> groups, bringing gifts: animals, cloth, vessels, metal goods. Experts debate exactly who each group is, and some identifications are uncertain.</li><li><b>The art of the procession:</b> The figures walk in a line, in <b>profile</b>, one after another. This repetition gives <b>rhythm</b>, like marching. Small changes in clothes, hair, gifts and animals show who is from where. The same figure may repeat with only small differences, which is how carvers made huge scenes in a short time.</li><li><b>Why carve it?:</b> The reliefs show a peaceful, orderly empire with the king at the centre. This is how the Persian court <i>wanted</i> to be seen: it is art, but it is also a message. Notice what is <b>not</b> shown: the taxes, the soldiers and the people who did not want to be ruled. Art is a source with a point of view.</li><li><b>Safe carving: three ways:</b> <b>Soap:</b> scrape away the background with a plastic knife or blunt skewer, leaving the figure raised. <b>Clay:</b> press shapes and lines into a flat slab, or add small pieces to build up. <b>Foil:</b> press a pattern into foil over a soft pad with a blunt pencil. Always carve <b>away from your body</b>, keep your tools on the table, and keep the shavings in the tray.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Relief Words</b> — Match each art word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Making a Soap Relief</b> — Put the steps of soap carving in a sensible order.</li><li><b>reveal: Reading the Procession</b> — Tap each card to see what a clue in the relief might tell us.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Relief Figure:</b> Carve a gift-bearer in soap or clay. Show the figure in profile with something to carry, and keep the background flat.</li><li><b>Path B — Foil Emboss Panel:</b> Create an embossed foil panel showing a repeating line of figures. Use rhythm and a careful repeat.</li><li><b>Path C — Class Procession Mural: ‘The Procession of the Caravans’:</b> Each Caravan designs one section of a class mural. Show figures bringing gifts that reflect this week’s learning, such as a daric, a scroll, a station horn or a leopard.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Psalm 72:10–11; Philippians 2:9–11; Mark 10:42–45 — The psalm pictures kings from far away bringing gifts and bowing to a righteous king. Paul writes that God has given Jesus the highest name, and that every knee will one day bow to him. In Mark, Jesus tells his friends that those who rule over nations often lord it over them, but that whoever wants to be great must serve, because he came to serve and give his life for many.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> The reliefs show nations honouring one king. How is Jesus’ kingship different? What does it mean to be a servant-king?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Compare how Darius displayed power (stone, gifts, a staircase) with how Jesus used power (washing feet, serving, giving himself). What might each tell us about what makes a ruler great? (Be fair: Persian art also showed order and a peaceful empire, and many people valued that.)</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose one way you can serve someone this week. Write it on your Caravan Log and tell a partner.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Relief Figure</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Carve a gift-bearer in soap or clay. Show the figure in profile with something to carry, and keep the background flat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My figure is in profile</li><li>The figure is raised from a flat background</li><li>I included a gift or an animal</li><li>I added at least three details (clothes, hair, pattern)</li><li>I used the tools safely and tidied up</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline to press into the soap or clay; add three details from the detail bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design and carve your own figure with three details and an artist’s statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Carve two figures with a contrasting gift and clothes, and explain how you made them look like people from different places.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch the figure in profile. 2 Press or draw the outline on the soap/clay. 3 Remove the background in thin layers. 4 Add details with incised lines. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My figure comes from ___ and carries ___ because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Foil Emboss Panel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create an embossed foil panel showing a repeating line of figures. Use rhythm and a careful repeat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At least four figures in a row</li><li>The figures are in profile</li><li>The spacing is even (rhythm)</li><li>I changed one detail on each figure</li><li>The foil is smooth with a clear raised pattern</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a single template figure and trace four times, changing one detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own figure and repeat it at least four times, changing a detail each time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Alternate two different figures (A-B-A-B) and add an incised border.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Draw one figure on paper. 2 Place foil over a soft pad. 3 Trace the outline with a blunt pencil. 4 Repeat with a changed detail. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I repeated my figure ___ times so that the panel feels …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Class Procession Mural: ‘The Procession of the Caravans’</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Each Caravan designs one section of a class mural. Show figures bringing gifts that reflect this week’s learning, such as a daric, a scroll, a station horn or a leopard.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Our section joins neatly to the next</li><li>All figures are in profile and moving the same way</li><li>Each gift links to something we learned this week</li><li>We used a limited palette</li><li>Everyone contributed and we shared jobs</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with figure templates and choose gifts from a bank; assign one job to each team member.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design the section as a team, with gifts linked to learning, and a short caption.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a caption panel that explains each gift and what it says about the week, and propose how the whole mural should be displayed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Agree the gifts (a daric, a scroll, a baton, a map). 2 Sketch with the figures in a line. 3 Assign roles: outlines, colour, details. 4 Join the sections. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> Our section shows ___ bringing ___ because …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 72:10–11; Phil 2:9–11; Mark 10:42–45 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the comparison respectful: the reliefs are a masterpiece of craft, and Darius was a skilled organiser. The contrast is about how kingship is understood, not about running down Persian culture. Be careful with language about ‘false’ kings. Acknowledge that Muslim students may honour Jesus (Isa) as a prophet; do not press anyone to agree with the passage.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with the way carvings in Aotearoa tell stories: whakairo on a wharenui carries whakapapa and history. Invite your Māori Education lead or a local carver to guide this conversation. Do NOT imitate sacred or tapu carving; focus on the idea that carving can carry meaning and honour people.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian reliefs come from a living cultural heritage, so avoid describing them as ‘exotic’. Soap and clay carving should use only blunt, plastic tools and close supervision. Some students may have faith traditions that restrict the making of human figures: offer a non-figure option (a pattern or animal) without making a fuss.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Soap bars or air-dry clay; plastic knives, blunt skewers, lolly sticks</li><li>Foil, soft pencils, pads or foam for embossing</li><li>Large paper/butcher’s paper for the mural; paints</li><li>Images of the Apadana reliefs (app or printed)</li><li>Plastic trays to catch soap shavings</li><li>Aprons or old shirts</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Were the figures in profile, with rhythm?</li><li>Who needed help with safe tool use?</li><li>How well did Caravans share the mural jobs?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: The Royal Relay":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: The Royal Relay — Pass the baton. Win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 3: The Royal Road (522–486 BC) · Behistun, Susa, Persepolis, the Sardis–Susa road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about Darius, the Royal Road, food webs and relief sculpture, and to work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about Darius’s tools, the Royal Road, predators and the Apadana reliefs.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan like a relay team, with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Relay Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the idea to new situations.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Royal Relay</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The last stretch of the Swift Road waits ahead, and at every station a fresh rider stands ready. ‘A relay is not won by the fastest runner,’ says Shirin, ‘but by the team that passes the baton well. Write your Creed, Courier. How will your Caravan carry the message? With care, with courage, with kindness to the one who falls behind?’ A horn sounds from the next station. The Showdown begins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Relay Creed on a strip and reads it aloud.</li><li>Teachers may display the Creeds beside the Week 1 Creeds.</li><li>Before starting, remind students of the three recall areas this week: Darius’s tools, the food web, and what we learned in earlier weeks.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q4, Q6, Q10, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Relay Creed: ONE sentence about how your team will pass the baton well, supporting each other so no one is left behind. Starters: We will pass the baton by… / When someone stumbles, we will… / Our Caravan will always… / We will cheer on…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Ezra 6:1–12; Isa 40:3–4; Dan 6:19–23; Mark 10:42–45 — This week we met a record found in an archive so that a promise could be kept, a road made ready for others, a man of courage among lions, and a servant-king who gives his life for others.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most important ‘road’, ‘record’ or ‘promise’ you noticed this week?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s ideas, an organised empire, a food web, or a servant-king, made you think hardest?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Relay Creed together. Pray for each other and for the journey ahead.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Susa, the southern end of the Royal Road, was long before the home of which ancient people (Week 1)? A) Romans · B) Vikings · <b>C) Elamites ✔</b> · D) Mongols <em>Susa was an Elamite city, one of the world’s earliest, long before Darius made it a royal centre at the end of the Royal Road from Sardis.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. The gold coin introduced by Darius was the… <b>A) daric ✔</b> · B) drachma · C) dollar · D) denarius <em>The daric was the Persian gold coin, showing a kneeling archer. The denarius was Roman and the drachma was Greek.</em> [SS-EW]</li><li>3. The Behistun Inscription was written in how many languages? A) Two · B) Ten · <b>C) Three ✔</b> · D) One <em>Three: Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian. Later scholars used the three versions to help read cuneiform.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>4. The governors who ran the provinces of the Persian Empire were called… A) magi · B) immortals · C) scribes · <b>D) satraps ✔</b> <em>Satraps ran the satrapies (provinces), collecting taxes and keeping order. The King’s Eyes and Ears checked on them.</em> [SS-ICO] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>5. Darius began building which great ceremonial city? <b>A) Persepolis ✔</b> · B) Jerusalem · C) Sparta · D) Troy <em>Persepolis was begun about 518 BC on a terrace at the foot of Mount Rahmat on the Marvdasht plain.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>6. According to Herodotus, royal riders could cross the Royal Road in about… A) a year · B) a decade · <b>C) a week ✔</b> · D) a day <em>Herodotus says royal riders took about a week, with fresh riders and horses at relay stations, while ordinary travellers needed about three months. Remember that he is a Greek source, so we say ‘about’.</em> [SS-PE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>7. Why did Daniel end up in the lions’ den in the Bible story? A) He insulted the king · <b>B) He kept praying to God ✔</b> · C) He ran away · D) He stole <em>Daniel kept praying to God, even after a law was passed forbidding it. The story shows his courage and God’s care.</em> [RE]</li><li>8. Darius’s officials searched the archives and found a decree made by which earlier king (Week 2)? A) Xerxes · <b>B) Cyrus ✔</b> · C) Alexander · D) Cambyses <em>Ezra 6 tells how Cyrus’s earlier decree was found at Ecbatana, so work on the temple could continue. Record-keeping mattered.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>9. An apex predator is… A) a kind of grass · B) a decomposer · C) the smallest animal · <b>D) a top predator with few natural enemies ✔</b> <em>Apex predators sit at the top of a food web and have few or no natural enemies. Lions and leopards were apex predators of the old Persian lands.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Which best explains how Darius governed a huge empire? A) Luck · B) Only armies · <b>C) Provinces, a standard coin, roads and records ✔</b> · D) Good weather <em>Darius used satrapies with governors, the gold daric, the Royal Road with relay stations, and written records. Armies mattered too, but they were not the only tool.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. In a food web, the arrows show… A) which animal is cutest · B) time · C) distance · <b>D) the flow of energy ✔</b> <em>Arrows show the flow of energy and point from the food to the animal that eats it, for example grass → ibex → leopard.</em> [SC-NoS-C]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — If the top predator is removed from a food web, often… <b>A) prey increase and plants may be overgrazed ✔</b> · B) nothing changes · C) all plants vanish at once · D) rivers dry up <em>Without the top predator, grazers may increase and overgraze plants, which can harm other animals too. Scientists say ‘may’ and ‘often’ because the exact effects vary.</em> [SC-LW-Eco] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (for example roads and food webs, both about ‘links’) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ezra 6:1–12; Isa 40:3–4; Dan 6:19–23; Mark 10:42–45 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful; celebrate teamwork and effort, not only winning.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week: how communities share information; ara and tracks; predator-free New Zealand; carving that carries stories.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown: keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. In a relay, remind students that every runner counts.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching on Monday (the direction of food-web arrows, or Herodotus’ numbers)?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 4: Pride Before the Fall":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Marathon, Thermopylae & Salamis: Whose Story?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Marathon, Thermopylae &amp; Salamis: Whose Story? — Same battle, two headlines — who do we believe?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 4: Pride Before the Fall (490–479 BC) · Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Respect; Inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how Persians and Greeks fought and to compare perspectives on the wars.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can put four events in order: Marathon (490 BC), Thermopylae and Salamis (480 BC), Plataea (479 BC).</li><li>I can say why the Greek version is not the only version.</li><li>I can write about one event from two different viewpoints.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Two Voices Report (or map/drama), the ‘Why they differ’ sentence, and observation of students using ‘perspective’ and ‘bias’ correctly.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names an event or two with support; notices that two reports differ.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Orders the four events correctly and writes from two viewpoints with different language.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why accounts differ using perspective and bias, and notes whose voices are missing.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates the reliability of Herodotus and applies the idea to a modern or NZ example.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>perspective</b> — The point of view someone tells a story from, shaped by who they are and what they want.</li><li><b>invasion</b> — When an army enters another people’s land to take control of it.</li><li><b>strait</b> — A narrow stretch of water joining two larger seas.</li><li><b>Hellespont</b> — The narrow strait (today called the Dardanelles) between Asia and Europe.</li><li><b>Herodotus</b> — A Greek writer, often called the ‘Father of History’, who wrote about the wars some decades later.</li><li><b>propaganda</b> — Information shaped to make one side look good and the other look bad.</li><li><b>primary / secondary source</b> — A primary source comes from the time; a secondary source is written later about it.</li><li><b>Immortals</b> — The Persian elite guard, said to number about 10,000.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Two Newspapers, One Battle</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Courier! The old harbour office is full of wet papers, and two of them caught my eye. One, from a Greek herald, shouts: ‘Brave defenders stand against a mighty horde!’ The other, from a Persian scribe, says: ‘Royal forces advance; the campaign continues as the king commands.’ Same battle. Different words. And look — a silver-edged boot print on this one. Someone has been reading these before us. Read both. What changed between them… and why?</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: print the two teacher-made headline cards (Greek Herald / Persian Scribe) about Thermopylae or Marathon. Make them short, vivid and clearly labelled as modern ‘model’ sources written for the lesson, not ancient quotations.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Week 3 — name the road that joined Sardis to Susa; who built Persepolis; one thing Darius organised.</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud. Hand each Caravan both cards. 4 minutes: highlight in one colour what is the SAME in both reports and another colour what is DIFFERENT (words, feelings, who is the hero).</li><li>Quick share: ‘Which words are loaded?’ (e.g. ‘horde’, ‘advance’). Do NOT name a winner for ‘truth’ yet — ask who might have written it, and why.</li><li>Share the LI/SC; students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Week 3: the Royal Road (Sardis to Susa); Darius’s organisation of the empire; Persepolis.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Four events, two sides, and a mountain of questions. Follow the armies — then ask who is telling the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline strip (4 min): add four date cards in order — 490 Marathon, 480 Thermopylae and Salamis, 479 Plataea. Mention Darius dying in 486 BC between the two invasions.</li><li>Story (6 min): tell the four events with the big map. Use phrases such as ‘according to Herodotus…’ and ‘historians think…’ for contested details (the whipped sea, the size of the army, the exact numbers at Thermopylae).</li><li>Sources (3 min): who is missing? Show the sorter: ‘Greek writer’ vs ‘Persian inscription’ vs ‘archaeology’ — what can each tell us, and what can’t it?</li><li>Balance (2 min): name courage on both sides. Persian soldiers came from many peoples in the empire and were often serving as ordered. Avoid ‘goodies and baddies’; ask ‘what did each side want?’</li><li>Model (2 min): write on the board one sentence from each side about the same moment (e.g. the pass). Students notice which words change.</li><li><b>490 BC: the beach at Marathon:</b> Darius sent a fleet across the Aegean Sea to punish Athens, which had helped Greek cities in Persian territory that rebelled a few years earlier. The Persians landed at the plain of <b>Marathon</b>. The Athenians (helped by a small force from Plataea) fought them and <b>won</b>. The Persian ships sailed home. For Darius it was a setback, not the end of the empire — he began planning a bigger campaign, but died in <b>486 BC</b>.</li><li><b>480 BC: the bridge of boats:</b> Darius’s son <b>Xerxes</b> gathered a huge army and fleet. To cross the narrow <b>Hellespont</b> his engineers built <b>bridges of boats</b> lashed together. Herodotus says a storm wrecked the first attempt, and that an angry Xerxes had the sea whipped. Historians doubt that detail — it makes a good story for people who wanted to show Xerxes as proud. The army’s size is also uncertain: Herodotus gave millions, but most modern scholars think it was far smaller (though still very large).</li><li><b>Thermopylae: the Hot Gates:</b> A narrow pass between mountain and sea, named for its hot springs. A small Greek force, led by the Spartan king <b>Leonidas</b>, held it against the Persians for several days. Then a local man showed the Persians a hidden path round the back, and the defenders were surrounded. The Greek story is one of heroic courage. Persian forces won the pass and marched on to burn Athens.</li><li><b>Salamis and Plataea:</b> In <b>480 BC</b> the Greek fleet lured Xerxes’s ships into the narrow <b>strait of Salamis</b>, where the crowded Persian ships struggled to move — a Greek victory. In <b>479 BC</b> the Greeks defeated the Persian army at <b>Plataea</b> and the invasion ended. Persia was <i>still</i> a huge, rich empire afterwards — the wars were a very big event for the Greeks, and a smaller one on the long map of Persian history.</li><li><b>Whose story do we have?:</b> Most of what we know comes from <b>Herodotus</b> (a Greek, writing decades later) and other Greek writers. Very few Persian accounts of these wars survive. Royal Persian inscriptions boast about kings but rarely describe defeats. Herodotus was <i>not</i> neutral — but he also tried to record what both sides said, and he is still our best long account. A good historian reads him carefully, checks him against archaeology, and remembers whose voices are missing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: Order the Wars</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>sort: Legend, Possible Fact or Fact?</b> — Sort each statement. Remember: a claim is only a ‘Fact’ when different kinds of evidence support it.</li><li><b>match: Whose Words?</b> — Match each word to what it means.</li><li><b>reveal: Spot the Loaded Word</b> — Tap each card. Decide what is different about the two ways of saying the same thing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Two Voices Report:</b> Write TWO short reports of the same event: one by ‘The Greek Herald’ and one by ‘The Persian Scribe’. Then add a note explaining why they sound different.</li><li><b>Path B — Campaign Map &amp; Timeline:</b> Draw a map of the Aegean and western Persian Empire. Show the invasion routes with arrows, label the battles and add a dated timeline strip along the bottom.</li><li><b>Path C — Hot-Seat Council Drama:</b> Perform Xerxes’s war council as a 2-minute scene: one adviser says ‘invade now’, another says ‘be careful’. Xerxes decides in the hot seat and the class asks him questions.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Prov 16:18; Prov 21:30–31; Mark 4:35–41 — Proverbs says that pride goes before destruction and a haughty spirit before a fall, and that a horse may be made ready for battle but victory belongs to the Lord. In Mark 4, a storm rises while Jesus and his friends cross the lake; Jesus speaks to the wind and waves, and they go calm. The disciples are amazed and ask, ‘Who is this?’</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why do powerful people so often fall? What is the difference between a leader who is strong and a leader who is proud?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Greek stories (which may not be true) say Xerxes had the sea whipped. Jesus did not punish the sea — he calmed it, with authority and care. What is the difference between trying to control creation and being its Lord? What does each story tell us about humility?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write about a time pride made something worse for you, and one way humility could help next time.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Two Voices Report</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write TWO short reports of the same event: one by ‘The Greek Herald’ and one by ‘The Persian Scribe’. Then add a note explaining why they sound different.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE event (Marathon, Thermopylae or Salamis)</li><li>My Greek report has a headline and 3–4 sentences</li><li>My Persian report has a headline and 3–4 sentences</li><li>The two reports use different words and feelings for the same facts</li><li>I wrote a ‘Why they differ’ note that mentions perspective or bias</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the sentence frames and the word bank (brave · mighty · defended · advanced · fleet · pass · strait). Write one report each with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write both reports independently, using at least two vocabulary words, and a clear ‘Why they differ’ note.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a third voice (an ordinary soldier, a farmer or a sailor) and explain what even a third viewpoint would still not tell us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Greek Herald headline:</b> … <b>Report:</b> Today at ___ our people … <b>Persian Scribe headline:</b> … <b>Report:</b> By the king’s command, our forces at ___ … <b>Why they differ:</b> The Herald wants readers to feel ___. The Scribe wants readers to feel ___. This is called ___ (perspective / bias).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Campaign Map &amp;amp; Timeline</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a map of the Aegean and western Persian Empire. Show the invasion routes with arrows, label the battles and add a dated timeline strip along the bottom.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose and key</li><li>Persian invasion arrows for 490 BC and 480 BC drawn clearly</li><li>Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis and Plataea labelled</li><li>Timeline strip with all four dates in the right order</li><li>I can explain one way geography affected a battle</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map with the sea and coastline; add arrows, five labels and a four-box timeline.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently with a key and a full timeline.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘What each side wanted’ panel and a ‘What we do not know’ panel that names two gaps in the evidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Places:</b> Susa · Sardis · Hellespont · Thermopylae · Athens · Marathon · Salamis · Plataea. <b>Dates:</b> 490 · 486 (Darius dies) · 480 · 479. <b>Say it:</b> At ___ in ___ BC, the ___ …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Hot-Seat Council Drama</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Perform Xerxes’s war council as a 2-minute scene: one adviser says ‘invade now’, another says ‘be careful’. Xerxes decides in the hot seat and the class asks him questions.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each speaker gave a clear reason for their view</li><li>Xerxes explained his choice with a ‘because’</li><li>We showed pride AND a cautious voice</li><li>Our scene lasted about 2 minutes</li><li>I can say what the scene shows about why leaders make rash choices</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the role cards with ready-made lines; swap parts and perform twice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own two reasons per adviser and perform with expression.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a short epilogue narrated by Herodotus and one by a Persian scribe describing the council differently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Bold adviser:</b> ‘Great king, we must go now because …’ <b>Cautious adviser:</b> ‘Great king, may I warn you …’ <b>Xerxes:</b> ‘I have decided … because …’ <b>Class question:</b> ‘Why did you ignore …?’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Prov 16:18; Prov 21:30–31; Mark 4:35–41 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Be careful not to make ‘Persians = proud, Greeks = good’. The Proverbs are about every human heart, including ours. Some details of the whipped-sea story are doubted by historians — name this honestly. Keep the tone reflective and gentle.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Think of a story in Aotearoa told differently by different groups (for example, an event recorded in both a newspaper and in local oral history). How do we respect more than one account? Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide this; avoid pan-Māori generalisations, and treat any taonga or tribal accounts as theirs to share.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Avoid ‘goodies and baddies’. Greek writers shaped how the West remembers Persia, and some of those images (‘barbarian horde’, ‘despot’) are stereotypes. Say clearly that courage and loss belong to people on both sides. Some students may be Iranian-NZ or from Muslim backgrounds; never ask them to speak for ‘Persian’ opinion.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two ‘headline’ cards: Greek Herald and Persian Scribe (teacher-made)</li><li>Aegean map and timeline strip with date cards</li><li>Campaign map outline (print from Teacher View)</li><li>Hot-Seat role cards for Path C (Xerxes, cautious adviser, bold adviser, scribe)</li><li>Perspective-sorter cards (app or printed)</li><li>Recording device for Path C (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain WHY two reports differ, not just that they do?</li><li>Who still thinks ‘the Greek version’ is the only version?</li><li>What will I adjust in tomorrow’s scale and map work?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Straits, Harbours & Chokepoints: The Scale of an Empire":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Straits, Harbours &amp; Chokepoints: The Scale of an Empire — How many New Zealands fit inside Persia — and why does a narrow place matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Settlements &amp; Cities</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 4: Pride Before the Fall (490–479 BC) · Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation; Ecological sustainability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how geography shapes war and settlement and to compare sizes of places using a scale.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how a strait or pass affects how an army moves.</li><li>I can compare the size of the empire with Aotearoa New Zealand using a scale.</li><li>I can explain why harbour towns grow.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Scale use (count and calculation), the reasoning in Path B or C (three reasons), and a photograph of the model or map.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Places cut-outs or identifies a strait with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Estimates the empire’s size using the scale and explains one chokepoint.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how geography helps or hinders armies and why harbour towns grow, with evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares two settlements or battles and evaluates trade-offs; uses scale calculations accurately.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>chokepoint</b> — A narrow place that everything has to pass through, like a pass or strait.</li><li><b>strait</b> — A narrow channel of water between two bigger bodies of water.</li><li><b>pass</b> — A gap between mountains that people can travel through.</li><li><b>harbour</b> — A sheltered place on the coast where ships can safely stop.</li><li><b>port</b> — A harbour town where goods and people come and go by ship.</li><li><b>scale</b> — How a distance or area on the map relates to the real one.</li><li><b>empire</b> — A very large area made up of many lands and peoples ruled by one government.</li><li><b>area</b> — The amount of flat surface something covers, measured in square kilometres.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>How Many NZs Fit in the Persian Empire?</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Shirin is standing on a table, with the empire spread beneath her. ‘Courier, quickly — everyone thinks the empire is huge, but nobody has measured it. Here are cut-outs of Aotearoa New Zealand. Place as many as you can on the map before the lamp burns out! My guess is… well, you tell me.’ Down on the quay, a new sign has been chalked: a silver arrow pointing towards the water. Someone is hurrying us along.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: print the empire map and NZ cut-outs to the same scale (the empire ≈ 5 million km², NZ ≈ 270,000 km², so about 18–20 NZs fit — a rough figure).</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — put Marathon, Thermopylae/Salamis and Plataea in order; what does ‘perspective’ mean; why is Herodotus not neutral?</li><li>Estimate (2 min): each Caravan writes a guess on a whiteboard BEFORE touching the cut-outs. Record guesses.</li><li>Tile the map (4 min): teams lay cut-outs across the empire (overlapping is fine, but encourage honest estimating). Compare their count with the guess. Reveal: roughly 20, depending on how the empire’s greatest extent is drawn.</li><li>Share the LI/SC. Make the point: the empire spanned from Greece’s coast to the Indus region; land this big needed roads, rivers and strong ports.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: the four events in order; perspective; why Herodotus is useful but not neutral.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The empire was enormous — but wars and trade still came down to a few narrow places. Read the map like a general, and like a harbour master.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scale (5 min): explain ‘area’ and ‘scale’. Demonstrate: if one NZ cut-out equals 270,000 km², what do 20 cut-outs equal? Link to multiplication (20 × 270,000 = 5,400,000 km²).</li><li>Chokepoints (6 min): on the map show the Hellespont, Thermopylae and Salamis. Use the app’s Chokepoint cards: why does being narrow help the defenders? What could go wrong (a hidden path)?</li><li>Harbours (4 min): ask what a good harbour needs (shelter, depth, land routes). Link to your own nearest harbour.</li><li>Close (3 min): quick whiteboard: ‘Name one way geography can beat a bigger army.’</li><li><b>An empire the size of twenty New Zealands:</b> At its greatest the Persian Empire covered about <b>5 million square kilometres</b> (scholars measure its edges a little differently), stretching from the Aegean coast and Egypt in the west to the Indus region in the east. Aotearoa New Zealand is about <b>270,000 km²</b>, so roughly <b>20 New Zealands</b> would fit inside! No empire before it had been so large.</li><li><b>The Hellespont: a gateway between two continents:</b> The <b>Hellespont</b> (today the Dardanelles) is a narrow strait between Asia and Europe — in places only about <b>1–2 km</b> wide. Armies moving west had to cross it, and so did ships carrying grain. Xerxes’s engineers lashed boats side by side and laid planks across them to make a <b>bridge of boats</b>. Strong winds and currents could easily wreck it.</li><li><b>Why narrow places help defenders:</b> At <b>Thermopylae</b> the pass was squeezed between a steep mountain and the sea. A big army can only attack as wide as the gap — so having more soldiers helps much less. At <b>Salamis</b> the strait was so narrow that large fleets got tangled. Narrow places are called <b>chokepoints</b>: they slow big armies and help the smaller side — unless a way around is found (as at Thermopylae).</li><li><b>Harbour towns and inland capitals:</b> Coastal cities such as <b>Miletus</b> and <b>Ephesus</b>, and <b>Athens</b>’s harbour, grew because ships brought trade, ideas and food. <b>Inland capitals</b> like Susa and Persepolis grew on rivers, roads and plains, and had mountains to protect them. A harbour needs <b>shelter</b> from storms, <b>deep water</b>, and <b>land routes</b> inland.</li><li><b>Aotearoa’s harbours:</b> Think of Lyttelton, Wellington or Tauranga. A good harbour is sheltered, deep and connected to roads and rail. Many Māori and later European communities settled by harbours for kai moana, safe landing and trade — each with its own history that belongs to the people of that place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>reveal: Chokepoint Cards</b> — Tap a card to see how each place squeezed an army or a fleet.</li><li><b>sort: Harbour or Inland Capital?</b> — Sort each feature into the kind of place it helps most.</li><li><b>match: Scale and Size</b> — Match each fact or word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Build a Bridge of Boats</b> — Put the steps in order to see how engineers might have bridged the Hellespont.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Empire vs NZ Map:</b> Overlay NZ cut-outs on the empire map using the scale. Write three observations about size, distance and what a ruler would need to govern so large an area.</li><li><b>Path B — Chokepoint Model:</b> Build a sand-tray or Lego model of Thermopylae or Salamis. Show the sea, the mountain, the narrow gap and where the armies or fleets would stand.</li><li><b>Path C — Design a Harbour Town:</b> Choose a spot on the NZ coast and design a harbour town. Justify your site with three reasons and compare it with a Greek or Persian-coast city such as Miletus or Athens’s harbour.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Job 38:8–11; Isa 40:15–17 — In Job, God asks who shut the sea behind doors and said, ‘This far and no further; here your proud waves must stop.’ In Isaiah, the nations are like a drop in a bucket and dust on the scales, and no empire is great in comparison with God.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> How big is God compared with the biggest empire ever? What do the sea’s limits tell us about who is in charge?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Xerxes bridged a sea and lashed its waves — but the sea stays within its edges. What is the difference between using the creation wisely and thinking we are above it?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a short, humble prayer of one or two sentences, thanking God for the sea, the shore or the harbour you know best.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Empire vs NZ Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Overlay NZ cut-outs on the empire map using the scale. Write three observations about size, distance and what a ruler would need to govern so large an area.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Map has title, compass rose and scale bar</li><li>I placed NZ cut-outs and counted them</li><li>My answer says ‘about 20’ and explains it is an estimate</li><li>I wrote 3 observations about size and distance</li><li>I wrote one question a ruler of such a large empire would face</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed map with a grid; count cut-outs and complete three sentence frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Overlay, count and write three observations in your own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the empire’s area from the scale (cut-outs × 270,000 km²), then compare it with a country of your choice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Count:</b> About ___ New Zealands fit in the empire. <b>Observation frames:</b> (1) The empire is much bigger than … (2) It would take ___ weeks to travel from … to … (3) A ruler would need … <b>Estimate note:</b> This is an estimate because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Chokepoint Model</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a sand-tray or Lego model of Thermopylae or Salamis. Show the sea, the mountain, the narrow gap and where the armies or fleets would stand.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I modelled the mountain, sea and narrow gap</li><li>I showed which side was the defenders and which the invaders</li><li>I used labels and a key</li><li>I explained why the narrow gap helped the defenders</li><li>I named one way the invaders might get round it</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with a base showing the mountain and sea; add pieces and four labels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build independently and explain with ‘because’ sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare Thermopylae (land) and Salamis (sea): what is the same, what is different about the chokepoint effect?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Say it:</b> The pass/strait is narrow, so the bigger army cannot … <b>What helped the defenders:</b> … <b>A way round:</b> The invaders could … (a hidden path, a longer route by sea).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Design a Harbour Town</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a spot on the NZ coast and design a harbour town. Justify your site with three reasons and compare it with a Greek or Persian-coast city such as Miletus or Athens’s harbour.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I chose one real or imagined NZ coastal site</li><li>I gave 3 reasons (shelter, depth, land routes, fresh water, food)</li><li>I named one hazard (storm, tsunami, erosion) and a plan</li><li>I compared with a Greek or Persian-coast harbour</li><li>I consulted local guidance about the history of the place</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pick from the reason bank (shelter · depth · routes · water · food) and complete the frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Give three reasons, one hazard and one comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw a simple plan of the town (wharf, market, road, warehouses) and explain how each part supports trade.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Reason frame:</b> I chose ___ because (1) …, (2) …, (3) …. <b>Hazard:</b> One risk is … so people could … <b>Compare:</b> Like Miletus/Athens’s harbour, my town …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Job 38:8–11; Isa 40:15–17 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Isaiah 40 is not belittling nations or people — it is celebrating the greatness of God. Avoid mocking Persia. Link to kaitiakitanga (guardianship) when talking about moana and harbours.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How big is your region compared with the Persian Empire? Compare the narrow passages of Aotearoa (Cook Strait, the Manawatū Gorge, Arthur’s Pass) with Thermopylae and the Hellespont. For the history of harbours near you, ask local iwi/hapū or your Māori Education lead; avoid generalising across iwi.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep focus on land and sea, not on a particular modern country’s politics. When naming present-day places (Turkey, Greece, Iran), use neutral language. Avoid describing the empire’s size as ‘better’ than any other people’s land.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large map of the empire at its greatest extent</li><li>Cut-out New Zealand shapes on card (to the map’s scale)</li><li>Rulers, atlases, string for measuring curves</li><li>Sand trays / Lego for Path B</li><li>NZ coastal map for Path C</li><li>Chokepoint cards (app or printed)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students use a scale to compare, or only count shapes?</li><li>Did students explain WHY a narrow place helps, not just name it?</li><li>Which scaffold helped the supported group most?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: The Desert Challenge: Adaptations in Dasht-e Kavir & Lut":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Desert Challenge: Adaptations in Dasht-e Kavir &amp; Lut — Who can survive where it never rains — and what makes the best fur?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Animal Communities &amp; Habitats</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 4: Pride Before the Fall (490–479 BC) · Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain desert adaptations and to carry out a fair test about insulation.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name three desert animals and give an adaptation for each.</li><li>I can plan a fair test and say what stays the same.</li><li>I can record data in a table and graph it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The fair-test planning sheet and results table; the graph; accurate use of ‘adaptation’, ‘nocturnal’ and ‘insulation’.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names a desert animal and one feature with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Names three desert animals with adaptations and records results in a table.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Plans a fair test, draws an accurate graph and explains how adaptations help survival.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates the fair test (reliability, improvements) and links desert adaptations to a different habitat.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>adaptation</b> — A feature or behaviour that helps a living thing survive in its habitat.</li><li><b>nocturnal</b> — Active at night and resting in the day.</li><li><b>abiotic factor</b> — A non-living part of a habitat, such as heat, water or sand.</li><li><b>insulation</b> — A material that slows down the movement of heat.</li><li><b>fair test</b> — An investigation where only one thing is changed and everything else stays the same.</li><li><b>variable</b> — Something in an experiment that can change.</li><li><b>conclusion</b> — What the results tell us, based on evidence.</li><li><b>dehydration</b> — When a body loses too much water.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Water for an Army</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A hot wind has blown the quay empty. Shirin unrolls a battered map and taps two blotches: Dasht-e Kavir and Dasht-e Lut — the two great deserts. ‘Courier, imagine an army marching across this. Soldiers, horses, camels, carts… and not a stream for days. How much water do you think it would need — in one day?’ Gandom the camel yawns, chews the map’s corner, and for once looks smug. Camels know something about deserts.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: set the water ratios on the board — a marching adult needs about 4 litres a day in heat; a horse about 35 litres; a camel can go several days without drinking (teacher guide: ‘a few days, depending on conditions’).</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — what is a chokepoint; roughly how many NZs fit inside the empire; why do harbour towns grow?</li><li>Calculate together (4 min): ‘An army of 10,000 soldiers and 1,000 horses — how many litres each day?’ (10,000 × 4 = 40,000 L; 1,000 × 35 = 35,000 L; total 75,000 L, about 75 tonnes of water!) Emphasise that Herodotus’s army numbers are far larger than most modern estimates; scholars disagree.</li><li>Discuss: why were deserts dangerous? Where would an army find water (springs, wells, qanats, rivers)?</li><li>Share the LI/SC; students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: chokepoint; scale and NZs in the empire; why harbour towns grow.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dasht-e Kavir and Dasht-e Lut: two of the harshest habitats on Earth. Meet the animals that make a home here.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Habitat (5 min): show desert photos. Ask for abiotic factors: heat, sand, salt, little water, big temperature swings between day and night.</li><li>Animals (6 min): use the app’s interactive desert scene; tap each animal for a body adaptation and a behaviour adaptation. Students tick them off on a desert-animal sheet.</li><li>Insulation link (4 min): ‘Why is fur good at keeping a cat warm at night? What about keeping the heat out in the day?’ Introduce insulation and the fair test for Path A.</li><li>Plan the fair test (5 min): decide on class rules — same water amount, same starting temperature, same beaker, same time intervals. Only the wrapping changes. Safety: the teacher handles warm water; do not use boiling water.</li><li>Animal Cards (1 min): introduce this week’s cards.</li><li><b>A habitat with big swings:</b> Deserts are shaped by <b>abiotic factors</b>: very little rain, scorching days and, in many places, <b>cold nights</b>. The <b>Dasht-e Lut</b> is one of the hottest places on Earth — satellites have recorded ground-surface temperatures above 70 °C (that is the ground, not the air). The <b>Dasht-e Kavir</b> is a vast salt desert, with salt crusts and mud flats. Life here must cope with heat, salt, and scarce water.</li><li><b>Sand cat: the barefoot desert hunter:</b> The <b>sand cat</b> is a small wild cat with thick fur growing between its toes — like <b>built-in sandshoes</b> that protect its paws from hot sand and help it walk without sinking. Large ears help it hear prey underground. It hunts at night, rests in burrows by day, and gets most of its water from its food.</li><li><b>Jerboa: the leaping night-owl:</b> The <b>jerboa</b> is a tiny, mouse-sized rodent with enormous hind legs and a long tail for balance. It is <b>nocturnal</b> (out at night, hidden in a burrow by day, which is cooler). It gets nearly all the water it needs from its food, so it rarely has to drink.</li><li><b>Bactrian camel: the desert giant:</b> The <b>Bactrian camel</b> has <b>two humps</b> (the one-humped kind is the dromedary). A hump stores <b>fat</b>, not water — the fat is an energy store. Camels have wide, padded feet that spread on sand, long eyelashes and closable nostrils against blowing sand, and can drink huge amounts quickly after a dry spell. Camels have carried people and goods across the dry lands of Asia for thousands of years.</li><li><b>Behaviour and body:</b> Adaptations come in two main kinds: <b>body adaptations</b> (fur, fat, feet, ears) and <b>behaviour adaptations</b> (hiding in the day, being active at night, digging burrows). Other desert-dwellers of Iran include the <b>Persian horned viper</b>, the <b>Asiatic cheetah</b> (critically endangered; only a few dozen survive in Iran) and the wild <b>onager</b>. Tonight’s Animal Cards: Bactrian camel, sand cat and jerboa.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>animalCards</li><li><b>match: Desert Adaptations</b> — Match each desert animal to its adaptation.</li><li><b>sort: Body or Behaviour?</b> — Is each adaptation a body adaptation or a behaviour adaptation?</li><li><b>order: Run a Fair Test</b> — Put the steps of a fair test about insulation in the right order.</li><li><b>reveal: Water for an Army</b> — Tap a card to see how much water was needed on a desert march. Remember: modern scholars debate how big the army really was.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Insulation Fair Test:</b> Wrap beakers in felt, foil, wool, bubble wrap and plain paper (and leave one with no wrapping as a control). Fill each with the same amount of warm water, record the temperature every 2 minutes for 10 minutes, graph it, and explain which ‘fur’ is best.</li><li><b>Path B — Desert Survival Guide:</b> Invent a new desert species and design a labelled guide page with five adaptations (body AND behaviour) that help it survive heat, cold nights, salt and scarce water.</li><li><b>Path C — Double Bubble: Desert vs High Country:</b> Draw a Double Bubble map comparing Dasht-e Kavir with an NZ habitat such as high-country tussockland. Show what is the same, what is different, and one adaptation in each.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Ps 63:1; Isa 41:18; Ps 104:10–13 — In Psalm 63 the writer says his soul thirsts for God in a dry and weary land without water. In Isaiah, God promises to open rivers on bare heights and springs in the middle of valleys, and to turn the desert into pools. Psalm 104 sings that God sends springs into the valleys, giving drink to every wild animal, and waters the mountains from his home.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> God provides water in dry places. What do you do when life feels dry — tired, lonely, or stuck? Where do you look for ‘water’?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Desert animals survive with little water — they use what they have wisely. How can we show respect for water, a gift we often take for granted?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose one water-saving action for this week and tell a partner (for example, a shorter shower or turning off the tap while brushing teeth).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Insulation Fair Test</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Wrap beakers in felt, foil, wool, bubble wrap and plain paper (and leave one with no wrapping as a control). Fill each with the same amount of warm water, record the temperature every 2 minutes for 10 minutes, graph it, and explain which ‘fur’ is best.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote a question and a prediction</li><li>I listed what I changed, measured and kept the same</li><li>I filled my results table accurately</li><li>I made a line graph with labelled axes</li><li>I wrote a conclusion that uses my data</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the prepared table and only test three materials plus a control; the teacher helps with reading thermometers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Test all materials, plot a line graph and write a conclusion using data.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the temperature drop for each material, rank them, and suggest improvements for a second test and why repeating the test improves reliability.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> Which material keeps warm water warm the longest? <b>Change:</b> the wrapping. <b>Measure:</b> temperature. <b>Keep the same:</b> amount of water, starting temperature, beaker, time. <b>Table:</b> Time (min) | none | felt | foil | wool | bubble wrap. <b>Conclusion frame:</b> The best insulator was ___ because the temperature dropped by only ___ °C.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Desert Survival Guide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Invent a new desert species and design a labelled guide page with five adaptations (body AND behaviour) that help it survive heat, cold nights, salt and scarce water.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My species has a name and a labelled drawing</li><li>I gave 5 adaptations (at least one behaviour and one body)</li><li>I explained how EACH adaptation helps survival</li><li>I wrote what it eats and what might eat it</li><li>I used the words habitat, adaptation and nocturnal where suitable</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn outline and a bank of adaptations; label three and write two ‘has… which helps…’ sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently with five adaptations and explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a food web with your species in it and explain what would happen to the community if a rare drought lasted a whole year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Frame:</b> The ___ has ___ which helps it ___. <b>Behaviour:</b> It ___ at night/in the day so that … <b>Food chain:</b> ___ → ___ → ___</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Double Bubble: Desert vs High Country</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a Double Bubble map comparing Dasht-e Kavir with an NZ habitat such as high-country tussockland. Show what is the same, what is different, and one adaptation in each.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Two main bubbles labelled</li><li>At least 3 differences (e.g. rainfall, temperature, plants, animals)</li><li>At least 2 similarities (e.g. open land, wide temperature swings)</li><li>One animal adaptation in each habitat</li><li>A sentence on what I learned</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a ready-made double bubble and a word bank; add two similarities and two differences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own double bubble and complete all sections.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Explain how the abiotic factors in each habitat shape the adaptations of animals there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Differences:</b> The desert has … but the tussockland has … <b>Similarities:</b> Both have … <b>Adaptation:</b> In the desert, the ___ … In the tussocklands, the ___ …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 63:1; Isa 41:18; Ps 104:10–13 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: These are poems of longing and promise, not scientific texts. Be sensitive: some students may have experienced real hardship; keep the language of ‘dry times’ gentle and invite, never require, personal sharing.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare the Kavir with the dry, wide-open tussocklands of Canterbury or Central Otago, where cold nights and drying winds also shape life. Link to kaitiakitanga: how do we look after water and fragile habitats in our rohe? Use local guidance for place-specific knowledge.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Asiatic cheetah is critically endangered — present this as a reason to care. Handle warm water safely; never use boiling water with children. Avoid describing deserts as ‘empty’ or ‘useless’: they are homes full of life.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Thermometers or temperature probes (one per beaker)</li><li>Beakers or identical jars, a kettle of warm (not boiling) water supervised by the teacher</li><li>Insulation materials: felt, foil, wool, bubble wrap, plain paper, rubber bands</li><li>Timers and recording tables; graph paper or a graphing app</li><li>Animal Cards (collect weekly)</li><li>Desert-animal photos and fact-file sheets</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students keep the test fair (same water, same start temperature)?</li><li>Could students describe the pattern in their graph with data?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before Week 5’s wetlands and rivers?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Glazed-Brick Immortals: A Never-Ending Frieze":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Glazed-Brick Immortals: A Never-Ending Frieze — Print a guard, repeat him, and make an army march across the wall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Persian Traditions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 4: Pride Before the Fall (490–479 BC) · Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to make a repeating frieze using printmaking.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can make a printing block.</li><li>I can repeat prints with even spacing.</li><li>I can choose a limited colour palette.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technique (block clarity, even spacing, controlled paint), rhythm of the finished frieze and the artist’s statement (1–2 lines) in the Caravan Log.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a simple print with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Prints a repeated shape with mostly even spacing and a limited palette.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls registration, rhythm and palette precisely and explains choices.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines two blocks or ideas with intent and explains how repetition and colour create meaning.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>frieze</b> — A long band of repeated pictures or patterns, often on a wall.</li><li><b>printmaking</b> — Making art by pressing an inked or painted block onto paper.</li><li><b>relief block</b> — A printing block with raised lines that pick up the paint.</li><li><b>registration</b> — Lining up each print in exactly the same position.</li><li><b>glazed brick</b> — A brick covered with a shiny coloured glaze, then fired in a kiln.</li><li><b>Immortals</b> — The Persian elite guard — said to stay at about 10,000 men.</li><li><b>rhythm</b> — The beat made by repeating shapes at even intervals.</li><li><b>layering</b> — Adding one colour over or next to another.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Guards of Susa</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A stone wall rises ahead, covered with shining coloured bricks: archers in long robes, spears upright, marching on and on, each exactly like the next. ‘Courier,’ says Shirin, ‘Darius’s craftsmen made this at Susa. These guards were called the Immortals. Not because they never died — but because whenever one fell, another stepped into his place, so the ranks never looked any smaller. Now it’s your turn to build a never-ending army… out of paint.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — name two desert animals and their adaptations; what makes a test fair.</li><li>Show images of the glazed-brick archers from Susa (now partly displayed in museums such as the Louvre and in Iran). Notice: colour, rhythm, repeated pose, patterned robes.</li><li>Explain Immortals (2 min): Herodotus says the elite unit kept to 10,000, replacing anyone lost, so it always looked the same size. Say ‘according to Herodotus’ — it is a Greek writer’s account.</li><li>Try it (4 min): students sketch one simple guard in profile. Which parts could be simplified for a printing block?</li><li>Share the LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: two desert animals and their adaptations; what stays the same in a fair test.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Immortals marched across the walls of Susa — and you can march them across yours. Learn the printmaker’s steps, and the rhythm that makes an army.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Demonstrate (6 min): carve a simple foam block live under the visualiser. Show the roll, press, lift sequence and common mistakes (too much paint, uneven press, slipping).</li><li>Registration (3 min): model marking light pencil dots. Practise two prints on scrap, check spacing with a ruler.</li><li>Palette (2 min): discuss three or four colours, then ask each Caravan to agree on a palette.</li><li>Model Path C (4 min): show the Armour-of-God item cards — belt, breastplate, shoes, shield, helmet, sword — and ask how they might repeat in a pattern.</li><li>Students choose Path A, B or C. Check drying space and clean-up routine.</li><li><b>Glazed bricks of Susa:</b> Craftspeople at Susa shaped clay bricks, painted them with glaze and <b>fired</b> them so the colours stayed bright for thousands of years. Pieces of the <b>Archers frieze</b> show figures in long patterned robes, carrying spears and quivers. The colours — <b>turquoise, yellow, white and deep blue</b> — are made from minerals and glazes.</li><li><b>Who were the Immortals?:</b> According to the Greek writer Herodotus, the <b>Immortals</b> were an elite unit of about <b>10,000</b> soldiers. When one died or became ill, another took his place — so their number <i>looked</i> unchanging. It is Herodotus’s account, so historians treat details carefully, but the idea fits the glazed-brick archers who march in perfect ranks.</li><li><b>Making a foam printing block:</b> 1. Draw a <b>simple guard</b> on paper. 2. Place it on a foam sheet and press along the lines with a blunt pencil to <b>engrave</b>. 3. Cut round the shape and glue it to card. 4. <b>Roll</b> a thin layer of paint across the foam. 5. <b>Press</b> face-down on paper, rub, and lift. Thin paint and even pressure make clear prints.</li><li><b>Repeat, register, rhythm:</b> To make a frieze, <b>mark spaces</b> on your paper with light pencil dots so each print lands in the same row. This is <b>registration</b>. Then print again and again. Even spacing makes a <b>rhythm</b>, like marching feet. Try alternating colours (blue, gold, blue, gold) for a pattern within the pattern.</li><li><b>Limited palette and layering:</b> Like the brick-makers, choose <b>three or four colours</b>. Print one colour first; let it dry; then <b>layer</b> a second colour over parts of it, or print gold borders along the top and bottom. A calm palette helps the repeated shapes shine.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: Print a Frieze</b> — Put the steps of printmaking in the right order.</li><li><b>match: Printmaker’s Words</b> — Match each art word with its meaning.</li><li><b>reveal: Immortals: What Do We Know?</b> — Tap each card to separate what Herodotus says from what the glazed bricks show.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Immortal Frieze:</b> Make a foam block of one guard and print him five times in a row on a long strip, with even spacing and a limited palette.</li><li><b>Path B — Glazed-Brick Collage:</b> Make a paper mosaic of an archer on a brick-shaped panel. Cut small rectangles in blues, golds and turquoise and arrange them to show the figure and his patterned robe.</li><li><b>Path C — Armour of God Frieze:</b> Design a repeating guardian pattern using the items of armour from Ephesians 6:10–18 (belt of truth, breastplate of righteousness, shoes of peace, shield of faith, helmet of salvation, sword of the Spirit). Print or draw them in a repeat.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Eph 6:10–18; John 11:25 — Paul tells believers to be strong in the Lord and to put on God’s full armour: truth like a belt, righteousness like a breastplate, readiness to share the good news of peace like shoes, faith like a shield, salvation like a helmet, and God’s word like a sword — and to pray at all times. In John 11, Jesus tells Martha that he is the resurrection and the life, and that anyone who trusts in him will live even though they die.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> The Persians called their guards ‘Immortals’ — yet every man could fall. Who really conquers death? What does it mean to be ‘protected’?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Compare human ‘immortality’ (always replacing the fallen) with Jesus’ promise of resurrection life. How is the armour in Ephesians different from the Immortals’ armour?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose which piece of the armour you need most this week and write one sentence about why.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Immortal Frieze</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a foam block of one guard and print him five times in a row on a long strip, with even spacing and a limited palette.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My foam block has clear, simple lines</li><li>I printed my guard 5 times in a row</li><li>The spacing is even (registration dots helped)</li><li>I used 3–4 colours only</li><li>I can say how repetition makes rhythm</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn guard template and a dotted line for spacing; practise on scrap first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own guard, print five times with accurate registration and add a border.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Alternate two blocks (guard and lotus, or two colours) in an A-B-A-B rhythm and write a statement on how the pattern gives the army power.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch a simple guard in profile. 2 Press lines into foam. 3 Roll a thin layer of paint. 4 Print, lift, repeat. 5 Add a top and bottom border. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My frieze repeats ___ times because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Glazed-Brick Collage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a paper mosaic of an archer on a brick-shaped panel. Cut small rectangles in blues, golds and turquoise and arrange them to show the figure and his patterned robe.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I sketched my archer first</li><li>I used small, evenly sized pieces (like bricks)</li><li>I used a limited palette</li><li>My robe has a clear pattern</li><li>I can describe how my collage is like glazed brick</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline and pre-cut tiles; fill the shape and add a border.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan and cut your own pieces; create a patterned robe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Make a linked pair of archers to show repeat, and explain what is lost and gained when a print becomes a mosaic.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch the archer on a brick-shaped panel. 2 Cut small rectangles. 3 Arrange before you glue. 4 Glue, leaving thin gaps for ‘mortar’. 5 Add a border. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My collage uses ___ colours to show …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Armour of God Frieze</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a repeating guardian pattern using the items of armour from Ephesians 6:10–18 (belt of truth, breastplate of righteousness, shoes of peace, shield of faith, helmet of salvation, sword of the Spirit). Print or draw them in a repeat.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I used at least three items of armour from Ephesians 6</li><li>I repeated them in an even rhythm</li><li>I used a limited palette</li><li>I can explain what each item stands for</li><li>I wrote or drew which piece I need most this week</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose three items from the picture cards; print each twice in a pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Print or draw all six items in a repeating frieze with a short label for each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Contrast the Immortals’ armour with Paul’s image, and write a paragraph on what ‘spiritual armour’ means as a picture rather than a literal weapon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Items:</b> belt (truth) · breastplate (righteousness) · shoes (peace) · shield (faith) · helmet (salvation) · sword (God’s word). <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I repeated ___ because … I need ___ most because …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Eph 6:10–18; John 11:25 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Paul’s armour is a metaphor for spiritual strength, not a call to violence or to fight people. Say this clearly. Respect the real courage of the Persian soldiers; the contrast is about the nature of the protection, not about worth.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with kowhaiwhai and tukutuku — repeated patterns that carry whakapapa and story. Invite a local artist or your Māori Education lead to guide this; do NOT copy tapu or personal designs. Focus on the idea that repetition can carry meaning.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian art comes from living cultures; use ‘exotic’ language sparingly. Avoid copying religious or sacred symbols into banners. The Armour of God is a picture of spiritual strength — do not encourage students to make weapons or play-fight with their artwork.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Foam printing sheets, pencils, scissors, card</li><li>Brayers or foam rollers; paint trays</li><li>Paint: blue, turquoise, gold, white, yellow, ochre</li><li>Long strips of paper (about 1.5 m) for friezes</li><li>Pictures of the Susa glazed-brick archers and lions (app or printed)</li><li>Paper in blues/golds for mosaic (Path B)</li><li>Armour-of-God item cards for Path C</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students manage thin paint and even pressure?</li><li>Who struggled with registration and spacing?</li><li>How well did Path C show thoughtful faith connections?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Storm at the Hellespont":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Storm at the Hellespont — The bridge is broken. Keep your lives, hold your nerve, win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 4: Pride Before the Fall (490–479 BC) · Marathon, Hellespont, Thermopylae, Salamis, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about the Greek wars, geography, desert science and printmaking, and to work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the wars, straits, desert animals and friezes of Week 4.</li><li>I can recall ideas from earlier weeks.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; updated Caravan Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately, including some recall from earlier weeks.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies ideas across subjects.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions that need reasoning and uses evidence from two sources.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Storm at the Hellespont</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The storm has torn the bridge of boats from its anchors. Planks float like scattered playing cards, and the sea heaves and growls. Shirin shouts over the wind: ‘Every Caravan has limited chances to cross, Courier! One wrong step and you will lose a life. Be humble. Be careful. And whatever happens — help each other up.’ Somewhere out in the spray, a silver thread flashes on a wave.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, Caravans add a line to their Creed on their strip and read it aloud.</li><li>Remind students: lives are about teamwork — discuss mistakes kindly together before choosing.</li><li>Teachers may display the updated Creeds with the Week 1 ones.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q3, Q7, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan adds a line to its Creed: ONE sentence about how you will stay humble when you are winning — and kind when you are losing. Starters: When we win, we will… / When we lose, we will… / Our Caravan will never boast about… / We will remember that…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought about pride and humility.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea (for example, why narrow places help defenders, or why we compare sources) to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Prov 16:18; Mark 4:35–41; Eph 6:10–18 — This week we met rulers who were proud and warned that pride comes before a fall. We heard of Jesus calming a storm with a word, and of Paul’s picture of the strength God gives.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most important thing you learned this week about being strong — and being humble?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s stories made you think hardest about how we treat people on the other side of a story?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Caravan Creed together. Pray for each other and for the journey ahead.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Which battle did Athens win against Darius’s army in 490 BC? A) Hastings · B) Troy · <b>C) Marathon ✔</b> · D) Gaugamela <em>At Marathon in 490 BC the Athenians (helped by a small force from Plataea) defeated the Persian landing force, and the Persian fleet sailed home.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. How did Xerxes’s army cross the Hellespont? <b>A) On a bridge of boats ✔</b> · B) In balloons · C) By swimming · D) Through a tunnel <em>Engineers lashed boats side by side and laid planks across them, making a bridge of boats across the narrow strait.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>3. Which 480 BC sea battle was won by the Greek ships? A) Marathon · B) Carrhae · <b>C) Salamis ✔</b> · D) Plataea <em>At Salamis the Greek fleet fought in a narrow strait where the large Persian fleet struggled to manoeuvre.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>4. Week 3 recall: the Royal Road ran from Sardis in the west to which royal city in the east? <b>A) Susa ✔</b> · B) Athens · C) Marathon · D) Salamis <em>Darius’s Royal Road linked Sardis to Susa — roughly 2,500 km — and Sardis was also close to the Greek cities at the heart of this week’s story.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>5. Why do we compare Greek and Persian accounts when we can? <b>A) They may show different perspectives and bias ✔</b> · B) Both are always false · C) To make lessons longer · D) Because maps are different <em>Every account has a point of view. Comparing different voices helps us see bias and gaps — and few Persian accounts of these wars survive.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>6. About how many New Zealands could fit inside the Persian Empire at its greatest? <b>A) About 20 ✔</b> · B) About 2 · C) About 2,000 · D) About 200 <em>The empire covered roughly 5 million km² and NZ about 270,000 km², so about 20 New Zealands would fit — a rough estimate.</em> [MA-G]</li><li>7. Why does a narrow pass like Thermopylae help the smaller army? <b>A) It limits how many enemy soldiers can attack at once ✔</b> · B) It turns the sea warm · C) It gives the larger army more room · D) It makes the mountain taller <em>A big army can only attack as wide as the gap — so numbers matter less. (At Thermopylae, a hidden path eventually let the Persians go around.)</em> [SS-PE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>8. The sand cat’s furry paws help it… A) Climb trees · <b>B) Walk on hot sand ✔</b> · C) Swim rivers · D) Fly short distances <em>Thick fur between the toes protects the sand cat’s paws from hot sand and helps it walk without sinking.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>9. Which of these is a BEHAVIOUR adaptation of a jerboa? <b>A) Being active at night ✔</b> · B) Long tail · C) Large back legs · D) Mouse-sized body <em>Being active at night (nocturnal) is something the jerboa does — a behaviour. Long legs and tail are body adaptations.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — In a fair test of insulation, what must stay the SAME in every beaker? A) Nothing — each should be different · <b>B) The starting temperature and amount of water ✔</b> · C) The wrapping material · D) The thermometer reading at the end <em>In a fair test only ONE thing changes (the wrapping). Starting temperature, water amount, beaker and timing must be the same so we can trust the result.</em> [SC-NoS-I]</li><li>11. Proverbs 16:18 says pride goes before… A) friends · B) wealth · C) breakfast · <b>D) destruction ✔</b> <em>Proverbs says pride goes before destruction and a haughty spirit before a fall — a warning that is true for every person and ruler.</em> [RE]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Greek writers said Xerxes had the sea whipped, while Jesus calmed the sea in Mark 4. What is the best thing to say about these two stories? A) Both are exactly the same kind of story · <b>B) Historians doubt the whipping story, but Mark records Jesus calming the sea with authority and care ✔</b> · C) Only the whipping story is believable · D) Neither tells us anything <em>The whipped-sea story comes from a Greek source and many historians think it is exaggerated. Mark tells of Jesus speaking to the storm and the disciples asking ‘Who is this?’ — the point is who has authority over creation, and how it is used.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought about pride and humility.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought about pride and humility</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (for example, geography and history) in one paragraph about why narrow places matter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that humility means…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that asks students to compare two sources or two perspectives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea (for example, why narrow places help defenders, or why we compare sources) to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic or poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Prov 16:18; Mark 4:35–41; Eph 6:10–18 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and warm. Celebrate effort and kindness in the Showdown, not only winning.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week (different accounts of the same event, Cook Strait and harbours, tussocklands, kōwhaiwhai and tukutuku).</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown — keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about losing ‘lives’; remind them that the whole Caravan shares the risk and remind them it is a game. Offer the ‘anonymise names’ option.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception (for example, ‘Greek version = the only version’) needs re-teaching?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 5: For Such a Time as This":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Queen Esther of Susa: Courage in the Palace":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Queen Esther of Susa: Courage in the Palace — Who dares to step forward when it might cost everything?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 5: For Such a Time as This (c. 483–473 BC) · Susa, the Karkheh &amp; Karun river plains, Lake Urmia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-R — English: Reading. Students read informational and narrative texts and make meaning from them.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Respect; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain the events of Esther in their Persian setting and why Esther’s choice mattered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can retell the key events (banquet, Esther made queen, Haman’s plot, Esther’s request, Purim).</li><li>I can point to Persian customs in the story.</li><li>I can explain what ‘providence’ means.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accurate retell (story map or interview); one clear use of evidence from Esther or another source; Council Fire observation notes.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Retells some events of Esther with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Retells the key events in order and names at least two Persian customs in the story.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why Esther’s choice mattered and uses the word ‘providence’ accurately, citing evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates what the story shows about courage and God’s guidance, and separates what the text says from what historians can confirm.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Ahasuerus</b> — The Hebrew form of the name of the Persian king we call Xerxes I (reigned 486–465 BC).</li><li><b>decree / edict</b> — An official order from the king that everyone in the empire must obey.</li><li><b>signet ring</b> — A ring with a carved seal, pressed into wax or clay to show an order was truly from the king.</li><li><b>providence</b> — God quietly guiding events for good, even when we cannot see Him doing it.</li><li><b>Purim</b> — The Jewish festival that remembers how the people were rescued in Esther’s time.</li><li><b>courage</b> — Doing the right thing even when you feel afraid.</li><li><b>advocate</b> — Someone who speaks up for another person.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Royal Summons</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Courier! The great doors of the hall of Susa are shut. Inside, the king sits on his throne with a golden sceptre across his knees. The rule is very old and very strict: nobody may walk in unless the king calls them. Not a servant, not a noble, not even the queen. Anyone who dares must be put to death, unless the king holds out the golden sceptre. Shirin whispers, ‘Imagine you had to cross that floor. Whose life would be worth the risk?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (3 min): from Stage 4, ask three quick questions on mini-whiteboards: Which king’s bridge was wrecked by a storm? What were the Immortals? Why does pride end badly (Prov 16)? Then link: ‘That same king, Xerxes, is the king of our story this week.’</li><li>The Royal Summons (7 min): sit at the front with the golden pointer. Explain the rule: no-one approaches unless summoned (Esth 4:11). Ask one volunteer (never pressure anyone) to step forward. Respond with the sceptre or without it, and pause.</li><li>Debrief in pairs (2 min): How did it feel to wait? How did it feel to walk up? What made it risky? What might make someone brave enough to do it anyway?</li><li>Take two shares. Praise ‘because’ sentences. Do not tell the story yet; the Discovery will.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria; students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Stage 4: Xerxes’ bridge at the Hellespont; the Immortals; ‘pride before a fall’ (Prov 16).</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Step into Susa, Courier. A queen with a secret, a villain with a plan, a king with a ring, and a God nobody mentions by name.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Story spine (6 min): retell the story in five beats on the board: BANQUET (Vashti) → ESTHER MADE QUEEN → HAMAN’S PLOT → ESTHER’S REQUEST → PURIM. Use the app’s story-order widget as a quick check afterwards.</li><li>Persian setting (4 min): show or describe the Susa palace. Draw out three customs: huge banquets, the signet-ring seal on unchangeable orders, and the courier network that carried edicts. Link each to Stage 3 and to Daniel 6.</li><li>Handle the threat sensitively (2 min): say plainly, ‘This is a story about people being treated unfairly because of who they were. The rescue matters because the danger was real.’ Keep descriptions of violence minimal and age-appropriate.</li><li>Providence (3 min): ask pairs to list three ‘coincidences’ and say, ‘Is this luck, or something more? Christians say providence; what do you think?’ Accept all respectful answers.</li><li>Hedge honestly: ‘Historians agree that Ahasuerus is Xerxes, but the Greek and Persian records do not mention Esther by name. Different people explain this in different ways.’</li><li><b>A king, a banquet and a queen who said no:</b> <b>Xerxes I</b> (<b>Ahasuerus</b> in Hebrew) ruled Persia from <b>486 to 465 BC</b>. Most of the book of Esther is set in <b>Susa</b>, one of the royal cities. It begins with a huge banquet that lasts for months. When Queen <b>Vashti</b> refuses to appear before the guests, the king’s advisers announce a search for a new queen. Scholars agree this fits what we know about Persian court life, though Greek writers use different names for the royal family, and historians still debate how the two sets of names line up.</li><li><b>Esther: an orphan who becomes queen:</b> <b>Esther</b> is a young Jewish woman, an orphan, who has been brought up by her older cousin <b>Mordecai</b>. Many Jewish families still lived in the empire, scattered across the cities of Persia, after the exile. Esther is chosen as queen, but she keeps her family background quiet because Mordecai advises her to. Mordecai works at the palace gate, where he overhears a plot against the king and reports it. The report is written down in the royal records.</li><li><b>Haman’s plot and the king’s ring:</b> <b>Haman</b>, a powerful official, is furious that Mordecai will not bow to him. He persuades the king to sign an edict against the Jewish people across the empire. The edict is sealed with the king’s <b>signet ring</b>, and Persian law said a royal order sealed this way could not simply be undone (compare Daniel 6:8). The order travels quickly on the empire’s <b>royal courier network</b>, the same kind of postal system you met with Darius in Stage 3.</li><li><b>Esther chooses:</b> Mordecai sends Esther a message: ‘You might be the one for such a time as this.’ Esther knows the rule: to enter the king’s court unsummoned could cost her life. She asks the people of Susa to fast and pray with her for three days, then she steps forward. The king holds out the golden sceptre. Over two banquets she tells him the truth, Haman’s plan is exposed, and a second edict is sent, allowing the Jewish people to defend themselves. The rescue is remembered every year at the festival of <b>Purim</b>.</li><li><b>God is never named… yet He is at work:</b> The book of Esther is unusual: <b>God is never named</b> in it. But look for the ‘coincidences’: a queen chosen at just the right moment, a plot overheard, a king who cannot sleep one night and has the royal records read to him. Christians call this <b>providence</b>: God quietly working behind events, often through the brave choices of ordinary people. Jewish readers have treasured the book for centuries as a story of survival and hope.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: Esther in order</b> — Put the main events of the book of Esther in the correct order.</li><li><b>match: Who or what is it?</b> — Match each word from the story with its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Legend, possible fact or fact?</b> — Sort each statement into the bin where it belongs. Be careful: some things are only tradition, and some are open questions.</li><li><b>reveal: Providence clues</b> — Flip each card. Is this luck, or could it be something more? Discuss with your partner before you flip.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — “Esther’s Courage” Story Map / Comic:</b> Make an 8-frame story map or comic of Esther. In every frame, write one sentence of what happens and add a key verse reference in the corner. Choose one frame to explain why it is the turning point.</li><li><b>Path B — Hot-Seat Interviews:</b> Work in a group of four. Each person plays Mordecai, Esther, Haman or Xerxes. Record a short ‘interview’ where a reporter asks each character three questions. Answers must match what the story says.</li><li><b>Path C — Persian Court Explainer:</b> Make a brochure or poster explaining how the Persian court worked. Use evidence from the book of Esther and at least one other source, such as the Susa excavations or Greek writers on the royal post.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Esther 4:12–16; Prov 31:8–9 — Mordecai tells Esther that if she stays silent, help for her people will come from somewhere else, and asks whether she might have come to her position for exactly such a time as this. Esther answers that she will go to the king, and if she must perish, she will perish. The wise sayings of Proverbs urge us to speak up for people who cannot speak for themselves, and to defend the rights of the poor and those in need.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does the story show about God when He is not named? Where do you think He might be at work in your own life without your noticing?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> How do the ‘coincidences’ in the plot fit with the idea of providence? Does providence mean people can sit back and wait, or does it call them to act?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Ask yourself: ‘Who needs my voice?’ Write ONE action you will take this week to speak up for someone, or to include someone who is left out.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: “Esther’s Courage” Story Map / Comic</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make an 8-frame story map or comic of Esther. In every frame, write one sentence of what happens and add a key verse reference in the corner. Choose one frame to explain why it is the turning point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew 8 frames in the right order</li><li>Each frame has one clear sentence in my own words</li><li>Each frame has a verse reference (e.g. Esth 4:14)</li><li>I marked the turning point and explained why</li><li>I included at least one Persian custom (banquet, ring, couriers)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the pre-numbered frames and sentence starters; choose 5 of the 8 frames to complete, adding a picture to each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete all 8 frames independently with a verse reference in each and a short turning-point explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ninth frame showing the story from Haman’s or Xerxes’ point of view. How would each of them tell the same events? Explain why every storyteller has a point of view.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Frames:</b> 1 Banquet · 2 Vashti refuses · 3 Esther becomes queen · 4 Mordecai overhears a plot · 5 Haman’s edict · 6 Esther fasts and steps forward · 7 The banquets and the request · 8 Purim. <b>Frame sentence:</b> First/Then/Next… <b>Turning point:</b> I think the turning point is frame __ because…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Hot-Seat Interviews</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Work in a group of four. Each person plays Mordecai, Esther, Haman or Xerxes. Record a short ‘interview’ where a reporter asks each character three questions. Answers must match what the story says.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Every character answers three questions</li><li>Answers match the Esther story (no made-up events)</li><li>Each character’s feelings and motives are clear</li><li>I used at least two vocabulary words (decree, providence, courage…)</li><li>We spoke clearly and respectfully, even for the villain</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Take Mordecai or Esther and use the question cards with a partner who plays the reporter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Prepare and record all four interviews; check answers against the story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a fifth voice, a historian, who explains which parts of the story match Persian customs and which parts we cannot check with other sources.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Reporter questions:</b> What did you decide, and why? What were you most afraid of? What would you say to the people of Susa? <b>Answer starters:</b> I chose to… because… · I was afraid that… · If I could do it again…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Persian Court Explainer</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a brochure or poster explaining how the Persian court worked. Use evidence from the book of Esther and at least one other source, such as the Susa excavations or Greek writers on the royal post.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I explained at least 3 features of the court (banquets, signet ring, couriers, royal records…)</li><li>Each feature names the evidence (Esther text OR another source)</li><li>I labelled any claim that scholars debate</li><li>My layout is clear for a visitor to a museum</li><li>I used words from the vocabulary list</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the evidence cards and complete three feature boxes with sentence frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete four features with evidence from at least two kinds of source.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘What we don’t know’ box. Explain why Esther’s name does not appear in Greek sources and how a historian should respond to that.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Feature frame:</b> The Persian court… We know this because… <b>Careful words:</b> probably · many scholars think · the text says · archaeology shows <b>Source note:</b> Source: ___</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Esth 4:12–16; Prov 31:8–9; Esth 8:8 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Esther is read as scripture by Jews and Christians. Treat Purim with respect: it is a living festival, not an ancient curiosity. Some students may be Jewish or may come from other faiths; do not ask any child to speak for their community. Be honest that the book never names God, and that readers explain this silence in different ways.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Think of people in Aotearoa who spoke up when it was risky, for example for fairness, for the environment or for their community. How do we make it safe for children and adults to speak up? Invite your Māori Education lead for stories of rangatira who stood up for their people, and avoid generalising about all Māori.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The story involves a threat to a whole people. Keep the focus on courage, justice and rescue, and avoid graphic description of the violence in chapters 7 and 9. Treat Jewish students and families with care; Purim is a living tradition. Muslim-background students may know the story of the tomb at Hamadan (tradition venerates it as Esther and Mordecai’s tomb; scholars cannot confirm it) so mention it only if the class raises it.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Esther read-aloud edition (chapters 1–9, in short sections)</li><li>A ‘golden pointer’ (cardboard sceptre) for the Royal Summons</li><li>Story-map frames (8 boxes) for Path A</li><li>Interview question cards for Path B</li><li>Persian-court evidence cards (Esther text + Persian sources) for Path C</li><li>Recording device or tablet for Path B</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students retell the story in order, and explain why Esther’s step mattered?</li><li>Did students separate ‘what the text says’ from ‘what historians can confirm’?</li><li>What will I change tomorrow so students link Susa’s palace to its city and its rivers?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Susa: The City of Lilies and the Story of a Tell":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Susa: The City of Lilies and the Story of a Tell — A city built on top of a city, on top of a city…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — River-Valley Settlements</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 5: For Such a Time as This (c. 483–473 BC) · Susa, the Karkheh &amp; Karun river plains, Lake Urmia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why river-valley cities lasted for thousands of years and how a tell forms.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can locate Susa and its rivers on a map.</li><li>I can explain what a tell is using a diagram.</li><li>I can name risks and benefits of settling beside a river.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tell diagram accuracy; benefits/risks reasoning; map conventions in Path B.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names the rivers or says what a tell is with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Locates Susa and its rivers and describes how a tell forms with a labelled diagram.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains benefits and risks of river settlement and why Susa lasted, using geographical evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares Susa with a NZ river or harbour town, weighs trade-offs and suggests ways to manage risks.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>tell</b> — A mound formed when many generations of people build new settlements on top of old ones.</li><li><b>stratum / layer</b> — One band of earth or building remains in a tell (plural: strata).</li><li><b>irrigation</b> — Bringing water to farmland through canals and channels.</li><li><b>silt</b> — Fine, fertile mud left on the land when a river floods.</li><li><b>floodplain</b> — Flat land beside a river that is covered by water when the river floods.</li><li><b>Mesopotamia</b> — Greek for ‘the land between the rivers’: the Tigris–Euphrates region.</li><li><b>mud-brick</b> — A brick made from mud and straw dried in the sun; it crumbles over time.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Layer Cake</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A chilly morning at Susa, and Shirin is standing on top of a big, strange, lumpy hill. ‘Guess what, Courier? This is not a hill. Dig down, and you will find houses under the houses, and streets under the streets. Whole cities, one on top of another, like the layers of a cake. Who lived on the bottom? And how did they get buried?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (3 min): Monday on mini-whiteboards: Who raised Esther? What is a signet ring for? What does ‘providence’ mean?</li><li>Layer Cake (8 min): give each team 6–8 coloured strips. Teams build a ‘tell’ in the middle of the table, adding one strip at a time and drawing a tiny house on each. Say ‘Each layer is a town. A flood or fire or war comes, and people rebuild on the same spot.’</li><li>Predict (2 min): ‘Which layer is the oldest? Which is the newest? If you were digging, where would you start, and why?’ Take guesses without correcting.</li><li>Share LI/SC (students read aloud).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: Mordecai; signet ring; providence.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Susa has been home to people for more than five thousand years. Why did people keep coming back to the same place?</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Locate (4 min): on the big map find Susa, the Karkheh, the Karun/Dez and the Tigris–Euphrates. Students point on their own maps and mark them.</li><li>Benefits/risks (5 min): play the flood benefit/risk cards. Ask: ‘Would you build here? What would you build to keep safe?’</li><li>Tell (6 min): use the app’s tell diagram. Drag layers on and off. Link to the Layer Cake: ‘Which is the oldest layer? Who lived there?’</li><li>Palace (3 min): show photos or drawings. Ask: ‘Why would a king want a second palace in a warmer city?’</li><li>Language care: say, ‘Scholars still discuss the exact meaning of some names. We say ‘may’ when we are not sure.’</li><li><b>Where is Susa?:</b> <b>Susa</b> lies on the <b>Khuzestan plain</b> in south-west Iran, in the foothills of the Zagros where the mountains meet the flat land of the river valleys. It sits between two rivers, the <b>Karkheh</b> and the <b>Dez/Karun</b>. People have lived here for over <b>5,000 years</b>. The Bible calls the city <b>Shushan</b> (Esth 1:2), and a later name for the place was ‘the city of lilies’ in some traditions (the word <i>shushan</i> may be related to ‘lily’; scholars discuss this).</li><li><b>The land between the rivers:</b> Further west lie the <b>Tigris</b> and <b>Euphrates</b>, a region the Greeks called <b>Mesopotamia</b>. Great cities such as <b>Babylon</b> grew up here. Rivers gave these cities water, fish, reeds, rich farmland and a route for boats. The rich <b>silt</b> that floods left behind made some of the best farmland in the ancient world.</li><li><b>Water brings blessings… and risks:</b> <b>Benefits:</b> water to drink, <b>irrigation</b> for crops, fish and reeds, transport by boat, flat land to build on. <b>Risks:</b> floods that wash away houses, rivers that change course, too much salt in over-watered soil, mosquitoes and illness, and enemies who find it easy to approach across flat land. People built canals, levees and fortified walls to manage these risks.</li><li><b>What is a tell?:</b> Most houses in ancient Susa were built of <b>mud-brick</b>. Over time they cracked and crumbled. When a house fell, people levelled the ground and built the next house on top. After hundreds of years, a <b>mound</b> grows. That mound is called a <b>tell</b>. Archaeologists dig through the layers (<b>strata</b>) from newest at the top to oldest at the bottom, a bit like reading a book backwards.</li><li><b>The palace of the kings:</b> Darius I built a huge <b>palace complex</b> at Susa, and his son Xerxes used it. Persian kings used Susa as a <b>winter capital</b>, enjoying its warm climate while the high plateau was cold. Inscriptions from Darius describe materials brought from across the empire: cedar from Lebanon, gold from Sardis and Bactria, ivory from Ethiopia and India. The Susa excavations also fit the palace setting in Esther, which helps us picture the story.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: How a tell forms</b> — Put these steps in the correct order, from the first to the last.</li><li><b>sort: River risks and rewards</b> — Sort each point into its bin. Some things can be both, depending on how people manage them.</li><li><b>match: Rivers and cities</b> — Match each word with its meaning.</li><li><b>reveal: Susa through time</b> — Flip each card to see who was here and what we know. Remember that dates are approximate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Tell Cross-Section:</b> Draw a cross-section diagram of a tell with at least five layers. Next to each layer, add a label saying who lived there, using a timeline of Susa’s peoples (Elamites, Persians and others).</li><li><b>Path B — River Cities Map:</b> Draw a map of south-west Asia that shows the Zagros, the Karkheh, the Karun, the Tigris, the Euphrates, Susa and Babylon. Label farmland and add a key. Then write the benefits and risks of a river site.</li><li><b>Path C — Palace City Plan:</b> Design a river-valley palace city with canals, farms, defences and a palace. Label each part and explain why you put it where you did.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 2:10–14; Neh 1:1; Dan 8:2 — In the account of Eden, a river flows out of the garden and divides into four, and the names include the Tigris and Euphrates. Nehemiah introduces himself as living in the citadel of Susa. Daniel describes seeing a vision while he was at the city of Susa, beside a canal. So the river valleys of Mesopotamia and Susa appear again and again in the Bible.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Many Bible people lived near these rivers. Why do rivers matter so much to people and places?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What do the layers of a tell tell us about history? What do they NOT tell us? How do digging and reading the Bible each add something to the picture?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Thank God for a river, creek or water source you rely on. Write one thing you will do to look after it.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Tell Cross-Section</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a cross-section diagram of a tell with at least five layers. Next to each layer, add a label saying who lived there, using a timeline of Susa’s peoples (Elamites, Persians and others).</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My diagram shows at least 5 layers, oldest at the bottom</li><li>Each layer has a label (people, buildings, objects)</li><li>I wrote a one-line timeline beside the diagram</li><li>I explained how a tell forms using the word mud-brick</li><li>I labelled the youngest and the oldest layer</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn five-layer outline and a label bank. Complete the sentence frame for how a tell forms.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently. Add a timeline with three dates and a short explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘What could an archaeologist find in each layer?’ panel, and explain why layers are not always perfectly flat (pits, floods, rebuilding).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Timeline bank:</b> Early village (c. 4000 BC) · Elamite city (c. 2700–600 BC) · Persian palace (c. 520 BC) · later periods. <b>Say it:</b> A tell forms when… The oldest layer is at the… because…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: River Cities Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a map of south-west Asia that shows the Zagros, the Karkheh, the Karun, the Tigris, the Euphrates, Susa and Babylon. Label farmland and add a key. Then write the benefits and risks of a river site.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose, key and scale bar</li><li>Rivers drawn and labelled correctly</li><li>Susa and Babylon located</li><li>Farmland and desert shown with colour</li><li>I wrote 3 benefits and 3 risks of a river site</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map with the rivers already drawn. Add labels and a key. Complete two benefit/risk sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with all map conventions and three benefits and risks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Explain why Susa lasted so long when other towns disappeared, using at least three geographical reasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Map conventions:</b> Title · Arrow (north) · Legend · Key · Edge · Scale. <b>Frame:</b> A benefit of living by a river is… A risk is… but people could manage it by…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Palace City Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a river-valley palace city with canals, farms, defences and a palace. Label each part and explain why you put it where you did.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My plan shows a river and at least two canals</li><li>I placed farmland where water can reach it</li><li>I included defences (walls, towers or levees)</li><li>I included a palace and a market</li><li>I explained three choices using water, soil, defence or trade</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a grid with the river already drawn. Place and label four features.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design independently with six features and three explained choices.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a flood plan: show what you would do in a flood year, and compare it with how a modern NZ river town manages floods.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan list:</b> river · canals · farmland · palace · market · walls · levees. <b>Choice frame:</b> I put the ___ here because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 2:10–14; Neh 1:1; Dan 8:2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: The identity of Eden’s four rivers is debated, and Christians read Genesis 2 in different ways. Keep the focus on the Bible’s repeated mention of this region and on the importance of water. Avoid implying that archaeology ‘proves’ Scripture; say it helps us picture the setting.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Many NZ towns and cities sit on rivers or harbours (Christchurch on the Ōtākaro/Avon, for example). How do people manage flooding today? How did Māori communities, and later settlers, choose places to live near water? Use local guidance from mana whenua and avoid generalising.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep references to modern Iran neutral and respectful; this week is about rivers and cities. Be careful when talking about the ancient name of Susa and the meaning of ‘lily’; say scholars discuss it. Do not suggest that flooded or damaged places today are ‘cursed’.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured card strips for the Layer Cake</li><li>Floor or wall map of Iran and Mesopotamia</li><li>Atlases and rulers</li><li>Tell diagram (app) with draggable layers</li><li>Flood benefit/risk cards</li><li>Grid paper for Path C city plan</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain how a tell forms in their own words?</li><li>Did they link rivers to both benefits and risks, or only benefits?</li><li>What map conventions do I need to revisit?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Rivers, Marshes & Lake Urmia: Wetland Communities":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rivers, Marshes &amp; Lake Urmia: Wetland Communities — Why is the water pink?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Wetland Communities</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 5: For Such a Time as This (c. 483–473 BC) · Susa, the Karkheh &amp; Karun river plains, Lake Urmia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe a wetland community and how people affect it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can build a wetland food web with decomposers.</li><li>I can explain interdependence.</li><li>I can describe a human impact on a lake.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Food-web accuracy (arrows and decomposers); investigation recording; vocabulary use in the script.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names some wetland animals with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Builds a food web with arrows pointing the right way, including a decomposer.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains interdependence and links the evaporation results to the shrinking of a lake.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Predicts the effects of a change on the whole community and evaluates how people could reduce the harm.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>wetland</b> — Land that is covered by shallow water or soaked with water for much of the year.</li><li><b>producer</b> — A living thing (like a plant or alga) that makes its own food using sunlight.</li><li><b>consumer</b> — A living thing that eats other living things.</li><li><b>decomposer</b> — A living thing (like bacteria or fungi) that breaks down dead matter and returns nutrients to the soil and water.</li><li><b>interdependence</b> — Living things depending on each other to survive.</li><li><b>salinity</b> — How salty the water is.</li><li><b>evaporation</b> — Liquid water turning into a gas (water vapour), faster when it is warm, dry and windy.</li><li><b>brine shrimp</b> — Tiny shrimp that live in very salty water and feed other animals, including flamingos.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Pink Mystery</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A dry wind. A huge shining lake. And the water… is pink! Shirin stares at it. ‘Courier, that is not paint. The lake is full of birds, thousands of them, standing on one leg in the shallows. Gandom is convinced the lake is full of strawberry milk. Naturally, she is wrong. What is turning it pink, and what are those birds eating?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (3 min): Tuesday on mini-whiteboards: What is a tell? Name two benefits of a river site. Name one risk.</li><li>Pink Mystery (5 min): show photos of flamingos at Lake Urmia. Ask: What are they eating? Why is the water pink? Hold the answer: tiny brine shrimp and algae.</li><li>Predict (2 min): teams write a hypothesis: ‘The water looks pink because…’ Share two. Do not correct yet.</li><li>Share LI/SC; students tick off vocabulary as it appears.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: tell; benefits and risks of settling by a river.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reeds, mud, shallow water and tiny creatures. A wetland is full of life, and everything depends on everything else.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Vocabulary (4 min): build the ladder: producer → consumer → decomposer → interdependence. Use the app’s food-web builder to drag arrows from food to eater.</li><li>Wetland community (5 min): go through each animal: where does it live, what does it eat, what eats it? Make sure students include decomposers.</li><li>Lake Urmia (4 min): show before-and-after photos if possible. Keep the focus on causes (drought, dams, farming) as a mix, and on solutions, not blame on any one group.</li><li>Introduce the Animal Cards (2 min): this week the flamingo and the water buffalo. Remind students to look after them.</li><li>Predict (2 min): ‘If the lake dries up further, what will happen to the brine shrimp? To the flamingos? To us?’</li><li><b>What is a wetland?:</b> A <b>wetland</b> is land covered by shallow water or soaked with water for much of the year. Marshes, reed beds and river deltas are wetlands. In south-west Iran, the <b>Karun</b> and other rivers feed wetlands that support fish, birds and water buffalo. Wetlands act like sponges, soaking up floodwater and cleaning the water as it passes through.</li><li><b>The wetland community:</b> <b>Producers:</b> reeds, grasses and algae. <b>Consumers:</b> <b>brine shrimp</b>, fish (such as those of the Karun river), <b>water buffalo</b> that graze on reeds, <b>otters</b>, <b>reed warblers</b> that hide in the reeds, <b>pelicans</b> and <b>flamingos</b>. <b>Decomposers:</b> bacteria and fungi in the mud that break down dead plants and animals and release nutrients that feed the next generation of reeds and algae.</li><li><b>The pink mystery solved:</b> <b>Lake Urmia</b> in north-west Iran is a very salty lake. Tiny <b>algae</b> and <b>brine shrimp</b> live in it. The greater <b>flamingo</b> filters the water and eats these. Pigments called <b>carotenoids</b> in the food give flamingo feathers their pink colour, and in very salty lakes some algae and bacteria can turn the water reddish or pinkish as well. If a flamingo did not eat the right food, its feathers would be paler.</li><li><b>Everything is linked:</b> <b>Interdependence</b> means living things rely on each other. Algae need sunlight; brine shrimp need algae; flamingos need brine shrimp; decomposers need dead matter; and algae need the nutrients that decomposers return. Take away one link and the rest feel it. A wetland is a web, not a ladder.</li><li><b>A lake that is shrinking:</b> Lake Urmia was once one of the largest salt lakes in the world, but over recent decades it has <b>shrunk dramatically</b>. Scientists point to a mix of causes: <b>drought</b>, <b>climate change</b>, dams and the use of river water for <b>farming</b>. As the water drops, it gets <b>saltier</b>, brine shrimp struggle and the flamingos have less food. Efforts to restore the lake have been made, and the level has risen and fallen over time. It reminds us that how we use water has consequences for living things far away.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>sort: Producer, consumer or decomposer?</b> — Sort each living thing by its role in the wetland community.</li><li><b>order: A food chain at Lake Urmia</b> — Put the links in the right order, from the energy source to the nutrients returning.</li><li><b>reveal: Why is Lake Urmia shrinking?</b> — Flip each card to see one cause or one effect. Which can people change?</li><li>animalCards</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Wetland Food Web Diorama / Chart:</b> Build a food-web diorama or chart of a wetland community. Include at least 8 living things (with at least one decomposer), draw arrows from food to eater, and explain one link.</li><li><b>Path B — Evaporation Model Investigation:</b> Run a fair test. Put the same amount of water in three trays: one in the shade, one in the sun, one in the wind (fan). Measure the water loss after 20 minutes (or a day) and relate your results to a shrinking lake.</li><li><b>Path C — Documentary Script: “A Day on the Lake”:</b> Write the narration for a 2-minute nature documentary called ‘A Day on the Lake’. Include at least 5 science facts and use the words habitat, interdependence and decomposer.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 1:20–22; Ps 104:25; Gen 2:15 — In the creation story God says, ‘Let the waters teem with living creatures’, and blesses the creatures of the sea and sky. The psalmist praises God for the sea, ‘vast and wide’, filled with countless living things, big and small. Genesis also says that God placed the first human in the garden to work it and take care of it.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it say about God’s care that the waters ‘teem with life’? What do you notice when you look closely at a wetland, stream or beach?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What happens when we use too much water? How does the call to ‘take care of’ the garden apply to rivers, lakes and wetlands today?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose one stewardship action for water this week, such as turning off a tap, picking up rubbish from a stream, or planting reeds or harakeke. Write it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Wetland Food Web Diorama / Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a food-web diorama or chart of a wetland community. Include at least 8 living things (with at least one decomposer), draw arrows from food to eater, and explain one link.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My web has at least 8 living things</li><li>I included producers, consumers AND decomposers</li><li>Arrows point from food to eater</li><li>I labelled each as producer, consumer or decomposer</li><li>I explained what would happen if one link disappeared</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the picture cards and pre-drawn arrows; complete the labels and one ‘If… then…’ sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create and label the web independently with a clear explanation of one link.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second web for a different season or after a drought, and explain which animals would be hit hardest and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Living things bank:</b> reeds · algae · brine shrimp · fish · flamingo · pelican · water buffalo · otter · reed warbler · bacteria and fungi. <b>Frame:</b> The ___ eats ___. If ___ disappeared, then ___ would…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Evaporation Model Investigation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Run a fair test. Put the same amount of water in three trays: one in the shade, one in the sun, one in the wind (fan). Measure the water loss after 20 minutes (or a day) and relate your results to a shrinking lake.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote a prediction</li><li>I kept the water amount and tray size the same (fair test)</li><li>I measured and recorded the water in a table</li><li>I wrote a conclusion that uses my data</li><li>I linked my results to Lake Urmia</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made table and sentence frames. A partner helps to measure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own table, record the results and write a conclusion linking to the lake.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Graph the results and explain why one test is not enough. Suggest how to improve the investigation (repeat trials, measure temperature).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Table:</b> Tray | Start (mL) | End (mL) | Water lost (mL). <b>Prediction:</b> I think the tray in the ___ will lose the most water because… <b>Conclusion:</b> My results show… so a lake in a hot, dry, windy place would…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Documentary Script: “A Day on the Lake”</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write the narration for a 2-minute nature documentary called ‘A Day on the Lake’. Include at least 5 science facts and use the words habitat, interdependence and decomposer.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It has an opening and a closing line</li><li>It includes 5 accurate science facts</li><li>I used habitat, interdependence and decomposer correctly</li><li>It mentions a human impact on the lake</li><li>I read it aloud with expression</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the script frame and fact bank. Read it with a partner and record together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own script with five facts and a human impact.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second narrator who is a scientist. Explain how scientists measure the lake’s health, such as its water level and salt levels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Opening:</b> As the sun rises over the lake… <b>Facts:</b> The flamingo eats… The brine shrimp feeds on… Decomposers… <b>Closing:</b> The lake reminds us that…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 1:20–22; Ps 104:25; Gen 2:15 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Present science and Scripture as partners, not rivals: science asks how a lake works and how it changes; the psalm praises the Creator. Be careful with blame on the Lake Urmia story: climate, farming, dams and policy interact in complex ways, and many Iranians have worked hard to protect it.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare Lake Urmia with a NZ wetland, such as Te Waihora/Lake Ellesmere in Canterbury. Both are shallow, productive lakes affected by water use and land use. What do kaitiaki (guardians) do to look after them? Use local guidance from mana whenua and avoid generalising about all iwi.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Present the shrinking of Lake Urmia as a complex problem with several causes, and avoid blaming any one group. Emphasise solutions and the efforts of people in Iran. Handle carefully any discussion of climate change; students and families may hold different views, so keep to the evidence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Photos of Lake Urmia, flamingos and wetland animals</li><li>Food-web cards and string (app or printed)</li><li>Three shallow trays, measuring jug, thermometer or scales</li><li>Fan, sunny spot and shady spot for the evaporation test</li><li>Recording sheets for Path B</li><li>Animal Cards (collect weekly)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students put the arrows the right way (food to eater)?</li><li>Which students used decomposers correctly?</li><li>Was the evaporation test fair, and what would I change?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Gold & Jewel: Persian Splendour":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gold &amp; Jewel: Persian Splendour — Beautiful things say what a kingdom values.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Persian Traditions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 5: For Such a Time as This (c. 483–473 BC) · Susa, the Karkheh &amp; Karun river plains, Lake Urmia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to design luxury objects in the style of Achaemenid metalwork.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use an animal form in a design.</li><li>I can show shine with colour and foil.</li><li>I can explain how objects showed royal power.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Craftsmanship (shape, detail, finish); use of contrast; the artist’s statement in the Caravan Log.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes an object or painting with an animal form with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Uses an animal form and shine in a finished piece and explains one choice.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Uses contrast and balance deliberately and explains how the object shows power.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates the object’s meaning, compares with other cultures and reflects on what makes something truly valuable.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>rhyton</b> — A drinking horn made of gold, silver or clay, ending in the head of an animal.</li><li><b>griffin</b> — A mythical creature with the body of a lion and the head and wings of an eagle.</li><li><b>repoussé</b> — A way of shaping metal by pushing it out from the back to make a raised pattern.</li><li><b>armlet</b> — A band of metal worn around the upper arm.</li><li><b>contrast</b> — A strong difference between two things, like light and dark or warm and cool colours.</li><li><b>warm / cool colours</b> — Reds, oranges and yellows feel warm; blues and greens feel cool.</li><li><b>balance</b> — Making the parts of a design feel equally weighted, often through symmetry.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Goblets of Gold</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The banquet hall glitters. Shirin leans close and reads from the old record: ‘The drinks were served in goblets of gold, and no two goblets were the same.’ Gandom is chewing a golden tassel. ‘Courier, imagine the craftspeople who made every single one. How would you make a cup fit for a king, and what would you put on it?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (3 min): Wednesday on mini-whiteboards: What does a decomposer do? Name two animals of a wetland. Why is Lake Urmia shrinking?</li><li>Hook (4 min): read Esther 1:7 aloud (from your translation), then show pictures of a rhyton and a griffin armlet. Ask: What animals do you see? Why might a king want these?</li><li>Quick sketch (4 min): students draw their ‘cup fit for a king’ on mini-whiteboards, adding an animal form.</li><li>Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: decomposers; two wetland animals; why Lake Urmia is shrinking.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Persian craftspeople were famous for gold, jewels and animals. Learn their tricks and use them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rhyton and griffin (5 min): show images. Ask: ‘What animals? What do they stand for? Why put an animal at the end of a cup?’ Invite ideas such as strength, speed and royal power.</li><li>Repoussé demo (4 min): press a pattern into foil on a folded tea-towel with a blunt pencil. Show the ‘front’ and the ‘back’.</li><li>Colour (4 min): show a swatch of gold next to blue and red. Which looks richer? Use the vocabulary: contrast, warm, cool.</li><li>Context (2 min): remind students that these objects come from real craftspeople. Avoid saying ‘exotic’. Say that the Oxus Treasure’s history is still discussed.</li><li>Model the first step of each path (3 min).</li><li><b>The rhyton: a drinking horn with a secret:</b> A <b>rhyton</b> is a drinking vessel that narrows to a point and ends in the head or front of an <b>animal</b>, often a lion, a ram, a bull, an ibex or a griffin. Many were made of silver, gold or clay. Persian rhytons were used at feasts, and the king’s banquets in Esther fit the world these objects come from. You met the ibex in Week 1; now you can make one a work of art.</li><li><b>Griffins and armlets:</b> A <b>griffin</b> mixes a lion’s body with an eagle’s head and wings. Famous <b>gold armlets</b> from the so-called ‘Oxus Treasure’ (now in the British Museum) have griffin heads at their ends and were once set with coloured stones or glass. Scholars think these were made in the Persian period, though exactly who owned them and where they were made is still discussed.</li><li><b>Repoussé: pushing metal from behind:</b> <b>Repoussé</b> (say ‘ruh-poo-SAY’) is a way of making a design by pressing a thin sheet of metal from the <b>back</b> so that the pattern stands out on the <b>front</b>. Add fine lines from the front and you can make feathers, fur and patterns. In class, you can do the same with aluminium foil on a soft surface and a blunt pencil.</li><li><b>Warm and cool: making gold glow:</b> Gold looks richer when you put it next to <b>cool</b> colours such as <b>lapis blue</b> and <b>turquoise</b>. A little <b>warm red</b> makes it glow even more. This is called <b>contrast</b>. A careful artist also thinks about <b>balance</b>: matching the left and the right, so the design feels steady.</li><li><b>What objects said about power:</b> A golden cup, an animal-headed horn or a jewelled armlet told everyone that the owner was wealthy and powerful. Rulers gave such gifts to reward loyal friends. But luxury is not the only way to measure a person. Esther’s story reminds us that the most precious thing in the palace was her courage.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Artist’s toolkit</b> — Match each art word with its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Warm, cool or shiny?</b> — Sort each colour or material into its bin. Which ones will you use in your design?</li><li><b>order: Making a repoussé armlet</b> — Put the steps in the right order.</li><li><b>reveal: What did these objects say?</b> — Flip each card. What did owners want other people to think?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Rhyton Model:</b> Model an animal-headed drinking horn in clay or foil. Choose a lion, ibex, ram or griffin. Add pattern around the rim and decide how your rhyton shows contrast and balance.</li><li><b>Path B — Griffin Armlet (Repoussé Foil):</b> Make a griffin armlet in repoussé foil. Press your design from the back, add jewels and curl the foil into an armband.</li><li><b>Path C — Banquet Painting:</b> Paint the banquet scene from Esther 5–7. Use warm and cool contrast and gold accents. Decide who is the focus and use colour and composition to show it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Esth 1:7; Matt 6:19–21; Prov 31:30 — The book of Esther describes a banquet where drinks were served in golden cups, each one different. Jesus says not to store up treasures on earth, where moths and rust destroy and thieves break in, but to store treasures in heaven, because wherever our treasure is, there our heart will be too. Proverbs says charm can fool us and beauty fades, but a person who respects the Lord deserves praise.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What do beautiful things tell us about what people value? Is there anything wrong with making or enjoying beautiful things?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> The palace was full of wealth, but the person who made the biggest difference was Esther, because of her character. What is the difference between valuing treasure and valuing people?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Name one thing you treasure that is not a thing. Write or draw it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Rhyton Model</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Model an animal-headed drinking horn in clay or foil. Choose a lion, ibex, ram or griffin. Add pattern around the rim and decide how your rhyton shows contrast and balance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My rhyton narrows to a point and ends in an animal head</li><li>The animal is clear and well formed</li><li>I added patterns around the rim</li><li>I used shine (foil or gold paint) and a contrasting colour</li><li>I can explain what the animal stands for</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made cone and a template animal head. Add pattern and paint.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Model your own animal and rim pattern; write an artist’s statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design a matching pair of rhytons (left and right), and explain how you used symmetry and balance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch the shape. 2 Form the cone from clay or foil. 3 Model the animal head. 4 Press the pattern. 5 Paint or add gold. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I chose a ___ because ___. I used ___ to show shine.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Griffin Armlet (Repoussé Foil)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a griffin armlet in repoussé foil. Press your design from the back, add jewels and curl the foil into an armband.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My griffin has a clear head, wings and body</li><li>I pressed the pattern from the BACK of the foil</li><li>I added fine lines from the front for detail</li><li>I added jewels (paper, beads or paint)</li><li>I can explain what repoussé means</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed griffin outline to trace through the foil, and add three jewels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and press your own griffin with jewel details.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a border of repeating patterns (see Week 1) and explain how pattern and animal work together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Draw the griffin on paper. 2 Place foil on a soft surface. 3 Trace and press from the back. 4 Add details. 5 Add jewels and curl. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I used repoussé to show…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Banquet Painting</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Paint the banquet scene from Esther 5–7. Use warm and cool contrast and gold accents. Decide who is the focus and use colour and composition to show it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My painting shows the banquet hall, the king and Esther</li><li>I used warm AND cool colours with clear contrast</li><li>I used gold accents for shine</li><li>The focus (Esther, Haman or the king) is clear</li><li>I wrote a short artist’s statement</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn outline and a limited palette. Paint with three colours and gold.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan, paint and write a statement about your use of contrast.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Paint two versions of the same moment (the king’s view and Esther’s view) and explain how colour shows each mood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Sketch the table and the people. 2 Choose a focus. 3 Paint cool colours for the background. 4 Add warm colours and gold. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I used ___ colours to show ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Esth 1:7; Matt 6:19–21; Prov 31:30 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Do not suggest that beauty or craft is wrong; Scripture celebrates skilled craft (Exod 31). The question is about the heart: what do we treasure most? Keep the tone open and honest. Persian craftsmanship was superb, and the objects come from a living cultural heritage.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with pounamu, whakairo (carving) and other treasured taonga in Aotearoa. Treasured objects can carry whakapapa and mana, not just value. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi to speak about taonga in your area, and do not copy sacred or tapu designs.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian metalwork comes from a living culture; avoid ‘exotic’ language. The Oxus Treasure has a complicated history, so say ‘scholars still discuss’. Do not make students feel that wealth or beauty is wrong; focus on what we treasure most. Do not reproduce sacred designs from any culture.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pictures of rhytons and the Oxus griffin armlets (museum websites)</li><li>Clay, foil, card, glue, jewel stickers or beads</li><li>Gold, red, turquoise and lapis paint</li><li>Paper for the banquet painting</li><li>Pencils and blunt tools for pressing foil</li><li>Esther 5–7 text for Path C</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students use contrast deliberately?</li><li>Who needed more help with foil technique?</li><li>Did their statements show understanding of what objects said about power?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: For Such a Time as This":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: For Such a Time as This — Half the term is behind you. Speak up, step forward, win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 5: For Such a Time as This (c. 483–473 BC) · Susa, the Karkheh &amp; Karun river plains, Lake Urmia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about Esther, Susa, wetlands and Persian art, and to review the first half of the term.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the people, places, animals and art of Week 5.</li><li>I can recall key ideas from earlier weeks.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan with integrity and encourage others.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; updated Caravan Creed; mid-term reflection circle notes.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately and recalls some earlier weeks.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies ideas across weeks.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions or explanations that connect several weeks and evaluates the evidence.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>For Such a Time as This</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The banquet hall is ready. Shirin hands you a golden pointer and says, ‘Courier, you have walked a long way. Today’s final question is worth triple points, so think carefully before you tap.’ A far-off horn sounds. Somewhere, a silver thread flutters in the draught.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team re-reads its Week 1 Caravan Creed and adds a new line about speaking up for each other.</li><li>After the quiz, hold a mid-term reflection circle: one thing I am proud of, one thing I want to get better at.</li><li>Remind students that the last question is worth triple points, so there is a comeback chance for every team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval; questions 10–11 look back at earlier weeks.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q4, Q8, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Mid-term moment: re-read your Caravan Creed from Week 1. Then add ONE line about how your Caravan will speak up for each other in the second half of the term. Starters: We will speak up when… / We will include… / Even when we are afraid, we will… / Our Caravan will stand beside…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Mid-Term Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on Week 5 and the first half of the term: what you know, what is still a mystery, and what you would say if you were Esther at the doors of the hall.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for the next Showdown bank. One should look back at Weeks 1–4.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea (for example ‘providence’, ‘a tell’ or ‘interdependence’) to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Esth 4:14; Prov 31:8–9; Ps 104:25 — This week we met a young queen who risked her position to speak up for her people, a God who works quietly behind the scenes, and a world of rivers and lakes that is full of life.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most important thing you learned about courage this week? Where have you seen someone step forward when it was difficult?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What stood out in the first half of the term? How have the different subjects helped you understand Persia better?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Read your Caravan Creed aloud. Pray for each other and for the second half of the journey, especially for people who need someone to speak up for them.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. In Hebrew, the Persian king Xerxes is called… <b>A) Ahasuerus ✔</b> · B) Cyrus · C) Darius · D) Haman <em>Ahasuerus is the Hebrew form of the name Xerxes I, who ruled Persia from 486 to 465 BC.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. In which Persian city is most of the story of Esther set? A) Athens · <b>B) Susa ✔</b> · C) Rome · D) Cairo <em>Susa was one of the royal cities of Persia, used by the kings as a winter capital.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>3. Who raised Esther after she was orphaned? A) Haman · <b>B) Mordecai ✔</b> · C) Ezra · D) Daniel <em>Mordecai, Esther’s older cousin, brought her up and later encouraged her to speak to the king.</em> [RE]</li><li>4. Why was it so dangerous for Esther to go to the king without being called? A) Only men were allowed in the hall · B) The palace was closed in winter · <b>C) Anyone who entered unsummoned could be put to death unless the king held out the sceptre ✔</b> · D) The king never saw anyone <em>Persian court rule said that coming before the king unsummoned risked death unless he held out his golden sceptre. Esther chose to go anyway, which is what makes her so brave.</em> [SS-DO] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>5. The villain who plotted against the Jewish people was… A) Nehemiah · B) Daniel · <b>C) Haman ✔</b> · D) Cyrus <em>Haman, a powerful official, persuaded the king to sign an edict against the Jewish people, but his plot was exposed by Esther.</em> [RE]</li><li>6. The Jewish people remember the events of Esther at the festival of… A) Christmas · <b>B) Purim ✔</b> · C) Easter · D) Nowruz only <em>Purim is a Jewish festival that remembers the rescue described in the book of Esther. (Nowruz is the Persian new year, a different festival.)</em> [RE]</li><li>7. Lake Urmia is famous for… A) whales · <b>B) flamingos and brine shrimp ✔</b> · C) kiwi and kākāpō · D) penguins and seals <em>Lake Urmia is a very salty lake in north-west Iran where brine shrimp live and flamingos feed. It has shrunk a lot in recent decades.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>8. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Why does a tell form? <b>A) People rebuild on top of old mud-brick settlements again and again ✔</b> · B) Volcanoes build mounds near cities · C) A river carries a hill to the city · D) Kings order mounds to be built <em>Mud-brick buildings crumbled, and people levelled the ground and built on the same spot. Over centuries the layers rose into a mound called a tell, and archaeologists read the layers from top (newest) to bottom (oldest).</em> [SS-PE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>9. A rhyton is… A) a sword · <b>B) a drinking horn that ends in an animal’s head ✔</b> · C) a wall · D) a coin <em>A rhyton is a drinking vessel that narrows to a point and ends in the head of an animal, such as a lion, ibex or griffin.</em> [VA-UC]</li><li>10. Review: Who named Cyrus long before he was born? A) Ezra · B) Esther · <b>C) Isaiah ✔</b> · D) Haman <em>The prophet Isaiah named Cyrus as a ruler who would help God’s people, long before Cyrus was born (Isaiah 44–45).</em> [RE]</li><li>11. Review: Which Persian king built the Royal Road and organised the empire into provinces? <b>A) Darius I ✔</b> · B) Nehemiah · C) Alexander · D) Cyrus <em>Darius I organised the empire and improved the Royal Road, the highway that carried the king’s messengers (see Stage 3).</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×3 — The book of Esther never names God, but the story shows… A) that God was absent · B) that the king was God · <b>C) God quietly working behind the scenes, through ordinary events and brave choices ✔</b> · D) that nothing matters <em>The ‘coincidences’ of the story, and Esther’s courage, point to providence: God quietly at work behind events. Readers explain the book’s silence about God in different ways, but many see His care in the timing.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Mid-Term Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on Week 5 and the first half of the term: what you know, what is still a mystery, and what you would say if you were Esther at the doors of the hall.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know from Weeks 1–5</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought about courage or providence</li><li>I wrote one goal for the second half of the term</li><li>I rated my effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture for one section.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all four sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two weeks (e.g. Cyrus’s decree and Esther’s edict) and explain how they show different sides of Persian rule.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God… <b>My goal:</b> In the next five weeks I will…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for the next Showdown bank. One should look back at Weeks 1–4.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>One question reviews an earlier week</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall, and explain why each decoy is wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea (for example ‘providence’, ‘a tell’ or ‘interdependence’) to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic or poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Esth 4:14; Prov 31:8–9; Ps 104:25 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and warm; celebrate effort, not only winning. Invite students to share one moment from the term when they saw a friend show courage.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to this week’s NZ connections (speaking up for others; river towns and flood management; Te Waihora and wetlands; taonga and what we treasure).</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point; keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. After the quiz, remind students that Purim is a living festival and the story of Esther is precious to many people.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which earlier-week ideas need revisiting after the mid-term check?</li><li>What will I change for the second half of the term?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 6: Rebuilding the Walls":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Home Again: Persian Rule, Ezra & Nehemiah":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Home Again: Persian Rule, Ezra &amp; Nehemiah — Why would a Persian king help rebuild a city that was not his?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 6: Rebuilding the Walls (458–432 BC) · Babylon → Jerusalem, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why Persian kings supported rebuilding Jerusalem and how Nehemiah led.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can sequence the three returns (538 BC, 458 BC, 445 BC).</li><li>I can explain Nehemiah’s leadership steps.</li><li>I can use a source and say what it shows.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Planning detail (Path A) or the letter or diary (B/C); a source sentence (‘I know… because…’); observation notes from the Council Fire.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names Ezra or Nehemiah and one thing they did, with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Sequences the three returns correctly and describes Nehemiah’s main steps.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why Persian kings supported rebuilding, using evidence and the idea of policy, and explains cause and effect in the Nehemiah story.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates Nehemiah’s leadership, weighs different explanations for the king’s support (policy and faith), and says what a source can and cannot tell us.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>exile</b> — Being made to live far from your homeland.</li><li><b>province</b> — A region of an empire run by a governor. Judah was a small Persian province called Yehud.</li><li><b>cupbearer</b> — A trusted royal servant who tasted and served the king’s wine. He was often close to the king and had to be loyal.</li><li><b>scribe</b> — A person trained to read, write and copy important texts. Ezra was a priest and a scribe.</li><li><b>decree</b> — An official order or announcement from a ruler.</li><li><b>governor</b> — An official put in charge of a province. Nehemiah later served as governor of Judah.</li><li><b>opposition</b> — People who work against a plan or try to stop it.</li><li><b>papyrus</b> — Ancient paper made from a reed; letters on papyrus have survived in Egypt’s dry sand.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Broken Decree</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The caravan reaches a tumbled stone gatehouse where a heap of old tablets and scraps lies under a tarpaulin. Shirin kneels. ‘Look, Courier. A royal decree, torn into strips by the wind. Put the pieces back in order, and work out what it permitted. Then tell me: if you were the king, what must happen NEXT for this to be more than words on a strip?’ Behind her on the hill, a city waits with no walls.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: write a short, simple ‘decree’ in 6–8 phrases on strips (for example: ‘By order of the king…’, ‘the people may go home…’, ‘and rebuild their house of worship…’, ‘timber will be given from the royal forest…’, ‘and the governors must help.’). Cut into strips and make one set per Caravan (teams of 4–5, the same teams as Weeks 1–5).</li><li>Retrieval (3 min): from Week 5 — which queen did God use ‘for such a time as this’? Which Persian city was her home palace? Take two ideas.</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud. Pause at ‘what must happen NEXT’.</li><li>Escape Room (5 min): teams put the decree strips in the order that makes best sense. The first team to agree shouts ‘Seal it!’ and explains their order. Other teams check their own.</li><li>Planning (2 min): on mini-whiteboards, teams write ‘What must happen next?’ (people, tools, materials, protection). Take quick shares without correcting.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria. Students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Week 5: Esther, Xerxes (Ahasuerus) and the city of Susa. What did a king’s decree do, and why did people have to obey it?</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Three journeys, one king’s support, one leader who prayed and then planned. Learn how Persian rule made the return possible, and how Nehemiah got a huge job done.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline (4 min): add three date cards to the class strip: 538 BC (Cyrus), 458 BC (Ezra), 445 BC (Nehemiah). Mini-whiteboards: ‘Who came first? How many years between Ezra and Nehemiah?’ (13). Say honestly: ‘Scholars debate Ezra’s date, so we say about.’</li><li>Persian policy (4 min): ask ‘Why might a king want a province to be peaceful and loyal?’ Draw out: a settled province pays taxes and does not rebel. Add: ‘Christians also read that God worked through the king’s decisions.’ Keep both ideas side by side.</li><li>Nehemiah’s steps (4 min): chart five steps on the board: HEAR the problem → PRAY → ASK (with a plan) → LOOK and SHARE the plan → ORGANISE everyone and PROTECT the builders. Ask: ‘Which step would you find hardest?’</li><li>Source (3 min): show the Elephantine papyri card. Ask: ‘What does a letter to officials tell us that a story does not? What does it leave out?’ Model a source sentence: ‘I know Persian provinces used letters because the Elephantine papyri show…’.</li><li>Model the first step of Paths A–C (2 min); students choose.</li><li><b>Three returns, three dates:</b> Around <b>538 BC</b>, <b>Cyrus</b> allowed exiled peoples, including Jewish families from Babylon, to go home and rebuild their temple (<b>Ezra 1–2</b>). About <b>458 BC</b> the priest and scribe <b>Ezra</b> led a second group under <b>Artaxerxes I</b> (<b>Ezra 7–8</b>). About <b>445 BC</b> <b>Nehemiah</b> came as an official with a mission to rebuild the walls. <i>Some scholars date Ezra’s journey to a different year, so historians say ‘about’.</i></li><li><b>A province called Yehud:</b> Judah was a small <b>province</b> of the Persian Empire, called <b>Yehud</b>. It sat in a huge region called <b>‘Beyond the River’</b> (west of the Euphrates), ruled from a satrapy centre. The Persian way was often to let local peoples keep their customs and worship while paying taxes. That policy helps explain why kings backed local temples, though Jewish and Christian readers also see <b>God moving the kings’ hearts</b>.</li><li><b>Ezra: the scribe who studied, did and taught:</b> <b>Ezra</b> was a priest and a skilled scribe. The book of Ezra says he had set his heart to <b>study</b> the Law, to <b>do</b> it, and to <b>teach</b> it (<b>Ezra 7:10</b>). He led a caravan from Babylon, praying and fasting before the dangerous journey instead of asking the king for soldiers. The trip took about <b>four months</b>.</li><li><b>Nehemiah: cupbearer, prayer-warrior, project leader:</b> <b>Nehemiah</b> served as <b>cupbearer</b> to King <b>Artaxerxes I</b> in Susa. When he heard Jerusalem’s walls were broken, he wept, fasted and prayed (<b>Neh 1</b>). Months later, when the king noticed his sad face, Nehemiah <b>prayed a quick prayer and then asked</b> for letters of safe passage and timber (<b>Neh 2</b>). He inspected the walls at night, shared the plan, and gave each family a section (<b>Neh 3</b>). The wall was finished in <b>52 days</b> (<b>Neh 6:15</b>).</li><li><b>Opposition and a historical source:</b> Not everyone cheered. <b>Sanballat</b>, <b>Tobiah</b> and <b>Geshem</b> mocked, plotted and sent messages to scare the builders (<b>Neh 4 and 6</b>). Nehemiah’s answer: <b>pray, post a guard and keep building</b> (<b>Neh 4:9</b>). A non-biblical source: the <b>Elephantine papyri</b>, letters from a Jewish community in Egypt, about 407 BC, writing to Persian officials in Judah to ask for help to rebuild their temple. They show how a Persian province really worked: letters, officials, permissions and replies.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: The Three Returns</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>match: Who or What Is It?</b> — Match each name or word to its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Nehemiah: Pray, Plan or Protect?</b> — Sort each action into the bin that fits best.</li><li><b>reveal: What Do the Sources Say?</b> — Tap each card to see what a source shows, and what it might leave out.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Nehemiah’s Project Plan:</b> Plan the wall like Nehemiah, inspired by Neh 3: choose roles, sections of wall, materials, steps and risks. Show who builds what, and what could go wrong.</li><li><b>Path B — Cupbearer’s Request Letter:</b> Write a polite, persuasive letter from Nehemiah to King Artaxerxes (Neh 2). Explain the problem, ask for what you need, and show you have a plan.</li><li><b>Path C — Returnee Diary:</b> Write three diary entries from a family on the road from Babylon to Jerusalem: leaving, the journey, and arriving at the broken city.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Neh 1:4–11; Neh 4:9; Ezra 7:10 — Nehemiah hears about Jerusalem’s broken walls and sits down and weeps. He fasts and prays, confessing his people’s failures and asking God to give him favour with the king. Later, when enemies threaten the builders, he says they prayed to God and also set a guard day and night. And Ezra had set his heart to study God’s law, to live it, and to teach it.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Nehemiah prayed AND planned. Why do you think he did both? What would have gone wrong if he had only prayed? What if he had only planned?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Think of a big team job, for example a school production or a garden. What does it look like to pray AND to plan and work hard? How is this different from asking God to do everything, or from trusting only yourself?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write: ‘One big job I need to pray and plan for is…’. Then write one prayer and one first step.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Nehemiah’s Project Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan the wall like Nehemiah, inspired by Neh 3: choose roles, sections of wall, materials, steps and risks. Show who builds what, and what could go wrong.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I listed at least 5 jobs or sections and who does each</li><li>I put the steps in a sensible order</li><li>I named 3 materials or tools</li><li>I named 2 risks (such as mockers, weather, shortage) and a plan for each</li><li>I explained why teamwork matters in a job this big</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the planning chart with the roles pre-printed; choose five and fill in who and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own plan with roles, steps, materials and two risks with responses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a timeline for 52 days (for example, week 1: clear rubble; week 2: …) and explain which job must be done first and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Project:</b> Rebuild the wall of ___. <b>Jobs and roles:</b> ___ builds ___ because ___. <b>Steps:</b> First… Then… Finally… <b>Risks:</b> One risk is ___. We will ___. <b>Prayer and plan:</b> We will ask God for ___ and we will ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Cupbearer’s Request Letter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a polite, persuasive letter from Nehemiah to King Artaxerxes (Neh 2). Explain the problem, ask for what you need, and show you have a plan.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I used a respectful greeting for a king</li><li>I explained the problem clearly</li><li>I made a specific request (permission, letters of passage, timber)</li><li>I gave at least two reasons the king would benefit</li><li>I ended politely and signed as Nehemiah</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the letter frame with starters for each paragraph; share your ideas with a partner first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write the letter independently using the frame, with a clear request and two reasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write TWO versions: one for the king’s ears (focus on the province’s benefits) and one prayer to God (what Nehemiah really felt). Explain why they are different.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Greeting:</b> To King Artaxerxes, may you live long. <b>Problem:</b> The city of my ancestors lies in ruins because… <b>Request:</b> If it pleases the king, I ask for… <b>Plan:</b> I will… and I will return to report… <b>Why it helps:</b> A peaceful, safe province will… <b>Sign-off:</b> Your faithful servant, Nehemiah.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Returnee Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write three diary entries from a family on the road from Babylon to Jerusalem: leaving, the journey, and arriving at the broken city.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote three dated entries (leaving, journey, arrival)</li><li>I included at least two true details (distance, desert, camp, dust, fear, joy)</li><li>I showed feelings, not only facts</li><li>I used the words exile, province or decree</li><li>I included a prayer or hope</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the entry starters and a word bank (dust · camp · camel · decree · hope · ruins) for each entry.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write three full entries with true details and feelings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write from two different family members (a child who has only known Babylon, and a grandparent who remembers a story of home) and show how their feelings differ.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Entry 1 (leaving):</b> Today we left Babylon because… I feel… <b>Entry 2 (journey):</b> The road was… We worry about… We were glad when… <b>Entry 3 (arrival):</b> When I saw the walls… I will…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Neh 1:4–11; Neh 2:4–8; Neh 4:9; Ezra 7:10 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Historians cannot confirm every detail of Ezra and Nehemiah from outside sources, and the date of Ezra’s journey is debated. Christians read the books as Scripture; others in the class may read them as history or as stories. Keep the focus on leadership and perseverance. Avoid suggesting that all Persian rulers shared Jewish faith, and never put students of other faiths on the spot.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How do communities in Aotearoa rebuild after something is lost or damaged, for example a marae, a church or community hall after an earthquake or flood? Who leads? How do whānau and neighbours share the work? (Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to share how rebuilding looks in your own rohe, and avoid generalising across iwi. Canterbury classes may also wish to talk about the rebuilding after the earthquakes.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Persian kings in this story are shown helping the Jewish people. Present Persian rule as complex: kings supported local temples for many reasons, including policy. Some students may be Iranian-NZ, Muslim, Jewish or from a Zoroastrian background; treat the story with respect and never ask a child to speak for a whole community. Do not suggest that the biblical account is the only way to see the history.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>‘Decree’ cut-up phrase strips (one set per team)</li><li>Timeline strip with new date cards (538 BC, 458 BC, 445 BC, 52 days)</li><li>Map of the three returns (app or printed)</li><li>Path A project-plan sheet and brick-pattern paper</li><li>Path B request-letter frame</li><li>Source card: Elephantine papyri (app or printed)</li><li>Neh 1–2 and Neh 6 reading (your own Bible)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students put the three returns in order and explain why each happened?</li><li>Who could give TWO reasons the king helped, not just one?</li><li>Did students show Nehemiah’s steps (hear, pray, ask, plan, organise) in their plan, letter or diary?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Jerusalem on a Hill: Walls, Gates & the Return Route":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jerusalem on a Hill: Walls, Gates &amp; the Return Route — Why was the city built on a hill, and what do walls and gates do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Settlements &amp; Cities</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 6: Rebuilding the Walls (458–432 BC) · Babylon → Jerusalem, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Ecological sustainability; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why a walled hill city was built where it was, and to map a long journey.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name four reasons Jerusalem’s site worked (hill, spring, defence, routes).</li><li>I can map the return route from Babylon to Jerusalem.</li><li>I can describe how gates and walls work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The map (conventions, accuracy of route and calculations); a site explanation using at least three of: hill, spring, defence, routes; photograph of the model.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one or two features of Jerusalem’s site with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Names four reasons the site worked and uses a compass and scale bar on a map.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how each feature helped the city, calculates journey time, and describes what walls and gates do.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares Jerusalem with another settlement, weighs advantages and disadvantages, and evaluates what else mattered.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>ridge</b> — A long, narrow stretch of high land.</li><li><b>spring</b> — Water that flows naturally out of the ground. Jerusalem’s main spring was the Gihon.</li><li><b>fortification</b> — A wall, tower or other structure that protects a place.</li><li><b>gate</b> — An opening in a wall that can be opened or closed. Gates were also markets and courts.</li><li><b>Fertile Crescent</b> — A curve of green farmland from the Persian Gulf up the rivers and round to the Mediterranean.</li><li><b>route</b> — A way from one place to another.</li><li><b>hazard</b> — Something that could cause harm or delay on a journey.</li><li><b>scale</b> — How map distance relates to real distance.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Build a City Wall</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The caravan arrives at a pile of fallen stones. Shirin tosses you a handful of blocks. ‘Nehemiah’s workers needed a wall that would stop an enemy, but also let friends in. You have five minutes. Build a wall around the village on the table, then we will test it with an invader.’ She holds up a small ball and grins. ‘Be ready. Walls always get tested.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: set out a block set per Caravan, a small cardboard ‘village’, and a small ball (the ‘invader’). Use a tray or table edge so the ball does not roll far.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — name two of Nehemiah’s steps; which king did he serve; how long did the wall take?</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch. Teams build a wall around the village (4 min): they must include one gate.</li><li>Test (3 min): roll the ball at each wall and at each gate. Which designs hold? Why did a gate become a weak point? Record observations on a mini-whiteboard.</li><li>Share the LI/SC and draw out ideas: shape, thickness, height, and the role of the gate. Say: ‘Cities do not only need walls: they need smart gates.’</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: Nehemiah’s steps (hear, pray, ask, plan, organise); his king; the 52 days.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A hill, a spring, deep valleys and busy roads: Jerusalem’s site gave it four big advantages, and every one of them shows up on a map.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Site (5 min): show the Jerusalem diagram. Point to the ridge, valleys and Gihon. Ask: ‘Which of the four reasons does each feature give: hill, spring, defence, routes?’ Mini-whiteboards: students match feature to reason.</li><li>Gates (3 min): read out a few gate names from Neh 3 and ask students to guess what came through each. Link to a market (Sheep Gate) or a water source (Water Gate).</li><li>Route (4 min): trace the Fertile Crescent on the big map, tracking with string. Calculate: ‘If a caravan walks 25 km a day, how many days is 1,400 km?’ (56). Compare with Ezra’s four months, including rests.</li><li>Local link (3 min): ‘Why would people choose a hill or a ridge?’ Collect ideas. Say clearly: ‘Pā sites were chosen by specific iwi for specific reasons. Our teacher will invite local mana whenua to share the stories of our own area.’</li><li><b>A city on a ridge:</b> Ancient <b>Jerusalem</b> sat on a rocky <b>ridge</b>, with deep <b>valleys</b> on several sides that worked like natural moats. An enemy had to climb up steep ground to attack. This is one reason people chose the site long before the Persians. Ancient Jerusalem was small, perhaps as little as a few hectares, much smaller than a modern city. <i>Archaeologists still debate how big the city was in Nehemiah’s time.</i></li><li><b>The Gihon spring:</b> Water was life. Just outside the old city lay the <b>Gihon spring</b>, a rare natural source in the hills. Over time, people dug channels and tunnels to bring spring water safely inside the walls. Having water inside a wall means a city can survive a <b>siege</b>.</li><li><b>Walls and gates:</b> A wall keeps a city safe, but a wall with no gates is just a fence! <b>Gates</b> let in traders, farmers and visitors. Gates were also the city’s <b>market</b> and <b>court</b>, where elders sat to settle disputes. Nehemiah 3 lists gates, including the <b>Sheep Gate</b>, <b>Fish Gate</b>, <b>Valley Gate</b>, <b>Water Gate</b> and <b>Horse Gate</b>. Each name hints at what came through it.</li><li><b>The return route:</b> From <b>Babylon</b>, travellers could not cross the Syrian Desert directly, so they followed the curve of the <b>Fertile Crescent</b>: up the <b>Euphrates</b>, round the north and then south toward Jerusalem. The distance was roughly <b>1,400 km</b>. Ezra’s group took about <b>four months</b>, including rests and camps (<b>Ezra 7:9</b>).</li><li><b>Hill city, hill pā:</b> Many people around the world chose high places for settlements: for defence, view and safety. In Aotearoa, many pā were built on ridges, headlands or other high ground, with ditches and palisades. Each pā was a choice made for a particular place and iwi, so we <b>ask local mana whenua</b> about pā in our own area.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Jerusalem’s Site</b> — Match each feature to how it helped the city.</li><li><b>order: The Road from Babylon</b> — Put the stages of the return journey in order.</li><li><b>sort: Wall or Gate?</b> — Is each description mostly about the wall or the gate?</li><li><b>reveal: Why Build Here?</b> — Tap each card to see how the site helped ancient Jerusalem.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Walled City Model with Gates:</b> Build a model of a walled hill city with at least three labelled gates. Show the ridge, the valleys and a water source.</li><li><b>Path B — Return Route Map:</b> Draw a map of the return route from Babylon to Jerusalem with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Mark at least six places, three hazards, and calculate the journey time.</li><li><b>Path C — Settlement Comparison: Jerusalem and a Hill Settlement at Home:</b> Compare Jerusalem with a hilltop or defended settlement in your own area (such as a pā site or a historic hill fort, chosen with guidance from local mana whenua or your Māori Education lead). How are their sites alike and different?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Ps 122; Ps 125:2; Neh 3 — Psalm 122 is a song of joy about going up to Jerusalem, a city joined firmly together, where people gather for peace. Psalm 125 says the hills surround Jerusalem and the Lord surrounds His people. In Nehemiah 3, priests, goldsmiths, perfumers, rulers and ordinary families repair the wall together, each taking a section.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What is the job of a wall? What is the job of a gate? What would happen to a city that had one but not the other?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> A good community has strong boundaries (to keep people safe) and open welcome (to let others in). How can a classroom or a school do both?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write or draw one way our class can be ‘a place with open gates’, where new people feel welcome and safe.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Walled City Model with Gates</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a model of a walled hill city with at least three labelled gates. Show the ridge, the valleys and a water source.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My model shows a hill or ridge</li><li>I included at least 3 gates and labelled what each is for</li><li>I showed a water source (spring or tunnel)</li><li>I included a key or labels</li><li>I can explain two ways the site helps defence</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made base with the hill shaped; add a wall, three gates and labels from the bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build and label independently with a key and a water source.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a cut-away showing how spring water could reach the city safely and explain why this matters in a siege.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label bank:</b> wall · gate · tower · spring · valley · ridge · market · road. <b>Say it:</b> The ___ gate is where ___ come in. The wall protects the city because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Return Route Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a map of the return route from Babylon to Jerusalem with a title, compass rose, scale bar and key. Mark at least six places, three hazards, and calculate the journey time.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose, scale bar and key</li><li>Route shown clearly around the desert</li><li>At least 6 labelled places (such as Babylon, the Euphrates, Jerusalem, the Mediterranean)</li><li>3 hazards (desert, rivers, bandits, thirst, weather)</li><li>Distance and travel days calculated</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map; trace the route, add compass and scale bar, and mark three places.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with all map conventions and 6 places, and calculate the days at 25 km a day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the journey at three speeds (20, 25, 30 km a day), compare with Ezra’s four-month trip, and explain what could account for the difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Route:</b> Babylon → along the Euphrates → around the desert → south to Jerusalem. <b>Calculate:</b> ___ km ÷ ___ km per day = ___ days. <b>Hazards:</b> One risk is ___ and travellers could ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Settlement Comparison: Jerusalem and a Hill Settlement at Home</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare Jerusalem with a hilltop or defended settlement in your own area (such as a pā site or a historic hill fort, chosen with guidance from local mana whenua or your Māori Education lead). How are their sites alike and different?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I described the site of each settlement</li><li>I gave two similarities and two differences</li><li>I named at least one way water, defence and routes mattered in each</li><li>I used local guidance and avoided generalising across iwi</li><li>I wrote a conclusion</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a two-column table with sentence starters; choose from a bank of features (hill, water, view, wall, route).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a paragraph comparing the two with two similarities and two differences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Argue which site was better defended and explain what other factors (water, food, trade) also mattered.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Similar:</b> Both… <b>Different:</b> But in ___, … whereas in ___… <b>Local guidance:</b> Our teacher invited ___ to tell us… <b>Conclusion:</b> People chose these places because…</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 122; Ps 125:2; Neh 3 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep a balanced tone. Walls can protect, and walls can also exclude. Do not draw modern political conclusions about walls or borders. Keep the focus on the ancient city, and the Psalm’s idea of peace and belonging.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare the choice of high places for settlement in Jerusalem with pā on ridges and headlands in Aotearoa. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to share the stories of pā or kāinga in your rohe, and avoid generalising across iwi. How did people there use water, food, defence and trade routes to choose where to live? Canterbury classes might explore how the Port Hills or Banks Peninsula shaped settlement.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Jerusalem is sacred to Jews, Christians and Muslims, and is a sensitive subject today. Keep this lesson to the ancient city and its landforms. Do not draw modern political conclusions, and never ask a student to speak for a whole community.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wooden or foam blocks; small ball or marble (‘invader’); sand tray (optional)</li><li>Large map of the Middle East; string; atlases</li><li>Cardboard, clay, glue, small sticks for gates</li><li>Return-route outline map and a hazard card set</li><li>Jerusalem hill-and-valleys diagram (app or printed)</li><li>Local-area map for Path C</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain WHY each feature mattered (not only name it)?</li><li>Did students use scale and calculations correctly in Path B?</li><li>How did students explain the role of gates, not only walls?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Rebuilding a Habitat: Restoration & Recovery":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rebuilding a Habitat: Restoration &amp; Recovery — What happens when a link in the web is lost, and how do we help it recover?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Habitats &amp; Restoration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 6: Rebuilding the Walls (458–432 BC) · Babylon → Jerusalem, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how habitats are damaged and how they can be restored.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name human causes of habitat damage.</li><li>I can explain one restoration step.</li><li>I can model what happens when a link in a web is lost.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Vocabulary use; the quality and sequence of restoration steps; Jenga data table and conclusion; photograph of Path products.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one human cause of habitat damage or one restoration step, with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes causes of damage and several restoration steps accurately, using the key vocabulary.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how losing one link affects others, and explains why each restoration step helps.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares two restoration stories, evaluates the limits of the Jenga model, and proposes how to test whether restoration worked.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>habitat</b> — The natural home of a living thing.</li><li><b>restoration</b> — Repairing a damaged place or habitat so living things can return.</li><li><b>ecosystem</b> — A community of living things plus the non-living parts (soil, water, air, sunlight) around them.</li><li><b>extinct</b> — No more living members of that kind of living thing exist.</li><li><b>endangered</b> — At risk of becoming extinct.</li><li><b>reintroduction</b> — Bringing an animal back into a place where it used to live.</li><li><b>introduced species</b> — A living thing brought by people to a place where it did not live before.</li><li><b>predator control</b> — Trapping or removing predators that harm native animals.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Jenga Web</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Under the oaks of a dry hill, Shirin stacks a tower of wooden blocks. ‘Each block is a living thing: grass, oak, deer, bee, fox, fungus, seed. Take it in turns to pull one out. The tower is the web of life. Be careful, Courier, because I have never seen the tower fall without somebody gasping.’ Gandom the camel leans in, nudges a block, and the tower begins to wobble.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: Label the blocks on a Jenga-style set with species (grass, oak, acorn-eating bird, fallow deer, beetle, fungus, shrub, wolf, bee, seed…). Use a marker; allow 12–15 labelled blocks per Caravan.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — name two features that helped Jerusalem’s defence; what is a gate for; how long is the return route?</li><li>Read the dispatch. Teams take turns removing a block (3 min per round) and saying which species they removed and what they think will happen. Record on a table: Round · block removed · did the tower wobble or fall?</li><li>Discuss (3 min): ‘When did the tower first wobble? Were all blocks equally important? Did removing blocks from the bottom or the middle matter more?’</li><li>Link: ‘In a real habitat, nature has many links, and it can lose them when people clear forests, drain wetlands or bring in new species.’ Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: ridge, spring, valleys and routes; the job of gates; about 1,400 km by the Fertile Crescent.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Habitats change. Some changes are natural. Others are caused by people. And some damaged places can be repaired, though it takes patience, teamwork and hard work, like rebuilding a wall.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Web discussion (4 min): reflect on the Jenga results. ‘What did we learn about links? Which blocks mattered most? Did we ever notice the tower fell after several small removals?’ Use ‘often’ and ‘may’: real ecosystems vary.</li><li>Causes (4 min): list human causes of habitat damage on the board. Students sort each as: clearing · grazing · draining · pollution · introduced species. Ask ‘Which of these happens in our region?’</li><li>Fallow deer (4 min): show the photo. Tell the rediscovery story, using hedged language. Ask: ‘Why might a small group be at risk even after it is protected?’ (small numbers, disease, one disaster).</li><li>Restoration steps (3 min): list the four steps. Link to the wall: ‘Protect, remove the cause, rebuild, monitor: Nehemiah’s steps again!’ Introduce Animal Cards (1 min).</li><li><b>Change, damage and recovery:</b> Habitats change naturally through fire, flood or drought. People can also change habitats by <b>clearing forests</b>, <b>overgrazing</b>, <b>draining wetlands</b>, <b>polluting water</b> or <b>bringing in new species</b>. A habitat that has been damaged can <b>recover</b>, but sometimes it needs help. <b>Restoration</b> is repair work: protect the place, replant, remove pests.</li><li><b>The Zagros oak woodland:</b> The slopes of the Zagros were once covered in <b>oak woodland</b> (Persian oak and wild pistachio). Over many centuries, woodland has been lost to <b>wood cutting</b>, <b>grazing</b> and <b>farming</b>. Without trees, soil washes off the slopes and streams can dry up. Today, projects in Iran work to <b>protect and replant</b> oak forest, though the work is slow.</li><li><b>The Persian fallow deer: a rediscovery:</b> The <b>Persian fallow deer</b> has <b>palmate antlers</b> (flat, like open hands) and a reddish-brown coat with white spots in summer. Hunting and loss of riverside woodland nearly wiped it out. It was thought to be <b>lost</b> until a few were found alive in <b>Iran in the 1950s</b>. Since then, it has been <b>protected</b> in reserves and some deer have been <b>reintroduced</b> to other places. It is still rare, so people keep a close watch. <i>Scientists say ‘thought lost’ because small numbers can hide in remote places.</i></li><li><b>Restoration steps:</b> Most restoration follows steps: <b>1 Protect</b> the place (fences, reserves). <b>2 Remove the cause</b> of harm (overgrazing, pests, pollution). <b>3 Replant</b> or <b>reintroduce</b> native species. <b>4 Monitor</b> and keep caring. Success takes years, and different groups, scientists, farmers, iwi and schools, must work together.</li><li><b>Restoration in Aotearoa:</b> New Zealand has restoration stories of its own: <b>predator control</b> to protect native birds, <b>native plantings</b> along streams, and <b>wetland restoration</b> such as <b>Travis Wetland</b> in Christchurch. Many of these projects are led by communities, volunteers, schools and <b>iwi</b>, often through the idea of <b>kaitiakitanga</b> (guardianship). Ask your local iwi or Māori Education lead how restoration works in your rohe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>sort: Damage or Repair?</b> — Does each action damage a habitat, or help restore it?</li><li><b>match: Restoration Words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Steps of Restoration</b> — Put these restoration steps in a sensible order.</li><li><b>reveal: The Persian Fallow Deer Story</b> — Tap each card to follow the deer’s story.</li><li>animalCards</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Restoration Plan:</b> Choose a damaged habitat (an oak woodland, a wetland, a stream or a local site). Write a restoration plan with steps, species, helpers and a timeline.</li><li><b>Path B — Jenga Web Investigation:</b> Investigate the Jenga web as a scientist. Record which blocks are removed and when the tower wobbles or falls, then analyse your data and draw a conclusion.</li><li><b>Path C — Two Habitats, One Problem:</b> Compare a Persian restoration story (Zagros oak woodland or the Persian fallow deer) with a New Zealand restoration story (such as Travis Wetland or predator control for a native bird). What problem do both share, and how are the solutions alike and different?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Isa 58:12; Rom 8:19–21; Gen 2:15 — Isaiah says that those who help others will be called ‘repairers of broken walls’ and ‘restorers of streets to live in’. Paul writes that the whole creation waits, as if with eager hope, to be set free from decay. In Genesis, God places the first human in the garden to work it and take care of it.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it mean to be a ‘repairer of broken walls and restorer of streets’? What did Nehemiah’s workers do, and what could we repair today?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Rebuilding walls and restoring habitats are both about repair and care. What do they share? What might be different (people and wildlife, buildings and ecosystems)?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose ONE hands-on restoration act you can do at school or home this term (for example, planting, weeding, rubbish collection, or building a bird box), and write who will help you.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Restoration Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a damaged habitat (an oak woodland, a wetland, a stream or a local site). Write a restoration plan with steps, species, helpers and a timeline.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I named the habitat and what damaged it</li><li>I wrote at least 4 steps in a sensible order</li><li>I named two species to protect or bring back</li><li>I named who would help (scientists, schools, iwi, farmers)</li><li>I explained how we would know if it worked</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the step frame and a species bank (oak · fallow deer · grass · beetle · bird · stream); complete each step with a sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write the plan independently with four steps, two species and two helpers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a 10-year timeline and explain which step must come first and why, and one thing that could go wrong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Habitat:</b> ___ <b>What went wrong:</b> It was damaged because… <b>Steps:</b> 1 Protect… 2 Remove… 3 Replant… 4 Monitor… <b>Helpers:</b> ___, ___ and ___ will help. <b>Success:</b> We will know it is working when…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Jenga Web Investigation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Investigate the Jenga web as a scientist. Record which blocks are removed and when the tower wobbles or falls, then analyse your data and draw a conclusion.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I recorded each round in a table (block removed · result)</li><li>I repeated the test at least twice</li><li>I noticed a pattern in the data</li><li>I wrote a conclusion that uses evidence</li><li>I explained how a Jenga tower is and is not like a real ecosystem</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made table and work with a partner to record; complete the sentence ‘The tower fell when…’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Run two trials, record the results, and write a conclusion with one limitation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design a fair test: change one variable (for example, remove only ‘top’ blocks) and explain what you kept the same.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> Does removing blocks from the bottom make the tower fall sooner? <b>Table:</b> Round · Block removed · Wobble? · Fell? <b>Pattern:</b> I noticed that… <b>Conclusion:</b> The data suggests… <b>Limitation:</b> A real ecosystem is different because…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Two Habitats, One Problem</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare a Persian restoration story (Zagros oak woodland or the Persian fallow deer) with a New Zealand restoration story (such as Travis Wetland or predator control for a native bird). What problem do both share, and how are the solutions alike and different?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I described the Persian story in my own words</li><li>I described a NZ story with a real example</li><li>I named one shared problem</li><li>I gave two similarities and one difference in the solutions</li><li>I used the words habitat, restoration and ecosystem</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a two-column table with a word bank (protect · replant · pests · deer · wetland · birds) to complete.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a short comparison with two similarities and a difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Evaluate which story shows the faster recovery and explain what else you would need to know to be sure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Persian story:</b> In Iran, … <b>NZ story:</b> In Aotearoa, … <b>Shared problem:</b> Both… <b>Similar:</b> Both used… <b>Different:</b> In ___, however, …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Isa 58:12; Rom 8:19–21; Gen 2:15 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Present stewardship as a gift and a responsibility, not guilt. Christians hold a range of views on how creation’s ‘waiting’ in Romans 8 should be read; keep the focus on care for creation. Link to kaitiakitanga with care and invite local voices, without generalising across iwi.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Aotearoa has many restoration stories: native plantings on riverbanks, wetlands like Travis Wetland, predator-free sanctuaries and kākāpō recovery. Which are in your area? Who is leading them? Link to kaitiakitanga and invite your local iwi, Māori Education lead or a local restoration group to visit or share; avoid generalising across iwi.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Habitat loss can feel heavy for some students. Frame it as ‘a problem people can help fix’, with real examples of recovery. The fallow-deer story is one of hope. Avoid blaming any country or people for environmental harm; these are shared problems.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jenga-style blocks and a marker pen per team</li><li>Recording table for the Jenga Web</li><li>Photos of Zagros oak woodland and Persian fallow deer</li><li>Photos of a NZ restoration site (a Canterbury or local example if possible)</li><li>Animal Cards (collect weekly)</li><li>Recording sheets and A3 paper</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students link ‘a lost block’ to ‘a lost species’ and explain the cascade?</li><li>Did students recognise that a model (Jenga) has limits?</li><li>Which restoration step did students find hardest to explain?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Build the Gate: Architecture & a Collaborative Gate of Welcome":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Build the Gate: Architecture &amp; a Collaborative Gate of Welcome — Draw it, build it, and make it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Architecture</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 6: Rebuilding the Walls (458–432 BC) · Babylon → Jerusalem, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>TE — Technology: Designing &amp; Making. Students design and make to meet a purpose.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to design and construct an architectural form using symmetry and repeating columns.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can draw a symmetrical gate with a ruler.</li><li>I can design a column capital.</li><li>I can work with a team on a shared structure.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Symmetry and proportion in the drawing or model; labelled column parts; evidence of teamwork (role in the group, the team plan); the artist’s statement.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a simple gate or column with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Draws or builds a symmetrical gate with labelled column parts and repeated columns.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls symmetry, proportion and repeat accurately and explains what the gate communicates.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Designs with intent, compares architecture from different cultures, and evaluates how the team plan contributed to the finished structure.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>architecture</b> — The art and science of designing buildings and structures.</li><li><b>column</b> — An upright support. It has a base, a shaft and a capital.</li><li><b>capital</b> — The decorated top of a column.</li><li><b>shaft</b> — The long main body of a column.</li><li><b>elevation</b> — A drawing of the front of a building, as if you stand facing it.</li><li><b>symmetry</b> — When one half of a design mirrors the other half.</li><li><b>proportion</b> — How the sizes of parts compare with each other and with the whole.</li><li><b>guardian figure</b> — A statue placed to ‘watch over’ an entrance (at Persepolis, huge winged bulls with human heads).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Gate of All Nations</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>On the terrace at Persepolis, Shirin holds up a drawing of a tremendous gate: two enormous bulls, each with wings and a bearded human head, stand guard on either side, with tall columns beyond. ‘Everyone who entered the royal city was greeted by guardians, Courier. The gate said: “You are about to meet the king, so be ready.” A good gate says something. Now study it. What do you see? What does it say?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — name a human cause of habitat damage; list two restoration steps; what is a reintroduction?</li><li>Show the Gate of All Nations at Persepolis (photo or app scene). Allow 60 seconds of silent looking. Then ask: ‘What do you see? What materials? How tall? What is the message?’</li><li>Think-pair-share (3 min): ‘If you were a traveller arriving here, how would you feel? Why might a king put guardians at the gate?’ Draw out: power, welcome, protection and meaning.</li><li>Teach the key idea (2 min): this gate was one of the entrances to the royal terrace, probably built under Xerxes. Persian architects used huge stone columns and guardians to impress visitors. Share the LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: causes of habitat damage; the four restoration steps; the Persian fallow deer’s rediscovery.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A strong gate needs a plan. Learn the secrets of columns, symmetry and proportion, then build something none of you could build alone.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Column anatomy (4 min): draw a column on the board: base, shaft, capital. Label each part. Ask: ‘What do the three parts do?’ Mini-whiteboards: students sketch a column with three labelled parts.</li><li>Symmetry and elevation (4 min): demonstrate drawing a symmetrical gate with a centre line and ruler. Model: draw the centre line, mark equal distances left and right, repeat columns at equal spacing.</li><li>Materials (3 min): show cardboard tubes, clay and PVA. Safety: scissors away from the body, glue guns teacher only.</li><li>Team plan (3 min): for Path C, each Caravan agrees on a job for each person (a column, a guardian, a roof beam, a pattern panel) before any building begins. Remind them: Nehemiah’s workers each had a section.</li><li><b>A gate with guardians:</b> The <b>Gate of All Nations</b> at Persepolis had huge guardian figures: <b>bulls with wings and human heads</b>, carved from stone. Guardians like these were also used in Assyrian and Babylonian palaces, and the Persian builders borrowed ideas from many lands. The gate had <b>four tall columns</b> inside. Visitors passed between the giants on their way to the king’s halls.</li><li><b>Parts of a column:</b> Every column has three parts: a <b>base</b> at the bottom, a <b>shaft</b> (the long middle) and a <b>capital</b> at the top. Persian columns were very tall and slim, some well over <b>twenty metres</b>, with <b>fluted</b> (grooved) shafts. Their capitals were carved with the front halves of <b>bulls</b> or <b>lions</b> back to back, which held up cedar beams.</li><li><b>Symmetry in buildings:</b> A <b>symmetrical</b> gate has a mirror line down the middle: the left side matches the right. Architects use symmetry to make a building feel calm, ordered and strong. A good drawing begins with a <b>centre line</b> and uses a ruler to keep matching parts the same size.</li><li><b>Proportion and repeating columns:</b> <b>Proportion</b> is about how parts fit together: how tall a column is compared with how wide, how far apart the columns stand. A row of <b>repeating columns</b> makes a strong <b>rhythm</b>, like a drumbeat you can see. Try drawing a column twice as tall as it is wide, then repeat it evenly.</li><li><b>Everyone built the part in front of their own house:</b> In <b>Nehemiah 3</b>, families repaired the wall <b>in front of their own house</b>. Each person did a small part, but together they finished a whole wall. The same is true when building a gate: each person adds a column, a stone or a panel, and the whole is bigger than any one part.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Column and Gate Words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Draw a Symmetrical Gate</b> — Put the steps of drawing a gate in a sensible order.</li><li><b>sort: Symmetrical or Not?</b> — Sort each description. Is it symmetrical or not symmetrical?</li><li><b>reveal: The Gate of All Nations</b> — Tap each card to explore the gate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — 3D Gate Model with Guardian Figures:</b> Build a 3D model of a gate with at least two columns and one pair of guardian figures. Show symmetry, repeating columns and the three parts of a column.</li><li><b>Path B — Elevation Drawing:</b> Draw a front elevation of a symmetrical gate using a ruler. Label the parts of the columns and show repeating columns with even spacing.</li><li><b>Path C — Design a ‘Gate of Welcome’ for Our School:</b> As a Caravan, design a ‘Gate of Welcome’ for our school. It should blend pattern ideas from this term (and from our community) and say ‘you belong here’. Take guidance from your Māori Education lead about cultural protocols.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Neh 3:28–30; 1 Pet 2:5 — Nehemiah 3 lists men who repair the wall each in front of their own house: priests, goldsmiths, merchants, even daughters work beside their fathers. Peter writes that those who trust in Christ are like living stones, being built into a spiritual house.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Everyone built the part in front of their own house. What does this tell us about teamwork? What would have happened if some people said, ‘Someone else will do it’?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Peter calls believers ‘living stones’ being built together. What does it mean to be a stone in a wall? How is our class like a wall, with each stone different but needed?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose ONE team job you will commit to this week (for example, tidying, encouraging, finishing your part), and write it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: 3D Gate Model with Guardian Figures</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Build a 3D model of a gate with at least two columns and one pair of guardian figures. Show symmetry, repeating columns and the three parts of a column.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My gate is symmetrical (a centre line works)</li><li>I built at least 2 columns with base, shaft and capital</li><li>I made a matching pair of guardian figures</li><li>I used materials safely and neatly</li><li>I can explain what my gate ‘says’ to a visitor</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use pre-cut card pieces and a template for the column; add guardians and decoration.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan and build independently, with matching columns and guardians.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a roof beam or lintel and a decorated capital, and explain how your design helps people feel welcome or safe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Draw the plan. 2 Build the base. 3 Make the columns. 4 Add the guardians. 5 Check both sides match. <b>Say it:</b> My gate shows ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Elevation Drawing</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a front elevation of a symmetrical gate using a ruler. Label the parts of the columns and show repeating columns with even spacing.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew a centre line and the left and right sides match</li><li>I drew at least 4 columns with even spacing</li><li>I labelled base, shaft and capital on one column</li><li>I used a ruler for straight lines</li><li>I wrote a short artist’s statement</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use squared paper so lines and spacing are easier; start with the centre line already drawn.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with a ruler, four columns and labels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a scale (1 cm = 1 m) and calculate the real height of the gate; add decoration with positive and negative shapes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Centre line. 2 Ground line. 3 Mark column positions at equal spacing. 4 Draw columns with a ruler. 5 Add capitals and details. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My gate is symmetrical because… I repeated ___ columns to show…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Design a ‘Gate of Welcome’ for Our School</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>As a Caravan, design a ‘Gate of Welcome’ for our school. It should blend pattern ideas from this term (and from our community) and say ‘you belong here’. Take guidance from your Māori Education lead about cultural protocols.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Our design has a centre line and symmetry</li><li>Everyone in our Caravan has a job and a section</li><li>We included a welcome idea (words, shapes or symbols)</li><li>We did not copy sacred or tapu designs</li><li>We can explain what our gate says to a new student</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start from a pre-made gate outline; each student designs one panel with a welcome word or shape.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design the gate as a team with a division of roles, and write a short statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a ‘guide card’ for visitors that explains the gate’s symbols and how they reflect your school values.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Agree the message. 2 Choose shapes and patterns. 3 Share out the parts (one each). 4 Build or paint. 5 Join the parts. <b>Statement:</b> Our gate says ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Neh 3:28–30; 1 Pet 2:5 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the conversation open. The ‘living stones’ image is Christian; other students may see the idea of community differently. Invite all to think about what makes any team strong. Avoid suggesting that Persian art is worse; the aim is to ask what a gate or a building says, and who it welcomes.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare gates and entrances in Aotearoa, such as the pare (carved lintel over a wharenui door) and waharoa (gateways) that welcome visitors. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide this conversation. Do NOT imitate sacred or tapu designs; focus on the idea that an entrance can carry a message of welcome. Persian and Māori traditions are both living cultures.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Gate of All Nations honours a king’s power, and the figures are guardian creatures, not worshipped objects in our classroom; avoid describing them as ‘pagan’ or ‘exotic’. Design tasks must not copy sacred symbols from any religion or culture. Group work: watch for students who take over, and make sure every student has a real part.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers, set squares, pencils, fine-liners</li><li>Cardboard, card tubes, air-dry clay, glue guns (teacher only) or PVA</li><li>Photos of the Gate of All Nations at Persepolis and column capitals</li><li>Large brown paper or board for the class gate</li><li>Templates for column parts (base, shaft, capital)</li><li>Paint in lapis, turquoise, gold and stone colours</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students use a ruler and a centre line to keep the gate symmetrical?</li><li>Could they name base, shaft and capital?</li><li>Which Caravans shared jobs well, and which need support with team planning?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: The Wall of 52 Days":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: The Wall of 52 Days — Build the wall together. Win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 6: Rebuilding the Walls (458–432 BC) · Babylon → Jerusalem, Susa</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about Ezra, Nehemiah, Jerusalem, restoration and architecture, and to work as one team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the returns, the wall, restoration and gates.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan, and with the whole class, with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Wall Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the ideas to new situations.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Wall of 52 Days</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Dawn on the hill of Jerusalem. The wall stands half-built and every gap is a dare. On the opposite ridge the mockers are laughing again. ‘One caravan cannot finish this wall alone, Courier,’ says Shirin, handing you a trowel. ‘Write your Creed. How will you build with the others? With patience, with courage, with kindness to the builder who falls behind?’ A horn sounds from the gate-tower. The Showdown begins, and every right answer lays a brick on the wall.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Wall Creed on a strip and reads it aloud. Display them beside the Creeds from Weeks 1–5.</li><li>OPTIONAL CLASS WALL: draw a wall of 52 bricks on the whiteboard. After each question, the class adds bricks for the number of Caravans that answered correctly. If the class reaches 52 bricks by the end, award every Caravan a bonus; if the wall is incomplete, the ‘mockers’ appear (a light-hearted cheer of ‘Keep building!’), and the class tries again next week. Keep this cooperative: Caravans cheer one another on.</li><li>Before starting, remind students of this week’s recall areas (Nehemiah, Jerusalem, restoration, gates and columns) and that Weeks 1–5 questions will appear too.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q5, Q6, Q10) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Wall Creed: ONE sentence about how your team will work with the whole class so that no gap is left in the wall. Starters: We will build together by… / When someone is mocked or struggling, we will… / Our Caravan will always… / We will cheer on…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Neh 4:9; Neh 6:15; Isa 58:12; 1 Pet 2:5 — This week we met builders who prayed and posted a guard, a wall finished by many families in 52 days, repairers of broken walls and restorers of streets, and living stones being built together.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most important lesson about teamwork, prayer or repair that you noticed this week?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s ideas, a wall built by many hands, a habitat that can recover, or a gate that welcomes, made you think hardest?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Wall Creed together. Pray for each other and for any big job your class is facing.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Who was cupbearer to King Artaxerxes and led the rebuilding of Jerusalem’s wall? A) Haman · B) Daniel · <b>C) Nehemiah ✔</b> · D) Mordecai <em>Nehemiah served the king in Susa as cupbearer, then asked for permission and supplies to rebuild Jerusalem’s wall (Nehemiah 1–2).</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. According to Nehemiah 6:15, the wall was finished in how many days? A) 12 · <b>B) 52 ✔</b> · C) 365 · D) 152 <em>The book of Nehemiah says the wall was completed in 52 days, an astonishing team effort by many families working together.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>3. Which was the main reason Jerusalem was built on a hill? A) It looked fashionable · <b>B) Defence and a nearby spring ✔</b> · C) Snow for skiing · D) It had a harbour <em>The ridge and deep valleys gave natural defence, and the Gihon spring supplied water.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>4. The Gate of All Nations at Persepolis was guarded by… A) Dragons · B) Elephants · C) Kiwis · <b>D) Human-headed winged bulls ✔</b> <em>Huge stone bulls with wings and human heads guarded the gate, a sign of power and protection.</em> [VA-UC]</li><li>5. What does habitat restoration mean? A) Draining a swamp · B) Building a shopping mall · C) Hunting animals · <b>D) Helping a damaged habitat recover ✔</b> <em>Restoration means repairing a damaged place, by protecting it, removing the cause of harm, replanting and monitoring, so that living things can return. The Persian fallow deer, thought lost and found again in Iran in the 1950s, is one story of recovery.</em> [SC-LW-Eco] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>6. What does Nehemiah 4:9 teach? <b>A) Pray and take action ✔</b> · B) Only fight · C) Run away · D) Wait and do nothing <em>Nehemiah’s people prayed to God and also posted a guard day and night. They trusted God and did their part.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>7. Which king allowed exiles to go home and rebuild in about 538 BC (Week 2)? A) Cambyses · B) Xerxes · <b>C) Cyrus ✔</b> · D) Alexander <em>Cyrus the Great issued the policy that let exiled peoples return home, including Jewish families from Babylon (Ezra 1).</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>8. Which gold coin was introduced by Darius to help run the empire (Week 3)? A) Drachma · <b>B) Daric ✔</b> · C) Denarius · D) Dollar <em>The daric was the Persian gold coin with a kneeling archer. The denarius was Roman and the drachma was Greek.</em> [SS-EW]</li><li>9. Esther was queen to Xerxes at the palace in Susa (Week 5). Before the Persians, Susa was a city of which ancient people (Week 1)? A) Mongols · <b>B) Elamites ✔</b> · C) Romans · D) Vikings <em>Susa was an Elamite city, one of the world’s earliest, long before it became a royal palace for the Persian kings. Both Esther and Nehemiah’s stories are set there.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Why did Persian kings often help rebuild local temples? A) They were scared of the Greeks · B) They were bored · C) It was an accident · <b>D) Policy of respecting local peoples (and Christians also see God moving their hearts) ✔</b> <em>Persian kings often let local peoples keep their customs and worship, which helped keep provinces peaceful and loyal. Believers also read the Bible as saying that God moved the kings’ hearts.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. In 480 BC the Greeks defeated Xerxes’ fleet in the narrow waters of which battle (Week 4)? A) Troy · B) Gaugamela · <b>C) Salamis ✔</b> · D) Marathon <em>The Battle of Salamis was a naval battle in narrow waters. Marathon (490 BC) was a land battle under Darius. Historians note that most of our detail comes from Greek writers.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — In a food web, removing many links often leads to… <b>A) ecosystem collapse ✔</b> · B) bluer skies · C) bigger animals · D) nothing at all <em>A food web works by links. When many links are lost, the community may collapse, which is why restoration tries to rebuild links.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (for example, rebuilding a wall and restoring a habitat, both about repair) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Neh 4:9; Neh 6:15; Isa 58:12; 1 Pet 2:5 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful; celebrate teamwork and effort, not only winning. If you use the class wall, keep the focus on ‘we’ finishing it together.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week: rebuilding after loss; hill-top pā and local settlement choices; restoration of wetlands, streams and forests; gates and entrances that welcome.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown: keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. If using the class wall, make sure no Caravan is blamed for gaps.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching on Monday (for example, Ezra vs Nehemiah, or what restoration means)?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 7: The Marketplace of the World":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Ordinary Lives in an Extraordinary Empire":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ordinary Lives in an Extraordinary Empire — Kings left statues. Who left the shopping lists?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 7: The Marketplace of the World (5th century BC) · Persepolis region, bazaars, Royal Road stations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-R — English: Reading. Students read informational and narrative texts and make meaning from them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe daily life for ordinary Persians using real evidence.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe three jobs or roles in the Persian Empire.</li><li>I can explain what the Persepolis Fortification Tablets show.</li><li>I can compare life then and now.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">An evidence-based description of daily life (fact vs inference), the Path product (photographed) and observation notes from the Council Fire talk.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names a job or a fact about daily life with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes three roles and what the tablets show, using evidence.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains what the tablets show AND what they leave out, separating evidence from inference.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates the tablets as a source, compares daily life then and now, and links fairness to real examples.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>ration</b> — A fixed amount of food or drink given to a worker or a family.</li><li><b>tablet</b> — A flat piece of clay with writing pressed into it.</li><li><b>Elamite</b> — The language of the Elamites. Most of the Persepolis tablets are written in it.</li><li><b>Aramaic</b> — A language that spread across the empire and was used by officials and traders for everyday business.</li><li><b>scribe</b> — A trained writer who kept records, wrote letters and counted goods.</li><li><b>artisan</b> — A skilled maker, such as a potter, weaver, stonecutter or metalworker.</li><li><b>evidence</b> — Facts or objects we can use to support an idea about the past.</li><li><b>inference</b> — A careful guess made by joining clues together.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Ration Chits</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The old storehouse door creaks. Inside, a scribe sits at a low table, pressing a reed into wet clay: tap, tap, tap. ‘Next!’ she calls. ‘Name, job, ration.’ Shirin hands you a small clay-coloured card. ‘This is your tablet, Courier. You are a worker now. Line up, hold out your hands, and let us see whether the day’s rations are fair.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: print or write ration tablet cards. Each shows a job (mason, weaver, farm helper, scribe, baker, camel driver, mother with a newborn, boy learning to ride) and a ration (3 to 6 scoops of barley or counters). Give the harder jobs and the new mothers a larger ration. Keep one scribe with a ledger.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Stage 6. What did Nehemiah’s people do together in 52 days? Take two answers on mini-whiteboards.</li><li>Ration line (5 min): students line up with their tablet card. The scribe reads each card aloud and serves the ration. Do not tell students the reason behind the amounts yet.</li><li>Fairness talk (3 min): turn and talk. Who got more? Who got less? Is that fair? Why might a builder get more than a helper, or a new mother more than someone else? Take three shares without correcting.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria. Students read them chorally.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Stage 6: Nehemiah and the wall of Jerusalem (52 days, the whole community working together); the exiles coming home.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>We know the names of kings, but most people in the empire were farmers, builders, mothers, bakers and scribes. Their lives are written in clay.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tablets (5 min): show the tablet photos. Ask: ‘What would a historian learn from a pile of ration lists?’ Reveal the ration pattern: jobs, skills, mothers. Link back to the ration line: ‘Now you know why some rations were bigger.’</li><li>Languages (3 min): use the world map. Mark places where Persian, Aramaic, Greek, Egyptian and Elamite were used. Ask: ‘What would make trade easier? What would make it harder?’ Aramaic is still spoken in a few places today.</li><li>Sources and gaps (4 min): use the ‘shows / leaves out’ source cards. Students sort: ‘The tablets show…’ and ‘The tablets leave out…’. Practise the sentence ‘The tablets suggest… but we do not know…’.</li><li>Then and now (3 min): compare a Persian worker with a worker today. Invite students to think of jobs in their own families. Never put a child on the spot about a parent’s job or wages.</li><li><b>Shopping lists in clay:</b> In 1933 archaeologists digging at <b>Persepolis</b> found thousands of clay tablets and fragments inside the fortification wall. They are called the <b>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</b> and date from about <b>509 to 493 BC</b>, in the reign of Darius I. Most are written in <b>Elamite</b> (remember Week 1?). They are mostly records of <b>rations</b>: barley, flour, fruit, beer or wine, and sheep paid to workers. They are not stories about kings. They are the paperwork of ordinary life.</li><li><b>Who is on the tablets?:</b> The tablets name <b>men, women and children</b>. There were builders, stonecutters, weavers, bakers, herders, carriers, scribes and officials. Skilled workers often received more than helpers. Some women, including new mothers, are recorded with extra rations. A few women, like one called <b>Irdabama</b>, seem to have managed large estates and many workers. Scholars still debate details, such as how free some of the workers were, so we say <i>‘the tablets suggest…’</i>.</li><li><b>A land of many tongues:</b> The empire held many languages. <b>Aramaic</b> became a common language for letters, trade and official business, so a merchant from one end of the empire could write to someone at the other. Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian were used for royal inscriptions. Think of a market where you might hear Persian, Aramaic, Greek, Egyptian and more, all in one morning.</li><li><b>How did children grow up?:</b> The Greek writer <b>Herodotus</b> says that Persian boys, at least in wealthy families, were taught to <b>ride, to shoot the bow and to tell the truth</b>. That is a Greek writer describing another people, so treat it with care. We know much less about what girls were taught, and almost nothing about the children of poor families. Good historians say: <i>‘This is what one source says, and here is what is missing.’</i></li><li><b>What the tablets can’t tell us:</b> Clay tablets record what officials wanted counted. They do not record laughter, songs, grief or dreams. They also do not tell us what workers felt about their jobs. When you write a diary or a museum caption today, you will use <b>evidence</b> for what is known and <b>inference</b> for what is a careful guess. Always say which is which.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Tablet Words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: What Do the Tablets Show?</b> — Sort each statement. Is it something the tablets suggest, or something they leave out?</li><li><b>order: From Wet Clay to Museum</b> — Put these steps in order, from the scribe’s desk to today’s museum.</li><li><b>reveal: Fact or Careful Guess?</b> — Tap each card to see whether it is evidence or an inference.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — A Day in the Life: Illustrated Diary:</b> Choose a role from the ration tablets (mason, weaver, baker, scribe, camel driver, farmer, mother, apprentice) and write a one-day diary with three illustrations. Use evidence from the tablets, and mark your inferences with ‘perhaps’ or ‘I imagine’.</li><li><b>Path B — The Ration Ledger:</b> Be the scribe. Use the practice ledger (invented numbers that follow the pattern of the tablets) to add, compare and share rations, then write a short explanation of whether the amounts are fair.</li><li><b>Path C — Museum Display: Five Everyday Items:</b> Create a small museum display of five everyday items from Persian life (a clay tablet, a pottery cup, a spindle, a bread oven, a seal). Write a caption for each: what it is, what it was used for, what it tells us, and one thing we are not sure about.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Jer 29:4–7; Col 3:23; Prov 31:10–31 — In Jeremiah, God tells the exiles in Babylon to build houses, plant gardens, and seek the good of the city where they live. In Colossians, Paul says to work at whatever you do with all your heart, as if working for the Lord. In Proverbs 31, a capable woman is praised for her work: weaving, trading, caring for her household and helping the poor.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What gives ordinary work dignity? Is a baker or a weaver less important to God than a king?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> The exiles were told to build houses and plant gardens in a foreign land. What does that say about daily faithfulness? Can doing ordinary things well be a way of honouring God?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose ONE ordinary task you will do wholeheartedly this week (tidying, homework, helping at home). Write it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: A Day in the Life: Illustrated Diary</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose a role from the ration tablets (mason, weaver, baker, scribe, camel driver, farmer, mother, apprentice) and write a one-day diary with three illustrations. Use evidence from the tablets, and mark your inferences with ‘perhaps’ or ‘I imagine’.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I chose a role from the evidence</li><li>I wrote about morning, midday and evening</li><li>I included at least two facts from the tablets</li><li>I used ‘perhaps’ or ‘I imagine’ for guesses</li><li>I drew three labelled illustrations</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the sentence frames and a pre-drawn diary template. Choose from three roles with a picture bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a three-part diary entry with two evidence facts and three drawings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two diary entries from different roles (such as a skilled mason and a helper) and compare how they might feel about the same ration.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Morning:</b> I wake before dawn. My job is… <b>Midday:</b> At work, I… I receive a ration of… <b>Evening:</b> I go home to… I am proud of… <b>Fact or guess?</b> I know ___ because ___. I imagine ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: The Ration Ledger</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Be the scribe. Use the practice ledger (invented numbers that follow the pattern of the tablets) to add, compare and share rations, then write a short explanation of whether the amounts are fair.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I added the rations for each group correctly</li><li>I found the difference between two jobs</li><li>I shared a total fairly among workers</li><li>I wrote an explanation using a fact from the tablets</li><li>I showed my working</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use counters to model each group, then record the total. Work with a partner on the explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete the ledger with written working, then write a three-sentence explanation of fairness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Invent a new ledger for 25 workers, add a rule for a bonus, and explain the effect on the total. Say what a scribe could not know from the numbers alone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Practice ledger (invented numbers):</b> 6 helpers × 20 units · 4 skilled workers × 30 units · 3 new mothers × 40 units. <b>Questions:</b> What is the total? How many more units does a skilled worker get than a helper? <b>Explain:</b> I think this is fair/unfair because…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Museum Display: Five Everyday Items</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a small museum display of five everyday items from Persian life (a clay tablet, a pottery cup, a spindle, a bread oven, a seal). Write a caption for each: what it is, what it was used for, what it tells us, and one thing we are not sure about.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Five items drawn or made</li><li>Each caption says what it is and how it was used</li><li>Each caption says what it tells us</li><li>At least one caption names something uncertain</li><li>Neat labels and clear order</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose from a bank of five pictures and complete the caption frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw or build five items and write full captions using the four-part frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘Curator’s note’ explaining which item is the best evidence for ordinary life and why, and what a museum visitor might wrongly assume.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Caption frame:</b> This is a ___. People used it to ___. It tells us that ___. We are not sure about ___. <b>Display tip:</b> Put the most important item first and give the display a title.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Jer 29:4–7; Col 3:23; Prov 31:10–31 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the tone respectful of every kind of job and every kind of family. Avoid ranking jobs. Some students’ families may work long or difficult hours, so honour all work. Be honest that scholars debate how free some Persepolis workers were, and that a command to ‘work with all your heart’ never excuses unfair treatment.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare roles and fairness in Aotearoa today: what jobs do people in our community do, and how do we keep pay and conditions fair? Keep it age-appropriate and never ask children about their family’s wages. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi to share how work and sharing were organised in your rohe, without generalising across iwi.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Daily life included hard labour and unequal roles. Scholars debate how free the workers were. Present this honestly and without sensationalism. Some students may have family members in tough jobs, so honour all work. Treat Persian and Iranian history with pride and respect.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ration ‘tablet’ cards (jobs and rations)</li><li>Barley, rice or counters</li><li>Photos of the Persepolis Fortification Tablets (app or printed)</li><li>Source cards: what the tablets show / what they leave out</li><li>Path B practice ledger (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Museum caption template for Path C</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students separate evidence from inference?</li><li>Which students could say what the tablets leave out?</li><li>What will I change tomorrow when we talk about trade and fairness?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Bazaars, Caravans & Trade Routes: How Trade Shapes Settlements":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bazaars, Caravans &amp; Trade Routes: How Trade Shapes Settlements — Who needs what? And who will carry it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Trade &amp; Settlements</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 7: The Marketplace of the World (5th century BC) · Persepolis region, bazaars, Royal Road stations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>MA-S — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Statistics. Students collect, organise, display and interpret data.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how trade shapes the growth of towns and cities.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can show trade flows on a map using arrows.</li><li>I can explain specialisation.</li><li>I can describe a trade hub and why it grew.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Specialisation vocabulary, the accuracy of the flow map (arrows from source to destination), the reasoning in Path B or C, and fairness talk.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names a trade good or route with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Shows trade with arrows correctly and defines specialisation.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why trade hubs grow and how specialisation leads to trade, using evidence.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates how trade shapes settlements, including benefits and trade-offs, and applies the idea to a new place.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>trade</b> — Exchanging goods or services, by swapping or by using money.</li><li><b>specialisation</b> — When people or places focus on what they make or do well.</li><li><b>caravan</b> — A group of traders and animals travelling together, often across deserts.</li><li><b>bazaar</b> — A market, often with many stalls, where people buy and sell.</li><li><b>trade hub</b> — A place where trade routes meet and many goods change hands.</li><li><b>caravanserai</b> — A roadside inn with a courtyard for travellers and animals. Large networks of these became common in later centuries.</li><li><b>export / import</b> — Goods sent out of a place / brought into a place.</li><li><b>barter</b> — Trading goods directly, without using money.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Bazaar Simulation</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Noise! Shouting! The bazaar smells of saffron, wool and warm bread. A seller waves a blue stone at you. ‘Lapis! The colour of the night sky! Who will swap me grain for it?’ Shirin whispers, ‘Remember yesterday’s tablets, Courier. People here have jobs, and what they make, others need. Now it is your turn to trade.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: give each Caravan 3 or 4 goods cards (lapis, turquoise, saffron, wool, grain, pottery, dates, cedar), and a secret ‘need card’ that lists two goods they must get. Make sure no team can finish alone.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday. What do the Fortification Tablets show? What language was common across the empire? Why is a ledger useful? (Mini-whiteboards.)</li><li>Round 1 (4 min): teams visit other tables to swap, one trade at a time. Rule: both sides must agree and say ‘fair’ or ‘no deal’. Record each trade on a tally sheet.</li><li>Round 2 (3 min): announce a change: a rockslide has blocked the mountain road, so lapis cannot move. How does that change the trading?</li><li>Debrief (3 min): Who finished their need card? Why did some teams struggle? What did you notice about specialisation?</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: the Persepolis Fortification Tablets (rations, jobs, women and children), and Aramaic as a common language.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Persian Empire was a gigantic marketplace. Wool, lapis, spices and grain crossed deserts and mountains, and towns grew where the roads met.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Goods and origins (5 min): put goods cards on the big map. Students place each card on its place of origin (use the hints: lapis far east, cedar west, wool and grain nearby). Draw arrows to the nearest hub.</li><li>Specialisation (4 min): ask three students to ‘do everything’ (make a pot, weave a cloth, grow grain) and compare with three who ‘specialise’. Debrief: ‘What is better? What is risky?’</li><li>Trade hubs (3 min): show the app’s map of Susa, Persepolis, Ecbatana and Babylon. Ask: ‘What do they share (water, roads, safe ground)?’</li><li>Caravanserai (2 min): show a later-period photo clearly labelled as such. Ask: ‘What would a traveller need at the end of a day on the road?’</li><li>Fair weights (2 min): demonstrate a balance with one ‘heavy’ weight. Ask: ‘Who would be cheated?’</li><li><b>Goods from every corner:</b> A foundation text from Darius’s palace at <b>Susa</b> lists materials brought from across the empire: <b>cedar</b> from Lebanon, <b>gold</b> from Sardis and Bactria, <b>lapis lazuli</b> and carnelian from Central Asia, <b>turquoise</b> from the east, <b>ivory</b> from lands to the south and east, and <b>silver</b> and ebony from Egypt. Wool, grain, dates, pottery and <b>saffron</b> moved along the roads too. Scholars agree on the general picture even where they debate details.</li><li><b>Specialisation: do what you do best:</b> <b>Specialisation</b> means a person or place focuses on what they do well. A potter makes pots, a farmer grows grain, a weaver spins and weaves. Then they <b>trade</b> to get what they do not make. Specialisation makes more and better goods, but it also means nobody can manage alone, so people must trade.</li><li><b>Trade hubs:</b> A <b>trade hub</b> grows where routes meet, often near <b>water</b> (a river, spring or qanat), <b>safe ground</b> and good roads. Susa, Persepolis, Ecbatana and Babylon were all big centres where goods and people met. Remember Week 3: stations grew into villages, towns and capitals. Trade fed that growth.</li><li><b>Caravans and roadside inns:</b> Traders travelled in <b>caravans</b>: many people and animals together, for safety and help. The Royal Road had official stations for the king’s messengers. Large networks of <b>caravanserais</b> (roadside inns with courtyards) became common in later centuries across Persia and beyond. We use the word to help imagine the Persian road, but we say plainly: <i>the big inns we picture are later than this stage.</i></li><li><b>Money, weights and honest scales:</b> Under Darius the gold <b>daric</b> and silver coins were used, but many people still <b>bartered</b> or were paid in grain and other goods. Honest <b>weights and measures</b> mattered, because a cheat could use a heavy weight to buy and a light one to sell. The Bible talks about this in Proverbs and Micah.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Trade Words</b> — Match each trade word to its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Specialise or Do-It-All?</b> — Sort each statement. Does it describe specialisation, or doing everything yourself?</li><li><b>order: How a Trade Hub Grows</b> — Put these steps in order to show how a trade hub might grow.</li><li><b>reveal: Where Did It Come From?</b> — Tap each card to see where the good came from and where it went.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Trade Route Map:</b> Draw a flow map of the empire’s trade. Use arrows to show where five goods came from and where they went, with a title, a legend and a scale or direction marker.</li><li><b>Path B — Bazaar Data:</b> Run three trade rounds with a partner team and record each trade. Make a bar graph of the goods traded and explain what the data shows about supply and demand.</li><li><b>Path C — Design a Caravan Stop:</b> Plan a stop for caravans at a crossroads. Draw a plan with water, stables, market, rest area and safety, and explain why you chose the location.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Prov 11:1; Prov 16:11; Mic 6:11 — Proverbs says that dishonest scales are hateful to the Lord, but an accurate weight pleases Him, and that honest balances and scales belong to God. Micah asks how God could excuse using dishonest scales and a bag of false weights.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What makes trade fair? Why does God care about scales and weights?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Honest scales or cheating: which matters in your daily life, even when you are just sharing or swapping at school? Who is harmed when someone cheats, and who is helped when everyone trades honestly?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write ONE fairness promise for swapping and sharing (for example, ‘I will give honest weight when I swap, and I will say if I have made a mistake’).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Trade Route Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a flow map of the empire’s trade. Use arrows to show where five goods came from and where they went, with a title, a legend and a scale or direction marker.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title and legend</li><li>Five goods shown with arrows</li><li>Arrows start at the source and point to the destination</li><li>Arrow thickness shows more or less trade (optional)</li><li>Two trade hubs labelled</li><li>I can explain why one hub grew</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map with the hubs marked. Draw arrows for three goods using the label bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw all five arrows with a legend and explain one hub.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add arrows for return trade (what flowed back) and explain how specialisation links two places.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Goods:</b> lapis · turquoise · saffron · wool · grain · cedar · pottery. <b>Hubs:</b> Susa · Persepolis · Ecbatana · Babylon. <b>Say it:</b> ___ travelled from ___ to ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Bazaar Data</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Run three trade rounds with a partner team and record each trade. Make a bar graph of the goods traded and explain what the data shows about supply and demand.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tally chart completed for three rounds</li><li>Bar graph with title, axes and labels</li><li>I found the most and least traded goods</li><li>I wrote a sentence explaining why</li><li>I suggested one way to make trading fairer</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn graph frame and tally chart. Work in a pair to explain.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw the bar graph independently and write a three-sentence explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Calculate the mean number of trades per round, and predict what a blocked road (no lapis) would do to the data.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Table:</b> Round | Good | Number of trades. <b>Graph:</b> Put goods on the bottom axis and number of trades up the side. <b>Explain:</b> The most traded good was ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Design a Caravan Stop</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan a stop for caravans at a crossroads. Draw a plan with water, stables, market, rest area and safety, and explain why you chose the location.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I chose a location and drew a plan</li><li>I included water, stables, a market and a rest area</li><li>I added a safety feature (a wall, a gate, a lookout)</li><li>I wrote three reasons for my location</li><li>I named one risk and how to manage it</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn courtyard outline and a services bank. Choose the site from three cards.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design the plan and give three reasons and one risk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw a second option on a different site and compare them, then recommend one with evidence. Link your plan to the four settlement questions (water, soil, defence, trade).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Services:</b> water · stables · market · rest rooms · storage · gate. <b>Reasons:</b> I chose this site because (1) …, (2) …, (3) …. <b>Risk:</b> One risk is …, but we could …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Prov 11:1; Prov 16:11; Mic 6:11 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the focus on honest dealing and fairness, not on shaming sellers or any group of traders. Persian trade was valued across cultures. Link to children’s own swaps (cards, snacks, stickers) and invite honest stories of when someone was treated fairly.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare pounamu trade routes in Aotearoa. Pounamu travelled widely from Te Wai Pounamu, and routes and stories belong to iwi, so invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi (in Canterbury, the mana whenua are Ngāi Tahu) to tell what they choose to share. Then look at New Zealand’s ports and exports today (dairy, meat, wine, kiwifruit, timber) and what we import.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Do not stereotype any people as ‘cheating traders’. Persian and Iranian merchants are part of a proud history. Avoid exotic or romanticised language about bazaars. Pounamu is taonga: do not treat Māori trade as a game or a model; ask local guidance.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Trade goods cards (lapis, turquoise, saffron, wool, grain, pottery, dates, cedar)</li><li>Large outline map of the empire and arrows (string or sticky arrows)</li><li>Sand-timer or class clock for trading rounds</li><li>Graph paper and tally sheets for Path B</li><li>Caravan-stop planning sheet for Path C</li><li>Trade-flow cards (app or printed)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain specialisation in their own words?</li><li>Did the flow-map arrows start at the source and point to the destination?</li><li>Which groups understood why trade hubs grow near water and roads?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Working Animals: Camels, Horses & Mules":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Working Animals: Camels, Horses &amp; Mules — The empire ran on four legs. How are they built for the job?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Working Animals</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 7: The Marketplace of the World (5th century BC) · Persepolis region, bazaars, Royal Road stations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-LW-LP — Science: Living World — Life Processes. Students understand that all living things have requirements to stay alive.</li><li>SC-NoS-I — Science: Nature of Science — Investigating. Students ask questions, find evidence, carry out fair tests and explain their findings.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how working animals suit their roles and how people should care for them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain at least three camel adaptations.</li><li>I can run a fair test on foot shape in sand.</li><li>I can describe the needs of a working animal and good animal welfare.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fair-test accuracy (what changed, what stayed the same), correct use of vocabulary (domesticated, adaptation, welfare), and clarity of diagrams and Venn charts.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names a camel feature or an animal need with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes three camel adaptations and runs a test with some help.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how adaptations suit the job and runs a fair test, controlling variables and drawing a conclusion.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates evidence, suggests improvements to the test and links animal welfare to responsibility.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>domesticated</b> — Bred over many generations to live with and work for people.</li><li><b>wild</b> — Living naturally without being tamed or bred by people.</li><li><b>dromedary</b> — A camel with ONE hump.</li><li><b>Bactrian camel</b> — A camel with TWO humps, from the colder lands of Central Asia.</li><li><b>onager</b> — A wild ass of the Persian plateau. It has never been successfully domesticated.</li><li><b>mule</b> — The offspring of a donkey and a horse. Strong and sure-footed.</li><li><b>fair test</b> — An experiment where only ONE thing changes and everything else stays the same.</li><li><b>welfare</b> — The health, comfort and wellbeing of an animal.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Camel Mystery Boxes</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The caravan yard is full of grumbling camels. Gandom the camel looks at you with enormous eyes. ‘She is the only one who has never lost a bet, Courier,’ says Shirin. She pushes four mystery boxes towards you. ‘Feel inside, and tell me which part of Gandom each belongs to: her foot, her eyelash, her fur, or her hump. And no peeking!’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: fill four boxes: (1) a wide, soft foam or rubber foot pad, (2) a long thick feather or brush (eyelash), (3) a tuft of fleece (fur), (4) a pouch of soft wax or lard in a bag (hump fat). Label boxes 1–4. Keep the answers hidden.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday. What is specialisation? Name two trade goods and where they came from. What is a trade hub?</li><li>Mystery boxes (5 min): in turn, teams feel each box without looking and write what they think it is and which part of the camel it matches. Do not reveal answers.</li><li>Share (3 min): reveal each box, linking to the adaptation (wide pad, thick lashes, insulating fur, fat store). Ask: ‘Why would a camel need this?’ Gandom ‘guesses’ and is wrong for two of them.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: specialisation; trade goods and their sources; trade hubs near water and routes.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Camels, horses, mules and donkeys carried Persian messages, loads and armies. Learn how they are built for the work, and what they need from us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Camel features (5 min): show the photos. Ask: ‘Which feature solves which problem? Sand, sun, thirst, food shortages?’ Match to the mystery boxes. Remind: the hump stores fat, not water.</li><li>Types of animal (4 min): sort photos into a three-column table: camels, horses, mules/donkeys. Ask: ‘Which job suits which animal?’ Use the word ‘suited’ rather than ‘best’.</li><li>Wild vs domesticated (3 min): Venn diagram on the board: onager and horse. Where do they overlap? What did people change by breeding?</li><li>Welfare (3 min): read the welfare checklist (food, water, rest, shelter, safe handling, vet care). Link to local farm animals and working dogs.</li><li>Fair-test set-up (2 min): show the sand tray, wide and narrow feet, equal weights. Ask: ‘What will we change? What must stay the same?’</li><li><b>Built for the desert: the camel:</b> <b>Wide, padded feet</b> spread the weight so the camel does not sink in sand. <b>Double rows of long eyelashes</b> and <b>nostrils that can close</b> keep out blown sand. <b>Thick fur</b> on the back shades it from the sun. The <b>hump stores fat</b> (not water!), which the camel can use as energy when food is scarce. Camels can also go a long time without drinking, then drink a very large amount at once.</li><li><b>Two kinds of camel:</b> <b>Dromedary</b> camels have ONE hump and thrive in hot deserts. <b>Bactrian</b> camels have TWO humps, thick winter coats and are suited to the cold, dry lands of Central Asia. Both were used by Persian travellers and armies, and both are part of the Persian story. You can collect them as Animal Cards, because you already met them in earlier stages.</li><li><b>Horses, mules and donkeys:</b> <b>Horses</b> were prized for speed. The Royal Road riders used them in relays, and Persian kings valued horses from places like Media. <b>Donkeys</b> carry loads steadily. A <b>mule</b> is the offspring of a male donkey and a female horse: strong, sure-footed and good on rocky tracks. Mules usually cannot have babies of their own.</li><li><b>Wild vs domesticated: onager and horse:</b> A <b>domesticated</b> animal has been bred over many generations to live with people. A <b>wild</b> animal has not. The <b>Persian onager</b> is a wild ass of the dry plains of Iran. Kings hunted it, and people have not been able to tame it the way they tamed horses and donkeys. Today the onager is <b>endangered</b>, so conservationists protect it.</li><li><b>What do working animals need?:</b> Every animal needs <b>food, water, rest, shelter, safe handling and care when sick</b>. A good owner does not overload an animal, and gives it rest. Today, animal welfare laws and vets help protect working animals. The Bible says a righteous person cares about the needs of their animals.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Camel Features</b> — Match each camel feature to its job.</li><li><b>sort: Wild or Domesticated?</b> — Sort each animal or description.</li><li><b>order: A Fair Sand Test</b> — Put the steps of the fair test in the correct order.</li><li><b>reveal: Camel Myths and Facts</b> — Tap each card to check whether it is true.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Foot-in-Sand Fair Test:</b> Test how foot shape affects sinking. Press a wide ‘foot’ and a narrow ‘foot’ with the SAME weight into the SAME sand, measure how deep each sinks, and explain what this tells us about camel feet.</li><li><b>Path B — Design a Transport Animal:</b> Imagine a new habitat (icy mountains, wetlands or hot dunes). Design a working transport animal for it, with five labelled adaptations and an explanation of how each helps.</li><li><b>Path C — Wild vs Domestic: Onager and Horse:</b> Compare the onager and the horse using a Venn diagram, then answer three questions: Why was the onager never domesticated? How did people change horses? What do we owe to wild animals?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 24:10–20; Prov 12:10 — In Genesis 24, a young woman named Rebekah offers water to a stranger and, without being asked, draws water for all ten of his camels as well. In Proverbs 12:10 we read that a righteous person cares about the needs of their animals.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it mean to care for animals? Why would God’s Word mention camels and donkeys?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Rebekah worked hard to water thirsty camels. How does her care link to farm animals and working animals today? What does good animal welfare look like for the animals you know?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose an animal-care action you will do this week (feeding a pet, helping with farm chores, putting a bird feeder out, or learning the welfare needs of a pet).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Foot-in-Sand Fair Test</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Test how foot shape affects sinking. Press a wide ‘foot’ and a narrow ‘foot’ with the SAME weight into the SAME sand, measure how deep each sinks, and explain what this tells us about camel feet.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote a question and a prediction</li><li>I changed only ONE thing (foot width)</li><li>I kept weight, sand and pressing method the same</li><li>I measured each depth at least three times</li><li>I recorded results in a table and found the average</li><li>I explained what this means for camels</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the planning frame and a pre-made table. Work with a partner to measure; the teacher checks the fair-test list.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Plan and run the test, record three trials each, and write a conclusion using the frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Test a third foot shape (such as a hoof-shaped foot) and explain which animal would sink most. Suggest how to make your measurements more accurate.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> Does a wider foot sink less in sand? <b>Change:</b> foot width. <b>Keep the same:</b> weight, sand depth, how you press. <b>Measure:</b> depth in mm (skewer or ruler). <b>Explain:</b> The wide foot sank ___ mm and the narrow foot sank ___ mm, so a camel’s wide pad helps it by ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Design a Transport Animal</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Imagine a new habitat (icy mountains, wetlands or hot dunes). Design a working transport animal for it, with five labelled adaptations and an explanation of how each helps.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I named the habitat and the job of my animal</li><li>I drew my animal with five labelled adaptations</li><li>Each adaptation says HOW it helps</li><li>I thought about food, water and rest</li><li>I compared my design to a real animal</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose a habitat from three cards, use the adaptation bank, and complete the ‘has… which helps…’ frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label five adaptations with explanations and a comparison.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a welfare plan (feeding, rest, shelter) and explain what could go wrong if the habitat changed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Habitat:</b> … <b>Job:</b> carrying … <b>Adaptations:</b> The ___ has ___ which helps it ___. <b>Compare:</b> My animal is like a ___ because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Wild vs Domestic: Onager and Horse</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare the onager and the horse using a Venn diagram, then answer three questions: Why was the onager never domesticated? How did people change horses? What do we owe to wild animals?</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Venn diagram with at least 3 facts in each part</li><li>Three questions answered in full sentences</li><li>I used the words wild and domesticated correctly</li><li>I included one fact about welfare or conservation</li><li>I checked my facts with a second source</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the fact bank to fill the Venn diagram and answer questions with sentence starters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete the Venn diagram independently and answer all three questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Research a second wild/domestic pair (such as wolf and dog, or red junglefowl and chicken) and compare how domestication changed them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Venn:</b> Onager only | Both | Horse only. <b>Questions:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Frame:</b> Wild animals are … Domesticated animals are … because people …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 24:10–20; Prov 12:10 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Gently allow for different family experiences (pets, farms, no animals, cultural views of animals). Avoid graphic discussions about animal suffering. Link care of animals to respect for creation without implying that using animals for work is wrong.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> New Zealand has working animals too: farm and working dogs (heading and huntaway dogs on high-country stations), horses, and the care that farmers give them. Invite a local farmer or a vet to talk about animal welfare and the rules we use today. Link to kaitiakitanga (guardianship) with local guidance.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Some students may have cultural or family views about particular animals. Keep the tone respectful and practical. Do not use the camel as a joke about any people. Present animal care and conservation matter-of-factly (the onager is endangered, which is a reason to care).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Camel mystery boxes (foot pad, eyelash, fur tuft, a pouch of ‘fat’ such as soft wax)</li><li>Sand tray, rulers, wide and narrow ‘feet’ (cardboard squares), equal weights</li><li>Animal welfare checklist (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Photos of dromedary, Bactrian, horse, donkey, mule and onager</li><li>Venn diagram sheet for Path C</li><li>Safe handling guidance for the sand tray (no flicking sand, wash hands)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students control the variables in their fair test?</li><li>Could they say WHY the hump stores fat and not water?</li><li>What do I need to revisit about domesticated vs wild?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Woven Worlds: Carpets & Textile Pattern":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Woven Worlds: Carpets &amp; Textile Pattern — Over, under, over, under: a whole world on a loom.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Persian Traditions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 7: The Marketplace of the World (5th century BC) · Persepolis region, bazaars, Royal Road stations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to design and make a symmetrical woven pattern.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can plan a design on squared paper.</li><li>I can weave over and under on a loom.</li><li>I can describe patterns used in Persian textiles.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Symmetry accuracy on the design, quality of the weaving or collage (over-under, edges, repeat), and the artist’s statement.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a simple woven or patterned piece with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Creates a symmetrical pattern, weaves over and under, and names warp and weft.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls symmetry on two axes, uses a limited palette, and explains how pattern is used in Persian textiles.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines pattern and craft with intent, compares with other textile traditions, and explains how design communicates identity.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>warp</b> — The threads that run lengthways on a loom, held tight.</li><li><b>weft</b> — The threads woven over and under the warp, from side to side.</li><li><b>loom</b> — A frame that holds the warp threads while you weave.</li><li><b>medallion</b> — A large central pattern, often a star or flower shape.</li><li><b>border</b> — A band of repeated pattern around the edge of a design.</li><li><b>motif</b> — A single design unit that is repeated.</li><li><b>symmetry</b> — A balanced pattern. Mirror symmetry has matching halves.</li><li><b>dye</b> — A colouring made from plants, insects or minerals used to colour yarn.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Why Do Patterns Repeat?</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A weaver sits in the shade of the bazaar, her fingers flying: over, under, over, under. A rich pattern grows on her loom, as if by magic. ‘Patterns repeat so that people remember,’ she tells you. ‘And so that mistakes hide. And so the cloth is beautiful.’ Shirin grins. ‘Look closely, Courier. What is the same on both sides? What is the same all the way round?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: prepare pictures of patterned textiles and carpets, clearly labelled with their period. Be honest that many famous medallion carpets are from later centuries.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday. What does a camel’s hump store? What is a fair test? What is the difference between wild and domesticated?</li><li>Pattern spotting (5 min): show the pictures. Ask: ‘What repeats? What is symmetrical? What are the colours?’ Students talk in pairs, then share two patterns.</li><li>Why repeat? (3 min): take three ideas (beauty, memory, skill, hiding mistakes, identity). Link to Week 1 and the Persepolis rosettes.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: the camel’s hump stores fat; a fair test changes only one thing; wild vs domesticated animals.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Weaving is one of the oldest crafts. Learn how a loom works, how symmetry builds a pattern, and why careful hands matter.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Loom basics (5 min): demonstrate over-under weaving on a card loom. Show how to keep edges straight and weft loose. Show how to change colour.</li><li>Symmetry (3 min): fold a squared paper medallion on two axes. Colour one quarter and ask students to predict the other three.</li><li>Pazyryk carpet (2 min): show a photo clearly labelled. Say: ‘Scholars debate where it was made. What can it tell us?’</li><li>Dyes (2 min): show examples of natural dyes (madder, indigo, weld) if available, or colour cards.</li><li>Model the first step of each Path (3 min). Students choose A, B or C.</li><li><b>Warp and weft:</b> Weaving has two sets of threads. The <b>warp</b> threads run lengthways and are held tight on a <b>loom</b>. The <b>weft</b> thread goes <b>over and under</b> the warp, from side to side, and is pressed down to make a firm cloth. Change the colour of the weft, and a pattern grows.</li><li><b>Symmetry on two axes:</b> A <b>medallion</b> design has a big pattern in the middle and matching patterns in the corners. If you fold it top to bottom, and then left to right, both halves match: <b>mirror symmetry across two axes</b>. A border repeats a <b>motif</b> all the way round.</li><li><b>The oldest carpet we know:</b> The <b>Pazyryk carpet</b>, found in a frozen tomb in the Altai mountains (Siberia), is about <b>2,400 years old</b>, from around the Persian period. It has patterns of deer, riders and flowers. Scholars debate exactly where it was made, but it shows that rich, knotted textiles existed in this world. Textiles rot, so few early ones survive. The famous Persian carpet patterns we know best come from later centuries.</li><li><b>Colour from nature:</b> Weavers made <b>dyes</b> from plants and other natural sources: <b>madder root</b> for reds, <b>indigo</b> for deep blues, and <b>weld</b> for yellows. Colour was a skill that took patience, and strong, lasting colours were valuable.</li><li><b>Why does pattern matter?:</b> Patterns can show <b>beauty</b>, <b>skill</b>, <b>place</b> and <b>family</b> identity. A weaver may carry knowledge handed from grandmother to child. Weaving was done by many hands, and it was part of ordinary life, just like the ration records you studied on Monday.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Weaving Words</b> — Match each weaving word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Weaving a Stripe</b> — Put these weaving steps in order.</li><li><b>sort: Symmetrical or Not?</b> — Sort each pattern description.</li><li><b>reveal: Dye Detectives</b> — Tap each card to see where the colour came from.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Cardboard Loom Carpet:</b> Make a small woven piece on a card loom. Plan a simple pattern with three colours, weave it over and under, and finish the edges.</li><li><b>Path B — Medallion Carpet Design:</b> Design a carpet on squared paper with a medallion in the centre, matching corners and a border. It must be symmetrical on both axes.</li><li><b>Path C — Textile Collage:</b> Layer fabric and paper pieces to make a carpet-style collage with a medallion, a border and a repeating motif.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Exod 35:25–26; Exod 35:30–35; Ps 139:13 — In Exodus 35, the women who were skilled at spinning brought spun blue, purple and scarlet yarn and fine linen, and others spun goat hair, all to help build the tent of worship. God filled Bezalel and Oholiab with skill to teach and to make beautiful things. In Psalm 139, the psalmist praises God for knitting him together in his mother’s womb.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> God values skilled hands, including the hands of spinners and weavers. What does that say about the importance of making things well?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What do handmade things say about the maker? What is the difference between something made quickly in a factory and something woven slowly by hand? Both have value, so what do we notice about each?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Thank someone who makes things for you (a parent who cooks or sews, a teacher who prepares, a maker in your community). Write or draw a thank-you in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Cardboard Loom Carpet</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a small woven piece on a card loom. Plan a simple pattern with three colours, weave it over and under, and finish the edges.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Loom is warped with even, tight threads</li><li>My weft goes over and under correctly</li><li>My pattern repeats or is symmetrical</li><li>I used a limited palette (3 colours)</li><li>Edges are straight and ends are tucked in</li><li>I can name warp and weft</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-warped loom and a thick yarn. Weave three stripes with a partner checking the over-under pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Warp and weave independently with a pattern of stripes or a simple repeat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Weave a symmetrical design (a diamond or stepped medallion) from a squared-paper plan, and explain how your plan guided your weaving.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Cut slits and thread the warp. 2 Weave one row over-under, the next under-over. 3 Press the weft down gently. 4 Change colour for a stripe. 5 Tuck the ends. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My weaving uses ___ colours and shows ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Medallion Carpet Design</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a carpet on squared paper with a medallion in the centre, matching corners and a border. It must be symmetrical on both axes.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Design is on squared paper with a clear central medallion</li><li>Left and right match (mirror line)</li><li>Top and bottom match (second mirror line)</li><li>Border repeats at least one motif</li><li>Limited palette (3–4 colours)</li><li>I can explain the symmetry</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with a quarter-design already drawn and complete the other three quarters.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and colour the full design with a border and two motifs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second border with a different repeat and write a short ‘maker’s note’ comparing your design with a textile from another culture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Draw the border. 2 Draw the medallion in one quarter. 3 Flip it to the other quarters. 4 Add motifs. 5 Colour. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My design has ___ lines of symmetry. I used the ___ motif because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Textile Collage</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Layer fabric and paper pieces to make a carpet-style collage with a medallion, a border and a repeating motif.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I planned my layout first</li><li>I used at least three different fabrics or papers</li><li>I included a border and a central design</li><li>My pattern repeats or is symmetrical</li><li>Pieces are glued neatly</li><li>I can say what my design shows</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use pre-cut shapes and a simple template. Choose three colours from a bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cut and layer your own pieces with a border and a medallion.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Combine a mirror-symmetrical medallion with a border of alternating motifs and explain how contrast makes it stand out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Choose three colours. 2 Cut the base shape. 3 Layer the border first. 4 Add the medallion. 5 Add small motifs. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My collage shows ___ and I chose the colours ___ because ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Exod 35:25–26; Exod 35:30–35; Ps 139:13 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Celebrate the skill of weavers across cultures, including the women who did most weaving. Be even-handed: Persian textile art is a fine tradition. Avoid ranking handmade over factory-made too strongly. Keep the connection to God’s gift of creative skill, as in Week 1.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with raranga (weaving with harakeke), tukutuku and whāriki. These are taonga with their own meanings and protocols. Invite your Māori Education lead or a local weaver to guide the conversation. Do NOT imitate sacred or tapu patterns, and focus on the idea that pattern and weaving carry meaning and identity.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian carpets belong to living cultures; avoid ‘exotic’ language. Be honest about which carpets are ancient and which are from later periods. Banner or carpet designs should not copy any religious or sacred symbols.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Shoe-box or card looms (notched card)</li><li>Wool or yarn in a limited palette (3–5 colours)</li><li>Blunt needles or craft sticks (shuttles)</li><li>Squared paper, rulers, coloured pencils</li><li>Fabric and paper offcuts, glue, scissors</li><li>Photos of textiles and carpets (app or printed), clearly labelled by period</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students weave over-under correctly?</li><li>Who used symmetry on two axes?</li><li>How did students talk about the maker’s skill?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Bazaar Bargains":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Bazaar Bargains — Trade fairly. Wager wisely. Win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 7: The Marketplace of the World (5th century BC) · Persepolis region, bazaars, Royal Road stations</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about daily life, trade, working animals and weaving, and to work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the tablets, trade, camels and weaving.</li><li>I can wager my Darics wisely and work with my Caravan with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Bazaar Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the idea to new situations.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Bazaar Bargains</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Sunset is coming. The bazaar is a swirl of colour and noise, and somewhere, wrapped in wool, the Fragment of the Market is waiting. ‘Wager well, Courier,’ says Shirin. ‘Bet bravely on what you know, and keep back what you are unsure of. But remember: even a bargain is not worth a lie. Write your Creed first.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Bazaar Creed on a strip and reads it aloud.</li><li>Explain the wager rules: teams decide how many Darics to bet on each question, so confidence matters. Remind teams that a wager is just a game of confidence, and that real life is not about gambling.</li><li>Before starting, remind students of recall areas: Elamites and sources (Week 1), Cyrus and the camels (Week 2), Darius and the Royal Road (Week 3), plus this week’s tablets, trade, camels and weaving.</li><li>Display the Creeds beside the earlier weeks’ Creeds.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Wager — Wager points before each question (coming Week 7).</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q10, Q11, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Bazaar Creed: ONE sentence about how your team will deal honestly, even when nobody is watching. Starters: We will always give honest… / We promise to share… / Even when we could win by cheating, we will… / We will cheer for…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Jer 29:4–7; Prov 11:1; Gen 24:10–20; Exod 35:30–35 — This week we met a God who tells His people to seek the good of the city where they live, who loves honest scales, who notices a young woman watering thirsty camels, and who gives skilled hands to the makers of beautiful things.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most wonderful thing you learned this week about ordinary people, trade, animals or weaving?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s ideas made you think hardest: fair trade, caring for animals, or the dignity of ordinary work?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Bazaar Creed together. Pray for each other, and for people who work to feed and clothe us.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. The Persepolis Fortification Tablets mostly record… A) poems · B) battles only · <b>C) rations paid to workers ✔</b> · D) recipes <em>The tablets are mostly records of rations (barley, flour, fruit, beer or wine, sheep) paid to workers, including women and children.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. Which language was widely used across much of the empire for letters and trade? A) Māori · B) Latin · C) English · <b>D) Aramaic ✔</b> <em>Aramaic was a common language for letters and official business across much of the empire.</em> [SS-ICO]</li><li>3. A group of traders and animals travelling together is a… <b>A) caravan ✔</b> · B) choir · C) crew · D) class <em>A caravan is a group of travellers and animals who go together for safety and help.</em> [SS-EW]</li><li>4. Camels have wide, padded feet. This helps them… <b>A) not sink in sand ✔</b> · B) climb trees · C) fly · D) swim <em>Wide pads spread the camel’s weight, so it does not sink into soft sand.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>5. Proverbs 11:1 says that… <b>A) dishonest scales are wrong, and honest weights please God ✔</b> · B) money is evil · C) everyone should swap everything · D) all trade is bad <em>Proverbs 11:1 says that dishonest scales are hateful to the Lord, but an accurate weight pleases Him.</em> [RE]</li><li>6. Most of the Persepolis tablets are written in the language of which people from Week 1? A) Vikings · B) Romans · <b>C) Elamites ✔</b> · D) Mongols <em>Most are in Elamite, the language of the Elamites of Susa, whom we met in Week 1.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>7. Which king organised the Royal Road, with its stations, as you learned in Week 3? A) Cyrus · B) Alexander · C) Xerxes · <b>D) Darius ✔</b> <em>Darius I organised the empire’s provinces, coinage and the Royal Road with relay stations.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>8. In Week 2, Herodotus says Cyrus used camels against the Lydian cavalry. Why would camels help? <b>A) Horses are frightened by the smell and look of camels ✔</b> · B) Camels are faster than horses · C) Camels can fly · D) Camels can breathe fire <em>According to Herodotus, horses are unsettled by the smell of camels. It is a Greek source, so we say ‘according to’.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>9. Jeremiah 29:7 told the exiles in Babylon to… A) hide away · B) fight the city · <b>C) seek the peace and prosperity of the city ✔</b> · D) leave at once <em>God told the exiles to build homes, plant gardens and seek the good of the city where they lived.</em> [RE]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Domesticated animals differ from wild animals because… A) they are only in stories · <b>B) people have cared for and bred them over many generations ✔</b> · C) they can’t breathe in the wild · D) they are always smaller <em>A domesticated animal has been bred by people over many generations to live and work with them. The onager is a wild ass that people have not been able to tame.</em> [SC-LW-Eco] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. Specialisation means… A) everyone does everything · <b>B) people focus on what they do well, then trade ✔</b> · C) there is no trade · D) nobody uses money <em>With specialisation, people (or places) concentrate on what they do well and trade for the rest. That is why trade matters.</em> [SS-EW] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Which step is correct for a fair test of foot shape in sand? A) Skip the measuring · <b>B) Use the same weight and sand each time ✔</b> · C) Change the weight each time · D) Use different sand each time <em>In a fair test only ONE thing changes (foot width). Weight, sand and how you press must stay the same, and you measure each depth.</em> [SC-NoS-I] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (such as trade and animals) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Jer 29:4–7; Prov 11:1; Gen 24:10–20; Exod 35:30–35 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful. Celebrate fair play and effort, not only winning. Remind students that wagering Darics is a classroom game, and that we talk about honest dealing in real life.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week: fair work and pay, pounamu trade routes (with local guidance) and NZ exports, farm and working animals, and raranga and tukutuku.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown: keep celebrating effort. Wagering is a classroom game with pretend Darics, so make clear that no real money or prizes are involved. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching on Monday (domesticated vs wild, or fair-test variables)?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 8: Fire on the Mountain":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: The Fall: Alexander and Darius III":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Fall: Alexander and Darius III — How does a mighty empire fall, and who tells the story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 8: Fire on the Mountain (334–330 BC) · Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li><li>EN-R — English: Reading. Students read informational and narrative texts and make meaning from them.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Integrity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why a huge empire fell and why accounts of the fall differ.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can put the key events in order: Granicus (334 BC), Issus (333 BC), Gaugamela (331 BC) and Persepolis (330 BC).</li><li>I can explain three causes of the fall of the empire.</li><li>I can compare two accounts of the burning of Persepolis.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The cause–effect chain or caption set; a ‘because’ sentence about which cause mattered most; observation of how students used evidence in the debate; the Path product (photographed).</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names the battles or a cause with support and a prompt.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Sequences the four key events and explains two or three causes of the fall in simple cause-and-effect sentences.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains several causes, says which mattered most with evidence, and compares two accounts of the burning of Persepolis, naming who wrote them and when.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates how reliable the sources are, explains why no single cause is certain, and applies the idea to another example from history.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>conquest</b> — Taking over a land or a people by force.</li><li><b>Macedonia</b> — A kingdom in the north of Greece. Alexander was its king.</li><li><b>Achaemenid</b> — The royal family (dynasty) of Cyrus, Darius I and Xerxes. Darius III was the last Achaemenid king.</li><li><b>phalanx</b> — A tight block of Macedonian soldiers carrying very long spears (sarissas).</li><li><b>cavalry</b> — Soldiers who fight on horseback.</li><li><b>satrap</b> — A governor of a Persian province (you met this word in Week 3).</li><li><b>Hellenistic</b> — ‘Greek-like’: the age after Alexander when Greek and Eastern cultures mixed.</li><li><b>cause and effect</b> — A cause is why something happens. An effect is what happens because of it.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Court of Inquiry</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Smoke is drifting across the plateau, and the caravan has been called to a tent of black felt. Shirin stands at a table spread with five clay cards. ‘An empire that stood for two hundred years has fallen in four,’ she says. ‘You are the Court of Inquiry, Courier. Here are the clues. No single clue is the whole truth, so you must weigh them and decide which mattered most. And remember, the writers who told us this story were not on the Persian side.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: print and cut one set of five Evidence Cards per Caravan: (1) Alexander’s tactics and leadership, (2) the decisions of King Darius III in battle, (3) the Persian army’s many peoples and divided commands, (4) distance, supplies and speed (including Persian cities that opened their gates), (5) luck and chance. Keep the same Caravans as Weeks 1–7.</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud. Pause after ‘no single clue is the whole truth’.</li><li>Teams read their five cards (3 min). Ask them to rank the cards from ‘mattered most’ to ‘mattered least’ and to write ONE ‘because’ for their top card on a mini-whiteboard.</li><li>Court session (4 min): each Caravan shows its ranking and gives its reason. Do not tell them who is right. Note the cards that most teams put at the top.</li><li>Tell the class that historians also disagree, and that today they will see why. Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria. Students read them chorally (1 min).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Week 7 link-back (two minutes, mini-whiteboards): What did the Persepolis Fortification Tablets record? What is specialisation? Why do camels have wide padded feet? Then: ‘Last week we saw the empire at its busiest and most prosperous. This week we see what happened to it.’</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>One young king, three great battles, and one burning terrace. Follow the road from the Hellespont to Persepolis, and notice who is telling you the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline (3 min): add the date cards to the strip you have used all term: 336 BC Alexander becomes king; 334 Granicus; 333 Issus; 331 Gaugamela; 330 Persepolis burned and Darius III killed; 323 Alexander dies. Place Xerxes (486–465 BC) well to the left so students see the 150-year gap.</li><li>The four dates (5 min): use the campaign map. Put a token at the Hellespont and walk it to each battle. Pronounce the names slowly together. Students show the date on mini-whiteboards after each stop.</li><li>Causes (4 min): return to the Evidence Cards from the dispatch. Ask: ‘Which cards did the discovery cards support?’ Stress that historians weigh several causes and avoid a single ‘because’. Ask for one cause on the Persian side and one on the Macedonian side.</li><li>Sources (3 min): who wrote these accounts, and when? Arrian wrote about 450 years after the events, Plutarch about 400 years, Diodorus about 300. None of them were Persian. Link to Week 1 (bias) and Week 3 (propaganda). Say honestly that Persian voices are mostly missing.</li><li>Sensitivity (1 min): say aloud that for Iranian-NZ students and some Zoroastrian or Muslim families this history is part of a living heritage, and that we will talk about it with care. Never ask a student to speak for a culture.</li><li><b>A young king from Macedonia:</b> <b>Alexander</b> became king of <b>Macedonia</b> in <b>336 BC</b>, when he was about <b>20</b>. His father, <b>Philip II</b>, had built a strong, well-trained army with a tight block of spearmen (the <b>phalanx</b>) and fast cavalry. In <b>334 BC</b> Alexander crossed into Asia with an army of roughly <b>40,000</b> soldiers (the numbers vary in the sources). Greek writers said the war was about paying back the Persians for Xerxes’ invasion 150 years earlier. It was also about land, power and wealth.</li><li><b>Darius III, the last Achaemenid king:</b> <b>Darius III</b> became king about <b>336 BC</b>, after a period of murders and plots at court. He ruled a land that was still enormous and rich, from Egypt to the Indus region. Greek writers often paint him as weak or cowardly, but they were writing about the enemy of their hero. At Issus and Gaugamela he led his army in person, and he raised new armies after each defeat. We should say that he faced a very dangerous opponent.</li><li><b>Four dates to remember:</b> <b>Granicus, 334 BC:</b> Alexander’s cavalry beat the local Persian governors’ army beside a river in north-west Anatolia (modern Türkiye). <b>Issus, 333 BC:</b> a battle on a narrow coastal plain, where Darius’s huge army had little room to move. <b>Gaugamela, 331 BC:</b> a battle on an open plain in what is now northern Iraq, after which Darius fled and the road to Babylon, Susa and Persepolis lay open. <b>Persepolis, 330 BC:</b> the great terrace was burned.</li><li><b>Why did the empire fall? Not just one reason:</b> Historians suggest several causes working together. <b>Tactics and leadership:</b> Alexander took risks, led charges himself and used cavalry and the phalanx together. <b>Command problems:</b> the Persian army was made up of many peoples and was hard to organise as one force, and Darius was a new king. <b>Distance and supplies:</b> the empire was so large that it was hard to defend everywhere, though Alexander also had to feed his army deep inside it. Some cities and governors, such as the governor of Babylon, chose to surrender. <b>Chance:</b> battles also turn on luck. The Greek writers do <i>not</i> agree on all of this.</li><li><b>Accident, revenge or political act?:</b> In <b>330 BC</b> Alexander’s army burned the palaces at <b>Persepolis</b>. Ancient writers disagree about why. <b>Plutarch</b> and <b>Diodorus</b> describe a feast, and a woman named <b>Thaïs</b> urging the king to burn the palace as payment for Xerxes burning the temples of Athens in 480 BC. <b>Arrian</b> says that Alexander’s older general <b>Parmenion</b> objected, and that Alexander explained it as revenge on the Persians. Some modern historians think it was a deliberate political act that sent a message to the Greek cities. Others think a drunken feast got out of hand. Archaeologists find burned layers in several buildings, but the burned stones cannot tell us <i>why</i>.</li><li><b>After the fire, and who remembers what:</b> Later in 330 BC <b>Darius III</b> was killed by his own officers, led by <b>Bessus</b>, the governor of Bactria. Alexander ordered a royal burial for him, and went on east. Alexander died in <b>Babylon in 323 BC</b>, and his generals divided his lands. One of them, <b>Seleucus</b>, ruled much of Iran. This was the start of the <b>Hellenistic</b> age. Memory of Alexander in Iran is mixed. Some later Persian stories treat him as a destroyer; the great poem <i>Shahnameh</i> turns him into a rightful king with a Persian father. <b>Every account was written afterwards, and almost all of them by Greeks and Romans, hundreds of years later.</b> No Persian account of the conquest survives.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: The Fall of the Empire</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>match: Words of the Conquest</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>sort: Agreed, Disputed, or Opinion?</b> — Sort each statement. Do most sources agree, do the sources disagree, or is it an opinion?</li><li><b>reveal: Who Wrote It, and When?</b> — Tap each card to see why we must be careful with each account.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Cause–Effect Chain and Explanation:</b> Choose THREE causes of the fall of the Persian Empire. For each, draw a link in a chain showing the cause and what it led to, then write a paragraph explaining which cause you think mattered most and why.</li><li><b>Path B — Campaign Map with Annotated Battles:</b> Draw an annotated map of Alexander’s route from the Hellespont to Persepolis. Show the four key places and write a short caption for each battle: when, where, who won and one reason why.</li><li><b>Path C — Debate: ‘Accident or Act?’:</b> Use the two source cards to prepare a debate about why Persepolis burned. Half your Caravan argues ‘a deliberate act’ and half argues ‘an accident or a drunken mistake’. Present for and against, then say what you think and how sure you are.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Daniel 2:31–45; Daniel 8:20–21; Daniel 2:44 — In Daniel 2, a king dreams of a great statue made of different metals, and Daniel explains that kingdoms will rise one after another. In Daniel 8, a vision of a ram and a goat is explained as the kings of Media and Persia, and then the king of Greece. And Daniel 2:44 says that in the days of those kings God will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Kingdoms rise and kingdoms fall. Persia once seemed unstoppable, and then it was gone in four years. What lasts when great empires do not?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Christians read Daniel in different ways. Some see these chapters as a vision of future kingdoms, and some see them as encouragement for God’s people living under foreign rulers, and scholars disagree about when they were written. But all of them see the same big idea: God is the ruler over history, and the biggest empires do not have the last word. How does it feel to read that when a great city has just burned?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write ONE thing you can build that will outlast you (for example kindness, a friendship, a promise, faith) and ONE small thing you will do this week to start building it.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Cause–Effect Chain and Explanation</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Choose THREE causes of the fall of the Persian Empire. For each, draw a link in a chain showing the cause and what it led to, then write a paragraph explaining which cause you think mattered most and why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I named three different causes</li><li>Each link shows a cause AND an effect (‘because… so…’)</li><li>I used at least two vocabulary words (for example phalanx, satrap, cavalry)</li><li>My paragraph says which cause mattered most and gives a reason</li><li>I said ‘historians suggest’ or ‘probably’ at least once, because no single cause is certain</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the three ready-made cause cards and put them into a chain. Complete the frames with a partner, and write two sentences for the ‘mattered most’ paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose your own three causes, draw the chain and write a full explanation paragraph with a reason for your top cause.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a counter-argument: ‘Someone might say that ___ mattered most because ___. I disagree because ___.’ Then compare the fall of this empire with one other empire or kingdom you know.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Chain frame:</b> Cause 1: ___ → so ___ → which led to ___. <b>Which mattered most?</b> I think ___ mattered most because ___. <b>But…</b> Other causes also mattered, such as ___. <b>Careful words:</b> probably · historians suggest · it seems that · one reason may be</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Campaign Map with Annotated Battles</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw an annotated map of Alexander’s route from the Hellespont to Persepolis. Show the four key places and write a short caption for each battle: when, where, who won and one reason why.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose, scale bar and key</li><li>The route is drawn and in the right order</li><li>Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela and Persepolis are all labelled with dates</li><li>Each has a caption of at least one sentence with a reason</li><li>I marked at least two other places for context (for example Babylon, Susa, Alexandria)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use an outline map with the four places already marked. Add the dates and write captions from the frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw the map independently with all conventions and four full captions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second line for Darius III’s journey and annotate where the two routes cross. Use the scale to estimate the distance from the Hellespont to Persepolis and show your working.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Caption frame:</b> In ___ BC, at ___, Alexander ___. This mattered because ___. <b>Map conventions:</b> T · A · L · K · E · S (Title · Arrow/compass · Legend · Key · Edge/border · Scale) as in Week 1. <b>Bank of places:</b> Hellespont · Granicus · Issus · Gaugamela · Babylon · Susa · Persepolis</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Debate: ‘Accident or Act?’</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the two source cards to prepare a debate about why Persepolis burned. Half your Caravan argues ‘a deliberate act’ and half argues ‘an accident or a drunken mistake’. Present for and against, then say what you think and how sure you are.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I stated my side clearly</li><li>I used at least two pieces of evidence from the source cards</li><li>I named WHO wrote each source and WHEN</li><li>I answered one point from the other side</li><li>I finished by saying how sure we can be, and what else a historian would need</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the evidence cards and the speech frame; one member of the pair reads the source aloud while the other states the reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Prepare your own argument with two pieces of evidence and a reply to the other side.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Argue the side you disagree with, then explain which side the evidence supports better and why. What would you need to find in the ground (archaeology) to be more certain?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Opening:</b> We argue that the burning was ___ because … <b>Evidence:</b> According to ___, who wrote about ___ years later, … <b>Reply:</b> The other side says ___, but … <b>Closing:</b> We cannot be certain, because …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Dan 2:31–45; Dan 8:20–21; Dan 2:44 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Daniel is read in many ways, so present this honestly: ‘Christians read Daniel in different ways, and we are going to look at what all of them agree on.’ Do not say that the Bible ‘predicts’ Alexander as if it were settled. Do not suggest that Persian rulers were evil: Cyrus and Darius I appear favourably in Ezra and Isaiah, as students learned in Weeks 2 and 3. Some students may be Jewish, Muslim or from Zoroastrian backgrounds, and may hold these stories differently. Invite them to listen respectfully and never put a student on the spot.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How do we remember the fall of something precious in Aotearoa? Different people tell the story of the same event in different ways: iwi, historians, newspapers. Ask your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū how the stories of your own rohe are told and by whom, and avoid generalising across iwi. Whose version of an event do we usually hear first?</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> This is the story of the destruction of a heritage that Iranian-NZ students (and Zoroastrian, Muslim and other students from Iranian backgrounds) may feel proudly and personally, so tell it with respect, and avoid describing Alexander as either hero or villain. Name that nearly all the sources are Greek or Roman and were written long afterwards. Keep battle talk factual and non-graphic; students need dates, places and reasons, not details of suffering.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Evidence cards for the Court of Inquiry (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Alexander campaign map and route tokens</li><li>Timeline strip with new date cards (336, 334, 333, 331, 330, 323 BC)</li><li>Two short source extracts on the burning of Persepolis (app or printed, simplified)</li><li>Path A cause-effect chain template; Path C debate prep sheet</li><li>Pronunciation guide: Gaugamela (GOW-guh-MEE-luh), Issus (ISS-us), Granicus (gran-EYE-cus)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students give three causes with a ‘because’, or only one?</li><li>Who used the words ‘probably’ or ‘historians suggest’ without being prompted?</li><li>What do I need to revisit tomorrow before we move from the fire to the rebuilding of cities?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Cities Under Attack: Destruction, Abandonment and Rebuilding":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cities Under Attack: Destruction, Abandonment and Rebuilding — When a city is broken, who decides what happens next?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Settlements and Change</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 8: Fire on the Mountain (334–330 BC) · Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Community &amp; participation; Ecological sustainability; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how events change settlements, and why people rebuild or leave places.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how war, fire and earthquakes can change a city.</li><li>I can explain why some places are rebuilt and others are abandoned.</li><li>I can compare an ancient and a modern rebuild.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Path product (photographed); a cause–effect explanation (‘The city was ___, so ___’); the quality of reasons for the rebuild priorities.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Describes one change to a settlement with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes how war, fire or an earthquake changed a place and gives one reason for rebuilding or leaving.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why places are rebuilt or abandoned using resources, location and identity, with examples.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Compares an ancient and a modern example, weighs the reasons for different outcomes and shows care for the people involved.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>ruin</b> — What is left of a building or city after it has been destroyed or has fallen down.</li><li><b>abandon</b> — To leave a place and not come back.</li><li><b>rebuild</b> — To build again something that was damaged or destroyed.</li><li><b>earthquake</b> — A shaking of the ground caused by sudden movement of rock deep in the earth.</li><li><b>tectonic plates</b> — The huge slabs of rock that make up the earth’s surface and slowly move.</li><li><b>adobe (mud brick)</b> — Bricks made from mud and straw, dried in the sun.</li><li><b>reconstruction</b> — Putting something back together, either in real life or as a drawing.</li><li><b>heritage</b> — Places, objects and traditions passed down from the past that people want to look after.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Before and After</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The caravan has climbed to the edge of the terrace. Below, where there was a forest of tall columns, there is smoke and grey stone, and the rooms are open to the sky. Shirin sets a roll of tracing paper in your hands. ‘Draw what you can see, Courier. Then draw what you think was here yesterday. Look for the clues: a base, a doorway, a fallen beam. A ruin is a puzzle, and you are the one who solves it.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday on mini-whiteboards: name two of the four key dates; name one cause of the fall; explain why we cannot be sure why Persepolis burned.</li><li>Show the Persepolis ruins photo. Ask: ‘Which parts are still standing? Which parts are missing? Why might stone survive and wood not?’ (3 min).</li><li>Before-and-after sketch (5 min): students draw the ruin on one half of the page and their idea of the whole building on the other half. They label two clues that helped them.</li><li>Take two shares. Praise ‘I think… because I can see…’. Do not correct guesses yet; the Discovery will help.</li><li>Share LI/SC (students read aloud).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: the four key dates (334, 333, 331, 330 BC); one cause of the fall of the empire; why we cannot be sure why Persepolis burned.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Cities can be broken by war, fire, flood or earthquake. Some are rebuilt, some are left, and some become something new. Work out what makes the difference.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Persepolis (4 min): return to the photo. Ask: ‘What lasted? What did not? Why?’ (stone vs wood and mud brick). Explain that the site was not rebuilt, and that people later chose other places nearby.</li><li>New cities (3 min): trace Alexandria on the map. Ask: ‘Which of the four settlement questions from Week 1 does it answer? (water, soil, defence, trade).’ Link to the settlement hierarchy from Week 3.</li><li>Earthquakes (4 min): show the Bam photos, then Napier. Say: ‘Many people in our community may know someone affected by an earthquake. You can always step out or listen.’ Use the word ‘recovery’ more than ‘disaster’. Do not use graphic images; show buildings and people rebuilding.</li><li>Reasons to rebuild (4 min): draw a three-column chart on the board: resources, location, identity. Students add examples from Persepolis, Alexandria, Bam and Napier.</li><li>Model the first step of each Path (2 min).</li><li><b>What the ruins of Persepolis tell us:</b> At Persepolis the stone columns, staircases and gateways have survived, but the wooden roofs, beams and the mud-brick walls burned or crumbled. Only a few of the great columns of the <b>Apadana</b> still stand. After the fire Persepolis was <b>not rebuilt</b> as a royal centre, and over the centuries it was partly buried by earth. The ruins were carefully excavated in the <b>1930s</b>, and in <b>1979</b> UNESCO listed Persepolis as a <b>World Heritage Site</b>. A nearby city, <b>Istakhr</b>, later became more important in the region.</li><li><b>Alexander’s new cities:</b> Alexander did not only destroy; he also founded cities. The most famous is <b>Alexandria</b> in Egypt, begun in <b>331 BC</b> on the Mediterranean coast beside the Nile delta. It had a good <b>harbour</b>, fertile land behind it and sea trade in front of it. Ancient writers say he founded many more cities, and many were named after him. Some faded away. Alexandria, because of its position, grew into one of the great cities of the ancient world, and it is still a major city today.</li><li><b>A land that shakes:</b> Iran lies where huge <b>tectonic plates</b> press together, so it has many earthquakes. The ancient mud-brick citadel of <b>Bam</b> (Arg-e Bam), in the south-east, was one of the largest adobe buildings in the world. In <b>December 2003</b> a powerful earthquake struck the city of Bam and killed more than 26,000 people, and the citadel was badly damaged. Since then, experts and local people have worked, with international help, to stabilise and rebuild parts of it.</li><li><b>Aotearoa also shakes:</b> New Zealand lies on the edge of two plates, so earthquakes are part of our story too. In <b>1931</b> a huge earthquake destroyed much of <b>Napier</b> and Hastings, and more than 250 people died. Napier was rebuilt within about two years, and its fashionable <b>Art Deco</b> buildings are famous today. In Canterbury, our own region, the 2010–2011 earthquakes changed Christchurch and the lives of many families. <i>Teacher: handle with whānau sensitivity; some students have lived this. Offer a choice to join in or simply listen.</i></li><li><b>Why do people rebuild, and why do they leave?:</b> People rebuild when a place has something they cannot easily replace: <b>resources</b> (fertile land, water, a harbour), a good <b>location</b> (on a trade route or at a crossroads), and <b>identity</b> (it is where ancestors, whānau and memories are). People leave when the resources have gone, when the danger is too great, when the reason for the city has ended (as when a capital falls), or when they are forced out. Often a place is partly rebuilt, partly changed, and partly remembered.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>sort: Rebuilt or Abandoned?</b> — Sort each place: was it rebuilt, left as a ruin, or both in part?</li><li><b>match: Settlement Change Words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: From Ruin to Recovery</b> — Put these steps of a rebuild in a sensible order.</li><li><b>reveal: Why Rebuild Here?</b> — Tap each card to see why people choose to rebuild a place.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Reconstruct Persepolis:</b> Draw a two-part diagram of part of Persepolis. On the left, draw the ruin as it looks today and label what has survived. On the right, draw your imagined whole building, and explain how you used the clues.</li><li><b>Path B — Rebuild Plan: A Town after a Disaster:</b> A small imagined town has been badly damaged by an earthquake. Using the rebuild-plan cards, decide what to rebuild first, second and third, and explain your priorities. Draw a plan map showing the new town.</li><li><b>Path C — Then and Now Venn:</b> Compare what happened after the burning of Persepolis with what happened after the 1931 Napier earthquake. Make a Venn diagram, then write three sentences to explain the most important similarity and difference.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Psalm 46:1–3; Isaiah 61:4 — The psalmist sings that God is our refuge and strength, always ready to help in trouble, so that even if the earth shakes and the mountains fall into the sea, we need not be afraid. Isaiah promises that God’s people will rebuild the ancient ruins and restore the places that have been devastated for generations.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Where do we find security when our homes or cities shake? What does it mean to say that God is a ‘refuge’?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> The psalm does not say that earthquakes never happen: it says that God is with people in the middle of them. Isaiah’s promise is about people rebuilding together. How does a faith that says ‘do not be afraid’ fit with being careful (for example planning safe buildings)? Is trusting God the same as ignoring risk?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a short prayer for people who are rebuilding after hardship, here or anywhere in the world. You may keep it private, or share it with your Caravan.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Reconstruct Persepolis</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a two-part diagram of part of Persepolis. On the left, draw the ruin as it looks today and label what has survived. On the right, draw your imagined whole building, and explain how you used the clues.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The ruin side shows at least three labelled surviving features (columns, steps, stone doorway…)</li><li>The reconstruction side adds what has been lost (roof, wooden beams, paint…)</li><li>I used two clues from the ruins to decide what to add</li><li>I explained why some materials survived and others did not</li><li>I wrote one sentence about why people today look after the ruins</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline of the ruin and a label bank. Draw the missing parts on tracing paper over the top and complete the clue frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw both halves independently with labels and explain two clues.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a third panel showing what archaeologists are still not sure about, and what evidence they would need to be more certain.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label bank:</b> column · base · staircase · doorway · terrace · roof beam · mud-brick wall. <b>Clue frame:</b> I added a ___ because I could see ___ in the ruins. <b>Why it survived:</b> The ___ lasted because it was made of ___. The ___ did not because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Rebuild Plan: A Town after a Disaster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A small imagined town has been badly damaged by an earthquake. Using the rebuild-plan cards, decide what to rebuild first, second and third, and explain your priorities. Draw a plan map showing the new town.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I ranked at least five priorities</li><li>I gave a reason for each of my top three</li><li>I thought about safety (for example not rebuilding on a fault or flood-prone ground)</li><li>My plan map has a title, key and compass rose</li><li>I thought about the people who live there and asked ‘What do they need?’</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the priority cards and a printed outline of the town; place the cards in order and complete the ‘because’ frames for your top three.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rank your own priorities, justify the top three, and draw the plan map with all conventions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘consultation’ section: write what a farmer, a nurse and a child might each want first, and explain how you would make a fair decision.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Priority ladder:</b> First: ___ because … Second: ___ because … Third: ___ because … <b>Safety:</b> We will build away from ___ because … <b>People:</b> The people need ___ so we will include ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Then and Now Venn</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare what happened after the burning of Persepolis with what happened after the 1931 Napier earthquake. Make a Venn diagram, then write three sentences to explain the most important similarity and difference.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Venn has at least three items in each circle and three in the middle</li><li>I compared the cause (fire and war vs earthquake)</li><li>I compared what happened next (abandoned vs rebuilt)</li><li>I gave one reason for the difference</li><li>I wrote with care and respect for people who lived through disasters</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the sorted cards (one for each fact) and place them in the Venn. Complete the three sentence frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own Venn using the Discovery notes and write three sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a third circle, either Alexandria or Bam, and decide which place shows the strongest ‘will to rebuild’. Use evidence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Think about:</b> Cause · Who rebuilt it? · Was it rebuilt? · Why / why not? · What do we still see today? <b>Sentence frames:</b> One similarity is … One difference is … I think Napier was rebuilt because … but Persepolis was not because …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 46:1–3; Isa 61:4 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Some students may have lived through earthquakes, either in Canterbury or in other countries, so use gentle language and offer a choice to participate. Do not promise that God prevents disasters. Psalm 46 is a poem of trust, not a promise of safety from every harm. Christians also take practical steps: building safely, helping neighbours, and rebuilding. Say that the same Psalm is loved by Jewish and Christian readers, and that many people of different faiths find comfort in a sense of refuge.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Napier’s Art Deco rebuild after 1931 and the changes to Christchurch after 2010–2011 are part of our national story. Ask: what did people want to keep, and what did they decide to change? Invite your Māori Education lead or local rūnanga to share how the earthquakes and the rebuild are remembered in your rohe, and let students choose whether to take part. Avoid generalising across iwi.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Natural disasters touch real lives. Some students and staff may have lost homes, loved ones or school buildings in the Canterbury earthquakes or elsewhere. Use recovery language, avoid distressing images, allow students to opt out, and be ready to talk with a student who becomes upset. Bam is an Iranian city whose people are still living there: treat it with respect, as a place of resilience.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Photo of the Persepolis ruins (app or printed)</li><li>Bam citadel (Arg-e Bam) photos, before and after</li><li>Photo of Napier’s Art Deco streets and a 1931 photo from a trustworthy source (check it is suitable)</li><li>Squared or tracing paper for reconstruction sketches</li><li>Rebuild-plan cards (priorities: homes, water, school, hospital, market, place of worship, park)</li><li>Venn diagram template; Path C comparison sheet</li><li>Local-area map for the NZ comparison</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students link the survival of materials to what they could see in the ruins?</li><li>Who named at least two reasons for rebuilding with an example?</li><li>Was the rebuild plan debate respectful and sensitive for all students?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Gone or Going: Why Animals Disappear and How We Can Help":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gone or Going: Why Animals Disappear and How We Can Help — Who is missing from the old Persian lands, and what can we do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Extinction and Conservation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 8: Fire on the Mountain (334–330 BC) · Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li><li>MA-S — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Statistics. Students collect, organise, display and interpret data.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Community &amp; participation; Inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain why species become endangered and what people can do to help.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can define extinct, endangered and critically endangered.</li><li>I can name three causes of species loss.</li><li>I can propose an action plan to help one species.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy of definitions (extinct, endangered); use of at least two pieces of evidence in the poster, letter or graph; the quality of the prediction and its reasoning.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names an endangered or extinct animal with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Defines extinct and endangered, and gives two causes with an example for each.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why species are lost and what helps, using at least three pieces of evidence, and uses data accurately.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates different solutions, explains trade-offs (for example people’s needs vs wildlife), and makes a reasoned prediction with careful language.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>extinct</b> — When every individual of a species has died, and the species is gone for ever.</li><li><b>endangered</b> — At serious risk of becoming extinct.</li><li><b>critically endangered</b> — At extremely high risk of becoming extinct soon.</li><li><b>habitat loss</b> — When the natural home of a species is cleared, damaged or broken up.</li><li><b>introduced species</b> — A plant or animal brought by people to a place where it did not live before.</li><li><b>conservation</b> — Protecting and caring for nature, and for species at risk.</li><li><b>kaitiakitanga</b> — Guardianship and care for the environment, a Māori concept. (Learn more with your local iwi or Māori Education lead.)</li><li><b>population</b> — All the living things of one kind in a place (from Week 1).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Zoo of the Missing</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>At dawn the caravan reaches an old royal hunting park, and it is silent. Shirin lines up four dark silhouettes on a stone wall. ‘Some of these animals still live in the old lands of Persia, and some do not. Which is which, Courier? And before you guess, remember: Gandom has already guessed, and she says they are all just clouds.’ The silhouettes wait, still and black against the morning light.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday on mini-whiteboards: name one reason a city is rebuilt; what survived at Persepolis and why; how does Alexandria answer the settlement questions?</li><li>Zoo of the Missing (6 min): show four silhouette cards one at a time: Caspian tiger, Asiatic cheetah, Persian leopard, Caspian seal. Students hold up one of three cards (EXTINCT / ENDANGERED / SAFE) for each. Ask for a ‘because’.</li><li>Reveal (2 min): Caspian tiger is extinct (gone by about the 1970s); the Asiatic cheetah is critically endangered, with only a handful surviving in Iran; the Persian leopard is endangered; the Caspian seal is also in serious trouble. Remind students that they have these animals as cards from Weeks 2 and 3. Gandom is wrong again.</li><li>Reassure: this lesson is about hope and action, not only sadness. Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: one reason a city is rebuilt; what survived at Persepolis and why; one example of a place that was rebuilt.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Some species are gone, some are hanging on, and some have been brought back from the brink. Find out why, and what makes the difference.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Definitions (3 min): build a three-step ladder on the board: safe → endangered → extinct. Add ‘critically endangered’ between endangered and extinct. Ask for an example of each from the Zoo of the Missing.</li><li>Causes (4 min): four corners activity. Label the four corners habitat loss, hunting, introduced species, climate and pollution. Call out an animal story and students move to the corner they think is the main cause. Remind them that many species have more than one cause.</li><li>Persian and NZ stories (5 min): use the species cards. Say clearly: ‘the Caspian tiger is gone, but the kākāpō and takahē show that recovery is possible.’ Be matter-of-fact about hunting and keep it free of blame or gore.</li><li>What works (3 min): ask students to match each solution to a problem: predator fence → introduced predators; reserve → habitat loss; breeding centre → tiny populations. Take three examples.</li><li>Hand out the Path materials, and model the first step of each (2 min).</li><li><b>Extinct, endangered, critically endangered:</b> <b>Extinct</b> means that every individual has died: the species is gone for ever. <b>Endangered</b> means that a species is at serious risk of extinction. <b>Critically endangered</b> means the risk is extremely high. Scientists keep lists of species and how safe they are, and the words matter because they tell us how urgent the help is.</li><li><b>Four big causes:</b> <b>Habitat loss:</b> forests, wetlands and grasslands are cleared for farms, towns and roads. <b>Hunting:</b> animals are killed for their skins, meat, horns or as trophies, or because people fear them. <b>Introduced species:</b> new predators, pests or diseases arrive and native animals cannot cope. <b>Climate and pollution:</b> a changing climate, polluted water and plastic can harm species, especially those living in one small place. Most species are in trouble for <i>more than one</i> reason.</li><li><b>The old Persian lands:</b> The <b>Caspian tiger</b> (a cousin of the Siberian tiger) lived in forests and reedbeds around the Caspian Sea. It was hunted, its forests were cleared and its prey was lost, and it was extinct by about the <b>1970s</b>. The <b>Asiatic cheetah</b> is critically endangered: today only a small number, perhaps a few dozen at most and likely fewer, survive in Iran. The <b>Persian leopard</b> is endangered, though more numerous. The <b>Caspian seal</b>, which lives only in the Caspian Sea, is seriously endangered because of fishing nets, pollution and a changing sea. Iranian scientists and rangers are working to protect them.</li><li><b>Aotearoa’s lost and found birds:</b> When people arrived in Aotearoa the land had no land mammals except bats, and the birds had few predators. The giant <b>moa</b> became extinct in a few hundred years after people arrived, through hunting and forest loss. The <b>huia</b> was lost by the early 1900s, and the last widely accepted sighting was in <b>1907</b>. But some stories are about hope: the <b>takahē</b>, thought extinct, was found again in Fiordland in <b>1948</b>, and now there are about <b>500</b>. The <b>kākāpō</b>, a flightless parrot, fell to about <b>50</b> birds in the 1990s, and has been brought back to more than <b>200</b> through intensive care and predator control.</li><li><b>What works?:</b> Conservation works best when it combines several things: <b>protected areas</b> (reserves, national parks, predator-free islands), <b>breeding programmes</b> (to raise numbers and spread the risk), <b>predator control</b> (traps and fences), and <b>community action</b> (farmers, schools, iwi and volunteers working together). In the south, Ngāi Tahu and the Department of Conservation are partners in caring for species such as the kākāpō. Ask your local Māori Education lead or rūnanga how kaitiakitanga is practised in your rohe, and take care not to generalise across iwi.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>sort: Extinct, Endangered or Common?</b> — Sort each animal into the right group.</li><li><b>match: Match the Conservation Idea</b> — Match each word to its meaning, or each solution to the problem it helps.</li><li><b>order: How a Species Slides Towards Extinction</b> — Put this chain of events in order.</li><li><b>reveal: Zoo of the Missing: Fact Files</b> — Tap each card to see the facts.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Awareness Campaign: Save the Asiatic Cheetah:</b> Create a poster or short advertisement script to raise awareness of the Asiatic cheetah. Include a clear message, two facts, and one thing people can do to help.</li><li><b>Path B — Persuasive Letter to a Decision-Maker:</b> Write a letter to a person who can help a threatened species (for example a minister, a mayor, or the head of a conservation organisation). Ask for a specific action and support your request with evidence.</li><li><b>Path C — Data Detectives: Kākāpō Numbers:</b> Use the rounded kākāpō population table to draw a line graph, and then predict what might happen next. Explain what the graph shows and what could change it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Genesis 1:26–28; Genesis 2:15; Psalm 72:12–14 — In Genesis 1, God makes people in His image and gives them the task of ruling over the living things of the earth. In Genesis 2, the first man is put in the garden to work it and to take care of it. In Psalm 72, the ideal king rescues the poor who cry for help, the weak and those who have no helper, and he cares about their lives.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does it mean to ‘rule’ over creation? What do you think God wants us to do for the animals of the earth?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Some people hear the word ‘rule’ and think of taking what they want. But the Bible puts it next to words like ‘work’ and ‘take care’, and the psalm describes a ruler who rescues the weak. How might that shape the way we treat vulnerable species? (Christians understand the word ‘rule’ in different ways, and many connect it to Jesus’ example of serving.)</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Sign a Kaitiaki Pledge: write ONE promise to care for a part of the natural world, and one small action you can start this week.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Awareness Campaign: Save the Asiatic Cheetah</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a poster or short advertisement script to raise awareness of the Asiatic cheetah. Include a clear message, two facts, and one thing people can do to help.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A clear slogan or headline</li><li>At least two accurate facts (for example how many remain, why it is in danger)</li><li>At least one action people can take</li><li>A picture that matches the message</li><li>I used careful, honest words (for example ‘only a few remain’ not ‘there are exactly …’)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the poster template with a slogan bank and two fact cards. Choose one action from the list.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own poster or script with two facts, one action and a strong slogan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design a three-part campaign (poster, slogan and social-media post) for different audiences, such as children, farmers and visitors, and explain how each is different.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Headline:</b> ‘Save the ___!’ <b>Facts:</b> The Asiatic cheetah is ___. It is in danger because ___. <b>Action:</b> You can help by … <b>Call to action:</b> Join us and …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Persuasive Letter to a Decision-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter to a person who can help a threatened species (for example a minister, a mayor, or the head of a conservation organisation). Ask for a specific action and support your request with evidence.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I addressed a real or imagined decision-maker by title</li><li>I opened with a clear request</li><li>I used at least three pieces of evidence</li><li>I explained what would happen if nothing is done</li><li>I ended politely, with a clear thank-you and a request for a reply</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the letter frame and the evidence cards. Choose three facts, then write sentences with your partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a complete letter with three pieces of evidence and a polite request.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a paragraph answering an objection (for example ‘it costs too much’) with a reasoned reply, and compare the cost with the value of the species.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Opening:</b> Dear ___, I am writing to ask you to … <b>Evidence:</b> First, … Second, … Third, … <b>If nothing is done:</b> Without help, the ___ could … <b>Closing:</b> Thank you for reading. I hope you will …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Data Detectives: Kākāpō Numbers</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the rounded kākāpō population table to draw a line graph, and then predict what might happen next. Explain what the graph shows and what could change it.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew labelled axes (years across, number of birds up)</li><li>I plotted all four points accurately</li><li>I joined the points and gave the graph a title</li><li>I wrote what the graph shows (‘The population has…’)</li><li>I made a prediction and gave a reason for it, using careful words such as ‘might’ or ‘probably’</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use pre-drawn axes with the scale marked. Plot the four points and complete the sentence frames.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw your own axes, plot, label and write a short explanation and prediction.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Work out the increase between each pair of years and compare the rates. Why might the rate change? What would you need to know to make a better prediction?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Rounded data (approximate; check the Department of Conservation website for the latest numbers):</b> 1995: about 50 · 2005: about 85 · 2015: about 125 · 2020: about 210 <b>Sentence frames:</b> Between ___ and ___ the population rose from ___ to ___. <b>Prediction:</b> I think the population might ___ because …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 1:26–28; Gen 2:15; Ps 72:12–14 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Genesis 1:26–28 has been understood in different ways: avoid presenting one reading as the only Christian view. The common thread to emphasise is care and responsibility. Say that kaitiakitanga is a Māori concept with its own meanings, and invite your Māori Education lead to explain it, rather than treating it as a synonym for ‘care’. Be matter-of-fact about hunting. Make sure students leave with hope: there are real success stories.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> New Zealand has some of the world’s most remarkable conservation stories: kākāpō, takahē, kiwi, and predator-free islands and sanctuaries (for example Zealandia). Our moa and huia are gone for ever. Invite DOC, a local conservation group, or your Māori Education lead to share how people in your rohe look after native species. Avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Extinction can feel sad or frightening. Present it as a reason to care and to act, not to panic. Keep hunting matter-of-fact. The Asiatic cheetah is a symbol of Iran: speak of conservation efforts by Iranian scientists and rangers with respect. Avoid giving exact counts for cheetahs or tigers that may change: say ‘only a few’ or ‘about’.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Silhouette cards for the Zoo of the Missing (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Endangered species data cards: Caspian tiger, Asiatic cheetah, Persian leopard, Caspian seal, moa, huia, kākāpō, takahē</li><li>Graph paper and rulers; the rounded kākāpō counts table (see Path C)</li><li>Persuasive letter planning sheet</li><li>Kaitiaki Pledge cards (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>The animal cards you have already collected: Caspian tiger, Asiatic cheetah, Persian leopard (look back at Weeks 2 and 3)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students define ‘extinct’ and ‘endangered’ without mixing them up?</li><li>Did their evidence show more than one cause for each species?</li><li>Which students ended with a realistic action, and which with a hope only?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Fire and Ruin: Light, Shadow and Hope":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fire and Ruin: Light, Shadow and Hope — Draw the darkness, and then draw the one small light.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Tone, Contrast and Light</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 8: Fire on the Mountain (334–330 BC) · Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to use tone and contrast to express feeling in a drawing.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use charcoal or chalk to build a range of tones from light to dark.</li><li>I can use light and dark together to make a scene dramatic.</li><li>I can explain the feeling my artwork shows.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Range and control of tone (the value strip compared with the final drawing); use of contrast to create a focal point; the artist’s statement (1–2 sentences) explaining the mood.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes marks in light and dark with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Uses at least three tones with some contrast and can name the feeling in the artwork.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls five or more tones, uses contrast deliberately to create a focal point, and explains the mood with art vocabulary.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines tone, silhouette and focal point with clear intent, explains how choices create meaning, and compares with other artworks.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>tone / value</b> — How light or dark something is.</li><li><b>value scale</b> — A strip that shows tone changing from white to black in steps.</li><li><b>contrast</b> — The difference between light and dark (or between colours) next to each other.</li><li><b>silhouette</b> — A solid dark shape seen against a lighter background.</li><li><b>focal point</b> — The part of the picture where the eye goes first.</li><li><b>smudge / blend</b> — To spread charcoal or chalk to make soft tones.</li><li><b>mood</b> — The feeling an artwork gives.</li><li><b>diptych</b> — An artwork made of two panels that go together.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>How Do You Draw Smoke?</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>It is dusk. The fires have sunk into red embers, and the ruined columns stand black against the sky. Shirin holds a charcoal stick in her fingers, and her hands are already grey. ‘Look carefully, Courier. How would you draw smoke? How would you draw firelight on a stone? An artist can make us feel a thing without saying a word. Try making the darkest mark you can, and then the lightest. How many different greys can you make in between?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday on mini-whiteboards: what does extinct mean; name one cause of species loss; name one species that was brought back by conservation.</li><li>Show 3–4 dramatic night paintings or photographs, including ruins at dusk. Ask: ‘How do you know it is night? Where is the light? What do you feel?’ Sensitivity: keep the images about ruins and light, not about violence (3 min).</li><li>Value scale challenge (5 min): students make a five-step strip from the lightest to the darkest using charcoal and tissue. Show that pressing hard and lightly make different tones.</li><li>Take two quick shares. Share LI/SC.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: what extinct means; one cause of species loss; one success story from conservation.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The darkest picture needs a spark of light. Learn how artists use tone, contrast and one small glow to tell a story of loss and hope.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Value scale (4 min): demonstrate on the board: lightest to darkest in five steps with chalk and charcoal. Students check their dispatch strips against it.</li><li>Contrast and focal point (4 min): show one dark picture with a single bright spot. Ask: ‘Where did your eye go first? Why?’ Cover the light and ask again.</li><li>Silhouettes (3 min): cut a column silhouette from black paper and hold it against an orange tissue sky. Students describe the mood.</li><li>Techniques (4 min): demonstrate smudging, lifting with an eraser, and drawing with the side of the charcoal. Remind students that charcoal is dusty: wipe hands, and keep it away from clothes and faces.</li><li>Hope in the dark (2 min): ask students to decide where their ‘one small light’ will go. Model the first step of each Path.</li><li><b>A scale of tones:</b> <b>Tone</b> (or <b>value</b>) means how light or dark something is. A <b>value scale</b> goes from white, through greys, to black. Using many steps makes a drawing look solid and real, while using only two makes it look flat. Charcoal is perfect for this: press lightly for pale tones, harder for deep ones, and smudge to blend.</li><li><b>Contrast and drama:</b> <b>Contrast</b> is the difference between light and dark that sit side by side. The stronger the contrast, the more dramatic the picture. A bright ember beside a black shadow shines, but the same ember in a bright field would not. Artists place the strongest contrast where they want you to look: the <b>focal point</b>.</li><li><b>Silhouettes:</b> A <b>silhouette</b> is a dark shape against a lighter background, so you can recognise it from the outline alone. Tall columns with bull-headed tops (like those at Persepolis), a broken doorway, a camel and a rider can all be powerful silhouettes against a glowing sky.</li><li><b>Drawing fire, smoke and ruin:</b> To draw <b>smoke</b>: smudge soft, curling shapes and lift out lighter areas with an eraser. To draw <b>firelight</b>: put the lightest tones right next to the darkest. For <b>ruins</b>: use broken, uneven lines and strong shapes. The ruin shows loss. The glow shows warmth. And the people or lamps in the drawing remind us that someone is still here.</li><li><b>The one small light:</b> Many artists add a <b>small spark of hope</b> to a dark scene: a lamp in a window, a green shoot in the ashes, a single star. It is small, but because the surroundings are dark, it becomes the focal point. This week our Courier carries a small lamp out of the fire. Where would you put yours?</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Art Words for Light and Dark</b> — Match each art word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Building a Charcoal Night Scene</b> — Put the steps in a sensible order.</li><li><b>sort: Drama or Calm?</b> — Sort each choice by the mood it usually creates.</li><li><b>reveal: How Do Artists Do It?</b> — Tap each card to see an artist’s trick.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Charcoal and Chalk Night Scene:</b> Draw a night scene of ruined columns under a smoky sky. Use charcoal for the darks and chalk for the lights, build at least five tones, and add one small light as your focal point.</li><li><b>Path B — Mixed-Media Collage: Ruins in Fire and Gold:</b> Make a collage of ruined columns in black paper against a glowing sky of orange and red tissue, with a touch of gold paint as your small light.</li><li><b>Path C — Ruins to Restoration Diptych:</b> Make a two-panel artwork: on the left, a ruined scene in dark tones; on the right, the same scene imagined as it was, or as it could be rebuilt, with warmer light. The two halves must clearly belong together.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Isaiah 61:3; Psalm 126 — Isaiah promises that God will give a crown of beauty in place of ashes, the oil of joy in place of mourning, and a garment of praise in place of a faint spirit. Psalm 126 recalls a time when God brought His people home, and says that those who sow in tears will reap with songs of joy.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What does ‘beauty for ashes’ mean? Can something beautiful come out of something broken?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Art can mourn and it can hope, and often it does both. Think of your drawing: does the darkness take away the light, or does it help the light to shine? Is it honest to show the dark parts of a story? Is it also honest to show hope?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a one-line hope statement to go with your artwork, such as ‘Even in the dark, ___.’ Share it with a partner if you wish.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Charcoal and Chalk Night Scene</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a night scene of ruined columns under a smoky sky. Use charcoal for the darks and chalk for the lights, build at least five tones, and add one small light as your focal point.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My drawing has at least five different tones</li><li>The strongest contrast is at my focal point</li><li>The columns are drawn as strong silhouettes</li><li>I used smudging for smoke and an eraser for highlights</li><li>I added one small light of hope</li><li>I can explain the mood in one sentence</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-toned page with a light pencil outline of the columns. Work with your value strip beside you, and use the artist’s statement frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with five tones, a focal point and an artist’s statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw two light sources (for example fire and moon) and show how each lights the stone differently. Explain how contrast controls the mood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Tone the whole page with soft grey. 2 Draw the horizon and the shapes of the columns lightly. 3 Fill the columns and the ground with deep black. 4 Smudge the smoke. 5 Lift out highlights and add chalk glow near the horizon. 6 Add the small light. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> My picture feels ___ because I used ___. The small light shows ___.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Mixed-Media Collage: Ruins in Fire and Gold</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a collage of ruined columns in black paper against a glowing sky of orange and red tissue, with a touch of gold paint as your small light.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Black paper silhouettes in a clear, strong shape</li><li>Layered tissue in at least three colours for the sky</li><li>Gold used sparingly as a focal point</li><li>The layers overlap in a pleasing way</li><li>I can explain my choice of colours and shapes</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use pre-cut column templates and pre-torn tissue. Choose where to glue and add your gold.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create the collage independently, with an artist’s statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second layer of texture (for example rubbing charcoal over the tissue, or pressing lines into the gold) and explain how texture adds to the mood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Tear and layer tissue on a white page, light at the horizon and darker at the top. 2 Cut or tear the black silhouettes. 3 Glue them over the sky. 4 Add one touch of gold. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I chose ___ for the sky because … The gold shows …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Ruins to Restoration Diptych</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a two-panel artwork: on the left, a ruined scene in dark tones; on the right, the same scene imagined as it was, or as it could be rebuilt, with warmer light. The two halves must clearly belong together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Both panels show the same scene from the same viewpoint</li><li>The left panel is dark and the right panel has more light</li><li>The composition matches across the two panels (the same landmarks)</li><li>I used contrast in both panels</li><li>I can explain what changes from left to right, and what stays the same</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline of the scene on both panels and focus on the tones. Complete the statement frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw both panels independently with matching composition and an artist’s statement.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a middle panel showing the moment of change, and explain how the three panels tell a story of hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> 1 Choose the scene (columns, a gate, a garden). 2 Sketch the outline lightly on both panels. 3 Use dark charcoal and strong contrast on the left. 4 Use lighter tones, chalk and a touch of colour on the right. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> The left shows ___ and the right shows ___. The thing that stays the same is …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Isa 61:3; Ps 126 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the tone gentle: students may be thinking about a real loss in their lives. Offer them the choice of drawing a ‘ruin’ that is just stone and shadow. Say that many faiths and cultures use light as a symbol of hope, including Zoroastrian, Jewish, Muslim and Christian traditions, without claiming they mean the same thing. Psalm 126 is a song of the exiles’ return, so link back to Weeks 2 and 6.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Artists in Aotearoa have made powerful works about loss and hope, for example after Napier’s 1931 earthquake or in Canterbury after 2010–2011. Waiata, whakairo and other art forms also help communities to remember and to heal. Invite a local artist or your Māori Education lead to share, and take care with whānau who may have lived through these events. Do not imitate sacred or tapu designs.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Ruin images must not be violent or graphic. Some students may connect a burning city with real fears (for example a house fire, a war on the news, or a refugee experience), so allow them to choose a gentler scene. Persepolis is a living heritage site for Iranian people: draw it as a place of beauty and memory, not just as a ruin. Be careful with fixative (teacher use only) and ventilation.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Charcoal sticks and compressed charcoal, white chalk or pastel, soft erasers, tissue for smudging</li><li>Grey or off-white sugar paper; black paper</li><li>Orange and red tissue paper, gold paint, glue sticks, scissors</li><li>Images of dramatic night scenes and ruins (app or printed), including photos of Persepolis columns at dusk</li><li>Value-scale strip templates (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Fixative (teacher use only) or hairspray for the finished work; aprons and wet wipes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students use more than three tones and can they say where the strongest contrast is?</li><li>Who needed a gentler subject, and how did we respond?</li><li>How did the artist’s statements show students linking mood and technique?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Fire on the Mountain":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Fire on the Mountain — Three lives each. Hold on to one another.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 8: Fire on the Mountain (334–330 BC) · Granicus, Issus, Gaugamela, Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our learning about Alexander and the fall of the empire, rebuilding, conservation and tone, and to support our Caravan when things go wrong.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the fall of the empire, cities, endangered animals and art.</li><li>I can use what I learned in earlier weeks (sources, Cyrus, the Royal Road, Xerxes, Esther).</li><li>I can support my Caravan when we lose a life, and explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Lives Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately, including some from earlier weeks.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong, applies the ideas to new situations, and shows how sources and causes connect.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations, and connects ideas across several weeks.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Fire on the Mountain</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Smoke is still rising over the terrace, but the wind has begun to turn. ‘Every Caravan has three lives today,’ says Shirin, and her voice is steady. ‘You will lose some. That is how the fire works. But a Caravan that holds together can survive what a single runner cannot. Write your Creed, Courier. How will you cling to one another? How will you cling to hope?’ Far off, a small lamp flickers on the ash. The Showdown begins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Lives Creed on a strip and reads it aloud.</li><li>Teachers may display the Creeds beside those from Weeks 1 and 3.</li><li>Remind students that in Lives mode a wrong answer costs one life, and that teams can talk and encourage each other. Losing a life is part of the game, not a punishment.</li><li>Before starting, remind students of the recall areas: Alexander and the fall of the empire, rebuilding cities, endangered animals, charcoal and tone, and the earlier weeks.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q8, Q10, Q11) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Lives Creed: ONE sentence about how you will hold on to each other and to hope when your team loses a life. Starters: When we lose a life, we will… / We will cling to each other by… / Even in the smoke, our Caravan will… / We will never blame…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Daniel 2:44; Psalm 46:1–3; Isaiah 61:3; Genesis 2:15 — This week we met a God who rules over the rise and fall of kingdoms, who is a refuge when the ground shakes, who gives beauty in place of ashes, and who puts people in the garden to take care of it.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What did you notice this week about hope in hard times?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which idea made you think hardest: an empire that fell, a city that was rebuilt, an animal that is almost gone, or a small light in the dark?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Lives Creed together. Pray for each other, for people who are rebuilding after hard times, and for the journey ahead.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Herodotus wrote about Persia, but he was a Greek. What should a good historian remember (Week 1)? <b>A) He may have his own point of view, so we compare him with other sources ✔</b> · B) Greek writers never wrote about Persia · C) He was Persian · D) His writing is always perfectly neutral <em>Every source has a point of view. Herodotus, Arrian and Plutarch were all Greek writers, so historians compare them with other evidence such as archaeology.</em> [SS-DO]</li><li>2. Which king let exiled peoples, including the Jews, return home (Week 2)? A) Darius III · <b>B) Cyrus ✔</b> · C) Xerxes · D) Alexander <em>Cyrus the Great allowed exiled peoples to return to their homelands, as in Ezra 1.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>3. The Royal Road ran from Sardis to which Persian city (Week 3)? A) Babylon · B) Jerusalem · C) Athens · <b>D) Susa ✔</b> <em>The Royal Road ran about 2,700 km from Sardis in the west to Susa in south-west Iran, with relay stations along the way.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>4. In 480 BC, Xerxes’ army burned which Greek city (Week 4)? <b>A) Athens ✔</b> · B) Corinth · C) Sparta · D) Troy <em>Xerxes’ army burned Athens in 480 BC. Greek writers say that this was later given as a reason for the burning of Persepolis, about 150 years later.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>5. In the book of Esther, Esther became queen in the palace city of… (Week 5) A) Memphis · <b>B) Susa ✔</b> · C) Sardis · D) Rome <em>Esther became queen at Susa, a royal city of the Persian kings.</em> [RE]</li><li>6. The last king of the Achaemenid Persian Empire was… <b>A) Darius III ✔</b> · B) Xerxes · C) Cyrus · D) Darius I <em>Darius III ruled from about 336 BC until he was killed in 330 BC. Darius I (Week 3) had ruled nearly two centuries earlier.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>7. Which battle in 331 BC opened the road to Babylon, Susa and Persepolis? A) Marathon · B) Thermopylae · C) Salamis · <b>D) Gaugamela ✔</b> <em>At Gaugamela in 331 BC Alexander defeated Darius III, and the road to the great Persian cities was open. Marathon, Salamis and Thermopylae were earlier battles in the Greek–Persian wars.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>8. Ancient writers disagree about why Persepolis burned in 330 BC. What does a good historian do? A) Only believe the Persian account · B) Say that nothing happened · C) Pick the most exciting story · <b>D) Compare the accounts, check the archaeology, and say how sure we can be ✔</b> <em>Some accounts say revenge, some say a drunken accident, and some modern historians say a deliberate political act. The burned layers show that there was a fire but not why. No Persian account survives, so we should say what we know and what we do not.</em> [SS-DO] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>9. Which famous Egyptian port city did Alexander begin to build in 331 BC? <b>A) Alexandria ✔</b> · B) Memphis · C) Thebes · D) Luxor <em>Alexandria was founded in 331 BC beside the Nile delta. Its harbour and position helped it grow into one of the great cities of the ancient world.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Which best explains how Alexander’s army defeated the much larger Persian Empire? A) A much bigger army · <b>B) Several causes together: tactics, leadership, command problems and distance ✔</b> · C) Luck only · D) Magic <em>Historians suggest several causes: Alexander’s tactics and bold leadership, the difficulty of commanding a huge army of many peoples, the size of the empire and the surrender of some cities, and chance. No single cause tells the whole story.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. The Caspian tiger is… A) Common in Iran today · B) A farm animal · C) Critically endangered with a few left · <b>D) Extinct (gone by about the 1970s) ✔</b> <em>The Caspian tiger is extinct. Hunting, loss of its forest habitat and loss of its prey all played a part. The Asiatic cheetah is critically endangered, and in Aotearoa the kākāpō has been brought back by intensive conservation, so we know that action can help.</em> [SC-LW-Eco] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — In a dark artwork, why might an artist add one small bright light, to match the idea of ‘beauty for ashes’ in Isaiah 61? A) It is just decoration · <b>B) It draws the eye and shows hope still alive in the middle of loss ✔</b> · C) It pretends that nothing sad happened · D) It hides the ruins completely <em>Strong contrast makes the small light the focal point. Art can mourn what was lost and still hold on to hope, which is the heart of Isaiah’s promise of beauty in place of ashes.</em> [VA-DI]</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (for example ruins and conservation, both about what we choose to protect) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for next week’s Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Dan 2:44; Ps 46:1–3; Isa 61:3; Gen 2:15 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and gentle, and end with hope. Some students may be carrying real sadness about loss, so celebrate how the Caravan held together, not just who won. Offer a quiet option for any student who needs it.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week: whose stories of the past we hear; Napier and Canterbury rebuilds; kākāpō, takahē and the lost moa and huia; art that holds both loss and hope.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown, and in Lives mode losing a life is part of the game. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings or about running out of lives, so offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. Check in with any student who has found this week’s themes (fire, earthquakes, extinction) difficult.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching on Monday (a single cause for the fall of the empire, or ‘extinct’ vs ‘endangered’)?</li><li>What will I change next week?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 9: Star-Watchers & Silk":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: Parthians & Sasanians: Persia Rises Again":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Parthians &amp; Sasanians: Persia Rises Again — The empire fell to Alexander. How did Persia stand up again?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 9: Star-Watchers &amp; Silk (247 BC – 651 AD) · Nisa, Ctesiphon, Gur (Firuzabad), the Silk Road, the Persian Gulf and the Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe how Persian empires rose again after Alexander and what made them powerful.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can put the Parthians (about 247 BC–224 AD) and the Sasanians (224–651 AD) in order on a timeline.</li><li>I can explain the ‘Parthian shot’.</li><li>I can compare the Parthians and Sasanians with the Achaemenids (Cyrus, Darius, Xerxes).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A completed comparison (chart, infographic or comic); a spoken or written ‘because’ sentence comparing two empires; notes from the Council Fire discussion about text versus tradition.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names the Parthians or the Sasanians with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Places the Parthians and Sasanians in order and explains the Parthian shot accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Compares all three Persian empires with specific facts and explains why the Parthians were powerful.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates whose voice we hear for each empire and how that shapes what we know; applies the idea to another culture.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Parthians</b> — A people from north-east Iran who built a large empire from about 247 BC to 224 AD.</li><li><b>Sasanians</b> — The Persian dynasty that ruled from 224 AD until about 651 AD; their capital was Ctesiphon.</li><li><b>Achaemenids</b> — The family of Cyrus, Darius and Xerxes, who ruled the first Persian Empire.</li><li><b>horse archer</b> — A warrior who shoots a bow while riding a horse.</li><li><b>Parthian shot</b> — Turning in the saddle to shoot backwards while appearing to retreat.</li><li><b>dynasty</b> — A line of rulers from the same family.</li><li><b>middleman</b> — A person or people who stand between buyers and sellers and earn money from the trade.</li><li><b>Church of the East</b> — An early Christian community in Persian lands that spread eastwards along the trade routes.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Parthian Shot Demo</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Under the first stars, a rider on a pale horse gallops across the plain, and then, as he rides away, he turns in the saddle and a single arrow flashes back over his horse’s tail. Shirin catches her breath. ‘The Parthians called that retreating. The Romans called it a nightmare. You think the rider is running away, Courier, and then the arrow comes. Why is that so hard to defend against?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: lay a masking-tape line across the room as the ‘horizon’. Have soft foam arrows ready. Remind students of safe rules: no aiming at faces, only at a target.</li><li>Retrieval (2 min): three whiteboard questions from Stage 8 (who defeated the Persian Empire in 330 BC; what did Alexander’s generals do after he died; one thing the Greeks learned from Persia).</li><li>Demonstrate (3 min): a volunteer ‘retreats’ along the tape, turns, and tosses a foam arrow at a target behind them. Ask the class to predict: ‘What does the enemy think is happening?’</li><li>Ride-and-turn sketch (3 min): pairs sketch stick-figure frames of a rider (1 retreating, 2 turning, 3 shooting). Add the caption ‘Why it works’.</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria; students read them aloud. Say that scholars date the start of Parthian power to about 247 BC, but the exact year is debated.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Stage 8: Alexander ended the Achaemenid Empire in 330 BC; his generals divided the lands (the Seleucids ruled much of Persia); the Greeks and Persians influenced each other’s art and ideas.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>After Alexander, who ruled the Persian lands? Meet two great dynasties: the horse-archers of Parthia and the builders of Sasanian Persia.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Timeline (4 min): on the floor strip, add 330 BC Alexander → about 247 BC Parthians → 53 BC Carrhae → 224 AD Sasanians → 260 AD Valerian → 651 AD end of Sasanian rule. Mark the Achaemenids 559–330 BC at the left.</li><li>Parthians (4 min): show the map, point to Nisa, then trace the Silk Road. Ask: ‘Why would sitting in the middle make a people rich and powerful?’ Name Carrhae and the Parthian shot again. Add: ‘The Parthian side of the story is mostly told by outsiders, so we say “probably” and “according to”.’</li><li>Sasanians (4 min): contrast Ardashir (new dynasty, 224 AD) with Cyrus (559 BC). Note Shapur and Valerian; say that both sides carved or wrote their own versions.</li><li>Faith and learning (3 min): name Zoroastrian, Jewish and Christian communities in the empire; mention Gundeshapur. Keep neutral and respectful about the end of the Sasanian era in 651 AD: ‘A new chapter began, and Persian culture continued.’ Invite, never require, students to share family connections.</li><li><b>After Alexander: the Seleucids:</b> When Alexander died in 323 BC, his generals split the empire. One of them, <b>Seleucus</b>, ruled much of old Persia, and his family are called the <b>Seleucids</b>. They built Greek-style cities, but many local people kept their own customs. A Persian-speaking people from the north-east, the <b>Parni</b>, began to push back.</li><li><b>The Parthians (about 247 BC–224 AD):</b> The <b>Parthians</b> rose from north-east Iran. Their early royal centre was <b>Nisa</b>, near today’s Ashgabat. They were famous <b>horse archers</b>, and their <b>Parthian shot</b> (turning to shoot backwards while riding away) shocked their enemies. In <b>53 BC</b> a Parthian army under a general called Surena defeated a larger Roman army at <b>Carrhae</b>, and the Roman leader Crassus was killed. Historians guess the Parthians held power for nearly 500 years, but we know less about them than the Achaemenids, because few of their own records survive.</li><li><b>Silk Road middlemen:</b> The Parthian lands lay between <b>China</b> and <b>Rome</b>. Silk, spices and other goods passed through their cities, and Parthian kings taxed the traders and sometimes sold goods on. That made them rich and gave them a say in who could trade with whom. Romans and Chinese often learned about each other through Parthian eyes.</li><li><b>The Sasanians (224–651 AD):</b> About <b>224 AD</b> a ruler from Fars (the old Persian homeland) called <b>Ardashir I</b> defeated the last Parthian king and founded the <b>Sasanian</b> dynasty. He saw himself as reviving Persia’s older glory. His capital area was <b>Ctesiphon</b> on the Tigris. His son <b>Shapur I</b> fought the Romans and, in <b>260 AD</b>, captured the Roman emperor <b>Valerian</b>. Shapur recorded it in great rock carvings, which are Persian propaganda as well as history. Roman writers told the story differently.</li><li><b>Learning and faith:</b> The Sasanian kings supported <b>Zoroastrianism</b> as the state religion, but their empire also had Jews, Christians and others. The <b>Church of the East</b> grew in Persian lands and sent teachers east along the trade routes. At <b>Gundeshapur</b>, a centre of learning, scholars from Persia, Greece, India and Syria studied medicine and other subjects. By about <b>651 AD</b> Sasanian rule ended after Arab armies arrived, and Iran slowly became a mostly Muslim land, with a rich culture that kept its Persian language and traditions.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: From Alexander to the Arabs</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>sort: Parthians or Sasanians?</b> — Sort each statement into the empire it belongs to.</li><li><b>match: Words from the Day</b> — Match each term to its meaning.</li><li><b>reveal: Whose Voice Do We Hear?</b> — Tap each card to see whose voice a source gives us, and what it leaves out.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Three Empires Chart:</b> Make a chart comparing the Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires. Fill in dates, capital, famous rulers, strengths, and one way each was different from the others.</li><li><b>Path B — Rome vs Persia Infographic:</b> Design an infographic comparing Rome and Parthia/Sasanian Persia: where they were, how they fought, one famous battle, and what they traded.</li><li><b>Path C — Comic: The Parthian Shot at Carrhae:</b> Draw a six-frame comic of the battle of Carrhae from a Parthian soldier’s point of view. Keep to what historians are fairly sure of: the Parthians used horse archers, the Romans were defeated, and Crassus died.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Matt 2:1–12; Acts 2:9–11 — Matthew tells how wise men, called magi, came from the east to Jerusalem asking where the child born king of the Jews was, because they had seen his star. They found Jesus, bowed down and gave him gifts. In Acts, on the day of Pentecost, the Jewish pilgrims in Jerusalem included people from Parthia, Media and Elam, who heard the good news about God’s deeds in their own languages.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Why might God invite star-watchers from the east to meet Jesus so early? What does it say about God’s care for people far from home?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Parthians, Medes and Elamites are named in Acts 2, so people from the old Persian lands were among the first to hear. Matthew does not say how many magi there were, what their names were, or that they were kings. What do we know from the text, and what has tradition added over time?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write about one person from another culture whom you want to honour and learn from this term. What could you ask them, or thank them for?</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Three Empires Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a chart comparing the Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires. Fill in dates, capital, famous rulers, strengths, and one way each was different from the others.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote the dates for all three empires</li><li>I named a capital or royal centre for each</li><li>I named at least one ruler for each</li><li>I listed a strength of each empire</li><li>I wrote one clear way they were different, with a ‘because’</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the chart with half the boxes pre-filled; choose words from a word bank (horse archers · satraps · Silk Road · Ctesiphon · Persepolis).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fill in the whole chart in your own words, with dates and a ‘because’ sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘source’ row: whose voice do we hear for each empire? Explain which empire we know least about from its own words and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Columns:</b> Achaemenids | Parthians | Sasanians. <b>Rows:</b> Dates · Capital · Rulers · How they stayed strong · Religion/culture · A source we have. <b>Say it:</b> The Parthians were different from the Achaemenids because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Rome vs Persia Infographic</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design an infographic comparing Rome and Parthia/Sasanian Persia: where they were, how they fought, one famous battle, and what they traded.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I included a small map with both empires</li><li>I named one battle (Carrhae or Valerian)</li><li>I explained one way each side fought</li><li>I included one trade link (the Silk Road)</li><li>I used at least two icons or pictures and short captions</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a template with the boxes and a picture bank; fill in four sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create the infographic independently, with accurate facts and short captions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘Whose version?’ strip, comparing how a Roman writer and a Persian rock carving might describe Shapur and Valerian differently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Layout:</b> Title · Map · Two columns (Rome | Persia) · Battle box · Trade box. <b>Frame:</b> At Carrhae, the Parthians used … so the Romans … <b>Say it:</b> Both empires needed … but they …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Comic: The Parthian Shot at Carrhae</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a six-frame comic of the battle of Carrhae from a Parthian soldier’s point of view. Keep to what historians are fairly sure of: the Parthians used horse archers, the Romans were defeated, and Crassus died.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Six frames in order</li><li>I showed horse archers and the Parthian shot</li><li>I showed the Roman army (infantry) in the heat and open ground</li><li>Captions are clear and accurate</li><li>I added a note about what we do NOT know</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the comic template with three captions provided; draw and add the rest.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all six captions yourself and include one new vocabulary word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a final ‘historian’s note’ frame saying which details come from Roman writers and why that might matter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Frames:</b> 1 The armies meet · 2 Arrows rain down · 3 The Romans form tight blocks · 4 The riders retreat and turn · 5 Rome is defeated · 6 What happened next. <b>Caption frame:</b> The Parthians … so the Romans …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Matt 2:1–12; Acts 2:9–11 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Matthew says ‘magi’ (Greek magoi) from ‘the east’. Scholars think they were learned men, perhaps priests or astronomers, probably from Persian or Babylonian lands, but the text does not say. Gently separate Matthew from later tradition (three kings, names, camels, a stable scene). Never say that magi were ‘Zoroastrian’ as a fixed fact; say ‘some scholars think they may have been connected with the Persian priests called magi’. Invite students from Zoroastrian, Muslim, Jewish or Iranian backgrounds only if they wish to share.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Aotearoa also has stories told by outsiders and by those who lived here. How do we balance accounts written by visitors with the voices of tangata whenua? (Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Treat Parthian and Sasanian Persia as part of a proud Iranian heritage, not as a footnote or a villain. Rome and Persia both had faults and strengths. Be sensitive to Muslim, Jewish, Christian and Zoroastrian-background students when discussing faith in the Sasanian era, and describe the end of Sasanian rule neutrally. The Valerian carvings and Carrhae accounts are one-sided sources, so say so.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Foam arrows and a tape line for the demo</li><li>Three-empires timeline strip (app or printed)</li><li>Map of Rome, Parthia and Sasanian Persia</li><li>Three Empires Chart template (Path A)</li><li>Comic template (6 frames) for Path C</li><li>Scripture cards for Council Fire (Matt 2:1–12; Acts 2:9–11)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain the Parthian shot in their own words?</li><li>Who placed the three empires in the right order without help?</li><li>What do I need to revisit tomorrow about the Silk Road before we start mapping?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Silk Road Cities, Ctesiphon & the Round City":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Silk Road Cities, Ctesiphon &amp; the Round City — Why do cities grow where roads cross, and how do you plan one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Cities &amp; Trade Routes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 9: Star-Watchers &amp; Silk (247 BC – 651 AD) · Nisa, Ctesiphon, Gur (Firuzabad), the Silk Road, the Persian Gulf and the Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-EW — Social Sciences: The Economic World. Students understand trade, specialisation and exchange, and how resources are used.</li><li>MA-G — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Geometry &amp; Measurement. Students use symmetry, scale, direction and distance.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to explain how crossroads cities grow and how cities were planned.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can plot a Silk Road route on a map.</li><li>I can explain why Ctesiphon was a major city.</li><li>I can draw a concentric circular city plan with a compass.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy of the compass work (concentric circles, even spokes); a map with conventions; a short written reason for why cities grow at crossroads.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Draws circles or places cities with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Draws accurate concentric circles and places Silk Road cities on a map with a key.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how crossroads, rivers and trade created cities, and explains Ctesiphon’s and Gur’s features.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates a circular plan against another design and explains trade-offs, using geographic reasoning.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Silk Road</b> — A network of trade routes (not one road) joining East Asia, Central Asia, Persia and the Mediterranean.</li><li><b>caravan</b> — A group of traders and animals travelling together.</li><li><b>caravanserai</b> — A roadside inn where caravans and their animals could rest.</li><li><b>crossroads</b> — A place where routes meet or cross.</li><li><b>Ctesiphon</b> — A great Parthian and Sasanian city on the Tigris River.</li><li><b>Taq Kasra</b> — A huge brick arch at Ctesiphon, part of a Sasanian palace.</li><li><b>concentric</b> — Circles that share the same centre.</li><li><b>city plan</b> — A map of how a city’s streets and buildings are arranged.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Compass Challenge: A City in a Circle</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The masked rider’s trail leads to a dry hilltop where you can see a ring of old foundations in the dust. Shirin hands out compasses: ‘A king once drew a whole city with one of these, Courier. Draw a circle, then another inside it, then another. Why would anyone plan a city as a circle?’ Far off, a small bell. A caravan is coming.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — what is the Parthian shot; when did the Sasanians begin; name one way the Sasanians differed from the Parthians.</li><li>Compass skills (3 min): model holding the compass at the top, keeping the needle point still, and swinging the pencil. Everyone draws three concentric circles on scrap paper.</li><li>Think–pair–share (3 min): ‘Why choose a circle for a city?’ Accept ideas: easy to defend, same distance from the centre to the walls, a clear centre for the ruler, symbolic order. Say that we are guessing from the shape and not from a written plan.</li><li>Share LI/SC; students read them aloud.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: the Parthian shot; Parthians (about 247 BC–224 AD) and Sasanians (224–651 AD); how the Sasanians differed from the Parthians.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Follow the caravan along the Silk Road, past a great arch on the Tigris, and into a round city drawn by a king.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Silk Road (5 min): place pins at Rome, Antioch, Ctesiphon, Merv, Samarkand and Chang’an on the large map, and run a string through them. Ask: ‘Which city sits in the middle? Why is the middle powerful?’ Make clear that the string is only one possible route.</li><li>Goods and ideas (3 min): hold up goods cards. Students decide whether each travelled east or west. Silk and paper travelled west, glass and horses and metalwork in various directions.</li><li>Ctesiphon (3 min): show the Taq Kasra photograph. Ask what a 37-metre arch tells us about the king’s power. Gently say that part of the palace collapsed in a flood in the 1880s.</li><li>Gur (4 min): show the aerial plan. Model the compass method: centre point, then circles at different radii. Draw spokes from the centre with a ruler and mark gates.</li><li><b>The Silk Road: many roads, not one:</b> The <b>Silk Road</b> was not a single road but a <b>network</b> of paths that linked <b>China</b>, <b>Central Asia</b>, <b>Persia</b> and the <b>Mediterranean</b>. Traders rarely travelled the whole way. Goods passed from one caravan to the next, through many hands. <b>Silk</b>, <b>spices</b>, <b>glass</b>, <b>paper</b>, <b>jade</b> and <b>horses</b> all travelled, and so did <b>ideas</b>, <b>religions</b> and <b>inventions</b>. The Parthians controlled important routes through Persia.</li><li><b>Crossroads and rivers build cities:</b> Cities grow where <b>routes meet</b>, where <b>rivers</b> give water and travel, and where <b>fertile land</b> can feed many people. A crossroads brings customers, news and wealth. A river gives water, food and boats. When several of these come together, a city can grow very large.</li><li><b>Ctesiphon on the Tigris:</b> <b>Ctesiphon</b> stood on the <b>Tigris River</b> (in modern Iraq), next to the Greek-style city of Seleucia. Parthian and then Sasanian kings used it as a winter capital. Its great landmark, the <b>Taq Kasra</b> (often called the Arch of Ctesiphon), is a huge brick arch about <b>37 metres</b> high, and it is often linked to the Sasanian king <b>Khosrow I</b> in the 500s AD, though historians are not sure of the exact date. It is the world’s largest single-span brick vault that survives.</li><li><b>Gur: a king’s round city:</b> In the south-west of Iran near today’s <b>Firuzabad</b>, the first Sasanian king <b>Ardashir I</b> built a <b>circular city</b> called <b>Gur</b> (also known as Ardashir-Khwarrah), about <b>2 km across</b>. It had a <b>wall</b> and <b>moat</b>, <b>roads</b> running out from a <b>centre</b>, and a tall monument at the heart. Archaeologists use aerial photographs and digs to study it. The round plan may have reflected ideas about order and kingship, but we cannot be sure.</li><li><b>Caravan hubs:</b> Between the big cities were <b>caravanserais</b>, roadside inns with a courtyard, a well and room for animals. Camels carried heavy loads, and caravans moved in groups for safety. Some cities, like <b>Merv</b> and <b>Hamadan</b>, grew rich as hubs. Today’s Silk Road towns often still show these older patterns.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Silk Road Goods and Places</b> — Match each item to where it came from or what it was.</li><li><b>order: Drawing a Round City</b> — Put the steps for drawing a circular city plan in order.</li><li><b>sort: Why Cities Grow</b> — Sort each feature: does it help a city grow, or hold it back?</li><li><b>reveal: Cities Along the Road</b> — Tap each city to see what made it important.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Circular City Plan:</b> Plan a circular city of your own with at least three concentric zones: a centre, a middle ring and an outer ring with a wall and four gates. Label what goes in each zone and explain your choices.</li><li><b>Path B — Silk Road Journey Map:</b> Draw a map of a Silk Road route with at least six city hubs, a compass rose, a scale bar and a key. Add goods that travelled between cities.</li><li><b>Path C — Postcard from Ctesiphon:</b> Create a postcard from a traveller arriving in Ctesiphon. On one side, draw a small map or picture. On the other, write about what you saw, heard and smelled, and why the city was busy.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Matt 5:14–16; Isa 60:1–3 — Jesus says that his followers are the light of the world, and that a city set on a hill cannot be hidden. No one lights a lamp and then hides it, but puts it on a stand so that everyone in the house can see. Isaiah calls God’s people to rise and shine, because God’s light has come, and he says that nations will come to that light.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> A city on a hill cannot be hidden. What does that say about the way we live at our school and in our community?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Light attracts travellers, like the star that drew the magi from far away. Isaiah says that nations will come to the light. Is light a bright show, or something that helps others see? What would ‘light’ look like in the playground?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write or draw one practical way you can be ‘light’ at school this week, such as including someone, telling the truth, or helping without being asked.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Circular City Plan</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Plan a circular city of your own with at least three concentric zones: a centre, a middle ring and an outer ring with a wall and four gates. Label what goes in each zone and explain your choices.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew three or more concentric circles with a compass</li><li>I marked a centre and gave it a purpose</li><li>I drew roads out from the centre to gates in the wall</li><li>I labelled what each ring is for</li><li>I wrote one sentence explaining why I chose a circle</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Start with the centre and one circle drawn; use a zone word bank (palace · market · homes · wall · gate).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label your own plan with at least four zones and a clear explanation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a scale (1 cm = 200 m) and calculate the real distance from centre to gate. Compare strengths and weaknesses of a circle city with a grid city.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Mark the centre. 2 Compass: radius 2 cm, 4 cm, 6 cm. 3 Ruler: spokes at 90° (4 gates). 4 Label zones: centre (palace/market), middle (homes), outer (farms/stores). <b>Say it:</b> I chose the centre for … because … The wall has … gates because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Silk Road Journey Map</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a map of a Silk Road route with at least six city hubs, a compass rose, a scale bar and a key. Add goods that travelled between cities.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title, compass rose, scale bar and key</li><li>Six or more labelled hubs from the Mediterranean to China</li><li>A coloured route line</li><li>Three trade goods with arrows showing direction</li><li>A note about one city and why it was important</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use an outline map with the route faintly printed; add labels and three goods.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw independently with all map conventions, six hubs and three goods.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second route (a sea route through the Gulf) and compare the two for speed, risk and cost.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>City bank:</b> Antioch · Ctesiphon · Hamadan (Ecbatana) · Merv · Samarkand · Kashgar · Chang’an. <b>Goods bank:</b> silk · paper · spices · glass · horses · jade. <b>Say it:</b> ___ was a hub because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Postcard from Ctesiphon</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a postcard from a traveller arriving in Ctesiphon. On one side, draw a small map or picture. On the other, write about what you saw, heard and smelled, and why the city was busy.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Picture or map shows the Tigris and the great arch</li><li>I wrote 5–6 sentences as a traveller</li><li>I included two geographic reasons the city grew</li><li>I used at least two vocabulary words</li><li>My writing feels like a real person speaking</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the postcard template with sentence starters and a picture bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write the postcard independently, with a drawn map on the front.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two postcards, one from a Roman trader and one from a Chinese merchant, and compare what each notices.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Start:</b> Dear ___, I have finally reached Ctesiphon, beside the river Tigris … <b>Include:</b> what you saw · who you met · what the market sold · why so many roads lead here. <b>Sign-off:</b> Until we meet again, ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Matt 5:14–16; Isa 60:1–3 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Both passages use the picture of light for God’s people. Keep the discussion about character and kindness, not about showing off. Explain that Isaiah 60 was first written for Jerusalem, and that Christians see it echoed in Matthew’s story of the magi. Keep the tone open: ‘Christians read this passage in different ways.’ Make sure students from other faiths feel respected and not targeted.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Where do roads and rivers meet in your region, and how did that shape your nearest town? Compare with the way early tracks and waterways (ara and awa) linked communities in Aotearoa. (Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to share local stories; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Silk Road carried horses, goods and also ideas and beliefs, including Buddhism, Zoroastrianism, Christianity and later Islam. Treat all faiths with respect and avoid ranking them. Ctesiphon and Gur are in present-day Iraq and Iran, so keep modern politics out of the lesson.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large map from Rome to China; string and coloured pins</li><li>Compasses, rulers, protractors and A3 paper</li><li>Silk Road goods cards (silk, spices, glass, horses, paper, jade)</li><li>Pictures of Ctesiphon (Taq Kasra) and the round plan of Gur (Firuzabad)</li><li>Postcard templates (Path C)</li><li>Teacher’s sample compass city on the board</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students use a compass to make accurate concentric circles?</li><li>Who explained why a crossroads or a river helps a city grow?</li><li>What do I need to preview for tomorrow’s coast and mangroves?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Gulf & Caspian: Mangroves, Pearls and Sea Creatures":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gulf &amp; Caspian: Mangroves, Pearls and Sea Creatures — What lives where the land meets the sea?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Coastal &amp; Marine Communities</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 9: Star-Watchers &amp; Silk (247 BC – 651 AD) · Nisa, Ctesiphon, Gur (Firuzabad), the Silk Road, the Persian Gulf and the Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to describe coastal habitats and explain why mangroves matter.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe a mangrove community and its parts.</li><li>I can explain what a nursery habitat is.</li><li>I can compare a mangrove with a New Zealand coast and name one way we look after it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Accuracy and labelling of the cross-section or food web; use of the vocabulary (nursery habitat, zonation, endemic); a short explanation of why mangroves matter.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one coastal animal or plant with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Describes a mangrove community and explains ‘nursery habitat’ using some vocabulary.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how mangroves and other parts of the coast depend on each other and compares two coasts accurately.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Predicts what would change if mangroves or seagrass were lost and evaluates ways to protect the coast.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>mangrove</b> — A salt-tolerant tree that grows in muddy shallow water on warm coasts.</li><li><b>estuary</b> — The place where a river meets the sea, with a mix of fresh and salt water.</li><li><b>nursery habitat</b> — A safe place where young animals live and grow before moving elsewhere.</li><li><b>zonation</b> — Bands of different living things at different heights or depths on a shore.</li><li><b>nacre</b> — The shiny layers (mother-of-pearl) that an oyster makes; pearls are made of nacre.</li><li><b>endemic</b> — Living naturally in one place and nowhere else.</li><li><b>threatened</b> — At risk of dying out in the wild.</li><li><b>kaitiakitanga</b> — Guardianship and care of the environment (a Māori concept).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Pearl Mystery</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The caravan has reached the shining waters of the Gulf, and the divers show Shirin a little grey oyster. She holds a perfect pearl up to a lantern. ‘This was once a speck of sand in an oyster’s soft body,’ she says. ‘Who put it there, and how did it turn into this?’ Gandom the camel sniffs the oyster and sneezes. She has an opinion, but it is wrong.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — what is a crossroads; why did Ctesiphon grow; what is a concentric circle?</li><li>Pearl Mystery (4 min): hand each team an envelope with a grain of sand, a nacre shell piece and a pearl picture. Teams guess the order of what happens: ‘What does the oyster do when something irritates it?’</li><li>Reveal (3 min): irritant gets inside → oyster coats it with nacre → layer upon layer builds up over years → a pearl forms. Real pearls take years to form. Mention that Gulf pearl diving is a very old tradition, and that people now mostly culture pearls, which are grown on purpose.</li><li>Share LI/SC; students tick off vocabulary as it appears.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: crossroads and rivers help cities grow; Ctesiphon was on the Tigris with the great arch; concentric circles share a centre.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Along the warm Gulf and the cool Caspian, the shore is full of life. Meet the mangroves, the sea-cows and the shy seals.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mangroves (5 min): show photographs. Students point out the breathing roots. Ask: ‘Why would a baby fish choose to hide here?’ Define nursery habitat. Build a quick food web on the board: mangrove leaves → small creatures → fish → bird.</li><li>Animals (4 min): show dugong, hawksbill, flamingo and Caspian seal. Use the Animal Cards (this week: dugong and Caspian seal). Say plainly that these are threatened and why we care, without scaring students.</li><li>Zonation (3 min): draw a three-band shore cross-section. Ask students to predict which band has the toughest conditions.</li><li>Compare with Aotearoa (3 min): our northern harbours have mānawa (mangroves), but the Canterbury coast does not; show an estuary (such as Ihutai, the Avon-Heathcote Estuary) with salt marsh and mudflats instead. Invite local guidance on how your rohe looks after kaimoana.</li><li><b>Hara mangroves: a living nursery:</b> On the Persian Gulf coast of Iran, near <b>Qeshm Island</b>, the <b>Hara mangrove forest</b> grows in salty, muddy water. The trees (<i>Avicennia marina</i>, called grey mangrove) have <b>roots that rise above the mud</b> to breathe, and they can handle salt. The tangled roots shelter <b>young fish, crabs and prawns</b>, so mangroves are a <b>nursery habitat</b>. They also slow waves and help protect the coast from erosion.</li><li><b>Dugongs, turtles, pearl oysters and flamingos:</b> The shallow Gulf is home to <b>dugongs</b>, gentle sea mammals that graze on seagrass (sometimes called sea-cows, and relatives of manatees, not whales). <b>Hawksbill turtles</b> visit the sandy shores, and <b>pearl oysters</b> live on rocky seabeds. <b>Flamingos</b> wade in the mudflats and eat small creatures. Many of these animals depend on the same coastline and seagrass beds.</li><li><b>The Caspian Sea:</b> North of Iran lies the <b>Caspian Sea</b>, the world’s largest inland body of water (it is a huge lake, though people call it a sea). It is less salty than the ocean. The <b>Caspian seal</b> lives only here, so it is <b>endemic</b>, and it is endangered. The Caspian is also famous for its <b>sturgeon</b>, large fish whose eggs are sold as caviar. Overfishing has made many sturgeon species critically endangered.</li><li><b>Zonation: bands along the shore:</b> On a coast, living things sit in bands. At the <b>top of the shore</b>, only tough things survive long dry spells. In the <b>middle</b> (the tidal zone) animals and plants cope with being covered and uncovered twice a day. <b>Lower down</b>, there is more water and a greater variety of life. Mangroves sit in the middle, where mud floods and drains with the tides.</li><li><b>Threats and care:</b> Mangroves, seagrass and sea creatures are threatened by <b>pollution</b>, <b>overfishing</b>, <b>building on the coast</b> and <b>rubbish</b> in the water. People in Iran and around the world protect them with reserves (Hara is a UNESCO biosphere reserve), local rules and education. Scientists track numbers and share what they find.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>animalCards</li><li><b>match: Coastal Words</b> — Match each word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: How a Pearl Forms</b> — Put the steps of pearl formation in order.</li><li><b>sort: Gulf or Caspian?</b> — Sort each animal or fact by the sea where you find it.</li><li><b>reveal: Why Mangroves Matter</b> — Tap each card to find a reason mangroves are important.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Coastal Zone Cross-Section:</b> Draw a labelled cross-section of a mangrove coast from the open sea to dry land. Show at least three zones and at least four living things, and label one adaptation.</li><li><b>Path B — Mangrove Food Web:</b> Draw a food web for a mangrove community with at least eight living things, and use arrows for the flow of energy. Highlight the nursery role.</li><li><b>Path C — NZ Coast Compare:</b> Compare a mangrove coast with an estuary or rocky shore in Aotearoa (use a local one if you can). Use a Venn diagram and add one practice of kaitiakitanga (such as caring for kaimoana) that you have learned from local guidance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Ps 8:3–8; Ps 148:7; Job 12:7–10 — The psalmist looks at the heavens, the moon and the stars that God has set in place, and wonders why God cares about human beings, then notes that people have been given the care of creatures on the land, in the air and in the sea. Another psalm calls on the sea creatures and all the depths to praise God. In Job, we are told to ask the animals and the birds, and the fish of the sea, and they will teach us that God holds every living thing in his hand.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What do sea creatures teach us about God’s creativity? What could a mangrove root or a dugong teach us?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Observation can be a kind of worship: looking carefully, naming things and being amazed. How is a scientist’s careful looking similar to praise? How does caring for creatures follow from the idea that they belong to God?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write or draw one thing you will do to care for local water, such as picking up rubbish, saving water or learning about your local awa or coast.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Coastal Zone Cross-Section</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a labelled cross-section of a mangrove coast from the open sea to dry land. Show at least three zones and at least four living things, and label one adaptation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Clear cross-section with sea, mangroves and land</li><li>Three labelled zones</li><li>Four living things drawn and labelled</li><li>One adaptation labelled (such as breathing roots)</li><li>I used the words habitat, zonation and nursery</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn outline with the zones marked; add labels from a word bank.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently, with an adaptation and a nursery label.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add tide levels (high and low) and explain how conditions change in each zone through the day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Zones:</b> Open water (seagrass, dugong) · Mangrove belt (crabs, young fish) · Mud flats (flamingo) · Dry land. <b>Say it:</b> The mangrove roots help by … Young fish live in the roots because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Mangrove Food Web</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Draw a food web for a mangrove community with at least eight living things, and use arrows for the flow of energy. Highlight the nursery role.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>At least eight living things</li><li>Arrows point from food to eater</li><li>One producer, one consumer and one decomposer labelled</li><li>Young fish or crabs marked as nursery animals</li><li>I wrote what might happen if mangroves were cleared</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use cut-out pictures and arrows to place on a template, then write two sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw your own web, label roles and write a prediction.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a human link (fishers, pearl divers) and show how people depend on the web.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Chain start:</b> mangrove leaf → crab → fish → heron. <b>Say it:</b> If the mangroves disappeared, the young fish would … so the birds would …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: NZ Coast Compare</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Compare a mangrove coast with an estuary or rocky shore in Aotearoa (use a local one if you can). Use a Venn diagram and add one practice of kaitiakitanga (such as caring for kaimoana) that you have learned from local guidance.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Venn diagram with at least three things in each section</li><li>I compared at least one animal and one plant</li><li>I named a similarity (for example nursery habitat)</li><li>I described one way people care for the coast</li><li>I credited where my local knowledge came from</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made Venn and a picture bank; add three words in each section.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete the Venn independently and write two sentences on care.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a short persuasive paragraph on why people should protect one habitat, using evidence from both coasts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Venn:</b> Only Gulf mangrove | Both | Only NZ shore. <b>Say it:</b> Both coasts … but only … <b>Care:</b> People look after our coast by … (ask your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Ps 8:3–8; Ps 148:7; Job 12:7–10 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Psalms and Job are poems and wisdom writing, not science textbooks. Science explains how living things fit their habitat; the poems praise the one who made them and call people to care. Keep the focus on wonder and care. Be honest that Christians differ on some questions about creation, and avoid debate about origins. Invite students of all faiths to share ideas of caring for nature.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare the Gulf mangroves with Aotearoa’s mānawa (found in warm northern harbours) and your local estuary or rocky shore. How do whānau and communities care for kaimoana and the coast? (Invite local iwi/hapū or your Māori Education lead to guide; avoid pan-Māori generalisations, and follow local rāhui and guidance.)</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> The Caspian seal and sturgeon are endangered; present this as a reason to care and not as something to panic over. Pearl diving was hard and sometimes dangerous work, so speak about divers with respect. Do not suggest that ancient peoples ‘ruined’ the sea; many modern pressures are new.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pictures of Hara mangroves, dugong, hawksbill turtle, Caspian seal, flamingos, sturgeon</li><li>Pearl Mystery envelopes (a grain of sand, a pearl, nacre shell piece)</li><li>Cross-section templates (coastal zones)</li><li>Food-web arrow cards</li><li>NZ estuary and rocky-shore pictures (use your own local ones if you can)</li><li>Animal Cards (dugong and Caspian seal unlock this week)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students explain a nursery habitat in their own words?</li><li>Did the food web show energy flowing the right way?</li><li>What do I need to prepare for tomorrow’s night-sky painting?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: Star-Gazers’ Night: Painting the Journey of the Magi":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Star-Gazers’ Night: Painting the Journey of the Magi — Paint the dark so the stars can shine.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — Night Sky &amp; Silver</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 9: Star-Watchers &amp; Silk (247 BC – 651 AD) · Nisa, Ctesiphon, Gur (Firuzabad), the Silk Road, the Persian Gulf and the Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>VA-PK — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Practical Knowledge. Students explore and use art-making conventions, processes and procedures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to paint atmosphere with watercolour resist and silhouette.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use wax resist to keep stars and shapes bright.</li><li>I can build a night gradient from deep blue to a glowing horizon.</li><li>I can design a silhouette caravan with a clear horizon line.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Technique: a clear wax resist, a smooth gradient, and a silhouette against a horizon; the artist’s statement; a short praise line from Council Fire.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Makes a night picture with support and some resist.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Uses wax resist and a night gradient with a silhouette caravan and a simple statement.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Controls resist, gradient and composition with care, and explains choices about colour and horizon.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Combines techniques with clear intent, compares with another art tradition and explains how atmosphere is created.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>resist</b> — A technique where wax or oil pastel repels paint, so the drawn lines stay clear.</li><li><b>wash</b> — A thin layer of watercolour spread smoothly over paper.</li><li><b>wet-on-wet</b> — Painting onto already damp paper so colours blend softly.</li><li><b>gradient</b> — A gradual change from one colour or tone to another.</li><li><b>silhouette</b> — A dark shape seen against a lighter background, with no inside detail.</li><li><b>horizon</b> — The line where land (or sea) meets the sky.</li><li><b>composition</b> — How the parts of a picture are arranged.</li><li><b>roundel</b> — A round decorative design, often framed with a border.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Look at the Night Sky</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The stars are out over the dunes. Shirin hands you a black sheet of paper and a stub of chalk. ‘Painters have always struggled with the night,’ she whispers. ‘It is dark, but it is full of light. How do you paint a star, Courier? How do you show that the sky goes on forever?’ From somewhere far away comes the sound of a bell: a caravan, walking by starlight.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — what is a nursery habitat; name a Gulf animal and a Caspian animal; what is nacre?</li><li>Look (4 min): show two or three night-sky paintings or photographs. Ask: ‘Where is the brightest part? Where is the darkest? How can we tell the sky is huge?’</li><li>Test (3 min): on scrap paper, students draw a star with white wax, then wash over it with watercolour. They see the resist happen.</li><li>Share LI/SC; remind students to keep wax and wet paint away from clothes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: nursery habitat; dugong and hawksbill (Gulf), Caspian seal (Caspian); nacre is the shiny layer that forms pearls.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The night sky is the painter’s challenge: dark and bright together. Learn the secrets of resist, gradient and silhouette.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Resist demo (4 min): draw a star with white wax on dark paper. Paint over it live. Stress that the wax must be pressed on firmly.</li><li>Gradient demo (4 min): dampen paper with a sponge, then brush the three colours and watch them blend. Use a clean brush to soften the joins.</li><li>Silhouette (3 min): cut a camel shape from black paper and place it on the horizon. Ask: ‘What happens to the picture if all shapes are the same size?’</li><li>Choose a path (4 min): show a quick example of each. Remind students to take care of tools and keep wet areas away from finished work.</li><li><b>Wax resist: hide the stars:</b> Wax and oil pastel <b>repel</b> water. Draw your stars and shapes in <b>white or gold wax first</b>. When you paint a watercolour wash over the top, the paint slides off the wax, and the stars shine through. It feels like magic, but it is simple science: wax and water do not mix.</li><li><b>The night gradient:</b> A night sky is not one flat colour. It is often <b>darkest at the top</b> and <b>lighter near the horizon</b>. Wet the paper, then brush <b>indigo</b> at the top, <b>blue</b> and <b>purple</b> in the middle, and a touch of <b>warm orange or pink</b> near the bottom. Let the colours blend. This is called <b>wet-on-wet</b>.</li><li><b>Silhouette and horizon:</b> A <b>silhouette</b> is a solid dark shape with no inside detail. Camels, riders, tents and palm trees make strong silhouettes. Place them along a low <b>horizon</b> to make the sky feel large. Overlap some shapes and vary their sizes to show distance.</li><li><b>Stars and composition:</b> Stars can be dots, small crosses, or <b>star shapes with four, six or eight points</b>. Make some big and bright, others tiny. Try to group some in clusters, like <b>constellations</b>. Keep the biggest star away from the exact centre, so the eye travels around the picture.</li><li><b>Sasanian silver:</b> Sasanian artists were famous for <b>silver plates and bowls</b> showing kings hunting, riders on horseback and animals in <b>circular frames</b>. Many have a figure in profile and a patterned border. They were made by hammering, casting and engraving. Collectors later carried these plates across Asia.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Art Words</b> — Match each art word to its meaning.</li><li><b>order: Wax Resist Night Sky</b> — Put the steps for making a wax resist painting in order.</li><li><b>sort: Night Scene: Does It Work?</b> — Sort each choice by whether it makes a night scene stronger or weaker.</li><li><b>reveal: Text or Tradition?</b> — Tap each card to see what Matthew says and what tradition added.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Watercolour Resist Night Sky:</b> Create a night scene using wax resist stars and a graded wash, with a silhouette caravan along a low horizon.</li><li><b>Path B — Sasanian-Style Silver Roundel:</b> Design a silver roundel inspired by Sasanian plates: a rider or an animal in profile inside a circle, with a patterned border. Use foil and a blunt pencil to emboss the design.</li><li><b>Path C — Star-Watcher’s Chart:</b> Design a decorative star wheel (a circular star chart) with a title, at least two constellation shapes you invent, and a decorated border. Label it as a ‘watcher’s chart’.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Num 24:17; Matt 2:2, 10–11; Ps 19:1 — In Numbers, a prophecy from outside Israel says that a star will come out of Jacob and a ruler will rise from Israel. In Matthew, the magi say they have seen his star rising and have come to worship him. When they see the star again they are filled with joy, they go into the house, and they bow down and offer him gifts. The psalmist sings that the heavens tell of God’s glory and the skies announce what his hands have made.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> The heavens declare God’s glory, and the magi followed a sign that led them to Jesus. Where does creation point us?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Matthew says ‘magi’. He does not give their names, the number of them, or say they were kings, and he says they found a child in a house. What do paintings and carols add that is not in the text? Is that a problem, or can we enjoy tradition while knowing what the Bible says?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a short praise line or a thank-you to God for the night sky, in your own words.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Watercolour Resist Night Sky</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Create a night scene using wax resist stars and a graded wash, with a silhouette caravan along a low horizon.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew stars and a moon or bright star in wax first</li><li>I painted a graded wash (dark at the top, lighter near the horizon)</li><li>I added a silhouette caravan (at least three figures or animals)</li><li>The horizon is low and the sky is big</li><li>I wrote a one-sentence artist’s statement</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn horizon line and a caravan template to trace; practise the resist on scrap first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own composition with a graded wash and a layered caravan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second light source (such as a campfire glow) and explain how it changes the colours around it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Wax stars and constellations. 2 Wet the paper. 3 Paint the gradient. 4 Let it dry. 5 Paint or cut the caravan silhouette. <b>Artist’s statement:</b> I used wax resist to … My caravan is travelling … because …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Sasanian-Style Silver Roundel</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a silver roundel inspired by Sasanian plates: a rider or an animal in profile inside a circle, with a patterned border. Use foil and a blunt pencil to emboss the design.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I drew a circle with a compass and added a border</li><li>My animal or rider is in profile</li><li>I designed a repeating border pattern</li><li>I embossed clearly (not tearing the foil)</li><li>I can explain what makes my roundel look ‘Sasanian-inspired’</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Trace a given animal outline and add a simple border pattern.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design and emboss an original rider or animal with a repeating border.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second metal layer (gold card) to form a raised centre, and explain how this changes the design.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Sketch on paper first. 2 Place the foil over a soft mat. 3 Trace the design with a blunt pencil. 4 Add border beads or leaves. 5 Mount on card. <b>Statement:</b> My roundel shows … in profile. The border repeats … to make rhythm.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Star-Watcher’s Chart</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a decorative star wheel (a circular star chart) with a title, at least two constellation shapes you invent, and a decorated border. Label it as a ‘watcher’s chart’.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A circle drawn with a compass</li><li>At least two invented constellations joined by lines</li><li>Stars drawn with different sizes</li><li>A decorated border or compass points (N, E, S, W)</li><li>A short caption explaining my favourite constellation</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a circle template with a few stars pre-placed; join and decorate.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create the whole chart with two or more constellations and a border.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a second ring showing the four seasons or compass directions, and research one real constellation to include (use Matariki, if you wish, with local guidance).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Steps:</b> 1 Compass circle. 2 Scatter stars lightly in pencil. 3 Join some into shapes (an animal, a tent, a camel). 4 Colour in gold or silver on dark paper. 5 Add a border. <b>Caption:</b> My constellation is called ___ because it looks like ___.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Num 24:17; Matt 2:2, 10–11; Ps 19:1 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Num 24:17 is a prophecy given through Balaam, a non-Israelite seer, and many Christians see it connected with Matt 2. The Bible says ‘magi’, not ‘three kings’, and names no names. Tradition added the numbers, the names and the crowns later. Be gentle: students may love the traditional nativity scenes, and the aim is not to spoil them. Say, ‘Christians love the story, and we can enjoy both the text and the traditions.’ Avoid claiming that the star was a particular comet or planet alignment, since scholars disagree.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare with Māori star knowledge: Matariki, and the way stars guided Pacific navigators. These are living traditions and carry deep meaning, so invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to guide, and avoid copying sacred designs or making general claims about all Māori.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Persian silverwork comes from a living culture and should be honoured and not mocked. Avoid caricatures of ‘Eastern wise men’. Do not draw the Magi as a stereotype. Offer alternatives to the nativity scene if some students prefer not to depict religious figures, such as the star and caravan alone.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dark blue or black heavy paper or watercolour paper</li><li>White, yellow and gold wax or oil pastels; candles (optional)</li><li>Watercolour paints (indigo, deep blue, purple, orange), large brushes, sponges, water pots</li><li>Foil, thin foam or card, blunt pencils (Path B)</li><li>Pictures of Sasanian silver plates (hunting kings, animals) and night-sky photographs</li><li>Star charts, circle templates, rulers (Path C)</li><li>Gandom’s caravan silhouette templates (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did students manage to keep the resist clear under the wash?</li><li>Who made strong silhouettes and a believable horizon?</li><li>What will I do to prepare students for tomorrow’s Showdown?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: Starlight Navigation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: Starlight Navigation — Follow the stars. Win the Stage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 9: Star-Watchers &amp; Silk (247 BC – 651 AD) · Nisa, Ctesiphon, Gur (Firuzabad), the Silk Road, the Persian Gulf and the Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply our week’s learning about the Parthians, Sasanians, Silk Road, coasts and the Magi, together with earlier weeks, and to work as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions about the Parthians and Sasanians, the Silk Road, coastal habitats and Matthew 2.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan, steering by the stars, with integrity.</li><li>I can explain my thinking when I get something wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Starlight Creed.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and applies the idea to new situations.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions with clear decoys and explanations.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Starlight Navigation</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Night is deep, the lamps are out, and the only guide is the sky. ‘A star does not shout, Courier,’ whispers Shirin. ‘It simply shines, and travellers who look up find their way. Write your Creed. How will your Caravan steer when the road is dark?’ Behind you, hoofbeats slow and stop. The Showdown begins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Starlight Creed on a strip and reads it aloud.</li><li>Teachers may display the Creeds beside the earlier ones.</li><li>Explain the Starlight idea: before each question, a Caravan may choose one star (safer), two stars (medium) or three stars (riskier, bigger reward). Use it as a talking point about courage and wisdom.</li><li>Remind students that several questions recall earlier stages: Elam and the Medes, Cyrus, Darius, Xerxes and Esther.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q2, Q4, Q12) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the quiz, each Caravan writes a Starlight Creed: ONE sentence about how your team will navigate through hard questions together, even in the dark. Starters: When we are lost, we will… / We will be a light by… / Our Caravan will never… / We will cheer on…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log:</b> Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for the final Showdown bank.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster:</b> Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Matt 2:1–12; Num 24:17; Matt 5:14–16; Ps 19:1 — This week we met star-watchers from the east who followed a sign to a child, a star promised long ago, a city on a hill that cannot be hidden, and heavens that tell of God’s glory.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What was the most wonderful thing you noticed this week about the sky, the sea or people from other lands?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Which of this week’s ideas made you think hardest: the Parthian shot, the Silk Road, mangroves, or what the Bible does and does not say about the magi?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Say your Starlight Creed together. Pray for each other and for the last stage of the journey.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. The ‘Parthian shot’ was a way of… <b>A) shooting backwards while riding away ✔</b> · B) playing a drum · C) minting a coin · D) making cheese <em>Parthian horse archers turned in the saddle and shot backwards while appearing to retreat. It surprised their enemies.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. The Parthians defeated the Romans at the battle of Carrhae in… A) 5 AD · <b>B) 53 BC ✔</b> · C) 53 AD · D) 530 BC <em>Carrhae was in 53 BC. The Parthians used horse archers against the Roman army. Most of what we read about it comes from Roman writers, so we remember that it is one side of the story.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>3. The Sasanian dynasty began, under Ardashir I, in about… A) 1000 AD · B) 247 BC · <b>C) 224 AD ✔</b> · D) 330 BC <em>Ardashir I founded the Sasanian dynasty about 224 AD, after defeating the last Parthian king. It lasted until about 651 AD.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>4. In Matthew 2, the visitors from the east who came to worship Jesus are called… <b>A) magi ✔</b> · B) fishermen · C) shepherds · D) soldiers <em>Matthew calls them magi (learned men from the east). He does not say they were kings or that there were three.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>5. Acts 2:9 lists Parthians, Medes and Elamites among the people in Jerusalem. Which two of those did we meet in Week 1? A) Romans and Greeks · <b>B) Medes and Elamites ✔</b> · C) Vikings and Franks · D) Parthians and Mongols <em>The Elamites (Susa) and the Medes (Ecbatana) were two of the peoples of ancient Iran we met in Week 1. They appear again in Acts 2.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>6. Mangroves are important because they… A) make a lot of noise · <b>B) shelter young fish and protect the coast ✔</b> · C) turn into deserts · D) are mines <em>Mangrove roots give young fish, crabs and prawns a safe nursery and slow waves, which helps protect the shore.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>7. The Silk Road was… A) one single straight road · B) a kind of fabric · C) a board game · <b>D) a network of trade routes ✔</b> <em>The Silk Road was a web of routes, not one road. Goods and ideas passed through many hands on the way between East and West.</em> [SS-EW]</li><li>8. Which Persian king, in Week 3, improved the Royal Road from Sardis to Susa and organised the empire into provinces? A) Ardashir · <b>B) Darius ✔</b> · C) Alexander · D) Shapur <em>Darius I (522–486 BC) organised provinces under satraps, introduced the daric and used the Royal Road with relay stations.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>9. In Week 2 we learned that Cyrus the Great is remembered for… A) inventing paper · <b>B) allowing exiled peoples, including many Jews, to return home ✔</b> · C) building the Taq Kasra · D) burning Athens <em>Ezra 1 and the Cyrus Cylinder (read carefully) show Cyrus allowing exiled peoples to go home and rebuild their temples.</em> [RE]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Matthew 5:14 says Jesus’ followers are… A) a caravan · B) a rock · <b>C) a city on a hill, a light ✔</b> · D) a camel <em>Jesus says his followers are the light of the world, like a city on a hill that cannot be hidden. He means that how they live can help others see God.</em> [RE]</li><li>11. Esther became queen in Susa under King Xerxes (Ahasuerus), the same king who had led an army to attack… A) Egypt · <b>B) Greece ✔</b> · C) China · D) Rome <em>Xerxes invaded Greece (Weeks 4 and 5), and later Esther became his queen in Susa. The Greek and Persian stories come from different sources.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — What does the Bible say about the magi? A) They were Roman soldiers · <b>B) They came from the east to worship Jesus ✔</b> · C) They were three kings with names · D) They rode only camels <em>Matthew says that magi came from the east to worship Jesus. The number, names, crowns and camels were added by later tradition.</em> [RE] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Reflect on your week: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I wrote 3 things I now know</li><li>I wrote 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought (what I learned about God or people)</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use sentence starters and draw a picture instead of writing one of the answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all three sections in full sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two subjects (for example the Silk Road and mangroves, both about ‘meeting places’) in one paragraph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I now know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This week I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys for the final Showdown bank.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked that the answer is correct</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame; write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that needs reasoning, not just recall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Explain the week’s big idea to a younger child using a comic or poster.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 2 pictures</li><li>My comic/poster is neat and clear</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions provided to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic/poster.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘quiz the reader’ question at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Matt 2:1–12; Num 24:17; Matt 5:14–16; Ps 19:1 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep this short and joyful; celebrate teamwork and effort, not only winning. Remind students that one stage is left.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to the NZ connections of the week: voices of visitors and tangata whenua; roads, rivers and ara; mānawa and our local coast; Matariki and Pacific navigation (with local guidance).</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Winning is not the point of the Showdown: keep celebrating effort. Some students may feel anxious about the three-star risk choice, so let teams decide together and say that any choice is fine. Some students may feel anxious about public rankings; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconception needs re-teaching (for example ‘three kings’)?</li><li>What will I change for the final stage?</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Week 10: The Gate of All Nations":{"label":"Day","children":{"Monday — History: What Persia Gave the World":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">What Persia Gave the World — What did a vanished empire leave in your kitchen, your cupboard and your language?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (History)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">History — Legacy</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 10: The Gate of All Nations (legacy &amp; today) · The Gate at Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li><li>EN-W — English: Writing. Students write for a purpose, organising ideas and using language features.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Inquiry &amp; curiosity; Respect; Community &amp; participation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to identify Persian contributions to our world and to judge how important they are.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name five Persian legacies.</li><li>I can explain one legacy in detail and say how we know.</li><li>I can choose a legacy to showcase and say why it matters.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The legacy explanation (oral or written), the museum label or booklet page, and notes from the Council Fire talk.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one or two legacies with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Names five legacies and explains one in detail with a source.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains how a legacy spread and uses hedge words accurately where scholars disagree.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Judges the importance of different legacies with reasons, and compares a Persian legacy with one from another culture.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>legacy</b> — Something handed down from people of the past that still matters today.</li><li><b>influence</b> — The power to change or shape something else, such as language, ideas or art.</li><li><b>loanword</b> — A word one language borrows from another (for example ‘bazaar’ in English).</li><li><b>origin</b> — Where something first came from. Historians often say ‘probably’ because origins can be hard to prove.</li><li><b>qanat</b> — An underground sloping tunnel that carries water from the mountains to a village.</li><li><b>windcatcher</b> — A tall tower on a building that catches breezes and draws cool air down inside.</li><li><b>epic</b> — A very long poem that tells the story of heroes and a people’s past.</li><li><b>algorithm</b> — A set of steps for solving a problem. The word comes from the name of the scholar al-Khwarizmi.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Mystery Bag</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Gate is a day’s ride away, and Shirin has pulled a heavy cloth bag from her saddle. ‘Reach in, Courier. No peeking! Feel it, smell it, shake it. Every single thing in this bag has a Persian story. Before the week is out you will be able to say what.’ Gandom the camel pokes her nose in first. She chews a pistachio shell and looks very pleased with herself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>BEFORE CLASS: fill the bag with 6–8 items (pistachios in a shell, a few saffron threads in a closed jar, a chess piece, a tulip bulb, pyjamas or a label, a spinach leaf, a lilac or jasmine photo). Check allergies: pistachio is a nut, so use a photo or a sealed packet if any student has a nut allergy.</li><li>Read Shirin’s dispatch aloud (1 min). Pass the bag round the Caravans: each student feels ONE item without looking and whispers a guess to their team (4 min).</li><li>Reveal the items one by one. Ask: ‘Which of these is a thing we eat? Wear? Play? Grow?’ Take quick answers (3 min).</li><li>Do not tell the Persian links yet. Say: ‘By the end of today you will know why each of these is in the bag.’</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria (students read them chorally) (2 min).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Quick link to the whole journey: ‘On a mini-whiteboard, write ONE thing Persia is famous for from any week.’ Collect three answers and say: ‘Today we turn those into legacies.’</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A legacy is something handed down. The Persian Empire ended long ago, but look around: Persia is still in our gardens, our words and our games.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Link the bag (4 min): hold up each item and give its Persian link (pistachios and saffron are crops long grown in Iran; chess/shatranj; tulip bulb and tulip’s link to a Persian word for turban, through Turkish; pyjamas from a Persian word for leg-garment; spinach).</li><li>Legacy cards (6 min): show the first three discovery cards on the big screen. Ask: ‘Which of these legacies do you use or see?’ Keep the language careful: ‘Persia helped spread…’, ‘scholars think…’.</li><li>Words (3 min): read the word list aloud and ask students to hum the beat of each syllable. Play ‘Persian or not?’ with 4 words on the mini-whiteboards.</li><li>Judging (2 min): ask students to pick their top legacy and rate it for importance (1–5) on a whiteboard. Hear one reason from each of three students.</li><li>Invite Iranian-NZ students to share ONLY if they wish. Never ask a child to speak for a whole culture.</li><li><b>Gardens and the word ‘paradise’:</b> The Persians loved walled gardens with water, shade and fruit trees. Their word for such a garden, <b>pairidaeza</b>, became our word <b>paradise</b>. The garden at <b>Pasargadae</b> is among the oldest known planned gardens, and UNESCO lists ‘The Persian Garden’ as World Heritage. Many later cultures, in many lands, copied the idea of a garden as a peaceful, watered place.</li><li><b>Qanats, coins and roads:</b> <b>Qanats</b> brought water underground to dry lands, and the idea spread widely and some still work today. The gold <b>daric</b> helped trade and payment across a huge empire. The <b>Royal Road</b> and its relay stations were an organised system of messages that later empires admired and copied. Many societies built roads and post systems, so we say Persia <i>helped to spread</i> these ideas, not that it invented them all.</li><li><b>Carpets, windcatchers, chess and polo:</b> Persian <b>carpets</b> are famous. The oldest almost complete knotted carpet, the <b>Pazyryk carpet</b> (found in Siberia, about 2,400 years old), may have been made in the Persian world, though scholars disagree. <b>Windcatchers</b> cool houses in desert cities such as Yazd. <b>Chess</b> reached Persia from India and became <i>shatranj</i>. <b>Polo</b> was played in Persia for many centuries, and its true beginnings are debated.</li><li><b>Stories and scholars:</b> The <b>Shahnameh</b> (‘Book of Kings’), a Persian epic poem of about 50,000 couplets, was finished by the poet <b>Ferdowsi</b> about the year 1010 AD. Later Persian-speaking scholars shaped mathematics, medicine and poetry. The word <b>algorithm</b> comes from the name of <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>, a scholar from the Persian-speaking world who worked in Baghdad about 1,200 years ago. Many peoples, including Arabs, Turks and Indians, shared in the same golden age of learning.</li><li><b>Persian words in English:</b> English has borrowed many Persian words, sometimes by way of other languages: <b>paradise, caravan, bazaar, pyjamas, khaki, lilac, jasmine, spinach, magic, checkmate</b> and <b>algorithm</b> (from a name). Checkmate comes from <i>shah mat</i>, which most scholars take to mean ‘the king is helpless’. <i>Magic</i> comes from the <b>magi</b>, the Persian priests we met in Week 9. Words travel along trade routes, just as people do.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Persian Words in English</b> — Match each English word to the Persian idea behind it.</li><li><b>sort: Persian Legacy or Not?</b> — Is each item a legacy of Persia and its neighbours, or does it come from somewhere else?</li><li><b>order: A Legacy Timeline</b> — Put these events in the order they happened.</li><li><b>reveal: Where Did It Come From?</b> — Tap each card to see the Persian link and what scholars still debate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — “Persia Gives” Museum Exhibit:</b> Make an exhibit for the class museum: one object (or a drawing or model) and a museum label that tells visitors what it is, where it comes from and why it matters. These exhibits become part of Thursday’s Gallery Night.</li><li><b>Path B — Word Detectives Booklet:</b> Make a little booklet of Persian words used in English. For each word, give the word, the Persian source and a sentence of your own. Finish with a mystery word for a friend to guess.</li><li><b>Path C — Time-Capsule Letter:</b> Write a letter to Cyrus or Nehemiah (or both) from 2,500 years in the future. Say what you would thank them for, what has lasted and what has changed.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 12:2–3; Ps 78:4–7; Rev 7:9 — God promises Abraham that He will make him a blessing, and that through his family every people on earth will be blessed. In Psalm 78, the singer says that the stories of what God has done must be told to the next generation so that they will trust Him. In Revelation 7, John sees a huge crowd from every nation, tribe, people and language standing together before God.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What do you want to hand down to the next generation? What would you like people to say about the stories you tell and the things you make?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persia left gardens, words and roads to the world, and the Bible says God’s plan blesses every nation. How does a legacy of kindness compare with a legacy of buildings and coins? Which lasts longer?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a one-sentence legacy statement for yourself: ‘I want to be remembered for…’. Keep it in your Caravan Log.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: “Persia Gives” Museum Exhibit</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make an exhibit for the class museum: one object (or a drawing or model) and a museum label that tells visitors what it is, where it comes from and why it matters. These exhibits become part of Thursday’s Gallery Night.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I chose ONE Persian legacy</li><li>My object or drawing is clear and neat</li><li>My label has a title, a 2–3 sentence description and a ‘why it matters’ line</li><li>I used a hedge word where scholars disagree (‘probably’, ‘many scholars think’)</li><li>I named how we know (a source)</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose from the picture bank (garden, qanat, coin, chess piece). Use the label frame and complete each line with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write your own label with all five parts and a clear drawing or model.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write TWO labels for the same object: a short one for a child and a longer one for an adult, and say what you left out of the short one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Label frame:</b> Title: … <b>What it is:</b> This is a … <b>Where it comes from:</b> It probably began in … <b>Why it matters:</b> We still … <b>How we know:</b> We know from …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Word Detectives Booklet</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a little booklet of Persian words used in English. For each word, give the word, the Persian source and a sentence of your own. Finish with a mystery word for a friend to guess.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I included at least 6 words</li><li>For each word I wrote the Persian source (or ‘from the name of’)</li><li>I wrote my own sentence for each word</li><li>I drew a picture for at least 3 words</li><li>I wrote one mystery word clue</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the word bank (paradise, caravan, bazaar, pyjamas, jasmine, spinach) and the page frame. Draw a picture for each word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose your own six words from the list and write your own sentences and sources.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Group your words by theme (food, clothes, travel, ideas) and add a note about why trade routes spread words so far. Include one word where scholars disagree about the route it took.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Page frame:</b> Word: … <b>From Persian:</b> … which meant … <b>My sentence:</b> … <b>Mystery clue:</b> I am a … that … Who am I?</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Time-Capsule Letter</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write a letter to Cyrus or Nehemiah (or both) from 2,500 years in the future. Say what you would thank them for, what has lasted and what has changed.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I started with a greeting and said who I am and when I am writing</li><li>I named 3 things that have lasted (legacies)</li><li>I said thank you for one specific thing</li><li>I told them one thing that has changed</li><li>I ended with a hope for the future</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the letter frame and tell about two legacies. Read your letter aloud to a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a letter of three paragraphs with three legacies and a specific thank-you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a short reply from Cyrus or Nehemiah that shows what he cared about, using what we know from Scripture and other sources, and say which parts are your imagination.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Dear …</b> I am writing from the year … in Aotearoa New Zealand. <b>Thank you for…</b> <b>Still with us today…</b> <b>Changed…</b> <b>My hope is…</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 12:2–3; Ps 78:4–7; Rev 7:9 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Genesis 12:3 and Revelation 7:9 are Christian texts about God’s global plan and are not teaching that Persian culture is Christian. Present the legacies as achievements of real Persian and Iranian peoples. Students of other faiths may take part in the legacy-statement task without writing about God.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> What have people brought to Aotearoa that is now part of everyday life, and what was here first? Think of words (te reo Māori words in English), foods, games and ideas. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to share how local knowledge is handed down; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Present Persian legacies as achievements of real peoples, not as ‘exotic’ curiosities. Where scholars disagree about origins (chess, polo, carpets), say so. Be careful with the Mystery Bag around nut allergies. Do not suggest that Persians ‘gave’ everything: ideas moved in many directions.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mystery Bag and items (see Materials)</li><li>Legacy cards (app or printed): gardens, qanats, coins, roads, carpets, windcatchers, chess, polo, Shahnameh, words</li><li>‘Persian words in English’ list (app or printed)</li><li>Museum label template (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Word Detectives booklet pages</li><li>Time-capsule letter paper</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students name five legacies and explain one with a source?</li><li>Who used a hedge word (‘probably’, ‘many scholars think’)?</li><li>What do I need to revisit before tomorrow’s modern Iran lesson?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Tuesday — Geography: Persia to Iran: Cities Then & Now":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persia to Iran: Cities Then &amp; Now — The old names are still on the map. Who lives there now?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Social Sciences (Geography)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Geography — Iran Today</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 10: The Gate of All Nations (legacy &amp; today) · The Gate at Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SS-ICO — Social Sciences: Identity, Culture &amp; Organisation. Students understand how cultural practices vary but meet shared needs, how groups organise and make decisions, and how culture and heritage are passed on.</li><li>MA-S — Mathematics &amp; Statistics: Statistics. Students collect, organise, display and interpret data.</li><li>SS-DO — Social Sciences: Inquiry practices (Do). Students ask questions, find and evaluate sources, consider perspectives and communicate findings.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Relating to others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Respect; Community &amp; participation; Diversity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to compare ancient and modern settlements and to speak about modern Iran respectfully.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name four modern Iranian cities and where they are.</li><li>I can describe what has changed and what has continued since ancient times.</li><li>I can compare data about an Iranian city and a New Zealand city.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matching accuracy; the comparative statement (‘Then…, now…, still…’); the graph or map; the language used in the Council Fire task.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names one or two modern places with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Names four modern cities and links two to ancient places.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Describes change and continuity with examples and compares data accurately.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Explains why places last for centuries and evaluates what graphs and maps do not show; speaks respectfully about people today.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>continuity</b> — Something that stays the same over time.</li><li><b>change</b> — Something that is different from before.</li><li><b>population</b> — The number of people living in a place.</li><li><b>urban</b> — To do with towns and cities.</li><li><b>capital city</b> — The city where a country’s government sits (Tehran for Iran; Wellington for New Zealand).</li><li><b>windcatcher (badgir)</b> — A tower on a building that catches breezes to cool it.</li><li><b>stereotype</b> — A fixed, unfair picture of a group of people.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Then &amp; Now Cards</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Gate is close, and the road runs through towns you have never visited. ‘The old names are still on the map, Courier,’ says Shirin, unrolling a worn card. ‘Persepolis is near a city called Shiraz. Ecbatana, where the Medes ruled, is today Hamadan. Susa is a town called Shush. Match the old with the new. And remember: real families live in these places today.’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Monday — name two legacies; what is a loanword; why use a hedge word?</li><li>Deal ancient-to-modern cards to the Caravans: Persepolis–near Shiraz; Ecbatana–Hamadan; Susa–Shush; Pasargadae–near Shiraz and Persepolis; Isfahan; Tehran; Yazd (6 min). Teams match, then mark the modern cities on the big map with sticky dots.</li><li>Say clearly: ‘We are learning about the land and cities of Iran today, and about the people who live there. We speak about them as we would about our own neighbours.’ (1 min)</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria (students read them chorally).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Monday: legacies; the word ‘legacy’; one Persian word in English.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Iran today is a large, varied country. Its cities are old and new at the same time. Let’s look at four of them.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Big picture (4 min): show the map and the six-times comparison with NZ. Ask: ‘What would the climate be like in a country this big?’ Keep to land, language and culture — avoid current politics.</li><li>Four cities (5 min): look at a photo of each (Tehran, Isfahan, Shiraz, Yazd). Students write ONE word for each on a mini-whiteboard. Discuss: ‘What does the photo show that surprises you?’</li><li>Then and now (3 min): use the matching answers to ask: ‘Why might a city grow in the same place for 2,500 years?’</li><li>Data skim (3 min): show the city data cards. Point out the difference between city and wider-area figures and say that numbers change. Always check against a current source and round numbers.</li><li><b>A big country with many peoples:</b> Iran is about <b>six times</b> the size of Aotearoa New Zealand and has about <b>90 million</b> people (check a current source). Persian (Farsi) is the main language, and many people also speak Azeri, Kurdish, Luri, Arabic, Baluchi and other languages. Iran has mountains, deserts, forests and two coastlines, so its cities look very different from each other.</li><li><b>Four modern cities:</b> <b>Tehran</b> is the capital and the biggest city (about 9 million people in the city, more in the wider area). <b>Isfahan</b> is famous for its beautiful bridges and a great public square, from the 1600s. <b>Shiraz</b> is near Persepolis and known for gardens and poetry. <b>Yazd</b> is a desert city with windcatchers, mud-brick lanes and a UNESCO-listed old town.</li><li><b>Then and now:</b> <b>Persepolis</b> (UNESCO site) sits about 60 km north-east of <b>Shiraz</b>. <b>Ecbatana</b> lies under the modern city of <b>Hamadan</b>. <b>Susa</b> is the town of <b>Shush</b>, where archaeologists still work. <b>Pasargadae</b>, Cyrus’s capital, is a quiet UNESCO site on a high plain. Old cities often grow on the same spot because the water, soil and routes are still good — as you learned in Week 1.</li><li><b>What has changed, what has stayed:</b> <b>Changed:</b> Cities now have motorways, apartments, universities and technology. <b>Stayed the same:</b> People still choose sites near water and trade routes, still use qanats and old bazaars in some places, and still love gardens, poetry and hospitality. The relay-station idea lives on in modern roads and rail.</li><li><b>Windcatchers at Yazd:</b> In hot, dry Yazd, builders designed tall <b>windcatchers</b> (<i>badgirs</i>) that catch breezes and draw cool air down into the house, sometimes over a pool of water. They are a clever adaptation to a desert habitat, just like the adaptations you studied in Week 4.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Then &amp; Now</b> — Match each ancient place with its modern link.</li><li><b>sort: Change or Continuity?</b> — Decide whether each item has changed since ancient times or has continued.</li><li><b>order: Biggest to Smallest</b> — Put these cities in order from the largest to the smallest population (approximate, check with your data card).</li><li><b>reveal: Windcatchers &amp; Water</b> — Tap each card to learn how desert cities stay cool.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Then &amp; Now Map Pair:</b> Make two linked maps: one of the ancient places we studied and one of the modern cities of Iran. Use arrows or numbers to show which place is which.</li><li><b>Path B — Data Comparison:</b> Use the data cards to graph the population of ONE Iranian city and ONE New Zealand city. Write three sentences comparing them and say what the graph does not tell us.</li><li><b>Path C — Travel Guide Page:</b> Make a one-page travel guide to a modern Iranian city. Show what a visitor might see, eat and learn, and what the ancient past adds to the story.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Rev 7:9; Matt 28:19; 1 Tim 2:1–2 — In Revelation 7, John sees a crowd from every nation, tribe, people and language standing together before God. In Matthew 28, Jesus sends His followers to all nations. In 1 Timothy 2, Paul asks Christians to pray for everyone, including kings and all who are in authority, so that people can live peaceful and quiet lives.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> People today live in the lands we have studied. How can we love them and treat them as our neighbours?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What does it mean to respect people whose beliefs and cultures may be different from ours? What would respectful language sound like when we talk about another country?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Write a short prayer for Iranian families and for Iranian-NZ neighbours. Students from other faith backgrounds may write a kind wish or a message of goodwill instead.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Then &amp;amp; Now Map Pair</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make two linked maps: one of the ancient places we studied and one of the modern cities of Iran. Use arrows or numbers to show which place is which.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Both maps have a title, compass rose, scale bar and key</li><li>I labelled at least 4 ancient places and 4 modern cities</li><li>I linked ancient and modern places with numbers or arrows</li><li>I wrote a sentence on one thing that changed and one that stayed the same</li><li>I used respectful, accurate language</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a printed outline map and pre-made dots; add labels and join pairs with arrows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw both maps with all conventions and link the pairs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a third layer: a trade route (the Royal Road or Silk Road) and explain how that route helped some cities grow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ancient:</b> Persepolis · Pasargadae · Susa · Ecbatana. <b>Modern:</b> Shiraz · Shush · Hamadan · Tehran · Isfahan · Yazd. <b>Sentence frame:</b> Then, … Now, … But … is still the same.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Data Comparison</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Use the data cards to graph the population of ONE Iranian city and ONE New Zealand city. Write three sentences comparing them and say what the graph does not tell us.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My graph has a title and labelled axes</li><li>My bars or points are accurate and use a sensible scale</li><li>I compared the two cities in three sentences</li><li>I used ‘about’ or ‘roughly’ for approximate numbers</li><li>I named one thing the graph does NOT tell us</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-made bar graph template with a scale already marked; add bars and one sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw your own bar graph, write three comparison sentences and one limitation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compare four cities (two Iranian, two NZ), calculate the ratio between the largest and smallest, and explain why population alone does not make a city better or worse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Graph:</b> Title · x-axis (city) · y-axis (people) · scale. <b>Sentence frames:</b> … is about … times bigger than … The graph does not show … I would also like to know …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Travel Guide Page</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a one-page travel guide to a modern Iranian city. Show what a visitor might see, eat and learn, and what the ancient past adds to the story.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Title and a welcoming heading</li><li>A map or locator showing where the city is</li><li>At least three things to see or do (from our sources)</li><li>One link to the ancient past</li><li>Respectful language and no stereotypes</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the template and choose from a picture bank of the city; write short captions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Design your own page with at least three attractions and one ancient link.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘Meet the locals’ box that describes everyday life using a respectful, researched approach, and say how you checked that your picture is not a stereotype.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Welcome to … </b> <b>Where it is:</b> … <b>Must-see:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>The ancient link:</b> … <b>Did you know?</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Rev 7:9; Matt 28:19; 1 Tim 2:1–2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep prayer focused on families, children, peace and welcome, and avoid politics. Matthew 28:19 is a Christian text, and a Christian school may present it as such; do not suggest that Muslim, Jewish or Zoroastrian-background students should change their beliefs. Frame the task as care for people. Never single out Iranian-NZ students.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare an old settlement in your rohe with the city or town that stands there today: what stayed (water, routes, soil) and what changed? Compare the sizes of Auckland, Wellington and Christchurch with Tehran, Isfahan and Shiraz. Invite local iwi or your Māori Education lead to share place names and stories; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Keep content cultural and geographic, not political. Do not use news images or comment on the government. Avoid stereotypes (deserts only, camels only, headlines only). Iranian-NZ students may have family in Iran and may feel anxious about world news; give them the option to share or stay quiet. Check city data against a current source; give rounded figures.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Floor or wall map of Iran with sticky dots</li><li>Ancient-to-modern matching cards (app or printed)</li><li>City data cards (check against a current source before use)</li><li>Graph paper, rulers, or a spreadsheet for Path B</li><li>Photos of modern Iranian cities: Tehran, Isfahan, Shiraz, Yazd</li><li>Travel guide template (print from the Teacher View)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students describe both change and continuity for a city?</li><li>Did the graphs use sensible scales?</li><li>Which words did students use for modern Iran? Was the language respectful?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Wednesday — Science: Wild Persia: Design a Sanctuary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wild Persia: Design a Sanctuary — You know the habitats. You know the food webs. Now: can you protect them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Science — Wild Persia</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 10: The Gate of All Nations (legacy &amp; today) · The Gate at Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>SC-NoS-C — Science: Nature of Science — Communicating. Students use scientific vocabulary, diagrams and conventions, and question science texts.</li><li>SC-NoS-P — Science: Nature of Science — Participating &amp; Contributing. Students use science to make decisions about issues (e.g. conservation).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ecological sustainability; Community &amp; participation; Innovation, inquiry &amp; curiosity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to apply what we know about habitats and communities to design a reserve and propose actions to protect wildlife.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can design a reserve with at least three habitats.</li><li>I can justify my choice of species using adaptations and food webs.</li><li>I can propose one real action that helps wildlife.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Application of concepts in the blueprint, field guide or pitch; justification using adaptations and food webs; the action committed to.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Names some animals and habitats with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Designs a reserve with three habitats and names core, buffer, corridor and water.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Justifies species choices with adaptations and food webs, and proposes realistic actions.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Evaluates trade-offs between people and wildlife and explains how a change would ripple through a community.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>reserve / sanctuary</b> — A protected area where wildlife and habitats are kept safe.</li><li><b>core area</b> — The most strictly protected heart of a reserve.</li><li><b>buffer zone</b> — A gentler area around the core, with careful use by people.</li><li><b>corridor</b> — A strip of habitat that joins two protected areas so animals can travel safely.</li><li><b>conservation</b> — Looking after nature so living things and their habitats survive.</li><li><b>endangered / extinct</b> — Very few left in the wild / none left anywhere.</li><li><b>kaitiakitanga</b> — Guardianship and care of the environment (a Māori concept; use with care and local guidance).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Quick-Fire Recap</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A hush has fallen over the road. In the dusk, a shape moves among the rocks: spots, long legs, a glance, then nothing. ‘An Asiatic cheetah,’ whispers Shirin. ‘There are fewer than twenty left in Iran. We have met so many creatures on this road, Courier. Now it’s your turn. If you were in charge of a sanctuary for wild Persia, how would you design it?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Tuesday — name two modern cities; one thing that has continued since ancient times.</li><li>Quick-fire recap (6 min): call out a word (habitat, community, adaptation, food web, apex predator, conservation) and have Caravans write a definition on a whiteboard in 20 seconds. Award a point for a clear definition and an example.</li><li>Show the Animal Cards collected so far and ask: ‘Which three animals have you met in each habitat? Which are endangered or extinct?’ (2 min).</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Tuesday: modern cities, change and continuity.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A good sanctuary is more than a fence. It is a plan for habitats, food webs and people working together.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Design principles (5 min): sketch a simple reserve on the board: core, buffer, corridor, water. Ask: ‘What would go wrong if the corridor were cut?’</li><li>Habitat tour (4 min): look at the three habitats and ask Caravans to match each animal to its habitat and food link.</li><li>Gulf, Caspian and cheetah (3 min): show the hawksbill turtle Animal Card; explain it is the new Stage 10 card. Say clearly that the cheetah numbers are small and can change.</li><li>Local link (3 min): show a DOC recovery story and a local trap-line or planting project. Invite a local Māori Education lead or iwi/hapū person to share local kaitiakitanga practice, avoiding pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Four ideas for a good reserve:</b> <b>1. A protected core:</b> a heart where animals are left in peace. <b>2. A buffer zone:</b> where careful, low-impact use is allowed. <b>3. Corridors:</b> strips of habitat that link areas so animals can find food, mates and new homes. <b>4. Water:</b> springs, rivers, wetlands and, in dry lands, watering points. Local people must be part of the plan or it will not last.</li><li><b>Wild Persia: three habitats, many animals:</b> <b>Mountains:</b> bezoar ibex, Persian leopard, brown bear, golden eagle (Week 1). <b>Plains and steppe:</b> Persian onager, goitered gazelle and the Asiatic cheetah (Week 2). <b>Desert and wetland:</b> sand cats and jerboas (Week 4), flamingos and water buffalo of the wetlands (Week 5). Each habitat has its own food web.</li><li><b>The gulf and the Caspian:</b> Iran has two seas. In the south, the Persian Gulf has <b>dugongs</b> (sea-cows) and the <b>hawksbill turtle</b>, which nests on the beaches of islands such as Qeshm and Hengam. In the north, the <b>Caspian seal</b> lives in the Caspian Sea. The hawksbill turtle is critically endangered worldwide: it feeds on sponges on coral reefs and has a beautiful patterned shell that humans once hunted. Protecting beaches, reefs and nests helps it.</li><li><b>The Asiatic cheetah:</b> Once cheetahs ranged across much of Asia. Today the <b>Asiatic cheetah</b> survives only in Iran, and fewer than about 20 are thought to remain (numbers change, so check a current source). Threats include lack of prey, roads and habitat loss. Iran’s scientists, rangers and local herders work to protect the cheetah and its prey.</li><li><b>Recovery is possible:</b> In Aotearoa, <b>kākāpō</b> and <b>takahē</b> were once thought lost or nearly gone, and careful work by DOC rangers, scientists and volunteers has helped their numbers recover. Predator control and sanctuaries matter. Every recovery story shows that human action can protect as well as harm.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>match: Reserve Words</b> — Match each reserve idea to what it means.</li><li><b>sort: Which Habitat?</b> — Sort each animal into the wild Persian habitat where it lives.</li><li><b>order: Recovery Steps</b> — Put these steps of a wildlife recovery in a sensible order.</li><li><b>reveal: Meet the Hawksbill Turtle</b> — Tap each card to learn about this Gulf visitor.</li><li>animalCards</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Reserve Blueprint:</b> Design a wildlife reserve on a map. Include at least three habitats, a protected core, a buffer zone, a corridor and a water source. Add a key and a short explanation.</li><li><b>Path B — Field Guide: Six Species:</b> Make a field guide page for six species of wild Persia. For each, give its habitat, an adaptation, what it eats and what might eat it. Join them in a food web.</li><li><b>Path C — Pitch: Save the Asiatic Cheetah:</b> Make a 2-minute pitch to a council of rangers and scientists to protect the Asiatic cheetah. Include what is wrong, three actions and one thing you will do yourself.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 2:15; Ps 24:1; Rev 22:2 — In Genesis, God puts the first man in the garden to work it and take care of it. The psalmist sings that the earth and everything in it belongs to the Lord. In Revelation 22, John sees the tree of life by the river of the city, and its leaves are for the healing of the nations.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> How can we act as caretakers of God’s world? What does it look like to care for a place for people we will never meet?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Compare Genesis 2:15 with the idea of kaitiakitanga at school. What is the same and what might be different? Where do we need local knowledge?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Choose a real action to carry out: a planting, a pest trap, a clean-up, or a letter. Write it in your Caravan Log with a date.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Reserve Blueprint</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Design a wildlife reserve on a map. Include at least three habitats, a protected core, a buffer zone, a corridor and a water source. Add a key and a short explanation.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My map has a title, compass rose and key</li><li>I showed at least 3 habitats</li><li>I marked core, buffer, corridor and water</li><li>I chose at least 3 species and gave reasons</li><li>I wrote one action local people could do</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a pre-drawn map and a label bank; add core, buffer, corridor and water using colours.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Draw and label independently and give three reasons for your layout.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a food web for each habitat and explain what would happen if one corridor were blocked.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Plan:</b> Title · habitats · core · buffer · corridor · water. <b>Say it:</b> I put the … in the … because … <b>Action:</b> Local people could …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Field Guide: Six Species</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a field guide page for six species of wild Persia. For each, give its habitat, an adaptation, what it eats and what might eat it. Join them in a food web.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Six different species (from at least 3 habitats)</li><li>One clear adaptation for each, with a ‘helps it…’ sentence</li><li>Diet and a predator or threat for each</li><li>A food web linking at least 4 of the species</li><li>Status (endangered, extinct or secure) where known</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the species cards and a 3-box frame for each page; draw arrows for a 3-step food chain.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete six pages with adaptations and link four in a food web.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘What if?’ page: what would happen to the community if one apex predator or one prey animal vanished, using careful words such as ‘may’ and ‘often’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Page frame:</b> Species … Habitat … <b>Adaptation:</b> It has … which helps it … <b>Food:</b> It eats … and is eaten by … <b>Status:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Pitch: Save the Asiatic Cheetah</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Make a 2-minute pitch to a council of rangers and scientists to protect the Asiatic cheetah. Include what is wrong, three actions and one thing you will do yourself.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My pitch has a strong opening line</li><li>I explained the problem using at least two facts</li><li>I gave three clear actions (prey, roads, local people)</li><li>I included one thing I will do myself</li><li>I spoke clearly within two minutes</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the pitch frame and rehearse with a partner. Read from your notes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write and present your own pitch with three actions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a response to a hard question (a herder worried about livestock) and show how both people and cheetahs can win.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Open:</b> Imagine … <b>Problem:</b> The Asiatic cheetah … because … <b>Three actions:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Close:</b> I will …</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 2:15; Ps 24:1; Rev 22:2 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Genesis 2:15 sets caring for creation as a human task. Take care to say that Christians hold a range of views on how to apply it. Kaitiakitanga is a Māori concept with its own meaning; invite a Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū to speak to it rather than explaining it for them. Keep the tone hopeful: recovery stories are real.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Compare Persian reserves with Aotearoa’s predator-free islands and sanctuaries, and with the recovery of kākāpō and takahē. What actions could your school or whānau take? Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū for kaitiakitanga in your rohe; avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Present endangered species as a reason to care, not to panic. Say that cheetah numbers are small and may change. Be matter-of-fact about food chains. Do not suggest that Iranians are careless about wildlife: Iranian scientists and rangers are active in conservation.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Large paper and a map outline for the Reserve Blueprint</li><li>Species cards from earlier weeks (ibex, leopard, cheetah, fallow deer, seals, dugong, hawksbill turtle)</li><li>Animal Cards (new: hawksbill turtle)</li><li>Food-web card set and wool</li><li>Field Guide template and pitch planner (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Photos of kākāpō and takahē recovery, plus a trap-line or planting photo from your school or area</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Could students use ‘corridor’, ‘core’ and ‘buffer’ correctly?</li><li>Did their species choices fit the habitat?</li><li>Which action will actually be carried out, and who will check?</li></ul></div></div>"},"Thursday — Art: The Great Exhibition: Persian Gallery Night":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Great Exhibition: Persian Gallery Night — Curate it. Explain it. Share it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Arts (Visual Arts)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Art — The Great Exhibition</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 10: The Gate of All Nations (legacy &amp; today) · The Gate at Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>VA-CI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Communicating &amp; Interpreting. Students share and interpret ideas, feelings and stories in their own and others’ work.</li><li>VA-DI — The Arts — Visual Arts: Developing Ideas. Students develop visual ideas from observation, imagination and the study of artists’ work.</li><li>EN-S — English: Speaking &amp; Presenting. Students speak and present with purpose for a known audience.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Using language, symbols &amp; texts; Managing self; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Excellence; Community &amp; participation; Respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to curate and explain artworks, and to talk about our own and others’ work respectfully.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can write a clear museum label.</li><li>I can talk about my artwork to a visitor.</li><li>I can describe a classmate’s work respectfully.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Label clarity (title, maker, materials, explanation); speaking to a visitor or recording; respectful comment on a classmate’s work.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Writes a short label with help and names one feature of an artwork.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Writes a clear label with title, maker, materials and a 2–3 sentence explanation, and speaks to a visitor.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Groups works by a theme, explains choices and asks open questions.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Curates with a clear idea, compares works from different weeks and uses precise art vocabulary.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Key Vocabulary</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>curator</b> — A person who chooses, arranges and explains the objects in a museum or gallery.</li><li><b>exhibition</b> — A public display of artworks or objects.</li><li><b>artist’s statement</b> — A short text where the artist explains what they made and why.</li><li><b>label</b> — A small card that gives the title, maker, materials and a short explanation.</li><li><b>theme</b> — A big idea that links several artworks together.</li><li><b>gallery guide</b> — A person or a leaflet that helps visitors explore an exhibition.</li><li><b>audio guide</b> — A recorded tour that visitors listen to as they look.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Gallery Rules &amp; Roles</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The Gate is almost in sight, and the road has turned into a stone hall. ‘Every great caravan leaves a mark, Courier,’ says Shirin. ‘Tomorrow we race at the Gate. Tonight, we show what we made along the road. A gallery has rules and jobs, and everyone has a part. Who will be curator? Who will guide visitors?’</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Retrieval (2 min): Wednesday — name a reserve idea; name one endangered animal.</li><li>Walk through ‘Gallery Rules’ together (4 min): look with your eyes, ask permission to touch, speak softly, say something kind, thank the artist.</li><li>Roles (3 min): assign or choose roles for each Caravan: curator (arranges), guide (talks to visitors), artist (answers questions), photographer (records with permission).</li><li>Share the Learning Intention and Success Criteria (1 min).</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> Wednesday: core, buffer, corridor; one endangered animal.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Discovery (15 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A great gallery tells a story. Today you will learn how curators choose, arrange and explain.</p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Curator’s job (4 min): look at two artworks side by side and ask: ‘What links them? What could the theme be?’</li><li>Label writing (5 min): model a label for one artwork on the board using the frame (title, maker, materials, explanation, question).</li><li>Arranging (3 min): show how to group by theme, and how to leave space so every work can be seen.</li><li>Guiding and respect (3 min): model a guide asking an open question and a visitor responding. Practise ‘I noticed… I wonder… I like…’ in pairs.</li><li><b>What does a curator do?:</b> A <b>curator</b> chooses which artworks to show, arranges them in a sensible order, and writes the labels. They ask: ‘What story do these objects tell together?’</li><li><b>Write a good label:</b> A museum label gives the <b>title</b>, the <b>maker</b>, the <b>materials</b>, a <b>short explanation</b> (2–3 sentences) and sometimes a <b>question</b> for visitors. Short, clear words work best. Use hedge words where we are not sure (‘probably’, ‘some scholars think’).</li><li><b>Group by theme:</b> Group artworks by idea: <b>Pattern and power</b> (rosettes, reliefs), <b>Writing and records</b> (cylinders, clay tablets), <b>Colour and craft</b> (glazed bricks, jewels), <b>Journeys</b> (maps, Magi paintings), <b>Legacy</b> (Persian words, museum exhibits).</li><li><b>Guiding questions:</b> A good guide asks open questions: ‘What do you notice first?’ ‘What colours has the artist chosen?’ ‘What do you wonder?’ This helps visitors look slowly, and it respects what they bring.</li><li><b>Respect for every maker:</b> Say what you notice before you say what you like. Never say a classmate’s work is bad. Use the sentence: ‘I noticed… I wonder… I like… because…’.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">App interactives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>order: Setting Up the Gallery</b> — Put the steps of preparing an exhibition in a sensible order.</li><li><b>match: Gallery Roles &amp; Words</b> — Match each gallery word to what it means.</li><li><b>sort: Kind or Unkind Feedback?</b> — Sort each comment into kind feedback that helps, or unkind feedback that does not.</li><li><b>reveal: Good Guide Questions</b> — Tap each card to see why the question works.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Choose Your Path (25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Curate a Gallery Wall:</b> Curate a wall or table for your Caravan: choose 5–8 artworks from the term, group them by a theme, arrange them with care and give the wall a title and an introduction panel.</li><li><b>Path B — Artist Statement &amp; Gallery Guide:</b> Write an artist’s statement for your best artwork and a one-page gallery guide that tells visitors which works to see and what to ask.</li><li><b>Path C — Audio Guide:</b> Record a 60-second audio tour of one gallery wall or table. Speak clearly, say something about each work and ask visitors one question.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Col 3:17; 1 Pet 4:10 — Paul says that whatever we do, in words or actions, we should do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God. Peter says that each of us has received a gift and should use it to serve others, as good stewards of God’s many-sided grace.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> Whose glory are our gifts for? Why might it matter who we are serving when we make or show something?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> Persian craftspeople served kings; Bezalel’s craft served God’s worship (Week 1). Our own gifts can serve our friends and families. How can serving others change the way we make things?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Thank a teacher or helper. Write them a short note naming one thing they did to help you.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Curate a Gallery Wall</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Curate a wall or table for your Caravan: choose 5–8 artworks from the term, group them by a theme, arrange them with care and give the wall a title and an introduction panel.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My wall has a title and a theme</li><li>I chose 5–8 artworks and arranged them in a clear order</li><li>Every artwork has a label</li><li>I wrote a 3-sentence introduction panel</li><li>I left enough space so each work can be seen</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a ready-made theme and choose five artworks from a printed bank; write labels with the frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose your own theme and write all the labels and the introduction panel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Curate a ‘conversation’ between two works from different weeks (for example a rosette and a relief) and explain what they say together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Title:</b> … <b>Theme:</b> This wall shows … <b>Introduction:</b> This wall is about… The works were made by… We want visitors to notice… <b>Order:</b> First … then … finally …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Artist Statement &amp;amp; Gallery Guide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write an artist’s statement for your best artwork and a one-page gallery guide that tells visitors which works to see and what to ask.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>My statement says what I made, how, and why</li><li>I named the Persian idea or technique that inspired me</li><li>My gallery guide lists at least 5 works and a question for each</li><li>I used clear, kind language</li><li>I proofread my work</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the statement frame and write a guide with three stops.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a statement of 4–5 sentences and a guide with five stops and questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a guide with two routes (a 10-minute route for young children and a 20-minute route for adults) and explain the choices.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Statement:</b> I made … using … I was inspired by … I want visitors to … <b>Guide:</b> 1 … Ask: … 2 … Ask: …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Audio Guide</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Record a 60-second audio tour of one gallery wall or table. Speak clearly, say something about each work and ask visitors one question.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I introduced myself and the wall</li><li>I described at least 3 works clearly</li><li>I included one fact from our learning</li><li>I asked visitors one question</li><li>My recording lasts about 60 seconds</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the script frame and rehearse with a partner before recording.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write and record your own 60-second tour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Record a tour for two audiences (a younger child and a visitor from another country) and explain what you changed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Hello and welcome to…</b> <b>First, look at…</b> <b>This was made by… using…</b> <b>Did you know?</b> … <b>What do you notice?</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Col 3:17; 1 Pet 4:10 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Keep the tone celebratory and humble: the aim is to serve and thank, not to compete. Invite students of other faiths to take part with their own words of thanks. Be sure that every child has at least one piece on show and a role at the exhibition.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> How do museums and marae in Aotearoa display taonga and share stories? Visit a local museum or invite a guide. Invite your Māori Education lead or local iwi/hapū for protocols around displaying taonga; do not imitate tapu designs, and avoid pan-Māori generalisations.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> Make sure every child’s work is displayed and respected, and nobody is ranked. Some students may feel nervous about speaking to visitors: offer the audio-guide option. Check food allergies and any whānau cultural or dietary needs before sharing refreshments. Get consent before any photographs of students are shared.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The whole term’s artwork: rosettes, clay cylinders, reliefs, glazed bricks, jewel work, Magi night paintings, carpets</li><li>Label cards and pens; small stands and tables</li><li>Role badges: curator, guide, artist, photographer</li><li>Audio recorders or tablets</li><li>Whānau invitations and gallery guide (print from the Teacher View)</li><li>Gallery-night checklist and certificates</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Did every student write a label that a visitor could read?</li><li>Who needs more support to speak confidently?</li><li>What do whānau say about the evening? Collect feedback.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Friday — Showdown: The Gate of All Nations":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown: The Gate of All Nations — All ten weeks. One Gate. One last race.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Curriculum Area</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cross-curricular review (team quiz)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Stage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stage 10: The Gate of All Nations (legacy &amp; today) · The Gate at Persepolis</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">60 minutes · Years 5–6 (NZC Level 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Achievement Objectives</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>SS-CC — Social Sciences: Continuity &amp; Change. Students understand how people remember and record the past in different ways, how events have causes and consequences, and that perspectives on events differ.</li><li>SS-PE — Social Sciences: Place &amp; Environment. Students understand how people interpret and represent places and environments, why people settle where they do, and how people’s interactions with places cause change.</li><li>SC-LW-Eco — Science: Living World — Ecology. Students explain how living things are suited to their habitat and respond to environmental change, both natural and human-induced.</li><li>VA-UC — The Arts — Visual Arts: Understanding Visual Arts in Context. Students investigate the purpose of and context for art from past and present cultures.</li><li>RE — Christian Character / Bible programme: Special character. Students engage with Scripture and Christian character as part of the school’s special character programme.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Key Competencies</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thinking; Managing self; Relating to others; Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Values</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Integrity; Community &amp; participation; Excellence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning to recall and apply what we have learned across the whole Persia unit, and to celebrate our journey as a team.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success criteria — I can…</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can answer questions from across all ten weeks of the unit.</li><li>I can work with my Caravan with integrity and encourage others.</li><li>I can explain what I have learned about God, people and the nations.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showdown data per concept tag (exportable); Victory Lap product; Gate Creed; reflection in the Caravan Log; the unit portfolio.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Rubric (Scout → Master of the Road)</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Scout:</b> Answers some questions with support.</li><li><b>Courier:</b> Answers most questions accurately across several weeks.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> Explains why wrong answers were wrong and links ideas from different weeks.</li><li><b>Master of the Road:</b> Writes strong questions that connect weeks, and reflects thoughtfully on learning and character.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Introduction — The Dispatch (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>The Gate of All Nations</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>The great carved bulls stand guard, and the Gate towers above your caravan. Zal waits in the shade with his hood down. ‘Before the Gate opens,’ says Shirin, ‘you must write your last Creed. Not how you will win, but how you will be remembered. Ten weeks ago you signed a Charter. Now it is time to look back and look forward.’ A horn rolls out across the plain. The Grand Finale begins.</p></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Teacher steps</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Before the lobby opens, each team writes its Gate Creed on a strip and reads it aloud. Display them beside the Week 1 and Week 3 Creeds.</li><li>Remind students that today’s questions come from all ten weeks, and that celebrating effort matters more than winning.</li><li>After the quiz: Council Fire, the Forgiveness Vote for Zal, the last fragment and the Seal made whole, then the team podium, the individual podium and the season awards. Close with a whānau toast or shared kai.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Retrieval:</b> The quiz is the retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Main Content</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Showdown (live team quiz, about 25 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mode: Classic — Speed + accuracy. Boss questions score ×2.</li><li>Open the host page on the projector (Live or Teacher-led mode); students join by QR code or PIN, choose a name and Caravan.</li><li>Draw the Fate card and read it aloud. Run the questions; at each teaching moment (Q2, Q7, Q10) stop and discuss. Boss rounds score double.</li><li>Caravan Creed: Before the final quiz, each Caravan writes a Gate Creed: ONE sentence about how you want to be remembered when the race is over. Starters: We want to be remembered for… / We will leave behind… / When the race is over, we will… / We will always treat other people…</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Victory Lap (choose a path)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Path A — Caravan Log: The Whole Journey:</b> Look back over all ten stages. Write about the road you have travelled: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought for the whole unit.</li><li><b>Path B — Question-Maker: The Hall of Fame:</b> Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys that could go into a ‘Persia Hall of Fame’ quiz for next year’s class.</li><li><b>Path C — Teach-Back Comic/Poster: The Big Story:</b> Tell the story of the Royal Road Race to a younger child in a comic or poster: the Seal, the nations, the Gate.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-navy-soft\">Council Fire (10 minutes)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>SEE:</b> Gen 12:3; Rev 7:9; Ps 78:4; 1 Pet 4:10; Prov 2:3–6 — God promised to bless all the nations of the earth, and John sees them gathered together before Him. The psalmist says we must tell the next generation about God’s deeds, and Peter says to use our gifts to serve one another. The Proverbs tell us that if we search for wisdom like silver and hidden treasure, we will find the knowledge of God, because the Lord gives wisdom.</li><li><b>WONDER:</b> What is the best thing you will carry out of this journey? Who will you tell?</li><li><b>WEIGH:</b> What did Persia teach us about nations, kindness and power? What do you think God wants the nations to be like at the end of the story?</li><li><b>RESPOND:</b> Hold your Seal piece. Say your Gate Creed together, and say thank you to someone in your Caravan. Take part in the Forgiveness Vote with a thoughtful heart (see Col 3:13: ‘forgive as the Lord forgave you’).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Showdown Questions (teacher copy)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>1. Long before the Persians rose, which ancient people lived at Susa (Week 1)? <b>A) Elamites ✔</b> · B) Mongols · C) Romans · D) Vikings <em>Susa was an Elamite city, one of the earliest cities in the world, long before Cyrus.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>2. The famous Cyrus Cylinder, found in Babylon, is made of… (Week 2) A) silk · B) gold · C) wood · <b>D) clay ✔</b> <em>The Cyrus Cylinder is a barrel of baked clay with cuneiform writing. It describes Cyrus entering Babylon in 539 BC. People debate how to describe it, but it is certainly an important source.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>3. The Royal Road from Sardis to Susa was about how long? (Week 3) A) 27,000 km · <b>B) 2,700 km ✔</b> · C) 27 km · D) 270 km <em>About 2,700 km. Royal riders using relay stations could cover it in about a week, according to Herodotus.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>4. Xerxes ordered a bridge of boats across which narrow strait, and a storm wrecked it? (Week 4) <b>A) Hellespont ✔</b> · B) Bass Strait · C) Panama Canal · D) Cook Strait <em>The Hellespont is the narrow strait between Asia and Europe. Greek writers say a storm destroyed the first bridges, and Xerxes had them rebuilt.</em> [SS-PE]</li><li>5. In the book of Esther, what did Queen Esther do for her people at Susa? (Week 5) A) She built a wall · B) She led an army · <b>C) She courageously went to the king to plead for her people ✔</b> · D) She hid from the king <em>Esther risked her own safety to speak to the king and ask him to save her people. Her story is about courage.</em> [RE]</li><li>6. How many days did it take Nehemiah’s people to rebuild the wall of Jerusalem? (Week 6) <b>A) 52 days ✔</b> · B) 52 months · C) 500 days · D) 5 days <em>Nehemiah 6 says the wall was finished in 52 days, even though some people mocked and threatened the builders.</em> [RE]</li><li>7. Which leader’s army defeated Darius III and then burned Persepolis in 330 BC? (Week 8) <b>A) Alexander the Great ✔</b> · B) Nehemiah · C) Xerxes · D) Cyrus <em>Alexander of Macedon defeated the last Achaemenid king, Darius III, and Persepolis burned in 330 BC. Historians still debate whether it was planned or a drunken act.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>8. The ‘Parthian shot’ was a famous trick. What was it? (Week 9) A) A type of cheese · B) A kind of drum · <b>C) Shooting backwards while riding away ✔</b> · D) A gold coin <em>Parthian horse archers pretended to retreat and then turned in the saddle to shoot arrows at pursuers.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>9. In what year did the country ask the world to call it ‘Iran’? (Week 1 and today) A) 535 · B) 1835 · <b>C) 1935 ✔</b> · D) 2035 <em>In 1935 the government asked other countries to use ‘Iran’. ‘Persia’ comes from Persis, the home region of the Persians. Both names matter.</em> [SS-CC]</li><li>10. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Which is the best evidence that Persia’s ideas travelled far beyond its borders? A) There are deserts in Iran · B) Persian kings were rich · C) Persia was a big country · <b>D) English borrowed words such as paradise, caravan and bazaar from Persian ✔</b> <em>Words travel with people and goods. ‘Paradise’ (from a Persian word for a walled garden), ‘caravan’ and ‘bazaar’ are Persian loanwords still used in English today. Size and wealth are not evidence of influence.</em> [SS-CC] — <b>Teaching moment</b></li><li>11. What is a ‘corridor’ in a wildlife reserve? (Week 10) A) A kind of trap · B) A hallway in a museum · <b>C) A strip of habitat that links two areas so animals can travel safely ✔</b> · D) The strictly protected core <em>A corridor is a strip of habitat that joins protected areas so animals can find food, mates and new homes safely.</em> [SC-LW-Eco]</li><li>12. <b>BOSS</b> BOSS ×2 — Revelation 7:9 describes a crowd that is… <b>A) from every nation, tribe, people and language, standing together before God ✔</b> · B) empty · C) from only one nation · D) made up of kings only <em>John sees a huge crowd from every nation, tribe, people and language standing together before God. It echoes God’s promise in Genesis 12:3 to bless all nations.</em> [RE]</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Student Agency Choices</div><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path A: Caravan Log: The Whole Journey</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Look back over all ten stages. Write about the road you have travelled: what you now know, what is still a mystery, and your Faith Thought for the whole unit.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I named 3 things I know now that I did not know in Week 1</li><li>I named 1 thing that is still a mystery</li><li>I wrote a Faith Thought about God and the nations</li><li>I named one moment I am proud of as a Courier</li><li>I rated my own effort honestly</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the sentence starters and draw one of your answers. Choose your favourite stage and write three sentences about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write all four sections in full sentences, with at least one example from each of three different stages.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Connect two stages that seem far apart (for example Cyrus and Nehemiah, or the Royal Road and the Silk Road) in one paragraph that explains what links them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>In Week 1 I thought…</b> <b>Now I know…</b> <b>It is still a mystery…</b> <b>My Faith Thought:</b> This unit I saw that God…</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path B: Question-Maker: The Hall of Fame</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Write 2 quiz questions with wrong-answer decoys that could go into a ‘Persia Hall of Fame’ quiz for next year’s class.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each question is clear and about a different week</li><li>I wrote one correct answer and three believable decoys</li><li>I wrote a one-line explanation</li><li>I checked the answer against our notes</li><li>One of my questions needs thinking, not only remembering</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use the question frame and write one question with a partner.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write two complete questions with explanations from two different weeks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Write a Boss question that asks next year’s class to compare two stages, and write the explanation a teacher could read aloud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Question:</b> … <b>A)</b> … <b>B)</b> … <b>C)</b> … <b>D)</b> … <b>Correct:</b> … <b>Because:</b> …</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"lt-subhead lt-bg-teal-soft\">Path C: Teach-Back Comic/Poster: The Big Story</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Tell the story of the Royal Road Race to a younger child in a comic or poster: the Seal, the nations, the Gate.</p></div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p><b>Success checklist</b></p></div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I picked ONE big idea from the unit</li><li>I used simple words a younger child would understand</li><li>I used at least 3 pictures</li><li>My comic or poster is neat and clear</li><li>I included a ‘quiz the reader’ question</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Supported</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Use a 4-panel comic template with captions to fill in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Standard</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Create your own comic or poster with a title and at least three pictures.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Extended</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Add a ‘Did you know?’ box that shows where scholars disagree or where we cannot be sure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Scaffold</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Big idea:</b> … <b>Three steps to explain it:</b> 1 … 2 … 3 … <b>Check:</b> Could a Year 2 understand this?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Faith Connection</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Scripture: Gen 12:3; Rev 7:9; Ps 78:4; 1 Pet 4:10; Prov 2:3–6 (paraphrased in the app — read from your school’s Bible translation)</li><li>Teacher note: Make this ceremony joyful and not a contest. Frame the Forgiveness Vote as a chance to discuss what forgiveness does and does not mean: it does not pretend a wrong did not happen, but it chooses mercy. Students of other faiths may take part in the spirit of kindness. Invite whānau to share the final toast or kai.</li><li>Use Scripture as one source among others, weighing it with archaeology, geography, science and art; keep questions open and never pressure personal faith statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Aotearoa Connection &amp; Sensitivity</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li><b>Hononga Aotearoa:</b> Link back to each week’s NZ connection: sources and whakapapa; the Southern Alps; the Royal Road and ara; sea straits and harbours; kākāpō and predator-free sanctuaries; kowhaiwhai; museums and taonga. Thank any guests from local iwi or your Māori Education lead.</li><li><b>Sensitivity note:</b> This is a celebration: winning is not the point. Some students may feel anxious about public podiums; offer the ‘anonymise names’ option. Make sure every child receives a certificate that names a real strength. Be mindful of allergies and cultural or dietary needs at the whānau toast or kai.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Teaching as Inquiry (reflect after the lesson)</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>What did the Showdown data show across the ten weeks? Which tags had the lowest success?</li><li>Which misconceptions should I address at the start of next term?</li><li>What would I change for the next group of Couriers?</li></ul></div></div>"}}}}}}}}},"homework":{"label":"Category","children":{"Weekly Wonder":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This remains the same size throughout our lifetime.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your Eyes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The human eye blinks an average of 4,200,000 times a year. This means if you were given 10 cents for every time you blinked you would make $420,000 annually.</li><li>Your eye is the fastest muscle in your body. Hence, the phrase: “In the blink of an eye.”</li><li>Your iris has 256 unique characteristics, while your fingerprint only has 40.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 2":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the first food to be eaten in space.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Applesauce</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Since it is high in pectin, apple sauce is a homemade remedy for children with diarrhea or stomach problems.</li><li>Why do apples float? It’s because they’re 25 percent air, giving us the ability to bob for apples in a barrel of water.</li><li>The largest apple peel was created by Kathy Wafler from New York in 1976. The prize-winning peel was 52.5m long.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 3":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Albert Einstein said this was the smartest person who ever lived.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac Newton</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>During the Great Plague, Isaac Newton spent two years in study and isolation at his home developing his theories on calculus, gravity, and the laws of motion.</li><li>His mother wanted him to be a farmer so took him out of school. He didn’t enjoy it so was soon back at school again.</li><li>In Isaac’s time the calendar was different, and even though by today’s calendar he was born in January, by that time’s calendar he was considered to be born on Christmas Day.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 4":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is one of the best pastimes for people who want to reduce the risk of having heart disease or a stroke.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cycling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The same energy that is expended for walking can be used when cycling to go three times faster.</li><li>Bicycles save over 900 million litres of fuel every year.</li><li>The fastest measured speed of riding a bicycle on a flat surface is 133.75 km/h.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 5":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over the centuries and around the world, lizard soup, blood-letting, leeches, turnips, plum pickle and the familiar chicken soup have all been used to try to heal this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Common Cold</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>There are plenty of good reasons to eat vitamin C packed foods like oranges and broccoli. However, there is currently little evidence to suggest that taking this vitamin reduces your chances of getting a cold.</li><li>A single sneeze can spray 100,000 germs into the air, which is why you should keep 2 meters distance from a sneezing sick person.</li><li>Being cold or wet doesn’t cause colds or make you more likely to get sick.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 6":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You are more likely to see this in the Summer than the Winter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rainbows</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You are less likely to see a rainbow in the winter – but why? Because of snow! A rainbow is the result of light passing through a spectrum – in most cases, a collection of raindrops.</li><li>The average rainbow is observable for less than an hour. But in 2017 in Taiwan, people observed a rainbow for 8 hours and 58 minutes.</li><li>A double rainbow appears when light is reflected twice in a raindrop. The secondary rainbow – which will be a little bit higher and fainter in color – will actually have its colors reversed.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 7":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This invertebrate was once displayed in a museum thought to be dead until it started moving several years later.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Snails</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Certain land snails can sleep for up to three years in hibernation, but generally, snails will sleep on and off in between periods of 13 to 15 hours. Afterwards, they experience a sudden jolt of energy for the next 30 hours, where they get all their snail chores done!</li><li>The speed of snails is around 12-20 mm per second. If they moved without stopping, it would take more than a week to complete 1 kilometer.</li><li>The mucus of the garden snail is used to treat wrinkles, spots, and scars on the skin.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 8":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This snack was invented because chocolate melted too fast in the sun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">M&amp;Ms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>M&amp;Ms were made for soldiers in WWII so chocolate wouldn’t melt in their hands.</li><li>The “M” stands for the names Mars and Murrie.</li><li>They were originally only made in the color brown.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 9":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This creature has blue blood!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Horseshoe Crabs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Horseshoe crabs have blue blood because it contains copper instead of iron.</li><li>Their blood is used to test medicine safety — it's super sensitive to bacteria.</li><li>They have strong shells and live in muddy ocean shores.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 10":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The world’s first hydroelectric power plant was created here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Niagara Falls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>More than 8 million visitors explore Niagara Falls State Park annually, coming from all over the world.</li><li>The first person to go over Niagara Falls in a barrel was 63-year-old schoolteacher Annie Edson Taylor — she suffered only a concussion and a minor cut on her head.</li><li>Fish do plunge over the Falls, and it’s estimated that almost all of them survive, thanks to their ability to flow with the water. Sometimes, however, they are a bit “stunned” after making the journey.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 11":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This wonder can be seen from space (with binoculars) if the conditions are right.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Great Wall of China</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Many workers lost their lives during the construction of the wall.</li><li>It stretches across Northern China and covers over 21,000 kms.</li><li>Construction started over 2000 years ago in the 7th century BC.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 12":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This travels at about 300,000 km’s per second.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Light</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The color of an object that we see is actually the color of the light that it reflects — e.g. a red apple appears red because it reflects red light and absorbs all other colors.</li><li>The Northern Lights, or Aurora Borealis, are caused by charged particles from the sun colliding with particles in the Earth's atmosphere, creating a beautiful display of light in the sky.</li><li>Different animals can see different ranges of light. For example, some birds can see ultraviolet light, which humans can't see.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 13":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This famous rugby player was knighted for their services to the Pacific community and youth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sir Michael Jones</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was known for his Christian beliefs, which meant he chose not to play on Sundays.</li><li>He played once for Western Samoa and 55 times for New Zealand.</li><li>He was part of the All Black team that won the very first Rugby World Cup in 1987.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 14":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">There are approximately 20,000 known species of these worldwide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Butterflies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some butterflies have a wingspan of 1.3 cms whilst another, named ‘Queen Alexandra’s’, has a wingspan of 30cm.</li><li>Monarch butterfly migrations can cover up to 4,800 kms (more than twice the length of NZ).</li><li>Fastest butterflies: Convolvulus hawk-moth = 100 km/hr, Skipper = 60 km/hr, Hawk Moth (general) = 50 km/hr.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 15":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You need to climb 251 steps to get to the top.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Leaning Tower of Pisa</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It was not originally leaning when it was built.</li><li>Pisa survived 4 earthquakes.</li><li>It took almost 2 centuries to build because of wars and battles that interrupted its construction.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 16":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Americans eat 350 pieces of this every second.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pizza</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Luis XIII in Salerno, Italy has the world’s most expensive pizza at $12,000! It includes three types of caviar, lobster, bufala mozzarella cheese, and grains of pink Australian sea salt.</li><li>At the World Pizza Championships, dough-spinning has its own professional-level sporting event, with five competitive events: Freestyle Acrobatic Dough Tossing, Fastest Dough, Largest Dough Stretch, Fastest Pizza Box Folding, and the Pizza Triathlon.</li><li>The Hawaiian pizza was invented in Canada. With only three toppings available — mushroom, bacon, or pepperoni — Sam Panopoulos decided to throw a can of pineapple on his pizza.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 17":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Extracts from these have been used to treat Alzheimer's disease.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daffodils</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The bulbs can be poisonous to animals. Squirrels won't eat daffodils because of this, and you should make sure you plant daffodil bulbs where cats and dogs can’t get them.</li><li>Don't mix them with other flowers in a vase — daffodils release latex from their stems once cut, which can shorten the life of other flowers.</li><li>Daffodils are one of the first signs of Spring and are also strongly associated with Easter because of this.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 18":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Known for their incredible jumping ability, water conservation skills, and nocturnal habits.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Kangaroo Rat</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The kangaroo rat gets all its water from seeds, and never needs to drink!</li><li>Their powerful hind legs allow them to leap up to 2.7 meters in one bound, helping them escape predators.</li><li>They have fur-lined food storage pouches in their cheeks to collect and transport seeds and other food items.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 19":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal has cube-shaped poop!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wombats</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wombats use cube-shaped poop to mark territory — it doesn’t roll away!</li><li>Their butt is their main form of defence — they can crush a predator’s skull with their behind.</li><li>They have strong back ends to block tunnels from predators.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 20":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Movies such as Mission Impossible Fallout, Lord of the Rings, The Lion Witch and the Wardrobe, Prince Caspian, and Wolverine were filmed here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Queenstown</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Lonely Planet made some statistics saying Lake Wakatipu has some of the cleanest water in the world.</li><li>There are still opportunities to make money gold prospecting, as gold is still found on the Shotover River.</li><li>Queenstown has the 4th busiest airport in New Zealand, which is impressive considering it only has the 27th largest urban population.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 21":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This country has a waterfall that is 3 times the height of Niagara Falls.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Scotland</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The highest proportion of redheads in the world are in Scotland.</li><li>The official animal of Scotland is the unicorn, and according to legend it is considered one of the only animals powerful enough to defeat formidable opponents, such as elephants, in battle.</li><li>The raincoat was invented in Scotland by a man named Charles Macintosh, who was born in Glasgow.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 22":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some cultures (most notably Japan) use the fiber from this food plant to make fabric and sometimes even paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bananas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bananas have around 30,000 genes — that’s more than humans!</li><li>Bananas are naturally slightly radioactive (thanks to their potassium content) — but the level of radiation is not high enough to cause harm.</li><li>If you rub the inside of a banana peel on a scrape or burn, it will help the pain go away, keep the swelling down, and keep the wound from getting infected.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 23":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This can be used underwater, in space, and upside down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pencil</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>On average a pencil can be sharpened 17 times, draw a line 56 kms long, and can write approximately 45,000 words.</li><li>The most expensive pencil in the world is the Graf von Faber-Castell Perfect Pencil. It’s crafted from 240-year-old olive wood and 18-karat white gold and costs $12,800.</li><li>An average size tree will make approximately 300,000 pencils.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 24":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This toy was inspired by a walking factory spring.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Slinky</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Slinky was invented when a spring fell off a desk and “walked.” It was originally being created as a part of a ship.</li><li>During the Vietnam War, soldiers used Slinkys as mobile antennas.</li><li>The first Slinky demonstration at Gimbel’s Department Store in Philadelphia in 1945 sold all 400 units in 90 minutes.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 25":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This tiny animal can survive freezing, heat, and even space!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tardigrades (Water Bears)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tardigrades (water bears) curl up into a dry ball called a 'tun.'</li><li>They’ve survived in space and came back alive!</li><li>They're so small you need a microscope to see them.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 26":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can regrow part of its brain!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Axolotls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Axolotls can regrow body parts like tails and limbs.</li><li>They breathe through gills and lungs!</li><li>They're known as the 'smiling salamander.'</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 27":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird can sleep while flying!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Frigatebird</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Frigatebirds nap by shutting down half their brain at a time.</li><li>They can fly for weeks without stopping.</li><li>Magnificent frigatebirds practice kleptoparasitism — stealing food (fish and squid) from other seabirds, like blue-footed boobies.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 28":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was inspired by an adopted black and white dog called Spike.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Snoopy (Peanuts)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Though we never saw the inside of Snoopy’s doghouse in the comic strip, it was revealed over the years that it held a lot of personal possessions, including records, books, an original Vincent van Gogh painting, and a pool table.</li><li>In 2015, Snoopy received a coveted star on the Walk of Fame. Appropriately, it’s located right next to Charles Schulz’s.</li><li>Snoopy has an active imagination. Sometimes he pretends to be college student Joe Cool, other times he thinks he’s Flying Ace, a World War I pilot.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 29":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Too many of these can make your skin turn yellow. The condition is also named after these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Carrots</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The most common carrots are orange, but they also come in white, yellow, and purple colors.</li><li>Baby carrots are just regular carrots that have been pre-shaped, cut, and peeled to look appealing.</li><li>Many say that raw vegetables are better for you than cooked ones, but the opposite is true for carrots.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 30":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In India, illegal mining of this item is the country's largest organized criminal activity. The black market for it is estimated to be worth 200-300 billion dollars per year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sand</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>If you put grains under a microscope you will see different shapes, colors, and even tiny fossils.</li><li>In only a few sand dunes around the world there are singing sand dunes. They make weird, loud humming noises when the sand moves.</li><li>Not all sand is yellow or white! It can be pink (Bahamas — from crushed shells), green (Hawai‘i — from olivine crystals), black (Iceland — from volcanic rock), or red (Australia — from iron-rich sandstone).</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 31":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">He was the first billionaire athlete.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Michael Jordan</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Jordan tried to get into the varsity basketball team. At the time he was 180.3 cm tall and quite skinny, so he wasn't allowed on the team.</li><li>Michael Jordan retired in 1993 from the Chicago Bulls after the death of his father, who was very close to him.</li><li>Jordan has a fear of water from a tragic incident during his childhood — he saw a good friend drown, and shortly after, he almost drowned himself while at baseball camp.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 32":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A dessert was named in honor of this famous dancer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Anna Pavlova</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Anna gave over four thousand ballet performances during the years between 1913 and 1930.</li><li>She had no children of her own, but following the devastation of World War I, she established a home for Russian orphans.</li><li>Worn out by touring and the stress of performing, she died of pneumonia in 1931 at the age of 51.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 33":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird got its common name from the loud territorial sound that it makes. The calls are often mistaken for many different animals, such as donkeys or monkeys.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Kookaburra</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>As kookaburras can be heard very early in the morning they are called the ‘bushman’s clock’.</li><li>The kookaburra uses its brown and white feathers to blend into its environment, making it harder for both prey and predators to spot.</li><li>Baby kookaburras are born naked and blind, but they have a very sharp hook at the end of their beak. The first chick that hatches may turn on their younger siblings, bite them, or even kill them if there isn’t enough food.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 34":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Climbing of this rock is prohibited, but Prince Charles and Princess Diana still climbed it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ayers Rock (Uluru)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It varies from year to year, but Uluru receives around 300 millimetres of rain on average each year. The rain creates waterfalls and brings new life to the rock and surrounding desert.</li><li>Towering 348 metres above the surrounding plain, Uluru is taller than the Eiffel Tower in Paris, the Chrysler Building in New York, and the Great Pyramid of Giza.</li><li>Uluru is 3.6 km long and 1.9 km wide, with a total circumference of 9.4 km. It takes about 3½ hours to walk around the base.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 35":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This was banned 3 times in Scotland because it interfered with military training.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Golf</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Tiger Woods took an interest in golf at only six months old, and his father started teaching him at two years old. He got his first hole in one at 8 years old.</li><li>Early golf balls were made of leather and feathers. Manufacturers would wet the feathers to make them stick, then wrap them around the leather.</li><li>The golf tee wasn’t created until the 1920s. Before that, golfers would use mounds of sand to elevate their golf balls for the opening shot.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 36":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People lose on average about 50-150 of these a day. In general, females lose more than males.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hair</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A single strand of hair can support up to 3 kgs of weight. That means a whole head of hair can support up to 2000 kgs!</li><li>About 95% of the total skin area is covered in hair. Who knew you were so, well, hairy.</li><li>Someone purchased a lock of Elvis’ hair at an auction for $15,000.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 37":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This city has more fountains than any other city on the planet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rome</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Harming cats is punishable by Roman law. They can stay where they want and you cannot move them.</li><li>A special light show happens at the Pantheon every year to celebrate Rome’s birthday.</li><li>Coins thrown into Trevi fountain add up to over 1 million euros a year.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 38":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This thing has its own national day on September 30th. National — day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gum</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The record for the most gum chewed at a time is 250 pieces.</li><li>Regular chewing gum is made of plastic — ‘polyethylene’, which is also used to make tires and glue.</li><li>Iran chews the most gum in the world. Shop keepers will give gum instead of change if they don’t have enough.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 39":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">New Zealand eats the most of this per capita (person) in the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ice Cream</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Many people think this was invented in Italy because of Gelato, but it was actually invented in China.</li><li>Japan has some of the most interesting flavors of ice cream, such as basil, eggplant, oyster, eel, beef tongue, coal, and Indian curry.</li><li>The average number of licks to finish a scoop of ice cream is 50.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 40":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Despite being one of the wealthiest artists of his time, he lived like a hermit and rarely met people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Michelangelo</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>His famous statue ‘David’ was made from a discarded block of marble.</li><li>When he was young, his nose was broken by a jealous rival — he was punched so hard he had the damage for the rest of his life.</li><li>Many of his works were created using his own likeness.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 41":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can reach speeds of up to 193 kms per hour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Golden Eagle</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The Golden Eagle can swim if it is totally necessary.</li><li>In winter and early spring they defend their territories with a ‘sky dance’.</li><li>Parents use their bodies to insulate chicks in extreme temperatures.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 42":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is one of the oldest sports in the world, and the first known usage of it was in 2340 BC by the Babylonians.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Archery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Olympic archers used to shoot at pigeons.</li><li>Archery has been said to be 3x safer than golf, with only 1 injury per 2000 participants. It is also safer than bowling.</li><li>Archery was the first sport to allow women to compete in the Olympic Games.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 43":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The standard measurement used to measure the height of this animal is ‘hands’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Horse</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Because of the one-way mode of their digestive tract, horses cannot burp.</li><li>You can estimate a horse’s age by its teeth, although if it’s old and its teeth are bad it will need dental work done.</li><li>Most Arabic breeds of horses have fewer bones than other breeds — in their tail, vertebrae, and ribs.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 44":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In America, these get lost, sat on, or broken every 14 minutes. That means over 37,500 every year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sunglasses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Only 25% of sunglasses actually protect your eyes from the sun’s harmful rays. The other 75% are only useful as a fashion accessory.</li><li>Different color lenses were developed by James Ayscough, in the hope of resolving color blindness.</li><li>They were invented 2000 years ago by the Inuit. They were made from animal bone, leather, and wood, with small slits cut so only a tiny amount of light could get through.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 45":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is the most stolen book in the world. It's regularly taken from hotels, hospitals, and book stores.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Bible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is also the most-read book in the world, with over 100 million copies sold or donated every year.</li><li>The Bible has about 40 different authors and was originally written in 3 different languages: Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek.</li><li>It is over 600,000 words long and has about 1189 chapters — 929 in the Old Testament and 260 in the New Testament.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 46":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ancient Egyptians and Mesopotamians were known to do this to their food to preserve it through long journeys and harsh seasons.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pickling (Pickles)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pickles are actually cucumbers that have been brined in a solution of vinegar, water, and spices.</li><li>Cleopatra supposedly credited her good looks to her love of pickles, and Julius Caesar was said to have fed pickles to his troops in the belief it would make them stronger.</li><li>In Connecticut, legend has it that state law requires that a pickle must bounce in order to legally be called a pickle.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 47":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This person is sometimes nicknamed ‘La Pulga’ (‘The Flea’) because of their quick and amazing movements.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lionel Messi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He had a growth deficiency growing up, but when he moved to Barcelona at 13 years old, the club paid for his growth hormone treatment.</li><li>Messi made his debut for Barcelona’s first team in 2004, when he was only 17 years old.</li><li>He has scored 665 goals with his left foot, 100 with his right, and 26 with his head.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 48":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In ancient times, soldiers in this country sometimes wore armour made from paper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">China</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Put together, all of China’s railway lines could loop around the world twice.</li><li>Parts of China can range from 49 degrees Celsius in summer to -27 degrees in winter.</li><li>In 2010, a 2,400-year-old pot of soup was unearthed in Xi’an, China.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 49":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Over 75 different pieces of wood are used to build a modern version of one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Violin</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Playing the violin burns about 170 calories per hour — an hour of walking burns between 200-300 calories.</li><li>Violin strings were originally made of sheep gut (commonly known as catgut), which was stretched, dried, and twisted.</li><li>The world record for cycling backwards while playing a violin is 60.45 kms, set in 5 hours and 8 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 50":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These can reach a temperature of almost 30,000 degrees Celsius — 5 times hotter than the surface of the sun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lightning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The flash we see travels at the speed of light, about 900,000,000 kph. The lightning itself only travels around 400,000 kph — at that speed it would take 55 minutes to reach the moon.</li><li>The actual width of a lightning bolt is only 2-3 cms, whereas its length averages 3-5 kms.</li><li>When lightning strikes sand or sandy soil, it fuses the grains together to create a small glass-like tube known as fulgurite.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 51":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The first of these were sticks used by ancient Egyptians, Greeks, Romans, and Indians — frayed on one end to make them work more effectively.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Toothbrush</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Until the early 1900s, toothbrush bristles were made from Siberian hog hair, until the Miracle Tuft soft-bristled toothbrush was invented.</li><li>One in ten people admit that they regularly forget to brush their teeth.</li><li>Dentists recommend keeping your toothbrush at least 2 meters away from the toilet, to prevent the spread of airborne bacteria when flushing.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 52":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A person with good eyesight can probably see between 2000-2500 of these at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stars</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Usually red means hot and blue means cold, but stars are the opposite — red stars are cooler and blue stars are hotter.</li><li>On a good night you can see 17,000,000,000,000,000 miles — that’s 17 quadrillion miles to the bright star Deneb.</li><li>Every star you can see in our night sky is bigger than our sun. Only a few faint stars are about the same size as the sun.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 53":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">His boat ‘The Endeavour’ almost sank when he hit sharp coral in the Great Barrier Reef, Australia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Captain Cook</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cook was famous for his mapmaking, and was the first European to define the outline of New Zealand.</li><li>NASA named two of their space shuttles after Cook’s ships, HMS Enterprise and Endeavour. Astronauts also wore special medals to honor Cook.</li><li>He was killed in Hawaii by local natives — beaten with clubs, and then stabbed in the back. He was later given a king’s burial.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 54":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This person refused to participate in his main event at the Olympics because the qualifying round was on a Sunday and he was a devout Christian.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Eric Liddell</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>He was born in China to Scottish parents who were missionaries there.</li><li>In 1943, during WW2, he was imprisoned in Weihsien internment camp, where he organised games for children, who called him ‘Uncle Eric’.</li><li>He was a gifted athlete, excelling at both running and rugby. He won an Olympic gold medal in the 400m — which wasn’t even his strongest event.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 55":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Because this country is the closest to the west side of the International Date Line, it is the first to see the new day — and therefore the first to celebrate the new year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samoa</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Samoa was known as Western Samoa up until 1997 — that’s why the currency ‘Samoan Tala’ is sometimes referred to as ‘WST’.</li><li>Samoans often sit on a mat on the floor with their legs crossed beneath them, with the lavalava covering their toes — it’s considered rude to point your toes at someone.</li><li>There is a special day in October called ‘White Sunday’. Children are celebrated on this day, made their favorite foods, and given special gifts.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 56":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This place is geographically unique because it is one of only 8 cities that have a near-exact antipodal city.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christchurch</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It's believed the Christchurch area was first settled in 1250 by tribes hunting the now-extinct moa.</li><li>Canterbury Pilgrims had goals of building a new city around a cathedral and college, like Christ Church, Oxford in England — hence the name Christchurch.</li><li>On July 31, 1856, Christchurch became the first official city in New Zealand, and it quickly grew as more European settlers arrived.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 57":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In 2013, Aaron ‘Wheelz’ Fotheringham became the first person to successfully complete a backflip using one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wheelchair</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Wheelchair basketball was invented by American World War II veterans with spinal cord injuries. The first official game was played in 1946.</li><li>In some countries, laws require public buildings to be wheelchair accessible — these laws have helped make public spaces more inclusive for people with disabilities.</li><li>The Guinness World Record for the fastest wheelchair speed is held by Heinz Frei from Switzerland, who reached 40.6 kms per hour in 2011.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 58":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Vincent Van Gogh painted four still-life paintings featuring these — one was called ‘The (this) Eaters’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Potatoes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Marie Antoinette liked potatoes so much that she wore their blossoms in her hair.</li><li>The Incas of Peru were the first to cultivate potatoes, around 8000 BC to 5000 BC.</li><li>Early growers discovered that just one acre of potatoes could feed ten people — much better than wheat or oats.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 59":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The South American one of these is so big that if it were a country, it would be the ninth biggest in the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Amazon Rainforest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It can take ten minutes for a falling raindrop to travel from the rainforest’s thick canopy down to the floor.</li><li>A quarter of the ingredients in modern medicines come from rainforest plants.</li><li>A tree known as the idiot fruit grows in Australia’s Daintree rainforest.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 60":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Laid end to end, the number of these sold in a year would reach more than five times around the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">LEGO</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The world’s tallest LEGO tower is 28.7m high, made from 465,000 bricks!</li><li>During the Christmas season, almost 28 LEGO sets are sold every second!</li><li>LEGO Minifigures are the world’s largest population, with over 4 billion of them around the world!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 61":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">These don’t have bones — their skeletons are made of cartilage, the same soft, flexible stuff your ears and the tip of your nose are made of.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sharks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Most sharks can see well in dimly lit areas, have fantastic night vision, and can see colors.</li><li>Shark skin feels exactly like sandpaper because it’s made up of tiny teeth-like structures called placoid scales, also known as dermal denticles.</li><li>When you flip a shark upside down, it goes into a trance-like state called tonic immobility.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 62":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Between 1784 and 1811, these were taxed in the UK. If you sold them you needed a special licence, and buyers had to pay a tax too — a way for the government to get money from the rich.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hats</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In Fargo, North Dakota, USA, it’s illegal to wear a hat while dancing — it’s also illegal to wear a hat in a place where dancing might take place.</li><li>There are over 100 types of hats, including bucket hats, beanies, and baseball caps — plus berets, fezzes, sombreros, cowboy hats, trilbies, and top hats!</li><li>The world’s tallest hat is 4.8 metres tall. It was made by Odilon Ozare, who had to wear it and walk over 10 metres to get the world record!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 63":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sport is so popular that it’s considered the second most-loved sport in the world, enjoyed by more than 2.5 billion fans in 180 countries.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Cricket</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Cricket actually began in 16th century England, when children used to play it on the streets.</li><li>Originally, cricket wickets had two stumps and only one bail — the third stump wasn’t introduced until 1775.</li><li>The first cricket World Cup was played by women, not men, two years before the men’s tournament arrived. The inaugural Women’s World Cup was held in 1973, hosted by England.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 64":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The world record for the longest of these goes to Kevin ‘Catfish’ McCarthy, who spent 340 hours and 40 minutes in one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Showers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Research has shown that 42% of people pee in the shower. Another study found that 7% of people openly admit to not bathing at all — be that a shower or a bath!</li><li>Showers are a great money saver compared to baths, which use around 170 litres of water — a 5-minute shower uses just 70–115 litres.</li><li>The world’s most expensive shower costs $160,400 NZD, from a company called Sliver Tag. With an array of super geeky gadgets, the only thing this shower doesn’t do is dry you afterwards!</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 65":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the right environment, these are known to live 100 years on average and can grow up to 46 metres high.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pine Trees (Christmas Trees)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>They can survive at elevations up to 13,000 metres, and are found in many mountain ranges and peaks.</li><li>Pines never shed their leaves — their leaves stay green all year round, and most pines have thick, scaly bark that bleeds resin.</li><li>Christmas trees are really pine trees — because they stay green year-round and are very hardy, they can handle all manner of decorations.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 66":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This doesn’t have an expiry date, but it will change over time — which is why you should use it within the first three years.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Perfume</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>You shouldn’t rub your wrists together after putting on perfume — the friction creates a reaction between the fragrance and your skin’s oils, which can change the scent.</li><li>The same scent can smell different on two different people, because perfume mixes with your skin’s own chemistry.</li><li>Research shows perfume can alter your mood depending on its ingredients — lavender helps you relax, while a citrus-infused scent awakens your senses.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 67":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">During World War II, a type of this plant fiber was used to stuff life jackets — about six times more buoyant than cork.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Weeds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Mustard weeds are edible — there are countless varieties of mustard, and while some are more palatable than others, many can be used in salads or for making mustard.</li><li>In 1941, a Swiss hiker came home from a walk in the Alps with burrs all over his clothes and his dog. Looking at the burrs under a microscope, he saw they were covered with millions of tiny hooks — and went on to invent Velcro.</li><li>Pigweed grows 1–3 inches a day, drawing huge amounts of water and nutrients. It can reach the size of a small tree, produces up to a million seeds per plant, and has become resistant to Roundup and several other herbicides.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 68":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">They have a highly specialized cardiovascular system to manage blood flow to their brains. Their hearts are incredibly strong and large — about 0.6 metres long and weighing around 11 kgs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Giraffes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Giraffes primarily feed on the leaves, fruits, and flowers of woody plants, with acacia species being a favorite. Their tongues can be up to 45 cms long.</li><li>Giraffes usually sleep standing up, which helps them escape predators quickly if needed. They typically sleep for just a few minutes at a time, totalling about 30 minutes to 2 hours in a 24-hour period — one of the shortest sleep requirements in the animal kingdom.</li><li>Giraffe pregnancies last about 15 months. The female gives birth standing up, so the newborn’s first experience outside the womb is almost a 2-metre drop to the ground.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 69":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A study by the University of Pennsylvania found that neighborhoods with more of these had lower crime rates.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas Lights</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In 1882, Edison’s colleague Edward H. Johnson strung 80 colored electric lights around a Christmas tree in Edison’s New York City laboratory. They were a huge success and quickly became a popular way to decorate for the holiday season.</li><li>Early electric Christmas lights were hand-wired with delicate glass bulbs, making them very expensive. It wasn’t until the early 1900s that they became affordable for the average family.</li><li>In 2014, a Christmas tree in Norway was decorated with 500,000 lights. The tree was over 200 feet tall and could be seen for miles.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 70":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sea creature has three hearts and blue blood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Octopuses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Octopus blood is blue because it's copper-based instead of iron-based like ours.</li><li>They have no bones, so they can squeeze through any gap bigger than their beak, the only hard part of their body.</li><li>Octopuses can taste things just by touching them with their arms and suckers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 71":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This snack can pop up to 3 feet into the air.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Popcorn</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Each kernel contains a tiny bit of water, which turns to steam and builds pressure until the kernel explodes.</li><li>Native Americans were popping corn thousands of years before Europeans ever arrived.</li><li>The world's largest popcorn ball on record weighed over 9,000 kgs — heavier than an elephant.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 72":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">You actually can't fully sink in this, no matter what the movies show.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Quicksand</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quicksand is just ordinary sand that's been saturated with water, which makes it behave like a liquid until you move.</li><li>Since your body is less dense than quicksand, you naturally float instead of sinking all the way — panicking and thrashing is what actually pulls people under.</li><li>The safest way out is to move slowly, lean back to spread your weight, and paddle toward firmer ground.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 73":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some of these shoot out strands of glass instead of lava.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Volcanoes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Certain eruptions produce thin strands of volcanic glass nicknamed ‘Pele’s hair’, after the Hawaiian goddess of volcanoes.</li><li>Volcanoes exist on other planets too — Mars is home to Olympus Mons, the largest volcano in the entire solar system.</li><li>There are roughly 1,500 potentially active volcanoes around the world, not even counting the ones hidden underwater.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 74":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Time moves slower the closer you get to one of these.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Black Holes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Black holes form when massive stars collapse in on themselves at the end of their life.</li><li>Nothing can escape a black hole once it crosses the ‘event horizon’ — not even light.</li><li>There's a supermassive black hole sitting at the very center of our own galaxy, the Milky Way.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 75":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The very first versions of this ride were made of ice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Roller Coasters</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>In 17th century Russia, people built giant ice slides and rode down them on sleds — an early ancestor of the roller coaster.</li><li>The first wheeled roller coaster was built in France after warmer weather made the ice slides impossible to keep running year-round.</li><li>The tallest roller coaster in the world today stands taller than a 45-story building.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 76":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This toy was once used as a hunting weapon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yo-Yos</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Ancient Filipino hunters are believed to have used large yo-yo-like weapons on ropes that could be thrown and pulled back.</li><li>The yo-yo is one of the oldest toys in the world, with versions dating back over 2,500 years to ancient Greece.</li><li>The world's largest working yo-yo weighs almost 2 tonnes.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 77":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This piece of equipment was studied to help train pilots and astronauts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Trampolines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Early trampolines were inspired by circus performers who used safety nets to bounce after trapeze acts.</li><li>NASA studied trampoline-style bouncing because it builds coordination in ways similar to moving in zero gravity.</li><li>Trampolining became an official Olympic sport in the year 2000.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 78":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sport started out being called ‘sidewalk surfing’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Skateboarding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Skateboarding began in California in the 1950s, when surfers wanted something to ride when the waves were flat.</li><li>Early skateboards were often homemade, using wooden boards with roller skate wheels bolted on.</li><li>Skateboarding became an official Olympic sport in 2020.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 79":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Despite the name, this creature isn't actually a worm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Glow Worms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Glow worms are really the larvae of certain beetles and flies, not true worms at all.</li><li>Their glow comes from a chemical reaction inside their bodies called bioluminescence.</li><li>They use their glow to attract prey — insects fly toward the light and get caught in sticky threads.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 80":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This plant can count to five.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Venus Flytraps</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A Venus flytrap's trap only snaps shut after something touches its trigger hairs twice within about 20 seconds — a built-in counter that stops it wasting energy on false alarms.</li><li>It takes several more touches after that before the trap actually starts digesting its prey.</li><li>Each individual trap can only close and reopen a handful of times before it stops working and falls off.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 81":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This creature isn't actually an eel at all.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Electric Eels</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Electric eels are more closely related to catfish and carp than to true eels.</li><li>They can produce a shock of up to 600 volts — enough to stun a horse.</li><li>They need to surface for air every so often, since they breathe partly through their mouths.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 82":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This mountain grows a few millimeters taller every single year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mount Everest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Everest is still growing because of the ongoing collision between the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates.</li><li>Its summit is so high that some planes fly below it.</li><li>Hundreds of climbers have died attempting to reach the top, and many remain on the mountain because it's too dangerous to bring them down.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 83":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal can hold its breath longer than a dolphin.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sloths</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sloths can slow their heart rate right down and hold their breath for up to 40 minutes underwater.</li><li>They only come down from the trees about once a week, usually just to go to the bathroom.</li><li>Algae often grows in a sloth's fur, giving it a greenish tint that helps camouflage it in the trees.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 84":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal has fingerprints that are almost identical to a human's.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Koalas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Koala fingerprints are so similar to human ones that they've occasionally confused crime scene investigators.</li><li>Koalas sleep between 18 and 22 hours a day.</li><li>Their diet is almost entirely eucalyptus leaves, which are toxic to most other animals.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 85":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal glows under UV light.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Platypuses</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Platypus fur glows a blue-green color under ultraviolet light, though scientists aren't fully sure why.</li><li>Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.</li><li>They're one of the only mammals in the world that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 86":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal's famous ‘horn’ is actually a giant tooth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Narwhals</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A narwhal's tusk is really an overgrown canine tooth that can grow up to 3 metres long.</li><li>Usually only males grow the tusk, though some females can too.</li><li>The tusk is packed with nerve endings and is thought to help narwhals sense changes in their environment.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 87":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Males of this bird lose their spectacular tail feathers every single year.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Peacocks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Peacocks molt their long tail feathers after breeding season and grow a brand new set before the next one.</li><li>Only males have the famous colorful tail — females, called peahens, are much more plain.</li><li>A peacock's tail feathers can make up over 60% of its total body length.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 88":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal doesn't mainly change color to hide from predators.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Chameleons</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Chameleons mostly change color to communicate mood or to help regulate their body temperature, not primarily for camouflage.</li><li>Their eyes can move independently, letting them look in two different directions at the same time.</li><li>A chameleon's tongue can shoot out faster than a Formula 1 car accelerates.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 89":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Some versions of this toy are designed to never come back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Boomerangs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Returning boomerangs are actually the exception — most boomerangs, historically used for hunting, were designed to fly straight and not return.</li><li>The curved shape of a returning boomerang works a bit like an airplane wing, creating lift as it spins through the air.</li><li>The oldest known boomerang is over 20,000 years old, found in a cave in Poland.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 90":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The colors in this display come from burning different metals.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fireworks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Different metal compounds create different colors — strontium makes red, copper makes blue, and barium makes green.</li><li>Fireworks were invented in China over 1,000 years ago, originally as a way to scare off evil spirits.</li><li>Blue is the hardest firework color to create, since the copper compounds involved are especially unstable at high heat.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 91":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This dinosaur had a bite force strong enough to crush a car.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">T-Rex</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A T-Rex's bite force is estimated at up to 12,800 kgs — among the strongest of any land animal ever.</li><li>Despite its huge size, a T-Rex's arms were surprisingly short — only about the length of an adult human's arm.</li><li>Scientists believe young T-Rexes may have had feathers.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 92":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This bird proposes to its mate with a pebble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Penguins</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Some penguin species, like Adelie penguins, offer a smooth pebble to a potential mate as a kind of proposal.</li><li>Penguins can't fly, but they ‘fly’ through water instead, reaching speeds of up to 35 km/h.</li><li>Emperor penguin dads keep their egg warm on their feet for around two months without eating, while the mothers hunt at sea.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 93":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This insect never sleeps.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Honey Bees</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Honey bees don't sleep the way humans do, but they do have rest periods where their antennae droop and they go still.</li><li>A single bee produces only about one twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in its entire lifetime.</li><li>Bees tell each other where to find food using a special ‘waggle dance’.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 94":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This sweet treat was originally made from a plant root.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Marshmallows</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Marshmallows get their name from the marsh mallow plant, whose root sap was originally used to make the treat.</li><li>Ancient Egyptians were among the first to combine marsh mallow sap with honey to create an early version of the candy.</li><li>Modern marshmallows don't use the plant at all anymore — they're made mainly from sugar, corn syrup, and gelatin.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 95":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This packing material was originally invented as wallpaper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bubble Wrap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Bubble wrap was invented in 1957 when two engineers tried to make textured wallpaper by sealing two shower curtains together.</li><li>After the wallpaper idea failed, they tried marketing it as greenhouse insulation before it finally caught on as packaging material.</li><li>There's an unofficial holiday called ‘Bubble Wrap Appreciation Day’, celebrated on the last Monday of January.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 96":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This puzzle has over 43 quintillion possible combinations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rubik's Cube</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A Rubik's Cube has roughly 43 quintillion possible arrangements — that's a 43 followed by 18 zeros.</li><li>It was invented in 1974 by a Hungarian professor, originally as a teaching tool to help explain 3D geometry.</li><li>The current world record for solving a Rubik's Cube is under 4 seconds.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 97":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This invisible force can make your hair stand straight up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Static Electricity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Static electricity happens when tiny charged particles called electrons build up on a surface and have nowhere to go.</li><li>Lightning is really just a giant, supercharged version of the same static shock you get from touching a doorknob.</li><li>Dry winter air makes static shocks more common, since moisture in the air normally helps the charge drain away harmlessly.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 98":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This animal is often called the world's happiest animal because of its permanent smile.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Quokkas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Quokkas are small marsupials native to Australia, and the shape of their face just naturally looks like a big smile.</li><li>They're famously unafraid of humans, which has made ‘quokka selfies’ a popular trend.</li><li>Quokkas are nocturnal and spend most of the day resting in shady spots.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 99":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Babies do this while they're still inside the womb.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hiccups</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unborn babies can hiccup for minutes at a time, and scientists think it might help develop their breathing muscles.</li><li>The longest recorded case of hiccups in a person lasted 68 years.</li><li>Hiccups happen when your diaphragm suddenly contracts, and the ‘hic’ sound is your vocal cords snapping shut right after.</li></ul></div></div>"},"Week 100":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Question</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This insect's light is nearly 100% efficient, wasting almost no energy as heat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Answer</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fireflies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Facts</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A regular light bulb wastes most of its energy as heat, but a firefly's glow is almost entirely light — one of the most efficient light sources known.</li><li>Firefly light comes from a chemical reaction involving a compound called luciferin.</li><li>Each species of firefly has its own unique flashing pattern, which helps them recognize potential mates in the dark.</li></ul></div></div>"}}},"Memory Verse":{"label":"Theme","children":{"Aroha · Love":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">1</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 3:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God so loved the world, that he gave his only born Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">2</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:19</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We love him, because he first loved us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">3</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mark 12:30–31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, with all your mind, and with all your strength. This is the first commandment. The second is like this: You shall love your neighbour as yourself. There is no other commandment greater than these.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">4</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 13:4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Love is patient and is kind. Love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">5</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 13:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But now faith, hope, and love remain — these three. The greatest of these is love.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">6</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 13:34</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another. Just as I have loved you, you also love one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">7</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 5:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But God shows his own love toward us, in that while we were still sinners, Christ died for us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">8</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beloved, let us love one another, for love is of God, and everyone who loves is born of God and knows God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">9</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 4:32</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Be kind to one another, tender-hearted, forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">10</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Above all these things, put on love, which is the bond of perfection.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">11</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Above all things be earnest in your love for one another, for love covers a multitude of sins.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">12</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 15:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">13</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 4:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Beloved, if God loved us this way, we also ought to love one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">14</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 17:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">A friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">15</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 5:22–23</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith, gentleness, and self-control. Against such things there is no law.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">16</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 136:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Give thanks to the LORD, for he is good, for his loving kindness endures forever.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">17</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In love of the family, be tenderly affectioned one to another. In honour, prefer one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">18</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 John 3:18</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Little children, let us not love in word only, or with the tongue only, but in deed and truth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">19</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Zephaniah 3:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD your God is among you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you with his love. He will rejoice over you with singing.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">20</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Jeremiah 31:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD appeared long ago to me, saying, “I have loved you with an everlasting love. Therefore I have continued to be loving and kind to you.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">21</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Deuteronomy 6:5</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your strength.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">22</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 14:15</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">If you love me, keep my commandments.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">23</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 16:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let all that you do be done in love.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">24</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 22:37–39</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind. This is the first and great commandment. And a second is like it: You shall love your neighbour as yourself.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">25</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 13:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Owe no one anything, except to love one another, for the one who loves his neighbour has fulfilled the law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Mōhio · Know":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">26</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Jeremiah 29:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For I know the plans I have for you, says the LORD: plans for peace, and not for disaster, to give you a future and a hope.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">27</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 3:5–6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make your paths straight.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">28</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 139:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I will give thanks to you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Your works are wonderful. My soul knows that very well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">29</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Genesis 1:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">30</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 1:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">31</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 40:31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Those who wait for the LORD will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run and not be weary. They will walk and not faint.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">32</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 119:105</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Your word is a lamp to my feet, and a light for my path.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">33</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Timothy 3:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Every Scripture is God-breathed and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">34</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 46:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Be still, and know that I am God. I will be exalted among the nations. I will be exalted in the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">35</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 55:8–9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">“For my thoughts are not your thoughts, and your ways are not my ways,” says the LORD. “For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are my ways higher than your ways, and my thoughts than your thoughts.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">36</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 100:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Know that the LORD, he is God. It is he who made us, and we are his. We are his people, and the sheep of his pasture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">37</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Exodus 20:3</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You shall have no other gods before me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">38</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Deuteronomy 6:4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Hear, O Israel: the LORD is our God. The LORD is one.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">39</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 90:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Before the mountains were born, before you had formed the earth and the world, from everlasting to everlasting, you are God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">40</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 1:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">By him all things were created, in the heavens and on the earth, visible and invisible. All things have been created through him and for him.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">41</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Hebrews 11:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now faith is being sure of what we hope for, and certain of what we do not see.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">42</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 8:28</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">43</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 139:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You formed my inmost being. You knit me together in my mother’s womb.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">44</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 14:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus said to him, “I am the way, the truth, and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me.”</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">45</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 41:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. I will help you. I will uphold you with my righteous right hand.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">46</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Psalm 23:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD is my shepherd; I shall lack nothing.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">47</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 6:33</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But seek first God’s Kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">48</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 1:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge, but fools despise wisdom and instruction.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">49</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>John 8:32</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You will know the truth, and the truth will set you free.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">50</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 5:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. See, all things have become new.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Manaaki · Serve":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">51</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">As each has received a gift, use it to serve one another, as good managers of God’s grace in its many forms.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">52</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 5:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For you were called to freedom. Only don’t use your freedom as an opportunity for the flesh, but through love serve one another.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">53</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:23</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whatever you do, work at it heartily, as for the Lord and not for men.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">54</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Mark 10:45</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For even the Son of Man came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">55</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Joshua 24:15</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Choose for yourselves today whom you will serve...but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">56</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 20:26–28</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant. Whoever wants to be first among you shall be your slave.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">57</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Having gifts that differ according to the grace given to us, let us use them well.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">58</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 2:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For we are God’s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God prepared beforehand, that we should walk in them.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">59</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 12:27</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now you are the body of Christ, and individually members of it.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">60</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 2:3–4</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Do nothing through selfish ambition or vanity, but in humility count others better than yourselves, each looking not to his own interests, but to the interests of others.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">61</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 6:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let us not grow weary in doing good, for we will reap a harvest in due season, if we don’t give up.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">62</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Timothy 4:12</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let no one look down on your youth, but be an example to the believers in word, in conduct, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">63</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Hebrews 6:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God is not unjust, so as to forget your work and the love you showed for his name, in that you served the saints, and still do serve them.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">64</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 4:11</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...so that in everything God may be glorified through Jesus Christ, to whom belong the glory and the power forever and ever. Amen.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">65</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>James 1:22</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But be doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving yourselves.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">66</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 25:40</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">67</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Proverbs 3:27</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is in your power to do it.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">68</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Luke 6:38</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Give, and it will be given to you: good measure, pressed down, shaken together, and running over, will be given to you.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">69</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 9:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let each one give as he has decided in his heart, not reluctantly or under compulsion, for God loves a cheerful giver.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">70</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:1</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I urge you, brothers and sisters, by the mercies of God, to offer your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to God — this is your true and proper worship.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">71</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 10:31</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">So whether you eat or drink, or whatever you do, do it all for the glory of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">72</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:16</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your light shine before others, that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">73</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Acts 20:35</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...It is more blessed to give than to receive.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">74</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 6:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">With good will doing service, as to the Lord, and not to men.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">75</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 3:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father through him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Pānga · Impact":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-amber\">Memory Verses</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data lt-tbl-striped\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:44px;\">#</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\" style=\"width:190px;\">Reference</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Verse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">76</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">77</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 28:19–20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Go and make disciples of all nations, baptising them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to obey everything I have commanded you. And remember, I am with you always, to the very end of the age.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">78</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 6:10</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your Kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth, as it is in heaven.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">79</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 4:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">80</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Corinthians 5:20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">We are therefore ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making his appeal through us. We beg you on Christ’s behalf: be reconciled to God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">81</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Isaiah 6:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Here I am. Send me!</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">82</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:13</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">83</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Acts 1:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">You will receive power when the Holy Spirit comes on you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">84</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 12:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Don’t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you may know what is the good, pleasing, and perfect will of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">85</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 2:8–9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For it is by grace you have been saved, through faith — and this is not from yourselves, it is the gift of God — not by works, so that no one can boast.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">86</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>James 2:17</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">So faith, if it has no works, is dead, being alone.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">87</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Peter 2:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">But you are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, God’s special possession, that you may proclaim the excellencies of him who called you out of darkness into his marvellous light.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">88</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called children of God.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">89</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Galatians 6:2</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Carry each other’s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">90</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>2 Timothy 1:7</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">For God gave us a spirit not of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">91</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Joshua 1:9</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be discouraged, for the LORD your God is with you wherever you go.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">92</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Philippians 1:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Being confident of this very thing, that he who began a good work in you will carry it on to completion until the day of Christ Jesus.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">93</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Esther 4:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Who knows whether you have come to the kingdom for such a time as this?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">94</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Matthew 5:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">95</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Romans 10:14</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">How then can they call on the one they have not believed in? And how can they believe in the one of whom they have not heard? And how can they hear without someone preaching to them?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">96</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>1 Corinthians 15:58</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Therefore, my beloved brothers and sisters, be steadfast, immovable, always excelling in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">97</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Ephesians 3:20</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Now to him who is able to do far more abundantly than all we ask or think, according to the power that works within us.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">98</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Micah 6:8</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...what does the LORD require of you, but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">99</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Colossians 4:6</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Let your speech always be gracious, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">100</td><td class=\"lt-td\"><strong>Revelation 21:5</strong></td><td class=\"lt-td\">...Behold, I am making all things new...</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"maths":{"label":"Chapter","children":{"Chapter 1: Numbers to 1 000 000 — Place Value":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students will be looking at numbers and their place value to 1 000 000. The chapter begins reviewing how to read and write numbers to 100 000, quickly moving onto numbers to 1 000 000. Time is spent using concrete materials to represent numbers to 1 000 000, including place-value counters and place-value charts. Students then compare numbers to 1 000 000 using their knowledge of place value in addition to bar model supports to assist them. Students complete the unit by making number patterns and rounding numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Reading and Writing Numbers to 100 000":{"label":"Lesson 1: Reading and Writing Numbers to 100 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">2 - 5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters and show them the Explore task. Ask them if Ruby will need more counters, or other types of counters. How many more? Allow students time to work on this and show how they would create the given number. Ask them if it is true that because the ten-thousands column holds the highest value, it will always require the most number of counters. How do we know? Tell them to come up with scenarios that disprove this statement. Ask students if they are able to tell you the exact number of counters needed to represent a number without counting the counters. How would they represent a number above 9999 without place-value counters for 10 000? Is it possible? Work through the Master section to recap how the given number can be represented using place-value counters and place-value cards. Explore the value of each digit in a number. Ask students how we would represent zero as a placeholder using a variety of methods: with counters, in words, in a place-value chart and in the\nexpanded form. During Guided Practice, students are showing numbers using place-value\ncounters and place-value charts, writing numbers in numerals and in\nwords, and determining the value of a digit in a number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Showing 5-digit numbers using place-value charts, and representing the numbers in expanded form; two of the numbers have zeros as placeholders.</li><li>Example 2: Writing 5-digit numbers in expanded form and in words.</li><li>Example 3: Identifying 5-digit numbers shown with place-value counters and writing the numbers in numerals and in words.</li><li>Example 4: Identifying the value of the digit 8 in 5-digit numbers.</li><li>Example 5: Writing numbers shown as words using numerals.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not know what to do with zeros in relationship to place value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 using place-value counters and place-value cards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers to 100 000. Students can represent numbers less than 100 000 using place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have difficulty saying numbers above 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 using a place-value chart.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers less than 100 000 using words and numerals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students show 56 thousand using 56 thousands rather than 5 ten thousands and 6 thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 100 000. Students can write numbers to 100 000 using words and numerals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 100 000 on a place-value chart. Students can recognise the values of digits in a place-value chart.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add the values of each place-value column together to make a number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 100,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">6 - 9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters and place-value charts. Show them the Explore task and allow them time to work on it. After a few minutes, ask students if they know a simpler way of showing the number, using only tens and ones. Ask them if this is possible and if it is easier. What might some of the challenges be in representing a large number using only tens and ones? Are you able to show the number using any other materials? Work through Master with the class showing the number using place-value counters, a place-value chart and the expanded form using addition. Allow them time to do this with another 6-digit number and discuss how they would do it. Recap how to represent zero as a placeholder using a variety of methods. During Guided Practice, students are writing and representing numbers in a variety of ways.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Writing 6-digit numbers in words and deciding on a</li><li>second way to show the numbers.</li><li>Example 2: Writing 6-digit numbers shown in words using expanded form.</li><li>Example 3: Identifying the value of the digit 9 in 6-digit numbers.</li><li>Example 4: Identifying 6-digit numbers shown as place-value counters and writing the numbers in numerals and in words.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 100,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 2 and 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10 - 13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and represent numbers to 1 000 000 using place-value counters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Allow them time to complete the main task creating the greatest and smallest numbers using six digit cards. After some time, introduce misconceptions. For example, state that they can make the greatest number by placing the 7 in the hundred-thousands column, and allow them time to discuss this. As students find the greatest and smallest numbers they can make, label the numbers with their names, for example, Lulu's group's greatest number, Charles's group's smallest number and so on. Ask them to explain why they believe their numbers are the greatest or smallest possible. Once they have found the greatest and smallest numbers, let them discuss and discover a generalisation (a 'rule') they can use to determine the next smallest and greatest numbers. Ask them to use this rule to determine the greatest and smallest numbers using the digit cards in Master 2. During Guided Practice, students are writing numbers in numerals and in words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students use 0 as a digit in the hundred thousands column when trying to find the smallest 6-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using a place-value chart. Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 1 000 000.</li><li>Students can write numbers to 1 000 000 using words and numerals.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students see the counters as representative of a place-value column.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to represent place value.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use 0 as a digit in the hundred thousands column when trying to find the smallest 6-digit number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers to 1 000 000 using a place-value chart. Students can identify the value of a digit in numbers to 1 000 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in a place-value chart. Students can use and apply the terms 'greatest' and 'smallest'.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers to 1 000 000 using words and numerals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can write numbers represented by place-value counters in words and numerals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lessons 1 and 2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">10 - 13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, give each student a 9-sided dice and ask them to work in pairs. Show them the Explore task and ask them to play the game against each other. Ask them to find the easiest way to compare numbers. After some time, introduce misconceptions. Ask for two numbers the students have made and write these on the board. Tell them your friend says the easiest way to compare numbers is by looking at the ones; the number with the greater amount of ones is the greater number. When they disagree ask them to explain why this is not correct. How does the position of a digit in a number affect its value? Does the greatest digit have the greatest value? Is this ever the case? Then ask them to work in pairs to come up with a rule for comparing numbers. Provide students with numbers that have the same digits in different place-value columns. Allow them time to work out what to do when this is the case and then let them share their methods with the rest of the class. During Guided Practice, students are comparing numbers using the terms 'greater than' and 'less than', arranging numbers in order and deciding whether it is possible to compare numbers with missing digits. smallest and greatest numbers. Ask them to use this rule to determine the greatest and smallest numbers using the digit cards in Master 2. During Guided Practice, students are writing numbers in numerals and in words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value chart (one each)</li><li>9-sided dice (one each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li><li>greater, greatest</li><li>smaller, smallest</li><li>greater than</li><li>less than</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>same.</li><li>Example 3: Arranging four 6-digit numbers in order from greatest to smallest.</li><li>Example 4: Comparing two 6-digit numbers and deciding which is the greater/smaller number. (a) The first digit of each number is the same. (b) The first two digits of each number are the same.</li><li>Example 5: Comparing two 6-digit numbers and deciding whether it is possible to identify the greater number. (a) One number has two digits missing and the other number has one digit missing. (b)–(c) Both numbers have one digit missing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place the higher-value digits in</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers using a</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">the place-value columns with the least</td><td class=\"lt-td\">place-value chart or digit cards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify 'greater than' and</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students look at the wrong position to</td><td class=\"lt-td\">'less than' when comparing two numbers</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">determine the value of a number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">that are partially formed.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 5: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">19 - 22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them time to work in pairs. Ask them what is different about the three numbers. Tell them your friend said they knew which one was the greatest number straight away. How did they know this? How can we describe these numbers? Students should notice that there are two 5-digit numbers and one 6-digit number. Do they know that a 6-digit number is always greater than a 5-digit number? Ask them how they can compare numbers if they have the same number of digits. Work through the Master section to reinforce students' understanding of\n5-digit and 6-digit numbers and the value of each digit within those\nnumbers. During Guided Practice, students are arranging numbers in order from\nsmallest to greatest and vice versa. The terms 'ascending' and\n'descending' are introduced. Students are also comparing 5-digit and\n6-digit numbers with at least one missing digit in each number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set each)</li><li>9-sided dice (one each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use the left-most digits to compare numbers when one number has 6 digits and the other only has 5 digits.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers using a place-value chart. Students can correctly use the &gt; and &lt;</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students look at the wrong position to determine the value of a number. Students incorrectly use the &gt; and &lt; signs to mean their opposites.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">signs to compare numbers. Students can identify 'greater than' and 'less than' when numbers are partially formed. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can construct 6-digit numbers and read them</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number. Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 6: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">23 - 26</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using pictorial representations and proportionality.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to compare the numbers using addition and subtraction with their partner. Tell them they can represent the problems using a bar model. Is this possible with such large numbers? Give the class some time to draw comparison models to represent the numbers and the difference between the number of visitors as you work through Master 1 and 2 with them. Ask students to represent 215 000 using a bar, then ask them how they can represent 100 000. If necessary, prompt them to recognise that 100 000 is just under half of the bar. Allow students time to work out how to show the proportions using fractions and breaking the bars into parts. Then ask them to compare the number of visitors the wildlife park and the water park had by drawing another bar model. Ask students if it is possible to represent large numbers on a number line. Ask them to show you what that might look like by using the numbers from Master 1 and 2, then work through Master 3 and 4 together. Students often find number lines confusing as they don't know what scale to use, what the end points are, where to place the marks and what the value is of the space in between the marks. It is worth spending time making sure this is fully understood as number lines can be an incredibly powerful tool once mastered. During Guided Practice, students are comparing two numbers and working out how much more or less one number is compared to the other. Students are also using &gt; and &lt; to compare two numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1–2: Comparing two 6-digit numbers; determining</li><li>how many ten thousands less one number is than the other. Number line provided with increments.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers. Students can order numbers in ascending and descending order. Students can recognise which part of a number will change if it is increased or</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 7: Comparing Numbers to 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">27 - 30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare numbers to 1 000 000 using number bonds and number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how the numbers can be compared. Is it easier to identify the smallest than the greatest number? Why? Where should we look to determine the greatest number? Allow students time to discuss the number of digits in each number and how this helps us to compare them. They should also discuss how they can use the place value of each digit to work systematically when comparing numbers. Allow students some time to come up with their own methods and then work through Master to demonstrate the methods used in the examples. What is the relationship between addition and subtraction when the terms 'more than' and 'less than' are used? Tell them your friend says that when they see 'more than' they always add and when they see 'less than' they always subtract. Does this work? Why or why not? (see wording in Guided Practice) During Guided Practice, students are comparing numbers in multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. They are also increasing or decreasing given numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they have to look at the entire number when its value increases or decreases by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify which digits will be affected by a change of multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can use place-value counters to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use number lines to change numbers by multiples of 10 000 and 100 000. Students can correctly identify whether a number will be 'greater than' or 'less than' another number.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ to compare numbers. Students can use the symbols &gt; and &lt; to compare numbers. Students can recognise which part of a number will change if it is increased or decreased by a multiple of 10 000 or 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, compare, and order whole numbers up to 1,000,000, and represent them using base 10 structure.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students learn that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds. They learn that 10 units in each place can be renamed to make a greater value unit and that 1 unit in each place can be renamed to 10 units of a lesser value. So, 10 tens becomes 1 hundred and 1 ten becomes 10 ones.</li><li>This provides the foundation for understanding decimal fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Making Number Patterns":{"label":"Lesson 8: Making Number Patterns","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">31 - 33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to make and identify increasing number patterns using knowledge of place value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with digit cards and ask them to make the first number in the pattern with their partner. Ask them what they will have to change to make the next number and then the number after that. Is the change the same? How can the change be described? Explain to the students that we call this change 'the rule'. Determine the rule of the number pattern with the students. Show students another number pattern where the digit in the thousands, ten thousands, or hundred thousands column changes. Have them come up with rules for the pattern which are easy to understand as shown in the Master section. Ask them if they are able to create patterns if the digit in the ones column changes. Would these numbers still work? How else\nwould they need to change? Ask them if they can create their own number\npattern using 5-digit or 6-digit numbers. If there is a pattern that is unique\nto the class, tell them you will name the pattern after that student and keep\nit posted in the classroom. During Guided Practice, students are completing increasing number\npatterns.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>number patterns</li><li>increases</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2: Finding the rule and the missing numbers in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are increasing by 100 000 each time. (b) The numbers are increasing by 4000 each time. (c) The numbers are increasing by 70 000 each time.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the rule and the 8th number in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are increasing by 5000 each time. (b) The numbers are increasing by 100 000 each time. (c) The numbers are increasing by 60 000 each time.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students only see ‘635’ (the last three digits of the number, which in this case, stay constant) as the number pattern and cannot detect the change in the other numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can recognise and complete number patterns by identifying what is changing, and what is not, in a sequence.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare numbers to 1 000 000. Students can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number. Students can find patterns in numbers greater than 10 000. Students can arrange numbers from smallest to greatest and vice versa. Students can complete number patterns.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recognise and describe the rule for a growing pattern using words, tables, and diagrams, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should notice a change in a particular place, reinforcing the base 10 nature of the number system. The pattern of change remains consistent in whole numbers and decimal numbers.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Making Number Patterns":{"label":"Lesson 9: Making Number Patterns","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">34 - 37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to make number patterns that decrease in multiples of 10 000 or 100 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Give them some time to find the 'rule' and then ask them to explain how they know it is the 'rule'. Ask them if it is possible for one of the numbers in the pattern to be 784 312. Why or why not? Allow them time to prove that this idea is correct or incorrect with support. Once they realise it is not possible, ask them if it is true that the pattern's smallest possible number is 24 312. How can we know this without counting backwards all the way? Ask the students what the starting number would need to be to have a\nsmaller smallest possible number. What would the starting number need to\nbe to have a larger smallest possible number? Allow students time to\nexplore these concepts and then work through Master with the class to\nreinforce learning. During Guided Practice, students are completing decreasing number\npatterns.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>0–9 digit cards (four sets between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>number pattern</li><li>decreases</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>number patterns with 6-digit numbers. Each pair will need four</li><li>sets of 0–9 digit cards for this activity.</li><li>Variation Example 1: Finding the rule and the missing numbers in each number pattern. (a) The numbers are decreasing by 1000 each time. (b) The numbers are decreasing by 40 000 each time.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Misconceptions Students get stuck when crossing the boundaries forwards and backwards.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Non-Negotiable Students can recognise and complete number patterns by identifying what is changing, and what is not, in a sequence.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Formative Assessment Students can count backwards by 1, 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can recognise number patterns that increase and decrease by 1, 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can recognise which numbers in a pattern are changing. Pupils can read numbers to 1 000 000. Students can determine the values of digits in a 6-digit number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recognise and describe the rule for a growing pattern using words, tables, and diagrams, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students should notice a change in a particular place, reinforcing the base 10 nature of the number system. The pattern of change remains consistent in whole numbers and decimal numbers.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 10 000":{"label":"Lesson 10: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 10 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">38 - 40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 10 think Holly worked out that there were 10 000 more marathon runners in 000 using number lines. New York than in Boston.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them how they Give them time to work on the problem with their partners. Ask students if they know of any special ways to round numbers. Suggest\nusing a number line. Is this possible? How would a number line need to be\nconstructed if we were rounding numbers to the nearest 10 000? What\nabout if we were rounding to the nearest 1000? Would the number line be\ndifferent? If so, why? Work through the Master section to show the class how number lines can\nbe used to round numbers.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using number lines to compare\nnumbers and round them to the nearest 10 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1–3: Rounding numbers to the nearest 10 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students only change the rounded digit and not all of the subsequent numbers. Students are unsure of which digit to consider for rounding to 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number line to round numbers by looking at which number is closest.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round numbers to the nearest 10, 100, 1000 and 10 000. Students can determine when rounding to the nearest 10, 100, 1000 or 10 000, which would be most appropriate. Students can use a number line to round numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to the nearest ten thousand, thousand, hundred, or ten, and round tenths to the nearest whole number.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 100 000":{"label":"Lesson 11: Rounding Numbers to the Nearest 100 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">41 - 44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 100 000 using number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the table from the Explore task. Give them some time to work on and prove whether they agree with Sam's statement. Can they think of a scenario in which this statement would no longer be true? What if we were rounding to the nearest 1000? Ask them if they are able to prove their thinking and answers by using a number line. What might that look like? Work through the Master section with the class. During Guided Practice, students are rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000. In the last question, students need to determine the smallest and greatest possible numbers that could be rounded to 200 000 when rounded to the nearest 100 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>rounded to the nearest 100 000</li><li>approximate number</li><li>approximately equal to</li><li>smallest/greatest possible number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000; number lines provided as support.</li><li>Example 2a: Rounding numbers to the nearest 100 000.</li><li>Example 2b: Determining the smallest and greatest possible numbers that could be rounded to 200 000 when rounded to the nearest 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students try to round to the nearest 1000 or 10 000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to a specified power of 10, and round tenths and hundredths to the nearest whole number or one decimal place.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Rounding Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 12: Rounding Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 - 49</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000 using number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask how we can get approximate amounts. In this situation would it be best to round to the nearest 100 000, 10 000, 1000, 100 or 10? How should we decide what we should round to? Discuss a situation in which rounding to these numbers would be reasonable. During Guided Practice, students are rounding numbers to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000. The Guided Practice could be extended into investigating under what conditions the rounded values are the same irrespective of the precision of the rounding.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>rounded to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000, and 100 000</li><li>approximate</li><li>approximately equal to</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Rounding to the nearest 100, 1000 and 10 000. Examples 2–3: Rounding to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students try to round to the wrong place.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number line to round numbers by looking at which number is closest.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round numbers to the nearest 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine, when rounding to the nearest 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 or</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to a specified power of 10, and round tenths and hundredths to the nearest whole number or one decimal place.</li><li>Use rounding and estimation to predict results and to check the reasonableness of calculations.</li><li>Use rounding, estimation, and benchmarks to predict results and to check the reasonableness of calculations.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students need to understand what place value a number is being rounded to. Once they know this, they learn to look at the next smallest place value to determine whether the number rounds up, rounds down or stays the same.</li><li>This rule applies to both whole numbers in this chapter and decimal numbers later on.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">50</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of numbers to 1 000 000 to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students need to work out what two numbers Jacob could be thinking of, based on the information given, and then what numbers Jacob could pick to give the greatest difference and the smallest difference between the two numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students need to work out what two numbers Jacob could be thinking of, based on the information given, and then what numbers Jacob could pick to give the greatest difference and the smallest difference between the two numbers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students consider a statement about rounding numbers, decide whether or not it is correct and explain their answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with pupils as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 2: Addition & Subtraction":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This chapter covers addition and subtraction. The chapter starts off with simple addition before moving on to addition where renaming is required. Subtraction is also covered in a similar way where simple subtraction is mastered before moving to subtraction where renaming is required. Once students master addition and subtraction, they start to look at problem-solving questions and practice using bar models. \n\nThis chapter uses three different ways to show addition and subtraction visually. This will help students develop flexibility, however, some students may need additional support and time in order to be able to use all of the methods fluently.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Counting On to Add":{"label":"Lesson 1: Counting On to Add","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">52 - 55</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add using the 'counting on' strategy to solve the problem with their partners. Tell them they can count on in with concrete materials and number lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time thousands to get to the final answer. Is this correct? Is this all we need to do? Ask students if there is a faster way of finding the answer by partitioning 24 000. How would they partition the number to make it easier to count on? Provide students with a number line and place-value counters. Ask them to make 32 541 with the place-value counters. How many thousands do we need to count on? How many ten thousands do we need? Work through Master to demonstrate how the place-value counters and number lines can be used to solve this problem. During Guided Practice, students are counting on in tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands and hundred thousands and adding mentally.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Counting on from the same number to get 6 more numbers in multiples of 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- ones</li><li>- tens</li><li>- hundreds</li><li>- thousands</li><li>- ten thousands</li><li>- hundred thousands</li><li>Example 2a–b: Adding mentally from the same number in multiples of 100 and 1000.</li><li>Example 2c–d: Adding mentally from the same number in multiples of 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- place value</li><li>- counting on</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers up to 1 000 000 by counting on in thousands and ten thousands.</li><li>Students can count on in multiples of 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000 depending on what's appropriate.</li><li>##### ● Students can add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Adding within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 2: Adding within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">56 - 58</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add numbers within 1 000 000 using approximate number of programmes required by rounding the numbers in rounding and concrete materials. the table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them some time to work on the solution. Ask students if they could find the How would they round the numbers in the table to find an approximate total? As there are no ones, tens or hundreds in the numbers would it be best to round to the nearest ten thousand or hundred\nthousand? Is it possible to round to both? Discuss why it would not be a\ngood idea to round to the nearest hundred thousand. Would it make sense\nto round the number of programmes for sailing, track cycling and table\ntennis to zero? Allow students to use place-value counters to help them\nfind the answer. Tell the class they could use a bar graph to help them round the numbers.\nWhat would that look like? Could we find the answer roughly if we did that?\nAsk them if it is possible to find the answer if we create all the numbers\nusing place-value counters and then just add them all together. Would that\nbe by adding the numbers roughly, or by adding exactly? Work through\nMaster to discuss the examples.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are adding numbers within 1 000 000.\nAsk students if it is possible to use a number line or place-value counters\nto add numbers of this size and if it is possible to add them mentally.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Adding using the column method. following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones ● tens Example 2: Adding by adding the thousands mentally. ● hundreds ● thousands ● ten thousands</li><li>hundred thousands</li><li>place value</li><li>approximate</li><li>rounding to the nearest 10 000</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add numbers greater than 10 000 accurately using place-value counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally.</li><li>Students add whole numbers up to 1 000 000, including using column addition.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to addition and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Lesson 3":{"label":"Lesson 3: Lesson 3","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">59 - 62</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add numbers within 1 000 000 using the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Before they begin solving the required task, ask them why the question uses the term 'approximately'. Tell them it is used because it means 'half'. Is this true? Does this make sense? Allow them time to discuss. Recap on what the term 'approximately' means and ask students if they can determine what range the numbers could be when rounded to 15 000 and 17 000. Give them some time to work this out and then provide them with place-value counters and ask them if they are able to solve the Explore task. Tell them they could add 20 000 and 20 000, as they want to get an approximate total. Is this a good idea? Why might this be a bad idea? Allow them some time to work out the approximate total. Next, provide students with the actual amounts from Master 2. Do they fall within the range of numbers we initially thought of? Ask them to add those numbers together to find the actual total. Tell them your friend said that to add numbers using the column method, we need to add one column at a time. Is this true? Does it matter where we start? Is there more than one way to show the totals? Work through Master to recap on the method used. During Guided Practice, students are adding with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Adding with renaming. Examples 1–2: Column method.</li><li>Example 3: No method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show how to rename when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add whole numbers with more than 4 digits mentally (up to 1 000 000) and by using formal written methods.</li><li>Students use rounding to estimate and check answers to addition and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Adding within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Adding within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">63 - 66</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and refine addition skills and place-value knowledge to solve addition problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, provide students with digit cards 0–9. Show them the Explore task and allow them to work in pairs or small groups. What 5-digit numbers could Tama have made? Give students time to play with the position of the numbers. Tell the students that your friend said that they knew that if the first digits of the two numbers were 3 and 4, then the second digits could only be certain numbers. They also said that if the first digits of the two numbers were 2 and 3 then the second digits could only be certain numbers. What did your friend mean by this? Ask students a variety of questions as they use the cards to find various solutions. Tasks can include: finding the greatest sum that Tama could have made; finding the smallest sum that Tama could have made; finding the greatest and smallest numbers Tama could have made. During Guided Practice, students are estimating the total of two 5-digit numbers, then using the column method to find the sum.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>place value</li><li>rename/renaming</li><li>estimation/estimate</li><li>round to the nearest 10 000</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when adding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000 and 10 000. Students can add using the column method. Students can add rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers when adding using place-value counters and the column method. Students can generate questions for addition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers up to 1 000 000, including using columnar addition.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to calculations and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Counting Backwards to Subtract":{"label":"Lesson 5: Counting Backwards to Subtract","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">67 - 70</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the 'counting number line. Are we going to count backwards or forwards to find the backwards' strategy with concrete materials.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to work on this. Ask the students how we could show this problem using a amount Matua Henare has in his account now? Once the initial amount is\nshown on the number line and it has been established that we will be\ncounting backwards, ask what we should count back in. Would it make\nsense to count back in ones? Is it possible to use multiple number lines\ncounting back in different amounts? Why do they think Charles has\npartitioned 730 000 in the way that he has? How could that help us to\ncount backwards? Ask the children to discuss how counting backwards in\nhundred thousands, ten thousands and thousands can be shown on a\nnumber line.\nOnce students understand how to represent the problem using a number\nline, give them place-value counters and ask them to represent the\nproblem using the counters. Ask them to count back as they remove the\ncounters. Students should be encouraged to see that they can use\nplace-value to subtract by counting backwards in hundred thousands, ten\nthousands and thousands as they have done when using the number\nlines.\nIn Master 3 students are shown how they can exchange 10 000 for ten\n1000s to count back using place-value counters.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are counting backwards in tens,\nhundreds, thousands, ten thousands and hundred thousands.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Blank number lines (increments marked)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>- ones</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Counting back from the same number to get 5 more numbers in multiples of 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- tens</li><li>- hundreds</li><li>- thousands</li><li>Example 2: Subtracting mentally from the same number in multiples of 100, 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</li><li>- ten thousands</li><li>- hundred thousands</li><li>- place value</li><li>- count back</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract numbers mentally with increasingly large numbers.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers up to 1 000 000 by counting backwards in thousands and ten thousands.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to calculations.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Subtracting within 1 000 000":{"label":"Lesson 6: Subtracting within 1 000 000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">71 - 74</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the column method we compare numbers? Are they attached or is one above the other? and place-value counters using numbers to 1 000 000. Allow students time to compare the number of runners with their partners.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them that when you are asked to compare numbers, you should add them together. Is this true? How can we know? What might a bar model diagram look like when Which ones have the greatest difference and by how much approximately? By how much exactly? Which numbers have the smallest difference? What are the approximate number of runners in each city? Are they the same? Should we round to the nearest 100, 1000, 10 000 or 100 000 when comparing these numbers? Work through Master to recap on the methods used. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting numbers requiring renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can show how to rename numbers when subtracting using place-value counters and the column method.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show how to rename numbers when subtracting using place-value counters and the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally. Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to subtraction and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7":{"label":"Lesson 7","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">75 - 79</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract using the column method&lt;br&gt;and place-value counters using numbers to 1&lt;br&gt;000 000.|hundreds is straightforward, but it becomes more difficult when we try to&lt;br&gt;subtract the thousands.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them: 'How might we find the answer?' Allow them time to work on this with their partners. Suggest to the students that subtracting the ones, tens and To be able to subtract using the column method\nand place-value counters using numbers to 1\n000 000. hundreds is straightforward, but it becomes more difficult when we try to\nsubtract the thousands. What is it about subtracting the thousands that\nmakes it more difficult? Once it has been established that there are not\nenough thousands to carry out the written calculation ask the students\nwhat they have done in previous years in similar situations. How do they\nknow when they need to rename?\nAsk students to discuss the renaming process they have used in previous\nyears and how that can be applied here.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are subtracting with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 5: A word problem involving subtracting a 5-digit number from a 6-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method to subtract.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000. Students can subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method. Students can generate questions for subtraction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li><li>Students use addition to check answers to subtraction.</li><li>Students use rounding to check answers to subtraction and to determine levels of accuracy in the context of a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8":{"label":"Lesson 8","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">80 - 84</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and refine subtraction by parcels returned and how can this be found? Students should see that skills and place-value knowledge to solve they are looking for the difference between two amounts. Ask the students subtraction problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask the students what they are trying to find in the final column of the table. What is meant how they can find the difference between two numbers. Once it has been established that subtraction can be used, ask the students if they can see that there are going to be some calculations that need renaming and some that don't. How do they know this by looking at the numbers without starting the calculations? Allow them time to work on this with their partners. During the Master section subtraction with and without renaming is reinforced. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting with renaming.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when subtracting. Students are uncertain of how to cross boundaries when subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method to subtract.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract using the column method. Students can subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename numbers using place-value counters and the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can generate questions for subtraction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar subtraction).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9":{"label":"Lesson 9","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">85 - 88</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to use addition and subtraction to solve comparison problems with numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them that to compare the number of spectators, they would need to use addition and subtraction. What does that mean? Why would they need to use more than one operation to solve the problem? Once students understand that they need to add together the number of spectators on each day before subtracting from another day, allow them time to complete the operations. Provide them with place-value counters to represent the number of spectators to help them with addition and subtraction. Ask students which day had the greatest number of spectators? Which day had the smallest number of spectators? What is the difference between the day with the greatest and the smallest number of spectators? Use Master to consolidate learning. During Guided Practice, students are adding and subtracting numbers to 1 000 000. Encourage them to try multiple methods, including mental methods, written methods and by using place-value counters.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Finding the difference using the column method. following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>thousands</li><li>difference Example 2: Adding or subtracting using any method(s).</li><li>Example 3: A word problem involving addition and subtraction.</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students place digits in the wrong place-value columns when adding and/or subtracting.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract numbers accurately using place-value counters.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to add and subtract numbers. Students can estimate and round numbers to the nearest 1000, 10 000 and 100 000.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract rounded numbers mentally.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">##### This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar addition and subtraction).</li><li>Students solve addition and subtraction multi-step problems in contexts, deciding which operations and methods to use and why.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10":{"label":"Lesson 10","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">89 - 93</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract using the column method and number bonds with numbers to 1 000 000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To begin this lesson, provide students with the Explore task. Ask the students what type of question is being asked. Will there be any renaming in any of the columns? Ask students to come up with questions based on the information in the Explore section. Tasks could include: addition questions with renaming; subtraction questions without renaming; subtraction with renaming; which two cities' populations would add to give the largest total, which two cities' populations would add to give the smallest total; which two cities' populations have the greatest difference; which two cities' populations have the smallest difference. Ask students to extend the questions beyond two cities and try to create an exhaustive list of possible questions that can be asked. During the Master section, examples of questions that could be asked are worked through. During Guided Practice, students are adding and subtracting numbers to 1 000 000.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract any whole numbers.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students understand that the value of a digit within a number is determined by the place it holds.</li><li>Students add and subtract whole numbers with more than 4 digits, including using formal written methods (columnar addition and subtraction).</li><li>Students solve addition and subtraction multi-step problems in contexts, deciding which operations and methods to use and why.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 11: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">93 - 94</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of addition and subtraction of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out the missing numbers in a subtraction calculation using the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out the missing numbers in a subtraction calculation using the column method.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students find some real life examples of where addition or subtraction of large numbers is needed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 3: Multiplication & Division":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this unit, students are multiplying and dividing 3- and 4-digit numbers by single- and double-digit numbers. The unit begins by finding and defining multiples, factors and common factors. Students begin to work with prime numbers and determine what makes a number prime or composite. \n\nAfter this, they work with square and cube numbers before moving on to multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000. When multiplying, students are encouraged to use a variety of methods, including: number bonds, column methods and the grid method. \n\nNumber bonds are used to represent multiplicative word problems. Students then move on to multiply by 2-digit numbers before beginning to divide by 10, 100 and 1000. The unit ends as students learn to divide, giving rise to remainders using multiple methods, including number bonds and long and short division.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Finding Multiples":{"label":"Lesson 1: Finding Multiples","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">96 - 100</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to consolidate and review multiplication; to be able to find the result of multiplying by a number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how many possible answers they can come up with. Tell them there are only 12 multiples of 8 because there are only 12 numbers in the times table. Is this true? How can we check? Let students discuss this idea and work on the task with their partner. Then tell them that the tenth multiple of 8 is 72. Is this true? How can we find out? What do we need to do? Give students time to determine what the tenth multiple of 8 is and then ask them if they know what the tenth number is, then what is the twentieth number and the thirtieth? Can they come up with a rule for multiples? What about the fifth multiple? Ask students if this works for every number and then allow them time to discuss this idea. During Guided Practice, students are finding multiples of other numbers to reinforce the concept.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters/cubes (one set between two)</li><li>Number square or multiplication square</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson: ● multiple</li><li>##### Variation</li><li>Example 1: Finding multiples of 7 in a number chart. Example 2: Finding specific multiples of a number.</li><li>Example 3: Deciding whether statements about multiples of specific numbers are true or false.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recall multiplication facts to at least 10 × 10 and corresponding division facts.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain how to identify a multiple of a number.</li><li>Students use knowledge of properties of number to solve problems in a range of contexts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Finding Factors":{"label":"Lesson 2: Finding Factors","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">101 - 103</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to consolidate and review multiplication; to be able to find the factors of a To begin this lesson, provide students with 24 counters or objects. Show them the Explore task and ask how many ways they can arrange the counters into equal groups. Allow students time to work on this and come up with a few answers. Then ask them to explore which statement is given number. correct: 'A factor is the number of counters in each group.' 'A factor is the\nnumber of groups.' Can both statements be correct? Give students time to\ndiscuss this. Ask them whether 24 is a factor of 24. How can we check?\nAsk students if they are able to find the factors of a greater number, such\nas 48. Do the number of factors always increase as the number increases?\nWhat about 31; would it have more or fewer factors than 28? Why? How\ncan we be sure? Can we represent that by using the counters? During Guided Practice, students are finding factors of different numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (24 each)</li><li>Dot cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2a: Finding all the factors of 12.</li><li>Example 2b: Finding all the factors of 16.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the number that has 2, 4 and 5 as factors and is less than 25.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify factors of numbers up to 100.</li><li>Identify square numbers and factors of numbers up to 125.</li><li>Recall multiplication facts to at least 10 × 10 and identify and describe the divisibility rules for 2, 3, 5, 9, and 10.</li><li>Identify and describe the divisibility rules for 2–11.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain what a factor is and how to use arrays and multiplication/division facts to find them.</li><li>Students explain how to systematically find all factors of a number and how they know when they have found them all.</li><li>Students use knowledge of properties of number to solve problems in a range of contexts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Finding Common Factors":{"label":"Lesson 3: Finding Common Factors","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">104 - 107</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to define and find common factors of numbers to 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them time to think about the answer. Ask them if they can find the common factors of 10 and 15 and allow them time to work it out. Tell students that 10 and 15 are both in the 5 times table, so the common factor is 5. Is this true? How can we find out? Ask them to see if it is always the case that numbers that are in a common times table have a common factor by checking if this rule works with 12 and 30. After students have had some time to find the answer, tell them that if 3 is a common factor for both numbers, then 6 must always be a factor. Can we prove that? When might it not be true? Ask them if they spot any patterns in the common factors of 12 and 30. Ask them to circle all of the common factors. Use Master to reinforce learning. Review Master 3 with students and ask them to describe the pattern that they spot. During Guided Practice, students are finding the common factors of a variety of numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (50 between two)</li><li>Number square or multiplication square (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>factor</li><li>common factor</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>40.</li><li>Example 2: Finding the factors of 18 and whether it has any common factors with 36.</li><li>Example 3: Finding two numbers that have 1, 2 and 5 as common factors.</li><li>Example 4: Finding a common factor of three numbers – 15, 12 and 9.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse the terms 'multiple' and 'factor'. Students find common multiples instead of common factors.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number square and knowledge of their times tables to find common factors. Students can record their thinking in an organised way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support finding common factors. Students can use a number square to help them identify common factors.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find the common factors of numbers to 20.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find the common factors of numbers to 40.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify factors of numbers up to 100.</li><li>Identify square numbers and factors of numbers up to 125.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain how to identify a common factor.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000":{"label":"Lesson 4: Multiplying by 10, 100 and 1000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">104 - 107</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to define and find common factors of numbers to 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Provide them with place-value counters for 10, 100 and 1000 and allow them time to represent the problem, discuss what they need to find out and solve it. What counters will you use to represent the packets? What about for each bag or each box? Tell students that your friend says that 100 is 10 times greater than 10. Is that true? How can you prove it? Work through Master 1–3 with the class, so students can check their answers. There are 5000 beads in 5 boxes. Then ask: 'What would happen if we added on more so that there were 11 packets in each bag and 11 bags in each box? Show me with the place-value counters.' During Guided Practice, students are multiplying single digits by 10, 100 and 1000 and multiples of 10, 100 and 1000. They are also completing equations by finding the missing multiplicand or multiplier and multiplying 2-digit numbers by 100.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 4a: Multiplying a single-digit number by 100.</li><li>Example 4b: Multiplying a 2-digit number by 100.</li><li>Example 4c: Multiplying a single-digit number by 1000.</li><li>Example 4d: Multiplying a 2-digit number by 100.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li><li>Multiply and divide numbers by 10, 100, and 1,000.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain how to multiply, and the effect of multiplying, a number by 10, 100 and 1000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5":{"label":"Lesson 5","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">111 - 114</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to multiply 2-digit and 3-digit To begin this lesson, provide students with place-value counters for 1, 10 and 100. It is important in this lesson that they see each value multiplied to numbers by a single-digit number using\nmultiple strategies. understand how and why the column method works in addition to other\nmethods.\nShow students the Explore task and ask them to determine which is the\nbest deal and why. Ask them what $14 would look like using place-value\ncounters and if they can show the total of $14 × 5. Ask students how many\nways they can represent this (using place-value counters, number bonds\nand the column method). Tell them you think that one way to find out the\nbest deal would be to multiply $14 by 5 and then see if that total is more\nthan the 'special deal' of buying 5 buckets of golf balls at the same time.\nWould it be better to buy 3 individual buckets at $14 each or buy 3 at the\nsame time? Can you think of another way to buy 5 buckets of golf balls?\nWork through Master so that students can check their answers.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are practising multiplying 2-digit and\n3-digit numbers by a single-digit number. Guided Practice 1 is structured to\nshow how breaking apart numbers is helpful when multiplying. Guide\nstudents to see this if they are unable to see it for themselves.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply a 2-digit or a 3-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no regrouping).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 6: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">115 - 117</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them how we could represent the number of loaves using a bar model. Is it possible to do this? Allow them time to work on this. Once they have constructed the\nappropriate bar model diagram, ask them what is missing. What\ninformation do we need? Prompt them to see that we either need the value\nof one unit to find the number of loaves the baker made in December, or\nwe need the total number of loaves made in December to find the value of\none unit. We can label the bar representing September, as the number of\nloaves made in September is given.\nAsk the class how we can use this information to find the total number of\nloaves made in December. Tell them you know an easier way to multiply a\nlarge number than using the column method. Is this true? Is there an\neasier way? Ask students to find different ways.\nPrompt students to see how 1232 can be split in different ways to multiply\nmore quickly; by 1000, 200, 30 and 2, or 1000, 100, 100, 30 and 2? Do\nthese work? What might make this an easier way to multiply? Can we\nmultiply numbers mentally using this method? Ask students to work out the\nnumber of loaves made in December, then use Master to demonstrate the\ncalculation and check students' understanding.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by a\nsingle-digit number in different ways. Guided Practice 2 is structured to show how to break apart larger numbers using our knowledge of 1, 10, 100 and 1000. Guided Practice 3 encourages the use of the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>product</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Finding the product of a multiple of 1000 and a single-digit number.</li><li>Example 2: Using the same multiplier to multiply single-digit,</li><li>2-digit, 3-digit and 4-digit numbers; the final answer can be</li><li>obtained by adding the first four products.</li><li>Example 3: Multiplying a 4-digit number by a single-digit number; column method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">● This is a key lesson. \n\n● This lesson is useful because it includes the partitioning method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 7: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">118 - 121</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number with renaming, using a variety of strategies.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could estimate the number of roof tiles needed for 3 new homes. Allow them to work on this with their partner. Ask the class to work out exactly how many roof tiles Amira's dad needs to order for 3 new homes. How many ways can we represent this? Can we use place-value counters, bar models, or the column method? Tell them that you think this problem is tricky because when you multiply the tens, you get 12. Where does the 10 go? Can we have 12 tens? How might we represent that? What might it look like using different methods? Does this happen again in this question? Will we have to rename? Highlight the different methods, showing the class examples of more than one way to do the column method in addition to place-value counters. Review Master 3 which shows a repeated addition method. Which method do you prefer? Which is most efficient? During Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by single-digit numbers using the expanded form and the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>0–9 digit cards (one set each)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">multiply the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers to multiply.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to multiply.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">- This is a key lesson. \n\n- Connections between different methods are shown in this lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 8: Multiplying 4-Digit Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">122 - 124</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and as a class identify the key information in the problem. Can we represent this problem using a bar model? What might that look like? Draw some bar models but label them incorrectly. Label the 9 bars with 1218 kg and the single unit tens and hundreds, using multiple methods. with a question mark. Proceed as if nothing is wrong and as you try to solve the problem using this diagram think out loud for the students to hear you correct yourself. It is important for them to hear what making a mistake and correcting it while solving a problem sounds like. Once you have re-labelled the bars correctly, allow students time to solve the problem with their partners and draw and label the bars correctly. Discuss the students' ideas as a class. Tell them you think it is easier to find the total mass of 10 cars and then subtract 1218 kg. Would this work? How can we be sure? What should we do when there are more than 9 ones, tens, hundreds, and so on, after we multiply? Can you show me what to do using place-value counters? Can you draw place-value counters to show how to rename? Does this work? Can we check? Work through Master to take the class through the process of multiplying and renaming. During Guided Practice, students are multiplying 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number using the column method and then have the choice of which method to use.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy. Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply multi-digit whole numbers (e.g., 54 × 112).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">- This lesson can be completed if time allows. \n\n- No new concepts are introduced in this lesson, therefore it can be omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 9: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">125 - 127</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply 2-digit numbers by 2-digit numbers using multiple methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them your friend says you can multiply 16 by 10 and then by 2 to find the answer. Is this true? The question suggests multiplying by 12, not 10 and 2. What does the friend mean? Can we prove this? Allow students time to work on this with their partners. Tell them you think that it can also work if you break 16 into 10 and 6, and then multiply by 12. Is this possible? Will that lead us to the same result? Which is the better number to partition and why? Give them some time to discuss this and then explain their thinking. Then discuss which method of partitioning would be better in Master 2. Ask students to compare the estimations in Master 3. Which estimate is closest? Which would you choose? Encourage students to explain why one estimate may be closer than another. During Guided Practice, students are using multiple methods and estimation to multiply.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two) ● Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>estimate/estimation</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 2: Using estimation to approximate the products; calculating the actual products using chosen method.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Pupils use estimation to support accurate calculation.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 10: Multiplying a 2-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">128 - 132</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>### To be able to multiply a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number using multiple methods, including the grid method, partitioning numbers To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and tell them they can use a grid to solve this problem. What does this mean? Is it possible to break apart 35 and 28 so those numbers are easier to multiply? What might that look like? How can we organise our thinking to solve the and column method, with renaming. problem?\nAllow students time to design a grid that might be useful with their\npartners. If they have never seen this method before, then model its design\nand provide support by completing some of the grid for them. Allow\nstudents to continue to complete the grid and determine how to use those\nnumbers to solve the problem. Once they have understood this method,\nask if they know of any other ways to solve the problem and allow them\ntime to try those other methods.\nTell the class you are more comfortable with using number bonds. Is that\npossible when both numbers are 2 digits? What might that look like? Most\nstudents will break 35 into 30 and 5, and 28 into 20 and 8. Anticipate this\nand then follow up with a question about whether or not we can make 30\neasier to use (10, 10 and 10). Prompt students to use the column method\nto show their thinking. Ask them if it is possible to multiply by 30 and then\nsubtract to get the answer. How can we show that? Use the Master section\nto work through the different methods.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using their knowledge of multiplying\nby tens to support multiplying by numbers that are greater than 10 but less\nthan 100.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1a–c: Multiplying a multiple of 10 and a 2-digit</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson: number with support.</li><li>grid/grid method</li><li>ones</li><li>tens Example 1d–e: Multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number</li><li>with support.</li><li>hundreds</li><li>multiple</li><li>double Example 2: Finding the product of two 2-digit numbers; no</li><li>suggested method.</li><li>halve</li><li>product</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students have difficulty in choosing the number to break down when multiplying.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers or the grid method, to solve multiplication problems in a more formal way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply 2-digit numbers by single-digit numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Multiplying a 3-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number":{"label":"Lesson 11: Multiplying a 3-Digit Number by a 2-Digit Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">133 - 135</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply a 3-digit number by a 2-digit number, using the column method as a key strategy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can find the number of taonga in the collection. Is it possible to represent this problem using place-value counters or a bar model? Allow students time to discuss and work on different methods they could use to solve the problem. Tell them that a bar model might work for this problem, but you find using the written column method easier. Is this true? Is it easier to use? What would it look like? How can we find out if the column method is easier to use? Ask them if they know any other methods. Allow students time to discuss the problem and the three methods shown in Master 1–3 to determine which is best for them. Ask them to talk through each method in turn and make links between them. During Guided Practice, students are using the column method as the key method for multiplying.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students round off 3-digit numbers to the nearest hundred instead of ten when estimating.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands. Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers, to solve multiplication in a more formal way.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and multiply the digits instead of their values. Students round off 3-digit numbers to the nearest hundred instead of ten when estimating.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent and solve multiplication using pictures or concrete materials and by breaking down a number into ones, tens, hundreds and thousands. Students can use a secondary method, such as partitioning numbers, to solve multiplication in a more formal way.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can multiply a single-digit number by a single-digit number. Students can use the column method as a strategy. Students can partition numbers to multiply. Students can use place-value counters to multiply. Students can multiply multiple-digit numbers by single-digit numbers. Students can use the grid method of multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 12. \n\nLessons 11 and 12 build upon previous lessons and do not introduce any new concepts, therefore these lessons can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Lesson 12":{"label":"Lesson 12: Lesson 12","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">136 - 138</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to multiply a 3-digit number by a 2-digit number with renaming, using the column method as the key strategy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the question. Then, take their thoughts as a class. What information do they have? What do they need to find out? Allow them some time to work on the problem with their partners to find out how many pencils are in the boxes altogether. Work through Master 1 highlighting the column method and the links to the place-value counters shown. The focus of this lesson is to use the column method as the key strategy for multiplication. Ask students to do the multiplications shown in Master 2 and 3 using the column method. During Guided Practice, students are solving a word problem involving multiplication linked to the Explore task. Students can choose the method that best suits them.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>1–5 digit cards (one set between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>After the Master section, ask students to work in pairs using</li><li>digit cards 1–5 to make one 3-digit and one 2-digit number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 11. \n\nLessons 11 and 12 build upon previous lessons and do not introduce any new concepts, therefore these lessons can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Dividing by 10, 100 and 1000":{"label":"Lesson 13: Dividing by 10, 100 and 1000","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">139 - 141</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to find thousands, hundreds and tens in a 4-digit number using concrete materials.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Tell them this question is suggesting that we break 3564 into thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. Is this possible? Can we use a number bond diagram to do this? How many thousands are there in 3564? Allow them time to work on the ##### problem with their partners. Continue along this line of questioning and work through the Master. Tell students that if we divide 3564 by 1, we would get 3564, so if we divide 3564 by 10, it must be 356. Is this true? Have I made a mistake? Can you show this using place-value counters? Can you draw this to show where the mistake is? What is the remainder? What should we do with the remainder? During Guided Practice, students are dividing multiples of 1000 by 1000, 100 and 10 and other multiples of 1000, 100 and 10. Encourage them to look for connections between the equations, For example, in Guided Practice 2, can they make a connection between 4000 divided by 2000 and 4000 divided by 20? Similarly in Guided Practice 3, can they use the answer to 5000 divided by 500 to help them with 5000 divided by 50?</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Dividing a multiple of 1000 by 1000, 100 and 10.</li><li>Example 2: Dividing a multiple of 1000 by 1000, 2000, 100, 200, 10 and 20.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and divide the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and divide by 10, 100 and 1000 using the number bond strategy.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide concrete materials into equal groups. Students can divide numbers to 100.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and/or use number bonds to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 100.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 1000.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Pupils explain how to divide, and the effect of dividing, a number by 10, 100 and 1000.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 14: Dividing without Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 14: Dividing without Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">142 - 144</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to divide 3-digit and 4-digit numbers by a single-digit number, using number bonds and long division as key methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to solve the problem. While they work suggest to them they can use number bonds to break apart the number, which will make dividing easier. How can we break it apart so that it is easy to divide? Allow students to discuss this and prompt them by looking at the divisor. Tell them that because 2 is the divisor, 640 can easily be broken into 600 and 40 because both of those numbers can be divided by 2 easily. Is there another way that we can break apart the number? Allow them to use place-value counters to represent the number and divide it into equal groups. Once students are comfortable with number bonds, show them the symbol for long division and place the numbers of the equation into the method. Guide them through this step by step, for example: 'First, we need to look at the hundreds column,' and so on. Ask them how many twos there are in 600. This may require some modelling if none of the children have used this method before. Use Master to show the class how the number bond method and long division are asking the same questions. During Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve division problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one set between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Pupils divide a three-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no exchanging, no remainders).</li><li>Pupils will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 15: Dividing without Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 15: Dividing without Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">145 - 148</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could work out how much each of the classes received. Allow them time to work on this with their partners. Work through Master 1 with them. Lulu's long division as the key methods. school raised $1968 and shared the money equally between 6 classes. Is\nthis possible? How do we know? Can we show this by using concrete\nmaterials or drawings? Can we think of other questions? Allow them time\nto come up with other questions.\nTell them that the easiest way to solve this question is by using number\nbonds. How would this look? Tell them it is quite simple — you just partition\nthe number into 1000, 900, 60 and 8. Will this work? How can we check?\nGuide students to recognise that all these numbers cannot be divided\nexactly by the divisor, which is 6, so we need to try again. Try different\nvariations with the students. Do 1100, 1200 or 1300 divide by 6 exactly?\nWhat about 1800? What would be left over? Can 168 be divided by 6\nexactly? What about 120? What would be left over, would it be 48? Can 48\nbe divided by 6 exactly?\nUsing number bonds as a strategy is pivotal in further mathematics. This is\na lesson where it is important for students to embed this convention\ndeeply. Ask students if it is possible to use long division to solve this\nproblem. How are the two strategies similar? How are they different? Use\nMaster to show students how the two methods are recorded.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve division\nproblems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li><li>tens</li><li>hundreds</li><li>thousands</li><li>partition</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Dividing a 4-digit number by single-digit number. Examples 1–2: Using both the number bond strategy and long division.</li><li>Example 3: No method suggested.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students divide a two-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no remainders).</li><li>Students divide a three-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and representations (no remainders). ● Students will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 16: Dividing with Remainder":{"label":"Lesson 16: Dividing with Remainder","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">149 - 152</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to review the problem and discuss what they are being asked. What information do they need? Once they have ascertained that the problem will require division, ask them to talk about their preferred method of remainders. calculation. Can you partition 469 into chunks that are easily divisible by 6\nor use a short division method? Allow them time to work on the problem\nwith their partners and encourage them to use both methods to find the\nanswer.\nOnce they are able to work out both methods, remind them of the initial\nquestion about dividing the kūmara into boxes of 6. Is this possible? Can\nwe divide 469 kūmara into boxes of 6? Why, or why not? Can the 1\nremaining kūmara be divided between the boxes equally? What if it was\nnot kūmara? Draw their attention to Master 2. Can we divide 1 gram of\nsugar flowers between 6 cakes? What might that look like using a bar\nmodel; would that help us work this out?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using short division and number\nbonds to solve division problems. In Guided Practice 4, using a bar model\nis expected.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>ones</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Dividing a 3-digit number by a single-digit number with a remainder. Number bond and short division answers are given as support with only the remainder to find.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard place value and divide the digits instead of their values.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and divide by 10, 100 and 1000 using the number bond strategy. Students can divide using the long division strategy.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide concrete materials into equal groups. Students can divide numbers to 100. Students can use their knowledge of times tables to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can partition numbers and/or use number bonds to support division.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide by 100.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a four-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 198 ÷ 7, 4154 ÷ 8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students divide a 3-digit number by a single-digit number using partitioning and short division (with exchanging and remainders).</li><li>Students solve short division problems accurately when the hundreds digit is smaller than the divisor.</li><li>Students will use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 17: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 17: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">153 - 154</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of multiplication and division of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out the solution to a word problem involving multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number, dividing a 4-digit number by a 2-digit number, and then multiplying a 3-digit number by a single-digit number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out the solution to a word problem involving multiplying a 2-digit number by a 2-digit number, dividing a 4-digit number by a 2-digit number, and then multiplying a 3-digit number by a single-digit number.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students decide how to partition a 3-digit number if they were dividing the number by 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 and explain their decisions. They then write a division story for one of the division calculations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 4: Multi-Step Word Problems":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students are solving word problems that involve multiple steps and a variety of operations. Students begin the unit by simply choosing the correct operation before moving onto representing the key information using bar models. \n\nApplying the strategies learned in previous units is key in solving the challenges. The chapter ends with complex representations of numbers and change using advanced bar models.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Solving Word Problems Using Multiplication and Division":{"label":"Lesson 1: Solving Word Problems Using Multiplication and Division","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">156 - 159</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations; to be able to identify the operation required to carry out the plan.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students an enlarged version of the Explore task. If that is not possible, ask them what information they will need to be able to solve the problem. Do we have enough information to solve it? Tell them that identifying the key information in a word problem is important. What does this mean? Allow students to explore the key information from the problem with their partner. How many boxes? How many bags? How many bags in each box? What do all of these questions lead us to do: add, subtract, multiply or divide? We also need to know how many lollies were packed into each bag. Do we need to multiply again to find the number of packets required? Will this tell us how many packets we need? Let students work out that this method is incorrect and identify that we need to divide. Allow them to\nemploy a range of strategies to multiply and divide, and then ask students\nif they can show you multiple ways to get to the answer, using as many\nstrategies as possible. Allow them time to work on the tasks with their\npartner. Work through Master to demonstrate the steps involved in problem solving. This lesson introduces the steps to problem solving set out by Polya in the 1950s. Emphasise to the children the four steps so that they have this in mind when they are problem solving. 1) Understand the problem. 2) Form a plan 3) Action the plan 4) Check your answer. During Guided Practice, students are exploring dividing a total number into different amounts.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters (one between two)</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>Multiply</li><li>Divide</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using the information provided about the number of</li><li>lollies in a packet to find the total number of lollies in a box of 12 packets; using this information to find out how many lollies in 8 boxes.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify key information from the problem, and describe the steps they must apply to solve the problem.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiply a three-digit by one-digit number and two two-digit whole numbers (e.g., 245 × 6, 34 × 83).</li><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with a remainder (e.g., 83 ÷ 5 = 16, remainder 3). &lt;u&gt;Ready to Progress&lt;/u&gt;</li><li>This lesson can be completed if time allows.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">##### Learning Outcomes \n\n● This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including: students identify key information in word problems</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>● This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including: students identify key information in word problems</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Solving Word Problems Using Bar Models":{"label":"Lesson 2: Solving Word Problems Using Bar Models","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">160 - 162</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiplication and division, using bar models as the main heuristic.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them what the question is asking and how to identify the key information that will help them solve the problem. Who has more money, Charles or Sam? How do we know? What do we need to find out to work out how much money Charles has saved? Once students have determined that first they need to use the information about Sam's spending to work out his savings, allow time for them to discuss what operation they need to use. Is it possible to represent this problem using a bar model? Allow students time to solve the task using a bar model heuristic. Work through Master to show children that they can show 4 quarters with a\nbar model to find Sam's total savings. Then move on to the next step. It says that Charles has 6 times as much\nsaved as Sam. Agree that a bar model can also be used for the second\npart of the problem. What would the bar model look like to work out how\nmuch money Charles has saved? If a student draws the model correctly,\nthen show it to the class for approval. Alternatively ask students to talk\nthrough the bar model in Master.\nHow can we check if we found the correct amount; should we multiply or\ndivide? Agree that as we used multiplication to work out the answer, we\nshould use division to check.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are given some other word problems.\nEncourage them to use the four-step process introduced in Lesson 1:\nUnderstand, Form a plan, Action the plan, Check the answer.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>Multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using multiplication and division to solve a problem Example 2: Using division and subtraction to solve a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa. Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify key information from the problem, and describe the steps they must apply to solve the problem. Students can represent numbers in the problem with bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem. Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students identify key information in word problems.</li><li>Students multiply and divide numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>Students use efficient strategies of division to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Lesson 3":{"label":"Lesson 3: Lesson 3","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">163 - 165</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations, identifying key information and representing information using bar model diagrams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them to work in pairs to point out the key information in the problem, and then discuss it with their partners. How many steps are needed? Which operations will you use? How can we tell? Allow students time to discuss their proposed plans. Share as a class, did you form similar plans? Give students time to solve the problem with their partner. Let students work through Master 1 to solve the problem. What have they discovered? Did they work it out the same way? Is there a better method? Did you use multiplication or addition to find the mass of the two cubes? Could number bonds help us? Work through Master 2 to 4 with students. For each word problem, encourage students to discuss each one first to make sure they understand the problem and have a plan. Can you use information from a previous problem to help? If not, can you use both? Can you use a bar model for the questions? During Guided Practice, students are applying their knowledge of problem solving to solve multi-step word problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</li><li>Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</li><li>Check the curriculum</li><li>National Curriculum</li><li>Recall multiplication and corresponding di</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers in a problem using bar models, to solve the problem.</li><li>vision facts for 4s and 6s.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both. Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in a problem using bar models, to solve the problem.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts. Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">vision facts for 4s and 6s.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem. Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division. Students can use place-value counters, or other concrete materials, to show division and multiplication.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Check the curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">National Curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Recall multiplication and corresponding di</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Recall multiplication and corresponding di vision facts for 4s and 6s.</li><li>Multiply a two-digit by one-digit number and two one-digit whole numbers (e.g., 23 × 5, 7 × 8).</li><li>Use doubling or halving to scale a quantity (e.g., to double or halve a recipe).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students multiply, divide, add and subtract numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>● Students identify key information in multi-step word problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Lesson 4":{"label":"Lesson 4: Lesson 4","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">166 - 168</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to solve word problems involving multiple operations, using bar models as the main heuristic to represent key information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we could work out the greatest number of bags Charles could fill. Allow them time to work on the problem with their partner, encouraging them to talk to each other about how to find the key information needed to answer the problem. Agree that first of all they will need to find out how many bags of 8 marbles can be made from 570 marbles. What operation will you use for this step? Once they know the greatest number of bags Charles can fill, ask them to discuss how they will find out the number of blue marbles he will need. Once the plan is formed, ask students to discuss how they are going to action the plan. What method will you use for the division? What about the multiplication? Work through Master 1 with them comparing their thinking with their friends in the book. Discuss using estimation to check the answer. Is your answer reasonable? Work through Master 2 in a similar fashion. During Guided Practice, students are applying their knowledge of problem solving to solve multi-step word problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters</li><li>Place-value chart (one between two)</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both.</li><li>Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</li><li>Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can represent numbers in the problem with pictures and solve.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe they need to multiply or divide, not both.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent numbers in the problem with pictures and solve.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can break problems down into their key parts.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students multiply when they should divide, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the key information in a word problem.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students incorrectly draw and/or label bar models.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can determine where they need to begin solving a problem. Students can use a variety of multiplication and division strategies to solve problems. Students can use bar models to represent multiplication and division. Students can use place-value counters, or other concrete materials, to show division and multiplication.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Divide up to a three-digit whole number by a one-digit divisor, with no remainder (e.g., 65 ÷ 5).</li><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply, divide, add and subtract numbers mentally drawing upon known facts.</li><li>Students identify key information in multi-step word problems.</li><li>Students use estimation to support accurate calculation.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">169 - 170</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of whole numbers to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students work out their solution to the problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students work out their solution to the problem.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write a word problem based on a given bar model.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any of them need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 5: Tables & Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students read and interpret information in tables and line graphs. The first lesson requires students to read and interpret information presented in a table showing flights between Singapore and London. In the next lesson, they are required to use the data to answer questions, however the data has restrictions and must be sorted. \n\nThe final lesson on tables leaves out key information, such as omitting a train time to indicate the train does not stop at a specific station. Then there are four lessons on line graphs, beginning with a single line to represent a given set of data, followed by constructing line graphs that have more than one data set to represent.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 1: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">172 - 175</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read the information presented in a table (including timetables) and interpret its meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to work in pairs to discuss the various items of information presented in the timetable. What can they see? What does it mean? Give them time to discuss and interpret what the different times mean. Tell them that the journey from Swanson to Britomart always stops at Newmarket. Is this true? How can we check? Where should we look for that information? Ask students to look at the 06:55 journey. If two friends both took the same train, but then one got off the train at Newmarket and the other got off at Britomart, how long was each of them on the train? Can we write these times in minutes, and in hours and minutes? What is the difference in journey times? Give students time to work out the answers. Allow students time to review the information presented in the train timetable, Then ask them to work in pairs work out the answers to the Explore questions. Encourage students to support one another in interpreting the information from the timetable to find which train each friend should take. Work through Master 1–4 to show students how to work out the best journey options for the characters who are travelling. During Guided Practice, students are using timelines and information from the table to answer the questions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Copy of actual train timetables (optional for advanced learners)</li><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important pupils are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Navigating a variety of information to determine the best car given the specific criteria.</li><li>Example 2: Calculating journey length when reading a timetable.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time. Students are unsure of how to manage the absence of information in a table. Students use addition or subtraction to find time instead of using a timeline i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information listed in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read the columns and rows in a table. Students can interpret the absence of information in a table. Students can interpret information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe a variety of data visualisations to make meaning from the data, identifying features, patterns, and trends in context, and including the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Read, interpret, and use timetables and charts that present information about duration.</li><li>Read, interpret, and use timetables, charts, and results that present information about duration.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 2: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">176 - 179</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and answer questions based on information presented in a table including finding start time, end time and duration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task which shows the start times of various classes at a dance studio, together with how long each class is. Allow them time to review the information presented in the studio timetable including noticing that not all the classes are in the morning. Then ask them to work in pairs to answer the Explore question. Encourage students to support one another in interpreting the information from the timetable, including the use of the 24-hour clock. Work through Master 1 together first to guide students in using a timeline to solve the problem and then go through Master 2–4 after students have attempted the questions so that they may check their answers. During Guided Practice, students are using the information in the timetable to answer the problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>24-hour time</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li><li>start time</li><li>end time</li><li>midday</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Interpreting information from a table; using information given to answer questions relating to time and duration.</li><li>Example 2: Interpreting information from a table; looking at information given to choose a departure time.</li><li>Example 3: Interpreting information from a table to answer a question when given set criteria.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read the columns and rows</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use addition or subtraction to</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">in a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">find time instead of using a timeline i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Create and describe data visualisations to make meaning from the data, with statements including the names of the variable and group of interest.</li><li>Create and describe a variety of data visualisations to make meaning from the data, identifying features, patterns, and trends in context, and including the variable and group of interest.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Reading Tables":{"label":"Lesson 3: Reading Tables","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">180 - 183</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and answer questions based on information presented in a table including finding start time, end time and duration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task which shows the start times of various classes at a dance studio, together with the duration of each class. Allow them time to review the information presented in the studio timetable. Allow students time to work out the answer. Work through Master 1 together to find out how long Lulu's mum will be at the dance studio. Talk about the two different methods shown. Which do you prefer, using the timeline or adding the 3 durations? Allow students time to use the two methods to answer Master 2. Ask students if they think it is a challenging question. What is difficult about the question? What makes it harder to solve than the first question? Ask them to explain their strategies for calculating the passage of time. Can they use minutes? Can they use hours and minutes? During Guided Practice, students are solving problems based on the information that is presented about Tama and the dance studio timetable.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Enlarged printed copy of the Explore timetable (between 2 or 1 each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>duration</li><li>24-hour time</li><li>hour</li><li>minute</li><li>a.m.</li><li>p.m.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Calculating time of departure using given criteria and information in a timetable.</li><li>Example 2: Calculating time of arrival by adding durations of time from a starting time.</li><li>Example 3: Calculating duration based on the answers to examples 1 and 2.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have trouble reading 24-hour time.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can locate and read information in a table when they are asked a specific question.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read simple tables. Students can read columns and rows in a</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use addition or subtraction to</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">find time instead of using a timeline or number bonds i.e. they use base 10 rather than calculate in minutes and</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the absence of information in a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">hours.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the information from a table.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use multivariate data to investigate summary, comparison, and time-series situations, by:</li><li>posing an investigative question that can be answered with data</li><li>making conjectures or assertions about expected findings&quot;.</li><li>Identify, explain, check, and, if necessary, improve features in others' data investigations (e.g., biased survey questions, misleading information or statements).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 4: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">184 - 187</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information provided in different types of graphs such as a strip graph, a pie chart and a line graph.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, give students some time to study the different data presentations in the Explore task. Ask if they are all presenting the same data and have students give examples to confirm this. Discuss with students some similarities and advantages of the different types of presentations. For example, highlight that a strip graph is similar to a pie chart because like pie charts, a strip graph is broken into sections which make up a whole. Discuss also why someone might choose to use a line graph instead of a bar chart. How might a line graph be more helpful? Next, get students to look at the question shown on the page, and have them share their thoughts. Then, go through Master 1 to discuss the answer and consolidate their understanding. Ask students how many other questions they can develop based on the information provided. Allow students time to develop their own questions. Use Master 2 to explore how the greatest and least number of passengers can be determined by looking at a pie chart. Ask students if it is easier to gather this information from a pie chart or a line graph. Use Master 3 to explore with students what the slope of the line graph tells us. Does the number of passengers go up when the line goes up between 11:00 and 12:00? What happens to the number of passengers between 12:00 and 13:00? Continue this discussion by exploring what a straight horizontal line means on a line graph. During Guided Practice, students are looking at data from a strip graph and placing that information into a table. They then answer different questions about the same data using different types of graphs.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank table to complete</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>strip graph</li><li>pie chart</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1a): Reading and interpreting data from a strip graph and entering it into a table.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer the investigative question, comparing findings with initial conjectures or assertions and their existing knowledge of the world.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Students read, interpret and complete information in a table. ● Students read and interpret a graph. ● Students solve problems using information from tables and graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 5: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">188 - 192</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented on a line graph, where the data is represented by more than one line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and give them some time to make sense of the information that is being presented. Ask students what they are able to tell from the line graph. What can they say about the sales of ice creams and candy floss? Is it easy to compare the two? Draw their attention to the y-axis. What do they notice? Confirm that the number of sales have been rounded to the nearest 100. Discuss the statements in Master 1. Are they correct? How can you check? Then work through Master 2, supporting students as they use the\ninformation in the line graph to answer the questions. Allow them to\nanswer the questions in Masters 3 and 4 before reviewing the questions\nwith them so they may check their answers. During Guided Practice, students are reading information from a line\ngraph, and then placing the information onto a table. They answer\nquestions based on the information and decide which questions are easier\nto answer using the table and which using the line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (two each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>line graph</li><li>slope</li><li>increased</li><li>decreased</li><li>axis</li><li>horizontal</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Taking information from the line graph and placing it into a table.</li><li>Examples 2–6: Answering questions relating to the information in the graph and table.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plan how to collect primary data or how to use provided data, including identifying the variables of interest and, for provided data:</li><li>identifying who the data was collected from</li><li>identifying the original investigator’s purpose for collecting the data</li><li>deciding if the source is reliable (e.g., by checking if survey questions appear to be biased towards a particular point of view)&quot;.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students read, interpret and complete information in a table.</li><li>● Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 6: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">193 - 199</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented on a line graph, where the data is represented by more than one line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them time to talk about what the table tells them. Ask them what we can say about the monthly sales of each of the different comics. Allow them time to study the information and ask them if they would be able to construct a line graph that represents all the data. Why might a line graph be useful here? How many lines would they need? What would each axis stand for? Tell students that the axes would be the months of the year and the number of comic sales. Is this correct? Would this be helpful? How would we represent each comic and the number of sales? Allow students to discuss this. Then display Master 1 to show what the line graph should look like. Work through the rest of Master 1 and then Masters 2–4 with the class. What does it mean when a line is horizontal on the graph? Is this information easier to see on the line graph or in the table? During Guided Practice, students are reading and interpreting the information presented in the line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank graph with pre-constructed x and y axis, labelled with the correct information from Explore</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students confuse one line for the other when there is more than one set of data.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can turn a table of information into a line graph with more than one data set (more than one line).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse one line for the other when there is more than one set of data.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can turn a table of information into a line graph with more than one data set (more than one line).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read basic information from a line graph. Students can put the information from a line graph into a table.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make inferences from the information shown on a line graph.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can see the data as cumulative and independent.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can interpret the relationship between two data sets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Answer the investigative question, comparing findings with initial conjectures or assertions and their existing knowledge of the world.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 7. \n\nLessons 6 and 7 are consolidation lessons and therefore can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>● Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Reading Line Graphs":{"label":"Lesson 7: Reading Line Graphs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">200 - 202</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and interpret information presented in a table and turn it into a line graph. To be able to determine the relationships between data sets.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss what is being shown in the table. Ask students if they are able to create a line graph to represent the information shown. Give them time to discuss and then construct the graph. Then display the graph in Master 1 to show them what it should look like. Work through Masters 2 and 3 for students to answer the questions to analyse the data shown in the line graph. During Guided Practice, students are analysing the weekly growth of a plant from a line graph. They are also required to read specific information from the graph to answer the questions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Blank graph with pre-constructed x and y axis, labelled with the correct information from Explore</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">relevant data on.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">into a line graph.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">a line graph. Students can put the information from a line graph into a table. Students can make inferences from the information shown on a line graph. Students can see the data as cumulative and independent. Students can interpret the relationship between two data sets. Students can create line graphs from a set of data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Plan how to collect primary data or how to use provided data, including identifying the variables of interest and, for provided data: ● identifying who the data was collected from</li><li>- identifying the original investigator’s purpose for collecting the data</li><li>- deciding if the source is reliable (e.g., by checking if survey questions appear to be biased towards a particular point of view).</li><li>Collect primary data and check for errors, and provide information about variables in secondary data (e.g., how data was collected for them and possible outcomes for them).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 6. \n\nLessons 6 and 7 are consolidation lessons and therefore can be combined or omitted if time is an issue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students read and interpret a line graph.</li><li>Students solve problems using information from tables and line graphs.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">203 - 204</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of line graphs to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students use the given information to draw a line graph.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use the given information to draw a line graph.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This journal task may take more than one lesson and could be completed in pairs or groups. Explain to students that they will be carrying out their own statistical investigation, requiring them to collect, record, and present the findings of their chosen investigative question. Discuss the importance of choosing a topic and creating an investigative question that can be answered with data, and how data-collection or survey questions need to be consistent. It may help to show students models of tables, surveys, and questionnaires. Once students have gathered their data, they decide the most appropriate way to display it (drawn by hand or created online) to help answer their investigative question, then discuss describing and analysing their data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 6: Fractions":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This chapter develops students' ability to handle more diverse problems involving fractions, including dividing and multiplying fractions by whole numbers. To begin the chapter, students divide whole numbers by whole numbers, giving rise to fractions. Students then show improper fractions and mixed numbers using pictures. \n\nAs they progress through the unit, they find equivalent fractions, compare and order fractions and utilise the number bond strategy, known as number pairs, in their work with fractions. Next, students review adding fractions, with a focus on fractions with different denominators and fractions that create improper fractions and mixed numbers. \n\nThen they subtract fractions that are different, finding common denominators and subtracting mixed numbers and improper fractions. At the end of the chapter, students begin to multiply fractions by whole numbers and multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers. The final lesson involves solving word problems that require multiple steps and bar model representations.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Dividing to Make Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 1: Dividing to Make Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">206 - 208</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to divide whole numbers to create fractions; to be able to create mixed numbers and improper fractions when dividing whole numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to describe how the pani popo have been arranged into the 4 boxes. How many pani popo are there? (8) How many boxes? (4) How many pani popo are in each box? (2) How can we show this as a fraction? What information must be included? Is the number of boxes here important? What about the number of pani popo? Can we show this by using a picture? What about using numbers? Allow students time to solve this problem using concrete materials, and then work through Master 1 and 2 together. Allow students time to discuss the problem in Master 3 and then ask them if they think it is possible share 7 pani popo equally between 4 plates. How can we be sure? What would that look like using pictures and numbers? Ask students what types of fractions they can create when dividing 7 pani popo among 4 plates. Tell them your friend says she can create improper fractions. What does that mean? What is improper about them? What is it if it is not an improper fraction? Work through Master 3 and 4 and discuss the different methods. During Guided Practice, students are creating improper fractions and mixed numbers by dividing.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper circles</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Cubes/counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>quarters</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not share items equally. Students divide items equally until there is a remainder and this is left out.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide each whole item by the number of people/plates it is being shared with to create a fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide an even number of objects equally between 2. Students can divide an even number of objects equally among 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide an even number of objects equally among 4. Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions in pictorial form.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can divide one object equally among more than one person.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain and represent how to divide 1 into different amounts of equal parts.</li><li>Students recognise mixed numbers and improper fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Writing Improper Fractions and Mixed Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 2: Writing Improper Fractions and Mixed Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">209 - 212</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to write improper fractions and mixed numbers using a number line and pictorial methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to work with a partner to label the number line. Give them time to explore together. Ask them if they thought the numbers were already in order. If not, why not? Tell them your friend says that there is only one number larger than 1 as there is only one number with a 1 before the fraction. Is that true? Refer to Master 1 and check students' understanding of proper fractions. Refer to Master 2 and check students' understanding of 5/5 = 1 and 10/5 = 2 Refer to Master 3: Tell the students that you think it is not possible to turn 1 2/5 into fifths only. Is this true? Is it possible to turn a mixed number into an improper fraction? What do we need to do? Allow students to discuss this. Can we draw a picture of 1 2/5? Would that be helpful in turning it into an improper fraction? Refer to Master 4: Relate the numbers to the bar models/fraction walls and to the number line. Refer to Master 5: Can students notice the multiples of 5 in the numerators when converting whole numbers into improper fractions? During Guided Practice, students are turning mixed numbers and whole numbers into improper fractions and vice versa, using the method they are most comfortable with.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Blank paper number lines</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Placing fractions on a number line.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students confuse the terms 'denominator', 'numerator', 'mixed numbers' and 'improper fractions'.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can convert mixed numbers into improper fractions, and vice versa, using multiplication and division.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions using bar models or other pictures.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add the whole number to the denominator and numerator to get the improper fraction, and vice versa.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can place fractions and mixed numbers on a number line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe that fifths are bigger than quarters becaue 5 is bigger than 4.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students explain and represent how to divide 1 into different amounts of equal parts.</li><li>Students recognise mixed numbers and improper fractions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Finding Equivalent Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 3: Finding Equivalent Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">213 - 216</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to find equivalent fractions using pictorial methods.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask students to think about the different ways 1/2 of the plot could be divided into smaller plots. Allow them to use paper to fold or draw on to recreate the vegetable plot in the picture, and give them time to make their own fractions. Refer to Master 1. Ask the students if the representation looks like theirs.\nWhy/Why not? Is it possible to cut half of the plot into smaller pieces, but\nnot the other half? Allow students to discuss what needs to happen. Agree\nthat when 1/2 is divided into 2 equal parts, 1/2 becomes 2/4. 1/2 and 2/4\nare equivalent fractions. Move to Master 2. What if we divide half of the plot into 3 equal pieces?\nWhat would we have? Why is this the case?\nMonitor as students work on Master 3 where they are asked to divide the\nwhole plot to make hundredths. How many hundredths would be in half of\nthe plot? How can we check?\nIf the size of the plot does not change, then what can we say about 1/2\nand 4/8? What about 1/2 and 5/10? Are there any other things we can say\nabout these fractions? Can we say that 4/8 and 50/100 are equivalent? Is\nthis possible? How can we check?\nUse Master 4 to further clarify and explain how to find equivalent fractions.\nRefer to Master 5 to show the equivalence on the number line. Students\nshould be encouraged to make the link between the equivalent fractions,\nnoticing that the numerator and denominator have the same proportional\nrelationship.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are finding equivalent fractions, where\nthe new denominator and numerator are multiples of the original.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper squares</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Blank paper number lines</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">plot into more pieces', and then use division to attempt this.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can simplify fractions using division.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">2-digit numbers by a single digit. Students can divide single-digit and</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">2-digit numbers by a single digit. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions using bar models or other pictures.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can place fractions and mixed numbers on a number line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find equivalent fractions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>● compare and order the fractions ● identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students explain the vertical relationship between numerators and denominators within equivalent fractions (1/5, 1/3 and equivalent).</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Comparing and Ordering Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 4: Comparing and Ordering Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">217 - 219</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare and order fractions using the pictorial method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them if they are able to draw or fold paper to show the amount of paper Charles and Tama each took. Give them some time to determine how this should look. Tell students that your friend thinks Tama uses 4/6 of the original paper. Is this true? How can we check? Review together the fraction strips in Master 1 and 2 to confirm that in order to compare the two amounts of paper used, the whole needs to be divided into 9 and that Tama uses 4/9 of the original paper. Ask students to compare 1/3 and 4/9. Which is greater? How can we tell? Ask students if they know how to draw 1/2, 1/3 and 5/12 to show which is the biggest. What do we need to ensure we do if we are going to compare fractions? Does the size of the whole matter? What if we use a big piece of paper to show 1/2, but a small piece of paper to show 3/4? Would that make a difference? How can we check? Which is the correct method? Use the images and methods from Master 3 to compare the fractions. Emphasise the importance of using the same size for the whole when comparing fractions. During Guided Practice, students are comparing and ordering fractions using the pictorial method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Paper circles</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using &gt;, &lt; or = to compare fractions using</li><li>pre-designed pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students rename the remaining pieces of the fraction and forget about the original number of pieces (for example, dividing 3 pieces into 6 pieces may make some students think the new fraction is sixths).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions in pictorial format from pre-designed pictures and their own creations.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a picture. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students divide part of the pictorial representation, but don't divide ALL parts.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide a fraction into its equivalence. Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions</li><li>identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions.</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Comparing and Ordering Improper Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 5: Comparing and Ordering Improper Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">220 - 222</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare and order improper fractions using the pictorial method.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Ask them if they are able to recreate the task by folding paper or using pictures. Allow students time to discuss their ideas and begin folding or drawing. Refer to Master 1: Tell them that Tama took 12 pieces, so he took more chocolate. Is this correct? Allow students to prove that this is incorrect. How much did Tama actually take? Ask them to compare the two amounts to see which is the largest. Tell students that because 1/5 is bigger than 1/10, 8/5 must be the largest. Is this true? How can we be sure? If this is true, what about 8/5 and 12/10? Is it true that because the numerator and the denominator are smaller in 8/5, then it must be the smaller fraction? How can we prove that this is incorrect? What is the relationship between the digit in the denominator's place and the size of the pieces? If students struggle with this, tell them that as the denominator gets smaller, the pieces get bigger. Is this true? Is it always true? How can we be sure? Work through Master 2 and 3 using the images to illustrate how to compare and order improper fractions. During Guided Practice, students are comparing fractions using a method they are comfortable with. They are also arranging fractions from smallest to greatest.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>common denominator</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Comparing fractions using &gt; or &lt; with pictorial</li><li>support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions</li><li>identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions.</li><li>Students correctly use the language of equivalent fractions.</li><li>Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Comparing and Ordering Mixed Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 6: Comparing and Ordering Mixed Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">223 - 224</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task. Allow them time to discuss the different amounts and how they can represent these amounts in different ways. Tell them that they can use bar models to show the common denominators, where one fraction is\nalready the common denominator for all\nfractions in the question. fractions and find out which is the smallest amount. What does that mean?\nHow can mixed numbers be represented in bar models? Can this be\ndone? What has to be the same about the models? What will be different?\nGive them time to work on this with their partner. Once students have determined how to compare the different amounts,\nallow them time to work out which recipe contains the least amount of flour.\nWork through Master 1 to check their answers and then Master 2 to find\nwhich is more — 2 3/4 or 2 5/8.\nNow ask students to look at Master 3. What can we do to make it easier to\ncompare these amounts? Allow students to discuss and solve the problem.\nAsk them if they are able to arrange the fractions using the symbols &gt; and\n&lt;. Is this possible? Allow some time for students to order the fractions and\nthen discuss as a class to check students' solutions and understanding.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are identifying greater and smaller\nfractions. The denominators can all be easily changed, but their placement\nvaries.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips ● Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>common denominator</li><li>equal</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Comparing mixed numbers using &gt; or &lt;. Example 2: Ordering three mixed numbers, from smallest to greatest.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe that the bigger the denominator, the bigger the fraction (for example, sixths are bigger than fifths).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions in pictorial format from pre-designed pictures and their own creations.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read mixed numbers and improper fractions. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>For fractions with denominators of 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, or 100:</li><li>compare and order the fractions ● identify when two fractions are equivalent.</li><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including: ● Students use representations to describe and compare two fractions. ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Making Number Pairs":{"label":"Lesson 7: Making Number Pairs","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">225 - 227</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them to discuss the important information in the question. Ask them if they are able to use bar models to show what the question is suggesting. How can we denominators. show 1 whole – 1/8? What do we need to do to the whole amount? Once\nstudents have been able to represent the lasagne appropriately, ask them\nto think about all the different ways that Tama and Ruby can share 7/8.\nAllow students some time to work this out, using pictures and concrete\nmaterials finding multiple suggestions.\nUse Master to show students some of the ways to form the number pairs\nof 7/8. Tell students that one way to share 7/8 is using sixteenths. Is this\npossible? Can eighths be converted into sixteenths? What is the maximum\nnumber of sixteenths that one person could get from 7/8? Allow students to\ndiscuss and then prove their answers. What about quarters? Give students\ntime to discuss the different ways that 7/8 can be shared between Tama\nand Ruby. How many number pairs can be made? In Master 3 students\ncan explore the numerous possible number pairs.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using number pairs to create\nfractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>fractions</li><li>share equally</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students believe the only way to make number pairs is by using fractions that have the same denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">To make number pairs (number bonds) with fractions that have different denominators.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read mixed numbers and improper fractions. Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Check the curriculum</td><td class=\"lt-td\">denominators to make up to one whole (e.g.,</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent mixed numbers and improper fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">National Curriculum Add and subtract fractions with the same</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare fractions using equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can arrange fractions from smallest to greatest and vice versa.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use bar models to make pairs of fractions with different denominators from a given fraction.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">3/8 + 3/8 + 2/8 = 8/8 = 1).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same denominators to make up to one whole (e.g., 3/8 + 3/8 + 2/8 = 8/8 = 1).</li><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 8: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">228 - 230</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a|To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to&lt;br&gt;identify the important information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to\nidentify the important information. Ask students if they can use counters or common denominator using pictorial methods. draw a picture to represent the box of doughnuts, and colour in what the\ntwo friends said they took. Would this be helpful? Would it allow us to see\nhow much was eaten exactly? Allow students to discuss and solve with the\nir partner.\nRefer to Master 1 &amp; 2 and discuss their methods, check students'\nunderstanding and clarify any misconceptions.\nRefer to Master 3. Ask the students if they can draw a box of doughnuts\nagain with 12 doughnuts in total. Tell them that three friends share it. The\nfirst friend eats 1/2 of the box, the second eats 1/6 and the third eats 3/12.\nIs this possible? Can we show this in our picture? Give them time to\ndiscuss and represent the amounts. Can we shade in sixths on a picture\nthat is divided into twelfths? What do we need to do?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are adding like and unlike fractions,\nwhere the common denominator is in the group of fractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Counters</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>denominator</li><li>common denominator</li><li>add</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Adding two fractions with pictorial support; a) same denominator, b) one denominator is a multiple of the other</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 9: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">231 - 232</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a|To begin this lesson, display the Explore task and ask students if they can&lt;br&gt;show how much Holly drinks in total.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions by finding a To begin this lesson, display the Explore task and ask students if they can\nshow how much Holly drinks in total. Allow students time to work on this common denominator using pictorial methods. and then discuss their methods as a class. Refer to Master 1: Is it possible to show 1/2 and 1/10 of two different jugs? How can we do this? Allow students time to determine what this might look like with their partner. Suggest to them that to add unlike fractions, the fractions must have the same name, so they need to turn tenths into halves. Is this correct? Is it possible to turn 1/10 into halves? Why, or why not? Which fraction should we change? Refer to Master 2: Next, tell students that one of your friends has said that Holly has drunk 6/10 l, but another friend says that she drank 3/5 l. Who is correct? Remind students that they should simplify fractions where possible. Can fifths become halves or tenths? Ask students to discuss how they will add the three fractions together and allow them time to work on it. During Guided Practice, students are adding like and unlike fractions. They are also exposed to the term 'sum'.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li><li>Maths Language Variation It is important students are able to understand and use the Example 1: Adding two fractions where both denominators are following mathematical terms in this lesson: the same ● simplify ● fraction Examples 2 &amp; 3: Adding two fractions where the first fraction</li><li>Examples 2 &amp; 3: Adding two fractions where the first fraction has the common denominator</li><li>denominator ● equal</li><li>common denominator</li><li>sum Example 4: Adding two fractions where the second fraction has</li><li>the common denominator</li><li>Example 5: Adding three fractions where one denominator is a</li><li>multiple of the other two denominators</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add fractions with the same denominator accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not find a common denominator when adding unlike fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 10: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">233 - 235</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them if they are able to identify the key information. What unit of measurement is being used? What solutions can we come up with? Give time for students to sum is greater than 1, creating mixed numbers think and work together. or improper fractions. Refer to Master 1: Ask students if they have represented both fractions using a bar model. Can you see 1/4 inside of 7/8 in the bar models you have drawn? How many eighths are equal to 1/4? Is that helpful when adding them together? Refer to Master 2: Ask students if they are able to show you the solution using an improper fraction and a mixed number. What is 9/8 as a mixed number? Allow them to discuss and solve this. Refer to Master 3: What method did Oak use During Guided Practice, students are using number pairs and addition to find the sum of two unlike fractions, where the sum is an improper fraction or mixed number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips ● Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>common denominator</li><li>add</li><li>sum</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students add both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction. Students do not find a common denominator when adding unlike fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator. Students can find an improper fraction when adding fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'. Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator. Students can find common denominators.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Adding Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 11: Adding Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">236 - 238</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add unlike fractions, which create|To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to&lt;br&gt;identify the important information.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To be able to add unlike fractions, which create To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to\nidentify the important information. What do we know? What amount are we improper fractions, and mixed numbers that starting with? What amount are we adding? Allow students to discuss this give rise to simplification. and attempt to represent this information using bar models. Encourage multiple methods. Discuss their ideas as a class. Refer to Master 1: Is this problem like others we have seen? How can we add tenths and fifths? Which fraction should we change? Can we simplify our fraction. Refer to Master 2: Work through this question together. During Guided Practice, students are adding unlike fractions that give rise to simplification.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students have difficulties drawing bar models for 4/5 of a kilogram and 7/10 a kilogram.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students have difficulties drawing bar models for 4/5 of a kilogram and 7/10 a kilogram.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can divide and share concrete materials. Students can represent fractions as a bar model.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the terms 'numerator' and 'denominator'.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read a fraction written in words and numbers. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators. Students can make equivalent fractions. Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 12: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">239 - 241</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to subtract fractions with different denominators; to be able to subtract fractions from whole numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can represent the information. How many pieces of ribbon are there to cut? What is difficult about subtracting these two amounts? Allow students time to discuss this and attempt to solve the problem with their partner. Suggest to them that they can subtract 1/6 from 1 whole because 6 sixths and 1 whole are the same amount; but you are confused about what to do when you have 5/6 left over and need to subtract 5/12. Can they help you? What do we need to do? Give students time to solve this, discuss their ideas as a class and then work through Master 1. Refer to Master 2: Ask the class how we can solve a similar problem but starting with 2 as the whole number rather than 1. How can we subtract 2/3 from 2? Is it possible? How can we represent it using pictures? What about using numbers? Allow students time to discuss and represent this idea with their partner. Tell them that as they are taking away less than 1 from 2, they will have 1 and something left over. Is this true? What would this look like in pictures? Refer to Master 3: Ask students if they are able to find the difference between two fractions, such as 5/8 and 1/4. Is it possible to do this? What do we have to do first? Do they have the same denominator? Is there a way to solve this using pictures before we use numbers? What might that look like? Work through Master 3 to illustrate this and present the formal way of recording solutions. During Guided Practice, students are subtracting unlike fractions from one another, where only one fraction needs to change to have the same denominator as the other.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>denominator</li><li>equal</li><li>common denominator</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 13: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">242 - 244</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the key information in the problem. Give them time to draw and label bars to represent the problem before working out the answer. use bar models as a key strategy for subtracting\nfractions. What do we know about the amount of pizza that Hannah and Ravi ate.\nWho ate more? By how much? Ask students what they should check next\nto find the difference in the amount of pizza the two friends ate. How can\nwe work with two different fractions on one bar? Is this possible? What\nwould be more helpful for us?\nUse Master 1 to work through, and solve, the problem, checking the 4\nsteps with them. Give students time to work on subtracting 5/12 from 3/4\nbefore working through Master 2 to show the solution.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using bar model diagrams to help\nthem visually represent the subtraction of fractions with different\ndenominators. They simplify their answers where necessary.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>common denominator; reducing the fraction to its simplest</li><li>form.</li><li>Example 1: Supported pictorially with bar models.</li><li>Example 2: No pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students subtract both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction. Students do not find a common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two fractions, when one of the fractions has the common denominator. Students can subtract fractions when given two parts, or one part and the original amount.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture. Students can subtract fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make equivalent fractions. Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 14: Subtracting Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 14: Subtracting Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">245 - 248</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how we can attempt to solve the problem. What information do we know? What's numbers from mixed numbers with different\ndenominators. different about this problem to ones you've approached previously? What\ndo you notice about the two masses of strawberries that the families eat?\nAllow students time to discuss and work on this with their partner,\nprompting them if necessary to talk about the mixed numbers.\nGive them time to discuss their approach and ask them if they are able to\nprove their answers by using a bar model or pictures. Is it possible?\nDiscuss their ideas as a class. Work through Master 1–4 to show students\nhow to work with mixed numbers and improper fractions. What is the\ndifference between these methods? What is the same about them?\nDuring Guided Practice, students are finding the difference between two\nmixed numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>find the difference</li><li>subtract</li><li>simplify</li><li>fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>denominator</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Subtracting mixed numbers.</li><li>Example 1: Using two different methods; supported pictorially with bar models</li><li>Example 2: No pictorial support, numbers in abstract form</li><li>Example 3: No pictorial support, word problem</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students subtract both the numerators and denominators to create a new fraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find a common denominator between two mixed numbers when one of the mixed numbers has the common denominator.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model. Students can recognise a fraction represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can find common denominators.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can make equivalent fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a times table chart to find common multiples.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract mixed numbers when their denominator is the same.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract fractions with the same or related denominators (e.g., 1/4 + 1/8).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome: ● Students use their knowledge of equivalent fractions to solve problems.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 15: Multiplying Whole Numbers by Proper Fractions":{"label":"Lesson 15: Multiplying Whole Numbers by Proper Fractions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">249 - 251</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them to discuss the task with a partner.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Allow students to explore the key To be able to multiply fractions by whole numbers creating other fractions, mixed\nnumbers or improper fractions. information from the problem. How could you represent the information to\nfind out what fraction of a sushi roll Jacob ate? Allow students time to work\non this task with their partner then share their thinking with the class.\nRefer to Master 1: Tell them that multiplication is the same operation as\nrepeated addition. If this is true, then how can we multiply 1/5? Can they\nshow you by using pictures? If we use pictures to find 1/5 times 3, we will\nget 3/15. Is this correct? What is wrong with this answer? Can we prove it\nis wrong?\nRefer to Master 2: Work on this question together and share methods.\nCan we get a mixed number after multiplying a fraction? Can we show that\nby using pictures? Use Master 3 to illustrate this.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying fractions by whole\nnumbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Multiplying fractions by whole numbers.</li><li>Example 1: Supported pictorially with fraction squares. Example 2: Supported pictorially with a bar model.</li><li>Example 3: No pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use known multiplication facts to scale a quantity.</li><li>Convert between mixed numbers and improper fractions with denominators of up to 10.</li><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students explain the relationship between repeated addition of a proper fraction and multiplication of fractions.</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 16: Multiplying Proper Fractions and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 16: Multiplying Proper Fractions and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">252 - 255</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them how they would represent the word problem.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask students to represent the To be able to multiply fractions by whole numbers, where the product is an improper\nfraction or mixed number. problem with a picture, and then allow them time to work out the answer to\nthe question with their partner.\nTell them that when multiplying a fraction, we only multiply the numerator.\nIs this true? Can they show this on their pictures?\nWork through Master 1 and 2, allowing the class to check and discuss their\nanswers. Emphasise to the children the commutative property here, so that\nthey understand that 3/4 × 6 = 6 × 3/4.\nAsk students to find the value of 2/3 × 12 and 2/3 × 13 (Master 3 and 5).\nGive them time to explore the numerous ways in which multiplication can\nbe done before showing them the answers. During Master 4, students will\nneed to combine their multiplication facts knowledge with their place value\nknowledge to support mental calculations.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are multiplying fractions by whole\nnumbers, giving rise to mixed numbers or improper fractions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the</li><li>following mathematical terms and phrases in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1 &amp; 2: Multiplying the same fraction by different whole</li><li>numbers; looking for patterns. Example 3: Multiplying a fraction by a whole number to solve a word problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students multiply the denominators when they are multiplying the fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent the multiplication of a fraction by a whole number in pictures to solve.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent fractions as a bar model or picture. Students can recognise a fraction when it is represented as a picture.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can repeatedly add fractions with the same denominator.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can represent improper fractions and mixed numbers using bar model diagrams or other pictures.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports this learning outcome:</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 17: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 17: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">256 - 257</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask if they are able to show you how to solve this problem using bar models.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Allow them To be able to multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers, creating larger mixed numbers. time to work on this with their partner.\nIs it possible to show mixed numbers as a bar model? Tell them they can\nshow this in pictures, but some of the bars would need be cut into pieces,\nwhile others wouldn't need to be. Is this true? What would this look like?\nWhy would some of the bars not need to be divided? Work through Master\nwith the class to show the two methods. Ask them which method they\npreferred: turning the mixed number into an improper fraction or dealing\nwith the wholes separately giving an explanation for their preference.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are using both methods to solve\nmultiplication problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li><li>simplify</li><li>multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1 &amp; 3: The mixed number comes first in the calculation</li><li>Examples 2 &amp; 4: The whole number comes first in the calculation</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>● Students multiply a mixed number by a whole number.</li><li>● Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 18: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 18: Multiplying Mixed Numbers and Whole Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">258 - 260</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task, and then ask them to write down all the information they have been given from the task.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Ask To be able to multiply mixed numbers by whole numbers in multi-step word problems. students how they can show 3 2/3 as far as 4 kilometres. What would that\nlook like? What would each third be worth? Is it possible to draw this as a\nbar model? Allow students to work together to draw this diagram.\nAllow students time to solve this problem and then share their ideas before\nworking through Master 1 and 2 with the class.\nDuring Guided Practice, students are solving word problems involving\nmulti-step multiplication. They are asked if there is more than one way to\nsolve each of the problems.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coloured paper strips</li><li>Fraction wall</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It is important students are able to understand and use the following mathematical terms in this lesson:</li><li>proper fraction</li><li>improper fraction</li><li>mixed number</li><li>whole number</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li><li>simplify</li><li>multiply</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Solving a word problem involving kilometres. Example 2: Multiplying mixed numbers by a whole number; no given context.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Find a fraction of a whole number, using multiplication and division facts and where the answer is a whole number (e.g., 2/3 of 24).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This is a key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson which supports a number of learning outcomes, including:</li><li>Students multiply a mixed number by a whole number.</li><li>Students multiply a proper fraction by a whole number.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 19: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 19: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">261 - 262</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of fractions to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students solve a multi-step word problem involving the addition of mixed numbers and the subtraction of that total from a whole number.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students solve a multi-step word problem involving the addition of mixed numbers and the subtraction of that total from a whole number.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students determine who — Meilani, Charles, Ravi or Hannah — has worked out the fraction addition problem correctly, and explain why the other answers are wrong.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 7: Decimals":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>In this chapter, students explore decimals. To begin this chapter, they learn to read and write decimal numbers. This is followed by comparing decimal numbers to find out which is greater, and which and smaller. Students also explore equivalent fractions, and in particular converting fractions to an equivalent with a denominator of 10 or 100. Students then add and subtract decimals with up to two decimal places. The chapter ends with students rounding decimals to the nearest whole number and nearest tenth.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Writing Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 1: Writing Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">2 - 5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to write decimal numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask how many of the flat 10 × 10 squares are needed to make the whole cube. Repeat this for the 10 rod and the ones cube. Introduce the terms ‘tenths’, ‘hundredths’ and ‘thousandths’. Explain that when you need to describe amounts that are not whole numbers, it can be useful to count using these parts. Discuss some simple examples, such as your height (or the ceiling height) which might be 1 metre and 6 tenths of a metre.</p><p>Work through Master 1–4, which illustrates the links between 1 whole, 1 tenth, 1 hundredth and 1 thousandth, how many of each make 1 whole, and how to write each as a fraction and a decimal. Recap on the terms ‘decimal’ and ‘decimal point’.</p><p>In Master 5, explain how the numbers illustrated can be written as decimals and in words.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are looking at concrete representations of ones, tenths, hundredths and thousandths. If students do not yet understand how to write decimals, it is fine for them to write the answers as words at this stage, for example, 3 hundredths and 3 thousandths.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base 10 materials</li><li>Place-value counters - 1, 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>It takes ___ of [100 square, 10 rod, 1 one] to make 1 whole.</li><li>This shows the number ___.</li><li>This shows ___ tenth(s) and ___ hundredth(s)/thousandth(s).</li><li>This shows ___ hundredth(s) and ___ thousandth(s).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Identifying the value of a set of base-10 blocks and writing each number as a decimal.</li><li>Example 2: Identifying the value of a set of base-10 blocks and writing each number as a decimal, then matching each decimal to the correct set of place-value counters.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students struggle to use the 10 × 10 × 10 block to represent 1, when they previously used it to represent 1000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can match base 10 (place-value) blocks to written decimal numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use base 10 blocks to represent decimal numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not understand how the different base 10 blocks relate to one another.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say what each base 10 block represents.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the relative sizes of base 10 blocks to identify their different values.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show decimal numbers using base 10 blocks or place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals as tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the decimal numbers represented by sets of base 10 blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, and represent fractions, decimals (to two places), and related percentages.</li><li>Identify, read, write, and represent fractions, decimals (to three places), and percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson introduces decimal fractions such as one hundredth.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 2: Reading and Writing Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">6 - 8</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and write decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask them if they think Lulu is correct. students should identify the hundredth and the thousandths. Ask them how many thousandths there are in the hundredth. Is Ruby correct? The key learning point here is that the decimal fractions can be combined.</p><p>Work through Master 1–2, which explain the answer to the Explore task with pictorial support. Focus on the equivalence of fractions, words and decimals.</p><p>Work through Master 3, which will be important for the Guided Practice questions. If the class understands this well, you could try examples involving tenths, for example, 3 tenths and 4 hundredths (34 hundredths), or 3 tenths 4 hundredths and 5 thousandths (345 thousandths).</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are looking at hundredths and thousandths, and what they represent as decimals.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base 10 materials</li><li>Place-value counters - 1, 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001</li><li>Digit cards - 0, 7 and 9</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>[place-value counters] is equal to ___ hundredth(s) and ___ thousandth(s).</li><li>___ hundredth(s) and ___ thousandth(s) is the same as ___ thousandths.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Identifying the number shown by base 10 blocks; using place-value counters to show each number then writing each number as a decimal and in words.</li><li>Example 2: Identifying the number shown by place-value counters, then writing each number as a decimal and in words.</li><li>Example 3a: Making 3 different decimal numbers using 3 given digits to fill the hundredths and thousandths places.</li><li>Example 3b: Showing each number made in (a) using base 10 blocks and place-value counters.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students struggle to use the 10 × 10 × 10 block to represent 1, when they previously used it to represent 1000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the value of a set of base 10 blocks using decimal numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use base 10 blocks to represent decimal numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not understand how the different base 10 blocks relate to one another.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say what each base 10 block represents.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the relative sizes of base 10 blocks to identify their different values.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show decimal numbers using base 10 blocks or place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals as tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths, hundredths and thousandths. Students can record decimal numbers as fractions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, and represent fractions, decimals (to three places), and percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson can be combined with Lesson 3.</li><li>These lessons show how to write decimals in tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 3: Reading and Writing Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">9 - 12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to read and write decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and allow them some time to solve the problem. Suggest to the class that because the 10-block has 10 squares it is a tenth. Is this true? Allow students time to disagree and work out that a 10-block is actually a hundredth. Why is this?</p><p>Ask the class what the blocks in the Explore task stand for. Then work through Master 1, which explains how to write the number as a decimal. Explain that 0.243 can be read as ‘243 thousandths’ or as ‘0, point, 2, 4, 3’.</p><p>In Master 2, ensure students understand the importance of the ‘0’ in the hundredths place, and that 0.12 is not the same as 0.012. Ask students to answer the question posed in the speech bubble. What other numbers could Ravi make using 3 blocks? There are a variety of answers, including 0.003 and 0.210.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are identifying and showing numbers using base 10 blocks and place-value counters, and writing numbers in decimals and in words.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base 10 materials</li><li>Place-value counters - 1, 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001</li><li>Digit cards - 0, 3 and 4</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We read [decimal number] as ___ hundredths/thousandths.</li><li>___ stands for ___ tenth(s) / ___ hundredth(s) / ___ thousandth(s).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Identifying the number shown by base 10 blocks; using place-value counters to show each number then writing each number as a decimal and in words.</li><li>Example 2: Identifying the number shown by place-value counters, then writing each number as a decimal and in words.</li><li>Example 3a: Making 4 different decimal numbers using 3 given digits to fill the tenths, hundredths and thousandths places.</li><li>Example 3b: Showing each number made in (a) using base 10 blocks and place-value counters.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students struggle to use the 10 × 10 × 10 block to represent 1, when they previously used it to represent 1000.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the value of a set of base 10 blocks using decimal numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use base 10 blocks to represent decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not understand how the different base 10 blocks relate to one another.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say what each base 10 block represents, using tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the relative sizes of base 10 blocks to identify their different values.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show decimal numbers using base 10 blocks or place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals as tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths, hundredths and thousandths. Students can record decimal numbers as fractions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, and represent fractions, decimals (to three places), and percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson completes the instruction on how to write decimals up to 3 decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Comparing Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 4: Comparing Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">13 - 15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to compare tenths and hundredths written as decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, ask the class which number is greater: 6 or 15? Then ask them which of these numbers is greater: 60 or 15? Show them the Explore task and discuss their ideas. If you need to prompt them, ask whether 6 tenths can be expressed using hundredths.</p><p>Next, work through the Master section, which uses base 10 materials to illustrate that 0.6 is greater than 0.15. Discuss with the class the easiest ways to compare two decimals. Suitable suggestions would be to convert everything to hundredths (or tenths or thousandths); to compare the tenths digits first before looking at other digits; or to add trailing zeros so that both numbers have the same number of decimal places.</p><p>Work through some other examples comparing decimals, relying as much as possible on input from the class. Some suitable examples include: 0.5 &gt; 0.08; 0.5 &lt; 0.8; 0.58 &lt; 0.85; 0.5 &lt; 0.55; 0.8 &gt; 0.58; 0.5 = 0.50.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are comparing simple decimals, where the number of decimal places varies.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Base 10 materials</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>greater than</li><li>smaller/less than</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>[decimal number] is ___ tenths / hundredths. ___ is greater / smaller than ___.</li><li>I compared these numbers by…</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Comparing two decimals; using base 10 materials if needed.</li><li>Examples 1–3: One number has one decimal place and the other has two decimal places.</li><li>Example 4: Both numbers have two decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not understand how the different base 10 blocks relate to one another.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can read decimal numbers and show them using base 10 blocks.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can show decimal numbers using base 10 blocks or place-value counters.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare pairs of decimal numbers using base 10 blocks and identify which is greater.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals as tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students think that numbers with more digits are always greater than those with fewer digits.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ symbols.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can record decimal numbers as fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students know that tenths are worth more than hundredths and that hundredths are worth more than thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ symbols.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compare and order tenths and hundredths as fractions and decimals, and convert decimal tenths and hundredths to fractions.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson can be combined with Lesson 5.</li><li>These lessons show how to compare decimals with up to 3 decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Pupils compare and order decimal numbers with hundredths.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Writing Fraction and Decimal Equivalents":{"label":"Lesson 5: Writing Fraction and Decimal Equivalents","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">16 - 21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to write fractions as decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>\"To begin this lesson, ask the class if they can remember how to write one half as a decimal. Regardless of whether they know the answer from memory, they should recall that 1/2 = 5/10 = 0.5.</p><p>Show students the Explore task and allow them some time to solve the problem. Discuss their ideas and address any misunderstandings about fractions, equivalent fractions and decimals. Lead them to see that ultimately it’s not possible to compare fifths, tenths, fiftieths and hundredths without converting them into the same unit. Ask you work through the Master section, you could complete a table that shows each distance as a simplified fraction, a decimal fraction and a decimal. students should see that it is easy to compare the fractions only when they have the same denominator, or when they are expressed as decimals.</p><p>In Master 3, a number line is used to support students' understanding of a number's position relative to the known numbers on the number line. If time allows, suggest some other numbers for students to place on the same number line. For example: 12/25, 0.09, one half, 0.8.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are converting fractions to decimals and vice versa, and comparing fractions and decimals to identify which is greater</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Fraction cards for Activity Time - 3/5, 18/20, 9/10, 2/10, 17/25, 4/20, 37/50, 11/25, 1/5, 31/50 (between two)</li><li>Decimal cards for Activity Time - 0.2, 0.2, 0.2, 0.6, 0.9, 0.9, 0.44, 0.62, 0.68, 0.74 (between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>fraction</li><li>numerator</li><li>denominator</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>greater than</li><li>less than</li><li>equivalent value</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>[fraction] is equivalent to [decimal].</li><li>[decimal] is equivalent to [fraction].</li><li>I converted this fraction to a decimal by…</li><li>I converted this decimal to a fraction by…</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Converting fractions (including mixed numbers), on a number from 0 to 2, to decimal numbers.</li><li>Example 2: Converting decimals, on a number line from 0 to 1.25, to fractions.</li><li>Example 3: Converting fractions in kilometres to decimals.</li><li>Example 4: Comparing pairs of fractions and decimals with up to 2 decimal places to determine which one is greater.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can convert fractions with denominators of 10 and 100 into decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals as tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students think that numbers with more digits are always greater than those with fewer digits.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can convert fractions with denominators that are factors of 10 or 100 into fractions with denominators of 10 or 100.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students disregard the denominator of fractions when comparing them.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students know that tenths are worth more than hundredths and that hundredths are worth more than thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the ‘greater than’ and ‘less than’ symbols.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify which number is greater by looking at the first decimal place.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can order decimal numbers from smallest to greatest.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, read, write, and represent fractions, decimals (to two places), and related percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson shows how to convert between fractions and decimals.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 6: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">22 - 24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract decimals with one decimal place.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, tell students to imagine that they have 10 oranges. They give two to a friend and eat one. How many do they have left? This same logic can be applied to the Explore task, using tenths rather than oranges.</p><p>Show students the Explore task and give them some time to think about the problem. The answer may be obvious for some students, but ask them for an explanation. Work through Master, which uses bar models to illustrate how to reach the answer. Allow students time to draw the bar model themselves.</p><p>You could ask similar questions to check students' understanding and discuss any misconceptions. For example: If Holly has 2 kg of flour, how much would she have left? If she made another recipe that needed 0.5 kg of flour, how much would she have left?</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are finding the sum and difference of tenths.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1 and 0.1</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>total</li><li>difference</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>___ tenth(s) and ___ tenth(s) make ___ tenths.</li><li>The difference between ___ tenth(s) and ___ tenth(s) is ___tenth(s).</li><li>I added these decimals by… I found the difference by...</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Adding and subtracting tenths in words and in numbers. A bar is given for pictorial support.</li><li>Example 2: Adding and subtracting tenths. Place-value counters are given for pictorial support.</li><li>Example 3: Finding one addend in an equation. No pictorial support.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add tenths less than 1 whole.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 1 decimal place in terms of tenths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with tenths and calculating with whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract tenths from tenths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students recognise that 1 whole is the same as 10 tenths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract decimals to one decimal place (e.g., 1.3 + 0.2 = 1.5).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson introduces basic arithmetic with decimals (tenths).</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 7: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">25 - 27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract decimals with up to 2 decimal places. To be able to add and subtract amounts in dollars and cents.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, tell the class to imagine that you have $3.40 in your pocket, all in 10c coins. How many 10c coins would you have? Ask them what is one tenth of $1. This will set students up with the right ideas for solving the Explore task.</p><p>Show students the Explore task and allow them some time to solve the problem before taking feedback. Encourage them to use place-value counters, 10c coins, bar models or other visual aids if they need them to solve the problem. Use the Master section to work through the different methods for finding the total cost, identifying as many similarities as possible with what they already know, such as adding whole numbers and counting in tenths. For Master 3, if students don't make the suggestion, ask them how to subtract 340 – 250 using a column method. This should be familiar. Then ask students whether they could use a similar method to solve $3.40 – $2.50.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are adding and subtracting decimals through thresholds.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1 and 0.1</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ones</li><li>tenths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>total</li><li>difference</li><li>column method</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The total cost of / difference between ___ and ___ is ___.</li><li>[decimal number] has ___ one(s) and ___ tenth(s).</li><li>I added these amounts of money by… I found the difference by...</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Examples 1–2: Finding the sum of 2 decimal numbers with 1 decimal place. Place-value counters given for the first decimal for pictorial support.</li><li>Example 3: Adding and subtracting decimal numbers with 1 decimal place.</li><li>Example 4: Solving a word problem by adding and subtracting decimal numbers with 2 decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add tenths less than 1 whole.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 1 decimal place in terms of tenths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with tenths and calculating with whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract tenths from tenths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not realise that they can rename one or $1 into tenths, in unfamiliar situations.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can regroup tenths and whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename ones and tenths, using place-value counters or a bar model if required.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename ones and tenths using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the column method to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract decimals to one decimal place (e.g., 1.3 + 0.2 = 1.5).</li><li>Add and subtract whole numbers and decimals to two decimal places (e.g., 32.55 – 21.21 = 11.34).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This lesson can be combined with Lesson 10.</li><li>These lessons show how to add and subtract decimals with up to 2 decimal places, which is essential for many tasks involving money.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 8: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">28 - 30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract amounts in dollars and cents when no renaming is required.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, ask the class how we use maths when dealing with money. For example, add up items to calculate a total price; subtract money to calculate change; multiply to find the total cost when buying a few of the same item; divide an amount to share between a group of friends. Conclude that it is important to know how to do arithmetic with decimals.</p><p>Show students the Explore task and give them some time to think about the problem before taking feedback. Ask them if they can solve it using techniques and facts that they already know. Work through Master 1, which illustrates the solution using place-value counters and the column method. Master 2 solves a similar question involving subtraction. Both of these questions do not require renaming. If the class needs more explanation before starting Guided Practice, pose further questions ensuring that they don’t require renaming. For example, if a chicken and avocado sandwich costs $3.20, what would be the total cost of this and an avocado sandwich?</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are using the column method to add and subtract decimal numbers, setting out their work in the appropriate manner. They will need to understand the importance of where to place the decimal when using the column method.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1 and 0.1</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ones</li><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>total</li><li>difference</li><li>column method</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The total cost of / difference between ___ and ___ is ___.</li><li>[decimal number] has ___ one(s), ___ tenth(s) and ___ hundredth(s).</li><li>I added these amounts of money by… I found the difference by...</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Finding the sum of 2 decimal numbers with 2 decimal places. Place-value counters are given for both decimal numbers.</li><li>Example 2: Finding the difference between 2 decimal numbers with 2 decimal places. Place-value counters are given for the first decimal number.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the sum of 2 decimal numbers with 2 decimal places, with no pictorial support.</li><li>Example 4: Finding the difference between 2 decimal numbers with 2 decimal places, with no pictorial support.</li><li>Example 5: Solving a word problem by adding and subtracting decimal numbers with 2 decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract tenths and hundredths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 2 decimal places in terms of tenths and hundredths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with decimals and calculating with whole numbers. In the column method, students right- or left-align the numbers rather than aligning the decimal points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can lay out decimal numbers accurately for the column method for addition and subtraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the column method to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract whole numbers and decimals to two decimal places (e.g., 32.55 – 21.21 = 11.34).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson shows how to add and subtract decimals with 2 decimal places, which is essential for many tasks involving money.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 9: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 9: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">31 - 34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract decimals with up to 2 decimal places. To be able to add and subtract amounts in dollars and cents.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, ask students how to add 79 + 58 using the column method. This should be revision, but the lesson today will expand on this idea by combining it with decimals. Which digit do we start with? What do we do with the ‘1’ when we write the ‘7’ from the ‘17’?</p><p>Show students the Explore task and allow them some time to solve the problem before taking feedback. Give prompts if necessary, for example, students might need a hint to realise that they should start by calculating $3.35 + $1.80. Ask them to explain their method and encourage them to use the column method even if they can do it quicker using another method. Work through Master 1, which illustrates how to find the total cost of the pencil case and coloured pens using place-value counters and the column method. Master 2 shows a different way of working out the total cost. Then work through Master 3 to find the final answer. Provide other examples if necessary to consolidate learning. For example, $2.85 + $1.65 and $5.40 – $1.85.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are using two different methods to add and subtract.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1 and 0.1</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>ones</li><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>total</li><li>difference</li><li>column method</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>The total cost of / difference between ___ and ___ is ___. [decimal number] has ___ one(s), ___ tenth(s) and ___ hundredth(s).</li><li>I added these amounts of money by… I found the difference by...</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Using 2 different methods to add 2 decimal numbers with up to 2 decimal places.</li><li>Example 2: Using 2 different methods to subtract 2 decimal numbers with up to 2 decimal places.</li><li>Example 3: Solving a word problem by adding and subtracting decimal numbers with 2 decimal places.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract tenths and hundredths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 2 decimal places in terms of tenths and hundredths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with decimals and calculating with whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can lay out decimal numbers accurately for the column method for addition and subtraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use their knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">In the column method, students right- or left-align the numbers rather than aligning the decimal points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename using decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename ones, tenths and hundredths, using place-value counters or a bar model if required.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use place-value counters to show renaming and help them use the column method.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename ones, tenths and hundredths using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use the column method to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use number bonds to balance equations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract whole numbers and decimals to two decimal places (e.g., 32.55 – 21.21 = 11.34).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be completed if time allows. It is useful but not essential at this stage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 10: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 10: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">35 - 37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to add and subtract decimals. To find number pairs that add up to 1.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, ask students to suggest pairs of numbers that add up to 10. For example, 2 + 8. This should be easy revision using number bonds. Then ask for pairs of numbers that add up to 100. Easy examples include 20 + 80 and 35 + 65. There is no need to prompt for more obscure examples.</p><p>Next, show students the Explore task and ask for possible answers. What is the question asking? If needed, clarify that there are 10 tenths of a kilogram in total and we are looking for amounts of tenths that sum to 10. Write these as decimals, for example 0.2 + 0.8, and link this to the earlier discussion on number bonds. If this is well understood, start thinking of the mass as 100 hundredths and take some suggestions from students for pairs of numbers that add up to 100 to generate answers such as 0.35 + 0.65 = 1.00 kg.</p><p>Work through Master 1–2, which shows two tables of various possible solutions; one for solutions with 1 decimal place and another with 2 decimal places with ‘5’ in the hundredths place. Show students how they can use subtraction to work out number pairs using the column method. Give them time to complete the tables. Master 3 focuses on finding number pairs with 2 decimal places, then Master 4 extends this to 3 decimal places. These can be solved using number bonds, the column method, or the ‘all from 9 and last from 10’ rule for subtracting from round numbers.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are finding number pairs that add up to 1.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1, 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>decimal place</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>___ plus ___ equals 1.</li><li>The difference between ___ and 1 is ___.</li><li>I found the sum of / difference between ___ and ___ by…</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Finding pairs of decimal numbers that add up to 1. (a–b) numbers with 1 decimal place (c–d) numbers with 2 decimal places (e–f) numbers with 3 decimal places</li><li>Example 2: Solving a word problem by finding a missing number in a pair of decimals (hundredths) that add up to 1.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add and subtract tenths and hundredths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 3 decimal places in terms of tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with decimals and calculating with whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename using decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">In the column method, students right- or left-align the numbers rather than aligning the decimal points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can lay out decimal numbers accurately in the column method for addition and subtraction.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths, hundredths and thousandths using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students struggle to link number bonds to 10 and 100 with number bonds to 1.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students know number bonds to 10.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify pairs of numbers that add up to 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not know the number bonds to 10.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify number pairs of tenths, hundredths and thousandths that add up to a total of 1.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract decimal numbers from whole numbers by renaming.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract decimals to three decimal places, with an emphasis on estimating before calculating.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson links integer arithmetic and decimal arithmetic in a new way.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 11: Adding and Subtracting Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 11: Adding and Subtracting Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">38 - 40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To apply knowledge of decimals in real-world problems, such as the perimeter of shapes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, tell students that your garden (or another place) is 30 m long and 10 m wide. What is its perimeter? This should be revision.Then show students the Explore task and allow them some time to start solving it before discussing their ideas as a class. They should be able to make some progress, using their knowledge of decimal arithmetic and perimeter. How can we find all four side lengths of the garden? Address any misconceptions, especially any regarding the use of decimals.</p><p>Work through Master 1–2, which show two ways to find the length of a short side of the garden then Master 3–4, which show two ways to find the perimeter. Master 4 is worth exploring, especially if students have made similar suggestions.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students subtracting decimals with 1 decimal place from whole numbers and finding the perimeter of rectangles. They will need to convert whole numbers to tenths and subtract.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Place-value counters - 1, 0.1, 0.01 and 0.001</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>decimal place</li><li>perimeter</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>[whole number / decimal number] equals ___ tenths.</li><li>I found the sum of / difference between ___ and ___ by…</li><li>I found the perimeter by...</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Subtracting tenths from a whole number using 2 different methods.</li><li>Example 2: Subtracting tenths from a whole number.</li><li>Example 3: Finding the perimeter of 2 rectangles.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not have a secure understanding of the base-10 number system, including decimals.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare decimals using an understanding of the place-value system.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 1 decimal place in terms of tenths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not appreciate the similarities between calculating with tenths and calculating with whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract decimals from whole numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use knowledge of place value to add and subtract decimals.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not realise that to find the perimeter of a rectangle, they need to add each length twice.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can add decimals, including renaming.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can rename tenths using the column method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify pairs of numbers that add up to 10.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify number pairs of tenths that add up to a total of 1.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can subtract decimal numbers from whole numbers by renaming.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Add and subtract decimals to one decimal place (e.g., 1.3 + 0.2 = 1.5).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>This lesson includes important practise involving decimal arithmetic.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 12: Rounding Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 12: Rounding Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">41 - 44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to round decimals to the nearest whole number. To be able to round numbers to the nearest tenth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>To begin this lesson, show students the Explore task and ask why we might prefer to say ‘about 5 kg’ rather than giving the precise values. You could give some examples of giving inappropriate levels of precision to illustrate the intention of rounding numbers. For example, many adults are approximately 2 m tall and many students in Year 5 are approximately 1 m tall. You could agree to meet a friend at 11:33:26, but it would be more straightforward to just say ‘half past 11’. Then ask the class to look at parcel C and ask them why this is approximately 5 kg rather than 4 kg.</p><p>Work through Master 1–2 with the class to show how the 3 masses all round to 5 kg and address any misconceptions. Point out that the hundredths digits did not need to be considered. Use Master 3 to show how to round to the nearest tenth. If the class understands this well, tell them that you can also round to the nearest hundredth. Set some further examples if needed, then ask them to compare the estimates from Sam and Ravi and discuss their answers to the questions posed by Charles.</p><p>During Guided Practice, students are rounding numbers to the nearest whole number and to the nearest tenth.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Number lines</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>tenths</li><li>hundredths</li><li>thousandths</li><li>decimal</li><li>decimal point</li><li>decimal place</li><li>estimate</li><li>round/rounded/rounding</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Sentence Starters</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>[decimal number] is between ___ and ___.</li><li>It is closer to ___ so we round it up / down to ___.</li><li>[decimal number] is approximately equal to ___ when rounded to the nearest whole number.</li><li>[decimal number] is approximately equal to ___ when rounded to the nearest tenth.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Example 1: Rounding decimal numbers, with up to 2 decimal places, to the nearest whole number.</li><li>Example 2: Rounding decimal numbers, with up to 2 decimal places, to the nearest tenth.</li><li>Example 3: Rounding prices of items with 2 decimal places to the nearest whole number and nearest tenth.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students struggle to identify the key digits in rounding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify the digit to be rounded to and the digit to be rounded.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can refer to decimals with 3 decimal places in terms of tenths, hundredths and thousandths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students do not realise why some digits are irrelevant when rounding.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify whether a digit should be rounded up or down.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can place decimal numbers on a number line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students always round down, even when the next digit is greater than 4.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can use a number line to round decimal numbers.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round decimals to the nearest whole number.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can round decimals to the nearest tenth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Round whole numbers to a specified power of 10, and round tenths and hundredths to the nearest whole number or one decimal place.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>This is a key lesson.</li><li>Rounding decimal numbers is a key skill in mathematics and science.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 13: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Textbook Page(s)</th><td class=\"lt-td\">45 - 46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">To be able to apply knowledge of decimals to solve problems.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students solve a logic puzzle, which uses the language learnt in the chapter, to determine what decimal number Elliott is thinking of.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students solve a logic puzzle, which uses the language learnt in the chapter, to determine what decimal number Elliott is thinking of.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write about when it is better to round to the nearest whole number or nearest tenth, giving examples of both situations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Complete this with students as a chapter summary and determine if any students need extra support.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 8: Percentages":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Introducing percentage as a proportion out of 100, and building fluency converting between fractions, decimals and percentages.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Writing Percentages":{"label":"Lesson 1: Writing Percentages","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a percentage expresses a quantity as a proportion out of 100, and will be able to move flexibly between fraction, decimal and percentage representations of the same value.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage (5 min): Students revisit a 100-square grid task with two fruit types, recalling how to count parts to find a numerator over a known denominator of 100.</p><p>Teach (15 min): Teacher formalises the term \"percentage\" as a fraction out of 100, modelling the fraction-decimal-percentage relationship and discussing benchmark percentages.</p><p>Guided Practice (15 min): Students work in pairs with grids and counters, identifying percentages of filled squares and converting these to decimals.</p><p>Independent Practice (10 min): Differentiated tasks identifying percentages of shapes and regions in grids of different sizes, and converting percentages to decimals.</p><p>Plenary (5 min): Pairs share a sentence explaining a conversion, using target vocabulary.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>100-square grids (one per pair)</li><li>Counters (approx. 99 per pair)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>per cent</li><li>percentage</li><li>fraction</li><li>hundredth</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Variation</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Extension pairs move to larger or irregular grid comparisons; pairs needing support remain on simpler shape-identification tasks with scaffolding.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may equate a raw count with the percentage itself, for example treating 40 out of 50 as 40 percent rather than 80 percent.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a percentage expresses a quantity as a proportion out of 100, and will be able to move flexibly between fraction, decimal and percentage representations of the same value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Formative, observation-based: teacher checks students can identify numerator and denominator from a grid, convert a hundredths fraction to a decimal and percentage, and articulate percentage as a measure of proportion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Achievement Objective (Number, Level 4): Understand addition and subtraction of fractions, decimals and percentages; apply simple linear proportions.</li><li>Related strand link: representing fractions, decimals (two decimal places) and percentages as different ways of expressing the same proportion.</li><li>Key Competency focus: Using language, symbols and texts; Thinking.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can state what fraction of a 100-square grid is represented by a given quantity.</li><li>I can convert a hundredths fraction into both a decimal and a percentage.</li><li>I can explain, using correct vocabulary, how I converted between forms.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Equivalent Fractions, Percentages and Decimals":{"label":"Lesson 2: Equivalent Fractions, Percentages and Decimals","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that fractions with denominators that are factors of 100 can be converted into equivalent hundredths, decimals and percentages.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students explore a grid that is one-quarter shaded, counting squares to find the equivalent hundredths fraction.</p><p>Teach: Teacher formalises the equivalence between one-quarter, 25 hundredths and 25 percent, then extends to three-quarters and 75 percent, before working through further denominators (2, 5, 10, 20, 25, 50) with worked examples.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert given fractions to percentages and decimals in pairs.</p><p>Independent Practice: Differentiated grid-based tasks identifying shaded proportion as a fraction, percentage and decimal; extension tasks work backwards from a percentage to find a missing numerator.</p><p>Plenary: Students share a conversion strategy with the class.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>100-square grids (one per pair)</li><li>Counters (approx. 99 per pair)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>per cent</li><li>percentage</li><li>fraction</li><li>decimal</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Confusing a raw count with the percentage itself.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that fractions with denominators that are factors of 100 can be converted into equivalent hundredths, decimals and percentages.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify numerator and denominator, simplify a fraction, find equivalent fractions with a denominator of 10 or 100, convert to a percentage, and articulate percentage as a measure of proportion.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Ignoring the denominator when comparing fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Achievement Objective (Number, Level 4): Identify, read, write and represent fractions, decimals (to two places) and related percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - students relate a wider variety of fractions to their equivalent percentages.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can show that a simple fraction, such as one quarter, is equivalent to a hundredths fraction and a percentage.</li><li>I can convert fractions with denominators of 2, 4, 5, 10, 20, 25, 50 or 100 into percentages and decimals.</li><li>I can explain my conversion method using correct vocabulary.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Comparing Proportions Using Percentages":{"label":"Lesson 3: Comparing Proportions Using Percentages","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that converting fractions to percentages allows more precise comparison of proportions than comparing fractions alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: A real-world comparison scenario (three groups with different proportions of an attribute) prompts discussion of which group has the greatest share, revealing that fraction estimates alone (more than half, more than a quarter) are not precise enough.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models converting each proportion to a percentage to compare precisely, then extends practice with further fraction-to-percentage and fraction-to-decimal conversions.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert fractions with denominators that are factors of 100 into percentages.</p><p>Independent Practice: Word problems requiring students to identify the highest equivalent percentage among options, and to convert fractions to percentages in context.</p><p>Plenary: Class discusses strategies used to compare proportions.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>100-square grids (one per pair)</li><li>Counters (approx. 99 per pair)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>per cent</li><li>percentage</li><li>fraction</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Confusing a raw count with the percentage itself.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that converting fractions to percentages allows more precise comparison of proportions than comparing fractions alone.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can write numerator and denominator correctly, simplify fractions, find equivalent fractions to a denominator of 10 or 100, convert to percentages, and understand percentage as a measure of proportion.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Ignoring the denominator when comparing fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Compare and order fractions, decimals (to two places) and percentages, and convert decimals and percentages to fractions.</li><li>Find a fraction or percentage of a whole number where the answer is a whole number (e.g. ⅜ of 48; 30 percent of $150).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert a fraction, with a denominator that is a factor of 100, into a percentage.</li><li>I can use percentages to compare and order proportions.</li><li>I can solve word problems that require comparing proportions.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 4: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of percentages developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>A comparison task requiring students to apply percentage reasoning to a novel context.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can compare proportions using percentages.</li><li>I can convert between fractions and percentages fluently to solve a problem.</li><li>I can explain my reasoning in writing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A comparison task requiring students to apply percentage reasoning to a novel context.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students independently solve a problem requiring conversion between fractions and percentages, then write an explanation of their method.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 9.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 9: Geometry":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>This is the final Year 6 geometry chapter. Students measure and classify angles, investigate angles on a line and at a point, draw angles accurately, and describe turns and direction using compass points, culminating in solving angle problems.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Types of Angles":{"label":"Lesson 1: Types of Angles","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles can be classified as acute, right, obtuse or reflex by comparing them to benchmark angles such as a right angle and a straight line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare a set of angles by eye and discuss which look larger, smaller or similar.</p><p>Teach: Teacher defines obtuse, reflex, right and acute angles by comparing each to a right angle and a straight line, then discusses why right angles are significant in everyday life.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students identify angle types on diagrams and draw shapes containing specified angle types.</p><p>Independent Practice: Differentiated tasks classifying angles on shapes and constructing shapes to given angle specifications.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain their angle classification reasoning using sentence starters such as \"I know this is an obtuse angle because…\"</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>acute angle</li><li>right angle</li><li>obtuse angle</li><li>reflex angle</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may only recognise a right angle when its lines are horizontal and vertical.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles can be classified as acute, right, obtuse or reflex by comparing them to benchmark angles such as a right angle and a straight line.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify and label acute, right, obtuse and reflex angles, distinguish between the four categories, and draw a shape that includes specified angle types.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to draw angles accurately without support.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may fail to recognise an angle type if it looks visually different from an example already seen.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe angles using the terms acute, right, obtuse, straight and reflex, comparing them with benchmarks of 90, 180 and 360 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - pupils must become familiar with the four angle categories.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name an angle as acute, right, obtuse or reflex.</li><li>I can explain why an angle fits a category by comparing it with a right angle or straight line.</li><li>I can draw a shape that includes a specified type of angle.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Measuring Angles":{"label":"Lesson 2: Measuring Angles","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles can be measured precisely in degrees using a protractor, rather than only judged by eye.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare three similar-looking acute angles in a scalene triangle and discuss how they might order or rank them precisely.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces the degree as the unit for measuring angles and demonstrates how to align and read a protractor, highlighting that a right angle measures 90 degrees.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students use a protractor to measure a range of angles.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students measure angles within triangles and angles meeting at a point, recording each measurement in degrees.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss how many degrees are in a straight line and the maximum size of a reflex angle.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>acute angle</li><li>right angle</li><li>obtuse angle</li><li>reflex angle</li><li>degrees</li><li>protractor</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may only recognise a right angle when its lines are horizontal and vertical.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles can be measured precisely in degrees using a protractor, rather than only judged by eye.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify the four angle types, line up and read a protractor accurately, and record angle measurements using the degree symbol.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may misread a protractor by using the wrong one of its two scales.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to line up a protractor accurately on the vertex of an angle.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe angles using acute, right, obtuse, straight and reflex, comparing them with benchmarks of 90, 180 and 360 degrees.</li><li>Visualise, measure and draw (to the nearest degree) the amount of turn in angles up to 360 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - students must be confident with the four angle categories before measuring them precisely.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that a right angle measures 90 degrees.</li><li>I can use a protractor to measure an angle accurately.</li><li>I can state the maximum possible size of a reflex angle.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Measuring Angles at a Point":{"label":"Lesson 3: Measuring Angles at a Point","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles meeting at a point on a straight line always add up to 180 degrees, and that a protractor's two scales can be used to measure two angles at once.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students examine a protractor and notice that its two scales always sum to 180 at any given mark.</p><p>Teach: Teacher shows two angles meeting at a point on a straight line and demonstrates measuring one on each scale, establishing that angles on a straight line total 180 degrees, and links this to a right angle being half of a straight line.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students use a protractor to measure a range of angles at a point.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students measure and calculate missing angles at a point on a straight line.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they used a protractor's two scales to measure adjacent angles.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>acute angle</li><li>right angle</li><li>obtuse angle</li><li>reflex angle</li><li>vertex</li><li>protractor</li><li>degrees</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse the two scales on a protractor.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that angles meeting at a point on a straight line always add up to 180 degrees, and that a protractor's two scales can be used to measure two angles at once.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify the four angle types, use the degree symbol correctly, line up a protractor accurately, and avoid confusing the two scales when measuring adjacent angles.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to line up a protractor accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may only recognise a right angle when its lines are horizontal and vertical.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, measure and draw (to the nearest degree) the amount of turn in angles up to 360 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - builds on protractor skills to establish that angles on a straight line add to 180 degrees.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name an angle, state its measurement in degrees, and explain how I measured it.</li><li>I can use both scales on a protractor to measure two angles meeting at a point without moving the protractor.</li><li>I can explain why angles on a straight line add to 180 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Turns and Compass Points":{"label":"Lesson 4: Turns and Compass Points","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that turns can be described both using compass points and using angles measured in degrees.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider a scenario of a person facing a particular direction and discuss how compass points could describe the direction faced.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces north, south, east and west, then models how a turn between two directions can be described using an angle, before extending to quarter, half, three-quarter and full turns and introducing additional compass points.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students describe turns and resulting directions using compass language and degrees.</p><p>Independent Practice: Differentiated tasks finding an end direction after a turn, with varying levels of compass point support provided.</p><p>Plenary: Students share strategies for working out an end direction after a turn.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>compass</li><li>compass points (north through to south)</li><li>full turn</li><li>half turn</li><li>quarter turn</li><li>three-quarter turn</li><li>clockwise</li><li>anticlockwise</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe the compass points move together with the object that is turning.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that turns can be described both using compass points and using angles measured in degrees.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify compass points, describe turns using quarter/half/three-quarter/full and relate these to degrees, locate a starting point using compass references, and accurately carry out a turn of a given number of degrees.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may assume every turn starts from north.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Interpret and create grid maps to plot positions and pathways, using grid references and directional language, including the four main compass points.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - describes turns as angles and introduces vocabulary for describing turns.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can name the compass points and use them to describe direction.</li><li>I can describe a turn as a quarter, half, three-quarter or full turn and relate this to degrees.</li><li>I can state the direction someone is facing after completing a described turn.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Drawing Lines and Acute Angles":{"label":"Lesson 5: Drawing Lines and Acute Angles","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand how to use a ruler and protractor together to draw lines and acute angles accurately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss the difference between acute and obtuse angles and the range of degree values for each, then consider how to draw a line of a given length accurately using a ruler.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models constructing an acute angle at a given vertex, emphasising that a protractor shows the same value on both scales and students must select the side that produces an acute angle rather than the obtuse alternative.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students draw angles and lines from diagrams and written descriptions.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students construct diagrams that combine an accurately drawn acute angle with two measured lines.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they ensured their constructed angle was accurate.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>acute angle</li><li>right angle</li><li>obtuse angle</li><li>reflex angle</li><li>vertex</li><li>protractor</li><li>degrees</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to line up a protractor accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand how to use a ruler and protractor together to draw lines and acute angles accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can line up and read a protractor accurately, use it to both measure and draw angles, and use a ruler to measure and draw lines accurately.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse the two scales on a protractor and draw an obtuse angle instead of the intended acute angle.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not use a ruler when drawing straight lines.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe angles using acute, right, obtuse, straight and reflex, comparing them with benchmarks of 90, 180 and 360 degrees.</li><li>Visualise, measure and draw (to the nearest degree) the amount of turn in angles up to 360 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 6, as both cover accurate construction of angles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use a ruler to draw a line of a given length accurately.</li><li>I can use a protractor to draw an acute angle of a given size.</li><li>I can choose the correct scale on a protractor to ensure I draw an acute rather than obtuse angle.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Drawing Lines and Obtuse Angles":{"label":"Lesson 6: Drawing Lines and Obtuse Angles","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">COMBINED</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand how to use a ruler and protractor together to draw lines and obtuse angles accurately, and consider how a reflex angle could be constructed indirectly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider a diagram and decide whether the angle involved is acute or obtuse before attempting to construct it, revising the previous lesson's approach.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models the steps for constructing an obtuse angle, discusses whether alternative construction methods are equally valid, and explores how a reflex angle can be drawn indirectly since a protractor only measures up to 180 degrees.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students draw angles and lines from diagrams and written descriptions.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students measure and redraw a given obtuse angle diagram, and construct a labelled diagram from written specifications.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss how they would approach constructing a reflex angle using their protractor.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Sheet of paper (each)</li><li>Protractors (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>acute angle</li><li>right angle</li><li>obtuse angle</li><li>reflex angle</li><li>vertex</li><li>protractor</li><li>degrees</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to line up a protractor accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand how to use a ruler and protractor together to draw lines and obtuse angles accurately, and consider how a reflex angle could be constructed indirectly.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can line up and read a protractor accurately, use it to both measure and draw angles, and use a ruler to measure and draw lines accurately.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse the two scales on a protractor.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not use a ruler when drawing straight lines.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe angles using acute, right, obtuse, straight and reflex, comparing them with benchmarks of 90, 180 and 360 degrees.</li><li>Visualise, measure and draw (to the nearest degree) the amount of turn in angles up to 360 degrees.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">This lesson can be combined with Lesson 5, as both cover accurate construction of angles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use a protractor to draw an obtuse angle of a given size.</li><li>I can use a ruler to draw a line of a given length accurately.</li><li>I can explain how a reflex angle could be constructed using the angle on the other side of a line.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 7: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of angles, angle measurement and turns developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students apply their knowledge of geometry to identify equal sides of a shape and justify their answer.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify and measure acute, right, obtuse and reflex angles.</li><li>I can apply my knowledge of angles to solve a geometric reasoning problem.</li><li>I can explain the properties of a shape using angle vocabulary.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students apply their knowledge of geometry to identify equal sides of a shape and justify their answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write about the properties of a square when one diagonal is drawn, applying angle vocabulary.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 10.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 10: Position & Movement":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students name and plot coordinates, then describe and carry out translations, reflections, successive reflections, rotations, and enlargements/reductions of shapes on a coordinate grid.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Naming and Plotting Points":{"label":"Lesson 1: Naming and Plotting Points","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a point on a grid can be named and plotted using an x-coordinate and a y-coordinate, written and read in that order.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students find the coordinates of three plotted points, recapping that a coordinate is a horizontal measurement followed by a vertical one.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models writing and plotting coordinates, including a point with a half-value, then plots a fourth point to complete a quadrilateral and discusses what makes it a trapezium.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students write the coordinates of a quadrilateral and plot points to draw one of their own.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students suggest coordinates for a missing point so a quadrilateral matches a given description, and identify whether a plotted quadrilateral is regular or irregular.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss alternative coordinates that would keep a quadrilateral the same type of shape.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>x-axis</li><li>y-axis</li><li>coordinates</li><li>x-coordinate</li><li>y-coordinate</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may write coordinates the wrong way around.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a point on a grid can be named and plotted using an x-coordinate and a y-coordinate, written and read in that order.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can plot and read coordinates on a grid, visualise an incomplete shape, and name basic quadrilaterals such as a parallelogram or trapezium.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may read from the wrong axis when plotting points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may lack confidence placing mixed numbers on an axis.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use grid references to identify regions and plot positions on a grid map.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - essential that students are confident with coordinates in two dimensions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can write the coordinates of a point on a grid.</li><li>I can plot a point accurately from given coordinates, including coordinates with halves.</li><li>I can use plotted points to identify and complete a quadrilateral.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Describing Translations":{"label":"Lesson 2: Describing Translations","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a translation moves a shape a given distance horizontally and vertically without rotating, stretching or otherwise changing it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Teacher demonstrates sliding an object on a desk to show what is and isn't a translation, then students describe how one point has moved to a new position.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models describing a translation in terms of horizontal and vertical movement, emphasising the shape itself does not change, and contrasts this with enlargement and reflection.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students describe a translation and write the coordinates of a trapezium after two successive translations.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students find missing coordinates of vertices following a translation, given only one translated point.</p><p>Plenary: Students share how they worked out the horizontal and vertical movement of a translation.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>x-axis</li><li>y-axis</li><li>coordinates</li><li>x-coordinate</li><li>y-coordinate</li><li>translation/translate</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may write coordinates the wrong way around.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a translation moves a shape a given distance horizontally and vertically without rotating, stretching or otherwise changing it.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can explain what a translation is, carry one out accurately, and record the resulting coordinates correctly, including halves.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may read from the wrong axis when plotting points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may lack confidence placing mixed numbers on an axis.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Interpret and describe pathways, including those involving half and quarter turns and the distance travelled.</li><li>Visualise, predict and identify which shape is a reflection, rotation or translation of a given 2D shape.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - translation is a basic geometric operation and gives valuable coordinate practice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe a translation as a movement of a number of units horizontally and vertically.</li><li>I can write the coordinates of a shape's vertices after it has been translated.</li><li>I can explain why a translation does not rotate or resize a shape.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Describing Reflections":{"label":"Lesson 3: Describing Reflections","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a reflection creates a mirror image of a shape, where every point stays the same distance from the mirror line as its original position.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare an original shape with its reflected image and discuss which points are closest to and furthest from the mirror line.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models checking a reflection using a small mirror placed along the mirror line, and discusses examples where an image looks like a reflection but is not reflected in the given line.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students identify images that are true reflections, draw reflected images, and find a mirror line for a given reflection.</p><p>Independent Practice: Paired work using tracing paper - students draw a shape, fold a line of reflection, and trace its reflected image.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain the difference between a reflection and a translation.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>axis</li><li>y-axis</li><li>coordinates</li><li>x-coordinate</li><li>y-coordinate</li><li>reflection/reflect</li><li>mirror line</li><li>image</li><li>horizontal</li><li>vertical</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may read from the wrong axis when plotting points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a reflection creates a mirror image of a shape, where every point stays the same distance from the mirror line as its original position.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify a mirror line, use the term reflection correctly, draw a reflection given a shape and mirror line, and reflect a point by counting its distance from the mirror line.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse translations and reflections.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may disregard the distance of a point from the mirror line.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to visualise reflections in different directions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, predict and identify which shape is a reflection, rotation or translation of a given 2D shape.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - reflection is a basic geometric operation and gives valuable coordinate practice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify whether an image is a true reflection of a shape in a given mirror line.</li><li>I can draw the reflection of a shape in a vertical or horizontal mirror line.</li><li>I can find the mirror line for a given reflection.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Describing Reflections (Coordinate Grids)":{"label":"Lesson 4: Describing Reflections (Coordinate Grids)","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that reflecting a shape on a coordinate grid keeps every vertex the same distance from the mirror line, and that the shape's properties do not change.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students predict whether a reflected trapezium will still be a trapezium and discuss which way its acute angle will point.</p><p>Teach: Teacher demonstrates finding a reflected shape by counting squares to the mirror line, emphasising that each vertex stays the same distance from the line and that the shape's properties are unchanged.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students plot coordinates to form a polygon, then reflect it in a vertical mirror line.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students reflect the same polygon in a horizontal mirror line and record the coordinates of both images.</p><p>Plenary: Students compare their two reflected images and discuss what stayed the same and what changed.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>x-axis</li><li>y-axis</li><li>coordinates</li><li>x-coordinate</li><li>y-coordinate</li><li>reflection/reflect</li><li>mirror line</li><li>image</li><li>horizontal</li><li>vertical</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may read from the wrong axis when plotting points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that reflecting a shape on a coordinate grid keeps every vertex the same distance from the mirror line, and that the shape's properties do not change.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can carry out a reflection using counting, measuring or tracing methods, and write the coordinates of the reflected vertices accurately.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse translations and reflections.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may disregard the distance of a point from the mirror line.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to visualise reflections in different directions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use grid references to identify regions and plot positions on a grid map.</li><li>Visualise, predict and identify which shape is a reflection, rotation or translation of a given 2D shape.</li><li>Interpret and create grid maps to plot positions and pathways, using grid references and directional language.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - reflection is a basic geometric operation and gives valuable coordinate practice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can reflect a polygon in a vertical mirror line and write the coordinates of the image.</li><li>I can reflect a polygon in a horizontal mirror line and write the coordinates of the image.</li><li>I can explain that a shape keeps the same properties after a reflection.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Describing Successive Reflections":{"label":"Lesson 5: Describing Successive Reflections","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a shape can be moved to a new position using two successive reflections in different mirror lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students describe how a shape appears to move between two positions, considering reflection, translation and rotation as possibilities.</p><p>Teach: Teacher demonstrates that the movement can be achieved using two successive reflections, then poses whether the order of the two reflections affects the outcome.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students draw the new positions of a letter reflected in two different mirror lines and plot the resulting coordinates.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students describe a sequence of two reflections for a triangle and find the coordinates of a point after each stage.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss whether reversing the order of two reflections changes the final position.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>x-axis</li><li>y-axis</li><li>coordinates</li><li>x-coordinate</li><li>y-coordinate</li><li>reflection/reflect</li><li>mirror line</li><li>image</li><li>horizontal</li><li>vertical</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may read from the wrong axis when plotting points.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a shape can be moved to a new position using two successive reflections in different mirror lines.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can carry out successive reflections accurately, use the term reflection correctly, and write the coordinates of a shape at each stage.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse translations and reflections.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to visualise a sequence of reflections when intermediate images are not shown.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, predict and identify which shape is a reflection, rotation or translation of a given 2D shape.</li><li>Visualise, create and describe 2D geometric patterns and tessellations, using rotation, reflection and translation, and identify properties of shapes that do not change.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - concludes this year's work on geometric transformations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can draw the position of a shape after it is reflected in two different mirror lines.</li><li>I can describe how a shape was moved using two reflections.</li><li>I can investigate whether the order of the two reflections changes the final position.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Describing Rotations":{"label":"Lesson 6: Describing Rotations","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a rotation turns a shape a given angle, in a given direction, around a fixed centre of rotation, without changing its size or shape.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students describe the movement needed for one triangle to cover another while a shared point stays fixed, identifying this as a turn around that point.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces the term rotation and centre of rotation, and models describing rotations using angles and their relationship to full and part turns.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students describe rotations using angles and directions.</p><p>Independent Practice: Differentiated tasks finding a missing angle or direction of rotation, with or without the direction of turn shown.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss the three pieces of information needed to describe a rotation accurately: centre, direction and angle.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rotation</li><li>angles</li><li>full turn</li><li>half turn</li><li>three-quarter turn</li><li>perpendicular</li><li>clockwise</li><li>anticlockwise</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think the shape slides rather than rotates.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a rotation turns a shape a given angle, in a given direction, around a fixed centre of rotation, without changing its size or shape.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can rotate a shape around a point accurately, describe the angle and direction of a rotation, and relate rotations to full or part turns.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not recognise or state the direction of rotation.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to relate a given angle to a full or part turn.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, predict and identify which shape is a reflection, rotation or translation of a given 2D shape.</li><li>Interpret and describe pathways, including those involving half and quarter turns and the distance travelled.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify the centre of rotation for a turned shape.</li><li>I can describe a rotation using an angle and a direction (clockwise or anticlockwise).</li><li>I can relate quarter, half, three-quarter and full turns to their equivalent angle in degrees.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Describing Enlargement and Reduction":{"label":"Lesson 7: Describing Enlargement and Reduction","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that an enlargement or reduction changes the size of a shape by multiplying or dividing every side length by the same scale factor.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare an original shape with an enlarged version and use grid measurements to describe how the side lengths have changed.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models describing the change using multiplication language (for example, twice the size, multiplied by two) and reduction as the inverse, dividing each side by the same factor.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students describe enlargements and reductions using given measurements.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students describe enlargements and reductions of rectangles and polygons using a given scale.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they identified the scale factor used in an enlargement or reduction.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Coordinate grid paper (each)</li><li>Squared paper (each)</li><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Small mirrors (shared)</li><li>Tracing paper (each)</li><li>Mirrors</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>enlargement</li><li>reduction</li><li>enlarge</li><li>reduce</li><li>original</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not enlarge or reduce all sides of a shape by the same factor.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that an enlargement or reduction changes the size of a shape by multiplying or dividing every side length by the same scale factor.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students understand that enlargement and reduction change a shape's size using multiplication or division, and can identify whether a given shape has been enlarged or reduced.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to relate the scale of a grid to the shapes drawn on it.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Resize (enlarge or reduce) a 2D shape.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify whether a shape has been enlarged or reduced.</li><li>I can describe an enlargement or reduction using multiplication or division language.</li><li>I can relate an enlargement and its corresponding reduction as inverse operations.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of coordinates, translations, reflections, rotations and enlargement/reduction developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students construct a polygon from four given shapes on a coordinate grid using only reflections, then construct the same polygon using only translations.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can construct the same polygon using different types of transformation.</li><li>I can describe how a reflection changes as the mirror line moves.</li><li>I can explain my reasoning about position and movement in writing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students construct a polygon from four given shapes on a coordinate grid using only reflections, then construct the same polygon using only translations.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students describe what happens to the reflection of a triangle on a square grid as the mirror line moves nearer to or further from the triangle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 11.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 11: Measurements":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students convert between metric units of length, mass and volume, then apply this knowledge to solve word problems, before extending to word problems involving time.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Converting Units of Length — Centimetres and Millimetres":{"label":"Lesson 1: Converting Units of Length — Centimetres and Millimetres","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that centimetres and millimetres can be converted between each other by multiplying or dividing by 10, and will select the most appropriate unit for measuring a given object.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students estimate the height of a small seedling using their hands, then discuss that 4 cm and 40 mm are both correct descriptions of the same length.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces millimetres as a unit suited to measuring small objects, and establishes that converting between centimetres and millimetres involves multiplying or dividing by 10.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students discuss whether classroom objects are best measured in centimetres or millimetres, depending on the purpose of the measurement.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students use a ruler to measure lines in both centimetres and millimetres, and arrange a set of lengths given in mixed units in order.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they converted a length between centimetres and millimetres.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>centimetre</li><li>millimetre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not see the connection between millimetres and centimetres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that centimetres and millimetres can be converted between each other by multiplying or dividing by 10, and will select the most appropriate unit for measuring a given object.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can line up and read a ruler accurately, understand that 10 mm equals 1 cm, and convert confidently between the two units.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to use a ruler to measure accurately.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply the number of millimetres by 10 because centimetres are the larger unit, rather than dividing.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces converting between centimetres and millimetres.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can read a ruler accurately in millimetres and centimetres.</li><li>I can convert a whole number of centimetres to millimetres, and a multiple of 10 millimetres to centimetres.</li><li>I can decide whether millimetres or centimetres is the more appropriate unit for measuring an object.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Converting Units of Length — Metres and Centimetres":{"label":"Lesson 2: Converting Units of Length — Metres and Centimetres","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that 1 metre is equivalent to 100 centimetres, and that the same length can be expressed in different but equivalent ways.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students share their own height in different formats and discuss why this can make lengths difficult to compare.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models converting lengths between metres and centimetres, establishing that 1 m equals 100 cm, and measures classroom objects using a metre ruler.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert given lengths between metres and centimetres.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students arrange a set of heights given in metres and centimetres in order, from shortest to tallest and vice versa.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain their method for converting a length with decimals between metres and centimetres.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>centimetre</li><li>metre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not see the connection between centimetres and metres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that 1 metre is equivalent to 100 centimetres, and that the same length can be expressed in different but equivalent ways.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can write measurements as decimals, know that 1 m equals 100 cm, and convert accurately between the two units, including partitioning large numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may incorrectly assume 10 cm equals 1 m, by analogy with millimetres and centimetres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply the number of centimetres by 100 because metres are the larger unit, rather than dividing.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces converting between centimetres and metres.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert a whole number of metres to centimetres, and a multiple of 100 centimetres to metres.</li><li>I can convert centimetres to metres using decimals.</li><li>I can compare and order lengths given in metres and centimetres.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Converting Units of Length — Kilometres and Metres":{"label":"Lesson 3: Converting Units of Length — Kilometres and Metres","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that 1 kilometre is equivalent to 1000 metres, and will use this to convert and compare distances.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare a set of jogging distances given in different formats and discuss which represents the shortest distance.</p><p>Teach: Teacher establishes that 1 km equals 1000 m and models converting distances between the two units, including decimal conversions.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert given distances between kilometres and metres.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students arrange a set of distances given in kilometres and metres in order, from shortest to longest and vice versa.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain their method for converting a distance with decimals between kilometres and metres.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>kilometre</li><li>metre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not see the connection between kilometres and metres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that 1 kilometre is equivalent to 1000 metres, and will use this to convert and compare distances.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can write measurements as decimals, know that 1 km equals 1000 m, and convert accurately between the two units, including partitioning large numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may incorrectly assume 100 m equals 1 km, by analogy with centimetres and metres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply the number of metres by 1000 because kilometres are the larger unit, rather than dividing.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces converting between kilometres and metres.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert a whole number of kilometres to metres, and a multiple of 1000 metres to kilometres.</li><li>I can convert metres to kilometres using decimals.</li><li>I can compare and order distances given in kilometres and metres.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Converting Units of Mass — Kilograms and Grams":{"label":"Lesson 4: Converting Units of Mass — Kilograms and Grams","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that mass is measured in kilograms for larger objects and grams for smaller objects, and that 1 kilogram is equivalent to 1000 grams.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss which units are appropriate for measuring the mass of a person compared with the mass of an insect.</p><p>Teach: Teacher establishes that 1 kg equals 1000 g, using the fact that 100 g equals 0.1 kg, and models converting a decimal amount of kilograms into grams and vice versa.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert given masses between kilograms and grams.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students arrange a set of masses given in kilograms and grams in order, from lightest to heaviest and vice versa.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain the difference between the everyday term weight and the more precise scientific term mass.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>mass</li><li>gram</li><li>kilogram</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not see the connection between grams and kilograms.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that mass is measured in kilograms for larger objects and grams for smaller objects, and that 1 kilogram is equivalent to 1000 grams.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students know that 1 kg equals 1000 g and that 100 g equals 0.1 kg, and can convert confidently between the two units including with decimals and fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may lack confidence using decimal numbers in the context of measures.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply the number of grams by 1000 because kilograms are the larger unit, rather than dividing.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces converting between kilograms and grams.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that 1 kg is the same as 1000 g, and that 100 g is the same as 0.1 kg.</li><li>I can convert between kilograms and grams, including with decimals.</li><li>I can compare and order masses given in kilograms and grams.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Converting Units of Volume — Litres and Millilitres":{"label":"Lesson 5: Converting Units of Volume — Litres and Millilitres","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume is measured in litres for large containers and millilitres for small containers, and that 1 litre is equivalent to 1000 millilitres.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss which units are appropriate for measuring the volume of liquid in containers of different sizes, such as a bathtub compared with a teaspoon.</p><p>Teach: Teacher establishes that 1 l equals 1000 ml and models converting between the two units, showing real containers of known volume to build a sense of scale.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert given volumes between litres and millilitres.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students compare different volumes of water to identify which container holds the greatest volume.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain their method for converting a volume with decimals between litres and millilitres.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>litre</li><li>millilitre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not see the connection between litres and millilitres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume is measured in litres for large containers and millilitres for small containers, and that 1 litre is equivalent to 1000 millilitres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students know that 1 l equals 1000 ml and that 100 ml equals 0.1 l, and can convert accurately between the two units including with decimals and fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may lack confidence using decimal numbers in the context of measures.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply the number of millilitres by 1000 because litres are the larger unit, rather than dividing.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces converting between litres and millilitres.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that 1 l is the same as 1000 ml, and that 100 ml is the same as 0.1 l.</li><li>I can convert between litres and millilitres, including with decimals.</li><li>I can compare the volume of liquid in different containers.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Solving Word Problems — Length, Mass and Volume":{"label":"Lesson 6: Solving Word Problems — Length, Mass and Volume","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their unit conversion knowledge to solve word problems involving length, mass and volume, including some simple conversions between metric and imperial units.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider why a delivery company might charge a special parcel rate and discuss what calculation this would require.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models solving problems that require adding measurements after converting units, then introduces a bar model to represent a comparison problem involving mass.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students solve word problems involving length, mass and volume that require unit conversion.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students solve differentiated word problems, including some requiring simple conversions between metric and imperial units.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss how they decided which unit conversion and operation to use for a given problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>kilometre</li><li>metre</li><li>centimetre</li><li>litre</li><li>millilitre</li><li>kilogram</li><li>gram</li><li>inch</li><li>foot/feet</li><li>pounds</li><li>pint</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to remember all the different measurement units and how they are connected.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their unit conversion knowledge to solve word problems involving length, mass and volume, including some simple conversions between metric and imperial units.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can convert confidently between metric units, make simple metric-imperial conversions, and connect a word problem to the appropriate mathematical method.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may lack confidence using decimal numbers in the context of measures.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may become confused about whether to multiply or divide when converting units.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Convert between common metric units for length, mass (weight) and capacity, and use decimals to express parts of wholes in measurements.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - concludes the chapter's work on length, mass and volume.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert units as needed to solve a word problem involving length, mass or volume.</li><li>I can use a bar model to represent and solve a word problem involving measurements.</li><li>I can make simple conversions between metric and imperial units where required.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 7: Solving Word Problems — Time":{"label":"Lesson 7: Solving Word Problems — Time","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their knowledge of units of time to solve word problems involving seconds, minutes, hours and days.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Teacher tests students' recall of time facts, such as the number of minutes in an hour and seconds in seven minutes.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models solving a word problem by converting everything to a common unit such as minutes, and discusses alternative solution methods and representations such as expressing 2 hours 15 minutes as 2 and a quarter hours.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students solve word problems requiring conversions between seconds, minutes, hours and days.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students solve differentiated word problems on time using the four operations.</p><p>Plenary: Students share different methods used to solve the same time-based word problem.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Rulers (each)</li><li>Metre ruler (each)</li><li>Containers of various sizes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>day</li><li>hour</li><li>minutes</li><li>second</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may use an incorrect conversion, such as believing there are 100 minutes in an hour or 12 hours in a day.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their knowledge of units of time to solve word problems involving seconds, minutes, hours and days.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students know key time facts (60 seconds in a minute, 60 minutes in an hour, 24 hours in a day) and can apply the four operations to solve time word problems.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse multiplication and division when converting between units of time.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Describe the differences in duration between units of time (days, weeks, months and years), and solve duration-of-time problems involving am and pm notation.</li><li>Convert between units of time and solve duration-of-time problems, in both 12- and 24-hour time systems.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Useful but not essential - can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert between seconds, minutes, hours and days.</li><li>I can use the four operations to solve word problems involving time.</li><li>I can express a duration such as 2 hours 15 minutes as a mixed number of hours.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 8: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of length, mass, volume and time measurement developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students solve a challenging word problem involving simultaneous relationships and a conversion between kilograms and pounds.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can convert confidently between metric units of length, mass and volume.</li><li>I can apply my knowledge of measurement conversions to solve an unfamiliar problem.</li><li>I can explain the relationship between metric and imperial units in writing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students solve a challenging word problem involving simultaneous relationships and a conversion between kilograms and pounds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write about the relationship between imperial and metric units for length, mass and volume.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 12.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 12: Area & Perimeter":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students calculate perimeter and area of rectangles and squares using counting and formulas, extend this to composite rectilinear shapes, and finally estimate the area of irregular shapes and interpret scale drawings.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Perimeter of Rectangles and Squares":{"label":"Lesson 1: Perimeter of Rectangles and Squares","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the perimeter of a rectangle is the total distance around its sides, and can be calculated as 2 times the sum of its length and breadth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss the properties of a rectangle and how a square differs from a rectangle.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces perimeter as the sum of a shape's side lengths and models the formula 2 times (length plus breadth) for a rectangle, then demonstrates finding a missing length from a known perimeter.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students find the perimeter of various rectangles, some with pictorial support.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students find a missing length given the perimeter of a rectangle, using small rectangular objects to measure and calculate perimeter where resources allow.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they found a missing side length from a given perimeter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Squared paper (each, optional)</li><li>Selection of small rectangular objects (optional)</li><li>Measuring tapes (optional)</li><li>Printout of Guided Practice shapes (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rectangle</li><li>square</li><li>perimeter</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may add only the length and breadth once, forgetting to double the total.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the perimeter of a rectangle is the total distance around its sides, and can be calculated as 2 times the sum of its length and breadth.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can measure or count squares to find a rectangle's perimeter, and calculate perimeter accurately from given side lengths.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse perimeter and area.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, estimate and measure the perimeter of polygons, using metric units (cm and m).</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces the concept of perimeter.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that a square is a rectangle with all sides the same length.</li><li>I can calculate the perimeter of a rectangle from its length and breadth.</li><li>I can find a missing side length when the perimeter of a rectangle is known.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Area of Rectangles and Squares":{"label":"Lesson 2: Area of Rectangles and Squares","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of a rectangle can be found by counting unit squares, or by multiplying its length and breadth, and is measured in square centimetres or square metres.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students compare two similarly-sized panels, one a square and one a longer, thinner rectangle, and discuss how to compare their areas.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models measuring area by counting squares on a grid, then introduces the terms square centimetre and square metre and the formula area equals length multiplied by breadth.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students calculate the area of a range of rectangles and squares.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students solve word problems involving the area of rectangles and squares, including finding a missing side length from a known area.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they found a missing length or breadth from a given area.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Squared paper (each, optional)</li><li>Selection of small rectangular objects (optional)</li><li>Measuring tapes (optional)</li><li>Printout of Guided Practice shapes (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>rectangle</li><li>square</li><li>area</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>square centimetre</li><li>square metre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse perimeter and area.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of a rectangle can be found by counting unit squares, or by multiplying its length and breadth, and is measured in square centimetres or square metres.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can count squares to find area, identify the length and breadth of a rectangle, and multiply these to calculate area, including finding a missing side length.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, estimate and measure the area of shapes covered with squares or half squares.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - shows how to calculate the area of a rectangle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can find the area of a rectangle by counting squares on a grid.</li><li>I can calculate the area of a rectangle using area equals length multiplied by breadth.</li><li>I can find a missing side length when the area of a rectangle is known.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Perimeter of Composite Shapes":{"label":"Lesson 3: Perimeter of Composite Shapes","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the perimeter of an irregular rectilinear shape can be found by working out any missing side lengths and adding all the sides together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students recall how to find the perimeter of a rectangle, then consider how this could extend to a six-sided rectilinear shape.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models identifying missing side lengths in a rectilinear shape using the properties of parallel and perpendicular sides, then adds all sides to find the perimeter.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students find the perimeter of irregular rectilinear shapes, calculating missing lengths where needed.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students measure the classroom using measuring tapes, rounding lengths to the nearest half-metre, and calculate its perimeter.</p><p>Plenary: Students share how they calculated a missing side length in a rectilinear shape.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Squared paper (each, optional)</li><li>Selection of small rectangular objects (optional)</li><li>Measuring tapes (optional)</li><li>Printout of Guided Practice shapes (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>perimeter</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>right angle</li><li>parallel</li><li>perpendicular</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse perimeter and area.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the perimeter of an irregular rectilinear shape can be found by working out any missing side lengths and adding all the sides together.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can find missing lengths in a rectilinear shape such as a floor plan, use measuring equipment accurately, and calculate the perimeter of an irregular polygon.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not understand a shape's properties well enough to work out missing lengths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may add only the marked lengths and disregard lengths that must be calculated.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Calculate the perimeter and area of composite shapes composed of triangles and rectangles.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - extends students' understanding of perimeter beyond rectangles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify parallel and perpendicular sides in a rectilinear shape.</li><li>I can calculate a missing side length in a rectilinear shape using known lengths.</li><li>I can find the perimeter of an irregular rectilinear shape by adding all its sides.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Area of Composite Shapes":{"label":"Lesson 4: Area of Composite Shapes","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of an irregular rectilinear shape can be found by splitting it into two or more rectangles and adding their areas.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider how they could find the area of an irregular shape by placing it on squared paper and counting squares.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models splitting a rectilinear shape into two rectangles, calculating any missing lengths, and finding the total area, before showing that a shape can sometimes be split in more than one valid way.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students calculate the area of irregular rectilinear shapes by splitting them into rectangles.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students find the area of rectilinear shapes and express their answer in square centimetres or square metres.</p><p>Plenary: Students compare different ways they split the same shape into rectangles.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Squared paper (each, optional)</li><li>Selection of small rectangular objects (optional)</li><li>Measuring tapes (optional)</li><li>Printout of Guided Practice shapes (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>area</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>right angle</li><li>parallel</li><li>perpendicular</li><li>square centimetre</li><li>square metre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse perimeter and area.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of an irregular rectilinear shape can be found by splitting it into two or more rectangles and adding their areas.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can count squares to estimate area, multiply length and breadth to find area, find missing lengths in a rectilinear shape, and correctly split a composite shape into rectangles.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not understand a shape's properties well enough to work out missing lengths.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may split a shape into rectangles but use an incorrect length or breadth for one part.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Calculate the perimeter and area of composite shapes composed of triangles and rectangles.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - extends students' understanding of area beyond rectangles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can split a composite rectilinear shape into rectangles.</li><li>I can calculate any missing lengths needed to find the area of each rectangle.</li><li>I can add the areas of the parts to find the total area of a composite shape.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Estimating Area and Drawing to Scale":{"label":"Lesson 5: Estimating Area and Drawing to Scale","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of an irregular shape can be estimated by counting whole and part squares on a grid, and that scale drawings represent real-life areas at a reduced size.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss how they might find the area of a shape with curved or irregular edges, such as a lake or a country on a map.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models a systematic method for estimating area by counting complete squares and judging partially covered squares, then introduces scale drawings by finding the real-life area of one square and multiplying by the number of squares.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students estimate the area of irregular shapes drawn on a grid.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students estimate area from scale drawings, converting the number of squares into a real-life area.</p><p>Plenary: Students compare their estimates and discuss why answers may vary slightly.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Squared paper (each, optional)</li><li>Selection of small rectangular objects (optional)</li><li>Measuring tapes (optional)</li><li>Printout of Guided Practice shapes (optional)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>area</li><li>estimate</li><li>square unit</li><li>square centimetre</li><li>square metre</li><li>scale</li><li>scale drawing</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to decide which partially covered squares to include in an estimate.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the area of an irregular shape can be estimated by counting whole and part squares on a grid, and that scale drawings represent real-life areas at a reduced size.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify complete and partially covered squares, use counting to estimate area of shapes with curved or irregular edges, and apply a given scale to convert a drawn area into a real-life area.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students who rely only on multiplication for area may struggle when a shape is irregular.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use known multiplication and division facts to scale a quantity.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces an alternative, approximate method for finding area.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can estimate the area of an irregular shape by counting complete and partially covered squares.</li><li>I can explain that my answer is an estimate rather than an exact area.</li><li>I can use a given scale to convert the area shown on a drawing into a real-life area.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 6: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of perimeter, area and scale drawings developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students use a scaled drawing of a room to find its actual measurements.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use a scale drawing to find the actual measurements of a real object or space.</li><li>I can identify real-life examples of scale drawings and explain their purpose.</li><li>I can explain my reasoning about area and perimeter in writing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students use a scaled drawing of a room to find its actual measurements.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students find and discuss real-life examples of scale drawings, considering why they are useful and how their scale might be chosen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 13.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 13: Volume":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students explore volume by counting unit cubes, then calculate the volume of cuboids using a formula, extend this to capacity and liquid volume, solve word problems involving volume, and identify prisms by their properties.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Volume of Solids":{"label":"Lesson 1: Volume of Solids","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume describes how much space a solid takes up, and can be measured by counting unit cubes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss statements about which objects have a large volume, considering objects that are tall or wide but do not have a large volume, such as a pencil.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces volume as the amount of space a solid takes up and models measuring the volume of shapes built from unit cubes by counting them.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students compare two solids built from different numbers of unit cubes to decide which has the greater volume.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students calculate the volume of various solids made from unit cubes, and use number cards to build as many different solids as possible from a given number of cubes.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they counted cubes to find the volume of a solid.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>cubic units</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse volume with height or length.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume describes how much space a solid takes up, and can be measured by counting unit cubes.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe volume as the space a solid takes up, recognise that volume can be measured in cubes, and compare and calculate volume by counting cubes.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think that the way cubes are arranged affects the total volume.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, estimate and measure the volume of shapes filled with centicubes, taking note of layers and stacking.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces the concept of volume.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that volume means how much space a solid takes up.</li><li>I can find the volume of a solid by counting the unit cubes it is made from.</li><li>I can compare the volume of two solids by comparing their number of cubes.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Volume of Solids in Cubic Units":{"label":"Lesson 2: Volume of Solids in Cubic Units","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume can be measured formally using cubic centimetres and cubic metres, and that hidden cubes must still be counted.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students estimate the size of a cubic centimetre and a cubic metre using everyday objects as reference points, such as a bean or a small cupboard.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models finding the volume of solids built from 1-cm cubes, including solids where some cubes are hidden from view, and introduces the connection between multiplication, area and volume.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students find the volume of various 3D shapes built from unit cubes.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students complete sentences to show understanding of volume measured in cubic centimetres and cubic metres, and estimate how many small cubes would fit inside a larger container.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss how they accounted for hidden cubes when finding a solid's volume.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>cubic centimetre</li><li>cubic metre</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may confuse volume with height or length.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that volume can be measured formally using cubic centimetres and cubic metres, and that hidden cubes must still be counted.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe volume as the space a solid takes up, compare volumes, find volume by counting cubes including hidden ones, and use cm cubed and m cubed notation correctly.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think the arrangement of cubes affects the total volume.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may forget to count cubes that are hidden from view.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may count corner cubes more than once.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, estimate and calculate the volume of rectangular prisms filled with centicubes, taking note of layers and stacking.</li><li>Visualise, estimate and calculate the area of rectangles and right-angled triangles, and the volume of rectangular prisms, by applying multiplication.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces metric units of volume.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can estimate the real-life size of a cubic centimetre and a cubic metre.</li><li>I can find the volume of a solid in cubic centimetres, including cubes that are hidden from view.</li><li>I can use the notation cm cubed and m cubed to record volume.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Finding the Volume of Cuboids":{"label":"Lesson 3: Finding the Volume of Cuboids","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the volume of a cuboid can be calculated directly using the formula volume equals length times breadth times height, without needing to count individual cubes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students discuss how a piece of clay could be reshaped into a cuboid made of 1-cm cubes without its volume changing.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces the formula volume equals length times breadth times height and models applying it to find the volume of a cuboid without needing to count individual cubes.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students calculate the volume of cuboids and cubes, with decreasing pictorial support.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students find the total volume of composite solids by adding the volume of separate cuboids, and investigate whether doubling one dimension of a cuboid doubles its volume.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss their findings about what happens to volume when one dimension of a cuboid is doubled.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>cuboid</li><li>cubic centimetre</li><li>cubic metre</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>height</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think the arrangement of cubes affects the total volume.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the volume of a cuboid can be calculated directly using the formula volume equals length times breadth times height, without needing to count individual cubes.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe volume as the space a solid takes up, use cm cubed and m cubed notation, and calculate the volume of a cuboid as the product of its length, breadth and height.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply arbitrary measurements rather than the length, breadth and height.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe a shape must be physically divided into small cubes before its volume can be found.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Visualise, estimate and calculate the area of rectangles and right-angled triangles, and the volume of rectangular prisms, by applying multiplication.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - can be combined with Lesson 4; shows how to use the formula volume equals length times breadth times height.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain that a shape's volume stays the same even if it changes shape.</li><li>I can calculate the volume of a cuboid using length times breadth times height.</li><li>I can find the total volume of a composite solid by adding the volumes of its parts.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Finding the Volume of Liquids":{"label":"Lesson 4: Finding the Volume of Liquids","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the capacity of a container can be measured in litres and millilitres, and relates to the volume of a cuboid-shaped container.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Teacher pours water from a larger container into a smaller one, prompting discussion of why it does not fit and introducing the vocabulary of volume and capacity.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces litres and millilitres as units of capacity, links these to cubic centimetres, and models converting between litres and millilitres.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students convert between cubic centimetres and units of capacity.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students use the formula capacity equals length times breadth times height to find the capacity of cuboid-shaped containers, including containers that are only partially full.</p><p>Plenary: Students compare containers of different sizes and discuss which has the greater capacity.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>cuboid</li><li>litre</li><li>millilitre</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>height</li><li>capacity</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think that volume and capacity are unrelated.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the capacity of a container can be measured in litres and millilitres, and relates to the volume of a cuboid-shaped container.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe capacity as the space available in a container, compare capacities, use litres and millilitres to record volume, and calculate capacity as the product of length, breadth and height.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply arbitrary measurements rather than the length, breadth and height.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe a shape must be physically divided into small cubes before its volume can be found.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use the metric measurement system to explore relationships between units, including relationships represented by benchmark fractions and decimals.</li><li>Visualise, estimate and calculate the area of rectangles and right-angled triangles, and the volume of rectangular prisms, by applying multiplication.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - can be combined with Lesson 3; shows how to use the formula volume equals length times breadth times height to find the capacity of a cuboid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the difference between volume and capacity.</li><li>I can calculate the capacity of a cuboid-shaped container using its length, breadth and height.</li><li>I can convert between litres and millilitres.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Solving Word Problems Involving Volume":{"label":"Lesson 5: Solving Word Problems Involving Volume","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their knowledge of volume and capacity to solve word problems, including problems where a container is not completely full.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students calculate the capacity of a tank and discuss why the amount of water in it may be less than its full capacity.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models a full worked solution to a volume word problem, then works through further problems that require converting between litres and millilitres and using given information such as a square base to find missing values.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students solve word problems requiring the volume formula and unit conversions.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students solve differentiated word problems involving volume and capacity, including finding a missing dimension.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss the key skills needed to solve a volume word problem, such as using the formula and converting units.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>volume</li><li>cuboid</li><li>cubic centimetre</li><li>cubic metre</li><li>litre</li><li>millilitre</li><li>length</li><li>breadth</li><li>height</li><li>capacity</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think that volume and capacity are unrelated.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will apply their knowledge of volume and capacity to solve word problems, including problems where a container is not completely full.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe capacity as the space available in a container, convert between cubic centimetres, millilitres and litres, and solve word problems involving capacity, including finding a missing dimension.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may multiply arbitrary measurements rather than the length, breadth and height.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may forget that the volume of water in a container depends on how full it is.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use the metric measurement system to explore relationships between units, including relationships represented by benchmark fractions and decimals.</li><li>Visualise, estimate and calculate the area of rectangles and right-angled triangles, and the volume of rectangular prisms, by applying multiplication.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Useful but not essential - can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can identify missing information in a word problem, such as using a square base to find an unknown side length.</li><li>I can calculate the volume or capacity of a cuboid to solve a word problem.</li><li>I can convert between litres, millilitres and cubic centimetres as needed to solve a problem.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5.1: Identifying Prisms (Supplementary Online Lesson)":{"label":"Lesson 5.1: Identifying Prisms (Supplementary Online Lesson)","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a prism is a 3D shape with two identical bases at opposite ends, flat faces and straight edges, and will use these properties to identify prisms.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students find a set of 3D shapes matching examples shown, and record what they notice about each one.</p><p>Teach: Teacher shares students' observations and confirms the defining properties of a prism, namely two identical bases at opposite ends, flat faces, and straight edges.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students sort and describe a set of 3D shapes according to their properties.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students sort 3D shapes into prisms and non-prisms, and explain why a particular shape is not a prism.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain why a cylinder is not a prism despite having two identical faces at opposite ends.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Unit cubes (between two)</li><li>Cards with numbers (between two)</li><li>10-cm cubical container</li><li>Small beaker</li><li>Water</li><li>Selection of containers (optional)</li><li>3D shapes including prisms and non-prisms</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>3D shape</li><li>3 dimensional shape</li><li>prism</li><li>square-based prism</li><li>rectangular prism</li><li>triangular prism</li><li>octagonal-based prism</li><li>straight edges</li><li>flat faces</li><li>cylinder</li><li>pyramid</li><li>sphere</li><li>cone</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think that all 3D shapes are prisms.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a prism is a 3D shape with two identical bases at opposite ends, flat faces and straight edges, and will use these properties to identify prisms.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can identify whether a 3D shape is a prism or not, recall the names of prisms by their base shape, and explain the properties of a prism.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may think a cylinder is a prism because it has two identical faces at opposite ends, forgetting that all edges of a prism must be straight.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Identify, classify and explain similarities and differences between 2D shapes, including different types of triangle, and between prisms and pyramids.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain the properties that define a prism.</li><li>I can sort 3D shapes into prisms and non-prisms.</li><li>I can name a prism according to the shape of its base.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 6: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 6: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of volume, capacity and prisms developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students are shown an irregular stack of cubes, some hidden, and find the smallest number of cubes that must be added to form a complete cuboid.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can find the smallest number of cubes needed to complete a cuboid from an irregular stack.</li><li>I can explain how the capacity of a cube changes when its side length is doubled.</li><li>I can explain my reasoning about volume in writing.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students are shown an irregular stack of cubes, some hidden, and find the smallest number of cubes that must be added to form a complete cuboid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students explain why doubling the side length of a cube makes its capacity more than double, leading to the idea that it becomes eight times larger.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support before moving to Chapter 14.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 14: Algebra":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students learn algebraic conventions through patterns, using a letter to represent a variable, then write expressions involving addition and multiplication and apply formulae to solve real-life problems.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Describing a Pattern — Using a Letter to Stand for a Number":{"label":"Lesson 1: Describing a Pattern — Using a Letter to Stand for a Number","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a growing pattern can be described using a rule in words, and that a letter can be used to represent an unknown position or value in the pattern.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students look for a pattern in an arrangement of squares and describe it in their own words.</p><p>Teach: Teacher encourages students to move from qualitative descriptions to quantitative ones, such as noticing that the bottom row always has one more square than the previous pattern, and models recording the pattern in a table.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students use counters to build the next terms of a pattern and explain the rule, then use their table to predict later terms, including an unknown term n.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students look at simple number and shape patterns and make a generalisation using a letter of their choice.</p><p>Plenary: Students share their rule for a pattern and discuss that any letter can be used to represent the unknown term.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>to stand for</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to identify the relationship between one term and the next.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a growing pattern can be described using a rule in words, and that a letter can be used to represent an unknown position or value in the pattern.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can use concrete materials to continue a pattern, explain how it progresses, make predictions from their generalisation, and express a rule using a letter in place of numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may be unable to describe the rule in a pattern using words.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may be unable to relate a symbol to an unknown value, believing that a letter such as x has one fixed value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use tables to recognise the relationship between the ordinal position and its corresponding element in a growing pattern, develop a rule for the pattern in words, and make conjectures about further elements or terms in the pattern.</li><li>Use tables, XY graphs and diagrams to recognise relationships in a linear pattern, develop a rule for the pattern in words, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces using a letter to stand for a number with very simple examples.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe how a pattern grows using quantitative language.</li><li>I can record a pattern in a table and use it to find a future term.</li><li>I can use a letter to represent an unknown term in a pattern, such as term n.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Describing a Pattern — Simple Algebraic Expressions":{"label":"Lesson 2: Describing a Pattern — Simple Algebraic Expressions","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a relationship between two changing quantities in a pattern can be expressed as a simple algebraic expression using any letter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students describe the next terms of a pattern involving two colours of squares, using words, drawings or concrete materials.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models expressing the relationship between the two quantities using different letters, such as p, z and a, to build comfort with letters as generic variables, and demonstrates checking an expression using a numerical example.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students use symbols and letters to express relationships between numbers, checking their expressions with numerical examples.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students repeat a counter activity with a new simple pattern, such as a rectangle of width 2 and length n, and write an expression to describe it.</p><p>Plenary: Students share how they checked their algebraic expression was correct using numbers.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>to stand for</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to identify the relationship between one term and the next.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that a relationship between two changing quantities in a pattern can be expressed as a simple algebraic expression using any letter.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can use concrete materials to continue patterns, explain how a pattern progresses, make predictions from their generalisations, and use letters, symbols and numbers to express relationships between numbers.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may be unable to describe the rule in a pattern using words.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe that a letter such as x has one fixed value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use tables to recognise the relationship between the ordinal position and its corresponding element in a growing pattern, develop a rule for the pattern in words, and make conjectures about further elements or terms in the pattern.</li><li>Create and use an algorithm for generating a pattern, procedure or pathway.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces simple arithmetic with variables, such as p plus 1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe the next terms of a pattern using words, drawings or concrete materials.</li><li>I can write a simple expression such as z plus 2 to describe a relationship between two quantities in a pattern.</li><li>I can check my expression is correct by substituting a numerical example.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Describing a Pattern — Consecutive Numbers":{"label":"Lesson 3: Describing a Pattern — Consecutive Numbers","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that consecutive numbers follow one another in order, and that an unknown consecutive number can be expressed algebraically in relation to a given letter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Teacher introduces the term consecutive and its Latin root meaning to follow, then asks students to decide whether given sets of numbers are consecutive.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models expressing a set of consecutive numbers algebraically using a letter such as x, clarifying why 2 must be added or subtracted for consecutive even or odd numbers, and repeats examples with different variable names to show they are arbitrary.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students write definitions of unknown numbers in sets of consecutive numbers where one number is labelled with a letter.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students solve problems involving consecutive even numbers and consecutive odd numbers.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain how they found a missing consecutive number using the given letter.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>consecutive</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may struggle to identify the relationship between one term and the next.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that consecutive numbers follow one another in order, and that an unknown consecutive number can be expressed algebraically in relation to a given letter.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can recognise patterns in numbers, describe how numbers relate to one another, and refer to unknown consecutive numbers using expressions such as m plus 1 and m minus 2.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may be unable to describe the rule in a pattern using words.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe that a letter such as x has one fixed value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use tables, XY graphs and diagrams to recognise relationships in a linear pattern, develop a rule for the pattern in words, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li><li>Form and solve true or false number sentences and open number sentences involving all four operations.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Useful but not essential - can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can explain what consecutive numbers are.</li><li>I can find missing numbers in a set of consecutive numbers, given one number represented by a letter.</li><li>I can express consecutive even or odd numbers algebraically, such as m plus 2.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 4: Describing a Pattern — Expressing with Multiplication":{"label":"Lesson 4: Describing a Pattern — Expressing with Multiplication","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">IF TIME ALLOWS</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the general term of a pattern can involve multiplication, and that an expression such as 3 times n is conventionally written as 3n.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students look at a pattern and consider that its general term could be found by multiplying the term position by 3.</p><p>Teach: Teacher introduces the convention of writing 3 times n as 3n, and clarifies why n3 would be an incorrect way to write this, before setting further examples using multiples of n or additions and subtractions from n.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students recognise repeating patterns, including patterns involving multiplication, and write expressions to describe them.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students use pictorial support to complete a table, find a rule, and use the rule to find an unknown value, then apply given values to algebraic expressions.</p><p>Plenary: Students explain why 3n and n times 3 represent the same value, but n3 does not.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Counters (between two)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>to stand for</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may be unable to describe the rule in a pattern using words.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the general term of a pattern can involve multiplication, and that an expression such as 3 times n is conventionally written as 3n.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can describe how numbers relate to one another, and use letters, numbers and symbols to express relationships including multiples such as 3 times p or 3p.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe that a letter such as n has one fixed value.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may not recognise that 3n means the same as 3 times n.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may write n3 to mean 3n.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Use tables, XY graphs and diagrams to recognise relationships in a linear pattern, develop a rule for the pattern in words, and make conjectures about further elements in the pattern.</li><li>Create and use an algorithm for generating a pattern, procedure or pathway.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Useful but not essential - can be completed if time allows.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can find the general term of a pattern that involves multiplying by a constant.</li><li>I can write an expression such as 3n instead of 3 times n.</li><li>I can substitute a given value into an algebraic expression to find its value.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 5: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of algebraic expressions and formulae developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students solve a word problem by writing an expression, forming an equation, and solving it.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can write an expression and form an equation to solve a word problem.</li><li>I can write a formula to describe a geometric relationship, not just calculate a single answer.</li><li>I can identify areas where I need further support with algebra.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students solve a word problem by writing an expression, forming an equation, and solving it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students write a formula for a simple geometry problem, focusing on expressing the general relationship rather than only calculating the perimeter of specific rectangles.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students complete a chapter summary and discuss with their teacher any areas that are not yet secure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Chapter 15: Probability":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Chapter Overview":{"label":"Chapter Overview","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Chapter Overview</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students describe the likelihood of events using the language of chance, express likelihood as a percentage, and use equivalent fractions to calculate probability as a percentage, before running their own trials and comparing results with predictions.</p></div></div>"},"Lesson 1: Expressing Likelihood of an Event Using Percentage":{"label":"Lesson 1: Expressing Likelihood of an Event Using Percentage","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the likelihood of an event can be described using the language of chance and also expressed as a fraction or a percentage, from 0 percent (impossible) to 100 percent (certain).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider what a set of given percentages might be describing, and are introduced to the term likelihood.</p><p>Teach: Teacher relates 0 percent to impossible events and 100 percent to certain events, and models describing likelihood using chance vocabulary as well as fractions and percentages.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students decide on percentages and allocate given events to those percentages based on their likelihood.</p><p>Independent Practice: Students use number lines and tables to show the likelihood of events, expressed in words and as percentages, and compare likelihoods.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss how percentages help compare the likelihood of two events more precisely than words alone.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bag of single coloured items</li><li>A bag of multicoloured items</li><li>3 coins with heads and tails</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>likelihood</li><li>certain</li><li>impossible</li><li>equally likely</li><li>likely</li><li>unlikely</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe that all events have an equal likelihood.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the likelihood of an event can be described using the language of chance and also expressed as a fraction or a percentage, from 0 percent (impossible) to 100 percent (certain).</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can give examples of certain and impossible events, use terms such as likely, unlikely and equally likely accurately, and express the likelihood of an event using percentages.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe the likelihood of an event is always the same for everyone.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Engage in chance-based investigations, including those with not equally likely outcomes, by posing an investigative question, identifying possible outcomes, finding probabilities as fractions, and reflecting on anticipated outcomes.</li><li>Identify, read, write and represent fractions, decimals to two places, and related percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces using percentages to describe the likelihood of an event happening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can use words such as certain, likely, unlikely and impossible to describe an event.</li><li>I can explain that 0 percent means an event is impossible and 100 percent means it is certain.</li><li>I can allocate a percentage to an event based on how likely it is to happen.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 2: Finding Probability of an Event in Percentage":{"label":"Lesson 2: Finding Probability of an Event in Percentage","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Type</th><td class=\"lt-td\">KEY LESSON</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the probability of an event can be calculated as a fraction of possible outcomes and then converted to a percentage using equivalent fractions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Engage: Students consider how likely it is that a particular coloured item would be picked from a bag, and discuss how to describe this more precisely than just unlikely.</p><p>Teach: Teacher models converting a probability fraction such as three tenths into an equivalent hundredths fraction and percentage, then works through further examples using equivalent fractions.</p><p>Guided Practice: Students find the probability of events as percentages and as fractions.</p><p>Independent Practice: In pairs, students predict the likely outcomes of tossing three coins, carry out the trials, record the results, and compare these with their predictions.</p><p>Plenary: Students discuss whether their trial results matched their predicted probabilities and why they might differ.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-green\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>A bag of single coloured items</li><li>A bag of multicoloured items</li><li>3 coins with heads and tails</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-gold\">Maths Language &amp; Variation</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Maths Language</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>likelihood</li><li>certain</li><li>impossible</li><li>equally likely</li><li>likely</li><li>unlikely</li><li>trial</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-maroon\">Assess Learning</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-data\"><thead><tr><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Misconceptions</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Non-Negotiable</th><th class=\"lt-th-col\">Formative Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe that all events have an equal likelihood.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will understand that the probability of an event can be calculated as a fraction of possible outcomes and then converted to a percentage using equivalent fractions.</td><td class=\"lt-td\">Teacher checks students can use chance vocabulary accurately, express probability as a percentage, use equivalent fractions to find a percentage, and describe probability using fractions.</td></tr><tr><td class=\"lt-td\">Students may believe the likelihood of an event is always the same for everyone.</td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td><td class=\"lt-td\"></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">National Curriculum</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Engage in chance-based investigations, including those with not equally likely outcomes, by generating all possible ways to get each outcome or undertaking a probability experiment, recording occurrences, and comparing findings with theoretical probabilities.</li><li>Identify, read, write and represent fractions, decimals to two places, and related percentages.</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Content Priority</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Key lesson - introduces using equivalent fractions to find percentages to describe the likelihood of an event happening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can express the probability of an event as a fraction of all possible outcomes.</li><li>I can convert a probability fraction to a percentage using equivalent fractions, such as tenths to hundredths.</li><li>I can carry out a simple trial, record results, and compare them with my predicted probability.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Lesson 3: Chapter Consolidation":{"label":"Lesson 3: Chapter Consolidation","content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Overview</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Lesson Objective</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students will consolidate and apply their understanding of likelihood and probability developed across the chapter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Lesson Approach</div><div class=\"lt-para\"><p>Students apply their knowledge of likelihood and percentages to a novel comparison problem, ordering several events from least to most likely.</p></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Check the Curriculum</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Learning Outcomes</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>I can describe and order the likelihood of several events using percentages.</li><li>I can find the probability of an event as a percentage using equivalent fractions.</li><li>I can compare a predicted probability with the results of a real trial and explain any differences.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-teal\">Extension &amp; Reflection</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-teal\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Mind Challenge</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students apply their knowledge of likelihood and percentages to a novel comparison problem, ordering several events from least to most likely.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-amber\"><th class=\"lt-th\">My Maths Journal</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students explain, in writing, why the results of a real trial do not always exactly match a predicted probability.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Review</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Whole-class recap of the chapter, used to identify any students who need further support with likelihood and probability.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"art":{"label":"Category","children":{"Maori Art":{"label":"Chapter","children":{}},"Art Concepts":{"label":"Chapter","children":{}}}},"pe":{"label":"Sport","children":{"Basketball":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Ball Handling & Footwork (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Home Base Dribble":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling — control at a stationary point</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while your dribbling hand feels clumsy at first, and encouraging teammates whose ball control looks different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread out — one ball's-width minimum gap when dribbling freely; clear grid boundaries so students don't collide</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to dribble the ball with control while standing in one spot.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dribble using my fingertips, not my palm</li><li>I can keep the ball below waist height without watching it constantly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Dribble Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'swap hands!' — students continue dribbling but switch to their other hand without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up ball-handling before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Push the ball with spread fingertips, not the palm.</td><td>Fingertip control gives better feel and prevents carrying or palming violations.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball below waist height.</td><td>A lower dribble is harder for defenders to reach and easier to control.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your head up, using peripheral vision to feel the ball.</td><td>Trains you to dribble without needing to watch the ball constantly.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fingertip Focus</h4><p>Standing in a personal space, students dribble continuously using only their fingertips, pushing the ball down rather than slapping it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own ball and a personal space marked by a cone, spread out in a grid roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 25-30 so no two students are within a ball's width of each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Spread your fingers and push the ball, don't slap at it with a flat hand</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger bounce, ball allowed higher</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Lower, quicker dribble</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate dribbling with fingers spread wide, pushing the ball down with the pads of the fingers rather than slapping it with a flat palm.</li><li>Students stand in their own marked space and begin dribbling continuously on the spot, no travelling required.</li><li>Keep the dribble below waist height throughout — if the ball pops up above the waist, students reset to a controlled dribble before continuing.</li><li>Coach the key message around the room: 'spread your fingers and push the ball, don't slap at it with a flat hand.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a bigger bounce with the ball rising higher; for 'harder', ask for a lower, quicker dribble.</li><li>Run for 3-4 minutes continuously, walking the grid to check individual technique rather than stopping the whole class.</li></ul><hr><h4>Eyes Up Count</h4><p>While dribbling on the spot, students must hold up fingers on their non-dribbling hand when the teacher calls 'show me!' — forcing them to keep their head up rather than watching the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same personal-space grid as Fingertip Focus, one ball per student, no partner needed — the teacher calls out to the whole class at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feel the ball with your fingertips, trust it without looking</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower dribble, more time to respond</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster dribble, quick-fire calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: students keep dribbling on the spot with their dominant hand while their other hand stays free.</li><li>On 'show me!', students hold up a number of fingers on their non-dribbling hand without stopping or looking down at the ball.</li><li>Call out a number (e.g. 'show me three!') and scan the room for correct, confident responses.</li><li>If a student loses control of the dribble while responding, they simply restart their dribble and keep going — no penalty.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'feel the ball with your fingertips, trust it without looking.'</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace of calls down; for 'harder', fire calls quickly and unpredictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Freeze &amp; Balance</h4><p>As the warm-up, but now with a partner watching — on 'freeze!' the dribbler must stop the ball dead under control (no travelling) and balance it on their fingertips for a count of three.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up within the same grid, partners standing about 1m apart, one ball per dribbling student — partners swap roles halfway through.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring the ball to a soft stop, don't let it bounce away</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, more warning</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the dribbler keeps dribbling on the spot exactly as in Fingertip Focus, but now a partner watches for the freeze.</li><li>The partner calls 'freeze!' at an unpredictable moment — the dribbler must bring the ball to a soft, controlled stop (no travelling with their feet).</li><li>Once stopped, the dribbler balances the ball on their fingertips (resting on top, not gripping) and holds it still for a count of three out loud.</li><li>If the ball wobbles off the fingertips or the feet move, the pair simply restarts the dribble and tries the freeze again.</li><li>Swap roles so both partners get turns dribbling and calling freeze.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring the ball to a soft stop, don't let it bounce away.' For 'easier', give more warning before calling freeze; for 'harder', call it faster and less predictably.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Musical Dribble</h3><p>Whole class dribbles freely in a large grid to music (or a continuous count); when the sound stops everyone must freeze their dribble under control — anyone whose ball gets away or who travels does 3 quick fingertip touches before rejoining. No eliminations — just a fun, repeated challenge.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class dribbles freely inside one large shared grid roughly 20m x 20m, each student with their own ball. A phone/speaker for music (or the teacher's own steady counting) sits at the front.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: everyone dribbles freely around the big grid while the music/count plays, using the fingertip technique from today's games.</li><li>When the music stops (or the count pauses), every student must freeze their dribble under control on the spot — no extra bounce, no travelling.</li><li>Anyone whose ball gets away from them, or who takes a travelling step after the freeze, does 3 quick fingertip touches on the ball where they stand before rejoining the next round.</li><li>There's no elimination — everyone keeps playing every round, so mistakes just mean a quick, friendly consequence rather than sitting out.</li><li>Restart the music/count as soon as everyone is set, and repeat for several rounds, mixing up how long the music plays each time.</li><li>Keep the tone upbeat and playful — this is a fun capstone to the lesson's fingertip-control focus, not a competitive elimination game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you keep control of the ball while standing still?</li><li>What's the difference between slapping the ball and pushing it with your fingertips?</li><li>Why does keeping your eyes up matter, even this early in learning to dribble?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses fingertips, not the palm</li><li>Keeps the ball below waist height</li><li>Can glance up briefly without losing control</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a bigger, slower bounce or a softer ball for students building hand strength/confidence; success is measured by control, not speed.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Hand Swap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling on the move / change of hand</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding what your own hands and feet are doing while moving with the ball, and noticing what changes when it starts to click.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions when moving through a shared space — remind students to check space before changing direction</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to dribble while moving and to swap between hands under control.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dribble while walking or jogging without losing the ball</li><li>I can swap the ball from one hand to the other without slapping it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Dribble Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'swap hands!' — students continue dribbling but switch to their other hand without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up ball-handling before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Push the ball slightly ahead of you as you walk or jog.</td><td>Stops the ball trailing behind and getting out of reach.</td></tr><tr><td>Swap hands low and in front of the body, not with a big slap.</td><td>A low, controlled swap keeps the ball protected during the change.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your eyes up while moving.</td><td>Lets you see space, teammates, and defenders while still controlling the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Walking Dribble Lanes</h4><p>Students dribble the length of a marked lane while walking, focusing purely on keeping control while moving; race back using the other hand.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 4-6 parallel lanes roughly 1.5m wide and 10m long using cones, one student dribbling in each lane at a time, one ball per student waiting at the start of their lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Push the ball slightly ahead of you as you walk, don't let it trail behind</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider lane, walk only</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower lane, light jog</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate walking forward while dribbling, pushing the ball slightly ahead of the body rather than letting it trail behind or bounce beside the feet.</li><li>Students walk the length of their lane dribbling with one hand, keeping the ball under control the whole way.</li><li>At the far end, they turn around and dribble back down the same lane using the other hand only.</li><li>If the ball gets away or a student has to chase it outside the lane, they walk back to where they lost control and continue from there rather than starting over completely.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'push the ball slightly ahead of you as you walk, don't let it trail behind.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the lane and keep to a walk; for 'harder', narrow the lane and allow a light jog.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cone Hand-Swap Slalom</h4><p>Dribbling through a zig-zag line of cones, students swap hands every time they change direction around a cone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a zig-zag line of 6-8 cones spaced roughly 2m apart per group of 4-5 students, with 2-3 parallel slalom lines if space allows so several students can go at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Swap the ball low and in front of your body, not with a big slap across</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider cone spacing, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower spacing, faster pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate dribbling in a zig-zag around the cones, switching the ball to the other hand low and in front of the body every time you change direction around a cone.</li><li>Students take turns weaving through their line's cones, swapping hands at each direction change rather than dribbling the whole slalom with one hand.</li><li>If the ball is swapped with a big slap across the body instead of a low, controlled switch, the student redoes that cone before continuing.</li><li>Once a student clears the last cone, they jog the ball back around the outside to the start and the next student in line begins.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'swap the ball low and in front of your body, not with a big slap across.'</li><li>For 'easier', space the cones wider and slow the pace; for 'harder', narrow the spacing and race against a partner group with a stopwatch.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shrinking Grid Dribble</h4><p>Whole class dribbles inside a grid that shrinks every 30 seconds; students must swap hands whenever the teacher calls 'swap!' while weaving to find space.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a starting grid of roughly 20m x 20m using cones for the whole class, one ball per student, with 4 extra cones ready to move inward to shrink the grid every 30 seconds.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use quick, low dribbles to find gaps as the grid shrinks</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shrink slower, fewer swap calls</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shrink faster, frequent swap calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that everyone dribbles freely inside the shared grid, weaving to find open space and avoid bumping into others.</li><li>Call 'swap!' at random intervals — every student must immediately change dribbling hands without stopping their dribble.</li><li>Every 30 seconds, move the boundary cones inward to shrink the playing area, forcing students to find gaps and dribble with tighter control.</li><li>If two students' balls collide or a student steps outside the shrinking boundary, they simply reset their dribble in the nearest open space and continue.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use quick, low dribbles to find gaps as the grid shrinks.'</li><li>For 'easier', shrink the grid more slowly with fewer swap calls; for 'harder', shrink faster with frequent swap calls.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Dribble Tag</h3><p>In a shared space, everyone dribbles their own ball while trying to gently tap other players' balls away (without grabbing or contacting hands) while protecting their own — no elimination, just count how many times your ball gets tapped versus how many you tap.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class in one shared space roughly 20m x 20m, every student with their own ball — no teams, everyone plays individually against everyone else.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: everyone dribbles their own ball while trying to gently tap other players' balls away using an open hand — no grabbing, no contact with hands or bodies.</li><li>At the same time, everyone protects their own ball using the shielding and hand-swap skills from today's games.</li><li>Play continuously for a set time (e.g. 2-3 minutes) — if your ball gets tapped away, simply collect it and rejoin straight away rather than sitting out.</li><li>Each student keeps a rough personal count of how many times they successfully tapped another ball away versus how many times their own ball was tapped.</li><li>After each round, ask a few students to share their tally, then reset and play another round.</li><li>Keep contact rules strict and remind students throughout: hands only on the ball, never on another player.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did moving while dribbling feel different from dribbling on the spot?</li><li>Which hand did you find easier to swap to, and why do you think that is?</li><li>What helped you protect your ball while others were nearby?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps the ball under control while walking/jogging</li><li>Swaps hands with a low, controlled push</li><li>Keeps the ball close to the body when others are nearby</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to practise the hand swap stationary before adding movement if the combined skill feels like too much at once.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Guard the Rock":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling under pressure (protect the ball)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills faithfully to give your partner a fair, honest challenge — not going so hard it isn't fun, not giving up either.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for grids</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No reaching in or grabbing at the ball — hands-off pressure only; no pushing with the shielding arm</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to protect the ball with our body while dribbling near a defender.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can keep my body between the ball and a defender</li><li>I can use my non-dribbling arm to shield the ball (without pushing)</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Dribble Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'swap hands!' — students continue dribbling but switch to their other hand without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up ball-handling before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your body between the ball and the defender at all times.</td><td>Uses your body as a shield so the defender must go around you.</td></tr><tr><td>Use your non-dribbling arm, bent not pushing, to feel where the defender is.</td><td>Gives you awareness without needing to look back, and keeps the shield legal.</td></tr><tr><td>Change pace or direction rather than trying to power through.</td><td>A change of pace beats a defender more reliably than strength alone.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Dribble</h4><p>Attacker dribbles freely; a 'shadow' defender stays an arm's length away and mirrors movement without reaching for the ball, just to get the dribbler used to pressure nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m for the whole class, one ball per pair, each pair given about 3m x 3m of working space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your body between the ball and the defender at all times</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stays further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner (the attacker) dribbles freely within their pair's space while the other partner (the shadow) stays about an arm's length away.</li><li>The shadow mirrors the attacker's movement closely but must keep their hands to themselves at all times — no reaching for the ball yet.</li><li>The attacker practises keeping their body between the ball and the shadow as they move, turning and shielding naturally as the shadow follows.</li><li>Swap roles every 45-60 seconds so both partners get equal attacking and shadowing turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your body between the ball and the defender at all times.'</li><li>For 'easier', the shadow stays further back; for 'harder', the shadow is allowed to get closer while still not reaching in.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Switch</h4><p>Dribbler practises turning their back slightly to the defender and switching the ball to the far hand to shield it, using the non-dribbling arm (bent, not pushing) for balance and protection.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and space as Shadow Dribble, one ball per pair, defender now allowed to move but never to reach for the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use your arm to feel where the defender is, not to push them away</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stationary</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender moves side to side</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate turning the back and shoulder slightly toward the defender while switching the ball to the hand furthest from them.</li><li>The dribbler uses their free (non-dribbling) arm bent up for balance and awareness of where the defender is — not to push them away.</li><li>Practise this shield-and-switch move slowly at first: dribbler turns, switches hands, and re-faces away from the defender.</li><li>The defender stays stationary at first, then is allowed to shuffle side to side to make the dribbler adjust their shield angle.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use your arm to feel where the defender is, not to push them away.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the defender stationary; for 'harder', let the defender move side to side.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Protect the Rock</h4><p>In a small square, one player dribbles and must protect the ball for a count of 10 seconds against a light-touch defender trying to tap it away, then swap roles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark small squares roughly 4m x 4m, one per pair, with one ball per pair — enough squares spread out so pairs don't interfere with each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Change of pace and shielding beat trying to out-muscle a defender</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger square, defender starts 2m back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller square, defender close from the start</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One player dribbles inside the square and must protect the ball for a count of 10 seconds against a light-touch defender trying to tap it away (no grabbing).</li><li>The defender may only use an open hand to tap at the ball, never grab or make body contact with the dribbler.</li><li>If the dribbler loses the ball or steps outside the square, the count restarts from zero.</li><li>After the 10-second count is reached (or attempted), swap roles so both students get a turn dribbling and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'change of pace and shielding beat trying to out-muscle a defender.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger square and start the defender 2m back; for 'harder', use a smaller square with the defender close from the start.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Keep-Away Basketball</h3><p>3v3 or 4v4, small court, no hoops — a team scores a point for every 5 consecutive dribbles/passes kept under pressure without the other team touching the ball. Protecting the ball under pressure directly determines success.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 6-8 for 3v3 or 4v4 games on a small marked court roughly 12m x 10m, no hoops needed — just cones marking the court edges. One ball per game, bibs in two colours to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring rule: a team scores a point for every 5 consecutive dribbles or passes they keep under pressure without the other team touching the ball.</li><li>Play begins with one team in possession; players use shielding, hand-swaps, and support passing to protect the ball from the other team's hands-off pressure.</li><li>If the ball is knocked away, intercepted, or goes out of the court area, possession switches to the other team and their own count starts from zero.</li><li>Call out the running count out loud as a team reaches it ('that's 5 — point!') so the game stays fast and the reward for good protection is obvious.</li><li>Rotate players on and off every few minutes if numbers are tight, so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep reminding both teams: hands-off pressure only — tapping at the ball, never grabbing or pushing.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you protect the ball when a defender was close?</li><li>How did shielding with your body (not your hands) make a difference?</li><li>What did you notice about giving your partner a fair, honest challenge?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps body between the ball and defender</li><li>Uses the non-dribbling arm to shield, not push</li><li>Changes pace/direction rather than freezing under pressure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start defenders further back (2m) and only close in once the dribbler is confident; allow a 'no-defender' version for students still consolidating basic control.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Two-Way Passer":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Chest &amp; bounce passing accuracy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your pass needs to land so your partner can catch it easily, not just where it's easiest to throw from.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passing lanes should not cross other pairs' lanes — space grids out</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to throw an accurate chest pass and bounce pass with two hands.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can push a chest pass out from my chest with two hands, stepping toward my target</li><li>I can bounce a pass so it arrives at my partner's waist height</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Dribble Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'swap hands!' — students continue dribbling but switch to their other hand without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up ball-handling before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Grip the ball with thumbs behind it, fingers spread.</td><td>Even contact gives a consistent, accurate release.</td></tr><tr><td>Step toward your target as you push the pass out.</td><td>The step adds power and directs the pass on line.</td></tr><tr><td>For a bounce pass, aim the ball to bounce roughly two-thirds of the way to the target.</td><td>This brings the ball up to a comfortable waist-height catch.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall of Targets</h4><p>Partners chest pass through a small cone gate at chest height from increasing distances, scoring a point each time the ball goes cleanly through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with roughly 3-4m between partners, one ball per pair, plus a set of 2 cones per pair set upright about 1m apart at chest height (or use a hoop/marker as the 'gate') to pass through.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Thumbs behind the ball, push straight out, step toward your target as you release</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gate, closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gate, further apart</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the chest pass: thumbs behind the ball, push straight out from the chest, stepping toward the target as the ball is released.</li><li>Partners stand either side of the cone gate and take turns chest-passing the ball cleanly through the gate to each other.</li><li>Score a point each time the ball passes cleanly through the gate without touching a cone.</li><li>Every 4-5 successful passes, take one step further apart to increase the challenge.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'thumbs behind the ball, push straight out, step toward your target as you release.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the gate and stand closer together; for 'harder', narrow the gate and stand further apart.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bounce to a Target</h4><p>Partners bounce pass to each other, aiming for the ball to bounce roughly two-thirds of the way between them and rise to waist height.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and spacing as Wall of Targets, one ball per pair, with an optional hoop or cone marker placed roughly two-thirds of the way between partners as a bounce-spot target.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Push down and out, not straight down — aim for the bounce spot, not your partner's feet</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer distance, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Greater distance, add a target hoop for the bounce spot</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the bounce pass: push the ball down and out (not straight down), aiming for a spot roughly two-thirds of the way to your partner so it rises to about waist height when it arrives.</li><li>Partners take turns bounce-passing to each other, aiming for the marked bounce spot if one is used.</li><li>If the ball bounces too close or too far and arrives too high or too low, the pair adjusts their aim on the next attempt rather than repeating the same mistake.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'push down and out, not straight down — aim for the bounce spot, not your partner's feet.'</li><li>For 'easier', stand closer together and pass at a slower pace; for 'harder', stand further apart and require the bounce to land inside a hoop marker.</li></ul><hr><h4>Choose Your Pass</h4><p>Partners alternate between chest and bounce passes based on a called instruction, building quick recognition of when to use each.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and spacing as the earlier two games, one ball per pair, no extra equipment needed since the call comes verbally from a partner or the teacher.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide the pass type before you catch, not after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer calls, more time to decide</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick-fire calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that partners will alternate between chest and bounce passes, but only on a called instruction ('chest!' or 'bounce!').</li><li>One partner calls the pass type just before throwing; the thrower must execute that exact pass type correctly.</li><li>Swap who is calling after every 4-5 passes so both partners practise deciding and both practise reacting to a call.</li><li>If the wrong pass type is thrown, the pair simply resets and tries the same call again.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide the pass type before you catch, not after.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer calls with more time to decide; for 'harder', fire calls quickly one after another.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Two-Pass-Type Basketball</h3><p>4v4, small court, no hoops — teams must use both a chest pass and a bounce pass at least once during a passing sequence before scoring, encouraging a full passing toolkit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, no hoops needed. One ball per game, bibs in two colours to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before a team can score (reaching a marked scoring zone or completing a set passing sequence), they must use both a chest pass and a bounce pass somewhere in that same possession.</li><li>Play begins with one team in possession, moving the ball with a mix of passing and light movement, no dribble-heavy play required.</li><li>If the ball is turned over before both pass types have been used, possession simply switches to the other team and their own sequence starts fresh.</li><li>Encourage players to call out which pass type they're about to throw, connecting back to the communication practised in Choose Your Pass.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep celebrating any successful sequence that uses both pass types, regardless of whether it leads to a score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What technique helped your passes go where you wanted?</li><li>When might a bounce pass be more useful than a chest pass?</li><li>What happens to the game when passes aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Thumbs behind the ball, not underneath or beside it</li><li>Steps toward the target as they release</li><li>Chooses an appropriate pass type for the distance/situation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Shorten distances for students still building arm strength/technique; the gate/target structures scale naturally by moving cones closer.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Catch & Turn":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Receiving a pass &amp; pivoting</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself and encouraging teammates while catching and pivoting confidence builds — everyone's hands and feet look different at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passes should stay below head height; no contact during pivoting practice — hands-off only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to receive a pass with strong hands and pivot on our landing foot to protect the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can show target hands and catch with both hands before bringing the ball in close</li><li>I can pivot on one foot without dragging or lifting it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Dribble Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'swap hands!' — students continue dribbling but switch to their other hand without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up ball-handling before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Show target hands early so the passer has a clear aim.</td><td>Speeds up the passer's decision and improves catch accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Catch with both hands and bring the ball in close.</td><td>Secures possession immediately against a defender's reach.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your pivot foot glued to the floor while turning your body around it.</td><td>This is the basketball travelling rule — lifting or dragging the pivot foot is a violation.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Target Hands Catch</h4><p>Partners pass; the receiver must show clear target hands (fingers spread, hands up) before every pass is thrown, then bring the ball in close to the body.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart, one ball per pair, enough space between pairs that passes don't cross into a neighbour's lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Show your hands early so your partner has an obvious target to aim for</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passes with more pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'target hands' — fingers spread, hands held up and out as a clear target — before every pass is thrown.</li><li>The receiver shows target hands before their partner throws; the passer waits for that signal before releasing the pass.</li><li>On the catch, the receiver brings the ball in close to the body immediately rather than leaving it out at arm's length.</li><li>Swap who is receiving after every 4-5 passes so both partners get equal practice showing target hands and catching.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'show your hands early so your partner has an obvious target to aim for.'</li><li>For 'easier', throw passes more slowly; for 'harder', add more pace to the pass.</li></ul><hr><h4>Static Pivot Practice</h4><p>Students catch a gentle pass, land, and practise pivoting 180 degrees on their landing foot without dragging it, then pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and spacing, one ball per pair, plenty of room around each pair so a 180-degree pivot doesn't clip a neighbour.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your pivot foot glued to the same spot as you spin your body around it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller pivot (90°)</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full 180° pivot, added speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate landing on catching the ball, then pivoting 180 degrees on the landing foot while keeping that same foot glued to the spot.</li><li>One partner passes gently; the receiver catches, lands, and pivots a full 180 degrees without dragging or lifting their pivot foot.</li><li>Once turned, the receiver passes back to their partner from the new facing direction.</li><li>If the pivot foot drags or lifts, the pair repeats that same pivot attempt rather than moving on.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your pivot foot glued to the same spot as you spin your body around it.'</li><li>For 'easier', practise a smaller 90-degree pivot; for 'harder', use the full 180-degree pivot with added speed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Pivot</h4><p>A shadow defender stands nearby (not touching) while the receiver catches, lands, and pivots to shield the ball with their body before passing away from the defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs, plus one extra student per pair acting as a light shadow defender (groups of 3 rotating through), one ball shared within each trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your body and the ball between you and the defender as you turn</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to move around the pivoter</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The passer throws gently to the receiver, who catches and lands as practised in Static Pivot Practice.</li><li>A shadow defender stands nearby without touching, applying visual pressure only — no reaching in.</li><li>The receiver pivots to shield the ball with their body, keeping it and their body between themselves and the defender, before passing away from the defender's side.</li><li>Rotate roles (passer, receiver, defender) every couple of minutes so everyone practises each part.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your body and the ball between you and the defender as you turn.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the defender further away; for 'harder', let the defender move around the pivoter.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Secure Catch Basketball</h3><p>4v4, small court, light shadow defence only — teams score for maintaining clean, secure catches and legal pivots under mild pressure, building toward full defending in later lessons.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, no hoops needed, one ball per game, bibs in two colours, with light shadow defenders only (no ball contact).</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the focus: this game rewards clean, secure catches and legal pivots rather than scoring — defenders may only shadow, not touch the ball.</li><li>Play begins with one team passing the ball around, using target hands and clean pivots practised in today's games to maintain possession under mild pressure.</li><li>Every catch should be followed, where appropriate, by a controlled pivot to shield the ball before the next pass.</li><li>If a catch is fumbled or a pivot foot drags, possession simply resets to the nearest teammate so play keeps flowing without a big stoppage.</li><li>Rotate which four students are on court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep coaching feedback focused on catching and pivoting technique — this lesson builds toward full defending in Block 3.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did showing target hands do to help your partner pass to you?</li><li>How did pivoting help you protect the ball after catching it?</li><li>What made pivoting under a bit of pressure trickier?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Shows clear target hands before the ball arrives</li><li>Catches with two hands and secures the ball close to the body</li><li>Pivots without dragging or lifting the landing foot</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a slightly underinflated or softer ball for students who find catching difficult, so the pivoting focus isn't lost to a hard catch.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Skills & Shooting (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Give and Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing on the move (give-and-go)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role and where you fit in the play — knowing when to move and why.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passing lanes clear of other groups when running through space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball and immediately move into space to receive it back.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass and then run into space, not stand still</li><li>I can time my pass so a moving teammate can control it easily</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Give-and-Go Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth (chest or bounce pass) while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pass, then move immediately into new space.</td><td>Staying still after passing removes you as a future option and makes you easy to guard.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your run to receive the return pass in stride.</td><td>Arriving too early or late forces an awkward, stationary catch.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate ('give and go!') so your teammate knows to return it.</td><td>Speeds up decision-making in a fast-moving situation.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall Pass Relay</h4><p>Pairs practise 'pass and move': Player A passes to Player B and immediately jogs to a new cone; B controls and passes back to A's new position. Repeat down a channel.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread along a marked channel roughly 10m long and 2m wide, one ball per pair, with a line of 3-4 cones down the middle marking spots for Player B to receive at.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move immediately after passing, don't watch the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a defender jogging alongside (not intercepting yet)</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Player A passes to Player B, then immediately jogs to a new position further down the channel rather than standing still and admiring the pass.</li><li>Player B controls the ball and passes it to Player A's new position, then moves themselves to a new spot.</li><li>Repeat this pass-and-move pattern all the way down the channel and back.</li><li>If a pass is misjudged because a player hasn't moved yet, the pair simply resets at their last good position and continues.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move immediately after passing, don't watch the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', walk through the pattern instead of jogging; for 'harder', add a defender jogging alongside without intercepting yet.</li></ul><hr><h4>Give and Go Gates</h4><p>In trios, two attackers combine give-and-go passes to move the ball through a series of gates down the court, racing another trio.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread along a course of 4-5 cone gates (each gate 1.5-2m wide) set out over roughly 15-20m, one ball per trio, with two parallel courses if space allows so trios can race.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pass early, before you're under real pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gates/no time pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gates, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that two attackers in the trio must combine give-and-go passes (pass then move into space) to move the ball through each gate in turn.</li><li>The third student acts as timekeeper/scorekeeper for their trio, or rotates in after the first run.</li><li>Attackers move the ball gate to gate purely by passing — no dribbling through the gates — moving into space immediately after every pass.</li><li>The trio completes the course when the ball has passed cleanly through every gate; race another trio if courses are side by side.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pass early, before you're under real pressure.'</li><li>For 'easier', use wider gates with no time pressure; for 'harder', narrow the gates and time the run.</li></ul><hr><h4>1-2 vs Shadow Defender</h4><p>Pairs attempt a give-and-go against one light-touch defender who can only intercept after the first pass is played (delayed pressure).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) spread across a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, enough grids for the whole class working at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The 'give' player should move into space immediately, not just stand and admire the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender starts further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close immediately</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the defender may only intercept after the first pass has been played, not on the first attacker's initial touch (delayed pressure).</li><li>The two attackers attempt a give-and-go — one passes, immediately moves into space, and looks to receive the return pass past the defender.</li><li>If the give-and-go succeeds (the moving player receives the ball past the defender), reset and try again from the start.</li><li>If the defender intercepts or the pass is misplaced, the group resets with attackers and defender swapping who defends.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'the give player should move into space immediately, not just stand and admire the pass.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender starts further back; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to close in immediately.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3v3 + 1 Target Player Basketball</h3><p>Small-sided game with small hoops/targets; encourage give-and-go combinations as a way to beat defenders, especially near the target player position. Coach highlights any successful give-and-go in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 7-8 for a 3v3 + 1 target player game on a court roughly 15m x 12m with small hoops or cone-marked target zones at each end, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target player role: one neutral or rotating player stands near each scoring target and can be passed to, but doesn't otherwise defend.</li><li>Play begins with one team building possession, using give-and-go combinations (from today's games) to move past defenders and combine with the target player.</li><li>Encourage attackers to look for the moving player after a pass, just as practised in the skill games, especially near the target player position.</li><li>If a give-and-go move works and leads to a score or a clean pass to the target player, call it out ('nice give-and-go!') so the whole group sees it recognised.</li><li>Rotate the target player role and defending/attacking teams every few minutes so everyone experiences each part.</li><li>Keep the coaching focus on spotting and rewarding successful give-and-go combinations, not just final scores.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a give-and-go work well today?</li><li>Why does moving after your pass matter?</li><li>Can you describe a moment you found space at the right time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves immediately after passing</li><li>Receives the return pass without needing to stop and reset</li><li>Communicates (calls for the ball) with a teammate</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole combination down to walking pace for students still building the timing/decision sequence; speed increases as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Perfect Form":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting form (set shot / lay-up steps)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising a skill faithfully, with focus and effort, even when it doesn't work first time — using your talent well takes repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Shooters should have clear space in front of them; rotate turns so no one is standing directly under the hoop while others shoot</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct shooting technique for a set shot, and the basic footwork steps for a lay-up.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold a balanced set shot stance with the ball above my head, elbow in</li><li>I can perform the simple 'step-step-shoot' footwork pattern for a lay-up</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Give-and-Go Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth (chest or bounce pass) while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Hold a balanced stance, ball above the head, elbow tucked in.</td><td>A consistent base and elbow position is what makes a shot repeatable.</td></tr><tr><td>Use the step-step-shoot footwork pattern for a lay-up.</td><td>This footwork pattern is the legal, balanced way to approach a lay-up.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through with fingers, not the palm.</td><td>Controls the ball's backspin and improves accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Balanced Stance Practice</h4><p>Students practise the set shot stance (feet shoulder-width, ball held above the head with fingers spread, elbow tucked in) without shooting yet, focusing purely on set-up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually across the space, one ball per student, each with about 2m of personal room to set up their shooting stance without a hoop needed yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Elbow in, eyes on the target, ball resting on your fingertips not your palm</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider stance, more time to set up</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker set-up, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the set shot stance: feet shoulder-width apart, ball held above the head resting on the fingertips (not the palm), elbow tucked in under the ball, eyes on an imaginary target.</li><li>Students set up in this stance individually, holding the position (without shooting) for a few seconds while checking their own elbow and hand position.</li><li>Walk around checking form — common fixes are elbow flaring out or the ball resting flat on the palm instead of the fingertips.</li><li>Students reset and repeat the stance-only set-up several times, focusing purely on getting the shape right before any shooting is added later in the lesson.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'elbow in, eyes on the target, ball resting on your fingertips not your palm.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a wider stance and more time to set up; for 'harder', time how quickly they can set up correctly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Step-Step-Shoot Walkthrough</h4><p>Students walk through the basic lay-up footwork pattern (two controlled steps toward the hoop, then a gentle release) without a defender, focusing purely on the foot sequence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually with about 3-4m of clear space each to walk through footwork, no ball needed at first, then one ball per student once added.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Same-side foot as your shooting hand takes the last, higher step</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower walk-through, no ball at first</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add the ball and a light jog approach</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the basic lay-up footwork: two controlled steps toward the hoop (or an imaginary hoop), with the same-side foot as the shooting hand taking the last, higher step, followed by a gentle upward release.</li><li>Students first walk through the two-step pattern with no ball, focusing purely on foot sequence and rhythm.</li><li>Once the footwork feels comfortable, add the ball: students walk through the same two steps while holding the ball, finishing with a soft release motion (no jumping or shooting at a real hoop yet if space is tight).</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough several times at walking pace before any speed is added.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'same-side foot as your shooting hand takes the last, higher step.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep it a slow walkthrough with no ball at first; for 'harder', add the ball and a light jog approach.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shot Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a shooting station, taking quick-fire set shots from a fixed spot; the group tracks consecutive 'clean technique' shots as a team streak (technique counts even if it misses).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 rotate through shooting stations at hoops/targets, one ball per group, with a fixed shooting spot marked by a cone at each station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your technique each shot, don't chase power</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer range, bigger target</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further range, smaller target zones worth bonus points</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each student takes a quick-fire set shot from the fixed spot, one after another, cycling through the group.</li><li>The group tracks a shared 'clean technique' streak — a shot counts toward the streak if the stance and release looked technically correct, even if it misses the hoop.</li><li>If a shot breaks technique (elbow flares, rushed release), the streak resets to zero and the group starts counting again.</li><li>Rotate through the group several times, celebrating when the group beats its own best streak.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'reset your technique each shot, don't chase power.'</li><li>For 'easier', shoot from closer range at a bigger target; for 'harder', shoot from further out with smaller target zones worth bonus points.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Shooting Basketball</h3><p>4v4 with hoops/targets; emphasis in coaching feedback is on clean set shot or lay-up technique, not just outcome — celebrate good technique attempts even if they miss.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with hoops or marked targets at each end, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for this game: clean set shot or lay-up technique matters more than whether the shot actually goes in.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with both teams attacking their target hoop and defending the other using skills built up so far in the unit.</li><li>Whenever a player attempts a shot with good technique (balanced stance or correct lay-up footwork), the coach calls it out and celebrates it regardless of the outcome.</li><li>If a shot misses but technique was clean, treat it exactly like a successful shot in terms of praise and feedback.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time and shooting opportunities.</li><li>Keep reminding players: good technique on every attempt beats rushing for power or forcing a shot.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'good technique' feel like when you shoot well?</li><li>What part of your shooting or lay-up technique are you most proud of today?</li><li>What will you keep practising?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds a balanced stance with the ball above the head, elbow in</li><li>Uses the step-step-shoot footwork pattern for a lay-up</li><li>Follows through with fingers, not the palm</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a lower hoop or closer distance for students building arm strength or confidence around shooting; success is measured by technique, not outcome.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Spot Shooter":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting accuracy from varied spots</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your shot will have the most impact rather than just reacting or always shooting from the same spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones marking shooting spots</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Rotate shooting spots so no one crosses in front of another shooter mid-attempt</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to shoot accurately from a range of spots around the hoop, not just one favourite position.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a target area before I shoot</li><li>I can adjust my technique slightly depending on my distance and angle to the hoop</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Give-and-Go Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth (chest or bounce pass) while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Reset your stance fully for every new shooting spot.</td><td>Reusing an old angle from a new position throws off your aim.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose your target area before you shoot.</td><td>Aiming at a specific spot is more accurate than shooting at the whole hoop generally.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your technique slightly for distance and angle, not your whole shot.</td><td>Small adjustments keep your shot consistent across positions.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Five Spot Shootout</h4><p>Students rotate through five marked spots around the hoop (varying angle and distance), taking one shot from each before rotating.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark five shooting spots around each hoop/target using cones — varying both distance and angle — one ball per student or pair sharing a hoop, enough hoops/targets for small groups to rotate through.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your stance for each new spot — don't reuse the same angle from a different position</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Spots closer together, closer to the hoop</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Spots further out, wider angles</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk students through the five marked spots, pointing out how each has a different angle and distance to the hoop.</li><li>Students take one shot from Spot 1, then move to Spot 2 and shoot, continuing around all five spots in order.</li><li>Encourage a quick reset of stance and aim at each new spot rather than rushing straight into the shot.</li><li>Once all five spots are done, the student (or pair) rotates out and the next student begins the same sequence.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'reset your stance for each new spot — don't reuse the same angle from a different position.'</li><li>For 'easier', place spots closer together and nearer the hoop; for 'harder', place spots further out at wider angles.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call Your Spot</h4><p>A partner calls out which of the five spots to shoot from next, building quick adaptation between different shooting angles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same five marked spots per hoop, students working in pairs, one ball per pair, one partner calling while the other shoots.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your target and adjust your feet before you catch the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, more time to reset</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick-fire calls between spots</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner calls out which of the five spots to shoot from next (e.g. 'Spot 3!'), rather than the shooter choosing their own order.</li><li>The shooter moves to that spot, resets their stance and aim, and takes the shot before the next call comes.</li><li>Swap roles after 5-6 calls so both partners get equal shooting and calling turns.</li><li>If the shooter moves to the wrong spot, they simply correct and shoot from the right one before the next call.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide your target and adjust your feet before you catch the pass.'</li><li>For 'easier', call more slowly with time to reset; for 'harder', fire calls quickly between spots.</li></ul><hr><h4>Spot Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups compete to make the most clean shots across all five spots combined within a time limit, encouraging consistency everywhere, not just one strong spot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 share a hoop with the same five marked spots, one ball per group, with a stopwatch or set time limit (e.g. 3 minutes) for the whole activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't rush your best spot — every spot deserves the same careful technique</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer spots, more time</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>All five spots, tighter time limit</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the group works together to make as many clean shots as possible across all five spots combined within the time limit.</li><li>Students rotate through the group, each taking a shot from a spot of their choice, then moving aside for the next teammate.</li><li>Every made shot from any spot adds to the group's combined total — encourage consistency across all spots rather than only shooting from a favourite one.</li><li>Keep a running tally out loud so the group knows how they're tracking against the clock.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'don't rush your best spot — every spot deserves the same careful technique.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer spots and more time; for 'harder', use all five spots with a tighter time limit.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Spot Value Basketball</h3><p>4v4 with marked shooting spots worth different point values (further/harder angles worth more) — teams choose which spots to attack based on what's open, rewarding accuracy from a range of positions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with hoops/targets, each with 5 marked shooting spots worth different point values (closer/easier spots worth less, further/harder angles worth more), one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring system: shots made from further or harder-angle spots earn more points than closer, easier spots, based on today's five-spot markings.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with both teams choosing which spots to attack based on what space and angle the defence gives up.</li><li>Encourage attacking teams to read the defence and select a spot that's open, rather than always driving for the same easy spot.</li><li>Keep score visibly (a simple tally on a whiteboard or called out loud) so students see the value of range and accuracy over just getting close.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach highlights good shot selection — choosing an open spot with good value — as much as the shot itself going in.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which spot did you find easiest, and which was hardest? Why do you think that is?</li><li>What did you adjust about your technique from different spots?</li><li>How did thinking ahead about your target area help your shooting?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Adjusts stance/angle for different spots</li><li>Chooses a target area before shooting</li><li>Maintains consistent technique across varied positions</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce the number of spots or move them closer to the hoop for students building confidence; the five-spot idea can scale down to three for a simpler version.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Triple Threat":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining dribble → pass → shoot</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual skills (dribble, pass, shoot) fit together with your team's play for a shared outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games small-sided (3v3 max) so the combined sequence comes thick and fast without overcrowding</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine dribbling, passing, and shooting smoothly in one sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dribble into space, then choose to pass or shoot based on what's open</li><li>I can move from a dribble into a shot without stopping awkwardly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Give-and-Go Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth (chest or bounce pass) while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Dribble with your head up to read what's actually open.</td><td>The sequence needs to respond to the game, not run on autopilot.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide early whether you're passing or shooting so your body can prepare.</td><td>Last-second indecision leads to a rushed, poor-technique action.</td></tr><tr><td>Move smoothly from one phase to the next without stopping the ball completely.</td><td>A stopped dribble gives defenders time to set up and close you down.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Dribble-Pass-Shoot Drill</h4><p>Player dribbles toward the hoop, then either passes to a moving teammate or pulls up for a shot, all in one continuous sequence, walked through slowly first.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread individually or in small groups approaching a hoop/target from roughly 8-10m out, one ball per student, cones marking the starting point and a decision point about 4m from the hoop.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Each part flows into the next — don't pause awkwardly between the dribble and your decision</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time between parts</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, less time</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk through the sequence slowly first: dribble from the start cone toward the hoop, then at the decision-point cone either pass to a moving teammate or pull up for a shot.</li><li>Students dribble in under control, keeping the ball low, and make their decision only once they reach the decision-point cone.</li><li>If passing, the receiving teammate should already be moving into space; if shooting, the student sets their stance quickly using today's earlier shooting technique.</li><li>Repeat the sequence several times, alternating between the pass option and the shoot option so both get practised.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'each part flows into the next — don't pause awkwardly between the dribble and your decision.'</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace down with more time between parts; for 'harder', increase pace and reduce decision time.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 Combo Challenge</h4><p>Two attackers against one defender — the ball-handler must combine a dribble, and then a pass or shot, to beat the defender's numbers disadvantage.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) in a grid roughly 10m x 8m with a small hoop or target at one end, one ball per group, enough grids for the whole class.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If the shot isn't on, the extra pass usually is — read it quickly</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower grid, faster defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: two attackers face only one defender, so there should always be an open option somewhere.</li><li>The ball-handler dribbles to engage the defender, then reads whether to pass to the free teammate or shoot themselves.</li><li>If the defender commits to the ball-handler, the pass to the open teammate should usually be the better read; if the defender hangs back, a shot may be on.</li><li>After each attempt (score or turnover), rotate which student defends so everyone gets attacking and defending turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if the shot isn't on, the extra pass usually is — read it quickly.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider grid with a slower defender; for 'harder', use a narrower grid with a faster defender.</li></ul><hr><h4>Continuous Combo Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where every scoring attempt must be preceded by a visible dribble-then-decision sequence, rewarding whichever team keeps combining the skills successfully.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups (3v3 or 4v4) in a grid roughly 12m x 10m with a hoop or target at each end, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always be thinking one step ahead — dribble, then what's my best option?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every scoring attempt must be preceded by a visible dribble followed by a decision (pass or shoot) — no scoring straight off a stationary catch.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where teams look to combine dribbling to create an opening, then either pass to a better-placed teammate or shoot themselves.</li><li>If a team scores without the required dribble-then-decision sequence, the score doesn't count and possession resets to the other team.</li><li>Keep play continuous, resetting quickly after any stoppage so the combination habit gets lots of repetitions.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always be thinking one step ahead — dribble, then what's my best option?'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with more time; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with faster decisions required.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 4v3 Combination Basketball</h3><p>Attacking team has a numbers advantage (4v3); success depends on combining today's three skills — dribbling to create an opening, then reading whether to pass or shoot cleanly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 7 for a 4v3 game (attacking team has the extra player) on a court roughly 15m x 12m with a hoop or target, one ball per game, bibs in two colours to show which team is attacking.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: the attacking team has four players against three defenders, so there should always be a free option if the ball moves well.</li><li>The attacking team works the ball using dribbling to create openings, then reads whether to pass to a free teammate or shoot, exactly as practised in today's games.</li><li>If the attack scores or the defence wins the ball back, rotate one attacker to become a defender (and vice versa) so teams stay fresh, or simply swap which team attacks after a set number of turns.</li><li>Encourage defenders to communicate and cover the extra attacker as best they can, even though they're outnumbered.</li><li>Coach highlights good combination play — dribble to create the opening, then a clean pass or shot — regardless of the final score.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone experiences both the numbers-up attacking role and the numbers-down defending role.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did dribbling, passing, and shooting work together today?</li><li>What happened when you tried to rush a decision instead of reading the situation?</li><li>What's one moment you combined all three skills well?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Dribble creates a genuine opening, not just movement for its own sake</li><li>Reads pass vs shoot appropriately</li><li>Sequence flows without an awkward pause</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce numbers (e.g. 1v0 with a feeder) for students still building confidence combining three skills at once, before scaling up to 2v1 or 4v3.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Pivot Escape":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Footwork — pivoting to escape pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Looking after your opponent by defending fairly and safely, and understanding your own footwork choices when under pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact — hands-off defending only; stay upright and controlled while pivoting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use pivoting footwork to protect the ball and find an escape option when pressured.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pivot in more than one direction to keep the ball away from a defender</li><li>I can find a passing option while pivoting under pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Give-and-Go Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth (chest or bounce pass) while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your pivot foot fixed on the floor.</td><td>Legal pivoting depends on that foot staying planted.</td></tr><tr><td>Turn your body and shield the ball away from the defender's pressure.</td><td>Uses your body as a barrier while you look for an escape option.</td></tr><tr><td>Look for an escape pass or dribble lane as you pivot.</td><td>Pivoting should create an option, not just delay the inevitable.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Multi-Direction Pivot</h4><p>Students catch a pass, land, and practise pivoting in several directions (not just one 180° turn) around their landing foot, scanning for an imaginary passing option each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with roughly 2-3m of space each, one ball per pair, enough room around each pair to pivot freely in any direction without clipping a neighbour.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your pivot foot planted and let your body/eyes do the scanning</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer pivot directions</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full range of pivot directions, added speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner passes gently; the receiver catches, lands, and establishes their pivot foot as practised in earlier lessons.</li><li>Instead of only one 180-degree turn, the receiver practises pivoting in several different directions in sequence (e.g. quarter-turn left, quarter-turn right, half-turn), each time as if scanning for an imaginary passing option.</li><li>The pivot foot must stay planted through every direction change — only the body and free foot move around it.</li><li>Swap roles after each student has had several pivot sequences so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your pivot foot planted and let your body/eyes do the scanning.'</li><li>For 'easier', practise fewer pivot directions; for 'harder', use the full range of directions with added speed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Pivot</h4><p>A shadow defender applies light, hands-off pressure while the receiver pivots to shield the ball and find a passing lane away from the defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs, plus one extra shadow defender per pair (trios rotating through), one ball shared within each trio, similar spacing to Multi-Direction Pivot.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pivot away from the defender's side, toward your open teammate</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to move around the pivoter</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The passer throws to the receiver, who catches, lands, and establishes a pivot foot.</li><li>A shadow defender applies light, hands-off pressure nearby — close enough to be a real presence, but never touching or reaching for the ball.</li><li>The receiver pivots to shield the ball with their body and scans for a passing lane away from the defender's side before passing.</li><li>Rotate roles (passer, receiver, defender) every couple of minutes so everyone practises each part.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pivot away from the defender's side, toward your open teammate.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender stays further away; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to move around the pivoter.</li></ul><hr><h4>Trapped Corner Escape</h4><p>Player receives the ball in a tight corner grid with a defender nearby and must pivot to find any legal passing option to escape the pressure, racing the clock.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a small corner grid roughly 3m x 3m using cones at one corner of the playing space, one ball and one defender per attacker, a stopwatch or simple count for timing.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay calm — a good pivot buys you time to find an option</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger corner grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tighter corner, quicker defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacker receives the ball while standing in the tight corner grid, with a defender positioned nearby applying hands-off pressure.</li><li>Set a short time limit (e.g. 5-8 seconds) for the attacker to pivot and find any legal passing option to escape the pressure.</li><li>The attacker uses pivoting and shielding from today's earlier games to protect the ball and locate an open passing lane before the time runs out.</li><li>If the attacker successfully passes out within the time limit, that's a win for that attempt; if not, reset and try again with a fresh time count.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay calm — a good pivot buys you time to find an option.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger corner grid with a slower defender; for 'harder', use a tighter corner with a quicker defender.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pivot Under Pressure Basketball</h3><p>4v4 — every player who catches the ball must legally pivot at least once (protecting it from a nearby defender) before passing or shooting, reinforcing composed footwork under real pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court roughly 15m x 12m, no hoops needed for this focus (or use hoops if available), one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every player who catches the ball must legally pivot at least once — protecting it from a nearby defender — before passing or shooting.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with both teams applying hands-off defensive pressure as players catch and look to move the ball on.</li><li>If a player passes or shoots without pivoting first (when a defender was applying pressure), that's a gentle reminder moment rather than a hard stoppage — coach cues the habit in the moment.</li><li>Keep play flowing continuously, resetting quickly after any turnover or out-of-bounds moment.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach highlights composed pivoting under pressure as the main thing to notice and praise during this game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made pivoting under pressure tricky today?</li><li>How did a good pivot buy you time to find an option?</li><li>What's the difference between pivoting calmly and panicking under pressure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps the pivot foot planted, no dragging/lifting</li><li>Shields the ball with body positioning while pivoting</li><li>Finds a legal option (pass or dribble start) rather than panicking</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole sequence to walking pace and use a visible cone as the defender's marker for students still building the pivot-under-pressure concept.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Defending, Attacking & Positional Play (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Wall Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — stance &amp; staying in front</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills fairly to give the attacker a genuine, respectful contest without crowding or contacting them.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact and no reaching in to grab the ball — hands-off defending, arm's length distance maintained at all times</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a strong, balanced defensive stance and how to stay in front of an attacker.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold a low, balanced defensive stance with knees bent and feet apart</li><li>I can slide my feet to stay in front of a moving attacker, without crossing my feet</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Defence Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them, echoing the defensive slide focus from this block.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible, using a low, athletic stance where they can.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay low, weight on the balls of your feet.</td><td>A low, balanced stance lets you react instantly to a change of direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Slide your feet rather than crossing them.</td><td>Crossing your feet is slower to recover from and can be beaten easily.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep an arm's length distance from the attacker.</td><td>Close enough to contest, far enough to react to a move.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Stance Check</h4><p>Students practise the low defensive stance (knees bent, feet shoulder-width, hands up and active) while a partner checks their form, holding the stance for short bursts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with about 2m each, no ball needed for this game, enough space for each pair to work without bumping neighbours.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low with your weight on the balls of your feet, ready to move in any direction</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter holds, more rest</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer holds, added movement</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the low defensive stance: knees bent, feet shoulder-width apart, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up and active in front of the body.</li><li>One partner gets into the stance and holds it for a short burst (e.g. 10-15 seconds) while the other partner checks their knee bend, foot width, and hand position.</li><li>Swap roles so both partners get a turn holding the stance and a turn checking form.</li><li>If the stance drifts (knees straighten, feet come together), the student resets and holds again for the same short burst.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay low with your weight on the balls of your feet, ready to move in any direction.'</li><li>For 'easier', use shorter holds with more rest between; for 'harder', hold for longer and add small side-to-side movement while maintaining the stance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Slide &amp; Stay</h4><p>An attacker moves side to side (no ball) along a line while the defender slides their feet to stay directly in front, without crossing their feet or standing up out of the stance.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs work along a marked line roughly 5-6m long using cones at each end, no ball needed, one attacker and one defender per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Slide, don't cross your feet — a crossed step is a moment you can be beaten</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower attacker movement</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable movement</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacker moves side to side along the line (walking or light jogging, no ball), while the defender mirrors them using the low defensive stance and sliding footwork.</li><li>The defender's feet must slide (step-together-step) rather than cross over — a crossed step is treated as 'beaten' for that moment and the pair simply continues.</li><li>The defender should stay directly in front of the attacker at all times, matching their side-to-side movement without standing upright out of the stance.</li><li>Swap roles after 45-60 seconds so both partners get attacking and defending turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'slide, don't cross your feet — a crossed step is a moment you can be beaten.'</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker moves more slowly and predictably; for 'harder', the attacker moves faster and less predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Stay in Front</h4><p>Attacker dribbles in a small grid trying to get past; defender's only job is to stay in front using good stance and sliding footwork (no reaching for the ball).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark small grids roughly 5m x 5m, one grid per pair, one ball per pair — enough grids spread out so pairs don't interfere with each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Focus on your feet and stance, not on grabbing at the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower attacker</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker attacker</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacker dribbles inside the grid trying to get past the defender to the far side; the defender's only job is to stay in front using the stance and slide footwork from today's earlier games.</li><li>The defender must not reach in or grab at the ball — the focus is purely on positioning and footwork, not stealing.</li><li>If the attacker gets past, they simply reset back to the start and try again; if the defender stays in front for a set time (e.g. 10 seconds), that's a win for the defender.</li><li>Swap roles after each attempt (or every couple of attempts) so both partners get equal attacking and defending turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'focus on your feet and stance, not on grabbing at the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with a slower attacker; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with a quicker attacker.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Stance &amp; Slide Basketball</h3><p>4v4 — coach highlights good defensive stance and sliding footwork in play ('great stance!'), reinforcing that staying in front fairly is as valuable as any steal attempt.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court roughly 15m x 12m, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: good defensive stance and sliding footwork are being watched for and celebrated just as much as any steal or turnover.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with defenders applying hands-off pressure using the stance and slide skills from today's games.</li><li>Whenever a defender stays in front of their attacker using a good low stance and clean sliding footwork, the coach calls it out ('great stance!') regardless of what happens to the ball next.</li><li>Keep reminding players that fair, controlled defending (no reaching, no grabbing) is the standard expected throughout.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach highlights defensive positioning as a core skill being built here, setting up next lesson's closing-out focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a low, balanced stance help you do as a defender today?</li><li>Why does sliding (rather than crossing your feet) matter when staying in front?</li><li>What's the difference between defending fairly and defending too aggressively?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds a low, balanced stance with hands active</li><li>Slides feet without crossing them</li><li>Stays an arm's length away, no reaching or grabbing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow defenders to start a step further back for students still building confidence with the stance and sliding footwork combination.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Close Out":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — closing out on a shooter</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about the safest, most controlled way to close out, rather than just sprinting in as fast as possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact and no touching the ball during a shot attempt — defenders may only contest with a raised hand at a safe distance</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to close out under control on a player about to shoot, without fouling or overrunning them.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can approach a shooter under control, with short, choppy steps as I get close</li><li>I can get my hand up to contest the shot without touching the ball or the shooter</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Defence Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them, echoing the defensive slide focus from this block.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible, using a low, athletic stance where they can.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Approach under control, not at a flat sprint.</td><td>Sprinting in makes you easy to blow past with a simple fake or drive.</td></tr><tr><td>Break down your steps into a low, balanced stance as you arrive.</td><td>Lets you contest the shot without fouling or losing balance.</td></tr><tr><td>Get a hand up to contest without lunging or reaching in.</td><td>Contests the shot legally while staying in defensive position.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Choppy Steps Approach</h4><p>Students practise closing out from a few metres away, using short, choppy steps to slow down under control as they approach an imaginary shooter (a cone).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually, each approaching a cone (standing in for an imaginary shooter) from about 4-5m away, no ball needed for this game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Slow your steps down as you get close — arriving under control beats arriving fast</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter approach distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer approach, added speed early</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate closing out: starting a few metres from the cone and using short, choppy steps to slow down under control as you approach, rather than sprinting all the way in.</li><li>Students practise closing out on their cone repeatedly, focusing on arriving under control with balanced feet rather than rushing.</li><li>The last 1-2 steps before arriving should be noticeably shorter and slower than the first few steps of the approach.</li><li>Repeat several times from slightly different starting distances so students get used to adjusting their choppy-step timing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'slow your steps down as you get close — arriving under control beats arriving fast.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a shorter approach distance; for 'harder', use a longer approach with added speed early on.</li></ul><hr><h4>Hand Up, No Touch</h4><p>A partner holds the ball in a shooting stance; the defender closes out and raises one hand to contest without touching the ball or getting closer than an arm's length.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with about 4-5m between them, one ball per pair, enough space so a closeout approach doesn't cross into another pair's space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One hand up, stay balanced — don't lunge or fly past</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more predictable closeout timing</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, reactive closeout on a cue</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner holds the ball in a shooting stance (from earlier lessons) a few metres away; the other partner practises the closeout approach from Choppy Steps Approach.</li><li>On arrival, the defender raises one hand up to contest the shot without touching the ball, staying at least an arm's length away and balanced (not lunging).</li><li>The 'shooter' can hold their stance or go through a slow shooting motion so the defender practises timing their hand-raise to the shot.</li><li>Swap roles after several closeouts so both partners get equal defending and shooting-stance turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'one hand up, stay balanced — don't lunge or fly past.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more predictable closeout timing; for 'harder', react to a quicker, less predictable cue.</li></ul><hr><h4>Closeout Relay</h4><p>Small groups take turns closing out on a shooter from different starting distances and angles, racing to complete controlled closeouts without fouling.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 taking turns closing out from marked starting cones at varying distances and angles to a central shooter spot, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Read the distance — a closer shooter needs a shorter, quicker approach</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fixed, known distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied, surprise distances</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up 2-3 different starting cones at different distances/angles from the central shooter spot.</li><li>Students take turns starting from a different cone each time, practising a controlled closeout to the shooter spot without fouling (no contact, one hand up only).</li><li>Race as a group to complete a full round of closeouts (one from each starting cone) as cleanly and quickly as possible.</li><li>If a closeout is uncontrolled (defender arrives off-balance or too fast), that attempt is redone before the group's round is considered complete.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'read the distance — a closer shooter needs a shorter, quicker approach.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a fixed, known distance each time; for 'harder', use varied, surprise distances.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Closeout Basketball</h3><p>4v4 with hoops/targets — defenders are coached explicitly on controlled closeouts, with bonus recognition (not points) for a well-controlled, fair contest on a shot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with hoops/targets, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: defenders closing out under control on a shooter earns recognition (not extra points), separate from whether the shot is contested successfully.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with defenders practising controlled closeouts on any attacker who receives the ball in a shooting position.</li><li>Whenever a defender closes out with good, balanced footwork and a fair one-handed contest (no fouling contact), the coach calls it out as a highlight.</li><li>Keep reminding players that a fast but reckless closeout (fouling or flying past) is worse than a slightly slower, controlled one.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to praise defensive stance and sliding footwork from last lesson alongside today's new closeout focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a closeout feel 'under control' rather than rushed?</li><li>How did short, choppy steps help you as you got close to the shooter?</li><li>What's the value of thinking ahead about how you approach, rather than just sprinting in?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Slows down with choppy steps on approach</li><li>Contests with one hand up, no contact</li><li>Stays balanced rather than flying past the shooter</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a stationary cone/marker as the 'shooter' first before adding a real person, so students focus purely on their own footwork and control.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Cut & Clear":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking — creating space (cutting)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding your own timing and decision-making — noticing what changes when your cut clicks into place.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep cutting lanes clear of other groups when running through shared space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to cut into space at the right moment to create a passing or scoring opportunity.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can cut into space when my defender isn't watching or is out of position</li><li>I can time my cut so a teammate can pass to me cleanly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Defence Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them, echoing the defensive slide focus from this block.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible, using a low, athletic stance where they can.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Read the defender's attention before you cut.</td><td>Cutting when a defender is watching the ball, not you, is far more effective.</td></tr><tr><td>Cut hard and decisively, not half-heartedly.</td><td>A sharp cut is what actually creates separation from your defender.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your cut to match when the passer can actually deliver the ball.</td><td>Cutting too early or late wastes the opportunity.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call &amp; Cut</h4><p>A passer calls 'now!' as the cue for their partner to cut sharply into space and receive the pass, building the timing connection between passer and cutter.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per pair, enough grids for the whole class working at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Cut hard on the call, not before or after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more obvious timing</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, subtler timing</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The passer holds the ball while the cutter stands nearby in a starting position.</li><li>The passer calls 'now!' as the cue — on this call, the cutter sprints sharply into open space to receive the pass.</li><li>The passer delivers the ball to the space the cutter is moving into, not to where they started.</li><li>Swap roles after 5-6 repetitions so both partners practise cutting and passing-on-the-call.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'cut hard on the call, not before or after.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more obvious timing; for 'harder', use a faster pace with subtler timing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Gap Cut</h4><p>Cutter watches their defender and cuts into space the moment the defender is out of position or looking away, without a verbal cue.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one light shadow defender (trios rotating through) in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your cut</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender moves predictably</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender moves unpredictably</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The cutter's defender shadows them lightly (no ball contact) while the cutter watches the defender's feet and body weight rather than the ball.</li><li>The moment the defender is out of position, looking away, or shifts their weight badly, the cutter cuts into the open space without any verbal cue.</li><li>The passer reads the cut and delivers the ball the moment the cutter moves.</li><li>Rotate roles (cutter, defender, passer) every couple of minutes so everyone practises reading a defender's positioning.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your cut.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender moves predictably; for 'harder', the defender moves unpredictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Cut Relay</h4><p>Groups of 3 rotate cutting into space to receive a pass, then immediately becoming the next passer, racing other triangles for clean completions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 spread out in a triangle shape roughly 5m per side, one ball per group, enough triangles for the whole class to work at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your cut early — decide your target space before you move</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student passes to a teammate who cuts into space to receive it, then that receiver immediately becomes the next passer for the third teammate to cut.</li><li>Keep the ball moving triangle-style, with a cut happening before every single pass — no standing still to receive.</li><li>Race against another triangle group to see who can complete the most clean pass-and-cut completions in a set time.</li><li>If a cut is mistimed and the pass is missed, the group resets to the last successful position and continues.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'prepare your cut early — decide your target space before you move.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace; for 'harder', use a faster pace with a timed race.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Timed Cut Basketball</h3><p>4v4 with defence — coach highlights well-timed cuts in play ('great cut!'), reinforcing that arriving in space at the right moment is as valuable as the pass itself.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court roughly 15m x 12m with light defence, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: cutting into space at the right moment is being watched for and celebrated just as much as any pass or score.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with full hands-off defence, encouraging attackers to cut into open space using the reading and timing skills from today's games.</li><li>Whenever a player makes a well-timed cut to receive the ball in space, the coach calls it out ('great cut!') regardless of what happens after.</li><li>Keep reminding players that arriving in the right space at the right time is just as valuable as the pass itself.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce defensive stance and closeouts from previous lessons alongside today's cutting focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you time your cut well today?</li><li>How did reading your defender's position help you decide when to cut?</li><li>What happens if you cut too early or too late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Times the cut to arrive as the ball becomes available</li><li>Reads the defender's position before cutting</li><li>Accelerates into space rather than drifting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a verbal cue system (passer calls 'now!') as training wheels for students still building the reading skill, removing the cue as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Pick Me":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Screening / using a teammate to get free</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Relating to others, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your individual role (screener or cutter) fits into a shared team outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Screens must be stationary and legal — no moving into a defender's path at the last second; screener holds a stationary, balanced position</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to set a simple, legal screen to help a teammate get free, and how to use a screen as the player being defended.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand still and hold a legal screening position for a teammate</li><li>I can use a teammate's screen by cutting close by it to lose my defender</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Defence Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them, echoing the defensive slide focus from this block.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible, using a low, athletic stance where they can.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Set a stationary, legal screen with feet planted.</td><td>A moving screen is an illegal, foul situation.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate the screen to your teammate.</td><td>They need to know which way to cut off it.</td></tr><tr><td>Cut off the screen tight and with a change of pace.</td><td>Cutting too wide loses the benefit of the screen entirely.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Statue Screen</h4><p>One student stands completely still (a 'statue') in a marked spot while a partner practises cutting close by the statue to lose an imaginary defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with about 3-4m of space each, no ball needed for this first game, a cone marking where the 'statue' should stand still.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Screener stays still and balanced; cutter brushes close by without contact</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space around the screen</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tighter space, cutter must change direction sharply off the screen</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student (the screener) stands completely still and balanced on the marked spot, arms in and not moving, like a statue.</li><li>The other student (the cutter) practises cutting close by the statue's shoulder to lose an imaginary defender, brushing near but not touching.</li><li>Repeat several times, cutting from different starting angles around the statue.</li><li>Swap roles so both partners practise being the still, balanced screener and the cutter using the screen.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'screener stays still and balanced; cutter brushes close by without contact.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space around the screen; for 'harder', use a tighter space where the cutter must change direction sharply off the screen.</li></ul><hr><h4>Screen &amp; Cut Combo</h4><p>In trios, one player sets a legal, stationary screen for a teammate who is being shadowed by a light-touch defender; the cutter uses the screen to get free and receive a pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread out with about 4-5m of working space, one ball per trio, one student acting as a light-touch shadow defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Cutter should brush shoulder-close to the screen, not go wide around it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts quickly, must fight through or around</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One player sets a legal, stationary screen (as in Statue Screen) for a teammate who is being shadowed by the defender.</li><li>The cutter uses the screen — brushing shoulder-close past it — to lose the shadow defender and get free to receive a pass from the third player.</li><li>The passer delivers the ball the moment the cutter is free of the defender.</li><li>Rotate roles (screener, cutter, defender, passer if a fourth is added) every couple of minutes so everyone practises each part.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'cutter should brush shoulder-close to the screen, not go wide around it.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender reacts slowly; for 'harder', the defender reacts quickly and must fight through or around the screen.</li></ul><hr><h4>Screen Rotation Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where players rotate roles (screener, cutter, defender) every couple of minutes, so everyone practises each part of the screen-and-cut combination.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group, with clearly defined roles (screener, cutter, defender, passer) that rotate.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with your teammate about where you're setting the screen</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer periods per role</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter periods, faster rotation</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a small-sided game where players rotate roles every couple of minutes so everyone practises screening, cutting, and defending against a screen.</li><li>Play continuously, with the screener and cutter combining to get free while the defender tries to stay with their mark using only legal, hands-off effort.</li><li>Every couple of minutes, call a rotation so players swap into a new role within their group.</li><li>If a screen is set illegally (moving into the defender's path at the last second), the group resets and the screener tries again from a stationary position.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate with your teammate about where you're setting the screen.'</li><li>For 'easier', use longer periods per role; for 'harder', use shorter periods with faster rotation.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pick &amp; Roll Basketball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — teams are encouraged to use at least one legal screen per possession to help a teammate get free, with the coach highlighting successful, legal screening plays.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 for a 4v4 or 5v5 game on a court roughly 15m x 12m with hoops if available, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching encouragement: teams are asked to use at least one legal, stationary screen per possession to help a teammate get free.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with attacking teams looking for opportunities to set a screen (as practised today) to create separation from a defender.</li><li>Whenever a legal screen leads to a teammate getting free, the coach highlights it as a successful, fair play — regardless of whether it leads to a score.</li><li>Keep reminding players that a screen must be stationary and set early — no last-second moving into a defender's path.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce cutting timing from last lesson alongside today's screening focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a screen effective (or not) today?</li><li>How did using a teammate's screen help you get free?</li><li>Why do you think a screen needs to be still and legal to work fairly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds a stationary, legal screening position</li><li>Cuts close by the screen rather than wide around it</li><li>Communicates with the teammate about the screen</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Practise the screener's stationary role first without any defender at all, so students build confidence with the legal, still positioning before adding pressure.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Box Out":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rebounding basics (positioning, boxing out)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Contesting a rebound fairly and safely — using your body position, not pushing or grabbing, to compete for the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No pushing, elbowing, or grabbing during boxing out — body contact must be controlled and balanced, not aggressive</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to box out an opponent and position ourselves to collect a rebound.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can turn and make contact with my body (not my arms) to box out an opponent</li><li>I can position myself between my opponent and the hoop before going for the rebound</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Defence Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them, echoing the defensive slide focus from this block.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible, using a low, athletic stance where they can.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Make contact with your opponent and hold your position before going for the ball.</td><td>Positioning first prevents them getting inside position for the rebound.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep a wide, balanced stance with arms up.</td><td>Makes you a bigger target for the ball and harder to move.</td></tr><tr><td>Go get the ball at its highest point, don't wait for it to come to you.</td><td>Attacking the ball wins more rebounds than passively waiting.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Turn and Box</h4><p>Facing a partner, students practise turning to make gentle, balanced body contact (a 'box out') and holding a wide, low stance to claim space in front of the hoop.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other with about 1m of space between them, no ball needed for this first game, enough room for each pair to turn and make contact safely.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Find your opponent, turn, and make contact with your body — don't watch the ball yet</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, walked-through turns</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker turn on a cue</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that boxing out means finding your opponent, turning your back to them, and making gentle, balanced body contact to claim the space in front of an imaginary hoop.</li><li>Partners face each other, then on a cue, one partner turns and makes light, controlled contact with their back/shoulder against their partner, settling into a wide, low stance.</li><li>The student boxing out holds that wide, low position for a few seconds, claiming the space rather than backing away.</li><li>Swap roles so both partners practise turning and boxing out, and being boxed out against.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'find your opponent, turn, and make contact with your body — don't watch the ball yet.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, walked-through turns; for 'harder', turn quickly on a sudden cue.</li></ul><hr><h4>Box Out &amp; Collect</h4><p>A partner tosses the ball off a wall or backboard; the student must box out an imaginary or light-touch opponent, then move to collect the rebound.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs near a wall or backboard (or use a soft toss instead if no backboard is available), one ball per pair, enough space for safe, controlled contact.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Box out first, then go get the ball — don't turn to watch the ball too early</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower tosses, more space</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker tosses, tighter space</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner tosses the ball off a wall or backboard so it rebounds unpredictably, while the other partner practises boxing out an imaginary or light-touch opponent (their partner) first.</li><li>Only after boxing out — turning, making contact, and claiming the space — does the student turn to look for and collect the rebound.</li><li>If the student turns to watch the ball before boxing out, the pair resets and tries the toss again.</li><li>Swap roles after several tosses so both partners get boxing-out and tossing turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'box out first, then go get the ball — don't turn to watch the ball too early.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower tosses with more space; for 'harder', use quicker tosses in a tighter space.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rebound Rumble</h4><p>Small groups compete to box out and collect the most rebounds off a series of tosses or missed shots, tracked as a friendly team tally.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 near a hoop/target or wall, one ball per group, a rough tally system (marks on paper or verbal count) to track rebounds.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low and wide after boxing out — don't stand up straight and lose your position</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players competing for each rebound</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more contested rebounds</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the friendly competition: the group tosses or shoots the ball so it comes off the hoop, backboard, or wall, and everyone competes to box out and collect the rebound.</li><li>Students apply the turn-and-box technique from earlier games before going for the ball, keeping contact controlled and balanced, never aggressive.</li><li>Each successful box-out-then-collect counts as one point for that student on the group's shared tally.</li><li>Rotate who tosses/shoots for the group so everyone gets a fair mix of rebounding attempts.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay low and wide after boxing out — don't stand up straight and lose your position.'</li><li>For 'easier', fewer players compete for each rebound; for 'harder', more players compete for a more contested rebound.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Box Out Basketball</h3><p>4v4 with hoops/targets — every missed shot is a live rebound opportunity; players are coached explicitly to box out before chasing the ball, reinforcing fair, safe positioning over raw scrambling.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with hoops/targets, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: every missed shot is treated as a live rebound opportunity, and players are explicitly coached to box out before chasing the ball.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with both teams applying the box-out technique from today's games whenever a shot goes up.</li><li>Whenever a player boxes out fairly and safely before collecting a rebound, the coach highlights it as good, safe positioning.</li><li>If any contact becomes aggressive (pushing, elbowing, grabbing), stop play immediately and reset with a reminder of the controlled, balanced contact expected.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce screening and cutting from previous lessons alongside today's rebounding focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did boxing out help you do before going for the rebound?</li><li>Why is body positioning more useful than just reaching for the ball?</li><li>What made a rebound contest feel fair and safe today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Turns and makes balanced body contact to box out</li><li>Positions between opponent and hoop before chasing the ball</li><li>Stays low and wide rather than standing up straight</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Practise the box-out turn without any ball or contest first, so students build the positioning habit before adding the scramble for a rebound.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Fast Break":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fast break basics (outnumbering the defence)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Being patient with a team while learning a multi-person pattern — timing takes a few tries to click for everyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking court thirds, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep the court clear of congestion during fast-paced running sections — space players out to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to recognise and use a fast break — moving the ball quickly up the court when we outnumber the defence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can sprint into space immediately after my team gains the ball</li><li>I can recognise when my team has more attackers than defenders and use the advantage</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Push the ball forward quickly after gaining possession.</td><td>The fast break only works if you exploit the defence before it's set.</td></tr><tr><td>Fill the outside lanes when running, not bunching in the middle.</td><td>Spreading out gives the ball handler passing options and stretches the defence.</td></tr><tr><td>Make the decision to pass or finish early based on the numbers.</td><td>Hesitating in a fast break lets the defence recover.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fast Break Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple fast break pattern slowly: after a rebound or steal, players sprint into lanes up the court while one dribbles/passes ahead, before adding any defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole team (or small group) walks through a marked court divided into thirds using cones, one ball per group, no defence yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone has a lane to sprint into — know yours before the ball moves</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk pace only</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light jog</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the fast break concept: after a rebound or steal, players sprint into their own lanes up the court rather than bunching together, while one player dribbles or passes the ball ahead.</li><li>Walk through the pattern slowly first: one player 'collects' an imaginary rebound, then everyone jogs (at walk pace for this first version) into their own lane up the court.</li><li>The ball-carrier dribbles or passes ahead to a teammate who has sprinted into a lane, working toward the far end.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough several times so everyone understands their lane and role before any pace is added.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone has a lane to sprint into — know yours before the ball moves.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep it to walk pace only; for 'harder', add a light jog.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v2 Numbers Up</h4><p>Three attackers push the ball up the court against only two defenders, practising quick decisions on when to pass and who has the advantage.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5 (three attackers, two defenders) on a court roughly 20m long with a hoop or target at the far end, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Spread out into your lanes — a bunched-up fast break loses its advantage</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders start further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders start closer, more realistic recovery speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Three attackers start with the ball and push it up the court against only two defenders, spreading into separate lanes rather than bunching together.</li><li>Attackers read the defenders' positioning and pass quickly to whichever teammate is left open by the numbers disadvantage.</li><li>If a shot or clean finish results, reset and go again with attackers and defenders rotating roles.</li><li>Encourage attackers to make their decision quickly rather than dribbling too long and letting the defence recover.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'spread out into your lanes — a bunched-up fast break loses its advantage.'</li><li>For 'easier', defenders start further back; for 'harder', defenders start closer with more realistic recovery speed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fast Break Race</h4><p>Groups practise executing a clean fast break sequence and finish (pass or shot) as fast and cleanly as possible, racing another group for the fastest clean sequence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two groups of 3-5 racing side by side down two parallel lanes on the court, one ball per group, a hoop/target at the far end of each lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate clearly about who's finishing the fast break</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each group executes a clean fast break sequence — spreading into lanes, moving the ball quickly, and finishing with a pass or shot at the far end.</li><li>Groups race each other to see who can complete their fast break sequence fastest while keeping it clean (no dropped passes, no bunching up).</li><li>If a group's sequence breaks down (a pass is dropped or players bunch together), they reset from the start of their lane.</li><li>Rotate group members through different roles (starter, lane-runner, finisher) between races.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate clearly about who's finishing the fast break.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace; for 'harder', use a faster pace with the race timed.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fast Break Basketball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — whenever a team gains possession off a rebound or turnover, they're encouraged to push the ball quickly up the court and recognise any numbers advantage before the defence recovers.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 for a 4v4 or 5v5 game on a full or near-full court with hoops if available, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching encouragement: whenever a team gains possession off a rebound or turnover, they should look to push the ball quickly up the court before the defence recovers.</li><li>Play a normal game, with both teams looking to recognise fast break opportunities and spread into lanes as practised in today's games.</li><li>Whenever a team successfully executes a fast break — spreading out, moving the ball quickly, and finishing before the defence sets up — the coach highlights it as a great team play.</li><li>If a fast break opportunity is missed (team walks the ball up instead), that's simply a coaching cue for next time, not a stoppage.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce rebounding and box-out habits from the previous lesson, since fast breaks often start from a rebound.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your fast break successful (or not) today?</li><li>How did spreading into lanes help keep the advantage alive?</li><li>What's the purpose of pushing the ball quickly rather than walking it up?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Sprints into a lane immediately after a change of possession</li><li>Recognises and uses a numbers advantage</li><li>Communicates clearly during the fast break</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify to a 2v1 version for teams still building confidence before adding a third attacker and defender.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Keep Away":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided possession games (keep-away)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual movement and passing choices affect your whole team's ability to keep the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games within a clearly marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep possession of the ball as a team using movement, passing, and communication.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move into space to give my teammate a passing option</li><li>I can communicate clearly to help my team keep the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball moving with quick, simple passes.</td><td>Extra dribbles or hesitation under pressure risk turnovers.</td></tr><tr><td>Spread out to keep passing lanes open.</td><td>Bunched-up players are easy for defenders to press all at once.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate before receiving to know the pressure situation.</td><td>Helps you decide your next move before the ball even arrives.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Triangle Support</h4><p>Groups of 3 keep the ball moving between them, with the two players off the ball always moving to form good passing angles (triangle shapes) for the ball carrier.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, enough grids for the whole class to work at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always offer at least two passing options for your teammate with the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, one light defender added</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the passing-triangle idea: whoever doesn't have the ball should be moving to create good passing angles for the ball carrier, not standing still.</li><li>The three students keep the ball moving between them with simple passes, while the two off the ball constantly reposition to offer clear angles.</li><li>At any moment, the ball carrier should have at least two visible passing options rather than one blocked line.</li><li>If the group loses the ball or a pass is poorly angled, they simply restart their triangle passing and keep working on movement.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always offer at least two passing options for your teammate with the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space at a slower pace; for 'harder', use a smaller space with one light defender added.</li></ul><hr><h4>4v2 Keep Away</h4><p>Four attackers keep the ball away from two defenders in a marked grid, focusing on movement and quick decisions to maintain possession.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 (four attackers, two defenders) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the ball quickly before the defence can close the gap</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Four attackers spread out around the grid and keep the ball away from two defenders using quick passing and constant movement.</li><li>Attackers should move into space immediately after passing, applying the triangle-support idea from the previous game.</li><li>Defenders try to intercept or force a mistake using hands-off pressure only.</li><li>If the defenders win the ball, rotate two attackers to become the new defenders and continue.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move the ball quickly before the defence can close the gap.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with slower defenders; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with quicker defenders.</li></ul><hr><h4>Possession Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small-sided keep-away game where the team tracks their longest streak of consecutive passes kept without losing the ball, trying to beat their own best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups (e.g. 4v2 or 3v2) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, a simple tally to track the team's longest streak of consecutive passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Celebrate a good streak, then reset your focus for the next one</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, fewer defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, more defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacking team keeps possession using passing and movement, with the group counting consecutive completed passes out loud as a shared streak.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, the streak resets to zero and the group starts counting again from the next pass.</li><li>Encourage the group to try to beat their own best streak from earlier in the activity.</li><li>Rotate which players are defending every couple of minutes so everyone gets both attacking and defending turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'celebrate a good streak, then reset your focus for the next one.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space with fewer defenders; for 'harder', use a smaller space with more defenders.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Keep Away Basketball</h3><p>4v4, small court — teams score a point for every 6 consecutive passes kept as a team, rewarding movement, communication, and decision-making over individual dribbling.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 12m x 10m, no hoops needed, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring rule: a team scores a point for every 6 consecutive passes kept as a team without the other team touching the ball.</li><li>Play begins with one team in possession, using the triangle-support movement and quick passing practised in today's games.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or knocked away, possession switches to the other team and their own count starts from zero.</li><li>Call out the running count out loud as a team approaches 6 ('four... five... six — point!') so the reward for good movement is obvious.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach highlights good movement and communication as the reason for a successful streak, more than any individual dribbling.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team keep possession under pressure today?</li><li>How did moving without the ball help your teammates?</li><li>What did good communication sound like in your team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves to create a passing option, not just watching the ball</li><li>Communicates clearly and early</li><li>Makes quick, sensible decisions under mild pressure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Increase the space or reduce defender numbers for teams still building confidence keeping the ball under any pressure at all.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Team Flow":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team offence patterns</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Thinking about how your decisions within the pattern affect whether the whole team's play succeeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep attacking patterns within a marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning simple attacking patterns that help our team move the ball toward the hoop.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run a simple attacking pattern with my team</li><li>I can adjust the pattern based on what the defence is doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Learn and run your team's planned pattern of movement.</td><td>Patterns create better spacing and options than random attacking.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust when the defence covers your first option.</td><td>Real defence requires adapting the plan, not abandoning it.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate throughout the pattern.</td><td>A silent pattern breaks down under any defensive pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pattern Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple attacking pattern (e.g. pass-cut-screen toward the hoop) slowly with cone markers before adding pace or defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 walk through a simple attacking pattern (e.g. pass-cut-screen toward a hoop or marked target) using cones to mark positions, one ball per group, no defence yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your role in the pattern before you start moving</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players, simpler pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more complex pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the simple pattern being learned: Player A passes to Player B, Player A then cuts toward the hoop, and Player C sets a screen to help free up whoever needs it.</li><li>Walk through the pattern slowly at walking pace with cone markers showing each position, so every player knows exactly where to be and when.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough several times, rotating which player takes each role (passer, cutter, screener) so everyone learns all parts.</li><li>Once the pattern feels familiar, add light pace (still no defence) to see the pattern flow more naturally.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your role in the pattern before you start moving.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players and a simpler pattern; for 'harder', use more players and a more complex pattern.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pattern vs Shadow Defence</h4><p>Team runs their attacking pattern against shadow defenders (not intercepting) to build confidence with defensive presence nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same small groups, plus shadow defenders added (groups of 6-8 total), one ball per group, same hoop/target markers as Pattern Walkthrough.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders closer, more realistic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Run the same attacking pattern from Pattern Walkthrough, but now with shadow defenders assigned to each attacker who apply hands-off pressure without intercepting.</li><li>Attackers practise running their pattern with a real defensive presence nearby, building confidence that the pattern still works under pressure.</li><li>If the pattern breaks down because a player hesitates due to the defender, reset and try the same pattern again.</li><li>Rotate roles between attackers and defenders every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears.'</li><li>For 'easier', defenders stay further back; for 'harder', defenders play closer and more realistically.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read &amp; Adjust</h4><p>Team runs their pattern, but must adjust on the fly if a defender blocks the planned option, practising a backup plan.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups and setup as Pattern vs Shadow Defence, with defenders now allowed to actively block the planned first option (still hands-off pressure only).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Unpredictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacking group starts running their pattern as before, but a defender is instructed to specifically block or cover the first planned passing option.</li><li>When the first option is covered, the attacking group must adjust on the fly — finding a simple backup option (a different pass or cutter) rather than forcing the original plan.</li><li>Reset and repeat several times so the group gets used to reading the defence and switching to their backup option smoothly.</li><li>Rotate which defender is assigned to block the first option, and rotate attacking roles as well.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered.'</li><li>For 'easier', make the defensive block predictable; for 'harder', make it unpredictable.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Offence Basketball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with full defence — attacking teams are encouraged to run at least one planned pattern per possession, adjusting when the defence covers their first option.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 for a 4v4 or 5v5 game with full defence on a court roughly 15m x 12m with hoops if available, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching encouragement: attacking teams are asked to run at least one planned pattern per possession (pass-cut-screen or similar) rather than playing purely off instinct.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with full defence, encouraging attackers to call or signal their pattern before starting a possession where possible.</li><li>If the defence covers the first planned option, the attacking team should adjust smoothly to a backup option, exactly as practised in Read &amp; Adjust.</li><li>Whenever a team runs a clear pattern successfully (or adjusts well when covered), the coach highlights it as a great team play.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce possession and passing habits from the previous lesson alongside today's pattern focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your attacking pattern successful (or not) against real defence today?</li><li>How did you adjust when your first option was covered?</li><li>What's the value of having a plan, even if you sometimes need to change it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Runs a recognisable attacking pattern with teammates</li><li>Adjusts the pattern when the defence covers the first option</li><li>Communicates with teammates during the pattern</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Walk the pattern through cone markers first for teams who need a visual/spatial reference before adding a live defender.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Team Wall":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team defence patterns</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Bringing everything together fairly — defending hard, defending safely, and defending for the team, not just for yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep defensive movement controlled — no sprinting into others' space; communication should be clear, not aggressive shouting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend as a connected team, not just as individuals marking their own player.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can communicate with my defensive teammates about where the ball and attackers are</li><li>I can adjust my defensive position based on what my teammates are doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly about who's covering whom.</td><td>Team defence relies on clear, updated roles in real time.</td></tr><tr><td>Cover for a teammate who's beaten, then recover to your own mark.</td><td>Helping without abandoning your job keeps the defence solid.</td></tr><tr><td>Move as a connected unit, not as isolated defenders.</td><td>Gaps appear exactly where defenders stop working together.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call the Danger</h4><p>Defensive team practises calling out loudly when an attacker is dangerous (open, about to receive, or about to shoot) so teammates can help cover.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 defenders working together in a grid roughly 10m x 10m against an equal number of light attackers, one ball per group, no scoring for this first game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the defensive communication idea: whenever an attacker becomes dangerous (open, about to receive a pass, or about to shoot), a nearby defender should call it out loudly and specifically.</li><li>As attackers move the ball around, defenders practise calling out danger early and by name ('watch number 3, she's open!') rather than staying silent.</li><li>Attackers move and pass at a light, cooperative pace so defenders have time to practise their calling habit without needing to fully contest yet.</li><li>Rotate attacking and defending groups every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing.'</li><li>For 'easier', attackers create more obvious, slower danger; for 'harder', attackers move faster and less obviously.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cover &amp; Recover</h4><p>One defender practises leaving their direct mark briefly to cover a bigger danger, while a teammate calls out and helps recover the original mark.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 defenders against an equal number of attackers in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time to communicate</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, quick decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One defender is assigned a direct mark (an attacker to stick with), but is asked to briefly leave that mark to cover a bigger, more dangerous attacker elsewhere.</li><li>While that defender covers the danger, a teammate calls out clearly and helps recover the original mark so no attacker is left completely open.</li><li>Practise this cover-and-recover pattern several times with different defenders taking the covering role.</li><li>If communication breaks down and an attacker is left open, the group resets and tries the same scenario again with clearer calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more time to communicate; for 'harder', use a faster pace with quick decisions required.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Defence Grid</h4><p>Small-sided game where the defending team's only job is to communicate and cover for each other, with the coach highlighting good team defensive moments.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups (e.g. 4v4) in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per group, no hoops needed — this game is purely about defensive communication.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Defence is a team job, not just your own player</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer attackers, easier to cover</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full attacking numbers</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the defending team's only job in this game is to communicate and cover for each other using the calling and cover-and-recover habits from today's earlier games.</li><li>Play a small-sided possession game where the defending team focuses entirely on talking, covering gaps, and helping teammates rather than individual steals.</li><li>The coach walks the sideline and highlights good team defensive moments out loud whenever they happen.</li><li>Rotate which team defends every few minutes so everyone practises the communication habit.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'defence is a team job, not just your own player.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer attackers, making it easier to cover; for 'harder', use full attacking numbers.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Defence Basketball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — defending team is coached explicitly on communication and covering for each other, while attackers try to exploit any gaps in the defensive structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 for a 4v4 or 5v5 game on a court roughly 15m x 12m with hoops if available, one ball per game, bibs in two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for the defending team: communication and covering for each other are being coached explicitly, alongside individual defending skills built earlier in the unit.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with the attacking team trying to exploit any gaps in the defensive structure while defenders call out danger and cover for each other.</li><li>Whenever the defending team communicates well and covers a gap successfully, the coach highlights it as a great team defensive moment.</li><li>If gaps open up because of poor communication, that's a coaching cue for the next possession rather than a stoppage.</li><li>Rotate players through every few minutes so everyone gets even game time, and swap which team is defending regularly.</li><li>Coach continues to reinforce attacking patterns from the previous lesson alongside today's team defence focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your team's defence effective (or not) today?</li><li>Why is communication important for a defensive team, not just individual marking?</li><li>How did covering for a teammate help your team defend better?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Communicates clearly with defensive teammates</li><li>Covers for a teammate when needed</li><li>Recovers to their original mark after helping</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole game to walking pace for students still learning the team defence concept before adding jogging speed.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Hoops Cup":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C &amp; D · All key competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Celebrating the whole team's effort and growth across the unit — thinking about how everyone's contribution, not just the scoreline, made the tournament work.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs (multiple colours for teams), cones, hoops/targets, a simple ladder/scoreboard</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Manage fatigue with rotations/subs; reinforce dribbling, footwork, and no-contact defending rules before the tournament begins</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills in a fun, competitive mini-tournament format.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full game applying skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>I can be a good teammate and good sport across multiple games</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply your best technique from across the unit in every play.</td><td>The tournament tests everything you've learned together under real pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly with teammates.</td><td>Communication becomes even more important when the stakes go up.</td></tr><tr><td>Play fairly and encourage teammates, win or lose.</td><td>Sportsmanship is as much a part of the unit as the physical skills.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Warm-In Circuit</h4><p>A short rotating circuit revisiting the unit's key skills (dribble, pass &amp; catch, shoot, defend) as a fun warm-in before the tournament starts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations around the space — dribble, pass &amp; catch, shoot, and defend — each with its own cones/equipment from earlier lessons, small groups rotating through every 1-2 minutes.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every station</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer stations, fewer skills</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter stations, all skills</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a fun revision circuit touching on the unit's four key skill areas before the tournament begins.</li><li>Groups rotate through all four stations, spending 1-2 minutes at each practising the skill using their best technique from across the unit.</li><li>At the dribble station, use fingertip control and hand-swaps; at pass &amp; catch, use target hands and clean passes; at shoot, use balanced stance and follow-through; at defend, use low stance and sliding footwork.</li><li>Rotate to the next station on a call from the teacher, keeping the pace brisk and energetic.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring your best technique from across the unit into every station.'</li><li>For 'easier', use longer stations covering fewer skills; for 'harder', use shorter stations covering all four skills quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Huddle</h4><p>Each team spends 2 minutes with their 'captain' (rotating role) planning one simple tactic to try in their first game — a light strategy touchpoint before playing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each tournament team gathers in their own small circle with their bibs on, no equipment needed beyond a couple of minutes of quiet space per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests the tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team chooses their own tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each team spends 2 minutes with a 'captain' (a rotating role across lessons/games) leading a short planning discussion.</li><li>The captain leads the team in choosing one simple tactic to try in their first game — for example, using a give-and-go, running a screen, or focusing on team defence communication.</li><li>Keep the plan to one clear, simple idea that every teammate understands and can picture doing.</li><li>Move straight into the first tournament game once the huddle is done.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.'</li><li>For 'easier', the coach suggests the tactic to the team; for 'harder', the team chooses their own tactic independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fair Play Check-In</h4><p>Brief between-games moment where teams acknowledge one good play from their opponent, reinforcing sportsmanship as part of the tournament structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Brief between-games moment where each tournament team gathers for under a minute, no equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific — name what your opponent did well</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Between games in the round robin, gather each team briefly for a quick fair play moment.</li><li>Ask each team to name one specific good play their opponent made in the game just finished.</li><li>Keep it brief and specific — a particular pass, a good defensive stance, or a well-timed cut, rather than a vague compliment.</li><li>Move straight into the next scheduled game once the check-in is done.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be specific — name what your opponent did well.'</li><li>This activity doesn't need easier/harder variants — it's a short, consistent sportsmanship routine between games.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Hoops Cup — Round Robin</h3><p>Full simplified basketball rules, small-sided round-robin mini-tournament (4v4 or 5v5) across several short games; ladder/results tracked lightly and playfully, with every team guaranteed multiple games regardless of results.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class split into tournament teams of 4-5 (using bibs in multiple colours), playing full simplified basketball rules in a small-sided round-robin format (4v4 or 5v5) across several short games on the available court(s), with hoops/targets and a simple ladder or scoreboard visible to all.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: every team plays several short games against different opponents, with results tracked lightly on a simple ladder or scoreboard.</li><li>Games run for a short, fixed time (e.g. 5-6 minutes) using full simplified basketball rules covering everything practised across the unit — dribbling, passing, shooting, defending, and team play.</li><li>Between games, run the Fair Play Check-In routine so sportsmanship stays part of the tournament structure throughout.</li><li>Rotate players within each team so everyone gets fair game time across the round robin, especially in larger teams.</li><li>Keep the ladder/results tracking light and playful — the emphasis is on every team getting multiple games and having fun, not just final standings.</li><li>Use this final session to celebrate growth across the whole unit, referencing specific skills or moments from earlier lessons where you can.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from across the whole unit you felt proudest of today?</li><li>How did your team work together across multiple games?</li><li>What would you want to work on if we played another basketball unit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a recognisable range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Shows good sportsmanship across wins and losses</li><li>Encourages and includes teammates throughout the tournament</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure team sizes/formats can flex on the day for uneven numbers; emphasise participation and effort in any informal recognition, not just results.</p>"}}}}},"Cricket":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Fielding & Throwing Fundamentals (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Bullseye":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Underarm and overarm throwing accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while your throwing technique feels new, and encouraging teammates whose accuracy looks different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified cricket ball per pair, cones, target markers (hoops or chalk circles)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread pairs well apart before throwing; throw only on a clear 'go' signal; never throw at another person, only at targets</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to throw a ball accurately to a target using underarm and overarm technique.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can throw underarm with a smooth, controlled release toward a target</li><li>I can throw overarm with my non-throwing shoulder pointing at the target</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Hands Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in a large circle or scattered space (roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 20-25), each student with a soft/modified ball. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the routine: on 'go,' students move around the space gently tossing their ball a short distance up and catching it themselves as they walk or jog.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'partner up' means quickly find a nearby partner and start gently underarm-tossing to each other; 'solo' means go back to tossing and catching alone while moving.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick hands' — for a few seconds, students toss and catch as fast as they safely can before returning to a normal pace.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light, continuous, and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on a ball, not testing catching technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up solo/partner changes and quick-hands bursts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm stretch (relevant to today's throwing and catching work) — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Step toward the target with the opposite foot as you release.</td><td>Aims the throw and adds a little natural power.</td></tr><tr><td>For overarm, point the non-throwing shoulder at the target before releasing.</td><td>Aligns your whole body toward accuracy rather than throwing side-on.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target after release.</td><td>Stopping the arm early reduces both accuracy and power.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Underarm Target Toss</h4><p>Standing a short distance from a hoop/cone target, students practise a smooth underarm throw, aiming to land the ball inside the target.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with about 3m between each pair, one soft/modified ball per pair. Each student stands roughly 4m from their own hoop or chalk-circle target laid flat on the ground.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Step toward the target with your opposite foot, release low and smooth.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the underarm action first: step toward the target with the opposite foot to the throwing hand, swing the arm low and smooth, and release toward the ground so the ball lands inside the target.</li><li>Students take turns throwing at their own target while their partner retrieves and resets the ball.</li><li>A throw only counts if the ball lands fully inside the hoop or circle — call out your own hits so your partner can keep a rough tally.</li><li>Swap thrower and retriever roles every 6-8 throws so both partners get even reps.</li><li>Coach the key message: step toward the target with your opposite foot, release low and smooth.</li><li>Easier: move the target closer and use a bigger hoop. Harder: move further back and shrink the target to a small cone gap.</li></ul><hr><h4>Overarm Line-Up</h4><p>Partners stand facing each other at a comfortable distance and throw overarm, focusing on pointing the non-throwing shoulder at the target before releasing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners face each other about 6m apart, spread out so no pair is within throwing range of another. One soft/modified ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Point your shoulder, step, then throw — don't throw side-on.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter distance, bigger target zone (partner's whole body).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer distance, aim for a smaller zone (chest height only).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the overarm cue first: point the non-throwing shoulder at the target, step in, then release — not a side-on throw.</li><li>One partner throws while the other receives, calling 'ready' before each throw so no one is caught off guard.</li><li>After each throw, the receiver gives one quick piece of feedback: did the shoulder point at the target before release?</li><li>Swap throwing and receiving roles every 6-8 throws.</li><li>Coach the key message: point your shoulder, step, then throw — don't throw side-on.</li><li>Easier: shorten the distance and let any throw at the partner's body count. Harder: increase the distance and only count throws to chest height.</li></ul><hr><h4>Target Rotation Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through 3 targets at different distances/heights, tallying accurate hits as a team total.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 students share a set of 3 targets (hoops or cones) placed at different distances (close, medium, far) and slightly different heights using a bucket or crate to raise one target. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Choose the right throw (underarm for accuracy close up, overarm for distance) for each target.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, closer targets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More targets, mixed distances, time limit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rotation: each group works through all 3 targets, choosing underarm for the close target and overarm for the further ones.</li><li>One student throws at a time while the rest of the group watches and calls out whether the throw landed inside the target.</li><li>Every hit adds one point to the group's shared team total — write the running total on a whiteboard or track verbally.</li><li>Rotate the thrower after every throw so everyone gets frequent turns across all three targets.</li><li>Coach the key message: choose the right throw — underarm for accuracy close up, overarm for distance.</li><li>Easier: use fewer, closer targets. Harder: add a time limit and mix in a bonus target for extra points.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Target Cricket Relay</h3><p>Small teams relay-race, each member throwing (underarm or overarm, their choice) at a set of targets before tagging the next teammate; fastest team to hit every target wins. Both accuracy and choosing the right throw for the situation decide the winner.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small teams of 3-4, each team lined up behind a start cone about 8-10m from their own set of 3 targets (hoops/cones at varied distances). One ball per team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: the first player runs to the throwing line, chooses underarm or overarm for each target, and must hit all 3 before running back to tag the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first player from each team goes; the rest of the team cheers and waits behind the start cone.</li><li>A target only counts as hit once the ball lands inside it — misses can be retried until it lands in, but the clock keeps running.</li><li>The next teammate can't start until the previous player is tagged in, so successful tags matter as much as accurate throws.</li><li>The first team to have every player complete all 3 targets wins — coach calls out the finishing order for excitement.</li><li>Reinforce the lesson focus: reward players who visibly choose the right throw type for each target, not just fast throwers.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your throws land accurately today?</li><li>When did you choose underarm over overarm, and why?</li><li>What was tricky about throwing at a target compared to just throwing far?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Steps toward the target before releasing</li><li>Chooses underarm or overarm appropriately for the distance</li><li>Follows through toward the target after release</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a bigger, softer ball and closer, larger targets for students building throwing confidence; success is measured by technique and effort, not just hits.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Safe Hands High":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Catching — high catches</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body — reading the flight of the ball and adjusting your position and hands to meet it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Use soft/modified balls only; spread pairs out so no one is under two balls at once; call 'mine!' loudly to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to catch a ball that arrives high, using safe hand technique.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a 'basket' shape with my hands above my head to catch a high ball</li><li>I can track the ball with my eyes all the way into my hands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Hands Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in a large circle or scattered space (roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 20-25), each student with a soft/modified ball. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the routine: on 'go,' students move around the space gently tossing their ball a short distance up and catching it themselves as they walk or jog.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'partner up' means quickly find a nearby partner and start gently underarm-tossing to each other; 'solo' means go back to tossing and catching alone while moving.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick hands' — for a few seconds, students toss and catch as fast as they safely can before returning to a normal pace.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light, continuous, and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on a ball, not testing catching technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up solo/partner changes and quick-hands bursts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm stretch (relevant to today's throwing and catching work) — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Form a soft 'basket' shape with your hands above your head.</td><td>Gives a secure, wide surface to receive a high ball safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Track the ball with your eyes all the way into your hands.</td><td>Looking away early is the most common cause of a drop.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet early to get under the ball.</td><td>Reaching at the last second is less reliable than being in position early.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Basket Hands Practice</h4><p>Partners gently lob the ball high to each other, receiver practises forming a soft 'basket' with fingers up and hands together above head height.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand about 3-4m apart with plenty of space between pairs (roughly 4m x 4m each) so lobbed balls don't cross paths. One soft/modified ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give with your hands on contact, don't snatch at the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Lower, gentler lobs.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Higher, further lobs requiring movement to get under the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the 'basket' shape first: fingers pointing up, hands together above head height, elbows soft.</li><li>One partner gently lobs the ball high and slightly in front of the receiver, who moves to get under it and catches with the basket shape.</li><li>After each catch, the receiver briefly checks their hand shape was fingers-up (not palms-down) before the next lob.</li><li>Swap thrower and catcher roles every 6-8 lobs so both partners get equal reps.</li><li>Coach the key message: give with your hands on contact, don't snatch at the ball.</li><li>Easier: lob lower and gentler. Harder: lob higher and further so the catcher has to move to get under it.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call &amp; Catch</h4><p>Small groups of 3-4 stand in a circle; one player lobs high, calling a name — that player must call 'mine!' and move under the ball to catch it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 stand in a circle with about 2m between each student, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet to get under the ball early, don't reach at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more predictable lobs.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable lobs, more players in the circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: whoever has the ball lobs it high into the air and calls out a teammate's name before it lands.</li><li>The named player must shout 'mine!' immediately and move quickly to get under the ball to catch it.</li><li>Anyone else nearby stops and gives the caller space rather than also going for the ball.</li><li>The player who caught it becomes the next thrower, choosing a new name to call.</li><li>Coach the key message: move your feet to get under the ball early, don't reach at the last second.</li><li>Easier: slower, more predictable lobs with fewer players in the circle. Harder: faster lobs and more players sharing the space.</li></ul><hr><h4>High Catch Streak</h4><p>Pairs count consecutive clean high catches in a row, trying to beat their own best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with about 3-4m between them, one ball per pair, enough room to move a few steps in any direction to get under the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your basket shape after every catch.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Lower lobs, bigger target area.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Higher lobs, must call 'mine!' every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (own record)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners lob the ball high back and forth, counting out loud together every clean catch in a row.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or mishandled, the streak resets to zero and the pair starts counting again.</li><li>Encourage pairs to remember their personal best streak and try to beat it across the activity.</li><li>After every catch, the receiver resets their basket hand shape ready for the next lob.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset your basket shape after every catch.</li><li>Easier: lower lobs with a bigger target area to stand in. Harder: higher lobs, and the receiver must call 'mine!' out loud on every single catch.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Around the Grid High Catch</h3><p>Small groups spread around a grid; a feeder lobs high catches to each player in turn while the group counts total clean catches as a team score, rotating the feeder role regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 5-6, each group spread around a large circle or grid (roughly 8m across) with one ball per group.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student in each group is the feeder, standing in the middle or at one side of the grid.</li><li>The feeder lobs a high catch to each group member in turn, working around the group in order.</li><li>The group calls out together and keeps a running team tally of every clean catch made.</li><li>Swap the feeder role every 2-3 minutes so several students get a turn feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: move early and use a soft basket shape — the whole group's score depends on everyone's catches, not just the strong catchers.</li><li>Easier: feeder lobs slower and closer. Harder: feeder mixes in faster or further lobs once the group is catching consistently.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you get into position under a high ball today?</li><li>How did your hand shape change your catching success?</li><li>What did you do differently for a higher ball versus a lower one?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Forms a soft basket shape with hands before the ball arrives</li><li>Tracks the ball with eyes into the hands</li><li>Moves feet early to get under the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a larger, very soft ball (e.g. a foam ball) and lower lobs for students building catching confidence; allow a bounce-first version before progressing to catching on the full.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Down Low":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Catching — low and ground-level catches</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Giving your partner a fair, honest feed so they get a genuine chance to practise — cricket relies on teammates setting each other up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Soft/modified balls only; check ground surface for hazards before ground-fielding games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to catch a ball that arrives low or along the ground.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can get my body low and in line behind a ground ball</li><li>I can scoop a low catch with soft hands close to the ground</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Hands Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in a large circle or scattered space (roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 20-25), each student with a soft/modified ball. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the routine: on 'go,' students move around the space gently tossing their ball a short distance up and catching it themselves as they walk or jog.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'partner up' means quickly find a nearby partner and start gently underarm-tossing to each other; 'solo' means go back to tossing and catching alone while moving.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick hands' — for a few seconds, students toss and catch as fast as they safely can before returning to a normal pace.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light, continuous, and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on a ball, not testing catching technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up solo/partner changes and quick-hands bursts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm stretch (relevant to today's throwing and catching work) — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get your body low and in line behind the ball.</td><td>Your body acts as a backstop if the ball gets past your hands.</td></tr><tr><td>Use soft hands to scoop rather than stab at the ball.</td><td>Soft hands absorb the ball instead of it bouncing away.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your technique for ground versus low bouncing balls.</td><td>A bouncing ball needs a slightly different read than one rolling flat.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Low Roller Scoop</h4><p>Partners roll the ball along the ground to each other, receiver gets low and scoops it up with two hands.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs face each other about 4-5m apart on a checked, hazard-free surface, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your body behind the ball as a backstop, don't just reach with your hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower rolls, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster rolls, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the scoop first: get low with knees bent, get the body behind the ball as a backstop, and scoop with two hands.</li><li>One partner rolls the ball firmly along the ground toward the other, who gets low and scoops it up cleanly.</li><li>If the ball squeezes through, the fielder still chases it down rather than leaving it — no half-effort stops.</li><li>Swap roller and fielder roles every 6-8 rolls.</li><li>Coach the key message: get your body behind the ball as a backstop, don't just reach with your hands.</li><li>Easier: slower rolls from a closer distance. Harder: faster rolls from further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Low Bounce Catch</h4><p>Partners throw a gentle low, bouncing ball for the receiver to catch just after the bounce.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand about 4-5m apart, one ball per pair, spread out so bouncing balls don't roll into another pair's space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the bounce and move to meet the ball early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable, straight bounces.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied angle/height bounces.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: throw a gentle underarm ball so it bounces once roughly halfway between the pair, then catch it just after the bounce.</li><li>One partner throws while the other watches the bounce closely and moves to meet the ball as it comes up.</li><li>Catch with two hands close to the body rather than reaching out stiff-armed.</li><li>Swap thrower and catcher roles every 6-8 throws.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the bounce and move to meet the ball early.</li><li>Easier: predictable, straight bounces aimed right at the catcher. Harder: bounces angled slightly left or right, or with more height.</li></ul><hr><h4>Ground Ball Relay</h4><p>Small teams relay a low rolled/thrown ball down a line, each player fielding cleanly before passing on; fastest clean team wins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 3-4 line up 2-3m apart along a straight line (roughly 10m total length), one ball per team, teams spaced well apart from each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get down low and in line with the ball every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, shorter distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, longer line.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: the ball is rolled or thrown low along the line from player to player, and every player must field it cleanly before passing it on.</li><li>On 'go', the first player fields (or starts with) the ball and rolls/throws it low to the next player in line.</li><li>If a player fumbles the ball, they must gather it cleanly before continuing — no rushing a messy pickup.</li><li>The fastest team to have the ball cleanly fielded by every player down the line wins that round.</li><li>Coach the key message: get down low and in line with the ball every time.</li><li>Easier: slower pace over a shorter line. Harder: faster pace over a longer line, or race two teams head-to-head.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Low &amp; High Mixed Catch Game</h3><p>Small groups feed a mix of high and low/ground balls to each player in rotation, scoring team points for clean catches of both types — testing the adaptability built across Lessons 2 and 3.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread around a grid roughly 8-10m across, one ball per group, with a designated feeder for each round.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder mixes up deliveries to each group member in turn — sometimes a high lob, sometimes a low roller or bounce.</li><li>Each player fields whatever comes their way, using the high-catch basket shape from Lesson 2 or the low scoop technique from today, whichever fits.</li><li>The group keeps a shared tally of clean stops across both catch types, calling out each success together.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role every 2-3 minutes so several students get a turn feeding a mix of deliveries.</li><li>Coach the key message: read what's coming early — high catches and low catches need different body shapes, and good fielders switch instantly between them.</li><li>Easier: feeder announces high or low before each delivery. Harder: feeder mixes deliveries with no warning.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did getting your body low help you do today?</li><li>How is catching a ground ball different from catching a high ball?</li><li>What made a good, fair feed from your partner?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gets body low and in line behind the ball</li><li>Uses soft hands to scoop rather than stab at the ball</li><li>Adjusts technique appropriately for ground vs. low bouncing balls</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a larger, slower-rolling ball for students building ground fielding confidence; allow kneeling as a starting technique before progressing to a full low crouch.</p>"},"Lesson 4: First to the Ball":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fielding — ground fielding technique</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — A clean, quick fielding effort has a real impact on the game — stopping runs and creating chances for your team.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; check the ground for hazards; call 'mine!' when approaching a shared ball</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct ground fielding technique — approaching, gathering, and preparing to throw in one movement.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can approach a ground ball at an angle that lets me gather and throw in one motion</li><li>I can gather the ball cleanly before looking up to release</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Hands Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in a large circle or scattered space (roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 20-25), each student with a soft/modified ball. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the routine: on 'go,' students move around the space gently tossing their ball a short distance up and catching it themselves as they walk or jog.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'partner up' means quickly find a nearby partner and start gently underarm-tossing to each other; 'solo' means go back to tossing and catching alone while moving.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick hands' — for a few seconds, students toss and catch as fast as they safely can before returning to a normal pace.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light, continuous, and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on a ball, not testing catching technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up solo/partner changes and quick-hands bursts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm stretch (relevant to today's throwing and catching work) — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Approach the ball at an angle, not straight on.</td><td>An angled approach means your body is already turning to throw.</td></tr><tr><td>Gather the ball cleanly with two hands before looking up.</td><td>Rushing to look up before gathering causes fumbles.</td></tr><tr><td>Move smoothly from gather into a throwing position.</td><td>One continuous motion is faster and cleaner than separate, stop-start actions.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Approach &amp; Gather</h4><p>Individual practice approaching a stationary/slow-rolling ball from an angle, gathering it cleanly with two hands, and stepping into a throwing position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual stations spread across the space (roughly 4-5m per student), one ball each, with a stationary or slow-rolling ball to approach from about 3-4m away.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Approach at an angle, not straight on, so your body is already turning to throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stationary ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rolling ball, increasing speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the angled approach: run in from an angle (not straight on) so the body is already turning toward the throw target as the ball is gathered.</li><li>Students place the ball a short distance away, jog back to a start point, then approach and gather it with two hands.</li><li>Immediately after gathering, step into a balanced throwing position facing the target, even without throwing yet.</li><li>Repeat several times, alternating which side the ball is approached from.</li><li>Coach the key message: approach at an angle, not straight on, so your body is already turning to throw.</li><li>Easier: use a stationary ball only. Harder: have a partner roll the ball at increasing speed to approach.</li></ul><hr><h4>Field &amp; Fire</h4><p>Partners field a rolled ball and immediately throw to a target, timing themselves from gather to release.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs work together with one thrower/roller and one fielder about 5-6m apart, plus a target (cone or stump) a further 4-5m from the fielder. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One smooth motion — gather, turn, throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer target, slower roll.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further target, faster roll.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner rolls the ball toward the fielder while the fielder times themselves (or the coach times) from gather to throw release.</li><li>The fielder gathers the ball using the angled approach, turns, and throws immediately at the target.</li><li>Partners compare times or simply aim to beat their own previous attempt.</li><li>Swap roller and fielder roles every 4-5 reps.</li><li>Coach the key message: one smooth motion — gather, turn, throw.</li><li>Easier: closer target and a slower roll. Harder: further target and a faster roll.</li></ul><hr><h4>Race to the Ball</h4><p>Two fielders start equal distance from a ball rolled between them; first to field it cleanly and hit a target scores a point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs of fielders stand an equal distance (about 4m) on either side of a target zone; the coach or a third student rolls the ball between them from a further distance so it arrives roughly in the middle. Cones mark a target for the throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call 'mine!' early and commit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower roll, closer targets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster roll, further target.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the contest: as soon as the ball is rolled, both fielders react and race to be first to field it cleanly.</li><li>Whoever gets there first calls 'mine!' immediately and commits — the other fielder backs off to avoid a collision.</li><li>The fielder who won the race then throws at the target; a point is scored for winning the race and another for hitting the target.</li><li>Rotate fielders after every roll so everyone gets multiple turns racing.</li><li>Coach the key message: call 'mine!' early and commit.</li><li>Easier: slower roll and closer targets. Harder: faster roll and a further target.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fielding Circuit Game</h3><p>Small-sided game (pairs cricket style) where the fielding team scores points for clean gathers and quick, accurate returns to a target stump/cone, rewarding the whole fielding sequence, not just stopping the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small pairs-cricket-style station: a batting/hitting area with a tee or gentle underarm feed, stumps or a target cone about 8-10m away, and 3-4 fielders spread around. Soft/modified balls and bats if available.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One player hits or the coach rolls a ball into the field for the fielding team to react to.</li><li>Fielders score points for a clean gather (using today's angled approach) and a further point for a quick, accurate return throw to the target stump or cone.</li><li>Points are awarded for the fielding sequence itself, not just for getting the batter out — a good gather-and-return earns credit even if the batter is safe.</li><li>Rotate fielding positions and the batter/hitter role every few minutes so everyone experiences both.</li><li>Coach the key message: the whole sequence matters — a rushed, messy gather undoes a good throw.</li><li>Easier: bigger fielding circle and more time between deliveries. Harder: tighter field and quicker deliveries.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you gather the ball cleanly today?</li><li>Why does the angle of your approach matter?</li><li>What made your gather-to-throw sequence smooth or clunky?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Approaches at an angle that sets up the throw</li><li>Gathers with two hands before looking up</li><li>Moves smoothly from gather into a throwing position</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the ball speed and shorten the approach distance for students building confidence; success is measured by a clean gather, not speed of release.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Backing Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fielding — backing up teammates &amp; communication</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role in relation to your teammates — reading where support is needed before it's needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones, stumps/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety for multiple simultaneous small games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to back up a teammate's throw and communicate clearly in the field.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can position myself behind a teammate receiving a throw, in case it's missed</li><li>I can call clearly to claim a ball or direct a teammate</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Hands Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in a large circle or scattered space (roughly 15m x 15m for a class of 20-25), each student with a soft/modified ball. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the routine: on 'go,' students move around the space gently tossing their ball a short distance up and catching it themselves as they walk or jog.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'partner up' means quickly find a nearby partner and start gently underarm-tossing to each other; 'solo' means go back to tossing and catching alone while moving.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick hands' — for a few seconds, students toss and catch as fast as they safely can before returning to a normal pace.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light, continuous, and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on a ball, not testing catching technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up solo/partner changes and quick-hands bursts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm stretch (relevant to today's throwing and catching work) — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Position yourself behind a teammate receiving a throw.</td><td>Gives your team a second chance if the first stop is missed.</td></tr><tr><td>Call clearly to avoid confusion.</td><td>Clear calls prevent collisions and missed responsibilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your backing-up position as play develops.</td><td>The right backup position changes as the ball and players move.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call It Loud</h4><p>Small groups practise calling clearly ('mine!', 'yours!', 'back up!') as a ball is fed toward two or more potential fielders.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 stand a few metres apart in a loose triangle or line, one ball per group, with a feeder (coach or student) ready to send the ball toward two potential fielders at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and loudly — hesitation causes mistakes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower feeds, obvious calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster feeds, players must decide who calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the calls before starting: 'mine!' means I've got it, 'yours!' hands it to a teammate, 'back up!' tells someone to get in position behind in case of a miss.</li><li>The feeder rolls or throws the ball toward a spot where two fielders could both reasonably go for it.</li><li>The nearer fielder must call clearly and immediately — teammates react to the call rather than both diving for the ball.</li><li>Rotate who feeds and who fields every few turns so everyone practises calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: call early and loudly — hesitation causes mistakes.</li><li>Easier: slower feeds with an obvious nearest fielder. Harder: faster, more even feeds where players must decide who calls.</li></ul><hr><h4>Backstop Positioning</h4><p>Trios practise: one throws to a target/stump, a second fields, a third positions behind as a backstop in case the throw is missed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread out with a thrower, a fielder about 5-6m away near a target stump/cone, and a backstop positioned a further few metres behind the target. One ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always know where your backstop is before you throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Backstop close and obvious.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Backstop must find their own position.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: the thrower aims at the stump/cone, the fielder is the intended receiver, and the backstop's job is to stop any missed throw from running away.</li><li>The thrower throws at the target; the fielder attempts to field or intercept it.</li><li>If the throw is missed, the backstop moves to collect it quickly rather than letting it roll far.</li><li>Rotate all three roles every 4-5 throws so everyone experiences throwing, fielding, and backing up.</li><li>Coach the key message: always know where your backstop is before you throw.</li><li>Easier: backstop stands in an obvious, close position. Harder: backstop has to read the situation and find their own position.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Field Communication</h4><p>Small-sided fielding game where the coach specifically rewards clear calling and correct backing-up positions, not just stopping the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided fielding scenario with 5-6 fielders spread across a modest area (roughly 15m x 15m) and a feeder hitting or rolling balls into the space for them to field.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk constantly — a quiet field is a field making mistakes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, faster play.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that points are awarded specifically for clear calling and good backing-up positions, not just for stopping the ball.</li><li>The feeder sends a ball into the field; fielders react, calling clearly and moving to back up teammates near the ball.</li><li>After each play, the coach calls out one example of good communication or positioning that was seen.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role regularly so different students get to send balls into different areas of the field.</li><li>Coach the key message: talk constantly — a quiet field is a field making mistakes.</li><li>Easier: bigger space and more time to react. Harder: smaller space and faster play.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Backed-Up Fielding Game</h3><p>Small-sided pairs cricket game where the fielding team earns bonus points any time a missed stop is cleanly backed up by a teammate, reinforcing that fielding is a team effort.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with two batters, stumps, and 4-5 fielders spread across a modest playing area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls and junior bats if available.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a standard short pairs cricket game: bowl, bat, and field as normal, with fielders using the calling and backing-up skills from today.</li><li>Whenever a fielder misses a stop but a teammate has backed up and cleanly collects the rebound, award the fielding team a bonus point on top of any normal scoring.</li><li>Call out these bonus moments immediately so the whole group sees why they were rewarded.</li><li>Rotate batting, bowling, and fielding positions every few overs so everyone experiences different roles.</li><li>Coach the key message: fielding is a team effort — a backed-up miss is nearly as good as a clean stop.</li><li>Easier: fewer fielders covering a smaller area with more obvious backing-up spots. Harder: a fuller field where players must find their own backing-up positions.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team communicate clearly in the field today?</li><li>Why does backing up a teammate matter, even when most throws are caught cleanly?</li><li>What did good team fielding look like when you saw it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Positions behind a teammate receiving a throw</li><li>Calls clearly and early to claim the ball or direct others</li><li>Adjusts backing-up position as play develops</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clear visual cues (a cone marking the backstop spot) for students who find reading support positioning challenging.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Batting Fundamentals (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Ready Stance":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting grip &amp; stance</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself as a new grip and stance feel awkward at first — good technique takes repetition to feel natural.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 junior bat and soft/modified ball per pair, batting tees, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spacing between pairs so no one is in a bat's swinging range; check grip/stance before any live hitting begins</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a correct batting grip and a balanced, ready stance.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold the bat with a 'V' grip, hands together</li><li>I can stand side-on in a balanced, ready stance before the ball arrives</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Swing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual space across the area (roughly 15m x 15m), each student holding a junior bat (no ball needed). Enough spacing between students that a shadow swing doesn't reach anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Students jog gently around the space at their own pace, keeping a light, continuous movement going.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'freeze and swing' — students stop, take their batting stance, and perform 2-3 smooth shadow swings before jogging on.</li><li>Occasionally call 'pulse up' (jog a little faster) or 'pulse down' (slow right back to a walk) between swing prompts to vary the heart-rate work.</li><li>Keep the swings light and controlled — this is about warming up the batting muscles and raising heart rate, not perfecting technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing jogging pace and swing prompts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs or individuals from the warm-up, enough space to stretch without touching neighbours. Bats set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across the chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch (useful after today's batting and running work).</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Hold the bat with hands together in a 'V' grip.</td><td>This grip gives control and a consistent swing path.</td></tr><tr><td>Stand side-on in a balanced, ready stance.</td><td>Side-on positioning is the base for both defensive and attacking shots.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your weight balanced, ready to move either foot.</td><td>A flat-footed stance is slow to react to the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Grip Check Partners</h4><p>Partners check each other's grip (hands together, 'V' shapes lined up on the bat) and side-on stance, giving friendly feedback.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand about 2m apart, each with a junior bat, spread out so bats can be held up without touching anyone else's space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Hands together, 'V' pointing up the bat, front shoulder toward the bowler.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach/visual reference for the grip shape.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students correct each other without a reference.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the grip and stance first: hands together low on the handle with the 'V' shapes (thumb and finger) both pointing up the bat, side-on stance with the front shoulder pointing toward an imaginary bowler.</li><li>One partner sets up their grip and stance while the other checks it against the two cues and gives one piece of friendly feedback.</li><li>Swap roles so both partners check and get checked.</li><li>Repeat 3-4 times, resetting the stance fresh each time rather than just holding one position.</li><li>Coach the key message: hands together, 'V' pointing up the bat, front shoulder toward the bowler.</li><li>Easier: use a poster, photo, or the coach's own stance as a visual reference. Harder: partners correct each other with no reference, just the verbal cues.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Swing Freeze</h4><p>Students take a stance and shadow-swing (no ball) on a call, freezing in a balanced follow-through position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually with about 2m of swinging space each, one bat each, no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Balance at the end of the swing shows a controlled technique.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower swings, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster swings, freeze called unpredictably.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a shadow swing: take the batting stance, swing the bat smoothly as if hitting a ball, and finish in a balanced follow-through.</li><li>Students shadow-swing on their own rhythm until the coach calls 'freeze.'</li><li>On 'freeze,' students hold their follow-through position completely still — the coach checks for balance (not falling forward or off to one side).</li><li>Repeat several times, mixing up how long students swing before the freeze call.</li><li>Coach the key message: balance at the end of the swing shows a controlled technique.</li><li>Easier: slower swings with more time before each freeze call. Harder: faster swings with the freeze called unpredictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Stance &amp; Ready Relay</h4><p>Small teams race to correctly demonstrate grip and stance in turn before their teammate can start, coach checks each rep.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 3-4 line up behind a start cone, one bat per team, with a checkpoint spot about 5m away where each player demonstrates their grip and stance.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get into position early, don't rush the setup.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simplify checkpoints.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a timing element per player.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: each player runs to the checkpoint, sets up a correct grip and stance, and waits for the coach (or a nominated checker) to approve it before running back.</li><li>Only once a rep is approved can that player tag the next teammate to start their turn.</li><li>The checker (coach or student) uses the two cues from Grip Check Partners to decide if the stance passes.</li><li>Continue until every player on the team has completed a checked rep.</li><li>Coach the key message: get into position early, don't rush the setup.</li><li>Easier: simplify to checking grip only. Harder: add a timing element, racing to see which team finishes all their checks fastest.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Tee Ball Stance Challenge</h3><p>Small groups rotate through a batting tee station where each hit is only counted if the grip and stance were correct beforehand — technique earns the point, not just contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3-4 batting tee stations spread around the space, each with a tee, a junior bat, and a soft/modified ball. Groups of 3-4 rotate through the stations.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before hitting, each batter sets up their grip and stance and a partner or the coach checks it against the grip/stance cues.</li><li>A hit only counts as a scoring hit if the grip and stance were correct beforehand — contact alone doesn't earn the point.</li><li>Batters take turns hitting off the tee within their group, with the checker calling out whether each attempt counts.</li><li>Rotate the checker role and rotate groups through different stations every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: technique earns the point today, not just contact — this sets up everything that follows in the unit.</li><li>Easier: coach or an older buddy does the checking. Harder: students check each other independently with no adult confirmation.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a balanced, ready stance feel like today?</li><li>How did your grip affect your control of the bat?</li><li>What was tricky about the new stance and grip?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Hands together in a 'V' grip</li><li>Side-on, balanced stance before the ball arrives</li><li>Maintains balance through a shadow swing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a modified two-handed 'shovel' grip initially for students building hand strength/coordination, progressing to the full grip as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Middle It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting — hitting off a tee or soft toss</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising a new skill faithfully, with effort, even when contact doesn't happen every time — batting takes real repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Junior bats, soft/modified balls, batting tees, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Batters swing only when clear; others wait well back from the swinging zone; one batter hits at a time per station</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to strike the ball cleanly off a tee or a soft toss.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can watch the ball onto the bat</li><li>I can swing level and follow through toward my target</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Swing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual space across the area (roughly 15m x 15m), each student holding a junior bat (no ball needed). Enough spacing between students that a shadow swing doesn't reach anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Students jog gently around the space at their own pace, keeping a light, continuous movement going.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'freeze and swing' — students stop, take their batting stance, and perform 2-3 smooth shadow swings before jogging on.</li><li>Occasionally call 'pulse up' (jog a little faster) or 'pulse down' (slow right back to a walk) between swing prompts to vary the heart-rate work.</li><li>Keep the swings light and controlled — this is about warming up the batting muscles and raising heart rate, not perfecting technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing jogging pace and swing prompts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs or individuals from the warm-up, enough space to stretch without touching neighbours. Bats set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across the chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch (useful after today's batting and running work).</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball onto the bat through contact.</td><td>Watching the ball, not the target, is what actually connects a clean hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Swing level through the ball, not chopping down or scooping up.</td><td>A level swing gives the most consistent, controlled contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer your weight into the shot as you swing.</td><td>Weight transfer adds control and a bit of natural power.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Tee Strike Technique</h4><p>Individual practice hitting off a stationary tee, focusing on watching the ball and a level swing, not power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual stations spread well apart (at least 4-5m of swinging room each), each with a batting tee, a junior bat, and a few soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Eyes on the ball right up to contact, swing level through the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball placed lower/more central.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball placed at varied heights.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the level swing first: eyes on the ball right up to contact, swinging level through the ball rather than chopping down or scooping up.</li><li>Students set up their stance, place the ball on the tee, and take a swing, watching the ball all the way to contact.</li><li>After each hit, reset the ball on the tee and repeat — focus on watching and swinging level, not on how far it travels.</li><li>Rotate turns if sharing a station, with the next student waiting well back until it's clearly their turn.</li><li>Coach the key message: eyes on the ball right up to contact, swing level through the ball.</li><li>Easier: place the ball lower and more central on the tee. Harder: vary the ball's height on the tee between swings.</li></ul><hr><h4>Soft Toss Timing</h4><p>A partner gently underarm-tosses the ball for the batter to strike, adding timing on top of technique.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs with one batter and one gentle underarm feeder, about 3-4m apart, one bat and ball per pair, plenty of space around each pair for the swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the toss all the way, don't guess when to swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slow, predictable tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied speed/height tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder tosses the ball gently underarm toward the batter, giving a clear 'ready' cue before each toss.</li><li>The batter watches the toss the whole way and swings to make contact, focusing on timing rather than power.</li><li>After each hit, the feeder collects the ball and resets before the next toss.</li><li>Swap batter and feeder roles every 6-8 tosses.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the toss all the way, don't guess when to swing.</li><li>Easier: slow, predictable tosses at the same height each time. Harder: vary the toss speed and height between deliveries.</li></ul><hr><h4>Clean Contact Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a hitting station, tallying clean, well-timed contacts as a team total.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 rotate through a shared hitting station with a tee, junior bat, and soft/modified balls, spaced well away from other stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your stance and focus fully before each hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tee only, bigger target zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Soft toss, target zone for placement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the group counts every clean, well-timed contact as a shared team total across everyone's turns.</li><li>Each student takes a turn hitting several balls off the tee (or soft toss if the group is ready) while the rest of the group watches and tallies clean hits.</li><li>A hit counts as 'clean' if the ball was struck with a level swing rather than badly mis-hit — the group agrees on this together.</li><li>Rotate through every group member, keeping a running total visible or called out.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset your stance and focus fully before each hit.</li><li>Easier: tee only, with a big imaginary target zone for any hit to count. Harder: use soft toss, and only hits into a marked placement zone count.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Tee &amp; Toss Mini-Game</h3><p>Small-sided pairs cricket game where batters choose tee or soft toss depending on the station, with fielders practising Lessons 1–5 skills as they field the hits — batting and fielding practised together live.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 2-3 small pairs-cricket stations, each with a batting area (tee or soft toss), junior bats, stumps or a target, and 2-3 fielders spread out to field hits. Soft/modified balls throughout.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>At each station, the batter chooses tee or soft toss depending on what the station has set up, and hits toward the fielders.</li><li>Fielders react to the hit using the gathering and throwing skills built in Lessons 1-5, fielding cleanly and returning the ball.</li><li>Batting and fielding run live together — batters focus on clean contact while fielders focus on quick, clean stops.</li><li>Rotate batter and fielders every few minutes so everyone gets both batting and fielding time.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is where batting and fielding meet — a clean hit only really counts if the fielders respond well too.</li><li>Easier: slower deliveries and a smaller fielding area. Harder: faster deliveries and a fuller field to cover.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you make clean contact with the ball today?</li><li>What's the difference between swinging hard and swinging well?</li><li>What will you keep practising with your batting?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Watches the ball onto the bat</li><li>Swings level rather than chopping down or up</li><li>Balanced follow-through toward the target</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a lighter/bigger bat and a larger, softer ball for students building strength/confidence; success is measured by clean technique, not distance hit.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Run!":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting — running between wickets</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — A clear call has a real impact on your partner's safety and success — good communication prevents run-outs and mix-ups.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Junior bats, soft/modified balls, stumps/markers, cones for creases</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear running lanes, no colliding paths between batters; running technique (not diving) at this age group</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to run safely and quickly between wickets, calling clearly with our batting partner.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run in a straight line to the crease, touching my bat/foot down</li><li>I can call 'yes,' 'no,' or 'wait' clearly to my batting partner</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Swing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual space across the area (roughly 15m x 15m), each student holding a junior bat (no ball needed). Enough spacing between students that a shadow swing doesn't reach anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Students jog gently around the space at their own pace, keeping a light, continuous movement going.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'freeze and swing' — students stop, take their batting stance, and perform 2-3 smooth shadow swings before jogging on.</li><li>Occasionally call 'pulse up' (jog a little faster) or 'pulse down' (slow right back to a walk) between swing prompts to vary the heart-rate work.</li><li>Keep the swings light and controlled — this is about warming up the batting muscles and raising heart rate, not perfecting technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing jogging pace and swing prompts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs or individuals from the warm-up, enough space to stretch without touching neighbours. Bats set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across the chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch (useful after today's batting and running work).</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call clearly ('yes', 'no', 'wait') and early.</td><td>Clear, early calls prevent run-outs from confusion between batters.</td></tr><tr><td>Run in a straight line and touch the crease with the bat.</td><td>This is required to complete a legal run safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Back up down the pitch as the bowler releases the ball.</td><td>Backing up gives you a head start if a run is called.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call &amp; Run Practice</h4><p>Pairs practise calling 'yes,' 'no,' or 'wait' and responding correctly by running or staying, without a ball yet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand at opposite ends of two creases marked with cones, about 10-12m apart, no ball needed for this activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The caller commits fully and calls loudly and early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach makes the call.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Batters decide and call themselves under mock pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three calls: 'yes' means run now, 'no' means stay, 'wait' means hold and see.</li><li>One partner is the caller and shouts one of the three calls; both partners respond correctly — running together on 'yes,' staying put on 'no,' or holding on 'wait.'</li><li>Repeat with different calls in different orders so students react rather than predict.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every 4-5 rounds.</li><li>Coach the key message: the caller commits fully and calls loudly and early.</li><li>Easier: the coach makes the call for everyone at once. Harder: batters make and call their own decision under a mock pressure countdown from the coach.</li></ul><hr><h4>Straight Line Sprint</h4><p>Individual practice sprinting a set running distance between two creases, touching down correctly each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual lanes marked with cones between two creases about 10-15m apart, enough lanes so students aren't sprinting shoulder to shoulder.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Run in a straight line, touch down without breaking stride unnecessarily.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer distance, timed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a proper 'touch down': run in a straight line and touch the bat (or a marker) down just past the crease line without overrunning wildly.</li><li>Students sprint from one crease to the other on a 'go' signal, touching down correctly at the far end.</li><li>Encourage a straight running line rather than drifting sideways, which wastes time and risks collisions.</li><li>Repeat several times, resting briefly between sprints.</li><li>Coach the key message: run in a straight line, touch down without breaking stride unnecessarily.</li><li>Easier: shorter distance at a comfortable pace. Harder: longer distance, timed for personal best.</li></ul><hr><h4>Run Decision Relay</h4><p>Pairs face a fielded ball and must decide together, calling clearly, whether to run 1, run 2, or hold — coach varies the fielding speed to test decisions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand ready to run between two creases about 10m apart, with a fielder (coach or student) positioned to field a ball at varying speeds. One ball for the group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your partner's call and commit immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower fielding, obvious decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster fielding, tighter decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The fielder fields (or pretends to field) a ball at a speed the coach controls, sometimes fast and sharp, sometimes slow.</li><li>Based on the fielding speed, the pair must decide together and call clearly whether to run 1, run 2, or hold their ground.</li><li>Both partners must commit to the same decision immediately once the call is made — no half-hearted setting off.</li><li>Rotate fielders and pairs regularly so everyone experiences a range of decision scenarios.</li><li>Coach the key message: trust your partner's call and commit immediately.</li><li>Easier: slower fielding with obvious decisions. Harder: faster fielding with tighter, less obvious decisions.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pairs Cricket — Running Focus</h3><p>Small-sided pairs cricket game where the coach specifically highlights clear calling and safe, decisive running between wickets, in addition to runs scored.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with two batting pairs, stumps, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls and junior bats.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal — bat, bowl, and field — with running between wickets happening naturally on every hit.</li><li>The coach specifically calls out and praises clear, loud calling and safe, decisive running, in addition to any runs scored.</li><li>If a pair calls late or runs unsafely (e.g. crossing paths dangerously), pause briefly to reset the calling habit before continuing.</li><li>Rotate batting pairs, bowlers, and fielders every few overs so everyone gets running practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: good calling and running is worth celebrating just as much as a big hit.</li><li>Easier: shorter running distance between creases. Harder: standard distance with tighter run/no-run decisions expected.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you and your partner run safely and quickly today?</li><li>Why does a clear, early call matter so much?</li><li>What happened when a call was late or unclear?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Calls clearly and early ('yes'/'no'/'wait')</li><li>Runs in a straight line and touches down correctly</li><li>Commits fully once a call is made</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use shorter running distances and simplified 'yes/no' only calls for students building confidence with the running/calling sequence.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Field It, Fire It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining fielding and throwing to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Enjoying the game and celebrating good fielding sequences from teammates, not just wickets or big hits.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones, stumps/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; clear throwing lanes toward targets</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine a clean field with an accurate throw to a target in one sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can field the ball cleanly and immediately set up to throw</li><li>I can throw accurately to a stump/target without pausing too long</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Swing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual space across the area (roughly 15m x 15m), each student holding a junior bat (no ball needed). Enough spacing between students that a shadow swing doesn't reach anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Students jog gently around the space at their own pace, keeping a light, continuous movement going.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'freeze and swing' — students stop, take their batting stance, and perform 2-3 smooth shadow swings before jogging on.</li><li>Occasionally call 'pulse up' (jog a little faster) or 'pulse down' (slow right back to a walk) between swing prompts to vary the heart-rate work.</li><li>Keep the swings light and controlled — this is about warming up the batting muscles and raising heart rate, not perfecting technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing jogging pace and swing prompts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs or individuals from the warm-up, enough space to stretch without touching neighbours. Bats set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across the chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch (useful after today's batting and running work).</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Gather the ball cleanly first, then turn toward the target.</td><td>Rushing the turn before gathering fully often causes a fumble.</td></tr><tr><td>Throw in one smooth motion straight after gathering.</td><td>A continuous action is faster and more accurate than separate, stop-start steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Call 'mine!' early when approaching a shared ball.</td><td>Prevents collisions and confusion with a nearby teammate.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Field-Turn-Throw Circuit</h4><p>Individual circuit fielding a rolled/thrown ball, turning, and throwing at a target stump, timed for personal best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual stations spread apart, each with a ball rolled or thrown from about 5m away toward a target stump/cone a further 5-6m beyond the fielder.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the sequence flowing — field, turn, throw as one motion.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simplify the circuit, closer target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a second fielding rep before throwing.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sequence first: field the ball cleanly, turn toward the target in the same motion, and throw — one flowing action rather than three separate stops.</li><li>A partner or the coach rolls/throws the ball to the fielder, who fields, turns, and throws at the target, timed from gather to release.</li><li>Students try to beat their own previous time on each repeat rather than compare directly with others.</li><li>Repeat several times, resetting to the start position each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep the sequence flowing — field, turn, throw as one motion.</li><li>Easier: simplify to a shorter, more direct circuit with a closer target. Harder: add a second fielding rep before the throw.</li></ul><hr><h4>Race the Runner</h4><p>A fielder fields a ball and throws at the stumps while a 'batter' runs between wickets — fielding team scores if the throw beats the runner.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a fielder about 5-6m from the stumps, with a 'batter' who runs between two creases roughly 10m apart. One ball, cones for creases.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide quickly, don't hesitate over a perfect throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Runner starts with a head start disadvantage (slower).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Even race.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>A ball is rolled or hit toward the fielder at the same moment the batter sets off running.</li><li>The fielder must field cleanly and throw quickly at the stumps to try to beat the runner reaching the far crease.</li><li>The fielding team scores a point if the throw beats the runner; the batter scores a point if they make it safely.</li><li>Rotate fielder and runner regularly so everyone experiences both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide quickly, don't hesitate over a perfect throw.</li><li>Easier: give the runner a head-start disadvantage (starts a beat late or has to run further). Harder: run it as an even race with no head start.</li></ul><hr><h4>Combo Points Challenge</h4><p>Small games where bonus points are awarded any time a team completes a genuine field-then-accurate-throw sequence, even if the batter is safe.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small fielding game across a modest area (roughly 15m x 15m) with 4-5 fielders and a feeder hitting or rolling balls into play.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the combination, not just the outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space/more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus: any time a fielder completes a genuine field-then-accurate-throw sequence to the target, the team earns a bonus point — even if a batter running isn't actually put out.</li><li>The feeder sends balls into different parts of the field for fielders to react to.</li><li>After each play, the coach calls out whether a bonus combo was completed and awards the point.</li><li>Rotate the feeder and fielding positions regularly.</li><li>Coach the key message: look for the combination, not just the outcome.</li><li>Easier: more space and time to react. Harder: smaller space, forcing quicker decisions.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Pairs Cricket — Combo Bonus</h3><p>Pairs cricket game where any fielding sequence that includes a clean gather and an accurate throw to the stumps in the same move earns bonus recognition, whether or not the batter is out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls throughout.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with the fielding team specifically aiming for the field-turn-throw sequence from today whenever a ball comes to them.</li><li>Any time a fielder completes a clean gather and an accurate throw to the stumps in one continuous move, award bonus recognition — call it out loud regardless of whether the batter was actually given out.</li><li>Keep normal batting/running scoring running alongside the bonus recognition so both sides of the game matter.</li><li>Rotate batting, bowling, and fielding roles every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: a clean field-and-throw combo is worth celebrating on its own, not just when it results in a wicket.</li><li>Easier: smaller field with fielders closer to the stumps. Harder: fuller field with longer throws required.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What combination of fielding and throwing worked best for you today?</li><li>How did you decide how quickly to release your throw?</li><li>What was a great fielding combination you saw a teammate do?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Links fielding and throwing smoothly, minimal pause between them</li><li>Throws with purpose at the target, not just anywhere</li><li>Reacts to the game situation (how urgent the throw is)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow extra time in the circuit and race-the-runner games for students still building confidence linking the two skills; fluency over speed at this stage.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Let It Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bowling — basic run-up and delivery, safe technique</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and movement — building a bowling action that's safe and repeatable, not just fast.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones marking a short run-up, stumps/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Straight-arm action only (no throwing action) to protect the elbow/shoulder; check spacing so no one bowls toward another group; safe run-up lanes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a safe, correct bowling action — a short run-up and a straight-arm delivery.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can bowl with a straight arm, not a throwing/jerking action</li><li>I can use a short, controlled run-up before releasing the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Swing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual space across the area (roughly 15m x 15m), each student holding a junior bat (no ball needed). Enough spacing between students that a shadow swing doesn't reach anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Students jog gently around the space at their own pace, keeping a light, continuous movement going.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'freeze and swing' — students stop, take their batting stance, and perform 2-3 smooth shadow swings before jogging on.</li><li>Occasionally call 'pulse up' (jog a little faster) or 'pulse down' (slow right back to a walk) between swing prompts to vary the heart-rate work.</li><li>Keep the swings light and controlled — this is about warming up the batting muscles and raising heart rate, not perfecting technique.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing jogging pace and swing prompts throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs or individuals from the warm-up, enough space to stretch without touching neighbours. Bats set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across the chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch (useful after today's batting and running work).</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use a consistent, controlled run-up every time.</td><td>Consistency in your approach leads to a more repeatable delivery.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the bowling arm straight through the delivery.</td><td>This is both the safe technique and the legal bowling action.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through safely away from the pitch after release.</td><td>Protects both the bowler and other players from collisions.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Straight Arm Shadow Bowl</h4><p>Individual practice of the bowling arm action without a ball, focusing on a straight arm from a short run-up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually with about 3m of arm-swing space each, no ball needed, facing the same direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the arm straight as it comes over — no bend at the elbow.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, walked-through action.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed action from a run-up.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the straight-arm bowling action slowly: arm stays straight (no bend at the elbow) as it comes over the top, unlike a throwing action.</li><li>Students shadow the action slowly without a ball, checking their own arm stays straight throughout.</li><li>Walk around and give individual feedback, watching specifically for elbow bend which is a safety issue as well as a technique one.</li><li>Once the slow action looks safe and straight, students can try it a little faster from a standing position.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep the arm straight as it comes over — no bend at the elbow.</li><li>Easier: a slower, walked-through action broken into stages. Harder: the full-speed action performed from a short run-up.</li></ul><hr><h4>Short Run-Up Delivery</h4><p>Students bowl from a short, marked run-up at a simple target (a hoop or cone) focusing on technique over speed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a short run-up lane (about 3-4m) with cones for each bowler, aiming at a simple target (a hoop or cone) roughly 5-6m from the release point. Soft/modified balls, spacing so no one bowls toward another group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Controlled run-up, balanced delivery stride, straight arm release.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>No run-up, bowl from a standing position.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full short run-up with delivery stride.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the full short sequence: a controlled run-up, a balanced delivery stride, and a straight-arm release toward the target.</li><li>Students bowl one at a time from their run-up lane, focusing on technique over speed or bounce power.</li><li>After each ball, briefly note whether the arm stayed straight and the stride was balanced, more than whether it hit the target.</li><li>Rotate bowlers through so everyone gets several deliveries, retrieving balls safely between turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: controlled run-up, balanced delivery stride, straight arm release.</li><li>Easier: bowl from a standing position with no run-up. Harder: use the full short run-up with a delivery stride.</li></ul><hr><h4>Technique Check Rotation</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a bowling station where a partner watches and gives one piece of feedback on the arm action each turn.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rotate through a bowling station with a short run-up lane and target, one partner watching from the side at a safe distance to observe the arm action.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give one specific, kind piece of feedback each time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach/teacher gives the feedback.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students give each other feedback.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student bowls while a partner stands to the side (not in the line of the ball) specifically watching the arm action.</li><li>After each ball, the watching partner gives one specific, kind piece of feedback — for example, 'your arm stayed straight' or 'try to keep your elbow locked a bit more.'</li><li>Bowler and watcher swap roles every few deliveries.</li><li>Rotate through the group so everyone gets watched and gives feedback.</li><li>Coach the key message: give one specific, kind piece of feedback each time.</li><li>Easier: the coach or teacher gives the feedback instead of a peer. Harder: students give each other feedback independently.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Bowl to the Target Game</h3><p>Small-sided game where bowlers deliver to a batter (or simply to the stumps if no batter yet) with all deliveries checked for safe, straight-arm technique before any accuracy is scored.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small bowling station aimed at a batter (or directly at the stumps if no batter is involved yet), with stumps/markers as the target roughly 6-8m from the bowler. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Bowlers deliver from their short run-up, aiming at the batter or stumps as appropriate.</li><li>Before any delivery is scored for accuracy, the coach or a nominated checker confirms the arm action was safe and straight — an unsafe action doesn't count even if it's accurate.</li><li>Once technique is confirmed safe, track accuracy (hitting the stumps or a good target zone) as a secondary score.</li><li>Rotate bowlers regularly so everyone gets several checked deliveries.</li><li>Coach the key message: safe technique always comes first — accuracy only counts once the action is safe.</li><li>Easier: no batter yet, bowling straight at the stumps. Harder: bowl to a batter, adding a live decision element.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a safe, straight-arm bowling action feel like today?</li><li>Why does technique matter more than speed at this stage?</li><li>What's one thing you'll keep working on with your bowling action?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Straight arm through the delivery, no throwing action</li><li>Short, controlled run-up</li><li>Balanced delivery stride at the point of release</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow bowling from a standing position (no run-up) for students still building the action, adding a short run-up once the arm technique is safe and consistent.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Bowling, Decision-Making & Game Reading (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: On the Money":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bowling — accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising faithfully, with effort and focus, even when accuracy doesn't come immediately — bowling well takes real repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones/target zones, stumps/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Same safe straight-arm technique requirements as Lesson 10; clear bowling lanes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bowl accurately at a target, building on our safe technique.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can land the ball on or near a target area most of the time</li><li>I can adjust my aim based on where my last ball landed</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Line &amp; Length Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair, with 2-3 cones per pair to mark short line-and-length style shuffle zones.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner calls out a zone (short, good length, full — matching today's bowling language) and the other must shuffle sideways or step forward/back to 'stand' in that zone as fast as possible.</li><li>The caller mixes up the order and pace of calls, keeping their partner moving continuously.</li><li>Swap caller and mover roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners get the movement work.</li><li>This builds the same quick, controlled footwork used in bowling run-ups and fielding, while raising heart rate through varied, reactive movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging quick, controlled shuffles rather than sloppy footwork.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balance used in bowling and fielding.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pick a consistent release point in your action.</td><td>A variable release point is the main cause of inaccurate bowling.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim for a specific target zone — line and length — not just 'at the batter'.</td><td>A specific target gives you something concrete to repeat and improve.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through in the same direction as your target.</td><td>Keeps your whole action aligned with accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Target Zone Bowling</h4><p>Students bowl at a marked target zone (e.g. a hoop around a good length), scoring a point for each ball landing inside it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Bowlers use their short run-up lane from Lesson 10, bowling at a marked target zone (a hoop laid around a 'good length' spot roughly 5-6m away). Soft/modified balls, stumps/markers optional behind the zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use the same run-up and arm action every time — consistency builds accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zone, closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target zone, further.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the straight-arm technique before starting, since accuracy work only counts with safe technique.</li><li>Bowlers deliver one at a time from their run-up, aiming to land the ball inside the marked zone.</li><li>Score one point for every ball that lands inside the hoop, kept as a running personal or pair total.</li><li>Rotate bowlers through steadily so everyone gets frequent deliveries.</li><li>Coach the key message: use the same run-up and arm action every time — consistency builds accuracy.</li><li>Easier: bigger target zone placed closer. Harder: smaller target zone placed further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Adjust &amp; Aim</h4><p>After each ball, students note where it landed and adjust their next delivery accordingly, with a partner giving feedback.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs share a bowling lane and target zone, with one bowling and one watching where each ball lands to give feedback. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small adjustments — don't change everything after one ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Partner tells them exactly what to adjust.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students self-correct without help.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The bowler delivers a ball; the partner notes exactly where it landed relative to the target zone (short, long, left, right).</li><li>Based on that feedback, the bowler makes a small adjustment to their next delivery — for example, a slightly shorter run-up or a small change in release point.</li><li>Continue through several deliveries, adjusting each time based on the previous result.</li><li>Swap bowler and watching-feedback roles halfway through.</li><li>Coach the key message: small adjustments — don't change everything after one ball.</li><li>Easier: the partner tells the bowler exactly what to adjust. Harder: the bowler watches their own result and self-corrects without help.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bowling Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a bowling station, tracking consecutive accurate deliveries as a team streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rotate through a shared bowling station with a target zone, tracking a team streak of consecutive accurate deliveries. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your focus fully after every ball, good or bad.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target, more attempts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target, fewer attempts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the streak: the group counts consecutive accurate deliveries (landing in the zone) across all members bowling in turn.</li><li>If a ball misses the zone, the streak resets to zero and the count restarts from the next delivery.</li><li>Each student bowls one ball at a time before rotating to the next bowler in the group.</li><li>Keep the running streak count visible or called out loudly so the group stays engaged.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset your focus fully after every ball, good or bad.</li><li>Easier: bigger target with more attempts allowed per turn. Harder: smaller target with only one attempt per turn.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Bowl-Off Mini-Game</h3><p>Small-sided game where bowlers take turns bowling at the stumps/target with a simple batter or fielder in place, coach highlights accuracy and consistency as much as wickets/dismissals.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided bowling game with a simple batter or fielder in place at the stumps, roughly 6-8m from the bowling run-up. Soft/modified balls, stumps/markers.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Bowlers take turns bowling at the stumps/target with a batter or fielder present to add a live element.</li><li>The coach highlights accuracy and consistency across a bowler's overs, not just whether a wicket/dismissal happens.</li><li>Call out specifically when a bowler lands several deliveries in a row in a good area, even if none result in a wicket.</li><li>Rotate bowlers and the batter/fielder role every few deliveries so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>Coach the key message: consistency is the win today — a string of good-length balls matters as much as a wicket.</li><li>Easier: no batter, bowling straight at the stumps only. Harder: bowl to a batter who is actively trying to score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your bowling accuracy improve today?</li><li>How did you adjust your aim after a ball didn't land where you wanted?</li><li>What does 'consistent' mean for a bowler, and why does it matter?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a repeatable run-up and action each ball</li><li>Lands the ball on or near the target area regularly</li><li>Makes small adjustments based on the previous delivery</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a bigger target zone and closer distance for students building bowling accuracy; success is measured by consistency of technique, not just hitting the target.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Behind the Stumps":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Wicketkeeping basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — The wicketkeeper has a unique impact on the whole team's fielding effort, even without the ball coming to them every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, stumps/markers, (optional) wicketkeeping gloves if available</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Use only soft/modified balls for keeping practice; keeper stays a safe distance back from the stumps until confident and comfortable with the role</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the basics of wicketkeeping — a ready stance and safe handling behind the stumps.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can take a low, balanced stance behind the stumps</li><li>I can gather the ball safely with soft hands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Line &amp; Length Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair, with 2-3 cones per pair to mark short line-and-length style shuffle zones.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner calls out a zone (short, good length, full — matching today's bowling language) and the other must shuffle sideways or step forward/back to 'stand' in that zone as fast as possible.</li><li>The caller mixes up the order and pace of calls, keeping their partner moving continuously.</li><li>Swap caller and mover roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners get the movement work.</li><li>This builds the same quick, controlled footwork used in bowling run-ups and fielding, while raising heart rate through varied, reactive movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging quick, controlled shuffles rather than sloppy footwork.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balance used in bowling and fielding.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay low in a balanced, ready crouch behind the stumps.</td><td>A ready position lets you react quickly to any delivery.</td></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball all the way from the bowler's hand.</td><td>Losing sight of the ball early is the main cause of missed takes.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your gloves to the ball, not just wait for it to arrive.</td><td>Active hands catch more cleanly than passive ones.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Ready Stance Practice</h4><p>Students practise a low, balanced wicketkeeping stance behind the stumps, receiving gentle underarm rolls/throws.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual stations behind a set of stumps, each student a safe distance back (about 2-3m) until confident, facing a partner who delivers gentle underarm rolls/throws. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low and balanced, hands ready and relaxed.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, closer deliveries.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, varied-height deliveries.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the keeping stance: low and balanced, knees bent, hands relaxed and ready in front of the body.</li><li>The partner rolls or gently throws the ball toward the stumps while the student practises holding the ready stance and receiving it.</li><li>After each rep, reset to the ready stance before the next delivery rather than staying crouched the whole time.</li><li>Swap keeper and feeder roles every 6-8 deliveries.</li><li>Coach the key message: stay low and balanced, hands ready and relaxed.</li><li>Easier: slower, closer deliveries. Harder: faster deliveries at varied heights.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gather &amp; Return</h4><p>Keeper gathers a ball (rolled or gently thrown) and quickly returns it to a target, practising the full sequence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs with a keeper behind the stumps and a feeder rolling or gently throwing the ball, a target a few metres away for the return throw. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Gather with soft hands first, then think about the return.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simplify to gather only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a quick, accurate return throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder sends a ball toward the keeper, who gathers it with soft hands first, prioritising a clean take over a rushed one.</li><li>Once gathered cleanly, the keeper turns and returns the ball to a target (a cone or the feeder's hands) with a quick, accurate throw.</li><li>Repeat several times, resetting the ready stance between each rep.</li><li>Swap keeper and feeder roles halfway through.</li><li>Coach the key message: gather with soft hands first, then think about the return.</li><li>Easier: simplify to gathering only, with no return throw required yet. Harder: add a quick, accurate return throw after every gather.</li></ul><hr><h4>Keeper Rotation Circuit</h4><p>Small groups rotate through the keeping role at a station facing gentle deliveries, everyone experiencing the position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rotate through a keeping station behind the stumps, one student keeping while others take turns feeding gentle deliveries. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Focus on watching the ball all the way in, every single time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very gentle, predictable deliveries.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied deliveries.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that everyone in the group will get a turn as keeper so the whole group experiences the position.</li><li>The current keeper faces a series of gentle deliveries from rotating feeders, focusing on watching the ball all the way in.</li><li>After a set number of deliveries (e.g. 6), rotate to the next student as keeper.</li><li>Continue until every group member has had a turn behind the stumps.</li><li>Coach the key message: focus on watching the ball all the way in, every single time.</li><li>Easier: very gentle, predictable deliveries for every keeper. Harder: vary the deliveries once keepers are comfortable.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Game — Everyone Keeps</h3><p>Small-sided pairs cricket game with rotating wicketkeepers (swap every few minutes) so every student experiences the position; coach reinforces how the keeper supports the whole fielding effort.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and a wicketkeeper position behind the stumps, plus 3-4 other fielders. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with a wicketkeeper stationed behind the stumps for every delivery.</li><li>Swap the wicketkeeper role every few minutes (or every over) so every student experiences the position during the game.</li><li>The coach reinforces how the keeper supports the whole fielding effort — for example, backing up throws and gathering balls that get past the batter.</li><li>Continue normal batting, bowling, and fielding scoring alongside the keeper rotation.</li><li>Coach the key message: the keeper is part of the fielding team, not a separate role — everyone should experience it.</li><li>Easier: keeper stands further back with gentler deliveries. Harder: keeper stands in a more standard position with full-pace deliveries.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about the wicketkeeping stance today?</li><li>What's tricky about keeping that you didn't expect?</li><li>How does a good keeper help the rest of the fielding team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Takes a low, balanced ready stance</li><li>Uses soft hands to gather the ball safely</li><li>Stays focused and watches the ball throughout</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Rotate wicketkeeping so no single student is 'stuck' in the role; use extra-soft balls and closer distances for any student anxious about the position.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Read the Gaps":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading the game — where to hit and where to field</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding the bigger picture of the game — reading the whole field, not just the ball in front of you.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, cones marking field positions</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety with bats and balls in play simultaneously</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read the field to decide where to hit as a batter, or where to position as a fielder.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can spot a gap in the field to aim my hit toward</li><li>I can position myself in the field based on where the batter is likely to hit</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Line &amp; Length Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair, with 2-3 cones per pair to mark short line-and-length style shuffle zones.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner calls out a zone (short, good length, full — matching today's bowling language) and the other must shuffle sideways or step forward/back to 'stand' in that zone as fast as possible.</li><li>The caller mixes up the order and pace of calls, keeping their partner moving continuously.</li><li>Swap caller and mover roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners get the movement work.</li><li>This builds the same quick, controlled footwork used in bowling run-ups and fielding, while raising heart rate through varied, reactive movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging quick, controlled shuffles rather than sloppy footwork.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balance used in bowling and fielding.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan the field before committing to a shot or fielding position.</td><td>Reading the situation first leads to better decisions than reacting blindly.</td></tr><tr><td>Look for gaps or overloads in the field placement.</td><td>Gaps are the space that batting or fielding decisions should target.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate what you see to your teammates.</td><td>Shared awareness helps the whole team make better decisions.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spot the Gap</h4><p>Batters look at a set fielding formation (cones marking fielders) and call out where the biggest gap is before hitting off a tee.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual batting station with a tee, junior bat, and cones set up around the hitting area to represent a fielding formation with one obvious gap. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the whole field, not just straight ahead.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious, exaggerated gap.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Subtle, evenly-spread field.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set the cone 'fielders' in a formation with a clear gap before each batter's turn.</li><li>The batter looks at the formation and calls out loud where they think the biggest gap is before hitting off the tee.</li><li>The batter then hits, aiming toward the gap they called.</li><li>Reset the field (moving cones slightly) between turns so batters keep reading fresh formations.</li><li>Coach the key message: look at the whole field, not just straight ahead.</li><li>Easier: obvious, exaggerated gap. Harder: subtle, evenly-spread field with no obvious gap.</li></ul><hr><h4>Field the Likely Hit</h4><p>Fielders practise reading a batter's stance/setup and positioning themselves where a hit is likely to go, coach confirms good reads.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of fielders spread around a batter who is in their stance (no hit yet), roughly 8-10m radius. No ball needed for this reading exercise.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the batter's setup for early clues about where they might hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach hints at the likely direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fielders read it independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The batter sets up in their normal stance and setup, as if about to hit.</li><li>Fielders study the batter's stance and body position for clues about where a hit is likely to go, then quietly move to a position they think covers it.</li><li>The coach confirms whether the read looks sound before revealing what the batter was actually thinking.</li><li>Rotate the batter and fielders regularly so everyone practises reading a stance.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the batter's setup for early clues about where they might hit.</li><li>Easier: the coach gives a hint about the likely direction. Harder: fielders read the setup completely independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gap-Hitting Challenge</h4><p>Batters score bonus points for placing a hit through a called gap rather than just making contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual or pairs batting station with a tee or soft toss, cones marking a fielding formation with clear gaps. Soft/modified balls, junior bats.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Placement matters as much as power here.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger gaps, more of them.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, more precise gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set a fielding formation using cones before each batter's turn, with the gaps clearly visible.</li><li>The batter calls a target gap, then hits, scoring a bonus point for placing the ball through the called gap rather than just making clean contact.</li><li>A clean hit that misses the called gap still counts as contact but doesn't earn the placement bonus.</li><li>Rotate turns and reset the field between batters.</li><li>Coach the key message: placement matters as much as power here.</li><li>Easier: bigger gaps, more of them available. Harder: smaller, more precise gaps to aim for.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Game — Placement Focus</h3><p>Pairs cricket game where the coach specifically celebrates batters who place a hit into space and fielders who correctly read and cover the likely hitting zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders positioned across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with fielders positioned in a realistic spread across the field.</li><li>The coach specifically celebrates batters who place a hit into open space to beat the field, and fielders who correctly read and cover the likely hitting zone before the ball is even hit.</li><li>Call out these moments as they happen so both batting and fielding reads are visibly valued, not just runs or wickets.</li><li>Rotate batting pairs, bowlers, and fielding positions every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: reading the game — where to hit, where to field — is a skill worth celebrating on its own.</li><li>Easier: fewer fielders with bigger obvious gaps. Harder: a fuller field where gaps are harder to spot and cover.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide where to hit (or where to field) today?</li><li>What helped you read the game rather than just reacting?</li><li>What happens when a batter or fielder doesn't read the field?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the field before committing to a hit or fielding position</li><li>Places a hit with purpose rather than just making contact</li><li>Positions in the field based on reading the batter, not standing still</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use simplified, exaggerated field setups initially for students building this reading skill, moving to realistic spacing as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Run or Not?":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making — run or not?</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — A good running decision has a real impact on your team's score and your partner's safety — thinking ahead matters here.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, stumps/markers, cones for creases</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; clear running lanes, no diving/sliding into creases</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make a quick, good decision about whether to run after hitting the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can judge how well the ball was fielded to decide run or no run</li><li>I can commit fully and communicate my decision to my partner immediately</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Line &amp; Length Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair, with 2-3 cones per pair to mark short line-and-length style shuffle zones.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner calls out a zone (short, good length, full — matching today's bowling language) and the other must shuffle sideways or step forward/back to 'stand' in that zone as fast as possible.</li><li>The caller mixes up the order and pace of calls, keeping their partner moving continuously.</li><li>Swap caller and mover roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners get the movement work.</li><li>This builds the same quick, controlled footwork used in bowling run-ups and fielding, while raising heart rate through varied, reactive movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging quick, controlled shuffles rather than sloppy footwork.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balance used in bowling and fielding.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch where the ball is fielded before deciding to run.</td><td>A rushed decision without checking the fielder's position often ends in a run-out.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate clearly and early with your batting partner.</td><td>Both batters need to agree before committing to a run.</td></tr><tr><td>Hold your ground if the fielding team has the ball under control.</td><td>A cautious 'no' is often safer than a risky run.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fielded Well or Not?</h4><p>Batters hit off a tee/toss and must instantly judge (based on where and how quickly the ball is fielded) whether to call for a run.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual or pairs batting station with a tee or soft toss, junior bat, and a fielder placed in the hitting area at a distance the coach controls. Soft/modified balls, cones for creases.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Read the fielder's position and speed the moment you hit, don't wait.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fielding deliberately slow/fast to make the decision obvious.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fielding varies unpredictably.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The batter hits off the tee or toss while a fielder (deliberately positioned well or poorly by the coach) reacts to field it.</li><li>The instant the ball is hit, the batter must judge how quickly and cleanly the ball is being fielded and decide whether to call for a run.</li><li>The batter calls their decision out loud immediately, then the coach confirms whether it was a sound read.</li><li>Rotate batters and fielders regularly, varying the fielding setup each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: read the fielder's position and speed the moment you hit, don't wait.</li><li>Easier: the coach deliberately fields obviously slow or fast to make the decision clear-cut. Harder: fielding varies unpredictably from hit to hit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Quick Call Challenge</h4><p>Pairs practise making and calling their run decision within a very short time limit after the hit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand ready to run between two creases about 10m apart, with a hit or feed triggering the decision, and a short time limit (a coach count or clap) for the call. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your first read and call it loudly.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time to decide.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Very short time to decide.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>After the ball is hit or fielded, the pair has a short, clearly-signalled time limit to make and call their run/no-run decision.</li><li>Both partners must respond to whichever call is made within the time limit — hesitation counts as a missed decision.</li><li>Repeat several times, keeping the time pressure consistent so students get used to deciding quickly.</li><li>Rotate pairs and who calls the decision.</li><li>Coach the key message: trust your first read and call it loudly.</li><li>Easier: allow a longer time to decide. Harder: shorten the time limit further.</li></ul><hr><h4>Decision Under Pressure</h4><p>Small-sided game where the coach occasionally asks a batter to explain their run/no-run decision straight after making it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game across a modest area with stumps, bats, and 4-5 fielders. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be ready to explain your reasoning, not just react.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer check-ins.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More frequent check-ins.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play the small-sided game as normal, with batters making run/no-run decisions on every hit.</li><li>Occasionally — not every time — the coach pauses play right after a decision and asks the batter to briefly explain their reasoning.</li><li>Accept any reasonable explanation, even if the outcome wasn't perfect — the goal is thinking it through, not being right every time.</li><li>Continue play after each brief check-in, rotating roles regularly.</li><li>Coach the key message: be ready to explain your reasoning, not just react.</li><li>Easier: check in less frequently. Harder: check in more often, keeping decision-making front of mind.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pairs Cricket — Decision Focus</h3><p>Small-sided pairs cricket game where the coach highlights good running decisions (even when the outcome wasn't perfect) as much as runs scored.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with running decisions happening naturally on every hit.</li><li>The coach highlights good running decisions specifically — including ones where the batter read the situation well even if the outcome (run out, no run gained) wasn't ideal.</li><li>Call these moments out separately from runs actually scored, so decision quality is visibly valued on its own.</li><li>Rotate batting pairs, bowlers, and fielders every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: a good decision is a good decision, whatever the outcome — that's what we're building today.</li><li>Easier: slower fielding gives batters more time to decide. Harder: sharper fielding forces tighter, faster decisions.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide whether to run today?</li><li>What helped you commit to your decision quickly?</li><li>Can you describe a moment your decision worked out well?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reads the fielding situation quickly after the hit</li><li>Commits fully and calls clearly once a decision is made</li><li>Can explain their reasoning when asked</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a brief 'freeze and decide' pause for students still building confidence with fast decision-making, gradually removing it as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Set the Field":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fielding placements &amp; team shape</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Serving the team through a fielding position that isn't glamorous but is genuinely needed — good field placement is a gift to the whole team.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, cones marking field zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety with bats and balls in play simultaneously</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to position a fielding team sensibly across the field to cover likely hitting areas.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can position myself in a fielding role that covers space, not just stand near a friend</li><li>I can adjust my position as the batter or game situation changes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Line &amp; Length Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair, with 2-3 cones per pair to mark short line-and-length style shuffle zones.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner calls out a zone (short, good length, full — matching today's bowling language) and the other must shuffle sideways or step forward/back to 'stand' in that zone as fast as possible.</li><li>The caller mixes up the order and pace of calls, keeping their partner moving continuously.</li><li>Swap caller and mover roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners get the movement work.</li><li>This builds the same quick, controlled footwork used in bowling run-ups and fielding, while raising heart rate through varied, reactive movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging quick, controlled shuffles rather than sloppy footwork.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balance used in bowling and fielding.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Understand your fielding position's specific job.</td><td>Knowing your job means faster reactions when the ball comes your way.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your position slightly based on the batter and situation.</td><td>Fielding shape should respond to the game, not stay fixed all match.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with nearby fielders about coverage.</td><td>Reduces gaps and overlaps in the team's fielding shape.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Cover the Zones</h4><p>Small groups position themselves across marked field zones, adjusting as a coach or captain calls changes, no ball yet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 stand across marked field zones (cones dividing a rough semicircle into 5-6 sections), no ball needed for this positioning exercise.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the whole field shape, not just your own spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer zones, obvious gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More zones, subtle adjustments needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Mark out the field zones with cones before starting, showing the group the full layout.</li><li>Students position themselves one per zone, then the coach or a caller announces a change (e.g. 'shift zones one right').</li><li>The whole group adjusts their positions together, aiming to keep every zone covered with no gaps.</li><li>Repeat with several different change calls, checking as a group whether zones stayed covered.</li><li>Coach the key message: look at the whole field shape, not just your own spot.</li><li>Easier: fewer zones with obvious gaps to notice. Harder: more zones with subtler adjustments needed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Field Set</h4><p>A rotating 'captain' sets the fielding positions for their team before each short game segment, explaining their thinking briefly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 with a rotating 'captain' role, cones to mark field positions, set up before each short game segment. No ball needed for the setting phase.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Base your field setting on where the batter is likely to hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach supports the captain's choices.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Captain sets the field independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student is nominated captain and sets the fielding positions for their team using cones, explaining briefly why each fielder is placed there.</li><li>The rest of the team takes up the positions the captain has set.</li><li>Play a short game segment with that field set, then reset and rotate to a new captain for the next segment.</li><li>Continue rotating captains so several students get a turn setting the field.</li><li>Coach the key message: base your field setting on where the batter is likely to hit.</li><li>Easier: the coach supports and guides the captain's choices. Harder: the captain sets the field completely independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gap Coverage Challenge</h4><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team earns bonus points for having no obvious gap left uncovered when a hit goes through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game with stumps, bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area, cones marking approximate field zones. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk to each other to check the whole field is covered.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler field, fewer positions.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full field, more positions to manage.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play the small-sided game as normal, with fielders responsible for covering the marked zones.</li><li>The fielding team earns a bonus point any time a hit goes through without an obvious gap being left uncovered — judged by the coach.</li><li>If a hit does find an obvious gap, briefly pause to discuss as a team how the field shape could adjust next time.</li><li>Rotate fielding positions and batters regularly.</li><li>Coach the key message: talk to each other to check the whole field is covered.</li><li>Easier: a simpler field with fewer positions to manage. Harder: a fuller field with more positions to manage and communicate about.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Game — Team Shape Focus</h3><p>Pairs cricket game where the coach specifically highlights good fielding team shape and communication, in addition to any wickets or stops.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with the fielding team setting and adjusting their field shape as the game goes on.</li><li>The coach specifically highlights good fielding team shape and communication — fielders talking to cover gaps and adjusting between deliveries — in addition to any wickets or stops made.</li><li>Call out visible examples of good team shape as they happen so the whole group sees what it looks like in a live game.</li><li>Rotate batting pairs, bowlers, and fielders every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is the whole block coming together — reading gaps, backing up, and now setting the field as a team.</li><li>Easier: fielders keep the same simple shape throughout. Harder: fielders actively re-set their shape between overs.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did good fielding team shape look like today?</li><li>How did you decide where to position yourself?</li><li>What happened when the field wasn't well covered?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Positions to cover space rather than clustering near others</li><li>Adjusts position as the batter/game situation changes</li><li>Communicates with teammates about field coverage</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a simplified field with fewer positions and clear markers for students who find spatial team-shape decisions challenging.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Pairs Play":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided modified games — pairs cricket</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Working together warmly with a batting or fielding partner — pairs cricket relies on genuine teamwork between just two people.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, stumps/markers, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard modified-game safety, soft/modified balls only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply our batting, bowling, and fielding skills together in a pairs cricket format.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a real small-sided game</li><li>I can support my batting partner and communicate clearly throughout</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a circle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the fielding-shape thinking from this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply your batting, bowling, and fielding technique in a real game setting.</td><td>This lesson tests the unit's skills together under game conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with your partner throughout.</td><td>Pairs cricket relies on constant coordination between batting partners.</td></tr><tr><td>Make decisions based on the real situation, not a fixed plan.</td><td>Reading the game beats sticking rigidly to a plan that doesn't fit.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pairs Rules Walkthrough</h4><p>In pairs, students walk through the basic pairs cricket format (shared batting, both running, retiring after a set number of runs) at a slow pace to learn the structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or small groups gathered around a set of stumps, junior bats, and soft/modified balls, walking through the format rather than playing at speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know the format before the pace increases.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach talks through each step.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Pairs run through independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the pairs cricket format step by step: two batters share the batting, both run together, and the pair retires after reaching a set number of runs (e.g. 10 or 20) to let another pair bat.</li><li>Walk through a slow-motion example: a hit, a call, a run, and a retirement, pausing to check understanding at each step.</li><li>Have a pair try the full sequence themselves at a slow, deliberate pace with the coach narrating each part.</li><li>Repeat with a different pair so more than one group tries the walkthrough.</li><li>Coach the key message: know the format before the pace increases.</li><li>Easier: the coach talks through each step as the pair goes. Harder: pairs run through the format independently, only checking in if unsure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mini Pairs Match</h4><p>Short pairs cricket matches (a few overs each) between multiple pairs on small stations, applying batting, bowling, and fielding together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up multiple small stations (2-3, depending on class size), each with stumps, junior bats, and soft/modified balls, running short pairs cricket matches of a few overs each.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate constantly with your batting partner.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More overs, simpler scoring.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fewer overs, full scoring rules.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Split the class into pairs and small fielding teams, sending pairs to different stations to play short matches simultaneously.</li><li>Play a set number of overs (e.g. 3-4) per match, applying batting, bowling, and fielding skills together for the first time this block.</li><li>Batting pairs communicate constantly, calling runs clearly as practised earlier in the unit.</li><li>Rotate pairs and fielding teams between stations after each mini match so different groups play each other.</li><li>Coach the key message: communicate constantly with your batting partner.</li><li>Easier: more overs per match with simpler scoring (just count runs). Harder: fewer overs with full scoring rules applied.</li></ul><hr><h4>Skill Spotlight Rotation</h4><p>Within the mini matches, the coach calls out a skill focus for a few balls at a time (e.g. 'placement focus,' 'running focus') for players to specifically apply.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Within the same mini-match stations from Mini Pairs Match, no extra equipment needed beyond what's already set up.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer focus changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Frequent focus changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>While mini matches continue, the coach periodically calls out a specific skill focus for the next few balls — for example 'placement focus' or 'running focus.'</li><li>Players adjust their play immediately to emphasise the called skill without stopping the match.</li><li>After a few balls, the coach calls a new focus (or 'free play') and players adapt again.</li><li>Continue cycling through different focuses across the remaining match time.</li><li>Coach the key message: adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</li><li>Easier: fewer focus changes, giving players more time in each one. Harder: frequent focus changes to test quick adaptation.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Pairs Cricket Match</h3><p>Multiple pairs play a full, timed pairs cricket match on small stations, rotating batting/bowling/fielding roles — the complete modified format in action.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up multiple small stations across the space, each with stumps, junior bats, soft/modified balls, and enough pairs/fielders for a full timed match. A stopwatch or visible clock for timing.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Run full, timed pairs cricket matches at each station simultaneously, using the complete format walked through earlier in the lesson.</li><li>Batting, bowling, and fielding roles rotate through the match as pairs retire and new pairs come in.</li><li>Keep matches moving at a good pace — remind pairs to call runs clearly and fielders to back each other up, drawing on the whole unit so far.</li><li>At the end of the timed period, briefly share how each station's matches went.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is the modified format in full action — everything from the unit is in play at once.</li><li>Easier: longer time per match with simpler scoring. Harder: shorter, sharper matches with full scoring rules.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you enjoy about playing a full pairs cricket match today?</li><li>How did you and your partner communicate and support each other?</li><li>What skill from earlier in the unit did you use most today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Communicates clearly with their batting/fielding partner</li><li>Understands and follows the pairs cricket format</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify the format (fewer overs, generous retiring scores) for classes newer to cricket; increase structure toward the full ANZ Junior Game format as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Line & Length":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bowling tactics — line and length concepts</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your bowling options and making an informed choice, rather than bowling the same way every time without thinking.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones/target zones, stumps/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Same safe technique requirements as earlier bowling lessons</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning simple bowling tactics — choosing a line (direction) and length (distance) to make it harder for a batter.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a consistent line and length for my deliveries</li><li>I can adjust my line/length based on how a batter is playing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a circle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the fielding-shape thinking from this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Choose a consistent line and length to bowl to.</td><td>Line and length together create the difficulty for the batter, more than raw pace.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust length based on what's troubling the batter.</td><td>Tactical bowling responds to what's actually happening in the game.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay consistent rather than trying something different every ball.</td><td>Consistency builds pressure over several balls, not just one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Line Choice Practice</h4><p>Bowlers practise aiming deliveries at different lines (toward the stumps, wide of the stumps) using target zones, focusing on choosing deliberately.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Bowlers use their run-up lane, with target zones marked to represent different lines — one toward the stumps, one wide of the stumps — roughly 5-6m from release. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your line before you start your run-up, not mid-delivery.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, more precise zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the two line options: aiming toward the stumps (attacking the wicket) or wide of the stumps (a different tactical choice).</li><li>Before each delivery, the bowler decides and states out loud which line they're going for.</li><li>The bowler runs up and delivers, aiming for the chosen line's target zone.</li><li>Rotate bowlers through steadily, mixing which line is chosen each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide your line before you start your run-up, not mid-delivery.</li><li>Easier: bigger target zones for each line. Harder: smaller, more precise zones.</li></ul><hr><h4>Length Ladder</h4><p>Bowlers aim for different length zones (short, good length, full) marked on the pitch, scoring points for landing in the called zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 2-3 length zones (short, good length, full) along the pitch using cones or tape, roughly evenly spaced between the bowler and the stumps. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A consistent length is more valuable than an occasional perfect one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, bigger length zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More, smaller length zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Show the three length zones clearly before starting, explaining what 'short,' 'good length,' and 'full' mean in terms of where the ball bounces.</li><li>Bowlers deliver one at a time, aiming to land the ball in whichever zone is called before the delivery.</li><li>Score a point for each ball landing in the correct called zone.</li><li>Rotate bowlers and call a variety of lengths across turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: a consistent length is more valuable than an occasional perfect one.</li><li>Easier: fewer, bigger length zones. Harder: more, smaller length zones.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Batter, Adjust</h4><p>Bowlers face a batter (or simulated batter) and must adjust their line/length based on how the batter is playing, coach discusses the choices afterward.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a bowler facing a batter (or a simulated batter using a cone/marker if no batter yet), stumps as the target, roughly 6-8m apart. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice what's working and adjust — don't repeat a plan that isn't working.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests adjustments.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bowler reads and adjusts independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The bowler delivers a short over of balls to the batter, watching closely how the batter responds to each delivery.</li><li>Based on what's working or not working, the bowler adjusts their line and/or length for the next ball.</li><li>After the over, the coach discusses with the bowler what they noticed and why they made the adjustments they did.</li><li>Rotate to a new bowler and repeat.</li><li>Coach the key message: notice what's working and adjust — don't repeat a plan that isn't working.</li><li>Easier: the coach suggests adjustments during the over. Harder: the bowler reads and adjusts completely independently.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Game — Bowling Tactics Focus</h3><p>Pairs cricket game where the coach specifically highlights good line/length choices and adjustments, not just wickets taken.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with bowlers applying line and length choices from today throughout the game.</li><li>The coach specifically highlights good line/length choices and in-game adjustments, not just wickets taken.</li><li>Call out moments where a bowler visibly changed their plan based on how a batter was playing.</li><li>Rotate bowlers, batting pairs, and fielders every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: smart bowling is about consistent, deliberate choices — celebrate the thinking, not just the results.</li><li>Easier: bigger target areas in mind for bowlers, more forgiving batters. Harder: batters actively trying to score, forcing sharper bowling choices.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What line and length did you choose today, and why?</li><li>How did you adjust your bowling based on what was happening in the game?</li><li>What did you notice about the value of consistency versus one great ball?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Chooses a deliberate line/length before bowling</li><li>Shows some consistency across deliveries</li><li>Adjusts their approach based on how the batter is playing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use simplified, larger target zones for students still building bowling accuracy and consistency, while keeping the decision-making element (choosing a zone) alive.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Find the Gap, Fill the Gap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting tactics — placement and hitting gaps</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where a hit will have the most impact, rather than just reacting without a plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, cones marking field positions</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety with bats and balls in play simultaneously</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning batting tactics — placing our hits into gaps in the field rather than just hitting hard.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can identify a gap in the field before I hit</li><li>I can adjust my shot to place the ball into that gap</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a circle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the fielding-shape thinking from this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan the field before deciding where to hit.</td><td>Placement decisions need information about where fielders are.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your shot slightly to find gaps, not just hit hard.</td><td>Smart placement beats power alone for finding boundaries or easy runs.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with your batting partner about running risks after placing a shot.</td><td>Placement decisions and running decisions are linked.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Gap Spotting</h4><p>Batters look at a set field (cones marking fielders) and call out the biggest gap before hitting off a tee/toss into it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual batting station with a tee or toss, junior bat, cones marking a set field formation with visible gaps. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the whole field, decide, then commit to your shot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious, exaggerated gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Subtle, evenly-spread field.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up a cone field formation before each turn, showing a clear gap somewhere in the layout.</li><li>The batter looks at the whole field, calls out where they see the biggest gap, then commits to their shot toward it.</li><li>The batter hits off the tee or toss, aiming for the gap they called.</li><li>Reset the field slightly differently between turns so batters keep reading fresh layouts.</li><li>Coach the key message: look at the whole field, decide, then commit to your shot.</li><li>Easier: obvious, exaggerated gaps. Harder: a subtle, evenly-spread field.</li></ul><hr><h4>Placement Points</h4><p>Batters score bonus points for placing hits into a called gap rather than just making clean contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual or pairs batting station with a tee or toss, cones marking field zones with defined gaps. Soft/modified balls, junior bats.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust your shot shape/direction to aim for the gap, not just swing the same way every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger gaps, more of them.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, more precise gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set the field formation with cones, showing the gaps clearly.</li><li>The batter hits, earning a bonus point specifically for placing the ball into a called gap, on top of any point for clean contact.</li><li>If the hit is clean but doesn't find the gap, only the contact point is earned, not the placement bonus.</li><li>Rotate turns, resetting the field between batters.</li><li>Coach the key message: adjust your shot shape/direction to aim for the gap, not just swing the same way every time.</li><li>Easier: bigger gaps, more of them available. Harder: smaller, more precise gaps to aim for.</li></ul><hr><h4>Field Adjusts, Batter Reads</h4><p>The field shifts between hits; batters must re-read the gaps each time before hitting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual batting station with cones representing the field, a helper (coach or student) who shifts 1-2 cones between each turn. Soft/modified balls, junior bats.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't assume the field is the same as last time — check again.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Field changes are obvious/announced.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Field changes are subtle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before each hit, the helper quietly shifts one or two of the cone 'fielders' to a new position, changing where the gaps are.</li><li>The batter must look again and re-read the field fresh each time, rather than assuming it's the same as the last turn.</li><li>The batter calls the gap they see, then hits toward it.</li><li>Repeat several times, shifting the field differently each turn.</li><li>Coach the key message: don't assume the field is the same as last time — check again.</li><li>Easier: field changes are obvious and announced out loud. Harder: field changes are subtle and unannounced.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Game — Placement Focus</h3><p>Pairs cricket game where the coach specifically celebrates batters who place hits into space to beat the field, alongside runs scored.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided pairs cricket game with stumps, junior bats, and 4-5 fielders positioned across a modest area (roughly 20m x 20m). Soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play pairs cricket as normal, with fielders positioned in a realistic, changing field shape.</li><li>The coach specifically celebrates batters who place hits into space to beat the field, in addition to runs scored normally.</li><li>Call out these placement moments as they happen, distinguishing a well-placed shot from a lucky one where possible.</li><li>Rotate batting pairs, bowlers, and fielders every few overs.</li><li>Coach the key message: this brings batting placement and reading the field together — the same skill from both sides of the ball this block.</li><li>Easier: fielders stay in a simple, predictable shape. Harder: fielders actively shift positions between deliveries.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide where to place your hits today?</li><li>What's the difference between hitting hard and hitting smart?</li><li>What did you notice about the field that helped you find a gap?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the field before committing to a shot</li><li>Adjusts shot placement rather than always hitting the same way</li><li>Shows awareness of where fielders are positioned</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a simplified field with fewer, larger gaps for students building placement awareness, adding complexity as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Full Match":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full small-sided games with tactics focus</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Serving your team throughout a full match — batting, bowling, and fielding — with faithful effort in whichever role is needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, stumps/markers, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard full modified-game safety, soft/modified balls only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills and tactics together in a full small-sided cricket match.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills and tactics from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can adjust my play based on what the game situation calls for</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a circle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the fielding-shape thinking from this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply the specific tactical focus called out during play.</td><td>This lesson is about applying tactics live, not just performing skills in isolation.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly in all three disciplines — batting, bowling, fielding.</td><td>Cricket's stop-start structure still relies on continuous team communication.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your play when the first approach isn't working.</td><td>Adapting tactics is what separates good cricket from just repeating a plan.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Reminder Circuit</h4><p>Quick-fire stations lightly revisiting throwing, catching, batting, and bowling from across the unit — a refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 quick stations (throwing, catching, batting, bowling) around the space, each with the relevant equipment from earlier in the unit, groups rotating through briskly.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more natural these feel now compared to early lessons.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain this is a light refresher, not new teaching — a chance to feel how much more natural these skills are now.</li><li>Split the class into small groups and send each group to a different station.</li><li>At each station, groups spend a short, fixed time (e.g. 90 seconds) lightly practising that skill before rotating on.</li><li>Rotate through all 4 stations so every group touches throwing, catching, batting, and bowling briefly.</li><li>Coach the key message: notice how much more natural these feel now compared to early lessons.</li><li>Easier: n/a — keep this station relaxed and low-pressure for everyone. Harder: n/a — this is intentionally a light refresher, not a challenge.</li></ul><hr><h4>Tactics Call-Out</h4><p>During small games, the coach periodically calls out a tactical focus (e.g. 'placement focus,' 'line and length focus,' 'running focus') for players to specifically apply.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game with stumps, bats, and 4-5 fielders across a modest area. Soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play the small-sided game as normal.</li><li>Periodically — every few minutes — the coach calls out a tactical focus from the unit, such as 'placement focus,' 'line and length focus,' or 'running focus.'</li><li>Players immediately adjust their play to emphasise the called focus without stopping the game.</li><li>Continue cycling through different focuses across the game.</li><li>Coach the key message: adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</li><li>Easier: n/a — keep focus changes gentle and well-spaced. Harder: n/a — this activity is inherently about quick adaptation for everyone.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Read</h4><p>Each team nominates a rotating captain who reads the game and calls one tactical focus for their team during a short segment.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Within the same small-sided game, each team nominates a rotating captain for short segments of play. No extra equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Base your call on what you're actually seeing in the game.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team names a captain for a short segment of the game (a few minutes or one over).</li><li>The captain watches how the game is unfolding and calls one tactical focus for their team to apply during that segment — for example, 'let's cover the gaps better.'</li><li>The team applies the captain's call during that segment of play.</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a new player for the next segment.</li><li>Coach the key message: base your call on what you're actually seeing in the game.</li><li>Easier: n/a — the coach can prompt captains with a question if they're unsure. Harder: n/a — this role is naturally a stretch for whoever holds it.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Small-Sided Cricket Match</h3><p>Multiple small teams play a full, timed modified cricket match (ANZ Junior Game style) with all of the unit's skills and tactics live and relevant — batting, bowling, fielding, and running all in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a full, timed small-sided cricket match (ANZ Junior Game style) across a suitably sized area, with stumps, junior bats, soft/modified balls, and enough players for full batting, bowling, and fielding teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a complete, timed match using the modified format, rotating batting, bowling, and fielding roles as the game progresses.</li><li>Encourage players to draw on everything from the unit — technique, calling, running decisions, line and length, placement, and field reading — as it comes up naturally.</li><li>The coach steps back into more of a match-day observer role, only stepping in for safety or major rule clarifications.</li><li>Keep the match moving at a good pace, with clear communication expected from both batting and fielding sides.</li><li>Coach the key message: everything from the unit is live and relevant right now — trust what you've practised.</li><li>Easier: shorter overs and more generous scoring. Harder: full-length overs and standard scoring rules.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What tactic or skill from this unit did you use most successfully today?</li><li>How did the game change when a new tactical focus was called?</li><li>What are you most looking forward to for the tournament?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>Adjusts play in response to called tactical focuses</li><li>Communicates and reads the game situation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow captains' tactical calls to be simple and concrete ('everyone move in') rather than abstract, so every student can act on them regardless of tactical experience.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Cricket Carnival":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament — applying the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Reflecting on the impact this whole unit has had — on your skills, your team, and how you treat others in a game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, stumps/markers, cones, bibs (multiple colours for round-robin teams)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard game safety; manage fatigue/hydration across a tournament format, ensure fair rotation so no team is overplayed</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything from this unit — throwing, catching, fielding, batting, bowling, and teamwork — in a full mini-tournament.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can play fairly and encouragingly in a competitive setting</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a circle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the fielding-shape thinking from this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills and tactics from across the whole unit.</td><td>The tournament brings batting, bowling, and fielding together under real competitive pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates and opponents.</td><td>Cricket has a strong tradition of fair play and sportsmanship alongside competition.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on improvement is as valuable as the final result of any one game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Unit Skills Warm-Up Carousel</h4><p>Quick-fire stations revisiting a taste of the unit's key skills (throwing, catching, batting, bowling) — a light refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 quick stations (throwing, catching, batting, bowling) around the space, mirroring the Lesson 19 refresher, groups rotating through briskly.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain this is a light refresher taste of the whole unit before the tournament begins, not new teaching.</li><li>Split the class into small groups and send each group to a different station.</li><li>At each station, groups spend a short, fixed time lightly practising that skill before rotating on.</li><li>Rotate through all 4 stations so every group touches throwing, catching, batting, and bowling briefly.</li><li>Coach the key message: notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</li><li>Easier: n/a — keep this light and low-pressure. Harder: n/a — this is intentionally an easy warm-up before the tournament.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Skill Relay</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining throwing, catching, and a batting strike in sequence — a fun, competitive combination of the whole unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 3-4 line up behind a start cone, with a short course combining a throw at a target, a catch from a partner, and a batting strike off a tee, all set up in sequence over about 10-15m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth handovers between skills.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay sequence: each player throws at a target, then catches a feed from a partner, then strikes a ball off a tee, before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go,' the first player from each team works through the sequence as fast and cleanly as possible.</li><li>A step only counts as complete if done properly (target hit, clean catch, clean contact) — rushing through sloppily doesn't save time if it has to be redone.</li><li>The next teammate can't start until the previous player has completed all three parts and tagged in.</li><li>Coach the key message: smooth handovers between skills.</li><li>Easier: n/a — this relay is designed as a fun combination challenge for everyone. Harder: n/a — the mixed sequence itself is the challenge, no separate variant needed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Challenge</h4><p>Each team nominates a captain who sets one focus for their team's tournament games (e.g. 'let's focus on calling our runs clearly today') — light strategic ownership before the tournament begins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each tournament team gathers briefly before the round-robin begins, no equipment needed beyond a moment of team time.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give every captain a genuine, valued choice.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team nominates a captain for the tournament ahead.</li><li>The captain sets one simple focus for their team's games — for example, 'let's focus on calling our runs clearly today.'</li><li>The team briefly discusses and agrees to carry that focus into their tournament matches.</li><li>Captains can be reminded of their team's focus between matches by the coach if needed.</li><li>Coach the key message: give every captain a genuine, valued choice.</li><li>Easier: n/a — the coach can suggest a couple of focus options if a captain is stuck. Harder: n/a — every captain gets equal ownership of this choice regardless of experience.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Multiple small teams rotate through short pairs/small-sided cricket matches against each other across 2–3 stations, scoring simply (runs or a fair-play points system). Every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 2-3 mini-tournament stations across the space, each with stumps, junior bats, and soft/modified balls, with teams in different bib colours rotating between stations across the tournament.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Split the class into multiple small teams (bibs to distinguish colours), and organise a round-robin roster showing which teams play which stations and in what order.</li><li>Run short pairs/small-sided matches at each station, scoring simply — either runs scored or a straightforward fair-play points system agreed at the start.</li><li>Rotate teams through 2-3 stations across the session, giving every team multiple short matches against different opponents.</li><li>Manage fatigue and hydration across the tournament — build in a water/rest break between rounds, and rotate so no single team is overplayed.</li><li>Coach the key message: every skill from the whole unit is relevant somewhere in these games — enjoy seeing it all come together.</li><li>Easier: shorter matches with simpler scoring. Harder: longer matches with full scoring rules applied.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging play look like in today's tournament?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other sports or games?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills from across the unit (not just one)</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure round-robin teams to be balanced/mixed-ability; consider a simple fair-play recognition (not just a 'winning team') to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Hand-Eye Coordination":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Tracking & Catching (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Eyes On It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Tracking an object with your eyes all the way into your hands</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/foam ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Soft balls only for this early tracking work; standard spacing between pairs</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to watch a ball all the way into our hands, not look away early.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>keep my eyes on the ball right until it touches my hands</li><li>catch a gently thrown ball cleanly using both hands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Track &amp; Toss Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own soft/foam ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate tossing the ball gently a short distance above your own head and catching it while jogging on the spot.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, each tossing and catching their own ball as they move.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'higher toss' (toss a little higher before catching), 'clap toss' (clap once before the catch), or 'partner toss' (quick toss to the nearest person, then keep jogging).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rates and warming up eyes-to-hands timing, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to reach without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, arms across the chest, calves, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball the whole way, right into your hands, not just until it's close.</td><td>Looking away early is the single biggest reason a catch is dropped — the eyes need to finish the job the hands are about to do.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your hands ready and in front of your body before the ball arrives.</td><td>Hands prepared early can adjust smoothly instead of stabbing late at a moving target.</td></tr><tr><td>Relax your hands rather than tensing up.</td><td>Stiff hands bounce a ball away; relaxed hands absorb it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Watch It In</h4><p>In pairs, one gently underarm throws a soft ball while the partner calls out the colour/pattern on the ball just before it lands in their hands.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3m apart across the space, one soft/foam ball per pair (ideally with a visible colour pattern or marking on it). No cones needed for this game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say it as it lands, not before.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, brighter ball, thrown from closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller ball, thrown from further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random fake throws (partner pretends to throw)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: underarm throw a soft ball gently to a partner and show how to call out the colour or pattern on the ball just as it lands in your hands, not before.</li><li>One partner starts with the ball and gently underarm throws it to the other.</li><li>The receiver watches the ball all the way in and calls out the colour/pattern the instant it lands in their hands.</li><li>There's no scoring — the focus is on the timing of the call, not speed or competition; if the call comes too early, the thrower says 'watch it all the way' and they try again.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'say it as it lands, not before.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger, brighter ball and throw from closer; for 'harder', use a smaller ball and throw from further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Eyes Stay On</h4><p>Standing in a circle, students pass a ball around while the teacher checks that eyes stay on the ball right up until it's caught, not on the thrower's face.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class (or groups of 8-10) in a circle roughly 5-6m across, one ball per circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Follow the ball's whole flight path.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, higher passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, flatter passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Teacher randomly calls 'switch direction'</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: eyes must stay on the ball for its whole flight, not flick to the thrower's face.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and passes it to anyone else in the circle, calling their name as they throw.</li><li>Students continue passing around the circle in this way, with the teacher walking around checking that eyes are following the ball, not the thrower.</li><li>There's no scoring — if the teacher spots eyes drifting, they call that student's name gently as a reminder and play continues without stopping.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'follow the ball's whole flight path.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, higher passes; for 'harder', use faster, flatter passes; the teacher can randomly call 'switch direction' to keep focus sharp (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Catch &amp; Freeze</h4><p>In small groups, players throw and catch on the move, freezing in a strong catching position the instant they've secured the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Freeze the moment it's in your hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walking pace only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Light jogging pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: catch a thrown ball and immediately freeze in a strong, balanced catching position (ball held firmly in front of the body).</li><li>Students move around slowly inside their group space while one player has the ball; that player throws to a teammate on the move.</li><li>The receiver catches the ball and freezes the instant it's secured — the group briefly checks the freeze looks stable before play continues.</li><li>Play resumes immediately after each freeze — the receiver becomes the new thrower and picks their next target.</li><li>Rotate who starts with the ball each new round so everyone gets equal turns as thrower and catcher.</li><li>For 'easier', keep to walking pace only; for 'harder', allow light jogging pace.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Track &amp; Catch Tag</h3><p>4v4 keep-away style game where a clean tracked catch (eyes visibly on the ball throughout) earns a bonus point on top of standard possession scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a standard keep-away game — teams try to keep possession by passing, while the other team tries to intercept.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Award standard points for consecutive completed passes, plus one bonus point any time a catch is made with eyes visibly tracked onto the ball the whole way in (spotted by the teacher or a nominated 'tracker' on the sideline).</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'eyes on it the whole way in earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What happened when you looked away from the ball too early?</li><li>How did getting your hands ready early change your catch?</li><li>What did a 'freeze' catch feel like compared to a rushed one?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps eyes on the ball right into the hands</li><li>Prepares hands early, in front of the body</li><li>Catches with relaxed, absorbing hands</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger, brighter, softer balls and slower throws for students building tracking confidence; allow two hands for every catch early on.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Soft Hands":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Cushioning/absorbing the ball on catching rather than catching rigid</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair (variety of ball sizes), cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep throws controlled and at a catchable height to avoid jammed fingers</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to catch with soft hands, giving a little as the ball arrives so it doesn't bounce away.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>let my hands 'give' slightly as I catch</li><li>catch without the ball bouncing out of my hands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Track &amp; Toss Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own soft/foam ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate tossing the ball gently a short distance above your own head and catching it while jogging on the spot.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, each tossing and catching their own ball as they move.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'higher toss' (toss a little higher before catching), 'clap toss' (clap once before the catch), or 'partner toss' (quick toss to the nearest person, then keep jogging).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rates and warming up eyes-to-hands timing, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to reach without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, arms across the chest, calves, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Let your hands move slightly backward as the ball arrives, absorbing its speed.</td><td>Giving with the catch spreads out the force so the ball settles instead of bouncing off stiff hands.</td></tr><tr><td>Cup your hands rather than holding them flat.</td><td>A cupped shape matches the ball better and stops it slipping straight through.</td></tr><tr><td>Bring the ball in toward your body once it's caught.</td><td>Securing it against your body locks the catch in, especially under any pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Give and Catch</h4><p>In pairs, one throws softly while the other practises pulling their hands back slightly on contact, exaggerating the 'give'.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3m apart across the space, one ball per pair (mix of sizes available so pairs can try different ones across the lesson).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Let it sink in, don't stop it dead.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very soft, slow throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Firmer throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Partner varies throw speed without warning</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: catch a ball and pull your hands back slightly at the moment of contact, 'giving' with the throw rather than stopping it dead.</li><li>One partner softly throws the ball while the other practises this exaggerated 'give' on every catch.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice as thrower and catcher.</li><li>There's no scoring — the focus is on feeling the difference between a soft, giving catch and a rigid, jarring one.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'let it sink in, don't stop it dead.'</li><li>For 'easier', use very soft, slow throws; for 'harder', use firmer throws; the thrower can vary throw speed without warning to keep the catcher adjusting (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Cup It</h4><p>Students catch a range of different sized balls (small, medium, large) focusing on shaping their hands into a cup before each catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, with a mix of small, medium, and large balls shared between each group (at least 3 different sizes per group).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Shape your hands before it arrives, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Same ball size each turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball size changes every throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mystery ball swap mid-round</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: shape your hands into a cup before the ball arrives, matching the shape to the size of the ball coming.</li><li>One student throws a ball of their choice to a teammate, calling out the size as they throw ('small one!').</li><li>The receiver shapes their hands into a cup before the ball arrives, based on that call, then catches.</li><li>Rotate the thrower role after every 4-5 throws so everyone gets equal turns catching different sizes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'shape your hands before it arrives, not after.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the same ball size each turn; for 'harder', change ball size every throw, or swap in a mystery ball mid-round for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Bounce-Out Battle</h4><p>In small groups, players count how many catches in a row they can make without the ball bouncing out of their hands, resetting the count if it does.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Soft hands beat a strong grab.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time between catches.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker succession of catches.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the group counts, out loud, how many catches in a row they can make without the ball bouncing out of anyone's hands.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and throws to a teammate; that student catches and immediately throws on to someone else.</li><li>The group keeps a shared running count out loud as the ball moves around; if it bounces out, the count resets to zero and play restarts immediately with the ball going to whoever dropped it.</li><li>Continue for the full activity time, with each group trying to beat their own best streak.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'soft hands beat a strong grab.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more time between catches; for 'harder', use quicker succession of catches.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Soft Hands Showdown</h3><p>4v2 keep-away where every clean, secure catch (no bounce-out) scores a bonus point, on top of possession scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 6, forming teams of 4 and 2 per game (4v2 keep-away). Mark a grid roughly 12m x 12m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: the team of 4 tries to keep possession against the team of 2 defenders inside the grid.</li><li>The team of 4 starts with the ball and passes it around, trying to keep it away from the two defenders.</li><li>Award standard possession points, plus a bonus point every time a catch is clean and secure with no bounce-out (spotted by the teacher).</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or bounces out uncaught, defenders swap in for two of the attacking players — restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate the two defenders every 2-3 minutes so everyone experiences both attacking and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'soft hands beat a strong grab — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did 'giving' with the catch actually feel like?</li><li>Did cupping your hands change how secure the catch felt?</li><li>What happened when you tried to catch with stiff or flat hands?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Hands give slightly on contact</li><li>Hands cupped before the ball arrives</li><li>Ball is secured against the body after catching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use larger, softer balls for students still building grip confidence; allow extra attempts without penalty while the 'give' technique develops.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Two Hands or One?":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Choosing appropriate catching technique (two-handed vs one-handed) for the situation</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; controlled throw heights when practising one-handed reaching catches</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning when to use two hands and when one hand is needed to catch successfully.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>use two hands when I have time to get in position</li><li>reach and catch with one hand when the ball is out of two-hand range</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Track &amp; Toss Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own soft/foam ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate tossing the ball gently a short distance above your own head and catching it while jogging on the spot.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, each tossing and catching their own ball as they move.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'higher toss' (toss a little higher before catching), 'clap toss' (clap once before the catch), or 'partner toss' (quick toss to the nearest person, then keep jogging).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rates and warming up eyes-to-hands timing, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to reach without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, arms across the chest, calves, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use two hands whenever you have time to get into position.</td><td>Two hands give the most secure, reliable catch, so they're the default whenever possible.</td></tr><tr><td>Reach with one hand only when the ball is genuinely out of two-hand range.</td><td>A one-handed reach should be a last resort for distance, not a shortcut when two hands were available.</td></tr><tr><td>Recover quickly into a balanced position after a one-handed catch.</td><td>A one-handed catch is only useful if you can do something with the ball straight afterwards.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Two Hands First</h4><p>In pairs, receivers must get their whole body in line and use two hands whenever the throw is within easy reach.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet, not just your hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Throws always within easy reach.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Occasional throws just out of easy reach to test judgement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: when a throw is within easy reach, step or shuffle so your whole body lines up behind the ball and catch with two hands.</li><li>One partner throws to the other, aiming throws that are within easy reach.</li><li>The receiver moves their feet to get in line with the ball and catches with two hands every time.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move your feet, not just your hands.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep throws always within easy reach; for 'harder', mix in occasional throws just out of easy reach so the receiver has to judge whether to stretch.</li></ul><hr><h4>Just Out of Reach</h4><p>Partner throws balls slightly wide so the receiver must judge whether to stretch with one hand or step to use two.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4-5m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Choose the option that actually gets the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Throws only slightly wide.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Throws further out, requiring a genuine reach decision.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mixed easy and hard throws in the same set</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: judging whether to stretch with one hand or step across to use two hands, depending on how wide the throw is.</li><li>One partner throws balls slightly wide of the receiver, left or right.</li><li>The receiver must decide in the moment — stretch with one hand, or step to get two hands on it — and go with whichever actually gets the ball.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'choose the option that actually gets the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', throw only slightly wide; for 'harder', throw further out to force a genuine reach decision; mixing easy and hard throws in the same set adds extra unpredictability (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Call It</h4><p>Before each throw, the receiver calls 'two' or 'one' based on where they think the ball will arrive, then attempts the catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your first read.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Thrower signals direction early.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No warning given.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Thrower occasionally fakes direction</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the receiver calls 'two' or 'one' out loud before each throw arrives, based on where they think the ball will land, then attempts that catch.</li><li>One partner prepares to throw; the receiver watches their set-up and calls their prediction just before or as the ball is released.</li><li>The receiver then attempts the catch using the method they called.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice predicting and catching.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust your first read.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the thrower signal direction early; for 'harder', give no warning at all; the thrower can occasionally fake direction for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Catch Call</h3><p>Small-sided keep-away game where players earn a bonus point for correctly judging and executing either a two-handed or one-handed catch based on the ball's position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a keep-away game where a correctly judged and executed catch — two-handed for close balls, one-handed for wide ones — earns a bonus point.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a player is seen choosing the right catch method for where the ball actually arrives.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'read where it's going, then choose the catch that gets it.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide between one hand and two?</li><li>When did a one-handed catch actually save the play?</li><li>What happens if you always try to catch with just one hand?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses two hands by default when there's time</li><li>Reaches with one hand only when genuinely needed</li><li>Recovers into a balanced position after catching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow two-handed catches for every attempt for students still building confidence; introduce one-handed reaching only once two-handed catching is secure.</p>"},"Lesson 4: High and Low":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Adjusting hands and body position to different flight heights (high balls, low balls, bouncing balls)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; keep bouncing-ball throws controlled so they don't bounce into other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to adjust our hands and body to catch balls that arrive high, low, or bouncing.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>adjust my hand position for a high, low, or bouncing ball</li><li>get my body behind the ball whenever I can</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Track &amp; Toss Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own soft/foam ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate tossing the ball gently a short distance above your own head and catching it while jogging on the spot.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, each tossing and catching their own ball as they move.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'higher toss' (toss a little higher before catching), 'clap toss' (clap once before the catch), or 'partner toss' (quick toss to the nearest person, then keep jogging).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rates and warming up eyes-to-hands timing, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to reach without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, arms across the chest, calves, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Turn your hands to match the height of the ball — fingers up for high balls, fingers down for low ones.</td><td>Hand shape needs to match the ball's approach angle, or it simply won't be secure.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your body behind the ball's line whenever possible.</td><td>A body positioned behind the ball gives a second chance to control it even if the hands don't get it clean.</td></tr><tr><td>Watch a bouncing ball all the way through the bounce, not just before it.</td><td>A bounce changes the ball's path, so tracking has to continue through it, not stop beforehand.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>High or Low</h4><p>Partner throws a mix of high, chest-height, and low balls, and the receiver adjusts hand shape each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Match your hands to where it's arriving.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Clearly high or clearly low throws only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Throws close to shoulder or waist height, harder to judge.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: shaping hands differently for a high ball (fingers up, hands above chest), a chest-height ball (hands out in front), and a low ball (fingers down, hands below waist).</li><li>One partner throws a mix of high, chest-height, and low balls in no particular order.</li><li>The receiver adjusts their hand shape each time based on where the ball is arriving, and catches.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice across all three heights.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'match your hands to where it's arriving.'</li><li>For 'easier', throw clearly high or clearly low balls only; for 'harder', throw closer to shoulder or waist height, which is harder to judge.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bounce Read</h4><p>In pairs, one bounces the ball to the other, who must track it all the way through the bounce before catching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch it hit the ground, not just before.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>One controlled bounce, close range.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varying bounce height and angle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Occasional double-bounce thrown in</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: watching a ball all the way through its bounce, not looking away just before it hits the ground.</li><li>One partner bounces the ball to the other, controlling the bounce so it arrives at a reasonable height.</li><li>The receiver tracks the ball all the way through the bounce and catches it after it rises back up.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 bounces so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch it hit the ground, not just before.'</li><li>For 'easier', use one controlled bounce at close range; for 'harder', vary the bounce height and angle; an occasional double-bounce thrown in adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Body Behind It</h4><p>Small group game where receivers must move their feet to get their whole body behind the ball's line before catching, rather than reaching sideways.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feet first, then hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower-moving balls, more time to adjust.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, more varied throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every minute</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: moving your feet to get your whole body directly behind the ball's flight line, rather than reaching sideways with just your arms.</li><li>One student throws to a teammate; the receiver must shuffle or step to line their body up behind the ball before catching.</li><li>If a receiver reaches sideways instead of moving their feet, the group pauses briefly, resets, and tries that throw again.</li><li>Rotate the thrower role regularly, and swap roles every minute so everyone gets equal turns catching.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'feet first, then hands.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower-moving balls with more time to adjust; for 'harder', use quicker, more varied throws.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: All Heights</h3><p>4v4 small-sided game where a mix of high, low, and bounced passes are all in play, with a bonus point for a clean, well-adjusted catch at any height.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a keep-away game where high, low, and bounced passes are all allowed and encouraged.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a player makes a clean, well-adjusted catch at any height (high, low, or bounced).</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'match your hands to the height it arrives at.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did your hands change shape for a high ball compared to a low one?</li><li>What did tracking a bounce all the way through actually look like?</li><li>Why does getting your body behind the ball help, even if your hands don't catch it cleanly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Adjusts hand shape to match ball height</li><li>Moves feet to get body behind the ball</li><li>Tracks a bouncing ball all the way through the bounce</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start with clearly high or clearly low throws before mixing heights; allow a short pause before each throw for students still building this judgement.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Catch It All":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining tracking, soft hands, catch-type choice, and height adjustment (Block 1 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls (various sizes), bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 1 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; combined activities revisited at a controlled pace</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bring together everything from this week — tracking, soft hands, and adjusting to height — into confident catching.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>use tracking, soft hands, and the right catch type together</li><li>catch confidently across a range of heights and speeds</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Track &amp; Toss Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own soft/foam ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate tossing the ball gently a short distance above your own head and catching it while jogging on the spot.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, each tossing and catching their own ball as they move.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'higher toss' (toss a little higher before catching), 'clap toss' (clap once before the catch), or 'partner toss' (quick toss to the nearest person, then keep jogging).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rates and warming up eyes-to-hands timing, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to reach without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, arms across the chest, calves, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Trust the tracking, soft hands, and height-adjustment skills you've built this week, all at once.</td><td>A confident catch isn't one skill — it's several skills happening together without having to think about each one separately.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the right catch type instantly, without pausing to decide.</td><td>By this point the decision between one or two hands should be fast and automatic, not a hesitation.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay relaxed and ready between catches, not just during them.</td><td>Good catchers are ready before the ball arrives, not scrambling once it does.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Coordination Carousel</h4><p>Teams rotate through short stations that each revisit a Block 1 focus — tracking, soft hands, catch-type choice, height adjustment.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations around the space (roughly 5m x 5m each), one for each Block 1 focus: tracking, soft hands, catch-type choice, and height adjustment. Split the class into teams of 4-5, one team starting at each station, with balls of varying sizes distributed across the stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use everything you've practised this week.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each station revisits before starting: Station 1 tracking (Watch It In style), Station 2 soft hands (Cup It style), Station 3 catch-type choice (Call It style), Station 4 height adjustment (High or Low style).</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and begin the activity immediately on 'go'.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 2-3 minutes), teams rotate clockwise to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed all 4 stations.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'use everything you've practised this week.'</li><li>For 'easier', give longer time per station; for 'harder', give shorter time with quicker rotation; a head-to-head mirror between two teams at the same station adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Bag Catching</h4><p>In pairs, throws vary randomly in height, speed, and distance so the receiver must combine all of this week's skills on every catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Read it fresh every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable pattern of throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fully randomised throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Caller occasionally shouts a trick instruction ('clap first!')</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: throws will vary randomly in height, speed, and distance, so the receiver has to combine everything from this week on every single catch.</li><li>One partner throws with no fixed pattern — sometimes high, sometimes low, sometimes fast, sometimes soft.</li><li>The receiver reads and adjusts to each throw individually, using tracking, soft hands, and the right catch method as needed.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 8-10 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'read it fresh every time.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a predictable pattern of throws; for 'harder', use fully randomised throws; the caller can occasionally shout a trick instruction like 'clap first!' for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Streak Catch</h4><p>Small groups count their longest run of clean, confident catches in a row, mixing heights and speeds.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay relaxed, don't rush.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, generous spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker pace, tighter spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the group counts out loud their longest run of clean, confident catches in a row, mixing heights and speeds.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and throws to a teammate, who catches and throws on to someone else.</li><li>The group keeps a shared running count out loud; if a catch is dropped or fumbled, the count resets to zero and play restarts immediately.</li><li>Continue for the full activity time, encouraging groups to try to beat their own best streak.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay relaxed, don't rush.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with generous spacing; for 'harder', use a quicker pace with tighter spacing.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Catch It All Games</h3><p>Small-sided matches where teams earn a bonus point for clean, confident catches across a range of heights and speeds, wrapping up the whole block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this small-sided match brings together everything from Block 1 — tracking, soft hands, catch-type choice, and height adjustment.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a player makes a clean, confident catch across a range of heights and speeds.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'bring together everything you've learned this week — that's what wraps up Block 1.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which of this week's catching skills do you feel most confident with now?</li><li>What did combining all of them together feel like today?</li><li>What's still tricky when the ball comes in fast or awkward?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Combines tracking, soft hands, and correct catch type together</li><li>Adjusts confidently across a range of heights</li><li>Stays relaxed and ready between catches</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to nominate their own 'easier' or 'harder' station variant based on confidence; celebrate streaks of any length to build confidence going into Block 2.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Striking & Contact (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Meet the Ball":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Watching the ball onto the striking surface (bat, hand, paddle) before making contact</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft bats/paddles or hands, soft balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spacing between pairs so swings don't connect with a neighbouring group; soft balls only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to watch the ball all the way onto our striking surface before we hit it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>keep my eyes on the ball right until contact</li><li>make clean contact more often than I miss</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Strike Prep Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to shuffle side-to-side without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light athletic shuffle stance: knees bent, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up ready — the same base position used before striking a ball.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, dropping into a quick side-to-side shuffle every time the teacher calls 'shuffle', then returning to jogging on 'jog'.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'shuffle left', 'shuffle right', or 'shadow swing' (a few gentle practice swings on the spot with an imaginary bat).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rate and warming up the ready stance used for striking, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Split into pairs spread out with enough room to stretch side-by-side without touching another pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball all the way onto your striking surface, not just until you start swinging.</td><td>Most mishits happen because the eyes leave the ball a fraction too early, right when accuracy matters most.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your striking surface (hand, bat, paddle) moving early so it's ready in time.</td><td>A late-starting swing forces a rushed, inaccurate contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim for the middle of the ball, not the edges.</td><td>Centre contact is what actually sends the ball where you intend.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Watch It Meet</h4><p>In pairs, one gently feeds a soft ball for the other to strike with an open hand or soft paddle, calling out the ball's colour just before contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, one soft ball per pair, and an open hand or soft paddle each. Extra room allowed on either side of each pair so swings don't reach a neighbouring group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say it right as you hit it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, closer feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, further feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: watching the ball all the way onto your striking surface (open hand or soft paddle) and calling out its colour just as contact happens, not before.</li><li>One partner gently feeds a soft ball underarm to the other.</li><li>The receiver strikes it with an open hand or soft paddle, calling the ball's colour right as they make contact.</li><li>There's no scoring — if the call comes early, the feeder says 'watch it all the way' and they try again.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say it right as you hit it.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, closer feeds; for 'harder', use faster, further feeds.</li></ul><hr><h4>Early Swing</h4><p>Students practise starting their swing or hand movement early, so it's already in motion before the ball arrives.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, one soft ball per pair, open hand or soft paddle each.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Start moving before it gets close.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder gives a clear countdown.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No countdown given.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Feeder varies timing of the feed</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: starting the swing or hand movement early, so it's already moving before the ball arrives, rather than waiting and reacting late.</li><li>One partner feeds the ball; the other starts their swing motion as soon as they see the feed begin, aiming to already be in motion by the time the ball arrives.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'start moving before it gets close.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the feeder give a clear countdown; for 'harder', give no countdown; the feeder can vary the timing of the feed for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Middle of the Ball</h4><p>In small groups, students aim to strike the centre of a stationary or gently rolling ball, with feedback on where contact was made.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, one ball per group, open hand or soft paddle each.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Picture the middle before you swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stationary ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Slow rolling ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: aiming to strike the exact centre of the ball, and how a centred hit feels and sounds cleaner than an off-centre one.</li><li>One student rolls or gently tosses the ball toward a teammate; that student aims to strike the centre of the ball.</li><li>After each strike, the group briefly gives feedback on where contact was made (centre, edge, top, or bottom).</li><li>Rotate the striker role every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'picture the middle before you swing.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a stationary ball; for 'harder', use a slow rolling ball.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Clean Contact Challenge</h3><p>Small-sided striking game (e.g. simple bat-and-ball or hand-strike format) where clean, centred contact earns a bonus point on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. Soft bats/paddles or hands for striking, one soft ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a simple bat-and-ball or hand-strike small-sided game with standard scoring for winning a point/rally.</li><li>Start with a gentle feed or serve to begin each point.</li><li>Play the point out with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player makes clean, centred contact (spotted by the teacher or a nominated player).</li><li>After each point, restart immediately with the next feed or serve — rotate who feeds each time.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to strike.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'clean, centred contact earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What happened when your eyes left the ball too early?</li><li>Did starting your swing earlier change how clean your contact was?</li><li>What did centre contact actually feel like compared to an edge hit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Eyes stay on the ball right until contact</li><li>Striking surface begins moving early</li><li>Contact is made near the centre of the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use larger, softer, slower-moving balls for students building striking confidence; allow a stationary ball to strike from before introducing movement.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Sweet Spot":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Finding the centre/sweet spot of the striking surface for cleaner, more controlled contact</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft bats/paddles, soft balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between striking pairs; soft equipment only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to find the sweet spot on our striking surface so our hits are cleaner and more controlled.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>make contact near the middle of my bat or hand</li><li>notice the difference between a sweet-spot hit and an edge hit</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Strike Prep Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to shuffle side-to-side without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light athletic shuffle stance: knees bent, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up ready — the same base position used before striking a ball.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, dropping into a quick side-to-side shuffle every time the teacher calls 'shuffle', then returning to jogging on 'jog'.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'shuffle left', 'shuffle right', or 'shadow swing' (a few gentle practice swings on the spot with an imaginary bat).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rate and warming up the ready stance used for striking, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Split into pairs spread out with enough room to stretch side-by-side without touching another pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know roughly where the sweet spot is on your bat, paddle, or hand.</td><td>You can't aim for something you haven't identified — knowing the target is the first step.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your body position so the ball arrives at that sweet spot, not wherever it happens to land.</td><td>Moving to the ball is more reliable than hoping the ball comes to a fixed swing.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice and describe the difference in feel and sound between a clean and a mishit strike.</td><td>Recognising the feedback helps you self-correct without needing to be told every time.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Find the Spot</h4><p>Students experiment striking a slow, gently fed ball at different points on their bat/paddle/hand, noticing where it feels and sounds cleanest.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice the difference, don't just swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very slow, close feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Slightly faster feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: striking a slow, close feed at a few different points on the bat/paddle/hand and noticing how each one feels and sounds different.</li><li>One partner gently feeds a very slow, close ball; the other experiments with striking it at different points on their striking surface.</li><li>After each strike, the striker says out loud whether that felt/sounded 'clean' (centred) or not.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice the difference, don't just swing.'</li><li>For 'easier', use very slow, close feeds; for 'harder', use slightly faster feeds.</li></ul><hr><h4>Move to the Spot</h4><p>In pairs, the feeder varies where the ball arrives slightly, and the striker must move their feet so the sweet spot meets the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet to the ball, not your bat to a fixed spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Small variations in feed position.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Larger variations.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Feeder calls 'freeze' — striker must adjust instantly</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the striker must move their feet so their sweet spot meets the ball, rather than reaching their bat out to a fixed spot.</li><li>The feeder varies where the ball arrives slightly — a little left, right, closer, or further — on each feed.</li><li>The striker steps or shuffles to adjust their position so the sweet spot of their bat meets the ball at the right point.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move your feet to the ball, not your bat to a fixed spot.'</li><li>For 'easier', use small variations in feed position; for 'harder', use larger variations; the feeder can call 'freeze' requiring an instant adjustment for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Sound Check</h4><p>Small groups strike in turn and call out 'clean' or 'edge' based on the sound and feel, checked against what the feeder saw.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust what you felt and heard.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder confirms after every hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Feeder only confirms occasionally.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: after each strike, the striker calls out 'clean' or 'edge' based on how it felt and sounded, and the feeder confirms what they actually saw.</li><li>One student feeds the ball while another strikes it, then immediately calls 'clean' or 'edge' before the feeder gives feedback.</li><li>The group checks whether the striker's own call matched what the feeder saw, building self-awareness of contact quality.</li><li>Rotate the striker and feeder roles every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust what you felt and heard.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the feeder confirm after every hit; for 'harder', have the feeder only confirm occasionally.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Sweet Spot Scoring</h3><p>Small-sided striking game where clean, sweet-spot contact (spotted by the teacher or partner) earns bonus points on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. Soft bats/paddles, one ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this small-sided striking game uses standard scoring, plus a bonus point for clean, sweet-spot contact.</li><li>Start each point with a gentle feed or serve.</li><li>Play the point out with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player's contact is spotted as clean/sweet-spot by the teacher or their partner.</li><li>After each point, restart immediately with the next feed or serve — rotate who feeds each time.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to strike.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'find the sweet spot — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did the sweet spot actually feel or sound like compared to an edge hit?</li><li>How did moving your feet help you find the sweet spot more often?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try to fix about your contact next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Identifies roughly where their sweet spot is</li><li>Moves feet to bring the sweet spot to the ball</li><li>Notices and describes clean versus mishit contact</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger striking surfaces (wider bats/paddles) for students still building contact consistency; allow extra feeds without penalty while this feel develops.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Set Up Early":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Getting body and hands/bat into position before contact (preparation and stance)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft bats/paddles, soft balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; controlled feed speeds while set-up habits are being built</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to set up our body and bat or hands early, before the ball arrives, not at the last second.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>get into a ready position before the ball is close</li><li>adjust my set-up if the ball's path changes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Strike Prep Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to shuffle side-to-side without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light athletic shuffle stance: knees bent, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up ready — the same base position used before striking a ball.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, dropping into a quick side-to-side shuffle every time the teacher calls 'shuffle', then returning to jogging on 'jog'.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'shuffle left', 'shuffle right', or 'shadow swing' (a few gentle practice swings on the spot with an imaginary bat).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rate and warming up the ready stance used for striking, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Split into pairs spread out with enough room to stretch side-by-side without touching another pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get into a ready stance the moment you know the ball is coming, not once it's already close.</td><td>A rushed, last-second set-up rarely produces clean contact — early preparation gives your body time to adjust.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep adjusting your set-up as the ball's path becomes clearer.</td><td>The first read isn't always perfect, so small adjustments as the ball approaches keep you lined up.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay balanced and light on your feet in your ready position.</td><td>A balanced stance lets you move quickly in any direction once you know exactly where the ball is going.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Ready Early</h4><p>In pairs, the striker must be in a set, ready stance before the feeder releases the ball, checked by the feeder.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be ready before I even let go.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder gives a clear verbal countdown.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No countdown, feeder chooses their own moment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: getting into a set, ready stance — balanced, hands/bat up, weight forward — before the ball is even released.</li><li>The striker gets into their ready stance; the feeder checks the striker is set before releasing the ball.</li><li>The feeder releases the ball only once the striker is visibly ready, and the striker strikes it.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be ready before I even let go.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the feeder give a clear verbal countdown; for 'harder', give no countdown, with the feeder choosing their own moment.</li></ul><hr><h4>Adjust and Strike</h4><p>Feeder varies the ball's path slightly after release, and the striker must make a small last-moment adjustment to their set-up before striking.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small adjustment, not a big scramble.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Small, gentle path changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Larger, later path changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Feeder occasionally fakes a path change</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the feeder will sometimes change the ball's path slightly after release, and the striker must make a small, last-moment adjustment to their set-up before striking.</li><li>The feeder releases the ball, occasionally curving or redirecting its path slightly partway through.</li><li>The striker watches closely and adjusts their stance in a small, controlled way to still meet the ball cleanly.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'small adjustment, not a big scramble.'</li><li>For 'easier', use small, gentle path changes; for 'harder', use larger, later path changes; the feeder can occasionally fake a path change for extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Balanced and Ready</h4><p>Small groups practise moving between drills while returning to a balanced, ready stance each time before the next feed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per group, moving between two simple drill spots within the group area.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Light feet, balanced base.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More time between feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker succession of feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every minute</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: returning to a balanced, ready stance every time after moving, before the next feed arrives.</li><li>Students move between two drill spots (e.g. a short shuffle or step) and must reset into a balanced, ready stance at the new spot before the next feed is delivered.</li><li>The feeder only releases the ball once the striker is visibly set and balanced.</li><li>Rotate the feeder and striker roles every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'light feet, balanced base.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow more time between feeds; for 'harder', use quicker succession of feeds; rotating roles every minute keeps things moving (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Set-Up Success</h3><p>Small-sided striking game where being visibly set up and ready before each ball arrives earns a bonus point, on top of standard scoring for clean contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. Soft bats/paddles, one ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: standard scoring applies for clean contact, plus a bonus point for visibly being set up and ready before each ball arrives.</li><li>Start each point with a gentle feed or serve.</li><li>Play the point out with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player is spotted in a balanced, ready stance before contact.</li><li>After each point, restart immediately with the next feed or serve — rotate who feeds each time.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to strike.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'be ready before it arrives — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What difference did being ready early make to your contact?</li><li>How did you adjust your set-up when the ball's path changed?</li><li>What does a balanced, ready stance actually feel like?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gets into a ready stance before the ball is close</li><li>Makes small adjustments as the ball's path becomes clear</li><li>Stays balanced and light on their feet</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Give extra verbal cues ('ready... ready... now') for students still building early set-up habits; allow more time between feeds in early rounds.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Follow Through":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Completing the strike smoothly with a full follow-through for control and accuracy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft bats/paddles, soft balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Extra spacing around each striker to allow for a full follow-through motion safely</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to follow all the way through our strike, not stop right at contact.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>continue my swing or hand motion smoothly after contact</li><li>notice how follow-through changes where the ball goes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Strike Prep Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to shuffle side-to-side without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light athletic shuffle stance: knees bent, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up ready — the same base position used before striking a ball.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, dropping into a quick side-to-side shuffle every time the teacher calls 'shuffle', then returning to jogging on 'jog'.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'shuffle left', 'shuffle right', or 'shadow swing' (a few gentle practice swings on the spot with an imaginary bat).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rate and warming up the ready stance used for striking, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Split into pairs spread out with enough room to stretch side-by-side without touching another pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Continue your swing or hand motion smoothly through and past the point of contact.</td><td>Stopping right at contact shortens control and often sends the ball off-target.</td></tr><tr><td>Point your follow-through toward where you want the ball to go.</td><td>The direction of your follow-through is closely linked to the direction the ball actually travels.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay balanced through the whole motion, from set-up to follow-through.</td><td>Losing balance partway through the swing undoes the accuracy that early preparation built.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Swing It Through</h4><p>Students shadow-swing without a ball first, exaggerating a full, smooth follow-through, then add a gentle feed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space (extra room for full swings), soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep going after you've hit it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shadow swings only, no ball at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add ball feeds sooner.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: a full, smooth shadow-swing (no ball) with an exaggerated follow-through, continuing the motion well past where contact would happen.</li><li>Students practise 3-4 shadow-swings each without a ball, focusing on a smooth, complete motion.</li><li>Once the shadow-swing looks smooth, the feeder adds a gentle feed and the striker repeats the same full follow-through with a real ball.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 swings so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep going after you've hit it.'</li><li>For 'easier', stay on shadow swings only for longer before adding a ball; for 'harder', add ball feeds sooner.</li></ul><hr><h4>Point the Target</h4><p>In pairs, the striker aims their follow-through toward a cone target and checks whether the ball actually travelled that way.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4-5m apart across the space, with one cone set up as a target a few metres beyond the striker, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Follow through toward where you're aiming.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Large, close target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, further target.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Target moved between turns</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the striker's follow-through should finish pointing toward the cone target.</li><li>The feeder feeds the ball; the striker hits it, aiming their follow-through toward the target cone.</li><li>After each strike, the pair checks together whether the ball actually travelled toward the target.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 strikes so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'follow through toward where you're aiming.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a large, close target; for 'harder', use a smaller, further target; moving the target between turns adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Smooth Not Stopped</h4><p>Small groups strike in turn, with a partner watching for a smooth, complete motion versus a stopped, chopped one.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth all the way, not a stop-start.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower-paced feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker succession of feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: a partner will watch each strike and call out whether the motion looked smooth and complete, or stopped and chopped right at contact.</li><li>One student feeds the ball while another strikes it in turn, and a third watches the swing.</li><li>The watcher gives quick feedback — 'smooth' or 'stopped' — right after each strike.</li><li>Rotate the feeder, striker, and watcher roles every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal practice in each role.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'smooth all the way, not a stop-start.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower-paced feeds; for 'harder', use quicker succession of feeds.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Follow-Through Focus</h3><p>Small-sided striking game where a visibly smooth, complete follow-through toward the intended direction earns a bonus point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones (extra room around each striker for a full swing). Soft bats/paddles, one ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: standard scoring applies, plus a bonus point for a visibly smooth, complete follow-through toward the intended direction.</li><li>Start each point with a gentle feed or serve.</li><li>Play the point out with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player's follow-through is spotted as smooth and complete.</li><li>After each point, restart immediately with the next feed or serve — rotate who feeds each time.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to strike.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'keep going after you've hit it — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What changed about your accuracy when you followed all the way through?</li><li>How did pointing your follow-through at a target help?</li><li>What did a 'stopped' swing feel like compared to a smooth one?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Continues the motion smoothly through contact</li><li>Follow-through points toward the intended direction</li><li>Stays balanced through the whole swing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use shadow swings without a ball for students still building the full motion; allow a slower-paced feed while follow-through habits develop.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Strike It Clean":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining watching the ball in, sweet-spot contact, early set-up, and follow-through (Block 2 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft bats/paddles, balls (various sizes), bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 2 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across the carousel, with extra room allowed for full follow-through swings</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bring together everything from this week — watching it in, sweet-spot contact, set-up, and follow-through — into a clean, confident strike.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>use set-up, contact, and follow-through together in one smooth strike</li><li>strike confidently and cleanly more often than not</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Strike Prep Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to shuffle side-to-side without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light athletic shuffle stance: knees bent, weight on the balls of the feet, hands up ready — the same base position used before striking a ball.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, dropping into a quick side-to-side shuffle every time the teacher calls 'shuffle', then returning to jogging on 'jog'.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'shuffle left', 'shuffle right', or 'shadow swing' (a few gentle practice swings on the spot with an imaginary bat).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the aim is raising heart rate and warming up the ready stance used for striking, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Split into pairs spread out with enough room to stretch side-by-side without touching another pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Trust the watching-in, sweet-spot, set-up, and follow-through skills you've built this week, all together in one motion.</td><td>A clean strike isn't separate steps — it's one smooth sequence once each part has been practised enough.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice which part of your strike is strongest and which still needs work.</td><td>Recognising your own pattern is what lets you keep improving after this unit ends.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay relaxed and confident, even after a mishit.</td><td>Tension after a miss usually causes the next strike to go wrong too — resetting calmly keeps your technique intact.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Strike Carousel</h4><p>Teams rotate through short stations that each revisit a Block 2 focus — watching it in, sweet spot, set-up, follow-through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations around the space (roughly 5m x 5m each), one for each Block 2 focus: watching it in, sweet spot, set-up, follow-through. Split the class into teams of 4-5, one team starting at each station, with soft bats/paddles and balls of varying sizes distributed across the stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use everything you've practised this week.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each station revisits before starting: Station 1 watching it in (Watch It Meet style), Station 2 sweet spot (Find the Spot style), Station 3 set-up (Ready Early style), Station 4 follow-through (Swing It Through style).</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and begin the activity immediately on 'go'.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 2-3 minutes), teams rotate clockwise to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed all 4 stations.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'use everything you've practised this week.'</li><li>For 'easier', give longer time per station; for 'harder', give shorter time with quicker rotation; a head-to-head mirror between two teams at the same station adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Clean Strike Streak</h4><p>In pairs, students count their longest run of clean, confident strikes in a row, combining all of this week's focuses.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One smooth thing, not four separate ones.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, closer feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, more varied feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the pair counts their longest run of clean, confident strikes in a row, combining watching it in, sweet spot, set-up, and follow-through.</li><li>One partner feeds the ball while the other strikes; both count out loud together.</li><li>If a strike is off-centre, rushed, or poorly followed through, the count resets to zero and play restarts immediately with the next feed.</li><li>Partners swap roles halfway through so both get equal practice as striker.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'one smooth thing, not four separate ones.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, closer feeds; for 'harder', use faster, more varied feeds.</li></ul><hr><h4>Self-Check Strike</h4><p>Students strike in small groups, briefly naming out loud which part of their strike felt strongest that turn.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, soft bats/paddles and soft balls, one set per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice it, don't judge it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers prompts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No prompts, own words only.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: after each strike, the striker briefly names out loud which part of their strike felt strongest that turn (watching it in, sweet spot, set-up, or follow-through).</li><li>One student feeds while another strikes, and the striker gives their self-check immediately afterward.</li><li>The group listens without judging or correcting — the goal is the student noticing their own strengths.</li><li>Rotate the feeder and striker roles every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice it, don't judge it.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher offer prompts; for 'harder', give no prompts, own words only.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Strike It Clean Games</h3><p>Small-sided striking matches where teams earn a bonus point for clean, well-combined strikes, wrapping up the whole block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. Soft bats/paddles, one ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this small-sided striking match brings together everything from Block 2 — watching it in, sweet-spot contact, set-up, and follow-through.</li><li>Start each point with a gentle feed or serve.</li><li>Play the point out with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player's strike is spotted as clean and well-combined across all four elements.</li><li>After each point, restart immediately with the next feed or serve — rotate who feeds each time.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to strike.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'bring together everything you've learned this week — that's what wraps up Block 2.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which part of your strike — set-up, contact, or follow-through — feels strongest now?</li><li>What did combining all of them together feel like today?</li><li>What's one thing you'd still like to smooth out?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Combines set-up, contact, and follow-through into one smooth strike</li><li>Strikes cleanly and confidently more often than not</li><li>Resets calmly after a mishit</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to nominate their own 'easier' or 'harder' station variant based on confidence; celebrate streaks of any length to build confidence going into Block 3.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Reaction & Timing (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Quick Hands":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fast reaction catching / reflex response to a sudden or unpredictable object</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Small reaction balls or tennis balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep reaction throws close-range and gentle to avoid jammed fingers; standard spacing</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to react quickly with our hands when a ball arrives suddenly or unexpectedly.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>react quickly to catch a suddenly thrown or dropped ball</li><li>stay ready rather than relaxed between reactions</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Reaction Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to change direction quickly without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a quick reactive change of direction: jogging forward, then instantly sprinting a few steps sideways or backward on a signal.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 15-20 seconds, call a sudden signal — a clap, a colour, or a number — and students react immediately with a quick change of direction or a short burst of speed.</li><li>Keep the pace playful and unpredictable — the aim is warming up quick reactions and raising heart rate, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, varying how often and how suddenly signals are called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and control used in reaction and timing work.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay in a light, ready position between reactions, not fully relaxed.</td><td>A body that's already alert reacts far faster than one that has to first 'wake up'.</td></tr><tr><td>React with your hands the instant you see the ball move, not after thinking about it.</td><td>Overthinking a reaction slows it down — trust the instant response.</td></tr><tr><td>Reset quickly and get ready again straight after each reaction.</td><td>Reaction drills often come in quick succession, so recovery speed matters as much as the reaction itself.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Drop Catch</h4><p>In pairs, one holds a ball at shoulder height and drops it without warning for the other to catch before it bounces twice.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other roughly 1m apart across the space, one small reaction ball or tennis ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>React the instant it drops.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Verbal warning before dropping.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No warning at all.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Occasional fake drop (hand moves, ball doesn't drop)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: holding the ball at shoulder height with one hand and letting it drop without warning, while the partner's hands stay ready and low.</li><li>One partner holds the ball at shoulder height; the other stands ready, hands loose and low, watching closely.</li><li>The holder drops the ball without warning; the catcher reacts instantly to catch it before it bounces twice.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 drops so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'react the instant it drops.'</li><li>For 'easier', give a verbal warning before dropping; for 'harder', give no warning at all; an occasional fake drop (hand moves, ball doesn't drop) adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Reaction Ball Rally</h4><p>Using a small reaction ball (or tennis ball), partners toss it low and short with unpredictable bounces for the other to react and catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other roughly 2-3m apart across the space, one small reaction ball or tennis ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay light on your feet, ready to move either way.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Softer, more predictable tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Livelier, less predictable tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: staying light on your feet, ready to move either way, before the toss even arrives.</li><li>One partner tosses the ball low and short with a gentle, unpredictable bounce toward the other.</li><li>The receiver reacts to the bounce and catches it, staying light on their feet throughout.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 8-10 tosses so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay light on your feet the whole time, ready to move either way.'</li><li>For 'easier', use softer, more predictable tosses; for 'harder', use livelier, less predictable tosses.</li></ul><hr><h4>Quick Hands Circle</h4><p>In a small circle, a ball is passed rapidly and randomly between players, rewarding fast, clean reactions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small circles of 5-6 students, roughly 3-4m across, one ball per circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Hands up and ready, always.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, predictable order.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, random order.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Two balls introduced</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the ball will be passed rapidly and randomly between players in the circle, not in a set order.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and passes it to anyone else in the circle without warning.</li><li>Students must keep their hands up and ready at all times, since the next pass could come to them at any moment.</li><li>Continue passing randomly for the full activity time, with the teacher checking hands stay ready between passes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'hands up and ready, always.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with a predictable order; for 'harder', use a faster pace with random order; introducing a second ball adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Reaction Relay</h3><p>Small teams race through a series of quick-reaction catching challenges in relay format, with clean reactive catches earning points toward the team total.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5, each team with a short relay lane roughly 10m long marked with cones, and a small reaction ball or tennis ball per team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: teams race through a series of quick-reaction catching challenges in relay format (e.g. drop-catch, then a short sprint, tag the next teammate).</li><li>The first player in each team completes a quick-reaction catch challenge, then sprints to tag the next teammate in line.</li><li>Each clean, reactive catch earns a point toward the team's running total, in addition to relay placing.</li><li>As soon as one player finishes, the next starts immediately — keep the relay moving with minimal stoppage.</li><li>Rotate the order of who goes first each round so everyone gets a mix of positions.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'quick hands and a quick reaction — that's what earns the points.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you react faster today — was it your position, your focus, or something else?</li><li>What happened when you tried to think about the catch instead of just reacting?</li><li>How did staying 'ready' between turns change your reaction time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stays in a light, ready position between reactions</li><li>Reacts instantly rather than hesitating</li><li>Resets quickly and gets ready again</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use larger, softer reaction balls and closer distances for students building reaction confidence; add clear warnings before drops or throws in early rounds.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Read the Bounce":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Predicting where a bouncing or deflecting object will arrive</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking a grid</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; controlled bounce heights to avoid balls bouncing into other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read how a ball bounces so we can predict where it will go next.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>predict roughly where a bounced ball will end up</li><li>move into position early based on that prediction</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Reaction Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to change direction quickly without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a quick reactive change of direction: jogging forward, then instantly sprinting a few steps sideways or backward on a signal.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 15-20 seconds, call a sudden signal — a clap, a colour, or a number — and students react immediately with a quick change of direction or a short burst of speed.</li><li>Keep the pace playful and unpredictable — the aim is warming up quick reactions and raising heart rate, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, varying how often and how suddenly signals are called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and control used in reaction and timing work.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the angle and speed of the ball just before it bounces.</td><td>Those two things — angle and speed — are what actually determine where a bounce will end up.</td></tr><tr><td>Predict roughly where the ball will arrive, then move there early.</td><td>Reacting after the bounce is often too late — reading it beforehand buys you time.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your prediction if the bounce surface or spin surprises you.</td><td>Not every bounce behaves exactly as expected, so staying ready to correct matters as much as the first read.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Bounce Predictor</h4><p>In pairs, one bounces the ball at varying angles while the other calls out and then moves to where they predict it will arrive.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, inside a grid marked with cones roughly 8m x 8m per pair, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call it before you move, then check.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Straight, predictable bounces.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Angled, less predictable bounces.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: calling out loud where you predict the ball will arrive before moving, then checking if your prediction was right.</li><li>One partner bounces the ball at a varying angle toward the other.</li><li>The receiver calls out where they predict it will arrive, then moves there to collect it.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 bounces so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call it before you move, then check.'</li><li>For 'easier', use straight, predictable bounces; for 'harder', use angled, less predictable bounces.</li></ul><hr><h4>Beat the Bounce</h4><p>Small groups race to be first to reach and control a bounced ball after it's released from a height or angle.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move on the read, not the bounce.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball bounced from a consistent spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball bounced from varying spots and angles.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Fake bounce release (ball held, not released)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the group races to be first to reach and control the ball after it's released from a height or angle.</li><li>One student releases the ball from a height or angle, without telling the others exactly where it will land.</li><li>The rest of the group reacts to the bounce and races to be first to control it cleanly.</li><li>Rotate who releases the ball every 4-5 turns so everyone gets equal chances to react.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move on the read, not the bounce.'</li><li>For 'easier', release from a consistent spot; for 'harder', vary the release spot and angle; an occasional fake bounce release (ball held, not released) adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Bad Bounce Recovery</h4><p>Partners deliberately introduce an occasional unpredictable bounce (off a slight surface change or spin) and the receiver practises adjusting mid-read.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair, on any available surface change (grass edge, painted line) if possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If it surprises you, adjust fast, don't freeze.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bad bounces flagged in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No warning given.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the partner will deliberately introduce an occasional unpredictable bounce, and the receiver must adjust mid-read rather than freezing.</li><li>One partner bounces the ball toward the other, mostly with normal, predictable bounces.</li><li>Occasionally, the thrower introduces spin or bounces off a slight surface change to create an unpredictable bounce.</li><li>The receiver reacts and adjusts quickly to still collect the ball cleanly.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if it surprises you, adjust fast, don't freeze.'</li><li>For 'easier', flag bad bounces in advance; for 'harder', give no warning at all.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; React Bounce Game</h3><p>Small-sided game incorporating bounced passes, where correctly predicting and reaching a bounce first earns a bonus point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a keep-away style game where bounced passes are encouraged, and correctly predicting and reaching a bounce first earns a bonus point.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a player is first to correctly predict and reach a bounced pass.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'read it early — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What told you where a bounce was going to end up?</li><li>How did moving early based on your prediction help?</li><li>What did you do when a bounce surprised you?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Watches angle and speed before the bounce</li><li>Predicts and moves early rather than reacting late</li><li>Adjusts quickly when a bounce is unexpected</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use straight, predictable bounces before introducing angled ones; allow extra thinking time in early rounds for students building this read.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Time It Right":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Timing a jump, swing, or reach to arrive exactly when the moving object does</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, soft bats/paddles (optional)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; controlled speeds while timing skills are still developing</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to time our jump, swing, or reach so it arrives exactly when the ball does.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>time my movement to meet the ball, not too early or too late</li><li>adjust my timing if the ball's speed changes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Reaction Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to change direction quickly without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a quick reactive change of direction: jogging forward, then instantly sprinting a few steps sideways or backward on a signal.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 15-20 seconds, call a sudden signal — a clap, a colour, or a number — and students react immediately with a quick change of direction or a short burst of speed.</li><li>Keep the pace playful and unpredictable — the aim is warming up quick reactions and raising heart rate, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, varying how often and how suddenly signals are called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and control used in reaction and timing work.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Judge the ball's speed early so you can plan your timing, not just react at the last second.</td><td>Good timing starts with an early read of speed — leaving it too late removes your ability to adjust.</td></tr><tr><td>Begin your jump, swing, or reach slightly before the ball arrives, so it's at full extension exactly on time.</td><td>Movements take a moment to complete — starting too late means arriving after the ball has already gone.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your timing if the ball speeds up, slows down, or changes path.</td><td>A single fixed plan doesn't work if the ball's behaviour changes — timing has to stay flexible.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Meet It There</h4><p>In pairs, one throws at varying speeds while the other times a jump or reach to meet the ball at its highest or furthest point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4-5m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Start moving before it gets there.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Consistent, predictable speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varying speed each throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: starting a jump or reach early enough to meet the ball right at its highest or furthest point, rather than reacting late.</li><li>One partner throws at a varying speed; the other times a jump or reach to meet the ball at the right moment.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'start moving before it gets there.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a consistent, predictable speed; for 'harder', vary the speed each throw.</li></ul><hr><h4>Timed Strike</h4><p>Using a soft bat or paddle, students time their swing to meet a gently rolled or tossed ball at exactly the right moment.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 3-4m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles (optional), one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Swing to meet it, not to chase it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, closer feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, more varied feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Feeder occasionally changes speed mid-approach</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: timing a swing so it meets a gently rolled or tossed ball exactly at the right moment, rather than chasing after it.</li><li>One partner rolls or tosses the ball gently; the other times their swing to meet it right at the correct moment.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'swing to meet it, not to chase it.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, closer feeds; for 'harder', use faster, more varied feeds; the feeder occasionally changing speed mid-approach adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Speed Change Read</h4><p>Partners vary a ball's speed unpredictably (fast, then slow) and the receiver must adjust their timing in real time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch for the change, don't assume it stays the same.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Speed change signalled in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No signal given.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the partner will vary the ball's speed unpredictably — fast, then slow — and the receiver must adjust their timing in real time.</li><li>One partner throws with a mix of fast and slow speeds in no fixed pattern.</li><li>The receiver watches closely and adjusts their timing for each throw individually.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch for the change, don't assume it stays the same.'</li><li>For 'easier', signal the speed change in advance; for 'harder', give no signal at all.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Perfect Timing Game</h3><p>Small-sided game where a well-timed jump, reach, or strike (arriving exactly with the ball) earns a bonus point on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. Balls, soft bats/paddles (optional), plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: standard scoring applies, plus a bonus point for a well-timed jump, reach, or strike that arrives exactly with the ball.</li><li>Start each point or passage of play with a gentle feed or throw-in.</li><li>Play with normal scoring, and award a bonus point any time a player's timing is spotted as exactly on the mark.</li><li>After each point/stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to time their actions.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'start moving before it gets there — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you judge the ball's speed early enough to time your movement?</li><li>What happened when you started your movement too early, or too late?</li><li>How did you adjust when the ball's speed changed unexpectedly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Judges speed early rather than reacting late</li><li>Times movement to arrive exactly with the ball</li><li>Adjusts timing when speed or path changes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use slower, more predictable speeds for students building timing confidence; allow a practice attempt before each scored turn.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Under Pressure":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Coordinating hand-eye skills while moving, being defended, or under time pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous pairs/groups; clear rules to avoid unsafe contact when defenders are added</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep our hand-eye coordination sharp even when we're moving, being defended, or under time pressure.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>catch or strike accurately even while moving</li><li>stay coordinated when a defender or the clock is adding pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Reaction Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to change direction quickly without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a quick reactive change of direction: jogging forward, then instantly sprinting a few steps sideways or backward on a signal.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 15-20 seconds, call a sudden signal — a clap, a colour, or a number — and students react immediately with a quick change of direction or a short burst of speed.</li><li>Keep the pace playful and unpredictable — the aim is warming up quick reactions and raising heart rate, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, varying how often and how suddenly signals are called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and control used in reaction and timing work.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your technique the same under pressure as it was without it.</td><td>Pressure tends to rush technique first — holding onto the basics is what keeps performance steady.</td></tr><tr><td>Slow your decision-making down mentally, even if your body is moving quickly.</td><td>A calm mind inside a fast-moving body is what actually produces accurate hand-eye skills under pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Accept that pressure will cause more mistakes than usual, and reset quickly.</td><td>Expecting perfection under pressure adds unnecessary tension — a quick reset keeps you performing well overall.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Catch on the Move</h4><p>In pairs, one throws while the other jogs or moves laterally, catching without stopping their movement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space with enough room to jog roughly 8-10m, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your technique the same, just add movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower movement, easier throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker movement, more varied throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: keeping the same catching technique used in earlier lessons, but now while jogging or moving laterally rather than standing still.</li><li>One partner throws while the other jogs or moves laterally, catching without stopping their movement.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your technique the same, just add movement.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower movement with easier throws; for 'harder', use quicker movement with more varied throws.</li></ul><hr><h4>Defended Catch</h4><p>A light passive defender waves their arms nearby (without touching) while a pair practises catching, adding visual distraction pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread out across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per trio, one ball per trio, with the third player acting as a light passive defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Focus on the ball, not the distraction.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stands further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender stands close and active.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Defender makes a surprise call-out</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the defender waves their arms nearby without touching, to add visual distraction while the pair practises catching.</li><li>One student throws to another while the third student (the defender) stands nearby, waving their arms without making any contact.</li><li>The receiver focuses on the ball, not the distraction, and completes the catch.</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 4-5 turns so everyone experiences defending and catching.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'focus on the ball, not the distraction.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender stand further away; for 'harder', have the defender stand close and active; a surprise call-out from the defender adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Beat the Clock</h4><p>Small groups attempt a set number of clean catches or strikes within a time limit, adding time pressure to the coordination task.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per group, one ball per group, plus a stopwatch or visible timer.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Fast hands, calm mind.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More time given.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Less time given.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Time limit shortened without warning</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the group attempts a set number of clean catches or strikes within a set time limit.</li><li>The teacher sets a target number of clean catches and a time limit, then starts the timer.</li><li>The group passes and catches as quickly and cleanly as possible, trying to reach the target before time runs out.</li><li>As soon as the time is up (or the target is reached), reset and try again with a fresh target.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'fast hands, calm mind.'</li><li>For 'easier', give more time; for 'harder', give less time; shortening the time limit without warning adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pressure Game</h3><p>Small-sided game combining light defensive pressure and quick tempo, where accurate hand-eye actions under this pressure earn bonus points.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this small-sided game combines light defensive pressure with a quick tempo, and accurate hand-eye actions under pressure earn bonus points.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team applies light, controlled defensive pressure without contact.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a player makes an accurate catch or pass despite the pressure and tempo.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'fast hands, calm mind — that's what earns the bonus under pressure.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What changed about your technique when pressure was added?</li><li>What helped you stay calm and accurate even while moving or defended?</li><li>How did you reset after a mistake made under pressure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps technique consistent under pressure</li><li>Stays mentally calm even while moving quickly</li><li>Resets quickly after a pressured mistake</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Introduce pressure gradually — movement first, then light distraction, then time pressure — for students building coordination confidence.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Fast & Focused":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining quick reactions, reading bounces, timing, and composure under pressure (Block 3 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 3 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across the carousel and games; controlled pace for higher-pressure stations</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bring together everything from this week — quick hands, reading bounces, timing, and staying composed — into fast, focused coordination.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>react, read, and time my movements together under pressure</li><li>stay focused and composed even when things happen quickly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Quick Reaction Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no equipment needed beyond enough room to change direction quickly without colliding.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a quick reactive change of direction: jogging forward, then instantly sprinting a few steps sideways or backward on a signal.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 15-20 seconds, call a sudden signal — a clap, a colour, or a number — and students react immediately with a quick change of direction or a short burst of speed.</li><li>Keep the pace playful and unpredictable — the aim is warming up quick reactions and raising heart rate, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, varying how often and how suddenly signals are called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and control used in reaction and timing work.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Trust the reaction, bounce-reading, and timing skills you've built this week, all working together under pressure.</td><td>Fast, focused coordination is these separate skills becoming automatic together, not something new.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay focused on the object itself, not on the pressure around it.</td><td>Pressure is a distraction — the actual task (catching, striking, timing) hasn't changed, so the focus shouldn't either.</td></tr><tr><td>Recover quickly from any mistake and get ready for the next moment.</td><td>Fast-paced coordination always includes some mistakes — what matters is getting back into a ready state immediately.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fast &amp; Focused Carousel</h4><p>Teams rotate through short stations that each revisit a Block 3 focus — quick hands, reading bounces, timing, composure under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations around the space (roughly 5m x 5m each), one for each Block 3 focus: quick hands, reading bounces, timing, composure under pressure. Split the class into teams of 4-5, one team starting at each station, with balls distributed across the stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use everything you've practised this week.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each station revisits before starting: Station 1 quick hands (Drop Catch style), Station 2 reading bounces (Bounce Predictor style), Station 3 timing (Meet It There style), Station 4 composure under pressure (Beat the Clock style).</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and begin the activity immediately on 'go'.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 2-3 minutes), teams rotate clockwise to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed all 4 stations.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'use everything you've practised this week.'</li><li>For 'easier', give longer time per station; for 'harder', give shorter time with quicker rotation; a head-to-head mirror between two teams at the same station adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Combined Pressure Challenge</h4><p>In pairs or small groups, students complete a fast sequence combining a reaction catch, a bounce read, and a timed strike or reach, one after another.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or small groups of 3 spread out across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One smooth sequence, not three separate stops.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Pause briefly between each part.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No pause, straight through.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the sequence before starting: a reaction catch, then a bounce read, then a timed strike or reach, one after another without stopping.</li><li>One student feeds each part of the sequence in turn while the other completes all three actions as one flowing sequence.</li><li>The group checks that all three parts were completed cleanly before swapping roles.</li><li>Rotate roles every 2-3 sequences so everyone gets equal practice as the one completing the sequence.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'one smooth sequence, not three separate stops.'</li><li>For 'easier', pause briefly between each part; for 'harder', go straight through with no pause.</li></ul><hr><h4>Composure Check</h4><p>Students complete short pressure challenges, briefly naming out loud how they stayed focused afterwards.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across the space, roughly 5m x 5m per group, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice what helped you stay calm.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers prompts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No prompts, own words only.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: after a short pressure challenge, each student briefly names out loud how they stayed focused.</li><li>The group completes a short pressure challenge together (e.g. a quick reaction/timing task from earlier this block).</li><li>Immediately afterward, each student takes a turn naming one thing that helped them stay calm and focused.</li><li>Rotate who leads the challenge each round so everyone gets equal turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice what helped you stay calm.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher offer prompts; for 'harder', give no prompts, own words only.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fast &amp; Focused Games</h3><p>Small-sided matches combining reaction, timing, and light pressure, where teams earn a bonus point for staying accurate and composed throughout, wrapping up the whole block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this small-sided match combines reaction, timing, and light pressure, wrapping up the whole block.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the grid; the other team applies light, controlled defensive pressure without contact.</li><li>Award standard possession points for completed passes, plus a bonus point any time a team stays accurate and composed under the pressure and pace.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of the grid, possession switches immediately to the other team from that spot — restart with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through attack and defence every few minutes so everyone gets equal time in both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'bring together everything you've learned this week — that's what wraps up Block 3.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which part of this week — reacting, reading, or timing — feels strongest now?</li><li>What helped you stay focused when things were happening quickly?</li><li>Looking back over this block, what's improved most in your coordination?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Combines reaction, bounce-reading, and timing skills under pressure</li><li>Stays focused on the task rather than the pressure</li><li>Recovers quickly from mistakes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to nominate their own 'easier' or 'harder' station variant based on confidence; model calm recovery language for students who are hard on themselves after a mistake.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Combining Skills & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Hands & Feet Together":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining hand-eye coordination with footwork and body movement</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; clear pathways for footwork drills to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine our hand skills with good footwork, moving our whole body to support a catch or strike.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>move my feet to get into the right position before using my hands</li><li>combine footwork and hand skills smoothly in one action</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Coordination Circuit Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, with 3-4 simple markers (cones) placed around the space to jog between. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light jog that weaves between the cones, adding a quick change of pace or direction at each one.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, weaving between the cones at their own pace.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'high knees' for a few steps, 'quick feet' shuffle at a cone, or 'freeze' for a two-second balanced hold.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this circuit-style jog reflects the multi-skill focus of Block 4, raising heart rate while touching on movement, balance, and quick changes.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the whole class into one large circle with room for everyone to stretch without touching their neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down, then gather into the circle.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — shoulders, calves, quads, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe.'</li><li>Finish with a round of applause or a group cheer celebrating the unit's progress, since this warm-down is used across the culminating lessons of the whole unit.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Move your feet first to get your body into position, then let your hands finish the job.</td><td>Good hand-eye actions are built on a base of good footwork — hands alone can only do so much.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your steps small and quick rather than one big lunge.</td><td>Small adjusting steps keep you balanced and ready to change direction if needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Combine the footwork and hand action into one smooth movement, not two separate steps.</td><td>A pause between moving your feet and using your hands usually means you're already too late.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Footwork First</h4><p>In pairs, receivers must take at least two small adjusting steps to get into position before every catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4m apart across the space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feet first, hands second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball thrown closer, less movement needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball thrown wider, more movement needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: taking at least two small adjusting steps to get into position before every catch, rather than reaching from a fixed spot.</li><li>One partner throws to the other, who must take at least two visible steps to adjust into position before catching.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'feet first, hands second.'</li><li>For 'easier', throw the ball closer, requiring less movement; for 'harder', throw the ball wider, requiring more movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shuffle &amp; Catch</h4><p>Students shuffle sideways along a line of cones, catching a ball thrown at random points along the line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A line of 5-6 cones spaced roughly 2m apart, with students working in pairs at each line, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay light on your feet the whole time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower shuffle, predictable throw points.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster shuffle, random throw points.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Thrower calls 'stop' — catch from a dead stop</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: shuffling sideways along the line of cones while staying light on the feet, ready to catch at any point.</li><li>One partner shuffles sideways along the cone line while the other throws the ball at random points along the line.</li><li>The shuffling partner catches the ball wherever it's thrown, then continues shuffling.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 throws so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay light on your feet the whole time.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower shuffle with predictable throw points; for 'harder', use a faster shuffle with random throw points; the thrower calling 'stop' for a catch from a dead stop adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Move to Strike</h4><p>Using a soft bat or paddle, students move their feet to get in line with a rolled or tossed ball before striking it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out roughly 4-5m apart across the space, soft bats/paddles and balls, one set per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your feet there before your bat does.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball rolled slowly, close range.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball rolled faster, wider range.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: moving your feet to get in line with a rolled or tossed ball before striking, rather than reaching or stretching to meet it.</li><li>One partner rolls or tosses the ball toward the other; the striker moves their feet to get in line with it before striking.</li><li>Partners swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both get equal practice.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'get your feet there before your bat does.'</li><li>For 'easier', roll the ball slowly at close range; for 'harder', roll the ball faster over a wider range.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Whole-Body Game</h3><p>Small-sided game where clean catches or strikes that clearly used good footwork to get into position earn a bonus point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a playing area roughly 15m x 15m with cones. Balls and soft bats/paddles (optional), plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: standard scoring applies, plus a bonus point for a clean catch or strike that clearly used good footwork to get into position.</li><li>Play a small-sided game with normal scoring rules for the format in use.</li><li>Award a bonus point any time a player is spotted moving their feet well to get into position before a catch or strike.</li><li>After each stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone gets equal chances to move and receive.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'feet first, hands second — that's what earns the bonus.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did moving your feet first change your catching or striking?</li><li>What did small, quick steps feel like compared to one big lunge?</li><li>When did footwork and hands come together smoothly today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves feet into position before using hands</li><li>Takes small, quick adjusting steps</li><li>Combines footwork and hand action smoothly</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce the distance balls are thrown/rolled for students still building footwork confidence; allow extra practice steps before each attempt.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Multi-Skill Circuit":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining catching, striking, and reaction skills across a rotating multi-station circuit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Movement skills</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls (various sizes), soft bats/paddles, cones, bibs — set up as circuit stations</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear station boundaries and rotation system; standard spacing at each station</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply catching, striking, and reaction skills across a range of different stations.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>apply the right hand-eye skill for each different station</li><li>move confidently between different types of hand-eye challenge</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Coordination Circuit Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, with 3-4 simple markers (cones) placed around the space to jog between. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light jog that weaves between the cones, adding a quick change of pace or direction at each one.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, weaving between the cones at their own pace.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'high knees' for a few steps, 'quick feet' shuffle at a cone, or 'freeze' for a two-second balanced hold.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this circuit-style jog reflects the multi-skill focus of Block 4, raising heart rate while touching on movement, balance, and quick changes.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the whole class into one large circle with room for everyone to stretch without touching their neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down, then gather into the circle.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — shoulders, calves, quads, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe.'</li><li>Finish with a round of applause or a group cheer celebrating the unit's progress, since this warm-down is used across the culminating lessons of the whole unit.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Recognise which hand-eye skill each station needs, and apply it.</td><td>Different challenges need different specific skills — a strong coordinator can tell the difference and adjust.</td></tr><tr><td>Move between stations calmly and get ready quickly for the next challenge.</td><td>A circuit works best when transitions are smooth, not rushed or chaotic.</td></tr><tr><td>Use feedback from one station to improve at the next.</td><td>These are all connected skills — a fix noticed in one spot often carries straight over to the next.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Station Rotation Warm-Up</h4><p>A quick rotation through three short stations (catching, striking, reaction) to warm into today's circuit format.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3 short stations around the space (roughly 5m x 5m each): one catching, one striking, one reaction. Split the class into teams of 4-5, one team starting at each station, with balls and soft bats/paddles distributed across the stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get the feel of each station before we go full circuit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly explain each station before starting so students know what to expect.</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and begin the activity immediately on 'go'.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 90 seconds), teams rotate to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed all 3 stations, getting a feel for each before the full circuit begins.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'get the feel of each station before we go full circuit.'</li><li>For 'easier', give longer time per station; for 'harder', give shorter time with quicker rotation.</li></ul><hr><h4>Full Circuit Round 1</h4><p>Students complete a full rotation through all circuit stations at a steady, controlled pace.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Use the same stations set up for the day's circuit (catching, striking, reaction, plus any additional stations), each roughly 5m x 5m, with equipment already distributed at each station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique to every station.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Less time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror between two groups on the same station</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the technique focus at each station before starting.</li><li>Teams rotate through every station in the circuit at a steady, controlled pace, spending roughly the same amount of time at each.</li><li>At each station, students focus on bringing their best technique rather than rushing.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed the full circuit once.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring your best technique to every station.'</li><li>For 'easier', give more time per station; for 'harder', give less time per station; a head-to-head mirror between two groups on the same station adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Full Circuit Round 2</h4><p>A second full rotation, with students aiming to beat their own personal result or streak from Round 1 at each station.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same circuit stations as Full Circuit Round 1, with each team's Round 1 result or streak noted (verbally or on a simple tally) so they have something to beat.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Try to beat your own number, not someone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Same difficulty as Round 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Slightly increased difficulty at each station.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives added to one station</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of their own Round 1 result or streak at each station before starting.</li><li>Teams complete a second full rotation through all stations, aiming to beat their own personal result or streak from Round 1 at each one.</li><li>Encourage students to focus on their own improvement rather than comparing to other teams.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed the full circuit a second time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'try to beat your own number, not someone else's.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the same difficulty as Round 1; for 'harder', slightly increase the difficulty at each station; adding bonus objectives to one station adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Circuit Challenge Game</h3><p>Teams combine their circuit results into a team score, then play a short small-sided game applying whichever circuit skills come up naturally in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a playing area roughly 15m x 15m with cones. Balls and soft bats/paddles (optional), plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: teams first combine their circuit results into a team score, then play a short small-sided game.</li><li>Add up each team's combined circuit results (catches, strikes, reactions) from Rounds 1 and 2 into one team score.</li><li>Play a short small-sided game afterward, applying whichever circuit skills come up naturally in play, using standard scoring for that format.</li><li>After each stoppage in the game, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions during the game so everyone gets equal involvement.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'everything from the circuit carries straight into the game.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which station felt strongest for you today, and which felt trickiest?</li><li>What did you carry over from one station to the next?</li><li>How did rotating between different challenges compare to practising just one thing?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies the correct hand-eye skill at each different station</li><li>Transitions between stations calmly and quickly</li><li>Uses feedback from one station to improve at the next</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow flexible station order so students can build confidence at an easier station first; offer a buddy system for stations that involve reaction speed.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Game Ready":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Applying hand-eye coordination skills inside a live, game-like context</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games; agreed, fair self-officiating routines</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use our catching, striking, and reaction skills inside a real, flowing game, not just in isolated drills.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>apply hand-eye skills accurately while a game is flowing around me</li><li>stay coordinated even as the game gets faster or more chaotic</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Coordination Circuit Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, with 3-4 simple markers (cones) placed around the space to jog between. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light jog that weaves between the cones, adding a quick change of pace or direction at each one.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, weaving between the cones at their own pace.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'high knees' for a few steps, 'quick feet' shuffle at a cone, or 'freeze' for a two-second balanced hold.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this circuit-style jog reflects the multi-skill focus of Block 4, raising heart rate while touching on movement, balance, and quick changes.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the whole class into one large circle with room for everyone to stretch without touching their neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down, then gather into the circle.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — shoulders, calves, quads, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe.'</li><li>Finish with a round of applause or a group cheer celebrating the unit's progress, since this warm-down is used across the culminating lessons of the whole unit.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring catching, striking, timing, and reaction skills into a live game setting, not just controlled drills.</td><td>The real test of hand-eye coordination is whether it holds up once a game adds unpredictability and speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay coordinated even as the pace and chaos of the game increase.</td><td>Games rarely happen at a nice, controlled drill pace — coordination has to hold up under real conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate clearly with teammates to support each other's coordination (calling for the ball, warning of a defender).</td><td>Good communication makes everyone's hand-eye actions easier and more accurate.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Warm Into the Game</h4><p>A shortened, simplified version of today's game format, letting players find their rhythm and reacquaint their hand-eye skills with live play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 6-8, forming small teams for a shortened, simplified version of today's game format, using a playing area roughly 12m x 10m per game marked with cones. Balls and bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use this to settle in, not to win.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer warm-in time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter warm-in, straight into full games.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: this shortened, simplified game is for settling in and finding a rhythm, not for winning.</li><li>Start play with a simple version of the day's game format, using relaxed or simplified rules.</li><li>Let students find their rhythm and reacquaint their hand-eye skills with live play, without the teacher stopping for correction unless there's a safety issue.</li><li>After each stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use this to settle in, not to win.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a longer warm-in time; for 'harder', use a shorter warm-in, going straight into full games.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call and Catch</h4><p>In small-sided games, players must call for the ball clearly before receiving it, reinforcing communication alongside coordination.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early, then catch clean.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower-paced games.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster-paced games.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: players must call for the ball clearly before receiving it, every time.</li><li>Play a small-sided game with normal scoring rules for the format in use.</li><li>Any player wanting the ball must call out clearly (their name, or 'here!') before a teammate passes to them; passes to a player who hasn't called don't count for scoring.</li><li>After each stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call early, then catch clean.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower-paced games; for 'harder', use faster-paced games.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bonus Skill Game</h4><p>A small-sided game where a bonus point is awarded any time a player clearly demonstrates a specific hand-eye skill from earlier in the unit (a soft-hands catch, a clean strike, a well-timed reach).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a playing area roughly 15m x 10m with cones. One ball per game, plus bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for chances to use your best skills, not just any skill.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher points out bonus moments.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Players self-identify bonus moments.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives rotate mid-game</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: a bonus point is awarded any time a player clearly demonstrates a specific hand-eye skill from earlier in the unit (a soft-hands catch, a clean strike, a well-timed reach).</li><li>Play a small-sided game with normal scoring rules for the format in use.</li><li>The teacher (or players themselves) watches for a clear demonstration of a specific unit skill and awards a bonus point when spotted.</li><li>After each stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look for chances to use your best skills, not just any skill.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher point out bonus moments; for 'harder', have players self-identify bonus moments; rotating bonus objectives mid-game adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Flowing Game</h3><p>A complete small-sided game where students apply the whole range of hand-eye skills from across the unit inside genuine, fast-flowing play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8-10 for full small-sided games, using a playing area roughly 20m x 15m with cones. Balls, bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: this is a complete, full-flowing small-sided game using the whole range of hand-eye skills from across the unit.</li><li>Play with standard scoring rules for the game format in use, at genuine game pace.</li><li>Encourage students to bring in tracking, soft hands, striking, reaction, and timing naturally as situations call for them.</li><li>After each stoppage, restart immediately with minimal fuss to keep the game flowing.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions during the game so everyone gets a range of roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'let everything you've learned come out naturally in the game.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was different about using these skills in a real game compared to a drill?</li><li>How did communicating with teammates help your coordination?</li><li>What happened to your coordination as the game got faster?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies hand-eye skills accurately within live, flowing play</li><li>Stays coordinated as pace and chaos increase</li><li>Communicates clearly to support teammates' coordination</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a simplified rule set or slower-paced group for students still building confidence in live-game coordination; celebrate effort and accurate attempts, not just successful outcomes.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Coordination Challenge":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team-based multi-skill challenge combining the whole unit as a trial run before the culminating games day</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, soft bats/paddles, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous team challenges; clear rotation system between challenge stations</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine every hand-eye skill from this unit into team challenges, as a trial run before tomorrow's games day.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>apply a range of hand-eye coordination skills together in a challenge</li><li>support my team's effort in a shared coordination challenge</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Coordination Circuit Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, with 3-4 simple markers (cones) placed around the space to jog between. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light jog that weaves between the cones, adding a quick change of pace or direction at each one.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, weaving between the cones at their own pace.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'high knees' for a few steps, 'quick feet' shuffle at a cone, or 'freeze' for a two-second balanced hold.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this circuit-style jog reflects the multi-skill focus of Block 4, raising heart rate while touching on movement, balance, and quick changes.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the whole class into one large circle with room for everyone to stretch without touching their neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down, then gather into the circle.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — shoulders, calves, quads, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe.'</li><li>Finish with a round of applause or a group cheer celebrating the unit's progress, since this warm-down is used across the culminating lessons of the whole unit.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Combine catching, striking, reacting, timing, and footwork into connected team challenges.</td><td>A real team challenge brings together everything the unit has built, often in quick succession — a genuine trial for tomorrow.</td></tr><tr><td>Support your team's shared effort, not just your own individual result.</td><td>Many of today's challenges rely on the whole team's coordination, not one person's.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect honestly on which parts of your coordination are strongest heading into the games day.</td><td>Knowing your own strengths and areas to watch is what lets you play smart tomorrow, not just hard.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Team Relay Challenge</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining a catch, a strike, and a reaction challenge in sequence, as a fun, competitive combination of the unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5, each team with a relay lane roughly 15m long marked with cones, set up with a catch station, a strike station, and a reaction station along the way. Balls, soft bats/paddles, and reaction balls distributed along each lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Support your teammate as much as you focus on your own turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler sequence, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer sequence, less time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror between two teams</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the sequence before starting: each team member completes a catch, a strike, and a reaction challenge in sequence along the relay lane.</li><li>The first player in each team completes all three challenges in order, then tags the next teammate to go.</li><li>Continue until every team member has completed the full sequence.</li><li>Keep the relay moving quickly — the next player starts as soon as they're tagged, with minimal stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'support your teammate as much as you focus on your own turn.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a simpler sequence with more time; for 'harder', use a longer sequence with less time; a head-to-head mirror between two teams adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Shared Streak Challenge</h4><p>Teams work together to build the longest combined streak of clean catches or strikes across the whole team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole teams of 4-5 spread across the space, roughly 6m x 6m per team, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone's turn matters to the total.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, generous spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker pace, tighter spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the team works together to build the longest combined streak of clean catches or strikes across the whole team, counted out loud as one shared total.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and passes/catches or strikes it around the team.</li><li>The team keeps a shared running count out loud; if a catch or strike is dropped or fumbled, the count resets to zero and play restarts immediately.</li><li>Continue for the full activity time, with each team trying to beat their own best combined streak.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone's turn matters to the total.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with generous spacing; for 'harder', use a quicker pace with tighter spacing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Skill Spotlight</h4><p>Each team nominates one member to demonstrate their strongest hand-eye skill from the unit to the group, with brief peer feedback.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole teams of 4-5 gathered in their own space, roughly 4m x 4m, no equipment needed beyond what the nominated student wants to use for their demonstration.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick your genuine strength, not just something easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher or peers suggest a focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Student justifies their own choice.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: each team nominates one member to demonstrate their strongest hand-eye skill from the unit to the group.</li><li>Teams briefly discuss and choose who will demonstrate and which skill they'll show.</li><li>The nominated student demonstrates their chosen skill to their team (or a smaller group), with a teammate feeding or assisting if needed.</li><li>The rest of the team gives brief, positive peer feedback after the demonstration.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pick your genuine strength, not just something easy.'</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher or peers suggest a focus; for 'harder', have the student justify their own choice.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Trial Challenge Games</h3><p>Full team challenge combining relay, streak, and small-sided game elements, drawing on catching, striking, reacting, timing, and footwork from across the whole unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5 for a combined event: a short relay section, a streak-counting section, and a small-sided game section, using the same lanes/areas already set up for this lesson's other activities.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before starting: this trial combines the relay, streak challenge, and a small-sided game, as a practice run for tomorrow's games day.</li><li>Run each team through a short version of the relay and streak challenge, recording simple results for each.</li><li>Move into a small-sided game section drawing on catching, striking, reacting, timing, and footwork from across the whole unit, using standard scoring for that format.</li><li>After each stoppage in the game section, restart immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions throughout so everyone experiences the full trial.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'this is your practice run — notice what you want to bring to tomorrow.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What part of your coordination are you most confident in going into tomorrow's games day?</li><li>Who supported their team really well in today's challenges?</li><li>What's one hand-eye skill you'd still like to work on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Combines a range of hand-eye skills together in team challenges</li><li>Supports the team's shared effort</li><li>Reflects honestly on personal strengths and areas to work on</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Match teams thoughtfully so challenges are competitive and enjoyable for everyone; offer flexible rules or equipment adjustments so every challenge remains genuinely achievable.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Hand-Eye Coordination Games Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating multi-game/challenge day celebrating hand-eye coordination across the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, soft bats/paddles, cones, bibs, simple rotation chart or draw sheet</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across multiple simultaneous stations/games; clear rotation system</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's hand-eye coordination skills in a games day setting, showing fair play and reflecting on our growth.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>apply a range of hand-eye coordination skills from across the unit in competitive challenges and games</li><li>play fairly and stay coordinated and confident throughout the games day</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Coordination Circuit Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, with 3-4 simple markers (cones) placed around the space to jog between. No other equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate a light jog that weaves between the cones, adding a quick change of pace or direction at each one.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space, weaving between the cones at their own pace.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'high knees' for a few steps, 'quick feet' shuffle at a cone, or 'freeze' for a two-second balanced hold.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this circuit-style jog reflects the multi-skill focus of Block 4, raising heart rate while touching on movement, balance, and quick changes.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the whole class into one large circle with room for everyone to stretch without touching their neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down, then gather into the circle.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — shoulders, calves, quads, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe.'</li><li>Finish with a round of applause or a group cheer celebrating the unit's progress, since this warm-down is used across the culminating lessons of the whole unit.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills from across the whole unit — tracking and catching, striking, reacting, timing, and combining hands and feet.</td><td>The games day brings everything the unit has built together under real, enjoyable pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and support teammates and opponents throughout.</td><td>Fair play and clear communication are as much a part of this unit as the coordination skills themselves.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth as a hand-eye coordinator compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on your own improvement in tracking, striking, and reacting is as valuable as the result of any one game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skill Circuit Warm-Up</h4><p>A quick circuit combining catching, striking, and reaction — a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3 short stations around the space (roughly 5m x 5m each): catching, striking, reaction. Split the class into teams of 4-5, one team starting at each station, with balls and soft bats/paddles distributed across the stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every touch.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students this is a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and begin the activity immediately on 'go'.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 60-90 seconds), teams rotate to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed all 3 stations.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring your best technique from across the unit into every touch.'</li><li>For 'easier', give longer time per station; for 'harder', give shorter time with quicker rotation; a head-to-head mirror, beat-the-clock format adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Coordination Challenge</h4><p>Before games begin, each team huddles briefly to agree one hand-eye skill they'll try to show off today.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 gathered in their own huddle space before games begin, no equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Make it specific and meaningful to your team.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: before games begin, each team briefly huddles to agree one hand-eye skill they'll try to show off today.</li><li>Give teams 30-60 seconds to huddle and agree on their chosen skill.</li><li>Each team briefly shares their chosen skill with the teacher or the wider group if time allows.</li><li>Move straight into the day's games once every team has decided.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'make it specific and meaningful to your team.'</li><li>For 'easier'/'harder' this activity stays the same for all groups (n/a); role rotation within the huddle discussion keeps everyone involved (chaos variant).</li></ul><hr><h4>Coordination Spotlight</h4><p>Throughout the games day, the teacher notices and briefly highlights one sharp piece of hand-eye coordination from each team to the whole group.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No separate space needed — this runs alongside the day's games, with the teacher circulating between playing areas.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice good technique, not just good outcomes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain to the class at the start of the day that the teacher will be watching for sharp pieces of hand-eye coordination throughout.</li><li>As games run, the teacher circulates and notices moments of strong technique — a tracked catch, a clean strike, a well-timed reach.</li><li>Briefly pause at a natural break to highlight one sharp piece of coordination from each team to the whole group.</li><li>Resume games immediately after each highlight so momentum isn't lost.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice good technique, not just good outcomes.'</li><li>For 'easier'/'harder' this activity stays the same for all groups (n/a); rotating which team gets a bonus objective spotlighted next adds extra challenge (chaos variant).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Coordination Games Day</h3><p>Students rotate through short games and challenges across multiple areas, using simple scoring or a fair-play points system. Every skill from the unit — tracking, catching, striking, timing, reacting, and combining hands and feet — is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5 and set up multiple short game/challenge areas around the space (roughly 15m x 10m each) drawing on activities from across the whole unit. Balls, soft bats/paddles, cones, bibs, and a simple rotation chart or draw sheet showing which team plays where and when.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before starting: teams rotate through short games and challenges across multiple areas using the rotation chart, with a simple scoring or fair-play points system.</li><li>Teams move to their first area according to the rotation chart and begin play immediately.</li><li>Use simple scoring for each game/challenge, plus fair-play points awarded by the teacher for good sportsmanship and technique.</li><li>On the teacher's signal (roughly every 5-6 minutes), teams rotate to their next area on the chart.</li><li>Continue until every team has completed every area, then gather the whole class to share overall results and fair-play points.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'every skill from the unit — tracking, catching, striking, timing, reacting, and combining hands and feet — is relevant somewhere today.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one hand-eye coordination skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did sharp, confident coordination look like in today's games day?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other games or sports?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of hand-eye coordination skills from across the unit, not just one</li><li>Plays fairly and communicates throughout a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth as a coordinator compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure the games day with balanced/mixed-ability groupings; consider a simple fair-play recognition alongside game results to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Hockey":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Stick & Ball Control (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Glue Stick":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling — control with the stick at a stationary point</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while your stick feels clumsy at first, and encouraging teammates whose ball control looks different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 stick + 1 ball per student, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread out — one stick's-length minimum gap when dribbling freely; sticks stay below knee height at all times; clear grid boundaries so students don't collide</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to control the ball with the flat face of our stick while it stays close to our feet.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can keep the ball within a small square around my feet as I tap it side to side</li><li>I can control the ball using only the flat face of my stick</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Stick Freeze Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one stick and ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, ball dead under the stick, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'switch hands position!' — students continue dribbling but adjust their grip or stance without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up stick control before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually, sticks carried safely at the side.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the stick close to the ball with soft, ready hands.</td><td>Close control means faster reaction if pressure arrives.</td></tr><tr><td>Use small taps with the flat face of the stick.</td><td>The flat face is the only legal contact surface and gives the most control.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your head up between touches.</td><td>Trains awareness of space and teammates without losing the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Tap Tap Control</h4><p>Standing in a personal space, students tap the ball gently side to side using only the flat face of the stick, keeping it within a small square around their feet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own stick and ball, standing inside a personal square roughly 1.5m x 1.5m marked by a cone at each corner, spread out across a grid about 15m x 15m for a class of 25-30 so no two squares touch.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Soft hands on the stick — let the ball come to you, don't stab at it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger square, slower taps</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller square, quicker taps</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate tapping the ball gently side to side using only the flat face of the stick, letting the ball come to the stick rather than jabbing at it.</li><li>Students stand in their own marked square and begin tapping the ball continuously on the spot, keeping it inside the square boundary.</li><li>Watch that the flat face stays in contact each tap — if a student catches the ball with the rounded edge, pause them and reset the grip.</li><li>Coach the key message around the room: 'soft hands on the stick — let the ball come to you, don't stab at it.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the square and slow the tap rhythm; for 'harder', shrink the square and call for quicker taps.</li><li>Run for 2-3 minutes continuously, walking the grid to spot-check individual technique rather than stopping the whole class.</li></ul><hr><h4>Eyes Up Taps</h4><p>While tapping the ball on the spot, students hold up fingers on their non-stick hand when the teacher calls 'show me!' — forcing them to keep their head up rather than watching the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same personal squares as Tap Tap Control, one stick and ball per student, no partner needed — the teacher calls out to the whole class at once from the front.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feel the ball through the stick, trust it without looking down</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower taps, more time to respond</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster taps, quick-fire calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: students keep tapping the ball on the spot with the flat stick face while their non-stick hand stays free at their side.</li><li>On 'show me!', students hold up a number of fingers on their non-stick hand without looking down at the ball or their hand.</li><li>Call out randomly every 5-10 seconds, checking that heads are staying up rather than dropping to watch the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'feel the ball through the stick, trust it without looking down.'</li><li>For 'easier', slow the tap pace and leave longer gaps between calls; for 'harder', speed up the taps and fire calls in quick succession.</li><li>Run for 2-3 minutes, mixing up the timing of the calls so students can't predict them.</li></ul><hr><h4>Freeze &amp; Balance</h4><p>As the warm-up, but now with a partner watching — on 'freeze!' the dribbler must stop the ball dead under the stick (no rolling away) and hold it still for a count of three.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs formed within the existing grid, one stick and ball between the pair — one student dribbles on the spot while their partner stands close by ready to call 'freeze!' and count.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring the ball to a soft stop with a give in your wrists, don't jab it dead</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, more warning</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the dribbler taps the ball on the spot as in the earlier games, but now a partner is watching and will call the stop.</li><li>The dribbler begins tapping the ball gently side to side while their partner watches closely, ready to call out at any moment.</li><li>On 'freeze!', the dribbler must stop the ball dead under the stick with no rolling away, then hold it completely still for a count of three said aloud by the partner.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring the ball to a soft stop with a give in your wrists, don't jab it dead.'</li><li>Swap roles after every 3-4 freezes so both partners get equal turns dribbling and calling.</li><li>For 'easier', give more warning before the call and slow the calls down; for 'harder', call faster and less predictably.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Musical Dribble</h3><p>Whole class dribbles freely in a large grid to music (or a continuous count); when the sound stops everyone must freeze their dribble under control — anyone whose ball gets away does 3 quick tap-taps before rejoining. No eliminations — just a fun, repeated challenge.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class dribbles freely inside one large shared grid roughly 20m x 20m, each student with their own stick and ball. A phone/speaker for music (or the teacher's own steady counting) sits at the front.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: everyone dribbles freely around the big grid while the music/count plays, using the tap-tap control from today's games.</li><li>When the music stops (or the count pauses), every student must freeze their dribble under control on the spot, ball held dead under the stick.</li><li>Anyone whose ball gets away from them does 3 quick tap-taps on the spot before rejoining the next round.</li><li>There's no elimination — everyone keeps playing every round, so a loose ball just means a quick, friendly consequence rather than sitting out.</li><li>Restart the music/count as soon as everyone is set, and repeat for several rounds, mixing up how long the music plays each time.</li><li>Keep the tone upbeat and playful — this is a fun capstone to the lesson's stationary-control focus, not a competitive elimination game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you keep control of the ball while standing still?</li><li>What's the difference between stabbing at the ball and gently tapping it with soft hands?</li><li>Why does keeping your eyes up matter, even this early in learning to dribble?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses the flat face of the stick, not the edge</li><li>Keeps the ball within a small, controlled area</li><li>Can glance up briefly without losing control</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a bigger, slower-rolling ball (e.g. a foam hockey ball) or a wider square for students building stick control/confidence; success is measured by control, not speed.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Indian Dribble":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling on the move using side-to-side stick control</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding what your own hands and feet are doing while moving with the ball, and noticing what changes when it starts to click.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 stick + 1 ball per student, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions when moving through a shared space — remind students to check space before changing direction; sticks stay below knee height</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to dribble the ball from side to side across our body while moving, using both sides of the stick face.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move the ball from my right side to my left side without losing control while walking or jogging</li><li>I can keep the ball close as I change the angle of my stick</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Stick Freeze Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one stick and ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, ball dead under the stick, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'switch hands position!' — students continue dribbling but adjust their grip or stance without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up stick control before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually, sticks carried safely at the side.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Push the ball from side to side in front of you as you move.</td><td>Keeps the ball protected and under control while advancing.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the stick face angled to control the ball, not just push it forward.</td><td>Control matters more than speed at this stage.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your eyes up while moving.</td><td>Lets you see space and options without losing the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Walking Indian Dribble Lanes</h4><p>Students dribble the length of a marked lane while walking, tapping the ball side to side across their body, focusing purely on keeping control while moving.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Marked lanes roughly 1.5m wide and 10m long using cones down each side, enough lanes for the whole class to work in pairs or trios at once, one stick and ball per student.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small taps, not big swipes — the ball should never leave the stick face</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider lane, walk only</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower lane, light jog</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the Indian dribble: small taps that move the ball side to side across the body as you walk forward, using both faces of the stick.</li><li>Students enter their lane one or two at a time and walk the length of the lane tapping the ball from side to side, keeping it inside the lane markers.</li><li>Watch that taps stay small and controlled — if the ball drifts wide of the lane, students slow down and reset before continuing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'small taps, not big swipes — the ball should never leave the stick face.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the lane and keep it to a walk only; for 'harder', narrow the lane and allow a light jog.</li><li>Rotate students through continuously, sending the next student in as soon as the lane ahead is clear.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cone Weave Dribble</h4><p>Dribbling through a zig-zag line of cones, students use the Indian dribble to move the ball from side to side around each cone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A zig-zag line of 5-6 cones spaced about 2m apart, one lane per group of 3-4 students who take turns, one stick and ball per student.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Angle the stick face early, before you reach the cone, not at the last second</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider cone spacing, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower spacing, faster pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate weaving through the cones using the Indian dribble, angling the stick face to move the ball around each cone in turn.</li><li>The first student dribbles through the full zig-zag while the rest of the group watches and waits at the start.</li><li>Coach the key message as students go: 'angle the stick face early, before you reach the cone, not at the last second.'</li><li>A clean run means the ball stays under control past every cone without knocking one over — if a cone is knocked, the student slows down and keeps going rather than restarting.</li><li>For 'easier', space the cones wider and encourage a slower pace; for 'harder', narrow the spacing and pick up the pace.</li><li>Rotate to the back of the line after each run so everyone gets repeated attempts.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shrinking Grid Dribble</h4><p>Whole class dribbles inside a grid that shrinks every 30 seconds, using the Indian dribble to weave and find space as the grid gets tighter.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>One shared grid starting around 15m x 15m marked with cones, whole class inside at once, one stick and ball per student, spare cones on hand to bring the boundary in.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use small, quick taps to find gaps as the grid shrinks</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shrink slower, bigger starting grid</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shrink faster, smaller starting grid</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that everyone will dribble freely inside the grid using the Indian dribble to weave around other players and find open space.</li><li>Start the whole class dribbling inside the full-size grid, spreading out and using small controlled taps.</li><li>Every 30 seconds, quickly move the boundary cones inward to shrink the grid, forcing tighter weaving and closer awareness of others.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use small, quick taps to find gaps as the grid shrinks.'</li><li>For 'easier', shrink the grid more slowly from a bigger starting size; for 'harder', shrink faster from a smaller starting size.</li><li>Reset to the original size after 2-3 minutes and repeat if time allows.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Dribble Tag</h3><p>In a shared space, everyone dribbles their own ball while trying to gently tap other players' balls away with the flat of their stick (no reaching for sticks or bodies) while protecting their own — no elimination, just count how many times your ball gets tapped versus how many you tap.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class inside one shared grid roughly 20m x 20m, each student with their own stick and ball, no goals or targets needed for this game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: everyone dribbles their own ball while trying to gently tap other players' balls away with the flat of their stick, while also protecting their own ball.</li><li>Set the rule clearly before starting: no reaching for another player's stick or body, taps are only at the ball, and everyone keeps their own ball moving.</li><li>Play continuously for 2-3 minutes at a time — there's no elimination, so a tapped-away ball is simply chased down and brought back into play.</li><li>Students keep a rough personal tally in their head of how many times their ball gets tapped versus how many times they successfully tap someone else's.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: use the shielding and control skills from today's other games to protect the ball while still scanning for opportunities.</li><li>Stop briefly midway to remind students to keep taps light and controlled if the game is getting too physical, then restart.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did moving while dribbling feel different from dribbling on the spot?</li><li>What helped you move the ball smoothly from one side to the other?</li><li>What helped you protect your ball while others were nearby?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps the ball under control while walking/jogging</li><li>Moves the ball side to side with small, controlled taps</li><li>Keeps the ball close to the stick when others are nearby</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to practise the Indian dribble stationary before adding movement if the combined skill feels like too much at once.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Shield the Ball":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling under pressure (protect the ball)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills faithfully to give your partner a fair, honest challenge — not going so hard it isn't fun, not giving up either.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 stick + 1 ball per pair, cones for grids</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No reaching in or swinging at the ball — sticks-off pressure only until a fair tackle attempt; no pushing with the body</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to protect the ball with our body and stick while dribbling near a defender.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can keep my body between the ball and a defender</li><li>I can angle my stick to shield the ball without obstructing illegally</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Stick Freeze Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one stick and ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, ball dead under the stick, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'switch hands position!' — students continue dribbling but adjust their grip or stance without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up stick control before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually, sticks carried safely at the side.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your body and stick between the ball and the defender.</td><td>Uses your body legally as a shield within hockey's rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Use quick, small touches rather than one big push when pressured.</td><td>Keeps the ball closer and less winnable.</td></tr><tr><td>Change direction or pace to escape pressure.</td><td>More reliable than trying to shield indefinitely against a persistent defender.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Dribble</h4><p>Attacker dribbles freely; a 'shadow' defender stays a stick's length away and mirrors movement without reaching for the ball, just to get the dribbler used to pressure nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, one stick and ball per pair (the attacker's), enough space between pairs that dribbling paths don't cross.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your body and stick between the ball and the defender at all times</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stays further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: one partner dribbles freely while the other acts as a 'shadow' defender who stays close but never reaches for the ball.</li><li>The attacker begins dribbling around their space while the shadow defender stays a stick's length away, mirroring the attacker's movement.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your body and stick between the ball and the defender at all times.'</li><li>The shadow defender's only job is to apply presence, not pressure — no attempts to touch the ball during this game.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender stays further back; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to get closer while still not touching the ball.</li><li>Swap roles after 1-2 minutes so both partners practise dribbling under nearby pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Turn</h4><p>Dribbler practises turning their back slightly to the defender and rolling the ball to the far side of their body to shield it, staying low and balanced.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread across the same grid, one stick and ball per pair, enough room for a dribbler to turn their body without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use your body to feel where the defender is, not to push them away</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stationary</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender moves side to side</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate shielding: turning the back and shoulder slightly toward the defender while rolling the ball to the far side of the body, staying low and balanced.</li><li>The dribbler starts with the ball and the defender stands nearby; on a signal, the dribbler shields and rolls the ball away from the defender's side.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use your body to feel where the defender is, not to push them away.'</li><li>The defender's job is simply to stand in a fixed spot (or move lightly) so the dribbler has something real to shield against.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender stays stationary; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to move side to side to test the shield.</li><li>Swap roles every few turns so both partners get repeated practice shielding.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Protect the Ball</h4><p>In a small square, one player dribbles and must protect the ball for a count of 10 seconds against a light-touch defender attempting a fair tackle, then swap roles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small squares roughly 4m x 4m marked with cones, one per pair, one stick and ball per pair, a stopwatch or the teacher's own count for timing.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Change of pace and shielding beat trying to muscle past a defender</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger square, defender starts 2m back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller square, defender close from the start</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: one player dribbles inside the square and must protect the ball for a count of 10 seconds against a light-touch defender attempting a fair tackle.</li><li>Start the count as soon as the dribbler has the ball under control, with the defender applying gentle, sticks-off pressure only.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'change of pace and shielding beat trying to muscle past a defender.'</li><li>If the defender wins the ball cleanly before 10 seconds is up, or the dribbler protects it for the full count, that round ends and roles swap.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger square and have the defender start 2m back; for 'harder', shrink the square and start the defender close from the beginning.</li><li>Reinforce fair, sticks-off tackling throughout — no reaching in or swinging at the ball.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Keep-Away Hockey</h3><p>3v3 or 4v4, small area, no goals — a team scores a point for every 5 consecutive dribbles/passes kept under pressure without the other team touching the ball. Protecting the ball under pressure directly determines success.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small areas roughly 10m x 10m marked with cones, groups of 3v3 or 4v4, one ball per group, sticks for every player, no goals needed.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: a team earns a point for every 5 consecutive dribbles or passes kept under pressure without the other team touching the ball.</li><li>Start with one team in possession inside their area; the other team applies sticks-off pressure only, trying to intercept or force a loose ball.</li><li>Play continues with a running count out loud — the possessing team calls their numbers as they string dribbles and passes together.</li><li>The moment the ball is lost or touched by the other team, possession switches and the count resets to zero for the new team.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: shielding and quick decisions under pressure are what earns the point, not just raw ball skill.</li><li>Rotate which teams play against each other every few minutes if running multiple small groups at once.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you protect the ball when a defender was close?</li><li>How did shielding with your body (not your stick swinging) make a difference?</li><li>What did you notice about giving your partner a fair, honest challenge?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps body and stick between the ball and defender</li><li>Shields without obstructing illegally (not blocking with the body alone)</li><li>Changes pace/direction rather than freezing under pressure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start defenders further back (2m) and only close in once the dribbler is confident; allow a 'no-defender' version for students still consolidating basic control.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Push Pass Precision":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Push pass accuracy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your pass needs to land so your partner can control it easily, not just where it's easiest to push from.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 stick + 1 ball per pair, cones for targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passing lanes should not cross other pairs' lanes — space grids out; push passes only, no lifted balls</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to play an accurate push pass along the ground using the flat face of the stick.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can push the ball out smoothly with a rolling wrist action, stepping toward my target</li><li>I can pass so the ball arrives flat along the ground at my partner's stick</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Stick Freeze Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one stick and ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, ball dead under the stick, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'switch hands position!' — students continue dribbling but adjust their grip or stance without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up stick control before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually, sticks carried safely at the side.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the stick close to the ball and push through it, not swing at it.</td><td>A push pass relies on a smooth transfer of weight, not a hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Step toward your target as you push.</td><td>The step adds accuracy and a little power.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target with the stick face open.</td><td>Keeps the ball flat and on target.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall of Targets</h4><p>Partners push pass through a small cone gate from increasing distances, scoring a point each time the ball rolls cleanly through and stays flat along the ground.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other across a marked distance starting at 5m, with a small cone gate (two cones about 1m apart) set up midway between them, one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Roll your wrists through the ball, step toward your target as you release</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gate, closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gate, further apart</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the push pass: rolling the wrists through the ball and stepping toward the target as the ball is released, keeping it flat along the ground.</li><li>Partners take turns pushing the ball through the cone gate to each other, starting from the closer distance.</li><li>A point is scored each time the ball rolls cleanly through the gate and stays flat on the ground without bouncing or lifting.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'roll your wrists through the ball, step toward your target as you release.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the gate and move partners closer; for 'harder', narrow the gate and move partners further apart.</li><li>Track scoring streaks as a fun way to build focus — see how many clean passes through the gate a pair can string together.</li></ul><hr><h4>Sweep to a Target</h4><p>Partners practise the sweep pass (a wider, sweeping motion for balls slightly to the side), aiming for the ball to arrive flat at their partner's stick.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and space as Wall of Targets, one stick and ball per pair, no gate needed — partners simply aim for each other's stick.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get low, sweep through the ball rather than jabbing at it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer distance, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Greater distance, add a target zone</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sweep pass: a wider, sweeping motion used for balls slightly to the side, getting low and sweeping through the ball rather than jabbing at it.</li><li>Partners take turns placing the ball slightly to one side of themselves and using the sweep pass to send it flat to their partner's stick.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'get low, sweep through the ball rather than jabbing at it.'</li><li>The receiving partner focuses on giving with their stick to cushion the pass as it arrives, keeping it under control.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the distance closer and the pace slower; for 'harder', increase the distance and add a small target zone to aim for.</li><li>Set a bonus objective, like naming a number of clean sweep passes in a row, to keep the challenge engaging.</li></ul><hr><h4>Choose Your Pass</h4><p>Partners alternate between push and sweep passes based on a called instruction, building quick recognition of when to use each.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and space as the earlier games, one stick and ball per pair, with the teacher or a rotating caller giving instructions from the front or within the pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide the pass type before the ball arrives, not after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer calls, more time to decide</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick-fire calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that partners will alternate between the push pass and the sweep pass depending on which one is called out before the pass.</li><li>One partner calls 'push!' or 'sweep!' just before receiving, and the passer must use that exact pass type to send the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide the pass type before the ball arrives, not after.'</li><li>Swap who is calling after every 4-5 passes so both partners practise deciding and executing quickly.</li><li>For 'easier', reduce how often calls change and give more time to decide; for 'harder', fire the calls quickly with little warning.</li><li>Keep the activity flowing continuously rather than stopping between every pass.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Two-Pass-Type Hockey</h3><p>4v4, small area, no goals — teams must use both a push pass and a sweep pass at least once during a passing sequence before scoring, encouraging a full passing toolkit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small areas roughly 10m x 10m marked with cones, groups of 4v4, one ball per group, sticks for every player, no goals needed.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before a team can score, their passing sequence must include at least one push pass and one sweep pass.</li><li>Start with one team in possession; the other team applies sticks-off pressure, trying to intercept or force mistakes.</li><li>As the team passes the ball around, players call out which pass type they're using so the requirement is clear and visible.</li><li>A point is scored once a team has used both pass types in a single possession and completes a set number of consecutive passes (agree the number with the class beforehand, e.g. 5).</li><li>Coach the key message: encourage players to recognise when a sweep pass suits a wider-angled option versus a straight push pass.</li><li>Rotate possession fairly and swap which teams play against each other every few minutes.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What technique helped your passes go where you wanted?</li><li>When might a sweep pass be more useful than a push pass?</li><li>What happens to the game when passes aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Rolls the wrists through the push pass rather than jabbing</li><li>Gets low for the sweep pass</li><li>Chooses an appropriate pass type for the distance/situation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Shorten distances for students still building the wrist action/technique; the gate/target structures scale naturally by moving cones closer.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Receive & Turn":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Receiving a pass &amp; controlling first touch</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself and encouraging teammates while receiving and turning confidence builds — everyone's hands and feet look different at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 stick + 1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passes should stay below knee height and along the ground; no contact during turning practice — sticks-off only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to receive a pass with soft hands and control our first touch before turning to play.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can cushion the ball to a stop with a soft, giving stick face</li><li>I can turn with the ball under control after receiving it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Stick Freeze Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one stick and ball per student, cones marking the outer boundary of the space.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone dribbles freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'freeze!' at random intervals — students immediately stop their dribble under control on the spot, ball dead under the stick, then resume dribbling on the next 'go.'</li><li>Occasionally call 'switch hands position!' — students continue dribbling but adjust their grip or stance without stopping.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up stick control before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually, sticks carried safely at the side.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get in line with the ball early with a soft, angled stick face.</td><td>Gives you time and surface to cushion the ball properly.</td></tr><tr><td>Give slightly with the stick on contact.</td><td>Absorbs the ball's speed instead of it bouncing away.</td></tr><tr><td>Control the ball into space away from pressure.</td><td>Keeps you moving and away from a defender rather than stopping dead.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Soft Hands Receive</h4><p>Partners pass; the receiver must cushion every pass to a dead stop under their stick before pushing it back.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 5m apart, one stick and ball per pair, spread out across a grid so passing lanes don't cross other pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give with the stick face as the ball arrives, don't meet it with a stiff stick</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passes with more pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate cushioning: giving slightly with the stick face as the ball arrives so it stops dead rather than bouncing away.</li><li>Partners take turns passing gently to each other; the receiver must bring every pass to a complete stop under their stick before pushing it back.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'give with the stick face as the ball arrives, don't meet it with a stiff stick.'</li><li>If a pass bounces away out of control, that receiver simply collects it and resets before continuing — no restart of the whole activity.</li><li>For 'easier', slow the passes right down; for 'harder', add more pace to each pass.</li><li>Swap who's passing first every few turns so both partners get equal receiving practice.</li></ul><hr><h4>Receive &amp; Open Up</h4><p>Students receive a gentle pass and practise turning their body and stick to face a new direction, controlling the ball onto their stick as they turn.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs and space as Soft Hands Receive, one stick and ball per pair, with a little extra room around each pair for the receiver to turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check over your shoulder before the ball arrives so you know which way you'll turn</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller turn (quarter turn)</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full half turn, added speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate checking over the shoulder before the ball arrives to know which way to turn once it's received.</li><li>One partner passes gently while the receiver checks over their shoulder, receives the ball, and turns their body and stick to face a new direction.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'check over your shoulder before the ball arrives so you know which way you'll turn.'</li><li>The receiver controls the ball onto their stick as they complete the turn, rather than stopping it first and turning afterward.</li><li>For 'easier', practise a smaller quarter turn; for 'harder', use a full half turn and add a bit more pace to the pass.</li><li>Swap roles regularly so both partners practise passing and receiving-and-turning.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Receive</h4><p>A shadow defender stands nearby (not touching) while the receiver controls the ball and turns to shield it with their body before passing away from the defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios (or pairs with the teacher rotating in a third) spread across the grid, one stick and ball per group, a shadow defender standing nearby but not touching.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your body and stick between you and the defender as you turn</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to move around the receiver</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: a passer, a receiver, and a shadow defender who stands close by without reaching for the ball.</li><li>The passer sends a gentle pass to the receiver, who controls it and turns while using their body to shield the ball from the shadow defender.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your body and stick between you and the defender as you turn.'</li><li>Once shielded and turned, the receiver passes the ball away from the defender to complete the sequence.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender stands further away; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to move around the receiver while still not touching the ball.</li><li>Rotate roles every few turns so everyone practises passing, receiving, and defending.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Secure Receive Hockey</h3><p>4v4, small area, light shadow defence only — teams score for maintaining clean, secure receives and controlled turns under mild pressure, building toward full defending in later lessons.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small areas roughly 10m x 10m marked with cones, groups of 4v4, one ball per group, sticks for every player, no goals needed, one light shadow defender per team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: teams earn points for clean, secure receives and controlled turns made under mild pressure from a nearby shadow defender.</li><li>Play possession-based small-sided hockey where defenders apply only light, sticks-off shadow pressure rather than full tackling.</li><li>Coach highlights good receives out loud as they happen — a clean stop, a good check-over-the-shoulder, a controlled turn — to reinforce the lesson's focus.</li><li>A point can be awarded (by the coach or a simple team tally) for a set number of clean receive-and-turn sequences completed under pressure.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: composure on the first touch is what sets up everything else in the game.</li><li>This game builds directly toward full defending in later lessons, so keep the defensive pressure deliberately light for now.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did soft hands do to help you control the ball cleanly?</li><li>How did turning help you protect the ball after receiving it?</li><li>What made turning under a bit of pressure trickier?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Cushions the ball to a controlled stop, not a stiff block</li><li>Turns with the ball under control</li><li>Checks over the shoulder before the ball arrives</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a slightly softer or slower-rolling ball for students who find receiving difficult, so the turning focus isn't lost to a hard first touch.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Skills & Shooting (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Give and Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing on the move (give-and-go)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role and where you fit in the play — knowing when to move and why.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passing lanes clear of other groups when running through space; push passes only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball and immediately move into space to receive it back.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass and then run into space, not stand still</li><li>I can time my pass so a moving teammate can control it easily</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Push-Pass Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that push passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One stick and ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start push-passing gently back and forth while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up, no equipment needed beyond sticks set safely aside, enough space for each pair to stretch without touching a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly with your partner for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Facing your partner, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — hamstring reach, quad stretch with a hand on the partner's shoulder for balance, and a gentle side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions calm and simple, checking that partners are supporting gently rather than pulling on each other.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask pairs how their combination play felt today, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with partners giving each other a quick high-five before sitting down for discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pass, then move immediately into new space.</td><td>Standing still after passing makes you easy to mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your run to receive the return pass in stride.</td><td>Arriving too early or late means an awkward, stationary receive.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate to your teammate that you want the return.</td><td>Speeds up decision-making under pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall Pass Relay</h4><p>Pairs practise 'pass and move': Player A passes to Player B and immediately jogs to a new cone; B controls and passes back to A's new position. Repeat down a channel.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Marked channels roughly 15m long and 3m wide using cones, pairs working within each channel, one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move immediately after passing, don't watch the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a defender jogging alongside (not intercepting yet)</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the give-and-go: Player A passes to Player B and immediately jogs to a new position further down the channel.</li><li>Player A starts with the ball and passes to Player B, who is standing a little further down the channel.</li><li>The moment the pass is played, Player A jogs ahead to a new cone marker while Player B controls the ball.</li><li>Player B then passes to Player A's new position, and the pattern repeats all the way down the channel.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move immediately after passing, don't watch the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', walk instead of jog; for 'harder', add a defender jogging alongside without intercepting yet.</li></ul><hr><h4>Give and Go Gates</h4><p>In trios, two attackers combine give-and-go passes to move the ball through a series of gates down the field, racing another trio.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios, with a series of 4-5 cone gates spaced roughly 4-5m apart down a marked channel about 20m long, one stick and ball per trio, a second trio racing alongside in a parallel channel.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pass early, before you're under real pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gates/no time pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gates, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup: two attackers combine give-and-go passes to move the ball through each gate in turn, while the third teammate watches and times the run.</li><li>The first attacker starts with the ball and passes to their partner as they move toward the first gate, immediately running into space for a return pass.</li><li>The pair continue combining give-and-go passes gate by gate until they reach the end of the channel.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pass early, before you're under real pressure.'</li><li>For 'easier', use wider gates with no time pressure; for 'harder', narrow the gates and time the run as a race against the neighbouring trio.</li><li>Rotate the third player into the pair each run so everyone gets multiple turns attacking.</li></ul><hr><h4>1-2 vs Shadow Defender</h4><p>Pairs attempt a give-and-go against one light-touch defender who can only intercept after the first pass is played (delayed pressure).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 10m x 8m, one per group of three (two attackers, one shadow defender), one stick and ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The 'give' player should move into space immediately, not just stand and admire the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender starts further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close immediately</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the delayed-pressure rule: the defender may only attempt to intercept after the first pass of the give-and-go has been played.</li><li>The two attackers start with the ball and attempt a give-and-go combination to move past the defender's zone.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'the give player should move into space immediately, not just stand and admire the pass.'</li><li>The defender waits until the first pass is played, then closes down to try to intercept or pressure the return pass.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender starts further back; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to close immediately once the first pass is played.</li><li>Rotate the defender role every few attempts so everyone practises attacking and defending.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3v3 + 1 Target Player Hockey</h3><p>Small-sided game with small goals/cone goals; encourage give-and-go combinations as a way to beat defenders, especially near the target player position. Coach highlights any successful give-and-go in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A small-sided area with small goals or cone goals at each end, groups of 3v3 plus one target player per team, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target player role: one player per team stays higher up the field as a focal point to combine with, especially near goal.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, encouraging attackers to look for give-and-go combinations rather than always dribbling solo.</li><li>Coach highlights any successful give-and-go out loud as it happens in play, especially combinations involving the target player.</li><li>Rotate who plays the target player role every few minutes so everyone gets a turn in that position.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: a well-timed pass-and-move can beat a defender that dribbling alone can't.</li><li>Keep goals or scoring simple so the focus stays on the passing pattern rather than just outcome.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a give-and-go work well today?</li><li>Why does moving after your pass matter?</li><li>Can you describe a moment you found space at the right time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves immediately after passing</li><li>Receives the return pass without needing to stop and reset</li><li>Communicates (calls for the ball) with a teammate</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole combination down to walking pace for students still building the timing/decision sequence; speed increases as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Perfect Push":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting technique (push shot)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising a skill faithfully, with focus and effort, even when it doesn't work first time — using your talent well takes repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, small goals/cone goals, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Shooters should have clear space in front of them; rotate turns so no one is standing directly in the shooting lane; push shots only — no lifted or swung hits</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct technique for a push shot at goal, using a low, balanced stance and a firm push through the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can get low with my stick close to the ball before pushing firmly through it</li><li>I can keep my shot flat and below knee height</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Push-Pass Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that push passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One stick and ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start push-passing gently back and forth while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up, no equipment needed beyond sticks set safely aside, enough space for each pair to stretch without touching a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly with your partner for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Facing your partner, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — hamstring reach, quad stretch with a hand on the partner's shoulder for balance, and a gentle side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions calm and simple, checking that partners are supporting gently rather than pulling on each other.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask pairs how their combination play felt today, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with partners giving each other a quick high-five before sitting down for discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get low with a wide, balanced stance over the ball.</td><td>A stable base lets you generate power safely and accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>Push through the ball with the stick face angled at the target.</td><td>This is the safe, legal, and most accurate way to shoot in modified hockey.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target.</td><td>Stopping early reduces both power and accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Balanced Stance Practice</h4><p>Students practise the push shot stance (low, knees bent, stick close to the ball, weight moving forward) without shooting hard yet, focusing purely on set-up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student with their own stick and ball, spread out in a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no shooting yet — this is stance and set-up only.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your body weight moving through the ball as you push, not just your arms</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider stance, more time to set up</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker set-up, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the push shot stance: low body position, knees bent, stick close to the ball, weight ready to move forward through the shot.</li><li>Students place their ball on the ground and practise getting into the stance without shooting, holding the position for a few seconds each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'get your body weight moving through the ball as you push, not just your arms.'</li><li>Walk the grid checking that knees are bent and the stick is close to the ball rather than reaching out awkwardly.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider stance with more time to set up; for 'harder', time how quickly students can get into a clean set-up position.</li><li>Run for 2-3 minutes, resetting the stance repeatedly rather than shooting.</li></ul><hr><h4>Push Shot Walkthrough</h4><p>Students walk through the push shot from a stationary ball at a target, focusing purely on a low, flat strike that stays on the ground.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small goals or cone goals set up around the space, students in pairs or small groups taking turns from a stationary ball at a marked spot roughly 6-8m from the target.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Follow through low, toward your target — don't lift the stick</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer range, bigger target</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further range, smaller target</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the push shot from a stationary ball, focusing on a low, flat strike that stays on the ground all the way to the target.</li><li>Each student walks through the push shot slowly at first, checking their stance before striking the ball toward the target.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'follow through low, toward your target — don't lift the stick.'</li><li>After a few slow walkthroughs, allow a normal-paced push shot while keeping the same low, controlled technique.</li><li>For 'easier', move closer with a bigger target; for 'harder', move further back with a smaller target.</li><li>Rotate turns quickly so everyone gets multiple repetitions rather than standing and waiting.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shot Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a shooting station, taking quick-fire push shots from a fixed spot; the group tracks consecutive 'clean technique' shots as a team streak (technique counts even if it misses).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 rotating through a shooting station with a fixed spot roughly 6-8m from a small goal or cone target, one ball shared per group, a way to track the group's streak (tally marks or calling numbers aloud).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your technique each shot, don't chase power</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer range, bigger target</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further range, smaller target zones worth bonus points</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the group is tracking consecutive shots with clean technique as a team streak — technique counts even if the shot misses the target.</li><li>Students take turns shooting quick-fire from the fixed spot, with the group calling out the streak count after each clean-technique attempt.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'reset your technique each shot, don't chase power.'</li><li>If a shot has poor technique (rushed, lifted, off-balance), the streak resets to zero and the group starts counting again.</li><li>For 'easier', shoot from closer with a bigger target; for 'harder', shoot from further with smaller target zones worth bonus points.</li><li>Rotate through the group quickly so the challenge stays fast-paced and everyone gets frequent turns.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Shooting Hockey</h3><p>4v4 with small goals; emphasis in coaching feedback is on clean push shot technique, not just outcome — celebrate good technique attempts even if they miss.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with small goals at each end, groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: clean push shot technique matters as much as the outcome, so good attempts get celebrated even when they miss.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with players looking for opportunities to shoot using the push shot technique from today's games.</li><li>Coach calls out and praises clean technique on shot attempts specifically, regardless of whether the shot goes in.</li><li>Encourage players to reset their stance quickly for each shooting opportunity rather than rushing a poor-technique attempt.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'reset your technique each shot, don't chase power.'</li><li>Rotate goalkeepers (if used) or defenders regularly so everyone experiences different roles.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'good technique' feel like when you shoot well?</li><li>What part of your shooting technique are you most proud of today?</li><li>What will you keep practising?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gets low with the stick close to the ball before shooting</li><li>Follows through flat and low toward the target</li><li>Keeps the shot below knee height</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a closer distance or a bigger target goal for students building confidence around shooting; success is measured by technique, not outcome.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Spot Shooter":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting accuracy from varied spots</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your shot will have the most impact rather than just reacting or always shooting from the same spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, small goals/cone goals, cones marking shooting spots</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Rotate shooting spots so no one crosses in front of another shooter mid-attempt; push shots only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to shoot accurately from a range of spots around the goal, not just one favourite position.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a target area before I shoot</li><li>I can adjust my technique slightly depending on my distance and angle to the goal</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Push-Pass Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that push passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One stick and ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start push-passing gently back and forth while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up, no equipment needed beyond sticks set safely aside, enough space for each pair to stretch without touching a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly with your partner for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Facing your partner, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — hamstring reach, quad stretch with a hand on the partner's shoulder for balance, and a gentle side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions calm and simple, checking that partners are supporting gently rather than pulling on each other.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask pairs how their combination play felt today, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with partners giving each other a quick high-five before sitting down for discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Reset your stance for each new shooting spot or angle.</td><td>Reusing the same angle from a different position throws off your aim.</td></tr><tr><td>Pick a specific target area before shooting.</td><td>A specific aim is more accurate than a general one.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust technique slightly for distance or angle.</td><td>Small adjustments keep the shot consistent from different spots.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Five Spot Shootout</h4><p>Students rotate through five marked spots around the goal (varying angle and distance), taking one shot from each before rotating.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Five marked shooting spots around a small goal or cone target, varying in angle and distance (roughly 4-8m out), one stick and ball per student rotating through, cones marking each spot clearly.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your stance for each new spot — don't reuse the same angle from a different position</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Spots closer together, closer to the goal</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Spots further out, wider angles</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate that each spot requires a slightly different stance and angle of approach compared to straight-on shooting.</li><li>Students rotate through all five spots in order, taking one shot from each before moving to the next.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'reset your stance for each new spot — don't reuse the same angle from a different position.'</li><li>Between spots, remind students to briefly check their feet and stick angle before shooting rather than rushing straight into it.</li><li>For 'easier', bring the spots closer together and closer to the goal; for 'harder', spread the spots further out at wider angles.</li><li>Track clean strikes across all five spots as a simple personal tally.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call Your Spot</h4><p>A partner calls out which of the five spots to shoot from next, building quick adaptation between different shooting angles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same five marked spots as Five Spot Shootout, pairs working together — one shooter, one caller — with a ball fed to the shooter for each attempt.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your target and adjust your feet before you receive the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, more time to reset</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick-fire calls between spots</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup: a partner calls out which of the five spots to shoot from next, and the shooter must move there and take the shot.</li><li>The caller names a spot, the shooter moves to it, receives a fed ball, and takes their shot from that exact position.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide your target and adjust your feet before you receive the pass.'</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 shots so both partners practise shooting and calling.</li><li>For 'easier', call more slowly with more time to reset between spots; for 'harder', fire the calls quickly between spots.</li><li>Keep the feed to the shooter simple and consistent so the challenge stays focused on the shooting decision.</li></ul><hr><h4>Spot Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups compete to make the most clean shots across all five spots combined within a time limit, encouraging consistency everywhere, not just one strong spot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same five marked spots, small groups of 3-4 competing together, one ball per group, a stopwatch or the teacher's own timer, a simple tally system to track clean shots.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't rush your best spot — every spot deserves the same careful technique</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer spots, more time</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>All five spots, tighter time limit</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the group competes to make the most clean shots across all five spots combined within a set time limit.</li><li>Students rotate through the group, each taking a shot from a called or self-chosen spot, with the group tracking a running tally of clean strikes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'don't rush your best spot — every spot deserves the same careful technique.'</li><li>Keep the group moving quickly between shooters so the time limit creates urgency without sacrificing technique.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer spots with more time; for 'harder', require all five spots within a tighter time limit.</li><li>Announce the final tally at the end and celebrate the group's total rather than comparing harshly between groups.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Spot Value Hockey</h3><p>4v4 with marked shooting spots worth different point values (further/harder angles worth more) — teams choose which spots to attack based on what's open, rewarding accuracy from a range of positions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with a small goal at one end, five marked shooting spots around it worth different point values (closer/straighter spots worth less, further/wider angles worth more), groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the point-value system before starting: spots further out or at harder angles are worth more points than easy, close spots.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey where teams choose which spot to attack based on what's open in the moment, rather than always going for the easiest option.</li><li>Coach the key message: reward accuracy from a range of positions, not just the closest or easiest chance.</li><li>Track points as a team tally across the game, celebrating both safe close-range shots and brave higher-value attempts.</li><li>Rotate defenders regularly so different players get to test different shooting spots under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone playful — the point values are there to encourage variety, not to punish players for choosing a simpler shot.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Which spot did you find easiest, and which was hardest? Why do you think that is?</li><li>What did you adjust about your technique from different spots?</li><li>How did thinking ahead about your target area help your shooting?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Adjusts stance/angle for different spots</li><li>Chooses a target area before shooting</li><li>Maintains consistent technique across varied positions</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce the number of spots or move them closer to the goal for students building confidence; the five-spot idea can scale down to three for a simpler version.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Combo Play":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining dribble → pass → shoot</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual skills (dribble, pass, shoot) fit together with your team's play for a shared outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, small goals/cone goals, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games small-sided (3v3 max) so the combined sequence comes thick and fast without overcrowding; push shots only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine dribbling, passing, and shooting smoothly in one sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dribble into space, then choose to pass or shoot based on what's open</li><li>I can move from a dribble into a shot without stopping awkwardly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Push-Pass Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that push passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One stick and ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start push-passing gently back and forth while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up, no equipment needed beyond sticks set safely aside, enough space for each pair to stretch without touching a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly with your partner for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Facing your partner, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — hamstring reach, quad stretch with a hand on the partner's shoulder for balance, and a gentle side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions calm and simple, checking that partners are supporting gently rather than pulling on each other.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask pairs how their combination play felt today, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with partners giving each other a quick high-five before sitting down for discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your head up throughout to read what's open.</td><td>The sequence has to respond to the real game situation.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide early — pass or shoot — so your body can prepare.</td><td>Late decisions cause rushed, poor technique.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball under close control between each phase.</td><td>Losing control mid-sequence gives defenders an easy chance to intervene.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Dribble-Pass-Shoot Drill</h4><p>Player dribbles toward the goal, then either passes to a moving teammate or pulls up for a shot, all in one continuous sequence, walked through slowly first.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3, a small goal or cone goal at one end, cones marking a dribbling channel roughly 10m long leading toward the goal, one stick and ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Each part flows into the next — don't pause awkwardly between the dribble and your decision</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time between parts</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, less time</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk the sequence through slowly first: dribble forward, then either pass to a moving teammate or pull up for a shot, all as one continuous action.</li><li>The ball-carrier dribbles down the channel while a teammate moves to offer a passing option near the goal.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'each part flows into the next — don't pause awkwardly between the dribble and your decision.'</li><li>The ball-carrier decides in the moment whether to pass to the moving teammate or shoot themselves, based on what's open.</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace with more time between parts; for 'harder', speed the whole sequence up with less time to decide.</li><li>Rotate roles after each attempt so everyone practises the ball-carrier and support roles.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 Combo Challenge</h4><p>Two attackers against one defender — the ball-carrier must combine a dribble, and then a pass or shot, to beat the defender's numbers disadvantage.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 12m x 10m with a small goal or cone goal at one end, groups of three (two attackers, one defender), one stick and ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If the shot isn't on, the extra pass usually is — read it quickly</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower grid, faster defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: two attackers face one defender and must use the extra player to create a scoring chance.</li><li>The two attackers start with the ball and combine a dribble, then a pass or shot, to beat the defender's numbers disadvantage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if the shot isn't on, the extra pass usually is — read it quickly.'</li><li>The defender applies fair, sticks-off pressure, trying to block the shot or force a mistake without fouling.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider grid with a slower defender; for 'harder', narrow the grid and speed up the defender.</li><li>Rotate the defender role regularly so everyone experiences defending against a numbers disadvantage.</li></ul><hr><h4>Continuous Combo Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where every scoring attempt must be preceded by a visible dribble-then-decision sequence, rewarding whichever team keeps combining the skills successfully.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with a small goal or cone goal, groups of 3v3 or 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always be thinking one step ahead — dribble, then what's my best option?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every scoring attempt must be preceded by a visible dribble-then-decision sequence, not just an immediate pass or shot.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey where the ball-carrier must show a clear dribble before choosing to pass or shoot.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always be thinking one step ahead — dribble, then what's my best option?'</li><li>If a team scores without a visible dribble beforehand, the coach can simply note it and remind them of the rule for next time rather than disallowing the goal.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with more time; for 'harder', shrink the grid and speed up decision-making.</li><li>Track scoring streaks or beat-the-clock challenges to keep the pace lively.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 4v3 Combination Hockey</h3><p>Attacking team has a numbers advantage (4v3); success depends on combining today's three skills — dribbling to create an opening, then reading whether to pass or shoot cleanly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with a small goal or cone goal, groups of 4v3 (attackers have the numbers advantage), sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: the attacking team has one extra player and should use today's three skills — dribble, pass, shoot — to exploit it.</li><li>Play with the attacking team building possession, dribbling to create an opening before deciding whether to pass or shoot.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always be thinking one step ahead — dribble, then what's my best option?'</li><li>Rotate which team has the extra player every few minutes so everyone experiences attacking with and without the advantage.</li><li>Coach highlights clean combination sequences out loud as they happen, especially ones that read the extra player well.</li><li>Keep scoring simple so the focus stays on the quality of the combination rather than just the final tally.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did dribbling, passing, and shooting work together today?</li><li>What happened when you tried to rush a decision instead of reading the situation?</li><li>What's one moment you combined all three skills well?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Dribble creates a genuine opening, not just movement for its own sake</li><li>Reads pass vs shoot appropriately</li><li>Sequence flows without an awkward pause</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce numbers (e.g. 1v0 with a feeder) for students still building confidence combining three skills at once, before scaling up to 2v1 or 4v3.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Reverse Stick Escape":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Footwork &amp; stickwork — using the reverse stick to escape pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Looking after your opponent by defending fairly and safely, and understanding your own stickwork choices when under pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact — sticks-off defending only; stay upright and controlled while practising reverse stick control</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to rotate the stick to play the ball on our reverse side to protect it and find an escape option when pressured.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can rotate my wrists to present the flat face of the stick on my reverse (open) side</li><li>I can find a passing option while controlling the ball on the reverse stick under pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Push-Pass Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that push passes and jogging don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One stick and ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start push-passing gently back and forth while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'go!' — on this call, the passer must immediately jog toward a new spot as if starting a give-and-go, and the receiver passes back to their new position.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and moving with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the give-and-go movement pattern, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pass-and-move calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up, no equipment needed beyond sticks set safely aside, enough space for each pair to stretch without touching a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly with your partner for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Facing your partner, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — hamstring reach, quad stretch with a hand on the partner's shoulder for balance, and a gentle side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions calm and simple, checking that partners are supporting gently rather than pulling on each other.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask pairs how their combination play felt today, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with partners giving each other a quick high-five before sitting down for discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Rotate the stick face to the reverse side smoothly.</td><td>This is a legal way to control the ball on your non-natural side.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball close during the rotation.</td><td>A wide reverse-stick touch is easily intercepted.</td></tr><tr><td>Look for your next option as you complete the escape.</td><td>The escape should lead somewhere, not just delay pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Reverse Stick Rolls</h4><p>Students dribble in a straight line and practise rolling the ball onto the reverse side of the stick and back again, scanning for an imaginary passing option each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student with their own stick and ball, spread out in a straight-line dribbling lane roughly 10m long, cones marking the lane edges.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Rotate your wrists, not your whole body, to find the reverse side</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, walking pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full jogging pace, added direction changes</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate rolling the ball onto the reverse side of the stick and back again while dribbling forward in a straight line.</li><li>Students dribble down the lane, practising rolling the ball to the reverse side and back, scanning as if looking for an imaginary passing option each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'rotate your wrists, not your whole body, to find the reverse side.'</li><li>Watch that the whole body isn't twisting awkwardly — the roll should come mainly from the wrists and stick angle.</li><li>For 'easier', dribble at a slow walking pace; for 'harder', dribble at a full jogging pace and add direction changes.</li><li>Rotate students through continuously, sending the next student in as soon as the lane ahead is clear.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Reverse</h4><p>A shadow defender applies light, sticks-off pressure while the dribbler uses the reverse stick to shield the ball and find a passing lane away from the defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 8m x 8m, pairs (one dribbler, one shadow defender), one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Roll the ball onto your reverse side away from the defender's pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to move around the dribbler</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: the dribbler uses the reverse stick to shield the ball while a shadow defender applies light, sticks-off pressure nearby.</li><li>The dribbler moves around the grid, rolling the ball onto the reverse side away from the defender's pressure whenever they get close.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'roll the ball onto your reverse side away from the defender's pressure.'</li><li>The shadow defender stays close without reaching for the ball, simply testing the dribbler's ability to shield and adjust.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender stays further away; for 'harder', the defender is allowed to move around the dribbler more actively.</li><li>Swap roles every 1-2 minutes so both partners practise dribbling and applying pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Trapped Corner Escape</h4><p>Player receives the ball in a tight corner grid with a defender nearby and must use the reverse stick to find any legal passing option to escape the pressure, racing the clock.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A tight corner grid roughly 4m x 4m marked with cones near a larger open area, pairs (one attacker, one defender), one stick and ball per pair, a stopwatch or the teacher's own count.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay calm — good stickwork buys you time to find an option</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger corner grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tighter corner, quicker defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scenario: the attacker receives the ball in a tight corner with a defender nearby and must escape using the reverse stick.</li><li>Feed the ball to the attacker in the corner while the defender applies light, sticks-off pressure from a fixed starting distance.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay calm — good stickwork buys you time to find an option.'</li><li>The attacker races against the clock to use the reverse stick and find any legal passing option or dribble out of the corner.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger corner grid with a slower defender; for 'harder', tighten the corner and speed up the defender.</li><li>Swap attacker and defender roles after each attempt so everyone practises both sides.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Reverse Stick Under Pressure Hockey</h3><p>4v4 — every player who receives the ball under pressure from a nearby defender is encouraged to use reverse stick control at least once before passing or shooting, reinforcing composed stickwork under real pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, no goals required though small goals can be added.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the encouraged behaviour: any player who receives the ball under pressure from a nearby defender should try using reverse stick control at least once before passing or shooting.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with defenders applying fair, sticks-off pressure on the ball-carrier.</li><li>Coach highlights moments where a player uses the reverse stick well under pressure, calling it out as it happens.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'stay calm — good stickwork buys you time to find an option.'</li><li>Rotate which players are under the most defensive attention so everyone gets chances to practise the skill live.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging — the goal is composed stickwork under pressure, not punishing mistakes when it doesn't come off.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made using the reverse stick under pressure tricky today?</li><li>How did good stickwork buy you time to find an option?</li><li>What's the difference between staying calm with the ball and panicking under pressure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Rotates the wrists smoothly to find the reverse side</li><li>Shields the ball with body positioning while using reverse stick control</li><li>Finds a legal option (pass or dribble) rather than panicking</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole sequence to walking pace and use a visible cone as the defender's marker for students still building the reverse-stick-under-pressure concept.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Defending, Attacking & Positional Play (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Jab Ready":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — stance &amp; staying goal-side</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills fairly to give the attacker a genuine, respectful contest without crowding or contacting them.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact and no swinging at the ball — sticks-off defending, stick's-length distance maintained until a fair tackle attempt</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a strong, balanced defensive stance and how to stay goal-side of an attacker.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold a low, balanced defensive stance with my stick down and active</li><li>I can slide my feet to stay in front of a moving attacker, without crossing my feet</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Tackle Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed, enough room for each pair to slide side to side without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>One partner leads with side-to-side movement while the other mirrors them using the low defensive slide from today's focus.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'swap!' so the leader and mirroring partner switch roles.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze and hold stance!' — both partners drop into a low defensive stance and hold it for a few seconds before continuing.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up defensive footwork before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the timing of swaps and freezes.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch and settle without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a few slow, deep breaths between stretches, encouraging students to notice their breathing settle after the more physical defending/attacking work.</li><li>Keep instructions calm: 'breathe, hold, balance' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall, taking one big breath in and out together before moving into discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay low, balanced, stick down and ready.</td><td>A ready stance lets you react instantly to the attacker's next move.</td></tr><tr><td>Position yourself goal-side of the attacker.</td><td>This cuts off their most direct route toward goal.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet to stay in position rather than lunging with the stick.</td><td>Lunging can foul and is easily beaten by a simple change of direction.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Stance Check</h4><p>Students practise the low defensive stance (knees bent, feet shoulder-width, stick down and active in front of the body) while a partner checks their form, holding the stance for short bursts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out in an open space, no ball needed for this game, enough room for each pair to hold the stance without bumping neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low with your weight on the balls of your feet, stick ready to jab</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter holds, more rest</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer holds, added movement</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the low defensive stance: knees bent, feet shoulder-width apart, stick down and active in front of the body.</li><li>One partner gets into the defensive stance while the other checks their form against the demonstrated model.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay low with your weight on the balls of your feet, stick ready to jab.'</li><li>The checking partner gives simple feedback — knees bent enough, stick down, weight forward — before the pair swap roles.</li><li>For 'easier', hold the stance for shorter bursts with more rest; for 'harder', hold it longer and add small movement while maintaining the stance.</li><li>Rotate through several holds so both partners get repeated checks on their form.</li></ul><hr><h4>Slide &amp; Stay</h4><p>An attacker moves side to side (no ball) along a line while the defender slides their feet to stay directly in front, stick down and active, without crossing their feet or standing up out of the stance.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs along a marked line roughly 6-8m long, no ball needed, cones marking the ends of the line.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Slide, don't cross your feet — a crossed step is a moment you can be beaten</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower attacker movement</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable movement</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: an attacker moves side to side along the line (no ball) while the defender slides to stay directly in front.</li><li>The attacker moves side to side unpredictably along the line while the defender mirrors them using sliding footwork, staying low.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'slide, don't cross your feet — a crossed step is a moment you can be beaten.'</li><li>Watch for defenders standing up out of the stance or crossing their feet, and gently correct as this happens.</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker moves more slowly and predictably; for 'harder', the attacker moves faster and less predictably.</li><li>Swap roles every 30-45 seconds so both partners practise the defensive slide.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Stay in Front</h4><p>Attacker dribbles in a small grid trying to get past; defender's only job is to stay in front using good stance and sliding footwork (no tackling yet).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 8m x 8m, pairs (one attacker, one defender), one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Focus on your feet and stance, not on lunging for the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower attacker</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker attacker</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the defender's only job for this game: stay in front of the attacker using good stance and sliding footwork — no tackling attempts yet.</li><li>The attacker dribbles freely around the grid trying to get past the defender, while the defender focuses purely on positioning.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'focus on your feet and stance, not on lunging for the ball.'</li><li>If the defender is beaten, they simply reset their stance and get back in front rather than chasing or lunging.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with a slower attacker; for 'harder', shrink the grid and speed up the attacker.</li><li>Swap roles every 1-2 minutes so both partners practise attacking and defending positioning.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Stance &amp; Slide Hockey</h3><p>4v4 — coach highlights good defensive stance and sliding footwork in play ('great stance!'), reinforcing that staying goal-side fairly is as valuable as any tackle attempt.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, no goals required though small goals can be added.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for this game: good defensive stance and sliding footwork are valued just as highly as a successful tackle.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with defenders applying fair, sticks-off pressure while staying goal-side of their attacker.</li><li>Coach calls out and praises good stance and sliding footwork specifically, using phrases like 'great stance!' as they happen.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: staying goal-side fairly is as valuable as any tackle attempt.</li><li>Rotate defenders regularly so everyone gets practice applying the stance and footwork from today's games in a live setting.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging so defenders feel confident prioritising positioning over risky lunges.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a low, balanced stance help you do as a defender today?</li><li>Why does sliding (rather than crossing your feet) matter when staying in front?</li><li>What's the difference between defending fairly and defending too aggressively?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds a low, balanced stance with the stick down and active</li><li>Slides feet without crossing them</li><li>Stays a stick's length away, no swinging or reaching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow defenders to start a step further back for students still building confidence with the stance and sliding footwork combination.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Block Tackle":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — timing a block tackle</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Making a positive impact through fair play — a well-timed, fair tackle is good for the game; a reckless one isn't.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Sticks below knee height at all times during tackling practice; teacher demonstrates and checks technique before live 1v1 play</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to time a block tackle so we win the ball cleanly and fairly.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can get my stick down early to block the ball as it comes toward me</li><li>I can make a fair, controlled tackle without swinging at the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Tackle Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed, enough room for each pair to slide side to side without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>One partner leads with side-to-side movement while the other mirrors them using the low defensive slide from today's focus.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'swap!' so the leader and mirroring partner switch roles.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze and hold stance!' — both partners drop into a low defensive stance and hold it for a few seconds before continuing.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up defensive footwork before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the timing of swaps and freezes.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch and settle without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a few slow, deep breaths between stretches, encouraging students to notice their breathing settle after the more physical defending/attacking work.</li><li>Keep instructions calm: 'breathe, hold, balance' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall, taking one big breath in and out together before moving into discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Approach with a low, balanced stance, stick down.</td><td>A ready position lets you react to the exact moment the ball is loose.</td></tr><tr><td>Time the tackle for when the ball is slightly away from close control.</td><td>Tackling when the ball is tucked in tight usually fouls the attacker.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the tackle controlled, not a wild swing.</td><td>A controlled block tackle is safer and more likely to actually win the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Block Tackle Practice</h4><p>Attacker dribbles slowly in a straight line; defender practises getting their stick down early and square to block the ball cleanly, at controlled pace.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread across a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one stick and ball per pair, teacher demonstrating technique to the whole class before pairs begin.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your stick down and still before the ball arrives, don't swing at it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very slow, predictable dribbling</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker adds occasional change of pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the block tackle: getting the stick down early and square to the ball, staying still and controlled rather than swinging.</li><li>The attacker dribbles slowly in a straight line while the defender times their approach to get the stick down and square to block the ball cleanly.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'get your stick down and still before the ball arrives, don't swing at it.'</li><li>Keep the pace controlled throughout — this is about timing and technique, not winning the ball at speed.</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker dribbles very slowly and predictably; for 'harder', the attacker adds occasional changes of pace.</li><li>Swap roles every few attempts so both partners practise the block tackle and being tackled against.</li></ul><hr><h4>Win It Clean 1v1</h4><p>Live 1v1 in a small grid; defender scores a point only for a clean, controlled block tackle (not just any contact).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 8m x 8m, pairs (one attacker, one defender), one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Patience — wait for the right moment rather than diving in early</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker limited to straight-line dribbling</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker free to use full range of moves from earlier lessons</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks (clean wins only)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring rule: the defender only scores a point for a clean, controlled block tackle — not just any contact with the ball.</li><li>The attacker dribbles live inside the grid while the defender looks for the right moment to attempt a block tackle.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'patience — wait for the right moment rather than diving in early.'</li><li>If the defender wins the ball cleanly, that's a point and the round restarts with the attacker back in possession.</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker is limited to straight-line dribbling; for 'harder', the attacker can use the full range of moves practised in earlier lessons.</li><li>Swap roles every few rounds and track clean wins only as the scoring streak.</li></ul><hr><h4>Tackle &amp; Transition</h4><p>As above, but the moment the defender wins the ball cleanly, they become the attacker and must dribble to a target — rewarding a good tackle with an immediate attacking opportunity.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 8m x 8m with a target cone or small zone marked at one end, pairs (one attacker, one defender), one stick and ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be ready to transition mentally the instant you win the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Target closer</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Target further, second defender added after transition</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the twist: the moment the defender wins the ball cleanly, they instantly become the attacker and must dribble to the target.</li><li>Play begins with the original attacker dribbling while the defender looks to win the ball with a clean block tackle.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be ready to transition mentally the instant you win the ball.'</li><li>As soon as the ball is won cleanly, the roles flip immediately and the new attacker dribbles toward the target as fast as they safely can.</li><li>For 'easier', place the target closer; for 'harder', place the target further away and add a second defender after the transition.</li><li>Rotate starting roles each round so everyone practises both defending and the quick transition to attack.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Controlled Approach Hockey</h3><p>4v4; coach specifically praises well-timed, clean block tackles, reinforcing timing and fairness together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, no goals required though small goals can be added.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for this game: well-timed, clean block tackles are specifically praised, reinforcing both timing and fairness.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with defenders looking for the right moment to attempt a fair block tackle.</li><li>Coach calls out and praises clean block tackles as they happen, being specific about the timing that made them successful.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'patience — wait for the right moment rather than diving in early.'</li><li>Rotate defenders regularly so everyone gets live practice applying the block tackle timing from today's games.</li><li>Gently remind players about controlled, sticks-below-knee-height tackling if the pace of the game starts to rush technique.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about the right moment to try a block tackle?</li><li>How did you keep your tackling fair and controlled?</li><li>What happened when you tackled too early or too late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gets the stick down early and square, not swinging</li><li>Attempts are controlled and below knee height</li><li>Shows patience (waits for the right moment) rather than constant attempts</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Keep early practice at walking/slow pace for all students until technique and fairness are consistent, regardless of overall confidence level.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Cut & Clear":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking — creating space (cutting)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding your own timing and decision-making — noticing what changes when your cut clicks into place.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep cutting lanes clear of other groups when running through shared space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to cut into space at the right moment to create a passing or scoring opportunity.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can cut into space when my defender isn't watching or is out of position</li><li>I can time my cut so a teammate can pass to me cleanly</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Tackle Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed, enough room for each pair to slide side to side without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>One partner leads with side-to-side movement while the other mirrors them using the low defensive slide from today's focus.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'swap!' so the leader and mirroring partner switch roles.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze and hold stance!' — both partners drop into a low defensive stance and hold it for a few seconds before continuing.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up defensive footwork before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the timing of swaps and freezes.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch and settle without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a few slow, deep breaths between stretches, encouraging students to notice their breathing settle after the more physical defending/attacking work.</li><li>Keep instructions calm: 'breathe, hold, balance' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall, taking one big breath in and out together before moving into discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Read where the defender's attention is before cutting.</td><td>Cutting when they're distracted creates the most effective separation.</td></tr><tr><td>Cut decisively with a change of pace.</td><td>A sharp cut is much harder to track than a gentle jog.</td></tr><tr><td>Time the cut to match when the ball can actually be played to you.</td><td>Cutting too early or late wastes the opportunity.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call &amp; Cut</h4><p>A passer calls 'now!' as the cue for their partner to cut sharply into space and receive the pass, building the timing connection between passer and cutter.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread across a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one stick and ball per pair, enough space for a cutting run without crossing another pair's path.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Cut hard on the call, not before or after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more obvious timing</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, subtler timing</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: a passer holds the ball while their partner waits, ready to cut into space on the passer's call.</li><li>The passer calls 'now!' as the exact cue for their partner to cut sharply into open space and receive the pass.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'cut hard on the call, not before or after.'</li><li>The passer delivers the ball into the space the cutter is running into, timing the release with the cut.</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace and make the timing more obvious; for 'harder', speed things up with subtler timing cues.</li><li>Swap roles every few reps so both partners practise cutting and passing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Gap Cut</h4><p>Cutter watches their defender and cuts into space the moment the defender is out of position or looking away, without a verbal cue.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small grids roughly 10m x 10m, pairs (one cutter, one defender) plus a passer, sticks and one ball per group, cones marking the grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your cut</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender moves predictably</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender moves unpredictably</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the cutter must watch their defender and cut into space the moment the defender is out of position, without any verbal cue.</li><li>The defender marks the cutter loosely while the passer waits with the ball, ready to find the cutter once they move.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your cut.'</li><li>The cutter reads the defender's positioning and bursts into space the instant an opening appears, and the passer delivers the ball.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender moves predictably; for 'harder', the defender moves unpredictably to make the read harder.</li><li>Rotate roles regularly so everyone practises cutting, defending, and passing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Cut Relay</h4><p>Groups of 3 rotate cutting into space to receive a pass, then immediately becoming the next passer, racing other triangles for clean completions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 forming a rough triangle roughly 8m per side, one stick and ball per group, a neighbouring triangle group for racing, a stopwatch or the teacher's own count.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your cut early — decide your target space before you move</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the pattern: one player cuts into space to receive a pass, then immediately becomes the next passer for the following cut.</li><li>The passer sends the ball to a cutting teammate, who receives it and quickly looks to pass to the next cutter in the triangle.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'prepare your cut early — decide your target space before you move.'</li><li>The group keeps the pattern rotating continuously, racing another triangle group for the most clean completions in a set time.</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace down; for 'harder', speed the pace up and time it as a race.</li><li>Reset and repeat for several rounds, celebrating clean completions rather than just speed.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Timed Cut Hockey</h3><p>4v4 with defence — coach highlights well-timed cuts in play ('great cut!'), reinforcing that arriving in space at the right moment is as valuable as the pass itself.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4 with defenders, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, small goals optional.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for this game: well-timed cuts into space are praised just as highly as the pass itself.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with off-the-ball players encouraged to cut into space when a gap appears.</li><li>Coach calls out and praises well-timed cuts as they happen, using phrases like 'great cut!' to reinforce the behaviour.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your cut.'</li><li>Rotate positions regularly so everyone gets chances to practise cutting into space during live play.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging so players feel confident making runs even if the pass doesn't always arrive.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you time your cut well today?</li><li>How did reading your defender's position help you decide when to cut?</li><li>What happens if you cut too early or too late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Times the cut to arrive as the ball becomes available</li><li>Reads the defender's position before cutting</li><li>Accelerates into space rather than drifting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a verbal cue system (passer calls 'now!') as training wheels for students still building the reading skill, removing the cue as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Dummy Run":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking — decoy runs to create space for a teammate</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Relating to others, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your individual role (decoy or receiver) fits into a shared team outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep dummy runs within a marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make an unmarked run away from the ball to draw a defender away and create space for a teammate.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a committed run that pulls my own defender out of position</li><li>I can recognise when a teammate's dummy run has created space for me</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Tackle Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed, enough room for each pair to slide side to side without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>One partner leads with side-to-side movement while the other mirrors them using the low defensive slide from today's focus.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'swap!' so the leader and mirroring partner switch roles.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze and hold stance!' — both partners drop into a low defensive stance and hold it for a few seconds before continuing.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up defensive footwork before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the timing of swaps and freezes.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch and settle without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a few slow, deep breaths between stretches, encouraging students to notice their breathing settle after the more physical defending/attacking work.</li><li>Keep instructions calm: 'breathe, hold, balance' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall, taking one big breath in and out together before moving into discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to your decoy run, even without receiving the ball.</td><td>A half-hearted decoy doesn't draw defenders away convincingly.</td></tr><tr><td>Run into space that genuinely occupies a defender.</td><td>The decoy only works if it actually pulls a defender out of position.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with the ball carrier about the decoy.</td><td>Helps the timing and understanding of the whole attacking move.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Committed Decoy</h4><p>One student makes a full-speed run into a space away from the ball (a 'dummy run'), while a partner notices the space that opens up and moves into it instead.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread across a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one stick and ball per pair, enough space for a full-speed run without colliding with neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Commit fully to the decoy run — a half-hearted run won't pull a defender</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, walked-through first</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tighter space, full speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the roles: one player makes a full-speed run into space away from the ball (a decoy run), while the partner notices the space that opens up.</li><li>The decoy player sprints hard into an open area away from the ball, drawing attention even though they won't receive a pass.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'commit fully to the decoy run — a half-hearted run won't pull a defender.'</li><li>The partner watches where the space opens up because of the decoy and moves into that space themselves.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space and walk through the pattern first; for 'harder', tighten the space and run at full speed.</li><li>Swap roles every few reps so both partners practise making the decoy run and reading the space it creates.</li></ul><hr><h4>Decoy &amp; Receive Combo</h4><p>In trios, one player makes a dummy run shadowed by a light-touch defender; a teammate reads the space created and moves in to receive a pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread across a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one stick and ball per group, a light-touch defender shadowing the decoy runner.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Receiver, wait for the decoy to commit before you move</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts quickly</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three roles: a decoy runner, a receiving teammate, and a light-touch shadow defender who follows the decoy without intercepting.</li><li>The decoy runner makes a committed run into space, shadowed loosely by the defender.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'receiver, wait for the decoy to commit before you move.'</li><li>Once the decoy has drawn the defender's attention, the receiving teammate reads the space created and moves in to receive a pass from the ball-holder.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender reacts slowly to the decoy; for 'harder', the defender reacts quickly, making the timing tighter.</li><li>Rotate all three roles regularly so everyone practises decoying, receiving, and defending.</li></ul><hr><h4>Role Rotation Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where players rotate roles (decoy, receiver, defender) every couple of minutes, so everyone practises each part of the dummy-run combination.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area roughly 15m x 12m, small groups with defined roles (decoy, receiver, defender) rotating every couple of minutes, sticks and one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with your teammate about who's making the run</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer periods per role</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter periods, faster rotation</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the small-sided game will run with players rotating through decoy, receiver, and defender roles every couple of minutes.</li><li>Play the small-sided game with players consciously practising their current role — decoys committing to runs, receivers reading space, defenders tracking movement.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate with your teammate about who's making the run.'</li><li>Call time every couple of minutes and rotate roles so everyone experiences each part of the dummy-run combination.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer periods per role with more time to think; for 'harder', shorten the periods for faster rotation and decision-making.</li><li>Keep encouraging communication between decoy and receiver so the combination feels planned rather than accidental.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Dummy Run Hockey</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — teams are encouraged to use at least one committed dummy run per possession to help a teammate get free, with the coach highlighting successful, well-timed decoy runs.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4 or 5v5, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, small goals optional.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the encouraged behaviour: teams should try to use at least one committed dummy run per possession to help a teammate get free.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with off-the-ball players looking for chances to make a decoy run that opens space for a teammate.</li><li>Coach highlights successful, well-timed decoy runs out loud as they happen, even when the decoy player themselves doesn't receive the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'commit fully to the decoy run — a half-hearted run won't pull a defender.'</li><li>Rotate positions regularly so everyone gets chances to practise making and reading decoy runs in live play.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging so players feel confident making unselfish runs that benefit a teammate rather than themselves.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a dummy run effective (or not) today?</li><li>How did noticing a teammate's run help you find space?</li><li>Why do you think a dummy run needs to be committed and convincing to work?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Commits fully to the decoy run rather than jogging half-heartedly</li><li>Recognises and moves into space created by a teammate's run</li><li>Communicates with the teammate about the run</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Practise the decoy runner's role first without any defender at all, so students build confidence with the committed running before adding pressure.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Second Ball":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Loose-ball basics (reacting &amp; positioning near the circle)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Contesting a loose ball fairly and safely — using your body position and stickwork, not pushing or swinging, to compete for the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, small goals/cone goals, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No pushing, swinging, or dangerous play when contesting a loose ball — body/stick contact must be controlled and balanced, not aggressive; sticks below knee height at all times</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to react quickly and position ourselves to win the 'second ball' after a shot or save.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move toward the goal as a shot is taken, ready to react to a rebound</li><li>I can position myself between my opponent and the ball before going for a loose ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Tackle Slide</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across a grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed, enough room for each pair to slide side to side without bumping a neighbouring pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>One partner leads with side-to-side movement while the other mirrors them using the low defensive slide from today's focus.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call 'swap!' so the leader and mirroring partner switch roles.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze and hold stance!' — both partners drop into a low defensive stance and hold it for a few seconds before continuing.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and warming up defensive footwork before today's lesson, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the timing of swaps and freezes.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch and settle without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a few slow, deep breaths between stretches, encouraging students to notice their breathing settle after the more physical defending/attacking work.</li><li>Keep instructions calm: 'breathe, hold, balance' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall, taking one big breath in and out together before moving into discussion.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>React the instant the ball becomes loose.</td><td>The first player to react usually wins the ball.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your body and stick into a strong, ready position near the circle.</td><td>Good positioning near goal creates more chances from loose-ball situations.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay alert and anticipate rather than watching passively.</td><td>Anticipation beats reaction when the ball could go several ways.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Anticipate &amp; React</h4><p>Facing a partner, students practise reacting quickly to a rolled ball released without warning, positioning their body and stick to get there first in a controlled way.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4-5m apart, one stick and ball per pair, enough space to move in any direction to collect the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball, react early, and get there under control — don't lunge</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more predictable releases</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, less predictable releases</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: one partner will roll the ball without warning, and the other must react quickly and get there first in a controlled way.</li><li>The rolling partner releases the ball toward their partner at an unpredictable moment, without a verbal countdown.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch the ball, react early, and get there under control — don't lunge.'</li><li>The receiving partner reacts immediately, moving their body and stick to collect the ball cleanly and under control.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more predictable releases; for 'harder', use quicker, less predictable releases.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 releases so both partners practise reacting and releasing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rebound &amp; Collect</h4><p>A partner pushes the ball off a wall or rebound board; the student must react and move to collect the loose ball ahead of an imaginary or light-touch opponent.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs near a wall or rebound board, one stick and ball per pair, enough space in front of the wall to move and collect the rebound.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>React the instant the ball rebounds — don't wait to see where it goes first</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower rebounds, more space</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker rebounds, tighter space</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup: one partner pushes the ball off the wall or rebound board while the other reacts to collect the loose rebound.</li><li>The passing partner pushes the ball firmly at the wall so it rebounds back into open space at an unpredictable angle.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'react the instant the ball rebounds — don't wait to see where it goes first.'</li><li>The collecting partner moves quickly to get to the loose ball ahead of an imaginary or light-touch opponent standing nearby.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower rebounds with more space; for 'harder', use quicker rebounds in a tighter space.</li><li>Swap roles every few attempts so both partners practise pushing and reacting.</li></ul><hr><h4>Loose Ball Rumble</h4><p>Small groups compete to win the most loose balls off a series of rolls or missed shots near the goal, tracked as a friendly team tally.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups near a goal or target, a series of rolls or missed shots set up by the teacher or a rotating feeder, sticks for every player, a simple tally to track wins.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low and ready — don't stand up straight and lose your reaction time</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players competing for each loose ball</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more contested balls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the group competes to win the most loose balls off a series of rolls or missed shots near the goal.</li><li>A feeder rolls or shoots a ball toward the goal area, and group members react to collect the loose ball ahead of the others.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay low and ready — don't stand up straight and lose your reaction time.'</li><li>Track a friendly team tally of who collects the most loose balls across the series of rolls.</li><li>For 'easier', reduce the number of players competing for each loose ball; for 'harder', increase the number of players contesting each ball.</li><li>Keep the physicality controlled and fair throughout — this is about reaction and positioning, not shoving.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Second Ball Hockey</h3><p>4v4 with small goals — every missed shot is a live loose-ball opportunity; players are coached explicitly to react and position first, reinforcing fair, safe positioning over raw scrambling.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with small goals, groups of 4v4, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: every missed shot creates a live loose-ball opportunity, and players are specifically coached to react and position first.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey with small goals, encouraging shots and expecting rebounds to be contested fairly afterward.</li><li>Coach calls out reminders to react and position early whenever a shot is missed, reinforcing the lesson's core message.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'stay low and ready — don't stand up straight and lose your reaction time.'</li><li>Reinforce fair, safe positioning over raw scrambling — no pushing, swinging, or dangerous play when contesting the ball.</li><li>Rotate defenders and attackers regularly so everyone practises reacting to loose balls in a live setting.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you react quickly to a loose ball today?</li><li>Why is body positioning more useful than just reaching for the ball?</li><li>What made a loose-ball contest feel fair and safe today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reacts quickly and moves early toward a loose ball</li><li>Positions between the opponent and the ball before contesting it</li><li>Stays low and controlled rather than lunging</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Practise the reaction/positioning habit without any contest first, so students build the habit before adding the scramble for a loose ball.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Fast Break":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fast break basics (outnumbering the defence)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Being patient with a team while learning a multi-person pattern — timing takes a few tries to click for everyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones marking field thirds, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep the field clear of congestion during fast-paced running sections — space players out to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to recognise and use a fast break — moving the ball quickly up the field when we outnumber the defence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can sprint into space immediately after my team gains the ball</li><li>I can recognise when my team has more attackers than defenders and use the advantage</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class organised into their tournament teams (from bibs), open space roughly 20m x 20m, sticks and balls available but not required for every part of the warm-up.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Teams jog together in their team shape around the space, staying loosely organised as a group rather than scattering individually.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call out a simple shape instruction — 'spread wide!' or 'bunch in!' — and teams adjust their jogging shape accordingly.</li><li>Occasionally call 'team huddle!' — teams quickly gather in a tight circle for a few seconds before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and building team cohesion before today's lesson or tournament game, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up shape calls and huddles throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class gathered in one large circle, no equipment needed, enough space for everyone to stretch comfortably within the circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly to form the circle, bringing heart rate down gradually as everyone gathers.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to share a highlight from the unit or the tournament while the group stretches, keeping the tone warm and reflective.</li><li>Finish the stretching with everyone reaching up together and taking one big breath in and out.</li><li>Close with a group cheer or round of applause to celebrate the unit, before moving into the final Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Push the ball forward quickly after gaining possession.</td><td>Exploits the defence before it's organised.</td></tr><tr><td>Spread into passing lanes rather than bunching together.</td><td>Gives the ball carrier real options and stretches the defence.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide early — pass or shoot — based on the numbers.</td><td>Hesitation lets the defence recover into position.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fast Break Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple fast break pattern slowly: after winning the ball, players sprint into lanes up the field while one dribbles/passes ahead, before adding any defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A field marked into thirds using cones, groups of 4-5 players walking through the pattern together, sticks and one ball per group, bibs optional.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone has a lane to sprint into — know yours before the ball moves</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk pace only</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light jog</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the pattern: after winning the ball, players sprint into lanes up the field while one player dribbles or passes the ball forward.</li><li>Walk the group through the pattern slowly first, with each player moving into their own lane rather than bunching together.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone has a lane to sprint into — know yours before the ball moves.'</li><li>One player dribbles or passes the ball forward through the thirds while the others move into supporting lanes at walking pace.</li><li>For 'easier', keep it to walk pace only; for 'harder', add a light jog once the pattern is understood.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times with players rotating through different lanes.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v2 Numbers Up</h4><p>Three attackers push the ball up the field against only two defenders, practising quick decisions on when to pass and who has the advantage.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A marked area covering roughly two-thirds of the field, three attackers against two defenders, sticks and one ball per group, small goal or cone target at the end.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Spread out into your lanes — a bunched-up fast break loses its advantage</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders start further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders start closer, more realistic recovery speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: three attackers push the ball up the field against only two defenders and must use the extra player wisely.</li><li>The attacking trio moves the ball forward, spreading into their lanes and looking to identify which defender is covering which attacker.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'spread out into your lanes — a bunched-up fast break loses its advantage.'</li><li>Attackers make quick decisions on when to pass to the free player and finish with a shot when the opportunity appears.</li><li>For 'easier', defenders start further back; for 'harder', defenders start closer with more realistic recovery speed.</li><li>Rotate which three players attack and which two defend every few reps.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fast Break Race</h4><p>Groups practise executing a clean fast break sequence and finish (pass or shot) as fast and cleanly as possible, racing another group for the fastest clean sequence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two parallel lanes covering roughly two-thirds of the field, one group per lane, sticks and one ball per group, a stopwatch or the teacher's own timer, a small goal or cone target at the end of each lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate clearly about who's finishing the fast break</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: each group executes a clean fast break sequence and finishes with a pass or shot as fast and cleanly as possible.</li><li>Both groups start together on a signal, sprinting into their lanes and moving the ball forward using the fast break pattern.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate clearly about who's finishing the fast break.'</li><li>The group that completes a clean sequence and finish first, without the pattern breaking down, wins that race.</li><li>For 'easier', run the race at a slower pace; for 'harder', run it at full pace and time it precisely.</li><li>Reset and repeat several times, rotating which players finish the sequence each round.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fast Break Hockey</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — whenever a team wins possession, they're encouraged to push the ball quickly up the field and recognise any numbers advantage before the defence recovers.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full-width small-sided area, groups of 4v4 or 5v5, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, small goals or cone goals at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the encouraged behaviour: whenever a team wins possession, they should look to push the ball quickly up the field before the defence recovers.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with teams encouraged to recognise and use any numbers advantage created by a turnover.</li><li>Coach calls out reminders to push forward quickly whenever a team wins the ball, reinforcing the fast break mindset.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'spread out into your lanes — a bunched-up fast break loses its advantage.'</li><li>Rotate positions regularly so everyone experiences both winning the ball and defending against a fast break.</li><li>Keep the pace lively and encourage communication about lanes and finishing responsibility during transitions.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your fast break successful (or not) today?</li><li>How did spreading into lanes help keep the advantage alive?</li><li>What's the purpose of pushing the ball quickly rather than walking it up?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Sprints into a lane immediately after winning the ball</li><li>Recognises and uses a numbers advantage</li><li>Communicates clearly during the fast break</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify to a 2v1 version for teams still building confidence before adding a third attacker and defender.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Keep Away":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided possession games (keep-away)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual movement and passing choices affect your whole team's ability to keep the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games within a clearly marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep possession of the ball as a team using movement, passing, and communication.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move into space to give my teammate a passing option</li><li>I can communicate clearly to help my team keep the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class organised into their tournament teams (from bibs), open space roughly 20m x 20m, sticks and balls available but not required for every part of the warm-up.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Teams jog together in their team shape around the space, staying loosely organised as a group rather than scattering individually.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call out a simple shape instruction — 'spread wide!' or 'bunch in!' — and teams adjust their jogging shape accordingly.</li><li>Occasionally call 'team huddle!' — teams quickly gather in a tight circle for a few seconds before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and building team cohesion before today's lesson or tournament game, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up shape calls and huddles throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class gathered in one large circle, no equipment needed, enough space for everyone to stretch comfortably within the circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly to form the circle, bringing heart rate down gradually as everyone gathers.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to share a highlight from the unit or the tournament while the group stretches, keeping the tone warm and reflective.</li><li>Finish the stretching with everyone reaching up together and taking one big breath in and out.</li><li>Close with a group cheer or round of applause to celebrate the unit, before moving into the final Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball moving with simple, quick passes.</td><td>Extra touches under pressure risk losing possession.</td></tr><tr><td>Spread out to keep passing lanes open.</td><td>Bunched players let one defender pressure multiple options at once.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate before receiving to know the pressure situation.</td><td>Helps you decide your first touch before the ball arrives.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Triangle Support</h4><p>Groups of 3 keep the ball moving between them, with the two players off the ball always moving to form good passing angles (triangle shapes) for the ball carrier.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 spread across a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one stick and ball per group, cones marking the grid boundary.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always offer at least two passing options for your teammate with the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, one light defender added</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the shape: the two players off the ball should always move to form good passing angles (a triangle shape) around the ball carrier.</li><li>The group keeps the ball moving between the three players, with the two off-ball players constantly adjusting position to offer angles.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always offer at least two passing options for your teammate with the ball.'</li><li>Watch that players aren't standing in a flat line, which removes passing angles — encourage spreading into a triangle shape.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space with a slower pace; for 'harder', shrink the space and add one light defender.</li><li>Rotate which player starts with the ball each round so everyone practises supporting and receiving.</li></ul><hr><h4>4v2 Keep Away</h4><p>Four attackers keep the ball away from two defenders in a marked grid, focusing on movement and quick decisions to maintain possession.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A marked grid roughly 12m x 12m, four attackers against two defenders, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish attackers from defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the ball quickly before the defence can close the gap</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: four attackers try to keep the ball away from two defenders using movement and quick decisions.</li><li>Attackers spread around the grid, using off-ball movement to stay open and passing quickly before the defence can close the gap.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move the ball quickly before the defence can close the gap.'</li><li>Defenders apply fair, sticks-off pressure, trying to intercept or force the attackers into a mistake.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with slower defenders; for 'harder', shrink the grid and speed up the defenders.</li><li>Rotate defenders every 2-3 minutes so everyone experiences both attacking and defending in the numbers-down role.</li></ul><hr><h4>Possession Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small-sided keep-away game where the team tracks their longest streak of consecutive passes kept without losing the ball, trying to beat their own best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A marked grid roughly 12m x 12m, small-sided groups, sticks and one ball per group, a simple tally system to track the team's best streak of consecutive passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Celebrate a good streak, then reset your focus for the next one</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, fewer defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, more defenders</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the team tracks their longest streak of consecutive passes kept without losing the ball, trying to beat their own best.</li><li>Play a small-sided keep-away game where the team counts passes out loud as they string them together.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'celebrate a good streak, then reset your focus for the next one.'</li><li>If the ball is lost, the streak resets to zero and the count starts again from the next successful pass.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space with fewer defenders; for 'harder', shrink the space and add more defenders.</li><li>Track and announce the team's best streak of the session as a fun way to close the game.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Keep Away Hockey</h3><p>4v4, small area — teams score a point for every 6 consecutive passes kept as a team, rewarding movement, communication, and decision-making over individual dribbling.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small areas roughly 10m x 10m marked with cones, groups of 4v4, one ball per group, sticks for every player, no goals needed.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: a team earns a point for every 6 consecutive passes kept as a team without the other team touching the ball.</li><li>Play possession-based small-sided hockey where the possessing team calls their pass count out loud as they string passes together.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: movement, communication, and quick decisions matter more here than individual dribbling.</li><li>The moment the ball is lost or intercepted, possession switches to the other team and their count starts fresh.</li><li>Coach highlights good off-ball movement and communication out loud as it happens, reinforcing the lesson's focus.</li><li>Rotate which teams play against each other every few minutes if running multiple small groups at once.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team keep possession under pressure today?</li><li>How did moving without the ball help your teammates?</li><li>What did good communication sound like in your team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves to create a passing option, not just watching the ball</li><li>Communicates clearly and early</li><li>Makes quick, sensible decisions under mild pressure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Increase the space or reduce defender numbers for teams still building confidence keeping the ball under any pressure at all.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Team Flow":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team offence patterns</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Thinking about how your decisions within the pattern affect whether the whole team's play succeeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep attacking patterns within a marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning simple attacking patterns that help our team move the ball toward the goal.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run a simple attacking pattern with my team</li><li>I can adjust the pattern based on what the defence is doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class organised into their tournament teams (from bibs), open space roughly 20m x 20m, sticks and balls available but not required for every part of the warm-up.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Teams jog together in their team shape around the space, staying loosely organised as a group rather than scattering individually.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call out a simple shape instruction — 'spread wide!' or 'bunch in!' — and teams adjust their jogging shape accordingly.</li><li>Occasionally call 'team huddle!' — teams quickly gather in a tight circle for a few seconds before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and building team cohesion before today's lesson or tournament game, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up shape calls and huddles throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class gathered in one large circle, no equipment needed, enough space for everyone to stretch comfortably within the circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly to form the circle, bringing heart rate down gradually as everyone gathers.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to share a highlight from the unit or the tournament while the group stretches, keeping the tone warm and reflective.</li><li>Finish the stretching with everyone reaching up together and taking one big breath in and out.</li><li>Close with a group cheer or round of applause to celebrate the unit, before moving into the final Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Learn and run your team's planned movement pattern.</td><td>Patterns create better spacing than random, individual attacking.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust when the defence covers your first option.</td><td>Real defence requires adapting, not abandoning the plan entirely.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate throughout the pattern.</td><td>A silent pattern breaks down under pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pattern Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple attacking pattern (e.g. pass-cut-dummy run toward the goal) slowly with cone markers before adding pace or defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A marked area roughly 15m x 12m with cone markers showing player positions, small groups walking through the pattern together, sticks and one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your role in the pattern before you start moving</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players, simpler pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more complex pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain a simple attacking pattern, such as pass-cut-dummy run toward the goal, using the cone markers to show each player's starting position.</li><li>Walk the group through the pattern slowly, with each player moving to their marked position and completing their part of the sequence.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your role in the pattern before you start moving.'</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times at walking pace before any pace or defence is added.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players and a simpler pattern; for 'harder', add more players and a more complex pattern.</li><li>Rotate players through different roles in the pattern so everyone understands each part.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pattern vs Shadow Defence</h4><p>Team runs their attacking pattern against shadow defenders (not intercepting) to build confidence with defensive presence nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same marked area as Pattern Walkthrough, small groups running the pattern against shadow defenders who don't intercept, sticks and one ball per group, bibs for defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders closer, more realistic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the team will now run their attacking pattern with shadow defenders present, but the defenders won't intercept the ball.</li><li>The attacking group runs their pattern at a controlled pace while shadow defenders shadow their opposite number without touching the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears.'</li><li>Watch that players complete the full pattern despite the defensive presence, rather than panicking and abandoning the plan.</li><li>For 'easier', defenders stay further back; for 'harder', defenders stay closer for a more realistic look.</li><li>Rotate attackers and defenders every few reps so everyone experiences both roles.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read &amp; Adjust</h4><p>Team runs their pattern, but must adjust on the fly if a defender blocks the planned option, practising a backup plan.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same marked area, small groups running the pattern against a defender who can actively block one option, sticks and one ball per group, bibs for defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Unpredictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the twist: the team runs their pattern, but a defender is now allowed to block the planned option, forcing a backup plan.</li><li>The attacking group begins their pattern as planned, while the defender actively steps in front of the first passing or cutting option.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered.'</li><li>When the first option is blocked, the ball-carrier and teammates adjust on the fly to find the backup option.</li><li>For 'easier', the defensive block is predictable; for 'harder', the block is unpredictable, forcing quicker reads.</li><li>Rotate which option gets blocked each round so different backup plans get practised.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Offence Hockey</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with full defence — attacking teams are encouraged to run at least one planned pattern per possession, adjusting when the defence covers their first option.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area with full defence, groups of 4v4 or 5v5, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, small goals optional.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the encouraged behaviour: attacking teams should run at least one planned pattern per possession, adjusting when the defence covers their first option.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey with full defensive pressure, encouraging attackers to use today's patterns rather than purely improvised play.</li><li>Coach highlights moments where a team successfully runs a pattern or adjusts well to a blocked option, calling it out as it happens.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered.'</li><li>Rotate positions regularly so everyone gets chances to lead and support the attacking pattern.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging so teams feel confident trying planned patterns even if the first attempt doesn't work perfectly.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your attacking pattern successful (or not) against real defence today?</li><li>How did you adjust when your first option was covered?</li><li>What's the value of having a plan, even if you sometimes need to change it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Runs a recognisable attacking pattern with teammates</li><li>Adjusts the pattern when the defence covers the first option</li><li>Communicates with teammates during the pattern</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Walk the pattern through cone markers first for teams who need a visual/spatial reference before adding a live defender.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Team Wall":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team defence patterns</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Bringing everything together fairly — defending hard, defending safely, and defending for the team, not just for yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep defensive movement controlled — no sprinting into others' space; communication should be clear, not aggressive shouting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend as a connected team, not just as individuals marking their own player.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can communicate with my defensive teammates about where the ball and attackers are</li><li>I can adjust my defensive position based on what my teammates are doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class organised into their tournament teams (from bibs), open space roughly 20m x 20m, sticks and balls available but not required for every part of the warm-up.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Teams jog together in their team shape around the space, staying loosely organised as a group rather than scattering individually.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call out a simple shape instruction — 'spread wide!' or 'bunch in!' — and teams adjust their jogging shape accordingly.</li><li>Occasionally call 'team huddle!' — teams quickly gather in a tight circle for a few seconds before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and building team cohesion before today's lesson or tournament game, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up shape calls and huddles throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class gathered in one large circle, no equipment needed, enough space for everyone to stretch comfortably within the circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly to form the circle, bringing heart rate down gradually as everyone gathers.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to share a highlight from the unit or the tournament while the group stretches, keeping the tone warm and reflective.</li><li>Finish the stretching with everyone reaching up together and taking one big breath in and out.</li><li>Close with a group cheer or round of applause to celebrate the unit, before moving into the final Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly about who's covering whom.</td><td>Team defence relies on clear, updated roles.</td></tr><tr><td>Cover for a teammate who's beaten, then recover to your own mark.</td><td>Helping without abandoning your job keeps the defence solid.</td></tr><tr><td>Move as a connected unit.</td><td>Gaps appear where defenders stop working together.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call the Danger</h4><p>Defensive team practises calling out loudly when an attacker is dangerous (open, about to receive, or about to shoot) so teammates can help cover.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area roughly 15m x 12m, groups of 4-5 defenders working together against attackers moving the ball, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the skill: defenders practise calling out loudly when an attacker is dangerous — open, about to receive, or about to shoot.</li><li>As attackers move the ball around, defenders watch for danger and call it out clearly and specifically to their teammates.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing.'</li><li>Teammates respond to the call by adjusting their positioning to help cover the danger that's been identified.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more obvious danger; for 'harder', make the danger faster and less obvious.</li><li>Rotate attackers and defenders regularly so everyone practises both creating and calling out danger.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cover &amp; Recover</h4><p>One defender practises leaving their direct mark briefly to cover a bigger danger, while a teammate calls out and helps recover the original mark.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of defenders working in pairs within a larger defensive unit, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time to communicate</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, quick decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the pattern: one defender briefly leaves their direct mark to cover a bigger danger elsewhere, while a teammate calls out and helps recover the original mark.</li><li>As play develops, one defender steps across to cover the bigger danger while calling out clearly to a nearby teammate.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open.'</li><li>The covering teammate recovers to pick up the original mark as quickly as possible once the swap is called.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more time to communicate; for 'harder', speed up the pace so decisions happen quickly.</li><li>Rotate roles regularly so everyone practises both covering and recovering.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Defence Grid</h4><p>Small-sided game where the defending team's only job is to communicate and cover for each other, with the coach highlighting good team defensive moments.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4 or similar, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, the defending team's sole focus being communication and coverage.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Defence is a team job, not just your own player</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer attackers, easier to cover</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full attacking numbers</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the defending team's only job in this game is to communicate and cover for each other — not to win the ball at all costs.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where attackers move the ball freely while the defending team focuses purely on positioning and calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'defence is a team job, not just your own player.'</li><li>Coach highlights good team defensive moments out loud as they happen, especially clear calls and successful covers.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer attackers, making it easier to cover; for 'harder', use full attacking numbers.</li><li>Rotate which team defends every few minutes so everyone practises the communication-focused defending.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Defence Hockey</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — defending team is coached explicitly on communication and covering for each other, while attackers try to exploit any gaps in the defensive structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided area, groups of 4v4 or 5v5, sticks and one ball per group, bibs to distinguish teams, small goals optional.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus for this game: the defending team is coached explicitly on communication and covering for each other.</li><li>Play small-sided hockey as normal, with the defending team encouraged to call out danger and cover for teammates.</li><li>Attackers try to exploit any gaps in the defensive structure, giving the defending team real decisions to communicate through.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open.'</li><li>Coach highlights clear defensive communication out loud as it happens, reinforcing the lesson's focus.</li><li>Rotate attacking and defending teams regularly so everyone gets practice with the team defence principles.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your team's defence effective (or not) today?</li><li>Why is communication important for a defensive team, not just individual marking?</li><li>How did covering for a teammate help your team defend better?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Communicates clearly with defensive teammates</li><li>Covers for a teammate when needed</li><li>Recovers to their original mark after helping</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole game to walking pace for students still learning the team defence concept before adding jogging speed.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Hockey Cup":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C &amp; D · All key competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Celebrating the whole team's effort and growth across the unit — thinking about how everyone's contribution, not just the scoreline, made the tournament work.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Sticks, balls, bibs (multiple colours for teams), cones, small goals/cone goals, a simple ladder/scoreboard</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Manage fatigue with rotations/subs; reinforce the flat-side, low-stick, and no-contact rules before the tournament begins</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills in a fun, competitive mini-tournament format.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full game applying skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>I can be a good teammate and good sport across multiple games</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class organised into their tournament teams (from bibs), open space roughly 20m x 20m, sticks and balls available but not required for every part of the warm-up.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Teams jog together in their team shape around the space, staying loosely organised as a group rather than scattering individually.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call out a simple shape instruction — 'spread wide!' or 'bunch in!' — and teams adjust their jogging shape accordingly.</li><li>Occasionally call 'team huddle!' — teams quickly gather in a tight circle for a few seconds before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and building team cohesion before today's lesson or tournament game, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up shape calls and huddles throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class gathered in one large circle, no equipment needed, enough space for everyone to stretch comfortably within the circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly to form the circle, bringing heart rate down gradually as everyone gathers.</li><li>Lead 3-4 simple static stretches as a whole group — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to share a highlight from the unit or the tournament while the group stretches, keeping the tone warm and reflective.</li><li>Finish the stretching with everyone reaching up together and taking one big breath in and out.</li><li>Close with a group cheer or round of applause to celebrate the unit, before moving into the final Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply your best technique from across the unit.</td><td>The tournament brings everything together under real pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly with teammates.</td><td>Communication matters even more when the stakes go up.</td></tr><tr><td>Play fairly and encourage teammates, win or lose.</td><td>Sportsmanship is as much a part of the unit as physical skill.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Warm-In Circuit</h4><p>A short rotating circuit revisiting the unit's key skills (dribble, pass &amp; receive, shoot, defend) as a fun warm-in before the tournament starts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Four stations set up around the space — dribble, pass &amp; receive, shoot, defend — each with cones and sticks/balls as needed, small groups rotating through together.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every station</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer stations, fewer skills</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter stations, all skills</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the circuit: a short rotating set of stations revisiting the unit's key skills as a fun warm-in before the tournament begins.</li><li>Groups start at a station and spend a set amount of time practising that skill before rotating to the next.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring your best technique from across the unit into every station.'</li><li>Keep the pace upbeat and encouraging — this is a warm-in, not a formal assessment of technique.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer stations covering fewer skills; for 'harder', use shorter stations covering all four skills.</li><li>Rotate through all stations before moving into the tournament proper.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Huddle</h4><p>Each team spends 2 minutes with their 'captain' (rotating role) planning one simple tactic to try in their first game — a light strategy touchpoint before playing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each team gathered in their own small huddle area, no equipment needed beyond team bibs, a rotating 'captain' role identified for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests the tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team chooses their own tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the huddle: each team spends 2 minutes with their rotating captain planning one simple tactic to try in their first game.</li><li>The captain leads a short discussion, gathering input but keeping the final plan simple and clear.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.'</li><li>Teams agree on their one tactic before heading into their first tournament game.</li><li>For 'easier', the coach suggests the tactic to get teams started; for 'harder', the team chooses their own tactic independently.</li><li>Rotate the captain role before each new game so everyone gets a turn leading the huddle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fair Play Check-In</h4><p>Brief between-games moment where teams acknowledge one good play from their opponent, reinforcing sportsmanship as part of the tournament structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A brief gathering moment between tournament games, no equipment needed, teams meeting briefly as a whole group or in their own team huddle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific — name what your opponent did well</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the purpose: teams take a moment between games to acknowledge one good play from their opponent.</li><li>After each game, gather teams briefly and invite a couple of players to name something their opponent did well.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be specific — name what your opponent did well.'</li><li>Keep this check-in short and genuine, reinforcing sportsmanship as a normal part of the tournament rhythm.</li><li>This activity doesn't have easier/harder variants — it's the same simple format between every game.</li><li>Move straight into the next game once the check-in is complete.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Hockey Cup — Round Robin</h3><p>Full simplified hockey rules, small-sided round-robin mini-tournament (4v4 or 5v5) across several short games; ladder/results tracked lightly and playfully, with every team guaranteed multiple games regardless of results.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full playing space divided as needed for several small-sided games, bibs in multiple colours to distinguish teams, sticks and balls for every group, a simple ladder or scoreboard visible to all.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the tournament format: full simplified hockey rules, small-sided round-robin games (4v4 or 5v5) played across several short matches.</li><li>Run each game for a set short time (e.g. 5-6 minutes) before rotating teams to their next round-robin fixture.</li><li>Track results lightly and playfully on the ladder/scoreboard, keeping the emphasis on participation and fun rather than high stakes.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout the tournament: apply the unit's best technique — dribbling, passing, shooting, defending — under real game pressure.</li><li>Reinforce the flat-side, low-stick, and no-contact rules before each game begins, and manage fatigue with rotations or substitutions.</li><li>Guarantee every team multiple games regardless of results, closing the tournament with a celebration of effort and sportsmanship across the whole unit.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from across the whole unit you felt proudest of today?</li><li>How did your team work together across multiple games?</li><li>What would you want to work on if we played another hockey unit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a recognisable range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Shows good sportsmanship across wins and losses</li><li>Encourages and includes teammates throughout the tournament</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure team sizes/formats can flex on the day for uneven numbers; emphasise participation and effort in any informal recognition, not just results.</p>"}}}}},"Netball":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Ball & Footwork Skills (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Landing Zone":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Footwork fundamentals (landing on one/two feet)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while learning a new rule that feels unusual at first, and encouraging teammates whose landings wobble on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread out to land safely — one body-width minimum gap when moving freely</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to land correctly on one or two feet when catching the ball, ready to apply the footwork rule.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can land on one foot and identify it as my 'landing foot'</li><li>I can land on two feet at the same time when that's how I choose to land</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Footwork Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no ball or equipment needed — this warm-up is about feet and landings, not hands.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' at random intervals — students immediately land and freeze on a single foot, then resume jogging on the next 'go.'</li><li>Call 'two feet!' the same way — students land and freeze on both feet at once.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and getting the landing habit into their bodies before today's lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Land firmly on one foot, or both feet simultaneously, and establish it as your landing foot.</td><td>Netball's footwork rule requires a clear, controlled landing foot before you move again.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your knees slightly bent on landing.</td><td>Absorbs the landing safely and keeps you balanced to move next.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide immediately what you'll do next — pivot, pass, or shoot.</td><td>Hesitating after landing wastes time and can lead to a footwork infringement.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Footwork Freeze</h4><p>Free movement in a grid; on 'one foot!' students land and freeze on a single foot, on 'two feet!' they land and freeze on both feet at once.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a grid roughly 15m x 15m per group of 10-12 students using 4 cones at the corners. No ball is needed for this game — everyone moves freely inside the shared grid at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bend your landing knee to absorb the landing softly</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, bigger grid</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster calls, mixed sequence</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate both landings first: freezing on one foot with a bent knee, and freezing on two feet at shoulder width with soft knees.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs or moves freely around the grid in their own space.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' — everyone must immediately land and freeze on a single foot and hold it until the next call. Call 'two feet!' for a two-foot landing instead.</li><li>Anyone who wobbles, hops, or drags a foot after freezing simply resets and waits for the next call — there's no elimination, just repetition.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'bend your landing knee to absorb the landing softly.'</li><li>For 'easier', space calls further apart and use a bigger grid; for 'harder', call faster and mix the order unpredictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Catch &amp; Land</h4><p>Partners toss the ball gently to each other; the receiver must call out which foot (or both) they landed on the moment they catch it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 2-3m apart with one ball between them, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs so tosses don't cross into another pair's space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your landing before the ball arrives, not after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Underarm toss, close distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Higher toss, greater distance</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a gentle underarm toss and the idea of deciding your landing foot before the ball actually arrives.</li><li>One partner tosses the ball; the receiver catches it and immediately calls out 'one!' or 'two!' depending on how they landed.</li><li>Partners swap roles after every catch so both get equal tossing and catching turns.</li><li>If the landing is unclear or the foot drags, the pair repeats that same toss again rather than moving straight on.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide your landing before the ball arrives, not after.'</li><li>For 'easier', use an underarm toss at close range; for 'harder', toss higher and increase the distance between partners.</li></ul><hr><h4>Landing Line Race</h4><p>Small groups race down a line of hoops/cones, jumping and landing correctly in each one (alternating one-foot and two-foot landings), racing another group.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Lay out a line of 6-8 hoops (or cones as markers) roughly 1m apart per group of 4-5 students, with two parallel lines set up 4-5m apart so two groups can race side by side.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stick the landing before moving to the next hoop</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger hoops, more space between</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller hoops, closer together</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate jumping into each hoop and alternating between a one-foot landing and a two-foot landing as you go down the line.</li><li>On 'go', the first student in each line jumps hoop to hoop, sticking each landing cleanly before moving to the next.</li><li>A landing only counts if it's stuck without a wobble or extra hop — otherwise the student repeats that hoop before continuing.</li><li>Once a student reaches the end, they jog back around the outside and tag the next teammate in their line to go.</li><li>The first group to get every student through the line wins; coach the key message 'stick the landing before moving to the next hoop.'</li><li>For 'easier', use bigger hoops with more space between them; for 'harder', use smaller hoops set closer together.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Freeze Catch Netball</h3><p>4v4, small court, no defending pressure yet. Every catch must be followed by a clear, controlled landing (caller announces 'one!' or 'two!') before the next pass — the game only flows if landings are clean.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small marked court roughly 15m x 10m (about a quarter of a full netball court). One ball per game — no bibs needed yet, since there's no defending pressure.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a passing game only — no defenders contesting the ball yet, just free passing as players move around the court.</li><li>One team starts with the ball and players pass freely to teammates as they move into space.</li><li>Every catch must be followed by a called, controlled landing — the catcher announces 'one!' or 'two!' out loud — before the next pass can be thrown.</li><li>If a player forgets to call their landing, that pass doesn't count and possession simply resets to the nearest teammate so play keeps flowing.</li><li>Rotate which four students are on court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the pace relaxed and the focus on clean, called landings rather than speed or scoring.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you land under control today?</li><li>What's the difference between a one-foot and a two-foot landing?</li><li>Why does a clean landing matter before you can pivot or pass?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Lands with a bent knee, not stiff-legged</li><li>Can identify which foot (or both) they landed on</li><li>Stays balanced immediately after landing, ready to move</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a bounce or roll instead of a toss for students still building catching confidence, so the footwork focus isn't lost to a difficult catch.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Target Chest":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Chest pass — accuracy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding what your own hands and body are doing during a pass, and noticing what changes when the technique clicks.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passing lanes should not cross other pairs' lanes — space grids out</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to throw an accurate chest pass using two hands.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can push the ball from my chest with two hands, thumbs behind the ball</li><li>I can step toward my target as I release the pass</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Footwork Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no ball or equipment needed — this warm-up is about feet and landings, not hands.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' at random intervals — students immediately land and freeze on a single foot, then resume jogging on the next 'go.'</li><li>Call 'two feet!' the same way — students land and freeze on both feet at once.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and getting the landing habit into their bodies before today's lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Hold the ball with both hands, thumbs behind, fingers spread.</td><td>This grip gives control and an even push through the ball.</td></tr><tr><td>Push the ball out from the chest, stepping toward the target.</td><td>The step adds power and directs the pass accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through with the arms toward the target.</td><td>Stopping the arms early causes the pass to fall short or stray off target.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall of Targets</h4><p>Partners chest pass through a small cone gate at chest height from increasing distances, scoring a point each time the ball goes cleanly through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand facing each other with a small cone gate (two cones about 1m apart) placed at chest height between them — use a raised bar, hoop held upright, or a partner's marked chest zone as the target. Start 2m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Thumbs behind the ball, push straight out, follow through with your hands</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gate, closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gate, further apart</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the chest pass technique: thumbs behind the ball, push straight out, follow through with the hands finishing pointing at the target.</li><li>Partners take turns chest-passing the ball cleanly through the gate at chest height, scoring a point each time it goes through without touching a cone.</li><li>After every 3 successful passes, both partners take one step further apart to increase the distance.</li><li>If a pass misses the gate or drops, the pair stays at the current distance and tries again rather than moving back.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'thumbs behind the ball, push straight out, follow through with your hands.'</li><li>For 'easier', widen the gate and stay closer together; for 'harder', narrow the gate and increase the starting distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Step &amp; Push</h4><p>Partners pass while explicitly stepping forward with the pass, exaggerating the transfer of weight into the throw.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 2-3m apart, one ball between them, with enough room between pairs (at least 2m) to step forward without bumping into another pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Step with the same-side foot as your throwing motion for balance</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>No step required, stationary pass</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Step and pass while moving sideways</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate stepping forward with the same-side foot as the throwing hand while pushing the pass, exaggerating the transfer of weight.</li><li>Partners pass back and forth, each one clearly stepping into every pass rather than passing from a static stance.</li><li>Watch for balance after the step — the passer should land ready to move, not off-balance or falling forward.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'step with the same-side foot as your throwing motion for balance.'</li><li>For 'easier', remove the step and pass from a stationary position; for 'harder', have partners shuffle sideways while stepping and passing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Chest Pass Race</h4><p>Groups of 3 complete 10 accurate chest passes around a triangle as fast as possible, racing other triangles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 form a triangle roughly 3-4m per side, marked with a cone at each point, with triangles spaced at least 3m apart from neighbouring groups. One ball per triangle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your hands early — call for it before it arrives</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller triangle</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger triangle, must alternate direction</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: complete 10 clean, accurate chest passes around the triangle as fast as possible, counting out loud as a group.</li><li>On 'go', the group starts passing corner to corner in order, with receivers showing target hands early to help the passer.</li><li>If a pass is dropped or off-target, the group doesn't restart the count — they simply recover the ball and continue from where they left off.</li><li>The first triangle to reach 10 clean passes calls out and is declared the winner for that round; run several rounds so different groups get to win.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'prepare your hands early — call for it before it arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a smaller triangle; for 'harder', use a bigger triangle and require the group to alternate the direction of travel on a call.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Possession Chest Pass Netball</h3><p>4v4, small court, no defenders — 1 point per 5 consecutive accurate chest passes kept as a team, rewarding technique over speed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court with no defenders, roughly 15m x 10m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring rule before starting: 1 point is awarded for every 5 consecutive accurate chest passes a team completes without a drop.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; players move into space and use chest passes only to build up their sequence.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or a pass isn't a clean chest pass, the count resets to zero for that team and play continues immediately from the nearest spot.</li><li>Keep track of each team's best streak during the game and celebrate when a team reaches 5.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Emphasise technique over speed — a slower, accurate sequence beats a fast, sloppy one.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What technique helped your chest pass go where you wanted?</li><li>How did stepping into the pass change its accuracy?</li><li>What happens to the game when passes aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Thumbs behind the ball, not underneath or beside it</li><li>Steps toward the target as they release</li><li>Pass arrives at chest height, not too high or low</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Shorten distances for students still building arm strength/technique; the gate structure scales naturally by moving cones closer.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Bounce & Loft":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bounce pass &amp; overhead pass</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using fair, honest effort to challenge your partner appropriately — an overhead pass thrown too hard isn't fun or safe for anyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Overhead passes should stay controlled — no throwing directly at another person's head</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning two more passing options — the bounce pass and the overhead pass — and when each is useful.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can bounce a pass so it reaches my partner around waist height</li><li>I can throw an overhead pass over a defender's reach</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Footwork Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no ball or equipment needed — this warm-up is about feet and landings, not hands.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' at random intervals — students immediately land and freeze on a single foot, then resume jogging on the next 'go.'</li><li>Call 'two feet!' the same way — students land and freeze on both feet at once.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and getting the landing habit into their bodies before today's lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>For a bounce pass, aim the ball to bounce roughly two-thirds of the way to your target.</td><td>This makes it rise to a comfortable catching height for the receiver.</td></tr><tr><td>For an overhead pass, release from above the head with a flat trajectory.</td><td>Keeps the pass fast and out of defenders' reach.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the pass type based on the defender's position.</td><td>Different pressure situations suit different pass types.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Bounce to a Target</h4><p>Partners bounce pass to each other, aiming for the ball to bounce roughly two-thirds of the way between them and rise to waist height.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3-4m apart with one ball each pair. Optionally place a hoop or cone on the ground roughly two-thirds of the way between them as a bounce-spot target.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Push down and out, not straight down — aim for the bounce spot, not your partner's feet</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer distance, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Greater distance, add a target hoop for the bounce spot</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the bounce pass technique: push down and out (not straight down), aiming for a spot roughly two-thirds of the way to your partner so the ball rises to waist height for the catch.</li><li>Partners take turns bounce-passing to each other, aiming for the target spot between them.</li><li>After every 3 clean bounce passes in a row, both partners take one step further apart.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'push down and out, not straight down — aim for the bounce spot, not your partner's feet.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the distance closer and pace slower; for 'harder', increase the distance and add a target hoop that the ball must bounce inside.</li></ul><hr><h4>Overhead Over the Rope</h4><p>Partners throw overhead passes over a raised rope or line (simulating a tall defender), focusing on height and control rather than power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3-4m apart with a rope or line raised between them at roughly head height (a skipping rope tied between two cones/posts works well, or a chalk/tape line on the ground as a simplified version). One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use your whole arm and follow through, not just a flick of the wrist</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Lower rope, closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Higher rope, further distance</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the overhead pass: ball held above and behind the head with two hands, using the whole arm and a full follow-through rather than just a wrist flick.</li><li>Partners take turns throwing overhead passes that clear the rope and land in their partner's hands with control.</li><li>Emphasise height and control over power — a soft, well-aimed pass that clears the rope beats a hard, wild one.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use your whole arm and follow through, not just a flick of the wrist.'</li><li>For 'easier', lower the rope and reduce the distance; for 'harder', raise the rope and increase the distance between partners.</li></ul><hr><h4>Choose Your Pass</h4><p>Partners alternate between chest, bounce, and overhead passes based on a called instruction, building quick recognition of when to use each.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3m apart with one ball between them, in the same pairs used across today's lesson.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide the pass type before you catch, not after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer pass types, more time to decide</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>All three types, quick-fire calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of all three passes practised today: chest, bounce, and overhead.</li><li>The coach (or app) calls out a pass type before each throw — 'chest!', 'bounce!', or 'overhead!' — and the passer must use that exact pass.</li><li>Partners keep passing back and forth, alternating who receives the call each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide the pass type before you catch, not after,' so receivers are ready for what's coming.</li><li>For 'easier', use only two pass types and allow more time to decide; for 'harder', use all three types with quick-fire calls.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Three-Pass-Type Netball</h3><p>4v4, small court, no defenders — teams must use all three pass types (chest, bounce, overhead) at least once during a passing sequence before scoring, encouraging a full passing toolkit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court with no defenders, roughly 15m x 10m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: during any passing sequence, a team must use all three pass types — chest, bounce, and overhead — at least once before they can score.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; players move and pass, mentally tracking which pass types they've already used in the current sequence.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, possession switches to the other team and the pass-type count resets to zero for the team receiving it.</li><li>Once a team has used all three pass types, their next successful pass into the marked scoring area is worth a point, then play restarts and the count resets.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Encourage players to call out which pass types are still needed to help their team track the sequence.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>When might a bounce pass be more useful than a chest pass?</li><li>What did height and power do to your overhead pass's accuracy?</li><li>Which pass type did you find hardest, and why?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Chooses an appropriate pass type for the situation</li><li>Controls the bounce so it arrives at a catchable height</li><li>Overhead pass has height and control, not wild power</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Let students focus on mastering one new pass type at a time if two feels like too much in a single lesson; the chest pass from Lesson 2 remains a safe fallback throughout.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Strong Hands":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Receiving a pass (strong hands, target-making)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Being patient with yourself and encouraging teammates while catching confidence builds — everyone's hands look different at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passes should stay below head height in early games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to receive a pass confidently by making a clear target and catching with strong hands.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can show 'target hands' before the ball arrives</li><li>I can catch with both hands and bring the ball in close to my body</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Footwork Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no ball or equipment needed — this warm-up is about feet and landings, not hands.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' at random intervals — students immediately land and freeze on a single foot, then resume jogging on the next 'go.'</li><li>Call 'two feet!' the same way — students land and freeze on both feet at once.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and getting the landing habit into their bodies before today's lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Show clear target hands early, fingers spread.</td><td>Gives the passer confidence and an obvious aim point.</td></tr><tr><td>Catch with both hands and bring the ball in close to the body.</td><td>Secures the ball immediately against defensive pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Land under control on the landing foot rule.</td><td>Connects the catch directly into legal footwork.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Target Hands Catch</h4><p>Partners pass; the receiver must show clear target hands (fingers spread, hands up) before every pass is thrown.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3m apart with one ball between them, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Show your hands early so your partner has an obvious target to aim for</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passes with more pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'target hands': fingers spread, hands held up and ready in front of the body, clearly showing the passer exactly where to aim.</li><li>Before every single pass, the receiver must show target hands — the passer waits until they see it before throwing.</li><li>Partners pass back and forth using any pass type, focusing purely on the receiver's hand position each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'show your hands early so your partner has an obvious target to aim for.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower passes with more time to set the target; for 'harder', increase the pace so hands must go up quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Catch &amp; Secure</h4><p>Receiver catches the ball and immediately brings it in close to the body (pivot-ready position) before passing back.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3m apart with one ball between them, in the same spacing as the previous game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball all the way into your hands, then secure it close to your chest</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes, closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster passes, greater distance</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate catching the ball with both hands and immediately pulling it in close to the chest — the 'pivot-ready' position — before doing anything else.</li><li>One partner passes; the receiver catches, secures the ball tight to their chest, pauses briefly, then passes back.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch the ball all the way into your hands, then secure it close to your chest.'</li><li>If the ball is caught loosely or held away from the body, that catch doesn't count — the pair repeats it.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower passes at a closer distance; for 'harder', use faster passes at a greater distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Receive</h4><p>As Skill Game 1, but a light 'shadow' defender stands nearby (not intercepting yet) to add visual pressure on the catch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs as Target Hands Catch, with one extra student per pair acting as a 'shadow' defender standing 1-2m to the side of the receiver — hands-off, not intercepting.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep eyes on the ball, not the defender</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close in after the pass is thrown</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the shadow defender is there to add visual distraction only — they don't move to intercept or touch the ball.</li><li>The passer throws to the receiver exactly as in Target Hands Catch, showing target hands first.</li><li>The receiver focuses on catching cleanly despite the defender's presence nearby.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep eyes on the ball, not the defender.'</li><li>Rotate roles every few throws so each student experiences passing, receiving, and shadowing.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the defender further away; for 'harder', allow the defender to move slightly closer after the pass is thrown.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Secure Catch Netball</h3><p>4v4, small court, light shadow defence only — teams score for maintaining clean, secure catches under mild pressure, building toward full defending in later lessons.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court, roughly 15m x 10m, with light shadow defenders only (hands-off, not intercepting). One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that defenders may stand near their mark and move with them, but must not touch or intercept the ball — the focus is on catching cleanly under mild visual pressure.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; players move into space and pass, aiming to maintain secure, well-caught possession.</li><li>A team scores a point each time they string together 5 clean, secure catches (ball brought in close each time) without a drop.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, the count resets and possession goes to whichever team is nearest the ball to keep the game moving.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>This sets up the fully live defending introduced in Lesson 10 — remind students that today's defenders are hands-off only.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did showing target hands do to help your partner pass to you?</li><li>How did securing the ball close to your body help you next?</li><li>What made catching under a bit of pressure trickier?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Shows clear target hands before the ball arrives</li><li>Catches with two hands, not trapping against the body</li><li>Brings the ball in close and secure immediately after catching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a slightly underinflated or softer ball for students who find catching difficult, so the footwork/target-hands focus isn't lost to a hard catch.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Pivot Power":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Pivoting to protect the ball</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Using your skills fairly to challenge a teammate at a level that helps them learn, not just to catch them out.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact — hands-off defending only; stay upright and controlled while pivoting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pivot on our landing foot to protect the ball from a defender.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pivot on my landing foot without dragging or lifting it</li><li>I can use my body to shield the ball from a defender while pivoting</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Footwork Freeze</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, no ball or equipment needed — this warm-up is about feet and landings, not hands.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of others nearby.</li><li>Call 'one foot!' at random intervals — students immediately land and freeze on a single foot, then resume jogging on the next 'go.'</li><li>Call 'two feet!' the same way — students land and freeze on both feet at once.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful; the goal is raising heart rate and getting the landing habit into their bodies before today's lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order and speed of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the landing/pivot foot planted without lifting or dragging it.</td><td>This is the footwork rule — lifting or dragging the pivot foot is an infringement.</td></tr><tr><td>Turn your body around the pivot foot to shield the ball from a defender.</td><td>Uses your body as a barrier between the ball and the defender.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball high and away from the defender's reach while pivoting.</td><td>Protects it from being intercepted mid-pivot.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Static Pivot Practice</h4><p>Students catch a gentle toss, land, and practise pivoting 180 degrees on their landing foot without dragging it, then pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 3m apart with one ball between them, with enough room (at least 2m) around each pair to pivot freely without bumping into others.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your landing foot glued to the same spot as you spin your body around it</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller pivot (90°)</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full 180° pivot, added speed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the pivot: catch the ball, land, plant one foot as the 'landing foot,' and spin the body 180 degrees around that same spot without lifting or dragging it.</li><li>One partner tosses gently; the receiver catches, lands, pivots a full 180 degrees, then passes back from the new facing direction.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your landing foot glued to the same spot as you spin your body around it.'</li><li>Watch closely for a dragged or lifted landing foot and reset the rep if it happens, rather than letting the habit build in.</li><li>For 'easier', reduce the pivot to 90 degrees; for 'harder', require the full 180-degree pivot done quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Pivot</h4><p>A shadow defender stands nearby (not touching) while the receiver catches, lands, and pivots to shield the ball with their body before passing away from the defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs as Static Pivot Practice, with one shadow defender standing 1-2m away from the receiver — hands-off, not touching.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep your body and the ball between you and the defender as you turn</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to move around the pivoter</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the defender's job is to stand near the pivoter without touching, so the receiver has to actually use their body to shield the ball.</li><li>The receiver catches, lands, and pivots so their body and the ball are positioned between themselves and the defender, then passes away from the defender's side.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep your body and the ball between you and the defender as you turn.'</li><li>Rotate roles every few reps so everyone experiences receiving, passing, and shadowing.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the defender further away; for 'harder', let the defender move around the pivoter to force a decision about which way to turn.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pivot Under Pressure Grid</h4><p>Small group game where every catch must be followed by a clean pivot before a pass is allowed, with a light defender adding decision pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 students in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, with one light defender per group (hands-off). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your pivot direction based on where the defender is, not out of habit</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, active defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every catch inside the grid must be followed by a clean pivot (no dragged or lifted landing foot) before a pass is allowed.</li><li>Players pass freely around the grid; the defender moves to add mild decision pressure without touching the ball or making contact.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide your pivot direction based on where the defender is, not out of habit.'</li><li>If a pivot is sloppy (foot drags or lifts), that player simply repeats the pivot on the spot before passing — no restart of the whole game.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid and a slower-moving defender; for 'harder', shrink the grid and let the defender move more actively.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pivot &amp; Protect Netball</h3><p>4v4, small court — every catch must include a clean pivot (no dragged/lifted landing foot) before the next pass; a called-out 'footwork!' resets possession to the other team, reinforcing the rule under game pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, hands-off defending only (no contact). One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the core rule before starting: every catch must include a clean pivot — landing foot planted, no dragging or lifting — before the next pass can be thrown.</li><li>Play flows normally with both teams passing and moving; the coach or a nominated caller watches for footwork.</li><li>If a player's landing foot drags or lifts during a pivot, the caller shouts 'footwork!' and possession resets immediately to the other team from that spot.</li><li>This mirrors the real footwork rule in netball, so let the game continue at pace rather than stopping for long explanations each time it's called.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Remind students that defending stays hands-off and no-contact throughout, consistent with the safety rule for this lesson.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did pivoting well feel like today?</li><li>How did shielding the ball with your body help protect it from a defender?</li><li>What happened when your landing foot lifted or dragged?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps the landing foot planted throughout the pivot</li><li>Uses body position to shield the ball from a defender</li><li>Recognises and self-corrects a footwork error</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find the footwork rule difficult, allow extra practice reps with no defender before adding pressure, and be generous with early on-court coaching rather than enforcing the rule strictly straight away.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Movement & Introducing Defence (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: On the Move":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing on the move / pass-and-move</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role in keeping the game moving — knowing when to move and why it helps your teammate.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passing lanes clear of other groups when moving through space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball and then move immediately into space, rather than standing still.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass and then move into space straight away</li><li>I can time my pass so a moving teammate can control it easily</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pass &amp; Pivot</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and pivots don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth using any pass type while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pivot!' — on this call, the receiver must land, pivot 180 degrees on the spot, and pass back from their new facing direction before continuing.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and pivoting with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the pass-and-pivot combination, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pivot calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pass, then immediately move to new space.</td><td>Standing still after passing makes you an easy, static target to mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your movement so you're available again quickly.</td><td>Netball's fast tempo rewards players who keep offering new options.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate where you're moving to.</td><td>Helps the ball carrier find you again after you move.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pass &amp; Move Relay</h4><p>Pairs practise 'pass and move': Player A passes to Player B and immediately jogs to a new spot; B controls and passes back to A's new position, repeating down a channel.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand at either end of a marked channel roughly 10m long and 2-3m wide, with channels spaced at least 2m apart from neighbouring pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move immediately after passing — don't stand and admire your pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light shadow defender jogging alongside</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'pass and move': Player A passes to Player B, then immediately jogs sideways to a new spot in the channel rather than standing still.</li><li>Player B controls the ball and passes it back to Player A's new position, then also moves to a new spot after passing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move immediately after passing — don't stand and admire your pass.'</li><li>Continue this pattern down the length of the channel and back.</li><li>For 'easier', have players walk instead of jog between passes; for 'harder', add a light shadow defender jogging alongside without touching the ball.</li></ul><hr><h4>Give and Regive Gates</h4><p>In trios, players combine pass-and-move sequences to move the ball through a series of marked gates down the court, racing another trio.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios stand at the start of a channel roughly 15m long with 4-5 cone gates (two cones about 2m apart) spaced evenly down its length, one channel per trio, spaced at least 2m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pass early, before you're crowded by a defender</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gates, no time pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gates, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: move the ball through every gate in order using pass-and-move combinations, racing another trio down a parallel channel.</li><li>Players advance by passing to a teammate who has moved ahead through the next gate, then following themselves.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pass early, before you're crowded by a defender' — even though there's no defender yet, this builds the habit.</li><li>The trio must pass through every gate cleanly; if the ball misses a gate, they return to the previous gate and try again.</li><li>For 'easier', use wider gates with no time pressure; for 'harder', use narrower gates and time the race.</li></ul><hr><h4>1-2 vs Shadow Defender</h4><p>Pairs attempt a pass-and-move combination against one light-touch defender who can only intercept after the first pass is played.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one extra student as a light-touch shadow defender, working in a grid roughly 10m x 8m. One ball per group of three.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move into space immediately, don't just stand and watch your pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender starts further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close immediately</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the defender's limit: they may only move to intercept after the first pass has been played, not before.</li><li>The pair attempts a pass-and-move combination — pass, move into new space, receive a return pass — while the defender tries to react and get in the passing lane.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move into space immediately, don't just stand and watch your pass.'</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 2-3 minutes so all three students experience defending and attacking.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender start further back; for 'harder', let the defender close in immediately after the first pass.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Continuous Movement Netball</h3><p>4v4 with light defence — extra points awarded for a passing sequence where every player moves after their own pass, encouraging constant, purposeful movement across the whole team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, with light hands-off defence. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule before starting: extra points are awarded for a passing sequence where every player who passes then visibly moves to a new position afterward.</li><li>Play flows normally, with both teams passing and defending lightly (no contact, no interceptions with contact).</li><li>The coach or a nominated caller watches for a full team sequence of pass-and-move and calls out 'bonus!' when it happens, awarding the extra point.</li><li>If any player stands still after passing, the bonus sequence is broken and the team must start building toward it again.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout play: constant, purposeful movement after every pass is what wins the bonus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a pass-and-move sequence work well today?</li><li>Why does moving after your pass matter in a game?</li><li>Can you describe a moment you found good space at the right time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves immediately after passing</li><li>Receives the return pass without needing to stop and reset</li><li>Communicates (calls) with a teammate for the next pass</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole combination down to walking pace for students still building the timing/decision sequence; speed increases as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Dodge & Dash":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dodging to lose a defender</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after yourself with patience while learning a tricky new skill, and encouraging teammates whose dodges don't come off yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact — dodges must be hands-off; watch for collisions in tight grids</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to dodge quickly to lose a defender and create space to receive a pass.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can change direction sharply to lose a defender</li><li>I can accelerate away immediately after my dodge</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pass &amp; Pivot</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and pivots don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth using any pass type while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pivot!' — on this call, the receiver must land, pivot 180 degrees on the spot, and pass back from their new facing direction before continuing.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and pivoting with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the pass-and-pivot combination, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pivot calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use a sharp change of direction to unbalance the defender.</td><td>A sudden change is much harder for a defender to react to than a straight run.</td></tr><tr><td>Push off explosively out of the dodge.</td><td>The dodge only creates separation if you accelerate away immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>Time the dodge just before you want to receive the ball.</td><td>Dodging too early lets the defender recover before the pass arrives.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Dodge</h4><p>A player dodges freely around a grid while a shadow defender mirrors from 1.5m away without touching, getting used to a defender's presence nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out in a grid roughly 10m x 10m per pair, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs. No ball needed for this game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the defender's hips, not their head, to time your dodge</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stays further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner is the mover, dodging freely around the grid with quick changes of direction; the other is the shadow defender, mirroring from about 1.5m away without touching.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look at the defender's hips, not their head, to time your dodge' — remind the mover this applies when they later add a defender reacting to them.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners experience dodging and shadowing.</li><li>Keep all movement hands-off — this is about getting comfortable with a defender's presence, not contact.</li><li>For 'easier', have the shadow stay further back; for 'harder', let the shadow get closer while still not touching.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cone Dodge Sprint</h4><p>Individual dodge run through a zig-zag line of cones as fast as possible, racing against their own best time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a zig-zag line of 6-8 cones roughly 2m apart per student, with several parallel lines so multiple students can go at once, spaced at least 2m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small, quick steps at each cone, longer strides between them</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider cone spacing</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower spacing/timed leaderboard</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate small, quick steps at each cone with longer strides in between, weaving through the zig-zag as fast as possible.</li><li>Each student runs the line individually, timed by a partner or the coach if a stopwatch is available.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'small, quick steps at each cone, longer strides between them.'</li><li>Students race against their own previous best time rather than against each other directly.</li><li>For 'easier', widen the spacing between cones; for 'harder', narrow the spacing and keep a running leaderboard of best times.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 to a Gap</h4><p>Attacker tries to dodge past a single defender to reach a marked space on the other side of a small grid.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a small grid roughly 6m x 6m, with a marked 2m-wide gap on the far side as the attacker's target. No ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Sell a fake direction with your eyes and shoulders before committing to your real dodge</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker gets 2 attempts before rotating</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacker starts on one side of the grid and tries to dodge past the defender to reach the marked gap on the other side.</li><li>The defender stands hands-off in the middle, moving to block the attacker's path without making contact.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'sell a fake direction with your eyes and shoulders before committing to your real dodge.'</li><li>Swap attacker and defender roles after every attempt (or every 2 attempts) so turns are even.</li><li>For 'easier', give the attacker 2 attempts before rotating; for 'harder', shrink the grid to make the dodge sharper and quicker.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Dodge to Receive Netball</h3><p>4v4 with light defence — attackers must use a genuine dodge to lose their defender before a pass can be thrown to them, rewarding clean, purposeful movement to get free.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, with light hands-off defence. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: a pass can only be thrown to an attacker once they've used a genuine dodge to lose their defender — a simple standing catch doesn't count.</li><li>Play flows with both teams attacking and defending; attackers must actively dodge (change direction, use a fake) before a teammate passes to them.</li><li>If an attacker receives a pass without dodging first, the coach or caller can call it back and have the team retry from that spot.</li><li>Coach the key message during play: 'sell a fake direction with your eyes and shoulders before committing to your real dodge.'</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep defending hands-off throughout, consistent with today's safety rule.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your dodge successful (or not) today?</li><li>How did selling a fake direction help your real dodge work?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try next time you're trying to lose a defender?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Plants a foot firmly before changing direction</li><li>Uses a fake/look before committing to the real dodge</li><li>Accelerates away immediately after the dodge</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For lower-confidence pairs, use passive defenders (walking pace, hands behind back) so the attacker experiences pressure without high stress.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Lead the Way":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Getting free — timing your lead</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Understanding your own timing and decision-making — noticing what changes when your lead clicks into place.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep leads within the group's grid to avoid colliding with other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to time a lead so we arrive in space at exactly the right moment to receive a pass.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can time my lead to arrive in space just as the ball is ready to be passed</li><li>I can recognise when my defender is out of position and lead immediately</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pass &amp; Pivot</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and pivots don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth using any pass type while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pivot!' — on this call, the receiver must land, pivot 180 degrees on the spot, and pass back from their new facing direction before continuing.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and pivoting with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the pass-and-pivot combination, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pivot calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Read when the ball carrier is ready to pass before you lead.</td><td>Leading too early means the defender has time to react and follow.</td></tr><tr><td>Lead with a strong change of pace into space.</td><td>A lazy lead is easy for a defender to track and cover.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate that you're ready for the ball.</td><td>Helps the passer's timing and decision-making.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call &amp; Lead</h4><p>A passer calls 'now!' as the cue for their partner to lead sharply into space and receive the pass, building the timing connection between passer and receiver.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out in a grid roughly 8m x 8m per pair, spaced at least 2m apart from neighbouring pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Lead on the call, not before or after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more obvious timing</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, subtler timing</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The passer holds the ball while their partner stands a few metres away, ready to move.</li><li>The passer calls 'now!' as the exact cue for their partner to lead sharply into open space and receive the pass.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'lead on the call, not before or after,' so the timing connection between passer and receiver becomes automatic.</li><li>Swap roles after every 4-5 reps so both partners practise passing and leading.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with a more obvious call; for 'harder', speed up the pace and make the timing window subtler.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Gap Lead</h4><p>Receiver watches their defender and leads into space the moment the defender is out of position or looking away, without a verbal cue.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one shadow defender working in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. One ball per group of three.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your lead</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender moves predictably</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender moves unpredictably</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The receiver watches their shadow defender rather than waiting for a verbal cue, and leads into space the moment the defender is out of position or looking away.</li><li>The passer waits for the receiver to lead before throwing, rather than passing on a fixed count.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch your defender's feet and weight, not the ball, to time your lead.'</li><li>Rotate roles every few minutes so all three students experience receiving, passing, and defending.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender move predictably; for 'harder', have the defender move unpredictably to make the read genuinely tricky.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Lead Relay</h4><p>Groups of 3 rotate leading into space to receive a pass, then immediately becoming the next passer, racing other triangles for clean completions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 form a triangle roughly 5m per side, marked with a cone at each point, spaced at least 3m from neighbouring triangles. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your lead early — decide your target space before you move</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the pattern: one player passes to a teammate who has led into space, then that receiver immediately becomes the next passer for the third player.</li><li>Rotate this leading-and-passing sequence continuously around the triangle, racing other triangles for the most clean completions in a set time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'prepare your lead early — decide your target space before you move.'</li><li>If a pass is dropped or a lead is mistimed, the group simply recovers the ball and keeps the sequence going rather than restarting.</li><li>For 'easier', run the sequence at a slower pace; for 'harder', speed it up and time the race.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Timed Lead Netball</h3><p>4v4 with defence — coach highlights well-timed leads in play ('great timing!'), reinforcing that arriving in space at the right moment is as valuable as the pass itself.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, with defence active. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with both teams attacking and defending.</li><li>The coach watches closely for well-timed leads — a player arriving into space at exactly the right moment to receive a pass — and calls out 'great timing!' when it happens.</li><li>Use these call-outs as teaching moments during play, briefly naming what made the timing work well.</li><li>Encourage attackers to think about when to lead, not just where, reinforcing that arriving in space at the right moment is as valuable as the pass itself.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the game flowing at a natural pace rather than stopping play for lengthy explanations.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you time your lead well today?</li><li>How did reading your defender's position help you decide when to lead?</li><li>What happens if you lead too early or too late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Times the lead to arrive as the ball becomes available</li><li>Reads the defender's position before leading</li><li>Accelerates into space rather than drifting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a verbal cue system (passer calls 'now!') as training wheels for students still building the reading skill, removing the cue as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Triple Combo":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining dodge + lead + receive</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual skills (dodge, lead, catch) fit together with your team's play for a shared outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games small-sided (3v3 max) so the combined sequence comes thick and fast without overcrowding</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine a dodge, a timed lead, and a clean catch into one smooth sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dodge, lead into space, and catch the ball in one connected sequence</li><li>I can adjust my sequence based on what my defender does</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pass &amp; Pivot</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and pivots don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth using any pass type while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pivot!' — on this call, the receiver must land, pivot 180 degrees on the spot, and pass back from their new facing direction before continuing.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and pivoting with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the pass-and-pivot combination, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pivot calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Dodge to create separation, then lead into open space.</td><td>The dodge and lead work together — one creates the gap, the other uses it.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your final movement to arrive as the ball is released.</td><td>Arriving too early or late breaks the timing of the whole sequence.</td></tr><tr><td>Show target hands as you receive.</td><td>Keeps the catch clean at the end of a well-timed combination.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Dodge-Lead-Catch Drill</h4><p>Player dodges a shadow defender, leads into space, and catches a pass from a partner, all in one continuous sequence, walked through slowly first.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (one dodger/receiver, one shadow defender, one passer) in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, spaced at least 2m apart from neighbouring groups. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Each part flows into the next — don't pause between the dodge and the lead</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time between parts</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, less time</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk through the sequence slowly first: the receiver dodges the shadow defender, leads into space, and catches a pass from the passer, all in one continuous action.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'each part flows into the next — don't pause between the dodge and the lead.'</li><li>Once the walkthrough is smooth, repeat the sequence at a light jog, then a full pace.</li><li>Rotate roles every few reps so all three students experience dodging/receiving, defending, and passing.</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace and add more time between each part; for 'harder', speed the whole sequence up.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 Combo Challenge</h4><p>Two attackers against one defender — the attacker without the ball must combine a dodge and lead to get free and receive a pass from their teammate.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If your first dodge doesn't lose the defender, adjust and try again immediately</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider grid, slower defender</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower grid, faster defender</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One attacker starts with the ball; the other attacker (without the ball) must combine a dodge and a lead to get free of the single defender.</li><li>The defender tries to track and mark the free attacker, using hands-off positioning only.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if your first dodge doesn't lose the defender, adjust and try again immediately' rather than freezing.</li><li>Once the free attacker receives the ball cleanly, reset and rotate which player defends.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider grid with a slower defender; for 'harder', use a narrower grid with a faster, more active defender.</li></ul><hr><h4>Continuous Combo Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where every reception must be preceded by a visible dodge-and-lead sequence, rewarding whichever team keeps combining the skills successfully.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 6-8 (3v3 max on court at once) in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, with the rest of the group waiting to rotate in. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always be thinking one step ahead — dodge, lead, catch, then what's next?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every reception during the game must be preceded by a visible dodge-and-lead sequence, not just a standing catch.</li><li>Play flows continuously; the coach or a caller watches for teams combining the skills successfully and calls out good examples.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'always be thinking one step ahead — dodge, lead, catch, then what's next?'</li><li>If a catch happens without a visible dodge-and-lead, the coach can call it back and have the team retry from that spot.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with more time; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with faster decisions required.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 4v3 Combination Netball</h3><p>Attacking team has a numbers advantage (4v3); success depends on combining today's three skills — dodging to create separation, leading with good timing, and catching cleanly to keep the sequence alive.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 7 for 4v3 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m, giving the attacking team a numbers advantage. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: the attacking team has 4 players against 3 defenders, and success depends on combining today's three skills — dodging, leading, and catching cleanly.</li><li>Play flows with the attacking team trying to keep the sequence alive by dodging free, leading with good timing, and catching securely under the numbers advantage.</li><li>Defenders play hands-off and try to cover as much space as they can despite being outnumbered.</li><li>Rotate which team is attacking (4) versus defending (3) every few minutes so everyone experiences both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: combine all three skills in sequence rather than relying on just one.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did dodging, leading, and catching work together today?</li><li>What happened when you tried to skip a step in the sequence?</li><li>What's one moment you combined all three skills well?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Dodge genuinely creates separation from the defender</li><li>Lead is timed to arrive as the ball becomes available</li><li>Catch is clean and secure, completing the sequence</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce numbers (e.g. 1v1 with a feeder) for students still building confidence combining three skills at once, before scaling up to 2v1 or 4v3.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Shadow the Pass":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — marking the pass</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Looking after your opponent by defending fairly and safely, respecting the marking distance rather than crowding them.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking distance, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact — hands-off defending only; maintain the 0.9m marking distance to keep it safe and fair</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend by standing off a player who has the ball and trying to intercept the pass they throw.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand the correct distance (0.9m) away from a player holding the ball</li><li>I can watch the ball to try to intercept a pass, without contacting my opponent</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pass &amp; Pivot</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes and pivots don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start passing gently back and forth using any pass type while jogging slowly on the spot.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pivot!' — on this call, the receiver must land, pivot 180 degrees on the spot, and pass back from their new facing direction before continuing.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue passing and pivoting with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the pass-and-pivot combination, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pivot calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay within the legal defending distance (arm's length, three feet).</td><td>This is netball's contact and defending distance rule.</td></tr><tr><td>Position your body between the attacker and the ball where possible.</td><td>Makes the passing lane to your player harder to use.</td></tr><tr><td>React the instant the ball is released, not before.</td><td>Reacting too early can be considered obstruction — timing matters.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Distance Check</h4><p>Partners practise standing exactly 0.9m (about 3 feet, or one long step) away from a player holding the ball, checking distance with a cone marker.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand together with one cone placed exactly 0.9m (about one long step) away from the ball-holder, one set per pair, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs. No ball movement needed yet — just positioning practice.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use a cone on the ground as a visual reminder of the correct distance</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slightly more generous distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Exact distance, checked by a partner</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner holds the ball still; the other practises standing exactly at the cone marker, checking they're at the correct 0.9m marking distance.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use a cone on the ground as a visual reminder of the correct distance.'</li><li>Swap roles every few reps so both partners feel what the correct distance looks and feels like from both sides.</li><li>Once comfortable, remove the cone and have the defending partner try to judge the distance by eye, checking against where the cone was.</li><li>For 'easier', allow a slightly more generous distance while learning; for 'harder', require the exact distance, checked by the partner each time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Pass</h4><p>Defender marks a player with the ball and tries to read which direction the pass will go, moving to intercept without contacting anyone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one defender working in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. One ball per group of three.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the passer's eyes and shoulders for clues about where the ball is going</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Passer telegraphs the pass obviously</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passer disguises the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The defender marks the player with the ball at the correct 0.9m distance, using cones as a reference if needed.</li><li>The passer looks for a passing option while the defender tries to read which direction the pass will go, moving to intercept without making contact.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch the passer's eyes and shoulders for clues about where the ball is going.'</li><li>Rotate roles every few reps so all three students experience passing, receiving, and defending.</li><li>For 'easier', have the passer telegraph the pass obviously; for 'harder', have the passer disguise where they're throwing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Marking Grid Game</h4><p>Small group game where every player with the ball is marked by a defender at the correct distance, practising the marking habit continuously.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, with one or two defenders rotating through. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay on the balls of your feet, ready to move either direction</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every player holding the ball must be marked by a defender standing at the correct 0.9m distance before play continues.</li><li>Players pass freely around the grid while defenders track and mark whoever currently holds the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay on the balls of your feet, ready to move either direction.'</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 2-3 minutes so everyone gets a turn marking and being marked.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with a slower pace; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with a faster pace.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Marked Pass Netball</h3><p>4v4 — every player with the ball must be marked by a defender at the correct distance before a pass can be thrown, introducing live defending pressure for the first time in the unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a small court roughly 15m x 10m — the first game in the unit with full live defending pressure. One ball per game, bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the headline rule before starting: every player with the ball must be marked by a defender at the correct 0.9m distance before a pass can be thrown.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game; defenders must track their mark closely but stay hands-off, no contact.</li><li>If a player passes while unmarked (no defender within the correct distance), the coach or caller can note it as an easy attacking win for that team.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'stay on the balls of your feet, ready to move either direction.'</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>This is the unit's first live defending pressure — keep reminding students that it's hands-off and no-contact throughout.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made marking at the correct distance tricky today?</li><li>How did reading the passer help you try to intercept?</li><li>What's the purpose of the 0.9m marking rule?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Maintains the correct marking distance</li><li>Reads passer's cues to anticipate the pass direction</li><li>Defends without contacting the opponent</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a visible cone or line as a distance reference for students still learning to judge 0.9m by eye.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Shooting, Space & Positional Play (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Read & Intercept":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — marking the shot/intercepting</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills fairly to give the shooter and passer a genuine, respectful contest without crowding or contacting them.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No contact and no touching the ball during a shot attempt — defenders may only intercept a pass, not block a shot at the hoop</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend a shooter and to read a pass in order to intercept it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can mark a shooter at the correct distance without touching the ball on its way to the hoop</li><li>I can move into the passing lane to try to intercept a pass</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Dodge Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement that echoes the dodging focus from Block 2.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay at the legal defending distance while marking the shooter.</td><td>Keeps the defence within the rules while still applying pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Read the passer's body shape to anticipate the pass direction.</td><td>Anticipation, not just reaction, is what creates real interception chances.</td></tr><tr><td>Move to intercept only when you have a genuine, safe chance.</td><td>Over-committing to a risky interception can leave your player completely free.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shooter Mark Practice</h4><p>Defender marks a shooter at the correct distance as they prepare to shoot, practising positioning without interfering with the shot itself.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs near a hoop or shooting target, with the shooter standing 2-3m from the hoop and the defender marking at the correct 0.9m distance. One ball and one hoop per pair, spaced well apart from other pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay balanced and ready to move — don't lunge at the shooter</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shooter shoots from close range</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shooter shoots from further out</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The defender takes up position marking the shooter at the correct distance, without touching the ball or interfering with the shot.</li><li>The shooter goes through their shooting stance and prepares to shoot as normal, getting used to a defender's presence nearby.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay balanced and ready to move — don't lunge at the shooter.'</li><li>Rotate shooter and defender roles every few attempts so both experience each role.</li><li>For 'easier', have the shooter shoot from close range; for 'harder', have them shoot from further out.</li></ul><hr><h4>Passing Lane Read</h4><p>Defender practises stepping into the passing lane between two attackers to try to intercept, based on reading the passer's body language.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) in a small area roughly 6m x 6m near the shooting circle, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Position yourself in the lane before the pass is thrown, not after</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Passer telegraphs the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passer disguises the pass</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The two attackers stand a few metres apart with the ball moving between them; the defender positions in the space between them, trying to read the passing lane.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'position yourself in the lane before the pass is thrown, not after.'</li><li>The defender attempts to step into the lane and intercept without making contact with either attacker.</li><li>Rotate the defender role every few reps so everyone experiences reading the lane.</li><li>For 'easier', have the passer telegraph the pass obviously; for 'harder', have the passer disguise where they're throwing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Intercept Relay</h4><p>Small group game where defenders rotate trying to intercept a series of passes between attackers, racing to reach a target number of interceptions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, with 1-2 defenders rotating through and trying to reach a target number of interceptions. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Anticipate, don't just react — where is this pass most likely to go?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious passes</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable passes</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target: defenders rotate through trying to reach a set number of interceptions (e.g. 3) as fast as possible while attackers pass freely.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'anticipate, don't just react — where is this pass most likely to go?'</li><li>Defenders stay hands-off except for legally intercepting passes in the air or reading the lane, no contact with players.</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 2-3 minutes so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more obvious passes; for 'harder', use faster, less predictable passing.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; Defend Netball</h3><p>4v4 with shooting circles marked — defenders focus explicitly on marking shooters fairly and reading passing lanes to intercept, while attackers try to find a clear passing or shooting option.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with a marked shooting circle at each end, roughly 15m x 12m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the dual focus before starting: defenders should mark shooters fairly at the correct distance, and look to read passing lanes to intercept.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with shooting allowed inside the marked circle only.</li><li>The coach highlights good defensive reads out loud during play — both fair shooter-marking and successful lane interceptions.</li><li>Attackers try to find a clear passing option into the circle or a clean shooting opportunity when marked fairly.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Reinforce the safety rule throughout: no contact, and no touching the ball during a shot attempt.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made marking a shooter different from marking a passer?</li><li>How did reading the passer's body language help you try to intercept?</li><li>What's the difference between defending fairly and defending too aggressively?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Marks a shooter without interfering with the shot</li><li>Positions in the passing lane before the pass is thrown</li><li>Defends fairly, without contact</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find reading passes difficult, start with an obvious, slow, telegraphed pass before introducing disguised passing.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Sweet Shot":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting technique (for shooting positions)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Being patient with yourself as shooting technique develops — even great shooters miss, and that's part of learning.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair or small group, hoops/targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Shooters should have clear space in front of them; rotate turns so no one is standing directly under the hoop while others shoot</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct shooting technique — how to shoot the ball cleanly toward the hoop.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold a balanced shooting stance with the ball above my head</li><li>I can release the ball with a soft arc using my fingers, not my palm</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Dodge Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement that echoes the dodging focus from Block 2.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stand balanced, ball held above the head with both hands, elbows in.</td><td>A stable base and consistent hand position build repeatable shooting technique.</td></tr><tr><td>Bend the knees slightly and use the legs to generate lift, not just the arms.</td><td>Leg drive gives more consistent power than arms alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through with a soft wrist flick towards the hoop.</td><td>The follow-through controls the ball's backspin and accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Balanced Stance Practice</h4><p>Students practise the shooting stance (feet shoulder-width, ball held above the head with fingers spread) without shooting yet, focusing purely on set-up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually near a hoop or target, one ball per pair or small group sharing a hoop, with hoops spaced at least 3m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Elbow in, eyes on the target, ball resting on your fingertips not your palm</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider stance, more time to set up</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker set-up, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the shooting stance: feet shoulder-width apart, ball held above the head resting on fingertips (not the palm), elbow tucked in, eyes on the target.</li><li>Students practise setting up this stance repeatedly without shooting yet, checking their own hand and foot position each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'elbow in, eyes on the target, ball resting on your fingertips not your palm.'</li><li>Have partners check each other's stance and give quick feedback before rotating.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider stance and allow more time to set up; for 'harder', require a quicker set-up, timed if possible.</li></ul><hr><h4>Soft Arc Shooting</h4><p>Students take shots from close range, focusing on a high, soft arc rather than a flat, hard shot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students in pairs or small groups near a hoop, shooting from close range (about 1.5-2m from the hoop), with hoops spaced at least 3m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Push up and slightly forward, follow through with your wrist</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer range, lower hoop if available</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further range, standard hoop height</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a shot with a high, soft arc rather than a flat, hard shot — pushing up and slightly forward with a wrist follow-through.</li><li>Students take turns shooting from close range, focusing purely on getting a high, soft arc rather than trying to shoot hard.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'push up and slightly forward, follow through with your wrist.'</li><li>Rebound and reset the ball for the next shooter quickly to keep turns coming often.</li><li>For 'easier', shoot from even closer range or use a lower hoop if available; for 'harder', shoot from further out at standard hoop height.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shot Count Challenge</h4><p>Small groups take turns shooting from a marked spot, counting clean makes out of 10 attempts, focusing on consistent technique rather than power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 taking turns shooting from a marked spot near the hoop, counting clean makes out of 10 attempts. Hoops spaced at least 3m apart between groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Repeat the same technique every time — consistency beats trying something new each shot</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer distance</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further distance</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each student takes 10 shots in a row from the same marked spot, with the group counting clean makes out loud.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'repeat the same technique every time — consistency beats trying something new each shot.'</li><li>Group members retrieve the ball quickly for the shooter to keep the pace of attempts high.</li><li>Record each student's score out of 10 if useful for tracking progress across the block.</li><li>For 'easier', shoot from a closer distance; for 'harder', shoot from further away.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Shooting Circle Netball</h3><p>Small-sided game with a designated shooting circle — only players in the circle may shoot, with the rest of the team focused on getting the ball there through good passing and movement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games on a court with one marked shooting circle at each end. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: only players inside the marked shooting circle may attempt a shot at the hoop.</li><li>The rest of the team focuses on moving the ball toward the circle through good passing and off-the-ball movement.</li><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided game, with a point scored for every clean shot made from inside the circle.</li><li>Encourage non-shooting players to think about how to get the ball to a teammate in the circle rather than trying to shoot from outside it.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time, including time inside the circle.</li><li>Keep the pace lively — this is the first game where shooting itself is the scoring method.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a balanced stance do to help your shooting accuracy?</li><li>How did a soft arc compare to a flat, hard shot?</li><li>What technique will you focus on next time you're shooting?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds a balanced stance with the ball above the head</li><li>Releases with fingers, not the palm</li><li>Shot has a soft, controlled arc</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a lower hoop or closer distance for students still building arm strength; celebrate good technique even when shots don't go in.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Pressure Shooting":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting under defensive pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding your own composure under pressure — noticing what helps you stay calm and make good decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, hoops/targets, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Defenders must maintain the correct marking distance and must not touch the ball during a shot attempt</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to maintain good shooting technique even with a defender marking us.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold my shooting technique steady with a defender nearby</li><li>I can decide whether to shoot or pass based on the pressure I'm under</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Dodge Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement that echoes the dodging focus from Block 2.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the same shooting technique regardless of the defender's presence.</td><td>Changing technique under pressure is what causes inconsistency.</td></tr><tr><td>Use your body to shield the ball from the defender before shooting.</td><td>Protects your shooting opportunity within the legal defending distance.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay balanced and composed rather than rushing the shot.</td><td>A rushed shot under pressure is far less accurate than a composed one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Shot</h4><p>Shooter practises their technique with a shadow defender standing nearby (not intercepting), getting used to a presence without pressure on the shot itself.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs near a hoop, shooter shooting from a set spot with a shadow defender standing 1-2m away (hands-off, not intercepting). Hoops spaced at least 3m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your technique — don't rush just because a defender is there</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The shooter sets up their shot exactly as practised in Lesson 12, with the shadow defender standing nearby but not moving to block.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust your technique — don't rush just because a defender is there.'</li><li>The shooter takes their shot at their normal pace, focusing on maintaining the same stance and follow-through.</li><li>Rotate shooter and defender roles every few attempts.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the defender further away; for 'harder', let the defender stand closer while still not touching.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shoot or Pass Decision</h4><p>Shooter with the ball near the circle must decide whether to shoot immediately or pass to a better-positioned teammate, based on defensive pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3 near the shooting circle — one shooter with the ball, one open teammate, one defender applying light pressure. Spaced at least 3m from neighbouring groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A good pass to a clear shot beats a rushed shot under pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts immediately</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The player with the ball near the circle must decide, based on the defender's position, whether to shoot immediately or pass to the better-positioned teammate.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a good pass to a clear shot beats a rushed shot under pressure.'</li><li>The defender reacts to the ball-holder's decision, trying to close down whichever option looks likeliest.</li><li>Rotate which player has the ball, which is open, and which defends every few reps.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender react slowly; for 'harder', have the defender react immediately to force a quick decision.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Shot Count</h4><p>Small groups take shots with a live defender marking (correct distance, no contact), counting clean makes under real pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 near a hoop, with a live defender marking the shooter at the correct distance (no contact). Hoops spaced at least 3m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stick to your technique from Lesson 12 — pressure doesn't change good form</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer defenders, more space</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full defensive pressure</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The shooter takes shots from a marked spot while the defender marks at the correct distance without touching the ball.</li><li>The group counts clean makes out loud, same as the Shot Count Challenge but now under real defensive pressure.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stick to your technique from Lesson 12 — pressure doesn't change good form.'</li><li>Rotate the shooter and defender roles regularly so everyone shoots under pressure.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer defenders and more space; for 'harder', apply full defensive pressure.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pressure Shooting Netball</h3><p>4v4 with a shooting circle and live defence — shooters are coached to maintain technique and make good shoot-or-pass decisions under realistic defensive pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 games on a court with a marked shooting circle at each end and live defence. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with shooting allowed only from inside the marked circle, defenders marking at the correct distance without contact.</li><li>Coach shooters explicitly to maintain their technique from Lesson 12 and to make good shoot-or-pass decisions under realistic pressure.</li><li>The coach highlights good decisions out loud during play — both smart shots taken and smart passes made instead of a rushed shot.</li><li>Reinforce the safety rule throughout: defenders must maintain the correct marking distance and must not touch the ball during a shot attempt.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time, including time inside the circle.</li><li>Keep encouraging composure — a calm, well-formed shot beats a rushed one every time.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What changed about your shot when a defender was there?</li><li>How did you decide whether to shoot or pass under pressure?</li><li>What helped you stay calm and controlled while defended?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Maintains shooting technique under mild defensive pressure</li><li>Makes a sensible shoot-or-pass decision</li><li>Stays composed rather than rushing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce defensive intensity (bigger marking distance, slower defender) for students still building shooting confidence before adding full pressure.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Know Your Third":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Understanding space — thirds of the court</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Being fair and honest about staying within the boundaries of your role, even when no one is strictly checking.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking thirds, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep movement controlled when transitioning between thirds — no sprinting through others' space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning how the netball court is divided into thirds and what that means for where we can play.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can name the three thirds of the court and where my position is allowed to go</li><li>I can move into the correct third at the correct time during play</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Dodge Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement that echoes the dodging focus from Block 2.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know which third you're allowed to play in for your position.</td><td>Netball's court-thirds rule restricts where each position can go.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay aware of the ball's position relative to your third.</td><td>Helps you anticipate when you'll become involved in play.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with teammates about court areas.</td><td>Reduces confusion about who covers which space.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Third Spotter</h4><p>Students walk/jog around a marked court, calling out which third they're standing in as the coach/app calls out different thirds to move to.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class (or half at a time) on a court marked into three equal thirds using cones along two lines, roughly 15m x 10m total space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know the boundary lines before the game starts — check them with your feet</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, clearer third markers</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More subtle markers, faster calls</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk the group along the court first, showing exactly where each third's boundary line is.</li><li>Students walk or jog around the marked court; the coach or app calls out a third (e.g. 'centre third!') and students move into that third and call out where they are.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know the boundary lines before the game starts — check them with your feet.'</li><li>Continue calling different thirds so students build a mental map of the space.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer, clearer third markers; for 'harder', use more subtle markers and call faster.</li></ul><hr><h4>Positional Boundary Game</h4><p>Small groups are assigned simplified positions (e.g. defence third only, centre third only) and practise passing the ball while respecting their thirds boundary.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 assigned to a single third (e.g. defence third only, centre third only), with one ball per group and thirds clearly marked with cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay aware of your positional boundary even while focused on the ball</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positions, more space</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More positions, tighter boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each group a simplified position tied to one third of the court and explain they must stay within that third's boundary while playing.</li><li>Groups pass the ball freely among themselves, staying inside their assigned third throughout.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stay aware of your positional boundary even while focused on the ball.'</li><li>Rotate which third each group is assigned to every few minutes so everyone experiences a different third.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer positions with more space; for 'harder', use more positions with tighter boundaries.</li></ul><hr><h4>Transition Third Relay</h4><p>Ball is passed down the court through all three thirds in sequence, with players entering and leaving the play as the ball crosses each boundary line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class on a marked court divided into three thirds, with groups of 3-4 students assigned to each third at the start. One ball for the whole relay.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with teammates about who's allowed in which third right now</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, walked through</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed race</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the ball must be passed down the court through all three thirds in sequence, from defence third to centre third to attack third.</li><li>As the ball crosses each boundary line, the players from that third step out and the players from the next third take over, so the ball is always in the hands of players allowed in that zone.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate with teammates about who's allowed in which third right now.'</li><li>Run the sequence there and back, timing how quickly the ball can travel down and back through all three thirds cleanly.</li><li>For 'easier', walk through the sequence slowly; for 'harder', run it at a faster pace and time it as a race.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Thirds-Aware Netball</h3><p>Small-sided game on a marked court where players are gently reminded of simplified positional boundaries (without full 7-a-side rules yet), building court awareness ahead of full positional play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games on a court marked into three thirds. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a small-sided game on the marked court, gently reminding players of the simplified positional boundaries as they play — this isn't full 7-a-side rules yet.</li><li>The coach reminds players which third they're currently allowed to be active in, without stopping play for long explanations.</li><li>Encourage players to stay generally aware of the thirds while still focusing on passing, moving, and scoring.</li><li>This game builds court awareness ahead of the full positional play introduced in Lesson 15.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the tone light — the goal is familiarity with the thirds, not strict enforcement yet.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about how the thirds shaped where the ball could go?</li><li>Why might a netball court be divided into thirds like this?</li><li>What was tricky about staying aware of your third while playing?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Can name the three thirds of the court</li><li>Moves into the correct third at appropriate times</li><li>Shows awareness of positional boundaries during play</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use highly visible markers (different coloured cones or chalk lines) for students who find spatial boundaries hard to judge.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Find Your Spot":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Positional roles &amp; where to stand</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C (Relationships with Other People) · Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Valuing every position on the team equally — a defender is just as important as a shooter, and everyone's contribution matters.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs marking positions, cones marking court areas</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Rotate positions regularly so no student is stuck in one role for the whole lesson</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning about the different positions in netball and where each one is allowed to play on the court.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can name at least 2 netball positions and where they're allowed to go</li><li>I can move to the correct area of the court for my assigned position</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Dodge Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement that echoes the dodging focus from Block 2.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in shooting and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know your position's typical starting area and role.</td><td>Understanding your job helps you make faster decisions in games.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your positioning based on where the ball is.</td><td>Positions aren't static — they shift with the flow of play within the allowed areas.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate your position or intentions to teammates.</td><td>Reduces overlaps and gaps in team shape.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Position Carousel</h4><p>In small groups, students rotate through simplified positions (e.g. shooter, centre, defender) every few minutes in a continuous small game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 on a small court roughly 12m x 8m, with bibs marking 3 simplified positions (e.g. shooter, centre, defender). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know what your current position is asking of you before the ball arrives</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer rotations, simpler positions</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter rotations, more positions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each student a starting simplified position using bibs, and explain each position's basic job for this game.</li><li>Play a continuous small-sided game where students rotate through the simplified positions every few minutes on a call.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know what your current position is asking of you before the ball arrives.'</li><li>When rotating, swap bibs quickly so play can resume with minimal stoppage.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer rotations with simpler positions; for 'harder', use shorter rotations with more positions to learn.</li></ul><hr><h4>Position Walkthrough</h4><p>Coach/app sets up a simple team structure and the team walks through where each position should stand as the ball moves around the court.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole team (5-7 students) on a marked court, bibs showing each assigned simplified position, walking pace only, no defenders yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk to your teammates about where you're going, not just where you are</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positions, walked through slowly</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More positions, at jogging pace</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up a simple team structure with each student in their assigned position and bib.</li><li>Walk the team slowly through where each position should stand and move to as the ball is passed around the court.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'talk to your teammates about where you're going, not just where you are.'</li><li>Pause and reset if a player drifts out of their positional area, showing them where to return to.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer positions and walk through slowly; for 'harder', use more positions and move at a light jogging pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Find Your Space Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where each player is assigned a position for that game only; points awarded when a player is found in the correct positional area when the ball arrives near them.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 6-8 on a court with positional areas marked by cones, bibs assigned per position. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A position isn't standing still — it's being in the right space at the right time</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positional boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full positional boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each player a position for the game only, with a bib and a marked area they're expected to be in.</li><li>Play flows as a small-sided game; a point is awarded whenever a player is found standing in their correct positional area at the moment the ball arrives near them.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a position isn't standing still — it's being in the right space at the right time.'</li><li>The coach or a nominated caller watches for correct positioning and calls out points as they happen.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer positional boundaries; for 'harder', enforce full positional boundaries strictly.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Positional Small-Sided Netball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with simplified assigned positions — teams are coached to recognise how their individual position contributes to the team's overall shape and success.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8-10 for 4v4 or 5v5 games on a full or near-full court, bibs assigned per simplified position. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign simplified positions to every player using bibs before starting, matching the positions practised in today's Skill Games.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with each player generally staying within their positional responsibility.</li><li>The coach highlights moments where a player's position clearly helped the team — e.g. a defender covering their zone, or a shooter staying ready in the circle.</li><li>Coach teams explicitly to recognise how their individual position contributes to the team's overall shape and success.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions every few minutes so everyone experiences more than one role.</li><li>Keep reminding players that positions are about being in the right space at the right time, not standing still.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did your position ask you to do differently from a teammate's position?</li><li>How did understanding positions help the team play better together?</li><li>What was it like trying a position you don't usually play?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Can describe their assigned position in simple terms</li><li>Positions themselves appropriately for their role</li><li>Supports and communicates with teammates in different positions</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a simplified two-position version (attacker/defender) for students who find multiple positions overwhelming, building up to full structure over the lesson.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Centre Stage":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Centre pass strategy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Being patient with a team while learning a multi-person pattern — timing takes a few tries to click for everyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking the centre third, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep the centre pass area clear of congestion — space players out to avoid collisions at the whistle</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a simple strategy for starting play well from the centre pass.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move into a good position to receive or support the centre pass</li><li>I can help my team plan a simple centre pass strategy</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Set up in planned starting positions for the centre pass.</td><td>A structured start creates better options than a random scramble.</td></tr><tr><td>Move immediately and with purpose once the whistle goes.</td><td>The centre pass has strict timing rules, so early, decisive movement matters.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate the planned play if your team has one.</td><td>Coordinated movement beats individuals guessing what happens next.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Centre Pass Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple centre pass pattern slowly: centre passes to a leading attacker, others move to support, before adding any defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole team (5-7 students) on the centre third of the court, no defenders yet, one ball per team, walk pace only.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone has a job on the centre pass — know yours before the whistle</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk pace only</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light jog</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up the team in their centre pass positions: one player taking the centre pass, one attacker leading, others in support positions.</li><li>Walk the team slowly through the pattern: the centre passes to the leading attacker while the rest of the team moves into supporting positions.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone has a job on the centre pass — know yours before the whistle.'</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times so every player is confident in their specific role.</li><li>For 'easier', keep it at walk pace only; for 'harder', add a light jog to the pattern.</li></ul><hr><h4>Centre Pass vs Shadow Defence</h4><p>Team practises their centre pass pattern against shadow defenders (not intercepting) to get used to defensive presence without losing the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole team on the centre third with shadow defenders from another group standing near their marks (hands-off, not intercepting). One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stick to the plan even with defenders nearby — trust your teammates' movement</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders closer, more realistic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The team runs their centre pass pattern from Centre Pass Walkthrough exactly as practised, now with defenders standing nearby.</li><li>Defenders mark their positions but do not intercept or move to block, simply adding a realistic visual presence.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'stick to the plan even with defenders nearby — trust your teammates' movement.'</li><li>Repeat the pattern several times, encouraging the team to stay composed rather than panicking at the sight of a defender.</li><li>For 'easier', have defenders stand further back; for 'harder', have defenders stand closer for a more realistic look.</li></ul><hr><h4>Centre Pass Race</h4><p>Groups practise executing a clean centre pass and first two passes as fast and cleanly as possible, racing another group for the fastest clean sequence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 5-7 running their centre pass pattern side by side in separate areas, timed by the coach or a stopwatch. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate clearly who's taking the first pass</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, timed</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team lines up in their centre pass positions, ready to execute the pattern as cleanly and quickly as possible.</li><li>On 'go', teams execute their centre pass and the first two passes of their pattern, aiming for a clean, error-free sequence.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate clearly who's taking the first pass' to avoid confusion at the start.</li><li>The fastest team to complete a clean sequence (no drops, no confusion) wins that round; run several rounds.</li><li>For 'easier', run at a slower pace; for 'harder', run at full pace and time every round.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Centre Pass Netball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — every restart uses a real centre pass; teams are encouraged to run their planned pattern each time, aiming to beat a drifting or disorganised defence early in the phase.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8-10 for 4v4 or 5v5 games on a full or near-full court with live defence. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: every restart of play (after a score, or after any stoppage) uses a real centre pass, run exactly as practised.</li><li>Teams are encouraged to run their planned pattern every time the centre pass restarts, rather than improvising.</li><li>The defending team tries to organise quickly to disrupt the pattern, giving attackers a real incentive to execute cleanly and quickly.</li><li>Coach the key message during play: run the plan, communicate clearly, and trust your teammates' movement.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Celebrate clean, well-organised centre pass sequences that beat a disorganised or slow-to-set defence.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your centre pass pattern successful (or not) against a defender today?</li><li>How did planning ahead help your team start well?</li><li>What's the purpose of having a plan for the centre pass?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves into a planned position for the centre pass</li><li>Communicates clearly with teammates before the whistle</li><li>Executes the pattern with reasonable timing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify the pattern to just two players (centre + one target) for teams still building confidence before adding more players to the plan.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Attack Patterns":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking play patterns</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your decisions within the pattern affect whether the whole team's play succeeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep attacking patterns within a marked area so groups don't collide with each other</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning simple attacking patterns that help our team move the ball toward the shooting circle.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run a simple attacking pattern with my team</li><li>I can adjust the pattern based on what the defence is doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Learn and follow your team's planned movement pattern.</td><td>Patterns create better space and options than random attacking.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust the pattern when the defence covers the first option.</td><td>Real games require adapting a plan, not abandoning it at the first sign of resistance.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate during the pattern so everyone stays connected.</td><td>A silent pattern breaks down easily under pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pattern Walkthrough</h4><p>Team walks through a simple attacking pattern (e.g. pass-and-move into the circle) slowly with cone markers before adding pace or defence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 within a marked area, cone markers laid out to show the attacking pattern (e.g. pass-and-move into the circle), no defenders yet. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your role in the pattern before you start moving</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players, simpler pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more complex pattern</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk the group slowly through the pattern using the cone markers as a guide — who passes first, who moves where, who ends up taking the shot.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your role in the pattern before you start moving.'</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times at walking pace until every player knows their part without needing reminders.</li><li>Once confident, add a light jog to the same pattern.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players and a simpler pattern; for 'harder', use more players and a more complex pattern.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pattern vs Shadow Defence</h4><p>Team runs their attacking pattern against shadow defenders (not intercepting) to build confidence with defensive presence nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups as Pattern Walkthrough, with shadow defenders from another group standing near their marks (hands-off, not intercepting). One ball per group, marked area at least 3m from neighbouring groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders further back</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders closer, more realistic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The group runs their attacking pattern exactly as walked through, now with defenders standing nearby to add visual pressure.</li><li>Defenders mark their positions but do not intercept or move to block, keeping the focus on the attacking team's execution.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust the pattern — don't abandon it the moment a defender appears.'</li><li>Repeat the pattern several times so the team gets comfortable running it with defenders present.</li><li>For 'easier', have defenders stand further back; for 'harder', have defenders stand closer for realism.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read &amp; Adjust</h4><p>Team runs their pattern, but must adjust on the fly if a defender blocks the planned option, practising a backup plan.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups, with one active (but hands-off) defender who can block the planned passing option. One ball per group, marked area at least 3m from neighbouring groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Unpredictable defensive block</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The team starts running their pattern; the defender positions themselves to block the first planned option (e.g. stands in the passing lane).</li><li>When the first option is blocked, the team must adjust and use a simple backup option instead of forcing the original plan.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'have a simple backup option in mind if your first choice is covered.'</li><li>Rotate the defender role every few reps so everyone experiences reading and adjusting under pressure.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender block in a predictable way; for 'harder', have the defender block unpredictably.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Attacking Pattern Netball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with full defence — attacking teams are encouraged to run at least one planned pattern per possession, adjusting when the defence covers their first option.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8-10 for 4v4 or 5v5 games on a full or near-full court with full live defence. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with full defensive pressure allowed (hands-off, correct marking distance).</li><li>Attacking teams are encouraged to run at least one planned pattern per possession, drawing on today's Skill Games.</li><li>When the defence covers the first option, attackers should adjust to a backup option rather than forcing a blocked pass.</li><li>The coach highlights good examples of teams running a pattern and good examples of teams adjusting smartly when defended.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep encouraging composure and communication as teams try their patterns under real game pressure.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your attacking pattern successful (or not) against real defence today?</li><li>How did you adjust when your first option was covered?</li><li>What's the value of having a plan, even if you sometimes need to change it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Runs a recognisable attacking pattern with teammates</li><li>Adjusts the pattern when the defence covers the first option</li><li>Communicates with teammates during the pattern</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Walk the pattern through cone markers first for teams who need a visual/spatial reference before adding a live defender.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Team Wall":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defensive team strategy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Thinking about how your individual defensive choices (helping or not helping) affect the whole team's structure.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep defensive movement controlled — no sprinting into others' space; communication should be clear, not aggressive shouting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend as a connected team, not just as individuals marking their own player.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can communicate with my defensive teammates about where the ball and attackers are</li><li>I can adjust my defensive position based on what my teammates are doing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly about who's marking whom.</td><td>Team defence relies on clear roles, updated in real time.</td></tr><tr><td>Cover for a teammate who's beaten, then recover to your own player.</td><td>Helping without abandoning your own responsibility keeps the defence solid.</td></tr><tr><td>Move as a connected unit, not as isolated defenders.</td><td>Gaps appear where defenders aren't working together.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call the Danger</h4><p>Defensive team practises calling out loudly when an attacker is dangerous (open, about to receive, or about to shoot) so teammates can help cover.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 defenders working together against 3-4 attackers on a marked court roughly 12m x 10m. One ball, bibs for attackers.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less obvious danger</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Defenders practise calling out loudly and clearly whenever an attacker looks dangerous — open, about to receive a pass, or about to shoot.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call early and specifically — name who and what you're seeing,' e.g. 'watch the shooter, she's open!'</li><li>Attackers move and pass as normal while defenders focus purely on communication rather than intercepting.</li><li>Rotate which students are defending versus attacking every few minutes.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more obvious danger for defenders to spot and call; for 'harder', use faster, less obvious danger.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cover &amp; Recover</h4><p>One defender practises leaving their direct mark briefly to cover a bigger danger, while a teammate calls out and helps recover the original mark.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 defenders against 3-4 attackers on a marked court, one designated defender practising the cover-and-recover movement at a time. One ball, bibs for attackers.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time to communicate</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, quick decisions</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One defender briefly leaves their direct mark to cover a bigger danger elsewhere on the court (e.g. an unmarked shooter).</li><li>A teammate calls out clearly and steps in to cover the original mark that's now been left open.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'communicate the swap clearly so no attacker is left completely open.'</li><li>Rotate which defender practises the cover-and-recover movement so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more time to communicate; for 'harder', use a faster pace requiring quick decisions.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Defence Grid</h4><p>Small-sided game where the defending team's only job is to communicate and cover for each other, with the coach highlighting good team defensive moments.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 8 (4 attackers, 4 defenders) in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball, bibs for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Defence is a team job, not just your own player</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer attackers, easier to cover</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full attacking numbers</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the defending team's only job in this game is to communicate and cover for each other — there's no separate attacking focus today.</li><li>Play flows as a small-sided game with defenders focusing purely on calling danger and covering gaps as a team.</li><li>The coach highlights good team defensive moments out loud as they happen, naming what made the cover or call effective.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'defence is a team job, not just your own player.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer attackers, making it easier to cover; for 'harder', use the full attacking numbers.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Defence Netball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — defending team is coached explicitly on communication and covering for each other, while attackers try to exploit any gaps in the defensive structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8-10 for 4v4 or 5v5 games on a full or near-full court with full live defence. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with the defending team explicitly coached to communicate and cover for each other throughout.</li><li>Attackers try to exploit any gaps that open up in the defensive structure, especially where defenders forget to communicate a cover.</li><li>The coach highlights strong team defensive moments and gently points out moments where a call or cover was missed.</li><li>Coach the key message during play: 'defence is a team job, not just your own player.'</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep communication calm and clear rather than aggressive shouting, consistent with today's safety note.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your team's defence effective (or not) today?</li><li>Why is communication important for a defensive team, not just individual marking?</li><li>How did covering for a teammate help your team defend better?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Communicates clearly with defensive teammates</li><li>Covers for a teammate when needed</li><li>Recovers to their original mark after helping</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole game to walking pace for students still learning the team defence concept before adding jogging speed.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Position Swap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full small-sided games with position rotation</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Bringing everything together fairly — playing hard, playing safe, and playing for the team, not just for yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs marking positions, cones for court boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Full positional rules apply — reinforce the footwork rule and marking distance before starting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything we've practised across a full small-sided game, rotating through different positions.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply at least one tactic we've learned in a live game situation</li><li>I can adapt my play when I rotate into a new position</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply your position's specific job wherever you're rotated to.</td><td>Rotating positions tests whether you understand the role, not just memorised movements.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate your new position clearly after rotating.</td><td>Helps teammates adjust their own play around you.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust quickly to a new position's space and rules.</td><td>Netball's court-thirds rules change depending on which position you're in.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Tactic Spotlight Rounds</h4><p>Short rounds of small-sided games where each round has a spotlighted tactic (e.g. 'today's focus: use your lead timing') that the coach/app calls out and looks for in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 8-10 on a full or near-full court, playing short rounds (e.g. 3-4 minutes each) with a new spotlighted tactic called at the start of each round. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Try the spotlighted tactic even if it doesn't work perfectly the first time</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer rounds, simpler tactic focus</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter rounds, combined tactics</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before each round, the coach or app calls out a spotlighted tactic drawn from earlier in the unit (e.g. 'today's focus: use your lead timing').</li><li>Teams play the round trying to consciously apply the spotlighted tactic whenever the opportunity arises.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'try the spotlighted tactic even if it doesn't work perfectly the first time.'</li><li>At the end of each round, briefly ask a couple of students where they used the tactic before starting the next round with a new focus.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer rounds with a simpler tactic focus; for 'harder', use shorter rounds with combined tactics called together.</li></ul><hr><h4>Coach's Freeze &amp; Reset</h4><p>During live small-sided games, the coach/app occasionally calls 'freeze' to briefly discuss the decision just made, then play resumes from that point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games of 8-10 on a full or near-full court, played continuously with the coach ready to pause play at key moments. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use the freeze as a learning moment, not a criticism</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer freezes, longer play periods</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More frequent freezes</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided game; the coach occasionally calls 'freeze!' at an interesting moment.</li><li>When frozen, briefly discuss the decision that was just made — what the options were and what happened — with the players involved.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use the freeze as a learning moment, not a criticism.'</li><li>Resume play immediately from exactly where it froze once the brief discussion is done.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer freezes with longer play periods in between; for 'harder', use more frequent freezes.</li></ul><hr><h4>Position Rotation Game</h4><p>Small-sided games where players rotate positions every few minutes, applying full rules and whichever tactics suit their current role.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games of 8-10 on a full or near-full court, bibs marking assigned positions that rotate on a timer. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk to your team between rotations about what worked</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer periods per position, fewer rotations</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter periods, more rotations</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each player a starting position with a bib, applying full rules learned across the unit.</li><li>Play continuously, rotating players through different positions every few minutes on a call.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'talk to your team between rotations about what worked' in their previous position.</li><li>Encourage players to apply whichever tactics suit their current role once they rotate.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer periods per position with fewer rotations; for 'harder', use shorter periods with more frequent rotations.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full-Rules Small-Sided Netball</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5, full simplified netball rules (footwork, marking distance, thirds) — teams are encouraged to consciously apply tactics from across the unit (dodge/lead, pass-and-move, team defence, patterns) in genuine competitive play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8-10 for 4v4 or 5v5 games on a full or near-full court, full simplified netball rules in play (footwork, marking distance, thirds). One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's game brings together every rule and tactic covered across the unit: footwork, marking distance, thirds, dodge/lead, pass-and-move, team defence, and attacking patterns.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game under full simplified rules, encouraging teams to consciously apply tactics from across the unit whenever they fit.</li><li>The coach highlights moments where a specific tactic from an earlier lesson clearly helped a team, naming it out loud.</li><li>Keep enforcing the core rules (footwork, marking distance) consistently, since this is full-rules play.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Use this as the final tune-up before tomorrow's tournament, keeping the tone encouraging and game-like.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What tactic did you try today, and how did it go?</li><li>What was different about playing a new position?</li><li>What's one thing your team could work on next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies at least one taught tactic recognisably in live play</li><li>Adjusts their play appropriately for a new position</li><li>Communicates with teammates during live play</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a slightly reduced pace or smaller-sided game (e.g. 3v3) for groups still building confidence with full rules before scaling to 5v5.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Netball Cup":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C &amp; D · All key competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Celebrating the whole team's effort and growth across the unit — thinking about how everyone's contribution, not just the scoreline, made the tournament work.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs (multiple colours for teams), cones, hoops/targets, a simple ladder/scoreboard</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Manage fatigue with rotations/subs; reinforce footwork and marking distance rules before the tournament begins</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills in a fun, competitive mini-tournament format.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full game applying skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>I can be a good teammate and good sport across multiple games</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call, echoing the team positioning focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills and positional understanding from across the unit.</td><td>The tournament brings every part of the unit together under real competitive pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates throughout.</td><td>Netball's fast pace relies heavily on constant, clear communication.</td></tr><tr><td>Play fairly within the rules you've learned.</td><td>Understanding and respecting the rules is part of playing netball well.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Warm-In Circuit</h4><p>A short rotating circuit revisiting the unit's key skills (pass &amp; catch, footwork, dodge &amp; lead, shoot, defend) as a fun warm-in before the tournament starts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>5 short stations set up around the space (pass &amp; catch, footwork, dodge &amp; lead, shoot, defend), each with the equipment needed (balls, cones, hoops), groups of 3-4 rotating through together.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every station</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer stations, fewer skills</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter stations, all skills</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain each station briefly before starting: pass &amp; catch, footwork, dodge &amp; lead, shoot, and defend, each revisiting a key skill from across the unit.</li><li>Groups spend a short, timed period at each station (e.g. 1-2 minutes) before rotating to the next on a signal.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'bring your best technique from across the unit into every station.'</li><li>Keep the mood light and energetic — this is a warm-in, not a formal assessment.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer stations covering fewer skills; for 'harder', use shorter stations covering all five skills quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Huddle</h4><p>Each team spends 2 minutes with their 'captain' (rotating role) planning one simple tactic to try in their first game — a light strategy touchpoint before playing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each tournament team gathers in their own huddle area for 2 minutes before their first game, with a rotating 'captain' role for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests the tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team chooses their own tactic</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team nominates or rotates in a 'captain' for this tournament session.</li><li>The captain leads a brief 2-minute huddle, helping the team agree on one simple tactic to try in their first game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.'</li><li>Teams head straight into their first game once the huddle finishes, trying to apply their chosen tactic early.</li><li>For 'easier', have the coach suggest the tactic; for 'harder', have the team choose their own tactic independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fair Play Check-In</h4><p>Brief between-games moment where teams acknowledge one good play from their opponent, reinforcing sportsmanship as part of the tournament structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Brief moment between games where each team gathers for under a minute, no equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific — name what your opponent did well</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Between games, gather each team briefly and ask them to name one good play their opponent made during the game just finished.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be specific — name what your opponent did well,' rather than a generic compliment.</li><li>Keep this check-in short so it doesn't eat into playing time, then move straight into the next game.</li><li>Repeat this fair play check-in after every game throughout the tournament.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Netball Cup — Round Robin</h3><p>Full simplified netball rules, small-sided round-robin mini-tournament (4v4 or 5v5) across several short games; ladder/results tracked lightly and playfully, with every team guaranteed multiple games regardless of results.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class split into teams of 4-6 for a round-robin mini-tournament across the full or near-full court, using bibs of multiple colours and a simple ladder/scoreboard visible to everyone. Full simplified netball rules apply.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: every team plays several short games against different opponents, with results tracked lightly on the ladder/scoreboard.</li><li>Run each game under full simplified rules (footwork, marking distance, thirds), keeping game lengths short so every team gets multiple games.</li><li>Between games, run the Fair Play Check-In quickly so it doesn't eat into playing time.</li><li>Update the ladder/scoreboard playfully after each round, keeping the tone fun and low-pressure regardless of results.</li><li>Guarantee every team gets to play multiple games, adjusting the schedule on the fly if a team finishes early.</li><li>Manage fatigue by rotating players on and off within each team's games, and remind teams of the footwork and marking distance rules before each game starts.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from across the whole unit you felt proudest of today?</li><li>How did your team work together across multiple games?</li><li>What would you want to work on if we played another netball unit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a recognisable range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Shows good sportsmanship across wins and losses</li><li>Encourages and includes teammates throughout the tournament</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure team sizes/formats can flex on the day for uneven numbers; emphasise participation and effort in any informal recognition, not just results.</p>"}}}}},"Rippa Rugby":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Ball Skills (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Cradle Carry":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Ball handling — carrying, passing basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while learning to handle a new shape of ball, and encouraging others (not laughing at drops) as everyone's hands look different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 rippa/junior rugby ball per pair, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passes below shoulder height; spread pairs out with at least 3m between grids</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to carry the ball securely in two hands and pass it accurately to a teammate.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can carry the ball in two hands, held away from my body ('long arms')</li><li>I can pass the ball sideways or backward with a smooth, two-handed action</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Two-Hand Carry Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own rippa/junior rugby ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-hand carry position briefly: ball held away from the body, elbows out, eyes up.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space carrying their own ball.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'pass to the nearest person' (a quick pass and receive, then keep jogging), 'speed up' (jog faster briefly), or 'slow down' (ease right off).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the goal is raising heart rates and getting comfortable holding the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Carry the ball in both hands, close to the body.</td><td>Two hands ready to pass at any moment, and holding it close prevents it being knocked loose.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball above waist height when carrying.</td><td>Makes for a faster, easier pass without needing to adjust your grip first.</td></tr><tr><td>Pass side-on with a flat trajectory, following through toward the receiver.</td><td>A flat, accurate pass is easier to catch in stride than a looping one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Long Arms Carry</h4><p>Free carry around a grid, ball held in two hands away from the body. On 'stop!', players pass to the nearest teammate and keep moving.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out a grid roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students using 4 cones per corner. Every student needs one rippa/junior rugby ball. No partners needed for this game — everyone carries their own ball inside the shared grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Elbows out, ball away from the chest, eyes up.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the carry position first: ball held in two hands, arms away from the body ('long arms'), elbows out, eyes up looking around rather than down at the ball.</li><li>On 'go', every student jogs freely around the grid in their own space, practising the carry.</li><li>Call 'stop!' at random intervals — on this call, each student must immediately find the nearest teammate and pass the ball to them, then both keep moving.</li><li>There's no scoring — the focus is on the quality of the carry position and a clean, controlled pass, not speed.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'elbows out, ball away from the chest, eyes up' — call this out as students carry past you.</li><li>For 'easier', widen the grid and call 'stop!' less often; for 'harder', shrink the grid slightly and call 'stop!' every few seconds so students are always ready to pass.</li></ul><hr><h4>Standing Pass Lines</h4><p>Partners face each other 3m apart and pass the ball backward/sideways with two hands; after every clean catch, the passer takes one step back.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand facing each other 3m apart, with enough space between pairs (at least 2m) that passes don't cross into another pair's lane. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Soft hands — 'give' with the catch instead of stabbing at the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stay at a fixed, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Take two steps back each time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Movement penalty/reward</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-handed backward/sideways pass: hands soft, ball released with a smooth push rather than a throw.</li><li>Partners begin passing back and forth from the starting 3m distance.</li><li>After every clean catch, the passer takes one step further back before passing again, gradually increasing the distance.</li><li>If a pass is dropped or off target, the pair resets to their current distance and tries again rather than starting all the way back at 3m.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'soft hands — give with the catch instead of stabbing at the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep the pair at a fixed, closer distance throughout; for 'harder', have them take two steps back after each clean catch instead of one.</li></ul><hr><h4>Circle Pass Race</h4><p>Groups of 5–6 stand in a circle and pass the ball around as fast as possible without dropping it, racing other circles for the fastest lap of 3.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 students form circles roughly 3-4m across, spaced well apart from other circles (at least 3m gap) so balls don't collide between groups. One ball per circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call your teammate's name before the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger circle, slower pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller circle, alternate pass direction on a call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the group must pass the ball all the way around the circle, in order, three times as fast as possible without dropping it.</li><li>On 'go', the student holding the ball starts passing to their neighbour in one direction and the group counts each completed lap out loud.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, the group picks it up and continues from that point — they don't restart the whole race.</li><li>The first circle to complete 3 clean laps calls out and is declared the winner; run several rounds so different circles get to win.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call your teammate's name before the ball arrives' so receivers are ready.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger circle and a slower pace; for 'harder', use a smaller circle and call an occasional direction-change to alternate which way the ball travels.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Continuous Pass Rugby</h3><p>4v4, walking/jogging pace, no tags yet. Teams try to complete as many clean passes as possible while moving toward the try line, scoring by grounding the ball over the line. Ball must be passed within 3 steps of receiving it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 8, forming two teams of 4 per game. Mark a small field roughly 20m x 15m with a try line (a row of cones) at each end. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rules before starting: walking or jogging pace only, no tags yet, and the ball must be passed within 3 steps of receiving it.</li><li>One team starts with the ball in the middle of the field; the other team spreads out to defend without touching or tackling.</li><li>Teams try to complete as many clean passes as possible while moving the ball toward their attacking try line.</li><li>A team scores by grounding the ball over the opposition's try line with a hand; after a score, the other team restarts with the ball from the halfway point.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or a pass goes forward, possession switches to the other team from that spot — restart play immediately with minimal fuss.</li><li>Rotate players through different positions on the field every few minutes so everyone experiences carrying, passing, and supporting.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you catch and carry the ball securely today?</li><li>What made a pass easy for your partner to catch?</li><li>What was tricky, and how did you keep trying?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Carries the ball in two hands away from the body</li><li>Passes with a smooth two-handed action, not a one-handed throw</li><li>Recovers quickly and keeps playing after a dropped ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a larger, softer ball or a slower pace for students newer to catching; pair confident carriers with less confident ones rather than same-ability pairs.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Flat & Back":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing accuracy (flat, backward pass)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding why the 'backward pass' rule exists in rugby, and noticing what your own hands and body are doing when you pass well.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passing lanes clear of other pairs; remind students to check space before releasing a pass</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball flat or backward so it's always legal and easy for a teammate to catch.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass so the ball travels level with or behind my teammate, never forward</li><li>I can adjust my pass depending on how fast my teammate is moving</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Two-Hand Carry Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own rippa/junior rugby ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-hand carry position briefly: ball held away from the body, elbows out, eyes up.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space carrying their own ball.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'pass to the nearest person' (a quick pass and receive, then keep jogging), 'speed up' (jog faster briefly), or 'slow down' (ease right off).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the goal is raising heart rates and getting comfortable holding the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Point your hands and follow through toward the target.</td><td>Aims the pass and keeps it flat rather than floating.</td></tr><tr><td>Release the ball slightly in front of the receiver's chest.</td><td>Lets them catch in stride without breaking speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Always pass backward or level, never forward.</td><td>This is the fundamental rule of rugby-style passing — a forward pass is an infringement.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Backward Gate Passing</h4><p>Pairs pass through a small cone gate placed level with or slightly behind the receiver, from increasing distances; a point is lost if the pass drifts forward.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand 3-4m apart with a small cone gate (two cones about 1m apart) placed level with or slightly behind the receiver. Space pairs at least 3m apart. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim the pass slightly behind a moving teammate so it arrives in their path, not ahead of them.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gate, players standing still.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gate, receiver jogging.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the target: the pass must travel through the gate and land level with or behind the receiver, never drifting in front of them.</li><li>Partners begin passing to each other, aiming each pass through their partner's gate.</li><li>A point is lost (or a coach calls it out) any time the pass drifts forward past the gate — reset and try again immediately, no long stoppage.</li><li>After every few successful passes through the gate, both partners take a step further apart to increase the distance.</li><li>Coach the key message: aim the pass slightly behind a moving teammate so it arrives in their path, not ahead of them.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider gate and keep both players standing still; for 'harder', narrow the gate and have the receiver jog gently as the pass arrives.</li></ul><hr><h4>Moving Line Pass</h4><p>A line of 4–5 players jogs across the field passing the ball along the line; every pass must travel backward relative to the direction of the run.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Lines of 4-5 players stand shoulder to width apart (roughly 2m gaps) at one end of the field, facing across it. One ball per line, cones marking the start and finish lines.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Run onto the pass rather than waiting for it, but never get ahead of the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Jog faster, add a second ball moving the other way.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate that every pass must travel backward relative to the direction the line is jogging — never forward.</li><li>The line begins jogging together across the field, starting with the ball at one end of the line.</li><li>Players pass the ball along the line one at a time as they jog, always releasing it backward or flat, never ahead of the receiver.</li><li>If a pass goes forward, the line stops, resets the ball with the player who made the error, and continues jogging from there.</li><li>Coach the key message: run onto the pass rather than waiting for it, but never get ahead of the ball.</li><li>For 'easier', have the line walk instead of jog; for 'harder', increase the jog pace or add a second ball moving the opposite way along the line.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Pass &amp; Move</h4><p>Groups of 3 form a triangle and complete 10 legal (backward/flat) passes as fast as possible, racing other triangles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 form a triangle with roughly 4-5m between each player, spaced well away from other triangles. One ball per triangle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your hands early — call for it before it arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller triangle.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger triangle, must alternate pass direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target: complete 10 legal (backward or flat) passes around the triangle as fast as possible.</li><li>On 'go', one player starts with the ball and the group begins passing, counting each legal pass out loud.</li><li>If a pass is forward or dropped, that pass doesn't count — the group keeps going without a long stoppage.</li><li>The first triangle to reach 10 legal passes calls out and wins that round; reset and run several rounds, racing other triangles each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: prepare your hands early — call for it before it arrives.</li><li>For 'easier', use a smaller triangle; for 'harder', use a bigger triangle and require players to alternate the direction the ball travels each round.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Continuous Pass Rugby v2</h3><p>As Lesson 1's big game, but coached and lightly scored on pass legality — any forward pass returns the ball to the other team, rewarding accurate backward/flat passing under a bit more pace.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same setup as Lesson 1's Continuous Pass Rugby: groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, a 20m x 15m field with a try line at each end, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the base rules from Lesson 1: walking/jogging pace, no tags, pass within 3 steps of receiving the ball.</li><li>Add today's focus: any forward pass results in an immediate turnover — the other team restarts from that spot with the ball.</li><li>One team starts with the ball at the halfway point; the other team spreads out to defend without tackling or touching.</li><li>Teams try to move the ball toward their try line using only backward or flat passes, scoring by grounding the ball over the line.</li><li>On a score or a forward-pass turnover, restart play quickly from that spot or the halfway line rather than pausing the game.</li><li>Coach pass legality actively during play, calling out 'good flat pass!' or 'that one drifted forward' so students connect today's focus to the live game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Why does a rugby pass have to go backward or flat, not forward?</li><li>What did you notice about timing your pass to a moving teammate?</li><li>What happens to the game when a pass goes forward?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Releases the ball level with or behind the receiver</li><li>Adjusts pass timing to the receiver's running speed</li><li>Recognises and self-corrects a forward pass</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students still building accuracy, allow a stationary receiver and a closer gate before adding movement.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Safe Hands":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Catching under pressure/movement</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills fairly to give your partner a realistic but manageable challenge — not throwing wildly just to catch them out.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passes should stay below head height; no diving for the ball — controlled movement only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to catch a moving ball cleanly while we ourselves are moving.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can catch the ball with two hands while jogging</li><li>I can get my hands ready ('target hands') before the ball arrives</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Two-Hand Carry Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own rippa/junior rugby ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-hand carry position briefly: ball held away from the body, elbows out, eyes up.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space carrying their own ball.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'pass to the nearest person' (a quick pass and receive, then keep jogging), 'speed up' (jog faster briefly), or 'slow down' (ease right off).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the goal is raising heart rates and getting comfortable holding the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Show target hands early, fingers spread.</td><td>Gives the passer a clear aiming point.</td></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball all the way into your hands.</td><td>Looking away early is the most common cause of a drop.</td></tr><tr><td>Catch and immediately secure the ball to the chest.</td><td>Reduces the chance of a defender knocking it loose right after the catch.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Target Hands Catch</h4><p>Partners jog side by side; the passer calls 'hands' before passing so the receiver presents target hands and catches without breaking stride.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners jog side by side, roughly 1m apart, with at least 3m between each pair. One ball per pair, open space with no cones needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball all the way into your hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Jog faster, no verbal call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'target hands' — receiver holds both hands up and slightly out to the side they want the ball, giving the passer a clear target before it's thrown.</li><li>Partners jog together side by side; the passer calls 'hands' just before passing so the receiver knows to present their target.</li><li>The receiver catches without breaking stride, watching the ball all the way in rather than looking at the passer.</li><li>Continue jogging and passing back and forth for the full activity, swapping who passes first every 30 seconds or so.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the ball all the way into your hands.</li><li>For 'easier', walk instead of jog; for 'harder', jog faster and drop the verbal 'hands' call so the receiver must anticipate the pass.</li></ul><hr><h4>Catch on the Move Relay</h4><p>Small groups jog down a channel passing and catching continuously, aiming to reach the far cone without a drop; if the ball is dropped, restart from that point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a channel roughly 15m long and 4m wide with cones. Small groups of 3-4 line up at one end, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reach forward slightly to meet the ball rather than waiting for it to arrive.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider channel, slower pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower channel, faster pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate reaching slightly forward to meet the ball as it arrives, rather than standing still and waiting for it.</li><li>The group jogs down the channel together, passing and catching continuously as they move toward the far cone.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, the group stops, picks it up from where it landed, and continues jogging and passing from that point — no restart from the beginning.</li><li>The group's goal is to reach the far cone without a drop; run several reps, timing the group each time if it helps motivation.</li><li>Coach the key message: reach forward slightly to meet the ball rather than waiting for it to arrive.</li><li>For 'easier', widen the channel and jog slower; for 'harder', narrow the channel and increase the pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Distraction Catch</h4><p>Receiver must catch a pass while a 'shadow' defender jogs alongside (not intercepting) to add visual pressure and distraction.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one 'shadow' defender per pair, jogging in an open space with at least 4m between groups. One ball per group of 3.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep eyes on the ball, not the defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close in as the pass is thrown.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the shadow defender's job: jog alongside the receiver without intercepting or touching, purely to add visual distraction.</li><li>The passer and receiver jog together as in Target Hands Catch, while the shadow jogs 1-2m to the side of the receiver.</li><li>The receiver must catch the pass while keeping their focus on the ball, not on the shadow moving beside them.</li><li>Rotate roles every few passes so each student gets a turn as passer, receiver, and shadow.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep eyes on the ball, not the defender.</li><li>For 'easier', have the shadow stay further away; for 'harder', let the shadow close in as the pass is thrown to add real pressure.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3-Pass Possession Rugby</h3><p>4v4, no tags, small field — every player must complete at least 3 clean catch-and-passes before their team can score, rewarding secure hands under game movement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, small field roughly 20m x 15m, no tags. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's rule addition: every player on the attacking team must complete at least 3 clean catch-and-passes before their team is allowed to score.</li><li>One team starts with the ball at the halfway point; the other team spreads out to defend without tackling.</li><li>Teams move the ball around, keeping a mental (or coach-tracked) tally of clean catches and passes completed by different players.</li><li>Once the 3-pass minimum has been met, the attacking team may ground the ball over the try line to score.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, restart immediately from that spot with the other team gaining possession — keep stoppages brief.</li><li>Coach calls out encouragement for secure hands under movement, e.g. 'great catch under pressure there!' to reinforce today's focus.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you catch cleanly while you were moving?</li><li>What did 'target hands' feel like when it worked well?</li><li>What will you focus on next time the ball comes to you at speed?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Presents hands early as a target</li><li>Catches with two hands, not trapping against the body</li><li>Keeps moving/jogging through the catch rather than stopping dead</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow players to slow to a walk for their first few catches before adding jogging speed; some students may prefer catching a slightly underinflated/softer ball first.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Jinking Feet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Evasion — footwork &amp; change of direction</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking about how your movement affects the flow of the game for everyone else sharing the space.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions in tight grids when changing direction — remind students to check space before cutting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to change direction quickly with the ball to create space away from a defender.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can plant my foot and change direction sharply while carrying the ball</li><li>I can accelerate away after a change of direction</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Two-Hand Carry Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own rippa/junior rugby ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-hand carry position briefly: ball held away from the body, elbows out, eyes up.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space carrying their own ball.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'pass to the nearest person' (a quick pass and receive, then keep jogging), 'speed up' (jog faster briefly), or 'slow down' (ease right off).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the goal is raising heart rates and getting comfortable holding the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Approach the defender at a controlled pace, not full speed.</td><td>Gives you the balance and control to change direction sharply.</td></tr><tr><td>Plant the outside foot and push off sharply to change direction.</td><td>This is what creates the sudden direction change that beats a defender.</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerate immediately after the change of direction.</td><td>The change of direction only works if you explode away from it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Cone Slalom Carry</h4><p>Individual ball carry through a zig-zag line of cones as fast as possible without losing control; race against your own best time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a zig-zag line of 6-8 cones, each about 2m apart, for each group of 4-5 students. One ball per student, with several parallel slalom lines if the class is large.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small, quick steps near the cone, longer strides in open space.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider cone spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower spacing/timed leaderboard.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate carrying the ball while weaving through the cones: small, quick steps close to each cone, longer strides in the open space between them.</li><li>Students line up at the start of their slalom and go one at a time, carrying their own ball through the zig-zag as fast as they can under control.</li><li>Encourage each student to race against their own best time on repeat attempts rather than comparing directly with others.</li><li>Once through the slalom, the student jogs back around the outside to rejoin the back of their line for another turn.</li><li>Coach the key message: small, quick steps near the cone, longer strides in open space.</li><li>For 'easier', space the cones further apart; for 'harder', narrow the spacing and keep an informal timed leaderboard.</li></ul><hr><h4>Number Call Cuts</h4><p>Students carry the ball freely; when their number is called, they must plant and cut sharply in a new direction before continuing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own ball and an assigned number (1-4 works well for a class of 20-24, in groups of 5-6 per number).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Plant the outside foot and drive off it explosively.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer numbers active at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two numbers called in quick sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each student a number before starting and explain that when their number is called, they must plant their outside foot and cut sharply into a new direction.</li><li>Students carry their ball freely around the space at a controlled jog.</li><li>Call out a number at random intervals — students with that number immediately plant and cut, then continue carrying in their new direction.</li><li>Remind students to check the space around them before cutting to avoid colliding with others.</li><li>Coach the key message: plant the outside foot and drive off it explosively.</li><li>For 'easier', have fewer numbers active in the space at once (e.g. split the class into two smaller groups); for 'harder', call two numbers in quick succession.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shrinking Grid Evade</h4><p>Whole class carries a ball each inside a grid that shrinks every 30 seconds; players use quick cuts to keep finding space (no elimination, just a challenge).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a large grid roughly 20m x 20m with cones for the whole class, each student carrying their own ball. Have a set of inner cones ready to bring the boundary in every 30 seconds.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Scan for the open space before you're boxed in.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shrink more slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shrink faster, add a 'space raider' calling out crowded zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain there's no elimination in this game — it's a continuous challenge to keep finding space as the grid gets smaller.</li><li>All students carry their ball freely inside the grid, using quick cuts and changes of direction to avoid crowding.</li><li>Every 30 seconds, move the cone boundary inward slightly to shrink the available space.</li><li>Students must keep scanning for open space and adjusting their movement as the grid tightens.</li><li>Coach the key message: scan for the open space before you're boxed in.</li><li>For 'easier', shrink the grid more slowly; for 'harder', shrink it faster and add a 'space raider' — a student (no ball) who calls out crowded zones for others to avoid.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: End Zone Evasion</h3><p>4v4, field split into thirds; ball carriers try to run the ball into the opposition's end zone using footwork to beat defenders (no tags yet — defenders must touch below the shoulder to stop play, then restart). Change of direction is essential through the middle third.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4. Field split into three even thirds (mark the two dividing lines with cones), roughly 25m long, with an end zone at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the focus: ball carriers use footwork to beat defenders, especially through the middle third where change of direction matters most.</li><li>One team starts with the ball at their end; defenders spread out across the thirds without tackling.</li><li>Defenders may only stop the ball carrier with a controlled touch below the shoulder — no grabbing or tackling — after which play restarts immediately with a pass from that spot.</li><li>The attacking team scores by carrying the ball into the opposition's end zone using cuts and changes of direction to beat defenders, especially in the middle third.</li><li>After a score or a touch-stoppage without a restart pass, reset possession quickly and keep the game flowing.</li><li>Coach calls out good footwork moments live, e.g. 'nice cut through the middle!' to connect today's skill to the game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a sharp change of direction do to help you find space?</li><li>Which direction (left or right cut) felt more natural to you?</li><li>What would you try differently next time you're being chased?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Plants foot firmly before cutting (not a slow drift)</li><li>Accelerates away after the change of direction</li><li>Keeps the ball secure in two hands through the cut</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students finding rapid changes hard, allow a wider, slower 'banana-shaped' cut rather than a single sharp plant-and-go.</p>"},"Lesson 5: The Step":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Evasion — using a step to beat a defender</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while learning a tricky new skill, and encouraging teammates whose steps don't come off yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for a channel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No shoulder contact — hands-off evasion only; stay upright, feet-only movement</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use a sidestep to beat a defender one-on-one.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can plant my outside foot and step past a defender</li><li>I can sell a fake direction before stepping the other way</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Two-Hand Carry Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, each student with their own rippa/junior rugby ball. No cones or grids needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-hand carry position briefly: ball held away from the body, elbows out, eyes up.</li><li>On 'go', everyone jogs freely around the space carrying their own ball.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, call a change: 'pass to the nearest person' (a quick pass and receive, then keep jogging), 'speed up' (jog faster briefly), or 'slow down' (ease right off).</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — the goal is raising heart rates and getting comfortable holding the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of calls.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Sell a subtle look or lean one way before stepping the other.</td><td>Unbalances the defender's weight in the wrong direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Plant firmly and push off low through the step.</td><td>A higher, softer step is slower and easier for a defender to react to.</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerate away immediately after the step.</td><td>The step only creates separation if you use it, not admire it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Step</h4><p>Ball carrier jogs down a channel; a 'shadow' defender stays 1.5m away and mirrors without touching, letting the carrier practise stepping past a nearby presence.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a channel roughly 15m long and 4m wide with cones. Pairs — one ball carrier, one shadow defender — one channel per pair, spaced well apart from other pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the defender's hips, not their head, to time the step.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stays further back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the shadow defender's job: stay about 1.5m away from the carrier and mirror their movement without touching, giving the carrier a safe presence to step past.</li><li>The ball carrier jogs down the channel while the shadow moves alongside, matching pace and direction.</li><li>The carrier practises a sidestep, watching the shadow's hips (not their head) to time the step past them.</li><li>Continue to the end of the channel, then swap roles and jog back for the next rep.</li><li>Coach the key message: look at the defender's hips, not their head, to time the step.</li><li>For 'easier', have the shadow stay further back; for 'harder', let the shadow move a little closer to add realistic pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Channel Step</h4><p>In a narrow channel, an attacker tries to get past a stationary-then-moving defender using a sidestep to reach the far cone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a narrow channel roughly 8m long and 3m wide with cones, one per pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Sell the fake — look one way, step the other.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker gets 2 attempts before the defender 'wins' the round.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower channel.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sidestep: look one way with eyes and shoulders to sell the fake, then push off and step the other way.</li><li>One partner starts as attacker with the ball at one end of the channel; the other starts as a stationary defender partway down.</li><li>The attacker tries to use a sidestep to get past the defender and reach the far cone; the defender may move to block but not tackle or grab.</li><li>Give the attacker two attempts before the defender is considered to have 'won' that round if the attacker hasn't got past.</li><li>Coach the key message: sell the fake — look one way, step the other.</li><li>For 'easier', give the attacker 2 attempts before a round ends; for 'harder', narrow the channel so there's less room to step around.</li></ul><hr><h4>King of the Grid (Evasion)</h4><p>Small group, everyone carries their own ball in a shared grid, trying to step past others without being touched below the shoulder; the 'king' is whoever keeps the longest streak untouched.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a grid roughly 12m x 12m per small group of 5-6 students, each with their own ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep scanning for the nearest threat.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, fewer players.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, more players.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, role rotation via re-entry</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: everyone carries their own ball inside the shared grid, trying to step past others without being touched below the shoulder.</li><li>Students move freely, using steps and changes of direction to avoid being touched by anyone else in the grid.</li><li>If touched below the shoulder, a student's streak ends — they simply continue playing and start counting a new streak from zero.</li><li>The 'king' is whoever is currently on the longest untouched streak — call this out periodically to keep energy and awareness high.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep scanning for the nearest threat.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with fewer players; for 'harder', shrink the grid or add more players to increase congestion.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 1v1 Alley Challenge</h3><p>Pairs take turns in a narrow 'alley' with a small scoring zone at each end — 1v1, first to 3 successful evasions or most in 2 minutes, then rotate partners. A clean step under real pressure decides most points.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark several narrow 'alleys' roughly 8m long and 3m wide, each with a small scoring zone (marked with cones) at both ends. Pairs rotate through in turns, one ball per alley.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: 1v1 in the alley, first to 3 successful evasions or whoever has the most in 2 minutes wins that head-to-head.</li><li>One player attacks with the ball from one end, trying to evade the defender using a clean step and reach the scoring zone at the far end.</li><li>The defender tries to touch the attacker below the shoulder before they reach the scoring zone — no tackling or grabbing.</li><li>After each attempt (successful evasion or a touch), both players reset to their starting ends and go again immediately.</li><li>Swap attacker/defender roles after each point, or after an agreed number of attempts, so both get equal turns.</li><li>Once the 2 minutes or 3-evasion target is reached, rotate to new partners so students face different opponents.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your step successful (or not) today?</li><li>How did it feel to defend fairly and try your best without being too rough?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try next time you're 1v1?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Plants the outside foot firmly before stepping</li><li>Uses a fake/look before committing to a direction</li><li>Accelerates away immediately after the step</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For lower-confidence pairs, use 'passive defenders' (hands behind back, walking pace) so the attacker experiences pressure without high stress.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Continuity, Support & Defence Basics (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Shadow & Shape":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Supporting the ball carrier (depth &amp; width)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role in supporting a teammate — knowing where you fit and why your position matters to the play.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep supporting lines spaced out from other groups to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to support a ball carrier by running at the right depth and width to receive a pass.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run alongside a ball carrier at a supporting angle, not directly behind them</li><li>I can time my run so I'm in a good position to receive a pass</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pop Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently, two hands, backward or flat.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle passes.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Position at an angle, not directly behind the ball carrier.</td><td>Gives a genuine passing option rather than a blocked one.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay close enough to be a real option, far enough to have space.</td><td>Too close and there's no room to work with; too far and the pass can't reach in time.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate that you're an option.</td><td>Helps decision-making happen faster under pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Triangle Support Run</h4><p>Three players jog down the field in a triangle shape, the ball carrier passing to either support runner who then becomes the new carrier, maintaining depth and width.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 spread across the field in a triangle shape, roughly 4-5m between each player, several triangles running down the field with at least 5m between groups. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Support runners stay slightly behind and to the side, never level or ahead illegally.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a shadow defender jogging alongside (not intercepting).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate correct support position: support runners stay slightly behind and to the side of the ball carrier, never level or ahead.</li><li>The group jogs down the field together in their triangle shape, starting with one player carrying the ball.</li><li>The carrier passes to either support runner, who becomes the new carrier while the others adjust to maintain the triangle shape.</li><li>Continue jogging and passing the length of the field, then jog back and repeat.</li><li>Coach the key message: support runners stay slightly behind and to the side, never level or ahead illegally.</li><li>For 'easier', walk instead of jog; for 'harder', add a shadow defender jogging alongside without intercepting, to simulate pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call for the Ball</h4><p>Pairs jog, one carries; the support player must call loudly and show a target hand before receiving, reinforcing communication alongside positioning.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs jog together down the field with at least 3-4m between pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early, not right as the pass is thrown.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, closer support distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, greater support distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling early and showing a target hand well before the pass arrives, not right as it's thrown.</li><li>One partner carries the ball while jogging; the support partner jogs alongside at a supporting distance.</li><li>The support player must call loudly and show a target hand before they receive the ball — the carrier waits for the call before passing.</li><li>Continue jogging and passing down the field, swapping who carries first each length.</li><li>Coach the key message: call early, not right as the pass is thrown.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace and keep support distance closer; for 'harder', jog faster and increase the support distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Depth &amp; Width Gates</h4><p>Groups of 3 pass through a series of marked 'support zones' (cones) that show correct depth/width, scoring bonus points for staying inside the zone while supporting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 with a series of 3-4 marked 'support zones' set out using cone gates along the field, each zone roughly 2m wide, showing the correct depth/width position for support runners.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Picture an imaginary line joining you to the ball carrier — stay slightly behind it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower zones, timed race.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the imaginary line concept: picture a line joining you to the ball carrier — support runners should stay slightly behind this line, inside their zone.</li><li>The group moves down the field, passing between players as they pass through each marked support zone.</li><li>Award a bonus point (or just verbal praise) each time a support runner is inside their zone when they receive the ball.</li><li>Continue through all the zones to the end of the field, then reset and go again.</li><li>Coach the key message: picture an imaginary line joining you to the ball carrier — stay slightly behind it.</li><li>For 'easier', make the zones wider; for 'harder', narrow the zones and time the group for a race against other groups.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3v2 Support Rugby</h3><p>Small-sided, no tags yet — attacking team always has an extra support player; the ball carrier must find and use support to keep the phase alive and score. Coach highlights good depth/width in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5 split into 3 attackers and 2 defenders, small field roughly 20m x 15m, no tags. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: the attacking team always has an extra support player (3v2), so there should always be a free support option.</li><li>Attackers start with the ball at one end; the 2 defenders spread out to defend without tackling or touching.</li><li>The ball carrier looks to use their extra support player to keep the phase alive, passing before contact rather than trying to beat both defenders alone.</li><li>The attacking team scores by grounding the ball over the defenders' try line; after a score, swap which 3 players attack and which 2 defend.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, restart immediately from that spot with defenders gaining the ball to keep pace up.</li><li>Coach highlights good depth and width in play as it happens, e.g. 'great width there, that gave you two passing options!'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your support run useful to the ball carrier today?</li><li>Why does depth and width matter for a support runner?</li><li>What did you notice about timing your call for the ball?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Runs at an angle, not directly behind the ball carrier</li><li>Calls clearly and early for the ball</li><li>Adjusts depth/width based on where the defenders are</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole shape down to walking pace for students still building the timing/positioning sequence; speed increases as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Quick Recycle":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Continuity — quick recycling of the ball</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Supporting a teammate the moment they need help — being ready and available so the team doesn't lose momentum.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, belts &amp; tags, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Introduce the tag rule carefully: after a tag, the carrier must stop and pass within a few steps — walk through it slowly before adding pace</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to quickly recycle (pass or place) the ball so play keeps flowing after a tag.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass the ball away quickly after being tagged</li><li>I can support a tagged teammate so the ball keeps moving</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pop Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently, two hands, backward or flat.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle passes.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get to the tackle area or ball quickly after a tag.</td><td>Quick support means faster recycling and keeps the attack moving.</td></tr><tr><td>Present or pass the ball immediately — don't hold onto it.</td><td>Holding on slows the whole team's attack down.</td></tr><tr><td>Call clearly for quick ball if you're the next receiver.</td><td>Communication speeds up the recycle under pressure.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Tag &amp; Pass</h4><p>Carrier jogs freely; when tagged (light touch to the belt/tag) by a shadow defender, they must stop and pass immediately to a nearby support player.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs — one carrier, one shadow defender wearing a tag belt — spread across an open space with at least 4m between pairs. One ball and one tag belt per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Have a support player already close by before you're tagged.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender delays the tag (counts to 3 first).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender tags earlier/faster.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk through the tag rule slowly first: after being tagged (a light touch to the belt/tag), the carrier must stop immediately and pass within a few steps to a nearby support player.</li><li>The carrier jogs freely with the ball while the shadow defender jogs nearby, ready to tag.</li><li>When tagged, the carrier stops straight away and passes to the nearest support player (use a third player if available, or pass back to the defender-turned-support for practice).</li><li>After the pass, play continues immediately — the new ball carrier keeps jogging and the process repeats.</li><li>Coach the key message: have a support player already close by before you're tagged.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender count to 3 before tagging, giving the carrier more time; for 'harder', have the defender tag earlier and faster.</li></ul><hr><h4>Recycle Relay</h4><p>Small groups move the ball down the field; every time a 'tag' cone is reached, the carrier must place/pass immediately and a new carrier continues, racing another group.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a series of 'tag cones' every 4-5m down a channel roughly 20m long. Small groups of 4-5 line up at the start, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>React immediately — no hesitation after the tag.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More space between tag cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Less space, faster recycle required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every time the carrier reaches a tag cone, they must immediately place the ball down or pass it, and a new carrier continues from there.</li><li>The group moves the ball down the field together, with the current carrier jogging to the first tag cone.</li><li>On reaching the cone, the carrier passes (or places, if playing a simplified version) the ball to the next player in the group, who continues to the next tag cone.</li><li>Continue this pattern all the way to the end of the channel, racing another group doing the same course.</li><li>Coach the key message: react immediately — no hesitation after the tag.</li><li>For 'easier', space the tag cones further apart; for 'harder', place them closer together so the recycle happens more often and faster.</li></ul><hr><h4>Quick Ball Grid</h4><p>Groups of 4 play in a grid where every catch counts as a 'tag' — the receiver must pass again within 3 seconds, keeping the ball moving continuously.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, several grids spread around the space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide who you're passing to before the ball even arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>5 seconds allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>2 seconds allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every catch counts as a 'tag' — the receiver must pass again within 3 seconds of catching the ball.</li><li>Students move around inside the grid, passing the ball to a teammate who must immediately look for their next pass.</li><li>Count down out loud ('3, 2, 1') if it helps students feel the time pressure, especially in early reps.</li><li>Keep the ball moving continuously around the grid — the goal is speed of decision, not scoring.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide who you're passing to before the ball even arrives.</li><li>For 'easier', allow 5 seconds instead of 3; for 'harder', reduce the time to 2 seconds.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Continuity Rugby</h3><p>4v4 with tags introduced — ball carrier who is tagged must stop and pass within 3 steps; teams score points for keeping continuous phases of play (no stoppage) as they move toward the try line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, tag belts worn by all players, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's rule addition: a tagged carrier must stop immediately and pass within 3 steps — walk through this once more before starting if needed.</li><li>One team starts with the ball at the halfway point; defenders spread out and may tag (not tackle) the ball carrier.</li><li>Teams score points not just for tries but for keeping a continuous phase of play going without a stoppage — coach can call out a phase count as the ball moves.</li><li>After a tag, the carrier must stop and pass within 3 steps to keep the phase alive; failing to do so ends the phase and hands possession over.</li><li>On a score or a broken phase, restart quickly from that spot to keep the game flowing.</li><li>Coach celebrates long unbroken phases as much as tries, e.g. 'that's 6 phases without a stop, brilliant continuity!'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you recycle the ball quickly after a tag today?</li><li>Why does the game slow down or stop if the ball isn't recycled fast?</li><li>What's one thing that made continuity easier for your team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stops immediately when tagged</li><li>Passes away within the allowed steps</li><li>Support players are already nearby, ready to receive</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start with a generous step count and a slow, walked-through tag rule before adding real pace; some students may need the tag rule demonstrated a few extra times.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Rip It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rip technique (defensive rip of the belt/tag)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking about how your actions (a safe or unsafe rip attempt) affect the safety and enjoyment of the game for everyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Belts &amp; tags (2 per student), balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No grabbing clothing or bodies — tag/belt only; no diving at the legs; approach from the front/side, not a blind-side dive</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the correct, safe technique to rip an opponent's tag from their belt.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can approach a ball carrier safely and rip the tag with one clean pull</li><li>I can call 'tag!' clearly and hold the tag up after ripping it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pop Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently, two hands, backward or flat.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle passes.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Reach with one clean hand to the tag or belt.</td><td>A single-handed rip is safe and effective; grabbing with the whole body risks contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Pull the tag away in one confident motion.</td><td>Hesitation lets the attacker evade the tag attempt.</td></tr><tr><td>Call 'tag!' clearly and hold the tag up.</td><td>Confirms to everyone the play has stopped, avoiding confusion or ongoing contact.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Static Rip Practice</h4><p>Partners stand still; the defender practises the rip technique — reaching for the tag, gripping firmly, pulling in one motion, then holding it up and calling 'tag!'.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand facing each other, one wearing a tag belt with two tags. Space pairs at least 2m apart. No ball needed for this drill — focus is purely on the rip technique.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim for the tag itself, not the belt or clothing around it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Carrier stands completely still.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Carrier shuffles side to side slightly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rip technique slowly: reach for the tag with one hand, grip it firmly, pull it away in one smooth motion, then hold it up and call 'tag!' loudly.</li><li>The carrier stands completely still while the defender practises reaching for and ripping the tag.</li><li>After each rip, the defender hands the tag back to the carrier, who reattaches it before the next attempt.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 attempts so both partners practise both ripping and being ripped.</li><li>Coach the key message: aim for the tag itself, not the belt or clothing around it, and remind students no grabbing at clothing or bodies is allowed — tag only.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the carrier completely still throughout; for 'harder', have the carrier shuffle gently side to side while the defender times their rip.</li></ul><hr><h4>Jog &amp; Rip</h4><p>Carrier jogs slowly in a straight line; the defender jogs alongside and practises timing the rip on a moving target.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs — one carrier wearing a tag belt, one defender — jogging in a straight line down a marked lane roughly 15m long, spaced well apart from other pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your rip hand ready early, don't lunge late.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Carrier jogs slower.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Carrier jogs faster or changes direction slightly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of safe technique: approach from the front or side (never a blind-side dive), reach for the tag, grip, and pull in one motion.</li><li>The carrier jogs slowly in a straight line while the defender jogs alongside, timing their rip attempt.</li><li>The defender reaches for the tag while jogging, ripping it cleanly and calling 'tag!' when successful.</li><li>After a successful rip, hand the tag back, reattach it, and go again from the start of the lane.</li><li>Coach the key message: get your rip hand ready early, don't lunge late.</li><li>For 'easier', have the carrier jog slower; for 'harder', have the carrier jog faster or add a slight change of direction.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rip Grid Game</h4><p>Small group, everyone carries a ball with two tags in a shared grid; defenders (rotating role) try to rip tags safely while carriers evade — a ripped tag means a short 'sin bin' pause before rejoining.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 in a grid roughly 12m x 12m, each carrier with a ball and two tags on their belt; 1-2 students per group rotate as defenders (no ball, no tags).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with other defenders so you're not both chasing the same carrier.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, more defenders active.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Tag/elimination-lite</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the safety rules first: no grabbing clothing or bodies, no diving at the legs — defenders approach from the front or side only.</li><li>Carriers move freely around the grid with their ball, using evasion to avoid the rotating defenders.</li><li>Defenders try to safely rip a tag from a carrier; once a tag is ripped, that carrier does a short 'sin bin' pause (e.g. 5 seconds standing still) before rejoining with their remaining tag.</li><li>If both tags are ripped, the carrier does a slightly longer pause before rejoining with a fresh pair of tags.</li><li>Coach the key message: communicate with other defenders so you're not both chasing the same carrier.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid and slower pace; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with more defenders active at once.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Rip &amp; Recycle Rugby</h3><p>4v4 full tag rules — defenders rip tags to stop the carrier, who must then pass within 3 steps (Lesson 7's continuity skill combines with today's rip technique). Emphasis on safe, clean rips and quick recycling after.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, full tag belts worn by all players, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Combine today's and last lesson's focus: defenders rip tags to stop the carrier (safe technique only — no grabbing clothing or diving at legs), who must then pass within 3 steps as in Continuity Rugby.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; defenders spread out and attempt to rip tags rather than simply touch.</li><li>When a tag is successfully ripped, the carrier stops immediately and passes within 3 steps to keep the phase alive.</li><li>Teams score by grounding the ball over the try line; after a score, restart from the halfway point.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or the 3-step rule is broken, possession switches to the other team from that spot — restart quickly.</li><li>Coach emphasises safe, clean rips and quick recycling after, praising both good defensive technique and fast reactions from the carrier.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a clean, safe rip technique feel like today?</li><li>Why is it important to only go for the tag, not the player?</li><li>How did communicating with other defenders help your team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reaches for the tag with a controlled, single-motion pull</li><li>Calls 'tag!' clearly and holds the tag up</li><li>Approaches safely from the front or side, not a reckless dive</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Walk through the rip motion in slow motion first with stationary partners before adding any movement; pair students of similar height/reach where possible for early practice.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Line Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defensive line — spacing &amp; communication</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after your teammates by communicating clearly — patience with players still learning where to stand and when to call.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Cones, bibs (2 colours), balls</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep defensive lines moving forward together at a controlled jog/walk pace — no sprinting into contact zones</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend as a connected line, with even spacing and clear communication.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand in a line with my teammates, evenly spaced</li><li>I can call out to organise my line before the attack arrives</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pop Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently, two hands, backward or flat.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle passes.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stand at an even, connected distance from your defensive teammates.</td><td>Gaps in the line are exactly what attackers look to exploit.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate who you're marking and any gaps you see.</td><td>A talking defensive line closes gaps faster than a silent one.</td></tr><tr><td>Move as a connected line, not as individuals.</td><td>A line that moves together keeps its shape; one player rushing ahead breaks it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Mirror Line Shift</h4><p>A line of 4–5 defenders shuffles sideways together, mirroring a caller's direction changes, staying evenly spaced throughout.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A line of 4-5 defenders stands shoulder-width apart (roughly 1.5-2m gaps) facing a caller, in an open space with room to shuffle sideways several metres each way.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the same gap as your neighbours — don't bunch or spread unevenly.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, bigger gaps allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster calls, tighter spacing required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: the whole line moves together sideways, staying evenly spaced, mirroring the caller's direction changes.</li><li>The caller stands facing the line and calls 'left' or 'right' at intervals, pointing in that direction.</li><li>The whole line shuffles sideways together in the called direction, each player keeping the same gap as their neighbours rather than bunching or spreading unevenly.</li><li>Continue calling direction changes for the full activity, watching for gaps opening or players drifting out of line.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep the same gap as your neighbours — don't bunch or spread unevenly.</li><li>For 'easier', call more slowly and allow bigger gaps; for 'harder', call faster and require tighter, more precise spacing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call the Line</h4><p>Defensive line jogs forward as attackers approach (no tagging yet); defenders must call out who they're 'on' (which attacker) before the attack arrives.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 players faces an attacking line of the same number, roughly 10m apart, with cones marking the width of the field. No tagging in this game — it's a walkthrough of communication only.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and loudly — communication beats speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attackers walk, more time to organise.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attackers jog, less time to organise.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: before the attack arrives, each defender must call out loudly which attacker they are 'on' (marking).</li><li>The attacking line begins walking or jogging forward toward the defensive line.</li><li>As they approach, defenders call out their assigned attacker clearly before the attack reaches them — no tagging occurs, this is purely about organisation.</li><li>Once the lines meet (or attackers reach a marked point), reset both lines and repeat with attackers approaching again.</li><li>Coach the key message: call early and loudly — communication beats speed.</li><li>For 'easier', have attackers walk, giving defenders more time to organise; for 'harder', have attackers jog, giving less time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gap Spot Game</h4><p>Defensive line stands still while attackers walk past looking for gaps; defenders practise shuffling to close any gap that opens, staying connected as a line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 stands still facing an attacking line of walkers, roughly 10-12m of width marked with cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move as a unit, not just the player nearest the gap.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider starting gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower starting gaps, faster attacker movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: defenders stay connected as a line, shuffling to close any gap that opens rather than just the nearest player reacting alone.</li><li>The defensive line stands still initially while attackers walk slowly across the width, looking for gaps between defenders.</li><li>As an attacker approaches a gap, the whole defensive line shuffles together to close it, not just the two players either side of the gap.</li><li>Continue for several passes of attackers walking across, resetting positions between each pass.</li><li>Coach the key message: move as a unit, not just the player nearest the gap.</li><li>For 'easier', start with wider gaps between defenders; for 'harder', start with narrower gaps and have attackers move faster.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Line Defence Rugby</h3><p>4v4 with tags — defending team focuses explicitly on staying connected, evenly spaced, and communicating who's marking whom, while attackers try to find and exploit any gap.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, tag belts worn by all players, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's defensive focus: the defending team must stay connected, evenly spaced, and call out who they're marking as attackers approach.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; the defending team organises their line and calls out their markers before the attack begins.</li><li>Attackers try to find and exploit any gap in the defensive line to break through and score, using passing and running.</li><li>Defenders use tags to stop the ball carrier, who must then pass within 3 steps to continue the phase.</li><li>After a score or a tag-stoppage, restart quickly from that spot, swapping which team defends each round if time allows.</li><li>Coach highlights good defensive organisation live, e.g. 'great call there, that closed the gap before it opened.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your defensive line hard to break through today?</li><li>How did calling out help your team defend better?</li><li>What happens to a defensive line if players don't communicate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Maintains even spacing with neighbouring defenders</li><li>Communicates clearly (calls) before and during defence</li><li>Shifts as a connected unit rather than individually</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students less confident calling out loudly, allow hand signals or shorter set phrases as an alternative to shouting.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Step & Support":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining evasion + support in small games</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your individual skill (the step) and your role in the team (supporting) fit together for a shared outcome.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, belts &amp; tags, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep games small-sided (3v3 max) so evasion and support decisions come thick and fast without overcrowding</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine evasion (stepping past a defender) with good support play in a small game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use a step to beat a defender and then look for support</li><li>I can support a teammate immediately after they beat a defender</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pop Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently, two hands, backward or flat.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle passes.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Evade using your best footwork or step, then immediately look for support.</td><td>Evasion alone doesn't finish the play — support keeps it going after you're tagged.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your pass to a support player before or as you're tagged.</td><td>Passing after being tagged keeps continuity rather than stopping play.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with your support before and during the play.</td><td>Your support runner needs to know when and where you might need them.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Step &amp; Pop</h4><p>Attacker steps past a shadow defender then immediately pops a short pass to a trailing support player who continues the run.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 — one carrier, one shadow defender, one trailing support player — in a channel roughly 10m long, spaced well apart from other groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The step creates the gap; the pass keeps the play alive — don't try to do everything yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender delayed, more time to step.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender live from the start.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the step technique from Lesson 5 and the support positioning from Lesson 6 before combining them today.</li><li>The carrier jogs down the channel with the shadow defender nearby, while the support player trails slightly behind and to the side.</li><li>The carrier uses a sidestep to get past the shadow defender, then immediately pops a short, flat pass to the trailing support player.</li><li>The support player becomes the new carrier and continues to the end of the channel.</li><li>Coach the key message: the step creates the gap; the pass keeps the play alive — don't try to do everything yourself.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender delayed (count to 2 before engaging) to give more time to step; for 'harder', have the defender live and reacting from the start.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v2 Evade &amp; Support</h4><p>Three attackers against two defenders in a channel — attackers combine stepping and support running to reach the far line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a channel roughly 15m long and 8m wide with cones. Groups of 5 — 3 attackers, 2 defenders — one channel per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If your step doesn't beat the defender, trust your support and pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider channel, slower defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower channel, faster defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: 3 attackers against 2 defenders means there should always be a free support option somewhere.</li><li>Attackers start with the ball at one end of the channel; defenders spread out to defend without tackling, just touching below the shoulder to stop play.</li><li>Attackers combine stepping past a defender directly, or passing to a free support runner, to reach the far line.</li><li>If a defender makes a touch, that phase ends — reset and go again from the start of the channel.</li><li>Coach the key message: if your step doesn't beat the defender, trust your support and pass.</li><li>For 'easier', widen the channel and slow the defenders down; for 'harder', narrow the channel and let defenders move at full pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Continuous Small Game</h4><p>3v3, tags on, small grid — a fast-paced mix of evasion and support with a quick recycle after every tag, rewarding whichever team keeps the phase alive longest.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 split into two teams of 3, tag belts worn, small grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always have two options: step yourself, or use your support.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the focus: a fast mix of stepping and support running, with a quick recycle after every tag.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; the other defends, using tags (not tackles) to stop the carrier.</li><li>After a tag, the carrier must pass within 3 steps to a nearby support player to keep the phase alive, using stepping to create gaps where possible.</li><li>The team that keeps their phase alive the longest before a stoppage or score wins that round.</li><li>Coach the key message: always have two options — step yourself, or use your support.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with more time; for 'harder', use a smaller grid requiring faster decisions.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 4v3 Combination Rugby</h3><p>Attacking team has a numbers advantage (4v3); success depends on combining today's two skills — stepping to create the gap, and using support to finish what the step started.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 7 split into 4 attackers and 3 defenders, small field roughly 20m x 15m, tag belts worn. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers advantage: attackers have 4 against 3 defenders, so success depends on combining stepping and support rather than relying on just one skill.</li><li>Attackers start with the ball; defenders spread out to defend, using tags to stop the ball carrier.</li><li>The ball carrier looks to step past a defender to create a gap, or pass to a free support player if the step doesn't fully beat them.</li><li>After a tag, the carrier passes within 3 steps to keep the phase going, using the numbers advantage to eventually break through and score.</li><li>After a score or a broken phase, restart quickly from that spot, rotating which players attack and defend each round.</li><li>Coach highlights moments where a step created space for a support pass to finish, connecting today's two combined skills.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did stepping and supporting work together today?</li><li>What happened when you tried to do everything yourself instead of using support?</li><li>What's one moment you combined both skills well?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a step to genuinely create space, not just for show</li><li>Looks for and finds support immediately after evading</li><li>Support players time their run to be useful, not just present</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce numbers (e.g. 2v1) for students still building confidence combining two skills at once, before scaling up to 3v2 or 4v3.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Attacking, Reading the Game & Decision-Making (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Spread the Line":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking shape — width in attack</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Being patient with the process of learning to read space — width doesn't always feel natural at first, and that's okay.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking field width, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Ensure the field is wide enough for genuine width — mark clear boundaries so players don't drift into other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to spread out across the field to create width and space in attack.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold my position in a spread-out attacking line</li><li>I can recognise when the defence is narrow and space is wide</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Evasion Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in evasion and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Spread across the field, not bunched together.</td><td>Width forces defenders to spread too, creating gaps.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep a consistent, even spacing between attacking players.</td><td>Uneven spacing creates either overcrowded or unsupported areas.</td></tr><tr><td>Time width-creating runs before the ball arrives.</td><td>Width has to exist before the pass, not be created after.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spread &amp; Pass</h4><p>A line of 5 attackers spreads to use the full width of a marked area, passing along the line while maintaining even spacing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A line of 5 attackers spreads across the full marked width of the field (roughly 20m), with cones marking the sideline boundaries. One ball per line.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Resist the urge to bunch toward the ball — hold your width.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Narrower field, closer spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full width field, greater spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the target spacing: attackers spread evenly across the full width, each roughly 4-5m from their neighbour.</li><li>The line jogs forward together, passing the ball along the line from one end to the other.</li><li>Players resist bunching in toward wherever the ball currently is, holding their width throughout.</li><li>Once the ball reaches the far end, reset and repeat, passing back the other direction.</li><li>Coach the key message: resist the urge to bunch toward the ball — hold your width.</li><li>For 'easier', use a narrower field with closer spacing between players; for 'harder', use the full width with greater spacing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Numbers Wide</h4><p>Attackers count off and must touch a cone at their assigned width position before receiving a pass, reinforcing spatial awareness of the whole line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 5 cones evenly spaced across the field width, each numbered 1-5 (write on cones or assign verbally). Attackers count off 1-5 and line up near their matching cone.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your number and your space before the play starts.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positions/cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More positions, faster ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: each attacker must touch their assigned numbered cone before they're allowed to receive a pass.</li><li>Attackers spread out to their positions, each near their numbered cone.</li><li>The ball moves along the line — before a player receives it, they must touch their cone, reinforcing awareness of their exact width position.</li><li>Continue passing along the line, resetting once it reaches the far end.</li><li>Coach the key message: know your number and your space before the play starts.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer positions/cones; for 'harder', add more positions and speed up the ball movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Find the Wide Gap</h4><p>3v2 in a wide channel — attackers must move the ball to the wide side where defenders are outnumbered, rather than running into a crowded middle.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a wide channel roughly 18m wide and 12m long. Groups of 5 — 3 attackers, 2 defenders — one channel per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look before you run — where are the extra defenders NOT standing?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider channel, slower defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower channel.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: attackers must move the ball toward whichever side has fewer defenders, rather than running into a crowded middle.</li><li>Attackers start with the ball; the 2 defenders position themselves somewhere across the width (not necessarily evenly).</li><li>Attackers scan the defensive line before passing, identifying which side is outnumbered, then move the ball that way.</li><li>Continue until the attackers reach the far line on the wide (outnumbered) side, then reset and go again with defenders repositioning differently.</li><li>Coach the key message: look before you run — where are the extra defenders NOT standing?</li><li>For 'easier', widen the channel and slow the defenders; for 'harder', narrow the channel.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Width Rugby</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5, wide pitch — teams are coached to spread across the full width; bonus points for scoring after the ball has travelled through at least 3 pairs of hands, rewarding genuine width in attack.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, wide field roughly 25m wide x 20m long, tag belts worn. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's bonus rule: extra points are awarded when a try is scored after the ball has travelled through at least 3 pairs of hands (3 separate passes).</li><li>One team starts with the ball; both teams are reminded to spread across the full width of the pitch rather than bunching centrally.</li><li>Teams move the ball using passing and running, with defenders using tags to stop the carrier, who passes within 3 steps to continue.</li><li>Coach or students keep a rough count of passes completed in the current attacking phase to track toward the bonus.</li><li>On a score, note whether the bonus (3+ passes) was achieved and celebrate it; restart quickly from the halfway point.</li><li>Coach actively encourages width during play, e.g. 'spread out, use the space — don't bunch in!'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did spreading out do to the space available for your team?</li><li>How did you know when to hold your width instead of drifting to the ball?</li><li>What made width hard for the defending team to deal with?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Holds an even attacking width rather than bunching</li><li>Recognises open space away from the ball</li><li>Times the run to arrive as the ball does, not too early or late</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use visible width markers (cones) as anchor points for students still learning to judge spacing without a reference.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Find the Gap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading the defensive line (where's the space?)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking about how your decisions (which gap you choose) affect whether your whole team's play succeeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep defensive numbers realistic (not overcrowded) so genuine gaps exist to find</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read a defensive line and identify where the space is before we run.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can spot a gap or a mismatch in the defensive line</li><li>I can choose to run at space rather than straight at a defender</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Evasion Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in evasion and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan the defensive line before deciding your next move.</td><td>Attacking blind means missing obvious gaps.</td></tr><tr><td>Look for numbers — is your side outnumbering the defence?</td><td>An overload of attackers over defenders is the easiest space to exploit.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate what you see to teammates.</td><td>One player's read can help the whole attacking line make a better decision.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spot the Gap</h4><p>Attackers walk toward a set defensive line and must call out loudly where they see the biggest gap before the whistle to move.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 stands in a set position facing an attacking group of the same number, roughly 10m apart, field width marked with cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the whole line, not just the defender directly in front of you.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, more obvious gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Subtler gaps, less time to spot them.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the task: attackers must look at the whole defensive line and call out loudly where they see the biggest gap, before a whistle signals them to move.</li><li>Attackers walk slowly toward the defensive line, scanning it as they approach.</li><li>Before the whistle, each attacker (or a nominated caller) calls out where they think the biggest gap is.</li><li>On the whistle, attackers move toward the gap they identified; reset and repeat with the defensive line repositioned slightly differently each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: look at the whole line, not just the defender directly in front of you.</li><li>For 'easier', make gaps bigger and more obvious; for 'harder', make gaps subtler and reduce the time to spot them before the whistle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Overload Read</h4><p>3v2 or 4v3 attack — attackers must identify and run at the side of the defensive line with fewer defenders.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a channel roughly 15m long and 10m wide. Groups of 5-6 — 3 or 4 attackers against 2 or 3 defenders — one channel per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Count defenders on each side before you commit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger numbers advantage (e.g. 4v2).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller advantage (e.g. 3v2).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the numbers: attackers must identify which side of the defensive line has fewer defenders and attack that side.</li><li>Attackers start with the ball at one end; defenders position themselves somewhere across the channel, not necessarily evenly.</li><li>Before running, attackers count the defenders on each side of the ball to identify the overload.</li><li>Attackers run and pass toward the side with fewer defenders to reach the far line, then reset and repeat with defenders repositioning.</li><li>Coach the key message: count defenders on each side before you commit.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger numbers advantage such as 4v2; for 'harder', use a smaller advantage such as 3v2.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shifting Line Read</h4><p>Defensive line shuffles sideways between reps; attackers must re-read the (now different) gap each time before running.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 faces attackers across a marked width of roughly 15-18m, with room for the defensive line to shuffle sideways between reps.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Re-check the line every single time — don't run the same gap out of habit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Line shifts slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Line shifts quickly, less time to read.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: the defensive line will shuffle sideways between each rep, so attackers must re-read the gap fresh every time rather than running the same spot out of habit.</li><li>The defensive line shuffles a few metres in one direction while attackers wait and observe.</li><li>Once the line settles, attackers scan again and run at whatever new gap has appeared.</li><li>Reset after each attempt, shift the line again, and repeat several times.</li><li>Coach the key message: re-check the line every single time — don't run the same gap out of habit.</li><li>For 'easier', shift the line slowly with plenty of time to observe; for 'harder', shift it quickly, giving less time to read the new gap.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; Run Rugby</h3><p>4v4 with tags — coach/app periodically calls 'freeze' for 2 seconds, prompting attackers to consciously scan the line and choose their run before play continues, reinforcing the reading habit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, tag belts worn, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the special rule: the coach (or app) will periodically call 'freeze' during play — on this call, all players stop moving for 2 seconds.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; play begins as normal with attackers running and passing, defenders tagging to stop the carrier.</li><li>When 'freeze' is called, everyone stops immediately for 2 seconds — attackers use this moment to consciously scan the defensive line and decide where to run once play resumes.</li><li>After the 2-second freeze, play continues immediately from exactly where it stopped.</li><li>Continue play with tags and the 3-step pass rule as normal between freezes, scoring by grounding the ball over the try line.</li><li>Coach reinforces the reading habit by asking a quick question after a freeze occasionally, e.g. 'where's the gap right now?', before letting play continue.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you spot a gap in the defensive line today?</li><li>How did reading the line change your decision about where to run?</li><li>What's the difference between running at a gap and running at a defender?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the full defensive line before committing to a run</li><li>Identifies numbers-up situations (more attackers than defenders)</li><li>Adjusts their run when the defensive line shifts</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find scanning difficult under pressure, use the 'freeze and point' version first — stop, point at the gap, then go.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Pass or Run?":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making — pass or run?</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself as decision-making speed develops — good decisions come with repetition, not instantly.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, belts &amp; tags, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Encourage decisive movement — hesitation causes more collisions than committed decisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to decide quickly whether to pass or run with the ball based on the space and defenders around us.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a quick decision to pass or run based on what I see</li><li>I can commit fully to my decision rather than hesitating</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Evasion Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in evasion and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Assess the gap and the defender's position before committing.</td><td>A rushed decision often picks the worse option.</td></tr><tr><td>Run if there's a clear gap; pass if you're about to be tagged with support outside.</td><td>This simple rule covers most game situations effectively.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to whichever decision you make.</td><td>Hesitating halfway through either option usually fails both.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Green Light / Red Light Decision</h4><p>Carrier runs toward a shadow defender; if there's clear space ('green light') they run, if the defender is set ('red light') they must pass — coach/app calls which scenario.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs — carrier and shadow defender — in an open lane roughly 10m long, spaced well apart from other pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide early, before the defender is right on top of you.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More obvious scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ambiguous scenarios requiring a genuine read.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the two scenarios: 'green light' means the defender is out of position with clear space to run through; 'red light' means the defender is set and ready, requiring a pass instead.</li><li>The carrier runs toward the shadow defender while the coach (or app) calls out which scenario applies for that rep.</li><li>On 'green light', the carrier runs through the space; on 'red light', the carrier must pass to an imaginary or real support player before reaching the defender.</li><li>Reset after each rep and repeat with the scenario called again, mixing up which one comes up.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide early, before the defender is right on top of you.</li><li>For 'easier', make the scenarios more obvious; for 'harder', make them ambiguous so students must make a genuine read.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 Decision Drill</h4><p>Classic 2v1 — the attacker with the ball must read the defender and either run themselves (if defender commits to the passer) or pass (if defender comes to them).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 — 2 attackers, 1 defender — in a channel roughly 10m long and 6m wide, one channel per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the defender's shoulders/feet, not their eyes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender slower to react.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the classic 2v1: the attacker with the ball must watch the defender's shoulders and feet (not their eyes) to read their movement.</li><li>The ball-carrying attacker runs toward the defender while the second attacker runs in support, slightly behind and to the side.</li><li>If the defender commits toward the ball carrier, the carrier passes to the free support player; if the defender shifts toward the support player, the carrier keeps the ball and runs.</li><li>Continue to the end of the channel, then reset with roles rotated so each student experiences both attacking roles and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the defender's shoulders/feet, not their eyes.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender react slowly; for 'harder', have the defender react immediately and decisively.</li></ul><hr><h4>Quick Call Grid</h4><p>Small group game where every carrier has 3 seconds to decide pass or run once tagged-and-recycled, building speed of decision-making under mild time pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, tag belts worn, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your first good read rather than second-guessing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>5 seconds allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>2 seconds allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: once tagged and having recycled the ball (pass within 3 steps), the new carrier has only 3 seconds to decide whether to pass again or run before being challenged again.</li><li>Students move around the grid; when tagged, the carrier passes within 3 steps as usual.</li><li>The new carrier then has 3 seconds to make their next decision (pass or run) before the defender can close in again.</li><li>Keep play continuous, cycling through tags and quick decisions for the full activity.</li><li>Coach the key message: trust your first good read rather than second-guessing.</li><li>For 'easier', allow 5 seconds to decide; for 'harder', reduce it to 2 seconds.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Decision Rugby</h3><p>4v4 full rules — coach highlights good pass-or-run decisions in the moment ('great read!') rather than just outcomes, reinforcing that a good decision is valuable even if it doesn't always result in a try.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, full tag rules, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's coaching focus: the coach will highlight good pass-or-run decisions in the moment, regardless of whether they result in a try.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; play proceeds under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle, backward/flat passing.</li><li>As the carrier approaches a defender, they must read whether to run (space available) or pass (defender set), just as practised in today's games.</li><li>Coach calls out 'great read!' whenever a good pass-or-run decision is made, whether or not it leads directly to a score.</li><li>Continue play with scores, restarts, and rotations as in previous full-rules games.</li><li>After the game, ask a couple of students to describe a decision they made and why, reinforcing that the decision itself is what's being coached.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you decide whether to pass or run today?</li><li>Can you describe a decision you made that worked out well?</li><li>What made a decision hard, and how did you handle it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reads the defender before deciding (not a random guess)</li><li>Commits fully once a decision is made</li><li>Can explain why they chose to pass or run in a given moment</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who freeze under decision pressure, slow the game to a walk and add a clear verbal cue system (defender says 'come to me' or 'I'm covering you') as training wheels.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Boot It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Kicking basics (grubber &amp; short kick)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own technique and how small changes (contact point, follow-through) change the outcome of a kick.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls (one between two), cones marking a kicking lane</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Kicking lanes must be clear of other groups; no kicking directly at another person — always kick into open space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the basic technique for a simple grubber (along-the-ground) kick.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can strike the ball along the ground with control using a grubber kick</li><li>I can judge roughly how far my kick will travel</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Evasion Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in evasion and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your eyes on the ball through contact.</td><td>Looking up early causes mis-kicks.</td></tr><tr><td>Strike a grubber with the toe or instep angled down slightly.</td><td>This makes the ball bounce and roll along the ground rather than fly up.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward your target.</td><td>Keeps the kick accurate and on line.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Static Grubber Practice</h4><p>Students practise the grubber kick technique individually into open space, focusing on contact point and follow-through rather than distance.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has a ball (or one between two if balls are limited) and a lane of open space roughly 8-10m long to kick into, with cones marking each student's lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Strike down and through the top half of the ball with the laces/top of the foot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball placed still on the ground.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball rolling slowly before the kick.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the grubber technique: strike down and through the top half of the ball using the laces or top of the foot, sending it rolling along the ground.</li><li>Students place their ball still on the ground and practise the kicking technique individually, focusing on where the foot contacts the ball.</li><li>Encourage a smooth follow-through after contact rather than a short jab.</li><li>Retrieve the ball after each kick and repeat, focusing on technique and contact point rather than how far it travels.</li><li>Coach the key message: strike down and through the top half of the ball with the laces/top of the foot.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the ball completely still before each kick; for 'harder', roll the ball slowly first and kick it while moving.</li></ul><hr><h4>Grubber to a Target</h4><p>Partners take turns grubber-kicking toward a marked target zone, scoring points for accuracy rather than power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners take turns kicking toward a marked target zone (a small cone square roughly 2m x 2m) placed 6-8m away. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Control beats power — a soft, accurate grubber is more useful in a game than a huge, wild one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zone, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target zone, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring focus: points are awarded for accuracy (landing in or rolling into the target zone), not for power or distance.</li><li>One partner grubber-kicks toward the target zone while the other watches and calls out whether it landed inside.</li><li>Retrieve the ball and swap turns, keeping a rough tally of successful hits for friendly competition.</li><li>Continue alternating turns for the full activity.</li><li>Coach the key message: control beats power — a soft, accurate grubber is more useful in a game than a huge, wild one.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger target zone at a closer distance; for 'harder', use a smaller target zone further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Chase the Kick</h4><p>Kicker grubbers the ball into space and a partner sprints to collect and control it before it goes dead, simulating a simple attacking kick-and-chase.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs — one kicker, one chaser — in an open lane roughly 15m long, spaced well apart from other pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Kick into space ahead of your chaser, not at them.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower/softer kick, more time to chase.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Firmer kick, less time to chase.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate kicking into space ahead of the chaser, not directly at them, so there's a genuine gap to sprint into.</li><li>The kicker grubbers the ball into open space down the lane while the chaser waits at the start.</li><li>On the kick, the chaser sprints to collect and control the ball before it goes dead (rolls off the marked lane or stops).</li><li>Swap roles after each attempt so both partners practise kicking and chasing.</li><li>Coach the key message: kick into space ahead of your chaser, not at them.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower/softer kick with more time to chase; for 'harder', use a firmer kick with less time to react.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Kick &amp; Chase Rugby</h3><p>4v4 modified game where teams may use one grubber kick per possession to try to regain territory or create a chase, alongside normal passing/running — introduces kicking as one more attacking option, not a requirement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, tag belts worn, small field roughly 20m x 15m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the new option: each team may use one grubber kick per possession to try to regain territory or create a chase, alongside normal passing and running.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; they may choose to run and pass as usual, or use their one grubber kick option to send the ball forward into space.</li><li>If a kick is used, both teams may chase to collect and control the ball, with whoever gathers it first gaining possession from that spot.</li><li>Play continues under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle — with the kick simply being one more attacking option, not a requirement.</li><li>On a score or the ball going dead after a kick, restart quickly from the appropriate spot.</li><li>Coach reminds teams the kick is optional, e.g. 'you don't have to kick — only use it if the space is there.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about the contact point that changed how the kick travelled?</li><li>When might a kick be a useful option instead of a pass or a run?</li><li>What made chasing and controlling a kicked ball tricky?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Strikes the ball with a controlled, low trajectory (not a high boot)</li><li>Judges kick distance reasonably for the situation</li><li>Chases and controls a kicked ball with soft hands/feet</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Kicking is optional in many junior rippa formats — students who aren't ready can focus purely on the chase-and-collect role, building the skill for later.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Over the Line":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Scoring plays — finishing near the line</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills fairly to give the defender a genuine, sporting contest right to the very end of the play, not just rushing past them.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, belts &amp; tags, cones marking a try line</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Slow players down near the line — no diving; ground the ball under control while upright or kneeling</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning how to finish an attacking play cleanly by grounding the ball over the try line.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can carry the ball with control right up to and over the line</li><li>I can choose the right finishing option (run it in, short pass, or draw-and-pass) near the line</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Mirror Evasion Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in evasion and defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Identify the space or gap near the line early.</td><td>Last-second decisions near the line are rushed and often fumbled.</td></tr><tr><td>Secure the ball firmly with both hands as you finish.</td><td>Reduces the chance of dropping it right at the crucial moment.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay composed and controlled in the final steps.</td><td>Rushing the finish causes more errors than the actual evasion did.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Grounding Practice</h4><p>Students practise carrying the ball at speed and grounding it correctly just over a marked line, without diving or unnecessary contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students carry a ball at a jog toward a marked try line (a row of cones), with a lane roughly 10m long for each student or pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Ball touches the ground under control — no need to dive to 'make sure'.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower approach speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster approach speed, tag defender chasing.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate correct grounding: the ball touches the ground under control while the player is upright or kneeling — no diving needed.</li><li>Students jog toward the try line carrying the ball, gradually building to a faster approach speed over several reps.</li><li>As they cross the line, they ground the ball under control, without diving or unnecessary contact with the ground.</li><li>Retrieve the ball and jog back to the start for another rep, increasing speed gradually as confidence builds.</li><li>Coach the key message: ball touches the ground under control — no need to dive to 'make sure'.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower approach speed; for 'harder', use a faster approach speed with a tag defender chasing from behind.</li></ul><hr><h4>Draw &amp; Pass Finish</h4><p>Attacker runs at a single defender near the line and must decide whether to draw the defender and pass to a support player, or finish themselves if the gap is there.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 — attacker, defender, support player — near a marked try line roughly 8m away, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Run AT the defender first — this is what creates the decision for them, not you running around.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the key idea: the attacker must run directly at the defender first, which is what creates the decision, rather than running around them.</li><li>The attacker runs at the single defender with the support player trailing behind and to the side.</li><li>As the defender reacts, the attacker decides: pass to the free support player if the defender commits toward them, or finish themselves if a gap opens.</li><li>Whoever finishes grounds the ball correctly over the line (as practised in Grounding Practice); reset and rotate roles after each attempt.</li><li>Coach the key message: run AT the defender first — this is what creates the decision for them, not you running around.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender react slowly; for 'harder', have the defender react immediately.</li></ul><hr><h4>2-on-1 Finish Race</h4><p>Small groups race to finish a 2v1 situation correctly near the line as many times as possible in 90 seconds, alternating attackers and defenders.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3 near a marked try line, with a defined attacking zone roughly 10m from the line. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Commit to your read and finish it — don't slow down at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More space near the line.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Less space, defender starts closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: complete a 2v1 situation correctly near the line as many times as possible in 90 seconds.</li><li>Two attackers start with the ball a short distance from the line; one defender tries to stop them without tackling (a touch below the shoulder ends that attempt).</li><li>Attackers use the draw-and-pass or run-through decision to finish over the line, then quickly reset for another attempt.</li><li>Count successful clean finishes over the 90 seconds, then rotate so each student gets turns attacking and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message: commit to your read and finish it — don't slow down at the last second.</li><li>For 'easier', give more space near the line; for 'harder', reduce the space and have the defender start closer.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Finish Line Rugby</h3><p>4v4 with a strong emphasis on the final phase — extra points awarded for a clean, controlled finish (correct decision + clean grounding) rather than just for scoring at all.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 split into two teams of 4, tag belts worn, small field roughly 20m x 15m with a clearly marked try line. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring focus: extra points are awarded for a clean, controlled finish (a good decision near the line plus correct, non-diving grounding), not just for scoring at all.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; play proceeds under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle, backward/flat passing.</li><li>As attackers approach the try line, they apply today's focus: run at the defender, then decide to pass or finish, and ground the ball correctly and under control.</li><li>Coach or a nominated helper notes whether each score included a clean decision and controlled grounding for bonus recognition.</li><li>On a score, celebrate a clean finish specifically if it happened, e.g. 'great decision AND a controlled grounding — that's a top finish!'</li><li>Restart quickly from the halfway point after each score to keep the game flowing.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a finish near the line clean and controlled today?</li><li>How did you decide whether to run it in yourself or pass to a support player?</li><li>What's the risk of diving or rushing a finish?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Approaches the line under control, not at reckless speed</li><li>Grounds the ball cleanly without diving</li><li>Makes a good draw-and-pass or run decision in the final moment</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find the final decision overwhelming, walk through a slow-motion 2v1 near the line first before adding pace.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Drift & Shift":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defensive strategy — drift defence</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking about how your individual movement (rushing vs. staying connected) affects the whole team's defensive structure.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep drift speed controlled — no sprinting through the line, which causes gaps and collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend as a team by drifting sideways together to cover an attacker's width.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can shift sideways in line with my teammates as the attack moves the ball</li><li>I can avoid rushing forward out of the defensive line</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Move sideways in line with the ball's movement, not straight forward.</td><td>Drifting keeps the defensive line connected while covering width.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with teammates to drift together.</td><td>One player drifting alone creates a gap rather than closing one.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay patient — don't rush in and break the line.</td><td>A broken line is easier to exploit than a solid, drifting one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Drift Shuffle</h4><p>A defensive line shuffles sideways together as a ball is passed along an attacking line opposite them (no live tags), practising staying connected while drifting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 faces an attacking line of the same number across a marked width of roughly 15-18m. No tags — this is a walkthrough drill. One ball for the attacking line.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move at the same pace as your neighbours — the line drifts as one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain drift defence: the defensive line shuffles sideways together as the ball moves along the attacking line opposite them, staying connected as a unit.</li><li>The attacking line passes the ball along its line from one end toward the other, without live tagging.</li><li>As the ball moves, the defensive line shuffles sideways at the same pace, matching the direction the ball is travelling.</li><li>Continue passing the ball back and forth along the attacking line, with defenders drifting to match each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: move at the same pace as your neighbours — the line drifts as one.</li><li>For 'easier', move the ball more slowly along the attacking line; for 'harder', move it faster, requiring quicker drift.</li></ul><hr><h4>Push Up, Don't Rush</h4><p>Defenders practise stepping forward together only when appropriate (ball passed, not held), resisting the urge to rush individually out of the line.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A defensive line of 4-5 faces an attacking line holding the ball, roughly 10m apart, open width marked with cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball, not the attacker in front of you, to time your push up.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach calls 'push!' clearly each time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No verbal cue — read it yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the key discipline: defenders should only step forward together when the ball is passed, not simply because an attacker is running at them.</li><li>The attacking line holds and passes the ball along their line while the defensive line watches, ready to push up.</li><li>Each time the ball is passed, the whole defensive line takes a controlled step forward together; when the ball is just held (not passed), the line holds its position.</li><li>Continue for several passes, resetting the lines to their start distance between reps.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the ball, not the attacker in front of you, to time your push up.</li><li>For 'easier', have the coach call 'push!' clearly each time the ball is passed; for 'harder', remove the verbal cue so students read it themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v3 Drift Practice</h4><p>Small-sided game where the defending team's only job is to drift and contain — no tagging allowed, just staying connected and pushing the attack toward the sideline.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 6 — 3 attackers, 3 defenders — in a field roughly 18m wide x 12m long, no tagging. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Herd the attack toward the sideline as a connected unit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider field, more time to drift.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower field, faster drift required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the defending team's only job today: drift and contain, staying connected and pushing the attack toward the sideline, without tagging.</li><li>Attackers move the ball with passing and running, trying to find space; defenders drift sideways together to match the ball's movement.</li><li>Rather than tagging, 'success' for defenders is herding the attack toward the sideline as a connected unit, using positioning alone.</li><li>Reset after the attackers reach the sideline or a set time limit, then swap which team attacks and defends.</li><li>Coach the key message: herd the attack toward the sideline as a connected unit.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider field with more time to drift; for 'harder', use a narrower field requiring faster drift.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Drift Defence Rugby</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 — defending team is coached explicitly on drift technique (staying connected, shifting with the ball, not rushing) while attackers try to beat the drift with width and timing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, tag belts worn, field roughly 22m wide x 18m long. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's coaching focus: the defending team is coached explicitly on drift technique — staying connected, shifting sideways with the ball, and not rushing individually.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; the defending team practises drifting as a connected line rather than defenders peeling off individually.</li><li>Attackers try to beat the drift using width and good timing of their passes, looking for the moment the drift is slow to react.</li><li>Defenders use tags to stop the carrier when they do get close enough, who then passes within 3 steps to continue.</li><li>On a score or a tag-stoppage, restart quickly from that spot, swapping which team defends periodically.</li><li>Coach calls out good drift technique live, e.g. 'great, you stayed connected and pushed them to the sideline!'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your defensive drift effective (or not) today?</li><li>Why is rushing forward individually risky for a defensive line?</li><li>How did staying connected as a line help your team defend?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Shifts sideways in line with teammates, not ahead or behind</li><li>Resists rushing forward out of the defensive structure</li><li>Communicates with neighbours to stay connected while drifting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole game to walking pace for students still learning the drift concept before adding jogging speed.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Switch It":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking strategy — switch plays</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with a teammate while learning a two-person pattern — timing takes a few tries to click for everyone.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Switch passes happen at close range — remind students to keep the exchange controlled, not rushed into a collision</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning a simple switch play, where two attackers change direction and hand the ball across to confuse the defence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run a switch pass with a teammate, changing our running lines</li><li>I can time the switch so it happens before the defender can react</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call the switch clearly before executing it.</td><td>Your team needs to know the direction of attack is about to change.</td></tr><tr><td>Move the ball quickly across the field with accurate passes.</td><td>A slow switch gives the defence time to adjust and cover it anyway.</td></tr><tr><td>Time support runners to the new side of attack.</td><td>A switch is only effective if there's support ready on the new side.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Switch Pass Walkthrough</h4><p>Pairs walk through the switch play slowly: Player A runs one way, Player B crosses behind and takes a short pass, changing the attack's direction.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs walk through the movement in an open lane roughly 10m long, spaced well apart from other pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The pass is short and flat — this is about changing direction, not distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk pace only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the switch play slowly: Player A runs in one direction, Player B crosses behind them and takes a short, flat pass, changing the direction of the attack.</li><li>Both players start walking together with Player A carrying the ball.</li><li>Player B crosses behind Player A at walking pace and receives a short pass, then continues walking in the new direction.</li><li>Repeat several times at walking pace, swapping who starts with the ball so both practise both roles.</li><li>Coach the key message: the pass is short and flat — this is about changing direction, not distance.</li><li>For 'easier', keep it to walk pace only; for 'harder', add a light jog once the movement pattern is understood.</li></ul><hr><h4>Switch vs. Shadow Defender</h4><p>Pairs execute the switch play against a single shadow defender who reacts to whichever direction the ball appears to be going.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs plus one shadow defender per pair, in an open lane roughly 10m long, spaced well apart from other groups. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Sell the first direction with your eyes and shoulders before switching.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students to sell the first direction with their eyes and shoulders before switching, to convince the defender to commit the wrong way.</li><li>The pair executes the switch play (as practised in the walkthrough) while a single shadow defender reacts to whichever direction the ball appears to be going.</li><li>The defender tries to react and move toward the attack's apparent direction, without tackling — just shadowing.</li><li>After each attempt, reset and swap which player starts with the ball, and rotate the defender role periodically.</li><li>Coach the key message: sell the first direction with your eyes and shoulders before switching.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender react slowly; for 'harder', have the defender react immediately to the first movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Switch Chain Relay</h4><p>Groups of 4 chain together two switch plays in a row moving down the field, racing another group for the fastest clean run-through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 in a lane roughly 20m long, chaining together two switch plays as they move down the field. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate clearly who's taking the ball each switch.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>One switch only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two switches, timed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: chain two switch plays in a row while moving down the field, racing another group for the fastest clean run-through.</li><li>The group starts at one end with one player carrying the ball, and executes the first switch play as practised.</li><li>Immediately after the first switch, the group executes a second switch play with different players to continue changing direction down the field.</li><li>The first group to complete both switches cleanly and reach the far end wins that round; reset and repeat, swapping starting roles.</li><li>Coach the key message: communicate clearly who's taking the ball each switch.</li><li>For 'easier', do one switch only; for 'harder', do two switches and time the group for a race.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Switch Play Rugby</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with tags — attacking teams are encouraged to call and run at least one switch play per possession, aiming to catch a drifting defence moving the wrong way.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, tag belts worn, field roughly 22m wide x 18m long. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain today's attacking encouragement: attacking teams should call and run at least one switch play per possession during the game.</li><li>One team starts with the ball; the defending team applies drift defence as practised in Lesson 16.</li><li>The attacking team looks for a moment to call and execute a switch play, aiming to catch the drifting defence moving the wrong way.</li><li>Play continues under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle — with the switch play being one attacking tool among others.</li><li>On a score or a tag-stoppage, restart quickly, swapping which team attacks and defends periodically.</li><li>Coach celebrates a well-timed switch specifically, e.g. 'that switch caught the drift perfectly, great timing!'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made the switch play successful (or not) against a defender today?</li><li>How did selling a fake direction help the switch work?</li><li>What's the purpose of a switch play against a drifting defence?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Times the switch pass to happen before the defender reacts</li><li>Communicates clearly with their switch partner</li><li>Changes direction convincingly, not half-heartedly</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Walk the pattern through cone markers first for students who need a visual/spatial reference before adding a live defender.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Know Your Role":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Roles within a team (structure)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C (Relationships with Other People) · Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Valuing every role on the team equally — a sweeper is just as important as a try-scorer, and everyone's contribution matters.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs marking roles (e.g. runners, support, sweeper), cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Rotate roles regularly so no student is stuck in one position for the whole lesson</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning about different roles within a rippa rugby team and how each role contributes to the team's structure.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can describe what my role is asking of me in a small-sided game</li><li>I can support teammates in different roles, not just my own</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Understand your specific role before the play starts.</td><td>Knowing your job means faster, more confident decisions in the moment.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate your role or position to teammates.</td><td>Reduces confusion about who is doing what.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your role based on what the team needs in the moment.</td><td>Rigid roles break down when the game situation changes.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Role Carousel</h4><p>In small groups, students rotate through simple roles (first receiver, support runner, sweeper/cover defender) every 90 seconds in a continuous small game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5, each player wearing a bib marking their current role (first receiver, support runner, sweeper/cover defender). Small grid roughly 15m x 12m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know what your current role is asking of you before the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer rotations, simpler roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter rotations, more roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three roles clearly before starting: first receiver takes the ball first, support runners offer options, and the sweeper/cover defender covers behind the play.</li><li>Students play a continuous small game inside the grid, each performing their currently assigned role.</li><li>Every 90 seconds, call a rotation — students swap bibs and take on a new role, continuing play without a long stoppage.</li><li>Continue rotating through all roles so every student experiences each one at least once.</li><li>Coach the key message: know what your current role is asking of you before the ball arrives.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer rotation periods and simpler roles; for 'harder', shorten the rotations and add more distinct roles.</li></ul><hr><h4>Structure Walkthrough</h4><p>Coach/app sets up a simple attacking structure (first receiver, two support runners, a sweeper) and the team walks through where each role should be as the ball moves.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A team of 4-5 walks through a marked-out structure (cones showing where a first receiver, two support runners, and a sweeper should be) across a field roughly 18m x 12m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk to your teammates about where you're going, not just where you are.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer roles, walked through slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More roles, at jogging pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up the structure: mark cone positions showing roughly where the first receiver, two support runners, and sweeper should be as the ball starts.</li><li>The team walks through where each role should move to as the ball is passed, following the marked structure.</li><li>Talk through each movement together as a team — where is the ball going, and where should each role be moving to next.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times, gradually increasing to a light jog as the pattern becomes familiar.</li><li>Coach the key message: talk to your teammates about where you're going, not just where you are.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer roles and walk through slowly; for 'harder', add more roles and move at jogging pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Find Your Space Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where each player is assigned a role for that game only; points awarded when a player is found in the correct structural position when the ball arrives near them.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 4-5 in a field roughly 18m x 14m, each player assigned a role for that game (bibs help identify roles), with light or no opposition. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Structure isn't standing still — it's being in the right space at the right time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer defenders, more time to find position.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full opposition.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each player a role for this specific game only (first receiver, support, sweeper, etc.) before starting.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where the team tries to move the ball using passing and running as normal.</li><li>Award a point (or verbal praise) whenever a player is found in the correct structural position relative to the ball when it arrives near them.</li><li>Continue play, rotating roles between games so different students experience different positions.</li><li>Coach the key message: structure isn't standing still — it's being in the right space at the right time.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer defenders and give more time to find position; for 'harder', play with full opposition.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Structured Small-Sided Rugby</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5 with simple assigned roles (e.g. first receiver, support, sweeper) — teams are coached to recognise how their individual role contributes to the team's overall shape and success.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, simple roles assigned by bib (first receiver, support, sweeper), tag belts worn, field roughly 22m x 18m. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: teams are encouraged to recognise how their individual assigned role contributes to the team's overall shape and success.</li><li>Assign roles within each team using bibs before starting; briefly remind students what their role's job is.</li><li>Play proceeds under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle, backward/flat passing — with players trying to stay true to their assigned role's positioning.</li><li>Coach highlights moments where a role was filled well, e.g. 'great sweeper cover there, that stopped a big gap.'</li><li>On a score or a tag-stoppage, restart quickly, rotating roles at the halfway point of the game so everyone experiences more than one role.</li><li>After the game, ask a couple of students what their role asked of them and how it helped the team.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did your role ask you to do differently from a teammate's role?</li><li>How did understanding your role help the team play better together?</li><li>What was it like trying a role you don't usually play?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Can describe their assigned role in simple terms</li><li>Positions themselves appropriately for their role</li><li>Supports and communicates with teammates in different roles</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a simplified two-role version (attacker/support) for students who find three or more roles overwhelming, building up to full structure over the lesson.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Game Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full-rules small-sided games with tactics focus</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B &amp; C · Thinking, Relating to others, Participating &amp; contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Bringing everything together fairly — playing hard, playing safe, and playing for the team, not just for yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Belts &amp; tags, balls, bibs, cones for field boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Full rules apply — reinforce safe rip technique and controlled finishing near the line before starting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything we've practised — passing, evasion, support, defence, and structure — in a full small-sided game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply at least one tactic we've learned in a live game situation</li><li>I can adjust my play based on what the game is asking for in the moment</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply the specific tactical focus the coach calls out.</td><td>This lesson is about putting tactics into practice, not just skills.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly with teammates during play.</td><td>Tactics only work if the whole team is coordinated.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust when the first plan doesn't work.</td><td>Good teams adapt rather than repeating a failing plan.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Tactic Spotlight Rounds</h4><p>Short rounds of small-sided games where each round has a spotlighted tactic (e.g. 'today's focus: use width') that the coach/app calls out and looks for in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, tag belts worn, field roughly 20m x 16m. One ball per game, with a way to announce the round's spotlighted tactic (e.g. write it on a small whiteboard or call it out).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Try the spotlighted tactic even if it doesn't work perfectly the first time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer rounds, simpler tactic focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter rounds, combined tactics.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Announce the spotlighted tactic for this round before play begins — e.g. 'today's focus: use width' or 'today's focus: try a switch play.'</li><li>Teams play a short round of the small-sided game (roughly 3-4 minutes), consciously trying to apply the spotlighted tactic during play.</li><li>Coach watches for and calls out moments where the tactic is used well, regardless of whether it leads to a score.</li><li>At the end of the round, briefly announce the next spotlighted tactic and start a new short round.</li><li>Coach the key message: try the spotlighted tactic even if it doesn't work perfectly the first time.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer rounds with a simpler single tactic focus; for 'harder', shorten the rounds and combine two tactics in one round.</li></ul><hr><h4>Coach's Freeze &amp; Reset</h4><p>During live small-sided games, the coach/app occasionally calls 'freeze' to briefly discuss the decision just made, then play resumes from that point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, tag belts worn, field roughly 20m x 16m. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use the freeze as a learning moment, not a criticism.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer freezes, longer play periods.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More frequent freezes.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the freeze rule: during live play, the coach will occasionally call 'freeze,' at which point everyone stops exactly where they are.</li><li>Play begins as a normal small-sided game under full rules.</li><li>When 'freeze' is called, the coach briefly discusses the decision that was just made — asking the player involved what they saw or why they chose that option.</li><li>After the brief discussion (10-15 seconds), play resumes immediately from that exact point.</li><li>Coach the key message: use the freeze as a learning moment, not a criticism.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer freezes with longer periods of uninterrupted play; for 'harder', freeze more frequently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mini League Round Robin</h4><p>Short round-robin games between groups of 4, rotating opponents every few minutes, applying full rules and whichever tactics suit the situation.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 forming several small teams, short round-robin games on a field roughly 18m x 14m per game, tag belts worn, ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk to your team between games about what worked.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer games, fewer rotations.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter games, more rotations/opponents.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: short games (roughly 3-4 minutes) played in a round robin, rotating opponents every few minutes.</li><li>Teams play under full rules, applying whichever tactics from across the unit suit the situation (width, support, stepping, switch, structure).</li><li>At the end of each short game, teams rotate to face a new opponent.</li><li>Between games, encourage teams to briefly talk about what worked in the previous game before starting the next.</li><li>Coach the key message: talk to your team between games about what worked.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer games with fewer rotations; for 'harder', use shorter games with more frequent opponent changes.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full-Rules Small-Sided Rippa Rugby</h3><p>4v4 or 5v5, full Rippa Rugby rules (tags, backward passing, 3-step recycle) — teams are encouraged to consciously apply tactics from across the unit (support, width, drift, switch, structure) in genuine competitive play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8-10 split into two teams of 4-5, full tag belts worn, field roughly 22m x 18m with clearly marked try lines. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind teams of the full Rippa Rugby rules: tags to stop the carrier, backward/flat passing only, and a 3-step recycle after every tag.</li><li>Encourage teams to consciously apply tactics learned across the whole unit — support positioning, width, drift defence, switch plays, and team structure — wherever the situation calls for them.</li><li>Play a continuous small-sided game under full rules, with teams scoring by grounding the ball over the try line.</li><li>Coach highlights moments where a specific unit tactic was applied well, connecting it back to the lesson it came from, e.g. 'that's the switch play from earlier in the unit!'</li><li>On a score or a broken phase, restart quickly from the relevant spot to keep the game flowing.</li><li>Rotate players through different roles/positions periodically so everyone experiences a variety of situations.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What tactic did you try today, and how did it go?</li><li>What did you notice about your team's decision-making under real game pressure?</li><li>What's one thing your team could work on next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies at least one taught tactic recognisably in live play</li><li>Adjusts decisions based on what the game situation demands</li><li>Communicates with teammates during live play</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a slightly reduced pace or smaller-sided game (e.g. 3v3) for groups still building confidence with full rules before scaling to 5v5.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Rippa Rugby Cup":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C &amp; D · All key competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Celebrating the whole team's effort and growth across the unit — thinking about how everyone's contribution, not just the scoreline, made the tournament work.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Belts &amp; tags, balls, bibs (multiple colours for teams), cones, a simple ladder/scoreboard</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Manage fatigue with rotations/subs; reinforce safe rip technique and controlled finishing before the tournament begins</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills in a fun, competitive mini-tournament format.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full game applying skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>I can be a good teammate and good sport across multiple games</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring your best skills and decision-making from across the unit.</td><td>The tournament tests everything you've learned together.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates throughout.</td><td>A competitive setting is a great test of teamwork and sportsmanship.</td></tr><tr><td>Adapt your play based on what each game actually needs.</td><td>Reading the game beats sticking to one fixed plan.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Warm-In Circuit</h4><p>A short rotating circuit revisiting the unit's key skills (carry &amp; pass, catch, evade, rip, support) as a fun warm-in before the tournament starts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 5 stations around the field, each themed on a key unit skill (carry &amp; pass, catch, evade, rip, support), with balls, tags, and cones at each. Groups of 4-5 rotate through stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every station.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer stations, fewer skills.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter stations, all 5 skills.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each station focuses on before starting: carry &amp; pass, catch, evade, rip, and support.</li><li>Groups spend roughly 2 minutes at each station, practising the skill using a simple version of a game already played earlier in the unit.</li><li>On a signal, groups rotate to the next station, bringing their best technique from across the unit to each one.</li><li>Continue until every group has visited all 5 stations.</li><li>Coach the key message: bring your best technique from across the unit into every station.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer stations covering fewer skills; for 'harder', use shorter stations covering all 5 skills.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Huddle</h4><p>Each team spends 2 minutes with their 'captain' (rotating role) planning one simple tactic to try in their first game — a light structure/strategy touchpoint before playing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each tournament team gathers in a huddle with their rotating 'captain' for that round, no equipment needed beyond a quiet space to talk for 2 minutes.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests the tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team chooses their own tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the huddle's purpose: the team spends 2 minutes planning one simple tactic to try in their first game of the tournament.</li><li>The rotating captain leads the discussion, encouraging teammates to suggest ideas from across the unit (width, support, switch, structure).</li><li>The team agrees on one simple, clear plan that everyone understands before heading out to play.</li><li>Rotate the captain role for future huddles across the tournament so different students get a turn leading.</li><li>Coach the key message: keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.</li><li>For 'easier', have the coach suggest the tactic for the team to discuss; for 'harder', have the team choose their own tactic independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fair Play Check-In</h4><p>Brief between-games moment where teams acknowledge one good play from their opponent, reinforcing sportsmanship as part of the tournament structure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Brief between-games moment where each team gathers for about 1 minute, no equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific — name what your opponent did well.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>After each tournament game, gather both teams briefly for a fair play check-in.</li><li>Each team takes a turn naming one specific good play their opponent made during the game just finished.</li><li>Encourage specific, genuine comments rather than generic praise — e.g. naming an actual moment from the game.</li><li>Move on to the next round of the tournament once both teams have shared.</li><li>Coach the key message: be specific — name what your opponent did well.</li><li>This activity has no easier/harder variation — it's a consistent sportsmanship routine between every game.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Rippa Rugby Cup — Round Robin</h3><p>Full Rippa Rugby rules, small-sided round-robin mini-tournament (4v4 or 5v5) across several short games; ladder/results tracked lightly and playfully, with every team guaranteed multiple games regardless of results.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class split into teams of 4-5, each team with a distinct bib colour, full tag belts worn, field roughly 22m x 18m per game, with a simple ladder or scoreboard visible to track results lightly.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the tournament format: full Rippa Rugby rules apply, and every team is guaranteed multiple short games across the round robin regardless of results.</li><li>Run a series of short games (roughly 4-5 minutes each) between different pairs of teams, rotating opponents through the round robin schedule.</li><li>Play each game under full rules — tags, 3-step recycle, backward/flat passing — scoring by grounding the ball over the try line.</li><li>Update the ladder/scoreboard lightly and playfully after each game, keeping the tone fun and low-pressure rather than intensely competitive.</li><li>Use the Captain's Huddle and Fair Play Check-In routines between games as the tournament progresses.</li><li>At the end of the round robin, celebrate the whole class's effort across the tournament rather than focusing only on the final ladder position.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from across the whole unit you felt proudest of today?</li><li>How did your team work together across multiple games?</li><li>What would you want to work on if we played another rippa rugby unit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a recognisable range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Shows good sportsmanship across wins and losses</li><li>Encourages and includes teammates throughout the tournament</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure team sizes/formats can flex on the day for uneven numbers; emphasise participation and effort in any informal recognition, not just results.</p>"}}}}},"Soccer":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Ball Control (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Glue Feet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Ball familiarisation &amp; close control</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while learning something new, and encouraging others (not laughing at mistakes) as everyone's ball control looks different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread out — one ball's-width minimum gap when dribbling freely; clear grid boundaries so students don't run into fences/walls</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep the ball close using different parts of both feet while moving.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can dribble without the ball going more than a small step away from me</li><li>I can use the inside, outside, and sole of my foot to control the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Colour Cone Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 4 different-coloured cones evenly around a large open space (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25). Every student needs their own ball. No lines or grids needed — the whole space is open for free movement.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the 4 colours before starting and assign each one a simple action, e.g. Red = dribble to a red cone and touch it with your foot; Blue = dribble to a blue cone and do a sharp turn around it; Yellow = dribble to a yellow cone and stop the ball dead; Green = dribble to a green cone and speed up away from it.</li><li>Demonstrate each action briefly with a ball so students see it once before starting.</li><li>On 'go', everyone dribbles freely around the space, choosing their own path between cones.</li><li>Call out a colour every 10–15 seconds — students dribble to the nearest cone of that colour and perform the matching action.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of colours called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students leave their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3–4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15–20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use the inside, outside, and sole of both feet to touch the ball.</td><td>Training all surfaces early prevents a one-footed dependency and builds a bigger toolkit for later dribbling.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball within half a metre of your feet at a walking pace.</td><td>Close control means you can react to pressure or change direction without the ball getting away from you.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your head up between touches, glancing down only briefly.</td><td>Soccer is played by seeing the space around you, not just watching the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Traffic Lights Dribble</h4><p>Free dribble in a grid; teacher calls 'green' (fast, light touches), 'yellow' (slow, tight control), 'red' (stop the ball dead under one foot).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out one grid per 6–8 students using 4 cones per grid (roughly 10m x 10m — big enough that students aren't colliding, small enough that they stay close to you). Every student needs their own ball inside the grid. Stand where the whole class can see and hear you, ideally on a slightly raised spot or the edge of the grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Eyes up between touches where possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, faster/mixed calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three calls before starting: 'GREEN' = dribble at a fast jog with light, quick touches; 'YELLOW' = slow right down, take small controlling touches, ball stays glued to the foot; 'RED' = stop completely, ball trapped dead under one foot, freeze.</li><li>Demonstrate all three yourself with a ball so students see the difference in speed and touch size before they start.</li><li>Send everyone into the grid dribbling their own ball on 'green' to begin.</li><li>Call out colours in a random order every 5–10 seconds. Watch for students bunching up or losing control and gently redirect them to open space.</li><li>On 'red', check that every ball is stopped dead under a foot before you call 'green' again — this is your checkpoint to see who needs help.</li><li>Run for the full time, mixing up the order and pace of your calls so students can't predict what's coming next.</li></ul><hr><h4>Sole Tag</h4><p>Partners each dribble their own ball in a small grid, trying to tap a partner's ball out while protecting their own — no standing still allowed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up and give each pair a small shared grid of about 5m x 5m marked with 4 cones (several grids side by side across the space). Each student in the pair needs their own ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the ball on your 'safe' foot, away from the tagger.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, no tagging (just avoid contact).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>3 players, 1 ball each, everyone taggable.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure / protecting possession</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: both partners dribble their own ball inside the same small grid at the same time, trying to tap the partner's ball out of the grid with their foot, while protecting their own ball from being tapped out.</li><li>Clarify what's not allowed: no pushing, no kicking a person, only the ball can be touched, and both feet must stay on the ground (no jumping in to tackle).</li><li>If a ball gets knocked out of the grid, that student quickly retrieves it, dribbles back in, and play continues straight away — there's no stopping or restarting for the whole class.</li><li>Start play on your signal and let pairs go continuously for 2–3 minutes.</li><li>Rotate students into new pairs partway through so they experience different opponents.</li><li>Stop the whole class together, not pair by pair, so nobody feels singled out for finishing early.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Gate Challenge</h4><p>Pairs in a small square with two cone gates on opposite sides; score by dribbling through either gate while your partner tries to poke the ball away.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>In pairs, mark out a small square (about 6m x 6m) with two 'gates' — each gate is two cones placed 1–1.5m apart on opposite sides of the square. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Change of pace to unbalance the defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender must stay 2m back until attacker starts moving.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Both gates defended by same player.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student starts as the attacker with the ball, the other as the defender.</li><li>The attacker scores a point by dribbling the ball through either gate under control (the ball must pass fully between the two cones).</li><li>The defender's only job is to use their feet to poke or block the ball — no pushing, holding, or sliding tackles allowed.</li><li>After a goal is scored, or if the defender wins the ball and dribbles it out of the square, restart with the attacker taking the ball again from the centre.</li><li>Play for a set time (e.g. 90 seconds) then swap attacker and defender roles.</li><li>Remind students both gates are always open — the attacker should look for whichever gate is easier to reach based on where the defender is standing.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Possession Soccer</h3><p>3v3 or 4v4, small pitch, no goals. A team scores a point for 5 consecutive touches/passes kept without the other team touching the ball. Close control determines who can keep the ball under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 6 or 8, and set up one small pitch per group (no goals needed) using cones for corners — roughly 15m x 10m works well for 3v3, slightly bigger for 4v4. Bibs to mark two teams within each group.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain scoring: a team earns 1 point every time they string together 5 consecutive touches/passes without the other team touching the ball. Call the count out loud as it happens so students can hear ('1...2...3...') to build anticipation.</li><li>If the ball goes out of play or the other team wins it, the count resets to zero for whatever team now has the ball.</li><li>There are no goals in this game — the only way to score is through consecutive possession, so the focus is entirely on keeping the ball under pressure using close control.</li><li>Restart after every point or turnover with a simple pass-in from the sideline nearest to where the ball went out, rather than stopping play for long resets.</li><li>Circulate between pitches, calling out and celebrating good close control moments you see, not just final point totals.</li><li>Run 2–3 short games (2–3 minutes each) with a quick water break and team reshuffle in between if time allows.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you keep the ball close today?</li><li>Who did you notice controlling the ball really well — what were they doing with their feet?</li><li>What was tricky, and how did you keep trying?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Ball stays within playing distance during free dribbling</li><li>Uses more than just the front of the foot</li><li>Recovers control quickly after a heavy touch</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a larger 'close control' tolerance for students newer to the ball; pair confident dribblers with less confident ones in Sole Tag rather than same-ability pairs.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Gear Shift":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling with change of direction/speed</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Knowing and understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing what your feet are doing and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions when changing direction in tight grids — remind students to check space before turning</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to change direction and speed while keeping control of the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can speed up and slow down without losing the ball</li><li>I can turn the ball sharply using the inside/outside of my foot</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Colour Cone Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 4 different-coloured cones evenly around a large open space (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25). Every student needs their own ball. No lines or grids needed — the whole space is open for free movement.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the 4 colours before starting and assign each one a simple action, e.g. Red = dribble to a red cone and touch it with your foot; Blue = dribble to a blue cone and do a sharp turn around it; Yellow = dribble to a yellow cone and stop the ball dead; Green = dribble to a green cone and speed up away from it.</li><li>Demonstrate each action briefly with a ball so students see it once before starting.</li><li>On 'go', everyone dribbles freely around the space, choosing their own path between cones.</li><li>Call out a colour every 10–15 seconds — students dribble to the nearest cone of that colour and perform the matching action.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of colours called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students leave their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3–4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15–20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use the outside or inside of the foot to push the ball into a new direction.</td><td>This lets you change direction without stopping the ball dead or slowing down too much.</td></tr><tr><td>Drop your hips and lower your centre of gravity before changing direction.</td><td>A lower body position lets you cut sharply without losing balance.</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerate for 2-3 touches immediately after the change of direction.</td><td>The change of direction only creates separation from a defender if you explode away from it straight after.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Cone Slalom Sprint</h4><p>Individual dribble through a zig-zag line of cones as fast as possible without losing control; race against own best time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4–6 parallel slalom lines of 5–6 cones each in a zig-zag pattern (cones about 1.5m apart), spaced well apart from each other so students aren't running into neighbouring lines. One ball per student, one line per student or small group taking turns.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small touches near cones, bigger touches in open space.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider cone spacing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower spacing / timed leaderboard.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the slalom once yourself: small, quick touches when weaving close to cones, bigger touches to cover ground in the open sections.</li><li>Students line up at the start of their slalom line.</li><li>On 'go', each student dribbles through their own zig-zag as fast as they can while keeping the ball under control — if they lose control badly, they simply collect the ball and continue rather than starting over.</li><li>Time individuals informally (a stopwatch or just counted 'Mississippis') so each student is racing their own previous attempt, not each other.</li><li>Rotate through the line 3–4 times, encouraging students to try beating their own best time or number of touches.</li><li>For safety, only one student per lane at a time — the next student waits until the lane is clear.</li></ul><hr><h4>Number Call Turns</h4><p>Each student has a number; dribbling freely, when their number is called they must do a sharp turn (inside-of-foot cut) before continuing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>One large open grid for the whole class (or split into two grids for bigger classes), each student with their own ball. Assign every student a number from 1 up (write numbers on a whiteboard or just count off at the start).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Plant foot beside the ball, turn body and ball together.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer numbers active at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two numbers called in sequence quickly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the turn technique first: plant the non-kicking foot beside the ball, use the inside of the other foot to drag/cut the ball in a new direction, turning the whole body with it.</li><li>Everyone dribbles freely inside the grid at a gentle pace.</li><li>Call out a number at random — every student with that number must immediately perform a sharp inside-of-foot turn before continuing to dribble.</li><li>Students without that number keep dribbling normally, watching where they're going so they don't collide with someone turning nearby.</li><li>Call numbers every 5–10 seconds in a random order so all students get several turns during the game.</li><li>Occasionally call two different numbers back-to-back for extra challenge once students are comfortable with the basic pattern.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shrinking Grid Dribble</h4><p>Whole class dribbles inside a grid that shrinks every 30 seconds; those still in full control 'survive' each round (no elimination — just a challenge marker).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out a large grid (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25) using cones at the corners and a few along each edge — the edge cones are what you'll move inward. One ball per student, all starting inside the grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use quick changes of direction to find space.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shrink slower.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shrink faster, add a second ball-less 'space raider'.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that everyone dribbles freely inside the grid, trying to keep control of their own ball while avoiding others.</li><li>Every 30 seconds, pause briefly and move the edge cones inward on all four sides to shrink the playing area.</li><li>Restart dribbling immediately after each shrink — there's no elimination, just an increasing challenge to find space and keep control as things get tighter.</li><li>Coach live during play: point out students using quick changes of direction well to find gaps.</li><li>Once the grid gets very small (or after 3–4 shrinks), reset it back to full size and start again, or move into the next activity.</li><li>For the 'harder' version, add one or two ball-less 'space raiders' who move through the grid (without touching anyone's ball) to make finding open space trickier.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: End Zone Dribble</h3><p>4v4, pitch split into thirds; teams try to dribble the ball into the opposition's end zone (not passing — must dribble it in) while defenders try to intercept. Change of pace/direction is essential to beat defenders in the middle third.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4, pitch divided into three even thirds using cones (roughly 25m x 15m total, so each third is about 8m deep). No goals — instead mark a clear 'end zone' line at each end of the pitch.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the objective: teams score by dribbling the ball into the opposition's end zone under their own control — passing the ball into the end zone does not count as a score, only dribbling it in.</li><li>Defenders try to win the ball back using feet only (no sliding), especially in the tight middle third.</li><li>When a team scores by dribbling into the end zone, that team keeps the point and the ball is walked back to the centre for a simple restart with the other team.</li><li>If the ball goes out of bounds, restart with a dribble-in (not a throw-in) for the team that didn't put it out, keeping the focus on dribbling skills.</li><li>Coach specifically for change of pace and direction in the tight middle third — point out students who slow down, then explode past a defender.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks, swapping which end each team attacks if needed for fairness.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did changing speed do to help you get past a defender or through a gap?</li><li>Which turn (inside/outside foot) felt most natural to you?</li><li>What would you try differently next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Clear change of pace (not just constant speed)</li><li>Uses inside/outside of foot to turn</li><li>Keeps head up briefly to spot space</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find rapid direction change hard, allow a 'two-touch turn' (slower, controlled) rather than a single sharp touch.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Escape Artist":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Dribbling under light pressure (1v1)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills faithfully to give your partner a fair, honest challenge — not going so hard it isn't fun, not giving up either.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for grids/gates</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No sliding/lunging tackles — feet-only pressure, stay upright</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep control of the ball while someone is trying to win it from us.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can shield the ball with my body from a defender</li><li>I can find a way past a defender using a change of direction</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Colour Cone Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 4 different-coloured cones evenly around a large open space (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25). Every student needs their own ball. No lines or grids needed — the whole space is open for free movement.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the 4 colours before starting and assign each one a simple action, e.g. Red = dribble to a red cone and touch it with your foot; Blue = dribble to a blue cone and do a sharp turn around it; Yellow = dribble to a yellow cone and stop the ball dead; Green = dribble to a green cone and speed up away from it.</li><li>Demonstrate each action briefly with a ball so students see it once before starting.</li><li>On 'go', everyone dribbles freely around the space, choosing their own path between cones.</li><li>Call out a colour every 10–15 seconds — students dribble to the nearest cone of that colour and perform the matching action.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of colours called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students leave their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3–4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15–20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your body between the ball and the defender.</td><td>Shields the ball so the defender has to go through or around you to win it.</td></tr><tr><td>Use small, controlled touches when a defender is close.</td><td>Big touches are easy for a nearby defender to intercept.</td></tr><tr><td>Look up briefly to see the space or a teammate before committing to a move.</td><td>Dribbling past a defender should open something up, not just be for its own sake.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Dribble</h4><p>Attacker dribbles freely; a 'shadow' defender stays 1.5m away and mirrors movement without touching the ball, just to get the attacker used to pressure nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, each pair with one ball. No cones needed — just enough room (roughly 8m x 8m per pair) for the attacker to move freely.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep body between ball and defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender stays further back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to get closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure (delayed contact)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner is the attacker with the ball, the other is the 'shadow' defender.</li><li>Explain the shadow's job: stay about 1.5m away from the attacker at all times, mirroring their movement, but never reaching in to touch the ball.</li><li>The attacker dribbles freely around the space, changing pace and direction, getting used to having someone close by without panicking.</li><li>Coach the attacker to keep their body between the ball and the shadow whenever the shadow gets close.</li><li>Run for about 60–90 seconds, then swap roles so both partners experience being the attacker.</li><li>For 'harder', let the shadow drift a little closer (still not tackling) to raise the pressure gradually.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 to Two Gates</h4><p>As Lesson 1's gate game but now the defender may actively try to win the ball (feet only), not just poke.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same square-and-two-gates layout as Lesson 1's gate game (6m x 6m square, two gates of two cones each on opposite sides). One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Shield with your body, use quick feet to protect.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker gets 2 'lives' before defender wins the round.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the gate-scoring rule from Lesson 1: dribble the ball cleanly through either gate to score.</li><li>New rule for today: the defender may now actively try to win the ball with their feet, not just poke at it — but still no sliding tackles, pushing, or holding.</li><li>Attacker starts with the ball in the middle of the square and looks to protect it while working toward a gate.</li><li>If the defender wins the ball and dribbles it under control for 2 seconds, that counts as a win for them — restart with the attacker taking the ball again.</li><li>Play for 90 seconds then swap attacker and defender.</li><li>For 'easier', give the attacker 2 'lives' — they can lose the ball once and simply restart before the defender's win properly counts.</li></ul><hr><h4>King of the Grid</h4><p>Small group (4–5), everyone dribbles their own ball in a shared grid; if your ball gets knocked out you retrieve it and rejoin — the 'king' has kept control the longest streak without being dispossessed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4–5 students share one grid (about 12m x 12m for a group of 5), each student with their own ball inside the grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Constant scanning for the nearest threat.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, fewer players.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, more players.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, role rotation via re-entry</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: everyone dribbles their own ball inside the shared grid while trying to knock other players' balls out using their feet, protecting their own ball at the same time.</li><li>If your ball gets knocked outside the grid, quickly retrieve it, dribble back in, and keep playing — there's no elimination or sitting out.</li><li>Play continuously for 2–3 minutes with everyone in the grid at once.</li><li>The 'king' is whoever can tell you they kept control the longest streak without being dispossessed — ask a couple of students to share their streak at the end rather than making it a strict competition.</li><li>Coach constant scanning: remind students to check around them regularly rather than only watching their own ball.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the grid or add players; for 'easier', enlarge the grid or reduce numbers.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 1v1 Alley Tournament</h3><p>Pairs take turns in a narrow 'alley' pitch with a small goal at each end — 1v1, first to 3 goals or most goals in 2 minutes, then rotate partners. Shielding and quick changes of direction under real pressure decide most points.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up several narrow 'alley' pitches (about 15m long x 5m wide) side by side, each with a small goal (two cones 1.5m apart, no need for a real goal frame) at both ends. Pair students up, one pair per alley, with spare pairs waiting to rotate in.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: 1v1 within the alley, first to score 3 goals wins, or most goals scored within a fixed 2-minute time limit if no one reaches 3.</li><li>Players can score in either goal they're attacking, and must defend both of their own goals.</li><li>No sliding tackles — feet-only challenges, staying upright at all times.</li><li>Start with a kick-off from the centre of the alley; after each goal, the scored-against player restarts from their own goal.</li><li>After the time limit or reaching 3 goals, rotate: winners could play winners, or simply rotate to a fresh partner from a waiting pair, so everyone gets 2–3 different matchups.</li><li>Coach shielding and quick changes of direction throughout — these are the skills that decide most points in this tight space.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What worked well to protect the ball from your partner?</li><li>How did it feel to defend fairly and try your best without being too rough?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try next time you're 1v1?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses body/shoulder to shield</li><li>Keeps ball on the far foot from the defender</li><li>Attempts a change of direction under pressure rather than just running into the defender</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For lower-confidence pairs, use 'passive defenders' (hands behind back, feet only, walking pace) so the attacker experiences pressure without high stress.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Laser Passes":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing — short, accurate ground passes</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — A good, accurate pass makes the game better for everyone else — thinking about how your actions impact your teammate, not just yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones for targets/gates</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passing lanes should not cross other pairs' lanes — space grids out</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball accurately along the ground to a partner.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can strike the ball with the inside of my foot so it travels straight</li><li>I can pass so my partner doesn't have to move far to receive it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Colour Cone Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 4 different-coloured cones evenly around a large open space (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25). Every student needs their own ball. No lines or grids needed — the whole space is open for free movement.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the 4 colours before starting and assign each one a simple action, e.g. Red = dribble to a red cone and touch it with your foot; Blue = dribble to a blue cone and do a sharp turn around it; Yellow = dribble to a yellow cone and stop the ball dead; Green = dribble to a green cone and speed up away from it.</li><li>Demonstrate each action briefly with a ball so students see it once before starting.</li><li>On 'go', everyone dribbles freely around the space, choosing their own path between cones.</li><li>Call out a colour every 10–15 seconds — students dribble to the nearest cone of that colour and perform the matching action.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of colours called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students leave their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3–4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15–20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Plant the non-kicking foot beside the ball, pointing at the target.</td><td>Your plant foot aims the pass — if it points the wrong way, the ball often follows it.</td></tr><tr><td>Strike the ball with the inside of the foot, ankle locked.</td><td>The inside of the foot gives the largest, flattest contact surface for accuracy; a locked ankle stops the ball ballooning up.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target.</td><td>Stopping the leg early can leave the pass short or send it off-line.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pass &amp; Retreat</h4><p>Partners face each other and pass; after every pass, the passer takes one big step backward and the receiver may only take one step sideways to collect it. Distance grows each successful pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with plenty of space between pairs (start about 4m apart facing each other), one ball per pair, in an open area or lined-up channels so passing lanes don't cross.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Strike with the inside of the foot, follow through toward the target.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Two steps sideways allowed for receiver.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passer takes two steps back each time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Movement penalty/reward</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners start facing each other about 4m apart and pass the ball back and forth using the inside of the foot.</li><li>After every pass, the passer takes one big step backward, increasing the passing distance a little each time.</li><li>The receiver is only allowed one step sideways to collect the ball under control before passing it back.</li><li>If a pass goes astray or the receiver needs more than one step, simply reset to the last successful distance and continue rather than starting from scratch.</li><li>Keep going until pairs reach a distance where it becomes genuinely challenging, then reset back to the start distance and try to beat their record.</li><li>Coach striking technique throughout: inside of the foot, follow through toward the target, plant foot pointing where the pass should go.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gate Passing</h4><p>Pairs pass through a small cone gate between them from increasing distances; score a point each time the ball goes cleanly through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs each set up one small gate (two cones about 1m apart) roughly halfway between them, starting about 5m apart on either side of the gate. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Plant foot points at the target.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gate.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gate, further apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners stand on opposite sides of their gate and pass the ball to each other, aiming to send it cleanly through the gate.</li><li>A point is scored each time the ball passes fully between the two cones without touching either one.</li><li>After a few successful passes at the starting distance, both partners take a step back to increase the distance and keep scoring points.</li><li>If the ball misses the gate, the receiver collects it and simply passes back — no need to reset distance immediately unless it's clearly too hard.</li><li>Coach plant-foot alignment: the non-kicking foot should point at the target (the gate) to help with accuracy.</li><li>For 'harder', narrow the gate width or increase the starting distance between partners.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Passing Race</h4><p>Groups of 3 form a triangle and must complete 10 accurate passes around the triangle as fast as possible, racing other triangles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 form a triangle using cones (each side about 3–4m), one ball per triangle, several triangles spread across the space so groups can see each other for the 'race' element.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Prepare your body/feet before the ball arrives so the return pass is quick.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller triangle.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger triangle, must alternate feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each group of 3 stands at the points of their triangle.</li><li>On 'go', the group passes the ball around the triangle in order, counting each accurate pass out loud.</li><li>The first group to reach 10 accurate passes calls out and puts their hands up to signal they've finished.</li><li>If a pass is miscontrolled or goes to the wrong person, the group doesn't lose their count — they just recover the ball and continue.</li><li>Run the race 2–3 times so groups can try to beat their own time or beat other groups.</li><li>Coach receivers to prepare their body and feet before the ball arrives so the next pass out can be quick — this is what makes teams fast.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Possession Passing Soccer</h3><p>4v4, small pitch, no goals — 1 point per 5 consecutive accurate passes kept as a team. Building on Lesson 1's version but now explicitly coached/scored on pass accuracy, not just touches.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with no goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams. Same layout as Lesson 1's possession game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the scoring rule: 1 point for every 5 consecutive accurate passes a team completes without the other team touching the ball.</li><li>New focus for today: passes must be accurate and controllable, not just any touch — coach specifically calls out and praises well-weighted, accurate passes rather than just counting touches.</li><li>Count passes out loud as they happen so students can hear the streak building.</li><li>If the ball is intercepted or goes out of play, the count resets to zero and restarts with a simple pass-in.</li><li>Circulate between groups giving specific feedback on plant foot and follow-through, connecting back to today's Skill Games.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks for water and quick team talk.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What technique helped your passes go where you wanted?</li><li>How did a good pass make it easier for your partner?</li><li>What happens to the game when passes aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses inside of the foot (not toe-poking)</li><li>Plant foot points toward target</li><li>Pass weight suits the distance (not always maximum power)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Shorten distances for students still building leg strength/technique; the game structures (gates, triangles) scale naturally by moving cones closer.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Soft Hands, Soft Feet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Receiving/first touch (cushioning the ball)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself (and encouraging teammates) when a first touch doesn't go to plan — everyone's still learning.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Passes should stay below head height in early games — no volleys/headers yet</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to control ('cushion') an incoming pass so the ball stays close to us.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can take the pace off an incoming ball using my foot, thigh, or chest</li><li>I can set up my next touch to face where I want to go</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Colour Cone Dribble</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 4 different-coloured cones evenly around a large open space (roughly 20m x 20m for a class of 20-25). Every student needs their own ball. No lines or grids needed — the whole space is open for free movement.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the 4 colours before starting and assign each one a simple action, e.g. Red = dribble to a red cone and touch it with your foot; Blue = dribble to a blue cone and do a sharp turn around it; Yellow = dribble to a yellow cone and stop the ball dead; Green = dribble to a green cone and speed up away from it.</li><li>Demonstrate each action briefly with a ball so students see it once before starting.</li><li>On 'go', everyone dribbles freely around the space, choosing their own path between cones.</li><li>Call out a colour every 10–15 seconds — students dribble to the nearest cone of that colour and perform the matching action.</li><li>Keep the pace light and playful — this is about raising heart rates and getting first touches on the ball, not testing skill.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the order of colours called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students leave their ball to the side and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3–4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15–20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get your body in line with the ball early.</td><td>Gives you more surface and time to control it rather than stretching at the last second.</td></tr><tr><td>Relax the receiving foot or surface and withdraw slightly on contact.</td><td>'Giving' with the touch absorbs the ball's speed instead of it bouncing away.</td></tr><tr><td>Cushion the ball into space away from pressure, not straight to your feet.</td><td>A touch into space keeps you moving and away from a defender, rather than stopping dead.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Cushion Catch</h4><p>Partners pass; receiver must 'cushion' the ball to a dead stop within one touch before passing back — a point is lost if the ball bounces away more than a big step.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4–5m apart, one ball per pair, open space with no obstacles.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Relax the receiving surface, withdraw the foot slightly on contact.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passes with more pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'cushioning' first: as the ball arrives, relax the receiving foot and withdraw it slightly on contact so the ball dies dead rather than bouncing away.</li><li>Partners pass the ball to each other using the inside of the foot.</li><li>The receiver must cushion the ball to a complete stop within one touch before passing it back.</li><li>If the ball bounces away more than about one big step from the receiver, that pass 'loses' the point for the pair — simply collect the ball and continue, no need to restart the whole game.</li><li>Keep count of consecutive clean cushions as a friendly team target ('let's get to 10 in a row').</li><li>For 'harder', partners pass with more pace once the basic technique is solid; for 'easier', slow the passes right down.</li></ul><hr><h4>First Touch, Second Touch, Go</h4><p>Receiver must control the ball with their first touch in a chosen direction (away from an imaginary defender) with their second touch, then dribble to a target cone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs with one ball, a target cone placed a few metres away from the receiver to dribble toward. Space pairs out so dribbling lanes don't cross.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Touch should go into space, not straight down.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Mark the 'away' direction with a cone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Passer calls the direction just before passing (reaction).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the sequence: first touch controls the ball away from an imaginary defender (to the side), second touch sets it up in the direction of the target cone, then dribble to the cone.</li><li>The passer plays a simple pass to the receiver to start each rep.</li><li>The receiver controls with their first touch, directs with their second touch, then dribbles to the target cone and back.</li><li>Swap roles so the passer becomes the receiver for the next rep.</li><li>For 'easier', mark the 'away' direction with a cone so students know exactly where to direct their first touch.</li><li>For 'harder', the passer calls out a direction just before passing so the receiver must react rather than plan ahead.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Receive</h4><p>As Skill Game 1, but a light 'shadow' defender stands nearby (not tackling yet) to add visual/decision pressure on the first touch.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pair setup as First Touch, Second Touch, Go, but add a third student as a light 'shadow' defender standing a few metres from the receiver.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check over your shoulder before the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close in after the pass is played.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up in threes: passer, receiver, and shadow defender standing nearby (not tackling).</li><li>The passer plays the ball in as before; the shadow defender stands close enough to be seen but does not attempt to win the ball.</li><li>The receiver checks over their shoulder to spot the defender before the ball arrives, then controls and directs their first touch away from that defender.</li><li>Rotate roles every 4–5 reps so each student experiences passing, receiving under pressure, and being the shadow.</li><li>For 'easier', the shadow starts further away; for 'harder', the shadow is allowed to close in after the pass is played (still without tackling).</li><li>Coach checking over the shoulder as the key habit — this is what separates confident receivers from surprised ones.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3-Touch Possession Soccer</h3><p>4v4, small pitch, no goals — each player is limited to 3 touches maximum before passing, rewarding a clean first touch that sets up the next action quickly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch, no goals, bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the touch limit: every player may take a maximum of 3 touches before they must pass — 1 (control), 2 (set up), 3 (release).</li><li>If a player takes a 4th touch, possession switches to the other team on the spot.</li><li>Play possession-style: no goals, just keeping the ball as a team under the touch limit.</li><li>Coach specifically on first touch quality — a clean first touch that sets up the next action quickly is what makes the 3-touch limit work.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after any touch-limit turnover or ball out of play.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds, reminding students this connects directly to the cushioning and first-touch work from today's Skill Games.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a 'good' first touch look and feel like today?</li><li>How did your first touch help (or make harder) your next move?</li><li>What will you focus on next time the ball comes to you?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Ball stays within a step of the receiver after first touch</li><li>Touch is directed with purpose, not just stopped dead</li><li>Body shape/shoulder check before the ball arrives</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find cushioning difficult, allow trapping under the sole of the foot as a valid 'good enough' first touch before progressing to inside-of-foot cushioning.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Skills & Attacking Basics (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Give and Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Passing on the move (give-and-go)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role and where you fit in the play — knowing when to move and why.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep passing lanes clear of other groups when running through space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to pass the ball and immediately move into space to receive it back.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can pass and then run into space, not stand still</li><li>I can time my pass so a moving teammate can control it easily</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pulse Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently using the inside of the foot.</li><li>Every 30–45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle taps.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3–4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15–20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pass, then immediately move into new space.</td><td>Standing still after passing makes you easy to mark and removes you as an option.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your run so you arrive as the return pass arrives.</td><td>Arriving too early or too late means you have to stop and wait, losing the tempo advantage.</td></tr><tr><td>Receive on the move with an open body shape.</td><td>Keeps your options — pass, dribble, shoot — available immediately rather than needing an extra touch to turn.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Wall Pass Relay</h4><p>Pairs practise 'pass and move': Player A passes to Player B and immediately jogs to a new cone; B controls and passes back to A's new position. Repeat down a channel.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread along a channel about 15m long marked with cones on each side. One ball per pair. Several cones placed along the channel as target markers for Player A to jog to.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move immediately after passing, don't watch the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk instead of jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a defender jogging alongside (not intercepting yet).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Player A starts with the ball, Player B stands a few metres away facing them.</li><li>Player A passes to Player B, then immediately jogs forward to the next cone marker down the channel — don't stand and admire the pass.</li><li>Player B controls the ball with one touch and passes it into the space where Player A has jogged to.</li><li>Repeat this pattern all the way down the channel: pass, move, receive, pass, move.</li><li>At the end of the channel, pairs jog back to the start and go again, or swap so Player B leads the movement this time.</li><li>For 'easier', walk instead of jog; for 'harder', add a third student jogging alongside as a passive defender (not intercepting yet).</li></ul><hr><h4>Give and Go Gates</h4><p>In trios, two attackers combine give-and-go passes to move the ball through a series of gates down the field, racing another trio.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers plus one passive/timing role, or simply two attackers working together), set up a line of 3–4 gates (two cones each, 1.5m apart) spaced down a channel about 20m long. One ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pass early, before you're under real pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gates/no time pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower gates, timed race.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The two attackers work together to move the ball through each gate in sequence using give-and-go passes: pass to your partner, run into space, receive the pass back.</li><li>The third group member can act as a timer/starter, or trios can race each other side by side down parallel channels.</li><li>A gate is 'passed' when the ball travels through it cleanly (either by pass or dribble) as part of the sequence.</li><li>Race down the channel through all gates as quickly as possible while still completing genuine give-and-go combinations, not just dribbling through.</li><li>Reset at the start and go again 2–3 times, timing informally to build a sense of pace.</li><li>For 'easier', widen the gates and remove time pressure; for 'harder', narrow the gates and add a clock.</li></ul><hr><h4>1-2 vs Shadow Defender</h4><p>Pairs attempt a give-and-go against one light-touch defender who can only intercept after the first pass is played (delayed pressure).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs of attackers plus one defender per group (groups of 3), working in a small area about 10m x 10m. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The 'give' player should move into space immediately, not just stand and admire the pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender starts further back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender allowed to close immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One attacker starts with the ball, their partner nearby ready to support, and the defender positioned between them and the target.</li><li>Explain the delayed-pressure rule: the defender may only try to intercept the ball after the first pass has been played, not before.</li><li>The attacker with the ball plays a pass to their partner (the 'give'), then immediately moves into space to receive it back (the 'go').</li><li>The receiving partner looks to return the pass into the space the first attacker has moved into, completing the give-and-go past the defender.</li><li>Rotate roles regularly so every student experiences both attacking roles and defending.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender starts further back; for 'harder', the defender can close in immediately once the first pass is played.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 3v3 + 1 Target Player Soccer</h3><p>Small-sided game with small goals; encourage give-and-go combinations as a way to beat defenders, especially near the target player position. Coach highlights any successful give-and-go in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8, small pitch with small goals at each end (about 20m x 15m), with one 'target player' zone marked near each goal. Bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target player idea: each team can use a designated target player positioned near the opponent's goal to combine with as they attack.</li><li>Encourage teams to use give-and-go combinations (pass, move into space, receive back) to beat defenders, especially around the target player.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with goals — teams score by getting the ball into the small goal under normal rules.</li><li>Whenever a successful give-and-go combination happens in play, stop briefly (or call it out without stopping) to celebrate it so students connect today's skill to real game moments.</li><li>Rotate which student plays the target player role every few minutes so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a give-and-go work well today?</li><li>Why does moving after your pass matter?</li><li>Can you describe a moment you found space at the right time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves immediately after passing</li><li>Receives the return pass without needing to stop and reset</li><li>Communicates (calls for the ball) with a teammate</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the whole combination down to walking pace for students still building the timing/decision sequence; speed increases as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Perfect Strike":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Striking technique (shooting form)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising a skill faithfully, with focus and effort, even when it doesn't work first time — using your talent well takes repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, small goals/targets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear 'shooting lanes,' no one stands behind/beside a goal while shots are being taken</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct shooting technique — how to strike the ball cleanly toward a target.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can place my non-kicking foot beside the ball</li><li>I can strike through the middle of the ball with the top of my laces or inside of my foot</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pulse Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently using the inside of the foot.</li><li>Every 30–45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle taps.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3–4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15–20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Plant the non-kicking foot alongside the ball, pointing at the target.</td><td>This foot aims the shot, just as it does for a pass.</td></tr><tr><td>Strike through the middle or lower half of the ball.</td><td>Contact point below centre controls trajectory and helps lift the ball if needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your head down and eyes on the ball through contact.</td><td>Lifting your head early is the most common cause of scuffed or mis-hit shots.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Technique Target Shots</h4><p>From a stationary ball, students shoot at a large target (e.g. a taped square on a wall or a big cone gate) focusing purely on technique, not power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up several shooting stations, each with a large target (a taped square on a wall, or a big cone gate about 2m wide) and a stationary ball placed a set distance away (start around 6–8m). Small groups rotate through stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Plant foot beside the ball, eyes on the ball at contact, follow through.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer to target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the technique: plant foot beside the ball pointing at the target, eyes on the ball at the moment of contact, strike through the middle of the ball, and follow through toward the target.</li><li>Students take turns shooting one at a time from a stationary ball at their station's target.</li><li>The focus is purely on technique, not power — a soft, well-struck shot that hits the target is better than a hard, scrappy one that misses.</li><li>After each shot, the student retrieves their ball and returns to the back of their station's line.</li><li>Give quick individual feedback on plant foot position and follow-through as students take their turns.</li><li>For 'easier', move closer to the target; for 'harder', move further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Roll and Strike</h4><p>A partner rolls the ball toward the shooter, who must strike a moving ball cleanly at a target — introducing timing on top of technique.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs at shooting stations, one ball per pair, a target set up a set distance from the shooter (similar to Technique Target Shots).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust the run-up to meet the ball, don't rush the strike.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slow, straight rolls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied speed/angle rolls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (varied delivery)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner stands off to the side with the ball, the other stands ready to shoot at the target.</li><li>The partner rolls the ball firmly toward the shooter along the ground, aimed so the shooter can strike it in one clean movement.</li><li>The shooter adjusts their run-up and timing to meet the moving ball rather than rushing the strike, then shoots at the target using the technique from Skill Game 1.</li><li>Swap roles after every 4–5 shots so both partners get rolling and shooting practice.</li><li>Coach timing specifically: 'don't rush to meet the ball — let it come to the right spot.'</li><li>For 'easier', roll slow and straight; for 'harder', vary the speed and angle of the roll.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shot Streak Challenge</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a shooting station, each getting quick-fire shots at a target from a feed; the group tracks consecutive 'clean technique' shots as a team streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups (3–4) rotate through a shooting station with one 'feeder' delivering balls and a target zone marked with cones. A ball supply nearby for quick-fire feeding.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your technique each shot, don't chase power.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower feeds, bigger target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker feeds, smaller target zones worth bonus points.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student feeds the ball (rolling or a simple pass) to the shooter, one student shoots at a time, others wait in line.</li><li>The shooter takes a quick shot at the target using good technique, then moves to the back of the line.</li><li>The group counts consecutive shots with 'clean technique' (good plant foot, contact, follow-through) as a team streak — this can be judged by the group itself or a nearby helper.</li><li>If a shot has poor technique (even if it scores), the streak resets to zero and the group starts counting again.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role every few reps so everyone experiences both feeding and shooting.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower feeds and a bigger target; for 'harder', use quicker feeds and smaller target zones worth bonus points.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Goals Soccer</h3><p>4v4 with small goals; emphasis in coaching feedback is on clean technique when shooting, not just outcome — celebrate good technique attempts even if they miss.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals at each end.</li><li>Explain to students that today's coaching feedback focuses on shooting technique, not just whether the shot goes in — a well-struck shot that's saved is still worth celebrating.</li><li>Circulate and call out good technique moments loudly and specifically ('great follow-through on that shot!') even when the shot misses or is saved.</li><li>Keep score normally, but remind the group that technique feedback matters as much as the scoreline today.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in or goal kick equivalent after goals or the ball going out.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'good technique' feel like when you strike the ball well?</li><li>What part of your shooting technique are you most proud of today?</li><li>What will you keep practising?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Non-kicking foot placed beside the ball</li><li>Eyes on the ball at the point of contact</li><li>Balanced follow-through (not falling backward)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a lighter/softer ball for students building leg strength or confidence around striking; success is measured by technique, not distance/power.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Pick Your Spot":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shooting accuracy &amp; placement</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your shot will have the most impact (an open space in goal) rather than just reacting.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, goals divided into zones (cones/markers), cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Same shooting-lane clearances as Lesson 7</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to place a shot in a specific part of the goal, not just strike it hard.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a target area before I shoot</li><li>I can place the ball into that area more often than not</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pulse Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently using the inside of the foot.</li><li>Every 30–45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle taps.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3–4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15–20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pick a specific target — a corner or gap — before you strike.</td><td>Aiming generally at the goal is far less accurate than committing to one spot.</td></tr><tr><td>Use the inside of the foot for placement, laces for power.</td><td>Different contact surfaces suit different goals — accuracy versus power.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay balanced through the strike, arms out for balance.</td><td>Losing balance sends the shot off target regardless of technique.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Zone Shooting</h4><p>Goal is divided into 4 zones with cones; teacher calls a zone before each shot, students must place the ball there.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a goal divided into 4 zones using cones or markers (e.g. top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right), with a ball supply and a shooting spot marked a set distance away.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Body/foot angle determines direction — line it up before striking.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zones, called right before the shot.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Students take turns at the shooting spot with a ball.</li><li>Before each shot, the teacher (or a partner) calls out a target zone ('bottom-left!').</li><li>The student must strike the ball so it lands in the called zone, using body and foot angle to direct it rather than just power.</li><li>After each shot, retrieve the ball and return to the line for the next turn.</li><li>Give feedback on body/foot alignment — remind students to line up their plant foot and shoulders toward the zone before striking.</li><li>For 'easier', use bigger zones; for 'harder', use smaller zones and call the zone right before the shot (less time to line up).</li></ul><hr><h4>Beat the Keeper Angles</h4><p>A student 'keeper' stands in different positions; the shooter must pick the open side of goal and place their shot there.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>One student acts as keeper in a small goal, one shooter at a marked spot a set distance away, others waiting in line to rotate through both roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the space, not the keeper.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Keeper stands still/central.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Keeper moves just before the shot.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The keeper stands in different starting positions within the goal (centre, slightly left, slightly right) before each shot.</li><li>The shooter looks at where the open space in the goal is — not directly at the keeper — and picks their shot to go into that open side.</li><li>The shooter strikes the ball toward the open side of the goal.</li><li>Rotate: after each shot, the shooter goes to the back of the line and the next student shoots; swap the keeper role every 4–5 shots.</li><li>Coach 'look at the space, not the keeper' as the key cue throughout.</li><li>For 'easier', keeper stays still and central; for 'harder', keeper can move slightly just before the shot.</li></ul><hr><h4>Points Target Shootout</h4><p>Zones worth different points (corners = 3, middle = 1); small groups compete for the highest team score in a set number of shots.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Divide a goal into zones with different point values marked using cones or tape (corners worth more, e.g. 3 points, middle worth less, e.g. 1 point). Small groups take a set number of shots each (e.g. 5 shots per student).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Balance risk (harder corner shot) vs. reliability (safer central shot) — a decision-making element.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer high-value zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Time pressure added.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the points system before starting: corner zones score more (3 points) because they're harder to hit, the middle scores less (1 point) because it's easier and more reliable.</li><li>Each student takes their set number of shots in turn, aiming for whichever zone they choose.</li><li>The group keeps a running total for team score, adding up everyone's zone points.</li><li>After each student's turns, the group tallies the points before the next student goes.</li><li>Coach the decision-making side: is it worth risking the harder corner shot for more points, or playing it safe for a reliable score?</li><li>For 'easier', reduce the number of high-value zones; for 'harder', add a time limit for each student's turn.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Goals Soccer with Zone Bonus</h3><p>4v4 small goals; a goal scored into a marked 'bonus corner' area is worth 2 points, encouraging placement decisions in a real game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals, one corner of each goal marked as a 'bonus corner' zone using cones or tape.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain the bonus rule: any goal scored into the marked bonus corner zone is worth 2 points instead of 1.</li><li>Keep a running score for each team, calling out bonus goals clearly when they happen.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in or equivalent after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Coach placement decisions in the moment — encourage students to notice when the bonus corner is open and try to place a shot there rather than blasting centrally.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks, tallying total scores at the end if useful for engagement.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide where to place your shot today?</li><li>What's the difference between shooting hard and shooting smart?</li><li>Did you ever choose a safer option over a riskier one — why?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Looks at the target space before striking</li><li>Adjusts body/foot angle to aim, not just power</li><li>Shows awareness of risk vs. reliability in shot choice</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use larger target zones and closer distances for students still building accuracy; keep the 'decision-making' element (pick a zone) even if the physical target is generous.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Triple Threat":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining dribble → pass → shoot</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Enjoying the game and encouraging teammates as everyone tries to link skills together — celebrating good combinations, not just goals.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, small goals, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard shooting-lane clearance; watch for congestion near goals in small-sided drills</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine dribbling, passing, and shooting smoothly in one sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose the right moment to dribble, pass, or shoot</li><li>I can link these actions together without stopping awkwardly between them</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pulse Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently using the inside of the foot.</li><li>Every 30–45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle taps.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3–4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15–20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Take the touch that sets up your next action, not just any touch.</td><td>Each touch should be purposeful and lead cleanly into the pass or shot.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your head up between actions to read what's actually open.</td><td>The sequence should respond to the game, not run on autopilot.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide early — pass or shoot — so your body can prepare for it.</td><td>Last-second indecision leads to a rushed, poor-technique action.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Dribble-Pass-Shoot Circuit</h4><p>Individual circuit: dribble through cones, pass to a rebound target/wall, control the return, then shoot at goal. Timed for personal best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up one circuit per group of 4–5: a short line of cones to dribble through, a wall or rebound target to pass against, and a small goal to shoot at, all in sequence over about 15–20m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the sequence flowing — don't over-control between actions.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simplify the circuit (fewer cones).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a second rebound/pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the full sequence: dribble through the cones, pass the ball against the wall/rebound target, control the ball as it comes back, then shoot at the goal.</li><li>One student goes at a time while others wait at the start of the circuit.</li><li>Time each student informally so they can try to beat their own personal best on repeat attempts.</li><li>After shooting, the student collects their ball and jogs back to the start, and the next student in line begins.</li><li>Coach flow — remind students not to over-control between each part of the sequence, keeping the ball moving smoothly.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer cones in the dribble section; for 'harder', add a second rebound/pass into the sequence.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 to Goal</h4><p>Two attackers vs. one defender, working the ball down the field using dribble/pass combinations before shooting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) with a small goal at the end of a marked area about 15m x 10m. One ball per group, groups rotating through the defender role.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The extra attacker should decide dribble vs. pass based on the defender's position.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender passive/delayed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender active from the start.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Two attackers start with the ball at one end, one defender positions themselves between the attackers and the goal.</li><li>Attackers work the ball down the area toward the goal using a mix of dribbling and passing, trying to create a shooting chance.</li><li>The extra attacker (the one without the ball) should read the defender's position and decide whether to ask for a pass or let their partner dribble.</li><li>The defender uses feet only (no sliding) to try to win the ball or block the shot.</li><li>After a goal, save, or the defender winning the ball, reset with the attackers back at the start.</li><li>Rotate roles regularly so everyone experiences attacking and defending. For 'easier', defender starts passive/delayed; for 'harder', defender is active from the start.</li></ul><hr><h4>Combo Points Challenge</h4><p>Small games (3v3) where bonus points are awarded any time a team completes a genuine dribble→pass→shoot sequence (even if it misses).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 (3v3), small pitch with a small goal at each end (about 15m x 12m), bibs for two teams within each group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the combination, not just any shot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger pitch/more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller pitch.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain the bonus rule: any time a team completes a genuine dribble, then a pass, then a shot in the same team move, they earn bonus recognition — even if the shot misses.</li><li>Coach and students together watch for and call out these combination sequences during play ('that's a combo — dribble, pass, shot!').</li><li>Keep a simple tally of combo bonuses alongside normal goals if useful for engagement, or just celebrate them verbally.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger pitch or more time; for 'harder', use a smaller pitch to force quicker decisions.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Combo Bonus</h3><p>4v4 small goals; any goal that includes a visible dribble, pass, and shot in the same team move earns bonus recognition (celebrated, not necessarily extra formal points).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain that any goal which includes a visible dribble, a pass, and a shot in the same team move earns bonus recognition — celebrated out loud rather than necessarily worth extra formal points.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out combo goals enthusiastically when they happen, reinforcing the link between today's Skill Games and the full game.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in or equivalent after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages so students get maximum touches.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What combination of skills worked best for you today?</li><li>How did you decide between dribbling, passing, or shooting in the moment?</li><li>What was a great combination you saw a teammate do?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Links actions smoothly (minimal unnecessary touches between phases)</li><li>Makes a decision rather than defaulting to the same action every time</li><li>Recognises and reacts to defender positioning</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow extra touches/time in the circuit and 2v1 games for students still building confidence linking skills; the goal is fluency over speed at this stage.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Body Shield":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Turning with the ball / protecting the ball (shielding)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your position relative to pressure/space — reading the situation around you before deciding which way to turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No shoulder-charging — shielding is positioning, not physical contact beyond fair, feet-based challenges</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to turn away from pressure and shield the ball with our bodies.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use my body to keep the ball away from a defender</li><li>I can turn into space rather than into pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pulse Pass</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, enough room between pairs that passes don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin passing the ball back and forth gently using the inside of the foot.</li><li>Every 30–45 seconds, call a change: 'pulse up' means pass a little faster/firmer, 'pulse down' means slow right back to gentle taps.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue passing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up passing muscles, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3–4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15–20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep your body side-on between the ball and the defender.</td><td>This uses your body as a barrier so the defender has to go around you.</td></tr><tr><td>Use your far foot to shield the ball while your body blocks access.</td><td>Keeps the ball protected on the side away from the defender.</td></tr><tr><td>Turn away from pressure using the outside of the foot once a gap opens.</td><td>Turning into a defender loses the ball; turning away uses the space you've shielded.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Turn Away Challenge</h4><p>Receiving a pass with a shadow defender close behind, the receiver must turn away from the defender's side and dribble to a target.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (passer, receiver, shadow defender), a target cone placed a few metres beyond the receiver's starting position. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check over your shoulder before the ball arrives to know which way to turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender starts further back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender closer/faster.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender / pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The shadow defender stands close behind the receiver (not touching, just present) before the pass is played.</li><li>The passer plays a simple pass to the receiver.</li><li>As the ball arrives, the receiver checks over their shoulder to see which side the defender is on, then turns away from that side using their first touch and dribbles to the target cone.</li><li>Rotate roles every 4–5 reps so each student experiences passing, receiving/turning, and being the defender.</li><li>Coach the shoulder-check as the key habit — remind students to look before the ball arrives, not after.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender starts further back; for 'harder', the defender stands closer and moves faster.</li></ul><hr><h4>Protect the Ball 1v1</h4><p>In a small grid, one player must keep the ball for as long as possible using shielding while a defender tries to win it (feet only); swap roles regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs share a small grid (about 6m x 6m), one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Body side-on to the defender, ball on the far foot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time before rotating.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One player starts with the ball and must keep it away from their partner (the defender) for as long as possible using shielding — body side-on, ball kept on the foot furthest from the defender.</li><li>The defender tries to win the ball using feet only (no sliding, no pushing).</li><li>Time each turn (e.g. 20–30 seconds), then swap roles so both players experience shielding and defending.</li><li>Coach body position throughout: 'side-on to the defender, ball on your far foot.'</li><li>Run several rounds so both students get multiple turns in each role.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid and give more time before rotating; for 'harder', use a smaller grid.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shield &amp; Release</h4><p>As above, but the shielding player must hold the ball for a set count then release a pass to a supporting teammate under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (shielding player, defender, supporting teammate) in a small grid (about 8m x 8m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know where your support is before you're pressured.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer hold time, supporter closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter hold time, supporter further/moving.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The shielding player receives the ball and must hold it under pressure from the defender for a set count (e.g. 5 seconds) using the shielding technique from the previous game.</li><li>The supporting teammate positions themselves somewhere useful to receive a pass once the count is up.</li><li>After the count, the shielding player releases a pass to the supporting teammate while still under pressure from the defender.</li><li>Rotate roles after each rep so every student experiences shielding, defending, and supporting.</li><li>Coach awareness: 'know where your support is before you're pressured, not after.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a longer hold time and keep the supporter close; for 'harder', use a shorter hold time with the supporter further away or moving.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Protect the Ball Bonus</h3><p>4v4; bonus recognition any time a player successfully shields the ball under pressure for 3+ seconds before releasing it to a teammate.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams. No goals needed unless preferred — can be played as possession or with small goals.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game.</li><li>Explain the bonus rule: any time a player successfully shields the ball under real pressure for 3 or more seconds before releasing it to a teammate, that's worth bonus recognition.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out shielding moments loudly when they happen, connecting them back to today's Skill Games.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after the ball goes out of play.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you protect the ball from a defender today?</li><li>How did you decide which way to turn?</li><li>When is it better to shield and hold vs. release the ball quickly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Body positioned between the ball and the defender</li><li>Turns away from pressure rather than into it</li><li>Aware of supporting teammates before releasing the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find shielding physically challenging, focus success criteria on positioning/awareness rather than 'winning' every contest.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Defending, Attacking & Decision-Making (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Patient Defender":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — jockeying &amp; body positioning</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Serving your team well through a role that isn't about scoring — good defending is a gift to the whole team.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No sliding tackles; feet-only, upright challenges throughout</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to defend patiently, staying goal-side and delaying an attacker rather than diving in.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stay on-balance and side-on while defending</li><li>I can delay an attacker without immediately trying to tackle</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60–90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay on the balls of your feet in a low, balanced stance.</td><td>Allows a quick reaction to a sudden change of direction from the attacker.</td></tr><tr><td>Show the attacker onto your stronger side or toward support.</td><td>Influencing their options, rather than reacting to all of them, makes defending easier.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay patient — jockey and delay rather than diving in.</td><td>A rushed tackle attempt is easy to beat; delaying buys time for teammates to help.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Jockey &amp; Freeze</h4><p>Defender jockeys an attacker dribbling in a channel; on 'freeze,' both stop — defender is scored on balance/positioning (side-on, low, goal-side), not on winning the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a channel about 10m long and 3–4m wide marked with cones. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small controlled steps, don't lunge.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower attacker.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker changes direction often.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'jockeying' first: defender stays side-on, low body position, small controlled steps, staying goal-side of the attacker without diving in.</li><li>The attacker dribbles down the channel while the defender jockeys alongside, staying between the attacker and the end of the channel.</li><li>Call 'freeze' at random moments — both players stop immediately.</li><li>On freeze, check the defender's position: are they side-on, balanced, low, and goal-side? Give quick feedback rather than judging whether they won the ball.</li><li>Continue play after each freeze check, repeating several times down the channel.</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker moves slower; for 'harder', the attacker changes direction often to test the defender's footwork.</li></ul><hr><h4>Delay the Attacker</h4><p>1v1 in a channel; defender's only job is to delay the attacker from reaching the end line for as long as possible (timed), not to win the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a channel (start around 12m long, 3–4m wide, marked with cones), one ball per pair, with a clear end line marked.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay goal-side, patient footwork.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer channel (more time/space to delay).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter channel.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (defender vs. own best delay time)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacker starts with the ball at one end of the channel and tries to dribble to the far end line.</li><li>The defender's only job is to delay the attacker — staying goal-side and using patient footwork to slow them down, not to actually win the ball.</li><li>Time how long it takes the attacker to reach the end line (informally counted or with a stopwatch).</li><li>After each attempt, reset both players to the start and swap roles so both experience attacking and defending.</li><li>Coach patience: 'stay goal-side, don't lunge in — every second you delay is a win.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a longer channel giving more time/space to delay; for 'harder', use a shorter channel.</li></ul><hr><h4>2 Defenders, Patient Press</h4><p>Small group game (2 defenders vs. 2 attackers in a limited zone) — defenders must stay connected and patient, only committing to a tackle when support arrives.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 (2 attackers, 2 defenders) in a limited zone (about 10m x 10m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with your defending partner.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zone, time limit for attackers to escape.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The two attackers start with the ball and try to keep possession or advance through the zone.</li><li>The two defenders must stay connected (not too far apart) and work together, only committing to a tackle when the second defender is close enough to support.</li><li>Defenders communicate verbally to coordinate who presses and who covers.</li><li>Play continuously for a set time (e.g. 60–90 seconds), then swap which pair is attacking and which is defending.</li><li>Coach communication specifically: 'talk to your defending partner — who's pressing, who's covering?'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger zone; for 'harder', use a smaller zone with a time limit for attackers to escape.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Defending Focus</h3><p>4v4; coach highlights good jockeying/delaying moments as much as tackles won, reinforcing that patient defending is valuable even without winning the ball outright.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game.</li><li>Explain to students that today's coaching feedback highlights good jockeying and delaying moments just as much as tackles won — patient defending counts even without winning the ball outright.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out good defensive positioning loudly when it happens, not just successful tackles.</li><li>No sliding tackles — feet-only, upright challenges throughout.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after the ball goes out of play.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does patient defending look like, compared to just chasing the ball?</li><li>How did staying on balance help you defend better?</li><li>Why is delaying an attacker useful for your team, even if you don't win the ball?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stays on balance (not overcommitting/lunging)</li><li>Maintains a goal-side position</li><li>Communicates with a defending partner when applicable</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students less confident defending, use the 'delay only' version (no live tackling) as the main experience, adding live 1v1 challenge later once technique is secure.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Perfect Timing":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defending — timing a tackle</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Making a positive impact through fair play — a well-timed, fair tackle is good for the game; a reckless one isn't.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Feet-only tackles, no sliding; teacher demonstrates and checks technique before live 1v1 play</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to time a tackle so we win the ball cleanly and fairly.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can wait for the right moment (when the ball is slightly away from the attacker's feet) to tackle</li><li>I can make a fair, feet-only challenge</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60–90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Wait for the ball to be slightly away from the attacker's close control.</td><td>Tackling when the ball is tucked in close usually just fouls the player.</td></tr><tr><td>Attack the ball, not the player.</td><td>Keeps the tackle fair, safe, and focused on winning possession.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay balanced through the tackle.</td><td>Over-committing means if you miss, you're beaten completely.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Poke Tackle Practice</h4><p>Attacker dribbles slowly in a straight line; defender practises timing a simple poke tackle when the ball is slightly loose from the attacker's feet, at controlled pace.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a straight-line channel (about 10m), one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball, not the attacker's feet/body.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very slow, predictable dribbling.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker adds occasional change of pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the poke tackle technique first: watch the ball (not the attacker's body), and time a simple poke with the foot when the ball is slightly loose from the attacker's feet.</li><li>The attacker dribbles slowly and predictably in a straight line down the channel.</li><li>The defender jogs alongside at a controlled pace, watching for the right moment to poke the ball away.</li><li>After each attempt, reset to the start and go again, giving the defender several tries.</li><li>Swap roles so both students practise the poke tackle.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the dribbling very slow and predictable; for 'harder', the attacker adds occasional changes of pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Win It Clean 1v1</h4><p>Live 1v1 in a small grid; defender scores a point only for a clean, feet-only win of the ball (not just any contact).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a small grid (about 6m x 6m), one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Patience — wait for the right moment rather than diving in early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker limited to straight-line dribbling.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker free to use full range of moves from earlier lessons.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks (clean wins only)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Live 1v1: the attacker dribbles freely within the grid, the defender tries to win the ball.</li><li>A point is only awarded to the defender for a clean, feet-only win of the ball — not for any contact or a scrappy 50/50.</li><li>Play continuously for 60–90 seconds, then swap attacker and defender.</li><li>Coach patience for the defender: 'wait for the right moment rather than diving in early.'</li><li>Keep a simple tally of clean wins if useful for engagement.</li><li>For 'easier', the attacker is limited to straight-line dribbling; for 'harder', the attacker can use the full range of moves practised in earlier lessons.</li></ul><hr><h4>Tackle &amp; Transition</h4><p>As above, but the moment the defender wins the ball cleanly, they become the attacker and must dribble to a target — rewarding a good tackle with an immediate attacking opportunity.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a small grid (about 6m x 6m) with a target cone placed a short distance outside the grid. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be ready to transition mentally the instant you win the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Target closer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Target further, second defender added after transition.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play the same live 1v1 as Win It Clean, but add a new rule: the moment the defender wins the ball cleanly, they immediately become the attacker.</li><li>The new attacker (former defender) dribbles the ball out of the grid to the target cone as quickly as possible.</li><li>The original attacker (now defender) can try to win it back once the transition happens.</li><li>Reset both players to the grid and go again after each transition sequence completes.</li><li>Coach mental readiness: 'the instant you win the ball, you're an attacker — think forward immediately.'</li><li>For 'easier', the target is closer; for 'harder', the target is further away and a second defender joins after the transition.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Fair Tackle Focus</h3><p>4v4; coach specifically praises well-timed, clean tackles, reinforcing timing and fairness together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game.</li><li>Explain that today's coaching feedback specifically praises well-timed, clean tackles — both the timing and the fairness of the challenge.</li><li>No sliding tackles — feet-only, upright challenges throughout, reinforced consistently.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out good clean tackles loudly when they happen.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after the ball goes out of play.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about the right moment to try a tackle?</li><li>How did you keep your tackling fair and controlled?</li><li>What happened when you tackled too early or too late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Watches the ball rather than lunging at the player</li><li>Attempts are feet-only and controlled</li><li>Shows patience (waits for the right moment) rather than constant attempts</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Keep early practice at walking/slow pace for all students until technique and fairness are consistent, regardless of overall confidence level.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Find the Gap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking — creating and finding space</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding the bigger picture of the game — where you fit and where the opportunities are, beyond just your own ball touches.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety — clear space awareness when multiple groups run patterns simultaneously</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to move into open space to make ourselves available as an attacking option.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise where the space is on the field</li><li>I can move into space at the right time (not too early, not too late)</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60–90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan around you before the ball arrives.</td><td>You need to know where space is before you receive, not after.</td></tr><tr><td>Move into space away from your marker, not just anywhere.</td><td>Space only helps if it's somewhere you can actually receive a pass.</td></tr><tr><td>Time your movement so you arrive in space just as the ball is about to be played.</td><td>Moving too early lets a defender follow you and close the space back down.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spot the Space</h4><p>Small groups (4) move around a grid without a ball; on a whistle, everyone must freeze in what they believe is the 'most open' spot — coach briefly confirms good choices.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4 share one grid (about 10m x 10m), no ball needed for this game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at where defenders/teammates are, not just run randomly.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players in the grid (more obvious space).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players (less obvious space).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the idea: students move around the grid freely, thinking about where the most 'open' space is relative to the other 3 players in their group.</li><li>Students jog/walk around the grid, constantly scanning where their groupmates are.</li><li>On a whistle, everyone must freeze immediately in what they believe is the most open spot in the grid.</li><li>The coach walks around briefly, confirming good choices out loud ('great spacing there — that's genuinely open') without needing to judge every single student.</li><li>Unfreeze and repeat 4–5 times so students get multiple chances to read the space.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players in the grid (more obvious space); for 'harder', add more players so space is less obvious.</li></ul><hr><h4>Third Player Movement</h4><p>Trios: two players pass while a third moves continuously to find space to receive the next pass, never standing still.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 in an open area (about 10m x 10m), one ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move as the pass is being played, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger area.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller area, add a passive defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Two players stand a few metres apart and pass the ball between themselves.</li><li>The third player continuously moves around the area, looking for space to receive the next pass — they should never stand still.</li><li>Whichever of the two passers has the ball should look to find the moving third player if a good passing lane opens up.</li><li>After the third player receives a pass, roles rotate: the player who passed to them becomes the new 'mover'.</li><li>Coach timing: 'move as the pass is being played, not after it arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger area; for 'harder', use a smaller area and add a passive defender who can block one lane.</li></ul><hr><h4>4v2 Space Rondo</h4><p>Four attackers keep possession against two defenders in a grid, scored on consecutive passes; attackers must constantly move to create passing angles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 (4 attackers, 2 defenders) in a grid (about 12m x 12m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't bunch together — spread to make the grid bigger for yourselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, 3 defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The 4 attackers keep possession of the ball while the 2 defenders try to win it inside the grid.</li><li>Count consecutive passes out loud as a team target for the attackers.</li><li>If a defender wins the ball, swap that defender in as an attacker and swap in a new defender (or simply swap roles with whichever attacker lost the ball).</li><li>Coach spacing: 'don't bunch together — spread out to make the grid feel bigger for yourselves and create passing angles.'</li><li>Play continuously for 2–3 minutes, resetting the pass count after each turnover.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid; for 'harder', use a smaller grid or add a third defender.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Space Bonus</h3><p>4v4 small goals; coach calls out and celebrates any player who clearly created/found space to receive a useful pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain that today's coaching feedback calls out and celebrates any player who clearly created or found space to receive a useful pass — not just goals or tackles.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out good movement into space loudly when it happens, connecting it back to today's Skill Games.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide where the space was today?</li><li>What helped you move into space at the right time?</li><li>What happens to a game when players don't create space for each other?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the area before/while moving</li><li>Moves with purpose into open space rather than following the ball</li><li>Recognises when NOT to move (avoiding crowding a teammate)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use verbal prompts/cues for students who find spatial awareness challenging ('look for the biggest gap'); pair with a supportive teammate in Third Player Movement.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Options Everywhere":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Support play — passing options/angles</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Actively looking out for your teammate with the ball — offering support is a form of care in the game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety for multiple simultaneous small games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to offer good passing angles to a teammate with the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can position myself so a teammate has a clear passing lane to me</li><li>I can offer more than one option at a time as a team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60–90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Position yourself at an angle to the ball carrier, not directly behind or in front.</td><td>An angled position gives the passer an actual passing lane.</td></tr><tr><td>Offer at least two options at different angles or distances.</td><td>Gives the ball carrier a genuine choice rather than one predictable pass.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your position as the ball carrier's situation changes.</td><td>Support has to move with the game, not stay static.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Angle Awareness</h4><p>In trios, the ball-carrier must have two supporting teammates positioned at different angles (not directly behind or in front of a defender's shadow); coach checks and adjusts positioning.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (ball-carrier plus two supporting teammates) in an open area (about 10m x 10m). One ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Avoid 'flat' support directly beside the ball-carrier — angle slightly ahead or behind.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk through positioning without a defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a passive defender who can block one passing lane.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain 'flat' vs 'angled' support: standing directly beside the ball-carrier is 'flat' and hard to pass to safely; standing slightly ahead or behind at an angle gives a better passing lane.</li><li>One student holds the ball as the ball-carrier; the other two position themselves at different angles around them.</li><li>Walk through positioning first without a defender, with the coach checking and adjusting each supporting player's angle.</li><li>Once positioning looks good, add gentle movement — the ball-carrier turns or shifts, and the supporters adjust their angle to stay useful.</li><li>Rotate the ball-carrier role every few minutes so everyone practises finding good angles.</li><li>For 'harder', add one passive defender who can block one of the passing lanes, forcing the other supporter to be used.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rondo with Two Targets</h4><p>Small group possession game where the team in possession must use two different supporting players (not the same one twice in a row) to keep the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups (5–6) share a grid (about 10m x 10m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Constantly recheck your angle as the ball moves.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, add a defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the team in possession must use two different supporting players to keep the ball — the same player cannot receive the ball twice in a row.</li><li>Play possession rondo-style within the grid, with the group keeping the ball among themselves.</li><li>If the same player receives twice in a row, that's a turnover — restart possession with the group.</li><li>Coach constant rechecking of angles as the ball moves — 'your best angle a second ago might not be your best angle now.'</li><li>Play continuously for 2–3 minutes.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid; for 'harder', use a smaller grid and add a defender.</li></ul><hr><h4>Support Under Pressure</h4><p>3v1 keep-away in a grid — three attackers must always offer the ball-carrier at least two passing options; defender tries to cut passing lanes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 (3 attackers, 1 defender) in a grid (about 10m x 10m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move to open a lane the moment it closes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, 2 defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure, shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The three attackers keep the ball away from the single defender inside the grid.</li><li>Explain the rule: the ball-carrier must always have at least two passing options available from their supporting teammates.</li><li>The defender's job is to move and try to cut off passing lanes, not just chase the ball directly.</li><li>Attackers must move to open a new lane the moment the defender closes one off.</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 60–90 seconds so everyone experiences defending.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with 2 defenders.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Support Bonus</h3><p>4v4; coach highlights moments where a player's off-the-ball movement clearly created a good passing option for a teammate.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game.</li><li>Explain that today's coaching feedback highlights moments where a player's off-the-ball movement clearly created a good passing option for a teammate — not just the pass itself.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out good supporting movement loudly when it happens.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after the ball goes out of play.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you offer good support to a teammate with the ball today?</li><li>What makes a passing angle 'good' or 'bad'?</li><li>How did the team work together to always have options?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Positions at an angle rather than directly flat to the ball-carrier</li><li>Continuously adjusts position as play moves</li><li>Communicates (calls) to offer as an option</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use cones to mark suggested 'good angle' zones initially for students building spatial understanding, removing the cues as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Cushion Control":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Aerial control (controlling a bouncing/lofted ball)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Persisting faithfully with a genuinely tricky skill — aerial control takes real practice and effort to serve the team well.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No heading in this program (age-appropriate); all aerial control is with feet/thigh/chest only. Space pairs out so balls don't collide between groups.</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to control a ball that arrives off the ground (bouncing or in the air).</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can cushion a bouncing ball down to my feet</li><li>I can bring a lofted ball under control within one or two touches</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shadow Runs</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'shadow' who must copy the leader's movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader jogs, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The shadow tries to mirror every change as quickly as possible.</li><li>Swap roles every 60–90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>This builds the same reading-movement instincts used in defending later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied running.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in defending.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get in line with the ball's flight path early.</td><td>Gives you time to adjust rather than stretching at the last moment.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the right surface — thigh, chest, or foot — for the ball's height.</td><td>Different heights suit different cushioning surfaces.</td></tr><tr><td>Relax and withdraw the surface slightly on contact.</td><td>Cushions the ball down instead of it bouncing away.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Bounce &amp; Trap</h4><p>Partners bounce the ball to each other (thrown, not kicked) and practise trapping it dead with the sole or cushioning it with the top of the foot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4–5m apart, one ball per pair, open space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Relax the foot/leg on contact, don't stab at the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Gentle, predictable bounces.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied bounce height/angle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner throws (does not kick) the ball so it bounces once in front of the other partner.</li><li>The receiver practises trapping it dead with the sole of the foot, or cushioning it with the top of the foot so it drops under control.</li><li>Once controlled, the receiver picks up the ball and becomes the thrower for the next rep, or passes it back along the ground depending on the space.</li><li>Coach relaxing the foot/leg on contact — 'don't stab at the ball, let it come to you and soften on landing.'</li><li>Repeat for several reps each, swapping roles regularly.</li><li>For 'easier', throw gentle, predictable bounces close by; for 'harder', vary the bounce height and angle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Thigh &amp; Chest Cushion</h4><p>Partners softly throw the ball for a thigh or chest cushion, letting it drop to feet under control, scoring a point for each clean control.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 3–4m apart, one soft-ish ball per pair if available (or normal ball thrown gently), open space.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the body part backward slightly on contact to absorb pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very soft, close throws.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer throws with more pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner softly throws the ball toward the other's thigh or chest height.</li><li>The receiver cushions the ball with the thigh or chest, letting it drop down under control to their feet.</li><li>A point is scored for each clean control where the ball drops within a comfortable playing distance of the feet.</li><li>Coach the absorbing motion: 'move the body part backward slightly on contact to take the pace off the ball.'</li><li>Swap thrower/receiver roles regularly so both students get practice.</li><li>For 'easier', use very soft, close throws; for 'harder', use longer throws with more pace.</li></ul><hr><h4>Aerial Control Relay</h4><p>Small teams relay-race through a sequence: receive a thrown ball out of the air, control it, dribble to a target, and return — fastest clean-control team wins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams (3–4) set up in a relay line with a thrower at one end and a target cone to dribble to and from further down. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Control first, speed second — a fumbled control costs more time than a careful one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simplify the sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a second aerial control step.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the sequence: receive a thrown ball out of the air, control it with foot/thigh/chest, dribble to the target cone, then dribble back and hand off to the next teammate.</li><li>The first student in each team receives a throw from a designated thrower (coach or rotating teammate), controls it, and completes the dribble sequence.</li><li>Once back, that student hands the ball to the next teammate in line, who repeats the sequence.</li><li>The fastest team to get all members through the sequence with clean control wins — but remind students a fumbled control costs more time than a careful one.</li><li>Rotate the thrower role so every student gets a turn both receiving and throwing.</li><li>For 'easier', simplify the sequence (just control, no dribble); for 'harder', add a second aerial control step before the dribble.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Feed From the Air</h3><p>4v4; every few minutes, the coach feeds a ball into play with a throw (bouncing or lofted) instead of a normal restart, requiring a controlled first touch before play continues.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams. Coach or a designated feeder positioned to throw balls into play.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game.</li><li>Every few minutes, instead of a normal restart, the coach feeds the ball into play with a throw — either a bounce pass or a lofted throw — to a player near where play stopped.</li><li>That player must control the throw with a good first touch (foot, thigh, or chest) before play continues normally.</li><li>Continue the game as normal between these feed moments.</li><li>Coach specifically on aerial control quality during these feed moments, connecting back to today's Skill Games.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks, using several coach-feed restarts per round.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you control a bouncing or lofted ball today?</li><li>Which body part did you find easiest to cushion the ball with — why?</li><li>What was frustrating, and how did you keep working at it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Relaxes the receiving surface rather than meeting the ball rigidly</li><li>Ball drops within playing distance after control</li><li>Attempts multiple body parts (foot/thigh/chest) appropriately</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to let the ball bounce once more than intended if needed while building confidence; celebrate any controlled outcome, not just first-touch perfection.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Last Line":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Goalkeeping basics (handling, positioning)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — The goalkeeper has a unique impact on the whole team — understanding how one role affects everyone else's game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, small goals, cones, (optional) soft/foam balls for early handling work</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Use softer balls for close-range keeper practice initially; no diving on hard ground — encourage safe, controlled movements</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the basics of goalkeeping — handling the ball safely and positioning well in goal.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can catch or block a shot using safe hand/body technique</li><li>I can position myself in the right spot in goal relative to the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Form a 'W' shape with your hands behind the ball.</td><td>Gives a wide, secure base to catch cleanly rather than fumbling.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your body behind the ball as a second barrier.</td><td>If the ball squirts through your hands, your body can still stop it.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet to get in line with the shot, not just reach with your hands.</td><td>Full-body positioning behind the ball is more reliable than arms alone.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Catch &amp; Roll</h4><p>Partners roll/gently throw the ball at a 'keeper' standing in a small goal, who practises catching with a 'W' hand shape and rolling the ball back out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs at a small goal, one student as keeper standing in the goal, the other as thrower a few metres away. Use a softer/foam ball if available for early sessions.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Hands behind the ball, 'give' slightly on catching.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, central deliveries.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied angles/pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the 'W' hand shape for catching: thumbs and index fingers close together behind the ball, forming a W shape to cradle it securely.</li><li>The thrower rolls or gently throws the ball toward the keeper.</li><li>The keeper catches with the W hand shape, letting the hands 'give' slightly on contact to absorb the pace.</li><li>After catching, the keeper rolls the ball back out to the thrower along the ground using an underarm bowling-style roll.</li><li>Swap roles every 5–6 attempts so both students practise being keeper.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, central deliveries; for 'harder', vary the angle and pace of deliveries.</li></ul><hr><h4>Angle Steps</h4><p>Keeper practises small side-steps to stay central to the ball as a partner moves the ball along a line outside the box, without a shot yet — purely positioning.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs at a small goal marked with cones, one student as keeper, the other moving along a line outside the goal box with the ball at their feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Imaginary line from the ball to the middle of the goal — stay on it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster/more unpredictable movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the concept: an imaginary line runs from the ball to the middle of the goal, and the keeper's job is to stay on that line using small side-steps.</li><li>The outside player moves the ball slowly along a line in front of the goal (no shot yet).</li><li>The keeper takes small, controlled side-steps to stay central relative to the ball's position, always on that imaginary line.</li><li>Continue for 30–60 seconds, then swap roles so both students practise the keeper positioning.</li><li>Coach positioning purely — this is not about diving or saving, just staying correctly positioned.</li><li>For 'easier', move the ball slowly; for 'harder', move faster and less predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shot Stopper Rotation</h4><p>Small group takes turns in goal facing gentle shots from close range, focusing on technique (hands, body behind the ball) over athleticism; everyone rotates through the keeper role.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small group (4–5) with one small goal, a shooting spot marked a short distance away, and a ball supply. One student in goal at a time.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get the whole body behind the ball where possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer, slower shots.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further, varied placement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student stands in goal, the rest take turns as shooters from close range.</li><li>Shooters take gentle, controlled shots — the focus is on the keeper's technique (hands ready, body positioned behind the ball), not on beating the keeper with power.</li><li>After each shot, the ball is collected and the next shooter takes their turn.</li><li>Rotate the keeper role every 4–5 shots so every student experiences being in goal.</li><li>Coach getting the whole body behind the ball where possible, not just reaching with hands.</li><li>For 'easier', shots are closer and slower; for 'harder', shots come from further away with varied placement.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Everyone Keeps</h3><p>4v4 with rotating goalkeepers (swap every few minutes) so every student experiences the position; coach reinforces that the goalkeeper's decisions affect the whole team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams. A simple rotation order for goalkeepers agreed before starting.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals and an actual goalkeeper for each team.</li><li>Swap the goalkeeper on each team every few minutes on a set rotation, so every student experiences the position during the game.</li><li>Coach reinforces that the goalkeeper's decisions (positioning, when to come out, rolling the ball back into play) affect the whole team, not just the individual.</li><li>Restart with a goalkeeper roll-out or simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Keep the rotation visible and fair (e.g. a set order written on a whiteboard or called out by the coach).</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks, making sure the rotation completes for everyone.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you notice about positioning in goal today?</li><li>How did catching/handling technique change what happened next in the game?</li><li>What's hard about being a goalkeeper that you didn't expect?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses safe hand technique (not just blocking with arms/body only)</li><li>Maintains central/appropriate positioning relative to the ball</li><li>Communicates with defenders in front of them</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Goalkeeping is rotated so no single student is 'stuck' in goal; use softer balls and closer distances for any student anxious about the position.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Keep Away Kings":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided possession play (keep-ball under pressure)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding the team's overall plan and your part in keeping/winning possession, not just your individual touches.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard small-sided game safety; ensure grids are well spaced from other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep the ball as a team under real pressure, using all our passing/control skills together.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can help my team keep possession by offering support and passing accurately</li><li>I can apply pressure as a team to try to win the ball back</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the ball moving — avoid unnecessary touches.</td><td>An extra touch under pressure gives defenders time to close you down.</td></tr><tr><td>Spread out to keep passing lanes open.</td><td>Bunching up makes it easy for one defender to press multiple players at once.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate before receiving the ball.</td><td>Helps the receiver decide their first touch before the ball even arrives.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>5v2 Rondo</h4><p>Five attackers keep the ball from two defenders in a grid, counting consecutive passes; defenders swap in when they win the ball or after a time limit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 7 (5 attackers, 2 defenders) in a grid (about 12m x 12m). One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use the skills from Lessons 13–14 — space and angles — together.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, shrinking space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The 5 attackers keep the ball away from the 2 defenders within the grid, using support and good angles from Lessons 13–14.</li><li>Count consecutive passes out loud as a team target.</li><li>If a defender wins the ball, or after an agreed time limit (e.g. 60 seconds), swap two attackers in as the new defenders.</li><li>Coach specifically on combining space-finding (Lesson 13) and good angles (Lesson 14) together in the same game.</li><li>Play continuously, resetting the pass count after each turnover or defender swap.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid; for 'harder', use a smaller grid.</li></ul><hr><h4>Possession Points Ladder</h4><p>Small teams (4v4) play possession-only (no goals) with a running points ladder for consecutive passes, resetting to zero when the other team wins the ball — teams try to beat their previous best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with no goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay patient, don't force risky passes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger pitch.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller pitch, time pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (own record), scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play possession-only — no goals, the aim is purely to keep the ball as a team.</li><li>Keep a running points ladder: count consecutive passes for whichever team has the ball, resetting to zero the moment the other team wins it.</li><li>Each team tries to beat their own previous best streak during the game, not just compete directly on a single score.</li><li>Coach patience: 'don't force a risky pass just to keep the streak — a safe reset is better than losing the ball cheaply.'</li><li>Restart immediately with a pass-in after any turnover so play keeps flowing.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger pitch; for 'harder', use a smaller pitch with time pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Press &amp; Win</h4><p>Same format, but this time the coach also scores/celebrates the defending team for quick, coordinated pressure that wins the ball back within 3 seconds of losing it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups of 8 (4v4), same small pitch, no goals, bibs for two teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The whole team presses together, not just the nearest player.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer 'press window.'</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter window.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play the same possession format as Possession Points Ladder.</li><li>Add a new coaching focus: the team that just lost the ball can earn recognition (extra celebration/points) if they win it back within 3 seconds through quick, coordinated pressure.</li><li>Coach the whole-team press: 'this isn't just the nearest player chasing — everyone needs to react together.'</li><li>Call out and celebrate successful quick-win-backs loudly when they happen.</li><li>Keep play flowing with quick restarts after turnovers.</li><li>For 'easier', allow a longer press window (e.g. 5 seconds); for 'harder', shorten the window (e.g. 2 seconds).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 4v4 Possession Soccer with Small Goals</h3><p>Combines everything — teams look to keep possession using support/angles, while also looking for moments to attack and score in small goals.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain that this game combines everything from today: teams should look to keep possession using good support and angles, while also looking for moments to attack and score.</li><li>Coach circulates giving balanced feedback — praising both good possession play and good attacking moments/goals.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team keep the ball under pressure today?</li><li>How did you work together, not just individually, to keep possession?</li><li>What did good team pressing look like when you were defending?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes to team shape/spacing, not just chasing the ball</li><li>Communicates with teammates</li><li>Shows patience in possession rather than forcing risky actions</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Adjust grid/pitch size so all ability levels can experience success keeping possession; rotate roles so every student experiences both possession and pressing.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Switch It On":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Transition — attack to defence and back</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Serving the team in the moments that matter most — reacting instantly for the team's benefit, not just when it's your turn on the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard small-sided game safety</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to react quickly when possession changes — switching mindset from attacking to defending (and back) immediately.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can react quickly the moment my team loses/wins the ball</li><li>I can help my team reorganise quickly after a transition</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>React immediately the moment possession changes.</td><td>The first few seconds after losing or winning the ball are the most valuable attacking or defending moments.</td></tr><tr><td>On losing the ball, the nearest player closes down immediately.</td><td>Prevents the opposition's easiest, fastest counter-attack.</td></tr><tr><td>On winning the ball, look forward immediately for a quick option.</td><td>The opposition is often disorganised straight after losing the ball — that's the moment to exploit.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Freeze &amp; React</h4><p>Small-sided possession game; on a whistle the moment possession changes, both teams must react to a call ('attack!'/'defend!') and adjust positioning immediately.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The first 2 seconds after losing/winning the ball matter most.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower/telegraphed calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rapid, unpredictable calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided possession game.</li><li>The coach blows a whistle at the exact moment possession changes from one team to the other.</li><li>Immediately after the whistle, the coach calls 'attack!' or 'defend!' and both teams must react and adjust their positioning right away based on which team now has the ball.</li><li>Continue play immediately after the call — there's no long stoppage, just a quick reaction moment.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'the first 2 seconds after losing or winning the ball matter most — react immediately, don't drift.'</li><li>For 'easier', make the calls slower and more telegraphed; for 'harder', make them rapid and unpredictable.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v3 Quick Switch</h4><p>Small games where goals can be scored at both ends immediately after a turnover — rewarding teams who transition mentally and physically the fastest.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 (3v3), small pitch with a small goal at each end (about 15m x 12m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't stand and admire a turnover — react immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger pitch.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller pitch.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a small-sided game where goals can be scored at both ends immediately after a turnover — there's no delay or reset period after winning the ball.</li><li>The moment a team wins possession, they can immediately attack either goal without waiting.</li><li>This rewards teams who transition mentally and physically the fastest after a turnover.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'don't stand and admire a turnover — react immediately and attack.'</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger pitch; for 'harder', use a smaller pitch.</li></ul><hr><h4>Counter-Attack Race</h4><p>After winning the ball in a small drill, a team has 8 seconds to attack and shoot before defenders reorganise — practising immediate, decisive transition.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups with a drill area and small goal set up (about 15m x 10m). One ball per group, a clear time-keeping method (stopwatch or counted seconds).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Quick decisions over perfect ones in transition moments.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time window.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter window.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Set up a simple drill where one team wins the ball (e.g. from a coach feed or a light challenge) and must then attack quickly.</li><li>The moment the ball is won, start an 8-second countdown out loud.</li><li>The attacking team has those 8 seconds to move the ball forward and get a shot off before defenders (who start slightly delayed) can reorganise.</li><li>If the shot happens within 8 seconds, celebrate the quick transition regardless of whether it scores; if the 8 seconds run out first, reset and try again.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'quick decisions over perfect ones in transition moments — speed matters more than the perfect pass here.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a longer time window (e.g. 12 seconds); for 'harder', shorten it (e.g. 6 seconds).</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer — Transition Focus</h3><p>4v4 small goals; coach specifically highlights and celebrates fast, well-organised reactions to turnovers in both directions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Explain that today's coaching feedback specifically highlights and celebrates fast, well-organised reactions to turnovers in both directions — winning the ball and losing it.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out good transition moments loudly when they happen.</li><li>Restart with a simple pass-in after goals or the ball going out of play.</li><li>Keep the game flowing with minimal stoppages so transition moments happen naturally and often.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you react quickly when the ball changed hands today?</li><li>Why does the moment right after a turnover matter so much in a game?</li><li>What was hard about switching your mindset quickly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reacts promptly to a change in possession</li><li>Adjusts position/shape rather than standing still</li><li>Communicates with teammates during the transition moment</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clear visual/verbal cues (coloured cones held up, loud calls) to support students who need extra processing time for quick transitions.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Set Piece Specialists":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Set plays — throw-ins &amp; corners</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Working together warmly as a team to plan something in advance — set plays are a team effort built on trust in each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones, bibs, small goals</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; check throw-in technique for back/shoulder safety (no excessive arching)</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct technique and simple team strategy for throw-ins and corners.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can perform a legal throw-in (both feet on the ground, ball behind and over the head)</li><li>I can execute a simple planned corner routine with my team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Point both feet behind the line, ball behind and over the head for a throw-in.</td><td>This is the legal throw-in technique — both feet on the ground, both hands, one motion.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim the throw to a moving or unmarked teammate, not just the nearest player.</td><td>A throw to a marked player is easily won back by the defence.</td></tr><tr><td>Time attacking runs to arrive as the ball is delivered from a corner.</td><td>Arriving too early lets defenders adjust; arriving late misses the ball.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Legal Throw-In Practice</h4><p>Pairs practise throw-in technique (both feet down, ball behind and over the head, released in front) aiming at a moving target partner.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, one ball per pair, open space with room for a moving target.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feet stay grounded through the throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stationary target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Moving target, increasing distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the legal throw-in technique: both feet stay in contact with the ground (behind the sideline), hold the ball with both hands behind the head, and release it evenly from over and behind the head, in front of the body.</li><li>One partner takes the throw-in, aiming at the other partner who acts as the target.</li><li>Start with the target standing still to focus purely on technique.</li><li>Check feet stay grounded throughout the throw — no jumping or lifting the back foot.</li><li>Swap roles after every 4–5 throws so both partners practise throwing and receiving.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the target stationary; for 'harder', have the target move and increase the throwing distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Corner Routine Design</h4><p>Small groups design and practise one simple, repeatable corner routine (e.g. short pass then cross, or direct to a specific zone) and rehearse it several times.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3–4 near a marked 'corner' spot and a small goal, with cones available to mark specific zones or player positions. No time pressure — this is a planning and rehearsal activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone needs to know their specific job in the routine.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler routine (2 players).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Routine involves 3–4 players with movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the task: each group designs one simple, repeatable routine for a corner-style restart — for example, a short pass followed by a cross, or a direct ball into a specific zone.</li><li>Give groups a couple of minutes to plan and agree who does what in the routine.</li><li>Each group rehearses their routine several times without opposition, refining timing and positioning.</li><li>Check in with each group to make sure every player knows their specific job in the routine — not just a vague idea.</li><li>Have groups show their routine to another group or to the class briefly if time allows.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the routine to just 2 players; for 'harder', involve 3–4 players with movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Set Piece vs Defence</h4><p>Groups run their throw-in/corner routines against a small defending team trying to intercept, adjusting the routine if it's not working.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same small groups as Corner Routine Design, now facing a small defending team near their goal (about 4 defenders per group, borrowed from another group or a subset of the class). Small goal in place.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be ready to adapt the plan, not repeat a routine that isn't working.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders passive at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full active defence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Groups run the throw-in or corner routine they designed and rehearsed, now against a live but limited defending team.</li><li>Defenders try to intercept or block the routine using normal fair-play rules (feet only, no sliding).</li><li>After each attempt, briefly discuss as a group: did the routine work? If not, what needs to change?</li><li>Adjust the routine on the spot if it isn't working rather than repeating something ineffective.</li><li>Rotate which group is attacking and which is defending every few attempts.</li><li>For 'easier', defenders start passive at first; for 'harder', defenders are fully active from the start.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Soccer with Set Piece Restarts</h3><p>4v4 small goals; every throw-in and any ball out for a corner-equivalent uses the practised technique/routines from today, applied live.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 (4v4), small pitch with small goals (about 20m x 15m), bibs for two teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a normal small-sided game with small goals.</li><li>Whenever the ball goes out for a throw-in, players use the legal technique practised today.</li><li>Whenever the ball goes out near the goal in a corner-equivalent situation, the team can use one of their practised routines from today's Skill Games if they choose.</li><li>Coach reinforces good technique on throw-ins and encourages teams to actually use their planned routines rather than reverting to random restarts.</li><li>Keep the game flowing, using these moments as natural teaching points rather than long stoppages.</li><li>Play 2–3 short rounds with brief breaks.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your throw-in or corner routine effective today?</li><li>How did your team work together to plan and execute a set play?</li><li>What did you adjust when your first plan didn't work?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Throw-in technique is legal and controlled</li><li>Understands their specific role in a team set-play routine</li><li>Communicates and adjusts the plan when needed</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Let every student have a defined, valued role in the group's set-play routine (not just the most confident players); rotate roles across attempts.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Soccer Showdown":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament — applying the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Reflecting on the impact this whole unit has had — on your skills, your team, and how you treat others in a game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, small goals, cones, bibs (multiple colours for round-robin teams)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard game safety; manage fatigue/hydration across a tournament format, ensure fair rotation so no team is overplayed</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything from this unit — control, passing, shooting, defending, and teamwork — in a full small-sided tournament.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can play fairly and encouragingly in a competitive setting</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring your best technique from across the unit into every touch.</td><td>A tournament is where all of the unit's individual skills get tested together under real pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Make decisions based on what the game is showing you, not a fixed plan.</td><td>Reading and reacting to the real situation beats sticking rigidly to a plan that doesn't fit.</td></tr><tr><td>Play fairly and encourage teammates throughout, win or lose.</td><td>A tournament is a great chance to practise sportsmanship under real competitive stakes.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Unit Skills Warm-Up Carousel</h4><p>Quick-fire stations revisiting a taste of the unit's key skills (close control, passing accuracy, shooting technique) — a light refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3–4 quick stations around the space, each revisiting one key skill from the unit (e.g. close control dribbling through cones, passing accuracy at a gate, shooting technique at a target). Small groups start at different stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain this is a light refresher, not new teaching — a chance to feel how much more comfortable these skills are now compared to Lesson 1.</li><li>Split the class into small groups, one group per station.</li><li>Each group spends 1–2 minutes at their station doing a simple version of that skill.</li><li>On your signal, groups rotate to the next station.</li><li>Continue until every group has visited every station.</li><li>Coach comments on visible improvement throughout — 'notice how much smoother that feels now compared to Lesson 1?'</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Skill Relay</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining dribbling, passing, and shooting in sequence — a fun, competitive combination of the whole unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up one relay circuit per team combining a short dribble section, a passing target (wall or partner), and a shot at a small goal, laid out over about 20m. One ball per team, teams of 4–5.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth handovers between skills.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the full sequence each team member must complete: dribble through the cones, pass to the target, then shoot at the small goal.</li><li>The first student in each team completes the sequence, then tags the next teammate to go.</li><li>Continue until every team member has completed the full sequence.</li><li>The first team to have everyone finish the sequence (with reasonable control, not just speed) is the winner.</li><li>Coach smooth handovers between the dribbling, passing, and shooting sections — a rushed transition often costs more time than it saves.</li><li>Run 1–2 rounds if time allows, mixing up team order.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Challenge</h4><p>Each team nominates a 'captain' who sets one focus for their team's tournament games (e.g. 'let's focus on passing accuracy today') — light strategic ownership before the tournament begins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same tournament teams as will be used in the Round-Robin Mini-Tournament, gathered briefly in team huddles before games begin.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give every captain a genuine, valued choice.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team nominates one student as their 'captain' for the day.</li><li>Give teams 2–3 minutes to huddle and have their captain lead a short discussion, choosing one genuine focus for their tournament games (e.g. 'let's focus on passing accuracy today' or 'let's focus on good communication').</li><li>Ask each captain to briefly share their team's chosen focus with the coach or the class.</li><li>Make sure every captain's choice is treated as genuine and valued, not corrected or overridden.</li><li>Remind teams to keep their focus in mind during the tournament games that follow.</li><li>Rotate the captain role in future units so different students get the experience over time.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Multiple small teams (3v3/4v4) rotate through short games against each other on 2–3 small pitches, scoring simply (goals or a fair-play points system). Every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Divide the class into multiple small teams of 3–4 (3v3 or 4v4), with 2–3 small pitches set up with small goals. A simple round-robin schedule prepared in advance so every team knows who they play and when.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the tournament format: teams rotate through short games against different opponents on the available pitches, following the round-robin schedule.</li><li>Score simply — either goals scored, or a fair-play points system (e.g. win = 3 points, draw = 1, plus bonus points for good sportsmanship) depending on what the class values most today.</li><li>Play each round for a fixed short time (e.g. 4–5 minutes), then rotate to the next scheduled match.</li><li>Remind students that every skill from the unit — dribbling, passing, shooting, defending, movement, set pieces — is relevant somewhere in these games.</li><li>Manage fatigue and hydration across the tournament with brief breaks between rounds, and make sure rotation is fair so no team is overplayed.</li><li>Finish with a simple wrap-up acknowledging effort, improvement, and each captain's focus from Skill Game 3, not just final results.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging play look like in today's tournament?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other sports or games?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills from across the unit (not just one)</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure round-robin teams to be balanced/mixed-ability; consider a simple fair-play recognition (not just a 'winning team') to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Strategy & Decision Making":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Reading the Game & Making a Choice (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Eyes Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Scanning before acting (looking up to check options before receiving or deciding)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (2 colours), 1 ball per group of 4, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing so scanning students don't collide while looking around; keep early activities at walking pace</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to look up and scan our surroundings before we get the ball, so we already know our options.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can look around to check my options before the ball arrives</li><li>I can name what I saw when asked (space, teammates, defenders)</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Scan &amp; Spot Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely and change direction without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'spot!', students point to something specific they can see — a cone colour, a classmate's shirt, the far fence — before continuing to jog.</li><li>This connects to today's scanning focus by getting eyes moving and checking the surroundings before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Repeat the 'spot!' call every 20-30 seconds, varying what students are asked to notice.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace light and continuous throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask a couple of students what they noticed when they scanned today, setting up the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan the space around you before the ball arrives, not after.</td><td>Deciding starts with seeing — a player who scans early already knows their options when the ball turns up.</td></tr><tr><td>Check for open teammates, space, and defenders, roughly in that order.</td><td>A simple scanning order stops the situation feeling overwhelming and speeds up decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep scanning even when you don't have the ball.</td><td>A good decision when the ball arrives depends on a picture already built beforehand.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Head Up Circle</h4><p>Standing in a circle passing a ball, students must lift their head and look at two other players before receiving, with the teacher checking eyes stay up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or groups of 8-10 stand in a circle roughly 4-5m across, one soft ball per circle, plenty of space between neighbouring circles.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look up before it arrives, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower passes, bigger circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two balls going at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random freeze — 'what did you see?'</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the standard: before catching, the receiver must lift their head and look at two other players in the circle, not just the ball coming toward them.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and passes underarm to anyone in the circle.</li><li>The receiver must show two clear head-turns to check other players before the ball arrives, then catches and passes on.</li><li>If a student catches without scanning first, the group pauses, the teacher points it out, and play resumes straight away — no big stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look up before it arrives, not after.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger circle with slower passes; for 'harder', run two balls in the same circle so scanning has to happen faster and more often.</li></ul><hr><h4>Scan &amp; Point</h4><p>In a moving grid, ball carriers dribble or jog while teammates without the ball silently point at open space they've spotted; the carrier passes toward a pointed space.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 in a marked grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, cones marking the grid corners.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust what you scanned, not what you guess.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walking pace only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a passive defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every minute</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the two roles: the ball carrier dribbles or jogs with the ball, while teammates without the ball silently point at open space they've spotted.</li><li>Play begins with one student carrying the ball and the other three jogging into different areas of the grid.</li><li>The carrier scans the pointing teammates and passes toward whichever pointed space looks most open, then that receiver becomes the new carrier.</li><li>Keep play continuous — there's no formal score, so if a pass goes astray, the nearest student collects the ball and carries on.</li><li>Rotate roles every minute or so on a whistle so everyone gets time as carrier and as a pointer.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust what you scanned, not what you guess.' For 'easier', keep it to walking pace only; for 'harder', add a passive defender who can't tackle but forces sharper scanning.</li></ul><hr><h4>What Did You See?</h4><p>In a small group possession game, the teacher randomly freezes play and asks a non-ball-carrier to say aloud what options they'd scanned.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 in a small marked area roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, playing simple keep-away possession.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Name the specific thing — a space, or a person.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Freeze more often, more time to answer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Freeze less often, faster recall needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: at any point the teacher may call 'freeze!' and point to a player who does not have the ball.</li><li>Groups begin a normal small-sided possession game, passing the ball around within their area.</li><li>When 'freeze!' is called, everyone stops immediately and the chosen non-ball-carrier says aloud what options they had scanned — a specific space or a specific teammate.</li><li>Play resumes straight after the answer, with the ball starting again from whoever was holding it at the freeze.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'name the specific thing — a space, or a person,' not a vague answer like 'I saw everyone.'</li><li>For 'easier', freeze more often and give more time to answer; for 'harder', freeze less often so recall has to be sharper and faster.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Scan &amp; Score</h3><p>4v2 keep-away where players earn a bonus point for passing to an option they'd clearly scanned before receiving (spotted by the teacher), on top of points for keeping possession.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 6 split into two teams of 3 attackers and 2 defenders plus one extra attacker floating (4v2), playing in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: the attacking team keeps standard points for maintaining possession, plus a bonus point whenever the teacher spots a pass going to a teammate the passer had clearly scanned before receiving.</li><li>The 4 attackers begin with the ball and try to keep it away from the 2 defenders using short passes within the grid.</li><li>Defenders press to intercept; if they win the ball, roles swap and the team that lost it becomes the defending pair.</li><li>On any stoppage (ball out, clean interception), restart quickly with the ball at the nearest attacker's feet so play doesn't drag.</li><li>Rotate the two defenders every 2-3 minutes so everyone gets a turn attacking and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: scanning early means the pass to a scanned option should look calm and deliberate, not lucky.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you actually see when you looked up today?</li><li>Did scanning early change how fast you could decide?</li><li>What happens to your decisions when you only look at the ball?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Lifts head to scan before receiving the ball</li><li>Can name what they scanned when asked</li><li>Scans repeatedly, not just once</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow more freeze-checks for students still building this habit; pair with a supportive partner who models scanning out loud.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Spot the Gap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Recognising space and open options as a decision cue</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (2 colours), 1 ball per group of 4, cones marking a grid</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear grid boundaries so scanning for gaps doesn't send students into walls, fences, or other groups' games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to spot open space and gaps so we know when there's a good option available.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can point out a gap or open teammate before acting</li><li>I can choose the option that gives my team the most space</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Scan &amp; Spot Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely and change direction without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'spot!', students point to something specific they can see — a cone colour, a classmate's shirt, the far fence — before continuing to jog.</li><li>This connects to today's scanning focus by getting eyes moving and checking the surroundings before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Repeat the 'spot!' call every 20-30 seconds, varying what students are asked to notice.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace light and continuous throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask a couple of students what they noticed when they scanned today, setting up the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Look for gaps in the defence, not just where the ball is.</td><td>The best decision often depends on space that has nothing to do with where the ball currently sits.</td></tr><tr><td>Compare more than one option before choosing.</td><td>The first option seen isn't always the best one — briefly comparing leads to better choices.</td></tr><tr><td>Act on the gap quickly once you spot it.</td><td>Gaps close fast, so noticing one is only useful if you use it straight away.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Gap Hunters</h4><p>Students jog through a grid of cones representing defenders, calling out 'gap!' as they move through the space between two cones.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-6 in a grid roughly 12m x 10m scattered with 8-10 cones representing defenders, spaced so there are clear gaps of varying width between them.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Eyes up, not down at your feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower, moving gaps (a partner shifts the cones).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Gaps randomly shift on a whistle</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: students jog through the grid and must call out 'gap!' the moment they move through the space between two cones.</li><li>Everyone starts jogging slowly around the outside of the grid, then begins weaving through the cone gaps.</li><li>Students keep moving and calling 'gap!' each time they pass through a new gap — there's no ball yet, this is purely about spotting and using space.</li><li>If two students head for the same gap at once, the first to call it has right of way and the other adjusts to a different gap.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'eyes up, not down at your feet' while weaving through.</li><li>For 'easier', use wider gaps; for 'harder', have a partner shift the cones slightly every 30 seconds so gaps keep changing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Best Option Pass</h4><p>In a 3v1 keep-away game, the passer briefly names two options aloud ('A or B?') before passing to whichever is more open.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 (3 attackers, 1 defender) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, cones marking corners.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say both options before you choose.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender walks only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus for spotting a gap the defender didn't expect</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before every pass, the ball carrier must briefly say two names aloud as options — for example 'A or B?' — then pass to whichever is more open.</li><li>The single defender starts in the middle of the grid; the three attackers keep possession using short passes.</li><li>The carrier scans, names the two options out loud, and passes to whichever teammate the defender is not closest to.</li><li>If the defender intercepts, swap them with the attacker who made the pass and continue straight away.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say both options before you choose,' so the decision process is visible, not silent guesswork.</li><li>For 'easier', have the defender walk only; for 'harder', add a second defender so there are fewer clearly open options.</li></ul><hr><h4>Race to the Gap</h4><p>Small teams compete to be first to move into a marked open gap when the teacher calls it out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 3-4 spread across a grid roughly 12m x 10m marked with several clearly labelled gaps between cone pairs, no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the instant you see it, don't wait.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Gaps stay marked and visible longer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Gaps appear and disappear quickly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Fake gap calls to test focus</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: when the teacher calls out a specific gap, the first team to have a player move fully into that gap scores a point for their team.</li><li>Teams start spread out around the edges of the grid, ready to move on the call.</li><li>The teacher calls a gap; players react immediately and the fastest team into that space wins the point for that round.</li><li>After each call, reset teams to starting positions and call a new gap to keep the pace high.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move the instant you see it, don't wait,' rewarding quick reaction over hesitation.</li><li>For 'easier', keep gaps marked and visible for longer before calling; for 'harder', have gaps appear and disappear quickly so students must track them continuously. The chaos variant occasionally calls a fake gap that doesn't exist, testing focus.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Gap Attack</h3><p>4v4 small-sided game where teams earn a bonus point for a pass or run into a clearly identified gap in the defence, on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4 in a grid roughly 18m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, small goals or end-zones marked with cones at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: standard points apply for scoring in the small-sided game, plus a bonus point whenever a pass or run clearly moves into an identified gap in the defence, as spotted by the teacher.</li><li>Kick or throw off from the centre of the grid to begin, with both teams set up in their own half.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, encouraging players to actively look for and call out gaps as they attack, just like in the Skill Games.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in from where the ball went out, keeping the game flowing.</li><li>Rotate which end each team attacks every few minutes so both sides get equal chances to practise spotting gaps in different directions.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: reward the player who calls or shows a gap out loud before using it, not just the one who happens to score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What makes a gap 'good' to use, not just open?</li><li>How did comparing two options change your decision?</li><li>What happened when you waited too long to use a gap?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Identifies gaps or open options before acting</li><li>Compares more than one option briefly</li><li>Acts on a spotted gap without hesitating</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use visual markers to highlight gaps for students who find spatial reading harder; allow verbal coaching cues ('look left') for those still building this skill.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Quick or Safe?":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Simple binary decision-making — choosing between a safe option and a riskier one</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; clear rules to prevent rushed or unsafe contact when moving quickly</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to choose between a safe option and a quicker, riskier one, based on what the game actually needs.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can explain why I chose the safe or risky option in a moment</li><li>I can make a decision quickly instead of freezing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Scan &amp; Spot Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely and change direction without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'spot!', students point to something specific they can see — a cone colour, a classmate's shirt, the far fence — before continuing to jog.</li><li>This connects to today's scanning focus by getting eyes moving and checking the surroundings before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Repeat the 'spot!' call every 20-30 seconds, varying what students are asked to notice.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace light and continuous throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask a couple of students what they noticed when they scanned today, setting up the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Notice when a situation calls for a safe option instead of a risky one.</td><td>Not every moment needs a big risk — reading the moment first is what makes a decision a good one.</td></tr><tr><td>Make your decision quickly rather than freezing between two options.</td><td>Hesitating usually loses both options — quick, decisive choices keep opportunities alive.</td></tr><tr><td>Be able to explain your choice afterwards.</td><td>Naming the reason behind a decision is what turns it into a lesson for next time, not just a guess.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Safe or Send It</h4><p>In pairs, the receiver calls 'safe' or 'go' as the ball approaches, and the passer must react instantly with a slower controlled pass or a faster driven one.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4-5m apart, one ball per pair, enough space between pairs to avoid collisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>React to the call, don't guess ahead.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Caller gives more time before the pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Caller changes their mind at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mixed calls mid-rally</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the two calls: 'safe' means a slower, controlled pass; 'go' means a faster, driven pass.</li><li>One partner starts with the ball and prepares to pass while the other, as receiver, calls 'safe' or 'go' just before the pass arrives.</li><li>The passer must react instantly to whichever call is made, without knowing in advance which one is coming.</li><li>After each pass, switch roles so both partners get turns calling and passing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'react to the call, don't guess ahead.'</li><li>For 'easier', the caller gives more time before the pass is thrown; for 'harder', the caller can change their mind at the last second, forcing a genuine reaction.</li></ul><hr><h4>Traffic Light Decisions</h4><p>In a small group game, the teacher or a peer holds up a coloured card (green = go for it, red = play safe) mid-play, and students adjust their next action instantly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 playing possession in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, a green and a red coloured card or cone held by the teacher or a rotating peer.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check the signal, then commit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Signal held up further in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Signal flashed briefly and randomly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Double signal — one true, one decoy</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the signal system before starting: green means 'go for it' (try something ambitious), red means 'play safe' (simple, controlled options only).</li><li>Groups begin a normal small-sided possession game with no signal shown yet.</li><li>Partway through play, the signal-holder raises green or red, and students must adjust their very next action to match the signal.</li><li>Play continues without stopping — students simply adapt on the move as new signals are shown.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'check the signal, then commit,' so decisions stay quick rather than hesitant.</li><li>For 'easier', hold the signal up further in advance; for 'harder', flash it briefly and randomly. The chaos variant shows two signals at once — one real, one a decoy — testing whether students check properly rather than just glancing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Why Did You Choose That?</h4><p>In short small-sided rounds, the teacher asks a player, right after a key moment, to explain in one sentence why they went safe or risky.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing short small-sided rounds (2-3 minutes each) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One clear reason is enough.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Multiple-choice reasons offered.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No prompts, own words only.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: right after any key moment (a pass, a shot, a dribble decision), the teacher may point to the player involved and ask them to explain their choice.</li><li>Groups play a normal short round of possession or a simple small-sided game.</li><li>When asked, the player gives one clear sentence explaining why they went safe or risky in that moment, then play resumes immediately.</li><li>Rotate through several rounds so different players get asked over the session.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'one clear reason is enough' — this isn't about a long explanation, just naming the thinking behind the choice.</li><li>For 'easier', offer multiple-choice reasons the player can pick from; for 'harder', require their own words with no prompts.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Risk or Rest</h3><p>Small-sided possession game where a flipped coloured cone mid-game signals whether the round rewards safe, controlled play or quick, risky play, and teams must adjust their decisions accordingly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, a single coloured cone in the middle that can be flipped between two colours.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: whichever colour the middle cone is showing decides the round's reward — one colour rewards safe, controlled possession, the other rewards quick, ambitious risk-taking.</li><li>Begin with the cone showing one colour and play a short round of small-sided possession or a simple scoring game matching that style.</li><li>Partway through, the teacher flips the cone to the other colour without pausing play, and teams must adjust their decision-making to match the new reward on the fly.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with the ball at the nearest player's feet, keeping the flipped-cone rule in force.</li><li>Rotate which team starts with possession each round so both sides get equal practice adjusting to the flip.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: notice how quickly a team can shift its whole approach when the reward changes, rather than sticking to one style regardless.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What told you a moment needed a safe choice instead of a risky one?</li><li>Did explaining your choice out loud change how you saw it?</li><li>When did hesitating cost your team an option today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Chooses between safe and risky options based on the situation</li><li>Decides quickly rather than freezing</li><li>Can explain the reasoning behind a decision</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow more thinking time in early rounds for students building decision confidence; accept a range of valid reasons rather than one 'correct' answer.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Read the Defender":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading an opponent's position and body language to inform a decision</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>1v1/2v1 activities kept to controlled speed, with clear space between groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read a defender's position and body language to decide our next move.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can notice which side a defender is favouring before I decide</li><li>I can choose a decision that uses what the defender is giving me</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Scan &amp; Spot Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely and change direction without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'spot!', students point to something specific they can see — a cone colour, a classmate's shirt, the far fence — before continuing to jog.</li><li>This connects to today's scanning focus by getting eyes moving and checking the surroundings before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Repeat the 'spot!' call every 20-30 seconds, varying what students are asked to notice.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace light and continuous throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask a couple of students what they noticed when they scanned today, setting up the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Notice which way a defender is facing or leaning before deciding.</td><td>A defender's position usually tells you which option they've already given up — reading it early makes the decision easier.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the option the defender is least protecting.</td><td>Working with what's already open is more reliable than trying to force through where they're strongest.</td></tr><tr><td>Recheck the defender's position if it changes.</td><td>Defenders move and adjust, so a decision based on an old picture can quickly become the wrong one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shadow Read</h4><p>In pairs, one student mirrors a slow-moving 'defender' while trying to read and call out which side is more open.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with roughly 3m of space each, no ball needed, enough room between pairs to move freely.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch hips and feet, not just the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender moves slowly and obviously.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender fakes direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role swap on a whistle</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: one partner plays a slow-moving 'defender,' shifting their body weight and stance from side to side, while the other reads them.</li><li>The reading partner watches the defender's hips and feet (not just which way they're facing) and calls out which side looks more open.</li><li>The defender then confirms whether the read was correct by stepping fully to that side.</li><li>Swap roles after every 4-5 reads so both partners get practice reading and defending.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch hips and feet, not just the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender moves slowly and obviously; for 'harder', the defender adds a fake change of direction to test the read. The chaos variant swaps roles on a random whistle rather than a fixed count.</li></ul><hr><h4>1v1 Gap Read</h4><p>A simple 1v1 grid game where the attacker chooses to go to the side the defender is showing them, scoring a bonus point for reading it correctly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a small grid roughly 6m x 4m with a marked line as the scoring end, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Go where they're not.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender must stay side-on the whole time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender can square up and reset.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for reading a fake</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the attacker starts with the ball facing the defender and must choose which side to attack based on which side the defender is showing them as open.</li><li>The attacker begins a controlled 1v1 dribble toward the defender.</li><li>If the attacker correctly reads and goes to the side the defender is actually leaving open, they earn a bonus point on top of getting past.</li><li>After each attempt, swap attacker and defender and reset to the starting line.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'go where they're not,' not where the defender's body is facing.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender must stay side-on the whole time, making the open side obvious; for 'harder', the defender can square up and reset their stance, making the read trickier. The chaos variant gives a bonus point for reading a genuine fake.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1 Decision</h4><p>A classic 2v1 keep-away game where the ball carrier reads the single defender's position to decide whether to pass or carry themselves.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (2 attackers, 1 defender) in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Read the defender, not just your teammate.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender must stay a set distance back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender can close down quickly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Defender allowed one 'fake' movement per round</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the classic 2v1 setup: two attackers keep the ball away from a single defender, using the extra player as an advantage.</li><li>The ball carrier starts play and must decide, based on the defender's position, whether to pass to the free teammate or carry the ball themselves.</li><li>The defender tries to cover as much space as possible, forcing a genuine decision rather than an automatic pass.</li><li>If the defender wins the ball, rotate them out and bring in a new defender from a queue, or simply reset and swap roles.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'read the defender, not just your teammate' — the decision should follow what the defender is doing, not habit.</li><li>For 'easier', the defender must stay a set distance back; for 'harder', the defender can close down quickly. The chaos variant allows the defender one genuine 'fake' movement per round to test if the attacker reads through it.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; React</h3><p>Small-sided game where teams earn a bonus point for attacking moves that visibly used a defender's position or body shape to make the decision, on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 18m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, small goals or end-zones marked at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: standard points apply for scoring, plus a bonus point whenever an attacking move visibly used a defender's position or body shape to make the decision, as spotted by the teacher.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with both teams set in their own half.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, encouraging attackers to watch defenders' hips, feet, and positioning before choosing where to go, just like in the Skill Games.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in from where the ball went out.</li><li>Rotate defensive and attacking roles or ends every few minutes so everyone practises reading defenders from different angles.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise the specific read out loud when you see it — 'you saw their hips open up and went there' — so students connect the decision to what they actually saw.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a defender's position tell you today?</li><li>How did reading the defender change your decision compared to guessing?</li><li>What happened when the defender's position changed quickly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Notices a defender's position or body shape before deciding</li><li>Chooses an option the defender is leaving open</li><li>Rechecks and adjusts if the defender's position changes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start with slow, obvious defender movement for students building this reading skill; use simple verbal cues ('which way are they facing?') to prompt the read.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Decide and Commit":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Committing to a decision without hesitation (Block 1 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 1 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; remind students that hesitating mid-action can cause more collisions than committing fully</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make a decision and commit to it fully, without hesitating or second-guessing halfway through.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can commit fully to a decision once I've made it</li><li>I can reflect on a decision afterwards without dwelling on a mistake</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Scan &amp; Spot Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely and change direction without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space at an easy pace, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'spot!', students point to something specific they can see — a cone colour, a classmate's shirt, the far fence — before continuing to jog.</li><li>This connects to today's scanning focus by getting eyes moving and checking the surroundings before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Repeat the 'spot!' call every 20-30 seconds, varying what students are asked to notice.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace light and continuous throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask a couple of students what they noticed when they scanned today, setting up the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Make your decision and commit to it fully, rather than changing your mind halfway.</td><td>A committed action, even an imperfect one, usually works out better than a hesitant, half-changed one.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect briefly on a decision after it happens, then move on.</td><td>A quick, honest reflection helps you learn without it turning into dwelling on a mistake.</td></tr><tr><td>Trust the scanning, gap-reading, and defender-reading skills you've already practised this week.</td><td>Good decisions come from trusting the picture you've already built, not from second-guessing it in the moment.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Commit Challenge</h4><p>In short small-sided rounds, the teacher calls out 'commit!' the instant a player begins an action, rewarding full, committed execution over hesitant half-actions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing short small-sided rounds (2 minutes each) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Once you've started, follow all the way through.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher calls decisions early to build confidence.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Decisions must be made and committed to instantly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus for recovering well after a committed decision doesn't quite work</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the moment a player begins any action — a pass, a dribble, a shot attempt — the teacher calls 'commit!' as a reminder to follow all the way through rather than pulling out halfway.</li><li>Groups play a normal short round of small-sided possession or a simple game.</li><li>Whenever the teacher spots a player starting to hesitate mid-action, the 'commit!' call is given as encouragement to finish the action fully.</li><li>Play continues without a full stop — the call is a verbal nudge, not a stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'once you've started, follow all the way through.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher calls decisions early to build confidence before students act; for 'harder', decisions must be made and committed to instantly with no lead-in time. The chaos variant gives a bonus for recovering well after a committed decision doesn't quite work out.</li></ul><hr><h4>Decision Carousel</h4><p>Teams rotate through short stations that each revisit a Block 1 focus — scanning, spotting gaps, safe vs risky choices, reading a defender.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Four stations set up around the space, each revisiting one Block 1 focus (scanning, spotting gaps, safe-vs-risky, reading a defender), teams of 3-4 rotating through all four, roughly 3-4 minutes per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use everything you've practised this week.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each of the four stations is testing before rotating begins, since this is a recap of the whole block.</li><li>Teams start at their assigned station and complete a short version of that block's key activity.</li><li>On the teacher's signal, teams rotate clockwise to the next station until all four have been completed.</li><li>Keep transitions between stations quick and orderly — equipment stays in place, only students move.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use everything you've practised this week,' connecting each station back to its earlier lesson.</li><li>For 'easier', allow longer time per station; for 'harder', shorten the time and speed up rotation. The chaos variant adds a head-to-head mirror challenge at one station.</li></ul><hr><h4>No Regrets Round</h4><p>In small games, after every decision — whether it worked or not — the player briefly says what they saw and why they chose it, with no criticism allowed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>State it, don't judge it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher models the first few.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick turnaround, no pause in play.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: after every decision — whether it worked or didn't — the player involved briefly says what they saw and why they chose it, with absolutely no criticism allowed from teammates.</li><li>Groups play a normal small-sided game or possession round.</li><li>After a notable decision (a pass, a shot, a dribble choice), that player quickly states their reasoning out loud before play resumes.</li><li>Teammates listen without judging the outcome — the focus is on the thinking, not whether it worked.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'state it, don't judge it,' reinforcing that naming a decision matters more than whether it succeeded.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher models the first few examples; for 'harder', keep a quick turnaround with no pause in play.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Commit &amp; Reflect Games</h3><p>Small-sided matches where teams earn a bonus point for committing fully to decisions and for giving each other quick, judgement-free feedback on choices, wrapping up the whole block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, small goals or end-zones marked at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for committing fully to decisions (no hesitant half-actions) and a further bonus for giving each other quick, judgement-free feedback on choices after the fact.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, drawing on scanning, spotting gaps, weighing safe-versus-risky, and reading defenders from across the whole block.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in, keeping the game moving.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games, so the wrap-up covers a range of matchups.</li><li>Coach the key message from the whole block: praise full commitment to a decision and honest, kind feedback afterwards, since this lesson brings together everything from Block 1 before moving on to Block 2's focus on risk and reward.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did 'committing' to a decision actually feel like today?</li><li>What's the difference between reflecting on a decision and dwelling on it?</li><li>Looking back over this block, what's improved most in how your team makes decisions?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Commits fully to a decision rather than hesitating or changing mid-action</li><li>Reflects briefly and constructively on decisions</li><li>Shows growth across Block 1 in scanning, reading, and choosing</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Model 'no regrets' reflection language for students who are hard on themselves after mistakes; allow extra thinking time in early rounds for those still building decision confidence.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Weighing Risk & Reward (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Worth the Risk?":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Weighing risk versus reward before attempting an ambitious option</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to weigh up whether a risky option is actually worth it before we try it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can weigh up the risk and reward before attempting an ambitious play</li><li>I can choose the safer option when the risk clearly outweighs the reward</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Choice Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that movement doesn't collide with a neighbouring pair. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly together, side by side or facing each other while shuffling backwards/forwards.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, one partner calls out 'safe' or 'go' and the pair changes their jogging pace to match — slow and controlled for 'safe', a quicker burst for 'go'.</li><li>This connects to today's risk-and-reward focus by getting the class used to reacting instantly to a quick call before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every couple of rounds so both partners get practice calling and reacting.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace varied and continuous.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners quickly sharing one risky or safe decision they remember making well today, before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Ask what you actually gain if a risky option works.</td><td>A risk is only worth it if the reward genuinely matters — decide that before you try it, not after.</td></tr><tr><td>Ask what your team loses if it doesn't work.</td><td>Knowing the cost of a failed risk is just as important as knowing the potential reward.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the safer option when the cost clearly outweighs the gain.</td><td>Good decision-makers aren't afraid of risk, but they don't take one that costs more than it's worth.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Risk Points</h4><p>Small-sided possession game where a risky pass or move scores double points if it works but costs a turnover-style penalty if it doesn't, while safe options score a single guaranteed point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 (mixed attackers/defenders) playing possession in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, two cones marking a designated 'risky pass' zone at one end.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Is the double worth the risk right now?</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller penalty for a failed risky option.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger reward and bigger cost, sharpening the decision.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus multiplier round announced at random</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before starting: a risky pass or move into the marked zone scores double points if it succeeds, but costs a turnover-style penalty (the ball goes straight to the other side) if it fails; safe options score a single guaranteed point.</li><li>Groups begin a normal possession game, with players free to choose safe or risky options as they play.</li><li>Players decide moment to moment whether to attempt the risky option for a bigger reward or stick with the safe, guaranteed point.</li><li>On a turnover from a failed risky attempt, restart immediately with the ball to the team that gained it — no long stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'is the double worth the risk right now?' — prompting genuine weighing up, not automatic risk-taking.</li><li>For 'easier', reduce the penalty for a failed risky option; for 'harder', increase both the reward and the cost to sharpen the decision. The chaos variant randomly announces a bonus multiplier round.</li></ul><hr><h4>Worth It? Card Call</h4><p>In a partner activity, before each attempt one partner quickly says 'worth it' or 'not worth it' and why, before the pair attempts the play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with roughly 4m of space each, one ball or simple challenge per pair (e.g. a longer pass, a trick move).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say the reason, not just the verdict.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example reasons.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick-turnaround verdicts, no pause.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule: before each attempt, one partner quickly says 'worth it' or 'not worth it' and gives a brief reason, before the pair attempts the play.</li><li>One partner proposes the attempt (a longer or trickier pass/move) and the other gives their quick verdict and reason.</li><li>The pair then attempts the play regardless of the verdict, to see how the prediction compares with the outcome.</li><li>Swap who gives the verdict after every 2-3 attempts so both partners practise judging worth.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say the reason, not just the verdict.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers example reasons students can borrow; for 'harder', require quick-turnaround verdicts with no pause before attempting.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cost of the Fail</h4><p>In small games, every failed risky attempt is briefly discussed — what did it actually cost the team?</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Name the cost honestly, then move on.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher prompts the discussion point.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Players self-identify the cost.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for the clearest cost explanation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: every time a risky attempt fails during play, the group briefly and honestly discusses what it actually cost the team (possession, a scoring chance, position).</li><li>Groups play a normal small-sided game, attempting risky and safe options as they choose.</li><li>After any failed risky attempt, pause briefly for the group to name the specific cost, then restart play straight away.</li><li>Keep these discussions short — a sentence or two is enough before continuing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'name the cost honestly, then move on' — this isn't about dwelling on mistakes.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher prompts the discussion point; for 'harder', players self-identify the cost without prompting.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: High Stakes Possession</h3><p>Small-sided game with a genuine risk/reward scoring system — bigger reward for ambitious plays, real cost for failed ones — pushing teams to weigh decisions rather than always choosing one or the other.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, a genuine risk/reward scoring system explained beforehand (bigger reward for ambitious plays, real cost for failed ones).</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: ambitious plays (long passes, dribbles past a defender, shots from distance) earn bigger rewards if they work, but genuinely cost the team possession or points if they fail; safe options always earn a smaller, guaranteed reward.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with players choosing moment to moment between safe and ambitious options based on the real stakes involved.</li><li>On any stoppage or turnover, restart quickly with the ball to the appropriate team, keeping the risk/reward system running throughout.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: notice out loud when a team weighs the decision well, whether they chose safe or risky, rather than only praising successful risky plays.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a risk 'worth it' today, and what made one not worth it?</li><li>Did thinking about the cost change which option you chose?</li><li>When did a safe choice turn out to be the smarter one?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Weighs risk and reward before attempting an ambitious play</li><li>Can explain what a risky option would cost if it failed</li><li>Chooses the safer option when the risk clearly isn't worth it</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer simplified risk/reward language (e.g. 'big prize, big cost' vs 'small prize, safe') for younger or less confident decision-makers; allow extra discussion time before attempts early on.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Numbers Game":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading numbers — attackers versus defenders — to decide when to go</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones marking zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across overlapping small-sided zones</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to count attackers versus defenders to decide when it's actually a good moment to attack.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise when my team has more attackers than defenders in a space</li><li>I can decide to attack or hold based on the numbers, not just instinct</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Choice Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that movement doesn't collide with a neighbouring pair. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly together, side by side or facing each other while shuffling backwards/forwards.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, one partner calls out 'safe' or 'go' and the pair changes their jogging pace to match — slow and controlled for 'safe', a quicker burst for 'go'.</li><li>This connects to today's risk-and-reward focus by getting the class used to reacting instantly to a quick call before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every couple of rounds so both partners get practice calling and reacting.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace varied and continuous.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners quickly sharing one risky or safe decision they remember making well today, before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Count how many attackers versus defenders are in the space before deciding to go.</td><td>Numbers up — more attackers than defenders — is one of the clearest, most reliable signals that a moment is worth attacking.</td></tr><tr><td>Hold or reset when the numbers aren't in your favour.</td><td>Attacking into numbers down usually costs possession — waiting for a better moment is often the smarter decision.</td></tr><tr><td>Call the numbers out loud to help teammates decide too.</td><td>A quick call like 'numbers up!' helps the whole team recognise and use a good moment together, not just one player.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Count and Call</h4><p>Small groups move through a grid with defenders scattered through it; before every action, students must call out the number of attackers versus defenders nearby.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 moving through a grid roughly 12m x 10m scattered with cones representing defenders, some cones grouped to create uneven numbers in different areas.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Count before you decide, every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Numbers stay obvious and fixed for longer.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Numbers shift quickly as players move.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random numbers-check freeze</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule: before any action (a pass, a move into space), students must quickly count and call out the number of attackers versus defenders nearby — e.g. '2 v 1 here!'</li><li>Students move through the grid in small groups, approaching different cone clusters as they go.</li><li>Before acting near a cluster, they call out the numbers they see, then act based on what they counted.</li><li>Keep the movement continuous — the calling happens on the move, not as a full stop.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'count before you decide, every time.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep numbers obvious and fixed for longer; for 'harder', have numbers shift quickly as players move through the grid. The chaos variant calls a random numbers-check freeze.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v1, 3v2 Rotation</h4><p>Rotating small-sided overload games — 2v1, then 3v2 — where the attacking side must recognise and use the numbers advantage to attack directly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 for 2v1 and groups of 5 for 3v2, rotating between the two set-ups in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Numbers up means go, don't overthink it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender must stay further back.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Numbers advantage reduced (e.g. 3v2 becomes tighter).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Sudden extra defender joins mid-play</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the overload principle: whenever attackers outnumber defenders, the attacking side should recognise the advantage and attack directly rather than passing sideways out of habit.</li><li>Start with the 2v1 set-up: two attackers try to beat the single defender using the extra player.</li><li>After a few minutes, rotate to the 3v2 set-up with an extra attacker and defender added, keeping the same overload principle.</li><li>If defenders win the ball, reset quickly with attackers restarting from the far end of the grid.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'numbers up means go, don't overthink it.'</li><li>For 'easier', the defender(s) must stay further back; for 'harder', reduce the numbers advantage (e.g. tighten the 3v2). The chaos variant adds a sudden extra defender mid-play.</li></ul><hr><h4>Numbers Up Call</h4><p>Possession game where a player who correctly calls 'numbers up!' in a genuinely favourable moment earns their team a bonus point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 playing possession in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call it the second you see it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher confirms calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Players must justify their own call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>False numbers announced by teacher to test genuine reading</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: any player who correctly calls 'numbers up!' the instant their side has a genuine attacking advantage earns their team a bonus point.</li><li>Groups play a normal possession game, with numbers naturally shifting as players move around the grid.</li><li>Players watch for genuine overload moments and call them out the instant they spot one.</li><li>The teacher (or a nominated peer) confirms whether the call was accurate before awarding the bonus point, then play continues without a long stop.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call it the second you see it,' rewarding sharp, immediate reads over delayed ones.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher confirms calls to build confidence; for 'harder', players must justify their own call. The chaos variant has the teacher occasionally announce a false 'numbers up' to test genuine reading.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Overload Game</h3><p>Small-sided game with regularly shifting overload situations (an extra attacker or defender rotated in), rewarding teams who recognise and attack genuine numbers-up moments rather than attacking blindly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams starting at 4v4 in a grid roughly 18m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, one or two extra players ready to rotate in and create overloads.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for recognising and attacking a genuine numbers-up moment (created when an extra player rotates in), on top of standard scoring.</li><li>Start with an even 4v4 game from the centre of the grid.</li><li>At intervals, rotate an extra attacker or defender into the game to create a temporary overload, without stopping play.</li><li>Players must notice the shifted numbers and adjust their decisions accordingly, attacking directly when the numbers favour them.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in, keeping the rotating-overload system running.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise the team that attacks the overload immediately rather than passing sideways out of habit.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did 'numbers up' actually look like today?</li><li>How did counting change your decision compared to just going for it?</li><li>What happened when your team attacked into numbers down?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises numbers-up situations before attacking</li><li>Holds or resets when numbers aren't favourable</li><li>Calls out numbers to help teammates decide</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use simple visual cues (an extra bib colour for 'attacker') to make counting easier for students still building this skill; keep early rounds at a walking pace to allow time to count.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Plan B":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Having a backup option ready if the first choice doesn't work</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; clear communication when switching plans to avoid confusion or collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to have a backup plan ready in case our first decision doesn't work out.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can think of a second option before I commit to my first</li><li>I can switch smoothly to my backup plan if my first choice is taken away</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Choice Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that movement doesn't collide with a neighbouring pair. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly together, side by side or facing each other while shuffling backwards/forwards.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, one partner calls out 'safe' or 'go' and the pair changes their jogging pace to match — slow and controlled for 'safe', a quicker burst for 'go'.</li><li>This connects to today's risk-and-reward focus by getting the class used to reacting instantly to a quick call before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every couple of rounds so both partners get practice calling and reacting.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace varied and continuous.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners quickly sharing one risky or safe decision they remember making well today, before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Have a second option in mind before you commit to your first one.</td><td>A defender can take away your first choice in an instant — having a backup ready means you're never caught with nothing.</td></tr><tr><td>Switch to your backup smoothly, without panicking.</td><td>A calm switch to Plan B looks and works far better than a rushed, panicked scramble.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate a plan change to your teammates quickly.</td><td>A backup plan only works for the team if teammates know it's happening too.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Plan A, Plan B</h4><p>In pairs, before each pass attempt the passer quietly names two options — Plan A and Plan B — to their partner, then switches to Plan B if a 'defender' cone blocks Plan A.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 5m apart with a single defender cone placed between them, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your Plan B before you need it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender cone placement announced in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender cone appears at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation halfway</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule: before each pass attempt, the passer quietly names two options to their partner — 'Plan A' (the direct pass) and 'Plan B' (an alternative angle).</li><li>The passer states both plans quietly, then looks to see if the defender cone is blocking Plan A's direct line.</li><li>If Plan A is blocked, the passer switches smoothly to Plan B without pausing to re-think from scratch.</li><li>Move the defender cone to a new position after every few attempts to keep testing the switch.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your Plan B before you need it.'</li><li>For 'easier', announce the defender cone's placement in advance; for 'harder', place it at the last second. The chaos variant swaps roles halfway through.</li></ul><hr><h4>'Blocked!' Reaction Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where the teacher or a defender can call or signal 'blocked!' on a player's intended option, forcing an instant, smooth switch to a backup.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing small-sided possession in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, an active defender able to call or signal 'blocked!'</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Switch calmly, don't freeze.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Blocked calls given with more warning.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Blocked calls given at the last possible moment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Double-block forces a third option</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the defender (or teacher) can call or signal 'blocked!' on a player's clearly intended option, forcing that player to instantly and smoothly switch to a backup.</li><li>Groups play a normal possession game, with the defender watching for a player committing to an obvious option.</li><li>When 'blocked!' is called, the player with the ball must immediately find and use a different option rather than freezing or forcing the original plan.</li><li>Play continues without a full stop — the call happens on the move.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'switch calmly, don't freeze.'</li><li>For 'easier', give blocked calls with more warning; for 'harder', call them at the last possible moment. The chaos variant occasionally uses a double-block, forcing a third option.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call the Switch</h4><p>Possession game where players must verbally call out when they're switching from their first plan to a backup, so teammates can adjust too.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing possession in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say it loud enough for your team to hear and react.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher prompts the call if forgotten.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fast-paced play, quick switches required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: whenever a player switches from their first intended plan to a backup, they must call it out loud (e.g. 'switching!') so teammates can adjust too.</li><li>Groups play a normal possession game, choosing and occasionally switching plans as the situation changes.</li><li>Whenever a switch happens, the player calls it clearly so nearby teammates can react and reposition.</li><li>Keep play continuous — the call is part of the action, not a stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say it loud enough for your team to hear and react.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher prompts the call if a student forgets; for 'harder', play at a faster pace requiring quicker switches.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Backup Ball</h3><p>Small-sided possession game where teams earn a bonus point for smoothly executing a backup plan after their first option is genuinely blocked, on top of standard scoring.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, small goals or end-zones marked at each end.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for smoothly executing a backup plan after their first option is genuinely blocked by a defender, on top of standard scoring.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, encouraging players to have a backup option ready and to switch to it smoothly when their first choice is shut down.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in from where the ball went out.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise calm, smooth switches to a backup plan over forced, hesitant attempts to force the original option through.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was your Plan B today, and when did you actually need it?</li><li>How did having a backup ready change how calm you felt?</li><li>What happened when a plan change wasn't communicated clearly?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Has a backup option in mind before committing to a first choice</li><li>Switches to a backup smoothly rather than panicking</li><li>Communicates a plan change to teammates</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow extra thinking time in early rounds to name both Plan A and Plan B out loud before playing; pair less confident decision-makers with a supportive partner who models calm switching.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Under the Pump":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making under time and defensive pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; remind students that rushing under pressure is a common cause of collisions, so stay controlled</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make good decisions even when we're under pressure and don't have much time.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a clear decision quickly when I'm under pressure</li><li>I can stay calm rather than rushing into a poor choice</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Choice Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that movement doesn't collide with a neighbouring pair. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly together, side by side or facing each other while shuffling backwards/forwards.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, one partner calls out 'safe' or 'go' and the pair changes their jogging pace to match — slow and controlled for 'safe', a quicker burst for 'go'.</li><li>This connects to today's risk-and-reward focus by getting the class used to reacting instantly to a quick call before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every couple of rounds so both partners get practice calling and reacting.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace varied and continuous.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners quickly sharing one risky or safe decision they remember making well today, before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Make a clear decision quickly, even when you feel rushed.</td><td>Pressure won't go away, so the goal is deciding well within it, not waiting for it to disappear.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay physically calm — controlled feet and breathing — even when the pressure is on.</td><td>A calm body usually leads to a clearer decision than a panicked one.</td></tr><tr><td>Trust the decision-making skills you've already built, rather than abandoning them under pressure.</td><td>Good habits, like scanning, reading numbers, and having a backup, matter most exactly when there's least time to think.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Beat the Clock Decisions</h4><p>Simple passing or possession drills with a shrinking time limit to make a decision before a defender or timer catches up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or small groups of 3 in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, a visible or verbal countdown timer.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Quick and calm, not rushed and sloppy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More time allowed per decision.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Time shrinks round by round.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Sudden extra defender added</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: students have a set time (starting around 5 seconds) to make and execute a decision — a pass or a move — before the timer or a chasing defender catches up.</li><li>Start with the ball at one player, who must decide and act before time runs out.</li><li>The teacher calls the countdown aloud or uses a visible timer, shrinking the allowed time round by round.</li><li>If the decision isn't made in time, that round is simply reset and play continues to the next attempt — no penalty beyond a quick restart.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'quick and calm, not rushed and sloppy.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow more time per decision; for 'harder', shrink the time round by round. The chaos variant adds a sudden extra defender.</li></ul><hr><h4>Closing Down</h4><p>Small-sided game where one or two defenders actively close down space quickly, forcing the ball carrier to decide fast using skills from earlier lessons.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 (including 1-2 active defenders) in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use what you already know, just faster.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders close down slowly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders close down immediately and aggressively.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation on a whistle</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: one or two defenders actively close down the ball carrier quickly, forcing a fast decision using skills already practised earlier in the unit (scanning, gaps, reading a defender).</li><li>The attacking group begins with possession while defenders start a short distance away.</li><li>On the teacher's signal, defenders close down quickly and attackers must decide fast — pass, dribble, or use a backup plan.</li><li>If defenders win the ball, rotate roles so different students get defending turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use what you already know, just faster.'</li><li>For 'easier', defenders close down slowly; for 'harder', defenders close down immediately and aggressively. The chaos variant rotates roles on a random whistle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Calm Under Pressure</h4><p>Partner drills where one player applies light pressure (shadowing, noise, time pressure) while the other practises staying physically relaxed while still deciding quickly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with roughly 3m of space each, no ball needed for the core drill (optional light ball work can be added).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice your own breathing and feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Light pressure only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Pressure increases gradually.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: one partner applies light pressure — shadowing closely, making noise, or counting down loudly — while the other practises staying physically relaxed while still deciding quickly.</li><li>The pressured partner focuses on keeping their shoulders and breathing relaxed rather than tensing up, while still responding to prompts from their partner.</li><li>Swap roles after 1-2 minutes so both partners experience applying and handling pressure.</li><li>Keep the activity light-hearted — this is about noticing physical tension, not a serious contest.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice your own breathing and feet.'</li><li>For 'easier', apply light pressure only; for 'harder', increase pressure gradually over the activity.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Pressure Cooker Possession</h3><p>Small-sided game with actively closing defenders and a shot-clock-style time pressure, rewarding teams who keep making clear, calm decisions rather than rushing or freezing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, a visible or verbal shot-clock-style timer for possessions.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams keep standard points for scoring, and are rewarded for making clear, calm decisions under the shot-clock pressure rather than rushing or freezing.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half, with defenders instructed to close down actively.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with a running shot-clock (e.g. 15-20 seconds) that resets on each change of possession, keeping decision-making under real time pressure.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in and reset the clock.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: notice and praise players who stay calm and controlled under pressure rather than rushing their decisions.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did good decision-making under pressure actually look like today?</li><li>What's the difference between deciding quickly and deciding rushed?</li><li>What helped you stay calm when the pressure was on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Makes a clear decision quickly under time or defensive pressure</li><li>Shows calm body language (controlled feet, breathing) under pressure</li><li>Uses established decision-making skills rather than abandoning them when rushed</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Build pressure gradually across the lesson so students aren't overwhelmed early; allow a brief 'reset breath' option before a decision for students who need it.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Team Choice":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Group decision-making — weighing options together as a team (Block 2 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 2 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make a shared decision as a team quickly and confidently, combining everyone's read of the situation.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can contribute my read of the situation to a team decision</li><li>I can commit to a team's shared decision even if it wasn't my first idea</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Choice Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that movement doesn't collide with a neighbouring pair. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly together, side by side or facing each other while shuffling backwards/forwards.</li><li>Every 20-30 seconds, one partner calls out 'safe' or 'go' and the pair changes their jogging pace to match — slow and controlled for 'safe', a quicker burst for 'go'.</li><li>This connects to today's risk-and-reward focus by getting the class used to reacting instantly to a quick call before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Swap who makes the call every couple of rounds so both partners get practice calling and reacting.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, keeping the pace varied and continuous.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners quickly sharing one risky or safe decision they remember making well today, before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Share your read of the situation quickly and clearly with your team.</td><td>A team decision is only as good as the information shared to make it — your read matters, so offer it.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to the team's decision, even if it wasn't your first idea.</td><td>A team that fully commits to one shared decision usually beats one where players are each doing something different.</td></tr><tr><td>Combine risk/reward thinking, numbers-reading, and backup plans as a team, not just individually.</td><td>This block's skills are most powerful when a whole team uses them together, not just one player.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Quick Team Call</h4><p>Small groups make a fast shared decision (attack down the left or right, go now or reset) using only a few seconds of quiet discussion before acting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 huddled briefly before short rounds of small-sided play in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Share your read fast, then commit together.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More discussion time allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Very short decision windows.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus for a genuinely shared, fast decision</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before each short round, groups get only a few seconds of quiet discussion to make a fast shared decision (e.g. attack down the left or right, go now or reset).</li><li>Groups huddle briefly, share their read of the situation quickly, and agree on the decision together.</li><li>The round then plays out with the team trying to follow through on their quick shared decision.</li><li>Reset and repeat for several short rounds, changing the decision each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'share your read fast, then commit together.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow more discussion time; for 'harder', use very short decision windows. The chaos variant gives a bonus for a genuinely shared, fast decision.</li></ul><hr><h4>Risk/Reward Vote</h4><p>Before a round, teams briefly weigh up a risky versus safe team option and must agree together before playing it out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 briefly discussing before a short round of small-sided play in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone gets a say, then the team commits.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers the two options to choose between.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams generate their own risk/reward options.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before a round, teams briefly weigh up a risky versus a safe team option (e.g. attack quickly versus build possession slowly) and must agree together before playing it out.</li><li>Groups discuss for a short, timed window and settle on one option as a team.</li><li>The round is then played trying to follow through on the agreed team option.</li><li>After the round, briefly note whether the chosen option worked out, then move to the next round.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone gets a say, then the team commits.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers the two options to choose between; for 'harder', teams generate their own risk/reward options.</li></ul><hr><h4>Backup Team Plan</h4><p>Teams plan a simple Plan A and Plan B together before a short game, then must switch to the team's Plan B smoothly if Plan A is blocked.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 planning briefly before a short game in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know the team's backup, not just your own.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Plans kept very simple.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Plans include multiple players' roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation mid-game</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: teams plan a simple Plan A and Plan B together before the short game starts, involving more than one player's role.</li><li>Groups huddle to agree both plans, keeping them simple and clear enough for everyone to remember.</li><li>During the game, if Plan A is clearly blocked by opponents, the team must smoothly switch to their agreed Plan B.</li><li>Play continues without a full stop when the switch happens — it should feel like a natural in-game adjustment.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know the team's backup, not just your own.'</li><li>For 'easier', keep plans very simple; for 'harder', include multiple players' roles in each plan. The chaos variant rotates roles mid-game.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Decision Cup</h3><p>Small-sided round-robin where teams huddle briefly before each game to agree a shared plan, combining risk/reward thinking, numbers-reading, and backup plans from across the block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, small goals or end-zones marked at each end, brief huddle time built in before each game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams huddle briefly before each round-robin game to agree a shared plan, combining risk/reward thinking, numbers-reading, and backup plans from across the block.</li><li>Give each team a short, timed huddle to agree their plan before the round begins.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with teams trying to follow through on their huddled plan and adjusting to a backup if needed.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in, keeping the round moving.</li><li>Rotate opponents between rounds so teams get to try their planning process against a range of opposition.</li><li>Coach the key message from the whole block: praise teams that clearly used their huddle time well, since this lesson wraps up Block 2's focus on weighing risk, reading numbers, and having a backup, before Block 3 moves into adapting tactics.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a team decision better than an individual one today?</li><li>How did it feel to commit to a decision that wasn't your first idea?</li><li>Looking back over this block, what's improved most in how your team decides together?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes their read of the situation to a team decision</li><li>Commits fully to a shared team decision</li><li>Combines risk/reward, numbers-reading, and backup planning as a team</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure quieter students get a genuine turn to contribute during team huddles, not just the most confident voices; keep huddle time short and structured (e.g. one idea each) so every voice fits in.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Adapting Tactics & Team Plans (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Read the Pattern":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Recognising patterns and tendencies in how an opponent plays</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for repeated small-sided rounds</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to notice patterns in how an opponent or another team tends to play, and use that to inform our decisions.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can notice a pattern in how an opponent tends to play</li><li>I can use a spotted pattern to make a better decision</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; Shift Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m using cones. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shift prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'shuffle sideways,' 'change direction' — groups adjust their jogging shape and direction on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on reading patterns and adapting tactics by getting students thinking about reacting and adjusting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shift calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order so it stays unpredictable.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure needed for reading patterns and adapting tactics calmly rather than rushing.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch for a pattern in how an opponent or team tends to play over a few turns.</td><td>Most players and teams fall into habits — noticing them early gives you information nobody else has yet.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a spotted pattern to predict what's likely to happen next.</td><td>A good prediction, even an educated guess, beats reacting blind every single time.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep checking the pattern still holds — opponents adjust too.</td><td>A pattern that was true five minutes ago might not be true now, especially once an opponent notices you're using it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pattern Watch</h4><p>Students play repeated short rounds of a simple movement game against the same partner, trying to notice and use a pattern in their partner's choices.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4m apart in a simple movement game (e.g. rock-paper-scissors-style or a side-step chase game), repeated across several short rounds.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch a few rounds before trying to use what you see.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Partner plays a more obvious, repeated pattern at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Partner deliberately breaks their own pattern.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Partner swap mid-activity</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: students play repeated short rounds of the same simple game against the same partner, trying to notice a pattern in their partner's choices over time.</li><li>Play the first 2-3 rounds normally, without commentary, so a genuine pattern (if any) has a chance to appear.</li><li>From round 4 onward, players try to use what they've noticed to predict and counter their partner's next choice.</li><li>Continue for 8-10 rounds total, keeping each round quick.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch a few rounds before trying to use what you see.'</li><li>For 'easier', the partner plays a more obvious, repeated pattern at first; for 'harder', the partner deliberately breaks their own pattern. The chaos variant swaps partners mid-activity.</li></ul><hr><h4>Predict &amp; Play</h4><p>In a small-sided game, students briefly predict aloud what an opponent is likely to do next, based on what they've noticed so far, before playing the point out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 playing a small-sided game in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, played over several short rounds against the same opponent.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Base your prediction on something you actually saw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher highlights an obvious pattern to notice.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Patterns are subtler and require more rounds to spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before each point or possession, students briefly predict aloud what an opponent is likely to do next, based on what they've noticed in earlier rounds.</li><li>Play a short round first without prediction so students have something to base their read on.</li><li>From the next round, the player about to face that opponent states a quick prediction before the point starts, then plays it out to see if the prediction holds.</li><li>Repeat over several rounds, encouraging students to update their prediction as they learn more.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'base your prediction on something you actually saw.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher highlights an obvious pattern to notice; for 'harder', patterns are subtler and require more rounds to spot.</li></ul><hr><h4>Break the Pattern</h4><p>In a partner activity, one player deliberately plays a genuine pattern for a few rounds, then breaks it, and the other must notice the pattern breaking.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs facing each other about 4m apart in a simple repeated movement or passing activity, no ball required unless preferred.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice the moment it changes, not just the pattern itself.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious pattern break.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Subtle pattern break.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the two roles: one player deliberately plays a genuine, repeated pattern for several rounds, then breaks it on purpose, while the other tries to notice exactly when the pattern changes.</li><li>The first player repeats a consistent choice or movement for 4-5 rounds so the pattern is real and learnable.</li><li>The first player then deliberately breaks the pattern, and the second player calls out the moment they notice the change.</li><li>Swap roles after each full sequence so both partners get to set and spot a pattern.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice the moment it changes, not just the pattern itself.'</li><li>For 'easier', use an obvious pattern break; for 'harder', make the break subtle. The chaos variant rotates roles unpredictably.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Scout &amp; Score</h3><p>Round-robin small-sided games where teams play the same opponent over multiple short rounds, earning a bonus point for decisions that clearly used a pattern spotted from an earlier round.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 playing several short round-robin games against the same opponent, in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for decisions that clearly used a pattern spotted from an earlier round against the same opponent, on top of standard scoring.</li><li>Play the first short round normally, giving both teams a chance to observe how the other side tends to play.</li><li>In later rounds against the same opponent, encourage players to use what they noticed to anticipate and counter the opponent's choices.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with the ball to the appropriate team.</li><li>Rotate to a new opponent after several rounds so students practise spotting fresh patterns against different teams.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise a specific, named observation ('they always pass right after two touches') over a vague guess.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What pattern did you notice in an opponent today?</li><li>How did using a pattern change your decision-making?</li><li>What happened when a pattern you were using suddenly changed?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Notices patterns in an opponent's play over multiple turns</li><li>Uses a spotted pattern to inform a decision</li><li>Notices and adjusts when a pattern changes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clearly exaggerated patterns in early rounds so the skill is easy to notice and build confidence; pair students thoughtfully so pattern-reading practice is genuinely useful for both.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Change It Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Adapting a plan mid-game when the first plan isn't working</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; clear signals for plan changes to avoid confusion mid-play</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to notice when a plan isn't working and change it, rather than sticking with it out of habit.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can notice when my team's plan isn't working</li><li>I can suggest or support a change of plan mid-game</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; Shift Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m using cones. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shift prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'shuffle sideways,' 'change direction' — groups adjust their jogging shape and direction on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on reading patterns and adapting tactics by getting students thinking about reacting and adjusting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shift calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order so it stays unpredictable.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure needed for reading patterns and adapting tactics calmly rather than rushing.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Notice honestly when a plan isn't working, rather than just repeating it.</td><td>Sticking with a failing plan out of habit is one of the most common and avoidable mistakes in team games.</td></tr><tr><td>Suggest a specific change, not just 'try something different.'</td><td>A specific new idea is far easier for a team to actually act on than a vague call for change.</td></tr><tr><td>Support a teammate's suggested change even if it wasn't your idea.</td><td>A team that adapts together, without arguing over whose idea it was, adapts faster and better.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Plan Fail Spot</h4><p>In small-sided games, the teacher periodically pauses play and asks teams to honestly rate whether their current plan is working.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be honest, not just optimistic.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers a simple yes/no check.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams must justify their rating with evidence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the teacher will periodically pause play and ask each team to honestly rate whether their current plan or approach is actually working.</li><li>Groups begin playing their small-sided game with whatever approach they've chosen.</li><li>On the pause, teams quickly and honestly rate their plan (working / not working / mixed) and briefly say why.</li><li>Play resumes immediately after the quick check — this isn't a long discussion.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be honest, not just optimistic,' since noticing a failing plan is the first step toward changing it.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers a simple yes/no check; for 'harder', teams must justify their rating with a specific piece of evidence.</li></ul><hr><h4>Switch Signal</h4><p>Teams agree a simple signal — a word or hand action — that any player can use mid-game to suggest 'let's change the plan,' and the whole team practises responding to it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 agreeing a simple team signal before playing a small-sided game in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Respond to the signal quickly, don't argue about it mid-play.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Only the designated leader can call the switch.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Any player can call it.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a switch that clearly improves the game</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Have each team agree a simple signal — a word or hand action — that any player can use mid-game to suggest 'let's change the plan.'</li><li>Groups begin playing their small-sided game using their current approach.</li><li>Any player can give the agreed signal during play when they think the plan isn't working, and the whole team practises responding to it quickly.</li><li>Keep play continuous — the signal is meant to trigger an in-game adjustment, not a stoppage.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'respond to the signal quickly, don't argue about it mid-play.'</li><li>For 'easier', only a designated leader can call the switch; for 'harder', any player can call it. The chaos variant gives a bonus point for a switch that clearly improves the game.</li></ul><hr><h4>New Idea, Old Idea</h4><p>Small groups practise proposing a specific new plan (not just 'do something else') after a plan clearly isn't working, and try it out immediately.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 practising in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Name the new plan clearly in one sentence.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example alternative plans.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams generate their own alternative.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: after a plan clearly isn't working, groups must propose a specific new plan — not just 'do something else' — and try it out immediately.</li><li>Groups start with their original plan and play until it's clear it isn't working (or the teacher signals a check).</li><li>The team quickly names a specific alternative plan in one sentence, then puts it into action straight away.</li><li>Repeat with a few different starting plans across the activity so students get practice both failing and adapting.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'name the new plan clearly in one sentence.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers example alternative plans; for 'harder', teams generate their own alternative. The chaos variant rotates roles.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Adapt or Lose</h3><p>Small-sided round-robin where teams that visibly notice a failing plan and adapt to a new one within the same game earn a bonus point, rewarding adaptability over stubbornness.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 playing round-robin small-sided games in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams that visibly notice a failing plan and adapt to a new one within the same game earn a bonus point, rewarding adaptability over stubbornness.</li><li>Start each round with teams choosing and briefly stating their initial plan.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, watching for a moment where a team's plan clearly isn't working.</li><li>When a team visibly switches to a new, named plan mid-game, the teacher awards the bonus point without stopping play for long.</li><li>Rotate opponents between rounds so teams practise adapting against a range of playing styles.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise the team that changes tack early over one that keeps trying a plan that clearly isn't working.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you know your plan wasn't working today?</li><li>What made it easier or harder to actually change the plan mid-game?</li><li>What happened when your team stuck with a plan that clearly wasn't working?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Notices honestly when a team plan isn't working</li><li>Suggests a specific alternative rather than a vague call for change</li><li>Supports a teammate's suggested change of plan</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Give quieter students a clear, low-pressure way to signal a plan change (e.g. a hand action) rather than relying only on speaking up; model honest, blame-free plan reviews.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Set Plays":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Using a simple pre-planned team strategy or set play</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones marking set positions</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; walk through set plays slowly before adding speed</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use a simple, pre-planned team play and execute it together on a signal.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can learn and remember my role in a simple team set play</li><li>I can execute a set play on a clear signal with my team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; Shift Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m using cones. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shift prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'shuffle sideways,' 'change direction' — groups adjust their jogging shape and direction on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on reading patterns and adapting tactics by getting students thinking about reacting and adjusting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shift calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order so it stays unpredictable.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure needed for reading patterns and adapting tactics calmly rather than rushing.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Learn your specific role within a simple team set play.</td><td>A set play only works if every player knows and trusts their own specific job within it.</td></tr><tr><td>Execute your role on a clear team signal, at the right moment.</td><td>Good timing is often what separates a set play that works from one that falls apart.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay ready to adapt the set play if the opposition reads it.</td><td>Even a great set play needs a backup once opponents start to recognise and defend it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Walk the Play</h4><p>Teams walk slowly through a simple 3-player set play — for example a decoy run, a pass, and a run into space — without pressure, learning their specific roles.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 3 walking through a marked-out grid roughly 10m x 8m with cones showing set starting positions for a simple 3-player set play (a decoy run, a pass, and a run into space).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your job in the play before adding any speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher talks the team through each step.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team must run it from memory without prompts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the full set play once at walking pace so students can see the whole sequence before splitting into roles.</li><li>Assign each of the 3 players a specific role — decoy runner, passer, and the player running into space.</li><li>Walk through the play slowly and without pressure, focusing on each player knowing exactly where to move and when.</li><li>Repeat the walk-through several times, swapping roles so everyone experiences each part of the play.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your job in the play before adding any speed.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher talks the team through each step as they walk; for 'harder', the team must run it from memory without prompts.</li></ul><hr><h4>Signal &amp; Execute</h4><p>Teams practise their set play at game speed, triggered only by a clear agreed signal — a call or hand action — from a teammate.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 3 in the same grid roughly 10m x 8m, now practising the set play at game speed, one ball per team, an agreed signal (a call or hand action) to trigger the play.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Wait for the signal, then commit fully.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Signal given with plenty of warning.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Signal given at an unpredictable moment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation within the play</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Confirm the team's agreed signal before starting — a specific word or hand action that any teammate can give to trigger the play.</li><li>Teams begin in a normal small-sided or passing situation, waiting for the signal.</li><li>When the signal is given, all three players execute their roles from the set play at full game speed.</li><li>Reset to starting positions after each execution and repeat several times.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'wait for the signal, then commit fully.'</li><li>For 'easier', give the signal with plenty of warning; for 'harder', give it at an unpredictable moment. The chaos variant rotates roles within the play.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Play</h4><p>One team runs their set play while an opposing pair tries to read and defend it after seeing it once or twice.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>One team of 3 running their set play against an opposing pair of defenders in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Have your backup ready if they read it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defenders react passively at first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defenders actively try to shut the play down.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for an attacking team that adapts their play successfully</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The attacking team runs their set play once or twice at game speed while the defending pair simply observes without full pressure.</li><li>After seeing it once or twice, the defending pair tries to read and actively defend the set play on subsequent attempts.</li><li>The attacking team notices whether their play is being read and, if so, tries a small adjustment to keep it working.</li><li>Swap which team is attacking and which is defending after several attempts.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'have your backup ready if they read it,' linking back to earlier lessons on backup plans.</li><li>For 'easier', defenders react passively at first; for 'harder', defenders actively try to shut the play down. The chaos variant gives a bonus point to an attacking team that adapts their play successfully.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Set Play Showdown</h3><p>Small-sided games where teams earn a bonus point for successfully executing a learned set play, and an extra bonus for adapting it smoothly once an opponent starts to read it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, each team having practised one simple set play beforehand.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for successfully executing their learned set play, and an extra bonus for adapting it smoothly once an opponent starts to read and defend it.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half, free to call their set play whenever the signal condition is right.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with teams triggering their practised set play at opportune moments.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in, keeping the game flowing.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise a team that notices their play being read and adjusts it, not just one that scores from the play working cleanly.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was your specific role in the set play today?</li><li>What made the timing of the play work well, or fall apart?</li><li>What did your team do when an opponent started reading the play?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Knows and executes their specific role in a set play</li><li>Times their action to a clear team signal</li><li>Helps adapt the play when it's being read by opponents</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use consistent, simple language and visual markers for each role so set plays are easy to remember; allow extra walk-through repetitions before adding speed or opposition.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Change of Plan":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading an opponent's tactics and adjusting the team's approach in response</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read how an opponent is playing and adjust our team's tactics to counter it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can notice a specific tactic an opponent is using against us</li><li>I can help my team adjust our tactics in response</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; Shift Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m using cones. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shift prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'shuffle sideways,' 'change direction' — groups adjust their jogging shape and direction on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on reading patterns and adapting tactics by getting students thinking about reacting and adjusting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shift calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order so it stays unpredictable.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure needed for reading patterns and adapting tactics calmly rather than rushing.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Notice a specific tactic an opponent is using, not just that 'they're good.'</td><td>A specific observation, like 'they're always doubling up on our left side,' is something a team can actually respond to.</td></tr><tr><td>Suggest a specific counter-adjustment as a team.</td><td>Matching a specific problem with a specific solution is what turns a good read into a useful tactical change.</td></tr><tr><td>Give the new adjustment a genuine go before judging whether it worked.</td><td>A tactical change needs a fair try before a team decides whether to keep it or try something else.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spot Their Tactic</h4><p>Short small-sided rounds against the same opponent, where teams pause briefly afterwards to name one specific tactic the opponent used.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4-5 playing short small-sided rounds against the same opponent in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific — where, when, how.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher helps identify the tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams identify it independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a short round (2-3 minutes) against the same opponent with no analysis yet, just normal play.</li><li>After the round, pause briefly and have each team name one specific tactic the opponent used — for example, always attacking down one side, or a particular passing pattern.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be specific — where, when, how,' rather than a vague statement like 'they were good at passing.'</li><li>Record or remember the spotted tactic, since it feeds directly into the next game, Counter It.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher helps identify the tactic; for 'harder', teams identify it independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Counter It</h4><p>After spotting an opponent's tactic, teams propose and try one specific counter-adjustment in the next short round.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups of 4-5 and same grid roughly 10m x 10m, continuing directly from Spot Their Tactic, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Match your adjustment to their specific tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example counters to choose from.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams generate their own counter.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a counter that clearly works</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Using the tactic identified in the previous game, each team proposes one specific counter-adjustment to try — for example, doubling up on the side the opponent favours.</li><li>Teams state their planned counter clearly before the next short round begins.</li><li>Play a further short round (2-3 minutes) attempting the counter-adjustment against the same opponent.</li><li>After the round, briefly note whether the counter worked, setting up the next game, Give It a Fair Go.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'match your adjustment to their specific tactic.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers example counters to choose from; for 'harder', teams generate their own counter. The chaos variant gives a bonus point for a counter that clearly works.</li></ul><hr><h4>Give It a Fair Go</h4><p>Teams commit to trying their tactical adjustment for a full short round before deciding whether to keep it, tweak it, or try something else.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same groups of 4-5 continuing from Counter It, in the same grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't judge it after one play — give it a real go.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer trial round.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter trial round, quicker decision needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind teams of the rule: they must commit to trying their tactical adjustment for a full short round before deciding whether to keep it, tweak it, or try something else.</li><li>Play a full short round (3-4 minutes) using the adjustment consistently, without switching back to the old approach partway through.</li><li>After the round, teams decide together whether to keep the adjustment as is, tweak it slightly, or move on to a different idea.</li><li>If time allows, play a further short round with the refined approach.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'don't judge it after one play — give it a real go.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a longer trial round; for 'harder', use a shorter trial round requiring a quicker decision.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Tactical Chess</h3><p>Round-robin small-sided games against rotating opponents, where teams earn a bonus point for identifying an opponent's tactic and successfully adjusting to counter it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 playing round-robin small-sided games against rotating opponents, in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for identifying an opponent's tactic and successfully adjusting to counter it, on top of standard scoring.</li><li>Play a short opening round against the first opponent to allow tactics to emerge naturally.</li><li>In the following round against a new opponent, encourage teams to spot and name a tactic, then try a specific counter-adjustment.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with the ball to the appropriate team.</li><li>Rotate opponents every couple of rounds so students practise this spot-and-counter process against a range of playing styles.</li><li>Coach the key message from the block's focus: praise a specific, named tactic and counter over a general 'we played better that time.'</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What specific tactic did you notice an opponent using today?</li><li>How did your team's counter-adjustment work out?</li><li>What's the difference between judging a new tactic too early and giving it a fair go?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Notices a specific tactic an opponent is using</li><li>Contributes to or supports a specific team counter-adjustment</li><li>Gives a new tactical adjustment a fair, genuine try</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Support teams in naming tactics specifically by offering simple example language ('they keep doing X'); ensure all voices are heard when proposing a counter-adjustment.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Captain's Call":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team-captain-style decision-making, combining reading, adapting, and set plays (Block 3 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones — combined equipment from Block 3 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games; agreed, fair rotation of the captain role</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make and communicate confident tactical decisions in a captain-style role, combining everything from this block.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a confident tactical call when it's my turn to lead</li><li>I can follow and support a teammate's tactical call when it's theirs</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; Shift Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m using cones. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shift prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'shuffle sideways,' 'change direction' — groups adjust their jogging shape and direction on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on reading patterns and adapting tactics by getting students thinking about reacting and adjusting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shift calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order so it stays unpredictable.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure needed for reading patterns and adapting tactics calmly rather than rushing.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Combine pattern-reading, adapting, and set plays into one confident tactical call when it's your turn to lead.</td><td>This block's skills come together fully in the role of making a real call for the team, not just contributing an idea.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate your call clearly and confidently to your team.</td><td>Even a good tactical idea needs to be communicated clearly to actually help the team.</td></tr><tr><td>Support a teammate's call fully when it's their turn to lead.</td><td>A team practises leadership best when every player gets a genuine turn, and every player backs whoever's leading.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Captain Carousel</h4><p>In small groups, the 'captain' role rotates every few minutes, with each captain responsible for calling one tactical decision — a pattern spotted, a plan change, a set play — during their turn.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, with a rotating 'captain' role.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Make your call, then trust your team to follow it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher supports the captain's first few calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Captain makes calls independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Rapid captain rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the captain role rotates every few minutes, and whoever is captain is responsible for calling one tactical decision — a spotted pattern, a plan change, or a set play — during their turn.</li><li>Assign the first captain and begin play, with the captain watching for a moment to make their call.</li><li>The captain makes their tactical call during play, and the team follows it as best they can.</li><li>Rotate the captain role on the teacher's signal (every 2-3 minutes) so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'make your call, then trust your team to follow it.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher supports the captain's first few calls; for 'harder', the captain makes calls independently. The chaos variant speeds up captain rotation.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call and Commit</h4><p>Small-sided games where whoever is captain that round must make one clear tactical call at least once during play, and the team must commit to it fully.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group, a designated captain for each round.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Commit to the call, question it afterwards, not during.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Captain given a menu of call options.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Captain generates their own call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: whoever is captain that round must make one clear tactical call at least once during play, and the whole team must commit to it fully.</li><li>Begin the round with the captain identified and play starting normally.</li><li>At some point during the round, the captain makes their one clear call, and teammates follow it without arguing mid-play.</li><li>After the round ends, the team can briefly discuss whether the call worked well.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'commit to the call, question it afterwards, not during.'</li><li>For 'easier', the captain is given a menu of call options; for 'harder', the captain generates their own call.</li></ul><hr><h4>Leadership Feedback</h4><p>After each captain's turn, teammates briefly and kindly share one thing that worked well about their tactical call.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5, gathering briefly after each captain's turn in or near the playing grid, no extra equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Specific and genuine feedback only.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher models the first round of feedback.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students lead feedback independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>After each captain's turn ends, gather the group briefly for feedback.</li><li>Each teammate shares one specific and kind thing that worked well about the captain's tactical call.</li><li>Keep feedback short — one sentence per student is enough — and always positive and specific, not critical.</li><li>Move on to the next captain's turn once feedback is done, keeping transitions quick.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'specific and genuine feedback only.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher models the first round of feedback; for 'harder', students lead feedback independently. The chaos variant rotates roles.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Captain's Cup</h3><p>Small-sided round-robin where the captain role rotates through every player across the games, and teams earn a bonus point for confident tactical calls and for the team's full commitment to whoever is leading.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 playing a small-sided round-robin in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, with the captain role rotating through every player across the games.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for confident tactical calls from whoever is captain, and for the whole team's full commitment to following whoever is leading.</li><li>Assign the first captain for each team and begin the round-robin games from the centre of the grid.</li><li>Play normally, with each captain making at least one tactical call during their turn as captain.</li><li>Rotate the captain role at set intervals (e.g. every game or every few minutes) so every player leads at least once.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart quickly with a short pass-in, keeping the round-robin moving.</li><li>Coach the key message from the whole block: praise confident calls and full team commitment to the leader, since this lesson wraps up Block 3's focus on reading patterns, adapting plans, and set plays, before Block 4 moves into full game plans and pressure.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was it like making a tactical call as captain today?</li><li>What made it easier to follow someone else's call, even if it wasn't your idea?</li><li>Looking back over this block, what's improved most in how your team reads and adapts?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Makes a confident tactical call when leading</li><li>Communicates a call clearly to the team</li><li>Supports and commits to a teammate's call when someone else is leading</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure the captain role rotates fairly so every student gets a genuine turn, including quieter students; offer sentence starters ('Let's try...') for students who find leading aloud harder.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Game Plans, Pressure & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Game Plan Huddle":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Building and communicating a team game plan before playing</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games; keep huddle time brief and structured</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to build a simple team game plan together before a game and communicate it clearly to everyone.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can contribute an idea to a team game plan</li><li>I can explain the team's game plan clearly if asked</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple team-shape prompts as they jog: 'spread into your positions,' 'group up,' 'shift as a unit' — teams adjust their jogging shape together on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on game plans and pressure by getting teams used to moving and reacting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, gradually increasing pace toward the end.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting shape.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with a brief team cheer or shared moment where each team calls out one thing they're proud of from today's games, setting a positive tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Contribute at least one clear idea to a team game plan before playing.</td><td>A game plan is stronger when it draws on more than one player's read of the game.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the plan simple enough that every player can actually remember it.</td><td>A complicated plan that nobody remembers under pressure isn't actually useful once the game starts.</td></tr><tr><td>Be able to explain the team's plan clearly if asked, not just follow it silently.</td><td>Understanding the plan, not just going along with it, is what lets you adapt it well later if needed.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Huddle Up</h4><p>Before short small-sided games, teams have one minute to huddle and agree a simple game plan — for example, one tactic to try, and one role each.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4-5 gathering for a timed one-minute huddle before short small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep it simple — one or two things, not ten.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers a simple plan template to fill in.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams build their plan entirely independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: each team gets exactly one minute to huddle and agree a simple game plan — for example, one tactic to try and one role each.</li><li>Give teams their one-minute huddle, timed clearly so it doesn't drag on.</li><li>At the end of the huddle, teams begin their short game trying to put the agreed plan into action.</li><li>On any stoppage during the game, restart quickly without a fresh huddle — the plan should carry through the whole game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep it simple — one or two things, not ten.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers a simple plan template to fill in; for 'harder', teams build their plan entirely independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Explain the Plan</h4><p>Immediately after huddling, the teacher randomly asks one player from each team to explain the team's plan in one or two sentences.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same small teams of 4-5, immediately after huddling in Huddle Up, in the same grid roughly 12m x 10m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know the plan well enough to say it simply.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher helps if a player gets stuck.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Player must explain without any prompting.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random player chosen each time</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Immediately after the huddle ends, the teacher randomly asks one player from each team to explain the team's plan in one or two sentences.</li><li>The chosen player states the plan clearly and briefly before the game begins.</li><li>If the explanation is vague, the teacher can ask one quick follow-up question to sharpen it.</li><li>Once every team has explained their plan, games begin normally.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know the plan well enough to say it simply.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher helps if a player gets stuck; for 'harder', the player must explain without any prompting. The chaos variant picks a random player each time so everyone needs to know the plan.</li></ul><hr><h4>Plan in Action</h4><p>Teams play a short game trying specifically to use their huddled plan, then briefly discuss afterwards how closely they followed it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same small teams of 4-5, playing a short game in the grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice where you stuck to the plan and where you didn't.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simple plan, generous time to try it.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams must adjust the plan mid-game if needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Teams play a short game (3-4 minutes) specifically trying to use the plan they huddled and explained.</li><li>Players focus on their agreed roles and the one tactic chosen, without over-complicating things mid-game.</li><li>After the short game ends, teams briefly discuss how closely they actually followed their plan.</li><li>Use this discussion to feed into the next huddle if playing another round.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice where you stuck to the plan and where you didn't.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a simple plan with generous time to try it; for 'harder', teams must adjust the plan mid-game if needed.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Huddle &amp; Play</h3><p>Small-sided round-robin where every team huddles briefly before each game to build and communicate a simple game plan, then plays it out, discussing afterwards how well the plan worked.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two or more teams of 4-5 playing a small-sided round-robin in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, with a brief huddle built in before each game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before play starts: every team huddles briefly before each round-robin game to build and communicate a simple game plan, then plays it out.</li><li>Give each team their timed huddle before the round begins.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game, with teams trying to follow through on their huddled plan.</li><li>After each game, teams discuss briefly how well the plan worked before huddling again for the next round.</li><li>Rotate opponents between rounds so teams get practice building fresh plans against different opposition.</li><li>Coach the key message from today's focus: praise teams whose plan is simple, clear, and actually followed through — not just an ambitious plan that falls apart in play.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did your team's game plan actually include today?</li><li>What made a plan easy or hard for everyone to remember?</li><li>What happened when you explained the plan to someone else?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes an idea to a team game plan</li><li>Keeps a plan simple enough for the whole team to remember</li><li>Can explain the team's plan clearly when asked</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Provide a simple plan template (one tactic, one role reminder) for teams who find open-ended planning harder; ensure every player gets a genuine chance to contribute during the huddle.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Clutch Calls":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making in high-pressure, late-game moments</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; remind students composure matters as much as speed under pressure</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make clear, confident decisions in high-pressure, late-game moments.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a clear decision in a high-pressure, late-game moment</li><li>I can stay composed and supportive of teammates under pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple team-shape prompts as they jog: 'spread into your positions,' 'group up,' 'shift as a unit' — teams adjust their jogging shape together on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on game plans and pressure by getting teams used to moving and reacting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, gradually increasing pace toward the end.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting shape.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with a brief team cheer or shared moment where each team calls out one thing they're proud of from today's games, setting a positive tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Make a clear decision even when a game moment feels high-pressure or 'clutch.'</td><td>The skills built across this whole unit matter most in exactly these moments — that's what they're for.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay composed rather than letting pressure rush or freeze your decision.</td><td>A composed player usually makes a better late-game decision than a panicked one, even under the same time pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay supportive of teammates in high-pressure moments, especially after a mistake.</td><td>A team that stays encouraging under real pressure keeps making good decisions for longer than one that turns on itself.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Final Minute</h4><p>Short small-sided games with a genuine time pressure element — a visible countdown or a 'final minute' call — requiring a clear decision before time runs out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4-5 playing short small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per game, a visible countdown timer or clock.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use your final seconds calmly, not in a panic.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time windows.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Very short, high-pressure windows.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Sudden score change announced to raise stakes</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the game includes a genuine time pressure element — a visible countdown or a 'final minute' call — requiring a clear decision before time runs out.</li><li>Play the game normally for the first portion of time.</li><li>As the countdown reaches its final stage (or the teacher calls 'final minute!'), teams must make and commit to a clear decision before time expires.</li><li>When time runs out, note the result and reset for another short round.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use your final seconds calmly, not in a panic.'</li><li>For 'easier', use longer time windows; for 'harder', use very short, high-pressure windows. The chaos variant announces a sudden score change to raise the stakes.</li></ul><hr><h4>Composure Check</h4><p>Partner or small-group activities where the teacher deliberately raises the pressure — noise, a countdown, an audience — and checks whether players' decision-making stays clear and composed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partner or small-group activities (groups of 3-4) in a space roughly 8m x 8m, no ball strictly required, optional noise-makers or an 'audience' of watching classmates.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice your own breathing and body language under pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Pressure introduced gradually.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Pressure introduced suddenly and intensely.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the idea: the teacher will deliberately raise the pressure — with noise, a countdown, or an audience — while checking whether players' decision-making stays clear and composed.</li><li>Students perform a simple decision-based activity (e.g. a quick pass-and-decide sequence) under normal conditions first.</li><li>The teacher then adds a pressure element — noise, a countdown, or onlookers — and students repeat the activity.</li><li>After each round, briefly ask how the added pressure felt and whether decisions stayed clear.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice your own breathing and body language under pressure.'</li><li>For 'easier', introduce pressure gradually; for 'harder', introduce it suddenly and intensely.</li></ul><hr><h4>Support After a Slip</h4><p>In small games, any mistake in a high-pressure moment must be met immediately with genuine, specific encouragement from teammates before play restarts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 playing small-sided games in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Encourage first, reset second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher prompts the encouragement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team must self-manage without prompts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for the fastest genuine encouragement</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: any mistake made during a high-pressure moment must be met immediately with genuine, specific encouragement from teammates before play restarts.</li><li>Groups play a normal small-sided game with genuine pressure moments arising naturally.</li><li>When a mistake happens under pressure, teammates immediately offer a specific piece of encouragement (not a generic 'good try') before the next action.</li><li>Play resumes straight after the encouragement — this should feel quick and natural, not staged.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'encourage first, reset second.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher prompts the encouragement; for 'harder', the team must self-manage without prompts. The chaos variant gives a bonus point for the fastest genuine encouragement.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Clutch Cup</h3><p>Small-sided games with genuine late-game pressure moments — close scores, visible countdowns — rewarding teams for clear decisions and supportive communication under real pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Two teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, close scores or a visible countdown built into the format to create genuine late-game pressure.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams are rewarded for clear decisions and supportive communication under real late-game pressure, on top of standard scoring.</li><li>Start play from the centre of the grid with teams in their own half, ideally with scores kept close through the scoring system used.</li><li>Play a normal small-sided game with a visible countdown or clearly announced close score to build genuine pressure as the game nears its end.</li><li>On any stoppage or mistake under pressure, restart quickly and expect teammates to offer quick encouragement before play resumes.</li><li>Rotate ends or opponents every few minutes if running multiple short games.</li><li>Coach the key message from today's focus: notice and praise composed decisions and supportive team communication in the pressure moments, not just who wins the close finish.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a genuinely clutch moment feel like today?</li><li>What helped you stay composed rather than rushing or freezing?</li><li>How did your team support each other under real pressure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Makes a clear decision in a high-pressure, late-game moment</li><li>Shows composure (calm body, clear head) under pressure</li><li>Stays supportive of teammates, especially after a pressure-moment mistake</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Build pressure gradually across the lesson so no student is overwhelmed early; model calm, supportive language for teammates to use after a high-pressure mistake.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Scout the Opposition":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading and adapting to an unfamiliar opponent's style</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous, rotating games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read an unfamiliar opponent's style quickly and adapt our approach to it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can identify something specific about an unfamiliar opponent's style early in a game</li><li>I can adjust my team's approach based on what we noticed</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple team-shape prompts as they jog: 'spread into your positions,' 'group up,' 'shift as a unit' — teams adjust their jogging shape together on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on game plans and pressure by getting teams used to moving and reacting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, gradually increasing pace toward the end.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting shape.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with a brief team cheer or shared moment where each team calls out one thing they're proud of from today's games, setting a positive tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch closely in the first few minutes against an unfamiliar opponent to identify something specific about their style.</td><td>Every opponent has tendencies, and the first few minutes are often the best chance to spot them before they adjust to you.</td></tr><tr><td>Share what you noticed with your team quickly.</td><td>A quick, specific observation shared early can shape the whole team's approach for the rest of the game.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your team's approach based on genuine observations, not assumptions.</td><td>Reacting to what an opponent is actually doing, rather than what you expected, leads to better tactical decisions.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>First Five Minutes</h4><p>Teams rotate through short games against unfamiliar opponents, focusing specifically on identifying one thing about the new opponent's style in the first few minutes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 rotating through short games against unfamiliar opponents, in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch closely before you judge.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer observation window.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter observation window, quicker read required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Opponent rotation speeds up</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: in the first few minutes against a new, unfamiliar opponent, teams focus specifically on identifying one thing about the opponent's style.</li><li>Begin the short game against the new opponent, playing normally while actively observing.</li><li>Within the observation window, players note something specific — a favourite side, a passing habit, their pace of play.</li><li>Continue playing the rest of the short game as normal once the observation window has passed.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch closely before you judge.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a longer observation window; for 'harder', use a shorter window requiring a quicker read. The chaos variant speeds up opponent rotation.</li></ul><hr><h4>Report Back</h4><p>Immediately after the observation window, each team quickly reports one specific thing they noticed about their opponent to the teacher or another team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same teams of 4-5, gathering briefly immediately after the observation window from First Five Minutes, near the playing grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be specific, not general.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example observation categories (speed, passing style, favourite space).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams generate their own categories.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Immediately after the observation window ends, gather each team briefly.</li><li>Each team quickly reports one specific thing they noticed about their opponent to the teacher or to another team.</li><li>Keep reports short and specific — a single clear sentence is enough.</li><li>Move quickly back into play once reports are given, so momentum isn't lost.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be specific, not general.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers example observation categories (speed, passing style, favourite space); for 'harder', teams generate their own categories.</li></ul><hr><h4>Adjust and Play</h4><p>Teams use their scouting observation to make one specific adjustment to their approach for the rest of the game against that opponent.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same teams of 4-5, continuing their game against the same opponent in the grid roughly 12m x 10m, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Match your adjustment to what you actually saw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher supports the adjustment idea.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams generate their own adjustment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Using the observation reported in Report Back, each team decides on one specific adjustment to their approach for the rest of the game against that opponent.</li><li>Teams state their planned adjustment clearly before play resumes.</li><li>Play the remainder of the game trying to put the adjustment into practice.</li><li>After the game, briefly note whether the adjustment worked, ready for the next opponent rotation.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'match your adjustment to what you actually saw.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher supports the adjustment idea; for 'harder', teams generate their own adjustment. The chaos variant rotates roles.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Scout &amp; Adapt Tournament</h3><p>Round-robin small-sided games against a rotating set of unfamiliar opponents, where teams earn a bonus point for specific, accurate observations and effective tactical adjustments.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Multiple teams of 4-5 playing a round-robin against a rotating set of unfamiliar opponents, in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before play starts: teams earn a bonus point for specific, accurate observations about a new opponent and for effective tactical adjustments made in response.</li><li>Start each round against a new, unfamiliar opponent from the centre of the grid.</li><li>In the opening minutes, teams observe and identify something specific about the new opponent's style, as practised in the Skill Games.</li><li>Teams then adjust their approach based on that observation for the rest of the round.</li><li>Rotate to a new opponent for each round of the round-robin, repeating the observe-and-adjust process each time.</li><li>Coach the key message from today's focus: praise teams whose adjustment clearly matches something they genuinely observed, not a random change.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What specific thing did you notice about an unfamiliar opponent today?</li><li>How did that observation change your team's approach?</li><li>What's the risk of assuming things about an opponent instead of watching first?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Identifies something specific about an unfamiliar opponent's style</li><li>Shares observations clearly and quickly with the team</li><li>Adjusts the team's approach based on genuine observation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer simple observation categories (speed, passing, favourite space) to support students who find open-ended scouting harder; rotate opponents fairly so every team gets a genuine scouting challenge.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Strategy Trial Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Practice run bringing the whole unit's skills together ahead of the culminating games day</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games; agreed, fair self-officiating routines to prevent disputes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bring together the whole unit's decision-making and tactical skills in full practice games.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply a range of strategy and decision-making skills together in a game</li><li>I can give and receive tactical feedback constructively with my team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple team-shape prompts as they jog: 'spread into your positions,' 'group up,' 'shift as a unit' — teams adjust their jogging shape together on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on game plans and pressure by getting teams used to moving and reacting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, gradually increasing pace toward the end.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting shape.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with a brief team cheer or shared moment where each team calls out one thing they're proud of from today's games, setting a positive tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring together scanning, risk/reward thinking, reading opponents, adapting plans, and composure under pressure into one connected game.</td><td>A real strategic game asks for all of these skills together, often in quick succession — this is the whole unit coming together.</td></tr><tr><td>Give tactical feedback to teammates constructively, and accept it the same way.</td><td>Honest, specific feedback is what helps a team sharpen its decision-making right up to the culminating day.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect honestly on which decisions worked and which didn't, without dwelling on mistakes.</td><td>A short memory for mistakes, combined with honest reflection, is what lets a team keep improving decision by decision.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Warm-Up Strategy Points</h4><p>A short warm-up game where students settle into rhythm and remind themselves of key decision-making points from across the unit before full games begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or large groups playing a short, low-stakes warm-up game in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use this to find your rhythm, not to win.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer warm-up time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter warm-up, straight into full games.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the purpose: this is a short warm-up game to settle into rhythm and remind students of key decision-making points from across the unit, not a competitive contest.</li><li>Begin a light small-sided game, encouraging students to naturally scan, weigh options, and communicate as they've practised all unit.</li><li>As play continues, briefly call out reminders of unit themes — scanning, numbers, backup plans, composure — without stopping play for long.</li><li>Keep the intensity moderate, saving full effort for the practice matches that follow.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use this to find your rhythm, not to win.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a longer warm-up time; for 'harder', shorten the warm-up and move straight into full games.</li></ul><hr><h4>Skill Focus Choice</h4><p>Each student picks one strategy or decision-making skill from the unit — scanning, reading numbers, staying composed — to focus on today, and gets brief feedback on it during play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual students playing within their small-sided teams in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, one ball per group, each student having chosen a personal focus beforehand.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick something you genuinely want to improve, not just something easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher or peer suggests a focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Student justifies their own choice of focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before play begins, each student picks one strategy or decision-making skill from the unit — scanning, reading numbers, staying composed — to focus on today.</li><li>Students play a normal small-sided game while trying to specifically apply their chosen focus.</li><li>During play, the teacher gives brief, individual feedback tied to each student's chosen focus when the opportunity arises.</li><li>Continue through the game, encouraging students to notice their own focus area in action.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pick something you genuinely want to improve, not just something easy.'</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher or a peer suggests a focus; for 'harder', the student must justify their own choice of focus. The chaos variant adds a head-to-head mirror challenge.</li></ul><hr><h4>Practice Match Sets</h4><p>Short practice games played with full rules and scoring, as a run-through before the culminating tournament.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full teams playing short practice games with complete rules and scoring, in a grid roughly 15m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Treat this like the real thing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter games, more generous rules.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-length games, standard rules.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the purpose: these are short practice games played with full rules and scoring, as a run-through before the culminating tournament.</li><li>Start the game from the centre of the grid with both teams in their own half, using standard rules and scoring throughout.</li><li>Play the game as if it were the real tournament, applying strategy and decision-making skills from across the whole unit.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart promptly using normal game procedures.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'treat this like the real thing.'</li><li>For 'easier', use shorter games with more generous rules; for 'harder', use full-length games with standard rules. The chaos variant adds a head-to-head mirror format.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Strategy Trial Games</h3><p>Students play complete small-sided matches, self-officiated, drawing on scanning, risk/reward thinking, reading opponents, adapting plans, and composure under pressure from across the whole unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full teams playing complete small-sided matches, self-officiated, in a grid roughly 18m x 12m, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before play starts: students play complete small-sided matches, self-officiated, drawing on everything from across the unit — scanning, risk/reward thinking, reading opponents, adapting plans, and composure under pressure.</li><li>Start the match from the centre of the grid with both teams in their own half, using standard rules and full scoring.</li><li>Play the complete match with students managing their own calls (out of bounds, fair play) as practice for the culminating games day.</li><li>On any stoppage, restart promptly following the agreed self-officiating approach, keeping disputes brief and fair.</li><li>If time allows, rotate opponents for a second complete match so more matchups are trialled before the real day.</li><li>Coach the key message from today's focus: notice out loud when a team draws on a specific unit skill during the match, connecting it back to where it was first practised.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What part of your decision-making are you most confident in going into tomorrow's games day?</li><li>Who made a really sharp tactical decision today?</li><li>What's one decision-making skill you'd still like to work on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a range of strategy and decision-making skills together in one game</li><li>Gives and receives tactical feedback constructively</li><li>Reflects honestly on decisions without dwelling on mistakes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Match teams thoughtfully so games are competitive and enjoyable for everyone; offer flexible rules/space adjustments so every game remains genuinely playable and inclusive.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Strategy & Decision-Making Games Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating multi-game tournament celebrating strategic thinking across the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones, simple draw sheet or rotation chart</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across multiple simultaneous games; clear rotation system so students know where to go next</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's strategy and decision-making skills in a games day setting, showing fair play and reflecting on our growth.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply a range of strategy and decision-making skills from across the unit in competitive games</li><li>I can play fairly and make composed, confident decisions throughout the games day</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple team-shape prompts as they jog: 'spread into your positions,' 'group up,' 'shift as a unit' — teams adjust their jogging shape together on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's focus on game plans and pressure by getting teams used to moving and reacting together before the lesson's main content begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, gradually increasing pace toward the end.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging and shifting shape.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours. No equipment needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Finish with a brief team cheer or shared moment where each team calls out one thing they're proud of from today's games, setting a positive tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>End with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills from across the whole unit — scanning, risk/reward thinking, reading opponents, adapting plans, set plays, and composure under pressure.</td><td>The games day brings everything the unit has built together under real, enjoyable pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate decisions and support teammates and opponents throughout.</td><td>Fair play and clear communication are as much a part of this unit as the decision-making skills themselves.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth as a decision-maker compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on your own improvement in reading and deciding is as valuable as the result of any one game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skill Circuit Warm-Up</h4><p>A quick circuit combining scanning, numbers-reading, and composure under pressure — a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Three short stations set up around the space combining scanning, numbers-reading, and composure under pressure, groups of 4-5 rotating through, roughly 2 minutes per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth transitions between stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Briefly remind students what each of the three stations is testing, since this is a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</li><li>Groups start at their assigned station and complete a short version of that station's activity.</li><li>On the teacher's signal, groups rotate to the next station until all three have been completed.</li><li>Keep transitions between stations quick and orderly — equipment stays in place, only students move.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'smooth transitions between stations.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow longer time per station; for 'harder', shorten the time and speed up rotation. The chaos variant adds a head-to-head, beat-the-clock element.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Strategy Challenge</h4><p>Before games begin, each team huddles briefly to agree one specific strategic focus they'll try to show off today.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full teams huddling briefly before the games day begins, in or near the main playing area, no extra equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Make it specific and meaningful to your team.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: before games begin, each team huddles briefly to agree one specific strategic focus they'll try to show off today.</li><li>Give each team a short, timed huddle to decide their focus.</li><li>Teams state their chosen focus to the teacher (or write it down) before the first game starts.</li><li>Throughout the day, teams try to visibly demonstrate their chosen focus during play.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'make it specific and meaningful to your team.'</li><li>The chaos variant rotates roles within the team for who leads the huddle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Decision Spotlight</h4><p>Throughout the games day, the teacher notices and briefly highlights one sharp decision from each team to the whole group.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Ongoing throughout the games day across all playing areas, no extra equipment needed beyond what each game already uses.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice good decisions, not just good outcomes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: throughout the games day, the teacher notices and briefly highlights one sharp decision from each team to the whole group.</li><li>As games progress, the teacher watches for a clear example of good decision-making from each team — a scan, a smart risk, a good adjustment.</li><li>At a natural break, briefly and positively highlight the spotted decision to the whole group, naming the team and what they did well.</li><li>Continue through the day so every team gets at least one spotlight moment.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice good decisions, not just good outcomes' — a good decision that didn't quite work still counts.</li><li>The chaos variant adds bonus objectives that teams can complete for extra recognition during the day.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Strategy Games Day</h3><p>Students rotate through short strategic games across multiple areas, using simple scoring or a fair-play points system. Every skill from the unit — scanning, risk/reward, reading opponents, adapting, set plays, and composure — is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Multiple teams of 4-5 rotating through short strategic games across several playing areas, bibs to separate teams, one ball per game, a simple scoring or fair-play points system displayed for the whole group.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before play starts: students rotate through short strategic games across multiple areas, using simple scoring or a fair-play points system, with every skill from the unit being relevant somewhere across the games.</li><li>Start the round-robin with teams at their first assigned game area.</li><li>Play each short game using standard rules and scoring, drawing on scanning, risk/reward, reading opponents, adapting, set plays, and composure as needed.</li><li>On the teacher's signal, rotate teams to the next game area, keeping transitions quick.</li><li>Continue rotating through all game areas so every team experiences a range of strategic challenges.</li><li>Coach the key message for the whole unit's culmination: celebrate the specific decisions and adaptations shown throughout the day, tying each one back to a skill first learned earlier in the unit, rather than only celebrating final scores.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one strategy or decision-making skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did sharp, composed decision-making look like in today's games day?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other team games or sports?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of strategy and decision-making skills from across the unit, not just one</li><li>Plays fairly and communicates decisions in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth as a decision-maker compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure the games day with balanced/mixed-ability groupings; consider a simple fair-play recognition alongside game results to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Team Play":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Communication, Space & Trust (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Talk It Out":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Clear, purposeful communication (calling names, short clear calls)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (2 colours), 1 soft ball per group of 4, cones for boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Calls should be clear but not shouted right next to another player's ear; spread out enough that thrown/rolled balls don't collide with other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use clear, simple calls and teammates' names so the right person knows what to do, when.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can call a teammate's name before I pass or ask for the ball</li><li>I can use short, clear words instead of long sentences when I'm playing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Name &amp; Claim Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's signal, students jog toward the nearest person, say that person's name clearly, and give a quick high-five or fist bump before jogging off to find someone new.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate while getting names into the group's ears before the lesson's communication focus begins.</li><li>If a student doesn't know a name yet, they simply ask — 'what's your name?' counts as the claim for that round.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to greet as many different classmates as possible.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call a teammate's name before you call for the ball or pass it.</td><td>A name tells one specific person the message is for them, so the right teammate reacts instead of everyone or no one.</td></tr><tr><td>Use short, clear words — 'here', 'man on', 'switch' — instead of long sentences.</td><td>Games move fast; short calls can be heard and acted on in the half-second a teammate actually has to react.</td></tr><tr><td>Match your voice and your body — look at who you're calling to, not just shout into space.</td><td>Combining eye contact with the call removes doubt about exactly who the message is for.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Name Pass Circle</h4><p>Standing in a circle, students pass a soft ball underarm, calling the receiver's name clearly before every pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or groups of 8-10 stand in a circle roughly 4-5m across, one soft ball per circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say the name first, then pass — not the other way around.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger circle, slower passes, teacher prompts names if forgotten.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two balls going at once in the same circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random reverse-direction call</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule first: say the receiver's name clearly, then pass underarm — never the other way around.</li><li>One student starts with the ball, looks at who they're passing to, says that person's name, then passes.</li><li>The receiver catches, then repeats the process — name first, then pass — to a different student.</li><li>If a pass happens without a name being called first, the group simply pauses, the passer says the name out loud, and play continues from there.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say the name first, then pass — not the other way around.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger circle with slower passes and let the teacher prompt names if forgotten; for 'harder', add a second ball into the same circle so two passing sequences run at once.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call &amp; Catch</h4><p>In moving pairs across a grid, the receiver calls their own name and 'here' to ask for the ball, and the passer waits for that call before throwing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand at either end of a marked grid roughly 8m x 5m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Passer — wait for the call, don't guess.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walking pace only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Light jog with a defender shadowing (no contact) to add distraction.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for an encouragement word added to the call</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the call: the receiver says their own name followed by 'here!' as the cue for the passer to throw.</li><li>Both partners move gently within the grid; the receiver only calls when they're ready and in space, and the passer waits for that exact call before throwing.</li><li>If the passer throws before hearing the call, that pass doesn't count and the pair simply resets and tries again.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'passer — wait for the call, don't guess.'</li><li>Swap who is calling and who is passing every few throws so both partners get equal turns.</li><li>For 'easier', keep both partners walking only; for 'harder', add a light jog and a shadow defender (no contact) to add distraction.</li></ul><hr><h4>Silent Minute</h4><p>Teams try a short small-sided keep-away game with absolutely no talking allowed, then immediately replay it with talking allowed, and compare how each version felt.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4 in a marked grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per team, with a second identical round to follow.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice exactly what got harder with no talking.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slightly bigger space and simpler rules for the silent round.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a light defender in the silent round to increase pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the two-round format: first a short keep-away game (about 1 minute) with absolutely no talking allowed, then immediately the same game again with talking allowed.</li><li>During the silent round, teams try to keep the ball away from a light defender (or another team) using only eye contact and movement — no words at all.</li><li>Stop the silent round after about a minute, then run the same game again straight away, this time encouraging normal calls and talking.</li><li>Afterwards, ask the team to quickly compare how each round felt before moving on.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'notice exactly what got harder with no talking.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a slightly bigger space and simpler rules for the silent round; for 'harder', add a light defender in the silent round to increase pressure.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Call It, Catch It</h3><p>4v4 keep-away possession game where every pass must be preceded by a clear name call; teams earn a bonus point for an encouraging call after a mistake, on top of points for consecutive completed passes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 keep-away games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs to tell teams apart.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: every pass must be preceded by the passer calling the receiver's name clearly.</li><li>One team starts with the ball and tries to keep possession through named passes while the other team tries to intercept (hands-off, no contact).</li><li>Award a point for every 5 consecutive named passes a team completes without a drop or an unnamed pass.</li><li>If a mistake happens (a drop, an unnamed pass, or a turnover), the team must give the player involved a quick, genuine encouraging word before play restarts — this earns a bonus point on top of the passing count.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: naming your teammate and encouraging them after a mistake both matter as much as the pass itself.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a call 'clear' today, and what made one confusing?</li><li>What did the Silent Minute teach you about talking during a game?</li><li>Who used a really clear call today that helped their team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a teammate's name before passing or asking for the ball</li><li>Uses short, clear words rather than long explanations</li><li>Keeps communicating even when a play doesn't work</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow students to use a nickname or a simple pointed signal alongside the verbal call if speaking up is hard for them; pre-teach names in mixed groups before the lesson starts.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Find Your Space":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Spatial awareness &amp; support positioning (spreading out to give options)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (2 colours), 1 ball per group of 4, cones to mark a grid</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear grid boundaries so spreading out doesn't send students into walls, fences, or other groups' games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to spread out and move into space so we're always a useful option for whoever has the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move away from a teammate who already has the ball rather than crowding them</li><li>I can find and move into new space where I can be seen by the ball carrier</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Name &amp; Claim Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's signal, students jog toward the nearest person, say that person's name clearly, and give a quick high-five or fist bump before jogging off to find someone new.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate while getting names into the group's ears before the lesson's communication focus begins.</li><li>If a student doesn't know a name yet, they simply ask — 'what's your name?' counts as the claim for that round.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to greet as many different classmates as possible.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Move away from a teammate who already has the ball rather than standing near them.</td><td>Two players in the same small space give the ball carrier only one real option instead of two.</td></tr><tr><td>Move to space where the ball carrier can actually see you.</td><td>Being open is only useful if the person with the ball knows you're there.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep moving into fresh space rather than stopping once you find a good spot.</td><td>Defenders adjust to cover space quickly, so staying useful means continually resetting where the good space is.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Grid Spread</h4><p>In a marked grid, students jog freely, keeping as much distance as possible from every other player at all times — teacher periodically freezes play to check spacing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or groups of 10-12 inside a marked grid roughly 15m x 15m, no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Picture an invisible bubble around you and each teammate.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, fewer players.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, more players, faster movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Freeze-and-check spacing spot-audits</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea of an 'invisible bubble' — staying as far as possible from every other player at all times while jogging freely.</li><li>Students jog around the grid, constantly adjusting position to keep maximum distance from everyone else.</li><li>Periodically call 'freeze!' — everyone stops immediately and the coach checks spacing, pointing out anyone who's drifted too close to another player.</li><li>Resume jogging on 'go' and repeat several freeze-and-check cycles.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'picture an invisible bubble around you and each teammate.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with fewer players; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with more players and faster movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Space</h4><p>One student has a ball and moves around the grid; teammates must constantly reposition to stay 'available' — in open space, visible to the ball carrier — without ever being told where to go.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If you can see the ball carrier's eyes, they can probably see you.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Ball carrier walks only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Ball carrier can jog and change direction suddenly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every 60 seconds</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student holds the ball and moves around the grid; the rest of the group are 'teammates' without the ball.</li><li>Without being told where to go, teammates constantly reposition to stay in open space, visible to the ball carrier, ready to receive if called.</li><li>The ball carrier occasionally calls a name to 'receive' a pretend or real pass to whoever looks best positioned.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if you can see the ball carrier's eyes, they can probably see you.'</li><li>Rotate who holds the ball every 60-90 seconds so everyone experiences both roles.</li><li>For 'easier', have the ball carrier walk only; for 'harder', let the ball carrier jog and change direction suddenly.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v1 Keep-Away</h4><p>Three attackers keep the ball from one defender in a grid, with attackers rewarded specifically for spreading into good triangle shapes rather than bunching near the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 4 (three attackers, one defender) in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If you're standing next to a teammate, one of you should move.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, defender must stay 2m back until the ball moves.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, two defenders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a triangle shape spotted by the teacher</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the target: the three attackers keep the ball from the single defender, specifically rewarded for spreading into a good triangle shape rather than bunching near the ball.</li><li>The defender moves to press and intercept (hands-off, no contact) while attackers pass and reposition.</li><li>If two attackers end up standing close together, the coach or teammates should prompt one of them to move — coach the key message: 'if you're standing next to a teammate, one of you should move.'</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 2-3 minutes so everyone gets a turn defending.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid and require the defender to stay 2m back until the ball moves; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with two defenders.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Spread &amp; Score</h3><p>Small-sided possession game where teams score bonus points not just for completed passes but for visibly good spacing — support players staying spread into open passing lanes rather than clustering around the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 possession games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring rule before starting: teams earn points not just for completed passes but for visibly good spacing — support players staying spread into open passing lanes rather than clustering around the ball.</li><li>Play flows as a normal possession game, with the coach or a nominated caller watching for good spacing and calling out bonus points when spotted.</li><li>If the ball is turned over, possession simply switches to the other team and play continues immediately without a stoppage.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: spreading out gives the ball carrier real options, not just extra bodies on the field.</li><li>Keep encouraging players to check their spacing relative to teammates, not just watch the ball.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did 'good space' actually look like today?</li><li>What happens to a game when everyone crowds around the ball?</li><li>Who found really useful space today, and what did they do to get there?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves away from a teammate who already has the ball</li><li>Finds space where they can be seen by the ball carrier</li><li>Keeps moving into new space rather than standing still</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use visual floor markers (spots/hoops) as space 'targets' for students who find open spatial awareness harder to judge; simplify to a bigger grid with fewer players for those still building this sense.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Same Team, Same Job":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Understanding and communicating roles (attacker / defender / supporter)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (3 colours or role markers/wristbands), balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear role boundaries in early games to avoid collisions between students moving into the same space unexpectedly</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to understand different roles within a team game and communicate our role clearly to teammates.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can explain what my role is doing at any point in a small game</li><li>I can call out a role change clearly so my team knows what's happening</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Name &amp; Claim Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's signal, students jog toward the nearest person, say that person's name clearly, and give a quick high-five or fist bump before jogging off to find someone new.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate while getting names into the group's ears before the lesson's communication focus begins.</li><li>If a student doesn't know a name yet, they simply ask — 'what's your name?' counts as the claim for that round.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to greet as many different classmates as possible.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know your current role and exactly what job it does in the game.</td><td>A team where everyone understands their own job first can then combine those jobs — confusion about your own role spreads confusion to the whole team.</td></tr><tr><td>Call out clearly when your role changes.</td><td>Teammates can only adjust to a new team shape if they know a role has actually changed.</td></tr><tr><td>Move and act in ways that match your current role, not your favourite role.</td><td>A team only works when players commit to what the team needs right now, not just what they enjoy doing most.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Role Call</h4><p>In small groups, students rotate through three labelled roles — attacker, defender, supporter — with the teacher calling freezes to check each student can state their current role and job.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 6 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with 3 coloured bibs or role markers (attacker, defender, supporter) shared among pairs of students within the group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your job before you worry about anyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Roles stay fixed for longer before rotating.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fast, unpredictable role-swap calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three roles and each one's basic job before starting: attacker, defender, supporter.</li><li>Students rotate through the three roles during light small-sided play, wearing or holding a marker that shows their current role.</li><li>Periodically call 'freeze!' and point to individual students, who must state their current role and its job out loud.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know your job before you worry about anyone else's.'</li><li>Resume play after each check and continue rotating roles regularly.</li><li>For 'easier', keep roles fixed for longer before rotating; for 'harder', use fast, unpredictable role-swap calls.</li></ul><hr><h4>Colour-Coded Roles</h4><p>Each role wears a different coloured bib; students practise a simple small-sided game where only the correct role can perform a certain action (e.g. only 'attackers' can score).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 6-8 in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, with bibs in 3 colours marking attacker, defender, and supporter roles. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check bib colours before you act, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer roles active at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>All three roles active with fast rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation mid-game on a signal</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: only the role wearing the 'attacker' colour may perform the scoring action (e.g. only attackers can score).</li><li>Play a simple small-sided game where students check bib colours before acting, since actions are restricted by role.</li><li>If a student acts outside their role (e.g. a defender tries to score), the coach simply reminds them of their current colour and play continues.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'check bib colours before you act, not after.'</li><li>Rotate bib colours around the group every few minutes so everyone experiences each role.</li><li>For 'easier', have fewer roles active at once; for 'harder', have all three roles active with fast rotation.</li></ul><hr><h4>3-Role Rotation Game</h4><p>A continuous small game where every successful action (score, tackle, pass) triggers an immediate role rotation among the group, forcing quick adjustment to a new job.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 6 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with 3 role markers rotating through the group. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call your new role out loud the second it changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, walking-pace version first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-pace version with quick rotations.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Double rotation on a bonus event</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the trigger: every successful action (a score, a tackle, a completed pass) causes an immediate role rotation among the group.</li><li>Play continuously; whenever the trigger event happens, the students involved swap roles right away and call out their new role.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call your new role out loud the second it changes.'</li><li>Keep play moving with minimal stoppage — the rotation should feel quick and natural, not like a formal pause.</li><li>Run a slower walking-pace version first before moving to full pace.</li><li>For 'easier', stay at walking pace; for 'harder', run the full-pace version with quick rotations.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Triangle Ball</h3><p>Small-sided game built around three clear roles (feeder, attacker, defender) that rotate regularly; points are earned both for game success and for clearly communicating role changes to the rest of the team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 6-8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, using 3 role markers (feeder, attacker, defender) that rotate regularly. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the two scoring sources before starting: points for normal game success, and points for clearly communicating role changes to the rest of the team.</li><li>Play flows with roles rotating regularly on a set trigger or a coach's call — students must call out their new role the moment it changes.</li><li>The coach or a nominated caller listens for clear role calls and awards the communication bonus point when heard.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time in every role.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: know your current job, and tell your team the moment that job changes.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging — the goal is clear, confident calling, not perfect execution.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was your role today, and what was its actual job?</li><li>What happened when someone forgot which role they were in?</li><li>How did calling out your role change help your team?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Can state their current role and its job when asked</li><li>Performs actions appropriate to their current role</li><li>Calls out role changes clearly to teammates</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use coloured bibs or wristbands as a visual reminder of role for students who find quick verbal processing harder; allow a 'buddy' to help call out role changes early on.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Trust the Pass":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Trust &amp; cooperative movement (committing to teammates' calls and movement)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, blindfolds/eye masks (optional) or 'eyes closed' agreement, cones for a simple safe pathway</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Guided movement activities kept slow and in a clear, obstacle-checked space; spotters used for anyone genuinely blindfolded</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to trust a teammate's call or movement and commit fully to our part of a shared plan.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can commit to a pass or movement once a teammate has called for it</li><li>I can guide a teammate clearly and safely using only my voice</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Name &amp; Claim Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's signal, students jog toward the nearest person, say that person's name clearly, and give a quick high-five or fist bump before jogging off to find someone new.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate while getting names into the group's ears before the lesson's communication focus begins.</li><li>If a student doesn't know a name yet, they simply ask — 'what's your name?' counts as the claim for that round.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to greet as many different classmates as possible.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to a pass or movement once a teammate has clearly called for it.</td><td>Hesitating after a clear call is often what causes a good plan to fail — trust means acting on the call, not double-checking it.</td></tr><tr><td>Give guidance in short, calm, specific instructions.</td><td>A guiding voice that stays calm and specific is far easier to trust and follow than a rushed or vague one.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay patient if a teammate is unsure, and encourage rather than criticise.</td><td>Trust is built or broken by how teammates respond when things feel uncertain, not just when things go smoothly.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Guide &amp; Go</h4><p>In pairs, one student closes their eyes or wears a soft blindfold while their partner guides them verbally along a simple, safe pathway of cones using only calm, clear instructions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs at one end of a simple, obstacle-checked pathway marked with cones roughly 8-10m long, spaced at least 3m from neighbouring pairs. Soft blindfolds/eye masks optional.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Short, calm instructions — one step at a time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Guide can also hold the mover's hand or shoulder.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Voice-only guidance across a slightly longer or more winding path.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation halfway</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Check the whole pathway is clear of trip hazards before starting, and assign a spotter (the guiding partner) to walk right beside the mover throughout.</li><li>One partner closes their eyes or wears a soft blindfold; the other guides them verbally along the cone pathway using calm, clear, one-step-at-a-time instructions.</li><li>The guide stays close enough to physically stop their partner if needed, even though guidance is mainly verbal.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'short, calm instructions — one step at a time.'</li><li>Swap roles halfway through so both partners experience guiding and being guided.</li><li>For 'easier', allow the guide to also hold the mover's hand or shoulder; for 'harder', use voice-only guidance across a slightly longer or more winding path.</li></ul><hr><h4>Follow the Leader Formation Jog</h4><p>Small groups jog in a simple formation, with the lead student changing direction and the rest trusting and mirroring the change immediately without being told individually.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 jogging together in a simple line or V-formation across an open space roughly 15m x 15m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the leader, not your feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, simple straight-line changes only.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker pace, sharper direction changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Leader rotates on a whistle</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: the lead student jogs and changes direction, while the rest of the group mirrors the change immediately without being told individually.</li><li>Groups jog together with one student leading; the leader changes direction, speed, or path, and the rest follow smoothly.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'watch the leader, not your feet.'</li><li>Rotate who leads every 60-90 seconds (or on a whistle) so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with simple straight-line changes only; for 'harder', use a quicker pace with sharper direction changes.</li></ul><hr><h4>Trust Toss</h4><p>Partners practise catching a softly thrown ball while relying on their partner's verbal call of 'ready' rather than watching the throw the whole way, building trust in teammates' timing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners stand 2-3m apart with one soft, larger ball between them, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust the call — start moving your hands on 'ready', not after you see the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, softer ball, shorter distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller ball, slightly greater distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random short delay before the call</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the trust cue: the thrower calls 'ready' just before releasing the ball, and the receiver starts moving their hands on that call rather than waiting to see the ball fully in the air.</li><li>One partner throws softly on their own call of 'ready'; the receiver reacts to the call, not just the ball's flight.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'trust the call — start moving your hands on ready, not after you see the ball.'</li><li>Swap roles every few throws so both partners practise throwing and receiving.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger, softer ball at a shorter distance; for 'harder', use a smaller ball at a slightly greater distance.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Blind Trust Relay</h3><p>Team relay where one blindfolded or eyes-closed 'runner' per leg must be guided safely to a marker and back using only their teammates' voices, combining trust, clear calling, and patience under mild time pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 set up for a short relay with a clear, obstacle-checked pathway to a marker and back, roughly 10-12m each way. No ball needed — this is a guided-movement relay.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Check every team's pathway is clear of hazards before starting, and confirm a spotter is assigned to each blindfolded or eyes-closed runner.</li><li>One team member at a time closes their eyes (or wears a soft blindfold) and is guided by their teammates' voices to the marker and back.</li><li>Teammates use calm, clear, one-step-at-a-time calls, exactly as practised in Guide &amp; Go, working together so multiple voices don't create confusion.</li><li>Once the runner returns safely, the next team member goes, continuing until the whole team has had a turn.</li><li>Add mild time pressure by tracking each team's total time, but prioritise safety and calm communication over speed at every point.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: trust, clear calling, and patience matter more than winning the relay quickly.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did it feel like to rely completely on a teammate's voice?</li><li>What made a good 'guide' today?</li><li>Why does trust matter in a team game, even when nobody's blindfolded?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Commits fully once a teammate calls for a pass or movement</li><li>Gives calm, clear verbal guidance to a partner</li><li>Stays patient and encouraging if a partner is nervous or unsure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Never require a student to wear a blindfold if they're uncomfortable — eyes-closed-and-trusted is an equally valid option; pair thoughtfully so guiding partners are calm, clear communicators.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Cheer Squad":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Encouragement &amp; positive communication under pressure (Block 1 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, bibs, cones — combined equipment from Block 1 games for the wrap-up carousel</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games; remind students encouragement is always genuine, never sarcastic</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to give specific, genuine encouragement to teammates, especially straight after a mistake.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can give a teammate specific praise, not just a generic 'good job'</li><li>I can encourage a teammate straight after they make a mistake</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Name &amp; Claim Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class spread out in an open space roughly 20m x 20m, no equipment needed. Enough room for everyone to jog freely without bumping into each other.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone jogs freely around the space in their own lane, staying aware of who's nearby.</li><li>On the coach's signal, students jog toward the nearest person, say that person's name clearly, and give a quick high-five or fist bump before jogging off to find someone new.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate while getting names into the group's ears before the lesson's communication focus begins.</li><li>If a student doesn't know a name yet, they simply ask — 'what's your name?' counts as the claim for that round.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to greet as many different classmates as possible.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle reach-up/side stretch — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Give praise that names something specific a teammate actually did.</td><td>Specific praise tells a teammate exactly what to keep doing; generic praise doesn't teach them anything.</td></tr><tr><td>Encourage a teammate straight after a mistake, before moving on.</td><td>The moment right after a mistake is when encouragement matters most — it's what keeps confidence and effort going.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep team energy positive even when the scoreline or game isn't going your way.</td><td>A team that stays encouraging under pressure keeps playing well for longer than one that turns on itself.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Compliment Circuit</h4><p>Students rotate through short partner activities, and after each one must give their partner one specific piece of praise about something they actually did well.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs rotate through 3-4 short partner activities (borrowed from earlier Block 1 games) set up around the space, roughly 2-3 minutes per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Name the specific thing, not just 'good job'.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example phrases to choose from.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No repeated compliments allowed across the circuit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation between rounds</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: after every activity at a station, each partner must give the other one specific piece of praise about something they actually did well.</li><li>Pairs complete the short activity at their current station, then pause and exchange a specific compliment before moving to the next station.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'name the specific thing, not just good job.'</li><li>If a student is stuck for a specific compliment, the teacher can offer example phrases to choose from.</li><li>Rotate pairs (not just stations) between rounds so students practise complimenting different classmates.</li><li>For 'easier', offer example phrases throughout; for 'harder', don't allow any compliment to be repeated across the whole circuit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bench Cheer Challenge</h4><p>While one team plays a short small-sided game, the resting team's job is to actively and specifically encourage them from the side — tracked by the teacher for genuine, specific calls.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into two teams of 4; one team plays a short small-sided game in a grid roughly 10m x 8m while the other team rests on the side, ready to cheer. One ball for the playing team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Cheer for effort and good decisions, not just goals or points.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher provides encouragement phrase ideas.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must call out a specific action, not a generic cheer.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the resting team's job: actively and specifically encourage the playing team from the sideline throughout the game.</li><li>The playing team plays a short small-sided game while the resting team calls out genuine, specific encouragement — naming what they see, not generic cheering.</li><li>The teacher listens and tracks genuine, specific calls from the resting team during play.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'cheer for effort and good decisions, not just goals or points.'</li><li>Swap which team plays and which team cheers after a set time, so everyone experiences both roles.</li><li>For 'easier', the teacher offers encouragement phrase ideas; for 'harder', require a specific action to be named, not a generic cheer.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mistake Reset Game</h4><p>In a small game, every time a mistake happens (dropped ball, missed pass), the team must give the player a specific encouragement before play restarts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, playing a simple small game with a ball, on the lookout for mistakes to trigger the encouragement rule.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Encouragement first, then get back into it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher prompts the encouragement if the team is slow to respond.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster restarts, less time to think of the encouragement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for the fastest genuine encouragement</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: every time a mistake happens (a dropped ball, a missed pass), the team must give that player a specific encouragement before play restarts.</li><li>Play flows normally; whenever a mistake occurs, play pauses briefly while a teammate offers a genuine, specific encouraging comment.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'encouragement first, then get back into it' — keep the pause short so the game keeps its rhythm.</li><li>If the team is slow to respond, the teacher can prompt the encouragement to keep things moving.</li><li>Resume play immediately once the encouragement is given.</li><li>For 'easier', allow the teacher to prompt if needed; for 'harder', use faster restarts with less time to think of the encouragement.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Team Challenge Carousel</h3><p>Teams rotate through short challenge stations that each revisit a Block 1 focus — clear calls, good spacing, role clarity, trusting a teammate — with genuine encouragement required at every station, wrapping up the whole block.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class rotates through 4 short challenge stations (roughly 3-4 minutes each) that each revisit a Block 1 focus — clear calls, good spacing, role clarity, trusting a teammate. Equipment combined from earlier Block 1 games, set up at each station.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the carousel format: teams rotate through the four stations, each one a short revisit of a skill from earlier in Block 1.</li><li>At every station, genuine encouragement is required — teams should be exchanging specific, positive comments throughout, not just completing the task.</li><li>Move teams to the next station on a time signal (roughly every 3-4 minutes), keeping transitions quick and smooth.</li><li>The coach circulates and highlights good examples of encouragement and skill application as they happen at each station.</li><li>Continue until every team has rotated through all four stations.</li><li>Use this as a natural wrap-up of Block 1's whole focus — communication, spacing, roles, and trust — all tied together with genuine encouragement.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's the difference between a generic 'good job' and specific praise?</li><li>How did it feel to be encouraged straight after a mistake?</li><li>Looking back over this whole block, what's improved most in how your team talks to each other?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gives specific, genuine praise rather than generic comments</li><li>Encourages a teammate immediately after a mistake</li><li>Shows growth across Block 1 in communication, spacing, roles, and trust</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Model specific praise phrases for students who find this awkward or new; ensure quieter students get genuinely included as both givers and receivers of encouragement, not just louder personalities.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Supporting Teammates & Cooperative Play (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Give and Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Supporting a teammate in possession (moving after passing to offer a new option)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones to mark simple grids</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions as multiple players move into space at once in small grids</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to move into a new position immediately after passing, so we stay useful to our team.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move to a new spot straight after I pass the ball, rather than standing still</li><li>I can offer a passing angle rather than standing directly in line</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Passing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly on the spot while passing a ball gently back and forth using any comfortable pass.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'switch!' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby and continue passing with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and getting comfortable passing with lots of different classmates before today's cooperative-play focus.</li><li>Encourage students to say their new partner's name each time they switch.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the pace and switching frequency.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Move to a new position the moment you release a pass.</td><td>Standing still after passing removes you as an option — moving keeps you useful to the play.</td></tr><tr><td>Support from an angle rather than directly in line with the passer.</td><td>A defender can block one straight line easily; an angled option is much harder to cut off.</td></tr><tr><td>Decide where you're moving to before the ball arrives, not after.</td><td>Quick decisions before receiving the ball again keep the whole sequence flowing instead of stalling.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Give &amp; Go Grid</h4><p>In pairs within a grid, students pass and immediately jog to a new open spot before receiving the ball back, repeating continuously.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a grid roughly 8m x 6m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The moment the ball leaves your hands, start moving.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, walking pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, add a light chasing defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock — count clean give-and-go cycles</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate 'give and go': pass the ball, then immediately jog to a new open spot in the grid before receiving it back.</li><li>One partner passes to the other, then moves; the receiver controls the ball and passes it back to the mover's new position, then also moves after passing.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'the moment the ball leaves your hands, start moving.'</li><li>Continue this continuous pass-and-move cycle for the full activity.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid at walking pace; for 'harder', use a smaller grid and add a light chasing defender.</li><li>For 'chaos', count clean give-and-go cycles against the clock and see how many the pair can complete.</li></ul><hr><h4>Angle Finder</h4><p>Students practise moving to an angled position rather than directly behind or in front of the passer, so the return pass isn't blocked by anyone in between.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a grid roughly 8m x 6m, with two cones marking angled 'target spots' near each partner. One ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A straight line is easy to block; an angle usually isn't.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Cones mark two clear angled 'target spots' to move to.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No marked spots — students must find their own angle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: moving to an angled position rather than directly behind or in front of the passer, so the return pass isn't blocked by anyone in between.</li><li>One partner passes; the receiver moves into an angled position (using the marked target spots) before receiving the ball back.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a straight line is easy to block; an angle usually isn't.'</li><li>Continue passing and moving to alternating angled spots, swapping who passes and who moves regularly.</li><li>For 'easier', keep the cone target spots marked clearly; for 'harder', remove the marked spots so students must find their own angle.</li></ul><hr><h4>2-Touch Support Game</h4><p>Small-sided possession game where players are limited to two touches, forcing quick decisions and immediate supporting movement after every pass.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide where you're moving to before the ball even arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Three touches allowed instead of two.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>One-touch only for confident players.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a clean give-and-go under pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the touch limit: every player gets a maximum of two touches on the ball before they must pass.</li><li>Play a small possession game with the two-touch limit in force, forcing quick decisions and immediate supporting movement after every pass.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide where you're moving to before the ball even arrives.'</li><li>If a player takes more than two touches, possession simply switches to the other team or player and play continues immediately.</li><li>For 'easier', allow three touches instead of two; for 'harder', allow one-touch only for confident players.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Overlap Ball</h3><p>Small-sided possession game where teams score bonus points specifically for completed give-and-go sequences — passing then immediately supporting into new space to receive again.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for 4v4 possession games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule before starting: teams score extra points specifically for completed give-and-go sequences — passing then immediately supporting into new space to receive again.</li><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided possession game, with both teams trying to keep the ball and build sequences.</li><li>The coach or a nominated caller watches for full give-and-go sequences and calls out the bonus point when one happens.</li><li>If the ball is turned over, possession switches to the other team and play continues immediately without a stoppage.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: move immediately after every pass — don't stand and admire it.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a give-and-go work well today?</li><li>Why is standing still after a pass a problem for your team?</li><li>Who found a really good angle to support from today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves to a new position immediately after passing</li><li>Chooses an angled supporting position rather than a blocked one</li><li>Stays ready to receive again after supporting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use marked 'target spots' for students still learning to judge angles independently; simplify to a bigger grid and slower pace so the movement pattern can be felt clearly first.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Numbers Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Creating and using overloads (2v1, 3v2) as a team</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones to mark grids</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing in small grids; ensure defenders aren't outnumbered unfairly without rotation breaks</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to recognise when our team has an extra player and use that advantage to keep the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise when my team has more players than the defenders in a space</li><li>I can use an extra player rather than crowding the ball</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Passing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly on the spot while passing a ball gently back and forth using any comfortable pass.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'switch!' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby and continue passing with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and getting comfortable passing with lots of different classmates before today's cooperative-play focus.</li><li>Encourage students to say their new partner's name each time they switch.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the pace and switching frequency.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Count defenders near the ball to recognise when your team has an overload.</td><td>Spotting a numbers advantage is the first step — you can't use an overload you haven't noticed.</td></tr><tr><td>Use the extra player rather than crowding around the ball yourself.</td><td>An overload only helps the team if the free player actually gets involved in the play.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate an overload to teammates when you see one.</td><td>A teammate on the ball can't always see the whole picture — your call can point them to the free option.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>2v1 Rondo</h4><p>Two attackers keep the ball from one defender in a small grid, focusing on recognising and using the extra player rather than both attackers crowding together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3 (two attackers, one defender) in a small grid roughly 6m x 6m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If you're both near the ball, one of you is wasting the overload.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, defender must stay back until the first pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, defender allowed to press immediately.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every 90 seconds</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: the two attackers keep the ball from the single defender, focusing on recognising and using the extra player rather than both attackers crowding together.</li><li>The defender presses to intercept (hands-off, no contact) while attackers pass and reposition to stay spread.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'if you're both near the ball, one of you is wasting the overload.'</li><li>Rotate the defender role every 90 seconds so everyone gets a turn.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid and require the defender to stay back until the first pass; for 'harder', use a smaller grid and let the defender press immediately.</li></ul><hr><h4>Overload Alley</h4><p>3v2 possession game in a narrow 'alley' grid, where the attacking team must spot and use their extra player to keep possession under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5 (three attackers, two defenders) in a narrow 'alley' grid roughly 12m long and 4m wide. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the free player before you look for a risky pass.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider alley, more time on the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower alley, quicker defensive pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a pass that clearly uses the extra player</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup: this is a 3v2 possession game in a narrow alley, where the attacking team must spot and use their extra player to keep possession under pressure.</li><li>Attackers pass and move within the alley while the two defenders press to intercept (hands-off, no contact).</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look for the free player before you look for a risky pass.'</li><li>Rotate which three students attack and which two defend every few minutes.</li><li>For 'easier', use a wider alley with more time on the ball; for 'harder', use a narrower alley with quicker defensive pressure.</li></ul><hr><h4>Spot the Extra Player</h4><p>Freeze-frame game: teacher periodically freezes a small-sided game and students must correctly point out which team currently has an overload in a nearby area.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class playing several small-sided games simultaneously across the space, with the teacher circulating to freeze and check understanding.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Count defenders near the ball, not just anywhere on the field.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher highlights the relevant area before freezing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Whole-field freezes with no area highlighted.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Let several small-sided games run at once across the available space.</li><li>Periodically freeze one nearby game and ask a few students from other games to correctly point out which team currently has an overload (extra attacker) in that area.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'count defenders near the ball, not just anywhere on the field.'</li><li>Unfreeze and let play continue, repeating the freeze-and-spot process every minute or two.</li><li>For 'easier', highlight the relevant area before freezing; for 'harder', do whole-field freezes with no area highlighted.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Numbers Game</h3><p>5v3 possession game where the team of five must recognise and exploit their numerical advantage to keep the ball, while the team of three focuses on compact defending — roles rotate partway through.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 8 for a 5v3 possession game in a grid roughly 15m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup before starting: the team of five must recognise and exploit their numerical advantage to keep the ball, while the team of three focuses on compact defending.</li><li>Play flows as a possession game; the larger team looks to spread out and use their overload, while the smaller team stays compact and communicates to cover gaps.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'look for the free player before you look for a risky pass' for the attacking team, and 'stay compact and cover for each other' for the defending team.</li><li>Swap which team has five players and which has three partway through the game so both group sizes are experienced.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging for the smaller defending team — being outnumbered is the point of the exercise, not a disadvantage to fix.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'numbers up' actually mean, and how did you spot it today?</li><li>What happens if a team has an overload but doesn't use it?</li><li>Who made a great decision today by using an extra player?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises when their team has more players than defenders nearby</li><li>Uses an extra player rather than crowding the ball</li><li>Communicates an overload to teammates when they spot one</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clearly marked, smaller grids so overloads are easier to visually spot for students still developing this awareness; allow verbal prompts ('you're free!') from teammates or teacher early on.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Cover Your Mate":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Defensive cooperation (covering space or a teammate who's been beaten)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided defensive games; avoid over-crowding one area which raises collision risk</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to cover space or a teammate when they've been beaten, rather than everyone chasing the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise when a teammate has been beaten and needs covering</li><li>I can communicate defensive information clearly to my team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Passing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly on the spot while passing a ball gently back and forth using any comfortable pass.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'switch!' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby and continue passing with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and getting comfortable passing with lots of different classmates before today's cooperative-play focus.</li><li>Encourage students to say their new partner's name each time they switch.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the pace and switching frequency.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Recognise the moment a teammate has been beaten and needs help.</td><td>Cover only works if it happens quickly — noticing the moment it's needed is the whole skill.</td></tr><tr><td>Recover into useful covering space, not just towards the ball.</td><td>Chasing the ball directly often leaves the real danger space open; covering space protects the team, not just the ball.</td></tr><tr><td>Call out defensive information clearly and immediately.</td><td>A teammate who's just been beaten may not see the danger behind them — your call can be the thing that stops it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Cover Shadow</h4><p>Pairs practise defensive cover — one defender pressures an attacker while the second 'shadows' just behind and to the side, ready to step in if the first is beaten.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs (plus one attacker) working in a small grid roughly 6m x 6m, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups. No ball needed for the core pattern, though an attacker with a ball can be added.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Don't stand square-on behind — angle yourself to cover the danger space.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Attacker moves at walking pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Attacker adds a change of direction to test the cover.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the cover position: one defender pressures the attacker directly while the second defender 'shadows' just behind and to the side, angled to cover the danger space rather than standing square-on.</li><li>The attacker moves around the grid; the first defender pressures while the second stays in the shadow-cover position, ready to step in if the first is beaten.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'don't stand square-on behind — angle yourself to cover the danger space.'</li><li>Rotate roles every few reps so all three students experience attacking, pressuring, and shadowing.</li><li>For 'easier', have the attacker move at walking pace; for 'harder', have the attacker add a change of direction to test the cover.</li></ul><hr><h4>Recovery Run Race</h4><p>After being 'beaten' in a 1v1, defenders race to recover into a useful covering position rather than chasing the ball directly, competing against a partner for who recovers into the smartest spot.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one attacker and one defender per pair, with a marked 'useful covering spot' as the target for the recovery run.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Recover into the space, not just towards the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower attacker, more recovery time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster attacker, less recovery time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a 1v1 where the defender is deliberately beaten, then must race to recover into a useful covering position rather than chasing the ball directly.</li><li>The attacker moves past the defender; the defender immediately recovers toward the covering spot rather than sprinting straight at the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'recover into the space, not just towards the ball.'</li><li>Time or compare recoveries between partners to see who recovers into the smartest spot, then swap roles.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower attacker with more recovery time; for 'harder', use a faster attacker with less recovery time.</li></ul><hr><h4>2v2 Cover Game</h4><p>Small 2v2 game where defenders must communicate and cover for each other whenever one is beaten, with points awarded for successful team recoveries.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4 (2v2) in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call it the moment your partner is beaten — don't wait.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, slower pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, faster pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a verbal cover call</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: defenders must communicate and cover for each other whenever one is beaten, earning points for successful team recoveries.</li><li>Play a small 2v2 game; when one defender is beaten, the other must call it and cover immediately.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call it the moment your partner is beaten — don't wait.'</li><li>Award a point to the defending pair whenever a successful cover recovery happens, tracked by the coach or the group itself.</li><li>Rotate which pair attacks and which defends every few minutes.</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger space at a slower pace; for 'harder', use a smaller space at a faster pace.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Last Line Ball</h3><p>Small-sided game where defending teams are specifically rewarded for cooperative cover — communicating and covering for a beaten teammate — rather than individual defenders simply chasing the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: defending teams are specifically rewarded for cooperative cover — communicating and covering for a beaten teammate — rather than individual defenders simply chasing the ball.</li><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided game; the coach or a nominated caller watches for good cover calls and awards bonus points when heard.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call it the moment your partner is beaten — don't wait.'</li><li>If a defender is beaten and no cover call happens, the coach can note it as a learning moment without stopping play.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep encouraging communication over individual heroics — the whole point is defending as a connected team.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'covering' for a teammate actually look like?</li><li>Why is chasing the ball as a whole defence usually a bad idea?</li><li>Who communicated really well defensively today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises when a teammate has been beaten and needs covering</li><li>Recovers into useful covering space rather than just chasing the ball</li><li>Calls out defensive information clearly to teammates</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify to a bigger space and slower pace while students learn to judge covering positions; use clear verbal cues from the teacher initially to reinforce the concept before expecting it independently.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Switch It Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rotating positions/roles fluidly as a team during play</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear calls before physical switches to avoid two players moving into the same space unexpectedly</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to switch positions or roles smoothly during a game, keeping our team balanced as we do.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise a good moment to switch roles or positions with a teammate</li><li>I can communicate a switch clearly so my team stays balanced</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Passing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly on the spot while passing a ball gently back and forth using any comfortable pass.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'switch!' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby and continue passing with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and getting comfortable passing with lots of different classmates before today's cooperative-play focus.</li><li>Encourage students to say their new partner's name each time they switch.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the pace and switching frequency.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Recognise a sensible moment to switch positions or roles with a teammate.</td><td>Switching at the right moment (not randomly) is what keeps the switch actually useful to the team.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate the switch clearly before physically moving.</td><td>A switch that isn't communicated can leave two players in the same space, or a gap where neither player is.</td></tr><tr><td>Settle into the new position quickly and keep the team shape balanced.</td><td>A switch is only successful if the team's overall shape stays solid during and after it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Position Swap Tag</h4><p>In small groups holding set positions in a grid, students practise calling and swapping positions cleanly with a teammate on a signal, without leaving gaps.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 5-6 holding set positions marked with cones in a grid roughly 10m x 8m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the swap before you move, not while you're already moving.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, teacher calls the swap.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, students decide when to swap themselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each student a starting position marked by a cone.</li><li>On a signal, two students call and swap positions cleanly with each other, without leaving a gap uncovered.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call the swap before you move, not while you're already moving.'</li><li>Continue calling swaps between different pairs of students throughout the activity.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with the teacher calling the swap; for 'harder', use a faster pace with students deciding when to swap themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rolling Rotation Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where players continuously rotate through two or three positions after set triggers (e.g. every successful pass), keeping the rotation smooth and communicated.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with 2-3 marked positions to rotate through. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know which position you're rotating into before it's your turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower rotation trigger.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable rotation trigger.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Double rotation on a bonus event</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rotation trigger: after every set event (e.g. every successful pass), players continuously rotate through two or three positions.</li><li>Play flows with the ball moving around; each trigger event causes a smooth, communicated rotation to the next position.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'know which position you're rotating into before it's your turn.'</li><li>Keep the rotation moving continuously without long stoppages.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower rotation trigger; for 'harder', use a faster, less predictable rotation trigger.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call &amp; Switch</h4><p>Partners practise switching roles mid-activity purely by calling out clearly to each other, with no other signal, building confidence in verbal-only switching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Partners in an open space roughly 8m x 8m, no ball needed — this is a role-switching communication drill.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A clear call is enough — trust it and move.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Only one type of switch practised.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Multiple switch types called in sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: partners switch roles mid-activity purely by calling out clearly to each other, with no other signal used.</li><li>Partners perform a simple paired activity (e.g. alternating jogging/jumping roles) and call out to switch roles whenever they choose.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a clear call is enough — trust it and move,' reinforcing that hesitation after a call slows the whole team down.</li><li>Practise several switches in a row so the calling becomes confident and automatic.</li><li>For 'easier', practise only one type of switch; for 'harder', chain multiple switch types together in sequence.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fluid Formation Game</h3><p>Small-sided game requiring students to rotate positions on a clear signal or situation (such as winning the ball back), keeping team shape balanced throughout the rotations.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 6-8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, with 2-3 marked positions to rotate through. One ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: students rotate positions on a clear signal or situation (such as winning the ball back), keeping team shape balanced throughout the rotations.</li><li>Play flows normally; whenever the rotation trigger happens, players call and swap positions cleanly, without leaving gaps.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call the swap before you move, not while you're already moving.'</li><li>The coach highlights moments where a smooth rotation kept the team's shape strong.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the tone light and encouraging — a slightly messy rotation is a normal part of learning this skill.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made a position switch go smoothly today?</li><li>What happened when a switch wasn't communicated clearly?</li><li>How did your team stay balanced while switching roles?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises a sensible moment to switch roles or positions</li><li>Communicates a switch clearly before moving</li><li>Keeps team shape balanced during and after a switch</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start with slower, teacher-cued switches for students who need more processing time; pair confident 'switchers' with those still building this skill so switches stay smooth for everyone.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Team Huddle":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reviewing &amp; adjusting a simple team plan mid-game (Block 2 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones — combined equipment from Block 2 games</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games; huddles kept brief and away from other games in progress</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use a short team huddle to review how a game is going and agree one clear adjustment.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can contribute one clear idea in a short team huddle</li><li>I can help my team agree on and commit to one adjustment</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Passing Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that passes don't collide with a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners jog slowly on the spot while passing a ball gently back and forth using any comfortable pass.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'switch!' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby and continue passing with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and getting comfortable passing with lots of different classmates before today's cooperative-play focus.</li><li>Encourage students to say their new partner's name each time they switch.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the pace and switching frequency.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Contribute one clear idea during a short team huddle.</td><td>A huddle works best with a few clear ideas rather than everyone talking over each other.</td></tr><tr><td>Help the team agree on and commit to a single adjustment.</td><td>Trying to change everything at once usually changes nothing — one clear adjustment is easier for a whole team to actually do.</td></tr><tr><td>Return to play and genuinely follow through on the agreed adjustment.</td><td>A huddle is only useful if the plan discussed actually shows up in how the team plays afterwards.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>60-Second Huddle Challenge</h4><p>Teams get exactly 60 seconds mid-game to huddle, review one thing that's working and one thing to change, and agree on it before restarting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 playing a short small-sided game in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with a clearly marked huddle spot just off to the side. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One clear idea beats five confusing ones.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers a prompt question to guide the huddle.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No prompts — team runs the huddle entirely themselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a short game for a set period, then call 'huddle!' at the midway point.</li><li>Teams have exactly 60 seconds to gather at their huddle spot, review one thing that's working and one thing to change, and agree on it.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'one clear idea beats five confusing ones.'</li><li>Restart the game immediately once the 60 seconds is up, with teams trying to apply what they just agreed.</li><li>For 'easier', offer a prompt question to guide the huddle; for 'harder', give no prompts and let the team run the huddle entirely themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Plan &amp; Adjust Game</h4><p>A small game is paused at the halfway point specifically for teams to hold a huddle and adjust something about how they're playing, before finishing the game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 playing a short small-sided game in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, paused once at the halfway point for a huddle. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust one thing, not everything at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher suggests possible adjustments to choose from.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team identifies its own adjustment with no suggestions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Start the game and let it run to the halfway point, then pause specifically for a team huddle.</li><li>During the pause, the team discusses and agrees on one adjustment to how they're playing, then resumes.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'adjust one thing, not everything at once.'</li><li>Finish the game with the team trying to apply their agreed adjustment.</li><li>For 'easier', suggest possible adjustments to choose from; for 'harder', have the team identify its own adjustment with no suggestions.</li></ul><hr><h4>Coach for a Minute</h4><p>One student per team takes a turn leading the huddle for a minute, practising giving clear, positive, useful input to their teammates.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 with a designated huddle spot, taking turns having one student lead the huddle for a minute. One ball per team, used before/after the huddle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Lead with something the team's doing well, then one change.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Rotate the 'coach' role often so it's low-pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Coach must justify their suggested adjustment to the team.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One student per team takes a turn as 'coach' for a minute, leading the huddle themselves.</li><li>The student-coach leads with something the team's doing well, then suggests one change, following the same structure as an adult coach would.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'lead with something the team's doing well, then one change.'</li><li>Rotate the coach role to a different student for the next huddle.</li><li>For 'easier', rotate the coach role often to keep it low-pressure; for 'harder', require the coach to justify their suggested adjustment to the team.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Adjust &amp; Win</h3><p>Round-robin mini-tournament where every match includes a compulsory halfway huddle; teams are encouraged to genuinely use it to adjust their play, wrapping up Block 2's cooperative play focus.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Round-robin mini-tournament in groups of 8, playing short matches in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: every match includes a compulsory halfway huddle, following the same structure practised in today's Skill Games.</li><li>Play each short match to the halfway point, pause for the huddle, then finish the match.</li><li>Teams are encouraged to genuinely use the huddle to adjust their play, not just go through the motions.</li><li>The coach highlights teams that clearly applied their huddle adjustment in the second half of a match.</li><li>Rotate through several short matches so every team plays multiple games.</li><li>This wraps up Block 2's cooperative play focus — coach the key message throughout: one clear idea, genuinely applied, beats five confusing ones.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made today's huddles useful rather than just noise?</li><li>What's one adjustment your team actually made and stuck to?</li><li>Looking back over this whole block, how has your team supported each other better?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes a clear, useful idea during a team huddle</li><li>Helps the team agree on and commit to one adjustment</li><li>Shows growth across Block 2 in supporting play, overloads, cover, and switching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Give quieter students a specific, low-pressure way to contribute (e.g. one sentence each) so huddles don't get dominated by the loudest voices; keep huddles short so they stay achievable for every team.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Team Problem-Solving & Tactics (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Read the Game":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading teammates' and opponents' movement to make shared decisions</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games; encourage looking up and around rather than only at the ball</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read where space is opening up and what our teammates are about to do, so we can decide together, early.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can spot space opening up before it's obvious to everyone</li><li>I can read a teammate's movement to predict what they're about to do</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shape &amp; Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'shuffle sideways' — groups adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's team problem-solving and tactics focus by getting students thinking about team shape before the lesson begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shuffling.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and focus needed for reading the game and problem-solving under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Glance up regularly to see the whole picture, not just the ball.</td><td>Reading the game is impossible if your eyes never leave the ball — the useful information is all around it.</td></tr><tr><td>Spot space opening up as early as possible.</td><td>Space usually only stays open briefly; noticing it early gives your team time to actually use it.</td></tr><tr><td>Read a teammate's body shape and movement as an early signal of intent.</td><td>Waiting for a verbal call is fine, but reading movement lets your team react even faster, together.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Eyes On</h4><p>In small groups, students practise glancing up regularly during simple ball activities to spot where space or a teammate's movement is developing, rather than watching only the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 in a grid roughly 8m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A quick glance up is worth more than a long stare down.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more time to look up.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, brief windows to look up.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the habit: glancing up regularly during a simple ball activity to spot where space or a teammate's movement is developing, rather than watching only the ball.</li><li>Students pass and move within the grid, deliberately practising quick glances up between touches.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a quick glance up is worth more than a long stare down.'</li><li>Periodically ask a student what they just saw when they looked up, to check the habit is genuinely forming.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace with more time to look up; for 'harder', use a faster pace with brief windows to look up.</li></ul><hr><h4>Space Spotter</h4><p>Students call out loud the moment they spot space opening up nearby during a small game, building the habit of noticing early rather than after the moment has passed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 4-6 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call it the second you see it, not once it's obvious.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher highlights likely space zones in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No hints — students must spot space independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for the earliest genuine spot</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: students call out loud the moment they spot space opening up nearby during the small game.</li><li>Play flows normally; whenever a student notices space developing, they call it out immediately, even if they're not the one about to use it.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'call it the second you see it, not once it's obvious.'</li><li>The coach or teammates can acknowledge particularly early or useful spots to reinforce the habit.</li><li>For 'easier', highlight likely space zones in advance; for 'harder', give no hints so students must spot space independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>React &amp; Read Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where students are specifically coached to react to a teammate's body shape or eye direction as an early signal, rather than waiting for the ball or a verbal call.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 4-6 in a grid roughly 10m x 10m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Body shape often tells you what's about to happen before any call does.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace to give more time to read cues.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full pace with subtler cues to read.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the focus: reacting to a teammate's body shape or eye direction as an early signal, rather than waiting for the ball or a verbal call.</li><li>Play a small-sided game with the coach specifically prompting students to watch teammates' shoulders and eyes for clues about what's coming next.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'body shape often tells you what's about to happen before any call does.'</li><li>Periodically pause briefly to point out a good example of someone reading a cue early.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace to give more time to read cues; for 'harder', use full pace with subtler cues to read.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; React Ball</h3><p>Small-sided invasion/possession game rewarding early decision-making — teams earn bonus points for visibly reading space or a teammate's movement and reacting before the moment becomes obvious.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided invasion/possession games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule before starting: teams earn bonus points for visibly reading space or a teammate's movement and reacting before the moment becomes obvious.</li><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided game; the coach or a nominated caller watches for early, well-read reactions and calls out the bonus point when spotted.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'a quick glance up is worth more than a long stare down,' and 'body shape often tells you what's about to happen before any call does.'</li><li>If the ball is turned over, possession switches to the other team and play continues immediately.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep encouraging players to look up and read cues early, rather than reacting only once things are obvious.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you 'read' the game a bit earlier today?</li><li>What cues did you notice from a teammate's movement or body shape?</li><li>Why does deciding early usually work better than deciding late?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Looks up regularly rather than only watching the ball</li><li>Spots space opening up before it's obvious</li><li>Reads a teammate's movement or body shape as an early signal</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the pace and simplify cues for students still building this awareness; use clear, exaggerated body-shape cues early on before expecting students to read subtler signals.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Pick a Plan":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Choosing a simple attacking tactic together before play</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones, simple 'plan cards' or verbal prompts (e.g. 'overload one side', 'quick start', 'find the target player')</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to agree on one simple attacking idea as a team before a game starts, and commit to it together.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can help my team agree on one simple attacking plan before we start</li><li>I can commit to the team's plan rather than doing my own thing</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shape &amp; Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'shuffle sideways' — groups adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's team problem-solving and tactics focus by getting students thinking about team shape before the lesson begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shuffling.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and focus needed for reading the game and problem-solving under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Help your team agree on one simple, clear attacking idea before starting.</td><td>A team with one shared idea moves with purpose; a team with no plan (or five different ones) tends to collide with itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit to the team's plan during play, even if your own instinct says something different.</td><td>A plan only works if everyone actually follows it — a great individual idea that ignores the team plan can undo it.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect honestly afterwards on how well the plan worked.</td><td>Noticing what worked (or didn't) about today's plan is what makes tomorrow's plan better.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Plan of the Day</h4><p>Before a short game, each team picks one simple attacking idea from a small set of options and briefly discusses how they'll try to use it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 gathered briefly before a short game, with a small set of simple plan options available (written on cards or given verbally). One ball per team, grid roughly 10m x 8m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick one plan and actually try it, rather than picking nothing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher assigns the plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team must justify why they chose their plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before the game, each team picks one simple attacking idea from the small set of options (e.g. 'overload one side', 'quick start', 'find the target player').</li><li>The team briefly discusses how they'll try to use their chosen plan during the upcoming short game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pick one plan and actually try it, rather than picking nothing.'</li><li>Play the short game with the team attempting to apply their chosen plan whenever the opportunity arises.</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher assign the plan; for 'harder', require the team to justify why they chose their plan.</li></ul><hr><h4>Target Player Game</h4><p>Teams practise a simple 'get the ball to the target player' plan, with one nominated player as the target that teammates try to consistently find and support.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with one nominated 'target player' per team. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the target early, not as a last resort.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Target player can move freely to get open.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Target player is shadowed by a defender.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation — target player changes each round</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the simple plan: teammates try to consistently find and support one nominated target player during the game.</li><li>Play flows with the team looking to move the ball toward their target player whenever a safe option exists.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look for the target early, not as a last resort.'</li><li>Rotate which student is the target player each round so everyone experiences the role.</li><li>For 'easier', let the target player move freely to get open; for 'harder', have the target player shadowed by a defender.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fast Start Challenge</h4><p>Teams practise a simple 'quick start' plan — moving the ball forward fast in the first few seconds of a restart — and reflect on how well they executed it as a team.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, practising restarts from a marked starting point. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide the plan before the restart, not during it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Extra time before the restart begins.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Restart begins with minimal warning.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the simple plan: move the ball forward fast in the first few seconds of a restart.</li><li>The team agrees on their fast-start plan before the restart, then executes it as quickly and cleanly as possible once play begins.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'decide the plan before the restart, not during it.'</li><li>After each restart, briefly reflect as a team on how well they executed the plan before resetting for another attempt.</li><li>For 'easier', give extra time before the restart begins; for 'harder', begin the restart with minimal warning.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Plan &amp; Play</h3><p>Series of short mini-matches where each team selects one simple attacking tactic before every game and is specifically reviewed afterwards on how well they committed to and executed it together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Series of short mini-matches in groups of 8, in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before every mini-match, each team selects one simple attacking tactic from today's Skill Games (target player, fast start, or their own plan).</li><li>Play the short mini-match with the team trying to commit to and execute their chosen tactic.</li><li>After each mini-match, the coach briefly reviews with each team how well they committed to and executed their plan together.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'pick one plan and actually try it, rather than picking nothing.'</li><li>Rotate opponents between mini-matches so teams get several short games in a row.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What plan did your team choose today, and why?</li><li>How well did your team actually commit to the plan?</li><li>What would you choose differently next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes to choosing a simple team attacking plan</li><li>Commits to the team's plan during play</li><li>Reflects honestly on how well the plan worked</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a small, clear set of plan options (rather than open-ended) for teams still building tactical thinking; allow shy students to vote rather than needing to propose ideas out loud.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Hold the Line":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Simple defensive shape &amp; shared responsibility as a team</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones to mark a simple defensive zone</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided defensive games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep a simple, connected defensive shape together as a team, rather than defending as separate individuals.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can help my team stay compact rather than too spread out defensively</li><li>I can communicate who I'm covering or marking to my teammates</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shape &amp; Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'shuffle sideways' — groups adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's team problem-solving and tactics focus by getting students thinking about team shape before the lesson begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shuffling.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and focus needed for reading the game and problem-solving under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stay compact and connected to your defensive teammates rather than chasing the ball alone.</td><td>A defence that stays connected closes down space as a unit; individuals chasing the ball leave gaps everywhere else.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate clearly who or what you're covering.</td><td>A defensive shape only holds together if everyone knows their job and their teammates' jobs too.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your position based on your teammates' positions, not just the ball's position.</td><td>Good defensive shape means reacting to the whole picture, since the ball alone doesn't tell you where the danger is.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shape Shuffle</h4><p>A small defending group practises shuffling sideways together as a connected line in response to the ball's movement, without any one player drifting far from the group.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small defending groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with an attacking group or coach moving a ball around to react to.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move as a line, not as individuals chasing separately.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower ball movement to track.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the connected-line idea: the defending group shuffles sideways together as a line in response to the ball's movement, without any one player drifting far from the group.</li><li>The ball (held by an attacker or the coach) moves side to side across the grid; the defending group shuffles to track it as a connected unit.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'move as a line, not as individuals chasing separately.'</li><li>Watch for any player drifting away from the line and gently guide them back into position.</li><li>For 'easier', use slower ball movement to track; for 'harder', use faster, less predictable ball movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Zone Hold</h4><p>Defenders are each given a simple zone to hold rather than a specific player to chase, practising staying compact and covering the zone as the ball moves around them.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small defending groups of 4 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, divided into individual marked zones using cones. One ball for an attacking side or coach to move.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Hold your zone — don't get pulled out of it chasing the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, more forgiving zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, tighter zones requiring more discipline.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for a zone genuinely held under pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Assign each defender a simple zone to hold, marked with cones, rather than a specific player to chase.</li><li>As the ball moves around the grid, defenders stay in their zone and cover it, rather than being pulled out of position chasing the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'hold your zone — don't get pulled out of it chasing the ball.'</li><li>If a defender does get pulled out, calmly guide them back to their zone once there's a pause in play.</li><li>For 'easier', use bigger, more forgiving zones; for 'harder', use smaller, tighter zones requiring more discipline.</li></ul><hr><h4>Connected Defence Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where the defending team is rewarded specifically for staying connected and compact as a shape, rather than for individual tackles or turnovers alone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided groups of 8 (4 attackers, 4 defenders) in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check the players either side of you, not just the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower-paced attacking team to defend against.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster-paced attacking team, tighter space.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation between defence and attack</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: the defending team is rewarded specifically for staying connected and compact as a shape, rather than for individual tackles or turnovers alone.</li><li>Play flows as a small-sided game; the coach or a nominated caller watches for good compact shape and awards points when spotted.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'check the players either side of you, not just the ball.'</li><li>Rotate which group of four attacks and which defends every few minutes.</li><li>For 'easier', defend against a slower-paced attacking team; for 'harder', defend against a faster-paced attacking team in tighter space.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Hold the Line</h3><p>Small-sided game where defending teams earn bonus points for maintaining a compact, connected defensive shape and clearly communicating coverage, on top of standard game outcomes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule before starting: defending teams earn bonus points for maintaining a compact, connected defensive shape and clearly communicating coverage, on top of standard game outcomes.</li><li>Play flows as a normal small-sided game; the coach or a nominated caller watches for compact shape and clear coverage calls.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move as a line, not as individuals chasing separately,' and 'check the players either side of you, not just the ball.'</li><li>Award the bonus points during play when the defending team demonstrates good connected shape.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time on both attack and defence.</li><li>Keep the tone encouraging — compact, connected defending is a genuinely difficult skill to build as a team.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did 'staying connected' as a defence actually feel like today?</li><li>What happened when someone drifted away from the group to chase the ball?</li><li>How did communicating coverage help your team's defensive shape?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stays compact rather than drifting far from the defensive group</li><li>Communicates who or what they're covering</li><li>Adjusts position based on teammates' positions, not just the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clear zone markers (cones/lines) for students still learning to judge defensive spacing independently; simplify to slower-paced attacking play while the shape concept is being learned.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Change of Plan":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Adapting tactics when the first plan isn't working (in-game problem solving)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to notice when our plan isn't working and adapt it together as a team, without blame.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise when our team's plan isn't working</li><li>I can help my team try something different, calmly and without blame</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shape &amp; Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'shuffle sideways' — groups adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's team problem-solving and tactics focus by getting students thinking about team shape before the lesson begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shuffling.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and focus needed for reading the game and problem-solving under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Notice honestly when your team's plan isn't working.</td><td>A team can't adapt to a problem it hasn't recognised — honest noticing is the first step.</td></tr><tr><td>Suggest or support a change calmly, without blaming a teammate.</td><td>Blame shuts down a team's ability to think clearly together; calm suggestions keep everyone focused on solving the problem.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit to the new approach quickly once the team agrees to it.</td><td>A good adaptation is wasted if the team doesn't actually follow through and try it properly.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Plan B Practice</h4><p>Teams start a short game with one plan, but the teacher signals partway through that it 'isn't working' and teams must quickly agree and switch to a different simple approach.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, starting a short game with an agreed initial plan. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Switch calmly — a change of plan isn't a failure.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher suggests the new plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team must come up with Plan B entirely themselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random signal timing</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Teams agree on one simple plan before starting a short game.</li><li>Partway through, the coach signals that the plan 'isn't working,' and the team must quickly agree on and switch to a different simple approach.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'switch calmly — a change of plan isn't a failure.'</li><li>Continue the game with the team trying to apply their new Plan B.</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher suggest the new plan; for 'harder', require the team to come up with Plan B entirely themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Adapt or Repeat</h4><p>After each short round, teams reflect honestly on whether their plan worked, and either adapt it slightly or repeat it deliberately, based on that honest reflection.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 playing several short rounds in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with a brief reflection pause between rounds. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Be honest about what actually happened, not what you hoped happened.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher guides the reflection with prompt questions.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team runs its own reflection with no prompts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a short round with the team's chosen plan, then pause briefly for honest reflection.</li><li>The team discusses honestly whether their plan worked, and either adapts it slightly or repeats it deliberately based on that reflection.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'be honest about what actually happened, not what you hoped happened.'</li><li>Play the next round applying the team's decision (adapted or repeated plan).</li><li>For 'easier', guide the reflection with prompt questions; for 'harder', let the team run its own reflection with no prompts.</li></ul><hr><h4>Signal to Switch</h4><p>Practising a simple, agreed team signal (word or gesture) that any player can use mid-game to suggest the team try something different, without needing a full stop in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, agreeing on one simple signal (word or gesture) before starting. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the signal quick and blame-free.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Only the designated 'captain' can give the signal.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Any player can give the signal at any time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before play, the team agrees on one quick, blame-free signal (a word or a gesture) that means 'let's try something different.'</li><li>Play a short game; any player can use the agreed signal mid-game to suggest the team try something different, without needing a full stop in play.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'keep the signal quick and blame-free.'</li><li>When the signal is given, the team adjusts smoothly and keeps playing rather than stopping to discuss at length.</li><li>For 'easier', only the designated 'captain' can give the signal; for 'harder', any player can give the signal at any time.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Adapt &amp; Overcome</h3><p>Small-sided games where the teacher deliberately changes a condition partway through (space, numbers, or rules), forcing teams to notice their plan isn't working and calmly adapt together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain before starting that the coach will deliberately change a condition partway through the game (the available space, the numbers on each side, or a rule).</li><li>Play flows normally until the coach makes the change, at which point teams must notice their plan isn't working under the new condition and calmly adapt together.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'switch calmly — a change of plan isn't a failure.'</li><li>Highlight teams that adapt smoothly and communicate well when the condition changes.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the changes fair and clearly announced so adapting feels like a shared team challenge, not a surprise trick.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did your team notice the plan wasn't working today?</li><li>What did a calm, blame-free change of plan sound like?</li><li>Why is adapting a plan a strength, not a sign of failure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises when a team plan isn't working</li><li>Suggests or supports an adaptation calmly, without blaming teammates</li><li>Helps the team commit to the new approach quickly</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Provide simple, ready-made 'Plan B' options for teams still building this flexibility; explicitly praise calm, blame-free adaptation when you see it to reinforce the norm.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Team Captain for a Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Shared leadership &amp; decision-making (Block 3 wrap-up)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones — combined equipment from Block 3 games</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make decisions that consider the whole team, and to communicate those decisions clearly and kindly.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make a decision that considers what the whole team needs, not just myself</li><li>I can accept it when the team's decision is different from my own idea</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Shape &amp; Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 5-6 spread out across the space, each group with a marked area roughly 8m x 8m. No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Groups jog together lightly within their area, staying reasonably close to each other as a unit.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'shuffle sideways' — groups adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This connects to today's team problem-solving and tactics focus by getting students thinking about team shape before the lesson begins.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage groups to communicate with each other while jogging and shuffling.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each group (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to breathe and balance without bumping neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the composure and focus needed for reading the game and problem-solving under pressure.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Make decisions that consider what the whole team needs.</td><td>Leadership in a team game means thinking beyond your own preference to what will actually help everyone.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate a decision clearly and kindly, even under time pressure.</td><td>A good decision is only useful if the team understands it — clarity and kindness both help it land well.</td></tr><tr><td>Accept it gracefully when the team's decision differs from your own idea.</td><td>Shared leadership means everyone gets heard, but not everyone's idea is chosen every time — accepting that is part of being a good teammate.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Captain's Call</h4><p>Each student takes a brief turn as 'captain', making one simple tactical call for their team (e.g. a plan choice or a positional adjustment) that the team then tries out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, taking brief turns as 'captain.' One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A good captain thinks about the whole team, not just their own game.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Captain chooses from a small set of suggested calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Captain must justify their call to the team.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation every 90 seconds</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each student takes a brief turn as captain, making one simple tactical call for their team (e.g. a plan choice or a positional adjustment).</li><li>The team then tries out the captain's call during a short period of play.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a good captain thinks about the whole team, not just their own game.'</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a new student every 90 seconds or so.</li><li>For 'easier', have the captain choose from a small set of suggested calls; for 'harder', require the captain to justify their call to the team.</li></ul><hr><h4>Leadership Carousel</h4><p>Students rotate through short leadership tasks — calling a huddle, suggesting a plan, organising a positional switch — practising different aspects of shared leadership.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 rotating through 3 short leadership tasks (calling a huddle, suggesting a plan, organising a positional switch), each lasting a minute or two.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Leadership can be quiet and clear — it doesn't have to be loud.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tasks kept simple and short.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tasks combined or extended for confident leaders.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three tasks students will rotate through: calling a huddle, suggesting a plan, and organising a positional switch.</li><li>Each student takes a turn leading one task before rotating to the next.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'leadership can be quiet and clear — it doesn't have to be loud.'</li><li>Rotate through the tasks until every student has led at least one.</li><li>For 'easier', keep tasks simple and short; for 'harder', combine or extend tasks for confident leaders.</li></ul><hr><h4>Decide Together</h4><p>Small teams are given a simple scenario and a short time limit to decide on one team response together, practising quick, inclusive decision-making under time pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4-5 given a simple scenario card or verbal prompt, with a short time limit to decide as a group. No ball needed for the decision-making itself.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Everyone gets heard, then the team decides and moves on.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time limit, fewer scenario options.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time limit, more complex scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Give each team a simple scenario (e.g. 'you're down by one goal with two minutes left — what's your plan?') and a short time limit.</li><li>The team discusses and decides on one team response together within the time limit, making sure everyone gets heard.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'everyone gets heard, then the team decides and moves on.'</li><li>Once decided, teams can briefly explain their decision before moving to the next scenario.</li><li>For 'easier', give a longer time limit with fewer scenario options; for 'harder', use a shorter time limit with more complex scenarios.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Captain's Cup</h3><p>Small tournament where a rotating student 'captain' on each team makes the key tactical calls each game, wrapping up Block 3's focus on shared decision-making, reading the game, and adapting tactics.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small tournament in groups of 8, playing short matches in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, with a rotating student captain per team. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before starting: a rotating student captain on each team makes the key tactical calls each game.</li><li>Play short matches with each team's captain making calls, drawing on today's leadership and decision-making practice.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'a good captain thinks about the whole team, not just their own game.'</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a new student for each new match so everyone experiences leading.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>This wraps up Block 3's focus on shared decision-making, reading the game, and adapting tactics — keep the tone encouraging for every captain's calls, confident or hesitant.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did good, shared leadership look like today?</li><li>How did it feel to make a call that the whole team then followed?</li><li>Looking back over this whole block, how has your team's decision-making improved?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Makes decisions that consider the whole team, not just themselves</li><li>Communicates decisions clearly and kindly</li><li>Shows growth across Block 3 in reading the game, planning, defensive shape, and adapting</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Give every student a genuine turn at a leadership task, including quieter students, with support structures (prompts, smaller decisions) so leadership feels achievable for everyone.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Challenges & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Mixed-Up Teams":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Working effectively with new or unfamiliar teammates</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C · Participating and contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs (multiple colours for re-mixing), balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games; ensure re-mixed teams stay balanced for fair, safe competition</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to quickly build a working team with people we haven't played with before.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can introduce myself and share what I'm good at with new teammates</li><li>I can adapt my role quickly to fit into a new team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the final block's culminating games.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Introduce yourself and quickly find out a new teammate's strengths.</td><td>A team works faster together once everyone knows roughly what everyone else brings to it.</td></tr><tr><td>Adapt your role to fit what a new team actually needs.</td><td>The role you play in a new group might be different from your usual one — flexibility helps the team come together fast.</td></tr><tr><td>Use the same communication and support skills you'd use with any team.</td><td>The teamwork skills built all unit — clear calls, space, roles, trust — work with any group of teammates, not just familiar ones.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Quick Team Quiz</h4><p>Newly mixed teams have 60 seconds to find out one strength from every teammate before their first game together.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Newly mixed teams of 4-5 gathered briefly before their first game together, no equipment needed for the quiz itself.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Ask, don't assume — you might be surprised what a new teammate is good at.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher provides simple prompt questions.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Teams design their own quick questions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>As soon as teams are mixed, give them exactly 60 seconds to find out one strength from every teammate.</li><li>Students go around the group quickly asking each other what they're good at or enjoy, listening for genuine answers.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'ask, don't assume — you might be surprised what a new teammate is good at.'</li><li>Once the 60 seconds is up, move straight into the team's first game together.</li><li>For 'easier', provide simple prompt questions; for 'harder', have teams design their own quick questions.</li></ul><hr><h4>New Faces Rondo</h4><p>Small possession games played entirely in freshly mixed groups, focusing on quickly re-establishing calls, spacing, and support with new teammates.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Freshly mixed small groups of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use the same clear calls you'd use with any team.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace to allow adjustment time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full pace, less settling-in time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a small possession game entirely in the newly mixed groups.</li><li>Focus specifically on quickly re-establishing calls, spacing, and support with these new teammates, using the same habits built earlier in the unit.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use the same clear calls you'd use with any team.'</li><li>Keep the game light and low-pressure — the goal is settling in, not competing hard yet.</li><li>For 'easier', use a slower pace to allow adjustment time; for 'harder', use full pace with less settling-in time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Fast Bonding Challenge</h4><p>Newly mixed teams complete a short cooperative challenge (not necessarily ball-based) that requires quick communication and trust before their first proper game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Newly mixed teams of 4-5 given a short cooperative challenge (not necessarily ball-based), with clear space to complete it. Simple equipment as needed for the chosen challenge.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A quick, genuine connection now pays off once the game starts.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler challenge, more time allowed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More complex challenge, tighter time limit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the short cooperative challenge to the newly mixed team, making clear it requires quick communication and trust.</li><li>The team works together to complete the challenge before their first proper game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a quick, genuine connection now pays off once the game starts.'</li><li>Move straight into the team's first game once the challenge is complete.</li><li>For 'easier', use a simpler challenge with more time allowed; for 'harder', use a more complex challenge with a tighter time limit.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Shuffle Cup</h3><p>Mini-tournament where teams are re-mixed between rounds, requiring students to quickly rebuild working communication, roles, and trust with new teammates each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mini-tournament in groups of 8, with teams re-mixed between rounds. Grid roughly 12m x 10m per game, one ball per game, multiple bib colours for re-mixing.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format before starting: teams are re-mixed between rounds, requiring students to quickly rebuild working communication, roles, and trust with new teammates each time.</li><li>Play a short round with the current team mix, then re-mix teams using new bib combinations before the next round.</li><li>Encourage each newly mixed team to briefly apply the Quick Team Quiz or Fast Bonding Challenge approach before their round starts.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'ask, don't assume,' and 'a quick, genuine connection now pays off once the game starts.'</li><li>Continue through several rounds of re-mixed teams.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes within each round so everyone gets even game time.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you settle into a new team quickly today?</li><li>What did you learn about a teammate you hadn't played with before?</li><li>Why is being able to work with anyone a genuinely useful skill?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Introduces themselves and shares strengths with new teammates</li><li>Adapts their role quickly to fit a new team's needs</li><li>Uses the same clear communication skills regardless of who they're playing with</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Mix teams thoughtfully so no student is repeatedly the only unfamiliar face in a group; give a little extra settling-in time for students who find new social groupings harder.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Under Pressure":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team composure &amp; communication under competitive pressure</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and encouraging with teammates whose skills or confidence look different from yours, and treating everyone with kindness whether the game is going well or not.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones, simple scoreboard or points system</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for competitive small-sided games; monitor tone to keep pressure fun and healthy rather than harsh</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to keep communicating calmly and support each other even when a game gets competitive or tense.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can keep talking to my team even when the pressure is on</li><li>I can use a calm reset after a mistake, especially under pressure</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the final block's culminating games.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep communicating clearly even as a game becomes more competitive.</td><td>Communication is often the first thing that breaks down under pressure — but it's exactly when a team needs it most.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a calm, agreed reset phrase immediately after a mistake.</td><td>A quick, calm reset stops one mistake from turning into several by keeping the team's focus on what's next.</td></tr><tr><td>Actively support a teammate who's visibly struggling under pressure.</td><td>Pressure affects teammates differently — noticing and supporting a struggling teammate keeps the whole team performing, not just individuals.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Pressure Cooker Game</h4><p>Short, closely scored small-sided games with a visible scoreboard, specifically coaching students to keep communicating clearly even as the pressure builds.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with a visible scoreboard or points tally. One ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk more, not less, when it gets tight.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Lower-stakes scoring, more relaxed pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tight scoring margins, faster pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock — sudden-death finish</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play short, closely scored small-sided games with a visible scoreboard, keeping the margin close where possible.</li><li>Specifically coach students to keep communicating clearly even as the pressure builds toward the end of the game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'talk more, not less, when it gets tight.'</li><li>Keep score visible and announce it periodically to maintain the pressure.</li><li>For 'easier', use lower-stakes scoring at a more relaxed pace; for 'harder', use tight scoring margins at a faster pace.</li><li>For 'chaos', add a sudden-death finish once the game is close.</li></ul><hr><h4>Calm Call Challenge</h4><p>Teams agree on one simple 'reset phrase' to use immediately after any mistake during a competitive small game, practising calm recovery under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 agreeing on one simple 'reset phrase' before starting a short competitive game. Grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Say the reset phrase, then move on — no dwelling on it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher suggests example reset phrases.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team creates and commits to their own phrase.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting, each team agrees on one simple 'reset phrase' to use immediately after any mistake during the game.</li><li>Play a short competitive small game; whenever a mistake happens, the team says their reset phrase and moves straight on.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'say the reset phrase, then move on — no dwelling on it.'</li><li>Keep an eye out for teams that use the phrase genuinely and calmly rather than sarcastically.</li><li>For 'easier', suggest example reset phrases; for 'harder', have the team create and commit to their own phrase.</li></ul><hr><h4>Comeback Game</h4><p>Teams start a short game already behind on an artificial scoring handicap and must communicate and support each other to work back into it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 starting a short game with an artificial scoring handicap already in place (e.g. starting 2 points behind). Grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A deficit is a challenge to solve together, not a reason to go quiet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller starting deficit.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger starting deficit, shorter time to close it.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random deficit size</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the setup: the team starts the short game already behind on an artificial scoring handicap.</li><li>The team must communicate and support each other to work back into the game from behind.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'a deficit is a challenge to solve together, not a reason to go quiet.'</li><li>Track the score throughout so the team can see their progress closing the gap.</li><li>For 'easier', use a smaller starting deficit; for 'harder', use a bigger starting deficit with a shorter time to close it.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: High Stakes Ball</h3><p>Competitive small-sided games with a visible scoreboard and genuine stakes, specifically coached and reviewed on how well teams maintained communication and composure under pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for competitive small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, with a visible scoreboard and genuine stakes. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play competitive small-sided games with real, visible scoring — these are meant to feel like genuine competition, not just practice.</li><li>Specifically coach and review how well teams maintained communication and composure under pressure throughout.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'talk more, not less, when it gets tight,' and 'say the reset phrase, then move on.'</li><li>After each game, briefly ask teams to reflect on how well they kept communicating when things got tight.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the tone genuinely competitive but positive — pressure should feel exciting, not harsh.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What changed in how your team talked to each other as the pressure went up?</li><li>How did your reset phrase help after a mistake today?</li><li>Who stayed calm and supportive under pressure today — what did you notice?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps communicating clearly as competitive pressure increases</li><li>Uses a calm reset after a mistake rather than dwelling on it</li><li>Supports teammates who are struggling under pressure</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Keep genuine stakes light and fun rather than high-stress for students who find competition anxiety-inducing; check in with any student who seems overwhelmed and adjust the pressure level for them.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Everyone Plays a Part":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Inclusive team play — valuing every teammate's contribution</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand C · Participating and contributing</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own strengths and how you fit into the team, and noticing what your teammates need from you in order to do their best.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing for small-sided games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make sure every teammate gets genuinely involved, and to value contributions beyond just scoring.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can help make sure every teammate gets a genuine touch or involvement in the game</li><li>I can recognise and value a teammate's contribution, even if it isn't a score</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the final block's culminating games.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Actively look for and include the teammate who hasn't been involved yet.</td><td>Inclusion doesn't happen automatically in a fast game — it takes a deliberate look for who's been left out.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice and value contributions that aren't scores — a good call, a smart cover, a great pass.</td><td>A team that only values scoring misses most of what actually makes it work well together.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to whatever role you're given, not just your favourite one.</td><td>A team needs every role filled properly — a team member who only tries hard in their preferred role weakens the whole team.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Everyone Touches Game</h4><p>Small-sided game where a team can only score after the ball has been touched by every teammate at least once in that possession, encouraging genuine inclusion.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the teammate who hasn't touched it yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players per team, more time per possession.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players per team, tighter time limits.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus point for the quietest player's contribution</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: a team can only score after the ball has been touched by every teammate at least once in that possession.</li><li>Play flows with teams working the ball around, deliberately looking for teammates who haven't touched it yet before attempting to score.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look for the teammate who hasn't touched it yet.'</li><li>If possession is lost before everyone has touched the ball, simply restart the count once the team regains possession.</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players per team with more time per possession; for 'harder', use more players per team with tighter time limits.</li></ul><hr><h4>Value Spotter</h4><p>After a short game, students identify one teammate's valuable contribution that wasn't a score — a good call, a smart cover, a great pass, strong encouragement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 gathered briefly after a short game, no equipment needed for the discussion itself.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look beyond the scoreboard for what actually helped the team.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher offers example contribution types to look for.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students identify contribution types independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a short small-sided game as normal.</li><li>Afterwards, each student identifies one teammate's valuable contribution that wasn't a score — a good call, a smart cover, a great pass, strong encouragement.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'look beyond the scoreboard for what actually helped the team.'</li><li>Go around the group so everyone both gives and receives a specific piece of recognition.</li><li>For 'easier', offer example contribution types to look for; for 'harder', have students identify contribution types independently.</li></ul><hr><h4>Inclusive Rotation Game</h4><p>Small-sided game with a rotation rule ensuring every player takes a turn in a key role (e.g. feeder, finisher, defender) regardless of confidence level.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, with a rotation rule ensuring every player takes a turn in a key role. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Every role matters — commit fully to whichever one is yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simple, predictable rotation order.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rotation triggered by unpredictable game events.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rotation rule: every player must take a turn in a key role (e.g. feeder, finisher, defender) regardless of confidence level.</li><li>Play flows with roles rotating on a set trigger or time interval, making sure no student is stuck in the same role the whole game.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'every role matters — commit fully to whichever one is yours.'</li><li>Keep track (informally) that every student has had a turn in each key role by the end of the game.</li><li>For 'easier', use a simple, predictable rotation order; for 'harder', trigger rotation by unpredictable game events.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: All In</h3><p>Small-sided games played under an 'everyone touches before scoring' rule, with the teacher specifically highlighting and celebrating non-scoring contributions from every player.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split into groups of 8 for small-sided games in a grid roughly 12m x 10m, playing under the 'everyone touches before scoring' rule. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule before starting: the ball must be touched by every teammate at least once in a possession before the team can score, exactly as practised in Everyone Touches Game.</li><li>Play flows with teams deliberately including every player before attempting to score.</li><li>The teacher specifically highlights and celebrates non-scoring contributions from every player throughout the game — a good call, a smart cover, a strong pass.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'look beyond the scoreboard for what actually helped the team.'</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Keep the tone warm and inclusive — every player's touch and contribution genuinely matters to whether the team can score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>Who made a valuable contribution today that wasn't a score — what did they do?</li><li>How did it feel to make sure everyone got genuinely involved?</li><li>Why does a team work better when everyone plays a real part?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Actively includes every teammate in play, not just confident players</li><li>Recognises and values non-scoring contributions</li><li>Commits fully to whichever role they're given, not just their favourite</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Ensure the 'everyone touches' rule is genuinely followed rather than symbolically — check in with quieter or less confident students directly to make sure their involvement feels real, not token.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Practice Team Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full run-throughs bringing together the whole unit's team-play skills</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after your teammates' success as much as your own — including, encouraging, and setting others up to succeed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across simultaneous games; agreed, fair self-officiating routines to prevent disputes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bring communication, support, tactics, and inclusion together into one connected game, as a practice run before our culmination day.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply a range of team-play skills together in one game</li><li>I can give and receive feedback constructively with my team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the final block's culminating games.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring together communication, support, tactics, and inclusion into one connected game.</td><td>A real team game asks for all of these skills together, often in quick succession — this is the whole unit coming together.</td></tr><tr><td>Give feedback to teammates constructively, and accept it the same way.</td><td>Honest, kind feedback is what helps a team keep improving right up to the culminating day.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay calm and reset after a mistake, rather than carrying frustration into the next play.</td><td>A short memory for mistakes is what lets a team play its best teamwork point after point.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Warm-Up Team Points</h4><p>A short warm-up game where students settle into rhythm and remind themselves of key teamwork points from across the unit before full games begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 in a grid roughly 10m x 8m, playing a short low-stakes warm-up game before the day's main practice matches. One ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use this to find your rhythm as a team, not to win.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer warm-up time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter warm-up, straight into full games.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play a short warm-up game where the focus is settling into rhythm as a team, not winning.</li><li>Use this time for students to remind themselves and each other of key teamwork points from across the whole unit — calls, spacing, roles, trust, support.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'use this to find your rhythm as a team, not to win.'</li><li>Keep the pace relaxed and encouraging as a lead-in to the day's practice matches.</li><li>For 'easier', allow longer warm-up time; for 'harder', use a shorter warm-up, moving straight into full games.</li></ul><hr><h4>Skill Focus Choice</h4><p>Each student picks one team-play skill from the unit (e.g. clear calls, covering a teammate, reading the game) to focus on today, and gets brief feedback on it during play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Individual students each choose one team-play skill from the unit to focus on today, within their team's practice game. Grid roughly 10m x 8m, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick something you genuinely want to improve, not just something easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher/peer suggests a focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Student justifies their own choice of focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each student picks one team-play skill from across the unit (e.g. clear calls, covering a teammate, reading the game) to focus on today.</li><li>During play, the student deliberately looks for opportunities to apply their chosen focus skill.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'pick something you genuinely want to improve, not just something easy.'</li><li>The teacher gives brief, specific feedback to each student on their chosen focus area during or after play.</li><li>For 'easier', have the teacher or a peer suggest a focus; for 'harder', have the student justify their own choice of focus.</li></ul><hr><h4>Practice Match Sets</h4><p>Short practice games played with full rules and scoring, as a run-through before the culminating tournament.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 or larger playing short practice games with full rules and scoring, in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Treat this like the real thing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter games, more generous rules.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-length games, standard rules.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Play short practice games using full rules and real scoring, as a genuine run-through before tomorrow's culminating tournament.</li><li>Encourage teams to treat this like a real competitive match, applying everything practised across the unit.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'treat this like the real thing.'</li><li>Rotate opponents between practice matches so teams experience playing different groups.</li><li>For 'easier', use shorter games with more generous rules; for 'harder', use full-length games with standard rules.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Team Trial Games</h3><p>Students play complete small-sided matches, self-officiated, drawing on communication, support play, tactics, cover, and inclusion from across the whole unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Complete small-sided matches, self-officiated, in groups of 8 in a grid roughly 12m x 10m. One ball per game, bibs for each team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain before starting that these are complete matches, self-officiated by the students themselves — this is a genuine trial run for tomorrow's tournament.</li><li>Play flows as a full small-sided match, drawing on communication, support play, tactics, cover, and inclusion from across the whole unit.</li><li>Encourage students to resolve any small disagreements themselves, fairly and calmly, as part of the self-officiating challenge.</li><li>The coach circulates and observes rather than directly running the game, stepping in only if genuinely needed.</li><li>Rotate players on and off court every few minutes so everyone gets even game time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: bring together everything from across the unit — this is the final tune-up before Team Play Games Day.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What part of your teamwork are you most confident in going into tomorrow's games day?</li><li>Who showed great communication and fair play today?</li><li>What's one team-play skill you'd still like to work on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a range of team-play skills together in one game</li><li>Gives and receives feedback constructively</li><li>Shows resilience and good communication after mistakes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Match teams thoughtfully so games are competitive and enjoyable for everyone; offer flexible rules/space adjustments so every game remains genuinely playable and inclusive.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Team Play Games Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating multi-game tournament celebrating teamwork across the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how your role today connects to the bigger team effort — a small contribution that matters once it's put together with everyone else's.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bibs, balls, cones, simple draw sheet or rotation chart</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across multiple simultaneous games; clear rotation system so students know where to go next</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's team-play skills in a games day setting, showing fair play and reflecting on our growth.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply a range of team-play skills from across the unit in competitive and cooperative games</li><li>I can play fairly and encourage teammates and opponents throughout the games day</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a triangle' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the final block's culminating games.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills from across the whole unit — communication, space, roles, trust, support, tactics, and inclusion.</td><td>The games day brings everything the unit has built together under real, enjoyable pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates and opponents throughout.</td><td>Fair play and encouragement are as much a part of this unit as the teamwork skills themselves.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on your own improvement as a teammate is as valuable as the result of any one game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skill Circuit Warm-Up</h4><p>A quick circuit combining calls, spacing, roles, and support play — a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>4 short stations set up around the space (calls, spacing, roles, support play), each with any needed equipment (balls, cones), groups of 4-5 rotating through together.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth transitions between stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain each station briefly before starting: calls, spacing, roles, and support play — a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before games begin.</li><li>Groups spend a short, timed period at each station (e.g. 1-2 minutes) before rotating to the next on a signal.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'smooth transitions between stations' — keep the changeovers quick and organised.</li><li>Keep the mood light and energetic — this is a warm-in, not a formal assessment.</li><li>For 'easier', use longer time per station; for 'harder', use shorter time with quicker rotation.</li><li>For 'chaos', add a head-to-head mirror element or race the clock between stations.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Encouragement Challenge</h4><p>Before games begin, each student nominates one specific thing they'll do to encourage a teammate or opponent during play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class or each team gathered briefly before games begin, no equipment needed for this activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Make it specific and genuine, not generic.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before games begin, each student nominates one specific thing they'll do to encourage a teammate or opponent during play.</li><li>Students say their nomination out loud to their team or a partner so it's a genuine, spoken commitment.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'make it specific and genuine, not generic.'</li><li>Move straight into the day's games once everyone has nominated their encouragement action.</li><li>Keep this brief so it doesn't eat into playing time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Focus Pick</h4><p>Each team picks one team-play skill from the unit they most want to show off today and keeps it in mind as their focus during games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each team gathered briefly before games begin, no equipment needed for this activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Choose something meaningful to your team, not just easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Each team picks one team-play skill from the unit they most want to show off today (e.g. clear calls, good cover, great spacing).</li><li>The team keeps this chosen focus in mind throughout their games today.</li><li>Coach the key message: 'choose something meaningful to your team, not just easy.'</li><li>Check in briefly with teams between games about how their focus skill is going.</li><li>Keep this brief so it doesn't eat into playing time.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Team Games Day</h3><p>Students rotate through short team games across multiple areas, using simple scoring or a fair-play points system. Every skill from the unit — communication, space, roles, trust, support, tactics, and inclusion — is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Whole class split into teams of 4-6 for a round-robin culminating tournament across the full or near-full space, using bibs of multiple colours and a simple draw sheet or rotation chart visible to everyone.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: every team plays several short games across multiple areas, with a simple scoring or fair-play points system tracked lightly.</li><li>Run each short game with students applying whichever skills from across the unit are relevant — communication, space, roles, trust, support, tactics, and inclusion are all fair game.</li><li>Use a clear rotation system (the draw sheet or rotation chart) so students always know where to go next between games.</li><li>Between games, briefly check in with teams about their chosen Focus Pick skill and their Team Encouragement Challenge commitment.</li><li>Guarantee every team gets to play multiple games, adjusting the schedule on the fly if a team finishes early.</li><li>Keep the tone fun, inclusive, and low-pressure regardless of results — this is a celebration of the whole unit's teamwork skills, not just a competition.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one team-play skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging teamwork look like in today's games day?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other team games or sports?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of team-play skills from across the unit, not just one</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure the games day with balanced/mixed-ability groupings; consider a simple fair-play recognition alongside game results to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Tee Ball":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Batting & Fundamental Skills (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Tee Time":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting stance &amp; swing off the tee</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B (Movement Concepts &amp; Motor Skills) · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while your stance and swing feel new and awkward, and encouraging teammates whose first hits look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 junior bat and soft/modified teeball per pair, batting tees, cones for spacing</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Batters spread well apart with a clear swinging radius; no one stands in front of or beside a batter at the tee; wait for a clear 'go' signal before hitting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to hold a balanced batting stance and swing level and controlled at a ball on a tee.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand side-on in a balanced stance with hands together on the bat</li><li>I can swing level through the ball rather than chopping down or scooping up</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Toss &amp; Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one soft/modified teeball per pair, enough room between pairs that tosses don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently tossing and catching the ball underarm with two hands.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'quick hands' means slightly faster tosses, 'soft hands' means slow right back to gentle, easy catches.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue tossing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up catching hands, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm reach for the throwing arm — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stand side-on with hands together in a balanced grip.</td><td>Side-on positioning and a joined grip give control and a consistent swing path.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your front shoulder pointing at the tee before swinging.</td><td>This alignment sets up a level, accurate swing.</td></tr><tr><td>Swing level through the ball rather than chopping down or scooping up.</td><td>A level swing gives the most consistent contact.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Grip &amp; Stance Check</h4><p>Partners take turns checking each other's grip (hands together, 'V' shapes lined up) and side-on stance without hitting yet, giving friendly feedback.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with 2m gaps, no bat or ball needed yet. Each pair has one printed grip/stance picture card (or the coach's demonstration to refer back to) placed on the ground beside them.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Hands together, front shoulder pointing toward where the ball sits on the tee.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Use a visual reference card for grip/stance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Partner corrects without a reference.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the grip (hands together, knuckle 'V' shapes lined up) and the side-on stance in front of the whole class first.</li><li>Partner A sets up their grip and stance while Partner B checks it against the reference card, without any bat swinging yet.</li><li>Partner B gives one piece of friendly feedback using the coaching cue: hands together, front shoulder pointing at an imaginary tee.</li><li>Swap roles so Partner B sets up and Partner A checks.</li><li>Rotate to a new partner every 2 minutes so students see a few different stances.</li><li>Easier: keep the reference card in hand the whole time. Harder: card is put away after the first check, so the partner corrects from memory.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Swing Freeze</h4><p>Students shadow-swing without a ball, freezing in a balanced position at the end of the swing on a call.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually with at least a full arm-span plus bat-length of space each, in rows facing the coach. No ball or tee used — bats only, or an imaginary bat if bats are limited.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Swing level through an imaginary line, not down and up.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower swings, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Freeze called unpredictably.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a level shadow-swing (no ball) and show what a 'balanced freeze' looks like at the end of it.</li><li>Students shadow-swing slowly on their own, focusing on a level path rather than chopping down or scooping up.</li><li>On the coach's call of 'Freeze!', everyone stops mid-motion or at the end of the swing and holds the position for 3 seconds.</li><li>Coach quickly scans the group and calls out 2-3 examples of good balance without singling anyone out negatively.</li><li>Repeat 6-8 times, gradually increasing the swing speed.</li><li>Easier: give students extra time before each freeze call. Harder: call 'Freeze!' at unpredictable points, including mid-swing.</li></ul><hr><h4>Tee Contact Count</h4><p>Small groups take turns hitting off the tee, counting only hits where the stance and swing technique were correct beforehand.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 at each batting tee station, spaced at least 5m apart. Each station needs 1 tee, 1 junior bat, and 4-5 soft/modified teeballs so the group doesn't stop to collect balls after every hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your stance fully before every single swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, softer ball, closer tee height check.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Standard ball, coach spot-checks technique each hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind the group of the stance and swing checkpoints from the first two activities before anyone hits.</li><li>One student bats while teammates stand well back outside the swinging radius and act as checkers.</li><li>Before each swing, the group calls out whether the stance looked correct — only hits with a correct starting stance and level swing count toward the group's tally.</li><li>After each swing, quickly reset the ball on the tee and the batter resets their stance fully before swinging again — no rushing between hits.</li><li>Rotate the batter every 4-5 swings so everyone gets several turns.</li><li>Easier: use a bigger, softer ball and lower the tee for an easier contact height. Harder: use the standard ball and have the coach spot-check technique on every single hit.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Tee Ball Technique Challenge</h3><p>Small groups rotate through batting-tee stations where a hit only counts toward the team total if the stance and swing were correct beforehand — technique earns the point, not just contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3-4 batting-tee stations around the space, each 5-6m apart with its own tee, junior bat, and 4-5 balls. Split the class into small groups of 3-4 that rotate through every station.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: a hit only earns the team a point if the stance and swing were correct before contact, not just if the ball was hit.</li><li>One group member bats at a time while the rest of the group act as technique-checkers, calling 'good technique' or 'reset' before each swing.</li><li>The batter takes 4-5 swings, resetting their stance fully between each one; the group keeps a running tally of technique-approved hits.</li><li>Rotate to the next station on the coach's signal (roughly every 3 minutes) so every group visits every station.</li><li>Coach circulates and reinforces the key cue: hands together, side-on, swing level through the ball.</li><li>Add up each group's technique-approved hit tally at the end for a friendly team total.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a balanced, side-on stance feel like today?</li><li>What's the difference between a level swing and a chop or a scoop?</li><li>What was tricky about the new grip and stance?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Hands together in a balanced grip</li><li>Side-on stance with front shoulder toward the tee</li><li>Swings level through the ball rather than chopping or scooping</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a wider stance and a lower/closer tee height for students building coordination and confidence; success is measured by technique, not power.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Line Drive":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting — contact &amp; direction</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own contact point and what changes when the ball goes where you intended rather than by accident.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Junior bats, soft/modified teeballs, batting tees, cones marking target zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Same batting-radius spacing rules as Lesson 1; only one batter hits at a time per tee station</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to make clean contact with the ball and direct our hit toward a target area.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can make contact with the middle of the bat, not the handle or the end</li><li>I can aim my hit toward a chosen target area by adjusting where I make contact</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Toss &amp; Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one soft/modified teeball per pair, enough room between pairs that tosses don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently tossing and catching the ball underarm with two hands.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'quick hands' means slightly faster tosses, 'soft hands' means slow right back to gentle, easy catches.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue tossing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up catching hands, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm reach for the throwing arm — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball onto the bat through contact.</td><td>Watching the contact point, not the target, is what actually connects a clean hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim to contact the middle of the bat, not the handle or end.</td><td>The middle of the bat gives the most control and power.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your contact point slightly to influence the ball's direction.</td><td>Small technical adjustments control direction better than changing your whole swing.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Sweet Spot Search</h4><p>Batters experiment with contact point on the bat, noticing how a hit off the middle of the bat feels and travels differently to a hit off the handle or end.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Batters work individually at their own tee station (or share one tee between 2 with soft foam balls if tees are limited), spaced at least 5m apart. Each station has 1 tee, 1 bat, and 4-5 balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball onto the bat, don't guess where it is.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger bat/target zone on the bat.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Call out where contact was made immediately after hitting.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Show students the middle of the bat ('the sweet spot') versus hitting near the handle or the end.</li><li>Batter hits 3 balls aiming to feel the difference, calling out after each swing whether contact felt solid (middle) or 'thin' (handle/end).</li><li>Encourage batters to watch the ball onto the bat rather than guessing where contact happened.</li><li>Reset the ball on the tee after every swing and pause briefly before the next one so each swing is a fresh, focused attempt.</li><li>Rotate turns every 3-4 swings if sharing a station.</li><li>Easier: use a bigger bat or mark a larger target zone on the bat face. Harder: batter must call out where contact was made immediately after hitting, before anyone else says it.</li></ul><hr><h4>Left, Right, Straight</h4><p>Batters aim their hit toward one of three marked zones (left, straight, right) called before each swing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each tee station has 3 cone-marked target zones set out roughly 8-10m in front of the tee: one to the left, one straight ahead, one to the right, each about 3-4m wide.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust your contact point slightly, not your whole swing, to change direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Wider zones, fewer choices.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Narrower zones, quick-fire calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Show the group the three target zones and explain that a slight change in contact point (not the whole swing) sends the ball a different direction.</li><li>A partner calls one zone ('Left!', 'Straight!', or 'Right!') just before the batter sets up for each swing.</li><li>Batter aims their hit toward the called zone, adjusting only their contact point on the ball.</li><li>After each swing, reset the ball on the tee and call the next zone before the batter is fully set, so they commit to a target rather than reacting to the ball.</li><li>Rotate the batter/caller roles every 4-5 swings.</li><li>Easier: use wider zones and only two choices (left/right). Harder: narrow the zones and call them quick-fire with less setup time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Direction Streak</h4><p>Small groups tally how many hits in a row land in the called target zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 share one tee station with the same 3 target zones marked from the previous activity. Keep 5-6 balls at the station to avoid stopping for retrieval.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Commit to your target before you swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zones, more attempts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zones, must call zone before seeing where teammates are fielding.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the group is now counting a shared tally of consecutive hits that land in the called zone.</li><li>One student calls the zone, the batter swings, and a teammate confirms out loud whether the ball landed in the correct zone.</li><li>Keep a running tally of the current streak; if a hit misses the called zone, the streak resets to zero but play continues without stopping to discuss it.</li><li>Rotate the batter after every 4-5 attempts so everyone gets a turn at building the group's streak.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out group streak totals to build friendly energy.</li><li>Easier: use bigger zones and allow more attempts before rotating. Harder: use smaller zones and require the batter to call their own target before seeing where teammates are standing to field.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Target Zone Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where batters score bonus points for placing a hit into a called or open target zone, rewarding controlled direction over just hitting the ball hard.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided field with the 3 target zones marked further out (roughly 12-15m from the tee) using cones, plus 2-3 fielders per batting group spread across the zones.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that batters score a bonus point for placing a hit into a called or open target zone, on top of any normal play.</li><li>Batter hits from the tee, either aiming at a zone called by a teammate or spotting an open zone themselves before swinging.</li><li>Fielders react and field the ball as normal; the coach or a scorer notes whether the hit landed in the intended zone for the bonus point.</li><li>After each hit, quickly reset the tee and rotate the next batter in — keep the pace moving so everyone gets several turns.</li><li>Rotate batting and fielding groups roughly halfway through so every student bats and fields.</li><li>Coach highlights examples of batters successfully placing the ball rather than just hitting it hard.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you make clean contact today?</li><li>How did changing your contact point change the direction of your hit?</li><li>What's the difference between hitting hard and hitting with control?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Makes contact near the middle of the bat</li><li>Adjusts contact point to influence direction</li><li>Commits to a target before swinging</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use larger target zones and a closer/lower tee for students building contact consistency; success is measured by clean contact before direction.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Bullseye Throw":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Throwing — accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Giving your partner a fair, honest target and a genuine effort to throw well to them — teammates rely on each other's accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified teeball per pair, cones, target markers (hoops or chalk circles)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread pairs well apart before throwing; throw only on a clear 'go' signal; never throw at another person, only at markers/gloves</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to throw accurately to a target using a controlled overarm technique.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can point my non-throwing shoulder at the target before releasing</li><li>I can step toward the target with my opposite foot as I throw</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Toss &amp; Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one soft/modified teeball per pair, enough room between pairs that tosses don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently tossing and catching the ball underarm with two hands.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'quick hands' means slightly faster tosses, 'soft hands' means slow right back to gentle, easy catches.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue tossing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up catching hands, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm reach for the throwing arm — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Point your non-throwing shoulder at the target before releasing.</td><td>Aligns your whole body toward accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Step toward the target with your opposite foot as you throw.</td><td>The step adds accuracy and a little natural power.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target after release.</td><td>Stopping the arm early reduces both accuracy and power.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shoulder Point Practice</h4><p>Standing side-on to a target, students practise pointing their non-throwing shoulder at it and stepping through before any ball is thrown.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually, each facing a cone or wall target about 5-8m away. No ball is used for this activity — bodyweight only.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Point, step, then throw — don't throw side-on without stepping.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, exaggerated steps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add the ball, quicker sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate standing side-on to the target with the non-throwing shoulder pointing directly at it.</li><li>Students practise the sequence: point the shoulder at the target, step toward it with the front foot, then mime a throwing action.</li><li>Repeat the point-step-throw sequence slowly 5-6 times, pausing to check shoulder alignment each time.</li><li>Coach circulates and reinforces the cue: point, step, then throw — don't throw side-on without stepping through.</li><li>Once the sequence looks consistent, add a gentle jog-through version to build rhythm.</li><li>Easier: exaggerate and slow down each part of the sequence. Harder: add a real ball and speed the sequence up.</li></ul><hr><h4>Target Toss</h4><p>Partners throw at a hoop or cone target from a set distance, scoring a point for a clean hit inside the target.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs each have 1 soft/modified teeball and a hoop or cone target set up 5-6m apart. Space pairs at least 3m apart from each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Follow through toward the target after release.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind students of the point-step-throw sequence from the previous activity before adding the target.</li><li>Partner A throws at the target from the set distance while Partner B stands well clear to retrieve.</li><li>A clean hit inside the target scores a point; the pair keeps a simple running tally.</li><li>After each throw, Partner B quickly returns the ball so the pair can keep a good pace without long pauses.</li><li>Swap thrower and retriever roles every 5 throws.</li><li>Easier: use a bigger target and move closer. Harder: use a smaller target and increase the throwing distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Moving Target Rotation</h4><p>Small groups rotate through three targets at different distances/angles, tallying accurate throws as a team total.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 share a set of 3 targets placed at different distances and angles (e.g. 4m, 6m, and 8m away, at slightly different angles), each marked with a cone or hoop.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your stance and aim fresh for every new angle.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, closer targets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More targets, mixed distances, time limit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the group rotates through all three targets, tallying accurate throws as one team total.</li><li>One student throws at Target 1 while teammates retrieve and reset; after 2-3 throws, rotate to Target 2, then Target 3.</li><li>Before each new target, the thrower resets their stance and aims fresh rather than carrying over the angle from the previous target.</li><li>Keep a shared tally of clean hits across all three targets for the group.</li><li>Rotate the throwing role after each full circuit of the three targets.</li><li>Easier: use fewer, closer targets. Harder: add all three targets with mixed distances and a time limit for the full circuit.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Bullseye Base Relay</h3><p>Small teams relay-throw at a set of base targets, tagging the next teammate only after a clean hit; fastest team to hit every target wins, rewarding accuracy under a bit of pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out 4-5 base-shaped targets in a wide relay formation, each about 6-8m apart, with small teams of 3-4 lined up at a start cone. Each team has 1 ball.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: the team must hit every base target cleanly before the relay is complete, tagging the next teammate only after a clean hit.</li><li>First thrower in each team throws at the nearest base target; if it's a clean hit, they tag the next teammate to go, who retrieves the ball and throws at the next target.</li><li>If a throw misses, that same thrower retrieves the ball and tries again before passing on — accuracy is required to progress.</li><li>Continue until the team has hit every base target in the sequence; the first team to finish calls out and wins that round.</li><li>Reset all teams to the start and run 2-3 more rounds, mixing up the target order each time.</li><li>Coach reminds students to throw only on a clear 'go' and never at another person, only at the markers.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your throws land accurately today?</li><li>How did pointing your shoulder and stepping change your throw?</li><li>What did a fair, genuine target from your partner look like?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Points non-throwing shoulder at the target before releasing</li><li>Steps toward the target with the opposite foot</li><li>Follows through after release</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a bigger, softer ball and closer, larger targets for students building throwing confidence; success is measured by technique and effort, not just hits.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Two Safe Hands":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Catching — two-handed catches</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about your hand shape and body position before the ball arrives, rather than just reacting at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified teeball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Use soft/modified balls only; spread pairs out; call 'mine!' loudly to avoid collisions</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to catch a ball cleanly using two hands and soft, giving hands.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can form a 'basket' or 'W' shape with my hands to receive the ball</li><li>I can give slightly with my hands and arms on contact rather than catching stiff-armed</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Toss &amp; Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one soft/modified teeball per pair, enough room between pairs that tosses don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently tossing and catching the ball underarm with two hands.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'quick hands' means slightly faster tosses, 'soft hands' means slow right back to gentle, easy catches.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue tossing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up catching hands, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm reach for the throwing arm — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Form a soft two-handed 'basket' shape to receive the ball.</td><td>Gives a secure, wide surface for a clean catch.</td></tr><tr><td>Give slightly with your hands and arms on contact.</td><td>Absorbs the ball's speed instead of it bouncing away.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet early to get in line with the ball.</td><td>Being in position early is more reliable than reaching at the last second.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Basket Hands Practice</h4><p>Partners gently toss the ball to each other, receiver practises forming a soft two-handed basket shape and giving on contact.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand 3-4m apart with 1 soft/modified teeball per pair, spaced at least 2m from neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give with your hands on contact, don't snatch or stab at the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, gentler tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Firmer, faster tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the two-handed 'basket' shape — fingers up for a ball above the waist, fingers down for a ball below the waist — and show giving with the hands on contact.</li><li>Partner A gently tosses underarm to Partner B, who catches with two hands, forming the basket shape and giving slightly on contact.</li><li>Partner B tosses back to Partner A, keeping a steady rhythm of gentle, catchable tosses.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: give with your hands on contact, don't snatch or stab at the ball.</li><li>Continue for 2-3 minutes, then swap who starts the toss so both partners get equal reps.</li><li>Easier: slow, gentle, close tosses. Harder: slightly firmer, faster tosses from the same distance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call &amp; Catch</h4><p>Small groups of 3-4 stand in a circle; one player tosses, calling a name — that player calls 'mine!' and moves to catch cleanly with two hands.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of 3-4 stand in a circle with roughly 2m between each student, 1 soft/modified teeball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet to get in line with the ball early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more predictable tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable tosses, more players in the circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that one student starts with the ball, tosses to any other student in the circle, and calls that student's name as they toss.</li><li>The named student calls 'mine!' loudly, moves their feet to get in line with the ball, and catches cleanly with two hands.</li><li>That student then becomes the new tosser, calling a different name and tossing to them.</li><li>Keep the ball moving around the circle for 2-3 minutes, making sure everyone gets multiple turns catching.</li><li>Coach reminds the group to call 'mine!' early to avoid two players going for the same ball.</li><li>Easier: slower, more predictable tosses to nearby players. Harder: faster, less predictable tosses, and add 1-2 extra players to the circle.</li></ul><hr><h4>Clean Catch Streak</h4><p>Pairs count consecutive clean two-handed catches, trying to beat their own best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand 3-4m apart with 1 soft/modified teeball, spaced well clear of neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your hand shape after every catch.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger, softer ball, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Standard ball, greater distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (own record)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Pairs toss the ball back and forth underarm, counting out loud each consecutive clean two-handed catch.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or caught with one hand, the pair calls out the number they reached and restarts the count from zero — no stopping to discuss it, just keep going.</li><li>Coach reminds pairs to reset their hand shape (basket ready) after every single catch, not just the first one.</li><li>Pairs try to beat their own best streak from earlier in the activity.</li><li>After 3 minutes, ask a few pairs to share their best streak for some friendly recognition.</li><li>Easier: use a bigger, softer ball and stand closer together. Harder: use the standard ball and increase the distance between partners.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Around the Diamond Catch</h3><p>Small groups spread around a diamond shape; a feeder throws to each player in turn while the group counts total clean two-handed catches as a team score, rotating the feeder role regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 spread out around a diamond shape (using bases or cones), roughly 5-6m between each point, with one student in the middle as the feeder holding the ball.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the feeder throws to each player around the diamond in turn, and the group counts total clean two-handed catches as a team score.</li><li>Feeder throws a gentle, catchable ball to the first player on the diamond, who catches with two hands and calls the tally number out loud.</li><li>The player then returns the ball to the feeder, who throws to the next player around the diamond in sequence.</li><li>Continue around the diamond, keeping the running team tally going even if a catch is missed — just move to the next player.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role every full lap of the diamond so everyone gets a turn to throw as well as catch.</li><li>Coach calls out team totals between rotations to build encouragement and energy.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you catch cleanly with two hands today?</li><li>How did giving with your hands change how the catch felt?</li><li>What did you do differently for a faster throw versus a gentle one?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Forms a soft two-handed shape before the ball arrives</li><li>Gives with hands and arms on contact</li><li>Moves feet early to get in line with the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a larger, very soft ball and gentler tosses for students building catching confidence; allow a bounce-first version before progressing to catching on the full.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Down and Scoop":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fielding ground balls</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself while ground fielding technique feels new, and encouraging teammates whose early attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 soft/modified teeball per pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Soft/modified balls only; check the ground surface for hazards before ground-fielding games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to field a ground ball cleanly by getting low and in line behind it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can get my body low and in line behind a ground ball</li><li>I can scoop the ball up with two soft hands close to the ground</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Toss &amp; Catch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one soft/modified teeball per pair, enough room between pairs that tosses don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently tossing and catching the ball underarm with two hands.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'quick hands' means slightly faster tosses, 'soft hands' means slow right back to gentle, easy catches.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue tossing with their new partner.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up catching hands, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing pulse changes and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their ball aside and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a gentle shoulder/arm reach for the throwing arm — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Move to get in line with the ball before getting low.</td><td>Your feet need to find the right position before your hands can do their job.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your body low and behind the ball as a backstop.</td><td>If the ball gets past your hands, your body can still stop it.</td></tr><tr><td>Scoop the ball up with two soft hands.</td><td>Soft hands absorb the ball rather than it bouncing away.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Low Roller Scoop</h4><p>Partners roll the ball along the ground to each other, receiver gets low and scoops it up with two hands.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand 4-5m apart with 1 soft/modified teeball, on a checked, hazard-free surface. Space pairs at least 2m apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get your body behind the ball as a backstop, don't just reach with your hands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower rolls, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster rolls, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate getting low with knees bent and body positioned behind the ball as a backstop, scooping with two hands rather than just reaching.</li><li>Partner A rolls the ball steadily along the ground toward Partner B.</li><li>Partner B moves to get behind the ball, gets low, and scoops it up with two hands rather than stabbing at it with one.</li><li>Partner B rolls the ball back to Partner A, keeping a steady rhythm.</li><li>Swap roller and fielder roles every 4-5 rolls.</li><li>Easier: slower rolls from a closer distance. Harder: faster rolls from further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>In Line &amp; Down</h4><p>Partners roll the ball slightly to one side, and the fielder must move to get in line with it before getting low to scoop.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand 4-5m apart with 1 soft/modified teeball, on the same checked surface used for Low Roller Scoop.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet first to get in line, then get low.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Rolls closer to the fielder's starting position.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Wider rolls requiring more movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this time the roll won't come straight at the fielder — they need to move to get in line with it first.</li><li>Partner A rolls the ball slightly to one side of Partner B rather than directly at them.</li><li>Partner B shuffles sideways to get their body in line with the ball's path before getting low to scoop it.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: move your feet first to get in line, then get low — don't reach across your body.</li><li>Swap roller and fielder roles every 4-5 rolls, alternating which side the roll goes to.</li><li>Easier: roll closer to the fielder's starting position so less movement is needed. Harder: roll wider, requiring a bigger lateral movement.</li></ul><hr><h4>Ground Ball Relay</h4><p>Small teams relay a rolled ball down a line, each player fielding cleanly before passing on; fastest clean team wins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 3-4 line up single file with roughly 3m between each teammate, 1 soft/modified teeball per team, on the checked surface.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get down low and in line with the ball every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, shorter distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster pace, longer line.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: the first player rolls the ball to the second, who must field it cleanly (get in line, get low, scoop) before rolling it on to the third player.</li><li>If a roll is fumbled, that player must gather it fully and cleanly before continuing — no passing on a bad gather.</li><li>Continue down the line until the ball reaches the last player, who raises it overhead to signal the team is finished.</li><li>Run 2-3 rounds, having the line reset and go again each time so everyone touches the ball multiple times.</li><li>Coach calls out the finishing order for some friendly competitive energy.</li><li>Easier: slower pace and a shorter line. Harder: faster pace, a longer line, or a head-to-head mirror race against another team's line.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Diamond Ground Ball Game</h3><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team scores points for clean ground ball gathers, rewarding the full technique — getting in line, getting low, and scooping softly — not just stopping the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided diamond area with 2-3 fielders per group and a feeder rolling or gently hitting ground balls from home plate, using the same checked, hazard-free surface.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the fielding team scores a point for every clean ground ball gather — getting in line, getting low, and scooping softly, not just stopping the ball anywhere.</li><li>Feeder rolls a ground ball toward one of the fielders in turn.</li><li>The fielder moves to get in line, gets low, and scoops the ball with two hands; a teammate or coach confirms whether it met the full technique to count for the point.</li><li>Fielder returns the ball to the feeder for the next roll, keeping the pace moving.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding positions every few minutes so everyone gets a turn in each role.</li><li>Coach highlights full-technique gathers over ones that just happen to stop the ball.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did getting your body low and in line help you do today?</li><li>How is fielding a ground ball different from catching a high ball?</li><li>What made a good, fair roll from your partner?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves to get in line with the ball before getting low</li><li>Gets body low behind the ball</li><li>Uses soft hands to scoop rather than stab at the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a larger, slower-rolling ball for students building ground fielding confidence; allow kneeling as a starting technique before progressing to a full low crouch.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Base Running & Fielding Combinations (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Call It & Cover":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Fielding — communication &amp; backing up</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role in relation to your teammates — reading where support is needed before it's needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones, base markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety for multiple simultaneous small games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to communicate clearly in the field and back up a teammate's throw.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can call clearly to claim a ball or direct a teammate ('mine!', 'yours!', 'back up!')</li><li>I can position myself behind a teammate receiving a throw in case it's missed</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Diamond Dash Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a simple diamond shape with 4 cones/bases roughly 8-10m apart per side. Split the class into small groups that jog the diamond together; no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain that groups will jog together around the outside of the diamond shape, staying loosely together as a group.</li><li>Groups jog a lap of the diamond at an easy pace, touching or running past each base as they go.</li><li>Every lap or so, call a light variation — 'high knees to first,' 'side-shuffle to second,' 'backward jog to third' — to add variety while keeping the pace easy.</li><li>Keep the activity continuous and playful, focusing on raising heart rate and loosening up leg muscles for base running later in the lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, rotating which base groups start from so everyone experiences the variations at different points.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call clearly and early to claim a ball or direct a teammate.</td><td>Clear calls prevent collisions and confusion between fielders.</td></tr><tr><td>Position yourself behind a teammate receiving a throw.</td><td>Gives your team a second chance if the first stop is missed.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your backing-up position as the play develops.</td><td>The right position changes as the ball and players move.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call It Loud</h4><p>Small groups practise calling clearly as a ball is fed toward two or more potential fielders.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 spread across a marked area roughly 8m x 8m, with 1 soft/modified ball per group and 2-3 cones marking rough fielding zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and loudly — hesitation causes mistakes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower feeds, obvious calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster feeds, players must decide who calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that when a ball could be fielded by more than one player, whoever is going for it must call loudly and early so teammates know to leave it to them.</li><li>A coach or student feeder rolls or tosses the ball toward a spot between two potential fielders.</li><li>Whichever fielder gets there first calls out clearly (e.g. 'Mine!' or 'Got it!') as early as possible, ideally before the ball arrives.</li><li>The other fielder(s) back off and let the caller take the ball cleanly.</li><li>Repeat with different feeds so different pairs of fielders get the chance to call.</li><li>Easier: slower feeds with an obvious closest fielder. Harder: faster feeds where it's less obvious who should call.</li></ul><hr><h4>Backstop Positioning</h4><p>Trios practise: one throws to a base/target, a second fields, a third positions behind as a backstop in case the throw is missed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios spread out with roughly 6-8m between the thrower and the target/base, and the backstop positioned a few metres behind the target. 1 soft/modified ball per trio.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always know where your backstop is before you throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Backstop close and obvious.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Backstop must find their own position.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the three roles: a thrower aiming at a base/target, a fielder near the target, and a backstop positioned behind in case the throw is missed.</li><li>The backstop finds and holds a position directly behind the target before the throw happens.</li><li>The thrower throws toward the target; if the fielder misses or the throw goes wide, the backstop retrieves it so the ball doesn't travel far.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: always know where your backstop is before you throw.</li><li>Rotate the three roles every 4-5 throws so everyone experiences each job.</li><li>Easier: backstop stands close and obvious. Harder: backstop must find and hold their own position without being told exactly where.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Field Communication</h4><p>Small-sided fielding game where the coach specifically rewards clear calling and correct backing-up positions, not just stopping the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided fielding groups of 4-5 spread across a mini-diamond area, with a feeder hitting or throwing balls into play. Cones mark rough zone boundaries.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk constantly — a quiet field is a field making mistakes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger space, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller space, faster play.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that points are earned specifically for clear calling and correct backing-up positions, not just for stopping the ball.</li><li>Feeder sends a ball into a zone between two or more fielders.</li><li>Fielders call loudly and early to establish who is taking the ball, while others move into backing-up positions.</li><li>Coach or a nominated scorer awards a point when the calling and backing-up were clear and correct, regardless of whether the ball was cleanly stopped.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding positions every few minutes.</li><li>Easier: bigger space and slower feeds give more time to call and move. Harder: smaller space and faster play demand quicker calls.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Backed-Up Fielding Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team earns bonus points any time a missed stop is cleanly backed up by a teammate, reinforcing that fielding is a team effort.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided fielding game on a mini-diamond with 4-5 fielders per team and a feeder/batter sending balls into play from home plate.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the fielding team earns bonus points any time a missed stop is cleanly backed up by a teammate, on top of normal fielding plays.</li><li>Feeder hits or throws the ball into play; fielders call and move to field it using the calling and backing-up habits from the earlier activities.</li><li>If the first fielder misses the ball, the backstop's clean recovery earns the team a bonus point, reinforcing that fielding is a team effort.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding positions every few minutes so everyone experiences different spots on the field.</li><li>Coach specifically praises backing-up plays out loud, not just clean first stops.</li><li>Keep a simple running tally of bonus points to add some friendly competitive energy.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team communicate clearly in the field today?</li><li>Why does backing up a teammate matter, even when most throws are caught cleanly?</li><li>What did good team fielding look like when you saw it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Calls clearly and early to claim the ball or direct others</li><li>Positions behind a teammate receiving a throw</li><li>Adjusts backing-up position as play develops</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clear visual cues (a cone marking the backstop spot) for students who find reading support positioning challenging.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Running Form":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Base running — technique &amp; speed</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Giving your full, honest effort every time you run, even in practice reps — your team benefits from your genuine effort on the bases.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bases/markers, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear running lanes; only one runner per lane at a time; stop safely well beyond the base</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct base running technique — a strong sprint path and running through first base.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can run in a straight, efficient line to the base without slowing early</li><li>I can run through first base rather than stopping on top of it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Diamond Dash Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a simple diamond shape with 4 cones/bases roughly 8-10m apart per side. Split the class into small groups that jog the diamond together; no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain that groups will jog together around the outside of the diamond shape, staying loosely together as a group.</li><li>Groups jog a lap of the diamond at an easy pace, touching or running past each base as they go.</li><li>Every lap or so, call a light variation — 'high knees to first,' 'side-shuffle to second,' 'backward jog to third' — to add variety while keeping the pace easy.</li><li>Keep the activity continuous and playful, focusing on raising heart rate and loosening up leg muscles for base running later in the lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, rotating which base groups start from so everyone experiences the variations at different points.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Run in a straight, efficient line to the base.</td><td>A wandering running line wastes time and effort.</td></tr><tr><td>Run through the base rather than stopping on top of it.</td><td>Slowing down early costs valuable time getting to base safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Drive low out of your first two steps, then rise into your sprint.</td><td>An explosive, low start gets you to top speed faster.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Sprint to the Bag</h4><p>Students practise a full sprint from home to first base, focusing on a straight running line and running through the base rather than stopping on it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a clear running lane from home plate to first base (roughly 15-18m), with only one runner using the lane at a time. Use cones to mark the lane edges.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Run through the bag, don't slow down to stop on it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter distance, walk-through first.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full distance, timed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a full sprint from home to first base, emphasising a straight line and running through the base rather than slowing down to stop on it.</li><li>One student at a time sprints the full distance while others wait clear of the lane.</li><li>Runner focuses on running through the bag and only slowing down safely well beyond it.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: run through the bag, don't slow down to stop on it.</li><li>Rotate runners quickly so everyone gets 2-3 full sprints.</li><li>Easier: shorter distance and a walk-through first. Harder: full distance, timed for a personal best.</li></ul><hr><h4>Explosive Start Practice</h4><p>Students practise the first few explosive steps out of a ready position before a full sprint to first base.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students line up individually at a start cone, each with a clear lane of at least 5-6m to practise the first few steps, spaced well apart from neighbours.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Drive low out of your first two steps, then rise into your sprint.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower first steps, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed reaction start.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a ready position and the first two explosive steps out of it, showing a low drive before rising into a sprint.</li><li>Students practise from a ready position, driving low out of the first two steps on a 'Go!' call, then rising into a short sprint.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: drive low out of your first two steps, then rise into your sprint.</li><li>Repeat 5-6 times, giving feedback on the explosiveness of the first steps rather than overall speed.</li><li>Progress to a full sprint to first base once the start looks solid.</li><li>Easier: slower first steps with more time to think about the position. Harder: a full-speed reaction start on an unpredictable 'Go!' signal.</li></ul><hr><h4>Timed Base Sprint</h4><p>Partners time each other's sprint from home to first base, trying to beat their own personal best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs share the home-to-first running lane, with one partner timing (phone stopwatch or simple count) while the other runs, spaced so only one pair uses the lane at a time.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Focus on your own technique and time, not comparing to others.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full distance, add a swing/contact before the sprint.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (own record)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that partners will time each other's sprint from home to first base and try to beat their own personal best.</li><li>Partner A sprints the full distance while Partner B times from the first movement to reaching the base.</li><li>Partner B tells Partner A their time immediately afterward.</li><li>Swap roles so Partner B runs and Partner A times.</li><li>Repeat 2-3 times each, encouraging students to focus on their own technique and time rather than comparing to others.</li><li>Easier: shorter distance. Harder: full distance, with a swing/contact off the tee added before the sprint begins.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Sprint &amp; Score Teeball</h3><p>Modified small-sided game where every hit requires a full sprint through first base, with coaching focus on running technique and effort rather than just the outcome of the hit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game with a tee, home plate, and first base marked at full running distance, with fielders spread to field hits.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that every hit in this game requires a full sprint through first base, and the coach is watching running technique and effort, not just the outcome of the hit.</li><li>Batter hits off the tee, then sprints immediately, running through the base rather than stopping on it.</li><li>Fielders field the ball and make a simple play, but the coaching focus stays on the runner's technique and effort.</li><li>Coach calls out specific examples of good running technique after plays, separate from whether the runner was 'safe.'</li><li>Rotate batters through quickly so everyone gets several sprints; rotate fielders partway through.</li><li>Keep the pace brisk so students get plenty of running reps in the available time.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did a strong sprint to first base feel like today?</li><li>Why does running through the base matter instead of stopping on it?</li><li>What did your personal best sprint time teach you about effort?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Runs in a straight, efficient line to the base</li><li>Runs through the base rather than slowing to stop on it</li><li>Shows genuine effort on every sprint rep</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a walk-through or shorter distance for students building running confidence or fitness; success is measured by technique and effort, not raw speed.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Go or No-Go":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Base running — reading when to run</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about the fielding situation before you commit to running, rather than just reacting without looking.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bases/markers, soft/modified balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard base running safety; runners must be able to see the fielding situation clearly before committing</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read a fielding situation and decide when it's safe to run to the next base.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can watch where the ball is fielded before deciding to run</li><li>I can hold up at a base when the fielding team has the ball under control</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Diamond Dash Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a simple diamond shape with 4 cones/bases roughly 8-10m apart per side. Split the class into small groups that jog the diamond together; no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain that groups will jog together around the outside of the diamond shape, staying loosely together as a group.</li><li>Groups jog a lap of the diamond at an easy pace, touching or running past each base as they go.</li><li>Every lap or so, call a light variation — 'high knees to first,' 'side-shuffle to second,' 'backward jog to third' — to add variety while keeping the pace easy.</li><li>Keep the activity continuous and playful, focusing on raising heart rate and loosening up leg muscles for base running later in the lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, rotating which base groups start from so everyone experiences the variations at different points.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch where the ball is fielded before deciding to run.</td><td>A decision made without checking the fielder's position often leads to being out.</td></tr><tr><td>Hold up at a base if the fielding team clearly has the ball under control.</td><td>A cautious hold is often safer than a risky, uninformed run.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit fully once you've made your decision.</td><td>Hesitating halfway through a decision is the worst outcome of all.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Watch &amp; Decide</h4><p>Runners practise watching a fielder gather a ball and deciding out loud whether it's a 'go' or 'no-go' situation, without actually running yet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 stand near a base, with one student acting as a fielder gathering a ball while others act as runners who watch but don't run yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the fielder's hands and body, not just the ball in flight.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious, slow fielding situations.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick, less obvious fielding situations.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that runners will practise reading a fielding situation and calling out whether it's a 'go' or 'no-go', without actually running.</li><li>A student fielder gathers a ball (rolled or gently thrown) in front of the group.</li><li>The 'runner' watches the fielder's hands and body position, then calls out loud 'Go!' or 'No-go!' based on what they see.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: watch the fielder's hands and body, not just the ball in flight.</li><li>Rotate the fielder and runner roles every few reps so everyone practises reading the situation.</li><li>Easier: use obvious, slow fielding situations. Harder: use quicker, less obvious fielding situations.</li></ul><hr><h4>Hold or Go Base Runs</h4><p>Runners practise a short run and must decide, based on a fielder's actions, whether to hold at the base or continue to the next one.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up two bases roughly 10-12m apart, with a fielder positioned to make plays and a runner starting at the first base.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A good decision beats blind speed every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Clear coach cue for the decision.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Runner must read the situation independently.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the runner will take a short lead or start a run and must decide, based on what the fielder is doing, whether to hold at the base or continue to the next one.</li><li>A feeder sends a ball toward the fielder while the runner watches and starts a short run.</li><li>The runner decides whether to hold at the current base or press on to the next one, based on the fielder's actions.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: a good decision beats blind speed every time.</li><li>Rotate the runner, fielder, and feeder roles every few reps.</li><li>Easier: coach gives a clear cue for the decision. Harder: runner must read the situation independently with no coach cue.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read the Field Relay</h4><p>Small teams take turns running bases while the fielding team tries to make clean plays, with runners scoring bonus points for good hold/go decisions regardless of outcome.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 3-4 take turns running the bases while another small group acts as the fielding team, using 2-3 bases spaced around a mini-diamond.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your read, even if the outcome doesn't go your way.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower fielding team.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed fielding team.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that runners score bonus points for good hold/go decisions, regardless of whether they end up safe or out.</li><li>One runner at a time runs the bases while the fielding team tries to make clean plays.</li><li>After each base decision, the coach or a nominated observer calls out whether it was a good decision, awarding a bonus point if so — separate from the outcome.</li><li>Rotate through all runners in the team, then swap the running and fielding teams.</li><li>Coach reinforces trusting a good read even when the outcome doesn't go the runner's way.</li><li>Easier: fielding team moves at a slower pace. Harder: fielding team plays at full speed.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Decision Diamond Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the coach specifically praises good hold/go decisions on the bases, separate from whether the runner was safe or out, reinforcing that reading the game matters as much as speed.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with bases at running distance and a full fielding team in position.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the coach will specifically praise good hold/go decisions on the bases, separate from whether the runner ended up safe or out.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where batters hit and then make base-running decisions based on the fielding situation.</li><li>After notable plays, the coach calls out specific examples of good decision-making on the bases, regardless of the outcome.</li><li>Continue play with normal rotations between batting and fielding teams.</li><li>Rotate all players through batting, running, and fielding roles across the game.</li><li>Reinforce throughout: reading the game matters as much as speed.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you decide between holding at a base or running today?</li><li>What did you watch to make that decision?</li><li>Can you describe a moment your read was right, even if the outcome wasn't perfect?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Watches the fielding situation before deciding to run</li><li>Holds up at a base when appropriate</li><li>Makes a clear, committed decision rather than hesitating halfway</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use clear coach cues ('go!' or 'hold!') initially for students building game-reading confidence, fading the cues as independent reading develops.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Field, Turn, Throw":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining fielding + throwing to a base</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual fielding and throwing skills combine into one smooth sequence that helps your whole team.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, bases/markers, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; check ground for hazards; call 'mine!' when approaching a shared ball</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine fielding a ground ball with turning and throwing accurately to a base.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can gather a ground ball cleanly before looking up to throw</li><li>I can turn my body toward the base as part of one smooth fielding-to-throwing motion</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Diamond Dash Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a simple diamond shape with 4 cones/bases roughly 8-10m apart per side. Split the class into small groups that jog the diamond together; no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain that groups will jog together around the outside of the diamond shape, staying loosely together as a group.</li><li>Groups jog a lap of the diamond at an easy pace, touching or running past each base as they go.</li><li>Every lap or so, call a light variation — 'high knees to first,' 'side-shuffle to second,' 'backward jog to third' — to add variety while keeping the pace easy.</li><li>Keep the activity continuous and playful, focusing on raising heart rate and loosening up leg muscles for base running later in the lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, rotating which base groups start from so everyone experiences the variations at different points.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Gather the ball cleanly with two hands before turning.</td><td>Rushing the turn before gathering fully often causes a fumble.</td></tr><tr><td>Turn your body toward the base as part of one smooth motion.</td><td>A continuous action is faster and more reliable than separate, stop-start steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Throw with a clear release straight after the turn.</td><td>Hesitating after turning wastes the time advantage of a clean gather.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Gather &amp; Turn</h4><p>Individual practice fielding a rolled ball, gathering it cleanly with two hands, and turning toward a base before throwing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students work individually with a ball each and a base/target marked roughly 6-8m away, spread out with plenty of space around each.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Gather first, then turn — don't rush the gather to turn early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stationary ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rolling ball, increasing speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate fielding a rolled ball with two hands, gathering it cleanly, then turning the body toward the base before any throw is made.</li><li>Roll the ball to yourself (or have it rolled gently by a partner) and practise gathering it with two hands.</li><li>After gathering, turn your whole body — feet and hips — to face the base target before miming or making a throw.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: gather first, then turn — don't rush the gather to turn early.</li><li>Repeat 5-6 times, starting with a stationary ball before adding movement.</li><li>Easier: use a stationary ball to start. Harder: use a rolling ball, gradually increasing its speed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Field &amp; Fire to Base</h4><p>Partners field a rolled ball and immediately throw to a base target, timing themselves from gather to release.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs stand with a base target 6-8m from the fielder and the roller positioned to send the ball toward them. 1 soft/modified ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>One smooth motion — gather, turn, throw.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer base, slower roll.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further base, faster roll.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that partners will field a rolled ball and immediately throw to a base target, timing the sequence from gather to release.</li><li>Partner A rolls the ball toward Partner B, who fields it, turns, and throws to the base target in one smooth motion.</li><li>Partner A can time the sequence with a phone stopwatch or simple count if available, to give friendly feedback.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: one smooth motion — gather, turn, throw.</li><li>Swap roller and fielder roles every 4-5 reps.</li><li>Easier: closer base and a slower roll. Harder: further base and a faster roll.</li></ul><hr><h4>Race to the Base</h4><p>Two fielders start equal distance from a ball rolled between them; first to field it cleanly and hit the base target scores a point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs of fielders start equal distance (roughly 4-5m) from a base target, with the ball rolled directly between them by a feeder.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call 'mine!' early and commit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower roll, closer base.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster roll, further base.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that two fielders will start equal distance from a rolled ball, and whoever fields it cleanly and hits the base target first scores a point.</li><li>Feeder rolls the ball squarely between the two fielders.</li><li>Both fielders react, with whoever gets there first calling 'Mine!' early and committing to the field-turn-throw sequence.</li><li>The fielder completes the sequence by hitting the base target; the other fielder backs off once the call is made.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding pairs every few rounds.</li><li>Easier: slower roll and a closer base. Harder: faster roll and a further base.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Fielding Circuit Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team scores points for clean gathers and quick, accurate throws to a base, rewarding the whole fielding sequence, not just stopping the ball.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided fielding circuit with several base targets around a mini-diamond and a feeder sending ground balls into different areas.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the fielding team scores points for clean gathers and quick, accurate throws to a base — the whole sequence, not just stopping the ball.</li><li>Feeder rolls or hits a ground ball toward a fielder.</li><li>Fielder gathers cleanly, turns, and throws to the relevant base target; a point is awarded if the whole sequence was clean and accurate.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding positions every few minutes.</li><li>Coach highlights the full sequence — gather, turn, throw — rather than praising just the stop or just the throw.</li><li>Keep a simple running team tally to add friendly competitive energy.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you gather the ball cleanly today?</li><li>Why does gathering fully before turning matter?</li><li>What made your gather-to-throw sequence smooth or clunky?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Gathers with two hands before looking up</li><li>Turns toward the base as part of one smooth motion</li><li>Throws with reasonable accuracy after fielding</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow the ball speed and shorten the fielding-to-base distance for students building confidence; success is measured by a clean gather-to-throw sequence, not speed.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Diamond Duty":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Infield positioning basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own role and where you fit in the team's fielding shape, not just chasing every ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bases/markers, cones, soft/modified balls, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; rotate positions regularly so games don't run too long in one spot</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning basic infield positions and what each position's job is.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand in a ready, balanced position at my infield spot before the ball is hit</li><li>I can describe what my infield position's main job is in a simple game</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Diamond Dash Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a simple diamond shape with 4 cones/bases roughly 8-10m apart per side. Split the class into small groups that jog the diamond together; no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain that groups will jog together around the outside of the diamond shape, staying loosely together as a group.</li><li>Groups jog a lap of the diamond at an easy pace, touching or running past each base as they go.</li><li>Every lap or so, call a light variation — 'high knees to first,' 'side-shuffle to second,' 'backward jog to third' — to add variety while keeping the pace easy.</li><li>Keep the activity continuous and playful, focusing on raising heart rate and loosening up leg muscles for base running later in the lesson.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, rotating which base groups start from so everyone experiences the variations at different points.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stand in a ready, balanced position at your spot before the ball is hit.</td><td>Being ready and balanced lets you react instantly.</td></tr><tr><td>Know your position's main job before play starts.</td><td>Understanding your role means faster, more confident decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Reset your ready position after every play.</td><td>Staying ready for the next ball matters as much as the last one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Find Your Spot</h4><p>Students walk through each infield position (pitcher's mound area, first, second, third, shortstop equivalent) and practise the ready stance at each.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out the infield positions (pitcher's mound area, first, second, third, shortstop equivalent) with cones on a mini-diamond. Students walk through as a whole class or in a small rotating group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay light on your feet, weight forward, ready to move any direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Walk through slowly with a coach cue at each spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rotate quickly through all positions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Walk the group to each infield position in turn, demonstrating the ready stance — light on the feet, weight forward, ready to move any direction.</li><li>At each position, students practise holding the ready stance for a few seconds before moving to the next spot.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: stay light on your feet, weight forward, ready to move any direction.</li><li>Once the group has visited every position slowly, repeat the walk-through at a slightly quicker pace.</li><li>Rotate small groups through if the whole class can't move through the positions at once.</li><li>Easier: walk through slowly with a coach cue at each spot. Harder: rotate quickly through all positions with less guidance.</li></ul><hr><h4>Position Job Match</h4><p>In small groups, students match a simple 'job' card (e.g. 'closest to first base') to the correct infield position, then demonstrate standing there.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 each have a set of simple 'job' cards (e.g. 'closest to first base', 'covers the middle') and stand near the marked infield positions.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your job before the ball is even hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positions, obvious matches.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>All positions, quick-fire matching.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each job card describes a simple responsibility that matches one infield position.</li><li>Give each group a job card and have them discuss which infield position it matches.</li><li>Once they agree, the group demonstrates by standing at that position together.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: know your job before the ball is even hit.</li><li>Rotate through several job cards so groups match multiple positions.</li><li>Easier: use fewer positions with obvious matches. Harder: use all positions with quick-fire matching.</li></ul><hr><h4>Live Ball Positioning</h4><p>A gentle live-hit game where infielders must move to and hold their correct position readiness before each hit, coach checking positioning.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a mini-diamond with infielders in their positions and a feeder hitting or throwing gentle balls into play from home plate.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your ready position after every play.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower hits, more time to reset.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker succession of hits.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that infielders must move to and hold their correct ready position before each hit, and the coach will check positioning.</li><li>Before each hit, the coach pauses briefly to check that every infielder is in a correct, ready position.</li><li>Feeder sends a gentle live ball into play; infielders react and field as normal.</li><li>After the play, infielders reset to their ready position before the next hit.</li><li>Rotate infield positions every few minutes so everyone experiences different spots.</li><li>Easier: slower hits with more time to reset position. Harder: quicker succession of hits with less reset time.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Positioned Infield Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team earns bonus points for every player who is in a correct, ready infield position before the ball is hit, alongside normal fielding plays.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with a full infield in position and bibs to distinguish fielding and batting teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the fielding team earns bonus points for every player in a correct, ready infield position before the ball is hit, in addition to normal fielding plays.</li><li>Before each hit, the coach or a nominated scorer quickly checks the infield positioning.</li><li>Batter hits and fielders play the ball as normal; bonus points are awarded based on the pre-hit positioning check.</li><li>Continue with normal rotations between batting and fielding.</li><li>Rotate infield positions regularly, and swap batting/fielding teams partway through.</li><li>Coach highlights good, alert positioning as much as good plays on the ball.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What is your infield position's main job?</li><li>What did a ready, balanced stance feel like at your position?</li><li>How did knowing your job help you react faster?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stands in a ready, balanced position before the hit</li><li>Can describe their position's main job simply</li><li>Moves appropriately for their position when the ball is hit</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use simple visual markers/cones for each infield spot, and allow students to stay in one position for longer while learning before rotating through all of them.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Positioning, Decisions & Tactics (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Deep Ground Basics":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Outfield positioning basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Covering ground fairly and honestly for your team, even when the ball isn't hit directly to you — outfielders support the whole team's fielding.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones, bases/markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Outfield spacing must be generous; call 'mine!' clearly before converging on a ball</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning basic outfield positioning and how to track and move to a fly or hit ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can stand in a ready position at an appropriate outfield depth and angle</li><li>I can move to get in line behind a ball hit into the outfield</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; React Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader' who moves and points/gestures toward an imaginary ball direction, the other 'reacts' by quickly shuffling that direction.</li><li>The leader varies their pointed direction and pace unpredictably — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The reactor tries to shuffle and adjust their body position as quickly as possible each time.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and react.</li><li>This builds the same reading-and-reacting instincts used in fielding and positioning later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more confident movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in fielding and positioning.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Stand at an appropriate depth and angle for your position.</td><td>Correct starting position reduces the ground you have to cover reactively.</td></tr><tr><td>Call clearly before converging on a hit ball.</td><td>Prevents collisions when more than one fielder could reach the ball.</td></tr><tr><td>Move with soft, balanced steps to get in line with the ball.</td><td>Balanced movement is more reliable than a rushed sprint and grab.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Depth &amp; Angle Practice</h4><p>Students practise standing at an appropriate outfield depth and adjusting their angle based on where a coach points, without a ball yet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out in an open outfield area with plenty of space, no ball used yet. Coach stands centrally where all students can see them point.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Read the angle early and adjust your feet, don't wait until the ball is hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach gives clear verbal cues.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Students read a silent pointed cue.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain appropriate outfield depth (not too close, not too far) and show how angle changes based on where the ball is likely to go.</li><li>Students stand at what they judge to be a sensible outfield depth for their position.</li><li>Coach points in different directions without a ball; students adjust their angle and a step or two of positioning based on the point.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: read the angle early and adjust your feet, don't wait until the ball is hit.</li><li>Repeat with several different pointed directions.</li><li>Easier: coach gives a clear verbal cue alongside the point. Harder: students read a silent pointed cue only.</li></ul><hr><h4>Track &amp; Call</h4><p>A coach or partner hits/throws a ball into the outfield; the fielder calls 'mine!' and tracks it into a clean gather.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or small groups spread out in the outfield area, with a coach or partner positioned to hit/throw balls into the outfield.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early, then move with soft, balanced steps to get under or in line with the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more predictable hits.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable hits/angles.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling 'mine!' as soon as a ball is identified and tracking it with soft, balanced steps into a clean gather.</li><li>A coach or partner hits or throws a ball out into the outfield toward a fielder.</li><li>The fielder calls 'mine!' early and moves with soft, balanced steps to get under or in line with the ball before gathering it cleanly.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: call early, then move with soft, balanced steps.</li><li>Rotate fielders through regularly so everyone gets several tracking reps.</li><li>Easier: slower, more predictable hits. Harder: faster, less predictable hits and angles.</li></ul><hr><h4>Outfield Coverage Game</h4><p>Small groups of outfielders practise covering ground between them, calling and adjusting position as balls are hit to different areas.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 outfielders spread across a wide zone (roughly 15m x 15m or larger), with a feeder hitting balls to different areas.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate constantly about who's taking the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer outfielders covering more obvious zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More balls in quicker succession, bigger area.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the group is covering ground between them and must call and adjust position as balls are hit to different areas.</li><li>Feeder hits a ball into one part of the zone; the closest outfielder calls it and the others adjust their coverage of the remaining space.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: communicate constantly about who's taking the ball.</li><li>Continue with feeds to different areas, encouraging outfielders to reposition between hits based on where gaps appear.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role periodically.</li><li>Easier: fewer outfielders covering more obvious zones. Harder: more balls in quicker succession across a bigger area.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Deep Field Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where outfielders are specifically coached and rewarded for good positioning, clear calling, and clean tracking of hit balls, alongside the normal game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game with a full outfield group in position, generous spacing, and a feeder/batter sending balls into the outfield as well as the infield.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that outfielders are specifically coached and rewarded for good positioning, clear calling, and clean tracking of hit balls, on top of the normal game.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where hits sometimes reach the outfield; outfielders apply depth, angle, calling, and tracking skills from the earlier activities.</li><li>Coach calls out specific examples of good outfield positioning and calling as they happen.</li><li>Continue play with normal batting and fielding rotations.</li><li>Rotate outfield positions regularly so everyone gets outfield reps.</li><li>Remind the group to call 'mine!' clearly before converging on any ball, given the generous outfield spacing.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you track and get in line with a hit ball today?</li><li>How did adjusting your depth and angle help you field better?</li><li>What did good outfield communication sound like?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stands at an appropriate ready depth/angle</li><li>Calls clearly before converging on a ball</li><li>Moves with balanced steps to get in line with the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Reduce outfield distances/depth for students building confidence tracking balls in open space, and use bigger, softer balls for early reps.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Force or Tag":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Force plays / tag plays concept</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about which type of play is actually available in a situation, rather than just reacting the same way every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Bases/markers, soft/modified balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>No diving or sliding; force plays are simply about reaching the base with the ball before the runner — no contact between fielder and runner</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the basic idea of a force play versus a tag play in teeball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can explain the simple difference between a force play and a tag play</li><li>I can make a correct, safe force play at a base</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; React Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader' who moves and points/gestures toward an imaginary ball direction, the other 'reacts' by quickly shuffling that direction.</li><li>The leader varies their pointed direction and pace unpredictably — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The reactor tries to shuffle and adjust their body position as quickly as possible each time.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and react.</li><li>This builds the same reading-and-reacting instincts used in fielding and positioning later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more confident movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in fielding and positioning.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Check whether the base behind the runner is occupied.</td><td>That's what determines whether a force play is actually available.</td></tr><tr><td>Get to the base early and hold it steady with the ball.</td><td>A force play just needs the fielder at the base before the runner, held safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Avoid diving or reaching dangerously for the play.</td><td>Safety at the base matters more than a marginal, risky play.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Force Play Walkthrough</h4><p>Coach walks students through simple force-play scenarios (runner must run because the base behind them is occupied), practising a fielder simply touching the base with the ball before the runner arrives.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up two bases roughly 10-12m apart on a mini-diamond, with a coach guiding a small group through slow-motion scenarios. No live running yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>You just need to beat the runner to the base with the ball in hand or glove.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slow-motion walkthrough, obvious scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed scenarios, quick decisions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the force-play concept: a runner must run to the next base because the base behind them is occupied, so a fielder can get them out by simply touching the base with the ball before they arrive.</li><li>Walk through a simple scenario slowly: a runner starts moving while a fielder gathers a ball and walks to touch the base ahead of the runner.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: you just need to beat the runner to the base with the ball in hand or glove — no tag needed.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough a few times with different students in the fielder and runner roles.</li><li>Remind the group throughout: no diving or sliding, and no contact between fielder and runner.</li><li>Easier: slow-motion walkthrough with obvious scenarios. Harder: full-speed scenarios requiring quick decisions.</li></ul><hr><h4>Force or Not?</h4><p>Small groups are shown quick scenarios and must call out whether a force play is 'on' at a particular base before acting it out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 gather around a mini-diamond with bases marked, and the coach sets up quick scenario cards or verbal descriptions (e.g. runner on first only, runners on first and second).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check if the base behind the runner is occupied — that's what makes it a force.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, clearer scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Multiple runners, quick-fire scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Present a quick scenario describing where runners are on the bases.</li><li>The group discusses and calls out whether a force play is 'on' at a particular base.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: check if the base behind the runner is occupied — that's what makes it a force.</li><li>Once the group has called it, have them briefly act out the scenario to confirm their answer.</li><li>Repeat with several different scenarios, mixing up which base is in question.</li><li>Easier: fewer, clearer scenarios. Harder: multiple runners and quick-fire scenarios.</li></ul><hr><h4>Live Force Play Reps</h4><p>A gentle live game where fielders practise clean, safe force-play attempts at a base while runners run at a controlled pace.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a mini-diamond with bases at running distance, controlled-pace runners, and fielders positioned to make force plays. Soft/modified balls only.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get to the base early and hold it steady, don't dive or reach dangerously.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower runners, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed runners.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind the group of the safety rule: no diving or sliding, and force plays are about reaching the base with the ball before the runner, with no contact.</li><li>A feeder sends a ball into play; the fielder gathers it and moves to the relevant base to attempt a safe, controlled force play.</li><li>Runner runs at a controlled pace toward the base, without contesting the play physically.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: get to the base early and hold it steady, don't dive or reach dangerously.</li><li>Rotate fielder, runner, and feeder roles every few reps.</li><li>Easier: slower runners with more time. Harder: full-speed runners.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Force Play Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game with simple force-play situations built in, coached explicitly on safe, correct technique at the base rather than just the outcome of the play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with simple force-play situations built into the rules, using soft/modified balls and no sliding.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the game includes simple force-play situations, and the coach will explicitly coach safe, correct technique at the base rather than just the outcome of the play.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where runners and fielders encounter force-play situations as they naturally arise.</li><li>Coach pauses briefly after force plays to reinforce safe technique — reaching the base early and holding it steady, no diving or contact.</li><li>Continue play with normal rotations between batting, running, and fielding.</li><li>Rotate all players through fielding and running roles across the game.</li><li>Reinforce the no-contact, no-sliding safety rule throughout.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's the simple difference between a force play and a tag play?</li><li>What made a force play successful (or not) today?</li><li>Why is safety at the base so important in this kind of play?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Can explain the basic force-play concept simply</li><li>Gets to the base safely and holds it steady with the ball</li><li>Reads whether a force play is available in a given situation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use walkthroughs and slow-motion scenarios for students who need more processing time before adding full-speed live reps.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Find the Gap":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting placement (hitting gaps)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Understanding how your individual batting choices connect to the bigger picture of the fielding team's positioning.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Junior bats, soft/modified balls, batting tees, cones marking gaps, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard batting-radius spacing rules</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to place our hit into open space rather than always hitting the same way.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can look at where fielders are positioned before I bat</li><li>I can adjust my contact point to aim for an open gap</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; React Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader' who moves and points/gestures toward an imaginary ball direction, the other 'reacts' by quickly shuffling that direction.</li><li>The leader varies their pointed direction and pace unpredictably — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The reactor tries to shuffle and adjust their body position as quickly as possible each time.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and react.</li><li>This builds the same reading-and-reacting instincts used in fielding and positioning later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more confident movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in fielding and positioning.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Scan the field before deciding where to hit.</td><td>Placement decisions need information about where fielders are standing.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your contact point slightly to aim for an open gap.</td><td>Small technical adjustments control direction more reliably than swinging harder.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit to your target before you swing.</td><td>Last-second changes of mind usually produce a worse result than either original option.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Scan Before You Swing</h4><p>Batters pause to look at where fielders are standing before each hit, calling out loud which gap looks open.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Batters at a tee station with fielders spread out at different depths and angles in front (using cones or actual students as visual reference), roughly 8-12m away.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Scan the field, then decide your target before you swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious, wide-open gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller, less obvious gaps.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that before each swing, batters will pause to look at where fielders are standing and call out loud which gap looks open.</li><li>Batter pauses at the tee, scans the field, and calls out the gap they've identified as open before setting up to swing.</li><li>Batter then sets up and hits, aiming for the called gap.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: scan the field, then decide your target before you swing.</li><li>Rotate the batter every 4-5 swings, moving fielders slightly between turns so gaps change.</li><li>Easier: use obvious, wide-open gaps. Harder: use smaller, less obvious gaps.</li></ul><hr><h4>Called Gap Challenge</h4><p>A partner calls a specific gap for the batter to aim for before each hit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Batter at a tee station with a partner standing where they can see the field and call gaps, fielders spread out as in the previous activity.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust your contact point slightly to send the ball toward the called gap.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer gap choices, closer targets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More gap choices, quick-fire calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this time a partner calls a specific gap for the batter to aim for before each hit.</li><li>Partner calls a gap (e.g. left, middle, right) just before the batter sets up.</li><li>Batter aims their hit toward the called gap, adjusting their contact point slightly rather than their whole swing.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: adjust your contact point slightly to send the ball toward the called gap.</li><li>Rotate batter and caller roles every 4-5 swings.</li><li>Easier: fewer gap choices and closer targets. Harder: more gap choices with quick-fire calls.</li></ul><hr><h4>Gap Finder Streak</h4><p>Small groups tally hits that successfully find an open or called gap in a row.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 share a tee station with fielders spread around, keeping a shared tally of gap-finding hits in a row.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Commit to your target and trust your technique.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger gaps, more attempts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller gaps, live fielders adjusting position.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the group is tallying consecutive hits that successfully find an open or called gap.</li><li>One student bats while another calls a gap (or the batter identifies an open one themselves); the rest of the group act as fielders and confirm the result.</li><li>Keep a running tally of the streak, resetting to zero (without stopping play) if a hit doesn't find the gap.</li><li>Rotate the batter every 4-5 attempts so everyone contributes to the group's streak.</li><li>Coach circulates and calls out group streak totals for encouragement.</li><li>Easier: bigger gaps and more attempts. Harder: smaller gaps with live fielders actively adjusting position between hits.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Gap Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the coach specifically celebrates batters who place hits into open space to beat the field, alongside runs scored.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with a full fielding team spread across the field, using bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the coach will specifically celebrate batters who place hits into open space to beat the field, alongside runs scored in the normal game.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where batters apply the scanning and gap-finding skills from the earlier activities.</li><li>Coach calls out specific examples of good placement hits as they happen, separate from just praising runs scored.</li><li>Continue play with normal batting and fielding rotations.</li><li>Rotate all players through batting and fielding roles across the game.</li><li>Encourage batters to scan and commit to a target before every swing, just like in the lead-up activities.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>How did you decide where to place your hits today?</li><li>What's the difference between hitting hard and hitting smart?</li><li>What did you notice about the field that helped you find a gap?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the field before committing to a hit</li><li>Adjusts contact point rather than always hitting the same way</li><li>Shows awareness of where fielders are positioned</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a simplified field with fewer, larger gaps for students building placement awareness, adding complexity as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Team Diamond Shape":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Team fielding shape &amp; communication</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your individual position fits into the team's whole fielding shape, not just your own patch of ground.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, cones, bases/markers, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety for a full small-sided fielding team</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to hold a connected team fielding shape and communicate as a whole infield/outfield unit.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can adjust my fielding position based on where my teammates are standing</li><li>I can communicate clearly with teammates about coverage and gaps</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; React Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader' who moves and points/gestures toward an imaginary ball direction, the other 'reacts' by quickly shuffling that direction.</li><li>The leader varies their pointed direction and pace unpredictably — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The reactor tries to shuffle and adjust their body position as quickly as possible each time.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and react.</li><li>This builds the same reading-and-reacting instincts used in fielding and positioning later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more confident movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in fielding and positioning.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Check your position relative to your teammates, not just your own spot.</td><td>Team shape is about the whole unit, not individual positioning alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly about coverage and gaps.</td><td>A talking fielding team closes gaps faster than a silent one.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your position based on where teammates are standing.</td><td>Your position should respond to the team's shape, not stay fixed.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shape Check</h4><p>The fielding team freezes on a coach's call and checks as a group whether their overall shape has any obvious big gaps or overlaps.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>The fielding team (4-6 players) spreads out across a mini-diamond in their positions, with a coach able to call a freeze at any point.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look left and right, not just at your own spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach highlights gaps directly.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team identifies and fixes gaps themselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that on a freeze call, the whole fielding team checks as a group whether their overall shape has any obvious big gaps or overlaps.</li><li>Coach calls 'Freeze!' at a random point during light movement or positioning practice.</li><li>Team holds their positions and looks around — left and right, not just at their own spot — to spot gaps or overlaps.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: look left and right, not just at your own spot.</li><li>Discuss briefly as a group what could be adjusted, then repeat with a new freeze.</li><li>Easier: coach highlights gaps directly. Harder: team identifies and fixes gaps themselves without hints.</li></ul><hr><h4>Communicate &amp; Cover</h4><p>Small-sided fielding practice where teammates must call out to cover for each other as hits are placed around the field.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided fielding team spread across a mini-diamond, with a feeder hitting or throwing balls to different spots around the field.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A busy field is a talking field — call constantly.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower hits, more time to communicate.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker hits, less time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that teammates must call out to cover for each other as hits are placed around the field.</li><li>Feeder sends a ball to one area; the nearest fielder calls it while others shift to cover the space that opens up.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: a busy field is a talking field — call constantly.</li><li>Continue with feeds to different areas, encouraging constant communication between plays.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role and fielding positions every few minutes.</li><li>Easier: slower hits with more time to communicate. Harder: quicker hits with less time to react.</li></ul><hr><h4>Whole Team Shape Game</h4><p>A gentle live game where the coach specifically highlights and rewards good team shape and communication, separate from individual plays.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A gentle live small-sided game on a mini-diamond with the full fielding team in position and a batter/feeder sending live hits.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Your teammates' positions should influence your own.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer live hits, more discussion time between.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Continuous live play.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the coach will specifically highlight and reward good team shape and communication, separate from individual plays.</li><li>Play live with a batter hitting and the fielding team reacting, applying the shape-checking and communication habits from earlier activities.</li><li>Coach pauses occasionally to point out good examples of team shape or communication, regardless of whether the ball was fielded cleanly.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: your teammates' positions should influence your own.</li><li>Rotate fielding positions and the batter role every few minutes.</li><li>Easier: fewer live hits with more discussion time between them. Harder: continuous live play with less pause time.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Connected Diamond Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the fielding team earns bonus points for maintaining good overall shape and clear communication across the whole field, not just for individual stops.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with the full fielding team in position across the whole field, using bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the fielding team earns bonus points for maintaining good overall shape and clear communication across the whole field, not just for individual stops.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where the fielding team applies shape-checking and communication skills throughout.</li><li>Coach or a nominated observer awards bonus points periodically for good team shape and communication, separate from normal fielding plays.</li><li>Continue with normal batting and fielding rotations.</li><li>Rotate all players through batting and fielding roles across the game.</li><li>Coach reinforces that good shape and talking are team achievements, not just individual ones.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made your team's fielding shape effective (or not) today?</li><li>Why does communication matter for the whole fielding team, not just individuals?</li><li>How did adjusting your position based on teammates help?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Adjusts position based on where teammates are standing</li><li>Communicates clearly about coverage and gaps</li><li>Recognises and helps fix gaps in the team shape</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a simplified field with fewer players/positions initially, adding full team numbers as the concept of team shape becomes clearer.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Where's the Play":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making — where to throw</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Making a fair, honest decision under pressure that genuinely helps your team, rather than just panicking and throwing anywhere.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, bases/markers, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard field safety; encourage controlled, accurate throws over rushed, wild ones</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to decide quickly and correctly where to throw the ball after fielding it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can look up after gathering the ball to see where the play is</li><li>I can choose the base that gives my team the best chance of an out</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Read &amp; React Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader' who moves and points/gestures toward an imaginary ball direction, the other 'reacts' by quickly shuffling that direction.</li><li>The leader varies their pointed direction and pace unpredictably — nothing too sharp or unsafe, just varied movement.</li><li>The reactor tries to shuffle and adjust their body position as quickly as possible each time.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and react.</li><li>This builds the same reading-and-reacting instincts used in fielding and positioning later in the lesson, while raising heart rate through varied movement.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more confident movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the body control used in fielding and positioning.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Gather the ball fully, then look up to assess the play.</td><td>Trying to do both at once often causes a fumble.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the base that gives the best chance of an out.</td><td>Not every base is equally valuable — some outs matter more than others.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit to your decision and throw with control.</td><td>Hesitating after deciding usually causes a rushed, inaccurate throw.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Look Up &amp; Decide</h4><p>After fielding a ball, students pause briefly to look up and call out loud which base they would throw to before actually throwing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students field a ball individually or in pairs, with 2-3 base options marked around a mini-diamond roughly 8-10m away.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Gather first, then look — don't try to do both at once.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Obvious, single-option scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Multiple base options to choose between.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that after fielding, students will pause briefly to look up and call out loud which base they would throw to before actually throwing.</li><li>A ball is rolled or gently hit to the fielder, who gathers it cleanly.</li><li>Fielder pauses, looks up at the base options, and calls out which base they would throw to.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: gather first, then look — don't try to do both at once.</li><li>Repeat with different scenarios so students practise the gather-then-look sequence several times.</li><li>Easier: obvious, single-option scenarios. Harder: multiple base options to choose between.</li></ul><hr><h4>Best Play Call</h4><p>Small groups are shown quick scenarios (runners on different bases) and must call out the base that gives the best chance of an out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 gather around a mini-diamond with runner positions described by the coach (e.g. using cones to represent runners on different bases).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Think about which out actually stops the biggest threat.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer runners/options.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Multiple runners, quick-fire scenarios.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Present a quick scenario showing runners on different bases (using cones as stand-ins).</li><li>The group discusses and calls out the base that gives the best chance of an out.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: think about which out actually stops the biggest threat.</li><li>Once the group has called it, briefly explain why that base was the best choice.</li><li>Repeat with several different scenarios, varying the number and position of runners.</li><li>Easier: fewer runners/options. Harder: multiple runners with quick-fire scenarios.</li></ul><hr><h4>Live Decision Fielding</h4><p>A gentle live game where fielders practise fielding and making a quick, correct throw decision under mild game pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A gentle live small-sided game on a mini-diamond with fielders in position, a feeder/batter, and runners moving at a controlled, mild pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your first good read rather than second-guessing under pressure.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, more thinking time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full pace, quicker decisions required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Mild opposition + decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Remind fielders of the gather-look-decide sequence from the earlier activities.</li><li>Feeder or batter sends the ball into play; fielder gathers it cleanly and looks up to make a quick throw decision.</li><li>Fielder throws to the base they judge gives the best chance of an out, under mild game pressure from a controlled-pace runner.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: trust your first good read rather than second-guessing under pressure.</li><li>Rotate fielding positions and the runner/feeder roles every few reps.</li><li>Easier: slower pace with more thinking time. Harder: full pace with quicker decisions required.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Decision Diamond Teeball</h3><p>Small-sided game where the coach specifically highlights good decision-making about where to throw, separate from whether the throw itself was perfectly accurate.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game on a mini-diamond with a full fielding team, runners, and a batter/feeder, using bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the coach will specifically highlight good decision-making about where to throw, separate from whether the throw itself was perfectly accurate.</li><li>Play a small-sided game where fielders apply the gather-look-decide sequence from the earlier activities as plays develop.</li><li>Coach calls out specific examples of good throw decisions as they happen, even if the throw itself wasn't perfectly accurate.</li><li>Continue with normal batting, running, and fielding rotations.</li><li>Rotate all players through batting, running, and fielding roles across the game.</li><li>Reinforce throughout: a good decision under pressure is a skill worth celebrating on its own.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you decide where to throw today?</li><li>What made a play a 'good decision' even if the outcome wasn't perfect?</li><li>How did thinking ahead about the game situation help you?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Looks up to assess the play after gathering the ball</li><li>Chooses a base with a clear, sensible reason</li><li>Commits to a decision rather than hesitating</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use simple, single-option scenarios for students building decision-making confidence, adding multiple-option scenarios as understanding grows.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Mini Diamond Games":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided modified games</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking about how everyone's contribution in a small team — batting, fielding, running — adds up to the team's success.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, batting tees, bases/markers, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard small-sided game safety; soft/modified balls only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply batting, fielding, and base running skills together in a small-sided modified game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a small-sided game</li><li>I can play my position and role within a small team</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a diamond shape' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/throwing-arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply batting, fielding, and running technique together in a real game.</td><td>This lesson tests the whole unit's skills working together.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with teammates about roles and coverage.</td><td>Small teams need very clear communication to avoid gaps.</td></tr><tr><td>Make decisions based on the actual situation, not a fixed plan.</td><td>Reading the game beats sticking rigidly to a plan that doesn't fit.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Reminder Circuit</h4><p>Quick-fire stations lightly revisiting throwing, catching, batting, and fielding from across the unit — a refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations (throwing, catching, batting, fielding), each with its own equipment, spread around the space. Small groups of 3-4 rotate through.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more natural these feel now compared to earlier lessons.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a quick refresher touching on throwing, catching, batting, and fielding from across the unit — not new teaching.</li><li>Each group spends about 90 seconds to 2 minutes at each station, doing a few light reps of the relevant skill using the cues learned earlier in the unit.</li><li>Coach circulates, reminding students of the key cue for each station (e.g. gather-turn-throw, basket hands, level swing, get low and in line).</li><li>Rotate groups to the next station on a signal, keeping the pace brisk.</li><li>Continue until every group has visited all 4 stations.</li><li>Coach reinforces: notice how much more natural these feel now compared to earlier lessons.</li></ul><hr><h4>Small Team Set-Up</h4><p>Teams of 4-5 organise themselves into simple fielding positions and a batting order before a short practice game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-5 gather together with access to a simple diagram or verbal list of fielding positions, before a short practice game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Talk as a team before you start — know your roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach assigns roles.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team decides roles themselves.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each team needs to organise itself into simple fielding positions and decide a batting order before the practice game begins.</li><li>Team discusses together and assigns each player a fielding position, making sure every key area is covered.</li><li>Team also agrees on a simple batting order.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: talk as a team before you start — know your roles.</li><li>Once organised, the team moves into position ready for the mini-game rounds that follow.</li><li>Easier: coach assigns roles directly. Harder: team decides roles themselves with no coach input.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mini Game Rounds</h4><p>Short, rotating mini-games (3-4 overs/turns each) so every team gets several quick reps at batting, fielding, and running.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 2-3 mini-diamonds if space allows, so multiple small teams can play short rounds simultaneously, each with bases, a tee, and soft/modified balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset and refocus for every new mini-round.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer rounds, fewer rotations.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter rounds, quicker rotations.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that teams will play short, rotating mini-games of 3-4 overs/turns each, so everyone gets several quick reps.</li><li>Run a short round of batting, fielding, and running using the team's chosen positions and order.</li><li>After 3-4 turns/overs, call time and rotate teams to a new mini-diamond or swap batting/fielding roles.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: reset and refocus for every new mini-round.</li><li>Repeat for several rounds so every team experiences both batting and fielding multiple times.</li><li>Easier: longer rounds with fewer rotations. Harder: shorter rounds with quicker rotations.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Diamond Teeball</h3><p>Small teams (4-5 players) play a full modified teeball game on a smaller diamond, using all core skills from across the unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a smaller-scale diamond (shorter base paths) for teams of 4-5, with tees, soft/modified balls, bases/markers, and bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that small teams will play a full modified teeball game on the smaller diamond, using all core skills from across the unit.</li><li>Teams take turns batting and fielding using the positions and batting order they organised earlier.</li><li>Play continues with normal teeball rules scaled to the smaller diamond and group size.</li><li>Coach circulates, offering light reminders of key cues from across the unit as needed.</li><li>Rotate batting and fielding roles at a sensible interval (e.g. every 4-5 batters) so every team gets fair time in both roles.</li><li>Keep the pace energetic — this is a chance to put the whole unit's skills together.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What skill from this unit felt most useful in today's small-sided game?</li><li>How did your team organise itself before playing?</li><li>What's tricky about playing a full game compared to practising skills alone?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Understands and plays their role within the small team</li><li>Communicates with teammates during the game</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Keep team sizes small and rotate roles frequently so every student gets varied, meaningful game time regardless of confidence level.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Full Rotation":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full-team fielding rotations</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient with yourself as you adjust to a new position each rotation, and encouraging teammates who are still learning positions you already feel confident in.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, bases/markers, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard full-team game safety; clear rotation signal so no position is left empty during play</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to rotate smoothly through different fielding positions across a full-team game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move to a new fielding position quickly and confidently when rotated</li><li>I can apply the correct positioning basics at whatever position I'm rotated to</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a diamond shape' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/throwing-arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know which position you're moving to before the rotation signal.</td><td>Knowing in advance means a faster, smoother transition.</td></tr><tr><td>Apply the correct positioning basics at whatever spot you rotate to.</td><td>Rotation only works well if you understand every position's job, not just one.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate with the teammate you're swapping with.</td><td>Coordinated swaps prevent gaps during the transition.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Position Rotation Walkthrough</h4><p>The whole fielding team walks through a full rotation cycle without live batting, practising moving smoothly between positions on a signal.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>The whole fielding team (5-6 players) stands in their positions on a mini-diamond, with the coach able to give a clear rotation signal.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know which position you're moving to before the signal, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious rotation pattern.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed, less predictable rotation order.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rotation pattern the team will use (e.g. each player moves one position clockwise) before any live play.</li><li>Team walks through the rotation slowly, without a live batter, moving smoothly between positions on the coach's signal.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: know which position you're moving to before the signal, not after.</li><li>Repeat the walkthrough 2-3 times to build familiarity with the pattern.</li><li>Once smooth, speed up the walkthrough slightly.</li><li>Easier: slower, more obvious rotation pattern. Harder: full-speed, less predictable rotation order.</li></ul><hr><h4>Quick Switch Fielding</h4><p>A light live game where the whole fielding team rotates positions every few plays, practising fast, confident position changes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>A light live small-sided game on a mini-diamond with the fielding team in position, a batter/feeder, and a clear rotation signal available.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate with the teammate you're swapping with.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time before each rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, more frequent rotations.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the whole fielding team will rotate positions every few plays during light live play.</li><li>Play continues with a batter/feeder sending balls into play as normal.</li><li>Every few plays, the coach gives the rotation signal and the whole fielding team moves to their next position confidently and quickly.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: communicate with the teammate you're swapping with.</li><li>Continue play with rotations happening at a steady, predictable interval.</li><li>Easier: longer time before each rotation. Harder: quicker, more frequent rotations.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rotation Under Pressure</h4><p>Full-team fielding practice where rotations happen mid-game at unpredictable moments, testing how smoothly the team adapts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full-team fielding practice on a mini-diamond with a batter/feeder, and the coach ready to call rotations at unpredictable moments.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay ready — the next rotation could come any time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach gives advance warning.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No warning, quick reaction required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that rotations will now happen mid-game at unpredictable moments, testing how smoothly the team adapts.</li><li>Play continues as normal with the batter/feeder sending balls into play.</li><li>The coach calls a rotation signal at an unpredictable point during play, and the team must adapt smoothly without a position being left empty.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: stay ready — the next rotation could come any time.</li><li>Continue play with several unpredictable rotation calls across the activity.</li><li>Easier: coach gives advance warning of an upcoming rotation. Harder: no warning, requiring a quick reaction.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Rotation Teeball</h3><p>Full-team small-sided game where every fielder rotates through multiple positions across the game, rewarded for smooth, confident transitions as much as individual plays.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a full-team small-sided game on a mini-diamond with every fielding position covered and bibs to distinguish teams.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that every fielder will rotate through multiple positions across the game, and the coach is rewarding smooth, confident transitions as much as individual plays.</li><li>Play a small-sided game with normal batting and fielding, using the rotation signal from the earlier activities at set intervals.</li><li>On each rotation signal, the whole fielding team moves smoothly to their next position, communicating with the teammate they're swapping with.</li><li>Coach calls out specific examples of smooth, confident transitions, alongside normal fielding plays.</li><li>Continue with normal batting rotations between teams.</li><li>Make sure every player experiences at least 2-3 different fielding positions by the end of the game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you move confidently into a new position today?</li><li>Which position did you feel most and least confident in, and why?</li><li>How did your team handle rotating under a bit of pressure?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves to new positions quickly and confidently</li><li>Applies basic positioning correctly at multiple spots</li><li>Supports teammates who are adjusting to a new position</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow some students to rotate through fewer, chosen positions initially if the full rotation feels overwhelming, building up to the full cycle.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Batting Order Tactics":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Batting order &amp; team strategy</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your own strengths and your teammates' strengths might fit together into a shared team plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Junior bats, soft/modified balls, batting tees, bases/markers, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard full-team game safety</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to think about batting order and simple team strategy as a group.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can contribute an idea to my team's batting order or strategy discussion</li><li>I can explain a simple reason behind a batting order decision</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a diamond shape' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/throwing-arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Contribute an idea and a reason to your team's discussion.</td><td>Understanding the 'why' behind a decision helps you apply it better in the game.</td></tr><tr><td>Try out your team's plan and be ready to adjust it.</td><td>Plans often need small changes once you see them in action.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate the agreed plan clearly to the whole team.</td><td>A strategy only works if everyone understands and follows it.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Team Strategy Huddle</h4><p>Small teams discuss and decide a simple batting order, giving a reason for at least one placement decision.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 4-5 gather in a huddle away from the playing area, with paper or a simple verbal format to note their batting order.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Listen to every teammate's idea before deciding together.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach offers a simple framework (e.g. 'strongest contact hitters mid-order').</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team builds their own reasoning from scratch.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the team needs to discuss and decide a simple batting order, giving a reason for at least one placement decision.</li><li>Team discusses together who will bat in what order, with each member sharing an opinion.</li><li>The team agrees on at least one specific reason for a placement (e.g. 'strongest contact hitter goes third').</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: listen to every teammate's idea before deciding together.</li><li>Team writes down or remembers their final order ready for the try-out.</li><li>Easier: coach offers a simple framework to guide the discussion. Harder: team builds their own reasoning from scratch.</li></ul><hr><h4>Order Try-Out</h4><p>Teams run a short practice batting sequence in their chosen order, then discuss afterward whether they'd change anything.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams move to a tee or mini-diamond station and run through their chosen batting order in a short practice sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice what worked and what you'd adjust next time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter sequence, more discussion time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer sequence, quicker turnaround to discussion.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the team will run a short practice batting sequence in their chosen order, then discuss afterward.</li><li>Each batter takes their turn in the agreed order, hitting off the tee or in a light live setting.</li><li>Once the sequence is complete, the team gathers briefly to discuss whether they'd change anything about the order.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: notice what worked and what you'd adjust next time.</li><li>Teams can adjust their order based on the discussion if they want to.</li><li>Easier: shorter sequence with more discussion time. Harder: longer sequence with a quicker turnaround to discussion.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Call</h4><p>Each team nominates a rotating 'captain' who leads a two-minute discussion on one simple tactic to try in their next mini-game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each team nominates a rotating 'captain' role, gathering briefly for a two-minute discussion before their next mini-game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests the tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Team chooses their own tactic.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each team nominates a captain who leads a short discussion on one simple tactic to try in their next mini-game.</li><li>The nominated captain leads a two-minute discussion, gathering input but ultimately helping the team settle on one clear, simple tactic.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.</li><li>Team moves into their next mini-game attempting to apply the chosen tactic.</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a different student for the next round.</li><li>Easier: coach suggests the tactic for the captain to present. Harder: team chooses their own tactic entirely.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Strategic Small-Sided Teeball</h3><p>Small teams play a modified game after choosing their own batting order and one simple team tactic, with the coach highlighting moments where the plan paid off.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small-sided game area with tees, bases/markers, and bibs, allowing teams of 4-5 to apply their chosen batting order and tactic.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that teams will play a modified game after choosing their own batting order and one simple team tactic, with the coach highlighting moments where the plan paid off.</li><li>Teams play using the batting order and tactic they agreed on in the earlier activities.</li><li>Coach watches for and calls out specific moments where the team's plan or order worked well.</li><li>Continue play with normal rotations between batting and fielding.</li><li>Briefly check in with teams partway through to see if they want to adjust their tactic.</li><li>Rotate all players through batting and fielding roles across the game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What was one idea your team discussed for your batting order or tactics?</li><li>How did having a simple plan change how your team played?</li><li>What would your team think about differently next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Contributes an idea to the team's strategy discussion</li><li>Can explain a simple reason behind a decision</li><li>Applies part of the agreed plan during the mini-game</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Provide simple discussion prompts or a basic decision framework for teams who need more structure in the strategy conversation.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Full Diamond Match":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full modified games</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand A, B, C · Thinking, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Serving your team throughout a full match — batting, fielding, and base running — with faithful effort in whichever role is needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, batting tees, bases/markers, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard full modified-game safety, soft/modified balls only</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills and tactics together in a full small-sided teeball match.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills and tactics from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can adjust my play based on what the game situation calls for</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a diamond shape' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/throwing-arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply the specific tactical focus called out during play.</td><td>This lesson is about applying tactics live, not performing skills in isolation.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly across batting, fielding, and running.</td><td>A full game relies on continuous team communication.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your play when the first approach isn't working.</td><td>Adapting tactics is what separates good teeball from just repeating a plan.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skills Reminder Circuit</h4><p>Quick-fire stations lightly revisiting throwing, catching, batting, fielding, and base running from across the unit — a refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 5 short stations (throwing, catching, batting, fielding, base running), each with its own equipment, spread around the space. Small groups of 3-4 rotate through.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more natural these feel now compared to early lessons.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a quick refresher touching on throwing, catching, batting, fielding, and base running from across the unit — not new teaching.</li><li>Each group spends about 90 seconds to 2 minutes at each station, doing a few light reps of the relevant skill using the cues learned earlier in the unit.</li><li>Coach circulates, reminding students of the key cue for each station as needed.</li><li>Rotate groups to the next station on a signal, keeping the pace brisk.</li><li>Continue until every group has visited all 5 stations.</li><li>Coach reinforces: notice how much more natural these feel now compared to early lessons.</li></ul><hr><h4>Tactics Call-Out</h4><p>During small games, the coach periodically calls out a tactical focus (e.g. 'placement focus,' 'communication focus,' 'running focus') for players to specifically apply.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games running across 2-3 mini-diamonds, with the coach positioned to observe and call out to all groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that during play, the coach will periodically call out a tactical focus (e.g. 'placement focus,' 'communication focus,' 'running focus') for everyone to apply.</li><li>Play continues as normal small-sided games across the diamonds.</li><li>When the coach calls a tactical focus, players immediately try to apply it in their next few plays (e.g. batters aim for gaps during 'placement focus').</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: adapt immediately when a new focus is called.</li><li>Call a new tactical focus every couple of minutes to keep players adjusting.</li><li>Continue with normal batting and fielding rotations throughout.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Read</h4><p>Each team nominates a rotating captain who reads the game and calls one tactical focus for their team during a short segment.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each team nominates a rotating captain during a short segment of small-sided play, with the captain positioned to observe the game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Base your call on what you're actually seeing in the game.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each team nominates a rotating captain who reads the game and calls one tactical focus for their team during a short segment.</li><li>The captain watches a short segment of play, thinking about what their team could focus on to improve.</li><li>Captain calls one tactical focus for their team (e.g. 'let's cover the gaps better'), based on what they've actually observed.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: base your call on what you're actually seeing in the game.</li><li>Team applies the captain's call for the next few plays.</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a new student for the next segment.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Small-Sided Teeball Match</h3><p>Multiple small teams play a full, timed modified teeball match with all of the unit's skills and tactics live and relevant — batting, fielding, base running, and decision-making all in play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up multiple small teams playing a full, timed modified teeball match across 2-3 diamonds, with bibs, tees, bases/markers, and soft/modified balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a full, timed modified teeball match bringing together all of the unit's skills and tactics — batting, fielding, base running, and decision-making all live and relevant.</li><li>Teams play a timed match using normal rules, applying skills and tactics practised across the whole unit.</li><li>Coach circulates between diamonds, calling out examples of good technique, decisions, and teamwork as they happen.</li><li>Continue play with normal batting, running, and fielding rotations for the set match time.</li><li>Briefly pause for a tactical call-out or captain's read partway through if time allows.</li><li>Wrap up with a quick note on standout moments from across the match.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What tactic or skill from this unit did you use most successfully today?</li><li>How did the game change when a new tactical focus was called?</li><li>What are you most looking forward to for the tournament?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills and tactics from across the unit</li><li>Adjusts play in response to called tactical focuses</li><li>Communicates and reads the game situation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow captains' tactical calls to be simple and concrete ('everyone move in') rather than abstract, so every student can act on them regardless of tactical experience.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Diamond Cup":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Reflecting on the impact this whole unit has had — on your skills, your team, and how you treat others in a game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Soft/modified balls, junior bats, batting tees, bases/markers, cones, bibs (multiple colours for round-robin teams)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard game safety; manage fatigue/hydration across a tournament format, ensure fair rotation so no team is overplayed</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything from this unit — batting, throwing, catching, fielding, base running, and teamwork — in a full mini-tournament.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can play fairly and encouragingly in a competitive setting</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple shape prompts as they jog: 'spread wide,' 'come together,' 'form a line,' 'form a diamond shape' — teams adjust their jogging shape on the call.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several shape calls over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/throwing-arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the block as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills from across the whole unit.</td><td>The tournament brings batting, throwing, catching, fielding, and running together under real pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates and opponents.</td><td>Fair play and encouragement are as much a part of the unit as the physical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on your own improvement is as valuable as the result of any one game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Unit Skills Warm-Up Carousel</h4><p>Quick-fire stations revisiting a taste of the unit's key skills (batting, throwing, catching, fielding, running) — a light refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 5 quick stations touching on batting, throwing, catching, fielding, and running, spread around the space. Small groups rotate through briefly.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a light refresher carousel touching on the unit's key skills — not new teaching.</li><li>Each group spends about 60-90 seconds at each station doing a few light reps.</li><li>Coach circulates with brief, encouraging reminders rather than detailed coaching at this stage.</li><li>Rotate groups to the next station on a signal, keeping the pace energetic and fun.</li><li>Continue until every group has visited all 5 stations.</li><li>Coach reinforces: notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Skill Relay</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining throwing, catching, and a batting strike in sequence — a fun, competitive combination of the whole unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of 3-4 line up at a start cone with a short relay course set up: a throwing target, a catching partner, and a tee to finish with a batting strike.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth handovers between skills.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay sequence: each team member throws at a target, then catches a toss from a partner, then finishes with a batting strike off the tee, before the next teammate goes.</li><li>First team member completes the throw-catch-strike sequence, then tags the next teammate to start.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: smooth handovers between skills.</li><li>Continue until every team member has completed the sequence.</li><li>Run 1-2 more rounds if time allows, mixing up the order.</li><li>Coach calls out finishing times/order for some friendly competitive energy, alongside a head-to-head mirror race against another team if space allows.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Challenge</h4><p>Each team nominates a captain who sets one focus for their team's tournament games (e.g. 'let's focus on calling our bases clearly today') — light strategic ownership before the tournament begins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each team nominates a captain before the tournament begins, gathering briefly as a team to hear the captain's chosen focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Give every captain a genuine, valued choice.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that each team nominates a captain who sets one focus for their team's tournament games.</li><li>Captain thinks of one simple, genuine focus for their team (e.g. 'let's focus on calling our bases clearly today') and shares it with the team.</li><li>Coach reinforces the cue: give every captain a genuine, valued choice.</li><li>Team briefly discusses how they'll apply the captain's focus during the tournament.</li><li>Move straight into the round-robin tournament with the focus in mind.</li><li>Rotate the captain role for future tournament rounds if there's time for more than one.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Multiple small teams rotate through short small-sided teeball matches against each other across 2-3 diamonds, scoring simply (runs or a fair-play points system). Every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 2-3 mini-diamonds for a round-robin format, with multiple small teams, bibs in different colours for each team, and full equipment (tees, bats, balls, bases/markers) at each diamond.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: teams rotate through short small-sided matches against different opponents across the available diamonds.</li><li>Run each mini-match for a set short time (e.g. 5-6 minutes), keeping score simply by runs or a fair-play points system agreed at the start.</li><li>At the end of each mini-match, rotate teams to a new diamond and a new opponent.</li><li>Coach circulates between diamonds, managing fatigue and hydration and ensuring fair rotation so no team is overplayed.</li><li>Continue through as many rounds as time allows, keeping a simple running record of results if desired.</li><li>Finish with a quick celebration of effort and skills shown across the whole unit, not just final results.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging play look like in today's tournament?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other sports or games?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills from across the unit (not just one)</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure round-robin teams to be balanced/mixed-ability; consider a simple fair-play recognition (not just a 'winning team') to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Tennis":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Racquet & Ball Control (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Shake Hands With Your Racquet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Racquet familiarisation &amp; grip (forehand/backhand grip)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself while a new skill feels awkward, and encouraging classmates whose attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 junior/Hot Shots racquet per student, cones for boundaries, spot markers</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Racquets carried head-down/by the throat when walking near others; a clear ‘swing space’ (arm's length plus racquet length) from anyone else before swinging</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to hold the racquet correctly for forehand and backhand, and to carry and control it safely while moving.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can show a correct forehand ‘shake hands’ grip and swap smoothly to a backhand grip</li><li>I can carry and control my racquet safely while moving around others</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bounce &amp; Balance Relay</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small relay teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a turnaround cone 8-10m away per team. Each runner carries a racquet, and one ball waits at the turnaround cone.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner jogs out to the turnaround cone, bounces the ball up on the racquet a set number of times (e.g. 5), then balances it still on the racquet strings to carry back before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team jogs out, completes the bounces, then carries the balanced ball back without dropping it.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues rather than restarting.</li><li>Once tagged, the next runner sets off immediately with a fresh set of bounces at the turnaround cone.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — this is about raising heart rates and getting comfortable moving with the ball on the racquet, not testing skill.</li><li>Run the relay for the full 5 minutes, rotating running order each round so it stays fair.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their racquet down beside them and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, forearms/wrists (important after racquet work), quads, and hamstrings — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>‘Shake hands’ with the racquet handle so the strings face sideways, like shaking someone's hand (forehand grip).</td><td>This grip lets the racquet face meet the ball squarely without twisting the wrist.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotate the hand slightly so the knuckles sit on top of the handle for backhand, or use two hands together for a two-handed backhand.</td><td>A stable, consistent grip is the foundation every other stroke sits on.</td></tr><tr><td>Hold the racquet with a relaxed hand — like holding a bird, not a hammer — gripping firmly only just before contact.</td><td>A tense grip the whole time tires the arm and wobbles the racquet face; relaxed-then-firm gives control and power together.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Racquet Freeze Parade</h4><p>Students jog around a marked area carrying their racquet correctly; on ‘freeze’ they show the grip the teacher calls (forehand or backhand) and hold a ready position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out one open space per 8-10 students, roughly 15m x 15m, with 4 cones at the corners. Every student needs their own racquet — no balls yet. Stand somewhere you can see the whole group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check your grip by feel, not by looking down.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower pace, only forehand grip called.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick grip swaps called in sequence (forehand-backhand-forehand).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Show the two grips before students move: forehand 'shake hands' grip (palm flat against the strings like shaking hands with the racquet), and backhand grip (a small quarter-turn of the hand from forehand).</li><li>Explain 'freeze' — on your call, everyone stops immediately, shows the named grip, and holds a ready position (knees soft, racquet up in front of the body).</li><li>Send students jogging around the space carrying their racquet correctly (head up, by the throat, away from others).</li><li>Call 'freeze — forehand!' or 'freeze — backhand!' every 8-10 seconds; walk around checking grips by feel, not by sight, and give a thumbs-up or a quick fix to individuals.</li><li>Coach the key message: check your grip by feel, not by looking down at your hand.</li><li>For 'harder', call quick sequences ('forehand — backhand — forehand') so students swap grips on consecutive freezes without a jog in between.</li></ul><hr><h4>Racquet Face Balance Walk</h4><p>Balance a ball on the racquet strings while walking a pathway of cones, using forehand- and backhand-side carries.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Lay out a pathway of 6-8 cones in a loose zig-zag line per group of 4-5 students, spaced about 1.5m apart. Each student needs a racquet and a low-compression ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Soft hands, slightly bent elbow, eyes on the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stand still and balance first before adding walking.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a change of direction or a light jog.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (count consecutive steps without dropping)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate balancing the ball still on the racquet strings first, holding the racquet flat like a tray.</li><li>Have students place the ball on the racquet face and stand still, getting the balance right before adding any movement.</li><li>Once balanced, students walk the cone pathway keeping the ball on the strings, switching to carry it on the 'backhand side' (racquet turned slightly) for part of the path.</li><li>If the ball drops, students pick it up calmly where it stopped and continue from that point — no restarting the whole pathway.</li><li>Coach the key message: soft hands, a slightly bent elbow, and eyes on the ball, not on your feet.</li><li>For 'harder', add a change of direction or a light jog partway along the path.</li></ul><hr><h4>Grip Swap Tag</h4><p>Partners jog together; on a signal, both swap grip (forehand to backhand or vice versa) as fast as possible and show it to their partner, who checks it's correct for a point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up across the same open space, each pair with one racquet each (no ball needed). Pairs spread out with at least 2m between neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Feel the shape change in your hand, don't just twist the racquet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Partner calls the grip out loud in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No verbal cue — react to a visual card held up by partner.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation (checker/mover swap)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners jog gently side by side, staying close enough to see each other's hands.</li><li>On your signal ('swap!'), both partners change grip as fast as possible — forehand to backhand or backhand to forehand — and hold it out to show their partner.</li><li>The partner checks the grip is correct and calls 'good' or gently helps fix it; award a point for a correct, fast swap.</li><li>Jog on for another 10-15 seconds before the next signal, and keep rotating which partner checks first.</li><li>Coach the key message: feel the shape change in your hand, don't just twist the racquet without thinking.</li><li>For 'harder', drop the verbal signal and instead hold up a visual card (F or B) that partners must react to instead.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Grip &amp; Go Relay</h3><p>Small teams relay: each runner sprints to a marked spot, shows the named grip correctly (checked by a teammate ‘coach’), balances the ball on the racquet back to the team, then tags the next runner. Combines today's two skills — grip and racquet control — under light time pressure and team encouragement.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5, each team behind a start cone. Place one marked spot 10-12m away per team with a low-compression ball waiting there. Racquets stay with the runners.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay before starting: one runner at a time sprints to the marked spot, shows the grip you call out (checked by the next teammate in line acting as 'coach'), balances the ball on the racquet strings, and carries it back without dropping it.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team sprints out, shows the correct grip to the waiting 'coach' teammate, then picks up and balances the ball back to the team.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues — no restart from the beginning.</li><li>The relay is complete for that runner once they tag the next teammate, who repeats the sequence with a new grip call.</li><li>Rotate the 'coach' role each turn so every student gets to check a grip as well as run it.</li><li>Keep the energy light and encouraging — celebrate teams for clean, controlled runs, not just speed.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did the ‘shake hands’ grip feel like once you got used to it?</li><li>Who in your team showed a really clear, correct grip today — what did you notice?</li><li>What was tricky about swapping grips quickly, and how did you work it out?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a recognisable ‘shake hands’ (forehand) grip without needing reminders</li><li>Can swap to a backhand-side grip when asked</li><li>Carries and controls the racquet safely around others</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a two-handed grip for both forehand and backhand if that feels more stable early on; some students may need a lighter/shorter racquet — prioritise grip correctness over perfect technique on day one.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Bounce Boss":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bouncing &amp; balancing the ball on the racquet (control)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing exactly what's happening in your grip, swing, or footwork, and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 racquet + 1 low-compression ball per student, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Enough personal space so a rolling ball doesn't cause a collision; balls collected calmly rather than chased through others' spaces</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to control a ball by bouncing it up on the racquet face and bouncing it down onto the ground, using a soft, controlled touch.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can bounce the ball up on my racquet face several times in a row without losing control</li><li>I can bounce the ball down onto the ground and back up using my racquet</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bounce &amp; Balance Relay</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small relay teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a turnaround cone 8-10m away per team. Each runner carries a racquet, and one ball waits at the turnaround cone.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner jogs out to the turnaround cone, bounces the ball up on the racquet a set number of times (e.g. 5), then balances it still on the racquet strings to carry back before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team jogs out, completes the bounces, then carries the balanced ball back without dropping it.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues rather than restarting.</li><li>Once tagged, the next runner sets off immediately with a fresh set of bounces at the turnaround cone.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — this is about raising heart rates and getting comfortable moving with the ball on the racquet, not testing skill.</li><li>Run the relay for the full 5 minutes, rotating running order each round so it stays fair.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their racquet down beside them and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, forearms/wrists (important after racquet work), quads, and hamstrings — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Keep the racquet face flat and steady, moving it slightly upward just as the ball touches it.</td><td>A flat, steady face sends the ball straight back up; tilting it sends the ball flying off sideways.</td></tr><tr><td>Use small, soft ‘give’ movements from the wrist and elbow rather than big swings.</td><td>Small controlled movements are what let you bounce the ball many times in a row instead of smashing it away.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your eyes on the ball the whole time, not on your feet or racquet.</td><td>In tennis you react to where the ball actually is — this habit starts here.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Up-Bounce Count</h4><p>Individually bounce the ball up off the racquet face as many times in a row as possible; track a personal best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student needs their own racquet and low-compression ball, spread out with at least an arm's length plus racquet length from anyone else across the open space. No grid needed — students find their own patch.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small taps, flat racquet face.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger/softer ball; a ground bounce is allowed between racquet taps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must also take three steps forward without stopping the bounce.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock / personal-best streak</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate bouncing the ball up off a flat racquet face with small, controlled taps, keeping the ball close to chest height.</li><li>Students start bouncing individually, counting each consecutive tap out loud or silently in their head.</li><li>If the ball drops or gets away, the student picks it up, resets, and starts their count again from zero.</li><li>After 60-90 seconds, ask students to remember their best consecutive count for the session.</li><li>Coach the key message: small taps, flat racquet face — don't chase the ball with a big swing.</li><li>For 'harder', once a student reaches a comfortable streak, add the challenge of taking three steps forward without losing the bounce.</li></ul><hr><h4>Down-Bounce Dribble</h4><p>Like a basketball dribble but with the racquet: bounce the ball down onto the ground and back up while walking a pathway of cones.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Lay out a pathway of 6-8 cones per group of 4-5 students, spaced about 1.5m apart in a loose line. Each student needs a racquet and a ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Push the ball, don't slap it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Stand still first before adding movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Dribble while changing direction or speed on a call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Show the down-bounce action: pushing the ball onto the ground with the racquet face and letting it bounce back up, like a basketball dribble.</li><li>Students start bouncing on the spot to get the rhythm and height consistent before moving.</li><li>Once controlled, students walk the cone pathway while continuing to down-bounce, weaving between cones.</li><li>If control is lost, students collect the ball calmly and restart from where they stopped, not from the beginning of the path.</li><li>Coach the key message: push the ball, don't slap it — a gentle, controlled push keeps the bounce predictable.</li><li>For 'harder', call out direction or speed changes partway through the pathway that students must react to mid-dribble.</li></ul><hr><h4>Balance &amp; Freeze</h4><p>Partners take turns bouncing the ball up on the racquet while walking; on ‘freeze’ they must balance the ball still on the racquet face without it rolling off, while their partner checks.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up across the open space, each pair sharing one racquet and one ball, with clear space between neighbouring pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Slightly cup the racquet face the instant you freeze.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer freeze calls, slower pace.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Freeze while standing on one foot.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Movement penalty/reward</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner walks slowly while bouncing the ball up on the racquet; the other watches and is ready to call 'freeze'.</li><li>On 'freeze', the mover must stop and balance the ball still on the racquet face without it rolling off, holding it for a count of three.</li><li>The watching partner checks the ball stays still and gives feedback — 'held it' or 'try again' — before swapping roles.</li><li>Repeat several rounds, alternating who moves and who calls freeze, so everyone practises both bouncing and balancing.</li><li>Coach the key message: slightly cup the racquet face the instant you freeze, rather than keeping it perfectly flat.</li><li>For 'harder', the mover must freeze while standing on one foot, adding a balance challenge on top of the ball control.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Bounce Relay Challenge</h3><p>Small teams relay a set distance, each runner keeping the ball under control with up-bounces before tagging the next teammate; team with the fewest drops (or fastest clean run) wins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a finish cone 12-15m away. Each runner carries a racquet and starts with a ball at the start cone.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner travels the set distance keeping the ball under control with up-bounces on the racquet before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team sets off, bouncing the ball continuously up off the racquet as they move toward the finish cone and back.</li><li>If the ball drops, the runner stops, picks it up, resets the bounce on the spot, and continues from there rather than going back to the start.</li><li>Once a runner returns and tags the next teammate, that student sets off immediately with a fresh bounce.</li><li>Track either the fewest total drops per team or the fastest clean run, depending on what will motivate your class most.</li><li>Coach the key message: rushing causes drops — a slightly slower, controlled bounce finishes faster overall than a fast, wild one.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you keep the ball under control today?</li><li>Who found a good rhythm with their bouncing — what did their racquet look like?</li><li>What did you do differently after you dropped the ball a few times?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a flat, steady racquet face rather than a tilted/slapping motion</li><li>Strings together several controlled bounces in a row</li><li>Eyes track the ball rather than the feet</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Let students who are struggling use a bigger, softer ball, or bounce off the ground between racquet touches; celebrate personal-best streaks rather than comparing students to each other.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Perfect Swing":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Forehand groundstroke technique (off a feed/self-drop)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after a partner's success as much as your own — feeding, rallying, and encouraging in a way that helps them succeed too.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 racquet + balls per pair, cones to mark a hitting line</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Feeders and hitters spaced well apart from neighbouring pairs; check the swing zone is clear before any swing</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the technique of a forehand groundstroke — turning side-on, swinging low-to-high, and making contact out in front of the body.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can turn side-on and swing low-to-high to hit a self-dropped or fed ball</li><li>I can make contact with the ball out in front of my body, not beside or behind it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bounce &amp; Balance Relay</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small relay teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a turnaround cone 8-10m away per team. Each runner carries a racquet, and one ball waits at the turnaround cone.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner jogs out to the turnaround cone, bounces the ball up on the racquet a set number of times (e.g. 5), then balances it still on the racquet strings to carry back before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team jogs out, completes the bounces, then carries the balanced ball back without dropping it.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues rather than restarting.</li><li>Once tagged, the next runner sets off immediately with a fresh set of bounces at the turnaround cone.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — this is about raising heart rates and getting comfortable moving with the ball on the racquet, not testing skill.</li><li>Run the relay for the full 5 minutes, rotating running order each round so it stays fair.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their racquet down beside them and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, forearms/wrists (important after racquet work), quads, and hamstrings — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Turn side-on to the ball with your non-hitting shoulder pointing toward where you want the ball to go.</td><td>A side-on turn lets you swing across your body with power and accuracy, instead of just poking at the ball facing forward.</td></tr><tr><td>Swing the racquet from low to high, like scooping upward, brushing up the back of the ball.</td><td>A low-to-high path naturally lifts the ball up and over the net with control instead of into the net or way over.</td></tr><tr><td>Make contact with the ball out in front of your body, roughly level with your front hip.</td><td>Hitting the ball out in front gives you the best combination of power and control — behind your body you lose both.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Self-Drop Target</h4><p>Each student self-drops and forehands the ball toward a large marked zone; count clean hits into the zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a large target zone (roughly 3m x 3m, using 4 cones) on the ground about 4-5m in front of each hitting line. Pairs or small groups share one hitting line, each student with a racquet and several balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Turn side-on before you drop it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zone, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target zone, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the self-drop forehand: turn side-on to the target, drop the ball from waist height in front of the body, and swing low-to-high to meet it after one bounce.</li><li>Students take turns self-dropping and hitting forehands, aiming to land the ball inside the marked zone.</li><li>Count clean hits into the zone out of a set number of attempts (e.g. 10 balls), collecting balls together between turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: turn side-on before you drop it — the turn happens before the ball leaves your hand.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the target zone and move the hitting line further back to increase the challenge.</li><li>Rotate who is hitting and who is collecting/watching for technique every set of attempts.</li></ul><hr><h4>Feeder &amp; Hitter</h4><p>One partner gently underarm feeds/bounces the ball, the other forehands it back toward a target; swap roles regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up along a hitting line, spaced at least 3m from neighbouring pairs, with one racquet and a supply of balls per pair. Mark a simple target zone on the ground for the hitter to aim at.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Good feeds get good hits — feed with care.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder stands very close with a slow, gentle feed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Feeder varies feed distance/height slightly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner (the feeder) gently underarm feeds or bounces the ball toward the hitter's forehand side.</li><li>The hitter turns side-on, tracks the bounce, and forehands the ball back toward the marked target.</li><li>After each hit, the feeder collects any loose balls and resets before the next feed — keep the pace calm, not rushed.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 feeds so both partners get equal hitting time.</li><li>Coach the key message: good feeds get good hits — feed with care and a soft, predictable toss.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder starts slightly varying the distance or height of the feed instead of keeping it identical each time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Swing Mirror</h4><p>Partners face each other and mirror a slow-motion forehand swing (no ball), checking each other's side-on turn and low-to-high path, then try it with a self-drop.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up facing each other, about 2m apart, spread across the space with no ball needed for the first part — each student still holds their own racquet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Match your partner's turn before you match their swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Call out each part of the swing step by step.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full-speed continuous shadow swings.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners face each other and one leads a slow-motion forehand swing: turn side-on, racquet back low, swing low-to-high.</li><li>The other partner mirrors the movement at the same time, while both check each other's side-on turn and swing path.</li><li>Swap who leads every 3-4 repetitions so both partners get to lead and to check.</li><li>Once the shape looks consistent, students try the same swing with an actual self-drop and ball.</li><li>Coach the key message: match your partner's turn before you match their swing — the turn is the foundation.</li><li>For 'harder', partners move to full-speed continuous shadow swings instead of slow motion, staying in time with each other.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Forehand Target Battle</h3><p>Small groups take turns self-dropping and hitting forehands at a shared set of scoring zones (close zone = 1pt, far zone = 3pt); technique — side-on turn, low-to-high swing — is what makes the harder zones reachable.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 2-3 scoring zones per group of 4 students, using cones: a close zone worth 1 point (about 4m out) and a further zone worth 3 points (about 6-7m out). Each group shares a hitting line and a supply of balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring before starting: a self-drop forehand landing in the close zone scores 1 point, and one landing in the far zone scores 3 points.</li><li>Students take turns self-dropping and hitting forehands at either zone, choosing their target before they drop the ball.</li><li>Track each student's running total on a simple tally as the group rotates through turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: the side-on turn and low-to-high swing from today's other games are exactly what make the harder, further zone reachable.</li><li>Rotate through several rounds so everyone gets multiple attempts at both zones.</li><li>Finish by asking students to compare their close-zone accuracy to their far-zone accuracy, celebrating improvement over the lesson rather than just the final score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did turning side-on do for your swing?</li><li>Who fed the ball really well for their partner today — what made it a good feed?</li><li>What's the difference between hitting the ball out in front versus beside you?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Turns side-on before swinging</li><li>Swings low-to-high with a visible upward brush on the ball</li><li>Contacts the ball out in front of the body</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow closer self-drop/feed distances and bigger target zones for students building confidence; pair students so feeders are calm and patient, rotating pairs if a combination isn't working well.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Target Ace":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Forehand accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how today's skill connects to the bigger game of tennis — a small piece that matters once it's put together with everything else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, cones/hoops or marked zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between hitting pairs; check the target/swing zone is clear before hitting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to aim a forehand groundstroke at a chosen target, using our follow-through and racquet face to control direction.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can aim my forehand toward a target on the left or right, not just anywhere</li><li>I can adjust my follow-through direction to help control where the ball goes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bounce &amp; Balance Relay</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small relay teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a turnaround cone 8-10m away per team. Each runner carries a racquet, and one ball waits at the turnaround cone.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner jogs out to the turnaround cone, bounces the ball up on the racquet a set number of times (e.g. 5), then balances it still on the racquet strings to carry back before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team jogs out, completes the bounces, then carries the balanced ball back without dropping it.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues rather than restarting.</li><li>Once tagged, the next runner sets off immediately with a fresh set of bounces at the turnaround cone.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — this is about raising heart rates and getting comfortable moving with the ball on the racquet, not testing skill.</li><li>Run the relay for the full 5 minutes, rotating running order each round so it stays fair.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their racquet down beside them and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, forearms/wrists (important after racquet work), quads, and hamstrings — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Choose a specific target — a hoop, cone gap, or zone — before you hit, not after.</td><td>Deciding where to aim before you swing is what turns technique into accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Point your follow-through (where your racquet finishes) toward your target.</td><td>The path your racquet finishes on is a strong clue to where the ball actually went — a controlled follow-through means a controlled shot.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust the angle of your racquet face slightly left or right depending on which side of the target you're missing.</td><td>Small face adjustments are what fine-tune direction once your basic swing is already working.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Zone Target Forehand</h4><p>Self-drop forehands aimed at three marked zones (left, middle, right) worth different points; call the zone before hitting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark three adjacent zones (left, middle, right) about 2m wide each, roughly 5m from the hitting line, using cones or hoops. Students work individually or in pairs with a racquet and a supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide, then swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Two wide zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Three narrower zones, called by a partner rather than chosen by the hitter.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives (harder zone = bonus points)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the points: assign different values to each zone (e.g. middle = 1 point, left/right = 2 points) since the outer zones require more precise aim.</li><li>Before each hit, the student calls out which zone they're aiming for.</li><li>The student self-drops and forehands the ball, aiming to land it in the called zone.</li><li>Tally points across a set number of attempts (e.g. 8-10 balls), collecting between turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide, then swing — pick your target before you drop the ball, not mid-swing.</li><li>For 'harder', a partner calls the zone just before each hit instead of the hitter choosing it themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Hoop Hunter</h4><p>A partner places or moves a hoop around a marked area; the hitter forehands from a self-drop to land the ball in or near the hoop, then roles swap.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up, each pair with one hoop, one racquet, and a supply of balls, spread out with clear space between pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim before you drop the ball, not after.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Hoop stationary and close.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Hoop moved between hits, further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (target moves)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner places the hoop somewhere in the marked hitting area, roughly 4-6m from the hitting line.</li><li>The hitter self-drops a forehand, aiming to land the ball in or near the hoop.</li><li>The placing partner retrieves the ball and either leaves the hoop or moves it slightly before the next attempt.</li><li>Swap roles after 5-6 attempts so both partners get to hit and to place the hoop.</li><li>Coach the key message: aim before you drop the ball, not after — pick your spot on the hoop first.</li><li>For 'harder', the hoop is moved further away and repositioned between every single hit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Streak Target Challenge</h4><p>Consecutive successful hits to the same called target before a miss resets the streak — a friendly challenge against your own best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Use the same marked target zone from earlier in the lesson (roughly 3m x 3m), 4-5m from the hitting line. Students work individually with a racquet and a supply of balls, tracking their own streak.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>After a miss, reset calmly and try the same target again.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Alternate between left and right targets each hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The student calls a target (e.g. 'left zone') and attempts to hit it with consecutive self-drop forehands.</li><li>Each successful hit into the called target adds one to their personal streak count.</li><li>A miss resets the streak to zero, and the student immediately tries again at the same target.</li><li>Give students 2-3 minutes to try and beat their own best streak from earlier attempts.</li><li>Coach the key message: after a miss, reset calmly and try the same target again — don't chase a bigger target out of frustration.</li><li>For 'harder', alternate between left and right targets on every single hit instead of staying on one target.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Forehand Precision Cup</h3><p>Small groups rotate through a mini target-course of forehand zones worth escalating points; team totals build across rounds, rewarding precision, not just power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a mini target-course per group of 4 students using cones: 2-3 zones at increasing distance from the hitting line, each worth escalating points (e.g. 1, 2, 3). Each group shares a supply of balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the course: students rotate through hitting at each zone in turn, self-dropping a forehand and aiming for the called or chosen zone.</li><li>Each group member takes a turn hitting a set number of balls (e.g. 3) at each zone before rotating to the next zone.</li><li>Track a running team total across all rounds, adding up points from every zone.</li><li>Coach the key message: precision beats power here — a controlled hit into the harder zone is worth more than a hard, wild swing.</li><li>Rotate hitting order each round so the same student isn't always going first.</li><li>Finish by comparing team totals and celebrating the team with the steadiest accuracy across rounds, not just the highest single score.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you actually hit the target you were aiming for?</li><li>Who improved their accuracy during the lesson — what did they change?</li><li>How does watching your follow-through help you understand your own shot?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Selects a target before swinging</li><li>Follow-through points toward the intended target</li><li>Makes small adjustments after a miss rather than repeating the same error</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Widen target zones or shorten the distance for students still building basic forehand technique; let confident hitters challenge themselves with narrower zones or longer distances.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Flip Side":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Backhand groundstroke technique</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself while a new skill feels awkward, and encouraging classmates whose attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Feeders and hitters spaced well apart from neighbouring pairs; check the swing zone is clear before any swing</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the technique of a backhand groundstroke — turning side-on the opposite way to forehand, and swinging low-to-high.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can turn side-on (the opposite way to forehand) for a backhand</li><li>I can swing low-to-high to hit a self-dropped or fed ball on my backhand side</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bounce &amp; Balance Relay</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into small relay teams of 4-5 behind a start cone, with a turnaround cone 8-10m away per team. Each runner carries a racquet, and one ball waits at the turnaround cone.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the relay: each runner jogs out to the turnaround cone, bounces the ball up on the racquet a set number of times (e.g. 5), then balances it still on the racquet strings to carry back before tagging the next teammate.</li><li>On 'go', the first runner in each team jogs out, completes the bounces, then carries the balanced ball back without dropping it.</li><li>If the ball drops on the way back, the runner stops, rebalances it on the spot, and continues rather than restarting.</li><li>Once tagged, the next runner sets off immediately with a fresh set of bounces at the turnaround cone.</li><li>Keep the pace light and continuous — this is about raising heart rates and getting comfortable moving with the ball on the racquet, not testing skill.</li><li>Run the relay for the full 5 minutes, rotating running order each round so it stays fair.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Walk &amp; Reach</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students set their racquet down beside them and walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — shoulders, forearms/wrists (important after racquet work), quads, and hamstrings — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, hold, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Turn side-on the opposite way to your forehand, with your hitting-side shoulder pointing toward the ball.</td><td>Backhand uses a mirror-image turn to forehand — getting the turn right matters even more here because it's less natural.</td></tr><tr><td>Use one hand (with a backhand grip) or two hands together if that feels more stable, and swing low-to-high.</td><td>Two hands can give extra stability and confidence while one-handed technique and balance develop.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your swing path low-to-high, brushing up the back/side of the ball, the same as forehand.</td><td>The same lifting path applies to both sides — it's the grip and turn that change, not the overall shape of the swing.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Backhand Self-Drop Target</h4><p>Same as a forehand self-drop target game, but on the backhand side — self-drop and backhand toward a large marked zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a large target zone (roughly 3m x 3m using 4 cones) about 4-5m in front of each hitting line, same as the forehand version. Pairs or small groups share a hitting line, each with a racquet and a supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Turn your hitting shoulder to the ball first.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zone, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target zone, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the self-drop backhand: turn the hitting shoulder toward the target, drop the ball in front of the body, and swing low-to-high after one bounce.</li><li>Students take turns self-dropping and hitting backhands, aiming to land the ball inside the marked zone.</li><li>Count clean hits into the zone out of a set number of attempts (e.g. 10 balls), collecting balls together between turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: turn your hitting shoulder to the ball first — the turn matters even more on the backhand side.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the target zone and move the hitting line further back.</li><li>Rotate who is hitting and who is watching/collecting every set of attempts.</li></ul><hr><h4>Feeder &amp; Hitter — Backhand</h4><p>One partner gently feeds to the backhand side, the other returns it toward a target; swap roles regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up along a hitting line, spaced at least 3m from neighbouring pairs, with one racquet and a supply of balls per pair. Mark a simple target zone for the hitter to aim at.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Two hands together if one hand feels shaky.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder stands close with a slow, gentle feed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Feeder varies feed distance/height slightly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner gently feeds or bounces the ball toward the hitter's backhand side.</li><li>The hitter turns their hitting shoulder toward the ball, tracks the bounce, and backhands it toward the marked target.</li><li>The feeder collects loose balls and resets calmly before the next feed.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 feeds so both partners get equal hitting time.</li><li>Coach the key message: two hands together if one hand feels shaky — a two-handed backhand is completely fine at this stage.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder starts slightly varying the distance or height of the feed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Forehand or Backhand?</h4><p>The feeder places the feed slightly to one side or the other; the hitter must read it and choose the correct grip/turn before swinging.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up along a hitting line, spaced at least 3m apart, with one racquet and a supply of balls per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide your side as early as possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Feeder calls the side out loud.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No call — read it from the feed alone.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls / decision-making</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder places each feed slightly to one side of the hitter's body without saying which stroke to use.</li><li>The hitter must read where the ball is going, choose the correct grip and turn, and play the appropriate stroke — forehand or backhand.</li><li>Give brief feedback after each hit on whether the read and stroke choice matched the feed.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 feeds so both partners practise reading and feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide your side as early as possible, ideally as soon as the ball leaves the feeder's hand.</li><li>For 'harder', remove the option of the feeder calling the side out loud — the hitter must read it purely from the feed itself.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Backhand Target Battle</h3><p>Small groups take turns self-dropping and hitting backhands at a shared set of scoring zones worth different points, rewarding a growing, controlled backhand turn.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 2-3 scoring zones per group of 4 students, using cones, at varying distances from the hitting line, worth different points (e.g. close = 1 point, far = 3 points). Each group shares a hitting line and a supply of balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: a self-drop backhand landing in the close zone scores fewer points than one landing in the far zone.</li><li>Students take turns self-dropping and hitting backhands at either zone, choosing their target before dropping the ball.</li><li>Track each student's running total on a simple tally as the group rotates through turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: a growing, controlled backhand turn is what makes today's harder zones reachable, just like the forehand a few lessons ago.</li><li>Rotate through several rounds so everyone gets multiple attempts at both zones.</li><li>Finish by asking students to notice one thing that felt easier on their backhand today compared to earlier in the block.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What felt different about the backhand turn compared to forehand?</li><li>Who used two hands well today, or one hand confidently — what worked for them?</li><li>What helped your backhand feel less awkward as the lesson went on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Turns side-on correctly for backhand</li><li>Uses one or two hands with a stable grip</li><li>Swings low-to-high with contact out in front</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Fully encourage a two-handed backhand as an equally valid technique, not a fallback; allow extra self-drop reps before adding a partner feed for less confident students.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Strokes, Footwork & Rallying (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Backhand Bullseye":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Backhand accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing exactly what's happening in your grip, swing, or footwork, and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, cones/hoops or marked zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between hitting pairs; check the target/swing zone is clear before hitting</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to aim a backhand groundstroke at a chosen target, using our follow-through to control direction.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can aim my backhand toward a target on the left or right, not just anywhere</li><li>I can adjust my follow-through direction to help control where the ball goes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Feed &amp; Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, each student with their own racquet. Enough room between pairs that feeds don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently feeding the ball back and forth to each other's hands (an underarm toss, not a hit).</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'jog swap' means both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue feeding with their new partner.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick feet' — partners add a few small side-shuffle steps between each feed instead of standing still.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the arm and legs together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing swaps and quick-feet calls throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Racquets and balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch, plus a gentle forearm/wrist stretch each.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Choose a specific target before you hit, not after.</td><td>Deciding where to aim before you swing is what turns technique into accuracy, on either side.</td></tr><tr><td>Point your follow-through toward your target.</td><td>A controlled, aimed follow-through is a strong sign of a controlled, aimed shot.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your racquet face angle slightly if you're consistently missing to one side.</td><td>Small face adjustments fine-tune direction once your basic backhand turn and swing are already working.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Zone Target Backhand</h4><p>Self-drop backhands aimed at three marked zones (left, middle, right) worth different points; call the zone before hitting.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark three adjacent zones (left, middle, right) about 2m wide each, roughly 5m from the hitting line, using cones or hoops. Students work individually or in pairs with a racquet and a supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Turn first, decide second, swing third.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Two wide zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Three narrower zones, called by a partner.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives (harder zone = bonus points)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the points: assign different values to each zone (e.g. middle = 1 point, left/right = 2 points).</li><li>Before each hit, the student calls out which zone they're aiming for.</li><li>The student self-drops and backhands the ball, aiming to land it in the called zone.</li><li>Tally points across a set number of attempts (e.g. 8-10 balls), collecting between turns.</li><li>Coach the key message: turn first, decide second, swing third — the sequence matters on the trickier backhand side.</li><li>For 'harder', a partner calls the zone just before each hit instead of the hitter choosing it themselves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Hoop Hunter — Backhand</h4><p>A partner places or moves a hoop; the hitter backhands from a self-drop to land the ball in or near the hoop, then roles swap.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up, each pair with one hoop, one racquet, and a supply of balls, spread out with clear space between pairs.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Follow through toward the hoop.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Hoop stationary and close.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Hoop moved between hits, further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (target moves)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner places the hoop somewhere in the marked hitting area, roughly 4-6m from the hitting line.</li><li>The hitter self-drops a backhand, aiming to land the ball in or near the hoop.</li><li>The placing partner retrieves the ball and either leaves the hoop or moves it slightly before the next attempt.</li><li>Swap roles after 5-6 attempts so both partners get to hit and to place the hoop.</li><li>Coach the key message: follow through toward the hoop — let the swing finish pointing at the target.</li><li>For 'harder', the hoop is moved further away and repositioned between every single hit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Streak Target Challenge — Backhand</h4><p>Consecutive successful backhand hits to the same called target before a miss resets the streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Use a marked target zone (roughly 3m x 3m), 4-5m from the hitting line. Students work individually with a racquet and a supply of balls, tracking their own streak.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset calmly after a miss and go again.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Alternate between left and right targets each hit.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The student calls a target and attempts to hit it with consecutive self-drop backhands.</li><li>Each successful hit into the called target adds one to their personal streak count.</li><li>A miss resets the streak to zero, and the student immediately tries again at the same target.</li><li>Give students 2-3 minutes to try and beat their own best streak from earlier attempts.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset calmly after a miss and go again — frustration makes the backhand tighten up.</li><li>For 'harder', alternate between left and right targets on every single hit.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Backhand Precision Cup</h3><p>Small groups rotate through a mini target-course of backhand zones worth escalating points; team totals build across rounds, rewarding precision on the trickier side.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a mini target-course per group of 4 students using cones: 2-3 backhand zones at increasing distance from the hitting line, each worth escalating points. Each group shares a supply of balls.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the course: students rotate through hitting backhands at each zone in turn.</li><li>Each group member takes a turn hitting a set number of balls (e.g. 3) at each zone before rotating to the next zone.</li><li>Track a running team total across all rounds, adding up points from every zone.</li><li>Coach the key message: the trickier backhand zones reward the same patience and turn you've been building all week.</li><li>Rotate hitting order each round so the same student isn't always going first.</li><li>Finish by comparing team totals and celebrating steady improvement in backhand accuracy across the two backhand lessons.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you aim your backhand where you wanted it to go?</li><li>Who improved their backhand accuracy during the lesson — what did they change?</li><li>Is backhand accuracy trickier than forehand for you? Why might that be?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Selects a target before swinging on the backhand side</li><li>Follow-through points toward the intended target</li><li>Makes small adjustments after a miss rather than repeating the same error</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Widen target zones or shorten the distance for students still building basic backhand technique; let confident hitters challenge themselves with narrower zones or longer distances.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Switch Hitter":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining forehand + backhand in a rally</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking, Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after a partner's success as much as your own — feeding, rallying, and encouraging in a way that helps them succeed too.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/rope-and-cone ‘nets’, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between rallying pairs; low nets/ropes checked to be secure and not a trip hazard</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to decide quickly between forehand and backhand depending on where the ball arrives, and to combine both inside a short rally.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can decide quickly whether to use forehand or backhand based on where the ball is</li><li>I can keep a short rally going by combining both strokes</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Feed &amp; Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, each student with their own racquet. Enough room between pairs that feeds don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently feeding the ball back and forth to each other's hands (an underarm toss, not a hit).</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'jog swap' means both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue feeding with their new partner.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick feet' — partners add a few small side-shuffle steps between each feed instead of standing still.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the arm and legs together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing swaps and quick-feet calls throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Racquets and balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch, plus a gentle forearm/wrist stretch each.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Read early which side the ball is coming to (forehand or backhand side of your body).</td><td>The earlier you decide, the more time you have to turn and set up properly.</td></tr><tr><td>Turn side-on on the correct side as soon as you've decided.</td><td>Committing to the turn early is what makes the actual stroke technique possible under time pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to a ready position — racquet up, light feet — between every shot.</td><td>A rally is a series of separate decisions; resetting means you're ready for whichever side the next ball comes to.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Left-Right Feed Choice</h4><p>A partner feeds alternately or randomly to the forehand or backhand side; the hitter must choose and use the correct stroke each time.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up along a hitting line, spaced at least 3m apart, with one racquet and a supply of balls per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide early, turn early.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable alternating pattern.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Random order.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that the feeder will send the ball to either the forehand or backhand side, and the hitter must react with the correct stroke.</li><li>Start with the feeder alternating sides in a predictable pattern (forehand, backhand, forehand...) so hitters can build confidence with the choice.</li><li>The hitter turns and plays the appropriate stroke each time, aiming back toward the feeder or a simple target.</li><li>Swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both partners practise choosing and feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: decide early, turn early — waiting to see the bounce is often too late.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder switches to a fully random order of forehand and backhand feeds.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cooperative Rally Count</h4><p>Partners rally back and forth over a low net/rope, counting consecutive controlled hits that combine both sides.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net or a rope-and-cone 'net' between pairs, with pairs standing about 3-4m apart on each side. Each pair shares one ball and both have a racquet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim to a spot your partner can actually reach.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target area, closer together.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further apart, deliberately alternating sides.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the goal: keep a cooperative rally going for as many consecutive controlled hits as possible, using both forehand and backhand as needed.</li><li>One partner starts with a gentle self-feed or underarm feed over the net to begin the rally.</li><li>Both partners aim their shots to a spot their partner can comfortably reach, rather than trying to win the point.</li><li>Count consecutive hits out loud together; if the rally breaks down, quickly restart with a gentle feed and begin counting again.</li><li>Coach the key message: aim to a spot your partner can actually reach — a rally is a shared achievement, not a contest.</li><li>For 'harder', partners stand further apart and deliberately alternate aiming to each other's forehand and backhand sides.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mirror Ready Position</h4><p>No ball — a partner calls or points forehand or backhand, and the student must snap into the correct ready turn as fast as possible.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up facing each other, about 2m apart, each holding their own racquet — no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Turn your shoulders, not just your feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, predictable calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fast, random calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner calls or points 'forehand' or 'backhand' without warning.</li><li>The other partner reacts as fast as possible, snapping into the correct ready turn for that stroke.</li><li>The calling partner checks the turn looks correct (shoulders turned, racquet ready) and gives quick feedback.</li><li>Swap roles every 8-10 calls so both partners practise reacting and calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: turn your shoulders, not just your feet — the shoulder turn is what really shows the stroke choice.</li><li>For 'harder', the calling partner speeds up and randomises the calls so there's less time to react.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Combo Rally Challenge</h3><p>Small groups/pairs try to build the longest cooperative rally combining forehand and backhand over a mini net, with the class tracking its best consecutive-hit total.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a mini net (or rope-and-cones) per pair or small group of 4, with cones marking a simple rally area on each side, roughly 4m x 4m.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: pairs try to build the longest cooperative rally combining forehand and backhand shots over the mini net.</li><li>One partner starts each rally with a gentle underarm feed to get play going.</li><li>Both partners rally back and forth, choosing forehand or backhand depending on where the ball comes to them.</li><li>Track each pair's best consecutive-hit count on a simple class tally as rallies restart after breakdowns.</li><li>Coach the key message: today's whole lesson has been about choosing the right stroke quickly — that's exactly what keeps a rally alive.</li><li>Celebrate the class's overall best rally total, not just individual pairs, to keep the focus cooperative.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you decide which side to hit from quickly?</li><li>Who kept a great rally going with their partner — what made it work?</li><li>What did resetting your ready position do for your next shot?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Reads which side the ball is arriving on early</li><li>Turns to the correct side without hesitation</li><li>Resets to a ready position between shots</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a bounce or two before requiring a stroke for less confident students; adjust net height and distance so rallies are genuinely achievable for every pair.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Quick Feet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Footwork — split step &amp; moving to the ball</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how today's skill connects to the bigger game of tennis — a small piece that matters once it's put together with everything else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, cones for movement patterns</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear space for movement drills; no loose equipment left underfoot in sprint/shuffle areas</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use a split step timed with our opponent's or feeder's contact, then move efficiently to the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can perform a small split-step hop timed with my feeder's or opponent's contact</li><li>I can move quickly and stay balanced to reach the ball, not just stretch with my arm</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Feed &amp; Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, each student with their own racquet. Enough room between pairs that feeds don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently feeding the ball back and forth to each other's hands (an underarm toss, not a hit).</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'jog swap' means both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue feeding with their new partner.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick feet' — partners add a few small side-shuffle steps between each feed instead of standing still.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the arm and legs together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing swaps and quick-feet calls throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Racquets and balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch, plus a gentle forearm/wrist stretch each.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Do a small, light hop (split step), landing on the balls of your feet just as the feeder/opponent hits the ball.</td><td>The split step puts you in a balanced, ready position so you can push off quickly in any direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Push off and move your feet to get your whole body behind or beside the ball, not just stretch your arm.</td><td>Good footwork means you arrive with time to set up your swing properly instead of lunging and mishitting.</td></tr><tr><td>Take small adjustment steps as you get close to the ball to find the right distance.</td><td>Big final steps overshoot the ball; small steps let you fine-tune your position right before contact.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Split-Step Echo</h4><p>A partner claps or mimes a hit; students split-step in response as fast as possible, checking for a small hop rather than a big jump.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up facing each other, about 3m apart, spread across the space. No ball or racquet needed for this drill — just movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Light and quick, not high.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, predictable timing.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Random timing, followed by a direction call.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the split-step: a small, light hop landing with feet shoulder-width apart, timed just as an opponent is about to hit the ball.</li><li>One partner claps or mimes a hitting action; the other reacts by split-stepping the instant they see or hear it.</li><li>The calling partner checks the hop is small and light, not a big jump, and gives quick feedback.</li><li>Swap roles every 8-10 reps so both partners practise the timing and the calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: light and quick, not high — a split-step is about being ready to move, not about height.</li><li>For 'harder', the calling partner adds a direction call (left/right) immediately after the clap, so the responder split-steps then moves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Cone Scatter Sprint</h4><p>Cones are scattered in a grid; the teacher calls a cone number or colour, and students split-step then move to that cone in a balanced hitting position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Scatter 8-10 numbered or coloured cones across a grid roughly 10m x 10m per group of 5-6 students.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small last steps to fine-tune your spot.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer cones, close together.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More cones, further apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that students will split-step on your call, then move to the named cone and arrive in a balanced, ready hitting position.</li><li>Call a cone number or colour; students split-step first, then move quickly but under control to that cone.</li><li>Check that students arrive with small, balanced final steps rather than a long final stride that throws off their balance.</li><li>Call a new cone every 8-12 seconds, keeping the movement continuous.</li><li>Coach the key message: small last steps to fine-tune your spot, rather than one big final stride.</li><li>For 'harder', use more cones spread further apart to increase the distance and decision-making required.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Rally Movement</h4><p>No ball — a partner points left/right/short/long, and the student split-steps and moves to mime a stroke in that position, resetting between each call.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up facing each other, about 2-3m apart, each holding their own racquet — no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your ready position every single time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer directions, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick sequence of directions.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror (compare speed/balance)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner points left, right, short, or long; the other split-steps and moves in that direction to mime a stroke.</li><li>After each mimed shot, the mover resets back to a central ready position before the next call.</li><li>The pointing partner checks the reset happens every time, not just on some reps.</li><li>Swap roles every 8-10 calls so both partners practise moving and calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset your ready position every single time — this habit is what makes real rallies possible later.</li><li>For 'harder', the pointing partner gives a quick sequence of 2-3 directions in a row before allowing a reset.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Movement Mini-Rally</h3><p>A real rally over a low net where the focus is purely on split-step and movement quality rather than stroke power; small teams earn bonus points for visibly good footwork, not just for winning the point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net or rope-and-cones per small team of 4, with a simple rally area marked on each side.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's mini-rally is scored on movement quality, not just on winning the point — teachers or peer observers watch for good split-steps and balanced footwork.</li><li>One player starts each rally with a gentle feed over the net.</li><li>Players rally, focusing on splitting and moving to the ball early rather than reaching or lunging at the last second.</li><li>Award bonus points during the rally for visibly good footwork moments, calling them out as they happen ('nice split-step!').</li><li>Coach the key message: the stroke can be simple — it's the movement underneath it that this lesson has been building.</li><li>Rotate players through server/rally/observer roles so everyone gets a turn being watched for footwork.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did the split-step help you do?</li><li>Who moved really well to the ball today — what did their feet look like?</li><li>What's the difference between reaching with your arm and moving your whole body?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Shows a small hop timed with the opponent's/feeder's contact</li><li>Moves the whole body to the ball rather than just reaching</li><li>Uses small adjustment steps near the ball</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow extra time or slower feeds for students building footwork confidence; use a verbal cue (‘hop… now move!’) for students who need an extra prompt.</p>"},"Lesson 9: Keep It Alive":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rallying with a partner (cooperative, low net/modified ball)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself while a new skill feels awkward, and encouraging classmates whose attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, modified/low nets or rope-and-cones, cones for court boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; low nets/ropes checked secure; call ‘mine’ if two players reach for the same ball</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine grip, stroke technique, and footwork to sustain a cooperative rally over a low net.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hit the ball back over a low net so my partner can return it</li><li>I can move and reset between shots to keep a rally going</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Feed &amp; Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, each student with their own racquet. Enough room between pairs that feeds don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently feeding the ball back and forth to each other's hands (an underarm toss, not a hit).</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'jog swap' means both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue feeding with their new partner.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick feet' — partners add a few small side-shuffle steps between each feed instead of standing still.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the arm and legs together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing swaps and quick-feet calls throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Racquets and balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch, plus a gentle forearm/wrist stretch each.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Aim your shots to your partner's side, not just anywhere over the net.</td><td>A rally only continues if the ball is genuinely hittable by your partner — accuracy is part of cooperation.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a controlled, moderate swing rather than hitting as hard as possible.</td><td>Power without control ends rallies quickly; a rally rewards consistency over power.</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to a ready position after every shot, watching your partner's contact to split-step and move.</td><td>This lesson combines everything so far — grip, stroke, footwork — into one continuous skill.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Count the Rally</h4><p>Pairs count consecutive controlled hits over a low net/marked boundary, trying to beat their own best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net or rope-and-cones per pair, with cones marking a rally area roughly 5m x 4m per side. Each pair shares one ball and both have a racquet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Consistency beats power here.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger court/target area, closer together.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller area, further apart.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner starts with a gentle underarm feed or self-feed over the net to begin.</li><li>Both partners rally back and forth, counting consecutive controlled hits out loud together.</li><li>If the rally breaks down, restart quickly with a gentle feed and begin the count again from zero.</li><li>Give pairs 2-3 attempts and ask them to try to beat their own best count each time.</li><li>Coach the key message: consistency beats power here — a soft, controlled shot that lands in beats a hard shot that doesn't.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the marked area and move pairs further apart.</li></ul><hr><h4>Target Zone Rally</h4><p>Pairs rally but must alternate aiming to a marked left/right zone on their partner's side.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a left and right zone on each side of the net using cones, within the existing rally area from the previous game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim to help the rally continue, not to win the point.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>One wide zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two narrow zones, alternating.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that pairs must alternate aiming their shots to the left zone, then the right zone, on their partner's side.</li><li>Start the rally with a gentle feed, then both partners continue alternating zones with each hit.</li><li>If a shot misses the called zone but the rally is still playable, keep going — the goal is the pattern, not perfection.</li><li>Coach the key message: aim to help the rally continue, not to win the point — a rally is a shared success.</li><li>Count consecutive on-pattern hits as a simple team score.</li><li>For 'harder', narrow to two smaller zones that must still be strictly alternated.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shrinking Court Rally</h4><p>The marked rally area gradually narrows as a pair's consecutive count increases, raising the challenge automatically.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a starting rally area of about 5m x 5m per pair using cones, with a few spare cones nearby to shrink the area during play.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smaller target, smaller swing — stay controlled.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shrink slowly, reset to a bigger area often.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shrink faster.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking/growing space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Pairs begin rallying inside the full-size marked area, counting consecutive hits as they go.</li><li>Every time the pair reaches a set milestone (e.g. 5 consecutive hits), move the boundary cones slightly inward to shrink the area.</li><li>Continue the rally inside the smaller area, still counting consecutively from where they left off.</li><li>If the rally breaks down, reset to a slightly bigger area than where it failed, rather than all the way back to the start.</li><li>Coach the key message: smaller target, smaller swing — stay controlled as the space shrinks rather than swinging harder.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the area faster, after fewer consecutive hits.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Class Rally League</h3><p>All pairs rally simultaneously for a set time, tracking their best consecutive count; pairs may swap partners once to rally with someone new, celebrating cooperative totals across the whole class rather than head-to-head competition.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up as many low nets or rope-and-cone dividers as needed so every pair in the class can rally simultaneously, each with a simple marked area.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that all pairs will rally at the same time for a set period (e.g. 5-6 minutes), tracking their own best consecutive count.</li><li>Pairs begin rallying, using a gentle feed to restart whenever their rally breaks down.</li><li>Partway through, call a 'partner swap' where each student moves to rally with someone new for a few minutes.</li><li>After the swap, students return to a partner (original or new) to try and beat the class's earlier totals.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is about the whole class's cooperative total, not head-to-head competition between pairs.</li><li>Finish by asking a few pairs to share their best consecutive count and celebrate the class's combined effort.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you and your partner do to help each other keep the rally going?</li><li>What was your best rally count, and what worked?</li><li>How is rallying together different from just hitting the ball as hard as you can?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Aims shots to be genuinely hittable by their partner</li><li>Uses a controlled swing rather than maximum power</li><li>Resets and moves between shots</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow bounces, bigger target zones, or closer distances for pairs still building consistency; pair thoughtfully so both partners can genuinely contribute to the rally.</p>"},"Lesson 10: First Strike":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Serving technique (underarm/modified serve)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing exactly what's happening in your grip, swing, or footwork, and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets, cones/markers for service boxes</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Always serve away from other groups; wait for a clear ‘go ahead’ before serving toward any shared space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct technique for an underarm/modified serve to start a point.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can toss or drop the ball to a consistent spot before serving</li><li>I can serve underarm with a controlled swing that clears the net into the service area</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Feed &amp; Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space (roughly 4-5m apart per pair), one ball per pair, each student with their own racquet. Enough room between pairs that feeds don't cross.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners stand facing each other and begin gently feeding the ball back and forth to each other's hands (an underarm toss, not a hit).</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call a change: 'jog swap' means both partners jog to swap positions with another nearby pair, then continue feeding with their new partner.</li><li>Occasionally call 'quick feet' — partners add a few small side-shuffle steps between each feed instead of standing still.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up the arm and legs together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing swaps and quick-feet calls throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Racquets and balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's ankle behind them for a quad stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch, plus a gentle forearm/wrist stretch each.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use a consistent, gentle toss or self-drop of the ball to roughly the same spot every time.</td><td>A consistent toss/drop is what makes the rest of the serve repeatable — an inconsistent toss means an inconsistent serve no matter how good your swing is.</td></tr><tr><td>Swing the racquet underarm with a smooth, low-to-high path, contacting the ball out in front of your body.</td><td>Same principle as groundstrokes — an out-front, lifting contact point gives control and clears the net safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward your target, the marked service area.</td><td>Following through toward the target keeps your swing purposeful and helps direct the serve rather than just hitting and hoping.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Toss &amp; Catch</h4><p>Practise the toss/drop alone or with a partner checking consistency, catching the ball instead of hitting it first to isolate the skill.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out individually or in pairs across the space, each with a racquet and a ball. No net needed yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Same spot, every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Use a self-drop instead of a toss.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Toss to a marked target spot in the air.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation (feedback partner)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate a consistent toss or self-drop: releasing the ball to roughly the same spot in front of the body each time.</li><li>Students practise tossing the ball up (or self-dropping it) and simply catching it again, without hitting it, to isolate the toss from the swing.</li><li>If working in pairs, one partner watches and checks whether the toss lands in the same spot each time, giving quick feedback.</li><li>Repeat 8-10 times, aiming for a consistent, repeatable toss rather than speed.</li><li>Coach the key message: same spot, every time — consistency in the toss is what makes the whole serve work later.</li><li>For 'harder', toss to a specific marked target spot in the air rather than just anywhere comfortable.</li></ul><hr><h4>Underarm Serve Target</h4><p>Serve underarm into a marked service box, counting clean serves in.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a service box (roughly 3m x 4m) using cones on the opposite side of a low net, about 6-8m from the server. Each student has a racquet and a supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Low-to-high swing, contact out in front.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger box, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller box, further distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the underarm serve: toss or self-drop the ball, then swing low-to-high, contacting the ball out in front of the body, aiming into the marked box.</li><li>Students take turns serving underarm into the box, counting clean serves in out of a set number of attempts (e.g. 8-10).</li><li>Collect balls together between turns to keep the group organised and safe.</li><li>Coach the key message: low-to-high swing, contact out in front — not overhead and not behind the body.</li><li>For 'harder', make the box smaller or move the server further back.</li><li>Remind students to always check the box/net area is clear before serving toward it.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve &amp; Rally Starter</h4><p>Serve to a partner who returns it, turning the serve into the start of a short rally — the serve doesn't have to be a ‘winner,’ just a good start.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up on opposite sides of a low net, each pair with one racquet each and a shared supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A controlled serve beats a hard, wild one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Serve from closer, partner returns gently.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Serve from full distance into a target.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner serves underarm toward the other, aiming for a controlled, in-play serve rather than a hard one.</li><li>The receiving partner returns the serve gently, and the pair continues the exchange for a few shots if it stays in play.</li><li>Remind students the serve doesn't need to be a winner — its job is just to start a good rally.</li><li>Swap serving roles every 4-5 serves so both partners practise starting the rally.</li><li>Coach the key message: a controlled serve beats a hard, wild one — control gets the point started.</li><li>For 'harder', the server moves back to full serving distance and aims for a marked target.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Serve Ladder Challenge</h3><p>Small groups take turns serving into progressively smaller or further zones for increasing points, building the serve toward starting real points in later lessons.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 2-3 service zones per group of 4 students at progressively smaller size or further distance, worth increasing points, on the far side of a low net.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the ladder: each zone is worth more points than the last because it's smaller or further away.</li><li>Students take turns serving underarm, choosing which zone to aim for and calling it before they serve.</li><li>Track a running total per student or per team across several rounds of serves.</li><li>Coach the key message: today's serve is the foundation for starting real points later in the unit — control now pays off then.</li><li>Rotate serving order each round so turns stay fair.</li><li>Finish by highlighting a few students who improved their zone accuracy across the rounds, not just the highest scorers.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you toss or drop the ball consistently?</li><li>Who served really well today — what did you notice about their routine?</li><li>Why does a consistent toss matter more than a powerful swing?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a consistent toss/drop location</li><li>Shows a low-to-high swing path with contact out in front</li><li>Serve clears the net into the target area</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a self-drop instead of a toss for anyone finding tossing tricky; shrink the serving distance for students building confidence.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Net Play, Reading the Ball & Decision-Making (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Corner Server":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Serving accuracy/placement</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after a partner's success as much as your own — feeding, rallying, and encouraging in a way that helps them succeed too.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets, cones/markers for zoned service boxes</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Always serve away from other groups; wait for a clear ‘go ahead’ before serving toward any shared space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to place serves into specific zones of the service area, not just anywhere inside it.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can aim my serve to a called zone — left, right, short, or deep</li><li>I can adjust my toss or swing slightly to change where the serve lands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Split-Step Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students. Each student needs their own racquet, no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the split-step shuffle before starting: a small, light hop landing with feet apart, ready to move in any direction.</li><li>Students spread out and begin light jogging on the spot.</li><li>Call 'split!' every 8-10 seconds — students perform one split-step hop, then immediately shuffle two steps in a direction you call (left, right, forward, back).</li><li>Keep the calls varied and the pace playful, focusing on quick, light feet rather than big movements.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, gradually mixing calls faster as students get comfortable with the pattern.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room to stretch and breathe comfortably.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a short guided breathing moment: 3 slow breaths in through the nose and out through the mouth, standing tall with eyes closed if comfortable.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and quiet, using this time to settle the group before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one final big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Choose a specific zone to aim for before you toss the ball.</td><td>Placement starts with a decision, the same as any other shot in tennis.</td></tr><tr><td>Shift your toss slightly left or right, or slightly further forward or back, to change your serve's direction and depth.</td><td>Small, deliberate toss adjustments are one of the most reliable ways to control where a serve lands.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your normal, controlled swing rather than swinging harder to reach a tricky zone.</td><td>Placement comes from where you aim and toss, not from extra power — swinging harder usually reduces accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Zone Serve Challenge</h4><p>Serve into one of several marked zones inside the service box, worth different points depending on difficulty.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark 3-4 zones of different sizes inside a service box using cones, roughly 6-8m from the server, behind a low net.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick your zone, then set your toss to match.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, bigger zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More, smaller zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the point values: smaller or more awkward zones are worth more points than larger, central ones.</li><li>Students take turns serving underarm, choosing a zone to aim for and calling it before serving.</li><li>Track points across a set number of serves (e.g. 8-10) per student.</li><li>Coach the key message: pick your zone, then set your toss to match — the toss should support the target, not just be automatic.</li><li>For 'harder', reduce the number of zones and make them smaller so there's less margin for error.</li><li>Always check the box and net area is clear before each serve.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve &amp; Call</h4><p>A partner calls a target zone just before the serve, and the server must aim there rather than choosing themselves.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up, one serving and one calling, on either side of the service box with a low net between them.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your routine even when the target changes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Same zone called several times in a row.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>A different zone called every serve.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The calling partner names a target zone just before the server tosses the ball.</li><li>The server must aim for that zone rather than choosing their own target.</li><li>The calling partner checks whether the serve landed in the called zone and gives quick feedback.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 serves so both partners practise serving under a called target.</li><li>Coach the key message: trust your routine even when the target changes — don't rush the toss just because the target is new.</li><li>For 'harder', the caller changes the target zone on every single serve rather than repeating one for a few serves.</li></ul><hr><h4>Streak Serve Target</h4><p>Consecutive successful serves into the same called zone before a miss resets the streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Use one marked zone within the service box, roughly 2m x 2m, with the server positioned at full serving distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset calmly after a miss and go again.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zone, or alternating zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The student calls their target zone and attempts consecutive successful serves into it.</li><li>Each successful serve adds to their personal streak; a miss resets the count to zero.</li><li>Students immediately try again at the same target after a miss, without a long pause.</li><li>Give students 2-3 minutes to try and beat their earlier best streak.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset calmly after a miss and go again — tension in the toss causes more misses.</li><li>For 'harder', use a smaller zone, or alternate between two zones instead of staying on one.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Serve Placement Showdown</h3><p>Small groups compete serving to escalating-value zones across several rounds, with encouragement for effort on the hardest, smallest zones as well as for successful serves.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up escalating-value zones (e.g. three sizes, each worth different points) inside the service box for each group of 4 students, behind a low net.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the competition: groups compete across several rounds, serving toward zones of their choice for points.</li><li>Students take turns serving, calling their target zone before each serve.</li><li>Track a running group total across rounds, adding points from successful serves.</li><li>Coach the key message: today celebrates good effort at the hardest zones just as much as clean successful serves.</li><li>Give specific encouragement when a student attempts (even if they miss) the smallest, hardest zone.</li><li>Finish with a quick class share of which zone felt hardest and why, connecting it back to toss consistency.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you aim your serve to a specific zone?</li><li>Who kept trying for a tricky zone even after missing — how did you encourage them?</li><li>What's one small adjustment that changed where your serve landed?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Aims serves to a specific zone rather than just ‘in’</li><li>Makes small toss/swing adjustments to change direction</li><li>Stays composed and keeps trying after a missed serve</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger zones or closer distances for students still building serve consistency; celebrate genuine attempts at harder zones, not just successes.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Net Ninja":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Volley technique (at the net)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how today's skill connects to the bigger game of tennis — a small piece that matters once it's put together with everything else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Feeds toward the net kept gentle and controlled; clear spacing between pairs working at the net</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to use a short, controlled ‘punch’ volley to return a ball at the net before it bounces.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hit a fed ball out of the air near the net using a short punching motion, not a big swing</li><li>I can hold a ready position — racquet up — at the net</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Split-Step Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students. Each student needs their own racquet, no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the split-step shuffle before starting: a small, light hop landing with feet apart, ready to move in any direction.</li><li>Students spread out and begin light jogging on the spot.</li><li>Call 'split!' every 8-10 seconds — students perform one split-step hop, then immediately shuffle two steps in a direction you call (left, right, forward, back).</li><li>Keep the calls varied and the pace playful, focusing on quick, light feet rather than big movements.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, gradually mixing calls faster as students get comfortable with the pattern.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room to stretch and breathe comfortably.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a short guided breathing moment: 3 slow breaths in through the nose and out through the mouth, standing tall with eyes closed if comfortable.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and quiet, using this time to settle the group before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one final big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Hold the racquet up in front of you at the net in a ready position, with no backswing.</td><td>A volley is a short punch, not a full swing — preparing without a big backswing keeps your reaction time fast.</td></tr><tr><td>Step toward the ball and punch or block it firmly using a short forward motion.</td><td>Because the ball is close and fast at the net, a short controlled punch is more reliable than a big swing.</td></tr><tr><td>Angle the racquet face slightly to direct the ball down and away rather than straight back.</td><td>A slight downward angle keeps volleys from flying long, using the net's height rather than the ball's speed.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Volley Prep Reps</h4><p>A close, gentle feed to the net; the student returns it with a short punch volley, no backswing.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up on either side of a low net, standing close to the net (roughly 1.5-2m back), each with a racquet and a shared supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Racquet up, short punch, no big swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Very close, slow feed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Feed varied slightly left/right.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the volley: racquet held up in front of the body, a short punch forward with no backswing, contacting the ball before it bounces.</li><li>One partner gently feeds the ball directly at the volleyer from close range.</li><li>The volleyer returns it with a short punch action, focusing on technique rather than power or placement.</li><li>Swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both partners practise the volley.</li><li>Coach the key message: racquet up, short punch, no big swing — the volley is a block, not a full stroke.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder varies the feed slightly left or right of the volleyer.</li></ul><hr><h4>Volley Target Zone</h4><p>Volley fed balls toward a marked zone on the other side of the net.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a target zone on the far side of the net, roughly 3m x 3m, with the volleyer standing close to the net on the near side.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Angle the face down slightly to keep it in.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zone, closer net.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zone, standard net.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>A partner feeds the ball to the volleyer from close range.</li><li>The volleyer punches a short volley aimed at the marked zone on the other side.</li><li>Count clean hits into the zone across a set number of feeds (e.g. 8-10).</li><li>Rotate the feeder and volleyer roles regularly so both get equal reps.</li><li>Coach the key message: angle the face down slightly to keep the volley in, since volleys naturally want to fly long.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the target zone and use a standard net height instead of a lowered one.</li></ul><hr><h4>Two-Touch Volley Rally</h4><p>Partners volley to each other at the net without letting the ball bounce, counting consecutive volleys.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up close to a low net (roughly 1.5-2m back on each side), each with a racquet, sharing one ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay light on your feet between volleys.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target area, softer ball speed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target area, quicker exchanges.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Partners volley the ball back and forth to each other without letting it bounce, starting with a gentle feed.</li><li>Both students count consecutive volleys out loud together.</li><li>If the rally breaks down, restart quickly with a gentle feed and count again from zero.</li><li>Encourage light, quick foot adjustments between volleys rather than standing flat-footed.</li><li>Coach the key message: stay light on your feet between volleys so you're ready to move to the next one.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the target area and pick up the pace of the exchanges.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Net Point Finisher</h3><p>Small-sided mini games where points start with a gentle feed and must be finished at the net with a volley, rewarding clean technique under a little game pressure.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a small marked mini-court on each side, roughly 4m x 4m, close enough to the net that points naturally finish at the volley.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: each point starts with a gentle feed and must be finished at the net with a volley to count.</li><li>One player feeds gently to start the point; both players may move to the net to contest the finish.</li><li>Points are won by a clean volley finish rather than a groundstroke winner from the baseline.</li><li>Coach the key message: today's whole focus — racquet up, short punch — is exactly what wins these points.</li><li>Rotate feeding duties every 2-3 points so both players get to start and finish points.</li><li>Keep score lightly (first to a small target like 5) so the focus stays on technique, not pressure.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did the short ‘punch’ motion feel like compared to a full groundstroke swing?</li><li>Who held a really strong ready position at the net today?</li><li>Why might a big swing not work well so close to the net?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a short punching motion rather than a full swing</li><li>Holds racquet up in a ready position between volleys</li><li>Angles the racquet face to control the volley's direction</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use gentler, closer feeds and bigger target zones for students building net confidence; reassure students that mishit volleys are a normal, expected part of learning this new skill.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Full Point Player":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining groundstrokes + volley in a point</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself while a new skill feels awkward, and encouraging classmates whose attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear communication when moving forward into a partner's space near the net; standard spacing between pairs</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to move forward from a baseline rally and finish a point with a volley at the net.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can recognise a good moment to move forward to the net</li><li>I can combine a groundstroke rally with a volley finish in the same point</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Split-Step Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students. Each student needs their own racquet, no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the split-step shuffle before starting: a small, light hop landing with feet apart, ready to move in any direction.</li><li>Students spread out and begin light jogging on the spot.</li><li>Call 'split!' every 8-10 seconds — students perform one split-step hop, then immediately shuffle two steps in a direction you call (left, right, forward, back).</li><li>Keep the calls varied and the pace playful, focusing on quick, light feet rather than big movements.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, gradually mixing calls faster as students get comfortable with the pattern.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room to stretch and breathe comfortably.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a short guided breathing moment: 3 slow breaths in through the nose and out through the mouth, standing tall with eyes closed if comfortable.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and quiet, using this time to settle the group before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one final big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Recognise a short or high ball during the rally as a good moment to move forward.</td><td>Choosing the right moment is what separates a purposeful approach from a random, risky one.</td></tr><tr><td>Move forward with balanced, controlled steps rather than a rushed sprint.</td><td>Arriving balanced means you're ready to volley properly instead of just reaching.</td></tr><tr><td>Split-step as you arrive near the net, then finish with a short punch volley.</td><td>This links today's skill directly back to your split-step and volley technique from earlier lessons.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Approach &amp; Finish</h4><p>A feed starts a short groundstroke exchange, then on a call (or after a set number of shots) the student moves forward and finishes the point with a volley.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up on either side of a low net, with a marked baseline roughly 6-7m back and a forward 'approach' line about 2m from the net.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move forward with purpose, not by accident.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fixed number of groundstrokes before moving forward.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Move forward whenever a shot feels short/easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives (finishing at net = bonus)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>One partner feeds the first ball from the baseline to start a short groundstroke exchange.</li><li>After a set number of groundstrokes (e.g. 2-3) or on a call from the feeder, the hitter moves forward to the approach line.</li><li>The feeder sends one more ball for the hitter to finish with a volley at the net.</li><li>Swap roles every few points so both partners practise both the groundstroke phase and the net finish.</li><li>Coach the key message: move forward with purpose, not by accident — the move forward is a decision, not a drift.</li><li>For 'harder', the hitter must judge for themselves when a shot is short/easy enough to move forward, rather than following a fixed count.</li></ul><hr><h4>Baseline to Net Relay</h4><p>Students hit a groundstroke from the baseline, sprint forward to a marked line, then volley a second feed to finish.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a baseline and a line about 4-5m closer to the net per pair, with a low net between the two sides. Each pair has one racquet each and a supply of balls.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Split-step as you arrive at the line.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter sprint distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Longer sprint distance, quicker second feed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The student starts at the baseline and hits a groundstroke off a feed from their partner.</li><li>Immediately after hitting, the student sprints forward to the marked line closer to the net.</li><li>The partner sends a second feed as the student arrives, who finishes it with a volley.</li><li>Swap roles after each attempt so both partners get to hit-and-move and to feed.</li><li>Coach the key message: split-step as you arrive at the line, don't just run straight into the volley.</li><li>For 'harder', increase the sprint distance and speed up the second feed.</li></ul><hr><h4>Point Construction Mini-Games</h4><p>Small-sided points that must include at least one groundstroke and one volley to score, encouraging the full pattern rather than staying on the baseline.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court on each side, roughly 5m x 5m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the moment to move in.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>More time/shots allowed before the volley requirement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fewer shots allowed before it.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: a point only counts if it includes at least one groundstroke and one volley from the scoring player.</li><li>Points start with a gentle feed or serve and play out normally from there.</li><li>If a point ends with only groundstrokes (no one came to the net), it doesn't count and is replayed.</li><li>Coach the key message: look for the moment to move in — after a shot that lands short or high is often the cue.</li><li>Rotate feeding/serving duties regularly so play stays fair.</li><li>For 'harder', reduce the number of groundstroke shots allowed before the volley requirement kicks in.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Construct the Point</h3><p>Small-sided games where points that combine a baseline rally with a net finish are specifically celebrated and lightly rewarded, encouraging students to build a full point pattern rather than just trading groundstrokes.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair or small group with a marked mini-court, roughly 5m x 6m on each side.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that points combining a baseline rally with a finish at the net are specifically celebrated during play, alongside normal scoring.</li><li>Points start with a gentle feed or serve and are played out normally.</li><li>When a student successfully builds a rally and finishes it at the net, call it out and award a small bonus point.</li><li>Coach the key message: today is about building the full point pattern, not staying safely on the baseline the whole time.</li><li>Rotate players through serving/feeding roles regularly.</li><li>Finish by asking a couple of students to describe a point where they successfully moved forward and finished it.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you recognise a good moment to move forward?</li><li>Who combined a rally and a net finish really well today?</li><li>What's tricky about switching from a groundstroke mindset to a volley mindset mid-point?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Recognises an opportunity to move forward during a rally</li><li>Moves forward with balanced footwork rather than a rushed sprint</li><li>Combines a groundstroke exchange with a volley finish</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow extra shots or a slower approach for students still building confidence moving forward; keep the ‘finish’ requirement flexible (a controlled volley attempt counts, whether or not it's a winner).</p>"},"Lesson 14: Ball Reader":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading the ball's bounce &amp; trajectory</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing exactly what's happening in your grip, swing, or footwork, and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; feeders vary trajectory gently and with control, not by hitting hard</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to predict where a ball will bounce or arrive based on its flight, and to move into position early.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move to the right spot before the ball bounces, not after</li><li>I can judge whether a ball will be high, low, short, or deep</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Split-Step Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students. Each student needs their own racquet, no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the split-step shuffle before starting: a small, light hop landing with feet apart, ready to move in any direction.</li><li>Students spread out and begin light jogging on the spot.</li><li>Call 'split!' every 8-10 seconds — students perform one split-step hop, then immediately shuffle two steps in a direction you call (left, right, forward, back).</li><li>Keep the calls varied and the pace playful, focusing on quick, light feet rather than big movements.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, gradually mixing calls faster as students get comfortable with the pattern.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room to stretch and breathe comfortably.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a short guided breathing moment: 3 slow breaths in through the nose and out through the mouth, standing tall with eyes closed if comfortable.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and quiet, using this time to settle the group before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one final big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the ball as it leaves the racquet or hand, not just as it approaches you.</td><td>The earliest, clearest information about where a ball is going comes right at the start of its flight.</td></tr><tr><td>Judge the trajectory — high or low, short or deep — and start moving toward the predicted spot immediately.</td><td>Moving early, based on a prediction, gives you time to arrive balanced instead of reacting late.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep adjusting your position with small steps as the ball gets closer and your prediction becomes clearer.</td><td>An early prediction gets you close; small adjustments get you exactly right.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call the Bounce</h4><p>A feeder sends a variety of trajectories; before the ball bounces, students call out ‘high,’ ‘low,’ ‘short,’ or ‘deep’ as a prediction.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up, one feeding and one predicting, on either side of a low net or simple hitting line.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the ball leave the racquet, not just where it lands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious trajectories.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, more varied trajectories.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder sends a variety of gentle, controlled trajectories — some higher, some lower, some shorter, some deeper.</li><li>Before the ball bounces, the predicting partner calls out 'high', 'low', 'short', or 'deep' based on what they see in flight.</li><li>The feeder confirms whether the prediction matched the actual bounce once it happens.</li><li>Swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both partners practise predicting and feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: watch the ball leave the racquet, not just where it lands — the flight gives the earliest clues.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder sends quicker, more varied trajectories with less obvious differences.</li></ul><hr><h4>Early Move Challenge</h4><p>Students must move to the predicted contact spot before the ball bounces, then play the shot from a balanced position.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up with one feeder and one mover, on either side of a low net or hitting line.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move on the flight, not the bounce.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower feeds, more reaction time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker feeds, less reaction time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder sends a ball with a clear trajectory (height, depth, or direction).</li><li>Before the ball bounces, the mover must move to where they predict the contact spot will be.</li><li>Once positioned, the mover plays the shot from a balanced ready position rather than lunging or reaching.</li><li>Swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both partners practise moving early and feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: move on the flight, not the bounce — waiting for the bounce is often too late.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder quickens the pace of feeds to reduce reaction time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Trajectory Tag</h4><p>A feeder deliberately varies trajectory (short, deep, high, low) and students react with correct positioning and stroke choice.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up with one feeder and one hitter, on either side of a low net or hitting line, in a marked area roughly 5m x 5m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Adjust your feet first, your racquet second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Predictable pattern of trajectories.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Fully randomised trajectories.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking/growing space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>The feeder deliberately varies trajectory — sometimes short, sometimes deep, sometimes high, sometimes low.</li><li>The hitter reacts with correct positioning (moving forward/back as needed) and an appropriate stroke choice.</li><li>The feeder gives quick feedback on whether the hitter's positioning matched the trajectory well.</li><li>Swap roles every 6-8 feeds so both partners get practice at feeding and reacting.</li><li>Coach the key message: adjust your feet first, your racquet second — footwork sets up the stroke.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder uses a fully randomised mix of trajectories with no pattern.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read &amp; React Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games where feeders/opponents deliberately vary trajectory, and reading it well early is highlighted by the teacher as a key reason points are won.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair or small group of 4 with a marked mini-court, roughly 5m x 6m on each side.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's small-sided games will highlight moments where a player reads the incoming ball well and reacts early.</li><li>Points start with a gentle feed or serve and play out normally from there.</li><li>When you see a student clearly read and react to a varied trajectory well, pause briefly to point it out to the group.</li><li>Coach the key message: reading the ball early is often the real reason a point is won, more than raw power.</li><li>Rotate feeding/serving duties so play stays fair.</li><li>Finish by asking students to name one trajectory (short, deep, high, or low) they found hardest to read today.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What clues helped you predict where the ball was going?</li><li>Who read the ball really well today — what were they watching for?</li><li>What happens if you wait until after the bounce to start moving?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Starts moving before the ball bounces, based on its flight</li><li>Correctly judges high/low/short/deep in most cases</li><li>Arrives at the ball in a balanced position, not stretching</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use slower, more obvious trajectories and extra reaction time for students building this skill; keep feeds controlled and predictable in easier variants so success is genuinely achievable.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Smart Shot":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Decision-making — where to place a shot</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after a partner's success as much as your own — feeding, rallying, and encouraging in a way that helps them succeed too.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets, cones to mark court zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing; any light opposition/pressure kept controlled and non-contact</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to choose where to aim a shot based on the situation — where our opponent is, and how balanced we are — rather than hitting to the same place every time.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can spot space on the other side of the court and aim there</li><li>I can choose a safer, higher-percentage shot when I'm off-balance</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Split-Step Shuffle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 15m x 15m per group of 8-10 students. Each student needs their own racquet, no ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the split-step shuffle before starting: a small, light hop landing with feet apart, ready to move in any direction.</li><li>Students spread out and begin light jogging on the spot.</li><li>Call 'split!' every 8-10 seconds — students perform one split-step hop, then immediately shuffle two steps in a direction you call (left, right, forward, back).</li><li>Keep the calls varied and the pace playful, focusing on quick, light feet rather than big movements.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, gradually mixing calls faster as students get comfortable with the pattern.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with enough room to stretch and breathe comfortably.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down.</li><li>Gather into a circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Add a short guided breathing moment: 3 slow breaths in through the nose and out through the mouth, standing tall with eyes closed if comfortable.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and quiet, using this time to settle the group before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one final big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Glance at where your opponent (or the marker) is positioned before you swing.</td><td>You can't aim for space if you haven't looked for it first.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose a safer, central shot when you're off-balance or rushed, and a more ambitious placed shot when you're set and balanced.</td><td>Matching your shot choice to your own balance is what keeps errors low while still allowing smart, attacking shots when you're ready.</td></tr><tr><td>Commit fully to your chosen shot rather than changing your mind mid-swing.</td><td>A confident, committed shot — even a simple one — is far more reliable than a hesitant, half-changed one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Spot the Space</h4><p>A cone or a partner marks an ‘opponent’ position on the other side; hitters must aim away from it into open space.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pair students up with one hitter and one 'opponent' marker (a cone or a standing partner), on either side of a low net or marked court roughly 6m x 6m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look for the gap before you swing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Opponent marker stays still.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Opponent marker moves between shots.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Place the opponent marker somewhere on the far side to represent where a real opponent might be standing.</li><li>The hitter receives a feed and must aim their shot away from the marker, into open space.</li><li>Give quick feedback on whether the shot found space or went toward the marker.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 hits so both partners practise hitting and placing the marker.</li><li>Coach the key message: look for the gap before you swing — decide where the space is first.</li><li>For 'harder', the marker (or partner) moves position between shots instead of staying still.</li></ul><hr><h4>Pressure Decision Rally</h4><p>A light, gentle chaser/defender applies mild pressure during a rally, forcing quicker placement decisions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a small marked court roughly 6m x 6m per group of 3, with a low net, one racquet each and a shared ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A simple, safe shot beats a risky, wild one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Defender reacts slowly, more decision time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Defender reacts quickly, less decision time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Two students rally normally while a third, gentle 'chaser' applies light, non-contact pressure by moving toward the ball side.</li><li>The rallying students must make quicker placement decisions to keep the rally going away from the pressure.</li><li>Rotate the chaser role every 1-2 minutes so all three students get a turn applying and responding to pressure.</li><li>Keep the chaser's pressure light and safe — no contact, just closing space calmly.</li><li>Coach the key message: a simple, safe shot beats a risky, wild one, especially under pressure.</li><li>For 'harder', the chaser reacts more quickly, giving hitters less decision time.</li></ul><hr><h4>Safe or Risky?</h4><p>Before each shot, students choose between a safe central shot (guaranteed point) or a risky angled shot (bonus points if successful, but no points if missed).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark a central 'safe' target zone and a smaller, angled 'risky' zone on the far side of a low net, per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Choose based on your balance, not just ambition.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Smaller bonus, safer risky option.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Bigger bonus, tighter risky option.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before each shot, the hitter chooses out loud whether they'll aim for the safe central zone (guaranteed point) or the risky angled zone (bonus points if successful).</li><li>A partner feeds the ball, and the hitter plays their chosen shot.</li><li>Track points, awarding a bonus for successful risky shots and a smaller, reliable amount for safe ones.</li><li>Swap roles every 5-6 hits so both partners practise choosing and feeding.</li><li>Coach the key message: choose based on your balance, not just ambition — only go risky when you're set up well.</li><li>For 'harder', make the risky zone smaller with a bigger bonus attached.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Decision Point Games</h3><p>Small-sided games scored partly on smart shot selection — the teacher or a peer observer calls out ‘smart shot!’ moments — not just on who wins each point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair or small group with a marked mini-court, roughly 5m x 6m on each side.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's small-sided games are scored partly on smart shot selection, not just on who wins each point.</li><li>Points start with a gentle feed or serve and play out normally.</li><li>When you or a peer observer notices a genuinely smart shot choice — finding space, playing safe under pressure — call out 'smart shot!' and note it.</li><li>Coach the key message: winning the point and playing the smart shot are both worth celebrating, even when they don't line up.</li><li>Rotate players through observer duty briefly if using peer observers, so everyone gets a turn watching for smart shots.</li><li>Finish by asking a couple of students to describe their own smart-shot moment from the game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you spot space on the other side?</li><li>Who made a really smart shot choice today — what made it smart?</li><li>When is it better to play it safe rather than go for a risky shot?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Looks for open space before choosing where to aim</li><li>Chooses a safer shot when off-balance or under pressure</li><li>Can explain the reasoning behind a shot choice, not just describe the result</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Simplify the decision (e.g. just ‘left or right’) for students who find rapid decision-making overwhelming; reduce pressure/speed for pairs still building confidence.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Match Play & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Singles Showcase":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided mini-tennis games (singles)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how today's skill connects to the bigger game of tennis — a small piece that matters once it's put together with everything else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/marked mini-courts, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard court spacing between simultaneous matches; balls collected calmly between points</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply groundstrokes, footwork, and shot placement together in a real 1v1 mini-tennis game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full mini-tennis point from serve through to a finish</li><li>I can use today's game skills — movement and placement — inside a real game, not just a drill</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, organised into the small teams/pairs that will be used for that lesson's matches.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the warm-up: teams jog together as a group around the space, staying loosely together rather than spreading out individually.</li><li>On 'go', each team jogs a lap or loop of the space together at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'shape!' — teams quickly form a shape you name (a line, a circle, a triangle) before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and social — this warm-up is as much about building team connection before matches as raising heart rate.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the shapes called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the whole class into one large circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down after matches.</li><li>Gather into one large circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders/forearms — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to briefly share a highlight from their matches while the group stretches.</li><li>Keep the tone celebratory and calm, marking the end of the unit rather than just another lesson.</li><li>Finish with the whole class giving a round of applause or cheer together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Start each point with your serving routine — consistent toss/drop, controlled swing.</td><td>A calm, consistent start sets up everything that follows in the point.</td></tr><tr><td>Use split-step and movement to stay balanced and ready throughout the rally.</td><td>This is where all the footwork practice from earlier lessons pays off inside a real point.</td></tr><tr><td>Look for a chance to place a shot smartly or move forward to finish, rather than rallying aimlessly.</td><td>A real point rewards purposeful play — using your skills with intent, not just keeping the ball going.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Mini-Match Warm Points</h4><p>Short practice points (serve, rally, finish) played to a low target score, with no formal scoring pressure yet.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court roughly 6m x 6m, generous in size for a comfortable, low-pressure start.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Play each point like it's the whole match.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Extra serves allowed, generous court.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>One serve only, standard court.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: short practice points including a serve, a rally, and a finish, played to a low target score (e.g. first to 3).</li><li>One player serves underarm to begin the point, and both players rally it out normally.</li><li>Points are scored simply and casually, with no formal match pressure yet.</li><li>Coach the key message: play each point like it's the whole match — build the habit of full focus now.</li><li>Swap serving after every point (or every 2 points) so both players get equal serving practice.</li><li>For 'harder', reduce to one serve only on a standard-size court instead of extra serves on a generous court.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve-Rally-Finish Circuit</h4><p>A structured circuit where students rotate through serving, rallying, and finishing at the net, reinforcing the full sequence of a point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 3 stations per group of 3-4 students, each with a low net: a serving station, a rallying station, and a net-finish station.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your ready position between each phase.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the circuit: students rotate through serving practice, then rallying practice, then net-finish practice, spending equal time at each.</li><li>At the serve station, students practise serving underarm into a marked box.</li><li>At the rally station, students rally cooperatively with a partner, counting consecutive hits.</li><li>At the finish station, a partner feeds and the student practises finishing with a volley at the net.</li><li>Coach the key message: reset your ready position between each phase, whichever station you're at.</li><li>For 'harder', shorten the time at each station so rotations happen more quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Scoring Practice Points</h4><p>Play short points using simple, modified scoring (e.g. first to 4), focusing on applying skills under real (light) game conditions.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court, using a simple modified scoring system (e.g. first to 4 points).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay controlled — you don't need a winner every point.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Lower score target, bigger court.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Higher score target, standard court.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the modified scoring system clearly before starting, including how a point is won.</li><li>One player serves to begin, and both players play the point out to a natural conclusion.</li><li>Both players call the score out loud after each point to build the habit of clear scorekeeping.</li><li>Coach the key message: stay controlled — you don't need a winner every point, a rally you win by outlasting your partner still counts.</li><li>Swap serving after every point or two so both players serve regularly.</li><li>For 'harder', raise the score target and reduce court size slightly for tighter margins.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Singles Mini-Tennis</h3><p>Students rotate through short 1v1 mini-tennis matches on modified courts, applying serving, rallying, movement, and placement together in a genuine game context.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up as many low nets/marked mini-courts as needed so every pair can play a short match simultaneously, roughly 6m x 6m each.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: students will rotate through several short 1v1 mini-tennis matches during the session.</li><li>Each match begins with a serve and is played out using simple scoring, applying serving, rallying, movement, and placement together.</li><li>At a set time or score, call 'rotate' and students move to a new court/opponent.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is the first time everything from the unit comes together in a real match — enjoy applying it.</li><li>Keep matches short (a few minutes each) so everyone gets multiple matches against different partners.</li><li>Circulate and give brief, positive feedback rather than stopping play to coach at length.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What part of a full point felt most natural to you today?</li><li>Who played a really smart, controlled singles game — what did you notice?</li><li>What's one thing from earlier lessons you used without even thinking about it?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Links serve, rally, and finish into one connected point</li><li>Applies footwork and placement decisions from earlier lessons in a real game</li><li>Shows fair play — accurate calls, encouraging opponents</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Adjust court size, net height, or scoring targets so every matchup is genuinely competitive and enjoyable; rotate opponents to avoid repeated uneven matchups.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Dynamic Duos":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided mini-tennis games (doubles) — communication &amp; positioning</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself while a new skill feels awkward, and encouraging classmates whose attempts look different from yours.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/marked mini-courts, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear, loud calling to prevent collisions between doubles partners; standard spacing between simultaneous matches</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to play doubles mini-tennis, communicating with a partner and covering court space together.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can call ‘mine’ or ‘yours’ clearly and communicate with my partner during play</li><li>I can position myself to cover space without colliding with my partner</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, organised into the small teams/pairs that will be used for that lesson's matches.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the warm-up: teams jog together as a group around the space, staying loosely together rather than spreading out individually.</li><li>On 'go', each team jogs a lap or loop of the space together at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'shape!' — teams quickly form a shape you name (a line, a circle, a triangle) before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and social — this warm-up is as much about building team connection before matches as raising heart rate.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the shapes called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the whole class into one large circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down after matches.</li><li>Gather into one large circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders/forearms — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to briefly share a highlight from their matches while the group stretches.</li><li>Keep the tone celebratory and calm, marking the end of the unit rather than just another lesson.</li><li>Finish with the whole class giving a round of applause or cheer together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call ‘mine’ or ‘yours’ clearly and as early as possible when a ball comes toward the middle.</td><td>An early, clear call prevents collisions and confusion far better than reacting silently.</td></tr><tr><td>Position yourself relative to your partner — side-by-side or one-up-one-back — to cover the court as a pair.</td><td>Doubles is a shared space; a good position depends on where your partner is, not just where the ball is.</td></tr><tr><td>Move and communicate together as the point develops, adjusting your shared coverage.</td><td>A doubles team that moves as a unit covers far more court than two players moving independently.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call It!</h4><p>Pairs practise calling ‘mine’ or ‘yours’ loudly and early as balls are fed toward the middle of their side.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up doubles pairs on one side of a low net per group of 4, with a feeder on the other side, court marked roughly 7m x 6m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call it the instant you decide, not after you've moved.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Balls fed clearly to one side or the other.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Balls fed down the middle, genuinely ambiguous.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: whichever player is going to play the ball must call 'mine' loudly and early; the other calls 'yours' or stays clear.</li><li>The feeder sends balls toward the middle of the doubles pair's side.</li><li>The pair practises calling clearly and early, before either player moves to the ball.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role regularly so all students get practice calling.</li><li>Coach the key message: call it the instant you decide, not after you've already moved — a late call causes collisions.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder sends balls genuinely down the middle where the call is less obvious.</li></ul><hr><h4>Court Coverage Shuffle</h4><p>Pairs practise a simple movement pattern (e.g. one up, one back, or side-by-side) shuffling to cover the court as a feeder varies shot direction.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up doubles pairs on one side of a low net per group of 4, with a feeder on the other side, court marked roughly 7m x 6m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move together, not just individually.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, predictable feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quicker, varied feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Shrinking/growing space</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain a simple coverage pattern to the pair — either one player up and one back, or side-by-side — before starting.</li><li>The feeder varies shot direction across the pair's side of the court.</li><li>The pair shuffles together to cover the court according to their chosen pattern, staying connected rather than both chasing the same ball.</li><li>Rotate the feeder role regularly so all students get practice moving as a pair.</li><li>Coach the key message: move together, not just individually — the pattern only works if both players shift together.</li><li>For 'harder', the feeder speeds up and varies feeds more unpredictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Doubles Feed Points</h4><p>Short doubles points started by a feed, focusing on communication and positioning rather than winning outright.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up doubles pairs on each side of a low net per group of 4, court marked roughly 7m x 6m.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A clear call beats a fast reaction with no call.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Extra time between feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Quick succession of feeds.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>A feed (from a teacher, coach, or rotating student) starts each short point.</li><li>The pair plays the point out, focusing on clear calling and good court positioning rather than trying to win outright.</li><li>After each point, give brief feedback on communication and coverage before the next feed.</li><li>Rotate feeding duties regularly so play stays fair and everyone gets to play.</li><li>Coach the key message: a clear call beats a fast reaction with no call — communication comes first.</li><li>For 'harder', feeds come in quicker succession, giving less time to reset between points.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Doubles Mini-Tennis Round Robin</h3><p>Pairs rotate through short doubles matches on modified courts, applying communication and court coverage inside real doubles points.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up as many low nets/marked doubles courts as needed, roughly 7m x 6m each, so every group of 4 can play simultaneously.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the round-robin format: pairs rotate through short doubles matches against different opponents during the session.</li><li>Each match begins with a serve or feed and is played out with pairs applying today's calling and court-coverage skills.</li><li>At a set time or score, call 'rotate' and pairs move to a new court/opponent pairing.</li><li>Coach the key message: good communication is often the difference between a smooth doubles point and a collision.</li><li>Keep matches short so everyone plays multiple rounds against different pairs.</li><li>Circulate and specifically praise clear, early calling when you hear it.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did calling ‘mine’ or ‘yours’ do for you and your partner today?</li><li>Who covered the court really well as a doubles pair — what did they do?</li><li>What's one thing that's different about doubles compared to singles?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Calls clearly and early during doubles points</li><li>Positions to cover space rather than crowding a partner</li><li>Communicates and encourages a partner during play</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Pair students thoughtfully so communication styles work well together; simplify positioning patterns (e.g. just ‘left side/right side’) for pairs still building confidence.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Game, Set, Smart":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Scoring &amp; game strategy basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your own body and how it moves — noticing exactly what's happening in your grip, swing, or footwork, and learning from it.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/marked mini-courts, cones, simple strategy cards</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between matches; clear, agreed scoring routines to avoid disputes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to understand and use simple tennis scoring, and to apply one basic strategic idea inside a game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can keep score using simple modified scoring</li><li>I can explain one simple strategy idea and try to use it in a game</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, organised into the small teams/pairs that will be used for that lesson's matches.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the warm-up: teams jog together as a group around the space, staying loosely together rather than spreading out individually.</li><li>On 'go', each team jogs a lap or loop of the space together at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'shape!' — teams quickly form a shape you name (a line, a circle, a triangle) before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and social — this warm-up is as much about building team connection before matches as raising heart rate.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the shapes called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the whole class into one large circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down after matches.</li><li>Gather into one large circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders/forearms — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to briefly share a highlight from their matches while the group stretches.</li><li>Keep the tone celebratory and calm, marking the end of the unit rather than just another lesson.</li><li>Finish with the whole class giving a round of applause or cheer together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Learn and use a simple, agreed scoring system, calling the score clearly after each point.</td><td>Clear scoring is what turns a casual hit-around into a real game with structure.</td></tr><tr><td>Choose one simple strategy idea before a game or set of points — for example, aiming to a particular side.</td><td>A single, clear strategic idea is easier to actually use in the moment than trying to think about everything at once.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice whether your strategy is working and adjust it if it isn't.</td><td>Strategy isn't a fixed plan — reading how it's going and adapting is the real skill being learned here.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Scoring Practice</h4><p>Play short points and practise keeping score aloud using a simple modified system (e.g. first to 4, win by 2).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court roughly 6m x 6m, using a simple modified scoring system (e.g. first to 4, win by 2).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the score clearly after every point.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simple point-count scoring.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Standard modified tennis scoring.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the modified scoring system clearly, including how a point, and eventually a game, is won.</li><li>One player serves to begin, and the pair plays short points using the system.</li><li>Both players call the score out loud clearly after every single point.</li><li>Coach the key message: call the score clearly after every point — this habit prevents disputes later.</li><li>Swap serving regularly so both players get equal serving practice.</li><li>For 'harder', move from simple point-count scoring to the standard modified tennis scoring system used in the unit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Strategy Card Points</h4><p>Before a short game, pairs draw a simple strategy card (e.g. ‘aim to your opponent's backhand,’ ‘bring them to the net’) and try to apply it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Prepare a small set of simple strategy cards per pair (e.g. 'aim to your opponent's backhand', 'bring them to the net'), plus a low net and marked mini-court.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Try the strategy even if it doesn't work every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>One simple, obvious strategy.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>A more specific or situational strategy.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before a short game, the pair draws one strategy card together and reads it aloud.</li><li>The pair plays a short game, with the receiving/returning student specifically trying to apply the strategy on the card.</li><li>After the short game, briefly discuss whether the strategy worked and why or why not.</li><li>Draw a new card and repeat for 2-3 rounds so students try a couple of different strategies.</li><li>Coach the key message: try the strategy even if it doesn't work every time — the goal is applying an idea on purpose.</li><li>For 'harder', use more specific or situational strategy cards that require reading the game state, not just a fixed target.</li></ul><hr><h4>Consistency Wins</h4><p>Points only end on a clear unforced error or a genuine winner — encouraging students to keep the ball in play rather than swinging wildly for winners.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court, generous in size to encourage longer rallies (roughly 7m x 6m).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>A rally you win by outlasting your opponent still counts.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger court, more margin for error.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller court, tighter margins.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: points only end on a clear unforced error (the ball goes out or into the net without pressure) or a genuine winner — no giving up on a rally early.</li><li>One player serves to begin, and the pair plays the point out under this rule.</li><li>Both players call the score and note whether the point ended in a winner or an error, to reinforce the pattern.</li><li>Coach the key message: a rally you win by outlasting your opponent still counts — patience is a real skill here.</li><li>Swap serving regularly so both players get equal serving practice.</li><li>For 'harder', shrink the court for tighter margins, making patience and placement matter even more.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Scored Mini-Matches</h3><p>Real scored matches using today's simple scoring system, with each pair encouraged to name and try one strategy idea during the match.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court roughly 6m x 6m, using the class's agreed modified scoring system.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's matches use real scoring, and each pair should name one strategy idea before they start.</li><li>Before the match, the pair briefly agrees on one simple strategy they'll try to apply during play.</li><li>Players serve, rally, and score using the modified system practised earlier in the lesson.</li><li>Coach the key message: naming a strategy in advance turns today's match into more than just hitting the ball back and forth.</li><li>Circulate and ask a couple of pairs mid-match whether they're managing to apply their chosen strategy.</li><li>Finish by asking a few students to share their strategy and whether it worked.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did you find tricky or straightforward about keeping score?</li><li>Who tried a strategy today — did it work, and how did they adjust?</li><li>Why might a simple strategy be more useful than just trying to hit the ball hard?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Keeps and calls score accurately and clearly</li><li>Can name a simple strategy they tried</li><li>Adjusts their play slightly based on how a strategy is or isn't working</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Offer a very simple scoring option (just a running point count) for students who find traditional tennis scoring confusing; keep strategy ideas concrete and simple (‘aim there’ rather than abstract concepts).</p>"},"Lesson 19: Match Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full modified games with scoring</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B, C · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Looking after a partner's success as much as your own — feeding, rallying, and encouraging in a way that helps them succeed too.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/marked mini-courts, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing between simultaneous matches; agreed, fair self-officiating routines to prevent disputes</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to play a complete modified match, applying the full range of skills learned across the unit.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play a full modified match using appropriate strokes, movement, and placement</li><li>I can score and self-officiate fairly during a match</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, organised into the small teams/pairs that will be used for that lesson's matches.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the warm-up: teams jog together as a group around the space, staying loosely together rather than spreading out individually.</li><li>On 'go', each team jogs a lap or loop of the space together at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'shape!' — teams quickly form a shape you name (a line, a circle, a triangle) before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and social — this warm-up is as much about building team connection before matches as raising heart rate.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the shapes called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the whole class into one large circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down after matches.</li><li>Gather into one large circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders/forearms — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to briefly share a highlight from their matches while the group stretches.</li><li>Keep the tone celebratory and calm, marking the end of the unit rather than just another lesson.</li><li>Finish with the whole class giving a round of applause or cheer together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Bring together serving, rallying, movement, and placement into one connected match, rather than treating them as separate skills.</td><td>A real match asks for all of these skills together, often in quick succession — this is the whole unit coming together.</td></tr><tr><td>Call the score and any line decisions honestly and clearly, even when a call goes against you.</td><td>Self-officiated tennis relies entirely on honesty — fair play is as much a part of the game as any stroke.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay calm and reset after a lost point, rather than carrying frustration into the next one.</td><td>A short memory for mistakes is what lets you play your best tennis point after point.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Warm-Up Rally Points</h4><p>A short rally-based warm-up where students settle into rhythm and remind themselves of key technique points before matches begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court roughly 6m x 6m, same as the match courts used later in the lesson.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use these points to find your rhythm, not to win.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer warm-up time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter warm-up, straight into matches.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>n/a</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that these points are for settling into rhythm and reminding themselves of key technique points, not for winning.</li><li>One player starts with a gentle serve or feed, and the pair rallies the point out normally.</li><li>Encourage players to consciously notice their grip, turn, and footwork during these points rather than just playing on autopilot.</li><li>Coach the key message: use these points to find your rhythm, not to win — save the competitive focus for the matches ahead.</li><li>Swap serving regularly so both players warm up serving as well as rallying.</li><li>For 'harder', shorten the warm-up time and move straight into matches sooner.</li></ul><hr><h4>Skill Focus Choice</h4><p>Each student picks one skill from the unit (e.g. serve placement, net finishing) to focus on today, and gets brief feedback on it during play.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment beyond the standard racquet, ball, and low net already in use for the lesson.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick something you want to genuinely improve, not just something easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Teacher/peer suggests a focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Student justifies their own choice of focus.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before play, each student picks one specific skill from the unit they want to focus on today (e.g. serve placement, net finishing, reading the ball).</li><li>Students briefly tell a partner or the teacher their chosen focus.</li><li>During play, the teacher or a peer gives brief, specific feedback tied to that chosen focus whenever it comes up.</li><li>Coach the key message: pick something you want to genuinely improve, not just something that's already easy.</li><li>Check in briefly with a few students partway through to see how their focus skill is going.</li><li>For 'harder', ask the student to explain in their own words why they chose that particular focus.</li></ul><hr><h4>Practice Match Sets</h4><p>Short practice sets (a small number of games) played with full scoring, as a run-through before the tournament.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up a low net per pair with a marked mini-court roughly 6m x 6m, using the class's agreed modified scoring system.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Treat this like the real thing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Shorter sets, generous court/net.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Standard sets, standard court/net.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that pairs will play a short practice set (a small number of games) using full scoring, as a run-through before the tournament.</li><li>Players serve, rally, and score using the modified system, alternating serve each game.</li><li>Encourage players to self-officiate fairly, calling their own lines and scores honestly.</li><li>Coach the key message: treat this like the real thing — this is good practice for tournament day.</li><li>Keep sets short so there's time for a debrief afterward.</li><li>For 'harder', use a standard court size and standard net height instead of a generous, lowered setup.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Modified Matches</h3><p>Students play complete modified matches (singles or doubles) with real scoring, self-officiated, drawing on serving, rallying, footwork, net play, and decision-making from across the whole unit.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up as many low nets/marked mini-courts as needed, roughly 6m x 6m each, so every pair or group of 4 can play a complete match simultaneously.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that today's matches use full, real scoring and can be played as singles or doubles depending on group size.</li><li>Players serve, rally, and score using the class's agreed system, self-officiating fairly throughout.</li><li>Encourage players to draw on every skill from the unit — serving, rallying, footwork, net play, and smart shot decisions.</li><li>Coach the key message: this is a genuine run-through for tournament day, so play it as seriously and fairly as you can.</li><li>Circulate to help resolve any scoring disagreements calmly and quickly, without stopping play for long.</li><li>Finish with a short debrief on how matches felt compared to earlier practice points.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What part of your game are you most confident in going into tomorrow's tournament?</li><li>Who showed great self-officiating and fair play today?</li><li>What's one thing you'd still like to work on?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Plays a complete match applying a range of skills from across the unit</li><li>Self-officiates honestly and fairly</li><li>Shows resilience after lost points, staying in the match mentally</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Match students thoughtfully so games are competitive and enjoyable for both players; offer flexible court/net/scoring adjustments so every match remains genuinely playable.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Tennis Tournament Day":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Seeing how today's skill connects to the bigger game of tennis — a small piece that matters once it's put together with everything else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>racquets, balls, low nets/marked mini-courts, cones, simple draw sheet or rotation chart</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard spacing across multiple simultaneous courts; clear rotation system so students know where to go next</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply the whole unit's skills in a tournament setting, showing fair play and reflecting on our own growth.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can apply a range of skills from across the unit in competitive and cooperative match play</li><li>I can play fairly and encourage opponents and teammates throughout the tournament</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Shape Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, organised into the small teams/pairs that will be used for that lesson's matches.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the warm-up: teams jog together as a group around the space, staying loosely together rather than spreading out individually.</li><li>On 'go', each team jogs a lap or loop of the space together at an easy pace.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'shape!' — teams quickly form a shape you name (a line, a circle, a triangle) before continuing to jog.</li><li>Keep the pace light and social — this warm-up is as much about building team connection before matches as raising heart rate.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing up the shapes called.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the whole class into one large circle.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have students walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring their heart rate down after matches.</li><li>Gather into one large circle and lead 3-4 simple static stretches — calves, quads, hamstrings, and shoulders/forearms — holding each for about 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Invite a few students to briefly share a highlight from their matches while the group stretches.</li><li>Keep the tone celebratory and calm, marking the end of the unit rather than just another lesson.</li><li>Finish with the whole class giving a round of applause or cheer together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply skills from across the whole unit — grip, strokes, footwork, serving, net play, and decision-making.</td><td>The tournament brings everything the unit has built together under real, enjoyable pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate and encourage teammates and opponents throughout.</td><td>Fair play and encouragement are as much a part of the unit as the physical skills.</td></tr><tr><td>Reflect on your growth compared to Lesson 1.</td><td>Reflecting on your own improvement is as valuable as the result of any one match.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Skill Circuit Warm-Up</h4><p>A quick circuit combining grip, bounce control, groundstrokes, and volleys — a whistle-stop reminder of the whole unit before matches begin.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up 4 short stations per group of 4-5 students: grip/ready position, bounce control, groundstrokes (self-drop target), and volleys (close feed).</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Smooth transitions between stations.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer time per station.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter time, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the circuit: a quick reminder of grip, bounce control, groundstrokes, and volleys before matches begin.</li><li>Students spend a short, equal amount of time at each station (e.g. 1-2 minutes), rotating on your signal.</li><li>At each station, briefly demonstrate or remind students of the key coaching cue from that lesson earlier in the unit.</li><li>Coach the key message: smooth transitions between stations — keep the energy up and the pace moving.</li><li>For 'harder', shorten the time at each station so rotations happen more quickly.</li><li>Finish the circuit with everyone feeling warmed up and reminded of the unit's key skills before matches start.</li></ul><hr><h4>Partner Encouragement Challenge</h4><p>Before matches, each student nominates one specific thing they'll do to encourage their opponent or partner during play (e.g. a genuine compliment on a good shot).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond what's already set up for matches.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Make it specific and genuine, not generic.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before matches begin, ask each student to think of one specific thing they'll do to encourage their opponent or partner during play.</li><li>Give an example — a genuine compliment on a good shot, rather than something generic like 'good game'.</li><li>Students share their idea briefly with a partner or the group before matches start.</li><li>During play, listen out for students following through on their encouragement idea and acknowledge it when you hear it.</li><li>Coach the key message: make it specific and genuine, not generic — notice something real your opponent did well.</li><li>Revisit this briefly in the closing discussion, asking a few students what encouragement they gave or received.</li></ul><hr><h4>Focus Pick</h4><p>Each pair/player picks one skill from the unit they most want to show off today and keeps it in mind as their personal focus during matches.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No extra equipment needed beyond what's already set up for matches.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Choose something meaningful to you, not just easy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Before matches, each pair or individual picks one skill from across the whole unit they most want to show off today.</li><li>Students briefly share their chosen focus with a partner or the teacher.</li><li>During matches, encourage students to keep their focus skill in mind, even in the middle of competitive play.</li><li>Coach the key message: choose something meaningful to you, not just easy — this is your personal highlight to aim for.</li><li>Check in briefly with a few students during matches to see how their focus skill is going.</li><li>Revisit this in the closing discussion, asking students whether they managed to show off their chosen skill.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Students rotate through short mini-tennis matches (singles and/or doubles) across multiple courts, using simple scoring or a fair-play points system. Every skill from the unit — grip, groundstrokes, footwork, serving, net play, reading the ball, and decision-making — is relevant somewhere in these matches.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Set up multiple low nets/marked mini-courts across the available space, enough for every student to be playing at once, plus a simple draw sheet or rotation chart visible to the class.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the tournament format clearly at the start: students will rotate through several short mini-tennis matches (singles and/or doubles) across multiple courts.</li><li>Use the draw sheet or rotation chart to guide students to their next court and opponent after each match or at set time intervals.</li><li>Players use simple scoring or the class's fair-play points system, self-officiating fairly throughout.</li><li>Coach the key message: every skill from across the whole unit — grip, groundstrokes, footwork, serving, net play, reading the ball, and decision-making — is relevant somewhere in these matches.</li><li>Circulate across courts giving brief, positive feedback and helping resolve any scoring questions quickly.</li><li>Close the tournament with a celebration of effort and improvement across the unit, not just match results.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging play look like in today's tournament?</li><li>What will you take from tennis into other sports or games?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills from across the unit, not just one</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure the round robin with balanced/mixed-ability groupings; consider a simple fair-play recognition alongside match results to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}},"Volleyball":{"label":"Block","children":{"Block 1: Foundational Ball Control (Lessons 1–5)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 1: Bubble Control":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Ball familiarisation — controlled taps/catches</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Looking after yourself with patience while learning something new, and encouraging others (not laughing at mistakes) as everyone's ball control looks different on day one.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 light/soft volleyball (or balloon indoors) per student, cones for grid boundaries</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Spread out with a full arm's reach of space; watch overhead ball paths so students don't collide while looking up; no diving on hard floors</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to control a ball using soft hands, catching and tapping it without losing control.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can catch a ball softly above my forehead without it bouncing off my arms</li><li>I can tap a ball straight up using two hands</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bubble Wrap Taps</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one light/soft ball (or balloon) per student, no other equipment needed — this warm-up is about gentle ball contact, not competition.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone spreads out with a full arm's reach of space and holds their own ball.</li><li>On 'go', students gently self-toss and tap their ball upward continuously, like a light 'bubble wrap' popping rhythm, moving around the space as needed to stay under the ball.</li><li>Keep the taps soft and controlled — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up soft hands, not maximum power.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze taps!' where students catch and hold, then resume on 'go' — this keeps attention sharp without stopping the flow for long.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to keep their ball moving the whole time.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Reach &amp; Roll</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead a slow, reaching stretch sequence — reach both arms overhead, roll gently down through the spine toward the toes, then roll back up slowly, repeating 3-4 times.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, roll, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use soft, spread fingers to control the ball on contact.</td><td>Soft hands absorb the ball's speed rather than it bouncing away uncontrolled.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your eyes on the ball all the way to contact.</td><td>Looking away early is the most common cause of a mis-hit or drop.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet to get under or behind the ball rather than reaching.</td><td>Full-body positioning gives more control than arms alone.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Catch &amp; Tap</h4><p>Toss the ball to yourself, catch it softly above your forehead with two hands, then tap it straight back up before catching again — build up reps.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own light/soft volleyball (or balloon indoors) and stands in their own space, at least a full arm's reach from the nearest neighbour. No grid lines needed for this one — just spread the class out across the hall or court.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Fingers spread and soft, catch the ball rather than trap it against the chest.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Catch every time, no tap required yet.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two taps before each catch.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate first: toss the ball gently to yourself, catch it softly above your forehead with two hands and spread fingers, then tap it straight back up before catching it again.</li><li>Students start by tossing to themselves and catching every time, getting used to where the ball lands above their forehead.</li><li>Once catching feels comfortable, add the tap: catch, tap straight up, then catch again, building up the number of reps in a row.</li><li>There's no formal stoppage — if the ball is dropped or goes wide, students simply pick it up and start again from a fresh toss.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'fingers spread and soft, catch the ball rather than trap it against the chest.'</li><li>For 'easier', students catch every time with no tap required; for 'harder', require two taps before each catch.</li></ul><hr><h4>Keep It Up</h4><p>Individually, keep the ball off the ground using taps only (no catching) inside a small grid, for as long as possible.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Mark out small individual grids roughly 2m x 2m using cones, one per student, spaced so no two grids touch. Each student keeps their own ball inside their grid.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet under the ball rather than reaching.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow one bounce between taps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must alternate a one-hand tap and a two-hand tap.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock (personal best)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate keeping the ball off the ground using taps only (no catching), staying inside the grid.</li><li>On 'go', each student starts their own count from a self-toss, tapping the ball upward repeatedly.</li><li>The only rule during play is: taps only, ball stays inside the grid, no catching.</li><li>If the ball touches the ground or leaves the grid, that student's count resets to zero and they restart immediately with a fresh toss — there's no waiting for a whistle.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move your feet under the ball rather than reaching.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow one bounce between taps; for 'harder', students must alternate a one-hand tap and a two-hand tap.</li></ul><hr><h4>Circle Pop-Up</h4><p>Groups of 4–5 stand in a circle and tap the ball to each other, calling the receiver's name before each tap, trying to keep it off the ground.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into groups of 4-5 and have each group form a circle roughly 3m across, with enough space between circles that a mistimed tap doesn't land in a neighbouring group. One ball per circle.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the name early, aim the tap gently above the receiver's head.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch between taps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No catching at all — clean tap-passes only.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation, scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the pattern: call a teammate's name, then tap the ball gently toward them, aiming it above their head so it's easy to control.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and calls a name before their first tap.</li><li>Play continues around the circle with each receiver calling the next name before they tap, always trying to keep the ball off the ground.</li><li>If the ball hits the ground, the group simply restarts with any student picking it up and calling a name to begin again — no formal scoring, just rebuilding the streak.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call the name early, aim the tap gently above the receiver's head.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch between taps; for 'harder', remove catching entirely so it's clean tap-passes only.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Circle Keep-It-Up Challenge</h3><p>Groups of 6–8 form large circles; each team counts consecutive taps kept off the ground as a group, without a break, racing other circles for the highest streak. Soft, controlled ball contact (today's skill) directly determines the team's score.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into teams of 6-8 and have each team form a large circle, roughly 5-6m across, with plenty of space between different teams' circles. One ball per team.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the challenge: as a group, count every consecutive tap that keeps the ball off the ground, without a break, and see which circle can reach the highest streak.</li><li>One student in each circle starts with a self-toss and the first tap; the ball can be tapped by anyone as it moves around or across the circle.</li><li>Every legal, controlled tap that keeps the ball airborne adds one to the team's running count, said out loud together as a group.</li><li>The moment the ball touches the ground, that attempt ends — the group notes their score, picks the ball up, and immediately restarts the count from zero.</li><li>Give each circle 3-4 attempts and celebrate whichever team beats their own previous best, not just the highest overall number.</li><li>Remind students this connects directly to today's skill: soft, controlled contact above the forehead is exactly what keeps a streak alive.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you keep control of the ball today?</li><li>Who did you notice using really soft hands — what did that look like?</li><li>What was tricky, and how did you keep trying?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Catches with soft hands above the forehead (not against the chest)</li><li>Fingers spread on contact</li><li>Moves feet to get under the ball rather than reaching with arms</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a balloon or very light ball for students newer to the skill so contact doesn't sting; allow unlimited catches between taps until confidence builds.</p>"},"Lesson 2: Forearm Fundamentals":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Forearm pass (bump) fundamentals</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Knowing and understanding your own body — noticing what your arms and legs are doing, and learning from how the ball responds.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student or pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep good spacing so swinging arms don't clash with a neighbour's</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bump a ball using flat forearms so it travels up and forward in a controlled way.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can put my forearms together flat, like a platform</li><li>I can use my legs, not my arms, to power the bump</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bubble Wrap Taps</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one light/soft ball (or balloon) per student, no other equipment needed — this warm-up is about gentle ball contact, not competition.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone spreads out with a full arm's reach of space and holds their own ball.</li><li>On 'go', students gently self-toss and tap their ball upward continuously, like a light 'bubble wrap' popping rhythm, moving around the space as needed to stay under the ball.</li><li>Keep the taps soft and controlled — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up soft hands, not maximum power.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze taps!' where students catch and hold, then resume on 'go' — this keeps attention sharp without stopping the flow for long.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to keep their ball moving the whole time.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Reach &amp; Roll</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead a slow, reaching stretch sequence — reach both arms overhead, roll gently down through the spine toward the toes, then roll back up slowly, repeating 3-4 times.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, roll, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Join your hands together and keep arms flat and straight.</td><td>A flat platform gives a consistent, predictable surface for the ball to bounce off.</td></tr><tr><td>Contact the ball on the forearms, not the wrists or hands.</td><td>The forearms give the largest, most stable contact area.</td></tr><tr><td>Use your legs to generate the movement, not just your arms.</td><td>Leg drive gives more control and reduces reliance on arm swing, which is less accurate.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Platform Freeze</h4><p>Students self-toss a low, gentle ball and bump it to themselves, freezing their platform (arms together, flat) after each contact so it can be checked.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students work individually or in pairs, each with one ball, spread out with enough room that a swinging arm platform can't clash with a neighbour. No other equipment needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Thumbs pressed together, elbows locked, bend at the knees not the waist.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Partner drops the ball gently instead of self-toss.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Self-toss slightly further away, forcing a small step to the platform.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (\"freeze!\")</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the platform first: thumbs pressed together, arms straight and flat, elbows locked, bending at the knees rather than the waist to get under the ball.</li><li>Students self-toss a low, gentle ball just above waist height and bump it to themselves using the platform.</li><li>After every contact, freeze the platform in place for a second so it can be checked — by a partner, the coach, or the student themselves.</li><li>If the ball drops or the platform breaks down, students simply reset with a fresh gentle toss and continue.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'thumbs pressed together, elbows locked, bend at the knees not the waist.'</li><li>For 'easier', have a partner drop the ball gently instead of self-tossing; for 'harder', self-toss slightly further away so a small step is needed to reach the platform.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bump to Self Streak</h4><p>Individually, bump the ball to yourself as many times in a row as possible, tracking a personal best streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own ball and their own space, roughly 2m x 2m, so a slightly wayward bump doesn't land in someone else's area.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Use leg power, keep the platform steady rather than swinging the arms hard.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a small bounce between bumps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must bump above head height each time.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate bumping the ball to yourself using leg power rather than swinging the arms, keeping the platform steady.</li><li>Students self-toss and begin bumping the ball to themselves, counting each successful bump out loud.</li><li>The count keeps going as long as the ball stays under control and off the ground.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or bumped away, that's the end of the streak — the student notes their personal best and restarts immediately with a fresh toss.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'use leg power, keep the platform steady rather than swinging the arms hard.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a small bounce between bumps; for 'harder', require every bump to go above head height.</li></ul><hr><h4>Partner Feed Bump</h4><p>In pairs, one gently tosses the ball underarm, the other bumps it back; swap roles after 6 attempts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3m apart, each pair with one ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the feet to get square to the ball before contacting it.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tosser stands closer, softer toss.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosser varies the toss slightly left/right, forcing a small movement first.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the roles: one partner gently tosses the ball underarm to the other, who bumps it back using a platform.</li><li>The tosser starts each rep with a soft, predictable underarm toss aimed at the receiver's midline.</li><li>The receiver moves their feet to get square to the ball before contacting it, then bumps it back to the tosser.</li><li>After 6 attempts, partners swap roles so both get equal practice tossing and bumping.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move the feet to get square to the ball before contacting it.'</li><li>For 'easier', the tosser stands closer and tosses more softly; for 'harder', the tosser varies the toss slightly left or right, forcing a small movement first.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Bump Battle Grids</h3><p>Pairs face off across a low rope/line in small grids, gently tossing/bumping the ball back and forth, scoring a point if the ball lands in the opponent's grid after a legal bump — a simple, low-pressure introduction to using the bump in a game. Clean forearm technique determines whose bumps stay controlled.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs face off across a low rope or line (set at roughly waist height) inside small grids about 3m x 3m per side. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: gently toss or bump the ball back and forth across the rope, scoring a point for your side if the ball lands inside the opponent's grid after a legal bump.</li><li>One partner starts by tossing the ball over the rope into the opponent's grid to begin the rally.</li><li>From there, every return must be a legal bump (platform contact, not a catch or throw) sent back over the rope.</li><li>A point is scored whenever the ball lands inside the opponent's grid after a controlled bump, or if the receiving side fails to return it legally — play then restarts with a fresh toss from whoever conceded the point.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students get to bump against a few different partners.</li><li>Keep the tone low-pressure — celebrate clean forearm contact and controlled placement over raw power, since that's what today's skill was building toward.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did your platform (arms) feel like when the bump went well?</li><li>What part of your body actually did the work — arms or legs?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try to fix next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Forms a flat platform with forearms together</li><li>Bends knees rather than swinging arms hard</li><li>Gets feet in position before contact</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find the platform shape uncomfortable, allow a two-hand 'catch and bump' hybrid at first (catch, reset, then bump) before removing the catch.</p>"},"Lesson 3: Target Bump":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Bump accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — A good, accurate bump makes the game better for your partner — thinking about how your actions help someone else, not just yourself.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones/hoops as targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Space grids apart so pairs aren't bumping into each other's target zones</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to bump the ball so it travels accurately toward a chosen target.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can aim my platform toward a target before I bump</li><li>I can bump the ball so a partner doesn't have to move far to receive it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bubble Wrap Taps</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one light/soft ball (or balloon) per student, no other equipment needed — this warm-up is about gentle ball contact, not competition.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone spreads out with a full arm's reach of space and holds their own ball.</li><li>On 'go', students gently self-toss and tap their ball upward continuously, like a light 'bubble wrap' popping rhythm, moving around the space as needed to stay under the ball.</li><li>Keep the taps soft and controlled — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up soft hands, not maximum power.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze taps!' where students catch and hold, then resume on 'go' — this keeps attention sharp without stopping the flow for long.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to keep their ball moving the whole time.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Reach &amp; Roll</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead a slow, reaching stretch sequence — reach both arms overhead, roll gently down through the spine toward the toes, then roll back up slowly, repeating 3-4 times.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, roll, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Angle your platform toward your target before contact.</td><td>The platform's angle directly controls the ball's direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet to get in line with the ball first.</td><td>Reaching sideways with the platform reduces accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the motion controlled — a small punch from the legs, not a big swing.</td><td>A big swing is harder to direct accurately.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Hoop Target Bump</h4><p>A partner tosses the ball; the bumper aims to land it inside a hoop or cone square a few metres away.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, each with one ball and one hoop (or a small cone square marking a target) placed a few metres from the bumper.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Angle the platform toward the target, don't just swing upward.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger hoop, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target, further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate angling the platform toward the target rather than just swinging the ball upward.</li><li>The tosser gently underarm-tosses the ball to the bumper, aiming to make the contact easy to control.</li><li>The bumper's job is to angle their platform so the ball lands inside the hoop or cone square.</li><li>After each attempt, retrieve the ball and go again — there's no formal stoppage, just repeated attempts building accuracy.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'angle the platform toward the target, don't just swing upward.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger hoop and stand closer; for 'harder', use a smaller target further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Bump to a Moving Partner</h4><p>Tosser jogs gently side to side; the bumper must track and bump accurately to wherever the tosser currently is.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with enough room for the tosser to move side to side, roughly 4-5m of lateral space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the target, not just the ball, in the split second before contact.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tosser moves slowly/predictably.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosser changes direction quickly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: the tosser jogs gently side to side while tossing, and the bumper must track where they currently are, not where they started.</li><li>The tosser begins moving at an easy, predictable pace side to side while tossing the ball toward the bumper.</li><li>The bumper watches the tosser's current position and adjusts their feet before each bump to stay square to wherever they've moved.</li><li>Continue for a set number of reps, then swap roles so both partners practise bumping to a moving target.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'watch the target, not just the ball, in the split second before contact.'</li><li>For 'easier', the tosser moves slowly and predictably; for 'harder', the tosser changes direction quickly and less predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Bump Relay</h4><p>Trios stand in a triangle; bump the ball around the triangle in order as many times as possible without it hitting the ground, racing other trios.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of three stand in a triangle formation, spaced roughly 2-3m apart, one ball per trio, with enough room between trios that a stray bump doesn't reach a neighbouring group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the next receiver's name before you bump.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch between bumps.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must go around the triangle in both directions on request.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling the next receiver's name clearly before bumping the ball toward them.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and calls the name of whichever teammate they're bumping to.</li><li>Play continues around the triangle, always calling the next name before contact, trying to keep the ball off the ground for as long as possible.</li><li>If the ball touches the ground, the trio simply restarts immediately with a fresh toss and keeps counting from zero.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call the next receiver's name before you bump.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch between bumps; for 'harder', the trio must be able to reverse direction around the triangle on request.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Target Bump Rally Court</h3><p>Small groups rally a ball across a low net/rope, scoring bonus points any time a bump lands inside a marked target zone on the other side, in addition to normal rally points. Accuracy — not just getting the ball over — is what earns the team extra points.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups (4-6 students, split into two even sides) rally across a low net or rope, inside a court roughly 6m x 4m, with a marked target zone (cones or a taped square) on each side of the net.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: normal rally points are earned as usual, but any bump that lands inside the marked target zone on the opponent's side earns an extra bonus point.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a gentle toss or bump over the net to begin play.</li><li>Players return the ball with legal bumps, aiming for the target zone whenever they get the chance, while still keeping the rally going.</li><li>A rally point is scored when the other side fails to return the ball legally; a bonus point is added on top any time a bump lands inside the target zone, whether or not that specific touch ends the rally.</li><li>Restart quickly after each point with a fresh toss from the side that conceded, keeping the pace up.</li><li>Coach students to notice that aiming for the target — not just getting the ball over — is what earns the extra points today.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you aim your bump toward the target today?</li><li>How did an accurate bump make it easier for your partner?</li><li>What happens to a rally when bumps aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Platform angled toward the target before contact</li><li>Consistent leg power rather than arm swing</li><li>Communicates before receiving/bumping in group games</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger targets and shorter distances for students still building consistency; the triangle/relay games scale naturally by moving players closer together.</p>"},"Lesson 4: Platform Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Overhead set fundamentals</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about your hand shape and body position before the ball arrives, rather than just reacting at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per student or pair, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep sets below a safe, controlled height early on — no wild high sets near other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct setting technique — using fingers (not palms) to push the ball up and forward from above the forehead.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can shape my hands like a \"window\" above my forehead before the ball arrives</li><li>I can push the ball up using my fingers and a small leg extension, not a slap</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bubble Wrap Taps</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one light/soft ball (or balloon) per student, no other equipment needed — this warm-up is about gentle ball contact, not competition.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone spreads out with a full arm's reach of space and holds their own ball.</li><li>On 'go', students gently self-toss and tap their ball upward continuously, like a light 'bubble wrap' popping rhythm, moving around the space as needed to stay under the ball.</li><li>Keep the taps soft and controlled — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up soft hands, not maximum power.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze taps!' where students catch and hold, then resume on 'go' — this keeps attention sharp without stopping the flow for long.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to keep their ball moving the whole time.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Reach &amp; Roll</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead a slow, reaching stretch sequence — reach both arms overhead, roll gently down through the spine toward the toes, then roll back up slowly, repeating 3-4 times.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, roll, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get in position under the ball early, hands up and ready.</td><td>Being in position before the ball arrives gives cleaner technique.</td></tr><tr><td>Form a triangle or window shape with your hands above your forehead.</td><td>This hand shape gives a soft, controlled contact for the set.</td></tr><tr><td>Extend your legs and arms together to push the ball up and out.</td><td>Using the whole body gives consistent power and control, not just the fingers.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Window Shape Freeze</h4><p>Students hold their \"window\" hand shape above their forehead, self-toss a gentle ball into it, and freeze on catch to check finger position before pushing it back up.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students work individually, each with a ball, spread out with enough room to freeze and check their hand shape without bumping a neighbour.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Thumbs and index fingers form a triangle/window, elbows out and slightly bent.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Catch first, check shape, then push.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Remove the catch — push straight through on contact.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (\"freeze!\")</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the 'window' hand shape: thumbs and index fingers forming a triangle above the forehead, elbows out and slightly bent.</li><li>Students self-toss a gentle ball so it drops into their window shape above their forehead.</li><li>On catching the ball in the window shape, freeze for a second to check finger position before pushing it back upward.</li><li>If the toss goes wide or the ball is dropped, simply reset with a fresh gentle toss and continue.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'thumbs and index fingers form a triangle/window, elbows out and slightly bent.'</li><li>For 'easier', catch first, check the shape, then push the ball back up; for 'harder', remove the catch and push straight through on contact.</li></ul><hr><h4>Set to Self Streak</h4><p>Individually, set the ball to yourself as many times in a row as possible using fingers only, tracking a personal best.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own ball and their own space, roughly 2m x 2m, spread well apart from neighbours.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get under the ball early, contact it above and slightly in front of the forehead.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a small bounce between sets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Must move one step between each set (no standing still).</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate setting using fingers only, contacting the ball above and slightly in front of the forehead.</li><li>Students self-toss and begin setting the ball to themselves, counting each successful set out loud.</li><li>The aim is to get under the ball early each time rather than reaching for it late.</li><li>If the ball is dropped or mishandled, that's the end of the streak — note the personal best and restart immediately with a fresh toss.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'get under the ball early, contact it above and slightly in front of the forehead.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a small bounce between sets; for 'harder', require one step of movement between every set — no standing still.</li></ul><hr><h4>Partner Feed Set</h4><p>In pairs, one gently underarm-tosses the ball slightly above head height, the other sets it back; swap after 6 attempts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3m apart, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move the feet to get square and underneath the ball before it arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tosser stands closer, softer toss.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosser varies the toss left/right slightly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the roles: one partner gently underarm-tosses the ball slightly above head height, the other sets it back using fingers only.</li><li>The tosser begins each rep with a soft, predictable underarm toss aimed just above the receiver's head.</li><li>The receiver moves their feet to get square and underneath the ball before it arrives, then sets it back cleanly.</li><li>After 6 attempts, partners swap roles so both get equal practice tossing and setting.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move the feet to get square and underneath the ball before it arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', the tosser stands closer with a softer toss; for 'harder', the tosser varies the toss slightly left or right.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Set Battle Grids</h3><p>Pairs face off across a low rope/line in small grids, tossing/setting the ball back and forth, scoring a point if the ball lands in the opponent's grid after a legal set — the setting equivalent of Lesson 2's bump battle. Clean finger technique determines whose sets stay controlled and legal (no obvious catches/throws).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs face off across a low rope or line inside small grids about 3m x 3m per side, same layout as Lesson 2's Bump Battle Grids. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the game: toss or set the ball back and forth across the rope, scoring a point for your side if the ball lands inside the opponent's grid after a legal set.</li><li>One partner starts by tossing the ball over the rope into the opponent's grid to begin the rally.</li><li>From there, every return must be a legal, clean finger-set — no obvious catches or throws — sent back over the rope.</li><li>A point is scored whenever the ball lands inside the opponent's grid after a legal set, or if the receiving side fails to return it legally; play then restarts with a fresh toss from whoever conceded the point.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students get to set against a few different partners.</li><li>Keep a close eye on legality — the game only works as intended if sets are genuinely clean finger contacts, not disguised catches.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What did your hand shape look like when your set went well?</li><li>What's the difference between how a bump and a set feel?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try to fix next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Hands form a \"window\" shape above the forehead</li><li>Contact is with fingers, not palms</li><li>Uses a small leg extension for power, not just the arms</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For students who find finger contact uncomfortable, allow a two-hand 'catch and push' version first before removing the catch; softer/lighter balls reduce sting and build confidence.</p>"},"Lesson 5: Set the Target":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Set accuracy to a target</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Being patient and kind with yourself (and encouraging teammates) when a set doesn't go to plan — everyone's still learning.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>1 ball per pair, cones/hoops as targets</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Space grids apart; sets should stay at a safe, controlled height near other groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to set the ball so it travels accurately toward a chosen target.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a target before I set</li><li>I can set the ball so a partner doesn't have to move far to receive it</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Bubble Wrap Taps</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Open space roughly 20m x 20m for the whole class, one light/soft ball (or balloon) per student, no other equipment needed — this warm-up is about gentle ball contact, not competition.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Everyone spreads out with a full arm's reach of space and holds their own ball.</li><li>On 'go', students gently self-toss and tap their ball upward continuously, like a light 'bubble wrap' popping rhythm, moving around the space as needed to stay under the ball.</li><li>Keep the taps soft and controlled — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up soft hands, not maximum power.</li><li>Occasionally call 'freeze taps!' where students catch and hold, then resume on 'go' — this keeps attention sharp without stopping the flow for long.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging students to keep their ball moving the whole time.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Reach &amp; Roll</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed beyond the space already in use. Gather the class into a loose circle or scattered spread with room for each student to stretch without touching anyone else.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down gradually.</li><li>Gather into a circle (or stay scattered) and lead a slow, reaching stretch sequence — reach both arms overhead, roll gently down through the spine toward the toes, then roll back up slowly, repeating 3-4 times.</li><li>Keep instructions simple and calm: 'reach, roll, breathe' rather than technical cues.</li><li>Use this quiet time to briefly ask how the session felt for a couple of students, setting a relaxed tone before the Discussion segment.</li><li>Finish with everyone standing tall and taking one big breath in and out together.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Square your body and shoulders to the target before setting.</td><td>Your body position influences where the set actually goes.</td></tr><tr><td>Contact the ball with fingertips, not palms.</td><td>Fingertip contact is a legal, controlled touch that shapes the set accurately.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward the target with your arms.</td><td>Stopping the motion early can send the set short or off-line.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Hoop Target Set</h4><p>A partner tosses the ball; the setter aims to land it inside a hoop or cone square a few metres away, using a clean finger-set.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, each with one ball and one hoop (or cone square) placed a few metres from the setter.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Face the target with your whole body before the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger hoop, closer distance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target, further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate facing the target with the whole body before the ball arrives, not just reaching with the arms.</li><li>The tosser gently underarm-tosses the ball toward the setter, giving them time to get positioned.</li><li>The setter's job is to face the hoop and use a clean finger-set to land the ball inside it.</li><li>After each attempt, retrieve the ball and go again, building accuracy through repetition.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'face the target with your whole body before the ball arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger hoop and stand closer; for 'harder', use a smaller target further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Set to a Moving Partner</h4><p>Tosser jogs gently side to side; the setter must track and set accurately to wherever the tosser currently is.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with enough room for the tosser to move side to side, roughly 4-5m of lateral space, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Get underneath the ball early rather than reaching at the last second.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tosser moves slowly/predictably.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosser changes direction quickly.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: the tosser jogs gently side to side while tossing, and the setter must track their current position, not where they started.</li><li>The tosser begins moving at an easy, predictable pace side to side while tossing the ball toward the setter.</li><li>The setter gets underneath the ball early rather than reaching at the last second, adjusting their feet to the tosser's current spot.</li><li>Continue for a set number of reps, then swap roles so both partners practise setting to a moving target.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'get underneath the ball early rather than reaching at the last second.'</li><li>For 'easier', the tosser moves slowly and predictably; for 'harder', the tosser changes direction quickly.</li></ul><hr><h4>Triangle Set Relay</h4><p>Trios stand in a triangle; set the ball around the triangle in order as many times as possible without it hitting the ground, racing other trios.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of three stand in a triangle formation, spaced roughly 2-3m apart, one ball per trio, with enough room between trios.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the next receiver's name before you set.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch between sets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Alternate direction around the triangle on request.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling the next receiver's name clearly before setting the ball toward them.</li><li>One student starts with the ball and calls the name of whichever teammate they're setting to.</li><li>Play continues around the triangle, always calling the next name before contact, trying to keep the ball off the ground for as long as possible.</li><li>If the ball touches the ground, the trio simply restarts immediately with a fresh toss and keeps counting from zero.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call the next receiver's name before you set.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch between sets; for 'harder', the trio must alternate direction around the triangle on request.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Target Set Rally Court</h3><p>Small groups rally across a low net/rope, scoring bonus points any time a set lands inside a marked target zone on the other side, alongside normal rally points. Accuracy is what earns the extra points, not just height or power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups (4-6 students, split into two even sides) rally across a low net or rope, inside a court roughly 6m x 4m, with a marked target zone on each side of the net.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the scoring: normal rally points are earned as usual, but any set that lands inside the marked target zone on the opponent's side earns an extra bonus point.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a gentle toss or set over the net to begin play.</li><li>Players return the ball with legal sets, aiming for the target zone whenever they get the chance, while still keeping the rally going.</li><li>A rally point is scored when the other side fails to return the ball legally; a bonus point is added on top any time a set lands inside the target zone.</li><li>Restart quickly after each point with a fresh toss from the side that conceded, keeping the pace up.</li><li>Coach students to notice that accuracy — not just height or power — is what earns the extra points today.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you aim your set toward the target today?</li><li>How did an accurate set make it easier for your partner?</li><li>What happens to a rally when sets aren't accurate?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Body faces the target before contact</li><li>Gets underneath the ball early</li><li>Consistent, controlled height on sets rather than wild variation</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger targets and shorter distances for students still building consistency; allow a 'catch and reset' version of the set for students not yet confident with clean finger contact.</p>"}}},"Block 2: Combining Skills & Serving (Lessons 6–10)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 6: Bump to Set":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Combining bump → set</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how the two skills work together — knowing which one to use and when, based on how the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep enough space between pairs/trios so sequences don't collide</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to combine a bump and a set together in a short sequence, like the start of a real rally.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can bump the ball under control to myself or a partner</li><li>I can follow my bump with a clean set straight after</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pepper</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that a stray ball doesn't reach a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start gently tossing and bumping/setting the ball back and forth to each other, keeping it light and continuous.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pepper switch!' — on this call, partners quickly alternate between using bumps and sets, whichever the coach calls next.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue the pepper rally with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up both touches together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing touch calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call which touch you're taking to avoid confusion with a teammate.</td><td>Clear communication prevents both players going for the same ball.</td></tr><tr><td>Bump the ball up high and central to give the setter time and a good position.</td><td>A low, off-target bump makes the following set much harder.</td></tr><tr><td>Move into position early for your touch, don't wait for the ball to arrive.</td><td>Being in position before the ball arrives gives cleaner technique for both touches.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Bump-Set to Self</h4><p>Individually, self-toss, bump the ball up to yourself, then set it — repeating the bump-then-set sequence as many times as possible.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Each student has their own ball and their own space, roughly 2m x 2m, spread well apart from neighbours.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The bump should send the ball high enough and controlled enough to set cleanly next.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a small bounce or catch between the two actions.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No catching at all between bump and set.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sequence: self-toss, bump the ball up to yourself, then set it — repeating bump-then-set over and over.</li><li>Students begin with a gentle self-toss, followed by one bump contact, then one set contact, in that order.</li><li>The bump should send the ball high enough and controlled enough that the set that follows is easy to execute cleanly.</li><li>If the ball drops at any point, that's the end of the sequence count — students note their best and restart with a fresh toss.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'the bump should send the ball high enough and controlled enough to set cleanly next.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a small bounce or catch between the two actions; for 'harder', remove catching entirely between bump and set.</li></ul><hr><h4>Partner Bump-Set Relay</h4><p>In pairs, Player A tosses, Player B bumps it back to themselves then sets it to Player A, who catches and repeats.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet into position for the set immediately after the bump.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch between the bump and set.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>B must move sideways one step between the bump and the set.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sequence: Player A tosses, Player B bumps it to themselves then sets it back to Player A, who catches.</li><li>Player A starts with a gentle toss toward Player B.</li><li>Player B bumps the ball up to themselves first, then follows with a set sent back to Player A.</li><li>Player A catches the set, then repeats the toss to keep the cycle going — swap who tosses and who bump-sets after several reps.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move your feet into position for the set immediately after the bump.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch between the bump and the set; for 'harder', Player B must move sideways one step between the bump and the set.</li></ul><hr><h4>Trio Sequence Tag</h4><p>Trios pass in sequence — bump, set, catch — cycling through all three players, trying to keep the sequence going for as long as possible without the ball touching the ground.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of three stand spread out roughly 2-3m apart in a small triangle or line, one ball per trio, with enough room between trios.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call which action you're about to use ('bump!' or 'set!').</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch anywhere in the sequence if needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No catching allowed at all.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the full cycle: bump, set, catch, passed between the three players in sequence, calling out which action is coming.</li><li>One student starts the sequence by calling 'bump!' and bumping the ball to a teammate.</li><li>That teammate calls 'set!' and sets it on to the third player, who catches it before the cycle repeats.</li><li>The group keeps cycling through bump, set, catch for as long as possible without the ball touching the ground, restarting immediately with a fresh toss if it drops.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call which action you're about to use (\"bump!\" or \"set!\").'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch anywhere in the sequence if needed; for 'harder', remove catching allowed at all.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 2-Touch Rally Court</h3><p>Small groups rally across a low net; each side must use exactly two touches (a bump then a set, in either order) before sending the ball over. Combining the two skills cleanly is what keeps a rally alive.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups (4-6 students, split into two even sides) rally across a low net, inside a court roughly 6m x 4m.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the rule: each side must use exactly two touches — a bump then a set, in either order — before sending the ball over the net.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a toss over the net to begin play.</li><li>The receiving side must use exactly two touches (bump then set, or set then bump) before returning it, calling out which touch they're using as they go.</li><li>A point is scored if the other side fails to return the ball legally within the two-touch limit, or the ball lands out; play restarts quickly with a toss from whoever conceded.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students get variety in who they're combining skills against.</li><li>Coach students that combining the two skills cleanly — not rushing either touch — is what keeps their rally alive under the two-touch rule.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you go smoothly from a bump into a set today?</li><li>How did you decide which touch to use first?</li><li>What was tricky about combining the two skills, and how did you keep trying?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Bump sends the ball to a controlled, settable height</li><li>Quick footwork adjustment between the two actions</li><li>Calls out which action is coming</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a catch between the bump and the set for as long as needed; remove it gradually as confidence grows rather than all at once.</p>"},"Lesson 7: Serve It Up":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Serving technique (underarm)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Practising a skill faithfully, with focus and effort, even when it doesn't work first time — using your talent well takes repetition.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes or serving lines, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear serving lanes — no one stands in front of or beside a server during practice</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning correct underarm serving technique — how to strike the ball cleanly and legally over a net.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can hold the ball still in one hand and strike it with the heel of my other hand</li><li>I can step forward as I serve to add controlled power</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pepper</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that a stray ball doesn't reach a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start gently tossing and bumping/setting the ball back and forth to each other, keeping it light and continuous.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pepper switch!' — on this call, partners quickly alternate between using bumps and sets, whichever the coach calls next.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue the pepper rally with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up both touches together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing touch calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Hold the ball in front of you at waist height in your non-hitting hand.</td><td>A consistent set-up leads to a consistent serve.</td></tr><tr><td>Strike the ball with a straight arm, stepping forward as you hit.</td><td>The step adds control and a bit of power to the serve.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward your target.</td><td>Keeps the serve accurate rather than sending it off-line.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Technique Target Serves</h4><p>From a stationary stance, students serve underarm at a large target (a taped zone on a wall, or over a low rope into a big grid), focusing purely on technique, not power.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread along a serving line, each with a ball, facing a large target — either a taped zone on a wall or a big grid marked on the other side of a low net/rope a few metres away.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Ball held steady in front, step with the opposite foot, strike through the middle-back of the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer to the target/net.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further away.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the underarm serve: ball held steady in front, step with the opposite foot, strike through the middle-back of the ball.</li><li>Students take turns serving underarm from a stationary stance toward the large target, focusing purely on clean technique rather than power.</li><li>After each serve, retrieve the ball and go again — there's no elimination, just repeated technique reps.</li><li>Coach individual students between turns on any part of the technique that needs adjusting.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'ball held steady in front, step with the opposite foot, strike through the middle-back of the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', move the serving line closer to the target/net; for 'harder', move it further away.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve &amp; Track</h4><p>Students serve to a partner who calls out where it landed (short/long/left/right) to build the server's sense of their own tendencies.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, one serving line and one receiving zone marked a suitable distance apart, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Consistent contact point every time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Partner stands closer, bigger target zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Partner stands further, smaller zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (varied target calls)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the roles: the server serves underarm, the partner calls out where it landed (short, long, left, or right).</li><li>The server serves underarm toward their partner using consistent technique each time.</li><li>The receiving partner calls out where the serve landed immediately after each attempt, helping the server notice their own tendencies.</li><li>Continue for several serves, then swap roles so both partners get to serve and track.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'consistent contact point every time.'</li><li>For 'easier', the partner stands closer with a bigger target zone; for 'harder', the partner stands further away with a smaller zone.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve Streak Challenge</h4><p>In small groups, students take turns serving over a low net, tracking consecutive 'clean, legal technique' serves (judged on technique, not just success) as a group streak.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 take turns at a serving line, each group with one low net or rope and one ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Reset your technique each serve, don't chase power.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Closer serving line, bigger net gap.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Further serving line, add a target zone for bonus points.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate resetting technique fully before every serve rather than chasing extra power.</li><li>Students take turns serving over the low net, with the group tracking a shared streak of 'clean, legal technique' serves — judged on technique, not just whether it lands in.</li><li>The coach or a nominated student judges each serve on technique and confirms whether it counts toward the group streak.</li><li>If a serve is judged as poor technique (regardless of whether it went over), the streak resets to zero and the group starts counting again from the next serve.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'reset your technique each serve, don't chase power.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a closer serving line and a bigger net gap; for 'harder', use a further serving line and add a target zone for bonus points.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Serve &amp; Rally Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games begin every point with an underarm serve over a low net; coaching feedback emphasises clean serving technique first, with the rally as a bonus outcome — celebrate good technique attempts even if the serve is out.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that every point begins with an underarm serve over the net, and coaching feedback will focus on serve technique first.</li><li>The serving team's player steps up and serves underarm, using the technique practised earlier in the lesson.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally once the serve is legally returned, with points scored as usual.</li><li>Even if a serve goes out, celebrate a clean technique attempt out loud before play restarts with a fresh serve from the same or next server.</li><li>Rotate the serving role through the team so everyone gets serving turns across the games.</li><li>Keep reinforcing that a well-struck serve — even an unsuccessful one — is more valuable right now than a lucky, messy one that happens to land in.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What does 'good technique' feel like when you serve well?</li><li>What part of your serving technique are you most proud of today?</li><li>What will you keep practising?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Ball held steady before contact</li><li>Steps forward with the opposite foot to the striking hand</li><li>Strikes through the middle-back of the ball with a flat hand/heel of the palm</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow a shorter serving distance or a lighter ball for students building arm strength/confidence; success is measured by technique and legality, not distance/power.</p>"},"Lesson 8: Ace Zone":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Serving accuracy/placement</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where your serve will have the most impact (an open space on the court) rather than just serving anywhere.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones/hoops marking target zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Clear serving lanes; make sure target zones don't overlap with another group's serving area</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to place a serve into a specific part of the court, not just get it over the net.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose a target zone before I serve</li><li>I can place the ball into that zone more often than not</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pepper</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that a stray ball doesn't reach a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start gently tossing and bumping/setting the ball back and forth to each other, keeping it light and continuous.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pepper switch!' — on this call, partners quickly alternate between using bumps and sets, whichever the coach calls next.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue the pepper rally with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up both touches together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing touch calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Pick a specific target zone in the opponent's court before serving.</td><td>Aiming generally is far less accurate than committing to a spot.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep your toss or ball position consistent every serve.</td><td>An inconsistent set-up leads to inconsistent serves.</td></tr><tr><td>Use the same, repeatable technique regardless of the target.</td><td>Changing technique to 'aim' often causes more errors than trusting your normal serve.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Zone Serve Challenge</h4><p>The court is divided into marked zones (e.g. left, middle, right, deep, short); students call their target zone before serving and score points for hitting it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Divide the receiving side of the court into marked zones (e.g. left, middle, right, deep, short) using cones or hoops, one server at a time per court, ball per student turn.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Aim your body and follow-through toward the chosen zone, not just 'at the net.'</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer, bigger zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More, smaller zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate aiming the body and follow-through toward a chosen zone, not just generally 'at the net.'</li><li>Before each serve, the student calls out which zone they're aiming for.</li><li>They then serve underarm, trying to land the ball inside that called zone.</li><li>A point is scored for hitting the called zone; whether or not it scores, retrieve the ball and go again with a new zone call.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'aim your body and follow-through toward the chosen zone, not just \"at the net.\"'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer, bigger zones; for 'harder', use more, smaller zones.</li></ul><hr><h4>Called Target Serves</h4><p>A partner or the coach calls a target zone just before each serve, forcing a quick decision and adjustment.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or small groups with one server at a time, court zones already marked from the previous game, one ball per server.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Pick your aim point in the split second before you toss/strike.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Call the zone earlier, with more time to prepare.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Call the zone right before the serve.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate picking the aim point in the split second before tossing and striking, once a zone is called.</li><li>A partner or the coach calls out a target zone just before each serve, giving the server little time to adjust their plan.</li><li>The server reacts to the call and serves underarm, aiming for that zone.</li><li>Continue serving with a new called zone each time, retrieving the ball between attempts.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'pick your aim point in the split second before you toss/strike.'</li><li>For 'easier', call the zone earlier with more time to prepare; for 'harder', call the zone right before the serve.</li></ul><hr><h4>Serve Ladder</h4><p>Serving pairs/small groups move 'up' a ladder of increasingly small target zones as they succeed, and step back down after a miss, tracking how high they can climb.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups or pairs at a serving line, with a series of increasingly small target zones marked in a line or ladder pattern using cones.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay relaxed — rushing to climb faster often reduces accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger zone steps between ladder levels.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller zone steps.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate staying relaxed rather than rushing, since rushing to climb the ladder faster often reduces accuracy.</li><li>Students start serving at the first, easiest (biggest) zone on the ladder.</li><li>A successful serve into the current zone moves them up to the next, smaller zone; a miss moves them back down one level.</li><li>Students track how high up the ladder they can climb over their turns, trying to beat their own previous best.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'stay relaxed — rushing to climb faster often reduces accuracy.'</li><li>For 'easier', use bigger zone steps between ladder levels; for 'harder', use smaller zone steps.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Zone Ace Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games where a serve landing inside a marked 'ace zone' (away from the receiver) scores a bonus point immediately, on top of normal rally scoring. Thinking ahead about placement — not just getting it over — is what earns the bonus.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, with one marked 'ace zone' (away from the receiver's usual position) on each side of the court.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule: a serve landing inside the marked ace zone earns an immediate bonus point, on top of normal rally scoring.</li><li>The serving player looks at where the receiving team is standing and aims their serve toward the ace zone if the opportunity is there.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally after the serve is returned, with usual points scored for winning the point.</li><li>The bonus point is awarded the instant a serve lands in the ace zone, regardless of how the rally that follows turns out.</li><li>Rotate the serving role through each team so everyone gets chances to look for the bonus.</li><li>Coach students to notice that thinking ahead about placement — not just serving it over — is what earns the bonus today.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you choose and hit your target zone today?</li><li>How did placing your serve well change what happened next in the rally?</li><li>What's one zone you want to get more accurate at next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Chooses a target before serving (not just swinging and hoping)</li><li>Body/follow-through points toward the chosen zone</li><li>Adjusts technique calmly after a miss rather than rushing the next serve</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use bigger zones and shorter serving distances for students still building consistency; the ladder game scales naturally by adjusting zone sizes per group.</p>"},"Lesson 9: First Contact":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Receiving a serve</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Staying calm and patient with yourself when a serve is tricky to receive — everyone misses some early on.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones marking a target 'receive zone'</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for players backing into each other while tracking an incoming serve — call 'mine!' early</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to receive an incoming serve cleanly using a bump, so it's ready for a teammate's next touch.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can move into position early to receive an incoming serve</li><li>I can bump the serve under control toward a target area, not just anywhere</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pepper</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that a stray ball doesn't reach a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start gently tossing and bumping/setting the ball back and forth to each other, keeping it light and continuous.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pepper switch!' — on this call, partners quickly alternate between using bumps and sets, whichever the coach calls next.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue the pepper rally with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up both touches together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing touch calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get into a ready position early, platform prepared.</td><td>Reacting late to a serve leads to rushed, poor technique.</td></tr><tr><td>Move your feet to get in line with the serve's path.</td><td>Reaching sideways with the arms alone is less reliable.</td></tr><tr><td>Angle the platform toward your target, usually the setter.</td><td>A good first touch sets up the whole rally, not just returns the serve.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Serve &amp; Receive Pairs</h4><p>One partner serves gently underarm, the other receives with a bump aimed at a target zone; swap roles regularly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, a marked target zone set up on the receiving side, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move your feet early to get square to the incoming ball, don't reach with just your arms.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, closer serves.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Serves with more pace/variety.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (varied serve speed/placement)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the roles: one partner serves gently underarm, the other receives with a bump aimed at the target zone.</li><li>The server serves underarm toward their partner using a controlled, predictable technique.</li><li>The receiver moves their feet early to get square to the incoming ball, then bumps it toward the marked target zone.</li><li>Swap roles regularly so both partners get equal serving and receiving practice.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move your feet early to get square to the incoming ball, don't reach with just your arms.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, closer serves; for 'harder', use serves with more pace and variety.</li></ul><hr><h4>Receive &amp; Track</h4><p>As above, but the receiver's bump is scored on both control and accuracy toward the target zone, tracked as a running tally.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs setup as Serve &amp; Receive Pairs, with a marked target zone, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Platform angled toward the target, not just up.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger target zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller target zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate angling the platform toward the target, not just up, so the bump has direction as well as control.</li><li>The server serves underarm toward their partner as before.</li><li>The receiver bumps the serve, and the pair tracks two things together: whether the bump was controlled, and whether it landed in the target zone.</li><li>Keep a running tally of successful controlled-and-accurate bumps across several serves, then swap roles.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'platform angled toward the target, not just up.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger target zone; for 'harder', use a smaller target zone.</li></ul><hr><h4>Group Serve-Receive Rotation</h4><p>Small groups rotate through server and receiver roles, with receivers calling 'mine!' before contact to build the habit of early communication.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4, one server and one or two receivers per rotation, one ball per group, with clear space between groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and clearly, then move.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer receivers, more space each.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Two receivers close together must decide who calls it.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling 'mine!' clearly and early, before moving to the ball, so teammates know who's taking the serve.</li><li>The server serves underarm toward the group of receivers.</li><li>Whichever receiver is taking the ball calls 'mine!' immediately and clearly before contact, then bumps it under control.</li><li>Rotate server and receiver roles regularly so everyone in the group gets a turn at each role.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call early and clearly, then move.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer receivers with more space each; for 'harder', place two receivers close together who must decide who calls it.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Serve-Receive Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games where every point starts with a serve; the receiving team scores a bonus point if their first touch is a controlled bump that lands in a marked target zone for their teammate. Clean receiving is what sets up everything that follows.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, with a marked target zone on the receiving side of each court.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule: every point starts with a serve, and the receiving team scores a bonus point if their first touch is a controlled bump landing in the marked target zone for a teammate.</li><li>The serving player serves underarm to begin the point.</li><li>The receiving team's first touch must be a bump — if it's controlled and lands in the target zone for a teammate, the bonus point is awarded immediately.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally regardless, with the usual point scored for winning the rally.</li><li>Rotate the serving role and receiving order through each team across the games.</li><li>Coach students to notice that a clean, well-placed first touch is what sets up everything the team does for the rest of the rally.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you get in position early to receive today?</li><li>Why does a good first touch on a serve matter for the rest of the rally?</li><li>What will you focus on next time a serve comes to you?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Moves into position early rather than reaching at the last second</li><li>Calls 'mine!' before contact in group settings</li><li>Bump is aimed at a target, not just returned anywhere</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow serves right down and reduce the target distance for students building confidence; allow a catch-and-bump hybrid for receiving until the clean bump is consistent.</p>"},"Lesson 10: Two-Touch Tango":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rally building — 2-touch games</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding how your skills fit together with a partner's — knowing your role in a two-touch sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep enough space between courts so rallies (and calls) don't interfere with neighbouring groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to build a short rally using exactly two touches per side, combining our skills so far.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use a bump and a set together to send the ball over in two touches</li><li>I can communicate with a partner about who takes which touch</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Partner Pepper</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, roughly 3-4m apart per pair with enough room between pairs that a stray ball doesn't reach a neighbouring pair. One ball per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Partners start gently tossing and bumping/setting the ball back and forth to each other, keeping it light and continuous.</li><li>Every 30-45 seconds, call 'pepper switch!' — on this call, partners quickly alternate between using bumps and sets, whichever the coach calls next.</li><li>Occasionally call 'jog swap' — both partners jog to find a new partner nearby, then continue the pepper rally with them.</li><li>Keep the whole activity light and continuous — the goal is raising heart rate and warming up both touches together, not technical correction.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, mixing touch calls and partner swaps throughout.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Partner Stretch Circle</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs from the warm-up (or new pairs), enough space to stretch side-by-side without touching. Balls set aside.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Have pairs walk together slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In pairs, lead 3-4 simple partner-assisted stretches — e.g. one partner gently holds the other's arm across their chest for a shoulder stretch, or both sit and reach toward each other's feet for a hamstring stretch.</li><li>Hold each stretch for about 15-20 seconds, then swap who is being assisted if the stretch requires it.</li><li>Keep contact gentle and consent-based — no forcing a stretch further than a partner is comfortable with.</li><li>Finish with partners saying one thing their partner did well in today's session before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call which touch you're taking.</td><td>Prevents confusion and collisions between teammates.</td></tr><tr><td>Aim your first touch to set up a teammate's second touch.</td><td>Each touch should have a clear purpose within the short rally.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay ready and moving between touches, not static.</td><td>Volleyball rallies move fast — staying ready keeps you involved.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>2-Touch Wall Rally</h4><p>Pairs rally against a wall (or with each other), using exactly two touches — a bump then a set, in either order — before the ball must go over/across.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs at a wall (or facing each other if no wall is available), one ball per pair, roughly 3-4m back from the wall or from each other.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Decide early which touch you'll use based on how the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow extra touches if needed while learning.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Strict two touches only, no more.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate deciding early which touch to use — bump or set — based on how the ball is arriving.</li><li>One partner starts the rally by hitting the ball against the wall (or across to their partner).</li><li>Each side gets exactly two touches — a bump then a set, or a set then a bump, in either order — before the ball must go back across or against the wall again.</li><li>If the ball is dropped, mishandled, or more than two touches are used, the pair simply restarts with a fresh toss and keeps rallying.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'decide early which touch you'll use based on how the ball arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow extra touches if needed while learning; for 'harder', enforce strict two touches only.</li></ul><hr><h4>Call Your Touch</h4><p>As above, but each player must call out ('bump!' or 'set!') which touch they're about to use before contact, building awareness of the choice being made.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Same pairs setup as 2-Touch Wall Rally, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call early and confidently — hesitation causes mistakes.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Coach suggests which touch to call.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Players choose freely and must justify their choice afterward.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling out 'bump!' or 'set!' clearly before each contact, building awareness of the choice being made.</li><li>Play the same two-touch rally as before, but each player must call out which touch they're about to use before contact.</li><li>Continue the rally as long as possible, with both touches called out loud every time.</li><li>If the ball drops, restart with a fresh toss and keep calling touches throughout.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call early and confidently — hesitation causes mistakes.'</li><li>For 'easier', the coach suggests which touch to call; for 'harder', players choose freely and must justify their choice afterward.</li></ul><hr><h4>2-Touch Grid Battle</h4><p>Small teams face off across a low net in a marked grid, playing short rallies using a strict 2-touch rule, scoring a point when the other side fails to return it legally.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams (2-3 per side) face off across a low net in a marked grid roughly 4m x 4m per side, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate loudly about who's taking which touch.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, more time between serves.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker restarts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate communicating loudly about who's taking which touch before the ball arrives.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a toss over the net.</li><li>Both sides must use the strict 2-touch rule (bump then set, or set then bump) before sending the ball back over.</li><li>A point is scored when the other side fails to return it legally within the two touches, and play restarts quickly with a toss from whoever conceded.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'communicate loudly about who's taking which touch.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with more time between serves; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with quicker restarts.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: 2-Touch Rally Tournament</h3><p>Small-sided games (2v2/3v3) across low nets, strictly 2 touches per side, with simple rally scoring. Combining skills cleanly under the two-touch limit is what keeps a team's rallies alive.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: strictly 2 touches per side, simple rally scoring, playing several short matches against rotating opponents.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Both sides must combine exactly two touches (bump then set, or set then bump) before returning the ball, calling out their touches as needed.</li><li>A point is scored when the other side fails to legally return within the two-touch limit or the ball lands out; play restarts quickly.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students play several different pairs or trios across the session.</li><li>Coach students that combining skills cleanly under the two-touch limit — not rushing either contact — is what keeps a team's rallies alive.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team keep a two-touch rally going today?</li><li>How did calling your touch out loud help (or not help) your team?</li><li>What's one thing your team will try to improve next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Decides and calls the touch early</li><li>Touches are clean and controlled (not rushed)</li><li>Communicates with teammates about roles/coverage</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Relax the strict two-touch rule for teams still building consistency, allowing a bonus catch-and-restart when needed, tightening the rule as the block progresses.</p>"}}},"Block 3: Rally Building, Movement & Positioning (Lessons 11–15)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 11: Bump-Set-Return":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rally building — 3-touch games (bump-set-return)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self, Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using your skills faithfully to serve your team's rally — taking your touch fully, not leaving it for someone else.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Keep clear space behind each player for the third touch/return so no one backs into another player</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to build a rally using the full three touches — bump, set, and a return touch — like a real team sequence.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use up to three touches to control, set up, and return the ball</li><li>I can move to cover a teammate's touch</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Court Ghosts</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'ghost' who must copy the leader's court movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader shuffles, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — echoing the footwork and space-reading focus of this block.</li><li>The ghost tries to mirror every change in direction and stance as quickly as possible, staying low and ready.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more court-realistic movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balanced footwork used throughout this block.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Use the right touch — bump, set, or hit — for each situation.</td><td>Each touch type suits a different moment in the rally.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate clearly between all three touches.</td><td>Three players need to coordinate smoothly for the sequence to work.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep each touch controlled and purposeful.</td><td>A rushed or careless touch early in the sequence makes the later touches much harder.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Trio 3-Touch Circuit</h4><p>Trios pass in a fixed sequence — bump, set, return (a light tap or hit) — trying to complete the full sequence cleanly as many times as possible.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of three spread out roughly 2-3m apart, one ball per trio, with enough room between trios.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>The first touch should make the second touch easy, and so on — think one step ahead.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Allow a catch between touches.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>No catching, continuous sequence.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the fixed sequence: bump, set, return (a light tap or hit), passed in that order between the three players.</li><li>One student starts the sequence with a bump to a teammate.</li><li>That teammate sets it on to the third player, who completes the sequence with a light return touch.</li><li>The trio tries to complete the full bump-set-return sequence cleanly as many times in a row as possible, restarting immediately with a fresh toss if the ball drops.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'the first touch should make the second touch easy, and so on — think one step ahead.'</li><li>For 'easier', allow a catch between touches; for 'harder', remove catching for a continuous sequence.</li></ul><hr><h4>3-Touch Wall Rally</h4><p>Pairs/trios rally against a wall using all three touch types across three consecutive contacts before it must go over/across again.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or trios at a wall (or facing each other), one ball per group, roughly 3-4m back from the wall.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate who's taking which touch before the ball arrives.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slow the pace, allow resets.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Add a time limit or shrink the space.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate communicating who's taking which touch before the ball arrives.</li><li>One player starts the rally by hitting the ball against the wall or across to the group.</li><li>The group uses up to three consecutive contacts — bump, set, and a return touch — before the ball must go over or against the wall again.</li><li>If the ball drops or more than three touches are used, restart with a fresh toss and keep going.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'communicate who's taking which touch before the ball arrives.'</li><li>For 'easier', slow the pace and allow resets; for 'harder', add a time limit or shrink the space.</li></ul><hr><h4>3-Touch Grid Battle</h4><p>Small teams play short rallies across a low net using up to three touches per side, scoring when the other side fails to legally return it.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams (2-3 per side) play across a low net in a marked grid roughly 4m x 4m per side, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>First touch under control, second touch sets up a teammate, third touch sends it over.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger grid, slower restarts.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller grid, quicker restarts.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the sequence idea: first touch under control, second touch sets up a teammate, third touch sends it over.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a toss over the net.</li><li>Each side can use up to three touches before returning the ball, aiming for a clean bump-set-return pattern where possible.</li><li>A point is scored when the other side fails to legally return the ball within three touches, and play restarts quickly.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'first touch under control, second touch sets up a teammate, third touch sends it over.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger grid with slower restarts; for 'harder', use a smaller grid with quicker restarts.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Rally Round-Robin</h3><p>Small-sided games (2v2/3v3) across low nets, up to three touches per side, simple rally scoring, rotating opponents every few minutes. Building a clean bump-set-return sequence is what keeps a team's rallies going the longest.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game, rotating opponents every few minutes.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: up to three touches per side, simple rally scoring, playing several short round-robin games against different opponents.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Both sides aim to build a clean bump-set-return sequence within their three allowed touches, communicating who's taking each touch.</li><li>A point is scored when the other side fails to legally return the ball, and play restarts quickly with a fresh toss.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students play several different teams across the round-robin.</li><li>Coach students that building a clean bump-set-return sequence — not just surviving the rally — is what keeps their team's rallies going the longest.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team build a full three-touch rally today?</li><li>How did your first touch set up the rest of the sequence?</li><li>What's one thing your team wants to improve about working together across three touches?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>First touch is controlled and sets up the next player</li><li>Communicates about who's taking which touch</li><li>Moves to cover space after their own touch, rather than standing still</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow teams to use a catch-and-restart on any touch that breaks down, tightening this as confidence with the full sequence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 12: Quick Feet":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Moving to the ball (footwork)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Managing self</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about where the ball is going and getting there early, so your whole team benefits from your positioning.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking movement patterns</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for collisions during fast footwork drills — keep enough space between pairs/groups</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to move our feet quickly and get into position under the ball before it arrives.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can shuffle sideways/forwards/backwards to get square to an incoming ball</li><li>I can be in position and ready before the ball reaches me, not reaching at the last second</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Court Ghosts</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'ghost' who must copy the leader's court movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader shuffles, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — echoing the footwork and space-reading focus of this block.</li><li>The ghost tries to mirror every change in direction and stance as quickly as possible, staying low and ready.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more court-realistic movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balanced footwork used throughout this block.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Take small, quick adjusting steps rather than one big lunge.</td><td>Small steps keep you balanced and ready to change direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Get your body in line with the ball before you make contact.</td><td>Positioning first means your technique doesn't have to compensate for being out of position.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay on the balls of your feet, ready to move any direction.</td><td>A flat-footed stance is slow to react.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Shuffle &amp; Freeze</h4><p>Students shuffle in ready position (low, on the balls of the feet) in response to called directions ('left! right! back! forward!'), freezing in a balanced, ready stance on each call.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students spread out across an open space, roughly 20m x 20m for the class, with enough room between students to shuffle without colliding.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Stay low, small quick steps rather than crossing feet.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower calls, bigger pauses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, mixed calls.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the ready position: low stance, on the balls of the feet, ready to move quickly in any direction.</li><li>Students start in the ready position, waiting for a direction call.</li><li>On each call ('left! right! back! forward!'), students shuffle a few steps in that direction using small quick steps, not crossing their feet.</li><li>On the freeze call, they stop and hold a balanced, ready stance until the next direction is called.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'stay low, small quick steps rather than crossing feet.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower calls with bigger pauses; for 'harder', use faster, mixed-up calls.</li></ul><hr><h4>Move &amp; Bump</h4><p>A partner tosses the ball to different spots around the receiver (not straight at them), forcing footwork before each bump.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, one ball per pair, with enough room for a tosser to send the ball to different spots around the receiver.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Move first, then set the platform — don't reach while still moving.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Tosses closer together, more predictable.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosses further apart, less predictable.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate moving first, then setting the platform, rather than reaching for the ball while still moving.</li><li>The tosser gently tosses the ball to different spots around the receiver, rather than straight at them.</li><li>The receiver reacts by moving their feet to get in position before setting their platform to bump.</li><li>Continue for several tosses, then swap roles so both partners get practice moving to the ball.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'move first, then set the platform — don't reach while still moving.'</li><li>For 'easier', tosses land closer together and more predictably; for 'harder', tosses land further apart and less predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>Footwork Relay Ladder</h4><p>Small groups race through a footwork pattern (shuffle-shuffle-sprint-recover) marked by cones before receiving a tossed ball at the end and bumping it to a target.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 3-4 at a footwork course marked with cones (shuffle-shuffle-sprint-recover pattern), one ball and one target zone at the end of each course.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Arrive under control, not off-balance, so the bump stays clean.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler pattern, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More complex pattern, timed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock, head-to-head mirror</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the footwork pattern first — shuffle, shuffle, sprint, recover — moving through the cones under control.</li><li>One student at a time races through the footwork pattern marked by cones.</li><li>At the end of the pattern, a tosser sends them a ball which they must bump toward a target while arriving under control, not off-balance.</li><li>The rest of the group waits their turn, cheering the racer through; rotate through the whole group.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'arrive under control, not off-balance, so the bump stays clean.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a simpler pattern with more time; for 'harder', use a more complex, timed pattern.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Move-to-Score Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided rally games across low nets where good footwork (arriving early, balanced, and square to the ball) is highlighted by the coach as the difference between a clean return and a scramble. Quick feet directly support every other skill in the game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: good footwork — arriving early, balanced, and square to the ball — will be highlighted throughout as the difference between a clean return and a scramble.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play a normal rally, with players trying to move their feet early to get set up for each touch rather than reaching at the last moment.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes, and specifically call out moments of good footwork as they happen.</li><li>Coach students that quick feet directly support every other skill in the game — a good platform starts with good positioning.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you get into position early today, instead of reaching at the last second?</li><li>How did good footwork make your bump or set easier?</li><li>What will you focus on with your feet next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Stays low and ready between touches</li><li>Uses small shuffle steps rather than crossing feet or lunging</li><li>Arrives balanced under the ball before contact</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Slow all footwork patterns down and reduce distances for students building coordination/confidence; celebrate early movement even if the resulting touch isn't perfect yet.</p>"},"Lesson 13: Mine!":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Communication — calling the ball</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Relating to others</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Speaking up clearly and kindly so teammates aren't confused, and trusting a teammate's call instead of competing for the same ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Watch for two players going for the same ball without calling — this is exactly the collision risk this lesson addresses</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to communicate clearly and early about who is taking the ball.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can call \"mine!\" loudly and early when I'm taking the ball</li><li>I can listen for a teammate's call and move out of their way</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Court Ghosts</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'ghost' who must copy the leader's court movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader shuffles, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — echoing the footwork and space-reading focus of this block.</li><li>The ghost tries to mirror every change in direction and stance as quickly as possible, staying low and ready.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more court-realistic movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balanced footwork used throughout this block.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Call 'mine!' clearly and early when you're taking the ball.</td><td>Early, clear calls prevent collisions and confusion.</td></tr><tr><td>Listen for teammates' calls and move out of the way if it's not yours.</td><td>Communication only works if everyone actually responds to it.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep calling throughout fast exchanges, not just once.</td><td>Fast rallies need constant communication, not a single call at the start.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Call &amp; Catch</h4><p>In small groups, a ball is tossed into a shared space; whoever calls 'mine!' first and loudest gets to take it, while others step back.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups of 4-5 stand spread out in a shared space roughly 4m x 4m, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call the instant you decide, don't wait to see if someone else calls first.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Clear pauses between tosses.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Tosses closer together/faster, more overlap.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling 'mine!' the instant you decide to go for the ball, rather than waiting to see if someone else calls first.</li><li>The coach or a nominated student tosses the ball up into the shared space.</li><li>Whoever calls 'mine!' first and loudest gets to take the ball, while everyone else steps back to give them room.</li><li>The catcher then tosses it back into the space for the next toss, and the group repeats.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call the instant you decide, don't wait to see if someone else calls first.'</li><li>For 'easier', use clear pauses between tosses; for 'harder', use tosses closer together and faster, with more overlap.</li></ul><hr><h4>Silent Freeze, Loud Call</h4><p>Pairs/trios stand still (no ball) until a toss arrives; the moment someone calls it, everyone else must freeze so the caller has clear space.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs or trios stand still spread out, roughly 3m apart, no ball needed until the toss.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your teammate's call — don't chase a ball someone else has claimed.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer players sharing the space.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More players, more overlap zones.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule: everyone stands still until a toss arrives, and the moment someone calls it, everyone else freezes so the caller has clear space.</li><li>The coach tosses a ball toward one of the small groups.</li><li>The player who wants it calls it clearly and immediately; everyone else in that group freezes in place.</li><li>The caller moves to take the ball, then it's tossed back for the next round with a different group.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'trust your teammate's call — don't chase a ball someone else has claimed.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer players sharing the space; for 'harder', use more players with more overlap zones.</li></ul><hr><h4>2-on-Ball Rally</h4><p>Pairs receive tosses/serves aimed into the space between them, practising calling early and clearly across a full rally sequence (bump, set, return).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out with a marked space between them, roughly 4m x 4m, one ball per pair.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Call before you move, not while you're already moving toward the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Clearly divide the space in half to start.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Remove the dividing line, full shared space.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate calling before moving toward the ball, not while already moving toward it.</li><li>A toss or serve is aimed into the space between the two partners.</li><li>Whoever is taking the ball calls it early and clearly, then moves to bump, set, or return it as part of a full rally sequence.</li><li>Continue the rally sequence (bump, set, return) as long as possible, calling every ball clearly throughout.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'call before you move, not while you're already moving toward the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', clearly divide the space in half to start; for 'harder', remove the dividing line for a full shared space.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Communication Cup Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games across low nets where the coach specifically praises clear, early calling ('mine!') as part of the scoring feedback — teams are encouraged to call constantly, not just when there's doubt. Clear communication is what prevents collisions and dropped balls.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: clear, early calling of 'mine!' will be specifically praised as part of the feedback, and teams should call constantly, not just when there's doubt.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play a normal rally, with players calling the ball loudly and early every time they go to make contact.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes, calling out great examples of clear, early communication as they happen.</li><li>Coach students that clear communication is what prevents collisions and dropped balls — it's a skill just like any touch.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What made calling 'mine!' easier or harder today?</li><li>How did it feel to trust a teammate's call and step back?</li><li>What will your team focus on communicating next time?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Calls early and loudly, before reaching the ball</li><li>Steps back/out of the way when a teammate calls</li><li>Doesn't compete for a ball once someone else has claimed it</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>For quieter/less confident students, practise the call in low-pressure pairs first before adding it into busier group games; celebrate any clear call, even a quiet one, as a good start.</p>"},"Lesson 14: Cover the Court":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Positioning — covering space as a team</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding your role and position within the team's shape — knowing where you fit so the whole team is covered.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, cones marking court zones, bibs (useful for identifying zones/positions)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard court spacing — ensure zones don't overlap with neighbouring games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to spread out and cover space as a team so the court doesn't have obvious gaps.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can find and stand in space rather than crowding a teammate</li><li>I can shift my position as the ball moves around the court</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Court Ghosts</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'ghost' who must copy the leader's court movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader shuffles, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — echoing the footwork and space-reading focus of this block.</li><li>The ghost tries to mirror every change in direction and stance as quickly as possible, staying low and ready.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more court-realistic movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balanced footwork used throughout this block.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know your area of responsibility on the court.</td><td>Understanding your zone reduces confusion about who covers what.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjust your position based on where the ball and teammates are.</td><td>Positioning should respond to the game, not stay fixed.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate about gaps or overlaps with teammates.</td><td>A talking team closes court gaps faster than a silent one.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Fill the Gap</h4><p>Small teams stand on a marked court with obvious 'gap' zones; on a call, they must reposition so every zone has someone in it, as quickly as possible.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams (4-5 students) stand on a marked court with obvious zones drawn using cones, leaving visible 'gap' zones unfilled at the start.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Scan the whole court, not just the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer zones, more time.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>More zones, timed.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate scanning the whole court for empty zones, not just watching the ball.</li><li>On a call, the team must quickly reposition so that every marked zone has at least one player standing in it.</li><li>The coach checks (or calls out) whether every zone is covered, then calls again to reset with a new random gap pattern.</li><li>Repeat several times, encouraging students to move quickly but under control each time.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'scan the whole court, not just the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', use fewer zones with more time; for 'harder', use more zones under a time limit.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow the Ball</h4><p>As a tosser moves the ball around the court (without hitting it into play), teammates shift their position to stay balanced and covered as a group, without being told individually where to stand.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams positioned on a marked court, one tosser (coach or student) who moves the ball around without hitting it into play.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>If a teammate moves toward the ball, someone should shift to cover the space they left.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower ball movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, less predictable movement.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea: as the ball moves, teammates shift their position as a group to stay balanced and covered, without being individually told where to stand.</li><li>The tosser walks or jogs around the court edge, carrying the ball to different spots without putting it into play.</li><li>The team on court shifts their positioning together to stay balanced, with whoever is nearest the ball's current position adjusting first and others covering the space they leave.</li><li>Continue for several minutes, with the tosser moving unpredictably to test the team's shifting.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'if a teammate moves toward the ball, someone should shift to cover the space they left.'</li><li>For 'easier', move the ball more slowly; for 'harder', move it faster and less predictably.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v3 Space-Finding Rally</h4><p>Small teams rally across a low net, with bonus points awarded any time good spacing (no obvious gaps, no two players standing together) is noticed during a point.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small teams of three play across a low net on a court roughly 6m x 4m per side, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check your position between every touch, not just when the ball comes to you.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Larger court/fewer players.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller court/more players.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate checking position between every touch, not just when the ball is coming to you.</li><li>One side starts the rally with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally, with the coach watching for good spacing — no obvious gaps, no two players standing together.</li><li>Bonus points are awarded any time the coach notices good spacing during a point, on top of normal rally scoring.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'check your position between every touch, not just when the ball comes to you.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a larger court with fewer players; for 'harder', use a smaller court with more players.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Covered Court Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games across low nets where team positioning/spacing is specifically coached and praised alongside the usual rally scoring — a team that stays well spread and covered will have far fewer gaps for the ball to drop into.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: team positioning and spacing will be specifically coached and praised alongside the usual rally scoring.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play a normal rally, with players actively working to stay spread out and cover the court as a team, not just as individuals.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball; the coach calls out well-covered court shape as it happens.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students practise spacing against a few different teams.</li><li>Coach students that a team that stays well spread and covered will have far fewer gaps for the ball to drop into.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team stay spread out and cover the space today?</li><li>How did you decide where to move when the ball moved?</li><li>What's one gap your team noticed and fixed today?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Scans the whole court, not just the ball</li><li>Shifts to cover a space a teammate has just left</li><li>Avoids standing directly next to another teammate</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use fewer, bigger zones for teams still learning to read space collectively; simple visual markers (cones/bibs per zone) help less confident students know where 'their' space is at first.</p>"},"Lesson 15: Directed Hit":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Attacking touch / directed hit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Using a stronger touch responsibly and fairly, with control, rather than just hitting as hard as possible.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones marking target zones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Attacking touches should stay controlled and at a safe height for this age group — no jumping/spiking with full force yet</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to direct a stronger touch (a light attacking hit) toward open space on the other side.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can choose where the open space is before I hit</li><li>I can direct my hit with more purpose than just sending it back anywhere</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Court Ghosts</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out across the space, no ball needed for this warm-up, roughly 5m x 5m of room per pair.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Explain the game: one partner is the 'leader,' the other is the 'ghost' who must copy the leader's court movement as closely as possible, staying about 1-2m behind or beside them.</li><li>The leader shuffles, jinks side to side, changes direction and pace — echoing the footwork and space-reading focus of this block.</li><li>The ghost tries to mirror every change in direction and stance as quickly as possible, staying low and ready.</li><li>Swap roles every 60-90 seconds so both partners lead and shadow.</li><li>Run for the full 5 minutes, encouraging bigger, more court-realistic movements as students warm up.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Breathe &amp; Balance</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>No equipment needed. Gather the class into a spread-out circle with room to balance without bumping others.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly around the space for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>Lead a few slow, controlled breathing cycles together: breathe in for 4 counts, hold for 2, breathe out for 4 — repeat 3-4 times.</li><li>Follow with 2-3 simple balance holds, e.g. standing on one foot for 10-15 seconds, then swap feet — this connects to the balanced footwork used throughout this block.</li><li>Keep the tone calm and unhurried — this is a good moment to help students settle before reflecting on the lesson.</li><li>Finish standing tall with one final deep breath together before moving into the Discussion segment.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Get into position early, side-on to your target.</td><td>Body position controls the direction of your hit.</td></tr><tr><td>Contact the ball with a firm wrist, aiming rather than just swinging hard.</td><td>Control and direction matter more than pure power at this stage.</td></tr><tr><td>Follow through toward your target area.</td><td>Stopping the arm early reduces accuracy.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Open Space Tap</h4><p>From a gentle self/partner toss, students direct a controlled tap/hit toward whichever half of a marked grid is currently open (marked by a mobile cone).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Students work in pairs or individually with a gentle self/partner toss, inside a marked grid roughly 5m x 5m split into two halves by a mobile cone.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Look at the open space before you hit, not at the ball the whole time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger open zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller/moving open zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate looking at the open space before hitting, not staring at the ball the whole time.</li><li>A gentle self-toss or partner toss sets up the ball for a controlled tap or hit.</li><li>The student looks toward whichever half of the grid is currently marked open (by the mobile cone) and directs their touch there.</li><li>After each attempt, move the cone to change which half is open and repeat.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'look at the open space before you hit, not at the ball the whole time.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger open zone; for 'harder', use a smaller or moving open zone.</li></ul><hr><h4>Set-and-Direct</h4><p>In trios, one sets the ball up nicely for a partner, who directs a controlled attacking touch toward a called target zone.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Groups of three, one ball per trio, with a marked target zone a few metres from the hitter.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Approach at an angle that lets you direct the hit, not just swat straight down.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Target called well in advance.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Target called at the last moment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate approaching the ball at an angle that lets you direct the hit, rather than swatting straight down.</li><li>One student sets the ball up nicely for a partner, giving them an easy, controllable ball to hit.</li><li>The hitting partner directs a controlled attacking touch toward a called target zone, using body angle to aim the shot.</li><li>Rotate through all three roles (setter, hitter, target-caller) regularly so everyone practises each part.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'approach at an angle that lets you direct the hit, not just swat straight down.'</li><li>For 'easier', call the target well in advance; for 'harder', call it at the last moment.</li></ul><hr><h4>Directed Hit Streak</h4><p>Small groups rotate through a set-and-hit station, tracking a team streak of 'controlled, well-directed' attacking touches (judged on control and direction, not power).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rotate through a set-and-hit station, one ball per group, with a marked target zone.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Control first, direction second, power a distant third.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, easier sets to hit from.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied sets requiring adjustment.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks, bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the priority order: control first, direction second, power a distant third.</li><li>A setter sets the ball up for the hitter, who attempts a controlled, well-directed attacking touch toward the target.</li><li>The group tracks a running streak of touches judged as 'controlled and well-directed' — judged on control and direction, not power.</li><li>If a touch is judged uncontrolled or poorly directed, the streak resets and the group keeps going, rotating roles regularly.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'control first, direction second, power a distant third.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, easier sets to hit from; for 'harder', use varied sets that require adjustment.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Direct &amp; Defend Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games across low nets where a legal, controlled directed hit into open space scores a bonus point, encouraging players to look for space rather than just returning the ball anywhere. Reading the open space and directing the touch is today's decisive skill.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the bonus rule: a legal, controlled directed hit into open space scores a bonus point, encouraging players to look for space rather than just returning the ball anywhere.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play a normal rally, with attacking players looking for open space before directing their touch, at a controlled height and force for this age group.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball; a bonus point is awarded immediately for a controlled, well-directed hit into open space.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students get to practise directing hits against different defensive setups.</li><li>Coach students that reading the open space and directing the touch — done under control, not force — is today's decisive skill.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped you spot open space before hitting today?</li><li>How is directing a hit different from just returning the ball anywhere?</li><li>What's one thing you'll try next time you get the chance to attack the ball?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Looks at the open space before contact</li><li>Directs the hit with a clear intention, not just reacting</li><li>Keeps control/technique even when trying to be more attacking</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Keep all attacking touches at chest height or below and emphasise placement over power for this age group; allow a firm-tap version instead of a full hit for students still building confidence/strength.</p>"}}},"Block 4: Team Tactics & Culmination (Lessons 16–20)":{"label":"Lesson","children":{"Lesson 16: Read & React":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Reading the opponent's return</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Thinking ahead about the impact of reading the game well — reacting early helps the whole team, not just you.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard court spacing; encourage controlled reactions rather than diving/lunging</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to read where an opponent's return is likely to go, and react early.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can watch an opponent's body/hand shape to predict where their return will go</li><li>I can start moving toward the likely landing spot before the ball arrives</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Rotation Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple position prompts as they jog: 'rotate!' (team shuffles jogging order by one spot), 'spread wide,' 'come together' — echoing the team rotation and tactics focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape and order before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several prompts over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Watch the opponent's body shape and arm swing before they hit.</td><td>Their technique often signals where the ball is going before contact.</td></tr><tr><td>Move early based on your read, not after the ball is already coming.</td><td>Reacting after the hit is often too late in a fast rally.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay in a ready, balanced position while reading.</td><td>You need to be able to move instantly once you've made your read.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Guess the Return</h4><p>A partner sets up to hit/direct a touch; before it happens, the other partner calls out a guess for where it will go, then reacts.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs spread out, one ball per pair, with room for a hitter to set up and a defender to react.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Watch the shoulders and hand shape, not just the ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Hitter exaggerates their aim/body shape as a visual clue.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Hitter tries to disguise the direction.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate watching the shoulders and hand shape of a hitter, not just the ball, to predict where a touch is going.</li><li>One partner sets up to hit or direct a touch toward the other.</li><li>Before contact, the defending partner calls out a guess for where the ball will go, then reacts and tries to get there.</li><li>Compare the guess to where the ball actually went, then swap roles and repeat.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'watch the shoulders and hand shape, not just the ball.'</li><li>For 'easier', the hitter exaggerates their aim/body shape as a visual clue; for 'harder', the hitter tries to disguise the direction.</li></ul><hr><h4>Shadow Defender Rally</h4><p>In small rallies, a defending player tries to anticipate and move toward the likely return before it's hit, without a ball at first (shadowing), then with real returns.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rally in a marked space roughly 5m x 5m, starting without a ball for the shadowing phase, then adding a ball.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Small, ready movements — don't commit too early or you can't adjust.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, more obvious hits to read.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, more varied hits.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Added defender/pressure</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate small, ready movements rather than committing too early, since over-committing means you can't adjust.</li><li>Start without a ball: the defending player shadows the likely return by anticipating and moving toward where a hit is probably going, based on the hitter's body shape.</li><li>Once the shadowing feels natural, add a real ball and small rallies, with the defender applying the same anticipation to real returns.</li><li>Rotate the defending role regularly through the group.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'small, ready movements — don't commit too early or you can't adjust.'</li><li>For 'easier', use slower, more obvious hits to read; for 'harder', use faster, more varied hits.</li></ul><hr><h4>Read &amp; Dig Streak</h4><p>Small groups rally continuously, tracking a team streak of successful 'reads' — moments a defender got into position early because they anticipated the return correctly.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small groups rally continuously in a marked space, one ball per group.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Trust your first read and commit to moving, rather than freezing.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Simpler, more predictable attacking patterns to read.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Varied, disguised attacking patterns.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Scoring streaks</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate trusting the first read and committing to moving, rather than freezing on the spot.</li><li>The group rallies continuously, with attacking touches directed by whoever has the ball.</li><li>Every time a defender gets into position early because they correctly anticipated the return, the group counts that as a successful 'read' toward their team streak.</li><li>Keep the rally and the streak count going as long as possible, restarting quickly if the ball drops.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'trust your first read and commit to moving, rather than freezing.'</li><li>For 'easier', use simpler, more predictable attacking patterns to read; for 'harder', use varied, disguised attacking patterns.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Read the Game Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Small-sided games across low nets where good anticipation/early movement is specifically highlighted and praised as part of feedback, alongside normal rally scoring. Reading the opponent is what turns a scramble into a controlled return.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games (2v2 or 3v3) across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 4m, one ball per game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the coaching focus: good anticipation and early movement will be specifically highlighted and praised as part of feedback, alongside normal rally scoring.</li><li>One side starts each point with a toss or serve over the net.</li><li>Play a normal rally, with defending players actively trying to read the hitter's body shape and move early rather than reacting late.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball; the coach calls out good reads and early movement as they happen.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes so students practise reading different hitting styles.</li><li>Coach students that reading the opponent is what turns a scramble into a controlled return.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What clues helped you predict where a return was going today?</li><li>How did reacting early (instead of late) change what you could do with the ball?</li><li>What will you watch for next time you're defending?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Watches the hitter's body/hand shape, not just the ball</li><li>Begins moving before the ball is actually struck</li><li>Adjusts quickly if the read turns out to be wrong</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Start with very obvious, exaggerated attacking cues for students building this skill, gradually introducing more disguised/varied returns as confidence grows.</p>"},"Lesson 17: Small Court Showdown":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Small-sided rally games (2v2/3v3)</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands B, C · Relating to others, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Aroha — Playing with patience and encouragement for your small team, especially when a rally doesn't go to plan.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones, bibs (2 colours)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard small-sided game safety; ensure adjacent courts have enough buffer space</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply our skills together in a small team, in a full small-sided rally game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can combine bump, set, and directed touches with my team in a real small-sided game</li><li>I can communicate and cover space with a partner throughout a rally</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Rotation Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple position prompts as they jog: 'rotate!' (team shuffles jogging order by one spot), 'spread wide,' 'come together' — echoing the team rotation and tactics focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape and order before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several prompts over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly about who's taking each ball.</td><td>Smaller teams need extra-clear communication to avoid gaps.</td></tr><tr><td>Use all three touches purposefully when possible.</td><td>A full sequence — bump, set, hit — gives your team the best attacking option.</td></tr><tr><td>Cover space efficiently as a small team.</td><td>Fewer players means every gap matters more.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>2v2 Skills Warm-In</h4><p>Pairs play short rallies against each other focusing on using all their skills so far (bump, set, serve, directed touch) without strict scoring — a light, game-like refresher.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Pairs of pairs (four students per court) play short rallies across a low net on a small court roughly 5m x 4m per side, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these skills feel now compared to early lessons.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a light refresher — pairs use all their skills so far (bump, set, serve, directed touch) without strict scoring.</li><li>One pair starts a point with a serve over the net.</li><li>Play a relaxed rally, using whichever skills feel natural — bump, set, serve, or a directed touch — without formally tracking a score.</li><li>Reset with a fresh serve whenever a rally ends, keeping the tone light and game-like rather than corrective.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'notice how much more comfortable these skills feel now compared to early lessons.'</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — rotate roles regularly instead so everyone gets an even mix of touches.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rotating Pairs Round-Robin</h4><p>Pairs rotate through quick mini-matches against several other pairs, playing short rally-scored games to a low target (e.g. first to 5).</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Several small courts set up around the space, pairs rotating through quick mini-matches, one ball per court.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Communicate constantly — call the ball, call coverage.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Longer games, more forgiving scoring.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Shorter games, quicker rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate constant communication — calling the ball, calling coverage — as the key habit for this format.</li><li>Each pair plays a short rally-scored game against another pair, first to a low target such as 5 points.</li><li>Points are scored with simple rally scoring — whichever side wins the rally gets the point, regardless of who served.</li><li>Once a mini-match finishes, pairs rotate to play a new opponent pair, continuing through several quick matches.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'communicate constantly — call the ball, call coverage.'</li><li>For 'easier', play longer games with more forgiving scoring; for 'harder', play shorter games with quicker rotation.</li></ul><hr><h4>3v3 Space &amp; Cover Challenge</h4><p>Trios play short rallies with a specific focus on spacing/coverage, with bonus points for good positioning noticed by the coach.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Trios play short rallies across a low net on a court roughly 6m x 5m per side, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Check in with teammates about who's covering which zone before each serve.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Bigger court, more space per player.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Smaller court, tighter spacing required.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate checking in with teammates about who's covering which zone before each serve.</li><li>Before each point, the trio briefly agrees who is covering which part of the court.</li><li>Play a short rally, with the coach watching specifically for good spacing and coverage.</li><li>Bonus points are awarded any time the coach notices good positioning, on top of normal rally scoring.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'check in with teammates about who's covering which zone before each serve.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a bigger court with more space per player; for 'harder', use a smaller court with tighter spacing required.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Small-Sided Rally League</h3><p>Full 2v2/3v3 small-sided games across low nets with simple rally scoring, rotating opponents through several short matches. Every skill from the unit so far is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Full 2v2 and 3v3 small-sided games across low nets, on courts roughly 6m x 5m per side, rotating opponents through several short matches.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: simple rally scoring, with teams playing several short matches, rotating opponents throughout.</li><li>One side starts each match with a serve over the net.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally, with players using whichever skills from the unit so far fit the situation — serve, bump, set, receive, or a directed touch.</li><li>Points are scored with simple rally scoring, and matches finish at an agreed low target (e.g. first to 7 or after a set time).</li><li>Rotate opponents every match so students play against several different teams through the league.</li><li>Coach students that every skill from the unit so far is relevant somewhere in these games — this is where it all comes together.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What combination of skills worked well for your team today?</li><li>How did communication help your small team during rallies?</li><li>What's one thing your team wants to work on before the next game?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Combines multiple skills smoothly within a real rally</li><li>Communicates and covers space consistently, not just occasionally</li><li>Stays encouraging with teammates through mistakes</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Allow flexible team sizes (2v2 or 3v3) depending on confidence/ability; consider mixed-ability pairs/trios so every team has a blend of experience.</p>"},"Lesson 18: Rotation Station":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Rotation basics</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strand B · Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Mōhio — Understanding the team's structure and your changing role within it — rotation only works if everyone knows their part.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets/ropes, cones/markers for the 6 rotation positions, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard court safety; clear position markers reduce confusion-related bumps</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning the basic idea of rotating positions after winning the serve, like in a real volleyball game.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can rotate one position clockwise when it's my team's turn to serve</li><li>I can find my new position quickly without confusion</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Rotation Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple position prompts as they jog: 'rotate!' (team shuffles jogging order by one spot), 'spread wide,' 'come together' — echoing the team rotation and tactics focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape and order before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several prompts over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Know your rotation order before play starts.</td><td>Volleyball's rotation rules require players to move in a set order.</td></tr><tr><td>Move to your new position promptly when rotating.</td><td>Delays in rotating can leave gaps in coverage.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate your new position to teammates.</td><td>Helps everyone adjust their own coverage around you.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Rotation Walk-Through</h4><p>Teams of 6 stand on marked position spots and physically walk through a clockwise rotation on each whistle, without a ball, until the pattern feels familiar.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of six, each standing on marked position spots (cones or floor markers arranged in the six rotation positions), no ball needed.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Always rotate the same direction (clockwise), one position at a time.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Slower, with position numbers marked on the floor.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Faster, no floor markers.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Random constraint calls (rotate on random whistle)</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the rule: always rotate the same direction (clockwise), one position at a time.</li><li>Each player starts standing on their marked position spot.</li><li>On a whistle, the whole team physically walks through a clockwise rotation, each player moving to the next position spot.</li><li>Repeat the walk-through on each whistle until the pattern feels familiar to the whole team.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'always rotate the same direction (clockwise), one position at a time.'</li><li>For 'easier', go slower with position numbers marked on the floor; for 'harder', go faster with no floor markers.</li></ul><hr><h4>Rotate &amp; Rally</h4><p>Teams play a short rally; every time their team wins the serve, they rotate one position before serving again.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of six (or a simplified smaller rotation for smaller teams) play a short rally across a low net, on a marked court with position spots.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Rotate quickly and calmly, then reset into ready position.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Rotate only after every few points instead of every serve win.</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Rotate after every single serve win, as in a real game.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate rotating quickly and calmly, then resetting into ready position before the next serve.</li><li>The team plays a short rally as usual.</li><li>Every time their team wins the serve (gains the right to serve), they rotate one position clockwise before serving again.</li><li>Continue playing and rotating through several serve-wins, keeping the rotation habit consistent.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'rotate quickly and calmly, then reset into ready position.'</li><li>For 'easier', rotate only after every few points instead of every serve win; for 'harder', rotate after every single serve win, as in a real game.</li></ul><hr><h4>Position Awareness Quiz-Rally</h4><p>During a short rally, play is occasionally paused and a player is asked to name their current position/role; teams get a bonus point for a correct, quick answer.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of six (or simplified rotation) play a short rally on a marked court with position spots, one ball per game.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Know your position, not just your teammates'.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>Fewer positions in play (e.g. 4-person rotation).</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>Full 6-person rotation.</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate that knowing your own position is the goal, not just knowing your teammates'.</li><li>The team plays a short rally as usual, rotating on serve wins as in the previous game.</li><li>Play is occasionally paused, and a player is asked to name their current position or role.</li><li>A correct, quick answer earns the team a bonus point before play resumes.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'know your position, not just your teammates'.'</li><li>For 'easier', use a simplified 4-person rotation; for 'harder', use the full 6-person rotation.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Rotation League Mini-Games</h3><p>Small-sided games (using simplified rotation appropriate to team size) where teams rotate correctly after winning serve, alongside normal rally scoring. Understanding rotation is what keeps the team's structure working smoothly across a whole game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided games using simplified rotation appropriate to team size, across low nets, courts roughly 6m x 5m per side.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: teams rotate correctly after winning serve, alongside normal rally scoring.</li><li>One side starts each point with a serve over the net.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally, with the serving team rotating one position after each new serve win, as practised earlier in the lesson.</li><li>A point is scored as usual when the other side fails to legally return the ball; the coach checks rotations are happening correctly.</li><li>Rotate opponents every few minutes, keeping team rotation consistent throughout each match.</li><li>Coach students that understanding rotation is what keeps the team's structure working smoothly across a whole game.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What helped your team rotate smoothly and quickly today?</li><li>What was confusing about rotation at first, and how did your team sort it out?</li><li>Why do you think volleyball uses rotation as part of the game?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Rotates in the correct direction, one position at a time</li><li>Finds their new position quickly and calmly</li><li>Helps a confused teammate find their spot</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Use a simplified 4-position rotation for teams still building the concept before introducing the full 6-position version; floor markers/numbers help students who find the spatial pattern tricky.</p>"},"Lesson 19: Full Rally Ready":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Full modified games with all touches</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C · Managing self, Relating to others, Thinking</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Manaaki — Giving your full effort and skill in service of your team's game today, using everything you've built across the unit.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets, cones, bibs</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard full-game safety; monitor fatigue and hydration across longer games</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to play a full modified volleyball game, using serving, receiving, bumping, setting, and directed touches together.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can play my position and use the right touch for each situation in a real game</li><li>I can support my team through communication and coverage across a whole game</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Rotation Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple position prompts as they jog: 'rotate!' (team shuffles jogging order by one spot), 'spread wide,' 'come together' — echoing the team rotation and tactics focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape and order before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several prompts over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply the right touch — bump, set, hit — for each situation.</td><td>This lesson tests using the whole toolkit together in a real game.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly throughout each rally.</td><td>Full games move fast and need constant coordination.</td></tr><tr><td>Cover your position and support teammates as the rally develops.</td><td>Team positioning is as important as individual technique in a full game.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>All-Skills Warm-In Circuit</h4><p>Quick-fire stations lightly revisiting the unit's key skills (bump, set, serve, receive, directed touch) — a refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Several quick-fire stations set up around the space, each lightly revisiting one unit skill (bump, set, serve, receive, directed touch), students rotating through in small groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a refresher, not new teaching — a light touch on everything learned so far.</li><li>Students rotate through each station in small groups, spending a short time at each one.</li><li>At each station, they lightly practise that station's skill using the technique already learned earlier in the unit.</li><li>Move on to the next station after a set time or number of reps, until every group has visited every station.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.'</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — adjust the time at each station if the group needs more or less practice.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Skill Relay</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining a serve, a bump-receive, a set, and a directed touch in sequence — a fun, competitive combination of the whole unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-6 at a relay course combining a serve, a bump-receive, a set, and a directed touch in sequence, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep each touch clean rather than rushing to the next one.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the full sequence once: a serve, a bump-receive, a set, and a directed touch, performed one after another.</li><li>The first team member serves the ball to begin their leg of the relay.</li><li>The next teammate receives it with a bump, the next sets it, and the next completes it with a directed touch, each keeping their touch clean rather than rushing to the next one.</li><li>The team races to complete the full sequence as a group, competing against other teams' times or completion.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'keep each touch clean rather than rushing to the next one.'</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — adjust team size or sequence length if needed for the group.</li></ul><hr><h4>Team Game Plan Huddle</h4><p>Each team spends a few minutes agreeing one simple focus for their upcoming game (e.g. 'let's call the ball loudly today,' or 'let's cover the back corners'), then tries it in a short practice rally.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams gather in a huddle for a few minutes, then move to a marked court for a short practice rally, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Bonus objectives</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the idea of a simple, shared focus — one clear idea everyone on the team understands and can act on.</li><li>Each team spends a few minutes discussing and agreeing on one simple focus for their upcoming game.</li><li>Once agreed, the team moves onto the court and tries out their focus in a short practice rally.</li><li>After the practice rally, briefly check in as a team on how the focus went before moving into the day's bigger games.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'keep the plan simple — one clear idea everyone understands.'</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — support younger or less confident teams with a suggested focus if they're stuck.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Full Modified Volleyball</h3><p>Small-sided or full-sided modified games (adjusted net height, court size, and rotation to suit the group) using serve, receive, bump, set, and directed touches together, with simple rally scoring across a full game. Every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in this game.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Small-sided or full-sided modified games, with net height, court size, and rotation adjusted to suit the group, using serve, receive, bump, set, and directed touches together, simple rally scoring across a full game.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the format: a full modified game using every skill from the unit, with net height, court size, and rotation adjusted to suit the group's level.</li><li>One side starts the game with a serve over the net.</li><li>Play continues as a normal full rally, with players using serve, receive, bump, set, and directed touches together as the situation requires.</li><li>Points are scored with simple rally scoring throughout the game.</li><li>Rotate positions and, if time allows, rotate opponents so students experience the full game in different roles.</li><li>Coach students that every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in this game — this is the whole picture coming together.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from across the whole unit you felt proudest of using today?</li><li>How did your team's game plan from the huddle work out?</li><li>What would you want to work on if we played another volleyball unit?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Uses a visible range of skills from across the unit (not just one)</li><li>Communicates and covers space consistently across a whole game</li><li>Applies a simple team plan, adjusting if it isn't working</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Adjust net height, court size, and team size to suit the group; ensure roles/positions rotate fairly so every student experiences a variety of touches during the full game.</p>"},"Lesson 20: Volleyball Volley-Off":{"content":"<table><tr><th>Focus</th><td>Culminating mini-tournament — applying the whole unit</td></tr><tr><th>NZC Link</th><td>Strands A, B, C, D · all five Key Competencies, drawn together</td></tr><tr><th>Value</th><td>Pānga — Reflecting on the impact this whole unit has had — on your skills, your team, and how you treat others in a game.</td></tr><tr><th>Equipment</th><td>Balls, low nets, cones, bibs (multiple colours for round-robin teams)</td></tr><tr><th>Safety</th><td>Standard game safety; manage fatigue/hydration across a tournament format, ensure fair rotation so no team is overplayed</td></tr></table><h3>Learning Intentions</h3><p><strong>WALT:</strong> We are learning to apply everything from this unit — ball control, serving, rallying, positioning, and teamwork — in a full small-sided tournament.</p><p><strong>WILF:</strong></p><ul><li>I can use skills from across the unit in a real game</li><li>I can play fairly and encouragingly in a competitive setting</li></ul><h3>Warm-up: Team Rotation Jog</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Split the class into their tournament/practice teams (groups of 4-6), each team with a marked-out area to jog in (cones optional). No ball needed.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Each team jogs together as a group around their area, staying reasonably close to each other rather than spreading out randomly.</li><li>Call out simple position prompts as they jog: 'rotate!' (team shuffles jogging order by one spot), 'spread wide,' 'come together' — echoing the team rotation and tactics focus of this block.</li><li>This gets students moving together as a unit and thinking about team shape and order before the tactical focus of today's lesson.</li><li>Rotate through several prompts over the 5 minutes, mixing up the order.</li><li>Keep energy high and encourage teams to communicate with each other while jogging.</li><li>Finish with each team jogging back to a central point together as one group.</li></ul><h3>Warm-down: Circle Stretch &amp; Cheer</h3><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Gather each team (or the whole class) into a circle with enough room to stretch without touching neighbours.</td></tr></table><ul><li>Walk slowly as a team/group for about 1 minute to bring heart rate down.</li><li>In the circle, lead 3-4 simple static stretches together — calves, quads, hamstrings, and a shoulder/arm stretch — holding each for 15-20 seconds.</li><li>Keep the group together and calm, using this as a settling-down moment after a high-energy tactical session.</li><li>Finish with a short team cheer or acknowledgment — each team says one thing they're proud of from today's session or the unit as a whole.</li><li>Move directly into the Discussion segment once the cheer is done.</li></ul><h3>Skill Breakdown</h3><table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Apply your best technique from across the unit in every touch.</td><td>The tournament tests everything the unit has built together.</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate constantly and encourage teammates.</td><td>Communication and encouragement both matter more under competitive pressure.</td></tr><tr><td>Stay composed and controlled rather than rushing under pressure.</td><td>Rushed technique breaks down exactly when it matters most.</td></tr></table><h3>Games</h3><h4>Unit Skills Warm-Up Carousel</h4><p>Quick-fire stations revisiting a taste of the unit's key skills (ball control, bump, set, serve) — a light refresher, not new teaching.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Several quick-fire stations set up around the space, each lightly revisiting one unit skill (ball control, bump, set, serve), students rotating through in small groups.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>Notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain that this is a light refresher, not new teaching — a taste of the unit's key skills before the tournament begins.</li><li>Students rotate through each station in small groups, spending a short time at each one.</li><li>At each station, they lightly practise that station's skill using the technique already learned earlier in the unit.</li><li>Move on to the next station after a set time, until every group has visited every station.</li><li>Coach the key message throughout: 'notice how much more comfortable these feel now compared to Lesson 1.'</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — adjust the time at each station if the group needs more or less warm-up.</li></ul><hr><h4>Mixed Skill Relay</h4><p>Teams complete a relay combining ball control, a bump, a set, and a serve in sequence — a fun, competitive combination of the whole unit's skills.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams of 4-6 at a relay course combining ball control, a bump, a set, and a serve in sequence, one ball per team.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Head-to-head mirror, beat the clock</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Demonstrate the full sequence once: ball control, a bump, a set, and a serve, performed one after another.</li><li>The first team member performs a short ball control sequence to begin their leg of the relay.</li><li>The next teammate performs a bump, the next a set, and the next finishes with a serve, each keeping their touch clean.</li><li>The team races to complete the full sequence as a group, competing against other teams' times or completion.</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — adjust team size or sequence length if needed for the group.</li><li>This activity doesn't have a specific coaching cue beyond the general focus on clean, controlled touches throughout the relay.</li></ul><hr><h4>Captain's Challenge</h4><p>Each team nominates a 'captain' (rotating role) who sets one focus for their team's tournament games (e.g. 'let's focus on calling the ball') — light strategic ownership before the tournament begins.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Teams nominate a rotating 'captain' role, gathering briefly before tournament games begin, no extra equipment beyond what the tournament courts already use.</td></tr><tr><th>Coaching cue</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Easier</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Harder</th><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><th>Chaos variation</th><td>Role rotation</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the role: each team nominates a captain (a role that rotates through the team across the tournament) who sets one focus for their team's games.</li><li>The nominated captain leads a brief huddle and picks one simple focus for their team's upcoming tournament games, such as 'let's focus on calling the ball.'</li><li>The team carries that focus into their tournament matches, with the captain reminding teammates of it between points if needed.</li><li>Rotate the captain role to a different student for later rounds so multiple students get the leadership turn.</li><li>This activity doesn't use easier/harder variants — support younger or less confident captains with a suggested focus if they're stuck.</li><li>This activity doesn't have a specific coaching cue beyond encouraging every student to have a turn leading.</li></ul><h3>Big Game: Round-Robin Mini-Tournament</h3><p>Multiple small teams (2v2/3v3) rotate through short games against each other across 2–3 small courts, scoring simply (rally points or a fair-play points system). Every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in these games.</p><table><tr><th>Setup</th><td>Multiple small teams (2v2 or 3v3) rotating through short games against each other across 2-3 small courts, using simple rally-point or fair-play scoring.</td></tr></table><p><strong>How to run it</strong></p><ul><li>Explain the tournament format: short round-robin games across several small courts, with teams rotating opponents throughout.</li><li>One side starts each game with a serve over the net.</li><li>Play continues as a normal rally, with players using every skill from across the unit as the situation requires.</li><li>Score each game with simple rally points or an agreed fair-play points system, recording results as teams move between courts.</li><li>Rotate teams through opponents and courts on a schedule so every team plays a similar number of games.</li><li>Coach students that every skill from the unit is relevant somewhere in these games — this is the celebration of everything they've built across the unit.</li></ul><h3>Discussion Questions</h3><ul><li>What's one skill from this whole unit you're most proud of improving?</li><li>What did fair, encouraging play look like in today's tournament?</li><li>What will you take from this unit into other sports or games?</li></ul><h3>Look-fors (Assessment)</h3><ul><li>Applies a visible range of skills from across the unit (not just one)</li><li>Plays fairly and encourages teammates/opponents in a competitive setting</li><li>Shows growth compared to Lesson 1 (informal, teacher's own judgement — no formal recording required)</li></ul><h3>Inclusion</h3><p>Structure round-robin teams to be balanced/mixed-ability; consider a simple fair-play recognition (not just a 'winning team') to keep the culminating day inclusive and values-aligned.</p>"}}}}}}},"writingfocus":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1 — Narrative":{"label":"Week","children":{"Unit Plan & Overview":{"content":"### Term 1 Unit Plan — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered”\n\n\n| Unit | |\n|---|---|\n| **Year level** | Years 5–6 (ages 9–12) · NZC English Phase 2 |\n| **Duration** | 10 weeks · 25 min × 5 days |\n| **Universal concept** | Reflections on Life — The Things That Are Important to Us |\n| **Anchor value** | Aroha — Love: God, Others, Self, World |\n| **Throughline question** | “What moment made me who I am — and how do I make you feel it too?” |\n| **Published outcome** | A small-moment personal narrative: best copy handwritten in books (Week 9), typed in Microsoft Word (Week 10). |\n\n#### Key Scripture\n\n- Psalm 139:13–16 — \"You knit me together\" (Week 1: your story began before you did)\n- Genesis 1:27 — made in His image, therefore makers ourselves\n- Deuteronomy 6:6–9 — telling our stories to the next generation (Week 2)\n- Proverbs 25:11 — \"A word fitly spoken…\" (Week 7: word choice)\n- Colossians 3:23 — \"Work at it with all your heart\" (Week 9: recrafting is care)\n\n#### NZC English — Phase 2 links\n\n- Composition — identify the audience and purpose for a text; plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting genre, register and word choice\n- Sentence structures — use a range of sentence structures and types to suit purpose and enhance impact; expand sentences using adverbial phrases\n- Punctuation — use speech marks and commas correctly for direct speech, with some support\n- Writing to entertain — draw on their own experiences to write texts that entertain; group ideas into paragraphs; use linking words\n- Writing craft — use figurative language such as simile and metaphor to create vivid images and engage the reader\n- Writing processes — plan with organisers; draft from the plan; revise at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level; edit using known punctuation; give and receive peer feedback\n- Handwriting — handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency, maintaining legibility across multi-paragraph texts\n- Oral language — give narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners; ask clarifying questions\n\n#### The arc of the term\n\n\n| Weeks | Stage | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weeks 1–2 | Stage 1 — Immersion & idea generation | Jotters, mentor texts, senses, oral storytelling. No drafting yet — filling the well. |\n| Week 3 | Stage 1 → 2 — Plan & audience | Story arc, planning organiser, audience card, oral rehearsal. |\n| Weeks 4–6 | Stage 2 — Drafting | Openings and setting · the heart of the moment · endings. Full first draft by Week 6. |\n| Week 7 | Stage 3 — Analyse | Deconstruct models, self-analysis against the rubric, two personal goals. |\n| Week 8 | Stage 3 → 4 — Feedback | Helping Circle, triage, revision plan, targeted workshops. |\n| Weeks 9–10 | Stage 4 — Recraft & publish | Real second draft, edit, best copy, typed publication, celebration, reflection. |\n\n#### The writing process this term\n\nOur writers don't just move through the writing process — they can name it, explain it, and say which stage they are standing in and why. That is taught explicitly, revisited in a 60-second Process Check in every single lesson, and assessed in Week 10 by asking a student to explain the whole thing to someone who has never heard of it.\n\n**FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE** — Four stages, four questions, four hand actions, one chant. The words are alliterative and the actions are physical, so the process is stored in more than one place in memory (dual coding + embodied learning).\n\n\n| Stage | Question | Hand action | What a writer does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **🪣 FILL** | What do I want to say? | Two cupped hands, like holding water. | I collect. I notice, I remember, I wonder, I choose. I am filling up with things to say — I'm not writing the story yet. |\n| **🎯 FIND** | Who do I want to say it to? | Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader. | I find my reader. I decide who is going to hear this and what I want them to feel. Then I draft fast and get it all down. |\n| **🔍 SHAPE** | How do I want to say it? | Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it. | I read my own writing like an editor. I hold it up against what strong writing does, and I find what's working and what isn't — ideas, structure, words, punctuation. |\n| **🎁 SHARE** | Is it ready to give away? | Open both hands and push them forward, offering. | I ask for help, I recraft it — as many times as it takes — I check the small things, and then I give it to my reader. |\n\n#### Teaching & recall schedule\n\n\n| Week | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | INTRODUCE all four stages: words, icons, colours, hand actions, chant, wall strip. Daily Process Check begins. |\n| **Week 2** | DEEP DIVE — Stage 1 FILL. What \"filling the well\" actually means, and why we don't draft yet. |\n| **Week 3** | DEEP DIVE — Stage 2 FIND. Audience, and the handover from FILL to FIND. |\n| **Week 4** | SPACED RECALL — 60-second quick-fire: four questions, four actions, no notes. |\n| **Week 5** | ELABORATION — explain the process to a partner in your own words, using the feast analogy. |\n| **Week 6** | SPACED RECALL + move the peg. \"We've just finished a stage. Which one, and how do you know?\" |\n| **Week 7** | DEEP DIVE — Stage 3 SHAPE. Reading your own writing as an editor. |\n| **Week 8** | DEEP DIVE — Stage 4 SHARE. Feedback and recrafting in cycles. |\n| **Week 9** | THE CYCLE — teach that 3 and 4 loop. Students draw the process as a cycle from memory. |\n| **Week 10** | ASSESS — \"Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it.\" Written in the reflection, and spoken to a partner. |\n\n#### Assessment — Week 10 — can they explain it?\n\n> Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Name all four stages, say what a writer actually does in each one, and give one example from your own narrative this term.\n\n**A strong answer…**\n\n- Names all four stages in order, with the question that belongs to each\n- Explains in their own words what a writer DOES in each stage — not just the label\n- Gives a real example from their own piece for at least two stages\n- Knows that Stages 3 and 4 repeat in cycles until the piece is ready\n\n#### Marking load across the term\n\n\n| When | What | Teacher time |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 3 Fri** | Plan gallery — a glance at plans, no written marking | 10 min for the class |\n| **Week 6 Thu** | Draft check: one of three stamps — On track / Needs a conference / Flying | ~15 sec per student |\n| **Week 8** | You mark nothing — the Helping Circle does the feedback | 0 |\n| **Week 10** | Published piece against the single-point rubric: tick criteria, one glow, one grow. Plus the spoken \"explain the process\" check. | ~3 min per student |\n\n#### Single-point rubric\n\n\n| Success criterion | What a strong piece does | Glow ✓ | Grow → |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ideas** | Tells one small moment, not a whole day — and it clearly mattered to the writer | | |\n| **Opening** | Drops the reader into the moment (action, dialogue, sound or image); no long wind-up | | |\n| **Setting** | Three specific details place the reader somewhere real | | |\n| **Showing** | Feelings are shown through action, body and speech, not named | | |\n| **The heart** | The key moment is slowed down and stretched | | |\n| **Dialogue** | At least one real exchange, punctuated with speech marks and commas | | |\n| **Structure** | Paragraphs move the reader — new time, place, speaker or focus | | |\n| **Language** | Precise verbs, and at least one deliberate simile or metaphor | | |\n| **Sentences** | A range of lengths and openers, used for effect | | |\n| **Ending** | Lands rather than stops; a reflective final line | | |\n| **Accuracy** | Capitals, full stops, tense consistency, known spellings | | |\n| **Audience** | A reader outside our class could follow it and feel it | | |\n| **Process** | Can name our four writing stages and explain which one they are in, and why | | |\n\n**Overall:** ☐ Working towards ☐ Meeting Phase 2 expectations ☐ Exceeding\n\n#### The Helping Circle\n\n- The writer reads aloud — no introduction, no apology, no \"it's not very good\". Listeners do not interrupt.\n- The writer asks their own specific question (written in Week 7 Friday).\n- Two stars — each listener names one specific thing that worked, using criteria language.\n- One wish — each listener offers one actionable next step.\n- The writer says thank you and writes it down. No defending, no explaining. The writer decides later what to use.\n\n**Stars — listener prompts**\n\n- The image I can still see is…\n- I knew exactly how you felt when you wrote…\n- The sentence that made me want to keep listening was…\n- Your ending made me think…\n\n**Wishes — listener prompts**\n\n- I wanted to be in the moment more at the part where…\n- I got confused about who was speaking when…\n- You told me you felt ____ — could you show me instead?\n- The beginning took a while to get going — could you start at…?\n- I wanted to know what you were thinking just before…\n\n*We talk about the writing, never the writer. Every wish must be something a person could actually do tomorrow. Everyone gets their full five minutes.*\n\n#### Differentiation\n\n\n| If a student… | Then… |\n|---|---|\n| Can't find a moment | Give the \"Still stuck?\" borrow-this scenario without ceremony. A borrowed starting point is a scaffold, not cheating. |\n| Freezes at the blank page | Oral first, always. Let them dictate the first paragraph to a partner or a device, then copy it. |\n| Writes fluently but shallowly | Stretch goal: the ten-second heart must fill half a page, and the piece must include one deliberate metaphor they can justify. |\n| Struggles with transcription | Reduce the volume, never the thinking. A strong 150 words beats a laboured 400. Scribe or speech-to-text the draft; shorten the handwritten best copy. |\n| Finishes early every week | A second piece from a different Week 1 seed — or become a Helping Circle facilitator for a group that needs one. |\n| Reluctant to share aloud | Reading to one partner counts. Author's Chair can be \"someone else reads my piece\" — the writing is still published. |\n| Can't explain the process | Go to the feast analogy, then the hand actions, then the chant. Have them teach it to a Year 3 buddy — teaching it is the fastest way to learn it. |\n\n#### Ten-week overview\n\n\n| Week | Focus | Stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | Every Person Is a Story | Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Week 2** | Filling the Well | Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Week 3** | Shape and Audience | Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Week 4** | Getting In: Openings & Setting | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Week 5** | The Heart of the Moment | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Week 6** | Landing It | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Week 7** | Reading Like an Editor | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Week 8** | The Helping Circle | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Week 9** | Recraft, Edit, Best Copy | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Week 10** | Publish & Celebrate | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n\n*The full ACS unit-planning document (Word and Markdown) downloads from the Writing Focus app toolbar and from the Writing Hub landing page.*\n"},"Week 1: Every Person Is a Story":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story\n\n> **Essential question:** What makes a moment worth writing about?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Moment That Stuck · Tue: Build Together: What Makes a Story Worth Telling · Wed: Stretch: The Jotter Habit · Thu: Write: Reading Like a Writer · Fri: Share & Reflect: Story Circle |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Think of a moment you have never forgotten — even though it only lasted a few minutes.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- How old were you? Where were you standing?\n- Who else was there — and what were they doing?\n- What is the one picture from that moment still stuck in your head?\n- Why do you think your brain decided to keep this one?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nvivid · ordinary · flicker · sudden · unforgettable · ache · fierce · still · rushed · quiet\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I still remember the moment when…\n- It only took a second, but…\n- Nobody else noticed, but I did.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** The day something you owned broke, got lost, or was given away.\n- **Picture this:** A photo of an empty playground at dusk.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner about the last time you laughed so hard it hurt. Now — what happened just before that?\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Tier 2 vocabulary: descriptive adjectives\n- **Tuesday** — Nouns & proper nouns\n- **Wednesday** — Simple sentence structure: subject + verb + object\n- **Thursday** — Proofreading: capitals & full stops\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n#### Teacher note\n\nMentor text: one short small-moment narrative, revisited all week. NZ options: an excerpt from Whale Rider, a School Journal small-moment story, or a picture book. One text used four times beats four texts skimmed once.\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Moment That Stuck":{"content":"### Launch: The Moment That Stuck\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a small moment — a few minutes of real life — can make a whole story.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Tier 2 vocabulary: descriptive adjectives |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to entertain (Phase 2): students draw on their own experiences and knowledge to write texts that entertain. Narrative writers select ONE significant moment rather than recounting a whole event. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nIn this morning's warm-up you upgraded plain adjectives. Watch how a real writer chooses one perfect word instead of three ordinary ones.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\n> **⚠ Teach the Process Spotlight below FIRST today.** This is the very first lesson of the programme — your students have never heard of FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE. Teach the spotlight BELOW first, then run the 60-second routine for the first time at the end of it (step 4 of the spotlight). From tomorrow, the routine comes first and the spotlight second.\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Hands up — which stage? (FILL). What's the question? (\"What do I want to say?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Introduce the whole process (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip up but covered (a cloth, or the cards turned around). Peg in your hand. Chant card by your teaching spot.\n\n1. Uncover the wall strip. Say: \"This is how writers work. Not just us — all of them. Four stages, and we're going to walk through all four this term.\"\n2. Go card by card. For each one: read the stage word, read its question, then do the hand action and have the class copy it. Keep it fast — about 30 seconds per card. FILL (two cupped hands, like holding water) — \"What do I want to say?\" FIND (hands to your eyes like binoculars) — \"Who do I want to say it to?\" SHAPE (both hands moulding a ball of clay) — \"How do I want to say it?\" SHARE (open hands pushed forward, offering) — \"Is it ready to give away?\"\n3. Teach the chant, twice. You call, they respond. First time is a mess; second time is usually good. Don't do it a third time today — leave them wanting it.\n   > \"Stage one!\" → \"What do I want to say — I FILL!\" · \"Stage two!\" → \"Who am I saying it to — I FIND!\" · \"Stage three!\" → \"How do I want to say it — I SHAPE!\" · \"Stage four!\" → \"Is it ready to give away — I SHARE!\"\n4. Put the peg under card one. Say the promise out loud, because it's what makes the daily routine work.\n   > \"Today we're standing in FILL. Every single day this term I'm going to ask you where we are — and by the end of the term you'll be able to explain all four to someone who's never heard of them.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Don't explain what the stages MEAN today — that's Weeks 2, 3, 7 and 8. Today is only names, questions and actions. Four minutes, then straight into the lesson.\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Tell a Ninety-Second Story (6 min)\n\nI'm going to tell you a true story from my own life. Time me — it will take less than two minutes. Then I'll show you what I left out.\n\n> \"I was seven. I was holding a double-scoop hokey pokey ice cream at the Mount, and a seagull came in low from behind me…\"\n\nNotice what I did NOT tell you. I didn't tell you about the whole holiday, or the drive, or what we had for dinner. I told you about ninety seconds. And notice where you leaned in — right at the moment the seagull arrived. That's the heart of the story, and everything else is just there to get you to it.\n\n**Tip:** A story worth telling is usually the SMALLEST true thing you remember most clearly.\n\n#### We Do — Find the Heart Together (7 min)\n\nLet's read our mentor text and hunt for the heart — the one sentence where everything turns. Call out when you think we've hit it.\n\n- Where does this story actually start — is it at the beginning of the day, or later?\n- Which sentence is the moment everything changes?\n- How long did this whole story last in real life — minutes, hours, or days?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How do you know that's the heart? What did the writer do to make you slow down there?\n\n#### You Do — Jotter Launch: Moment Seeds (8 min)\n\nYour jotter is yours. I will never mark it. Today it gets its first page: five moments you have never forgotten. One line each — not stories yet, just seeds.\n\n- Write 5 \"moment seeds\" — one line each (\"The night the power went out.\" \"When Koro let me drive the ute.\")\n- Star the one you would most want to hear someone else tell.\n- ✦ Bonus: add a sixth seed that nobody else in this class could possibly have.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read one seed aloud. No commentary. \"Tomorrow we find out which seeds have a story hiding inside them.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Draw on own experience**: NZC Phase 2: \"draw on their own experiences and knowledge… to write texts\" — Years 5–6 writers are expected to mine personal experience as their primary source of narrative material, and to select for significance rather than sequence.\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Narrative retelling**: NZC Phase 2 Oral Language: \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners.\" Oral storytelling precedes written composition throughout Stage 1.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: What Makes a Story Worth Telling":{"content":"### Build Together: What Makes a Story Worth Telling\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge which ideas are worth writing about, using criteria we build ourselves.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Nouns & proper nouns |\n| **NZC link** | Composition — audience, purpose and task (Phase 2): students identify the purpose for a text and begin to select content that serves that purpose, rather than including everything they can remember. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nNouns name specific things — and specific names are what make a story feel real. Not \"a dog\". Rusty.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Which stage? (FILL — hands cupped.) Why are we not writing our stories yet? (\"Because we're still collecting.\")*\n\n#### I Do — Vague vs Specific (5 min)\n\nWatch me put two versions of the same moment side by side, and listen for which one you can actually see.\n\n> Version 1: \"We went to the beach and it was fun.\"\nVersion 2: \"The tide had gone out so far that the sand looked like it went on forever.\"\n\nVersion 1 tells me a fact. Version 2 puts a picture in my head. I didn't add MORE words — I added a more exact one. \"Fun\" is a word about me. \"The tide had gone out so far\" is a word about the world, and the reader gets to stand in it.\n\n**Tip:** If your sentence could describe anybody's day, it isn't specific yet.\n\n#### We Do — Build the \"Worth Telling\" Chart (8 min)\n\nWe're making a chart that stays on the wall all term. What did our mentor text have that made us care? Shout out your ideas and I'll scribe.\n\n- Something CHANGED — it isn't the same at the end as at the start.\n- Someone FELT something strongly.\n- It happened in a REAL PLACE we can picture.\n- There was a problem, a surprise, or a turn.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of these does our mentor text do best? Can a story still work if it's missing one of them?\n\n#### You Do — Test Your Seeds (8 min)\n\nTake the chart to your own seeds. Be honest — some of them won't pass, and that's useful information.\n\n- Tick every seed that meets at least three things on the chart.\n- Cross out any seed that meets none — you've just saved yourself three weeks.\n- Add 3 new seeds now that you know what you're looking for.\n- ✦ Bonus: rewrite one seed so it names the exact place it happened.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose seed changed after today? Whose crossed-out seed turned out to be the best one?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text forms and genre* — **Purpose of texts that entertain**: NZC Phase 2 Reading: \"identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform, and persuade.\" Reading a mentor text as a writer is how Phase 2 students learn what their own genre requires.\n- *Writing — Word choice* — **Specific over general**: NZC Phase 2 Writing craft: \"select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen.\" Specific nouns and named places do more work than general description.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Jotter Habit":{"content":"### Stretch: The Jotter Habit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to collect raw material like writers do — noticing, wondering and recording without worrying about being perfect.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Simple sentence structure: subject + verb + object |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" The jotter is where notes live before they are ever shaped into text (Loane Ch 3). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSubject + verb + object. Every noticing you write today can be exactly that simple — a jotter isn't for perfect sentences.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Which stage are we in? (FILL.) Name one thing a writer DOES in this stage. (Collect, notice, remember, choose.)*\n\n#### I Do — My Real Jotter (5 min)\n\nThis is my actual jotter. It's messy. It's got a shopping list in it. Watch what I wrote on the way to my car yesterday.\n\n> \"14 Aug — dead cicada on the path, still perfect. / Why does the car park smell like rain before it rains? / Mrs T's laugh through two walls.\"\n\nThree lines. None of them a full sentence. None of them a story. But any one of them could become one. I never mark your jotter, and I never will — it's yours, and it's where writers keep the raw stuff.\n\n**Tip:** A noticing is anything your brain stopped on for half a second. Write it before it goes.\n\n#### We Do — Noticing Walk (7 min)\n\nFive minutes outside (or at the window). Eyes and ears only, no talking. Then we collect ten noticings on the board — I'll write them exactly as you say them.\n\n- Something you could SEE that most people would walk past.\n- Something you could HEAR that isn't a person.\n- Something that has changed since last week.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which noticing on our board has a story hiding inside it?\n\n#### You Do — Noticings · Wonderings · Feelings (9 min)\n\nThree columns in your jotter. Three entries in each. Words and fragments are fine — nobody is checking your spelling in here.\n\n- 3 noticings from today.\n- 3 wonderings (things you don't understand and would like to).\n- 3 feelings, and what caused each one.\n- For your jotter (faith prompt): Where did I see love — aroha — today? Where was it missing?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow you meet a story that's only 200 words long — and it will still get you.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Making notes**: NZC Phase 2: \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" Jotter work builds the habit of capturing material in note form so that drafting later has something to draw on.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Curious questioning**: Asking curious questions and problem-finding, not just answer-seeking. The \"wonderings\" column trains students to notice what they don't yet understand.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Reading Like a Writer":{"content":"### Write: Reading Like a Writer\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read a text as a writer — asking why the author made each choice, not just what happened.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Proofreading: capitals & full stops |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features.\" Close reading of a model is the reading–writing link at the centre of Stage 1 and 2 (Loane Ch 5). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYou proofread for capitals and full stops this morning. Today, watch where a real writer breaks those rules on purpose.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Still FILL — but what are we filling up with today? (Techniques we can steal, not just ideas.)*\n\n#### I Do — Why Did She Start HERE? (6 min)\n\nI'm only going to read you the first three sentences of our mentor text, and then I'm going to think out loud about them.\n\n> [Read the mentor text's opening three sentences only.]\n\nShe didn't start with \"One day\". She didn't tell me who anybody is. She started in the middle of something already happening, and now I'm doing the work of catching up — which is exactly what she wants, because a reader who is catching up is a reader who is paying attention.\n\n**Tip:** Ask of every sentence: why did the writer choose THIS, and what would be lost without it?\n\n#### We Do — Three Level Guide (quick version) (6 min)\n\nThree questions, three levels. We answer them together — and each level is harder than the last.\n\n- ON the page: what actually happened in this story?\n- BETWEEN the lines: how did the character feel — and how do we know, if the writer never told us?\n- BEYOND the page: what does this writer want us to understand about people?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which level was hardest — and why is the third one the one that makes it literature?\n\n#### You Do — Your First Telling (9 min)\n\nTake your strongest seed. Write for five minutes without stopping. This is NOT the draft — it's a first telling, and it's allowed to be terrible.\n\n- Set the timer. Write for 5 minutes. Do not stop, do not cross out, do not fix spelling.\n- If you get stuck, write \"and then\" and keep going.\n- When the timer stops, draw a line under it. Don't tidy it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nNo sharing today. \"Close the jotter. Let it sit.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure and features* — **How ideas are conveyed**: NZC Phase 2 Reading: \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements.\" Students examine a mentor text for the decisions behind it.\n- *Reading — Making connections* — **Responding to texts**: NZC Phase 2: \"respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts.\" The Three Level Guide moves students from literal to interpretive to applied response.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Story Circle":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Story Circle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a story out loud to a listener — and to listen in a way that helps the teller.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Every Person Is a Story  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & idea generation |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners\" and \"make comments that encourage discussion.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups gave us better adjectives, specific nouns and clean sentences. All three are story tools.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *From memory, in order: FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — First recall — all four, from memory (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Cover the wall strip BEFORE the students come in. Don't announce it.\n\n1. Point at the covered strip. Say: \"Four stages. In order. Word and action. Go.\" Whole class together, hands first, words after.\n2. Expect it to fall apart around stage three — that's normal on the first recall and you should say so: \"That's exactly what a first try looks like. Again.\"\n3. Second attempt, this time with the chant rhythm. Then ask: \"Which one are we standing in — and what have we DONE this week that proves it?\" Take two or three answers.\n4. Uncover the strip and let them check themselves. Say: \"Next time I'll ask you with no warning.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Do NOT help them during the first attempt, even when it's uncomfortable. The struggle is the part that builds the memory — filling the gap for them removes the benefit entirely.\n\n#### I Do — How to Listen (3 min)\n\nListening is a skill and I'm going to show you what good listening physically looks like.\n\n> Eyes up. Body turned towards the teller. Nothing in your hands. No interrupting — not even to agree.\n\nWatch me listen badly first: eyes wandering, fiddling with a pen, jumping in with \"oh that happened to me too!\" Now watch me listen well. Which teller would you rather be?\n\n**Tip:** The listener's only job is to catch the picture.\n\n#### We Do — Diagnose a Dull Story (5 min)\n\nI'm going to tell you a true story badly on purpose. Your job is to work out exactly what's missing.\n\n- No specific place — it could have happened anywhere.\n- Nothing changed between the start and the end.\n- The teller told us the feeling instead of showing it.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which one of these would fix it fastest?\n\n#### You Do — Story Circle (11 min)\n\nGroups of four. Each person TELLS their moment in 60 seconds — no reading, no notes. Listeners have one job only.\n\n- Tell your moment in 60 seconds.\n- Listeners: say back the one image you can still see.\n- After your turn, write that image at the top of your jotter page — it's the bit that landed.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which of your moments do you want to actually write? You don't have to decide until Friday next week.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Interpersonal communication* — **Listening and responding**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions… consider the impact of their words on others.\" The Story Circle is deliberately structured listening.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Making space**: Listening carefully, paraphrasing others' ideas and making space for others to contribute — the groundwork for the Helping Circle in Week 8.\n"}}},"Week 2: Filling the Well":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 2 — Filling the Well\n\n> **Essential question:** How do writers make a reader feel something?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Imaging · Tue: Build Together: Show, Don't Tell · Wed: Stretch: Stories We Inherit · Thu: Write: Freewrite the Feeling · Fri: Share & Reflect: Choosing the Moment |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Take your moment and put me inside it. What could I see, hear, smell and feel if I was standing next to you?\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Close your eyes. What's the first thing you see in this memory?\n- What sound was in the background that you'd forgotten?\n- What could you feel — on your skin, in your stomach?\n- If this moment had a colour and a temperature, what would they be?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\ndamp · sharp · humming · flickering · thick · bitter · echoing · scorching · gritty · hushed\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The air smelled like…\n- Somewhere behind me, I could hear…\n- My hands were…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** The first time you were somewhere brand new — a new school, a new house, someone else's family.\n- **Picture this:** A kitchen at night with one light on.\n- **Talk it out first:** Describe your bedroom to a partner with your eyes shut. Now do exactly that to your memory.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Commas in lists\n- **Tuesday** — Verbs: action vs state\n- **Wednesday** — Expanding sentences with adjectives\n- **Thursday** — Editing: fixing run-on sentences\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Imaging":{"content":"### Launch: Imaging\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to go back inside a memory and collect what our senses recorded.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Filling the Well  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Commas in lists |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — word choice (Phase 2): \"select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen.\" Imaging and the senses (Loane Ch 7) is how Stage 1 writers generate that material. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCommas in lists — perfect, because today we're listing what our senses caught.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Why don't we draft in this stage? (Because you can't pour from an empty well.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Deep dive — Stage 1: FILL (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Board space for a quick drawing. Peg on Stage 1.\n\n1. Draw a well on the board — a circle, a rope, a bucket. Lower the bucket. Bring it up empty.\n2. Say it plainly.\n   > \"That's what a blank page feels like. And here's the thing everyone gets wrong: when you're stuck for words, you're not actually stuck for WORDS. You're stuck for STUFF. There's nothing in the well yet.\"\n3. Ask: \"Name three things we've done in the last week that put water in the well.\" Take three answers and write them beside the well — jotter seeds, the noticing walk, the Story Circle, the mentor text.\n4. Land it: \"That's Stage One. FILL. We are not being slow — we're filling the well, because you can't pour out of an empty one. We start drafting in three weeks and we'll be ready.\"\n\n**Watch for:** This is the lesson that stops the \"when do we get to actually write?\" question. Say the timeline out loud — students cope with a long build-up far better when they know it's deliberate.\n\n#### I Do — Guided Visualisation (6 min)\n\nEyes shut. I'm going to walk you into one of my own memories, one sense at a time. Then I'll show you the sentences I got out of it.\n\n> \"I'm standing in my grandmother's hallway. Look down — brown carpet, one worn patch. Listen — a clock, and a radio two rooms away. Breathe in — pipe tobacco and wet wool coats…\"\n\nHere's what I wrote afterwards: \"The hallway smelled like pipe smoke and wet wool. Somewhere behind a door, a clock was losing time.\" I did not invent that. I went back and LOOKED. That's the difference between describing and remembering.\n\n**Tip:** Don't reach for adjectives. Reach for the memory, then write down what's there.\n\n#### We Do — Five Senses Map (6 min)\n\nLet's do it together on a memory we all share — swimming sports, the fire drill, a wet-day lunchtime. Five boxes, and we fill them out loud.\n\n- SEE — what was in the corner of the picture, not the middle?\n- HEAR — what was going on in the background?\n- SMELL / TASTE — this is the one everybody forgets, and it's the strongest memory trigger we have.\n- FEEL — on your skin, and in your stomach.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which box was hardest to fill? Which detail surprised somebody?\n\n#### You Do — Your Sensory Map (8 min)\n\nSame five boxes, your moment. Words are fine — these do not have to be sentences.\n\n- Fill all five boxes with at least two entries each.\n- Circle the ONE detail nobody else in the room could have written.\n- ✦ Bonus: give the moment a colour and a temperature.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose sense box surprised you the most?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Where and when**: NZC Phase 2: \"select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen.\" Sensory detail is the raw material for precise place-and-time language.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Making thinking visible**: Visualisation and imaging make internal thinking visible and retrievable — a Stage 1 strategy named explicitly in the ACS writing process.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Show, Don't Tell":{"content":"### Build Together: Show, Don't Tell\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to show a feeling through action, body and speech instead of naming it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Filling the Well  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Verbs: action vs state |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — language features (Phase 2): students \"use figurative language… to purposefully create vivid images and effects to engage the reader,\" and choose action verbs over stative reporting of emotion. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAction verbs versus state verbs — this is today's lesson exactly. \"I was scared\" is a state. \"My hands shook\" is an action.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL — and what's the question that belongs to this stage? (\"What do I want to say?\")*\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Swap a Feeling for a Picture (5 min)\n\nI'm going to write a telling sentence, then throw it away and try again three times.\n\n> \"I was really excited.\"\n\nAttempt one: \"I was so happy.\" — that's the same sentence wearing a hat. Attempt two: \"I smiled a lot.\" — closer, but flat. Attempt three: \"I couldn't stop bouncing on my toes.\" Now you can SEE me. The reader works out \"excited\" for themselves — and a reader who works something out believes it.\n\n**Tip:** If you can put the word \"felt\" in front of it, it's telling. Find the body instead.\n\n#### We Do — Show-Don't-Tell Relay (8 min)\n\nI'll fire a telling sentence at you. You fire back a showing version. Fast — first thought, not best thought.\n\n- He was angry.\n- She was nervous.\n- It was boring.\n- They were proud.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which showing version made the picture sharpest — and what part of the body did it use?\n\n#### You Do — Fix Your Own Telling (7 min)\n\nGo back to your first telling from last Thursday. Hunt for the emotion words.\n\n- Underline every place you NAMED a feeling (scared, happy, sad, excited).\n- Rewrite two of them as showing — use the body, an action, or something you said.\n- ✦ Bonus: rewrite one using something in the room instead of your body (what did your eyes land on?).\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nTwo volunteers read a before and an after. Class says which body part did the work.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Vivid images**: NZC Phase 2: \"use figurative language such as simile and metaphor to purposefully create vivid images and effects to engage the reader.\" Showing rather than telling is the foundation this sits on.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Verbs that carry meaning**: Phase 2 writers are expected to vary structure and word class for effect; replacing \"was + emotion\" with an action verb is the single highest-impact revision at this level.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Stories We Inherit":{"content":"### Stretch: Stories We Inherit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that stories are handed down, and that our own stories sit inside bigger ones.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Filling the Well  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Expanding sentences with adjectives |\n| **NZC link** | Reading and responding (Phase 2) plus Mātauranga Māori: whakapapa and kōrero tuku iho — narrative as inheritance. Tūrangawaewae anchors a story in place. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYou expanded sentences with adjectives this morning. Careful — today we're after precise, not more.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Where does today's water come from? (Other people's memories, not just mine.)*\n\n#### I Do — A Story That Was Told to Me (5 min)\n\nI'm going to tell you a story from my family that I was not there for. Listen for how I know it.\n\n> \"My grandfather walked eleven kilometres to his own wedding because the bus didn't come. I've never seen it. I've heard it forty times.\"\n\nI wasn't born. I can still tell you the road, the shoes, the time he arrived — because the story was handed to me. That's whakapapa and kōrero tuku iho: stories passed down. Deuteronomy 6 tells parents to talk about what matters when they sit at home and walk along the road. Families have always done this.\n\n**Tip:** The stories your family keeps telling are the ones worth writing down.\n\n#### We Do — Why Do Some Stories Survive? (7 min)\n\nSome family stories last fifty years and some are forgotten by Tuesday. Let's work out why.\n\n- It's funny — genuinely funny, every time.\n- Someone was brave, or foolish, or both.\n- It explains why our family does something the way we do.\n- Someone in it is no longer here.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of these does your own family story have?\n\n#### You Do — My Whānau, My Place (9 min)\n\nTwo jotter tasks today — one about people, one about place.\n\n- Write one line of a story someone in your family tells. (If you don't know one, tonight's job is to ask.)\n- Tūrangawaewae: where is the place your story stands? Name it exactly, and write three things about it.\n- ✦ Bonus: whose voice tells that family story best, and what words do they always use?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"On Friday you choose your moment for the whole term. Start narrowing it down.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Retelling with detail**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners.\" Retelling an inherited family story is a demanding retelling task.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Place and belonging**: Tūrangawaewae: identifying the places, people and connections that give us a place to stand — the unit's Mātauranga Māori link, and a rich source of narrative material.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Freewrite the Feeling":{"content":"### Write: Freewrite the Feeling\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write fast and without stopping, to get past our own editor.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Filling the Well  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: fixing run-on sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): \"transfer ideas from planning organisers… into sentences.\" Fluency-first drafting (Loane: \"fast and furious\") protects volume and voice before craft. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRun-on sentences — sometimes in a rush of feeling that's exactly what your brain does. Today we let it, and fix it later.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Is a freewrite a draft? (No — it's still collecting. Nobody sees it.)*\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Write Badly (4 min)\n\nThree minutes, pen never stops, on the visualiser. You are going to see me write rubbish, and you are not going to laugh. (You can laugh.)\n\n> \"the hallway the hallway smelled like — I don't know what it smelled like — old coats and something else something sharp maybe vinegar no not vinegar…\"\n\nLook at that. Repetition, no punctuation, arguing with myself on the page. And right there in the middle — \"something sharp\" — is the only bit I'll keep. That's why we do this. You have to write nine bad lines to find the tenth.\n\n**Tip:** When you get stuck, write the last word again until a new one arrives.\n\n#### We Do — Set the Rules Together (4 min)\n\nWe agree the freewrite rules now so nobody breaks them by accident.\n\n- No crossing out. Ever.\n- No stopping — if the pen stops, write \"I don't know what to write\" until it moves.\n- No worrying about spelling, punctuation or neatness.\n- No reading back over it while you're writing.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which rule will be hardest for you personally?\n\n#### You Do — Eight-Minute Freewrite (12 min)\n\nSensory map open beside you. Timer on. Eight minutes on your chosen moment. I'm writing too.\n\n- Write for the full 8 minutes without stopping.\n- When time is up, read it back once and underline the ONE sentence you didn't expect to write.\n- ✦ That sentence is usually the real story. Write it again at the bottom of the page.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if you found a sentence you didn't know was in there.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Fluency first**: NZC Phase 2 expects students to transfer planning into connected sentences. Freewriting builds the stamina and fluency that make that transfer possible.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new.\" Writing without stopping — and without correcting — is genuinely uncomfortable for careful students, and worth naming as a risk.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Choosing the Moment":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Choosing the Moment\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose one idea and let the others go, and to say why.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Filling the Well  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Imaging, senses, choosing the moment |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Choosing and justifying is the first act of authorship. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups gave us precise verbs and cleaner sentences — the two tools showing needs most.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FILL, starting FIND. What is FIND's question? (\"Who do I want to say it to?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Handover — FILL to FIND (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 1.\n\n1. Say: \"We're at the end of a stage. Look at the strip — which one comes next, and what's its question?\" Whole class answers.\n2. Ask one student to come up and move the peg so it sits between Stage 1 and Stage 2, and to say why: \"Because we've chosen our moment, so we've finished collecting.\"\n3. Say: \"Next week the question changes. It stops being 'what do I want to say' and becomes 'who am I saying it to'. That changes everything about how you write.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Always have a STUDENT move the peg, never you. It's a two-second job that makes the stage change theirs rather than yours.\n\n#### I Do — Choosing, Out Loud (3 min)\n\nI have two moments I could write. Watch me choose one and genuinely let the other go.\n\n> Moment A: the seagull and the ice cream. Moment B: the last time I saw my grandmother.\n\nA is funnier and easier. B is the one I keep thinking about. Against our Worth Telling chart, B has more change and more feeling — and honestly, B is the one I'm slightly scared of, which usually means it's the real one. I'm choosing B. A goes back in the jotter for another term.\n\n**Tip:** The one you're a bit nervous about is usually the one with the story in it.\n\n#### We Do — Two-Minute Pitches (6 min)\n\nIn pairs. Pitch your top two moments — 60 seconds each. Your partner decides which one they want to hear more of.\n\n- Pitch 1: \"One moment I could write about is… and it matters because…\"\n- Pitch 2: same again for your second moment.\n- Partner: \"I want to hear more about ___ , because ___ .\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did your partner choose the one you expected?\n\n#### You Do — Commit (10 min)\n\nFresh page in the jotter. This page gets read again in Week 10, so write it properly.\n\n- \"My moment for this term is…\" — one or two sentences.\n- Three reasons it matters to me.\n- One sentence: what I want a reader to feel when they finish it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room — everyone says their moment in one sentence. Fast, no commentary. Hearing 25 stories in 90 seconds builds real momentum.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Setting goals from reflection**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Committing to a moment, in writing, with reasons, is a goal-setting act.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Knowing your values**: \"Know your own values and why they are important to you.\" Students justify their choice against what matters to them — the Aroha thread of this unit.\n"}}},"Week 3: Shape and Audience":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 3 — Shape and Audience\n\n> **Essential question:** Who am I writing for, and how does that change how I write?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Story Arc · Tue: Build Together: Building the Plan · Wed: Stretch: Who Am I Writing For? · Thu: Write: Oral Rehearsal · Fri: Share & Reflect: Plan Gallery |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Map your moment: where does it start, where does it turn, and where does it land?\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the very last calm second before your moment changes?\n- What is the problem, surprise or turn?\n- What did you do about it?\n- What was different afterwards — even if only inside you?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nsuddenly · until then · at first · without warning · afterwards · ever since · the moment · by the time · just as · finally\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Everything was normal until…\n- That was when…\n- I've thought about it a lot since.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** A moment when you got something wrong in front of other people.\n- **Picture this:** A bike lying on its side on a driveway.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your moment to your partner in exactly three sentences: before · the turn · after.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Synonyms: choosing stronger words\n- **Tuesday** — Subject–verb agreement\n- **Wednesday** — Compound sentences (and / but / so)\n- **Thursday** — Editing: upgrading vague words\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Story Arc":{"content":"### Launch: The Story Arc\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the shape a narrative takes, and where our own moment sits on it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Shape and Audience  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Synonyms: choosing stronger words |\n| **NZC link** | Reading — text structure (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed.\" Writing processes — planning: \"use organisers to organise and plan writing.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSynonyms this morning — hold that thought, you'll need a stronger word than \"said\" by Wednesday.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What is a writer looking for in this stage? (Their reader — and the shape.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Deep dive — Stage 2: FIND (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg moved onto Stage 2 at the start.\n\n1. Recap in one line: \"Last week we filled. This week we FIND — and we find two things: the shape of the story, and the reader.\"\n2. Teach the action properly: both hands up to your eyes like binoculars, and look around the room for your reader. Class copies. Say: \"In FILL your hands are full of your own stuff. In FIND you're looking OUT — searching for the person this is for. Feel the difference.\"\n3. Ask the thinking question: \"What would happen if we skipped FILL and started here?\" Take answers. Land on: you'd have a shape with nothing to put in it.\n4. Say what's coming: \"In this stage we plan, we say it out loud, and then we draft fast. Drafting lives in FIND — it's still first ideas. The fixing comes later, in SHAPE.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Students often assume drafting must be Stage 3 because it feels like the \"real work\". Correct that here — it saves confusion for three weeks.\n\n#### I Do — One Arc, Two Names (8 min)\n\nI'm drawing the shape every story has, and I'm going to name it twice — once in story words, once in the bigger story we're part of.\n\n> Orientation → Complication → Climax → Resolution → Reflection\nSomething good → Something breaks → Something is put right → Something is made new\n\nWatch me walk our mentor text across it. Here's the good — everything ordinary and fine. Here's the break, right here in this sentence. Here's where it turns. And here's the last line, where the writer tells us what it meant. Creation, Fall, Redemption, Restoration — the shape of the biggest story there is, and stories keep borrowing it because it's true to how life actually goes.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't find the \"break\" in your moment, you may have chosen a memory instead of a story.\n\n#### We Do — Map a Story We All Know (6 min)\n\nLet's put a story everybody knows onto the arc. Call out which part goes where.\n\n- The Prodigal Son (Luke 15) — where's the good, the break, the turn, the new thing?\n- Or a film everyone has seen — same four questions.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which part of the arc gets the most TIME in the telling? (Almost always the break and the turn.)\n\n#### You Do — Your Moment on the Arc (7 min)\n\nDraw the arc in your jotter. Drop your own moment onto it — five short phrases, not sentences.\n\n- Label all five points with a phrase each.\n- Put a star on the point where everything changes.\n- ✦ Bonus: which point will need the MOST words when you write it?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone find their moment has no turn? Come and see me — we'll find it, or we'll find you a better moment.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Using organisers**: NZC Phase 2: \"use organisers (e.g., graphic organisers or mnemonics) to organise and plan writing.\" The story arc is the organiser this unit uses.\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Structure of narrative**: NZC Phase 2: students determine how texts are structured and how ideas are conveyed — here, applied to the orientation / complication / climax / resolution shape.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Building the Plan":{"content":"### Build Together: Building the Plan\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to turn a story shape into a working plan in key words.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Shape and Audience  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Subject–verb agreement |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"use organisers to organise and plan writing\" and \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSubject–verb agreement. In planning we write scraps — but the moment we draft, every sentence needs its subject and verb to agree.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Is a plan the same as a draft? (No — a plan is key words. A draft is sentences.)*\n\n#### I Do — Arc to Organiser (5 min)\n\nWatch me turn my arc into five boxes — and watch me delete one.\n\n> Box 1: hallway, brown carpet, clock / Box 2: door opens — she doesn't know me / Box 3: I say her name twice / Box 4: she says \"oh, it's you\" / Box 5: the drive home, nobody talking\n\nLook at box 4 — I've written a whole sentence there. That's too much for a plan. I'm cutting it back to three words. And I'm deleting the box I had about the car park, because it doesn't earn its place — nothing changes there.\n\n**Tip:** Five boxes maximum. If you need a sixth, one of the others isn't pulling its weight.\n\n#### We Do — Build a Real Plan, Together (8 min)\n\nA volunteer brings their arc up. We build their organiser as a class, and I'll keep asking them questions.\n\n- \"What's actually in box one? Not the story — the picture.\"\n- \"Where exactly does it turn? Which second?\"\n- \"What can we cut that you won't miss?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Watch what happens to the plan when the writer answers out loud — it gets better every time they speak.\n\n#### You Do — Your Organiser (8 min)\n\nFive boxes, key words only. If you write a full sentence, cross half of it out.\n\n- Complete all five boxes in key words.\n- Draw a big X on any box that doesn't change anything.\n- Partner check: can your partner tell your story back using only your boxes?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"If your partner got lost, that's a gift — fix that box before Thursday.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Notes, not sentences**: NZC Phase 2: \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" A plan written in full sentences is a draft in disguise and stops students from composing freely later.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Constructive response**: \"Share ideas and respond constructively to those of others\" — the class builds a peer's plan with them, in front of them.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Who Am I Writing For?":{"content":"### Stretch: Who Am I Writing For?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that the audience changes the words, the detail and the tone.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Shape and Audience  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Compound sentences (and / but / so) |\n| **NZC link** | Composition — audience, purpose and task (Phase 2): \"plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice\"; \"understand that different audiences may have a very different response to the same text.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCompound sentences with and / but / so — \"but\" and \"so\" are the two words a story turns on.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND — and today we find the actual person. Who is our reader this term?*\n\n#### I Do — One Moment, Three Audiences (6 min)\n\nSame three sentences, three different listeners. Listen for what changes.\n\n> To my best mate: \"So the seagull just NAILED me, dead centre, ice cream everywhere.\"\nTo a five-year-old: \"A big bird came and took my ice cream right out of my hand!\"\nTo my grandmother: \"I was quite proud of that ice cream, and a seagull removed it in one pass.\"\n\nSame event, three sets of words. The mate gets slang and speed. The five-year-old gets short sentences and no sarcasm. My grandmother gets the joke told slowly. That's called REGISTER — and choosing it is not being fake, it's being understood.\n\n**Tip:** Write the name of your reader at the top of your page, and keep looking at it.\n\n#### We Do — Choose Our Real Audience (7 min)\n\nWe need a real audience, not an imaginary one. I'm proposing the Week 10 celebration — the school community, plus one family reader. Let's work out what they need.\n\n- They weren't there, so they need enough context to follow it.\n- No in-jokes that only our class understands.\n- Language that works for a Year 3 and for an adult in the same room.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What's one thing our audience does NOT need that you were going to include?\n\n#### You Do — The Audience Card (8 min)\n\nAt the top of your plan, three lines. This card stays visible every time you write from now on.\n\n- I am writing this for ____ .\n- They need to know ____ .\n- I want them to feel ____ .\n- ✦ Bonus: name one word you'd use with your mates that you'll change for this reader.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read only their \"I want them to feel…\" line.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Register and word choice**: NZC Phase 2: \"plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice to best communicate the intended meaning.\" This is the core Phase 2 audience expectation.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Different audiences respond differently**: NZC Phase 2: \"understand that different audiences may have a very different response to the same text\" — taught here by telling one moment three ways.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Oral Rehearsal":{"content":"### Write: Oral Rehearsal\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell our whole story aloud before we write it, and to fix what we hear.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Shape and Audience  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: upgrading vague words |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners.\" Oral rehearsal is the bridge from plan to draft (Loane Ch 5). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nVague words got upgraded this morning. Listen for your own vague words when you say your story out loud today.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Why do we tell it before we write it? (Because your ears catch problems your eyes miss.)*\n\n#### I Do — Telling It From the Plan (4 min)\n\nI'm going to tell my whole story from my five boxes. Watch me find a problem while I'm talking.\n\n> [Tell the story from the organiser, not reading it word for word.]\n\nDid you hear that? I got to box three and there was nothing there — I had to invent something on the spot. That's a hole in my plan, and I only found it because I said it out loud. I also said \"and then\" four times, which tells me boxes two and three are the same box.\n\n**Tip:** If you have to say \"um\" at the same place twice, that place needs work.\n\n#### We Do — Rehearsal Rules (4 min)\n\nSet the roles clearly before we start, so the listener actually helps.\n\n- Teller: tell it from the plan — don't read it out.\n- Listener: hold the plan and tick each box as it arrives.\n- Listener: name any box that got skipped, and any box that took ages.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What's the difference between listening to enjoy and listening to help?\n\n#### You Do — Oral Rehearsal in Pairs (12 min)\n\nBoth directions, then fix your plan while it's fresh.\n\n- Tell your story to your partner from the plan (about 2 minutes).\n- Swap.\n- 3 minutes: change your plan based on what you heard yourself say.\n- Write one line: \"The bit I couldn't explain was…\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if telling it changed your plan.\" (Most hands.) \"That's the job doing its work.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Retelling with specific detail**: NZC Phase 2: narrative retellings with specific details that actively engage listeners. Saying it aloud reveals gaps a plan hides.\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Complete sentences with connectives**: NZC Phase 2: \"communicate in complete sentences, incorporating a range of conjunctions and connectives, and varying sentence structure and length to create effect.\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Plan Gallery":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Plan Gallery\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to give useful feedback on a plan before any of the writing happens.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Shape and Audience  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Planning and audience awareness |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"make simple revisions to their texts… using audience and peer feedback.\" Feedback at the planning stage is the cheapest revision there is. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nStronger words, compound sentences, agreement — all go live on Monday, because Monday we draft.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What have we found this week? (The shape, the reader, and the holes.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Three Plan Questions (3 min)\n\nOnly three questions are allowed at a plan gallery. Here they are, and here's why.\n\n> 1. Can I follow it? · 2. Where's the turn? · 3. What do I want to know more about?\n\nNotice what's not on the list: \"is it good?\" That's useless. \"Can I follow it\" is answerable. \"Where's the turn\" makes the reader find the arc. And \"what do I want to know more about\" is a compliment and an instruction at the same time.\n\n**Tip:** A useful comment always points at a specific box.\n\n#### We Do — Model on One Plan (5 min)\n\nOne plan on the visualiser. We run the three questions across it together, out loud.\n\n- \"I could follow it until box three, where I lost who was talking.\"\n- \"The turn is box three — it's the shortest box, and it might need to be the longest.\"\n- \"I want to know more about what was in the room.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those three comments would help the writer most on Monday?\n\n#### You Do — Gallery Walk (12 min)\n\nPlans out on desks with a sticky note beside each. Visit three plans — no more, so it doesn't become a stroll.\n\n- Leave one sticky note on each plan you visit.\n- On it: one thing that made you want to read it, and one question.\n- Back at your desk: read your notes and mark the box you'll fix first.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Keep those notes in your jotter. On Monday, you write.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revisions made \"using audience and peer feedback,\" and \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers.\" A plan gallery makes feedback low-stakes and fast.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Respectful response**: Responding constructively to others' ideas — the rehearsal for the Helping Circle protocol in Week 8.\n"}}},"Week 4: Getting In: Openings & Setting":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting\n\n> **Essential question:** How do writers make a reader want the next sentence?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Four Ways to Open · Tue: Build Together: Setting That Works · Wed: Stretch: Drafting Fast & Furious · Thu: Write: Drafting + First Conferences · Fri: Share & Reflect: Author's Chair (Openings) |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Start in the middle of something. Drop your reader into your moment with no explaining.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is happening in the very first second of your moment?\n- Could you begin with a sound? A line of speech? An action?\n- What does the reader absolutely need in the first three sentences — and what can wait?\n- What's the last sentence of your opening — the one that makes them keep going?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\ncrouched · sprawled · rattling · looming · faded · cluttered · narrow · glaring · muddy · deserted\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The first thing I noticed was…\n- Nobody was supposed to be there.\n- It started with a sound.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Start with one line of dialogue someone actually said to you, then step back and explain where you were.\n- **Picture this:** A hallway with a door slightly open.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your first sentence to a partner three different ways. Keep the one they reacted to.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Apostrophes for possession\n- **Tuesday** — Pronouns: personal & possessive\n- **Wednesday** — Writing topic sentences\n- **Thursday** — Pronoun–antecedent agreement\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Four Ways to Open":{"content":"### Launch: Four Ways to Open\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a narrative can open in several ways, and that the choice is deliberate.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Apostrophes for possession |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to entertain (Phase 2) and writing craft: openings are a structural choice that serves purpose and audience. Sentence variety and word choice create the hook. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nApostrophes for possession — you'll need them: Dad's ute, Nana's kitchen, my brother's fault.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND — and now we're drafting. Which stage does drafting belong to? (Still FIND — first ideas, fluency, voice.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Openings, One Moment (8 min)\n\nI'm writing the SAME moment four ways. Watch me, then watch me choose.\n\n> ACTION: \"I was already running before I knew why.\"\nDIALOGUE: \"“Did you shut it?”\"\nSOUND: \"The screen door banged twice.\"\nIMAGE: \"The gate was open, swinging slightly, the way gates do when someone has gone through them in a hurry.\"\n\nAll four work. I'm choosing the sound, because a bang makes you look up — and because the door mattering is the whole point of the story. The image one is beautiful but it's slow, and slow is wrong for a story about panic.\n\n**Tip:** Choose your opening AFTER you know what your story is about, not before.\n\n#### We Do — Rescue a Weak Opening (6 min)\n\nHere's a terrible opening. Let's rewrite it four ways, together.\n\n- \"One day I went to the beach with my family and we had a really good time.\"\n- Now: as an ACTION opening.\n- Now: as a DIALOGUE opening.\n- Now: as a SOUND opening.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version made you want the next sentence — and can you say why?\n\n#### You Do — Write Three Openings (7 min)\n\nNot one. Three. You cannot choose the best one until you have written a worse one.\n\n- Write three different openings to your own piece — different types, not three versions of the same one.\n- Read all three to your partner.\n- Partner picks the strongest. You don't argue — you just listen.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"The one your partner picked isn't automatically right — but if you disagree, you now have to be able to say why.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Structuring for effect**: NZC Phase 2 writers group ideas deliberately and open texts in ways suited to purpose. A narrative opening is chosen, not defaulted to.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Range of sentence types**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact\" — visible immediately in how a piece begins.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Setting That Works":{"content":"### Build Together: Setting That Works\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build a setting from three specific details rather than a long description.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Pronouns: personal & possessive |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — word choice (Phase 2): \"select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen.\" Precision, not quantity. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPronouns this morning. Watch out — if you write \"he\" too early, your reader doesn't know who he is yet.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What's the question? (\"Who do I want to say it to?\") How is that changing your setting choices?*\n\n#### I Do — The Three-Detail Rule (5 min)\n\nI'm going to read you a bloated setting, then the trimmed one. Count the details in each.\n\n> BLOATED: \"It was a hot sunny day with blue sky and a few white clouds and the grass was green and dry and there were trees around the edge of the field and some birds and the sun was very bright…\"\nTRIMMED: \"The field had gone the colour of straw, and the shade under the trees was full of people who'd given up.\"\n\nThe first one has nine details and I can't see any of them. The second has two, and you can see the whole afternoon. Three details is plenty — but they have to be details only YOUR moment could have.\n\n**Tip:** Cross out any detail that could belong to any old place.\n\n#### We Do — Build It, Then Cut It (9 min)\n\nLet's build a setting paragraph for somewhere we all know — the school field on a hot day. Give me everything. Then we cut it to three.\n\n- Everything you can see, hear, smell.\n- Now: which three details do the most work?\n- Now: rewrite it with only those three.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What did we lose in the cut? Did we lose anything that mattered?\n\n#### You Do — Your Setting (7 min)\n\nDraft your setting into the piece, using exactly three details from your Week 2 sensory map.\n\n- Choose three details — circle them on your sensory map first.\n- Write the setting into your draft (2–3 sentences).\n- Test: could any of your three details belong to any old place? Replace it if so.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your three details to your neighbour. They should be able to name the place — or nearly.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Where and when**: NZC Phase 2: \"select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen.\" Three exact details place a reader faster than ten general ones.\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Grouping ideas**: NZC Phase 2: \"introduce a topic, and group information together using simple paragraphs.\" Setting is a paragraph with a job, not decoration.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Drafting Fast & Furious":{"content":"### Stretch: Drafting Fast & Furious\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to draft at speed, keeping the words moving instead of perfecting as we go.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Writing topic sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): \"transfer ideas from planning organisers or mnemonics into sentences.\" Drafting fluency is a Phase 2 stamina expectation. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTopic sentences this morning — the first sentence of each new paragraph tells the reader where they now are.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four from memory: FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall — quick-fire (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered. No warning.\n\n1. \"No notes, no wall. Four stages, in order, with actions. Go.\" Whole class, twenty seconds.\n2. Then: \"One person — explain the whole process in under twenty seconds. I'm timing you.\" Pick someone who isn't your usual volunteer. Class counts down.\n3. Uncover the strip. One line only: \"Two weeks since we learned it. Look how much stuck.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Keep it playful and fast — this is a game, not a test. If a student stalls, ask the class for the first WORD only, not the whole answer, and let the student finish it.\n\n#### I Do — The Fast-Draft Rule (4 min)\n\nSixty seconds of me drafting fast. Watch what I do when I can't think of a word.\n\n> \"He was standing by the ___ , holding the thing you put chips in, and his face did the ___ thing it does when…\"\n\nI left two blanks and kept going. If I'd stopped to find \"vinegar-soaked paper cone\" I'd have lost the whole sentence. Momentum first. The perfect word will still be there on Friday.\n\n**Tip:** Leave a blank, draw a squiggle, keep moving.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Signals (4 min)\n\nWe agree what to do in every situation, so nobody has to put a hand up during a long write.\n\n- Stuck for a word → squiggle and keep going.\n- Finished a plan box → start the next box, don't reread.\n- Timer stops → draw a line. Don't tidy.\n- Genuinely stuck → reread your plan, say the next bit out loud in your head, write exactly that.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of these will you personally need most today?\n\n#### You Do — Sustained Drafting (13 min)\n\nLongest write of the term so far. Music off, timer visible. I'm writing for the first half, then I'll move quietly.\n\n- Draft from your plan, box by box.\n- No rubbing out, no rereading from the start.\n- When the timer stops, note your word count in the margin.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nWord count noted. No sharing today — we protect the flow.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Plan into sentences**: NZC Phase 2: \"transfer ideas from planning organisers or mnemonics into sentences\" — the plan is used, box by box, rather than abandoned.\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\" Sustained writing blocks build that stamina deliberately.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Drafting + First Conferences":{"content":"### Write: Drafting + First Conferences\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to keep a draft moving by rereading our last paragraph before writing the next.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Pronoun–antecedent agreement |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting and revising (Phase 2): \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — used here as a drafting strategy, not a final check. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPronoun agreement — as you reread today, check every \"they\" and \"it\" actually has an owner.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Are we allowed to fix things yet? (A little — but SHAPE is where the real fixing happens.)*\n\n#### I Do — Reread Before You Write (4 min)\n\nWatch what I do before I add a single new sentence.\n\n> [Read your last paragraph aloud, then continue writing from it.]\n\nI read the last paragraph — not the whole thing, just the last one. It puts me back in the voice, and it stops me starting the next paragraph in a different tense, which is exactly what I did last time I didn't do this.\n\n**Tip:** Last paragraph only. Reading the whole thing from the top is how you spend a lesson rewriting sentence one.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Box (3 min)\n\nFast round the room — no discussion, just the box number you're finishing today.\n\n- \"Box two, the bit before it goes wrong.\"\n- \"Box three — I'm on the turn.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone still on box one? Come to the table with me first.\n\n#### You Do — Independent Drafting (14 min)\n\nKeep drafting. I'll be doing two-minute conferences — if I sit next to you, keep writing until I speak.\n\n- Reread your last paragraph, then keep going.\n- Aim to finish the box you named.\n- If you finish early: go back and add one sensory detail to each paragraph.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone finish the sentence you're on. Not the paragraph. The sentence.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Rereading**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level.\" Rereading the previous paragraph before continuing keeps voice and tense consistent.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Pronoun clarity**: Phase 2 writers vary sentence structure while keeping meaning clear; unanchored pronouns are the most common Year 5–6 clarity fault.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Author's Chair (Openings)":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Author's Chair (Openings)\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read our own writing aloud to an audience, and to give specific praise.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Getting In: Openings & Setting  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engaging listeners with specific detail; making comments that encourage discussion. Writing processes: giving feedback to peers. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups sharpened possession, pronouns and topic sentences — all three show up in a strong opening.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What will happen to these drafts in SHAPE? (We'll read them like editors.)*\n\n#### I Do — Author's Chair Rules (3 min)\n\nToday is praise only. No suggestions — not one. Here's why: you can't take advice on something you're not yet proud of.\n\n> Writer reads → everyone claps → two people say one SPECIFIC thing that worked.\n\n\"That was good\" is not specific. \"The word 'banged' made me jump\" is specific. Watch me take praise properly: I say thank you, and I don't explain, apologise, or tell you what I meant to do.\n\n**Tip:** Point at a word or a sentence when you praise. Always.\n\n#### We Do — Practise on Me (4 min)\n\nI'll read my opening. You give me two specific pieces of praise, and I'll take them without flinching.\n\n- \"The sentence that worked for me was…\"\n- \"The word that made me see it was…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did anyone want to give me advice? Hold onto it — Week 8 is when it becomes useful.\n\n#### You Do — Openings-Only Author's Chair (13 min)\n\nJust your opening — 30 seconds each. Fast rotation, 6–8 readers today. Everybody reads at some point this term.\n\n- Read your opening (the one you chose on Monday).\n- Listeners: two specific praises per reader.\n- Readers: write down the exact words people praised.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which opening today made you want to hear the rest? Tell that person on the way out.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Specific response**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion… and consider the impact of their words on others.\" Praise must point at something specific to count.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new.\" Reading your own writing aloud is, for many Year 5–6 students, the biggest risk of the term.\n"}}},"Week 5: The Heart of the Moment":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I slow down the part that matters most?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Slow Motion · Tue: Build Together: Dialogue That Does a Job · Wed: Stretch: Sentence Length as a Tool · Thu: Write: Independent Drafting · Fri: Share & Reflect: Trios |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Find the ten seconds that everything hinges on. Now stretch them across half a page.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the exact second the moment turns?\n- What did your body do — before your brain caught up?\n- Did anyone say anything? What were the exact words?\n- What were you thinking that you did NOT say out loud?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nfroze · lurched · slammed · whispered · muttered · gasped · trembling · thundering · churning · pounding\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- That was when…\n- I couldn't move.\n- Nobody said anything for a long time.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Write the moment in slow motion, one body part at a time — hands, then stomach, then eyes.\n- **Picture this:** A smashed cup on a wooden floor.\n- **Talk it out first:** Act out the ten seconds with a partner, then write only what a camera would have caught.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Exclamation & question marks\n- **Tuesday** — Degrees of comparison\n- **Wednesday** — Sentence variety: short vs long\n- **Thursday** — Peer-checking capitals & punctuation\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Slow Motion":{"content":"### Launch: Slow Motion\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to stretch the most important seconds of a story across many sentences.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Exclamation & question marks |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to entertain (Phase 2): pacing and proportion — the most significant events receive the most text. Sentence structures are varied to enhance impact. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nExclamation marks! Warning: in strong writing they're rare. The tension should come from the words, not the punctuation.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. We're deep in drafting — what's the one rule of this stage? (Get it down; don't polish yet.)*\n\n#### I Do — Zooming In (8 min)\n\nTwo versions of the same ten seconds. The first is one sentence. The second is eight. Count what's different.\n\n> FAST: \"I ran to the corner and I was really scared.\"\nSLOW: \"I ran. The concrete was hot and I didn't care. At the corner I stopped. A car went past. Another one. My heart was doing something strange in my throat. I couldn't remember how to breathe properly. So I kept running.\"\n\nI didn't add new events — there are no more things happening in version two. I added the INSIDE of the ten seconds: the feet, the cars, the throat, the breathing. Zooming in means writing what a camera and a heart monitor would both have recorded.\n\n**Tip:** Ask: what did my body do before my brain caught up?\n\n#### We Do — Stretch a Fast Sentence (6 min)\n\nOne boring sentence. Let's make it eight together. Nobody is allowed to add a new event.\n\n- \"I fell off my bike.\"\n- What happened in the half-second before?\n- What did the ground feel like on the way down?\n- What was the first thing you heard afterwards?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** We've written eight sentences about one second. Is it better or just longer — and how do we tell?\n\n#### You Do — Stretch Your Ten Seconds (7 min)\n\nFind the ten-second heart of your own moment. Minimum six sentences on ten seconds.\n\n- Mark the ten seconds in your draft with a bracket.\n- Rewrite them across at least six sentences.\n- Use at least one thing your body did without asking you.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nOne reader. Class names the slowest sentence in it.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Pacing for impact**: NZC Phase 2 narrative writers give the most words to the most significant moment, rather than reporting events at even speed.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Structures that enhance impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Dialogue That Does a Job":{"content":"### Build Together: Dialogue That Does a Job\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write and punctuate dialogue that reveals character, not just fills space.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Degrees of comparison |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures and punctuation (Phase 2): \"use speech marks and commas correctly for direct speech, with some support.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nComparisons this morning — bigger, biggest, more frightening. Dialogue is where those come alive.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Whose voice are we finding today? (The people in the story — and my own.)*\n\n#### I Do — Punctuate It, Then Choose It (6 min)\n\nFirst the rules, narrated out loud as I write. Then the harder part: deciding what a person actually says.\n\n> \"Did you shut it?\" Mum's voice came from the kitchen window, already sharp.\n\n\"I thought I did.\"\n\nSpeech marks open. Capital letter for the first spoken word. Question mark INSIDE the speech marks. New speaker, new line — every time. Now the choice: I could have written \"I thought I did,\" I said sadly. I cut \"sadly\" and let \"already sharp\" do the work instead. Real people don't speak in full explanations. They speak in four words and a silence.\n\n**Tip:** If your dialogue could be spoken by anyone in the story, it isn't doing its job yet.\n\n#### We Do — Four Lines, One Argument (8 min)\n\nTwo people arguing about something small. You give me the words, I'll punctuate them out loud as I write.\n\n- Speaker A says something short.\n- Speaker B doesn't answer the question.\n- Speaker A repeats themselves.\n- Speaker B says something that ends it.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Now let's delete every \"said angrily / happily / sadly\" and replace it with something a body did instead.\n\n#### You Do — One Real Exchange (7 min)\n\nAdd ONE exchange to your draft — two to four lines only. Punctuate it correctly.\n\n- Write the exchange into your draft.\n- Check: new line for each new speaker?\n- Check: comma or question mark inside the speech marks?\n- ✦ Bonus: replace one speech tag with an action beat.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nTwo writers read their dialogue aloud with a partner playing the other voice.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Punctuation* — **Direct speech**: NZC Phase 2: \"use speech marks and commas correctly for direct speech, with some support\" — the explicit punctuation expectation for Years 5–6 narrative.\n- *Writing — Writing craft* — **Voice and character**: Word choice reveals character. Real speech in narrative is short, interrupted and unfinished; \"said\" plus an action beat outperforms an adverb.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Sentence Length as a Tool":{"content":"### Stretch: Sentence Length as a Tool\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to control pace by varying sentence length on purpose.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Sentence variety: short vs long |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures (Phase 2): \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact\"; \"expand sentences using adverbial phrases.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nShort versus long sentences was this morning's warm-up — this lesson is that warm-up, put to work.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. How many days of drafting left? (Thursday, then all of next week — the draft finishes Week 6.)*\n\n#### I Do — Hear It Hit (5 min)\n\nListen to this with your eyes shut. Tell me where you tensed up.\n\n> \"He waited at the edge of the light, listening to the house settle around him, telling himself it was nothing. Nothing. Then the door moved.\"\n\nLong sentence — you're comfortable, you're drifting. One word: \"Nothing.\" You sit up. Then four words and it's over. Long sentences build. Short ones strike. If everything is medium-length, the reader's heart rate never changes.\n\n**Tip:** One short sentence per tense paragraph. Two is a machine gun.\n\n#### We Do — Long, Long, Short (8 min)\n\nHere's a breathless paragraph with no variation. Let's rebuild it as long–long–short and read both aloud.\n\n- Read the original aloud. What does it sound like?\n- Where should the short sentence go — before the moment, or after it?\n- Read the rebuilt version. What changed in the SOUND?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does the short sentence work better after the long ones than before them?\n\n#### You Do — Place One Short Sentence (8 min)\n\nFind your tensest paragraph and put one very short sentence in it, deliberately.\n\n- Find the tensest paragraph in your draft.\n- Add one sentence of five words or fewer.\n- Read the paragraph aloud in a whisper. Did it change?\n- ✦ Bonus: find a paragraph where every sentence starts the same way, and fix one.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your short sentence to your neighbour. Just that one.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Variety for impact**: NZC Phase 2: a range of sentence structures and types used deliberately to suit purpose and enhance impact — including the deliberate short sentence.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Expanding with phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"expand sentences using adverbial phrases\" and \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase.\"\n"},"Thursday: Write: Independent Drafting":{"content":"### Write: Independent Drafting\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sustain a piece of writing and get ourselves unstuck without help.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Peer-checking capitals & punctuation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2), and handwriting: increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility across multi-paragraph texts. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYou peer-checked punctuation this morning. Today you're the only checker — so read it aloud in your head as you go.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If you get stuck today, which stage do you go back to for help? (FILL — your jotter, your sensory map.)*\n\n#### I Do — Getting Unstuck (3 min)\n\nWatch the three-step unsticking routine. Learn it, because I'm not going to rescue you today.\n\n> 1. Reread the plan box. 2. Say the next bit out loud (quietly). 3. Write exactly what you just said.\n\nNotice step three: write exactly what I SAID, not a better version of it. Trying to think of a better sentence than the one in your mouth is what stops writers dead. Get the said-version down; improve it in SHAPE.\n\n**Tip:** Your talking voice is a better writer than your stuck voice.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Box (3 min)\n\nRound the room. Which plan box are you finishing today? One sentence each, fast.\n\n- \"Box four — the bit after it goes wrong.\"\n- \"I've finished my boxes, so I'm going back to add detail.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone who says \"I don't know\" — you're with me at the back table.\n\n#### You Do — Sustained Drafting + Small-Group Workshop (15 min)\n\nLongest write yet. I'll take a small group at the back table — if I invite you, bring your draft and a pencil.\n\n- Draft towards the end of your story.\n- Use the three-step routine when you stall.\n- Mark your hardest paragraph with an H — you'll read it tomorrow.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow you read your hardest paragraph to two people. Choose it now.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina and legibility**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\"\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Self-regulation**: Using self-talk and known strategies to solve a stuck moment without adult help — an explicit Phase 2 oral-language and self-management indicator.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Trios":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Trios\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to listen to a piece of writing and describe what we saw and what we wondered.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — The Heart of the Moment  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the turn |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): listening and responding — clarifying questions and comments that encourage discussion. Writing processes: giving and receiving peer feedback. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPunctuation, comparison, sentence variety — the three things your listeners will hear today.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Explain all four stages in your own words, to a partner, without notes.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Elaboration — explain it in your own words (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered.\n\n1. In pairs: \"Explain our whole writing process to your partner, in your own words. You may use the feast analogy. Sixty seconds each — go.\"\n2. Circulate and listen for who is reciting labels versus who is explaining. Those are very different things and you'll hear it immediately.\n3. Pick two students to explain it to the class. The rest of the class holds up fingers for how many stages were named correctly, and says one thing that was explained really well.\n\n**Watch for:** Reciting \"FILL, FIND, SHAPE, SHARE\" is not explaining. Push with: \"That's the name. What does a writer actually DO?\" Explaining in their own words is the skill being assessed in Week 10, so this is the rehearsal.\n\n#### I Do — The Trio Protocol (3 min)\n\nThree people, three jobs. Watch the shape of it before you do it.\n\n> Writer reads → Listener 1 says the image they saw → Listener 2 asks one question.\n\nListener 1 is not allowed to say \"it was good\". They must describe a picture: \"I saw the gate swinging.\" Listener 2 is not allowed to give advice. They ask: \"What were you thinking while you ran?\" A question is more useful than advice, because the writer answers it in their next draft.\n\n**Tip:** An image and a question. That's the whole job.\n\n#### We Do — Practise Once, Together (4 min)\n\nI'll read my hardest paragraph. Someone gives me the image; someone else gives me the question.\n\n- \"The image I saw was…\"\n- \"My question is…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those two will change my writing more?\n\n#### You Do — Trios (13 min)\n\nGroups of three. Everyone reads their marked hardest paragraph. Rotate roles.\n\n- Read your H paragraph aloud.\n- Receive the image and the question — write the question down.\n- Rotate until all three have read.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"One thing I'm going to change on Monday is…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Clarifying questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions\" — the trio protocol makes both compulsory and structured.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — practised in a small, low-risk format before the Helping Circle.\n"}}},"Week 6: Landing It":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 6 — Landing It\n\n> **Essential question:** How does a story end without just stopping?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Four Ways to End · Tue: Build Together: The Reflective Line · Wed: Stretch: Paragraphing the Whole Piece · Thu: Write: Finish the Draft · Fri: Share & Reflect: Draft Day Celebration |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Finish it. Then find the one sentence that tells me why this moment still matters to you.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What happened straight after the moment ended?\n- Who or what was different afterwards?\n- What do you know now that you didn't know before it?\n- If you could say one sentence to the person you were in that moment, what would it be?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nafterwards · ever since · eventually · realised · understood · changed · carried · remembered · learned · still\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I've never told anyone that…\n- Ever since then…\n- It's strange what you remember.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Return to the very first image of your story and show how it looks different now.\n- **Picture this:** The same empty playground from Week 1, in morning light.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner what the moment taught you — then cut that sentence in half and write it.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Word families: roots, prefixes, suffixes\n- **Tuesday** — Adverbs: how, when, where\n- **Wednesday** — Adding detail with who / where / when phrases\n- **Thursday** — Editing: tightening wordy sentences\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Four Ways to End":{"content":"### Launch: Four Ways to End\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that endings are chosen, and that a story can land instead of stopping.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Landing It  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Word families: roots, prefixes, suffixes |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to entertain (Phase 2): texts \"have a concluding statement\" that serves the purpose of the piece rather than simply reporting the last event. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPrefixes and suffixes this morning — un-, re-, -less. Endings often live in words like unfinished, returned, fearless.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. This is the last week of drafting — what comes next, and what's its question? (SHAPE: \"How do I want to say it?\")*\n\n#### I Do — Four Endings, One Story (8 min)\n\nFour endings for my story. One of them is deliberately terrible.\n\n> CIRCLE BACK: \"I still check that gate twice. Every single time.\"\nCHANGED IMAGE: \"The gate was shut. It stayed shut all summer.\"\nHONEST THOUGHT: \"I've never told anyone how long I stood there before I ran.\"\nLAST LINE OF SPEECH: \"“You found him,” she said, and that was all either of us said about it.\"\nTERRIBLE: \"Then we went home and had tea. It was a good day in the end.\"\n\nThe terrible one isn't terrible because it's untrue — we did have tea. It's terrible because it tells me nothing changed. The other four all leave something in your hands. I'm choosing the circle back, because gates are what this story is about.\n\n**Tip:** A good ending gives the reader something to carry out of the room.\n\n#### We Do — Spot the Real One (5 min)\n\nHere are four possible endings for our mentor text. Three I wrote. One is the real one. Which — and why?\n\n- Vote with your hands.\n- Defend your vote with a reason, not a feeling.\n- Now I'll show you the real one.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What did the real ending do that mine didn't?\n\n#### You Do — Draft Two Endings (8 min)\n\nTwo different endings for your own piece. Different types — not the same one twice.\n\n- Write ending A (one type).\n- Write ending B (a different type).\n- Read both to a partner. Don't tell them which you prefer.\n- Write one line: why you'll choose the one you choose.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which ending did you WANT to be true? That's a clue.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Concluding statement**: NZC Phase 2: texts \"have a concluding statement.\" In narrative this means resolution plus reflection, not \"and then I went home.\"\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Deliberate final words**: Word choice at the end of a text carries disproportionate weight; Phase 2 writers are expected to choose words for effect and connotation.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Reflective Line":{"content":"### Build Together: The Reflective Line\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write a final line that says why the moment mattered — in as few words as possible.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Landing It  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Adverbs: how, when, where |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to entertain and writing craft (Phase 2): a concluding statement with deliberate, precise word choice. Links to the ACS value Aroha and to the unit's throughline. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAdverbs tell us how. Careful today — a strong reflective line usually needs none.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Point at the wall strip — how many stages have we been through, and how many are left?*\n\n#### I Do — Cut It in Half, Twice (5 min)\n\nWatch me write a final line and then cut it down twice.\n\n> v1: \"That day taught me that I should always be more careful with things I love, because you never know when you might lose them.\"\nv2: \"I learned to be careful with the things I love.\"\nv3: \"I still check that gate twice.\"\n\nVersion one explains the lesson to you like a school report. Version three doesn't explain anything — and it says more. Trust your reader. The short one hits because it makes THEM do the last bit of thinking. Where's the aroha in this story? It's in the checking. I never had to say the word \"love\" at all.\n\n**Tip:** If your last line contains the word \"learned\", try again without it.\n\n#### We Do — Five Candidates (8 min)\n\nFive possible final lines for our shared class story. We write them, then we vote and defend.\n\n- One that circles back to the first image.\n- One that's a single short sentence.\n- One that's a line of speech.\n- One that names a feeling (we'll see why it's weakest).\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is the one that names the feeling almost always the weakest?\n\n#### You Do — Your Final Line (8 min)\n\nWrite it. Then rewrite it shorter. Then shorter again.\n\n- Write your final line.\n- Rewrite it in half the words.\n- Rewrite it again, shorter still.\n- Circle the version you'll use.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room: everyone reads ONLY their last line. Do this one properly — it's the best two minutes of the term.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precision and connotation**: NZC Phase 2: vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect; students consider connotation and precision. Cutting a reflective line in half forces exactly that.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Knowing your values**: \"Know your own values and why they are important to you.\" The reflective line asks the writer to name what the moment revealed.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Paragraphing the Whole Piece":{"content":"### Stretch: Paragraphing the Whole Piece\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning when a new paragraph is needed, and why.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Landing It  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Adding detail with who / where / when phrases |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): \"use layout conventions to indicate paragraphs\"; writing to entertain: \"group information together using simple paragraphs.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nWho / where / when phrases this morning — those phrases are how you signal a new paragraph has moved us somewhere new.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FIND. Next is SHAPE — \"How do I want to say it?\"*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall + move the peg (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg still on Stage 2.\n\n1. \"We finish a whole stage this week. Which one are we finishing, and which one are we walking into?\" Whole class.\n2. One student comes up, moves the peg, and has to justify the move out loud: \"We're finishing FIND because our drafts are done. Next is SHAPE, because now we read them like editors.\"\n3. Ask the class: \"Do we agree? Has anything been left behind in FIND?\" (If some students haven't finished drafting, that's a real and useful answer.)\n\n**Watch for:** The justification is the assessment here, not the peg. If the student can't justify it, take a second volunteer rather than explaining it yourself.\n\n#### I Do — The Four Triggers (5 min)\n\nWatch me read a draft and mark every place a new paragraph should start. There are only four reasons.\n\n> NEW TIME · NEW PLACE · NEW SPEAKER · NEW FOCUS\n\nHere — we've jumped from the driveway to the corner. New place, new paragraph. Here — Mum speaks. New speaker, new line, every single time. And here, nothing has moved but the story turns inward to what I was thinking. New focus. Four reasons, and that's all there are.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't name which of the four it is, it probably isn't a new paragraph.\n\n#### We Do — Paragraph a Block (7 min)\n\nHere's a piece of writing with no paragraphs at all. Call out where the breaks go and name the trigger each time.\n\n- \"Break there.\" — Which trigger?\n- \"Break there.\" — Which trigger?\n- Any place where two triggers happen at once?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How many paragraphs did we end up with? Does it look easier to read?\n\n#### You Do — Mark Your ¶ (9 min)\n\nMark your own draft. No rewriting today — just mark and name.\n\n- Put a ¶ at every place a new paragraph should start.\n- Beside each ¶, write which of the four triggers it is.\n- Count your paragraphs. Fewer than three usually means the story hasn't moved enough.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone with one enormous paragraph — that's tomorrow's first job.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Paragraph conventions**: NZC Phase 2: \"use layout conventions to indicate paragraphs (each begins on a new line and is indented from the margin).\"\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Grouping ideas**: NZC Phase 2: \"introduce a topic, and group information together using simple paragraphs\" — in narrative, grouping follows time, place, speaker and focus.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Finish the Draft":{"content":"### Write: Finish the Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to finish a whole piece of writing — finished, not perfect.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Landing It  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: tightening wordy sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): completing a multi-paragraph draft from a plan, with increasing handwriting stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nWordy sentences got tightened this morning. If a sentence made you take two breaths, it's two sentences.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Last day of FIND. What have we produced in this stage? (A whole first draft, written for a real reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Goal, Plainly (2 min)\n\nOne sentence of instruction today, because the rest of the lesson is yours.\n\n> \"Today, every person finishes the first draft.\"\n\nFinished, not perfect. A finished rough draft beats a beautiful half. Next week we become editors — and you cannot edit something that isn't there.\n\n**Tip:** If you're nearly done, don't start improving. Finish first.\n\n#### We Do — Boxes Remaining (2 min)\n\nFast round the room — how many plan boxes have you got left?\n\n- \"None — I'm adding detail.\"\n- \"One — the ending.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone with three or more left, sit near me.\n\n#### You Do — The Long Write (18 min)\n\nEighteen minutes. This is protected time — I'll only ask one question and it's always the same one.\n\n- Finish your draft, including the ending you chose on Monday.\n- Add the final line you crafted on Tuesday.\n- Finished early? Reread from the start and add one sensory detail per paragraph.\n- Date it and title it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nCelebrate out loud. Every person in this room has written a complete story this week — say it, and mean it.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Completing a text**: NZC Phase 2 writers take a piece from planning organiser to a complete multi-paragraph draft with an opening, grouped paragraphs and a concluding statement.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Working to completion**: Seeing yourself in the big picture and finishing what you began — the draft-day milestone is named and celebrated deliberately.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Draft Day Celebration":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Draft Day Celebration\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build the success criteria we will be judged against.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Landing It  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Students co-construct the criteria they will use in Stage 3. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups all pointed at tightening and detail — the exact tools next week uses.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *We've completed FIND. Two stages done, two to go — name the remaining two and their questions.*\n\n#### I Do — Name What's Next (3 min)\n\nA short one, because today belongs to your writing.\n\n> \"Next week, we stop being the writer for a while and become the reader.\"\n\nThat's SHAPE. It's the stage where writing actually gets good — not by adding more, but by looking hard at what's there. And you cannot do it while you're still in love with your draft, which is why we do it a week later.\n\n**Tip:** Distance makes you a better editor of your own work.\n\n#### We Do — Build \"What Makes a Strong Narrative\" (5 min)\n\nEverything you've learned in six weeks — call it out. I'll scribe. This list becomes the rubric we use, so make it good.\n\n- What does a strong OPENING do?\n- What does the reader need from the SETTING?\n- How do we show feelings?\n- What makes an ENDING land?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Is there anything on our list that we'd be sad to be judged on? (Then it stays.)\n\n#### You Do — Best-Bit Share (12 min)\n\nEvery single person reads their favourite two sentences. Every person. It's fast, and it's the best twelve minutes of the term.\n\n- Choose your best two sentences.\n- Read them aloud — just those two.\n- Listen to all 25. Notice what other people did that you didn't think of.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The bit of my story I'm proudest of is… The bit I'm least sure about is…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Criteria and goals**: NZC Phase 2: students set specific writing goals based on reflection. Co-constructing the success criteria means the goals are theirs, in their words.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Sharing and discussing the elements of quality writing — students interpret and evaluate rather than receive a checklist.\n"}}},"Week 7: Reading Like an Editor":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I know if my writing is working?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Deconstructing a Model · Tue: Build Together: The Criteria Become a Tool · Wed: Stretch: Word Choice & Sentence Audit · Thu: Write: Setting Personal Goals · Fri: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt this week — your draft is the work. Read it like an editor, not like the person who wrote it.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Where does my reader first know what this story is about?\n- Which paragraph is my strongest? What exactly makes it strong?\n- Which paragraph would a stranger skim? Why?\n- What is one thing my reader will still be confused about?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nimpact · voice · pace · precise · vivid · clarity · effective · deliberate · flow · convincing\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- This part works because…\n- A reader might get lost when…\n- I chose this word because…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read your piece aloud to yourself in a whisper and mark every place your voice hesitates — that's where the writing is broken.\n- **Picture this:** Swap drafts with a partner and read THEIRS aloud to them.\n- **Talk it out first:** Hearing your own words in someone else's mouth is the fastest diagnostic there is.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Colons & semicolons\n- **Tuesday** — Tense consistency\n- **Wednesday** — Complex sentences: main + subordinate\n- **Thursday** — Checking tense consistency in a passage\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Deconstructing a Model":{"content":"### Launch: Deconstructing a Model\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to take a text apart and name the techniques the writer used.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Colons & semicolons |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features.\" Deconstruction of models with effective analysis (Loane Ch 11). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nColons and semicolons this morning — watch for one in today's model, and ask why the writer chose it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. What's the question? (\"How do I want to say it?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Deep dive — Stage 3: SHAPE (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg moved onto Stage 3.\n\n1. Say: \"In FILL you were a collector. In FIND you were a teller. In SHAPE you're something new — you're an EDITOR.\"\n2. Teach the action: both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it. Class copies. \"You're not making new clay. You're shaping the clay you've got.\"\n3. Ask the question that makes this stage safe: \"What's the difference between being MEAN about your writing and being HONEST about it?\" Take three or four answers. Land on: honest names the thing and says what to do about it; mean just names the writer.\n4. Say: \"This week I'm going to show you a weakness in my own writing, out loud, in front of all of you. That's what editors do, and nobody is going to laugh — because on Thursday, you'll be doing it too.\"\n\n**Watch for:** This is the stage students find emotionally hardest. Spend the four minutes on the \"honest vs mean\" distinction — it's what makes Week 7 and the Helping Circle possible.\n\n#### I Do — Annotate the Mentor Text (7 min)\n\nSame text we read in Week 1. This time we're not reading it for the story — we're reading it for the machinery.\n\n> [Annotate live: hook · setting detail · showing not telling · dialogue · paragraph move · short sentence · ending.]\n\nI'm labelling as I go — but watch that I keep asking WHY, not just where. Here's a short sentence. Why here? Because the paragraph before it was long, and she wants us to stop. Naming a technique is Level 1. Explaining why it's there is Level 2, and that's where you're headed.\n\n**Tip:** Anyone can spot a simile. The skill is saying what it's doing.\n\n#### We Do — Diagnose the Weak Exemplar (7 min)\n\nI wrote this one badly on purpose. Find everything that's wrong with it — you have our criteria, so be specific.\n\n- \"I was really scared\" appears three times. What should it be instead?\n- A whole paragraph of dog facts. What job is it doing? (None.)\n- Where should the paragraph breaks go?\n- How does it end, and why doesn't that work?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If you could only fix ONE thing in this piece, which would change it most?\n\n#### You Do — Annotate the Strong One (7 min)\n\nThe strong exemplar, on your own. Mark three techniques and name what each one does.\n\n- Find and label three techniques.\n- Beside each, write what it DOES to the reader.\n- Star the one technique you're going to steal for your own piece.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which technique are you stealing? Say it out loud — then it's a promise.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Deconstruction**: NZC Phase 2: determine how texts are structured and how ideas are conveyed. Analysing a model against shared criteria is the reading half of the reading–writing link.\n- *Key competency — Using language, symbols & text* — **Matching features to purpose**: \"Identify the purpose for which a text was constructed\" and \"match a text's features with its purpose.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Criteria Become a Tool":{"content":"### Build Together: The Criteria Become a Tool\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge our own writing honestly against shared success criteria.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Tense consistency |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\"; setting goals based on reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTense consistency. Most drafts slip between past and present at least once — you'll find yours today.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Who are you when you're in this stage? (The editor, not the writer.)*\n\n#### I Do — Marking Up My Own Writing (5 min)\n\nMy draft, our rubric, on the visualiser. I'm going to find something that isn't working and say so out loud.\n\n> \"Opening — tick, I'm happy with that. Setting — three details, tick. Showing… ah. Right here I've written 'I was terrified'. That's a tell. That's a grow.\"\n\nWatch what I didn't do: I didn't apologise, and I didn't get embarrassed. I found a weakness in my own writing and I wrote it down as a job. That's what editors do. If I can do it in front of 25 of you, you can do it in your own book.\n\n**Tip:** Finding a fault in your writing is a skill, not a failure.\n\n#### We Do — Glow and Grow Together (8 min)\n\nBack to the weak exemplar, but now with the rubric. We fill in the glow and grow columns as a class.\n\n- Find one genuine GLOW — there's always something.\n- Find the single most important GROW.\n- Write the grow as an instruction: \"Next time, try…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why do we only write ONE grow, when we can see five?\n\n#### You Do — Begin Your Self-Analysis (8 min)\n\nRubric beside your draft. Work through it honestly. Nobody else sees this.\n\n- Tick every criterion you can prove with a line from your draft.\n- In the margin, mark the line that proves it.\n- Leave blank anything you can't prove — a blank is information, not a fail.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone name one glow out loud. Just the glow today — grows are tomorrow.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Rereading at every level**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level.\" The rubric gives students a lens for each level.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Strengths and limitations**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations.\" Honest self-assessment is taught by the teacher modelling it on their own writing first.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Word Choice & Sentence Audit":{"content":"### Stretch: Word Choice & Sentence Audit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to audit our own writing for weak verbs, repeated openers and vague nouns.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Complex sentences: main + subordinate |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — word choice (Phase 2): vocabulary chosen deliberately for effect. Sentence structures: variety of type and opener. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nComplex sentences — main clause plus subordinate. Look for whether ALL of your sentences are the same shape.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Are we adding new ideas in this stage? (No — we're improving the ones we've got.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Three-Colour Audit (5 min)\n\nThree colours, three passes, one paragraph. Watch what turns up.\n\n> CIRCLE weak verbs (was, went, got, did, said) · UNDERLINE repeated sentence openers · BOX vague nouns (thing, stuff, place, people)\n\nLook at that — four circles in six sentences. \"Went\" three times. And three of my sentences start with \"I\". Now the fix: \"went to the corner\" becomes \"ran to the corner\"; the third \"I\" sentence starts with the place instead. Proverbs says a word fitly spoken is like apples of gold — the RIGHT word, not more words.\n\n**Tip:** Fix the verbs first. Verbs carry more meaning than any adjective you could add.\n\n#### We Do — Audit and Fix, Live (7 min)\n\nShared paragraph on the board. We audit it in three passes, then fix it in front of everyone.\n\n- Pass 1 — circle the weak verbs. How many?\n- Pass 2 — underline the openers. Any repeats?\n- Pass 3 — box the vague nouns.\n- Now: fix three things only.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the three fixes made the biggest difference for the fewest words changed?\n\n#### You Do — Audit Two Paragraphs (9 min)\n\nYour strongest paragraph and your weakest. Three colours, both.\n\n- Audit both paragraphs with the three colours.\n- Rewrite three words.\n- Rewrite one sentence opener.\n- ✦ Bonus: find a \"was + feeling\" and turn it into an action.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read the sentence you fixed. Just the fixed version, not the original.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precision**: NZC Phase 2: words and phrases are chosen deliberately for effect, with attention to precision and connotation. Weak verbs are the highest-yield target.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Varying openers**: NZC Phase 2: \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase\" — visible in whether sentence openers repeat.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Setting Personal Goals":{"content":"### Write: Setting Personal Goals\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose the two changes that will improve our writing most, and ignore the rest.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Checking tense consistency in a passage |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYou checked tense across a whole passage this morning. Do the same to your own draft now — whole passage, not one sentence.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. What will you take into SHARE next week? (My draft, plus two goals I chose myself.)*\n\n#### I Do — Choose Two, Ignore the Rest (4 min)\n\nI found nine problems in my draft. Watch me choose two and deliberately walk away from seven.\n\n> \"I will show fear through my body instead of naming it in paragraph three.\"\n\"I will break my one giant paragraph into three, at the time changes.\"\n\nBoth of my goals are specific and both name a place in the draft. Compare that with \"I will make it better\" — which tells me nothing and can't be finished. Two goals, done properly, beats nine goals half-attempted. That's how real writers work.\n\n**Tip:** A goal you can't tick off on Friday isn't a goal.\n\n#### We Do — What Are We All Finding? (3 min)\n\nCall out the grows you're seeing in your own drafts. I'll tally them — this decides next week's workshops.\n\n- \"Telling instead of showing.\"\n- \"My ending stops, it doesn't land.\"\n- \"Dialogue punctuation.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which grow got the most hands? That's Thursday's workshop.\n\n#### You Do — Two Goals, Written Down (14 min)\n\nFinish the self-analysis, then write two goals at the top of your draft in \"I will…\" form.\n\n- Complete the rubric self-analysis.\n- Write GOAL 1 as \"I will ____ in ____ [where].\"\n- Write GOAL 2 the same way.\n- Read them to a partner — their job is to remember them next week.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Your partner is now holding you to those. That's the deal.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning/Revising)* — **Specific writing goals**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes\" — the explicit goal-setting expectation for Years 5–6.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: \"Metacognition: being aware of your own thinking.\" Choosing two goals from nine possible faults is a metacognitive act, and needs modelling.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning how to receive feedback well, and how to ask for the feedback we actually need.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading Like an Editor  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing strengths & weaknesses |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers.\" Oral Language: asking clarifying questions and considering the impact of our words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAll four warm-ups this week were about accuracy — next week is about impact. Both matter, in that order.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing SHAPE, entering SHARE — \"Is it ready to give away?\"*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Handover — SHAPE into SHARE (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 3.\n\n1. \"SHAPE told us what needs fixing. What does SHARE do?\" Take answers, land on: we get help, and we actually fix it.\n2. Move the peg to sit between 3 and 4, and draw an arrow on the board that runs BACK from 4 to 3.\n3. Say: \"These two work together and they repeat. Analyse, get feedback, recraft, analyse again. We'll come back to that loop in Week 9.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Plant the loop idea here in one sentence, but don't teach it yet — Week 9 Wednesday is where it's taught properly.\n\n#### I Do — Teach the Protocol (4 min)\n\nThree steps, plus one thing the writer must do and one thing the writer must NOT do.\n\n> READ ALOUD → TWO STARS → ONE WISH. Writer says thank you and writes it down. Writer does not defend, explain, or say \"yeah but\".\n\nThe \"no defending\" rule is the important one. The second you explain what you MEANT, you've stopped listening — and the feedback is about what's on the page, not what's in your head. Say thank you, write it down, decide later.\n\n**Tip:** Feedback is information, not a verdict.\n\n#### We Do — Fishbowl (on me) (7 min)\n\nFour volunteers form a circle around me. I'll read a real piece of my own writing and take real feedback.\n\n- Reader 1: two stars.\n- Reader 2: two stars.\n- Reader 3: one wish.\n- Reader 4: one wish.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Watch my face when I get the wish. Did I defend myself? Did I explain? (No — and neither will you.)\n\n#### You Do — Choose Your Section and Your Question (10 min)\n\nTwo jobs, and the second one matters more than the first.\n\n- Mark the section you'll read on Monday (about 60 seconds' worth).\n- Write ONE specific question you want answered — e.g. \"Does my ending make sense?\"\n- Test it: if the answer could be \"yes\" or \"no\" and nothing else, make it sharper.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Bring your draft, your question, and your listening ears on Monday.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Giving and receiving feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — an explicit expectation, taught here as a protocol rather than left to chance.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Respect and listening**: \"Show respect for others' ways of being · listen carefully and paraphrase ideas of others.\" The writer's job while receiving feedback is to listen and record, not defend.\n"}}},"Week 8: The Helping Circle":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 8 — The Helping Circle\n\n> **Essential question:** How can other readers make my writing better?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1 · Tue: Helping Circle, Day 2 · Wed: Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan · Thu: Write: Targeted Revision Workshops · Fri: Share & Reflect: Before and After |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is feedback, and turning feedback into a plan.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which piece of feedback did I already secretly agree with?\n- Did two people say the same thing? That's not an opinion — that's a fact about my writing.\n- Which change would make the biggest difference to a reader?\n- Which feedback am I choosing to leave — and why is that okay?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nspecific · actionable · confusing · unclear · powerful · memorable · rushed · vivid · honest · convincing\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The part that worked for me was…\n- I wanted to be in the moment more when…\n- Could you show me instead of telling me…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Start with the one piece of feedback you already agreed with before anyone said it.\n- **Picture this:** Two drafts side by side, one covered in pencil.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your number-one change out loud as an action: \"I am going to add / cut / reorder / replace ____ .\"\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Homophones & commonly confused words\n- **Tuesday** — Prepositional phrases\n- **Wednesday** — Fronted adverbials\n- **Thursday** — Adding missing detail to bare sentences\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1":{"content":"### Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to give specific praise and one useful next step to a fellow writer.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Homophones & commonly confused words |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): giving and receiving peer feedback. Oral Language: comments that encourage discussion; considering the impact of our words on others. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nHomophones — their/there, your/you're. Note them if you spot one in a peer's piece, but don't lead with them. We're after impact first.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's the question? (\"Is it ready to give away?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Deep dive — Stage 4: SHARE (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg moved onto Stage 4.\n\n1. Teach the action: open both hands and push them forward, offering something. Class copies. \"This is a giving action. Notice it's the opposite of FILL, which was holding.\"\n2. Ask the question that catches them out: \"Why is this stage called SHARE when we're not publishing until Week 10?\" Let them wrestle with it.\n3. Land it: \"Because sharing with your helpers is how it gets ready. You share it with four people this week so that in two weeks it's good enough to share with everybody.\"\n4. Say the rule for the week: \"In SHARE, the writer is still the boss. You'll be given advice. You choose what to use. But you have to be able to say why.\"\n\n**Watch for:** The \"writer is still the boss\" line is what stops students either ignoring all feedback or blindly obeying it. Say it today, and repeat it on Wednesday.\n\n#### I Do — Restate the Protocol (4 min)\n\nSame shape as Friday's fishbowl, with one addition — the writer's own question comes first.\n\n> 1. Writer reads. 2. Writer asks their question. 3. Two stars each. 4. One wish each. 5. Thank you, and write it down.\n\nThe question first matters. If I ask \"does my ending make sense?\" you know where to listen. Without it you'll tell me about my opening, which I didn't need help with.\n\n**Tip:** A star must point at a word, a sentence or a moment. \"It was good\" costs the writer nothing and gives them nothing.\n\n#### We Do — Form the Circles (3 min)\n\nGroups of four, seated facing each other, timer on the board. Roles named before we start.\n\n- Who reads first in your group?\n- Who's watching the timer?\n- Everyone: where's your pencil for writing feedback down?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Five minutes per writer — two writers today, two tomorrow.\n\n#### You Do — Helping Circle, Round 1 (14 min)\n\nTwo writers per group today. Full protocol, no shortcuts.\n\n- Read your marked section aloud.\n- Ask your question.\n- Receive two stars and one wish from each listener.\n- Write every piece of feedback down in your own words — immediately.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Write it down NOW, before it evaporates. Tomorrow the other two read.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Impact of our words**: NZC Phase 2: \"give and respond to natural prompts for turn-taking in conversation and consider the impact of their words on others.\"\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Using peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revisions are made \"using audience and peer feedback\" — feedback is only useful if it is specific enough to act on.\n"},"Tuesday: Helping Circle, Day 2":{"content":"### Helping Circle, Day 2\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to make our feedback actionable — something a writer could actually do tomorrow.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Prepositional phrases |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): peer feedback that supports revision for clarity, audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPrepositional phrases — under the bridge, before the bell. Listen for whether a peer's writing tells you WHERE and WHEN.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Is this the last time we'll ask for feedback? (No — SHARE repeats until it's ready.)*\n\n#### I Do — Make the Wish Actionable (3 min)\n\nOne upgrade to yesterday's protocol, based on what I heard.\n\n> NOT a wish: \"Make it more interesting.\"\nA wish: \"I wanted to know what you were thinking while you stood at the gate.\"\n\nThe first one is a feeling with no instructions. The second one names a place in the piece and a thing to add. If your wish doesn't point at a spot in their writing, it isn't finished yet.\n\n**Tip:** Every wish should be able to start with \"at the part where…\".\n\n#### We Do — Regroup (2 min)\n\nBack into the same circles, timer reset, remaining two writers ready.\n\n- Readers three and four — draft and question out.\n- Listeners — pencils ready to give, not just to take.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Same protocol. Same five minutes each.\n\n#### You Do — Helping Circle, Round 2 (16 min)\n\nThe remaining writers read. Listeners: every wish must point at a spot in the writing.\n\n- Read, ask your question, receive stars and wishes.\n- Write down every piece of feedback in your own words.\n- As a listener, check each of your wishes names a specific part.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everybody now has three pieces of feedback and a question answered. Tomorrow we decide what to do with it.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revision for clarity**: NZC Phase 2: \"make simple revisions to their texts to improve the clarity for the intended audience and purpose (e.g., replacing words, adding sentences, and using audience and peer feedback).\"\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Clarifying questions**: Listeners ask clarifying questions rather than issuing verdicts — the questions themselves often do the revision work.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan":{"content":"### Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sort feedback and decide, as the author, what to act on.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Fronted adverbials |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): making revisions using peer feedback while retaining authorship of the piece. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nFronted adverbials — \"Without warning, …\" / \"Later that night, …\" — a very fast way to fix a paragraph that starts flat.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who decides what changes? (The writer. Always.)*\n\n#### I Do — Triage (5 min)\n\nThree piles. Watch me sort real feedback into them, out loud.\n\n> DO IT · THINK ABOUT IT · POLITELY LEAVE IT\n\n\"Show the fear instead of naming it\" — do it, two people said it. \"Add more about the dog's breed\" — leave it, it's not what my story is about. \"Maybe start at the gate\" — think about it; I'm not sure, so I'll try it and see. Leaving something isn't laziness, but I do have to be able to say WHY.\n\n**Tip:** If two people said the same thing, it goes straight in the DO pile.\n\n#### We Do — Sort a Sample Set (6 min)\n\nHere are six comments from an imaginary Helping Circle. Sort them together.\n\n- \"I loved the bit with the seagull.\"\n- \"You should add a dragon.\"\n- \"I got confused about who was speaking.\"\n- \"Maybe make the ending shorter.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which pile does each go in — and which comment isn't feedback at all?\n\n#### You Do — Your Revision Plan (10 min)\n\nThree changes, in order, written as actions — not as feelings.\n\n- Sort your feedback into the three piles.\n- Write change 1, 2 and 3 as actions (\"Add two sentences of what I was thinking before I opened the door\").\n- Number them in the order you'll do them — biggest difference first.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Show me your number one change with a finger on the page.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Deciding what to revise**: NZC Phase 2 writers use feedback to revise for clarity and audience — selecting from feedback is part of the skill, not an avoidance of it.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Think before acting**: \"Think before acting\" and \"know your own values\" — the writer stays in charge of their piece, and must justify what they leave.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Targeted Revision Workshops":{"content":"### Write: Targeted Revision Workshops\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the four revision moves: add, cut, reorder and replace.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Adding missing detail to bare sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"make simple revisions to their texts to improve the clarity for the intended audience and purpose (e.g., replacing words, adding sentences…).\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nBare sentences got detail added this morning — exactly the move most of your revision plans need.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What are we doing to the writing now? (Recrafting it — this is the loop.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Moves, Four Hand Signals (3 min)\n\nEvery revision you will ever make is one of four moves. Learn the signals — I'll use them all year.\n\n> ADD (hands apart) · CUT (scissors) · REORDER (swap hands) · REPLACE (one hand over the other)\n\nWatch me do all four on one paragraph. Add: two sentences of what I was thinking. Cut: the whole sentence about the weather. Reorder: the dialogue comes before the description, not after. Replace: \"went\" becomes \"bolted\". Same paragraph, unrecognisable.\n\n**Tip:** Most students only ever ADD. The best revision this week will probably be a CUT.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Move (2 min)\n\nRound the room. Your number one change — which of the four moves is it?\n\n- \"Mine's an ADD.\"\n- \"Mine's a REORDER — my ending should be earlier.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anybody's move a CUT? Good — cutting is the bravest one.\n\n#### You Do — Revision Workshops (16 min)\n\nEverybody revises. I'm running two six-minute workshops at the back table — come if the topic is your grow, whoever you are.\n\n- Work through your revision plan in order.\n- Name the move each time before you make it.\n- Workshop 1 (6 min): endings that stop rather than land.\n- Workshop 2 (6 min): dialogue punctuation.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Did anyone's story change SHAPE today, not just its words? Tell us in one sentence.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revision moves**: NZC Phase 2 names replacing words and adding sentences explicitly; cutting and reordering complete the set of moves Years 5–6 writers need.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Adding detail with phrases**: \"Expand sentences using adverbial phrases\" and vary the position of phrases — the mechanics behind the ADD move.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Before and After":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Before and After\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to see and describe the difference our revision made.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and feedforward |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): students articulate the effect of revisions on clarity and impact for the reader. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were all detail-adders. Every one of them is a revision tool.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. How many times can this loop happen? (As many as the piece needs — that's next week.)*\n\n#### I Do — Model a Before and After (3 min)\n\nOld version, new version, and the name of the move. That's the whole share.\n\n> BEFORE: \"I was terrified.\"\nAFTER: \"I couldn't remember how to breathe properly, so I just kept running.\"\nMOVE: replace.\n\nNotice I read the OLD one first without cringing. The old version isn't shameful — it's the version that got me to the new one. Every good sentence has a worse sentence underneath it.\n\n**Tip:** Naming the move is the bit that makes it stick.\n\n#### We Do — What Changed? (4 min)\n\nI'll read a pair. You tell me what changed and what it does to you as a reader.\n\n- \"What's different?\"\n- \"What does the new version make you feel that the old one didn't?\"\n- \"Which move was it?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Could that move work on someone else's piece too?\n\n#### You Do — Before/After Share (13 min)\n\nIn pairs first, then four volunteers to the class. Old version, new version, name the move.\n\n- Read your before and after to a partner.\n- Name the move you used.\n- Listen to your partner's — did they use a different move than you?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The feedback that helped me most was… because…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Evaluating a revision**: Phase 2 writers reread and revise for clarity; being able to say WHY the new version is better is what makes the skill transferable.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: Naming the move used and its effect makes the revision strategy conscious and repeatable.\n"}}},"Week 9: Recraft, Edit, Best Copy":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy\n\n> **Essential question:** What does it look like to make my writing better rather than just neater?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Real Second Draft · Tue: Build Together: Cohesion & Flow · Wed: Stretch: The Edit (GPS) · Thu: Write: Best Copy in Books · Fri: Share & Reflect: Finishing & Reading Aloud |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is the real second draft, the edit, and the best copy in your book.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which paragraph needs rewriting rather than patching?\n- Where does my voice stumble when I read it aloud?\n- Have I checked capitals, speech marks, tense and spelling — one pass each?\n- Would I be happy for a stranger to read this in my handwriting?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nrecraft · rewrite · polish · smooth · precise · consistent · legible · deliberate · careful · finished\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The version I like better is…\n- I rewrote this part because…\n- I checked for…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Cover your old paragraph with paper and rewrite it from memory. What you remember is what mattered.\n- **Picture this:** A page with one paragraph crossed out and a better one beside it.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read the paragraph aloud to a partner and stop the moment either of you stumbles.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Onomatopoeia & sound words\n- **Tuesday** — Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions\n- **Wednesday** — Paragraph cohesion\n- **Thursday** — Identifying and removing repetition\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Real Second Draft":{"content":"### Launch: The Real Second Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that recrafting means rewriting a section, not just patching words in the old one.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Onomatopoeia & sound words |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revisions at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level to improve clarity for the intended audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nOnomatopoeia — crack, hiss, thud. One well-placed sound word can carry a paragraph. One is plenty; five is a cartoon.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Which move is a rewrite? (REPLACE — at paragraph size.)*\n\n#### I Do — Rewrite, Don't Tinker (6 min)\n\nWatch me cover my paragraph with a piece of paper and write it again from scratch.\n\n> OLD: \"I ran down the road and I was looking everywhere and I couldn't see him and I was really worried.\"\nNEW (written fresh, not edited): \"I ran. Past the letterboxes, past the church, past the dairy where he sometimes waited for chips.\"\n\nIf I'd tried to fix the old sentence I'd have kept its shape and just swapped a word or two. Covering it forces me to write what I actually mean now, with everything I've learned since. It's faster too — patching takes longer than rewriting, nearly every time.\n\n**Tip:** When a paragraph is broken in more than two places, cover it and start again.\n\n#### We Do — Which Is Stronger, and Why? (5 min)\n\nTwo versions on the board. Vote, then justify with criteria language — not \"it sounds better\".\n\n- Which one shows instead of tells?\n- Which one varies its sentence length?\n- Which one would our audience follow more easily?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Can you use one of our rubric words to explain your choice?\n\n#### You Do — Begin the Second Draft (10 min)\n\nStart with revision plan change #1. Cover the old paragraph if it needs a rewrite rather than a patch.\n\n- Complete change #1 from your revision plan.\n- If it's a rewrite, cover the original and write fresh.\n- Read the new version and the old one side by side.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who rewrote rather than patched? What was that like?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Whole-text revision**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" and revise for clarity — recrafting a whole paragraph is a whole-text-level move.\n- *Writing — Writing craft* — **Deliberate craft choices**: Rewriting rather than patching lets students apply everything learned in Weeks 4–7 at once, instead of protecting the original wording.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Cohesion & Flow":{"content":"### Build Together: Cohesion & Flow\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to test our writing by ear and smooth the places a reader would stumble.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures (Phase 2): combining sentences and varying phrase position; \"begin to use linking words and phrases\" for cohesion. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCoordinating and subordinating conjunctions — and/but/so versus because/although/while. The second set makes writing sound older.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's the difference between revising and editing? (Revising = ideas and shape. Editing = surface. Revising first.)*\n\n#### I Do — Test It By Ear (5 min)\n\nI'm going to read my piece aloud and mark every place my own voice trips.\n\n> [Read aloud; stop, mark with a dot, keep going. Don't fix anything yet.]\n\nThree dots. And here's the thing — I couldn't SEE any of those on the page, but my mouth found all three. Your ear knows more about writing than your eye does. Mark first, fix afterwards; if you stop to fix, you lose the flow and miss the next one.\n\n**Tip:** Whisper-read. It works just as well and the room stays sane.\n\n#### We Do — Smooth a Choppy Paragraph (7 min)\n\nHere's a paragraph where every sentence is the same length and starts the same way. Fix it together.\n\n- Combine two short sentences into one with \"because\" or \"although\".\n- Change one sentence to start with a phrase instead of \"I\".\n- Add a linking word between two paragraphs.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Read the original and the new one aloud. What changed in the SOUND?\n\n#### You Do — Changes 2 and 3, Then Read Aloud (9 min)\n\nFinish your revision plan, then whisper-read the whole piece and dot every stumble.\n\n- Complete revision changes #2 and #3.\n- Whisper-read your whole piece, marking stumbles with a dot.\n- Fix the two worst dots.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many dots did you find? That number is your to-do list.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to entertain* — **Linking words and phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"begin to use linking words and phrases (also, another, together with)\" — and, in narrative, time connectives that move a reader between paragraphs.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Combining and varying**: NZC Phase 2: \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase\" — the exact fix for choppy writing.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Edit (GPS)":{"content":"### Stretch: The Edit (GPS)\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to edit in separate passes — one thing at a time.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Paragraph cohesion |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\"; \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nParagraph cohesion this morning — today we check the same thing on our own page.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Draw the process as a cycle. Where does the loop happen? (Between SHAPE and SHARE.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — The cycle — it's a loop, not a line (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Board space. Jotters open.\n\n1. Draw the four stages as a straight line, left to right. Then say: \"This is what everyone thinks writing is. It's wrong.\"\n2. Draw a big arrow curving back from SHARE to SHAPE. \"Real writers go round this more than once. Analyse, feedback, recraft — then analyse again. Some writers go round six times before they're happy.\"\n3. Ask: \"How many times have YOU been round it with this piece?\" Take a show of hands for once, twice, three times.\n4. Jotters: every student draws the process as a cycle, from memory, labelling all four stages and drawing the loop arrow in the right place. Ninety seconds.\n\n**Watch for:** This is the lesson that stops students finishing too early. The drawing is quick and it's the first time they've had to produce the whole process themselves rather than recognise it.\n\n#### I Do — One Pass Per Thing (5 min)\n\nFour passes, in this order. Watch how much I find on pass two that I completely missed on pass one.\n\n> Pass 1: capitals & full stops · Pass 2: speech punctuation · Pass 3: tense · Pass 4: spellings I'm unsure of\n\nReading for everything at once means you find nothing. On pass one I'm only looking at the start and end of sentences — I'm not even reading for meaning. Pass two, only speech marks. It feels slower and it's twice as fast.\n\n**Tip:** Put a finger under each line on the punctuation passes. Slow eyes catch more.\n\n#### We Do — Four Passes Together (6 min)\n\nShort passage on the board with errors in it. We do one pass at a time and count what each pass finds.\n\n- Pass 1 — how many capitals or full stops are wrong?\n- Pass 2 — how many speech-mark errors?\n- Pass 3 — any tense slips?\n- Pass 4 — which words would you check in a dictionary?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which pass found the most? Which found the errors you'd have been most embarrassed by?\n\n#### You Do — Edit Your Own, Four Passes (10 min)\n\nFour separate passes on your own piece. Word cards and dictionaries out. Then swap for one last pass.\n\n- Pass 1: capitals and full stops.\n- Pass 2: speech punctuation.\n- Pass 3: tense consistency.\n- Pass 4: spellings — check, don't guess.\n- Peer swap: partner does one fresh pass for anything you missed.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Editing isn't the boring bit — it's the respect bit. Colossians 3:23: whatever you do, work at it with all your heart.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Editing with known punctuation**: NZC Phase 2: \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\" — everything taught in the Term 1 warm-ups is now the checklist.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Checking spellings**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words\" — checking, not guessing, is the taught behaviour.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Best Copy in Books":{"content":"### Write: Best Copy in Books\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to present finished writing carefully, in our best handwriting.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Identifying and removing repetition |\n| **NZC link** | Transcription — handwriting (Phase 2): \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRepetition got removed this morning. Final check: did you start three sentences in a row the same way?\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Is a best copy the same as publishing? (No — publishing is next week, when a reader gets it.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Presentation Standard (3 min)\n\nHere's exactly what a finished page looks like. I'll write the first three lines so you can see the pace.\n\n> Title · date · margin respected · joined, legible handwriting · each paragraph on a new line and indented.\n\nWatch how slowly I'm writing. Best copy is not a race, and it's not the same as fast copying — this is the version a stranger will read.\n\n**Tip:** If you make a mistake, one neat line through it. No scribbles, no correction fluid battles.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Checklist (2 min)\n\nFour things go on the board and stay there for the lesson.\n\n- Title and date.\n- Paragraphs on new lines, indented.\n- Joined, legible handwriting.\n- No crossings-out except one neat line.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anything else we want on the list?\n\n#### You Do — Best Copy Into Writing Books (18 min)\n\nQuiet and calm. Take the whole time. This is the version that goes in your book for the year.\n\n- Write your best copy into your writing book.\n- Follow the four-point checklist.\n- Reread as you go — you'll still catch things.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"If you're not finished, that's fine — finish tomorrow. Nobody rushes a best copy.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Legibility and stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\" The best copy is the assessment of this indicator.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Layout conventions**: NZC Phase 2: \"use layout conventions to indicate paragraphs (each begins on a new line and is indented from the margin).\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Finishing & Reading Aloud":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Finishing & Reading Aloud\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read our writing aloud well — with pace, pauses and eye contact.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively with specific detail; vary sentence structure and length for effect — heard in delivery. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were the polish tools: sound, conjunctions, cohesion, repetition.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's left? (Publishing it — giving it to the reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Reading Voice (2 min)\n\nSame paragraph, read badly and then well. Tell me the three differences.\n\n> [Read fast, flat, eyes down. Then: slower, pausing at the full stops, looking up at the last line.]\n\nSlower than feels natural. A real pause at every full stop. And look up on the last sentence — that's the one you want them to keep.\n\n**Tip:** Nerves make everyone speed up. Decide now to go slower than feels right.\n\n#### We Do — Practise Together (4 min)\n\nOne shared paragraph, whole class, twice — once flat, once with the reading voice.\n\n- Everyone reads it flat.\n- Now with pauses at every full stop.\n- Now looking up on the last line.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version would you rather listen to for twenty-five readers?\n\n#### You Do — Finish and Rehearse (14 min)\n\nFinish your best copy if it isn't done. If it is, rehearse your reading in pairs.\n\n- Finish the best copy.\n- Rehearse reading your piece (or your 60-second extract) aloud to a partner.\n- Give your partner ONE tip on delivery. Take theirs.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: Microsoft Word, and the celebration. Bring your best reading voice.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — delivery is part of engaging them.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Preparing to perform**: Rehearsing a reading, taking a tip from a peer and acting on it — self-management ahead of a public moment.\n"}}},"Week 10: Publish & Celebrate":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate\n\n> **Essential question:** What has this term made me, as a writer?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Typing as a Publishing Skill · Tue: Build Together: Presentation & Proofing · Wed: Stretch: Final Proof & Print · Thu: The Celebration: Author's Chair · Fri: Reflect: What Kind of Writer Am I Becoming? |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Publish it. Type the piece you handwrote, proof it properly, and read it to your audience.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Is my filename right — Surname_Firstname_Narrative_T1?\n- Have I read it backwards, sentence by sentence, to catch what my eye skips?\n- Does it look finished — title, name, one clear font, paragraphs separated?\n- Which 60 seconds will I read aloud at the celebration?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\npublished · audience · presentation · proofread · format · author · final · celebrate · share · finished\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- By ____ , Room ____\n- I'd like to read the part where…\n- Thank you for listening.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read only your favourite paragraph at the celebration. That counts as published.\n- **Picture this:** A printed page with your name on it.\n- **Talk it out first:** Practise your opening line three times to a partner before you read to the room.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Review: Term 1 punctuation\n- **Tuesday** — Review: Term 1 grammar\n- **Wednesday** — Review: Term 1 sentence types\n- **Thursday** — Full-passage edit using the Term 1 checklist\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Typing as a Publishing Skill":{"content":"### Launch: Typing as a Publishing Skill\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to type and save our published writing properly.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 1 punctuation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — publishing: transferring an edited handwritten text to a digital format, maintaining paragraph conventions and accuracy. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis morning's warm-up reviewed every punctuation mark from Term 1. You're about to need all of them.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. We're at the very end of the loop — what does publishing mean? (Giving it to the reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Publish (6 min)\n\nScreen up. I'll open Word, set a title, type my first paragraph and save it — narrating every step.\n\n> Title, then my name. Enter twice between paragraphs. File → Save As → Surname_Firstname_Narrative_T1.\n\nNotice I typed the title FIRST and saved BEFORE typing anything else. Saving an empty document takes four seconds; retyping a lost page takes twenty minutes.\n\n**Tip:** Save now, then save every time you finish a paragraph.\n\n#### We Do — Filename and Location (5 min)\n\nEveryone says the convention out loud, then finds the folder before they type a word.\n\n- Surname_Firstname_Narrative_T1\n- Where does it get saved?\n- What do you do first, before typing?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What happens if two people save with the same name?\n\n#### You Do — Start Typing (10 min)\n\nType from your book copy. Don't rewrite as you type — that version is already finished.\n\n- Create, title and save the document BEFORE typing.\n- Type from your handwritten best copy.\n- Save again at the end of every paragraph.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Save. Then save again. That's the lesson.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Layout conventions**: Paragraph conventions carry across to the typed version: each paragraph begins on a new line, separated clearly for the reader.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Organisation**: Saving with an agreed filename in an agreed place — a small, teachable habit that saves an enormous amount of time later.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Presentation & Proofing":{"content":"### Build Together: Presentation & Proofing\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to format a document for a reader and to use a spell-checker critically.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 1 grammar |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): using tools to check spelling, while remaining the decision-maker about the text. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nGrammar review this morning — the squiggly red and blue lines in Word are a suggestion, not a boss. You decide.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who is this document for? (Our celebration audience and a family reader — the same people from Week 3.)*\n\n#### I Do — Font, Title, and Being the Boss of the Spell-Checker (5 min)\n\nWatch me format, then watch me refuse the computer's advice — twice.\n\n> Word suggests changing \"dairy\" to \"diary\". Word underlines \"Koro\" in red.\n\nThe computer doesn't know what a dairy is, and it doesn't know te reo Māori. It's a helper, not a boss. But when it flags a word I genuinely wasn't sure about, I check it properly.\n\n**Tip:** Every red line is a question, not an instruction.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Standard (5 min)\n\nFour presentation decisions, agreed as a class so the published set looks like a set.\n\n- Title and author name at the top.\n- One clear, readable font at a sensible size.\n- Paragraphs clearly separated.\n- No decorative fonts that fight the words.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does a whole class using one standard look better than 25 different designs?\n\n#### You Do — Type and Format (11 min)\n\nKeep typing. Format as you go, using the agreed standard.\n\n- Finish typing your piece.\n- Apply the four-point presentation standard.\n- Deal with every red and blue line — accept or refuse, deliberately.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found a mistake while typing that they'd missed on paper? That's why we type second, not first.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Checking spelling**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words\" — a spell-checker is another such tool, and it needs judgement.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Presenting for a reader**: Presentation is part of writing with the audience in mind: a readable font, a clear title and separated paragraphs serve the reader.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Final Proof & Print":{"content":"### Stretch: Final Proof & Print\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to proofread a finished piece using a technique that catches what our eyes skip.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 1 sentence types |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): making final edits using known punctuation, with peer checking. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSentence types review — read your typed piece and check you haven't got ten sentences of exactly the same shape.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Tomorrow the loop ends. Where does the writing go? (To the reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — Read It Backwards (4 min)\n\nThe last proofreading trick of the term, and the strangest one.\n\n> Start at the LAST sentence. Read it. Then the second-to-last. Work backwards to the top.\n\nReading backwards breaks the story, so my brain stops predicting what comes next — and prediction is exactly why we don't see our own mistakes. Watch: I've read this piece eleven times and I've just found a missing full stop.\n\n**Tip:** Backwards for errors, forwards for flow. Different jobs, different directions.\n\n#### We Do — Partner Proof Protocol (3 min)\n\nAgree how the swap works before we start.\n\n- Swap documents; read the other person's backwards.\n- Mark, don't fix — it's their piece.\n- Hand it back and explain what you marked.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why do we mark rather than fix?\n\n#### You Do — Proof, Check, Print (14 min)\n\nBackwards proof, partner check, then print or save to the class folder.\n\n- Proofread your own piece backwards.\n- Partner check — swap and mark.\n- Fix what your partner found.\n- Print or save to the class folder. Optional: a title-page illustration.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow, your writing meets its audience.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Final proofreading**: NZC Phase 2 editing expectations applied to a finished, published text — including peer checking as a final pass.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Variety check**: A final read for sentence variety: Phase 2 writers are expected to use a range of structures and types for impact, not one repeated shape.\n"},"Thursday: The Celebration: Author's Chair":{"content":"### The Celebration: Author's Chair\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to give our writing to a real audience.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Full-passage edit using the Term 1 checklist |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engaging listeners with specific detail; listening and responding respectfully to others. Writing: publication for a real audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYour last warm-up was a full-passage edit — and today the passage is finished. Look what you made.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the last step of the last stage. Say the whole process, all four, one more time.*\n\n#### I Do — Set the Tone (3 min)\n\nI read first. Then I'll tell you the one job the audience has.\n\n> [Read your own piece — properly, with the reading voice.]\n\nConsider inviting another class, the principal or whānau. This is an event, not a lesson. Applause for every single reader, no exceptions — that's the rule for today.\n\n**Tip:** The teacher reading first makes the first student reader far braver.\n\n#### We Do — The Audience's Job (2 min)\n\nTwo rules for listeners, agreed before the first reader stands up.\n\n- Listen all the way through — no talking, no fiddling.\n- After each reader, one person names one specific thing they'll remember.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What does \"specific\" mean again?\n\n#### You Do — Author's Chair Celebration (17 min)\n\nEveryone reads. 60–90 seconds each — the whole piece or your chosen extract. Split across today and tomorrow if the class is large.\n\n- Read your published piece (or your 60-second extract).\n- Take the applause. Don't apologise for anything.\n- Listen to every other reader.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nName the value out loud: this is Aroha — we gave our stories to each other. Soli Deo Gloria.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging an audience**: NZC Phase 2: narrative retellings with specific details that actively engage listeners — delivered here to a genuine audience.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Contributing to a community**: Students contribute their work to a shared class and school occasion, and respond respectfully to the contributions of others.\n"},"Friday: Reflect: What Kind of Writer Am I Becoming?":{"content":"### Reflect: What Kind of Writer Am I Becoming?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to explain our writing process and to reflect on how we have grown as writers.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 1 — Narrative: “A Moment That Mattered” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Celebrate  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" This lesson assesses the process knowledge taught all term. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nLook back across all ten weeks of warm-ups: every single one of them turned up inside a real piece of writing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages, in order, in your own words — this is the assessment.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Assessment — can they explain it? (5 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered. This is the assessment, so treat it as one.\n\n1. Give the task exactly as written.\n   > \"Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Name all four stages, say what a writer actually does in each one, and give one example from your own narrative this term.\"\n2. Out loud first: 60 seconds each to a partner, both directions. Listen in on four or five students — that listening IS your assessment record for most of the class.\n3. Then in writing, as question 1 of the Week 10 reflection.\n4. Finish by uncovering the wall strip and doing the chant one last time, all four, together.\n\n**Watch for:** A strong answer: all four stages in order with their questions · what a writer DOES in each, in their own words · a real example from their own piece for at least two stages · knows Stages 3 and 4 repeat in cycles. Record it on the \"Process\" row of the rubric.\n\n#### I Do — Reflect Honestly (3 min)\n\nMy own reflection first, including the part I found hard.\n\n> \"The hardest stage for me was SHAPE. I didn't want to admit that my third paragraph wasn't working, because I'd spent so long on it.\"\n\nReflection isn't a list of achievements. It's the truth about what was hard, and what you'd do differently. That's the version worth writing.\n\n**Tip:** \"It was good\" is not a reflection. Name a stage, name a moment, name a change.\n\n#### We Do — What We Know About Narrative Now (5 min)\n\nBuild the wall chart we'll compare against in Term 2. Everything we know about narrative — call it out.\n\n- What makes a moment worth telling?\n- How do we show instead of tell?\n- What makes an ending land?\n- What are the four stages of our process?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What do you know now that you didn't know in Week 1?\n\n#### You Do — The Writer's Reflection (12 min)\n\nIn your jotter, then into your portfolio. Take it seriously — this is the piece you'll read again next year.\n\n- Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it (all four stages, what a writer does in each, one example from your own piece).\n- The moment I chose was… and I chose it because…\n- The part of my story I'm proudest of is…\n- The biggest change I made was…\n- One thing I can do now that I couldn't do in Week 1 is…\n- My goal for Term 2 is…\n- Finally: turn back to your Week 2 page and read what you wrote ten weeks ago.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRead the Soli Deo Gloria line together: we made something with the words we were given. Close in prayer or a shared thank-you.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflecting on content and process**: NZC Phase 2: goals are set \"based on reflection on their own writing content and processes\" — students must therefore be able to describe the process itself.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: \"Metacognition: being aware of your own thinking.\" Explaining the four stages in your own words, with examples from your own piece, is the term's metacognitive assessment.\n"}}}}},"Term 2 — Explanation & Report":{"label":"Week","children":{"Unit Plan & Overview":{"content":"### Term 2 Unit Plan — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?”\n\n\n| Unit | |\n|---|---|\n| **Year level** | Years 5–6 (ages 9–12) · NZC English Phase 2 |\n| **Duration** | 10 weeks · 25 min × 5 days |\n| **Universal concept** | Wonder and Order — The World Is Made of Systems We Can Understand |\n| **Anchor value** | Mōhio — Know: God, His story and our place in it |\n| **Throughline question** | “How does it work — and can I make someone else understand it?” |\n| **Published outcome** | An illustrated explanation written for a Year 3 buddy: best copy handwritten with a labelled diagram (Week 9), typed in Microsoft Word and presented to the buddy class (Week 10). |\n\n#### Key Scripture\n\n- Psalm 19:1 — \"The heavens declare the glory of God\" (Week 1: the world is worth looking at closely)\n- Proverbs 25:2 — \"It is the glory of God to conceal a matter; to search out a matter is the glory of kings\" (Week 2: research is a noble thing)\n- Job 12:7–10 — \"Ask the animals, and they will teach you\" (Week 3: creation itself is a teacher)\n- Psalm 111:2 — \"Great are the works of the Lord, studied by all who delight in them\" (Week 6)\n- Colossians 1:16–17 — \"In Him all things hold together\" (Week 10: order is not an accident)\n\n#### NZC English — Phase 2 links\n\n- Writing to inform — introduce a topic and group information together using simple paragraphs; use linking words and phrases; use topic-related vocabulary; have a concluding statement\n- Composition — identify the audience and purpose for a text; plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting genre, register and word choice\n- Sentence structures — use a range of sentence structures and types to suit purpose; expand sentences using adverbial phrases; combine sentences varying the position of the phrase\n- Punctuation — use commas correctly for complex sentences, phrases and transition words, with some support\n- Writing processes (Planning) — use organisers to organise and plan; make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases\n- Writing processes (Drafting) — write non-fiction paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences and a concluding sentence; use layout conventions to indicate paragraphs\n- Writing processes (Revising & Editing) — reread at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level; revise for clarity for the intended audience; give and receive peer feedback\n- Reading — identify the audience and purpose of texts that inform; determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements; identify the central message and key details in sequence\n- Oral language — give descriptions with specific details to actively engage listeners; use precise vocabulary; ask clarifying questions\n\n#### The arc of the term\n\n\n| Weeks | Stage | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weeks 1–2 | Stage 1 — FILL · Wonder & research | Deconstruct explanation texts, build a wonder-question bank, research and make notes in key words. No drafting yet. |\n| Week 3 | Stage 1 → 2 — Plan & audience | Choose the topic, sequence it on a flow-chart organiser, and meet the real audience: a Year 3 buddy. |\n| Weeks 4–6 | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting | Opening definition · the how-it-works sequence · the labelled diagram and the closing wonder statement. Full draft by Week 6. |\n| Week 7 | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analyse | Deconstruct a model, run the clarity audit against the rubric, set two goals. |\n| Week 8 | Stage 3 → 4 — SHARE · Feedback | Helping Circle with the buddy lens, the \"explain it without your paper\" test, targeted workshops. |\n| Weeks 9–10 | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recraft & publish | Second draft, four-pass edit, best copy with diagram, typed publication, and reading it to the buddy class. |\n\n#### The writing process this term\n\nSame process, new genre. Term 1 taught the four stages inside a narrative; Term 2 proves they belong to writing itself, not to stories. Students are expected to arrive already able to name FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE, so this term's spotlights are about TRANSFER — what each stage looks like when you're explaining how something works rather than telling a story.\n\n**FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE** — Unchanged from Term 1 — same words, same questions, same hand actions, same chant. What changes is what a writer actually does inside each stage when the purpose is to inform rather than to entertain.\n\n\n| Stage | Question | Hand action | What a writer does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **🪣 FILL** | What do I want to say? | Two cupped hands, like holding water. | I wonder, I ask, I find out, I take notes in my own words. In a story I filled up with memories. In an explanation I fill up with FACTS — and I have to know where each one came from. |\n| **🎯 FIND** | Who do I want to say it to? | Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader. | I find my reader — this term it's a Year 3 buddy — and I find the ORDER. An explanation has to go in the right sequence or nobody can follow it. |\n| **🔍 SHAPE** | How do I want to say it? | Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it. | I read it like an editor — but this time I'm checking whether it's CLEAR, not whether it's exciting. Would my buddy understand every word? Is anything in the wrong order? Did I explain the hard bit or skip it? |\n| **🎁 SHARE** | Is it ready to give away? | Open both hands and push them forward, offering. | I test it on a real person, I recraft it as many times as it takes, I check the small things — and then I give it to my buddy and watch whether they actually understand. |\n\n#### Teaching & recall schedule\n\n\n| Week | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | COLD RECALL — all four stages after the break, no notes, no strip. Then re-establish the daily Process Check and put the wall strip back up. |\n| **Week 2** | TRANSFER — FILL in a new genre. In a story you filled with memories; in an explanation you fill with facts. Same stage, different bucket. |\n| **Week 3** | TRANSFER — FIND in a new genre. The reader is now a real eight-year-old, and \"shape\" now means the correct ORDER. |\n| **Week 4** | SPACED RECALL — 60-second quick-fire, no notes. |\n| **Week 5** | TEACH IT — explain the four stages to your Year 3 buddy in your own words. |\n| **Week 6** | SPACED RECALL + move the peg, justified out loud. |\n| **Week 7** | TRANSFER — SHAPE in a new genre. In narrative you asked \"is it vivid?\" Now you ask \"is it clear?\" |\n| **Week 8** | TRANSFER — SHARE in a new genre. The feedback question changes from \"did you feel it?\" to \"did you understand it?\" |\n| **Week 9** | THE CYCLE — draw it from memory again, and mark where a confused reader sends you back to. |\n| **Week 10** | ASSESS — explain the process AND explain how it changed between a story and an explanation. |\n\n#### Assessment — Week 10 — can they explain it, and can they transfer it?\n\n> Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Then explain how each stage was DIFFERENT this term, when you were explaining how something works instead of telling a story.\n\n**A strong answer…**\n\n- Names all four stages in order, with the question that belongs to each\n- Explains in their own words what a writer DOES in each stage\n- Names at least two real differences between the narrative version and the explanation version (e.g. \"in FILL I collected memories, this term I collected facts and had to check them\")\n- Knows that Stages 3 and 4 repeat in cycles — and that a confused reader sends you back to SHAPE\n\n#### Marking load across the term\n\n\n| When | What | Teacher time |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 2 Fri** | Notes check — a glance at jotter notes for \"own words, not copied\". No written marking. | ~10 min for the class |\n| **Week 3 Fri** | Plan gallery — a glance at flow-chart organisers during the gallery walk. | ~10 min for the class |\n| **Week 6 Thu** | Draft check: one of three stamps — On track / Needs a conference / Flying. | ~15 sec per student |\n| **Week 8** | You mark nothing — the Helping Circle and the buddy test do the feedback. | 0 |\n| **Week 10** | Published explanation against the single-point rubric: tick criteria, one glow, one grow. Plus the spoken process check. | ~3 min per student |\n\n#### Single-point rubric\n\n\n| Success criterion | What a strong piece does | Glow ✓ | Grow → |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Title & opening** | Names the topic and opens with a clear definition sentence — the reader knows exactly what is being explained | | |\n| **Sequence** | Steps are in an order that works; nothing important is missing and nothing is out of place | | |\n| **Paragraphs** | Each paragraph has a topic sentence, detail sentences and does one job | | |\n| **Connectives** | Time and cause words used to link steps (first, next, because, so, as a result, this means that) | | |\n| **Technical vocabulary** | Uses the proper words for the topic — and explains each one the first time it appears | | |\n| **Present tense & general nouns** | Written as a general truth (\"water evaporates\"), not as a story about one day | | |\n| **Diagram** | A labelled diagram or picture that adds information the words don't carry, with a caption | | |\n| **Clarity for the audience** | A Year 3 reader could follow it without help — no unexplained jargon, no missing step | | |\n| **Closing statement** | Ends with why it matters, or a line of genuine wonder — not \"and that is how it works, the end\" | | |\n| **Accuracy** | Facts are correct and in the writer's own words; capitals, full stops, commas and known spellings checked | | |\n| **Process** | Can name our four writing stages, explain which one they're in and why, and say how this term's version differed from Term 1's | | |\n\n**Overall:** ☐ Working towards ☐ Meeting Phase 2 expectations ☐ Exceeding\n\n#### The Helping Circle\n\n- The writer reads their explanation aloud — no introduction, no apology. Listeners do not interrupt.\n- The writer asks their own specific question (written in Week 7 Friday).\n- Two stars — each listener names one specific thing that made it clear, using criteria language.\n- One wish — each listener names one place where they got lost, confused, or wanted more.\n- The writer says thank you and writes it down. No defending, no explaining — especially not \"oh, what I MEANT was…\", because that's exactly the sentence that proves the writing isn't clear yet.\n\n**Stars — listener prompts**\n\n- The step I understood best was…\n- The word you explained really well was…\n- Your diagram helped me understand…\n- I could picture it when you said…\n\n**Wishes — listener prompts**\n\n- I got lost at the part where…\n- You used the word ____ and I don't know what it means.\n- I think there's a step missing between ____ and ____ .\n- Could your diagram show ____ as well?\n- I still don't know WHY ____ happens.\n\n*We talk about the writing, never the writer. \"I got lost\" is always allowed and is never rude — it is the most useful sentence in this room. Every wish must be something a person could actually do tomorrow.*\n\n#### Differentiation\n\n\n| If a student… | Then… |\n|---|---|\n| Can't choose a topic | Offer the borrow-a-question list from the Week 1 wonder bank. A borrowed question is a scaffold, not cheating — the explaining is still theirs. |\n| Copies from the source | This is a comprehension problem, not a cheating problem. Cover the source and ask them to tell you the fact out loud. Write down what they say. That sentence is their note. |\n| Chooses something far too big (\"space\") | Narrow it with the buddy test: \"What's ONE question about space a Year 3 would ask?\" Big topics become good writing only when they shrink. |\n| Finishes early every week | A second explanation on a related question, or become the class's \"clarity checker\" — the student who reads other people's drafts as if they were eight. |\n| Struggles with transcription | Reduce the volume, never the thinking. A clear 150-word explanation with a strong diagram beats a laboured 400. The diagram carries real content here — let it. |\n| Can't explain it without their paper | That's the diagnosis, not the problem — they don't understand it yet. Send them back to FILL for one lesson: watch the clip again, ask the question again, then re-tell it. |\n| Can't explain the process | Feast analogy, then the directions analogy, then the hand actions. Then have them teach it to their Year 3 buddy — teaching it is the fastest way to learn it. |\n\n#### Ten-week overview\n\n\n| Week | Focus | Stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | The Wonder Bank | Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Week 2** | Finding Out | Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Week 3** | Getting It In Order | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Week 4** | Opening and First Steps | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Week 5** | Cause and Effect | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Week 6** | Diagrams and Endings | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Week 7** | Reading It As a Stranger | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Week 8** | The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Week 9** | Recraft, Edit, Best Copy | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Week 10** | Publish & Present | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n\n*The full ACS unit-planning document (Word and Markdown) downloads from the Writing Focus app toolbar and from the Writing Hub landing page.*\n"},"Week 1: The Wonder Bank":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 1 — The Wonder Bank\n\n> **Essential question:** What makes a question worth explaining?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Story or Explanation? · Tue: Build Together: How an Explanation Is Built · Wed: Stretch: Questions Worth Answering · Thu: Write: The Explain-a-thon · Fri: Share & Reflect: Meet the Audience |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> What happens around you every single day that you cannot actually explain?\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What did you see on the way to school that you've never thought about?\n- What in your house works, but you don't know how?\n- What does your body do without asking you?\n- Which of your questions starts with HOW or WHY — not WHAT?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nsystem · process · cause · effect · energy · pressure · cycle · function · structure · reaction\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I've always wondered how…\n- Nobody has ever explained why…\n- It happens every day, but…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Pick one: How does a rainbow happen? Why do we get goosebumps? How does a fridge stay cold? Why does bread rise? How do birds know where to fly?\n- **Picture this:** A photo of ordinary condensation running down a window.\n- **Talk it out first:** Ask your partner three questions about their body they can't answer. One of them is your topic.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Tier 2 vocabulary: words for movement & action\n- **Tuesday** — Nouns: collective & abstract\n- **Wednesday** — Simple sentences: action-verb focus\n- **Thursday** — Proofreading: capitals for proper nouns\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n#### Teacher note\n\nMentor texts this term: two or three short explanation texts, revisited all term — a School Journal explanation article, a well-written encyclopaedia entry, or a \"how it works\" page. Keep one of them beside the strong exemplar in the Teacher tab. You'll also need one narrative text about a similar topic, for Monday's contrast.\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Story or Explanation?":{"content":"### Launch: Story or Explanation?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning what makes an explanation different from a story, and why the difference matters.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — The Wonder Bank  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Tier 2 vocabulary: words for movement & action |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform, and persuade.\" Writing to inform (Phase 2): students write to inform by introducing a topic and grouping information, drawing on their own and acquired knowledge. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nMovement and action words this morning — explanations are full of them, because explanations describe things HAPPENING: rises, spreads, bends, escapes.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Same four stages as last term — but what will we be filling up with this time? (Facts, not memories.)*\n\n#### I Do — Two Texts, One Topic (6 min)\n\nI'm going to read you two short pieces about exactly the same thing. Your job is to work out how they're different — and it's not the topic.\n\n> A: \"The morning the frost came, I found my bike frozen to the fence and my breath hanging in the air like smoke.\"\nB: \"Frost forms when the temperature of a surface drops below freezing point and water vapour in the air turns directly into ice crystals.\"\n\nText A wants me to FEEL the morning. Text B wants me to UNDERSTAND the frost. Look at the tense: A is \"the morning the frost came\" — one day, in the past. B is \"frost forms\" — every time, always. That's the biggest signal of all. A story happened once. An explanation is true every time.\n\n**Tip:** If you can put \"one day\" in front of your sentence, you've slipped into a story.\n\n#### We Do — Build the Difference Chart (7 min)\n\nTwo columns on the board: STORY and EXPLANATION. Call out the differences you can hear, and I'll scribe. This chart stays up all term.\n\n- Story: one time, in the past. Explanation: every time, present tense.\n- Story: I, me, my. Explanation: it, they, the water, the bird.\n- Story: wants you to feel. Explanation: wants you to understand.\n- Story: surprises you. Explanation: never surprises you — it goes in order.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which one is harder to write, and why? (Most classes say explanation — because you have to actually know the thing.)\n\n#### You Do — Wonder Bank, page one (8 min)\n\nNew section at the back of your jotter: THE WONDER BANK. Ten lines. Questions only — no answers, and no questions you already know the answer to.\n\n- Write 5 questions that start with HOW.\n- Write 5 questions that start with WHY.\n- Star the one you'd most like to know the answer to.\n- ✦ Bonus: one question about something in this room.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read one wonder question. If anyone in the class knows the answer, the question comes off that student's list — it has to be something you genuinely don't know.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text forms and genre* — **Purpose of texts that inform**: NZC Phase 2 Reading: \"identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform, and persuade.\" Students must be able to hear the difference between a text that entertains and one that informs before they can write one.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Writing to inform**: NZC Phase 2: students \"draw on their own experiences and knowledge, as well as acquired knowledge, to write texts to inform\" — a shift from Term 1, where the material came only from memory.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: How an Explanation Is Built":{"content":"### Build Together: How an Explanation Is Built\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the shape of an explanation text: definition, sequence, and why it matters.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — The Wonder Bank  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Nouns: collective & abstract |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements.\" Writing to inform: introduce a topic, group information into paragraphs, and finish with a concluding statement. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCollective and abstract nouns this morning — explanations are full of abstract nouns: pressure, energy, evaporation. They name things you can't point at.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Why are we reading other people's explanations before writing our own? (To steal how they're built.)*\n\n#### I Do — Take a Text Apart (6 min)\n\nOur mentor explanation, on the visualiser. I'm going to label its parts as I read, and think aloud about why each part is there.\n\n> ¶1 DEFINITION — what it is, in one sentence.\n¶2–4 THE SEQUENCE — what happens, in order, with a reason attached to each step.\n¶5 WHY IT MATTERS — the closing thought.\nPlus: a diagram with labels, and a caption.\n\nNotice the first sentence does one job only: it tells me exactly what is being explained. It doesn't hook me, it doesn't surprise me. In a story that would be a weak opening; here it's a strong one, because a reader who doesn't know what you're explaining is lost from sentence two. Different purpose, different rules.\n\n**Tip:** Every paragraph in an explanation answers one question. If you can't say which question, the paragraph isn't finished.\n\n#### We Do — Label a Second Text Together (8 min)\n\nA different explanation text. You tell me where each part starts and ends, and how you know.\n\n- Where does the definition stop and the sequence start?\n- How many steps are in the sequence? Could any be swapped around?\n- What job is the diagram doing that the words don't?\n- How does it end — and does that ending do a job, or just stop?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If we deleted the diagram, what would the reader lose?\n\n#### You Do — Label Your Own Copy (7 min)\n\nYour own copy of the mentor text. Mark it up like a writer, not like a reader.\n\n- Bracket and label: DEFINITION · SEQUENCE · WHY IT MATTERS.\n- Circle every connective that links two steps (because, so, this means, as a result).\n- Underline one sentence you could borrow the SHAPE of for your own writing.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which part of that text will be the hardest for you to write? Say it out loud — that's where I'll help you first.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Structure of information texts**: NZC Phase 2: \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements\" — headings, diagrams, captions and paragraph order are all structural signals.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Topic, paragraphs, conclusion**: NZC Phase 2: \"introduce a topic, and group information together using simple paragraphs… have a concluding statement.\"\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Questions Worth Answering":{"content":"### Stretch: Questions Worth Answering\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a thin question from a question worth explaining.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — The Wonder Bank  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Simple sentences: action-verb focus |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals.\" Key competency Thinking: asking curious or critical questions, and problem-finding rather than answer-seeking. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSimple sentences with strong action verbs this morning. In explanations the verb does almost all the work: heat RISES, pressure DROPS, water FREEZES.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\n> **⚠ Teach the Process Spotlight below FIRST today.** Teach the spotlight FIRST today. The wall strip is not up yet and the routine cannot be run until the class has tried to recall the stages from memory — running it first would hand them the answer. Normal order resumes tomorrow.\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. What did a writer DO in this stage last term, and what will you do this term?*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Cold recall — what survived the holidays (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip NOT yet on the wall. Keep the cards face down on your desk. Say nothing about them until step 3.\n\n1. No warning, no warm-up. Just: \"Last term we learned four stages of the writing process. Nothing on the wall, no notes. Write down as many as you can, in order, in your jotter. Ninety seconds.\" Everyone writes.\n2. Then: \"Hands up if you got four. Three. Two.\" Take the count without commenting on it. A class that gets 60% after a break is completely normal — say that out loud so nobody feels caught out.\n3. Now put the cards up, one at a time, and have the class do the hand action for each as it goes up. Finish with the chant, twice.\n   > \"Stage one!\" → \"What do I want to say — I FILL!\" … and so on through all four.\n4. Land the term's big idea: \"Same four stages. Completely different writing. This term you're not telling a story — you're explaining how something works — and we'll find out that every stage does a slightly different job.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Resist the urge to reteach it all today. What you want is the retrieval attempt first — the forgetting is what makes the relearning stick. The cards go up AFTER they've tried.\n\n#### I Do — Thin, Fat, and Just Right (5 min)\n\nThree questions on the board. Watch me judge each one, out loud, and say why.\n\n> THIN: \"What colour is a rainbow?\" — answered in four words.\nTOO FAT: \"How does weather work?\" — that's a book, not a page.\nJUST RIGHT: \"Why does a rainbow only appear when the sun is behind you?\"\n\nThe thin one has no sequence — nothing happens in order, so there's nothing to explain. The fat one has fifty sequences. The good one has one process with about four or five steps, and I can feel the shape of the writing already: first this, then this, which means this. That's what I'm listening for.\n\n**Tip:** A good question needs an answer with STEPS. If your answer has no steps, choose a different question.\n\n#### We Do — Sort the Wonder Bank (7 min)\n\nI'll read out questions from the class's wonder banks (no names). You call out: thin, too fat, or just right — and then fix the thin and fat ones.\n\n- \"What is lightning?\" — thin or just right? How would you fix it?\n- \"How does the internet work?\" — too fat. Narrow it to one part.\n- \"Why do we yawn?\" — is there a sequence in the answer?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How could you turn a thin question into a good one? (Usually: put HOW or WHY in front of it.)\n\n#### You Do — Audit and Grow Your Bank (9 min)\n\nBack to your wonder bank with the three labels.\n\n- Mark each of your questions: THIN · TOO FAT · JUST RIGHT.\n- Rewrite two thin questions so they need a sequence to answer.\n- Narrow one fat question down to one part of the process.\n- Add 3 new questions now that you know what a good one looks like.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many JUST RIGHT questions do you have now? You only need one — but you need it to be a good one.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Curious questioning**: \"Asking curious or critical questions / problem finding (not just looking for answers)\" — an explicit Phase 2 key-competency indicator, and the whole engine of this unit.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Purpose**: NZC Phase 2: \"identify the audience and purpose for a text.\" A question that can be answered in one sentence has no purpose for a 300-word explanation.\n"},"Thursday: Write: The Explain-a-thon":{"content":"### Write: The Explain-a-thon\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that explaining something out loud shows us exactly which bits we don't understand yet.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — The Wonder Bank  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Proofreading: capitals for proper nouns |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): \"give descriptions… with specific details to actively engage listeners\" and \"use precise vocabulary.\" Oral rehearsal exposes gaps in understanding before drafting begins. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCapitals for proper nouns — you'll need them the moment you name a real place, a scientist or a species.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. If you find a hole in your explanation today, what does that tell you? (That the well isn't full yet — go and find out.)*\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Fall Over (5 min)\n\nI'm going to explain something I only HALF understand, out loud, without notes. Count the number of times I get vague.\n\n> \"So a microwave heats your food because it sends out… waves, sort of… and the waves make the water in the food… move? And moving makes heat. Somehow.\"\n\nThree \"sort ofs\" and a \"somehow\". Every one of those is a hole in what I know — and if I'd started writing instead of talking, I'd have papered over them with big words and hoped nobody noticed. Talking is the fastest lie detector a writer has.\n\n**Tip:** \"Sort of\", \"kind of\", \"somehow\" and \"basically\" are the four words that mark a hole.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Listening Job (5 min)\n\nSet the listener's role before we start, because it's the listener who does the useful work here.\n\n- Listen for \"sort of\", \"somehow\", \"kind of\", \"basically\".\n- Listen for a step that got skipped: \"wait — how did it get from there to there?\"\n- Ask ONE question at the end: the one you'd genuinely still like answered.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is \"I don't get it\" a helpful thing to say, not a rude one?\n\n#### You Do — Explain-a-thon (11 min)\n\nIn pairs. Explain your starred wonder question as far as you can — even if you get 20 seconds in and stop. Then swap.\n\n- Explain your topic for up to 60 seconds. Don't pretend.\n- Listener: count the vague words and ask your one question.\n- Both: write the holes in your jotter as questions (\"How exactly does the light get out of the drop?\").\n- Those questions are your research list for next week.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone should now have between two and five holes. That's not bad news — that's your job list.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Explaining with detail**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions, recounts and narrative retellings with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — applied here to explaining a process aloud.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Knowing what you don't know**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations.\" Discovering the hole in your own understanding is the point of this lesson, not a failure in it.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Meet the Audience":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Meet the Audience\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning who we are writing for this term, and what they will need from us.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — The Wonder Bank  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Immersion & questions |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Composition — audience, purpose and task (Phase 2): \"identify the audience and purpose for a text\" and \"understand that different audiences may have a very different response to the same text.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups gave us action verbs, abstract nouns and clean simple sentences — the three things explanation writing leans on hardest.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. We haven't written a word yet. Is that a problem? (No — we're still filling.)*\n\n#### I Do — Announce the Audience (4 min)\n\nThis term your writing is going to a real person, and I'm telling you now so that everything you write is aimed at them.\n\n> \"Every one of you will write an explanation for a Year 3 buddy. In Week 10 you'll read it to them, and we'll find out whether they understood it.\"\n\nWatch what that does to your writing straight away. You can't write \"the electromagnetic radiation is absorbed by the dipolar water molecules\" for an eight-year-old. But you also can't write \"it gets hot because of magic\". You have to actually understand it well enough to say it simply — and that is much harder, and much more useful, than sounding clever.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't explain it to an eight-year-old, you don't understand it yet.\n\n#### We Do — What Does a Year 3 Reader Need? (6 min)\n\nBuild the audience chart together. Think about a real eight-year-old you know.\n\n- Short sentences — but not baby language. They hate being talked down to.\n- Every hard word explained the first time it's used.\n- A picture that shows what the words can't.\n- No steps skipped — they can't fill in the gaps yet.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What's the difference between making it SIMPLE and making it BABYISH?\n\n#### You Do — The Buddy Test on Someone Else's Writing (11 min)\n\nRead the weak exemplar (Teacher tab) as if you were eight years old. Mark it up.\n\n- Circle every word an eight-year-old wouldn't know.\n- Put a ? at every place you'd have said \"wait, I don't get it\".\n- Write one sentence: the biggest single problem with this piece.\n- Then: write your own audience card — \"I am writing for ____ , who is ____ years old.\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week we find out things. Bring your question — and bring your holes.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Identifying the audience**: NZC Phase 2: \"identify the audience and purpose for a text… plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice.\"\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Respect for the reader**: Considering the impact of your words on others — here, the very concrete impact of using a word an eight-year-old doesn't know.\n"}}},"Week 2: Finding Out":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 2 — Finding Out\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I find out something properly, and put it in my own words?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Where Facts Come From · Tue: Build Together: Notes in Key Words · Wed: Stretch: Cover It and Say It · Thu: Write: Research Time · Fri: Share & Reflect: Teach It in Sixty Seconds |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Fill your fact file. Find out what you need to know — and write it down in words you'd actually say.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What are the STEPS? Can I list them in order?\n- What causes each step to happen?\n- Which words do the experts use — and what do they mean in kid language?\n- What would a Year 3 ask me that I still can't answer?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nsource · evidence · reliable · define · process · stage · cause · result · therefore · according to\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- According to ____ ,…\n- The first thing that happens is…\n- This happens because…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Use the class fact-file frame: WHAT IT IS · THE STEPS IN ORDER · WHY EACH STEP HAPPENS · THE PROPER WORDS · ONE SURPRISING FACT.\n- **Picture this:** A page of handwritten notes with arrows and crossings-out — real research is messy.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read one paragraph, close the book, and tell your partner what it said. Write down what you told them.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Commas after fronted adverbials\n- **Tuesday** — Future tense: will & going to\n- **Wednesday** — Compound sentences: new contexts & conjunctions\n- **Thursday** — Editing: fixing comma splices\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Where Facts Come From":{"content":"### Launch: Where Facts Come From\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to find information and to judge whether a source can be trusted.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Finding Out  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Commas after fronted adverbials |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" Reading: identify the purpose of texts that inform and locate key details. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCommas after fronted adverbials — \"When the temperature drops, …\" That's the shape of half the sentences you'll write this term.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Last term the well filled with memories. What fills it this term? (Facts — and I have to know where each one came from.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — what FILL means in a new genre (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 1. Term 1 narrative chart still visible.\n\n1. Draw the well on the board again. \"Same picture as last term. What went in the bucket then?\" (Memories, noticings, senses, moments.)\n2. Now draw a second bucket beside it. \"What goes in this term?\" Take answers until someone says facts. Write: FACTS · SOURCES · THE PROPER WORDS · WHAT I DON'T KNOW YET.\n3. Ask the harder question: \"What's the biggest difference between filling with memories and filling with facts?\" Push until someone gets near: you can't be wrong about a memory, but you can absolutely be wrong about a fact. Which means checking.\n   > \"In Term 1 the well was already inside you. This term you have to go out and get the water — and make sure it's clean.\"\n4. Finish with the Proverbs line: \"It is the glory of God to conceal a matter; to search out a matter is the glory of kings.\" Researching properly is not the boring bit before writing. It IS the work.\n\n**Watch for:** If students say \"it's the same\", that's fine — press for what's different rather than correcting them. The stage IS the same; the material isn't. Both halves matter.\n\n#### I Do — Three Sources, One Question (6 min)\n\nSame question, three places to look. Watch me decide which I'd trust, and say why out loud.\n\n> 1. A science page written for kids by a museum.\n2. A video by someone with a lot of followers and no name.\n3. A friend who \"definitely heard it somewhere\".\n\nI trust the museum most — not because it's boring, but because someone put their name and their organisation on it. The video might be right, but I can't check. And the friend is how most wrong facts travel. Here's my rule: if I can't find it in two places, I don't write it down as true. Proverbs says it's the glory of kings to search a matter out — searching means checking, not just finding.\n\n**Tip:** Two sources agree = write it down. Only one = write it down with a question mark.\n\n#### We Do — Build the Fact File Frame (7 min)\n\nFive boxes, agreed together, and everybody copies this frame into their jotter. It's the same frame all term.\n\n- WHAT IT IS — one sentence definition.\n- THE STEPS, IN ORDER — numbered.\n- WHY EACH STEP HAPPENS — the cause behind each one.\n- THE PROPER WORDS — technical word + what it means in kid language.\n- ONE SURPRISING FACT — the bit that makes a reader look up.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which box will be hardest to fill for your question?\n\n#### You Do — Start the Fact File (8 min)\n\nSet up the frame and start with what you already know — before you look anything up.\n\n- Copy the five-box frame into your jotter.\n- Fill in everything you already know, from memory, in pencil.\n- Mark the boxes that are empty — those are today's research targets.\n- Write your question at the top, in its final form.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose 'what it is' box is still empty? That's fine — that's the box research is FOR.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Gathering information**: NZC Phase 2: students draw on \"acquired knowledge\" as well as their own — which means finding it, recording it and being able to say where it came from.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Asking critical questions of a source — who wrote this, do they know, and does another source agree — is a Phase 2 thinking indicator.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Notes in Key Words":{"content":"### Build Together: Notes in Key Words\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to reduce a paragraph of information down to a few key words.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Finding Out  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Future tense: will & going to |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nFuture tense this morning — and here's a warning: explanations almost never use it. \"The water will evaporate\" is usually just \"the water evaporates\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Are notes the same as writing? (No — notes are collecting. The writing starts in FIND.)*\n\n#### I Do — Six Words From a Paragraph (6 min)\n\nI'll read a paragraph of real information, then take notes from it in front of you. Count how many words I write.\n\n> SOURCE: \"When sunlight enters a raindrop it slows down and changes direction. This bending of light is known as refraction, and because each colour of light bends by a different amount, the colours separate.\"\nMY NOTES: light enters drop → slows → bends (= refraction) → each colour bends differently → colours separate\n\nSix or seven words and three arrows, from thirty-eight words of source. And notice: I have not written a single sentence, so when I write this up next month I'll be FORCED to make my own sentence. That's the whole trick. Notes that are already sentences turn into copying without you ever deciding to copy.\n\n**Tip:** Arrows are your best friend. An explanation is mostly arrows.\n\n#### We Do — Reduce It Together (8 min)\n\nA paragraph on the board. You call out the key words; I'll write only what you say — nothing more.\n\n- What is the one thing this paragraph is actually about?\n- Which words could we throw away and still understand it?\n- What's the cause here — what makes it happen?\n- Can we get it under eight words?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** We just deleted 80% of the words. Did we lose any meaning?\n\n#### You Do — Reduce Your First Source (7 min)\n\nYour source, your fact file, key words only. If you write a full sentence, cross out half of it.\n\n- Take notes from one source into your fact file — key words and arrows only.\n- Nothing longer than five words in a row.\n- Note where it came from (the book, the site, the person).\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nPartner check: \"Show your partner your notes. Can they see any full sentences? Cross them down.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Reducing to key words**: NZC Phase 2: \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases\" — the explicit note-making expectation for Years 5–6.\n- *Reading — Summarising* — **Main idea and key details**: NZC Phase 2 Reading: \"identify the central message or main idea in a text and provide the key details in sequence.\"\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Cover It and Say It":{"content":"### Stretch: Cover It and Say It\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to put information into our own words, which proves we understand it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Finding Out  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Compound sentences: new contexts & conjunctions |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): notes are reduced to key words and then transferred into the writer's own sentences. Reading: summarising the main idea and key details in sequence. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCompound sentences with and / but / so — \"so\" is the explanation writer's favourite word, because it carries cause straight into effect.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. If you can't say it in your own words, what does that mean? (I haven't understood it yet — keep filling.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cover It and Say It (5 min)\n\nWatch the three-step routine that stops copying dead. It takes ten seconds and it works every time.\n\n> READ the paragraph → COVER it with your hand → SAY it out loud to yourself → WRITE what you said.\n\nWatch me. [Read a paragraph.] Now I cover it. Now I say it: \"so the light bends, and each colour bends a different amount, so they fan out.\" Now I write that. It's not as clever as the book — and that's exactly right, because it's mine, and my Year 3 buddy will understand mine.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't say it with the book covered, read it again. You're not ready to write it yet.\n\n#### We Do — Spot the Copying (7 min)\n\nThree student-style sentences on the board (I wrote all three). Which one is copied, which is fake-own-words, and which is real?\n\n- \"Refraction is the phenomenon whereby electromagnetic radiation is deviated at the interface between two media.\"\n- \"Refraction is the phenomenon whereby light is deviated when it goes into something else.\"\n- \"Refraction just means the light bends when it goes into the water.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How can you tell number two is fake? (The writer swapped two words and kept the rest — they still don't know what it means.)\n\n#### You Do — Own-Words Pass (9 min)\n\nGo through your fact file and do the cover-and-say routine on every note that still sounds like a book.\n\n- Cover each source, say the fact aloud, rewrite it as you said it.\n- Fill the PROPER WORDS box: technical word + your own kid-language meaning.\n- Star any fact you still can't say without reading it — that one needs more work.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your kid-language definition of your hardest word. If we understand it, it's ready.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Summarising and drawing conclusions* — **Own words**: NZC Phase 2: \"identify the central message or main idea in a text and provide the key details in sequence, explaining how the details support the main idea\" — restating in your own words is the proof of comprehension.\n- *Key competency — Using language, symbols & text* — **Making meaning**: \"Make meaning from two or more representations of the same idea or event\" — reading it, saying it, then writing it.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Research Time":{"content":"### Write: Research Time\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to work through our own research list independently.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Finding Out  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: fixing comma splices |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): planning and note-making sustained independently; setting and working towards specific goals based on reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nComma splices got fixed this morning. Watch for them in your notes — two facts jammed together with a comma is the most common note-taking error there is.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL — the last big research day. What has to be true by Friday? (My fact file is full and it's in my own words.)*\n\n#### I Do — Working the List (3 min)\n\nMy research list from Week 1, with three holes on it. Watch me choose which to chase first.\n\n> Hole 1: how does the light get out of the drop? · Hole 2: why is it an arc? · Hole 3: what's the order of the colours?\n\nI'm doing hole one first, because it's in the middle of my sequence — if I can't explain it, my whole piece has a gap in the middle. Hole three is a detail; if I run out of time I can live without it. Chase the hole that would break your sequence.\n\n**Tip:** Fix the hole in the middle before the hole at the edge.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your First Hole (3 min)\n\nRound the room, fast. Say the one thing you're going to find out today.\n\n- \"Why bread rises when it's warm but not when it's cold.\"\n- \"What's actually inside a battery.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anybody with no holes left? Then your job is the SURPRISING FACT box.\n\n#### You Do — Independent Research (14 min)\n\nSources out. Cover-and-say for everything. I'll conference with anyone who's stuck on a hole.\n\n- Work down your holes list, hardest first.\n- Everything goes into the fact file in key words, in your own words.\n- Fill the SURPRISING FACT box if you haven't yet.\n- Note your sources — you'll need them.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everybody: is your sequence complete now — can you get from the start to the end with no gaps?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Working towards goals**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes\" — here, the holes found in Week 1 become the goals.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Self-direction**: Working through a self-generated list without adult prompting, and knowing when to ask for help.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Teach It in Sixty Seconds":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Teach It in Sixty Seconds\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to test our own understanding by teaching it to someone else.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Finding Out  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Research & note-making |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~22 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engaging listeners with specific detail and precise vocabulary; asking clarifying questions. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week: fronted adverbials, compound sentences, comma splices. All three are about joining two facts properly — which is what an explanation is made of.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FILL. What's the next stage, and what will change? (FIND — I find my reader and the order.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Sixty-Second Teach (3 min)\n\nSame explanation as last Thursday, when I was full of \"sort of\". Listen to the difference a week of research makes.\n\n> [Explain the same process again, this time cleanly and in order.]\n\nNo \"somehow\" this time. And notice I went in ORDER, and I explained each hard word as I used it. That's not because I'm a better talker than last week — it's because I actually know it now. The talking got better because the knowing got better.\n\n**Tip:** Fluency is a symptom of understanding, not a substitute for it.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Listener's Job (4 min)\n\nSame as last week, with one addition.\n\n- Listen for vague words: sort of, somehow, basically.\n- Listen for a missing step.\n- NEW: put your hand up the moment you hear a word you wouldn't have known in Year 3.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is the hand-up rule useful, even though it's annoying?\n\n#### You Do — The Explain-a-thon, Round 2 (12 min)\n\nNew partner. Sixty seconds. No notes in front of you.\n\n- Explain your topic in 60 seconds, in order, without your fact file.\n- Listener: hands up on any Year-3-unfriendly word; one question at the end.\n- Swap.\n- Write in your jotter: the words I'll need to explain, and the step I still stumble on.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week you plan it — and you meet your buddy.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Precise vocabulary**: NZC Phase 2: \"use precise vocabulary to describe\" — the technical words are practised aloud before they are written.\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Clarifying questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions.\" The listener's question is the diagnostic.\n"}}},"Week 3: Getting It In Order":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 3 — Getting It In Order\n\n> **Essential question:** What order does my reader need this in?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Flow Chart · Tue: Build Together: The Definition Sentence · Wed: Stretch: Meet Your Buddy · Thu: Write: Rehearse the Sequence · Fri: Share & Reflect: Spot the Missing Step |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Put your explanation in the order a reader needs — not the order you found it out in.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What has to be true before step one can happen?\n- What is the very first thing that happens?\n- What causes the next step — and the next?\n- If I removed one step, would my reader still get there?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nfirst · next · then · after that · meanwhile · because · so · this means · as a result · finally\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- It begins when…\n- This happens because…\n- As a result,…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Use the four-box frame: WHAT IT IS → WHAT STARTS IT → WHAT HAPPENS (in steps) → WHAT YOU END UP WITH.\n- **Picture this:** A flow chart with arrows drawn on a whiteboard.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner your steps while they write each one on a separate sticky note. Then shuffle them and put them back in order together.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Antonyms & shades of meaning\n- **Tuesday** — Subject–verb agreement: tricky cases\n- **Wednesday** — Expanding sentences using \"when\" and \"because\"\n- **Thursday** — Editing: upgrading bland verbs\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Flow Chart":{"content":"### Launch: The Flow Chart\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sequence an explanation so each step causes the next.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Getting It In Order  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Antonyms & shades of meaning |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"use organisers (e.g., graphic organisers or mnemonics) to organise and plan writing.\" Reading: determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAntonyms and shades of meaning this morning — precision matters: \"warm\" is not \"hot\", and in an explanation that difference can be the whole cause.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Two things to find this term — name them. (My reader, and the ORDER.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — what FIND means in a new genre (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Move the peg onto Stage 2 before the lesson.\n\n1. \"FIND has always meant two things. Last term, which two?\" (The shape of the story, and the reader.) \"This term the two things are the same — but they look completely different.\"\n2. Take them one at a time. THE SHAPE: last term an arc — good, broken, put right, made new. This term, arrows — first, next, because, so. Draw both on the board side by side. \"A story arc has surprise in it. A flow chart has no surprises at all, and that's correct.\"\n3. THE READER: last term it was the school community. This term it's one eight-year-old with a name. \"Which is harder to write for, and why?\" Most classes land on the buddy — because you can't hide behind a big word.\n4. Do the hand action and say: \"FIND still points outwards. Same action, and this term you can see exactly who you're pointing at.\"\n\n**Watch for:** The insight to fish for: an explanation must have NO surprises, whereas a narrative needs them. Same stage, opposite requirement. If a student says that, stop and make the class repeat it.\n\n#### I Do — Arrows, Not a Story Arc (7 min)\n\nLast term we drew an arc. This term we draw arrows. Watch me build my flow chart and say the linking word out loud between every box.\n\n> sun shines on rain → light enters drop → light SLOWS and BENDS → each colour bends differently → colours separate → light reflects off the back → light leaves and bends again → we see an arc\n\nListen to what I say between the boxes: \"which means\", \"because\", \"so\". If I can't say a linking word between two boxes, they're not really next to each other — either I've skipped a step, or I've put them in the wrong order. The arrows are the test.\n\n**Tip:** Say \"because\" or \"so\" out loud between every pair of boxes. If neither fits, something's missing.\n\n#### We Do — Fix a Scrambled Sequence (6 min)\n\nSix steps of a process, out of order on the board. Put them in order — and prove each move with a linking word.\n\n- Which one has to be first? How do you know?\n- Which two are in the wrong order — and what gave it away?\n- Is there a step missing? Where?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is \"I found this out first\" a terrible reason to put something first?\n\n#### You Do — Build Your Flow Chart (7 min)\n\nBoxes and arrows in your jotter. Key words only — no sentences.\n\n- Draw 4–6 boxes for your steps, in order.\n- Write the linking word on each arrow (because / so / this means / which causes).\n- Circle any arrow where you couldn't think of a linking word — that's a gap.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose chart has an arrow with nothing written on it? That's tomorrow's first job.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Using organisers**: NZC Phase 2: \"use organisers (e.g., graphic organisers or mnemonics) to organise and plan writing.\" A flow chart is the organiser that matches this genre — a story arc would not.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Cause and effect thinking**: \"Cause and effect thinking: working forwards or backwards to/from possible causes and consequences\" — the exact thinking an explanation text is built from.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Definition Sentence":{"content":"### Build Together: The Definition Sentence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to open an explanation with one sentence that tells the reader exactly what we're explaining.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Getting It In Order  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Subject–verb agreement: tricky cases |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): \"introduce a topic\" — the opening of an information text names and defines its subject rather than hooking the reader. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSubject–verb agreement with tricky subjects — \"each of the raindrops bends the light\", not \"bend\". Explanations are full of these.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who exactly is reading this? Say your buddy's name out loud.*\n\n#### I Do — Three Openings, One Winner (6 min)\n\nThree ways to open the same explanation. Two of them are wrong for this genre — see if you can tell which before I say.\n\n> A: \"Have you ever wondered about rainbows? I bet you have!\"\nB: \"The morning after the storm, a rainbow appeared over the hill.\"\nC: \"A rainbow is an arc of coloured light that appears in the sky when sunlight passes through rain.\"\n\nA is a question with no information — my buddy still doesn't know what I'm explaining. B is beautiful, and it's a story, which is the wrong genre. C tells you WHAT it is, WHERE it appears and WHEN it happens, in one sentence. Boring? No — useful. In this genre, useful IS good.\n\n**Tip:** The formula: [Thing] is a [category] that [what it does / when it happens].\n\n#### We Do — Write Definition Sentences Together (8 min)\n\nUsing the formula, we'll write definitions for three topics as a class. I'll scribe; you supply the words.\n\n- A volcano is a…\n- Condensation is a…\n- Hibernation is a…\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which category word did we choose for each — and could a different category word be better?\n\n#### You Do — Your Definition Sentence (7 min)\n\nWrite it three times, three ways. Then choose.\n\n- Write your definition sentence using the formula.\n- Write a second version that starts with a different word.\n- Read both to a partner. Ask: \"Do you know exactly what I'm explaining?\"\n- Circle the one you'll use, and copy it onto your flow chart.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room: everyone reads their definition sentence. Anyone whose partner still didn't know the topic comes to me.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Introducing a topic**: NZC Phase 2: \"introduce a topic, and group information together using simple paragraphs.\" In explanation, the introduction is a definition, not a narrative hook.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Topic-related vocabulary**: NZC Phase 2: \"use topic-related vocabulary\" — the definition sentence is where the topic's key noun is named for the first time.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Meet Your Buddy":{"content":"### Stretch: Meet Your Buddy\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning what our real reader already knows, and what they will need us to explain.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Getting It In Order  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Expanding sentences using \"when\" and \"because\" |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): \"plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice\"; \"understand that different audiences may have a very different response to the same text.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nExpanding sentences with \"when\" and \"because\" — the two words your buddy will use most when they ask you questions.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Why does meeting the reader change the writing? (Because now I know what they don't know.)*\n\n#### I Do — Interview the Reader (5 min)\n\nIf a Year 3 class is joining us, here's the interview. If not, we run it with a partner playing an eight-year-old.\n\n> Three questions to ask your buddy: 1. \"What do you already know about ____ ?\" 2. \"What would you want to know about it?\" 3. [After explaining in 30 seconds] \"Which bit didn't make sense?\"\n\nQuestion three is the important one, and it's the one everybody skips because it's uncomfortable. You're not asking them to be nice. You're asking them to show you the hole. Write down their exact words — an eight-year-old's confusion is more useful than an adult's advice.\n\n**Tip:** Write down what they SAY, not what you wish they'd said.\n\n#### We Do — Set Up the Interview (6 min)\n\nAgree the protocol before we start, and practise question three on me.\n\n- Ask what they already know. Don't correct them.\n- Explain your topic in 30 seconds.\n- Ask: \"Which bit didn't make sense?\" — and wait. Count to five.\n- Write their exact words down.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What will you do if they say \"it all made sense\"? (Ask: \"what's one word I said that you weren't sure about?\")\n\n#### You Do — Interview and Audience Card (10 min)\n\nRun the interview, then fill in your audience card while it's fresh.\n\n- Interview your buddy (or your partner in role) using the three questions.\n- Audience card: \"I am writing for ____ , aged ____ . They already know ____ . They will need me to explain ____ .\"\n- List the words you used that they didn't know.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read out one word your buddy didn't know. Those words are now on your must-explain list.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Writing for a real audience**: NZC Phase 2: planning and writing with a specific audience in mind, adjusting register and word choice. A real, named audience makes this concrete rather than theoretical.\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Asking clarifying questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions\" — the interview with the buddy is a structured version of exactly this.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Rehearse the Sequence":{"content":"### Write: Rehearse the Sequence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to say our whole explanation aloud from our flow chart, and to fix what we hear.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Getting It In Order  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: upgrading bland verbs |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): communicate in complete sentences with connectives, varying structure for effect. Writing processes: transfer ideas from planning organisers into sentences. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nBland verbs got upgraded this morning — in an explanation, precise verbs ARE the content: rises, escapes, expands, dissolves.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Tomorrow ends the planning. What do you need to be able to do by then? (Say the whole thing in order, without a gap.)*\n\n#### I Do — Rehearse and Catch Myself (4 min)\n\nI'll say my whole explanation from my flow chart. Watch me catch a problem while I'm talking.\n\n> [Explain from the boxes, out loud, using the linking words.]\n\nThere — I said \"and then\" twice in a row. \"And then\" isn't a cause, it's just a queue. Boxes three and four aren't linked by anything, which means I don't actually know why one leads to the other. That's a research hole I thought I'd filled.\n\n**Tip:** \"And then\" between two boxes is a warning light. Explanations run on \"because\" and \"so\".\n\n#### We Do — The Listener Holds the Chart (4 min)\n\nSet up the roles. The listener does the checking, not the teller.\n\n- Teller: explain from the chart, using a linking word between every step.\n- Listener: hold the chart and tick each box as it arrives.\n- Listener: say \"STOP\" the moment you hear \"and then\".\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does the listener need the chart in their hand?\n\n#### You Do — Oral Rehearsal in Pairs (13 min)\n\nBoth directions, then fix your chart while it's fresh.\n\n- Explain your whole process from the chart (about 2 minutes).\n- Swap.\n- 3 minutes: fix any box, gap or \"and then\" your rehearsal exposed.\n- Write the linking words you'll need onto the chart itself.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if the telling changed your chart.\" (Most hands.) \"Same as last term — the ears catch what the eyes don't.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Connectives**: NZC Phase 2: \"communicate in complete sentences, incorporating a range of conjunctions and connectives\" — heard first, before it's written.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Plan into sentences**: NZC Phase 2: \"transfer ideas from planning organisers or mnemonics into sentences.\" Oral rehearsal is the halfway step that makes the transfer work.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Spot the Missing Step":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Spot the Missing Step\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to find the gaps in someone else's plan before they write it up.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Getting It In Order  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Sequencing and audience |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revising for clarity for the intended audience, using peer feedback. Feedback at planning stage is the cheapest revision there is. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups: shades of meaning, agreement, \"when\" and \"because\", stronger verbs. All four are about being exact — which is what an explanation is.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What have we found this week? (The order, and the reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Three Gallery Questions (3 min)\n\nOnly three questions allowed at a plan gallery. Here they are.\n\n> 1. Could I follow it? · 2. Is there a step missing? · 3. Which word would my Year 3 buddy not know?\n\nNotice what's not there: \"is it interesting?\" Wrong question for this genre. \"Could I follow it\" and \"is a step missing\" are the only two that matter, and question three is our audience doing the marking for us.\n\n**Tip:** If you can follow a plan you know nothing about, it's a good plan.\n\n#### We Do — Model on One Chart (5 min)\n\nOne flow chart on the visualiser. We run the three questions across it, out loud.\n\n- \"I could follow it until box three, where I didn't know what made it happen.\"\n- \"I think there's a step between four and five.\"\n- \"'Molecule' — my buddy wouldn't know that word.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those three comments will change the writing most?\n\n#### You Do — Gallery Walk (12 min)\n\nCharts out on desks with a sticky note beside each. Visit three charts only.\n\n- Leave one sticky note per chart: one thing that made sense, and one gap or hard word.\n- Back at your desk: read your notes and mark the box you'll fix first.\n- Get your fact file, chart and audience card ready — Monday you draft.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"On Monday you start writing. You've spent three weeks getting ready, and it's going to make the writing fast.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" and revise \"using audience and peer feedback.\"\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Systems thinking**: \"Systems thinking: uncovering hidden connections between events and ideas\" — a missing step in a sequence is a hidden connection nobody has noticed yet.\n"}}},"Week 4: Opening and First Steps":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 4 — Opening and First Steps\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I start an explanation so my reader knows exactly where they are?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Title, Definition, and the Set-Up · Tue: Build Together: Non-Fiction Paragraphs · Wed: Stretch: The \"Every Time\" Voice · Thu: Write: Drafting the Sequence · Fri: Share & Reflect: Does It Follow? |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Write the opening and the first steps. Definition first, then what starts the process off.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is this thing, in one sentence?\n- What does my reader need to know before step one makes sense?\n- What is the very first thing that happens?\n- What makes it happen?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nconsists of · occurs · begins · contains · produces · releases · absorbs · travels · forms · requires\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- A ____ is a ____ that…\n- The process begins when…\n- Before this can happen,…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Use the sentence frame: \"A ____ is a ____ that ____ . It happens when ____ .\"\n- **Picture this:** The first paragraph of your mentor text, with the definition underlined.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your first two sentences to a partner. If they ask \"what IS that?\", your definition isn't finished.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Apostrophes for contraction\n- **Tuesday** — Relative pronouns: who, which, that\n- **Wednesday** — Topic sentences\n- **Thursday** — Editing: removing unnecessary apostrophes\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Title, Definition, and the Set-Up":{"content":"### Launch: Title, Definition, and the Set-Up\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write an opening paragraph that defines the topic and sets the reader up.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and First Steps  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Apostrophes for contraction |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): introduce a topic; use topic-related vocabulary; group information into paragraphs. Layout conventions signal each new paragraph. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nApostrophes for contraction this morning — and a genre note: formal explanations usually write \"do not\" rather than \"don't\". Yours can go either way, but be consistent.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. We're drafting now. Which stage does drafting live in? (Still FIND — first ideas and fluency.)*\n\n#### I Do — Build the Opening Paragraph (6 min)\n\nWatch me turn my definition sentence into a whole opening paragraph — and watch how little I add.\n\n> \"A rainbow is an arc of coloured light that appears in the sky when sunlight passes through rain. Sunlight looks white, but it is not. It is actually made of many colours mixed together, and we only see them separately when the light is bent.\"\n\nSentence one: what it is. Sentences two and three: the one fact my reader MUST have before step one makes any sense. That's all an opening does — name it, then hand the reader the key. I'm not hooking anybody. I'm unlocking the door.\n\n**Tip:** Ask: what would my buddy need to know before my first step makes sense? That, and nothing else, goes in the opening.\n\n#### We Do — Title It (7 min)\n\nTitles for explanation texts do one job: they ask or name the question. Let's write and judge some.\n\n- \"Rainbows\" — names the topic, but not the question.\n- \"How Does a Rainbow Happen?\" — names the question. Better.\n- \"The Amazing Magical Rainbow Mystery!!\" — what's wrong with this one?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which title tells a Year 3 exactly what they're about to find out?\n\n#### You Do — Title and Opening Paragraph (8 min)\n\nTitle, definition sentence, and the background your reader needs. Nothing more.\n\n- Write your title as a question.\n- Write your definition sentence.\n- Add 1–2 sentences of the background your reader must have.\n- Read it to a partner and ask: \"Do you know what I'm about to explain?\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read their opening. Class answers only: \"Do we know what's being explained?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Introducing a topic**: NZC Phase 2: \"introduce a topic, and group information together using simple paragraphs\" — the opening paragraph establishes the subject and any background the reader needs.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Topic vocabulary**: NZC Phase 2: \"use topic-related vocabulary\" — introduced and defined in the opening, not saved for later.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Non-Fiction Paragraphs":{"content":"### Build Together: Non-Fiction Paragraphs\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build a paragraph with a topic sentence, detail sentences and a linking end.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and First Steps  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Relative pronouns: who, which, that |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): \"write non-fiction paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences, and a concluding sentence.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRelative pronouns — who, which, that. \"The light, WHICH has slowed down, bends.\" That's how you slip a definition into a sentence without stopping.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What's the question that belongs to this stage? (\"Who do I want to say it to?\")*\n\n#### I Do — One Step, One Paragraph (6 min)\n\nOne box from my flow chart becomes one paragraph. Watch the three jobs each paragraph does.\n\n> \"When sunlight hits a raindrop, it slows down and bends. [TOPIC] This bending is called refraction. Because each colour of light bends by a slightly different amount, the colours begin to spread out inside the drop. [DETAIL] By the time the light reaches the back of the drop, the colours are no longer travelling together. [LINK TO NEXT]\"\n\nTopic sentence tells you what this paragraph is about. Detail sentences explain HOW and WHY. And the last sentence hands you over to the next paragraph — it's a bridge, not a full stop. One box, one paragraph, three jobs.\n\n**Tip:** If a paragraph is doing two steps, split it. One box, one paragraph.\n\n#### We Do — Build a Paragraph Together (8 min)\n\nTake one box from a volunteer's flow chart. We write the paragraph together — I scribe, you supply.\n\n- Give me the topic sentence: what is this paragraph about?\n- Two detail sentences: how does it happen, and why?\n- One link sentence: how do we hand over to the next step?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the three sentences was hardest to write — and why is it usually the \"why\"?\n\n#### You Do — Draft Two Paragraphs (7 min)\n\nBoxes one and two of your flow chart. One box each, three jobs each.\n\n- Draft the paragraph for box 1.\n- Draft the paragraph for box 2.\n- Underline your topic sentence in each.\n- Check: does each paragraph end by handing over to the next?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read just your topic sentences aloud, one after the other. Do they tell the story of your process on their own?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Non-fiction paragraph structure**: NZC Phase 2: \"write non-fiction paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences, and a concluding sentence\" — the explicit paragraph expectation for Years 5–6 information writing.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Linking words and phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"begin to use linking words and phrases (also, another, together with)\" — plus the cause connectives this genre needs.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The \"Every Time\" Voice":{"content":"### Stretch: The \"Every Time\" Voice\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write in the present tense with general nouns, so our explanation is true every time.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and First Steps  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Topic sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): information texts are written as general truths. Sentence structures: subject–verb agreement and consistent tense across a multi-paragraph text. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTopic sentences this morning — same skill, and today you'll check that every paragraph has one.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages from memory, no strip, no notes.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall — quick-fire (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Cover the wall strip before the students arrive. No warning.\n\n1. \"No notes, no wall. Four stages, in order, with actions. Go.\" Twenty seconds, whole class.\n2. \"One person — explain the whole process in under twenty seconds. I'm timing.\" Pick someone who isn't your usual volunteer; the class counts down.\n3. Uncover the strip. One line: \"Four weeks in. Look how much you're carrying without even trying.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Playful and fast — a game, not a test. If a student stalls, give the class the first WORD only and let the student finish it themselves.\n\n#### I Do — Story Voice vs Explanation Voice (5 min)\n\nSame information, two voices. Listen for the tense and the nouns.\n\n> STORY VOICE: \"Yesterday the sun came out and a rainbow appeared over our school. The light went into the raindrops and bent.\"\nEXPLANATION VOICE: \"When the sun shines through falling rain, a rainbow appears. Light enters each raindrop and bends.\"\n\n\"Came out, appeared, went, bent\" — all past, all one day. \"Shines, appears, enters, bends\" — all present, and all every time. And notice \"our school\" became nothing at all, because it doesn't matter where. Explanation writing zooms out from one day to always.\n\n**Tip:** Test every sentence with \"every time\": \"every time, light enters the drop\" ✓. \"Every time, yesterday the sun came out\" ✗.\n\n#### We Do — Convert It (7 min)\n\nStory-voice sentences on the board. Convert each one to explanation voice together.\n\n- \"The bread I made rose because I put it near the heater.\"\n- \"My dog panted after we ran because he was hot.\"\n- \"The ice cube I left out melted really fast.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What happened to the words \"I\", \"my\" and \"we\" in every single one?\n\n#### You Do — The Every-Time Check (9 min)\n\nGo through everything you've drafted so far with one question in your head.\n\n- Read each sentence and ask: is this true EVERY time?\n- Fix any past-tense verbs.\n- Remove any \"I\", \"my\" or \"we\" that turned your explanation into a story.\n- ✦ Bonus: find a place where \"a\" or \"each\" would work better than \"the\".\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found a story sentence hiding in their explanation? Read us the before and after.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Tense consistency**: Phase 2 writers maintain consistent tense across multi-paragraph texts; explanation requires the timeless present (\"water evaporates\"), which is a genuinely new muscle after a term of past-tense narrative.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **General nouns**: Information writing generalises (\"raindrops\", \"a bird\") rather than particularising (\"the raindrop I saw on Tuesday\") — a key marker of the genre.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Drafting the Sequence":{"content":"### Write: Drafting the Sequence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to keep a draft moving by working box by box through our plan.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and First Steps  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: removing unnecessary apostrophes |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): transfer ideas from organisers into sentences; use layout conventions for paragraphs; write with increasing stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nUnnecessary apostrophes got removed this morning. Watch for it: plurals in explanations are everywhere — raindrops, molecules, birds — and none of them need an apostrophe.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If you hit a step you can't explain today, which stage do you go back to? (FILL — go and find out.)*\n\n#### I Do — Reread the Last Paragraph First (3 min)\n\nSame rule as last term, and it matters more here. Watch me before I write anything new.\n\n> [Read your last paragraph aloud, then continue from it.]\n\nI read the last paragraph so I don't repeat myself and so I stay in the same tense. In an explanation, repeating a step is worse than in a story — the reader thinks it's a NEW step and gets lost.\n\n**Tip:** Reread one paragraph. Not the whole thing.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Box (3 min)\n\nFast round the room: which box are you drafting today?\n\n- \"Box three — where the colours separate.\"\n- \"I'm still on box two, the hard one.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone stuck on a box because they don't understand it? You're with me at the back table.\n\n#### You Do — Independent Drafting + Conferences (14 min)\n\nDraft box by box. I'll run two-minute conferences with the students who found gaps on Monday.\n\n- Draft the next boxes of your flow chart, one paragraph each.\n- Reread your previous paragraph before starting each new one.\n- Leave a blank if a technical word won't come — keep moving.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Finish the sentence you're on. Then mark which box you'll start on Monday.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Sustained drafting**: NZC Phase 2: transferring a full plan into connected multi-paragraph prose, using paragraph layout conventions.\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Does It Follow?":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Does It Follow?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to listen to an explanation and tell the writer exactly where we got lost.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and First Steps  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): listening and responding with clarifying questions. Writing processes: giving and receiving peer feedback to improve clarity. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — contractions, relative pronouns, topic sentences — are all in your draft already. Point at one of each.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Are we in SHAPE yet? (No — but if someone got lost today, note it for when we are.)*\n\n#### I Do — \"I Got Lost\" Is a Gift (3 min)\n\nTeach the one sentence that makes this whole genre work.\n\n> Not: \"It was good.\" Not: \"I liked it.\" Just: \"I got lost at the part where…\"\n\n\"I got lost\" is never rude, and the writer must never say \"well, what I MEANT was…\" — because your buddy won't be sitting next to the page to hear what you meant. If a listener gets lost, the writing has a hole. That's information, not an insult.\n\n**Tip:** Writers: when someone says they got lost, say \"thank you\" and mark the spot.\n\n#### We Do — Practise on Me (4 min)\n\nI'll read a paragraph with a step deliberately missing. Find it.\n\n- Where exactly did you get lost?\n- What did you need to know that I didn't tell you?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How did you know there was a step missing rather than just a hard idea?\n\n#### You Do — Trios: Where Did You Get Lost? (13 min)\n\nGroups of three. Read what you've drafted so far. Listeners mark the moment they lose the thread.\n\n- Read your draft so far.\n- Listeners: say \"I got lost at…\" or \"I didn't know the word…\".\n- Writers: say thank you, write it down, don't explain.\n- Rotate until all three have read.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The place people got lost was… so on Monday I'll…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Clarifying questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions\" — \"where did you get lost?\" is the highest-value question in this genre.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Clarity for the audience**: NZC Phase 2: \"make simple revisions to their texts to improve the clarity for the intended audience and purpose.\"\n"}}},"Week 5: Cause and Effect":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 5 — Cause and Effect\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I show my reader WHY each step happens?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Cause Words · Tue: Build Together: Explaining the Hard Words · Wed: Stretch: Sentences That Don't All Sound the Same · Thu: Write: The Hard Middle · Fri: Share & Reflect: Teach It to Your Buddy |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Don't just tell me what happens next. Tell me what MAKES it happen.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What causes this step — what would happen if it were missing?\n- Which connective fits: because, so, this means, as a result?\n- Is there a technical word here I need to explain?\n- Could my buddy explain this step back to me?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nbecause · therefore · as a result · this means that · consequently · causes · allows · prevents · which is why · due to\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- This happens because…\n- As a result,…\n- ____ , which means that…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Take any two steps and force a link: \"____ happens, which means ____ .\" If you can't finish it, that's the fact you still need.\n- **Picture this:** Two dominoes mid-fall.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your steps to a partner using only the word \"because\" between them. No \"and then\" allowed.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Brackets & dashes for extra information\n- **Tuesday** — Comparative & superlative adjectives\n- **Wednesday** — Combining short sentences for variety\n- **Thursday** — Peer editing: punctuation variety\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Cause Words":{"content":"### Launch: The Cause Words\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to link steps with cause words instead of just listing them.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Cause and Effect  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Brackets & dashes for extra information |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): \"begin to use linking words and phrases.\" Sentence structures: expand sentences using adverbial phrases; use commas for transition words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nBrackets and dashes this morning — the fastest way to explain a hard word without stopping: \"refraction (the bending of light)\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Half way through drafting. What's the one rule of this stage? (Keep it moving — the fixing comes in SHAPE.)*\n\n#### I Do — \"And Then\" vs \"Because\" (6 min)\n\nSame three steps, written twice. Listen for what the second version tells you that the first doesn't.\n\n> A: \"The air gets warm. And then it rises. And then it cools down again.\"\nB: \"The air gets warm. Warm air is lighter than cold air, so it rises. As it rises it moves further from the ground, which means it cools down again.\"\n\nVersion A is a list. It tells me the order but not the machinery. Version B answers \"why?\" at every step — and notice I had to KNOW something to write it. That's why \"and then\" is such a useful warning light: it usually means the writer doesn't know the cause yet.\n\n**Tip:** Ban \"and then\" from your draft this week. You'll be forced to find the real link.\n\n#### We Do — Choose the Right Connective (8 min)\n\nSentence pairs on the board. Which connective joins them best — and is more than one possible?\n\n- Warm air is lighter than cold air. / It rises. → because? so? which means?\n- The water reaches 100°C. / It turns into steam. → when? as a result?\n- The seed has no light. / It cannot make food. → so? therefore? which is why?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Does the connective change WHERE the comma goes? (Yes — check each one.)\n\n#### You Do — Hunt Your \"And Thens\" (8 min)\n\nGo through your draft and upgrade every weak link.\n\n- Find and circle every \"and then\" and every \"next\" in your draft.\n- Replace at least three with a real cause connective.\n- If you can't find the cause for one of them — write it on your holes list.\n- Check the comma after any transition word at the start of a sentence.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found an 'and then' that turned out to be a hole in what you know? That's a good find, not a bad one.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Linking words and phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"begin to use linking words and phrases (also, another, together with)\" — extended in this genre to the cause-and-effect connectives that hold an explanation together.\n- *Writing — Punctuation* — **Commas for transition words**: NZC Phase 2: \"use commas correctly… when using transition words, with some support\" — e.g. \"As a result, the air rises.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Explaining the Hard Words":{"content":"### Build Together: Explaining the Hard Words\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to use the proper technical word AND explain it in the same sentence.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Cause and Effect  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Comparative & superlative adjectives |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): \"use topic-related vocabulary.\" Composition: word choice selected for the audience, so a technical term is defined at first use. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nComparatives this morning — explanations use them constantly: warmer air, denser water, the longer the wavelength, the more it bends.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Say your reader's age out loud. Does every word in today's writing pass that test?*\n\n#### I Do — Three Ways to Define on the Move (6 min)\n\nYou do NOT dodge the proper word. You use it and explain it in the same breath. Three ways to do that.\n\n> BRACKETS: \"The light is refracted (bent) as it enters the drop.\"\nDASH: \"The light is refracted — that is, bent — as it enters the drop.\"\nWHICH MEANS: \"The light is refracted, which means it bends as it enters the drop.\"\n\nAll three keep the reader moving. Compare it with the bad version: \"The light is refracted.\" — my buddy stops dead. Or the other bad version: \"The light does a bending thing.\" — now I've hidden the real word from them, and they deserve to know it. Use the word, then hand over the meaning.\n\n**Tip:** Every technical word gets explained ONCE — the first time you use it. After that, use it freely.\n\n#### We Do — Define These Live (8 min)\n\nHard words from around the class. Define each one three ways — brackets, dash, \"which means\".\n\n- evaporate\n- friction\n- hibernate\n- (add two words from your students' own topics)\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the three methods sounds most natural for each word? (It changes word by word.)\n\n#### You Do — Define Your Words (7 min)\n\nEvery technical word on your PROPER WORDS list, defined inside a sentence, on the move.\n\n- List every technical word you've used so far.\n- For each: is it explained the FIRST time it appears?\n- Fix any that aren't — using brackets, a dash, or \"which means\".\n- ✦ Bonus: check you haven't explained the same word twice.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read us one sentence where you used a big word AND explained it. That's exactly the skill.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Topic vocabulary**: NZC Phase 2: \"use topic-related vocabulary\" — Years 5–6 writers are expected to use the subject's real words, not to avoid them.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Word choice for audience**: NZC Phase 2: word choice is selected \"to best communicate the intended meaning\" for the audience — which means defining a technical term the first time it appears.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Sentences That Don't All Sound the Same":{"content":"### Stretch: Sentences That Don't All Sound the Same\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to combine and vary our sentences so an explanation doesn't become a list.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Cause and Effect  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Combining short sentences for variety |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures (Phase 2): \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose\"; \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCombining short sentences this morning — this lesson is that warm-up, applied straight to your own draft.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Two weeks of drafting left. What has to exist by the end of next week? (A complete draft with a diagram.)*\n\n#### I Do — Same Length, Same Shape, Same Boredom (5 min)\n\nListen to this paragraph with your eyes shut and tell me what's wrong with it. It's not the facts.\n\n> BEFORE: \"The sun heats the water. The water evaporates. The vapour rises. The vapour cools. The vapour forms clouds.\"\nAFTER: \"The sun heats the water until it evaporates. As the vapour rises, it moves into colder air. When it cools enough, it forms clouds.\"\n\nFive sentences became three, and the three tell you MORE — because the joins now carry the causes: \"until\", \"as\", \"when\". Combining isn't just about sounding better. In this genre, the join is where the meaning lives.\n\n**Tip:** Three short sentences in a row is a signal. Try joining two of them with a cause word.\n\n#### We Do — Combine These (8 min)\n\nChoppy sentence sets on the board. Combine each set two different ways, and compare.\n\n- \"The bird eats a lot. It is going to migrate. It needs energy.\"\n- \"Yeast is a living thing. It eats sugar. It makes gas. The dough rises.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version puts the CAUSE in the strongest position?\n\n#### You Do — Vary Your Own (9 min)\n\nFind the choppiest paragraph in your draft and rebuild it.\n\n- Find three short sentences in a row and combine two of them with a cause word.\n- Find a paragraph where three sentences start the same way and change one opener.\n- ✦ Bonus: start one sentence with an adverbial phrase (\"Inside the drop, …\").\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read the paragraph you rebuilt. Class: did it get clearer, or just longer?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Combining and varying**: NZC Phase 2: \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase\" and \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact.\"\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Expanding with adverbial phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"expand sentences using adverbial phrases\" — e.g. \"Inside the raindrop, the colours spread out.\"\n"},"Thursday: Write: The Hard Middle":{"content":"### Write: The Hard Middle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to keep going through the most difficult part of our explanation.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Cause and Effect  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Peer editing: punctuation variety |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): sustained drafting from a plan; rereading at paragraph level; increasing handwriting stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPeer punctuation editing this morning — today you're on your own, so read each sentence in your head as you finish it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If today's step is the one you don't fully understand, what's the honest move? (Back to FILL for ten minutes.)*\n\n#### I Do — Write the Hard Bit Badly First (3 min)\n\nThe middle step is always the hardest. Here's what I do instead of stalling.\n\n> \"OK so basically the colours end up at different angles [I DON'T GET THIS YET] and that's why we see them separately.\"\n\nI wrote a bracket note to myself and kept going. I have not solved it — but I haven't lost the whole lesson to it either. Tomorrow I go back and fix that one bracket, and everything around it is already written.\n\n**Tip:** A square bracket note beats a blank page. Keep moving, come back.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Hardest Box (3 min)\n\nRound the room: which box are you dreading, and why?\n\n- \"Box four — I know it happens but I don't know why.\"\n- \"The last one — I don't know how to finish it.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Everyone who said \"I don't know why\" — that's a FILL problem, and I'll come to you first.\n\n#### You Do — Sustained Drafting (15 min)\n\nLongest write of the term so far. I'll take a small group on cause connectives at the back table.\n\n- Draft through your hardest box.\n- Use square brackets for anything you can't yet explain — don't stop.\n- Every step must have a cause word attached to it.\n- Note your word count in the margin.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many square brackets have you got? Those are Monday's jobs, and they're the last research you'll do.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Sustained drafting**: NZC Phase 2: transferring the full plan into connected paragraphs across multiple sessions, maintaining structure and tense.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Persistence**: \"Think before acting\" and self-regulation: using known strategies to get through the hardest section rather than avoiding it.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Teach It to Your Buddy":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Teach It to Your Buddy\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that teaching something to a younger reader shows us exactly how clear our explanation is.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Cause and Effect  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the middle |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): giving descriptions with specific details to engage listeners; using precise vocabulary; responding to questions. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — brackets, comparatives, combining sentences — all show up when you explain something out loud too.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Your buddy has never heard of FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE. Today you teach it to them.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Teach the process to your buddy (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Buddy session already organised for this lesson. If no buddy class, students teach a partner \"in role\" as an eight-year-old.\n\n1. Before the buddies arrive: \"You're going to teach a Year 3 our four stages. Ninety seconds. What will you leave out?\" Take answers — they'll usually say the long explanations, and they're right.\n2. Model it in twenty seconds, badly-simple on purpose.\n   > \"Writers do four things. FILL — you find out stuff. FIND — you work out who you're telling and what order. SHAPE — you read it again and fix it. SHARE — you give it to them. Want to learn the hand actions?\"\n3. Students teach their buddy: the four words, the four actions, and the chant. Then they explain their own topic.\n4. Afterwards, one question back in class: \"What did you have to change to make an eight-year-old understand it?\" That answer is the whole lesson — and it's the same skill their writing needs.\n\n**Watch for:** Teaching it to a younger child is the highest-value retrieval in the whole programme — they cannot recite their way through it, they have to actually understand it. Protect the time.\n\n#### I Do — Explaining to a Younger Listener (3 min)\n\nTwo versions of the same 30 seconds: one aimed at you, one aimed at an eight-year-old.\n\n> TO YOU: \"Refraction occurs at the boundary between two media of different densities.\"\nTO AN EIGHT-YEAR-OLD: \"When light goes from air into water, it slows down — and when it slows down, it bends. That bending has a name: refraction.\"\n\nSame fact, same real word, completely different delivery. I didn't dumb it down — I built a ramp up to it. And notice the second one takes longer. Clear takes more words than clever.\n\n**Tip:** Short sentences, real words, one idea at a time. Never baby language.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Buddy Protocol (4 min)\n\nIf the Year 3s are here, this is the plan. If not, partners play the role.\n\n- Explain your process in about a minute, using your diagram-in-progress if you have one.\n- Ask: \"What's one thing you still want to know?\"\n- Write their exact question down.\n- Then teach them our four writing stages — hand actions and all.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What will you do if they look blank? (Stop. Ask which bit. Go again from there.)\n\n#### You Do — Buddy Teaching (12 min)\n\nTwo jobs: teach your topic, and teach our process.\n\n- Explain your topic to your buddy in about a minute.\n- Ask for their one remaining question and write it down word for word.\n- Teach them FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE with the hand actions. Get them to do the chant with you.\n- Back at your desk: write the question they asked at the top of your draft.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose buddy asked a question you couldn't answer? Good — that's the most useful thing that happened all week.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging a listener**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions… with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — with a genuinely unfamiliar audience, which raises the difficulty considerably.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Adapting to the listener**: \"Show respect for others' ways of being · make space for others to contribute\" — adjusting language in real time for a younger child.\n"}}},"Week 6: Diagrams and Endings":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings\n\n> **Essential question:** What can a picture explain that my words cannot?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Diagrams That Do a Job · Tue: Build Together: Captions and Cross-References · Wed: Stretch: Why Does This Matter? · Thu: Write: Finish the Draft · Fri: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Draw what your words can't say. Then finish with why any of this matters.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which step is hardest to picture from my words alone?\n- What would a labelled picture show that a sentence can't?\n- What does my reader now understand that they didn't before?\n- Why is this worth knowing?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\ndiagram · label · caption · stage · cross-section · arrow · key · sequence · overall · finally\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The diagram below shows…\n- Now that you know this, you can…\n- The next time you see ____ , you will know that…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Draw the single step your buddy got most confused about. Label three parts and add one arrow showing movement.\n- **Picture this:** A cross-section diagram with four labels and a caption.\n- **Talk it out first:** Describe your diagram to a partner without showing it. Whatever you have to say out loud is a label you're missing.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Greek & Latin roots in everyday words\n- **Tuesday** — Adverbial phrases for time & manner\n- **Wednesday** — Who / where / when phrases\n- **Thursday** — Editing: replacing overused words\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Diagrams That Do a Job":{"content":"### Launch: Diagrams That Do a Job\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to draw a diagram that adds information our words cannot carry.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Greek & Latin roots in everyday words |\n| **NZC link** | Reading & writing (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements.\" Information texts use visual language purposefully. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nGreek and Latin roots this morning — thermo (heat), hydro (water), photo (light). Half the technical words in your topic are built from them, and knowing the root helps you explain the word.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Is a diagram part of the writing, or an extra? (Part of it — it's carrying meaning.)*\n\n#### I Do — Decoration vs Diagram (6 min)\n\nTwo pictures for the same explanation. One is doing a job; one is just pretty.\n\n> A: a colourful rainbow over a hill, with no labels.\nB: a single raindrop drawn large, with an arrow for the light going in, labels for \"light bends here\", \"reflects off the back\", \"bends again\", and a caption: \"Inside one raindrop.\"\n\nA is lovely and tells my reader nothing they didn't already know — they've seen a rainbow. B shows the one thing my words struggle with: what happens INSIDE the drop, which nobody can see. That's the test. Draw the bit your reader can't picture from words alone.\n\n**Tip:** Draw the invisible bit. Never draw the bit everyone has already seen.\n\n#### We Do — What Would We Draw? (7 min)\n\nThree topics from around the class. For each: what's the ONE thing worth drawing, and what are its labels?\n\n- If your topic is bread rising — what's the invisible bit? (Inside the dough.)\n- If it's hibernation — what's hard to picture? (What happens inside the body.)\n- Now yours: what can't your reader see?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How many labels is enough? (Three to six. More than that and it's a mess.)\n\n#### You Do — Draft Your Diagram (8 min)\n\nPencil, half a page, rough. This is a first draft of a diagram, not the final art.\n\n- Draw the invisible step, big.\n- Add 3–6 labels naming the parts or the movement.\n- Add at least one arrow showing what moves where.\n- Show a partner without speaking. Ask them what they think it shows.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose partner worked out the diagram with no explanation? That means the labels are doing their job.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Visual elements**: NZC Phase 2: \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements\" — students read diagrams as text, then create their own.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Text features**: Information writing uses labels, captions and diagrams as part of the text, not as decoration added at the end.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Captions and Cross-References":{"content":"### Build Together: Captions and Cross-References\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to connect our diagram to our writing so they work as one text.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Adverbial phrases for time & manner |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): grouping information and using text features; layout conventions that help a reader navigate a text. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAdverbial phrases for time and manner — \"As the light enters…\", \"Slowly, the dough expands.\" That's caption language.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who is the diagram for? (The same eight-year-old — so the labels have to pass the same test.)*\n\n#### I Do — A Caption Is a Sentence With a Job (5 min)\n\nThree captions for the same diagram. Only one of them is worth printing.\n\n> A: \"My diagram.\"\nB: \"A raindrop.\"\nC: \"Inside a single raindrop, light bends, reflects off the back, and bends again as it leaves.\"\n\nA tells the reader nothing. B names it but does nothing. C is a whole sentence that could almost stand alone — and here's the test: if a reader looked ONLY at your diagram and its caption, would they learn something true? With C, yes.\n\n**Tip:** A caption is a full sentence, and it says what is happening — not what the picture is of.\n\n#### We Do — Write and Place (8 min)\n\nWrite a caption together for one student's diagram, then work out where the diagram belongs in their draft.\n\n- What's happening in this picture, in one sentence?\n- Which paragraph does it belong beside?\n- How do we point at it in the writing? (\"The diagram below shows…\" / \"as shown in the diagram\")\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Should a diagram go before or after the paragraph it explains?\n\n#### You Do — Caption and Cross-Reference (8 min)\n\nFinish the job so the picture and the words are one text.\n\n- Write a full-sentence caption for your diagram.\n- Mark on your draft where the diagram will sit.\n- Add one sentence in the writing that points to it.\n- Check every label passes the Year 3 test.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read us your caption. Could someone learn something from just the picture and that sentence?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Captions and references**: Information texts guide the reader between prose and image (\"as the diagram shows\"), a Phase 2 text-feature expectation.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Adverbial phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"expand sentences using adverbial phrases\" — captions are usually a phrase plus a verb: \"Inside the raindrop, light bends twice.\"\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Why Does This Matter?":{"content":"### Stretch: Why Does This Matter?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to finish an explanation with a statement that gives the reader something to keep.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Who / where / when phrases |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): texts \"have a concluding statement.\" The conclusion serves purpose and audience rather than simply signalling the end. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nWho / where / when phrases this morning — a strong ending often uses one: \"The next time you see steam rising off a road…\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FIND this week. Name the next stage and its question.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall + move the peg (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg still on Stage 2.\n\n1. \"We finish a whole stage this week. Which one, and what's next?\" Whole class.\n2. A student moves the peg and justifies it out loud: \"We're finishing FIND because our drafts are done. Next is SHAPE, because now we read them as the person who doesn't know.\"\n3. Push one further this term: \"What will SHAPE ask us that it didn't ask us last term?\" Fish for: last term \"is it vivid?\", this term \"is it clear?\"\n\n**Watch for:** The justification is the assessment, not the peg. If the student can't justify it, take a second volunteer rather than explaining it for them.\n\n#### I Do — Four Endings, One Winner (5 min)\n\nFour ways to finish. One is genuinely bad and I've written it on purpose.\n\n> STOPS: \"So that is how a rainbow works. The end.\"\nUSE IT: \"The next time you see a rainbow, look behind you — the sun will always be there.\"\nWONDER: \"The rainbow is not in a place at all. It is in the angle between you, the water and the light.\"\nCONNECT: \"This is the same bending of light that makes a straw look broken in a glass of water.\"\n\nThe first one just announces that it's over. The other three all give the reader something to carry away: a thing to do, a thing to marvel at, a link to something they already know. Psalm 111 says the works of the Lord are studied by those who delight in them — the delight is allowed to show up in your last sentence.\n\n**Tip:** Never announce the end. Give them something to take with them.\n\n#### We Do — Write Three Endings (7 min)\n\nFor one shared topic, write one of each type as a class, then vote.\n\n- A USE IT ending — what can the reader now do or notice?\n- A WONDER ending — what's genuinely amazing here?\n- A CONNECT ending — what else works the same way?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which type suits YOUR topic best? (It differs — some topics are useful, some are astonishing.)\n\n#### You Do — Your Closing Statement (9 min)\n\nTwo endings, different types, then choose.\n\n- Write ending A (use it, wonder, or connect).\n- Write ending B — a different type.\n- Cut whichever you choose in half. Short endings land harder.\n- Check: does it announce the end? If so, rewrite it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room: everyone reads only their last sentence.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Concluding statement**: NZC Phase 2: information texts \"have a concluding statement\" — in explanation, this is the \"so what\" that turns information into understanding.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Systems thinking**: \"Uncovering hidden connections between events and ideas\" — the closing statement is where the writer names the connection between this process and the reader's own life.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Finish the Draft":{"content":"### Write: Finish the Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to complete a whole explanation — finished, not perfect.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: replacing overused words |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): completing a multi-paragraph information text from a plan, with a diagram, an opening definition and a concluding statement. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nOverused words got replaced this morning — in explanations the usual offenders are \"stuff\", \"things\" and \"gets\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Last day of FIND. What will exist by the end of today? (A whole draft, with a diagram.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Goal, Plainly (2 min)\n\nOne sentence, then the room is yours.\n\n> \"Today every person finishes the draft — opening, all the steps, the diagram and the ending.\"\n\nFinished, not perfect. Next week we become editors, and you can't edit something that isn't there. Any square brackets left over become questions for next week, not reasons to stop.\n\n**Tip:** If you're nearly done, don't start improving. Finish first.\n\n#### We Do — What's Left? (2 min)\n\nFast round the room: what's missing from your draft right now?\n\n- \"The ending.\"\n- \"Two labels on my diagram.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone missing more than two things, sit near me.\n\n#### You Do — The Long Write (18 min)\n\nEighteen minutes, protected. I'll circulate with one question: \"what happens next?\"\n\n- Finish every step of your sequence.\n- Add your closing statement.\n- Finish and label your diagram, with its caption.\n- Date it and title it. Finished early? Reread and check every step has a cause word.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nCelebrate it out loud — a complete, researched, illustrated explanation is a serious piece of work for Year 5–6.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **A complete information text**: NZC Phase 2: a text that introduces a topic, groups information into paragraphs, uses linking words and topic vocabulary, and ends with a concluding statement.\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to decide what makes an explanation strong, and to judge our own against it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Diagrams and Endings  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Students co-construct the criteria. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — roots, adverbial phrases, who/where/when, replacing weak words — are all polish tools, and polish starts next week.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *We've completed FIND. Two stages done, two to go — name them and their questions.*\n\n#### I Do — Name What's Next (3 min)\n\nShort, because today belongs to your writing.\n\n> \"Next week you stop being the person who knows this, and start reading as the person who doesn't.\"\n\nThat's SHAPE. In narrative we asked \"is it vivid?\" This term the question is different: \"is it CLEAR?\" Those are not the same question and they sometimes pull in opposite directions.\n\n**Tip:** Clarity beats cleverness in this genre. Every time.\n\n#### We Do — Build \"What Makes a Strong Explanation\" (5 min)\n\nSix weeks of learning — call it out and I'll scribe. This list becomes our rubric.\n\n- What does a strong OPENING do in an explanation?\n- What makes the SEQUENCE work?\n- How do we handle hard words?\n- What makes an ENDING worth reading?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Is there anything on our list we'd be unhappy to be judged on? (Then it stays.)\n\n#### You Do — Best-Bit Share (12 min)\n\nEverybody reads their favourite two sentences or shows their diagram. Everybody.\n\n- Choose your best two sentences, or your diagram.\n- Share them with the class — fast rotation.\n- Notice one thing someone else did that you could use.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The part of my explanation I'm proudest of is… The part I'm least sure about is…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Criteria and goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals are set from reflection on content and process — co-constructing the criteria makes them the students' own.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Sharing and discussing the elements of quality writing; interpreting and evaluating rather than receiving a checklist.\n"}}},"Week 7: Reading It As a Stranger":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger\n\n> **Essential question:** Would someone who knows nothing about this understand it?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model · Tue: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria · Wed: Stretch: The Clarity Audit · Thu: Write: Setting Two Goals · Fri: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt this week — your draft is the work. Read it as the person who doesn't already know.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which word have I used that my buddy has never heard?\n- Where is there a jump between two steps?\n- Which paragraph is doing two jobs instead of one?\n- Have I slipped back into story voice anywhere?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nclarity · precise · sequence · accurate · define · consistent · logical · gap · assumption · evidence\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- This part is clear because…\n- A reader might get lost when…\n- I used the word ____ without explaining it.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read your explanation aloud to yourself in a whisper and stop at every word you'd have to explain out loud. Circle it.\n- **Picture this:** A draft covered in three colours of pencil.\n- **Talk it out first:** Give your draft to a partner and have them read it aloud to you. Every place they stumble is a place your reader will.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Colons to introduce lists\n- **Tuesday** — Progressive tenses: past & present continuous\n- **Wednesday** — Complex sentences: although, unless, while\n- **Thursday** — Editing: checking tense consistency\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model":{"content":"### Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge an explanation against criteria, and to name exactly what is wrong with a weak one.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Colons to introduce lists |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed; identify the main idea and key details in sequence. Deconstruction of models with effective analysis (Loane Ch 11). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nColons to introduce lists this morning — useful in explanations: \"Three things are needed: light, water and an angle.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Last term the question was \"is it vivid?\" What is it this term? (\"Is it clear?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — what SHAPE means in a new genre (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Move the peg onto Stage 3. Have both the strong and weak exemplars ready.\n\n1. \"In SHAPE you stop being the writer and become the editor. Same as last term. But the question the editor asks has changed.\" Write both on the board: IS IT VIVID? / IS IT CLEAR?\n2. Ask: \"Could a piece of writing be vivid but not clear?\" Yes — lots of description, no understanding. \"Could it be clear but not vivid?\" Yes — and this term, that's fine. That's the genre doing its job.\n3. Teach the specific move: \"This term, editing means reading it as the person who does NOT already know. That is genuinely difficult, because you can't un-know your own topic. So we use tricks — reading aloud, giving it to a partner, and asking an actual eight-year-old.\"\n   > \"You are the worst possible judge of whether your own explanation is clear. That's not an insult — it's true of every writer alive.\"\n4. Do the clay hand action. \"Same stage, same action, new question.\"\n\n**Watch for:** The idea that you cannot un-know something is the key insight of this stage, and Year 5–6 students find it genuinely interesting. Give it the full minute.\n\n#### I Do — Annotate the Strong One (7 min)\n\nOur WAGOLL, on the visualiser, against the criteria you built on Friday. I'll label AND say why each thing is there.\n\n> [Annotate live: definition sentence · present tense · technical word explained on the move · one step per paragraph · cause connectives · the diagram's job · the closing wonder line.]\n\nWatch that I keep asking WHY, not just WHERE. Here's a bracket around \"bent\" — why? Because \"refracted\" arrives in that same sentence and the reader needs it immediately, not in a glossary at the end. Naming a feature is easy. Saying what it's doing for the reader is the actual skill.\n\n**Tip:** Every feature in a good explanation is there to stop a reader getting lost.\n\n#### We Do — Diagnose the Weak One (7 min)\n\nThe weak exemplar (Teacher tab). I wrote it badly on purpose. Find everything — you've got the criteria, so be specific.\n\n- Where does it slip into a story? What gave it away?\n- Which technical words are used but never explained?\n- Where exactly is the missing step?\n- What's wrong with how it ends?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If you could fix only ONE thing in this piece, which would help a Year 3 reader most?\n\n#### You Do — Annotate the Strong Model (7 min)\n\nYour own copy. Mark three techniques and say what each does.\n\n- Label three features you can name.\n- Beside each, write what it DOES for the reader.\n- Star the one you're going to steal for your own piece today.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Name the technique you're stealing. Saying it out loud makes it a promise.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Deconstruction**: NZC Phase 2: determining how texts are structured and how ideas are conveyed — applied to a strong and a deliberately weak model against shared criteria.\n- *Key competency — Using language, symbols & text* — **Features and purpose**: \"Match a text's features with its purpose\" — students name which features serve the purpose of explaining, and which don't.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria":{"content":"### Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge our own explanation honestly against the rubric.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Progressive tenses: past & present continuous |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\"; set goals based on reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nProgressive tenses this morning — \"is rising\", \"are forming\". Useful, but check you haven't drifted out of the plain present: \"rises\" is usually stronger.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Who are you when you're in this stage? (The reader who knows nothing — not the writer who knows everything.)*\n\n#### I Do — Find a Fault in My Own Work (5 min)\n\nMy draft, our rubric, out loud — including the bit that isn't working.\n\n> \"Definition — tick. Sequence — tick. Technical vocabulary… I've used 'wavelength' in paragraph three and never explained it. My buddy has no idea what that means. That's a grow.\"\n\nNotice what I did not do: I didn't apologise, and I didn't get defensive. I found a hole in my own writing and turned it into a job. You'll do the same in a minute, and nobody in this room is going to laugh — because everybody's got one.\n\n**Tip:** Finding a fault in your own writing is a skill, not a failure.\n\n#### We Do — Glow and Grow the Weak Exemplar (8 min)\n\nFill in the glow and grow columns for the weak exemplar as a class.\n\n- Find one genuine glow — there is always one.\n- Find the single most important grow.\n- Write the grow as an instruction: \"Next time, try…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why only ONE grow, when we can all see five?\n\n#### You Do — Self-Analysis (8 min)\n\nRubric beside your draft. Tick only what you can PROVE with a line from your writing.\n\n- Work down the rubric; tick what you can prove.\n- In the margin, mark the line that proves it.\n- Leave blank anything you can't prove — a blank is information.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone name one glow out loud. Grows are tomorrow.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Rereading at every level**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — the rubric gives a lens for each level.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Strengths and limitations**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations\" — modelled first by the teacher on their own writing, so honesty is safe.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Clarity Audit":{"content":"### Stretch: The Clarity Audit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to audit our writing for unexplained words, missing steps and weak links.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Complex sentences: although, unless, while |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revise to improve clarity for the intended audience and purpose; sentence structures varied for purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nComplex sentences with although / unless / while — very useful for conditions: \"Unless the sun is behind you, you will not see it.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Are we adding new facts in this stage? (No — we're making the ones we've got understandable.)*\n\n#### I Do — Three Colours, Three Passes (5 min)\n\nThree separate passes over one paragraph. One thing at a time — reading for everything at once finds nothing.\n\n> PASS 1 (blue): circle every word my buddy wouldn't know.\nPASS 2 (red): mark every jump where a step is missing.\nPASS 3 (green): underline every weak link — \"and then\", \"next\", \"after that\".\n\nLook what turns up: four blue circles, one red jump, three green links. And here's the important bit — I can fix the blue ones in five minutes with brackets, but the red one might send me back to FILL, because I skipped that step for a reason: I don't fully understand it.\n\n**Tip:** Blue is quick. Red is honest. Do them in that order so the honest one doesn't stop you starting.\n\n#### We Do — Audit a Shared Paragraph (7 min)\n\nOne paragraph on the board. Three passes, out loud, then we fix three things.\n\n- Pass 1 — which words would an eight-year-old not know?\n- Pass 2 — is there a jump? Where exactly?\n- Pass 3 — how many weak links?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which fix made the biggest difference for the smallest change?\n\n#### You Do — Audit Two Paragraphs (9 min)\n\nYour strongest and your weakest paragraph. Three colours, three passes each.\n\n- Run all three passes on both paragraphs.\n- Fix three blue circles (bracket, dash or \"which means\").\n- Fix one green link with a real cause word.\n- If you found a red jump, write it on your goals list — that's the big one.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found a red jump? That's the most valuable thing you'll find this week.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising for clarity**: NZC Phase 2: \"make simple revisions to their texts to improve the clarity for the intended audience and purpose (e.g., replacing words, adding sentences…).\"\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precision for audience**: Word choice judged against the reader: the right technical word, explained — not the impressive word, unexplained.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Setting Two Goals":{"content":"### Write: Setting Two Goals\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose the two changes that will help our reader most.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: checking tense consistency |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTense consistency across a passage this morning — do the same to your whole draft now. Any past-tense verb is a story sneaking back in.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. What will you carry into SHARE next week? (My draft, plus two goals I chose myself.)*\n\n#### I Do — Two Goals, Not Nine (4 min)\n\nMy audit found nine problems. Watch me choose two and walk away from seven.\n\n> \"I will explain 'wavelength' the first time I use it, in paragraph three.\"\n\"I will add the missing step between the light reflecting and the light leaving the drop.\"\n\nBoth goals name a place and a change. Compare that with \"I will make it clearer\" — which can't be finished and can't be checked. And notice I picked the missing step, even though it's the hardest, because it's the one that actually breaks the reader.\n\n**Tip:** Pick the fault that breaks the reader, not the one that's easiest to fix.\n\n#### We Do — What Are We All Finding? (3 min)\n\nCall out the grows you're seeing. I'll tally them — this decides next week's workshops.\n\n- \"Unexplained words.\"\n- \"A missing step in the middle.\"\n- \"Paragraphs doing two jobs.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which grow got the most hands? That's Thursday's workshop.\n\n#### You Do — Write Your Two Goals (14 min)\n\nFinish the self-analysis and write two goals at the top of your draft in \"I will…\" form.\n\n- Finish the rubric self-analysis.\n- GOAL 1: \"I will ____ in ____ [where].\"\n- GOAL 2: the same.\n- Read them to a partner — they hold you to them next week.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Your partner is now holding you to those two. That's the deal.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Specific writing goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection, written specifically enough to be completed and checked.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: \"Being aware of your own thinking\" — choosing two goals from a page of possible faults requires the writer to rank by impact on the reader.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to ask for the feedback we actually need, and to receive it without defending.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Reading It As a Stranger  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing for clarity |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers.\" Oral Language: clarifying questions; considering the impact of our words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAll four warm-ups this week were about accuracy and consistency. Next week is about whether a real person understands it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing SHAPE, entering SHARE — \"Is it ready to give away?\"*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Handover — SHAPE into SHARE (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 3.\n\n1. \"SHAPE told us what needs fixing. What does SHARE do?\" (We get help, we test it on a real person, and we fix it.)\n2. Move the peg between 3 and 4, and draw the arrow running back from 4 to 3.\n3. Say the genre-specific version: \"This term SHARE has a test in it that Term 1 didn't have. Every time a real reader doesn't understand something, you go straight back to SHAPE. Confusion is not an opinion you can argue with.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Plant the loop here in one sentence; Week 9 teaches it properly.\n\n#### I Do — The One Sentence You Must Not Say (4 min)\n\nThe protocol, and then the rule that matters most in this genre.\n\n> READ ALOUD → YOUR QUESTION → TWO STARS → ONE WISH → thank you, write it down.\nThe banned sentence: \"Oh, what I MEANT was…\"\n\nIf you have to explain what you meant, the writing hasn't done it — and your Year 3 buddy won't have you sitting beside them. So when someone says \"I got lost\", the only correct response is \"thank you\" and a mark on the page. Explaining out loud is how writers avoid fixing things.\n\n**Tip:** \"I got lost\" is data. Never argue with data.\n\n#### We Do — Fishbowl (on me) (7 min)\n\nFour volunteers make a circle around me. I read my draft; they give me real feedback.\n\n- Two of you: what made it clear?\n- Two of you: where did you get lost, or what word didn't you know?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Watch me take the wish. Did I explain myself? (No — and neither will you.)\n\n#### You Do — Choose Your Section and Your Question (10 min)\n\nMark the 60 seconds you'll read, and write the question you want answered.\n\n- Mark the section you'll read on Monday.\n- Write ONE specific question: \"Does the bit about ____ make sense?\"\n- Test it: if the answer could only be yes or no, sharpen it.\n- ✦ Best question of all: \"Which word did I use that you didn't know?\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Bring your draft, your question and your listening ears on Monday.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Giving and receiving feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — taught as a protocol, with the writer's own question driving it.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Listening without defending**: \"Listen carefully and paraphrase ideas of others\" — the writer records the feedback rather than arguing with it.\n"}}},"Week 8: The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test\n\n> **Essential question:** Does a real reader actually understand this?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1 · Tue: Circle Day 2 + The No-Paper Test · Wed: Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan · Thu: Write: Targeted Revision Workshops · Fri: Share & Reflect: Before and After |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is feedback, the buddy test, and turning both into a plan.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which piece of feedback did I already know was coming?\n- Did two people get lost in the same place? That's a fact, not an opinion.\n- Which change would help my buddy most?\n- What am I choosing to leave, and why is that okay?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nclarify · expand · reorder · define · simplify · specific · confusing · missing · smooth · check\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I got lost at the part where…\n- You used the word ____ and I didn't know it.\n- I think a step is missing between ____ and ____ .\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Start with the thing two different people said. Two people is not an opinion.\n- **Picture this:** A draft with three sticky notes on it.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your number-one change out loud as an action: \"I am going to add / cut / reorder / replace ____ .\"\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Spelling: tricky high-frequency words\n- **Tuesday** — Prepositions of direction & movement\n- **Wednesday** — Making vague sentences specific\n- **Thursday** — Editing: strengthening openings\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1":{"content":"### Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a writer clearly where we got lost, and what made sense.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Spelling: tricky high-frequency words |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): peer feedback used to revise for clarity. Oral Language: comments that encourage discussion; considering the impact of our words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTricky spellings this morning — note them if you spot one in a peer's draft, but don't lead with spelling. Understanding comes first.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Last term the feedback question was \"did you feel it?\" What is it now? (\"Did you understand it?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — what SHARE means in a new genre (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 4.\n\n1. Open hands action, whole class. \"Same giving action. But listen to how the feedback question has changed.\" Write both up: DID YOU FEEL IT? / DID YOU UNDERSTAND IT?\n2. Ask: \"Which of those two can a listener be wrong about?\" Discuss. Land it: you can't really be wrong about whether you felt something — but you absolutely know whether you understood something. That makes this term's feedback harder to dodge.\n3. Teach the banned sentence, again and firmly.\n   > \"When someone says 'I got lost', you do NOT say 'oh, what I meant was…'. Your buddy will not have you sitting next to them. If it isn't on the page, it doesn't exist.\"\n4. Then the rule that keeps the writer in charge: \"You still choose what to change. But if two people got lost in the same place, that's not an opinion any more. That's a fact about your writing.\"\n\n**Watch for:** \"I got lost\" must feel completely safe to say in this room. Praise the first student who says it, out loud, by name.\n\n#### I Do — The Protocol, With the Genre Lens (4 min)\n\nSame five steps as last term, with explanation-specific prompts.\n\n> STARS: \"The step I understood best was…\" / \"The word you explained really well was…\"\nWISHES: \"I got lost at…\" / \"You used ____ and I don't know what it means.\" / \"I think a step is missing between ____ and ____ .\"\n\nNotice the wishes are all about the READER's experience, not about the writer's ability. \"I got lost\" describes what happened to me. That's why it's never rude and never arguable.\n\n**Tip:** Point at the exact sentence where you got lost. Not \"somewhere in the middle\".\n\n#### We Do — Form the Circles (3 min)\n\nGroups of four, seated facing each other, timer on the board, roles named.\n\n- Who reads first?\n- Who's on the timer?\n- Everyone: pencil ready for writing feedback down.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Five minutes per writer — two writers today, two tomorrow.\n\n#### You Do — Helping Circle, Round 1 (14 min)\n\nTwo writers per group today. Full protocol.\n\n- Read your marked section aloud.\n- Ask your question.\n- Receive two stars and one wish from each listener.\n- Write every piece of feedback down in your own words, immediately.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Write it down NOW, before it evaporates. Tomorrow the other two read — and then the real test.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Impact of our words**: NZC Phase 2: \"consider the impact of their words on others\" — feedback is specific and about the writing, never about the writer.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Using peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revisions made \"using audience and peer feedback\" to improve clarity for the intended audience.\n"},"Tuesday: Circle Day 2 + The No-Paper Test":{"content":"### Circle Day 2 + The No-Paper Test\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that if we can explain it without our paper, we understand it — and if we can't, we don't.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Prepositions of direction & movement |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): communicate in complete sentences with connectives; use precise vocabulary. Writing processes: peer feedback informing revision. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPrepositions of direction this morning — into, through, across, out of. That's the language of every process: \"light travels THROUGH the drop and out of the back.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Is this the last feedback we'll get? (No — SHARE loops until it's ready.)*\n\n#### I Do — The No-Paper Test (3 min)\n\nHere's the test that decides whether you're finished. Put your draft face down.\n\n> Explain your whole process from memory, in order, in under 60 seconds — with nothing in front of you.\n\nIf you can do it, your explanation is genuinely yours and your writing will be clear. If you can't — if you get to a step and stall — that's the step your reader will stall at too. It's the most accurate five seconds of assessment in this whole unit.\n\n**Tip:** The step you can't say out loud is the step you haven't understood.\n\n#### We Do — Regroup (2 min)\n\nBack into the same circles, timer reset, remaining two writers ready.\n\n- Readers three and four — draft and question out.\n- Then everyone does the no-paper test.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Same protocol, same five minutes.\n\n#### You Do — Circle Round 2, then the No-Paper Test (16 min)\n\nFinish the circle, then everyone turns their draft over and tests themselves on a partner.\n\n- Remaining writers: read, ask, receive, record.\n- Then: draft face down, explain your whole process to a partner in 60 seconds.\n- Mark the step where you stalled — that's your first job tomorrow.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if you stalled somewhere. Good — you've just found the exact place your buddy would have stalled too.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Explaining without support**: NZC Phase 2: giving descriptions with specific detail to engage listeners — unsupported, which tests genuine understanding rather than memorised text.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Honest self-assessment**: Recognising the difference between having written something and having understood it.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan":{"content":"### Stretch: Turning Feedback Into a Plan\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sort feedback and decide, as the author, what to act on.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Making vague sentences specific |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): using feedback to revise while retaining authorship of the text. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nMaking vague sentences specific this morning — that IS today's lesson. \"It gets hot\" → \"the metal reaches 200°C\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who decides what changes? (The writer — but a confused reader is very hard to argue with.)*\n\n#### I Do — Triage (5 min)\n\nThree piles. Watch me sort real feedback, out loud.\n\n> DO IT · THINK ABOUT IT · POLITELY LEAVE IT\n\n\"I didn't know 'wavelength'\" — do it, and two people said it. \"Add more about rainbows in the Bible\" — leave it politely; it's interesting but it isn't the question I'm answering. \"Maybe start with the diagram\" — think about it; I'll try it and see. And note: in this genre, ANY \"I got lost\" goes straight in the DO pile. Confusion isn't a matter of taste.\n\n**Tip:** Two people lost in the same place = fix it, no debate.\n\n#### We Do — Sort a Sample Set (6 min)\n\nSix comments from an imaginary circle. Sort them together.\n\n- \"I loved your diagram.\"\n- \"You should write about volcanoes instead.\"\n- \"I got lost between step two and step three.\"\n- \"What does 'molecule' mean?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of these is not feedback at all? And which two are the same problem?\n\n#### You Do — Your Revision Plan (10 min)\n\nThree changes, in order, written as actions.\n\n- Sort your feedback (and your no-paper stall) into the three piles.\n- Write changes 1, 2 and 3 as actions: \"Add a sentence explaining ____ before ____ .\"\n- Order them: whatever breaks the reader most goes first.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Point at your number one change with a finger on the page.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Selecting revisions**: NZC Phase 2 writers use peer and audience feedback to revise for clarity — selecting from feedback is part of the skill, not an avoidance of it.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Think before acting**: Deciding, justifying and sequencing changes rather than either ignoring or blindly obeying feedback.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Targeted Revision Workshops":{"content":"### Write: Targeted Revision Workshops\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the four revision moves: add, cut, reorder and replace.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: strengthening openings |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"make simple revisions… replacing words, adding sentences, and using audience and peer feedback.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nStrengthening openings this morning — check yours still defines the topic in the first sentence.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What are we doing to the writing right now? (Recrafting — this is the loop.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Moves, Four Signals (3 min)\n\nThe same four moves as last term. Watch all four on one paragraph.\n\n> ADD (hands apart) · CUT (scissors) · REORDER (swap hands) · REPLACE (one hand over the other)\n\nAdd: a sentence explaining \"wavelength\". Cut: the fact about double rainbows — true, but it isn't answering my question. Reorder: the diagram reference now comes before the paragraph, not after. Replace: \"goes\" becomes \"travels\". In explanation, REORDER is the strongest move and nobody ever uses it — if a reader got lost, ask whether something is simply in the wrong place.\n\n**Tip:** When someone gets lost, try moving something before you try adding something.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Move (2 min)\n\nRound the room: your number one change — which of the four moves is it?\n\n- \"Mine's an ADD — I have to explain a word.\"\n- \"Mine's a REORDER — my third step should be second.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone's a CUT? Cutting a true fact that doesn't serve the question is the bravest move in this genre.\n\n#### You Do — Revision Workshops (16 min)\n\nEverybody revises. Two six-minute workshops at the back table — come if it's your grow.\n\n- Work through your revision plan in order.\n- Name the move before you make it.\n- Workshop 1: explaining technical words on the move.\n- Workshop 2: finding and fixing a missing step.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Did anyone's explanation change ORDER today? Tell us what moved and why.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revision moves**: NZC Phase 2 names replacing words and adding sentences; cutting and reordering complete the set. In explanation, REORDER is the most powerful and most neglected.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Expanding with phrases**: \"Expand sentences using adverbial phrases\" — the mechanics behind most ADD moves in this genre.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Before and After":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Before and After\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to see and describe the difference our revision made to a reader.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & the Buddy Test  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and recrafting |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): articulating the effect of revisions on clarity for the intended audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were all specificity tools. Every one of them is a revision move.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. How many times can this loop run? (As many as it needs — and next week is the last one.)*\n\n#### I Do — Before, After, Move (3 min)\n\nOld version, new version, name the move. That's the whole share.\n\n> BEFORE: \"The light is refracted.\"\nAFTER: \"The light is refracted — which means it bends — as it enters the drop.\"\nMOVE: add.\n\nI read the old one without cringing. The old version isn't shameful; it's the version that got me to the new one. And the new one is longer, which in this genre is often correct: clear takes more words than clever.\n\n**Tip:** Name the move. That's what makes it stick for next time.\n\n#### We Do — What Changed? (4 min)\n\nI'll read a pair. You name what changed and what it does for a reader.\n\n- What's different?\n- What can the reader do now that they couldn't before?\n- Which move was it?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Would that same move help anyone else's piece?\n\n#### You Do — Before/After Share (13 min)\n\nPairs first, then four volunteers to the class.\n\n- Read your before and after to a partner.\n- Name the move you used.\n- Notice whether your partner used a different move for the same kind of problem.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The feedback that helped me most was… because…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Evaluating a revision**: Phase 2 writers revise for clarity and can say why the new version serves the reader better — which is what makes the strategy transferable.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: Naming the move used and its effect makes the revision conscious and repeatable.\n"}}},"Week 9: Recraft, Edit, Best Copy":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy\n\n> **Essential question:** What does it look like to make my writing clearer rather than just neater?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Real Second Draft · Tue: Build Together: Flow and the Dangerous \"It\" · Wed: Stretch: The Four-Pass Edit · Thu: Write: Best Copy and Final Diagram · Fri: Share & Reflect: Rehearse for Your Buddy |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is the real second draft, the edit, and the best copy with your finished diagram.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which paragraph needs rewriting rather than patching?\n- Does every \"it\" clearly point at something?\n- Have I checked capitals, commas, tense and spelling — one pass each?\n- Is my diagram neat enough for an eight-year-old to read?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nrecraft · rewrite · consistent · legible · accurate · precise · labelled · checked · careful · finished\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The version I like better is…\n- I rewrote this part because…\n- I checked for…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Cover the paragraph and write it again from memory, as if you were saying it to your buddy. What you remember is what matters.\n- **Picture this:** A page with one paragraph crossed out and a clearer one beside it.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read the paragraph aloud to a partner and stop the moment either of you stumbles.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Alliteration & assonance\n- **Tuesday** — Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions: review\n- **Wednesday** — Paragraph cohesion: pronouns as linking devices\n- **Thursday** — Editing: identifying weak sentence openings\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Real Second Draft":{"content":"### Launch: The Real Second Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to rewrite a section for our reader rather than patching words in the old one.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Alliteration & assonance |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revisions at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level to improve clarity for the intended audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAlliteration and assonance this morning — lovely in poetry, and mostly a distraction here. If a phrase sounds clever but explains less, cut it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Which move is a rewrite? (REPLACE — at paragraph size.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cover It and Write It Again (6 min)\n\nWatch me cover my worst paragraph and rewrite it from scratch, as if I were talking to my buddy.\n\n> OLD: \"The light is refracted at the interface and undergoes dispersion, resulting in the separation of the constituent wavelengths.\"\nNEW: \"When the light enters the raindrop it bends. Each colour bends by a slightly different amount, so the colours spread apart — like a fan opening.\"\n\nIf I'd tried to fix the old sentence I'd have kept its shape and swapped a word or two, and it would still have been unreadable. Covering it forces me to write what I actually mean, to a real person. Longer, simpler, clearer. That's the trade in this genre and it's always worth it.\n\n**Tip:** When a paragraph is broken in more than two places, cover it and start again.\n\n#### We Do — Which Is Clearer, and Why? (5 min)\n\nTwo versions on the board. Vote, then justify in criteria language — not \"it sounds better\".\n\n- Which one explains its technical word?\n- Which one would a Year 3 follow?\n- Which one keeps the cause in it?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Is the longer one always the clearer one? (No — but here it is, and we should say why.)\n\n#### You Do — Second Draft Begins (10 min)\n\nRevision plan change #1. Cover and rewrite if it needs a rewrite.\n\n- Complete change #1.\n- If it's a rewrite, cover the original and write it fresh, as if speaking to your buddy.\n- Read both versions side by side.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who rewrote rather than patched? Was it faster or slower than you expected?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Whole-text revision**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" and revise for clarity — recrafting a whole paragraph is a whole-text-level move.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Register for the audience**: Rewriting \"as if saying it to the buddy\" is the most direct way to apply the Phase 2 expectation of selecting register and word choice for an audience.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Flow and the Dangerous \"It\"":{"content":"### Build Together: Flow and the Dangerous \"It\"\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to link paragraphs smoothly and to make sure every pronoun points at something clear.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions: review |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures and cohesion (Phase 2): combining sentences, varying phrase position, and using linking words; pronoun clarity across paragraphs. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPronouns as linking devices this morning — and the warning that goes with it: in an explanation with four moving parts, \"it\" can point at any of them.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's the difference between revising and editing? (Revising = ideas and clarity. Editing = surface. Revising first.)*\n\n#### I Do — Hunt the Homeless \"It\" (5 min)\n\nWatch me read my draft looking for one word only.\n\n> AMBIGUOUS: \"The light hits the drop and it bends.\" — what bends, the light or the drop?\nCLEAR: \"The light hits the drop, and the light bends.\"\nBETTER: \"As it enters the drop, the light bends.\"\n\nI know what I meant. My buddy doesn't. Every \"it\", \"they\" and \"this\" needs to point at exactly one thing, and if there's any doubt, name the thing again. Repeating a noun is not bad writing here — it's kindness.\n\n**Tip:** Circle every \"it\". Ask: could this point at more than one thing? If yes, name it.\n\n#### We Do — Smooth a Choppy Passage (7 min)\n\nA passage with abrupt paragraph joins and two ambiguous pronouns. Fix both problems together.\n\n- Where does the reader get jolted between paragraphs?\n- Which linking word would carry them across?\n- Which \"it\" could point at two things?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Read it aloud before and after. What changed for the listener?\n\n#### You Do — Changes 2 and 3, then the Whisper Read (9 min)\n\nFinish your revision plan, then whisper-read the whole piece and mark every stumble.\n\n- Complete revision changes #2 and #3.\n- Circle every \"it\" and \"they\" — fix any that are ambiguous.\n- Whisper-read the whole text; dot every stumble.\n- Fix the two worst dots.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many homeless 'its' did you find? Every one of them was a place your buddy would have paused.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Linking words and phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"begin to use linking words and phrases\" — used here to carry a reader between steps without a jolt.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Pronoun clarity**: Phase 2 writers keep meaning clear across multi-paragraph texts; in explanation, an unanchored \"it\" is the single most common source of confusion.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Four-Pass Edit":{"content":"### Stretch: The Four-Pass Edit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to edit in separate passes — one thing at a time.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Paragraph cohesion: pronouns as linking devices |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\"; \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCoordinating and subordinating conjunctions reviewed this morning — because / although / while are the ones your writing leans on.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Draw the process as a cycle. Where does a confused reader send you back to? (SHAPE.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — The cycle — and where a confused reader sends you (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Board space. Jotters open.\n\n1. Draw the four stages in a straight line. \"This is what everyone thinks writing is. Still wrong.\"\n2. Draw the arrow curving back from SHARE to SHAPE. \"Real writers go round more than once.\" Then add the Term 2 version: a second arrow running all the way back from SHARE to FILL. \"And sometimes a reader asks a question you can't answer — and that sends you all the way back to the start.\"\n3. Ask: \"Has that happened to anyone this term? Did a buddy ask something that sent you back to research?\" Take two or three stories.\n4. Jotters: draw the process as a cycle from memory, labelling all four stages, the SHAPE↔SHARE loop, and the long arrow back to FILL. Ninety seconds.\n\n**Watch for:** The long arrow back to FILL is new this term and it's the honest picture of how information writing actually works. Students who've lived it this term will recognise it immediately.\n\n#### I Do — One Pass Per Thing (5 min)\n\nFour passes, in this order, on my own draft.\n\n> Pass 1: capitals & full stops · Pass 2: commas (after transition words, in lists, in complex sentences) · Pass 3: tense — is it all present? · Pass 4: technical words, spelled right.\n\nPass three matters more this term than last, because past tense is how a story sneaks back in. And pass four is genre-specific: if you spell your topic's key word wrong, you look like you don't know it. Check the ones you can't picture.\n\n**Tip:** One pass, one thing. Reading for everything at once finds nothing.\n\n#### We Do — Four Passes Together (6 min)\n\nShort passage with errors. One pass at a time; count what each finds.\n\n- Pass 1 — capitals and full stops?\n- Pass 2 — where are the missing commas?\n- Pass 3 — any past-tense slips?\n- Pass 4 — which technical words need checking?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which pass found the errors you'd have been most embarrassed by?\n\n#### You Do — Edit Your Own (9 min)\n\nFour separate passes, then swap for one final pass.\n\n- Pass 1: capitals and full stops.\n- Pass 2: commas.\n- Pass 3: tense — every verb in the present.\n- Pass 4: technical spellings — check, don't guess.\n- Peer swap for one fresh pass.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Editing isn't the boring bit — it's the respect bit. Colossians 3:23: work at it with all your heart.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Editing with known punctuation**: NZC Phase 2: \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\" — everything from the Term 1 and Term 2 warm-ups is now the checklist.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Checking spellings**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words\" — technical vocabulary must be spelled correctly, and it is often unfamiliar.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Best Copy and Final Diagram":{"content":"### Write: Best Copy and Final Diagram\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to present finished information writing carefully, including a clear labelled diagram.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: identifying weak sentence openings |\n| **NZC link** | Transcription — handwriting (Phase 2): \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\" Layout conventions for paragraphs and text features. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nWeak sentence openings this morning — last check: do three of your paragraphs start with \"The\"? Change one.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Is a best copy the same as publishing? (No — publishing is next week, when your buddy actually gets it.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Presentation Standard (3 min)\n\nWhat a finished page looks like. I'll write the first lines so you can see the pace.\n\n> Title (as a question) · date · margin respected · paragraphs indented · diagram in pencil first, then pen · labels printed neatly, not joined · caption underneath.\n\nDiagram labels get printed, not joined — an eight-year-old reads printing more easily. And do the diagram in pencil first. Nobody draws it right first time.\n\n**Tip:** One neat line through a mistake. No scribbles.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Checklist (2 min)\n\nFive things on the board for the lesson.\n\n- Title as a question.\n- Paragraphs indented, on new lines.\n- Diagram in pencil first, labels printed.\n- Caption as a full sentence.\n- Joined, legible handwriting for the prose.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anything else worth adding?\n\n#### You Do — Best Copy Into Writing Books (18 min)\n\nQuiet and calm. Take the whole time — this is the version that stays in your book.\n\n- Write your best copy into your writing book.\n- Draw and label your final diagram; add the caption.\n- Follow the five-point checklist.\n- Reread as you go — you'll still catch things.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Not finished? Finish tomorrow. Nobody rushes a best copy.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Legibility and stamina**: NZC Phase 2: legible, fluent handwriting sustained across a multi-paragraph text — assessed on the best copy.\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Text features**: The diagram, its labels and its caption are part of the finished text and are presented to the same standard as the prose.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Rehearse for Your Buddy":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Rehearse for Your Buddy\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read an explanation aloud so a younger listener can follow it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Best Copy  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting in cycles |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners with specific details; vary sentence structure and length for effect; respond to questions. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were the polish tools: conjunctions, cohesion, pronouns, openings.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's left? (Publishing — and finding out whether they understood.)*\n\n#### I Do — Reading to a Younger Listener (2 min)\n\nSame paragraph twice: once at my normal pace, once at buddy pace.\n\n> [Read fast and flat. Then: slower, pausing at every full stop, holding up the diagram at the right moment, looking up at the end.]\n\nSlower than feels natural. Pause after every step so their brain catches up. And hold the diagram up at the exact moment you mention it — not at the end.\n\n**Tip:** Point at your diagram while you read the step it shows.\n\n#### We Do — Practise the Pauses (4 min)\n\nOne shared paragraph, whole class, twice — flat, then with step-pauses.\n\n- Read it flat.\n- Now pause after every step.\n- Now hold up the diagram at the right moment.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version would an eight-year-old follow?\n\n#### You Do — Finish and Rehearse (14 min)\n\nFinish the best copy if it isn't done. If it is, rehearse in pairs.\n\n- Finish the best copy and diagram.\n- Rehearse reading your piece aloud, at buddy pace, with the diagram.\n- Give your partner ONE delivery tip. Take theirs.\n- Decide where you'll pause for questions.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: Microsoft Word, and then your buddy. Bring your reading voice.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: giving descriptions with specific details that actively engage listeners — here, an audience five years younger.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Preparing to present**: Rehearsing, taking a peer's tip and acting on it before a public reading.\n"}}},"Week 10: Publish & Present":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 10 — Publish & Present\n\n> **Essential question:** Did my reader actually understand it?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Typing and Saving · Tue: Build Together: Presentation and the Diagram · Wed: Stretch: Final Proof and Rehearsal · Thu: The Celebration: Reading to Your Buddy · Fri: Reflect: Same Process, New Genre |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Publish it. Type it, format it, proof it, and read it to the person you wrote it for.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Is my filename right — Surname_Firstname_Explanation_T2?\n- Have I read it backwards, sentence by sentence?\n- Does my diagram appear beside the step it explains?\n- Which part will I read aloud, and where will I pause?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\npublished · audience · presentation · proofread · format · caption · final · present · explain · understand\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- By ____ , Room ____\n- This explains how…\n- Do you have any questions?\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read only your opening and one step to your buddy, then show the diagram. That counts as published.\n- **Picture this:** A printed page with a diagram beside the text.\n- **Talk it out first:** Practise your first two sentences three times before you read to your buddy.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Review: Term 2 punctuation\n- **Tuesday** — Review: Term 2 grammar\n- **Wednesday** — Review: Term 2 sentence structures\n- **Thursday** — Full-passage edit: Term 2 checklist\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Typing and Saving":{"content":"### Launch: Typing and Saving\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to type and save our published explanation properly.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Present  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 2 punctuation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — publishing: transferring an edited handwritten text to a digital format while maintaining paragraph conventions and accuracy. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTerm 2 punctuation reviewed this morning — you're about to need every mark of it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the very end of the loop. What does publishing mean? (Giving it to the reader.)*\n\n#### I Do — Title, Save, Type (6 min)\n\nScreen up. Watch the order I do things in — it matters.\n\n> Title (as a question) → my name → File → Save As → Surname_Firstname_Explanation_T2 → then start typing.\n\nSave the empty document first. Four seconds now, twenty minutes saved later. Then type from your book copy — do not rewrite as you type. That version is finished.\n\n**Tip:** Save at the end of every paragraph.\n\n#### We Do — Filename and Folder (5 min)\n\nEveryone says the convention out loud and finds the folder before typing a word.\n\n- Surname_Firstname_Explanation_T2\n- Where does it get saved?\n- What do you do BEFORE typing?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What happens if two people use the same filename?\n\n#### You Do — Start Typing (10 min)\n\nType from your book copy. Leave a gap where your diagram will go.\n\n- Create, title and save the document before typing.\n- Type your opening and first steps.\n- Leave a labelled gap: [DIAGRAM HERE].\n- Save at every paragraph.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Save. Then save again.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Layout conventions**: Paragraph conventions carry into the typed version: each paragraph on a new line, clearly separated for the reader.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Organisation**: Saving with an agreed filename in an agreed place — a small habit that saves a great deal of time later.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Presentation and the Diagram":{"content":"### Build Together: Presentation and the Diagram\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to format an information text so a reader can navigate it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Present  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 2 grammar |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to inform (Phase 2): text features — headings, paragraphs, diagrams and captions — used to help the reader. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTerm 2 grammar reviewed this morning — the red and blue squiggles in Word are a suggestion, not a boss. You decide.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who is this document for? (The same buddy, from Week 3.)*\n\n#### I Do — Format, and Overrule the Computer (5 min)\n\nWatch me format the page, then refuse the spell-checker twice.\n\n> Word wants to change \"kererū\" and flags my topic's technical term as a mistake.\n\nThe computer doesn't know te reo Māori and it doesn't know your subject. It's a helper. But when it flags a word I genuinely wasn't sure of, I check it properly — in a dictionary, not by guessing.\n\n**Tip:** Every squiggle is a question, not an instruction.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Standard (5 min)\n\nFour decisions, agreed as a class, so the published set looks like a set.\n\n- Title as a question, plus author name.\n- One clear, readable font.\n- Paragraphs clearly separated.\n- Diagram (scanned, photographed or hand-drawn on the printout) beside the step it explains, with its caption.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does one class standard look better than 25 different designs?\n\n#### You Do — Type and Format (11 min)\n\nFinish typing and apply the standard. Sort out how your diagram will get onto the page.\n\n- Finish typing your explanation.\n- Apply the four-point standard.\n- Place your diagram (or leave a neat space to add it by hand after printing) with its caption.\n- Deal with every squiggle — accept or refuse, deliberately.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found a mistake while typing that they'd missed on paper? That's why we type second.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform* — **Text features**: NZC Phase 2 information texts use layout, headings and visual elements purposefully; the diagram is placed beside the step it explains.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Using tools critically**: Spell-checkers are a tool, not an authority — particularly with technical vocabulary and te reo Māori words.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Final Proof and Rehearsal":{"content":"### Stretch: Final Proof and Rehearsal\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to proofread a finished text and to prepare to present it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Present  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Review: Term 2 sentence structures |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): final edits with peer checking. Oral Language: preparing to engage listeners. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTerm 2 sentence structures reviewed this morning — check you haven't got ten sentences of exactly the same shape.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Tomorrow the loop ends. Where does the writing go? (To my buddy.)*\n\n#### I Do — Read It Backwards (4 min)\n\nThe last proofreading trick, and the strangest one.\n\n> Start at the last sentence. Read it. Then the second-to-last. Work backwards to the top.\n\nReading backwards breaks the sequence, so your brain stops predicting — and prediction is exactly why we can't see our own mistakes. I've read this eleven times and I've just found a missing full stop.\n\n**Tip:** Backwards for errors, forwards for flow.\n\n#### We Do — Partner Proof and Pause Plan (3 min)\n\nAgree the swap protocol, and mark where you'll pause tomorrow.\n\n- Swap; read the other person's backwards; mark, don't fix.\n- Then mark two places in your own text where you'll stop and ask \"any questions?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why mark rather than fix?\n\n#### You Do — Proof, Check, Print, Rehearse (14 min)\n\nFinish the document, then get ready for tomorrow.\n\n- Proofread backwards.\n- Partner check — swap and mark.\n- Fix what they found; print or save to the class folder.\n- Mark your two pause points and rehearse once with a partner.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow your writing meets the person you wrote it for.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Final proofreading**: NZC Phase 2 editing expectations applied to a finished, published text, including a peer check.\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Preparing to present**: Rehearsing delivery — pace, pauses and use of the diagram — so the listener can follow.\n"},"Thursday: The Celebration: Reading to Your Buddy":{"content":"### The Celebration: Reading to Your Buddy\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to present our explanation to a real reader and find out whether it worked.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Present  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Full-passage edit: Term 2 checklist |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners with specific detail; respond to questions; consider the impact of your words. Writing: publication for a real audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYour last warm-up was a full-passage edit — and today the passage is finished. Look what you made.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the last step of the last stage. Say all four stages one more time.*\n\n#### I Do — Set It Up as an Event (3 min)\n\nI read first, to one buddy, while everyone watches. Then the rules.\n\n> Read → hold up the diagram at the right moment → pause → \"Do you have any questions?\" → answer honestly, including \"I don't know\".\n\nIf your buddy asks something you can't answer, the correct response is \"that's a good question and I don't know — I'd have to find out.\" That's what real explainers say. Pretending is the only wrong answer.\n\n**Tip:** Ask them at the end: \"What's one thing you learned?\" Write their answer down.\n\n#### We Do — The Presenter's Job (2 min)\n\nThree things, agreed before the buddies arrive.\n\n- Read slowly, pause at each step.\n- Show the diagram when you mention it.\n- Ask: \"What's one thing you learned?\" and write down what they say.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What do you say if they don't understand a bit?\n\n#### You Do — Present to Your Buddy (17 min)\n\nOne-to-one or in small groups. Every student presents.\n\n- Read your explanation to your buddy, at buddy pace, with the diagram.\n- Pause at your two marked points and take questions.\n- Ask: \"What's one thing you learned?\" Write their exact words on the back of your copy.\n- Give them your printed copy to keep.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nName the value out loud: this is Mōhio — we came to know something, and then we gave that knowing away. Soli Deo Gloria.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Responding to questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions\" — and answer them, live, for a younger listener.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Contributing to a community**: Contributing work to a genuine audience beyond the classroom, and receiving their response.\n"},"Friday: Reflect: Same Process, New Genre":{"content":"### Reflect: Same Process, New Genre\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to explain our writing process and how it changed between a story and an explanation.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 2 — Explanation & Report: “How Does That Work?” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Present  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" This lesson assesses the process knowledge taught across two terms. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nLook back at all ten weeks of warm-ups: every one of them turned up inside a real piece of writing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages, in order, in your own words — plus how each one differed this term.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Assessment — explain it, and explain what changed (5 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered. Term 1 and Term 2 charts both visible. This is the assessment — treat it as one.\n\n1. Give the task exactly as written.\n   > \"Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Then explain how each stage was DIFFERENT this term, when you were explaining how something works instead of telling a story.\"\n2. Out loud first: 60 seconds each to a partner, both directions. Listen in on four or five students — that listening is your assessment record for most of the class.\n3. Then in writing, as questions 1 and 2 of the reflection.\n4. Finish by uncovering the strip and doing the chant one last time.\n\n**Watch for:** Part one (naming the stages) should now be easy for nearly everyone. Part two (naming the differences) is the real assessment: \"in FILL I collected memories, this term I collected facts and had to check them\" is a strong answer. Record on the \"Process\" row of the rubric.\n\n#### I Do — What Was Different This Term (3 min)\n\nMy own reflection first, honestly, including the hard part.\n\n> \"The hardest stage for me was FILL, and it surprised me. Last term I already had my material — it was my own memory. This term I had to go and get it, and check it, and understand it before I could write a word.\"\n\nThat's the reflection worth writing: not \"I liked this unit\", but a real difference you noticed between two pieces of work you actually did.\n\n**Tip:** Name a stage, name a moment, name a change.\n\n#### We Do — Two Charts, Side by Side (5 min)\n\nPut the Term 1 \"strong narrative\" chart beside a new \"strong explanation\" chart. Compare them.\n\n- What's on BOTH lists?\n- What's only on the narrative list?\n- What's only on the explanation list?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which things turn out to be true of all good writing, whatever the genre?\n\n#### You Do — The Writer's Reflection (12 min)\n\nIn your jotter, then into your portfolio.\n\n- Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it — all four stages, what a writer does in each.\n- Then: name two ways each stage was DIFFERENT this term, explaining instead of storytelling.\n- The question I chose was… and I chose it because…\n- The part I'm proudest of is…\n- My buddy said they learned… (their exact words).\n- One thing I can do now that I couldn't do in Week 1 is…\n- My goal for Term 3 is…\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRead the Soli Deo Gloria line together: we came to know something about the way the world is made, and we gave it away. Close in prayer or a shared thank-you.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflecting on process**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection on content and process — which requires students to be able to describe the process itself, in two different genres.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition and transfer**: \"Being aware of your own thinking\" — and recognising the same process working differently in a new context, which is the strongest evidence of real learning.\n"}}}}},"Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches":{"label":"Week","children":{"Unit Plan & Overview":{"content":"### Term 3 Unit Plan — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things”\n\n\n| Unit | |\n|---|---|\n| **Year level** | Years 5–6 (ages 9–12) · NZC English Phase 2 |\n| **Duration** | 10 weeks · 25 min × 5 days |\n| **Universal concept** | Voice and Service — Words Are One of the Ways We Serve People |\n| **Anchor value** | Manaaki — Serve faithfully with our gifts and talents |\n| **Throughline question** | “What needs to change — and can my words move someone to change it?” |\n| **Published outcome** | A persuasive piece written for a real decision-maker: best copy handwritten (Week 9), typed in Microsoft Word (Week 10), AND delivered as a 2-minute spoken speech to a live audience in Week 10. |\n\n#### Key Scripture\n\n- Proverbs 31:8–9 — \"Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves\" (Week 1: the point of a voice)\n- Ephesians 4:29 — \"Let no unwholesome talk come out of your mouths, but only what is helpful for building others up\" (Week 2: the rules of engagement)\n- Proverbs 15:1 — \"A gentle answer turns away wrath\" (Week 6: how to treat the other side)\n- Micah 6:8 — \"Act justly, love mercy, walk humbly\" (Week 3: choosing an issue worth the effort)\n- Colossians 4:6 — \"Let your conversation be always full of grace, seasoned with salt\" (Week 10: how we speak when we want to win)\n\n#### NZC English — Phase 2 links\n\n- Writing to persuade — state a clear position, support it with reasons and evidence, address the reader directly, and end with a call to action\n- Composition — identify the audience and purpose for a text; plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting genre, language register and word choice; understand that different audiences may respond very differently to the same text\n- Writing processes (Drafting) — write paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences and a concluding sentence (taught here as PEEL)\n- Sentence structures — use a range of sentence structures and types to suit purpose and enhance impact; expand sentences using adverbial phrases; combine sentences, varying the position of the phrase\n- Punctuation — use commas correctly for complex sentences, phrases and transition words, with some support\n- Writing craft — vocabulary chosen deliberately for effect, including connotation; figurative language used purposefully\n- Writing processes (Revising & Editing) — reread at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level; revise for clarity and impact for the intended audience; give and receive peer feedback\n- Reading — identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform and persuade; respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts\n- Oral language — communicate in complete sentences, varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning; give descriptions with specific details to actively engage listeners; use precise vocabulary\n\n#### The arc of the term\n\n\n| Weeks | Stage | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weeks 1–2 | Stage 1 — FILL · Issues & evidence | Deconstruct persuasive texts and speeches, build an issue bank, learn the difference between an opinion and a reason, and gather evidence — including the other side's. |\n| Week 3 | Stage 1 → 2 — Plan & audience | Choose the issue, choose the person who could actually say yes, and plan the argument. Oral rehearsal. |\n| Weeks 4–6 | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting | Opening and claim · PEEL reason paragraphs with evidence · counter-argument and the call to action. Full draft by Week 6. |\n| Week 7 | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analyse | Is it convincing? Evidence audit, rhetorical devices, tone check, two goals. |\n| Week 8 | Stage 3 → 4 — SHARE · Feedback + speech begins | Helping Circle with the persuasion lens, then turning the written argument into a spoken script. |\n| Weeks 9–10 | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recraft, publish, deliver | Second draft, edit, best copy, cue cards, delivery craft — then the speeches. |\n\n#### The writing process this term\n\nThird genre, same four stages. By now most students can name FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE without thinking. This term adds one genuinely new idea: the piece does not finish on the page. SHARE ends with a human being listening to you — which changes what SHAPE and SHARE have to check for.\n\n**FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE** — Unchanged for the third term running. What changes is the end point: in Term 1 the reader read it, in Term 2 a buddy read it, and this term someone LISTENS to it and has to be moved by it.\n\n\n| Stage | Question | Hand action | What a writer does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **🪣 FILL** | What do I want to say? | Two cupped hands, like holding water. | I find something that actually bothers me, and then I find out whether I'm right. I collect reasons and evidence — including the evidence for the other side, because I need to know what they'll say. |\n| **🎯 FIND** | Who do I want to say it to? | Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader. | I find the person who could actually say yes — not \"everyone\". Then I find the order that will move THEM: what they already think, what they're worried about, and what would change their mind. |\n| **🔍 SHAPE** | How do I want to say it? | Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it. | I read it as the person who disagrees with me. Where would they stop believing me? Where have I said \"everyone knows\" instead of giving proof? Does my tone make them want to listen, or want to argue? |\n| **🎁 SHARE** | Is it ready to give away? | Open both hands and push them forward, offering. | I test it on real listeners, I recraft it, I turn it into a script I can say out loud — and then I stand up and give it away with my voice, not just my page. |\n\n#### Teaching & recall schedule\n\n\n| Week | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | COLD RECALL — all four stages after the break, no notes. Then re-establish the daily Process Check. |\n| **Week 2** | TRANSFER — FILL for persuasion. In Term 2 you collected facts; now you collect facts AND the other side's facts. |\n| **Week 3** | TRANSFER — FIND for persuasion. \"Everyone\" is not an audience. Who could actually say yes? |\n| **Week 4** | SPACED RECALL — 60-second quick-fire, no notes. |\n| **Week 5** | THREE GENRES — explain each stage as it worked for a story, an explanation and a speech. |\n| **Week 6** | SPACED RECALL + move the peg, justified out loud. |\n| **Week 7** | TRANSFER — SHAPE for persuasion. Read it as the person who disagrees with you. |\n| **Week 8** | TRANSFER — SHARE for persuasion. This term SHARE has a stage in it: your voice is part of the piece. |\n| **Week 9** | THE CYCLE — plus the speaking loop: every rehearsal sends you back to SHAPE. |\n| **Week 10** | ASSESS — explain the process across all three terms, and what stayed the same every time. |\n\n#### Assessment — Week 10 — three genres, one process\n\n> Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Then explain what each stage looked like in all three terms — a story, an explanation and a speech — and say what stayed exactly the same every time.\n\n**A strong answer…**\n\n- Names all four stages in order, with the question that belongs to each\n- Gives a real example of each stage from at least two different terms\n- Names what stays the same across every genre (you always fill before you draft; you always write for a specific someone; you always read it as the person who doesn't already agree; you always test it on a real person before you publish)\n- Knows the process is a cycle, and can say what sends a writer backwards\n\n#### Marking load across the term\n\n\n| When | What | Teacher time |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 2 Fri** | Evidence check — a glance at the case-building page for \"reason + evidence\", not just opinions. | ~10 min for the class |\n| **Week 3 Fri** | Plan gallery — a glance at argument plans during the gallery walk. | ~10 min for the class |\n| **Week 6 Thu** | Draft check: one of three stamps — On track / Needs a conference / Flying. | ~15 sec per student |\n| **Week 8** | You mark nothing — the Helping Circle and the \"were you convinced?\" test do the feedback. | 0 |\n| **Week 10** | Published piece against the rubric (ticks, one glow, one grow) PLUS the delivery row scored live during the speech. | ~3 min per student, most of it during the speeches |\n\n#### Single-point rubric\n\n\n| Success criterion | What a strong piece does | Glow ✓ | Grow → |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Position** | States one clear claim early — the reader knows exactly what is being argued and what is being asked for | | |\n| **Opening** | Hooks the audience (question, statistic, short story or direct address) and leads straight into the claim | | |\n| **Reasons** | Three strong reasons, each in its own paragraph, ordered with the most powerful last | | |\n| **Evidence** | Every reason is backed with something real — a fact, a number, an example, an expert or a story — not \"everyone knows\" | | |\n| **PEEL paragraphs** | Point · Evidence · Explain · Link — each paragraph does all four jobs | | |\n| **Counter-argument** | Names the strongest objection fairly, then answers it | | |\n| **Persuasive language** | Deliberate use of modal verbs, connotation, rule of three, rhetorical questions and direct address — used, not scattered | | |\n| **Tone** | Firm but respectful. Convinces the reader rather than attacking them (Proverbs 15:1) | | |\n| **Call to action** | Ends by asking for something specific that this audience can actually do | | |\n| **Accuracy** | Facts correct and sourced; capitals, commas, paragraphing and known spellings checked | | |\n| **Delivery (speech)** | Audible and clear · paced with real pauses · eye contact · cue cards not a script · finishes on the call to action | | |\n| **Process** | Can name our four stages, explain which one they are in and why, and how the process worked across all three genres | | |\n\n**Overall:** ☐ Working towards ☐ Meeting Phase 2 expectations ☐ Exceeding\n\n#### The Helping Circle\n\n- The writer reads their argument aloud — standing up, because this term it is heading for a stage.\n- The writer asks their own specific question (written in Week 7 Friday).\n- Two stars — each listener names one specific thing that convinced them, using criteria language.\n- One wish — each listener names the moment they doubted, or the objection the writer hasn't answered.\n- The writer says thank you and writes it down. No arguing — the irony of arguing with feedback on a persuasive piece is worth naming out loud.\n\n**Stars — listener prompts**\n\n- The reason that convinced me most was…\n- Your evidence for ____ was really strong because…\n- The line I'd remember afterwards is…\n- You changed my mind about…\n\n**Wishes — listener prompts**\n\n- I started doubting you when…\n- You said ____ but you didn't prove it.\n- Someone who disagrees would say ____ — you haven't answered that yet.\n- Your call to action isn't something I could actually do — could you ask for something smaller?\n- That bit sounded like you were telling me off rather than convincing me.\n\n*We talk about the argument, never the person. Disagreeing with someone's position is completely allowed and is not unkind — attacking the person holding it is. \"I started doubting you when…\" is the most useful sentence in the room.*\n\n#### Differentiation\n\n\n| If a student… | Then… |\n|---|---|\n| Can't find an issue | Use the borrow list, then narrow with the yes-test: who could actually change this? An issue with no decision-maker is a rant, not an argument. |\n| Only has opinions, no evidence | Send them back to FILL for one lesson with one job: find one number, one example or one quotation. A single real piece of evidence transforms a whole draft. |\n| Chooses something enormous (world hunger) | Keep the passion, shrink the ask. \"What could OUR school do about this by the end of term?\" The big issue can stay as the reason; the call to action must be small. |\n| Writes an attack rather than an argument | Proverbs 15:1 and the audience card. Ask: \"Would the person you're writing to want to help you after reading this?\" It's a strategy question, not a manners lecture. |\n| Terrified of speaking | Scale the audience, not the task: read to one partner · to a group of four · to the class from your seat · from the front. Every step counts as delivered. Cue cards and a rehearsed opening line remove most of the fear. |\n| Struggles with transcription | Reduce volume, never thinking. Two strong reasons beats four weak ones, and the speech carries equal weight in this unit's assessment. |\n| Finishes early every week | Write the opposing speech — argue the other side, properly. It is the hardest and most useful extension in persuasive writing. |\n| Can't explain the process | Three terms of examples now exist. Ask what they did in FILL for their narrative, then their explanation, then this. The pattern is easier to see across three than one. |\n\n#### Ten-week overview\n\n\n| Week | Focus | Stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | What Needs to Change? | Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Week 2** | Building a Case | Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Week 3** | Who Can Say Yes? | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Week 4** | Opening and Claim | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Week 5** | Reasons That Land | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Week 6** | The Other Side and the Ask | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Week 7** | Would It Convince Me? | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Week 8** | The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Week 9** | Recraft, Edit, Rehearse | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Week 10** | Publish & Deliver | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n\n*The full ACS unit-planning document (Word and Markdown) downloads from the Writing Focus app toolbar and from the Writing Hub landing page.*\n"},"Week 1: What Needs to Change?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 1 — What Needs to Change?\n\n> **Essential question:** What is worth arguing about?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Persuasion Is Everywhere · Tue: Build Together: How a Speech Works · Wed: Stretch: Complaint or Argument? · Thu: Write: The 30-Second Case · Fri: Share & Reflect: Whose Voice, and For Whom? |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> What is one thing you would change if someone actually listened to you?\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is unfair, wasteful, unsafe or unkind — at school, at home, in your town?\n- Who does it affect, and how many of them are there?\n- Who has the power to change it?\n- What would you ask them to do — exactly?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nunfair · wasteful · unsafe · overlooked · urgent · avoidable · affects · deserve · should · must\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- It doesn't make sense that…\n- Nobody seems to have noticed that…\n- We could fix this by…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Pick one: the bottom field · lunchtime rubbish · not enough library books for Year 5–6 · the length of morning tea · plastic in the tuck shop · road safety at the school gate · screen time rules · what happens to the school's food scraps.\n- **Picture this:** A photo of an overflowing school rubbish bin at 1pm.\n- **Talk it out first:** Complain to your partner for 30 seconds about something at school. Then ask them: \"who could fix that?\"\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Similes for vivid comparison\n- **Tuesday** — Punctuating direct speech\n- **Wednesday** — Sentence openers: -ing clauses\n- **Thursday** — Editing: show, don't tell\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n#### Teacher note\n\nMentor texts this term: one strong written persuasive text (a letter to a newspaper, a school newsletter opinion piece, a campaign page) AND one short speech — 60–90 seconds of a real speech, watched twice. NZ options: a student speech competition winner, a Parliament youth speech, or a short excerpt of a well-known address. Watching a speech in Week 1 makes Week 10 feel inevitable rather than terrifying.\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Persuasion Is Everywhere":{"content":"### Launch: Persuasion Is Everywhere\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to recognise when a text is trying to change our minds, and how it is doing it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — What Needs to Change?  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Similes for vivid comparison |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform, and persuade.\" Writing to persuade: a persuasive text states a position and works on a specific audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSimiles this morning — persuasive writers use them to make a problem vivid: \"the court at lunchtime is like a supermarket carpark on Christmas Eve\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Third term, same four stages. What will we be filling up with this time? (Issues, reasons and evidence.)*\n\n#### I Do — Three Texts, One Question (6 min)\n\nThree short things I've collected. For each one, I'll ask the same question out loud: what does this want me to DO?\n\n> 1. A cereal box. 2. A sign at the pools. 3. A letter in the local paper about a pedestrian crossing.\n\nThe cereal box wants me to buy it. The sign wants me to walk, not run. The letter wants the council to paint a crossing outside a school — and notice, that one names exactly who has to act. That's the difference between complaining and persuading: a complaint says \"this is bad\", persuasion says \"this is bad, and YOU can fix it, and here's how\".\n\n**Tip:** Every persuasive text has a target: someone it wants to move, and something it wants them to do.\n\n#### We Do — Build the \"How Persuasion Works\" Chart (7 min)\n\nWe'll read our mentor letter together and list every technique we can spot. I scribe; you call out. This chart stays up all term.\n\n- Where does the writer say what they actually want?\n- Where do they use a number or a fact instead of a feeling?\n- Where do they speak straight to the reader — \"you\"?\n- Where do they admit the other side has a point?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which technique made you most likely to agree — and why that one?\n\n#### You Do — The Issue Bank (8 min)\n\nNew section at the back of your jotter: THE ISSUE BANK. Things that are unfair, wasteful, unsafe or unkind. No solutions yet — just the problems.\n\n- Write 5 issues from school.\n- Write 3 issues from home, your street or your town.\n- Beside each one, write who it affects.\n- ✦ Bonus: star the one that genuinely annoys you, not the one that sounds most impressive.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read one issue. The class's only response: \"who could fix that?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text forms and genre* — **Purpose of persuasive texts**: NZC Phase 2 Reading: \"identify the audience and purpose of texts that entertain, inform, and persuade.\" Recognising the purpose is the first step to writing for it.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical questioning**: \"Asking curious or critical questions\" — noticing that a text wants something from you, and asking what and why, is critical literacy.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: How a Speech Works":{"content":"### Build Together: How a Speech Works\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning how a spoken argument is built, and how it is different from a written one.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — What Needs to Change?  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Punctuating direct speech |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): \"communicate in complete sentences… varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning.\" Reading: determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPunctuating direct speech this morning — useful today, because a speech often quotes someone: \"Our newsletter said, 'active play improves concentration'.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Why are we watching other people's speeches before writing our own? (To steal how they're built.)*\n\n#### I Do — Watch It Twice (6 min)\n\nWe'll watch 60–90 seconds of a real speech. First time: just listen. Second time: I'll pause it and think aloud about what the speaker is doing.\n\n> [Play the speech excerpt twice — the second time with pauses.]\n\nHear that pause? Three whole seconds. On paper that's a full stop; out loud it's a spotlight. And listen to the sentence lengths — long, long, then four words. A listener can't reread you, so a speech has to be more obvious than an essay: signposts, repetition, short sentences at the important bits.\n\n**Tip:** A reader can go back. A listener cannot. That single fact explains almost everything about speech writing.\n\n#### We Do — Speech vs Written Argument (8 min)\n\nTwo columns on the board: WRITTEN and SPOKEN. What's different? Call it out.\n\n- Written: I can reread it. Spoken: it's gone as soon as it's said.\n- Spoken: signposts out loud — \"there are three reasons… firstly…\".\n- Spoken: shorter sentences, more repetition, more direct address.\n- Spoken: the pause does what a paragraph break does.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** In Week 10 you'll do both — same argument, two forms. Which one do you think will be harder?\n\n#### You Do — Grow the Issue Bank (7 min)\n\nBack to your bank, now that you've seen what a real argument sounds like.\n\n- Add 3 more issues.\n- For each of your top three: write WHO could fix it (a person or a group with actual power).\n- Cross out any issue where you cannot name anybody who could fix it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone lose an issue because nobody could fix it? That's the yes-test doing its job.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Varying structure for effect**: NZC Phase 2: \"communicate in complete sentences, incorporating a range of conjunctions and connectives, and varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning\" — audible in every good speech.\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Structure of an argument**: Determining how a text is structured and how ideas are conveyed — applied to a spoken text, where structure has to be even clearer because a listener cannot go back.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Complaint or Argument?":{"content":"### Stretch: Complaint or Argument?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the difference between complaining about something and arguing for a change.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — What Needs to Change?  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Sentence openers: -ing clauses |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): identify the audience and purpose for a text. Key competency Thinking: \"Argumentation: making a claim and supporting it with evidence.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\n-ing clause openers this morning — \"Standing in the lunch queue for eleven minutes, I started counting.\" A great way to open a speech with a picture.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\n> **⚠ Teach the Process Spotlight below FIRST today.** Teach the spotlight FIRST today. The recall has to happen before anything reminds them of the answer — including the routine itself. Normal order resumes tomorrow.\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. What does a writer DO in this stage — and what will it mean this term?*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Cold recall — third time, no warning (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip up but with no peg on it. Say nothing about it until step 3.\n\n1. \"Four stages, in order, in your jotter. No notes, no looking at the wall. Sixty seconds.\" Everyone writes.\n2. Count hands for four, three, two. It should be much stronger than last term's cold recall — tell them so, and tell them why: \"you've retrieved this about a hundred times now. That's why it's still there.\"\n3. Chant twice, with actions, to reset the routine for the term.\n4. Then the term's new idea.\n   > \"Same four stages again. But this term SHARE ends somewhere new — not with someone reading your work, but with someone LISTENING to you. In Week 10 you stand up and say it out loud to a real audience. Everything we do from now on is heading for that.\"\n5. A student places the peg on Stage 1 and says why.\n\n**Watch for:** Announcing the speech in Week 1 is deliberate. Ten weeks of knowing is far kinder than three days of knowing — and it makes every Friday's standing-up practice feel purposeful rather than random.\n\n#### I Do — Same Feeling, Two Versions (5 min)\n\nOne thing that annoys me, written twice. Listen for what changes between them.\n\n> COMPLAINT: \"The bottom field being closed is so unfair and nobody cares about us.\"\nARGUMENT: \"The bottom field has been closed for eleven weeks since the drainage work finished. Reopening the top half would give 84 children somewhere safe to play at lunchtime.\"\n\nThe first one is about how I feel. The second one is about what happened, how many people it affects and what should change. Both are honest — but only one of them can be answered. A complaint asks for sympathy. An argument asks for a decision.\n\n**Tip:** If your sentence has no number, no name and no ask, it's still a complaint.\n\n#### We Do — Upgrade the Complaints (7 min)\n\nI'll read complaints (no names, from your issue banks). Together we turn each into an argument.\n\n- \"The lunch queue is so slow.\" → what's the number, who's affected, what's the ask?\n- \"There's rubbish everywhere.\" → same three questions.\n- \"We never get enough time to read.\" → same three questions.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the three questions was hardest to answer — and what does that tell us we need?\n\n#### You Do — Upgrade Your Own (9 min)\n\nTake your top three issues and turn each one into an argument sentence.\n\n- For each: [What is happening — with a number if you can] + [what should change].\n- Underline the part you can't prove yet. That's next week's research.\n- ✦ Bonus: which of your three would be the hardest to say no to?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose issue got stronger the moment you added a number? That's the whole lesson.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Argumentation**: \"Argumentation: making a claim and supporting it with evidence\" — named explicitly in the Phase 2 Thinking indicators, and the central skill of this whole unit.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Purpose**: NZC Phase 2: \"identify the audience and purpose for a text.\" A complaint has no audience and no purpose beyond release; an argument has both.\n"},"Thursday: Write: The 30-Second Case":{"content":"### Write: The 30-Second Case\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to state a case out loud, briefly, and to hear where it is weak.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — What Needs to Change?  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: show, don't tell |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners with specific details and precise vocabulary. Speaking an argument aloud exposes the gaps before drafting begins. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nShow, don't tell got edited this morning. In persuasion, \"showing\" is evidence: don't tell me it's unfair, show me the numbers.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. If your case falls over today, what does that mean? (I need more evidence — back to filling.)*\n\n#### I Do — Thirty Seconds, Standing Up (5 min)\n\nI'll stand and make my case in 30 seconds. Count how many times I say something I can't back up.\n\n> \"The bottom field should reopen. It's been closed since March, the work finished in June, and 84 of us share one court. I think someone's going to get hurt — actually, I know they will, because it's already happened.\"\n\nTwo things to notice. First: I stood up, because everything this term ends standing up. Second: I said \"I think someone's going to get hurt\" and then caught myself, because \"I think\" is the sound of an argument with no evidence behind it. Next week I'll go and find out how many collisions there've actually been.\n\n**Tip:** Count your \"I think\"s. Each one is a piece of evidence you haven't found yet.\n\n#### We Do — The Listener's Job (4 min)\n\nSet the roles. The listener is not being nice today; the listener is being useful.\n\n- Listen for \"I think\", \"everyone knows\", \"it's obvious\".\n- Listen for a number, a name or an example — did you hear ANY?\n- At the end, ask one question: \"how do you know?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is \"how do you know?\" more useful than \"I agree\"?\n\n#### You Do — Stand and State It (12 min)\n\nIn pairs, standing. 30 seconds each: what's wrong, who it affects, what should change. Then swap.\n\n- Stand up and make your case in 30 seconds.\n- Listener: count the unproved claims and ask \"how do you know?\"\n- Write every unproved claim in your jotter as a research question.\n- Swap and repeat.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone should have two to five things to prove. That's your job for next week.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions… with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — applied here to a short spoken case.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new\" — standing up and stating a position, briefly, on day four of the term makes Week 10 far less frightening.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Whose Voice, and For Whom?":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Whose Voice, and For Whom?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that persuasive writing is a way of serving people, not just winning.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — What Needs to Change?  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Issues and how persuasion works |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): audience and purpose. Key competency Participating and contributing: taking responsible action within a community. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups gave us similes, speech punctuation, -ing openers and showing rather than telling — four tools a speech leans on hard.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Name one thing you collected this week that you didn't have on Monday.*\n\n#### I Do — Speaking Up for Someone Else (4 min)\n\nOne question before you choose your issue, and it changes what most people pick.\n\n> Proverbs 31:8–9 — \"Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves… defend the rights of the poor and needy.\"\n\nMost of us naturally argue for the thing that annoys US. That's allowed. But notice what happens to an argument when the person you're serving isn't yourself: it gets stronger, because you're not just asking for what you want. This term's value is Manaaki — serving faithfully with our gifts. Words are the gift. Who could yours serve?\n\n**Tip:** Ask: who else benefits if I win this? If the answer is \"only me\", look again.\n\n#### We Do — Who Does This Serve? (6 min)\n\nWe'll take three issues from the class bank and ask the same question of each.\n\n- Who benefits if this changes?\n- Is there anyone in this school who can't ask for this themselves?\n- Could this issue serve someone younger, quieter or newer than you?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did any issue get stronger when you thought about who else it helps?\n\n#### You Do — Shortlist and Reflect (11 min)\n\nNarrow the bank to a shortlist, then reflect in your jotter.\n\n- Choose your top three issues.\n- For each: who could say yes, and who does it serve?\n- Jotter: \"The issue I keep coming back to is… because…\"\n- ✦ For your jotter (faith prompt): where did I see someone this week who needed a voice?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week you find out whether you're actually right. Bring your unproved claims.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Writing that acts**: NZC Phase 2 writers choose genre and register to achieve a purpose in the world — here, an actual change requested of an actual person.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Responsible action**: Contributing to healthy communities by taking responsible and critical action — the ACS value Manaaki expressed as writing.\n"}}},"Week 2: Building a Case":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 2 — Building a Case\n\n> **Essential question:** What's the difference between an opinion and an argument?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Opinion, Reason, Evidence · Tue: Build Together: Four Kinds of Evidence · Wed: Stretch: The Other Side · Thu: Write: Evidence Hunt · Fri: Share & Reflect: The Case Test |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Prove it. Find the evidence that turns your opinion into an argument — and find out what the other side would say.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is my main reason — and is it a reason, or just my feeling again?\n- What evidence backs it up: a number, an example, an expert or a story?\n- Who disagrees with me, and what is their best point?\n- Can I answer their best point honestly?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nevidence · reason · statistic · example · expert · survey · source · objection · however · admittedly\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- According to…\n- Last term alone,…\n- Some people argue that… but…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** The four kinds of evidence: a NUMBER (count it yourself if you have to) · an EXAMPLE (something that actually happened) · an EXPERT (someone who knows) · a STORY (one person it happened to).\n- **Picture this:** A tally chart on a clipboard.\n- **Talk it out first:** Have your partner argue the OPPOSITE of your position for 60 seconds. Write down everything they say.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Personification\n- **Tuesday** — Active and passive voice\n- **Wednesday** — Joining ideas with semicolons\n- **Thursday** — Editing dialogue punctuation\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Opinion, Reason, Evidence":{"content":"### Launch: Opinion, Reason, Evidence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell the difference between an opinion, a reason and evidence.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Building a Case  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Personification |\n| **NZC link** | Key competency Thinking (Phase 2): \"Argumentation: making a claim and supporting it with evidence.\" Writing processes: gathering and recording information. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPersonification this morning — persuasive writers use it carefully: \"the field sits empty behind a fence, waiting\". Powerful, but one per speech.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Last term you collected facts. What extra thing do you have to collect this term? (The other side's facts.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — FILL when you're arguing (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 1. Board space for two columns.\n\n1. \"Term 2, FILL meant collecting facts. Who remembers the rule about facts?\" (Two sources. Own words.) \"This term FILL has one extra job, and it's the one nobody does.\"\n2. Two columns on the board: MY EVIDENCE / THEIR EVIDENCE. \"You have to collect the other side's case as well as your own.\"\n3. Ask why. Let them wrestle with it. Land on both reasons: you can't answer an objection you haven't heard, AND you might turn out to be wrong — which is not a disaster, it's what evidence is for.\n   > \"An opinion is where you START. It is not what you hand in.\"\n4. Finish with Proverbs 31:8–9 and the term's value: Manaaki. \"Whose case are you filling up with? Is there anyone in this school who can't argue for themselves?\"\n\n**Watch for:** The idea that changing your mind is a success, not a failure, needs saying explicitly and more than once. Praise loudly the first student who says \"actually, my evidence went against me\".\n\n#### I Do — The Three-Layer Test (6 min)\n\nOne idea, sorted into three layers. Watch which layer does the actual work.\n\n> OPINION: \"The bottom field should be open.\"\nREASON: \"Because the court is too crowded at lunchtime.\"\nEVIDENCE: \"Our duty teacher logged nine collisions on the court in one week.\"\n\nEveryone in this room can produce the opinion. Most people can produce the reason. Almost nobody bothers with the third one — and the third one is the only layer that can't be argued with. An opinion invites disagreement. Evidence ends it.\n\n**Tip:** Opinion = what I want. Reason = why. Evidence = proof. You need all three, in that order.\n\n#### We Do — Sort and Supply (8 min)\n\nStatements on the board. Sort each into opinion, reason or evidence — then supply the missing layers.\n\n- \"School lunches are terrible.\"\n- \"Because there's never a vegetarian option.\"\n- \"Last Thursday 23 of the 84 lunches ordered were sent back.\"\n- Now: for the first one, what evidence would we need?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Where could you actually GET that evidence, in this school, this week?\n\n#### You Do — Start the Case File (7 min)\n\nThree columns in your jotter: REASON · EVIDENCE I HAVE · EVIDENCE I NEED.\n\n- Write your three strongest reasons.\n- Beside each: what evidence do you already have?\n- Beside each: what evidence do you still need, and where would you get it?\n- Star the reason with the weakest evidence — that's Thursday's job.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose best reason has no evidence at all? Good — now you know what to go and find.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Claim and evidence**: \"Argumentation: making a claim and supporting it with evidence\" — the Phase 2 indicator this entire term is built to develop.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Gathering evidence**: NZC Phase 2: students draw on acquired knowledge as well as their own experience, recording it in notes rather than copying it.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Four Kinds of Evidence":{"content":"### Build Together: Four Kinds of Evidence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the four kinds of evidence and which one suits our argument.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Building a Case  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Active and passive voice |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): support a position with evidence appropriate to the audience. Writing processes: make notes, reducing information to key words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nActive and passive voice this morning — \"nine collisions were logged\" versus \"our duty teacher logged nine collisions\". The active version names who, and is harder to dismiss.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Is a survey you run yourself real evidence? (Yes — often the strongest kind, because it's about your own school.)*\n\n#### I Do — Number, Example, Expert, Story (6 min)\n\nFour kinds of evidence for the same reason. Watch which one hits hardest — and note that it isn't always the number.\n\n> NUMBER: \"84 children share one court.\"\nEXAMPLE: \"Last Tuesday the court was closed for a Year 6 game and 60 children had nowhere to go.\"\nEXPERT: \"Our school newsletter said active play improves concentration.\"\nSTORY: \"Ana is seven. She stopped going outside at lunchtime because she kept getting knocked over.\"\n\nThe number proves the scale. The story makes someone care. Use both — the number stops them arguing, the story stops them forgetting. And notice the expert I chose: the school's OWN newsletter. Quoting your audience back to themselves is the most powerful evidence there is.\n\n**Tip:** Best combination: one number and one story. Everything else is a bonus.\n\n#### We Do — Find Four for One Reason (8 min)\n\nTake one class issue. Together, work out what each of the four kinds of evidence would be, and where we'd get it.\n\n- What NUMBER could we count ourselves this week?\n- What EXAMPLE has actually happened that we could describe?\n- Which EXPERT would our audience actually believe?\n- Whose STORY is this, and would they let us tell it?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the four is easiest to get by Thursday? Start there.\n\n#### You Do — Plan Your Evidence Hunt (7 min)\n\nFor your own argument: what will you go and get, and how?\n\n- Write one NUMBER you could count or find, and how you'd get it.\n- Write one EXAMPLE or STORY, and who you'd need to ask.\n- Write one EXPERT source your audience would trust.\n- Turn each into a job with a day: \"Wednesday — count bins at 1pm.\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose evidence hunt needs permission from an adult? See me now, and I'll help you ask.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Notes from sources**: NZC Phase 2: \"make notes, reducing information into key words and phrases\" — and, in persuasion, recording where each fact came from so it can be cited.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Evaluating evidence**: Asking critical questions of a source: who says so, how do they know, and would my audience believe them?\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Other Side":{"content":"### Stretch: The Other Side\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to find the strongest argument against us, and to take it seriously.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Building a Case  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Joining ideas with semicolons |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"acknowledge others' responses and respond respectfully to differences.\" Writing to persuade: address the counter-argument rather than ignoring it. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSemicolons this morning — handy for balancing two sides: \"The grass does need protecting; the top half was never dug up.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Why would I collect evidence for the OTHER side? (Because I have to answer it — and because I might be wrong.)*\n\n#### I Do — Argue Against Myself (6 min)\n\nI'll make the best possible case AGAINST my own position. Watch how seriously I take it.\n\n> \"The field was closed for a reason. The new grass cost the school money and if 84 children run on it in July it will be mud by August, and then nobody gets a field for a year instead of a term.\"\n\nThat's a good argument. It's not stupid and I'm not going to call it stupid — because the moment I sneer at the other side, my audience stops trusting me. Instead I'm going to answer it: the top half was never dug up. Notice that the objection actually made my argument BETTER, because it forced me to be more specific about what I'm asking for.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't argue the other side convincingly, you don't understand your own argument yet.\n\n#### We Do — Steelman It (7 min)\n\nTake a class issue. Everyone argues the OTHER side, as strongly as they can. No sarcasm allowed.\n\n- What would the person who has to say yes be worried about?\n- What does it cost — money, time, safety, fairness to someone else?\n- What's the best reason to say no that has nothing to do with laziness?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did the argument get weaker or stronger once we'd heard the other side properly?\n\n#### You Do — The Opposition Page (9 min)\n\nA full page in your jotter, written as if you were the person who says no.\n\n- Write the three best reasons AGAINST your position.\n- Star the one you'd find hardest to answer.\n- Write one honest sentence: \"They have a point about ____ .\"\n- Then: what could you offer or change so that objection loses its force?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone's position actually change today? That's not losing. That's what evidence is for.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Making connections and interpretations* — **Respecting difference**: NZC Phase 2: \"acknowledge others' responses and respond respectfully to differences\" — the reading indicator that underpins fair counter-argument.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Perspective-taking**: \"Show respect for others' ways of being · listen carefully and paraphrase ideas of others\" — you cannot answer an objection you have not honestly understood.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Evidence Hunt":{"content":"### Write: Evidence Hunt\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to gather our own evidence and record where it came from.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Building a Case  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing dialogue punctuation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): make notes, reducing information to key words; set and work towards specific goals based on reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nDialogue punctuation got edited this morning — you'll need it the moment you quote someone you interviewed.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Last big collecting day. What has to be true by tomorrow? (Every reason has evidence attached.)*\n\n#### I Do — Count It Yourself (4 min)\n\nThe most underrated move in persuasive writing: collect your own data.\n\n> A tally sheet. Three columns. Ten minutes at lunchtime. \"Children turned away from the court: ||||  ||||  |||\"\n\nI didn't need the internet. I needed a clipboard and ten minutes. And this evidence is better than anything I could have found online, because it's about THIS school, THIS term — and the person I'm writing to can't say \"that's from overseas\".\n\n**Tip:** Evidence you collected yourself is the hardest kind to dismiss.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Job (3 min)\n\nFast round the room: what evidence are you getting today, and how?\n\n- \"Counting how many books are on the Year 5–6 shelf.\"\n- \"Asking Mr Patel how much the recycling costs.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone who says \"I'll look it up\" — what exactly are you looking up, and would our audience believe that source?\n\n#### You Do — Gather (15 min)\n\nWork your list. Record every piece of evidence WITH its source. I'll conference with anyone stuck.\n\n- Collect the evidence on your list — count, ask, read, survey.\n- Record each piece with where it came from and the date.\n- Fill any gap in the REASON column with the evidence you found.\n- If a piece of evidence contradicts you, write it down anyway. Honestly.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Did anyone find evidence that made their argument weaker? Tell me — that takes real courage and it makes the final piece stronger.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Working towards goals**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes\" — the evidence jobs written on Tuesday become today's goals.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Self-direction**: Working through a self-made list, including asking an adult or peer for information, without step-by-step prompting.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: The Case Test":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: The Case Test\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to test whether our case would stand up to someone who disagrees.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Building a Case  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Reasons, evidence and the other side |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): make comments that encourage discussion, ask clarifying questions, and consider the impact of your words on others. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week: personification, active voice, semicolons, dialogue punctuation. All four turn up in a strong argument.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FILL. What's next, and what will change? (FIND — my audience and my order.)*\n\n#### I Do — Polite and Relentless (3 min)\n\nHow to challenge someone's argument without being unkind. Three questions, and you can use them all term.\n\n> \"How do you know that?\" · \"What would someone who disagrees say?\" · \"What exactly are you asking for?\"\n\nNone of those are rude. All three are hard to answer if the case is thin. And when someone asks you them, the correct response is not to get defensive — it's to write the question down, because your real audience will ask exactly the same thing.\n\n**Tip:** A friendly challenge now beats a real \"no\" later.\n\n#### We Do — Practise on Me (4 min)\n\nI'll state my case in 30 seconds. You use the three questions on me.\n\n- \"How do you know that?\"\n- \"What would someone who disagrees say?\"\n- \"What exactly are you asking for?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which question was hardest for me to answer? What does that tell me to do next?\n\n#### You Do — The Case Test, in Trios (13 min)\n\nGroups of three. One states the case standing up; two challenge with the three questions. Rotate.\n\n- State your case in 60 seconds, standing.\n- Take all three questions. Write down any you can't answer well.\n- Rotate until all three have gone.\n- Jotter: \"The question I couldn't answer was… so I need to…\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week you choose your issue for good, and you choose the person you're going to convince.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Clarifying questions**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions\" — here in their most demanding form: challenging a claim politely.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Argumentation**: Making a claim and supporting it with evidence, under questioning from a peer.\n"}}},"Week 3: Who Can Say Yes?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?\n\n> **Essential question:** Who am I trying to move, and what will move them?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Yes-Test · Tue: Build Together: The Claim Sentence · Wed: Stretch: Building the Plan · Thu: Write: Rehearse It on a Real \"No\" · Fri: Share & Reflect: Would You Say Yes? |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Name the person who could actually say yes — then plan the argument that would move THEM.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Who has the power to change this — a person, not \"the school\"?\n- What do they already think about it?\n- What are they worried about?\n- What exactly am I asking them to do, and is it something they could do this term?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nshould · must · could · propose · request · consider · recommend · urge · allow · agree\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Mrs ____ , …\n- I am asking you to…\n- I understand that…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** The four-box plan: MY CLAIM (what I want) → THREE REASONS (each with evidence) → THE OBJECTION (and my answer) → MY ASK (small, specific, this term).\n- **Picture this:** A letter addressed to one named person, not \"To whom it may concern\".\n- **Talk it out first:** Have your partner play the decision-maker and say no. Ask them why. That \"why\" is your counter-argument.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Metaphors\n- **Tuesday** — Modal verbs\n- **Wednesday** — Sentence openers: -ed clauses\n- **Thursday** — Editing for active voice\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Yes-Test":{"content":"### Launch: The Yes-Test\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose an audience who could actually grant what we are asking for.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Metaphors |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): \"identify the audience and purpose for a text\"; \"plan and write with the audience in mind, selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nMetaphors this morning — one good metaphor can carry a whole argument: \"our court at lunchtime is a motorway with no lanes.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who is \"everyone\"? (Nobody. Name a person.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — FIND when you're arguing (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg moved onto Stage 2 before the lesson.\n\n1. \"FIND has always meant two things — the reader and the order. Both change again this term.\"\n2. THE READER: Term 1 was the school community, Term 2 was one eight-year-old, and this term it's the one person who can say yes. Write \"EVERYONE\" on the board and cross it out. \"Everyone is not an audience. Everyone means nobody.\"\n3. THE ORDER: not an arc, not a flow chart — a case. Claim, reasons, the objection, the ask. And the order matters differently: \"which reason goes last, and why?\" (The strongest — because the last thing is what they remember.)\n4. Hand action, whole class: \"FIND still points outwards. This term you're pointing at one specific adult who has the power to change something.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Students default to \"everyone\" because it feels safest. Push every single student to name a person or a role today — the whole term's quality depends on it.\n\n#### I Do — Three Audiences, Three Versions (6 min)\n\nSame request, three different people. Listen to what changes — and what stays exactly the same.\n\n> TO A FRIEND: \"The field should be open, it's ridiculous.\"\nTO THE PRINCIPAL: \"Mrs Tui, could we reopen the top half of the bottom field this term?\"\nTO THE BOARD: \"We are requesting that the undug section of the lower field be reopened for lunchtime use.\"\n\nThe claim never changes — the field should open. Everything else does: how formal I am, how much background I give, what I think they're worried about. And notice the middle one names her. A letter to a person gets answered. A letter to \"the school\" gets filed.\n\n**Tip:** If you can't name your audience, you don't have one yet.\n\n#### We Do — Run the Yes-Test (7 min)\n\nClass issues on the board. For each, we work out exactly who could say yes — and whether we can reach them.\n\n- Who has the power to change this? Name a role, then a person.\n- Could a Year 5–6 student actually get a message to that person?\n- If the real decision-maker is unreachable, who's the next best?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What happens to an argument aimed at somebody who can't say yes?\n\n#### You Do — Choose and Commit (8 min)\n\nToday you choose your issue for the term. It goes on a fresh jotter page — you'll read it again in Week 10.\n\n- \"My issue is…\" and three reasons it matters.\n- \"The person who could say yes is…\" — a name, or a role you can name.\n- \"What I am asking them to do is…\" — one sentence.\n- ✦ Check: could they do it this term? If not, make the ask smaller.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room: everyone says their ask in one sentence. Fast. Hearing 25 asks in 90 seconds is genuinely energising.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Identifying the audience**: NZC Phase 2: \"identify the audience and purpose for a text… plan and write with the audience in mind.\" In persuasion the audience is not a reader in general — it is the one person who can grant the request.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Register**: NZC Phase 2: selecting \"the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice to best communicate the intended meaning\" — a principal, a council and a parent each require a different register.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Claim Sentence":{"content":"### Build Together: The Claim Sentence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write one clear sentence that states our position and our request.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Modal verbs |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): state a clear position. Sentence structures: modal verbs used to signal degrees of obligation and possibility. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nModal verbs this morning — and this is the lesson they were made for. Should, must, could: each one asks for a different amount.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Say your audience's name out loud. Would they understand every word you write today?*\n\n#### I Do — How Hard Should I Push? (6 min)\n\nSame claim, four modal verbs. Listen to how the relationship with my audience changes each time.\n\n> \"The top half of the field COULD be reopened this term.\"\n\"…SHOULD be reopened this term.\"\n\"…MUST be reopened this term.\"\n\"…OUGHT TO be reopened this term.\"\n\n\"Could\" is polite but weak — it lets them say \"yes, it could\" and do nothing. \"Must\" is strong, and to a principal it might sound like a demand from a ten-year-old, which loses me the room. I'm choosing \"should\", and then I'm making the ask itself concrete. Strong claim, respectful verb. That's the balance all term.\n\n**Tip:** Push hard with your evidence, not with your verbs.\n\n#### We Do — Write Claims Together (8 min)\n\nThree class issues. For each, we write a claim sentence with the right modal verb for the audience.\n\n- [Thing] should [change] because [one-line reason].\n- Now say it to a principal. Does the verb still fit?\n- Now cut it to under 20 words.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which verb would you use writing to a friend? To the Prime Minister? Why the difference?\n\n#### You Do — Your Claim Sentence (7 min)\n\nThree versions, three modal verbs. Then choose.\n\n- Write your claim with \"could\", then \"should\", then \"must\".\n- Read all three to a partner playing your audience. Which one would they listen to?\n- Circle your choice and copy it onto your plan page.\n- ✦ Check: under 25 words, and it names what you want.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your claim. If your neighbour can't repeat back what you're asking for, it's not clear enough yet.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Modal verbs**: Modal verbs (should, must, could, ought to) carry the force of a claim; Phase 2 writers choose them deliberately for effect and audience.\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **Stating a position**: A persuasive text states its position early and unmistakably, so the audience knows what is being asked before the reasons arrive.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Building the Plan":{"content":"### Stretch: Building the Plan\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to order our reasons so the argument gets stronger as it goes.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Sentence openers: -ed clauses |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): \"use organisers to organise and plan writing.\" Writing to persuade: reasons sequenced for cumulative effect, with a counter-argument and a call to action. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\n-ed clause openers this morning — \"Faced with a crowded court every lunchtime, 84 children have nowhere to go.\" A strong way to start a reason paragraph.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Two things to find — name them. (My audience, and my order.)*\n\n#### I Do — Save the Best for Last (6 min)\n\nMy three reasons, and me deciding what order to put them in. Listen for why the strongest goes last.\n\n> R1: the field isn't being used for anything. R2: the crowding is causing injuries. R3: playing outside helps us learn.\nMY ORDER: 1 (easy to agree with) → 3 (they already believe this) → 2 (the one that would actually change their mind).\n\nA listener remembers the last thing best. So I open with the reason nobody can argue with, warm them up with the one they already agree with, and land on the injuries — because that's the one that makes doing nothing feel uncomfortable. Ordering isn't decoration. It's strategy.\n\n**Tip:** Strongest reason LAST. Easiest-to-agree-with reason FIRST.\n\n#### We Do — Order Someone's Reasons (7 min)\n\nA volunteer reads their three reasons. Together we decide the order and justify it.\n\n- Which reason is impossible to disagree with?\n- Which one does the audience already believe?\n- Which one would actually change their mind?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Does the order change if the audience changes?\n\n#### You Do — The Four-Box Plan (8 min)\n\nFour boxes, key words only. This plan is what you draft from next week.\n\n- BOX 1 — my claim and my ask.\n- BOX 2 — three reasons, each with its evidence, in order (strongest last).\n- BOX 3 — the objection, and my honest answer to it.\n- BOX 4 — my call to action: exactly what I want them to do, and when.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Partner check: can they tell your whole argument back to you using only your boxes?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Using organisers**: NZC Phase 2: \"use organisers (e.g., graphic organisers or mnemonics) to organise and plan writing\" — here a four-box argument plan.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Cause and effect**: \"Cause and effect thinking: working forwards or backwards to/from possible causes and consequences\" — used to predict how an audience will respond to each reason.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Rehearse It on a Real \"No\"":{"content":"### Write: Rehearse It on a Real \"No\"\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to test our argument aloud on someone playing the person who could refuse.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing for active voice |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): communicate in complete sentences with connectives; respond to questions. Writing processes: revise the plan in response to audience feedback. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing for active voice this morning — \"the field was closed\" hides who closed it. In an argument, name the actor when it helps you and use passive when naming would sound like blame.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Why rehearse before drafting? (Because a no I hear today saves me a week of writing the wrong thing.)*\n\n#### I Do — Get Told No (4 min)\n\nI'll make my case; a student plays my principal and says no. Watch what I do with the reason they give.\n\n> \"No — because the new grass won't survive it.\" → I write that down. That objection is now Box 3.\n\nI didn't argue back. I wrote it down. Every \"no\" you can collect NOW is an objection you can answer in the draft — and an objection you answer in advance is one your real audience never gets to use.\n\n**Tip:** Collect the noes. They're free practice.\n\n#### We Do — How to Play the Audience (4 min)\n\nSet the roles before we start. The partner's job is to be difficult, but realistic.\n\n- Listen to the whole case without interrupting.\n- Then say no, and give a real reason — the reason that person would actually give.\n- Ask: \"what exactly are you asking me to do?\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is a realistic no more useful than a harsh one?\n\n#### You Do — Rehearse and Revise (13 min)\n\nIn pairs, both directions, then fix your plan while it's fresh.\n\n- Present your case from the four-box plan (about 90 seconds).\n- Partner plays your audience: listens, says no with a reason, asks what you're asking for.\n- Swap.\n- 4 minutes: update Box 3 and Box 4 based on what you heard.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if the 'no' you got is now written in Box 3. That's exactly what today was for.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Interpersonal communication* — **Responding to others**: NZC Phase 2: \"make comments that encourage discussion, and ask clarifying questions… consider the impact of their words on others\" — practised in role, under mild pressure.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising the plan**: NZC Phase 2: revise \"using audience and peer feedback\" — the cheapest possible revision happens here, before a word is drafted.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Would You Say Yes?":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Would You Say Yes?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge whether an argument would actually persuade its audience.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — Who Can Say Yes?  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Audience and plan |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): peer feedback used to improve a text for its intended audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — metaphors, modal verbs, -ed openers, active voice — are all in your plan already. Point at one.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What have we found this week? (The person, the order, and the objection.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Three Gallery Questions (3 min)\n\nThree questions only at a plan gallery, and they're different from last term's.\n\n> 1. Would this convince the person it's aimed at? · 2. Which reason is weakest? · 3. Is the ask something they could actually do?\n\n\"Which reason is weakest\" is the one people avoid asking, and it's the most useful. Every argument has a weakest reason — knowing which one it is means you can strengthen it or cut it. Three strong reasons beat four with a soft one in the middle.\n\n**Tip:** A soft reason weakens the strong ones beside it. Cutting is allowed.\n\n#### We Do — Model on One Plan (5 min)\n\nOne plan on the visualiser. We run the three questions and give real answers.\n\n- \"I'd say yes — but only because of reason three.\"\n- \"Reason two is the weakest; there's no evidence.\"\n- \"The ask is too big — they'd need a board meeting.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which comment would help this writer most on Monday?\n\n#### You Do — Gallery Walk (12 min)\n\nPlans on desks, sticky note beside each. Visit three only.\n\n- On each note: would you say yes, which reason is weakest, is the ask doable?\n- Back at your desk: read your notes and mark the reason you'll strengthen first.\n- Get your plan, case file and audience card ready — Monday you draft.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Three weeks of preparation. On Monday it turns into writing, and it's going to be fast.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — at the planning stage, where feedback is cheapest to act on.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Evaluating an argument**: Judging a claim against its evidence, and predicting how a specific audience would respond.\n"}}},"Week 4: Opening and Claim":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 4 — Opening and Claim\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I open so my audience actually listens?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Four Ways to Open · Tue: Build Together: Signposting the Argument · Wed: Stretch: PEEL · Thu: Write: Drafting the Opening and Reason One · Fri: Share & Reflect: Openings, Standing Up |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Open with something they can't ignore — then tell them exactly what you want.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the one number, image or moment that shows this problem?\n- Could I open with a question? A statistic? A story? Their own name?\n- Where does my claim go — and is it in the first five sentences?\n- Would my audience keep listening after my first line?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nfirstly · furthermore · however · clearly · in fact · consider · imagine · surely · therefore · most importantly\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- There are 84 of us and one court.\n- Imagine standing in the same queue every lunchtime for eleven weeks.\n- Mrs ____ , I am writing to ask you to…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Open with your best number, then your claim. Two sentences. It works every time and it never sounds silly.\n- **Picture this:** The opening line of your mentor letter, underlined.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your first sentence to three different people. Keep the one that made someone look up.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Connectives for persuasive writing\n- **Tuesday** — Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions\n- **Wednesday** — PEEL paragraphs for persuasive writing\n- **Thursday** — Editing PEEL paragraphs\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Four Ways to Open":{"content":"### Launch: Four Ways to Open\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to open a persuasive piece in a way that makes the audience want to keep listening.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and Claim  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Connectives for persuasive writing |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): engage the audience from the first line and state the position early. Writing craft: deliberate word choice for effect. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nConnectives for persuasive writing this morning — firstly, furthermore, however, most importantly. They're the signposts your listener needs.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. We're drafting now — which stage does drafting live in? (Still FIND: first ideas, aimed at one person.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Openings, One Argument (8 min)\n\nFour ways into the same case. I'll write all four, then choose one and tell you why.\n\n> STATISTIC: \"There are 84 children in Years 5 and 6, and one court between us.\"\nQUESTION: \"Where are 84 children supposed to play at lunchtime?\"\nSTORY: \"Ana is seven. She stopped going outside because she kept getting knocked over.\"\nDIRECT ADDRESS: \"Mrs Tui, I'd like to ask you for eleven weeks of your field back.\"\n\nThe story is the most moving, but I'm writing to a principal who has thirty things to read this week — so I'm choosing the statistic, because it's fast, it's checkable, and it makes the scale undeniable in one line. I'll use Ana later, where it can do its work on someone who is already listening.\n\n**Tip:** Choose the opening that suits your AUDIENCE, not the one you like best.\n\n#### We Do — Rescue a Weak Opening (6 min)\n\nA weak opening on the board. We rewrite it four ways.\n\n- \"I think the field should be open because it is unfair.\"\n- Now as a statistic.\n- Now as a question.\n- Now as direct address to a named person.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version would survive being read by a busy adult?\n\n#### You Do — Write Three Openings (7 min)\n\nThree different types. Not three versions of the same one.\n\n- Write three openings — different types.\n- Read all three to a partner playing your audience.\n- Partner picks the one that made them look up.\n- Write your claim sentence straight after the winner.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your opening line only. Class: did you want the next sentence?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **Engaging the audience**: A persuasive text must earn attention before it can earn agreement; the opening is chosen for the specific audience, not defaulted to.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Structures for impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Signposting the Argument":{"content":"### Build Together: Signposting the Argument\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell our audience where the argument is going, so they can follow it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and Claim  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Coordinating vs subordinating conjunctions |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): \"begin to use linking words and phrases\"; structure signalled explicitly for the reader or listener. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCoordinating versus subordinating conjunctions this morning — \"although\" and \"because\" do more argumentative work than \"and\" ever will.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who is this paragraph for? Picture them reading it.*\n\n#### I Do — Tell Them the Plan (6 min)\n\nWatch me tell my audience exactly what's coming. It feels obvious on paper; out loud it's essential.\n\n> \"There are three reasons the top half should reopen: it is not being used, the crowding is unsafe, and outdoor play helps us learn. Firstly, …\"\n\nI've just told them the whole argument in one sentence, and I haven't lost anything by doing it. A listener who knows there are three reasons can follow all three. A listener who doesn't know is still wondering where the first one went. Signpost, then deliver.\n\n**Tip:** Say how many reasons there are. Then number them out loud as you go.\n\n#### We Do — Build the Signpost Bank (8 min)\n\nWe'll sort the class's connectives into jobs on the board. Which does what?\n\n- STARTING: firstly · to begin with\n- ADDING: furthermore · what is more · in addition\n- TURNING: however · although · on the other hand\n- LANDING: most importantly · above all · finally\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which group is the one everybody forgets? (Usually \"turning\" — and it's where counter-arguments live.)\n\n#### You Do — Signpost Your Argument (7 min)\n\nWrite the sentence that tells your audience what's coming, then label your plan with connectives.\n\n- Write your \"there are three reasons…\" sentence.\n- Write the connective you'll use at the start of each reason.\n- Choose your \"landing\" connective for the strongest reason.\n- ✦ Bonus: choose the \"turning\" word you'll use for the objection.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your signpost sentence. If it takes more than one breath, it's too long.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to inform/persuade* — **Linking words and phrases**: NZC Phase 2: \"begin to use linking words and phrases\" — in persuasion these carry the structure: firstly, furthermore, however, most importantly.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Conjunctions**: NZC Phase 2: coordinating and subordinating conjunctions used to join ideas and show relationships between them.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: PEEL":{"content":"### Stretch: PEEL\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build a paragraph that makes a point, proves it, explains it and links on.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and Claim  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | PEEL paragraphs for persuasive writing |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — drafting (Phase 2): \"write non-fiction paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences, and a concluding sentence.\" PEEL is that structure, named for persuasion. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPEEL paragraphs this morning — this lesson is that warm-up, put straight to work on your own argument.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages from memory, no strip, no notes.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall — quick-fire (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Cover the wall strip before they arrive. No warning.\n\n1. \"No notes, no wall. Four stages, in order, with actions. Go.\" Twenty seconds.\n2. \"One person — the whole process in under twenty seconds. I'm timing.\" Choose someone who hasn't done it before; the class counts down.\n3. Uncover. One line: \"Third term. You're carrying this without effort now — that's what a hundred retrievals does.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Fast and playful. If a student stalls, give the first word only.\n\n#### I Do — All Four Jobs (5 min)\n\nOne reason, one paragraph, four jobs. Watch me label each sentence as I write it.\n\n> \"Secondly, the crowding is unsafe. [POINT] Our duty teacher logged nine collisions on the court in one week last month. [EVIDENCE] When 84 children share a space built for one netball game, the youngest get pushed to the edges — and it is Year 1s who end up at the sick bay. [EXPLAIN] If we had the top half of the field, that pressure would disappear overnight. [LINK]\"\n\nMost students write P and E and stop. The EXPLAIN is where the persuading actually happens — it's where I tell the reader what the evidence MEANS. Nine collisions is a number; \"the youngest get pushed to the edges\" is the reason a principal will act.\n\n**Tip:** Evidence without explanation is just a fact. Explain what it means for the person you're writing to.\n\n#### We Do — Build a PEEL Together (8 min)\n\nTake one reason from a volunteer's plan. We write the paragraph as a class — I scribe, you supply each job.\n\n- POINT — what's this paragraph's reason, in one sentence?\n- EVIDENCE — what's the proof?\n- EXPLAIN — so what? Why does that matter to our audience?\n- LINK — how do we hand over to the next reason?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the four jobs was hardest — and is it always the same one?\n\n#### You Do — Your First PEEL (8 min)\n\nReason one from your plan, as a full PEEL paragraph.\n\n- Write the paragraph.\n- Label each sentence P, E, E or L in the margin.\n- Any job missing? Write it now.\n- ✦ Check: is your EXPLAIN longer than your EVIDENCE? It usually should be.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who's missing an E? Which one — evidence, or explain?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Paragraph structure**: NZC Phase 2: \"write non-fiction paragraphs that have a topic sentence, detail sentences, and a concluding sentence\" — Point, Evidence, Explain, Link.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Claim and evidence**: \"Argumentation: making a claim and supporting it with evidence\" — PEEL is that competency turned into a paragraph shape.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Drafting the Opening and Reason One":{"content":"### Write: Drafting the Opening and Reason One\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to draft steadily from a plan, keeping our audience in mind.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and Claim  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing PEEL paragraphs |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): transfer ideas from planning organisers into sentences; reread at paragraph level; write with increasing stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing PEEL paragraphs this morning — you'll do exactly that to your own in a moment.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If a reason feels thin as you write it, what does that mean? (The evidence is weak — back to FILL for that one.)*\n\n#### I Do — Read Your Audience Card First (3 min)\n\nOne small habit before every drafting session this term.\n\n> [Read the audience card aloud: who they are, what they think, what they're worried about.]\n\nTen seconds, and it changes every sentence I write afterwards. Without it I drift back into writing for my friends — which is how a letter to a principal ends up saying \"it's so unfair\".\n\n**Tip:** Card first, pen second. Every time.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Box (3 min)\n\nRound the room: what are you drafting today?\n\n- \"Opening and my claim.\"\n- \"Reason one — I've got the evidence, I need the explain.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone drafting a reason with no evidence behind it? Come and see me first.\n\n#### You Do — Draft (14 min)\n\nOpening, claim, signpost sentence, and reason one as a full PEEL. I'll run two-minute conferences.\n\n- Read your audience card.\n- Draft the opening and claim.\n- Add your signpost sentence.\n- Draft reason one as a full PEEL paragraph.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Finish the sentence you're on. Mark which reason you'll start on Monday.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Plan into sentences**: NZC Phase 2: \"transfer ideas from planning organisers or mnemonics into sentences\" — the four-box plan becomes connected prose.\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Openings, Standing Up":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Openings, Standing Up\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read our work aloud standing up, and to give specific feedback on what convinced us.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Opening and Claim  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting begins |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners with detail and precise vocabulary; make comments that encourage discussion. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — connectives, conjunctions, PEEL — are the skeleton of everything you drafted.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Where does this term's SHARE end up? (Standing in front of people — so we start practising now.)*\n\n#### I Do — Stand, Breathe, Look Up (3 min)\n\nThree rules for reading aloud that we'll use every Friday from now on.\n\n> Stand. Breathe before your first word. Look up at the end of your last sentence.\n\nWatch me do it badly first — hunched, straight in, eyes down, trailing off. Now properly. Same words, completely different effect. You have eight Fridays to make this automatic before it counts.\n\n**Tip:** The breath before your first word is the most important second of any speech.\n\n#### We Do — Specific Praise Only (4 min)\n\nToday is praise only — but it must be specific and it must be about persuasion.\n\n- \"The number that convinced me was…\"\n- \"Your first line made me want to hear more because…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is \"that was good\" useless to a writer?\n\n#### You Do — Openings Author's Chair (13 min)\n\n6–8 students read their opening and claim — standing, 30 seconds each.\n\n- Stand, breathe, read your opening and claim.\n- Look up on the last line.\n- Listeners: two specific pieces of praise per reader.\n- Readers: write down the exact words people praised.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose opening would you keep listening to? Tell them on the way out.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners actively — the first of many short standing performances that build towards Week 10.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new\" — standing to read is deliberately rehearsed from Week 4 so the speech in Week 10 is a small step, not a cliff.\n"}}},"Week 5: Reasons That Land":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 5 — Reasons That Land\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I make my evidence actually mean something to my audience?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Words That Carry Weight · Tue: Build Together: Putting Evidence Into Sentences · Wed: Stretch: So What? · Thu: Write: Reasons Two and Three · Fri: Share & Reflect: Were You Convinced? |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Give me your reasons — and make me feel what the evidence means.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the proof for this reason?\n- So what? What does that proof MEAN for my audience?\n- Which words carry the feeling I want — without exaggerating?\n- What happens if nothing changes?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\ncrowded · cramped · wasted · neglected · avoidable · preventable · overlooked · stretched · unused · repeatedly\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- According to…\n- This means that…\n- As a result, …\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Use the \"so what\" ladder: state the fact → \"which means\" → \"which means\" → stop when you reach something your audience cares about.\n- **Picture this:** A tally chart beside a photograph of the thing it counts.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your evidence to a partner. Their only reply is \"so what?\" — three times. Write down your third answer.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Connotation: positive vs negative word choice\n- **Tuesday** — Nominalisation\n- **Wednesday** — Cause and effect sentences\n- **Thursday** — Editing for formal register\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Words That Carry Weight":{"content":"### Launch: Words That Carry Weight\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose words whose connotation supports our argument, without exaggerating.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Reasons That Land  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Connotation: positive vs negative word choice |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): vocabulary is chosen deliberately for effect; students consider the connotations and precision of words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nConnotation this morning — positive versus negative word choice. This lesson is that warm-up, aimed at a real audience.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Whose words are these for? Would your audience recognise themselves in how you've described the problem?*\n\n#### I Do — Same Fact, Three Temperatures (6 min)\n\nOne fact, written three ways. Listen for where honest description turns into exaggeration.\n\n> COLD: \"The field has not been in use since June.\"\nWARM: \"The field has sat empty since June.\"\nTOO HOT: \"The field has been abandoned and left to rot since June.\"\n\n\"Sat empty\" is accurate and it does work — I can picture it. \"Abandoned and left to rot\" is untrue, and the moment my principal reads it she stops trusting everything else I've written. Loaded words are a tool, not a volume knob. Turn it up one notch, never three.\n\n**Tip:** If your audience could say \"that's not true\", you've gone one word too far.\n\n#### We Do — Turn the Dial (8 min)\n\nNeutral sentences on the board. We write a warm version and a too-hot version of each, then decide where the line is.\n\n- \"Some children wait to use the court.\"\n- \"There are 200 books for two year groups.\"\n- \"The bins are emptied once a day.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How can you tell the difference between strong writing and exaggeration?\n\n#### You Do — Warm Up Your Draft (7 min)\n\nGo through what you've drafted and turn the dial up one notch — and check nothing is untrue.\n\n- Find three neutral words and upgrade them by one notch.\n- Find any word that exaggerates and pull it back.\n- Read the paragraph aloud: does it still sound like someone your audience would believe?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read us one word you changed, and the word it replaced.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Connotation**: NZC Phase 2 Writing — Vocabulary: words are chosen deliberately for effect, with attention to connotation and precision. In persuasion, connotation is the argument's temperature.\n- *Key competency — Using language, symbols & text* — **Language and purpose**: \"Match a text's features with its purpose\" — persuasive word choice is a feature serving a purpose, and it can be overdone.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Putting Evidence Into Sentences":{"content":"### Build Together: Putting Evidence Into Sentences\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to weave evidence into our writing so it reads as part of the argument.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Reasons That Land  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Nominalisation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): support claims with evidence; use topic-related vocabulary. Sentence structures: combining sentences and varying phrase position. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nNominalisation this morning — \"the crowding\", \"the closure\", \"the delay\". Handy in formal writing, but don't let it hide who did what.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What are the two things we're finding this term? (The person, and the order that moves them.)*\n\n#### I Do — Dumped vs Woven (6 min)\n\nThe same statistic, used badly and then properly.\n\n> DUMPED: \"Nine collisions were logged. This shows the court is crowded.\"\nWOVEN: \"Our duty teacher logged nine collisions on the court in a single week last month — and that was a week with two wet days, when half the school stayed inside.\"\n\nThe first version tells you the number and then tells you what to think. The second builds it into a sentence, names the source so you'll believe it, and adds the detail that makes it worse. Same fact. Completely different force.\n\n**Tip:** Attribute your evidence. \"Our duty teacher logged\" is far stronger than \"it was found that\".\n\n#### We Do — Weave These (8 min)\n\nBare pieces of evidence on the board. We fold each into a sentence, with attribution.\n\n- \"23 lunches sent back last Thursday.\"\n- \"200 books for two year groups.\"\n- \"Eleven weeks since the work finished.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which attribution would your audience trust most — and why does the source matter as much as the number?\n\n#### You Do — Weave Your Own (7 min)\n\nEvery piece of evidence in your draft: attributed and built into a sentence.\n\n- Find each piece of evidence in your draft.\n- Add its source: who counted it, who said it, when.\n- Rewrite any that sits on its own as a bare fact.\n- ✦ Bonus: put one piece of evidence in the middle of a sentence rather than at the start.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read one woven sentence. Class: would you believe it?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Combining and varying**: NZC Phase 2: \"combine sentences orally, inserting phrases and varying the position of the phrase\" — how a statistic gets folded into a sentence rather than dumped beside it.\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **Citing evidence**: Evidence is attributed (\"according to…\", \"our own newsletter said…\") so an audience can check it and is more likely to accept it.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: So What?":{"content":"### Stretch: So What?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to explain what our evidence means for the person we are trying to convince.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Reasons That Land  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Cause and effect sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): the explanation of evidence — cause and effect reasoning made explicit for the audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCause and effect sentences this morning — exactly what an EXPLAIN sentence is made of.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Halfway through drafting. Are we fixing things yet? (No — SHAPE is for that.)*\n\n#### I Do — The So-What Ladder (6 min)\n\nI'll take one fact and ask \"so what?\" three times, out loud, until I reach something my audience cares about.\n\n> \"84 children share one court.\"\nSo what? → \"There isn't room to play a game.\"\nSo what? → \"The little ones get pushed out to the edges.\"\nSo what? → \"Year 1s are ending up at the sick bay, and it's happening every week.\"\n\nThe fact is where I START. Three \"so whats\" later I've arrived at injured five-year-olds, which is something a principal has to act on. Most students write the fact and stop, and then wonder why nobody was moved. Keep laddering until you hit the thing your audience actually loses sleep over.\n\n**Tip:** Ask \"so what?\" until your audience would say \"oh — that's a problem.\"\n\n#### We Do — Ladder Together (7 min)\n\nOne piece of class evidence. We ladder it three rungs as a class.\n\n- Fact: \"The tuck shop sells 300 plastic-wrapped items a week.\"\n- So what? … and so what? … and so what?\n- Where did we land — and would our audience care about that?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which rung is the one worth writing down?\n\n#### You Do — Ladder Your Reasons (8 min)\n\nEvery reason in your draft gets the ladder treatment.\n\n- Take each reason's evidence and ask \"so what?\" three times in your jotter.\n- Write the third answer into your draft as your EXPLAIN sentence.\n- Check: does your EXPLAIN mention your audience or the people they're responsible for?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your third rung. That's the sentence that does the persuading.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Cause and effect**: \"Cause and effect thinking: working forwards or backwards to/from possible causes and consequences\" — the EXPLAIN in PEEL is this competency in sentence form.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Cause and effect sentences**: NZC Phase 2: expanding and combining sentences with adverbial phrases and connectives to show consequence: \"which means\", \"as a result\", \"if… then\".\n"},"Thursday: Write: Reasons Two and Three":{"content":"### Write: Reasons Two and Three\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sustain an argument across several well-built paragraphs.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Reasons That Land  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing for formal register |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): sustained drafting from a plan; paragraphs with topic, detail and concluding sentences; increasing stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing for formal register this morning — check your draft for \"heaps\", \"kids\", \"stuff\" and \"gonna\". Your audience decides how formal you need to be.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If a reason won't come out, is it a writing problem or an evidence problem? (Usually evidence — go back to FILL for ten minutes.)*\n\n#### I Do — Read the Card, Then the Last Paragraph (3 min)\n\nTwo-step routine before every drafting session from now on.\n\n> Audience card → last paragraph → keep writing.\n\nThe card keeps me writing to a person. The last paragraph keeps my tone and my connectives consistent. Ten seconds, and it saves me a rewrite.\n\n**Tip:** Never start a new paragraph cold.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Reason (3 min)\n\nRound the room: which reason are you drafting today, and what's its evidence?\n\n- \"Reason two — I've got a number from the office.\"\n- \"Reason three, and it's my strongest.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone drafting a reason whose evidence is still \"everyone knows\"? See me.\n\n#### You Do — Sustained Drafting (15 min)\n\nLongest write of the term so far. I'll take a small group on the EXPLAIN sentence at the back table.\n\n- Draft reasons two and three as full PEEL paragraphs.\n- Each one: point, evidence with attribution, so-what explanation, link.\n- Save your strongest reason for last.\n- Note your word count in the margin.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who's got all three reasons drafted? Next week: the other side, and the ask.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Sustained drafting**: NZC Phase 2: transferring a full plan into connected multi-paragraph prose, each paragraph structured and linked.\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **Cumulative argument**: Reasons ordered so the argument gains force, with the strongest placed last for maximum effect on the audience.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Were You Convinced?":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Were You Convinced?\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to listen to an argument and say honestly whether it moved us, and where it didn't.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Reasons That Land  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting the case |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): listening and responding with clarifying questions. Writing processes: peer feedback used to revise for the intended audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week: connotation, nominalisation, cause and effect, formal register. Every one of them turned up in your reasons.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Explain one stage as it worked in a story, an explanation and a speech.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Three genres, one process (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** All three criteria charts visible. Wall strip covered.\n\n1. In pairs: \"Explain ONE stage — I'll tell you which — three ways: how it worked for your narrative, your explanation, and this speech. Sixty seconds each.\"\n2. Give different pairs different stages, so all four get covered around the room.\n3. Take four students, one per stage, to report back to the class. The class listens for what's the SAME in all three versions.\n4. Write the constants on the board as they emerge: you always fill before you draft · you always write to a particular someone · you always read it as the person who doesn't already agree · you always test it on a real human before you publish.\n\n**Watch for:** Those four constants are the answer to the Week 10 assessment. Getting the class to generate them themselves in Week 5 makes the assessment a recall task rather than a new one.\n\n#### I Do — The Two Feedback Sentences (3 min)\n\nOnly two sentence starters allowed today, and they're both about the listener.\n\n> \"The reason that convinced me most was…\" · \"I started doubting you when…\"\n\nNotice both are about what happened to ME as a listener. Neither is a verdict on the writer. \"I started doubting you\" isn't rude — it's the single most useful thing you can tell someone whose job is to convince people.\n\n**Tip:** Say where you doubted. Don't say how to fix it — that's the writer's job.\n\n#### We Do — Practise on Me (4 min)\n\nI'll read one reason paragraph, standing. You give me both sentences.\n\n- \"The reason that convinced me was…\"\n- \"I started doubting you when…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did anyone doubt me at the same place? Two people doubting the same line is a fact, not an opinion.\n\n#### You Do — Trios, Standing (12 min)\n\nGroups of three. Read your strongest reason paragraph standing up. Listeners give both sentences.\n\n- Stand, breathe, read your strongest reason.\n- Listeners: the convinced sentence and the doubting sentence.\n- Write down where people doubted you.\n- Rotate.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"People doubted me at… so on Monday I'll…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Honest response**: NZC Phase 2: making comments that encourage discussion, considering the impact of your words — including the useful discomfort of \"I wasn't convinced\".\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Audience feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revise \"using audience and peer feedback\" to improve clarity and impact.\n"}}},"Week 6: The Other Side and the Ask":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I answer the people who disagree — and what exactly am I asking for?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Concede and Turn · Tue: Build Together: The Ask · Wed: Stretch: Rhythm and the Rule of Three · Thu: Write: Finish the Draft · Fri: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Answer your strongest opponent. Then ask for exactly what you want.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the best argument against me — the fair one, not the silly one?\n- What part of it can I honestly agree with?\n- How do I answer the rest?\n- What is the smallest, most specific thing I can ask my audience to do?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nadmittedly · however · nevertheless · while · although · propose · request · trial · agree · by Friday\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Some people worry that…\n- That concern is fair, and…\n- I am asking you to…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** The concede-and-turn frame: \"Some people say ____ . They have a point — ____ . However, ____ .\"\n- **Picture this:** Two people shaking hands over a piece of paper.\n- **Talk it out first:** Have your partner give you their best objection. Answer it out loud, twice. The second answer is the one to write.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Formal vs informal register\n- **Tuesday** — Reported (indirect) speech\n- **Wednesday** — Balancing sentence rhythm\n- **Thursday** — Editing for cohesion\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Concede and Turn":{"content":"### Launch: Concede and Turn\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to name the strongest objection fairly and then answer it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Formal vs informal register |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): acknowledge and address counter-arguments. Reading: \"acknowledge others' responses and respond respectfully to differences.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nFormal versus informal register this morning — a counter-argument is where tone matters most. Sarcasm here loses you the whole audience.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Why put my opponent's best argument in MY writing? (Because answering it first means they can't use it.)*\n\n#### I Do — Three Ways to Handle an Objection (7 min)\n\nOne objection, three responses. Two of them lose the argument.\n\n> OBJECTION: \"The new grass will be ruined.\"\nIGNORE IT: [say nothing and hope] → they think of it anyway, and now they don't trust me.\nSNEER: \"That's a stupid reason, grass grows back.\" → they stop listening.\nCONCEDE AND TURN: \"That concern is fair — the school paid for that grass and it needs time. That is why we are not asking for the seeded section. We are asking for the top half, which was never dug up.\"\n\nWatch what conceding actually does. It costs me nothing, it makes me sound reasonable, and it lets me be MORE specific about what I want. My argument got better by admitting the other side had a point. Proverbs says a gentle answer turns away wrath — that's not just kindness, it's tactics.\n\n**Tip:** Concede the true part. Answer the rest. Never sneer.\n\n#### We Do — Concede and Turn, Together (6 min)\n\nObjections from around the class. We write the concede-and-turn for three of them.\n\n- \"Some people say ____ .\"\n- \"They have a point — ____ .\"\n- \"However, ____ .\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which part is hardest to write honestly — and what happens if you skip it?\n\n#### You Do — Your Counter-Argument Paragraph (8 min)\n\nUse your Week 2 opposition page. Take the objection you starred as hardest.\n\n- Write the objection fairly, in the words its holder would use.\n- Write the part you genuinely agree with.\n- Write your answer — with evidence if you have it.\n- ✦ Check: could the person who holds that objection read this without getting annoyed?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your concede sentence only. Did it sound genuine, or gritted-teeth?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Making connections and interpretations* — **Respecting difference**: NZC Phase 2: \"acknowledge others' responses and respond respectfully to differences\" — the disposition that makes counter-argument work rather than backfire.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Subordinating conjunctions**: \"Although\", \"while\" and \"even though\" are the structures that let a writer concede a point and turn it in a single sentence.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Ask":{"content":"### Build Together: The Ask\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to finish with a call to action that is specific, small and possible.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Reported (indirect) speech |\n| **NZC link** | Writing to persuade (Phase 2): end with a clear call to action suited to the audience and purpose. Writing to inform/persuade: texts \"have a concluding statement\". |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nReported speech this morning — useful for a call to action: \"Mrs Tui said in assembly that she wanted student ideas. This is one.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What's your ask, in one sentence? Say it to your neighbour.*\n\n#### I Do — Four Endings, One Winner (6 min)\n\nFour ways to finish. Three of them fail, and each fails differently.\n\n> TOO BIG: \"Reopen all the school fields permanently.\"\nTOO VAGUE: \"Please think about what I've said.\"\nA PLEA: \"Please please please, we really really want this.\"\nTHE ASK: \"Could we meet with you this Friday to walk the field together and agree a line? Eighty-four of us would be very grateful.\"\n\nToo big gets refused. Too vague gets nothing — \"I thought about it\" is a complete answer to it. The plea makes me sound like a small child instead of someone with evidence. The real ask is tiny: fifteen minutes on Friday. And a tiny yes is where every big change starts.\n\n**Tip:** Ask for the smallest step that moves things forward. A meeting. A trial. One term.\n\n#### We Do — Shrink the Ask (8 min)\n\nBig asks from the class. We shrink each into something a real person could say yes to this term.\n\n- \"Ban all plastic in the tuck shop.\" → what's the first small step?\n- \"Give us more library books.\" → what's the smallest useful yes?\n- \"Change the timetable.\" → what could be trialled?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Does shrinking the ask weaken the argument, or strengthen it?\n\n#### You Do — Write Your Call to Action (7 min)\n\nTwo versions, then choose the smaller one.\n\n- Write your ask.\n- Now write a smaller version of the same ask.\n- Add a WHEN — a day, a week, or \"this term\".\n- Read it to a partner playing your audience: could they say yes on the spot?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRound the room: everyone reads only their ask. Fast.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **Call to action**: A persuasive text ends by asking for a specific action the audience is able to take — not with a summary or a plea.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Fit for the audience**: NZC Phase 2: writing with the audience in mind — the ask must be inside their power to grant, and small enough to say yes to.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Rhythm and the Rule of Three":{"content":"### Stretch: Rhythm and the Rule of Three\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to use sentence rhythm and repetition so our words are remembered.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Balancing sentence rhythm |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures (Phase 2): use a range of sentence structures and types to enhance impact. Oral language: vary sentence structure and length to create effect. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nBalancing sentence rhythm this morning — read every one of today's sentences out loud. If it doesn't sound right, it isn't right.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FIND this week. Name the next stage and what it asks.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall + move the peg (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg still on Stage 2.\n\n1. \"We finish a stage this week. Which one, and what's next?\" Whole class.\n2. A student moves the peg and justifies it: \"We're finishing FIND because the draft is done. Next is SHAPE — reading it as the person who disagrees.\"\n3. Push the comparison: \"What did SHAPE ask us in Term 1? In Term 2? What will it ask this term?\" (Vivid → clear → convincing.) Write all three on the board and leave them there for Week 7.\n\n**Watch for:** The three-question progression — vivid, clear, convincing — is the neatest summary of the year's learning. It's worth a permanent spot on the wall.\n\n#### I Do — Three, and Then a Short One (6 min)\n\nTwo devices that make a line memorable. Listen with your eyes shut.\n\n> RULE OF THREE: \"It is unused, it is unfair, and it is unnecessary.\"\nSHORT SENTENCE AFTER A LONG ONE: \"We have counted the collisions, we have read the newsletter, and we have waited eleven weeks. It is time.\"\n\nThree has a rhythm your ear expects and remembers — two feels incomplete, four feels like a list. And the short sentence after the long one lands like a full stop you can hear. These are speech tools, and in five weeks you're standing up, so start writing lines you'd want to say.\n\n**Tip:** One rule of three per piece. Two is a pattern; three is a party trick.\n\n#### We Do — Build Some Threes (7 min)\n\nClass issues. We build a rule-of-three line for each, then test it out loud.\n\n- Three adjectives: \"It is ____ , it is ____ , and it is ____ .\"\n- Three actions: \"We have ____ , we have ____ , and we have ____ .\"\n- Now add a four-word sentence after each.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Say both versions aloud. Which one would you remember tomorrow?\n\n#### You Do — One Memorable Line (8 min)\n\nFind the place in your draft that most needs to be remembered, and build one line for it.\n\n- Write one rule-of-three line for your argument.\n- Place a short sentence (five words or fewer) straight after your longest sentence.\n- Read the whole paragraph aloud in a whisper. Fix anything your mouth stumbles on.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your rule-of-three line. Class: hands up if you'd remember it tomorrow.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Structures for impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact\" — including the deliberate short sentence and the tricolon.\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Varying length for effect**: NZC Phase 2: \"varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning\" — heard rather than seen, which is why this lesson is read aloud.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Finish the Draft":{"content":"### Write: Finish the Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to complete a whole argument — finished, not perfect.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing for cohesion |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): completing a multi-paragraph persuasive text from a plan, with opening, reasons, counter-argument and call to action. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing for cohesion this morning — check your connectives actually signal what's coming: \"however\" must be followed by a turn.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Last day of FIND. What will exist by the end of today? (A complete argument, aimed at one person.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Goal, Plainly (2 min)\n\nOne sentence, then the room is yours.\n\n> \"Today every person finishes: opening, claim, three reasons, the objection answered, and the ask.\"\n\nFinished, not perfect. Next week you become the person who disagrees with you — and you cannot argue with something that isn't there yet.\n\n**Tip:** Nearly done? Finish. Don't start improving.\n\n#### We Do — What's Missing? (2 min)\n\nFast round the room: what's left in your draft?\n\n- \"The counter-argument.\"\n- \"My ask — I keep making it too big.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone missing more than two pieces, sit near me.\n\n#### You Do — The Long Write (18 min)\n\nEighteen protected minutes. I'll circulate with one question: \"what would they say back?\"\n\n- Finish every reason paragraph.\n- Add your counter-argument paragraph.\n- Add your call to action.\n- Date it and title it. Finished early? Read it aloud and mark anything you stumble on.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nCelebrate it out loud — a researched, evidenced, structured argument is serious work for Year 5–6.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing to persuade* — **A complete argument**: A finished persuasive text: hook, claim, signposted reasons with evidence, a fairly-handled counter-argument, and a specific call to action.\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Stamina**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\"\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to decide what makes an argument strong, and to judge our own against it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — The Other Side and the Ask  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the draft |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — register, reported speech, rhythm, cohesion — are all polish tools, and polish starts next week.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *We've completed FIND. Two stages done — name the two to come, and their questions.*\n\n#### I Do — Name What's Next (3 min)\n\nShort, because today belongs to your writing.\n\n> \"Next week you become the person who disagrees with you.\"\n\nThat's SHAPE. In Term 1 we asked \"is it vivid?\", in Term 2 \"is it clear?\" This term the question is \"would this convince someone who doesn't already agree with me?\" — and that person is much harder to satisfy than a friendly reader.\n\n**Tip:** Your friends are the wrong test audience. Read it as your opponent.\n\n#### We Do — Build \"What Makes a Strong Argument\" (5 min)\n\nSix weeks of learning — call it out and I'll scribe. This becomes our rubric.\n\n- What does a strong OPENING do?\n- What makes a REASON convincing?\n- How should we handle the OTHER SIDE?\n- What makes a CALL TO ACTION work?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anything on the list we'd be unhappy to be judged on? Then it stays.\n\n#### You Do — Best-Line Share (12 min)\n\nEverybody reads their single best line — standing. Everybody.\n\n- Choose the one line you're proudest of.\n- Stand, breathe, read it, look up.\n- Listen to all 25. Notice which lines the class reacts to.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The strongest part of my argument is… The weakest part is…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Criteria and goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection on content and process; co-constructing criteria makes the standard the students' own.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Sharing and discussing the elements of quality writing — interpreting and evaluating rather than receiving a checklist.\n"}}},"Week 7: Would It Convince Me?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?\n\n> **Essential question:** Would this convince someone who doesn't already agree with me?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model · Tue: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria · Wed: Stretch: The Credibility Audit · Thu: Write: Setting Two Goals · Fri: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt this week — your draft is the work. Read it as the person who disagrees with you.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Where have I claimed something without proving it?\n- Where have I exaggerated, and would my audience catch it?\n- Where does it sound like I'm attacking them rather than convincing them?\n- Which reason would my opponent attack first?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nunproved · exaggerated · vague · convincing · credible · fair · respectful · specific · evidence · tone\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- This part is convincing because…\n- My opponent would say…\n- I claimed ____ without proving it.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read your draft in the voice of the person you're writing to. Every time you'd have said \"hmm, really?\", put a dot in the margin.\n- **Picture this:** A draft covered in three colours of pencil.\n- **Talk it out first:** Give your draft to a partner and ask them to argue against it. Write down every point they make.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Onomatopoeia\n- **Tuesday** — Expanded noun phrases\n- **Wednesday** — Rhetorical questions\n- **Thursday** — Editing repetitive sentence openers\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model":{"content":"### Launch: Strong Model, Weak Model\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge a persuasive text against criteria and name exactly why a weak one fails.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Onomatopoeia |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): identify the audience and purpose of persuasive texts; determine how ideas are conveyed. Deconstruction of models (Loane Ch 11). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nOnomatopoeia this morning — rare in persuasive writing, and worth noticing that: this genre wins on evidence, not sound effects.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Term 1 asked \"is it vivid?\", Term 2 \"is it clear?\" What does this term ask? (\"Would it convince someone who disagrees?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — SHAPE when you're arguing (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg onto Stage 3. Both exemplars ready. The three SHAPE questions still on the board from Week 6.\n\n1. Point at the three questions. \"Vivid. Clear. Convincing. Same stage, three jobs. Which is hardest?\" Let them argue about it briefly — most land on convincing, and they're right.\n2. Teach the specific move: \"In Term 2 you read it as someone who didn't already know. This term you read it as someone who doesn't already AGREE. That's a different person, and they're much harder to satisfy.\"\n3. Give them the test question they'll use all week.\n   > \"Read every sentence and ask: would my opponent nod, or would they say 'prove it'?\"\n4. Then the tone warning, which is genuinely new this term: \"There's one thing here that Terms 1 and 2 never had. You can be RIGHT and still lose, if you sound like you're attacking the person who has to decide. Proverbs says a gentle answer turns away wrath — that's strategy as much as kindness.\"\n\n**Watch for:** \"You can be right and still lose\" is the sentence that sticks. Say it slowly, and refer back to it every time you see an attacking sentence in a draft this week.\n\n#### I Do — Annotate the Strong One (7 min)\n\nOur WAGOLL against the criteria you built on Friday. I'll label AND say why each move works on this audience.\n\n> [Annotate live: the number in line one · the claim with a deadline · signposting · evidence with attribution · \"most importantly\" before the strongest reason · the concede-and-turn · the small, dated ask.]\n\nWatch the concede-and-turn again. \"That is fair\" costs the writer nothing and buys enormous credibility — and it lets them narrow the ask to the top half, which makes yes much easier to say. Naming a technique is easy; saying what it does to the person reading it is the real skill.\n\n**Tip:** Ask of every move: what does this do to the person who has to decide?\n\n#### We Do — Diagnose the Weak One (7 min)\n\nThe weak exemplar. I wrote it badly on purpose. Be specific — you have the criteria.\n\n- Find every claim with no evidence behind it.\n- Find where it attacks the audience. What would that do to a real principal?\n- Find the counter-argument. What's wrong with how it's handled?\n- What exactly is it asking for?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If you could fix only ONE thing, which would make the biggest difference?\n\n#### You Do — Annotate the Strong Model (7 min)\n\nYour own copy. Three techniques, named, with their effect.\n\n- Label three techniques.\n- Beside each, write what it does to the decision-maker.\n- Star the one you're going to steal today.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Name the technique you're stealing. Out loud makes it a promise.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Deconstruction**: NZC Phase 2: determining how texts are structured and how ideas are conveyed — applied to a strong and a deliberately weak persuasive text against shared criteria.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Evaluating an argument's evidence and tone rather than reacting to whether you already agree with its position.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria":{"content":"### Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge our own argument honestly against the rubric.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Expanded noun phrases |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): reread at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level; set goals based on reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nExpanded noun phrases this morning — \"the undug top half of the lower field\" is more precise, and precision is what makes an ask easy to grant.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Who are you reading as today? (The person who disagrees.)*\n\n#### I Do — Find the Hole in My Own Case (5 min)\n\nMy draft, our rubric, out loud — including the part that doesn't hold up.\n\n> \"Opening — tick. Reasons — three, ordered. Evidence… reason three has none. I've written 'playing outside helps us learn' and my only proof is that I think it's true.\"\n\nThat's an unproved claim sitting in my strongest position. I have two choices: find evidence, or cut the reason. Both are fine. What isn't fine is leaving it there and hoping nobody notices — because the person deciding will notice, and then they'll doubt my other two.\n\n**Tip:** One unproved claim makes a reader doubt your proved ones too.\n\n#### We Do — Glow and Grow the Weak Exemplar (8 min)\n\nFill in glow and grow for the weak exemplar as a class.\n\n- Find one genuine glow — there always is one.\n- Find the single most damaging problem.\n- Write the grow as an instruction: \"Next time, try…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why only ONE grow when we can see six?\n\n#### You Do — Self-Analysis (8 min)\n\nRubric beside your draft. Tick only what you can PROVE with a line.\n\n- Work down the rubric; tick what you can prove.\n- Mark the line that proves it.\n- Leave blank what you can't — a blank is information.\n- Circle any claim in your draft with no evidence attached.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone name one glow out loud. Grows tomorrow.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Rereading at every level**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — the rubric gives a lens for each level.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Strengths and limitations**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations\" — modelled by the teacher first, on their own writing, so honesty is safe.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Credibility Audit":{"content":"### Stretch: The Credibility Audit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to find the three things that make an audience stop believing us.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Rhetorical questions |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revise to improve clarity and impact for the intended audience. Writing craft: word choice and tone judged against purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRhetorical questions this morning — powerful in a speech, and easy to overdo. One or two. Never four in a row.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Are we adding new reasons in this stage? (No — we're making the ones we have believable.)*\n\n#### I Do — Three Colours, Three Passes (5 min)\n\nThree separate passes over one page. One thing at a time.\n\n> PASS 1 (blue): circle every claim with no evidence.\nPASS 2 (red): mark every exaggeration — \"everyone\", \"always\", \"the worst\".\nPASS 3 (green): underline anything that sounds like an attack on my audience.\n\nGreen is the one that surprises people. Here — \"if the teachers actually cared\". I felt that when I wrote it, and it would end my chances instantly. Firm is fine. Angry is fine in your jotter. On the page that goes to a decision-maker, it has to convince, not accuse.\n\n**Tip:** Blue costs you evidence. Red costs you trust. Green costs you the whole audience.\n\n#### We Do — Audit the Weak Exemplar (7 min)\n\nThree passes over the weak model, out loud, counting as we go.\n\n- Pass 1 — how many unproved claims?\n- Pass 2 — how many exaggerations?\n- Pass 3 — where does it attack the reader?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which pass found the most? Which would you fix first?\n\n#### You Do — Audit Your Own (9 min)\n\nThree passes on your whole draft. Then fix the cheapest ones.\n\n- Run all three passes with three colours.\n- Fix every red (exaggeration) — those take seconds.\n- Fix every green (attack) — rewrite as a concern, not an accusation.\n- Blue circles go on your goals list: find evidence or cut the claim.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many greens did you find? Read us one, and the fixed version.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising for impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"make simple revisions to their texts to improve the clarity for the intended audience and purpose\" — in persuasion, credibility is the thing being repaired.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Tone and connotation**: Words carry attitude; a word that reads as an attack costs the writer the audience regardless of how strong the evidence is.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Setting Two Goals":{"content":"### Write: Setting Two Goals\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose the two changes that will most improve our chance of a yes.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing repetitive sentence openers |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRepetitive sentence openers got edited this morning — check whether three of your paragraphs start with \"I think\" or \"Also\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. What will you carry into SHARE next week? (My draft, two goals — and next week it starts becoming a speech.)*\n\n#### I Do — Choose Two (4 min)\n\nNine problems, two goals. Watch me choose the two that change the decision.\n\n> \"I will find evidence for reason three, or cut it and expand reason one instead.\"\n\"I will rewrite the sentence about teachers not caring so it names the problem, not the people.\"\n\nBoth name a place and an action. And notice the first one allows me to CUT — students almost never consider cutting a whole reason, and two evidenced reasons beat three with a soft one in the middle.\n\n**Tip:** Cutting a weak reason is a legitimate goal. It usually improves the piece.\n\n#### We Do — What Are We All Finding? (3 min)\n\nCall out your grows. I'll tally — it decides next week's workshops.\n\n- \"Unproved claims.\"\n- \"My tone in one paragraph.\"\n- \"My ask is too big.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which got the most hands? That's the workshop.\n\n#### You Do — Two Goals, Written Down (14 min)\n\nFinish the self-analysis, then write two goals at the top of your draft.\n\n- Finish the rubric self-analysis.\n- GOAL 1: \"I will ____ in ____ [where].\"\n- GOAL 2: the same.\n- Read them to a partner — they hold you to them next week.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Your partner is holding you to those two. That's the deal.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Specific goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection, written specifically enough to be completed and checked.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: Ranking possible changes by their effect on a specific audience — a genuinely strategic judgement.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to ask for the feedback we need and receive it without arguing.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Would It Convince Me?  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Analysing the argument |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): give and receive peer feedback. Oral Language: clarifying questions; considering the impact of our words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAll four warm-ups this week sharpened precision and tone. Next week real listeners tell you whether it worked.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing SHAPE, entering SHARE — and this term SHARE ends on a stage.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Handover — SHAPE into SHARE, with a stage (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 3.\n\n1. \"SHAPE told us what's weak. What does SHARE do?\" (We test it on real people and fix it.)\n2. Move the peg between 3 and 4; draw the loop arrow back from 4 to 3.\n3. Add this term's version: \"And SHARE has one more thing in it now. Next week your argument stops being only a page and starts becoming a speech. Same argument. Two forms.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Plant \"same argument, two forms\" here. Students who think the speech is a second, separate piece of work will panic; it isn't and they shouldn't.\n\n#### I Do — The Irony Rule (4 min)\n\nThe protocol, and the one rule that matters most in a persuasive unit.\n\n> READ ALOUD (standing) → YOUR QUESTION → TWO STARS → ONE WISH → thank you, write it down.\nThe irony rule: you may not argue with feedback on your persuasive writing.\n\nThink about it. You've spent seven weeks learning to change people's minds with evidence. If someone says \"I wasn't convinced\", arguing with them proves your writing didn't do the job — you're doing it live instead, which isn't the test. Write it down. Fix the page.\n\n**Tip:** If you have to defend it out loud, it isn't finished on the page.\n\n#### We Do — Fishbowl (on me) (7 min)\n\nFour volunteers circle me. I read standing; they give me real feedback on a real argument.\n\n- Two of you: what convinced you?\n- Two of you: where did you start doubting me?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Watch me take the doubt. Did I explain myself? (No — and neither will you.)\n\n#### You Do — Choose Your Section and Question (10 min)\n\nMark the 60 seconds you'll read, and write the question you want answered.\n\n- Mark the section you'll read standing on Monday.\n- Write ONE specific question — \"Is my evidence for reason two strong enough?\"\n- Sharpen it: if it can be answered yes or no, ask \"where\" instead.\n- ✦ Best question in this genre: \"Where did you start doubting me?\"\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Bring your draft, your question, and the ability to say thank you.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Giving and receiving feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — as a protocol, driven by the writer's own question.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Listening without defending**: \"Listen carefully and paraphrase ideas of others\" — the writer records rather than rebuts.\n"}}},"Week 8: The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage\n\n> **Essential question:** Does it convince a real listener — and can I say it out loud?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1 · Tue: Circle Day 2 and Triage · Wed: Stretch: Revision Workshops · Thu: Write: From Page to Stage · Fri: Share & Reflect: The First Run-Through |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is feedback, revision, and turning your argument into something you can say standing up.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Where did real listeners start doubting me?\n- Which change would most improve my chance of a yes?\n- What can I cut so this fits in two minutes?\n- Which sentences are hard to say out loud?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nconvincing · doubt · evidence · cut · script · cue · pause · emphasis · signpost · deliver\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I started doubting you when…\n- The reason that convinced me was…\n- I am going to cut…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Start with whatever two different people doubted. Two people is not an opinion.\n- **Picture this:** A page of writing beside four small cue cards.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read your piece aloud with a timer. Anything that takes you past two minutes has to go.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Idioms and figurative expressions\n- **Tuesday** — Parenthesis review: commas, brackets, dashes\n- **Wednesday** — Embedding clauses for sophistication\n- **Thursday** — Self-editing with a full checklist\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1":{"content":"### Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a writer honestly what convinced us and where we began to doubt.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Idioms and figurative expressions |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): peer feedback used to revise. Oral Language: comments that encourage discussion; considering the impact of our words on others. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nIdioms this morning — careful in persuasive writing. \"At the end of the day\" fills space; a fresh image earns attention.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's new about SHARE this term? (It ends with me standing up and saying it.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — SHARE when it ends on a stage (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 4. Stand in the speaking spot to do this one.\n\n1. Open hands action, whole class. \"Same giving action. But look where I'm standing.\" Deliver the spotlight from the speaking spot — the point lands physically.\n2. Compare the three feedback questions on the board: DID YOU FEEL IT? (T1) · DID YOU UNDERSTAND IT? (T2) · WERE YOU CONVINCED? (T3). \"Which one can a listener lie about most easily?\" (All of them — but \"were you convinced\" is the one they'll answer honestly if you make it safe to.)\n3. Teach the irony rule.\n   > \"You may not argue with feedback on your persuasive writing. If you have to convince someone out loud that your argument was convincing, then the writing didn't do it.\"\n4. Then the reassurance, said plainly: \"In two weeks you stand up. Everyone in this room will do it, everyone will be listened to in silence, and everyone gets the same applause. You've been practising standing up every Friday since Week 4 — that was on purpose.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Name the nerves out loud here rather than pretending they don't exist. The students who are dreading it need to hear that the room is safe and that the practice has been deliberate.\n\n#### I Do — The Protocol, Persuasion Version (4 min)\n\nSame five steps, and this term you read STANDING — because that's where this is heading.\n\n> STARS: \"The reason that convinced me most was…\" / \"Your evidence for ____ was strong because…\"\nWISHES: \"I started doubting you when…\" / \"You said ____ but didn't prove it.\" / \"Someone who disagrees would say ____ .\"\n\nNotice all the wishes describe what happened to the LISTENER. None of them say \"you're wrong\". A listener who doubted you is not attacking you — they're giving you the exact sentence your real audience would have thought silently.\n\n**Tip:** Point at the sentence where the doubt started. Not \"somewhere in the middle\".\n\n#### We Do — Form the Circles (3 min)\n\nGroups of four, timer on the board, roles named, and everyone stands to read.\n\n- Who reads first?\n- Who's on the timer?\n- Everyone: pencil ready to write feedback down.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Five minutes per writer — two today, two tomorrow.\n\n#### You Do — Helping Circle, Round 1 (14 min)\n\nTwo writers per group. Full protocol, standing to read.\n\n- Stand, breathe, read your marked section.\n- Ask your question.\n- Take two stars and one wish from each listener.\n- Write every piece of feedback down in your own words, immediately.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Write it down NOW. Tomorrow the other two read.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Impact of our words**: NZC Phase 2: \"consider the impact of their words on others\" — disagreeing with an argument while respecting the person making it.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Using peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revisions made \"using audience and peer feedback\" to improve impact for the intended audience.\n"},"Tuesday: Circle Day 2 and Triage":{"content":"### Circle Day 2 and Triage\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to sort feedback and decide, as the author, what to act on.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Parenthesis review: commas, brackets, dashes |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): using feedback to revise while retaining authorship of the text. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nParenthesis review this morning — commas, brackets and dashes let you slip in a fact without breaking your sentence: \"the field — closed since March — is still fenced\".\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who decides what changes? (The writer — but two people doubting the same line is a fact.)*\n\n#### I Do — Triage, Persuasion Style (4 min)\n\nThree piles, sorted out loud — with one rule specific to this genre.\n\n> DO IT · THINK ABOUT IT · POLITELY LEAVE IT. The rule: any \"I started doubting you\" goes straight into DO IT.\n\nSomeone doubting you is not a matter of taste. It's a report from inside your reader's head, and it's exactly what your real audience will feel. Everything else you can weigh. Doubt you fix.\n\n**Tip:** Two people, same doubt, same place — fix it, no debate.\n\n#### We Do — Regroup (3 min)\n\nSame circles, timer reset, remaining two writers ready.\n\n- Readers three and four — stand, read, ask your question.\n- Then everyone sorts their feedback into the three piles.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Same protocol, same five minutes.\n\n#### You Do — Circle Round 2, then Triage (15 min)\n\nFinish the circle, then build your revision plan.\n\n- Remaining writers: read, ask, receive, record.\n- Sort all your feedback into the three piles.\n- Write changes 1, 2 and 3 as actions, biggest first.\n- Beside anything you're leaving, write why in four words.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Point at your number one change with a finger on the page.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Selecting revisions**: NZC Phase 2 writers use peer and audience feedback to revise — choosing from feedback is part of the skill, and the choices must be justifiable.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Think before acting**: Deciding and sequencing changes rather than either ignoring feedback or obeying all of it.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Revision Workshops":{"content":"### Stretch: Revision Workshops\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning the four revision moves and using them on our own argument.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Embedding clauses for sophistication |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"make simple revisions… replacing words, adding sentences, and using audience and peer feedback.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEmbedding clauses this morning — \"the field, which has been fenced since March, is still empty\". One sentence, two facts.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What are we doing right now? (Recrafting — the loop.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Moves, One Paragraph (4 min)\n\nAll four moves on a single paragraph. Watch the CUT particularly.\n\n> ADD (hands apart) · CUT (scissors) · REORDER (swap hands) · REPLACE (one hand over the other)\n\nAdd: the evidence I found for reason three. Cut: my whole second paragraph, because it repeated reason one in different words. Reorder: the objection now comes BEFORE my strongest reason, so I end on strength. Replace: \"teachers don't care\" becomes \"this hasn't been reviewed since March\". The cut is the one that improved it most, and it's the one nobody wants to make.\n\n**Tip:** If two paragraphs make the same point, you have one paragraph and some padding.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Move (3 min)\n\nRound the room: your number one change — which move?\n\n- \"An ADD — I found evidence.\"\n- \"A REPLACE — my tone in paragraph four.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone's a CUT? That's the bravest one in this genre.\n\n#### You Do — Revision Workshops (16 min)\n\nEverybody revises. Two six-minute workshops at the back table — come if it's your grow.\n\n- Work through your revision plan in order.\n- Name the move before you make it.\n- Workshop 1: finding evidence for a soft reason (or cutting it well).\n- Workshop 2: turning an attack into a concern.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Did anyone cut a whole reason today? Tell us what it cost and what it bought.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revision moves**: NZC Phase 2 names replacing words and adding sentences; cutting and reordering complete the set. In persuasion, CUT is the most underused.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Embedding clauses**: NZC Phase 2: combining sentences and varying phrase position — embedding a clause lets a writer add evidence without adding a sentence.\n"},"Thursday: Write: From Page to Stage":{"content":"### Write: From Page to Stage\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to turn a written argument into a script we can say out loud.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Self-editing with a full checklist |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): communicate in complete sentences, varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning; use precise vocabulary. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSelf-editing with a full checklist this morning — you're about to run a different checklist: the ear's.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Same argument, second form. Which stage are we still in? (SHARE — it's all preparing to give it away.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cut a Third, Say the Rest (6 min)\n\nMy written paragraph, then the spoken version. Count what I lose and what I add.\n\n> WRITTEN: \"Furthermore, the crowding on the court is causing avoidable injuries; our duty teacher logged nine collisions in a single week last month, which suggests that the space is inadequate for the number of children using it.\"\nSPOKEN: \"Second reason. Nine collisions. One week. That's what our own duty teacher counted — and that was a week when half the school was inside because of rain.\"\n\nI cut about a third of the words and I added two things: a signpost (\"second reason\") and a pause you can hear. Long clauses die out loud. Numbers survive. And \"our own duty teacher\" works even better spoken than written, because I can lean on \"own\".\n\n**Tip:** Rule of thumb: 2 minutes is about 250–280 spoken words. Cut to fit.\n\n#### We Do — Convert One Paragraph (5 min)\n\nOne volunteer's paragraph, converted to speech together. Read both aloud.\n\n- Where does the signpost go?\n- Which long sentence should become two or three short ones?\n- What can we cut entirely without losing the argument?\n- Where's the pause?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did the argument get weaker when it got shorter? (Usually the opposite.)\n\n#### You Do — Write Your Script (12 min)\n\nConvert your whole argument into a spoken script. Time it.\n\n- Rewrite your opening as a spoken opening (a number or a question works best).\n- Signpost each reason out loud: \"First… Second… Most importantly…\"\n- Break long sentences into short ones.\n- Read it aloud with a timer. Cut until it's under 2 minutes.\n- Mark // wherever you'll pause.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"What was your time? Anyone over two and a half minutes has cutting to do tonight.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Structure for the ear**: NZC Phase 2: \"varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning\" — a spoken text needs shorter sentences, clearer signposts and more repetition than a written one.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Genre and register**: NZC Phase 2: selecting genre and register for the audience — the same argument is re-registered for listening rather than reading.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: The First Run-Through":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: The First Run-Through\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to say our argument aloud from a script and hear what needs to change.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & From Page to Stage  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback, revision, and the speech begins |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively with specific details and precise vocabulary; respond to feedback about spoken communication. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — idioms, parenthesis, embedded clauses — all read well on paper. Today you find out which of them survive out loud.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. When you stumble reading aloud today, which stage does that send you back to? (SHAPE — the ear found something the eye missed.)*\n\n#### I Do — Stumble and Mark (3 min)\n\nI'll read my script and deliberately not fix my stumbles — I'll mark them instead.\n\n> [Read; stumble; mark the spot with a dot; keep going.]\n\nThree dots. Every one of them is a sentence my mouth can't manage — usually too long, or a clause buried in the middle. My ear just did my editing for me. This is the fastest revision tool in the whole programme and it costs nothing.\n\n**Tip:** Never fix while reading. Mark, finish, then fix.\n\n#### We Do — The Listener's Job (4 min)\n\nTwo jobs today, and neither is being nice.\n\n- Hold the timer and say the time at the end.\n- Say where you stopped listening — not where it was wrong, where you drifted.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is \"I drifted here\" different from \"this bit is bad\"?\n\n#### You Do — Full Run-Through in Trios (13 min)\n\nGroups of three, standing. Full script, timed. Everyone runs it once.\n\n- Stand and deliver your whole script from the page.\n- Mark every stumble with a dot as you go.\n- Listeners: give the time, and say where they drifted.\n- Fix your three worst dots before you leave.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"My time was… I drifted people at… I'm cutting…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions… with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — the first full run of the spoken argument.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new\" — a first full run-through in a small group, five weeks after the first 30-second stand.\n"}}},"Week 9: Recraft, Edit, Rehearse":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse\n\n> **Essential question:** What does it look like to make my argument stronger rather than just neater?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Real Second Draft · Tue: Build Together: The Four-Pass Edit · Wed: Stretch: Best Copy · Thu: Write: Cue Cards and the Speaking Voice · Fri: Share & Reflect: Rehearsal With Feedback |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is the real second draft, the edit, the best copy, and learning to say it well.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which paragraph needs rewriting rather than patching?\n- Have I checked capitals, commas, tense and spelling — one pass each?\n- Where should my voice go up, slow down, or stop?\n- Can I say my opening line without looking down?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nrecraft · precise · consistent · legible · pace · pause · emphasis · projection · eye contact · rehearse\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The version I like better is…\n- I rewrote this because…\n- I'm going to pause after…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Cover the paragraph and write it again from memory, as if you were saying it to the person who has to decide.\n- **Picture this:** Four cue cards, numbered, with five words on each.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your first two sentences to a partner without looking at your page. That is your opening, memorised.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Expanding noun phrases for detail\n- **Tuesday** — Passive voice\n- **Wednesday** — Sequencing sentences\n- **Thursday** — Peer editing for text-type structure\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Real Second Draft":{"content":"### Launch: The Real Second Draft\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to rewrite a section rather than patching words in the old one.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Expanding noun phrases for detail |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revisions at sentence, paragraph and whole-text level to improve clarity and impact for the intended audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nExpanding noun phrases this morning — precision makes an ask easy to grant. \"The field\" is vague; \"the top half, which was never dug up\" is a decision someone can make.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Which move is a rewrite? (REPLACE — at paragraph size.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cover It, Write It Again (6 min)\n\nMy weakest paragraph, covered, rewritten from scratch — this time aimed straight at the person deciding.\n\n> OLD: \"It is also really unfair that we have to wait so long and nothing has happened for ages which is annoying for everyone.\"\nNEW: \"The drainage work finished in June. Since then the field has been fenced for eleven weeks, and nobody has told us why.\"\n\nThe old one is a feeling with no facts. The new one is two facts and a fair question, and it's shorter. Patching that first sentence would have kept its shape — covering it let me write what I actually mean now, after seven weeks of evidence.\n\n**Tip:** Broken in more than two places? Cover it and start again.\n\n#### We Do — Which Would Convince You? (5 min)\n\nTwo versions on the board. Vote, then justify in criteria language.\n\n- Which one has evidence?\n- Which one would annoy the person deciding?\n- Which one is shorter — and does that help?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Is the more emotional version always the more persuasive one?\n\n#### You Do — Second Draft Begins (10 min)\n\nRevision change #1. Cover and rewrite if it needs a rewrite.\n\n- Complete change #1 from your revision plan.\n- If it's a rewrite, cover the original and write fresh, aimed at your audience.\n- Read both versions aloud, one after the other.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who rewrote rather than patched? Was the new version shorter?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Whole-text revision**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — recrafting a whole paragraph is a whole-text-level move.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Expanded noun phrases**: NZC Phase 2: expanding sentences with phrases to add precision — \"the undug top half of the lower field\" rather than \"the field\".\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Four-Pass Edit":{"content":"### Build Together: The Four-Pass Edit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to edit in separate passes — one thing at a time.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Passive voice |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\"; \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPassive voice this morning — \"mistakes were made\" hides the actor. In your argument, use it deliberately: to avoid blaming a person you still need on your side.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's the difference between revising and editing? (Revising = argument. Editing = surface. In that order.)*\n\n#### I Do — One Pass Per Thing (5 min)\n\nFour passes, in this order, on my own draft.\n\n> Pass 1: capitals & full stops · Pass 2: commas, especially after connectives · Pass 3: apostrophes and speech punctuation · Pass 4: spellings — including the names of any people or places I've named.\n\nPass four matters more this term than ever, because I'm writing to a real person. Spelling my principal's name wrong is the fastest way to look like I don't care about this. Check names first, and check them twice.\n\n**Tip:** Get the names right. Everything else is recoverable.\n\n#### We Do — Four Passes Together (6 min)\n\nShort passage with errors. One pass at a time; count what each pass finds.\n\n- Pass 1 — capitals and full stops?\n- Pass 2 — commas after However, Furthermore, Most importantly?\n- Pass 3 — apostrophes?\n- Pass 4 — names and tricky words?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which pass found the errors that would have embarrassed you most in front of a principal?\n\n#### You Do — Edit Your Own (10 min)\n\nFour separate passes, then swap for one final pass.\n\n- Pass 1: capitals and full stops.\n- Pass 2: commas after connectives.\n- Pass 3: apostrophes and any quoted speech.\n- Pass 4: spellings — names first, then anything you can't picture.\n- Peer swap for a fresh final pass.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Editing is the respect bit — you're handing this to a real person.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Editing with known punctuation**: NZC Phase 2: \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\" — three terms of warm-up learning is now the checklist.\n- *Writing — Punctuation* — **Commas for transitions**: NZC Phase 2: \"use commas correctly… when using transition words, with some support\" — persuasive writing is full of them: However, Furthermore, Most importantly.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Best Copy":{"content":"### Stretch: Best Copy\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to present a finished argument carefully, in our best handwriting.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Sequencing sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Transcription — handwriting (Phase 2): \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility when writing multi-paragraph texts.\" Layout conventions for paragraphs. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSequencing sentences this morning — last chance to check your reasons are still in the order you planned, strongest last.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Draw the cycle. Where does a stumble in rehearsal send you? (Back to SHAPE.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — The cycle — and the speaking loop (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Board space. Jotters open.\n\n1. Draw the four stages in a line. \"Still wrong.\" Add the loop back from SHARE to SHAPE, and the long arrow back to FILL from Term 2.\n2. Now add this term's arrow: a small tight loop on SHARE itself, labelled \"rehearsal\". \"Every time you say it out loud and stumble, you've just edited it. Your ear catches what your eye lets through.\"\n3. Ask: \"Who has already changed something because of how it sounded out loud?\" Take three or four examples — there will be plenty after last Friday.\n4. Jotters: draw the whole cycle from memory, labelled, with all three arrows. Ninety seconds.\n\n**Watch for:** The rehearsal loop is the most useful thing a young writer can learn about speech-making, and it transfers straight back to writing: reading aloud is editing.\n\n#### I Do — What a Finished Page Looks Like (3 min)\n\nPresentation standard, written live so you can see the pace.\n\n> Their name and title at the top · the date · your claim in the first paragraph · paragraphs indented · your name at the bottom.\n\nSlow. This is the version that could actually be delivered to the person it names — and several of you are going to deliver yours for real, which changes how much the presentation matters.\n\n**Tip:** One neat line through a mistake. Never scribble.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Checklist (2 min)\n\nFive things on the board for the lesson.\n\n- Addressed to a named person.\n- Dated.\n- Paragraphs indented, on new lines.\n- Joined, legible handwriting.\n- Signed at the bottom.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anything else a real letter needs?\n\n#### You Do — Best Copy Into Writing Books (15 min)\n\nQuiet and calm. Take the whole time.\n\n- Write your best copy into your writing book.\n- Follow the five-point checklist.\n- Reread as you go — you'll still catch things.\n- Finished early: start memorising your opening two sentences.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Not finished? Finish tomorrow. Nobody rushes a best copy.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Legibility and stamina**: NZC Phase 2: legible, fluent handwriting sustained across a multi-paragraph text — assessed on the best copy.\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Presentation for a reader**: A letter or argument going to a real decision-maker is presented as such: addressed, dated, signed.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Cue Cards and the Speaking Voice":{"content":"### Write: Cue Cards and the Speaking Voice\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to speak from cue cards rather than reading a script.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Peer editing for text-type structure |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): communicate in complete sentences, varying structure and length for effect; use precise vocabulary; engage listeners actively. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPeer editing for text-type structure this morning — check your speech still has all four parts: hook, claim, reasons, ask.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Why cue cards instead of the whole script? (Because I can't look at them and at my audience at the same time.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Cards, Five Words Each (6 min)\n\nWatch me turn a two-minute script into four cue cards — and then say the whole thing from them.\n\n> CARD 1: \"84 children. One court.\" · CARD 2: \"Not used — 11 weeks\" · CARD 3: \"9 collisions // little ones\" · CARD 4: \"Grass — top half only // Friday meeting?\"\n\nFive words a card, and the // marks my pauses. If I write full sentences on the cards I'll read them, and the moment I read, I stop persuading anybody. The words come out slightly differently each time, and that's fine — that's what makes it sound like a person talking instead of a page talking.\n\n**Tip:** Memorise your first sentence and your last sentence word for word. Everything in between can breathe.\n\n#### We Do — Voice, Pace, Pause, Eyes (5 min)\n\nFour delivery moves, practised as a class on one shared sentence.\n\n- VOICE: say it to the back wall, not to your shoes.\n- PACE: slower than feels natural — nerves speed everyone up.\n- PAUSE: three seconds after your best line. Count it.\n- EYES: three people, three seconds each. Not the ceiling.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of the four will be hardest for you? Say it out loud — naming it makes it smaller.\n\n#### You Do — Make Your Cards (11 min)\n\nFour or five cards, numbered, five words maximum on each.\n\n- Write your cue cards — five words each, numbered.\n- Mark // for every pause.\n- Memorise your opening sentence and your call to action, word for word.\n- Run it once quietly from the cards.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Say your opening line to your neighbour without looking down. That's the hardest bit done.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Speaking to engage**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners with specific details — which requires looking at them, and therefore not reading from a page.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Self-regulation**: \"Use discussion and self-talk to find solutions for challenges\" — managing nerves with preparation rather than avoidance.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Rehearsal With Feedback":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Rehearsal With Feedback\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to give and act on feedback about delivery, not just content.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Recraft, Edit, Rehearse  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Recrafting on the page and on your feet |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): listening and responding; making comments that encourage discussion; considering the impact of our words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were the polish tools. Today the polish is on your voice, not your page.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's left? (Publishing it, and standing up.)*\n\n#### I Do — Two Stars and a Pause (3 min)\n\nThe rehearsal feedback protocol. Only three things, and one is a pause.\n\n> \"One thing you did well with your VOICE…\" · \"One thing you did well with your EYES…\" · \"One place where a pause would help.\"\n\nNotice it's two positives and one specific technical suggestion. Delivery feedback lands very personally — \"you were boring\" is useless and cruel. \"A pause after your number would help\" is something anyone can do tomorrow.\n\n**Tip:** Feedback on delivery names a MOVE, never a personality.\n\n#### We Do — Practise on Me (4 min)\n\nI'll deliver 30 seconds. You give me the three responses.\n\n- Voice star.\n- Eyes star.\n- One place for a pause.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did anyone want to say something about my content? Save it — today is delivery only.\n\n#### You Do — Rehearse in Fours (13 min)\n\nGroups of four, standing, cue cards only. Everyone runs their whole speech once.\n\n- Deliver your speech from cue cards — no script.\n- Listeners: two delivery stars and one pause suggestion.\n- Note your time.\n- Write one delivery goal for next week on card 1.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: it gets typed, and then you stand up for real.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Constructive response**: NZC Phase 2: comments that encourage discussion — here about delivery, where feedback can feel personal and must be handled carefully.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Encouragement**: \"Make space for others to contribute\" — a rehearsal audience's job is to make the next speaker braver, not more nervous.\n"}}},"Week 10: Publish & Deliver":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 10 — Publish & Deliver\n\n> **Essential question:** Can my words move someone?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Typing and Sending · Tue: Build Together: Final Proof · Wed: Stretch: Dress Rehearsal · Thu: The Celebration: Speech Day · Fri: Reflect: Three Genres, One Process |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Publish it, then deliver it. Same argument, two forms — one for reading, one for hearing.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Is my filename right — Surname_Firstname_Persuasive_T3?\n- Have I read it backwards for errors?\n- Do my cue cards have five words a line and my pauses marked?\n- Do I know my first sentence and my last sentence by heart?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\npublished · delivered · audience · presentation · proofread · projection · pause · persuade · request · respond\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Mrs ____ , …\n- I am asking you to…\n- Thank you for listening.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Deliver from your seat, or to a group of four rather than the whole class. Both count as delivered.\n- **Picture this:** A person standing still, cards in one hand, looking up.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your first line three times before you stand up. Nerves live in the first sentence and nowhere else.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Term review: choosing the best figurative device\n- **Tuesday** — Term review: mixed grammar\n- **Wednesday** — Term review: constructing a flexible paragraph\n- **Thursday** — Whole-piece edit: applying the term's checklist\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Typing and Sending":{"content":"### Launch: Typing and Sending\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to publish a persuasive text properly — and to consider actually sending it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Deliver  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Term review: choosing the best figurative device |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — publishing: transferring an edited handwritten text to digital format, maintaining conventions and accuracy for a real audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTerm review of figurative devices this morning — check you've used one or two deliberately, not five by accident.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the very end. What does publishing mean this term? (Two things: the page goes to them, and I stand up and say it.)*\n\n#### I Do — Type It Like It's Going Somewhere (6 min)\n\nScreen up. Watch the order — and the last step, which is the one that matters.\n\n> Title → their name and title → the date → save as Surname_Firstname_Persuasive_T3 → type from the book copy.\n\nSave the empty document first. Then type from the best copy — don't rewrite as you type. And here's the real question: is yours good enough to actually send? Some of you have arguments that deserve to reach the person they name. We'll talk about that on Thursday.\n\n**Tip:** Check the spelling of your audience's name. Twice.\n\n#### We Do — Filename and Standard (5 min)\n\nAgree the convention and the presentation standard together.\n\n- Surname_Firstname_Persuasive_T3\n- Named recipient, date, signed.\n- One clear font; paragraphs separated.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If this were going to be handed to a real adult, what else would it need?\n\n#### You Do — Start Typing (10 min)\n\nType from your book copy. Cue cards stay on your desk — you'll need them Wednesday.\n\n- Create, title and save the document before typing.\n- Type from your handwritten best copy.\n- Save at every paragraph.\n- Check the recipient's name and title carefully.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Save. Then save again.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **A real audience**: NZC Phase 2: writing with the audience in mind — and here, potentially, actually delivering it to them, which is what makes the whole unit real.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Organisation**: Saving with an agreed filename in an agreed place; preparing a document that could be handed to an adult.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Final Proof":{"content":"### Build Together: Final Proof\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to proofread a finished text using a technique that catches what our eyes skip.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Deliver  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Term review: mixed grammar |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): final edits using known punctuation, with peer checking. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nMixed grammar review this morning — everything you've been taught this term is on the checklist you're about to use.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Two days until you deliver. What still needs doing?*\n\n#### I Do — Read It Backwards (5 min)\n\nLast proofreading trick of the year, and the strangest.\n\n> Start at the last sentence. Read it. Then the one before. Work up to the top.\n\nReading backwards breaks the argument, so my brain stops predicting — and prediction is why we can't see our own mistakes. Eleven readings, and I've just found \"there\" instead of \"their\" in my second paragraph.\n\n**Tip:** Backwards for errors. Forwards for flow. Different jobs.\n\n#### We Do — Partner Proof Protocol (5 min)\n\nAgree the swap, then agree the delivery order for Thursday.\n\n- Swap; read theirs backwards; mark, don't fix.\n- Then: draw for speaking order, so nobody is waiting all week to find out.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Would you rather go first or last? Both are fine — but decide now, not on the day.\n\n#### You Do — Proof, Check, Print (11 min)\n\nFinish and proof the document, then print two copies: one to hand in, one to keep.\n\n- Proofread backwards.\n- Partner check — swap and mark.\n- Fix what they found.\n- Print or save to the class folder.\n- Finished? Rehearse your speech quietly from cards.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow is dress rehearsal. Bring your cards, not your script.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Final proofreading**: NZC Phase 2 editing expectations applied to a finished, published text, including a peer check.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Variety check**: A final read for sentence variety and opener repetition — Phase 2 writers use a range of structures for impact.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Dress Rehearsal":{"content":"### Stretch: Dress Rehearsal\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to deliver a full speech to a real audience under real conditions.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Deliver  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Term review: constructing a flexible paragraph |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners with specific details and precise vocabulary; vary sentence structure and length for effect; respond to an audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nConstructing a flexible paragraph this morning — your speech is one flexible paragraph after another, held together by signposts.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Anything you stumble on today goes back to which stage? (SHAPE — fix it tonight.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Full Thing, Properly (4 min)\n\nI'll deliver my whole speech exactly as I would tomorrow. Watch the beginning and the end especially.\n\n> [Stand. Wait for silence. Breathe. Opening line to the back wall. Pause after the number. Cards low. End on the ask, then stop.]\n\nTwo things students almost always skip: waiting for silence before starting, and stopping cleanly at the end instead of trailing off with \"um, yeah, thanks\". Wait, deliver, stop. The silence at the start is yours — take it.\n\n**Tip:** Wait for silence. Deliver. Stop. Don't apologise at either end.\n\n#### We Do — The Audience Contract (3 min)\n\nAgree how we listen tomorrow, and hold it today.\n\n- Total silence while someone speaks.\n- Applause for every single speaker, the same for everyone.\n- Afterwards, one person names one specific thing they'll remember.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What would make you feel safest standing up tomorrow?\n\n#### You Do — Dress Rehearsal in Halves (15 min)\n\nHalf the class speaks to the other half, then swap. Cards only, standing, timed.\n\n- Deliver your full speech to half the class.\n- Note your time and any stumble.\n- Fix your cards tonight if a card let you down.\n- Practise your opening line five more times before tomorrow.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone who wants to rehearse again at lunchtime, I'll be here. Tomorrow you'll be glad you did.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging an audience**: NZC Phase 2: giving descriptions and reasoning aloud with details that actively engage listeners, sustained across a two-minute delivery.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Managing nerves**: Rehearsing under conditions close to the real thing is the single most effective way to reduce performance anxiety.\n"},"Thursday: The Celebration: Speech Day":{"content":"### The Celebration: Speech Day\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to deliver our argument to a live audience and ask for what we want.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Deliver  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Whole-piece edit: applying the term's checklist |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively; use precise vocabulary; respond to questions. Writing: publication and delivery for a real audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYour last warm-up was a whole-piece edit — and today the piece is finished. Look what you made.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the last step of the last stage, for the third time this year. Say all four stages once more.*\n\n#### I Do — Set It Up as an Event (3 min)\n\nConsider inviting the audience: the principal, another class, whānau, or the person named in the speeches. I'll speak first.\n\n> [Deliver your own two-minute speech, properly, to the room.]\n\nIf the person you're arguing to is actually in the room, that changes everything — for the better. And if a speech deserves to be sent or delivered for real afterwards, we will do that. This is not a pretend exercise.\n\n**Tip:** Teacher goes first. It makes the first student speaker far braver.\n\n#### We Do — The Audience's Job (2 min)\n\nTwo rules, agreed before the first speaker stands.\n\n- Silence while someone speaks; applause for everyone, equally.\n- After each speech, one person names one specific thing they'll remember.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What does \"specific\" mean?\n\n#### You Do — Deliver (17 min)\n\nEvery student delivers. Two minutes each — split across today and tomorrow for a full class.\n\n- Wait for silence. Breathe. Deliver from your cards.\n- End on your ask, then stop.\n- Take the applause without apologising.\n- Listen to every other speaker.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nName the value out loud: this is Manaaki — serving with the gift you've got. Some of you asked for something today that somebody can actually give. Soli Deo Gloria.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Sustained spoken delivery**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners with specific detail across a sustained spoken text — the term's oral assessment.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Contributing to a community**: Contributing to healthy communities by taking responsible and critical action — an argument, made publicly, asking for a change.\n"},"Friday: Reflect: Three Genres, One Process":{"content":"### Reflect: Three Genres, One Process\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to explain our writing process across three genres and say what never changes.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 3 — Persuasive & Speeches: “Words That Change Things” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish & Deliver  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publication and performance |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" This lesson assesses process knowledge across the year. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nLook back at three terms of warm-ups. Every single one of them turned up inside a real piece of writing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages, in your own words, with an example from each of the three terms.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Assessment — three genres, one process (5 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered. All three criteria charts visible. This is the assessment.\n\n1. Give the task exactly as written.\n   > \"Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Then explain what each stage looked like in all three terms — a story, an explanation and a speech — and say what stayed exactly the same every time.\"\n2. Out loud first: 90 seconds each to a partner, both directions. Listen in on five or six students; that listening is your record for most of the class.\n3. Then in writing, as questions 1–3 of the reflection.\n4. Finish with the chant, all four, one last time — and then point at the four constants the class generated back in Week 5.\n\n**Watch for:** The top band is the student who can name the constants: you always fill before you draft, you always write to a particular someone, you always read it as the person who doesn't already agree, and you always test it on a real human before you publish. Record on the \"Process\" row of the rubric.\n\n#### I Do — What Never Changed (3 min)\n\nMy reflection first, and it's the same shape as the one I'm asking you for.\n\n> \"Three terms, three completely different pieces. And every single time: I filled before I drafted, I wrote to one particular person, I read it as someone who didn't already agree, and I tested it on a real human being before I published.\"\n\nThat's the thing worth carrying into Term 4 and into high school. The genres change. The process doesn't. If you can say that sentence, you know something about writing that a lot of adults don't.\n\n**Tip:** Name a stage, name a term, name a moment.\n\n#### We Do — Three Charts, Side by Side (5 min)\n\nPut all three criteria charts up together — narrative, explanation, argument. Compare.\n\n- What's on ALL THREE lists?\n- What's only on one?\n- Which term did you find hardest, and why?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What turns out to be true of all good writing, whatever you're writing?\n\n#### You Do — The Writer's Reflection (12 min)\n\nIn your jotter, then your portfolio. This one covers the whole year so far.\n\n- Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it.\n- Give one real example of each stage from a different term.\n- What stayed the same in all three genres?\n- The issue I chose was… and I chose it because…\n- The hardest part of standing up was… and what helped was…\n- One thing I can do now that I couldn't do in Term 1 is…\n- My goal for Term 4 is…\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRead the Soli Deo Gloria line together: we were given words, and this term we used them to serve somebody. Close in prayer or a shared thank-you.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflecting on process**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection on content and process — across three genres, which is where transfer becomes visible.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition and transfer**: \"Being aware of your own thinking\" — recognising the invariant structure beneath three very different pieces of writing.\n"}}}}},"Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection":{"label":"Week","children":{"Unit Plan & Overview":{"content":"### Term 4 Unit Plan — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts”\n\n\n| Unit | |\n|---|---|\n| **Year level** | Years 5–6 (ages 9–12) · NZC English Phase 2 |\n| **Duration** | 10 weeks · 25 min × 5 days |\n| **Universal concept** | Attention and Gratitude — Noticing Is the Beginning of Everything |\n| **Anchor value** | Pānga — Impact: bringing God's Kingdom to earth |\n| **Throughline question** | “What did I notice this year that nobody else did — and can I make it land in ten words?” |\n| **Published outcome** | TWO things. (1) An ANTHOLOGY: each student polishes three poems, handwritten as best copies (Week 9) and typed for a class anthology (Week 10), performed aloud at an end-of-year celebration. (2) A YEAR-IN-REVIEW REFLECTION: students choose their best piece from all four terms and write honestly about how they have changed as a writer. |\n\n#### Key Scripture\n\n- Psalm 8:3–4 — \"When I consider your heavens… what is mankind that you are mindful of them?\" (Week 1: noticing as worship)\n- Matthew 6:28 — \"Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow\" (Week 2: look closely at small things)\n- Philippians 4:8 — \"Whatever is true, noble, right, pure, lovely… think on these things\" (Week 5: what we choose to notice)\n- Psalm 19:14 — \"May the words of my mouth and the meditation of my heart be pleasing to you\" (Week 8: reading aloud)\n- 1 Thessalonians 5:18 — \"Give thanks in all circumstances\" (Week 10: the year's last word is gratitude)\n\n#### NZC English — Phase 2 links\n\n- Writing craft — use figurative language such as simile and metaphor to purposefully create vivid images and effects to engage the reader\n- Writing craft (word choice) — select and use words and phrases that clearly express where and when things happen; vocabulary chosen deliberately for effect, including connotation and precision\n- Sentence structures — use a range of sentence structures and types to suit purpose and enhance impact; demonstrate understanding of incomplete (fragment) sentences and use them deliberately\n- Composition — identify the audience and purpose for a text; understand that different audiences may respond very differently to the same text\n- Writing processes (Revising) — reread at sentence, line and whole-text level; revise by replacing words and cutting; use peer feedback\n- Writing processes (Editing) — edit using known punctuation; use a dictionary for unknown spellings\n- Writing processes (Planning & goals) — set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes\n- Transcription — handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency, maintaining legibility for publication\n- Reading — respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts; determine how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements (layout, line breaks, white space)\n- Oral language — use precise vocabulary; vary sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning; engage listeners when reading aloud\n\n#### The arc of the term\n\n\n| Weeks | Stage | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weeks 1–2 | Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing | What poetry actually does; the noticing jotter; small things seen closely; fresh images instead of borrowed ones. No finished poems yet. |\n| Week 3 | Stage 1 → 2 — Form & audience | Line breaks, stanzas and white space; choosing forms; who is this for and who will hear it read aloud? |\n| Weeks 4–6 | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting three poems | A small-thing poem · a wonder/gratitude poem · a person-or-year poem. Draft set complete by Week 6. |\n| Week 7 | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Cutting | Does every word earn its place? Deconstruct model poems, test line breaks aloud, cut hard. |\n| Week 8 | Stage 3 → 4 — SHARE · Feedback & voice | Helping Circle with the poetry lens, then learning to read a poem aloud so it lands. |\n| Weeks 9–10 | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publish, reflect, celebrate | Best copies and the anthology; the year-in-review reflection; the celebration performance. |\n\n#### The writing process this term\n\nFourth genre, same four stages, final term. Poetry is where students discover that the process is bigger than the rules: a poem can be a fragment, can ignore a full stop, can be nine words long — and it still goes through FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE. That is the year's last and best lesson about writing.\n\n**FILL · FIND · SHAPE · SHARE** — Unchanged for the fourth term. Four genres, one process. By Week 10 students should be able to explain it, give an example of every stage from every term, and name the four things that never changed.\n\n\n| Stage | Question | Hand action | What a writer does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **🪣 FILL** | What do I want to say? | Two cupped hands, like holding water. | I notice. I slow down and look at something small until I see it properly — and I write down exactly what I saw, not what I'm supposed to say about it. |\n| **🎯 FIND** | Who do I want to say it to? | Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader. | I find my reader — and this term they will HEAR it, not just read it. I also find the shape: where the lines break, where the white space goes, how it looks on the page. |\n| **🔍 SHAPE** | How do I want to say it? | Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it. | I ask one question about every single word: does this earn its place? In a poem, cutting is the main way of improving. Nine good words beat thirty ordinary ones. |\n| **🎁 SHARE** | Is it ready to give away? | Open both hands and push them forward, offering. | I read it to people and watch what lands. Then I make it beautiful on the page, put it in our anthology, and read it out loud at the celebration — and I write honestly about how I've changed as a writer this year. |\n\n#### Teaching & recall schedule\n\n\n| Week | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | COLD RECALL — the fourth and final one. Then re-establish the daily Process Check. |\n| **Week 2** | TRANSFER — FILL for poetry. You are not collecting facts or memories now. You are collecting things you actually SAW. |\n| **Week 3** | TRANSFER — FIND for poetry. The reader will HEAR this; the shape is on the page as well as in the words. |\n| **Week 4** | SPACED RECALL — quick-fire, no notes. |\n| **Week 5** | FOUR GENRES, ONE PROCESS — each stage explained four ways; the four constants revisited. |\n| **Week 6** | SPACED RECALL + move the peg, justified out loud. |\n| **Week 7** | TRANSFER — SHAPE for poetry. Cutting is the main move, and it is the hardest one to do to your own work. |\n| **Week 8** | TRANSFER — SHARE for poetry. A poem is not finished until someone has heard it. |\n| **Week 9** | THE WHOLE YEAR — the cycle drawn one final time, over four terms of the students' own work. |\n| **Week 10** | ASSESS + HAND IT ON — explain the process across four genres, then write it down for next year's Year 5s. |\n\n#### Assessment — Week 10 — four genres, one process, and hand it on\n\n> Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Give an example of every stage from a different term. Say what stayed exactly the same in all four genres. Then write the version you would give to next year's Year 5s.\n\n**A strong answer…**\n\n- Names all four stages in order with the question that belongs to each\n- Gives a real example of each stage, drawn from at least three of the four terms\n- Names the four constants: you always fill before you draft · you always write to a particular someone · you always read it as the person who doesn't already agree · you always test it on a real human before you publish\n- Explains the cycle, and can say what sends a writer backwards\n- Writes a version a Year 5 could actually follow next February\n\n#### Marking load across the term\n\n\n| When | What | Teacher time |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 2 Fri** | Jotter glance — is the noticing bank full of things actually seen, or general statements? No written marking. | ~10 min for the class |\n| **Week 6 Thu** | Draft-set check: three poems drafted — On track / Needs a conference / Flying. | ~15 sec per student |\n| **Week 8** | You mark nothing — the Helping Circle carries the feedback. | 0 |\n| **Week 10** | Anthology set + reflection against the rubric: tick criteria, one glow, one grow. Reading-aloud row scored live at the celebration. | ~4 min per student, much of it during the performance |\n\n#### Single-point rubric\n\n\n| Success criterion | What a strong piece does | Glow ✓ | Grow → |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Noticing** | The poem is built on something the writer actually saw, heard or felt — not on a general idea about a topic | | |\n| **Image** | At least one image is fresh and precise enough that a reader can see it; comparisons are the writer's own, not borrowed | | |\n| **Word choice** | Every word earns its place. Precise verbs and concrete nouns; no filler, no decoration | | |\n| **Line breaks & shape** | Lines break where the poem needs breath or emphasis; the shape on the page is a deliberate choice | | |\n| **Sound** | Rhythm, repetition or sound patterning used on purpose — the poem is written for the ear | | |\n| **Honesty & voice** | It sounds like this writer and no one else; it says something true rather than something expected | | |\n| **Ending** | The last line lands — it turns, surprises or settles, rather than explaining the poem | | |\n| **Range** | Three poems that are genuinely different from each other in subject, form or mood | | |\n| **Presentation** | Best copy and anthology page laid out with care: title, name, deliberate spacing, legible | | |\n| **Reading aloud** | Read so it lands: slow, clear, with pauses at the line breaks and a held silence at the end | | |\n| **Reflection** | The year-in-review piece is specific and evidenced — names real pieces, real changes and real next steps | | |\n| **Process** | Can name the four stages, explain which one they are in and why, and how the process worked across all four genres | | |\n\n**Overall:** ☐ Working towards ☐ Meeting Phase 2 expectations ☐ Exceeding\n\n#### The Helping Circle\n\n- The writer reads the poem aloud — twice, slowly, with a pause between readings. Poetry always gets two readings.\n- Silence for five seconds. Everybody just sits with it. (This is the hardest rule and the most important one.)\n- Two stars — each listener names the image, line or sound that stayed with them.\n- One wish — each listener names a place where a word could go, or where they lost the picture.\n- The writer says thank you and writes it down. No explaining what the poem \"means\" — if it needs explaining, that is useful information.\n\n**Stars — listener prompts**\n\n- The image I can still see is…\n- The line I'd remember tomorrow is…\n- I liked the sound of…\n- The ending made me…\n\n**Wishes — listener prompts**\n\n- I lost the picture at…\n- I think ____ could go — the poem would still say it.\n- That comparison felt like one I'd heard before.\n- Could the line break somewhere else here?\n- The last line explained it to me — could you trust me instead?\n\n*Two readings, then five seconds of silence. We talk about the poem, never the poet. \"I lost the picture\" is always allowed. Nobody has to explain their poem, and nobody may ask them to.*\n\n#### Differentiation\n\n\n| If a student… | Then… |\n|---|---|\n| \"I can't think of anything\" | Don't ask for an idea, ask for an object. Put something real on their desk — a leaf, a key, a shoe — and give them two minutes to write only what they can see. Poetry begins with looking, not with inspiration. |\n| Only writes rhyming poems | Not a problem to ban — a problem to test. \"Read it aloud and cross out every word you chose only because it rhymed.\" Whatever survives is the poem. Most students choose free verse voluntarily after that. |\n| Writes one long block | Read it aloud and mark every place they breathe. Those are the line breaks. This one move converts prose into a poem faster than any explanation. |\n| Struggles with transcription | This is the term that suits them best — a strong nine-line poem is a complete, publishable piece. Volume genuinely does not matter here, and it is worth saying so out loud. |\n| Writes something raw or private | Poetry invites this. Two rules: nobody has to share anything, and a poem may be published under \"Anonymous\" in the anthology. Have a quiet word rather than a public one, and follow your school's pastoral process if it raises a concern. |\n| Finishes early every week | A fourth poem in a form they have never tried, or become an anthology editor — laying out other people's poems teaches white space faster than any lesson. |\n| Can't explain the process | Four terms of their own work now exists. Open the portfolio, put the four pieces side by side, and walk the stages across them. The pattern is unmissable with the evidence in front of them. |\n\n#### Ten-week overview\n\n\n| Week | Focus | Stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Week 1** | Looking Properly | Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Week 2** | Fresh Images | Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Week 3** | The Shape on the Page | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Week 4** | Poem One: A Small Thing | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Week 5** | Sound | Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Week 6** | Poem Three: Someone, or a Year | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Week 7** | Cutting | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Week 8** | The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Week 9** | Best Copies and the Year Behind Us | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Week 10** | Publish, Perform, Hand It On | Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n\n*The full ACS unit-planning document (Word and Markdown) downloads from the Writing Focus app toolbar and from the Writing Hub landing page.*\n"},"Week 1: Looking Properly":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 1 — Looking Properly\n\n> **Essential question:** What does a poem actually do?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: What a Poem Actually Does · Tue: Build Together: Two Minutes of Looking · Wed: Stretch: Show Me, Don't Tell Me About It · Thu: Write: Noticings Into Lines · Fri: Share & Reflect: Two Readings and a Silence |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Write down three things you actually saw today that nobody else in this room noticed.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What did you look at for longer than a second today?\n- What was in the corner of the picture, not the middle?\n- What was small, ordinary, and still there when you looked twice?\n- Which of your three would you like to look at again tomorrow?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\ncracked · folded · peeling · still · crooked · worn · bare · stacked · tilted · bent\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- On the way to school I saw…\n- Nobody else noticed…\n- It was still there when I looked again.\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Pick something within two metres of you right now — a shoe, a chair leg, a window catch, a crack in the paint — and write only what you can see. No feelings, no meaning.\n- **Picture this:** A cicada shell still gripping a fence post.\n- **Talk it out first:** Describe one object to your partner so precisely that they could pick it out of a pile of twenty.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Precise verb choice: replacing weak verbs\n- **Tuesday** — Colons — introducing lists and elaboration\n- **Wednesday** — Using a colon to introduce and expand an idea\n- **Thursday** — Editing: upgrading weak verbs\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n#### Teacher note\n\nMentor texts this term: SHORT poems, read aloud twice, all term. NZ options — Hone Tuwhare, Glenn Colquhoun, a School Journal poem, or any short free-verse poem about an ordinary object. Avoid famous rhyming poems in Weeks 1–3: students copy the rhyme and lose the looking. Keep the strong exemplar (Teacher tab) beside the weak one for Week 7.\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: What a Poem Actually Does":{"content":"### Launch: What a Poem Actually Does\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a poem shows something real rather than describing something general.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Looking Properly  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Precise verb choice: replacing weak verbs |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts; determine how ideas are conveyed through text features. Writing craft: figurative language used purposefully to create vivid images. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPrecise verbs this morning — poems are almost entirely made of them. \"The shell is holding on\" does more than four adjectives could.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Fourth term, same four stages. What are we filling up with this time? (Things we actually saw.)*\n\n#### I Do — Two Poems, One Subject (6 min)\n\nTwo short poems about nature. I'll read both twice. Your job is to work out which one the writer actually looked at something to write.\n\n> A: \"Nature is so beautiful and amazing, / The trees are green and the sky is blue…\"\nB: \"The cicada shell / is still holding on / to the fence post, / legs locked, / empty as a glove.\"\n\nA could have been written indoors, in about a minute, by anyone. B could only have been written by someone who stopped and looked at one particular shell on one particular fence. Look at the words: \"legs locked\". Nobody writes that unless they've seen it. And \"empty as a glove\" — that's a comparison I've never heard before, which means it's this writer's own.\n\n**Tip:** A poem is proof that somebody looked.\n\n#### We Do — Build the \"What a Poem Does\" Chart (7 min)\n\nCall out what you noticed about poem B. I'll scribe. This chart stays up all term.\n\n- It shows one small real thing, not a big general topic.\n- It uses ordinary words in an exact order.\n- It never tells you how to feel about it.\n- It stops before it explains itself.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those four do you think will be hardest for us to do?\n\n#### You Do — The Noticing Jotter (8 min)\n\nNew section at the back of your jotter: NOTICINGS. Three things you actually saw today. Not what they mean — just what they were.\n\n- Write 3 things you genuinely saw today, in one line each.\n- Cross out any word that says how you FELT about it.\n- Star the one you could still describe with your eyes shut.\n- ✦ Bonus: one thing you saw that you'd never noticed before.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nThree students read one noticing. No commentary — we just listen and see it.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Making connections and interpretations* — **Responding to texts**: NZC Phase 2: \"respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts\" — with poetry the response comes first and the analysis second.\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Vivid images**: NZC Phase 2: \"use figurative language such as simile and metaphor to purposefully create vivid images and effects to engage the reader.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Two Minutes of Looking":{"content":"### Build Together: Two Minutes of Looking\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that looking for longer produces better writing than thinking harder.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Looking Properly  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Colons — introducing lists and elaboration |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — word choice (Phase 2): select and use precise words and phrases; vocabulary chosen deliberately for effect. Loane Ch 7: imaging and using the senses. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nColons this morning — poets use them to open a door: \"The fence post holds one thing: an empty shell.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Why aren't we writing poems yet? (Because you can't write about something you haven't looked at properly.)*\n\n#### I Do — Watch Me Look at a Leaf (6 min)\n\nOne ordinary object, two minutes of looking, out loud. Count how the noticings change as the time goes on.\n\n> 0–20 seconds: \"It's a leaf. It's green. It's got veins.\"\n60 seconds: \"One edge is chewed. There's a hole shaped like a comma.\"\n120 seconds: \"The stem is darker where it was attached. It's curling from one side only, like a hand starting to close.\"\n\nThe first twenty seconds got me nothing — those are the words everyone has. The good stuff arrived after a minute, when I'd run out of obvious things to say and had to actually look. That's the whole technique. Poets aren't more imaginative. They just stay longer.\n\n**Tip:** Your first three noticings are everyone's. Keep going.\n\n#### We Do — Two Minutes on One Object (7 min)\n\nEveryone gets the same object (or looks at the same thing in the room). Two minutes of silent looking, then we collect noticings in the order they were found.\n\n- First 20 seconds — what did everyone get?\n- After a minute — what started appearing?\n- Whose noticing could only have come from looking?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why did the good noticings come last?\n\n#### You Do — Your Own Two Minutes (7 min)\n\nChoose an object you can hold, or something in the room. Two minutes, silent, pen moving.\n\n- Write everything you can see. Not one word about feelings.\n- Circle the three noticings that came after the first minute.\n- Add one detail you can only get by touching it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read the noticing that surprised you. Was it early or late?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precision**: NZC Phase 2: vocabulary chosen deliberately for effect, with attention to precision — poetry is the genre where imprecision is most visible.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Making thinking visible**: Imaging and close observation make internal noticing visible and writable — the Stage 1 strategy named in the ACS writing process.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Show Me, Don't Tell Me About It":{"content":"### Stretch: Show Me, Don't Tell Me About It\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to replace a statement about something with an image of it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Looking Properly  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Using a colon to introduce and expand an idea |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): create vivid images; select words that express where and when things happen. Sentence structures: fragments used deliberately for effect. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nColons to expand an idea this morning — and a poetry note: in a poem, a line break can do the same job as a colon, with no punctuation at all.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\n> **⚠ Teach the Process Spotlight below FIRST today.** Teach the spotlight FIRST today. The recall must happen before anything reminds the class of the answer — including the routine itself. Normal order resumes tomorrow.\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. What does a writer DO in this stage — and what does it look like in poetry?*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Cold recall — the fourth and final one (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip up but with no peg. Say nothing about it until step 3.\n\n1. \"Four stages, in order, in your jotter. No notes, no looking at the wall. Sixty seconds.\" Everyone writes.\n2. Count hands for four, three, two. It should be almost everyone now. Say so, and say why: \"That is what about a hundred and fifty retrievals does. You will still have this next year, and the year after.\"\n3. Chant twice, with actions.\n4. Then the term's idea, which is the year's biggest one.\n   > \"This term is poetry. A poem can be nine words long. It can be a fragment. It can ignore a full stop. And it STILL goes through all four stages. That's how you know the process belongs to writing itself, not to any one kind of writing.\"\n5. A student places the peg on Stage 1 and says why.\n\n**Watch for:** The point to make and repeat: poetry is the genre that looks least like the others, which makes it the strongest test of the process. If it survives here, it survives anywhere.\n\n#### I Do — Statement into Image (5 min)\n\nFour statements, each turned into something a reader can see. Watch what I have to know in order to do it.\n\n> \"The classroom was messy.\" → \"Someone's jersey has been under the same chair since Tuesday.\"\n\"My grandad is old.\" → \"He peels an orange in one piece.\"\n\"It was cold.\" → \"The gate handle stuck to my hand.\"\n\nEach swap needs a real detail — which means I can't do it from my desk. The statement version can be written by anyone about anything. The image version can only be written by someone who was there. That's why we spent yesterday looking.\n\n**Tip:** If your line could be true of a hundred classrooms, it isn't a poem line yet.\n\n#### We Do — Turn These Into Images (7 min)\n\nStatements on the board. Together we replace each with something seeable, using details from our own school.\n\n- \"The playground is fun.\"\n- \"Our classroom is busy in the morning.\"\n- \"It rained a lot last week.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which image made the room go \"ooh\"? What did it have that the others didn't?\n\n#### You Do — Swap Your Statements (9 min)\n\nBack to your noticings. Hunt for statements hiding among them.\n\n- Find any noticing that is really a statement (nice, boring, beautiful, fun).\n- Replace two of them with something a reader could see.\n- Write three new noticings, image-only.\n- ✦ Bonus: write one that is a fragment — no verb at all. Notice that it still works.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your best image. Class: could you see it?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Concrete over abstract**: NZC Phase 2: words chosen for precision and effect; concrete nouns and precise verbs carry meaning that abstract statements only assert.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Fragments for effect**: NZC Phase 2: \"demonstrate understanding of incomplete (fragment) and run-on sentences\" — poetry is where a fragment becomes a deliberate, correct choice.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Noticings Into Lines":{"content":"### Write: Noticings Into Lines\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a poem often begins as a few true lines with the boring parts removed.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Looking Properly  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: upgrading weak verbs |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): drafting from collected material; layout and line as a compositional choice. Writing craft: word choice for effect. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nWeak verbs got upgraded this morning. In a poem, the verb is usually the only interesting word in the line — so choose it last and choose it hard.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Is what you write today a finished poem? (No — it's raw material. We're still collecting.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cross Out Until It's a Poem (5 min)\n\nOne of my noticings, written as prose. Watch me delete rather than add.\n\n> BEFORE: \"There was an old cicada shell that was still stuck onto the fence post outside and its legs were locked on really tightly and it was completely empty inside like an empty glove.\"\nAFTER: \"The cicada shell / is still holding on / to the fence post, / legs locked, / empty as a glove.\"\n\nI added nothing. I deleted \"there was\", \"that was\", \"really\", \"completely\", \"inside\" — and then I broke the lines where my voice paused. Thirty-two words became fourteen, and now you can see it. Poetry is mostly subtraction.\n\n**Tip:** Write it as a sentence first if that's easier. Then cross out everything that isn't doing a job.\n\n#### We Do — Cross Out Together (5 min)\n\nOne student's noticing, written as prose on the board. The class calls out what to delete.\n\n- Which words are just holding the sentence together?\n- Which adjective is doing nothing that the noun isn't already doing?\n- Where does your voice pause? That's a line break.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did deleting change the meaning, or just uncover it?\n\n#### You Do — Three Sets of Lines (11 min)\n\nTake three noticings and turn each one into a few lines. Not poems yet — just lines that work.\n\n- Write each noticing as one prose sentence.\n- Cross out every word not doing a job.\n- Break the lines where your voice pauses.\n- Read all three aloud, quietly, and mark the one that sounds best.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose piece got shorter and better? That's the whole term in one lesson.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **From notes into text**: NZC Phase 2: transferring collected material into composed text — in poetry, by selecting and arranging rather than expanding.\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Layout and visual elements**: NZC Phase 2: \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements\" — line breaks and white space are those features in a poem.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Two Readings and a Silence":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Two Readings and a Silence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning how to listen to a poem, and how to say what stayed with us.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 1 — Looking Properly  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Noticing |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~22 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners; use precise vocabulary. Reading: respond to texts by sharing personal thoughts and feelings; acknowledge others' responses respectfully. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were all about precision — exact verbs, colons that open an idea. Poems live on that.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Name one thing in your noticing jotter that wasn't there on Monday.*\n\n#### I Do — The Two-Readings Rule (4 min)\n\nEvery poem we ever read in this room gets read twice, with a silence after it. Here's why.\n\n> Read the mentor poem. Pause five seconds. Read it again. Pause five seconds. Only then speak.\n\nThe first reading you're just working out what's happening. The second one is where you actually hear it. And the silence afterwards is not awkward — it's the poem still landing. We're going to be a class that can sit in a five-second silence, and that takes practice.\n\n**Tip:** Never respond to a poem after one reading. Nobody can.\n\n#### We Do — Practise the Silence (5 min)\n\nI read a short poem twice. We hold five seconds. Then two people say what stayed with them.\n\n- \"The image I can still see is…\"\n- \"The line I'd remember tomorrow is…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How did the second reading differ from the first?\n\n#### You Do — Lines Circle (10 min)\n\nGroups of four. Read your best set of lines twice, with a silence. Listeners use only the two star sentences.\n\n- Read your lines twice, slowly.\n- Hold the silence — count to five in your head.\n- Listeners: name the image or line that stayed.\n- Write down the exact words people said stayed with them.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week we go hunting for comparisons nobody has used before.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Responding to a text**: NZC Phase 2: making comments that encourage discussion — with poetry, the first response is an image or a feeling, not an interpretation.\n- *Reading — Making connections* — **Personal response**: NZC Phase 2: \"respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts.\"\n"}}},"Week 2: Fresh Images":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 2 — Fresh Images\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I make a comparison that is mine and nobody else's?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Borrowed vs True · Tue: Build Together: Making the Comparison Do a Job · Wed: Stretch: Nouns and Verbs Do the Work · Thu: Write: The Image Bank · Fri: Share & Reflect: The Image Gallery |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Find a comparison for something ordinary that nobody in this room would have thought of.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What does it actually look like — not what is it supposed to look like?\n- What else in the world has that same shape, sound, or way of moving?\n- Is my comparison one I've heard before? (If yes, throw it out.)\n- Can I make the comparison do a job — say something true, not just something pretty?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nhunched · spilled · stitched · shrugged · clenched · rusted · humming · swollen · flattened · patient\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- It looks like…\n- It sits there like…\n- The way it ____ reminds me of…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Take one object and finish this five times, fast, without judging: \"It is like ____ .\" The fifth one is usually the good one.\n- **Picture this:** A bike lying on its side with one wheel still turning.\n- **Talk it out first:** Describe an object to your partner without naming it. Their guesses will show you which of your images are working.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Pathetic fallacy: weather and setting reflecting mood\n- **Tuesday** — Verb tense consistency\n- **Wednesday** — Past perfect for flashback\n- **Thursday** — Editing for tense consistency\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Borrowed vs True":{"content":"### Launch: Borrowed vs True\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a worn-out comparison from a true one, and to make our own.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Fresh Images  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Pathetic fallacy: weather and setting reflecting mood |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): \"use figurative language such as simile and metaphor to purposefully create vivid images and effects to engage the reader\" — purposefully being the operative word. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPathetic fallacy this morning — weather matching mood. Careful in poetry: it's powerful once and a cliché twice. Rain does not have to be sad.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Term 2 you collected facts, Term 3 evidence. What now? (Things I actually saw — and comparisons that are mine.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — FILL for poetry (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 1. An ordinary object on your desk — a leaf, a key, a cup.\n\n1. Three columns on the board: MEMORIES (T1) · FACTS (T2) · EVIDENCE (T3). \"What goes in the fourth column?\"\n2. Take answers until someone says something close to \"things you saw\". Write: THINGS I ACTUALLY LOOKED AT.\n3. Hold up the object. \"This is a whole term's material if you look at it for two minutes. That's the difference this term: FILL isn't about remembering or researching, it's about ATTENTION.\"\n   > \"Poets don't have better ideas than everybody else. They just look for longer.\"\n4. Finish with Matthew 6:28 — \"consider the lilies\" — and the term's value, Pānga. \"Noticing is the beginning of everything. You can't be grateful for something you never looked at.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Students who say \"I can't think of anything\" are almost always trying to THINK of something. Redirect them to looking — hand them an object. It works nearly every time.\n\n#### I Do — Five Comparisons, One Object (6 min)\n\nOne object. I'll write five comparisons fast without judging them, then choose. Watch where the good one appears.\n\n> A rolled-up rug: \"like a sleeping bag\" / \"like a big sausage\" / \"like a fallen pillar\" / \"like something wrapped up to be posted\" / \"like a body nobody wants to mention\".\n\nOne and two are the ones everybody gets. Three sounds impressive but means nothing. Four is getting somewhere. Five is uncomfortable and true — and it tells you something about the room the rug is in. The first comparisons out of your head belong to everyone. Keep going to five.\n\n**Tip:** If you've heard it before, it isn't yours. Cross it out and go again.\n\n#### We Do — The Cliché Graveyard (7 min)\n\nWe list every worn-out comparison we can think of, and put them in the graveyard on the wall. Banned all term.\n\n- as busy as a bee · as light as a feather · as old as time\n- hair like silk · eyes like stars · quiet as a mouse\n- Now: give me a TRUE comparison for \"quiet\".\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does a worn-out comparison stop a reader seeing anything at all?\n\n#### You Do — Five Fast, Then Choose (8 min)\n\nTake an object from your noticing jotter. Five comparisons, fast, no judging. Then choose.\n\n- Write \"It is like…\" five times, quickly.\n- Cross out any you've heard before.\n- Circle the one that is truest — not the prettiest.\n- Do the whole thing again with a second object.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your fifth comparison. Not your first — your fifth.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Simile and metaphor**: NZC Phase 2: figurative language used purposefully to create vivid images — which requires the comparison to be the writer's own rather than a stock phrase.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Creative thinking**: \"Creative thinking: imagining a range of options\" — generating five comparisons and choosing the truest is that indicator in practice.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Making the Comparison Do a Job":{"content":"### Build Together: Making the Comparison Do a Job\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a good image says something true, not just something decorative.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Fresh Images  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Verb tense consistency |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): figurative language used purposefully to create effects; vocabulary chosen for connotation as well as meaning. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTense consistency this morning — poems often sit in the present tense, even about the past: \"He peels an orange\" is happening now, every time you read it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Is a comparison something you invent, or something you find? (Find — by looking harder.)*\n\n#### I Do — Pretty vs True (6 min)\n\nTwo comparisons for the same thing. One is prettier. The other one is doing a job.\n\n> My grandfather's hands: \"like soft old leather gloves\" / \"two maps\".\nThe empty classroom at 3.30: \"as quiet as a library\" / \"still warm, like a chair someone just left\".\n\n\"Soft old leather gloves\" is nice and tells you nothing. \"Two maps\" tells you he's been places, that his life is written on him, that I can read it — one two-word image doing the work of a paragraph. Ask of every comparison: what does this let me say without saying it?\n\n**Tip:** The best image says the poem's meaning without ever stating it.\n\n#### We Do — Choose the Working Image (8 min)\n\nPairs of comparisons on the board. Which one is doing a job — and what job?\n\n- The school bag by the door: \"like a rock\" / \"slumped, like it's waiting to be picked up again.\"\n- Rain on the roof: \"like drums\" / \"like someone upstairs who can't sleep.\"\n- A cracked phone screen: \"like a spider web\" / \"like ice you shouldn't stand on.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** In each pair, what does the second image tell you that the first doesn't?\n\n#### You Do — Make Yours Work (7 min)\n\nTake your two circled comparisons and push them one step further.\n\n- For each: write what the comparison lets you say WITHOUT saying it.\n- If it says nothing, generate five more and choose again.\n- Write one line of poetry that uses it — just one line.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your one line. Class: what did it tell you that it didn't say?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Purposeful effect**: NZC Phase 2: figurative language \"to purposefully create vivid images and effects\" — the image must serve the poem's meaning, not decorate it.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Connotation**: Words carry attitude; the comparison a poet chooses tells the reader how to feel without the poet naming a feeling.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Nouns and Verbs Do the Work":{"content":"### Stretch: Nouns and Verbs Do the Work\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that concrete nouns and precise verbs carry a poem, and most adjectives don't.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Fresh Images  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Past perfect for flashback |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft — word choice (Phase 2): select and use words that clearly express where and when things happen; vocabulary chosen for precision. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPast perfect this morning — \"had waited\", \"had gone\". Useful in a poem about something already finished.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. What's the difference between this term's collecting and Term 1's? (Then: memories. Now: exact things, exactly described.)*\n\n#### I Do — Delete Every Adjective (5 min)\n\nMy line, with every adjective removed. Listen to what survives — and what has to get stronger to replace them.\n\n> BEFORE: \"The big old rusty gate made a horrible loud noise in the strong wind.\"\nAFTER: \"The gate shrieked in the wind.\"\nTHEN: \"The gate shrieked all night and nobody came.\"\n\nSix adjectives gone, one verb upgraded, and now you can hear it. And once the clutter was gone I could see what the line was actually about — so I added something true instead. Cutting adjectives doesn't shrink a poem; it makes room in it.\n\n**Tip:** One adjective per line, maximum, and only if the noun can't do it alone.\n\n#### We Do — Strip and Strengthen (7 min)\n\nAdjective-heavy lines on the board. Strip them, then upgrade one verb each.\n\n- \"The small fluffy grey cat sat on the cold hard concrete step.\"\n- \"The bright yellow sun shone down on the lovely green field.\"\n- \"The tall dark scary trees moved in the strong wind.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which stripped line still needs one adjective back — and which one is better bare?\n\n#### You Do — Strip Your Own (9 min)\n\nEvery set of lines you've written so far.\n\n- Delete every adjective. All of them.\n- Read what's left aloud.\n- Put back only the ones the line genuinely needs — usually one or none.\n- Upgrade two weak verbs (was, went, looked, got) to precise ones.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who has a line with no adjectives at all that still works? Read it.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precise verbs**: NZC Phase 2: precise, deliberately chosen vocabulary — the verb carries more meaning per word than any other class, which is why poems live on them.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Economy**: Phase 2 writers use structures for impact; in poetry, impact usually comes from removing rather than adding.\n"},"Thursday: Write: The Image Bank":{"content":"### Write: The Image Bank\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build a bank of images we could turn into poems.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Fresh Images  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing for tense consistency |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — planning (Phase 2): make notes and collect material; set goals from reflection on their own content. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTense consistency got edited this morning — in a poem, pick a tense and stay in it unless you're moving on purpose.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FILL. Last big collecting day. What has to be true by Friday? (I have more images than I need.)*\n\n#### I Do — More Than I Need (4 min)\n\nMy image bank, and the reason it's twice as long as it has to be.\n\n> Fourteen images collected. Three poems will use maybe five of them.\n\nNine of these will never be used, and that's exactly right. A poet who collects three images has to use all three, even the weak one. A poet who collects fourteen gets to choose. Choosing is what makes the poems good — and you can only choose if you've over-collected.\n\n**Tip:** Collect twice as much as you need. Choosing is the luxury that makes it good.\n\n#### We Do — Where Images Come From (3 min)\n\nFast round the room: name a place you could find an image today.\n\n- Somewhere in this room nobody looks at.\n- Something in your bag.\n- Something on the way home.\n- Something a person in your house does the same way every day.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those will give you the best one?\n\n#### You Do — Fill the Bank (14 min)\n\nAim for ten images by the end of the lesson. Two minutes of looking each, minimum.\n\n- Collect 10 images — object, place, or a person's habit.\n- Each one gets a precise verb.\n- Mark the three you'd most like to write about.\n- ✦ For your jotter (faith prompt): what did I notice this week that I would never have noticed in February? (Psalm 8:3–4)\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"How many images have you got? Anyone under six, take your jotter home tonight.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Collecting material**: NZC Phase 2: note-making and collecting as a distinct stage before composition — a poem's material is images rather than facts.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Working independently**: Sustaining a self-directed collecting task and judging your own material against a standard set by the class.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: The Image Gallery":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: The Image Gallery\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge which images are strong enough to build a poem on.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 2 — Fresh Images  ·  Stage 1 — FILL · Comparisons and precision |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🪣 Stage 1 — FILL: “What do I want to say?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Reading & writing (Phase 2): sharing responses to texts; giving and receiving peer feedback; evaluating quality against shared criteria. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week: pathetic fallacy, tense, past perfect. All three showed up in your images whether you noticed or not.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 1 — FILL**: *“What do I want to say?”*  \nHand action: Two cupped hands, like holding water.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 1, FILL — What do I want to say?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FILL. What's next, and what changes? (FIND — my reader, and the shape on the page.)*\n\n#### I Do — Which Images Have a Poem In Them? (3 min)\n\nThree of my images. Only one has a poem inside it, and I'll say why.\n\n> \"A stack of chairs\" — nothing yet. \"The cicada shell holding on\" — something. \"My grandfather peeling an orange in one piece\" — definitely.\n\nThe strong ones have a small mystery in them, or something that isn't finished. The shell asks: what left, and where did it go? The orange asks: why does he do it that way, and why do I care? A poem needs a question living inside the image.\n\n**Tip:** Choose the image you can't stop thinking about, not the one that sounds most poetic.\n\n#### We Do — Test Three Images (5 min)\n\nVolunteers read one image each. The class asks the two questions.\n\n- Can I see it?\n- Is there a question living inside it?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which image made the room want to know more?\n\n#### You Do — Gallery Walk (12 min)\n\nBest three images on a page on your desk. Visit three desks; leave one sticky note each.\n\n- On each note: the image you could see most clearly, and the one you wanted to know more about.\n- Back at your desk: read your notes.\n- Circle the image you'll build your first poem from on Monday.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: line breaks — and how a poem decides what it looks like.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — at the collecting stage, where feedback is cheapest and most useful.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Judging one's own material against the criteria the class built, and choosing rather than settling.\n"}}},"Week 3: The Shape on the Page":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 3 — The Shape on the Page\n\n> **Essential question:** Where should the lines break, and who is going to hear this?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Where the Line Breaks · Tue: Build Together: Stanzas and Silence · Wed: Stretch: Choosing a Form · Thu: Write: The Audience Card, and a First Poem · Fri: Share & Reflect: The Line-Break Test |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Take your best image and find its shape: where do the lines break, and where does the silence go?\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Where does my voice pause when I read it aloud?\n- Which word do I want sitting at the end of a line, where it gets noticed?\n- Where should there be a gap — a whole line of nothing?\n- Who is going to hear this read out, and what will they need?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nline · stanza · break · pause · silence · gap · shape · rhythm · breath · space\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- It sits there /\n- Nobody /\n- And then /\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Write your image as one prose sentence, then break the line every time your voice takes a breath. That is a first draft of a poem.\n- **Picture this:** A poem on a page with a lot of white space around it.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read your lines to a partner twice. Ask them where they heard you pause — mark those spots.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Emotive language\n- **Tuesday** — Conditional sentences\n- **Wednesday** — Counter-argument and rebuttal structure\n- **Thursday** — Editing: strengthening a weak argument\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Where the Line Breaks":{"content":"### Launch: Where the Line Breaks\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a line break is a choice, and that it changes how a poem is read.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — The Shape on the Page  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~27 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Emotive language |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements.\" Writing: layout as a deliberate compositional choice. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEmotive language this morning. Poetry note: a poem earns emotion through images rather than emotive words. Cut \"devastating\"; show me the thing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Two things to find. What are they this term? (My reader — who will HEAR it — and the shape.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — FIND for poetry (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg moved onto Stage 2.\n\n1. \"FIND has meant the reader and the shape, all year. Both change again — and this time the shape is visible.\"\n2. THE READER: Term 1 the school, Term 2 a buddy, Term 3 a decision-maker. This term: an audience who will HEAR it, once, at the celebration. \"What does that mean for your images?\" (They have to land the first time.)\n3. THE SHAPE: not an arc, not a flow chart, not a case — the actual shape on the page. Write a sentence on the board and break it three ways to show that the words don't change but the poem does.\n4. Hand action: \"FIND still points outwards. This term you're pointing at people who are listening.\"\n\n**Watch for:** The insight worth fishing for: this is the first genre where the LAYOUT is part of the meaning. If a student says that, stop and make the class repeat it.\n\n#### I Do — Same Words, Three Shapes (7 min)\n\nOne sentence, broken three different ways. Listen to what changes — the words never do.\n\n> A: \"The gate was open all night and nobody noticed.\"\nB: \"The gate was open / all night / and nobody noticed.\"\nC: \"The gate\nwas open\nall night\n\nand nobody\nnoticed.\"\n\nVersion A is information. In B, \"all night\" gets its own space and starts to feel long. In C, \"noticed\" is alone at the end and it lands like an accusation — and that gap before the last two lines is a held breath. Same nine words. Three completely different poems.\n\n**Tip:** The word at the END of a line gets noticed. Put the important one there.\n\n#### We Do — Break It Together (6 min)\n\nOne prose sentence on the board. We break it three ways and read each aloud.\n\n- \"My brother left his shoes by the door again and they are still there.\"\n- Break 1 — where does your voice pause?\n- Break 2 — put the most important word at the end of a line.\n- Break 3 — add a gap. What does the gap say?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which version would you want to hear read out loud?\n\n#### You Do — Three Shapes (7 min)\n\nYour chosen image, broken three ways. Read each aloud quietly before you decide.\n\n- Write your image as one prose sentence.\n- Break it three different ways.\n- Read all three aloud in a whisper.\n- Circle the version that sounds like you meant it.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your best version. Class: which word landed hardest?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Visual elements**: NZC Phase 2: \"determine the structure of texts and how ideas are conveyed through text features and visual elements\" — in poetry, line breaks and white space ARE the structure.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Structure for impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact\" — a line break can do the work of punctuation.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Stanzas and Silence":{"content":"### Build Together: Stanzas and Silence\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that the white space between stanzas does a job.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — The Shape on the Page  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Conditional sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Reading & writing (Phase 2): text features and visual elements convey meaning; layout conventions used deliberately. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nConditionals this morning — \"if… then\". A whole poem can turn on one: \"If I had asked him, / he would have told me.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Your poem will be read ALOUD at the celebration. Does that change where you'd put a gap?*\n\n#### I Do — What the Gap Does (6 min)\n\nOne poem, then the same poem with the stanza break moved. Listen to where the meaning shifts.\n\n> \"The cicada shell / is still holding on / to the fence post, / legs locked, / empty as a glove.\n\nWhatever left it / did not look back.\"\n\nThat gap is where you realise the poem isn't about a shell at all. If I delete the gap, the last two lines are just more description. With the gap, they're a turn — the poem stops looking and starts thinking. A stanza break is a small silence, and silence is where a reader does their own thinking.\n\n**Tip:** Use a stanza break when the poem changes direction, changes time, or wants you to think.\n\n#### We Do — Move the Gap (7 min)\n\nOne poem on the board with no stanza breaks. We try the gap in three different places.\n\n- Put the gap early — what does that do?\n- Put it just before the last line — now what?\n- Try two gaps. Is it better or is it broken?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Where did the poem want the silence?\n\n#### You Do — Find Your Gap (8 min)\n\nTake your shaped image and try a stanza break in two different places.\n\n- Write your poem-so-far with one stanza break.\n- Now move the break somewhere else and write it again.\n- Read both aloud. Which silence says more?\n- ✦ Bonus: try a one-line stanza. Notice how much attention it gets.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose poem changed meaning when the gap moved? Read us both.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **White space**: Phase 2 readers determine how visual elements convey ideas; poets use the gap between stanzas as a pause, a turn, or a change of time.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Layout conventions**: NZC Phase 2: \"use layout conventions\" — in poetry the convention is chosen by the writer rather than given by the genre.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Choosing a Form":{"content":"### Stretch: Choosing a Form\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that different poem shapes suit different subjects.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — The Shape on the Page  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Counter-argument and rebuttal structure |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): identify the audience and purpose, selecting the appropriate genre and form. Reading: identify how structure conveys ideas. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCounter-argument structure this morning — and a poetry version: some of the best poems argue with themselves. \"I said I didn't mind. / I minded.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Does the shape come before the words, or after? (After — you find the shape the poem wants.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Shapes a Poem Can Take (6 min)\n\nFour forms we'll use this term. I'll show one line of each so you can hear the difference.\n\n> FREE VERSE — lines break where the voice does; no rules.\nLIST POEM — \"Things my grandfather keeps: / string, / a broken watch, / every card we ever made him.\"\nSMALL-MOMENT POEM — one instant, stretched.\nSHAPE / SPACED POEM — the layout on the page is part of the meaning.\n\nA list poem suits collecting — objects, habits, things somebody keeps. A small-moment poem suits one instant you can't forget. The subject usually tells you which. When you don't know, try it two ways; the wrong form feels like wearing someone else's shoes.\n\n**Tip:** Don't choose the cleverest form. Choose the one that gets out of your poem's way.\n\n#### We Do — Match Subject to Form (7 min)\n\nSubjects on the board. Which form would suit each, and why?\n\n- Everything in the bottom of my school bag.\n- The second before the ball hit the window.\n- My walk home, which takes eleven minutes.\n- The sound of the house at 6am.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Could any of these work in two forms? Which two, and what would change?\n\n#### You Do — Try Two Forms (8 min)\n\nYour circled image, drafted in two different forms.\n\n- Draft it as free verse.\n- Draft the same image as a list poem or a small-moment poem.\n- Read both aloud.\n- Write one line: which form suits it, and why.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who found the form changed what the poem was about? That happens more than you'd think.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Selecting a form**: NZC Phase 2: \"selecting the appropriate genre, language register, and word choice\" — in poetry, choosing the form is part of that selection.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Imagining options**: \"Creative thinking: imagining a range of options\" — trying a subject in two forms before committing.\n"},"Thursday: Write: The Audience Card, and a First Poem":{"content":"### Write: The Audience Card, and a First Poem\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning who our poems are for, and drafting freely towards them.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — The Shape on the Page  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing: strengthening a weak argument |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): plan and write with the audience in mind. Oral language: writing intended to be read aloud engages listeners. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nStrengthening a weak argument this morning. A poem is the opposite move: strengthen by removing, not by adding.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who hears this in Week 10? (Our celebration audience — and they only get to hear it once.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Audience Card, Poetry Version (4 min)\n\nThree lines at the top of your poetry page, same as every term — but with one difference.\n\n> I am writing for: our celebration audience — families, and Room 12.\nThey will HEAR this once, not read it twice.\nI want them to: see the shell on the fence post and go quiet for a second.\n\n\"Hear it once\" changes everything. A reader can go back over a hard line; a listener can't. It doesn't mean simplifying — it means making sure the images are clear enough to land the first time.\n\n**Tip:** Write for the ear. If a line only works on the page, it will disappear at the celebration.\n\n#### We Do — What Does a Listener Need? (3 min)\n\nQuick chart: what does an audience hearing a poem once need from us?\n\n- Images they can picture immediately.\n- A title that tells them where they are.\n- Short enough to hold in one listening.\n- An ending they can hear coming to a stop.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of those is hardest?\n\n#### You Do — Draft Freely (14 min)\n\nAudience card first, then draft. Aim for two full poems in rough — quantity today, quality later.\n\n- Write your audience card at the top of your poetry page.\n- Draft your chosen image into a full poem.\n- Then start a second, from a different image in your bank.\n- Don't fix anything. Just get them down.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Two rough poems is a good day. They're supposed to be rough — that's what next week is for.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Writing for an audience**: NZC Phase 2: \"plan and write with the audience in mind\" — this term the audience will hear the work as well as read it.\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Writing for the ear**: NZC Phase 2: varying structure and length to create effect and support meaning — a poem written to be heard is composed with the listener's breath in mind.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: The Line-Break Test":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: The Line-Break Test\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to test our line breaks by reading aloud to a listener.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 3 — The Shape on the Page  ·  Stage 1 → Stage 2 — FIND · Line, stanza and form |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners; use precise vocabulary. Writing processes: revise using peer feedback. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups came from persuasive writing — emotive language, conditionals, counter-argument. Poems can use every one of them; they just have to earn them.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What have we found this week? (The shape, the form, and who's listening.)*\n\n#### I Do — Mark Where I Breathe (3 min)\n\nI read my poem aloud; a student marks my page every time I pause. Then we compare with where I put the line breaks.\n\n> [Read the poem; a student marks the pauses; compare.]\n\nTwo of my breaks matched my breathing. One didn't — I put a break there because it looked tidy, and my mouth ignored it. That's the test: the page can lie to you, but your voice can't.\n\n**Tip:** If you don't pause there when you read it, it isn't a line break.\n\n#### We Do — Practise the Marking (4 min)\n\nPairs, one round on my poem, so the protocol is clear before you use it on your own work.\n\n- Reader reads slowly, twice.\n- Listener marks a slash where they hear a pause.\n- Compare with the actual line breaks.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does the second reading give better marks than the first?\n\n#### You Do — The Line-Break Test (13 min)\n\nIn pairs. Read your poem twice while your partner marks your page.\n\n- Read your poem twice, slowly.\n- Partner marks every pause they hear.\n- Compare with your line breaks; change any that your voice ignored.\n- Swap, then do it again with your second poem.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The break I changed was… because my voice…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Listening for structure**: NZC Phase 2: making comments that encourage discussion — here, reporting where a listener heard the poem pause.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising from feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revise \"using audience and peer feedback\" — line breaks are revised by ear, not by rule.\n"}}},"Week 4: Poem One: A Small Thing":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I start and end a poem?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Titles and First Lines · Tue: Build Together: Endings That Land · Wed: Stretch: Poem One, Finished · Thu: Write: Starting Poem Two · Fri: Share & Reflect: Poem One Aloud |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Write a poem about one small ordinary thing — and never say why it matters.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is the thing, exactly? Name it precisely.\n- What is it doing, or what was done to it?\n- What does it make me think of that I can't quite explain?\n- Where could I stop the poem one line earlier than I want to?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nhinge · handle · seam · edge · corner · crack · thread · lid · strap · chip\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Still there:\n- Somebody left…\n- It has been ____ since…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Write about the thing in your bag you never take out. Ten lines. No explaining.\n- **Picture this:** A single shoe under a chair.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner about the object without saying what it means to you. They'll tell you what it means — that's how you know it worked.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Technical vocabulary\n- **Tuesday** — Present tense conventions\n- **Wednesday** — Structuring a full paragraph\n- **Thursday** — Editing for clarity and accuracy\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Titles and First Lines":{"content":"### Launch: Titles and First Lines\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that a title and a first line do different jobs in a poem.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Technical vocabulary |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): deliberate word choice; the opening establishes the subject and the register. Reading: how text features convey ideas. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTechnical vocabulary this morning — and poems want it too. Not \"the bit of the bike\": the chain guard, the spoke, the valve. The exact word IS the poetry.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Drafting again — which stage is drafting in? (Still FIND: first ideas, aimed at a listener.)*\n\n#### I Do — Three Titles, Three Poems (6 min)\n\nOne poem, three possible titles. Watch how each one changes the whole thing.\n\n> The poem: the cicada shell on the fence post.\nTitle A: \"Cicada Shell\" — names it.\nTitle B: \"Small Thing\" — frames it.\nTitle C: \"What He Left\" — changes everything; now the poem might not be about an insect.\n\nTitle A is honest and slightly boring. Title B tells you how to look. Title C does the most work of all — it makes you read the whole poem twice. A title isn't a label. It's the first line you're allowed to break the rules in.\n\n**Tip:** Try a title that isn't in the poem. See what it does.\n\n#### We Do — Title These (7 min)\n\nTwo class poems (anonymous). We write three titles each: a naming one, a framing one, and one that isn't in the poem at all.\n\n- A naming title — what is it?\n- A framing title — how should we look at it?\n- A title using words that don't appear in the poem.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which title made you want to reread the poem?\n\n#### You Do — Title and Open (8 min)\n\nThree titles for your poem, and two possible first lines.\n\n- Write three titles (naming / framing / not-in-the-poem).\n- Write two possible first lines — one that starts in the middle of things, one that starts still and quiet.\n- Read both openings to a partner. Which made them lean in?\n- Choose, and write the poem out with your choices.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read just your title and first line. Two seconds each. Which pair pulled us in?\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Deliberate choices**: NZC Phase 2: words chosen deliberately for effect — nowhere more visible than a title, which is often the shortest text a writer produces.\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Titles as features**: Phase 2 readers determine how text features convey ideas; a poem's title can name, frame, or deliberately withhold.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Endings That Land":{"content":"### Build Together: Endings That Land\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to end a poem without explaining it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Present tense conventions |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): deliberate word choice and effect; texts have a concluding element suited to purpose — in poetry, one that turns or settles rather than summarises. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPresent tense conventions this morning — poems often stay in the present, which keeps the moment happening while it's being read.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who's hearing this once? What does that mean for your last line? (It has to land audibly.)*\n\n#### I Do — Cut the Last Line (6 min)\n\nMy poem with its explaining ending, then with the explaining line removed.\n\n> WITH: \"…empty as a glove. / Whatever left it did not look back. / It made me think about growing up and moving on.\"\nWITHOUT: \"…empty as a glove. / Whatever left it did not look back.\"\n\nThat last line was me not trusting you. I'd made the image; you were already thinking about leaving and growing; and then I explained it, which insults you and flattens the poem. Nine times out of ten, the real ending is one line earlier than the one you wrote.\n\n**Tip:** Delete your last line. Read it again. Usually it's better.\n\n#### We Do — Where Should It Stop? (7 min)\n\nThree poems with explaining endings. Together we find the real last line.\n\n- Read to the end. Now read stopping one line earlier.\n- Which version leaves something for us to do?\n- Is there a poem here where the explaining line IS the poem?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How can you tell the difference between an ending that explains and one that turns?\n\n#### You Do — Test Your Ending (8 min)\n\nBoth your drafted poems.\n\n- Delete the last line of each. Read them aloud.\n- Keep the deletion if it's better. Put it back if it genuinely isn't.\n- For one poem, write a completely different ending — a turn, an image, or a line of speech.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hands up if deleting your last line improved the poem.\" (Most hands.) \"Remember that feeling in Week 7.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft* — **Effect and restraint**: NZC Phase 2: figurative and precise language used purposefully — a poem's ending trusts the reader to draw the meaning.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Restraint**: Choosing not to explain is a discipline; students find it genuinely hard and it is worth naming as a skill.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Poem One, Finished":{"content":"### Stretch: Poem One, Finished\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to take a poem from rough draft to something we would read aloud.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Structuring a full paragraph |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): drafting and revising at line and whole-text level; goals set from reflection on one's own work. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nStructuring a full paragraph this morning. A stanza is a poem's paragraph — one idea, one shape, then a silence.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages from memory, no strip, no notes.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall — quick-fire (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Cover the wall strip before they arrive. No warning.\n\n1. \"No notes, no wall. Four stages, in order, with actions. Go.\" Twenty seconds.\n2. \"One person — the whole process in under twenty seconds.\" Choose someone who hasn't done it this year; the class counts down.\n3. Uncover. \"Fourth term. Nobody is even having to think about this any more.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Fast and warm. If a student stalls, give the first word only and let them finish.\n\n#### I Do — The Three Questions (5 min)\n\nThree questions I ask before I call a poem finished. Watch me use them on mine.\n\n> 1. Can a stranger see it? 2. Does every word earn its place? 3. Does it stop at the right moment?\n\nQuestion one: I check every image against \"could someone who wasn't there picture this?\" Question two: I read it with a pencil and cross out anything I could live without. Question three: I try ending it one line earlier, again. Three questions, three minutes, and the poem gets better every time.\n\n**Tip:** These same three questions work on all three poems. Learn them, not the answers.\n\n#### We Do — Run the Questions on One Poem (5 min)\n\nA volunteer's poem on the visualiser. We run all three questions on it, out loud.\n\n- Can we see it? Where did the picture go fuzzy?\n- Which word is doing the least work?\n- Could it stop earlier?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** How many words did we cut — and did we lose any meaning?\n\n#### You Do — Finish Poem One (10 min)\n\nRun all three questions on your strongest poem, then write it out clean.\n\n- Question 1: mark any image a stranger couldn't see, and fix it.\n- Question 2: cross out every word you can live without.\n- Question 3: try ending one line earlier.\n- Write the poem out cleanly with its title. This is poem one of three.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"One poem down. Two to go — and they get easier.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising at line level**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — in poetry, the line replaces the sentence as the unit.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Precision**: Words chosen deliberately for effect, with weak verbs and unnecessary adjectives removed.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Starting Poem Two":{"content":"### Write: Starting Poem Two\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to begin a new poem from a different image, using what we learned from the first.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing for clarity and accuracy |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): applying learning from one piece to the next; setting goals from reflection on one's own writing. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing for clarity this morning — and the poetry version of clarity is: can a stranger see it? Same question, different genre.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. If poem two comes out like poem one, what should you change? (The form, the subject, or the mood — range is assessed.)*\n\n#### I Do — Choose Something Different (3 min)\n\nMy second poem starts by looking at what my first one was, and going the other way.\n\n> Poem one: an object, still, quiet, five lines. So poem two: a person, moving, longer, and it's going to have some sound in it.\n\nIf all three of my poems are quiet five-liners about objects, the set is boring even if each poem is good. Deliberately choose difference — different subject, different form, different mood.\n\n**Tip:** Look at what you've made, then make its opposite.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Difference (3 min)\n\nRound the room: what will be different about poem two?\n\n- \"Mine's about a person instead of a thing.\"\n- \"Mine's going to be a list poem.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone whose poem two sounds exactly like poem one? Choose again.\n\n#### You Do — Draft Poem Two (15 min)\n\nNew image from your bank, deliberately different from poem one. Long write.\n\n- Choose an image that's different in subject or mood.\n- Draft the poem — rough is fine.\n- Try one form you haven't used yet.\n- Read it aloud once and mark your line breaks.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Two poems drafted. Next week we bring sound into it.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Planning)* — **Goals from reflection**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\"\n- *Writing — Writing craft* — **Range**: Writing a second piece in a different form or mood develops range, which the term's rubric assesses directly.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Poem One Aloud":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Poem One Aloud\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read a finished poem aloud so that a listener can follow it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 4 — Poem One: A Small Thing  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Drafting |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~22 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively; use precise vocabulary; vary structure and length for effect. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were explanation tools — exact words, present tense, clear structure. All three make a poem easier to hear.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Where do your poems end up in Week 10? (Read aloud, at the celebration — so we practise now.)*\n\n#### I Do — Slow, and Then Stop (3 min)\n\nHow to read a poem aloud. Three rules, and the third one is the one everybody forgets.\n\n> Slower than feels natural · a real pause at the line breaks · after the last word, stand still and say nothing for three seconds.\n\nWatch me rush it and mumble to a stop. Now properly. The three seconds of silence at the end is where the poem lands — if you say \"um, that's it\" you take it away from your listener.\n\n**Tip:** End on the last word. Then silence. Don't apologise for your own poem.\n\n#### We Do — Practise the Ending Silence (4 min)\n\nWhole class reads one shared poem aloud together, then holds three seconds of silence. Twice.\n\n- Read slower than feels right.\n- Pause at each line break.\n- Three seconds at the end — count them.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Was the silence uncomfortable? It gets easier — and it's the most important part.\n\n#### You Do — Poem One Aloud, in Fours (12 min)\n\nGroups of four. Read poem one twice, with a silence. Listeners use the star sentences only.\n\n- Read your finished poem twice, slowly.\n- Hold the three-second silence.\n- Listeners: \"the image I can still see is…\" / \"the line I'd remember tomorrow is…\"\n- Write down the words people said stayed with them.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose poem made the room go quiet? That's the whole art form, right there.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Reading aloud**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners with specific details — for poetry, the delivery is part of how the text conveys meaning.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Taking a risk**: \"Take a risk and try something new\" — reading your own poem aloud, six weeks before it happens in front of an audience.\n"}}},"Week 5: Sound":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 5 — Sound\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I make a poem sound like something, not just mean something?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Long Line, Short Line · Tue: Build Together: Repetition That Builds · Wed: Stretch: Sound Without Showing Off · Thu: Write: Finish Poem Two · Fri: Share & Reflect: Four Genres, One Process |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Write a poem about something ordinary that you are quietly glad about — and make it sound good out loud.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What is something small you would miss if it disappeared?\n- What does it sound like, or what sound goes with it?\n- Which words in my poem are worth repeating?\n- Where should the line get short so the listener sits up?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nhum · clatter · drip · creak · thud · rustle · click · whirr · scrape · hush\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- Every morning…\n- Still, and still, and still…\n- I would miss…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Write about a sound in your house that means somebody is home.\n- **Picture this:** A kettle just before it boils.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read any three lines of your poem aloud twice. Where did your mouth enjoy itself? Build from there.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Synonyms for said — precise dialogue tags\n- **Tuesday** — Concessive clauses\n- **Wednesday** — Building tension with sentence length variation\n- **Thursday** — Editing narrative pacing\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Long Line, Short Line":{"content":"### Launch: Long Line, Short Line\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to use line length to control how a poem sounds.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Sound  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Synonyms for said — precise dialogue tags |\n| **NZC link** | Sentence structures (Phase 2): use a range of structures and vary length to enhance impact. Oral language: varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSynonyms for \"said\" this morning — and a poetry warning: \"exclaimed\" and \"proclaimed\" are usually worse than \"said\". In a poem, plain words in a good order beat fancy ones.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Who hears this? And what does a listener notice that a reader doesn't? (The sound of it.)*\n\n#### I Do — Where the Short Line Goes (6 min)\n\nOne poem, read twice. Listen for the short line and what it does to you.\n\n> \"Every morning the kettle starts its long climb towards boiling / and the house makes the sounds it always makes, / the pipes, the gate, my father's radio through two walls. /\n\nThen nothing. /\n\nHe has already gone.\"\n\nThree long lines, all comfortable, then two words: \"Then nothing.\" Your ear was expecting another long one and it fell into a hole. That's what a short line does — and it only works because the lines before it were long. Rhythm is a promise, and a short line is you breaking the promise on purpose.\n\n**Tip:** One short line per poem, in the place you most want a listener to sit up.\n\n#### We Do — Move the Short Line (7 min)\n\nA poem with all-medium lines. Together we make one line very short, in three different places.\n\n- Short line at the start — what happens?\n- Short line in the middle — what happens?\n- Short line at the end — is it powerful or is it a full stop?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which position worked, and why do you think that is?\n\n#### You Do — Find Your Short Line (8 min)\n\nTake either of your poems and build a rhythm on purpose.\n\n- Read your poem aloud and mark where the lines are all similar lengths.\n- Make one line very short — three words or fewer.\n- Read it aloud again. Did your voice change?\n- ✦ Bonus: make one line noticeably long, just before the short one.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read the two lines either side of your short one. That's the moment.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Length for impact**: NZC Phase 2: \"use a range of sentence structures and types, to suit the purpose of the writing and enhance its impact\" — in poetry the line replaces the sentence as the unit of rhythm.\n- *Oral Language — Sentence structure* — **Varying length for effect**: NZC Phase 2: \"varying sentence structure and length to create effect and support meaning.\"\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Repetition That Builds":{"content":"### Build Together: Repetition That Builds\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to use repetition on purpose so a poem gathers force.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Sound  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Concessive clauses |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): language features used purposefully for effect. Oral language: structures that support meaning when heard. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nConcessive clauses this morning — \"although\", \"even though\". A poem can turn on one: \"Even though nobody asked, / he set a place for me.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Poem two is due this week. What makes it different from poem one?*\n\n#### I Do — Say It Three Times (6 min)\n\nThe same starting words, three times, building. Listen to what happens on the third one.\n\n> \"I would miss the gate that never shuts properly. / I would miss the radio through two walls. / I would miss the sound of somebody else awake.\"\n\nOne is a statement. Two is a pattern. Three is where a listener feels something, because they've learned the shape and now they're waiting for it. Then — and this is the trick — you either land the third one hard, or you break the pattern on purpose. Never do four. Four is a list.\n\n**Tip:** Repetition works in threes. On the third one, say the thing you actually meant.\n\n#### We Do — Build a Three (8 min)\n\nChoose a repeating phrase as a class, then build three lines from it — the third one has to land.\n\n- \"I would miss…\" · \"Nobody notices…\" · \"Still there:…\" · \"Every morning…\"\n- Line 1 — something ordinary.\n- Line 2 — something more particular.\n- Line 3 — the one that means something.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does the order matter so much? What happens if we put the strongest one first?\n\n#### You Do — Repetition in Poem Two (7 min)\n\nDraft or redraft poem two using a repeated phrase as its spine.\n\n- Choose your repeating phrase.\n- Write three lines that begin with it, ordered ordinary → particular → meaningful.\n- Read them aloud. Does the third one land?\n- ✦ Bonus: break your own pattern in the last line and see what it does.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your three. We're listening for the third one.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Repetition for effect**: NZC Phase 2: language devices used purposefully to create effects — repetition is the device a listening audience notices most.\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: Repetition gives a listener a handrail through a text they can only hear once.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Sound Without Showing Off":{"content":"### Stretch: Sound Without Showing Off\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to use sound patterns sparingly so they help the poem instead of taking it over.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Sound  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Building tension with sentence length variation |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): language features used purposefully; word choice for effect including the sound of words. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nBuilding tension with sentence length this morning — exactly Monday's lesson, from the other direction. Same tool, different genre.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. Are we polishing yet? (No — SHAPE is polishing. This is still finding.)*\n\n#### I Do — One Sound, Not Six (5 min)\n\nTwo versions. One uses sound to help; the other one has taken over the poem.\n\n> TOO MUCH: \"The slippery silver sea slid slowly, silently, softly on the sand.\"\nENOUGH: \"The sea slid back and left the sand ticking.\"\n\nIn the first one I stopped meaning anything about halfway through — I was just choosing words that started with S. The second has two S sounds, and it also has \"ticking\", which is a real thing wet sand does. Sound should sneak up on a listener, not wave at them.\n\n**Tip:** If a listener notices the technique before the meaning, it's too much.\n\n#### We Do — Turn It Down (7 min)\n\nOverloaded lines on the board. We strip each back to one sound effect that earns its place.\n\n- \"The bright blue birds barely brushed the branches.\"\n- \"Crunching, crashing, clattering, the cans came clanging.\"\n- Now: keep ONE sound each and make the rest mean something.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did the line get better or worse when we removed most of the pattern?\n\n#### You Do — One Sound Each (9 min)\n\nBoth poems. Hunt for sound, and keep the sound that means something.\n\n- Read both poems aloud and mark any sound pattern you hear.\n- Keep the one that helps; delete the rest.\n- Find one place where a sound word (thud, hush, click) would carry real meaning, and put it there.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read the line where your sound does a job.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Sound patterning**: NZC Phase 2: devices used purposefully — alliteration and assonance support meaning when used sparingly and distract when overused.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Choosing for sound and sense**: A word must be right in meaning first and pleasing in sound second; the reverse produces nonsense that scans.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Finish Poem Two":{"content":"### Write: Finish Poem Two\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to bring a second poem to a finished draft using the three questions.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Sound  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Editing narrative pacing |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): revise at line and whole-text level; apply learning from a previous piece; increasing stamina. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nEditing narrative pacing this morning — a poem's pacing is its line lengths and its silences. Same skill, fewer words.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. One poem to go after this. What's still missing from your set of three?*\n\n#### I Do — Three Questions Again (3 min)\n\nThe same three questions from Week 4. They never change.\n\n> 1. Can a stranger see it? 2. Does every word earn its place? 3. Does it stop at the right moment?\n\nI'm not teaching you anything new today. I'm showing you that the same three questions work on every poem you will ever write — which is the point of learning them properly the first time.\n\n**Tip:** Three questions, three minutes, every poem.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Gap (3 min)\n\nRound the room: what does your set of three still need?\n\n- \"Something about a person.\"\n- \"Something longer.\"\n- \"Something that isn't sad.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone with three poems the same shape? Poem three is where you fix that.\n\n#### You Do — Finish Poem Two, Begin Poem Three (15 min)\n\nRun the three questions on poem two, write it clean, then start the third from a fresh image.\n\n- Three questions on poem two.\n- Write poem two out cleanly with a title.\n- Choose a third image — deliberately different again.\n- Draft the opening lines of poem three.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Two finished, one started. You're ahead of where you think you are.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Applying the same tools**: NZC Phase 2: revising for clarity and impact — the same three questions applied independently, which is the transfer being assessed.\n- *Writing — Writing craft* — **Gratitude and attention**: Philippians 4:8 as the term's frame: what a writer chooses to notice shapes what a reader will value.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Four Genres, One Process":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Four Genres, One Process\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that the same four stages have carried us through four completely different kinds of writing.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 5 — Sound  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Writing for the ear |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): set and work towards goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes — across a full year of work. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were narrative tools — dialogue tags, tension, pacing. Poems borrow from every genre; that's allowed.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Explain one stage four ways — story, explanation, speech, poem.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Four genres, one process (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** All four criteria charts visible (the poetry one is not built yet — leave its space blank and point at it). Wall strip covered.\n\n1. Assign one stage per pair: \"Explain your stage FOUR ways — narrative, explanation, speech, poem. Sixty seconds each.\"\n2. Four students report back, one per stage. The class listens for what stays the same across the four versions.\n3. Write the constants on the board as they emerge — the same four the class generated in Term 3: you always fill before you draft · you always write to a particular someone · you always read it as the person who doesn't already agree · you always test it on a real human before you publish.\n4. Then the harder question: \"Which of those four is hardest in poetry?\" (Usually the third — because a poem doesn't argue, so \"someone who doesn't agree\" becomes \"someone who wasn't there\".)\n\n**Watch for:** This is the rehearsal for the Week 10 assessment. Generating the constants themselves in Week 5 turns the final assessment into recall rather than something new.\n\n#### I Do — The Same Question, Four Times (3 min)\n\nOne stage, asked four ways, so you can hear what stays the same.\n\n> SHAPE in Term 1: \"Is it vivid?\" · Term 2: \"Is it clear?\" · Term 3: \"Would it convince someone who disagrees?\" · Term 4: \"Does every word earn its place?\"\n\nFour different questions — and underneath, exactly the same move: stop being the writer and become the reader. That's what SHAPE is, in any genre, forever.\n\n**Tip:** The questions change. The move never does.\n\n#### We Do — Report Back (5 min)\n\nAfter the spotlight pairs, four students report one stage each. The class listens for what stays the same.\n\n- FILL — what was collected, in each of the four terms?\n- FIND — who was the reader each time?\n- SHAPE — what was the question each time?\n- SHARE — how was it given away each time?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** So what never changed? Write the four constants on the board.\n\n#### You Do — Poem Two Aloud (11 min)\n\nFours. Read poem two twice, with the silence. Star sentences only.\n\n- Read poem two twice, slowly, with the three-second silence.\n- Listeners: the image that stayed, and the line they'd remember.\n- Note what people heard — it tells you which images are working.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: the last poem — and it's the one people usually remember.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflecting on process**: NZC Phase 2: reflection on one's own content and processes — the strongest evidence of transfer is being able to describe the same process working in four genres.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: \"Metacognition: being aware of your own thinking\" — comparing across four contexts turns a routine into knowledge.\n"}}},"Week 6: Poem Three: Someone, or a Year":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year\n\n> **Essential question:** How do I write about a person without listing their good qualities?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: People Are Their Habits · Tue: Build Together: The Turn · Wed: Stretch: The Set of Three · Thu: Write: Finish the Set · Fri: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Write about a person you know — or about this year — using only things you have actually seen them do.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- What does this person do the same way every time?\n- What do they keep, wear, carry or say?\n- What is one small thing they do that nobody else would notice?\n- What do I still want to ask them?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nkeeps · always · still · hums · folds · saves · waits · forgets · repeats · hands\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- He always…\n- Things she keeps:\n- Nobody else would notice that…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Write a list poem: \"Things ____ keeps.\" Five real objects. That's the whole poem.\n- **Picture this:** An orange peeled in one unbroken spiral.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner one habit of someone in your house. Their reaction will tell you if it's poem-sized.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Cohesive substitution — avoiding repetition\n- **Tuesday** — Relative clauses for embedded definitions\n- **Wednesday** — Topic sentences and paragraph-closing sentences\n- **Thursday** — Adapting a piece for a different audience\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: People Are Their Habits":{"content":"### Launch: People Are Their Habits\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to show a person through what they do, not through adjectives about them.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Cohesive substitution — avoiding repetition |\n| **NZC link** | Writing craft (Phase 2): precise word choice and figurative language to create vivid images; showing rather than asserting. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCohesive substitution this morning — avoiding repetition. Careful: in poetry, deliberate repetition is a tool. Know which one you're doing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. The last drafting week. What does your set of three still need?*\n\n#### I Do — Adjectives vs Habits (6 min)\n\nThe same grandfather, described two ways. One of them you'll forget by lunchtime.\n\n> A: \"My grandfather is kind and generous and always has time for me. He is the best.\"\nB: \"He peels an orange in one piece / and hands me the whole spiral, / still warm.\"\n\nA tells you he's kind. B shows you a man who takes his time, who does something carefully for somebody else, and who hands it over warm — and you worked all of that out yourself, which is why you'll remember it. Never write \"kind\". Write the orange.\n\n**Tip:** Delete every adjective about the person. Replace each with one thing they actually do.\n\n#### We Do — Turn Adjectives Into Habits (7 min)\n\nClass-generated adjectives about people. For each one, we find a habit that shows it instead.\n\n- \"funny\" → what do they actually DO?\n- \"tidy\" → what would you see?\n- \"tired\" → what have you watched them do?\n- \"proud of me\" → what did they do, once?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which habit made the person appear in the room?\n\n#### You Do — Habit Hunt (8 min)\n\nChoose your person. Then collect only habits — no adjectives allowed on the page.\n\n- Write 6 things this person actually does, wears, keeps or says.\n- Cross out any that could be true of most people.\n- Star the one nobody else would have noticed.\n- ✦ Bonus: write down one thing you still want to ask them.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your starred habit. We should be able to see the person.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Showing a person**: NZC Phase 2: words chosen deliberately for effect — a specific habit conveys character where an adjective merely claims it.\n- *Reading — Making connections* — **Personal response**: NZC Phase 2: responding to texts by sharing personal thoughts and feelings; poems about people invite exactly this response.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Turn":{"content":"### Build Together: The Turn\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to build a poem that changes direction near the end.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Relative clauses for embedded definitions |\n| **NZC link** | Reading & writing (Phase 2): how ideas are conveyed and developed through structure; texts shaped so the ending carries the meaning. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nRelative clauses this morning — \"who\", \"which\", \"that\". Poems use them to slip in a fact quietly: \"my grandfather, who never learned to swim, …\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND. What have you found about your person that you didn't know on Monday?*\n\n#### I Do — Where the Poem Turns (6 min)\n\nA poem that looks like it's about hands, until the last two lines. Listen for the moment it changes.\n\n> \"Two maps. / Every line a road / he took before I was born. //\nHe peels an orange in one piece / and hands me the whole spiral, / still warm. //\nI keep meaning / to ask him about the scar.\"\n\nFor two stanzas it's a warm poem about a grandfather. Then the last two lines turn it: now it's about time running out, and about the things we don't ask. The turn is small — eight words — and it makes you reread everything above it. That's what a last stanza is for.\n\n**Tip:** A turn is not a twist. It's a step sideways into what the poem was really about.\n\n#### We Do — Add a Turn (7 min)\n\nA flat class poem that just describes. Together we write three possible turns for it.\n\n- A turn that moves in time (\"Twenty years from now…\" / \"That was in March.\")\n- A turn that asks something (\"I never asked…\")\n- A turn that names one small honest thing.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which turn made the poem worth rereading?\n\n#### You Do — Draft Poem Three (8 min)\n\nHabits first, then a turn at the end. Aim for short.\n\n- Draft the poem from your habits — no adjectives about the person.\n- Add a gap before the last two or three lines.\n- Write your turn: a question, a shift in time, or one honest small thing.\n- Read it aloud. Does the ending make you want to reread the start?\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read only your turn. Two lines. That's plenty.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Structure carrying meaning**: NZC Phase 2: determining how ideas are conveyed through structure — the turn is the structural move that gives a short poem its depth.\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Structures for impact**: A short final stanza, or a single line after a gap, is a structural choice that creates the turn.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Set of Three":{"content":"### Stretch: The Set of Three\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose and order three poems so they work as a set.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Topic sentences and paragraph-closing sentences |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): identify audience and purpose; select and arrange texts for a publication. Writing processes: goals set from reflection. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nTopic and closing sentences this morning — a poem has both too: its first line opens the door, its last line closes it behind you.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing FIND this week. Name the next stage and its question.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Spaced recall + move the peg (3 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg still on Stage 2.\n\n1. \"We finish a stage this week — and it's the last time we'll ever finish FIND together. Which one is next?\"\n2. A student moves the peg and justifies it: \"We're finishing FIND because the three poems are drafted. Next is SHAPE — cutting.\"\n3. Put the four SHAPE questions on the board together for the first time: vivid → clear → convincing → does every word earn its place? \"Four questions. One move: stop being the writer, become the reader.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Leave those four questions up until Week 10. They are the tidiest one-line summary of the year's learning anywhere in the programme.\n\n#### I Do — Three That Belong Together (5 min)\n\nMy three poems, laid out, and me deciding on their order.\n\n> Small thing (short, quiet) → Wonder (longer, out at night) → Grandfather (warm, then the turn).\n\nI'm putting the shortest first, because it teaches a listener how to listen. The person poem goes last, because it's the one that will stay with them. And I've checked they're not all the same mood — if all three were sad, the set would flatten out even if each poem worked. Order matters, just like reasons did last term.\n\n**Tip:** Shortest first. Strongest last. Never three of the same mood.\n\n#### We Do — Test a Set (6 min)\n\nA volunteer's three poems. As a class we check for range and choose an order.\n\n- Are they different in subject?\n- Are they different in mood?\n- Are they different in shape on the page?\n- Which one should go last?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** If two of them are too similar, what should the writer do with the second one?\n\n#### You Do — Choose and Order (9 min)\n\nLay your poems out. Check the range, choose the three for the anthology, and set the order.\n\n- Lay out everything you've drafted.\n- Check: different subjects? different moods? different shapes?\n- Choose your three and number them 1, 2, 3.\n- Write one line: why that order.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Anyone whose three are too alike? You've got tomorrow to fix it — that's what tomorrow is for.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Selecting for publication**: NZC Phase 2: writing with the audience in mind — choosing which pieces go in the anthology and in what order is an authorial decision.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Judging one's own work against criteria the class built, and choosing rather than including everything.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Finish the Set":{"content":"### Write: Finish the Set\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to complete a set of three finished drafts.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Adapting a piece for a different audience |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): completing multiple drafts; revising at line and whole-text level; increasing stamina and independence. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nAdapting a piece for a different audience this morning — worth remembering: these poems will be HEARD by families in Week 10, not read in silence.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Last day of FIND, for the whole year. What exists by the end of today? (Three drafted poems.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Goal, Plainly (2 min)\n\nOne sentence, then the room is yours.\n\n> \"Today everyone finishes three drafted poems, with titles.\"\n\nDrafted, not perfect. Next week we cut them — and you cannot cut a poem that isn't written.\n\n**Tip:** Titles count. A poem without a title is not finished.\n\n#### We Do — What's Missing? (2 min)\n\nFast round the room: what's left?\n\n- \"A title for number two.\"\n- \"The ending of poem three.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone missing a whole poem? Sit near me.\n\n#### You Do — The Long Write (18 min)\n\nEighteen protected minutes. I'll circulate quietly and read over shoulders without interrupting.\n\n- Finish all three poems in draft.\n- Give every poem a title.\n- Read each one aloud once and fix any line your mouth stumbles on.\n- Date the page.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nCelebrate it — three drafted poems is a real body of work for a Year 5 or 6 writer.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Completing a body of work**: NZC Phase 2 writers take multiple pieces to completed draft, applying the same revision tools independently.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Working to completion**: Seeing yourself in the big picture; finishing a set rather than an item.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Build the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to decide what makes a poem strong, and to judge our own against it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 6 — Poem Three: Someone, or a Year  ·  Stage 2 — FIND · Completing the set |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎯 Stage 2 — FIND: “Who do I want to say it to?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Students co-construct the criteria. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were cohesion tools. Next week we use the opposite skill — taking things out.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 2 — FIND**: *“Who do I want to say it to?”*  \nHand action: Hands up to your eyes like binoculars, looking for your reader.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 2, FIND — Who do I want to say it to?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *FIND is finished for the year. Name the two stages left, and what they ask.*\n\n#### I Do — Name What's Next (3 min)\n\nShort, because today belongs to your poems.\n\n> \"Next week we cut. Not add — cut.\"\n\nIn Term 1 SHAPE meant \"is it vivid?\", in Term 2 \"is it clear?\", in Term 3 \"would it convince?\" This term it's the shortest question of the lot: does every word earn its place? And it is the hardest, because the words are yours and you like them.\n\n**Tip:** Come on Monday ready to lose words you're fond of.\n\n#### We Do — Build \"What Makes a Strong Poem\" (5 min)\n\nSix weeks of learning — call it out and I'll scribe. This becomes our rubric.\n\n- What does a strong poem's IMAGE do?\n- What do we know about WORD CHOICE now?\n- Where do LINE BREAKS go?\n- What does a good ENDING do — and not do?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anything on the list we'd be unhappy to be judged on? Then it stays.\n\n#### You Do — Best-Line Share (12 min)\n\nEverybody reads one line — their best. Everybody. It takes four minutes and it's worth it.\n\n- Choose the single best line you've written this term.\n- Stand, read it once, sit down.\n- Listen to all 25. Notice how many are about small things.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"The poem of mine I'd most like someone to hear is… because…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Criteria and goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals from reflection; criteria the students build themselves are the ones they can actually apply.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Critical evaluation**: Discussing the elements of quality writing; interpreting and evaluating rather than receiving a checklist.\n"}}},"Week 7: Cutting":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 7 — Cutting\n\n> **Essential question:** What can I take out?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Strong Poem, Weak Poem · Tue: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria · Wed: Stretch: The Cutting Pass · Thu: Write: One Goal Per Poem · Fri: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt this week — your three poems are the work. Take out everything that isn't earning its place.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which words are only holding the sentence together?\n- Which line could go entirely without losing the poem?\n- Which comparison have I heard before?\n- Where am I explaining something the reader already worked out?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nearn · cut · trim · precise · bare · spare · exact · unnecessary · borrowed · honest\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- I cut ____ because…\n- The poem still works without…\n- This word is doing nothing:\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Copy your poem out and delete every third word without thinking. Read what's left. You will keep about half your deletions.\n- **Picture this:** A poem with a third of it crossed out in pencil.\n- **Talk it out first:** Read your poem to a partner and ask: which line could you live without?\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Hyperbole for effect\n- **Tuesday** — Sentence fragments for deliberate effect\n- **Wednesday** — Opening hooks for different text types\n- **Thursday** — Structural edit — whole-text level\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Strong Poem, Weak Poem":{"content":"### Launch: Strong Poem, Weak Poem\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge a poem against criteria and name exactly why a weak one fails.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Cutting  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Hyperbole for effect |\n| **NZC link** | Reading (Phase 2): determine how ideas are conveyed through text features; respond to texts with personal thoughts. Deconstruction of models (Loane Ch 11). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nHyperbole this morning — deliberate exaggeration. In a poem it works once, as a joke or a shock. Twice and nobody believes you.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Vivid, clear, convincing — and this term? (\"Does every word earn its place?\")*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — SHAPE for poetry (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg onto Stage 3. Both exemplar poems ready. The four SHAPE questions still on the board from Week 6.\n\n1. Point at the four questions. \"Same stage, four jobs. This term's is the shortest and the hardest: does every word earn its place?\"\n2. Explain why it's hardest.\n   > \"In the other three terms, improving usually meant ADDING — more evidence, more detail, another step. In poetry, improving nearly always means taking away. And the words you have to take away are yours, and you like them.\"\n3. Demonstrate on one line: read it, cut two words, read it again. Ask the class which version they'd rather hear.\n4. Set the week's test question: \"Read every word and ask — if I deleted this, would anyone notice? If the answer is no, it goes.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Some students experience cutting as loss rather than improvement. Make the before/after audible every time — the ear convinces where the argument doesn't.\n\n#### I Do — Annotate the Strong One (7 min)\n\nOur WAGOLL poems against the criteria you built on Friday. I'll label and say what each move does to a listener.\n\n> [Annotate live: the concrete opening · the precise verb · the writer's own comparison · the line break landing on the important word · the gap before the turn · the ending that doesn't explain.]\n\nLook at \"legs locked\". Two words, two hard sounds, and a picture. Compare it with what most of us would have written: \"its legs were still stuck on tightly.\" Six words, no picture, no sound. The strong poem isn't using clever words — it's using ordinary words that have all earned their place.\n\n**Tip:** Nothing in a good poem is there by accident. Ask of every line: what is this doing?\n\n#### We Do — Diagnose the Weak One (7 min)\n\nThe weak exemplar. Be specific — and be fair, because this poem is doing what a lot of school poetry teaches.\n\n- Which words tell us how to feel instead of showing us?\n- Find the borrowed comparisons.\n- Which words were chosen only because they rhymed?\n- What is it doing in its last two lines?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Here's the real question: what would we have to CUT to find a real poem hiding inside it?\n\n#### You Do — Find the Poem Inside It (7 min)\n\nTake the weak exemplar and cut it down to whatever is true.\n\n- Cross out every line that shows you nothing.\n- Cross out every borrowed comparison.\n- Write out whatever survives — it might be three lines.\n- Is what's left better than the original? Say why.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read your survivor version. Three lines can be a whole poem.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Deconstruction**: NZC Phase 2: determining how texts are structured and how ideas are conveyed — applied to a strong and a deliberately weak poem against shared criteria.\n- *Writing — Language features and devices* — **Judging figurative language**: NZC Phase 2: figurative language used purposefully — students learn to tell a working image from a decorative one.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria":{"content":"### Build Together: Marking Up Against the Criteria\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to judge our own poems honestly against the rubric.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Cutting  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Sentence fragments for deliberate effect |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): reread at line and whole-text level; set goals from reflection on one's own writing. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSentence fragments this morning — and in poetry they're not errors, they're choices. \"Two maps.\" is a complete poetic line and an incomplete sentence, and it's correct.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Who are you reading as? (Someone who has never seen the thing you're describing.)*\n\n#### I Do — Find the Weak Line in My Own Poem (5 min)\n\nMy poem, our rubric, out loud — including the line that isn't working.\n\n> \"Image — tick. Word choice… line four has 'really' in it, and 'really' has never improved a poem in the history of the world. And line seven is my own explaining. Both go.\"\n\nIt's easier to be honest about a poem than about a story, because a poem is short enough that you can see the whole thing at once. Use that. Find your weakest line before somebody else does — and then be glad, because now you can fix it.\n\n**Tip:** Every poem has a weakest line. Knowing which one it is doubles your control over it.\n\n#### We Do — Glow and Grow the Weak Exemplar (8 min)\n\nGlow and grow for the weak poem, as a class.\n\n- Find one genuine glow — there is always one.\n- Find the single most damaging habit.\n- Write the grow as an instruction: \"Next time, try…\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why only ONE grow, when there are six?\n\n#### You Do — Self-Analysis (8 min)\n\nRubric beside your three poems. Tick only what you can prove with a line.\n\n- Work down the rubric for each poem.\n- Mark the line that proves each tick.\n- For each poem, underline your weakest line.\n- Leave blank anything you can't prove.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Everyone name one glow out loud. Grows tomorrow.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Rereading at every level**: NZC Phase 2: \"reread to check at the sentence, paragraph, and whole-text level\" — for poetry, word, line, stanza and whole poem.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Strengths and limitations**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations\" — modelled by the teacher on their own poem first, so honesty is safe.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: The Cutting Pass":{"content":"### Stretch: The Cutting Pass\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning that cutting is the main way a poem gets better.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Cutting  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Opening hooks for different text types |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): revise by replacing words and removing what does not serve the purpose; word choice for precision. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nOpening hooks this morning — and a poetry note: a poem's hook is usually its first concrete image, not a question or an exclamation.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. Are we adding this week? (No. We're taking away.)*\n\n#### I Do — Delete a Third (5 min)\n\nMy poem, and a rule that sounds brutal: I'm going to delete about a third of the words, on purpose.\n\n> BEFORE (34 words): \"The old cicada shell is still there and it is holding on very tightly to the wooden fence post, its little legs are locked on and it is completely empty inside just like an empty glove.\"\nAFTER (14 words): \"The cicada shell / is still holding on / to the fence post, / legs locked, / empty as a glove.\"\n\nTwenty words gone. Which ones? \"Old\", \"very tightly\", \"little\", \"completely\", \"inside\", \"just\", and every word whose only job was grammar. And notice the poem didn't lose anything — it lost the padding that was hiding it.\n\n**Tip:** The words that go first: really, very, just, so, little, old, beautiful, amazing.\n\n#### We Do — Cut One Together (7 min)\n\nOne volunteer poem on the visualiser. The class calls out deletions; the writer has a veto.\n\n- Which words are only doing grammar?\n- Which adjective is the noun already saying?\n- Which whole line could go?\n- Now read it without them.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Writer — which deletion did you want to veto, and were you right?\n\n#### You Do — Cut All Three (9 min)\n\nOne pass per poem. Pencil, not rubber — cross out so you can see what you cut.\n\n- Poem 1: cross out every word doing no work. Read what's left.\n- Poem 2: same. Then cut one whole line.\n- Poem 3: same.\n- For each, count the words before and after and write both numbers.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Who cut the most? Read us the shortened version — and tell us if you miss anything.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising by cutting**: NZC Phase 2: revisions include \"replacing words\" and removing what does not serve clarity or effect — in poetry, deletion is the primary revision move.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Every word earning its place**: Deliberate vocabulary choice taken to its limit: if a word can go without loss, it should.\n"},"Thursday: Write: One Goal Per Poem":{"content":"### Write: One Goal Per Poem\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to choose one change per poem that will improve it most.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Cutting  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Structural edit — whole-text level |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nStructural edit at whole-text level this morning — for a poem that means: is it in the right order, and does it start in the right place?\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHAPE. What do you take into SHARE next week? (Three cut poems, and one goal each.)*\n\n#### I Do — One Goal, Not Five (4 min)\n\nThree poems, three goals. One each — no more.\n\n> Poem 1: \"I will replace 'empty as a glove' if I can find something truer — and keep it if I can't.\"\nPoem 2: \"I will cut the last line so it stops one line earlier.\"\nPoem 3: \"I will find one habit to replace the word 'kind' in line two.\"\n\nEach one names a place and a change. And notice the first one allows me to keep what I have if the alternative isn't better — that's not laziness, that's a real writer's decision.\n\n**Tip:** One goal per poem. Three small changes will do more than twelve big intentions.\n\n#### We Do — What Are We All Finding? (3 min)\n\nCall out the grows you're seeing. I'll tally — it decides next week's workshops.\n\n- \"Explaining at the end.\"\n- \"Borrowed comparisons.\"\n- \"Too many adjectives.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which got the most hands?\n\n#### You Do — Goals, Then Act on Them (14 min)\n\nWrite one goal at the top of each poem, then start on them.\n\n- Write one \"I will…\" goal per poem.\n- Do the first one now.\n- Read that poem aloud afterwards to check.\n- Show a partner both versions; ask which one they'd rather hear.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Your partner is holding you to the other two goals. Same deal as every term.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Specific goals**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection, specific enough to be completed and checked.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: Choosing the change with the biggest effect requires judging one's own work against a standard — the hardest metacognitive move at this age.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Preparing for the Helping Circle\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning how to listen to a poem in a circle, and how to receive what people say about it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 7 — Cutting  ·  Stage 3 — SHAPE · Does every word earn its place? |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🔍 Stage 3 — SHAPE: “How do I want to say it?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): give and receive peer feedback. Oral language: comments that encourage discussion; responding respectfully. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups — hyperbole, fragments, hooks, structural editing — all show up in the poems you've just cut.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 3 — SHAPE**: *“How do I want to say it?”*  \nHand action: Both hands moulding a ball of clay, turning it as you shape it.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 3, SHAPE — How do I want to say it?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Finishing SHAPE, entering SHARE — for the last time this year.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Handover — SHAPE into SHARE (2 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 3.\n\n1. \"SHAPE told us what to cut. What does SHARE do?\" (We test it on real listeners and then give it away.)\n2. Move the peg between 3 and 4; draw the loop arrow from 4 back to 3.\n3. Add the poetry version: \"In poetry that loop is the fastest it's been all year. Read it aloud, hear a word that doesn't belong, cut it, read again. Sometimes three times in one lesson.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Name it as the last handover of the year. Small ceremonies like this matter more in Term 4 than at any other time.\n\n#### I Do — Two Readings, a Silence, and No Explaining (4 min)\n\nThe poetry version of the protocol, and the one rule that's different from every other term.\n\n> Read twice → five seconds of silence → two stars → one wish → thank you.\nThe rule: nobody has to explain their poem, and nobody may ask them to.\n\nIf two people take different things from your poem, that's not a fault — that's what poems do. And if someone didn't get it at all, that's information, not an insult. The one thing you never do is explain what you meant. The poem is what you meant.\n\n**Tip:** \"I lost the picture at…\" is the most useful sentence in a poetry circle.\n\n#### We Do — Fishbowl (on me) (7 min)\n\nFour volunteers circle me. I read one of my poems twice; we hold the silence; they give me stars and a wish.\n\n- Two of you: the image or line that stayed.\n- Two of you: where you lost the picture, or a word that could go.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Did anyone want to ask me what it meant? Notice that I didn't tell you — and notice you're still thinking about it.\n\n#### You Do — Choose and Prepare (8 min)\n\nChoose which poem goes to the circle on Monday, and write your question.\n\n- Choose the poem you most want help with — not your best one.\n- Write ONE specific question: \"Does the ending land, or does it explain?\"\n- Practise reading it twice, slowly, with the silence.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Bring the poem you're least sure about. That's the one worth five minutes of four people's attention.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Giving and receiving feedback**: NZC Phase 2: \"give feedback to and receive feedback from peers\" — with poetry the protocol includes two readings and a held silence.\n- *Reading — Making connections* — **Respecting responses**: NZC Phase 2: \"acknowledge others' responses and respond respectfully to differences\" — two readers may take completely different things from one poem, and both are right.\n"}}},"Week 8: The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud\n\n> **Essential question:** What lands when someone hears this?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1 · Tue: Circle Day 2 and Triage · Wed: Stretch: Revision Workshops · Thu: Write: Reading a Poem So It Lands · Fri: Share & Reflect: Hearing It in Someone Else's Voice |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> No new prompt — this week is feedback, revision, and learning to read a poem so it lands.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which image did people say stayed with them?\n- Where did more than one person lose the picture?\n- Which change would I make if I only made one?\n- Where should I pause when I read this aloud?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nlanded · stayed · lost · pause · slow · quiet · clear · trust · silence · voice\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- The image I can still see is…\n- I lost the picture at…\n- I'm going to pause after…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Start with whatever two different people said. Two people is not an opinion.\n- **Picture this:** A poem with three pencil marks where a listener paused.\n- **Talk it out first:** Have someone else read your poem aloud to you. You will hear it properly for the first time.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Vocabulary of concession\n- **Tuesday** — Subordination vs coordination\n- **Wednesday** — Combining techniques — planning before drafting\n- **Thursday** — Peer editing for voice and style\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1":{"content":"### Launch: Helping Circle, Day 1\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to tell a poet which image stayed with us and where we lost the picture.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Vocabulary of concession |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): peer feedback used to revise. Reading: responding to texts with personal thoughts and feelings, respectfully. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nVocabulary of concession this morning — \"although\", \"even so\". Useful in feedback too: \"Even though I lost the picture there, the ending worked.\"\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's different about giving away a poem? (It has to be HEARD, and it only gets one pass.)*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Transfer — SHARE for poetry (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Peg on Stage 4.\n\n1. Open hands action, whole class. Then the four feedback questions on the board: DID YOU FEEL IT? · DID YOU UNDERSTAND IT? · WERE YOU CONVINCED? · WHAT STAYED WITH YOU?\n2. \"Notice the fourth one doesn't ask whether the poem worked. It asks what happened to YOU. That's because a poem can mean different things to different listeners and still be doing its job.\"\n3. Teach the two poetry-specific rules of the circle.\n   > \"Two readings, then five seconds of silence. And nobody has to explain their poem — the poem IS the explanation.\"\n4. Then the honest bit: \"Some of you will write something this term that matters to you. Nobody has to share anything, and anything in the anthology can go in under 'Anonymous'. That offer stands all term.\"\n\n**Watch for:** Make the anonymity offer out loud, in front of everyone, so no student has to ask for it privately. Follow your school's pastoral process for anything that raises a concern.\n\n#### I Do — The Protocol, One More Time (4 min)\n\nFive steps, and the two that are unique to poetry.\n\n> Read TWICE → five-second SILENCE → two stars → one wish → thank you and write it down.\n\nThe two readings and the silence are the poetry-specific bits, and they're the ones people rush. Don't. A poem read once hasn't really been heard, and a response given instantly is a response to the last line only.\n\n**Tip:** Count the silence in your head. Five seconds is longer than you think and shorter than it feels.\n\n#### We Do — Form the Circles (3 min)\n\nGroups of four, seated close, timer on the board, roles named.\n\n- Who reads first?\n- Who counts the silence?\n- Everyone: pencil ready to write feedback down.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Five minutes per poet — two today, two tomorrow.\n\n#### You Do — Helping Circle, Round 1 (14 min)\n\nTwo poets per group today. Two readings, silence, stars, wish.\n\n- Read your poem twice, slowly.\n- Ask your question.\n- Take two stars and one wish from each listener.\n- Write down the exact words people used about your images.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Write it down now. The exact words matter — they tell you which pictures are working.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Reading — Making connections and interpretations* — **Personal response**: NZC Phase 2: \"respond to texts by sharing opinions and personal thoughts and feelings about ideas in texts.\"\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Using peer feedback**: NZC Phase 2: revisions made \"using audience and peer feedback\" to improve effect for a reader.\n"},"Tuesday: Circle Day 2 and Triage":{"content":"### Circle Day 2 and Triage\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to decide, as the poet, what to change and what to keep.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Subordination vs coordination |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): using feedback to revise while retaining authorship. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nSubordination versus coordination this morning — poems often prefer neither: two short lines side by side, with the connection left for the reader to make.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who decides what changes in your poem? (You. Always. But two people lost in the same place is a fact.)*\n\n#### I Do — Triage, Poetry Style (4 min)\n\nThree piles, sorted out loud — with the poetry rule attached.\n\n> DO IT · THINK ABOUT IT · POLITELY LEAVE IT. The rule: if two people lost the picture in the same place, fix it. If two people read it differently, that's fine — leave it.\n\nThat second half matters. In an explanation, two readers understanding different things is a failure. In a poem, it can be the whole point. Confusion about the PICTURE is a problem; difference in what the poem MEANS to someone is not.\n\n**Tip:** Fix the picture. Don't fix the meaning.\n\n#### We Do — Regroup (3 min)\n\nSame circles, timer reset, remaining two poets.\n\n- Readers three and four — read twice, hold the silence.\n- Then everyone sorts their feedback into three piles.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Same protocol. Same five minutes.\n\n#### You Do — Circle Round 2, then Triage (15 min)\n\nFinish the circle, then decide what you're changing.\n\n- Remaining poets: read, ask, receive, record.\n- Sort your feedback into the three piles.\n- Write your one change per poem, as an action.\n- Beside anything you're leaving, write why in four words.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Point at your number one change with a finger on the page.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Selecting revisions**: NZC Phase 2 writers use peer feedback to revise — and choosing what not to change is part of the skill.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Think before acting**: Weighing responses that may genuinely conflict, and deciding on the basis of the poem rather than the loudest voice.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Revision Workshops":{"content":"### Stretch: Revision Workshops\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to make the changes we chose, using the four moves.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Combining techniques — planning before drafting |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — revising (Phase 2): \"make simple revisions… replacing words, adding sentences, and using audience and peer feedback.\" |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nCombining techniques this morning — planning before drafting. In poetry the planning was the noticing, six weeks ago. That's why this week is possible.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What are we doing right now? (Recrafting — the loop, for the last time this year.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Moves, One Poem (4 min)\n\nAll four moves on one short poem. Watch how small each change is.\n\n> ADD (hands apart) · CUT (scissors) · REORDER (swap hands) · REPLACE (one hand over the other)\n\nAdd: three words of a habit in the second stanza. Cut: my explaining last line. Reorder: the two stanzas swap, so the poem ends on the person rather than the object. Replace: \"walked\" becomes \"shuffled\". Four tiny changes, and the poem is twice as good — and the biggest of them was the cut.\n\n**Tip:** In poetry the strongest move is nearly always CUT, and the second strongest is REORDER.\n\n#### We Do — Name Your Move (3 min)\n\nRound the room: your number one change — which move is it?\n\n- \"A CUT — my last line.\"\n- \"A REPLACE — 'nice' has to go.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone REORDERING two stanzas? That's a big move for two seconds' work.\n\n#### You Do — Revision Workshops (16 min)\n\nEverybody revises all three poems. Two six-minute workshops at the back table.\n\n- Work through your one change per poem.\n- Name the move before you make it.\n- Workshop 1: endings that explain (how to stop earlier).\n- Workshop 2: replacing a borrowed comparison with a true one.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read a before and after — one line each. Name the move.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revision moves**: ADD · CUT · REORDER · REPLACE — the same four moves as every term, with CUT dominant in this genre.\n- *Writing — Writing craft (Word choice)* — **Replacing precisely**: NZC Phase 2: replacing words to improve effect — a single replaced verb can rescue a line.\n"},"Thursday: Write: Reading a Poem So It Lands":{"content":"### Write: Reading a Poem So It Lands\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to read a poem aloud with pace, pause and silence.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Peer editing for voice and style |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively; vary structure and length for effect; use precise vocabulary. Delivery as part of how a poem conveys meaning. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPeer editing for voice and style this morning — and today \"voice\" means your actual voice.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Where do these poems end up? (Read aloud at the celebration, and printed in our anthology.)*\n\n#### I Do — Four Things, and the Fourth Is Silence (6 min)\n\nOne poem, read badly and then properly. Count the differences.\n\n> SLOW — slower than feels natural.\nPAUSE — a real breath at each line break.\nQUIET — poems are read quietly; you don't have to project like a speech.\nSILENCE — three seconds at the end, standing still.\n\nWatch the bad version: fast, flat, eyes down, \"um, yeah, thanks\" at the end. Now the proper one. Same words. And notice the last thing — I said nothing for three seconds and looked up. That silence is where the poem finishes, and if you talk over it you've thrown it away.\n\n**Tip:** Announce your title, pause, then begin. And never apologise for your poem.\n\n#### We Do — Mark the Script (5 min)\n\nEveryone marks their reading copy with the same three symbols.\n\n- / = short pause · // = long pause\n- Underline the ONE word in the poem you most want heard.\n- Draw a box around the last line — that's where you stop and hold.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Where's your longest pause, and why there?\n\n#### You Do — Rehearse in Pairs (9 min)\n\nRead all three of your poems aloud to a partner, using your marks.\n\n- Read each poem with your marked pauses.\n- Partner: one thing that worked, and one place to slow down.\n- Adjust your marks.\n- Practise announcing your title and holding the final silence.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Whose reading made the room go quiet at the end? That's the goal.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Engaging listeners**: NZC Phase 2: \"give descriptions… with specific details to actively engage listeners\" — for poetry, delivery carries a large share of the meaning.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Preparing to perform**: Rehearsing deliberately, marking a script, and acting on a peer's tip before a public reading.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Hearing It in Someone Else's Voice":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Hearing It in Someone Else's Voice\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning what our poem sounds like when someone else reads it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 8 — The Helping Circle & Reading Aloud  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Feedback and voice |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): listening and responding; reading aloud to engage a listener. Writing processes: revising from what is heard. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were style tools. Today somebody else's voice tests every one of them.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. When someone else stumbles reading your poem, what does that tell you? (Back to SHAPE — that line needs work.)*\n\n#### I Do — Someone Else Reads Mine (3 min)\n\nA student reads my poem to me. I listen without a pen, then say what I heard.\n\n> [A student reads the teacher's poem aloud, twice.]\n\nThat was uncomfortable and extremely useful. They paused in a place I didn't expect, which means my line break is in the wrong spot — and they stumbled on line five, which means it's too long. I didn't have to be told anything; I just had to hear it from outside my own head.\n\n**Tip:** Every stumble a reader makes is a line you need to look at.\n\n#### We Do — Set the Swap (4 min)\n\nRules for reading someone else's poem: read it as if you mean it, and don't perform it as a joke.\n\n- Read it twice, slowly, the way it's marked.\n- Writer: listen with your pen down.\n- Afterwards, writer says what they heard — reader says what was hard to read.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why is this different from just reading it yourself?\n\n#### You Do — The Poem Swap (13 min)\n\nSwap poems with a partner. Read each other's aloud, twice. Then mark what you learned.\n\n- Read your partner's poem aloud, twice, properly.\n- Writer: mark every place the reader paused or stumbled.\n- Swap and repeat.\n- Fix your two worst stumbles before you leave.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nJotter: \"When someone else read my poem I heard… so I changed…\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Listening and responding* — **Listening critically to one's own text**: Hearing your own words in another voice removes the writer's memory of intention and exposes what is actually on the page.\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Revising)* — **Revising from the ear**: NZC Phase 2: rereading and revising — the ear catches what the eye, which knows what it meant, does not.\n"}}},"Week 9: Best Copies and the Year Behind Us":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us\n\n> **Essential question:** How have I changed as a writer this year?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: The Final Edit · Tue: Build Together: The Anthology Page · Wed: Stretch: Four Terms, One Writer · Thu: Write: The Year-in-Review Reflection · Fri: Share & Reflect: Rehearsal and Reading |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Make your poems beautiful on the page. Then look back at four terms of your own writing and tell the truth about what changed.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Which piece from this year am I proudest of, and why that one?\n- What can I do now that I genuinely could not do in February?\n- What is still hard for me as a writer?\n- What would I tell myself at the start of the year?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nnoticed · changed · improved · struggled · learned · braver · slower · clearer · honest · next\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- In February I would have…\n- The piece I'm proudest of is…\n- I still find it hard to…\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Put your Term 1 narrative and your Term 4 poems side by side. Write down three differences you can actually see on the page.\n- **Picture this:** Four pieces of writing spread out on a desk.\n- **Talk it out first:** Tell your partner one specific thing you can do now that you couldn't do in Term 1. Make them ask \"how do you know?\"\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Year vocabulary review — choosing your best tool\n- **Tuesday** — Year grammar review\n- **Wednesday** — Planning and drafting a chosen text type\n- **Thursday** — Full publication-ready edit\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: The Final Edit":{"content":"### Launch: The Final Edit\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to edit a poem without flattening it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Year vocabulary review — choosing your best tool |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — editing (Phase 2): make edits using known punctuation; use a dictionary for unknown spellings. Sentence structures: fragments used deliberately. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYear vocabulary review this morning — choose your best tool. In a poem you only get about forty words, so every one of them has to be your best tool.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's the difference between revising and editing? (Revising = the poem. Editing = the spelling. In that order.)*\n\n#### I Do — Editing a Poem Is Different (6 min)\n\nTwo passes over my poem. One fixes real errors; the other one would ruin it.\n\n> REAL ERRORS: \"cicarda\" → cicada · a missing capital on a name · \"its\" vs \"it's\".\nNOT ERRORS: no full stop at the end of a line · a fragment with no verb · a line starting with \"and\" · a whole stanza that is one sentence.\n\nThis is the only genre where I have to tell you which rules you're allowed to break. Spelling and names still matter — a misspelled word in a printed anthology is just a mistake. But a line without a full stop is a choice, and a fragment is a choice. You have to be able to say WHY you broke it, and then it's fine.\n\n**Tip:** If you can explain why you broke the rule, it's a choice. If you can't, it's an error.\n\n#### We Do — Error or Choice? (6 min)\n\nLines on the board. For each: is it an error, or a deliberate choice?\n\n- \"Two maps.\" (no verb)\n- \"the gate was open all night\" (no capital at the start)\n- \"empty as a gloove\"\n- \"And nobody came.\"\n\n**Discussion prompt:** For each choice, can we say what it does for the poem?\n\n#### You Do — Three Passes (9 min)\n\nThree passes over all three poems. One thing at a time.\n\n- Pass 1: spelling — check anything you can't picture, in a dictionary.\n- Pass 2: names and capitals for people and places.\n- Pass 3: every place you broke a rule — write \"choice\" beside it if you can say why, and fix it if you can't.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read us a rule you broke on purpose, and tell us why.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Editing)* — **Editing for publication**: NZC Phase 2: \"make edits to draft paragraphs using known punctuation\" and \"use a word card or simple dictionary to find the spellings of unknown words.\"\n- *Writing — Sentence structures* — **Deliberate rule-breaking**: NZC Phase 2: \"demonstrate understanding of incomplete (fragment) and run-on sentences\" — understanding is what makes deliberate breaking possible.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: The Anthology Page":{"content":"### Build Together: The Anthology Page\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to lay a poem out on a page so the white space works for it.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Year grammar review |\n| **NZC link** | Writing (Phase 2): layout conventions and visual elements used deliberately; presentation for publication. Transcription: legible handwriting for a published piece. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYear grammar review this morning — everything you've been taught since February is on the checklist you're using today.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Who sees this page? (Families, at the celebration — and it stays in the anthology afterwards.)*\n\n#### I Do — One Poem, One Page (5 min)\n\nThe same poem laid out two ways. One is crowded; the other lets it breathe.\n\n> CROWDED: poem squeezed into the top corner, title the same size as the poem, no margins.\nBREATHING: title at the top with space under it, poem sitting in the upper-middle of the page, name small at the bottom, wide white margins all round.\n\nA poem needs room around it. On a crowded page your eye can't settle and the poem looks smaller than it is. One poem per page, even if it's four lines — especially if it's four lines.\n\n**Tip:** White space is not wasted space. It's the frame.\n\n#### We Do — Agree the Anthology Standard (4 min)\n\nFour decisions so the anthology looks like one book rather than 25 different ones.\n\n- Title at the top, in the same style by everyone.\n- One poem per page.\n- Name at the bottom.\n- Generous margins — nothing within two fingers of the edge.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Should we allow illustrations? If yes, what rule keeps them from swamping the poems?\n\n#### You Do — Best Copies (12 min)\n\nThree pages, one poem each, in your best handwriting. Take your time.\n\n- Write out each poem, one per page, following the standard.\n- Check your line breaks match your draft exactly — this is where they get lost.\n- Reread each page against your edit passes.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Not finished? Finish tomorrow. Nobody rushes a best copy — last time I'll say it this year.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Transcription (Handwriting)* — **Legibility for publication**: NZC Phase 2: \"handwrite with increasing stamina and fluency while maintaining legibility\" — assessed on the anthology best copy.\n- *Reading — Text structure, style and features* — **Layout as meaning**: Phase 2: visual elements convey ideas — a poem's position on the page is part of its effect.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Four Terms, One Writer":{"content":"### Stretch: Four Terms, One Writer\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to look at a year of our own writing and see what changed.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~26 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Planning and drafting a chosen text type |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\" Reflection with evidence. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPlanning and drafting a chosen text type this morning — you've now got four to choose from, which is the whole point of the year.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *Draw the cycle one last time. Which stage did you get best at this year?*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — The whole year (4 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Board space. Every student's portfolio out on the desk — four terms of published work.\n\n1. Draw the cycle from memory on the board, or better, get a student to. All four stages, the SHAPE↔SHARE loop, and the long arrow back to FILL.\n2. \"Now put your four pieces in front of you.\" Give them thirty seconds to lay out narrative, explanation, argument and poems.\n3. Walk the stages across the four pieces out loud: \"Point at where FILL happened for your narrative. Now for your explanation. Now this term.\" Students physically point at their own work.\n4. Ask the question that lands the year: \"Which stage did YOU get best at this year?\" Take five or six answers. Write them down — they belong in Thursday's reflection.\n\n**Watch for:** Students who cannot articulate the process suddenly can when the evidence is physically in front of them. This lesson exists so that Thursday's written reflection is evidenced rather than vague.\n\n#### I Do — Two Pieces, Side by Side (5 min)\n\nMy Term 1 writing beside my Term 4 writing. Three differences I can actually point at.\n\n> \"In February I wrote 'it was really scary'. In November I wrote 'my hands wouldn't work'. In February my paragraphs were one long block. Now I break them on purpose. In February I wrote until I ran out. Now I stop one line early.\"\n\nNotice I'm pointing at things on the page, not saying \"I got better at writing\". \"I got better\" is what everyone writes and it means nothing. Three specific differences you can point at — that's a real reflection.\n\n**Tip:** Point at the page. If you can't point at it, you can't claim it.\n\n#### We Do — What Should a Reflection Do? (5 min)\n\nBuild the criteria for the reflection together.\n\n- Name a specific piece, not \"my writing\".\n- Quote or point to an actual line.\n- Say what's still hard — honestly.\n- Say what you'd tell yourself in February.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Why does \"I improved a lot\" tell a reader nothing?\n\n#### You Do — The Portfolio Spread (9 min)\n\nGet out all four pieces: your narrative, your explanation, your argument, your poems. Lay them out.\n\n- Read your Term 1 narrative right through. Don't wince.\n- Choose the piece you're proudest of and mark why with a sticky note.\n- Write three differences you can point at between February and now.\n- Write one thing that is still hard.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Which piece surprised you when you reread it? Most people say their Term 1 one — in both directions.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflection with evidence**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection on one's own content and processes — with four terms of work available, the reflection can be evidenced rather than asserted.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Seeing yourself in the big picture**: \"See yourself in the big picture\" — an explicit Phase 2 indicator, and precisely what a year-in-review portfolio task develops.\n"},"Thursday: Write: The Year-in-Review Reflection":{"content":"### Write: The Year-in-Review Reflection\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to write an honest, evidenced reflection on our own growth as a writer.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Full publication-ready edit |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): reflection on content and process; goal-setting. Composition: writing with a real audience in mind (next year's teacher, and yourself). |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nPublication-ready editing this morning — your reflection gets the same treatment as your poems, because next year's teacher will read it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Is a reflection a piece of writing? (Yes — it goes through the same stages, just faster.)*\n\n#### I Do — Honest Beats Impressive (5 min)\n\nTwo reflections. One sounds good and says nothing; the other is true.\n\n> A: \"This year I have improved a lot at writing. I have learned many new skills and I am proud of all my work.\"\nB: \"In February I wrote 'it was really scary' three times in one page. I didn't know there was another way to do it. Now I'd write what my hands were doing. The hardest thing for me is still endings — I want to explain them, and I have to make myself stop.\"\n\nA could have been written by anyone about any year. B could only be written by someone who has actually looked at their own work. And notice B admits something hard — that's not a weakness in a reflection, it's the strongest part of it.\n\n**Tip:** Name a piece, quote a line, admit one hard thing.\n\n#### We Do — The Five Questions (3 min)\n\nThe structure. Five questions, in order.\n\n- 1. The piece I'm proudest of is… because…\n- 2. Three things I can do now that I couldn't do in February…\n- 3. The thing that is still hard for me is…\n- 4. The most useful thing I learned about HOW to write was…\n- 5. What I'd tell myself in February…\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Which of these will be hardest to answer honestly?\n\n#### You Do — Write the Reflection (14 min)\n\nPortfolio open beside you. Answer all five properly — this goes in your portfolio and to next year's teacher.\n\n- Write all five answers in full sentences.\n- Quote at least one actual line from your own writing.\n- For question 4, use the process language — FILL, FIND, SHAPE, SHARE.\n- Reread and cut anything that could have been written by anyone.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Read question 3 to a partner — the hard one. It's usually the best paragraph anyone writes today.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Goal-setting from reflection**: NZC Phase 2: \"set and work towards specific writing goals based on reflection on their own writing content and processes.\"\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Identify strengths and limitations**: \"Describe personal strengths and limitations\" — with four terms of evidence to draw on, at the end of a year.\n"},"Friday: Share & Reflect: Rehearsal and Reading":{"content":"### Share & Reflect: Rehearsal and Reading\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to rehearse for a real audience and support each other's nerves.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 9 — Best Copies and the Year Behind Us  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · Publishing, and looking back |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~23 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners; use precise vocabulary; respond to feedback about delivery. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThis week's warm-ups were the whole year's tools reviewed. Next week you use them one last time.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. What's left? (Type it, print it, and read it to people.)*\n\n#### I Do — Announce, Read, Hold (3 min)\n\nThe whole shape of a reading, done properly once.\n\n> \"This one's called 'Small Thing'.\" — pause — read — three seconds of silence — sit down.\n\nAnnounce the title, then pause before you start. It gives the room time to settle and it gives you time to breathe. And the silence at the end is not optional — it's the last line of the poem.\n\n**Tip:** Say the title. Pause. Read. Hold the silence. Sit down. Don't say \"that's it\".\n\n#### We Do — Choose Your Celebration Poem (4 min)\n\nDecide which of your three you'll read aloud next week, and why.\n\n- Which one sounds best out loud?\n- Which one is short enough to land in one hearing?\n- Which one do you actually want people to hear?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** It doesn't have to be your best poem on the page — it has to be your best poem in the air.\n\n#### You Do — Full Rehearsal in Fours (13 min)\n\nGroups of four. Announce, read, hold the silence. Twice each.\n\n- Rehearse your celebration poem twice.\n- Listeners: one thing that worked, one place to slow down.\n- Mark your reading copy with any new pauses.\n- Finish your best copies if they aren't done.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Next week: we type it, we print it, and then people hear it. Last week of the year.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Reading for an audience**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners actively — rehearsed, with pauses and a held silence at the end.\n- *Key competency — Relating to others* — **Encouragement**: \"Make space for others to contribute\" — a rehearsal audience's job is to make the next reader braver.\n"}}},"Week 10: Publish, Perform, Hand It On":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview & Prompt":{"content":"### Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On\n\n> **Essential question:** What have I made this year — and what would I tell the next Year 5s?\n\n\n| This week | |\n|---|---|\n| **Writing process stage** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Week focus** | Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **This week’s lessons** | Mon: Launch: Typing for the Anthology · Tue: Build Together: Making the Anthology · Wed: Stretch: Dress Rehearsal · Thu: The Celebration: The Anthology Launch · Fri: Reflect: Four Genres, One Process — and Hand It On |\n\n#### The weekly prompt\n\n> Publish your poems in the anthology, read one aloud to a real audience, and write down our writing process for the students who come after you.\n\n**Layer 2 — question ladder**\n\n- Is my anthology page laid out the way we agreed?\n- Have I checked the line breaks against my best copy?\n- Which poem am I reading, and where do I pause?\n- What would a Year 5 need to know in February?\n\n**Layer 3 — word bank**\n\nanthology · published · launch · audience · perform · silence · applause · gratitude · handover · next\n\n**Sentence starters**\n\n- This one's called…\n- Thank you for listening.\n- Dear next year's Year 5s,\n\n**Layer 4 — only if you need it**\n\n- **Borrow this idea:** Read your shortest poem. Four lines is a complete performance.\n- **Picture this:** A printed anthology with everyone's name in the contents.\n- **Talk it out first:** Say your title and your first line three times before you stand up.\n\n#### Warm-Up links this week\n\n- **Monday** — Year celebration — vocabulary Hall of Fame\n- **Tuesday** — Year celebration — grammar mastery challenge\n- **Wednesday** — Year celebration — flexible writing showcase\n- **Thursday** — Portfolio piece — the whole-year best edit\n\n*The Daily Warm-Up runs Monday–Thursday, so Friday’s bridge looks back across the whole week.*\n\n*Printable student worksheet for this week: open the Writing Focus app → Weekly Prompt → 📝 Print student worksheet.*\n"},"Monday: Launch: Typing for the Anthology":{"content":"### Launch: Typing for the Anthology\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to type a poem so its shape survives.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **Day** | Monday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Year celebration — vocabulary Hall of Fame |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes — publishing: transferring an edited text to digital format while preserving layout and line breaks. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nVocabulary Hall of Fame this morning — nominate the best word you used all year. Some of them are in your poems.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Final week. What does publishing mean this term? (The anthology, and reading it aloud.)*\n\n#### I Do — Where Poems Get Broken (6 min)\n\nTyping a poem, and the three places the shape gets lost.\n\n> 1. Word \"helpfully\" capitalising the start of every line. 2. A line wrapping because the font is too big — that is NOT a line break. 3. Stanza gaps disappearing when you paste.\n\nThis is the only genre where typing can break the writing. Check every line against your handwritten copy. If a line wraps, make the font smaller or the margins wider — don't let the computer decide where your poem breaks.\n\n**Tip:** Type it, then hold it beside your best copy and check the shape line by line.\n\n#### We Do — Filename and Layout (4 min)\n\nAgree the convention and the anthology page format.\n\n- Surname_Firstname_Poems_T4\n- Title in bold at the top, poem below, name at the bottom.\n- One poem per page. Turn off autocapitalise if it fights you.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What do we do if a poem is only four lines — does it still get a whole page? (Yes.)\n\n#### You Do — Type Your Three (11 min)\n\nType all three poems from your best copies. Check the shape as you go.\n\n- Create, title and save the document before typing.\n- Type each poem on its own page.\n- Check every line break against your handwritten copy.\n- Fix any line the computer wrapped.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Hold your typed page beside your handwritten one. Same shape? Good.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes (Drafting)* — **Layout conventions**: Preserving line breaks and stanza gaps in a typed version is a genuine technical skill and a real risk point for poetry.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Organisation**: Saving with an agreed filename, checking against the handwritten copy, meeting a publication deadline.\n"},"Tuesday: Build Together: Making the Anthology":{"content":"### Build Together: Making the Anthology\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to assemble a class publication that we are all part of.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **Day** | Tuesday |\n| **Length** | ~24 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Year celebration — grammar mastery challenge |\n| **NZC link** | Composition (Phase 2): writing with the audience in mind; presentation for publication. Participating and contributing to a shared product. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nGrammar mastery challenge this morning — and your reflection needs that mastery, because next year's teacher reads it.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Whose anthology is this? (Everybody's — which is why everybody has a page.)*\n\n#### I Do — What a Book Needs (5 min)\n\nThe parts of the anthology, and the jobs that go with them.\n\n> A cover with a title the class chooses · a contents page with everyone's name · the poems · a short \"about the writers\" page · a dedication.\n\nThe contents page matters more than you'd think — it's where a family member looks first, for their child's name. And a dedication is worth doing: \"For everyone who read to us this year.\" Small thing, big effect.\n\n**Tip:** Every student's name in the contents. Nobody gets left out of a class anthology.\n\n#### We Do — Decide and Assign (5 min)\n\nClass decisions and volunteer jobs.\n\n- What's our anthology called?\n- Who's designing the cover?\n- Who's typing the contents page?\n- Who do we dedicate it to?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Anyone want the editor's job — checking every page matches the standard?\n\n#### You Do — Finish and Hand In (11 min)\n\nFinish your pages and your reflection. Hand in when they're right, not when they're done.\n\n- Final check of your three typed poems against the standard.\n- Proofread your reflection — spelling, and does it point at real evidence?\n- Print or submit to the class folder.\n- Help with an anthology job if you're finished.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow we rehearse properly. Thursday, people hear it.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Audience, purpose and task* — **Publication for a real audience**: NZC Phase 2: writing with a specific audience in mind — the anthology goes to families and stays in the school.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Contributing to something shared**: Every student's work appears in a single published object that belongs to the class.\n"},"Wednesday: Stretch: Dress Rehearsal":{"content":"### Stretch: Dress Rehearsal\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to perform a reading under the conditions of the real event.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **Day** | Wednesday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Year celebration — flexible writing showcase |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners actively; vary pace and length for effect; respond to an audience. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nThe flexible writing showcase this morning — you have four genres in your hands now. That's the year in one sentence.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE. Anything that goes wrong today sends you back where? (SHAPE — one last time, tonight.)*\n\n#### I Do — The Whole Thing, Properly (4 min)\n\nI'll do my reading exactly as I would tomorrow, from where I'll stand.\n\n> [Walk up. Wait for silence. \"This one's called…\" Pause. Read. Three seconds. Walk back.]\n\nThe walking counts. Get to your spot, wait until the room is properly quiet, and don't start until it is. The waiting feels like an hour and it is two seconds — and it makes everyone listen.\n\n**Tip:** Wait for the silence before you begin. It is yours to take.\n\n#### We Do — The Running Order (3 min)\n\nSet the order and the audience contract.\n\n- Draw or decide the reading order; write it on the board.\n- Silence while someone reads; applause for everyone, the same.\n- Three-second silence after each poem before anyone claps — practise it.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** Would you rather be early or late in the order?\n\n#### You Do — Full Dress Rehearsal (15 min)\n\nHalf the class reads to the other half, in the real order, from the real spot. Then swap.\n\n- Walk up, wait, announce, read, hold the silence, sit down.\n- Note anything that went wrong and fix it tonight.\n- Listeners: practise the three-second silence before applause.\n- Everyone reads once.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\n\"Tomorrow your families hear a poem that did not exist ten weeks ago.\"\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Sustained delivery**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners — rehearsed under conditions close to the real event, which is the most effective way to reduce anxiety.\n- *Key competency — Managing self* — **Managing nerves**: Rehearsal, a known running order and a marked script convert most performance anxiety into ordinary readiness.\n"},"Thursday: The Celebration: The Anthology Launch":{"content":"### The Celebration: The Anthology Launch\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to give our writing away to a real audience.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **Day** | Thursday |\n| **Length** | ~25 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Portfolio piece — the whole-year best edit |\n| **NZC link** | Oral Language (Phase 2): engage listeners; respond respectfully to others. Writing: publication for a real audience and purpose. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nYour last warm-up of the year was a whole-piece edit. Everything you were taught since February is in the book you're holding.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *SHARE — the last step of the last stage of the year. Say all four stages one final time.*\n\n#### I Do — Open the Event (3 min)\n\nWelcome the audience, explain what they're about to hear, and read first.\n\n> \"Every poem you'll hear started as something one of us actually looked at. Some of them are four lines long. Listen for the pictures.\" Then read your own.\n\nTelling the audience what to listen for makes them better listeners — and it protects the shortest poems, which are often the best. And the teacher reading first means no student has to go first.\n\n**Tip:** Ask the audience to hold their applause until after the silence. Explain why, once, at the start.\n\n#### We Do — The Audience's Job (2 min)\n\nTwo rules, said to the room before the first reader.\n\n- Silence while someone reads; three seconds after, then applause for everyone equally.\n- Afterwards, tell a writer one image you'll remember.\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What does \"specific\" mean when you tell someone what you liked?\n\n#### You Do — Read (17 min)\n\nEvery student reads one poem. Anthologies handed out at the end.\n\n- Walk up, wait for silence, announce your title.\n- Read slowly, with your marked pauses.\n- Hold the three seconds. Walk back.\n- Listen to every other reader.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nName the value out loud: this is Pānga — impact. Twenty-five people noticed something this term that nobody else did, and now a room full of people has seen it too. Soli Deo Gloria.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Oral Language — Verbal reasoning* — **Performing a text**: NZC Phase 2: engaging listeners with specific detail and precise vocabulary — the term's oral assessment.\n- *Key competency — Participating & contributing* — **Contributing to a community**: Contributing a finished piece of work to a shared occasion beyond the classroom.\n"},"Friday: Reflect: Four Genres, One Process — and Hand It On":{"content":"### Reflect: Four Genres, One Process — and Hand It On\n\n> **WALT:** We are learning to explain our writing process across four genres, and to pass it on to the writers who come next.\n\n\n| Lesson | |\n|---|---|\n| **Term** | Term 4 — Poetry & Reflection: “Every Word Counts” |\n| **Week** | Week 10 — Publish, Perform, Hand It On  ·  Stage 4 — SHARE · The anthology and the celebration |\n| **Day** | Friday |\n| **Length** | ~28 minutes |\n| **Writing process** | 🎁 Stage 4 — SHARE: “Is it ready to give away?” |\n| **Warm-up today** | Friday — looks back across the whole week |\n| **NZC link** | Writing processes (Phase 2): reflection on content and processes; goal-setting for the future. Composition: writing for a specific audience — next year's Year 5s. |\n\n#### Warm-Up Bridge (2 min)\n\nLook back at forty weeks of warm-ups. Every single one of them turned up inside a real piece of writing.\n\n#### Process Check (1 min)\n\nToday we are in **Stage 4 — SHARE**: *“Is it ready to give away?”*  \nHand action: Open both hands and push them forward, offering.\n\n- **Ask with your hands** — “Hands — which stage are we in?” Students answer silently with the hand action.\n- **Name it together** — whole class, out loud: “Stage 4, SHARE — Is it ready to give away?”\n- **One student justifies it** — “How do you know we’re in that stage today?” The answer must point at what the class is actually doing.\n- **Today’s extra question** — *All four stages, four examples, and the four constants.*\n\n#### Process Spotlight — Assessment — four genres, and hand it on (5 min)\n\n**Before the lesson:** Wall strip covered. All four criteria charts and the four SHAPE questions visible. Fresh paper for the handover letters.\n\n1. Give the task exactly as written.\n   > \"Explain our writing process to someone who has never heard of it. Give an example of every stage from a different term. Say what stayed exactly the same in all four genres. Then write the version you'd give to next year's Year 5s.\"\n2. Out loud first: 90 seconds each to a partner. Listen in on six or seven students — that listening is your record for most of the class.\n3. Then in writing, as part of the reflection, and as the handover letter.\n4. Finish the year: the chant one last time, then hand the four wall cards to four students who can explain them, to teach the incoming class.\n\n**Watch for:** Top band: names all four stages and their questions, gives examples from at least three terms, names the four constants, explains the cycle, and writes something a Year 5 could actually follow in February. Record on the \"Process\" row of the rubric and pass the handover letters to next year's teacher.\n\n#### I Do — What Never Changed (3 min)\n\nMy year, in four sentences.\n\n> \"Four completely different pieces of writing. And every single time: I filled before I drafted. I wrote to one particular person. I read it as someone who didn't already agree. And I tested it on a real human being before I published it.\"\n\nThose four sentences are what I actually want you to take to intermediate, to college, and into every essay and application and letter you ever write. The genres will keep changing. That process won't.\n\n**Tip:** Learn the four constants. They outlast every genre you'll ever be set.\n\n#### We Do — Four Charts, One Wall (5 min)\n\nAll four criteria charts up together — narrative, explanation, argument, poem. Compare them one last time.\n\n- What appears on ALL FOUR lists?\n- What's on only one?\n- Which term was hardest, and why?\n- Which stage did this class get best at?\n\n**Discussion prompt:** What's true of all good writing, whatever you're writing?\n\n#### You Do — Hand It On (12 min)\n\nTwo things: finish the year's reflection, then write the process down for next year's Year 5s.\n\n- Finish any of the five reflection questions still open.\n- Then, on a fresh page: \"Dear next year's Year 5s — here is how we write.\"\n- Explain all four stages in your own words, with one example each from your own year.\n- Add the one piece of advice you most wish you'd had in February.\n- These pages go up on the wall for the incoming class.\n\n#### Close (1–2 min)\n\nRead the Soli Deo Gloria line together, and 1 Thessalonians 5:18 — give thanks. Four terms, four kinds of writing, one process, and a book with everyone's name in it. Close in prayer or a shared thank-you.\n\n#### Learning Objectives (AOs)\n\n- *Writing — Writing processes* — **Reflection and transfer**: NZC Phase 2: goals set from reflection on one's own writing content and processes — the year's final assessment of process knowledge.\n- *Key competency — Thinking* — **Metacognition**: \"Metacognition: being aware of your own thinking\" — explaining a process across four genres and writing it for a novice audience is the strongest available evidence of it.\n"}}}}}}},"devotions2027":{"label":"Term","children":{"Term 1":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: In the Beginning":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1:1–2:3; 2:15–25; Psalm 19:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Creator</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wow — A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: telling God what is amazing about HIM.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Let there be…</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning that God made everything simply by speaking.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Let there be… → No light at all → Genesis 1:1–5 → Days 1–3: God speaks → How does God make things? → Why the repeating? → Let there be… → Light into darkness → Head · Heart · Hands → And it was good → Memory verse peek → Wow prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Made Like God</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what God made on Days 4, 5 and 6.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Made Like God → Recap: Days 1–3 → Genesis 1:26–27, 31 → Days 4–6: God fills the world → Spot the difference → Which day? → Made like God → Even them? → Head · Heart · Hands → Very good → Build the verse → Wow prayer (one sentence aloud)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Garden</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning about the Garden of Eden and the work God gave Adam.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Garden → All by yourself → Genesis 2:15, 18 → Zoom in on Eden → Not good → The Garden → Work in paradise → Wasn’t God enough? → Head · Heart · Hands → Someone like me → Verse with actions → Wow prayer (open hands)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Rest and Wonder</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why God rested on the seventh day.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Rest and Wonder → Seven days in order → Genesis 2:2–3 · Psalm 19:1 → Day 7 and a shepherd’s song → Why rest? → What is the sky saying? → This week’s Big Idea → Is rest a waste? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → The heavens are telling → Praying a psalm</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Let there be…":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1:1–2:3; 2:15–25; Psalm 19:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let there be…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wow — A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: telling God what is amazing about HIM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that God made everything simply by speaking.</li><li>We are learning what Genesis 1 shows us about what God is like.</li><li>I can retell what God made on Days 1, 2 and 3.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking ‘God said… and that’s what happened’ to God’s power, and naming at least one thing from each of Days 1–3.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch for the ‘light’ group</li><li>Optional: blue fabric for the sea, green paper leaves for the plants</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Let there be…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome to the very first devotion of 2027! Let the title animate, then say: ‘This year we’re asking one big question: Who is God? And we’re starting at the very beginning.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: No light at all</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine a world with no light at all. No sun. No stars. No torch. Not even a phone screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s the first thing you would miss?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 3–4 quick answers. You’re aiming for: you couldn’t see, nothing could grow, it would feel scary and empty. Then land it: ‘That is exactly how the Bible begins — in darkness. Let’s see what God does about it.’ If there’s silence, try: ‘What would breakfast be like?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optional: switch the lights off for 5 seconds as students imagine it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 1:1–5</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\">In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">The earth was barren, with no form of life; it was under a roaring ocean covered with darkness. But the Spirit of God was moving over the water.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">God said, “I command light to shine!” And light started shining.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">God looked at the light and saw that it was good. He separated light from darkness and named the light “Day” and the darkness “Night.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">Evening came, then morning—that was the first day.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read each line as it appears (click to reveal). Pause after ‘I command light to shine!’ — let it land.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Days 1–3: God speaks</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Before there was a Monday… before there was a morning… before there was anything at all — there was God. No sun. No sky. No ground under your feet. The Bible says the earth was empty, with no form of life, under a roaring ocean covered with darkness. Thick, black darkness. And yet God was there. The Spirit of God was moving over the water, like a bird hovering over its nest.</p><p>Then God spoke. Not a grunt of effort. Not a struggle. Just words: ‘I command light to shine!’ And light started shining. Imagine it — the very first light ever, flooding across that dark ocean. God looked at the light and saw that it was good. He named the light ‘Day’ and the darkness ‘Night’. Evening came, then morning — that was the first day.</p><p>On the second day God spoke again. He commanded a great dome to separate the water above from the water below, and he named it ‘Sky’. Look up at the sky after school today. That huge ceiling over your head was God’s idea. It is there because he said so.</p><p>On the third day God spoke again: ‘I command the water under the sky to come together in one place, so there will be dry ground.’ The waters rushed together and the land rose up out of them — hills, valleys, beaches and mountains. Every beach from Cape Reinga to Bluff started here. God named the dry ground ‘Land’ and the water ‘Ocean’. Then he commanded the earth to grow plants — fruit trees and grain and every kind of green, growing thing. Picture the very first tree pushing up out of the ground. And again the Bible says it: God looked at what he had done, and it was good.</p><p>Did you notice the pattern? God said… and it happened. God said… and it happened. Nobody helped him. There was nothing there to start with. God didn’t need tools, or a plan drawn on paper, or a million years of trying. He spoke, and creation obeyed.</p><p>That tells us something huge about God, and we haven’t even finished the first page. God is powerful. God is good. And everything that exists belongs to him — including you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Before anything at all… there was God. · 2. God said, “I command light to shine!” · 3. Day 2: God stretched out the sky. · 4. Day 3: Land! Sea! Plants! · 5. God said… and it happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click once per beat. Read the narration (shown here) at a steady pace — about 3 minutes. Imagined detail: ‘like a bird hovering over its nest’ is a common way of picturing the Hebrew word for ‘moving’; the beach line is a local touch, not in the text.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How does God make things?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Genesis 1, HOW does God make things?<br>A) He builds them with his hands &nbsp; B) He speaks — and it happens ✓ &nbsp; C) The angels help him &nbsp; D) He mixes things that were already there</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B. Ask students to prove it from the text: ‘God said, “I command light to shine!” And light started shining.’ Follow-up: ‘Can you or I make anything just by speaking?’ Follow-up 2: ‘So what does that tell us about God compared to us?’ If silent: re-read verse 3 slowly and ask them to spot the two things that happen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why the repeating?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–8:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Genesis keeps repeating: “God said… and that’s what happened.” Why do you think it says it over and over?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: so we remember it; to show God is powerful; nothing can say no to God; God keeps his word — what he says always happens. Follow-up: ‘If God’s words always happen, what does that mean for the promises he makes us?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Is there anything God can’t do?’ If silent: ‘When your coach or parent repeats something, why do they do it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen. Skip this one if you’re short on time — the role-play makes the same point.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Let there be…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Let there be…</b> (Genesis 1:1–13). The creation ‘characters’ (Light, Sky, Sea, Land) are imagined for drama. God’s words are quoted from Genesis 1 (CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story (a confident reader)<br><b>Voice of God</b> — God’s words from Genesis — read by the teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Light</b> — A small group (2–4) who burst up shining<br><b>Sky</b> — A small group who stretch high and wide<br><b>Sea</b> — A small group who wave and gather together<br><b>Land</b> — A small group who rise up out of the water<br><b>Everyone</b> — The rest of the class — sound effects and big lines</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: start crouched low and silent · Everyone: make a deep ocean roar when cued · Everyone: grow like seedlings on Day 3<br>Props: Optional: a torch for Light · Optional: blue fabric for Sea · Optional: green paper leaves<br>Cast quickly (use Pick volunteers). Groups can be 2–4 students each. Teacher reads Voice of God unless you have a confident reader. Keep it moving — about 4 minutes once cast.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Everyone crouches low, still and silent. The world is dark and empty.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The earth was empty — under a roaring ocean covered with darkness.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooooooosh… <i>(a deep, rolling ocean roar)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But the Spirit of God was moving over the water.</li><li class='dir'>Everyone goes completely silent…</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I command light to shine!</li><li><b>Light:</b> Here we are — SHINING! <i>(jump up, arms burst open)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooooh! <i>(shield your eyes)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God looked at the light and saw that it was good.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It was GOOD!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Evening came, then morning — that was the first day.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Light — what is it like being the very first thing God spoke into the dark? (20s)</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I command a dome to separate the water above it from the water below it.</li><li><b>Sky:</b> I’m the sky — big, wide and WAY up high! <i>(stretch arms high and wide)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God named it ‘Sky’. Evening came, then morning — that was the second day.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I command the water under the sky to come together in one place, so there will be dry ground.</li><li><b>Sea:</b> Splish! Splash! Everybody in together! <i>(wave arms and rush into one group)</i></li><li><b>Land:</b> And here I come — rising out of the water! <i>(rise slowly from a crouch)</i></li><li><b>Sea:</b> Oi, Land — nice hills!</li><li><b>Land:</b> Cheers, Sea. Nice waves.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God named the dry ground ‘Land’ and the water ‘Ocean’. Then God commanded the earth to grow all kinds of plants.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Grow… grow… GROW! <i>(rise from the floor like seedlings, arms opening like leaves)</i></li><li><b>Land:</b> Look at me now — I’m covered in green!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God looked at what he had done, and it was good.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It was GOOD!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Evening came, then morning — that was the third day.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Who did ALL the speaking in this scene? And who did all the obeying? (15s)</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God said… and it HAPPENED!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did you notice about God in that scene?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Listen for: he only had to speak once; nothing argued with him; he called everything good; he was in charge.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many tries did God need before something worked?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: None — one word, every time. Contrast with how WE make things (drafts, mistakes, rubbing out).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Light into darkness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t leave the world dark and empty — he spoke light into it. When do people today feel ‘dark and empty’ inside? What might God want to do about that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect: when sad, lonely, scared, when someone dies, when parents argue. Gently connect: the same God who spoke light into the dark still brings light — through his presence, his words, and people who care. Follow-up: ‘Where else in the Bible is Jesus called light?’ (John 8:12). Follow-up 2: ‘How could you be a bit of light for someone this week?’ If silent: share a short, safe example of your own.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is so powerful he made everything just by speaking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Wow, God — you made all of this, and it’s good!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On the way home today, spot one thing God made on Days 1–3 — light, sky, water, land or plants — and say ‘Wow, God’ in your head.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read the three cards as they reveal. Ask two students what they’ll look for.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: And it was good</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Day 1: light.  Day 2: sky.  Day 3: land, sea and plants.<br>And God looked at what he had done — and it was GOOD.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch slide: a quick visual recap before the verse peek.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Teacher says each chunk; class echoes it back. Do it twice — the second time in a whisper, then a shout.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Wow prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: you tell God what is amazing about HIM. You’re not asking for anything — it’s like cheering for God. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Get comfortable. Close your eyes, or look at the floor. Take one slow breath in… and out. God is here, and he is listening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Picture the very first light shining into the dark.<br>Prompts: God, you made light just by speaking — you are powerful! / God, you made the sky and the oceans — you are huge!<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God! You spoke — and the light shone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made a good world. Sometimes we don’t treat it — or each other — as good.<br>Prompts: Sorry for grumbling instead of noticing your good gifts. / Sorry for wasting or wrecking things you made.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I don’t notice your good gifts.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Picture Days 1 to 3: light, sky, sea, land, plants.<br>Prompts: Thank you for sunlight in the morning. / Thank you for the beach, the hills and the trees.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for light, and sky, and sea, and every growing thing.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help you see his world with new eyes this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice the things you made. / Please help me look after your world.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me notice your world this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish together. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model one silent sentence first by saying it aloud: ‘Wow, God, you made light just by talking!’ Reassure: short and simple is great. Advance each phase when the ring finishes.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Days 4–6 — sun, moon, stars, creatures, and people made to be like God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Made Like God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1:1–2:3; 2:15–25; Psalm 19:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Made Like God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wow — A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: telling God what is amazing about HIM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what God made on Days 4, 5 and 6.</li><li>We are learning what it means that people are made ‘to be like’ God.</li><li>I can explain one way people are different from the rest of creation.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that only people were made ‘like God’ — to know him, love, create and care for his world — and linking that to how we treat others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: mini-whiteboards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Made Like God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play, then go straight into the recap.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap: Days 1–3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God spoke… and things happened!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Show me with actions: Day 1? Day 2? Day 3?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Day 1 — light (burst hands open). Day 2 — sky (stretch arms up and wide). Day 3 — land, sea and plants (rise up, wave arms, grow like a seedling). Praise the best actions. Then: ‘Today the world is ready… but it’s empty. Let’s see what God fills it with.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 1:26–27, 31</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\">God said, “Now we will make humans, and they will be like us. We will let them rule the fish, the birds, and all other living creatures.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">So God created humans to be like himself; he made men and women.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">God looked at what he had done. All of it was very good!</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read with weight on ‘like us’ and ‘very good’. Point out: this is the only time God says ‘very good’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Days 4–6: God fills the world</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we watched God fill the dark with light, stretch out the sky, and lift the land out of the sea. Now the world is ready — but it’s empty. So God starts to fill it.</p><p>On the fourth day God said, ‘I command lights to appear in the sky.’ And there they were: the sun, blazing and bright, to rule the day, and the moon, cool and silver, to rule the night. And then, almost like a small detail, the Bible says: ‘He also made the stars.’ Scientists think there are more stars than grains of sand on all the beaches on earth — and Genesis mentions them in five words. That’s how big God is.</p><p>On the fifth day God said, ‘I command the ocean to be full of living creatures, and I command birds to fly above the earth.’ Suddenly the sea was splashing with life — tiny silver fish, octopus, dolphins, and what the Bible calls ‘giant sea monsters’. Think of whales bigger than a bus. The sky filled with wings too: kererū, hawks, sparrows, and albatross with wings wider than a car. God blessed them and told them to spread out everywhere.</p><p>On the sixth day God said, ‘I command the earth to give life to all kinds of tame animals, wild animals, and reptiles.’ Cows and lions, sheep and lizards, beetles and elephants. Each one designed. Each one different. And God looked at what he had done, and it was good.</p><p>But God wasn’t finished. Listen to how the words change. Up till now God has said, ‘I command…’ But now he says, ‘Now we will make humans, and they will be like us.’ Nothing else was made like that. Not the sun. Not the whales. Not the lions. Only people. So God created humans to be like himself; he made men and women.</p><p>What does it mean to be made like God? It doesn’t mean we look like God, or that we are God. It means we are made to reflect him — like a mirror catching sunlight. We can think and create, love and choose, and care for the world. And most of all, we are made to know God and talk with him. Your dog can’t pray. A dolphin can’t say thank you to its Maker. You can.</p><p>God gave people a job too: to rule over the fish, the birds and the animals — not to wreck them, but to look after them for him, like a ranger looks after a national park.</p><p>Then God looked at everything — the stars, the sea, the kererū, the lions, and the people. And this time the Bible doesn’t just say ‘good’. It says: ‘All of it was very good!’ That includes the person sitting next to you. And it includes you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Day 4: Sun, moon… and the stars. · 2. Day 5: The sea splashes with life. The sky fills with wings. · 3. Day 6: Animals of every kind! · 4. Then God did something different. · 5. All of it was VERY good!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click once per beat; about 3½ minutes of narration. Imagined/added details: the star comparison is a popular science comparison, and the NZ birds are a local touch — the text says ‘every kind of bird’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Spot the difference</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look closely at Genesis 1. What’s different about how God makes PEOPLE?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Everything else</th><th>People</th></tr><tr><td>“I command the earth to give life…”</td><td>“Now we will make humans…”</td></tr><tr><td>Made ‘all kinds’</td><td>Made “to be like himself”</td></tr><tr><td>God saw that it was good</td><td>“All of it was VERY good!”</td></tr><tr><td>Animals are looked after</td><td>People are given the job of looking after</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">People are the only part of creation made to be like God — made to know him and care for his world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal one row at a time. Before each reveal, ask: ‘What do you think goes on the People side?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which day?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On which day did God make people? Point to a corner of the room!<br>A) Day 3 &nbsp; B) Day 5 &nbsp; C) Day 6 ✓ &nbsp; D) Day 7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: Day 6, along with the land animals. Corners: front-left = A, front-right = B, back-left = C, back-right = D. Follow-up: ‘What else was made on Day 6?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why might it matter that people came LAST — once everything was ready?’ If silent: re-read Genesis 1:31.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Vote with your feet · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Made like God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made people “to be like himself.” What can people do that shows we’re made like God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: think, imagine and create (art, music, inventions); love and be kind; choose right from wrong; look after the world; talk with God (pray). Follow-up: ‘Which of these can animals NOT do?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What happens when people use these gifts badly?’ (a hint towards next week). If silent: ‘What did you make or create this week?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Even them?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If EVERY person is made to be like God — does that include people who are mean to us? Why does that matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yes — every person. It doesn’t make meanness okay, but it means no one is worthless, and no one is ‘rubbish’. Follow-up: ‘How might this change what we say about someone behind their back?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Does it change how you think about yourself on a bad day?’ If silent: ‘Is there anyone God didn’t make?’ Handle any personal stories gently; follow up privately if needed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made people — including me — to be like him and to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I’m not an accident. You made me on purpose.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when you see someone left out or put down — at school, at sport or online — remember they’re made like God, and say one kind thing to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask: ‘Where might you see someone put down this week?’ Get one or two real settings named.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Very good</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only people were made to be like God.<br>All of it was VERY good!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch slide. Ask: ‘What do you think Adam and Eve were feeling, looking at all that?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Each click adds a chunk. Class reads the growing verse together each time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Wow prayer (one sentence aloud)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Wow prayers can start with “God, you are…” and add one true thing about him. Today, anyone who wants to can say ONE sentence out loud. Short is perfect. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Breathe in slowly… and out. Today we’ll pray out loud, one sentence each — only if you want to. Silent is fine too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about the stars, the whales, and people made like God.<br>Prompts: God, you made the stars — you are so big! / God, you made people like you — you are kind and good!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you made the stars — and you made me. Wow!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes we treat people — or ourselves — like we don’t matter.<br>Prompts: Sorry for putting down someone you made. / Sorry for not liking how you made me.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I’ve treated someone like they don’t matter.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people and creatures he made.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you made me on purpose. / Thank you for animals — whales, kererū, dogs and cats!<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you made me on purpose.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help us see people the way he does.<br>Prompts: Please help me treat everyone as someone you made. / Please help anyone who feels like they don’t matter.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me treat everyone as someone you made.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you for praying out loud! Let’s finish together: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Go first with a one-sentence example. Let 3–5 volunteers pray per phase; don’t force anyone.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: we zoom into the Garden of Eden — Adam names the animals, and God says something is ‘not good’.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Garden":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1:1–2:3; 2:15–25; Psalm 19:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Garden</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wow — A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: telling God what is amazing about HIM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the Garden of Eden and the work God gave Adam.</li><li>We are learning that God made people for friendship — with him and with each other.</li><li>I can explain why God said ‘it isn’t good for the man to live alone’.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Adam needed a partner ‘like him’, and linking God’s design for friendship to including others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: animal masks or ears for the role-play</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Garden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: All by yourself</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine the most amazing place you’ve ever been — the beach, a theme park, the snow…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now imagine being there completely by yourself. What would be missing?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 3–4 answers: someone to share it with, someone to talk to, someone to say ‘Look at that!’ to. Land it: ‘Today, in the most perfect garden ever, God says something is NOT good. Let’s find out what.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 2:15, 18</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\">The LORD God put the man in the Garden of Eden to take care of it and to look after it.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">The LORD God said, “It isn’t good for the man to live alone. I will make a suitable partner for him.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Point out: after all the ‘good’ and ‘very good’ of Genesis 1, this is the first ‘not good’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Zoom in on Eden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Genesis 1 gave us the big picture — like a drone shot of the whole world being made. Genesis 2 zooms right in, to one man in one garden.</p><p>God formed the first man from the dust of the ground and breathed life into him. Then the LORD God put the man in the Garden of Eden to take care of it and to look after it. Picture it: rivers running through, trees heavy with fruit, flowers everywhere, birdsong all day long. And the man — Adam — had a job. Work was part of God’s good plan from the very beginning. Not boring work: gardening the most beautiful place that ever existed.</p><p>But then, for the first time in the whole Bible, God says something is NOT good. After all those ‘good’s and ‘very good’s, God says: ‘It isn’t good for the man to live alone. I will make a suitable partner for him.’</p><p>So the LORD brought the animals and the birds to the man, to see what names he would give each of them. Imagine the parade! Something huge and grey with a nose like a hose. Something with a shaggy mane and a massive roar. Something small and round that couldn’t fly. And Adam named them — the tame animals, the birds and the wild animals. That’s how they got their names. God let Adam be creative, just like God is.</p><p>But by the end of the parade there was a problem. None of these was the right kind of partner for the man. The lion couldn’t talk with him. The elephant couldn’t share a joke. Adam was surrounded by life — and still alone.</p><p>So the LORD God made the man fall into a deep sleep. He took one of the man’s ribs, closed up his side, and made a woman. Then God brought her to the man.</p><p>And Adam said the very first human words the Bible records — and they were a shout of joy: ‘Here is someone like me! She is part of my body, my own flesh and bones.’</p><p>God didn’t make us to be alone. He made us for friendship — with each other, and most of all with him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Genesis 2 zooms in on one garden. · 2. For the first time, God says: “It isn’t good…” · 3. The great animal parade! · 4. But none of them was like him. · 5. “Here is someone like me!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click once per beat; about 3 minutes. Imagined detail: the descriptions of the animals in the parade. Genesis 2:7 (dust and breath) is brought in briefly for context.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not good</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: For the first time in the Bible, God said something was NOT good. It was…<br><i>Answer: …that the man was alone. (Genesis 2:18)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: ‘It isn’t good for the man to live alone.’ Follow-up: ‘But Adam had God, and a perfect garden, and every animal — so what was missing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did God do about it?’ If silent: re-read Genesis 2:18 from the earlier slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Garden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Garden</b> (Genesis 2:15–23). The animals’ lines (and a very Kiwi bird) are imagined for fun. Adam’s words when he meets the woman, and God’s words, are quoted from Genesis 2 (CEV). She is named Eve later, in Genesis 3:20.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Voice of God</b> — God’s words — teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Adam</b> — The first man — curious, delighted, then lonely<br><b>Lion</b> — Big, proud and dramatic<br><b>Elephant</b> — Slow, wise, makes trumpet noises<br><b>Kiwi</b> — Small, round, can’t fly — very fast<br><b>Eve</b> — The first woman — appears near the end<br><b>Everyone</b> — The rest of the class — animal noises and big lines</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: animal noises in the parade · Everyone: a soft ‘shhh… zzzz’ when Adam falls asleep<br>Props: Optional: animal ears or masks · Optional: a leafy branch for the garden<br>Use Pick volunteers. Lion, Elephant and Kiwi love to ham it up — let them. Keep the Eve moment warm and simple.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The garden is quiet. Adam stands in the middle, looking around in wonder.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The LORD God put the man in the Garden of Eden to take care of it and to look after it.</li><li><b>Adam:</b> Rivers… fruit trees… flowers everywhere… This place is AMAZING! <i>(spin slowly, arms out)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> It isn’t good for the man to live alone. I will make a suitable partner for him.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So the LORD brought the animals and birds to the man, to see what names he would give them.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> ROAR! Moo! Tweet tweet! Baaaa! <i>(animal noises)</i></li><li><b>Lion:</b> ROOOAAARRR! <i>(prowl in, shake your mane)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> Whoa! Big mane, big voice… I’ll call you… LION!</li><li><b>Lion:</b> Lion. I like it. Sounds strong.</li><li><b>Elephant:</b> Pfffrrrooo! <i>(swing your arm like a trunk)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> Your nose is… a hose? I’ll call you ELEPHANT.</li><li><b>Elephant:</b> Couldn’t have picked it better myself.</li><li><b>Kiwi:</b> Peep! I can’t fly — but I’m VERY fast! <i>(zoom around with tiny flapping wings)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> Small, round, long beak, no tail… You’re a KIWI!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Kiwi! Kiwi! Kiwi!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Adam named the tame animals and the birds and the wild animals. That’s how they got their names.</li><li><b>Adam:</b> So many animals… but none of them are like me. <i>(sit down, chin in hands)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Adam has a perfect garden and every animal in the world. Why is he still lonely? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> None of these was the right kind of partner for the man.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So the LORD God made the man fall into a deep sleep.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhhh… zzzzzz… <i>(softly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God took one of the man’s ribs and made a woman. Then the LORD God brought her to the man.</li><li><b>Eve:</b> Hello? <i>(walk in slowly, looking around)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> Here is someone like me! She is part of my body, my own flesh and bones! <i>(wake up and leap to your feet)</i></li><li><b>Eve:</b> Someone like ME!</li><li><b>Lion:</b> Finally — someone he can actually talk to.</li><li><b>Elephant:</b> Pfffrrrooo! <i>(a happy trumpet)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God didn’t make people to be alone. He made them for friendship — with each other, and with him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It was VERY good!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God let Adam choose the animals’ names himself?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God shares his creativity with us; he trusts people with real responsibility; Adam is made like God — a maker and namer.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Adam need that the animals couldn’t give him?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Someone like him — to talk with, share with, work with. Friendship and family.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Work in paradise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gave Adam a job — “take care of it and look after it” — in a PERFECT garden, before anything went wrong. What does that tell us about work, like chores, schoolwork or jobs?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: work is part of God’s good plan, not a punishment; God made us to make and care for things; doing work well can be a way of honouring God. Follow-up: ‘What job has God given YOU to take care of?’ (pets, siblings, classroom, bedroom!) Follow-up 2: ‘How would you do it differently if you were doing it for God?’ If silent: ‘Is there any work you actually enjoy?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Wasn’t God enough?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Adam had God himself as a friend. So why did he still need a human friend? Wasn’t God enough?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Big idea: God himself said ‘it isn’t good’ — he DESIGNED us to need both him and other people. Needing friends isn’t weakness or a lack of faith; it’s how God made us. Follow-up: ‘How does God often care for us THROUGH other people?’ Follow-up 2: ‘So what could you be for someone this week?’ If silent: ‘Who has God used to look after you?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made us for friendship — with him and with each other.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for the people you’ve put in my life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At lunch today or tomorrow, look for someone on their own and invite them in — to your game, your table or your conversation.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask: ‘Where in our school do people end up on their own?’ Make it concrete.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Someone like me</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Here is someone like me!”<br>God made us for friendship.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn one action per chunk, then say the whole verse with actions twice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Wow prayer (open hands)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You can pray with your body too. Holding your hands open says, “God, everything I have comes from you.” Your body can help your heart pray. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit up tall. Hold your hands open on your lap, palms up. Breathe in… and out. Everything good in the garden came from God — and everything good in our lives does too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about the God who made a perfect garden — and made people for friendship.<br>Prompts: God, you are the friend-maker! / God, you gave Adam good work and a beautiful home — you are generous!<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you made us for friendship!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes we leave people out, or aren’t a good friend.<br>Prompts: Sorry for leaving someone out. / Sorry for the times I wasn’t a good friend this week.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I’ve left people out.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With open hands, picture the people God has given you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my whānau and friends. / Thank you that I’m never truly alone, because you are with me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for my family and my friends.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help you include others — and to be close to anyone who’s lonely.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice who is lonely at school. / Please be close to anyone who feels alone today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me notice who is lonely, and be their friend.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, like you’re holding on to what God gave you. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model the open-hands posture. It’s optional — some students may prefer eyes closed, hands still.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Day 7 — God rests — and a shepherd’s song about the sky. Plus this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Rest and Wonder":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 1:1–2:3; 2:15–25; Psalm 19:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rest and Wonder</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wow — A ‘Wow’ prayer is praise: telling God what is amazing about HIM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why God rested on the seventh day.</li><li>We are learning how creation ‘tells’ us about God (Psalm 19).</li><li>I can retell the seven days in order and say the Big Idea of the week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can put the seven days in order, recite Genesis 1:1, and explain the Big Idea in their own words.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards (print from the challenge slide)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Rest and Wonder</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Seven days in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quick! Can you put the seven days in order?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Light → Sky → Land, sea &amp; plants → Sun, moon &amp; stars → Fish &amp; birds → Animals &amp; people → God rests</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out which comes next; click to reveal each day in order. Option: give 7 students a card each and have them line up first. Celebrate — then ask: ‘Which day are you most amazed by?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 2:2–3 · Psalm 19:1</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 2:2</b> By the seventh day God had finished his work, and so he rested.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 2:3</b> God blessed the seventh day and made it special, because on that day he rested from his work.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 19:1</b> The heavens keep telling the wonders of God, and the skies declare what he has done.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Two different books! Point out that Psalm 19 was written many years later by a shepherd looking at the same sky.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Day 7 and a shepherd’s song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After six days of speaking light and sky and sea and stars and creatures and people into being, something unusual happens on the seventh day. God rests.</p><p>Now — was God tired? Did he need a nap? No! God never gets tired. The Hebrew word for ‘rested’ means ‘stopped’. God stopped because his work was finished. Everything was complete. Nothing was missing. And God blessed the seventh day and made it special — a day to stop, enjoy what he had made, and enjoy him. He was showing people a rhythm: work, then rest. Make, then enjoy. And he gave that rhythm to us as a gift.</p><p>Hundreds of years later, a shepherd named David — yes, the David who would one day face Goliath — spent his nights out on the hills looking after his sheep. No street lights. No screens. Just a sky absolutely packed with stars. And David wrote a song about it. You can read it in Psalm 19: ‘The heavens keep telling the wonders of God, and the skies declare what he has done.’</p><p>David noticed something: the sky is preaching! It doesn’t speak a word, but every sunrise says, ‘Look how great God is.’ Every starry night says, ‘Look how big God is.’ The sky has been telling that message every single day since Genesis 1 — and it will be telling it over Aotearoa again tonight.</p><p>So this week’s challenge is simple: listen to what the sky — and everything else God made — is saying.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Day 7: God rested. · 2. God made the seventh day special. · 3. Years later, a shepherd looked up… · 4. “The heavens keep telling the wonders of God.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Imagined detail: David on the hills at night is based on his life as a shepherd (1 Samuel 16–17); the psalm itself doesn’t say where he wrote it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why rest?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did God rest on the seventh day?<br>A) He was worn out &nbsp; B) His work was finished, and he made the day special ✓ &nbsp; C) He ran out of ideas &nbsp; D) Someone told him to</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B — Genesis 2:2–3 says he had finished his work, and he blessed the day and made it special. Follow-up: ‘Can God get tired?’ (No — Isaiah 40:28.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why might God want US to have a rhythm of rest?’ If silent: re-read Genesis 2:2.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What is the sky saying?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Psalm 19 says the sky “keeps telling the wonders of God” — without using any words. What is a sunrise, or a starry night, ‘saying’ about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is big; God is powerful; God is beautiful and creative; God is faithful — the sun comes up every day; God is bigger than my problems. Follow-up: ‘What else in creation “tells” us about God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think so many people never notice?’ If silent: show the optional starry scene or ask students to picture last night’s sky.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God made everything by his word — he spoke, and it happened.</li><li>Everything God made was good — and people were made to be like him, to know him.</li><li>God rested and made a day special — he gives us rest, rhythm and wonder.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made everything by his word, it was good, and he made people to know him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the three points, then the Big Idea stamps in. Have the class read the Big Idea together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is rest a waste?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Resting is a waste of time.” Move to the side of the room that matches what you think.<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let both sides explain. Draw out: God rested (so it can’t be bad!); rest isn’t laziness — it’s stopping to enjoy God and his gifts; too much screen-time isn’t always real rest. Follow-up: ‘What does REAL rest look like for you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could you use rest to notice God?’ If silent: ‘How do you feel after a whole weekend of no sleep?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Notice it — then thank him. Every day until next Monday, notice THREE things God made, and say thank you to him for each one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The sky keeps telling the wonders of God. This week, be a listener!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Print the take-home cards (button on the slide). Next Monday’s check-in will ask what students noticed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” — Genesis 1:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Each click makes more words disappear. Can the class still say it when every word is gone?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The heavens are telling</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">They don’t speak a word…<br>…yet their message reaches all the earth. (Psalm 19:3–4)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch slide. Psalm 19:3–4 (CEV): ‘They don’t speak a word, and there is never the sound of a voice. Yet their message reaches all the earth, and it travels around the world.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying a psalm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You can pray the Bible’s own words back to God! The psalms are prayers and songs — when you borrow their words, you’re praying like David did. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Get comfortable and still. Today we’re going to pray Psalm 19 together. I’ll say a line, and you echo it back to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me — say it TO God.<br>Prompts: “The heavens keep telling the wonders of God…” / “…and the skies declare what he has done.”<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, your sky tells me you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, the heavens keep telling your wonders, and the skies declare what you have done!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes we rush past God’s wonders without noticing.<br>Prompts: Sorry for rushing past your world without noticing. / Sorry for calling your world ‘boring’.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I don’t notice your wonders.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one thing he made that you love.<br>Prompts: Thank you for sunrises and starry nights. / Thank you for rest and weekends.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the sky, the stars, and rest.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice three things you made every day. / Please help me rest well and enjoy you this weekend.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me notice and thank you this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say our memory verse as our final prayer: “In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read each Wow line with a short pause for the class to echo. Celebrate the end of Week 1!</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on the challenge. Week 2 — Broken Friendship (Genesis 3).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2: Broken Friendship":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Seeker</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sorry — A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what we’ve done wrong — and trusting him to forgive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Serpent’s Question</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how the snake twisted God’s words in Genesis 3.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Serpent’s Question → Tricked! → Genesis 3:1–6 → One rule, one question → How did the snake start? → Why a question? → The Serpent’s Question → Why one rule? → Head · Heart · Hands → Did God really say…? → Memory verse peek → Sorry prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Where Are You?</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what sin is and how it broke Adam and Eve’s friendship with God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Where Are You? → Recap: the question → Genesis 3:8–10 → Hiding from God → Before and after → What did they do? → Why ask? → Ways we hide → Head · Heart · Hands → Where are you? → Build the verse → Write a sorry</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>It Wasn’t Me!</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Adam and Eve answered God when he asked what they had done.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>It Wasn’t Me! → Who did this? → Genesis 3:11–13 → The blame chain → The blame chain → God Walks in the Garden → What could they say? → Why questions? → Head · Heart · Hands → Nobody said it → Verse with actions → Sorry prayer (heads bowed)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Promise in the Dark</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about God’s promise of a rescuer in Genesis 3:15.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Promise in the Dark → True or false? → Genesis 3:15, 21 · 1 John 1:9 → The first promise → What did God make? → Care after sin → This week’s Big Idea → Like it never happened? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Not the end → Sorry and forgiven</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Serpent’s Question":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Serpent’s Question</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sorry — A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what we’ve done wrong — and trusting him to forgive.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the snake twisted God’s words in Genesis 3.</li><li>We are learning what it means to trust what God says.</li><li>I can explain how temptation often starts with a question that makes God sound bad.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students noticing how the snake changed God’s words, and naming a real-life ‘Did God really say…?’ moment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a piece of fruit for the role-play</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Serpent’s Question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Say: ‘Last week everything was very good. This week… something goes wrong.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Tricked!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been tricked? A prank, a fake ad, a friend who said “Trust me…”</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the trick work — what did it make you believe?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how God made everything — and it was very good. Your challenge: notice three things God made each day and thank him. What did you notice?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Start with the check-in: take 3–4 answers about what they noticed and thanked God for (celebrate them!). Then the hook: take a few trick stories. Land it: ‘Tricks usually work by making something false sound true. Today we meet the first trickster in the Bible — and he starts with a question.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 3:1–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:1</b> The snake was sneakier than any of the other wild animals that the LORD God had made. One day it came to the woman and asked, “Did God tell you not to eat fruit from any tree in the garden?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:2–3</b> The woman answered, “God said we could eat fruit from any tree in the garden, except the one in the middle.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:4</b> “No, you won’t!” the snake replied.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:6</b> The woman stared at the fruit. It looked beautiful and tasty… and she ate some of the fruit. Her husband was there with her, so she gave some to him, and he ate it too.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 3 is shortened on screen; verse 6 is abridged (‘…’). Read slowly — let the snake’s ‘No, you won’t!’ land.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: One rule, one question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Last week we ended in a perfect garden. Everything God made was very good. Adam and Eve had food, work, each other — and best of all, they had God. They could walk and talk with him.</p><p>God had given them just one rule. ‘You may eat fruit from any tree in the garden,’ he said, ‘except the one in the middle.’ One tree. That’s all. Everything else was a yes.</p><p>Then, one day, a snake came to Eve. The Bible says it was sneakier than any other animal God had made. And it didn’t start with a lie. It started with a question: ‘Did God tell you not to eat fruit from any tree in the garden?’</p><p>Listen to how sneaky that is. God had said yes to every tree but one. The snake made it sound like God had said no to all of them. It twisted God’s words to make God sound mean.</p><p>Eve answered, ‘God said we could eat from any tree except the one in the middle. If we eat from that one, we will die.’</p><p>Then the snake said the boldest thing: ‘No, you won’t!’ It called God a liar. It told her God was keeping something good from her — that if she ate, she would be wise, just like God.</p><p>Eve looked at the fruit. It looked beautiful. It looked tasty. And now she wondered: maybe God wasn’t telling the whole truth. Maybe she knew better.</p><p>She reached out. She took the fruit. She ate it. Adam was right there with her — he didn’t stop her, and he didn’t speak up for God. She gave some to him, and he ate it too.</p><p>For the first time in the whole world, people chose their own way instead of God’s way. The Bible has a word for that: sin. And as soon as they had eaten, they knew something had gone terribly wrong.</p><p>The snake is still asking the same kind of question today: ‘Did God really say…?’ ‘Would God really mind?’ ‘Just this once.’ Learning to spot that voice is one of the most important things you will ever learn.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. God gave Adam and Eve one rule. · 2. Then a sneaky voice asked a question. · 3. “No, you won’t!” · 4. She stared at the fruit… · 5. For the first time, someone said no to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Keep the tone serious but not scary. The Bible doesn’t describe what the snake looked like beyond ‘sneaky’; Revelation 12:9 later links it with the devil — mention only if asked.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How did the snake start?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the snake start its trick?<br>A) It bit Eve &nbsp; B) It asked a question that twisted God’s words ✓ &nbsp; C) It offered her a present &nbsp; D) It climbed the tree and shook it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B — ‘Did God tell you not to eat fruit from any tree in the garden?’ Follow-up: ‘What had God actually said?’ (Every tree except one.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why is a question sneakier than an order?’ If silent: re-read Genesis 3:1 from the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why a question?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the snake started with a question instead of just saying, “Eat the fruit”?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: a question makes you doubt; it gets you thinking God might be mean; it doesn’t look like a trick; it lets you ‘decide for yourself’. Follow-up: ‘What did the question make God sound like?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where do you hear questions like that today?’ If silent: ‘If I asked, “Did your mum REALLY say no screens?” — what am I trying to do?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Serpent’s Question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Serpent’s Question</b> (Genesis 3:1–7). The snake’s and Eve’s words are kept close to Genesis 3 (CEV); Adam’s thoughts and the actions are imagined. Keep the snake sneaky, not scary.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Serpent</b> — Sneaky, smooth, twists every word<br><b>Eve</b> — Curious, then unsure<br><b>Adam</b> — Right there — but silent until the end<br><b>Everyone</b> — The rest of the class — hisses and gasps</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: a long, quiet hiss whenever the snake speaks · Everyone: gasp when the fruit is eaten<br>Props: Optional: a piece of fruit<br>Use Pick volunteers. The snake should be smooth and sly — let the class enjoy the hissing, but keep the ending quiet and serious.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Eve is walking in the garden. Adam is nearby. The tree in the middle is heavy with fruit.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The snake was sneakier than any of the other wild animals that the LORD God had made.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Sssssssss… <i>(a long, quiet hiss)</i></li><li><b>Serpent:</b> Did God tell you not to eat fruit from any tree in the garden? <i>(slide in slowly, smooth voice)</i></li><li><b>Eve:</b> God said we could eat fruit from any tree in the garden — except the one in the middle.</li><li><b>Eve:</b> He told us not to eat from that tree or even touch it. If we do, we will die.</li><li><b>Serpent:</b> No, you won’t!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Sssssssss…</li><li><b>Serpent:</b> God knows that when you eat it, you’ll know right from wrong — just like God does.</li><li><b>Eve:</b> Just like God…? <i>(look at the fruit)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The snake has just changed God’s words. What did God REALLY say? And what should Eve say now? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The woman stared at the fruit. It looked beautiful and tasty.</li><li><b>Eve:</b> Maybe God is keeping something good from us… <i>(reach out slowly)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> … <i>(stand there, saying nothing)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> She ate some of the fruit. Her husband was there with her, so she gave some to him, and he ate it too.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Gasp! <i>(hands over mouths)</i></li><li><b>Serpent:</b> Sssss… my work here is done. <i>(slide away)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At once they saw what they had done.</li><li><b>Adam:</b> What have we done? Why didn’t I say something? <i>(hands on head)</i></li><li><b>Eve:</b> Everything feels… different. <i>(wrap arms around yourself)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They sewed fig leaves together to cover themselves. For the first time, they felt ashamed.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Everything has changed. <i>(softly)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What lie did the snake want Eve to believe about God?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: That God was lying, mean, and keeping good things from her — that she knew better than God.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Adam was standing right there. What could he have done?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Spoken up for God; reminded Eve what God said; walked away. Silence is a choice too.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why one rule?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gave Adam and Eve a whole garden and only ONE rule. Why do you think God gave them a rule at all?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: a rule gave them a real choice to trust and love God; God knew what was good for them; love that isn’t chosen isn’t really love. Follow-up: ‘Are rules always the opposite of freedom?’ (Road rules, pool rules.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does the one rule show about how generous God was?’ If silent: ‘Why does the pool have a “no running” rule?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s words are true and good — even when something else sounds more exciting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me trust what you say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when a voice says “Just this once — nobody will know,” stop and ask: what does God say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask for one real example of a ‘just this once’ moment kids face.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Did God really say…?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Did God tell you…?”<br>Temptation often starts by making God sound bad.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce this week’s verse: teacher says each chunk, class echoes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Sorry prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what you’ve done wrong. He already knows — and he isn’t trying to shame you. Saying sorry opens the door back to him. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s be still. Close your eyes or look down. Breathe in… and out. This is just between you and God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gave Adam and Eve everything good. Think about how generous he is.<br>Prompts: God, you are so generous — you give us more yeses than nos. / God, your words are always true.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — your words are always true!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think quietly: was there a time this week you chose your way instead of God’s way?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I listened to ‘just this once’. / Sorry for not trusting what you say.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I chose my way instead of yours.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God doesn’t give up on us. Thank him for that.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you listen when I say sorry. / Thank you for your good rules that protect me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you listen when I say sorry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God for help to spot the sneaky voice this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice when something is twisting your words. / Please give me courage to say no.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me trust you and say no to wrong things.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish together. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep the Sorry phase gentle. Say: ‘You don’t have to tell anyone else — this is between you and God.’</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Adam and Eve hide — and God comes looking with a question of his own: “Where are you?”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Where Are You?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Where Are You?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sorry — A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what we’ve done wrong — and trusting him to forgive.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what sin is and how it broke Adam and Eve’s friendship with God.</li><li>We are learning that God came looking for Adam and Eve, even after they disobeyed.</li><li>I can explain why God asked “Where are you?” when he already knew.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students describing sin as choosing my way over God’s way, and noticing that God came looking rather than leaving them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: mini-whiteboards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Where Are You?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap: the question</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday a sneaky snake asked a question…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who can remember the question? And what did God really say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Did God tell you not to eat fruit from any tree in the garden?’ God really said: every tree except the one in the middle. Then ask: ‘What happened when they ate it?’ — they saw what they had done and covered themselves with fig leaves. ‘Today, someone comes walking in the garden.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 3:8–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\">Late in the afternoon, when the breeze began to blow, the man and woman heard the LORD God walking in the garden. So they hid behind some trees.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">The LORD God called out to the man and asked, “Where are you?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\">The man answered, “I was naked, and when I heard you walking through the garden, I was frightened and hid!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pause after ‘Where are you?’ — ask the class to whisper it the way they think God said it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Hiding from God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday, Adam and Eve ate the fruit God had told them not to eat. And straight away, something changed inside them. The Bible says they saw what they had done. Before, they had felt completely safe and unashamed. Now they felt exposed. So they grabbed fig leaves, sewed them together, and tried to cover themselves.</p><p>Then, late in the afternoon, as the cool breeze began to blow, they heard a sound they knew well. Footsteps. The LORD God was walking in the garden. Before, that sound would have made them run to meet him. Their best friend was coming! But now? Now they ran the other way. They hid behind the trees.</p><p>That’s what sin does. Sin isn’t just breaking a rule, like forgetting to put your name on your work. Sin is choosing my way instead of God’s way. It’s saying, ‘God, I don’t trust you — I know better.’ And when we do that, it breaks something. It breaks our friendship with God. Suddenly we don’t want to be near him. We want to hide.</p><p>Think about the very first time you hid after doing something wrong. Maybe you were tiny, and you broke something, and you pushed the pieces under the couch. Maybe you went very quiet at dinner. Have you ever done something wrong and then avoided someone? Stayed in your room? Changed the subject? Pretended you were busy? That’s hiding. People have been doing it ever since this day in the garden. And here’s the strange thing: hiding never actually makes us feel better. The worried feeling just sits there, getting heavier. Adam and Eve were hiding from the one person who could actually help them.</p><p>But here’s the amazing part. God didn’t say, ‘Fine, if they want to hide, let them.’ He didn’t stomp off. He called out: ‘Where are you?’</p><p>Now — did God know where they were? Of course he did. God knows everything. He knew which tree they were behind. He knew what they had done. So why ask?</p><p>Because it was an invitation. ‘Where are you?’ means ‘Come out. Come and talk to me.’ It is the question of a God who wants his friends back.</p><p>Adam answered, ‘I heard you, and I was frightened, so I hid.’ Fear and hiding — that’s what sin brings.</p><p>But God’s first move after the very first sin was not to walk away. It was to come looking. And God is still the same. When we hide, he still comes looking for us, calling, ‘Where are you? Come back to me.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Something inside them had broken. · 2. Footsteps in the evening garden… · 3. What is sin? · 4. “Where are you?” · 5. God comes looking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Handle ‘naked’ simply: ‘they felt ashamed and exposed’. The fig leaves come from Genesis 3:7.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Before and after</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare Genesis 2 with Genesis 3. What did sin change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Before (Genesis 2)</th><th>After (Genesis 3)</th></tr><tr><td>Walked and talked with God</td><td>Hid from God behind the trees</td></tr><tr><td>Felt no shame</td><td>Covered themselves with fig leaves</td></tr><tr><td>Trusted God</td><td>“I was frightened”</td></tr><tr><td>“Someone like me!” — close to each other</td><td>Soon blaming each other (tomorrow!)</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sin doesn’t just break a rule — it breaks a friendship.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal one row at a time. Ask before each: ‘What changed?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did they do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Adam and Eve do when they heard God walking in the garden?<br>A) Ran to meet him &nbsp; B) Hid behind the trees ✓ &nbsp; C) Climbed the tree &nbsp; D) Called out to him</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B — ‘So they hid behind some trees’ (3:8). Follow-up: ‘What did they use to cover themselves?’ (fig leaves). Follow-up 2: ‘Why would you hide from your best friend?’ If silent: re-read the first line of the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why ask?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God knew exactly where Adam and Eve were. So why do you think he asked, “Where are you?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to invite them out; to give them a chance to be honest; because he wanted to talk, not just punish; because he still loved them. Follow-up: ‘How might God have sounded — angry or sad or kind?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this question show about what God is like?’ If silent: ‘When a parent asks “Is there something you want to tell me?” do they already know?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Ways we hide</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some ways kids ‘hide’ — from God or from people — after doing something wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect: lying, staying quiet, avoiding people, blaming, deleting messages, acting extra good, pretending it didn’t happen. Follow-up: ‘Does hiding make us feel better or worse?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would “coming out from behind the tree” look like?’ (owning up). If silent: share a light, safe example of your own (e.g. hiding a broken mug).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God comes looking for us, even when we hide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I don’t need to hide from you. Here I am.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you’ve been hiding something this week, come out from behind the tree: tell God tonight before you sleep — or tell a trusted adult.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be sensitive. If a student discloses something serious, follow it up privately and through your school’s processes.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Where are you?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Where are you?”<br>Not ‘Where are you hiding?’ — but ‘Come back to me.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch slide: ask, ‘How would you feel hearing God call your name?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Each click adds a chunk. Read the growing verse together each time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a sorry</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You can write a prayer. Writing ‘sorry’ to God can help you say what’s hard to say out loud. Nobody else will read it — then you can scrunch it up, because God has heard and forgives. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a scrap of paper or your whiteboard. We’ll pray by writing. Nobody will see what you write.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one word about what God is like from today’s story.<br>Prompts: Patient / Searching / Kind<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you came looking for Adam and Eve — you are kind!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a short sorry to God — about anything you’ve hidden or done wrong.<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding from you. / Sorry for…<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I’ve hidden from you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now scrunch your paper up tight. God has heard you. Thank him.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you forgive. / Thank you that you come looking for me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you come looking for me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help you come out of hiding this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me be honest. / Please help anyone who feels far from you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me be honest with you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put your scrunched paper in the bin on the way out — it’s gone. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have a bin near the door. Don’t read any papers.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God talks with Adam and Eve — and everyone starts blaming someone else.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: It Wasn’t Me!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">It Wasn’t Me!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sorry — A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what we’ve done wrong — and trusting him to forgive.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Adam and Eve answered God when he asked what they had done.</li><li>We are learning why blaming others makes things worse.</li><li>I can say what an honest ‘sorry’ sounds like.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students identifying the ‘blame chain’ in Genesis 3:11–13 and describing an honest response.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: It Wasn’t Me!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Who did this?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Someone asks, “Who did this?” — and before you can think, your mouth says…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why is it SO easy to blame someone else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them say it: ‘It wasn’t me!’ Take answers: fear of trouble, embarrassment, wanting people to like us. Land it: ‘Blaming is one of the oldest habits in the world. Today we see where it started.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 3:11–13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:11</b> God asked, “Did you eat any fruit from that tree in the middle of the garden?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:12</b> “It was the woman you put here with me,” the man said. “She gave me some of the fruit, and I ate it.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:13</b> “The snake tricked me,” she answered, “and I ate some of that fruit.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 11 is shortened on screen to God’s second question.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The blame chain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God called out, ‘Where are you?’ and Adam answered from behind the trees: ‘I was frightened, so I hid.’</p><p>So now Adam and Eve came out and stood in front of God. Imagine how that felt. Heads down. Hearts thumping. Fig leaves rustling.</p><p>God asked a very simple question: ‘Did you eat any fruit from that tree in the middle of the garden?’ It was a yes-or-no question. All Adam had to say was, ‘Yes. I did. I’m sorry.’</p><p>But that’s not what came out. Adam said, ‘It was the woman you put here with me. She gave me some of the fruit, and I ate it.’</p><p>Did you catch that? Adam blamed Eve. And then he even blamed God: ‘the woman YOU put here.’ As if it was God’s fault for giving him a wife! Only a few days earlier he had been shouting for joy: ‘Here is someone like me!’ Now he was pointing his finger at her.</p><p>So God turned to Eve: ‘What have you done?’ And Eve said, ‘The snake tricked me, and I ate some of that fruit.’ She blamed the snake.</p><p>The man blamed the woman. The woman blamed the snake. Nobody said the two most powerful words in the world: ‘It was me.’</p><p>Sin had broken their friendship with God, and now it was breaking their friendship with each other. That’s what sin does — it spreads, like a crack running across a window. One wrong choice leads to a lie, the lie leads to blaming, the blaming leads to an argument. Have you ever watched that happen on the playground? Someone gets hurt, and in thirty seconds everyone is pointing at everyone else, and nobody is listening. That is exactly what happened in the garden.</p><p>Then God told them sad news. The snake would crawl in the dust. Having children would be painful. Work would become hard, sweaty and frustrating. And one day, people would die. The world was no longer the way God had made it.</p><p>But listen carefully: God didn’t walk away from them, even then. He was still talking with them. And tomorrow we’ll hear about something hidden in God’s words — a promise of hope.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Adam and Eve came out from the trees. · 2. “Did you eat from that tree?” · 3. “It was the woman YOU put here!” · 4. “The snake tricked me!” · 5. Sin brings sadness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Keep the consequences brief and gentle (Genesis 3:14–19). Don’t dwell on death; say ‘one day people would die’ and move to hope.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The blame chain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: Adam blamed ______, and Eve blamed ______.<br><i>Answer: Adam blamed the woman (and God, who put her there). Eve blamed the snake.</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer from Genesis 3:12–13. Follow-up: ‘Who did nobody blame?’ (Themselves!) Follow-up 2: ‘What two words did nobody say?’ (‘It was me.’) If silent: point back to the scripture slide lines.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: God Walks in the Garden</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>God Walks in the Garden</b> (Genesis 3:8–13). God’s questions and Adam and Eve’s answers are from Genesis 3 (CEV); the actions and the snake’s final line are imagined. God’s voice should sound sad and kind, not shouty.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a calm, strong reader — gentle, not angry<br><b>Adam</b> — Afraid, then quick to blame<br><b>Eve</b> — Ashamed, also blames<br><b>Serpent</b> — Tries to slither away unnoticed<br><b>Everyone</b> — The breeze, and the pointing fingers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: the evening breeze — a soft ‘whoooosh’ · Everyone: point at someone else on every ‘blame’ line<br>Props: <br>Adam’s ‘I’m sorry’ near the end is imagined — point that out: ‘The Bible doesn’t record it. What do you think would have happened if he had said it first?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Narrator:</b> Late in the afternoon, when the breeze began to blow…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooooosh… <i>(a soft evening breeze)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> …the man and woman heard the LORD God walking in the garden. So they hid behind some trees.</li><li><b>Adam:</b> Shh! Get down! <i>(crouch behind ‘trees’)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Where are you?</li><li><b>Eve:</b> He’s calling us. <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> I heard you walking through the garden. I was frightened and hid! <i>(step out slowly)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Did you eat any fruit from that tree in the middle of the garden?</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! This is a yes-or-no question. What SHOULD Adam say right now? (20s)</li><li><b>Adam:</b> It was the woman you put here with me! She gave me some of the fruit, and I ate it. <i>(point at Eve)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It wasn’t me! <i>(everyone points at someone else)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> What have you done? <i>(to Eve)</i></li><li><b>Eve:</b> The snake tricked me, and I ate some of that fruit. <i>(point at the snake)</i></li><li><b>Serpent:</b> Ssss… don’t look at me… <i>(try to slither away)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The man blamed the woman. The woman blamed the snake. Nobody said, “It was me.”</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It was me. <i>(very quietly, hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then God told them sad news. Life outside his way would now be hard.</li><li><b>Eve:</b> We’ve ruined everything. <i>(head in hands)</i></li><li><b>Adam:</b> I’m… I’m sorry. <i>(finally, quietly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But God did not walk away. He kept talking with them. And hidden in his words was a promise.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> A promise?</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Find out tomorrow!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think God felt during that conversation?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Sad, hurt, disappointed — but still loving. He kept talking with them.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who did Adam blame? Who did Eve blame?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Adam blamed the woman and God; Eve blamed the snake.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What could they say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What could Adam and Eve have said instead of blaming? How might that have changed the conversation?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: ‘Yes, I ate it. I’m sorry. It was my choice.’ It would show honesty, take responsibility, stop the blame spreading, and show trust in God. Follow-up: ‘Why is “I’m sorry, but…” not a real sorry?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does a real sorry sound like?’ If silent: model one: ‘I took your pencil. That was wrong. I’m sorry.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why questions?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God already knew everything. Why do you think he asked questions instead of just announcing the punishment?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: to give them a chance to be honest; because he wanted a relationship, not just justice; to let them see what they’d done; like a good parent. Follow-up: ‘What does that tell us about how God treats us when we do wrong?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could you use questions when a friend has hurt you?’ If silent: ‘Why do teachers ask “What happened?” before deciding?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God wants honesty, not excuses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when it was me — help me say so.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Next time you’re asked “Who did this?” and it was you, say “It was me. I’m sorry” — without adding “but…”.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise it out loud together: ‘It was me. I’m sorry.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Nobody said it</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man blamed the woman. The woman blamed the snake.<br>“It was me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn one action per chunk, then say it twice with actions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Sorry prayer (heads bowed)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bowing your head is a way of praying with your body. It says, ‘You’re God, and I’m not. I’m ready to be honest.’ <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you’re comfortable, bow your head and fold your hands. Breathe slowly. God isn’t looking for excuses — just honesty.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God stayed and talked with Adam and Eve. He is patient.<br>Prompts: God, you are patient with me. / God, you never walk away from your people.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are patient, and you never walk away!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a time you blamed someone else. Tell God what really happened.<br>Prompts: God, it was me when… / Sorry for blaming others.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I blamed someone else.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that honesty brings us close to him again.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I can tell you the truth. / Thank you for people who forgive me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that I can always tell you the truth.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask for courage to own up this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me say ‘It was me’. / Please help me fix friendships I’ve hurt.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give me courage to say ‘It was me’.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your head. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Head bowing is optional — some students may prefer eyes closed.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the consequences — and a promise of hope that points all the way to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: A Promise in the Dark":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Promise in the Dark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sorry — A ‘Sorry’ prayer is being honest with God about what we’ve done wrong — and trusting him to forgive.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about God’s promise of a rescuer in Genesis 3:15.</li><li>We are learning that God cared for Adam and Eve even after they sinned.</li><li>I can explain what happens when we confess our sins to God (1 John 1:9).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell Genesis 3 in order, name God’s acts of care (looking, clothing, promising), and recite 1 John 1:9.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards (print from the challenge slide)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Promise in the Dark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand up for TRUE, sit down for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The snake told Eve the truth about God. — <b>False</b> (The snake twisted God’s words and said, “No, you won’t!”)<br>God gave Adam and Eve only one rule. — <b>True</b> (Every tree was a yes — except the one in the middle.)<br>Adam and Eve hid when they heard God walking. — <b>True</b> (“So they hid behind some trees.”)<br>Adam said, “It was me. I’m sorry.” — <b>False</b> (He blamed the woman — and God.)<br>God made clothes for Adam and Eve. — <b>True</b> (Clothes made from animal skins (Genesis 3:21).)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Each statement: students stand (true) or sit (false), then click to reveal. Ask one student to explain each answer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 3:15, 21 · 1 John 1:9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:15</b> God said to the snake: “You and this woman will hate each other; your descendants and hers will always be enemies. One of hers will strike you on the head, and you will strike him on the heel.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 3:21</b> Then the LORD God made clothes out of animal skins for the man and his wife.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 John 1:9</b> But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Genesis 3:15 is sometimes called ‘the first good news’ in the Bible.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The first promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Adam and Eve had to leave the garden. Life would be harder now. But if you listen carefully to what God said, there’s something amazing hidden in the sad news.</p><p>God told the snake that one day, one of the woman’s descendants — one of her great-great-great-grandchildren — would strike the snake on the head. The snake would strike his heel, but he would crush the snake. In other words: evil would not win. A Rescuer was coming.</p><p>Christians have always believed this points to Jesus. Thousands of years later, Jesus came. He was hurt — that’s the heel — when he died on the cross. But by dying and rising again, he defeated sin and evil — that’s the head. On the very first sad day, God was already promising the happiest day.</p><p>And then God did something else. Adam and Eve were wearing scratchy fig leaves. So God made them proper clothes from animal skins. He was still looking after them.</p><p>That’s what God is like. When we sin, there are often consequences. But God doesn’t stop caring. And this week’s verse tells us what to do: ‘If we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.’ Confess means to say honestly, ‘I did it. I’m sorry.’ And forgiven means God takes it away — completely.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Hidden in the sad news… a promise. · 2. A Rescuer was coming. · 3. God made them clothes. · 4. Sorry — and forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. ‘Descendants’ = children, grandchildren and so on. Linking Genesis 3:15 to Jesus is the traditional Christian reading — present it as what Christians believe.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did God make?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did God make for Adam and Eve before they left the garden?<br>A) New fig leaves &nbsp; B) Clothes from animal skins ✓ &nbsp; C) A house &nbsp; D) A boat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Genesis 3:21). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think he bothered?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does that show about God’s heart, even when people disobey?’ If silent: re-read the second line of the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Care after sin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God made clothes for Adam and Eve — and made a promise — even after they disobeyed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he still loved them; he’s kind; he wanted to fix what was broken; he had a rescue plan. Follow-up: ‘Do we deserve God’s kindness when we sin?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What’s the word for kindness we don’t deserve?’ (Grace.) If silent: ‘How does a parent look after a child who has just been told off?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>The snake twisted God’s words — and Adam and Eve chose their way instead of God’s.</li><li>Sin broke their friendship with God. They hid, and they blamed.</li><li>But God came looking, cared for them, and promised a Rescuer.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sin breaks our friendship with God, but God comes looking for us and promises a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea. Read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Like it never happened?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If I say sorry to God, it’s like it never happened.” Move to the side that matches what you think.<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both sides have truth! God completely forgives and takes sin away (1 John 1:9) — he doesn’t hold it against us. But there can still be consequences (Adam and Eve still left the garden) and we may need to fix things with people. Follow-up: ‘If I break a window and say sorry, is the window fixed?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does God do with our sins when we confess?’ If silent: give the window example first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sorry — and forgiven. This week, when you get something wrong, say sorry to God AND to the person you hurt. Then believe God when he says you’re forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Print take-home cards. Next Monday’s check-in asks how it went (no one has to share details).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if we confess our sins to God, he can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away.” — 1 John 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Each click removes more words — can the class still say 1 John 1:9?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Not the end</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">They had to leave the garden…<br>…but God’s rescue plan had already begun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Sorry and forgiven</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After a sorry prayer, don’t keep carrying it! Thank God that he has forgiven you. 1 John 1:9 is a promise you can pray back to him. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. I’ll say a line, and you say it after me — out loud, as a class.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me.<br>Prompts: God, you came looking for Adam and Eve. / You are faithful and kind.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you came looking for us. You are faithful and kind!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quietly, in your head, tell God one thing you’re sorry for.<br>Prompts: (silent) / God, I’m sorry for…<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we confess our sins to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now say 1 John 1:9 together as a thank-you.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you can always be trusted… / …to forgive us and take our sins away.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you can always be trusted to forgive us and take our sins away!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Please help me say sorry to people, not just to you. / Please help me believe I’m forgiven.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me say sorry — and believe I’m forgiven.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the end of Week 2. Remind them: forgiven means forgiven.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on the challenge. Week 3 — Rescue in the Storm (Noah).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3: Rescue in the Storm":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6:5–9:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faithful</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanks — A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so — for big rescues and small, everyday gifts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Noah Hears God</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning why God was heartbroken about the world in Noah’s time.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Noah Hears God → Laughed at → Genesis 6:5–9, 13–14 → A world gone wrong → What did Noah do? → Holy and kind → Noah Hears God → Strange obedience → Head · Heart · Hands → Knock, knock, knock → Memory verse peek → Thanks prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>God Shut the Door</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Noah built the boat and brought the animals in.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>God Shut the Door → Recap → Genesis 7:1, 5, 15–16 → All aboard → Who did what? → Who closed the door? → Safe inside → The people outside → Head · Heart · Hands → Two by two → Build the verse → Thanks in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Dove and the Olive Leaf</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God remembered Noah during the flood.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Dove and the Olive Leaf → Waiting → Genesis 8:1, 8–11 → Forty days of rain → What was in its beak? → The Dove Comes Home → Not forgotten → Waiting well → Head · Heart · Hands → A green leaf → Verse with actions → Popcorn thanks</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Rainbow Promise</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about God’s rainbow promise to Noah and every living creature.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Rainbow Promise → Put it in order → Genesis 9:11–16 → A sign in the sky → First thing → Why a sign? → This week’s Big Idea → Promises → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Every rainbow → A thank-you list</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Noah Hears God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6:5–9:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Noah Hears God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanks — A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so — for big rescues and small, everyday gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why God was heartbroken about the world in Noah’s time.</li><li>We are learning how Noah trusted and obeyed God when others laughed.</li><li>I can explain what it means to obey God even when it seems strange.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that God is both fair (sin matters) and kind (he made a way to be saved), and that Noah obeyed even when mocked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a hammer sound (knock on the desk)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Noah Hears God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Laughed at</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine everyone at school laughing at you for doing what you knew was right.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you keep going — or give up? Why?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how Adam and Eve hid and blamed — and how God came looking. Your challenge: say sorry to God AND to the person, and believe you’re forgiven. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: invite 2–3 volunteers to share (no details needed — ‘I said sorry and it felt…’). Then the hook: take a few honest answers. Land it: ‘Today we meet a man who spent years doing something everyone thought was crazy — because God told him to.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 6:5–9, 13–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 6:5</b> The LORD saw how bad the people on earth were and that everything they thought and planned was evil.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 6:8</b> But the LORD was pleased with Noah.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 6:9</b> Noah was the only person who lived right and obeyed God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 6:14</b> God said: “Get some good lumber and build a boat.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. Pause after ‘But…’ — that little word changes everything.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A world gone wrong</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After Adam and Eve left the garden, you might hope things got better. They didn’t. Sin spread like a sickness. People grew more and more selfish. Brothers hurt brothers. Cruelty and violence spread everywhere, until the Bible says that everything people thought and planned was evil, all the time.</p><p>And how did God feel? Not cold. Not bored. The Bible says God was sorry he had made people. His heart was broken. Imagine building something beautiful and watching people smash it — and smash each other.</p><p>God is holy. That means he is perfectly good, and he cannot pretend evil doesn’t matter. A good judge doesn’t ignore cruelty. So God decided the violence had to stop. He would send a flood.</p><p>But then comes one of the most important little words in the Bible: ‘But.’ ‘But the LORD was pleased with Noah.’ Noah wasn’t perfect, but he lived right and he obeyed God. In a world where nobody listened to God, Noah listened.</p><p>And God told Noah his plan: ‘Get some good lumber and build a boat.’ Not a dinghy. A boat about one hundred and thirty metres long — longer than a rugby field — and three storeys high. God promised that Noah, his wife, his three sons and their wives would be kept safe inside it, along with animals of every kind.</p><p>Think about how hard that was. Noah wasn’t a young man. He had a family to feed, fields to look after, and now a giant building project that would take years. Every morning he had to decide again: will I keep trusting God today? Now picture it. Noah lived nowhere near the sea. There was no rain in the forecast. And he starts building a gigantic boat in his backyard. What do you think the neighbours said? We don’t know their exact words, but you can imagine the laughing and the pointing.</p><p>Yet the Bible says something simple and powerful: ‘Noah did everything God told him to do.’ Not some of it. Not the easy parts. Everything. Day after day, year after year, hammer, saw, wood, tar — because he trusted that God tells the truth.</p><p>That’s what faith looks like. It’s not just believing God exists. It’s doing what God says, even when it looks strange, even when people laugh.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. After the garden, things got worse. · 2. God was heartbroken. · 3. But… · 4. “Build a boat.” · 5. Noah did everything God told him to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The neighbours’ mockery is imagined — the Bible doesn’t record it (though 2 Peter 2:5 calls Noah ‘a preacher’). Measurements from Genesis 6:15 (CEV: 133 metres long).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Noah do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does the Bible say Noah did when God told him to build a boat?<br>A) Asked for a smaller job &nbsp; B) Did everything God told him to do ✓ &nbsp; C) Waited until it rained &nbsp; D) Ran away</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B — ‘Noah did everything God told him to do’ (6:22). Follow-up: ‘Why does the word “everything” matter?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What made the job hard?’ (size, years, no rain, people laughing). If silent: re-read the last line of the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Holy and kind</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God was heartbroken about the evil in the world. What does that tell us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he cares; he is good and hates cruelty; he takes evil seriously; he isn’t distant. Follow-up: ‘Would a good God ignore cruelty?’ Follow-up 2: ‘And what did God do for Noah?’ (made a way to be saved). If silent: ‘How do you feel when you see someone being bullied?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Noah Hears God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Noah Hears God</b> (Genesis 6:9–22; 7:1). God’s words are from Genesis 6–7 (CEV). The neighbours, Mrs Noah’s and Shem’s lines are imagined — the Bible doesn’t record what people said to Noah.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Noah</b> — Old, steady, determined<br><b>Mrs Noah</b> — Practical, a bit worried, loyal<br><b>Shem</b> — Noah’s son — puzzled, then helpful<br><b>Neighbour</b> — Loud, mocking, thinks Noah is crazy<br><b>Everyone</b> — Hammering, and the laughing crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: hammering — knock knock knock on the desk · Everyone (as neighbours): laugh and point when cued<br>Props: Optional: a ruler as a hammer<br>Keep the mocking light. Point out that the neighbours’ words are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Noah is on a hill at sunset, alone, listening.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Get some good lumber and build a boat.</li><li><b>Noah:</b> A… boat, Lord? Here? <i>(look around at the dry land)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I solemnly promise that you, your wife, your sons, and your daughters-in-law will be kept safe in the boat.</li><li><b>Noah:</b> Then I’ll build it. <i>(nod firmly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Noah went home to tell his family.</li><li><b>Mrs Noah:</b> A boat? Noah, we live in the middle of nowhere!</li><li><b>Shem:</b> Dad, how big?</li><li><b>Noah:</b> Longer than a rugby field. Three storeys high.</li><li><b>Shem:</b> We’re going to need a LOT of wood.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Knock! Knock! Knock! <i>(hammer on the desks)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Oi, Noah! Expecting a flood, are you? Ha! <i>(point and laugh)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ha ha ha! <i>(laugh and point)</i></li><li><b>Mrs Noah:</b> They’re laughing at us again. <i>(sigh)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Everyone is laughing at Noah’s family. What would make YOU keep going? (20s)</li><li><b>Noah:</b> Let them laugh. God has never lied to us.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Knock! Knock! Knock!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Days became months. Months became years.</li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Still building your silly boat, old man?</li><li><b>Noah:</b> Still building. And there’s room for you, if you’d listen to God.</li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Pfft. No thanks. <i>(walk off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Noah did everything God told him to do.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Take your whole family with you into the boat.</li><li><b>Shem:</b> Dad… look at the sky. <i>(point up slowly)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh… <i>(look up)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What helped Noah keep going when people laughed?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He trusted that God tells the truth; God had made a promise; he listened to God more than to people.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Noah said there was room for the neighbour. What does that show?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Imagined line — but it reflects God’s heart: the door was open; God wants people to be saved (2 Peter 3:9).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Strange obedience</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Building a boat on dry land looked crazy. Is there anything God asks people to do today that might look strange to others?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: forgiving someone who hurt you, praying, going to church, telling the truth when a lie would be easier, being kind to someone unpopular, giving money away. Follow-up: ‘Why might people laugh?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could Noah’s story help you?’ If silent: ‘Is it strange to say sorry first when it wasn’t all your fault?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God always tells the truth — so obeying him is always wise, even when it looks strange.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me care more about what you think than what people think.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, do one right thing even if someone laughs — like sticking up for someone or telling the truth.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask: ‘What might the laughing sound like at our school?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Knock, knock, knock</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Year after year…<br>Noah did everything God told him to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce this week’s verse with echo.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so. Try to be specific — not just ‘thanks for everything’, but ‘thank you for…’ one real thing. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Close your eyes or look down. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is good — he hates cruelty and cares about the world.<br>Prompts: God, you are good and fair. / God, you always tell the truth.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are good, and you always tell the truth!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a time you cared more about what people thought than what God thought?<br>Prompts: Sorry for going along with the crowd. / Sorry for laughing at someone.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I followed the crowd instead of you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one specific thing God has given you or done for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for keeping my family safe. / Thank you for people who do the right thing.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for keeping your promises.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God for Noah-style courage.<br>Prompts: Please help me obey you, even when it’s hard. / Please help me keep going when people laugh.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me obey you even when it’s hard.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a specific thanks: ‘Thank you, God, for the sunshine on my drive this morning.’</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the building of the boat, the animals arrive — and God shuts the door.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: God Shut the Door":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6:5–9:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God Shut the Door</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanks — A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so — for big rescues and small, everyday gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Noah built the boat and brought the animals in.</li><li>We are learning what ‘the LORD closed the door’ shows about God.</li><li>I can explain why the boat is a picture of being safe with God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking God closing the door to God protecting Noah, and describing the boat as God’s way of rescue.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: God Shut the Door</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God gave Noah a very strange job…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was it — and what did the neighbours think?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Build a boat — huge, on dry land. The neighbours (in our role-play) laughed. Ask: ‘What did Noah do?’ — everything God told him. ‘Today the boat is finished… and the animals arrive.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 7:1, 5, 15–16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 7:1</b> The LORD told Noah: Take your whole family with you into the boat, because you are the only one on this earth who pleases me.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 7:5</b> Noah… did everything the LORD had told him to do.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 7:15–16</b> Noah took a male and a female of every living creature with him, just as God had told him to do.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 7:16</b> And when they were all in the boat, the LORD closed the door.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The last line is the key line today. Read it slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: All aboard</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday, God told Noah to build a boat, and Noah did everything God told him to do. Today, let’s imagine the building. There were no power tools, no cranes, no hardware shop down the road. Noah and his three sons — Shem, Ham and Japheth — had to cut down the trees, shape every plank, and fit the giant wooden ribs together by hand. Then they covered the whole thing, inside and out, with sticky tar to keep the water out. It took years.</p><p>God also told Noah to store up food — lots of food. Enough for eight people and a whole zoo of animals, for a very long time. Imagine the sacks of grain, the hay, the jars. Noah was preparing for something nobody else believed was coming.</p><p>Then, one day, the boat was finished. And God said, ‘Take your whole family with you into the boat.’</p><p>And then something incredible happened. The animals began to come. A male and a female of every kind. Picture it: elephants lumbering up the ramp, giraffes ducking their heads, lions padding along calmly, sheep, lizards, beetles, and birds of every kind swooping in through the window. The Bible says they came to Noah. Noah didn’t have to chase them with a net. God brought them.</p><p>When everyone was inside — every family member, every animal — the Bible tells us something tiny but important: ‘And when they were all in the boat, the LORD closed the door.’</p><p>Not Noah. God. God himself shut the door. Why does that matter? Because it means Noah’s safety didn’t depend on Noah being strong enough to hold the door shut in a storm. It depended on God. God had promised to keep them safe, and God sealed them in with his own hand.</p><p>Inside, it must have been dark and noisy and crowded. Can you imagine the sounds? Lions yawning, sheep bleating, birds chattering, elephants rumbling, and somewhere in the middle, Mrs Noah wondering where to put everyone. But they were inside. And they were together. The boat was God’s way of rescue. Everyone inside was safe. Sadly, the people outside had not listened to God, and the flood would sweep them away. That is a serious and sad part of this story, and it’s okay to feel sad about it. God takes evil seriously.</p><p>But here’s the good news. Christians see the boat as a picture of Jesus. Just as Noah’s family was safe inside the boat, everyone who trusts in Jesus is safe with God — and nothing can wash us away from his love.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Year after year, the boat grew. · 2. Food for everyone — for a long time. · 3. Then the animals came. · 4. “The LORD closed the door.” · 5. Safe inside.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Handle the people outside gently and honestly; don’t describe drowning. Sons’ names from Genesis 6:10. ‘The boat as a picture of Jesus’ is a traditional Christian reading (1 Peter 3:20–21).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who did what?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look closer at Genesis 6–7. What did Noah do, and what did God do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Noah did…</th><th>God did…</th></tr><tr><td>Built the boat</td><td>Gave the plan and the measurements</td></tr><tr><td>Stored up food</td><td>Promised to keep the family safe</td></tr><tr><td>Went into the boat</td><td>Brought the animals to Noah</td></tr><tr><td>Trusted and obeyed</td><td>Closed the door</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Noah obeyed — but it was God who did the saving.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Ask: ‘Who was really doing the rescuing?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who closed the door?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who closed the door of the boat?<br>A) Noah &nbsp; B) Mrs Noah &nbsp; C) An elephant &nbsp; D) The LORD ✓</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: D — ‘the LORD closed the door’ (7:16). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think the Bible bothers to tell us that?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might Noah have felt when he heard it shut?’ If silent: re-read the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Safe inside</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why does it matter that GOD closed the door, not Noah?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the family’s safety depended on God, not on Noah’s strength; God keeps his promises; God was protecting them; nothing could open it. Follow-up: ‘Who are you trusting to keep you safe?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is Jesus like the boat?’ If silent: ‘Would you rather a toddler or a firefighter hold the door shut in a storm?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The people outside</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Some people find this part of the story hard: the people outside the boat. Is it fair? What do we learn about God here?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow honest feelings. Key ideas: the people had chosen violence for a long time; God waited patiently (years of building); Noah’s boat was a warning; God takes evil seriously because he loves what he made; God made a way to be saved. Follow-up: ‘What would it say about God if he never stopped cruelty?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How does Jesus show God still wants to rescue people?’ If silent: share that grown-ups find this hard too — and that’s okay.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is the one who keeps us safe — not our own strength.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, that I can be safe with you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you feel worried this week — about a test, a game, or something at home — say quietly, “God, you’ve shut the door. I’m safe with you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Teach the line and have the class say it together once.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Two by two</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The animals came to Noah…<br>…and the LORD closed the door.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch: ‘Which animal would you have most liked to see walk up the ramp?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Genesis 9:12–13 chunk by chunk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying with a partner is a great way to grow. Take turns: one person says a short thank-you, then the other. Short is perfect. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to the person next to you. Take turns praying one sentence each. If you’d rather pray silently, that’s fine too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Partner A: tell God one thing he is like from today.<br>Prompts: God, you keep us safe. / God, you keep your promises.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you keep us safe, and you keep your promises!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: anything you need to say sorry for?<br>Prompts: Sorry for trusting myself instead of you. / Sorry for worrying instead of praying.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I trusted myself instead of you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Partner B: thank God for something that keeps you safe.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my home. / Thank you for people who look after me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for my home and the people who look after me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your partner: ask God to keep them safe this week.<br>Prompts: Please keep (name) safe. / Please help (name) not to worry.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep my friend safe this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pair students thoughtfully. Allow silent participation.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the storm, the long wait — and a dove with something in its beak.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Dove and the Olive Leaf":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6:5–9:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Dove and the Olive Leaf</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanks — A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so — for big rescues and small, everyday gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God remembered Noah during the flood.</li><li>We are learning how Noah waited patiently for God’s timing.</li><li>I can explain what the olive leaf meant.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling the raven and dove sequence and explaining that ‘God did not forget’ Noah.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a green leaf for the dove</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Dove and the Olive Leaf</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play — the storm mood has rain and lightning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Waiting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the longest you have ever had to wait for something?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s the hardest part about waiting?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few: Christmas, a birthday, a sibling being born, a long car trip. Hardest part: not knowing when, getting bored, worrying. Land it: ‘Noah’s family waited in that boat for more than a year. Today we find out how they knew it was nearly over.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 8:1, 8–11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 8:1</b> God did not forget about Noah and the animals with him in the boat. So God made a wind blow, and the water started going down.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 8:8–9</b> Noah… sent out a dove. Deep water was still everywhere, and when the dove could not find a place to land, it flew back to the boat.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 8:10–11</b> Seven days later Noah sent the dove out again. It returned in the evening, holding in its beak a green leaf from an olive tree.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged (‘…’).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Forty days of rain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>When everyone was safe inside and God had closed the door, the rain began. The Bible says the water under the earth started gushing out, and the sky opened like windows. Rain poured down for forty days and forty nights.</p><p>This was not a normal storm. It wasn’t a wet afternoon in Christchurch. It was as if the whole world was being washed. Imagine being inside that boat. The drumming of rain on the roof. The creaking of the wood. The smell of animals — lots of animals! The waves lifting the boat higher and higher until it floated above everything.</p><p>Then the rain stopped. But the water didn’t go away. Day after day there was nothing but sea in every direction. Weeks turned into months. Do you think Noah ever wondered, ‘Has God forgotten us?’</p><p>Here is one of the most beautiful sentences in this story: ‘God did not forget about Noah and the animals with him in the boat.’ God sent a wind, and slowly, slowly, the water started going down. One day the boat bumped and settled on a mountain called Ararat.</p><p>Noah wanted to know if it was safe to go out. So he opened a window and sent out a raven. The raven just kept flying around and around. Then he sent out a dove. But the dove couldn’t find anywhere to land, so it flew back, and Noah reached out his hand and gently brought it inside.</p><p>Noah waited seven more days. He sent the dove out again. That evening it came back — and in its beak was a fresh green leaf from an olive tree! Trees were growing again! The earth was coming back to life.</p><p>Noah waited another seven days and sent the dove out once more. This time it didn’t come back. It had found a home.</p><p>Can you imagine the cheering inside the boat when that olive leaf arrived? Shem and Ham and Japheth shouting. Mrs Noah laughing. The animals stamping and squawking. After all those months, here was proof in a little green leaf: God was making the world new. Noah was patient. He didn’t rush out early. He trusted that God’s timing was right. And God hadn’t forgotten him for a single day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The rain began. · 2. Forty days and nights. · 3. “God did not forget about Noah.” · 4. Is it over yet? · 5. A green olive leaf!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Timeline details from Genesis 7:11–8:12. The whole time in the boat was just over a year (Genesis 7:11; 8:14).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was in its beak?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When the dove came back the second time, what was in its beak?<br>A) A worm &nbsp; B) A green olive leaf ✓ &nbsp; C) A piece of bread &nbsp; D) Nothing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (8:11). Follow-up: ‘What did the olive leaf tell Noah?’ (trees were growing — the water was going down). Follow-up 2: ‘What happened the third time?’ (the dove didn’t come back). If silent: re-read the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Dove Comes Home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Dove Comes Home</b> (Genesis 8:1–19). The events follow Genesis 8 (CEV). The family conversation and the birds’ lines are imagined for fun; God’s final words are from Genesis 8:15–16.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Noah</b> — Patient and trusting<br><b>Mrs Noah</b> — Tired of the smell!<br><b>Shem</b> — Impatient to get out<br><b>Raven</b> — Restless — never lands<br><b>Dove</b> — Gentle; returns to Noah<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Everyone</b> — Rain, snores and cheers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: rain on the roof — patter fingers on the desk · Everyone: animal snores when cued<br>Props: Optional: a green leaf<br>God’s line is a paraphrase of Genesis 8:16 (CEV: ‘You, your wife, your sons, and your daughters-in-law may now leave the boat’).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Everyone:</b> Pitter-patter-pitter-patter… <i>(fingers drumming on the desks)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Inside the boat, the rain had stopped. But there was still water everywhere.</li><li><b>Shem:</b> Dad, how much longer? I’ve counted every plank in this boat. Twice.</li><li><b>Mrs Noah:</b> And the smell… I never want to smell another goat as long as I live.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Zzzzz… snort… zzzz… <i>(animal snores)</i></li><li><b>Noah:</b> God did not forget about us. We wait for his time.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Noah opened a window and sent out a raven.</li><li><b>Raven:</b> Caw! Caw! I’ll just keep flying round and round and round… <i>(flap in circles)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So Noah sent out a dove.</li><li><b>Dove:</b> Coo… water, water everywhere. Nowhere to land! <i>(fly back, tired)</i></li><li><b>Noah:</b> Come here, little one. <i>(hold out a hand gently)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Shem wants to rush out. Noah says wait. Who’s right? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Seven days later, Noah sent the dove out again.</li><li><b>Dove:</b> Coo! Coo! Look what I found! <i>(hold up a leaf)</i></li><li><b>Shem:</b> A green olive leaf! Things are growing!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hooray! <i>(cheer)</i></li><li><b>Mrs Noah:</b> Can we go out now?</li><li><b>Noah:</b> Not yet. We wait for God to say so.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Noah waited seven more days and sent the dove out again. This time it did not return.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Go out of the boat with your wife, your sons, and your daughters-in-law.</li><li><b>Noah:</b> God has kept his promise. Everyone out!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Thank you, God! <i>(arms up)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Noah waited for God to say ‘Go out’?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He trusted God’s timing more than his own; God had kept them safe so far; patience is part of faith.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many times did Noah send out the dove?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Three times: it came back with nothing, then with an olive leaf, then didn’t return.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not forgotten</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God did not forget about Noah.” Why might Noah have wondered if God had forgotten him? Why do people today sometimes feel forgotten by God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Noah: months of waiting, nothing but water, no word from God. Today: when prayers aren’t answered quickly, when life is hard, when we feel alone. Key truth: God never forgets his people (Isaiah 49:15). Follow-up: ‘What could help you remember God hasn’t forgotten you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How was the olive leaf like a message from God?’ If silent: ‘Have you ever been the last one picked up from school?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Waiting well</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s the difference between waiting well and waiting badly?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Waiting badly: grumbling, giving up, rushing ahead, blaming. Waiting well: trusting, being patient, praying, being thankful for what you have now. Follow-up: ‘How did Noah wait well?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What are you waiting for right now?’ If silent: compare two kids waiting for a long car trip to end.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God never forgets his people — even in a long wait.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, that you remember me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Next time you’re waiting (in a queue, for your turn, for a reply), say a quiet thank-you instead of a grumble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise swapping a grumble for a thank-you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A green leaf</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God did not forget about Noah.<br>A green leaf — new life!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn the actions; say the verse twice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Popcorn thanks</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">‘Popcorn prayer’: anyone can pop up with a short thank-you, out loud, one at a time — like popcorn popping. One sentence is perfect. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Eyes closed or open. When you’re ready, ‘pop’ a short prayer out loud. I’ll start.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pop a ‘Wow, God’ about what he’s like.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you never forget us! / Wow, God, you are patient!<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you never forget us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for grumbling or rushing?<br>Prompts: Sorry for grumbling when I wait. / Sorry for being impatient.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for grumbling when I have to wait.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pop a thank-you — anything at all!<br>Prompts: Thank you for animals! / Thank you for rain and for sunshine! / Thank you for my family!<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for remembering us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pop a ‘please’ for someone who is waiting for something.<br>Prompts: Please help someone who is waiting to get better. / Please help me be patient.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help everyone who is waiting.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Start the popcorn yourself. Keep it moving and light.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: out of the boat — and God’s rainbow promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Rainbow Promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 6:5–9:17</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Rainbow Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanks — A ‘Thanks’ prayer notices what God has done and says so — for big rescues and small, everyday gifts.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about God’s rainbow promise to Noah and every living creature.</li><li>We are learning what a covenant is.</li><li>I can retell Noah’s story in order and say why God is faithful.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can sequence the story, explain ‘covenant’ in their own words, and recite Genesis 9:12–13.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Rainbow Promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put it in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can you put Noah’s story in order?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God sees the violence → “Build a boat” → Animals come in → God closes the door → 40 days of rain → Dove brings a leaf → Rainbow promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next event; click to lock each into place. Option: seven volunteers hold cards and line up first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 9:11–16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 9:11</b> I promise every living creature that the earth and those living on it will never again be destroyed by a flood.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 9:12–13</b> The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 9:16</b> When I see the rainbow in the sky, I will always remember the promise that I have made to every living creature.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The CEV combines verses 12 and 13.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A sign in the sky</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>When God finally said, ‘Go out of the boat,’ the door opened, and out came Noah and his family, and all the animals — stretching, sniffing, galloping, flying off into a brand-new world.</p><p>And what was the very first thing Noah did? He built an altar and worshipped God. Before building a house, before planting a garden — he said thank you.</p><p>Then God made a covenant. A covenant is a serious promise agreement. God promised Noah, his family, and every living creature that he would never again destroy the earth with a flood. And God said, ‘As long as the earth remains, there will be planting and harvest, cold and heat, winter and summer, day and night.’</p><p>Then God gave a sign. He put a rainbow in the clouds. ‘When I see the rainbow,’ God said, ‘I will always remember the promise.’</p><p>Every rainbow you have ever seen — over the Port Hills, over the beach, in a sprinkler on a sunny day — is a reminder: God keeps his promises. He kept his promise to Noah. He is still keeping it today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Out of the boat — onto dry ground! · 2. God made a covenant. · 3. The rainbow! · 4. God always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. ‘Covenant’ = a serious promise agreement. Seasons promise from Genesis 8:22. Change the local reference if you’re not near the Port Hills!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: First thing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the first thing Noah did after leaving the boat?<br>A) Built a house &nbsp; B) Built an altar to worship God ✓ &nbsp; C) Planted a garden &nbsp; D) Went for a swim</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Genesis 8:20). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think thanking God came first?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would be your first thing after a year in a boat?’ If silent: share your own funny answer first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why a sign?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God gave a rainbow as a sign? What does it show about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: so we can see the promise; it appears after rain/storms; it’s beautiful; it reaches across the whole sky; God wants us to trust him. Follow-up: ‘God says HE will remember when he sees it — what does that show?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What promises of God do you know?’ If silent: ‘Why do people wear wedding rings?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God is holy — he takes evil seriously and was heartbroken by it.</li><li>God saved Noah’s family in the boat — and he closed the door himself.</li><li>God made a rainbow promise — and he always keeps his promises.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is holy and fair, yet he saves those who trust him — and he always keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Promises</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “It’s okay to break a small promise.” Move to the side that matches what you think.<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let both sides speak. Draw out: small broken promises damage trust; God keeps every promise, big and small; sometimes we can’t keep one — then we should be honest and say sorry. Follow-up: ‘How do you feel when someone breaks a promise to you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does God’s promise-keeping teach us?’ If silent: ‘If I promised you a game on Friday and forgot, would that matter?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Promise-keeper. Think of one promise you’ve made — to a parent, a friend, a coach — and keep it this week. If you can’t, be honest and say sorry.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God keeps every promise. When we keep ours, we show people what he is like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Print the cards. Next Monday’s check-in will ask which promise they kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The rainbow that I have put in the sky will be my sign to you and to every living creature on earth. It will remind you that I will keep this promise forever.” — Genesis 9:12–13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Can the class still say Genesis 9:12–13 as the words disappear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Every rainbow</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I will always remember the promise…”<br>Every rainbow is a reminder: God keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A thank-you list</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like Noah, start with thanks! Writing a thank-you list helps you notice how much God has given you. Keep it and add to it this week. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On a scrap of paper or your whiteboard, we’re going to write a thank-you list to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At the top, write one word about what God is like.<br>Prompts: Faithful / Promise-keeper / Rescuer<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are faithful — you keep every promise!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there a promise you’ve broken? Tell God.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the promise I broke. / Sorry for not trusting your promises.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the promises I haven’t kept.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write as many thank-yous as you can — big and small!<br>Prompts: Thank you for… / Thank you for… / Thank you for…<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 60s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for every good thing you give us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help you keep your promise this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me keep my promise. / Please help me remember you when I see a rainbow.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me keep my promises like you do.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s read our memory verse together as a closing prayer. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite students to keep their lists and add to them all week.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on promises kept. Week 4 — The Promise-Maker (Abraham).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4: The Promise-Maker":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9; 15:1–6; 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Promise-Maker</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please — A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need — for ourselves and for others — and trusts him with the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Go!</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning about God’s call and promise to Abram in Genesis 12.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Go! → Moving away → Genesis 12:1–4 → Leaving everything → What did God say? → Why go? → Go! → One step at a time → Head · Heart · Hands → Wherever God leads → Memory verse peek → Please prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Count the Stars</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God encouraged Abram while he waited for a child.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Count the Stars → Recap → Genesis 15:1–6 → A sky full of promises → Word study: believe → God’s first words → Why stars? → Honest prayers → Head · Heart · Hands → Count them! → Build the verse → Please — out loud</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Sarah Laughed</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning about the three visitors who came to Abraham’s tent.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Sarah Laughed → Impossible! → Genesis 18:10–14 → Three visitors → Why laugh? → Three Visitors and a Laugh → Too difficult? → Laughing at God’s promises → Head · Heart · Hands → Nothing too difficult → Verse with actions → Please — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Isaac Means Laughter</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God kept his promise when Isaac was born.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Isaac Means Laughter → Who said it? → Genesis 21:1–6 → A baby called Laughter → How old? → Two laughs → This week’s Big Idea → Waiting well → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: scramble → Joyful laughter → Please — for each other</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Go!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9; 15:1–6; 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Go!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please — A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need — for ourselves and for others — and trusts him with the answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about God’s call and promise to Abram in Genesis 12.</li><li>We are learning that faith means trusting God enough to act.</li><li>I can explain what Abram had to leave behind, and why he went.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming what Abram left behind and linking his leaving to trust in God’s promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a backpack or walking stick as props</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Go!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Moving away</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine your family packs everything into the car, and you drive away from your home, your school and your friends — and you don’t know where you’re going.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How would you feel? What would you miss most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how God kept his rainbow promise to Noah. Your challenge: keep one promise you made. Which promise did you keep?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate 2–3 kept promises. Then the hook: take honest feelings — scared, excited, sad. Some students may have moved countries or towns; invite them to share if they want. Land it: ‘Today God asks a man to do exactly that.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 12:1–4</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 12:1</b> The LORD said to Abram: Leave your country, your family, and your relatives and go to the land that I will show you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 12:2</b> I will bless you and make your descendants into a great nation. You will become famous and be a blessing to others.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 12:3</b> Everyone on earth will be blessed because of you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 12:4–5</b> Abram was 75 years old when the LORD told him to leave… He obeyed and left with his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot…</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses 3–5 abridged on screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Leaving everything</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Long after Noah, there lived a man called Abram. He lived in a city called Haran with his wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, and lots of servants and animals. Abram was rich and comfortable. He had a home. He had family nearby. He knew where the best water was and which neighbours were friendly. Life was settled.</p><p>There was just one sadness: Abram and Sarai had no children. And they were old. Abram was seventy-five.</p><p>Then one day, the LORD spoke to Abram. And what God said turned his whole life upside down: ‘Leave your country, your family, and your relatives, and go to the land that I will show you.’</p><p>Notice what God didn’t say. He didn’t say where. He didn’t give a map. He didn’t say how long the trip would take. He just said, ‘Go… to the land I will show you.’ Abram would have to trust God one step at a time.</p><p>But God didn’t just give a command. He gave a promise — a huge one. ‘I will bless you and make your descendants into a great nation. You will become famous and be a blessing to others. Everyone on earth will be blessed because of you.’</p><p>Now think about that. A great nation — from a man with no children? Everyone on earth blessed — through one old man? It sounded impossible.</p><p>So what did Abram do? The Bible says it simply: ‘He obeyed.’ He packed up the tents. He loaded the camels. He gathered the sheep and goats. He said goodbye to his relatives. And at seventy-five years old, he walked out of Haran with Sarai and Lot, heading into the unknown.</p><p>That’s what faith is. Faith isn’t just saying ‘I believe in God’. Faith is trusting God enough to move — to actually do what he says, even when you can’t see the whole path.</p><p>And here’s a secret about that promise — ‘everyone on earth will be blessed because of you’. Thousands of years later, one of Abram’s descendants would be born in Bethlehem. His name was Jesus. Through him, people from every nation — including us in Aotearoa — would be blessed. God was already planning it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A comfortable life in Haran. · 2. Then God spoke: “Leave… and go.” · 3. And a huge promise. · 4. Abram was 75 — and he obeyed. · 5. Faith is trusting God enough to move.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Abram and Sarai are renamed Abraham and Sarah in Genesis 17 — explain on Wednesday. The link to Jesus via Galatians 3:8.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did God say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did God tell Abram to go?<br>A) To Egypt &nbsp; B) Back to the garden &nbsp; C) To the land that God would show him ✓ &nbsp; D) To the sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C — ‘go to the land that I will show you’ (12:1). Follow-up: ‘Did Abram know where that was?’ (No!) Follow-up 2: ‘How old was he?’ (75). If silent: re-read the first line of the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why go?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abram didn’t know where he was going. Why do you think he went anyway?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he trusted God; God made a big promise; he believed God was good; he wanted the blessing; God would show him the way. Follow-up: ‘What did it cost him to go?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this teach us about faith?’ If silent: ‘Would you follow a guide through a dark cave if you trusted them?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Go!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Go!</b> (Genesis 12:1–9). God’s words are from Genesis 12 (CEV). Sarai’s, Lot’s and the servant’s lines are imagined to bring the scene to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Abram</b> — Old, thoughtful, decides to trust<br><b>Sarai</b> — His wife — surprised but loyal<br><b>Lot</b> — His nephew — keen for adventure<br><b>Servant</b> — In charge of the camels — worried about packing<br><b>Everyone</b> — Relatives waving goodbye, and the camels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: camel noises and clip-clopping when the caravan sets off · Everyone: wave goodbye<br>Props: Optional: a backpack, a walking stick<br>God’s line in Canaan paraphrases Genesis 12:7 (‘I will give this land to your family forever’).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Haran. Evening. Abram sits outside his tent.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Abram. Leave your country, your family, and your relatives and go to the land that I will show you.</li><li><b>Abram:</b> Go… where, Lord? <i>(stand up slowly)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I will bless you and make your descendants into a great nation. Everyone on earth will be blessed because of you.</li><li><b>Abram:</b> A great nation? But I don’t even have one child… <i>(shake head in wonder)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Abram doesn’t know where he’s going. What questions would YOU ask God right now? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Abram went inside to tell Sarai.</li><li><b>Abram:</b> Sarai — we’re moving.</li><li><b>Sarai:</b> Moving? Where to?</li><li><b>Abram:</b> I don’t know. God says he’ll show us.</li><li><b>Sarai:</b> We’re seventy-something years old, Abram!</li><li><b>Abram:</b> I know. But God has promised. And God doesn’t lie.</li><li><b>Sarai:</b> …Then I’ll start packing. <i>(small smile)</i></li><li><b>Lot:</b> An adventure? Count me in, Uncle Abram!</li><li><b>Servant:</b> Master, how many camels? How much water? How far? <i>(flustered)</i></li><li><b>Abram:</b> All of them. Lots. And… as far as God says.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Goodbye! Goodbye! <i>(wave)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hrrmph! Clip-clop, clip-clop… <i>(camel noises)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Abram was 75 years old. He obeyed and left with his wife Sarai and his nephew Lot.</li><li><b>Lot:</b> Uncle, which way?</li><li><b>Abram:</b> Wherever God leads. One step at a time.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When they reached the land of Canaan, God spoke again.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I will give this land to your family forever.</li><li><b>Abram:</b> Then here I will build an altar and worship the LORD. <i>(kneel)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> He trusted God!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think was the hardest thing about leaving for Abram and Sarai?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Leaving family, home, safety, not knowing where; being old; no children yet.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Abram do when he arrived in Canaan?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He built an altar and worshipped God (Genesis 12:7–8).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: One step at a time</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t give Abram the whole map — just “Go.” Why might God lead people one step at a time instead of showing everything at once?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: so we keep trusting him daily; so we stay close to him; we couldn’t handle the whole picture; faith grows by stepping out. Follow-up: ‘Would it have been easier with a map? Better?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where might God be asking you to take one step?’ If silent: compare a torch in the dark — it only lights the next few steps.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith means trusting God enough to do what he says.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to follow you — even when I can’t see the whole path.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, take one step of trust: try the new thing, join the new group, or talk to the new kid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask: ‘What new or scary thing might you step into this week?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Wherever God leads</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go to the land that I will show you.”<br>He obeyed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Genesis 15:6 with echo.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Please prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need. God loves it when we ask him — just like a good parent wants their kids to ask for help. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Close your eyes or look down. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made a huge promise to Abram. Think about how big God is.<br>Prompts: God, you can do impossible things. / God, you lead us one step at a time.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you lead us, and you keep your promises!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there something God has asked you to do that you’ve been avoiding?<br>Prompts: Sorry for not trusting you. / Sorry for staying comfortable instead of obeying.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I didn’t trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for leading you so far.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my home and family. / Thank you that Jesus came through Abram’s family to bless the world.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you bless the whole world through Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God for something you need this week — or for someone who is moving or changing schools.<br>Prompts: Please help me be brave in new things. / Please help someone who is starting somewhere new.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help everyone who is starting somewhere new.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a simple ‘please’: ‘Please, God, help my friend who is moving house.’</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Abram is still waiting for a child — and God takes him outside to count the stars.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Count the Stars":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9; 15:1–6; 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Count the Stars</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please — A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need — for ourselves and for others — and trusts him with the answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God encouraged Abram while he waited for a child.</li><li>We are learning what it means that Abram ‘believed the LORD’.</li><li>I can explain the difference between seeing and believing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Abram believed God’s promise before he saw it, and that God accepted him because he believed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch (switch lights off for the stars)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Count the Stars</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optional: dim the lights for today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God told Abram to GO — and made him a huge promise.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did God promise? And what was the problem?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God promised a great nation and that everyone on earth would be blessed through him. The problem: Abram and Sarai had no children and were old. ‘Today, years have passed — and still no baby.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 15:1–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 15:1</b> The LORD spoke to Abram in a vision, “Abram, don’t be afraid! I will protect you and reward you greatly.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 15:2</b> But Abram answered, “LORD All-Powerful, you have given me everything I could ask for, except children.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 15:5</b> Then the LORD took Abram outside and said, “Look at the sky and see if you can count the stars. That’s how many descendants you will have.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 15:6</b> Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 1 slightly abridged. Verse 6 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A sky full of promises</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday, Abram left his home and followed God to a new land called Canaan. God had promised, ‘I will make your descendants into a great nation.’ Descendants means your children, and their children, and their children, all the way down the line.</p><p>But years went by. Abram got older. Sarai got older. And there was still no baby. Not one. Every year, Abram must have looked at his tents and wondered, ‘When, God? Did I hear you right?’</p><p>Then one night God spoke to Abram in a vision, a kind of dream from God. And the first thing God said was, ‘Abram, don’t be afraid!’ Isn’t that kind? God knew Abram was worried. Before Abram said a word, God spoke to his fear. ‘I will protect you and reward you greatly.’</p><p>And Abram was honest with God. He said, ‘LORD All-Powerful, you have given me everything I could ask for — except children. When I die, my servant Eliezer will get everything I own.’ Abram didn’t pretend to be fine. He told God exactly how he felt. God doesn’t mind honest prayers.</p><p>God answered, ‘No, your servant won’t. You will have a son of your own.’</p><p>Then God did something wonderful. He took Abram outside, into the night. Picture it: no city lights, no street lamps, just the desert, and above it a sky so packed with stars it looked like spilled sugar. ‘Look at the sky,’ God said, ‘and see if you can count the stars. That’s how many descendants you will have.’</p><p>Can you imagine Abram trying? One, two, three… fifty… a thousand… He’d have given up very quickly. You can’t count them. Scientists today say there are more stars in the universe than grains of sand on every beach in the world — and the God who made every one of them was standing with Abram, making him a promise. And that was the point. God’s promise was bigger than Abram could ever measure. And every single night after this, when Abram looked up, the sky would remind him: God said so.</p><p>Now here comes the most important sentence: ‘Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.’</p><p>Abram still didn’t have a baby. Nothing had changed on the outside. But on the inside, Abram chose to trust God’s word. He believed before he saw.</p><p>And God was pleased with him — not because Abram was perfect, not because he had earned it, but because he trusted God. That’s how it still works today. We are made right with God not by being good enough, but by trusting him — trusting Jesus. That’s what faith is.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Years went by. · 2. “Don’t be afraid, Abram.” · 3. “Look at the sky…” · 4. “That’s how many descendants you will have.” · 5. Abram believed the LORD.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. ‘Accepted him’ / ‘credited to him as righteousness’ — Paul uses this verse in Romans 4:3 to explain faith. Keep it simple: God accepts us when we trust him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Word study: believe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does it mean to ‘believe the LORD’? Look at what Abram had and didn’t have.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What Abram could SEE</th><th>What Abram BELIEVED</th></tr><tr><td>No children</td><td>“You will have a son of your own”</td></tr><tr><td>An old man and an old wife</td><td>God can do the impossible</td></tr><tr><td>A sky full of stars</td><td>Descendants too many to count</td></tr><tr><td>Years of waiting</td><td>God keeps his promises</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith is trusting God’s promise before you can see it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God’s first words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were God’s first words to Abram in the vision?<br>A) “Hurry up!” &nbsp; B) “Abram, don’t be afraid!” ✓ &nbsp; C) “Count the sheep.” &nbsp; D) “Go back home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (15:1). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think God started there?’ (Abram was worried.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was Abram worried about?’ If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why stars?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God took Abram outside to look at the stars, instead of just telling him again?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to give a picture he could remember every night; to show how big the promise was; to show God’s power — he made the stars; a reminder whenever he doubted. Follow-up: ‘What would Abram think every night after this?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What reminders of God’s promises do we have?’ If silent: ‘Why do people keep photos of special moments?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Honest prayers</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abram told God honestly, “You’ve given me everything — except children.” Is it okay to tell God when you’re disappointed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yes! The Bible is full of honest prayers (Psalms). God already knows; honesty brings us closer; Abram was honest AND then believed. Follow-up: ‘Is there a difference between honest and rude?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did God do after Abram was honest?’ (reassured him, showed him the stars). If silent: ‘Do your best friends want you to pretend you’re fine?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is pleased when we trust him — before we see the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I believe you, even when I’m waiting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, look up at the night sky (or out a window) and thank God that his promises are bigger than you can count.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage students to really do this tonight — even just through a window.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Count them!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“See if you can count the stars.”<br>Abram believed the LORD.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch: everyone try to count to the number of stars you can see on a clear night in the countryside (a few thousand!).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Genesis 15:6 chunk by chunk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Please — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like Abram, you can tell God honestly what you’re waiting for. A ‘Please’ prayer can be one sentence: ‘Please, God, help…’ <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, anyone who wants to can pray one short ‘please’ out loud. Silent is fine too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made the stars and counts them all.<br>Prompts: God, you made every star! / God, your promises are bigger than I can count.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you made every star, and your promises are huge!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: any times you stopped trusting God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for doubting you. / Sorry for giving up on prayer.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I stopped trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that he listens to honest prayers.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I can tell you how I feel. / Thank you that you say ‘don’t be afraid’.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that I can tell you how I really feel.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell God one thing you are waiting or hoping for.<br>Prompts: Please, God, help my family with… / Please, God, I’m waiting for…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 55s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us trust you while we wait.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model an honest ‘please’ yourself. Be ready for sensitive requests; follow up privately if needed.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: three mysterious visitors arrive — and someone laughs.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Sarah Laughed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9; 15:1–6; 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Sarah Laughed</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please — A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need — for ourselves and for others — and trusts him with the answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the three visitors who came to Abraham’s tent.</li><li>We are learning that nothing is too difficult for the LORD.</li><li>I can explain why Sarah laughed — and what God said back.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students quoting ‘Is anything too hard for the LORD?’ (CEV: ‘There is nothing too difficult for me’) and linking it to Sarah’s doubt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl and bread as props</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Sarah Laughed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Impossible!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Someone tells you something so unbelievable that you can’t help it…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s the most unbelievable thing someone has told you? Did you laugh?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few fun answers. Land it: ‘Today someone hears news so impossible she laughs out loud — and the person who said it was God.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 18:10–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 18:10</b> “I’ll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 18:11–12</b> Abraham and Sarah were very old… So she laughed and said to herself, “Now that I am worn out and my husband is old, will I really know such happiness?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 18:13–14</b> The LORD asked Abraham, “Why did Sarah laugh?… I am the LORD! There is nothing too difficult for me.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged (‘…’).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Three visitors</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>More years passed. By now God had given Abram and Sarai new names: Abraham, which means ‘father of many’, and Sarah, which means ‘princess’. Imagine being called ‘father of many’ when you have no children! Every time someone said his name, it was a reminder of God’s promise.</p><p>One scorching hot afternoon, Abraham was sitting in the shade at the entrance to his tent, near some great trees. He looked up and saw three men standing nearby. Abraham jumped up — at ninety-nine years old! — ran to meet them, and bowed low.</p><p>‘Please stay,’ he said. ‘Let me get water to wash your feet. Rest under the tree. I’ll bring you something to eat.’ Then Abraham hurried around like a busy chef. ‘Sarah, quick, bake some bread!’ He ran to his herd, chose the best calf, and had a servant cook it. He brought yoghurt and milk. It was a feast for strangers.</p><p>But these weren’t ordinary visitors. The Bible tells us that one of them was the LORD himself, and the others were angels.</p><p>While they ate, they asked, ‘Where’s your wife Sarah?’</p><p>‘She’s right there in the tent,’ said Abraham.</p><p>Then the LORD said, ‘I’ll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.’</p><p>Sarah was listening just behind the tent door. And when she heard that, she laughed. Not out loud — just to herself. ‘Me? Have a baby? I’m worn out, and my husband is old! Will I really know such happiness?’ It sounded impossible. Ninety-year-olds don’t have babies.</p><p>Then the LORD said, ‘Why did Sarah laugh? Does she doubt she can have a child in her old age? I am the LORD! There is nothing too difficult for me.’</p><p>Sarah was frightened. ‘I didn’t laugh,’ she said.</p><p>‘Yes, you did,’ said the LORD.</p><p>But notice: God didn’t take the promise away because Sarah laughed. He didn’t say, ‘Right, no baby for you.’ He simply reminded her who he is. ‘I am the LORD. There is nothing too difficult for me.’ And that sentence is just as true today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A hot afternoon. Three visitors. · 2. A feast for strangers. · 3. “Sarah will have a son.” · 4. She laughed! · 5. “There is nothing too difficult for me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Name changes from Genesis 17:5, 15. Abraham was 99 (Genesis 17:1); Sarah about 90 (17:17). ‘One of them was the LORD’ (18:1, 13) — the other two are called angels in 19:1. Keep visuals reverent.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why laugh?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: Sarah laughed because…<br><i>Answer: …she thought she and Abraham were too old to have a baby. (Genesis 18:12)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>From 18:11–12: ‘Now that I am worn out and my husband is old…’ Follow-up: ‘What did God say back?’ (‘There is nothing too difficult for me.’) Follow-up 2: ‘Did God take the promise away?’ (No.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Three Visitors and a Laugh</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Three Visitors and a Laugh</b> (Genesis 18:1–15). The visitors’ words and Sarah’s words are from Genesis 18 (CEV). Abraham’s rushing about and the servant’s lines are imagined. The visitor who speaks for the LORD uses the ‘voice of God’ style.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Abraham</b> — 99 years old, but rushes about like a chef<br><b>Sarah</b> — Listening behind the tent door<br><b>The LORD (visitor)</b> — The visitor who speaks for God — calm and kind<br><b>Visitor</b> — One of the two angel visitors (one line)<br><b>Servant</b> — Running to cook the feast<br><b>Everyone</b> — The heat, and the giggles</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: fan yourselves — it’s HOT · Everyone: muffled giggles when Sarah laughs<br>Props: Optional: a bowl, some bread<br>Sarah’s last line is imagined. Keep the visitors dignified.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Narrator:</b> One hot summer afternoon, Abraham was sitting by the entrance to his tent.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Phew! So hot! <i>(fan yourselves)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Abraham looked up and saw three men standing nearby.</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Welcome, welcome! Please — rest in the shade! Let me bring water and food! <i>(run and bow low)</i></li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Sarah! Quick — bake some bread! Servant — the best calf! <i>(clap hands)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> Yes, master! Running! <i>(dash off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Abraham served his guests a feast and stood near them under the tree while they ate.</li><li><b>Visitor:</b> Where’s your wife Sarah?</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> She is right there in the tent.</li><li><b>The LORD (visitor):</b> I’ll come back about this time next year, and when I do, Sarah will already have a son.</li><li><b>Sarah:</b> Hee hee… Me? A baby? Now that I am worn out and my husband is old? <i>(hand over mouth, giggling quietly)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hee hee hee… <i>(muffled giggles)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Sarah thinks it’s impossible. Is it? Why or why not? (20s)</li><li><b>The LORD (visitor):</b> Why did Sarah laugh? Does she doubt that she can have a child in her old age?</li><li><b>The LORD (visitor):</b> I am the LORD! There is nothing too difficult for me.</li><li><b>Sarah:</b> I didn’t laugh! <i>(step out, frightened)</i></li><li><b>The LORD (visitor):</b> Yes, you did!</li><li><b>Sarah:</b> …I did. <i>(quietly, looking down)</i></li><li><b>The LORD (visitor):</b> I’ll come back next year at the time I promised, and Sarah will already have a son.</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Sarah… did you hear that? Nothing is too difficult for him! <i>(take her hands)</i></li><li><b>Sarah:</b> Then I’ll stop laughing at the promise — and start trusting it. <i>(small smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Nothing is too difficult for God!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God didn’t take the promise away when Sarah laughed?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God is patient and gracious; his promise depended on him, not on Sarah being perfect.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah said “I didn’t laugh.” Why do people lie when they’re caught?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Fear, embarrassment — like Adam and Eve hiding in Week 2. God already knew; honesty is better.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Too difficult?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God said, “There is nothing too difficult for me.” What does that tell us about God — and about the things that worry us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is all-powerful; he can do impossible things; no problem is too big for him; we can bring anything to him. Note: it doesn’t mean God gives us everything we want — but that nothing is beyond his power. Follow-up: ‘What things seem impossible to you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could this sentence help you when you’re worried?’ If silent: ‘Could God make the sun rise in the west?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Laughing at God’s promises</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham believed God’s promise last time (Genesis 15:6). Sarah laughed this time. Are there times we ‘laugh’ at God’s promises — thinking they can’t be true for us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: thinking God can’t forgive something; thinking God won’t help; thinking our prayers don’t matter; thinking we’re too young or too ordinary. Follow-up: ‘What does God say back?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which of God’s promises do you find hardest to believe?’ If silent: give an example: ‘God says he’ll never leave us — sometimes that’s hard to believe when we feel alone.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There is nothing too difficult for the LORD.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I bring you the thing that feels impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write down (or tell God) one ‘impossible’ thing — a friendship, a worry, a family problem — and pray about it every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep the ‘impossible’ prayers private.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Nothing too difficult</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Will I really know such happiness?”<br>“There is nothing too difficult for me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn the actions for Genesis 15:6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Please — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Holding your hands open is a way of saying, ‘God, I’m handing this to you.’ It helps when you pray about something that feels too big. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands open on your lap. Imagine you’re holding the thing that feels impossible. Breathe slowly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Nothing is too difficult for God.<br>Prompts: God, you can do anything. / God, you keep your promises — even impossible ones.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — there is nothing too difficult for you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a promise of God you’ve laughed at or doubted?<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you can’t help. / Sorry for doubting your promises.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for doubting your promises.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for a time he helped with something hard.<br>Prompts: Thank you for helping me when… / Thank you that you are patient with doubters.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are patient with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With open hands, hand God your ‘impossible’ thing.<br>Prompts: Please, God, help with… / Please help my friend / family member with…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 55s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, we hand you the things that feel impossible.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently. God has heard. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be ready for sensitive ‘impossible’ things (illness, family breakdown). Follow up privately if needed.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a baby is born — and everyone laughs for joy!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Isaac Means Laughter":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 12:1–9; 15:1–6; 18:1–15; 21:1–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaac Means Laughter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Please — A ‘Please’ prayer asks God for what we need — for ourselves and for others — and trusts him with the answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God kept his promise when Isaac was born.</li><li>We are learning how to wait well for God.</li><li>I can retell Abraham’s story and say what it shows about God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can match quotes to speakers, explain why Isaac’s name means ‘laughter’, and recite Genesis 15:6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Isaac Means Laughter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Leave your country, your family, and your relatives and go to the land that I will show you.” — <b>God</b><br>“You have given me everything I could ask for, except children.” — <b>Abram</b><br>“Look at the sky and see if you can count the stars.” — <b>God</b><br>“Will I really know such happiness?” — <b>Sarah</b><br>“There is nothing too difficult for me.” — <b>God</b><br>“God has made me laugh. And now everyone will laugh with me.” — <b>Sarah</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘When did they say it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 21:1–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 21:1</b> The LORD was good to Sarah and kept his promise.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 21:2</b> Although Abraham was very old, Sarah had a son exactly at the time God had said.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 21:3</b> Abraham named his son Isaac.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 21:6</b> Sarah said, “God has made me laugh. And now everyone will laugh with me.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Isaac’ sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘he laughs’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A baby called Laughter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>One year later — exactly when God said — it happened. The LORD was good to Sarah and kept his promise. Sarah had a baby boy!</p><p>Abraham was one hundred years old. Imagine a one-hundred-year-old dad changing nappies! And Sarah, about ninety, held her own son in her arms.</p><p>They named him Isaac, which sounds like ‘he laughs’. Remember how Sarah laughed when she heard the promise? That was a doubting laugh — ‘Yeah, right.’ But now she laughed a completely different laugh. ‘God has made me laugh,’ she said. ‘And now everyone will laugh with me!’ It was a laugh of pure joy.</p><p>It had been twenty-five years since God first said ‘Go’ in Haran. Twenty-five years of waiting. There were times Abraham and Sarah doubted. But God never forgot his promise, not for one day. And Isaac was just the beginning — from him would come a family as many as the stars.</p><p>So when you’re waiting for God, remember Isaac. God’s timing isn’t always our timing. But God keeps his promises.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The LORD kept his promise. · 2. A 100-year-old dad! · 3. From a doubting laugh… to a joyful laugh! · 4. Worth the wait.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. 25 years: Abraham was 75 in Genesis 12:4 and 100 in 21:5.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How old?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How old was Abraham when Isaac was born?<br>A) 25 &nbsp; B) 60 &nbsp; C) 75 &nbsp; D) 100 ✓</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: D — 100 (21:5). Follow-up: ‘How old was he when God first said “Go”?’ (75 — so 25 years of waiting.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does Isaac’s name mean?’ (‘He laughs’.) If silent: point to the story slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two laughs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sarah laughed twice in this story. What was different about the two laughs?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>First laugh (Genesis 18): doubting, ‘that’s impossible’. Second laugh (Genesis 21): joy, ‘God did it!’. God turned her doubt into delight. Follow-up: ‘What changed?’ (God kept his promise.) Follow-up 2: ‘Has God ever turned something sad or doubtful into joy for you?’ If silent: act out the two laughs!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God told Abram to GO — and he obeyed, not knowing where.</li><li>Abram believed God’s star-promise before he saw it — and God accepted him.</li><li>Nothing was too difficult for God: Isaac was born, right on time.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God makes promises and keeps them — even the ones that seem impossible.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Waiting well</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If God doesn’t answer my prayer quickly, it means he isn’t listening.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out: God listened to Abraham for 25 years; ‘not yet’ is different from ‘no’; God’s timing is wise; sometimes God answers differently from what we asked. Follow-up: ‘How did Abraham keep trusting while he waited?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps you wait well?’ If silent: ‘Does a parent saying “not yet” to dessert mean they’re not listening?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Waiting on God. Name one thing you are waiting for or hoping for. Pray a ‘please’ prayer about it every day this week — and trust God with the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“There is nothing too difficult for me.” — Genesis 18:14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Print the cards — there’s space to write the ‘please’. Monday’s check-in asks how it went.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: scramble</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abram believed the LORD, so the LORD was pleased with him and accepted him.” — Genesis 15:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Scramble: put the chunks back in order. The chunks are scrambled — each click puts the next one in order. Then say it together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Joyful laughter</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God has made me laugh!”<br>God keeps his promises — right on time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Please — for each other</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">‘Please’ prayers aren’t only for ourselves. Praying for someone else is one of the kindest things you can do. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Tell them one thing you’re waiting or hoping for (only if you want to). Then you’ll pray for each other.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together, tell God what he’s like from this week.<br>Prompts: God, you keep your promises. / God, nothing is too hard for you.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you keep every promise!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for impatience or doubt.<br>Prompts: Sorry for being impatient. / Sorry for laughing at your promises.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for being impatient.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for a promise he has kept.<br>Prompts: Thank you for keeping your promise to Abraham. / Thank you for answered prayers.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for keeping your promises.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your partner’s ‘waiting’ — one sentence each.<br>Prompts: Please, God, help (name) with… / Please give (name) patience and hope.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 60s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help my friend as they wait.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow students to pray silently for their partner if they prefer.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on the ‘waiting’ prayers. Week 5 — God Is in Control (Joseph).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5: God Is in Control":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37; 39–41; 45; 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In Charge</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">All four in order — Put all four together: Wow (who God is) → Sorry (what I’ve done) → Thanks (what he’s given) → Please (what we need).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Dreams and a Coat</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how jealousy grew in Joseph’s family.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Dreams and a Coat → Not fair! → Genesis 37:3–4, 5–8 → Into the pit → The coat → Who was wrong? → Dreams and a Coat → Pulling the weed → Head · Heart · Hands → Here comes the dreamer → Memory verse peek → All four (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The LORD Was with Joseph</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened to Joseph as a slave and a prisoner in Egypt.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The LORD Was with Joseph → Recap → Genesis 39:2–4, 20–23 → Down, down, down → Spot the pattern → Who was with him? → With him — in prison? → Doing right anyway → Head · Heart · Hands → Not forgotten → Build the verse → All four — out loud</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Pharaoh’s Dreams</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God used Joseph to explain Pharaoh’s dreams.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Pharaoh’s Dreams → Weird dreams → Genesis 41:15–16, 39–41 → From prison to palace → Who gets the credit? → Pharaoh’s Dreams → Looking back → Credit where it’s due → Head · Heart · Hands → Prison to palace → Verse with actions → All four — standing</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>God Meant It for Good</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Joseph forgave his brothers.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>God Meant It for Good → Joseph’s journey → Genesis 45:4–5 · 50:19–21 → “I am Joseph!” → Dream come true → Why forgive? → This week’s Big Idea → Forgive and forget? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Reunited → All four — write it</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Dreams and a Coat":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37; 39–41; 45; 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Dreams and a Coat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">All four in order — Put all four together: Wow (who God is) → Sorry (what I’ve done) → Thanks (what he’s given) → Please (what we need).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how jealousy grew in Joseph’s family.</li><li>We are learning what happened when Joseph’s brothers acted on their jealousy.</li><li>I can explain how jealousy can grow into something much worse.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students tracing how favouritism, boasting and jealousy built up, and naming a way to stop jealousy early.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a colourful jacket or blanket as Joseph’s coat</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Dreams and a Coat</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Not fair!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Your brother or sister gets something brand new… and you don’t.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What feeling shows up first? And what do people sometimes DO with that feeling?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how God kept his impossible promise to Abraham and Sarah. Your challenge: pray the same ‘please’ every day. Did you keep praying? Did anything happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate anyone who prayed every day — answered or not. Then the hook: ‘jealous’, ‘it’s not fair’. What do people do? Sulk, say mean things, break the thing, tell on them. Land it: ‘Today a family lets jealousy grow — and it gets really dangerous.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 37:3–4, 5–8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 37:3</b> Jacob loved Joseph more than he did any of his other sons… Jacob had even given Joseph a fancy coat which showed that Joseph was his favourite son.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 37:4</b> And so Joseph’s brothers hated him and would not be friendly to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 37:6–7</b> Joseph said, “We were out in the field, tying up bundles of wheat. Suddenly my bundle stood up, and your bundles gathered around and bowed down to it.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 37:8</b> Now they hated Joseph more than ever because of what he had said about his dream.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. NZ spelling ‘favourite’ used on screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Into the pit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Do you remember Abraham and Isaac? Isaac grew up and had a son called Jacob. And Jacob had a big family — twelve sons! But Jacob made a big mistake: he had a favourite. Joseph. Jacob loved Joseph more than all his other sons, and he didn’t hide it. He gave Joseph a fancy coat — a special, expensive one — that shouted to everybody, ‘This is my favourite son.’</p><p>How do you think the other brothers felt? The Bible says they hated Joseph and wouldn’t even say a friendly word to him.</p><p>Then Joseph made things worse. He had a dream, and he couldn’t wait to tell his brothers. ‘We were out in the field tying up bundles of wheat. Suddenly my bundle stood up, and your bundles gathered around and bowed down to it!’ His brothers snapped, ‘Do you really think you’re going to be king and rule over us?’ Then he had another dream: the sun, the moon and eleven stars bowed down to him. Now they hated him more than ever.</p><p>Jealousy is like a weed. If you don’t pull it out when it’s small, it grows. And this weed grew and grew.</p><p>One day Jacob sent Joseph to check on his brothers, who were far away looking after the sheep. They saw him coming from a long way off — you couldn’t miss that coat — and they said, ‘Look, here comes the dreamer! Let’s get rid of him. Then we’ll see what happens to his dreams.’</p><p>The oldest brother, Reuben, said, ‘No, don’t harm him. Just throw him into this dry pit.’ Reuben secretly planned to rescue him later. So when Joseph arrived, they grabbed him, ripped off his coat, and threw him into the pit.</p><p>Then, while they were eating their lunch — eating lunch! — some traders came by on camels, heading to Egypt. Another brother, Judah, said, ‘Let’s sell him.’ So they pulled Joseph out and sold their own brother for twenty pieces of silver.</p><p>Joseph was seventeen. Taken from his home, his father, everything he knew, on his way to a foreign country as a slave. It looked like the end of his story. But it wasn’t. God was still in control — even in the pit.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jacob’s favourite son. · 2. “Your bundles bowed down to mine!” · 3. Jealousy grew… and grew. · 4. Thrown into a dry pit. · 5. Off to Egypt.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The brothers’ plan to kill Joseph (37:18–20) is softened to ‘get rid of him’ — adjust to your class. Joseph’s age from 37:2.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The coat</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Joseph’s fancy coat show?<br>A) That he was the oldest &nbsp; B) That he was his father’s favourite ✓ &nbsp; C) That he was a king &nbsp; D) That it was cold</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (37:3). Follow-up: ‘How did the brothers feel about it?’ (They hated him.) Follow-up 2: ‘What two other things made them angrier?’ (His dreams.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who was wrong?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob had a favourite. Joseph boasted about his dreams. The brothers were jealous. Who did something wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>All of them made mistakes — favouritism, boasting, jealousy — but the brothers’ actions (the pit, selling him) were by far the worst. Being provoked never makes cruelty okay. Follow-up: ‘What could each person have done differently?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which is easiest to see in yourself?’ If silent: give each character a ‘do-over’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Dreams and a Coat</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Dreams and a Coat</b> (Genesis 37:3–28). Joseph’s dream, the brothers’ words, Reuben’s and Judah’s plans follow Genesis 37 (CEV, simplified). Some lines are imagined. The brothers’ plan is softened for this age group.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jacob</b> — Old father — loves Joseph best<br><b>Joseph</b> — 17, excited, a bit show-offy<br><b>Reuben</b> — Oldest brother — secretly wants to save Joseph<br><b>Judah</b> — Brother — suggests selling him<br><b>Trader</b> — Merchant on the way to Egypt<br><b>Everyone</b> — The other brothers — grumbling</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as brothers): fold arms and grumble · Everyone: camel noises when the traders arrive<br>Props: Optional: a bright jacket or blanket for the coat<br>Keep the pit moment brief. Some students may relate to sibling conflict — keep it safe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Jacob:</b> Joseph, my son — this fancy coat is for you! <i>(hand over the coat)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Wow! Thanks, Dad! Look at me, everyone! <i>(twirl)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Grrrr. Dad’s favourite. <i>(fold arms)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Listen to my dream! Our bundles of wheat — yours all bowed down to mine!</li><li><b>Reuben:</b> Do you really think you’re going to be king and rule over us?</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Grrrrrr!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Later, the brothers were far away with the sheep. Jacob sent Joseph to check on them.</li><li><b>Judah:</b> Look, here comes the hero of those dreams! <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Let’s get rid of him! <i>(angry mutter)</i></li><li><b>Reuben:</b> Don’t harm him. Just throw him into a well out here in the desert. <i>(thinking: I’ll rescue him later)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Hi, brothers! Dad sent me to — hey! My coat! <i>(coat pulled off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They pulled off his fancy coat and threw him into a dry well.</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Help! Please! Get me out! <i>(from ‘down below’)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The brothers are sitting down to eat lunch while Joseph calls for help. How did jealousy get this far? (20s)</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hrrmph! Clip-clop, clip-clop… <i>(camels arriving)</i></li><li><b>Trader:</b> Spices for Egypt! Anything to trade?</li><li><b>Judah:</b> What will we gain if we hurt him? Let’s sell him to the traders.</li><li><b>Trader:</b> Twenty pieces of silver for the boy. <i>(count coins)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> No! Please! I’m your brother! <i>(led away)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> And the traders took Joseph to Egypt.</li><li><b>Reuben:</b> Where is he? The well is empty! What have you done? <i>(return, shocked)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Joseph was alone, far from home. But he was not alone. God was with him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God was still in control. <i>(quietly)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the brothers’ jealousy grow, step by step?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Hating → not being friendly → plotting → the pit → selling him. Small feelings grew into big actions.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Reuben wanted to rescue Joseph but didn’t speak up enough. What could he have done?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Spoken up clearly, stayed with Joseph, gone for help, refused to go along.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Pulling the weed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jealousy is like a weed — it grows if you leave it. How could you ‘pull out’ jealousy while it’s still small?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: notice it and name it; tell God about it; be thankful for what you have; celebrate the other person; talk to someone; don’t feed it with mean thoughts. Follow-up: ‘What “feeds” jealousy?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could the brothers have done on day one?’ If silent: ‘What do you do with a weed in the garden?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jealousy grows if we feed it — but God can help us pull it out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I tell you honestly when I feel jealous.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you feel jealous this week, say one genuine nice thing to (or about) that person instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise: ‘Nice one — you deserved that!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Here comes the dreamer</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Look, here comes the hero of those dreams!”<br>Jealousy grew… and grew.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Genesis 50:20 — tell students they’ll find out on Thursday who said it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: All four (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we put all four together in order: Wow → Sorry → Thanks → Please. It’s a pattern you can use any time, anywhere. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s be still. This week, notice the order: Wow, Sorry, Thanks, Please.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God was with Joseph, even in the pit.<br>Prompts: God, you are always with me. / God, you are in charge — even when things go wrong.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are with us, even in the pit!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there someone you’ve been jealous of?<br>Prompts: Sorry for feeling jealous of… / Sorry for boasting.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for being jealous.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pull out the jealousy weed by thanking God for what you have.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my family, even when it’s hard. / Thank you for the things I have.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for everything you’ve given me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for anyone who feels alone or far from home.<br>Prompts: Please be with kids who feel alone. / Please help families who argue.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with everyone who feels alone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Name the four steps aloud as you go.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joseph in Egypt — a slave, then a prisoner. Where is God?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The LORD Was with Joseph":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37; 39–41; 45; 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD Was with Joseph</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">All four in order — Put all four together: Wow (who God is) → Sorry (what I’ve done) → Thanks (what he’s given) → Please (what we need).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened to Joseph as a slave and a prisoner in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning what ‘the LORD was with Joseph’ means.</li><li>I can explain how Joseph kept doing what was right in hard places.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students noticing the repeated phrase ‘the LORD was with Joseph’ and connecting it to Joseph’s faithfulness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The LORD Was with Joseph</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday: a coat, two dreams, jealous brothers, a pit…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where was Joseph taken — and how did he get there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>To Egypt, sold to traders for 20 pieces of silver by his own brothers. Ask: ‘If you were Joseph, what would you be thinking on that long walk?’ ‘Today — what happens in Egypt?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 39:2–4, 20–23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 39:3</b> Potiphar realized that the LORD was helping Joseph to be successful in whatever he did.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 39:20</b> Potiphar… threw Joseph in the same prison where the king’s prisoners were kept.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 39:21</b> While Joseph was in prison, the LORD helped him and was good to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 39:23</b> The LORD was with Joseph and made him successful in all that he did.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Spot the repeated idea! Ask students to count how many times God’s help is mentioned.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Down, down, down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Joseph arrived in Egypt, a seventeen-year-old slave in a strange land. Different language, different food, different gods. He was sold to a man named Potiphar, an important official — the captain of the king’s guard.</p><p>Now, Joseph could have sulked. He could have been lazy, or angry, or bitter. He could have spent every day thinking, ‘This isn’t fair. I don’t deserve this. Why should I try?’ Who would blame him? He had been betrayed by his own family. But he didn’t. Joseph made a choice. He couldn’t choose where he was, but he could choose who he would be — and he chose to keep trusting God and doing what was right. Joseph worked hard. He was honest. He did his best in every job.</p><p>And here’s the phrase the Bible keeps repeating: the LORD was with Joseph. Potiphar noticed that everything Joseph did went well. The LORD was helping him. So Potiphar put Joseph in charge of his whole house — every room, every servant, every coin. The slave was now running the place!</p><p>But then things went wrong again. Potiphar’s wife wanted Joseph to do something wrong, and Joseph said no. He said, ‘How could I do such a terrible thing and sin against God?’ He did the right thing. But she was angry, and she told a lie about him. And Potiphar believed the lie.</p><p>So Joseph — who had done nothing wrong — was thrown into prison. Down, down, down. First the pit. Then slavery. Now prison. Have you ever been blamed for something you didn’t do? It feels so unfair, doesn’t it? Imagine that, but locked up in a dark cell, far from home, with no way to prove you’re innocent.</p><p>But listen — the Bible says it again: ‘While Joseph was in prison, the LORD helped him and was good to him.’ God didn’t take Joseph out of prison straight away. But God was with him in prison. The jailer started to like Joseph and trust him. Soon Joseph was in charge of all the other prisoners!</p><p>While he was there, two of the king’s servants — his cupbearer, who served him drinks, and his baker — were thrown into prison too. One night they each had a strange dream. Joseph noticed they looked worried and asked what was wrong. God helped Joseph explain their dreams, and everything happened just as Joseph said. The cupbearer was set free. ‘Please remember me,’ Joseph asked him, ‘and tell the king about me.’</p><p>But the cupbearer forgot. Two whole years went by.</p><p>Two years is a long time. Imagine every morning waking up in that cell, wondering if today would be the day someone remembered. Joseph was forgotten by his brothers. Forgotten by the cupbearer. But never forgotten by God.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Sold as a slave in Egypt. · 2. Joseph worked hard — and did right. · 3. A lie. Prison. · 4. “The LORD was with Joseph.” · 5. Forgotten? Not by God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Potiphar’s wife (39:7–20) is handled very gently — ‘wanted Joseph to do something wrong’. Adjust to your class. Cupbearer and baker from Genesis 40.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Spot the pattern</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph kept going DOWN. But something stayed the same every time. What was it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Where Joseph was</th><th>What God was doing</th></tr><tr><td>In the pit</td><td>Kept him alive (the traders came by)</td></tr><tr><td>A slave in Potiphar’s house</td><td>“The LORD was helping Joseph”</td></tr><tr><td>In prison for something he didn’t do</td><td>“The LORD helped him and was good to him”</td></tr><tr><td>Forgotten by the cupbearer</td><td>Never forgot Joseph</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Wherever Joseph went, the LORD was with him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who was with him?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does Genesis 39 keep saying about Joseph?<br>A) He was always lucky &nbsp; B) The LORD was with him ✓ &nbsp; C) He was very rich &nbsp; D) He wanted to go home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B. Follow-up: ‘How many times can you find it on the scripture slide?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Did “God with him” mean nothing bad happened?’ (No — he still went to prison.) If silent: read the last scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: With him — in prison?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If God was with Joseph, why did Joseph still end up in prison?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Key ideas: God being with us doesn’t mean nothing bad happens; it means we’re not alone in it; God was working out a bigger plan Joseph couldn’t see yet; God helped him in the prison. Follow-up: ‘How did God show he was with Joseph in prison?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might God be with someone going through something hard today?’ If silent: ‘When you’re sick, does having Mum or Dad with you make the sickness go away? What does it change?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Doing right anyway</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph did the right thing — and got punished for it. Is it still worth doing the right thing when it costs you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students wrestle. Draw out: Joseph chose to honour God; doing right is about who we are, not just results; God saw and was with him; in the end, God brought good from it. Follow-up: ‘Can you think of someone who did right and paid a price?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helped Joseph keep going?’ If silent: ‘If you tell the truth and get in trouble, was it still right?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is with me in hard places — even when he doesn’t take the hard thing away straight away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I’m never alone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like Joseph, do your best in a job you don’t like this week — chores, homework, a tough training — as if you’re doing it for God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask students to name the job they’ll do ‘for God’ this week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Not forgotten</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Forgotten by the cupbearer…<br>…but never forgotten by God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Genesis 50:20.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: All four — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Wow, Sorry, Thanks, Please. Today, try saying one sentence out loud in any of the four parts. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray the four steps together. Anyone can say a sentence out loud in any part.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God was with Joseph in prison.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you never leave! / Wow, God, you see everything.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you never leave us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently or aloud: sorry for giving up or sulking.<br>Prompts: Sorry for sulking when things are unfair. / Sorry for not doing my best.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for sulking when things are hard.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for being with you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for being with me at school. / Thank you for helping me in hard times.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for being with me everywhere I go.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for people who are treated unfairly or are in prison.<br>Prompts: Please help people who are blamed unfairly. / Please be with people in prison.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help people who are treated unfairly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Praying for prisoners may be personal for some families — keep it general and kind.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: two strange dreams — and Pharaoh needs Joseph’s help.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Pharaoh’s Dreams":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37; 39–41; 45; 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Pharaoh’s Dreams</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">All four in order — Put all four together: Wow (who God is) → Sorry (what I’ve done) → Thanks (what he’s given) → Please (what we need).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God used Joseph to explain Pharaoh’s dreams.</li><li>We are learning that Joseph gave God the credit.</li><li>I can explain how God turned Joseph’s story around.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students quoting Joseph’s ‘I can’t do it myself, but God can’ and explaining how God raised Joseph up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a paper crown and a ‘ring’ for Pharaoh</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Pharaoh’s Dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Weird dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the weirdest dream you’ve ever had?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Did you wonder what it meant?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few (keep it light and appropriate!). Land it: ‘Today the most powerful man in Egypt has a dream so weird nobody can explain it — except a forgotten prisoner.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 41:15–16, 39–41</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 41:15</b> The king said, “I had a dream, yet no one can explain what it means. I am told that you can interpret dreams.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 41:16</b> “Your Majesty,” Joseph answered, “I can’t do it myself, but God can give a good meaning to your dreams.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 41:39</b> The king told Joseph, “God is the one who has shown you these things. No one else is as wise as you are.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 41:41</b> “You are now governor of all Egypt!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged slightly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: From prison to palace</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Two years after the cupbearer forgot Joseph, the king of Egypt — Pharaoh — had a dream. He was standing by the River Nile, and out of the water came seven fat, healthy cows. Then seven skinny, ugly cows climbed out and ate the fat ones! And the skinny cows were still just as skinny. Then he had a second dream: seven full, plump heads of grain, swallowed up by seven thin, dried-out ones.</p><p>Pharaoh woke up worried. He called all his wise men and magicians. ‘What does it mean?’ Nobody knew.</p><p>And suddenly the cupbearer went, ‘Oh! I forgot! There’s a young Hebrew man in prison. He explained my dream, and it came true!’</p><p>So Pharaoh sent for Joseph. They rushed him out of prison. He shaved, changed his clothes, and stood before the most powerful man in the world. Pharaoh said, ‘I hear you can explain dreams.’</p><p>Now, this was Joseph’s big chance. He could have said, ‘Yes, I’m brilliant at dreams!’ Instead he said, ‘I can’t do it myself, but God can.’ After everything — the pit, the slavery, the prison — Joseph still gave God the credit.</p><p>Then God helped Joseph explain. ‘Both dreams mean the same thing. There will be seven years with plenty of food. Then there will be seven years of famine — no rain, no crops, terrible hunger. God is warning you so you can prepare. Find a wise person to store up grain in the good years, so people won’t starve in the bad years.’</p><p>Pharaoh looked at Joseph and said, ‘Who could do this better than Joseph? The Spirit of God is with him.’ He took off his own royal ring and put it on Joseph’s finger. He dressed him in fine clothes and put a gold chain around his neck. ‘You are now governor of all Egypt! Only I will be greater than you.’</p><p>In one single day, Joseph went from the prison to the palace. Thirteen years after the pit, at thirty years old, the forgotten prisoner was in charge of the greatest country on earth. God hadn’t forgotten. God had been in control all along.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Pharaoh had a nightmare. · 2. Nobody could explain it. · 3. “I can’t do it myself — but God can.” · 4. Seven good years. Seven hungry years. · 5. From prisoner to governor!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Joseph was 30 (Genesis 41:46) — about 13 years after being sold at 17.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who gets the credit?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish Joseph’s sentence: “Your Majesty, I can’t do it myself, but…”<br><i>Answer: “…God can give a good meaning to your dreams.” (Genesis 41:16)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer from 41:16. Follow-up: ‘Why didn’t Joseph take the credit?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Pharaoh notice about Joseph?’ (‘The Spirit of God is with him.’) If silent: point to the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Pharaoh’s Dreams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Pharaoh’s Dreams</b> (Genesis 41:1–43). Joseph’s and Pharaoh’s key words are from Genesis 41 (CEV, simplified). The magicians’ and cupbearer’s lines are imagined, and the cows are definitely imagined!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Pharaoh</b> — King of Egypt — proud but worried<br><b>Magician</b> — Wise man who has no idea<br><b>Cupbearer</b> — Suddenly remembers Joseph<br><b>Joseph</b> — Humble — gives God the credit<br><b>Everyone</b> — Cows, then the royal court</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as cows): ‘Moo!’ — fat cows loud, skinny cows weak · Everyone (as court): bow when Joseph is made governor<br>Props: Optional: a paper crown, a ring<br>Have fun with the cows and the magician, then slow down for Joseph’s answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Pharaoh is asleep in his palace…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> MOOOO! <i>(fat, healthy cows)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> mooo… <i>(skinny, weak cows — then ‘gulp!’)</i></li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Aaah! What a dream! Bring me my magicians! <i>(sit up in fright)</i></li><li><b>Magician:</b> Er… fat cows… skinny cows… it means… you should eat more beef?</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Useless! Does ANYONE know what it means?</li><li><b>Cupbearer:</b> Oh! Your Majesty — I forgot! There’s a young Hebrew in prison. He explained my dream, and it came true!</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Bring him. Now!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Joseph was quickly brought out of jail. He shaved, changed his clothes, and went to the king.</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> I had a dream, yet no one can explain what it means. I am told that you can interpret dreams.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! This is Joseph’s big chance to impress the king. What should he say? (20s)</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Your Majesty, I can’t do it myself, but God can give a good meaning to your dreams.</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> There will be seven years with plenty of food — then seven years of terrible hunger.</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Store up grain in the good years, so your people will not starve.</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Who could possibly handle this better than Joseph? The Spirit of God is with him!</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> I’m putting you in charge of my palace. You are now governor of all Egypt! <i>(put a ring on Joseph’s finger)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Make way for the governor! <i>(bow low)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Thirteen years… and God never forgot me. <i>(look up, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Cupbearer:</b> Sorry I forgot you for two years… <i>(sheepish)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> God didn’t forget. That’s what matters.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God was in control!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Joseph said ‘God can’ instead of ‘I can’?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was humble; he knew his gift came from God; he wanted Pharaoh to know about God.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the two dreams mean?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Seven years of plenty, then seven years of famine — store grain to prepare.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Looking back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look back at Joseph’s story: pit → slave → prison → palace. How was God working, even in the bad parts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the traders took him to Egypt, where he needed to be; Potiphar’s house taught him to manage; prison is where he met the cupbearer; the cupbearer led him to Pharaoh. God used every step. Follow-up: ‘Could Joseph see this while he was in prison?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Does this mean bad things are good?’ (No — but God can bring good out of them.) If silent: draw the steps on the board as a staircase.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Credit where it’s due</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you’re good at something — sport, maths, drawing, music — who should get the credit? Is it wrong to be proud of yourself?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out: our gifts come from God; it’s fine to enjoy doing well and work hard; the danger is boasting or forgetting God. Joseph worked hard AND gave God the credit. Follow-up: ‘How could you give God credit without being weird about it?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How was Joseph different from the boasting teenager in Genesis 37?’ If silent: ‘Who gave you your legs/brain/eyes?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God can use even the bad parts of my story for good.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for my gifts. They came from you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when someone praises you, say thank you — and quietly thank God for the gift too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask students to name a gift God has given them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Prison to palace</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I can’t do it myself, but God can.”<br>From prisoner to governor — in one day!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn the actions for Genesis 50:20.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: All four — standing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You can pray standing up! Standing is a way of showing respect — like standing when someone important walks in. God is the King of kings. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand up. Stand tall and still. We’re praying to the King of all kings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh was powerful — but God is greater.<br>Prompts: God, you are King over every king. / God, you are in control.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are King over every king!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Any times you took credit for what God gave you?<br>Prompts: Sorry for boasting. / Sorry for forgetting you when things go well.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for boasting and forgetting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one gift he’s given you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for making me good at… / Thank you that you can use my whole story.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the gifts you’ve given me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for leaders — of our country, our school, our families.<br>Prompts: Please give our leaders wisdom. / Please help our school leaders.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give our leaders wisdom like Joseph’s.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen! You can sit down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional for students who need to sit.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the brothers come to Egypt — and Joseph has a choice to make.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: God Meant It for Good":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Genesis 37; 39–41; 45; 50:15–21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God Meant It for Good</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">All four in order — Put all four together: Wow (who God is) → Sorry (what I’ve done) → Thanks (what he’s given) → Please (what we need).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Joseph forgave his brothers.</li><li>We are learning how God brought good out of the evil done to Joseph.</li><li>I can explain Genesis 50:20 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can sequence Joseph’s story, explain what forgiveness looked like for Joseph, and recite Genesis 50:20.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: God Meant It for Good</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Joseph’s journey</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Joseph’s journey in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Coat &amp; dreams → Thrown in a pit → Sold to traders → Prison → Explains Pharaoh’s dreams → Governor of Egypt → Forgives his brothers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out: down, down, down… then up!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 45:4–5 · 50:19–21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 45:4–5</b> Yes, I am your brother Joseph, the one you sold into Egypt. Don’t worry or blame yourselves for what you did. God is the one who sent me ahead of you to save lives.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 50:19</b> But Joseph told them, “Don’t be afraid! I have no right to change what God has decided.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 50:20</b> You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 50:21</b> Don’t be afraid! I will take care of you and your children.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Genesis 50:20 is our memory verse — now students know who said it!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: “I am Joseph!”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The seven years of plenty came, and Joseph stored up mountains of grain. Then the seven hungry years came — just as God had said. The famine reached Canaan too, where Joseph’s family lived. So Jacob sent his sons to Egypt to buy food.</p><p>They came before the governor and bowed down to the ground. They had no idea it was Joseph! But Joseph knew them. His dream had come true — his brothers were bowing.</p><p>Joseph could have had his revenge. He had all the power. But after testing whether his brothers had changed — and they had — Joseph couldn’t hold it in. He sent everyone else out and burst into tears. ‘I am Joseph! Is my father still alive?’</p><p>The brothers were terrified. But Joseph said, ‘Come close. Don’t blame yourselves. God sent me ahead of you to save lives.’ Later he said the words of our memory verse: ‘You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people.’</p><p>Then old Jacob came to Egypt, and he hugged the son he thought he had lost forever. God had been in control all along.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The famine came — even to Canaan. · 2. “I am Joseph!” · 3. Forgiveness. · 4. The whole family — saved.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Joseph’s testing of his brothers (Genesis 42–44) is summarised. Important pastoral note: forgiving doesn’t mean pretending harm was okay or staying somewhere unsafe — if someone is hurting you, tell a trusted adult.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Dream come true</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When Joseph’s brothers came to Egypt, what did they do — just like in his dream?<br>A) Ran away &nbsp; B) Bowed down to him ✓ &nbsp; C) Gave him a new coat &nbsp; D) Threw him in a pit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B — his dream of the bowing bundles came true (42:6). Follow-up: ‘Did they recognise him?’ (No!) Follow-up 2: ‘What could Joseph have done with all his power?’ If silent: recall Monday’s dream.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why forgive?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joseph had all the power to get revenge. Why do you think he forgave his brothers instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he saw God’s bigger plan; God had been with him; he loved his family; revenge wouldn’t fix anything; he trusted God to be the judge (‘I have no right to change what God has decided’). Follow-up: ‘Was it easy for Joseph?’ (He wept — no!) Follow-up 2: ‘Does forgiving mean what they did was okay?’ (No.) If silent: ‘What would revenge have looked like?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jealousy grew in Joseph’s brothers until they sold him.</li><li>In the pit, as a slave, in prison — the LORD was with Joseph.</li><li>God turned it around for good — and Joseph forgave.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">People may mean harm, but God is working for good — and he is with us even in the pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Forgive and forget?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Forgiving someone means pretending it never happened.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree is the stronger answer: Joseph named what happened (‘You tried to harm me’), and he tested whether they had changed. Forgiving means letting go of revenge and handing justice to God — not pretending, and not staying unsafe. Follow-up: ‘What did Joseph say about the harm?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who can help us when forgiving is really hard?’ Pastoral: if a student is being harmed, follow up privately.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let it go. Think of one thing someone did that hurt or annoyed you. Ask God to help you forgive them — and let go of getting even.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best.” — Genesis 50:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Remind students: if someone is hurting them, forgiving doesn’t mean staying quiet — tell a trusted adult.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best, so that he could save all these people, as he is now doing.” — Genesis 50:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Can they say Genesis 50:20 as the words disappear?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Reunited</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jacob hugged the son he thought was gone.<br>God was in control all along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: All four — write it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a prayer with four lines: one Wow, one Sorry, one Thanks, one Please. You’ve just written a whole prayer — and you can use this pattern forever! <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a piece of paper. Write W, S, T, P down the side. We’ll write one line for each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">W: Write what God is like from Joseph’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you are in control. / God, you bring good out of bad.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are in control, and you bring good out of bad!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">S: Write a sorry — maybe for holding a grudge.<br>Prompts: Sorry for wanting to get even. / Sorry for being jealous.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for wanting to get even.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">T: Write a thanks.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you were with Joseph — and with me. / Thank you for my family.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you’re with me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">P: Write a please — maybe for help to forgive someone.<br>Prompts: Please help me forgive… / Please help me let it go.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help me forgive.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read your four lines quietly to God. You just prayed a whole Wow-Sorry-Thanks-Please prayer! Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage students to take their prayer home.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on forgiving. Week 6 — The God Who Rescues (Moses).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6: The God Who Rescues":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:22–2:10; 3–4; 5–12; 14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rescuer</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for others by name — God cares about people by name. Praying for someone by name is one of the most loving things you can do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Baby in a Basket</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how God protected baby Moses when God’s people were slaves in Egypt.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Baby in a Basket → Brave and small → Exodus 2:1–6 → Hidden in the reeds → Who found him? → Coincidence? → Baby in a Basket → Small but brave → Head · Heart · Hands → Step out → Memory verse peek → Praying by name (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Burning Bush</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God called Moses from the burning bush.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Burning Bush → Recap → Exodus 3:2–5, 10–12 → A bush that didn’t burn → Excuses and answers → What was strange? → God sees and hears → My excuses → Head · Heart · Hands → Holy ground → Build the verse → By name — out loud</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Let My People Go!</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Moses and Aaron asked Pharaoh to let God’s people go.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Let My People Go! → Stubborn → Exodus 5:1–2 → Ten disasters → Pharaoh’s answer → Let My People Go! → A hard heart → Who is the LORD? → Head · Heart · Hands → Plagues → Verse with actions → By name — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Lamb and the Sea</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about the Passover and how God rescued Israel through the sea.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Lamb and the Sea → True or false? → Exodus 12:13 · 14:21–22 · John 1:29 → Passover and the sea → On the doorframe → The Lamb of God → This week’s Big Idea → Trapped → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → A wall on each side → A song of rescue</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Baby in a Basket":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:22–2:10; 3–4; 5–12; 14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Baby in a Basket</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for others by name — God cares about people by name. Praying for someone by name is one of the most loving things you can do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God protected baby Moses when God’s people were slaves in Egypt.</li><li>We are learning how Miriam showed courage and quick thinking.</li><li>I can explain how God was already planning a rescue.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the basket rescue and noticing God’s hand in the ‘coincidences’ (the princess, Miriam, his own mother as nurse).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a basket and a doll</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Baby in a Basket</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Brave and small</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Can a kid be brave enough to talk to a princess — to save someone’s life?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When have you seen someone young do something really brave?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how Joseph forgave his brothers — ‘God made it turn out for the best.’ Your challenge: let go of one grudge. Was it hard? Did God help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in: invite 1–2 to share (no names of who hurt them). Then the hook: brave kids — standing up for someone, calling 111, telling the truth. Land it: ‘Today a girl about your age stands in the reeds of a river and changes history.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 2:1–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 2:1–2</b> A woman from the Levi tribe… had a baby boy. He was a beautiful child, and she kept him inside for three months.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 2:3</b> When she could no longer keep him hidden, she made a basket out of reeds and covered it with tar.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 2:3–4</b> She put him in the basket and placed it in the tall grass along the edge of the Nile River. The baby’s older sister stood off at a distance to see what would happen to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 2:5</b> One of the king’s daughters came down to take a bath in the river… She saw the basket in the tall grass.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged (‘…’).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Hidden in the reeds</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Remember how Joseph brought his whole family to Egypt to escape the famine? They stayed. They had children, and grandchildren, and great-great-grandchildren. Hundreds of years went by, and Jacob’s family — called the Israelites, or Hebrews — grew into a huge nation, just as God had promised Abraham.</p><p>But then a new king came to power in Egypt — a Pharaoh who didn’t remember Joseph at all. He looked at all these Israelites and got scared. ‘There are too many of them,’ he said. So he made them slaves. He forced them to make bricks and build cities, and he made their lives bitter with hard work.</p><p>And then Pharaoh gave a cruel order: the Hebrew baby boys were not to be allowed to grow up. It was a terrible, frightening time for God’s people.</p><p>In one Hebrew family, a mother had a baby boy. He was a beautiful baby, and she couldn’t bear to lose him. So she hid him. For three months she kept him hidden — imagine trying to keep a baby quiet for three months! But babies get bigger and louder.</p><p>So she had an idea. She made a little basket out of river reeds and covered it with tar so it wouldn’t leak — a tiny boat, just like Noah’s ark, only baby-sized. She laid her son inside, and she placed the basket among the tall reeds at the edge of the River Nile.</p><p>The baby’s big sister, Miriam, hid nearby to watch.</p><p>And who should come down to the river to bathe? Pharaoh’s own daughter, the princess! She saw the basket and sent a servant to fetch it. She opened it… and the baby was crying. Her heart melted. ‘This must be one of the Hebrew babies,’ she said.</p><p>That’s when Miriam was brave. She stepped out of the reeds, walked right up to the princess, and said, ‘Would you like me to find a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby for you?’ ‘Yes,’ said the princess. And Miriam ran and fetched… the baby’s own mum! The princess even paid her to look after her own son!</p><p>When the boy was older, he went to live in the palace, and the princess named him Moses. God was already planning a rescue — and it started with a baby in a basket.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Joseph’s family grew… and grew. · 2. A cruel order. · 3. One mother hid her baby. · 4. His sister Miriam watched. · 5. “Shall I find a woman to nurse him?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Pharaoh’s order (Exodus 1:22) is described gently — adjust to your class. The sister is named Miriam later (Exodus 15:20). The ark/basket link: the Hebrew word for ‘basket’ here is the same word used for Noah’s ark.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who found him?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who found the baby in the basket?<br>A) Pharaoh &nbsp; B) A fisherman &nbsp; C) Pharaoh’s daughter, the princess ✓ &nbsp; D) A crocodile</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (2:5). Follow-up: ‘Why is that surprising?’ (Her father made the cruel order!) Follow-up 2: ‘Who did Miriam fetch to look after him?’ (His own mother.) If silent: re-read the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Coincidence?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The princess came at just the right time, felt sorry for the baby, and his own mum got to look after him. Coincidence — or God at work?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Bible doesn’t even mention God in Exodus 2:1–10 — but his fingerprints are everywhere. God works through ordinary people and ‘coincidences’. Follow-up: ‘Where else have we seen God working behind the scenes?’ (Joseph!) Follow-up 2: ‘Could God be working in your life in ways you don’t notice?’ If silent: list the ‘coincidences’ on the board.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Baby in a Basket</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Baby in a Basket</b> (Exodus 2:1–10). The events follow Exodus 2:1–10 (CEV). Most of the conversation is imagined; the princess’s words ‘This must be one of the Hebrew babies’ are from 2:6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mother</b> — Moses’ mum (Jochebed) — brave and heartbroken<br><b>Miriam</b> — Big sister — watchful and quick-thinking<br><b>Princess</b> — Pharaoh’s daughter — kind-hearted<br><b>Servant</b> — The princess’s maid<br><b>Everyone</b> — The river and the baby!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: the river — gentle ‘shhh-shhh’ water sounds · Everyone: baby crying ‘Waaah!’ when cued<br>Props: Optional: a basket, a doll, a blanket<br>Keep the mood hopeful. Pharaoh’s order is in the background only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Night. A small house in Egypt. A mother rocks her baby.</li><li><b>Mother:</b> Shh, little one. Three months I’ve hidden you… but you’re getting too loud.</li><li><b>Miriam:</b> Mum, what are we going to do?</li><li><b>Mother:</b> I’ve made a basket from reeds and covered it with tar. We’ll trust God with him.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> She placed the basket in the tall grass at the edge of the Nile River.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhh… shhh… shhh… <i>(gentle river sounds)</i></li><li><b>Mother:</b> Miriam, watch him. See what happens.</li><li><b>Miriam:</b> I will, Mum. <i>(crouch in the ‘reeds’)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then the king’s daughter came down to the river to bathe.</li><li><b>Princess:</b> What’s that in the reeds? Bring it to me.</li><li><b>Servant:</b> A basket, Your Highness! <i>(lift it carefully)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Waaaah! <i>(baby crying)</i></li><li><b>Princess:</b> Oh! A baby! This must be one of the Hebrew babies. <i>(softly)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Miriam is watching. The princess has her brother. What should Miriam do — stay hidden or step out? (20s)</li><li><b>Miriam:</b> Your Highness… shall I find a Hebrew woman to nurse the baby for you? <i>(step out bravely)</i></li><li><b>Princess:</b> Yes. Go!</li><li><b>Miriam:</b> Mum! Mum! Come quickly! <i>(run)</i></li><li><b>Princess:</b> Take this child and nurse him for me. I will pay you.</li><li><b>Mother:</b> I will look after him… with all my heart. <i>(hug the baby, trying not to cry)</i></li><li><b>Miriam:</b> She’s paying you to look after your own baby! <i>(whisper, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When the boy grew older, the princess adopted him and named him Moses.</li><li><b>Princess:</b> I pulled him out of the water.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God was planning a rescue!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was brave about what Miriam did?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She was a slave girl speaking to the daughter of the king who gave the cruel order; she thought fast and acted.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does the name Moses mean?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘Pulled out’ — ‘I pulled him out of the water’ (2:10).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Small but brave</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Miriam was young, but God used her bravery to save her brother. How might God use kids your age today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: standing up for someone being bullied; telling a trusted adult when something is wrong; welcoming a new kid; praying for people; helping at home; telling friends about God. Follow-up: ‘What helps you be brave?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where does courage come from?’ If silent: ‘Who in this room has helped someone this week?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God works through ordinary people — even kids — to rescue others.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, use me to help someone this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like Miriam, keep an eye out for someone who needs help this week — and step out of the reeds to help them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask: ‘Who might need someone to step out for them?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Step out</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The baby’s sister stood at a distance…<br>…then she stepped out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Exodus 15:2 — the first song in the Bible!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying by name (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we practise praying for others BY NAME. God knows every person’s name — and he loves it when we bring them to him. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s be still. Think of people you know by name. We’re going to bring them to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God protected baby Moses.<br>Prompts: God, you protect the helpless. / God, you know every person’s name.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you know every person by name!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there someone you’ve ignored when they needed help?<br>Prompts: Sorry for not helping when I could have. / Sorry for staying ‘in the reeds’.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I didn’t help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one person who looks after you — by name.<br>Prompts: Thank you for (name), who looks after me. / Thank you for brave people.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who look after us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one baby or little child you know. Pray for them by name.<br>Prompts: Please keep (name) safe. / Please help (name) grow up knowing you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep the little children we know safe.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it: ‘Please, God, keep my nephew Sam safe.’</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Moses grows up — and meets God in a burning bush.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Burning Bush":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:22–2:10; 3–4; 5–12; 14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Burning Bush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for others by name — God cares about people by name. Praying for someone by name is one of the most loving things you can do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God called Moses from the burning bush.</li><li>We are learning God’s name ‘I Am’ and his promise ‘I will be with you’.</li><li>I can explain how God answered Moses’ excuses.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling God’s promise ‘I will be with you’ and his answer to ‘I’m not a good speaker’.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Burning Bush</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The fire mood has drifting embers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday: a baby, a basket, a brave sister, a princess…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who was the baby — and where did he grow up?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Moses — in Pharaoh’s palace, as the princess’s son. ‘But Moses never forgot he was a Hebrew. And one day, he ran away into the desert. Today, something very strange catches his eye.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 3:2–5, 10–12</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 3:2</b> Moses saw that the bush was on fire, but it was not burning up.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 3:5</b> “Don’t come any closer. Take off your sandals — the ground where you are standing is holy.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 3:10</b> “Now go to the king! I am sending you to lead my people out of his country.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 3:11–12</b> But Moses said, “Who am I to go to the king…?” God replied, “I will be with you.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A bush that didn’t burn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Moses grew up in Pharaoh’s palace. He had the best food, the best clothes, the best teachers in Egypt. But he never forgot that he was a Hebrew. One day he saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew slave, and Moses lost his temper and did something terrible — he killed the Egyptian. When Pharaoh found out, Moses ran for his life, far away into the desert of Midian.</p><p>There he married, had children, and became a shepherd. Forty years went by. The prince of Egypt was now an ordinary shepherd in the middle of nowhere, eighty years old. He probably thought God had finished with him. Have you ever felt like that — like you’ve made too many mistakes, or you’re too ordinary, for God to use you? Moses felt that way. He had a past he wasn’t proud of, a stammer when he spoke, and forty years of nothing but sheep.</p><p>But God hadn’t forgotten Moses. And God hadn’t forgotten his people, still slaves back in Egypt.</p><p>One day Moses was leading his sheep near a mountain called Sinai. And he saw something strange: a bush on fire. Bushes catch fire in the desert sometimes. But this bush was burning and burning — and it wasn’t burning up! The leaves weren’t turning to ash. ‘This is strange!’ said Moses. ‘I’ll go over and see.’</p><p>As he came closer, a voice called from the bush: ‘Moses! Moses!’</p><p>‘Here I am,’ he answered.</p><p>‘Don’t come any closer. Take off your sandals — the ground where you are standing is holy.’ God was there. Moses was so afraid that he hid his face.</p><p>Then God said something amazing: ‘I have seen how my people are suffering. I have heard them crying out. I feel sorry for them, and I have come down to rescue them.’ God sees. God hears. God rescues. All those years of slavery, God had been listening to every cry.</p><p>Then God said, ‘Now go to the king! I am sending you.’</p><p>And Moses started making excuses. ‘Who am I to go to the king?’ God said, ‘I will be with you.’</p><p>‘What if they ask your name?’ God said, ‘I Am. Tell them I Am has sent you.’ God’s name means he always was, always is, and always will be.</p><p>‘What if they don’t believe me?’ God turned Moses’ walking stick into a snake and back again!</p><p>‘But I’m not a good speaker. I’m slow at speaking.’ And God said, ‘Who makes people able to speak? Isn’t it me? Now go! I will be with you and give you the words to say.’</p><p>Every excuse, God answered with himself. Not ‘you’re good enough’, but ‘I will be with you.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Forty years in the desert. · 2. A bush on fire — that didn’t burn up! · 3. “Take off your sandals.” · 4. “I have seen… I have heard… I have come to rescue.” · 5. “Who am I?” — “I will be with you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Moses killing the Egyptian (Exodus 2:11–15) is mentioned briefly — omit if inappropriate for your class. ‘I Am’ from 3:14. God also sent Aaron to help (4:14–16).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Excuses and answers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses made excuse after excuse. How did God answer each one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Moses said…</th><th>God said…</th></tr><tr><td>“Who am I to go to the king?”</td><td>“I will be with you.”</td></tr><tr><td>“What if they ask your name?”</td><td>“I Am has sent you.”</td></tr><tr><td>“What if they don’t believe me?”</td><td>Turned his stick into a snake!</td></tr><tr><td>“I’m not a good speaker.”</td><td>“I will be with you and give you the words to say.”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t say, ‘You’re good enough.’ He said, ‘I will be with you.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was strange?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was strange about the burning bush?<br>A) It was blue &nbsp; B) It was on fire but not burning up ✓ &nbsp; C) It was singing &nbsp; D) It was underwater</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (3:2). Follow-up: ‘What did God tell Moses to take off, and why?’ (Sandals — holy ground.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does “holy” mean?’ (Set apart, completely pure — God’s presence.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God sees and hears</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God said, “I have seen… I have heard… I have come down to rescue.” What does that tell us about how God feels when people suffer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God notices; he cares; he isn’t far away; he acts; he feels sorry for them. Follow-up: ‘Why did it take so long?’ (Honest: we don’t always know; God’s timing.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who might be crying out to God today?’ If silent: ‘How does a parent react when they hear their child cry?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: My excuses</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What excuses do kids make when they know God wants them to do something? Write one down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect: I’m too young, too shy, I’m not good at it, someone else will, people will laugh, I’ll do it later. Follow-up: ‘What would God say back to that excuse?’ (‘I will be with you.’) Follow-up 2: ‘Which excuse do you use most?’ If silent: share one of your own excuses.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God sees, hears and rescues — and he promises, “I will be with you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when I make excuses, remind me that you are with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, do the thing you’ve been making excuses about — and say, “God is with me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage students to name their thing privately.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Holy ground</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Take off your sandals — the ground where you are standing is holy.”<br>God was there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch: what would you feel standing on holy ground?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Exodus 15:2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: By name — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God heard his people crying out. Today we’ll say people’s names out loud to God. Just the name is enough — God knows what they need. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray. In the ‘Please’ part, anyone can say a first name out loud — someone who needs God’s help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s name is ‘I Am’.<br>Prompts: God, you are the great I Am. / God, you see and you hear.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are the great I Am! You see and you hear!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for excuses.<br>Prompts: Sorry for making excuses. / Sorry for thinking I’m too small to matter.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for making excuses.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that he hears every cry.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you hear me. / Thank you that you rescue.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you hear us when we cry out.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say a first name out loud — someone who needs God’s help right now.<br>Prompts: Please help… (name) / Please help… (name)<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 60s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help every person we’ve named.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God has heard every name. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow quiet space. Some names may be sensitive — just receive them.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Moses faces Pharaoh — “Let my people go!”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Let My People Go!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:22–2:10; 3–4; 5–12; 14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Let My People Go!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for others by name — God cares about people by name. Praying for someone by name is one of the most loving things you can do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Moses and Aaron asked Pharaoh to let God’s people go.</li><li>We are learning about the plagues and Pharaoh’s stubborn heart.</li><li>I can explain what a ‘hard heart’ means.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining Pharaoh’s repeated refusals and what a ‘hard heart’ is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a stick for Moses’ staff</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Let My People Go!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Stubborn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever known you were wrong… but refused to admit it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why is it so hard to back down?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take answers: pride, not wanting to lose, embarrassment. Land it: ‘Today we meet the most stubborn king in history. No matter what happens, he won’t back down.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 5:1–2</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 5:1</b> Moses and Aaron went to the king of Egypt and told him, “The LORD God says, Let my people go into the desert, so they can honour me with a celebration there.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 5:2</b> “Who is this LORD and why should I obey him?” the king replied. “I refuse to let you and your people go!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>NZ spelling ‘honour’ on screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Ten disasters</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Moses went back to Egypt with his brother Aaron to help him speak. Imagine it: an eighty-year-old shepherd with a walking stick, walking into the throne room of the most powerful king on earth. Golden columns, guards with spears, Pharaoh high on his throne.</p><p>Moses and Aaron said, ‘The LORD God says: Let my people go.’</p><p>And Pharaoh laughed in their faces. ‘Who is this LORD, and why should I obey him? I refuse!’ Then, to punish them, Pharaoh made the slaves work even harder. Before, they were given straw to make bricks. Now they had to find their own straw — and still make the same number of bricks. The Israelites were furious with Moses. Things had gotten worse, not better. Even Moses was discouraged. He went to God and said, ‘Why did you send me? Since I spoke to Pharaoh, everything’s worse — and you haven’t rescued anyone!’ Have you ever obeyed God and found things got harder at first? Moses knew that feeling.</p><p>But God said to Moses, ‘Now you will see what I will do.’</p><p>So God sent disasters — ten of them. The Bible calls them plagues. First, the River Nile turned to blood. Then frogs — frogs everywhere! In beds, in ovens, in bowls of dough. Then tiny gnats. Then swarms of flies. Then the animals got sick. Then painful sores. Then hail crashing down. Then locusts, millions of them, eating every green thing. Then darkness — thick darkness you could almost feel, for three days.</p><p>Each plague showed that the gods of Egypt were powerless and that the LORD alone is God. The Egyptians worshipped the River Nile — God turned it to blood. They worshipped a frog goddess — God sent frogs until they were sick of them. They worshipped the sun — God turned the sky black. One by one, God showed Egypt who the true God is. Even Pharaoh’s own magicians eventually said, ‘This is the finger of God!’</p><p>And each time, Pharaoh would say, ‘Okay, okay — your people can go!’ But as soon as the plague stopped, he changed his mind. ‘No! They stay!’</p><p>The Bible says Pharaoh’s heart became hard. Like clay that’s left out in the sun — the longer it’s left, the harder it gets, until it can’t be shaped at all. Every time Pharaoh said no to God, his heart got harder.</p><p>Pharaoh had asked, ‘Who is this LORD?’ He was about to find out.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Let my people go!” · 2. “Who is this LORD?” · 3. Blood. Frogs. Gnats. Flies… · 4. A hard heart. · 5. Pharaoh asked, “Who is this LORD?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Plagues summarised from Exodus 7–10. The tenth plague is tomorrow (handled gently). ‘Hard heart’: Exodus 8:15, 32 — Pharaoh hardened his own heart.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Pharaoh’s answer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish Pharaoh’s answer: “Who is this LORD and why should I…?”<br><i>Answer: “…obey him? I refuse to let you and your people go!” (Exodus 5:2)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>From 5:2. Follow-up: ‘What did Pharaoh do next to the slaves?’ (Made them work harder.) Follow-up 2: ‘Name three plagues.’ If silent: point to the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Let My People Go!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Let My People Go!</b> (Exodus 5–10 (selected)). Moses’ and Pharaoh’s first words are from Exodus 5:1–2 (CEV). The plague scenes are summarised and Pharaoh’s other lines are simplified from Exodus 7–10. The magician’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Moses</b> — Old shepherd — nervous but obedient<br><b>Aaron</b> — Moses’ brother — the speaker<br><b>Pharaoh</b> — Proud, stubborn king<br><b>Magician</b> — Pharaoh’s adviser — getting nervous<br><b>Everyone</b> — Frogs, flies, locusts and the dark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: frog croaks ‘Ribbit!’ · Everyone: buzzing flies ‘Bzzzz!’ · Everyone: cover your eyes for the darkness<br>Props: Optional: a stick for Moses’ staff, a crown for Pharaoh<br>Have fun with the plagues, but slow down for the freeze-frame.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The throne room of Egypt. Pharaoh sits high on his throne.</li><li><b>Aaron:</b> The LORD God says, Let my people go into the desert, so they can honour me there.</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Who is this LORD and why should I obey him? I refuse to let you and your people go! <i>(laugh)</i></li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> And now the slaves can find their own straw. Same number of bricks!</li><li><b>Moses:</b> Lord, it’s worse now! Why did you send me? <i>(look up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God told Moses: Now you will see what I will do.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ribbit! Ribbit! Ribbit! <i>(frogs everywhere)</i></li><li><b>Magician:</b> Your Majesty, there’s a frog in your bed. And your soup. And your crown.</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Fine! Get rid of the frogs and the people can go!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But when the frogs were gone…</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Changed my mind. They stay! <i>(fold arms)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Bzzzzzzz! <i>(flies)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Crunch crunch crunch! <i>(locusts eating everything)</i></li><li><b>Magician:</b> Your Majesty, this is the finger of God! Please let them go! <i>(tremble)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Pharaoh has seen frogs, flies, locusts… Why do you think he still keeps saying no? (20s)</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> No! I am Pharaoh! Nobody tells me what to do!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> … <i>(cover eyes — thick darkness)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Darkness covered Egypt for three days. But where the Israelites lived, there was light.</li><li><b>Moses:</b> The LORD says: let my people go.</li><li><b>Pharaoh:</b> Get out! And never come back! <i>(point to the door)</i></li><li><b>Moses:</b> You’re right. I won’t come back.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Pharaoh’s heart was hard. But God was about to rescue his people.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Let my people go!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Pharaoh kept changing his mind?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Pride, stubbornness, didn’t want to lose his slaves, didn’t want to admit God was greater — his heart grew hard.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses complained that things got worse at first. Have you ever obeyed God and had things get harder?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Obeying doesn’t always make things easier straight away; God was still working.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A hard heart</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible says Pharaoh’s heart became hard. What do you think a ‘hard heart’ is — and how does a heart get hard?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: refusing to listen to God; being stubborn; saying no again and again until you stop caring. A heart gets hard by ignoring God over and over. Follow-up: ‘What’s the opposite?’ (A soft, open heart.) Follow-up 2: ‘How can we keep our hearts soft?’ (Listening, saying sorry quickly, obeying.) If silent: use the clay-in-the-sun picture.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is the LORD?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pharaoh asked, “Who is this LORD?” If someone asked you that question today, what would you say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students try: the God who made everything (Week 1); who comes looking (Week 2); who keeps promises (Weeks 3–4); who is in control (Week 5); who sees, hears and rescues (this week). Follow-up: ‘What’s one word that describes God for you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could your life answer that question?’ If silent: brainstorm from the term’s Big Ideas.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Saying no to God again and again makes a heart hard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, keep my heart soft toward you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when you’re told to do something right — by God, a parent or a teacher — do it the FIRST time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Challenge: first-time obedience.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Plagues</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Disaster after disaster…<br>…and still Pharaoh said NO.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn the actions for Exodus 15:2.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: By name — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask your partner: ‘Who can I pray for?’ Then pray for that person by name. You’re carrying someone else’s worry to God for them. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Each of you tell the other one person’s first name you’d like prayer for.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is more powerful than any king.<br>Prompts: God, you are stronger than Pharaoh. / God, you rescue your people.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are stronger than any king!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: any ‘hard heart’ moments?<br>Prompts: Sorry for being stubborn. / Sorry for not listening the first time.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for being stubborn.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one person who helps you — like Aaron helped Moses.<br>Prompts: Thank you for (name), who helps me. / Thank you for brothers, sisters and friends.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who help us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now pray for your partner’s person — by name.<br>Prompts: Please help (name) with… / Please be close to (name).<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 60s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help everyone we’ve prayed for by name.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow silent praying for partners.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the Passover — and the great escape through the sea!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Lamb and the Sea":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 1:22–2:10; 3–4; 5–12; 14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Lamb and the Sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for others by name — God cares about people by name. Praying for someone by name is one of the most loving things you can do.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the Passover and how God rescued Israel through the sea.</li><li>We are learning how the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus is called ‘the Lamb of God’.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can explain the Passover (the lamb’s blood on the door), the sea crossing, and the link to Jesus as the Lamb of God; they can recite Exodus 15:2.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Lamb and the Sea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand for TRUE, sit for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses’ mum hid him in a basket in the river reeds. — <b>True</b> (Exodus 2:3)<br>Pharaoh’s daughter was angry and sent the baby away. — <b>False</b> (She felt sorry for him and adopted him.)<br>The burning bush burned right down to ash. — <b>False</b> (It was on fire, but not burning up!)<br>Moses said he wasn’t a good speaker. — <b>True</b> (God said, “I will be with you and give you the words.”)<br>Pharaoh said yes the very first time. — <b>False</b> (“Who is this LORD? I refuse!”)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stand/sit for each, then reveal. Ask a student to explain each answer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 12:13 · 14:21–22 · John 1:29</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 12:13</b> The blood on the houses will show me where you live, and when I see the blood, I will pass over you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 14:21</b> The LORD sent a strong east wind that blew all night until there was dry land where the water had been.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 14:22</b> The Israelites walked through on dry land with a wall of water on each side.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 1:29</b> John saw Jesus coming toward him and said: Here is the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>From the Passover to the sea to Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Passover and the sea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God told Moses that one final, terrible disaster would come to Egypt — and this time Pharaoh would let the people go. But God gave his people a way to be safe. Each family was to choose a lamb, cook it, and eat it together that night, dressed and ready to leave. And they were to paint some of the lamb’s blood on the doorframe of their house. ‘When I see the blood,’ God said, ‘I will pass over you.’ That’s why it’s called the Passover.</p><p>That night, every family sheltering under the lamb’s blood was safe. And Pharaoh finally said, ‘Go!’</p><p>The Israelites marched out — but Pharaoh changed his mind one last time and chased them with his army. The people were trapped: the sea in front, chariots behind. They panicked. But Moses said, ‘Don’t be afraid! The LORD will fight for you.’ Moses stretched out his hand, God sent a strong wind all night, and the sea opened up. The people walked through on dry ground, with a wall of water on each side!</p><p>Hundreds of years later, John the Baptist saw Jesus and said, ‘Here is the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!’ Jesus is our Passover lamb. When we trust him, we are safe — rescued from sin, forever.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. One last night in Egypt. · 2. “When I see the blood, I will pass over you.” · 3. Trapped at the sea! · 4. The Lamb of God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. The tenth plague (Exodus 12:29) is mentioned only as ‘one final, terrible disaster’. Handle with care; some children may ask directly — answer simply and honestly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: On the doorframe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did each Israelite family put on their doorframe at the Passover?<br>A) A sign saying ‘Hebrews’ &nbsp; B) The lamb’s blood ✓ &nbsp; C) A palm branch &nbsp; D) A candle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (12:7, 13). Follow-up: ‘What did God promise when he saw it?’ (To pass over them.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why is it called Passover?’ If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The Lamb of God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think John called Jesus “the Lamb of God”? How is Jesus like the Passover lamb?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the lamb’s blood kept families safe — Jesus’ death on the cross keeps us safe from sin; the lamb was given in their place — Jesus died in our place; Passover was a rescue from slavery — Jesus rescues us from sin. Follow-up: ‘What do we need rescuing from?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When do Christians remember this?’ (Easter, communion.) If silent: compare the two on the board.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God protected baby Moses — he was planning a rescue all along.</li><li>God saw, heard, and sent Moses: “I will be with you.”</li><li>Through the Passover lamb and the sea, God rescued his people — and Jesus is our Lamb.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God hears his people’s cries and rescues them — and the Passover lamb points to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Trapped</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “When I feel trapped, God can still make a way.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students explain honestly — some may say ‘but sometimes he doesn’t seem to’. Draw out: God made a way at the sea; ‘a way’ isn’t always the way we expect; God promises to be with us (Week 6 Tue) even when the sea doesn’t part. Follow-up: ‘What did Moses say to the frightened people?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can you remember that when you feel stuck?’ If silent: ‘What does it feel like to be trapped?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray by name. Choose three people — someone at home, someone at school, and someone who is going through something hard — and pray for each of them by name every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God hears every cry — and he knows every name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. The take-home card has three lines to write the names.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me.” — Exodus 15:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Can they still say Exodus 15:2 as it fades?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A wall on each side</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD will fight for you.”<br>They walked through on dry land!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A song of rescue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After crossing the sea, Moses and Miriam sang to God! Exodus 15 is the first song in the Bible. We can pray it back to God. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray Exodus 15:2 together. I’ll say a line, you echo it — like Miriam’s choir.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me.<br>Prompts: “The LORD is my strength,” / “the reason for my song!”<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “The LORD is my strength, the reason for my song, because he has saved me!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for forgetting who rescued you.<br>Prompts: Sorry for forgetting you. / Sorry for panicking instead of trusting.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for forgetting that you rescue.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus, the Lamb of God.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for taking away my sin. / Thank you for rescuing me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus — Lamb of God — for rescuing us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the names of your three challenge people to God, silently.<br>Prompts: Please help (name 1)… / Please help (name 2)… and (name 3)…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, rescue and help the people we’ve named.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen! (Maybe even a Miriam-style cheer!)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optional: let students shake imaginary tambourines at the end, like Miriam (Exodus 15:20).</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on prayers by name. Week 7 — God Lives with His People.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7: God Lives with His People":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 16:1–21; 17:1–7; 19–20; 25:8–9; 40:34–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Provider</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanking God for daily needs — Every day God gives us what we need — food, water, home, breath. Noticing it and saying thanks is like gathering manna each morning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Bread from Heaven</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how God fed his people with manna in the desert.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Bread from Heaven → Grumble grumble → Exodus 16:2–4, 14–15 → What is it? → What is it? → Enough for today → What Is It? → Grumbling vs thanking → Head · Heart · Hands → Every morning → Memory verse peek → Thanks for daily needs (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Water from the Rock</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God gave water from a rock at Rephidim.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Water from the Rock → Recap → Exodus 17:1–7 → Is the LORD with us? → Two responses → What did Moses do? → Is the LORD with us? → Testing God → Head · Heart · Hands → Water from the rock → Build the verse → Thanks — out loud</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Mountain Meeting</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God met his people at Mount Sinai.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Mountain Meeting → Rules! → Exodus 19:16–18 · 20:2–3 → Thunder on Sinai → How it began → The Mountain Meeting → Rules from a rescuer → Fence or cage? → Head · Heart · Hands → Ten good words → Verse with actions → Thanks — standing in awe</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>God Moves In</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about the tabernacle — the tent where God lived among his people.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>God Moves In → Who said it? → Exodus 25:8 · 40:34, 38 · John 1:14 → A tent in the middle → Where did God live? → God in the middle → This week’s Big Idea → With us today? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Cloud by day → Thanks before eating</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Bread from Heaven":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 16:1–21; 17:1–7; 19–20; 25:8–9; 40:34–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bread from Heaven</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanking God for daily needs — Every day God gives us what we need — food, water, home, breath. Noticing it and saying thanks is like gathering manna each morning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God fed his people with manna in the desert.</li><li>We are learning the difference between grumbling and trusting.</li><li>I can explain why God gave just enough manna for each day.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining ‘daily bread’ — enough for today — and contrasting grumbling with thankful trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl of rice bubbles or coconut flakes as ‘manna’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Bread from Heaven</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Grumble grumble</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the thing you grumble about most? Lunch? Homework? Being bored?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think grumbling is so easy?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how God rescued his people through the sea. Your challenge: pray for three people by name every day. Did you? Did anything happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate the praying. Then the hook — keep it fun: everyone do a 5-second grumble at once! Then ask why it’s easy: it feels good for a moment, we notice what’s missing, we forget what we have. Land it: ‘Today God’s people become champion grumblers — just weeks after the most amazing rescue in history.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 16:2–4, 14–15</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 16:2</b> There in the desert they started complaining to Moses and Aaron.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 16:4</b> The LORD said to Moses, “I will send bread down from heaven like rain.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 16:14–15</b> The desert was covered with thin flakes that looked like frost. The people had never seen anything like this, and they started asking each other, “What is it?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 16:15</b> Moses answered, “This is the bread that the LORD has given you to eat.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Manna’ sounds like the Hebrew for ‘What is it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: What is it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God’s people were free! They had walked through the sea on dry ground. They had danced and sung with Miriam. But now they were in the desert. And deserts don’t have supermarkets. There were no farms, no bakeries, no corner dairies — and there were around two million people, plus their animals, all needing to eat.</p><p>It only took about six weeks before the grumbling started. ‘We wish the LORD had killed us in Egypt!’ they moaned to Moses and Aaron. ‘At least there we could sit down and eat all the bread and meat we wanted. You’ve brought us out here to starve!’</p><p>Can you believe it? Six weeks earlier they were slaves, crying out to God. Now they were saying slavery was better! When we grumble, we forget all the good things God has done.</p><p>But look at how God responded. He didn’t say, ‘Ungrateful lot — sort yourselves out.’ He said to Moses, ‘I will send bread down from heaven like rain.’</p><p>That evening, flocks of quails — small birds — landed all over the camp, so there was meat to eat. And the next morning, when the dew dried up, the ground was covered with thin white flakes, like frost. The people came out of their tents, picked it up, sniffed it and said, ‘What is it?’ In their language, ‘What is it?’ sounds like ‘manna’ — so that became its name! The Bible says it tasted like wafers made with honey. Delicious!</p><p>Moses said, ‘This is the bread the LORD has given you to eat.’ And God gave some rules with it. Gather just what you need for today. Don’t keep any overnight. And on the sixth day, gather double, because the seventh day is a day of rest.</p><p>Some people didn’t listen. They hid extra manna in their tents just in case. And in the morning? It was stinking and full of worms! Yuck.</p><p>Why did God give just enough for one day? Because he wanted his people to learn to trust him every single day. And for forty years, every morning, the manna was there. Not once did God forget breakfast.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Free! But in the desert… · 2. Grumble, grumble, grumble. · 3. “I will send bread from heaven like rain.” · 4. “What is it?” · 5. Enough for today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Numbers: ‘600,000 men’ (Exodus 12:37) suggests around two million people. Manna rules from 16:16–30; taste from 16:31. Jesus later says ‘I am the bread that gives life’ (John 6:35).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What is it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to manna that people kept overnight (except before the rest day)?<br>A) It turned to gold &nbsp; B) It grew bigger &nbsp; C) It went stinky and full of worms ✓ &nbsp; D) It flew away</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (16:20). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think God made it go off?’ (To teach them to trust him daily.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did manna taste like?’ (Wafers made with honey.) If silent: recall the role-play or story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Enough for today</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God gave manna one day at a time, instead of a whole year’s supply at once?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to teach daily trust; so they’d remember God every morning; to stop greed and hoarding; to keep them close to him. Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus teach us to pray?’ (‘Give us today our daily bread.’) Follow-up 2: ‘What “daily bread” does God give you?’ If silent: ‘Would you remember to thank someone who gave you a year’s pocket money at once, or one who gave you some every day?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: What Is It?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>What Is It?</b> (Exodus 16). God’s and Moses’ key words are from Exodus 16 (CEV). The grumblers and ‘Greedy Gus’ are imagined characters to show what the Israelites did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Moses</b> — Tired leader, trusts God<br><b>Grumbler</b> — Complains about EVERYTHING<br><b>Child</b> — First to find the manna — delighted<br><b>Greedy Gus</b> — Hides extra manna ‘just in case’<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Everyone</b> — The grumbling crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: grumble loudly on cue — ‘Mumble grumble!’ · Everyone: ‘Ewww!’ at the worms<br>Props: Optional: a bowl of ‘manna’ (coconut flakes)<br>If you have ‘manna’ (coconut or rice bubbles), let the child character offer some around — check allergies first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The desert. Six weeks after crossing the sea.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mumble grumble! Mumble grumble! <i>(grumble loudly)</i></li><li><b>Grumbler:</b> Moses! We wish the LORD had killed us in Egypt! At least there we had bread and meat!</li><li><b>Grumbler:</b> You’ve brought us out here to starve!</li><li><b>Moses:</b> You’re not grumbling against me — you’re grumbling against the LORD. <i>(sigh)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I will send bread down from heaven like rain.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The next morning…</li><li><b>Child:</b> Mum! Dad! Come and look! The ground is covered in… something! <i>(run in excitedly)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> What is it? <i>(crowd around, puzzled)</i></li><li><b>Moses:</b> This is the bread that the LORD has given you to eat.</li><li><b>Child:</b> Mmm! It tastes like honey wafers! <i>(munch)</i></li><li><b>Moses:</b> Gather only what you need for today. Don’t keep any till morning.</li><li><b>Greedy Gus:</b> Hmm… just in case… I’ll hide a bit extra in my tent. <i>(sneakily fill pockets)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Gus thinks he’s being clever. What does it show about whether he trusts God? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The next morning…</li><li><b>Greedy Gus:</b> Ugh! What’s that SMELL? My manna is full of worms! <i>(hold nose)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ewwwww! <i>(pinch noses)</i></li><li><b>Child:</b> But look — there’s fresh manna outside again!</li><li><b>Grumbler:</b> …Well. I suppose that’s… quite good. <i>(grudgingly)</i></li><li><b>Moses:</b> Every morning. God won’t forget. Trust him for today.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> For forty years, God sent manna every single morning.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Thank you, God, for daily bread!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was Greedy Gus really saying about God when he hid extra manna?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘I don’t trust God to give me more tomorrow.’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Moses say the people were really grumbling against?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Against the LORD, not just Moses (16:8).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Grumbling vs thanking</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses said, “You’re not grumbling against me — you’re grumbling against the LORD.” What does grumbling say about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Grumbling can say: ‘God isn’t good’, ‘God doesn’t care’, ‘God hasn’t given enough’. Thanking says: ‘God is good, God provides.’ It’s okay to tell God honestly when things are hard (like Abram did) — that’s different from grumbling. Follow-up: ‘What’s the difference between an honest prayer and a grumble?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could you turn one grumble into a thank-you?’ If silent: practise turning ‘I hate this lunch’ into a thanks.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gives us what we need, day by day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for my daily bread.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Grumble swap: every time you catch yourself grumbling this week, swap it for one thing you’re thankful for.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise a grumble swap together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Every morning</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“What is it?”<br>For forty years, God never forgot breakfast.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Exodus 20:2–3 — the start of the Ten Commandments.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks for daily needs (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week’s prayer skill: thanking God for everyday needs — things we usually don’t notice, like food, water, a bed and breath. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s be still. Take a deep breath — that breath is a gift from God too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God fed two million people every day for 40 years.<br>Prompts: God, you never forget what I need. / God, you are generous and faithful.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you never forget what we need!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of something you’ve grumbled about this week.<br>Prompts: Sorry for grumbling about my food. / Sorry for forgetting what you’ve given me.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for grumbling.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for three everyday things — breakfast, water, a bed…<br>Prompts: Thank you for my breakfast this morning. / Thank you for clean water and a warm bed.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for food, water and a bed tonight.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for people who don’t have enough to eat.<br>Prompts: Please feed hungry families. / Please help me share what I have.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give daily bread to people who are hungry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be sensitive — some students may experience food insecurity. Keep it gentle.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: no water — and the people ask, “Is the LORD really with us?”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Water from the Rock":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 16:1–21; 17:1–7; 19–20; 25:8–9; 40:34–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Water from the Rock</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanking God for daily needs — Every day God gives us what we need — food, water, home, breath. Noticing it and saying thanks is like gathering manna each morning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God gave water from a rock at Rephidim.</li><li>We are learning about the question ‘Is the LORD really with us?’</li><li>I can explain how God answered that question.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that God answered ‘Is the LORD with us?’ by providing — and naming ways God shows he is with us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a jug of water and cups</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Water from the Rock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday the people grumbled — and God sent bread from heaven.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was it called, and what did it taste like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Manna — ‘What is it?’ — like wafers made with honey. Ask: ‘What happened to the extra manna?’ (Worms!) ‘Today they have food… but no water.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 17:1–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 17:1</b> They camped at Rephidim, but there was no water for them to drink.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 17:4</b> Then Moses prayed to the LORD, “What am I going to do with these people?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 17:6</b> “Strike the rock with the stick, and water will pour out for the people to drink.” Moses did this while the leaders watched.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 17:7</b> The people had complained and tested the LORD by asking, “Is the LORD really with us?”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 1 abridged.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Is the LORD with us?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been really, truly thirsty? Like after cross-country on a hot day, when your mouth feels like sandpaper and all you can think about is water? Now imagine that feeling — but in the desert, under a blazing sun, with no tap, no drink bottle, and no idea where the next water is coming from.</p><p>God’s people moved on through the desert, following God’s cloud. And they camped at a place called Rephidim. But there was a problem: there was no water. Not a stream. Not a well. Not a puddle. Two million thirsty people, and thousands of thirsty animals. Babies crying. Sheep bleating. Throats burning.</p><p>And do you know what the people did? They didn’t pray. They didn’t say, ‘God gave us bread from heaven — surely he can give us water.’ They grumbled. Again. ‘Give us water!’ they shouted at Moses. ‘Did you bring us out of Egypt just to let us and our kids and our animals die of thirst?’</p><p>It got so bad that the crowd was angry enough to throw stones at Moses. So what did Moses do? He did the best thing anyone can do when things are falling apart. He prayed. He cried out to God: ‘What am I going to do with these people? They’re about to stone me!’</p><p>Notice the difference. The people grumbled about God. Moses talked to God. Same problem, very different response.</p><p>And God answered. ‘Take some of the leaders and go ahead of the people. Take your walking stick — the same one you used at the Nile. I will be standing there in front of you at the rock. Strike the rock, and water will pour out.’</p><p>So Moses walked to a great big rock. All the leaders watched. He lifted his stick and — WHACK! — he struck the rock.</p><p>And water gushed out. Not a trickle — a flood! Clear, cool water pouring out of solid rock in the middle of the desert. People came running with jars and cups and cupped hands. Children splashing, mums and dads drinking, animals lapping it up. Enough for everybody.</p><p>Moses gave that place two names: Massah, which means ‘testing’, and Meribah, which means ‘complaining’. Because the people had tested God by asking, ‘Is the LORD really with us or not?’</p><p>Was he? Of course he was! He had parted the sea. He sent bread every morning. His cloud led them every day. And now he made water flow from a rock. God answered their question — not with words, but with water.</p><p>When life gets hard, we might wonder the same thing: ‘Is God really with me?’ The answer is still yes. Look back at what he has already done — and talk to him, like Moses did.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. No water. · 2. “Give us water!” · 3. Moses prayed. · 4. Strike the rock! · 5. “Is the LORD really with us?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Paul later calls Jesus ‘the rock’ that gave them water (1 Corinthians 10:4) and Jesus offers ‘living water’ (John 4:14) — optional links for older students.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Two responses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Same problem — no water. Compare what the people did with what Moses did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>The people…</th><th>Moses…</th></tr><tr><td>Complained to Moses</td><td>Prayed to God</td></tr><tr><td>Wanted to go back to Egypt</td><td>Asked God, “What should I do?”</td></tr><tr><td>Asked, “Is the LORD really with us?”</td><td>Trusted God to answer</td></tr><tr><td>Forgot the sea and the manna</td><td>Remembered God’s power (the same stick)</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When things go wrong, don’t grumble about God — talk to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Moses do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When the people were angry and thirsty, what did Moses do first?<br>A) Ran away &nbsp; B) Shouted back &nbsp; C) Prayed to the LORD ✓ &nbsp; D) Dug a well</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C — ‘Then Moses prayed to the LORD’ (17:4). Follow-up: ‘What did God tell him to do?’ (Strike the rock.) Follow-up 2: ‘What two names did Moses give the place?’ (Testing and Complaining.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is the LORD with us?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The people asked, “Is the LORD really with us?” What evidence did they already have?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Evidence: the ten plagues; the Passover; the sea parting; the cloud and fire leading them; manna every morning; quail. They forgot! Follow-up: ‘Why do we forget what God has done?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What evidence do YOU have that God is with you?’ If silent: list the Week 6–7 events on the board.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Testing God</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Moses named the place ‘Testing’ because the people tested God. What’s the difference between testing God and honestly asking God for help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Testing: demanding proof, ‘if you’re real, then…’, grumbling, refusing to trust what he’s already shown. Asking: bringing needs to him trustingly, like Moses did. Follow-up: ‘Which one did Moses do?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Can you think of a “testing” prayer and a “trusting” prayer?’ If silent: give two example prayers for the class to sort.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is with us — and when things go wrong, we can talk to him, not grumble about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when I wonder if you’re with me, help me remember what you’ve already done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every time you have a drink of water today, say a silent ‘Thank you, God’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optional: pour everyone a cup of water and drink it together with a thank-you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Water from the rock</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Is the LORD really with us?”<br>God answered with a river from a rock.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Exodus 20:2–3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Saying thanks out loud helps everyone notice God’s gifts. One everyday thing is perfect: ‘Thank you for water.’ <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, in the ‘Thanks’ part, anyone can say one everyday thing out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made water flow from a rock.<br>Prompts: God, you can do anything! / God, you are with us.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: any times you grumbled about God instead of talking to him?<br>Prompts: Sorry for grumbling about you. / Sorry for forgetting what you’ve done.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for grumbling instead of praying.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one everyday thing you’re thankful for!<br>Prompts: Thank you for water! / Thank you for my drink bottle! / Thank you for rain!<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for every everyday gift!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for places that don’t have clean water.<br>Prompts: Please give clean water to people who need it. / Please help people in droughts.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give clean water to everyone who needs it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage a quick ‘thanks’ from lots of students.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: thunder, lightning and a trumpet blast — God meets his people at Mount Sinai.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Mountain Meeting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 16:1–21; 17:1–7; 19–20; 25:8–9; 40:34–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Mountain Meeting</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanking God for daily needs — Every day God gives us what we need — food, water, home, breath. Noticing it and saying thanks is like gathering manna each morning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God met his people at Mount Sinai.</li><li>We are learning what the Ten Commandments are and why God gave them.</li><li>I can explain how God’s rules come from God’s rescue.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students noticing that the commandments begin with ‘I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of Egypt’ — rescue comes first, then rules.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a whistle or recorder for the ‘trumpet’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Mountain Meeting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The storm mood has lightning flashes!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Rules!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine a game with NO rules. No boundaries, no referee, no fouls.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would it be fun? What would happen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take ideas: chaos, people get hurt, cheating, no one knows who wins. Land it: ‘Good rules help us play — and live — well. Today God gives his people ten good rules, in the most dramatic way imaginable.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 19:16–18 · 20:2–3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 19:16</b> On the morning of the third day there was thunder and lightning. A thick cloud covered the mountain, a loud trumpet blast was heard, and everyone in camp trembled with fear.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 19:18</b> Mount Sinai was covered with smoke because the LORD had come down in a flaming fire… and the whole mountain shook.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 20:2–3</b> I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Exodus 20:2–3 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Thunder on Sinai</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Three months after leaving Egypt, the people arrived at a mountain called Sinai. Does that name ring a bell? It was the very mountain where God spoke to Moses from the burning bush! God had said, ‘When you bring my people out, you will worship me on this mountain.’ And here they were. God keeps his promises.</p><p>God told Moses that in three days he would come down and meet his people. They washed their clothes. They got ready. And they were told not to touch the mountain — because God is holy.</p><p>On the morning of the third day — BOOM! Thunder rolled. Lightning cracked across the sky. A thick black cloud covered the mountain. And then a trumpet blast, louder and louder and louder, until everyone in the camp was shaking. Smoke poured out of the mountain like a furnace, because the LORD had come down in fire. The whole mountain trembled.</p><p>This was the God who made the universe — coming to meet with them.</p><p>And then God spoke. Listen to how he began. Not ‘Here are my rules.’ He began: ‘I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves.’</p><p>Do you see? First God reminded them of who he is and what he’d done. He rescued them first. The rules came after. God didn’t say, ‘Obey these rules and then I’ll rescue you.’ He said, ‘I’ve already rescued you — now here’s how to live as my free people.’</p><p>Then God gave ten commandments — ten good words. The first four are about loving God: no other gods, no idols, honour God’s name, and keep a day of rest. The other six are about loving people: respect your parents, don’t murder, be faithful in marriage, don’t steal, don’t lie about others, and don’t be greedy for what others have.</p><p>Later, God wrote them on two stone tablets, and Moses carried them down the mountain. These weren’t rules to spoil the people’s fun. They were like the fence at the top of a cliff — there to keep them safe and free.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Back to the burning-bush mountain. · 2. Thunder. Lightning. A trumpet blast! · 3. “I am the LORD your God, who brought you out.” · 4. Ten good words. · 5. Rules from a rescuer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Simplified list of the commandments from Exodus 20:3–17. ‘Be faithful in marriage’ is the CEV wording of the seventh commandment.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How it began</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish God’s first words at Sinai: “I am the LORD your God, the one who…”<br><i>Answer: “…brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves.” (Exodus 20:2)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>From 20:2 — our memory verse. Follow-up: ‘Why start with the rescue instead of the rules?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What was the very first commandment?’ (No other gods.) If silent: point to the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Mountain Meeting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Mountain Meeting</b> (Exodus 19–20). God’s words are from Exodus 19–20 (CEV, the commandments simplified). The people’s lines are imagined; Exodus 20:19 records them asking Moses to speak to God for them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Moses</b> — Goes up the mountain to meet God<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader — big and clear<br><b>Girl</b> — An Israelite girl — amazed and scared<br><b>Boy</b> — Her brother — trying to be brave<br><b>Elder</b> — An old leader of the people<br><b>Everyone</b> — Thunder, trumpet and the trembling crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: thunder — drum fists on desks · Everyone: a trumpet blast — ‘Toooot!’ getting louder · Everyone: tremble<br>Props: Optional: a whistle for the trumpet<br>Moses’ reply paraphrases Exodus 20:20. Encourage BIG sound effects — then quiet for God’s voice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Mount Sinai. The third morning.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Rumble rumble BOOM! <i>(drum fists — thunder)</i></li><li><b>Girl:</b> Look at the mountain! It’s covered in cloud — and lightning! <i>(point, wide-eyed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Tooooooooot! <i>(trumpet blast, getting louder)</i></li><li><b>Boy:</b> I’m… I’m not scared. Okay, I’m a bit scared. <i>(tremble)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Mount Sinai was covered with smoke because the LORD had come down in a flaming fire.</li><li><b>Elder:</b> Don’t touch the mountain. The LORD is holy.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves.</li><li><b>Girl:</b> He’s the one who rescued us! <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Do not worship any god except me.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Do not make idols. Do not misuse my name. Remember that the Sabbath Day belongs to me.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Respect your father and your mother.</li><li><b>Boy:</b> Uh oh. That includes me. <i>(gulp)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Do not murder. Be faithful in marriage. Do not steal. Do not tell lies about others.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Do not desire to possess anything that belongs to another person.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! God has just given ten rules. Which one do you think kids find hardest? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Rumble rumble BOOM! <i>(thunder)</i></li><li><b>Elder:</b> Moses — you speak to God for us! We’re too afraid!</li><li><b>Moses:</b> Don’t be afraid. God has come to show you how to live — so you won’t sin.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Moses climbed up into the cloud to meet with God.</li><li><b>Girl:</b> The God who made the mountain… came down to talk to us.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Wow.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God came in thunder, fire and trumpet blasts?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: To show his greatness and holiness; so they’d take his words seriously; so they’d know he is real and powerful.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many commandments are about loving God, and how many about loving people?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Four about God, six about people (Jesus summed them up in Matthew 22:37–39).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Rules from a rescuer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God rescued his people FIRST and gave rules SECOND. Why does that order matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: we don’t obey to earn God’s love — he already loves and rescued us; rules are a ‘thank you’ way of living; the rules come from someone who has proved he’s good. Follow-up: ‘What would it be like if the rules came first?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is this like Jesus?’ (He rescues us, then teaches us to live his way.) If silent: ‘Would you rather follow rules from someone who loves you, or a stranger?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fence or cage?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Are God’s commandments more like a CAGE (to trap us) or a FENCE at the top of a cliff (to keep us safe)? Move to a side!<br>A) Cage &nbsp; B) Fence</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students argue it. Draw out: they can FEEL like a cage when we want our own way, but they protect us and others (lying, stealing and greed hurt people). God gave them to a people he had just set free. Follow-up: ‘Which commandment protects you the most?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How did Jesus sum them up?’ (Love God, love your neighbour.) If silent: describe a cliff-top playground with and without a fence.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s good rules come from the God who rescued us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for showing me how to live well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one commandment to practise on purpose this week — like respecting your parents by doing what they ask the first time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students choose their commandment privately.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Ten good words</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Four about loving God…<br>…six about loving people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Learn the actions for Exodus 20:2–3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks — standing in awe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Standing in awe means being amazed at how great God is. Stand up as a way of saying, ‘God, you are holy and powerful.’ <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand. Imagine standing at the foot of Mount Sinai. Be still.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God came down in fire and thunder.<br>Prompts: God, you are holy. / God, you are powerful — and you came close.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are holy, and you came close to us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one of the ten rules you’ve broken this week.<br>Prompts: Sorry for not respecting my parents. / Sorry for being greedy.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for breaking your good rules.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for rescuing you and showing you how to live.<br>Prompts: Thank you for your good rules. / Thank you for my parents.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for rescuing us and showing us how to live.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask for help keeping one commandment this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me tell the truth. / Please help me be content.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us love you and love people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen. Please sit down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God moves into the middle of the camp!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: God Moves In":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Exodus 16:1–21; 17:1–7; 19–20; 25:8–9; 40:34–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God Moves In</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Thanking God for daily needs — Every day God gives us what we need — food, water, home, breath. Noticing it and saying thanks is like gathering manna each morning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the tabernacle — the tent where God lived among his people.</li><li>We are learning what God’s glory-cloud meant.</li><li>I can explain how Jesus is ‘God with us’.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can explain why God wanted a tabernacle, describe the cloud filling it, link it to Jesus (John 1:14), and recite Exodus 20:2–3.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: God Moves In</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The temple mood has golden dust drifting in light.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Call out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We wish the LORD had killed us in Egypt!” — <b>The grumbling people</b><br>“I will send bread down from heaven like rain.” — <b>God</b><br>“What is it?” — <b>The grumbling people</b><br>“What am I going to do with these people?” — <b>Moses</b><br>“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt.” — <b>God</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out the speaker; click to reveal. Ask: ‘What was happening when they said it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Exodus 25:8 · 40:34, 38 · John 1:14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 25:8</b> I also want them to build a special place where I can live among my people.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 40:34</b> Suddenly the sacred tent was covered by a thick cloud and filled with the glory of the LORD.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Exodus 40:38</b> No matter where the people traveled, the LORD was with them. Each day his cloud was over the tent, and each night a fire could be seen in the cloud.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 1:14</b> The Word became a human being and lived here with us.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Traveled’ is the CEV’s spelling — it’s Scripture, so we leave it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A tent in the middle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>At Mount Sinai God gave Moses a building plan. Not for a palace. Not for a fortress. For a tent. ‘I want them to build a special place where I can live among my people.’ It was called the tabernacle — the Tent of Meeting.</p><p>Think about that. The holy God, whose voice shook the mountain, wanted to live right in the middle of the camp, with all the tents of his people around him. Not far away. Not up on the mountain. In the middle.</p><p>Everyone helped build it. People brought gold, silver, coloured cloth, wood and jewels. Skilled workers sewed and carved and shaped. The people gave so much that Moses had to say, ‘Stop! We have more than enough!’</p><p>When it was finished, something amazing happened. A thick cloud covered the tent, and the glory of the LORD filled it — so bright and holy that even Moses couldn’t go in. And from then on, every day the cloud rested over the tent, and every night it glowed with fire. Wherever they travelled, God was with them.</p><p>Hundreds of years later, God moved in even closer. John wrote: ‘The Word became a human being and lived here with us.’ That’s Jesus — God with us, not in a tent, but as a person.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. “Build a place where I can live among my people.” · 2. Everyone helped. · 3. The glory of the LORD filled the tent! · 4. God with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. ‘Lived here with us’ in John 1:14 literally means ‘pitched his tent among us’ — a great link to the tabernacle. Generous giving from Exodus 36:5–7.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did God live?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did God choose to ‘live’ among his people?<br>A) On top of the mountain &nbsp; B) In the tabernacle, in the middle of the camp ✓ &nbsp; C) Back in Egypt &nbsp; D) In the sky only</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (25:8). Follow-up: ‘What filled the tent when it was finished?’ (The glory of the LORD.) Follow-up 2: ‘What could the people see day and night?’ (Cloud and fire.) If silent: re-read the first two scripture lines.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God in the middle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God wanted to live right in the middle of his people — not far away on the mountain?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he loves them; he wants to be close; to lead and protect them; so they could meet with him; to remind them every day he’s with them. Follow-up: ‘How does Jesus show the same thing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where does God live now?’ (Christians believe his Spirit lives in his people — 1 Corinthians 3:16.) If silent: ‘Why do families want to live in the same house?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God provided bread from heaven and water from a rock — every day.</li><li>God gave ten good rules from a rescuer’s heart.</li><li>God moved in — his glory filled the tent — and in Jesus, he came to live with us.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God provides for his people, gives them good rules for living, and moves in to live among them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: With us today?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Israelites could SEE God’s cloud every day. We can’t. How can we know God is with us today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20); his Spirit lives in those who trust him; we see his care in daily provision; the Bible; answered prayers; his people (church). Follow-up: ‘Would it be easier with a cloud? Why might God not do that now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When have you felt God was with you?’ If silent: list the week’s ‘daily needs’ as evidence.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Daily bread. Before every meal this week, stop and thank God for your food — out loud or in your head. And thank him for one other everyday thing each day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God provided manna every morning for 40 years. He still gives us what we need every day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Monday’s check-in will ask how many meals they remembered!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the LORD your God, the one who brought you out of Egypt where you were slaves. Do not worship any god except me.” — Exodus 20:2–3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Exodus 20:2–3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Cloud by day</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Each day his cloud was over the tent…<br>…and each night, a fire.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thanks before eating</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Saying ‘grace’ is a short thank-you prayer before eating. Today we’ll learn a simple one you can use at every meal. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray. In the Thanks part, we’ll learn a simple grace together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God lives among his people.<br>Prompts: God, you are with us. / God, your glory is amazing.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you came to live with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for forgetting to thank God.<br>Prompts: Sorry for eating without saying thanks. / Sorry for taking things for granted.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for forgetting to say thanks.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Learn this grace — repeat after me.<br>Prompts: “Thank you, God, for this food,” / “and for being with us. Amen.”<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for this food, and for being with us. Amen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray that every child in our school has enough to eat.<br>Prompts: Please give every family enough. / Please help us share.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give every family enough food today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen! Use that grace at lunch today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Use the grace at lunch today if your class eats together.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on thanking God for daily needs. Week 8 — The Shepherd-King (David).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8: The Shepherd-King":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 16:1–13; 17; Psalm 23; John 10:11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Shepherd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying a psalm — The psalms are prayers and songs. When we pray them, we borrow words God gave us — and make them our own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>God Looks at the Heart</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how God chose David to be king.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>God Looks at the Heart → Picking teams → 1 Samuel 16:6–7, 12–13 → The one nobody invited → What God looks at → Not invited → God Looks at the Heart → Judging the outside → Head · Heart · Hands → He’s the one! → Memory verse peek → A heart prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The LORD Is My Shepherd</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about David’s life as a shepherd.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The LORD Is My Shepherd → Recap → Psalm 23 → Songs in the fields → Shepherd and God → What David fought → Dark valleys → Sheep?! → Head · Heart · Hands → You are with me → Build the verse → Praying Psalm 23</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>David and the Giant</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how David faced Goliath trusting in God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>David and the Giant → Giants → 1 Samuel 17:32, 37, 45, 47 → One stone → What David took → David and the Giant → Where courage comes from → Is this about me? → Head · Heart · Hands → In the name of the LORD → Verse with actions → A brave prayer — standing</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Good Shepherd</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how David points forward to Jesus, the Good Shepherd.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Good Shepherd → True or false? → Psalm 23:5–6 · John 10:11, 14 → A greater Shepherd → What Jesus said → David and Jesus → This week’s Big Idea → Know his voice → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Whoosh! → Psalm 23 — written</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: God Looks at the Heart":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 16:1–13; 17; Psalm 23; John 10:11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God Looks at the Heart</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying a psalm — The psalms are prayers and songs. When we pray them, we borrow words God gave us — and make them our own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God chose David to be king.</li><li>We are learning that God looks at the heart, not appearances.</li><li>I can explain the difference between how people judge and how God judges.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students quoting or explaining 1 Samuel 16:7 and applying it to how they judge others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small bottle of oil and a horn/cup for Samuel</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: God Looks at the Heart</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Picking teams</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Two captains are picking teams. Who usually gets picked first? Who gets picked last?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are people looking at when they pick?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how God gave manna every morning. Your challenge: thank God before every meal. How many meals did you remember?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first — have fun with the meal-count. Then the hook: tallest, fastest, most popular. People look at the outside. Land it: ‘Today God goes to a family to pick a king — and he picks the one nobody even invited.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 16:6–7, 12–13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 16:6</b> Samuel noticed Jesse’s oldest son, Eliab. “He has to be the one the LORD has chosen,” Samuel said to himself.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 16:7</b> But the LORD said, “Samuel, don’t think Eliab is the one just because he’s tall and handsome… People judge others by what they look like, but I judge people by what is in their hearts.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 16:12</b> As soon as David came, the LORD told Samuel, “He’s the one! Get up and pour the olive oil on his head.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 16:13</b> Samuel poured the oil on David’s head while his brothers watched.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 7 abridged slightly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The one nobody invited</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hundreds of years after Moses, God’s people were living in the land God had promised Abraham. They had asked for a king, and God had given them one — a man called Saul. Saul was tall and handsome and looked exactly like a king should look. But Saul didn’t listen to God. He did things his own way. So God said to his prophet Samuel, ‘I have rejected Saul. Fill your horn with olive oil and go to Bethlehem, to a man named Jesse. I have chosen one of his sons to be king.’</p><p>In those days, a new king was chosen by pouring oil on his head. It was called anointing. It meant, ‘God has chosen you.’</p><p>So Samuel went to Bethlehem and invited Jesse and his sons to a special ceremony. Jesse’s oldest son, Eliab, walked in. He was tall, strong, handsome — the kind of person who gets picked first. Samuel thought, ‘This must be him! He looks like a king!’</p><p>But God said something that changed everything: ‘Samuel, don’t think Eliab is the one just because he’s tall and handsome. People judge others by what they look like, but I judge people by what is in their hearts.’</p><p>So Jesse sent his next son, Abinadab. ‘No, the LORD hasn’t chosen him.’ Then Shammah. ‘Not him either.’ One by one, seven sons walked past Samuel. Seven strong, grown-up, impressive young men. And God said no to every single one.</p><p>Samuel was puzzled. ‘Jesse — do you have any other sons?’</p><p>‘Well,’ said Jesse, ‘there’s the youngest. He’s out looking after the sheep.’ The youngest hadn’t even been invited! He was out in the fields, doing the smelly, boring job nobody else wanted.</p><p>‘Send for him!’ said Samuel. ‘We won’t start until he gets here.’</p><p>So they fetched him. In came a teenage boy, a bit breathless, sunburnt, smelling of sheep, with a sparkle in his eyes. His name was David. And God said, ‘He’s the one! Pour the oil on his head.’</p><p>So Samuel poured the oil on David’s head while his big brothers watched with their mouths open. And from that day on, the Spirit of the LORD was with David in a powerful way.</p><p>God didn’t choose the tallest or the oldest or the most impressive. He chose the one whose heart was turned towards him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Israel needed a new king. · 2. Seven tall, strong sons. · 3. “I judge people by what is in their hearts.” · 4. “Do you have any other sons?” · 5. “He’s the one!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Jesse’s reply and Samuel’s ‘Send for him’ follow 16:11 closely; some wording is simplified. David became king years later (2 Samuel 5).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What God looks at</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to 1 Samuel 16:7, what does God look at when he judges people?<br>A) How tall they are &nbsp; B) How old they are &nbsp; C) What is in their hearts ✓ &nbsp; D) How rich they are</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C. Follow-up: ‘What do people usually look at?’ (What they look like.) Follow-up 2: ‘Where was David when Samuel arrived?’ (Looking after the sheep.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not invited</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">David wasn’t even invited to the ceremony. What does it show about God that he chose the one everyone forgot?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God sees people others ignore; he doesn’t care about status; he values faithfulness in small jobs; nobody is too young or unimportant for God. Follow-up: ‘Where else this term has God chosen unlikely people?’ (Moses the stammerer, old Abraham.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who might feel forgotten in our school?’ If silent: ‘How do you think David felt when he was called in?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: God Looks at the Heart</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>God Looks at the Heart</b> (1 Samuel 16:1–13). God’s and Samuel’s words are from 1 Samuel 16 (CEV). The brothers’ show-off lines are imagined for fun.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Voice of God</b> — Teacher or a strong reader<br><b>Samuel</b> — Old prophet — easily impressed at first<br><b>Jesse</b> — Proud dad of eight sons<br><b>Eliab</b> — Oldest — tall, handsome, confident<br><b>Shammah</b> — Another brother — also confident<br><b>David</b> — Youngest — out with the sheep<br><b>Everyone</b> — The other brothers — and the sheep!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: stand tall and flex when the brothers walk past · Everyone: ‘Baa!’ when David arrives<br>Props: Optional: a cup of ‘oil’ (water) for Samuel<br>Pick a tall student for Eliab for a laugh — and maybe the smallest volunteer for David (check they’re happy with it).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Bethlehem. Samuel arrives at Jesse’s house.</li><li><b>Jesse:</b> Welcome, Samuel! Here are my sons — strong, every one of them!</li><li><b>Eliab:</b> Hello, Samuel. I’m Eliab. The oldest. And the tallest. <i>(stand tall, flex)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> He has to be the one the LORD has chosen! <i>(to himself, impressed)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> Samuel, don’t think Eliab is the one just because he’s tall and handsome. He isn’t the one I’ve chosen.</li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> People judge others by what they look like, but I judge people by what is in their hearts.</li><li><b>Eliab:</b> Wait… what? <i>(deflate)</i></li><li><b>Shammah:</b> My turn! Look at these muscles! <i>(strut past)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> The LORD hasn’t chosen him either.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Us! Pick us! <i>(the other brothers strut and flex)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Jesse, the LORD hasn’t chosen any of these young men.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Seven sons — all rejected. Who do you think God wants? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Do you have any other sons?</li><li><b>Jesse:</b> Well… the youngest. David. He’s out taking care of the sheep.</li><li><b>Eliab:</b> David? He’s just a kid. He smells like sheep! <i>(laugh)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Send for him! We won’t start until he gets here.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Baa! Baa! <i>(sheep noises)</i></li><li><b>David:</b> You called for me, Dad? <i>(run in, out of breath)</i></li><li><b>Voice of God:</b> He’s the one! Get up and pour the olive oil on his head.</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> David — the LORD has chosen you. <i>(pour ‘oil’ on David’s head)</i></li><li><b>David:</b> Me? But I’m only a shepherd… <i>(amazed)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At that moment, the Spirit of the LORD took control of David and stayed with him from then on.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God looks at the heart!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think God saw in David’s heart?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Love for God, trust, faithfulness in small jobs, courage, humility.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think Eliab felt? Is it hard when someone younger is chosen over you?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Embarrassed, jealous (remember Joseph’s brothers!) — a chance to talk about handling disappointment.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Judging the outside</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God judges by the heart. But we can’t see hearts! So how should WE treat people we don’t know well?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: don’t judge by clothes, looks, skills or popularity; give people a chance; get to know them; remember God sees their heart; treat everyone as made like God (Week 1!). Follow-up: ‘When have you been judged by the outside?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can we see a bit more of someone’s heart?’ (Listening, time.) If silent: ‘Is the kid sitting alone at lunch boring or just shy?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God looks at the heart, not the outside.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, make my heart the kind you love.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, choose someone who usually gets picked last — for your team, your game, or your group.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage kindness without making anyone feel like a ‘charity pick’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: He’s the one!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Seven sons — not him, not him, not him…<br>“He’s the one!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Psalm 23:1 — David wrote it!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A heart prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we pray the psalms. Psalm 51:10 is a short prayer David prayed later: ‘Create in me a clean heart.’ You can pray it any time. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s be still. God sees our hearts. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God sees everyone — even the ones left out in the fields.<br>Prompts: God, you see my heart. / God, you notice people others forget.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you see every heart!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there someone you’ve judged by the outside?<br>Prompts: Sorry for judging people by how they look. / Sorry for leaving someone out.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for judging people by the outside.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that he chooses ordinary people.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you chose David the shepherd. / Thank you that you can use me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you choose ordinary people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray David’s prayer for your own heart.<br>Prompts: “Create in me a clean heart.” / Please make my heart love you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, create in me a clean heart.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Psalm 51:10 phrase is quoted in a common form — Psalm 51 itself is for older students.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: David the shepherd boy — and the song he wrote about God, Psalm 23.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The LORD Is My Shepherd":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 16:1–13; 17; Psalm 23; John 10:11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD Is My Shepherd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying a psalm — The psalms are prayers and songs. When we pray them, we borrow words God gave us — and make them our own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about David’s life as a shepherd.</li><li>We are learning Psalm 23 line by line.</li><li>I can explain what it means that God is like a shepherd.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students connecting each line of Psalm 23 to something a shepherd does — and to what God does for them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a picture or toy sheep</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The LORD Is My Shepherd</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God chose a king…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who was chosen — and what does God look at?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>David, the youngest, out with the sheep. God looks at the heart (16:7). ‘Today we go out into the fields with David — and hear the song he wrote there.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Psalm 23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:1</b> You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:2–3</b> You let me rest in fields of green grass. You lead me to streams of peaceful water, and you refresh my life.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:4</b> I may walk through valleys as dark as death, but I won’t be afraid. You are with me, and your shepherd’s rod makes me feel safe.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:6</b> Your kindness and love will always be with me each day of my life, and I will live forever in your house, LORD.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 5 is on Thursday. Read slowly, almost like a song.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Songs in the fields</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Before he was ever a king, David was a shepherd. Every day he led his father’s sheep out into the hills around Bethlehem. Every night he stayed out with them under the stars.</p><p>Let’s think about what a shepherd actually does, because it’s harder than it sounds. Sheep are not very smart. They wander off. They get stuck in bushes. They fall into holes. They don’t know where to find food or water by themselves. And they have no way to defend themselves — no sharp teeth, no claws, not even very fast legs.</p><p>So a good shepherd has to do everything for them. He finds them green grass to eat. He leads them to calm water where they can drink without being swept away. He walks in front, so they know which way to go. He counts them every night, and if even one is missing, he goes looking. And he protects them from wild animals — with his rod, a club for fighting off attackers, and his staff, a long hooked stick for pulling sheep out of trouble.</p><p>David was a brave shepherd. Later, he told King Saul, ‘When a lion or a bear drags off one of my sheep, I go after it and rescue the sheep. The LORD rescued me from the claws of lions and bears.’ Imagine a teenager chasing a lion to rescue a sheep!</p><p>Out there in the fields, David had lots of time to think. He would play his small harp, called a lyre, and make up songs to God. And as he looked at his sheep, he realised something wonderful: ‘This is what God is like! God is my shepherd!’</p><p>So he wrote a song. We still have it in the Bible. It’s called Psalm 23.</p><p>‘You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.’ — just like David made sure his sheep had everything they needed.</p><p>‘You let me rest in fields of green grass. You lead me to streams of peaceful water.’ — God gives us rest, and what we need.</p><p>‘I may walk through valleys as dark as death, but I won’t be afraid. You are with me.’ — even in the scariest places, the Shepherd walks with his sheep.</p><p>‘Your kindness and love will always be with me.’ — following us every day, like a good sheepdog!</p><p>David wasn’t just writing a nice poem. He was saying, ‘I trust God the way my sheep trust me.’ And if a teenage shepherd could look after sheep like that — how much more will God look after us?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Long days. Lonely nights. · 2. A shepherd’s job: · 3. David played his harp and sang to God. · 4. A lion! A bear! · 5. “The LORD is MY shepherd.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. David’s lion-and-bear speech is from 1 Samuel 17:34–37 (CEV, simplified). The sheepdog image for ‘follow me’ is a playful aside — Psalm 23:6 literally says goodness and love will ‘pursue’ us.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Shepherd and God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Psalm 23 compares God to a shepherd. Match what a shepherd does to what God does.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>A shepherd…</th><th>God…</th></tr><tr><td>Finds green grass and calm water</td><td>Gives me what I need — “I will never be in need”</td></tr><tr><td>Walks in front to lead</td><td>“Leads me along the right paths”</td></tr><tr><td>Stays with the sheep in dark valleys</td><td>“You are with me”</td></tr><tr><td>Fights off lions with a rod</td><td>Protects me — “your rod makes me feel safe”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God looks after us the way a good shepherd looks after his sheep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What David fought</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What wild animals did David say he rescued his sheep from?<br>A) Wolves and eagles &nbsp; B) Lions and bears ✓ &nbsp; C) Snakes and scorpions &nbsp; D) Crocodiles</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Samuel 17:34–35). Follow-up: ‘Who did David say really rescued him?’ (The LORD.) Follow-up 2: ‘What’s a shepherd’s rod for?’ If silent: recall the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Dark valleys</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Psalm 23 says, “I may walk through valleys as dark as death, but I won’t be afraid. You are with me.” What might a ‘dark valley’ be for a kid today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: being sick, a family member in hospital, parents arguing or separating, someone dying, being bullied, moving, feeling lonely. Key: the psalm doesn’t say there are no dark valleys — it says God is WITH us in them. Follow-up: ‘How does knowing the Shepherd is there help?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who can be a “shepherd” for someone in a dark valley?’ Pastoral: be alert to students sharing real dark valleys; follow up privately.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Sheep?!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sheep aren’t very clever and can’t protect themselves. Is it insulting to be compared to a sheep? Why might God use that picture?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students react! Draw out: we really do need God more than we think; it’s not about being dumb — it’s about needing care, guidance, protection; the picture is about how good the Shepherd is, not how bad the sheep are. Follow-up: ‘What are some ways people “wander off” from God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does a sheep need to do?’ (Stay close to the shepherd and listen.) If silent: ‘Would you rather be an independent lion or a well-cared-for sheep?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is my Shepherd — he provides, leads, protects and stays with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">LORD, you are MY shepherd.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight before you sleep, say Psalm 23:1 to God: “You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage students to say it tonight — it’s this week’s challenge in miniature.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: You are with me</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I may walk through valleys as dark as death…”<br>“…but I won’t be afraid. You are with me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stretch: how would a sheep feel seeing the shepherd standing between it and the lion?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Psalm 23:1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying Psalm 23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying a psalm: I say a line, you echo it back — but say it TO God. You’re praying David’s song! <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your eyes if you like. We’re going to pray Psalm 23 together. Echo after me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me.<br>Prompts: “You, LORD, are my shepherd.” / “I will never be in need.”<br>Starter: <b>LORD, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sheep wander off. Silently, tell God where you’ve wandered.<br>Prompts: Sorry for wandering away from you. / Sorry for not listening to your voice.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, I’m sorry for wandering away from you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me.<br>Prompts: “You let me rest in fields of green grass.” / “You lead me to streams of peaceful water.”<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, LORD, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, LORD — you let me rest, and you lead me to peaceful water.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo — then silently pray for someone in a dark valley.<br>Prompts: “You are with me.” / Please be with (name) in their dark valley.<br>Starter: <b>Please, LORD, be with…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, be with everyone walking through a dark valley today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your kindness and love will always be with me each day of my life.” Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pause for the echo after each line.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a giant, an army too scared to fight — and a shepherd boy with a sling.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: David and the Giant":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 16:1–13; 17; Psalm 23; John 10:11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">David and the Giant</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying a psalm — The psalms are prayers and songs. When we pray them, we borrow words God gave us — and make them our own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how David faced Goliath trusting in God.</li><li>We are learning where David’s courage came from.</li><li>I can explain what David meant by ‘the LORD will help me’.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that David trusted God (not his own strength), because God had helped him before.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: five ‘stones’ (scrunched paper) and a ‘sling’ (scarf)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: David and the Giant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The storm mood adds drama.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Giants</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Everyone has ‘giants’ — things that seem too big to face.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What are some ‘giants’ kids face?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take answers: a test, a bully, a speech, a sports final, a scary situation at home, fear. Land it: ‘Today a whole army hides from a giant — and a shepherd boy walks out to face him. What does he know that they don’t?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 17:32, 37, 45, 47</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 17:32</b> “Your Majesty,” he said, “this Philistine shouldn’t turn us into cowards. I’ll go out and fight him myself!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 17:37</b> The LORD has rescued me from the claws of lions and bears, and he will keep me safe from the hands of this Philistine.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 17:45</b> You’ve come out to fight me with a sword and a spear and a dagger. But I’ve come out to fight you in the name of the LORD All-Powerful.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 17:47</b> Everybody here will see that the LORD doesn’t need swords or spears to save his people.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. We leave out the battle threats in 17:46.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: One stone</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Israel’s army was camped on one hill. On the other hill was the army of the Philistines, their enemies. And between them was a valley.</p><p>Every morning and every evening, a giant walked out into that valley. His name was Goliath. He was nearly three metres tall — taller than the ceiling of this classroom. He wore a bronze helmet and armour so heavy that you and I couldn’t lift it. His spear was as thick as a fence post. And he shouted across the valley: ‘Send someone to fight me! If he wins, we’ll be your slaves. If I win, you’ll be ours!’</p><p>And King Saul and all the soldiers of Israel? They shook with fear. For forty days, nobody went out.</p><p>Then one day, David arrived. He wasn’t a soldier — he was too young. His dad had sent him with bread and cheese for his three oldest brothers in the army. And just as David got there, Goliath came out and roared his challenge again.</p><p>David couldn’t believe it. ‘Who does this man think he is, insulting the army of the living God?’ His big brother Eliab got cross: ‘Why are you here? Go back to your sheep!’ But David wouldn’t go.</p><p>He went to King Saul and said, ‘Your Majesty, I’ll go out and fight him myself!’</p><p>Saul said, ‘You’re only a boy. He’s been a soldier all his life.’</p><p>But David said, ‘I’ve looked after my father’s sheep. When a lion or a bear came, I went after it. The LORD rescued me from lions and bears — and he’ll keep me safe from this Philistine too.’</p><p>So Saul gave David his own armour. But it was far too big and heavy — David could hardly walk! So he took it off. Instead he picked up his shepherd’s stick, went down to a stream, and chose five smooth stones. He put them in his bag and took out his sling.</p><p>Goliath laughed. ‘Am I a dog, that you come at me with a stick?’</p><p>David answered, ‘You’ve come to fight me with a sword and a spear. But I’ve come in the name of the LORD All-Powerful. Everyone will see that the LORD doesn’t need swords or spears to save his people!’</p><p>Then David ran toward the giant. He put a stone in his sling, swung it round — and let it fly. The stone hit Goliath right in the forehead, and the giant crashed face-down to the ground.</p><p>The whole valley went silent. Then Israel’s army cheered and charged, and the Philistines ran away. One shepherd boy, one stone — and a very big God.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A giant called Goliath. · 2. For 40 days, nobody moved. · 3. “I’ll go out and fight him myself!” · 4. Five smooth stones. · 5. One stone. One giant down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Goliath’s height: about 2.9 m (17:4). The ending is kept at Goliath falling; the rest of 17:50–51 is omitted for this age group.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What David took</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did David take to fight Goliath?<br>A) King Saul’s armour and sword &nbsp; B) A spear and a shield &nbsp; C) His shepherd’s stick, five stones and a sling ✓ &nbsp; D) Nothing at all</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (17:40). Follow-up: ‘Why didn’t he use Saul’s armour?’ (Too big — not what he was used to.) Follow-up 2: ‘In whose name did David fight?’ (The LORD All-Powerful.) If silent: recall the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: David and the Giant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>David and the Giant</b> (1 Samuel 17). David’s and Saul’s key words are from 1 Samuel 17 (CEV, simplified). Goliath’s threat is shortened and the fight ends with the giant falling. The soldiers’ lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Goliath</b> — The giant — a BOOMING voice (stand on a chair, with care!)<br><b>Saul</b> — The king — scared and doubtful<br><b>Eliab</b> — David’s big brother — annoyed<br><b>David</b> — Shepherd boy — calm and confident in God<br><b>Everyone</b> — The trembling army of Israel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as soldiers): shake with fear · Everyone: ‘THUD!’ — stamp feet when the giant falls · Everyone: cheer!<br>Props: Optional: a scarf as a sling, scrunched-paper ‘stones’<br>Goliath can stand on a sturdy chair (supervised) for height — great fun. Keep the fall comic (a stamp), not violent.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The Valley of Elah. Two armies on two hills.</li><li><b>Goliath:</b> Send someone to fight me! Anyone? Are you all cowards? <i>(BOOMING voice)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Eeek! <i>(shake with fear, hide behind hands)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Forty days… and no one will fight him. Not even me.</li><li><b>David:</b> Hi! Dad sent bread and cheese. What’s happening? <i>(arrive with a ‘basket’)</i></li><li><b>Goliath:</b> Send me a man! I’ll fight him!</li><li><b>David:</b> Who does he think he is? He’s insulting the army of the living God!</li><li><b>Eliab:</b> David! Why are you here? Go back to your sheep! <i>(cross)</i></li><li><b>David:</b> Your Majesty, this Philistine shouldn’t turn us into cowards. I’ll go out and fight him myself!</li><li><b>Saul:</b> You don’t have a chance against him. You’re only a boy.</li><li><b>David:</b> The LORD has rescued me from the claws of lions and bears, and he will keep me safe from this Philistine.</li><li><b>Saul:</b> Then… go. And may the LORD be with you.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! A boy against a giant. Where does David’s confidence come from? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> David picked up five smooth rocks and put them in his bag. Then, with his sling in his hand, he went straight toward Goliath.</li><li><b>Goliath:</b> A stick? Do you think I’m a dog? <i>(laugh)</i></li><li><b>David:</b> You’ve come to fight me with a sword and a spear and a dagger.</li><li><b>David:</b> But I’ve come out to fight you in the name of the LORD All-Powerful!</li><li><b>David:</b> Everybody here will see that the LORD doesn’t need swords or spears to save his people!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> David ran toward the giant. He put a stone in his sling, swung it round… <i>(whirl the ‘sling’)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoosh… whoosh… whoosh… <i>(faster and faster)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The stone hit Goliath on the forehead, and he fell face-down on the ground.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> THUD! <i>(stamp feet together)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hooray! The LORD saved us! <i>(cheer)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The army saw a giant too big to beat. What did David see?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: A giant insulting the living God — and a God bigger than any giant.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think David remembered the lions and bears before facing Goliath?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Remembering God’s past help builds trust for the next challenge.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where courage comes from</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">David wasn’t bigger, stronger or better armed. So where did his courage come from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: trusting God; remembering how God helped him before (lions and bears); caring about God’s name more than his own safety; knowing God is bigger than any giant. Follow-up: ‘Is being brave the same as not being scared?’ (No — it’s trusting God while scared.) Follow-up 2: ‘What “lions and bears” has God helped you with before?’ If silent: list David’s reasons from 17:37 and 17:45.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is this about me?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Some people say, “Be like David and you’ll beat your giants!” Others say, “This story is mostly about how great God is.” Which do you think?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both have truth! The main hero is God — ‘the LORD doesn’t need swords’ — David says so himself. We learn from David’s trust, but our confidence isn’t in being like David; it’s in David’s God. Christians also see Jesus as the greater David who defeated the biggest giants — sin and death. Follow-up: ‘Who got the glory at the end?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would it look like to trust David’s God with your giant?’ If silent: ask who the army cheered for.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is bigger than any giant I face.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I trust you with my giant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name your ‘giant’ to God today. Then remember one ‘lion or bear’ he’s helped you with before.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Giants can be private — no sharing needed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: In the name of the LORD</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve come out to fight you in the name of the LORD All-Powerful.”<br>One boy. One stone. One BIG God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Psalm 23:1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A brave prayer — standing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand tall like David in the valley. Your body can say, ‘I’m not facing this alone — God is with me.’ <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand up tall. Imagine your ‘giant’ in front of you — and God standing with you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is the LORD All-Powerful.<br>Prompts: God, you are bigger than any giant. / God, you don’t need swords to save.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are the LORD All-Powerful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: times you’ve been too scared to trust God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for trusting my own strength. / Sorry for hiding like the army.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for forgetting how big you are.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for a ‘lion or bear’ he’s helped you with.<br>Prompts: Thank you for helping me when… / Thank you that you’ve rescued me before.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for every time you’ve helped us before.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name your giant to God.<br>Prompts: Please help me face… / Please be with me tomorrow when…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us face our giants — trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Psalm 23:1 together: “You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” Amen! Sit down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the greatest Shepherd-King of all.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Good Shepherd":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 16:1–13; 17; Psalm 23; John 10:11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Good Shepherd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying a psalm — The psalms are prayers and songs. When we pray them, we borrow words God gave us — and make them our own.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how David points forward to Jesus, the Good Shepherd.</li><li>We are learning what Jesus meant when he said he gives his life for his sheep.</li><li>I can explain how Jesus is the greater Shepherd-King.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can compare David and Jesus as shepherds, explain John 10:11, and recite Psalm 23:1.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Good Shepherd</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand for TRUE, sit for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God chose Eliab because he was tall and handsome. — <b>False</b> (God looks at the heart — he chose David.)<br>David was out looking after the sheep when Samuel came. — <b>True</b> (He wasn’t even invited!)<br>Psalm 23 says, “The LORD is my shepherd.” — <b>True</b> (CEV: “You, LORD, are my shepherd.”)<br>David wore King Saul’s armour to fight Goliath. — <b>False</b> (It was too big — he took a stick, a sling and five stones.)<br>David said the LORD needs swords and spears to save. — <b>False</b> (“The LORD doesn’t need swords or spears to save his people.”)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stand/sit, then reveal. Ask a student to explain each.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Psalm 23:5–6 · John 10:11, 14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:5</b> You treat me to a feast, while my enemies watch. You honour me as your guest, and you fill my cup until it overflows.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 23:6</b> Your kindness and love will always be with me each day of my life, and I will live forever in your house, LORD.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 10:11</b> I am the good shepherd, and the good shepherd gives up his life for his sheep.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 10:14</b> I am the good shepherd. I know my sheep, and they know me.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus is speaking in John 10. NZ spelling ‘honour’ on screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A greater Shepherd</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>David grew up and became king of Israel. He was a shepherd-king — he looked after God’s people the way he had looked after his sheep. He was brave, he loved God, and he wrote many of the songs in the book of Psalms.</p><p>But David wasn’t perfect. When he was older, he made some terrible choices and hurt people. God’s people needed an even better shepherd-king — one who would never fail them.</p><p>And God promised one would come from David’s family. About a thousand years later, a baby was born in David’s town of Bethlehem, from David’s family line. His name was Jesus. And one day Jesus said, ‘I am the good shepherd.’</p><p>Then he said something David never could: ‘The good shepherd gives up his life for his sheep.’ David risked his life fighting lions for his sheep. Jesus actually gave his life on the cross — to rescue us from sin, the biggest giant of all. And then he rose again!</p><p>So when we pray Psalm 23, ‘The LORD is my shepherd,’ we can think of Jesus. He knows us by name. He leads us. He stays with us in every dark valley. And one day, we will live forever in his house.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. David became a great king. · 2. But David wasn’t perfect. · 3. “I am the good shepherd.” · 4. He gave up his life for his sheep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. David’s failures (2 Samuel 11) are mentioned only generally. Jesus as ‘Son of David’ — Matthew 1:1.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What Jesus said</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In John 10, what does Jesus say the good shepherd does?<br>A) Sells his sheep &nbsp; B) Gives up his life for his sheep ✓ &nbsp; C) Sleeps all day &nbsp; D) Lets the wolves in</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (John 10:11). Follow-up: ‘How did Jesus do that?’ (He died on the cross.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does Jesus say about knowing his sheep?’ (‘I know my sheep, and they know me.’) If silent: re-read the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: David and Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is Jesus like David? How is Jesus even greater than David?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Like: both shepherds; both from Bethlehem; both kings; both rescuers; both brave. Greater: Jesus never sinned; David risked his life — Jesus gave his; David beat a giant — Jesus beat sin and death; David’s kingdom ended — Jesus’ never ends. Follow-up: ‘Which “giant” did Jesus defeat?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does it mean that Jesus knows you by name?’ If silent: make a two-column list together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God chose David the shepherd — because God looks at the heart.</li><li>Psalm 23: the LORD is my Shepherd — he provides, leads, protects and stays.</li><li>David trusted God against a giant — and Jesus is the greater Shepherd-King.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God looks at the heart — and he is the Good Shepherd who protects and leads his people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Know his voice</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said his sheep know him and listen to his voice (John 10:4, 14). How can a kid today ‘hear’ Jesus’ voice?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: reading the Bible; prayer; quiet time; listening to wise Christians (parents, teachers, church); our conscience and the Holy Spirit; noticing God’s care. Not usually an audible voice! Follow-up: ‘How do sheep learn their shepherd’s voice?’ (Spending lots of time with him.) Follow-up 2: ‘What could help you spend time with Jesus this week?’ If silent: ‘How do you recognise your mum’s voice on the phone?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Shepherd at night. Every night this week before you sleep, pray Psalm 23:1 — then tell the Good Shepherd one thing you need him to look after.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the good shepherd. I know my sheep, and they know me.” — John 10:14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Take-home card includes Psalm 23:1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You, LORD, are my shepherd. I will never be in need.” — Psalm 23:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Psalm 23:1. Bonus: can anyone say Psalm 23:2 as well?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Whoosh!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">David beat a giant…<br>…Jesus beat the biggest giants of all: sin and death.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Psalm 23 — written</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make Psalm 23 your own: write it with your own words. ‘You, LORD, are my shepherd. You give me…’ <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On paper, write: ‘You, LORD, are my shepherd.’ Then we’ll add our own lines.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write: ‘You, LORD, are my shepherd.’ Add one thing he’s like.<br>Prompts: You, LORD, are my shepherd. You are kind. / You know my name.<br>Starter: <b>You, LORD, are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “You, LORD, are my shepherd. You know my name!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write: ‘Sorry for when I wander…’<br>Prompts: Sorry for wandering away. / Sorry for not listening to your voice.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, I’m sorry for when I wander.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own ‘green grass and peaceful water’ — what has God given you?<br>Prompts: You give me my family. / You give me rest and food.<br>Starter: <b>You give me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Good Shepherd, for everything you give us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one ‘dark valley’ you’d like him to walk through with you.<br>Prompts: Please walk with me through… / Please stay with (name).<br>Starter: <b>Please be with me when…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Good Shepherd, please walk with us through every dark valley.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read your psalm quietly to God. Take it home and pray it every night. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their personal Psalm 23.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on praying Psalm 23. Week 9 — Promises Spoken Long Ago.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9: Promises Spoken Long Ago":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaiah 9:2–7; 53:4–6; Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:1–12; Jeremiah 31:31–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Promise-Keeper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying God’s promises back to him — When you find a promise in the Bible, you can pray it back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It’s like reminding him — and reminding yourself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Child Is Born</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning what a prophet is and what Isaiah promised.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Child Is Born → Spoilers! → Isaiah 9:2, 6–7 → A voice in the dark → What is a prophet? → Four names → A Prophet in the Marketplace → Waiting 700 years → Head · Heart · Hands → A great light → Memory verse peek → Promise prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Servant Who Carries Our Wrongs</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning Isaiah’s promise of a servant who would suffer for others.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Servant Who Carries Our Wrongs → Recap → Isaiah 53:4–6 → A surprising King → Isaiah and Jesus → Like sheep → For us → The quiet years → Head · Heart · Hands → Waiting → Build the verse → Thank-you prayer — hands open</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Wise Men Ask Directions</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the wise men followed a star to find Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Wise Men Ask Directions → Lost! → Matthew 2:1–2, 4–6 → Follow the star → Where? → Wise Men Ask Directions → Two kings → Knowing vs going → Head · Heart · Hands → Thrilled → Verse with actions → Promise prayer — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Who Is the Promise About?</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about God’s promise of a new agreement in Jeremiah 31.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Who Is the Promise About? → True or false? → Jeremiah 31:31, 33–34 → All about Jesus → Written where? → Who is it about? → This week’s Big Idea → Promises for us? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Little Bethlehem → Promise prayers — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A Child Is Born":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaiah 9:2–7; 53:4–6; Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:1–12; Jeremiah 31:31–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Child Is Born</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying God’s promises back to him — When you find a promise in the Bible, you can pray it back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It’s like reminding him — and reminding yourself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a prophet is and what Isaiah promised.</li><li>We are learning the names Isaiah gave the promised child.</li><li>I can explain how Isaiah’s promise points to Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining what a prophet is and linking Isaiah 9:6 names to Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a rolled-up paper ‘scroll’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Child Is Born</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Spoilers!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine someone told you how a movie would end — 700 years before it was even made.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you believe them? What would it take?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met David the shepherd-king — and Jesus, the Good Shepherd. Your challenge: pray Psalm 23:1 every night. Can someone say it for us? Did you remember?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: have a volunteer say Psalm 23:1. Then the hook: it would take it actually coming true! Land it: ‘This week we hear promises God spoke hundreds of years before Jesus was born — and watch them come true.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Isaiah 9:2, 6–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 9:2</b> Those who walked in the dark have seen a bright light. And it shines upon everyone who lives in the land of darkest shadows.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 9:6</b> A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 9:6</b> His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 9:7</b> His power will never end; peace will last forever. He will rule David’s kingdom.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Isaiah 9:6 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A voice in the dark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After King David, God’s people had many more kings. Some of them loved God. But many didn’t. They worshipped idols, treated poor people cruelly, and forgot the God who had rescued them from Egypt. Enemy armies were growing stronger. It was a dark, frightening time.</p><p>So God sent messengers. The Bible calls them prophets. A prophet wasn’t a fortune teller. A prophet was someone God gave a message to, so they could tell the people. Sometimes the message was a warning: ‘Turn back to God!’ And sometimes it was a promise — a promise about the future, so that people would have hope.</p><p>One of the greatest prophets was a man called Isaiah. He lived about seven hundred years before Jesus was born. Imagine Isaiah standing in a busy marketplace in Jerusalem, surrounded by people buying bread and arguing about prices, and lifting his voice: ‘Those who walked in the dark have seen a bright light!’</p><p>The people must have looked up, puzzled. Light? What light? Things were getting darker!</p><p>But Isaiah kept going. ‘A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler.’ A king was coming — not just any king. Listen to his names: ‘Wonderful Advisor’ — he will always know what to do. ‘Mighty God’ — this child would somehow be God himself! ‘Eternal Father’ — he will care for his people forever. ‘Prince of Peace’ — he will bring real peace, between people and God, and between people and each other.</p><p>And Isaiah said, ‘His power will never end; peace will last forever. He will rule David’s kingdom.’ So this child would come from King David’s family — just like God had promised David.</p><p>People waited. A hundred years. Two hundred. Five hundred. Isaiah died. His words were written on scrolls and copied again and again. Did people wonder if God had forgotten?</p><p>But about seven hundred years later, in David’s little town of Bethlehem, a baby was born. And when people met Jesus, they realised: this is the child Isaiah was talking about. The light had come into the darkness. God had never forgotten his promise.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Dark days for God’s people. · 2. God sent messengers called prophets. · 3. “A great light is coming!” · 4. “A child has been born for us.” · 5. 700 years later…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Isaiah prophesied about 740–700 BC. The marketplace scene is imagined; the words are Isaiah 9 (CEV). Matthew 4:16 quotes Isaiah 9:2 about Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What is a prophet?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is a prophet?<br>A) A fortune teller &nbsp; B) Someone who gives God’s messages to people ✓ &nbsp; C) A king’s soldier &nbsp; D) A kind of priest who sacrifices animals</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B. Follow-up: ‘Name the prophet in today’s story.’ (Isaiah.) Follow-up 2: ‘How long before Jesus did Isaiah live?’ (About 700 years.) If silent: recall Samuel from last week — he was a prophet too!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Four names</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah gave the promised child four names: Wonderful Advisor, Mighty God, Eternal Father, Prince of Peace. Choose one. What does it tell us about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Wonderful Advisor — wise, always knows the right way; Mighty God — Jesus is God himself, powerful; Eternal Father — loves and cares forever; Prince of Peace — brings peace with God and between people. Follow-up: ‘Which name do you need most right now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where in Jesus’ life do we see that name?’ (e.g., calming the storm — Mighty God.) If silent: assign one name per table.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: A Prophet in the Marketplace</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>A Prophet in the Marketplace</b> (Isaiah 9:2–7). This scene is a dramatisation: the marketplace and the crowd’s lines are imagined. Isaiah’s words are from Isaiah 9 (CEV).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Isaiah</b> — The prophet — bold and hopeful<br><b>Merchant</b> — Busy fruit seller — sceptical<br><b>Mother</b> — Worried about the future<br><b>Child</b> — Curious — asks the best questions<br><b>Soldier</b> — Thinks only armies can save them<br><b>Everyone</b> — The busy marketplace crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: busy market noise — ‘Fresh bread! Figs! Fish!’ · Everyone: gasp at ‘Mighty God’<br>Props: Optional: a paper scroll for Isaiah<br>Remind students the crowd lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Jerusalem, about 700 years before Jesus. A busy marketplace.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Fresh bread! Figs! Fish! Cheap sandals! <i>(busy market noise)</i></li><li><b>Mother:</b> Did you hear? Enemy armies are getting closer. I’m scared for my children.</li><li><b>Soldier:</b> We need more soldiers. More swords. That’s the only hope.</li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> Listen! Listen, everyone! I have a message from the LORD! <i>(stand on a step, hold up a scroll)</i></li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Oh no, it’s Isaiah again. <i>(roll eyes)</i></li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> Those who walked in the dark have seen a bright light!</li><li><b>Child:</b> Mum, what light? It’s all dark and scary.</li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler.</li><li><b>Soldier:</b> A baby? We need an army, not a baby! <i>(scoff)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The soldier wants an army. God promises a baby. Why might God’s plan be so different? (20s)</li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mighty GOD?! <i>(gasp)</i></li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> …Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.</li><li><b>Mother:</b> Peace… that’s what we need.</li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> His power will never end; peace will last forever. He will rule David’s kingdom.</li><li><b>Child:</b> When is he coming?</li><li><b>Isaiah:</b> I don’t know when. But God has promised. And God keeps his promises.</li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Hmph. We’ll see. <i>(go back to selling)</i></li><li><b>Child:</b> I’m going to remember it. And tell my kids. And they’ll tell their kids. <i>(to Mum)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> And they did. For 700 years, people remembered Isaiah’s promise.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> A child has been born for us!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God promised a child instead of an army?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God’s rescue would be different — not just from enemies, but from sin; a king who brings peace, not war; God himself would come.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Whose kingdom would the child rule?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: David’s kingdom (Isaiah 9:7) — Jesus was from David’s family.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Waiting 700 years</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made this promise 700 years before Jesus came. Why might God wait so long to keep a promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Honest answer: we don’t fully know. Ideas: God’s timing is perfect (Galatians 4:4 ‘when the time was right’); preparing the world; giving people time; building expectation; God sees the whole story. Follow-up: ‘How might people have felt during the wait?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this teach us about God’s promises to us?’ (He doesn’t forget, even if it’s long.) If silent: remember Abraham’s 25-year wait.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made promises about Jesus long before he came — and kept every one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, you are my Wonderful Advisor and Prince of Peace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when you feel worried, pray one of Jesus’ names: “Prince of Peace, please give me peace.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise the prayer together once.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A great light</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Those who walked in the dark…”<br>“…have seen a bright light.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Isaiah 9:6 is long — echo it in chunks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Promise prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying God’s promises back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It isn’t bossing God — it’s trusting what he said. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Think about light shining into the dark.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus has four amazing names.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you are Wonderful Advisor. / Jesus, you are Mighty God and Prince of Peace.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus — Wonderful Advisor, Mighty God, Eternal Father, Prince of Peace!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a time you didn’t trust God’s promises?<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you forgot me. / Sorry for trusting other things more than you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for doubting your promises.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for keeping his promise about Jesus.<br>Prompts: Thank you for sending Jesus. / Thank you that you never forget.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for keeping your promise and sending Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray a promise back: ‘You promised peace — please bring peace to…’<br>Prompts: Prince of Peace, please bring peace to my family. / Please bring peace where there is war.<br>Starter: <b>You promised… so please…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Prince of Peace — you promised peace, so please bring peace to our world.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model the ‘You promised… so please…’ structure.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a promised servant who would carry our wrongs — and 400 very quiet years.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Servant Who Carries Our Wrongs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaiah 9:2–7; 53:4–6; Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:1–12; Jeremiah 31:31–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Servant Who Carries Our Wrongs</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying God’s promises back to him — When you find a promise in the Bible, you can pray it back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It’s like reminding him — and reminding yourself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning Isaiah’s promise of a servant who would suffer for others.</li><li>We are learning about the ‘quiet years’ between the Old and New Testaments.</li><li>I can explain how Isaiah 53 points to Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking ‘he was wounded because of our sins’ to Jesus on the cross, explained simply and hopefully.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a backpack loaded with books (for the ‘carrying’ demonstration)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Servant Who Carries Our Wrongs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Isaiah promised a child…</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can you remember the four names?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Wonderful Advisor, Mighty God, Eternal Father, Prince of Peace. ‘Today Isaiah tells us something surprising about what this King would do.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Isaiah 53:4–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 53:4</b> He suffered and endured great pain for us, but we thought his suffering was punishment from God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 53:5</b> He was wounded and crushed because of our sins; by taking our punishment, he made us completely well.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 53:6</b> All of us were like sheep that had wandered off. We had each gone our own way, but the LORD gave him the punishment we deserved.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read gently and slowly. Notice the sheep picture from last week!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A surprising King</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard Isaiah’s promise of a child who would be a mighty King. But later in Isaiah’s book, there’s something that would have surprised everyone. Isaiah talks about a servant of God — someone who would suffer. Who would be hurt. Who would be treated badly. And he would go through it not because of anything he did wrong, but for other people.</p><p>Listen again: ‘He suffered and endured great pain for us.’ For us. ‘He was wounded and crushed because of our sins. By taking our punishment, he made us completely well.’</p><p>Remember Week 2, when Adam and Eve chose their own way instead of God’s? Isaiah says we’re all a bit like that. ‘All of us were like sheep that had wandered off. We had each gone our own way.’ Do you remember last week, how sheep wander off and get into trouble? That’s us. Every one of us has done wrong things — sins. And sin breaks our friendship with God and deserves a punishment.</p><p>But then Isaiah says something amazing: ‘The LORD gave him the punishment we deserved.’</p><p>Let me show you what that means. Imagine you had a heavy backpack full of everything wrong you’ve ever done. Every lie. Every unkind word. Every selfish choice. It’s far too heavy. You can’t carry it. And then someone comes along and says, ‘Give it to me. I’ll carry it for you.’ And he takes it — all of it — onto his own back.</p><p>That is what Isaiah promised the servant would do. And when Jesus came, that is exactly what he did. On the cross, Jesus carried our sins, so that we could be forgiven and made completely well. Isaiah wrote that about seven hundred years before it happened.</p><p>That’s a lot to think about, so let’s go gently. The important thing is this: it was done out of love. And it isn’t the end of the story — because Jesus didn’t stay dead. He rose again.</p><p>Now, after Isaiah, God sent a few more prophets. Then the last prophet of the Old Testament, a man called Malachi, finished speaking. And then… silence. For about four hundred years, there were no new prophets. No new messages from God. People kept reading the old scrolls. They kept waiting. Some kept hoping. Others gave up.</p><p>Imagine waiting four hundred years for a promise. That’s about twenty generations — your great-great-great-great… (keep going a lot) …grandparents.</p><p>But God was not gone. He was quiet, but he was getting everything ready. And when the time was exactly right, the silence would be broken — first by an angel, and then by the cry of a newborn baby in Bethlehem.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A King who would… suffer? · 2. Like sheep that wandered off. · 3. He took our punishment. · 4. Then… silence. · 5. Still waiting. Still hoping.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Isaiah 53 is handled gently: ‘hurt’ and ‘carried’ language. Optional demonstration: a heavy backpack, which you take from a volunteer. The ‘400 years’ is an approximation (Malachi c. 430 BC).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Isaiah and Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah wrote about the servant 700 years before Jesus. Match the promise to what happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Isaiah promised…</th><th>With Jesus…</th></tr><tr><td>“He suffered… for us”</td><td>Jesus suffered on the cross for others</td></tr><tr><td>“Because of our sins”</td><td>Jesus never sinned — he died for OUR sins</td></tr><tr><td>“Like sheep that had wandered off”</td><td>Jesus called himself the Good Shepherd</td></tr><tr><td>“He made us completely well”</td><td>Through Jesus we can be forgiven and made right with God</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah was describing Jesus — 700 years early.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Keep the tone hopeful.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Like sheep</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah 53:6 says all of us are like what?<br>A) Lions that roar &nbsp; B) Sheep that wandered off ✓ &nbsp; C) Birds that fly away &nbsp; D) Fish in the sea</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B. Follow-up: ‘What does wandering off mean for people?’ (Going our own way instead of God’s.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who did Jesus say he was?’ (The Good Shepherd — last week!) If silent: re-read the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: For us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah keeps saying the servant suffered “for us” and took “our punishment”. What does that show about how much God loves people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God loves us enough to take our punishment himself; he doesn’t just ignore sin — he deals with it by carrying it; it’s the biggest kindness ever. Follow-up: ‘How is this like the Passover lamb (Week 6)?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might you respond to someone who did that for you?’ Pastoral: keep it gentle; some students may have questions about death — answer simply.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The quiet years</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">For 400 years God sent no new prophets. Why might God be quiet sometimes? Does quiet mean he’s gone?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out: quiet doesn’t mean absent; God was preparing (Roman roads, a common language — the world was ready); people still had his written words; times of quiet can grow trust. Follow-up: ‘Have you ever felt like God was quiet?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What can we do when God seems quiet?’ (Keep reading, praying, trusting his promises.) If silent: compare to a parent quietly preparing a surprise party.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus carried my wrongs so I could be forgiven and made well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, Jesus, for carrying what I couldn’t.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, help carry something for someone — literally (their bag, the washing) or by listening to their worries.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Link: we carry others’ loads because Jesus carried ours (Galatians 6:2).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Waiting</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">400 quiet years…<br>God was quiet — but not gone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Isaiah 9:6 — it’s a long one!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Thank-you prayer — hands open</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine handing your heavy backpack to Jesus. Open hands say, ‘I’m letting go — you carry it.’ <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands tight, like you’re holding something heavy. Now slowly open them. Jesus carries it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is the servant who carried our wrongs.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you are so kind. / Jesus, you carried what I couldn’t.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus — you carried our wrongs because you love us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell Jesus what’s in your backpack.<br>Prompts: Sorry for going my own way. / Sorry for…<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for going our own way.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank him for taking the punishment.<br>Prompts: Thank you for making me completely well. / Thank you for the cross.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for carrying our sins and making us well.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray a promise back: ‘You promised to forgive (1 John 1:9) — please…’<br>Prompts: You promised to forgive — please forgive me. / Please help someone carrying something heavy.<br>Starter: <b>You promised… so please…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, you promised to forgive — thank you that we are forgiven.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it gentle and hopeful.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: wise men follow a star — and ask a jealous king for directions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Wise Men Ask Directions":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaiah 9:2–7; 53:4–6; Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:1–12; Jeremiah 31:31–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wise Men Ask Directions</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying God’s promises back to him — When you find a promise in the Bible, you can pray it back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It’s like reminding him — and reminding yourself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the wise men followed a star to find Jesus.</li><li>We are learning how Micah’s promise told them where to go.</li><li>I can compare how the wise men and Herod responded to Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students connecting Micah 5:2 to Bethlehem and contrasting the wise men’s worship with Herod’s jealousy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: three ‘gifts’ (boxes) and a paper star</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Wise Men Ask Directions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Lost!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>You’re on a long trip and you’re lost. No GPS. No phone. Just… the sky.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How would you find your way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take ideas: ask someone, follow the sun, follow stars, look at a map. Land it: ‘Today some travellers follow a star across the desert — and then ask the worst possible person for directions.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 2:1–2, 4–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 2:1–2</b> Some wise men from the east came to Jerusalem and said, “Where is the child born to be king of the Jews? We saw his star in the east and have come to worship him.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 2:3</b> When King Herod heard about this, he was worried.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 2:5</b> They told him, “He will be born in Bethlehem, just as the prophet wrote.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Micah 5:2</b> Bethlehem Ephrath, you are one of the smallest towns in the nation of Judah. But the LORD will choose one of your people to rule the nation.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The scribes were quoting Micah, written about 700 years earlier.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Follow the star</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Far away to the east — maybe in the land we now call Iraq or Iran — lived some wise men. They studied the stars. They were scholars, maybe advisers to kings. And one night they saw something new: a star they had never seen before. Somehow they knew what it meant. A great King had been born — the King of the Jews.</p><p>So they packed up and set off on an enormous journey. Hundreds of kilometres across deserts and rivers, probably on camels, carrying expensive gifts. Weeks, maybe months, of travelling.</p><p>When they got to Jerusalem, the capital city, they thought, ‘A king would be born in the palace, surely!’ So they went to the palace and asked, ‘Where is the child born to be king of the Jews? We saw his star and have come to worship him.’</p><p>But the king in the palace was Herod. And Herod was a jealous, cruel king who did not want anyone else being called king. The Bible says when Herod heard this, he was worried — and everyone in Jerusalem was worried with him.</p><p>Herod called all the chief priests and teachers of God’s law. ‘Where will the Messiah — the promised King — be born?’ And they didn’t even need to think. They pulled out the old scrolls and read the words of a prophet called Micah, written seven hundred years earlier: ‘Bethlehem… you are one of the smallest towns… but the LORD will choose one of your people to rule the nation.’</p><p>Bethlehem! David’s little town. God had told them hundreds of years in advance.</p><p>Herod called the wise men secretly. ‘Go to Bethlehem and search carefully for the child. When you find him, let me know — so I can come and worship him too.’ But Herod was lying. He didn’t want to worship Jesus. He wanted to get rid of him.</p><p>The wise men set off — and the star went ahead of them again, until it stopped over the place where the child was. The Bible says they were thrilled and excited! They went inside, saw the child with Mary his mother, and knelt down and worshipped him. They opened their treasures: gold, frankincense and myrrh.</p><p>Then God warned them in a dream not to go back to Herod. So they went home another way.</p><p>Two kings. One was afraid of Jesus. The other was Jesus — and wise people came from far away to worship him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A new star in the east. · 2. A long journey west. · 3. “Where is the child born to be king?” · 4. “In Bethlehem, just as the prophet wrote.” · 5. They found him — and worshipped.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The number of wise men isn’t given — ‘three’ comes from the three gifts. They likely arrived some time after the birth (Matthew 2:11 says ‘house’). Herod’s later cruelty (2:16) is omitted.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: The priests told Herod the King would be born in ______, because the prophet ______ wrote it long ago.<br><i>Answer: Bethlehem — because the prophet Micah wrote it about 700 years earlier. (Matthew 2:5–6; Micah 5:2)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘Whose hometown was Bethlehem?’ (King David’s — last week!) Follow-up 2: ‘How did the wise men find the exact house?’ (The star went ahead of them.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Wise Men Ask Directions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Wise Men Ask Directions</b> (Matthew 2:1–12). The wise men’s and Herod’s key words are from Matthew 2 (CEV). The banter between the wise men is imagined. Herod is played as sly and worried, not frightening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Wise Man 1</b> — The oldest — studied the stars for years<br><b>Wise Man 2</b> — Practical — keeps the map<br><b>Wise Man 3</b> — The youngest — impatient and excited<br><b>Herod</b> — Jealous king — smooth and sneaky<br><b>Scribe</b> — Expert in the old scrolls<br><b>Everyone</b> — Camels, and amazed crowds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as camels): ‘Hrrrmph!’ and plodding · Everyone: ‘Ooooh!’ when the star appears<br>Props: Optional: three gift boxes, a paper star on a stick<br>Herod’s wink is a fun way to show he’s lying. Keep him sly, not scary.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hrrrmph… plod… plod… plod… <i>(camels walking)</i></li><li><b>Wise Man 3:</b> Are we nearly there yet?</li><li><b>Wise Man 2:</b> You’ve asked that every day for three months.</li><li><b>Wise Man 1:</b> The star led us west. A king must be born in the capital — Jerusalem.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They arrived at King Herod’s palace.</li><li><b>Wise Man 1:</b> Where is the child born to be king of the Jews? We saw his star in the east and have come to worship him.</li><li><b>Herod:</b> A… new… king? <i>(freeze, then fake smile)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When King Herod heard about this, he was worried.</li><li><b>Herod:</b> Scribe! Where will the Messiah be born? <i>(snap fingers)</i></li><li><b>Scribe:</b> He will be born in Bethlehem, just as the prophet wrote. <i>(unroll scroll)</i></li><li><b>Scribe:</b> “Bethlehem… you are one of the smallest towns… But the LORD will choose one of your people to rule.”</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The scribes KNEW the promise — but did they go to Bethlehem? What does that tell you? (20s)</li><li><b>Herod:</b> Go to Bethlehem and search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, let me know. <i>(smooth voice)</i></li><li><b>Herod:</b> I also want to go and worship him. <i>(wink at the audience — he’s lying!)</i></li><li><b>Wise Man 3:</b> Look! The star! It’s moving ahead of us! <i>(point up)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooooh! <i>(look up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The star went on ahead of them until it stopped over the place where the child was. They were thrilled and excited.</li><li><b>Wise Man 1:</b> This is him. The promised King. <i>(kneel)</i></li><li><b>Wise Man 2:</b> Gold — for a king. <i>(offer gift)</i></li><li><b>Wise Man 3:</b> Frankincense — for worship. And myrrh. <i>(offer gifts)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> God warned them in a dream not to go back to Herod. So they went home by another road.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> They found the King!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The scribes knew where the King would be born — but didn’t go. The wise men travelled for months. What’s the difference?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Knowing about God isn’t the same as coming to worship him. The wise men acted on what they knew.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What three gifts did they bring?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Gold, frankincense and myrrh (Matthew 2:11).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two kings</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Herod was worried about Jesus. The wise men were thrilled. Why do you think people react to Jesus so differently?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Herod wanted to stay in charge — Jesus threatened his power; the wise men were searching for the truth and happy to worship. Some people don’t want anyone telling them what to do. Follow-up: ‘How do people react to Jesus today?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What might Jesus being King mean for your life?’ If silent: ‘Who would be worried if a new principal arrived — and who’d be excited?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Knowing vs going</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The scribes knew the Bible well but stayed home. The wise men knew less but travelled far. Is it possible to know a lot about God but not really follow him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yes — knowing facts isn’t the same as trusting and following. The wise men acted on what they knew. Follow-up: ‘What could “going to Bethlehem” look like for you?’ (Praying, following Jesus, worshipping.) Follow-up 2: ‘How can we make sure we don’t just know ABOUT God?’ If silent: compare knowing about a famous person vs being their friend.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s promises pointed the way to Jesus — and wise people still seek him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want to come to you, not just know about you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give Jesus a ‘gift’ this week: time (a few minutes reading about him), talent (using a skill for someone), or treasure (giving something away).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students choose their ‘gift’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Thrilled</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The star went on ahead of them…<br>They were thrilled and excited!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Isaiah 9:6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Promise prayer — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With a partner, take turns praying a promise back to God: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Choose one promise from this term together — like ‘I will be with you’ or ‘I will never be in need’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together, tell God how he kept his promises about Jesus.<br>Prompts: God, you told them it would be Bethlehem! / God, you always keep your word.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you kept your promise right down to the town!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for knowing about God but not following.<br>Prompts: Sorry for staying at home like the scribes. / Sorry for not seeking you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for knowing about you but not coming to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one promise he has kept for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you’re with me. / Thank you that you forgive.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for every promise you keep.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray your chosen promise for your partner.<br>Prompts: You promised to be with us — please be with (name). / You promised peace — please give (name) peace.<br>Starter: <b>You promised… so please…</b> · 55s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you promised to be with us — so please be with every person here.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have a list of the term’s promises on the board to help.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a brand-new promise — and the big question, ‘Who is all this about?’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Who Is the Promise About?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Isaiah 9:2–7; 53:4–6; Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:1–12; Jeremiah 31:31–34</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who Is the Promise About?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying God’s promises back to him — When you find a promise in the Bible, you can pray it back: ‘God, you promised… so please…’ It’s like reminding him — and reminding yourself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about God’s promise of a new agreement in Jeremiah 31.</li><li>We are learning how many Old Testament promises point to Jesus.</li><li>I can answer the question, ‘Who is this promise about?’</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can name at least three promises from the term that point to Jesus, explain the ‘new agreement’, and recite Isaiah 9:6.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Who Is the Promise About?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand for TRUE, sit for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah promised a child who would be called Prince of Peace. — <b>True</b> (Isaiah 9:6)<br>Micah said the ruler would be born in Jerusalem. — <b>False</b> (Bethlehem — one of the smallest towns.)<br>Isaiah said the servant would suffer for other people’s sins. — <b>True</b> (“He was wounded and crushed because of our sins.”)<br>King Herod was thrilled to hear about a new king. — <b>False</b> (He was worried — and he lied to the wise men.)<br>During the quiet 400 years, God forgot his promises. — <b>False</b> (God was quiet, but never forgot.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Stand/sit, then reveal.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Jeremiah 31:31, 33–34</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Jeremiah 31:31</b> The time will surely come when I will make a new agreement with the people of Israel and Judah.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Jeremiah 31:33</b> I will write my laws on their hearts and minds. I will be their God, and they will be my people.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Jeremiah 31:34</b> I will forgive their sins and forget the evil things they have done.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus spoke about this ‘new agreement’ (new covenant) at the Last Supper (Luke 22:20).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: All about Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God gave another prophet, Jeremiah, one more amazing promise. ‘The time will come when I will make a new agreement with my people.’ Remember the old agreement at Mount Sinai, with the commandments written on stone? The people kept breaking it. So God promised something new: ‘I will write my laws on their hearts and minds.’ Not rules on the outside — a changed heart on the inside. And then: ‘I will forgive their sins and forget the evil things they have done.’</p><p>Now let’s look back over the whole term. In Week 2, God promised someone who would defeat the snake. In Week 4, he promised Abraham that the whole world would be blessed through his family. In Week 6, the Passover lamb. In Week 8, a shepherd-king from David’s family. This week: a child called Mighty God, born in Bethlehem; a servant who would carry our sins; a new agreement written on hearts.</p><p>So, who is the promise about? Every single one points to one person: Jesus.</p><p>And next week — we finally get to Bethlehem.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A new agreement. · 2. “I will forgive… and forget.” · 3. Who is this promise about? · 4. Jesus!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. This is a great ‘term so far’ moment — consider pointing to the term’s Big Ideas on a wall.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Written where?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the new agreement, where did God promise to write his laws?<br>A) On stone tablets &nbsp; B) On their hearts and minds ✓ &nbsp; C) On the temple walls &nbsp; D) In the sky</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Jeremiah 31:33). Follow-up: ‘Where were the old commandments written?’ (Stone — Week 7.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did God promise to do with their sins?’ (Forgive and forget.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is it about?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With your partner, list as many promises from this term as you can that point to Jesus. Who can find the most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Week 2: someone to crush the snake (Gen 3:15). Week 4: all nations blessed through Abraham. Week 6: the Passover lamb. Week 7: God living with his people. Week 8: the Good Shepherd from David’s line. Week 9: the child (Isaiah 9), the servant (Isaiah 53), Bethlehem (Micah 5), the new agreement (Jeremiah 31). Follow-up: ‘Which one amazes you most?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about God?’ If silent: give each pair a week to look back on.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Isaiah promised a child: Wonderful Advisor, Mighty God, Eternal Father, Prince of Peace.</li><li>Isaiah promised a servant who would carry our sins; Micah promised Bethlehem.</li><li>Jeremiah promised a new agreement — and every promise points to Jesus.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hundreds of years before, God’s messengers promised a King and Saviour — and God never forgot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal the points, then the Big Idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Promises for us?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “The promises in the Old Testament were only for people back then — not for me.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out: many promises were for a particular time, but they’re all ‘yes’ in Jesus (2 Corinthians 1:20); the new agreement is for everyone who trusts Jesus; God’s character doesn’t change. Follow-up: ‘Which promise from this term do you think is for you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can we tell?’ (Look at Jesus.) If silent: ‘Does God still forgive today?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Promise prayers. Each day, choose one of God’s promises from this term and pray it back to him: “God, you promised… so please…”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every one of God’s promises is ‘yes’ in Jesus. — 2 Corinthians 1:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. The take-home card has a list: Gen 9:13, 1 John 1:9, Ps 23:1, Exodus 3:12 ‘I will be with you’, Isaiah 9:6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A child has been born for us. We have been given a son who will be our ruler. His names will be Wonderful Advisor and Mighty God, Eternal Father and Prince of Peace.” — Isaiah 9:6 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Isaiah 9:6 — a big achievement if they can do it!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Little Bethlehem</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“One of the smallest towns…”<br>Next week: the promise comes true!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Promise prayers — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, anyone can pray a promise back to God out loud. Start with ‘God, you promised…’ <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray. I’ll start with one promise, then anyone can add one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God keeps every promise.<br>Prompts: God, you never forget a promise. / God, you planned Jesus from the very beginning.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you planned Jesus from the very beginning!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Remember the new agreement: God forgives and forgets.<br>Prompts: Sorry for… (silent) / Thank you that you forgive and forget.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry — and we thank you that you forgive and forget.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for Jesus — the promise come true.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Jesus. / Thank you for writing your ways on my heart.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Jesus — your promise come true!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray a promise out loud: ‘God, you promised… so please…’<br>Prompts: You promised to be with us — please be with my family. / You promised to forgive — please help me forgive others.<br>Starter: <b>God, you promised… so please…</b> · 55s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you promised, and you always keep your word — so we trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have the list of promises visible.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next Monday: check in on promise prayers. Week 10 — God Keeps His Promise (Jesus is born!).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10: God Keeps His Promise":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:26–56; 2:1–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Saviour</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praise like Mary — Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–49) is a model of praise: it’s all about who God is and what he has done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Gabriel Visits Mary</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how the angel Gabriel announced Jesus’ birth to Mary.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Gabriel Visits Mary → Big news → Luke 1:30–33, 38 → The silence breaks → His name → Promises coming true → Gabriel Visits Mary → Saying yes → Head · Heart · Hands → Mary’s yes → Memory verse peek → Praise like Mary (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Mary’s Song</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Mary visited Elizabeth and how Elizabeth’s baby leapt for joy.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Mary’s Song → Recap → Luke 1:41–42, 46–49 → A song of praise → Inside Mary’s song → Leap for joy → All about God → Upside down → Head · Heart · Hands → With all my heart → Build the verse → Write a praise song</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Born in Bethlehem</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus was born in Bethlehem, just as Micah promised.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Born in Bethlehem → Who hears first? → Luke 2:4–7, 10–11 → No room — but good news → Bed of hay → Born in Bethlehem → A humble King → Good news for everyone → Head · Heart · Hands → Glory! → Verse with actions → Praise like the angels — standing</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Simeon, Anna — and Who Is God?</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Simeon and Anna recognised Jesus as God’s promised Saviour.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Simeon, Anna — and Who Is God? → Who said it? Term 1 edition → Luke 2:25–26, 28–30, 38 → Worth the wait → Simeon’s promise → Who is God? → Term 1’s Big Idea → Worth the wait? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Kept his promise → A term of praise</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Gabriel Visits Mary":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:26–56; 2:1–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gabriel Visits Mary</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praise like Mary — Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–49) is a model of praise: it’s all about who God is and what he has done.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the angel Gabriel announced Jesus’ birth to Mary.</li><li>We are learning how Mary trusted God’s surprising plan.</li><li>I can explain what Gabriel said about who Jesus would be.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling Gabriel’s description of Jesus (Son of God, King forever) and Mary’s response of trust.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch for Gabriel’s light</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Gabriel Visits Mary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The last week of term! Build the excitement.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Big news</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the most surprising news you’ve ever received?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did you react? What did you say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard promises spoken long ago — all pointing to Jesus. Your challenge: pray a promise back to God each day. Which promise did you pray?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 which promise they prayed. Then the hook: new baby, moving, winning something. Land it: ‘After 400 quiet years, God breaks the silence — and sends an angel with the most surprising news in history to a teenage girl in a tiny village.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 1:30–33, 38</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:30–31</b> The angel told Mary, “Don’t be afraid! God is pleased with you, and you will have a son. His name will be Jesus.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:32</b> “He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:33</b> “He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:38</b> Mary said, “I am the Lord’s servant! Let it happen as you have said.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Notice ‘his ancestor David’ and ‘never end’ — Isaiah 9:7 coming true!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The silence breaks</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>For four hundred years, God had been quiet. No new prophets. No angels. Just the old promises on the old scrolls, and people waiting.</p><p>Then, in a tiny village called Nazareth — so small that people made jokes about it — the silence broke.</p><p>There lived a young woman named Mary. She was probably a teenager. She was engaged to marry a carpenter called Joseph, who came from King David’s family. Just an ordinary girl in an ordinary town.</p><p>One day, without any warning, an angel appeared in front of her. Not just any angel — the angel Gabriel, one of God’s great messengers. ‘You are truly blessed!’ he said. ‘The Lord is with you.’</p><p>Mary was confused and frightened. What did that mean? Why would an angel come to her?</p><p>Gabriel said, ‘Don’t be afraid! God is pleased with you. You will have a son, and his name will be Jesus.’ The name Jesus means ‘The LORD saves’. Then Gabriel said, ‘He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High. The Lord God will make him king, as his ancestor David was. He will rule forever, and his kingdom will never end.’</p><p>Do those words sound familiar? A king from David’s family? A kingdom that never ends? That’s exactly what Isaiah promised seven hundred years earlier! God was keeping his promise — right now, right here, through Mary.</p><p>Mary asked an honest question: ‘How can this happen? I’m not even married!’</p><p>Gabriel explained: ‘The Holy Spirit will come down to you, and God’s power will come over you. So your child will be called the holy Son of God.’ And then he told her more news: ‘Your relative Elizabeth, who everyone thought was too old, is going to have a baby too. Nothing is impossible for God!’ — just like God said to Sarah in Week 4.</p><p>Now Mary had a choice. Saying yes would be hard. People might not understand. Joseph might not understand. It could cost her a lot.</p><p>But Mary said, ‘I am the Lord’s servant! Let it happen as you have said.’</p><p>What a yes! Like Abraham setting out, like Noah building, like Moses going back to Egypt — Mary trusted God enough to say yes to his plan, even without knowing how it would all work out.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Nazareth: a tiny village. · 2. Suddenly — an angel! · 3. “You will have a son. His name will be Jesus.” · 4. “How can this happen?” · 5. “I am the Lord’s servant.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mary’s age isn’t given; teenage engagement was common. Keep the explanation of the virgin birth simple: ‘The Holy Spirit… God’s power’ (1:35). Nazareth jokes: John 1:46.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: His name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Gabriel say the baby’s name would be?<br>A) David &nbsp; B) Jesus ✓ &nbsp; C) Isaiah &nbsp; D) Samuel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1:31). Follow-up: ‘What does the name Jesus mean?’ (‘The LORD saves.’) Follow-up 2: ‘What else did Gabriel say about him?’ (Son of God, king like David, kingdom never ends.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Promises coming true</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which promises from earlier this term did Gabriel’s message make come true?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Isaiah 9:6–7 — a child, a son, ruling David’s kingdom forever; David’s family (Week 8); ‘Nothing is impossible for God’ echoes Sarah (Week 4); God with us (Week 7). Follow-up: ‘How do you think people who knew Isaiah’s words would feel?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about God?’ If silent: put the term’s promises on the board.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Gabriel Visits Mary</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Gabriel Visits Mary</b> (Luke 1:26–38; Matthew 1:18–24). Gabriel’s and Mary’s words are from Luke 1 (CEV). Joseph’s worry and the angel in his dream are summarised from Matthew 1:19–24; the neighbour’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Gabriel</b> — God’s angel messenger — bright and kind<br><b>Mary</b> — A young woman — surprised, then trusting<br><b>Joseph</b> — Carpenter, engaged to Mary — worried, then obedient<br><b>Neighbour</b> — Nosy villager<br><b>Everyone</b> — Angel light, and whispering neighbours</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: a soft ‘ahhh’ of angel light when Gabriel appears · Everyone: whispering neighbours<br>Props: Optional: a torch for Gabriel<br>The angel in Joseph’s dream is unnamed in Matthew 1 — we use Gabriel’s character for simplicity; mention this if asked. Keep the ‘not married’ part simple.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Nazareth. A small stone house. Mary is working quietly.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ahhhhhh… <i>(soft, rising — angel light appears)</i></li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> You are truly blessed! The Lord is with you.</li><li><b>Mary:</b> Who… who are you? What does this mean? <i>(step back, confused)</i></li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> Don’t be afraid! God is pleased with you, and you will have a son. His name will be Jesus.</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> He will be great and will be called the Son of God Most High.</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> He will rule the people of Israel forever, and his kingdom will never end.</li><li><b>Mary:</b> How can this happen? I am not even married!</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> The Holy Spirit will come down to you, and God’s power will come over you.</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> Your relative Elizabeth is also going to have a son, even though she is old. Nothing is impossible for God!</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Saying yes could make life very hard for Mary. What would you say if you were her? (20s)</li><li><b>Mary:</b> I am the Lord’s servant! Let it happen as you have said. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> And the angel left her.</li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Did you hear? Mary is going to have a baby — and she’s not married yet! <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Psst… psst… psst… <i>(whispering neighbours)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Mary… I don’t understand. Maybe I should quietly call off the wedding. <i>(sad, head down)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But that night, an angel spoke to Joseph in a dream.</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> Joseph, don’t be afraid to take Mary as your wife. Her baby is from the Holy Spirit.</li><li><b>Gabriel:</b> Name him Jesus — he will save his people from their sins.</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Then I’ll marry her. And I’ll name him Jesus. <i>(wake up, determined)</i></li><li><b>Mary:</b> God is keeping his promise — through us! <i>(take Joseph’s hands)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Nothing is impossible for God!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mary and Joseph both said yes to God. What did it cost them?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Gossip, misunderstanding, their reputations, their plans. They trusted God anyway.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the angel tell Joseph that Jesus would do?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Save his people from their sins (Matthew 1:21).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Saying yes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mary said “Let it happen as you have said” without knowing how everything would work out. What makes it hard to say yes to God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: not knowing what will happen; what people will think; it might cost us; we like being in control; fear. What helped Mary: trusting God’s goodness, his promises, ‘nothing is impossible for God’. Follow-up: ‘Who else this term said yes without knowing the whole plan?’ (Noah, Abraham, Moses.) Follow-up 2: ‘What might God be asking you to say yes to?’ If silent: list this term’s ‘yes’ people.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Nothing is impossible for God — and Jesus is the promised King.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, like Mary, I want to say, “I am yours.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say a ‘Mary yes’ this week: when God (or a parent or teacher) asks something of you, answer with a willing heart, not a grumble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise the line: ‘Let it happen as you have said.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Mary’s yes</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Nothing is impossible for God!”<br>“I am the Lord’s servant.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 2:10–11 — the angel’s message to the shepherds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praise like Mary (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we learn praise from Mary. Praise is telling God how great he is — like a ‘Wow’ prayer that keeps going! <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. God broke 400 years of silence for this moment. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Nothing is impossible for God.<br>Prompts: God, nothing is impossible for you. / Jesus, your kingdom will never end.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — nothing is impossible for you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there something you’ve said ‘no’ to God about?<br>Prompts: Sorry for saying no to you. / Sorry for grumbling instead of trusting.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I’m sorry for the times I said no to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for sending Jesus — the promise come true.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Jesus. / Thank you for keeping your promises.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for sending Jesus!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Mary’s yes for yourself.<br>Prompts: “I am the Lord’s servant.” / Please help me trust your plan.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I am your servant — let it happen as you have said.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage students to pray Mary’s words as their own.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Mary visits Elizabeth — and sings one of the greatest songs ever written.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Mary’s Song":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:26–56; 2:1–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mary’s Song</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praise like Mary — Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–49) is a model of praise: it’s all about who God is and what he has done.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Mary visited Elizabeth and how Elizabeth’s baby leapt for joy.</li><li>We are learning Mary’s song of praise (Luke 1:46–49).</li><li>I can write a praise prayer modelled on Mary’s song.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students identifying what Mary praised God for (who he is and what he has done) and using that pattern in their own praise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper for writing praise songs</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Mary’s Song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday an angel visited Mary.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the angel’s name — and what did Mary say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Gabriel. ‘I am the Lord’s servant! Let it happen as you have said.’ ‘Gabriel also told Mary about her old relative Elizabeth. So today — Mary hurries off to see her.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 1:41–42, 46–49</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:41</b> When Elizabeth heard Mary’s greeting, her baby moved within her. The Holy Spirit came upon Elizabeth.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:42</b> In a loud voice she said to Mary: God has blessed you more than any other woman! He has also blessed the child you will have.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:46–47</b> Mary said: With all my heart I praise the Lord, and I am glad because of God my Savior.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 1:49</b> God All-Powerful has done great things for me, and his name is holy.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Mary’s song is often called ‘the Magnificat’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A song of praise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After Gabriel left, Mary didn’t sit around. She packed up and hurried off on a long journey — several days’ walk — south to the hill country of Judea. She was going to see her relative Elizabeth.</p><p>Remember what Gabriel said? Elizabeth was old. Everyone thought she could never have children — a bit like Sarah, back in Week 4. But now she was six months pregnant! Elizabeth’s husband, Zechariah, was a priest. When an angel told him about the baby, he didn’t believe it, so he couldn’t speak until the baby was born. Imagine a whole nine months without talking!</p><p>Mary arrived at their house, walked in, and called out, ‘Hello, Elizabeth!’</p><p>And the moment Elizabeth heard Mary’s voice, the baby inside her jumped! He leapt for joy! That baby would grow up to be John the Baptist — the one who would one day point at Jesus and say, ‘Here is the Lamb of God!’ (Remember Week 6?) And even before either of them was born, John was excited about Jesus.</p><p>Then the Holy Spirit filled Elizabeth, and she shouted out, ‘God has blessed you more than any other woman! He has blessed the child you will have! Why should the mother of my Lord come to me?’</p><p>How did Elizabeth know? Mary hadn’t told her anything yet! God had shown her. Mary’s baby was her Lord.</p><p>And then Mary did something beautiful. She sang. Out came a song of praise — one of the most famous songs in the whole world. People have been singing it for two thousand years. It begins:</p><p>‘With all my heart I praise the Lord, and I am glad because of God my Saviour. God cares for me, his humble servant. From now on, all people will say God has blessed me. God All-Powerful has done great things for me, and his name is holy.’</p><p>Notice something about Mary’s song. It isn’t about Mary. It isn’t ‘Look at me, I’m special.’ It’s all about God. Who he is — ‘my Saviour’, ‘All-Powerful’, ‘holy’. And what he has done — ‘he cares for me’, ‘he has done great things’.</p><p>Mary’s song keeps going. She sings about how God turns things upside down: he lifts up humble people and brings down proud people. He feeds hungry people. And he remembers his promises — ‘the promise he made to our ancestors, to Abraham and his family forever.’ Abraham! Week 4! Mary knew that her baby was God keeping his very old promise.</p><p>That’s what praise is: noticing who God is and what he has done, and telling him — with all your heart.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Mary hurried to the hill country. · 2. The baby leapt for joy! · 3. “God has blessed you!” · 4. Mary sang. · 5. A song all about God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Zechariah’s story is from Luke 1:5–25. The end of the Magnificat (1:50–55) is summarised. ‘Saviour’ in narration uses NZ spelling; the CEV on screen has ‘Savior’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Inside Mary’s song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mary’s song is a model of praise. What does she praise God for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Who God is</th><th>What God has done</th></tr><tr><td>“God my Savior”</td><td>“God cares for me, his humble servant”</td></tr><tr><td>“God All-Powerful”</td><td>“Has done great things for me”</td></tr><tr><td>“His name is holy”</td><td>Lifts up the humble, feeds the hungry</td></tr><tr><td>Faithful to his promises</td><td>Kept his promise to Abraham</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praise = telling God who he is and what he has done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Leap for joy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened when Elizabeth heard Mary’s greeting?<br>A) She fainted &nbsp; B) Her unborn baby moved for joy ✓ &nbsp; C) She ran away &nbsp; D) Zechariah started singing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1:41, 44). Follow-up: ‘Who was that baby?’ (John the Baptist.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Elizabeth call Mary?’ (Mother of my Lord.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: All about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mary had just received amazing news about HERSELF. Why do you think her song is all about God, not about her?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she was humble; she knew God was the one doing the great thing; she was a ‘servant’; praise points away from ourselves. Follow-up: ‘How is this like Joseph saying “God can” in Week 5?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could you praise God when something good happens to you?’ If silent: compare a sports star thanking the team vs only talking about themselves.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Upside down</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mary sang that God lifts up humble people and brings down proud people. Where have we seen God do that this term?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Examples: David chosen over his tall brothers; Joseph raised from prison; Pharaoh brought down; Mary (an ordinary girl from Nazareth) chosen; shepherds told first (tomorrow). Follow-up: ‘Why does God love to work through humble people?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does humble look like at school?’ If silent: list the term’s ‘unlikely’ heroes.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praise means telling God who he is and what he has done.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With all my heart I praise you, Lord!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own two-line praise song: line 1 about who God is, line 2 about what he has done for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This leads straight into today’s prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: With all my heart</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“With all my heart I praise the Lord…”<br>“…God All-Powerful has done great things for me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 2:10–11.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a praise song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own ‘Magnificat’! Start like Mary: ‘With all my heart I praise you, Lord…’ <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write at the top: ‘With all my heart I praise the Lord.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about WHO God is.<br>Prompts: You are my Saviour. / You are All-Powerful and holy.<br>Starter: <b>You are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “With all my heart I praise you, Lord — you are my Saviour!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for being proud or forgetting to praise.<br>Prompts: Sorry for making things about me. / Sorry for forgetting to praise you.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, we’re sorry for forgetting to praise you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about WHAT God has done for you.<br>Prompts: You have done great things for me. / You gave me…<br>Starter: <b>You have…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God All-Powerful has done great things for us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line asking God to help someone humble or hungry.<br>Prompts: Please lift up people who feel small. / Please feed the hungry.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Lord, lift up the humble and feed the hungry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read your song quietly to God. That’s praise! Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite a few volunteers to read theirs aloud.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the journey to Bethlehem, no room — and a night full of angels.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Born in Bethlehem":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:26–56; 2:1–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Born in Bethlehem</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praise like Mary — Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–49) is a model of praise: it’s all about who God is and what he has done.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus was born in Bethlehem, just as Micah promised.</li><li>We are learning why the angels told the good news to shepherds first.</li><li>I can explain the angel’s message in Luke 2:10–11.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Bethlehem to Micah 5:2, explaining the angel’s message, and noticing that the news came first to ordinary shepherds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a doll in a box of hay, tea towels for shepherds</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Born in Bethlehem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Who hears first?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If the most important person in the world was born today, who would the news go to first?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Prime Minister? TV news? Famous people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take ideas. Land it: ‘When Jesus was born, God sent an army of angels… to a group of smelly shepherds on a hillside at night. Let’s find out why.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 2:4–7, 10–11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:4</b> Joseph had to leave Nazareth… and go to Bethlehem in Judea. Long ago Bethlehem had been King David’s hometown.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:7</b> She dressed him in baby clothes and laid him on a bed of hay, because there was no room for them in the inn.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:10</b> “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:11</b> This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 2:10–11 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: No room — but good news</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Mary’s baby was nearly due. But then the Roman emperor, Augustus, gave an order: everyone in his empire had to go back to their family’s hometown to be counted. Joseph was from King David’s family — so he had to go to Bethlehem, David’s town.</p><p>So Joseph and Mary set off — about a hundred and fifty kilometres, on foot, with Mary heavily pregnant. It probably took about a week. And who had promised, seven hundred years earlier, that the King would be born in Bethlehem? The prophet Micah! A Roman emperor thought he was in charge — but God was using even him to keep his promise.</p><p>When they finally got to Bethlehem, the town was packed with travellers. There was no room for them in the guest room. So when the baby came, Mary wrapped him in cloths and laid him on a bed of hay — in a feeding trough for animals, called a manger.</p><p>Think about that. Jesus — the Mighty God from Isaiah’s promise, the King whose kingdom will never end — was born not in a palace, but in a place where animals were kept. He came small, poor and humble.</p><p>That same night, out in the fields near Bethlehem, some shepherds were looking after their sheep. Shepherds weren’t important people. They were dirty and smelly, and people looked down on them. But remember who else was a shepherd from Bethlehem? David!</p><p>Suddenly an angel appeared, and the brightness of God’s glory blazed all around them. The shepherds were terrified! But the angel said, ‘Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Saviour was born for you. He is Christ the Lord. You will find him dressed in baby clothes and lying on a bed of hay.’</p><p>Then the whole sky filled with angels — an army of them — praising God: ‘Praise God in heaven! Peace on earth to everyone who pleases God!’</p><p>When the angels left, the shepherds looked at each other and said, ‘Let’s go to Bethlehem and see!’ They hurried off and found Mary and Joseph — and the baby, lying on the hay, exactly as the angel said. Then they went out and told everyone they met. And Mary treasured all these things in her heart.</p><p>The news went first, not to the emperor or the king, but to ordinary shepherds. Because this good news is for everyone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Everyone back to their hometown! · 2. No room. · 3. A baby on a bed of hay. · 4. Shepherds in the fields. · 5. “A Savior was born for you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The ‘inn’ may have been a guest room in a family home (the Greek word ‘kataluma’); the Bible doesn’t mention an innkeeper — he appears in our role-play as an imagined character. Distance Nazareth–Bethlehem about 150 km.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Bed of hay</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the angel’s message: “This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is…”<br><i>Answer: “…Christ the Lord.” (Luke 2:11)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘What is King David’s hometown?’ (Bethlehem.) Follow-up 2: ‘Where would the shepherds find him?’ (On a bed of hay.) If silent: point to the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Born in Bethlehem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Born in Bethlehem</b> (Luke 2:1–20). The angel’s words are from Luke 2 (CEV). The innkeeper and the shepherds’ conversation are imagined — the Bible doesn’t mention an innkeeper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Joseph</b> — Tired, worried, protective<br><b>Mary</b> — About to have a baby!<br><b>Innkeeper</b> — Busy, flustered — but kind in the end<br><b>Shepherd 1</b> — Old and grumpy, until the angels come<br><b>Shepherd 2</b> — Young and excited<br><b>Angel</b> — God’s messenger — dazzling<br><b>Everyone</b> — Crowds, sheep, then an army of angels</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as crowds): busy, noisy Bethlehem · Everyone (as sheep): ‘Baa!’ · Everyone (as angels): ‘Praise God in heaven!’<br>Props: Optional: a doll, a box of hay, tea towels for shepherds<br>The innkeeper is imagined. A big, joyful role-play — let the angels be LOUD.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li><b>Everyone:</b> Move along! Make way! Where’s my room?! <i>(busy crowd)</i></li><li><b>Joseph:</b> Excuse me — have you got any room? My wife is about to have a baby! <i>(knock)</i></li><li><b>Innkeeper:</b> Room? The whole town’s full! It’s this silly census! <i>(flustered)</i></li><li><b>Mary:</b> Joseph… the baby’s coming! <i>(hold tummy)</i></li><li><b>Innkeeper:</b> Oh dear… there’s the place out the back where we keep the animals. It’s dry, at least. <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> She dressed him in baby clothes and laid him on a bed of hay, because there was no room for them in the inn.</li><li><b>Joseph:</b> His name is Jesus. <i>(softly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> That night, in the fields near Bethlehem…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Baa… baa… <i>(sleepy sheep)</i></li><li><b>Shepherd 1:</b> Another cold night. Nothing ever happens out here. <i>(grumble)</i></li><li><b>Angel:</b> Don’t be afraid! <i>(appear, dazzling)</i></li><li><b>Shepherd 2:</b> Aaaah! Who — what — ?! <i>(fall over)</i></li><li><b>Angel:</b> I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> You will find him dressed in baby clothes and lying on a bed of hay.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Praise God in heaven! Peace on earth to everyone who pleases God! <i>(arms up — an army of angels!)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The shepherds have just seen the sky full of angels. What do you think they’ll do next? (20s)</li><li><b>Shepherd 2:</b> Let’s go to Bethlehem and see!</li><li><b>Shepherd 1:</b> Bring the sheep! Hurry! <i>(run)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They found Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying on the bed of hay.</li><li><b>Shepherd 1:</b> It’s him. Exactly like the angel said. <i>(kneel, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Shepherd 2:</b> Wait till I tell EVERYONE! <i>(run off, shouting)</i></li><li><b>Mary:</b> I’ll keep all of this in my heart forever. <i>(hold the baby close)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> A Saviour is born!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think God told shepherds first, not the king?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The good news is for everyone, especially ordinary and overlooked people; Jesus would be the Good Shepherd; God lifts up the humble (Mary’s song!).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the shepherds do after they saw Jesus?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They told everyone (Luke 2:17) and praised God (2:20).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A humble King</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is the King whose kingdom will never end. Why do you think he was born in a place for animals, not a palace?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to show he came for ordinary and poor people; humility; so anyone could come to him (even smelly shepherds); God’s upside-down kingdom (Mary’s song). Follow-up: ‘How is this different from Herod?’ (Week 9.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does it show about what God values?’ If silent: ‘Who could visit a baby in a stable that couldn’t visit a palace?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Good news for everyone</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The angel said this good news “will make everyone happy”. Why is Jesus’ birth good news for everyone — even people today?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: a Saviour — someone to rescue us from sin; God with us; peace with God; every promise this term fulfilled; not just for one nation — for everyone. Follow-up: ‘What did the shepherds do with the good news?’ (Told everyone.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you tell?’ If silent: re-read Luke 2:10–11.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, the Saviour, was born for everyone — just as God promised.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, for good news that’s for me too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like the shepherds, tell one person this week one thing you’ve learned about Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Glory!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Praise God in heaven!”<br>“Peace on earth to everyone who pleases God.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 2:10–11.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praise like the angels — standing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The angels praised God together. Let’s stand and praise God together too — like a choir of angels! <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand. Imagine the night sky full of angels praising God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say it together, like the angels:<br>Prompts: “Praise God in heaven!” / “Peace on earth!”<br>Starter: <b>Praise God…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Praise God in heaven! Peace on earth to everyone who pleases God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for keeping good news to ourselves?<br>Prompts: Sorry for being too shy to talk about you. / Sorry for looking down on ‘ordinary’ people.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for keeping your good news to ourselves.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for Jesus the Saviour.<br>Prompts: Thank you for sending Jesus as a baby. / Thank you that the good news is for everyone.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that a Saviour was born for us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for someone you could share the good news with.<br>Prompts: Please help me tell (name) about Jesus. / Please let everyone hear the good news.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help the whole world hear the good news of Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen! Please sit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: two old people who had waited their whole lives — and our big term celebration!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Simeon, Anna — and Who Is God?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 1:26–56; 2:1–38</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Simeon, Anna — and Who Is God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praise like Mary — Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–49) is a model of praise: it’s all about who God is and what he has done.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Simeon and Anna recognised Jesus as God’s promised Saviour.</li><li>We are learning what this whole term has shown us about God.</li><li>I can answer the question ‘Who is God?’ using what I’ve learned this term.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell Simeon and Anna’s story, name at least five things they’ve learned about God this term, and recite Luke 2:10–11.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: the term’s ‘God is…’ words on cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Simeon, Anna — and Who Is God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The final devotion of Term 1 — celebrate!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it? Term 1 edition</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? The whole-term edition!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Here is someone like me!” — <b>Adam</b><br>“God has made me laugh.” — <b>Sarah</b><br>“You tried to harm me, but God made it turn out for the best.” — <b>Joseph</b><br>“You, LORD, are my shepherd.” — <b>David</b><br>“I am the Lord’s servant! Let it happen as you have said.” — <b>Mary</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; the class calls out the speaker; click to reveal. Ask: ‘Which week was that?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 2:25–26, 28–30, 38</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:25</b> A man named Simeon was living in Jerusalem. Simeon was a good man.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:26</b> God’s Spirit came to him and told him that he would not die until he had seen Christ the Lord.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:28–29</b> Simeon took the baby Jesus in his arms and praised God: “Lord, I am your servant, and now I can die in peace, because you have kept your promise to me.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:38</b> Anna came in and praised God. She spoke about the child Jesus to everyone who hoped for Jerusalem to be set free.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Worth the wait</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>When Jesus was about six weeks old, Mary and Joseph took him to the temple in Jerusalem to dedicate him to God.</p><p>In the temple there was an old man called Simeon. Years before, God’s Spirit had promised him: ‘You will not die until you have seen the Saviour.’ So Simeon waited. Year after year. Like all of God’s people in the quiet years.</p><p>That day, the Spirit nudged him: ‘Go to the temple — now.’ He saw a young couple with a tiny baby — and he knew. He took Jesus in his arms, and with tears in his eyes he praised God: ‘Lord, I can die in peace, because you have kept your promise to me. With my own eyes I have seen what you have done to save your people.’</p><p>Then an even older woman came over — Anna, eighty-four years old, who prayed in the temple day and night. She praised God, and told everyone who was waiting that the Saviour had come.</p><p>And so we come to the end of the term, and the big question we started with: Who is God? He is the Creator. The Seeker who comes looking. Faithful. The Promise-Maker. In charge. The Rescuer. The Provider. The Shepherd. The Promise-Keeper. And in Jesus, he is our Saviour — God with us.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. An old man named Simeon. · 2. One day, in the temple… · 3. “You have kept your promise.” · 4. Who is God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Mary and Joseph’s visit follows the Law of Moses (Luke 2:22–24). The ten ‘God is…’ words are this term’s weekly ‘God is’ words — display them if you can.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Simeon’s promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What had God’s Spirit promised Simeon?<br>A) He would become king &nbsp; B) He would not die until he had seen Christ the Lord ✓ &nbsp; C) He would be very rich &nbsp; D) He would see a rainbow</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 2:26). Follow-up: ‘What did Simeon say when he held Jesus?’ (‘You have kept your promise.’) Follow-up 2: ‘How old was Anna?’ (84.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is God?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our big question this term was: “Who is God?” With your partner, list as many answers as you can from the ten weeks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Weekly ‘God is’ words: Creator (W1), Seeker (W2), Faithful (W3), Promise-Maker (W4), In Charge (W5), Rescuer (W6), Provider (W7), Shepherd (W8), Promise-Keeper (W9), Saviour (W10). Also: holy, patient, forgiving, powerful, with us. Follow-up: ‘Which one matters most to you right now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which story helped you see it?’ If silent: give each pair two weeks to look back on.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: Term 1’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God made everything good — and when sin broke our friendship, he came looking.</li><li>Through Noah, Abraham, Joseph, Moses and David, God rescued, provided and kept his promises.</li><li>Every promise pointed to Jesus — the Saviour, God with us.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is the promised Saviour — God himself coming to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This recap covers the whole term. Then the Big Idea for Week 10 stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Worth the wait?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Simeon and Anna’s long wait was worth it.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Most will agree — draw out WHY: God kept his promise; seeing Jesus was worth a lifetime; they waited faithfully (praying, serving) not grumpily. Follow-up: ‘What helped them keep waiting?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Are you waiting for anything from God? What can you learn from Simeon and Anna?’ If silent: recall Abraham’s 25-year wait.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell someone. Like the shepherds and Anna, tell one person — at home, or a friend — one thing you have learned about God this term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.” — Luke 2:10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Term 2’s first Monday will check in on this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy. This very day in King David’s hometown a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.” — Luke 2:10–11 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 2:10–11. Bonus: can anyone say Genesis 1:1 from Week 1?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Kept his promise</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who is God?<br>The God who keeps every promise — and came to be with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A term of praise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Like Simeon and Anna, let’s finish the term by praising God together for everything we’ve learned about him. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together to finish Term 1. In the Wow part, anyone can call out one thing God is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is — from any week this term!<br>Prompts: God, you are the Creator! / God, you are our Shepherd! Our Rescuer! Our Saviour!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are Creator, Rescuer, Shepherd and Saviour!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: anything you want to say sorry for before the holidays?<br>Prompts: Sorry for… / Thank you that you forgive (1 John 1:9).<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry — and we thank you that you forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Simeon’s words with me:<br>Prompts: “You have kept your promise.” / Thank you for Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, you have kept your promise. Thank you for Jesus!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for the holidays — rest, safety, and growing closer to God.<br>Prompts: Please keep us safe in the holidays. / Please help me keep praying.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep us safe and close to you over the holidays.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Luke 2:10–11 together as our final prayer of Term 1. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate! Consider a small treat or certificate for memory-verse champions.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Term 2: Trusting God — heroes of faith, starting with ‘What Is Faith?’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"Term 2":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: What Is Faith?":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:1–6; Genesis 22:1–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Provider</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take the words of a Bible verse and turn them into your own prayer — ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for’ becomes ‘God, make me sure of you.’ God loves to hear his own words prayed back to him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Faith Museum</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning what the Bible means by faith (Hebrews 11:1).<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Faith Museum → The trust test → Hebrews 11:1, 4–6 → Welcome to the Faith Museum → The sign above the door → Faith that does → Welcome to the Faith Museum → Why faith pleases God → Head · Heart · Hands → The sign above the door → Memory verse peek → Praying the museum sign (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Here I Am</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God tested Abraham and how Abraham answered, “Here I am.”<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Here I Am → Recap → Genesis 22:1–3, 7–8 → The hardest test → Word study: “Here I am” → Early the next morning → We will come back → Why a test? → Head · Heart · Hands → Here I am → Build the verse → Praying Genesis 22 back (line by line)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Mount Moriah</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God stopped Abraham and provided a ram on Mount Moriah.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Mount Moriah → The last second → Genesis 22:11–14 → God steps in → The name of the place → Mount Moriah → In his place → Super-strong Abraham? → Head · Heart · Hands → God provided → Verse with actions → Open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The LORD Will Provide</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning that Abraham called God ‘The LORD Will Provide’.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The LORD Will Provide → Abraham’s test → Genesis 22:14 · John 1:29 · John 3:16 → The Lamb God provides → The Lamb of God → Same and different → This Week’s Big Idea → If he gave us Jesus… → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → The Lamb of God → One-sentence prayers (out loud)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Faith Museum":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:1–6; Genesis 22:1–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Faith Museum</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take the words of a Bible verse and turn them into your own prayer — ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for’ becomes ‘God, make me sure of you.’ God loves to hear his own words prayed back to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the Bible means by faith (Hebrews 11:1).</li><li>We are learning how Abel, Enoch and Noah showed their faith by what they did.</li><li>I can explain faith as trusting God enough to act on what he says.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students defining faith in their own words (trusting God enough to act, even when you can’t see how) and naming what Abel, Enoch or Noah did because they trusted God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a chair for the ‘trust test’</li><li>Optional: a torch or lanyard for the museum guide</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Faith Museum</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome back! A new term and a new big question: ‘Can I trust him?’ Build the sense of a fresh start.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The trust test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Would you sit on a chair you had never seen before — without checking it first?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What makes you trust a chair? What makes you trust a person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last term ended with Simeon, Anna and the shepherds telling everyone the good news about Jesus. Your challenge: tell one person one thing you learned about God in Term 1. Who did you tell — and what did you say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students who they told and what they said; celebrate honest ‘I forgot’ answers too. Then the hook — if you have a chair, invite someone to sit on it. Take answers: it looks strong, it held me before, someone I trust made it. Land it: ‘Believing a chair will hold you isn’t enough — trusting it means sitting down. This term’s big question is “Can I trust God?” Today we visit a museum full of people who sat their whole weight on God.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Hebrews 11:1, 4–6</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:1</b> Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:4</b> Because Abel had faith, he offered God a better sacrifice than Cain did. God was pleased with him and his gift, and even though Abel is now dead, his faith still speaks for him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:5</b> Enoch had faith and did not die. He pleased God, and God took him up to heaven. That’s why his body was never found.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:6</b> But without faith no one can please God. We must believe that God is real and that he rewards everyone who searches for him.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hebrews 11:1 is our memory verse. ‘Faith’ = trusting God. ‘Sacrifice’ = a gift offered to God. Read slowly — this chapter is the ‘map’ for the whole term.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Welcome to the Faith Museum</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome to Term 2! Last term our big question was ‘Who is God?’ This term the question is: ‘Can I trust him?’</p><p>To help us answer it, we’re going to visit a museum. It isn’t a real building — it’s an imaginary one. But every exhibit inside comes from a real chapter of the Bible: Hebrews 11. Some people call it ‘the Hall of Faith’.</p><p>Hebrews was a letter written to Christians who were having a hard time. Some were being picked on for following Jesus. Some were tired. Some were thinking about giving up. So the writer took them on a tour. ‘Look,’ he said, ‘at all the people who trusted God before you.’</p><p>And before the tour begins, he puts a sign above the door. It explains one word — faith. ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.’</p><p>Faith isn’t pretending. It isn’t just a nice feeling. Faith is trusting God — who is real, and who keeps his promises — so much that you actually do what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</p><p>Exhibit one: Abel. Remember Adam and Eve’s sons? Abel gave God the very best of his flock — not the leftovers, his best. He trusted that God was worth it. And thousands of years later, the writer says, Abel’s faith still speaks.</p><p>Exhibit two: Enoch. Genesis says Enoch ‘walked with God’ — day after day, year after year, like two friends walking side by side. And one day God simply took him home to heaven. He never died!</p><p>Exhibit three: Noah. Remember Week 3 last term? God warned Noah about a flood nobody had ever seen. Not a cloud in the sky. But Noah trusted God, picked up his tools and built an enormous boat — and it saved his family.</p><p>Then comes another sign, in big letters: ‘Without faith no one can please God. We must believe that God is real and that he rewards everyone who searches for him.’</p><p>Did you notice what is the same about every exhibit? Each person did something. Abel gave. Enoch walked. Noah built. Their faith wasn’t only in their heads. It moved their hands and feet.</p><p>And here’s the best part: the museum isn’t finished. There are still empty frames on the walls.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Welcome to the Faith Museum! · 2. The sign above the door: · 3. Exhibit 1: Abel gave his best. · 4. Exhibit 2: Enoch walked with God. · 5. Exhibit 3: Noah built a boat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The museum and guide are imagined — a frame for touring Hebrews 11 all term; say so clearly. Abel: Genesis 4:2–4. Enoch: Genesis 5:21–24 (‘walked with God… God took him’). Noah: Genesis 6–9 (Term 1 Week 3). Hebrews was written to Christians under pressure (Hebrews 10:32–36).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The sign above the door</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to Hebrews 11:1, what does faith do?<br>A) Makes us feel happy all the time &nbsp; B) Makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see ✓ &nbsp; C) Lets us see everything clearly &nbsp; D) Makes every problem disappear</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Hebrews 11:1). Follow-up: ‘What are some things we can’t see but still trust are real?’ (Wind, air, love, gravity, someone’s promise.) Follow-up 2: ‘What do you think Abel, Enoch and Noah were hoping for?’ If silent: re-read the first scripture line together, slowly, and ask which words sound the most sure.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Faith that does</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abel gave. Enoch walked. Noah built. Why do you think the Bible shows faith by what people DID, not only by what they thought?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: real trust shows in actions; if you really believe the chair will hold you, you sit; saying ‘I trust God’ but never doing what he says isn’t really trusting; actions show what’s in our hearts. Follow-up: ‘What would Noah’s faith have looked like if he believed God but never built the ark?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could faith look like in your life this week?’ If silent: go back to the chair — ‘Is it trust if you only look at the chair?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Welcome to the Faith Museum</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Welcome to the Faith Museum</b> (Hebrews 11:1–7). The museum, the guide and the three visitors are imagined — a frame for touring Hebrews 11. The guide’s quotes from Hebrews 11 are from the CEV; the details about Abel, Enoch and Noah are summarised from Genesis 4–6.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Guide</b> — The Faith Museum guide — cheerful, loves her exhibits<br><b>Tama</b> — A visitor who asks LOTS of questions<br><b>Ruby</b> — A visitor who only believes what she can see<br><b>Grandad</b> — Ruby’s grandad — slow, kind and wise<br><b>Everyone</b> — Creaky doors, museum echoes, hammering</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: the creaky museum doors · Everyone: the museum echo ‘Ooooh…’ · Everyone: Noah’s hammering<br>Props: Optional: a torch for the guide’s lantern · Optional: a lanyard name badge<br>The guide returns all term, so cast a confident reader. Let Everyone make the creaky doors and echoes big. If asked about Enoch: Genesis 5:24 simply says ‘God took him’ — keep it simple and wonder-filled.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The doors of a grand old museum swing open. A guide with a lantern steps out.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Creeeeeak… <i>(doors opening slowly)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Welcome, welcome! Welcome to the Faith Museum. Mind the step — and keep your eyes open. <i>(lift lantern)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> What’s faith? Is it like… wishing really, really hard? <i>(hand up)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Good question! Read the sign above the door with me. <i>(point up)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> Proof of what we CAN’T see? That makes no sense. I only believe what I can see. <i>(arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Grandad:</b> Can you see the wind, Ruby? But you believe it when it blows your hat off. <i>(chuckle)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Faith is trusting God — who is real — enough to do what he says. Come and see.</li><li class='dir'>Exhibit one: a shepherd’s crook beside a little stone altar.</li><li><b>Guide:</b> This is Abel. He gave God the very best lamb from his flock. Not the skinny one — the best.</li><li><b>Tama:</b> Why would anyone give away their best?</li><li><b>Guide:</b> Because he trusted that God was worth it. And the sign says his faith still speaks for him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooooh… <i>(museum echo)</i></li><li class='dir'>Exhibit two: a pair of old sandals on a path that disappears into light.</li><li><b>Guide:</b> Enoch walked with God — every day, for years. Then one day, God took him home.</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> Wait — he just… went? He didn’t die? <i>(lean in)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Enoch had faith and did not die. He pleased God, and God took him up to heaven.</li><li class='dir'>Exhibit three: a wooden ark so big it fills the whole hall.</li><li><b>Grandad:</b> Now THAT’S a boat. Imagine building that with not a cloud in the sky! <i>(look way up)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> God warned Noah about something no one had ever seen. Noah trusted God — and picked up his hammer.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Bang! Bang! Bang! <i>(hammering on desks)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Abel gave, Enoch walked, Noah built. What is the same about all three exhibits? (20s)</li><li><b>Tama:</b> They all DID something because they trusted God! <i>(jump up)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Exactly. Without faith no one can please God. We must believe that God is real…</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> …and that he rewards everyone who searches for him. <i>(reading the sign slowly)</i></li><li><b>Ruby:</b> Okay. Maybe I’ll keep searching. <i>(small smile)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> Why are there empty frames on that wall? <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Because the museum isn’t finished. There’s room for more people who trust God. Maybe even you.</li><li><b>Grandad:</b> Same time tomorrow? I hear the next exhibit is Abraham.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Faith makes us sure of what we hope for!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruby said, “I only believe what I can see.” How would you answer her?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: We trust lots of things we can’t see (wind, love, gravity). Faith isn’t blind: God has shown himself in creation, in promises kept (all of Term 1) and most of all in Jesus.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Abel, Enoch and Noah each DO because of their faith?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Abel gave his best; Enoch walked with God; Noah built the ark (Hebrews 11:4–7).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why faith pleases God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hebrews says, “Without faith no one can please God.” Why do you think trusting God matters to him more than being clever, strong or good?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God wants a friendship, and friends trust each other; trusting God says ‘you are good and true’; not trusting him is like calling him a liar; we can never be good enough to earn God’s love — faith receives what he gives, especially Jesus. Follow-up: ‘How would you feel if your best friend never believed a word you said?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does verse 6 say God does for people who search for him?’ (Rewards them.) If silent: ask ‘Which would make a parent happier — a child who obeys grumpily, or one who trusts them?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith is trusting God so much that I do what he says — even when I can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to believe you are real, and keep searching for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, do one thing simply because God says it — tell the truth when a lie would be easier, or give your best instead of your leftovers like Abel — at home, in the playground or online.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask 2–3 students to name their ‘Abel’ — one thing they could give their best in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The sign above the door</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for…”<br>“…and gives us proof of what we cannot see.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Hebrews 11:1 — the sign above the museum door. Echo it chunk by chunk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying the museum sign (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we learn to pray Scripture back — taking the Bible’s own words and turning them into our prayer. Today we pray the signs in the Faith Museum. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. The museum is quiet now. Breathe in… and out. God is real, and he loves it when we search for him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hebrews 11:6 says God is real. Pray it back to him.<br>Prompts: God, you are real — even though I can’t see you. / You reward everyone who searches for you.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are real, and you reward everyone who searches for you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a time you didn’t trust God, or didn’t do what he said?<br>Prompts: Sorry for only believing what I can see. / Sorry for giving you my leftovers, not my best like Abel.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we didn’t trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the real people in his museum.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Abel, Enoch and Noah — real people who trusted you. / Thank you that you walked with Enoch — and you walk with me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you walk with us, like you walked with Enoch.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Hebrews 11:1 back: ask God to make you sure.<br>Prompts: God, make me sure of what I hope for. / Please give me faith to do what you say this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, make me…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, make us sure of what we hope for — and sure of you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model praying a verse back first: read the verse, then say it to God using ‘you’ and ‘me’. Short and simple is perfect.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the guide leads us to Abraham’s exhibit — and the hardest thing God ever asked him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Here I Am":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:1–6; Genesis 22:1–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Here I Am</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take the words of a Bible verse and turn them into your own prayer — ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for’ becomes ‘God, make me sure of you.’ God loves to hear his own words prayed back to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God tested Abraham and how Abraham answered, “Here I am.”</li><li>We are learning that faith obeys God even when his command is very hard to understand.</li><li>I can explain what Abraham meant when he said, “God will provide the lamb.”</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students noticing Abraham’s ‘Here I am’ readiness, his early-morning obedience, his ‘We will come back’, and his trust that God would provide.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small bundle of sticks</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Here I Am</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is serious — set a calm tone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we toured the Faith Museum.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does the sign above the door say — and which three exhibits did we see?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hebrews 11:1: ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.’ Abel gave, Enoch walked, Noah built. Then: ‘Today the guide leads us to the biggest exhibit in the hall — Abraham. And it begins with the hardest thing God ever asked him. Here’s a secret before we start: this story has a happy ending.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 22:1–3, 7–8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:1</b> Some years later God decided to test Abraham. So he spoke to Abraham, and Abraham answered, “Here I am, LORD.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:2</b> The LORD said, “Go get Isaac, your only son, the one you dearly love! Take him to the land of Moriah…”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:3</b> So Abraham got up early the next morning and chopped wood for the fire.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:7–8</b> Isaac said, “Father, we have the coals and the wood, but where is the lamb for the sacrifice?” “My son,” Abraham answered, “God will provide the lamb.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 2 goes on: God tells Abraham to offer Isaac as a sacrifice. The story slide explains this carefully. Stress the first words: ‘God decided to TEST Abraham’ — we know something Abraham didn’t.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The hardest test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Do you remember Isaac? Last term, God promised old Abraham and Sarah a son — and when Isaac was born, Sarah laughed for joy. Isaac means ‘laughter’. He was the promise come true. Through Isaac, God said, Abraham’s family would grow to be as many as the stars.</p><p>Now Isaac was growing up — perhaps a boy about your age. And Abraham loved him more than anything.</p><p>Then one day, God spoke. ‘Abraham!’</p><p>And Abraham answered with three little words: ‘Here I am.’ That means: ‘I’m listening. I’m ready. Whatever you say.’</p><p>But what God said next was the hardest thing Abraham had ever heard. ‘Take Isaac, your only son, the one you dearly love, and go to the land of Moriah. Offer him to me there as a sacrifice.’</p><p>A sacrifice was a gift given to God on an altar — usually an animal. People who worshipped false gods sometimes even gave their children. But the true God had never asked for anything like this. Abraham must have felt sick inside. Isaac was the promise! How could God keep his promise if Isaac was gone?</p><p>But the Bible tells us something Abraham didn’t know yet: ‘God decided to test Abraham.’ God was never going to let Isaac be harmed. This was a test of trust. Would Abraham hold on to God — or hold on to the gift more tightly than the Giver?</p><p>So what did Abraham do? He didn’t argue. He didn’t hide. The Bible says he ‘got up early the next morning’. He chopped the wood, saddled his donkey and set off with Isaac and two servants.</p><p>They walked for three days. Three long days to think. On the third day Abraham saw the mountain in the distance. He said to the servants, ‘Stay here with the donkey. My son and I will go over there to worship. We will come back.’</p><p>Did you catch that? ‘WE will come back.’ Not ‘I will.’ The book of Hebrews tells us Abraham was sure God could even bring Isaac back from the dead if he had to. God had promised — so somehow, God would keep that promise.</p><p>Abraham put the wood on Isaac’s back. He carried the hot coals for the fire himself. And the two of them walked on together, up the mountain.</p><p>Then Isaac said, ‘Father?’</p><p>‘Yes, my son?’</p><p>‘We have the coals and the wood — but where is the lamb?’</p><p>Abraham’s answer is one of the bravest sentences in the Bible: ‘God will provide the lamb, my son.’</p><p>And the two of them walked on together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Abraham!” “Here I am.” · 2. “Take Isaac, your only son…” · 3. Early the next morning… · 4. Three days of walking. · 5. “Where is the lamb?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. HANDLE WITH CARE: tell students early that God never intended Isaac to be harmed — the narrator tells us ‘God decided to test Abraham’ (22:1). Isaac’s age isn’t given; he is strong enough to carry the wood up a mountain. Hebrews 11:17–19 says Abraham reasoned that God could raise the dead. God’s law later forbids child sacrifice completely (Deuteronomy 12:31). If a child is upset, reassure them how the story ends.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Word study: “Here I am”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the Hebrew Bible, Abraham gives the same short answer three times in this story. Who was he answering?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Who called</th><th>Abraham’s answer</th></tr><tr><td>God: “Abraham!” (22:1)</td><td>“Here I am, LORD.”</td></tr><tr><td>Isaac: “Father?” (22:7)</td><td>“Here I am, my son.”</td></tr><tr><td>The LORD’s angel: “Abraham! Abraham!” (22:11)</td><td>“Here I am!”</td></tr><tr><td>God calls you</td><td>What will you say?</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Here I am” means: I’m listening, I’m ready, I trust you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Hebrew word is ‘hinneni’ — ‘Here I am.’ The CEV words 22:7 a little differently, but the Hebrew repeats it three times: to God, to his son, and to God again. Reveal row by row; pause on the last row.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Early the next morning</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Abraham do the morning after God spoke to him?<br>A) Stayed in bed and worried &nbsp; B) Got up early, chopped wood and set off ✓ &nbsp; C) Ran away to Egypt &nbsp; D) Asked Sarah to decide for him</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Genesis 22:3). Follow-up: ‘What does getting up EARLY tell us about Abraham?’ (He didn’t delay; he obeyed straight away even though it was hard.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who went with him?’ (Isaac, two servants and a donkey.) If silent: re-read the third scripture line and ask students to spot the time of day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: We will come back</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham told the servants, “We will come back.” Why do you think he said WE, not I?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he trusted God’s promise that Isaac’s family would be as many as the stars; Hebrews 11:19 says he was sure God could raise the dead; faith hopes for more than it can see. Some may say he didn’t want to scare Isaac — accept it, then point to Hebrews. Follow-up: ‘What promise was Abraham holding on to?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is this like the sign above the museum door?’ If silent: write ‘WE will come back’ on the board and underline WE.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why a test?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God already knows everything. So why do you think God tested Abraham?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: a test doesn’t make your learning — it shows it; a bridge is tested before people trust it; the test showed Abraham (and us) that he trusted God more than even God’s best gift; a test is not a trick — God provided the answer himself. Follow-up: ‘Is a test the same as a trick? What’s the difference?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Is there something you love so much it would be hard to hand it to God?’ If silent: compare a swimming test — the teacher already thinks you can swim, but the test lets YOU find out.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith says “Here I am” to God — and trusts him even when it’s hard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to hold on to you more tightly than anything you’ve given me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When a parent, coach or teacher calls your name this week, answer ‘Here I am!’ and do what they ask straight away — like Abraham ‘early the next morning’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise it now: call a few names; students answer ‘Here I am!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Here I am</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abraham!”<br>“Here I am, LORD.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Hebrews 11:1. Ask: ‘What was Abraham hoping for that he couldn’t see yet?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying Genesis 22 back (line by line)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we pray Bible words back line by line. The teacher reads a line from Abraham’s story; then we turn it into our own prayer. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get quiet. God called Abraham by name — and he knows yours too. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Teacher reads: “God… spoke to Abraham.” Pray it back:<br>Prompts: God, you know my name, like you knew Abraham’s. / You always keep your promises — even when I can’t see how.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you know our names, and you keep your promises!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Teacher reads: “Abraham got up early the next morning.” Is there something you’ve been slow to do?<br>Prompts: Sorry for saying ‘in a minute’ when you ask me to do something. / Sorry for loving your gifts more than I love you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for being slow to obey you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Teacher reads: “Isaac, your only son, the one you dearly love.” Thank God for someone you love.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my family — every one of them is a gift from you. / Thank you that you love me even more than Abraham loved Isaac.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people we love.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Teacher reads: “Here I am, LORD.” Pray Abraham’s words as your own.<br>Prompts: Here I am, LORD — I’m listening. / When I can’t see how, help me say, ‘God will provide.’<br>Starter: <b>Here I am, LORD. Please…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Here I am, LORD. You will provide.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read each ‘Teacher reads’ line slowly, then pause for students to pray it back silently or in a whisper.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Abraham and Isaac reach the top of Mount Moriah — and God steps in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Mount Moriah":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:1–6; Genesis 22:1–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mount Moriah</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take the words of a Bible verse and turn them into your own prayer — ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for’ becomes ‘God, make me sure of you.’ God loves to hear his own words prayed back to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God stopped Abraham and provided a ram on Mount Moriah.</li><li>We are learning that God tested Abraham’s faith — and God himself provided.</li><li>I can explain what Abraham named the place, and why.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that God stopped Abraham, that the ram took Isaac’s place, and why Abraham named the place ‘The LORD Will Provide’ (after God, not himself).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bundle of sticks for Isaac</li><li>Optional: a picture of a ram’s curly horns</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Mount Moriah</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Calm, hushed start. Remind students the story ends well.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The last second</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever had to wait until the very last second to find out how something would turn out?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did it feel while you were waiting?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take ideas: a penalty shoot-out, waiting for test results, a lost pet. Then recap: ‘Yesterday Abraham and Isaac walked up the mountain. Isaac asked, “Where is the lamb?” Abraham said, “God will provide the lamb.” Today we reach the top — and God steps in at exactly the right moment. Remember: God never let Isaac be hurt.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 22:11–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:11</b> But the LORD’s angel shouted from heaven, “Abraham! Abraham!” “Here I am!” he answered.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:12</b> “Don’t hurt the boy or harm him in any way!” the angel said. “Now I know that you truly obey God, because you were willing to offer him your only son.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:13</b> Abraham looked up and saw a ram caught by its horns in the bushes. So he took the ram and sacrificed it in place of his son.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:14</b> Abraham named that place “The LORD Will Provide.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Notice ‘in place of his son’ — the ram took Isaac’s place. Hold that thought for Thursday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: God steps in</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Abraham and Isaac reached the top of the mountain. Quietly, Abraham gathered stones and built an altar — a kind of stone table where gifts were offered to God. He laid the wood on it, piece by piece.</p><p>By now Isaac must have understood. There was no lamb. Yet the Bible doesn’t say he ran away or fought. Isaac was strong enough to carry a load of firewood up a mountain — strong enough to get away from an old man. But he let his father place him on the altar. Isaac trusted his father, just as his father was trusting God.</p><p>Abraham’s heart must have been breaking. This was his son. His only son. The promise. But Abraham had decided: God is good. God is faithful. God will keep his promise somehow — even if he has to bring Isaac back to life.</p><p>Abraham got ready to do the hardest thing he had ever done.</p><p>And at that very moment a voice called from heaven: ‘Abraham! Abraham!’</p><p>For the third time in this story he answered, ‘Here I am!’</p><p>‘Don’t hurt the boy!’ said the angel of the LORD. ‘Don’t harm him in any way! Now I know that you truly obey God, because you were willing to offer him your only son.’</p><p>Can you imagine how fast Abraham lifted Isaac down? How tightly he hugged him?</p><p>Then Abraham looked up. There, caught by its curly horns in a thorny bush, was a ram — a male sheep. God had provided, exactly as Abraham had said.</p><p>Abraham offered the ram to God instead of his son. The ram took Isaac’s place. Isaac walked away alive because something else died in his place. Hold on to that idea — it will matter tomorrow.</p><p>Then Abraham gave the mountain a name. Not ‘The Place Where I Was Brave’. Not ‘Where Abraham Passed the Test’. He named it after God: ‘The LORD Will Provide.’</p><p>And God spoke again from heaven, repeating his great promise: ‘Because you obeyed me, I will bless you. Your family will be as many as the stars in the sky and the grains of sand on the beach. And through your family, all nations on earth will be blessed.’</p><p>Then Abraham and Isaac walked down that mountain together — two people who had found out, on the hardest day of their lives, that God can be trusted.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The top of the mountain. · 2. Abraham’s heart was breaking. · 3. “Abraham! Abraham!” · 4. A ram, caught in the bushes. · 5. “The LORD Will Provide.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. HANDLE WITH CARE: the narration never describes harm — ‘got ready’ is enough, and the angel interrupts immediately. Stress that God stopped Abraham and never wanted Isaac harmed. Isaac’s willingness is implied by his strength (he could have run). The ‘angel of the LORD’ speaks as God himself (22:12, 16). If a child is upset: God shows here that HE provides the sacrifice — and later forbids child sacrifice completely (Deuteronomy 18:10).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The name of the place</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Abraham named that place…”<br><i>Answer: “…The LORD Will Provide.” (Genesis 22:14)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘What did God provide?’ (A ram caught by its horns in the bushes.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think Abraham named the place after God, not after himself?’ (Because God was the one who did the great thing — Abraham’s part was to trust.) If silent: point to the last scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Mount Moriah</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Mount Moriah</b> (Genesis 22:1–19). The angel’s words and Abraham’s and Isaac’s main lines are from Genesis 22 (CEV), lightly shortened. The servant’s lines and Isaac’s feelings at the top are imagined. Danger is implied, never acted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Abraham</b> — Old, loving, trusting God on the hardest day<br><b>Isaac</b> — Abraham’s son — curious, then brave<br><b>Servant</b> — Waits at the bottom with the donkey<br><b>Angel</b> — The angel of the LORD — heard from heaven, never seen<br><b>Everyone</b> — Footsteps, wind, and a very surprised ram</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: tired footsteps up the mountain (pat knees slowly) · Everyone: the wind on the mountaintop · Everyone: the ram’s ‘Baaa!’<br>Props: Optional: a bundle of sticks for Isaac<br>SENSITIVE SCRIPT: no one mimes the altar or lifts anything — the Narrator’s ‘got ready’ line is enough, and the Angel interrupts at once. Choose a confident Isaac and let the Angel’s ‘Abraham! Abraham!’ ring out. Giggles at the ram’s ‘Baaaa!’ are fine — it is a moment of relief.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Three days of walking. Abraham sees the mountain far away.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Step… step… step… <i>(pat knees slowly — tired feet)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> Master, is this the place? Shall I unload the donkey?</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Stay here with the donkey, while my son and I go over there to worship. We will come back.</li><li><b>Servant:</b> “WE will come back”… he said WE. <i>(whisper to the audience)</i></li><li class='dir'>Abraham put the wood on Isaac’s back. He carried the fire himself.</li><li><b>Isaac:</b> Father?</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Yes, my son?</li><li><b>Isaac:</b> We have the coals and the wood, but where is the lamb for the sacrifice? <i>(look around)</i></li><li><b>Abraham:</b> My son, God will provide the lamb. <i>(hand on Isaac’s shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooosh… <i>(wind on the mountaintop)</i></li><li class='dir'>At the top, Abraham built an altar and laid the wood on it.</li><li><b>Isaac:</b> Father… there isn’t a lamb, is there? <i>(quietly)</i></li><li><b>Abraham:</b> I don’t understand it, Isaac. But I trust God. He always keeps his promises. <i>(voice shaking)</i></li><li><b>Isaac:</b> Then… I will trust you, Father.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Abraham can’t see how this will work out. What is he holding on to? (20s)</li><li class='dir'>Abraham got ready to do the hardest thing he had ever done.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> Abraham! Abraham!</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> Here I am! <i>(freeze, look up)</i></li><li><b>Angel:</b> Don’t hurt the boy or harm him in any way!</li><li><b>Angel:</b> Now I know that you truly obey God, because you were willing to offer him your only son.</li><li><b>Isaac:</b> Father! Look — in the bushes! <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Baaaaa! <i>(a very surprised ram)</i></li><li><b>Abraham:</b> A ram, caught by its horns. God has provided! <i>(hug Isaac tight)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Abraham offered the ram in place of his son.</li><li><b>Abraham:</b> I will name this place “The LORD Will Provide.” <i>(arms raised)</i></li><li><b>Angel:</b> Because you obeyed, I will bless you. Your family will be as many as the stars in the sky.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> And through your family, all nations on earth will be blessed.</li><li><b>Isaac:</b> Come on, Father. Let’s go home — together. <i>(take his hand)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> They’re back! Both of them! <i>(jump up)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The LORD will provide!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaac asked, “Where is the lamb?” How did God answer his question on the mountain?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God provided a ram to take Isaac’s place — just as Abraham said. Tomorrow we’ll see an even bigger answer: Jesus, the Lamb of God.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the angel tell Abraham NOT to do?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: “Don’t hurt the boy or harm him in any way!” (Genesis 22:12).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: In his place</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The ram was offered “in place of” Isaac. What do you think Isaac felt as he watched — and what might he have learned about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: relief, wonder, thankfulness, love for his dad; he learned God provides, God keeps his promises, God is kind, God doesn’t want child sacrifice, someone else can take your place. Follow-up: ‘How might Isaac tell this story to his own children one day?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where else last term did an animal die so people could live?’ (The Passover lamb, Term 1 Week 6.) If silent: ask ‘If you were Isaac, what would you say to God on the way down the mountain?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Super-strong Abraham?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Abraham passed the test because he was a super-strong person.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students move or vote, then dig. Abraham wasn’t perfect — last term he lied about Sarah and tried to get a son his own way. His strength was in his GOD, not himself: Hebrews says ‘Because Abraham had faith…’ Follow-up: ‘So where does strong faith come from?’ (Knowing God keeps his promises; years of walking with him.) Follow-up 2: ‘Can someone with small faith trust a big God?’ If silent: remind them of Abraham and Sarah laughing at God’s promise in Term 1 Week 4.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God provides what we need — exactly when we need it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I trust you to provide, even when I can’t see how.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name one worry for this week — a test, a friendship, something at home. Each time it pops into your head, say quietly, ‘The LORD will provide.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge. Students don’t need to share their worry aloud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: God provided</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abraham! Abraham!”<br>“The LORD Will Provide.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Hebrews 11:1. Ask: ‘What did Abraham hope for on the mountain?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham held Isaac with open hands — he trusted God with what he loved most. Today we pray with our hands, as a sign that we trust God with everything. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably. Rest your hands on your lap. We’ll use them to pray.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Genesis 22:14 back to God:<br>Prompts: You are the LORD who provides. / You stepped in at exactly the right moment.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, LORD — you are the God who provides!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands tight, like holding on to something you won’t give to God.<br>Prompts: Sorry for holding on to things more tightly than I hold on to you. / Sorry for not trusting you to provide.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for holding on so tightly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now open your hands. Thank God for what he provides.<br>Prompts: Thank you for providing a ram for Isaac. / Thank you for providing my food, my home and my family.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for everything you provide for us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands open, palms up: give God one worry.<br>Prompts: God, I give you my worry about… / Please provide what I need, like you did on the mountain.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, we give you our worries — you will provide.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your hands open as we say: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Demonstrate the closed-then-open hands. Some students may like to hold their hands out a little, as if handing something over.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: ‘The LORD will provide’ — and the Lamb God provided for the whole world.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The LORD Will Provide":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hebrews 11:1–6; Genesis 22:1–19</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD Will Provide</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take the words of a Bible verse and turn them into your own prayer — ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for’ becomes ‘God, make me sure of you.’ God loves to hear his own words prayed back to him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Abraham called God ‘The LORD Will Provide’.</li><li>We are learning how the ram on Mount Moriah points to Jesus, the Lamb of God.</li><li>I can say Hebrews 11:1 and explain what faith is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can put Genesis 22 in order, explain how Isaac and the ram point to Jesus, and recite Hebrews 11:1 with its meaning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: small slips of paper for ‘worry’ prayers</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The LORD Will Provide</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate: our first week in the Faith Museum!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Abraham’s test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Abraham’s test in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God calls: “Abraham!” → Up early, chopping wood → Three days’ walk → “Where is the lamb?” → An altar on the mountain → “Don’t hurt the boy!” → A ram in the bushes → “The LORD Will Provide”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. After the ram, ask: ‘Who provided?’ (God!)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Genesis 22:14 · John 1:29 · John 3:16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Genesis 22:14</b> Abraham named that place “The LORD Will Provide.” And even now people say, “On the mountain of the LORD it will be provided.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 1:29</b> The next day, John saw Jesus coming toward him and said: Here is the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 3:16</b> God loved the people of this world so much that he gave his only Son, so that everyone who has faith in him will have eternal life and never really die.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Notice ‘only Son’ in John 3:16 — the same words God used about Isaac. ‘Sin’ = the wrong things we do that break our friendship with God.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The Lamb God provides</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Abraham and Isaac walked down Mount Moriah together. Can you imagine that walk home? Isaac alive. The ram in his place. And a new name for God ringing in their ears: ‘The LORD Will Provide.’</p><p>But Isaac’s question — ‘Where is the lamb?’ — was bigger than anyone knew. That day, God provided a ram. Years later, King Solomon built God’s temple on Mount Moriah, in Jerusalem, and for a thousand years lambs were offered there. Why? Because people’s sin — the wrong things we do that break our friendship with God — had to be dealt with. And still everyone waited for the real answer.</p><p>Then one day, by the Jordan River, John the Baptist saw Jesus walking towards him and shouted, ‘Here is the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!’</p><p>There it is — the answer to Isaac’s question. On a hill just outside Jerusalem, near that same mountain, God the Father did what he stopped Abraham from doing. He gave his only Son, the one he dearly loved. Isaac walked free because a ram took his place. We can walk free because Jesus took ours.</p><p>And then — just as Abraham believed God could do — God raised Jesus back to life.</p><p>So faith isn’t trusting a God who is far away and stingy. It’s trusting a God who has already given us his very best. If he gave us Jesus, the Bible says, he will surely give us everything else we need. The LORD will provide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Down the mountain, together. · 2. “Where is the lamb?” · 3. Two thousand years later… · 4. “Here is the Lamb of God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. 2 Chronicles 3:1 places Solomon’s temple on Mount Moriah. Jesus was crucified just outside Jerusalem’s walls — ‘near that same mountain’ is a careful, fair link. ‘Will surely give us everything else’ is Romans 8:32. Jesus is not drawn in this scene — only the empty cross.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The Lamb of God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did John the Baptist call Jesus?<br>A) The Lion of the Jungle &nbsp; B) The Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world ✓ &nbsp; C) The new Abraham &nbsp; D) The best shepherd in Israel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (John 1:29). Follow-up: ‘Why would John call a person a LAMB?’ (Lambs were offered to God in people’s place — Jesus would take our place.) Follow-up 2: ‘What question did Isaac ask that this answers?’ (‘Where is the lamb?’) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Same and different</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is the story of Abraham and Isaac like the story of Jesus? How is it different?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Same: an only son, dearly loved; wood carried up a hill (Isaac carried wood, Jesus carried a cross); a father giving; someone taking another’s place; ‘on the third day’ (Genesis 22:4). Different: Isaac was spared, Jesus wasn’t; Jesus chose to give himself; Jesus really died and rose again; Jesus takes away sin for the whole world. Follow-up: ‘Why did God stop Abraham, but not stop the cross?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about how much God loves us?’ If silent: draw two columns on the board — ‘Same’ and ‘Different’ — and start one each.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says — even when you can’t see how.</li><li>Abraham said “Here I am”, obeyed early, and trusted that God would provide.</li><li>God provided a ram for Isaac — and Jesus, the Lamb of God, for us.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith is trusting God so much that you act on what he says, even when you can’t see how it will work out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Ask a student to say it in their own words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: If he gave us Jesus…</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible says that if God gave us Jesus, he will give us everything else we need. Why does that help us trust God with smaller worries?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: if God gave his very best, he won’t hold back smaller things; he has already proved how much he loves us; our worries are never too big or too small for him. Note: ‘everything we need’ isn’t ‘everything we want’. Follow-up: ‘What’s the difference between what we need and what we want?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Write one worry you could trust God with this week.’ If silent: compare a friend who gives you their whole lunch — would they refuse you a pencil?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Trust God with one worry each day. Every day this week, hand one worry to God in prayer and say, “The LORD will provide.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Abraham named that place ‘The LORD Will Provide.’” — Genesis 22:14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday (Rahab and the spies) checks in on this. Students could keep a tally on the card.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Faith makes us sure of what we hope for and gives us proof of what we cannot see.” — Hebrews 11:1 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Hebrews 11:1. Bonus: who can explain it in their own words?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The Lamb of God</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Where is the lamb?”<br>“Here is the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: One-sentence prayers (out loud)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying Scripture back can be just one sentence. Take a Bible line, turn it towards God, and say it out loud. Short is great! <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In the Wow and Thanks parts, anyone can pray one sentence out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one line from this week’s verses back to God.<br>Prompts: You are the LORD who provides! / You are real, and you reward everyone who searches for you!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you are the God who provides!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: have you worried about something instead of trusting God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for worrying instead of bringing it to you. / Sorry for forgetting that you already gave me Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for worrying instead of trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray John 1:29 back to Jesus, out loud:<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus — you are the Lamb of God. / Thank you for taking my place and taking away my sin.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus — the Lamb of God who takes away our sin!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">One-sentence prayers for this week’s worries — yours or a friend’s.<br>Prompts: Please provide for my family with… / Please help (name) trust you with…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you will provide — help us trust you this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish by praying Hebrews 11:1 together: ‘Faith makes us sure of what we hope for…’ Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first. Don’t force anyone to pray aloud — silent prayers count.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith That Marches — Rahab, Joshua and the walls of Jericho.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2: Faith That Marches":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:1–9; 2; 3:14–17; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Us</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take God’s promise in Joshua 1:9 and pray it back to him: ‘I will be there to help you wherever you go’ becomes ‘God, you are with me — at school, at home, wherever I go.’ Praying his words back reminds us that he means them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Red Rope</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Rahab hid the two spies from Israel (Joshua 2).<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Red Rope → Whose side are you on? → Joshua 2:9, 11–12, 21 → Rahab and the spies → Hidden on the roof → Why help the enemy? → The Red Rope → The wrong person? → Head · Heart · Hands → On the rooftop → Memory verse peek → Praying Rahab’s words (in pairs)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Feet in the River</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God told Joshua, “Be strong and brave,” and promised to be with him (Joshua 1).<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Feet in the River → Recap → Joshua 1:2, 5, 9 · 3:15–16 → Be strong and brave → Word study: “With you” → The moment their feet touched → Step in first → Where courage comes from → Head · Heart · Hands → God speaks to Joshua → Build the verse → Standing firm like the priests</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Round and Round Jericho</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Israel obeyed God’s strange plan to march around Jericho (Joshua 6).<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Round and Round Jericho → A strange set of instructions → Joshua 6:3–5, 20 → The walls fall down → Count the sevens → Round and Round Jericho → Why such a strange plan? → The loudest shout? → Head · Heart · Hands → The walls fall → Verse with actions → Marching in silence</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Rahab’s Family Tree</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning that Rahab has a place in the Faith Museum (Hebrews 11:30–31).<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Rahab’s Family Tree → True or false? → Hebrews 11:30–31 · Matthew 1:5 → From Jericho to Jesus → Rahab’s famous family → Into the museum → This Week’s Big Idea → Under the red rope → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → From Rahab to Jesus → Write a “You are with me” prayer</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Red Rope":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:1–9; 2; 3:14–17; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Red Rope</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take God’s promise in Joshua 1:9 and pray it back to him: ‘I will be there to help you wherever you go’ becomes ‘God, you are with me — at school, at home, wherever I go.’ Praying his words back reminds us that he means them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Rahab hid the two spies from Israel (Joshua 2).</li><li>We are learning that Rahab trusted the LORD because she believed he “rules heaven and earth”.</li><li>I can explain why Rahab’s red rope was a sign of her faith.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Rahab hid the spies because she believed the LORD was the true God, and that the red rope in her window showed she trusted the spies’ promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a length of red wool or ribbon</li><li>Optional: a cloth to be the flax on the roof</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Red Rope</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome back to the Faith Museum. If you have red wool, hang it somewhere visible for the whole week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Whose side are you on?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever had to choose between doing what everyone around you was doing — and doing what you knew was right?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What makes that kind of choice so hard?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we climbed Mount Moriah with Abraham and Isaac, and Abraham named the place “The LORD Will Provide.” Your challenge: hand God one worry each day and say, “The LORD will provide.” Did you try it? What happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students how the worry challenge went; celebrate small stories and honest ‘I forgot’ answers. Then the hook — take ideas: fear of being left out, being laughed at, getting in trouble. Land it: ‘Today’s exhibit in the Faith Museum is a piece of red rope. It belonged to a woman who lived in the wrong city, among the wrong people — and she chose to trust the true God when no one else around her did.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Joshua 2:9, 11–12, 21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 2:9</b> I know that the LORD has given Israel this land.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 2:11</b> The LORD your God rules heaven and earth.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 2:12</b> Please promise me in the LORD’s name that you will be as kind to my family as I have been to you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 2:21</b> She tied the red rope to the window.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>These are Rahab’s own words. ‘The LORD’ in capitals is God’s personal name. Notice: Rahab isn’t from Israel — yet she calls Israel’s God the ruler of heaven and earth.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Rahab and the spies</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome back to the Faith Museum. Today’s exhibit is a little unusual. There’s no sword, no crown, no shepherd’s staff. Just a piece of red rope, hanging in a window. It belonged to a woman called Rahab.</p><p>After forty years in the desert, God’s people, Israel, were camped beside the Jordan River. Across the river was the land God had promised to Abraham. Moses had died, and God had chosen a new leader: Joshua.</p><p>But right in the way stood Jericho — a city with huge, thick walls. So Joshua sent two spies to look around.</p><p>The spies slipped into Jericho and went to Rahab’s house, which was built right into the city wall. Rahab was not the kind of person most people expected God to use. She wasn’t from Israel. She hadn’t grown up hearing about the true God. And she had lived the kind of life that made people look down on her.</p><p>That night someone told the king, ‘Spies from Israel are in the city!’ The king sent his men straight to Rahab’s door. ‘Bring out those men!’</p><p>Now Rahab had to choose. Hand the spies over and be safe? Or protect them and risk her own life?</p><p>Quickly she took the spies up onto her flat roof and hid them under piles of flax — long stalks drying in the sun. Then she sent the king’s men off on a wild chase towards the river.</p><p>Why would she do that? Rahab told the spies, ‘I know that the LORD has given Israel this land. We heard how he dried up the Red Sea for you. Everyone here is terrified. The LORD your God rules heaven and earth.’</p><p>Do you see? Everyone in Jericho had heard the same stories. Everyone was afraid. But only Rahab turned towards God. She believed he was the real God — the God of heaven and earth — and she decided to trust him.</p><p>‘Please,’ she said, ‘promise in the LORD’s name that you will be kind to my family, as I have been kind to you.’</p><p>The spies promised. ‘Tie this red rope in your window,’ they said. ‘Bring your whole family into this house. Everyone inside will be safe.’</p><p>Rahab let the spies down from her window on a rope, over the wall, and they escaped into the hills.</p><p>Then Rahab tied the red rope in her window. And she waited — trusting the God she had only just come to know.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Two spies slip into Jericho. · 2. “Bring out those men!” · 3. “The LORD your God rules heaven and earth.” · 4. “Tie this red rope in your window.” · 5. Rahab tied the red rope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The Bible calls Rahab a prostitute (Joshua 2:1); for this age group ‘she had lived the kind of life that made people look down on her’ is honest and enough. Rahab told the king’s men a lie (2:4–5) — the Bible praises her faith (Hebrews 11:31), not her lie; if a student asks, say exactly that. The Red Sea is Term 1 Week 6.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Hidden on the roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did Rahab hide the two spies?<br>A) In a cellar under the house &nbsp; B) Under stalks of flax on her flat roof ✓ &nbsp; C) In the king’s palace &nbsp; D) Down a well</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Joshua 2:6). Follow-up: ‘What was Rahab risking by hiding them?’ (Her own life — she was going against the king.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did the spies get out of the city?’ (Down a rope from her window in the city wall.) If silent: show the first story picture again and ask students to describe what Rahab is doing.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why help the enemy?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone in Jericho had heard what God did for Israel. Everyone was afraid. Why do you think only Rahab chose to help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she believed the stories were true; she decided the LORD was the real God, not just a scary one; she wanted to be on God’s side; she cared about her family; fear made others hide, but faith made Rahab act. Follow-up: ‘What is the difference between being afraid of God and trusting God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Rahab say about God in verse 11?’ (He rules heaven and earth.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line and ask, ‘What does Rahab believe?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Red Rope</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Red Rope</b> (Joshua 2:1–21). The main events and Rahab’s words about God are from Joshua 2 (CEV), lightly shortened. The spies are unnamed in the Bible; their exact conversation and the soldier’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Rahab</b> — Quick-thinking and brave — she has decided to trust Israel’s God<br><b>Spy One</b> — A spy from Israel — nervous<br><b>Spy Two</b> — A spy from Israel — calm<br><b>Soldier</b> — The king of Jericho’s soldier — loud and bossy<br><b>Everyone</b> — Tiptoes, door-banging and a swishing rope</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: tiptoeing feet · Everyone: banging on the door · Everyone: the rope swishing down the wall<br>Props: Optional: red wool or ribbon for the rope · Optional: a cloth over two chairs for the flax<br>Cast a confident Rahab and a loud Soldier. Keep the soldiers’ part playful, not scary. If a student notices Rahab’s lie to the soldiers, say: ‘The Bible tells us what she did, and praises her faith — not her lie.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Night in Jericho. Two spies from Israel slip through the city gate.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Tiptoe… tiptoe… tiptoe… <i>(fingers tiptoeing on desks)</i></li><li><b>Spy One:</b> Quick — in here. This house is built right into the city wall. <i>(look over shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Rahab:</b> You’re not from Jericho, are you? You’re from Israel. <i>(hands on hips)</i></li><li><b>Spy Two:</b> Please — we only need somewhere to stay tonight.</li><li class='dir'>But someone has seen them. The king sends his soldiers.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> BANG! BANG! BANG! <i>(pound fists on desks)</i></li><li><b>Soldier:</b> Open up, in the name of the king! Bring out the men who came to your house!</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> Quick — up to the roof! Hide under the flax! <i>(whisper, point up)</i></li><li><b>Spy One:</b> Flax? It’s scratchy! <i>(wriggle)</i></li><li><b>Spy Two:</b> Scratchy is better than caught. Get down!</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> Some men did come here. But they left when the gate was closing. Hurry, and you might catch them! <i>(point far away)</i></li><li><b>Soldier:</b> After them! To the river! <i>(run on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Thud, thud, thud, thud… <i>(footsteps fading away)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Rahab has just risked her life for two strangers. Why would she do that? (20s)</li><li class='dir'>Rahab climbs up to the roof.</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> I know that the LORD has given Israel this land. Everyone here is shaking with fear.</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> We heard how the LORD dried up the Red Sea for you. <i>(eyes wide)</i></li><li><b>Rahab:</b> The LORD your God rules heaven and earth. <i>(look up)</i></li><li><b>Spy Two:</b> You believe in our God? Here — in Jericho?</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> I do. Please promise in the LORD’s name to be kind to my family, as I have been kind to you.</li><li><b>Spy One:</b> We promise. Keep our secret, and we’ll keep you safe. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Spy Two:</b> Tie this red rope in your window. Bring your whole family inside this house.</li><li class='dir'>Rahab lets them down from her window on a rope, right over the city wall.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Swish… creak… swish… <i>(rope sliding down)</i></li><li><b>Rahab:</b> Hide in the hills for three days. Then go home.</li><li><b>Spy One:</b> Thank you, Rahab! Remember — the red rope! <i>(wave)</i></li><li class='dir'>Rahab ties the red rope in her window. And she waits.</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> The LORD rules heaven and earth. I will trust him. <i>(tie a knot)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The red rope! <i>(point to the window)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab said, “I will trust him.” What did her trust make her DO?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She hid the spies, sent the soldiers away, asked for a promise in the LORD’s name, and tied the red rope in her window — faith that acted.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the spies tell Rahab to put in her window?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: A red rope — the sign that everyone in her house would be kept safe (Joshua 2:18).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The wrong person?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab came from the ‘wrong’ city and had done wrong things — yet she ended up in the Faith Museum. What does that tell us about who God welcomes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God welcomes anyone who turns to him, not only ‘good’ people or people from the right family; it’s faith, not your past, that matters to God; no one is too far away; God loves to rescue people others look down on. Follow-up: ‘Who might people today think is too far from God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How should that change the way we treat people?’ If silent: ask ‘Did Rahab earn her rescue, or did she trust God for it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith means choosing God’s side, even when everyone around me is doing something else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, you rule heaven and earth — I want to be on your side.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when the people around you are doing something unkind — in the playground, at home or online — be the one who chooses differently, like Rahab.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask 2–3 students for a real example of ‘choosing differently’ — without naming anyone who was unkind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: On the rooftop</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Quick — hide under the flax!”<br>“The LORD your God rules heaven and earth.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Joshua 1:9 — God’s promise to Joshua, the leader who sent the spies. Echo it chunk by chunk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying Rahab’s words (in pairs)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We’re still learning to pray Scripture back. Today we take Rahab’s words about God and pray them back to him — with a partner, one sentence each. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. You’ll take turns praying one short sentence each. Rahab trusted God in the dark of night — let’s trust him now.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Joshua 2:11 back to God:<br>Prompts: LORD, you rule heaven and earth. / You are the true God — the God Rahab chose to trust.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you rule…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you rule heaven and earth!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: was there a time you went along with the crowd instead of choosing God’s way?<br>Prompts: Sorry for joining in when others were unkind. / Sorry for being afraid of what people think more than trusting you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for following the crowd instead of you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Partner one, then partner two: thank God for welcoming Rahab — and us.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you welcomed Rahab, even though others looked down on her. / Thank you that no one is too far away for you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, that you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you welcome everyone who trusts you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Rahab’s request from Joshua 2:12 for your own family.<br>Prompts: Please be kind to my family and keep them safe. / Please help (name) in my family to trust you too.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, be kind to…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, please be kind to our families, as you were to Rahab’s.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pairs pray quietly, one sentence each. Anyone who prefers can pray silently while their partner prays. Model a one-sentence prayer first.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Joshua hears God say, “Be strong and brave!” — and the priests step into a flooded river.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Feet in the River":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:1–9; 2; 3:14–17; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Feet in the River</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take God’s promise in Joshua 1:9 and pray it back to him: ‘I will be there to help you wherever you go’ becomes ‘God, you are with me — at school, at home, wherever I go.’ Praying his words back reminds us that he means them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God told Joshua, “Be strong and brave,” and promised to be with him (Joshua 1).</li><li>We are learning how God stopped the Jordan River when the priests stepped in (Joshua 3).</li><li>I can explain where Joshua’s courage came from.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Joshua’s courage came from God’s promise to be with him, and that the priests had to step into the water BEFORE the river stopped.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl of water or a blue cloth for the river</li><li>Optional: twelve small stones</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Feet in the River</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today has a big ‘wow’ moment — build up to the priests’ first step.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we found a red rope in the Faith Museum.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who did Rahab hide — and what did she believe about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>She hid two spies from Israel under flax on her roof. She said, ‘The LORD your God rules heaven and earth.’ She tied a red rope in her window and waited. Then: ‘Today we go back to the man who sent those spies — Joshua. He has a huge job, a flooded river in front of him, and a promise from God.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Joshua 1:2, 5, 9 · 3:15–16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 1:2</b> My servant Moses is dead. Now you must lead Israel across the Jordan River into the land I’m giving to all of you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 1:5</b> I will always be with you and help you as I helped Moses.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 1:9</b> I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 3:15–16</b> But as soon as the priests stepped into the water, the river stopped flowing.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Joshua 1:9 is our memory verse. ‘Discouraged’ = losing heart, wanting to give up. Stress ‘as soon as’ in the last line: the priests stepped in FIRST.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Be strong and brave</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Before Israel could reach Jericho, they had to do something big. They had to cross a river.</p><p>But first, let’s meet Joshua properly. For forty years he had been Moses’ helper. He was there when God split the Red Sea. Years earlier he had been one of only two spies who said, ‘We can do this — God is with us!’ Now Moses had died. And suddenly Joshua was in charge of a whole nation.</p><p>How do you think Joshua felt? Maybe excited. Maybe scared stiff.</p><p>Then God spoke to him. ‘My servant Moses is dead. Now you must lead Israel across the Jordan River into the land I’m giving you. I will always be with you and help you, as I helped Moses.’</p><p>Three times God said, ‘Be strong and brave!’ And then he said the words that are our memory verse: ‘I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.’</p><p>Notice something. God didn’t say, ‘Be brave because you’re a great leader.’ He said, ‘Be brave because I am with you.’ Joshua’s courage would come from God.</p><p>Now for the river. It was harvest time, and the Jordan was flooded — running high, fast and muddy, right over its banks. There was no bridge. There were no boats. And God’s plan sounded very strange.</p><p>The priests were to carry the sacred chest — the gold-covered box that reminded Israel that God was with them — and walk straight into the river. Not wait for the water to stop. Walk in first.</p><p>Imagine being one of those priests. The river is roaring. The chest is heavy on your shoulders. Everyone is watching. You step closer… and closer… and you put your foot into the cold, rushing water.</p><p>And as soon as their feet touched the water, the river stopped flowing! Far upstream the water piled up in a heap. Below them, the riverbed began to dry. The priests walked out into the middle and stood there on dry ground, holding the chest.</p><p>Then the whole nation walked across — mums and dads, grandparents, children, babies, sheep and goats — while the priests stood firm in the middle. Not one person got wet feet.</p><p>When everyone was across, Joshua had twelve men carry twelve big stones from the riverbed and pile them up on the other side. ‘One day,’ he said, ‘your children will ask, “What do these stones mean?” Tell them: God stopped the Jordan River so we could cross, just as he dried up the Red Sea.’</p><p>God had kept his word. He was with Joshua, exactly as he had been with Moses. And soon he would show it again — at Jericho.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Be strong and brave!” · 2. A flooded river. · 3. The priests step in… · 4. Everyone crossed on dry ground. · 5. Twelve stones.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Joshua and Caleb were the two faithful spies in Numbers 13–14. ‘Three times’ = Joshua 1:6, 7 and 9. The ‘sacred chest’ is the ark of the covenant (Joshua 3:3) — not Noah’s ark; explain the difference if asked. The flood at harvest time is Joshua 3:15; the water heaped up ‘at the town of Adam’ (3:16). Twelve stones: Joshua 4:1–7.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Word study: “With you”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God did the same things for Joshua that he did for Moses. Can you match them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>God with Moses</th><th>God with Joshua</th></tr><tr><td>“I will be with you” (Exodus 3:12)</td><td>“I will always be with you” (Joshua 1:5)</td></tr><tr><td>God parted the Red Sea</td><td>God stopped the Jordan River</td></tr><tr><td>Moses led God’s people OUT of Egypt</td><td>Joshua led God’s people INTO the promised land</td></tr><tr><td>God was with them then…</td><td>…and God is with us now</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Joshua’s courage didn’t come from Joshua. It came from God being WITH him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. The key idea of the week: God is ‘With Us’. Jesus’ last words in Matthew 28:20 echo it: ‘I will be with you always.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The moment their feet touched</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened as soon as the priests’ feet touched the water of the Jordan?<br>A) They were swept away &nbsp; B) The river stopped flowing ✓ &nbsp; C) A bridge appeared &nbsp; D) It started to rain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Joshua 3:15–16). Follow-up: ‘What did the priests carry into the river?’ (The sacred chest — a sign that God was with them.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Joshua build on the other side, and why?’ (Twelve stones, so children would ask and hear what God did.) If silent: re-read the last scripture line and ask students to spot the moment the river stopped.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Step in first</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t stop the river first and then send the priests in. They had to step in FIRST. Why do you think God did it that way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it showed whether they really trusted God; faith acts before it sees; it made it clear God did it, not them; it’s like the chair from last week — you have to sit down. Follow-up: ‘How do you think the priests felt just before that first step?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What “first step” might God be asking you to take?’ If silent: ask ‘Would it be faith if the river had already stopped?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where courage comes from</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t promise Joshua, “Nothing will ever be scary.” He promised, “I will be there to help you.” Why is that a better promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: scary things will still come, but we are never alone in them; God being with us is bigger than any problem; courage isn’t ‘no fear’ — it’s trusting God while you feel afraid; God keeps that promise to us through Jesus, who said ‘I will be with you always.’ Follow-up: ‘Can you be brave and scared at the same time?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where do you most need to remember God is with you?’ If silent: think quietly about a scary place, then imagine God standing right beside you there.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">My courage doesn’t come from me — it comes from God being with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, you are with me wherever I go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When something feels scary this week — a new sport, a hard task in class, talking to someone new — say Joshua 1:9 quietly and take the first step, like the priests.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite students to whisper ‘The LORD is with me’ before they stand up from their seats at break.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: God speaks to Joshua</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be strong and brave!”<br>“I will be there to help you wherever you go.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Joshua 1:9. Ask: ‘Which part of this verse did Joshua need most at the river?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Standing firm like the priests</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The priests stood firm in the middle of the river. Today we stand to pray, and we pray Joshua 1:9 back to God: ‘You are with me.’ <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone stand up. Plant your feet like the priests in the riverbed. God is with us right here.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Joshua 1:9 back: “I am the LORD your God.”<br>Prompts: You are the LORD my God — and you stopped a river! / You are with me wherever I go.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, LORD — you are with us wherever we go!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bow your head: is there something God has asked you to do that you’ve been too scared to start?<br>Prompts: Sorry for waiting for the river to stop before I trust you. / Sorry for being discouraged and wanting to give up.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for being afraid to take the first step.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your head: thank God for times he has been with you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for being with me when I felt scared. / Thank you that you helped Joshua just as you helped Moses.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are always with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Feet firm: ask God for courage for one real thing this week.<br>Prompts: Please make me strong and brave when I… / Please help me take the first step with…<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help me be strong and brave…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, make us strong and brave — you are with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stay standing as we say Joshua 1:9 together… Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students who can’t stand can sit up tall with feet flat on the floor. Keep the energy calm — strong, not silly.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the strangest battle plan in the Bible — marching round and round Jericho.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Round and Round Jericho":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:1–9; 2; 3:14–17; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Round and Round Jericho</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take God’s promise in Joshua 1:9 and pray it back to him: ‘I will be there to help you wherever you go’ becomes ‘God, you are with me — at school, at home, wherever I go.’ Praying his words back reminds us that he means them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Israel obeyed God’s strange plan to march around Jericho (Joshua 6).</li><li>We are learning that the walls fell because of God’s power, not Israel’s strength.</li><li>I can explain how God kept his promise to rescue Rahab and her family.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the plan (once a day for six days, seven times on day seven, trumpets and a shout), saying that God brought the walls down, and that Rahab’s family was rescued because of the promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a cardboard-box ‘wall’</li><li>Optional: a toy trumpet or rolled paper horn</li><li>Optional: red wool for Rahab’s window</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Round and Round Jericho</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If you built a box wall, put it out now. Keep today about God’s power and Rahab’s rescue.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: A strange set of instructions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If someone told you to knock down a castle wall by walking around it and shouting, what would you say?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you do it? Why or why not?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take answers — expect laughter and ‘That won’t work!’. Then recap: ‘Yesterday the priests stepped into a flooded river, and God stopped it. Now Israel is standing in front of Jericho, with its high walls and locked gates. And Rahab’s red rope is still hanging in her window. Today God gives Joshua the strangest plan in the Bible.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Joshua 6:3–5, 20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 6:3</b> March slowly around Jericho once a day for six days.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 6:4</b> But on the seventh day, march slowly around the town seven times while the priests blow their trumpets.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 6:5</b> When the priests give a loud blast on the trumpets, everyone else will shout. Then the wall will fall down.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Joshua 6:20</b> The walls of Jericho fell flat.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The ‘trumpets’ were rams’ horns (a shofar). Count the sevens together. God’s instructions are lightly shortened here.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The walls fall down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Now Israel was across the river. And there, in front of them, stood Jericho.</p><p>Its gates were shut tight. Nobody went out, and nobody came in. Its walls were high and thick — so thick that people, like Rahab, had houses built right into them. How could a nation of travellers and shepherds ever get past walls like that?</p><p>God gave Joshua a plan. And it might be the strangest plan in the whole Bible.</p><p>‘March around the city once a day for six days. Seven priests will carry trumpets made from rams’ horns and walk in front of the sacred chest. On the seventh day, march around the city seven times. Then the priests will blow a long blast, everyone will shout — and the walls will fall down.’</p><p>No ladders. No tunnels. No battering rams. Just walking, trumpets and a shout. Oh — and one more thing. Joshua told the people, ‘Don’t say a word until I give the signal.’</p><p>So on day one, Israel marched. The priests blew their horns, the chest went round, and thousands of feet tramped around the city in silence. Then everyone went back to camp.</p><p>Day two — the same. Day three, four, five, six. Round once, and home again.</p><p>Can you imagine the people of Jericho watching from the walls? Maybe they laughed at first. Maybe by day four they were getting nervous. And somewhere up on the wall, beside a window with a red rope hanging from it, Rahab was watching too — and trusting.</p><p>Then came day seven. The people got up at dawn. Once around… twice… three times… four, five, six… seven times.</p><p>The priests lifted their horns and gave a long, loud blast. Joshua called out, ‘Shout! The LORD has given you this city!’</p><p>And the people shouted — the biggest shout you can imagine. And the great walls of Jericho shook… cracked… and fell down flat!</p><p>Nobody pushed them. No machine knocked them over. God did it.</p><p>Then Joshua remembered the promise. He sent the two spies straight to the house with the red rope. They brought out Rahab, her father and mother, her brothers and sisters and everyone with her — every single one safe. Rahab had trusted God, and God had kept her safe.</p><p>From that day on, Rahab and her family lived among God’s people. The woman from Jericho had become part of Israel.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Round and round Jericho. · 2. Day 2… 3… 4… 5… 6… · 3. Day 7: seven times around! · 4. “SHOUT!” · 5. The house with the red rope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. HANDLE WITH CARE: Joshua 6:21 records the destruction of the city. This lesson deliberately keeps the focus on God’s power and Rahab’s rescue, as the brief asks. If a student asks what happened to the people of Jericho, answer simply and calmly: ‘The Bible says God was judging the city for a long time of great wrongdoing. Everyone had heard about God — Rahab said so — and the one person who turned to him was rescued with her whole family.’ Rahab’s rescue: Joshua 6:22–25.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Count the sevens</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “On the seventh day, they marched around Jericho…”<br><i>Answer: “…seven times” — then the priests blew a long blast, the people shouted, and the walls fell flat. (Joshua 6:4–5, 20)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘How many times did they march around on each of the first six days?’ (Once.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Joshua tell the people NOT to do while they marched?’ (Not to say a word until his signal — Joshua 6:10.) If silent: count the days on your fingers together, then point to the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Round and Round Jericho</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Round and Round Jericho</b> (Joshua 6:1–25). God’s instructions, Joshua’s commands and the rescue of Rahab are from Joshua 6 (CEV), lightly shortened. Miri, the Israelite girl, is imagined, and so are Rahab’s lines from the wall. The fall of the city is kept to the walls falling — no fighting is acted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>God</b> — Heard, never seen<br><b>Joshua</b> — Israel’s leader — strong, because God is with him<br><b>Priest</b> — Blows the ram’s horn trumpet<br><b>Miri</b> — An Israelite girl with sore feet and lots of questions (imagined)<br><b>Rahab</b> — Watching from her window with the red rope<br><b>Everyone</b> — Marching feet, counting, and one ENORMOUS shout</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: marching on the spot (quietly!) · Everyone: counting the seven laps on fingers · Everyone: the great shout and the walls tumbling down<br>Props: Optional: rolled-paper trumpets for the Priest · Optional: red wool for Rahab’s window<br>SENSITIVE SCRIPT: the play ends with the walls falling and Rahab’s rescue — no fighting is acted. The God voice can be read by the teacher, from the back of the room, for an ‘unseen’ feel. Let the counting build and the shout be HUGE (warn the class next door!).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Jericho’s gates are shut tight. No one goes out. No one comes in.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> CLANG! <i>(gates slamming shut)</i></li><li><b>God:</b> Joshua, with my help you will capture Jericho.</li><li><b>God:</b> March around the town once a day for six days. Seven priests with trumpets will go in front of the sacred chest.</li><li><b>God:</b> On the seventh day, march around seven times. Then the trumpets will blast, everyone will shout — and the wall will fall down.</li><li><b>Joshua:</b> Yes, LORD. <i>(bow head)</i></li><li><b>Joshua:</b> Listen, everyone! Priests, take your trumpets. Everyone else — not a word until I give the signal! <i>(arm raised)</i></li><li><b>Miri:</b> Not a word? How do you knock down a wall by… walking? <i>(scratch head)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Stomp… stomp… stomp… <i>(march quietly on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Priest:</b> Toooot! Toooot! <i>(blow a pretend horn)</i></li><li class='dir'>High on the wall, Rahab watches. Her red rope hangs in the window.</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> They’re marching. Just… marching. And not one of them is talking! <i>(lean out of window)</i></li><li class='dir'>Day two. Day three. Four. Five. Six. Around once, then back to camp.</li><li><b>Miri:</b> My feet hurt. Are we sure this is the plan? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Joshua:</b> It’s God’s plan. It seems strange, I know. But remember the river? God has never let us down. <i>(whisper back)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The plan seems strange. Why might God choose a plan like this? (20s)</li><li class='dir'>Day seven. Up at dawn. Around they go…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> One… two… three… four… five… six… SEVEN! <i>(count on fingers)</i></li><li><b>Priest:</b> Toooooooooot! <i>(one long blast)</i></li><li><b>Joshua:</b> SHOUT! The LORD has given you this town! <i>(both arms up)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> HOORAY! <i>(the biggest shout ever)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Rumble… rumble… CRASH! <i>(hands tumbling down like falling walls)</i></li><li><b>Miri:</b> The walls fell flat! And nobody even touched them! <i>(jump up and down)</i></li><li><b>Rahab:</b> Everyone stay together! The red rope is still in the window! <i>(hug your family)</i></li><li><b>Joshua:</b> Go to Rahab’s house and bring out her and her family, just as you promised her.</li><li class='dir'>The two spies run to the house with the red rope.</li><li><b>Rahab:</b> You came! You kept your promise! <i>(arms wide)</i></li><li><b>Joshua:</b> God kept HIS promise, Rahab. You and your family are safe with us now.</li><li><b>Miri:</b> So the strange plan wasn’t so strange after all. <i>(big smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Be strong and brave! The LORD is with us!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Miri asked, “Are we sure this is the plan?” Why did Israel keep marching anyway?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They trusted God — he had already stopped the Jordan River. Hebrews 11:30: ‘Because of their faith, the people walked around the city of Jericho for seven days, and its walls fell down.’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who was rescued from Jericho, and why?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Rahab and her whole family — because she trusted God and hid the spies, and they kept their promise (Joshua 6:22–25).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why such a strange plan?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God could have knocked the walls down any way he liked. Why do you think he chose marching, trumpets and a shout?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: so everyone would know GOD did it, not soldiers or clever machines; to test whether Israel would trust and obey; to give Jericho seven days to see and turn to God, like Rahab; strange plans make faith visible. Follow-up: ‘How do you think it felt to march in silence for six days?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this teach us when God’s way seems odd to us?’ If silent: ask ‘If Israel had used a battering ram, who would get the credit?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The loudest shout?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “The walls fell because the Israelites shouted really loudly.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students vote, then dig. The shout didn’t have magic power — the power was God’s. Hebrews 11:30 says it happened ‘because of their faith’: their marching and shouting were trust in action, and God did the rest. Follow-up: ‘So what part did Israel play, and what part did God play?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What is one thing God asks you to do, where you have to trust him for the rest?’ If silent: remind them of the priests stepping into the river — they stepped, God stopped it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith keeps obeying God’s plan, even when it seems strange or slow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I trust your way — even when I don’t understand it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one thing God asks that seems ‘strange’ — like being kind to someone who wasn’t kind to you first — and do it this week, at school, at home or in a team.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge. Brainstorm a few ‘strange’ acts of obedience as a class: forgive, share, tell the truth when it costs you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The walls fall</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“SHOUT!”<br>The walls of Jericho fell flat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Joshua 1:9 — finish by marching on the spot for ‘wherever you go’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Marching in silence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Israel marched around Jericho in silence, trusting God. Today we pray in silence too — praying Joshua 1:9 back to God in our heads. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your eyes. Imagine walking quietly around Jericho, step by step. No talking — just you and God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently pray back: “I am the LORD your God.”<br>Prompts: You are the LORD — you made the walls of Jericho fall flat. / Your power is bigger than any wall.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, your power…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, LORD — no wall is too big for you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there a time you grumbled or gave up because God’s way seemed strange?<br>Prompts: Sorry for complaining when obeying you felt slow. / Sorry for trusting my own plans more than yours.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for grumbling instead of trusting.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently thank God for keeping his promise to Rahab.<br>Prompts: Thank you for rescuing Rahab and her whole family. / Thank you that you always keep your promises.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you keep every promise.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray Joshua 1:9 back: “You are with me.” Name one ‘wall’ in your life.<br>Prompts: You are with me, God — please help me with the wall of… / Please help me keep obeying, even when I don’t understand.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are with me. Please…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you are with us — help us keep marching.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now, after the silence — one big whispered shout: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read each lead slowly and leave real silence. The whispered ‘Amen!’ is a fun echo of Israel’s shout.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what happened to Rahab next — all the way to Jesus’ family tree.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Rahab’s Family Tree":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joshua 1:1–9; 2; 3:14–17; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rahab’s Family Tree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying Scripture back — Take God’s promise in Joshua 1:9 and pray it back to him: ‘I will be there to help you wherever you go’ becomes ‘God, you are with me — at school, at home, wherever I go.’ Praying his words back reminds us that he means them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Rahab has a place in the Faith Museum (Hebrews 11:30–31).</li><li>We are learning that Rahab became part of Jesus’ family line (Matthew 1:5).</li><li>I can say Joshua 1:9 and explain what it promises.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can sort true and false statements about the week’s stories, explain why Rahab is in Hebrews 11 and Jesus’ family tree, and recite Joshua 1:9.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: a long piece of red wool to make a ‘family tree’ line</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Rahab’s Family Tree</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate: another exhibit added to the Faith Museum!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? March on the spot for TRUE, sit still for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab hid the spies under stalks of flax on her roof. — <b>True</b> (She hid them on her flat roof (Joshua 2:6).)<br>God told Joshua, “Be strong and brave.” — <b>True</b> (He said it three times (Joshua 1:6, 7, 9).)<br>The Jordan River stopped before the priests stepped in. — <b>False</b> (It stopped as soon as their feet touched the water.)<br>Israel shouted all the way around Jericho every day. — <b>False</b> (They marched in silence until Joshua gave the signal.)<br>Rahab and her whole family were rescued. — <b>True</b> (The spies kept their promise (Joshua 6:22–25).)<br>The walls fell because the soldiers pushed them over. — <b>False</b> (God made them fall flat.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students march for true, sit for false; click to reveal. Ask for the ‘why’ before you show it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Hebrews 11:30–31 · Matthew 1:5</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:30</b> Because of their faith, the people walked around the city of Jericho for seven days, and its walls fell down.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:31</b> Rahab… had faith and welcomed the spies.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 1:5</b> Salmon and Rahab were the parents of Boaz. Boaz and Ruth were the parents of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 1 is Jesus’ family tree. Jesse’s son is King David (Matthew 1:6), and Jesus is ‘the son of David’. Only a few women are named in the list — Rahab is one of them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: From Jericho to Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Rahab’s story could have ended with the walls falling: rescued, safe and living with God’s people. But God wasn’t finished with her.</p><p>Rahab married an Israelite man called Salmon, and they had a son called Boaz. Boaz married a kind woman called Ruth, who was also from another nation. Their son was Obed. Obed’s son was Jesse. And Jesse’s youngest son was a shepherd boy who fought a giant — David! Rahab was King David’s great-great-grandmother.</p><p>And the family kept going. About a thousand years later, from that same family, in David’s town of Bethlehem, a baby was born. His name was Jesus. When Matthew wrote down Jesus’ family tree, he made sure to include Rahab’s name.</p><p>Think about that. A woman from the wrong city, who had done wrong things, whom people looked down on — and God put her into the family of his own Son.</p><p>Then, many years later, when the writer of Hebrews built his Faith Museum, he gave Rahab her very own exhibit, close to Abraham and Moses. ‘Rahab… had faith and welcomed the spies.’</p><p>Rahab’s faith wasn’t big or perfect. But she trusted the right God, and she acted on it. She hid the spies. She tied the red rope. She waited. And when the walls came down, God kept every promise made to her.</p><p>That’s good news for us too. Nobody is too far away for God. Nobody’s past is too messy. Jesus came from a family full of people who needed rescuing — and he came to rescue people like us. All we need to do is what Rahab did: trust him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Rescued — and welcomed. · 2. Rahab → Boaz → Obed → Jesse → David… · 3. …and one day, Jesus. · 4. Nobody is too far away for God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Matthew 1:5–6 lists Salmon, Boaz, Obed, Jesse and David; some families in biblical lists skip generations, so ‘great-great-grandmother’ follows Matthew’s list. Ruth (from Moab) is another outsider welcomed into God’s family. Hebrews 11:31 calls Rahab ‘an immoral woman’; ‘had done wrong things’ is enough for this age. Jesus is not drawn in this scene — only the glowing stable.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Rahab’s famous family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which famous king came from Rahab’s family?<br>A) King Saul &nbsp; B) King David ✓ &nbsp; C) The king of Jericho &nbsp; D) King Pharaoh</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Matthew 1:5–6). Follow-up: ‘And who was born, much later, from David’s family?’ (Jesus.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who was Rahab’s son, and whom did he marry?’ (Boaz, who married Ruth.) If silent: read the third scripture line slowly and follow the names with your finger.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Into the museum</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hebrews 11 puts Rahab in the Faith Museum alongside Abraham and Moses. Why do you think she earned an exhibit?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she trusted the true God when everyone around her didn’t; her faith was brave — she risked her life; her faith acted — she hid the spies and tied the rope; she shows faith isn’t only for ‘religious’ or ‘good’ people. Follow-up: ‘What would you put in Rahab’s display case?’ (The red rope, the flax.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who else this week showed faith by doing something?’ (The priests, Joshua, the marchers.) If silent: re-read Hebrews 11:31 on the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — like stepping into a river or marching round a wall.</li><li>Joshua could be strong and brave because God promised, “I will be there to help you wherever you go.”</li><li>Rahab trusted the true God, was rescued, and became part of Jesus’ family.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith obeys God’s plan, even when it seems strange — because God is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Ask a student to say it in their own words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Under the red rope</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rahab was safe because she trusted God and stayed in the house with the red rope. How is that like trusting Jesus to rescue us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Rahab couldn’t save herself — she needed a rescue and a promise; we can’t fix our wrong things ourselves — Jesus rescues us; she had to trust the promise and stay ‘under’ it; Jesus welcomes anyone who trusts him, whatever their past. Note: like the Passover blood on the doorposts (Term 1), the red rope marked a house of rescue. Follow-up: ‘What did Rahab have to do to be saved?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Write one sentence: Jesus rescues people like…’ If silent: ask ‘Was Rahab saved because she was good, or because she trusted?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do one “strange” act of obedience — something God asks that seems odd. For example, be kind to someone who wasn’t kind to you first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday (Gideon hiding in the winepress) checks in on this. Brainstorm ideas: forgive someone, share when you’d rather not, own up when no one would know.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve commanded you to be strong and brave. Don’t ever be afraid or discouraged! I am the LORD your God, and I will be there to help you wherever you go.” — Joshua 1:9 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Joshua 1:9. Bonus: who can say it while marching on the spot?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: From Rahab to Jesus</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Rahab… had faith and welcomed the spies.”<br>Salmon and Rahab were the parents of Boaz…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a “You are with me” prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We finish our two weeks of praying Scripture back by writing one. Take Joshua 1:9 and turn it into your own prayer: “You are with me…” <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Grab a pencil and a scrap of paper. Write as little or as much as you like — God reads every word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write Joshua 1:9 as a ‘Wow’: “You are the LORD my God…”<br>Prompts: You are the LORD my God — you stopped a river and brought down a wall. / You are with me wherever I go.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, LORD, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you are our God, and you are with us wherever we go!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: was there a time this week you were afraid or discouraged instead of trusting God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for forgetting you were with me. / Sorry for thinking I was too far away or too messy for you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting that you are with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank-you for Rahab’s story.<br>Prompts: Thank you for welcoming Rahab into your family — and me too. / Thank you that Jesus came to rescue people like us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for coming to rescue us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish: “Because you are with me, please help me…” — think of your strange act of obedience.<br>Prompts: Because you are with me, please help me be kind to… / Please make me strong and brave when…<br>Starter: <b>Because you are with me, please help me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Because you are with us, LORD, help us obey you this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray Joshua 1:9 together, turned into a prayer: “LORD, you are with me wherever I go.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite volunteers to read one line aloud — never force it. Students can take their prayer home with the challenge card.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith When You Feel Small — Gideon, the farmer hiding in a winepress whom God called a mighty warrior.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3: Faith When You Feel Small":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Judges 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Strong</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when you feel small — Gideon told God exactly how small he felt (Judges 6:15) — and God answered, “I am going to help you!” You can pray honestly about feeling small, then lean on God’s strength.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Found Hiding</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning why God’s people were in trouble in the time of the judges.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Found Hiding → Too small? → Judges 6:11–16 → The strong warrior in the pit → Where was Gideon? → Strong warrior? → Found Hiding → Good enough or God with me? → Head · Heart · Hands → Who, me? → Memory verse peek → Small hands, big God</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Fleece</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Gideon obeyed God, even though he was still afraid.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Fleece → Recap → Judges 6:36–40 → Wet wool, dry wool → Look closer: two tests → The first test → Patient God → Honest doubts → Head · Heart · Hands → A bowl full of dew → Build the verse → An honest prayer (write)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Too Many Soldiers!</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God made Gideon’s army smaller on purpose.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Too Many Soldiers! → Pick your team → Judges 7:2, 7, 13–15 → From 32,000 to 300 → Too big → Too Many Soldiers! → A kind God → Harder on purpose? → Head · Heart · Hands → The barley loaf → Verse with actions → Pray for your partner</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Trumpets and Jars</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God gave Gideon’s 300 men victory with trumpets, jars and torches.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Trumpets and Jars → Gideon’s story → Judges 7:18, 20–21 · 2 Corinthians 12:9 → Crash! Blaze! Shout! → Strange weapons → Who won? → This week’s Big Idea → Brave and strong? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Light from broken jars → Small me, big God (out loud)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Found Hiding":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Judges 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Found Hiding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when you feel small — Gideon told God exactly how small he felt (Judges 6:15) — and God answered, “I am going to help you!” You can pray honestly about feeling small, then lean on God’s strength.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why God’s people were in trouble in the time of the judges.</li><li>We are learning how God called Gideon while he was hiding and feeling small.</li><li>I can explain what God promised Gideon when Gideon said he was too weak.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Gideon felt weak and unimportant, and that God’s answer was not ‘you’re great’ but ‘I am going to help you’ (Judges 6:16).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a sheaf of grass or straw for Gideon to ‘thresh’</li><li>Optional: a red or orange cloth for the fire</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Found Hiding</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week’s hero is someone who felt very, very small.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Too small?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been asked to do something and thought, “I’m way too small for this”?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was it? What did you do — hide, say no, or have a go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we marched round Jericho with Joshua and met brave Rahab. Your challenge: do one ‘strange’ act of obedience — something God asks that seems odd. What did you do? How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students about their ‘strange’ obedience (being kind first, forgiving, helping without being asked) and celebrate any attempt. Then the hook: speaking in assembly, a new sports team, standing up to someone bigger. Land it: ‘Today we meet a man who was hiding in a hole when God called him a strong warrior.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Judges 6:11–16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:11</b> Gideon was nearby, threshing grain in a shallow pit, where he could not be seen by the Midianites.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:12</b> The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:15</b> “But how can I rescue Israel? My clan is the weakest one in Manasseh, and everyone else in my family is more important than I am.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:16</b> “Gideon,” the LORD answered, “you can rescue Israel because I am going to help you!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Judges 6:12 is our memory verse. Point out the gap: Gideon is hiding — but God calls him a strong warrior. ‘LORD’ in capitals stands for God’s special personal name.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The strong warrior in the pit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After Joshua died, God’s people lived in the land God had promised them. But again and again they forgot him. They started worshipping idols — statues of made-up gods. And every time they turned away from God, trouble came.</p><p>This time the trouble was the Midianites. Every year, just when the crops were ready, they swept in from the desert on camels — so many that the Bible says they were like a swarm of locusts. They trampled the fields and took the grain, the sheep, the cattle and the donkeys. This went on for seven years. God’s people were so frightened that they hid in caves up in the hills. At last they cried out to the LORD for help.</p><p>Now meet Gideon. Gideon was threshing wheat — beating the stalks to knock the grain loose. Normally you would do that on a windy hilltop, so the breeze could blow the husks away. But Gideon was down in a shallow stone pit, where the Midianites couldn’t see him. He was hiding.</p><p>Then he realised he wasn’t alone. Someone was sitting under the big oak tree. It was the angel of the LORD. And the angel said, ‘The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.’</p><p>A strong warrior? Gideon probably looked over his shoulder to see who the angel was talking to!</p><p>Gideon answered honestly: ‘Sir, if the LORD is helping us, why have all these awful things happened? We’ve heard how he rescued our ancestors from Egypt. But those things happened long ago.’</p><p>Then the LORD said, ‘Gideon, you will be strong, because I am giving you the power to rescue Israel.’</p><p>‘But how can I rescue Israel?’ said Gideon. ‘My clan is the weakest one in Manasseh, and everyone else in my family is more important than I am.’</p><p>Notice what God did NOT say. He didn’t say, ‘Don’t be silly, you’re amazing!’ He said, ‘I am going to help you!’ Gideon’s strength would come from God being with him.</p><p>Gideon brought out a gift of meat and bread. The angel touched it with the tip of his stick — and fire flared up from the rock and burned it all! Then the angel was gone. Gideon was terrified, but the LORD said, ‘Don’t be afraid.’ So Gideon built an altar — a stone table for worshipping God — and called it ‘The LORD Is Peace.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Raiders on camels! · 2. God’s people hid in caves. · 3. A farmer, hiding in a pit. · 4. “The LORD is helping you, strong warrior!” · 5. Fire from the rock!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. ‘Judges’ were leaders God raised up to rescue his people — explain if asked. Most Bibles say Gideon was threshing ‘in a winepress’ (a stone pit for squashing grapes); the CEV says ‘a shallow pit’. The angel of the LORD speaks as the LORD himself (6:14) — keep it simple: God was speaking to Gideon. Altar name from 6:24 (worded differently in different Bibles).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where was Gideon?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where was Gideon when the angel found him?<br>A) On a windy hilltop &nbsp; B) Hiding in a pit, beating wheat ✓ &nbsp; C) Leading an army &nbsp; D) Asleep in a cave</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Judges 6:11). Follow-up: ‘Why was he down there instead of on a windy hill?’ (So the Midianites couldn’t see him — he was scared.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the angel call him?’ (A strong warrior.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Strong warrior?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gideon was hiding — but the angel called him a “strong warrior”. Why do you think God spoke to him like that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God saw what Gideon could become; God’s strength would make him strong; God was with him, so he wasn’t weak any more; God sees us differently from how we see ourselves. Follow-up: ‘What did Gideon think of himself?’ (Weakest, least important.) Follow-up 2: ‘Whose opinion matters more?’ If silent: ask, ‘If God says you’re strong, are you?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Found Hiding</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Found Hiding</b> (Judges 6:1–24). Gideon’s words and the words of the angel and the LORD are from Judges 6:12–23 (CEV), lightly shortened. The neighbour and the raiders’ shouts are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Gideon</b> — Young farmer — nervous, honest, feels tiny<br><b>Angel of the LORD</b> — Speaks God’s words — seen only as light<br><b>Neighbour</b> — Jumpy villager who keeps an eye out for raiders<br><b>Everyone</b> — Midianite raiders, then fire from the rock</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as raiders): galloping camels and shouting · Everyone: a big ‘WHOOSH!’ as fire bursts from the rock<br>Props: Optional: straw or a bundle of grass for Gideon · Optional: a red cloth for the fire<br>The angel’s line ‘Peace! Don’t be afraid’ is spoken by the LORD in 6:23 — we keep one voice for simplicity. In Bible times people believed seeing God’s angel face to face meant death; God reassures Gideon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Israel. Harvest time. But nobody is smiling.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hoo-hoo-HOO! Grab the grain! Take the sheep! <i>(gallop on the spot — raiders on camels)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> They’re back! Run for the caves! <i>(panic, point to the hills)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Down in a stone pit, a young farmer beats his wheat as quietly as he can.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Thump… thump… Shh! If they see me, they’ll take the lot. <i>(crouch low, glance around)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Under the oak tree sits a stranger. It is the angel of the LORD.</li><li><b>Angel of the LORD:</b> The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Me? A strong warrior? <i>(look behind you — who is he talking to?)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Him?! <i>(point at Gideon, puzzled)</i></li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Sir, if the LORD is helping us, why have all these awful things happened?</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> We’ve heard how the LORD rescued our ancestors from Egypt. But those things happened long ago.</li><li><b>Angel of the LORD:</b> Gideon, you will be strong, because I am giving you the power to rescue Israel from the Midianites.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Gideon feels tiny. What excuse do you think he’s about to give? (20s)</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> But how can I rescue Israel? My clan is the weakest one in Manasseh. <i>(shrink down small)</i></li><li><b>Gideon:</b> And everyone else in my family is more important than I am.</li><li><b>Angel of the LORD:</b> Gideon, you can rescue Israel because I am going to help you!</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Then please — wait here. Let me bring you a gift. <i>(hurry off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Gideon brought meat and bread and set them on a rock. The angel touched them with the tip of his stick…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> WHOOSH! <i>(hands burst upward like flames)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Fire flared from the rock and burned it all up. And the angel was gone.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> I’ve seen the LORD’s angel face to face! I’m done for! <i>(fall to your knees)</i></li><li><b>Angel of the LORD:</b> Peace! Don’t be afraid. You are not going to die.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Then I’ll build an altar right here — and call it ‘The LORD Is Peace’. <i>(stack invisible stones)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Gideon? Out of the pit? What’s got into you? <i>(peek out, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Not what — who. The LORD is with me.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The LORD is helping you — strong warrior!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gideon gave God two reasons he couldn’t do it. What did God say back?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Gideon said his clan was the weakest and he was the least important. God didn’t argue — he said, ‘I am going to help you!’ God’s help, not Gideon’s size, was the point.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to Gideon’s gift on the rock?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Fire flared up from the rock and burned it — a sign that it really was God speaking (Judges 6:21).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Good enough or God with me?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t tell Gideon, “You’re good enough.” He said, “I am going to help you.” Why is “God is with me” better than “I’m good enough”?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: ‘I’m good enough’ falls apart when we fail or feel weak; ‘God is with me’ stays true on bad days; God is strong even when we aren’t; it means we don’t have to pretend. Follow-up: ‘When might a kid your age need to hear “God is with me”?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who else heard God say “I am with you”?’ (Joshua, last week — Joshua 1:9.) If silent: compare a little kid on a bike with a parent holding the seat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s strength doesn’t depend on how strong I feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when I feel small, you are with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when something feels too big (a test, a new kid to talk to, a hard job at home), whisper “God, you are with me” — then have a go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge. Practise the whisper together once.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Who, me?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”<br>Gideon was hiding in a pit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Judges 6:12. Say it to a partner and point at them on ‘strong warrior’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Small hands, big God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you feel small, you can tell God exactly how you feel — just like Gideon did. Try holding your hands cupped and small, then open them wide to God. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Cup your hands together, small, in your lap. That’s how Gideon felt. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gideon felt small. God is strong.<br>Prompts: God, you are strong when I am weak. / God, you saw Gideon hiding — and you see me too.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are strong, and you are with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there something you’ve hidden from or said ‘I can’t’ to?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding when you wanted me to help. / Sorry for forgetting you when I felt scared.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we hid instead of trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Slowly open your hands. Thank God for his promise to Gideon.<br>Prompts: Thank you for saying, “I am going to help you.” / Thank you that you find people who are hiding.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are going to help us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your open hands out. Name one thing that feels too big this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me with (the big thing). / Please help (a friend) who feels small.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, I feel small — but you are with me. Please help me have a go.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model your own ‘too big’ thing first so students feel safe naming theirs silently.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Gideon still isn’t sure — so he asks God for a sign with a woolly sheepskin.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Fleece":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Judges 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Fleece</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when you feel small — Gideon told God exactly how small he felt (Judges 6:15) — and God answered, “I am going to help you!” You can pray honestly about feeling small, then lean on God’s strength.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Gideon obeyed God, even though he was still afraid.</li><li>We are learning how Gideon brought his honest doubts to God with a sheepskin test.</li><li>I can explain how God showed patience to Gideon when he felt unsure.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling both fleece tests correctly and explaining that God was patient with Gideon’s honest doubts — and that we now have God’s promises in the Bible and in Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a piece of wool or a woolly jumper and a bowl of water</li><li>Paper and pencils for the prayer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Fleece</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday God found Gideon hiding in a pit.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the angel call him — and what did God promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A ‘strong warrior’. ‘I am going to help you!’ Then: ‘Gideon said yes… but he was still nervous. Today he does something brave at night — and then asks God for a very strange sign.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Judges 6:36–40</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:36–37</b> “I know that you promised to help me rescue Israel, but I need proof.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:37</b> “If you really will help me rescue Israel, then tomorrow morning let there be dew on the skin, but let the stone floor be dry.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:39</b> “Don’t be angry with me,” Gideon said.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 6:40</b> That night, God made the stone floor wet with dew, but he kept the sheep skin dry.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. A ‘fleece’ is a woolly sheepskin. Dew is the water that settles on things overnight.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Wet wool, dry wool</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Gideon had said yes to God. But God’s first job for him was a scary one.</p><p>Gideon’s own father had an altar to Baal — a made-up god that lots of people worshipped. Next to it stood a tall wooden pole for another false god. God told Gideon: pull them down, and build a proper altar to the LORD instead.</p><p>Gideon obeyed. But he was afraid of his family and the people in his town — so he did it at night! He took ten servants, and in the dark they pulled the whole thing down.</p><p>In the morning the townspeople were furious. ‘Who did this? Gideon! Bring him out!’ But Gideon’s father, Joash, stood up for him: ‘If Baal really is a god, let him stand up for himself!’ Of course, nothing happened. Baal was just stone and wood.</p><p>Then the Midianites came again, with their allies, and camped in a huge valley. And the Bible says the Spirit of the LORD took control of Gideon. The nervous farmer blew a trumpet, and thousands of men came running to follow him.</p><p>But inside, Gideon was still unsure. So he prayed: ‘I know that you promised to help me rescue Israel, but I need proof.’</p><p>He had an idea. A threshing floor was a flat area of stone. ‘Tonight I’ll put a woolly sheepskin on the floor. If you really will help me, let there be dew on the skin tomorrow morning, but let the stone floor be dry.’</p><p>The next morning Gideon got up early and ran to look. The stone floor was dry. And the sheepskin? Soaking! He squeezed it, and filled a whole bowl with water.</p><p>You’d think that would be enough. But Gideon thought, ‘Maybe wool just soaks up water anyway…’ So he prayed again, a bit embarrassed: ‘Don’t be angry with me. Let me try one more time. This time, let the skin stay dry and the floor be wet.’</p><p>That was much harder! And that night, God made the stone floor wet with dew, but he kept the sheepskin dry.</p><p>Now here’s the interesting thing. God had already made Gideon a promise. Gideon didn’t really need a test — he needed to trust. But God didn’t shout at him or give up on him. God was patient. He knew Gideon felt small and scared, and he met him right where he was.</p><p>We don’t need to put out sheepskins. We have something better: God’s promises written in the Bible, and Jesus, who shows us exactly what God is like. But we can still bring God our honest questions. He is patient with people who feel small.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A brave job — at night. · 2. The trumpet sounds! · 3. “I need proof.” · 4. Wet wool, dry floor. · 5. “Don’t be angry with me…”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. The altar story is Judges 6:25–35, summarised. Be careful not to teach ‘test God with a fleece’ as a method: the point is God’s patience with honest weakness. Jesus is similarly patient with doubting Thomas (Week 10).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Look closer: two tests</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gideon asked God for two signs. What did he ask — and how did God answer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What Gideon asked</th><th>How God answered</th></tr><tr><td>Test 1: wet sheepskin, dry floor</td><td>A soaking skin — a whole bowl of water!</td></tr><tr><td>“Don’t be angry with me”</td><td>God wasn’t angry — he answered again</td></tr><tr><td>Test 2: dry sheepskin, wet floor (much harder!)</td><td>Dry skin, wet floor — exactly as asked</td></tr><tr><td>“I need proof”</td><td>God had already promised: “I am going to help you!”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is patient with people who feel small — and his promise was true all along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Optional demo: dip wool in water and squeeze it into a bowl.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The first test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Gideon’s first test, what did he ask God to do?<br>A) Make the sheepskin wet and the floor dry ✓ &nbsp; B) Send fire from the sky &nbsp; C) Make it rain on the Midianites &nbsp; D) Turn the wool to gold</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A (Judges 6:37). Follow-up: ‘What was the second test?’ (Dry skin, wet floor — the opposite.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why was the second test harder?’ (Wool soaks up water naturally; keeping it dry while everything else is wet is impossible without God.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Patient God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God had already promised to help Gideon. Why do you think God answered his tests — twice — instead of getting cross?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is patient and kind; he knew Gideon was scared; he wanted to build Gideon’s trust; he understands we are weak; he wanted Gideon ready for what came next. Follow-up: ‘What does this show us about God?’ (Patient, gentle with weak people.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Jesus treat people who doubted?’ If silent: ask how a good coach treats a nervous beginner.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Honest doubts</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is it wrong to have doubts about God? What’s the difference between bringing your doubts TO God and using them to run AWAY from God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: everyone has questions sometimes; Gideon brought his doubts to God in prayer — he didn’t walk away; doubts become a problem when we use them as an excuse not to obey. Gideon still obeyed. Follow-up: ‘Where can we look when we have doubts?’ (God’s promises in the Bible, what Jesus did.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you talk to about a big question?’ If silent: share a simple honest question you have had.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is patient with people who feel weak and unsure.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, here is my honest question — please help me trust you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight at bedtime, tell God one honest worry or doubt in your own words — then say his promise to Gideon: “I am going to help you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This leads straight into today’s written prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A bowl full of dew</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I know that you promised… but I need proof.”<br>God was patient with Gideon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Judges 6:12. Ask: ‘Was Gideon a strong warrior when the angel said it?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: An honest prayer (write)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gideon was honest with God about feeling unsure. You can write God an honest prayer — he already knows what you feel, and he is patient. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write: ‘Dear God, you are strong.’ Then follow the prompts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing that shows God is strong or patient.<br>Prompts: You made the sheepskin wet and the floor dry. / You were patient with Gideon.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are strong and patient with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for not trusting what God has already said?<br>Prompts: Sorry for not believing your promises. / Sorry for using my fear as an excuse.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for not trusting your promises.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank-you for one of God’s promises.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you said, “I am going to help you.” / Thank you for Jesus, who shows me what you’re like.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that your promises are true!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your honest question or worry — then ask for help to trust.<br>Prompts: I’m not sure about… please help me trust you. / Please help me be brave like Gideon.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “God, when we feel unsure, please help us trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your prayer and keep it. That’s between you and God. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t collect these — they’re private. Invite volunteers to share a Wow or Thanks line only.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God shrinks Gideon’s army from thousands to 300 — and sends him spying in the night.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Too Many Soldiers!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Judges 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Too Many Soldiers!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when you feel small — Gideon told God exactly how small he felt (Judges 6:15) — and God answered, “I am going to help you!” You can pray honestly about feeling small, then lean on God’s strength.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God made Gideon’s army smaller on purpose.</li><li>We are learning how God encouraged Gideon with an enemy soldier’s dream.</li><li>I can explain why God wanted only 300 soldiers.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining Judges 7:2 — God made the army small so no one could boast — and noticing that God encouraged Gideon’s fear without being asked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bread roll for the barley loaf</li><li>Optional: a cup of water for the drinking test</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Too Many Soldiers!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Pick your team</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>You’re picking a team for a huge match. Would you pick 300 players — or 32,000?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which would you pick, and why? What if the other team had more than 100,000?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a quick show of hands — almost everyone will want more players! Land it: ‘Gideon had 32,000 soldiers. The enemy had far more. And God said: your army is too BIG.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Judges 7:2, 7, 13–15</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:2</b> “Gideon, your army is too big. I can’t let you win with this many soldiers. The Israelites would think that they had won the battle all by themselves.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:7</b> “I’m going to rescue Israel by helping you and your army of 300 defeat the Midianites.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:13</b> “I had a dream about a flat loaf of barley bread that came tumbling into our camp. It hit the headquarters tent, and the tent flipped over.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:15</b> “Let’s go! The LORD is going to let us defeat the Midianite army.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected and slightly shortened.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: From 32,000 to 300</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Gideon now had thirty-two thousand soldiers. That sounds like a lot — until you look at the Midianites. Their army filled the valley like a swarm of locusts, and their camels were as many as the grains of sand on the beach.</p><p>Then God said something very surprising: ‘Gideon, your army is too big. I can’t let you win with this many soldiers. The Israelites would think that they had won the battle all by themselves.’</p><p>Too big? Gideon must have gulped.</p><p>God said, ‘Tell anyone who is afraid that they can go home.’ And twenty-two thousand soldiers went home! Ten thousand were left.</p><p>‘There are still too many,’ said God. ‘Take them down to the water.’ At the spring, most of the men knelt down and put their faces to the water to drink. But a few scooped up the water in their hands and lapped it. God said, ‘Those three hundred are your army. Send the others home. I’m going to rescue Israel by helping you and your army of three hundred.’</p><p>Three hundred. Against an army too big to count.</p><p>Why would God make it harder on purpose? Because he wanted everyone to know who really won. When something impossible happens with just three hundred people, nobody can say, ‘Look how strong we are!’ They have to say, ‘Look how strong God is!’</p><p>That night God spoke again: ‘Get up! Attack the Midianite camp. I am going to let you defeat them.’ And then God said something kind. He knew Gideon was scared — even though Gideon hadn’t said so. ‘If you’re afraid, take your servant Purah and creep down to the camp. Listen to what they’re saying.’</p><p>So Gideon and Purah crept through the dark, right to the edge of the enemy camp. They heard two soldiers whispering.</p><p>‘I had a dream,’ said one. ‘A flat loaf of barley bread came tumbling into our camp. It hit the headquarters tent, and the tent flipped over and fell to the ground!’</p><p>Barley bread was cheap bread — poor farmers’ bread. Like Gideon!</p><p>The other soldier said, ‘Your dream must have been about Gideon. It means God will let him and his army defeat us.’</p><p>The enemy was scared of Gideon! Right there in the dark, Gideon bowed down and worshipped God. Then he ran back to his three hundred men: ‘Let’s go! The LORD is going to let us defeat the Midianite army!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. 32,000 soldiers. · 2. Anyone afraid can go home. · 3. Down to the water. · 4. 300 versus an army like locusts. · 5. A dream in the dark.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The drinking test (7:4–8) is not about who was the ‘best’ soldier — it was simply God’s way of choosing a small number. The enemy numbers (7:12; 8:10) were huge; keep the focus on God’s power, not battle details.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Too big</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish what God said: “Your army is too big… The Israelites would think that they had…”<br><i>Answer: “…won the battle all by themselves.” (Judges 7:2)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘How many soldiers did Gideon end up with?’ (300.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did God choose them?’ (The ones who scooped water in their hands and lapped it.) If silent: point to the first scripture line and read it slowly together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Too Many Soldiers!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Too Many Soldiers!</b> (Judges 7:1–15). The LORD’s words and the soldiers’ dream are from Judges 7 (CEV), lightly shortened. Purah’s lines and the crowd’s farewells are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>The LORD</b> — Heard, never seen<br><b>Gideon</b> — Commander — still nervous<br><b>Purah</b> — Gideon’s servant — loyal, a bit jumpy<br><b>Midianite 1</b> — Enemy soldier who had a strange dream<br><b>Midianite 2</b> — His friend — works out what it means<br><b>Everyone</b> — Israel’s soldiers, then the sleeping enemy camp</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as soldiers): march, then most wave goodbye and sit down · Everyone: slurp the water · Everyone (as the enemy camp): snoring and grumbling camels<br>Props: Optional: a cup of water · Optional: a bread roll for the barley loaf<br>Purah is named in Judges 7:10. Let the ‘going home’ moment be funny: most of the class sits down, leaving just a few ‘soldiers’ standing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Gideon’s camp beside a spring. Thirty-two thousand soldiers.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> We’re ready! We’re ready! <i>(march on the spot, chests puffed out)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Gideon, your army is too big.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Too BIG? LORD, have you seen how many Midianites there are? <i>(point across the valley)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> The Israelites would think that they had won the battle all by themselves.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Listen, everyone! If you’re afraid, you may go home. <i>(cup hands, shout)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Bye! See you! I’m off! <i>(most of the class waves and sits down)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Twenty-two thousand went home. Ten thousand were left.</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> There are still too many. Take them down to the water.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Sluuurp! <i>(most kneel; only a few scoop water in their hands)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Send the others home. I’m going to rescue Israel by helping you and your army of 300.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Three… hundred? <i>(count on your fingers, gulp)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! 300 against an army like a swarm of locusts. If you were one of the 300, how would you feel? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> That night, the LORD spoke again.</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Get up! Attack the Midianite camp. I am going to let you defeat them.</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> But if you’re afraid, take Purah and go down to the camp. Listen to what they’re saying.</li><li><b>Purah:</b> Er… master… you’re not afraid, are you? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Just… come with me. Quietly. <i>(tiptoe)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Zzzz… honk… grrrumph… zzzz… <i>(a snoring camp and grumbling camels)</i></li><li><b>Midianite 1:</b> I had a dream about a flat loaf of barley bread that came tumbling into our camp. <i>(sit up, shaky)</i></li><li><b>Midianite 1:</b> It hit the headquarters tent, and the tent flipped over and fell to the ground.</li><li><b>Midianite 2:</b> Your dream must have been about Gideon, the Israelite commander.</li><li><b>Midianite 2:</b> It means God will let him and his army defeat us! <i>(shiver)</i></li><li><b>Purah:</b> Did you hear that? THEY’RE scared of YOU! <i>(grab Gideon’s arm)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Right there in the dark, Gideon bowed down and worshipped God.</li><li><b>Gideon:</b> Let’s go! The LORD is going to let us defeat the Midianite army! <i>(run back, fist raised)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The LORD is with us!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God knew Gideon was afraid before Gideon said a word. What did God do about it?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He gave him a way to be encouraged — sent him to hear the dream (7:10–11). God was gentle with Gideon’s fear instead of scolding him.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the barley loaf in the dream do?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: It tumbled into the camp, hit the big tent and knocked it flat (7:13). It meant God would give Gideon the victory.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A kind God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made Gideon’s army tiny — and then sent him to overhear the enemy’s dream. What does this show us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is strong — he doesn’t need big numbers; God wants the credit to go to him, not people; God is kind and understands fear; God gives courage when we need it. Follow-up: ‘Why does it matter who gets the credit?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How has God encouraged you when you were scared?’ If silent: ask, ‘Was God being mean to Gideon or kind?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Harder on purpose?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was it fair of God to send most of the army home? Why would God make something harder on purpose?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: so everyone would see God won the battle, not people (7:2); so Gideon would have to trust God completely; because big numbers make us proud. Some may say it seems unfair — that’s fine; ask what Gideon learned. Follow-up: ‘Where else in the Bible does God use something small?’ (David and Goliath, Term 1.) Follow-up 2: ‘When might God let things be hard for us?’ If silent: re-read 7:2.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When God works through small things, everyone can see the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I don’t need to be big — you are strong.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When something goes well this week (a goal in sport, a good test, a kind act), say “Thank you, God” before you say “Look at me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Link to 7:2: giving God the credit.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The barley loaf</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A flat loaf of barley bread came tumbling into our camp…”<br>The enemy was afraid of Gideon.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Judges 6:12. Flex your arms on ‘strong warrior’ — then point up, because the strength comes from God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Pray for your partner</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God sent Purah with Gideon so he wasn’t alone. Today, pray in pairs — for each other’s ‘too big’ thing. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Tell them one thing that feels too big for you this week. Keep it short.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together, tell God how strong he is.<br>Prompts: God, you won with just 300. / God, you don’t need big numbers to do big things.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you don’t need big armies. You are strong!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for wanting the credit for ourselves?<br>Prompts: Sorry for showing off. / Sorry for trusting in myself instead of you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for wanting to look big instead of trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the ways he encourages us when we’re afraid.<br>Prompts: Thank you for knowing when I’m scared. / Thank you for friends who go with me, like Purah.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for being kind when we’re afraid.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s ‘too big’ thing.<br>Prompts: Please help (name) with… / Please remind (name) that you are with them.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be strong for each of us this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen. Give your partner a thumbs up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow students to pray silently for their partner if they’re shy. One sentence is plenty.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: 300 trumpets, 300 jars, 300 torches — and a battle won without a fight.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Trumpets and Jars":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Judges 6–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Trumpets and Jars</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when you feel small — Gideon told God exactly how small he felt (Judges 6:15) — and God answered, “I am going to help you!” You can pray honestly about feeling small, then lean on God’s strength.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God gave Gideon’s 300 men victory with trumpets, jars and torches.</li><li>We are learning why the glory belonged to God, not to Gideon.</li><li>I can explain how God uses people who feel weak so that everyone sees his power.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell Gideon’s story in order, explain who really won the battle, and recite Judges 6:12.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: a torch and a paper cup to show the ‘jar and torch’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Trumpets and Jars</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Gideon’s story</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Gideon’s story in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Midianites raid Israel → God finds Gideon hiding → Fire from the rock → Wet sheepskin, dry floor → 32,000 shrink to 300 → The barley-loaf dream → Trumpets, jars and torches</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Ask after each: ‘Was Gideon feeling big or small here?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Judges 7:18, 20–21 · 2 Corinthians 12:9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:18</b> “Wait for me to blow a signal on my trumpet. As soon as you hear it, blow your trumpets and shout, ‘Fight for the LORD! Fight for Gideon!’”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:20</b> “Fight with your swords for the LORD and for Gideon!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Judges 7:21</b> The enemy soldiers started yelling and tried to run away.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>2 Corinthians 12:9</b> “My power is strongest when you are weak.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. 2 Corinthians 12:9 is Jesus speaking to Paul — the same truth as Gideon’s story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Crash! Blaze! Shout!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Gideon split his three hundred men into three groups. He gave each man a trumpet, and an empty clay jar with a burning torch hidden inside. No big army. No clever weapons. Just trumpets, jars and light.</p><p>In the middle of the night, just after the enemy guards had changed, the three hundred crept to the edge of the camp and spread out all the way around it.</p><p>Then — Gideon blew his trumpet. Three hundred trumpets blared! They smashed their jars — CRASH! — and three hundred torches blazed in the dark. They shouted, ‘Fight for the LORD! Fight for Gideon!’</p><p>And here’s the amazing part: the three hundred just stood still. They didn’t charge in. The enemy woke up to noise and light on every side, and they panicked. The LORD threw the whole camp into confusion, and the huge Midianite army ran away into the night.</p><p>Who won the battle? Not Gideon. Not the three hundred. God did. That’s what ‘glory’ means — the praise and honour belong to God.</p><p>Hundreds of years later, Jesus said to a man called Paul, ‘My power is strongest when you are weak.’ Jesus won the biggest victory of all in a way that looked weak — dying on a cross — and then rising again. Like light shining out of broken jars, God’s power shines through people who know they are small.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. 300 men. Strange equipment. · 2. CRASH! · 3. The enemy ran! · 4. The glory is God’s.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Sensitive: Judges 7:22 says the enemy turned on each other in the confusion. Keep it implied — ‘the camp was thrown into confusion and they ran’. If students ask about the battle, focus on God giving the victory without the 300 fighting.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Strange weapons</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Gideon’s 300 men carry into battle?<br>A) Swords and shields &nbsp; B) Trumpets, and clay jars with torches inside ✓ &nbsp; C) Bows and arrows &nbsp; D) Loaves of barley bread</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Judges 7:16). Follow-up: ‘What did they shout?’ (‘Fight for the LORD! Fight for Gideon!’) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the 300 do when the enemy ran?’ (They stood still around the camp.) If silent: show the story art again.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who won?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who really won the battle — Gideon, the 300, or God? How do you know from the story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God won: he chose 300 on purpose (7:2); he gave the dream; the 300 only stood still; the LORD threw the enemy into confusion. Good answers mention evidence from the story. Follow-up: ‘Why did God want everyone to know he won?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is Jesus’ victory on the cross like this?’ (Looked weak, but was God’s power.) If silent: list the clues together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God found Gideon hiding and called him a strong warrior — because God would be with him.</li><li>God was patient with Gideon’s doubts and kind to his fears.</li><li>God won with just 300, so everyone could see the power was his — and his power is strongest when we are weak.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God uses people who feel weak, so everyone can see that the power is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Brave and strong?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “God only uses people who are brave and strong.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Most will disagree — draw out evidence: Gideon was hiding, doubting and afraid; God used him anyway. Brave isn’t ‘not scared’; it’s trusting God while scared. Follow-up: ‘Who else did God use who felt small?’ (Moses, David, Mary.) Follow-up 2: ‘Does God want us to stay scared forever?’ (He gives courage as we trust him.) If silent: re-read Gideon’s ‘weakest’ line from Monday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Small me, big God. When you feel too small for something this week, pray “God, you are with me” — and have a go.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.” — Judges 6:12</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this. Encourage students to write down what they had a go at.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The angel appeared and spoke to Gideon, “The LORD is helping you, and you are a strong warrior.”” — Judges 6:12 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Judges 6:12. Bonus: can anyone say last week’s verse, Joshua 1:9?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Light from broken jars</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who won the battle?<br>The glory is God’s.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Small me, big God (out loud)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Prayer when you feel small can be really short: “God, you are with me.” Today, anyone can pray one sentence out loud. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, a few people can pray one sentence out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something about God’s strength from Gideon’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you won with trumpets and jars! / God, your power is strongest when we are weak!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are strong — the glory is yours!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for hiding, or for taking the credit?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding when I should trust you. / Sorry for wanting people to look at me instead of you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for hiding and for taking your glory.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for how he treated Gideon.<br>Prompts: Thank you for finding Gideon in the pit. / Thank you for being patient with our doubts.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you use people who feel small!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray about one ‘too small’ moment coming up.<br>Prompts: Please be with me at (sport, school, home). / Please help me say, “God, you are with me” — and have a go.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are with me. Please help me have a go.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Judges 6:12 together as our final prayer. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Thank every student who prays aloud — praying out loud can feel like a ‘too small’ moment too!</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith That Prays and Waits — Hannah’s tearful prayer, and a boy who hears God call his name in the night.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4: Faith That Prays and Waits":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 1:1–2:10; 3:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listening</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and waiting — Hannah poured out her heart to God for years — tears and all — then trusted him with the answer (1 Samuel 1:10–18). You can pray the same honest prayer again and again, and leave the timing to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Tears at Shiloh</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning why Hannah was so sad, and how she brought her sadness to God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Tears at Shiloh → The long wait → 1 Samuel 1:2, 10–17 → A prayer without a sound → How did Hannah pray? → Not sad any more? → Tears at Shiloh → Misunderstood → Head · Heart · Hands → A silent prayer → Memory verse peek → A silent prayer like Hannah’s</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Asked of God</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God answered Hannah’s prayer, and what the name Samuel means.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Asked of God → Recap → 1 Samuel 1:20, 27–28; 2:1–2 → The boy God gave → Look closer: before and after → What’s in a name? → Song about God → A hard promise → Head · Heart · Hands → “Do you remember me?” → Build the verse → Praying Hannah’s song</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>I’m Listening</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God called young Samuel by name in the night.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>I’m Listening → Who’s calling? → 1 Samuel 3:1, 4–5, 9–10 → A voice in the night → What Samuel said → I’m Listening → Learning to listen → How does God speak today? → Head · Heart · Hands → “Samuel! Samuel!” → Verse with actions → Open hands, listening hearts</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Praying and Waiting</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Hannah and Samuel kept trusting God while they waited.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Praying and Waiting → Who said it? → 1 Samuel 3:19 · Luke 18:1 · Luke 22:42 → Keep praying, keep trusting → Every year → Why wait? → This week’s Big Idea → Pray hard enough? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Still listening → My same-every-day prayer (write)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Tears at Shiloh":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 1:1–2:10; 3:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tears at Shiloh</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and waiting — Hannah poured out her heart to God for years — tears and all — then trusted him with the answer (1 Samuel 1:10–18). You can pray the same honest prayer again and again, and leave the timing to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Hannah was so sad, and how she brought her sadness to God.</li><li>We are learning how Eli misunderstood Hannah — but God did not.</li><li>I can explain that God hears honest prayers, even tearful or silent ones.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Hannah told God exactly how she felt (1 Samuel 1:15), that Eli got her wrong but God understood her, and that after praying she trusted God and was no longer sad.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a scarf or shawl for Hannah</li><li>Optional: a chair by the ‘doorway’ for Eli</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Tears at Shiloh</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week’s hero is a woman who prayed — and waited.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The long wait</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the longest you’ve ever had to wait for something you really, really wanted?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What makes waiting so hard? What do you do while you wait?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Gideon, hiding in a pit — and God called him a strong warrior. Your challenge: when you felt too small for something, pray “God, you are with me” and have a go. When did you try it? What happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students about their ‘too small’ moment (a test, sport, talking to someone new) and celebrate any attempt, even if it didn’t go perfectly. Then the hook: birthdays, a pet, a visit from family, getting better after being sick. Land it: ‘Today we meet a woman who waited years and years — and kept talking to God the whole time.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 1:2, 10–17</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:2</b> Elkanah had two wives, Hannah and Peninnah. Although Peninnah had children, Hannah did not have any.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:10–11</b> Hannah was brokenhearted and was crying as she prayed, “LORD All-Powerful, I am your servant, but I am so miserable! Please let me have a son.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:16</b> “I’ve been praying all this time, telling the LORD about my problems.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:17</b> “You may go home now and stop worrying. I’m sure the God of Israel will answer your prayer.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected and slightly shortened. ‘LORD’ in capitals stands for God’s special personal name. ‘LORD All-Powerful’ is the first time this name for God appears in the Bible.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A prayer without a sound</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Once a year, a man called Elkanah took his whole family up to Shiloh. Shiloh was where the sacred tent stood — the tabernacle, God’s special place of worship. There they would worship God and share a big feast together.</p><p>Elkanah had two wives. In those days some men did. One wife, Peninnah, had sons and daughters. The other wife, Hannah, had no children at all. Hannah longed for a child, and she had waited for years and years. Every year she hoped. Every year her arms were still empty.</p><p>And Peninnah made it worse. Every year at Shiloh she teased Hannah and made her feel miserable. So Hannah cried, and she couldn’t eat.</p><p>Elkanah loved Hannah very much. ‘Hannah, why are you crying?’ he asked. ‘Why won’t you eat? Don’t I mean more to you than ten sons?’ He meant to be kind, but he couldn’t take away the ache.</p><p>After the meal, Hannah slipped away to the tabernacle. Old Eli the priest was sitting on his chair by the doorpost. Hannah was brokenhearted, and she cried as she prayed. ‘LORD All-Powerful, I am your servant, but I am so miserable! Please let me have a son. If you do, I will give him back to you for as long as he lives.’</p><p>She prayed for a long time. She prayed silently — her lips were moving, but no sound came out.</p><p>Eli watched her and frowned. He thought she had been drinking too much wine! ‘How long are you going to stay drunk?’ he said. Imagine how that must have felt. Hannah was already hurting so much — and now even the priest thought badly of her.</p><p>‘Sir, I’m not drunk,’ said Hannah. ‘I feel miserable and terribly upset. I’ve been praying all this time, telling the LORD about my problems.’</p><p>Eli had got her completely wrong. But God hadn’t. God had heard every silent word. God doesn’t need loud words or fancy words. He hears tears. He hears whispers. He even hears the prayers we can’t put into words.</p><p>Now Eli spoke kindly. ‘You may go home now and stop worrying. I’m sure the God of Israel will answer your prayer.’</p><p>And something changed. Nothing had happened yet — no baby, no answer she could see. But Hannah had poured out her heart to God, and she left it with him. She went back, ate something, and she was no longer sad.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Every year, up to Shiloh. · 2. Hannah’s heart hurt. · 3. Tears at the doorway. · 4. Eli got it wrong. · 5. No longer sad.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. SENSITIVE: some children in the class may have family experiences of waiting a long time for a baby, of babies who died, of IVF, adoption or fostering, or of families who never had children. Keep the tone gentle. Don’t suggest that having no children means God was unhappy with Hannah, and don’t present the story as a promise that praying hard always brings a baby. The focus is on God hearing Hannah’s honest prayer. ‘In those days some men had more than one wife’ — the Bible tells this honestly, and it caused real hurt in this family; no need to explore further. Eli’s ‘drunk’ comment can be kept light: he thought she’d had too much wine.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How did Hannah pray?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Hannah pray at the tabernacle?<br>A) She shouted so everyone could hear &nbsp; B) She prayed silently, crying, with her lips moving ✓ &nbsp; C) She sang a happy song &nbsp; D) She asked Eli to pray for her</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Samuel 1:10, 13). Follow-up: ‘What did Eli think was going on?’ (He thought she was drunk.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Hannah say she was really doing?’ (Telling the LORD about her problems.) If silent: re-read the second and third scripture lines together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not sad any more?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah didn’t have a baby yet — but she went home and was no longer sad. Why do you think she felt different after praying?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she had told God everything and felt lighter; she trusted God had heard her; Eli’s kind words encouraged her; she had left the problem with God instead of carrying it alone. Follow-up: ‘Had anything changed on the outside?’ (No — only inside.) Follow-up 2: ‘Have you ever felt better just from telling someone what was wrong?’ If silent: compare carrying a heavy schoolbag and then putting it down.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Tears at Shiloh</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Tears at Shiloh</b> (1 Samuel 1:1–18). Hannah’s, Elkanah’s and Eli’s words are from 1 Samuel 1 (CEV), lightly shortened. Peninnah’s exact words and the travellers’ lines are imagined — the Bible only says she made Hannah feel miserable.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Hannah</b> — Sad, honest, and full of faith<br><b>Elkanah</b> — Hannah’s husband — loves her, wants to help<br><b>Peninnah</b> — Elkanah’s other wife — unkind to Hannah<br><b>Eli</b> — Old priest — sleepy, then kind<br><b>Everyone</b> — Travellers on the road to Shiloh</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as travellers): trudge uphill, puffing · Everyone: a quiet ‘mmm’ hum while Hannah prays · Everyone: a soft, warm ‘Amen’ at the end<br>Props: Optional: a scarf or shawl for Hannah · Optional: a chair for Eli by the ‘doorway’<br>Cast Peninnah carefully — pick a confident student and keep the teasing short; don’t let the class join in. Eli says sorry here as an imagined softening; the Bible shows his tone change in 1:17. Hannah’s thank-you is close to 1:18.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The road to Shiloh. Elkanah’s family is going up to worship God, like every year.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Up the hill… puff, puff… nearly there! <i>(trudge on the spot, wipe your brow)</i></li><li><b>Elkanah:</b> Here we are — the LORD’s tent! Time to worship, and time to feast.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Elkanah gave everyone a share of the meal. He gave Hannah a special share, because he loved her.</li><li><b>Peninnah:</b> A big share for Hannah? Well. Who’s she sharing it with? Oh, that’s right — nobody. <i>(toss your head)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Year after year, Peninnah made Hannah feel miserable. And year after year, Hannah cried.</li><li><b>Hannah:</b> I’m not hungry. <i>(push the plate away, look down)</i></li><li><b>Elkanah:</b> Hannah, why are you crying? Why won’t you eat? <i>(kneel beside her)</i></li><li><b>Elkanah:</b> Don’t I mean more to you than ten sons?</li><li><b>Hannah:</b> You’re kind, Elkanah. But there’s something I need to tell the LORD. <i>(stand slowly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At the doorway of the tabernacle sat Eli the priest, on his chair by the doorpost.</li><li><b>Eli:</b> Hmm. Another visitor. <i>(settle back, half-asleep)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Hannah was brokenhearted. She cried as she prayed — but silently. Only her lips moved.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mmmmm… <i>(a soft, quiet hum)</i></li><li><b>Hannah:</b> LORD All-Powerful, I am your servant, but I am so miserable! Please let me have a son. <i>(lips moving, hands together)</i></li><li><b>Hannah:</b> If you do, I will give him back to you, for as long as he lives.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Eli is watching Hannah’s lips move with no sound. What do you think he’s thinking? (20s)</li><li><b>Eli:</b> How long are you going to stay drunk? Put away the wine! <i>(point, frowning)</i></li><li><b>Hannah:</b> Sir, I’m not drunk! I feel miserable and terribly upset.</li><li><b>Hannah:</b> I’ve been praying all this time, telling the LORD about my problems.</li><li><b>Eli:</b> Oh… I see. I’m sorry. <i>(lean forward, gentle now)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> You may go home now and stop worrying. I’m sure the God of Israel will answer your prayer.</li><li><b>Hannah:</b> Sir, thank you for being so kind to me. <i>(small bow)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Hannah went back to the family. She ate something. And she was no longer sad.</li><li><b>Elkanah:</b> Hannah? You’re smiling!</li><li><b>Hannah:</b> Nothing has changed yet. But I’ve told the LORD everything. I’m leaving it with him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Amen. <i>(soft and warm)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Eli got Hannah completely wrong. Did God get her wrong?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: No — God heard every silent word and understood her exactly. People can misunderstand us; God never does (1 Samuel 1:13–17).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Hannah promise God if he gave her a son?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She would give him back to God — to serve him for as long as he lived (1 Samuel 1:11).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Misunderstood</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Eli looked at Hannah and got her completely wrong. Why is it good news that God never misunderstands our prayers — even silent or messy ones?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: people only see the outside, but God sees our hearts; we don’t need the right words — God understands tears and silence; we can be honest with God without being judged; God knows what we mean even when we can’t explain it. Follow-up: ‘Has anyone ever got you wrong when you were upset?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who in the Bible understood people perfectly?’ (Jesus — he saw people’s hearts.) If silent: ask, ‘Do you need fancy words to pray?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God hears every prayer — even tears, whispers and silence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I can tell you exactly how I feel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when something makes you sad or worried (at home, in a friendship, at school), tell God about it in your own words — even if it’s just a whisper in bed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Remind students that they don’t need special words. Hannah just told God the truth.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A silent prayer</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’ve been praying all this time, telling the LORD about my problems.”<br>Eli didn’t understand. God did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce 1 Samuel 2:2 — these are words Hannah prayed later, after God answered her. A ‘rock’ is something solid that won’t move or let you down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A silent prayer like Hannah’s</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah prayed without making a sound — and God heard every word. You can pray silently, in your head, anywhere: on the bus, in bed, in the playground. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your eyes or look at the floor. Today we’ll pray silently, like Hannah. Only God will hear. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently tell God what is amazing about him.<br>Prompts: God, you heard Hannah’s silent prayer. / God, you understand me even when no one else does.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you hear even our silent prayers!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there someone you’ve teased or misunderstood, like Peninnah or Eli?<br>Prompts: Sorry for making someone feel small or left out. / Sorry for judging someone before I knew what was going on.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve hurt or misjudged others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that you can bring him anything.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I can tell you about my problems. / Thank you for kind people, like Elkanah, who care when I’m sad.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you always listen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently tell God about one thing that makes you sad or worried.<br>Prompts: Please help me with (the thing that’s worrying me). / Please be close to (someone) who is sad today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “God, here is our heart. We leave it with you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone, quietly: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Allow a longer silence in the Please phase. Some students may be carrying something heavy — don’t ask anyone to share. Be available afterwards if a child wants to talk.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: God answers Hannah’s prayer — and she keeps an amazing promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Asked of God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 1:1–2:10; 3:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Asked of God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and waiting — Hannah poured out her heart to God for years — tears and all — then trusted him with the answer (1 Samuel 1:10–18). You can pray the same honest prayer again and again, and leave the timing to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God answered Hannah’s prayer, and what the name Samuel means.</li><li>We are learning how Hannah kept her promise and gave Samuel back to God.</li><li>I can compare Hannah’s tearful prayer with her happy song of praise.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Samuel’s name reminds Hannah that God heard her, that she kept her promise, and that her song praises God (not the baby) as holy and a rock.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small child’s tunic or a baby blanket</li><li>Hannah’s song lines on screen for the psalm prayer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Asked of God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Hannah prayed at Shiloh — silently, with tears.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who misunderstood her? And how did she feel when she went home?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Eli thought she was drunk; she explained she was telling the LORD her problems. She went home no longer sad — even before anything changed. Then: ‘Today we find out what God did — and what Hannah did next.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 1:20, 27–28; 2:1–2</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:20</b> She named him Samuel because she had asked the LORD for him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:27</b> “I asked the LORD to give me a child, and he answered my prayer.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 1:28</b> “Now I am giving him to the LORD, and he will be the LORD’s servant for as long as he lives.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 2:2</b> “No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. 1 Samuel 2:2 is our memory verse. ‘Holy’ means completely pure and set apart — there is no one like God.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The boy God gave</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hannah and Elkanah went home from Shiloh. And the Bible says that the LORD remembered Hannah’s prayer.</p><p>In time, Hannah had a baby — a little boy! Can you imagine her face? After all those years of waiting and crying, she held her son in her arms. She counted his tiny fingers and toes. She probably laughed and cried at the same time.</p><p>She named him Samuel. In Hebrew, the language Hannah spoke, Samuel sounds a lot like ‘asked of God’ or ‘God heard’. She said, ‘I asked the LORD for him.’ So every time she called him in for dinner — ‘Samuel!’ — she remembered: God heard me.</p><p>But Hannah hadn’t forgotten her promise. She had told God, ‘If you give me a son, I will give him back to you.’</p><p>So she waited until Samuel was a little older and no longer a baby — probably about three. Then she packed up gifts to worship God — a young bull, a big sack of flour and a jar of wine — and she took Samuel up the long road to Shiloh. Little Samuel probably walked some of the way, holding his mum’s hand, and rode on the donkey when his legs got tired.</p><p>There was old Eli, still sitting by the door of the tabernacle.</p><p>‘Sir,’ said Hannah, ‘do you remember me? I’m the woman who stood here praying. I asked the LORD to give me a child, and he answered my prayer. Now I am giving him to the LORD, and he will be the LORD’s servant for as long as he lives.’</p><p>That must have been very hard. Hannah loved Samuel so much. But she trusted God with him, just as she had trusted God with her tears. Samuel would grow up at the tabernacle, helping Eli and learning to serve God. And Hannah would visit every single year. She went home with empty arms — but a full heart, because she knew Samuel was in God’s hands.</p><p>Then Hannah prayed again. But this prayer was not tears — it was a song! ‘No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.’</p><p>Look at what she sang about. Not ‘I got what I wanted!’ Not even mostly about the baby. She sang about God — how holy he is, how strong and steady he is, like a rock. She sang that God lifts up people who are poor and sad, and that he would one day give strength to his chosen king.</p><p>About a thousand years later, another young woman sang a song very much like Hannah’s. Her name was Mary, and she was going to be the mother of Jesus — God’s chosen King. Hannah’s song was pointing forward to him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. God remembered Hannah. · 2. His name: Samuel. · 3. A promise to keep. · 4. “Do you remember me?” · 5. Hannah sang!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. ‘The LORD remembered her’ (1:19) doesn’t mean God had forgotten — it means he acted on her prayer. The meaning of ‘Samuel’ is a play on words (‘asked of God’ / ‘heard by God’). SENSITIVE: Hannah giving Samuel to serve at the tabernacle may raise questions for children who are adopted, fostered or live apart from a parent. Reassure: Samuel was loved and cared for, Hannah visited every year, and this was a special job she had promised. Avoid suggesting God always gives babies to those who pray — the focus is God hearing and Hannah trusting. Mary’s song (Luke 1:46–55) was Term 1 Week 10.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Look closer: before and after</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah prayed twice at Shiloh. Compare her first prayer with her song.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Before: Hannah’s tearful prayer</th><th>After: Hannah’s song</th></tr><tr><td>“I am so miserable!”</td><td>“You make me strong and happy, LORD.”</td></tr><tr><td>Crying, silent, lips moving</td><td>Singing praise out loud</td></tr><tr><td>All about her problem</td><td>All about who God is</td></tr><tr><td>“Please let me have a son.”</td><td>“The LORD is our mighty rock.”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both prayers went to the same God — the one who listens to sad prayers and happy ones.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Point out that both prayers were real and honest — God welcomes both.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What’s in a name?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did Hannah call her son Samuel?<br>A) It was his grandfather’s name &nbsp; B) Because she had asked the LORD for him ✓ &nbsp; C) Because Eli chose it &nbsp; D) It means ‘strong warrior’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Samuel 1:20). Follow-up: ‘What would Hannah remember every time she said his name?’ (That God heard her prayer.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Hannah do when Samuel was a little older?’ (Took him to Shiloh to serve God, as she had promised.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Song about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When God answered, Hannah’s song was mostly about God — not about the baby. Why do you think she sang about God instead of about what she got?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she knew the baby was a gift from God; she wanted to thank and praise the Giver; she had learned God is holy and strong while she waited; the answer made her love God more, not just the gift. Follow-up: ‘When you get something good, who do you usually talk about?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What words did she use for God?’ (Holy, mighty rock.) If silent: ask, ‘If a friend gave you a great present, would you only talk about the present?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A hard promise</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah had waited years for Samuel — then gave him to serve God at the tabernacle. Why would she keep such a hard promise?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: she trusted God completely; she had made a promise to God and wanted to be faithful; she knew Samuel belonged to God first; she still saw him every year; she believed God’s plan for Samuel was good. Some may say they couldn’t do it — that’s an honest answer. Follow-up: ‘What does this show about Hannah’s faith?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who else in Term 2 trusted God with a son?’ (Abraham, Week 1.) If silent: re-read 1:27–28 slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God hears our prayers — and he is worth praising even more than the things he gives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, you are my rock. Thank you for answering prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one prayer God has already answered for you (or your family). Tonight at home, thank him for it and tell one person about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If a student can’t think of an answered prayer, suggest everyday ones: a safe trip, a good friend, getting better after being sick.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “Do you remember me?”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I asked the LORD to give me a child, and he answered my prayer.”<br>Hannah kept her promise.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build 1 Samuel 2:2. Ask: ‘Why would Hannah call God a rock?’ (Solid, steady, never moves — someone you can stand on.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying Hannah’s song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah turned her thank-you into a song. You can pray words from the Bible line by line, making them your own — today we’ll pray with Hannah’s song. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray with Hannah’s words. I’ll read a line from her song; then you say it back to God, or add your own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah sang: “No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is.”<br>Prompts: God, there is no one like you. / God, you are holy — perfect and pure.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “No other god is like the LORD. The LORD is our mighty rock!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah sang that God sees what people really think. Tell him honestly.<br>Prompts: Sorry for forgetting to thank you when you answer. / Sorry for caring more about the gifts than about you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting to thank you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah sang: “You make me strong and happy, LORD.”<br>Prompts: Thank you for hearing Hannah — and for hearing me. / Thank you for (a prayer you have answered).<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “You make us strong and happy, LORD!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hannah sang that God lifts up people who are poor and sad.<br>Prompts: Please lift up (someone) who is sad or lonely. / Please help me trust you, my rock, while I wait.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, our rock, please lift up those who are sad.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish with Hannah’s words: “The LORD is our mighty rock.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hannah’s song (1 Samuel 2:1–10) is summarised in the leads; 2:1 and 2:2 are quoted. Read each lead slowly with a pause for students to echo.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: young Samuel hears a voice in the night — three times!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: I’m Listening":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 1:1–2:10; 3:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">I’m Listening</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and waiting — Hannah poured out her heart to God for years — tears and all — then trusted him with the answer (1 Samuel 1:10–18). You can pray the same honest prayer again and again, and leave the timing to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God called young Samuel by name in the night.</li><li>We are learning how Eli helped Samuel learn to listen to God.</li><li>I can explain what it means to say to God, “I’m listening.”</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the three calls and the fourth answer, explaining that Samuel didn’t yet recognise God’s voice, and suggesting real ways we can ‘listen’ to God today (the Bible, Jesus, prayer, wise people).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch or battery candle for the lamp</li><li>Optional: a blanket or mat for Samuel</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: I’m Listening</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Dim the lights if you can.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Who’s calling?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine someone calls your name in the middle of the night. You wake up. Who would you think it was?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you do? How would you know who it really was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect: Mum, Dad, a brother or sister, a dream. Ask: ‘How do you recognise someone’s voice?’ (You’ve heard it lots.) Land it: ‘Today young Samuel hears his name in the dark — and gets it wrong three times.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 3:1, 4–5, 9–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 3:1</b> In those days, the LORD hardly ever spoke directly to people, and he did not appear to them in dreams very often.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 3:4</b> The LORD called out Samuel’s name. “Here I am!” Samuel answered.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 3:9</b> “If someone speaks to you again, answer, ‘I’m listening, LORD. What do you want me to do?’”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 3:10</b> “Samuel! Samuel!” “I’m listening,” Samuel answered. “What do you want me to do?”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected and slightly shortened. Many Bibles translate 3:10 as ‘Speak, for your servant is listening.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A voice in the night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Little Samuel grew up at the tabernacle in Shiloh. He helped old Eli the priest — opening doors, fetching things, and learning how to serve God. Every year his mum, Hannah, came to visit and brought him a new little robe she had made.</p><p>But those were sad days. Most people had stopped listening to God. Even Eli’s own sons, who were priests too, didn’t respect God at all. And the Bible says that in those days, the LORD hardly ever spoke directly to people. It was as if the whole country had gone quiet, and nobody was expecting God to say anything at all.</p><p>By now Eli was very old and almost blind. One night Eli was asleep in his room. Samuel was asleep in the tabernacle, near the sacred chest — the special box that reminded everyone that God was with his people. It was nearly morning, but the lamp in God’s house was still burning.</p><p>Then a voice called: ‘Samuel!’</p><p>Samuel jumped up. ‘Here I am!’ He ran to Eli. ‘Here I am. What do you want?’</p><p>Eli blinked. ‘I didn’t call you. Go back to bed.’</p><p>So Samuel lay down, puzzled. He was sure he had heard his name. Then: ‘Samuel!’ Again he ran to Eli. ‘Here I am. What do you want?’</p><p>‘I didn’t call you, son. Go back to bed.’</p><p>Why didn’t Samuel know who it was? The Bible says the LORD had not spoken to him before. He didn’t yet recognise God’s voice. He knew about God, but he had never heard God speak to him personally.</p><p>A third time: ‘Samuel!’ A third time Samuel ran to Eli.</p><p>And this time old Eli understood. It was the LORD calling the boy! He said, ‘Go back and lie down. If someone speaks to you again, answer, “I’m listening, LORD. What do you want me to do?”’</p><p>So Samuel went back and lay down in the glow of the lamp, wide awake now. His heart must have been thumping. What would God say? Would he hear it again?</p><p>Then the LORD came and stood there, and called just as before: ‘Samuel! Samuel!’</p><p>And Samuel answered, ‘I’m listening. What do you want me to do?’</p><p>That night God began to speak to Samuel. And from then on, Samuel kept listening — for the rest of his life.</p><p>And God still speaks today — most clearly through the Bible and through his Son, Jesus. The question is: are we listening?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A boy at God’s house. · 2. The lamp was still burning. · 3. “Samuel!” · 4. “I didn’t call you.” · 5. “I’m listening.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Samuel is perhaps 10–12 here (the Bible doesn’t say). The ‘sacred chest’ is the ark of the covenant. Hannah’s yearly robe is from 2:19. Eli’s sons (2:12–17) — keep it to ‘they didn’t respect God’. The message God gave Samuel (3:11–18) was a hard one about Eli’s family; Samuel told Eli everything honestly. Mention only if asked. God is ‘heard’, never shown — the art uses light only.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What Samuel said</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish what Samuel said the fourth time God called: “I’m listening…”<br><i>Answer: “…What do you want me to do?” (1 Samuel 3:10)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘How many times did Samuel run to Eli?’ (Three.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who worked out that it was God calling?’ (Eli — on the third time.) If silent: point to the last scripture line and read it slowly together, then try again with eyes closed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: I’m Listening</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>I’m Listening</b> (1 Samuel 3:1–10). The LORD’s, Samuel’s and Eli’s words are from 1 Samuel 3:1–10 (CEV), lightly shortened. Samuel’s thoughts and the night-time sounds are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Samuel</b> — Young helper at the tabernacle — eager and sleepy<br><b>Eli</b> — Very old priest — nearly blind, very tired<br><b>The LORD</b> — Heard, never seen<br><b>Everyone</b> — The sounds of the night and the flickering lamp</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the night): crickets, an owl, a gentle snore · Everyone: the flicker of the lamp — ‘fff… fff…’ · Everyone: whisper “I’m listening” together at the end<br>Props: Optional: a torch or battery candle for the lamp · Optional: a mat or blanket for Samuel<br>The LORD’s lines are a voice only — have the reader stand out of sight or at the back. Keep the night sounds soft so the voice stands out. ‘Stood there’ (3:10) — God made his presence known; art shows light only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Shiloh. Night. In those days the LORD hardly ever spoke to people.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Chirp… chirp… hoo-hoo… fff… fff… <i>(crickets, an owl, a flickering lamp)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Old Eli was asleep in his room. Young Samuel was asleep near the sacred chest.</li><li><b>Eli:</b> Zzzz… hrrrmph… zzzz… <i>(snore gently)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Samuel!</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Here I am! <i>(jump up, rub your eyes)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Eli! Here I am. What do you want? <i>(run to Eli)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> Hmm? I didn’t call you. Go back to bed. <i>(wave him away sleepily)</i></li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Funny. I was sure I heard my name. <i>(shuffle back, lie down)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Chirp… chirp… fff… fff…</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Samuel!</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Here I am! Eli, what do you want? <i>(hurry back)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> I didn’t call you, son. Go back to bed. <i>(yawn)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Samuel did not yet know the LORD’s voice. The LORD had not spoken to him before.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Why do you think Samuel keeps running to Eli? What would you do? (20s)</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Samuel!</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> Eli! Here I am — you DID call me this time! <i>(run, out of breath)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> No, son. I didn’t… <i>(slowly sit up)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> Wait. Samuel — it’s the LORD who is calling you. <i>(eyes wide)</i></li><li><b>Eli:</b> Go back and lie down. If someone speaks to you again, answer, ‘I’m listening, LORD. What do you want me to do?’</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> I’m listening, LORD… I’m listening… I’m listening… <i>(whisper as you walk, practising)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Samuel lay down by the glowing lamp, wide awake now.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> fff… fff… <i>(very quietly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then the LORD came and stood there, and called just as before.</li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Samuel! Samuel!</li><li><b>Samuel:</b> I’m listening. What do you want me to do? <i>(sit up straight, open hands)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> And from that night on, the LORD spoke to Samuel — and Samuel listened all his life.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> I’m listening, LORD. <i>(whisper together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God called Samuel four times. What does that show us about God?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God is patient — he didn’t give up when Samuel got it wrong. He knew Samuel’s name and wanted to speak to him personally (1 Samuel 3:10).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who helped Samuel know how to answer God?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Old Eli — he worked out it was the LORD and taught Samuel what to say (1 Samuel 3:8–9).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Learning to listen</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Samuel didn’t recognise God’s voice at first, and old Eli helped him. Why do you think God used Eli to help Samuel listen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Eli had served God for a long time and knew what to do; God often uses older, wiser people to help young people; Samuel needed a guide; even Eli, who had made mistakes, could still point someone to God. Follow-up: ‘Who are the “Elis” in your life who help you know God?’ (Parents, grandparents, teachers, church leaders.) Follow-up 2: ‘How could you help someone younger?’ If silent: ask, ‘What would have happened if Eli had just said “Go back to bed” again?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How does God speak today?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God called Samuel out loud in the night. Does God still speak today? How could you “listen” to God this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God speaks mainly through the Bible; Hebrews 1:1–2 says God has spoken to us through his Son, Jesus; through prayer (listening, not just talking); through wise Christians; through a nudge to do what’s right. Some students may say they’ve never heard God — that’s normal and honest. Follow-up: ‘What could you read to hear what God says?’ (A Bible story, a Gospel.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does listening look like in prayer?’ (Being quiet.) If silent: read Hebrews 1:1–2 aloud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God knows my name, and he speaks — most clearly through the Bible and through Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">I’m listening, LORD. What do you want me to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Once this week (at bedtime or before school), read a short Bible story, then sit quietly for 30 seconds and pray, “I’m listening, LORD.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Suggest a Gospel story or a Psalm for those who have a Bible at home; others could reread this week’s verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “Samuel! Samuel!”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I’m listening. What do you want me to do?”<br>The lamp of God was still burning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for 1 Samuel 2:2. Stack your fists hard on ‘mighty rock’. Ask: ‘Samuel grew up singing his mum’s song — what do you think he learned about God from it?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands, listening hearts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Prayer isn’t only talking — it’s listening too. Samuel said, “I’m listening.” Today, after each part, we’ll stop and be quiet before God with open hands. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit up straight like Samuel. Rest your hands open on your lap, ready to receive. Whisper, “I’m listening, LORD.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God knew Samuel’s name. He knows yours.<br>Prompts: God, you know my name. / God, you kept calling Samuel — you are patient.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you know our names, and you speak!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a time you didn’t listen — to God, or to someone who loves you?<br>Prompts: Sorry for being too busy to listen to you. / Sorry for not listening when my parents or teacher spoke.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we didn’t listen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who help you know him, like Eli helped Samuel.<br>Prompts: Thank you for (a person) who helps me know you. / Thank you for the Bible, where you speak to me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for speaking to us in the Bible and through Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands open. Ask God to help you listen — then wait quietly.<br>Prompts: Please help me hear you when I read the Bible. / What do you want me to do today, God?<br>Starter: <b>I’m listening, LORD…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “I’m listening, LORD. What do you want me to do?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, as if holding something precious. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>In the Please phase, allow about 20 seconds of real silence. Reassure students that if they don’t ‘hear’ anything, that’s fine — listening to God is something we keep practising.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what Hannah and Samuel teach us about praying — and waiting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Praying and Waiting":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Samuel 1:1–2:10; 3:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and Waiting</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying and waiting — Hannah poured out her heart to God for years — tears and all — then trusted him with the answer (1 Samuel 1:10–18). You can pray the same honest prayer again and again, and leave the timing to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Hannah and Samuel kept trusting God while they waited.</li><li>We are learning that God hears every prayer and answers in his own time and way.</li><li>I can explain how Jesus shows us how to pray and trust our Father.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say who said key lines from the week, explain that God hears prayer and answers in his time (yes, wait, or something different), and recite 1 Samuel 2:2.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Small cards or sticky notes for writing a prayer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Praying and Waiting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Hannah, why are you crying? Don’t I mean more to you than ten sons?” — <b>Elkanah</b><br>“LORD All-Powerful… please let me have a son.” — <b>Hannah</b><br>“You may go home now and stop worrying.” — <b>Eli</b><br>“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is.” — <b>Hannah</b><br>“Samuel! Samuel!” — <b>The LORD</b><br>“I’m listening. What do you want me to do?” — <b>Samuel</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘When did they say it — before or after the waiting?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Samuel 3:19 · Luke 18:1 · Luke 22:42</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Samuel 3:19</b> As Samuel grew up, the LORD helped him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:1</b> Jesus told his disciples a story about how they should keep on praying and never give up.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 22:42</b> “Father, if you will, please don’t make me suffer by having me drink from this cup. But do what you want, and not what I want.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 22:42 is Jesus praying in the garden the night before he died. ‘This cup’ means the suffering ahead of him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Keep praying, keep trusting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Every year, Hannah made Samuel a little robe and brought it to Shiloh. Can you picture her measuring it against him — ‘You’ve grown again!’ She kept trusting God with her son, year after year.</p><p>Think about how long Hannah had prayed before Samuel was born. Year after year she went to Shiloh. Year after year she cried and prayed. Did God hear her the first time? Yes. He heard every single prayer. But the answer came in God’s time.</p><p>Sometimes God says yes straight away. Sometimes he says wait. And sometimes he gives something different from what we asked — because he sees what we can’t see. Hannah didn’t know that her son would become one of the greatest leaders in Israel, or that one day he would anoint a shepherd boy called David as king.</p><p>And Samuel kept listening. As he grew up, the LORD helped him, and everyone in Israel knew God spoke through him.</p><p>Jesus told his friends to keep on praying and never give up. And Jesus showed us how. The night before he died, he prayed in a garden, honestly: ‘Father, please don’t make me go through this. But do what you want, and not what I want.’ He trusted his Father completely. And God’s plan — Jesus dying and rising again — was the greatest rescue of all.</p><p>So keep praying. Tell God the truth, tears and all. Then trust him with the answer. Like Hannah, you can say: ‘God, you are my rock. I’ll keep praying, and I’ll wait for you.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A little robe, every year. · 2. Years of waiting. · 3. Yes. Wait. Something better. · 4. Samuel kept listening.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. SENSITIVE: be careful not to imply that if we pray hard enough we always get what we ask for. Some children (or their families) may have prayed for a baby, or for someone to get better, and the answer was not the one they hoped for. Emphasise that God always hears and always cares, even when his answer is ‘wait’ or ‘no’, and that Jesus himself prayed ‘do what you want’. The robe is from 1 Samuel 2:19; Samuel anointing David is 1 Samuel 16 (Term 1).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Every year</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Hannah bring Samuel every year at Shiloh?<br>A) A new pair of sandals &nbsp; B) A little robe she had made ✓ &nbsp; C) A lamb &nbsp; D) A letter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Samuel 2:19). Follow-up: ‘What does that show about Hannah?’ (She still loved him and cared for him.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the LORD do as Samuel grew up?’ (He helped him — 1 Samuel 3:19.) If silent: show the story art again and ask what Hannah is holding.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why wait?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If God heard Hannah’s very first prayer, why do you think he made her wait so long for the answer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God’s timing is best; God had a plan for Samuel at the right moment in Israel’s history; waiting taught Hannah to trust and pray; God sees what we can’t see. It’s fine to say ‘We don’t always know why’ — that’s honest. Follow-up: ‘What did Hannah do while she waited?’ (Kept praying, kept going to Shiloh.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Jesus pray when things were hard?’ (‘Do what you want, not what I want.’) If silent: ask what a gardener does while waiting for seeds to grow.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Hannah poured out her heart to God with tears — and God heard every silent word.</li><li>God answered in his time, and Hannah praised him: “The LORD is our mighty rock.”</li><li>God called Samuel by name, and Samuel learned to say, “I’m listening.”</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God hears real prayers, even tearful ones, and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Pray hard enough?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If you pray hard enough, God will always give you exactly what you ask for.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out: God always hears, but he is not a vending machine; he answers yes, wait, or something different because he is wise and loving; Jesus prayed ‘do what you want, not what I want’. Handle gently — some may have prayed for someone to get better and they didn’t. Follow-up: ‘Why is it good that God doesn’t just give us everything?’ Follow-up 2: ‘So why keep praying?’ (Because God listens, and prayer grows our trust.) If silent: re-read Luke 22:42.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray and wait. Choose one thing you really care about, and pray the same prayer every day this week — then trust God with the answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this. Students write their prayer on a card (today’s prayer slide) to keep by their bed or in their bag.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No other god is like the LORD or as holy as he is. The LORD is our mighty rock.” — 1 Samuel 2:2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out 1 Samuel 2:2. Bonus: can anyone say last week’s verse, Judges 6:12?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Still listening</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep praying. Never give up.<br>God hears — and answers in his time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: My same-every-day prayer (write)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying and waiting means bringing the same request to God again and again, like Hannah — and trusting him with the answer. Writing it down helps you remember to keep praying. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your card, write: ‘Dear God, you are my rock.’ Then follow the prompts. This is the prayer you’ll pray every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing about God from Hannah’s song or Samuel’s night.<br>Prompts: You are holy — no one is like you. / You heard Hannah’s silent prayer and called Samuel by name.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are our mighty rock!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for giving up on prayer, or for not listening?<br>Prompts: Sorry for giving up when I didn’t get an answer straight away. / Sorry for doing all the talking and none of the listening.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for giving up too quickly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank-you for a time God has heard you.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you heard Hannah — and you hear me. / Thank you for Jesus, who showed me how to trust you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you always hear us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your one prayer for this week — the one you’ll pray every day.<br>Prompts: Please… (the thing I really care about). / Help me trust you with the answer and the timing.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we will keep praying — and we trust you with the answer.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your card somewhere you’ll see it each day. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t collect the cards — they’re private. Gently steer students towards prayers for others or for things they genuinely care about. If a child writes something worrying, follow up quietly afterwards.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith on the Mountain — Elijah, a drought, and fire from heaven.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5: Faith on the Mountain":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Kings 17:1–16; 18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Only God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persistent prayer — Elijah prayed and sent his servant to look seven times (1 Kings 18:43). Persistent prayer means keep asking and keep watching — God hears the first time, and he loves it when we don’t give up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ravens and a Jar of Flour</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Elijah stood up to King Ahab and announced a drought from God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Ravens and a Jar of Flour → Who controls the weather? → 1 Kings 17:1, 6, 13–14, 16 → No rain — but enough → Bird delivery → Who sends rain? → Ravens and a Jar of Flour → Giving first → Head · Heart · Hands → Caw! → Memory verse peek → Open hands (like the widow)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Challenge Is Set</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Elijah challenged Ahab and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Challenge Is Set → Recap → 1 Kings 18:17–18, 21, 24 → Showdown on Carmel → Baal vs the LORD → The rules → Both ways → One against 450 → Head · Heart · Hands → Choose! → Build the verse → A written ‘I choose you’ prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Fire Falls</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Baal failed to answer and how the LORD answered Elijah with fire.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Fire Falls → Make it harder → 1 Kings 18:26, 36–38 → The fire of the LORD → Three times → Fire Falls on Carmel → Why the fire? → Fire today? → Head · Heart · Hands → No answer → Verse with actions → One-sentence prayers (out loud)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Small Cloud, Big Rain</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Elijah kept praying for rain and sent his servant to look seven times.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Small Cloud, Big Rain → Elijah’s adventure in order → 1 Kings 18:42–44 · James 5:17–18 → Look again → How big? → Why keep praying? → This Week’s Big Idea → Small clouds → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Rain! → Seven-times prayer (pairs)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Ravens and a Jar of Flour":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Kings 17:1–16; 18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ravens and a Jar of Flour</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persistent prayer — Elijah prayed and sent his servant to look seven times (1 Kings 18:43). Persistent prayer means keep asking and keep watching — God hears the first time, and he loves it when we don’t give up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Elijah stood up to King Ahab and announced a drought from God.</li><li>We are learning how God fed Elijah through ravens and a poor widow.</li><li>I can explain how the widow showed faith before she saw the miracle.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining why the drought showed that Baal was powerless, and how the widow trusted God’s promise by giving first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small jar and a bottle for the widow; black paper ‘wings’ for the ravens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Ravens and a Jar of Flour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A new hero for the Faith Museum: Elijah the prophet. Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Who controls the weather?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If you could control the weather for one day, what would you do with it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Could anyone REALLY control the rain? Who does?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard how Hannah poured out her heart to God, and how young Samuel said, “Speak, LORD, your servant is listening.” Your challenge: pray the same prayer every day. Did you keep going? What happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate anyone who kept praying the same prayer — answered yet or not. Link it: ‘This week’s hero kept praying too.’ Then the hook: snow day, sunshine for sports day. Land it: ‘In Elijah’s time, people believed a god called Baal controlled the rain. The LORD was about to show them who really does.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Kings 17:1, 6, 13–14, 16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 17:1</b> Elijah… went to King Ahab and said, “I am a servant of the living LORD, the God of Israel. And I swear in his name that it won’t rain until I say so.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 17:6</b> Ravens brought him bread and meat twice a day, and he drank water from the creek.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 17:13</b> Elijah said, “Everything will be fine… But first, make a small piece of bread and bring it to me.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 17:16</b> The LORD kept the promise that his prophet Elijah had made, and she did not run out of flour or oil.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. ‘The living LORD’ is a deliberate contrast with Baal, a statue that could not see, hear or act.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: No rain — but enough</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Israel had a king called Ahab, and the Bible says he did more wrong than any king before him. He married a princess named Jezebel, and together they led God’s people to worship Baal. Baal was a made-up god. People believed he sent the rain and the storms and made the crops grow. They built him statues and altars, and they forgot the LORD — the real God who had rescued them from Egypt.</p><p>Then one day a wild-looking man strode into the palace. His name was Elijah, and he was a prophet — someone who carries God’s messages. Even his name was a message: Elijah means ‘My God is the LORD’.</p><p>Elijah looked the king in the eye. ‘I am a servant of the living LORD, the God of Israel. And I swear in his name that it won’t rain until I say so.’</p><p>People thought Baal was in charge of the sky. Now the LORD would show who really was.</p><p>The rain stopped. Ahab was furious, so God sent Elijah to hide beside a little creek out in the wild. ‘Drink from the creek,’ God said, ‘and I have told the ravens to bring you food.’ Ravens! Big black birds that usually steal food, not share it. But every morning and every evening, they flapped down with bread and meat.</p><p>With no rain, even the creek dried up. Drip… drip… nothing. So God said, ‘Go to Zarephath. I have told a widow there to feed you.’ A widow is a woman whose husband has died. And Zarephath was in Jezebel’s home country — Baal country!</p><p>At the town gate, Elijah saw a thin woman picking up sticks. ‘Please bring me some water,’ he called, ‘and a piece of bread.’</p><p>‘I don’t have any bread,’ she said. ‘Just a handful of flour and a little oil. I’m cooking one last meal for my son and me. After that, we will starve.’</p><p>Elijah said, ‘Everything will be fine. But first, make a small piece of bread for me. The LORD has promised that your flour won’t run out and your oil won’t dry up until he sends rain.’</p><p>Her last handful — given away first? But she trusted God’s word, and she did it. And day after day, the jar never ran out.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. King Ahab worshipped Baal. · 2. “It won’t rain until I say so!” · 3. Ravens with bread and meat! · 4. The creek dried up. · 5. One handful of flour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Ahab’s wickedness: 1 Kings 16:30–33. Baal was worshipped as a storm-and-rain god, so a drought was a direct challenge to him. Zarephath was near Sidon, Jezebel’s home (16:31). The raising of the widow’s son (17:17–24) is outside this week’s passage; mention it only if asked.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Bird delivery</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did God feed Elijah while he hid by the creek?<br>A) He caught fish &nbsp; B) Ravens brought him bread and meat ✓ &nbsp; C) King Ahab sent him food &nbsp; D) Manna fell from the sky</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Kings 17:6) — twice a day, morning and evening. Follow-up: ‘Why is it surprising that ravens shared food?’ (Ravens usually steal food!) Follow-up 2: ‘When the creek dried up, where did God send Elijah next?’ (To a widow in Zarephath.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who sends rain?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The people thought Baal sent the rain. Why do you think God chose to stop the rain — and not some other miracle?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to show Baal had no power over the very thing he was supposed to control; to show the LORD is the real God of the sky; to call the people back to him. Follow-up: ‘How long would it take people to notice Baal wasn’t helping?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do people today trust instead of God?’ (Money, luck, being popular.) If silent: ‘If your “weather god” couldn’t make it rain for three years, what would you start to think?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Ravens and a Jar of Flour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Ravens and a Jar of Flour</b> (1 Kings 17:1–16). Elijah’s, God’s and the widow’s main words follow 1 Kings 17 (CEV, slightly shortened). Ahab’s lines and the son’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Elijah</b> — Fearless prophet of the LORD — wild hair, rough cloak<br><b>Ahab</b> — Proud king who worships Baal<br><b>The LORD</b> — Heard, never seen<br><b>Widow</b> — Poor mother in Zarephath — worried, then trusting<br><b>Son</b> — The widow’s hungry little boy<br><b>Everyone</b> — Dry wind, ravens, and the dripping creek</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: a hot, dry wind · Everyone (as ravens): ‘Caw! Caw!’ and flapping arms · Everyone: the creek going drip… drip… silent<br>Props: Optional: a small jar and a bottle · Optional: a few sticks for the widow<br>Keep the ‘starve’ line calm and simple; the point is God’s care. If students have experienced hunger or hardship, emphasise that God sees and cares for people who have little.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>King Ahab’s palace. Statues of Baal stand everywhere.</li><li><b>Ahab:</b> More offerings for Baal! He’ll keep the rain coming. <i>(lounging on the throne)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Suddenly, a wild-looking man strides in.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> I am a servant of the living LORD, the God of Israel. <i>(point to the sky)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> And I swear in his name that it won’t rain until I say so.</li><li><b>Ahab:</b> What?! Guards — grab him! <i>(leap up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But Elijah was gone. And the rain stopped.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooosh… hot, dusty wind… <i>(blow softly)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Elijah, hide by the creek. Drink its water. I have told the ravens to bring you food.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Caw! Caw! Caw! <i>(flap your arms — ravens!)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Bread and meat — morning and evening! Thank you, LORD! <i>(catch the food)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But with no rain, even the creek dried up.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Drip… drip… drip… <i>(quieter and quieter, then silence)</i></li><li><b>The LORD:</b> Go to Zarephath. I have told a widow there to give you food.</li><li class='dir'>The town gate of Zarephath. A thin woman is picking up sticks.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Please bring me a drink of water — and a piece of bread.</li><li><b>Widow:</b> I don’t have any bread. Only a handful of flour and a little olive oil. <i>(hold up the jar)</i></li><li><b>Widow:</b> I’m cooking one last meal for my son and me. After that, there’s nothing.</li><li><b>Son:</b> Mum… I’m so hungry. <i>(tug her sleeve)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Everything will be fine. Go and cook. But first, make a small piece of bread for me.</li><li><b>Widow:</b> For YOU? First?! <i>(shocked)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> The LORD has promised: your flour won’t run out and your oil won’t dry up until he sends rain.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! She has one handful of flour left. Would you give it away first? What would help you trust God’s promise? (20s)</li><li><b>Widow:</b> All right. I’ll trust the God of Israel. <i>(deep breath)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> She baked the bread for Elijah. Then she looked in the jar…</li><li><b>Son:</b> Mum! There’s still flour in it! <i>(peek into the jar)</i></li><li><b>Widow:</b> And oil in the bottle! Again! <i>(amazed)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Day after day, the jar never ran out. The LORD kept his promise.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The LORD keeps his promises!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God fed Elijah with ravens and a poor widow — not a rich king. What does that show about God?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He can use anything and anyone; he looks after his people; he doesn’t need Baal or Ahab; he cares for the poor and for outsiders (the widow wasn’t even an Israelite). Jesus mentions her in Luke 4:25–26.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the widow have to do BEFORE she saw the miracle?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Make bread for Elijah first, with her last flour — she trusted God’s promise before she could see it. That’s faith (Hebrews 11:1).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Giving first</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was it fair of Elijah to ask a starving widow to feed HIM first? Why do you think God asked her to give before she saw the miracle?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them disagree! Draw out: it sounded unfair, but it came with God’s promise; giving first was how she showed faith; the miracle fed all three of them for a long time. Follow-up: ‘What would have happened if she had said no?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When is it hard for you to give first — time, snacks, turns?’ If silent: re-read 1 Kings 17:13–14 and ask what she was really trusting — Elijah or God?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The LORD is the living God who sees and provides — even in a drought.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to trust you like the widow did, even when I only have a little.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Give first this week: at lunch or at home, share your ‘last handful’ — a snack, a turn, your time — before anyone asks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask for one example of a ‘last handful’ they could give today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Caw!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Caw! Caw!<br>“I have told the ravens to bring you food.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce 1 Kings 18:39. ‘We’ll find out on Wednesday what the people SAW that made them shout this!’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands (like the widow)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week’s prayer skill is persistent prayer — keep asking, keep trusting. Today, pray with open hands, like the widow holding out her last handful to God. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands open on your lap, palms up. Open hands say, ‘God, everything I have is yours.’ Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The LORD is the living God — not a statue.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you control the rain and the sky. / You even told ravens what to do!<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are the living God, and you rule the sky!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there something you trust more than God — or keep for yourself?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hanging on to things instead of sharing. / Sorry for trusting other things more than you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for trusting other things more than you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the ‘jar of flour’ he gives you every day.<br>Prompts: Thank you for food in my lunchbox today. / Thank you that you looked after Elijah and the widow.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you always provide!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for people who are running out — of food, money or hope.<br>Prompts: Please provide for families who don’t have enough. / Please help me give first, like the widow.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, provide for everyone who is running out today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, as if God has filled them. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be sensitive: some students’ families may be struggling. Keep the ‘running out’ prayer general and hopeful.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: three dry years later — Elijah sets up the biggest challenge in Israel’s history.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Challenge Is Set":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Kings 17:1–16; 18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Challenge Is Set</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persistent prayer — Elijah prayed and sent his servant to look seven times (1 Kings 18:43). Persistent prayer means keep asking and keep watching — God hears the first time, and he loves it when we don’t give up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Elijah challenged Ahab and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel.</li><li>We are learning what it means to ‘have things both ways’ instead of following the LORD.</li><li>I can explain the rules of the contest and why it would prove who the real God is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining the contest (two bulls, no fire, the god who answers with fire is God) and why the people couldn’t keep following both Baal and the LORD.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a fork in the road drawn on the board</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Challenge Is Set</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday the rain stopped — and God fed Elijah in surprising ways.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who brought Elijah food — twice? And what never ran out?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The ravens, then the widow of Zarephath. Her jar of flour and bottle of oil never ran out. ‘Now it’s been three years with no rain. The whole land is brown and dusty — and Elijah is about to go straight back to the king.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Kings 18:17–18, 21, 24</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:17</b> When Ahab saw Elijah, he shouted, “There you are, the biggest troublemaker in Israel!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:18</b> Elijah answered: “You’re the troublemaker—not me! You and your family have disobeyed the LORD’s commands by worshiping Baal.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:21</b> “How much longer will you try to have things both ways? If the LORD is God, worship him! But if Baal is God, worship him!” The people did not say a word.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:24</b> “The one who answers by starting the fire is God.” Everyone agreed.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged; the CEV’s ‘worshiping’ keeps its US spelling on screen. ‘Have things both ways’ — other versions say ‘waver between two opinions’ or ‘limp between two sides’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Showdown on Carmel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Three years went by. Three whole years with no rain. The rivers became dry, cracked mud. The crops died. The animals grew thin. Everyone was hungry and thirsty — and still the people prayed to Baal, and still Baal did nothing.</p><p>Then the LORD spoke to Elijah: ‘Go and meet King Ahab. I will soon send rain.’</p><p>Now, Queen Jezebel had been hunting down the LORD’s prophets. But a man named Obadiah, who worked in the palace and loved the LORD, had secretly hidden one hundred of them in caves and brought them bread and water. When Obadiah met Elijah on the road, he was terrified: ‘If you disappear again, the king will blame me!’ But Elijah said, ‘I promise — today I will meet Ahab.’</p><p>So the king and the prophet came face to face. Ahab sneered, ‘There you are, the biggest troublemaker in Israel!’</p><p>Elijah didn’t back down. ‘You’re the troublemaker — not me! You have disobeyed the LORD and worshipped Baal.’ Then he gave the king an order: ‘Bring all the people to Mount Carmel. And bring the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal.’</p><p>So they came. Thousands and thousands of people climbed the mountain. The prophets of Baal marched up in their fancy robes. And on the other side stood just one man: Elijah.</p><p>Elijah turned to the crowd. ‘How much longer will you try to have things both ways? If the LORD is God, worship him! But if Baal is God, worship him!’</p><p>Silence. Not one person said a word. They wanted to keep a bit of the LORD and a bit of Baal — like trying to walk down two roads at the same time.</p><p>So Elijah set up a test. ‘Bring two bulls. The prophets of Baal can prepare one and put it on the wood. I’ll prepare the other one. But nobody lights a fire! Then each side will pray to its god. The one who answers by starting the fire is God.’</p><p>Think about that. Baal was supposed to be the god of storms and lightning. Fire from the sky should be easy for him! Elijah was letting Baal play on his home ground. And there were four hundred and fifty of them — and only one of Elijah.</p><p>The people looked at each other. Then they shouted, ‘That’s a good idea!’ Everyone agreed.</p><p>The bulls were brought. The wood was piled up. The whole mountain held its breath. Who would answer — Baal, or the LORD?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Three years. No rain. · 2. “There you are, troublemaker!” · 3. Everyone to Mount Carmel! · 4. “How much longer will you try to have things both ways?” · 5. The rules: no fire allowed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Obadiah (18:3–16) is summarised; Jezebel’s attacks on the prophets are implied, not described. The animal sacrifice was the normal way of worship then — explain simply that offerings were given to God (or gods) on an altar, and Jesus later became the final sacrifice, so we don’t need to do this now.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Baal vs the LORD</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look closer: what did people believe about Baal — and what was really true?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What people believed about Baal</th><th>What was really true</th></tr><tr><td>Baal sends rain and storms</td><td>No rain for three years — Baal did nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Baal throws lightning — fire is easy for him</td><td>The contest would be about fire!</td></tr><tr><td>450 prophets must be right</td><td>Being popular doesn’t make something true</td></tr><tr><td>You can follow Baal AND the LORD</td><td>“If the LORD is God, worship him!”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There is only one true God — and he can’t be shared with anything else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Baal was worshipped as a storm god, often shown holding a thunderbolt.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The rules</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the rules of Elijah’s contest?<br>A) The fastest runner wins &nbsp; B) Pray — and the god who answers with fire is God ✓ &nbsp; C) Whoever has more prophets wins &nbsp; D) Make it rain first</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Kings 18:23–24). Two bulls, two piles of wood, no one lights the fire. Follow-up: ‘Why do you think Elijah let the prophets of Baal choose their bull and go first?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How many prophets did Baal have — and how many did the LORD have on the mountain?’ (450 vs one.) If silent: re-read the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Both ways</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Elijah asked, “How much longer will you try to have things both ways?” What did he mean — and why did the people stay silent?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they wanted the LORD AND Baal, just in case; they didn’t want to choose; they were scared of the king and queen; they knew Elijah was right but didn’t want to admit it. Follow-up: ‘Can you follow two leaders who want opposite things?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What might “both ways” look like for a kid today?’ (Praying on Sunday but being unkind all week; trusting luck charms.) If silent: draw a fork in the road and ask someone to walk down both paths at once.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: One against 450</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Elijah stood alone against 450 prophets, an angry king and a silent crowd. Was he brave, foolish — or something else? Vote, then explain.<br>A) Brave &nbsp; B) Foolish &nbsp; C) Trusting God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect a mix. Draw out: it wasn’t about Elijah being tough — he was trusting God’s word (‘I will soon send rain’). Faith isn’t the same as showing off. Follow-up: ‘What made Elijah so sure?’ (God had kept his word about the drought and the jar of flour.) Follow-up 2: ‘When might you be the only one standing up for what’s right?’ If silent: ask, ‘Who was really on Elijah’s side?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The LORD is the only true God — we can’t follow him ‘both ways’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I don’t want to sit on the fence. I choose you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Notice one ‘both ways’ moment this week — like being kind to a friend’s face and mean behind their back — and choose the LORD’s way instead.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This leads into today’s written prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Choose!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If the LORD is God, worship him!”<br>The people did not say a word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build 1 Kings 18:39 with the actions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A written ‘I choose you’ prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Persistent prayer is easier when you write it down — then you can pray it again tomorrow, and the next day, like Elijah who kept praying for rain. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write at the top: ‘LORD, you are God.’ We’ll add a line for each part.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about why the LORD is the only real God.<br>Prompts: You are the living God — Baal is just a statue. / You hold back the rain and send it again.<br>Starter: <b>LORD, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, you are the only true God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have you tried to have things ‘both ways’?<br>Prompts: Sorry for saying I follow you but not living it. / Sorry for staying silent when I should have spoken up.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, we’re sorry for sitting on the fence.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank you for someone who stood up for God — like Obadiah or Elijah.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who hid and fed your prophets. / Thank you for brave people in my life who follow you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for brave people who follow you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you will keep praying about all week.<br>Prompts: Please help me choose you every day. / Please send ‘rain’ on a dry situation I know about.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us choose you — every day.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your paper and keep it. Pray it again tomorrow — that’s persistent prayer! Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students will reuse these papers on Thursday. Collect them if they might get lost.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the contest begins. Will Baal answer — or the LORD?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Fire Falls":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Kings 17:1–16; 18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Fire Falls</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persistent prayer — Elijah prayed and sent his servant to look seven times (1 Kings 18:43). Persistent prayer means keep asking and keep watching — God hears the first time, and he loves it when we don’t give up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Baal failed to answer and how the LORD answered Elijah with fire.</li><li>We are learning why Elijah soaked his altar with water before he prayed.</li><li>I can explain what the people learned when they shouted, “The LORD is God!”</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students contrasting the noisy, endless prayers to Baal with Elijah’s short, trusting prayer, and explaining that the fire showed the LORD alone is God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: an empty jug for ‘pouring’ water; red and orange scarves for fire</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Fire Falls</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The big day! Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Make it harder</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If you wanted to prove you could do something amazing, would you make it EASIER or HARDER for yourself?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why would anyone make it harder on purpose?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take ideas: blindfold tricks, one-handed catches — so nobody can say it was luck or a trick. Recap quickly: two bulls, no fire, 450 prophets vs one. Land it: ‘Today Elijah makes it MUCH harder — on purpose. Watch why.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Kings 18:26, 36–38</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:26</b> They prayed to Baal all morning, asking him to answer them. They danced around the altar and shouted, “Answer us, Baal!” But there was no answer.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:36</b> Elijah prayed: “Our LORD, you are the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:37</b> “Please answer me, so these people will know that you are the LORD God, and that you will turn their hearts back to you.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:38</b> The LORD immediately sent fire, and it burned up the sacrifice, the wood, and the stones. It scorched the ground everywhere around the altar and dried up the water in the ditch.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. Notice how short Elijah’s prayer is compared with a whole day of shouting.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The fire of the LORD</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The prophets of Baal went first. They prepared their bull, laid it on the wood, and began to pray. ‘Answer us, Baal! Answer us, Baal!’ All morning they danced around the altar and shouted.</p><p>Nothing happened.</p><p>By midday, Elijah couldn’t help himself. ‘Pray louder!’ he said. ‘Baal must be a god! Maybe he’s daydreaming. Maybe he’s gone on a trip. Or maybe he’s asleep and you have to wake him up!’</p><p>So they shouted even louder. They did all sorts of wild things to get Baal’s attention. They kept going all afternoon — hour after hour after hour.</p><p>But there was no answer. No voice. Nobody was listening. Because Baal wasn’t real.</p><p>Then Elijah called out, ‘Everyone, come over here.’ The people crowded round. The LORD’s old altar had been smashed, so Elijah rebuilt it with twelve stones — one for each of the twelve tribes of Israel, to remind them they were all God’s people. He dug a ditch around it. He put the wood and the offering on top.</p><p>Then he said something strange. ‘Fill four big jars with water and pour it over the offering and the wood.’ Water? In a drought? They poured it. ‘Do it again!’ They did. ‘Do it a third time!’ Water ran down the stones and filled the ditch. Now nobody could ever say it was a trick.</p><p>It was time for the evening offering. Elijah didn’t dance. He didn’t shout all day. He stepped forward and prayed a short, simple prayer:</p><p>‘Our LORD, you are the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel. Now prove that you are the God of this nation, and that I am your servant. Please answer me, so these people will know that you are the LORD God, and that you will turn their hearts back to you.’</p><p>And the LORD immediately sent fire! It blazed down from the sky and burned up the offering, the wood — even the stones! It scorched the ground and dried up every drop of water in the ditch.</p><p>The people fell flat on their faces and shouted, ‘The LORD is God! The LORD is God!’</p><p>Baal never answered. The LORD answered the first time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Answer us, Baal!” · 2. No answer. No voice. Nothing. · 3. Twelve stones. A ditch. Water — three times! · 4. A short, simple prayer. · 5. FIRE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. 18:28 says the prophets of Baal cut themselves — we say ‘wild things’; explain gently only if asked. 18:40 records that the prophets of Baal were put to death; this is not told here. If a student knows it, say: ‘They had led a whole nation away from God and harmed God’s people. The Bible is honest about this hard part — but today we focus on what God showed about himself.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Three times</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Elijah told them to pour water over the offering and the wood… ”<br><i>Answer: “…three times — until the ditch was full!” (1 Kings 18:33–35)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘How many stones did Elijah use, and why?’ (Twelve — one for each tribe of Israel.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to the water when the fire fell?’ (It dried up — even in the ditch.) If silent: point to the third story beat and re-read it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Fire Falls on Carmel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Fire Falls on Carmel</b> (1 Kings 18:25–39). Elijah’s prayer and the people’s shout are from 1 Kings 18 (CEV). The prophets’ and Ahab’s individual lines are imagined; the Bible tells us they shouted, ‘Answer us, Baal!’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Elijah</b> — Calm, confident, a bit cheeky<br><b>Baal Prophet 1</b> — Proud and loud — at first<br><b>Baal Prophet 2</b> — Getting more and more desperate<br><b>Ahab</b> — The king, watching — and doubting<br><b>Everyone</b> — The huge crowd on Mount Carmel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the crowd): chanting ‘Answer us, Baal!’, getting louder · Everyone: ‘Splash!’ as the water is poured · Everyone: ‘WHOOSH!’ as the fire falls, then bow low<br>Props: Optional: an empty jug for pouring · Optional: red and orange scarves for the fire<br>Let the crowd be LOUD for Baal, then very quiet for Elijah’s prayer — the contrast is the point. Keep the teasing light-hearted. The prophets’ self-harm (18:28) and their deaths (18:40) are left out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Mount Carmel. Morning. Thousands of people. Two piles of wood. No fire.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Prophets of Baal, you go first. Pray to your god. But don’t light the fire!</li><li><b>Baal Prophet 1:</b> Easy! Baal is the god of storms. Fire from the sky is his speciality! <i>(swagger)</i></li><li><b>Baal Prophet 2:</b> Answer us, Baal! <i>(start dancing round)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Answer us, Baal! Answer us, Baal! <i>(chant, getting louder)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> All morning they danced and shouted. Nothing happened.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Pray louder! Maybe Baal is daydreaming. Or away on a trip. Or asleep — wake him up! <i>(hands cupped, teasing)</i></li><li><b>Baal Prophet 2:</b> BAAAAL! WAKE UP! <i>(shout at the sky)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They shouted until evening. But there was no answer. No one was listening.</li><li><b>Baal Prophet 1:</b> Why… isn’t… he… answering? <i>(flop down, exhausted)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Everyone, come over here. <i>(wave the crowd closer)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Elijah rebuilt the LORD’s altar with twelve stones and dug a ditch around it.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Fill four big jars with water. Pour it on the offering and the wood!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Splash! <i>(pour)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Do it again!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> SPLASH! <i>(pour)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> And one more time!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> SPLAAASH! <i>(biggest pour yet)</i></li><li><b>Ahab:</b> Has he gone mad? Now it will NEVER burn! <i>(shake head)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Why would Elijah make it HARDER for the fire to start? What is he trusting? (20s)</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Our LORD, you are the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel. <i>(kneel, calm voice)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> Please answer me, so these people will know that you are the LORD God.</li><li><b>Elijah:</b> And that you will turn their hearts back to you.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The LORD immediately sent fire!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> WHOOOOOSH! <i>(arms shoot up — fire falls!)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> It burned up the offering, the wood, the stones — and dried up the water in the ditch!</li><li><b>Baal Prophet 2:</b> The stones… the stones are gone! <i>(back away, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The LORD is God! The LORD is God! <i>(bow down low)</i></li><li><b>Elijah:</b> There is only one God. Turn your hearts back to him!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare the two ways of praying. What was different about Elijah’s prayer?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Baal’s prophets: loud, long, all day, desperate — to a god who wasn’t there. Elijah: short, calm, trusting, all about God’s name and the people’s hearts. Jesus said we aren’t heard because of many words (Matthew 6:7–8).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the people do and say when the fire fell?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They bowed down and shouted, ‘The LORD is God! The LORD is God!’ — our memory verse (18:39).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why the fire?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Elijah prayed “so these people will know that you are the LORD God.” Who was the fire really for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: for the people, not for Elijah’s fame; so they would stop sitting on the fence; so their hearts would turn back to God; to show God is real and listens. Follow-up: ‘What did Elijah NOT ask for?’ (To be famous, or for Ahab to be punished.) Follow-up 2: ‘What do you hope people will learn about God when he answers YOUR prayers?’ If silent: re-read 18:37 slowly and ask, ‘Whose name is Elijah thinking about?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fire today?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God doesn’t usually send fire from the sky today. How has God shown people that he is the one true God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: in Jesus — God came himself; Jesus’ miracles; above all, Jesus rising from the dead; the Bible; answered prayers; changed lives. Interesting link: Elijah appears with Jesus on a mountain in Luke 9:28–36, and God says, ‘This is my chosen Son. Listen to what he says!’ Follow-up: ‘What “fire” would convince you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why might God want faith instead of fire every day?’ If silent: ask, ‘What is the biggest thing God has ever done to prove he loves us?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">LORD, you are God. Turn my heart back to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray a short, Elijah-style prayer before something hard this week — a test, a game, a tricky conversation: ‘LORD, show that you are God.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a 15-word prayer. Short and trusting beats long and showy.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: No answer</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Answer us, Baal!”<br>But there was no answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for 1 Kings 18:39 — now everyone knows what the people SAW!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: One-sentence prayers (out loud)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Elijah’s prayer took less than a minute — and God answered! Today anyone can pray one short sentence out loud. Short prayers are real prayers. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone who wants to can pray one short sentence out loud. Then we’ll say the class line together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Shout it like the crowd on Carmel — but say it to God.<br>Prompts: LORD, you are God! / You answered with fire — you are real and you listen.<br>Starter: <b>LORD, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about anything you’ve put in his place.<br>Prompts: Sorry for treating other things like they matter more than you. / Sorry for praying to show off instead of to talk to you.<br>Starter: <b>LORD, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “LORD, turn our hearts back to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">One sentence out loud: thank God for answering prayer.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you hear short prayers. / Thank you for a prayer you have already answered for me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, LORD, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, LORD, that you hear and answer!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">One sentence out loud: ask God to show himself to someone.<br>Prompts: Please show my friends that you are real. / Please turn people’s hearts back to you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, LORD, …</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please answer us, LORD, so people will know you are God.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone together: The LORD is God! Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t force anyone to pray aloud. Model a really short sentence first.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Elijah climbs back up the mountain to pray for rain — seven times.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Small Cloud, Big Rain":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">1 Kings 17:1–16; 18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Small Cloud, Big Rain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Persistent prayer — Elijah prayed and sent his servant to look seven times (1 Kings 18:43). Persistent prayer means keep asking and keep watching — God hears the first time, and he loves it when we don’t give up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Elijah kept praying for rain and sent his servant to look seven times.</li><li>We are learning that faith keeps praying and watching before it sees an answer.</li><li>I can recite 1 Kings 18:39 and explain what this week taught me about God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell Elijah’s story in order, explain the ‘small cloud’ as a picture of persistent, believing prayer, and recite 1 Kings 18:39.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Tuesday’s written prayers (if collected)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Small Cloud, Big Rain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Elijah’s adventure in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Elijah’s adventure in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Elijah warns Ahab: no rain → Ravens bring food → The widow’s jar never runs out → “There you are, troublemaker!” → The contest is set on Carmel → Baal doesn’t answer → Water poured three times → Fire falls from the LORD</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Cards start scrambled. Students call out what comes next; click to place it. After each card, ask: ‘What did that show about God?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: 1 Kings 18:42–44 · James 5:17–18</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:42–43</b> Elijah climbed back to the top of Mount Carmel. Then he stooped down with his face almost to the ground and told his servant, “Look toward the sea.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>1 Kings 18:43–44</b> Elijah told him to look seven more times. After the seventh time the servant replied, “I see a small cloud coming this way. But it’s no bigger than a fist.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>James 5:17</b> Elijah was just as human as we are, and for three and a half years his prayers kept the rain from falling.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>James 5:18</b> But when he did pray for rain, it fell from the skies and made the crops grow.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>James 5 shows that Elijah’s prayers were behind both the drought and the rain — and that he was an ordinary person like us.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Look again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After the fire fell, Elijah told King Ahab, ‘Go and eat. I hear a heavy rain coming.’ But there wasn’t a single cloud in the sky.</p><p>Elijah climbed back to the top of Mount Carmel. He crouched down with his face almost to the ground, and he prayed. God had promised rain — and now Elijah was asking for it.</p><p>‘Go and look toward the sea,’ he told his servant. The servant looked. ‘Nothing.’</p><p>‘Go again.’ Nothing. Again. Nothing. Again… and again… and again. Six times — nothing. But Elijah kept praying.</p><p>The seventh time, the servant came running. ‘I see a small cloud coming this way. But it’s no bigger than a fist.’</p><p>That was enough for Elijah! ‘Tell Ahab to get his chariot ready and go — or the rain will stop him!’ Soon the sky went black with clouds, the wind blew, and a heavy rain poured down. Then the power of the LORD came on Elijah, and he ran all the way to Jezreel — ahead of the king’s horses!</p><p>The Bible says Elijah was just as human as we are. He wasn’t a superhero. He was an ordinary person who trusted God and kept on praying. Jesus taught the same thing: keep asking, keep looking, don’t give up. Sometimes God answers straight away, like the fire. Sometimes he asks us to keep praying and keep watching, like the rain. Both come from the same faithful God.</p><p>So who is God? The only God. The God who sees, provides, answers with fire — and sends rain after one small cloud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. “I hear a heavy rain coming.” · 2. Face to the ground, Elijah prayed. · 3. Seven times! · 4. Rain!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Jezreel was about 25–30 km away. Jesus on persistent prayer: Luke 18:1 (‘Pray and never give up’) and Matthew 7:7.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How big?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After the seventh look, how big was the cloud the servant saw?<br>A) As big as a mountain &nbsp; B) No bigger than a fist ✓ &nbsp; C) As big as the sky &nbsp; D) There was no cloud</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (1 Kings 18:44). Follow-up: ‘How many times did the servant look before he saw it?’ (Seven.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Elijah do when he heard about the tiny cloud?’ (Told Ahab to hurry before the rain stopped him — he believed it was coming.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why keep praying?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God had already promised rain. So why do you think Elijah still prayed — and kept praying seven times?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God likes us to ask; praying is trusting; Elijah wanted to be part of what God was doing; prayer isn’t forcing God, it’s agreeing with what he has said. Follow-up: ‘What would you have thought on the fourth or fifth “Nothing”?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is this like Hannah praying year after year last week?’ If silent: ask, ‘When a parent promises a treat, do you stop talking about it?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>The LORD stopped the rain and fed Elijah and a widow when there was nothing — he provides.</li><li>On Mount Carmel, Baal was silent but the LORD answered with fire — he is the only God.</li><li>Elijah kept praying until a small cloud became a great rain — God answers those who trust him.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The LORD alone is God — and he answers those who trust him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Small clouds</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A ‘small cloud’ is the first little sign that God is answering. What is something only God can do that you could keep praying about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Write it on a mini-whiteboard. Ideas: a friend or family member coming to know God; peace in a family argument; healing; a country at war; rain for farmers in a drought. Follow-up: ‘What might a “small cloud” look like for that prayer?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What if the answer takes much longer than a week?’ (Keep trusting — God’s timing.) If silent: share a ‘small cloud’ answer from your own life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Small cloud prayers. Each day, pray one ‘small cloud’ prayer for something only God can do — and keep watching for the first little sign of his answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, ‘The LORD is God! The LORD is God!’” — 1 Kings 18:39</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday will check in on this. Suggest writing the prayer on the take-home card.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When the people saw this, they bowed down and shouted, “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”” — 1 Kings 18:39 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out 1 Kings 18:39. Bonus: who can say last week’s verse, 1 Samuel 2:2?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Rain!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I see a small cloud…”<br>Then the sky turned black — and the rain poured down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Seven-times prayer (pairs)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Persistent prayer means asking again and again. Elijah prayed while his servant watched — two people working together. Today pray in pairs, and keep asking. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Each tell the other your ‘small cloud’ prayer from the question we just did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns to tell God one thing this week showed you about him.<br>Prompts: LORD, you are the only God. / You control the rain and the fire.<br>Starter: <b>LORD, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “The LORD is God! The LORD is God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: are there prayers you gave up on too quickly?<br>Prompts: Sorry for giving up on praying. / Sorry for doubting that you hear me.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for giving up too quickly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God together for something he has answered before.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you answered Elijah. / Thank you for a time you answered my prayer.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, LORD, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, LORD, that you hear and answer prayer!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your PARTNER’s small-cloud prayer — then pray it again.<br>Prompts: Please, LORD, do what only you can do for (name). / Please help us keep watching for your answer.<br>Starter: <b>Please, LORD, …</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, LORD — we’ll keep praying and keep watching.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say 1 Kings 18:39 together as our closing prayer. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage pairs to agree to pray for each other every day this week.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: “Even If He Doesn’t” — three friends, a golden statue, a blazing furnace and a den of lions (Daniel 3 and 6).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6: “Even If He Doesn’t”":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel 3; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Able</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even if” prayers — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego told God (and the king) exactly what they believed: “God can save us… but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.” An “even if” prayer asks God honestly for what we want — and then trusts him whatever his answer is. Jesus prayed like this too: “Do what you want, and not what I want.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Golden Statue</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused to bow to King Nebuchadnezzar’s gold statue.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Golden Statue → Everyone else is doing it → Daniel 3:1, 6, 16–18 → Three who stood → Who stood? → Just a quick bow? → The Golden Statue → Even if → Head · Heart · Hands → Bow down! → Memory verse peek → Standing prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Fourth Man in the Fire</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God was with Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego in the furnace.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Fourth Man in the Fire → Recap → Daniel 3:24–25, 27–28 → Walking in the fire → What the king expected → How many? → In, not out → The fourth man → Head · Heart · Hands → Not even a smell of smoke → Build the verse → Praying the friends’ words</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Daniel Opens His Window</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how jealous officials tricked King Darius into making a law against prayer.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Daniel Opens His Window → Nothing to find → Daniel 6:4–5, 10, 16 → The window that faced Jerusalem → Three times a day → Daniel Opens His Window → Why not close the window? → Quietly brave → Head · Heart · Hands → Sealed → Verse with actions → Open-window prayer (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Morning at the Lions’ Den</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how God rescued Daniel from the pit of lions.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Morning at the Lions’ Den → Who said it? → Daniel 6:20, 22–23 → Was he able? → Who kept the lions back? → Two kings changed → This Week’s Big Idea → When God says ‘not like that’ → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Not a scratch → My ‘even if’ prayer (written)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Golden Statue":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel 3; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Golden Statue</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even if” prayers — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego told God (and the king) exactly what they believed: “God can save us… but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.” An “even if” prayer asks God honestly for what we want — and then trusts him whatever his answer is. Jesus prayed like this too: “Do what you want, and not what I want.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused to bow to King Nebuchadnezzar’s gold statue.</li><li>We are learning why worshipping only God mattered so much to them.</li><li>I can explain what the friends meant when they said, “But even if he doesn’t…”</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the friends believed God COULD rescue them, but would obey him even if he didn’t — and that this is what real trust looks like.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a gold paper crown for the king; a kazoo or recorder for the ‘music’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Golden Statue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Three new heroes for the Faith Museum this week — plus an old friend of theirs on Wednesday. Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Everyone else is doing it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been the only one NOT doing something everyone else was doing?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did it feel to be the only one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we watched Elijah on Mount Carmel — fire fell, and then a cloud no bigger than a fist turned into a great rain. Your challenge: pray one ‘small cloud’ prayer each day for something only God can do, and keep watching. Did you keep praying? Did you spot any small clouds?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate anyone who kept praying their ‘small cloud’ prayer, answered or not — keep praying! Then the hook: the only one not laughing at a joke, not joining a game, not going along with a dare. Land it: ‘Today three friends are the only three people standing in a crowd of thousands — and the king is watching.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Daniel 3:1, 6, 16–18</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:1</b> King Nebuchadnezzar ordered a gold statue to be built ninety feet high and nine feet wide.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:6</b> “Anyone who refuses will at once be thrown into a flaming furnace.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:16</b> Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego replied, “Your Majesty, we don’t need to defend ourselves.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:17–18</b> “The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. Ninety feet is about 27 metres — roughly a nine-storey building. The last line is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Three who stood</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Long ago, God’s people were taken far from home. The army of Babylon had marched into Jerusalem and carried many young people away to serve the king. Among them were four friends: Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah. The Babylonians even changed their names. Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah became Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. But they didn’t change who they belonged to. They still loved the LORD.</p><p>The king of Babylon was Nebuchadnezzar — the most powerful man in the world. One day he had a giant idea. He built a statue out of gold, twenty-seven metres tall — about as high as a nine-storey building! He set it up on a wide, flat plain, where it blazed in the sun.</p><p>Then he called every leader in the kingdom to come and see it. Governors, judges, treasurers, officials — thousands of them stood in front of the shining statue.</p><p>A herald shouted: ‘People of every nation, listen! When you hear the horns, the flutes, the harps and all the other instruments, bow down and worship the gold statue. Anyone who refuses will at once be thrown into a flaming furnace!’</p><p>The music began. And like a wave rolling across the sea, thousands of people dropped to the ground.</p><p>Except for three.</p><p>Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego stayed standing. They weren’t being rude, and they weren’t showing off. But God’s first commandment says, ‘Do not worship any god except me.’ They would not bow to a statue — not even a gold one, not even for a king.</p><p>Some officials couldn’t wait to tell. ‘Your Majesty, those three Jews you put in charge — they won’t bow down!’</p><p>Nebuchadnezzar was furious. He sent for the three friends. ‘I’ll give you one more chance. Bow down when you hear the music, and everything will be all right. If you don’t, you will be thrown into the flaming furnace. No god can save you from me!’</p><p>The three friends looked at the king. Then they answered calmly.</p><p>‘Your Majesty, we don’t need to defend ourselves. The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.’</p><p>Even if he doesn’t. They believed God COULD rescue them. But they would trust him and obey him either way.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Far from home, in Babylon. · 2. A gold statue, nine storeys tall! · 3. Thousands bowed down. · 4. “No god can save you from me!” · 5. “Even if he doesn’t…”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Babylon conquered Jerusalem around 605–586 BC; the friends were probably taken as teenagers (Daniel 1). Their new names honoured Babylonian gods — but their hearts stayed with the LORD. ‘The God we worship can save us’ is CEV; other versions say ‘is able to deliver us’ — which is why this week’s godIs word is ‘Able’. Keep the furnace threat matter-of-fact, not scary.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who stood?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When the music played and everyone bowed down, what did Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego do?<br>A) They bowed down too &nbsp; B) They ran away and hid &nbsp; C) They stayed standing ✓ &nbsp; D) They knocked the statue over</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (Daniel 3:12). They didn’t argue, run or cause trouble — they simply wouldn’t bow. Follow-up: ‘Who told the king about them?’ (Some officials who were watching.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was the punishment for not bowing?’ (The flaming furnace.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line and point to the third story beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Just a quick bow?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Someone might say, “Just bow quickly — you don’t have to MEAN it.” Why do you think the three friends wouldn’t do even that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: bowing to a statue IS worshipping it, whatever you think inside; God’s first commandment says worship only him; everyone watching would think the LORD didn’t matter; they wanted to be the same on the outside as on the inside. Follow-up: ‘What would the crowd have learned if they had bowed?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When is it tempting to go along with something “just this once”?’ If silent: ask, ‘If you said you loved your family but pretended not to know them in front of others, would that matter?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Golden Statue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Golden Statue</b> (Daniel 3:1–18). The herald’s announcement, the king’s threat and the friends’ answer follow Daniel 3 (CEV, slightly shortened). The friends’ individual lines to each other and the officials’ exact words are imagined — the Bible gives their reply as one shared answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Nebuchadnezzar</b> — Proud, powerful king of Babylon<br><b>Herald</b> — The king’s loud announcer<br><b>Shadrach</b> — Calm and brave<br><b>Meshach</b> — Steady and thoughtful<br><b>Abednego</b> — Quiet but firm<br><b>Official</b> — A jealous tell-tale<br><b>Everyone</b> — The band and the huge bowing crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the band): ‘Toot-toot! Twang!’ on imaginary horns and harps · Everyone (as the crowd): bow down low — all at once · Everyone: a low ‘Ooooh…’ when the friends keep standing<br>Props: Optional: a gold paper crown for the king · Optional: a kazoo or recorder for the music<br>Have the three friends stand very still while everyone else bows — it’s a powerful picture. Keep the king’s anger dramatic but fun. If students ask what happens next, say: ‘Come back tomorrow!’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The plain of Dura, in Babylon. A giant gold statue shines in the sun.</li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> Look at it! Twenty-seven metres of pure gold. Isn’t it magnificent? <i>(arms spread wide)</i></li><li><b>Herald:</b> People of every nation, listen! When you hear the music, bow down and worship the gold statue! <i>(unroll a scroll)</i></li><li><b>Herald:</b> Anyone who refuses will at once be thrown into a flaming furnace!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Toot-toot! Twang! Boom-boom! <i>(play imaginary instruments)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Thousands of people dropped to the ground…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoomph! <i>(bow down low, all together)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Except for three.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh… <i>(peek up at the three)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> Your Majesty! Those three Jews you put in charge — they won’t bow down! <i>(point, tell-tale style)</i></li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> WHAT?! Bring them here! <i>(leap up)</i></li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> Is it true? You won’t worship my gods or my gold statue?</li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> I’ll give you one more chance. Bow down when the music plays, and everything will be all right.</li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> If you don’t — into the flaming furnace! No god can save you from me! <i>(point to the furnace)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The king is furious. The furnace is roaring. What would YOU be thinking right now? What would make it hard to stay standing? (20s)</li><li><b>Shadrach:</b> Your Majesty, we don’t need to defend ourselves. <i>(calm, steady voice)</i></li><li><b>Meshach:</b> The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace.</li><li><b>Abednego:</b> But even if he doesn’t… <i>(small pause)</i></li><li><b>Shadrach:</b> …we still won’t worship your gods…</li><li><b>Meshach:</b> …or the gold statue you have set up.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Gasp! <i>(hands over mouths)</i></li><li><b>Nebuchadnezzar:</b> You dare to say no to ME?! <i>(face red, fists clenched)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The three friends stood together. They knew God could rescue them.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But even if he didn’t, they would trust him anyway.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Even if he doesn’t — we will trust God!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends said God CAN save us… but EVEN IF he doesn’t, we’ll still trust him. Why is that bigger faith than just ‘God will save us’?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Their faith wasn’t a deal (‘We’ll obey if you rescue us’). They trusted God because of who he is, not because of what he would do for them. They loved God more than their own safety.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the king say no god could do?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘No god can save you from me!’ (Daniel 3:15). Tomorrow we find out if he was right.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Even if</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends believed God COULD rescue them, but they weren’t sure he WOULD. Is it still faith if you don’t know how God will answer?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them wrestle with it. Draw out: yes — faith is trusting God himself, not being sure of the outcome; God is good even when we don’t get what we hoped; Jesus prayed ‘Do what you want, and not what I want’ before the cross. Follow-up: ‘Can you think of a time when God didn’t answer the way you wanted?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps you keep trusting God then?’ If silent: ask, ‘Do you trust a good friend only when they do what you want?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is able to rescue — and he is worth trusting even when he doesn’t do what we expect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to trust you, not just what you can do for me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When everyone around you is going along with something wrong this week, choose to ‘stay standing’ — quietly and kindly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask for one ‘everyone’s doing it’ moment they might face this week, and what staying standing would look like.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Bow down!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Toot-toot! Twang!<br>Thousands bowed down. Three stood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Daniel 3:17–18 — the friends’ own words to the king. It’s a long one, so just echo it today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Standing prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week’s prayer skill is the ‘even if’ prayer: ask God for what you hope, then tell him you’ll trust him whatever happens. Today we pray standing up — like three friends who wouldn’t bow to anything except God. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand up, tall and still. We only bow to the true God. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is greater than the most powerful king on earth.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you are greater than any king. / You are the only one worth worshipping — no statue can compare.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are able — and you alone are God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: when have you gone along with something wrong because everyone else did?<br>Prompts: Sorry for bowing to what my friends think instead of you. / Sorry for following the crowd when I knew it was wrong.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for following the crowd instead of you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for people who stand up for what is right.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego’s courage. / Thank you for someone who has stood up for me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for brave people who trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Try an ‘even if’ prayer: ask for something, then add ‘but even if…’<br>Prompts: Please help my friend who is sick — but even if it takes a long time, I’ll trust you. / Please help me stay standing for you this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God… and even if…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us. And even if you answer differently, we will trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stay standing tall. Everyone together: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model an ‘even if’ prayer first. Be gentle: some students may be praying about hard things (illness, family trouble). Reassure them that asking God for good things is right — the ‘even if’ simply means trusting he is good whatever happens.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the furnace is heated seven times hotter — and the king sees something he can’t explain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Fourth Man in the Fire":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel 3; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Fourth Man in the Fire</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even if” prayers — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego told God (and the king) exactly what they believed: “God can save us… but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.” An “even if” prayer asks God honestly for what we want — and then trusts him whatever his answer is. Jesus prayed like this too: “Do what you want, and not what I want.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God was with Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego in the furnace.</li><li>We are learning how King Nebuchadnezzar changed his mind about the God of Israel.</li><li>I can explain the difference between God keeping us OUT of trouble and God being WITH us in it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the friends were not kept out of the fire but were not alone in it, and that the king ended up praising the God he had said could not save them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch and an orange cloth to show the ‘fire’ glow</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Fourth Man in the Fire</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday three friends stayed standing while thousands bowed down.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did they say to the king? Can anyone remember the ‘even if’?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘The God we worship can save us… But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods.’ Then: ‘The king said, “No god can save you from me!” Today we find out who was right.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Daniel 3:24–25, 27–28</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:24</b> King Nebuchadnezzar jumped up and shouted, “Weren’t only three men tied up and thrown into the fire?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:25</b> “But I see four men walking around in the fire. They are not tied up, and they are not hurt. The fourth one even looks like a god.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:27</b> The fire had not harmed them. Not a hair on their heads was scorched, and their clothes didn’t even smell like smoke.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 3:28</b> King Nebuchadnezzar said: “Praise their God for sending an angel to rescue his servants! They trusted their God.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged and lightly shortened. Notice the king who said ‘No god can save you’ now says ‘Praise their God’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Walking in the fire</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>When Nebuchadnezzar heard ‘even if he doesn’t’, his face twisted with rage. Nobody spoke to the king like that!</p><p>‘Heat the furnace seven times hotter than usual!’ he roared. Then he ordered his strongest soldiers to tie up Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. Still wearing their robes and caps, the three friends were thrown into the blazing furnace. The fire roared so fiercely that no one could go near it.</p><p>The king had won. Nobody would ever disobey him again. He sat down to watch.</p><p>Then he jumped to his feet. ‘Weren’t there only three men tied up and thrown into the fire?’</p><p>‘Yes, Your Majesty,’ his advisors said.</p><p>‘But I see four men walking around in the fire,’ the king said. ‘They are not tied up, and they are not hurt. The fourth one even looks like a god.’</p><p>Walking around! In the fire! The ropes were gone — but the friends were fine. And someone else was in there with them.</p><p>The king went as close as he dared to the furnace door and shouted, ‘Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego, servants of the Most High God, come out!’</p><p>Out they walked. All the governors and officials crowded round to look. They stared. They poked. They sniffed. The fire had not harmed them. Not one hair on their heads was scorched. Their clothes weren’t burned. They didn’t even smell like smoke!</p><p>Nebuchadnezzar — the king who had said, ‘No god can save you from me’ — now said something very different. ‘Praise their God for sending an angel to rescue his servants! They trusted their God and refused to obey my commands. They were willing to die rather than worship any god except their own.’</p><p>Then the king made a new law: nobody in his kingdom was allowed to say anything against the God of Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. ‘No other god can save people like this!’ And he gave the three friends even more important jobs.</p><p>Think about what happened. God did not keep his friends OUT of the fire. He met them IN the fire. They still had to be brave. They still went through the door. But they were not alone.</p><p>Who was the fourth man? The king said God had sent an angel. Some Christians think it might even have been God’s Son, long before he was born as baby Jesus. We don’t know for sure. But we do know this: Jesus promised his followers, ‘I will be with you always.’ He doesn’t promise we will never face hard things. He promises we will never face them alone.</p><p>The friends had said, ‘Even if he doesn’t rescue us, we will still trust him.’ That day God did rescue them — and the proudest king in the world saw that the God of Israel is able.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Seven times hotter!” · 2. Into the furnace. · 3. “I see FOUR men!” · 4. “Come out!” · 5. “Praise their God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Daniel 3:22 tells what happened to the soldiers at the furnace door; this is deliberately left out. If a student knows it, say simply: ‘The fire was so hot it was dangerous for anyone near it — which shows how big God’s rescue was.’ The king’s harsh threat in 3:29 is also left out. ‘I will be with you always’ is Matthew 28:20.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: What the king expected</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look closer: what did the king expect — and what actually happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What the king expected</th><th>What actually happened</th></tr><tr><td>Three men, tied up</td><td>Four men, walking around free</td></tr><tr><td>Burned by a fire seven times hotter</td><td>Not a hair scorched — not even a smell of smoke</td></tr><tr><td>“No god can save you from me!”</td><td>“Praise their God for sending an angel to rescue his servants!”</td></tr><tr><td>Everyone will be scared to disobey</td><td>Everyone heard about the God who is able to save</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t keep them out of the fire — he was with them in it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. The third row is the big turnaround of the whole chapter.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How many?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many men did the king see walking around in the fire?<br>A) Two &nbsp; B) Three &nbsp; C) Four ✓ &nbsp; D) Seven</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C — four (Daniel 3:25). Three were thrown in; a fourth was with them. Follow-up: ‘What did the king say the fourth one looked like?’ (Like a god — he later said God sent an angel.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was missing when they came out?’ (The ropes! And any burns or smell of smoke.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: In, not out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God could have stopped them being thrown into the furnace at all. Why do you think he let them go IN — and then met them there?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: so everyone could see God’s power; so the king would learn no one can beat God; to show God is with his people in hard places; their faith was tested and became stronger. Follow-up: ‘Who learned the most from the fire — the friends, or the king?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What “fires” do kids go through — and how might God be with them there?’ (Moving schools, family trouble, sickness, being left out.) If silent: ask, ‘Would the king have praised God if nothing had happened?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The fourth man</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think quietly: Jesus is called ‘Immanuel’, which means ‘God with us’. How is the fourth man in the fire like what Jesus does for us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give 30 seconds of silent thinking, then take answers. Draw out: Jesus came INTO our world, with all its pain; he goes with us through hard things; he promised ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20); on the cross he went through the worst ‘fire’ for us. Follow-up: ‘Does God being with us mean nothing bad will happen?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might knowing Jesus is with you change how you face something scary?’ If silent: read Isaiah 43:2 — ‘When you walk through fire, you won’t be burned’ — and ask what it reminds them of.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is able to save — and he is with his people even in the fire.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you that I am never alone in hard things.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be a ‘fourth person’ for someone this week: notice someone going through something hard and stay with them — sit with them, walk with them, check in on them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This leads into today’s prayer, which uses Daniel 3:17–18.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Not even a smell of smoke</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sniff, sniff… nothing!<br>“Praise their God for sending an angel to rescue his servants!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Daniel 3:17–18 with the actions. Point out where the ‘even if’ comes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying the friends’ words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We can pray the Bible back to God. Today we turn the friends’ ‘even if’ words into our own prayer: God, you CAN… and even if… <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">I’ll say a line, and you echo it back to God. Quiet voices — this isn’t talking to the king, it’s talking to the King of kings.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me.<br>Prompts: “God, you are able.” / “You walked with your friends in the fire.”<br>Starter: <b>God, you are able to…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “The God we worship can save us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for trusting God only when things go my way.<br>Prompts: Sorry for only trusting you when I get what I want. / Sorry for being scared of what people think more than trusting you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for trusting you only when things go our way.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for being with us — Immanuel.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, that you never leave me alone. / Thank you that you were with the friends in the fire.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, that…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus — God with us — that we are never alone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a ‘fire’ you or someone you know is facing. Pray: ‘You CAN… but even if…’<br>Prompts: God, you can fix this problem — but even if you don’t, I will trust you. / Please be with (name) in the hard thing they are facing.<br>Starter: <b>God, you can… but even if…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you can save us. But even if you don’t, we will still trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: The God we worship can save us! Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Some students may be facing real ‘fires’. Let the Please phase be silent if that feels safer. Follow up privately with anyone who seems troubled.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: years later, their friend Daniel — now an old man — faces a new law and a new king.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Daniel Opens His Window":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel 3; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel Opens His Window</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even if” prayers — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego told God (and the king) exactly what they believed: “God can save us… but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.” An “even if” prayer asks God honestly for what we want — and then trusts him whatever his answer is. Jesus prayed like this too: “Do what you want, and not what I want.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how jealous officials tricked King Darius into making a law against prayer.</li><li>We are learning how Daniel kept praying to God, even though it was against the law.</li><li>I can explain how Daniel stood up for God in a quiet, steady way.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Daniel didn’t hide or show off — he simply kept praying as he always had — and why the officials could only trap him through his faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a cardboard ‘window’ frame; a gold ring or bottle top for the king’s seal</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Daniel Opens His Window</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Daniel was the fourth friend from Monday — now much older.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Nothing to find</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine someone watched EVERYTHING you did for a whole week, trying to catch you doing something wrong. What might they find?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Could anyone live so well that there was nothing to find?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Laugh together — we’d all be caught! Then: ‘Today a group of jealous men watched one man for a long time and couldn’t find a single thing wrong. So they had to invent a trap.’ Recap briefly: Daniel was the friend of Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Daniel 6:4–5, 10, 16</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:4</b> The other governors and officials… could not find anything wrong. Daniel was honest and did everything he was supposed to do.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:5</b> “We will never be able to bring any charge against Daniel, unless it has something to do with his religion.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:10</b> Daniel… went upstairs and prayed in front of the window that faced Jerusalem. In the same way that he had always done, he knelt down in prayer three times a day, giving thanks to God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:16</b> Darius said to Daniel, “You have been faithful to your God, and I pray that he will rescue you.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. The CEV calls the lions’ den a ‘pit of lions’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The window that faced Jerusalem</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Many years passed. Babylon fell, and a new king named Darius ruled. Daniel — the friend of Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego — was now an old man, about eighty. But he still loved God, and he still worked hard.</p><p>King Darius chose one hundred and twenty officials to help rule his kingdom, and three leaders to watch over them. Daniel was one of the three. In fact, Daniel did such a good job that the king planned to put him in charge of the whole kingdom.</p><p>The other officials were jealous. They watched Daniel closely, hoping to catch him doing something wrong. But they couldn’t find anything! He was honest. He was hard-working. He never cheated.</p><p>Finally they said, ‘We will never be able to bring any charge against Daniel — unless it has something to do with his religion.’</p><p>So they made a sneaky plan. They went to the king. ‘King Darius, live forever! You should make a new law. For thirty days, no one may pray to any god or any person except you. Anyone who disobeys will be thrown into a pit of lions.’</p><p>The king liked the sound of that. He signed the law — and a law of the Medes and Persians could never be changed. Not even by the king.</p><p>Daniel heard about the law. So what did he do? He went home, climbed the stairs, and opened his window — the one that faced Jerusalem, his faraway home city. And just as he had always done, he knelt down and prayed three times a day, giving thanks to God.</p><p>He didn’t hide. He didn’t shout or show off either. He simply kept praying, the way he always had.</p><p>The officials were watching. They caught him praying and hurried back to the king. ‘Daniel ignores you and your law! He still prays to his God three times a day!’</p><p>Darius felt terrible. He realised he had been tricked. All day, until sunset, he tried to find a way to save Daniel. But the officials reminded him: ‘Your Majesty, the law cannot be changed.’</p><p>So Daniel was taken to the pit of lions. Darius said to him, ‘You have been faithful to your God, and I pray that he will rescue you.’</p><p>A heavy stone was rolled over the opening. The king sealed it with his own ring, so nobody could change anything.</p><p>Then everything went dark.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Daniel, about eighty years old. · 2. “We can’t find anything wrong!” · 3. A new law: pray only to the king! · 4. Daniel opened his window… · 5. A stone. A seal. Darkness.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Daniel was taken to Babylon as a teenager around 605 BC and served under several kings, so he was probably in his eighties by Daniel 6. Keep the lions offstage today — the story ends with the stone and the dark. Darius is not in our character list, so his look is described in the art prompt.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Three times a day</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Daniel went upstairs, opened the window that faced Jerusalem, and…”<br><i>Answer: “…knelt down in prayer three times a day, giving thanks to God — just as he had always done.” (Daniel 6:10)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘Why did Daniel face towards Jerusalem?’ (It was his home city, where God’s temple had been; God’s people prayed towards it.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was Daniel doing in his prayer, besides asking?’ (Giving thanks — even with a death threat hanging over him!) If silent: point to the fourth story beat and re-read the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Daniel Opens His Window</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Daniel Opens His Window</b> (Daniel 6:1–17). The officials’ plan, the law and Darius’s words to Daniel follow Daniel 6 (CEV, shortened). Daniel’s spoken prayer and the officials’ whispered lines are imagined — the Bible tells us he knelt, prayed and gave thanks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Daniel</b> — Old, wise, honest and calm<br><b>King Darius</b> — Likes Daniel — but likes feeling important too<br><b>Official 1</b> — Jealous plotter — the sneaky ideas person<br><b>Official 2</b> — Jealous plotter — the spy<br><b>Everyone</b> — The whispering city</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the city): whisper ‘Psst… psst…’ as the plotters sneak around · Everyone: ‘Thud!’ as the law is stamped · Everyone: a slow, heavy ‘Rrrrumble…’ as the stone is rolled<br>Props: Optional: a cardboard window frame · Optional: a ring or bottle top for the king’s seal<br>Keep the lions offstage and unnamed in action — the scene ends with the stone. Play the plotters as comic villains. Daniel’s line ‘My God is able’ is imagined but echoes this week’s theme.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The palace of King Darius. Two officials are whispering in a corner.</li><li><b>Official 1:</b> The king wants Daniel in charge of the WHOLE kingdom. We have to stop him! <i>(arms folded, scowling)</i></li><li><b>Official 2:</b> I’ve watched him for weeks. He doesn’t cheat. He doesn’t lie. He doesn’t even run late!</li><li><b>Official 1:</b> Then we’ll trap him with the one thing he never stops doing… praying to his God. <i>(sly smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Psst… psst… psst… <i>(whisper and sneak)</i></li><li><b>Official 2:</b> King Darius, live forever! <i>(deep, fake bow)</i></li><li><b>Official 1:</b> For thirty days, let no one pray to any god or person — except you, O King!</li><li><b>Official 2:</b> And anyone who disobeys gets thrown to the lions.</li><li><b>King Darius:</b> Only me? For thirty days? I like it! Where do I sign? <i>(puff out chest)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> THUD! <i>(stamp one foot — the law is sealed)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Daniel heard about the law. He went home, climbed the stairs, and opened his window.</li><li><b>Daniel:</b> LORD God, thank you for another day. You are faithful. <i>(kneel at the window)</i></li><li><b>Daniel:</b> Whatever happens, I will trust you. Even if… <i>(calm, quiet voice)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Daniel knows the law. He knows about the lions. He could close the window, or pray secretly. Why do you think he kept praying just as he always had? (20s)</li><li><b>Official 2:</b> Gotcha! He’s praying to his God! <i>(point up at the window)</i></li><li><b>Official 1:</b> Your Majesty! Daniel ignores you and your law. He still prays three times a day!</li><li><b>King Darius:</b> Daniel? Oh no… What have I done? You tricked me! <i>(head in hands)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> All day, until sunset, the king tried to find a way to save Daniel.</li><li><b>Official 1:</b> Your Majesty, you know the law of the Medes and Persians cannot be changed. <i>(smug)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So Daniel was taken to the pit of lions.</li><li><b>King Darius:</b> Daniel, you have been faithful to your God. I pray that he will rescue you. <i>(voice shaking)</i></li><li><b>Daniel:</b> My God is able, Your Majesty. <i>(steady and calm)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Rrrrumble… <i>(slowly roll the heavy stone)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The king sealed the stone with his own ring. And everything went dark.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Daniel trusted God — even in the dark.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Daniel didn’t hide — but he didn’t march up and down shouting about it either. Why is that a good way to stand up for God?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He just kept doing what was right, calmly and faithfully. He wasn’t trying to pick a fight or look brave — he was being the same Daniel he always was. Quiet faithfulness can be very powerful.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the only thing the officials could find to trap Daniel with?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: His faith — his praying to God (Daniel 6:5). He was so honest that his love for God was the only ‘charge’ they could bring.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why not close the window?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Daniel had three choices. Vote: which would you have chosen? Then — why did Daniel choose to keep praying just as before?<br>A) Close the window and pray secretly &nbsp; B) Stop praying for 30 days &nbsp; C) Keep praying just as before</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them vote honestly — no shame for choosing safety! Draw out: closing the window would look like he was scared of the king more than he trusted God; stopping prayer would mean the king mattered more than God; he wanted to show that nothing comes between him and God. Follow-up: ‘Would it have been wrong to pray secretly?’ (Not wrong to pray privately — but for Daniel, changing his habit would have been giving in.) Follow-up 2: ‘What habit of yours shows that you love God?’ If silent: ask, ‘What did Daniel’s open window say to everyone watching?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Quietly brave</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What is one small, quiet way a kid your age could stand up for God — at school, online or at home?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Write ideas on mini-whiteboards. Ideas: praying before lunch even if no one else does; refusing to join in mean jokes; telling the truth when a lie would be easier; being kind to the kid everyone ignores; saying ‘I go to church’ or ‘I believe in God’ when it comes up. Follow-up: ‘Which of these would be hardest for you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can we stand up for God without being unkind to people who believe differently?’ If silent: give one example from your own life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is worth trusting, even when obeying him costs us something.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to be faithful to you like Daniel — every day, not just on big days.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep one ‘open window’ habit this week: pray at the same times each day — morning, lunch and bedtime — giving thanks to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Suggest an ‘open window’ reminder — e.g. praying when they open their curtains in the morning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Sealed</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I pray that he will rescue you.”<br>The king sealed the stone with his own ring.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Daniel 3:17–18. Ask: ‘Could Daniel have said these same words to King Darius?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open-window prayer (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Daniel prayed quietly, faithfully, giving thanks — even when trouble was coming. An ‘even if’ prayer can be very quiet. Today we pray silently, like Daniel at his window. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine you are opening a window towards God. Close your eyes or look down. Breathe in… and out. Just you and God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God was faithful to Daniel his whole life — from teenager to old man.<br>Prompts: God, you are faithful every single day. / You hear every quiet prayer — even from an upstairs window.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are faithful — and you always hear us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a time you hid that you love God, because you were worried what people would think?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding that I belong to you. / Sorry for being jealous of others, like the officials were.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve hidden our love for you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Daniel gave thanks even on the worst day. What can you thank God for — even today?<br>Prompts: Thank you that I am free to pray to you. / Thank you for one good thing today, even if today is hard.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God — even on hard days, you are good.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for Christians around the world who are not allowed to pray freely.<br>Prompts: Please give courage to Christians who could get into trouble for praying. / Please help me keep praying, like Daniel — even if it’s hard.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with everyone who prays in secret and in danger.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Imagine closing the window gently. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Many Christians around the world still face trouble for praying. Keep this general and hopeful; avoid naming countries unless you are confident and it is age-appropriate.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a long, dark night — and a king who runs to the pit at sunrise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Morning at the Lions’ Den":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Daniel 3; 6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morning at the Lions’ Den</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even if” prayers — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego told God (and the king) exactly what they believed: “God can save us… but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.” An “even if” prayer asks God honestly for what we want — and then trusts him whatever his answer is. Jesus prayed like this too: “Do what you want, and not what I want.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God rescued Daniel from the pit of lions.</li><li>We are learning how King Darius came to praise the living God.</li><li>I can recite Daniel 3:17–18 and explain what an ‘even if’ faith is.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can identify who said key lines from Daniel 3 and 6, explain that God is able to save and worth trusting even when he doesn’t rescue the way we hope, and recite Daniel 3:17–18.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Morning at the Lions’ Den</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Recap: the stone, the seal, the dark.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“No god can save you from me!” — <b>King Nebuchadnezzar</b><br>“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace.” — <b>Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego</b><br>“But I see four men walking around in the fire.” — <b>King Nebuchadnezzar</b><br>“We will never be able to bring any charge against Daniel, unless it has something to do with his religion.” — <b>The jealous officials</b><br>“You have been faithful to your God, and I pray that he will rescue you.” — <b>King Darius</b><br>“My God knew that I was innocent, and he sent an angel to keep the lions from eating me.” — <b>Daniel</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘When did they say it — and what happened next?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Daniel 6:20, 22–23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:20</b> “Daniel, you were faithful and served your God. Was he able to save you from the lions?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:22</b> “My God knew that I was innocent, and he sent an angel to keep the lions from eating me. Your Majesty, I have never done anything to hurt you.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Daniel 6:23</b> Daniel was not harmed, because he had trusted in his God.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. ‘Was he able?’ is the question of the whole week — and the answer is yes.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Was he able?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>That night, King Darius couldn’t eat. He sent the musicians away. He couldn’t sleep. All night he thought about Daniel, alone in the dark with the lions.</p><p>As soon as the sun began to rise, the king jumped up and ran to the pit. He called out, his voice shaking: ‘Daniel, you were faithful and served your God. Was he able to save you from the lions?’</p><p>Silence. And then — a voice!</p><p>‘Your Majesty, I hope you live forever! My God knew that I was innocent, and he sent an angel to keep the lions from eating me.’</p><p>The king was overjoyed! He ordered his servants to pull Daniel out. They looked him over. Not a scratch. Not a bite. Daniel was not harmed, because he had trusted in his God. The king then punished the men who had plotted against Daniel.</p><p>Then Darius wrote to every nation in his kingdom: ‘Everyone must respect the God of Daniel. He is the living God, and his kingdom will never end. He rescues people, and he does mighty miracles.’</p><p>Do you notice something? A stone rolled over a dark pit. A seal so no one could open it. Then, early in the morning, someone came — and the one they thought was finished was alive! Hundreds of years later, the same thing happened to Jesus. His tomb was sealed with a stone. And early on the third morning, he was alive!</p><p>God does not always rescue people the way he rescued Daniel. Some of God’s people have suffered and even died for following him. But because Jesus rose again, even death is not the end. That’s why we can say, ‘God is able — and even if he doesn’t do what we hope, we will still trust him.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A sleepless night. · 2. At dawn, the king ran. · 3. “Your Majesty, I hope you live forever!” · 4. Not a scratch.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Daniel 6:24 describes the fate of the accusers and their families; it is summarised only as ‘the king punished the men who had plotted against Daniel’. Do not elaborate. Darius’s letter is Daniel 6:25–27, paraphrased. If students raise people who were not rescued — including people they know who have died — acknowledge it gently: God loves them, Jesus understands suffering, and his resurrection gives real hope.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who kept the lions back?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Daniel say God did to keep him safe?<br>A) Made the lions fall asleep by magic &nbsp; B) Sent an angel to keep the lions from eating him ✓ &nbsp; C) Gave Daniel a sword &nbsp; D) Sent the lions away</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Daniel 6:22). Follow-up: ‘What reason does the Bible give for Daniel not being harmed?’ (‘Because he had trusted in his God’ — 6:23.) Follow-up 2: ‘What question did the king shout at dawn?’ (‘Was he able to save you from the lions?’) If silent: re-read the second scripture line and point to the fourth story beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two kings changed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both King Nebuchadnezzar and King Darius ended up praising the God of Israel. What made them change their minds?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they saw God rescue his people in impossible situations; they saw how faithful and brave the friends and Daniel were; God showed he is greater than any king or law. Follow-up: ‘What part did the friends’ and Daniel’s faithfulness play?’ (People noticed how they lived — and then they noticed their God.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who might notice God because of how YOU live?’ If silent: re-read Darius’s letter from the story and ask, ‘What did Darius learn about God?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego said, “God can save us — but even if he doesn’t, we will still trust him.”</li><li>God didn’t keep them out of the fire; he was with them in it — and he shut the lions’ mouths for Daniel.</li><li>God is able. Real faith trusts him whether or not he rescues the way we hope — because Jesus has beaten even death.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Real faith trusts God whether or not he rescues in the way we hope.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: When God says ‘not like that’</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sometimes God doesn’t rescue people the way we hope. How can someone still trust God when that happens?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Go gently. Draw out: God is still good and still with us (the fourth man in the fire); he sees the bigger picture; Jesus himself suffered, so he understands; because Jesus rose, death is not the end for those who trust him; we can tell God honestly how we feel. Follow-up: ‘What could you say to a friend who prayed and didn’t get the answer they wanted?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How does the “even if” prayer help here?’ If silent: share a time (age-appropriate) when you kept trusting God even though things didn’t go the way you hoped.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quietly brave. Stand up for God in one small, quiet way this week — like Daniel at his window. Pray before lunch, refuse to join in unkindness, tell the truth, or stand with someone who’s left out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The God we worship can save us… But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods.” — Daniel 3:17–18</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday will check in on this. Suggest writing their ‘quietly brave’ idea on the take-home card.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The God we worship can save us from you and your flaming furnace. But even if he doesn’t, we still won’t worship your gods and the gold statue you have set up.” — Daniel 3:17–18 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Daniel 3:17–18. Bonus: who can say last week’s verse, 1 Kings 18:39?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Not a scratch</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“My God… sent an angel to keep the lions from eating me.”<br>Daniel was not harmed, because he had trusted in his God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: My ‘even if’ prayer (written)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">An ‘even if’ prayer has two parts: ‘God, you CAN…’ and ‘But even if…’. Today write your own — then take it home and keep praying it. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write at the top: ‘God, you are able.’ We’ll add a line for each part.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing this week showed you that God is able to do.<br>Prompts: You are able to save from fire and lions. / You are the living God, and your kingdom will never end.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are able to…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are able — you are the living God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there a time you only trusted God when things went your way?<br>Prompts: Sorry for giving up on you when I didn’t get what I wanted. / Sorry for being afraid to stand up for you.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for trusting you only when it’s easy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank you for Jesus, who came out of the sealed tomb alive.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, that you rose again on the third morning. / Thank you that you were with Daniel in the dark — and you are with me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus — you are alive, and you are with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own ‘even if’ prayer about something real.<br>Prompts: Please, God, you can… But even if you don’t, I will trust you. / Please help me be quietly brave for you this week.<br>Starter: <b>God, you can… But even if…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you can do anything. But even if you don’t, we will still trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Daniel 3:17–18 together as our closing prayer. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students take their prayers home with the challenge card. Some may write about very personal things — don’t ask them to share.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: “For Such a Time as This” — a young Jewish woman becomes queen of Persia, and God works behind the scenes (Esther 2–8).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7: For Such a Time as This":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esther 2–8 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">At Work</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for your place/role — Mordecai told Esther that God might have put her in the palace for a reason (Esther 4:14). Praying for your place means asking, ‘God, why have you put me here?’ — in this class, this family, this team — and asking him to use you there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>An Orphan Becomes Queen</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Esther, a Jewish orphan, became queen of Persia.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>An Orphan Becomes Queen → Was it luck? → Esther 2:7, 17; 3:2 → Right place, right time → Who raised Esther? → Just luck? → The Queen and the Man Who Wouldn’t Kneel → Standing alone → Head · Heart · Hands → The only one standing → Memory verse peek → Silent prayer: where you have put me</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Message for the Queen</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Haman tricked the king into making a law against all the Jewish people.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Message for the Queen → Recap → Esther 4:1, 11, 14 → The ring, the law and the message → Spot the hidden hand → Why afraid? → For a time like this → Stay quiet or speak up? → Head · Heart · Hands → The message → Build the verse → A written ‘my places’ prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>If I Perish, I Perish</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Esther and her people fasted and prayed before she went to the king.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>If I Perish, I Perish → Brave or scared? → Esther 4:16; 5:2–3 → Into the throne room → Three days → The Queen Who Walked In → Esther and Jesus → Why wait? → Head · Heart · Hands → Three days of prayer → Verse with actions → Stand-up prayer (like Esther in the courtyard)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Tables Turn</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how a sleepless night and a forgotten book turned Esther’s story around.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Tables Turn → Who said it? → Esther 6:1, 3; 7:3 → A sleepless night → The sleepless night → Coincidence? → This Week’s Big Idea → My ‘time like this’ → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Sadness turned to joy → Pray for each other’s places (pairs)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: An Orphan Becomes Queen":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esther 2–8 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">An Orphan Becomes Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for your place/role — Mordecai told Esther that God might have put her in the palace for a reason (Esther 4:14). Praying for your place means asking, ‘God, why have you put me here?’ — in this class, this family, this team — and asking him to use you there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Esther, a Jewish orphan, became queen of Persia.</li><li>We are learning how Mordecai stood firm and would not kneel to Haman.</li><li>I can notice where God is quietly at work in a story that never mentions his name.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling how Esther became queen and why Mordecai refused to kneel, and for anyone spotting ‘coincidences’ that might be God at work.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a paper crown for Esther; a scroll (rolled paper) for the king’s record book</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: An Orphan Becomes Queen</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A new hero for the Faith Museum: Queen Esther. Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Was it luck?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been in exactly the right place at exactly the right time? What happened?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Was it just luck — or could someone have been planning it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, who wouldn’t bow to the golden statue, and Daniel, who kept praying at his window. Your challenge: stand up for God in one small, quiet way. Did you get a chance? What happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate small, quiet acts — saying grace at lunch, refusing to join in teasing, praying before a test. Link it: ‘This week someone else refuses to bow — just like the three friends.’ Then the hook: found money, met a friend unexpectedly, caught the bus just in time. Land it: ‘This week’s story is full of “right place, right time” moments. Watch for them!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Esther 2:7, 17; 3:2</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 2:7</b> Mordecai had a cousin named Esther… whose father and mother had died, and Mordecai had raised her as his own daughter.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 2:17</b> The king liked Esther more than he did any of the other young women… He placed a crown on her head and made her queen.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 3:2</b> The king had ordered his officials to… kneel down to Haman, and all of them obeyed except Mordecai.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged and lightly simplified from the CEV. Esther’s Hebrew name was Hadassah (2:7). Vashti, the queen before Esther (chapter 1), is not part of today’s story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Right place, right time</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Long ago, after God’s people had been carried away from their land, many Jewish families lived far from home in the huge Persian empire. It stretched from India all the way to Africa — one hundred and twenty-seven provinces! Its king was Xerxes, and he lived in a glittering palace in the city of Susa.</p><p>In Susa lived a Jewish man called Mordecai. He worked at the king’s gate, and he was raising his young cousin, because her mother and father had died. Mordecai loved her like his own daughter. Her name was Esther.</p><p>One day the king decided he needed a new queen. Messengers went out across the empire, and young women were brought to the palace — Esther was one of them. Before she went, Mordecai told her, ‘Don’t tell anyone you are Jewish.’ Every day he walked up and down outside the palace courtyard, just to find out how she was.</p><p>Everyone who met Esther liked her. And when she was brought to King Xerxes, he liked her more than any of the others. He placed a crown on her head and made her queen. A Jewish orphan girl — now queen of the greatest empire in the world! Was that just luck? Keep watching.</p><p>Not long after, Mordecai was sitting at the king’s gate when he overheard two guards whispering. They were planning to harm the king! Mordecai told Queen Esther, and Esther told the king that Mordecai had found it out. The plot was stopped, and the whole thing was written down in the king’s record book. Then everyone forgot about it… for now.</p><p>Some time later, the king gave a man named Haman the most important job in the empire. Haman was proud and loved being important. The king ordered all his officials to kneel down to Haman whenever he walked past.</p><p>So they did. Every official in the gateway dropped to his knees. Every one — except Mordecai. He stayed standing.</p><p>‘Why won’t you obey the king?’ the others asked him, day after day. Mordecai told them he was Jewish. When Haman found out, he was furious.</p><p>Here’s something strange about the book of Esther: it never once mentions God’s name. But watch closely. God is at work behind every scene.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Far from home, in Persia. · 2. An orphan named Esther. · 3. A crown for Esther! · 4. A secret plot — stopped! · 5. Everyone knelt to Haman…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Esther 2:5–3:6. Why Mordecai wouldn’t kneel isn’t fully explained; the text links it to his being Jewish (3:4), and many think he would not give Haman the kind of honour that belongs to God alone — say ‘probably’. The plotters’ punishment (2:23) is left out. Keep the ‘brought to the palace’ part simple: the king chose a new queen from many young women.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who raised Esther?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who raised Esther after her mother and father died?<br>A) King Xerxes &nbsp; B) Her cousin Mordecai ✓ &nbsp; C) Haman &nbsp; D) The palace guards</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Esther 2:7) — Mordecai raised her as his own daughter. Follow-up: ‘How do we know Mordecai still cared about her after she went to the palace?’ (He walked outside the courtyard every day to find out how she was.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Mordecai overhear at the king’s gate?’ (A plot to harm the king.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Just luck?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The book of Esther never says God’s name. So why do you think it is in the Bible — and where might God be quietly at work so far?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: an orphan becoming queen; Mordecai being at the gate just when the plot was whispered; the plot being written in the king’s book. These look like coincidences, but the Bible shows God working even when no one sees him. Follow-up: ‘Can someone be at work even when you can’t see them?’ (A cook in the kitchen, a parent planning a surprise.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why might God sometimes work quietly instead of with fire from heaven, like last week?’ If silent: ask, ‘Who do you think got Esther to the palace?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Queen and the Man Who Wouldn’t Kneel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Queen and the Man Who Wouldn’t Kneel</b> (Esther 2:5–3:6). The story follows Esther 2–3 closely. Most spoken words are imagined, but Mordecai’s warning to Esther (2:10), the plot (2:21–23) and the officials’ question (3:3) come from the text.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mordecai</b> — Esther’s wise older cousin — loyal and firm<br><b>Esther</b> — A young Jewish orphan who becomes queen<br><b>King Xerxes</b> — The powerful king of Persia<br><b>Haman</b> — The king’s proud top official<br><b>Official</b> — Another worker at the king’s gate<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd and the officials at the gate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the crowd): ‘Long live Queen Esther!’ · Everyone (as officials): kneel down whenever Haman walks past · Everyone: a shocked ‘Ooooh!’ when Mordecai stays standing<br>Props: Optional: a paper crown · Optional: a rolled-paper scroll for the king’s record book<br>Keep Haman a bit of a pantomime villain — proud and puffed up rather than scary. His last line hints at the plan without detail; tomorrow explains it simply.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The city of Susa, in the mighty Persian empire. Outside the palace gate.</li><li><b>Mordecai:</b> Esther, the king’s messengers have chosen you to go to the palace.</li><li><b>Esther:</b> The palace? Me? But I’m just an orphan girl. <i>(wide eyes)</i></li><li><b>Mordecai:</b> Listen carefully. Don’t tell anyone that you are Jewish. <i>(hand on her shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Esther:</b> I’ll do what you say, Mordecai. You’ve always looked after me.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Everyone who met Esther liked her. Then she was brought to the king.</li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Of all the young women in my empire, I choose you. You will be queen! <i>(place the crown)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Long live Queen Esther! <i>(cheer)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Later, Mordecai was sitting at the king’s gate. He heard two guards whispering…</li><li><b>Mordecai:</b> A plot to harm the king! I must tell Esther — quickly! <i>(hurry off)</i></li><li><b>Esther:</b> Your Majesty, my cousin Mordecai has found out about a plot against you.</li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Stop those guards! And write this in my record book. <i>(point to the scroll)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> It was written down… and then forgotten.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then the king gave Haman the most important job in the empire.</li><li><b>Haman:</b> Make way! The king’s top official is coming through! <i>(strut, chin up)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> Kneel, everyone! The king has commanded it!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Yes, Lord Haman! <i>(kneel down low)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Every official knelt down. Every one — except Mordecai.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh! <i>(gasp)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Everyone is kneeling. Mordecai is still standing. What might he be thinking? Would you stand? (20s)</li><li><b>Official:</b> Mordecai, why do you disobey the king’s command? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Mordecai:</b> I am Jewish. I will not kneel to Haman. <i>(calm and firm)</i></li><li><b>Haman:</b> WHAT? That man will not kneel to ME? <i>(red-faced, stamp a foot)</i></li><li><b>Haman:</b> A Jew, is he? Then I will deal with all of his people!</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Haman was furious. And he began to make a terrible plan.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Uh-oh…</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did Mordecai say he would not kneel to Haman?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He told the officials he was Jewish (Esther 3:4). Many think he would not give a proud man the honour that belongs to God alone — like the three friends and the golden statue last week.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mordecai saved the king’s life — but nobody thanked him. Why do you think the Bible bothers to tell us it was written in the king’s book?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: It’s a clue! That forgotten note will matter later in the story (we’ll see it on Thursday). God doesn’t forget, even when people do.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Standing alone</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mordecai was the only one standing while everyone else knelt. Was he being brave, stubborn — or faithful? Could his choice cause trouble for others?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them disagree! Draw out: it took courage; he probably would not give a proud man the honour that belongs to God; but it did make Haman angry with all the Jewish people. Doing right doesn’t always make life easier — and God was still in control. Follow-up: ‘How is Mordecai like Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego last week?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When might standing up for God make things harder for you at first?’ If silent: re-read Esther 3:2 and ask, ‘What did Mordecai risk?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is quietly at work — he put Esther in the palace and Mordecai at the gate.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me notice where you have put me — and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, look for one ‘right place, right time’ moment at school or at home — someone who needs help that you happen to be next to — and help them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask for one example they might notice today: someone alone at lunch, a dropped pencil case, a younger sibling who needs a hand.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The only one standing</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every official knelt down…<br>…except Mordecai.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Esther 4:14 — Mordecai’s famous words. ‘We’ll find out tomorrow why he said it!’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Silent prayer: where you have put me</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week’s prayer skill is praying for your place. God put Esther in the palace and Mordecai at the gate. Today, pray silently about the places God has put you. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit still. Picture the places you go every week — home, this classroom, the playground, your team. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is at work even when we can’t see him.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you looked after Esther when she had no mum or dad. / You were at work in the palace, even when no one said your name.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are always at work, even when we can’t see you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a place where you’ve forgotten God is with you?<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you don’t care about my ordinary days. / Sorry for kneeling to what everyone else wants instead of doing what’s right.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting that you are with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for people like Mordecai who look after you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who care for me, like Mordecai cared for Esther. / Thank you for putting me in this class and this family.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people and places you have given us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to use you in one of your places this week.<br>Prompts: Please show me why you have put me in this class. / Please help me stand firm like Mordecai when it’s hard.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, use us right where you have put us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish together. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be sensitive: some students may not live with their parents. Esther was raised by a cousin — families come in many shapes, and God looks after each one.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Haman makes a terrible plan — and Mordecai sends Esther a message that will change everything.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: A Message for the Queen":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esther 2–8 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Message for the Queen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for your place/role — Mordecai told Esther that God might have put her in the palace for a reason (Esther 4:14). Praying for your place means asking, ‘God, why have you put me here?’ — in this class, this family, this team — and asking him to use you there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Haman tricked the king into making a law against all the Jewish people.</li><li>We are learning what Mordecai’s message to Esther meant: ‘for a time like this’.</li><li>I can find clues that God was quietly at work, even before Esther knew it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining why Esther was afraid to go to the king, and what Mordecai meant when he said she might have been made queen ‘for a time like this’.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a toy ring or a stamp; a pair of dice</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Message for the Queen</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday an orphan became queen — and one man refused to kneel.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who became queen? And who wouldn’t kneel to Haman?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Esther became queen of Persia. Mordecai, her cousin, wouldn’t kneel to proud Haman. ‘Haman was furious. Today we find out what he did about it — and Esther gets a message that changes her life.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Esther 4:1, 11, 14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 4:1</b> Mordecai… tore his clothes in sorrow and put on sackcloth.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 4:11</b> “Anyone who goes in to see the king without being invited… The only way… is for the king to hold out the gold scepter.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 4:14</b> “It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses heavily abridged — lines kept short; check against a printed CEV if reading aloud word-for-word. ‘Scepter’ keeps the CEV spelling on screen. Sackcloth was rough, scratchy cloth worn to show great sadness.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The ring, the law and the message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Haman could not stop thinking about Mordecai. Every day Haman walked through the gate, and every day Mordecai stayed standing. But punishing one man wasn’t enough for Haman. He knew Mordecai was Jewish — so he decided to get rid of all the Jewish people in the whole empire.</p><p>First, Haman wanted to choose the right day. So he had people throw lots — a bit like rolling dice. The lots were called ‘pur’. Roll after roll, they finally landed on a day almost a whole year away. Haman probably thought, ‘Never mind. I can wait.’ But that long wait would turn out to be very important.</p><p>Then Haman went to the king. ‘Your Majesty, there is a group of people scattered all over your empire. They have their own laws, and they don’t obey yours. Make a law to get rid of them, and I will pay a huge amount of silver into your treasury.’</p><p>The king didn’t even ask who these people were. He pulled off his royal ring — the ring that sealed his laws — and handed it to Haman. ‘Keep your money. Do whatever you like with these people.’</p><p>So the law was written and stamped with the king’s ring. In Persia, once a law was sealed with the king’s ring, no one could cancel it. Messengers galloped out to every province. The king and Haman sat down to have a drink together — while the city of Susa was confused and upset.</p><p>When Mordecai heard, he tore his clothes and put on sackcloth — rough, scratchy cloth people wore to show deep sadness. He walked through the city crying loudly. All across the empire, Jewish families did the same. They wept, and they went without food.</p><p>Inside the palace, Queen Esther heard that Mordecai was upset, but she didn’t know why. She sent a servant called Hathach to find out. Mordecai gave him a copy of the law. ‘Show this to Esther,’ he said. ‘Tell her to go to the king and beg him to save her people.’</p><p>Esther sent back a worried reply. ‘Everyone knows the rule. Anyone who goes to the king without being invited could lose their life — unless the king holds out his golden sceptre. And the king hasn’t asked for me for thirty days.’</p><p>Mordecai’s answer came back, strong and clear. ‘Don’t think that living in the palace will keep you safe. If you stay silent, help will come from somewhere else. But who knows? It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!’</p><p>Esther read the words again. For a time like this. Maybe nothing had been an accident. Maybe she was in the palace for a reason.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Haman rolled the lots. · 2. The king handed over his ring. · 3. Mordecai wept at the gate. · 4. “I can’t just walk in!” · 5. “For a time like this!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Esther 3:7–4:14. Keep Haman’s plan at the level of ‘a law to get rid of all the Jewish people’ — no further detail. Haman offered 10,000 talents of silver (3:9) — a huge fortune. ‘Help will come from somewhere else’ (4:14) is the closest the book comes to naming God: Mordecai trusts that God will rescue his people, one way or another. If students ask about the Jewish people being targeted later in history, acknowledge it honestly and briefly, and land on God’s care for his people.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Spot the hidden hand</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look closer: God’s name isn’t in the story — but can you spot his fingerprints?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What happened</th><th>Clue: God at work?</th></tr><tr><td>A Jewish orphan girl became queen</td><td>She is in the palace just before her people need her</td></tr><tr><td>Mordecai overheard the plot against the king</td><td>It was written in the king’s book — saved for later</td></tr><tr><td>Haman’s lots landed almost a year away</td><td>Nearly a whole year to undo his plan!</td></tr><tr><td>“Help will come from somewhere else”</td><td>Mordecai trusted that God would not abandon his people</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is at work behind the scenes — even when nobody says his name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. The ‘lots’ clue: Proverbs 16:33 says people roll the dice, but every answer comes from the LORD. Optional: roll a real dice to show how random lots seemed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why afraid?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why was Esther afraid to go and see the king?<br>A) She didn’t like the king &nbsp; B) Going in uninvited was against the law and very dangerous ✓ &nbsp; C) The palace was too far away &nbsp; D) Haman was guarding the door</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Esther 4:11). Only if the king held out his golden sceptre would someone who came uninvited be safe — and he hadn’t asked for Esther in thirty days. Follow-up: ‘What did Mordecai want Esther to ask the king?’ (To save her people.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Mordecai and Esther send messages?’ (Through the servant Hathach.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: For a time like this</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mordecai said, “It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” What did he mean?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: maybe God put Esther in the palace on purpose; her crown wasn’t just for her own comfort — it was so she could help others; this was her moment to act. Follow-up: ‘Mordecai also said help would come from somewhere else. Who do you think he was trusting?’ (God.) Follow-up 2: ‘If God could rescue his people anyway, why would he want Esther to be part of it?’ If silent: ask, ‘Why do you think a Jewish orphan ended up as queen just before this law?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Stay quiet or speak up?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If you were Esther, would you stay quiet and safe in the palace, or speak up even though it was risky? Vote, then explain.<br>A) Stay quiet and safe &nbsp; B) Speak up, even if it’s risky &nbsp; C) Not sure</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect honest answers — staying safe is a very human choice! Draw out: Esther had a lot to lose; her people had more to lose; Mordecai reminded her she couldn’t hide forever; courage isn’t ‘not being scared’, it’s doing right while scared. Follow-up: ‘What would make it easier to speak up?’ (Knowing others are praying; trusting God.) Follow-up 2: ‘When is it hard for you to speak up for someone — on the playground or online?’ If silent: share a time you had to speak up when it was scary.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God puts people in their places for a reason — and he can use me where I am.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, why have you put me here? Show me how to help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one place you go every week — your class, your team, your family, your group chat. Name one person there you could help or speak up for this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This leads into today’s written prayer. Let students name the place quietly in their heads.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The message</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!”<br>Esther read the words again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Esther 4:14 chunk by chunk with the actions. ‘Today we heard who said it — Mordecai!’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A written ‘my places’ prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying for your place means praying about the real places God has put you. Writing them down helps you remember — you can pray the list again all week. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write at the top: ‘God, you put me here.’ Underneath, write three places: home, school, and one more.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about how God was at work in Esther’s story.<br>Prompts: You put Esther in the palace just in time. / You can turn even a roll of the dice into part of your plan.<br>Starter: <b>God, you…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are always at work — even when we can’t see it!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: have you stayed quiet when you could have helped someone?<br>Prompts: Sorry for staying quiet when someone needed me to speak up. / Sorry for thinking my places are just for me.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for staying silent when we should have helped.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Next to each place on your list, write one thank you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my family at home. / Thank you for my friends and teachers at school.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for every place you’ve put us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a ‘please’ for one person in one of your places.<br>Prompts: Please help me help (name) in my class this week. / Please make me brave like Esther when it’s my time to speak.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, use us for a time like this.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your paper and keep it. Pray your list again tomorrow. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students can reuse these lists on Thursday. Collect them if they might get lost.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Esther makes her decision — and walks into the throne room uninvited.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: If I Perish, I Perish":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esther 2–8 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">If I Perish, I Perish</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for your place/role — Mordecai told Esther that God might have put her in the palace for a reason (Esther 4:14). Praying for your place means asking, ‘God, why have you put me here?’ — in this class, this family, this team — and asking him to use you there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Esther and her people fasted and prayed before she went to the king.</li><li>We are learning how Esther showed courage by going to the king uninvited.</li><li>I can explain how courage means doing what is right even when you are scared.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the three days of fasting were a way of praying, that Esther went to the king knowing the danger, and that courage is trusting God while scared — not being fearless.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a cardboard tube or ruler for the golden sceptre; a paper crown</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: If I Perish, I Perish</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The big day! Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Brave or scared?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Can you be brave and scared at the same time?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s something you’ve done even though your knees were shaking?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few stories: first day at a new school, a speech, a big jump at the pools, saying sorry. Recap quickly: Haman’s law, Mordecai’s message, ‘for a time like this.’ Land it: ‘Esther was scared. Today we see what she did with that fear.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Esther 4:16; 5:2–3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 4:16</b> “Don’t eat or drink for three days and nights. My servant girls and I will do the same. Then I will go in to see the king.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 5:2</b> When the king saw her, he was pleased and held out the gold scepter to her. So Esther came up and touched the tip of it.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 5:3</b> The king said, “Esther, what brings you here? Just ask, and I will give you as much as half of my kingdom.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged. ‘If I perish, I perish’ (4:16) is the well-known wording from other versions; the CEV puts it more plainly. ‘Scepter’ keeps the CEV spelling on screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Into the throne room</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Esther thought about Mordecai’s message. Then she sent back her answer.</p><p>‘Go and gather all the Jewish people in Susa. Go without food for me — don’t eat or drink for three days and nights. My servant girls and I will do the same. Then I will go in to see the king, even though it’s against the law. And if I perish, I perish.’</p><p>That means, ‘Whatever happens to me, I will do what is right.’</p><p>Going without food like this is called fasting. People fasted when they wanted to pray very seriously — to show God, ‘This matters more than my dinner!’ The story still doesn’t say God’s name. But why else would a whole city go without food? They were praying.</p><p>For three days, all over Susa, people fasted and prayed. And in the palace, the queen got ready.</p><p>On the third day, Esther put on her royal robes. She walked to the inner courtyard, right outside the throne room. Her heart must have been pounding. Through the doorway she could see the king on his throne.</p><p>She stepped into view and stood there, waiting.</p><p>King Xerxes looked up. He saw Queen Esther standing in the courtyard… and he was pleased! He held out the gold sceptre towards her. Esther came forward and touched the tip of it. She was safe!</p><p>‘Esther, what brings you here?’ the king asked. ‘Just ask, and I will give you as much as half of my kingdom!’</p><p>Half the kingdom! But Esther didn’t blurt it all out. She said, ‘Your Majesty, please come today to a dinner I have prepared for you — and bring Haman too.’</p><p>So the king and Haman came to Esther’s dinner. Over the meal, the king asked again, ‘Now, what do you want? I’ll give you anything — up to half my kingdom!’</p><p>Esther took a breath. ‘Please come to another dinner tomorrow, you and Haman. Then I will tell you.’</p><p>Why wait? The Bible doesn’t say. Maybe Esther was choosing her moment carefully. But that one extra day would turn out to be exactly what was needed — because something was about to happen that night that nobody had planned… except God.</p><p>Haman went home feeling very pleased with himself. Dinner with the king and queen — twice! He had no idea what was coming.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Three days without food. · 2. “If I perish, I perish.” · 3. One step into the courtyard. · 4. The golden sceptre! · 5. “Come to my dinner.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Esther 4:15–5:8 (5:9–14, Haman’s boasting at home, is left out). ‘If I perish, I perish’ sounds dramatic — explain it as ‘whatever happens, I’ll do what’s right’ and keep the focus on courage and trust, not danger. Esther’s reasons for waiting are not given; the ‘maybe’ is deliberate.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Three days</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Before Esther went to the king, she asked all the Jewish people in Susa to… ”<br><i>Answer: “…go without food and drink for three days and nights — to fast and pray with her.” (Esther 4:16)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘What did the king do when he saw Esther in the courtyard?’ (He was pleased and held out the gold sceptre.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Esther ask for first — the big thing, or a dinner?’ (A dinner, with the king and Haman.) If silent: point to the first story beat and re-read it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Queen Who Walked In</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Queen Who Walked In</b> (Esther 4:15–5:8). Esther’s message, the king’s offer and Esther’s invitations follow Esther 4–5 (CEV, shortened). The servant girl’s and guard’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Esther</b> — The queen — scared, but brave<br><b>Mordecai</b> — Esther’s cousin, outside the palace<br><b>Servant Girl</b> — One of Esther’s loyal helpers<br><b>King Xerxes</b> — The powerful king on his throne<br><b>Guard</b> — Stands at the throne-room door<br><b>Haman</b> — Proud — and very pleased with himself<br><b>Everyone</b> — The Jewish people of Susa, and a nervous heartbeat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (as the people of Susa): bow heads and whisper ‘We will pray’ · Everyone: a heartbeat — tap your chest, getting faster · Everyone: a big sigh of relief when the sceptre comes out<br>Props: Optional: a cardboard tube or ruler as the golden sceptre · Optional: a paper crown<br>Make the heartbeat build tension, then let the ‘Phew!’ be big. Keep ‘if I perish, I perish’ calm and firm rather than frightening; explain it as ‘whatever happens, I’ll do what is right.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Esther has made her decision. She sends a message to Mordecai.</li><li><b>Esther:</b> Gather all the Jewish people in Susa. Don’t eat or drink for three days and nights.</li><li><b>Esther:</b> My servant girls and I will do the same. Then I will go in to see the king.</li><li><b>Esther:</b> Even though it’s against the law. And if I perish, I perish. <i>(quiet but firm)</i></li><li><b>Mordecai:</b> Everyone! The queen asks us to fast — and pray. <i>(call to the crowd)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> We will pray. <i>(bow heads, whisper)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Three days later. Esther puts on her royal robes.</li><li><b>Servant Girl:</b> My queen, your hands are shaking. <i>(fix the crown)</i></li><li><b>Esther:</b> I know. But this is the time. Walk with me to the courtyard.</li><li><b>Guard:</b> Halt! The king has not called for you, Your Majesty! <i>(step forward)</i></li><li><b>Esther:</b> I know. I will stand here and wait. <i>(take a deep breath)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ba-dum. Ba-dum. Ba-dum. <i>(tap chests — a nervous heartbeat)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Esther is standing in the courtyard. The king hasn’t seen her yet. What do you think she is praying right now? (20s)</li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Is that… Queen Esther? <i>(look up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The king was pleased. He held out his golden sceptre.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Phew! <i>(big sigh of relief)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Esther came forward and touched the tip of it. <i>(reach out slowly)</i></li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Esther, what brings you here? Just ask — I’ll give you up to half my kingdom!</li><li><b>Esther:</b> Your Majesty, please come to a dinner I have prepared. And bring Haman too.</li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Guard! Fetch Haman at once!</li><li><b>Haman:</b> Dinner with the king AND the queen? How wonderful — for me! <i>(puff out chest)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At the dinner, the king asked again.</li><li><b>King Xerxes:</b> Now, Esther, what do you want? Anything — up to half my kingdom!</li><li><b>Esther:</b> Come to another dinner tomorrow, both of you. Then I will tell you. <i>(glance at Haman)</i></li><li><b>Haman:</b> Another dinner! I am the most important man in the empire! <i>(strut off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Haman had no idea what was coming. But God was already at work.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esther asked everyone to fast and pray before she went to the king. What does that show about where she found her courage?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She didn’t trust her own beauty or her crown — she depended on God, and on her people praying. Courage often comes from knowing God is with us and others are praying for us.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the king do when he saw Esther — and what did she ask for?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was pleased and held out his golden sceptre; she touched the tip. She asked the king and Haman to come to a dinner — then another one the next day (Esther 5:2–8).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Esther and Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esther risked her life to go to the king and save her people. Who else in the Bible gave himself to save his people — and how was he even greater than Esther?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Point to Jesus. Esther was willing to risk her life; Jesus actually gave his life on the cross to save us, and then rose again. Esther went to an earthly king for her people; Jesus now speaks to God the Father for us (Hebrews 7:25). Follow-up: ‘Esther was nervous to approach the king. How can WE come to God, because of Jesus?’ (Boldly and welcome — Hebrews 4:16.) Follow-up 2: ‘How is that like the golden sceptre held out?’ If silent: ask, ‘Who do we talk about at Easter?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why wait?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The king offered Esther half his kingdom — but she waited and invited him to dinner, twice. Why do you think she didn’t ask straight away?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she was choosing her moment wisely; she wanted the king relaxed and happy; she wanted Haman there when she spoke; she may still have been nervous; God was giving time for something else to happen. Follow-up: ‘Is it ever wise to wait before saying something important?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can you tell the difference between waiting wisely and being too scared to act?’ If silent: ask, ‘Would you ask your parents for something big while they’re grumpy or while they’re happy?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gives courage to people who trust him — even when they are scared.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I feel scared sometimes. Help me be brave like Esther, and trust you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Before one scary thing this week — speaking up for someone, telling the truth, joining a new group — pray first, ask a friend to pray for you, then do it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it: ‘Before my meeting today I’ll pray first.’ Encourage pairs to swap one scary thing to pray about.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Three days of prayer</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Then I will go in to see the king.”<br>All over Susa, God’s people were praying.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Esther 4:14. Ask: ‘What was Esther’s “time like this”?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Stand-up prayer (like Esther in the courtyard)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esther stood in the courtyard and waited for the king. We never have to wait outside — God always welcomes us. Today, stand up as you pray, as a way of saying, ‘Here I am, God, ready to be used.’ <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone stand up, feet steady, hands by your sides. Esther stood and waited. We can stand and pray — and God is already pleased to hear us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is the King of all kings — and he always welcomes us.<br>Prompts: Wow, God, you are greater than King Xerxes. / You never make us wait outside — you always hold out your welcome.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are the King of kings, and you welcome us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there a time you chose to stay safe instead of doing what was right?<br>Prompts: Sorry for staying quiet because I was scared. / Sorry for only thinking about keeping myself safe.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times fear stopped us doing right.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for Jesus, who did even more than Esther.<br>Prompts: Thank you that Jesus gave his life to save us. / Thank you for people who pray for me, like Susa prayed for Esther.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for giving yourself to save us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God for courage in the place he has put you.<br>Prompts: Please make me brave for the scary thing I have to do this week. / Please help me use my place to help someone else.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, make us brave for a time like this.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand tall. Everyone: Here I am, God. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students who can’t stand can sit tall instead. Don’t force anyone to pray aloud.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a king who can’t sleep, a forgotten book — and the tables turn.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Tables Turn":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Esther 2–8 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Tables Turn</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for your place/role — Mordecai told Esther that God might have put her in the palace for a reason (Esther 4:14). Praying for your place means asking, ‘God, why have you put me here?’ — in this class, this family, this team — and asking him to use you there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a sleepless night and a forgotten book turned Esther’s story around.</li><li>We are learning that God was at work behind the scenes, even though his name is never mentioned.</li><li>I can recite Esther 4:14 and explain how God can use me where he has put me.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say who said key lines from the story, explain at least two ‘hidden hand’ moments (the sleepless night, the record book), and recite Esther 4:14.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Tuesday’s ‘my places’ prayer lists (if collected)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Tables Turn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t tell anyone that you are Jewish.” — <b>Mordecai</b><br>“Why do you disobey the king’s command?” — <b>The king’s officials</b><br>“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — <b>Mordecai</b><br>“Then I will go in to see the king.” — <b>Esther</b><br>“Just ask, and I will give you as much as half of my kingdom.” — <b>King Xerxes</b><br>“Who would the king want to reward more than me?” — <b>Haman (thinking to himself)</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘When did they say it — and what happened next?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Esther 6:1, 3; 7:3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 6:1</b> That night the king could not sleep, and he had a servant read him the records of what had happened since he had been king.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 6:3</b> The king asked, “What has been done to reward Mordecai for this?” “Nothing, Your Majesty!” his servants answered.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Esther 7:3</b> Esther answered: “Your Majesty, if you really care for me and are willing to help, you can save me and my people.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses abridged; wording kept short — check against a printed CEV if reading word-for-word. Notice: a king who can’t sleep, the exact page about Mordecai — just in time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A sleepless night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>That very night, the king could not sleep. Of all the nights! So he asked a servant to read to him from the royal record book. And of all the pages, the servant read the one about Mordecai — the man who had saved the king’s life from the plot.</p><p>‘What has been done to reward Mordecai?’ the king asked. ‘Nothing, Your Majesty!’</p><p>Just then, Haman arrived early to ask the king for something nasty against Mordecai. But the king spoke first: ‘Haman, what should I do for a man I really want to reward?’</p><p>Haman thought, ‘Who would the king want to reward more than me?’ So he said, ‘Dress him in the king’s robe, put him on the king’s horse, and lead him through the city shouting, “This is how the king rewards someone!”’</p><p>‘Perfect!’ said the king. ‘Go and do all that — for Mordecai.’ And Haman had to lead Mordecai through the streets!</p><p>At Esther’s second dinner, the king asked again what she wanted. Esther spoke up bravely: ‘Please save me and my people! Someone has made a law to get rid of us all.’ ‘Who would dare?’ asked the king. Esther pointed. ‘Haman!’</p><p>Haman’s plan was turned back on him, and he was punished. The king gave his ring to Mordecai, and a new law was sent out so that the Jewish people would be safe. Sadness turned into joy, and they celebrated — and Jewish people still celebrate this every year at a festival called Purim.</p><p>God’s name is never written in the book of Esther. But a sleepless night, the right page, perfect timing? God was at work behind every scene.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The king could not sleep. · 2. “What was done for Mordecai?” · 3. Haman led Mordecai through the city! · 4. Esther spoke up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Esther 6–8 (and 9:20–28 for Purim). Keep Haman’s fate exactly at ‘his plan was turned back on him and he was punished’ — no further detail. Chapters 8–9 also describe the Jewish people defending themselves; this is not told here. If asked, say simply: ‘The king gave them a new law so they could protect themselves, and God kept his people safe.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The sleepless night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When the king couldn’t sleep, what did he find out from his record book?<br>A) That Haman was a hero &nbsp; B) That Mordecai had saved his life and was never rewarded ✓ &nbsp; C) That Esther was Jewish &nbsp; D) That it was his birthday</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Esther 6:1–3). Follow-up: ‘Who had to lead Mordecai through the city on the king’s horse?’ (Haman — the man who wanted to get rid of him!) Follow-up 2: ‘When did Esther tell the king about Haman’s plan?’ (At her second dinner — the extra day made all the difference.) If silent: re-read the first two scripture lines.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Coincidence?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A king who can’t sleep. The exact page about Mordecai. Haman arriving at just that moment. Were these coincidences — or was God at work? What does this show us about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God was quietly arranging things; he is in charge even of small things like sleep and timing; he had not forgotten his people even when no one said his name; he turns bad plans around. Follow-up: ‘Why do you think God didn’t just send fire from heaven, like he did for Elijah?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where might God be quietly at work in your life right now?’ If silent: list the ‘coincidences’ on the board and ask, ‘Could ALL of these be luck?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God put an orphan girl in the palace and Mordecai at the gate — right place, right time.</li><li>Esther fasted, prayed and bravely went to the king ‘for a time like this’.</li><li>God’s name is never written in Esther, yet he was at work behind every scene — and he turned Haman’s plan around.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is at work behind the scenes, and he places us where we are for a reason.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Link to Jesus: the cross looked like the worst day ever, but God turned it into the best news ever (Genesis 50:20 says something similar about Joseph).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: My ‘time like this’</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Esther was made queen ‘for a time like this.’ Where has God put you — and what might your ‘time like this’ be this week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Write it on a mini-whiteboard. Ideas: being the friend of someone who is new; being the big brother or sister at home; being in a team where someone is left out; knowing a friend who is sad. Follow-up: ‘What small thing could you do there tomorrow?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Do you have to be a queen or a leader for God to use you?’ (No — Mordecai was just a man at the gate.) If silent: share one place God has put you and how you hope to help there.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">For such a time as this. Each morning, ask, “God, why have you put me here today?” — and do one helpful thing for someone where you are.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday will check in on this. Suggest writing the morning question on the take-home card and sticking it by the bed or on the fridge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It could be that you were made queen for a time like this!” — Esther 4:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Esther 4:14. Bonus: who can say last week’s verse, Daniel 3:17–18?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Sadness turned to joy</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Their sadness turned into joy!<br>God was at work behind every scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Pray for each other’s places (pairs)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying for your place can be done for each other! Mordecai and the people of Susa prayed for Esther. Today, pray for your partner and the place God has put them. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Each tell the other the place you wrote about — your ‘time like this’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns to tell God one thing this week showed you about him.<br>Prompts: God, you are always at work, even when we can’t see you. / You turned Haman’s plan around and kept your people safe.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are at work — behind every scene!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: have you thought God has forgotten you?<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you weren’t there when I couldn’t see you. / Sorry for not using my place to help others.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting that you are always at work.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God together for the ‘right place, right time’ moments in your life.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you kept Esther and her people safe. / Thank you for a time you put me in the right place at the right time.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you never forget your people!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your PARTNER’s place — by name.<br>Prompts: Please use (name) in their class and at home this week. / Please make (name) brave like Esther when it’s their time.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help (name)…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, use each one of us for a time like this.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Esther 4:14 together — then: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Encourage partners to ask each other on Monday how the challenge went.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: “Faith That Brings Friends to Jesus” — four friends, a hole in the roof, and a soldier who trusted Jesus’ word (Mark 2; Luke 7).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8: Faith That Brings Friends to Jesus":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 2:1–12; Luke 7:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Compassionate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends by name — The four friends carried their friend to Jesus. We can’t carry our friends on a mat — but we can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name. Say their name, then say what they need.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Through the Roof</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how four friends brought a man who couldn’t walk to Jesus — even through a roof.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Through the Roof → Good friends → Mark 2:1–5 → Four friends and a roof → How did they get in? → Faith you can see → Through the Roof → Was it right? → Head · Heart · Hands → The crowded door → Memory verse peek → Carrying a friend to Jesus (hands)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Forgiven First</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why Jesus forgave the man’s sins before he healed him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Forgiven First → Recap → Mark 2:6–12 → Which is easier? → Which is easier to say? → What were they thinking? → Forgiven first → Who is Jesus? → Head · Heart · Hands → Forgiven → Build the verse → Quiet prayer for a friend</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Just Say the Word</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how a Roman army officer asked Jesus to heal his servant.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Just Say the Word → Who’s in charge? → Luke 7:2–9 → The officer’s message → Just say… → Just Say the Word → A surprising hero → Faith for someone else → Head · Heart · Hands → The message → Verse with actions → One sentence out loud</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Bring Them to Jesus</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning that Jesus has authority — the right and power to forgive and to heal.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Bring Them to Jesus → Who said it? → Mark 2:10–11 · Luke 7:7–8 → The One in charge → Authority → Two kinds of friends → This week’s Big Idea → Deserves it? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Well → Write a friend’s name</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Through the Roof":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 2:1–12; Luke 7:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Through the Roof</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends by name — The four friends carried their friend to Jesus. We can’t carry our friends on a mat — but we can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name. Say their name, then say what they need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how four friends brought a man who couldn’t walk to Jesus — even through a roof.</li><li>We are learning that faith in Jesus shows up in love for other people.</li><li>I can retell the story of the four friends and say the first thing Jesus said to the man on the mat.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the story in order (crowded house, roof, lowering, Jesus’ words) and noticing that Jesus ‘saw how much faith they had’ — the friends’ faith showed up in what they did for their friend.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a blanket or towel for the ‘mat’ (no-one is actually carried in the role-play)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Through the Roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This week we move from the Old Testament heroes into the Gospels — the books about Jesus’ life. Our heroes this week are four friends nobody remembers the names of, and a Roman soldier.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Good friends</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the hardest thing a friend has ever done to help you — or you’ve done to help a friend?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How far would you go for a friend who really needed help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Queen Esther, who found out God had put her in the palace ‘for such a time as this’. Your challenge: each morning ask, “God, why have you put me here today?” and do one helpful thing for someone where you are. Did you ask it? What helpful thing did you do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: take 2–3 stories of helpful things done last week (Esther-style ‘where God put me’ — classroom, home, sport). Then the hook: carrying a bag, sticking up for someone, staying with a friend who was hurt. Land it: ‘Today we meet four friends who went to crazy lengths for their friend — they wrecked someone’s roof to get him to Jesus!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Mark 2:1–5</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:1</b> Jesus went back to Capernaum, and a few days later people heard that he was at home.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:2</b> Then so many of them came to the house that there wasn’t even standing room left in front of the door.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:3–4</b> Four people came up, carrying a crippled man on a mat. But because of the crowd, they could not get him to Jesus.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:4</b> So they made a hole in the roof above him and let the man down in front of everyone.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:5</b> When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Capernaum = a fishing town on the Lake of Galilee where Jesus lived for a while. The CEV says ‘crippled’ — explain it is an old-fashioned word that can hurt, so in our own talk we say ‘a man who couldn’t walk’ or ‘paralysed’ (his body couldn’t move).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Four friends and a roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Capernaum was a busy fishing town beside the Lake of Galilee. Jesus lived there for a while, and news travelled fast. A few days after he came back, the word went round: ‘Jesus is home!’</p><p>People came from everywhere. They squeezed into the house. They filled the doorway. They packed the street until there wasn’t even standing room left in front of the door. Inside, Jesus was teaching them God’s message.</p><p>Somewhere in that town lived a man who couldn’t walk. His body was paralysed — it wouldn’t move the way he wanted it to. He couldn’t get himself to Jesus. But he had something very precious: four friends.</p><p>We don’t know their names. But we know what they did. They said, ‘Jesus is in town. We’re taking you to him.’ Each friend took a corner of the mat, and together they carried him through the streets.</p><p>Then they reached the house — and stopped. A wall of people. No way in. Most people would have given up and gone home. Not these four.</p><p>‘The roof!’</p><p>Houses in Capernaum had flat roofs, with stairs up the outside. The roofs were wooden beams covered with branches and packed-down mud. So up they went, carefully carrying their friend. Then they started to dig. Scraping. Pulling. Chunks of dried mud dropped into the room below — onto people’s heads! Imagine Jesus, in the middle of teaching, looking up as sunlight pours through a growing hole in the ceiling.</p><p>When the hole was big enough, the friends tied ropes to the mat. Slowly, gently, they lowered their friend down… down… down… until he lay right in front of Jesus.</p><p>The room went silent. Everyone stared. What would Jesus do?</p><p>Jesus looked up at four dusty faces peering through the roof. Mark says Jesus saw how much faith they had. Their faith wasn’t just words. It had sore arms and dirty hands. It climbed stairs and dug through a roof. Their faith in Jesus showed up as love for their friend.</p><p>Then Jesus looked at the man on the mat. And the very first thing he said was this: ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’</p><p>My friend. Jesus didn’t see a problem, or a nuisance who had wrecked a roof. He saw a person he loved.</p><p>And everyone in that room was about to get a big surprise.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Jesus is home!” · 2. Four friends. One mat. No way in. · 3. “The roof!” · 4. Down… down… down… · 5. “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mark says ‘four people’ carried him; that they were his friends is the natural reading and fits the story. The roof details (beams, branches, packed mud, outside stairs) match ordinary houses of the time; the falling dust is imagined. DISABILITY — please read: present the man with dignity. He is not a ‘problem’ in this story; he is the person Jesus calls ‘my friend’. Notice that Jesus’ first words are about forgiveness and friendship with God, not about his legs. Some students use wheelchairs or have disabled family members — avoid language like ‘broken’ or ‘fixed’, and never suggest that a person who can’t walk is worth less, or that their disability is caused by their own sin (Jesus says the opposite in John 9:3).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How did they get in?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The house was too crowded to get in. What did the four friends do?<br>A) Waited until everyone went home &nbsp; B) Shouted until Jesus came outside &nbsp; C) Made a hole in the roof and lowered him down ✓ &nbsp; D) Climbed in through a window</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (Mark 2:4). Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus see when he looked up?’ (How much faith they had.) Follow-up 2: ‘How many friends carried the mat — and why does it matter that there were four?’ (One for each corner; he needed all of them; love is often a team effort.) If silent: re-read the fourth scripture line together and ask them to find the action word — ‘made a hole’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Faith you can see</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mark says Jesus “saw how much faith they had.” Faith is inside you — so how could Jesus SEE it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: their faith showed in what they did — carrying, climbing, digging, not giving up; they believed Jesus could help, so they acted; faith that loves other people is visible. Follow-up: ‘What would the friends have done if they didn’t really believe Jesus could help?’ (Given up at the door.) Follow-up 2: ‘What might faith look like that others could see in our class?’ If silent: ask ‘If I said I believed it was going to rain, what would you see me do?’ (Take an umbrella — belief shows up in action.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Through the Roof</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Through the Roof</b> (Mark 2:1–5). Jesus’ words are from Mark 2:5 (CEV). The friends’ names, the name Tobias for the man on the mat, and all their chat are imagined — the Bible doesn’t name any of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Tobias</b> — The man on the mat — can’t walk, quick sense of humour, loves his friends<br><b>Asher</b> — Friend — the one with the big ideas<br><b>Jonah</b> — Friend — strong, worries about the rules<br><b>Simeon</b> — Friend — never gives up<br><b>Jesus</b> — Teaching in the crowded house — kind and calm (the teacher may read Jesus’ lines)<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd packed into the house</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the packed crowd — squash together, ‘Oi! No room!’ · Everyone: be the dust falling — look up and brush off your heads · Everyone: fall completely silent when Jesus speaks<br>Props: Optional: a blanket or towel held at four corners as the ‘mat’ — the actor playing Tobias walks alongside it, nobody is lifted<br>Do NOT physically carry or lift any student. Tobias walks alongside the ‘mat’ (a towel held by the friends) or simply stands centre. Choose Tobias carefully: if you have a student with a physical disability, don’t cast them as Tobias unless they’ve asked to be, and let any student say no. Play Tobias as a full person with a sense of humour, not a helpless victim — he is the one Jesus calls ‘my friend’. End on the whispered crowd line; the argument comes tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Capernaum. Word has gone round that Jesus is home. Four friends arrive at Tobias’ door.</li><li><b>Asher:</b> Tobias! Jesus is back in town. We’re taking you to him. Right now.</li><li><b>Tobias:</b> Now? Just like that? I haven’t even had breakfast! <i>(grin)</i></li><li><b>Simeon:</b> Grab a corner, everyone. One, two, three — lift! <i>(four friends take the corners of the mat)</i></li><li><b>Jonah:</b> He’s heavier than he looks. <i>(pretend to puff)</i></li><li><b>Tobias:</b> I heard that, Jonah. <i>(laugh)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But when they reached the house, there wasn’t even standing room left in front of the door.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> No room! Full up! Can’t get in! <i>(squash together, arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Jonah:</b> It’s no good. We’ll come back another day.</li><li><b>Simeon:</b> No. We’re not giving up on Tobias.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The door is blocked. If you were one of the friends, what would you do now? (20s)</li><li><b>Asher:</b> The roof! The stairs go up the outside. We’ll go in from the top!</li><li><b>Jonah:</b> We can’t dig through someone’s roof! That’s not allowed… is it?</li><li><b>Tobias:</b> Well, I’m not going anywhere without you lot. <i>(shrug)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So they carried him up and started to dig. Branches. Mud. Dust.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hey! What’s falling on my head? <i>(look up, brush off your hair)</i></li><li><b>Simeon:</b> Nearly there… make it wider… now the ropes!</li><li><b>Asher:</b> Slowly. Gently. Hold on, Tobias. <i>(lower the mat hand over hand)</i></li><li><b>Tobias:</b> Don’t you dare drop me! <i>(half laughing, half nervous)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They lowered him down, right in front of Jesus. The room went silent.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ohhhh… <i>(stare in amazement, then silence)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus looked up at the four dusty faces. He saw how much faith they had.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> My friend, your sins are forgiven. <i>(kneel down beside Tobias, gently)</i></li><li><b>Tobias:</b> He called me his friend. <i>(whisper, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Asher:</b> Wait. Did he say ‘forgiven’? <i>(peer down through the hole)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Can he say that? <i>(whisper to each other)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends could have given up at the door. Why do you think they kept going?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They loved Tobias; they believed Jesus could help him; they were a team; they didn’t care what people thought. Their faith showed up as love.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the first words Jesus said to the man on the mat?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’ (Mark 2:5) — not ‘get up’, but forgiveness and friendship first. Tomorrow we find out why.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Was it right?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends dug a hole in someone else’s roof! Was that the right thing to do, or did they go too far?<br>A) Right thing to do &nbsp; B) Went too far</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students disagree. Went too far: it wasn’t their roof; they interrupted Jesus; people got dust on their heads. Right thing: their friend’s need was bigger than a roof; roofs like that could be mended; Jesus didn’t tell them off — he praised their faith. Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus think of what they did?’ (He saw their faith.) Follow-up 2: ‘Is there a difference between breaking rules to be naughty and going out of your way for someone in need?’ If silent: ‘Would you rather have friends who give up at the door, or friends who find a way?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for other people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, give me friends-through-the-roof kind of love.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one friend or whānau member who needs help this week. Do one thing to ‘carry’ them — help with a job at home, include them at lunch, sit with them if they’re sad — and pray for them by name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask students to think of one name silently. Tomorrow’s and Thursday’s prayers will come back to it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The crowded door</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">No room — not even at the door.<br>“We’re not giving up on our friend!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Mark 2:5. Point out the two halves: what Jesus SAW (their faith) and what Jesus SAID (‘My friend, your sins are forgiven’). Mention that ‘crippled’ is the Bible’s old word; we say it only when reading the verse.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Carrying a friend to Jesus (hands)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we learn to pray for friends by name. Today, hold out your hands as if you’re carrying one corner of a mat — and ‘carry’ a friend to Jesus in prayer. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably. Hold your hands out in front of you, palms up, as if you’re holding a corner of a mat. Think of one friend or whānau member who needs Jesus’ help. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus called the man on the mat ‘my friend’.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you notice people everyone else walks past. / Jesus, you can forgive sins — wow!<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you call us your friends!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The four friends didn’t give up. Have you ever given up on someone?<br>Prompts: Sorry for walking past a friend who needed help. / Sorry for giving up when helping got hard.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we gave up on someone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a friend who has ‘carried’ you when you needed it.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my friend (name), who helped me when… / Thank you that you saw the friends’ faith and welcomed them.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for the friends who carry us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now ‘carry’ your one friend to Jesus. Say their name in your head, then what they need.<br>Prompts: Jesus, I bring you (name). Please help them with… / Please show me one way to help (name) this week.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I bring you (name)…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we bring you our friends. Please help each one by name.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Gently lift your hands a little, as if lowering your friend down to Jesus. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep friends’ names and needs silent and private today — no sharing of personal details. Some students may want to pray for someone who is ill or disabled; that’s lovely, and it’s also fine to pray for them to know Jesus’ love and to have good friends, not only for healing.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus said ‘your sins are forgiven’ first — and the argument that followed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Forgiven First":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 2:1–12; Luke 7:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgiven First</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends by name — The four friends carried their friend to Jesus. We can’t carry our friends on a mat — but we can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name. Say their name, then say what they need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Jesus forgave the man’s sins before he healed him.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus has the right to forgive sins — something only God can do.</li><li>I can explain what ‘forgiven’ means and why it was the man’s biggest need.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that only God can forgive sins, that the healing showed Jesus had that right, and that everyone’s biggest need is to be forgiven and made friends with God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a rolled-up towel or yoga mat for the ‘pick up your mat’ moment</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Forgiven First</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday four friends lowered their friend through a roof to Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the first words Jesus said to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’ Then: ‘Some people in that room were very unhappy about those words. Today we find out why — and what Jesus did next.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Mark 2:6–12</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:6–7</b> Some of the teachers of the Law of Moses were sitting there. They started wondering, “Why would he say such a thing? He must think he is God! Only God can forgive sins.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:9</b> “Is it easier for me to tell this crippled man that his sins are forgiven or to tell him to get up and pick up his mat and go on home?</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:10</b> I will show you that the Son of Man has the right to forgive sins here on earth.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:10–11</b> So Jesus said to the man, “Get up! Pick up your mat and go on home.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:12</b> The man got right up. He picked up his mat and went out while everyone watched in amazement. They praised God and said, “We have never seen anything like this!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Teachers of the Law of Moses = experts in God’s laws in the Old Testament. Son of Man = the name Jesus used most often for himself; it comes from Daniel 7, where ‘one like a son of man’ is given power and a kingdom by God.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Which is easier?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s step back into that crowded room. Dust is still drifting down through the hole in the roof. The man is lying on his mat in front of Jesus. Four dusty faces are peering down from above. And Jesus has just said, ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’</p><p>Maybe the friends were puzzled. ‘Forgiven? We brought him here so he could walk!’ But Jesus had seen something deeper. Everyone has a problem bigger than legs that don’t work. It’s called sin — doing, saying and thinking things our own way instead of God’s way. Sin breaks our friendship with God. To be forgiven means God wipes the wrong away and welcomes us back as his friends. That was what this man needed most. And it’s what every person in that room needed too.</p><p>Now, sitting along the wall were some teachers of the Law of Moses — experts in God’s laws. They didn’t say anything out loud. But they were thinking, ‘Why would he say such a thing? He must think he is God! Only God can forgive sins.’</p><p>And here’s the thing. They were right — only God can forgive sins. If you break my window, you can say sorry to me and I can forgive you. But this man had never met Jesus before. How could Jesus forgive him? Only if every sin is first of all against God… and Jesus is God.</p><p>Right away, Jesus knew what they were thinking. ‘Why are you thinking such things?’ he asked. ‘Is it easier for me to tell this man that his sins are forgiven, or to tell him to get up and pick up his mat and go on home?’</p><p>Think about it. Anyone can SAY ‘your sins are forgiven’. Nobody can see whether it happened. But if you say ‘Get up and walk,’ everyone will see straight away whether you have the power.</p><p>So Jesus said, ‘I will show you that the Son of Man has the right to forgive sins here on earth.’ He turned to the man and said, ‘Get up! Pick up your mat and go on home.’</p><p>And the man got right up. He rolled up his mat, put it under his arm and walked out through the crowd, while everyone watched in amazement. Up on the roof, four friends must have been cheering!</p><p>The whole crowd praised God and said, ‘We have never seen anything like this!’</p><p>The healing everyone could see proved the forgiving nobody could see. Jesus really can forgive sins. And one day he would go to the cross, so that anyone who comes to him can hear the same words: ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “My friend, your sins are forgiven.” · 2. “Only God can forgive sins!” · 3. “Which is easier?” · 4. “Get up! Pick up your mat and go on home.” · 5. “We have never seen anything like this!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. IMPORTANT: Jesus forgiving first does NOT mean the man’s paralysis was caused by his sin — the Bible says plainly that illness and disability are not a punishment for a person’s own sin (John 9:1–3). Make sure no student leaves thinking that. Also be careful with ‘the man got up’: the point is Jesus’ authority and kindness, not that people who can’t walk need ‘fixing’ to be valuable. He was Jesus’ ‘friend’ before he stood up. Jesus’ healings were signs of God’s kingdom, a sneak preview of the day God makes all things new (Revelation 21:4). The friends cheering is imagined.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Which is easier to say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus asked, “Which is easier to say?” Compare his two sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>“Your sins are forgiven”</th><th>“Get up and go on home”</th></tr><tr><td>Nobody can see if it worked</td><td>Everyone can see if it worked</td></tr><tr><td>Only God can really do it</td><td>Only God’s power can really do it</td></tr><tr><td>Mends his friendship with God</td><td>Sends him home, walking, with his mat</td></tr><tr><td>Said first — the biggest need</td><td>Said second — the proof</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus did what everyone could see to prove what nobody could see: he has the right to forgive sins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. After the last row ask: ‘So if Jesus can do the right-hand side, what does that tell us about the left-hand side?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What were they thinking?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were the teachers of the Law thinking when Jesus said, “Your sins are forgiven”?<br>A) “What a kind thing to say!” &nbsp; B) “Only God can forgive sins!” ✓ &nbsp; C) “Who’s going to fix that roof?” &nbsp; D) “We should go home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Mark 2:7). Follow-up: ‘How did Jesus know what they were thinking?’ (Right away he knew — he knows our hearts.) Follow-up 2: ‘Were they right that only God can forgive sins?’ (Yes! Their mistake was not seeing who Jesus is.) If silent: point to the first scripture line and read it together slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Forgiven first</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The friends brought the man to Jesus so he could walk. Why do you think Jesus forgave his sins FIRST?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: being forgiven is everyone’s biggest need; Jesus cared about the whole person, not just his body; he wanted to show he is God; he was already ‘my friend’ before anything else happened; forgiveness lasts forever. Make clear it doesn’t mean his sins caused his disability (John 9:3). Follow-up: ‘Who else in that room needed forgiving?’ (Everyone — including the teachers of the Law.) Follow-up 2: ‘What would have been sad if he’d been healed but not forgiven?’ If silent: ask ‘What’s worse — a broken arm or a broken friendship?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is Jesus?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The teachers said, “He must think he is God!” If Jesus was just a good teacher, they’d be right to be upset. So who is Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them wrestle. Good answers: Jesus isn’t just a good man — he forgives sins the way only God can, and the healing proves it; he knew their thoughts; the crowd praised God. A good man who said ‘I can forgive your sins’ would be lying or confused — unless he really is God. Follow-up: ‘What did the crowd do at the end?’ (Praised God.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think some people still find Jesus’ claims hard to accept?’ If silent: ‘If I walked up to someone in the playground and said “your sins are forgiven”, what would people think?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus has the right to forgive sins — and he calls forgiven people ‘my friend’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you that you forgive me and call me your friend.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, tell Jesus one thing you’re sorry for and hear his words: ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’ Then forgive one person who has hurt you — at home, at school or online.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give 20 seconds of silent thinking. Forgiving someone doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt; if someone is unsafe, tell a trusted adult.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Forgiven</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“My friend, your sins are forgiven.”<br>He was Jesus’ friend before he ever stood up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Mark 2:5. Ask: ‘Whose faith did Jesus see?’ (THEIR faith — the friends’.) ‘That’s why our prayers for friends matter.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Quiet prayer for a friend</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus saw the friends’ faith and blessed their friend. When you pray for a friend by name, you’re doing what those four friends did — bringing someone to Jesus. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your eyes or look at the floor. Picture that sunny hole in the roof — and Jesus kneeling by the mat. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only God can forgive sins — and Jesus did.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you have the right to forgive sins. / Jesus, you know what everyone is thinking.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — only God can forgive sins, and you do!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently tell Jesus one thing you’re sorry for. Then listen to his words: ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’<br>Prompts: Sorry for something I said or did this week… / Sorry for thinking I don’t need forgiving.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry. Thank you that you forgive.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for calling us his friends.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you forgive me and call me ‘my friend’. / Thank you that you went to the cross so I could be forgiven.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you call us your friends.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of yesterday’s friend. Bring them to Jesus by name.<br>Prompts: Jesus, please help (name) to know you forgive and love them. / Please help (name) with…<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, help (name)…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Jesus, help our friends know you love them.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep the Sorry phase silent and private. Reassure students who aren’t sure whether they believe that they can simply listen.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a Roman army officer who believed Jesus could help — without even coming to the house.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Just Say the Word":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 2:1–12; Luke 7:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Just Say the Word</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends by name — The four friends carried their friend to Jesus. We can’t carry our friends on a mat — but we can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name. Say their name, then say what they need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how a Roman army officer asked Jesus to heal his servant.</li><li>We are learning that the officer trusted Jesus’ word, because he understood that Jesus had authority.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus was amazed at the officer’s faith.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the officer believed Jesus only had to ‘say the word’, linking it to how orders work in an army, and noticing that he asked for help for someone else, not himself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a toy helmet or a cloak for the officer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Just Say the Word</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Who’s in charge?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Your coach says, “Two laps of the field — go!” Why do you run, even though the coach doesn’t run with you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What makes someone’s words powerful?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 3–4 ideas: they’re in charge; you trust them; there are consequences; they have the right to tell you. Land it: ‘That right to give orders is called authority. Today we meet a Roman army officer who knew all about authority — and believed Jesus’ words were powerful enough to heal from a distance.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 7:2–9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 7:2</b> In that town an army officer’s servant was sick and about to die. The officer liked this servant very much.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 7:6</b> “Lord, don’t go to any trouble for me! I am not good enough for you to come into my house.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 7:7</b> Just say the word, and my servant will get well.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 7:9</b> When Jesus heard this, he was so surprised that he turned and said to the crowd following him, “In all of Israel I’ve never found anyone with this much faith!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The CEV says ‘army officer’; other Bibles say ‘centurion’ — a Roman officer in charge of about 100 soldiers. A servant here means someone who worked in his household (many servants then were slaves).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The officer’s message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>We’re back in Capernaum, the town by the lake. Living there was a Roman army officer — a centurion, in charge of about a hundred soldiers. The Romans ruled the country, and most Jewish people didn’t like Roman soldiers one bit. But this officer was different. He was kind to the people of the town, and he respected their God.</p><p>He had a servant who was very sick — so sick he was about to die. And Luke tells us the officer liked this servant very much. In those days servants were often treated like property. Not by this man. He cared.</p><p>The officer had heard about Jesus. So he sent some Jewish leaders from the town to ask Jesus to come and heal his servant. They found Jesus and begged him. ‘This man deserves your help! He loves our nation and even built us a meeting place.’ A Roman soldier who paid to build a synagogue for the Jews! So Jesus went with them.</p><p>But as Jesus got close to the house, the officer sent some friends with another message. Listen to what he said: ‘Lord, don’t go to any trouble for me! I am not good enough for you to come into my house. And I am certainly not worthy to come to you. Just say the word, and my servant will get well.’</p><p>Then he explained why. ‘I have officers who give orders to me, and I have soldiers who take orders from me. I can say to one of them, “Go!” and he goes. I can say to another, “Come!” and he comes.’</p><p>Do you see what he meant? A soldier understands authority. When the person in charge gives an order, it happens — even if he isn’t standing there. The officer believed that Jesus was in charge of sickness, the way he was in charge of his soldiers. Jesus didn’t need to come. He didn’t need to touch. His word was enough.</p><p>When Jesus heard this, he was so surprised that he turned round to the crowd following him and said, ‘In all of Israel I’ve never found anyone with this much faith!’</p><p>The officer’s friends went back to the house. And they found the servant well.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A Roman officer. A very sick servant. · 2. “This man deserves your help!” · 3. “Lord, don’t go to any trouble for me!” · 4. “Just say the word.” · 5. “I’ve never found anyone with this much faith!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. In Luke the officer never meets Jesus face to face — he sends Jewish leaders, then friends. (Matthew 8:5–13 tells the same event more briefly.) ‘Meeting place’ = synagogue, where Jewish people met to pray and learn God’s law. Notice that Jesus was surprised — Luke uses the same word elsewhere for people amazed at Jesus; here Jesus is amazed at someone else!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Just say…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the officer’s message: “Just say the word, and…”<br><i>Answer: “…my servant will get well.” (Luke 7:7)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘Who did the officer ask Jesus to help — himself or someone else?’ (His servant — he was praying for someone else!) Follow-up 2: ‘Why didn’t he want Jesus to come into his house?’ (He felt he wasn’t good enough; he believed Jesus’ word was enough.) If silent: point to the third scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Just Say the Word</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Just Say the Word</b> (Luke 7:1–10). The leaders’ words, the officer’s message and Jesus’ words are from Luke 7 (CEV). The names Marcus and Gaius, the scene in the officer’s house, and the servant’s lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Marcus</b> — The Roman army officer — strong, used to giving orders, but humble<br><b>Elder</b> — A Jewish leader of the town who respects the officer<br><b>Gaius</b> — The officer’s friend who carries his message<br><b>Jesus</b> — On his way into town — kind, amazed (the teacher may read Jesus’ lines)<br><b>Servant</b> — Very sick at first — then well!<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd following Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the crowd following Jesus — walking on the spot, chatting · Everyone: gasp when Jesus says how much faith the officer has · Everyone: cheer when the servant is well<br>Props: Optional: a cloak or toy helmet for the officer<br>Luke has the officer send friends with the message; it’s imagined that one friend (Gaius) speaks for them all and that Marcus whispers the message to him. Keep the servant’s illness simple — ‘very sick’ — and the ending joyful. If a student has a seriously ill family member, be sensitive and don’t press them to play the servant.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Capernaum. In a Roman officer’s house, a servant lies very sick.</li><li><b>Servant:</b> Master… I’m sorry… I can’t get up today. <i>(weak voice, lying down)</i></li><li><b>Marcus:</b> Don’t you dare apologise. Rest. I’ll find help. <i>(kneel beside him)</i></li><li><b>Marcus:</b> Elder! They say Jesus of Nazareth heals the sick. Will you ask him to come?</li><li><b>Elder:</b> For you, Marcus? Of course. You’ve been good to our town. <i>(hurry off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The leaders went to Jesus and begged him to do something.</li><li><b>Elder:</b> This man deserves your help! He loves our nation and even built us a meeting place.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> A Roman built our meeting place? Really? <i>(whisper to each other)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> So Jesus went with them. The crowd followed.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Where’s he going? Follow him! <i>(walk on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Marcus:</b> Gaius! Jesus is coming here — to my house. I’m not good enough for that. <i>(pacing)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! A powerful Roman officer says he’s ‘not good enough’ for Jesus to visit. Why do you think he feels like that? (20s)</li><li><b>Marcus:</b> Run and stop him. Tell him this… <i>(whisper in Gaius’ ear)</i></li><li><b>Gaius:</b> Lord, don’t go to any trouble! He’s not good enough for you to come into his house. <i>(out of breath, bow)</i></li><li><b>Gaius:</b> Just say the word, and his servant will get well.</li><li><b>Gaius:</b> He says, ‘I give orders to my soldiers. I say “Go!” and he goes. I say “Come!” and he comes.’</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When Jesus heard this, he was so surprised that he turned to the crowd.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> In all of Israel I’ve never found anyone with this much faith! <i>(turn to the crowd, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ohhh! <i>(gasp)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The officer’s friends went back to the house.</li><li><b>Servant:</b> Master! Look — I’m well! I feel completely well! <i>(sit up, then stand)</i></li><li><b>Marcus:</b> He only said the word… and you’re well. <i>(amazed, quiet)</i></li><li><b>Gaius:</b> He’s more than a teacher, Marcus. His word is in charge.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Just say the word! <i>(cheer)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The officer had power and money. Why did he say he wasn’t good enough for Jesus?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was humble; he knew Jesus was greater than him; as a non-Jew he didn’t want to put Jesus in an awkward spot; he trusted Jesus’ word was enough.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Jesus say to the crowd when he heard the officer’s message?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘In all of Israel I’ve never found anyone with this much faith!’ (Luke 7:9)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A surprising hero</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The officer was a Roman soldier — part of the army ruling over the Jewish people. Was it fair that Jesus helped him and praised his faith more than anyone in Israel?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them disagree. Unfair: the Romans were the enemy; Jewish people had waited for their Messiah. Fair: Jesus helps anyone who trusts him; the officer loved people and was humble; God’s love was always meant for every nation (Genesis 12:3). Follow-up: ‘Who might be a surprising “outsider” today that Jesus would welcome?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about who Jesus came for?’ (Everyone — including us in Aotearoa New Zealand.) If silent: ‘Imagine the person you least expect turned out to have the most faith. How would you feel?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Faith for someone else</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The four friends on Monday and the officer today both came to Jesus for SOMEONE ELSE. What does that teach us about faith?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: real faith loves other people; you can trust Jesus on behalf of a friend; praying for others is one of the most important things we do; Jesus listens when we ask for others. Follow-up: ‘What did it cost the friends? What did it cost the officer?’ (Effort, a roof, pride, asking Jewish leaders for help.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you bring to Jesus this week?’ If silent: ‘How is praying for a friend like carrying their mat?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ word has authority — he can help anyone, anywhere, even far away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I trust that your word is enough.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone far away — a grandparent, a cousin, a friend who moved. Pray for them by name tonight, trusting that Jesus can help them from where he is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask 2–3 students to share who they’ll pray for (first names or ‘my nan’ is enough).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The message</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Just say the word, and my servant will get well.”<br>Jesus was amazed at his faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Mark 2:5. Say it once as the four friends (strong, determined), once as the crowd (amazed whisper).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: One sentence out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The officer’s prayer was short: “Just say the word.” Today, anyone who wants to can pray one sentence out loud for a friend, by first name only. Short prayers are real prayers. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Think of one person — near or far — who needs Jesus’ help. You’ll have a chance to say their first name out loud, or to pray silently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ word is so powerful that he healed someone without even going there.<br>Prompts: Jesus, your word is in charge of everything. / Jesus, you can help people anywhere, near or far.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, your word…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you only have to say the word!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: the officer was humble. Have you been proud or bossy?<br>Prompts: Sorry for acting like I’m more important than others. / Sorry for not trusting what you say.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for being proud.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus that he listens when we ask for others.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you healed the officer’s servant. / Thank you that you listen when I pray for my friends.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you hear us when we pray for others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now, one at a time, anyone who wants to: say a friend’s first name and one need.<br>Prompts: Jesus, please help (name) with… / Jesus, just say the word for (name)…<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, please help (name)…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, just say the word for all the people we love.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish with the officer’s words: “Just say the word.” Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first: ‘Jesus, please help my friend Sam with his sore back.’ Remind students: first names only, nothing private. Nobody has to pray aloud — silent prayers count.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what ‘authority’ means, who Jesus really is, and this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Bring Them to Jesus":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 2:1–12; Luke 7:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bring Them to Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends by name — The four friends carried their friend to Jesus. We can’t carry our friends on a mat — but we can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name. Say their name, then say what they need.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning that Jesus has authority — the right and power to forgive and to heal.</li><li>We are learning that real faith in Jesus shows up in love for other people.</li><li>I can recite Mark 2:5 and pray for a friend by name.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell both stories, explain what ‘authority’ means and why both stories show Jesus has it, recite Mark 2:5, and name one friend they will pray for this week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Pencils for writing a friend’s name on the card</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Bring Them to Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“My friend, your sins are forgiven.” — <b>Jesus</b><br>“He must think he is God! Only God can forgive sins.” — <b>The teachers of the Law</b><br>“We have never seen anything like this!” — <b>The crowd</b><br>“This man deserves your help! He loves our nation and even built us a meeting place.” — <b>The Jewish leaders</b><br>“Just say the word, and my servant will get well.” — <b>The army officer (through his friends)</b><br>“In all of Israel I’ve never found anyone with this much faith!” — <b>Jesus</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Which story is that from — the roof or the officer?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Mark 2:10–11 · Luke 7:7–8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:10</b> “I will show you that the Son of Man has the right to forgive sins here on earth.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 2:11</b> So Jesus said to the man, “Get up! Pick up your mat and go on home.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 7:7–8</b> “Just say the word, and my servant will get well. I have officers who give orders to me, and I have soldiers who take orders from me.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both readings are about authority — the right and the power to command. Jesus has authority over sin and over sickness.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The One in charge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This week we met two very different people. Four ordinary friends with dusty hands. And a powerful Roman army officer. But they had something in common. They both came to Jesus — not for themselves, but for someone else.</p><p>The friends carried their friend through a roof. The officer sent message after message for his servant. Their faith in Jesus showed up as love for other people. Neither of them could solve it on their own — but they knew who to turn to. And Jesus responded.</p><p>Both stories are also about authority. Authority means the right and the power to say what happens. The officer understood it. He said, ‘I give orders and they are obeyed — so, Jesus, just say the word.’ And from a distance, with only a word, Jesus healed the servant.</p><p>In Capernaum, Jesus showed an even bigger authority. ‘The Son of Man has the right to forgive sins here on earth.’ Only God can do that. Jesus is in charge of sickness and in charge of sin, because Jesus is God, come close.</p><p>And Jesus is compassionate. That means he feels for people and acts to help them. He called a man on a mat ‘my friend’. He went to a Roman soldier’s house. Later, he went to the cross, so that anyone — anyone! — can be forgiven and become his friend.</p><p>So here’s the question. Who will you bring to Jesus? You can’t carry your friends through a roof. But you can carry them to Jesus in prayer, by name, every day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. One word from Jesus. · 2. Authority · 3. Four friends. One officer. · 4. Who will you bring?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. ‘Compassionate’ is this week’s ‘God is…’ word — write it on the board. Remind students that Jesus doesn’t always heal in the way we ask; we still bring our friends to him, because he loves them and knows what they need most.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Authority</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the officer say he could do that helped him understand Jesus?<br>A) Build a meeting place &nbsp; B) Give orders to his soldiers, and they obey ✓ &nbsp; C) Make his servant well &nbsp; D) March faster than anyone</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 7:8). Follow-up: ‘How did that help him trust Jesus?’ (If his own orders are obeyed, Jesus’ word could command sickness to go.) Follow-up 2: ‘In Mark 2, what did Jesus say he had the right to do?’ (Forgive sins here on earth.) If silent: read the third scripture line and ask what ‘give orders’ means.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two kinds of friends</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare the four friends and the officer. How did each of them show faith in Jesus by loving someone else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the friends carried, climbed, dug and lowered — faith that wouldn’t give up; the officer cared for a servant others might ignore, asked for help humbly, and trusted Jesus’ word; both put someone else’s need first; both believed Jesus could and would help. Follow-up: ‘Which one is more like you — the hands-on friends, or the officer who sent a message?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do both stories tell us about Jesus?’ (He is compassionate and has authority.) If silent: point to the two pictures and ask ‘What is the same in these two stories?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Four friends brought their friend to Jesus — and Jesus called him ‘my friend’ and forgave his sins.</li><li>A Roman officer trusted Jesus to ‘just say the word’ — and his servant was healed.</li><li>Jesus has authority over sin and sickness, and he is compassionate: he loves to help when we bring others to him.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in: ‘Faith in Jesus shows up in love for others — and Jesus responds.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Deserves it?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The leaders said the officer DESERVED Jesus’ help. The officer said he was NOT good enough. Who was right — and does anyone ‘deserve’ Jesus’ help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let them disagree. Leaders: he’d been generous and kind. Officer: he knew Jesus was far greater than him. Best answer: nobody earns Jesus’ help by being good enough — the man on the mat hadn’t done anything either; Jesus helps because he is compassionate, and he loves humble faith. That’s good news — we don’t have to be good enough to bring a friend to him. Follow-up: ‘How is this like the man on the mat being forgiven?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why might it matter that the officer was humble?’ If silent: ‘Can you earn a present? What makes it a present?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bring a friend’s need to Jesus in prayer, by name, every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, ‘My friend, your sins are forgiven.’” — Mark 2:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday will check in on this. Students write a friend’s first name (just the name — no private details) on their card and keep it somewhere they’ll see each day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“When Jesus saw how much faith they had, he said to the crippled man, “My friend, your sins are forgiven.”” — Mark 2:5 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Mark 2:5 with the actions. Last round: say it without the screen, holding up four fingers at ‘how much faith they had’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Well</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Just say the word.”<br>They found the servant well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a friend’s name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Writing someone’s name helps you remember to pray for them. Today you’ll write a friend’s name and one thing to pray for — and you can pray it every day this week. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your challenge card and pencil. Write one friend’s first name, and one word for what they need. Then put your pencil down and let’s pray.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we saw that Jesus is compassionate and has authority.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you have the right to forgive sins. / Jesus, your word heals — even from far away.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you are kind and you are in charge!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: have you forgotten to care for people who needed help?<br>Prompts: Sorry for only praying about myself. / Sorry for giving up on a friend at the ‘door’.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for forgetting other people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for the friends in this week’s stories — and for yours.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the four friends who didn’t give up. / Thank you that you call forgiven people your friends.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for our friends — and for being our friend.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at the name on your card. Bring that friend to Jesus.<br>Prompts: Jesus, I bring you (name). Please help with… / Please let (name) know you love them.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I bring you (name)…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we bring you the friends on our cards. Please just say the word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your card in your hands. Let’s say it together: “Jesus, we bring them to you.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Collect nothing — the cards go home. Remind students: first names only, and nothing private. If anyone is worried about a friend’s safety, they should tell you or another trusted adult as well as praying.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith in the Storm — Jesus asleep in a boat in a wild storm, and Peter steps out onto the water!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9: Faith in the Storm":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:22–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lord of the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when afraid — You don’t have to feel calm to pray. When you’re scared, tell Jesus the truth — even one sentence like “Save me, Lord!” is a real prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Asleep in the Storm</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning what happened when a storm struck the disciples’ boat on the Lake of Galilee.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Asleep in the Storm → Wild weather → Mark 4:35–38 → The storm on the lake → Where was Jesus? → Sleeping in a storm → Asleep in the Storm → “Don’t you care?” → Head · Heart · Hands → Asleep → Memory verse peek → Praying when afraid (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>“Quiet! Be Still!”</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened when Jesus spoke to the wind and the waves.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>“Quiet! Be Still!” → Recap → Mark 4:39–41 → Who is this? → Who rules the sea? → Two questions → More afraid than ever → Faith and fear → Head · Heart · Hands → Flat calm → Build the verse → Open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Peter Steps Out</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus walked on the water and called Peter to come to him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Peter Steps Out → Would you? → Matthew 14:27–31 → Walking on the water → Peter’s prayer → Peter Steps Out → Brave or silly? → Eyes on the wind → Head · Heart · Hands → Come on! → Verse with actions → Pairs: praying for each other’s fears</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>“Why Did You Doubt?”</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus caught Peter and what he said to him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>“Why Did You Doubt?” → True or false? → Matthew 14:31–33 · Psalm 56:3 → The hand that catches → At once → Up close → This week’s Big Idea → When storms don’t stop → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Caught → Praying Psalm 56</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Asleep in the Storm":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:22–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Asleep in the Storm</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when afraid — You don’t have to feel calm to pray. When you’re scared, tell Jesus the truth — even one sentence like “Save me, Lord!” is a real prayer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when a storm struck the disciples’ boat on the Lake of Galilee.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus was with his friends in the storm, even when it looked like he didn’t care.</li><li>I can retell the storm story and say what the disciples asked Jesus.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the storm in order (crossing, storm, Jesus asleep, ‘Don’t you care?’, calm) and noticing that Jesus was in the boat the whole time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bucket or bowl for ‘bailing’, a cushion for sleeping Jesus</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Asleep in the Storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Second-to-last week of term. This week is all about fear — and what to do with it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Wild weather</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the scariest weather you’ve ever been in?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where were you — and what did it feel like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met four friends who carried their friend to Jesus — right through a roof — and a Roman officer who trusted Jesus to ‘just say the word’. Your challenge: bring a friend’s need to Jesus in prayer, by name, every day. Who did you pray for? Did you keep it up?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students who they prayed for (first names only, no private details) and whether they managed every day. Then the hook: Canterbury nor’westers, hail, thunder, a rough ferry crossing. Land it: ‘Today some tough fishermen get caught in a storm so wild they think they’re going to die — and Jesus is in the boat… asleep.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Mark 4:35–38</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:35</b> That evening, Jesus said to his disciples, “Let’s cross to the east side.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:37</b> Suddenly a windstorm struck the lake. Waves started splashing into the boat, and it was about to sink.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:38</b> Jesus was in the back of the boat with his head on a pillow, and he was asleep.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:38</b> His disciples woke him and said, “Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disciples = Jesus’ close followers and students (explain for those who haven’t heard the word). The ‘lake’ is the Lake (or Sea) of Galilee.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The storm on the lake</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It had been a long, busy day beside the Lake of Galilee. Since early morning, Jesus had been teaching huge crowds — so many people that he sat in a boat just off the shore so everyone could see and hear him. He told story after story about God’s kingdom.</p><p>As the sun went down, Jesus said to his disciples, ‘Let’s cross to the east side.’ So they left the crowd behind and pushed out onto the water, with Jesus still in the boat. A few other boats followed along.</p><p>Several of the disciples were fishermen — Peter, Andrew, James and John. They had grown up on this lake. They knew every bay and every rock. And they knew that the lake could be calm as glass one minute — and the next, a wind could come roaring down from the hills and whip the water into a fury.</p><p>That is exactly what happened. Suddenly a windstorm struck the lake. The boat tipped and rolled. Waves crashed over the sides. Water sloshed around their feet, then their ankles. The disciples grabbed buckets and bowls and bailed as fast as they could, but the waves kept coming. The boat was about to sink.</p><p>And where was Jesus? He was in the back of the boat, with his head on a pillow… fast asleep.</p><p>Imagine that! Thunder, wind, spray, men shouting — and Jesus sleeping. Jesus was truly human. He had worked hard all day, and he was tired, just like you get tired. But he was also completely at peace. He wasn’t worried at all.</p><p>The disciples were terrified. These were tough fishermen — if they were scared, this was a terrible storm. They shook Jesus awake and shouted, ‘Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?’</p><p>Don’t you care? Have you ever felt like that — like something scary is happening, and God must be asleep, or not listening, or not caring?</p><p>Jesus got up. He stood in the rocking boat and ordered the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down. And the wind stopped. The waves lay down flat. Everything was calm.</p><p>Then Jesus turned to his friends and asked, ‘Why were you afraid? Don’t you have any faith?’</p><p>Tomorrow we’ll think hard about that question. But for now, notice this: the storm was real, and the danger was real — and Jesus was in the boat the whole time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Let’s cross to the east side.” · 2. Suddenly — a windstorm! · 3. And Jesus? · 4. “Teacher, don’t you care?” · 5. Jesus got up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mark names the boat’s passengers only as ‘his disciples’; that Peter, Andrew, James and John were fishermen comes from Mark 1:16–20. The details of bailing with buckets and bowls are imagined but fit ‘waves started splashing into the boat’. The Lake of Galilee sits low between hills, and sudden storms are well known there.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where was Jesus?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where was Jesus when the storm hit?<br>A) Steering the boat &nbsp; B) Asleep in the back, on a pillow ✓ &nbsp; C) Swimming to shore &nbsp; D) Still on the beach</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Mark 4:38). Follow-up: ‘What did the disciples say when they woke him?’ (‘Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?’) Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think Mark bothers to tell us about the pillow?’ (It’s an eyewitness detail — Jesus was really, peacefully asleep.) If silent: re-read the third scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Sleeping in a storm</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone else was panicking — but Jesus was asleep. What does that show us about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he was truly human and really tired; he was at peace because he trusted his Father completely; he wasn’t afraid of the storm because he was in charge of it; he knew they would be safe. Follow-up: ‘Can you sleep when you’re really worried?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would it take for you to be that peaceful?’ If silent: ask ‘What does a baby asleep in its parent’s arms show about how it feels?’ — safe, trusting.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Asleep in the Storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Asleep in the Storm</b> (Mark 4:35–41). Jesus’ words and the disciples’ cry are from Mark 4 (CEV). ‘Quiet! Be still!’ is how many Bibles translate Jesus’ command in 4:39 (the CEV says he ‘ordered the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down’). Which disciple says which line, and the chat before the storm, are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Tired after a long day — calm and in charge (the teacher may read Jesus’ lines)<br><b>Peter</b> — Big, loud fisherman — usually knows what to do<br><b>James</b> — Fisherman — practical, starts bailing<br><b>John</b> — James’ younger brother — notices everything<br><b>Everyone</b> — The wind and the waves</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the wind and waves — soft whooshing, then a roaring storm · Everyone: sway from side to side like a rocking boat · Everyone: fall completely silent when Jesus speaks<br>Props: Optional: a cushion for Jesus, a bowl for bailing<br>A loud, fun role-play — but make the silence after ‘Quiet! Be still!’ really silent: hold it for three seconds before the Narrator speaks. If any students are genuinely frightened of storms, let them be ‘Jesus’ helpers’ who say the calm lines.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Evening on the Lake of Galilee. A small fishing boat pushes off from the shore.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Let’s cross to the east side.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Right! Everyone in. James, grab that rope! <i>(push the boat off)</i></li><li><b>James:</b> Look at that sunset. Perfect night for sailing. <i>(stretch, relaxed)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus lay down in the back of the boat, with his head on a pillow.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Wake me when we get there. <i>(yawn, lie down)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooosh… <i>(soft wind)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> Did anyone else feel that wind change? <i>(look up at the sky)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> WHOOOOSH! CRASH! SPLASH! <i>(roaring storm — sway hard)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Storm! Get the sail down! Hold on!</li><li><b>James:</b> Water’s coming in! Bail! Bail! <i>(scoop water frantically)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> It’s up to my ankles! We’re going to sink!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> CRASH! CRASH! CRASH! <i>(waves over the side)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> I’ve fished this lake my whole life. I’ve never seen it this bad!</li><li><b>James:</b> Where’s Jesus?</li><li><b>John:</b> He’s… asleep. He’s actually asleep! <i>(point, amazed)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The boat is filling with water and Jesus is asleep. What are you feeling right now — and what would you say to him? (20s)</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Teacher! Wake up! <i>(shake Jesus’ shoulder)</i></li><li><b>James:</b> Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus got up. He ordered the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Quiet! Be still! <i>(stand, arm stretched out — calm, strong voice)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhhhhh…… <i>(the storm dies away — then total silence)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The wind stopped, and everything was calm.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Why were you afraid? Don’t you have any faith? <i>(gently, looking at each of them)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> I… I don’t know what to say. <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Even the wind and the waves obey him! <i>(whisper, amazed)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why could Jesus sleep in the storm when everyone else was terrified?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He trusted his Father completely; he was in charge of the storm; he knew they were safe with him. The disciples had Jesus in the boat — but forgot what that meant.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened the moment Jesus spoke to the wind and waves?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The wind stopped and everything was calm (Mark 4:39) — instantly, not slowly.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: “Don’t you care?”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples asked Jesus, “Don’t you care?” Was that a fair thing to say? When do people feel like God doesn’t care?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students disagree. Fair: they were really scared, and it’s honest to tell Jesus how you feel. Unfair: Jesus was right there, he had chosen them, he had healed people all day — of course he cared. People feel God doesn’t care when someone is sick, parents argue, prayers seem unanswered. Follow-up: ‘Did Jesus get angry at their question?’ (No — he calmed the storm, then asked about their faith.) Follow-up 2: ‘What’s the difference between telling Jesus “I’m scared” and accusing him of not caring?’ If silent: ‘Have you ever felt alone when something scary happened?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is with us in the storm — even when it feels like he’s asleep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, when I’m scared, help me remember you’re in the boat with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when something scares you — a test, a dark room, a big game, a worrying phone call — whisper: “Jesus, you’re in the boat with me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise the whisper together once so it’s ready to use.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Asleep</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?”<br>Jesus was in the boat the whole time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Psalm 56:3. King David wrote it when he was in real danger. It’s short — most students will know it by Wednesday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying when afraid (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we learn to pray when we’re afraid. You don’t have to be calm to pray — the disciples prayed by shouting “Wake up!” Jesus listened anyway. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Imagine the boat after the storm — the water flat, the stars out. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even the wind and the waves obey Jesus.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you are stronger than any storm. / Jesus, the wind and the waves obey you.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — even the wind and the waves obey you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples thought Jesus didn’t care. Have you ever thought that?<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you don’t care about me. / Sorry for panicking instead of coming to you.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we thought you didn’t care.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus that he stayed in the boat with his friends.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you were in the boat the whole time. / Thank you that you heard them even when they shouted.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you are with us in the storm.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a ‘storm’ in your life right now? Tell Jesus about it.<br>Prompts: Please calm the storm that’s worrying me: … / Please be with someone I know who’s scared right now.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Jesus, be with everyone here who feels afraid today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Some students will have real ‘storms’ at home. Keep the Please phase silent and private; follow up gently with any student who seems troubled.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the moment the storm stopped — and the disciples’ huge question, “Who is this?”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: “Quiet! Be Still!”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:22–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Quiet! Be Still!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when afraid — You don’t have to feel calm to pray. When you’re scared, tell Jesus the truth — even one sentence like “Save me, Lord!” is a real prayer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Jesus spoke to the wind and the waves.</li><li>We are learning why the disciples asked, “Who is this?” — and what the Old Testament says about who rules the sea.</li><li>I can explain the difference between being afraid and having no faith.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Jesus’ power over the storm to God’s power in Psalm 107, and explaining that faith means bringing our fear to Jesus rather than never feeling fear.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a jug of water and a tray to show how waves keep rolling after wind stops</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: “Quiet! Be Still!”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. ‘Quiet! Be still!’ is the NIV wording of Mark 4:39; the CEV on screen says Jesus ‘ordered the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday a storm nearly sank the disciples’ boat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where was Jesus — and what did the disciples shout?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Asleep in the back of the boat on a pillow. ‘Teacher, don’t you care that we’re about to drown?’ Then: ‘Jesus got up. Today we slow right down and look at what happened next — and the question it left the disciples asking all night.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Mark 4:39–41</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:39</b> Jesus got up and ordered the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:39</b> The wind stopped, and everything was calm.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:40</b> Jesus asked his disciples, “Why were you afraid? Don’t you have any faith?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Mark 4:41</b> Now they were more afraid than ever and said to each other, “Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Point out the two kinds of fear: fear of the storm (v.38) and ‘more afraid than ever’ of the One who stopped it (v.41).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who is this?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s go back into that boat. The wind is screaming. Waves are crashing over the sides. Everyone is soaked and shaking. And Jesus, who was fast asleep a moment ago, stands up.</p><p>Notice what he does. He doesn’t grab a bucket. He doesn’t pray, ‘Father, please help us!’ He speaks straight to the storm, the way you would speak to a dog that’s barking too much. He orders the wind and the waves to be quiet and calm down. Many Bibles put it like this: ‘Quiet! Be still!’</p><p>And the storm obeys.</p><p>Now here’s something fishermen know. When a strong wind stops, the waves don’t stop with it. They keep rolling and slapping for hours. But Mark says the wind stopped, and everything was calm. Not slowly — at once. The lake went flat like a mirror. Imagine the silence. Water dripping off your hair. Your heart still thumping. And not a ripple on the lake.</p><p>Then Jesus turned to his friends and asked two questions. ‘Why were you afraid? Don’t you have any faith?’</p><p>Was Jesus saying it’s wrong to feel scared? No. Being scared in a storm is normal. The problem was that they had Jesus in the boat — and forgot what that meant. They thought, ‘He doesn’t care.’ Faith would have thought, ‘He’s with us. We’ll be all right.’</p><p>And now the disciples were more afraid than ever. But this was a new kind of fear — not the fear of drowning, but the fear you feel standing next to something enormous and holy. They whispered to each other, ‘Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!’</p><p>These men knew their Bibles. They knew Psalm 107, about sailors caught in a terrible storm. Their ship goes up to the sky and down to the depths, and they are terrified. So they cry out to the LORD — and the LORD makes the storm stop and the waves go still.</p><p>Who stills the storm in the Old Testament? Only God. The LORD who made the sea is the only one who can tell it what to do.</p><p>So the disciples were left with a huge question. A man had just done what only God can do. Who is Jesus? Mark wants you to answer that question too. Jesus isn’t just a good teacher or a brave leader. He is God himself, come close — close enough to sleep in a fishing boat, strong enough to stop a storm with a word.</p><p>And that changes what we do with fear. Faith doesn’t mean you never feel afraid. Faith means that when you are afraid, you bring it to the One who rules the storm.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus stood up in the rocking boat. · 2. He spoke. · 3. Flat calm. · 4. “Who is this?” · 5. Only God rules the sea.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Psalm 107:23–30 is summarised, not quoted — read it from a Bible if you have time. The point about waves continuing after wind stops is a well-known fact about real lakes and seas; it helps students see how complete the calm was. Mark 4:39 literally has Jesus ‘rebuke’ the wind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who rules the sea?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare the sailors in Psalm 107 with the disciples in Mark 4. What do you notice?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Psalm 107:23–30 (the LORD)</th><th>Mark 4:35–41 (Jesus)</th></tr><tr><td>Sailors caught in a terrible storm</td><td>Fishermen caught in a windstorm</td></tr><tr><td>Huge waves — they are terrified</td><td>Waves splash in — the boat is about to sink</td></tr><tr><td>They cry out to the LORD</td><td>They wake Jesus: “Don’t you care?”</td></tr><tr><td>The LORD makes the storm stop and the sea go still</td><td>Jesus orders the wind and waves — everything is calm</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only God rules the wind and the waves — so Jesus is God with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Ask after the last row: ‘In Psalm 107 the LORD does it. In Mark 4, who does it?’ Let the penny drop.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two questions</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After the storm stopped, what did Jesus ask his disciples?<br>A) “Who left the sail up?” &nbsp; B) “Why were you afraid? Don’t you have any faith?” ✓ &nbsp; C) “Why did you wake me?” &nbsp; D) “Can we go home now?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Mark 4:40). Follow-up: ‘What question did the disciples then ask each other?’ (‘Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!’) Follow-up 2: ‘Do you think Jesus was cross, or sad, or gentle when he asked?’ (The text doesn’t say — let them imagine, but notice he had just saved them.) If silent: point to the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: More afraid than ever</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The storm was over — but the disciples were MORE afraid than ever. Why do you think that was?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they realised Jesus had power only God has; they were in the boat with someone far bigger than the storm; it was ‘awe’ — the shaky feeling of being near something holy and enormous; they didn’t understand who he was yet. Follow-up: ‘Is this kind of fear bad or good?’ (It’s the right response to God — the Bible calls it ‘the fear of the LORD’, deep respect and wonder.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did they answer their own question by the end of Matthew 14?’ (Tomorrow’s story — ‘You really are the Son of God!’) If silent: ask ‘How would you feel if your friend told a volcano to stop — and it did?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Faith and fear</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If you really have faith, you will never feel afraid.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let both sides speak. The best answer: disagree. Feeling afraid is human — even David wrote ‘Even when I am afraid’ (our memory verse). Faith isn’t the absence of fear; it’s where you take your fear. The disciples’ problem wasn’t feeling scared — it was thinking Jesus didn’t care. Follow-up: ‘What does our memory verse say we do when we’re afraid?’ (Keep on trusting.) Follow-up 2: ‘What’s one thing that helps you trust when you’re scared?’ If silent: ‘Was Jesus ever troubled or upset?’ (Yes — in the garden before the cross; he prayed.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith isn’t having no fear — it’s bringing my fear to Jesus, who rules the storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want to trust you more than I fear the storm.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name one ‘storm’ you’re facing — a worry about a friend, a test, something at home. Tonight before bed, tell Jesus about it in one honest sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give students 20 seconds to silently name their ‘storm’ — no sharing needed. This leads straight into today’s prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Flat calm</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Quiet! Be still!”<br>“Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Psalm 56:3. Ask: ‘David doesn’t say IF I am afraid. He says EVEN WHEN. What does that tell you?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When we’re scared we often clench our fists. Today we pray with open hands — as if we’re handing our fears to Jesus, who rules the storm. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make two fists and squeeze them tight — that’s what fear feels like. Now slowly open your hands, palms up, on your lap.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Only God can tell a storm to stop — and Jesus did.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you rule the wind and the waves. / Jesus, you are God, come close.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you rule the wind and the waves!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus asked, “Don’t you have any faith?”<br>Prompts: Sorry for trusting my worries more than you. / Sorry for forgetting you’re with me.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for forgetting you’re with us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a time a ‘storm’ in your life calmed down.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the time you helped me when… / Thank you that nothing is too big for you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that no storm is too big for you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Picture placing your ‘storm’ in your open hands. Now hand it to Jesus.<br>Prompts: Jesus, I give you my worry about… / Please help me keep on trusting you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, take…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we give you our worries. Even when we are afraid, we will keep on trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your open hands a little, as if giving something away. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The ‘open hands’ posture is a simple, physical way to show trust. Keep it optional and low-key for students who feel self-conscious.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: another night, another storm — and Peter climbs out of the boat!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Peter Steps Out":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:22–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Peter Steps Out</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when afraid — You don’t have to feel calm to pray. When you’re scared, tell Jesus the truth — even one sentence like “Save me, Lord!” is a real prayer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus walked on the water and called Peter to come to him.</li><li>We are learning why Peter began to sink — and what he did when he did.</li><li>I can explain what it means to keep my eyes on Jesus when I’m afraid.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Peter walked while he looked at Jesus and sank when he looked at the wind, and that his cry ‘Save me, Lord!’ was a prayer of faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a strip of blue fabric or paper for ‘the water’, a chair for the boat</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Peter Steps Out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Would you?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine it’s 3 o’clock in the morning, on a boat in the middle of a stormy lake. Someone calls, “Come on — step out!” Would you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you need to know first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a quick hands-up vote, then ideas: who is calling, is it safe, can they catch me? Land it: ‘Tonight’s story happens on the same lake, in the dark, in the wind — and one disciple actually does it. What he needed to know was simply: is it really Jesus?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 14:27–31</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:27</b> At once, Jesus said to them, “Don’t worry! I am Jesus. Don’t be afraid.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:28–29</b> Peter replied, “Lord, if it is really you, tell me to come to you on the water.” “Come on!” Jesus said.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:30</b> But when Peter saw how strong the wind was, he was afraid and started sinking. “Save me, Lord!” he shouted.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:31</b> At once, Jesus reached out his hand. He helped Peter up.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is a different night from Monday’s storm — Matthew 14 happens after Jesus fed the five thousand.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Walking on the water</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This story happens on a different night, on the same lake. Jesus had just fed more than five thousand people with five small loaves and two fish. Straight away, he made his disciples get into the boat and start back across the lake, while he sent the crowds home. Then Jesus went up a mountain to be alone and pray.</p><p>Night came. By now the boat was a long way from shore, and a strong wind was blowing against it. The disciples rowed and rowed, but the waves tossed them around and they hardly moved. Hour after hour. Their arms ached. And Jesus wasn’t in the boat this time.</p><p>A little while before morning, in the darkest, coldest part of the night, one of them saw something. A shape, out on the water. Coming closer. Walking — on top of the waves.</p><p>‘It’s a ghost!’ They were terrified and started screaming.</p><p>At once, a voice came across the water: ‘Don’t worry! I am Jesus. Don’t be afraid.’</p><p>Everyone froze. Then Peter — of course it was Peter — called out, ‘Lord, if it is really you, tell me to come to you on the water.’</p><p>Think about that. Peter didn’t ask to walk on water to show off. He wanted to be where Jesus was. And he knew that if Jesus told him to come, Jesus could make it possible.</p><p>Jesus said one word: ‘Come on!’</p><p>So Peter swung one leg over the side of the boat. Then the other. He let go of the boat. And he stood up — on the water! He took a step. And another. Peter, the fisherman, was walking on the lake towards Jesus!</p><p>But then Peter noticed the wind. He felt it pushing against him. He saw the waves rising and the spray flying. He stopped looking at Jesus and started looking at the storm. And as soon as he did, he was afraid — and he started to sink.</p><p>Down to his knees. Down to his waist. The cold water closing over him.</p><p>Peter did the only thing he could do. He didn’t try to swim. He didn’t pretend he was fine. He shouted the shortest prayer in the Bible: ‘Save me, Lord!’</p><p>And what did Jesus do? We’ll find out in the role-play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus went up the mountain to pray. · 2. Before dawn — someone walking on the waves! · 3. “Lord, if it is really you…” · 4. Peter walked on the water! · 5. “Save me, Lord!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. ‘I am Jesus’ is the CEV’s wording; the Greek is literally ‘I am’ or ‘It is I’ — the same words God used to name himself to Moses (Exodus 3:14). Older students may enjoy that link. Peter’s gradual sinking (knees, waist) is imagined detail.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Peter’s prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish Peter’s cry: When he started sinking, he shouted, “…”<br><i>Answer: “Save me, Lord!” (Matthew 14:30)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘What made Peter start to sink?’ (He saw how strong the wind was and became afraid.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why is “Save me, Lord!” a great prayer?’ (Short, honest, aimed at the right person — anyone can pray it, anywhere.) If silent: point to the third scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Peter Steps Out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Peter Steps Out</b> (Matthew 14:22–33). Jesus’ and Peter’s words are from Matthew 14 (CEV). The other disciples’ lines and actions are imagined, but fit the story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Walking on the water — calm, strong, kind (the teacher may read Jesus’ lines)<br><b>Peter</b> — Bold, eager — then terrified<br><b>Andrew</b> — Peter’s brother — exhausted from rowing<br><b>Thomas</b> — Likes to be sure about things<br><b>Everyone</b> — The wind, the rowers, and the worshippers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the wind — whooshing louder when Peter looks at it · Everyone: row together, then scream ‘Ghost!’ · Everyone: kneel and worship at the end<br>Props: Optional: two chairs for the boat, a strip of blue fabric for the water<br>Thomas is in the role-play as one of the Twelve; Matthew doesn’t name who said what apart from Peter and Jesus. Make sure the class kneels quietly for the last line — it’s the climax of the whole week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Night on the lake. The disciples have been rowing against the wind for hours.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Heave… heave… heave… <i>(row slowly, tired)</i></li><li><b>Andrew:</b> My arms are going to fall off. We’ve hardly moved!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooosh… <i>(wind)</i></li><li><b>Thomas:</b> Wait. What’s that — out on the water? <i>(point, squint)</i></li><li><b>Andrew:</b> Someone’s… walking. On the waves.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It’s a GHOST! Aaaaah! <i>(scream, cling to each other)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Don’t worry! I am Jesus. Don’t be afraid.</li><li><b>Thomas:</b> Is it really him? How can it be him?</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Lord, if it is really you, tell me to come to you on the water. <i>(stand up in the boat)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Come on! <i>(hold out a hand)</i></li><li><b>Andrew:</b> Peter, are you mad? Sit down! <i>(grab his arm)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Peter got out of the boat and started walking on the water towards Jesus.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> I’m doing it… I’m actually doing it! <i>(slow, wobbly steps, eyes on Jesus)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> WHOOOOOSH! <i>(the wind roars)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> The wind… look at the waves… oh no… <i>(look away from Jesus, at the wind)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Peter has stopped looking at Jesus. What is he looking at instead — and what should he do now? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When Peter saw how strong the wind was, he was afraid and started sinking.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Save me, Lord! <i>(sinking, arm up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At once, Jesus reached out his hand and helped Peter up.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> You surely don’t have much faith. Why do you doubt? <i>(gently, holding Peter’s arm)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When Jesus and Peter got into the boat, the wind died down.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhhhh…… <i>(wind fades to silence)</i></li><li><b>Andrew:</b> He walked on the water. He stopped the wind.</li><li><b>Thomas:</b> Now I’m sure. <i>(kneel)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> You really are the Son of God! <i>(kneel and worship)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter was walking fine — then he started to sink. What changed?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He looked at the wind and the waves instead of at Jesus, and fear took over. The storm hadn’t changed — where he was looking had.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Jesus do the moment Peter cried for help?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘At once, Jesus reached out his hand’ (14:31). He didn’t wait or let him sink to teach a lesson.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Brave or silly?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter was the only one who got out of the boat — and the only one who sank. Was stepping out brave faith, or a silly mistake?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite disagreement. Brave faith: he asked Jesus first and only stepped when Jesus said ‘Come on!’; he walked on water — no one else did; even when he sank, he knew who to call. Silly: he could have stayed safe; he panicked. Best answer: it was real faith, even though it was small faith — and Jesus didn’t scold him for stepping out, only for doubting. Follow-up: ‘What would the others in the boat have missed?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Is it better to step out and sink, or never step out?’ If silent: ‘Who learned more about Jesus that night — Peter or the ones who stayed in the boat?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Eyes on the wind</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter was fine while he looked at Jesus. He sank when he looked at the wind. What kinds of ‘wind’ make kids take their eyes off Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: worry about tests; what friends think; scary news; arguments at home; online stuff; fear of being left out; thinking about everything that could go wrong. Looking at Jesus means remembering who he is (Lord of the storm), what he’s promised (he’s with us), and talking to him. Follow-up: ‘How can you “look at Jesus” in a real moment at school?’ (A one-line prayer, remembering a verse, our memory verse!) Follow-up 2: ‘Does looking at Jesus make the wind go away?’ (Not always — but it stops it from being the biggest thing.) If silent: give an example of your own.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When I keep my eyes on Jesus I can do brave things — and when I sink, he catches me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, help me look at you, not at the wind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one ‘step out of the boat’ this week — sitting with someone new at lunch, owning up to something, having a go at something hard — and pray one sentence before you do it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ask 2–3 students to say their ‘step out’ out loud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Come on!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t worry! I am Jesus. Don’t be afraid.”<br>Jesus saw them struggling — and came to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Psalm 56:3. Try it once ‘shivery and scared’, then once strong and confident.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Pairs: praying for each other’s fears</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter didn’t pray a long prayer — just three words: “Save me, Lord!” Today, pray one short sentence for your partner’s fear. Short prayers are real prayers. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Each tell the other one thing that sometimes makes you afraid — keep it simple, like ‘the dark’ or ‘tests’ or ‘being left out’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together, say what Jesus did on the water.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you walked on the waves. / Jesus, you reached out your hand at once.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you walked on the water and you caught Peter!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: when have you looked at the ‘wind’ instead of Jesus?<br>Prompts: Sorry for looking at my worries instead of you. / Sorry for doubting you.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we doubted you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus that he never lets go.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you catch me when I sink. / Thank you for coming to your friends in the dark.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you catch us when we sink.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now pray one sentence for your partner’s fear, using their name.<br>Prompts: Jesus, please help (name) when they feel afraid of… / Please help (name) keep their eyes on you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, help (name)…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Jesus, help each of us keep our eyes on you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish with Peter’s prayer, all together: “Save me, Lord!” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pair students thoughtfully. If anyone doesn’t want to share a fear, they can say ‘something I’m worried about’. Students can pray silently for their partner if they’d rather not speak.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jesus’ hand, Jesus’ question — “Why do you doubt?” — and what to do when you’re afraid.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: “Why Did You Doubt?”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:22–33</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Why Did You Doubt?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying when afraid — You don’t have to feel calm to pray. When you’re scared, tell Jesus the truth — even one sentence like “Save me, Lord!” is a real prayer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus caught Peter and what he said to him.</li><li>We are learning what to do when we feel afraid, using Psalm 56:3.</li><li>I can recite Psalm 56:3 and use it as a prayer when I’m scared.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell both storm stories, explain that Jesus is with us and in charge when we’re afraid, and recite Psalm 56:3 from memory.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: small cards for students to copy Psalm 56:3 onto</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: “Why Did You Doubt?”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand up for TRUE, sit down for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the first storm, Jesus was asleep in the back of the boat. — <b>True</b> (His head was on a pillow (Mark 4:38).)<br>Jesus helped the disciples bail out the water. — <b>False</b> (He ordered the wind and the waves to calm down — and they did.)<br>After the storm stopped, the disciples were more afraid than ever. — <b>True</b> (“Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!”)<br>When Jesus walked on the water, the disciples thought he was a ghost. — <b>True</b> (They were terrified and started screaming (Matthew 14:26).)<br>Peter sank because the water was too deep. — <b>False</b> (He saw how strong the wind was and became afraid.)<br>Jesus waited a while before he helped Peter. — <b>False</b> (“At once, Jesus reached out his hand.”)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Each statement: students stand (true) or sit (false), then click to reveal. Ask one student to explain each answer and which story it comes from.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 14:31–33 · Psalm 56:3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:31</b> At once, Jesus reached out his hand. He helped Peter up and said, “You surely don’t have much faith. Why do you doubt?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:32</b> When Jesus and Peter got into the boat, the wind died down.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 14:33</b> The men in the boat worshiped Jesus and said, “You really are the Son of God!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 56:3</b> Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 14:33 answers Tuesday’s question from Mark 4:41, ‘Who is this?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The hand that catches</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Peter was sinking. He shouted, ‘Save me, Lord!’ And Matthew tells us that at once, Jesus reached out his hand. Not after a minute. Not after a lecture. At once. He caught Peter and pulled him up.</p><p>Then, while he was still holding him, Jesus said, ‘You surely don’t have much faith. Why do you doubt?’ Notice where Jesus said it — not from far away, but up close, holding Peter tight. Peter’s faith was small and wobbly. But it was enough to cry out to the right person. And that’s all it takes.</p><p>They climbed into the boat, and the wind died down. Then every disciple in that boat knelt and worshipped Jesus. ‘You really are the Son of God!’ That was the answer to the question they had asked after the first storm: ‘Who is this?’</p><p>So what do we do when we’re afraid? A thousand years before Peter, King David was in real danger, far from home among his enemies. And he wrote our memory verse: ‘Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.’</p><p>Notice: David doesn’t pretend he isn’t scared. He says it. Then he looks to God. Then he keeps on trusting.</p><p>Jesus never promised there would be no storms. But he did promise, ‘I will be with you always.’ And Jesus went into the biggest storm of all for us — the cross — so that nothing could ever pull us away from God. When you are afraid, say it, look to Jesus, and pray. He is with you, and he is in charge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. At once, Jesus reached out his hand. · 2. “Why do you doubt?” · 3. “You really are the Son of God!” · 4. When I am afraid…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Psalm 56’s heading says David wrote it when the Philistines captured him in Gath (1 Samuel 21:10–15). ‘I will be with you always’ is from Matthew 28:20.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: At once</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the disciples say when Jesus and Peter got back into the boat?<br>A) “Let’s go home!” &nbsp; B) “You really are the Son of God!” ✓ &nbsp; C) “That was close!” &nbsp; D) “Peter, you’re so brave!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Matthew 14:33). Follow-up: ‘Which question from Tuesday does this answer?’ (‘Who is this?’ from Mark 4:41.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did they do as they said it?’ (They worshipped him — knelt in awe.) If silent: read the third scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Up close</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus asked “Why do you doubt?” while he was holding Peter up. What does that show about how Jesus treats us when our faith is small?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he rescues first and teaches second; he’s gentle, not harsh; he doesn’t let go of us even when we doubt; small faith is still faith when it’s aimed at him; he wants our faith to grow, not to shame us. Follow-up: ‘How is that like a parent teaching a child to ride a bike?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might this help someone who feels their faith is too small?’ If silent: ask ‘Imagine Jesus said it from the boat while Peter was still sinking. How would that be different?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>In the storm, Jesus was in the boat the whole time — even when he seemed asleep.</li><li>Jesus rules the wind and the waves, because he is the Son of God.</li><li>When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus and cry out to him — and he catches us.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in: ‘When we’re afraid, we can look to Jesus — he’s with us and he’s in charge.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: When storms don’t stop</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus stopped these storms. But sometimes scary things don’t go away when we pray. Does that mean Jesus doesn’t care?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Handle gently — some students may be thinking of illness, family breakup or grief. Good answers: no — he’s still with us in the boat; he sometimes calms us instead of the storm; he went through the cross himself, so he understands suffering; he promised ‘I will be with you always’; one day he’ll end every storm for good. Follow-up: ‘What did Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego say a few weeks ago?’ (‘Even if he doesn’t…’ — Week 6.) Follow-up 2: ‘What helps you keep trusting when a prayer isn’t answered the way you hoped?’ If silent: share a simple, appropriate example from your own life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you feel afraid this week, say Psalm 56:3 — “Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you” — and then pray one sentence to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday will check in on this. Suggest students write the verse on a card for their pocket, pencil case or bedside.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” — Psalm 56:3 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Psalm 56:3. It’s short — challenge them to say it in a whisper, then strong, then ‘as if you’re in a storm’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Caught</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Save me, Lord!”<br>At once, Jesus reached out his hand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying Psalm 56</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">David turned his fear into a prayer. Today we pray his psalm line by line — you can borrow the Bible’s words when you don’t have your own. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray, using David’s words. I’ll say a line; you repeat it after me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">David praised God even in danger. Repeat after me:<br>Prompts: “I praise your promises!” (Psalm 56:4) / Jesus, you are the Son of God, Lord of the storm.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “I praise your promises! Jesus, you really are the Son of God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: Jesus asked Peter, “Why do you doubt?”<br>Prompts: Sorry for doubting you when I’m scared. / Sorry for looking at the wind instead of you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, we’re sorry for doubting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for catching Peter — and for catching us.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you reach out your hand at once. / Thank you that you are with me always.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you are with us always.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now pray the memory verse as your own prayer — and add one sentence about something that scares you.<br>Prompts: “Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” / Please help me when I feel afraid of…<br>Starter: <b>Even when I am afraid…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say it together one last time: “Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Repeat-after-me works well here. ‘I praise your promises!’ is from Psalm 56:4 (CEV).</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Faith Without Seeing — Thomas meets the risen Jesus, and our term finale!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10: Faith Without Seeing":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 20:19–31; Hebrews 11:1; 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Trustworthy</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Lord, help my unbelief” — A worried father once said to Jesus, “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more” (Mark 9:24; many Bibles say, “Lord, I believe — help my unbelief!”). You can bring your doubts and questions to Jesus honestly, and ask him to grow your faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Locked Room</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how the risen Jesus came to his frightened disciples in a locked room.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Locked Room → Too good to be true? → John 20:19–22 → Behind locked doors → Hands and side → Through a locked door → The Locked Room → Hard to believe → Head · Heart · Hands → Locked in → Memory verse peek → Help my unbelief (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>My Lord and My God!</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Thomas doubted and how Jesus came back for him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>My Lord and My God! → Recap → John 20:25–29 → The doubter → Word for word → Thomas’ words → Kind to doubters → Doubting Thomas? → Head · Heart · Hands → Arms folded → Build the verse → Write a ‘help my unbelief’ prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Faith Museum Returns</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how all of this term’s heroes trusted God, using Hebrews 11 and 12:1.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Faith Museum Returns → A statue in our classroom → Hebrews 11:32–34, 39 · 12:1 → One last tour → The crowd → The Faith Museum Returns → Never saw the ending → What they all shared → My own exhibit → The new wing → Verse with actions → Standing with the crowd</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Eyes on Jesus</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what Hebrews 12:1–3 means by running the race with our eyes on Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Eyes on Jesus → Ten heroes in order → Hebrews 12:1–3 → Run the race → Eyes on… → God is… → Term 2’s Big Idea → What slows you down? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → The finish line → A term of trust</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Locked Room":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 20:19–31; Hebrews 11:1; 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Locked Room</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Lord, help my unbelief” — A worried father once said to Jesus, “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more” (Mark 9:24; many Bibles say, “Lord, I believe — help my unbelief!”). You can bring your doubts and questions to Jesus honestly, and ask him to grow your faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the risen Jesus came to his frightened disciples in a locked room.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is really alive, with a real body that still bore the marks of the cross.</li><li>I can retell what Jesus said and did in the locked room (John 20:19–23).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the locked-room story in order (fear, locked doors, Jesus appears, ‘Peace’, hands and side, joy, sending, Holy Spirit) and noticing that Thomas was missing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a chair or desk to be the ‘locked door’</li><li>Optional: a torch to dim the lights for the evening scene</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Locked Room</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The last week of Term 2! This week we meet Thomas, return to the Faith Museum, and celebrate the whole term on Thursday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Too good to be true?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever heard news that was so good you just couldn’t believe it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would it take to convince you it was true?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we were out on the stormy lake: Jesus calmed the wind and the waves, and Peter walked on the water — then sank and cried, ‘Save me, Lord!’ Your challenge: when you felt afraid, say Psalm 56:3 and pray one sentence to Jesus. When did you use it? Can anyone still say the verse?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students when they used Psalm 56:3 (‘Even when I am afraid, I keep on trusting you’) and say it together. Then the hook: a surprise holiday, a lost pet coming home, winning a prize. Land it: ‘Today Jesus’ friends hear the best news in history — that he is alive — and they’re so scared they lock themselves in a room.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: John 20:19–22</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:19</b> The disciples were afraid of the Jewish leaders, and on the evening of that same Sunday they locked themselves in a room.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:19–20</b> Suddenly, Jesus appeared in the middle of the group. He greeted them and showed them his hands and his side.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:20</b> When the disciples saw the Lord, they became very happy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:21–22</b> After Jesus had greeted them again, he said, “I am sending you, just as the Father has sent me.” Then he breathed on them and said, “Receive the Holy Spirit.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘That same Sunday’ = the day Jesus rose from the dead (John 20:1). ‘Disciples’ = Jesus’ close followers. ‘He greeted them’ — many Bibles give his actual words: ‘Peace be with you!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Behind locked doors</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It was Sunday evening in Jerusalem — the most confusing day of the disciples’ lives.</p><p>On Friday, Jesus had been nailed to a cross. They had watched him die. His body had been laid in a tomb cut into the rock, and a huge stone was rolled across the doorway. Everything they had hoped for seemed finished.</p><p>But that morning, strange news had come. Mary Magdalene ran to them, out of breath: the stone had been rolled away! Peter and John raced to the tomb and found it empty — just the burial cloths lying there. Then Mary came back with even wilder news: ‘I have seen the Lord!’</p><p>Could it be true? Or was it just wishful thinking?</p><p>Now it was evening. The disciples were together in a room, and they were afraid. The same leaders who had arrested Jesus might come for them next. So they locked the doors. Every footstep outside made them jump.</p><p>And then — suddenly — Jesus was standing in the middle of the room.</p><p>Nobody opened the door. Nobody heard a key turn. He was simply there.</p><p>He greeted them with the words people used to say hello: ‘Peace be with you!’ Peace — to a room full of frightened people.</p><p>They must have wondered if they were seeing a ghost. So Jesus showed them his hands and his side — the marks from the nails and the spear. It was really him. The same Jesus who had died was alive, with a real body they could see and touch.</p><p>John tells us, ‘When the disciples saw the Lord, they became very happy.’ Imagine the laughing, the crying, the hugging!</p><p>Then Jesus gave them a job. ‘I am sending you, just as the Father has sent me.’ He breathed on them and said, ‘Receive the Holy Spirit.’ The Holy Spirit is God himself, coming to live with his people and give them courage. These scared, hiding people would soon be the bravest people in the world, telling everyone about Jesus.</p><p>But one disciple wasn’t there that night. His name was Thomas. And when the others told him, ‘We have seen the Lord!’ — he didn’t believe a word of it.</p><p>Tomorrow we’ll find out why.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Sunday evening. The doors are locked. · 2. “I have seen the Lord!” · 3. Suddenly — Jesus! · 4. His hands. His side. · 5. “I am sending you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The empty tomb and Mary Magdalene’s news are from John 20:1–18. ‘Peace be with you’ is the usual greeting (the CEV says simply ‘he greeted them’). The marks of the cross: keep it simple and never graphic — ‘the marks from the nails’. Luke 24:36–43 tells the same evening and adds that Jesus ate a piece of fish to show he was not a ghost.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Hands and side</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When Jesus appeared in the locked room, what did he show his disciples?<br>A) A map of where to go &nbsp; B) His hands and his side ✓ &nbsp; C) A loaf of bread &nbsp; D) The key to the door</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (John 20:20). Follow-up: ‘Why do you think Jesus showed them his hands and side?’ (To prove it was really him, and really alive — not a ghost or a dream.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did the disciples feel when they saw the Lord?’ (Very happy!) If silent: re-read the second scripture line together slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Through a locked door</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The doors were locked — but Jesus came in anyway. What does that tell us about the risen Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: his risen body is real but new and amazing; nothing can keep him out; he comes to his friends even when they’re hiding; their fear didn’t stop him; he knew exactly where they were. Follow-up: ‘What is the first word he said to scared people?’ (Peace.) Follow-up 2: ‘What “locked doors” do people hide behind today?’ (Fear, shame, sadness.) If silent: ask ‘If you were hiding and scared, what would you most want someone to say to you?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Locked Room</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Locked Room</b> (John 20:18–25). Jesus’ words, Mary’s ‘I have seen the Lord!’ and Thomas’ demand are from John 20 (CEV, slightly shortened). ‘Peace be with you!’ is the usual greeting many Bibles give for 20:19. Which disciple says which worried line, the knocking, and exactly when Thomas arrives are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Risen and alive — calm, warm, full of peace (the teacher may read Jesus’ lines)<br><b>Peter</b> — Big fisherman — jumpy and pacing<br><b>John</b> — Saw the empty tomb this morning — puzzled<br><b>Mary Magdalene</b> — Has seen the risen Jesus — bursting with news<br><b>Thomas</b> — Missed it all — and won’t believe without proof<br><b>Everyone</b> — Nervous whispers, knocking, gasps and cheers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: nervous whispers — ‘Shhh!’ · Everyone: knock on desks for the door · Everyone: a huge gasp — then a cheer<br>Props: Optional: a chair as the barred door · Optional: dim the lights<br>Cast a confident reader as Thomas — his line is the cliffhanger for Tuesday. Keep the ‘nail scars’ line matter-of-fact, not gruesome. Mary Magdalene’s visit to the disciples happened earlier that day (John 20:18); we bring it into the room for drama.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Sunday evening in Jerusalem. The door is locked and barred. The disciples huddle inside, whispering.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhhh! <i>(nervous whisper, look at the door)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Is the door locked? Check it again, John. <i>(pacing)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> It’s locked, Peter. And barred. Nobody is getting in here.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Knock! Knock! Knock! <i>(knock on desks)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Who’s there?! <i>(freeze, terrified)</i></li><li><b>Mary Magdalene:</b> It’s me — Mary! Let me in!</li><li><b>Mary Magdalene:</b> I have seen the Lord! <i>(burst in, breathless)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> The tomb was empty this morning. I saw it. But seeing HIM? <i>(unsure)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> The leaders killed Jesus. If they find us, we’re next. <i>(slump down)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Suddenly, Jesus appeared in the middle of the group.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> GASP! <i>(jump back)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Peace be with you! <i>(warm, arms open)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> Is it… a ghost? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus showed them his hands and his side.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Look at my hands. It’s really me. <i>(hold out hands gently)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! You locked the door because you were scared — and now Jesus is standing right here. What are you feeling? (20s)</li><li><b>Peter:</b> It’s you. It’s really you! <i>(fall to knees, laughing and crying)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When the disciples saw the Lord, they became very happy.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> He’s alive! He’s ALIVE! <i>(cheer)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> I am sending you, just as the Father has sent me.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then he breathed on them.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Receive the Holy Spirit.</li><li class='dir'>But one disciple had missed it all. Later, Thomas came back.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Knock! Knock! <i>(knock on desks)</i></li><li><b>Thomas:</b> Why is everyone smiling? What did I miss? <i>(suspicious)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> We have seen the Lord!</li><li><b>Thomas:</b> First, I must see the nail scars in his hands. I won’t believe unless I do! <i>(arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> To be continued…</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At the start everyone was scared. At the end they were cheering. What changed?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Nothing outside changed — the leaders were still dangerous. What changed was that they SAW Jesus was alive. When Jesus is with us, fear doesn’t get the last word.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What job did Jesus give his disciples — and what did he give them to help?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘I am sending you, just as the Father has sent me’ — and he breathed on them: ‘Receive the Holy Spirit’ (John 20:21–22).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Hard to believe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus had told his friends he would rise again. Mary said she had seen him. So why were they still hiding behind locked doors?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students disagree. Ideas: they were terrified of the leaders; dead people don’t come back, so it sounded impossible; they were too sad to hope; they hadn’t really understood what Jesus said; fear can be louder than good news. Follow-up: ‘Is it wrong to find good news hard to believe?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What finally changed their minds?’ (Jesus came to them himself.) If silent: ask ‘When you’re really scared, is it easy or hard to remember what someone told you?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is alive — and no locked door can keep him away from his friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, come into the places where I feel scared or stuck.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when you feel ‘locked in’ by worry — at bedtime, before a test, or when something at home feels hard — pray: “Jesus, you’re alive, and you’re here. Peace.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Practise the short prayer together once so students can use it straight away.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Locked in</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">They locked the doors — afraid.<br>“Peace be with you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Hebrews 12:1–2 (a shortened portion). Remind them Hebrews 11 was the Faith Museum from Week 1 — this verse comes straight after it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Help my unbelief (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we learn the prayer “Lord, help my unbelief.” In our Bible it’s “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more.” You can tell Jesus honestly when believing feels hard. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Imagine that locked room — and Jesus standing in the middle, saying ‘Peace.’ Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is alive — and locked doors can’t keep him out.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you beat death — you are alive! / Jesus, you came to your scared friends and brought peace.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus — you are alive, and you bring peace!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The disciples hid instead of trusting. Have you ever done that?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding from you when I’m scared. / Sorry for finding it hard to believe your good news.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we hid instead of trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for coming to his friends — and to us.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you showed them your hands — you really died and really rose. / Thank you for sending the Holy Spirit.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you are alive and with us by your Spirit.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is believing hard for you sometimes? Tell Jesus honestly.<br>Prompts: I do have faith — please help me to have even more. / Please come into the place where I feel locked in: …<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we do have faith — please help us to have even more.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reassure students that honest questions are welcome in prayer — Jesus came to doubting, scared people. Some students aren’t sure what they believe yet; invite them simply to listen or to pray, ‘Jesus, if you’re real, show me.’</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Thomas, who wasn’t there — his doubts, his demands, and the moment Jesus came back for him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: My Lord and My God!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 20:19–31; Hebrews 11:1; 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My Lord and My God!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Lord, help my unbelief” — A worried father once said to Jesus, “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more” (Mark 9:24; many Bibles say, “Lord, I believe — help my unbelief!”). You can bring your doubts and questions to Jesus honestly, and ask him to grow your faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Thomas doubted and how Jesus came back for him.</li><li>We are learning what Jesus meant by “The people who have faith in me without seeing me are the ones who are really blessed!”</li><li>I can explain why John wrote his book (John 20:31) and what it means for me.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Jesus treated Thomas’ doubts kindly, that Thomas responded ‘You are my Lord and my God!’, and that Jesus’ blessing is for people like us who haven’t seen him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper and pencils for the prayer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: My Lord and My God!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today is about doubt — make the room feel safe for honest questions.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Jesus came through a locked door.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did he show his disciples — and who missed it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>His hands and his side; he said ‘Peace’, sent them out and gave them the Holy Spirit. Thomas wasn’t there — and when the others said ‘We have seen the Lord!’, he said he wouldn’t believe unless he saw and touched the nail scars. ‘Today: what happened to Thomas.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: John 20:25–29</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:25</b> Thomas said, “First, I must see the nail scars in his hands and touch them with my finger… I won’t believe unless I do this!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:26–27</b> A week later… Jesus said to Thomas, “Put your finger here and look at my hands! Put your hand into my side. Stop doubting and have faith!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:28</b> Thomas replied, “You are my Lord and my God!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 20:29</b> Jesus said, “Thomas, do you have faith because you have seen me? The people who have faith in me without seeing me are the ones who are really blessed!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Shortened with ‘…’. ‘Blessed’ = deeply happy and favoured by God. John 20:29 is the heart of this week — we are the people who haven’t seen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The doubter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Thomas was one of Jesus’ twelve closest friends. John calls him ‘Thomas the Twin’. We don’t know who his twin was — but we do know a few things about Thomas.</p><p>He was brave. Once, when going back near Jerusalem was dangerous, Thomas said to the others, ‘Let’s go too, so we can die with him.’ And he was honest. When Jesus talked about going away, Thomas was the one who said, ‘Lord, we don’t even know where you are going! How can we know the way?’ Thomas didn’t pretend. If he didn’t understand, he said so.</p><p>So when the other disciples ran up to him, faces shining — ‘We have seen the Lord!’ — Thomas folded his arms.</p><p>‘First, I must see the nail scars in his hands and touch them with my finger. I must put my hand where the spear went into his side. I won’t believe unless I do this!’</p><p>Can you blame him? He had seen Jesus die. Dead people don’t come back. Maybe the others were so sad they were imagining things. Thomas wanted proof.</p><p>A whole week went by. Imagine that week for Thomas — everyone else full of joy, and him stuck outside it, wondering.</p><p>Then, a week later, the disciples were together again. This time Thomas was with them. The doors were locked — and once again, Jesus came and stood in the middle of the room. ‘Peace be with you!’</p><p>And then Jesus turned straight to Thomas. He knew exactly what Thomas had said, word for word. ‘Put your finger here and look at my hands! Put your hand into my side. Stop doubting and have faith!’</p><p>Jesus didn’t tell Thomas off. He didn’t say, ‘You should have known better.’ He came back for him — and offered him exactly the proof he had asked for.</p><p>The Bible never says Thomas touched him. He didn’t need to. He said, ‘You are my Lord and my God!’</p><p>That is one of the biggest things anyone says in the whole Bible. Thomas the doubter was the first of Jesus’ friends in John’s book to call him ‘my God’.</p><p>Then Jesus said something for everyone who wasn’t in that room. ‘Thomas, do you have faith because you have seen me? The people who have faith in me without seeing me are the ones who are really blessed!’</p><p>Who is Jesus talking about? He’s talking about us. We haven’t seen Jesus with our eyes. And John tells us that’s exactly why he wrote his book: so that you will put your faith in Jesus as the Messiah — God’s promised King — and the Son of God, and have true life.</p><p>Thomas saw, and believed. We read what Thomas saw — and we can believe too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “I won’t believe unless I see.” · 2. A whole week went by. · 3. Locked doors again. Then — Jesus. · 4. “You are my Lord and my God!” · 5. “…without seeing me…”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Thomas’ bravery: John 11:16. His honest question: John 14:5 (Jesus answers, ‘I am the way, the truth, and the life’). John 20:30–31 explains why John wrote his Gospel. That Thomas knelt is imagined (and shown in the art) — the text gives only his words. Church tradition says Thomas later took the good news as far as India (label it ‘tradition says’ if you mention it).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Word for word</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare what Thomas said with what Jesus said a week later. What do you notice?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Thomas said (20:25)</th><th>Jesus said (20:27)</th></tr><tr><td>“I must see the nail scars in his hands…”</td><td>“Look at my hands!”</td></tr><tr><td>“…and touch them with my finger.”</td><td>“Put your finger here.”</td></tr><tr><td>“I must put my hand where the spear went into his side.”</td><td>“Put your hand into my side.”</td></tr><tr><td>“I won’t believe unless I do this!”</td><td>“Stop doubting and have faith!”</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus heard every word Thomas said — and came back for him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. After the last row, ask: ‘Jesus wasn’t in the room when Thomas said this. How did he know?’ Let the penny drop — Jesus knows and hears us too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Thomas’ words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Thomas say when Jesus came back and showed him his hands?<br>A) “I knew it all along!” &nbsp; B) “You are my Lord and my God!” ✓ &nbsp; C) “I still don’t believe it.” &nbsp; D) “Where have you been all week?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (John 20:28). Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus say to Thomas straight after?’ (‘Do you have faith because you have seen me? The people who have faith in me without seeing me are the ones who are really blessed!’) Follow-up 2: ‘Why is calling Jesus “my God” such a big deal?’ (Thomas is saying Jesus is God himself.) If silent: point to the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Kind to doubters</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus could have told Thomas off. Instead he came back and offered him exactly what he asked for. What does that show about how Jesus treats people who have doubts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he is patient and kind; he doesn’t give up on people; he meets us where we are; he welcomes honest questions; he wants us to believe, not to feel ashamed; he still said ‘Stop doubting’, so he cares about our faith growing. Follow-up: ‘How is this like Jesus catching Peter last week?’ (He rescued first, then asked ‘Why do you doubt?’) Follow-up 2: ‘What could someone with questions about God do?’ If silent: ask ‘Imagine Jesus had ignored Thomas for ever. What would we think about Jesus then?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Doubting Thomas?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Thomas deserves the nickname ‘Doubting Thomas’.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let both sides speak. Agree: he did refuse to believe his friends for a whole week, and Jesus said ‘Stop doubting’. Disagree: he was brave (John 11:16), honest (John 14:5), and once he saw Jesus he made the biggest statement in the book — ‘My Lord and my God!’ Maybe ‘Believing Thomas’ fits better! Follow-up: ‘Is it better to hide your doubts, or bring them to Jesus?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What nickname would you give Thomas?’ If silent: ask ‘Which disciple said the greatest thing about Jesus in this story?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is my Lord and my God — and he is patient with my questions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lord, I do have faith — please help me to have even more.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write down one question you have about God or Jesus. This week, ask a parent, teacher, church leader or Christian friend about it — and tell Jesus about it in prayer too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Collect anonymous questions in a box if you like — they make a great Friday discussion. This leads into today’s writing prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Arms folded</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I won’t believe unless I do this!”<br>A week later, Jesus came back — for Thomas.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Hebrews 12:1–2. Ask: ‘Who is the crowd of witnesses?’ (The people from Hebrews 11 — our Faith Museum heroes.) ‘And who do we keep our eyes on?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a ‘help my unbelief’ prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thomas’ doubts didn’t stop Jesus coming to him. Today we write an honest prayer: what we believe, what we find hard, and asking Jesus to grow our faith. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write Thomas’ words at the top: ‘My Lord and my God.’ Now we’ll add a line for each part of our prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line telling Jesus who he is.<br>Prompts: You are my Lord and my God. / You are alive, and you know every word I say.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, you are our Lord and our God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there a time you didn’t trust what Jesus said?<br>Prompts: Sorry for saying ‘I won’t believe’ to you. / Sorry for trusting only what I can see.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we refused to trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line thanking Jesus for how he treated Thomas.<br>Prompts: Thank you for coming back for Thomas — and for me. / Thank you that I can bring you my questions.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you are patient with our questions.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your honest ‘help my unbelief’ line. What do you find hard to believe?<br>Prompts: I do have faith — please help me to have even more about… / Please help me trust you without seeing you.<br>Starter: <b>I do have faith! Please help me…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, we do have faith — please help us to have even more.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read your prayer silently to Jesus. Fold it up and keep it. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students don’t share these unless they want to. Students unsure about faith can write, ‘Jesus, if you are real, show me.’ That is an honest prayer too.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the Faith Museum opens its doors one last time — every exhibit from the term, and a brand-new wing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Faith Museum Returns":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 20:19–31; Hebrews 11:1; 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Faith Museum Returns</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Lord, help my unbelief” — A worried father once said to Jesus, “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more” (Mark 9:24; many Bibles say, “Lord, I believe — help my unbelief!”). You can bring your doubts and questions to Jesus honestly, and ask him to grow your faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how all of this term’s heroes trusted God, using Hebrews 11 and 12:1.</li><li>We are learning that the heroes of faith were ordinary people who trusted a trustworthy God.</li><li>I can describe my own ‘exhibit’ — one way I can trust God this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming several of the term’s heroes and what they trusted God for, explaining that the heroes weren’t perfect, and linking ‘a large crowd of witnesses’ to Hebrews 11.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch or lanyard for the museum guide</li><li>Optional: paper ‘picture frames’ for each student’s exhibit</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Faith Museum Returns</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Faith Museum from Week 1 returns. Remind the class it is an imaginary building — but the people in it are real.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: A statue in our classroom</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If we could put up a statue of ONE of this term’s faith heroes in our classroom, who would you choose?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What object would you put next to it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 4–5 answers and jot the heroes on the board — you’ll use the list in the role-play. Land it: ‘Remember the Faith Museum from Week 1? The guide has unlocked the doors one last time. Every one of your heroes has an exhibit now — and there’s a wing nobody has seen yet.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Hebrews 11:32–34, 39 · 12:1</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:32</b> What else should I say? There isn’t enough time to tell about Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, Samuel, and the prophets.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:33–34</b> They closed the jaws of lions and put out raging fires…</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 11:39</b> All of them pleased God because of their faith!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 12:1</b> Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us!</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Spot the heroes: Gideon (Week 3), Samuel (Week 4), the prophets (Elijah, Week 5), lions’ jaws (Daniel) and raging fires (Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, Week 6). Rahab is named in 11:31. ‘Witnesses’ = people who have seen and can tell what God did.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: One last tour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Do you remember the Faith Museum from the first week of term? The imaginary building with real exhibits from Hebrews 11? Today the guide has unlocked the doors one last time.</p><p>This time the great hall is full. Along the walls stand the exhibits from our whole term. Firewood and a ram’s horn: Abraham, who trusted that the LORD would provide. A red rope at a window: Rahab, who hid the spies, and Joshua, who marched round Jericho. A clay jar and a torch: Gideon, who felt small but found God was strong. A little hand-made coat: Hannah, who prayed and waited, and her son Samuel, who listened. A tiny cloud: Elijah, who kept praying for rain. A rope from a furnace and a stone from a lions’ den: Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego and Daniel. A golden sceptre: Esther. A hole in a roof and a soldier’s helmet: four friends and a Roman officer who brought their needs to Jesus. A wet footprint: Peter. And a door key: Thomas, who said, ‘My Lord and my God!’</p><p>The writer of Hebrews ran out of room too. ‘What else should I say? There isn’t enough time to tell about Gideon… Samuel, and the prophets.’ They closed the jaws of lions. They put out raging fires.</p><p>Now look closely at the people in the exhibits. Not one of them was perfect. Gideon was scared. Elijah got tired. Peter sank. Thomas doubted. The museum isn’t about amazing people. It’s about an amazing God who can be trusted — and ordinary people who trusted him.</p><p>Then the writer says something surprising: ‘All of them pleased God because of their faith!’ — yet many of them never saw the end of the story. They were waiting for something better: Jesus.</p><p>And then he turns the whole museum round. These people aren’t just pictures on a wall. ‘Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us!’ It’s as if they’re in the stands of a giant stadium, cheering on the people still running. Cheering on — us.</p><p>At the end of the hall is a new wing. The walls are covered with empty frames, each with a name underneath. And the guide holds up her lantern and smiles: ‘These are for you.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The museum doors open one last time. · 2. “There isn’t enough time…” · 3. Not perfect people. · 4. A large crowd of witnesses. · 5. A brand-new wing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The museum, guide and visitors are imagined (from Week 1). Hebrews 11 names some of our heroes (Abraham, Rahab, Gideon, Samuel) and hints at others (lions, fires); Hannah, Elijah, Esther, Peter and Thomas aren’t named there — the guide includes them because they trusted the same God. Hebrews 11:39–40 says they ‘died without being given what had been promised’ because God had ‘something better’ — Jesus. Hannah’s little coat: 1 Samuel 2:19.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The crowd</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish Hebrews 12:1: “Such a large crowd of… is all around us!”<br><i>Answer: “…witnesses” (Hebrews 12:1)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘Who are the witnesses?’ (All the faith heroes of Hebrews 11 — the people in our museum.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is a witness?’ (Someone who has seen something and can tell others it’s true — they saw God keep his promises.) If silent: point to the last scripture line and read it together, slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Faith Museum Returns</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Faith Museum Returns</b> (Hebrews 11:1–12:2). The museum, the guide and the three visitors are imagined (from Week 1). The lines from Hebrews 11–12 and John 20 are from the CEV; the exhibits are objects from each week’s Bible story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Guide</b> — The Faith Museum guide — cheerful, loves her exhibits<br><b>Tama</b> — A visitor who asks LOTS of questions<br><b>Ruby</b> — A visitor who only believes what she can see — changing her mind<br><b>Grandad</b> — Ruby’s grandad — slow, kind and wise<br><b>Everyone</b> — Creaky doors, museum echoes, a cheering crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: the creaky museum doors · Everyone: the museum echo ‘Ooooh…’ · Everyone: the cheering crowd of witnesses<br>Props: Optional: a torch for the guide’s lantern · Optional: an empty picture frame<br>If students named heroes in the hook, let them shout out the exhibit before the character says it. Esther’s ‘for such a time as this’ (Esther 4:14) and the Week 6 ‘even if he doesn’t’ (Daniel 3:18) are quoted in many Bibles — the CEV wording differs a little. Ruby isn’t forced to a decision; keep it gentle for students who aren’t sure what they believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Ten weeks later. The museum doors swing open one last time.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Creeeeeak… <i>(doors opening slowly)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Welcome back! The hall is full now. Every exhibit from your whole term. <i>(lift lantern)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> Firewood and a ram’s horn! That’s Abraham — the LORD will provide! <i>(run to the first case)</i></li><li><b>Grandad:</b> And a red rope in a window. Rahab trusted God — and Jericho’s walls fell down.</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> A clay jar and a torch. Gideon. He was scared, wasn’t he? <i>(lean in)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Very scared. But God made him strong.</li><li><b>Tama:</b> Hannah’s little coat! She prayed and waited. And Samuel said, ‘I’m listening.’</li><li><b>Grandad:</b> A tiny cloud in a frame. Elijah prayed and prayed — and down came the rain!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooooh… <i>(museum echo)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> They closed the jaws of lions and put out raging fires. <i>(point to a stone and a rope)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> Daniel! And Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego — ‘Even if he doesn’t’!</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> A golden sceptre. Esther. ‘For such a time as this.’</li><li><b>Grandad:</b> A hole in a roof, a soldier’s helmet — and a wet footprint. Who’s that? <i>(puzzled)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> Four friends, the Roman officer — and Peter, who walked on water!</li><li class='dir'>The last case holds a single door key.</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> Who’s this one? <i>(quietly)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> Thomas. He said, ‘I won’t believe unless I see.’</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> He was like me! He only believed what he could see. <i>(surprised)</i></li><li><b>Guide:</b> And Jesus came back for him. Thomas said, ‘You are my Lord and my God!’</li><li><b>Guide:</b> Then Jesus said: the people who have faith in me without seeing me are really blessed.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Ruby has never seen Jesus. Can she still trust him? What would you tell her? (20s)</li><li><b>Grandad:</b> None of these people were perfect, Ruby. They just trusted a God who keeps his promises.</li><li><b>Guide:</b> And now they’re not just in frames. Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hooray! Keep going! You can do it! <i>(stand and cheer like a stadium crowd)</i></li><li><b>Tama:</b> Who are they cheering for?</li><li><b>Guide:</b> For you. Come and see the new wing. <i>(open a door)</i></li><li class='dir'>Rows of empty frames — and under each one, a name.</li><li><b>Ruby:</b> That one says ‘Ruby’. <i>(point, slow smile)</i></li><li><b>Ruby:</b> I haven’t seen him. But I think… I want to trust him. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Keep your eyes on Jesus!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruby said Thomas was ‘like me’. Why do you think his exhibit helped her most?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Thomas had the same doubts — and Jesus didn’t reject him. It shows doubters are welcome and that faith can grow. Jesus’ blessing in John 20:29 is for people like Ruby who haven’t seen.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does Hebrews 12:1 call all the heroes in the museum?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘A large crowd of witnesses’ — all around us, like a stadium crowd cheering on the runners.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Never saw the ending</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hebrews 11 says many of these heroes died without being given everything God had promised. Why would they keep trusting God when they never saw the end of the story?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they knew God had kept promises before; they trusted who God is, not just what they could see; they believed God was planning ‘something better’ (Hebrews 11:40) — Jesus; faith is being sure of what we hope for (Hebrews 11:1 from Week 1). Follow-up: ‘How is that like us trusting Jesus without seeing him?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Is it harder to trust God when you can’t see the ending?’ If silent: ask ‘Would you plant a tree you knew you’d never sit under? Why might someone do that?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What they all shared</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our heroes were very different — young and old, rich and poor, brave and scared. What did they ALL have in common?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they trusted God and acted on it; none of them were perfect; they all faced something hard or scary; God was faithful to every one of them; their faith was in God, not in themselves. Follow-up: ‘Which hero surprised you most this term?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which hero is most like you — and why?’ If silent: list three very different heroes (old Abraham, young Esther, fisherman Peter) and ask what links them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: My own exhibit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The same God who was trustworthy for Abraham, Esther and Thomas is trustworthy for me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want my life to be an exhibit of trusting you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Design your own exhibit: draw one object that could go in your frame — a time you trusted God, or one thing you’ll trust him with this week at home, school or sport.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If time allows, give out paper ‘frames’ for students to draw their object in. Display them as ‘our new wing’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The new wing</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“These are for you.”<br>The museum isn’t finished yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Hebrews 12:1–2. Let students really ‘run on the spot’ for line four, then freeze and point up for ‘eyes on Jesus’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Standing with the crowd</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The heroes of faith are like a crowd cheering us on. Let’s stand together and pray — and bring God our faith and our doubts, like Thomas. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s stand up. Imagine the great crowd of witnesses all around us. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every hero trusted the same God. Say it together:<br>Prompts: God, you provided for Abraham and made Gideon strong. / Jesus, you are Lord and God — like Thomas said.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you were faithful to every one of them!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: the heroes weren’t perfect. Neither are we.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I trusted myself instead of you. / Sorry for giving up too quickly.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we didn’t trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one hero from the term who helped you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for (hero), who showed me… / Thank you for the people in my life who trust you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the great crowd of witnesses!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help you trust him without seeing — and to grow your faith.<br>Prompts: I do have faith — please help me to have even more. / Please help me keep my eyes on Jesus this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, we do have faith — please help us to have even more.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen! Please sit.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional. Encourage students to name a different hero each in the Thanks phase if they pray aloud.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: run the race with your eyes on Jesus — and our big Term 2 celebration!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Eyes on Jesus":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">John 20:19–31; Hebrews 11:1; 12:1–3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Eyes on Jesus</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Lord, help my unbelief” — A worried father once said to Jesus, “I do have faith! Please help me to have even more” (Mark 9:24; many Bibles say, “Lord, I believe — help my unbelief!”). You can bring your doubts and questions to Jesus honestly, and ask him to grow your faith.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Hebrews 12:1–3 means by running the race with our eyes on Jesus.</li><li>We are learning what this whole term has shown us about trusting God.</li><li>I can answer the term’s big question, ‘Can I trust God?’, using the term’s heroes and ‘God is…’ words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can put the term’s ten heroes in order, match several heroes to the term’s ‘God is…’ words, explain ‘keep our eyes on Jesus’, and recite Hebrews 12:1–2.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: the term’s ten ‘God is…’ words on cards</li><li>Optional: small cards for students’ faith statements</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Eyes on Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The final devotion of Term 2 — celebrate!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Ten heroes in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put the term’s ten faith heroes in order — Week 1 to Week 10!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Abraham — “The LORD will provide” → Rahab and Joshua — the walls fall down → Gideon — jars, torches and trumpets → Hannah and Samuel — praying and listening → Elijah — fire on the mountain → Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego and Daniel — “even if…” → Esther — “for such a time as this” → Four friends and the centurion — bringing needs to Jesus → Peter — walking on the water → Thomas — “My Lord and my God!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next hero; click to place it. For each one, ask: ‘What did they trust God for?’ Keep it quick — about 15 seconds per hero.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Hebrews 12:1–3</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 12:1</b> Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! So we must get rid of everything that slows us down, especially the sin that just won’t let go.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 12:1</b> And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 12:2</b> We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Hebrews 12:2</b> He endured the shame of being nailed to a cross, because he knew that later on he would be glad he did. Now he is seated at the right side of God’s throne!</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Sin’ = the wrong things we do that break our friendship with God. ‘Endured’ = put up with something hard all the way to the end. Verse 3 adds: ‘So keep your mind on Jesus… Then you won’t get discouraged and give up.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Run the race</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hebrews 12 takes us out of the museum and into a stadium. Picture a huge running track, and every seat packed. Abraham is there. Rahab. Gideon. Hannah and Samuel. Elijah. Daniel and his three friends. Esther. Peter. Thomas. A large crowd of witnesses — people who have already run their race — all cheering.</p><p>And who is on the track? We are.</p><p>The writer gives three pieces of running advice. First: get rid of everything that slows you down — especially sin, the wrong things that ‘just won’t let go’. Nobody runs a race carrying a heavy backpack. Second: be determined. Faith isn’t a quick sprint; it’s a long race. Third, and most important: keep your eyes on Jesus.</p><p>Not on the crowd. Not on the other runners. On Jesus. He leads us, because he ran the race first. And he makes our faith complete. When our faith is wobbly like Peter’s, or full of questions like Thomas’, Jesus is the one who finishes it.</p><p>How did Jesus run his race? All the way to the cross. He put up with the shame because he knew the joy that was coming — rescuing us. Now he is seated at the right side of God’s throne. He finished. He won.</p><p>So — our big question this term: Can I trust God? Look at the evidence. God provided for Abraham. He was with Joshua. He made Gideon strong. He listened to Hannah. He showed he was the only God on Elijah’s mountain. He was able to save in the fire. He was at work for Esther. He was compassionate to a man who couldn’t walk. He was Lord of the storm. And he came back for Thomas.</p><p>Yes. God is trustworthy. Keep your eyes on Jesus — and keep running.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Out of the museum — into a stadium. · 2. Three pieces of running advice. · 3. Jesus ran first. · 4. Can I trust God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. The ten ‘God is…’ words of the term are in the last paragraph — point to them on cards or the board if you have them. Hebrews 12:2 in many Bibles calls Jesus ‘the author and finisher (or perfecter) of our faith’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Eyes on…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to Hebrews 12:2, who must we keep our eyes on as we run the race?<br>A) The crowd in the stands &nbsp; B) The other runners &nbsp; C) Jesus ✓ &nbsp; D) Ourselves</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (Hebrews 12:2). Follow-up: ‘What two things does Jesus do for our faith?’ (He leads us and makes our faith complete.) Follow-up 2: ‘What must we get rid of because it slows us down?’ (Everything that slows us down, especially sin.) If silent: read the third scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God is…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term, each hero showed us something about God. With your partner, match as many heroes as you can to the term’s ‘God is…’ words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answers: Provider (Abraham, W1), With Us (Rahab and Joshua, W2), Strong (Gideon, W3), Listening (Hannah and Samuel, W4), The Only God (Elijah, W5), Able (Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego and Daniel, W6), At Work (Esther, W7), Compassionate (the four friends and the centurion, W8), Lord of the Storm (Peter, W9), Trustworthy (Thomas, W10). Follow-up: ‘Which one matters most to you right now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which story helped you see it best?’ If silent: give each pair two weeks to look back on.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: Term 2’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Faith is trusting God enough to act on what he says — even when we can’t see how it will work out.</li><li>From Abraham to Thomas, ordinary, imperfect people trusted God, and he never let them down.</li><li>We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — he leads us and makes our faith complete.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We haven’t seen Jesus, but we can trust him — and we’re surrounded by people who did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This recap covers the whole term. Then the Big Idea for Week 10 stamps in. The term’s big question was ‘Can I trust God?’ — let the class answer it out loud: ‘Yes!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What slows you down?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hebrews says some things slow us down in the race of faith, and some people get discouraged and give up. What slows kids down in following Jesus — and how does keeping your eyes on him help?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give 30 seconds of silent thinking, then take answers. Ideas: being too busy; screens; what friends think; doubts that we never talk about; a habit of sin like lying or meanness; disappointment when prayers aren’t answered how we hoped. Keeping our eyes on Jesus means remembering what he did, talking to him, reading about him, and trusting he finishes what he starts. Follow-up: ‘Which hero this term nearly gave up — and what helped?’ (Elijah, Gideon, Peter.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is one thing you could drop to run better?’ If silent: share an honest example of your own.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own one-sentence faith statement — like Thomas’ “My Lord and my God!” — and put it somewhere you’ll see it every day: on your bedroom wall, in your pencil case or on the fridge.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:2</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Term 3’s first Monday will check in on this. Give examples if needed: ‘I can trust God because he never let Abraham down.’ ‘Jesus, you are my Lord and my God.’ Students unsure about faith could write a question they want to keep exploring.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Such a large crowd of witnesses is all around us! And we must be determined to run the race that is ahead of us. We must keep our eyes on Jesus, who leads us and makes our faith complete.” — Hebrews 12:1–2 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Hebrews 12:1–2. Bonus round: can anyone still say Hebrews 11:1 from Week 1 — the sign above the museum door?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The finish line</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can I trust God?<br>Keep your eyes on Jesus — and keep running.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A term of trust</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish the term by praying out loud together — and using the ‘help my unbelief’ prayer we learned this week. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together to finish Term 2. In the Wow part, anyone can call out one of this term’s ‘God is…’ words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is — from any week this term!<br>Prompts: God, you are our Provider! You are With Us! You are Strong! / Jesus, you are Lord of the Storm — my Lord and my God!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are Provider, Strong, Able and Trustworthy!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there anything slowing you down that you want to give to God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the sin that won’t let go: … / Thank you that Jesus went to the cross to forgive me.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry — and we thank you that Jesus forgives us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the heroes — and for Jesus, who ran first.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the great crowd of witnesses. / Thank you, Jesus, that you finished the race for us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you lead us and make our faith complete!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for the holidays — and for faith that keeps running.<br>Prompts: I do have faith — please help me to have even more. / Please keep us safe in the holidays and help us keep our eyes on Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, we do have faith — please help us to have even more. Keep our eyes on Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Hebrews 12:1–2 together as our final prayer of Term 2. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate! Consider a small certificate for memory-verse champions or a display of the students’ ‘new wing’ exhibits from Wednesday.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Term 3: Having Faith in God — Jesus’ famous parables, starting with The Sower.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"Term 3":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: The Sower":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:1–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Generous</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our Father — Jesus taught us to start praying with two words: “Our Father.” We aren’t talking to a faraway boss. We’re talking to a God who is close, who listens, and who loves to hear from us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Farmer Went Out to Sow</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning the story Jesus told about a farmer and four kinds of ground.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Farmer Went Out to Sow → What does a seed need? → Matthew 13:3–9 → Jesus teaches from a boat → Four places → A strange ending → A Farmer Went Out to Sow → Which ground? → Head · Heart · Hands → Seed everywhere → Memory verse peek → Our Father (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Did Jesus Tell Stories?</b> · 17.5 min<br>We are learning what a parable is: an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Did Jesus Tell Stories? → Recap → Matthew 13:10, 13, 16, 19, 23 → An earthly story, a heavenly meaning → Who’s who? → What’s the seed? → Why a story? → Digging and pulling → Head · Heart · Hands → A hundred times over → Build the verse → Our Father (writing)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Garden Club Assembly</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story about the sower could happen in a school today.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Garden Club Assembly → Same message, different kids → Luke 8:11, 15 · Matthew 13:21–22 → A message at assembly → Who kept going? → The Garden Club Assembly → Roots → Whose fault? → Head · Heart · Hands → Aroha keeps going → Verse with actions → Our Father (open hands)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Be Good Soil</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the story of the sower shows us about God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Be Good Soil → Put the story in order → Luke 8:15 · Matthew 13:23 → What it shows about God → Good soil does… → Seed everywhere → This Week’s Big Idea → Born good? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Good soil → Our Father (line by line)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A Farmer Went Out to Sow":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:1–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Farmer Went Out to Sow</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our Father — Jesus taught us to start praying with two words: “Our Father.” We aren’t talking to a faraway boss. We’re talking to a God who is close, who listens, and who loves to hear from us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning the story Jesus told about a farmer and four kinds of ground.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus often taught with stories about everyday things.</li><li>I can retell what happened to the seed on the path, the rocks, the thorns and the good ground.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling all four kinds of ground in order, saying what happened to the seed in each, and noticing the surprising size of the harvest at the end.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a handful of seeds or dried beans to scatter on a tray</li><li>Optional: a small bag to be the farmer’s seed bag</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Farmer Went Out to Sow</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Welcome to Term 3! A new term and a new way of learning: this term Jesus himself tells the stories. Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: What does a seed need?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever planted a seed and waited for it to grow? What does a tiny seed need to become something big?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What could go wrong along the way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last term ended with Thomas saying to Jesus, “My Lord and my God!” Your challenge: write your own one-sentence faith statement and put it somewhere you’ll see it. Who wrote one — and where did you put it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students where their statement lives (fridge, bedroom wall, pencil case) and celebrate honest ‘I forgot’ answers too. Then welcome them to Term 3: ‘Having Faith in God — What does Jesus teach about life with God?’ This term Jesus himself tells stories, ten of them. Take answers about seeds: soil, water, sun, time, space, no birds. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about a farmer, a bag of seed and four very different kinds of ground. Listen for what happens to the seed.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:3–9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:3</b> Jesus used stories to teach them many things. He said: A farmer went out to scatter seed in a field.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:4</b> While the farmer was scattering the seeds, some of them fell along the road, and birds came and ate them.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:5–6</b> Other seeds fell on thin, rocky ground and soon started growing because the soil was not deep. But when the sun came up, the plants were scorched and dried up, because they had no roots.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:7</b> Some other seeds fell where thornbushes grew up and choked the plants.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:8–9</b> But some seeds fell on good ground where the plants produced a hundred or sixty or thirty times as much as was scattered. If you have ears, pay attention!</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read slowly, then ask the class to listen for the four places the seed lands. Matthew 13 is a whole chapter of Jesus’ stories; today we stay with the first one.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Jesus teaches from a boat</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture a lake — the Sea of Galilee — on a bright morning. Waves slap the pebbles, fishing boats bob, and all along the shore there are people. Hundreds of them. Farmers and fishermen, mums with babies on their hips, kids wriggling to the front, curious neighbours. Word had spread: Jesus is teaching today.</p><p>The crowd grew so big and so pushy that Jesus did something clever. He climbed into a fishing boat, pushed out a few metres from the shore and sat down to teach. The water carried his voice across the stones, and everyone could see him and hear him.</p><p>And he didn’t give a long speech. He told a story, about something every person on that beach knew well: a farmer sowing seed.</p><p>‘Listen!’ said Jesus. ‘A farmer went out to scatter seed in a field.’</p><p>In Jesus’ day, farmers didn’t plant seeds one at a time. They walked across the field with a bag at their hip and flung handfuls in great wide arcs, and ploughed it in afterwards. So seed landed everywhere.</p><p>Some fell on the path, the hard, trampled strip where people walked through the field. It just lay there on top, and down swooped the birds and gobbled it up. Gone.</p><p>Some fell on rocky ground, a thin layer of dirt over solid stone. It sprouted fast. Bright green shoots! But the soil wasn’t deep, so the roots had nowhere to go. When the hot sun came up, the little plants were scorched and shrivelled.</p><p>Some fell among thorns. The seed and the thorns started growing together, but the thorns grew faster and taller, and choked the baby plants until they gave up.</p><p>Three lots of seed wasted. You’d think this was going to be a very sad farming story.</p><p>But then some seed landed on good ground, soft and deep and ready. And those plants grew strong, and produced a harvest. One seed became thirty. Or sixty. Or even a hundred!</p><p>Then Jesus said something short and strange: ‘If you have ears, then pay attention!’</p><p>And that was it. No explanation. Some people nodded. Some looked puzzled. Some wandered home thinking about farming. But a few wondered, ‘Wait. Is this story about more than seeds?’</p><p>Jesus told lots of stories like this. They’re called parables: earthly stories with a heavenly meaning. Tomorrow we’ll find out what this one really means.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A huge crowd by the lake. · 2. “A farmer went out to scatter seed.” · 3. Seed everywhere! · 4. Three lots of seed lost. · 5. A harvest of a hundred times over!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Broadcast sowing (scattering by hand, then ploughing the seed in) was normal in Galilee, and paths often ran right through fields. Jesus teaching from a boat is in Matthew 13:1–2. Only introduce the word ‘parable’ lightly today (earthly story, heavenly meaning); Tuesday teaches it properly. Don’t explain the four grounds yet.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Four places</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to the seed that fell on the hard path?<br>A) It grew into a tall plant &nbsp; B) Birds came and ate it ✓ &nbsp; C) The sun scorched it &nbsp; D) Thorns choked it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Matthew 13:4). Follow-up: ‘Where did the other seeds land, and what happened to each?’ (Rocks: scorched. Thorns: choked. Good ground: a harvest.) Follow-up 2: ‘Which place would you have worried about most?’ If silent: re-read the second scripture line and let students say the answer together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A strange ending</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus ended with “If you have ears, pay attention!” Everyone on that beach had ears. What do you think he meant?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: not just hearing sounds but really listening; the story has a hidden meaning; he wants them to think about it and ask; hearing is easy, listening is harder. Follow-up: ‘When is hearing different from listening in your life?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why might Jesus tell a story instead of just giving instructions?’ If silent: ask ‘Is it possible to hear your teacher and still not listen?’ and let them laugh first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: A Farmer Went Out to Sow</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>A Farmer Went Out to Sow</b> (Matthew 13:3–9). Jesus’ words are from Matthew 13:3–9 (CEV, lightly shortened). The farmer, the Listener and Peter’s whispered comments are imagined to help us act out the story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Teaching from the boat — calm and clear<br><b>Farmer</b> — Hardworking, hopeful, easily surprised<br><b>Peter</b> — A fisherman, one of Jesus’ closest friends<br><b>Listener</b> — A crowd member who wanted a miracle<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd, the birds, the plants and the thorns</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (crowd): a hushed ‘shhh’ as Jesus begins · Everyone (birds): flap your arms and squawk · Everyone (plants): sink slowly down as the sun scorches · Everyone (thorns): wiggle your fingers and squeeze in close<br>Props: Optional: a small bag or scarf for the farmer to ‘scatter’ · Optional: a handful of scrunched paper as seed<br>Peter and the Listener are imagined; Jesus’ words are from Matthew 13. Cast confident readers as Jesus and the Narrator. Let Everyone be big and noisy, then quiet again for Jesus’ lines. The farmer is not Jesus’ character, just a story character, so there’s no need to explain who he stands for yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A pebbly lakeshore in Galilee. A huge crowd sits waiting. Jesus climbs into a boat and begins to teach.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Murmur, murmur, murmur… shhhh! <i>(crowd settling down)</i></li><li><b>Listener:</b> A story about farming? I was hoping for a miracle. <i>(grumble)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Shh! Listen. Jesus is teaching. <i>(elbow the Listener)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Listen! A farmer went out to scatter seed in a field.</li><li><b>Farmer:</b> Round and round goes my arm! Seed for the field, seed for tomorrow’s bread! <i>(big scattering arm)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> While the farmer was scattering the seeds, some of them fell along the road, and birds came and ate them.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Squawk! Squawk! Gobble, gobble! <i>(birds swooping, arms flapping)</i></li><li><b>Farmer:</b> Hey! That seed was meant for bread! <i>(wave arms at the birds)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Other seeds fell on thin, rocky ground and soon started growing, because the soil was not deep.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Up pop the green shoots. They look strong. They look bright. They look perfect.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> But when the sun came up, the plants were scorched and dried up, because they had no roots.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ohhhh… wilt… <i>(slowly sink down like wilting plants)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Some other seeds fell where thornbushes grew up and choked the plants.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Squeeze! Squeeze! Squeeze! <i>(wiggle fingers like thorns, crowding in)</i></li><li><b>Farmer:</b> Three handfuls gone! Should I just give up and go home? <i>(scratch head)</i></li><li><b>Listener:</b> Don’t stop now! You’ve still got one handful left! <i>(stand up, getting interested)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Three handfuls wasted. What do you think will happen to the last handful? (20s)</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> But some seeds fell on good ground where the plants produced a hundred or sixty or thirty times as much.</li><li><b>Farmer:</b> A hundred times?! From ONE little seed?! <i>(fall on knees, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> A hundred! Sixty! Thirty! <i>(cheer, arms up like tall wheat)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> If you have ears, then pay attention!</li><li><b>Listener:</b> I heard every word. So why don’t I understand it? <i>(scratch head)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Stay close to him. I think this story is about more than seeds.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus smiled. Some would understand soon. Some would need to ask. And the seed was still being scattered.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> If you have ears, pay attention!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to the seed in each of the four places?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Path: birds ate it. Rocky ground: scorched, no roots. Thorns: choked. Good ground: a harvest of a hundred, sixty or thirty times as much (Matthew 13:4–8).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The farmer kept scattering seed even though so much was wasted. What was that like?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Good answers: generous; hopeful; not stingy; he didn’t only throw seed where it was sure to grow. Hold this thought: Thursday shows what this tells us about God.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which ground?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus didn’t say what the story meant. What do you think the seed, the farmer and the four kinds of ground might stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take guesses without correcting: seed might be words or a message; ground might be people; birds might be distractions; the farmer might be God or Jesus. Praise careful thinking. Follow-up: ‘What clue in the story makes you think that?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which of the four places would you guess is most like a normal person?’ If silent: ask ‘If the ground was people, what would the seed be?’ Tomorrow Jesus explains.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus tells stories about everyday things to help us understand what God is like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to really listen when I hear something good.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, when a teacher or parent says something important, stop for three seconds, look at them and listen all the way to the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A small first step: practise being a ‘good listener’ before we find out why Jesus cared about this.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Seed everywhere</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A farmer went out to scatter seed in a field.”<br>“If you have ears, pay attention!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 8:15. It’s Luke’s version of the end of this story: say it line by line and let the class echo you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our Father (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we’ll learn the prayer Jesus taught, one phrase a week. Today’s phrase: “Our Father.” Prayer starts by remembering who we’re talking to: a God who is close and loves to listen. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Close your eyes or look at the floor. Imagine the farmer’s hand flinging seed, wide and free. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The farmer scattered seed everywhere, even on hard places. Think about a God who gives that freely.<br>Prompts: Father, you give good things to everyone. / Father, you are generous. Your message is for the whole crowd.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Father, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Father, you are generous to everyone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The path, the rocks, the thorns. Which one is closest to your heart today?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I hear you but don’t really listen. / Sorry for letting busyness and worry crowd you out.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, I’m sorry for the times I haven’t listened.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone who has scattered good words into your life.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who have told me about you. / Thank you for your word, your seed, given to us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Father, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, for the good words you have given us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask your Father for soft, deep soil in your heart.<br>Prompts: Father, please make my heart soft soil for your word. / Please help me really listen this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, please help me to listen to you this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first. Some children may not have a good experience of a father at home; gently say that God is the kind of Father every child deserves, perfectly loving and always listening, and that other words (like ‘God’) are fine.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story, who was listening, and what the seed and the soil really stand for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Did Jesus Tell Stories?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">17.5 minutes (18.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:1–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Did Jesus Tell Stories?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our Father — Jesus taught us to start praying with two words: “Our Father.” We aren’t talking to a faraway boss. We’re talking to a God who is close, who listens, and who loves to hear from us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what a parable is: an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</li><li>We are learning who heard the story of the sower and what Jesus said it meant.</li><li>I can say who or what the seed and the four kinds of ground stand for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students defining a parable in their own words, matching the seed to God’s message and the soils to different hearts, and noticing the surprises (a quiet kingdom, an enormous harvest).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a tray of soil with a seed in it to hold up during the story</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Did Jesus Tell Stories?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard a story about a farmer, a bag of seed and four kinds of ground.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to the seed in each place? Who can remember all four?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Path (birds ate it), rocky ground (scorched, no roots), thorns (choked), good ground (a hundred, sixty or thirty times as much). Then: ‘Jesus ended with “If you have ears, pay attention!” and left everyone wondering. So his friends asked him the question we’re asking today: what does it mean?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:10, 13, 16, 19, 23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:10</b> His disciples came and asked, “Why do you use stories to teach the people?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:13</b> Jesus answered, “I use stories because they look, but don’t really see. They listen, but they don’t hear or understand.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:16</b> “But God has blessed you, because your eyes can see and your ears can hear.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:19</b> “The seeds that fell along the road are the people who hear the message about God’s kingdom but don’t understand it.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:23</b> “The seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear and understand the message. They produce a harvest.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Kingdom = the place where God is King: wherever people trust and follow him. ‘Disciples’ = Jesus’ close friends and students. Verses selected and shortened.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: An earthly story, a heavenly meaning</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard a story about a farmer and four kinds of ground. Today we find out why Jesus told it, and what it means.</p><p>First: what is a parable? A parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. The story is about everyday things like seeds, sheep, coins and bread, but it points to something much bigger: God and his kingdom. The kingdom just means the place where God is King, wherever people trust and follow him.</p><p>Who was listening? A huge crowd by the lake: ordinary people, farmers, fishermen, mums and dads, kids. Some had come to be healed. Some were just curious. Some of the religious leaders were already deciding they didn’t like Jesus. And there were his closest friends, the disciples.</p><p>That evening the disciples came to Jesus, puzzled. ‘Why do you tell stories?’ they asked.</p><p>Jesus said a story works like a seed. It lands in you and you have to decide what to do with it. Some people look but never really see, and listen but never really hear. But to his friends he said, ‘God has blessed you, because your eyes can see and your ears can hear.’ Then he told them what the story meant.</p><p>The farmer is the one who spreads God’s message, and that day it was Jesus himself. The seed is the message about God’s kingdom. And the four kinds of ground are four kinds of hearts.</p><p>The path: people who hear the message but don’t understand it, and it’s snatched away before it can sink in.</p><p>The rocky ground: people who are gladly excited at first but have no roots. When life gets hard, or friends laugh at them, they give up.</p><p>The thorns: people who hear the message, but worries and wanting more stuff crowd it out.</p><p>The good ground: people who hear, understand and hold on, and a harvest grows.</p><p>Now here’s the twist. Many people expected God’s kingdom to arrive with a bang: a mighty king, an army, the Romans kicked out. Jesus said it arrives like a seed. Small. Quiet. Easy to miss. And whether it grows depends on the ground, which means the heart.</p><p>The second twist is the harvest. Even with three-quarters of the seed seemingly wasted, the farmer still gets thirty, sixty, a hundred times what he planted. God’s word is powerful.</p><p>And one more thing: soil can change. Paths can be dug up, rocks cleared, thorns pulled. Jesus isn’t sorting people into good and bad ground forever. He’s asking: what kind of ground am I today?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. What is a parable? · 2. Who was listening? · 3. Who’s who? · 4. The twist: a hundred times over. · 5. Soil can change.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. The ‘evil one’ in Matthew 13:19 is Satan, God’s enemy; we say ‘the message is snatched away’ and keep it low-key. The religious leaders had already begun opposing Jesus (Matthew 12). A hundredfold harvest was astonishing and in the Old Testament is a sign of God’s special blessing (Genesis 26:12). Be gentle: no child should leave feeling labelled as a path or a rock. Jesus’ own explanation (13:18–23) is the source for the ‘who’s who’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus explained his story. Match each thing in the story to what it stands for.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>The seed</td><td>God’s message about his kingdom</td></tr><tr><td>The hard path: birds eat the seed</td><td>Hears the message but doesn’t understand it, and it’s snatched away</td></tr><tr><td>Rocky ground: no roots</td><td>Glad at first, but gives up when life gets hard</td></tr><tr><td>Thorns that choke</td><td>Worries and wanting more stuff crowd the message out</td></tr><tr><td>Good ground: a harvest</td><td>Hears, understands and holds on, so a harvest grows</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Same seed, four kinds of hearts.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row and ask the class to guess the right-hand side first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What’s the seed?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ explanation, what does the seed stand for?<br>A) Money &nbsp; B) The message about God’s kingdom ✓ &nbsp; C) The little plants &nbsp; D) The birds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Matthew 13:19). Follow-up: ‘What do the four kinds of ground stand for?’ (Four kinds of people’s hearts as they hear the message.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to the seed on the good ground?’ (A harvest.) If silent: point to the fourth scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why a story?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus could have just explained everything straight away. Why do you think he told a story first?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: a story sticks in your memory; it makes you think and ask; it shows who is really keen to understand (the disciples asked!); it uses everyday things we already know; a story is like a seed itself, it has to land in us. Follow-up: ‘Which stories from school do you still remember?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did the disciples do that the crowd didn’t?’ (They asked.) If silent: ask ‘Do you remember a list of facts, or a good story?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Digging and pulling</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Soil can change: paths can be dug up, rocks cleared, thorns pulled out. What might that look like for a real heart on a busy school day?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: put the phone or game away so you can listen; ask a question when you don’t understand; let someone help; say sorry; make a plan to keep going (roots); give a worry to God instead of carrying it. Note: we can’t do it all alone, and we can ask God’s help. Follow-up: ‘Who or what can help us pull out thorns?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which would be hardest for you?’ If silent: ask ‘What is the “birds” in your day?’ and let them name distractions.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–13:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning, and the seed is God’s message.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want my heart to be good soil. Help me to listen and understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose one ‘bird’ that steals your attention (a screen, a game, a chatty friend) and put it away for five minutes tonight when someone is talking to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is a first pass. Thursday’s challenge builds on it. Keep it small and doable.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A hundred times over</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“…a hundred or sixty or thirty times as much.”<br>A tiny seed. A quiet kingdom. A harvest beyond belief.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 8:15 chunk by chunk. Ask: which words describe good soil?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our Father (writing)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–18:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we write our prayer. Start with the two words Jesus taught: “Our Father.” Writing slows our thoughts down, and God reads every word, even the messy ones. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take paper or a mini-whiteboard. At the top, write: ‘Our Father…’ Then take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about what the sower is like.<br>Prompts: Father, you never stop scattering your word. / Father, you are generous to people who ignore you.<br>Starter: <b>Father, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Our Father, you scatter your word everywhere!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: which ground is hardest for you, the path, the rocks or the thorns?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’m too busy to listen. / Sorry for the thorns of worry and wanting more stuff.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry for the times we haven’t listened.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line of thanks about the harvest.<br>Prompts: Thank you that your word grows more than we expect. / Thank you that Jesus explains his stories to people who ask.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Father, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, that your word grows!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one request to be good soil.<br>Prompts: Please help me ask when I don’t understand. / Please help me pull out my worries and put down roots.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, please make our hearts good soil.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold up your prayer or read it quietly to God. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Collect a few volunteers’ lines aloud. Let students who prefer not to write draw a picture of good soil instead.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the same story, told in a modern New Zealand school, with four kids who hear one message and respond very differently.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Garden Club Assembly":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:1–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Garden Club Assembly</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our Father — Jesus taught us to start praying with two words: “Our Father.” We aren’t talking to a faraway boss. We’re talking to a God who is close, who listens, and who loves to hear from us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story about the sower could happen in a school today.</li><li>We are learning why the same message can grow in one person and not in another.</li><li>I can name something that stops me listening, and one thing that helps me keep going.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Jun to the path, Sione to the rocks, Mia to the thorns and Aroha to the good ground, and explaining that the difference was in the soil, not the message.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a tea towel for Mrs Hohepa’s gardening scarf</li><li>Optional: a watering can for Aroha</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Garden Club Assembly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Same message, different kids</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine your school has a visitor at assembly with one big, exciting idea. Will everyone do something about it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think will actually happen to the idea by next week?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 3–4 answers: some will forget, some start and quit, some are too busy, some follow through. Land it: ‘Jesus’ story about the sower isn’t just for farmers 2,000 years ago. Today we’ll see what it looks like in a school in Aotearoa, with four kids who hear exactly the same words.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 8:11, 15 · Matthew 13:21–22</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 8:11</b> This is what the story means: The seed is God’s message.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:21</b> They don’t have any roots, and they don’t last long. As soon as life gets hard or the message gets them in trouble, they give up.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:22</b> They start worrying about the needs of this life and are fooled by the desire to get rich. So the message gets choked out.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 8:15</b> But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 8:15 is our memory verse. Matthew 13:21–22 are Jesus’ words about the rocky ground and the thorns, which we’ll see in Sione and Mia today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A message at assembly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It’s Monday morning at assembly, and the whole school is crammed into the hall. Year 1s sit on the floor at the front, Year 8s are squashed at the back. On the stage stands a visitor in gumboots: Mrs Hohepa, who runs the community garden down the road.</p><p>‘Kia ora, everyone!’ she says. ‘Our school has a brand-new vegetable garden, and I need helpers. Here’s the deal. Everyone who joins gets one seedling. Water it every single day this term, even when it’s wet, even when it’s boring, even when your friends are playing. Do that, and by the end of term you’ll be eating your own kai.’</p><p>Four kids in the same row heard exactly the same words.</p><p>Jun was fiddling with a yo-yo under his jacket, and he missed almost all of it. By morning tea he’d forgotten the whole thing. ‘Garden? What garden?’ The message was heard, but it never sank in. Like seed on the path, it was gone before it could take root.</p><p>Sione heard it and lit up. ‘I’m in! Best gardener ever!’ For two days he watered his seedling like a champion. On day three it rained, his mates were playing footy, and somebody laughed, ‘Gardening? Really?’ He skipped one day, then another. Lots of excitement, but no roots.</p><p>Mia wanted to join. She really did. But then she thought about her spelling test, her art entry, piano and the group project. ‘There’s just no room.’ Her worries grew taller than her good intentions. Thorns.</p><p>And Aroha? She listened hard. At lunch she signed up and wrote WATER IT on her hand. Every morning before the bell, rain or shine, she knelt in the garden with a watering can. Some days it was boring. One day a classmate teased her. She kept going.</p><p>Three weeks later her seedling was thick and green, and kids wandered over to have a look.</p><p>Same message. Same assembly. Four different responses. The difference wasn’t the message. It was the soil.</p><p>But here’s the good bit: there are still seedlings left in the garden, and nobody is ever too late to start.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Monday morning assembly. · 2. “Water your seedling every day.” · 3. Four responses. · 4. Three weeks later… · 5. The soil made the difference.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The school, Mrs Hohepa and the Garden Club are imagined, a modern ‘earthly story’ that mirrors Jesus’ one. Kia ora = hello. Kai = food. Portray all four children kindly: nobody is the bad kid. Each one is a normal way hearts respond to a message.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who kept going?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the story, which kid watered her seedling every day, even when it was wet or boring?<br>A) Jun &nbsp; B) Sione &nbsp; C) Mia &nbsp; D) Aroha ✓</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: D, Aroha. Follow-up: ‘What did she write on her hand?’ (WATER IT.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to Sione’s seedling, and why?’ (He quit when it got hard, so it had no ‘roots’.) If silent: ask who in the story wrote on her hand and let them find the name on the slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Garden Club Assembly</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Garden Club Assembly</b> (Matthew 13:3–9, 18–23). This is a modern retelling in Aotearoa New Zealand of Jesus’ story of the sower. The school, Mrs Hohepa and the four children’s conversations are all imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mrs Hohepa</b> — Visiting gardener — warm and a bit funny<br><b>Aroha</b> — Listens carefully and keeps going<br><b>Sione</b> — Big-hearted, jumps in fast, loves footy<br><b>Mia</b> — Kind and creative, but always worrying<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky, easily distracted, loves his yo-yo<br><b>Everyone</b> — The assembly crowd, school bell, mates and thorns</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone (assembly): shuffle, cough and whisper, then go quiet · Everyone (school bell): ‘Brrrring!’ · Everyone (thorns): wiggle fingers and crowd round Mia · Everyone (mates): call out ‘Footy!’<br>Props: Optional: a watering can · Optional: a yo-yo for Jun · Optional: a pen to write on Aroha’s hand<br>All four children are shown with warmth: nobody is ‘the bad one’, and Sione, Jun and Mia all get a second chance. Play the ending as hopeful. Mrs Hohepa is imagined. Everyone’s parts make for lots of noise, so give them a clear ‘freeze’ signal.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Monday morning assembly. The hall is hot and crowded. A visitor in gumboots steps onto the stage.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shuffle, shuffle, cough, whisper… <i>(assembly noises, then silence)</i></li><li><b>Mrs Hohepa:</b> Kia ora, everyone! Our school has a brand-new garden, and I need helpers.</li><li><b>Mrs Hohepa:</b> Everyone who joins gets a seedling. Water it every single day this term, even when it’s wet and even when it’s boring.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Watch this! I’m about to do walk-the-dog… <i>(yo-yo clatters on the floor)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Jun, shh! Did you hear any of that? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Any of what? I nearly did walk-the-dog!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Brrrring! <i>(the school bell: everyone leaps up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> By morning tea, Jun’s head is full of yo-yos. Not one word about the garden has stuck.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> A garden? I’m in! I’m going to grow the biggest pumpkin in New Zealand! <i>(fist pump)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Day one, Sione waters like a champion. Day two, too. Day three, it rains.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Sione! Footy! Come on! <i>(his mates, calling from far away)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Gardening in the rain? Nah. Just this once. <i>(drop the watering can)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> I’d love to join, but there’s my spelling test, my art entry, piano, the group project…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Worry, worry, worry! <i>(wiggle fingers like thorns around Mia)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> There’s just no room. Maybe next term. <i>(sigh)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Mrs Hohepa, what if it’s too wet? Or too dry? Or really boring?</li><li><b>Mrs Hohepa:</b> Then you water it anyway. A little every day beats a lot once.</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Every day. Got it. <i>(write WATER IT on her hand)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Same assembly, four kids. Which one are you most like in a normal week? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Three weeks later. A drizzly Tuesday. Aroha is kneeling in the garden with her watering can.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Aroha! Your seedling is huge! Mine’s… kind of crispy. <i>(hold up a droopy brown stalk)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I just kept going. Even when it rained. Even when it was boring. <i>(shrug and smile)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Wait. That garden thing was in assembly? I totally missed it!</li><li><b>Mia:</b> I wish I’d said yes. But I’m so behind on everything now. <i>(sad)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> There are still seedlings left. Mrs Hohepa says anyone can start today.</li><li><b>Mrs Hohepa:</b> The seed is good. The garden is ready. Who wants to dig in?</li><li><b>Sione:</b> I’ll start again, and this time I’ll water it even in the rain! <i>(roll up sleeves)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Okay, I’m listening this time. What’s the plan? <i>(put the yo-yo in his pocket)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Dig in! <i>(cheer, fists in the air)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mrs Hohepa gave the same message to all four. Why did it grow in Aroha but not in the others?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The difference wasn’t the message or the teacher. It was how each one received it: Jun was distracted, Sione had no roots, Mia’s worries crowded it out, Aroha listened and kept going.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was different about what Aroha did?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She listened carefully, wrote it down, and watered every day, even when it was wet or boring. She kept going.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Roots</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–12:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sione started stronger than anyone, yet his seedling didn’t make it. Why do you think big excitement wasn’t enough?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: excitement fades when it gets hard; he had no ‘roots’ like the plants on rocky ground; he gave up when his mates laughed or it rained; keeping on matters more than starting fast; roots grow slowly, day by day. Follow-up: ‘What helps a person grow roots?’ (Habits, friends who encourage, remembering why.) Follow-up 2: ‘Have you ever started something strong and quit?’ If silent: ask them to think of a hobby they tried and dropped.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Whose fault?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:30–14:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mrs Hohepa gave the same message to everyone, but only Aroha’s seedling grew. Is it Mrs Hohepa’s fault, the seed’s fault, or the kids’ fault? What does that say about hearing God’s message?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: not the message or the teacher (the farmer did his job); the ground matters; but it’s not that Jun, Sione and Mia are ‘bad’. They can start again; hearts can change; we can’t blame the seed. Follow-up: ‘What would the farmer say?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could help Jun, Sione and Mia be good soil next time?’ If silent: ask ‘Is it fair to say one of them was just a worse person?’ (No: soil can change.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The same message lands differently depending on the heart that hears it, and soil can change.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Father, make my heart good soil.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one good thing you hear this week (a Bible verse, or a good word from a teacher or parent), write it on your hand or in your planner, and do one small thing about it before bed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Preview Thursday’s challenge. Students who aren’t church kids can use something good a teacher says.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Aroha keeps going</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“A little every day beats a lot once.”<br>She listened. She kept going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 8:15. Let students vote on their favourite action.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our Father (open hands)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open hands can show a heart that’s ready to receive. Hold your hands out, palms up, like soft soil waiting for rain. Palms down is for putting worries down. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably and rest your hands on your knees. When I say, turn your palms up. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up. Say it with me in your head: ‘Our Father.’<br>Prompts: Father, you give seed and rain and sunshine to everyone. / Father, you are patient with people who start again.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Father, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Our Father, you are generous and patient!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms down. Which is hardest for you: forgetting like Jun, quitting like Sione, or worrying like Mia?<br>Prompts: Sorry for forgetting what I hear. / Sorry for quitting when it gets hard.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry for forgetting, quitting and worrying.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up. Thank God for a second chance.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I can start again. / Thank you for people like Mrs Hohepa who help us grow.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Father, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, for second chances.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up. Ask for roots, and a heart with room for him.<br>Prompts: Please help me keep going on the boring, wet days. / Please take my worries and make room in my heart for you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, please help me listen and keep going.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, as if holding a seed. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Palms-up and palms-down are optional; students can simply sit still. Keep it calm.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God, and what it means to be good soil. Plus this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Be Good Soil":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:1–23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Be Good Soil</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our Father — Jesus taught us to start praying with two words: “Our Father.” We aren’t talking to a faraway boss. We’re talking to a God who is close, who listens, and who loves to hear from us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the story of the sower shows us about God.</li><li>We are learning what ‘good soil’ looks like: listen, hold on, keep going.</li><li>I can say Luke 8:15 and choose one way to be good soil this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the four kinds of ground in order, say that the sower’s generosity shows what God is like, and name one specific way they’ll listen, hold on and keep going this week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: a pot of soil and a seed to plant for the classroom</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Be Good Soil</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put the story in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Jesus’ story of the sower in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The farmer scatters seed → Birds eat the seed on the path → Plants wither on the rocky ground → Thorns choke the plants → Good ground grows a huge harvest → “If you have ears, pay attention!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Ask: ‘How many of the four places did the seed fail?’ (Three.) ‘And what happened in the last one?’ (A harvest!)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 8:15 · Matthew 13:23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 8:15</b> But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:23</b> “The seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear and understand the message. They produce a harvest.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 8:15 is our memory verse. Notice the three things good soil does: hear it, keep it, last.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: What it shows about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today we ask the big question of the week: what does Jesus’ story show us about God?</p><p>Look at the farmer. He is incredibly generous. He doesn’t only throw seed on the best soil. He throws it everywhere: the path, the rocks, the thorns. He doesn’t sort people first. In Jesus’ story, God keeps scattering his word over and over, to everyone, even to people who might ignore it, even to us on our grumpy days. Jesus himself was scattering that seed to the crowd on the lake, and he has never stopped.</p><p>Look at the seed, too. God’s word is powerful. One small seed can become a hundred.</p><p>And look at what Jesus asks of us. Luke tells this story and ends it like this: the seed on good ground stands for people who ‘hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts’. Listen. Hold on. Keep going. Good soil isn’t a better kind of person. It’s a heart that stays open, soft and deep. Like Aroha with her watering can, or Mia on the beach with her Bible and her sketchbook, taking a few quiet minutes to listen.</p><p>But what if your heart feels like a hard path today? Soil can change. Farmers dig, water and pull out weeds. And the One who sows is also the One who can soften hard ground. Jesus came to scatter God’s life everywhere, and he went all the way to the cross so even hard hearts could be made new. That’s why we can pray, ‘Father, make my heart good soil.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The farmer scatters everywhere. · 2. The seed is powerful. · 3. Good soil: hear it, keep it, last. · 4. And if my heart feels hard?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes (roughly 280 words). This is the ‘heavenly meaning’ day. Be gentle with students who feel like the path or the thorns: soil can change, and God helps with that. If asked: the ‘farmer’ in Jesus’ explanation is the one spreading God’s message, which Jesus himself was doing that day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Good soil does…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to Luke 8:15, what do people who are like good ground do with the message?<br>A) Forget it quickly &nbsp; B) Hear it and keep it in good and honest hearts ✓ &nbsp; C) Argue with it &nbsp; D) Write it on the wall</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 8:15). Follow-up: ‘What else does Luke say good soil does?’ (They last and produce a harvest.) Follow-up 2: ‘Which word in that verse is about not giving up?’ (Last.) If silent: read the first scripture line together and let students find the answer in the words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Seed everywhere</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The farmer threw seed on the path, the rocks and the thorns, not only on the best soil. What does that show us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is generous; he gives everyone a chance; he doesn’t give up on people; his word is for everyone, not only ‘good’ people; he keeps scattering even when some ignore it. Follow-up: ‘Would you have kept scattering after three failures?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is that like how Jesus treats people?’ If silent: ask ‘What if the farmer only threw seed on the best soil, who would miss out?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This Week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>A parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. The seed is God’s message, and the ground is our hearts.</li><li>God is generous: he scatters his word on everyone, and it grows a harvest where hearts listen, hold on and keep going.</li><li>Soil can change, and the One who sows can soften hard ground.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God scatters his word generously to everyone, and it grows a great harvest in a heart that listens, holds on and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Ask a student to say it in their own words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Born good?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Some people are just born good soil and others can never change.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Most will disagree. Draw out why: soil can change (dig up the path, clear the rocks, pull the thorns); Jun, Sione and Mia all got a second chance; the Sower can soften hearts; even Aroha had to keep working at it every day. Follow-up: ‘What could help a hard heart soften?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who might God use to help someone be good soil?’ If silent: ask a student to point to where in the story someone started again.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be good soil: each day, listen to one Bible verse (or something good a teacher says) and do one small thing about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“…the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next week’s first Monday will check in on this. Give examples: read one verse at breakfast and say thank you to someone; hear a teacher say ‘be kind’ and share your pencil; read Luke 8:15 and write it on your hand. Students unsure about the Bible can use the good words a teacher says.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But the seeds that fell on good ground are the people who hear the message and keep it in good and honest hearts. They last and produce a harvest.” — Luke 8:15 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 8:15. Ask: which word do you find hardest to remember? Which action helps?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Good soil</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Hear the message. Keep it. Last.”<br>Listen. Hold on. Keep going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our Father (line by line)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ prayer starts with “Our Father in heaven.” Today we pray line by line, saying it after each other, and adding our own words. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together, line by line. I’ll pray a line, and you’ll echo it. Then we’ll add our own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo after me: ‘Our Father in heaven…’<br>Prompts: Our Father in heaven, you are generous. / You scatter your word on everyone.<br>Starter: <b>Our Father, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Our Father in heaven, you are generous and good!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, tell your Father which ground you’ve been: the path, the rocks or the thorns.<br>Prompts: Father, sorry for not listening when you speak. / Sorry for giving up when it gets hard.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry. Please forgive us and help us start again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank him for the seed he has scattered in you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for every good word I’ve heard about you. / Thank you for Jesus, who came to make hearts new.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Father, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, for your word and for Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray this week’s challenge into your own words.<br>Prompts: Father, please make my heart good soil. / Help me listen, hold on and keep going every day this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, please make our hearts good soil, and help us listen, hold on and keep going.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Luke 8:15 together as our last line. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Say each line slowly and let the class echo. Keep the ‘our own words’ part short and sincere.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Good Samaritan. Jesus tells a story about a neighbour nobody expected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2: The Good Samaritan":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 10:25–37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Merciful</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hallowed be your name — The Lord’s Prayer begins “Our Father in heaven…”, and the next words ask that God’s name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy — set apart as the most special name of all. This week we honour God’s name by saying what he is like: merciful, and kind to people nobody else stops for.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Who Is My Neighbour?</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how an expert in God’s Law asked Jesus, “Who are my neighbours?”<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Who Is My Neighbour? → Late for school → Luke 10:27–37 → The man on the road → Who stopped? → Why walk by? → The Man on the Jericho Road → Why did he do it? → Head · Heart · Hands → The dangerous road → Memory verse peek → Hallowed be your name (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Hero Nobody Expected</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why Jesus told the parable of the Good Samaritan and who was listening.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Hero Nobody Expected → Recap → Luke 10:25, 29, 33, 37 → A story with a twist → Who’s who in the parable → The expert’s motive → The shock → A different question → Head · Heart · Hands → Walking past → Build the verse → Write a “hallowed” prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Stop at the Sideline</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the parable of the Good Samaritan could happen at a rugby match in Aotearoa today.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Stop at the Sideline → The other team → Luke 10:33–34, 37 → The Eagles and the Wolves → Showed pity → The Wolf in the Mud → What it cost → Fear, then courage → Head · Heart · Hands → Sione turns back → Verse with actions → Praying for each other (pairs)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Greatest Samaritan</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the Good Samaritan shows us about God’s mercy.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Greatest Samaritan → True or false? → Luke 10:35, 37 · Romans 5:8 → Mercy that pays → The promise → What God is like → This week’s Big Idea → Does mercy cost? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Mercy on the road → Open hands</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Who Is My Neighbour?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 10:25–37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who Is My Neighbour?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hallowed be your name — The Lord’s Prayer begins “Our Father in heaven…”, and the next words ask that God’s name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy — set apart as the most special name of all. This week we honour God’s name by saying what he is like: merciful, and kind to people nobody else stops for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how an expert in God’s Law asked Jesus, “Who are my neighbours?”</li><li>We are learning how Jesus answered with a parable — an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</li><li>I can retell what the priest, the temple helper and the man from Samaria each did.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the three travellers in order and noticing that the man from Samaria — not the religious leaders — was the one who stopped to help.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a tea towel for a bandage, a toy donkey</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Who Is My Neighbour?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Another parable this week — an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Late for school</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>You’re walking to school and you see someone sitting on the footpath, hurt and crying. You’re already late. What do you do?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be honest — what would most people do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week Jesus told the story of the Sower — a farmer scattered seed on the path, the rocks, the thorns and the good soil. Your challenge: be good soil — each day listen to one Bible verse (or something good a teacher says) and do one small thing about it. How did you go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students which verse or good word they listened to, and what small thing they did about it. Then the hook: take honest answers (hurry past, tell an adult, stop). Land it: ‘Today an expert asks Jesus a famous question — and Jesus answers with a story about a hurt man on a road, and the three people who saw him.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 10:27–37</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:27</b> “You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:29</b> The man wanted to show that he knew what he was talking about. So he asked Jesus, “Who are my neighbors?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:31</b> A priest happened to be going down the same road. But when he saw the man, he walked by on the other side.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:33</b> A man from Samaria then came traveling along that road. When he saw the man, he felt sorry for him and went over to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:35</b> The next morning he gave the innkeeper two silver coins and said, “Please take care of the man. If you spend more than this on him, I will pay you when I return.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:37</b> “The one who showed pity,” the man answered. Jesus said, “Go and do the same!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Selected verses. The expert’s answer in verse 27 is our memory verse. Samaria was a region north of Judea — we find out more tomorrow. Read verse 31 gently: the man on the road is ‘badly hurt’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The man on the road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus was teaching a crowd when an expert in God’s Law — a man who had spent his whole life studying the scrolls of Moses — stood up. He wasn’t really asking because he wanted to learn. The Bible says he wanted to test Jesus. ‘Teacher,’ he said, ‘what must I do to have eternal life?’</p><p>Jesus turned the question back. ‘What does the Law say?’</p><p>The man knew his scrolls. ‘Love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength and mind. And love your neighbour as you love yourself.’</p><p>‘That’s right,’ said Jesus. ‘Do this and you will live.’</p><p>But the expert wanted to look good. So he asked, ‘And who are my neighbours?’ In other words: who counts? Who do I have to be kind to — and who can I skip?</p><p>Jesus didn’t give him a list. He told a story. Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</p><p>‘A man was travelling down the road from Jerusalem to Jericho. It was a steep, lonely road with plenty of rocks to hide behind, and robbers attacked him. They grabbed everything he had and ran off. He lay beside the road, badly hurt.</p><p>Soon a priest came along — someone who worked at God’s temple. He saw the man. And he went by on the other side of the road.</p><p>Then a temple helper, called a Levite, came past. He looked too. And he also went by on the other side.</p><p>Then a man from Samaria came travelling down the road. He saw the hurt man — and his heart filled with pity. He went straight over. He poured olive oil and wine on the wounds, to clean and soothe them, and bandaged them up. Then he lifted the man onto his own donkey, took him to an inn, and looked after him all night.</p><p>The next morning he gave the innkeeper two silver coins. ‘Please take care of him,’ he said. ‘If it costs more, I will pay you when I come back.’’</p><p>Then Jesus asked the expert one more question: ‘Which of these three was a real neighbour to the man the robbers attacked?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. An expert stands up. · 2. “Who are my neighbours?” · 3. A dangerous road. · 4. Two people walk by. · 5. A man from Samaria stops.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The robbery is implied, never graphic — say ‘badly hurt’ (CEV says ‘half dead’ in verse 30). A Levite was from the tribe of Levi and helped with the work of the temple (CEV: ‘temple helper’). Reuse the phrase from Week 1: a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. Don’t explain the Samaritan twist yet — Tuesday does that. Small imagined details: the rocks to hide behind and the donkey’s pace.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who stopped?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who stopped to help the hurt man?<br>A) The priest &nbsp; B) The temple helper (Levite) &nbsp; C) A man from Samaria ✓ &nbsp; D) The robbers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (Luke 10:33). Follow-up: ‘What did the priest and the temple helper do?’ (They saw him, but went by on the other side.) Follow-up 2: ‘Name two things the man from Samaria did.’ (Bandaged him, took him to an inn, paid.) If silent: re-read the fourth scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why walk by?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the priest and the temple helper walked by on the other side?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus doesn’t say why, so honest guesses are welcome: afraid the robbers were still close, in a hurry, didn’t want to get dirty or break religious rules about touching someone who might be dead, ‘someone else will help’. Follow-up: ‘Do we ever give good-sounding reasons for not helping?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would it have cost them to stop?’ If silent: ask ‘What excuses do people use at school?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Man on the Jericho Road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Man on the Jericho Road</b> (Luke 10:25–37). The expert’s and Jesus’ words follow Luke 10 closely (some wording adapted to NZ spelling). The priest’s and Levite’s excuses, the traveller’s calls for help and the innkeeper’s reply are imagined — the Bible doesn’t record them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Expert</b> — An expert in God’s Law — clever, but wants to look good<br><b>Jesus</b> — The teacher with a story — calm and kind<br><b>Traveller</b> — The man on the road — hurt and weak<br><b>Priest</b> — A temple priest — respectable, but in a hurry<br><b>Levite</b> — A temple helper — nervous and full of excuses<br><b>Samaritan</b> — A man from Samaria — kind, practical, generous<br><b>Innkeeper</b> — Runs the inn — surprised by the coins<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd, the footsteps and the donkey</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: a big gasp — “Ooooh!” — at the expert’s question · Everyone: stomping footsteps as the travellers walk by · Everyone: clip-clop, clip-clop for the donkey<br>Props: Optional: a tea towel for a bandage, a toy donkey<br>Jesus is played reverently — a calm, kind teacher. The Levite is explained in the character list (a temple helper). Keep the traveller’s injuries unspoken: ‘badly hurt’ only. The priest’s and Levite’s excuses are imagined; they stand for how easy it is for any of us to walk by.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A busy village square. An expert in God’s Law stands up to test Jesus.</li><li><b>Expert:</b> Teacher, what must I do to have eternal life? <i>(arms folded, testing)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> What does the Law say? How do you understand it?</li><li><b>Expert:</b> Love the Lord your God with everything you’ve got. And love your neighbour as you love yourself. <i>(proud of his answer)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> That’s right! Do this and you will live.</li><li><b>Expert:</b> But… who are my neighbours? <i>(wanting to look clever)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh! <i>(crowd gasp)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> A man was going down the road from Jerusalem to Jericho when robbers attacked him.</li><li class='dir'>The robbers grabbed everything he had and ran off. The traveller lay by the road, badly hurt.</li><li><b>Traveller:</b> Help… please… somebody help me… <i>(weak voice)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Stomp… stomp… stomp… <i>(footsteps coming closer)</i></li><li><b>Priest:</b> Oh dear. A man in the road. I’m late for the temple, and I mustn’t get messy. <i>(cross to the far side)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The priest saw him — and walked by on the other side.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Stomp… stomp… stomp… <i>(footsteps coming closer again)</i></li><li><b>Levite:</b> Not my problem. Surely someone else will help him. I’d better hurry. <i>(scurry past)</i></li><li><b>Traveller:</b> Please… don’t go… <i>(reach out, barely a whisper)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Two people have walked past. How do you think the man on the road feels? What do you think is going through his mind? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then a man from Samaria came along, leading his donkey.</li><li><b>Samaritan:</b> Oh, you poor man! I feel so sorry for you. <i>(rush over)</i></li><li><b>Samaritan:</b> Let me clean your wounds with olive oil and wine, and bandage them. <i>(gently wrap a bandage)</i></li><li><b>Samaritan:</b> Climb on my donkey. I’ll take you to the inn and look after you. <i>(help him up)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Clip-clop… clip-clop… clip-clop… <i>(slow donkey)</i></li><li><b>Samaritan:</b> Please take care of him. Here are two silver coins. If it costs more, I’ll pay you when I come back. <i>(next morning, hand over coins)</i></li><li><b>Innkeeper:</b> Two silver coins! He must care a lot. Don’t worry — I’ll look after him. <i>(amazed)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Which of these three was a real neighbour to the man the robbers attacked?</li><li><b>Expert:</b> The one who showed pity. <i>(quietly — he can’t say ‘Samaritan’)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Go and do the same!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Go and do the same!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the man from Samaria do for the traveller — and what did it cost him?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He felt pity, went over, cleaned and bandaged the wounds, lifted the man onto his donkey, took him to an inn and paid two silver coins with a promise of more. It cost him time, money, comfort and safety.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The expert said, “The one who showed pity” — he didn’t say the word Samaritan. Why do you think he avoided it?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He couldn’t bring himself to say it. Samaritans were people many Jews looked down on — we find out why tomorrow. Follow-up: ‘What does that tell you about how the expert felt?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who might we find it hard to say something good about?’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why did he do it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man from Samaria took a risk, spent his time and gave his money for a stranger. Why do you think he did all that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he felt real pity; he saw a person, not a problem; he didn’t need a reason. Mercy means kindness to someone in need who can’t pay you back. Follow-up: ‘What did the Samaritan not ask the man?’ (His name, his team, his religion, whether he could pay.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who is the hardest person for you to feel pity for?’ If silent: ask ‘What would you have had to give up?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A neighbour is anyone who needs help — and a real neighbour shows mercy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, open my eyes to see people who need help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, notice one person who needs help — at school, in sport or at home — and take one small step: a hello, a hand, or telling a teacher.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. This previews Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The dangerous road</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Help… please… somebody…”<br>Jerusalem to Jericho — a steep, lonely road.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 10:27 — the expert’s own answer. Echo it line by line.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Hallowed be your name (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus taught us to start with God: “Our Father in heaven…”, then asking that his name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy. Today we honour God’s name by thinking about what he is like. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Think of the man by the road — and of the God who never walks by on the other side. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s name is holy. Tell him — silently — what he is like.<br>Prompts: God, you are merciful — you stop for people others walk past. / God, your name is holy and your kindness never runs out.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Hallowed be your name, God — you are merciful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of a time you saw someone who needed help and walked by.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I pretended not to see someone who needed help. / Sorry for making excuses like ‘it’s not my job’.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we walked by on the other side.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for people who have stopped for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for someone who helped me when I was hurt or sad. / Thank you that you never walk by on the other side.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who stopped to help us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to open your eyes.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice someone who needs a neighbour this week. / Please give me the courage to stop, like the man from Samaria.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us notice people who need a neighbour.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first. Short and simple is great. If a student’s Sorry is very personal, keep it between them and God.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — and the twist that shocked everyone who heard it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: A Hero Nobody Expected":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 10:25–37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Hero Nobody Expected</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hallowed be your name — The Lord’s Prayer begins “Our Father in heaven…”, and the next words ask that God’s name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy — set apart as the most special name of all. This week we honour God’s name by saying what he is like: merciful, and kind to people nobody else stops for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Jesus told the parable of the Good Samaritan and who was listening.</li><li>We are learning why a Samaritan hero shocked the crowd.</li><li>I can explain who’s who in the parable and how Jesus changed the expert’s question.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Jews and Samaritans were enemies, that the hero was the person the crowd least expected, and that Jesus changed the question from ‘Who is my neighbour?’ to ‘Who was a neighbour?’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Mini-whiteboards or paper</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Hero Nobody Expected</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today: who was listening, and the twist.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday an expert asked Jesus a question, and Jesus answered with a parable.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the expert’s question — and who stopped to help the hurt man?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>‘Who are my neighbours?’ A man from Samaria stopped, while a priest and a temple helper walked by. Today: why Jesus told it, who was listening, and the twist that would have shocked the crowd.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 10:25, 29, 33, 37</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:25</b> An expert in the Law of Moses stood up and asked Jesus a question to see what he would say. “Teacher,” he asked, “what must I do to have eternal life?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:29</b> The man wanted to show that he knew what he was talking about. So he asked Jesus, “Who are my neighbors?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:33</b> A man from Samaria then came traveling along that road. When he saw the man, he felt sorry for him and went over to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:37</b> “The one who showed pity,” the man answered. Jesus said, “Go and do the same!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Notice verse 29: the expert wanted to prove himself. And notice the surprising word in verse 33: Samaria.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A story with a twist</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s go back to the crowd. Who was listening to this story? And why did Jesus tell it?</p><p>First, the expert in God’s Law. He already knew the right answer: love your neighbour. But the Bible says he wanted to show that he knew what he was talking about — he wanted to prove he was already good enough. And if ‘neighbour’ only meant certain people, people like him, he could tick the box. Neighbours are my family, my friends, my people. Everyone else? Not my problem.</p><p>Now picture the road. It dropped steeply — about a kilometre in height over twenty-seven kilometres — through bare, rocky hills, and everyone knew it was dangerous. So when Jesus said a man lay hurt beside it, every listener thought: that could be me.</p><p>Then came the priest, who served God at the temple in Jerusalem — one of the most respected people in the country. Then the Levite, a temple helper. Jesus doesn’t tell us why they walked by. Maybe they were scared. Maybe they were in a hurry. Maybe they worried about religious rules. But whatever the reason, they chose rules and rushing over a person. Some listeners might even have smirked — it’s always fun when the important people look bad.</p><p>Now the crowd would have expected the third person to be an ordinary Jewish man: a priest, a Levite, then someone like us. A neat pattern.</p><p>But Jesus said: a Samaritan.</p><p>Gasp! Jews and Samaritans had been bitter enemies for hundreds of years. They argued about where to worship God and whose scrolls were right. Many Jews wouldn’t even eat with a Samaritan. To the crowd, calling a Samaritan the hero was like making your worst enemy the good guy in the story.</p><p>And this Samaritan did everything. He felt pity, bandaged, carried, paid, and promised to come back. Think about what he gave up. The oil and wine were his own travel supplies. The donkey was his own ride. Two silver coins was about two days’ pay for a worker — a lot to hand to a stranger. And if the robbers were still close by, he could have been next. Yet he stopped, and he stayed.</p><p>Then Jesus flipped the question. The expert had asked, ‘Who is my neighbour? Who counts?’ Jesus asked, ‘Who was a neighbour?’ The question isn’t who deserves my help. It’s whether I will be the one who shows mercy.</p><p>And the expert couldn’t even say the word ‘Samaritan’. He said, ‘The one who showed pity.’</p><p>‘Go,’ said Jesus, ‘and do the same.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Why did Jesus tell it? · 2. A road everyone feared. · 3. Who would be next? · 4. A Samaritan! · 5. Jesus flipped the question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Original audience: a crowd of Jewish listeners, including this expert in the Law. Samaritans were descendants of people from the northern kingdom of Israel who had mixed with other peoples; they worshipped on Mount Gerizim, not Jerusalem. Keep it simple and never mock Samaritans — the point is how startling Jesus’ choice was. Why the priest and Levite passed is not stated; if asked, say we don’t know. John 4:9 notes that Jews did not share dishes with Samaritans.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who in the parable</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who’s who? Match each person in the story to what the crowd would have thought.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>What the crowd would have thought</th></tr><tr><td>The hurt traveller</td><td>Anyone in need — no name, no team, no way to pay anyone back</td></tr><tr><td>The priest</td><td>A top religious leader from the temple — surely he’ll help!</td></tr><tr><td>The Levite (temple helper)</td><td>Another religious worker — a second chance for the “good” people</td></tr><tr><td>The man from Samaria</td><td>An enemy and outsider — the last person they expected to stop</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The hero of Jesus’ story is the person the listeners least expected — the enemy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Notice what Jesus does NOT tell us about the traveller: nothing about his nationality or religion — he is simply ‘a man’ in need.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The expert’s motive</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to Luke 10:29, why did the expert ask, “Who are my neighbors?”<br>A) He wanted to learn something new &nbsp; B) He wanted to show that he knew what he was talking about ✓ &nbsp; C) He wanted a drink of water &nbsp; D) He was lost</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 10:29). Follow-up: ‘What had Jesus just told him to do?’ (Love God and love his neighbour.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why might he want to make “neighbour” mean only certain people?’ If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The shock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why would it have been such a shock for the crowd that the hero was a Samaritan?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jews and Samaritans were enemies; they would have expected a Jewish hero; Jesus made the enemy the good one and the religious leaders the ones who failed; he made them think about their own hearts. Follow-up: ‘How would you feel if the hero was someone your team can’t stand?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What was Jesus teaching about who counts as a neighbour?’ If silent: ask ‘Who would be the shocking hero in your world?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A different question</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The expert asked, “Who is my neighbour?” Jesus asked, “Who was a neighbour?” On your whiteboard: why does that change matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the first question asks who I can skip; the second asks what I will do; mercy isn’t about who deserves it but about who needs it; being a neighbour is something we do. Follow-up: ‘Which question is easier to live with?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would asking Jesus’ question look like in the playground?’ If silent: have pairs share their whiteboards first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s mercy crosses every line — his kindness isn’t only for people like us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I’m sorry for the times I’ve decided who counts. Help me see people the way you do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone at school who is often left out or isn’t in your usual group. This week, notice them and do one kind thing: say hello, offer a seat, or invite them to join in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t ask for names aloud. Encourage students to picture the person quietly. This feeds straight into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Walking past</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Not my problem…”<br>Jesus doesn’t tell us why they walked by.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 10:27. Point out the two loves in the verse: God, and our neighbour.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a “hallowed” prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Writing a prayer slows us down and helps us choose our words. You can write it on paper or in your head — God reads both. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your paper. At the top write: ‘Hallowed be your name.’ Remember, hallowed means holy — the most special name of all.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line honouring God’s name — what is he like?<br>Prompts: You are merciful — your kindness reaches strangers and enemies too. / You are holy — your name is the most special name there is.<br>Starter: <b>You are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Hallowed be your name — you are merciful to everyone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there someone you’ve decided doesn’t ‘count’?<br>Prompts: Sorry for looking down on people who are different from me. / Sorry for deciding who deserves my kindness.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for deciding who counts and who doesn’t.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thank-you for the mercy God has shown you.<br>Prompts: Thank you that you showed mercy to me when I didn’t deserve it. / Thank you for people who were kind to me when I felt like an outsider.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the mercy you’ve shown us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a request for someone who is left out or in need.<br>Prompts: Please help the person at school who often sits alone. / Please help me be a real neighbour to someone this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us be neighbours to people who are left out.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your prayer or keep it in your book. God has read every word. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite one or two volunteers to read a line aloud, but make it optional.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the same story in Aotearoa — a muddy Saturday rugby match where everyone walks past, until Sione stops.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Stop at the Sideline":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 10:25–37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stop at the Sideline</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hallowed be your name — The Lord’s Prayer begins “Our Father in heaven…”, and the next words ask that God’s name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy — set apart as the most special name of all. This week we honour God’s name by saying what he is like: merciful, and kind to people nobody else stops for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the parable of the Good Samaritan could happen at a rugby match in Aotearoa today.</li><li>We are learning that being a neighbour means stopping even when it’s awkward or costs us.</li><li>I can explain how Sione showed mercy to someone from the other team.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Sione’s choice to the Samaritan’s, naming what it cost him, and noticing that Mia’s first reaction (fear) and second reaction (coming back) are both part of being a neighbour.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a rugby ball, a tea towel for a sling</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Stop at the Sideline</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today the parable moves to a muddy rugby ground in Aotearoa.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The other team</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Your team is playing the team you really want to beat. Someone from the other side goes down hurt. What happens?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who would stop? Who would keep walking?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few honest answers. Land it: ‘Yesterday the hero was the enemy. Today we’ll see the same story in Aotearoa — on a Saturday morning at the rugby, in the drizzle.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 10:33–34, 37</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:33</b> A man from Samaria then came traveling along that road. When he saw the man, he felt sorry for him and went over to him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:34</b> He treated his wounds with olive oil and wine and bandaged them. Then he put the man on his own donkey and took him to an inn, where he took care of him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:37</b> “The one who showed pity,” the man answered. Jesus said, “Go and do the same!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Listen for these in today’s role-play: pity, practical help and cost.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The Eagles and the Wolves</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It’s Saturday morning at the club rugby ground. It’s drizzling, the pitch is a swamp, and the parents on the sideline are wrapped in raincoats holding hot drinks. Today it’s the Eastside Eagles against the Westgate Wolves — the team that has beaten the Eagles three years in a row.</p><p>Sione plays number eight for the Eagles. He’s big, he’s fast, and right now he really wants to win. Aroha is on the sideline, handing out the half-time oranges and cheering louder than anyone. Mia has come along with her sketchbook. She’s drawing the match from under her hood, because crowds make her nervous.</p><p>With a minute to go, the Wolves’ winger, a boy called Ryan, gets tackled and lands hard on his ankle. The final whistle goes. Ryan is on the ground, holding his ankle and trying very hard not to cry.</p><p>And everyone walks past. The Wolves’ players trudge off towards their van, thinking about hot chips. The Eagles’ coach hurries by, calling out about the sausage sizzle. Mia sees Ryan, and she freezes. What if she does something wrong? What if everyone stares at her? ‘Somebody else will go,’ she thinks, and she hurries off to the clubrooms. She isn’t unkind. She’s scared, and sometimes being scared feels exactly like not caring.</p><p>Ryan sits alone in the rain. He’s a Wolf. He’s the enemy.</p><p>Then Sione stops. He’s halfway to the sausage sizzle, tummy rumbling, when he looks back and sees Ryan in the mud. He could keep walking. Nobody would blame him. Instead he turns around and jogs back.</p><p>‘You right, bro? Can you stand?’</p><p>Ryan can’t. So Sione crouches and puts Ryan’s arm over his big shoulders. Aroha spots them and sprints over to take the other side. Together they help him hobble to the sideline, all three of them muddy to the knees.</p><p>And Mia? Mia stops in the clubroom doorway, takes a big breath, and runs back to fetch the parent with the first-aid bag.</p><p>Sione missed the sausage sizzle queue that day. He sat in the cold with Ryan until Ryan’s mum arrived. It cost him. But he’d stopped. Ryan limped off with a tea-towel bandage on his ankle and called back, ‘Cheers, Eagle.’ Sione just grinned and said, ‘Sweet as.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Eagles v Wolves. · 2. A Wolf goes down. · 3. Sione turns back. · 4. Helping him off. · 5. Who was the neighbour?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. This is a modern retelling set in Aotearoa — every detail (names, teams, Mia’s run) is imagined. In real life, if someone has a neck or back injury, don’t move them: stay with them and get an adult. Ryan’s ankle injury is mild. Mia’s freeze is understandable — being scared is not the same as being unkind; she comes back.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Showed pity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the expert’s answer: “The one who showed…”<br><i>Answer: “…pity.” (Luke 10:37) — and Jesus said, “Go and do the same!”</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘Who showed pity in today’s story?’ (Sione, and then Aroha and Mia.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Sione do first?’ (Stopped and turned back.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Wolf in the Mud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Wolf in the Mud</b> (Luke 10:25–37 (retold in Aotearoa today)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable. The teams, the characters’ names and every word they say are imagined — Jesus’ story is only the pattern (Luke 10:30–37).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Sione</b> — Samoan Kiwi boy, 11 — big-hearted, wants to win<br><b>Aroha</b> — Māori girl, 11 — confident and kind<br><b>Mia</b> — Pākehā girl, 10 — nervous, sketchbook in hand<br><b>Ryan</b> — The Wolves’ winger — hurt, trying to be brave<br><b>Coach</b> — The Eagles’ coach — hurried and cheerful<br><b>Everyone</b> — Sideline crowd and the rain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: cheer “Eagles! Eagles!” from the sideline · Everyone: drip, drip, drip for the rain · Everyone: freeze when the pause appears<br>Props: Optional: a rugby ball, a tea towel for a sling<br>Set in modern Aotearoa; all names and events imagined. Light touches of te reo and Kiwi speech (“bro”, “yeah nah”, “Kia kaha, e hoa”) are used naturally and respectfully. Jesus’ story is only referred to by the Narrator. Mia’s arc is deliberate: fear first, then courage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Saturday morning. Drizzle. A muddy rugby ground. The final whistle has just blown.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Eagles! Eagles! Eagles! <i>(sideline cheer)</i></li><li><b>Coach:</b> Great effort, team! Hot chocolate and sausages in the clubrooms — move, move, move! <i>(clapping, hurrying)</i></li><li class='dir'>But one player is still on the ground. Ryan, from the Wolves, is holding his ankle.</li><li><b>Ryan:</b> Ow… my ankle… I can’t get up. <i>(trying to be brave)</i></li><li><b>Coach:</b> Sorry, mate — can’t stop! Your own coach will be along in a minute. <i>(hurry past)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Oh no, he’s really hurt. But what if I do it wrong? Somebody else will go. <i>(nervous, hugging her sketchbook)</i></li><li class='dir'>Mia hurries off to the clubrooms. Ryan is alone in the rain.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Drip… drip… drip… <i>(rain)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Two people have walked past. How might Ryan feel? And what is Sione about to decide? (20s)</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Mmm, sausages… wait. That’s Ryan. The Wolves’ winger. He flattened me last year. <i>(stops and looks back)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> He’s still on the ground and nobody’s stopped. Yeah nah — I can’t leave him. <i>(turns around)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> You right, bro? Can you stand? <i>(crouch beside him)</i></li><li><b>Ryan:</b> You’re an Eagle. Why are you helping me? <i>(surprised)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Because you’re hurt. The scoreboard doesn’t matter right now. Arm over my shoulder. <i>(lift gently)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Sione, wait! I’ll take the other side. Kia kaha, e hoa — stay strong, friend. <i>(run over)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Slowly now. Left foot… right foot… nearly at the sideline. <i>(hobbling together)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Wait! I’m coming back! I can fetch the first-aid bag. Mrs Taylor has one! <i>(sprint, sketchbook dropped)</i></li><li class='dir'>Mia ran faster than she’d ever run. Mrs Taylor met them at the sideline with the first-aid bag.</li><li><b>Ryan:</b> Thanks, you guys. I thought everyone had gone home. <i>(wipe his eyes)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Take my jacket, bro. I’ll stay with you until your mum gets here. <i>(hands over his dry jacket)</i></li><li class='dir'>Sione missed the sausage queue and sat in the cold with Ryan. It cost him — but he stopped.</li><li><b>Ryan:</b> Seriously, thanks. Next time we play, I’ll still smash you. But… thanks. <i>(grinning)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Bring it on, bro! <i>(big grin)</i></li><li class='dir'>Jesus asked, ‘Which of the three was a real neighbour?’</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> The one who showed pity. <i>(quietly, nodding)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Go and do the same!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Coach, Mia and Sione all saw Ryan. What made it harder for some to stop than others?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Busy, in a hurry, scared of getting it wrong, he’s from the other team, someone else will do it. Sione had every reason to keep walking. Follow-up: ‘What did it cost Sione?’ (Sausages, warmth, time, his jacket.) Follow-up 2: ‘Is it okay to be scared and still help?’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sione helped someone from the team he wanted to beat. Who might be the “other team” in your world?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Rival schools, a different friendship group, someone you’ve fallen out with, a sibling. No names please. Mercy crosses the line. Follow-up: ‘What would stopping look like?’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What it cost</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In today’s story, why was it hard for Sione to stop — and what did it cost him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Ryan was on the other team; it was cold and wet; he wanted the sausage sizzle; nobody else was stopping; it cost him time, comfort and his jacket. Real mercy usually costs something. Follow-up: ‘What did the Samaritan give up?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Would you have stopped?’ If silent: ask ‘What would most people have done at the rugby?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fear, then courage</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mia walked past, then came back and ran for help. What does that tell us about being a neighbour when we’re scared?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: being scared is normal; we can change our mind; we don’t have to do everything — Mia could fetch help; small, brave steps still count. Follow-up: ‘What is one thing you could do even if you’re nervous?’ (Tell an adult, fetch help, sit with them.) Follow-up 2: ‘Do you think Mia was a bad neighbour?’ If silent: ask ‘What did Mia do second?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A neighbour is anyone who needs help — and mercy means stopping even when it costs us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, give me the courage to stop, even if I’m nervous like Mia.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">At sport, on the playground or on the bus this week, notice someone from “the other team” or outside your group who needs help, and do one small thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Small steps count: fetching an adult, offering to carry a bag, sitting with someone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Sione turns back</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Yeah nah — I can’t leave him.”<br>He stopped. It cost him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Use the actions for Luke 10:27. Ask: which action matches ‘neighbours’?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for each other (pairs)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying with a partner reminds us we’re not praying alone. Take turns, and keep it short — one sentence is plenty. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. One of you prays first, then swap. Remember: ‘hallowed be your name’ means God’s name is holy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell God (out loud, quietly, to your partner’s ears) what he is like.<br>Prompts: God, you stop for people nobody else stops for. / Hallowed be your name — you are merciful and kind.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Hallowed be your name — you are merciful and kind.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: a time you were too busy or too scared to help, like Coach or Mia.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I walked past someone who needed me. / Sorry for being too scared or too busy to stop.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we walked past.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell your partner, and God, about someone who has been a neighbour to you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for someone who stopped and helped me when I was hurt, lonely or left out. / Thank you that you showed mercy to me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who stop and help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your partner by name: one place they could be a neighbour this week.<br>Prompts: Please give (partner’s name) courage to stop and help this week. / Please help me notice someone from the “other team”.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to stop and help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pair students with a friend or someone nearby. Model a short prayer first. Students who don’t want to pray aloud can pray silently.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what this story shows about God — and about Jesus, the greatest Samaritan — plus this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Greatest Samaritan":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 10:25–37</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Greatest Samaritan</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hallowed be your name — The Lord’s Prayer begins “Our Father in heaven…”, and the next words ask that God’s name be treated as holy. “Hallowed” is an old word for holy — set apart as the most special name of all. This week we honour God’s name by saying what he is like: merciful, and kind to people nobody else stops for.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the Good Samaritan shows us about God’s mercy.</li><li>We are learning how Jesus is the greatest Samaritan — he came to our rescue and paid the cost himself.</li><li>I can name one way I will be a neighbour this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable in order, explain that God is merciful and crosses every line, and name one specific neighbour-act for this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Greatest Samaritan</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand up for TRUE, sit down for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The priest and the temple helper both stopped to help. — <b>False</b> (They saw the man and went by on the other side (Luke 10:31–32).)<br>The man who helped came from Samaria. — <b>True</b> (A man from Samaria — an enemy of the Jews — felt sorry for him.)<br>The Samaritan helped a little and then left. — <b>False</b> (He bandaged the man, took him to an inn, stayed the night and paid two silver coins.)<br>Jesus ended by saying, “Go and do the same!” — <b>True</b> (Luke 10:37 — Jesus told the expert to be a neighbour.)<br>In our Wednesday story, Sione stopped to help a boy from the other team. — <b>True</b> (Ryan was a Wolf, and Sione wanted to win — but he stopped anyway.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Each statement: students stand (true) or sit (false), then click to reveal. Ask one student to explain each answer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 10:35, 37 · Romans 5:8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:35</b> The next morning he gave the innkeeper two silver coins and said, “Please take care of the man. If you spend more than this on him, I will pay you when I return.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 10:37</b> “The one who showed pity,” the man answered. Jesus said, “Go and do the same!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Romans 5:8</b> But God showed how much he loves us by having Christ die for us, even though we were sinners.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Romans 5:8 shows what God’s mercy looked like in Jesus. Savior is the CEV spelling; we say Saviour.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Mercy that pays</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think about everything the man from Samaria did. He didn’t only feel sorry. He stopped. He got his hands dirty. He used his own oil and wine, his own donkey and his own money — and he walked while the hurt man rode. He even promised, ‘If it costs more, I will pay you when I come back.’ That’s mercy: kindness to someone in need who can’t pay you back. And it costs the one who gives it.</p><p>Here’s what Jesus’ story shows us about God. Our God is merciful. He doesn’t walk by on the other side. When we were helpless — hurt by our own wrong choices and unable to fix them — God didn’t pass us by.</p><p>Christians have long seen a picture of Jesus in the Samaritan. Jesus is the greatest Samaritan. He left heaven and came down onto our dangerous road. People rejected him, and some even called him a Samaritan as an insult. And he stopped for us. The Bible says, ‘God showed how much he loves us by having Christ die for us, even though we were sinners.’ Jesus paid the cost — not with two silver coins, but with his own life — and he promised to return.</p><p>Now listen to Jesus’ last words to the expert: ‘Go and do the same.’ We don’t show mercy to earn God’s love. We show mercy because we’ve been shown mercy.</p><p>And mercy can look small. Mia, on her street, carrying heavy shopping bags up the steps for an elderly neighbour. That’s Luke 10:27 in action: love God with everything, and love the neighbour right in front of you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The Samaritan paid. · 2. God is merciful. · 3. Jesus: the greatest Samaritan. · 4. Go and do the same.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes (roughly 300 words). Jesus’ main point in the parable is ‘be a neighbour’. Seeing Jesus as the greatest Samaritan is a gospel echo that Christians have long noticed, not the parable’s main meaning, so keep it gentle. John 8:48 records people calling Jesus a Samaritan as an insult. Mia’s act is imagined.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the Samaritan promise the innkeeper?<br>A) To come back and pay any extra cost ✓ &nbsp; B) To bring a new donkey &nbsp; C) To send the robbers to jail &nbsp; D) To find the man’s family</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A (Luke 10:35). Follow-up: ‘How much did he leave first?’ (Two silver coins.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does that show about how much he cared?’ (He didn’t do the minimum.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What God is like</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does this story show us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God is merciful; he doesn’t walk by on the other side; his kindness crosses every line; he pays the cost for us; all of us are like the hurt traveller. Follow-up: ‘How is Jesus like the Samaritan?’ (He came to us, rescued us, paid the price, promised to return.) Follow-up 2: ‘What difference does God’s mercy make for how we treat others?’ If silent: ask ‘Who stopped for you?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>A neighbour is anyone who needs help — not just people like us.</li><li>God is merciful: his kindness crosses every line, even towards enemies.</li><li>Jesus is the greatest Samaritan — he paid the cost to rescue us, and says, “Go and do the same.”</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s mercy crosses every line — so loving our neighbour means showing mercy to anyone in need, even people we’d rather avoid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Remember: a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning — what is the heavenly meaning here?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Does mercy cost?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Showing mercy should cost us something.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Draw out WHY. Agree: the Samaritan gave time, money and comfort; Sione gave up the sausage sizzle and his jacket; Jesus gave his life. Disagree: some kindness is free, like a smile. Follow-up: ‘What did mercy cost Jesus?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What small thing could you give up this week?’ If silent: ask ‘What did the Samaritan give up?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be a neighbour. This week, do one kind thing for someone who isn’t one of your usual friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday (Lost Sheep &amp; Lost Coin) checks in on this. Brainstorm ideas: invite someone new to play, sit with someone at lunch, help a younger child, thank the caretaker, help a neighbour with their bags. Encourage students to choose a specific person today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You must love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, strength, and mind. You must also love your neighbors as much as you love yourself.” — Luke 10:27 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 10:27. Say it once together, then in pairs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Mercy on the road</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who is my neighbour?<br>Anyone God puts in front of you — go and do the same.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open hands remind us that God gives us mercy freely — and that we can pass it on. Hold your hands open as you pray. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s sit quietly and hold our hands open, palms up. God is merciful to us — and we can be merciful to others.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands up high: tell God what he is like.<br>Prompts: God, you are merciful — you never walk by on the other side. / Jesus, you are the greatest Samaritan — you paid the cost for us.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Hallowed be your name — you are merciful, and Jesus, you paid the cost for us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands closed, then slowly open them. Tell God what you’re sorry for.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I decided someone wasn’t my neighbour. / Sorry for the times I knew what to do and didn’t do it.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry — thank you for your mercy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands open to receive: thank God for what he has given.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for coming down our road to rescue us. / Thank you for the people who have shown me mercy.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for rescuing us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands held out in front of you: ask God to use you.<br>Prompts: Please help me do my neighbour challenge this week. / Please show mercy through me to someone who needs it.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, show your mercy through us this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together: ‘Our Father in heaven… hallowed be your name.’ Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Open hands are optional. This week’s Lord’s Prayer phrase is ‘hallowed be your name’; next week it’s ‘your kingdom come’.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Lost Sheep &amp; Lost Coin — a shepherd leaves ninety-nine sheep to search for just one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3: Lost Sheep & Lost Coin":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Seeking</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your kingdom come — Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his good rule — his kindness, welcome and rescue — into our school, our homes and our country. This week we pray it for people who feel lost.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Lost Sheep</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning why Jesus told a story about a shepherd and one lost sheep.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Lost Sheep → Lost it! → Luke 15:1–7 → Ninety-nine and one → The grumble → Why one? → The Lost Sheep → Who does the finding? → Head · Heart · Hands → Eating with sinners → Memory verse peek → Your kingdom come (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Who Was Listening? And the Lost Coin</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told this parable, and why it shocked them.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Who Was Listening? And the Lost Coin → Recap → Luke 15:2, 8–10 → The twist that shocked them → Who’s who? → Heaven’s happiness → Two stories, one ending → Do we all need finding? → Head · Heart · Hands → A party for one → Build the verse → Write a ‘found’ prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Lost at the Beach</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the lost sheep parable might look in a story from Aotearoa today.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Lost at the Beach → Dropped everything → Luke 15:5–7, 9 → Where’s Ana? → Where was Ana? → Lost at the Beach → Why not wait? → Who is ‘Ana’ at our school? → Head · Heart · Hands → Found! → Verse with actions → Your kingdom come — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>God Goes Looking — Welcome In</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the lost sheep and lost coin show us about God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>God Goes Looking — Welcome In → Put the parable in order → Luke 15:10; 19:10; John 10:11 → Look who’s coming → What did they do? → How does God feel? → This week’s Big Idea → Who needs welcoming? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → God goes looking → Your kingdom come — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Lost Sheep":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Lost Sheep</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your kingdom come — Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his good rule — his kindness, welcome and rescue — into our school, our homes and our country. This week we pray it for people who feel lost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Jesus told a story about a shepherd and one lost sheep.</li><li>We are learning what the shepherd did when he found it.</li><li>I can explain what Jesus said happens in heaven when one person turns to God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that the leaders grumbled because Jesus ate with sinners, that the shepherd left the ninety-nine to search until he found the one, and that there is joy in heaven over one person who turns to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a soft toy sheep for the shepherd</li><li>Optional: a scarf to wear as a shepherd’s head cloth</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Lost Sheep</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week the question is: what is God like when we are lost?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Lost it!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever lost something that really mattered to you — a phone, a toy, a pet, a favourite hoodie?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did you do to find it? How did it feel when you did?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week Jesus told us about the Good Samaritan, who showed mercy to a stranger. Your challenge: be a neighbour — do one kind thing for someone who isn’t one of your usual friends. What did you do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask 2–3 students what kind thing they did, and celebrate honest ‘I forgot’ answers too. Then the hook: take a few stories. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about something lost — and what happens when it’s found. Remember: a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:1–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:1–2</b> Many tax collectors and other sinners were coming to listen to Jesus. So the Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses started grumbling, “This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:4</b> “Suppose one of you has a hundred sheep, and one of them gets lost. Won’t you leave the other ninety-nine in the field and go look for the lost sheep until you find it?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:5</b> “And when you find it, you will be so glad that you will put it on your shoulder and carry it home.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:7</b> “In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. ‘Pharisees’ = very strict religious leaders. ‘Tax collectors’ = men who collected money for the Roman rulers, often cheating people. ‘Sinners’ here means people who were known to have turned away from God’s way. Luke 15:7 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Ninety-nine and one</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine a crowded house beside the Sea of Galilee, a long time ago. Jesus is having dinner, and the guests are not the people you’d expect. There are tax collectors, who collected money for the Roman rulers and often kept extra for themselves, so almost everyone hated them. There are others the crowd called ‘sinners’ — people known for turning away from God’s way. And Jesus is laughing with them, eating with them, listening to them.</p><p>At the doorway stand the Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses. Pharisees were very strict religious leaders who worked hard to keep all of God’s rules, and they looked down on people who didn’t. They fold their arms and start grumbling: ‘This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them!’</p><p>Jesus hears them. He doesn’t argue. He tells a parable — an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</p><p>‘Suppose one of you has a hundred sheep, and one of them gets lost. Won’t you leave the other ninety-nine in the field and go look for the lost sheep until you find it?’</p><p>Picture the shepherd at sunset, counting his sheep the way he does every night. Ninety-seven, ninety-eight, ninety-nine… and he stops. He counts again. Still ninety-nine. One is missing.</p><p>He doesn’t shrug. He doesn’t say, ‘Ninety-nine is plenty.’ He leaves the others safe and goes out into the hills, over rocks and through thorns, calling and listening. Hour after hour. He does not give up until he finds it.</p><p>There it is, a little lamb, tired and scared and stuck on a ledge. Does the shepherd scold it? No. Jesus says, ‘When you find it, you will be so glad that you will put it on your shoulder and carry it home.’ The lamb doesn’t even have to walk.</p><p>And when the shepherd gets home, he can’t keep the news to himself. He calls his friends and neighbours: ‘Let’s celebrate! I’ve found my lost sheep!’</p><p>Then Jesus said something that must have stung. There will be more happiness in heaven over one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.</p><p>Around the table, the tax collectors listened with wide eyes. Somebody was looking for them. Somebody would be glad when they were found.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Dinner with the wrong crowd. · 2. The leaders grumbled. · 3. One sheep out of a hundred. · 4. Out into the dark hills. · 5. On his shoulders, all the way home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Luke 15:3–7. ‘Sinners’ is the label used by the crowd — be gentle with it: Jesus is not saying these people were worse than anyone else. Pharisees are not villains for all time; they stand for anyone who trusts themselves. Re-use the phrase ‘an earthly story with a heavenly meaning’ from Week 1.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The grumble</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did the Pharisees and teachers grumble about Jesus?<br>A) He ate with tax collectors and sinners ✓ &nbsp; B) He was late for dinner &nbsp; C) He wouldn’t tell a story &nbsp; D) He wore the wrong clothes</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A (Luke 15:2). Follow-up: ‘What did the shepherd do when one sheep went missing?’ (Left the ninety-nine and searched until he found it.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he do when he found it?’ (Carried it home and called a party.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why one?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The shepherd still had ninety-nine sheep. Why do you think he went to so much trouble for just one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: each sheep matters to him; he cares about each one by name; one missing sheep is in real danger; he is not willing to lose any. Follow-up: ‘Who is the shepherd meant to be like?’ (God.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does it say about how God sees each person?’ If silent: ask, ‘If you were the one missing, what would you want your family to do?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Lost Sheep</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Lost Sheep</b> (Luke 15:1–7). Jesus’ words and the grumbling of the leaders are from Luke 15 (CEV), lightly shortened. The shepherd’s acted-out story, the tax collector’s line and the neighbour’s advice are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Kind, calm storyteller<br><b>Pharisee</b> — A strict religious leader — frowning, arms folded<br><b>Teacher of the Law</b> — Another strict leader — shocked<br><b>Tax Collector</b> — Used to being avoided — amazed to be welcome<br><b>Shepherd</b> — In Jesus’ story: never gives up<br><b>Neighbour</b> — In Jesus’ story: thinks one sheep isn’t worth it<br><b>Everyone</b> — Dinner guests, then sheep, then a party crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: happy dinner chatter · Everyone (as sheep): ‘Baa!’ · Everyone: a big party cheer at the end<br>Props: Optional: a soft toy sheep · Optional: a scarf for the shepherd<br>Keep the Pharisee’s and Teacher’s lines playful rather than nasty. They are not villains for all time — they stand for anyone who trusts themselves. Choose confident readers for the Pharisee roles and let the Tax Collector be quiet and sincere at the end. The shepherd’s story is a parable inside the dinner scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A crowded house. Tax collectors and friends are eating dinner with Jesus.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Pass the bread! Ha ha ha! <i>(happy dinner chatter)</i></li><li><b>Tax Collector:</b> Nobody ever eats with me. Why is Jesus sitting at my table? <i>(amazed)</i></li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them! <i>(arms folded, frowning)</i></li><li><b>Teacher of the Law:</b> Shocking! A holy teacher should stay far away from people like that. <i>(shake head)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Suppose one of you has a hundred sheep, and one of them gets lost.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Won’t you leave the other ninety-nine and go look for the lost sheep until you find it?</li><li class='dir'>Let’s act out Jesus’ story. Sunset. The shepherd counts his sheep.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Baa! Baa! Baa! <i>(wriggle like sheep)</i></li><li><b>Shepherd:</b> Ninety-seven… ninety-eight… ninety-nine. Wait. I’m one short! <i>(count on fingers)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Ninety-nine is plenty! Forget it. It’s only one sheep. <i>(shrug)</i></li><li><b>Shepherd:</b> One sheep matters to me. I’m going to find it. <i>(grab staff)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Over the rocks, through the thorns, calling in the dark.</li><li><b>Shepherd:</b> Little one! Where are you? Call out so I can find you! <i>(hands cupped to mouth)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Baaaa… <i>(one small, wobbly sheep voice, far away)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The shepherd has found the lamb, stuck on a ledge. What do you think he does — scold it, or something else? (20s)</li><li><b>Shepherd:</b> There you are! No scolding. Up you come. <i>(lift the lamb onto your shoulders)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> He carries it home on his shoulders, full of joy.</li><li><b>Shepherd:</b> Friends! Neighbours! Let’s celebrate! I’ve found my lost sheep! <i>(arms wide)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hooray! <i>(big party cheer)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> There is more happiness in heaven over one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.</li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> Hmm. Is he talking about… us? <i>(shuffle, uncomfortable)</i></li><li><b>Tax Collector:</b> Somebody was looking for me. <i>(look up, quietly amazed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Heaven celebrates when one is found!</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the shepherd do when he found his lamb?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He didn’t scold it. He put it on his shoulders, carried it home and called a celebration (Luke 15:5–6).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How do you think the tax collector felt at the end?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Surprised, relieved, wanted. Someone was glad he was there. Jesus’ story was a welcome for him.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who does the finding?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story the sheep did nothing at all. The shepherd did all the searching and the carrying. What does that tell us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God takes the first step; he searches before we ask; we can’t rescue ourselves, but he can; he doesn’t scold, he carries; he is glad. Follow-up: ‘Who is the shepherd meant to be?’ (God — and Jesus, who came to find us.) Follow-up 2: ‘How might that feel to someone who has gone their own way?’ If silent: ask, ‘Can a lost sheep find its own way home?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God goes looking for every lost person, and he is glad when one is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I matter so much to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look for the ‘one’ this week: when your group is together, check whether anyone is on the edge, and go to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This quietly previews Thursday’s ‘Welcome in’ challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Eating with sinners</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He even eats with them.”<br>Jesus welcomed the people everyone else avoided.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 15:7. Echo it line by line in a ‘shepherd calling’ voice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Your kingdom come (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his kindness and welcome into our world. Today we pray it for people who feel lost. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Imagine the shepherd out in the dark hills, still looking for one lost sheep. That’s how much God cares about each person. Breathe in… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about what God is like — the Shepherd who never gives up.<br>Prompts: God, you go looking for lost people. / You are glad when even one comes home.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you never give up on anyone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Have you walked past someone who was alone, or wandered off from God yourself?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I ignored someone who was left out. / Sorry for going my own way.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we went our own way.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for searching for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for looking for me. / Thank you that I matter to you, even when I feel small.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for finding us and carrying us home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray “Your kingdom come” for someone who feels lost or lonely.<br>Prompts: Your kingdom come: please find the lonely ones. / Please show (name) that you are looking for them.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, find…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Your kingdom come, God — please find everyone who feels lost.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Jesus’ words together: “Your kingdom come.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Explain ‘kingdom’ simply: where God is King and his good ways rule. Model a one-sentence prayer first. Reassure students that short prayers are great.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: who was really listening when Jesus told this story — and the twist that shocked them. Plus a lost coin!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Who Was Listening? And the Lost Coin":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who Was Listening? And the Lost Coin</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your kingdom come — Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his good rule — his kindness, welcome and rescue — into our school, our homes and our country. This week we pray it for people who feel lost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told this parable, and why it shocked them.</li><li>We are learning who is who in the stories of the lost sheep and the lost coin.</li><li>I can explain what the shepherd and the woman show us about God.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming the two audiences (tax collectors and sinners; Pharisees and teachers), explaining at least one surprise in the story (leaving the ninety-nine, a party for one), and saying that the shepherd and the woman stand for God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper and pencils for the prayer</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Who Was Listening? And the Lost Coin</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Jesus told a story about a shepherd and a lost sheep.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who grumbled about Jesus eating with sinners — and what did the shepherd do when he found his sheep?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses grumbled. The shepherd carried the lamb home on his shoulders and called his friends to celebrate. ‘Today we find out who was really listening, and what shocked them. Then Jesus tells a second story — about a coin.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:2, 8–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:2</b> The Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses started grumbling, “This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:8</b> “Suppose a woman has ten silver coins, and she loses one. Won’t she light a lamp, sweep the floor, and look carefully until she finds it?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:9</b> “Then she will call in her friends and neighbors and say, ‘Let’s celebrate! I’ve found the coin I lost.’”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:10</b> “In the same way, God’s angels are happy when even one sinner turns to God.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The twist that shocked them</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Who was listening when Jesus told this story? Luke tells us. Tax collectors and other sinners were crowding in to hear him. And standing at the edge, arms folded, were the Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses. Two very different groups in one place.</p><p>The Pharisees were not cartoon villains. Many of them loved God and worked hard to obey his rules. But they believed that to stay close to God you had to keep away from people who broke the rules. So when Jesus ate dinner with tax collectors, they were horrified.</p><p>So Jesus told them a parable — an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. And it was full of surprises.</p><p>First, the shepherd. Shepherding was hard, smelly work, and strict religious people often looked down on shepherds. Yet Jesus began, ‘Suppose one of you has a hundred sheep…’ He asked them to imagine themselves as the shepherd!</p><p>Second, what the shepherd does. He leaves ninety-nine sheep out in the open country and goes after one. Any sensible person would say, ‘Cut your losses!’ Not this shepherd. And when he finds it, he doesn’t drive it home. He carries it joyfully on his shoulders.</p><p>Third, the party. Who throws a party for one sheep? But this shepherd calls the whole village: ‘Let’s celebrate!’</p><p>Then came the sting. There is more happiness in heaven over one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to. Who were the ninety-nine good people? The Pharisees thought they were. Jesus was gently asking: do you really not need finding?</p><p>Then he told a second parable, about a woman. She has ten silver coins — about ten days’ wages — and she loses one. Her house is small and dark, with a dirt floor and hardly any windows. So she lights a lamp, sweeps the floor, and looks carefully until she finds it. Then she calls her friends and neighbours: ‘Let’s celebrate! I’ve found the coin I lost.’ Jesus said, ‘In the same way, God’s angels are happy when even one sinner turns to God.’</p><p>So who’s who? The shepherd is God. The woman with the lamp is God. And the sheep and the coin? They’re the lost. Notice that neither one finds its own way home. God does the finding. And the people in the room who had been told their whole lives that they weren’t welcome heard something wonderful: somebody is looking for you, and heaven is ready to throw a party.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Two crowds, one table. · 2. Twist one: leave the ninety-nine? · 3. Twist two: a party for one sheep! · 4. Then a woman and a coin. · 5. Who’s who?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Shepherds were often looked down on by strict religious people in Jesus’ day — a commonly noted background detail. A silver coin (drachma) was about a day’s wages. Some scholars think the woman’s coins may have been part of a headdress, but the text doesn’t say so. Comparing God to a woman sweeping a floor would have been surprising. Be fair to the Pharisees: they stand for anyone who trusts their own goodness.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ parables are earthly stories with heavenly meanings. Who is who in these two stories?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In Jesus’ story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The shepherd</td><td>God — and Jesus, who came to find us</td></tr><tr><td>The lost sheep</td><td>Anyone who is far from God, like the tax collectors</td></tr><tr><td>The ninety-nine</td><td>People who think they’re fine and don’t need finding</td></tr><tr><td>The woman, her lamp and her broom</td><td>God searching carefully in every corner</td></tr><tr><td>The party</td><td>The joy of heaven when one person turns back to God</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is the Seeker. We don’t find our own way home — he finds us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Remind students that ‘parable’ means an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Heaven’s happiness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish what Jesus said: “There is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who…”<br><i>Answer: “…turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” (Luke 15:7)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘How many silver coins did the woman have, and how many did she lose?’ (Ten, and she lost one.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did she do when she found it?’ (Called her friends to celebrate.) If silent: point to the memory verse and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two stories, one ending</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told one story about a shepherd and another about a woman, and they end the same way. Why do you think he told both?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to make the point stronger; so everyone could see themselves — men and women, rich and poor; the shepherd searches outside, the woman inside the house, so God searches everywhere; both end with a party. Follow-up: ‘What is the same in both stories?’ (Something lost, a careful search, joy.) Follow-up 2: ‘What would the tax collectors have felt?’ If silent: ask which story they prefer and why.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Do we all need finding?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Even people who think they are good need God to find them.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Most will agree; draw out why: nobody is perfect; being good doesn’t fix a distance from God; Jesus invited the Pharisees to the party too. Pharisees aren’t the bad guys — they stand for anyone who trusts themselves. Follow-up: ‘Why can it be hard to say ‘I need help’?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What happens if the ninety-nine won’t come to the party?’ If silent: give thinking time and ask for a thumb up or down.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God goes looking for lost people, and Jesus is the Shepherd who finds us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, for searching for me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone who might feel lost or left out at school — a new student, or someone on their own. Today, say hello to them and use their name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t name children in the room. Suggest ‘someone I know of’ or ‘a person I could look out for’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A party for one</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Let’s celebrate! I’ve found my lost sheep!”<br>Heaven is that glad when one person comes home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 15:7 line by line.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write a ‘found’ prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Writing a prayer slows us down so we can think about each word. Today’s prayer is “Your kingdom come” — asking God to bring his welcome to places where people feel lost. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, draw a small coin or a sheep. Underneath, write: ‘Your kingdom come.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about what God is like in these stories.<br>Prompts: You are the Shepherd who goes looking. / You search carefully in every corner.<br>Starter: <b>You are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are the Shepherd who never stops looking for us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for thinking you only look for ‘other’ people, or that you don’t need finding.<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking I don’t need your help. / Sorry for ignoring people who feel left out.<br>Starter: <b>Sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for thinking we can find our own way home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line to thank God for finding you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for never giving up on me. / Thank you that heaven is glad when someone comes home.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are glad when we come home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a ‘Your kingdom come’ prayer for someone on their own.<br>Prompts: Your kingdom come: please find someone who feels lost. / Please help me notice someone who is left out.<br>Starter: <b>Please…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Your kingdom come, God — please find everyone who feels lost.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your paper and keep it. When you see someone on their own, remember it. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite a few volunteers to read their ‘Your kingdom come’ line aloud.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a modern retelling at a crowded Aotearoa beach — a little cousin goes missing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Lost at the Beach":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lost at the Beach</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your kingdom come — Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his good rule — his kindness, welcome and rescue — into our school, our homes and our country. This week we pray it for people who feel lost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the lost sheep parable might look in a story from Aotearoa today.</li><li>We are learning how a whānau searched for one lost child and celebrated when she was found.</li><li>I can explain how the beach story is like the parable Jesus told.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking the beach story to the parable: Ana is the lost sheep, Aroha and the whānau are the searchers, and the celebration shows how valuable one person is.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bucket and a toy crab for Ana</li><li>Optional: a beach towel or sunhat</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Lost at the Beach</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today is our modern retelling, set on an Aotearoa beach.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Dropped everything</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>If someone in your family went missing at a crowded beach, what would you do?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you stay put, or search? Would you keep eating your lunch?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take a few quick answers. Everyone will say they would drop everything. Land it: ‘That’s exactly what Jesus’ story is about. Today we hear it in a modern Aotearoa version, with some kids you know.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:5–7, 9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:5</b> “And when you find it, you will be so glad that you will put it on your shoulder and carry it home.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:6</b> “Then you will call in your friends and neighbors and say, ‘Let’s celebrate! I’ve found my lost sheep.’”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:7</b> “In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:9</b> “Let’s celebrate! I’ve found the coin I lost.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 15:7 is our memory verse. Today’s story is a retelling, not a Bible passage, so we keep the parable words in mind as we listen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Where’s Ana?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It was a hot Saturday, and Aroha’s whānau had taken over a big patch of sand at the beach. Nana was in charge of the picnic. The cousins were digging holes. The sausages were sizzling on a little barbecue. Everything was perfect.</p><p>Aroha’s cousin Ana was five years old, and she was building a sandcastle with a very serious face. Her mum, Aunty Moana, hurried over. ‘Aroha, can you keep an eye on Ana while I grab the sunscreen?’</p><p>‘Sweet as, Aunty,’ said Aroha, and she meant it.</p><p>Then Sione came bounding up with his rugby ball. ‘Aroha! We need a fullback!’ Aroha looked at Ana. Ana looked happy. Just one pass, thought Aroha. One pass turned into ten. Then fifteen.</p><p>When Aunty Moana came back, she looked at the sandcastle. There was a bucket, a spade… and no Ana.</p><p>Aroha’s stomach dropped like a stone. ‘She was right here!’</p><p>Everyone froze for one second. Then everyone moved. Nana had the other cousins safe at the picnic, and Aroha said, ‘Nobody eats a single sausage until Ana is found.’ Even Sione agreed, and Sione really loves sausages.</p><p>They spread out across the beach. Aroha climbed on a driftwood log and called, ‘Ana! Ana!’ Sione and Jun raced through the dunes and the marram grass. Aunties and uncles checked the edge of the water. Jun was scared, and when Jun is scared he gets snappy. ‘You were meant to be watching her!’ he said. Aroha’s eyes stung. ‘I know,’ she whispered. ‘But yelling won’t find her.’</p><p>Then quiet Mia, who had been sketching on a towel nearby, spoke up in a small voice. ‘I think I saw her. I drew her.’ She flipped open her sketchbook. There was a little girl with a stick in her hand, following a seagull towards the rock pools. Mia had almost kept quiet because she was scared of being wrong. But she was brave, and that mattered.</p><p>They ran. Past the lifeguard flag, past the pōhutukawa, all the way down to the rock pools. And there, crouched over a pool with her bucket full of shells, was Ana.</p><p>‘Look, Aroha!’ she said. ‘I found a crab. His name is Gary.’</p><p>Aroha dropped to her knees and hugged her tight, and the whole whānau came running. There were tears, then laughing, then Aunty Moana calling, ‘Ice creams all round! Let’s celebrate!’ And yes — Sione finally got his sausage.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A sunny Saturday at the beach. · 2. One pass… then ten. · 3. No Ana! · 4. Mia’s sketchbook. · 5. Found!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. This is a modern retelling and it is entirely imagined. If a student has ever been lost, reassure them: Ana is found quickly and safely, and everyone is glad. A good safety note if needed: if you’re ever lost, stay in one place and find a safe adult. Keep the focus on the whānau’s joy rather than on blaming Aroha.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where was Ana?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did Mia’s sketchbook show that Ana had gone?<br>A) Towards the rock pools, following a seagull ✓ &nbsp; B) Into the sea &nbsp; C) Back to the car &nbsp; D) To the ice cream shop</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A. Mia had drawn Ana following a seagull towards the rock pools. Follow-up: ‘What did Aroha say nobody would do until Ana was found?’ (Eat a single sausage.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Aunty Moana say when Ana was found?’ (Ice creams for everyone — let’s celebrate!) If silent: tell the first part of the story again briefly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Lost at the Beach</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Lost at the Beach</b> (Luke 15:1–7 (retold)). This is an imagined, modern retelling of Jesus’ parable of the lost sheep. Every character, line and event is made up for today — it is not a Bible passage.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Aroha</b> — Confident, kind — feeling guilty<br><b>Sione</b> — Big-hearted rugby fan — a bit impulsive<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky and quick-tempered — snappy when scared<br><b>Mia</b> — Artist, shy and anxious — brave at the right moment<br><b>Aunty Moana</b> — Ana’s mum — worried, then relieved<br><b>Ana</b> — Aroha’s five-year-old cousin — cheerful and curious<br><b>Everyone</b> — Beach noise, the searching whānau, and the celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: beach noise — splashing waves, seagulls, ‘Ice creams here!’ · Everyone: call ‘Ana! Ana!’ while searching · Everyone: big celebration cheer<br>Props: Optional: a bucket for Ana · Optional: a sketchbook for Mia · Optional: a rugby ball for Sione<br>Keep Jun’s snappy line short and let Aroha’s reply be gentle, not preachy. Let Ana be very small and cheerful. Everyone can make beach noises and search together. If the class is large, use ‘Everyone’ for the whānau.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A sunny Saturday. Aroha’s whānau picnic on a crowded beach. Little Ana is building a sandcastle.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Splash! Whoosh! Ice creams here! <i>(busy beach noise)</i></li><li><b>Aunty Moana:</b> Aroha, keep an eye on Ana while I grab the sunscreen?</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Sweet as, Aunty. I’ll watch her. <i>(thumbs up)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Aroha! Come play beach rugby! We need a fullback! <i>(spin the ball)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Just one pass! <i>(run off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> One pass turned into ten. Then fifteen. Then Aunty Moana came back.</li><li><b>Aunty Moana:</b> Aroha, where’s Ana? <i>(hold up sunscreen, worried)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> She was right here! There’s her bucket… but no Ana! <i>(spin around)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Maybe she’s hiding! Little kids are sneaky. Ana! Ana, where are you? <i>(hands cupped to mouth)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Nana has the other cousins. Nobody eats a single sausage until we find Ana. <i>(firm voice)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Not even one sausage? …Okay. Yeah. Not even one. <i>(sad face)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ana! Ana! Ana! <i>(spread out, calling and searching)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> You were meant to be watching her, Aroha! <i>(snappy, then frown)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I know, Jun. I know. But yelling won’t find her. Keep looking. <i>(deep breath)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Um… I think I saw her. But I’m not sure I’m right. <i>(hold sketchbook, nervous)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Anything, Mia. Please tell us. <i>(gentle)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> She followed a seagull towards the rock pools. I drew it. Look! <i>(show the sketchbook)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Mia almost kept quiet because she was scared of being wrong. What would you say to Mia? (20s)</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Mia, you’re a legend! Everybody, to the rock pools! <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They ran past the lifeguard flag and down to the rock pools. And there she was.</li><li><b>Ana:</b> Look, Aroha! I found a crab. His name is Gary. <i>(hold up bucket)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Ana! I found you! You are not allowed to disappear ever again! <i>(kneel and hug)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> She’s found! Hooray! <i>(big celebration cheer)</i></li><li><b>Aunty Moana:</b> Ice creams for everyone, my shout! Let’s celebrate! <i>(arms wide)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Now can we eat the sausages? <i>(hopeful)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Yes, Sione. But first, I’d search this whole beach again for Ana. She matters to us. <i>(hold Ana’s hand)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who knew where Ana had gone, and how did they help?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Mia. She had drawn Ana following a seagull to the rock pools, and she was brave enough to speak up.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How is this story like the parable of the lost sheep?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Ana is the lost one; Aroha, the whānau and friends are the searchers; Nana and the others were safe; the whānau didn’t stop until she was found; then they celebrated.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why not wait?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Aroha could have waited for Ana to come back by herself. Why did the whānau drop everything to search instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Ana is small and could be in danger; she matters too much to leave; she couldn’t find her own way back; love goes looking. Follow-up: ‘Who in Jesus’ story did the same?’ (The shepherd.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does that show about how God feels about each person?’ If silent: ask, ‘Would a five-year-old know how to find the picnic again?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is ‘Ana’ at our school?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your answer without a name: who might feel like ‘Ana’ — on their own, unseen, or left out? What is one small thing someone could do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: a new student; someone with no one to play with; someone quiet at the edge of the group; someone who has been left out. Small acts: say hello, invite them in, sit with them. Follow-up: ‘What is easier — noticing, or doing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What stops us?’ If silent: give an example, like asking someone to join your game, and let them write.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every person matters so much that it’s worth stopping everything to find one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, that I matter to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look for the ‘Ana’ this week: notice one person drifting to the edge of a group, and go and stand with them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Gently note: if any student feels lost or left out themselves, there are caring adults who will look for them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Found!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Look, Aroha! I found a crab!”<br>She’s found! Let’s celebrate!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 15:7. Try it once slowly, then once fast.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Your kingdom come — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying with a partner means you can pray for each other. We’re still praying “Your kingdom come” — asking God to bring his welcome to people who feel lost. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. You can pray out loud in a whisper, or silently and then share one line.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell your partner one thing God is like from today, then say it to God.<br>Prompts: God, you know exactly where each of us is. / God, you search for us and celebrate when we’re found.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you know where we are and you never stop looking.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for any time you left someone out or didn’t look after someone.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I forgot to look out for someone. / Sorry for the times I stayed quiet when I should have spoken.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we forgot to look out for others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who’ve come looking for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for whānau who notice when I’m missing. / Thank you for friends who include me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who come looking for us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with your partner for someone who feels left out: “Your kingdom come at our school.”<br>Prompts: Your kingdom come: please help someone feel welcome at our school. / Please help us notice who is alone.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Your kingdom come at our school, God — please help us notice who is alone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your partner and say, ‘God is looking for you.’ Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pair up carefully so no one is left without a partner — the lesson’s point! Triples are fine.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the stories show us about God — and how we can welcome people in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: God Goes Looking — Welcome In":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:1–10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God Goes Looking — Welcome In</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your kingdom come — Jesus taught us to pray, “Your kingdom come.” A kingdom is wherever a king rules, so we are asking God to bring his good rule — his kindness, welcome and rescue — into our school, our homes and our country. This week we pray it for people who feel lost.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the lost sheep and lost coin show us about God.</li><li>We are learning that Jesus is the Shepherd who came to find us.</li><li>I can think of one way to welcome someone in this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can say that God goes looking for lost people and celebrates when they are found, name Jesus as the Shepherd, recite Luke 15:7, and choose one way to welcome someone in.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: God Goes Looking — Welcome In</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put the parable in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Jesus’ story of the lost sheep in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A sheep goes missing → The shepherd leaves the ninety-nine → He searches until he finds it → He carries it home on his shoulders → He calls his friends and neighbours → Everyone celebrates</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out: the shepherd does all the searching and carrying, and the story ends in a party. Then ask what the woman did with the coin.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:10; 19:10; John 10:11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:10</b> “In the same way, God’s angels are happy when even one sinner turns to God.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 19:10</b> “The Son of Man came to look for and to save people who are lost.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>John 10:11</b> “I am the good shepherd. A good shepherd is willing to die for the sheep.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 6:10</b> “Bring in your kingdom so that everyone on earth will obey you, as you are obeyed in heaven.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. ‘Son of Man’ is a name Jesus used for himself. The Matthew 6:10 line is the part of the Lord’s Prayer we pray this week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Look who’s coming</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s put the two stories together. A shepherd lost one sheep out of a hundred. A woman lost one coin out of ten. Both of them searched until they found what was lost. And both of them threw a party.</p><p>Why did Jesus tell us this? Because he was showing us what God is like. God is the one who goes looking. Not because we’re clever, and not because we found our own way home. A sheep can’t climb off a ledge by itself, and a coin can’t jump out from under a bench. God finds us. And when even one person turns back to him, heaven celebrates. The angels are happy!</p><p>Jesus didn’t just tell this story. He lived it. He ate with tax collectors, and he said, ‘The Son of Man came to look for and to save people who are lost.’ Later he said, ‘I am the good shepherd. A good shepherd is willing to die for the sheep.’ That’s how far Jesus was willing to go to find us.</p><p>When we pray, ‘Your kingdom come’, we ask for a world that works like this one, where nobody is left outside.</p><p>Imagine Aroha and Mia at their school welcome, called a pōwhiri, for a new family. A kuia, a respected older woman, calls the family on, and the whole school stands to greet them. Mia notices one of the new boys standing alone at morning tea. For once, she goes first. ‘Want to play handball?’</p><p>That’s the kingdom of God, arriving at one school, one lunchtime.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God goes looking. · 2. Jesus is the Shepherd. · 3. Your kingdom come. · 4. Welcome in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. A pōwhiri is a Māori welcome ceremony; a karanga is the call of welcome by a kuia (respected older woman). If you have a local marae connection or a Māori colleague, invite them to share how welcome works at your school. Keep the focus on welcome, not on making anyone feel guilty.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did they do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the shepherd and the woman both do when they found what was lost?<br>A) They called their friends to celebrate ✓ &nbsp; B) They went back to sleep &nbsp; C) They told it off &nbsp; D) They sold it</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A (Luke 15:6, 9). Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus say happens in heaven?’ (More happiness over one person who turns to God.) Follow-up 2: ‘How many coins did the woman have?’ (Ten.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How does God feel?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Heaven celebrates over every single person who comes home to God. What does that tell us about how God feels about each person?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: each person matters to him; he is glad, not angry; he never gives up; it’s more like a party than a telling-off. Follow-up: ‘How is that different from thinking God is only cross with us?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does Jesus the Shepherd show us about this?’ (He came to look for people and save them.) If silent: ask, ‘How did the shepherd and Aroha feel when they found what was lost?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus ate with people everyone else avoided, and some leaders grumbled.</li><li>Like the shepherd and the woman, God searches for lost people until they are found.</li><li>Heaven throws a party over every single person who turns back to God.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God goes looking for the lost, and heaven throws a party when even one person is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who needs welcoming?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently think: who do you know of that might feel on their own? What is one way you could welcome them in?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is a silent, personal thought. Possible ideas: invite them to your game; sit with them at lunch; say their name; ask a question. Follow-up if anyone wants to share: ‘What might get in the way?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would you want someone to do for you?’ If silent: that is the point — give them quiet time and then ask who has a first step in mind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome in. Notice someone on their own and invite them in — to your game, your table or your group — once this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“There is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God…” — Luke 15:7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday’s story (The Lost Son) will check in on this. Brainstorm real examples: asking someone to join your game, sitting with someone new, learning a name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“In the same way, there is more happiness in heaven because of one sinner who turns to God than over ninety-nine good people who don’t need to.” — Luke 15:7 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 15:7. Bonus: say it while turning around on the spot.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: God goes looking</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God goes looking.<br>And heaven throws a party when one is found.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Your kingdom come — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we pray out loud together. “Your kingdom come” can be a big prayer or a very small one: for your school, your whānau, your town and our country. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In the Wow part, anyone can call out one thing God is. In the Please part, we’ll pray “Your kingdom come” for the places we live.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is, from the stories this week.<br>Prompts: God, you are the Shepherd who goes looking! / God, you are the one who celebrates!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are the Shepherd, the Seeker and the one who celebrates!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: sorry for any time you’ve left someone out or stayed on the edge yourself.<br>Prompts: Sorry for leaving someone out. / Sorry for the times I’ve gone my own way.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry — thank you that you always welcome us home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people and the Shepherd who found you.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for coming to look for us. / Thank you for people who welcome me in.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for coming to look for us and carrying us home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray “Your kingdom come” for our school, our whānau and our country.<br>Prompts: Your kingdom come at our school: help us welcome new people. / Your kingdom come in Aotearoa: please find the lonely ones.<br>Starter: <b>Your kingdom come…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Your kingdom come in our school, our homes and our country.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Your kingdom come. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Invite a few students to call out their ‘Wow’ words and their ‘Please’ places. Let the class say the final line together.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Lost Son — a father, two sons, and a very surprising welcome home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4: The Lost Son":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:11–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Welcoming Father</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your will be done — Jesus taught us to pray, “Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven” (Matthew 6:10). It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. The younger son wanted his own way first — and then found that the Father’s way was home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Son Who Left</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how the younger son in Jesus’ story left home and lost everything.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Son Who Left → My way! → Luke 15:11–13, 17, 20 → The road home → Rock bottom → Letting go → The Lost Son → Coming to his senses → Head · Heart · Hands → Come to his senses → Memory verse peek → Your will be done (silent)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Father Who Runs</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why Jesus told the story of the lost son — and who was listening.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Father Who Runs → Recap → Luke 15:1–2, 28, 31–32 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → A running father → Why did he run? → Was he right to be angry? → Head · Heart · Hands → Everything I have is yours → Build the verse → Write to the Father</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Jun’s Long Walk Home</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the story of the lost son could happen in Aotearoa today.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Jun’s Long Walk Home → Today’s version → Luke 15:17–20, 24 → A long walk in the rain → A long way off → Jun’s Long Walk Home → What Dad shows us → Ling’s arms → Head · Heart · Hands → Dad runs! → Verse with actions → Pray for your partner</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Home — and a Party</b> · 17.5 min<br>We are learning what the lost son story shows us about God and his kingdom.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Home — and a Party → Who said it? → Luke 15:22–24, 31–32 → Two sons, one Father → Three gifts → Both lost → The Big Idea → Which son? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → A robe, a ring, sandals → Open hands</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Son Who Left":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:11–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Son Who Left</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your will be done — Jesus taught us to pray, “Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven” (Matthew 6:10). It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. The younger son wanted his own way first — and then found that the Father’s way was home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the younger son in Jesus’ story left home and lost everything.</li><li>We are learning what the father did when his son came home.</li><li>I can explain what it means that the son “came to his senses”.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling the story in order (asking for the money, wasting it, the pigs, deciding to go home, the running father) and noticing that the father welcomed his son before he finished his speech.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: an empty drawstring bag for the son’s money</li><li>Optional: a tea towel for the father’s robe</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Son Who Left</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This is probably the most famous of all Jesus’ stories — build the anticipation.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: My way!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever wanted to do things completely your own way — no rules, no one telling you what to do?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you do first? What might go wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week Jesus told us about the shepherd who searched for one lost sheep and the woman who swept her house for one lost coin. Your challenge was “Welcome in”: notice someone on their own and invite them in. Who did you welcome, and what happened?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: celebrate 2–3 students who invited someone in (keep names light; don’t pressure those who didn’t). Then the hook: later bedtime, no veges, all the screen time. Take a couple of answers, including what could go wrong. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about a son who wants exactly that — and what happens next.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:11–13, 17, 20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:11–12</b> Jesus also told them another story: “Once a man had two sons. The younger son said to his father, ‘Give me my share of the property.’”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:13</b> Not long after that, the younger son packed up everything he owned and left for a foreign country, where he wasted his money on wild living.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:17</b> Finally, he came to his senses and said, “My father’s workers have plenty to eat, and here I am, starving to death!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:20</b> But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt sorry for him. He ran to his son and hugged and kissed him.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. Luke 15 is where Jesus tells three ‘lost’ stories in a row — last week’s sheep and coin, and today’s son. ‘Wild living’ is the Bible’s own gentle phrase; no need to add detail.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The road home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus was sitting down to a meal with some tax collectors and other people that everyone called ‘sinners’. The religious leaders stood at the edge and grumbled. So Jesus told them a story. It begins like this.</p><p>A man had two sons. They lived on a big farm, with fields and animals and workers. One day the younger son said to his father, ‘Give me my share of the property.’</p><p>That was a shocking thing to say. Normally a son only got his share after his father died. It was like saying, ‘I wish you weren’t here — I just want your stuff.’ And what did the father do? He didn’t shout. He didn’t lock the gate. Sadly, he divided his property between his sons, and let the younger one go.</p><p>Not long after that, the younger son packed up everything he owned and left for a faraway country. He didn’t look back. And there, the Bible says, he wasted his money on wild living. Parties, new clothes, new ‘friends’ who loved him as long as he was paying. One coin after another, until the bag was empty.</p><p>Then a famine hit that land — a time when there wasn’t enough food for anyone. His ‘friends’ vanished. He was hungry and alone, so he took the only job he could find: feeding pigs. For a Jewish man, pigs were unclean animals. This was as low as life could get. He was so hungry that he would have been glad to eat what the pigs were eating. But no one gave him anything.</p><p>And there, in the mud, the Bible says something wonderful. He came to his senses. He thought, ‘My father’s workers have plenty to eat, and here I am, starving! I’ll go home. I’ll say, “Father, I have sinned against God and against you. Treat me like one of your workers.”’</p><p>So he got up and started back, practising his speech the whole way.</p><p>But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him. The father had been watching the road. He felt sorry for him — and he ran to his son, and hugged and kissed him.</p><p>The son began his speech. But his father was already calling to the servants: ‘Hurry! Bring the best clothes! A ring! Sandals! Let’s have a feast — this son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found!’ And they began to celebrate.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. “Give me my share.” · 2. Every coin spent. · 3. A famine. Hungry. Feeding pigs. · 4. He came to his senses. · 5. His father ran!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Imagined details (parties, new clothes, ‘friends’ vanishing, the father watching the road) are plausible fill-ins from the text. Keep ‘wild living’ vague — if asked, say ‘he spent his money on whatever felt fun at the time and it didn’t last.’ Pigs were unclean under the Law of Moses, so a Jewish listener would hear this as the very bottom. Some students may have a dad who is absent or difficult: remind them God is the perfect Father, not a copy of anyone’s dad.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Rock bottom</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What job did the younger son end up with when his money ran out?<br>A) Baking bread &nbsp; B) Feeding pigs ✓ &nbsp; C) Sailing a boat &nbsp; D) Guarding a palace</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 15:15). Follow-up: ‘What had happened in that land?’ (A famine, so there was no food.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the son wish he could eat?’ (The pigs’ food.) If silent: re-read the third scripture line and ask where he ended up.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Letting go</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The father knew his son was making a bad choice, but he let him go. Why do you think he did that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: love can’t be forced; a son has to choose to stay; the father trusted he might come home; he respected the choice even though it hurt. Follow-up: ‘What did the father do while his son was away?’ (He kept watching the road.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about God?’ If silent: ask ‘Could you make someone love you by locking the door?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Lost Son</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Lost Son</b> (Luke 15:11–24). The father’s, son’s and narrator’s key words are from Luke 15 (CEV). The farmer, the party friends and the pig noises are imagined to fill out the scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Father</b> — Loving, watchful — he never stops hoping<br><b>Younger Son</b> — Restless and cocky, then hungry and humble<br><b>Farmer</b> — A stranger who owns the pigs<br><b>Servant</b> — Works for the father — quick and delighted<br><b>Everyone</b> — Party friends, hungry pigs, then the big celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: party cheers when the son has money · Everyone (as pigs): ‘Oink! Snort!’ · Everyone: a big celebration at the end<br>Props: Optional: an empty bag for the money · Optional: a tea towel robe for the son at the end<br>The older brother is deliberately left out today — he walks into the story on Tuesday. Keep the son’s ‘wild living’ light-hearted in performance (party noises), nothing more. The farmer’s line is imagined; the Bible only says the son went to work for someone who sent him to feed pigs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A big farm. A father has two sons. The older one is out working in the fields.</li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> Dad, give me my share of the property. I want it now. <i>(arms folded, impatient)</i></li><li><b>Father:</b> Oh, my son. Very well. Here is your share. <i>(sad, but gentle)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Not long after that, the younger son packed up everything and left for a faraway country.</li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> Goodbye! I’m going to live it up! <i>(stride off, never look back)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Party! Party! Party! <i>(cheer — the ‘friends’ who love his money)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> He wasted his money on wild living, until every coin was gone.</li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> Hey — where did everyone go? My bag is empty! <i>(turn the bag upside down)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then a famine hit. There was hardly any food anywhere.</li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> Please — any work at all? I’ll do anything. <i>(beg)</i></li><li><b>Farmer:</b> You can look after my pigs. That’s all I’ve got for you. <i>(point)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Oink! Oink! Snort! <i>(hungry pigs crowd round)</i></li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> The pigs eat better than I do. My father’s workers have plenty to eat — and here I am, starving! <i>(slump down)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The son has just come to his senses. What do you think he decides to do? (20s)</li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> I’ll go home. I’ll say, ‘Father, I have sinned against God and against you. Treat me like one of your workers.’ <i>(stand up slowly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> He got up and started for home, practising his speech the whole way.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> While he was still a long way off, his father saw him. He had been watching the road.</li><li><b>Father:</b> My son! My son is home! <i>(run to him, arms wide!)</i></li><li><b>Younger Son:</b> Father, I have sinned against God and against you. I am no longer good enough to be called your son. <i>(head down)</i></li><li><b>Father:</b> Servants — hurry! Bring the best clothes. Put a ring on his finger and sandals on his feet. <i>(hug him tight)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> Yes, sir! The best robe — right away! <i>(dash off)</i></li><li><b>Father:</b> Prepare the best calf. Let’s have a feast and celebrate!</li><li><b>Father:</b> This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> He was lost — but now he is found! Hooray! <i>(cheer and dance)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the son plan to say to his father — and what did the father do before he could finish?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He planned to ask to be treated like a worker (15:19). His father ran, hugged him and called for a robe, ring and sandals before he finished (15:20–22). He never got to say the ‘worker’ part.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did you notice about the father in that scene?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was watching, he ran, he forgave without a lecture, he threw a party. He did not wait for the son to prove himself first.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Coming to his senses</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The son only decided to go home when he was starving. Why is it so hard to say sorry and turn back — and what helped him do it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: pride; embarrassment; fear of the reaction; hoping no one finds out; it took hitting rock bottom. What helped: remembering his father’s kindness even to his workers. Follow-up: ‘What did he expect his father to say?’ (Treat me like a worker.) Follow-up 2: ‘What do you think made him believe he could go home at all?’ If silent: ask who has ever been scared to say sorry.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is like the father who watches the road and runs to welcome us home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Father, I want to come home to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, when someone asks you to do something you don’t want to do (tidy up, get off a screen, come in from play), try the ‘your will be done’ choice: do it the first time without a grumble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Connect it to today’s prayer phrase: the younger son said ‘my share, my way’. ‘Your will be done’ is the opposite.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Come to his senses</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“…and here I am, starving to death!”<br>He came to his senses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 15:24 — the father’s words at the party.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Your will be done (silent)</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we’re praying through the Lord’s Prayer. This week’s line is “Your will be done”. It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. Today’s story shows us what happens when we run off with ‘my way’ — and a Father who is waiting. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s get still. Close your eyes or look at the floor. Imagine a long road — and a father standing at the end of it, watching.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is a Father who watches the road. Think about that.<br>Prompts: God, you are a Father who never stops loving his children. / God, you run to meet us when we turn toward home.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are a Father who runs to welcome us home!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The son said, ‘Give me my share.’ Is there a time you wanted your own way more than God’s way?<br>Prompts: Sorry for wanting my own way. / Sorry for the times I’ve walked away from you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry for the times we walked away from you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that the road home is always open.<br>Prompts: Thank you for watching the road for me. / Thank you for welcoming me back — not as a worker, but as your child.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, that the road home is always open.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together: ‘Your will be done.’<br>Prompts: Please help me trust your way, even when I want my own. / Please help me come to my senses quickly when I go the wrong way.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, your will be done — help me trust your way.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first: ‘Father, thank you that I can always come home to you.’ Reassure students that short and simple is perfect.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — and the shocking twists that made the religious leaders go quiet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Father Who Runs":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:11–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father Who Runs</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your will be done — Jesus taught us to pray, “Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven” (Matthew 6:10). It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. The younger son wanted his own way first — and then found that the Father’s way was home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Jesus told the story of the lost son — and who was listening.</li><li>We are learning who each character stands for, and the twist that would have shocked the crowd.</li><li>I can explain what the story teaches us about God the Father.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students identifying the original audience (Pharisees and teachers grumbling about Jesus welcoming sinners), matching father = God, younger son = the lost, older son = the Pharisees, and explaining why a running father was shocking.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper for the prayer-writing time</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Father Who Runs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday a younger son left home and lost everything.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did he end up — and what did his father do when he came home?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>He ended up feeding pigs, starving. His father saw him a long way off, ran to him, hugged and kissed him, and called for the best robe, a ring and sandals. Take one or two answers, then: ‘Today we find out why Jesus told this story — and the part we left out.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:1–2, 28, 31–32</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:1–2</b> Tax collectors and sinners were all crowding around to listen to Jesus. So the Pharisees and the teachers of the Law of Moses started grumbling, “This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:28</b> The older brother got so angry that he would not even go into the house. His father came out and begged him to come in.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:31</b> His father replied, “My son, you have always been with me, and everything I have is yours.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:32</b> But we had to celebrate and be glad. Your brother was dead, but now he is alive. He was lost, but now he is found.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected. The whole of Luke 15 begins with the grumbling in verse 2. That is the reason Jesus tells all three ‘lost’ stories.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Before we get to the twists, let’s ask: why did Jesus tell this story at all?</p><p>Luke chapter 15 begins with a crowd. Tax collectors and ‘sinners’ were pushing in close to listen to Jesus. Tax collectors were Jewish men who collected money for the Roman rulers — and often took extra for themselves, so most people couldn’t stand them. ‘Sinners’ meant people who didn’t keep the religious rules. Everyone else kept their distance. But Jesus welcomed them. He even ate meals with them.</p><p>Standing at the edge were the Pharisees and the teachers of the Law. The Pharisees were religious leaders who worked incredibly hard at keeping every rule. People respected them. And they were grumbling: ‘This man is friendly with sinners. He even eats with them!’</p><p>So Jesus told three stories in a row: a lost sheep, a lost coin, and a lost son. And the last story had twists that would have made the crowd gasp.</p><p>Twist number one: the request. In Jesus’ world, asking for your share of the property while your father was still alive was a terrible insult. Everyone listening expected the father to be furious.</p><p>Twist number two: the pigs. Pigs were unclean animals to Jewish people. A Jewish son feeding pigs, wishing he could eat their food? You couldn’t fall any lower.</p><p>Twist number three — the biggest: the father runs. In those days, an important older man didn’t run. It was undignified; he would have to hitch up his long robe in front of the whole village. But this father doesn’t care what anyone thinks. He runs to a son who has shamed him. And before the son can finish his speech, he gives him the best robe, a ring and sandals. He is not a servant. He is a son, welcomed home.</p><p>Then comes the final twist. The older brother. He’s been out working in the field. He hears the music and dancing, and he refuses to go in. He’s furious! ‘I’ve worked for you like a slave, and you never even gave me a little goat!’ And what does the father do? He leaves his own party, walks out into the dark, and begs him to come in. ‘My son, you have always been with me, and everything I have is yours. But we had to celebrate. Your brother was dead, but now he is alive.’</p><p>And then the story just stops. Does the older brother go in? Jesus doesn’t say.</p><p>Because Jesus was talking to the Pharisees. They were the older brother — standing outside with their arms folded while the ‘sinners’ were welcomed to the feast. The father’s door was still open for them, too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A grumbling crowd. · 2. Three shocking twists. · 3. The father RUNS. · 4. Robe. Ring. Sandals. · 5. The older brother stays outside.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. The ‘father never ran’ detail is widely taught as the cultural background (a respected older man would not hitch up his robes to run), but the Bible simply says he ran — present it as ‘would have shocked them’. Pharisees are not villains for all time: they stand for anyone who trusts their own goodness. ‘Servant sandals’: many scholars note that sandals and a ring marked a son and free person. Tax collectors and Pharisees are explained here for the first time this week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every story Jesus told had a meaning. Who does each character stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In Jesus’ day</th></tr><tr><td>The father</td><td>God — the Father who welcomes</td></tr><tr><td>The younger son</td><td>Tax collectors and ‘sinners’ — people who knew they’d wandered</td></tr><tr><td>The older son</td><td>The Pharisees — and anyone who thinks they’re good enough</td></tr><tr><td>The party</td><td>The joy in heaven when someone comes home</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus was saying: this is what God is like — he runs to welcome those who come home, and he invites everyone to the party.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Remind them of ‘an earthly story with a heavenly meaning’ from Week 1.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A running father</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the father do when he saw his son still a long way off?<br>A) Waited at the gate &nbsp; B) Sent a servant to check &nbsp; C) Ran to him and hugged him ✓ &nbsp; D) Locked the door</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C (Luke 15:20). Follow-up: ‘What did he feel when he saw him?’ (He felt sorry for him — compassion.) Follow-up 2: ‘What three things did he give his son?’ (Best clothes, a ring, sandals.) If silent: re-read the Luke 15:20 line from yesterday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why did he run?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ day, a dignified father didn’t run. Why do you think Jesus made the father in his story run?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to show how much God loves us; he doesn’t care how he looks; he wanted to reach his son before the village could shame him; he doesn’t wait for us to clean up first. Follow-up: ‘What does the running show about what God is like?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How might the Pharisees have felt hearing it?’ If silent: ask ‘Would you run for someone you love?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Was he right to be angry?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “The older brother had a good reason to be angry.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both answers are fine if explained. Agree: he worked hard and was never thrown a party. Disagree: his brother was safe and home, and he refused to celebrate. The deeper point is that he thought he’d earned it. Follow-up: ‘What was he missing?’ (He was always with the father; everything was his already.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think Jesus leaves the ending open?’ If silent: ask where they’ve seen someone get a treat and felt cross.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is a Father who runs to welcome us — and he invites everyone to his party.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Father, thank you for not waiting at the gate with your arms folded.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When someone gets praised or a treat and you feel your own arms folding like the older brother, unfold them and say ‘Well done’ to that person at school, in sport or at home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge (celebrate good news for someone else).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Everything I have is yours</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“My son, you have always been with me.”<br>“…and everything I have is yours.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 15:24.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Write to the Father</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying “your will be done” doesn’t mean pretending you have no wishes. Jesus told his Father exactly what he wanted, and then trusted his Father’s plan. Today, write honestly, then hand it over. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your paper, write at the top: ‘Dear Father…’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about the kind of Father God is.<br>Prompts: You run to meet us. / You never wait with your arms folded.<br>Starter: <b>You are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, you run to meet us — you are wonderful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one honest sorry — like the son: ‘I have sinned against God and against you.’<br>Prompts: Father, I’m sorry for… / Sorry for being like the older brother — proud and cross.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry for turning away, and for folding our arms.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line thanking him for the robe, the ring and the sandals — for treating you like his child.<br>Prompts: Thank you for making me part of your family. / Thank you for the party — there is always room.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, that there’s always a place for us at your table.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write ‘Your will be done’ — and one thing you want to hand over to God.<br>Prompts: Please help me hand over (a worry) to you. / Please help me celebrate when others get good things.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Father, your will be done — and help us celebrate with others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your note and keep it somewhere safe. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t collect the notes — they’re private. Invite a couple of volunteers to read out just their ‘Wow’ line.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Jun’s story — what the lost son looks like in Aotearoa today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Jun’s Long Walk Home":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:11–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jun’s Long Walk Home</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your will be done — Jesus taught us to pray, “Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven” (Matthew 6:10). It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. The younger son wanted his own way first — and then found that the Father’s way was home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the story of the lost son could happen in Aotearoa today.</li><li>We are learning what Jun’s dad shows us about God the Father.</li><li>I can say who in Jun’s story is like the father, the younger son and the older son.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students matching Jun, Dad and Ling to the younger son, the father and the older son, and explaining what Dad’s running tells us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a hoodie and a wet-look jacket for Jun</li><li>Optional: a bag of ‘birthday money’ (play money)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Jun’s Long Walk Home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Today’s version</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What if the lost son story happened in Aotearoa today, to a kid like you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where might a modern ‘lost son’ run to? What might he spend his money on?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take 3–4 ideas: the mall, the arcade, online shopping, bubble tea. Quickly recap Tuesday: a father who runs, and an older brother outside. Land it: ‘Meet Jun. He’s ten, he rides a bike, and he’s got a birthday coming up. This is a made-up story — but the shape is Jesus’ story.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:17–20, 24</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:17</b> Finally, he came to his senses and said, “My father’s workers have plenty to eat, and here I am, starving to death!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:18</b> I will go to my father and say to him, ‘Father, I have sinned against God in heaven and against you.’</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:20</b> But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt sorry for him. He ran to his son and hugged and kissed him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:24</b> This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 15:24 is our memory verse. Verse 18 is partly quoted.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A long walk in the rain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jun had just turned ten, and his Gong Gong and Po Po had given him two hundred dollars for his birthday. It was the most money he’d ever had. Jun had big plans. A new game, a new skate helmet, bubble tea for everyone.</p><p>But Dad had other plans. ‘Half goes into savings, mate,’ he said at breakfast. ‘And we’ll buy a proper bike lock with the rest.’</p><p>‘It’s MY money!’ Jun shouted. ‘You never let me decide anything!’ His older sister Ling called from the couch, ‘Jun, you’re going to regret this.’ But Jun grabbed the envelope, stuffed it in his backpack, and slammed the door.</p><p>He caught the bus to the mall. And for a few hours, Jun was a legend. He bought tokens for the arcade and handed them around. He shouted bubble tea for kids from his school. ‘Jun, you’re the best!’ they said. ‘Sweet as!’ He bought a fancy light-up gadget that he didn’t even like very much. The money disappeared note by note. Every time Jun heard ‘Sweet as, Jun!’ he felt like the king of the mall, and he didn’t once stop to count what was left.</p><p>By mid-afternoon, Jun reached into his pocket and found fifty cents. His phone was flat. His new mates had somehow wandered off. And then the sky turned grey and the rain began.</p><p>Jun had no bus fare. So he started to walk. It was a long way. His shoes squelched, his hood dripped, and with every block he thought about what he’d said to Dad. By the time he turned into his own street, he’d practised a speech: ‘Dad, I wrecked it. I’ll do chores to pay it back. You don’t have to treat me like your son anymore.’</p><p>Jun stopped at the end of the street, soaked and shaking. And then the front door flew open.</p><p>Dad had been watching from the window. He didn’t grab an umbrella. He didn’t even grab his shoes. He ran down the driveway in his socks, straight through the puddles, and threw his arms around his drenched son.</p><p>Up on the porch, Ling leaned against the doorframe with her arms crossed. She was not smiling — not yet.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. An argument at home. · 2. A day at the mall. · 3. Empty pockets. Rain. · 4. Dad RAN! · 5. Ling folded her arms.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. This is an imagined, modern retelling written for this lesson — Jun, Dad, Ling and the mates are made up; the shape follows Luke 15:11–32. Gong Gong and Po Po are Cantonese for maternal grandfather and grandmother; use ‘Grandpa and Grandma’ if you prefer. Some students may not live with a dad or may find Dad difficult: say ‘a caring adult’ where you need to, and remind them God is the perfect Father.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A long way off</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “While Jun was still a long way off, his dad saw him and…”<br><i>Answer: “…ran to him and hugged him.” (Like Luke 15:20.)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Follow-up: ‘What was Dad wearing on his feet?’ (Just his socks.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was Jun planning to say?’ (That he’d do chores and wasn’t good enough to be called Dad’s son.) If silent: point to the last beats of the story slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Jun’s Long Walk Home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Jun’s Long Walk Home</b> (Luke 15:11–32 (modern retelling)). This is a modern retelling written for this lesson. Jun, Dad, Ling and the mates are imagined; the story follows Jesus’ parable in Luke 15:11–32.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jun</b> — Ten, cheeky and quick-tempered — then soaked and sorry<br><b>Dad</b> — Loving and a bit protective — runs when it counts<br><b>Ling</b> — Jun’s older sister — responsible and a bit cross<br><b>Mate</b> — A kid from school who loves a free bubble tea<br><b>Everyone</b> — Arcade noises, rain, then the celebration</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: arcade beeps and bubbles · Everyone: rain sounds by tapping fingers on desks · Everyone: cheer for the fish and chips<br>Props: Optional: a hoodie for Jun · Optional: play money in an envelope<br>Keep ‘Dad’ warm, not saintly or comic. The Ling role is a gift to a student who wants a bit of drama — encourage them to play it honestly, not as a villain. Nobody is stereotyped: Jun is a cheeky ten-year-old, not a ‘type’. Say ‘a caring adult’ if students’ home situations are sensitive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Saturday morning at Jun’s house. Jun has two hundred dollars of birthday money in an envelope.</li><li><b>Dad:</b> Half of that goes into savings, mate. We’ll buy a proper bike lock with the rest. <i>(calm, gentle)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> It’s MY birthday money! You never let me decide anything! <i>(stomp)</i></li><li><b>Ling:</b> Jun, you’re going to regret this. Just saying. <i>(calling from the couch)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Forget it. I’ll spend it myself. See ya! <i>(grab the envelope, slam the door)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jun caught the bus to the mall with every dollar in his pocket.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ding-ding-ding! Beep! Zoom! <i>(arcade noises)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Games for everyone! Bubble tea, too. My shout! <i>(wave the cash around)</i></li><li><b>Mate:</b> Jun, you’re the best! Sweet as, bro! <i>(high-five)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> New mates came and went. So did the dollars.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Wait. I’ve only got fifty cents? And my phone’s flat! <i>(turn pockets inside out)</i></li><li><b>Mate:</b> Cool story. Catch you later, Jun! <i>(wander off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> No money, no bus fare — and it started to rain.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Pitter-patter… pitter-patter… pitter-patter… <i>(rain sounds, getting louder)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> I’ll go home and say sorry. ‘Dad, I wrecked it. I’ll do chores to pay it back.’ <i>(shiver, keep walking)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Jun is soaked and nervous at the end of his street. What do you think Dad will do? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Dad had been watching from the window. When he saw a soggy figure, he flung open the door.</li><li><b>Dad:</b> JUN! You’re home! <i>(run down the driveway in socks)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Dad, I’ve been so stupid. I wasted all of — <i>(choke up)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Doesn’t matter. I thought I’d lost you. You’re home. That’s all that matters. <i>(big hug)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> I’ll do the dishes for a year. I’ll wash the car, I’ll — <i>(babbling)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Ling! Put the fish and chips on. We’re having a party! <i>(shout to the house)</i></li><li><b>Ling:</b> Seriously? He blows the lot and gets fish and chips? <i>(arms crossed on the porch)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Come here, love. You’re always with me. But Jun was lost and now he’s found. We have to celebrate. <i>(gentle)</i></li><li><b>Ling:</b> …Fine. But he’s still doing the dishes. <i>(half a smile, uncross arms)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Fish and chips! Hooray! <i>(cheer)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who in Jun’s story is like the father, the younger son and the older son — and what is the same?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Dad = the father (he runs, forgives, throws a party); Jun = younger son (wasted it, came home); Ling = older son (fair complaint, arms folded, still invited). Same: the welcome, the party, the cross sibling.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Dad do when he saw Jun, and what did he say about the party?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He ran in his socks, hugged him, and said ‘Jun was lost and now he’s found, we have to celebrate’ — the very words of Luke 15:32.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What Dad shows us</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jun expected Dad to be angry, or to make him earn his way back. What did Dad do instead — and what does that show about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Dad ran, hugged, forgave, celebrated; he cared more about Jun than the money; God does the same — he doesn’t make us earn our way back. Follow-up: ‘What was the first thing Dad said to Jun?’ (‘You’re home!’) Follow-up 2: ‘Does Dad pretend it didn’t happen?’ (No — Jun still wants to make it right; love and honesty go together.) If silent: ask ‘What would you have expected?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Ling’s arms</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ling stood with her arms crossed. On your whiteboard, write what you think Ling is feeling — and what Dad wants her to do.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Likely: it’s not fair; she did the right thing and got no party; she’s worried Jun is just getting away with it. Dad wants her to join the celebration because Jun is safe, and she’s always been loved. Follow-up: ‘Have you ever felt like Ling?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What might help her unfold her arms?’ If silent: ask them to think of a time a sibling got a treat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is a Father who runs to us — even when we’ve wasted what he gave us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Father, thank you that I can always come home to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When someone comes back after a mess-up (a friend who said something mean, a sibling who broke your things), be the one who runs to meet them: say ‘It’s okay, welcome back’ instead of ‘I told you so’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This previews Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Dad runs!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“JUN! You’re home!”<br>Dad didn’t even stop for his shoes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 15:24.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Pray for your partner</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your will be done” is hard to say when we’re upset or when we want our own way. Today, pray with a partner: be the kind of friend who prays for someone else’s good. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Take turns: one prays a sentence, then the other. If you don’t want to pray out loud, pray silently and say ‘Amen’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell God one thing about him from Jun’s story.<br>Prompts: Father, you run to us in the rain. / Father, you’d rather have us home than get the money back.<br>Starter: <b>Father, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, you run to meet us — you are so good!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there something you need to say sorry for — like Jun’s ‘I wasted it’?<br>Prompts: Sorry for wasting what you’ve given me. / Sorry for storming off when I didn’t get my way.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry for the times we storm off and go our own way.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for one person who welcomed you back after a mistake.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who say ‘you’re home’ instead of ‘I told you so’. / Thank you that your door is never locked.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Father, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, that your door is never locked.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your partner by name: ‘Father, may your will be done in (name)’s life this week.’<br>Prompts: Please show (name) your way this week. / Please bring home anyone who feels far away from you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Father, your will be done in our lives this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pairs can be silent if students prefer. Pair carefully so no one is left out.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God — and your challenge for the week.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Home — and a Party":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">17.5 minutes (18.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 15:11–32</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Home — and a Party</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Your will be done — Jesus taught us to pray, “Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven” (Matthew 6:10). It means: God, I trust your way more than my own. The younger son wanted his own way first — and then found that the Father’s way was home.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the lost son story shows us about God and his kingdom.</li><li>We are learning why both sons needed the father’s grace.</li><li>I can say one way to come home to God and one way to celebrate with someone else.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the three signs of welcome (robe, ring, sandals), explain that both sons needed grace, say what the story shows about God, and recite Luke 15:24.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Home — and a Party</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Younger son, father or older son?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Give me my share of the property.” — <b>The younger son</b><br>“Hurry and bring the best clothes and put them on him.” — <b>The father</b><br>“For years I have worked for you like a slave.” — <b>The older son</b><br>“My son, you have always been with me, and everything I have is yours.” — <b>The father</b><br>“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive!” — <b>The father</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; the class calls out the speaker; click to reveal. Ask: ‘When did he say it?’ and ‘What does it show about him?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 15:22–24, 31–32</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–3:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:22</b> But his father said to the servants, “Hurry and bring the best clothes and put them on him. Also put a ring on his finger and sandals on his feet.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:24</b> “This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” And they began to celebrate.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:31</b> “My son, you have always been with me, and everything I have is yours.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 15:32</b> “But we had to celebrate and be glad. Your brother was dead, but now he is alive. He was lost, but now he is found.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verses selected.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Two sons, one Father</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:30–5:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s finish the story and ask: what does it show us about God?</p><p>The father gave his son a robe, a ring and sandals. They weren’t rewards. They said: ‘You belong. You’re my child.’ The son had come home planning to be a worker. The father gave him a place at the table.</p><p>That’s what we call grace. Grace is love and welcome that we haven’t earned. The younger son needed grace because he had run away and wasted everything. But the older son needed grace too. He never left the farm, but he stood outside with his arms folded, thinking he’d earned his reward. Both sons were lost — one far away, one right in the yard. And the father loved them both. He ran to the one, and he walked out into the dark to the other.</p><p>That is what God the Father is like. And here is how Jesus makes the way home. In the story, the older brother stayed home. But Jesus, God’s own Son, left his Father’s house to come looking for us. He said, ‘The Son of Man came to look for and to save people who are lost.’ He didn’t wait at the gate. He came the whole way.</p><p>And Jun and Ling? Later that night in the kitchen, Ling slid a plate of fish and chips across the bench. ‘Dishes. Tomorrow,’ she said. Jun grinned. It wasn’t a perfect ending. But it was a start. Ling had unfolded her arms.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A robe. A ring. Sandals. · 2. Both sons were lost. · 3. Jesus came to find us. · 4. Back at Jun’s place…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. ‘Grace’ is introduced here: undeserved welcome and kindness. The quote about the Son of Man is Luke 19:10. Careful: Jesus ‘left his Father’s house’ is a gentle picture (John 1:14; Philippians 2) — don’t over-press the older brother comparison. Also the pigs and ‘wild living’ are not to be revisited in detail.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Three gifts</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which three things did the father give his son when he came home?<br>A) A map, a bag and a horse &nbsp; B) A robe, a ring and sandals ✓ &nbsp; C) A sword, a crown and a coin &nbsp; D) A tent, a goat and a cloak</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B (Luke 15:22). Follow-up: ‘What do these show?’ (He is a son, not a servant — he belongs.) Follow-up 2: ‘What else did the father give?’ (A feast.) If silent: re-read the first line on the scripture slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Both lost</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–8:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both sons needed the father’s grace. How was the older brother lost, even though he never left home?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he thought he’d earned everything; he wouldn’t celebrate; he saw his father as a boss, not a dad; he stood outside in the dark; he was close in body but far in his heart. Follow-up: ‘What did the father do for each son?’ (Ran to one; went out to the other.) Follow-up 2: ‘Which son finds it easier to say sorry?’ If silent: sketch two lost sons: one far away, one outside.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: The Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God is a Father who watches the road, runs to meet us, and welcomes us home as his children.</li><li>Both sons needed grace — one ran away, the other stayed with his arms folded.</li><li>Jesus came to look for and save people who are lost. He makes the way home.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in: The Father runs to welcome us home — and both sons needed his grace.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which son?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:30–11:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think quietly: this week, are you more like the younger son, the older son, or the Father? Why?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>No one needs to share. If students do: younger = ran off, hiding something; older = resentful, comparing; father = welcoming, forgiving, celebrating. Follow-up: ‘What might it look like to come home?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would the Father say to you?’ If silent: model it by saying where you’ve been like the older brother.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:00–13:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Come home. Pray an honest “sorry” to God where you need to — then, like the Father, celebrate good news for someone else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive! He was lost, but now he has been found.” — Luke 15:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 15:24.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A robe, a ring, sandals</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“This son of mine was dead, but now he is alive!”<br>“He was lost, but now he has been found.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–18:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands closed tightly: that’s ‘my will’. Open them, palms up: that’s ‘your will be done’. Praying with open hands helps us hand over what we want. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands into fists. Now slowly open them, palms up, resting on your knees.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">With your hands open, tell God what he is like.<br>Prompts: Father, you are the one who runs to meet us. / Father, you welcome the proud and the lost.<br>Starter: <b>Father, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, you run to welcome us — you are wonderful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: hold something you need to say sorry for in your closed hands. Then slowly open them.<br>Prompts: Sorry for running off. / Sorry for folding my arms when someone else was celebrated.<br>Starter: <b>Father, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Father, we’re sorry — and we thank you for welcoming us home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the robe, the ring and the sandals.<br>Prompts: Thank you for making me your child. / Thank you for Jesus, who came to find us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Father, for welcoming us — and for Jesus, who came to find us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray together with open hands: ‘Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.’<br>Prompts: Please help me say sorry where I need to. / Please help me celebrate with someone else this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Father, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven. Amen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Open hands are optional. If it suits your class, finish by saying the line of the Lord’s Prayer together.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Two Builders — Jesus’ story about a wise builder, a foolish builder and a storm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5: Two Builders":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 7:24–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rock</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our daily bread — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Give us our food for today” (Matthew 6:11, CEV) — the line people often call “our daily bread”. Daily bread means asking God for what we need today, not for everything at once, and trusting him to give again tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Wise and the Foolish Builder</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Jesus ended the Sermon on the Mount with a story about two builders.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Wise and the Foolish Builder → It looked great… until → Matthew 7:24–27 → Two builders → What was underneath? → Slower, but wiser → The Two Builders → Hearing without doing → Head · Heart · Hands → The end of the sermon → Memory verse peek → Our daily bread — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Jesus Told It</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told the two builders, and why he told it.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Jesus Told It → Recap → Matthew 7:24, 26, 28–29 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → Authority → Same, until the storm → Does the rock mean no storms? → Head · Heart · Hands → The storm comes → Build the verse → Our daily bread — write a list</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Hut-Building Day</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story about two builders looks in a modern school bush setting.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Hut-Building Day → Rushed it → Matthew 7:24, 26–27 → Hut-building day → What did Sione tie to? → Hut-Building Day → Hearing isn’t enough → A hand to rebuild → Head · Heart · Hands → Still standing → Verse with actions → Our daily bread — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Rock Solid</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the two builders show us about God and about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Rock Solid → True or false? → Matthew 7:24–25 · 6:11 → Built on the rock → What made the difference? → Jesus’ words are rock → This week’s big idea → All by myself? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Rock solid → Our daily bread — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Wise and the Foolish Builder":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 7:24–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Wise and the Foolish Builder</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our daily bread — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Give us our food for today” (Matthew 6:11, CEV) — the line people often call “our daily bread”. Daily bread means asking God for what we need today, not for everything at once, and trusting him to give again tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus ended the Sermon on the Mount with a story about two builders.</li><li>We are learning what made the difference between the house that stood and the house that fell.</li><li>I can explain what it means to build on solid rock (Matthew 7:24).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that both builders heard Jesus’ words, that one house stood on rock and one fell on sand, and that the difference was doing what Jesus says, not just hearing it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a tray of sand and a stone to show sand and rock</li><li>Optional: a pretend pickaxe (a ruler or a rolled-up newspaper)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Wise and the Foolish Builder</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have a tray of sand and a stone, put them on the table where students can see them all week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: It looked great… until</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever built something — a sandcastle, a Lego tower, a hut — that looked amazing, and then fell down? What went wrong?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think was missing underneath?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard The Lost Son — the father who ran to welcome his boy home. Your challenge: pray an honest “sorry” where you need to, and celebrate good news for someone else. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students which they tried, an honest sorry or celebrating with someone, and how it felt. Then the hook: tide coming in on a sandcastle, a tower with a wobbly base. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about two builders, and what’s underneath matters more than what you can see.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 7:24–27</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:24</b> Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:25</b> Rain poured down, rivers flooded, and the wind blew hard against that house. But it did not fall, because it was built on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:26</b> Anyone who hears my teachings and doesn’t obey them is like a foolish person who built a house on sand.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:27</b> Rain poured down, rivers flooded, and the wind blew hard against that house. Finally, it fell with a crash.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 7:24 is our memory verse. Notice the two halves are almost identical: same words heard, same storm, same houses. Only one thing differs.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Two builders</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture a grassy hillside above the Sea of Galilee. Jesus has been teaching for a long time — so long that we call it the Sermon on the Mount. He has told the crowd who is truly happy in God’s kingdom. He has taught them to love their enemies, to stop worrying, and even how to pray. Now he is nearly finished.</p><p>But before he stops, Jesus wants everyone to know what to do with all those words. So he tells one last story. Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. This one is about two builders.</p><p>Both builders want the same thing: a house of their own. Both are strong. Both have good tools. And both have heard exactly the same words from Jesus.</p><p>The first builder looks at the ground. He knows the rainy season is coming, and in that country a dry riverbed can turn into a roaring river overnight. So he doesn’t build on the nice flat sand. He grabs a pickaxe and digs. Down and down, through dust, through gravel, hour after hour, his arms aching and his shirt wet with sweat, until his pickaxe goes clunk. Solid rock! Then he hauls heavy stones, one at a time, and builds his house right on top.</p><p>The second builder looks at the ground too. He sees a lovely, smooth, sandy riverbed. Flat. Easy. Digging? Who needs digging! He stacks up his walls in half the time, and soon his house is finished. He puts his feet up. And honestly? From the outside, it looks just as good as the other one.</p><p>Then one day the sky turns black. Rain pours down. The rivers flood. The wind howls and slams into both houses.</p><p>The house on the rock shakes in the wind, but it does not fall. It is built on solid rock.</p><p>The house on the sand? The water swirls around its foundations and washes the sand away. Finally, it falls with a crash. Everything that builder worked for is gone.</p><p>Jesus tells the crowd what the story means. The wise builder is anyone who hears his words and does them. The foolish builder is anyone who hears his words and doesn’t.</p><p>Not the person who hears the most. Not the person who knows the most. The person who does what Jesus says.</p><p>So here is the question Jesus leaves hanging over that hillside, and over our classroom this morning: when the storm comes, what will your life be built on?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus finishes the greatest sermon. · 2. One last story: two builders. · 3. One digs down. One builds fast. · 4. Then the storm. · 5. One house stands. One falls.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Matthew does not say the wise builder dug down; Luke 6:48 adds that detail, and we use it because it makes the story vivid. The dry riverbed flooding fast is true to the land (rainy-season flash floods). Keep the storm exciting but not scary: the story doesn’t mention anyone being hurt, it’s about the houses. If a student has lost a home to a flood, fire or earthquake, keep the focus on foundations, not on loss.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was underneath?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, what did the wise builder build his house on?<br>A) Sand &nbsp; B) Solid rock ✓ &nbsp; C) A grassy hill &nbsp; D) A wooden raft</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, solid rock (Matthew 7:24). Follow-up: ‘What did the foolish builder build on?’ (Sand.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to each house in the storm?’ (Rock house stood; sand house fell with a crash.) If silent: re-read the first and third scripture lines.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Slower, but wiser</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Digging down to rock took the wise builder much longer. Why do you think Jesus called him “wise” anyway?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he planned for the storm; he cared about what would last, not what looked quick; he knew the weather would come. Follow-up: ‘When have you taken the slow way because it was worth it?’ (practising an instrument, a sports skill, careful homework.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about being wise?’ If silent: ask which house they would want to be inside in a storm.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Two Builders</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Two Builders</b> (Matthew 7:24–27). Jesus’ words are from Matthew 7:24–27 (CEV). The builders’ chatter, the neighbour, and the foolish builder’s “I never did it” and the wise builder’s offer to help are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Teaching on the hillside — calm and clear<br><b>Wise Builder</b> — Careful and patient — digs deep<br><b>Foolish Builder</b> — Quick and cheerful — hates digging<br><b>Neighbour</b> — A villager who watches both builders<br><b>Everyone</b> — Digging, wind, rain and a mighty crash</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: swing pretend pickaxes — “dig, dig, dig!” · Everyone: rain with your fingers, wind with your voice, then a huge CRASH<br>Props: Optional: a ruler or rolled-up newspaper as a pickaxe<br>Jesus’ two speeches are the CEV text of Matthew 7:24 and 7:26. The ending where the wise builder offers help is not in the parable; we add it gently, and Thursday shows that it is true to how Jesus treats people. Let the CRASH be big and silly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A hillside by the lake. Jesus has finished his teaching. Now he tells one last story.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Two builders. Both want a house. Both heard Jesus’ words.</li><li><b>Wise Builder:</b> The rainy season is coming. I’d better find something solid to build on. <i>(prod the ground with a stick)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Builder:</b> Look at that lovely flat sand! Perfect. Why would anyone build anywhere else? <i>(arms wide, delighted)</i></li><li><b>Wise Builder:</b> Dig, dig, dig! Come on, shoulders! <i>(swing a pretend pickaxe)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Dig! Dig! Dig! … CLUNK! <i>(swing pickaxes, then freeze: it hit rock!)</i></li><li><b>Wise Builder:</b> Solid rock! Now the stones. One… two… heave! <i>(lift a heavy stone)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Builder:</b> Stack, stack, stack! Walls up already. I’m the fastest builder in the whole valley! <i>(stack pretend bricks fast)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> That was quick! Did you dig a foundation? The rainy season is coming. <i>(scratch head)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Builder:</b> Dig? Nah. It looks great. Nobody can tell the difference. <i>(put feet up)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Hmm. Well… it does look just like his. <i>(look from house to house)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Both houses were finished. Both looked strong. Then one day the sky turned black.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whooosh! Rain! Wind! Flood! <i>(wiggle fingers for rain, whoosh for wind)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The storm is hitting both houses. Which house do you think will still be standing? How do you know? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Rain poured down, rivers flooded, and the wind blew hard against the house on the rock.</li><li><b>Wise Builder:</b> Hold on, house! Hold on! <i>(lean on the wall, braced)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But it did not fall, because it was built on solid rock.</li><li><b>Foolish Builder:</b> Uh-oh. The sand is washing away. My walls! My roof! <i>(wobble)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> CRRRASH! <i>(everyone drops down together)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Builder:</b> I heard what Jesus said. I just never did it. <i>(sit up, soggy and sorry)</i></li><li><b>Wise Builder:</b> Come in out of the rain. We’ll help you build again, on the rock. <i>(hold out a hand)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Anyone who hears my teachings and doesn’t obey them is like a foolish person who built a house on sand.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hear it — and do it! Build on the rock! <i>(stamp once, fist on fist)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The foolish builder heard Jesus’ words too. What did he do differently?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He heard, but he didn’t do. He took the quick, easy way and never dug down. Hearing without doing is the sand.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the neighbour notice about the two houses at the start?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They looked the same. The difference only showed up when the storm came.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Hearing without doing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The foolish builder heard every word Jesus said. Why is it so easy to hear something good and not do it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: doing is harder and slower than hearing; we forget; we think we’ll do it later; we think hearing is enough; nobody can see our foundation. Follow-up: ‘When have you heard a good instruction and not done it?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps you actually do it?’ If silent: ask about a time a teacher gave clear instructions and a student didn’t follow them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ words are solid rock, and building on them means doing them, not just hearing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want my life built on you, not on sand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Practise “hear and do” today: when a teacher or a parent asks you to do something, do it the first time, without arguing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable: hear, then do, the first time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The end of the sermon</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine…”<br>…is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Matthew 7:24. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our daily bread — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All term we’re praying the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time. This week: “Give us our food for today” — often called “our daily bread”. Open hands show we’re ready to receive what God gives. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands out in front of you, open and empty, like you’re ready to receive a gift. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your open hands. God gives, and he never moves.<br>Prompts: God, you are a Rock — solid and safe in every storm. / God, you give us what we need, one day at a time.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are our Rock, and you give us what we need!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn your hands over, palms down. Sorry for the times we heard and didn’t do.<br>Prompts: Sorry for hearing your words and not doing them. / Sorry for taking the quick, easy way, like the builder on the sand.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for hearing your words and not doing them.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up again. Thank God for what he has already put in your hands today.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the food we ate this morning. / Thank you for the people who give us a home and a safe place to sleep.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the food and the home you give us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together: Give us our food for today.<br>Prompts: Please give us what we need today, just today. / Please help me build my life on Jesus’ words, starting today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us our food for today, and help us build on Jesus’ words.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, like holding a gift. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Explain daily bread simply: bread means the food and things we need; “daily” means for today. Be aware that some students may not have enough food at home: keep the tone gentle, and keep the focus on trusting God, with no assumptions about anyone’s kitchen.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — who was listening, and the surprise that left the crowd amazed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Jesus Told It":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 7:24–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus Told It</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our daily bread — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Give us our food for today” (Matthew 6:11, CEV) — the line people often call “our daily bread”. Daily bread means asking God for what we need today, not for everything at once, and trusting him to give again tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told the two builders, and why he told it.</li><li>We are learning the twist that surprised the crowd, and who is who in the story.</li><li>I can explain why Jesus called his own words the solid rock.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming the crowd (ordinary people and religious teachers), explaining that both builders heard the same words and the houses looked alike until the storm, and noticing that Jesus said “my teachings” with authority.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Jesus Told It</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met two builders who both heard Jesus’ words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the same about the two houses at the start, and what decided which one stood?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both heard; both built; both houses looked finished. The foundation decided it: rock or sand. ‘Today we find out who was listening when Jesus told this story, and the surprise that left the crowd amazed.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 7:24, 26, 28–29</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:24</b> Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:26</b> Anyone who hears my teachings and doesn’t obey them is like a foolish person who built a house on sand.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:28</b> When Jesus finished speaking, the crowds were surprised at his teaching.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:29</b> He taught them like someone with authority, and not like their teachers of the Law of Moses.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Authority = the right to be listened to and obeyed. Teachers of the Law = experts in God’s laws who taught by quoting other famous teachers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s zoom out. Where was Jesus when he told this story?</p><p>He was on a hillside near the Sea of Galilee. Everything he said that day, which we call the Sermon on the Mount, is written down in Matthew chapters five, six and seven. Jesus told people who is truly blessed. He told them to love their enemies. He taught them the Lord’s Prayer, the one we’re learning line by line this term. He told them not to worry about food and clothes, because their heavenly Father knows what they need each day. And he finished with this story of two builders.</p><p>Who was listening? Not kings or soldiers, and not only church people. Ordinary Galilean people: fishermen and farmers, mums with little kids on their hips, workers who had walked all day to get there. Some sick people, hoping to be healed. Jesus’ closest friends, the disciples. And standing at the edges, some of the teachers of the Law. They were experts in God’s rules, and they taught by saying, ‘Rabbi So-and-So says this, and Rabbi So-and-So says that.’</p><p>Now here is the first surprise. Everyone in that crowd had just heard the same teaching. Everyone was, in a way, a builder. The foolish builder wasn’t a stranger who had never heard of God. He was someone who had listened to every word, maybe even nodded along.</p><p>Here is the second surprise. Jesus makes the two houses look exactly the same. On a sunny day, you couldn’t tell them apart. You might even think the quick builder was the clever one, because he finished first! It is only when the storm hits that the difference shows. Hearing without doing looks fine, until it doesn’t. That is the same for us: a person can sit through a whole assembly, nod at every word, and still be built on sand.</p><p>And here is the third surprise, the one that left the crowd stunned. Look at what Jesus calls the rock: ‘these teachings of mine’. The teachers of the Law always pointed back to someone else. But Jesus says: my words are the rock. All through the Psalms, only God himself is called the Rock, the one who never moves. So Jesus is saying that his words are as solid as God’s own.</p><p>Luke remembers Jesus asking, ‘Why do you keep calling me Lord, and not do what I say?’ Calling Jesus ‘Lord’ is wonderful, but he wants more than a nice word. He wants us to follow him, and to do what he says.</p><p>When Jesus finished, Matthew tells us the crowds were amazed. He taught as someone with real authority, and every person on that hillside went home with a choice to make: hear and do, or just hear.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The end of the Sermon on the Mount. · 2. Who was listening? · 3. Surprise one: it looked fine. · 4. Surprise two: the storm shows the truth. · 5. Surprise three: “my teachings”.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Luke 6:46–49 is Luke’s version of the same story (the Sermon on the Plain); Luke 6:46 is paraphrased here. Teachers of the Law were also called scribes. Sensitive: a storm and a collapsed house; no person is hurt in the story. If your students have lived through floods or earthquakes, reassure them that this is a story about foundations.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The wise builder</td><td>Anyone who hears Jesus’ words and does them</td></tr><tr><td>The foolish builder</td><td>Anyone who hears Jesus’ words and doesn’t do them</td></tr><tr><td>The solid rock</td><td>Jesus’ teachings, which are true and never move</td></tr><tr><td>The storm</td><td>Hard times in life, and one day God’s judgment</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both builders heard. The one who did what Jesus said stood.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Judgment = the day God sets everything right; say it plainly and calmly: choices matter, and God is fair and kind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Authority</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “He taught them like someone with ________, and not like their teachers of the Law.”<br><i>Answer: “…authority.” (Matthew 7:29)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: authority. Follow-up: ‘How did the crowds react to Jesus’ teaching?’ (They were surprised.) Follow-up 2: ‘What kind of teachers was he different from?’ (The teachers of the Law.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Same, until the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jesus made the two houses look the same until the storm?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: you can’t tell a person’s foundation from the outside; being nice at church or school isn’t the same as obeying Jesus; real life tests us. Follow-up: ‘What kinds of things look fine on the outside but aren’t solid underneath?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about God, who sees the foundation?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Does the rock mean no storms?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If you follow Jesus, you won’t have any storms.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: the storm hit both houses (Matthew 7:25, 27). The difference is not whether the storm comes, but whether you stand. Follow-up: ‘What helps you stay standing in a storm?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who is with the wise builder in the storm?’ (Jesus promises to be with us, Matthew 28:20.) If silent: ask for one hard thing a kid might face.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Anyone who hears Jesus’ words has a choice: build on the rock by doing them, or on the sand by ignoring them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want your words to be the rock under my feet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing Jesus said that you already know, such as “love your enemies” (Matthew 5:44) or “don’t worry” (Matthew 6:34), on a sticky note. Put it where you’ll see it tomorrow morning.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out sticky notes. Help students find a teaching of Jesus they already know: love your enemies (Matthew 5:44), don’t worry (Matthew 6:34), do to others what you want them to do to you (Matthew 7:12), ask and keep on asking (Matthew 7:7). This leads into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The storm comes</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Same words heard. Same storm. Different foundation.<br>Finally, it fell with a crash.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Matthew 7:24 chunk by chunk, like building a house: one chunk at a time, one block on another.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our daily bread — write a list</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a “daily bread” list: what do you need today? Jesus said we need God’s words as much as we need food (Matthew 4:4), so put both on your list. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top of your paper or sticky note, write: “Give us our food for today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are my Rock and you never move. / God, your words are true and strong.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are our Rock, and your words are solid.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where do you hear Jesus’ words but not do them?<br>Prompts: Sorry for hearing and not doing. / Sorry for building my life on things that don’t last.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for hearing your words and not doing them.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write three things you’re thankful for today: food, a place to live, people.<br>Prompts: Thank you for breakfast, lunch, and the people who make it. / Thank you for your words, which feed my heart.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for what we need today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you need today, and one thing someone else needs.<br>Prompts: Please give me what I need for today, and help me trust you for tomorrow. / Please help someone who has too little food or no safe home.<br>Starter: <b>Please give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us our food for today, and help people who go without.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your list and keep it in your pocket today. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their list private. Be gentle about food and home: some students may be hungry or unsettled at home; keep it light and confident about God’s care.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a school hut-building day in the bush, a southerly storm, and two very different mornings.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Hut-Building Day":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 7:24–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hut-Building Day</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our daily bread — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Give us our food for today” (Matthew 6:11, CEV) — the line people often call “our daily bread”. Daily bread means asking God for what we need today, not for everything at once, and trusting him to give again tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story about two builders looks in a modern school bush setting.</li><li>We are learning why hearing the instructions isn’t the same as following them.</li><li>I can say what “hear and do” looks like in my own life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Sione to the foolish builder and Aroha to the wise builder, and explaining that Sione heard the instructions but didn’t do them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: rope or string for pretend lashing</li><li>Optional: a few sticks</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Hut-Building Day</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is a retelling in modern Aotearoa; Jesus’ parable is the same, with new builders.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Rushed it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever rushed a job just to finish first, and then had to do it all again?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened? What did you wish you’d done?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories: flat-pack furniture, a science project, a messy room, a hut at the beach. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned that both builders heard Jesus, and the houses looked the same until the storm. Today the same story happens in a school bush block.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 7:24, 26–27</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:24</b> Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:26</b> Anyone who hears my teachings and doesn’t obey them is like a foolish person who built a house on sand.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:27</b> Rain poured down, rivers flooded, and the wind blew hard against that house. Finally, it fell with a crash.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story is not in the Bible. It is Jesus’ parable retold with new builders, to help us see how it works in real life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Hut-building day</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It was Thursday afternoon, and Room 14 had hiked down to the school’s patch of native bush. Tree ferns hung over the track, and the air smelled of damp leaves. It was hut-building day.</p><p>Mr Walker stood them in a circle and gave three rules. ‘One: tie your hut to a living tree, not a dead stump. Two: lash every joint with a proper knot. Three: lean your whole weight on it to test it. And listen: there’s a southerly coming tonight. These huts stay up until morning for our hot chocolate inspection. So build them to last.’</p><p>Everyone heard exactly the same words. Every single person in the circle nodded.</p><p>Aroha picked a big, thick tree. Her friend Mia was nervous about the southerly, but Aroha handed her a rope and said, ‘That’s why we do it properly.’ They tied each joint twice. Mia’s fingers, so good at drawing, turned out to be great at knots. It took forever. Aroha’s hands were scratched and sore, and Sione’s laughing drifted over the ferns.</p><p>Next to them, Sione was flying. He leaned a pile of loose branches against an old punga stump, stepped back, and threw up his arms like he’d scored a try. ‘Done! Ten minutes! Check it out, a new world record!’ He was thinking about rugby training, and about getting to the field before everyone else. ‘Aroha, you’re still on your first wall!’</p><p>‘Mr Walker said test it,’ she said, and leaned her whole weight on hers. It creaked a little, then held. She tightened one more knot and tested it again. Still nothing.</p><p>At four o’clock, the sun was shining, and both huts looked finished. Honestly, from the track, you couldn’t tell them apart.</p><p>That night, the southerly arrived. The wind howled. The rain came sideways. The bush roared until dawn.</p><p>Next morning, the whole class crunched down the track, boots squelching in the mud, dripping with ferns on both sides. Sione stopped dead. Where his hut had been, there was a heap of sticks. ‘No way. NO way.’</p><p>A few steps away, Aroha’s hut stood firm. And Mia was inside it, grinning out of the doorway. ‘It’s dry!’</p><p>Sione looked at his sticks. ‘I heard every word,’ he said. ‘I just didn’t do it.’</p><p>Aroha didn’t laugh. ‘Come on, bro. We’ll rebuild it at lunchtime. Properly.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Hut-building day in the bush. · 2. Sione is done in ten minutes. · 3. At four o’clock, both huts looked fine. · 4. The next morning… · 5. “I heard it. I just didn’t do it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mr Walker, Aroha, Sione and Mia are imagined. Portray Sione with warmth: he’s big-hearted, just impulsive, and he’s not “the bad kid”. Southerly = a cold storm wind from the south, common in New Zealand. Punga = tree fern; a dead punga stump is soft and rotting, which is why it makes a poor anchor.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Sione tie to?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Sione lean his branches against?<br>A) A living tree &nbsp; B) An old punga stump ✓ &nbsp; C) A fence post &nbsp; D) Mr Walker’s car</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, an old punga stump, not a living tree as the rules said. Follow-up: ‘Which of Mr Walker’s rules did he skip?’ (Tie to a living tree, lash every joint, test it.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Aroha do instead?’ (Chose a big tree, tied each joint twice and tested it.) If silent: re-read the rules from the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Hut-Building Day</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Hut-Building Day</b> (Matthew 7:24–27 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (hearing and doing, rock and sand) comes from Matthew 7:24–27.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mr Walker</b> — The teacher — calm, clear and a bit funny<br><b>Aroha</b> — Careful and kind — follows the rules<br><b>Sione</b> — Fast, funny and big-hearted — loves a shortcut<br><b>Mia</b> — A worrier and a great artist — good with her fingers<br><b>Everyone</b> — The class, then the southerly</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: pretend to tie knots — “tie, tie, tie!” · Everyone: be the southerly: wind, rain and a storm roar<br>Props: Optional: a length of rope or string · Optional: a few sticks<br>Imagined retelling. Sione should be funny, not foolish: he is a lovable kid who took a shortcut, not a villain. Mr Walker, Aroha, Sione and Mia are imagined. Keep the southerly sounds fun, and stop the sounds quickly when Mr Walker speaks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The school bush block, Thursday afternoon. Mr Walker has gathered the class under the tree ferns.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Three rules. Tie to a living tree. Lash every joint. Then lean your whole weight on it to test it.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> A southerly is coming tonight. These huts stay up till morning, so build them to last.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Easy! Got it, got it. Aroha, race you. Loser carries the rugby ball! <i>(tuck an imaginary ball under the arm)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I’m picking this big tree, because rule one says a living tree. Mia, grab the rope?</li><li><b>Mia:</b> Mr Walker said southerly. What if it’s a really big one? My hands are shaking. <i>(push glasses up)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> That’s why we do it properly. Pass the rope. We’ll tie every joint twice.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Tie, tie, tie! Lash, lash, lash! <i>(pretend to tie knots)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Done! Ten minutes! Mr Walker, check it out. New world record! <i>(arms up like scoring a try)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Hmm. What did you tie it to, Sione?</li><li><b>Sione:</b> That old punga stump. It’s sort of standing up. And there’s heaps of sticks. Sweet as!</li><li><b>Mia:</b> Honestly, from here, it looks just the same as ours. <i>(look from hut to hut)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At four o’clock both huts looked finished, and the sun was shining. Everyone went home.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whooosh! Splash! Crash! <i>(the southerly arrives: wind and rain sounds)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The southerly blows all night. Which hut do you think will still be standing in the morning? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Friday morning. The bush is dripping.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> No way. NO way. Where’s my hut?! <i>(stare at the heap of sticks)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Aroha! Yours is still up! I’m inside it, and it’s dry! <i>(wave from the doorway)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> The knots didn’t move once. Not even in that wind.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Same instructions. Same bush. Same southerly. Sione, did you hear what I said yesterday?</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Every word, sir. I heard it. I just didn’t do it. <i>(sheepish)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Come on, bro. At lunchtime we’ll help you rebuild. Properly this time.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Seriously? Sweet as! Now teach me that knot. <i>(grin)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hear it, and do it! <i>(stamp once, fist on fist)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How was Sione like the foolish builder, and Aroha like the wise builder?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Both heard the same instructions. Sione rushed and skipped the hard parts; Aroha did them. The storm showed the difference.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Aroha do when Sione’s hut fell?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She didn’t laugh or say “told you so”. She offered to help him rebuild properly.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Hearing isn’t enough</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sione heard every word of Mr Walker’s instructions, but his hut still fell. Why isn’t hearing enough?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: hearing is easy and doing takes effort; we think we’ll remember; shortcuts look fine until the test; instructions only protect us if we follow them. Follow-up: ‘What did Aroha do that Sione didn’t?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is this like what Jesus said in Matthew 7:26?’ If silent: ask which rule Sione skipped, and why he skipped it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A hand to rebuild</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Aroha didn’t laugh at Sione. She offered to help him rebuild. Why does that matter, and what does it say about how Jesus treats people who’ve got it wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: failing doesn’t have to be the end; the wise builder in the role-play helped the foolish one; Jesus forgives and helps us start again; we shouldn’t gloat. Follow-up: ‘When has someone helped you start again?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you help rebuild this week?’ If silent: ask how Sione would have felt if Aroha had said ‘told you so’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hearing is the first step; the rock is in the doing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, show me one thing I know I should do but keep putting off.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one job you’ve been putting off (tidying your room, saying sorry to a sibling, finishing a task) and do it before tomorrow, properly, not rushed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and specific. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Still standing</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It’s dry!”<br>“I heard every word. I just didn’t do it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Matthew 7:24: ear, stamp, book, head, roof, rock.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our daily bread — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Ask, “What do you need today?” Then each pray one sentence for the other. That’s asking God for daily bread for someone else. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. First, quietly tell your partner one thing you need today. It can be small.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you are a Rock. You didn’t move in the southerly. / God, you are strong and you hold us up.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are our Rock!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where did you rush or skip the hard part?<br>Prompts: Sorry for taking shortcuts. / Sorry for hearing your words and not doing them.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the shortcuts we take.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who help us rebuild when we get it wrong.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Aroha who help us start again. / Thank you for forgiving us and helping us rebuild.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who help us start again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s need today.<br>Prompts: Please give (partner’s name) what they need today. / Please help us both to hear your words and do them.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us all our food for today.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a partner’s need is private or sensitive, they can say “help with something at home” and leave it there.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God, the Rock, and how we build one day at a time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Rock Solid":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 7:24–29</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Rock Solid</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Our daily bread — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Give us our food for today” (Matthew 6:11, CEV) — the line people often call “our daily bread”. Daily bread means asking God for what we need today, not for everything at once, and trusting him to give again tomorrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the two builders show us about God and about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning how to build our lives on Jesus’ words, one day at a time.</li><li>I can choose one thing Jesus said and plan how to do it this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable, explain that Jesus’ words are solid rock, say that hearing and doing go together, and recite Matthew 7:24.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky notes from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Rock Solid</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand up for TRUE, sit down for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both builders heard the same words from Jesus. — <b>True</b> (The difference wasn’t hearing. It was doing (Matthew 7:24, 26).)<br>The house on the sand looked terrible from the start. — <b>False</b> (It looked just as good as the other house. The storm showed the difference.)<br>The storm only hit the foolish builder’s house. — <b>False</b> (Rain, floods and wind hit both houses; only one stood.)<br>The wise builder was the one who built fastest. — <b>False</b> (He dug down to rock, which took longer.)<br>In our hut-building story, Sione didn’t hear Mr Walker’s rules. — <b>False</b> (He heard every word. He just didn’t do them.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students stand for true and sit for false; reveal the answer and the why. Ask: ‘Which story was that part from, Monday’s parable or Wednesday’s hut?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 7:24–25 · 6:11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:24</b> Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 7:25</b> Rain poured down, rivers flooded, and the wind blew hard against that house. But it did not fall, because it was built on solid rock.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 6:11</b> Give us our food for today.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 6:11 is from the Lord’s Prayer, the line we pray this week: the old words are “give us this day our daily bread”.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Built on the rock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does Jesus’ story show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, God is a Rock. All through the Psalms, God is called a rock: somewhere solid and safe that never moves, no matter what blows in. And Jesus says his words are that solid. When we build our lives on what Jesus says, we are building on God himself.</p><p>Second, storms come to every house. Rain fell on the wise builder too. Being on the rock doesn’t mean no storms. It means you are still standing after them.</p><p>Third, and this is the best news, the greatest Builder of all is Jesus. He heard his Father’s words and did every one of them. And he went through the biggest storm there has ever been, on the cross, for us. Three days later, the empty tomb showed that nothing could knock him down. He is the one we build on. And when our house falls, when we hear and don’t do, like Sione, Jesus doesn’t say ‘too bad’. He forgives us, and he helps us rebuild.</p><p>Now, how do you build a house? One brick at a time. Remember the prayer we’re learning? ‘Give us our food for today.’ Daily bread: just enough for today, and God gives it again tomorrow, like the manna in Term 1. Jesus’ words are daily bread too. Not a giant feast once a year, but a little each day. Mia, who worries a lot, picks one thing Jesus said: ‘Don’t worry about tomorrow.’ She reads it at breakfast, and she does it. When worry comes, she stops and says thank you for today. One thing. One day at a time. That’s how you build on the rock.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God is a Rock. · 2. Storms come to every house. · 3. Jesus did every word. · 4. One thing. One day at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. “Rock” for God: for example Psalm 18:2 (not quoted here). Matthew 6:34, “don’t worry about tomorrow”, is paraphrased. Mia is imagined. Keep the cross simple and reverent: Jesus did what his Father asked, even to death, and rose again.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What made the difference?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, what made the difference between the two houses?<br>A) The size of the house &nbsp; B) What each one was built on ✓ &nbsp; C) The colour of the roof &nbsp; D) Who had better tools</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, what each one was built on, rock or sand (Matthew 7:24, 26). Follow-up: ‘What does the rock stand for?’ (Jesus’ words.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does building on the rock look like?’ (Hearing and doing.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Jesus’ words are rock</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In the Psalms, God is called a Rock. Jesus says his own words are the rock. What does that tell us about who Jesus is?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus’ words are as reliable as God’s; Jesus speaks with God’s authority; he is God’s Son and can be trusted completely. Follow-up: ‘Why would a Rock be a good picture of God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could we find out what Jesus’ words are?’ (Read the Gospels.) If silent: ask what makes a rock different from sand.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus’ words are solid rock; they don’t move when the storm comes.</li><li>Hearing isn’t enough. Building on the rock means doing what Jesus says, one day at a time.</li><li>Jesus himself stood through the biggest storm for us, and he helps us rebuild when we get it wrong.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus’ words are solid rock — hearing them isn’t enough; building your life on them means doing them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: All by myself?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “I can build my life on the rock all by myself.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: we can’t do it perfectly alone; Jesus is the one who did it perfectly, and he forgives us and helps us (his Holy Spirit). Follow-up: ‘What happens when we fall short?’ (Jesus forgives and helps us start again.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who else can help you hear and do?’ (Friends, family, church.) If silent: ask what Aroha did for Sione.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hear it, do it. Pick one thing Jesus said, such as “love your enemies” or “don’t worry”, and actually do it, every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the sticky notes from Tuesday. Ideas: love your enemies (Matthew 5:44); don’t worry (Matthew 6:34); do to others what you want them to do to you (Matthew 7:12). Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Anyone who hears and obeys these teachings of mine is like a wise person who built a house on solid rock.” — Matthew 7:24 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Matthew 7:24. Bonus: say Luke 8:15 from Week 1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Rock solid</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hear it. Do it.<br>Build your life on Jesus’ words: solid rock.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our daily bread — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the whole line out loud together: “Give us our food for today.” Daily bread means what we need for today, and God gives again tomorrow. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is. Think of the rock.<br>Prompts: God, you are our Rock! / God, you are strong, and you never move.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are our Rock, solid and strong!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about the words of Jesus you’ve heard but haven’t done.<br>Prompts: Sorry for hearing and not doing. / Thank you that you forgive us and help us start again.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you help us start again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something God gave you today.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the food on our tables. / Thank you for Jesus, who stood in the storm for us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for what you give us each day.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together, then ask for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Give us our food for today. / Please help me do the one thing of Jesus’ I’ve chosen.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Give us our food for today, and help us do what Jesus says.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say the line together once more: “Give us our food for today.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Servant Who Wouldn’t Forgive — a king cancels a gigantic debt, and a forgiven servant won’t forgive a tiny one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6: The Servant Who Wouldn’t Forgive":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 18:21–35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgiving</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgive us as we forgive — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “forgive us our debts”: a debt is something we owe, and wrongdoing is like owing God. We pray this line honestly: we tell God sorry, and we ask him to help us let go of what others owe us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>How Many Times?</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning why Peter asked Jesus how many times he should forgive, and how Jesus answered.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>How Many Times? → Is there a limit? → Matthew 18:21–22, 24, 28 → The king and the servant → How much? → More than he asked → The Unforgiving Servant → Forgiven, but still grabbing → Head · Heart · Hands → Peter’s question → Memory verse peek → Forgive us as we forgive — heavy hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Jesus Told It</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who Jesus was talking to when he told the story of the unforgiving servant, and why.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Jesus Told It → Recap → Matthew 18:21–22, 35 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → Seventy-seven → Why such a big number? → Was it fair? → Head · Heart · Hands → He wouldn’t forgive → Build the verse → Forgive us as we forgive — write it down</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Broken TV</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story about the unforgiving servant looks in a modern New Zealand home and school.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Broken TV → Let off → Matthew 18:22, 28, 35 → The TV and the sketchbook → What did Dad say? → The Broken TV → Is it different? → The quiet question → Head · Heart · Hands → Splash! → Verse with actions → Forgive us as we forgive — quiet reflection</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Forgiven, So Forgive</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the unforgiving servant shows us about God and about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Forgiven, So Forgive → Who said it? → Luke 6:36 · Matthew 6:12 · Luke 23:34 → Forgiven, so forgive → What did the king do? → Why forgive? → This week’s big idea → Too big to forgive? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Forgiven, so forgive → Forgive us as we forgive — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: How Many Times?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 18:21–35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">How Many Times?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgive us as we forgive — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “forgive us our debts”: a debt is something we owe, and wrongdoing is like owing God. We pray this line honestly: we tell God sorry, and we ask him to help us let go of what others owe us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Peter asked Jesus how many times he should forgive, and how Jesus answered.</li><li>We are learning the story of a king who cancelled a servant’s enormous debt.</li><li>I can explain what the servant did wrong after he was forgiven (Matthew 18:21–35).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling Peter’s question and Jesus’ answer of seventy-seven times, that the first servant owed a huge debt the king cancelled, and that he then refused to forgive a friend who owed him very little.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a long strip of paper to be the debt scroll</li><li>Optional: seven counters or fingers to count to seventy-seven</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: How Many Times?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have a long strip of paper, hold it up as the “debt scroll” later in the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Is there a limit?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine someone keeps accidentally treading on your toes, or borrowing your pencils and losing them. How many times would you forgive them before you said, “That’s it, no more!”?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Is there a number? What would your number be?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard The Two Builders — one house on rock, one on sand. Your challenge: pick one thing Jesus said and actually do it, every day this week. Which thing did you choose, and did you manage to do it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students which saying of Jesus they chose and whether they managed to do it; celebrate honest ‘I forgot on Wednesday’ answers too. Then the hook: take a few numbers (once, three times, ten). Land it: ‘Today a man called Peter asks Jesus exactly this question, and Jesus’ answer surprises everyone.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 18:21–22, 24, 28</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:21</b> Peter came up to the Lord and asked, “How many times should I forgive someone who does something wrong to me? Is seven times enough?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:22</b> Jesus answered: Not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:24</b> One servant was brought in who owed him fifty million silver coins.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:28</b> He found another servant who owed him a hundred silver coins. So he grabbed the man and said, “Pay me what you owe!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Peter’s question comes at the end of Jesus’ teaching on how to treat a brother or sister who wrongs you (Matthew 18:15–20). The “he” in verse 28 is the servant the king had just forgiven. A debt is money that you owe someone. Watch the two amounts: fifty million and one hundred.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The king and the servant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture a dusty road in Galilee. Jesus is walking along with his friends, and Peter has been thinking hard. Jesus has just been teaching them what to do when someone hurts you, and Peter has an idea. He hurries up, counting on his thick fingers. ‘Lord, how many times should I forgive someone who does something wrong to me? Is seven times enough?’ Peter is rather proud of that number. Seven is very generous!</p><p>Jesus looks at him kindly. ‘Not just seven times,’ he says, ‘but seventy-seven times!’ Peter’s mouth drops open. And then, as they walk, Jesus tells a story. Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. This one starts in a palace.</p><p>A king decides to check his servants’ accounts, and one servant is brought in who owes him fifty million silver coins. That is an impossible amount. The record-keepers have stacked up scrolls and tablets of numbers, higher than the servant’s head. He has no way to pay. So the king says that everything he owns must be sold to pay it back.</p><p>The servant falls on his knees. ‘Have pity on me! Give me time, and I will pay you every cent!’ He can’t, of course. Nobody could. But look what the king does. He doesn’t just give him more time. His heart is full of pity, and he cancels the whole debt. Every single coin. The servant walks out free.</p><p>On his way out, he spots a fellow servant who owes him a hundred silver coins. That is a real amount, a few months of wages, but it’s nothing like fifty million. The forgiven servant grabs the man roughly. ‘Pay me what you owe!’</p><p>The fellow servant falls on his knees, just as the first one did only minutes before. ‘Have pity on me, and I will pay you back.’ But the first servant refuses. He has his friend put in jail until he can pay.</p><p>The other servants see it all, and it makes them so upset that they go and tell the king everything. The king sends for the first servant. ‘I cancelled your debt because you begged me. Shouldn’t you have shown pity to that man, as I did to you?’ And the king hands him over to be punished.</p><p>Then Jesus looks at Peter and the others. ‘That is how my Father in heaven will treat you, unless you forgive your brothers and sisters from your heart.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Peter has a question. · 2. “Not seven times — seventy-seven times!” · 3. A servant owes fifty million silver coins. · 4. The king cancels it. All of it. · 5. He grabs a friend over a hundred coins.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The words of Peter, Jesus, the servant and the fellow servant follow Matthew 18 closely; the king’s final words are paraphrased from the CEV so that they are short enough to say aloud. In the text the king’s first order is that the servant, his family and all he owns be sold to pay the debt; we say only “everything he owns”. Verse 34 says he is handed over to be punished: say it plainly and calmly, with no detail. The details of the stacked scrolls and the shocked servants are imagined.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How much?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How much did the first servant owe the king?<br>A) A hundred silver coins &nbsp; B) Fifty million silver coins ✓ &nbsp; C) One silver coin &nbsp; D) Nothing at all</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, fifty million silver coins (Matthew 18:24). Follow-up: ‘How much did the other servant owe him?’ (A hundred silver coins.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the king do with the debt?’ (Cancelled all of it.) If silent: point back to the third scripture line and read it aloud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: More than he asked</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The servant only begged for more time. The king cancelled the whole debt. What does that show us about the king?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the king is generous, kind and full of pity; he gives far more than we ask; he cares more about the servant than about the money. Follow-up: ‘Why do you think he didn’t just give him more time?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who do you think the king stands for?’ If silent: ask how the servant must have felt walking out of the palace.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Unforgiving Servant</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Unforgiving Servant</b> (Matthew 18:21–35). Peter’s question, Jesus’ answer and the servants’ and king’s main words come from Matthew 18 (CEV). The courtiers’ chorus, the actions and some of the king’s words are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Peter</b> — Eager and honest — asks the big question<br><b>Jesus</b> — Calm and kind — answers Peter and tells the story<br><b>King</b> — Powerful, and full of pity<br><b>Servant</b> — Terrified at first, then too pleased with himself<br><b>Fellow Servant</b> — A friend who owes only a little<br><b>Everyone</b> — The king’s court — gasps, whispers and shocked faces</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the king’s court. Gasp, whisper and shake your heads at the right moments · Peter: hold up seven fingers, then slowly lower them<br>Props: Optional: a long strip of paper to tear as the debt scroll<br>Imagined staging of Jesus’ parable. Let Peter be warm and a bit funny, not silly. The jail and the king’s sentence are only implied: the text says the servant is handed over to be punished, and we leave it there. The King is a parable character, not God the Father; keep him strong and sad, not scary. If a student finds the ending upsetting, say that Jesus tells the story to help us, and that Thursday shows the good news.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A dusty road in Galilee. Peter hurries to catch up with Jesus, counting on his fingers.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Lord, how many times should I forgive someone who does something wrong to me? Is seven times enough? <i>(hold up seven fingers, proudly)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Seventy-seven?! <i>(gasp and hold up lots of fingers)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then Jesus tells a story. A king is checking his servants’ accounts, and one servant is brought in.</li><li><b>King:</b> Let me see the books. Ah. You owe me fifty million silver coins. Where is it? <i>(unroll a huge scroll)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> I… I don’t have it, Your Majesty. I could never pay that much. <i>(gulp)</i></li><li><b>King:</b> Then everything you own will be sold to pay the debt. <i>(serious and still)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> Have pity on me! Give me time, and I will pay you every cent! <i>(drop to your knees, hands together)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Fifty million? He’ll never pay that! <i>(whisper loudly to each other)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The king looks at the servant on his knees. His heart fills with pity.</li><li><b>King:</b> Stand up. I won’t make you wait. I won’t make you pay. The whole debt is cancelled. <i>(tear the scroll in two)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> All of it? Thank you, Your Majesty! Thank you! <i>(leap up, delighted)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The servant has just been forgiven fifty million silver coins. How do you think he feels? What will he do next? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Outside the palace, the servant meets a fellow servant who owes him a hundred silver coins.</li><li><b>Servant:</b> There you are! Pay me what you owe. Right now! <i>(grab his cloak)</i></li><li><b>Fellow Servant:</b> Please, have pity on me! Give me time, and I will pay you back. <i>(drop to your knees)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> No! Pay up, or you’re going to jail. <i>(fold your arms and turn away)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shame! He was just forgiven fifty million! <i>(gasp and shake your heads)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The other servants are shocked. They hurry to tell the king everything.</li><li><b>King:</b> I cancelled your whole debt because you begged me. Shouldn’t you have shown pity to him, as I did to you? <i>(stern, but sad)</i></li><li><b>Servant:</b> I… I didn’t think… <i>(shrink down)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The king hands him over to be punished. It is a sad ending.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> That is how my Father in heaven will treat you, unless you forgive your brothers and sisters from your heart.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Seventy-seven times. Wow. <i>(slowly lower your fingers)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Forgiven? Then forgive! <i>(stamp once, hand on heart)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was wrong with what the first servant did, straight after the king forgave him?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He had just been given a gigantic gift of forgiveness, and he treated his friend with no pity at all. He forgot how much he had been forgiven.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How were the two debts different?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Fifty million silver coins against a hundred. One was impossible to pay; the other was small by comparison and could be paid in time.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Forgiven, but still grabbing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The servant had just been forgiven fifty million coins. Why do you think he still grabbed his friend over a hundred?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: he forgot how much he’d been given; he thought only about himself and what he was owed; he felt powerful; he never really believed it was a gift. Follow-up: ‘When have you seen someone be forgiven and then not forgive?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could he have done instead?’ If silent: ask what the servant might have said if he’d remembered the king’s pity.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The king cancelled a huge debt, and anyone who has been forgiven that much should forgive others.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for your pity. Help me not to hold on to what others owe me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Notice one small thing today that someone does that annoys you: a bump, a borrowed rubber, a rude word. Choose to let it go and say nothing about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable: let one small thing go.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Peter’s question</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Is seven times enough?”<br>“Not seven times — seventy-seven times!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 6:36. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Merciful = full of pity and kindness, especially when someone doesn’t deserve it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Forgive us as we forgive — heavy hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All term we’re praying the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time. This week: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “debts”: a debt is something we owe. Use your hands to pray: heavy and pressed down, then open and light. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold both hands out in front of you as though you’re carrying something very heavy. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look up. The king in the story is like God: full of pity for people who owe more than they can pay.<br>Prompts: God, you are like a king full of pity, who doesn’t want us crushed by what we owe. / God, you are forgiving. You tear up debts and let people go free.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are full of pity, and you forgive!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your hands heavy and pressed down. Tell God about one wrong you’re carrying.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the wrong things I’ve done that I could never pay back myself. / Sorry for the times I held on to what someone did instead of letting it go.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the wrong we’ve done, and for the grudges we hold.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now turn your palms up and open them wide. The weight is gone, like a debt that’s been cancelled.<br>Prompts: Thank you for cancelling the whole debt, not just a little of it. / Thank you that when I say sorry, you forgive me and let me go free.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you cancel our debts and let us go free.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your hands open and light. Pray the line together: Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.<br>Prompts: Please help me forgive someone who has hurt me, even in a small way. / Please help me be like the king, and not like the servant who grabbed his friend.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rest your hands, light and open. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Explain debts simply: a debt is something you owe. Sorry is not a performance; God already knows, and says “forgiven”. Keep the Sorry phase private and gentle. Some students may be carrying something heavy at home or at school; there is no need to name it aloud.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — who was listening, and the shocking size of the debt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Jesus Told It":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 18:21–35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus Told It</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgive us as we forgive — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “forgive us our debts”: a debt is something we owe, and wrongdoing is like owing God. We pray this line honestly: we tell God sorry, and we ask him to help us let go of what others owe us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Jesus was talking to when he told the story of the unforgiving servant, and why.</li><li>We are learning who is who in the story, and what shocking surprises were in it.</li><li>I can explain what the huge debt and the small debt stand for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming Peter and the disciples as the first audience, matching the king to God and the first servant to us, and explaining why the first debt was so much bigger than the second.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Cards or scrap paper</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Jesus Told It</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Peter asked how many times to forgive, and Jesus told a story about a king and two servants.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the first servant do after he was forgiven, and how big were the two debts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expected: he had a fifty million coin debt cancelled, then grabbed a friend over a hundred coins and had him jailed. Follow-up: ‘What did the king say when he heard?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Jesus say Peter’s answer should be?’ ‘Today we find out who was listening, and the surprises that would have made the listeners gasp.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 18:21–22, 35</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:21</b> Peter came up to the Lord and asked, “How many times should I forgive someone who does something wrong to me? Is seven times enough?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:22</b> Jesus answered: Not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:35</b> That is how my Father in heaven will treat you, unless you forgive your brothers and sisters from your heart.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 35 is Jesus’ own explanation of the parable. “From your heart” means for real, not just in words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s go back to that road in Galilee and ask: why did Jesus tell this story, and who was listening?</p><p>Matthew puts this story right after Jesus has been teaching his closest friends, the twelve disciples, about how to treat each other. He told them about a shepherd who goes looking for one lost sheep. He told them what to do when a brother or sister wrongs you. And Peter, always the first to speak, wants an exact number. In Peter’s day, many teachers taught that you only had to forgive someone three times. Peter doubles that, adds one, and thinks he’s being generous. Seven was a special number in the Bible, the number of ‘complete’.</p><p>Surprise number one: Jesus says, ‘Seventy-seven times.’ That isn’t a number to count up to. It means: stop keeping score at all. And there is an echo here. Way back in Genesis, a man named Lamech boasted that he would take revenge seventy-seven times. Jesus turns it around: forgive seventy-seven times.</p><p>Now, who is who? The king is God, our Father in heaven. The first servant stands for each of us, Peter included. The fellow servant stands for anyone who does us wrong.</p><p>Surprise number two is the size of the first debt. In Jesus’ own words it was ten thousand talents, the biggest number the Greek language had a word for. Our Bible says fifty million silver coins. Either way, it is more than anyone could earn in a thousand lifetimes. The other servant’s debt was a hundred silver coins, a few months of wages. That’s real money, and being wronged really does hurt. But set the two side by side. One debt is a mountain. The other is a pebble.</p><p>Surprise number three is the king. In that world, kings did not cancel debts. They collected them. But this king felt deep pity for the servant, the kind that turns your stomach over, and he cancelled everything. Notice that he paid the cost himself. Forgiving always costs the one who forgives.</p><p>And surprise number four: a man forgiven a mountain couldn’t forgive a pebble. The crowd would have gasped, ‘How could he?’ But Jesus is looking right at Peter, and at us. Have we ever asked God to forgive us, and then held on to a grudge?</p><p>Jesus ends: ‘unless you forgive your brothers and sisters from your heart.’ It isn’t a trick to earn forgiveness. It is a warning: someone who really knows how much they’ve been forgiven will find it very hard to hold on to a grudge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Peter wants a number. · 2. The king is God. The servant is us. · 3. Surprise: the debt was impossible. · 4. Surprise: he still wouldn’t forgive. · 5. “From your heart.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. “Many teachers taught three times” reflects a Jewish teaching of the time (based on how God’s forgiveness of repeated sin was read); say “many teachers” rather than “all”. The Lamech echo is from Genesis 4:23–24; many Bible teachers see Jesus reversing it. Ten thousand talents is the Greek: a talent was about 6,000 days’ pay and a hundred-coin debt about 100 days’ pay, so the first debt was roughly 600,000 times bigger. The CEV renders it as fifty million silver coins. The Greek word for the king’s pity is the gut-level one Luke uses for the Samaritan and the father. Sensitive: debt and punishment; say calmly that Jesus tells the story to help us.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The king</td><td>God, our Father in heaven, who is full of pity</td></tr><tr><td>The first servant</td><td>Each of us (Peter too), who owe God more than we could ever pay</td></tr><tr><td>The huge debt</td><td>All the wrong we’ve done — a mountain we could never pay back</td></tr><tr><td>The fellow servant’s small debt</td><td>What others do to us — it can really hurt, but it’s small beside what we’ve been forgiven</td></tr><tr><td>The torn scroll</td><td>Forgiveness: God cancels the debt, and bears the cost himself</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We have been forgiven a mountain, so we can forgive a pebble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Be careful with row four: Jesus isn’t saying that what hurts us is nothing. A hundred silver coins was a real amount. He is saying that we’ve been forgiven far more. Keep the tone gentle.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Seventy-seven</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Not just seven times, but ________ times!”<br><i>Answer: “…seventy-seven times!” (Matthew 18:22)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: seventy-seven. Follow-up: ‘What number did Peter suggest?’ (Seven.) Follow-up 2: ‘Do you think Jesus wanted Peter to count to seventy-seven and then stop?’ (No: he meant keep forgiving, and stop keeping score.) If silent: point to the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why such a big number?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jesus made the first debt so enormous, and the second one so small by comparison?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to show we owe God more than we could ever repay; to show how small other people’s wrongs are next to what we’ve been forgiven; to make the servant’s refusal shocking. Follow-up: ‘What would it have been like to hear the number fifty million?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about God’s forgiveness?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Was it fair?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Vote: was it fair for the king to cancel the whole debt?<br>A) Fair &nbsp; B) Not fair</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Either answer can be good. Not fair: the servant owed it. Fair or not, it was merciful, and the king paid the cost himself. Mercy gives what we don’t deserve. Follow-up: ‘Who paid for the cancelled debt?’ (The king.) Follow-up 2: ‘Where have we seen someone pay the cost of our forgiveness?’ (Jesus.) If silent: ask which they would have wanted if they were the servant.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God has forgiven us a debt we could never repay, so we can forgive what others owe us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for forgiving me. Help me let go of something I’m holding on to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On a card, write one small thing someone did that you’re still holding on to. No names. Keep it private, and think about letting it go this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hand out cards. Students keep them private. Reassure students that they don’t have to share. If a child has been seriously hurt, bullied or unsafe, forgiving doesn’t mean going back to that person or staying quiet: encourage them to tell a trusted adult. Aim for small hurts today.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: He wouldn’t forgive</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Forgiven a mountain…<br>…and he wouldn’t forgive a pebble.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 6:36 chunk by chunk. Say: ‘merciful means kind and full of pity, just like the king in the story.’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Forgive us as we forgive — write it down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a private prayer. After you’ve said sorry, write the word FORGIVEN across it. That’s a picture of what God does: he cancels what we owe. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a card or scrap paper. At the top, write: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about the king in the story. He is like God.<br>Prompts: God, you are more generous than any king: you cancel debts nobody could pay. / God, you take the cost of forgiveness on yourself.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are generous, and you cancel what we owe.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently write, or just think: one wrong thing you’re sorry to God for. Keep it private.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the wrong things I’ve done, big and small. / Sorry for holding on to something small after you’ve forgiven me so much.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the wrong we’ve done.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now write the word FORGIVEN in big letters across what you wrote.<br>Prompts: Thank you that when I’m sorry, you say “forgiven”. / Thank you that Jesus paid the cost, so my debt is cancelled.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On a fresh line write one person you find hard to forgive: a first name or initial only. Ask for help.<br>Prompts: Please help me forgive that person for what they did. / Please be good to them, and help me to be kind, even though it hurt.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, forgive us, and help us to forgive others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your card and keep it somewhere private. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Nobody reads another student’s card. Be gentle with the “someone I find hard to forgive” line: a child may be thinking of a family member; they can write a word like “someone at home” or leave it blank. Never press for a name.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a modern retelling in Aotearoa. Mia breaks something big at home, and then something of hers is spoiled at school.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Broken TV":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 18:21–35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Broken TV</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgive us as we forgive — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “forgive us our debts”: a debt is something we owe, and wrongdoing is like owing God. We pray this line honestly: we tell God sorry, and we ask him to help us let go of what others owe us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story about the unforgiving servant looks in a modern New Zealand home and school.</li><li>We are learning why it can be hard to forgive someone straight after we have been forgiven.</li><li>I can say what it means to let someone off a small debt after being let off a big one.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Dad to the king, Mia to the servant and Jun to the fellow servant, and explaining that Mia forgot how much she had been forgiven.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a damp tea-towel or a pretend “wet” sketchbook (a folded sheet of paper)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Broken TV</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is a retelling in modern Aotearoa; Jesus’ parable is the same, with new characters.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Let off</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think of a time someone let you off, when you’d broken or messed up something and expected them to be cross.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did it feel? And did it change how you treated anyone else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories: a broken cup, a lost library book, a window or a ball over a fence. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned the king is God, and that the servant forgot how much he’d been forgiven. Today we watch what that looks like at home and at school.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 18:22, 28, 35</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:22</b> Jesus answered: Not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:28</b> He found another servant who owed him a hundred silver coins. So he grabbed the man and said, “Pay me what you owe!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 18:35</b> That is how my Father in heaven will treat you, unless you forgive your brothers and sisters from your heart.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story is not in the Bible. It is Jesus’ parable retold with a new setting and new characters, to help us see how it works in real life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The TV and the sketchbook</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Saturday afternoon, and Mia’s house was quiet. Dad was in the kitchen, and in the lounge Mia was bored. She had a soccer ball. She had been told, very clearly, not to kick it inside. But just one little kick…</p><p>The ball sailed straight at the big TV. There was a thump, a wobble, and then the whole TV toppled off its cabinet and hit the floor with a crack. A long silver line ran right across the screen. Mia froze, hands over her mouth.</p><p>Dad appeared in the doorway. Mia felt sick. She waited for the shouting. ‘I’m so sorry!’ she blurted. ‘I’ll pay for it! Every cent of my pocket money, I promise!’</p><p>Dad crouched down. ‘Mia, that TV cost more than you could save in years. You couldn’t pay it back if you tried.’ She gulped. And then Dad did something she did not expect. He smiled, a tired, kind smile. ‘And I’m not asking you to. It’s only a TV. You matter more. We’ll sort it out.’ Mia hugged him so hard he wobbled.</p><p>On Monday morning at school, Mia felt light as a feather. She sat under the covered walkway with her sketchbook, the one with every drawing she had done this term. Her best work was in there: the harakeke by the field, the tūī in the kōwhai, Dad asleep in his chair.</p><p>Then Jun came skidding around the corner, his bike helmet still on, his drink bottle in his hand with the lid not quite on. ‘Sorry! Coming through!’ Splash!</p><p>A whole bottle of orange juice. All over the sketchbook. Ink ran and pages stuck together.</p><p>‘My sketchbook!’ Mia snatched it up, dripping. Jun’s grin vanished. ‘It was an accident! I’m really sorry, Mia. I’ll buy you a new one.’</p><p>‘A new one?’ Mia’s voice shook. ‘You can’t buy back what’s in this! You’re going to pay for every single page. And I’m telling everybody what you did.’ She stomped off. The kids watching went ‘ooooh’. Jun said sorry five times that day. Mia didn’t look at him once.</p><p>That night she was quiet at dinner. ‘What’s up?’ asked Dad. She told him all about it. Dad put down his fork, and a tiny smile crept onto his face. ‘Hmm. Is this the same Mia who broke my TV on Saturday?’</p><p>Mia looked at her plate. ‘…Oh.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Crash. The TV is cracked. · 2. “It’s only a TV. You matter more.” · 3. Monday. Splash! · 4. “You’re going to pay for every page.” · 5. “Is this the same Mia who broke my TV?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mia, Jun and Dad are imagined. Portray Mia with warmth: she is not a bad kid, her sketchbook really matters to her, and being hurt is real. Jun is a cheeky, quick-tempered boy who really is sorry. Dad isn’t perfect either; he is a picture of the king’s pity. Harakeke = flax; tūī = a native bird with a white throat tuft; kōwhai = a native tree with yellow flowers in spring.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Dad say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Mia’s dad do when he saw the broken TV?<br>A) Grounded her for a month &nbsp; B) Made her pay it off from her pocket money &nbsp; C) Said it was only a TV, and she didn’t have to pay ✓ &nbsp; D) Didn’t notice at all</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C. He said she couldn’t pay it back and that he wasn’t asking her to, because she mattered more than the TV. Follow-up: ‘What did Mia offer to do?’ (Pay every cent of her pocket money.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who in Jesus’ story does Dad match?’ (The king.) If silent: re-read Dad’s words from the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Broken TV</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Broken TV</b> (Matthew 18:21–35 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (a huge debt forgiven, a small one refused) comes from Matthew 18:21–35.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mia</b> — A good artist who worries a lot — and is stubborn when hurt<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky and fast — truly sorry, but gets frustrated<br><b>Dad</b> — Calm, kind and a bit funny<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crash, the splash and the classmates</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: clap loudly once for the CRASH, and shout SPLASH when the bottle spills · Everyone: be the classmates. Go “ooooh” when Mia stomps away<br>Props: Optional: a soccer ball · Optional: a drink bottle and a folded sheet of paper as the sketchbook<br>Imagined retelling. Keep Mia sympathetic: her hurt is real, and her sketchbook matters to her. Jun is cheeky but truly sorry. The ending is open on purpose: Mia’s choice comes tomorrow. Keep the CRASH and SPLASH fun, and stop the sounds as soon as someone speaks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Saturday afternoon in Mia’s lounge. Dad has said no ball games inside. Mia has a soccer ball anyway.</li><li><b>Mia:</b> Just one little kick. What could go wrong? <i>(kick an imaginary ball)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> CRASH! <i>(clap loudly once)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Oh no. Oh no, no, no. Dad’s big TV! <i>(hands over mouth)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Mia? What was that noise? <i>(appear in the doorway)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> I’m so sorry! I’ll pay for it! Every cent of my pocket money, I promise! <i>(wring your hands)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Mia, that TV costs more than you could save in years. You can’t pay it back. <i>(gently)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> And I’m not asking you to. It’s only a TV. You matter more. We’ll sort it out. <i>(a kind smile)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Really? You’re not angry? Thanks, Dad. Thanks so much! <i>(hug)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Monday morning at school. Mia sits under the walkway with her sketchbook, full of a whole term of drawings.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Sorry! Coming through! Whoa, whoa, whoa! <i>(skid in, holding a drink bottle)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> SPLASH! <i>(make a big splash with your hands)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> My sketchbook! A whole term of drawings, soaked! <i>(snatch it up, dripping)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> It was an accident! My lid wasn’t on. I’m really sorry, Mia. <i>(palms up)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Sorry doesn’t fix it! You ruined it. You’re going to pay for every single page. <i>(glare)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> I’ll buy you a new one! Or help dry it. Or— <i>(hold out a hand)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> No. Not good enough. And I’m telling everyone what you did. <i>(turn your back)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mia, that’s harsh! He said sorry! <i>(frown at Mia)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Hey! It was an accident! I’ve said sorry five times already! <i>(voice rising)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> I don’t care. I’m not talking to him until he pays. <i>(stomp off)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! On Saturday Mia was forgiven for something huge. What do you notice about how she treats Jun on Monday? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> That evening at the dinner table, Mia is very quiet. Dad notices.</li><li><b>Dad:</b> You’re quiet tonight. Everything all right at school? <i>(pass the peas)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Jun spilt his drink all over my sketchbook. I told him he has to pay for every page. <i>(poke at your food)</i></li><li><b>Dad:</b> Hmm. Is that the same Mia who broke my TV on Saturday? <i>(a gentle smile)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> …Oh. <i>(stare at your plate)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Her soggy sketchbook is drying, crinkly, on the radiator. Mia has a choice to make.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh, Mia! <i>(slowly shake your heads)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Mia found it so hard to forgive Jun, straight after Dad had forgiven her?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She was hurt, and her sketchbook mattered to her. She forgot how much Dad had just let her off. When we hold our own hurt tightly, we forget the gift we’ve been given.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Dad ask Mia at dinner, and how did she react?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He asked if she was the same Mia who broke the TV; she realised. He didn’t shout; he asked a quiet question that helped her see.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is it different?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mia says her sketchbook matters more than any telly. Dad let her off a broken TV. Was Jun’s spill really different? What would you say to Mia?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the sketchbook really mattered and it was a real hurt; but the TV cost much more, and Dad let her off, and Jun’s spill was an accident and he was sorry. Follow-up: ‘What did Dad do that Mia didn’t do?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could Mia say to Jun?’ If silent: ask which is easier to forgive, an accident or something done on purpose.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The quiet question</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dad didn’t shout at Mia at dinner. He asked a gentle question. Why do you think that helped her more than telling her off?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: a question lets us see it ourselves; being told off makes us defend ourselves; Dad had shown her mercy first, so she trusted him; Jesus often asked questions too, like the king in the story. Follow-up: ‘When has a gentle question helped you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How would Mia have reacted if Dad had shouted?’ If silent: ask how they would feel if they were Mia.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When I remember how much I’ve been forgiven, it’s easier to forgive what others do to me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for letting me off. Help me see who needs me to do the same.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one small thing someone did to you this week, like an accident, a rude word or a lost thing. Say, “It’s okay, I forgive you,” before the end of tomorrow.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it to small hurts. If it is a big hurt, encourage the student to talk to a trusted adult first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Splash!</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You’re going to pay for every page!”<br>“I’ve said sorry five times!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 6:36: heart, open hands, link fingers, Father, heaven, wide arms.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Forgive us as we forgive — quiet reflection</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray silently. You don’t have to say anything aloud. God hears what you think. Imagine that you’re Mia at the dinner table, listening to a quiet question. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably and close your eyes, or look at the floor. Imagine you’re at the dinner table with Mia, and listen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about Dad’s kind face and the king’s pity. God is like that, only more.<br>Prompts: God, you are kinder than the kindest dad: you don’t shout, you forgive. / God, you let us off a debt we couldn’t pay.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are kind and you forgive us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have you been like Mia on Monday? Forgiven, but not forgiving?<br>Prompts: Sorry for holding on to what someone did, even though you’ve forgiven me so much. / Sorry for treating people as if they owe me.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the grudges we hold.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone who has let you off for something. Thank God for them.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Dad who forgive and still love us. / Thank you that you forgive me every time I come back to you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of someone you need to forgive. Ask God to help you.<br>Prompts: Please help me forgive that person, even if it takes a while. / Please help me say the words, “I forgive you”, when it’s time.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, forgive us as we forgive others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Slowly open your eyes. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Silence is the point. Keep the pace slow. Remind students they don’t have to name anyone; God knows. Some students may be thinking of a hurt at home: that’s okay, and you can offer to talk with them afterwards.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God, how Jesus paid our debt, and what Mia chooses to do next.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Forgiven, So Forgive":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 18:21–35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgiven, So Forgive</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgive us as we forgive — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others” (Matthew 6:12, CEV). Some Bibles say “forgive us our debts”: a debt is something we owe, and wrongdoing is like owing God. We pray this line honestly: we tell God sorry, and we ask him to help us let go of what others owe us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the unforgiving servant shows us about God and about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning why being forgiven helps us to forgive.</li><li>I can choose one person to forgive and pray for them by name.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable, explain that God has cancelled a debt we could never pay through Jesus, say that forgiving doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and recite Luke 6:36.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Cards from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Forgiven, So Forgive</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Is seven times enough?” — <b>Peter</b><br>“Not just seven times, but seventy-seven times!” — <b>Jesus</b><br>“Have pity on me, and I will pay you every cent!” — <b>The first servant</b><br>“The whole debt is cancelled.” — <b>The king</b><br>“It’s only a TV. You matter more.” — <b>Mia’s dad</b><br>“I’ve said sorry five times already!” — <b>Jun</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Was that from Monday’s parable or Wednesday’s retelling?’ Some quotes are paraphrased from Wednesday’s story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 6:36 · Matthew 6:12 · Luke 23:34</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 6:36</b> Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 6:12</b> Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 23:34</b> Father, forgive these people! They don’t know what they’re doing.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 6:36 is our memory verse. Matthew 6:12 is the line of the Lord’s Prayer we pray this week. Luke 23:34 is what Jesus said on the cross about the people who put him there. Merciful = full of pity and kindness, especially to someone who doesn’t deserve it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Forgiven, so forgive</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does this story show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, God is like the king, and he forgives. Not a little: all of it. Everything we could ever owe him for the wrong things we’ve done, he cancels. The Lord’s Prayer says: ‘Forgive us for doing wrong.’ Some Bibles say ‘forgive us our debts’.</p><p>Second, forgiving always costs someone. When the king tore up the scroll, he didn’t pretend the money wasn’t owed. He took the loss himself. And that is what Jesus did. On a cross outside Jerusalem, Jesus took the whole cost of our wrong on himself. He even prayed for the people who put him there: ‘Father, forgive these people! They don’t know what they’re doing.’ Our debt wasn’t ignored: it was paid. Peter knew this better than anyone. On the night Jesus was arrested, Peter said three times that he didn’t even know him. But after Jesus rose again, he asked Peter three times, ‘Do you love me?’, and gave him a fresh start.</p><p>Third, because we are forgiven, we can forgive. Not to earn it, but because we’ve been given so much. And forgiving doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt. The next morning, Mia took her dried, crinkly sketchbook to Jun. ‘It really hurt,’ she said. ‘That was my whole term. But I forgive you. And I’m sorry I was so mean.’ Jun let out a huge breath. ‘Seriously? Sweet as. Can I help you draw a new one?’ They found a wobbly ink blot on page three that looked a bit like a dragon, and both cracked up.</p><p>Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God is the forgiving King. · 2. Forgiving costs someone. · 3. Peter was forgiven too. · 4. Forgiven, so we forgive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Keep the cross simple and reverent: Jesus took the cost of our wrong and rose again; no detail. Peter’s denial is in Luke 22:54–62 and his restoring in John 21:15–19 (paraphrased). Mia’s apology and Jun’s reply are imagined. Teacher: forgiving is letting go of revenge and wishing someone well; it doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, or that a person is safe to be near. If a student has been seriously hurt, they should talk to a trusted adult.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did the king do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the king do with the first servant’s huge debt?<br>A) Made him pay it back slowly &nbsp; B) Cancelled all of it ✓ &nbsp; C) Doubled it &nbsp; D) Gave half of it to his friend</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, he cancelled it all (Matthew 18:27). Follow-up: ‘Why did he do it?’ (He felt pity.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who paid the cost?’ (The king did, and Jesus did for us.) If silent: re-read the second line of the story and ask what the king’s hands did to the scroll.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why forgive?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said, “Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” How does knowing God has forgiven us make it easier to forgive others?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: we remember how much we’ve been given; we don’t need to get even; we know we’re not perfect either; God helps us. Follow-up: ‘Why was the first servant not able to forgive?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does mercy look like at lunchtime in the playground?’ If silent: ask what Mia remembered at dinner when Dad asked her his question.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God has forgiven us a debt we could never repay, and Jesus paid the cost on the cross.</li><li>Forgiving doesn’t pretend it didn’t hurt. It lets go of getting even.</li><li>Because we’re forgiven, we forgive others: be merciful, just as your Father is merciful.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God has cancelled an enormous debt we could never pay, so we forgive others as we have been forgiven.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Too big to forgive?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Some hurts are too big to forgive.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Be gentle. Big hurts can take a long time, and a person may need help and safety first; but Jesus forgave even those who hurt him most, and he helps us. Follow-up: ‘Who can help us when forgiving is hard?’ (God, a trusted adult, a friend.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is the first small step?’ If silent: ask whether forgiving and trusting are the same thing.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose to forgive one person for one thing, even a small one, and pray for them by name every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the cards from Tuesday privately. Keep it small: an accident, a rude word, a lost thing. If a student’s hurt is big, encourage them to talk to a trusted adult. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Be merciful, just as your Father in heaven is merciful.” — Luke 6:36 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 6:36. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Forgiven, so forgive</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A debt we could never pay… cancelled.<br>Be merciful, just as your Father is merciful.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Forgive us as we forgive — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the whole line out loud together: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.” You can’t pray it wrong. A short, honest sentence is perfect. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is. Think of the king and the cross.<br>Prompts: God, you are merciful, and you cancel our debts! / God, you are the Father who forgives, and Jesus paid the cost.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are merciful, and you forgive!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about the wrong you’ve done and the grudges you hold.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the wrong things I’ve done. / Sorry for holding on to grudges when you’ve forgiven me so much.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for about forgiveness.<br>Prompts: Thank you that Jesus paid our debt on the cross. / Thank you for the people who have forgiven me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for forgiving us through Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together, then ask for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others. / Please help me forgive the one person I’ve chosen, and bless them.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others, and help us do it this week.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say the line together once more: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers. Don’t push anyone to share who they’re forgiving.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Three Servants — a master hands out bags of gold, and one servant buries his.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7: The Three Servants":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:14–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Generous Master</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead us, deliver us — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” (Matthew 6:13, CEV) — the line people often say as “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide the good things God gave us. We ask God to lead us and to rescue us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Five, Two and One</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story of the three servants begins: a master trusts his servants with his gold and goes away.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Five, Two and One → Trusted with something big → Matthew 25:15–21 → A master, a journey and three servants → How many bags? → Why different amounts? → The Three Servants → Safe, but not wise → Head · Heart · Hands → Look after this for me → Memory verse peek → Lead us, deliver us — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Jesus Told It</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning where Jesus told this story and who was listening.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Jesus Told It → Recap → Matthew 25:21, 24–25 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → What was he? → Fear’s picture of the master → Is playing it safe wise? → Head · Heart · Hands → The master comes home → Build the verse → Lead us, deliver us — write a note</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Gala Day Twenty</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story of the three servants looks in a modern New Zealand school gala.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Gala Day Twenty → Scared to get it wrong → Matthew 25:15, 21, 25 → The gala day twenty → Where’s the twenty? → The Gala Day Twenty → Where did the fear come from? → How do you picture God? → Head · Heart · Hands → Safe in the drawer → Verse with actions → Lead us, deliver us — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Well Done!</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the three servants show us about God and about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Well Done! → Put it in order → Matthew 25:21 · 6:13 → A generous Master → What did the master say? → A generous God → This week’s big idea → Do I have a gift? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Trust it, use it, share it → Lead us, deliver us — stand up</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Five, Two and One":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:14–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Five, Two and One</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead us, deliver us — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” (Matthew 6:13, CEV) — the line people often say as “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide the good things God gave us. We ask God to lead us and to rescue us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story of the three servants begins: a master trusts his servants with his gold and goes away.</li><li>We are learning what each servant did with the gold he was given.</li><li>I can retell the story and say which servant did something different, and what the master said to each.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that the servants were given five, two and one bags of gold, that two of them put it to work and one buried his, and that the master said exactly the same “good and faithful servant” words to the first two.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: three small cloth bags or paper cups with 5, 2 and 1 coins</li><li>Optional: a small spade or spoon for the digging actions</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Five, Two and One</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have three small bags or cups with 5, 2 and 1 coins, set them out where students can see them all week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Trusted with something big</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine your neighbours go away for a month and ask you to look after something precious to them — their pet, or their garden. How would you feel?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Would you be excited, nervous, or both? What would you do to look after it well?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard The Servant Who Wouldn’t Forgive — the servant forgiven a gigantic debt who wouldn’t forgive a tiny one. Your challenge: choose to forgive one person for one thing, even a small one, and pray for them by name. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students how the forgiving went. Keep it light and never ask anyone to name the person; celebrate honest ‘it was hard’ answers as much as success stories. Then the hook: take a few answers about excitement and nerves, and land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about a master who trusts his servants with a fortune and goes away, and the three servants each do something very different.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:15–21</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:15</b> The man knew what each servant could do. So he handed five thousand coins to the first servant, two thousand to the second, and one thousand to the third. Then he left the country.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:16</b> As soon as the man had gone, the servant with the five thousand coins went out and used them to earn five thousand more.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:18</b> But the servant with one thousand coins dug a hole and hid his master’s money in the ground.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:21</b> “Wonderful!” his master replied. “You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 25:21 is our memory verse. The CEV says “five thousand coins”; the original word is “talents”, a huge weight of silver, and we say “bags of gold” in the story. We meet the size of the gift tomorrow. This reading is shortened; the whole parable is Matthew 25:14–30.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A master, a journey and three servants</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture a grand stone courtyard at sunrise. A wealthy master is about to leave on a very long journey, so long that nobody knows exactly how many months he will be away. Beyond the open gate, his camels are loaded and waiting. But before he goes, he calls in his three servants.</p><p>Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. This one begins with a master who trusts his servants completely.</p><p>The master knows his servants well. He knows what each of them can do. So he sets heavy bags of gold on a wooden table. To the first servant he gives five bags. To the second, two bags. To the third, one bag. ‘Look after this for me,’ he says. ‘I’ll be back.’ Then he strides out through the gate, and the caravan disappears over the hill.</p><p>The first servant doesn’t waste a minute. He grabs his five bags and heads straight for the market. He buys, he sells, he bargains with merchants all day long. Before long, his five bags have turned into ten.</p><p>The second servant is quieter, but he does the same thing. Jesus doesn’t tell us how, so we can imagine: maybe he sets up a little workshop, and his skilful hands go to work. Slowly, his two bags become four.</p><p>Then there is the third servant. He looks at his single bag. It is heavy. It is gold. And it isn’t his. What if he loses it? What if something goes wrong? His stomach twists with worry. So that night he creeps out beneath an old olive tree with a spade. He digs a hole, lowers his master’s bag into it, covers it with dirt and pats it flat. He glances over his shoulder. Safe, he thinks. Nobody can touch it now.</p><p>A long time goes by. Then one day the gates swing open, and the master is home.</p><p>He calls his servants in. The first servant comes forward with ten bags. ‘Sir, you gave me five. Look, I’ve earned five more!’ And the master’s whole face lights up. ‘Wonderful! You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!’</p><p>The second servant steps up with four bags, and the master says exactly the same words to him.</p><p>Then the third servant shuffles in, holding a small, dirty bag. His hands are shaking. What will the master say to him? Let’s find out in the role-play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A master leaves on a long journey. · 2. Five bags. Two bags. One bag. · 3. Two get to work. One digs a hole. · 4. A long time goes by. · 5. “Well done, good and faithful servant!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The story is Matthew 25:14–30. Imagined details: the market and the workshop (Jesus only says the first two servants “earned” more), the olive tree, the master’s caravan and the servant’s worry. Say so. Keep the master warm and kind: tomorrow’s twist depends on it. The ending, where the third servant ends up outside in the dark, comes in the role-play: state it plainly and gently, as a true warning, never as a scare.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How many bags?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many bags of gold did the three servants each receive?<br>A) 5, 3 and 1 &nbsp; B) 5, 2 and 1 ✓ &nbsp; C) 10, 5 and 1 &nbsp; D) 3, 3 and 3</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, five, two and one (Matthew 25:15). Follow-up: ‘What did the first two servants do with their gold?’ (Put it to work and earned the same amount again.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the third servant do?’ (Dug a hole and hid it.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line slowly and ask them to count along.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why different amounts?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The master gave five, two and one, not three equal piles. Why do you think he gave different amounts?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the Bible says the man knew what each servant could do (Matthew 25:15); he gave each as much as they could handle; it wasn’t about favourites, it was about knowing his servants well. Follow-up: ‘Would equal piles have been fairer?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do you think the master wanted from each one?’ If silent: ask who has ever been given a job just the right size for them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Three Servants</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Three Servants</b> (Matthew 25:14–30). The master’s and servants’ words from Matthew 25:15–27 are kept close to the CEV, and shortened. The market, the workshop, the servants’ worries and the actions are imagined, to bring the story to life. We leave out one harsh word the master uses, so no student is cast as its target.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Master</b> — A wealthy, warm, generous master<br><b>First Servant</b> — Gets five bags — bold and busy<br><b>Second Servant</b> — Gets two bags — quiet and skilful<br><b>Third Servant</b> — Gets one bag — nervous and careful<br><b>Everyone</b> — Clinking gold, market noise, digging</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: tap the desk like clinking gold coins · Everyone: dig slowly with both hands, then pat the ground<br>Props: Optional: three small bags or paper cups as the bags of gold · Optional: a chair as the master’s big seat<br>Jesus’ own words in the master’s lines are CEV Matthew 25:21 and 25:27, and the third servant’s are CEV 25:24–25 (shortened). Play the master warmly for the first two servants, and calm and sad, not shouting, with the third. The ending (outside, in the dark) is stated plainly and gently: choices matter and Jesus is telling us the truth because he cares. Matthew’s full wording is stronger; we do not dwell on it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A grand courtyard at sunrise. A wealthy master is leaving on a long journey. His camels wait at the gate.</li><li><b>Master:</b> My friends, I’m going away for a long time. I’m trusting you with everything I own. <i>(spread arms warmly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> He knew what each servant could do. So he handed out the gold.</li><li><b>Master:</b> Five bags of gold for you. Two for you. And one for you. Look after it for me. <i>(hand out three pretend bags)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Clink, clink, clink! Jingle, jingle! <i>(tap desks like gold coins)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then the master left. The camels groaned, the caravan moved off, and the dust settled.</li><li><b>First Servant:</b> Five bags! Off to the market. We’ll buy, sell and bargain all day! <i>(rub hands, march on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Second Servant:</b> Two bags is a good start. I’ll set up a workshop and get to work. <i>(hammer on the desk)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Buy! Sell! Bargain! Hammer! Work, work, work! <i>(busy hands everywhere)</i></li><li><b>Third Servant:</b> One bag. It’s so heavy. What if I lose it? What if I get it wrong? <i>(hug the bag, look worried)</i></li><li><b>Third Servant:</b> I’ll bury it. Then it’s safe. Nobody can blame me for losing it. <i>(dig with both hands, glance back)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Dig… dig… dig… pat, pat, pat. <i>(slow, quiet digging)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Three servants, three choices. Which servant is most like you right now? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> A long time went by. Then one day the gates opened. The master was home.</li><li><b>Master:</b> Come in, all of you. Tell me what you’ve done with my gold. <i>(sit on a big chair)</i></li><li><b>First Servant:</b> Sir, you gave me five bags, and look! I’ve earned five more! <i>(proud, arms wide)</i></li><li><b>Master:</b> Wonderful! You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little. <i>(beaming)</i></li><li><b>Master:</b> But now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!</li><li><b>Second Servant:</b> Sir, you gave me two bags, and I’ve earned two more! <i>(bow)</i></li><li><b>Master:</b> Wonderful! You are a good and faithful servant! Come and share in my happiness! <i>(exactly the same warm smile)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The third servant came in last, holding a small, muddy bag. His hands were shaking.</li><li><b>Third Servant:</b> Sir, I know that you are hard to get along with. I was frightened and went out and hid your money in the ground. <i>(eyes down)</i></li><li><b>Third Servant:</b> Here is every single coin! <i>(hold out the muddy bag)</i></li><li><b>Master:</b> You could have at least put my money in the bank, so that I could have earned interest on it. <i>(sad and calm, not shouting)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Jesus said that servant ended up outside in the dark, sad and sorry. It is a serious warning.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Don’t bury it. Use it! <i>(dig, then throw both hands open)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The third servant didn’t lose a single coin. Why do you think the master still wasn’t pleased?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He didn’t use what he was given. He played it safe because he was afraid. Tomorrow we find out what he believed about his master.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the master say to the first two servants, and was it the same?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: It was exactly the same: “You are a good and faithful servant!” Five bags or two, he welcomed both.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Safe, but not wise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Burying the gold kept it safe. So what do you think the third servant got wrong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: he didn’t use what he was given; he let fear decide; safe isn’t the same as faithful; the master wanted his gold to grow, not sit in a hole. Follow-up: ‘Is it ever wise to play it safe?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do you think the servant believed about his master?’ (Tomorrow’s surprise.) If silent: ask what the first two servants did that the third didn’t.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God trusts us with good things — and he wants us to use them, not bury them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I don’t want to hide what you’ve given me because I’m afraid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, notice one thing you’re good at, such as drawing, listening or running fast, and use it to help someone at home, at school or at sport.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. Help students who say “I’m not good at anything” by naming small things: kindness, a sense of humour, patience, being on time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Look after this for me</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Look after this for me. I’ll be back.”<br>Five bags. Two bags. One bag.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Matthew 25:21. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Tell them these are the master’s happy words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Lead us, deliver us — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All term we’re praying the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time. This week: “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” — many people say “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide. “Deliver” means rescue. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make a tight fist, like you’re gripping something you’re scared to lose. Take a slow breath and notice how it feels.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Slowly open one hand. God holds everything, and he gives it away freely.<br>Prompts: God, you are generous. You trusted three servants with a fortune before the master even left. / God, you are a Master who trusts us, not one who watches us nervously.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are generous, and you trust us with so much!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your hands. Silently tell God about something good you’ve hidden because you were afraid.<br>Prompts: Sorry for burying what you gave me, like the servant with the spade. / Sorry for the times I thought you were harsh and didn’t trust you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve hidden what you gave us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now open both hands, palms up. Thank God for what he has already put in them.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the things I’m good at, even the small ones. / Thank you for the people who trust me with jobs and let me try.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the gifts you’ve put in our hands.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together: Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil.<br>Prompts: Please rescue me from the fear that makes me hide. / Please lead me to use one thing I have for someone else today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, keep me from…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep us from fear, and lead us to use what you gave us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your hands open for a moment, ready to receive and ready to give. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optional: hold a coin or small object in a closed fist, then open the hand. Explain “tempted” simply: a pull towards the wrong thing. Be gentle: some students are anxious, and being scared is not a failure. The invitation is to bring the fear to God.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — who was listening on the Mount of Olives, and the three surprises that would have shocked them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Jesus Told It":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:14–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus Told It</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead us, deliver us — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” (Matthew 6:13, CEV) — the line people often say as “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide the good things God gave us. We ask God to lead us and to rescue us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning where Jesus told this story and who was listening.</li><li>We are learning the three surprises: the huge size of the gift, the same “Well done!”, and the servant who was afraid of the wrong master.</li><li>I can explain who the master, the servants and the gold stand for.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming the Mount of Olives and the disciples, explaining that even one bag was a fortune, noticing the identical praise, and saying that the third servant hid his gold because he thought the master was harsh when he was really generous.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Jesus Told It</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday a master handed out five, two and one bags of gold and went on a long journey.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did each servant do with the gold, and which servant surprised you most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The first two put their gold to work and earned the same again; the third buried his. Take two or three answers about who surprised them. ‘Today we find out who was listening when Jesus told this story, and three surprises that would have shocked his friends.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:21, 24–25</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:21</b> “Wonderful!” his master replied. “You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:24</b> “Sir, I know that you are hard to get along with. You harvest what you don’t plant and gather crops where you haven’t scattered seed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:25</b> I was frightened and went out and hid your money in the ground. Here is every single coin!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>“Hard to get along with” means harsh and tough. This is the servant’s opinion of his master. Notice that the master never agrees that it is true. Today’s big question: what did the third servant believe about his master?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s zoom out. Where was Jesus when he told this story, and who was listening?</p><p>It is the last week of Jesus’ life. Within days he will be arrested and put on a cross. Jesus has been teaching in Jerusalem all day, and now he is sitting on the Mount of Olives, a hill just outside the city. Below him, the temple glows in the evening sun. With him are his closest friends, the disciples, including Peter, Andrew, James and John. They have asked him a big question: when are you coming back, and what should we do while we wait?</p><p>So Jesus tells them a story about a master who goes away and comes back. The disciples would have caught the meaning straight away. Jesus himself was about to go away, back to his Father, and one day he will return. So what should his friends do in the meantime? Here are three surprises.</p><p>Surprise number one: the size of the gift. The word for the money in the story is ‘talent’. One talent was an enormous weight of silver, roughly twenty years of pay for a normal worker. So even the servant with one bag was handed a fortune! Nobody was given scraps. And here is a fun fact: our English word ‘talent’, meaning a gift or skill, comes from this very parable.</p><p>Surprise number two: the master’s reaction. To the servant with ten bags and the servant with four bags, he says exactly the same words: ‘You are a good and faithful servant!’ He isn’t comparing them. He wasn’t after the biggest result. He was pleased that both had used what they were given. And look at the reward: more to look after, and a share in the master’s own happiness.</p><p>Surprise number three is the biggest. The third servant didn’t steal a thing. He didn’t waste a thing. He handed back every coin. He was the most careful servant in the story! So why was the master disappointed? Listen to what the servant says: ‘Sir, I know that you are hard to get along with… I was frightened.’ He pictured a harsh master who would punish every mistake. So he played it safe, and did nothing. But look at the master we actually met. He is generous. He trusted them with a fortune before he even left. The servant was afraid of a master who didn’t exist, and that fear made him hide the very thing the master wanted him to use.</p><p>Jesus doesn’t soften the ending. That servant ended up outside, in the dark, sad and sorry. He says it plainly because choices matter, and because he cares. So who is who? The master is Jesus. The servants are his followers. The gold is everything he gives us. And hiding it out of fear is the one choice that costs us the joy.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The last week of Jesus’ life. · 2. Who was listening? · 3. Surprise one: even one bag was a fortune. · 4. Surprise two: the very same “Well done!” · 5. Surprise three: afraid of the wrong master.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Matthew 24:3 says the disciples came to Jesus privately on the Mount of Olives; Mark 13:3 names Peter, James, John and Andrew. A talent was about 6,000 denarii, and a denarius was a day’s wage, so roughly twenty years’ pay: say “roughly”. The word “talent” for a skill really does come from this parable. Sensitive: the “outside in the dark” ending. State it plainly, gently and briefly: choices have consequences, and Jesus tells us the truth because he loves us. Never describe it graphically.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The master</td><td>Jesus, who went back to his Father and will return</td></tr><tr><td>The servants</td><td>Jesus’ followers, including us</td></tr><tr><td>The bags of gold</td><td>Everything God trusts us with: gifts, time, kindness, his good news</td></tr><tr><td>The long journey</td><td>The time between Jesus going and coming back</td></tr><tr><td>The hole in the ground</td><td>Hiding what God gave us because we’re afraid</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The master was generous. The third servant was afraid of a master who wasn’t there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Say plainly that Jesus is the master, and that being his servant here means being his friend and follower who is trusted with real responsibility. Don’t spend long on the judgment: the heart of the story is trust versus fear.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was he?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “I was ________ and went out and hid your money in the ground.”<br><i>Answer: “…frightened.” (Matthew 25:25)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: frightened. Follow-up: ‘What did the third servant say his master was like?’ (Hard to get along with, harsh.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he do with the money?’ (Hid it in the ground.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fear’s picture of the master</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The third servant lost nothing, but the master was disappointed. Why do you think what he believed about his master mattered so much?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: if you think someone is harsh, you’re too scared to try; fear made him hide instead of using the gift; his picture of the master was wrong, because the master had trusted him with a fortune; what we believe about God changes what we do. Follow-up: ‘What did the master actually do that showed he was generous?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could the servant have found out the truth?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is playing it safe wise?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Playing it safe is always the wisest choice.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree for this story: the safe choice was the one that wasted the gold, and the master wanted it used. But careful thinking isn’t the problem; fear is. Follow-up: ‘When is being careful wise, and when is it just fear?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would trusting the master have looked like?’ If silent: ask what the first two servants risked, and what they got.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The master is generous and the gold is a gift, so we can use what God gave us without being afraid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, help me believe that you are good and kind, not harsh.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On a sticky note, write one thing you’re good at, or one thing you have, such as time, a skill or kindness. Beside it, write one person it could help. Keep it for Thursday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out sticky notes. Help students who say “I’m not good at anything”: gifts include listening, cheering people up, being patient, being fast, being tidy. This feeds into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The master comes home</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The same “Well done!” for ten bags and for four.<br>He was afraid of a master who wasn’t there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Matthew 25:21 chunk by chunk, like stacking bags of gold: each chunk a bag, until the verse is built.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Lead us, deliver us — write a note</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your prayer as a short note to God. Because God is generous and not harsh, you can be completely honest: say what you’re afraid of, ask for courage, and thank him for the gifts you’ve found. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top of your paper or sticky note, write: “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are generous. You give before you ask for anything. / God, you are kind, not harsh. You are not the master the third servant feared.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are generous and kind, not harsh!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, write one thing you’ve hidden or avoided because you were scared to get it wrong.<br>Prompts: Sorry for thinking you’re hard on me when you are kind. / Sorry for hiding what you gave me because I was scared to try.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for hiding what you gave us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write three gifts God has given you: things you’re good at, or things you have.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the gifts I’ve been given, big and small. / Thank you for trusting me with them.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the gifts you trusted us with.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one fear you want God to rescue you from, and one person you could help.<br>Prompts: Please rescue me from the fear that makes me hide. / Please lead me to one person I can help with my gift this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, keep me from…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep us from fear, and lead us to help someone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your note and keep it in your pocket today. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their notes private. Some students are anxious about failing. Reassure them that being nervous is normal, and that the master in the story wanted his servants to try, not to be perfect.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a school gala day in modern Aotearoa, where everyone is trusted with twenty dollars and one student hides his in a drawer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Gala Day Twenty":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:14–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Gala Day Twenty</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead us, deliver us — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” (Matthew 6:13, CEV) — the line people often say as “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide the good things God gave us. We ask God to lead us and to rescue us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story of the three servants looks in a modern New Zealand school gala.</li><li>We are learning why fear can make us hide the good things we’ve been trusted with.</li><li>I can name one thing I could grow or share instead of hiding.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Aroha and Sione to the first two servants and Jun to the third, explaining that Jun hid his twenty dollars because he was afraid, and noticing that Mr Walker is kind, not harsh.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: three envelopes with a pretend twenty-dollar note</li><li>Optional: a chair for a drawer-sliding action</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Gala Day Twenty</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is a retelling in modern Aotearoa; Jesus’ parable is the same, with new servants.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Scared to get it wrong</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been trusted with something important, such as looking after a little cousin or a friend’s pet, and been scared of getting it wrong?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did you do? Did the fear help or hurt?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned the third servant wasn’t lazy or a thief. He was afraid of a master who was really generous. Today the same story happens at a school gala.’ Notice if anyone mentions hiding or avoiding the job; that is the third servant’s choice.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:15, 21, 25</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:15</b> The man knew what each servant could do.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:21</b> “Wonderful!” his master replied. “You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:25</b> I was frightened and went out and hid your money in the ground.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story is not in the Bible. It is Jesus’ parable retold with new servants. In Jesus’ story the amounts differ because the master knew each servant. Mr Walker gives everyone twenty dollars to keep the story simple; the point is the same.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The gala day twenty</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It was gala day at Room 14’s school, and the whole field looked like a festival. Bunting flapped between the trees. A bouncy castle wobbled on the grass. Stalls lined the walkway, and the smell of sausages floated across the playground.</p><p>But first, Mr Walker gathered his class in a circle, holding a stack of small white envelopes. ‘Today I’m trusting you with something,’ he said. ‘Each of you gets twenty dollars to start with. It’s yours to use for the school library fund, however you like. Grow it as much as you can. At half past two, we’ll meet in the hall to see what you’ve done.’ Then he grinned. ‘I can’t wait to see what you come up with.’</p><p>Aroha knew straight away. She loves baking, and her nan has a famous fudge recipe. She spent the twenty dollars on butter, condensed milk and chocolate, and by ten o’clock she had a stall full of paper bags tied with ribbon. Families queued all the way down the walkway.</p><p>Sione is no baker. But he is big, strong, and never scared of getting wet. He bought sponges, buckets and car-wash soap, and set up a sign that said SUPER SUDS at the edge of the car park. By eleven o’clock he was soaked from head to toe, grinning, and scrubbing every dad’s ute that rolled in.</p><p>And Jun? Jun looked at his envelope for a long time. He was good at heaps of things: fixing bikes, telling jokes, riding faster than anyone. But a little voice in his head kept going. What if I lose it? What if I spend it and nobody buys anything? What if Mr Walker is cross? Jun had lost a library book once, and he still remembered how awful that felt. So he did what felt safest. He slipped back to the empty classroom, slid open his drawer, and tucked the envelope right at the back. ‘Safe,’ he whispered. Then he sat by the window and watched everyone else have the best day of the term.</p><p>At half past two, the class gathered in the hall. ‘Aroha?’ said Mr Walker. ‘Eighty-five dollars!’ The class cheered. ‘Sione?’ ‘Sixty-four!’ Mr Walker shook their hands. ‘Well done! You used what I trusted you with, and look how it grew.’</p><p>Then Jun came in slowly, holding out one crumpled envelope. ‘It’s still twenty dollars,’ he mumbled. ‘I didn’t lose it. I was scared you’d be mad.’</p><p>Mr Walker looked at him kindly. ‘Jun, I’d never be mad if you tried and lost it. I’m only sad that your twenty dollars did nothing.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Gala day: twenty dollars each. · 2. Aroha bakes. Sione scrubs. · 3. And Jun? He’s not at the gala. · 4. Half past two. Count-up time. · 5. “I was scared you’d be mad.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mr Walker, Aroha, Sione and Jun are imagined, as are the figures and Jun’s lost library book. Portray Jun with warmth: he’s capable and funny, and his fear has a reason. He is not the “bad kid”. Mr Walker must be clearly kind: the surprise of the parable is that the master wasn’t the harsh man the servant imagined. A sausage sizzle and a gala are common New Zealand school fundraisers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where’s the twenty?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did Jun put his twenty dollars?<br>A) In the bouncy castle ticket box &nbsp; B) At the back of his desk drawer ✓ &nbsp; C) In his bike helmet &nbsp; D) Back in Mr Walker’s hand</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, at the back of his desk drawer. Follow-up: ‘What did Aroha and Sione do with their twenty dollars?’ (Aroha made fudge; Sione ran a car wash.) Follow-up 2: ‘Which servant in Jesus’ story is Jun like?’ (The third, who buried his bag.) If silent: re-read the beat about Jun in the classroom.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Gala Day Twenty</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Gala Day Twenty</b> (Matthew 25:14–30 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (a trusted gift used or hidden, and a kind master) comes from Matthew 25:14–30.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mr Walker</b> — The teacher: warm, funny and fair<br><b>Aroha</b> — Loves baking, quick to act<br><b>Sione</b> — Big-hearted, loud, loves a challenge<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky and clever, but scared of getting it wrong<br><b>Everyone</b> — The class: stalls, cheers and drawer sounds</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: scrub, stir and call out like market sellers · Everyone: cheer and clap for “Well done!”<br>Props: Optional: three envelopes · Optional: a sponge or tea towel for the car wash<br>Imagined retelling. Play Jun with warmth and a bit of cheek, not as a failure. Mr Walker must be clearly kind: that is the surprise. The “missed the gala” line is the gentle consequence: hiding what we’re trusted with costs us the joy. Keep the sound effects short, and stop them quickly when Mr Walker speaks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Gala day at school. Bunting flaps, the bouncy castle wobbles and the sausage sizzle smells amazing. Room 14 stands in a circle.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> I’m trusting each of you with twenty dollars. Grow it for the library. We count up at half past two. <i>(hand out pretend envelopes)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Twenty bucks? Easy! I’m running a car wash. Super Suds, here I come! <i>(flex arms)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I’m making Nan’s fudge. Butter, chocolate, condensed milk. Every bag will sell itself. <i>(stir an imaginary bowl)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Twenty bucks! I could buy heaps… but what if I lose it? What if nobody buys anything? <i>(clutch the envelope)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Scrub, scrub! Stir, stir! Sell, sell! <i>(scrub, stir and call out like market sellers)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Fresh fudge! Two dollars a bag! Get it while it’s warm! <i>(hold up a bag)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Super Suds car wash! Dads, your ute needs a bath! <i>(scrub a pretend ute, soggy)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Meanwhile, Jun slipped back to the empty classroom with his envelope still sealed.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Mr Walker trusts me. But I lost a library book once, and I felt terrible. <i>(kick the floor)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> I’ll keep it safe. Back of the drawer. Then nobody can say I lost it. <i>(slide the envelope into a pretend drawer)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Creeeak… THUD. <i>(drawer closing)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Aroha and Sione are busy. Jun’s money is in a drawer. What do you think Mr Walker will say at half past two? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Half past two. The class gathers in the hall. Mr Walker has a big smile and a calculator.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Aroha first. What did your twenty dollars turn into?</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Eighty-five dollars, sir! Nan’s fudge sold out! <i>(hold up pretend cash)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Sixty-four dollars! And I’m soaked right through. I’ll never be dry again. <i>(shake like a wet dog)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Well done, both of you! You used what I trusted you with, and look how it grew.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Well done! <i>(cheer and clap)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then Jun walked in slowly. He held out one crumpled envelope.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> It’s still twenty dollars. I didn’t lose it. I was scared you’d be mad. <i>(eyes down)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Mad? Jun, I’m a teacher, not a dragon! I’d never be mad if you tried and lost it. <i>(kind smile)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> I’m just sad it did nothing. Twenty dollars in a drawer can’t help anybody, and you missed the gala.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Sorry, sir. I thought you’d be really strict about money. <i>(shrug)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> I gave it to you because I trusted you, Jun. I still do.</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Come on, Jun. Help us count the takings. Next year, we’re doing a stall together. <i>(wave him over)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Don’t hide it. Use it! <i>(open both hands wide)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did Jun hide his twenty dollars? What was he really afraid of?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Losing it, getting it wrong, and Mr Walker being cross. He pictured a strict teacher. Like the third servant, he was afraid of a master who wasn’t really like that.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How was Mr Walker different from the teacher Jun imagined?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was kind, not harsh. He said he would never have been mad if Jun had tried and lost it, and he still trusted him.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did the fear come from?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mr Walker was kind, but Jun was scared of him. Where do you think Jun’s fear came from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he lost a library book once and remembered the bad feeling; he imagined the worst; fear can make someone seem harsher than they really are; he didn’t know how Mr Walker would respond. Follow-up: ‘Has fear ever made you picture someone as harsher than they were?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could Jun have found out the truth?’ (He could have asked or tried.) If silent: ask what Jun might have felt sitting by the window.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How do you picture God?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your whiteboard, write one word for how a scared person might picture God. Then write one word from Jesus’ story that shows what God is really like.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Scared words: strict, angry, grumpy, watching, harsh. True words from the story: generous, trusting, kind, happy, welcoming. Follow-up: ‘What is the difference between a master who watches for mistakes and one who says “Well done”?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do you think the third servant would have done if he’d known the master well?’ If silent: share two words yourself and ask which feels closer to Jesus’ master.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fear makes us hide what we’ve been given; trust sets it free.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me believe you’re kind, so I’m not scared to try.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one thing you’ve been holding back because you’re scared of getting it wrong, such as joining in at sport, sharing a drawing or offering to help, and give it a go before Friday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and specific. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Safe in the drawer</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Safe. Nobody can say I lost it.”<br>Everyone else was having the best day of the term.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Matthew 25:21: point to yourself, thumbs up, hands close together, hands wide, arms up high, open arms.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Lead us, deliver us — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Tell your partner one thing you’re nervous to try. Then each pray one sentence for the other: “Keep (name) from being afraid, and lead them.” That’s how we pray this line for each other. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Quietly share one thing you’re a bit nervous to try. It can be small.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you are kind, like Mr Walker, only so much more. / God, you are generous. You trust us with so much.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are kind and generous!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think of a time fear made you hide something good.<br>Prompts: Sorry for hiding things because I was scared you’d be cross. / Sorry for not trusting that you are kind.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve hidden the good things you gave us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who say “Well done” and who still trust us when we get it wrong.<br>Prompts: Thank you for teachers and coaches who encourage us. / Thank you for people like Mr Walker who give us a second chance.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who trust and encourage us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s nervous thing.<br>Prompts: Please keep (partner’s name) from being afraid to try. / Please lead us both to use what you gave us, for others.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, keep…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep us from fear, and lead us to use what you gave us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a partner’s nervous thing is private or sensitive, they can say “something I’m a bit scared of” and leave it there.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God, our Generous Master, and your challenge: name a gift and use it for someone else.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Well Done!":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:14–30</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Well Done!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lead us, deliver us — This week’s line from the Lord’s Prayer is “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil” (Matthew 6:13, CEV) — the line people often say as “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”. Temptation is a pull towards the wrong thing, and fear can pull us to hide the good things God gave us. We ask God to lead us and to rescue us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the three servants show us about God and about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning to use our gifts out of trust, not fear.</li><li>I can name one gift God gave me and plan how to use it for someone else.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable in order, say that God is a generous Master who says “Well done!”, explain that we use our gifts out of trust and not fear, name one gift and one person to help with it, and recite Matthew 25:21.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky notes from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Well Done!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put it in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put the parable in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The master hands out five, two and one bags → The master goes on a long journey → Two servants put their gold to work → One servant buries his bag → The master comes home → “Well done!” for the faithful servants → The frightened servant hands back one bag</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out the order: the master trusted first, and the praise came before the problem.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:21 · 6:13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:21</b> “Wonderful!” his master replied. “You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 6:13</b> Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 6:13 is the line of the Lord’s Prayer we pray this week. The older words are “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A generous Master</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does Jesus’ story show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, God is a generous Master. Before the master left, he gave a fortune to all three servants, even the one with the smallest share. God does the same. He gave you life, a mind, a body and gifts: maybe music, maths, kindness, speed or patience. A gift isn’t a test. It’s a present with a purpose.</p><p>Second, God loves to say ‘Well done!’ He said the same words to ten bags and to four. He isn’t comparing you with the person next to you. He’s looking for someone who trusts him enough to use what they’ve been given.</p><p>Third, we use our gifts out of trust, not fear. The third servant pictured a harsh master, so he hid. But Jesus shows us a very different Master. He is the one who took everything his Father gave him, even his own life, and gave it away for us. And when we hide, Jesus doesn’t throw us out. Peter was so frightened one night that he said he didn’t know Jesus. But after Jesus rose again, he found Peter, forgave him, and trusted him with his friends again. Peter later wrote that each person has a gift to use to serve others.</p><p>So what does that look like? Aroha loves music. This week she took her ukulele to the juniors’ classroom and played for the five-year-olds, while her friend Mia held up a big picture to go with the song. Jun found out that his gift is fixing bikes. He spent his lunchtime fixing a Year 1 boy’s chain. Sione spent his lunchtime helping little kids learn to catch a rugby ball. Nobody had to be asked twice.</p><p>So what’s your gift? And who could it help?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God is a generous Master. · 2. He loves to say “Well done!” · 3. Trust, not fear. · 4. Use it for someone else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Peter’s denial and restoration: Luke 22:54–62 and John 21:15–17 (paraphrased). 1 Peter 4:10 is paraphrased. The modern kids’ examples (Aroha’s ukulele, Mia’s picture, Jun’s bike repair and Sione’s rugby help) are imagined. Keep the cross simple and reverent: Jesus gave everything for us, and rose again.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did the master say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the master say to the servants who used their gold?<br>A) “Wonderful! You are a good and faithful servant!” ✓ &nbsp; B) “You were lucky.” &nbsp; C) “Next time, bury it.” &nbsp; D) “You owe me more.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A (Matthew 25:21). Follow-up: ‘What was his reward for them?’ (More to look after, and a share in his happiness.) Follow-up 2: ‘Did he say it to both of them?’ (Yes, the same words.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A generous God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The third servant thought his master was harsh. What has Jesus done that shows God is generous and not harsh?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus gave his whole life for us; he forgives us and trusts us again, like Peter; God gives gifts before we’ve done anything; he welcomes people who come to him. Follow-up: ‘What did the master give the servants before he left?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could knowing God is generous change what you do?’ If silent: ask what Jesus did for Peter after Peter was afraid.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God is a generous Master: he trusts us with real gifts before we’ve done anything.</li><li>He loves to say “Well done!” to anyone who uses what they were given, big or small.</li><li>We use our gifts out of trust, not fear, and Jesus, who gave everything for us, welcomes us back when we’ve hidden.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is a generous Master who trusts us with gifts and loves to say “Well done!” — so we use what he has given us for others, out of trust, not fear.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Do I have a gift?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “I don’t have any gift worth using.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: even the servant with one bag had a fortune; gifts include kindness, listening, cheering people up, being fast, being patient, drawing, music; if you’re not sure, ask a friend or teacher what they notice. Follow-up: ‘What do people thank you for?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you ask what your gift is?’ If silent: share one gift you notice in the class, and invite students to name one in a neighbour.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Name one gift God gave you and use it for someone else, twice this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the sticky notes from Tuesday: the gift and the person it could help. Ideas: music, drawing, fixing, cheering people up, helping a younger child, being patient. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You are a good and faithful servant! I left you in charge of only a little, but now I will put you in charge of much more. Come and share in my happiness!” — Matthew 25:21 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Matthew 25:21. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Trust it, use it, share it</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Trust the Master. Use the gift.<br>“Come and share in my happiness!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Lead us, deliver us — stand up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the whole line together: “Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil.” Fear can tempt us to hide our gifts. Standing is a way of saying, “God, I’m ready to step out.” <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up tall with your feet planted. Take a slow breath in and out. We’ll pray standing, and take one step forward at the end.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand tall. Say who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are a Generous Master. You give before we do anything. / God, you are good and not harsh. You say “Well done” to those who try.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are a Generous Master, and you love to say “Well done”!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bow your head. Silently tell God about a gift you’ve hidden because you were afraid.<br>Prompts: Sorry for burying what you gave me. / Sorry for thinking you’d be angry if I got it wrong.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for hiding what you gave us. Thank you for trusting us again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your hands. Call out one gift God gave you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for my gift. Name it out loud or in your heart. / Thank you for Jesus, who gave everything for us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the gifts you gave us, and for Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the line together, then ask for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Keep us from being tempted and protect us from evil. / Please help me use my gift twice this week for someone else.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Keep us from fear, and help us use our gifts for others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take one step forward, ready to use your gift. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Anyone who can’t stand can pray sitting up tall.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Ten Bridesmaids — ten friends wait for the groom at midnight, and five of them run out of oil.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8: The Ten Bridesmaids":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Coming Again</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yours is the kingdom — This week we pray the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer: “For yours is the kingdom, the power and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen.” The CEV gives it as “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t have this line, but it is a wonderful way to finish: it says God is King, he is in charge, and he is coming. Today you don’t ask for anything; you just say the kingdom is his.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Waiting for the Groom</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Jesus told a story about ten bridesmaids waiting for a groom who was late.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Waiting for the Groom → What would you bring? → Matthew 25:1–13 → Ten bridesmaids → Who fell asleep? → Foolish, but not bad → The Ten Bridesmaids → Fair or not? → Head · Heart · Hands → Waiting with lamps → Memory verse peek → Yours is the kingdom — stand ready</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Jesus Told It</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told the ten bridesmaids, and how weddings worked in his day.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Jesus Told It → Recap → Matthew 25:2, 8–10, 12–13 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → The doors → Why did they all sleep? → Borrowed faith? → Head · Heart · Hands → The door is shut → Build the verse → Yours is the kingdom — write a lamp</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Glow-worm Walk</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story about ten bridesmaids looks in a modern school camp.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Glow-worm Walk → Forgot something → Matthew 25:8–10, 13 → Glow-worm night → What went wrong? → The Glow-worm Walk → Not at the last minute → Was it fair? → Head · Heart · Hands → Left behind on the track → Verse with actions → Yours is the kingdom — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Keep Your Lamp Lit</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the ten bridesmaids show us about God and about Jesus’ return.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Keep Your Lamp Lit → Who said it? → Matthew 25:10, 13 · 6:13 → The feast is coming → What’s coming? → Why a wedding? → This week’s big idea → What makes the difference? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Always be ready → Yours is the kingdom — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Waiting for the Groom":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Waiting for the Groom</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yours is the kingdom — This week we pray the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer: “For yours is the kingdom, the power and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen.” The CEV gives it as “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t have this line, but it is a wonderful way to finish: it says God is King, he is in charge, and he is coming. Today you don’t ask for anything; you just say the kingdom is his.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus told a story about ten bridesmaids waiting for a groom who was late.</li><li>We are learning what the five wise bridesmaids had that the five foolish ones did not.</li><li>I can explain what Jesus meant when he said, “Always be ready” (Matthew 25:13).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that all ten bridesmaids had lamps and all ten fell asleep, that five had extra oil and five did not, that the groom came at midnight, and that the five who were ready went in to the feast while the door was shut on the others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small torch or battery tea-light for the prayer</li><li>Optional: a jar or small bottle to be the ‘oil’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Waiting for the Groom</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have a small torch or tea-light, switch it on at the front of the room and leave it there all week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: What would you bring?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine you have to wait a really long time for something exciting — a late bus, a birthday party, a parent coming to pick you up in the dark. What would you want to have with you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What if the wait was much longer than you expected?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard The Three Servants — the master who trusted his servants with gifts. Your challenge: name one gift God gave you and use it for someone else, twice this week. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students what gift they used and who it helped; celebrate honest ‘I forgot’ answers too. Then the hook: snacks, a charged phone, a jacket, a book. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells a story about waiting at a wedding, and about what you need to have with you when the wait is long.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:1–13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:1</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happened one night when ten young women took their oil lamps and went to a wedding to meet the groom.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:2–4</b> Five of them were foolish and five were wise. The foolish ones took their lamps, but no extra oil. The ones who were wise took along extra oil for their lamps.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:5–6</b> The groom was late arriving, and the young women became drowsy and fell asleep. Then in the middle of the night someone shouted, “Here’s the groom! Come to meet him!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:10</b> The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the doors were closed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:13</b> So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 25:13 is our memory verse. ‘Kingdom of heaven’ = God’s rule, where he is King and everything is put right. A groom is the man getting married. Bridesmaids = young women who were the bride’s friends and helped at the wedding. Today we read a trimmed selection; the full parable is Matthew 25:1–13.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Ten bridesmaids</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus is sitting on the Mount of Olives, a hill outside Jerusalem, looking out over the city. In a few days he will be arrested, and his closest friends, the disciples, have come to ask him a big question: what will it be like when you come back? So Jesus tells them a story. Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. This one is about a wedding.</p><p>In Jesus’ day, a village wedding was the biggest party of the year. The groom would walk with his friends to the bride’s home, and then bring her back to his family’s house for a feast. But nobody knew exactly when he would arrive. The bridesmaids, young women who were friends of the bride, waited with lamps to light the way and join the procession.</p><p>In the story, ten bridesmaids are waiting outside the house as the sun goes down. They chat and laugh and compare their beautiful robes. Each one holds a little clay lamp, the kind with a tiny flame floating on olive oil. Five of them have also tucked a jar of extra oil under their arm. Jesus calls those five wise. The other five have only their lamps. Jesus calls them foolish. Not because they are bad people, but because they haven’t planned for a long wait.</p><p>The evening goes on. The stars come out. No groom. Nine o’clock. Ten. Eleven. One by one their eyes grow heavy, and all ten bridesmaids fall asleep against the wall. All ten. The wise ones too.</p><p>Then, in the middle of the night, a shout rings out: ‘Here’s the groom! Come and meet him!’</p><p>Ten young women jump up. Ten lamps need trimming. The wise bridesmaids pour in their extra oil, and their flames spring up bright and strong. But the foolish ones look down at their lamps and see the flames sputtering. ‘Quick!’ they say. ‘Let us have some of your oil! Our lamps are going out!’</p><p>And the wise ones say something surprising. ‘There’s not enough for all of us. Go and buy some for yourselves.’</p><p>So the five foolish bridesmaids hurry off into the dark to find oil. And while they are gone, the groom arrives. The five who are ready go in with him, into the light and the music and the feast. And the door is shut.</p><p>Later, the other five come running back. They knock and knock. ‘Sir, sir! Open the door for us!’ But the groom answers, ‘I don’t even know you!’</p><p>Then Jesus looks at his friends and says, ‘So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus tells a story about a wedding. · 2. Five wise. Five foolish. · 3. The groom is late. All ten fall asleep. · 4. Midnight! “Here’s the groom!” · 5. One group goes in. The door is shut.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The Mount of Olives setting is from Matthew 24:3 and 25:1; the details of the village wedding are what historians think weddings were like, and Jesus’ listeners knew them well. Handle the shut door gently: say it plainly and calmly, and tell students this is a story with a serious warning told by someone who loves the listeners and wants every one of them inside the feast. Don’t dwell on the outside of the door; tomorrow and Thursday show why Jesus tells it and where the good news is.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who fell asleep?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, how many of the ten bridesmaids fell asleep while they waited?<br>A) Only the five foolish ones &nbsp; B) All ten ✓ &nbsp; C) None of them &nbsp; D) Only the five wise ones</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, all ten (Matthew 25:5). Follow-up: ‘So did falling asleep make the foolish ones foolish?’ (No. All ten slept.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was different about the five wise ones?’ (They had brought extra oil.) If silent: re-read the scripture line about the groom being late and everyone becoming drowsy.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Foolish, but not bad</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The five foolish bridesmaids weren’t bad people. They came to the wedding and brought their lamps. Why do you think Jesus still called them “foolish”?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: they didn’t plan ahead; they only prepared for a short wait; they expected the groom to arrive on time; they assumed someone else would sort it out. Follow-up: ‘What could the five foolish ones have done on that long afternoon?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When have you been caught out because you didn’t prepare?’ If silent: ask what the wise ones did differently before the evening even began.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Ten Bridesmaids</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Ten Bridesmaids</b> (Matthew 25:1–13). Jesus’ words follow Matthew 25:1–13 (CEV), shortened. The bridesmaids’ chatter, their worry about the oil, the groom’s welcome and the sad way he answers are imagined to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Telling the story — calm and clear<br><b>Wise Bridesmaid</b> — Thoughtful — plans for a long night<br><b>Foolish Bridesmaid</b> — Cheerful — thinks it will all be fine<br><b>Groom</b> — Late, but full of joy<br><b>Everyone</b> — The other bridesmaids, and the midnight crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: yawn and slump when the bridesmaids fall asleep · Everyone: shout “Here’s the groom!” at midnight · Everyone: hold up a pretend lamp at the end<br>Props: Optional: a small jar as the oil · Optional: a torch or tea-light as a lamp<br>Imagined retelling that stays close to Matthew 25. The groom’s last line is straight from Jesus’ story (‘I don’t even know you!’); play it quietly and sadly, never scary. The Wise Bridesmaid’s firm answer comes straight from the text too. Let the midnight shout and the cheer from inside be big and joyful.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A village at dusk. Ten young women wait outside a house for the groom. Jesus tells the story.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happened one night when ten young women took their oil lamps and went to a wedding.</li><li><b>Foolish Bridesmaid:</b> I’ve got my lamp, and it’s lit. How long can a groom be? <i>(swing a pretend lamp, grinning)</i></li><li><b>Wise Bridesmaid:</b> He might be late. I’m bringing a jar of extra oil, just in case. <i>(tuck a jar under one arm)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Where is he? Is he coming yet? Can anyone see him? <i>(peer down the road)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The evening went on. The stars came out. The groom was late.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Yaaaawn… zzzzzz… <i>(all ten slump and fall asleep)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> All ten fell asleep. The wise ones too.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! All ten look exactly the same, fast asleep. What is the one big difference between them that we can’t see? (20s)</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Here’s the groom! Come to meet him! <i>(jump up and shout together)</i></li><li><b>Wise Bridesmaid:</b> Up, up, up! Trim your lamps! <i>(pour in pretend oil, flame leaps up)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Bridesmaid:</b> Oh no! My lamp is going out! Let us have some of your oil! <i>(hold out a dark lamp)</i></li><li><b>Wise Bridesmaid:</b> There’s not enough oil for all of us! Go and buy some for yourselves. <i>(sorry but firm)</i></li><li><b>Foolish Bridesmaid:</b> Buy some? Now? At midnight? Run! <i>(scamper off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> While they were gone, the groom arrived. The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the doors were closed.</li><li><b>Groom:</b> Welcome, welcome! Come in. The feast is ready! <i>(arms wide, big smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Music! Dancing! Hooray! <i>(cheer softly, like it’s coming from inside)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Later, the other five came running back.</li><li><b>Foolish Bridesmaid:</b> Sir, sir! Open the door for us! <i>(knock on the air)</i></li><li><b>Groom:</b> I don’t even know you! <i>(say it quietly and sadly, not angrily)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The five who weren’t ready stood outside in the dark, and the music played on without them.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Be ready! <i>(hold up one pretend lamp)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All ten bridesmaids had a lamp and all ten fell asleep. So what was the real difference between them?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Five had brought extra oil and five hadn’t. The difference was what they had ready, and you couldn’t see it until the shout came at midnight.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the wise bridesmaids say when the others asked for oil?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘There’s not enough oil for all of us! Go and buy some for yourselves.’ We’ll find out tomorrow why Jesus put that surprising line in the story.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fair or not?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The wise bridesmaids wouldn’t share their oil. Do you think that was fair or unkind? What would you have done?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let students disagree. Some will say unkind, they should have shared; others will say there truly wasn’t enough. Don’t settle it: tomorrow we find out why Jesus included this. Follow-up: ‘What would happen to all ten lamps if they shared?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could the foolish ones have done earlier?’ If silent: ask them to vote with thumbs, up for fair and down for unkind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus will come again, and the wise are the ones who are ready for a long wait.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want to be ready when you come.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight at bedtime, switch on a small lamp or torch for ten seconds and say one sentence to God, such as “Jesus, I’m ready for you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. A light you can switch on is a gentle reminder all week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Waiting with lamps</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ten bridesmaids. Ten lamps. One long wait.<br>Five had brought extra oil.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Matthew 25:13. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Yours is the kingdom — stand ready</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All term we’re praying the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time. This week is the ending: “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Standing is a way of being ready, like the bridesmaids waiting at the door. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up quietly and hold one hand in front of you, as if you’re holding a small lamp. Take a slow breath. We’re ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Standing ready, tell God who he is. He is King, and he’s the groom who is coming.<br>Prompts: God, you are the King, and your kingdom will never end. / Jesus, you promised you’re coming back, and you keep your promises.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are the King, and you are coming!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Still standing, think about the times you’ve been like the foolish bridesmaids and not got ready.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t bothered to get ready for you. / Sorry for leaving it to later when I should have started today.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we weren’t ready.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God that the party is for you, and that the invitation is real.<br>Prompts: Thank you that Jesus is coming back to bring joy and a feast. / Thank you for the people who help me stay close to you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you’ve invited us to your feast.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your open hand like holding a lamp. Ask God for oil that lasts.<br>Prompts: Please help me to be ready, not just tonight but every day. / Please help the people I love to be ready too.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to be ready, and keep our lamps burning.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lower your hand slowly, as if carrying a lamp carefully. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional: anyone who prefers can stay seated and still hold a hand out like a lamp. Keep the mood hopeful. Being ready is about longing for Jesus’ coming, not being frightened of it.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story — who was listening, how weddings worked in his day, and the surprising thing the wise bridesmaids said.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Jesus Told It":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus Told It</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yours is the kingdom — This week we pray the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer: “For yours is the kingdom, the power and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen.” The CEV gives it as “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t have this line, but it is a wonderful way to finish: it says God is King, he is in charge, and he is coming. Today you don’t ask for anything; you just say the kingdom is his.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told the ten bridesmaids, and how weddings worked in his day.</li><li>We are learning who is who in the story, and the surprising twist about the oil.</li><li>I can explain why being ready for Jesus can’t be borrowed from anyone else.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming the disciples as the audience, saying the groom stands for Jesus, explaining that all ten fell asleep so being ready is not about staying awake, and explaining that the wise could not share because each person needs their own faith and readiness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens or pencils</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Jesus Told It</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met ten bridesmaids waiting at a wedding for a groom who was late.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the five wise bridesmaids bring that the five foolish ones didn’t?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Extra oil for their lamps (Matthew 25:3–4). All ten fell asleep, the groom came at midnight, the ready five went into the feast, and the door was shut. ‘Today we find out who was listening when Jesus told this story, why he told it, and the twist at the heart of it.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:2, 8–10, 12–13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:2</b> Five of them were foolish and five were wise.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:8–9</b> The foolish ones said to the others, “Let us have some of your oil! Our lamps are going out.” Those who were wise answered, “There’s not enough oil for all of us! Go and buy some for yourselves.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:10</b> The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the doors were closed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:12</b> But the groom replied, “I don’t even know you!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:13</b> So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Notice who Jesus is speaking to in verse 13: ‘my disciples’. This story is for people who already follow him. ‘Disciples’ = students and followers of Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s zoom out and ask: who was Jesus talking to, and why did he tell this story?</p><p>It is the last week of Jesus’ life, though no one around him knows that yet. He is sitting on the Mount of Olives, looking out over Jerusalem. His disciples have come to him privately with a huge question: when will you come back, and how will we know? Jesus has just told them that nobody knows the day or the hour. Then he tells this parable to show them what to do while they wait. So the listeners are a small group of close friends who will have to wait a very long time, and who will need to stay faithful even when nothing seems to be happening.</p><p>Now think about what weddings were like in their world. A wedding was the happiest event in the village. The groom came to collect his bride, usually in the evening, with a noisy procession, and the whole village joined the party, which could go on for days. But nobody knew exactly when he would arrive. Bridesmaids waited with lamps to light the way through the dark streets. That is why Jesus’ listeners could picture every detail.</p><p>It also tells us who is who. The groom is Jesus himself. Jesus once called himself the groom (Matthew 9:15), and the Old Testament often pictures God as a groom who delights in his people. The bridesmaids are all of us who say we are waiting for him. The late groom is the long wait before Jesus returns. The wedding feast is the joy of God’s kingdom. The lamp is a faith that shows. And the oil? Jesus doesn’t say. Most Christians through history have understood it as real, personal trust in God, the part on the inside that keeps burning when no one is watching.</p><p>Now for the surprises. First, all ten fall asleep, even the wise ones. So being ready isn’t about never resting or worrying all night. It’s about having what you need when the shout comes.</p><p>Second, the wise bridesmaids say no. We might think that’s unkind, but it makes Jesus’ point: you can’t borrow readiness from someone else at the last minute. Your parents’ faith, your friend’s faith, even your church’s faith can’t be poured into your lamp. Each person needs their own.</p><p>Third, the door is shut. That is hard to hear. But Jesus tells it so seriously because he wants every one of those friends inside the feast. He isn’t trying to frighten them. He’s waking them up while there is still time. And then he says it plainly: ‘Always be ready!’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus is on the Mount of Olives. · 2. Weddings: the biggest party of the year. · 3. Who’s who: the groom is Jesus. · 4. Surprise one: all ten fall asleep. · 5. Surprise two: they can’t borrow oil. · 6. Surprise three: the door is shut.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Matthew 24:3 gives the disciples’ question on the Mount of Olives; 24:36 is where Jesus says nobody knows the day or hour but the Father. Jesus calling himself the groom: Matthew 9:15. The wedding customs are what historians think they were; scholars differ on small details. The meaning of the oil is not stated by Jesus: say so honestly. Sensitive: the shut door. State it plainly and calmly, then point to Jesus’ heart: he tells this story because he wants everyone inside, and Thursday shows the good news.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The groom</td><td>Jesus, who promised he will come back</td></tr><tr><td>The ten bridesmaids</td><td>Everyone who says they are waiting for Jesus</td></tr><tr><td>The late groom</td><td>The long wait before Jesus returns</td></tr><tr><td>The lamp and the oil</td><td>A faith that shows, and the real trust inside that keeps it burning</td></tr><tr><td>The wedding feast</td><td>The joy of God’s kingdom, with Jesus</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus will come back like a groom to a wedding, with joy. The question is whether we’re ready.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Be honest that Jesus doesn’t explain the oil; ‘real trust in God’ is the most common reading. Don’t let it turn into a code to crack; the heart of it is ‘be ready’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The doors</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the ________ were closed.”<br><i>Answer: “…doors.” (Matthew 25:10)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: doors. Follow-up: ‘Who went in?’ (The five who were ready.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the others say when they came back?’ (‘Sir, sir! Open the door for us!’) If silent: point to the middle scripture lines and read them aloud together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why did they all sleep?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus made all ten bridesmaids fall asleep, even the wise ones. Why do you think he told it that way?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: being ready isn’t about never resting; the wise weren’t better at staying awake, only at preparing; the waiting was long for everyone; what matters is what you have when the shout comes. Follow-up: ‘What does being ready look like if you can’t stay awake all the time?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about how kind Jesus is to tired people?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Borrowed faith?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “I’m ready for Jesus because my family goes to church.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: the wise bridesmaids couldn’t lend their oil. Your family’s faith is a wonderful gift and help, but each of us needs our own faith and our own friendship with God. Follow-up: ‘What could your own oil look like?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can your family help you without doing it for you?’ If silent: ask what kind of things you can’t borrow at the last minute, like revising for a test.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Being ready for Jesus can’t be borrowed; each person needs their own faith and their own friendship with God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want my own faith, not just my family’s or my friends’.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw a little lamp on a sticky note and write one word on it that stands for your own time with God, such as “thanks” or “help”. Stick it where you’ll see it at bedtime.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out sticky notes. This leads into Thursday’s challenge: two quiet minutes with God every day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The door is shut</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Sir, sir! Open the door for us!”<br>A serious warning, from a Jesus who wants everyone inside.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Matthew 25:13 chunk by chunk, like lighting lamps one at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Yours is the kingdom — write a lamp</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Draw a small lamp and write your prayer inside the flame. Writing slows you down so you can think about what you really want to say. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your sticky note or paper and a pen. Draw a small lamp. At the top, write: “Yours is the kingdom.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line inside the flame about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are the King, and you are coming back. / Jesus, you are the groom who gives a joyful feast.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are the King, and you’re coming back.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think about where your own oil is running low.<br>Prompts: Sorry for relying on other people’s faith instead of my own. / Sorry for the days I forget to talk to you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we forget you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thank you for the people who show you what a lit lamp looks like.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who pray for me and show me what faith looks like. / Thank you that Jesus’ invitation to the feast is for me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who help us follow Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’d like God to do in you this week.<br>Prompts: Please give me my own faith that keeps burning, day after day. / Please help me to be ready when Jesus comes.<br>Starter: <b>Please give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us faith that keeps burning.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put your lamp prayer somewhere you’ll see it tonight. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. Some may feel they aren’t ‘ready’ enough; reassure them that Jesus is kind and wants them at his feast, and that the way to keep a lamp lit is simple and small.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a school camp glow-worm walk in the bush, two torches that work, and two that don’t.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Glow-worm Walk":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Glow-worm Walk</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yours is the kingdom — This week we pray the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer: “For yours is the kingdom, the power and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen.” The CEV gives it as “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t have this line, but it is a wonderful way to finish: it says God is King, he is in charge, and he is coming. Today you don’t ask for anything; you just say the kingdom is his.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story about ten bridesmaids looks in a modern school camp.</li><li>We are learning why being ready can’t be done for you at the last minute.</li><li>I can say what keeping my own lamp lit looks like in my life.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Aroha and Jun to the wise bridesmaids and Sione and Mia to the foolish ones, and explaining that no one could get Sione and Mia ready at the last minute because readiness has to happen beforehand and for yourself.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch</li><li>Optional: a pillow for the pillow fight</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Glow-worm Walk</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is a retelling in modern Aotearoa; Jesus’ parable is the same, with new characters.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Forgot something</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever got to the start of something really exciting and realised you’d forgotten something important?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was it? Could someone else have fixed it for you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories: a sports kit, a library book, a swimming towel, a ticket. Notice if anyone could sort it out in the last minute. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned that readiness can’t be borrowed. Today it happens at a school camp.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:8–10, 13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:8</b> The foolish ones said to the others, “Let us have some of your oil! Our lamps are going out.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:9</b> Those who were wise answered, “There’s not enough oil for all of us! Go and buy some for yourselves.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:10</b> The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the doors were closed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:13</b> So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story is not in the Bible. It is Jesus’ parable retold with new characters, to help us see how it works in real life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Glow-worm night</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Wednesday night at school camp. Room 14 had been waiting for this all week: the glow-worm walk. A track through the bush in the dark, down to a gully where thousands of tiny blue-green lights shine from the banks like a sky full of stars.</p><p>At seven o’clock, Mr Walker gathered everyone in the dining hall. ‘Listen carefully,’ he said. ‘Every person carries their own working torch. Check yours before you come. We leave at half past seven sharp. The track is narrow and we go as one group, so we can’t turn back for anyone.’</p><p>Everyone heard. Everyone nodded.</p><p>Back in the cabin, Aroha sat on her bunk testing her torch. It flickered. She swapped in the spare batteries her mum had packed, clicked it on, and a strong white beam lit the ceiling. Jun was already done. ‘Tested mine at lunchtime,’ he said with a grin. ‘Fresh batteries, bro. Easy.’</p><p>On the other side of the cabin, Mia was nervous about walking in the dark, so she did what she often did when she was nervous: she got silly. She threw a pillow at Sione. Sione threw one back, and then it was a full pillow fight, feathers everywhere, both of them laughing so hard they forgot all about the clock. Their two torches lay on the floor where they had left them the night before, after a long game of torch tag.</p><p>‘Guys, check your torches!’ called Aroha.</p><p>‘Heaps of time!’ yelled Sione. ‘Sweet as!’</p><p>At twenty-five past seven, Mr Walker blew his whistle. Aroha and Jun lined up, their beams bright. Sione clicked his torch. Nothing. Mia clicked hers. A dim orange glow, then darkness.</p><p>‘Aroha! Jun! Can we borrow yours?’</p><p>Jun held his torch tight. ‘Then I’d be the one in the dark.’</p><p>‘I wish I could,’ said Aroha. ‘But Mr Walker keeps spare batteries in the office. Run!’</p><p>They sprinted across the grass. The office was locked and dark. Behind them, the group set off, a line of little lights bobbing along the track, and Sione and Mia stood at the start of it, shaking their dead torches as the lights disappeared into the ferns.</p><p>A few minutes later, Mrs Ngata, the camp cook, found them and walked them back for hot chocolate. ‘I knew the plan,’ Sione said quietly. ‘I just didn’t get ready.’ Mia nodded. ‘I thought there’d be time.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Camp night. The glow-worm walk. · 2. Aroha and Jun check their torches. · 3. Sione and Mia: pillow fight! · 4. The whistle blows. Click… nothing. · 5. The group leaves without them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mr Walker, Mrs Ngata, Aroha, Jun, Sione and Mia are imagined. Portray Sione and Mia warmly: they’re not bad kids. Mia’s silliness comes from nerves, so be gentle with her. Aroha and Jun are kind; they can’t share because there is only one working torch each and they can’t walk the track for someone else. Make sure students know that Sione and Mia are never in danger: Mrs Ngata is nearby and takes care of them. Glow-worms are a real NZ treasure (Arachnocampa luminosa), found in bush and caves.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What went wrong?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why didn’t Sione’s and Mia’s torches work at half past seven?<br>A) They left them at home &nbsp; B) They hadn’t checked them, and the batteries were flat ✓ &nbsp; C) Mr Walker took them away &nbsp; D) They dropped them in a stream</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B. Their torches had been left on the floor after torch tag and they didn’t check them. Follow-up: ‘What did Aroha and Jun do differently?’ (They checked their torches and Aroha packed spare batteries.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Mr Walker tell the class to do?’ (Check your torch before you come.) If silent: reread the start of the story together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Glow-worm Walk</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Glow-worm Walk</b> (Matthew 25:1–13 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (being ready, and readiness that can’t be borrowed) comes from Matthew 25:1–13.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mr Walker</b> — The teacher — calm, clear and a bit funny<br><b>Aroha</b> — Prepared and kind — packs spare batteries<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky and quick — tested his torch at lunch<br><b>Sione</b> — Big-hearted, impulsive — ‘plenty of time!’<br><b>Mia</b> — Nervous, goes silly when she’s worried<br><b>Everyone</b> — The class: pillow fight, the rush, the glow-worms</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: whisper-roar “Fight! Pillows!” and then freeze · Everyone: give a long, soft “Ooooh!” when the glow-worms appear<br>Props: Optional: a pillow · Optional: a torch for Aroha and Jun<br>Imagined retelling. Sione and Mia should be funny and likeable, not foolish; Aroha and Jun are kind, not smug. Mia’s silliness is nerves: play her gently. The story ends warmly: no one is in danger, Mrs Ngata takes care of them, and Aroha offers help for next time. Keep the pillow fight quick and quiet, and stop it fast when Mr Walker speaks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>School camp, Wednesday night. In the dining hall, Mr Walker is explaining the glow-worm walk.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Every person carries their own working torch. Check yours before you come. We leave at half past seven sharp.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> The track is narrow and we go as one group. We can’t turn back for anyone.</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Jun, let’s test ours. Mum packed me spare batteries, just in case. <i>(click torch on)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Mine’s already done. I tested it at lunch. Fresh batteries, bro. <i>(flash the torch proudly)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> It’s so dark out there. What if I get scared? Let’s do something silly instead. <i>(push glasses up, grab a pillow)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Pillow fight! Prepare to be flattened! <i>(swing a pillow)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Fight! Fight! Pillows! Feathers! <i>(whisper-roar, then freeze)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Guys, check your torches!</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Heaps of time! Sweet as! <i>(wave her off)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At twenty-five past seven, the whistle blew.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Two minutes! Torches on, please. Line up at the start of the track.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Mine’s not working. Mia, is yours? <i>(click, click, nothing)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Just a little orange glow… and now nothing! Aroha, Jun, can we borrow yours? <i>(shake the torch)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> If I give you mine, then I’m the one in the dark. Sorry! <i>(hold torch tight)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I wish I could. Mr Walker keeps spare batteries in the office. Run!</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Run! Run! Run! <i>(pat your thighs fast)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The group leaves in one minute. What can Aroha and Jun not do for Sione and Mia right now, even though they care about them? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The office was locked and dark. The line of torch beams set off down the track without them.</li><li><b>Mia:</b> They’re going! Wait! Our torches are dead! <i>(shake the dead torch)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> I heard every word he said. I just didn’t get ready. <i>(shoulders drop)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooooh! <i>(a long soft gasp, far away: the glow-worms)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Next time, I’m checking your batteries myself. Deal?</li><li><b>Mia:</b> Deal. I’ll even check them at lunchtime. <i>(small smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Be ready! <i>(hold up one pretend torch)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How were Sione and Mia like the foolish bridesmaids, and Aroha and Jun like the wise ones?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Both pairs heard the same instructions. Aroha and Jun got ready ahead of time; Sione and Mia left it too late. When the moment came, no one could get them ready.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Aroha say when Sione and Mia asked to borrow her torch?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She said she wished she could, and told them where the spare batteries were. She wasn’t mean: she couldn’t get them ready for them, and she didn’t gloat afterwards.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not at the last minute</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Aroha and Jun couldn’t get Sione and Mia ready at the last minute. What is something you can’t get ready at the last minute, or borrow from someone else?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: revising for a test; training for sport; saying sorry in a friendship; learning a speech; trust between friends; a friendship with God. Follow-up: ‘What would getting ready early have looked like for Sione and Mia?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which of these is like having your own faith in Jesus?’ If silent: ask what Sione and Mia could have done at lunchtime.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Was it fair?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Mr Walker said the group couldn’t turn back for anyone. Was that fair? Why might he have such a firm rule?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: the track is narrow and dark so everyone has to stay together; the rule was clear and everyone heard it; Sione and Mia still got looked after; some things have a deadline. Follow-up: ‘What did Mr Walker do to look after Sione and Mia?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How is that like Jesus warning us because he loves us?’ If silent: ask whether Aroha could have fixed it for them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Being ready for Jesus is something I do ahead of time and for myself; nobody else can do it for me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I don’t want to leave my faith until the last minute.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick a regular time for two quiet minutes with God, such as before breakfast, after brushing your teeth, or just before lights out, and try it tonight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and specific. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Left behind on the track</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I heard every word. I just didn’t get ready.”<br>A line of little lights, gone into the ferns.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Matthew 25:13: open arms, lamp up high, shrug, watch, sweep, clap.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Yours is the kingdom — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Take turns being the “torch” for each other: ask your partner what they’d like God’s help with, then pray one sentence for them. That’s another way we help each other stay ready. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Hold up one hand like a torch. Take a slow breath together. Each of you will say a few short sentences.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns saying who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are the King and your kingdom has no end. / God, you came to find us, and you’re coming back for us.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are the King, and you are coming back!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think about where you left something too late.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I thought there would be plenty of time. / Sorry for leaving my time with you until last.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for leaving things until the last minute.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the helpers who look after us when we get it wrong.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Mrs Ngata who look after us. / Thank you for friends who help us keep going.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who look after us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s lamp.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) to keep their lamp burning. / Please help us both to make time for you every day.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us all to keep our lamps burning.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a partner’s need is private or sensitive, they can pray something general such as “please help (name) with something at home”.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what the story shows about God, the wedding feast, and what keeping your own lamp lit really looks like.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Keep Your Lamp Lit":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 25:1–13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Keep Your Lamp Lit</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Yours is the kingdom — This week we pray the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer: “For yours is the kingdom, the power and the glory, for ever and ever. Amen.” The CEV gives it as “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” Some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t have this line, but it is a wonderful way to finish: it says God is King, he is in charge, and he is coming. Today you don’t ask for anything; you just say the kingdom is his.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the ten bridesmaids show us about God and about Jesus’ return.</li><li>We are learning that being ready means keeping our own lamp lit, a little every day.</li><li>I can choose a way to spend two quiet minutes with God each day this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable, say that Jesus will come again like a groom to a joyful wedding feast, explain that being ready means keeping your own faith alive, and recite Matthew 25:13.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky-note lamps from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Keep Your Lamp Lit</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Let us have some of your oil! Our lamps are going out.” — <b>The foolish bridesmaids</b><br>“There’s not enough oil for all of us! Go and buy some for yourselves.” — <b>The wise bridesmaids</b><br>“I don’t even know you!” — <b>The groom</b><br>“Tested mine at lunchtime. Fresh batteries, bro.” — <b>Jun</b><br>“Heaps of time! Sweet as!” — <b>Sione</b><br>“So, my disciples, always be ready!” — <b>Jesus</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Was that from Monday’s parable or Wednesday’s glow-worm walk?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 25:10, 13 · 6:13</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:10</b> The five who were ready went into the wedding, and the doors were closed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 25:13</b> So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 6:13 (the ending in some copies)</b> The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The last line is the ending that many churches add to the Lord’s Prayer; it appears in a note in the CEV because some of the oldest copies of Matthew don’t include it. It is the line we pray this week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The feast is coming</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does the story of the ten bridesmaids show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, Jesus is coming back. He promised his friends that he would, and the angels repeated it when he went up to heaven. The groom hasn’t forgotten. Nobody knows when, so Jesus doesn’t ask us to guess. He asks us to be ready.</p><p>Second, it will be a party. A wedding feast, with music and dancing, long tables of food and every lamp blazing. When the Bible’s last book describes the day Jesus returns, it uses the same picture: a great wedding supper. So being ready isn’t about being scared. It’s about not wanting to miss the best party there will ever be. And here is the good news at the centre of it all: Jesus already did the hard part. He came the first time, died for us and rose again, so the invitation could go to everyone. He isn’t looking for reasons to keep people out. He’s the one who sent the invitations.</p><p>Third, being ready means keeping your own lamp lit. Not one big blaze once a year at Christmas or camp, but a little, often. Think of Jun after the glow-worm walk. Back at home, he knelt beside his bed with his small lamp glowing. Two minutes. He said thanks for the glow-worms. He told God honestly that he felt bad for Sione and Mia. And he read one verse. That’s oil. Day after day, it adds up. No one can do it for you, but you’re never alone doing it, because Jesus is the one who meets you in it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus is coming back. · 2. It’s a party, not a punishment. · 3. Be ready: keep your lamp lit. · 4. Jun’s two minutes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. The angels’ promise is in Acts 1:11; the wedding supper picture is in Revelation 19:9; both are paraphrased. Jun’s prayer is imagined, but it matches this week’s challenge. Keep the tone warm and hopeful: Jesus’ return is good news for his friends.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What’s coming?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, what kind of event were the bridesmaids waiting for?<br>A) A funeral &nbsp; B) A wedding feast ✓ &nbsp; C) A sports day &nbsp; D) A market day</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, a wedding feast (Matthew 25:1, 10). Follow-up: ‘Is a wedding a sad event or a happy one?’ (Happy!) Follow-up 2: ‘What does that tell you about Jesus’ coming?’ (It’s a joyful celebration.) If silent: point to the first scripture line about the wedding.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why a wedding?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus uses a wedding to describe his return. What does that tell us about what he is like, and how he feels about us?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he is joyful and generous; he wants a party with us, not distance; he loves us the way a groom loves his bride; his coming is good news. Follow-up: ‘What does a groom want to see when he arrives?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What kind of King throws a wedding feast?’ If silent: ask what a wedding feast is like, with food, music and people you love.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus will come back like a groom to a wedding, and it will be a joyful feast.</li><li>Nobody knows the day, so we are always ready by keeping our own faith alive: a little, every day.</li><li>Jesus did the hard part already; he came, died and rose, and he sends the invitation to everyone.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus will come again, and it will be a joyful wedding feast — so be ready by keeping your own faith alive, because nobody else can be ready for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What makes the difference?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus has already invited everyone to the feast. So what makes the difference between the five who went in and the five who didn’t?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the five who went in had prepared and were still looking out for the groom; the others weren’t ready when he came; the invitation is for all, but each person has to be ready for themselves. Follow-up: ‘What does being ready look like for you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who helps you keep your lamp lit?’ If silent: ask what the wise ones had in their jars.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your own lamp lit. Spend two quiet minutes with God every day this week: pray, read a verse, or just say thanks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Help students choose a time and a place: before breakfast, on the way to school, after brushing teeth, or at bedtime. Use the sticky-note lamps from Tuesday as a reminder. Ideas for a verse: Matthew 25:13, Luke 8:15 or last week’s verse. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“So, my disciples, always be ready! You don’t know the day or the time when all this will happen.” — Matthew 25:13 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Matthew 25:13. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Always be ready</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be ready!<br>The groom is coming, and the feast is for you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Yours is the kingdom — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the whole ending of the Lord’s Prayer out loud together: “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever. Amen.” It’s like cheering for God. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing about God’s kingdom. Think of the feast.<br>Prompts: God, you are the King, and your kingdom will never end! / Jesus, you are the groom who is coming back!<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours forever!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, tell God where your lamp has run low.<br>Prompts: Sorry for leaving my time with you until last. / Thank you that you’re kind and you help us start again.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you help us start again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something to thank God for about the feast.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the invitation to your feast. / Thank you that Jesus came, died and rose again for us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for inviting us to your feast.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your own lamp, and for someone you’d love to see at the feast.<br>Prompts: Please help me keep two quiet minutes with you every day this week. / Please help (name) to be ready for you too.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to be ready, and keep our lamps burning.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say the ending together once more: “The kingdom, the power, and the glory are yours forever.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers. If students want to pray for someone by name, let them do it quietly.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Kingdom Is Worth Everything — a tiny seed, a pinch of yeast, a treasure in a field and a priceless pearl.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9: The Kingdom Is Worth Everything":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:31–33, 44–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Treasure</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The whole prayer, in my own words — For nine weeks we have prayed the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time (Matthew 6:9–13, CEV). This week we put the lines together and then say each one in our own words. Jesus gave us the prayer as a pattern, not just a poem to recite: every line is a way to talk to God honestly, in your own voice.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Seed and the Yeast</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus used a tiny mustard seed and a pinch of yeast to describe God’s kingdom.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Seed and the Yeast → Small things, big things → Matthew 13:31–33 → A seed and a pinch of yeast → What did the seed become? → Why so small? → The Seed and the Yeast → Trusting what you can’t see → Head · Heart · Hands → A seed on a fingertip → Memory verse peek → The Lord’s Prayer, line by line</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Hidden Treasure</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who first heard Jesus tell the story of the hidden treasure.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Hidden Treasure → Recap → Matthew 13:44 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → Happy! → Why so happy? → Do I have to give it all away? → Head · Heart · Hands → Everything must go → Build the verse → The Lord’s Prayer — silent treasure</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Garage Sale</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the pearl merchant’s story looks in a modern Kiwi driveway.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Garage Sale → Swap it all? → Matthew 13:45–46 → The pearl and the garage sale → What did Jun want? → The Garage Sale → Lost or gained? → Worth more than a bike → Head · Heart · Hands → Best swap ever → Verse with actions → The Lord’s Prayer — in my own words</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Worth Everything</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the four kingdom stories show us about God and about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Worth Everything → Which picture? → Matthew 13:44–46 → Small to big, hidden to found → Not a kingdom picture → Four pictures → This week’s big idea → Fun or God first? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Small becomes huge → The Lord’s Prayer — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Seed and the Yeast":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:31–33, 44–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Seed and the Yeast</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The whole prayer, in my own words — For nine weeks we have prayed the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time (Matthew 6:9–13, CEV). This week we put the lines together and then say each one in our own words. Jesus gave us the prayer as a pattern, not just a poem to recite: every line is a way to talk to God honestly, in your own voice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus used a tiny mustard seed and a pinch of yeast to describe God’s kingdom.</li><li>We are learning that God’s kingdom can start small and hidden, and still grow very big.</li><li>I can retell the mustard seed and the yeast, and say what they show about God’s kingdom.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that the mustard seed is the smallest seed but grows into a tree for the birds, that a little yeast works through all the dough, and that both stories say God’s kingdom starts small and grows big.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a few mustard seeds, poppy seeds or sesame seeds in a small dish</li><li>Optional: a pinch of dried yeast and a bowl of warm water to watch bubble</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Seed and the Yeast</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have seeds or yeast, put them where students can see them. This week has four short parables about the kingdom of heaven: two today, one tomorrow, one on Wednesday, and we put them side by side on Thursday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Small things, big things</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What is the smallest thing you can think of that grew into something huge? A seed? A spark? A rumour? An idea?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How could something so small turn into something so big?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met the Ten Bridesmaids — five who were ready with oil for their lamps, and five who weren’t. Your challenge: keep your own lamp lit with two quiet minutes with God every day: pray, read a verse, or say thanks. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students which they tried (a prayer, a verse or a thank-you) and what it was like; celebrate honest ‘I forgot’ answers too. Then the hook: take quick answers such as an acorn into an oak, a spark into a bonfire, a snowball into an avalanche. Land it: ‘Today Jesus tells two stories about how God’s kingdom starts really small and ends up really big.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:31–33</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:31</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone plants a mustard seed in a field.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:32</b> Although it is the smallest of all seeds, it grows larger than any garden plant. It becomes a tree, and birds nest in its branches.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:33</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when a woman mixes a little yeast into three batches of flour. Finally, all the dough rises.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Kingdom of heaven = Matthew’s phrase for the kingdom of God: the place and time where God is King and what he wants gets done. Yeast = a tiny living ingredient that makes dough rise. Remind students: a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A seed and a pinch of yeast</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It’s a bright morning beside the Sea of Galilee, and a huge crowd has squeezed onto the shore. Jesus is sitting in a fishing boat, telling stories. Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. Today’s stories are about the kingdom of heaven. That’s Matthew’s way of saying the kingdom of God: the place where God is King, and what God wants gets done.</p><p>Jesus’ friends are wondering what the kingdom will look like. Will it arrive with a roar, with armies and trumpets? Jesus has a different answer.</p><p>He reaches into a pouch and picks out one tiny seed. He holds it up on his fingertip, and a little kid at the front leans in to squint at it. ‘This,’ says Jesus, ‘is a mustard seed. The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone plants one in a field.’</p><p>The farmers in that crowd nod. Mustard seeds are the smallest seeds they ever sow. You could lose one in the crease of your thumb. Nobody would ever notice it. But plant it, and it grows larger than any garden plant. It becomes a tree, with branches so thick that birds come to build their nests in them. A speck becomes a home for a whole flock.</p><p>Then Jesus tells a second story, and this time the hero is a woman in her kitchen. She takes a pinch of yeast, a tiny living ingredient that makes dough puff up, and mixes it into three batches of flour. That’s a gigantic amount, enough to feed a whole street. She kneads it in, covers the bowl with a cloth, and walks away. For a long while, nothing seems to happen. But the yeast is working, quietly, from the inside. Hours later she lifts the cloth, and every last bit of the dough has risen.</p><p>Two stories. One tiny seed. One pinch of yeast. Jesus is telling his friends that God’s kingdom may start small and hidden: a carpenter from Nazareth, a handful of fishermen, a crowd on a beach. But it will not stay small. It is growing now, and one day it will be bigger than anyone could imagine.</p><p>So here’s something to think about this morning: what small thing might God be growing in you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus holds up one tiny seed. · 2. “The kingdom of heaven is like this seed.” · 3. A woman, a pinch of yeast, three batches of flour. · 4. Hours later, every bit has risen. · 5. The kingdom starts small. It won’t stay small.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mustard seeds are tiny (about a millimetre or two across) and the plant grows to a few metres: a tall garden shrub, not a giant forest tree, which is part of the humour. ‘Three batches’ is about 20 litres of flour, enough bread for a very big family or a street; ‘enough to feed a whole street’ is our friendly estimate. The crowd’s faces, the little kid and the woman’s kitchen details are imagined. Many Bible readers hear the birds in the branches as a picture of people from every nation finding a home in God’s kingdom.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did the seed become?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, what did the tiny mustard seed grow into?<br>A) A little flower &nbsp; B) A tree where birds nest ✓ &nbsp; C) A thorny weed &nbsp; D) A giant pumpkin</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, a tree where birds nest (Matthew 13:32). Follow-up: ‘What was special about the seed to start with?’ (It was the smallest of all seeds.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the woman mix into the flour?’ (A little yeast.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line slowly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why so small?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jesus chose the tiniest seed and a pinch of yeast to describe God’s kingdom?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the kingdom starts small and easy to miss; it grows without a big show; God does big things from small beginnings; we shouldn’t judge by size. Follow-up: ‘What would people have expected a kingdom to look like?’ (Armies, palaces, noise.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was small about how Jesus began?’ (A few friends, a carpenter’s son.) If silent: hold up a seed and ask what it makes them think of.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Seed and the Yeast</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Seed and the Yeast</b> (Matthew 13:31–33). Jesus’ words are from Matthew 13:31–33 (CEV). The farmer, the child, the neighbour and the baker, and everything they say, are imagined to bring the two stories to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Teaching beside the lake — calm and warm<br><b>Farmer</b> — A weathered farmer who sows the seed<br><b>Child</b> — A curious kid who asks all the questions<br><b>Baker</b> — A busy woman in her kitchen<br><b>Neighbour</b> — A villager who keeps being amazed<br><b>Everyone</b> — The growing tree, the birds and the rising dough</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: crouch small as a seed, then slowly grow tall with arms out as branches · Everyone: be the dough and rise, puffing up from a crouch to standing<br>Props: Optional: a seed or two in a dish · Optional: a tea towel to cover the ‘dough’<br>Jesus’ lines are the CEV of Matthew 13:31–33. The farmer, child, baker and neighbour are imagined. Keep it playful: the tree and the dough are the stars, and the Everyone moves are the fun part. Stop the noise quickly when Jesus speaks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Beside the Sea of Galilee. Jesus sits in a boat while the crowd waits on the shore.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone plants a mustard seed in a field.</li><li><b>Child:</b> That seed is tiny! I can hardly see it. How can that have anything to do with a kingdom? <i>(squint at the seed)</i></li><li><b>Farmer:</b> Smallest seed I ever sow. You’d lose it in your pocket. But I plant it anyway. <i>(drop the seed in the soil)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Days pass. Nothing much to see. Then one morning, a small green shoot pokes up.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Grow, grow, grow! <i>(crouch down, then slowly stand with arms stretched out like branches)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> That’s no little herb! It’s taller than the farmer. It’s a tree! <i>(look up, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Although it is the smallest of all seeds, it grows larger than any garden plant.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Tweet tweet! Flap flap! <i>(flap arms like birds landing in the branches)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> It becomes a tree, and birds nest in its branches.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! A tiny seed became a tree full of birds. What does that tell us about the way God’s kingdom grows? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then Jesus tells a second story. This one is set in a kitchen.</li><li><b>Baker:</b> Just a pinch of yeast. You can hardly see it in all this flour. <i>(mix with both hands)</i></li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Three whole batches of flour? And only a pinch of yeast? That will never be enough!</li><li><b>Baker:</b> Watch. Yeast works quietly, from the inside. Now we cover the bowl and wait. <i>(drape a cloth)</i></li><li><b>Child:</b> Nothing’s happening. Is it broken? I’m bored of waiting! <i>(flop on the floor)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Hours go by. Then the woman lifts the cloth.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Puff! Rise! Whoosh! <i>(start crouched low and puff up to standing)</i></li><li><b>Baker:</b> All of it! Every last bit of the dough has risen! <i>(hold up both floury hands)</i></li><li><b>Child:</b> Look at all those loaves! There’s enough for the whole village! <i>(jump up and down)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when a woman mixes a little yeast into three batches of flour.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Finally, all the dough rises.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Small now. Huge later! <i>(crouch tiny, then leap up with arms wide)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the farmer’s tiny seed and the baker’s pinch of yeast each turn into?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The seed became a tree where birds nest; the yeast made every bit of the dough rise. Both started very small and hidden.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did you notice about God’s kingdom in those two scenes?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: It starts small and quiet, works from the inside, and ends up much bigger than anyone expected. It cannot be stopped.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Trusting what you can’t see</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The seed and the yeast both grow quietly, where nobody can see. Is it easier to trust God when you can see him working, or when you can’t? Why?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: it’s easier when we can see results; waiting is hard; faith means trusting before we see (Term 2’s big idea); a seed grows underground before anything shows. Follow-up: ‘When have you waited for something to grow or happen?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could God be doing that we can’t see yet?’ If silent: ask what a seed is doing underground the week after it’s planted.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s kingdom starts small and hidden, and it grows into something big.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me trust you with small beginnings I can’t see growing yet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Plant one small seed of good today: do one quiet kind thing for someone at school, such as holding a door, wiping a table or sharing a pencil, and don’t tell anyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Like the yeast, the kind act works quietly from the inside. Keep it small and doable.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A seed on a fingertip</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone plants a mustard seed in a field.”<br>The smallest of all seeds…</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Matthew 13:44, which we’ll meet tomorrow in the story of the treasure. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The Lord’s Prayer, line by line</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we pray the Lord’s Prayer slowly, line by line, like a psalm. I read a line, then we pause so you can add your own words. It’s Jesus’ prayer, and it’s yours too. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Get comfortable. Close your eyes or look down. Take a slow breath. We’ll pray a line, then pause to add our own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray together: “Our Father in heaven.” Pause. Then add your own words about God.<br>Prompts: Our Father, you can turn the smallest seed into a tree full of birds. / Father, you work quietly, like yeast, and nothing can stop your kingdom growing.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Our Father in heaven, you make big things from small beginnings!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.” Pause. Tell God honestly in your own words.<br>Prompts: Sorry for giving up on something good because it looked too small to matter. / Sorry for wanting everything big and quick, and not trusting your slow, quiet work.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we gave up too soon.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Give us our food for today.” Pause. Thank God in your own words.<br>Prompts: Thank you for bread, and for the little bit of yeast that makes it rise. / Thank you for seeds that become food, and for small good things you’ve already grown in my life.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for bread and seeds and everything that grows.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Let your kingdom come and let your will be done on earth as it is done in heaven.” Pause. Ask in your own words.<br>Prompts: Please grow your kingdom in our class, even if it starts small. / Please grow something good in me today, even something tiny.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, grow…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, let your kingdom come, in our class and in our hearts.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish the way Jesus taught us: “Let your kingdom come.” Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a short own-words line after each phrase. Matthew 6:9–13 (CEV) is the source of the lines; use the lines you know well and keep your pace slow. Some students may not know the prayer yet: that’s fine, they can echo you.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the hidden treasure — who heard Jesus tell it, and the surprising thing the man does when he finds it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Hidden Treasure":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:31–33, 44–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Hidden Treasure</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The whole prayer, in my own words — For nine weeks we have prayed the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time (Matthew 6:9–13, CEV). This week we put the lines together and then say each one in our own words. Jesus gave us the prayer as a pattern, not just a poem to recite: every line is a way to talk to God honestly, in your own voice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who first heard Jesus tell the story of the hidden treasure.</li><li>We are learning the surprising things the man does, and why the kingdom is the treasure.</li><li>I can explain why the man sold everything and was happy about it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the treasure stands for God’s kingdom, that the man sold everything gladly because it was worth more, and that Jesus did not say everyone must give away all their belongings.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li><li>Optional: a small box or ‘treasure chest’</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Hidden Treasure</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you have a small box, put it on the table: students will wonder what is inside, which is a good hook for the day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard two stories: a mustard seed and a pinch of yeast.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did both stories show us about how God’s kingdom starts and how it grows?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Both start small and hidden: a tiny seed, a pinch of yeast. Both grow big: a tree full of birds, a whole batch of risen dough. ‘Today’s story is about something hidden too, but this time someone finds it, and what he does next surprised everyone.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:44</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:44</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:44</b> A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 13:44 is our memory verse. Treasure = something very valuable that is hidden. Underline the three small words ‘is happy and’: the joy is the engine of the whole story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s zoom out. Where was Jesus when he told this story, and who was listening?</p><p>Matthew chapter 13 is one long day of stories. In the morning Jesus sat in a boat beside the lake while crowds stood on the shore, and he told them about a farmer sowing seed, a mustard seed, and a pinch of yeast. Then, Matthew tells us, Jesus went into a house, and his disciples gathered round him. That is where the stories about the treasure and the pearl come next. So the first listeners were probably his closest friends: fishermen, a tax collector, ordinary working people. Many of them had already walked away from their jobs to follow him.</p><p>Now think about what they knew about treasure. In Jesus’ day there were no banks. When armies marched through the land, people buried their coins and jewels in jars in the ground. And sometimes the owner never came back to dig them up. So a farmhand could be ploughing someone else’s field when, clunk, his plough hits a buried chest.</p><p>Here is the first surprise. The man in the story doesn’t grab the treasure and run. He covers it up again. Then he goes home and sells everything. His table. His animals. His tools. Everything! His neighbours must have thought he’d lost his mind. Who sells everything for a field?</p><p>Here is the second surprise, and it’s the best one. Jesus says the man is happy. He is not sad about what he’s giving up, and he’s not gritting his teeth. He is full of joy, because he knows something nobody else knows: what’s buried in that field is worth far more than everything he owns. Selling it all isn’t a loss. It’s the best bargain of his life.</p><p>And here is the third surprise. The treasure was hidden. Like yesterday’s seed and yeast, the kingdom of heaven is easy to walk straight past. People had walked across that field for years without knowing what was underneath. Many people looked at a carpenter from Nazareth and saw nothing special. Jesus’ friends were beginning to see that standing right in front of them was the King, and with him the kingdom itself.</p><p>So what is the treasure? Jesus says it’s the kingdom of heaven: life with God, the King who loves us. Finding it is worth more than anything we could own. Jesus isn’t saying that everyone must give away every toy and phone. He’s saying that when you really see how good God is, nothing else comes close, and letting go of whatever stands in the way feels like joy, not loss.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. A day of stories by the lake. · 2. Who was listening? · 3. Clunk! Treasure in someone else’s field. · 4. Surprise one: he buries it again, then sells everything. · 5. Surprise two: he’s happy. · 6. Surprise three: it was hidden all along.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Matthew 13:36 says Jesus went into the house and his disciples came to him; the treasure and pearl parables follow in that scene (13:44–46), so ‘probably’ is honest. Burying valuables in the ground was a common custom in ancient times. Some listeners ask whether the man cheated the field’s owner; Jesus doesn’t comment on that: the point is the treasure’s value and the man’s joy. The clothing and objects (table, goat, tools) are imagined. Sensitive: some students come from families with very little. Say clearly that Jesus is not asking anyone to be poor, and that nobody is a better Christian for owning less.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The hidden treasure</td><td>God’s kingdom: life with God the King, worth more than anything</td></tr><tr><td>The man who finds it</td><td>Anyone who discovers how good God’s kingdom is</td></tr><tr><td>Selling everything</td><td>Putting God first and letting go of whatever stands in the way</td></tr><tr><td>“Is happy”</td><td>Joy: it’s a bargain, not a loss</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The kingdom is worth more than everything, and finding it makes us glad.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus doesn’t explain this parable himself, so this is our best understanding; most Bible teachers agree on treasure = the kingdom and joy = the driver. Reveal row by row. Don’t let ‘selling everything’ become a rule about toys: the heart of it is what comes first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Happy!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “A person like that is ________ and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.”<br><i>Answer: “…happy.” (Matthew 13:44)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: happy. Follow-up: ‘What did the man do after he found the treasure?’ (Buried it again, then sold everything.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he buy?’ (The field.) If silent: point to the second scripture line and ask who can spot the missing word.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why so happy?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man sold everything he owned, and Jesus says he was happy. Why do you think he wasn’t sad?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the treasure was worth far more than everything else put together; he got something better; he wasn’t losing, he was swapping; the joy of finding it. Follow-up: ‘When have you given up something small for something much better?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about how Jesus wants us to feel about God?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Do I have to give it all away?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Jesus is saying that everyone has to give away all their stuff to follow God.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: it’s a story about how valuable the kingdom is, not a rule about stuff; the man’s joy is the point; Jesus did ask one rich young man to let go of money because it was blocking him (Mark 10), so the question is what comes first for me. Follow-up: ‘What could crowd God out?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Does having things make you bad?’ (No.) If silent: ask for one thing that could take the first place in a kid’s heart.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s kingdom is a hidden treasure, worth more than everything, and finding it brings joy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, show me how good you are. Help me want you most.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On a sticky note, write the three things you love most. Draw a small star by anything that sometimes crowds out time with God, and pray about that star tonight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out sticky notes. Reassure students that loving things and people is fine: God gives good gifts. The question is only what comes first. Collect nothing: notes stay private.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Everything must go</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He sells everything!”<br>A person like that is happy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Matthew 13:44 chunk by chunk, like uncovering treasure: one chunk at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The Lord’s Prayer — silent treasure</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">You can pray silently, with no words out loud. God hears the thoughts of your heart. Today, quietly tell God what you treasure. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your eyes or look down. Imagine you’re kneeling in a field, brushing soil off a hidden treasure. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently pray: “Our Father in heaven.” Tell him what makes him more precious than anything.<br>Prompts: God, you are worth more than everything I own. / Father, you are the real treasure, hidden in plain sight.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are worth more than anything!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently pray: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.” Where have other things come first?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I wanted stuff more than I wanted you. / Sorry for the times I walked straight past your kingdom without noticing.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for putting other things first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently pray: “Give us our food for today.” Thank God for the good things he gives.<br>Prompts: Thank you for good gifts: family, friends, food, and a place to sleep. / Thank you for letting us find your kingdom, and for the joy it brings.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for your good gifts and for your kingdom.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently pray: “Let your kingdom come.” Ask God to help you put him first.<br>Prompts: Please help me want you more than anything else. / Please show someone in our class how good your kingdom is.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to put you first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open your eyes slowly. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Silent prayer can feel long: keep the pace gentle and don’t fill every silence. Encourage students to picture the treasure, not to feel guilty. Be gentle about possessions: some students have few, some have many.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the pearl merchant, and a garage sale in modern Aotearoa where Jun sells everything he owns.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Garage Sale":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:31–33, 44–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Garage Sale</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The whole prayer, in my own words — For nine weeks we have prayed the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time (Matthew 6:9–13, CEV). This week we put the lines together and then say each one in our own words. Jesus gave us the prayer as a pattern, not just a poem to recite: every line is a way to talk to God honestly, in your own voice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the pearl merchant’s story looks in a modern Kiwi driveway.</li><li>We are learning why finding something worth more than everything makes giving up the rest a joy.</li><li>I can say what is worth more than anything, and why.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Jun to the pearl merchant, explaining that Jun was happy to sell everything because he’d found something worth more, and saying that God’s kingdom is worth even more than the best thing you can buy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a few ‘for sale’ items on a table</li><li>Optional: pretend coins</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Garage Sale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story starts with Jesus’ parable of the pearl merchant, and then is retold in modern Aotearoa with new characters.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Swap it all?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine you’ve been hunting for one special thing for years, and today you find it. Would you swap everything else you own for it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would that one thing have to be?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three answers: a pet, a bike, a place in a team, a trip. Recap: ‘Yesterday a man found treasure in a field and happily sold everything. Today another story has the same idea, with a shop owner and a pearl, and then we see it in a New Zealand street.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:45–46</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:45</b> The kingdom of heaven is also like what happens when a shop owner is looking for fine pearls.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:46</b> After finding a very valuable one, the owner goes and sells everything in order to buy that pearl.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A pearl is a smooth, shiny gem that grows inside an oyster; in Jesus’ day the best ones were extremely valuable. Today’s retelling is not in the Bible: it’s Jesus’ parable in a New Zealand street, with new characters.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The pearl and the garage sale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story begins with another story from Jesus. Picture a crowded, noisy market under striped awnings. There’s a merchant, someone who buys and sells for a living, and he deals in pearls. A pearl is a small, shining treasure that grows inside an oyster deep in the sea. This merchant has spent years hunting through every market for the finest pearl in the world.</p><p>One day a trader unrolls a cloth, and there it is. One perfect pearl. The light catches it, and the merchant’s breath stops. He knows at once that this is what he’s been searching for. So he hurries off and sells everything he has, and then he comes back and buys that one pearl. Jesus says, ‘The kingdom of heaven is also like what happens when a shop owner is looking for fine pearls. After finding a very valuable one, the owner goes and sells everything in order to buy that pearl.’</p><p>Now let’s bring that story to Aotearoa. It’s Saturday morning on a quiet suburban street, and there’s a garage sale on Jun’s driveway. And what’s for sale? Everything Jun owns: his skateboard, his comics, his games, and about two thousand trading cards. He’s been collecting for years.</p><p>Sione and Mia stop at the letterbox with their jaws on the ground. ‘Jun, you said you’d never sell your cards!’ says Mia.</p><p>‘That was before I found it,’ grins Jun. For two years he has been hunting for the perfect dirt-jump bike. Yesterday he found it, second-hand at the bike shop, and the price is exactly everything on that table.</p><p>Sione doesn’t get it. ‘You’re giving it all away? Your skateboard? Your shiny cards? Bro, are you feeling okay?’ Mia has stopped drawing in her sketchbook to stare. But Jun is whistling. Every time he hands over a game or a card, he grins even wider. By lunchtime the table is empty.</p><p>Half an hour later, Jun rolls back up the street on the new bike. ‘Best swap ever,’ he says.</p><p>Was it hard to let go? Jun shrugs. ‘Not even a bit. I just kept thinking about the bike.’ When you find something worth more than everything, giving up the rest isn’t sad. It’s the happiest deal you’ll ever make.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A merchant hunts for pearls. · 2. One perfect pearl. He sells everything. · 3. Now: a Saturday garage sale. · 4. “That was before I found it.” · 5. Best swap ever.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Pearl-diving was dangerous and pearls were costly; we only say the pearl was rare and valuable. Jun, Sione, Mia, the bike and the price are imagined. Be clear: Jun’s bike is an echo of the merchant’s joy, not the kingdom itself. The pearl in Jesus’ story stands for God’s kingdom, which is worth more than any bike. Portray Jun warmly: cheeky, generous with his stuff, a bit of a showman.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Jun want?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Jun find that made him sell everything he owned?<br>A) A new phone &nbsp; B) A dirt-jump bike ✓ &nbsp; C) A rugby ball &nbsp; D) A trampoline</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, a second-hand dirt-jump bike. Follow-up: ‘How long had Jun been looking for it?’ (Two years.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the pearl merchant do when he found his pearl?’ (Sold everything and bought it.) If silent: re-read the story beat ‘That was before I found it’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Garage Sale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Garage Sale</b> (Matthew 13:45–46 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (finding something worth more than everything, and being glad to give up the rest) comes from Matthew 13:45–46.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jun</b> — Cheeky biker who sells everything — happy about it<br><b>Sione</b> — Big-hearted and a bit baffled<br><b>Mia</b> — An artist who notices everything<br><b>Aroha</b> — A kind, confident friend who buys the skateboard<br><b>Everyone</b> — The bargain-hunters on the street</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the bargain-hunters and call “Bargain! Bargain!” · Everyone: shake pretend coins and say “Ka-ching!”<br>Props: Optional: a few items on a table · Optional: pretend coins<br>Imagined retelling. Jun is cheeky and generous, not foolish. The bike is a picture of the merchant’s joy; it is not the kingdom itself, and Thursday makes that link. The Everyone lines are great for noise: stop it quickly when someone speaks. Sione is baffled, not mocking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Saturday morning on a quiet suburban street. Jun’s driveway is crammed with games, comics, cards and a skateboard.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Jun! Is that your whole card collection? And your skateboard? Are you moving house, bro? <i>(stare at the table)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Everything must go! Skateboard, games, comics, cards, the lot. I’ve been collecting since I was five. <i>(wave an arm like a market seller)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> But you showed me those cards last week! You said you’d never, ever sell them. <i>(push glasses up)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> That was before I found it. <i>(grin)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Found what?</li><li><b>Jun:</b> The bike. A proper dirt-jump bike, second-hand, at the bike shop. I’ve searched for two years.</li><li><b>Mia:</b> You already have a bike. Why sell everything else?</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Because it costs exactly everything on this table. I counted twice.</li><li><b>Sione:</b> But you love your skateboard! You can’t sell your skateboard! <i>(clutch heart)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> I do love it. But I’d swap the lot for that bike in a heartbeat.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Bargain! Bargain! Everything must go! <i>(hold out hands like market shoppers)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I’ll take the skateboard, ten dollars? Jun, why do you look so happy about it? <i>(hand over coins)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Because I’m not losing anything. I’m getting something so much better!</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Jun is selling everything he owns, and he’s smiling. Is he crazy, or does he know something we don’t? (20s)</li><li><b>Sione:</b> Honestly, bro, I don’t get it. All those years collecting, and now you’re giving it away?</li><li><b>Mia:</b> Last card, Jun. The shiny one. You’re sure? <i>(hold it up to the light)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Take it. Honestly? I haven’t even missed them yet.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> By lunchtime the table is empty. Jun walks to the bike shop with a bag of coins and notes clutched tight.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ka-ching! <i>(shake pretend coins)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Half an hour later Jun comes rolling back up the street, grinning from ear to ear.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Told you. Best swap ever! <i>(ring a pretend bell)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> That’s the happiest anyone has ever looked at an empty table. <i>(laugh)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Can I have a go? I’ll swap you my whole lunchbox!</li><li><b>Jun:</b> One lap. Then go find your own. This one cost me everything I had.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Worth everything! <i>(arms up like scoring a try)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How was Jun like the pearl merchant in Jesus’ story?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Both searched for something special, both found it, both sold everything to get it, and both were happy. They knew the treasure was worth more than what they gave up.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Sione and Mia think when they first saw the garage sale?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They were baffled. Why would Jun sell everything? They didn’t know yet what he had found.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Lost or gained?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jun sold everything he owned, and he was smiling. Did Jun lose something or gain something? Explain.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he gained, because the bike was worth more to him than everything else together; he gave up lots of little things for one big thing; it didn’t feel like a loss because he wanted it so much. Follow-up: ‘What did Sione and Mia not know yet?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How was Jun’s face different from someone who gives something up grudgingly?’ If silent: ask what Jun would have looked like if he was sad about it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Worth more than a bike</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jun’s bike is great, but bikes get too small, break or get stolen. Jesus says the kingdom is worth more than anything. What could be worth more than even the best thing you can buy?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: being loved by God; knowing him as Father; forgiveness; a friendship with Jesus that never ends; joy that can’t be stolen; life with God for ever. Follow-up: ‘What’s the difference between things that wear out and things that last?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where else does Jesus talk about treasure that lasts?’ (Matthew 6:19–21: treasure in heaven.) If silent: ask what lasts longer, a bike or a friendship.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The kingdom is the one thing worth more than everything, and finding it is joyful, not sad.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, help me want you more than anything I can buy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose one thing that can crowd out time with God, such as screens or a game, and today put God first: say a one-sentence prayer before you pick it up.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and positive: a one-sentence prayer before the screen. This builds toward Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Best swap ever</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“That was before I found it.”<br>“Honestly? I haven’t even missed them yet.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Matthew 13:44: crown, dig, cover, smile, open hands, wide arms.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The Lord’s Prayer — in my own words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today you write your own version of the Lord’s Prayer: one short line for each part, in your own words. Jesus gave us the pattern, and God loves to hear it in your voice. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and paper. Write “Dear God” at the top. We’ll write one line for each part, in your own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is. (Our Father in heaven…)<br>Prompts: God, you are worth more than every pearl in the world. / God, you are my Father, and you are the best treasure there is.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are worth more than anything!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line of sorry. (Forgive us for doing wrong…)<br>Prompts: Sorry for loving my stuff more than you. / Sorry for the times I grabbed something good and forgot about the best thing.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for putting other things first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line of thanks. (Give us our food for today…)<br>Prompts: Thank you for the things I enjoy: my games, my friends, my sport. / Thank you that your kingdom is a treasure I can find.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for what you give us every day.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line of asking. (Your kingdom come, your will be done…)<br>Prompts: Please help me put you first in one choice today. / Please keep me from letting small things become bigger than you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us put you first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your prayer and keep it somewhere safe. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students’ prayers are private: nobody has to share. Offer a sentence frame for anyone stuck. Notice who needs help getting started and sit beside them.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: all four parables side by side, what they show about God, and this week’s challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Worth Everything":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Matthew 13:31–33, 44–46</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Worth Everything</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The whole prayer, in my own words — For nine weeks we have prayed the Lord’s Prayer one line at a time (Matthew 6:9–13, CEV). This week we put the lines together and then say each one in our own words. Jesus gave us the prayer as a pattern, not just a poem to recite: every line is a way to talk to God honestly, in your own voice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the four kingdom stories show us about God and about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning how to put God first, and how to notice small seeds of good.</li><li>I can say Matthew 13:44 and choose how I will live this week’s challenge.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can name the four parables (mustard seed, yeast, treasure, pearl), say that the kingdom starts small and is worth more than anything, and recite Matthew 13:44.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky notes from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Worth Everything</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Which picture?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which picture of the kingdom is it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It starts as the smallest seed and ends as a tree full of nesting birds.” — <b>The mustard seed</b><br>“It works quietly, hidden inside, until all the dough has risen.” — <b>The yeast</b><br>“Someone is ploughing a field and bumps into something buried.” — <b>The hidden treasure</b><br>“A shop owner searches for years, then finds one worth more than all he owns.” — <b>The pearl</b><br>“A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything.” — <b>The man with the treasure</b><br>“That was before I found it! Honestly, I haven’t even missed them.” — <b>Jun, at the garage sale</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each line; students call out which picture it is; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Which two are about growing, and which two are about finding?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 13:44–46</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:44</b> The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:45</b> The kingdom of heaven is also like what happens when a shop owner is looking for fine pearls.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 13:46</b> After finding a very valuable one, the owner goes and sells everything in order to buy that pearl.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 13:44 is our memory verse. Read it with the joy in your voice: ‘happy’ is the key word.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Small to big, hidden to found</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what do these four little stories show us about God? Let’s put them side by side.</p><p>The mustard seed and the yeast tell us that God’s kingdom starts small and hidden, but it grows, and nothing can stop it. The treasure and the pearl tell us that the kingdom is worth more than anything, and that anyone who finds it is glad.</p><p>Small to big. Hidden to found. And the one who brings the kingdom is Jesus. He started small too: a baby in a stable, a carpenter in a little town. Yet he is King of the whole world. And there’s another way to hear these stories. Jesus is like the man who found the treasure and the merchant who found the pearl, because he gave up everything to get us. He left heaven’s glory and gave his life on the cross, because to him we are the treasure. And the same Jesus invites us into his kingdom.</p><p>So how do we live it? Two small things. First, put God first in one real choice a day. Maybe Jun says a one-sentence prayer before he picks up a screen. Second, notice the seeds. Aroha’s class plants tiny seeds in pots at school. Sione keeps asking, ‘Is anything even happening?’ Then a week later: a green shoot. Small good things grow quietly in God’s hands. Nobody can see a seed growing, but one day you look, and it’s a tree. And the kind word you said this morning could be one of them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Four pictures, one kingdom. · 2. The kingdom grows. · 3. The kingdom is worth everything. · 4. Jesus gave everything for us. · 5. Put God first. Notice the seeds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Reading Jesus as the one who gave everything to gain us is a long Christian tradition and fits passages like 2 Corinthians 8:9 and Philippians 2:6–8 (not quoted here); the plain meaning of the parable is about the kingdom’s worth, and we offer both gently. Keep the cross simple and reverent. Jun, Aroha and Sione are imagined; Aroha’s class planting is imagined too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not a kingdom picture</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which of these did Jesus NOT say the kingdom of heaven is like?<br>A) A mustard seed &nbsp; B) A little yeast &nbsp; C) Treasure in a field &nbsp; D) A great stone castle ✓</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: D, a great stone castle. Jesus said the kingdom is like a mustard seed, a little yeast, treasure in a field and a very valuable pearl. Follow-up: ‘Which two stories were about growing?’ (Seed and yeast.) Follow-up 2: ‘Which two were about being worth everything?’ (Treasure and pearl.) If silent: re-read the scripture lines.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Four pictures</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Jesus gave four different pictures of God’s kingdom instead of just one?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: each shows a different side: small to big, quiet and powerful, worth everything, found with joy; one picture isn’t enough for something so big; stories stick in our memory. Follow-up: ‘Which picture helps you most, and why?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What do all four say about God?’ (He is generous, he is in charge, and knowing him is worth more than anything.) If silent: ask them to pick their favourite picture and say why.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God’s kingdom starts small and hidden, like a seed and a pinch of yeast, but it grows big and nothing can stop it.</li><li>The kingdom is worth more than anything, like treasure or a pearl, and finding it brings joy.</li><li>Jesus gave everything for us, and we put God first one real choice at a time.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s kingdom starts small, grows big, and is worth more than anything — so whoever finds it is glad to put God first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Fun or God first?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If I put God first, I can’t enjoy my games, my friends or my sport.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: the man in the story was happy; God gives good gifts to enjoy (1 Timothy 6:17); putting God first puts everything else in the right place, so friends and sport become better. Follow-up: ‘What does putting God first look like before a big game?’ Follow-up 2: ‘When might it feel hard?’ If silent: ask whether Jun looked like he was missing out on fun.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put God first in one real choice each day, and notice one small “seed” of good growing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the sticky notes from Tuesday. Ideas for ‘God first’ choices: a prayer before screens or a game, choosing kindness over winning, saying sorry first, speaking up for someone. Ideas for ‘seeds’: a kind word that cheered someone, a friendship growing, a habit getting stronger. Next week’s Monday check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The kingdom of heaven is like what happens when someone finds treasure in a field and buries it again. A person like that is happy and goes and sells everything in order to buy that field.” — Matthew 13:44 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Matthew 13:44. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Small becomes huge</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Small now. Huge later.<br>The kingdom is worth everything.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The Lord’s Prayer — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we pray the whole Lord’s Prayer together, then add our own words aloud. Short and honest is perfect: you can’t pray it wrong. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence in their own words.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray together: “Our Father in heaven.” Then call out one thing about God.<br>Prompts: God, you are the treasure worth more than everything! / Father, you grow your kingdom from tiny seeds.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Our Father in heaven, you are worth more than everything!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Forgive us for doing wrong, as we forgive others.” Tell God quietly what you’re sorry for.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I put other things first. / Thank you that you forgive us and gave everything for us.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Give us our food for today.” Call out something God gave you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Jesus, who gave everything for us. / Thank you for good gifts we get to enjoy every day.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Jesus and for everything you give us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray: “Let your kingdom come and let your will be done.” Then ask for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Please help me put you first in one real choice today. / Please help me notice the small seeds of good that you’re growing.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, let your kingdom come, and help us put you first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray the whole prayer together one more time, in your own words or in Jesus’ words. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage honest, short prayers. If a student wants to say the Lord’s Prayer in full from memory, invite the class to join.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Two Men Who Prayed — a proud man and a humble man both go to pray, and only one goes home right with God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10: Two Men Who Prayed":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 18:9–14 (18:1–8 as a bridge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Merciful to the Humble</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The tax collector’s prayer — This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Pharisee and a Tax Collector</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Jesus told a story about two very different men who prayed in the temple.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Pharisee and a Tax Collector → Proud or thankful? → Luke 18:9, 11, 13–14 → Two men went to pray → Where did he stand? → Two prayers → Two Men Who Prayed → Telling the truth → Head · Heart · Hands → “God, have pity on me” → Memory verse peek → The tax collector’s prayer — silent</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Why Jesus Told It</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told this story, and why he told it.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Why Jesus Told It → Recap → Luke 18:1, 9, 14 → Who was listening? → Who’s who? → Finish the line → Flipped → Too many good deeds? → Head · Heart · Hands → Going home → Build the verse → The tax collector’s prayer — write it</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The End-of-Term Prayer Circle</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Jesus’ story of two men who prayed looks in a modern Aotearoa classroom.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The End-of-Term Prayer Circle → Listing your good points → Luke 18:13–14 → The prayer circle → What did Sione pray? → The End-of-Term Prayer Circle → What was wrong? → Put it right → Head · Heart · Hands → A quiet sorry → Verse with actions → The tax collector’s prayer — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ten Stories, One God</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the story of the two men who prayed shows us about God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Ten Stories, One God → Ten parables in order → Luke 18:13–14 → Ten stories, one God → Who went home? → Ten stories, one God → Term 3’s Big Idea → What is humble? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Humble and honoured → A term of thanks — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: A Pharisee and a Tax Collector":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 18:9–14 (18:1–8 as a bridge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Pharisee and a Tax Collector</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The tax collector’s prayer — This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus told a story about two very different men who prayed in the temple.</li><li>We are learning what the Pharisee said to God and what the tax collector said to God.</li><li>I can explain which prayer pleased God and what Jesus said about putting yourself above others (Luke 18:14).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that both men went to the temple to pray, that the Pharisee listed his good deeds and looked down on the tax collector, that the tax collector asked God for pity, and that Jesus said the tax collector went home pleasing to God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a chair or a spot at the back of the room to stand “far off” for the role-play</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Pharisee and a Tax Collector</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This is the last week of the term and the last parable. Build a sense of occasion: ten stories, and today is number ten.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Proud or thankful?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What’s the difference between being proud of something you worked hard for, and showing off?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where do you hear that difference — in the words, or the tone of voice?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard The Kingdom Is Worth Everything: the mustard seed, the yeast, the hidden treasure and the pearl. Your challenge: put God first in one real choice each day, and notice one small “seed” of good growing. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students about a choice where they put God first and a small seed they noticed. Then the hook: a try in rugby, a drawing, a swimming ribbon. Land it: ‘Today’s story is about two men who prayed, and one of them was very proud. Jesus told it to people who thought they were better than everyone else.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 18:9, 11, 13–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:9</b> Jesus told a story to some people who thought they were better than others and who looked down on everyone else.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:11</b> The Pharisee stood over by himself and prayed: “God, I thank you that I am not greedy, dishonest, or unfaithful in marriage like everyone else. And I am really glad that I am not like that tax collector.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:13</b> The tax collector stood off at a distance and did not think he was good enough even to look up toward heaven. He was so sorry for what he had done that he pounded his chest and prayed, “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> “When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Pharisee (say FARE-ih-see) = a respected religious teacher who worked hard to keep God’s laws. Tax collector = a man who collected taxes for the Roman rulers, and who was often accused of taking extra for himself. ‘Sinner’ = someone who has done wrong and knows it. Verse 14 is our memory verse; we will learn it this week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Two men went to pray</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus told this story to some people who were very sure they were better than everyone else. They looked down on other people. Maybe you have felt that way, standing next to someone and thinking, ‘Well, at least I’m not like them.’ Remember, a parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. So here is an earthly story about prayer.</p><p>Picture the temple in Jerusalem: a huge courtyard with tall stone pillars, shining in the afternoon sun. This is God’s house, the place people came to pray. Two men walk in at the same time.</p><p>The first is a Pharisee. Pharisees were respected religious teachers who worked hard to keep every one of God’s rules. Most people looked up to them. This man walks right into the middle of the courtyard, where everyone can see him. He lifts his chin, and he prays: ‘God, I thank you that I am not greedy, dishonest, or unfaithful in marriage like everyone else. And I am really glad that I am not like that tax collector. I go without eating for two days a week, and I give you one tenth of all I earn.’</p><p>Listen to that prayer. It starts with ‘thank you’, but it is really a list of his good deeds. He is not talking about God. He is talking about himself, and he glances over his shoulder to look down on someone else.</p><p>That someone is the second man, a tax collector. Tax collectors collected money for the Roman army that ruled the land, and many of them took extra for themselves. Everyone knew it, and nobody liked them. This man stays far back, beside a pillar, in the shadows. He does not think he is good enough even to look up toward heaven. He thumps his chest, because he is so sorry for what he has done. And he prays one short sentence: ‘God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.’</p><p>No list. No excuses. Just the truth, and a cry for mercy.</p><p>Then Jesus says something that made his listeners gasp: ‘When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God. If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honoured.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jesus tells a story about prayer. · 2. The Pharisee stands in the middle. · 3. The tax collector stands far back. · 4. “God, have pity on me!” · 5. Who went home pleasing to God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The Pharisee in this story is not a cartoon villain: his deeds were real and good, and Pharisees were widely respected. His problem is where he put his trust (Tuesday). Tell it with respect, not mockery. Details about the temple courts are drawn from the setting; the Bible gives only the two prayers and Jesus’ closing words. Verse 13 mentions he “pounded his chest”, a sign of deep sorrow.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did he stand?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did the tax collector stand to pray?<br>A) In the middle of the courtyard &nbsp; B) Off at a distance ✓ &nbsp; C) Right next to the Pharisee &nbsp; D) Outside the temple gates</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, off at a distance, far from the Pharisee (Luke 18:13). Follow-up: ‘What did he do with his eyes?’ (He didn’t even look up toward heaven.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he do with his hand?’ (Pounded his chest because he was so sorry.) If silent: re-read the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two prayers</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Both men were in God’s house, saying words to God. Why do you think Jesus said only one of the two prayers pleased God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the Pharisee’s prayer was really about himself; the tax collector was honest about who he was; one asked for nothing, the other asked for mercy; God looks at the heart, not the list. Follow-up: ‘Who was the Pharisee really talking to?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did the tax collector have in his hands to show God?’ (Nothing.) If silent: ask them to count how many times the Pharisee says ‘I’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Two Men Who Prayed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Two Men Who Prayed</b> (Luke 18:9–14). The two prayers and Jesus’ closing words are from Luke 18 (CEV). The bystander, the crowd’s whispering and the Pharisee’s and tax collector’s final reactions are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — The storyteller: calm and clear<br><b>Pharisee</b> — Respected, confident, very sure of himself<br><b>Tax Collector</b> — Quiet, ashamed, honest<br><b>Bystander</b> — A worshipper who whispers about both men<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd in the temple courts</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: murmur quietly as worshippers praying · Everyone: gasp together when Jesus says the surprising ending<br>Props: Optional: a pillar (a chair or a classroom post) for the tax collector to stand beside<br>Play the Pharisee with confidence, not as a monster; he sincerely believes he is doing well. Play the tax collector with quiet dignity, not comedy. The Bystander, the crowd’s whispers and the last three reactions are imagined; the Bible gives only the two prayers and Jesus’ closing words. Be careful that nobody is made to feel mocked: if a student is cast as the tax collector, thank them afterwards.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The temple courts in Jerusalem. Afternoon sun, tall stone pillars. People are coming to pray.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Two men went into the temple to pray. One was a Pharisee and the other a tax collector.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Murmur, murmur, murmur… <i>(quiet whispering, like people praying)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The Pharisee walks to the middle of the courtyard, where everyone can see him.</li><li><b>Bystander:</b> That’s the Pharisee! He goes without food two days a week. What a holy man. <i>(whisper, impressed)</i></li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> God, I thank you that I am not greedy, dishonest, or unfaithful in marriage like everyone else. <i>(chin up, arms lifted)</i></li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> And I am really glad that I am not like that tax collector. <i>(glance back, give a sniff)</i></li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> I go without eating for two days a week, and I give you one tenth of all I earn. <i>(count on fingers)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Far at the back, beside a pillar, the tax collector stands alone.</li><li><b>Bystander:</b> Ugh, a tax collector. They cheat people for the Romans. Stay away from him. <i>(whisper, step back)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Tut, tut, tut… <i>(shake heads and edge away)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> He does not think he is good enough even to look up toward heaven.</li><li><b>Tax Collector:</b> God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner. <i>(eyes down, hand pressed on chest)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Two men, two prayers. Which prayer do you think God heard, and how can you tell? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The two men went home. And then Jesus said something nobody expected.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God.</li><li><b>Bystander:</b> Wait. What? The tax collector? Not the Pharisee?! <i>(jaw drops)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> WHAT?! <i>(gasp together)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.</li><li><b>Pharisee:</b> Hmm. I need to think about that. <i>(lower arms, thoughtful)</i></li><li><b>Tax Collector:</b> Thank you, God. <i>(look up, a small smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God, have pity on us. Amen. <i>(heads bowed, quietly)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Pharisee really had done lots of good things. What was wrong with his prayer?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was telling God how great he was, not talking about God. He trusted his own list, and he looked down on someone else. The good deeds were not the problem. Where he put his trust was.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the tax collector say, and what did he do while he said it?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” He stood far off, would not look up, and pounded his chest in sorrow (Luke 18:13).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Telling the truth</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God already knows everything about us. So why is it so hard to tell him the honest truth, like the tax collector did?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: we feel ashamed; we want to look good, even to God; we worry he will be angry; it feels safer to list our good points; we don’t like admitting we got it wrong. Follow-up: ‘What do you think happens when we are honest with God?’ (He welcomes us, as with the tax collector.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who else in this term’s stories came home honestly?’ (The lost son, Week 4.) If silent: ask what it feels like to say sorry to a friend and mean it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God welcomes people who come to him honestly, not people who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want to tell you the truth about me, because you already know and you are kind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Notice one “at least I’m not like them” thought today, at school or at home, and swap it for a quiet “thank you, God, for mercy”.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small. This week’s prayer line is the tax collector’s: honest truth, a cry for mercy, then thanks.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “God, have pity on me”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.”<br>He went home pleasing to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Luke 18:14. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. This is the last verse of Term 3.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The tax collector’s prayer — silent</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Get comfortable and close your eyes, or look down at the floor like the tax collector. You don’t need fancy words. Breathe in slowly… and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about God’s house, where anyone could come to pray.<br>Prompts: God, you hear every prayer, even a whisper from the back of the room. / God, you are merciful to people who come to you honestly.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you hear us, and you are merciful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Pharisee told God how good he was. The tax collector told God the truth. Quietly tell God one true thing.<br>Prompts: God, I’m sorry for times I thought I was better than someone else. / God, I’m sorry for something I did and haven’t owned up to.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, have pity on us. We are sorry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The tax collector went home pleasing to God. Thank God that mercy is for everyone who asks.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I don’t need a list of good deeds to come to you. / Thank you that you welcome people others look down on.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you welcome everyone who comes to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray the tax collector’s prayer in your own words.<br>Prompts: Please have pity on me, God. / Please help me see myself honestly and never look down on anyone.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, have pity on me. Please help me be honest and humble.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Teach the three parts: truth, mercy, thanks. Don’t ask anyone to share what they prayed. Some students may carry guilt or shame; reassure them that God’s mercy is waiting for anyone who asks, and that humble does not mean thinking you’re rubbish.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: why Jesus told this story, who was listening, and the twist that would have shocked them — plus a story about a widow who never gave up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Why Jesus Told It":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 18:9–14 (18:1–8 as a bridge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Why Jesus Told It</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The tax collector’s prayer — This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who was listening when Jesus told this story, and why he told it.</li><li>We are learning the twist that would have shocked the crowd, and who is who in the story.</li><li>I can explain what Jesus meant by “put yourself above others” and “humble yourself”.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students saying that Jesus told the story to people who trusted in themselves and looked down on others, that the crowd expected the Pharisee to win, that the tax collector stands for anyone who asks for mercy, and that God is not like the unfair judge in the widow’s story.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Small cards or paper</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Why Jesus Told It</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday two men went to the temple to pray.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which man went home pleasing to God, and what did he pray?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The tax collector: ‘God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.’ The Pharisee listed his good deeds and thanked God that he wasn’t like other people. ‘Today we find out who was listening, what the crowd expected, and the twist that would have shocked them.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 18:1, 9, 14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:1</b> Jesus told his disciples a story about how they should keep on praying and never give up.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:9</b> Jesus told a story to some people who thought they were better than others and who looked down on everyone else.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> “When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Verse 1 introduces the widow’s story, which comes just before. ‘Humble’ means you see yourself truthfully and don’t push yourself above others. It does not mean thinking you’re useless.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who was listening?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s zoom out. Luke tells us exactly who Jesus was talking to: ‘some people who thought they were better than others and who looked down on everyone else.’ They were sure they were right with God. Being ‘righteous’ means being right with God, in good standing with him. These listeners were not bad people. They prayed, they gave, they kept God’s rules. But their trust was in themselves.</p><p>First, a bridge. Right before this story, Luke records another one about prayer. Jesus told his disciples a story about how they should keep on praying and never give up. In it, a widow keeps going to a judge who does not care about God or people. She has no money and no power, but she keeps knocking on his door. Finally the judge gives in, just to get some peace. Then Jesus says: if even a grumpy judge gives in, will God, who loves his people, ignore them? Of course not. Notice that God is not like that judge. We don’t have to nag a reluctant God. We keep praying because we trust a good Father who listens.</p><p>Now the twist. Who would the crowd have expected to go home right with God? The Pharisee, easy. In their eyes Pharisees were the champions of faith. And the tax collector? A cheat who worked for the Romans, the enemy army. The crowd had picked the winner before Jesus finished. And then Jesus flipped it.</p><p>So who’s who? The Pharisee stands for anyone who trusts in themselves: ‘I’m good enough, I don’t need mercy, I’m better than them.’ That is not only Pharisees. Some Pharisees, like Nicodemus and later Paul, came to love Jesus. The tax collector stands for anyone who knows they need God’s mercy, and asks.</p><p>The Pharisee’s problem was not his fasting or his giving; those are good things. His problem was where he put his trust: in his own list, not in God’s mercy. And he used his goodness to look down on others. The tax collector had empty hands, and God filled them.</p><p>And where does the mercy come from? From Jesus himself. Jesus welcomed tax collectors. Matthew, one of his twelve closest friends, was a tax collector. So was Zacchaeus, who climbed a tree to see Jesus and ended up putting right everything he had done wrong. Jesus said he came to look for people who are lost. And on the cross he took the punishment for our wrongs, so that when we pray ‘God, have pity on me’, mercy is real.</p><p>The road to God doesn’t run through our report card. It runs through an honest, humble ‘God, have pity on me.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. First, a bridge: a widow and a judge. · 2. God is not like that judge. · 3. Now the crowd. · 4. The twist: the tax collector goes home right with God. · 5. Empty hands, filled by mercy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. The widow’s story (Luke 18:2–8) is told in summary; the details of the door and the judge’s reaction follow Luke’s account. Matthew the tax collector is in Matthew 9:9–13; Zacchaeus is in Luke 19:1–10. Nicodemus, a Pharisee, appears in John 3 and 19:39; Paul says he was a Pharisee in Philippians 3:5. These show that Pharisees are not villains for all time. The tax collector’s word for “have pity” is linked in the Greek to the mercy that comes through sacrifice, which Christians understand to be fulfilled in Jesus’ death; keep that to one calm sentence for this age group.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus told an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. What does each part stand for?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>In the story</th><th>In real life</th></tr><tr><td>The Pharisee</td><td>Anyone who trusts in themselves and looks down on others</td></tr><tr><td>The tax collector</td><td>Anyone who knows they need mercy and asks God for it</td></tr><tr><td>The temple</td><td>God’s house, where we come to meet him; today we can pray anywhere</td></tr><tr><td>Going home pleasing to God</td><td>Being forgiven, accepted and right with God</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Pharisee trusted his own list. The tax collector trusted God’s mercy. Only one went home right with God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Say plainly: most of us are a bit of both. The Pharisee isn’t only a person from long ago. Any of us can slip into “at least I’m not like them”.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Finish the line</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish Jesus’ words: “If you put yourself above others, you will be ________.”<br><i>Answer: “…put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” (Luke 18:14)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: put down (Luke 18:14). Follow-up: ‘And what happens if you humble yourself?’ (You will be honoured.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who was Jesus talking to when he said it?’ (People who thought they were better than others, Luke 18:9.) If silent: point to the fourth scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Flipped</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The crowd expected the Pharisee to be the winner. Why do you think Jesus flipped the ending?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: to shock them awake; to show that God looks at the heart; so people who trusted themselves would see themselves in the Pharisee; to show that mercy is for anyone who asks. Follow-up: ‘How do you think the people who looked down on others felt?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does the ending tell us about God?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Too many good deeds?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “The Pharisee’s mistake was doing too many good things.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: fasting and giving are good (Luke 18:12). The mistake was trusting his own goodness instead of God’s mercy, and looking down on the tax collector. Follow-up: ‘So what should he have prayed?’ (Thank you for your mercy, and help me love others.) Follow-up 2: ‘Does God want us to stop doing good things?’ (No.) If silent: ask which two things he did wrong in his prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, and he turns away no one who comes honestly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, please keep me from looking down on people and teach me to say, “have pity on me”.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make a small prayer card with three words: Truth. Mercy. Thanks. Put it in your pencil case or on your bedside table, and use it every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hand out the cards and pens. Truth = tell God the truth about you. Mercy = ask for his pity. Thanks = say thank you. This leads into today’s prayer and into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Going home</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“…it was the tax collector… who was pleasing to God.”<br>Humble yourself and you will be honored.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Luke 18:14 chunk by chunk. After the build, ask: ‘Which part of the verse is a warning, and which part is a promise?’</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The tax collector’s prayer — write it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the tax collector’s prayer in your own words. Start with the truth, then ask for mercy, then say thank you. Writing it down can help you say it honestly. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and a small card. At the top, write: “God, have pity on me.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is, based on today’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you are not like the judge: you love to hear us. / God, you are merciful, and you lift up people who are humble.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you listen, and you are merciful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, write or think one honest thing that you’d like to say sorry for.<br>Prompts: Sorry for sometimes thinking I’m better than others. / Sorry for the things I do and then hide.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry. Have pity on us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about the mercy you’ve been shown.<br>Prompts: Thank you that Jesus welcomed tax collectors, and he welcomes me. / Thank you that you give mercy to anyone who asks.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for your mercy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’d like to keep praying about, like the widow who never gave up.<br>Prompts: Please keep teaching me to be humble. / Please help me to keep on praying and never give up.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to keep on praying.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your card where you’ll see it tonight. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. The widow’s “keep on praying” gives the Please phase its theme. If a student is unsure what to write, give them the prompt: “God, have pity on me. Thank you that you listen.”</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the end-of-term prayer circle in Room 14, where one prayer is long and one is very short.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The End-of-Term Prayer Circle":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 18:9–14 (18:1–8 as a bridge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The End-of-Term Prayer Circle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The tax collector’s prayer — This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus’ story of two men who prayed looks in a modern Aotearoa classroom.</li><li>We are learning the difference between a prayer that lists our good points and a prayer that asks for mercy.</li><li>I can say how being honest with God can lead us to put things right with people.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students linking Aroha to the Pharisee and Sione to the tax collector, noticing that Aroha’s deeds were true but her prayer looked down on others, and seeing that Sione’s honest prayer led him to put things right with Jun.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: chairs in a circle for the role-play</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The End-of-Term Prayer Circle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is a retelling in modern Aotearoa. Jesus’ parable is the same; the people are new.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Listing your good points</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever listed all your good points to make yourself look good, or to get out of trouble?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did it work? And how did you feel afterwards?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories: “but I tidied my room”, “I always do the dishes”. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned that Jesus told the story to people who trusted themselves and looked down on others, and that God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy. Today the same story happens in a Kiwi classroom.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 18:13–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:13</b> The tax collector stood off at a distance and did not think he was good enough even to look up toward heaven. He was so sorry for what he had done that he pounded his chest and prayed, “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> “When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today’s story is not in the Bible. It is Jesus’ parable retold with new people, to help us see how it works in real life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The prayer circle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It was the last Friday of Term 3, and Room 14 had pushed the desks back and pulled the chairs into a circle for the end-of-term prayer. Mr Walker had a simple rule. ‘Anyone can pray out loud, one sentence or two. Or you can just listen. No pressure.’</p><p>Aroha went first. She stood up, because she liked to stand when she prayed, and took a big breath. ‘God, thank you that I’m not like some people. I did the recycling every Tuesday this term. I got the reading badge. I helped the new kid find his way around. I gave up my lunch order for the food drive. And I’m really glad I’m not like people who forget their homework or lose their temper.’ She glanced across the circle. Jun, who had been picking at his shoelace, looked up quickly. Over by the bookshelf, Sione looked at the floor.</p><p>Everyone said ‘Amen’. Aroha sat down, looking pleased. Every word she had said was true. So what could be wrong?</p><p>By the bookshelf, Sione had gone very quiet. Usually he was the loudest kid in the room. Today his rugby ball sat untouched beside his chair. When Mr Walker nodded at him, he whispered, so softly the others had to lean in: ‘God… have mercy on me. I did something wrong, and I let someone else get the blame. I’m sorry.’ That was all.</p><p>Nobody clapped. Mr Walker just said, gently, ‘Thanks, Sione.’ And somehow it was the quietest the room had been all term.</p><p>After school, Aroha found Sione sitting alone on the classroom steps. Something had been bothering her since the circle. She sat down next to him. ‘I think my prayer was more like a trophy list,’ she said. ‘And I was looking at you when I said the bit about people who forget things.’</p><p>Sione gave a small, sad smile. ‘It’s true, though. I broke the library window with my rugby ball. Jun got detention for it, and I let him.’</p><p>A tūī chattered in the tree above them. Aroha took a breath. ‘Then you need to tell him. I’ll come with you. And I need to say sorry too, for how I prayed.’</p><p>Both of them sat there, heads down, quiet. For the first time all term, they were saying the same kind of prayer.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Last Friday of term. A prayer circle. · 2. Aroha prays first, standing tall. · 3. Sione whispers at the back. · 4. Nobody clapped. · 5. After school, on the steps.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Mr Walker, Aroha, Sione, Jun and the broken window are imagined. Portray Aroha with warmth: she really does good things and she’s learning, not a villain. Portray Sione with warmth too; his honesty is brave. The deeds Aroha lists are all good; the problem is the look-down-on-others part. If a student has been blamed for something they didn’t do, acknowledge that gently.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Sione pray?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Sione do when it was his turn to pray?<br>A) Prayed a long list of good things &nbsp; B) Whispered a short, honest prayer for mercy ✓ &nbsp; C) Passed and stayed silent &nbsp; D) Prayed only for Aroha</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, he whispered a short, honest prayer asking God for mercy. Follow-up: ‘What did he say he had done?’ (Let someone else get the blame.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Aroha pray differently?’ (She listed her good deeds and glanced at others.) If silent: re-read the story beat about Sione.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The End-of-Term Prayer Circle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The End-of-Term Prayer Circle</b> (Luke 18:9–14 (retold)). This is a modern retelling of Jesus’ parable, set in New Zealand. All the characters and their words are imagined; only the idea (a proud prayer and an honest prayer for mercy) comes from Luke 18:9–14.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Mr Walker</b> — The teacher: calm, kind, no lectures<br><b>Aroha</b> — Kind and confident; lists her good deeds<br><b>Sione</b> — Usually loud; today quiet and ashamed<br><b>Jun</b> — Quick-tempered; was blamed for the window<br><b>Mia</b> — An observant artist with a sketchbook<br><b>Everyone</b> — The class in the circle</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: say a quiet “Amen” after each prayer · Everyone: sit still in a circle, heads bowed, for the quiet moment<br>Props: Optional: chairs in a circle<br>Imagined retelling. Aroha should be warm and likeable, not a villain; the Pharisee’s problem is a trap anyone can fall into. Sione’s whispered prayer should be played quietly and sincerely, not as comedy. Jun’s temper flare is a quick moment, then he calms. There is no teacher lecture: Mr Walker only says “Thanks”. If anyone in the class has been blamed unfairly, keep the tone gentle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Room 14, last Friday of term. Chairs in a circle. Mr Walker sits in the middle.</li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Our end-of-term prayer circle. Pray one sentence or two out loud, or just listen. No pressure.</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> God, thank you that I’m not like some people. <i>(stand tall, chin up)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I did the recycling every Tuesday. I got the reading badge. I helped the new kid. I gave up my lunch for the food drive. <i>(count on fingers)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> And I’m really glad I’m not like people who forget their homework or lose their temper. <i>(glance across the circle)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> Was that about me? <i>(whisper to Mia, scowling)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> Shh. I think it was mostly about Aroha. <i>(push up glasses)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Amen. <i>(quiet, polite)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Thanks, Aroha. Sione, would you like to pray?</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Sione is by the bookshelf. His rugby ball sits untouched. He looks at his hands.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Sione has gone very quiet. What do you think is on his mind? What would you pray if you were him? (20s)</li><li><b>Sione:</b> God… have mercy on me. <i>(head down, very quiet)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> I did something wrong, and I let someone else get the blame. I’m sorry. <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Mr Walker:</b> Thanks, Sione. <i>(gently)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Nobody clapped. It was the quietest the room had been all term.</li><li class='dir'>After school. Sione is sitting alone on the classroom steps.</li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Can I sit here? <i>(carefully)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> I think my prayer was more of a trophy list. I was looking at you when I said that bit. <i>(quiet)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> It’s true, though. I broke the library window with my rugby ball. <i>(stare at the ground)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> Jun got detention for it. And I let him. <i>(shake his head)</i></li><li><b>Aroha:</b> Then you need to tell him. I’ll come with you. And I need to say sorry too, for how I prayed.</li><li><b>Jun:</b> Tell me what? <i>(wheel up on a bike, suspicious)</i></li><li><b>Sione:</b> It was me, Jun. The window. I’m really sorry I let you take the blame. <i>(stand up, look him in the eye)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> WHAT?! Bro, I missed bike club for that! <i>(temper flares)</i></li><li><b>Jun:</b> …Okay. Okay. I’m still annoyed. But thanks for telling me. That took guts. <i>(breathe out, calm down)</i></li><li><b>Mia:</b> The shortest prayer today changed the most. I’m drawing that. <i>(flip open sketchbook)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God, have mercy on us. Amen. <i>(heads bowed, quietly)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How was Aroha’s prayer like the Pharisee’s, and Sione’s like the tax collector’s?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Aroha listed her good deeds and looked down on others, as the Pharisee did. Sione came with empty hands and asked for mercy, as the tax collector did. Aroha’s deeds were true; the looking down was the problem.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Aroha do after the prayer circle?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: She sat with Sione, admitted that her prayer was like a trophy list, and said sorry for how she prayed. She also offered to go with Sione to tell Jun.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was wrong?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everything Aroha said in her prayer was true. So what was wrong with it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: she was telling God how good she was, not talking about God; she compared herself with other people; she looked down on those who forget homework or lose their temper; she didn’t ask God for anything. Follow-up: ‘What did the Pharisee say that was like Aroha’s prayer?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would a humbler version of her prayer sound like?’ If silent: ask which word Aroha used most (I).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Put it right</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sione was honest with God. Then he and Aroha knew he had to tell Jun. Why does being truly sorry to God often lead us to put things right with people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: honesty with God makes it harder to hide from people; saying sorry to God isn’t a shortcut; being forgiven makes us brave; Zacchaeus put things right after meeting Jesus. Follow-up: ‘What helped Sione to say it to Jun?’ (Aroha going with him.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who could go with you if you had to put something right?’ If silent: ask how Jun would have felt if Sione only prayed and never told him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God welcomes honest prayers from people who know they need him, and honesty with God leads to honesty with people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, show me where I have been proud, and where I need to be honest.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray your truth-mercy-thanks prayer tonight. If you’ve let someone else take the blame, or owe someone a sorry, plan to put it right this week, and ask a friend or adult to go with you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. Offer the idea of a friend or adult going along, like Aroha. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A quiet sorry</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“My prayer was more like a trophy list.”<br>Then you need to tell him. I’ll come with you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Luke 18:14: point to yourself, hand high, hand lowered, finger up, bow, look up smiling.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: The tax collector’s prayer — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Say one thing you’re thankful for, and then pray one kind sentence for your partner. Praying for someone else is the opposite of looking down on them. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Take a breath. Today the prayers are gentle and short.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you hear a whisper from the back of the room. / God, you are kind to people who tell the truth.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are merciful!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think of one time you looked down on someone, or listed your good points.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I thought I was better than someone else. / Sorry for the times I let someone else take the blame.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, have mercy on us. We are sorry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for people who help us start again.<br>Prompts: Thank you for friends like Aroha who come with us to put things right. / Thank you that you forgive honest prayers.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for friends who help us say sorry.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner, using their name.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) to be honest and brave. / Please keep us humble, and help us not to look down on anyone.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us to be honest and humble.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. Sorry is silent, so nobody has to confess out loud. If a student doesn’t want to pray aloud with a partner, they can simply say “Please help us both be honest” and leave it there.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: our term celebration! All ten parables, who told them, and what each one shows about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Ten Stories, One God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Luke 18:9–14 (18:1–8 as a bridge)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ten Stories, One God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The tax collector’s prayer — This week’s prayer is the shortest and most honest in the Bible: “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.” (Luke 18:13). A humble prayer has three parts: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thank you.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the story of the two men who prayed shows us about God.</li><li>We are learning what all ten of Jesus’ parables this term showed us about God.</li><li>I can say the tax collector’s prayer and choose how I will pray honestly this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector, name at least five of the term’s parables and what each showed about God, explain that God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, and recite Luke 18:14.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: the term’s ten “God is…” words on cards</li><li>Optional: a small treat or certificates for the term celebration</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Ten Stories, One God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The final devotion of Term 3. Celebrate!</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Ten parables in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put the term’s ten parables in order, Week 1 to Week 10. Who told them all? And what did each show about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Sower — God is Generous → The Good Samaritan — God is Merciful → The Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin — God is Seeking → The Lost Son — God is a Welcoming Father → The Two Builders — God is a Rock → The Servant Who Wouldn’t Forgive — God is Forgiving → The Three Servants — God is a Generous Master → The Ten Bridesmaids — God is Coming Again → The Kingdom Is Worth Everything — God’s kingdom is Treasure → Two Men Who Prayed — God is Merciful to the Humble</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next parable; click to place it. For each one ask: ‘Who told it?’ (Jesus, every time.) Then: ‘What did it show about God?’ Keep it quick, about 15 seconds each.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 18:13–14</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:13</b> The tax collector stood off at a distance and did not think he was good enough even to look up toward heaven. He was so sorry for what he had done that he pounded his chest and prayed, “God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> “When the two men went home, it was the tax collector and not the Pharisee who was pleasing to God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 18:14</b> If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Our memory verse, Luke 18:14, is the last line. “Honored” is CEV’s American spelling; we say “honoured”.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Ten stories, one God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It’s the last day of term, so let’s look back. Ten weeks ago Jesus began telling stories on a hillside above a lake, and we have heard ten of them. A parable is an earthly story with a heavenly meaning. Who told every one? Jesus. And every story showed us something about God.</p><p>God is generous, like the sower who throws seed everywhere. He is merciful, like the Samaritan who stopped. He is seeking, like the shepherd and the woman who searched until they found. He is a welcoming Father who runs down the road. He is a rock we can build on. He forgives a debt too big to pay. He is a generous Master who trusts us with his gifts. He is coming again, so we keep our lamps lit. His kingdom is a treasure worth everything. And in today’s story, God is merciful to the humble: to anyone who comes with empty hands and says, ‘God, have pity on me.’</p><p>Put the stories side by side and you’ll notice something. Over and over, the people who thought they had it all together, like the older brother, the man who wouldn’t forgive and the Pharisee, missed out. And the ones who knew they needed help, like the lost son and the tax collector, were welcomed home.</p><p>Why can God be so merciful? Because of Jesus. He didn’t only tell stories about a welcoming Father; he lived them. He ate with tax collectors, and one of them, Matthew, became his friend. Then he went to the cross, taking the punishment for our wrongs, so that mercy is real, not just a nice idea. When you pray the tax collector’s prayer, you are praying to a God who has already made a way.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Ten stories. One Storyteller. · 2. Each one showed us God. · 3. Mercy for the humble. · 4. Which story stuck with you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. If you have the ten “God is…” words on cards or the board, point to each as you say it. Jesus welcoming tax collectors: Matthew 9:9–13; Luke 19:1–10. Keep the cross simple and reverent: Jesus took the punishment for wrongs so that God’s mercy is real, and he rose again.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who went home?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Jesus’ story, which man went home pleasing to God?<br>A) The Pharisee &nbsp; B) The tax collector ✓ &nbsp; C) Both of them &nbsp; D) Neither of them</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, the tax collector (Luke 18:14). Follow-up: ‘What did he pray?’ (God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the Pharisee do with his prayer?’ (Listed his good deeds and looked down on the tax collector.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Ten stories, one God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one parable from this term. With your partner, say what it shows us about God, and why it matters to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God is words: Generous (Sower), Merciful (Samaritan), Seeking (Sheep and Coin), Welcoming Father (Lost Son), Rock (Builders), Forgiving (Unforgiving Servant), Generous Master (Three Servants), Coming Again (Bridesmaids), Treasure (Kingdom), Merciful to the Humble (Two Men). Follow-up: ‘Which one matters most to you right now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who told it?’ If silent: give each pair a parable to start with.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: Term 3’s Big Idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus told ten stories, earthly stories with heavenly meanings, and every one shows us what God is like.</li><li>God welcomes people who come to him honestly and humbly and ask for mercy, not people who trust in themselves.</li><li>Jesus lived every story: he seeks us, forgives us, and made mercy real on the cross.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy, not those who trust in themselves.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This recap covers the whole term. Then the Big Idea for Week 10 stamps in. The term’s big question was “What does Jesus teach about life with God?”</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What is humble?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Being humble means thinking you’re useless.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: the tax collector didn’t say he was useless; he told the truth about himself and trusted God’s mercy. Humble means seeing yourself truthfully, without pushing yourself above others. One way to say it: not thinking less of yourself, but thinking about yourself less. Follow-up: ‘Can you be good at something and still be humble?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How does saying thanks help?’ If silent: ask what Aroha learned.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one honest, humble prayer every day: tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thanks.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the prayer cards from Tuesday. The challenge carries through the holidays, so there is no Monday check-in until Term 4 begins. It is also a good moment to hand out any term certificates or treats.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If you put yourself above others, you will be put down. But if you humble yourself, you will be honored.” — Luke 18:14 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Luke 18:14. Bonus: can anyone say Luke 8:15 from Week 1, or Matthew 7:24 from Week 5?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Humble and honoured</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ten stories. One God.<br>God welcomes the humble who ask for mercy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: A term of thanks — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish the term by praying out loud together. Short and honest is perfect: truth, mercy, thanks. Anyone can call out one sentence. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together to finish Term 3. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is, from any of the ten parables.<br>Prompts: God, you are generous, merciful and seeking! / God, you are our Rock, our welcoming Father and our coming King.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 50s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are generous, merciful, and you welcome us home!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray the tax collector’s prayer together, quietly.<br>Prompts: God, have pity on me! I am such a sinner. / Thank you that you give mercy to anyone who asks.<br>Starter: <b>God, have pity on me…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, have pity on us. Thank you that you forgive.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for from this term’s stories.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for telling us stories about your Father. / Thank you that mercy is for the humble, and for everyone who asks.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for your stories and your mercy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for the holidays, and for next term as we follow Jesus.<br>Prompts: Please keep us safe and close to you these holidays. / Please help me to keep praying honestly, every day.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please keep us safe, keep us humble, and keep us close to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Luke 18:14 together as our final prayer for Term 3. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate! Consider a small treat or a certificate for memory-verse champions. Encourage short, honest prayers.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Term 4: Trusting and Following Jesus — Acts to Aotearoa, starting with Peter and John.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}},"Term 4":{"label":"Week","children":{"Week 1: Peter and John: Bold Because of the Spirit":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 1:8; 2; 3:1–10; 4:1–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Gives Power</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying boldly — Praying like the early church starts this week: boldly. Bold prayer is not loud prayer. It means talking to God honestly and confidently, because Jesus has opened the way, and asking for big things, such as the courage to speak up (Acts 4:29–31).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Wind, Fire and Many Languages</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Jesus kept his promise to send the Holy Spirit on the day of Pentecost.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Wind, Fire and Many Languages → Can’t keep it in → Acts 1:8 · 2:1–4 → The Spirit comes → What did they see? → From afraid to brave → The Day of Pentecost → Power for what? → Head · Heart · Hands → Wind and flames → Memory verse peek → Praying boldly — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Peter Preaches</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what Peter said to the crowd on the day of Pentecost.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Peter Preaches → Recap → Acts 2:36–38, 41 → Peter speaks up → Same Peter? → How many believed? → Good news to run to → Who is it for? → Head · Heart · Hands → Three thousand → Build the verse → Praying boldly — write it</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Man at the Beautiful Gate</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God used Peter and John to help a man who had never walked.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Man at the Beautiful Gate → What I do have → Acts 3:6–8 → At the Beautiful Gate → What did Peter say? → At the Beautiful Gate → A better gift → Whose power? → Head · Heart · Hands → Walking and leaping → Verse with actions → Praying boldly — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>We Cannot Stop Speaking</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened when Peter and John stood before the council.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>We Cannot Stop Speaking → Put the week in order → Acts 4:12–13, 19–20 → Before the council → What did they notice? → Nothing to say → This week’s big idea → Bold or brave? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Bold because of the Spirit → Praying boldly — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Wind, Fire and Many Languages":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 1:8; 2; 3:1–10; 4:1–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Wind, Fire and Many Languages</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying boldly — Praying like the early church starts this week: boldly. Bold prayer is not loud prayer. It means talking to God honestly and confidently, because Jesus has opened the way, and asking for big things, such as the courage to speak up (Acts 4:29–31).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Jesus kept his promise to send the Holy Spirit on the day of Pentecost.</li><li>We are learning what changed for Jesus’ followers when the Spirit came.</li><li>I can explain Jesus’ promise in Acts 1:8 in my own words.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Jesus promised the Holy Spirit would give his followers power, that the Spirit came with a sound like wind and flames of light, that people heard the good news in their own languages, and that the frightened followers were changed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a small paper fan or a sheet of paper to wave for “wind”</li><li>Optional: red and orange paper strips for “flames”</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Wind, Fire and Many Languages</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today starts a new term and a new part of the Bible: the book of Acts, which tells what Jesus’ followers did next. Welcome the class warmly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Can’t keep it in</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever been so excited about good news that you just couldn’t keep it in?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was it, and who was the first person you told?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome to Term 4! Our big question this term is “Trusting and Following Jesus: What does it look like to follow Jesus for real?” This term is about real people who followed Jesus, in the Bible, in history, and right here in Aotearoa. Last term finished with Two Men Who Prayed. Your challenge: pray one honest, humble prayer every day. Tell God the truth, ask for mercy, say thanks. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: welcome the class to Term 4, then ask two or three students how their honest, humble prayers went, and what they noticed. Then the hook: a new puppy, a goal in a game, a holiday plan. Land it: ‘Today a small group of frightened friends of Jesus get news and power they can’t keep inside.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 1:8 · 2:1–4</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 1:8</b> But when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, you will be filled with power. Then you will tell everyone about me in Jerusalem, in all Judea, in Samaria, and everywhere in the world.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:1</b> On the day of Pentecost all the Lord’s followers were together in one place.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:2</b> Suddenly there was a noise from heaven like the sound of a mighty wind! It filled the house where they were meeting.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:3</b> Then they saw what looked like fiery tongues moving in all directions, and a tongue came and settled on each of them.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:4</b> The Holy Spirit took control of everyone, and they began speaking whatever languages the Spirit let them speak.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus said Acts 1:8 just before he went back to heaven. Pentecost is an old Jewish harvest festival, fifty days after Passover, when Jerusalem filled with visitors. The Holy Spirit is God himself, living in and working through people.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The Spirit comes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus has risen from the dead. For forty days he has appeared to his friends, and now it is time for him to return to his Father in heaven. On a hillside near Jerusalem he gives them a promise, and a job. ‘The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and you will be filled with power. Then you will tell everyone about me.’ Then Jesus is taken up into the sky, and a cloud hides him from sight.</p><p>The Holy Spirit is God himself, living and working inside people. So Jesus’ friends do exactly what he said. They go back to Jerusalem, to an upstairs room, and they wait and pray together. About a hundred and twenty of them are there. Peter is there, the fisherman who said three times that he didn’t even know Jesus. John is there too. Only a few weeks ago, they were hiding behind locked doors, afraid. Now they wait for a promise.</p><p>Ten days pass. Then comes Pentecost, an old Jewish harvest festival, and Jerusalem is packed with visitors from countries all over the world.</p><p>Suddenly, a noise from heaven fills the whole house. It sounds like a mighty wind, roaring and rushing, though nothing is blowing in the street. Then they see what looks like tongues of fire, small flames of light, and one rests on each person. The Holy Spirit takes control of everyone, and they begin speaking in languages they have never learned.</p><p>They can’t keep it in! They hurry out of the house and into the street, praising God. And the crowd stops and stares. A traveller from Egypt hears his own language. Another from Parthia hears his. People from Crete, from Arabia, from Rome, all hear about the wonderful things God has done, in the language of their own home.</p><p>‘What does this mean?’ they ask one another. But some people laugh and say, ‘They are drunk!’</p><p>Who are these people? They are the same friends of Jesus who ran away on Good Friday. So what changed? Jesus kept his promise. The Holy Spirit came, and he filled them with power. Tomorrow, one man will stand up and speak to the whole crowd, and it will be the very man you least expect: Peter.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A promise, then a wait. · 2. Pentecost. Jerusalem is packed. · 3. A noise like a mighty wind. · 4. They speak languages they never learned. · 5. “What does this mean?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Documented in Acts 1–2: the promise, the wait, wind and flames of light, other languages, the crowd’s question and the mockers (2:13). The number 120 is in Acts 1:15. The places named (Egypt, Parthia, Crete, Arabia, Rome) are in Acts 2:9–11. Keep the Ascension simple: Jesus went back to his Father. Explain: the Holy Spirit is God with us and in us, not a ghost. Some students may find the idea of speaking in other languages unfamiliar; say it plainly and calmly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did they see?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What came to rest on each of Jesus’ followers at Pentecost?<br>A) Small flames of light ✓ &nbsp; B) Falling stones &nbsp; C) White doves &nbsp; D) Drops of rain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, what looked like fiery tongues, small flames of light (Acts 2:3). Follow-up: ‘What did they hear first?’ (A noise like a mighty wind.) Follow-up 2: ‘What began to happen when the Spirit filled them?’ (They spoke in other languages.) If silent: re-read Acts 2:2–4 together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: From afraid to brave</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A few weeks earlier, Jesus’ friends were hiding behind locked doors. Why do you think the Holy Spirit made such a difference to them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus’ promise gave them power they didn’t have by themselves; they knew God was with them; they weren’t alone any more; they had good news they couldn’t keep in. Follow-up: ‘What changes when you know someone is with you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who did Jesus say would give them power?’ If silent: ask when a friend beside them made something scary easier.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Day of Pentecost</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Day of Pentecost</b> (Acts 1:8; 2:1–13). Jesus’ words are Acts 1:8 (CEV). The wind, the flames, the travellers’ amazement, “What does this mean?” and the mocking “drunk” remark come from Acts 2. The conversation between Peter and John in the upper room, Peter’s shaking, and the names “Traveller from Egypt” and “Traveller from Parthia” are imagined framing. Peter denying Jesus is in the Gospels (Luke 22:54–62).</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Jesus</b> — Heard in the believers’ memory — teacher or a calm, strong reader<br><b>Peter</b> — A fisherman who let Jesus down — nervous, then bold<br><b>John</b> — Younger and steady — hopeful and loyal<br><b>Traveller from Egypt</b> — A visitor to Jerusalem — amazed<br><b>Traveller from Parthia</b> — Another visitor — curious and delighted<br><b>Local Mocker</b> — A Jerusalem resident who doesn’t get it<br><b>Everyone</b> — Believers, wind and the crowd in the street</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the wind, whoosh with your voice and sweep your arms · Everyone: flames above your head, wiggle your fingers upward<br>Props: Optional: a sheet of paper or small fan to wave for wind · Optional: red and orange paper strips as flames<br>Jesus’ two speeches are the CEV text of Acts 1:8. Wind, flames, languages, the crowd’s question and the mockers’ remark are all in Acts 2:1–13. The upper-room conversation and names of the two travellers are imagined. Let the wind be big and silly, and stop the noise quickly when the pause comes. Peter can show shaking hands, but make sure he ends the scene ready, not afraid.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>An upstairs room in Jerusalem. Jesus has gone back to heaven. About a hundred and twenty followers wait and pray.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> But when the Holy Spirit comes upon you, you will be filled with power.</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Then you will tell everyone about me in Jerusalem, in all Judea, in Samaria, and everywhere in the world.</li><li><b>John:</b> That was ten days ago, Peter. I keep hearing his voice. Do you think we’re ready?</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Ready? I said three times I didn’t know him, John. I’m not sure I’m ready for anything. <i>(look at the floor)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> He promised us power. He never breaks a promise. So we wait, and we pray. <i>(put a hand on Peter’s shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then comes the day of Pentecost. Suddenly there is a noise from heaven.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whooosh! A mighty rushing wind! <i>(sweep arms, wind noise)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> Look above your head, John! It looks like fire, but it doesn’t burn! <i>(stare up, amazed)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> The Holy Spirit! He has come, just like Jesus said! <i>(arms up, wiggle fingers like flames)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The Spirit fills them all, and they hurry into the street, speaking languages they have never learned.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Imagine you are a visitor from far away, and you hear your own language from a stranger. What would you feel? (20s)</li><li><b>Traveller from Egypt:</b> Wait! These men are all from Galilee, aren’t they? But I hear my own language! <i>(turn to the crowd)</i></li><li><b>Traveller from Parthia:</b> And I hear mine! They’re telling us about the wonderful things God has done! <i>(eyes wide)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> How can this be? <i>(turn to each other, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Traveller from Egypt:</b> What does all this mean?</li><li><b>Local Mocker:</b> It means they’ve had too much wine, that’s all! <i>(wave a hand, laugh)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> John, I’m shaking. But it’s not from fear this time. Something has changed inside me. <i>(hand on chest)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> Then let’s tell them, Peter. Whatever Jesus has done, they need to hear it.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Tomorrow, Peter will stand up and speak. But today, everyone is wondering: what does this mean?</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Filled with power! Ready to tell! <i>(stand tall, hands up)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter said, “I’m not sure I’m ready for anything.” What changed for him when the Spirit came?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He wasn’t shaking from fear any more. The Holy Spirit gave him power and courage, just as Jesus promised in Acts 1:8. God didn’t wait for Peter to be perfect first.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the travellers hear when the believers spoke?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They each heard their own language, telling about the wonderful things God has done. God wanted the good news to reach people from every place.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Power for what?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said the Spirit would give his followers power so they could tell everyone about him. Why do you think God gave power for telling, and not just for feeling strong?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: the good news is for everyone; it’s too good to keep to ourselves; God’s power is for others, not for showing off; we need his help to speak. Follow-up: ‘What kind of power was it: muscle power, or something else?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who did Jesus say should hear?’ (Everyone, everywhere.) If silent: ask what good news they’d want to tell a friend.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus keeps his promises: he gave his followers the Holy Spirit and the power to tell others about him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I don’t have to be brave on my own.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Notice one moment today when you feel nervous, and say quietly: “God, I need your help.” Then do the next small thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. We aren’t asking anyone to be loud. The hands step is a short, honest prayer asking for help.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Wind and flames</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“You will be filled with power.”<br>The Holy Spirit took control of everyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Acts 4:20. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Tell them that on Thursday we will find out who said these words and why.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying boldly — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we learn to pray like the early church. This week: praying boldly. Bold prayer is honest, confident prayer. Open hands show we are ready to receive what God promises, just as the believers waited for the Spirit. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands out in front of you, open, like the believers waiting in the upstairs room. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your open hands. God keeps his promises.<br>Prompts: God, you sent your Holy Spirit with wind and fire, just as Jesus promised. / God, you are strong enough to change frightened people into brave ones.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you keep your promises, and you send your Spirit!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn your hands over, palms down. Sorry for times we’ve hidden, like the disciples behind locked doors.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I stay quiet when I should speak up for what is right. / Sorry for forgetting that you are with me when I feel afraid.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we hide when we feel afraid.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up again. Thank God for what he has already given.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the Holy Spirit, who is with Jesus’ friends everywhere. / Thank you that Peter got a fresh start after he let Jesus down.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for your Spirit and for fresh starts.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray boldly. Ask God for something big and good.<br>Prompts: Please help me to be brave when I feel nervous this week. / Please fill me with your Spirit’s power, like Jesus promised.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us courage and fill us with your Spirit.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, like you’re holding a gift. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Explain boldly simply: bold doesn’t mean loud or pushy. It means honest and not afraid to ask. Some students may be nervous about praying aloud: silent prayers are completely fine.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Peter stands up and speaks to the crowd, and about three thousand people believe in Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Peter Preaches":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 1:8; 2; 3:1–10; 4:1–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Peter Preaches</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying boldly — Praying like the early church starts this week: boldly. Bold prayer is not loud prayer. It means talking to God honestly and confidently, because Jesus has opened the way, and asking for big things, such as the courage to speak up (Acts 4:29–31).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Peter said to the crowd on the day of Pentecost.</li><li>We are learning why about three thousand people believed in Jesus that day.</li><li>I can explain what Peter told the crowd to do (Acts 2:38).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Peter was the same man who had denied Jesus, that his message was that God raised Jesus and made him Lord and Messiah, that the crowd asked what to do, and that Peter said to turn back to God and be baptised.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Small cards or sticky notes</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Peter Preaches</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday the Holy Spirit came with wind and flames of light, and the believers spoke in languages from all over the world.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What question did the crowd ask, and who do you think will answer it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The crowd asked ‘What does this mean?’ and some people laughed. Take two or three answers. ‘Today we hear Peter’s answer, and it is the bravest speech of his life.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 2:36–38, 41</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:36</b> Everyone in Israel should then know for certain that God has made Jesus both Lord and Messiah.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:37</b> When the people heard this, they were so upset that they asked Peter and the other apostles, “What shall we do?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:38</b> Peter said, “Turn back to God! Be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ, so that your sins will be forgiven. Then you will be given the Holy Spirit.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 2:41</b> Many of them accepted Peter’s message and were baptized. That day about three thousand people were added to the group of followers.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Messiah is the Hebrew word for God’s promised King, the Chosen One (Christ is the Greek word). Baptized means dipped in water, a picture of being washed clean and starting again with Jesus. Apostles are Jesus’ twelve closest followers, whom he sent out.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Peter speaks up</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It is the morning of Pentecost, and the street outside the upstairs room is packed. People are amazed, and some are laughing at the believers. Then someone stands up. It is Peter.</p><p>Remember Peter? A few weeks ago, he said three times, ‘I don’t know him.’ Now he stands in front of thousands of people, with the eleven other apostles beside him, and his voice rings out across the street.</p><p>‘These people are not drunk,’ says Peter. ‘It’s only nine o’clock in the morning!’ A few people chuckle. Then he gets serious. What they are seeing, he says, is exactly what God promised long ago through a prophet called Joel: that one day God would pour out his Spirit on all kinds of people, young and old, men and women.</p><p>Then Peter does the bravest thing of all. He tells them about Jesus. Jesus from Nazareth did amazing miracles, and God showed everyone through those miracles that Jesus came from him. Many in the crowd had seen it with their own eyes. Peter reminds them of the old promises, too: long ago, God’s prophets said that a King, chosen by God, would come, and that death could not hold him. Peter says plainly that Jesus was put to death on a cross, but that was not the end. God raised him back to life! ‘We have all seen him,’ says Peter. ‘We are witnesses.’ A witness is someone who tells what they have seen with their own eyes.</p><p>‘So everyone should know for certain,’ says Peter, ‘that God has made Jesus both Lord and Messiah.’ Messiah means God’s promised King, the Chosen One, and Lord means he is in charge of everything.</p><p>The crowd goes very quiet. Peter’s words have gone deep into their hearts. ‘What should we do?’ they ask.</p><p>And listen to Peter’s answer. It isn’t ‘Too late!’ It’s good news. ‘Turn back to God! Be baptised in the name of Jesus, so that your sins will be forgiven. Then you will be given the Holy Spirit.’ Baptised means being dipped or washed in water, a picture of being made clean and starting again. And the promise, says Peter, is for you, for your children, and for everyone far away. That means us, too.</p><p>That day, about three thousand people believed and were baptised. A little group of a hundred and twenty became a church of thousands in a single day. They gathered to learn, to share meals, and to pray together, and no one who believed was left on their own. And the difference was not Peter’s courage. It was Jesus, and the Holy Spirit, working through an ordinary fisherman.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Peter stands up. · 2. “These people are not drunk.” · 3. The big news about Jesus. · 4. “What should we do?” · 5. About three thousand believe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Documented in Acts 2:14–41: the nine o’clock remark, Joel’s promise, Jesus’ miracles, the cross and resurrection, “Lord and Messiah”, the crowd’s question, Peter’s answer, about three thousand baptised. Handle the cross gently and plainly. Peter, Jesus and every early believer were Jewish, and Peter is speaking to fellow Jews in Jerusalem; this must never become blaming any people today. Peter himself had let Jesus down, and Jesus forgave him. Be sensitive to students from other faith backgrounds: this is what Peter said that day, and we are learning what Christians believe.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Same Peter?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Compare Peter on Good Friday with Peter at Pentecost. What changed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Good Friday Peter</th><th>Pentecost Peter</th></tr><tr><td>Hid in the shadows</td><td>Stood up in front of thousands</td></tr><tr><td>Said, “I don’t know him”</td><td>Said, “God has made Jesus both Lord and Messiah”</td></tr><tr><td>Afraid of what people would say</td><td>Spoke out clearly and kindly</td></tr><tr><td>On his own and ashamed</td><td>Filled with the Holy Spirit, forgiven and free</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Same Peter. What changed wasn’t his courage: it was that Jesus forgave him and the Holy Spirit filled him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Peter’s denial is in the Gospels (Luke 22:54–62), and Jesus’ forgiveness of Peter is in John 21. After the last row ask: ‘Where did Peter’s boldness come from?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How many believed?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “That day about ________ people were added to the group of followers.”<br><i>Answer: “…three thousand.” (Acts 2:41)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: three thousand. Follow-up: ‘What did Peter tell the crowd to do?’ (Turn back to God and be baptised.) Follow-up 2: ‘What would they be given?’ (Forgiveness of sins and the Holy Spirit.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Good news to run to</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The crowd asked, “What shall we do?” Why do you think Peter’s answer, “Turn back to God, and you will be forgiven,” was such good news?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it wasn’t too late; they could start again; forgiveness is free; God wanted them back; Jesus had died and risen for them. Follow-up: ‘How does it feel to be forgiven?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about how God sees people?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is it for?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter said God’s promise was for the crowd, for their children, and for everyone far away. Who do you think “everyone far away” includes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: people in other countries, and people living long after them, which includes us; no one is too far from God; God’s promise isn’t only for one group. Follow-up: ‘How far away are we from Jerusalem?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about God’s welcome?’ If silent: ask them to look at a map of the world and name a faraway place.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter told the crowd about Jesus, and anyone who turns back to God is forgiven and welcomed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you that it’s never too late to start again with you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of one thing Jesus has done: in a Bible story, for someone you know, or for you. Write it in one sentence on a card, and keep it for Thursday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hand out cards. Help students find a “Jesus has done…” sentence: forgave sins, healed people, calmed storms, rose from the dead, answered a prayer, brought comfort. Some students may not have a story of their own yet. Bible stories are perfect. This card leads into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Three thousand</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Turn back to God!”<br>About three thousand people were added to the group of followers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Acts 4:20 chunk by chunk. Remind them: Peter said something like this very soon, to the very leaders who had put Jesus on trial.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying boldly — write it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a bold prayer on your card or in your book. Bold prayers say true things and ask for big things, just like Peter’s speech. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top, write: “God, I want to speak to you boldly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you raised Jesus from the dead, and you are stronger than anything. / God, you forgive, and you welcome everyone who turns back to you.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you raised Jesus to life, and you welcome everyone.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where do you need to turn back to God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’ve turned away from you. / Sorry for the times I let you down, like Peter, and thank you for forgiving.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for turning away, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write three things Jesus has done that you’re thankful for.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for dying and rising again so my sins can be forgiven. / Thank you for the Holy Spirit, who gives courage to your followers.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for forgiveness and a fresh start.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one bold request: for yourself, and for one person you know.<br>Prompts: Please help me to be brave about what I’ve seen of Jesus. / Please let (a friend’s name) hear good news about Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>Please give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us courage, and let our friends hear good news.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your page and keep it in your pocket. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. If a student can’t think of a friend to pray for, they can pray for the whole class.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Peter and John go to the temple and meet a man who has never walked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: The Man at the Beautiful Gate":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 1:8; 2; 3:1–10; 4:1–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Man at the Beautiful Gate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying boldly — Praying like the early church starts this week: boldly. Bold prayer is not loud prayer. It means talking to God honestly and confidently, because Jesus has opened the way, and asking for big things, such as the courage to speak up (Acts 4:29–31).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God used Peter and John to help a man who had never walked.</li><li>We are learning why Peter said “In the name of Jesus” and what that shows us about where the power comes from.</li><li>I can say what Peter and John gave the man, and why it was better than money.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the man asked for money but received healing, that Peter and John had no silver or gold, and that the power came from Jesus’ name and not from the apostles themselves.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a woven mat or a towel to be the man’s mat</li><li>Optional: a small bowl</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Man at the Beautiful Gate</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: What I do have</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine a friend is in trouble and you don’t have any money or a fancy gift to give them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What could you still give them? What do you have?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three ideas: time, a listening ear, a kind word, a prayer, an invitation to join in. Recap: ‘Yesterday Peter preached and three thousand believed. Today Peter and John meet one man who needs something money can’t buy.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 3:6–8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 3:6</b> Peter said, “I don’t have any silver or gold! But I’ll give you what I do have. In the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, get up and walk.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 3:7</b> Peter then took him by the right hand and helped him up. At once the man’s feet and ankles became strong.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 3:8</b> He jumped up, stood on his feet, and started walking. He went with Peter and John into the temple, walking and jumping and praising God.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The temple was the place where Jewish people went to pray; the early believers still went there. “In the name of” means with the power and authority of that person.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: At the Beautiful Gate</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After Pentecost, the believers couldn’t stop meeting together. They shared their food, they prayed, they learned from the apostles, and every day more people joined them. They still went to the temple too, because they were Jewish, and the temple was where Jewish people went to pray.</p><p>One afternoon, around three o’clock, Peter and John are walking up to the temple for the afternoon prayer time. Crowds are streaming in. And there, beside one of the grandest gates, the one called the Beautiful Gate, sits a man on a mat.</p><p>Every morning, some friends carry this man to the gate and put him down, because he has never been able to walk. He has been unable to walk since the day he was born. All day long he holds out a bowl and asks everyone who passes for a few coins. Hundreds of people hurry by. Many don’t even look down.</p><p>Peter and John come to the gate. The man holds out his bowl, as he always does. And Peter stops. He looks straight at him. John does too. ‘Look at us,’ says Peter.</p><p>The man looks up, hopeful. He is expecting money.</p><p>But Peter says, ‘I don’t have any silver or gold! But I’ll give you what I do have. In the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, get up and walk!’</p><p>Then Peter takes the man’s right hand and helps him up. And at that very moment, the man’s feet and ankles become strong! He jumps to his feet. He takes a step. Then another! He is standing! He is walking! Then he is leaping, right through the gate and into the temple, praising God at the top of his voice.</p><p>Everyone who has ever walked past that gate knows who he is. They stare. They whisper. ‘Isn’t that the man who always sat and begged at the Beautiful Gate?’ They are amazed, and they can hardly believe it.</p><p>Notice what Peter and John did not say. They did not say, ‘Watch what we can do.’ They said the name of Jesus. They had no silver or gold, but they had something far better to give, because Jesus was with them. Peter, the man who once ran away, stopped, looked at a man everyone else ignored, and gave him everything he had.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Three o’clock, the hour of prayer. · 2. A man who has never walked. · 3. “Look at us.” · 4. “In the name of Jesus… get up and walk!” · 5. He leaps. He walks. He praises God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Documented in Acts 3:1–10. The time (three o’clock, the hour of prayer), the gate, the carrying and begging, Peter’s words and the healing all come from the text. Say calmly that the man had not been able to walk from birth; avoid pity language, and portray him with dignity. Sensitive: some students or family members may have a disability. Keep the focus on Jesus’ power and the man’s joy, and not on being “fixed”. We don’t suggest that every prayer for healing is answered in the same way.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Peter say?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Peter say he did have to give the man?<br>A) Silver and gold &nbsp; B) A new house &nbsp; C) The name of Jesus Christ ✓ &nbsp; D) A job at the temple</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C, he gave the man what he did have: ‘In the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, get up and walk’ (Acts 3:6). Follow-up: ‘What did Peter not have?’ (Silver or gold.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the man do when he stood up?’ (Walked, jumped and praised God.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: At the Beautiful Gate</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>At the Beautiful Gate</b> (Acts 3:1–10). Peter’s words in 3:4 and 3:6, the man’s walking and praising, and the crowd’s astonishment are from Acts 3 (CEV). The man’s feelings and his everyday lines, his friend who carries him, the passer-by’s comments and John’s encouraging remarks are imagined. The characters’ names are descriptive only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Peter</b> — Strong and kind — looks right at the man<br><b>John</b> — Younger and steady — stays close to Peter<br><b>Man at the Gate</b> — Has begged here for years — tired, then overjoyed<br><b>Friend</b> — The friend who carries him to the gate each day<br><b>Worshipper</b> — A temple-goer who recognises the man<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd at the gate</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: pretend to walk past the gate without looking down · Everyone: leap and cheer when the man stands up<br>Props: Optional: a mat or towel for the man to sit on · Optional: a small bowl for coins<br>Peter’s words are the CEV text of Acts 3:6. Everything else is imagined framing around Acts 3:1–10. The man should be played with dignity: tired but hopeful at the start and overjoyed at the end, never a joke. Let the leaping be big but safe. Remind students that Peter and John say it is Jesus’ name and not their own power.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The temple in Jerusalem, three o’clock. Crowds pour through the Beautiful Gate. A man sits on a mat with a bowl.</li><li><b>Friend:</b> Here you are. Same spot as always. I’ll be back this evening. <i>(set down the mat gently)</i></li><li><b>Man at the Gate:</b> Thank you, my friend. Coins for the bowl, please. Anything helps. <i>(hold out the bowl)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hurry, hurry, we’ll be late for prayers! <i>(walk briskly past, not looking down)</i></li><li><b>Worshipper:</b> Sorry, not today. <i>(keep walking)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Then two men stop. Peter and John.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Look at us. <i>(bend down, look straight at him)</i></li><li><b>Man at the Gate:</b> They’re looking at me! Maybe they’ll give me something. <i>(hold the bowl higher)</i></li><li><b>Peter:</b> I don’t have any silver or gold! But I’ll give you what I do have.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> In the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, get up and walk! <i>(reach out his right hand)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> Take his hand. Jesus is with us. <i>(stand close, smiling)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The man has never walked in his life. What do you think he feels in his feet at this very moment? (20s)</li><li><b>Man at the Gate:</b> My feet! My ankles! They’re strong! <i>(stare down at his feet)</i></li><li><b>Man at the Gate:</b> I’m standing! I’m walking! Look at me! <i>(walk, then leap)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> He’s walking! He’s leaping! <i>(cheer, point, jump)</i></li><li><b>Man at the Gate:</b> Praise God! Praise God! Thank you, Jesus! <i>(arms flung up)</i></li><li><b>Worshipper:</b> Isn’t that the man who always sat at the Beautiful Gate? How can it be? <i>(stare, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Friend:</b> He’s walking! He’s walking, and he’s praising God! <i>(hands to cheeks)</i></li><li><b>John:</b> It isn’t our power. It’s the name of Jesus. <i>(smile and gesture upward)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The man went into the temple with Peter and John, walking and jumping and praising God.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> In the name of Jesus! <i>(everyone leaps once together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The man asked for coins. What did he get instead, and why was it better?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He asked for money but got a new life: strong feet, and a chance to walk, work and worship. Peter and John gave what they did have, the power of Jesus.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the crowd do when they saw him?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They stared, recognised him as the man from the Beautiful Gate, and were amazed. Everyone could see what had happened.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A better gift</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter and John had no silver or gold, but they gave the man something better. What does that tell us about the kind of things that really matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: friendship, health, hope and joy are worth more than money; Jesus’ power matters more than anything we own; we can give others what we do have, like time and kindness. Follow-up: ‘What did the man want?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Jesus want for him?’ If silent: ask what they would rather have, a coin or strong legs.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Whose power?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Peter and John healed the man because they were very powerful people.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: they said it was in the name of Jesus, and later Peter said it was faith in Jesus’ name (Acts 3:16). Follow-up: ‘Why did they keep saying Jesus’ name?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does that show us about who gives power?’ (God does; he gives it to ordinary people.) If silent: ask who got the credit in the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We may not have silver or gold, but we can give what we do have, because Jesus is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, show me someone who needs what I can give.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look for someone today who is on their own or sitting out. Give them what you do have: a smile, an invitation to join in, or a kind word.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and specific. The point is to notice people others walk past, as Peter and John did at the gate.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Walking and leaping</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Look at us.”<br>“In the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, get up and walk!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Acts 4:20: shake head, speak, point, hands on chest, eyes, ear. Tomorrow we find out who said it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying boldly — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Peter and John prayed boldly for a man by name, in the name of Jesus. Pray for each other, honestly and boldly. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Tell them one thing you’d like God’s help with this week. It can be small.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you healed a man who had never walked. / God, your name is the most powerful name in the world.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are powerful, and Jesus’ name is stronger than anything!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where did you walk past someone this week without noticing?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’ve walked past someone who needed a friend. / Sorry for thinking I have nothing to give.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we don’t notice people who need help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who stop and notice us.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who look at us and really see us, like Peter and John. / Thank you that Jesus’ power is for ordinary people like us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who notice us and for Jesus’ power.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one bold sentence for your partner’s need.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) with what they told me. / Please give us courage to speak up and help others, in Jesus’ name.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us, and give us courage to help others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a partner’s need is private or sensitive, they can say “something at home” and leave it there. Keep the prayers short and warm.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Peter and John face the council — and say, “We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: We Cannot Stop Speaking":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 1:8; 2; 3:1–10; 4:1–22</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">We Cannot Stop Speaking</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying boldly — Praying like the early church starts this week: boldly. Bold prayer is not loud prayer. It means talking to God honestly and confidently, because Jesus has opened the way, and asking for big things, such as the courage to speak up (Acts 4:29–31).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened when Peter and John stood before the council.</li><li>We are learning what this story shows us about God, who gives power.</li><li>I can choose one thing Jesus has done and plan who to tell.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the week in order, explain that Peter and John were bold because of the Holy Spirit and because they had seen Jesus, say that God gives power, and recite Acts 4:20.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Cards from Tuesday (“one thing Jesus has done”)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: We Cannot Stop Speaking</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put the week in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put this week’s story in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The believers wait in the upstairs room → Wind, flames of light and many languages → Peter preaches to the crowd → About three thousand believe and are baptised → A man at the Beautiful Gate walks → Peter and John stand before the council</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Ask: ‘What did Jesus promise before the very first step?’ (Acts 1:8: the Holy Spirit and power.) Point out how each step led to the next.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 4:12–13, 19–20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:12</b> Only Jesus has the power to save! His name is the only one in all the world that can save anyone.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:13</b> The officials were amazed to see how bold Peter and John were, and they could tell that these two men were ordinary people and not well educated. They realized that these men had been with Jesus.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:19</b> Do you think God wants us to obey you or to obey him?</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:20</b> We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 4:20 is our memory verse; now students know who said it. The officials were the Jewish council, the same group that had put Jesus on trial. Verses 4:12 and 4:19–20 are Peter’s and John’s words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Before the council</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Peter and John are still speaking to the crowd at the temple when some priests and the temple guards arrive. They are upset that these two are teaching about Jesus rising from the dead, so they take them and lock them up for the night.</p><p>Next morning, the leaders of the nation gather in a great half-circle. Some of them are the same men who had put Jesus on trial. In the middle stand Peter and John, and next to them the man who was healed, on his own two feet. ‘By what power, or in whose name, did you do this?’ they ask.</p><p>Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, answers without fear. It was by the name of Jesus Christ from Nazareth, he says. Only Jesus has the power to save.</p><p>The leaders are amazed. These two are ordinary working men with no fancy training. But they can tell that Peter and John have been with Jesus. And the healed man is standing right there, so they have nothing to say. They order Peter and John never to speak about Jesus again.</p><p>Peter and John answer calmly: ‘Do you think God wants us to obey you or to obey him? We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.’ The leaders warn them again, then let them go.</p><p>So what does this story show us about God? He gives power. Not power to show off, but power to be brave, to love, and to tell the truth. God gave his Spirit to ordinary people, and they were never alone.</p><p>Now imagine a Year 6 girl, nervous at lunchtime. Something good has happened, and she thinks it was Jesus. She takes a breath and says to one friend, kindly, ‘I think Jesus helped me.’ That is speaking up. It doesn’t need a crowd, only one friend and one true thing.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Arrested, then questioned. · 2. “Only Jesus has the power to save.” · 3. “We cannot stop speaking.” · 4. God gives power. · 5. One thing. One friend.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Documented in Acts 4:1–22. Death and violence are not part of this passage; only arrest and a warning, then release. Explain calmly: Peter and John were told to stop telling the truth about Jesus, and chose to keep telling it. This does not mean we disobey parents or teachers; here, the leaders were ordering them to stop saying what was true. The Year 6 girl in the last paragraph is imagined, to help us picture speaking up today. Some students may have family from places where speaking about Jesus is dangerous or where people hold other beliefs. Say that no one is ever pushed to share, and that speaking up always means being kind and listening too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did they notice?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the officials realise about Peter and John?<br>A) They had been to a famous school &nbsp; B) They were very rich &nbsp; C) They had been with Jesus ✓ &nbsp; D) They were afraid of the council</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C, they had been with Jesus (Acts 4:13). Follow-up: ‘What did the officials notice about how they spoke?’ (They were bold.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did they say about their education?’ (Ordinary people, not well educated.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Nothing to say</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The council could see the healed man standing right there, so they had nothing to say. Why do you think a changed life is such a strong witness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: you can argue about words but not about what people can see; a changed life shows Jesus’ power; it’s true and real; people trust what they see. Follow-up: ‘What did the man’s walking prove?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What might people see in you that points to Jesus?’ If silent: ask what a friend notices when you’re kind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus kept his promise and gave his followers the Holy Spirit, who gave them power.</li><li>Ordinary people like Peter and John became bold, because they had been with Jesus and had seen what he had done.</li><li>When you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can speak up about it, kindly, to one person at a time.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God gives his Holy Spirit to ordinary people, so they can boldly tell what Jesus has done — and when you have seen and heard what Jesus has done, you can’t keep quiet about it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Bold or brave?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Being bold means you’re never nervous.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Peter was nervous in the upper room, and was bold because the Spirit helped him, not because he felt fearless. Follow-up: ‘What helps you do a hard thing even when you’re nervous?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who was with Peter and John?’ (Jesus’ Spirit, and each other.) If silent: ask about a time they were nervous and did it anyway.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Speak up about one thing Jesus has done. Tell one person.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the cards from Tuesday. It can be a Bible story (the man at the gate, Peter’s forgiveness), or something from their own life. This is an invitation, never a pressure: students from other faith backgrounds can choose to share something they have learned this week. Telling one person is plenty. Next Monday’s check-in will ask how it went.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” — Acts 4:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Acts 4:20. Bonus: say Luke 18:13 from Term 3, Week 10.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Bold because of the Spirit</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.<br>God gives power to ordinary people.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying boldly — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">After the council let them go, the believers prayed together and asked God for boldness (Acts 4:23–31). Today we pray like them: out loud, together, honest and bold. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is. Think of power and promises.<br>Prompts: God, you give power to ordinary people. / God, you are with us, just as you were with Peter and John.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you give power, and you are always with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about a time you stayed quiet when you should have spoken kindly.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I stay silent because I’m afraid of what others will think. / Thank you that you forgive us, like you forgave Peter.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you forgive us and help us start again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing Jesus has done that you’re thankful for.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for the man at the gate who walked and praised God. / Thank you for the Holy Spirit, who helps us be brave.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for everything you have done.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray a bold prayer for your challenge this week.<br>Prompts: Please give me courage to tell one person what you have done. / Please help my friends to be open to good news about Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us courage, and help us tell what we have seen and heard.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say our verse together once more: “We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers. The believers’ prayer in Acts 4:29–31 is paraphrased in the tip, not quoted; they asked for boldness, and the Spirit filled them again.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Stephen: Faithful to the End — a man chosen to serve who stays faithful, even when it is costly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 2: Stephen: Faithful to the End":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 6:1–8:4 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgiving</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for those who hurt us — Jesus said, “Love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you” (Matthew 5:44, CEV). Stephen did exactly that. Praying for someone who has hurt us doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and it doesn’t mean staying where we’re not safe. It means asking God to be kind to them, and to heal our own hearts too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Seven Helpers Chosen</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how the first church fixed an unfair problem with sharing food.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Seven Helpers Chosen → Missed in the line → Acts 6:5–7 → Who gets missed? → How many helpers? → Why helpers? → The Seven Helpers → Is serving ‘spiritual’? → Head · Heart · Hands → The food problem → Memory verse peek → Praying for those who hurt us — fist to open hand</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Face Like an Angel</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Stephen served and spoke about Jesus with grace and power.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Face Like an Angel → Recap → Acts 6:8, 10, 15 → Stephen and the council → Four big words → A face like… → Calm in the room → Will everyone like us? → Head · Heart · Hands → A face like an angel → Build the verse → Praying for those who hurt us — write it down</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Before the Council</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Stephen answered the council and what he saw.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Before the Council → When it’s not fair → Acts 7:55–56, 60 → Before the council → What did Stephen see? → Before the Council → Where did the strength come from? → Is forgiving fair? → Head · Heart · Hands → “I see heaven open” → Verse with actions → Praying for those who hurt us — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Forgiven and Sent</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what Stephen’s story shows us about God and about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Forgiven and Sent → Stephen’s story in order → Matthew 5:44 · Luke 23:34 · Acts 8:4 → Forgiven and sent → Stephen’s last prayer → Like Jesus → This week’s big idea → Pretend it never hurt? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Sent out → Praying for those who hurt us — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Seven Helpers Chosen":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 6:1–8:4 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Seven Helpers Chosen</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for those who hurt us — Jesus said, “Love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you” (Matthew 5:44, CEV). Stephen did exactly that. Praying for someone who has hurt us doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and it doesn’t mean staying where we’re not safe. It means asking God to be kind to them, and to heal our own hearts too.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the first church fixed an unfair problem with sharing food.</li><li>We are learning who Stephen and Philip were, and why they were chosen.</li><li>I can explain why God cares about people who get overlooked.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that some widows were being missed in the daily food, that the church chose seven helpers full of the Spirit and wisdom, and that Stephen was the first name on the list.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a basket and a loaf of bread to pass along the line</li><li>Optional: seven chairs in a row for the role-play</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Seven Helpers Chosen</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Read the Wednesday and Thursday teacher notes before this week: Stephen’s story ends with his death. We tell it gently and implied only, and we keep the focus on Jesus and on forgiveness. Some students may have family from places where Christians are mistreated today, so keep the tone calm and hopeful.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Missed in the line</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever stood in a line — for food, for a turn, for a ball — and watched the person in charge miss you? Maybe nobody meant to. How did it feel?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you want someone to do about it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Peter and John, who were bold because of the Holy Spirit and wouldn’t stop talking about Jesus, even when the council told them to be quiet. Your challenge: speak up about one thing Jesus has done, and tell one person. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students who they told and what they said. Celebrate small steps. Then the hook: tuckshop lines, the end of a queue, being picked last. Land it: ‘Today the very first church has this exact problem, and we find out how they fixed it, and who they chose to help.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 6:5–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:5</b> They chose Stephen, who had a lot of faith and was filled with the Holy Spirit.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:6</b> Then they brought these men to the apostles, who prayed and placed their hands on them.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:7</b> God’s message continued to spread.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Apostles = Jesus’ closest followers, whom he sent out. The Holy Spirit = God’s own Spirit, who lives in Jesus’ followers and gives them power. Placing hands on someone while praying was a way of saying, ‘God is with you, and we’re behind you.’ Acts 6:3–5 also gives the full list of seven names. We are only reading the lines most central to our story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Who gets missed?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture Jerusalem a few years after Jesus rose from the dead. Peter and John’s boldness has done its work, and thousands of people now follow Jesus. They are called believers, because they believe, or trust, that Jesus is God’s Son and the world’s true King. They meet every day. They pray, they eat together, and they share what they have so that nobody goes without.</p><p>Every morning there is a daily food share, and it is especially for the widows. A widow is a woman whose husband has died. In those days she might have nobody to earn money or buy food for her, so the church looks after her. It is a beautiful idea. But the church has grown so fast that something has slipped.</p><p>Many of the believers speak Greek and grew up in other countries. Many others speak Hebrew and have lived in Jerusalem all their lives. Day after day, the Greek-speaking widows are being missed in the food line. Nobody means to do it. But it happens, and the Greek-speaking believers begin to complain. And honestly? They are right. It isn’t fair.</p><p>So who will fix it? The twelve apostles, Jesus’ closest followers, are busy teaching and praying, and that job matters too. So they call everyone together. They don’t ignore the complaint, and they don’t argue. They say, ‘This is a real problem, and we should solve it properly. Choose seven men you trust, men who are wise and full of God’s Spirit, and we will put them in charge of the food.’</p><p>Everyone likes the idea. And look at who they choose. The very first name is Stephen, a man with a lot of faith, filled with the Holy Spirit. Then comes Philip, whom we’ll meet again next week. Then five more. And here is something many Bible teachers notice: all seven have Greek names. The group that was missed is trusted to put things right!</p><p>The seven stand in a row in front of the apostles. The apostles pray and place their hands on them, a way of saying, ‘God is with you, and we’re behind you.’ Then the seven roll up their sleeves, pick up the baskets of bread, and get to work.</p><p>Jesus taught that whoever wants to be great must be a servant. So handing out bread is no small job in God’s kingdom. And the book of Acts tells us what happened next: God’s message kept spreading, and more and more people trusted Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Jerusalem. The church is growing fast. · 2. A problem: some widows are missed. · 3. The apostles call everyone together. · 4. Seven helpers. First name: Stephen. · 5. They pray. Then they get to work.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. The apostles’ words are a paraphrase of Acts 6:2–4; Luke does not name Peter as the speaker. Detail from Acts 6:1–7 only; the idea that the seven all have Greek names is a common observation, not a statement in the text. Jesus’ teaching on serving is Mark 10:43–44 (paraphrased). This is a gentle, hopeful opening to a week with a hard ending: let students enjoy it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How many helpers?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How many helpers did the church choose to look after the food?<br>A) Three &nbsp; B) Seven ✓ &nbsp; C) Twelve &nbsp; D) Seventy</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, seven (Acts 6:3, 5). Follow-up: ‘Who was the first name on the list?’ (Stephen.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was the problem they were fixing?’ (Greek-speaking widows were being missed in the daily food.) If silent: re-read the scripture slide and count the names: Stephen, Philip and five more.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why helpers?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The apostles could have ignored the complaint, or tried to fix everything themselves. Why do you think they chose seven helpers instead?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: the problem was real and fair to raise; the apostles’ job was teaching and praying and they couldn’t do everything; sharing jobs means more people are included; they trusted others. Follow-up: ‘What did the apostles do first, before choosing anyone?’ (They listened and called everyone together.) Follow-up 2: ‘When has someone listened to your complaint and fixed it?’ If silent: ask what would have happened if nobody had listened.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Seven Helpers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Seven Helpers</b> (Acts 6:1–7). The big events are from Acts 6:1–7: widows missed, the apostles’ plan, the seven chosen, prayer with hands laid on. The widow, the server, and all the spoken words are imagined to bring the story to life; Stephen’s and Philip’s lines are imagined too.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Peter</b> — An apostle — fair and calm<br><b>Stephen</b> — Gentle, kind and full of the Spirit<br><b>Philip</b> — Cheerful and energetic<br><b>Widow</b> — Patient, but tired of being missed<br><b>Server</b> — Rushed and sorry — means well<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd in the line, then the helpers’ cheer-squad</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: grumble and murmur while standing in the line · Everyone: cheer quietly, then hold up a loaf of bread together<br>Props: Optional: a basket and a loaf of bread to pass along the line · Optional: seven chairs in a row for the helpers<br>Acts 6:1–7 gives the problem, the plan, the seven, and the prayer. Everything the characters say is imagined; Peter is used as a speaker for ‘the twelve’. Keep it light and warm. Let students enjoy the grumble and the cheer. Stephen’s kindness to the person at the back sets up the rest of the week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A courtyard in Jerusalem, early morning. Believers line up with baskets for the daily food share.</li><li><b>Widow:</b> Excuse me, I’ve been waiting since sunrise. Is there any bread left for me? <i>(hold out an empty basket)</i></li><li><b>Server:</b> Sorry! There are so many people today. I didn’t see you at the back. <i>(rush past with a tray)</i></li><li><b>Widow:</b> That’s the third day I’ve been missed. And I’m not the only one. <i>(sigh)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> It isn’t fair! The Greek-speaking widows keep getting missed! <i>(grumble and murmur, arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The complaint reaches the apostles. Peter calls everyone together.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Friends, you’re right. This matters. But we must keep teaching and praying, so we can’t do it all ourselves.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Choose seven men you trust, men who are wise and full of God’s Spirit. We’ll put them in charge of the food.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Yes! Good idea! <i>(cheer quietly and nod)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They choose seven. The first name is Stephen, full of faith and the Holy Spirit. Next is Philip.</li><li><b>Stephen:</b> Me? I’m not sure I’m ready. But if the church needs bread carried, I’ll carry it. <i>(stand up slowly)</i></li><li><b>Philip:</b> We’ll do it together, Stephen. This time, nobody gets missed. <i>(clap him on the shoulder)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Seven people are about to be trusted with an important job. Why would the church choose people ‘full of the Spirit’ just to hand out bread? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The seven stand in a row. The apostles pray and place their hands on their shoulders.</li><li><b>Peter:</b> Lord, be with these seven. Fill them with your Spirit, and help them serve with love. <i>(hand on a shoulder, head bowed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Amen! <i>(bow heads together)</i></li><li><b>Stephen:</b> Right. Let’s start with the widow at the back of the line. <i>(pick up a basket and walk to the back)</i></li><li><b>Widow:</b> Oh! Thank you. I thought everyone had forgotten me. <i>(look up, surprised)</i></li><li><b>Stephen:</b> God hasn’t forgotten you, and neither have we. Here, take some bread. <i>(hand over the loaf)</i></li><li><b>Philip:</b> Next! Who’s waiting? Every single person gets a share today. <i>(wave the line forward)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> And that’s how God’s message kept spreading: the people at the back were seen.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Nobody gets missed! <i>(hold the loaf high together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the problem in the food line, and who noticed it?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The Greek-speaking widows were being missed each day. They noticed and complained; the apostles listened.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Stephen show he cared, right from his first job?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He went to the back of the line first, to the person who had been missed. He served quietly, which showed what kind of person he was.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is serving ‘spiritual’?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Handing out food doesn’t sound like a ‘spiritual’ job, yet the church chose people full of the Holy Spirit to do it. What does that tell us about how God sees everyday kindness?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God cares about people’s bodies and hearts; serving is part of following Jesus, who served; nobody is too important for small jobs; being full of the Spirit shows in how we treat people. Follow-up: ‘What small jobs at school or home are really service?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who has served you quietly this week?’ If silent: ask what would happen to the widows if only ‘important’ people helped.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The first church noticed people who were being missed and fixed it, and God cares about everyone, including those at the back of the line.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, open my eyes to see the people who get missed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, find one person who’s at the back, on the edge of a game, or sitting alone, and include them by name.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small: one person, today. Ask on Tuesday who noticed someone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The food problem</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“It isn’t fair!”<br>The church noticed — and fixed it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Acts 7:60. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Tell them these are Stephen’s words, and we’ll hear the story behind them on Wednesday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for those who hurt us — fist to open hand</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make a fist and think of a time someone missed you or hurt you. Then slowly open your hand. An open hand is a picture of giving it to God. We’ll practise praying for people who hurt us all week. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably. Make a fist in your lap, then take a slow breath. We’re going to pray with our hands.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your open hands. God sees every person in the line, even the one at the back.<br>Prompts: God, you see the people everyone else misses. / God, you chose ordinary helpers and filled them with your Spirit.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you see everyone, even the people at the back!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think about someone you have missed or left out.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t noticed someone who was left out. / Sorry for complaining about people instead of helping.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve missed people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who have noticed you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who include me. / Thank you for helpers like Stephen and Philip, who served quietly.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who notice us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make a fist, then slowly open it. Ask God to help someone who feels missed, or who has missed you.<br>Prompts: Please help someone who feels left out today, and show me how to include them. / Please be kind to anyone who has overlooked me, and help me let it go.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help everyone who feels left out, and help us let hurts go.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Rest your open hands on your knees. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep this gentle. A fist opening is a simple, wordless picture of letting go. Don’t ask students to name who hurt them aloud. Some may have been hurt badly: they can pray silently and simply say ‘help me’.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Stephen, full of grace and power — and the people who told lies about him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: A Face Like an Angel":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 6:1–8:4 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Face Like an Angel</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for those who hurt us — Jesus said, “Love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you” (Matthew 5:44, CEV). Stephen did exactly that. Praying for someone who has hurt us doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and it doesn’t mean staying where we’re not safe. It means asking God to be kind to them, and to heal our own hearts too.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Stephen served and spoke about Jesus with grace and power.</li><li>We are learning why some people told lies about him.</li><li>I can explain what it means that Stephen’s face looked like the face of an angel.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Stephen served and spoke about Jesus, that people who couldn’t win an argument told lies about him, and that his face looked calm and peaceful in front of the council because Jesus was with him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Face Like an Angel</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday the church chose seven helpers so that no widow would be missed. Stephen was the first name on the list.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think they chose people ‘full of the Spirit’ just to look after bread?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Expect: because how we serve shows who we follow; God cares about everyday kindness; the Spirit gives wisdom. Move on: ‘Today we find out that Stephen did more than hand out bread, and that some people didn’t like it.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 6:8, 10, 15</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:8</b> God gave Stephen the power to work great miracles and wonders among the people.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:10</b> The Spirit was helping Stephen speak with wisdom, and no one could win an argument with him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 6:15</b> Everyone in the council was staring at Stephen. They saw that his face looked like the face of an angel.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Miracles = amazing things only God can do. The council was the Jewish court of leaders in Jerusalem. An angel is a messenger from God. These lines are our best recollection of the CEV; check against your own Bible if you want the exact wording.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Stephen and the council</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday, Stephen was handed a basket of bread. Today we find out that his work didn’t stop with the bread. Everywhere Stephen went in Jerusalem, people noticed something about him. The book of Acts says he was full of God’s grace, which means God’s free kindness, and full of God’s power. When Stephen spoke about Jesus, people listened. And God worked amazing miracles through him, to show that Jesus is alive.</p><p>But not everyone liked what Stephen was saying. Some men from the Jewish meeting places in the city, places where people from many different countries gathered to pray and read the Scriptures, began to argue with him. They didn’t believe Jesus was God’s promised King. Stephen answered every question. The Bible says the Spirit helped him speak with wisdom, and nobody could win an argument against him.</p><p>So what do you do when you can’t win an argument? Some of those men decided to cheat. They secretly paid people to tell lies. ‘We heard Stephen say terrible things about Moses and about God!’ they claimed. It wasn’t true. But a lie can spread fast. Soon the crowd, the elders and the teachers of the Law were all stirred up. They grabbed Stephen and brought him in front of the council.</p><p>The council was the court of Jewish leaders in Jerusalem. It was the same group that had questioned Peter and John last week, and the same kind of court that had put Jesus on trial. Remember, Jesus had faced false witnesses too. Stephen was walking the very same path as his Lord.</p><p>More liars stood up. ‘This man never stops speaking against our holy temple and against the Law!’ they said. ‘We heard him say that Jesus will destroy this place and change what Moses taught us!’</p><p>Imagine standing in that room. Every face is angry. Every finger is pointing. You are all alone, and everyone is saying something false about you. Most of us would feel terrified. We might shout, or cry, or want to run away.</p><p>But look at Stephen. Acts tells us that everyone in the council stared at him, and they saw that his face looked like the face of an angel. It doesn’t say he glowed like a lamp. It says his face was calm, peaceful and gentle, like someone who has just come from God’s presence. Stephen wasn’t afraid, because he knew who was with him. Jesus was in that room, and Stephen knew it.</p><p>The high priest turns to Stephen. The whole room is waiting. And Stephen takes a deep breath, and he begins to speak.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Stephen: full of grace and power. · 2. Not everyone liked it. · 3. They couldn’t win the argument. · 4. “We heard him speak against God!” · 5. Every eye on Stephen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. From Acts 6:8–15: Stephen’s grace, power and wisdom, the argument, the paid liars, the council, the angel face. The meeting places are the ‘synagogues’ of Acts 6:9 (Jewish meeting places, often for people from other countries). Wording of the false claims paraphrases Acts 6:11–14. The council = the Sanhedrin. The parallel with Jesus’ trial (Mark 14:55–59) is our own observation, and it points to Jesus. Sensitive: false accusations and being alone. Reassure students that this is a story, and that tomorrow we hear how it ends. If a student has been lied about or bullied, keep the focus on Stephen’s peace, not on the lies.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Four big words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today’s story has some churchy words. What do they mean?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>Grace</td><td>God’s free kindness, which we don’t have to earn</td></tr><tr><td>The council</td><td>The court of Jewish leaders in Jerusalem</td></tr><tr><td>A false witness</td><td>Someone who tells lies about another person, especially in court</td></tr><tr><td>An angel</td><td>A messenger who comes from God’s presence</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen was full of God’s kindness, and God’s kindness showed on his face.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Tie each word back to the story: grace (Acts 6:8), council (6:12), false witnesses (6:13), angel (6:15).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A face like…</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “They saw that his face looked like the face of an ________.”<br><i>Answer: “…angel.” (Acts 6:15)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: angel. Follow-up: ‘Who was staring at Stephen?’ (Everyone in the council.) Follow-up 2: ‘What had the liars said about him?’ (That he spoke against the temple, Moses and God.) If silent: show the scripture slide again and ask them to read the last line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Calm in the room</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen was lied about in front of everyone and still looked peaceful. What do you think made it possible for him to stay calm?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he knew Jesus was with him; he was full of the Holy Spirit; he trusted God more than the crowd; he knew the truth. Follow-up: ‘What do you do to stay calm when something is unfair?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about Jesus, who was with him?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Will everyone like us?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If we follow Jesus, everybody will like us.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: not everyone liked Jesus, and not everyone liked Stephen; following Jesus can mean being misunderstood or left out. But we are never alone, and we can still be kind. Follow-up: ‘How should we treat people who don’t like us?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who was with Stephen?’ If silent: ask what Jesus said about loving enemies (Matthew 5:44).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen served with kindness and spoke with truth, and when people lied about him, God’s peace showed on his face.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, you are with me, even when people are unfair.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">If someone says something untrue about you this week, take one slow breath and pray silently, “Jesus, you’re with me,” before you answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and practical. Give out sticky notes so students can write “Jesus, you’re with me” and keep it in a pocket or book. If a student discloses repeated bullying, follow your school’s process and speak to them privately afterwards.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A face like an angel</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Everyone was staring at Stephen.”<br>His face looked like the face of an angel.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Acts 7:60 chunk by chunk. Remind them: Stephen will say this tomorrow. Practise it as a quiet, strong prayer, not a shout.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for those who hurt us — write it down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a short prayer for someone who has been unkind. You don’t have to forgive everything at once. Start with their name. Only you and God need to see it. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your sticky note and pen. Write at the top: “Jesus, you’re with me.” Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is, remembering the room where Stephen stood.<br>Prompts: God, you stay with us even when everyone is against us. / God, you gave Stephen peace on his face.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are with us in every room, even the scary ones.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: think about untrue or unkind things you have said about someone.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’ve said unkind things about someone when they weren’t there. / Sorry for believing a rumour without checking.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the untrue and unkind things we say.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’re thankful for about Stephen, or about someone who stood up for the truth.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Stephen, who served and told people about Jesus. / Thank you for people who tell the truth and stick up for others.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who stand up for the truth.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the first name of someone who has been unkind to you, or write ‘someone who hurt me’. Ask God to be kind to them.<br>Prompts: Please be kind to the person who hurt me, and help me not to hold on to it. / Please give peace to anyone who is being lied about today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be kind to those who hurt us, and give us peace.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your note and keep it somewhere safe. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their notes private. If a student can’t think of a name, ‘someone who hurt me’ is fine. Praying for someone doesn’t mean what they did was okay; make that clear if the question comes up. If a student seems distressed, check in quietly afterwards.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Stephen stands before the council, and sees something amazing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Before the Council":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 6:1–8:4 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Before the Council</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for those who hurt us — Jesus said, “Love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you” (Matthew 5:44, CEV). Stephen did exactly that. Praying for someone who has hurt us doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and it doesn’t mean staying where we’re not safe. It means asking God to be kind to them, and to heal our own hearts too.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Stephen answered the council and what he saw.</li><li>We are learning how Stephen prayed for the very people who were against him.</li><li>I can say what Stephen asked God to do for them (Acts 7:60).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Stephen told God’s story and the council became angry, that he saw heaven open and Jesus standing, and that his last prayer asked God not to blame the people who were against him.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a chair for the high priest</li><li>Optional: a torch for the light from heaven</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Before the Council</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story reaches Stephen’s death, told gently and implied only: we never describe how or what happened to him, and we end at his prayer. Read the notes on the story and role-play slides before you begin.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: When it’s not fair</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think of a time you were blamed or laughed at when you hadn’t done anything wrong. What did you want to do back?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s the hardest thing to do when someone is unkind to you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three answers, keeping them light: wanting to shout back, hide, or get even. Recap: ‘Yesterday Stephen was brought before the council because of lies. Today he answers, and we see how he treats the people who are against him.’ Heads-up: ‘Today’s story has a sad part. We’ll tell it gently, and it’s okay to look away or take a breath.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 7:55–56, 60</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 7:55</b> But Stephen was filled with the Holy Spirit. He looked toward heaven, where he saw our glorious God and Jesus standing at his right side.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 7:56</b> Then he said, “I see heaven open and the Son of Man standing at the right side of God.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 7:60</b> He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 7:60 is our memory verse. Son of Man = Jesus’ favourite name for himself, from Daniel 7. We are not reading verses 57–59 or the end of verse 60 aloud, because they describe how Stephen was harmed. Teacher wording for the end: ‘Stephen gave his life, trusting Jesus.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Before the council</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The room is silent. The high priest, the leader of the council, asks Stephen one question: ‘Are these things true?’</p><p>Stephen does not panic. He stands tall and, instead of defending himself, he tells the council a story they all know. He starts with Abraham, who followed God into a new land. He tells about Joseph, whose brothers were unkind to him, but God was with him. He tells about Moses, whom God sent to rescue the people, even though some of them wouldn’t listen. All the way through, Stephen shows one thing: God is faithful. God keeps his promises, and God sends people to help his people. And again and again, God’s people said no.</p><p>Then Stephen says the hard part, and he says it plainly. ‘You are stubborn,’ he tells them. ‘You keep saying no to the Holy Spirit, just as your ancestors did. They turned against the prophets, God’s messengers. And now you have turned against the one God promised, Jesus.’</p><p>The council members are furious. They grind their teeth. They cover their ears. The noise in the room grows louder and louder.</p><p>But Stephen isn’t looking at them any more. He is filled with the Holy Spirit. He looks up, past the ceiling and past the sky, and sees something nobody else can see. ‘Look!’ he says. ‘I see heaven open, and the Son of Man standing at the right side of God!’</p><p>The Son of Man is Jesus’ own favourite name for himself. And Jesus is standing! Many Bible teachers think that Jesus is standing because he is welcoming his faithful friend. Stephen isn’t alone in that room, or anywhere. Jesus sees him.</p><p>Now the crowd takes Stephen out of the city. Outside the walls, a young man named Saul stands nearby, looking after people’s coats. Remember his name.</p><p>And Stephen? Here is what the Bible tells us he does. He kneels down. He doesn’t run. He doesn’t shout back. He prays out loud, so that everyone can hear: ‘Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!’ He is praying for the very people who are against him, just as Jesus prayed for the people who hurt him.</p><p>Stephen gave his life that day, trusting Jesus to the end.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. “Are these things true?” · 2. Stephen tells God’s story. · 3. Then the hard truth. · 4. Stephen looks up. · 5. He kneels and prays. · 6. Stephen gave his life, trusting Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. DEATH IMPLIED ONLY: Acts 7:57–60 says the crowd threw stones; we do not say this, and we end at the prayer. Use the phrases ‘he gave his life’ and ‘he died, trusting Jesus’; never describe the method, pain or injuries. Stephen’s speech in Acts 7 is long; our summary of verses 2–50 and 51–53 is in our own words. The words ‘Are these things true?’ (7:1), ‘I see heaven open…’ (7:56) and the final prayer (7:60) are Scripture. Saul is named in Acts 7:58, so he is real; ‘looking after people’s coats’ is from the text. Some students may have family from places where Christians are mistreated today: reassure them that this story shows God with his people, and that Jesus is with Stephen. If a student is upset, invite them to take a breath or look away.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did Stephen see?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Stephen say he saw?<br>A) A great army &nbsp; B) Heaven open and Jesus standing at God’s right side ✓ &nbsp; C) A chariot of fire &nbsp; D) A lamp in the dark</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, heaven open and Jesus standing at God’s right side (Acts 7:55–56). Follow-up: ‘Who else could see it?’ (Nobody else in the room.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Stephen do next?’ (He knelt down and prayed for the people against him.) If silent: re-read the scripture slide line 2 together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Before the Council</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Before the Council</b> (Acts 7:1, 51–60). The high priest’s question, Stephen’s words ‘I see heaven open…’ and his final prayer are from Acts 7 (CEV). Stephen’s history summary is our own paraphrase of Acts 7:2–53. The council member and the shouts are imagined, and Saul does not speak. The role-play stops at Stephen’s prayer; his death is implied only.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story, calm and clear<br><b>Stephen</b> — Calm, strong and gentle — not angry<br><b>High Priest</b> — The council’s leader — serious and stern<br><b>Council Member</b> — Angry and impatient<br><b>Everyone</b> — The council, then the quiet</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: murmur and grumble with arms folded, then cover your ears · Everyone: freeze, bow your head, and whisper the last line<br>Props: Optional: a chair for the high priest · Optional: a torch for the light from heaven<br>Stephen’s role: calm, not angry. Let the Everyone parts be loud, then very quiet. DEATH IMPLIED ONLY: we end the role-play with the prayer and one line from the Narrator; do not act out any harm. Stage the kneeling gently and have students bow their heads after. Debrief quietly. If anyone seems affected, invite them to take a breath and offer a private chat afterwards. Words that are Scripture: the high priest’s question, Stephen’s ‘I see heaven open…’ and his final prayer. Everything else is imagined framing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The council hall in Jerusalem. Stephen stands in the middle. The leaders sit in a half-circle, arms folded.</li><li><b>High Priest:</b> Are these things true? Speak, Stephen. <i>(lean forward)</i></li><li><b>Stephen:</b> God appeared to Abraham, and God was faithful. God was with Joseph, even when his brothers were unkind. <i>(stand tall, speak calmly)</i></li><li><b>Council Member:</b> We know our history, Stephen. Get to the point. <i>(tap fingers on arm)</i></li><li><b>Stephen:</b> God sent Moses to rescue his people. Yet again and again, they said no to the ones God sent. <i>(open hands)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mutter, mutter, grumble. <i>(murmur with arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Stephen:</b> You keep saying no to the Holy Spirit. And now you have said no to Jesus, the one God promised.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> How dare you! <i>(shout and cover your ears)</i></li><li><b>High Priest:</b> Enough! He speaks against God and against our Law! <i>(stand up)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The shouting grows louder. But Stephen is filled with the Holy Spirit, and he lifts his eyes.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Stephen is surrounded by angry faces, yet he looks up. What do you think he sees? What do you think he feels? (20s)</li><li><b>Stephen:</b> Look! I see heaven open and the Son of Man standing at the right side of God! <i>(face lifted, arms open, calm)</i></li><li><b>Council Member:</b> Take him out of the city! We won’t listen to any more! <i>(point to the door)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The crowd takes Stephen outside the city walls. A young man named Saul stands nearby, minding people’s coats.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Stephen doesn’t run. He doesn’t shout back. He kneels down.</li><li><b>Stephen:</b> Lord, don’t blame them for this sin! <i>(kneel, face lifted, voice strong and gentle)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhh… <i>(freeze with heads bowed, in silence)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Stephen gave his life that day, trusting Jesus. His last words were a prayer for the people against him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Faithful to the end. <i>(whisper together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Stephen see, and what did he say in his last prayer?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He saw heaven open and Jesus standing at God’s right side. He prayed, ‘Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen prayed for the people who were against him. Why is that so surprising?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: People usually want to get even. Stephen asked God to forgive them. It’s like Jesus’ prayer on the cross (Luke 23:34).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did the strength come from?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen could have shouted back or tried to escape. Instead, he looked up and prayed for the people against him. Where do you think he found the strength?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he saw Jesus and knew he wasn’t alone; the Holy Spirit gave him strength; he had learned from Jesus how to forgive; he trusted God. Follow-up: ‘What does it mean that Jesus was standing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What can we do when we feel alone?’ If silent: ask who Stephen was looking at.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is forgiving fair?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen forgave people who weren’t sorry. Is that fair? What do you think forgiving someone means, and what do you think it doesn’t mean?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: forgiving means letting go of getting even and asking God to be kind to them; it doesn’t mean saying it was okay, or going back to being hurt. God is fair, and forgiveness leaves justice to him. Follow-up: ‘Why is it so hard to forgive?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who forgave us first?’ (Jesus.) If silent: ask what Jesus prayed on the cross. Safeguarding: forgiveness never means staying near someone who is hurting you; tell a trusted adult.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen looked to Jesus and prayed for the people who were against him.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, help me look to you when I’m hurt, instead of getting even.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, think of one person who has been unkind. Quietly say, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin,” and ask God to be kind to them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small, private and doable. Don’t ask for names aloud. Thursday’s challenge builds on this.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “I see heaven open”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I see heaven open!”<br>“Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Acts 7:60: kneel or bow, shout cup, lift a hand, open palms, point then open, brush. Keep it reverent and quiet.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for those who hurt us — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. You don’t need to say who hurt you. Just say, “I’m praying for someone who hurt me,” and your partner prays with you for them. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Take a slow breath. Each of you will say one short sentence, and you’ll take turns.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God, thinking of what Stephen saw.<br>Prompts: God, you are with us in every room, even when we feel alone. / God, you are forgiving, and you forgave us first.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are with us, and you forgive!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where do you hold on to something someone did?<br>Prompts: Sorry for holding grudges and wanting to get even. / Sorry for the times I haven’t prayed for people who were unkind.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for holding on to hurts.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for Jesus, who prayed for the people who hurt him.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Jesus, who forgave us at great cost. / Thank you for Stephen, who showed us how to pray for the people who hurt us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Jesus, who forgives us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s ‘someone’, and one for Christians anywhere who are treated unkindly for following Jesus.<br>Prompts: Please be kind to the person my partner is praying for, and give them peace. / Please be close to Christians anywhere who are treated unfairly because they follow Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be kind to those who hurt us, and be close to Christians who are treated unfairly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying with you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. Partners don’t need to know who the ‘someone’ is. If a student says they are hurting badly, don’t press: note their name and follow up privately. Keep any mention of persecuted Christians calm and hopeful, with no scary detail.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what Stephen’s forgiveness shows us about God, and how the church kept going.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Forgiven and Sent":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 6:1–8:4 (selected)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Forgiven and Sent</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for those who hurt us — Jesus said, “Love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you” (Matthew 5:44, CEV). Stephen did exactly that. Praying for someone who has hurt us doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt, and it doesn’t mean staying where we’re not safe. It means asking God to be kind to them, and to heal our own hearts too.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Stephen’s story shows us about God and about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning how the church kept spreading the good news after Stephen.</li><li>I can plan to pray for one person who has hurt me or been unkind.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the main events in order, explain that Stephen’s prayer was like Jesus’ prayer on the cross, say that God is forgiving and that the church kept sharing the good news, and recite Acts 7:60.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky notes from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Forgiven and Sent</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Stephen’s story in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Stephen’s story in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Widows are missed in the food line → Seven helpers are chosen → Stephen serves with grace and power → Liars accuse him before the council → Stephen speaks to the council → Stephen sees heaven open and prays for them → The church scatters and keeps sharing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out: serving first, then speaking, then forgiving, then spreading.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 5:44 · Luke 23:34 · Acts 8:4</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 5:44</b> But I tell you to love your enemies and pray for anyone who mistreats you.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 23:34</b> Father, forgive these people! They don’t know what they are doing.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 7:60</b> He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:4</b> The Lord’s followers who had been scattered went from place to place, telling the good news.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 23:34 is Jesus’ prayer on the cross; some old manuscripts leave it out, but the CEV includes it. Stephen’s prayer is like Jesus’ prayer. Acts 8:4 is our best recollection of the CEV wording: check against your own Bible if you want it exact.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Forgiven and sent</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>What does Stephen’s story show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, God is forgiving. Stephen asked God not to blame the people who had hurt him. Where did he learn to pray like that? From Jesus. On the cross, Jesus prayed, ‘Father, forgive these people! They don’t know what they are doing.’ Jesus taught his followers to love their enemies and pray for anyone who was unkind to them. Stephen did what Jesus did, because Jesus’ Spirit was in him. And that’s the good news: God doesn’t wait for us to be perfect. Jesus forgave us at a great cost, so that we can forgive others.</p><p>Second, God is with his people. Stephen saw Jesus standing at God’s right side. He was never alone.</p><p>Third, God is faithful, and nothing can stop his good news. After Stephen, trouble came to the church in Jerusalem. Many believers had to leave their homes, and they scattered over the countryside around Judea and Samaria. The people against the church thought it would disappear. But the opposite happened. Everywhere the believers went, they kept telling people about Jesus. A church that had once met in one city now grew up along many roads and in many villages.</p><p>And that young man, Saul, who looked after the coats? He was one of the people trying to stop the church. But God wasn’t finished with Saul. Soon we’ll meet him again, and his story is a big surprise. Stephen’s forgiveness shows us that nobody is too far away for God’s love.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God is forgiving. · 2. God is with his people. · 3. The church is scattered. · 4. Wherever they go, they share.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Acts 8:1–4: the church scattered, and the believers kept sharing. Saul’s part (8:1, 3) is told calmly and briefly; we don’t describe what he did. Saul becomes Paul in Week 4. Do not say that Stephen’s forgiveness ‘caused’ Saul’s change: say only that God wasn’t finished with Saul, which is true to Acts 9. Teacher note: some students may have family from places where Christians are mistreated today. Reassure them that the church keeps growing, that God is with his people, and that praying for them matters.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Stephen’s last prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In Stephen’s last prayer, what did he ask God to do?<br>A) Punish the people against him &nbsp; B) Not blame them for this sin ✓ &nbsp; C) Send an army to rescue him &nbsp; D) Take him home at once</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, not blame them for this sin (Acts 7:60). Follow-up: ‘Whose prayer on the cross was it like?’ (Jesus’: ‘Father, forgive these people!’) Follow-up 2: ‘Who was Stephen praying for?’ (The people who were against him.) If silent: re-read the memory verse together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Like Jesus</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen’s last prayer was almost the same as Jesus’ prayer on the cross. What does that tell us about who Jesus was to Stephen?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus was his Lord and example; Stephen wanted to be like Jesus; Stephen trusted that Jesus was alive and with him; Jesus’ forgiveness changes how we treat others. Follow-up: ‘What did Stephen see just before he prayed?’ (Jesus standing.) Follow-up 2: ‘How does Jesus’ forgiveness help us forgive?’ If silent: ask who Stephen was looking at when he prayed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Stephen served, spoke about Jesus, and stayed faithful to the end.</li><li>Like Jesus, he prayed for the people who hurt him: God is forgiving.</li><li>The church scattered, but God’s good news kept spreading.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stephen trusted Jesus right to the end, forgave the people who hurt him, and God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Pretend it never hurt?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Forgiving someone means pretending it never hurt.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Stephen’s story is honest about the lies and the hurt. Forgiving means asking God to be kind to the person and not holding it against them; it doesn’t mean pretending it was okay, or staying where we’re not safe. Follow-up: ‘What helps you let go?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who can you tell if someone keeps hurting you?’ (A trusted adult.) If silent: ask what Stephen did with his hurt.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for one person who has hurt you or been unkind, by name, every day this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He knelt down and shouted, ‘Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!’” — Acts 7:60</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the sticky notes from Tuesday: students write the name (or ‘someone who hurt me’) and keep it private. Prayer can be as short as: ‘God, be kind to ___.’ Praying for someone does not mean what they did was okay, and it does not mean staying where we are not safe: if someone is seriously hurting a student, speak to the student privately and follow your school’s process. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He knelt down and shouted, “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!”” — Acts 7:60 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Acts 7:60. Bonus: say Acts 4:20 from Week 1.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Sent out</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Scattered, but still sharing.<br>God’s good news kept spreading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for those who hurt us — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray out loud together, using short sentences. When we pray for someone who hurt us, we can use Stephen’s words: “Lord, don’t blame them.” You can say their name silently. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. Anyone can call out one short sentence in each part. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing about God that Stephen saw, or learned from Jesus.<br>Prompts: God, you are forgiving, and you forgave us first. / God, you are with your people, and you never leave them.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are forgiving, and you are always with us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about a hurt you’ve held on to, or a time you wouldn’t forgive.<br>Prompts: Sorry for wanting to get even. / Thank you that you forgive us, and help us to start again.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for about Stephen’s story.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Stephen, who stayed faithful to the end. / Thank you for Jesus, who prayed for the people who hurt him.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Stephen, and for Jesus, who forgives.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say the name of someone who has hurt you silently in your heart, then pray aloud together.<br>Prompts: Lord, don’t blame them for this sin. Please be kind to them. / Please be close to Christians anywhere who are treated unfairly, and keep your good news spreading.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Lord, don’t blame them. Please be kind to them, and keep your good news spreading.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say the verse together: “Lord, don’t blame them for this sin!” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Names stay silent. Keep mention of persecuted Christians calm and hopeful, with no scary detail. Be aware that some students may have family from places where Christians are mistreated today: let them know their families are being prayed for.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Philip and the Ethiopian — Philip meets an official on a desert road, and a scroll opens up the good news.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 3: Philip and the Ethiopian":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 8:4–8, 26–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Guiding</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends who don’t know Jesus — Philip didn’t argue anyone into following Jesus. He listened to God, went where he was sent, and answered a question. We can do the same for friends who don’t know Jesus yet: say their name to God, ask him to help them find out who Jesus is, and ask him to show us a kind thing to do or say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>“Go South”</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how God sent Philip from a busy city to a quiet desert road.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>“Go South” → Out of the blue → Acts 8:26–31 → A desert road → Where to? → Why not stay? → Go to That Chariot! → Listening for God → Head · Heart · Hands → Go south → Memory verse peek → Praying for friends — stand and go</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Lamb on the Scroll</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what the Ethiopian official was reading and why it puzzled him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Lamb on the Scroll → Recap → Acts 8:32–35 → Starting where he was → Who’s who? → Finish the sentence → Starting where he was → Can we explain everything? → Head · Heart · Hands → The lamb → Build the verse → Praying for friends — write a name</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>“Look, Here’s Water!”</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the Ethiopian official answered the good news about Jesus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>“Look, Here’s Water!” → A fresh start → Acts 8:36–39 → Look, here’s water! → What did he ask? → Look, Here’s Water! → What does baptism show? → Joy that stays → Head · Heart · Hands → Baptised and rejoicing → Verse with actions → Praying for friends — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Who’s Your Chariot?</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what Philip’s story shows us about God, who guides.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Who’s Your Chariot? → Put it in order → Acts 8:35, 39–40 → What it shows → Who did Philip meet? → God guides → This week’s big idea → Is it my job to convince? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Going on his way → Praying for friends — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: “Go South”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 8:4–8, 26–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go South”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends who don’t know Jesus — Philip didn’t argue anyone into following Jesus. He listened to God, went where he was sent, and answered a question. We can do the same for friends who don’t know Jesus yet: say their name to God, ask him to help them find out who Jesus is, and ask him to show us a kind thing to do or say.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God sent Philip from a busy city to a quiet desert road.</li><li>We are learning who Philip met there and what question Philip asked him.</li><li>I can say what Philip did when an angel told him to go (Acts 8:26–31).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students retelling that an angel sent Philip south on the desert road, that Philip met an Ethiopian official reading Isaiah in a chariot, that the Spirit told him to go to the chariot, and that Philip asked, “Do you understand what you are reading?”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a map showing Jerusalem, Gaza and Ethiopia (Kush, south of Egypt)</li><li>Optional: a rolled-up piece of paper as a pretend scroll</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: “Go South”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today we are in the book of Acts again, with Philip, one of the seven helpers from last week.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Out of the blue</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Has someone ever asked you a question out of the blue that you were glad to be asked, like “Do you want to play?” or “Can I help you with that?”</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What made it feel good that they came over?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we heard about Stephen: Faithful to the End, who forgave the people who hurt him. Your challenge: pray for one person who has hurt you or been unkind, by name, each day. How did it go? What was it like to pray for them?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students how praying for a person by name went, and keep it gentle; no need to say who. Then the hook: a new kid being asked to play, someone offering help. Land it: ‘Today God sends a man hurrying down a desert road to ask a stranger a question, and it changes the stranger’s life.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 8:26–31</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:26</b> An angel from the Lord said to Philip, “Go south along the desert road that leads from Jerusalem to Gaza.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:27–28</b> Now an important Ethiopian official, who took care of the treasures of the Queen of Ethiopia, was returning home from Jerusalem, where he had worshiped. He was sitting in his chariot, reading the Book of Isaiah.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:29</b> The Spirit told Philip, “Go to that chariot!”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:30</b> Philip ran up to the chariot and heard the man reading from the Book of Isaiah. Philip asked him, “Do you understand what you are reading?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:31</b> The official answered, “How can I understand unless someone helps me?” He invited Philip to sit beside him.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ethiopia in the Bible means the ancient kingdom of Kush, south of Egypt (in what is now Sudan), not exactly modern Ethiopia. “Queen” is the title Candace, used for the queen mother of Kush. The words are CEV; lines are shortened where the passage is long.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A desert road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Last week we saw the church in Jerusalem scattered after Stephen gave his life. But the believers did not go quiet. Wherever they went, they told people the good news about Jesus. One of them was Philip, one of the seven helpers we met last week. He went north to the region of Samaria and told the people there about Christ. Crowds listened. Sick people were healed. The Bible says there was great joy in that city.</p><p>Now imagine you are Philip. Things are going wonderfully. The crowds are listening and there is so much still to do. And then an angel of the Lord speaks to you. ‘Go south,’ the angel says, ‘along the desert road that leads from Jerusalem to Gaza.’</p><p>South? A desert road? That is not where the crowds are. It is a long, hot, nearly empty track. Philip has no idea who he will find there, or why. But here is what I love. The Bible does not say Philip argued, or asked for a map, or waited for a better plan. It says he got up and went.</p><p>Hour after hour he walked. The sun climbed. The hills turned brown and rocky. And then, far down the road, Philip saw dust, and a chariot rolling along. Inside sat an important man from Ethiopia, a country far to the south of Egypt. He was in charge of all the treasure of the queen of his land. He had travelled for weeks to Jerusalem to worship the God of Israel, and now he was making the long journey home.</p><p>Philip did not know any of that yet. But the Holy Spirit said to him, ‘Go to that chariot!’</p><p>So Philip ran. Sandals slapping, cloak flying, he ran alongside the wheels, and he heard something surprising. The official was reading out loud from a scroll. In those days people often read aloud, and this man was reading from the prophet Isaiah.</p><p>Out of breath, Philip called up to him, ‘Do you understand what you are reading?’</p><p>The man could have said, ‘Mind your own business!’ But he didn’t. He looked down, honest and curious, and said, ‘How can I understand unless someone helps me?’ And he invited Philip to climb up and sit beside him.</p><p>God had known about this man’s questions all along. He had sent Philip down a lonely road for him. And the scroll in the official’s hands was open at a very special page.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Philip is in Samaria. · 2. “Go south.” · 3. Philip gets up and goes. · 4. A chariot. A scroll. A question.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Philip is one of the seven chosen in Acts 6:5; Acts 8:5 says he went to Samaria. The people of Samaria were looked down on by many Jews, so the good news going there is itself a surprise (Acts 1:8). The official’s travel time, the sandals and the cloak are imagined colour. Isaiah is the long prophet book we will see tomorrow.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where to?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which way did the angel tell Philip to go?<br>A) North to the mountains &nbsp; B) South along the desert road ✓ &nbsp; C) West to the sea &nbsp; D) Back to Jerusalem</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, south along the desert road that leads from Jerusalem to Gaza (Acts 8:26). Follow-up: ‘Who did Philip find there?’ (An important Ethiopian official in a chariot.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was the man doing?’ (Reading the book of Isaiah.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why not stay?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Philip was in a city where lots of people were listening. Why do you think he was willing to leave and go to an empty desert road?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he trusted God’s plan; he believed God had someone in mind; he knew God sees what he can’t. Follow-up: ‘What would be hard about leaving a crowd for an empty road?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How do you think Philip knew it was God speaking?’ If silent: ask whether they would rather stay where it’s going well or go where God says.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Go to That Chariot!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Go to That Chariot!</b> (Acts 8:26–31). The angel’s words, the Spirit’s words, Philip’s question and the official’s answer are from Acts 8:26–31 (CEV). The driver, the chatter about the heat, and Philip’s and the official’s other lines are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Angel</b> — Heard, calm and clear — a strong reader<br><b>Philip</b> — Eager and willing — ready to go<br><b>Official</b> — A dignified Ethiopian official — curious and honest<br><b>Driver</b> — The official’s chariot driver — practical and a bit puzzled<br><b>Everyone</b> — The dusty road, the walkers and the runners</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: walk on the spot — “walk, walk, walk!” · Everyone: jog on the spot — “run, run, run!”<br>Props: Optional: a rolled-up paper as the scroll<br>God’s voice here is the angel and the Spirit; use a calm, strong reader for the Angel. Scripture words: Acts 8:26, 29, 30, 31. Everything else is imagined, and the pretend scroll is just a prop. The official’s attitude, polite and open, is what Acts shows us by his answer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Philip has been telling the good news in Samaria. Crowds are listening. Then an angel of the Lord speaks to him.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> Go south along the desert road that leads from Jerusalem to Gaza.</li><li><b>Philip:</b> South? A desert road? There is hardly anyone out there. But God knows what he is doing. <i>(scratch head, then nod)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Walk, walk, walk! Hot sun, dusty road! <i>(walk on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Philip walks for hours. The road is empty. Then, far ahead, a cloud of dust. A chariot is coming.</li><li><b>Driver:</b> Steady, horses. Sir, the road is rough here. Hold on to your scroll. <i>(tug the reins)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> I’m holding it tightly. I have waited a long time for these words. Let me read some more. <i>(unroll the pretend scroll)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The Spirit told Philip, “Go to that chariot!”</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Run, run, run! Catch that chariot! <i>(jog on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Driver:</b> Sir, there is a man running behind us. He is waving. He looks harmless. <i>(look over the shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Philip:</b> Excuse me, sir! I couldn’t help hearing you read. Do you understand what you are reading? <i>(puffing, jogging beside the chariot)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! A stranger has just asked a very personal question. How might the official answer: rudely, or honestly? Why? (20s)</li><li><b>Official:</b> How can I understand unless someone helps me? <i>(put the scroll down, smiling)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> Please, come up and sit beside me. Tell me what you know. <i>(make room on the seat)</i></li><li><b>Philip:</b> Thank you! I would love to. God sent me here for you. <i>(climb up beside him)</i></li><li><b>Driver:</b> Well, I did not expect that. On we go, then. <i>(shake the reins gently)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The wheels roll on. The scroll is open at a very special page.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> God guides! Go where he sends! <i>(all point ahead together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the official’s answer show he was ready to learn?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He didn’t pretend to know it all. He said he needed help, and he invited Philip to sit beside him.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Philip do when the Spirit said, “Go to that chariot”?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He ran straight to it, without arguing, and asked a question.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Listening for God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Philip had to listen to God and then act fast. How might God show someone today what to do next, and how could we tell it’s really from him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: through the Bible, through advice from wise Christians, through a quiet nudge to be kind, through circumstances; check it matches what Jesus teaches and is loving. Follow-up: ‘What might a small nudge feel like?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you ask if you weren’t sure?’ If silent: ask what Philip did right away.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God guides his people, and he cares about every person on every road, even the quiet ones.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, help me listen and be willing to go where you send me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, when you feel a small nudge to be kind to someone, do it straight away, like Philip getting up and going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable: one kind thing, done promptly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Go south</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go south along the desert road.”<br>Philip got up and went.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Acts 8:35. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. The verse is about tomorrow’s story, so tell them they will see it happen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for friends — stand and go</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we are learning to pray like the first Christians. This week’s skill is praying for friends who don’t know Jesus. Philip started with a listening heart. Today, stand and picture one friend by name. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up. Take a slow breath. Think of one friend or family member who doesn’t know Jesus yet. Hold their name in your mind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take one step forward, like Philip getting up and going.<br>Prompts: God, you see every road and every person on it. / God, you guide your people to exactly the right place.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you see every road and every person, and you guide us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stay still. Silently: where have we ignored a nudge from God?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I didn’t listen to you. / Sorry for not caring about a friend who doesn’t know you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we haven’t listened.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take another step forward. Thank God for people who helped you learn about him.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who explained your good news to me. / Thank you for sending Philip to the official.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who told us about Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say your friend’s name in your mind, then ask God to guide them.<br>Prompts: Please help my friend find out who Jesus is. / Please show me one kind thing to do for them this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, guide…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, guide our friends to find Jesus, and show us how to help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take one last step forward, ready to go where God sends you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students think of a friend privately and do not need to say a name aloud. Some students may have family members who have strong views about faith; keep this light and kind, with no pressure on anyone.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what was on the scroll, and how Philip explained the good news about Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Lamb on the Scroll":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 8:4–8, 26–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Lamb on the Scroll</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends who don’t know Jesus — Philip didn’t argue anyone into following Jesus. He listened to God, went where he was sent, and answered a question. We can do the same for friends who don’t know Jesus yet: say their name to God, ask him to help them find out who Jesus is, and ask him to show us a kind thing to do or say.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what the Ethiopian official was reading and why it puzzled him.</li><li>We are learning how Philip explained the good news about Jesus, starting where the man was.</li><li>I can say who the lamb in Isaiah 53 points to.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the official was reading Isaiah, that he asked who the prophet meant, that Philip began right at that place and explained the good news about Jesus, and that Jesus is the Lamb who gave his life for us.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a toy lamb or a picture of a lamb</li><li>Paper and pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Lamb on the Scroll</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday an angel sent Philip south to a desert road, where he ran up to a chariot.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the official doing, and what did Philip ask him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>He was reading the book of Isaiah out loud; Philip asked, ‘Do you understand what you are reading?’ The official answered, ‘How can I understand unless someone helps me?’ Today: ‘We find out what was on the scroll, and how Philip helped him understand.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 8:32–35</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:32</b> The man was reading the passage that said, “He was led like a sheep on its way to be killed. He was silent like a lamb whose wool is being cut off, and he never said a word.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:34</b> The official said to Philip, “Was the prophet talking about himself or about someone else?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:35</b> Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 8:35 is our memory verse. The quote in verse 32 is from Isaiah 53:7. The CEV wording of the Isaiah quotation is given as closely as we can recall.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Starting where he was</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we left Philip jogging beside a chariot in the desert, and an important official from Ethiopia, who had just invited him to climb up and sit beside him. Today they open the scroll together.</p><p>The scroll was a very precious thing. Books in those days were copied by hand, and they cost a fortune. Imagine how much this man must have wanted to know God, to carry something so valuable all the way from Jerusalem. He had come to worship. Perhaps he had often felt like an outsider, a foreigner a very long way from home. But he was reading, and he was searching.</p><p>Here was the part that puzzled him, from the prophet Isaiah. ‘He was led like a sheep on its way to be killed. He was silent like a lamb whose wool is being cut off, and he never said a word.’</p><p>The official turned to Philip. ‘Please tell me,’ he said. ‘Was the prophet talking about himself, or about someone else?’</p><p>What a question! Isaiah had written those words about seven hundred years before. People had wondered for a very long time who they meant. But Philip knew.</p><p>Here is the Bible’s short, wonderful sentence. ‘Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.’</p><p>Notice how Philip started. Not with a long speech. Not with a list of rules. He started right where the man was, with the man’s own scroll and the man’s own question. And then he told him about Jesus.</p><p>Picture Philip pointing at the words. The lamb is Jesus. Jesus lived and healed and taught, and he never did anything wrong. Yet he went quietly and willingly to the cross, like a lamb. He did it for us. Nobody forced him; he loved us and chose it. He took our place, so that our sins could be forgiven and we could be friends with God. The official had seen lambs given as gifts to God in the Jerusalem temple. Now he heard that Jesus is the true Lamb of God, who gave his life once for all.</p><p>And Philip told him the very best part. Jesus did not stay in the tomb. On the third day, God raised him to life. He is alive today, and he welcomes anyone who trusts him, from every nation, near or far.</p><p>Philip did not rush. He answered each question the official asked, and the two men talked as the horses plodded on, the sun slowly sinking. The wheels rolled on across the desert, and the scroll lay open across both men’s knees. The man who had come asking questions was about to find answers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Side by side in the chariot. · 2. “Who is this about?” · 3. A lamb. · 4. Philip begins right there.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Isaiah 53 was written roughly 700 years before Jesus. John the Baptist called Jesus “the Lamb of God” (John 1:29). Keep the cross gentle and reverent: Jesus gave his life, and then rose again. The official’s feelings of being an outsider are imagined; Acts does not say how he felt. If you wish, note that Isaiah 56:3–5 promised God’s welcome to foreigners.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who’s who?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah wrote it. Philip explained it. Match the words on the scroll with what they point to.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>On the scroll</th><th>What it points to</th></tr><tr><td>A lamb, led quietly and without a fight</td><td>Jesus, who chose to give his life for us</td></tr><tr><td>“Was the prophet talking about himself?”</td><td>No: the words point to Jesus</td></tr><tr><td>A scroll written about 700 years earlier</td><td>God’s plan, made long before Jesus came</td></tr><tr><td>“Philip began at this place”</td><td>Starting with the person’s own question</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Isaiah’s lamb is Jesus, and Philip began right where the man was reading.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. If helpful, say that “good news” (gospel) means that God’s own Son took our place and rose again, so we can be forgiven.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Finish the sentence</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about ________.”<br><i>Answer: “…Jesus.” (Acts 8:35)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: Jesus. Follow-up: ‘What book was the official reading?’ (Isaiah.) Follow-up 2: ‘What question did he ask Philip?’ (Whether the prophet was talking about himself or someone else.) If silent: point to the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Starting where he was</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Philip started with the man’s own scroll and question, instead of just giving a speech?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it showed Philip cared about him; it started with something the man already cared about; people listen better when they are asked and heard. Follow-up: ‘What might happen if Philip had lectured him?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could we start a conversation with a friend?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Can we explain everything?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “To tell someone about Jesus, you need to know the answer to every question.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Philip answered one man’s question with one story about Jesus, and that was enough. We can say what Jesus has done for us, and say ‘I don’t know, but let’s find out.’ Follow-up: ‘What is one thing about Jesus you could share?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who could you ask for help?’ If silent: ask what Philip did first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The good news is about Jesus, the Lamb who gave himself for us, and we can share it by starting where a person is.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you for taking my place. Help me tell someone about you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On paper, write one sentence about what Jesus means to you, like “Jesus forgives me.” Keep it in your pocket to use this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students write one honest sentence. It can be very simple. Some students may be new to Christian faith, so keep it open: ‘what you’ve learned about Jesus so far’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The lamb</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“He was silent like a lamb.”<br>Jesus, the Lamb who gave his life for us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Acts 8:35 chunk by chunk, like unrolling a scroll: one phrase at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for friends — write a name</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the name of one friend who doesn’t know Jesus, and keep it private. Philip started with a person’s own question. We can ask God, “What does my friend wonder about?” <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top of a small piece of paper, write one friend’s name. Fold it so only you can see it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you answer the questions people ask. / God, you send people to help us understand.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you answer our questions, and you guide us to Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have we been too shy or too busy to care about a friend’s questions?<br>Prompts: Sorry for ignoring my friend’s questions. / Sorry for being afraid to talk about Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we’ve been too shy or too busy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’re thankful for about Jesus.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, that you were like a lamb for us. / Thank you that you rose again and are alive.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for taking our place and for rising again.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Under the name, write one thing you’re asking God to do for your friend.<br>Prompts: Please help my friend wonder about Jesus. / Please give me the right words, and the right moment.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help our friends find out about Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your folded paper somewhere safe, like in your pocket or Bible. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The paper is private. Students who can’t think of a friend can write the name of anyone they know who doesn’t yet follow Jesus, such as a neighbour or relative. Some may feel awkward; keep it low pressure.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the official sees some water and asks a big question.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: “Look, Here’s Water!”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 8:4–8, 26–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Look, Here’s Water!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends who don’t know Jesus — Philip didn’t argue anyone into following Jesus. He listened to God, went where he was sent, and answered a question. We can do the same for friends who don’t know Jesus yet: say their name to God, ask him to help them find out who Jesus is, and ask him to show us a kind thing to do or say.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the Ethiopian official answered the good news about Jesus.</li><li>We are learning what baptism is and what it shows.</li><li>I can retell what happened at the water and how the official went on his way (Acts 8:36–39).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students saying that the official believed and asked to be baptised, that Philip baptised him in the water, that the Spirit took Philip away, and that the official went on his way full of joy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl of water and a towel to show what baptism looks like</li><li>Optional: a map</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: “Look, Here’s Water!”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: A fresh start</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think about a time you had a fresh start: a new school year, a new haircut, a clean page in your book.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does a fresh start feel like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three answers: a new term, a clean uniform, a new notebook, a clean slate after a mistake. Recap: ‘Yesterday Philip explained the good news about Jesus, the Lamb who gave his life for us. Today we see how the official answered, and he gets a fresh start.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 8:36–39</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:36</b> Further along the road, they came to a place where there was some water. The official said, “Look! Here is some water. Why can’t I be baptized?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:38</b> He ordered the chariot to stop. Then they both went down into the water, and Philip baptized him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:39</b> When they came out of the water, the Lord’s Spirit took Philip away. The official never saw him again, but he was very happy as he continued on his way.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Baptism = being dipped in or covered with water as a sign that you belong to Jesus: washed clean and starting new life with him. Some Bible copies have a verse 37 where the official says he believes; it is not in the oldest manuscripts, so we skip it. The CEV wording is quoted as closely as we can recall.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Look, here’s water!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The chariot rolled on across the desert. Philip had explained the good news about Jesus, the Lamb who gave his life, who rose again, and who welcomes anyone who trusts him. The official was quiet for a while. Then he began asking question after question. Every answer made his face brighter.</p><p>He was not just hearing about Jesus. He was believing in him. Something deep inside him had changed. The questions he had carried all the way from Jerusalem had been answered, and a heavy weight he hadn’t known how to put down was gone. He knew Jesus had taken his place and forgiven him, and he knew he belonged.</p><p>Then, further along the road, they came to a place where there was some water. A small pool, shining in the sun, with palm trees around its edges. It was probably a rare sight in that dry country.</p><p>The official stood up in the chariot and pointed. ‘Look!’ he said. ‘Here is some water. Why can’t I be baptised?’</p><p>Let’s stop for a moment. What is baptism? It is a way of showing everyone that you belong to Jesus. The water is a picture. Going down into the water shows that our old life, with all its sin, is washed away. Coming up again shows new life with Jesus. The water itself is not magic. It is a promise, and a public way of saying, ‘I trust Jesus. I belong to him.’</p><p>The official did not want to wait. He had travelled so far to find God, and now he had found him. So he ordered the chariot to stop, and the driver pulled the horses up in the shade of the palms. Then he and Philip went down together into the water, and Philip baptised him.</p><p>Can you picture it? The sparkling water. The splash. The official coming up with his head thrown back, laughing, with water running off his fine robes.</p><p>When they came out of the water, something astonishing happened. The Lord’s Spirit took Philip away. In a moment he was gone. The official never saw him again. But the Bible says he was very happy as he continued on his way.</p><p>He did not need to see Philip again. He had met Jesus. And Philip? He turned up in a town called Azotus, and then he kept on going, telling the good news in every town all the way to Caesarea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. The chariot rolls on. · 2. “Look! Here is some water.” · 3. “Why can’t I be baptised?” · 4. Down into the water. · 5. He went on his way rejoicing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Acts 8:36–40. Philip’s next stops are Azotus and Caesarea (Acts 8:40). Tradition, not Scripture, says the official took the good news back to his homeland; the Ethiopian Orthodox Church honours this story. Baptism customs vary between churches (immersion, pouring, babies, believers): keep to the simple meaning here and invite students to ask their own church or family. The official’s laughing is imagined colour.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did he ask?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the official say when he saw the water?<br>A) “I’m thirsty. Let’s stop.” &nbsp; B) “Why can’t I be baptized?” ✓ &nbsp; C) “Philip, please come home with me.” &nbsp; D) “This is not on my map.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, ‘Look! Here is some water. Why can’t I be baptized?’ (Acts 8:36). Follow-up: ‘What did he do next?’ (Ordered the chariot to stop.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did he feel at the end?’ (Very happy.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Look, Here’s Water!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Look, Here’s Water!</b> (Acts 8:35–39). The official’s question about baptism, the stopping of the chariot, the going down into the water, and Philip being taken away come from Acts 8:36–39 (CEV). The driver, the official’s other lines and Philip’s other words are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Philip</b> — Warm and joyful<br><b>Official</b> — A dignified Ethiopian official — now full of joy<br><b>Driver</b> — The chariot driver — practical and surprised<br><b>Everyone</b> — The splashing water and the cheering crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: splash the water — wiggle your fingers · Everyone: raise both hands high and say “Praise God!”<br>Props: Optional: a blue scarf or cloth for the pool<br>Scripture words: Acts 8:36, 38, 39. The official’s declaration of trust and joy is imagined framing, though Acts says he was very happy. Keep it joyful, not solemn. If some students have been baptised, invite them to say what they remember, but never pressure those who haven’t.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Hours have passed. Philip has explained the good news about Jesus. The official’s face is bright.</li><li><b>Official:</b> So Jesus is the Lamb Isaiah wrote about. He gave his life for us, and he is alive again?</li><li><b>Philip:</b> Yes! And he welcomes everyone who trusts him, from every nation, near or far.</li><li><b>Official:</b> Then I trust him. I want to belong to Jesus. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Further along the road, they came to a place where there was some water.</li><li><b>Driver:</b> Sir, look! A little pool ahead, with palm trees. Shining water in the desert! <i>(point ahead)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> Look! Here is some water. Why can’t I be baptized? <i>(stand up, pointing)</i></li><li><b>Philip:</b> No reason at all! Jesus welcomes you. Let’s go down to the water together. <i>(smile broadly)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> Driver, stop the chariot!</li><li><b>Driver:</b> Whoa, steady! Stop here, everyone. Quietly now. <i>(pull gently on the reins)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Splash! Splash! Down to the water! <i>(wiggle fingers like water)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The official is about to go down into the water. What do you think baptism shows? What might he be feeling? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They both went down into the water, and Philip baptised him.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Splash! Up he comes! Praise God! <i>(raise both hands high)</i></li><li><b>Official:</b> I feel new! Washed clean! Jesus is my Lord! <i>(laugh, head thrown back)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> When they came out of the water, the Lord’s Spirit took Philip away.</li><li><b>Official:</b> Philip? Where did he go? Thank you, God, for sending him down this road! <i>(look all around)</i></li><li><b>Driver:</b> Shall we go on, sir?</li><li><b>Official:</b> Yes! Drive on! I’m going home full of joy! <i>(raise the pretend scroll)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The official never saw Philip again, but he was very happy as he continued on his way.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Praise God! Jesus is Lord! <i>(hands up, cheer)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the official was so happy, even after Philip had gone?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He didn’t need Philip any more. He had met Jesus himself, was forgiven and belonged to him, and Jesus is always with his people.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the official say when he saw the water?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘Look! Here is some water. Why can’t I be baptized?’ He was eager and didn’t want to wait.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What does baptism show?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you think going down into the water and coming up again shows about following Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: our old life is washed away; new life with Jesus; we belong to him; it’s a public way of saying yes. Follow-up: ‘Why do you think he wanted it done straight away?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Why is it good to say it out loud?’ If silent: remind them that down is the old life and up is new life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Joy that stays</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The official never saw Philip again, but the Bible says he was very happy. What do you think made his joy last?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: he knew Jesus personally; Jesus’ forgiveness doesn’t depend on Philip; God’s Spirit was with him; he had good news to take home. Follow-up: ‘What could he do to keep growing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who helps you keep going?’ If silent: ask what the official had found.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus welcomes anyone who trusts him, and he gives us a fresh start and a joy that doesn’t depend on anyone else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you for welcoming me, and for the joy of belonging to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Share something joyful about Jesus with one person today: a smile, a thank-you, or one honest sentence about what he means to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it open for all students. A smile or kindness counts.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Baptised and rejoicing</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Look! Here is some water!”<br>He went on his way, very happy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Acts 8:35: go, point to the spot, scroll, explain, good news, Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for friends — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Each of you thinks of one friend who doesn’t know Jesus yet. You don’t have to say their name aloud: just say “my friend” if you prefer. Then pray one sentence for your partner’s friend. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Tell them quietly: “I’m thinking of one friend.” You don’t need to say who.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence.<br>Prompts: God, you led the official to water in the desert. / God, you are good at giving people a fresh start.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you give fresh starts, and you find people in the desert!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have we thought a friend wouldn’t care about Jesus?<br>Prompts: Sorry for giving up on a friend too quickly. / Sorry for thinking some people wouldn’t listen.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for giving up on our friends.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who told you about Jesus.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who showed me you are good. / Thank you that the official went on his way full of joy.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for everyone who has told us about Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s friend.<br>Prompts: Please help my partner’s friend to find Jesus. / Please make us kind and ready, like Philip.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help our friends find Jesus, and make us ready.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for your friend. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. Friends may stay unnamed. If a student has no friend in mind, they can pray for a neighbour or someone they don’t know very well.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: what this story shows about God, and your own chariot challenge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Who’s Your Chariot?":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 8:4–8, 26–40</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Who’s Your Chariot?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for friends who don’t know Jesus — Philip didn’t argue anyone into following Jesus. He listened to God, went where he was sent, and answered a question. We can do the same for friends who don’t know Jesus yet: say their name to God, ask him to help them find out who Jesus is, and ask him to show us a kind thing to do or say.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Philip’s story shows us about God, who guides.</li><li>We are learning how to be ready to share Jesus with a friend, with kindness and honesty.</li><li>I can plan one way to pray for a friend and look for a chance to be kind or to say one thing.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the story in order, say that God guided Philip to the official, explain that the good news is about Jesus, say what “your chariot” might mean, and recite Acts 8:35 (or most of it).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Paper from Tuesday (with a friend’s name)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Who’s Your Chariot?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put it in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Philip’s desert journey in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Preaches in Samaria → An angel says “Go south” → Philip walks the desert road → The Spirit says “Go to that chariot” → Philip asks, “Do you understand?” → Reads Isaiah together → Baptised at the water → The official goes home rejoicing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out how God guided Philip at every step.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 8:35, 39–40</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:35</b> Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:39</b> The official never saw him again, but he was very happy as he continued on his way.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 8:40</b> Philip ended up in Azotus. Then he went on to Caesarea, telling the good news in every town along the way.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 8:35 is our memory verse. The CEV wording of verses 39–40 is quoted as closely as we can recall.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: What it shows</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does this story show us about God? Three things.</p><p>First, God guides. He knew about an official on a desert road, and he knew about Philip in a busy city. He put them together at exactly the right time. God still guides people today, through his Word, through wise friends, and through quiet nudges to be kind.</p><p>Second, God cares about one person. Philip left a crowd for a single traveller. To God, no one is too far away or too far from home. The official was a foreigner on a long road, and God went after him.</p><p>Third, the good news is about Jesus. Philip didn’t start with himself. He started with the scroll, and he ended with Jesus, the Lamb who gave his life and rose again, and who welcomes anyone who trusts him.</p><p>Now, what about us? Philip had a chariot. Who might be yours? A friend who has never been to church. A neighbour who is lonely. A new kid at school. The challenge is not to argue anyone into believing. It is to pray for them, and to be ready.</p><p>Picture a Year 6 kid at the school gate, who sees a new student sitting alone with a book. The kid quietly prays, ‘Jesus, is this my chariot?’ Then walks over and says, ‘Want to hang out?’ That’s it. A kind step. And maybe, one day, a chance to say one thing about Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God guides. · 2. God cares about one person. · 3. Jesus is the good news. · 4. Who is your chariot?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. The school-gate kid is imagined. Make sure students know that sharing Jesus is not pushing or arguing: Philip asked a question, listened and answered. Keep it gentle for students whose families hold other faiths or none; kindness and honesty are the point.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who did Philip meet?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who did Philip meet on the desert road?<br>A) A Roman soldier &nbsp; B) An Ethiopian official in a chariot ✓ &nbsp; C) A shepherd with a flock &nbsp; D) The apostle Peter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, an important Ethiopian official in a chariot, who looked after the queen’s treasure (Acts 8:27). Follow-up: ‘What was he reading?’ (Isaiah.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Philip explain to him?’ (The good news about Jesus.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line from Monday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God guides</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God guided Philip step by step to the official. What does that tell us about what God is like?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God cares about individual people; he knows what we don’t; he works behind the scenes; he is patient. Follow-up: ‘What would have happened if Philip had stayed in Samaria?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What small step might God ask us to take?’ If silent: ask what Philip did each time God spoke.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time.</li><li>The good news is about Jesus, the Lamb who gave himself for us and rose again.</li><li>We can be ready: pray for a friend, be kind, and say one honest thing.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God guides ordinary people to the right person at the right time — and when we are ready to listen, go and share, Jesus’ good news reaches people far from home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is it my job to convince?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “It’s my job to make my friends believe in Jesus.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Philip asked, listened and explained; the Spirit did the guiding and the official chose for himself. Our part is to pray, be kind and share honestly. Follow-up: ‘What is our part?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What is God’s part?’ If silent: ask what Philip did, and what the Spirit did.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask Jesus who your “chariot” is. Pray for one friend who doesn’t know him, and look for a chance to be kind or to say one thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the paper from Tuesday if students kept one. Remind them it’s about kindness, prayer and honesty, never pressure. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Philip began at this place in the Scriptures and explained the good news about Jesus.” — Acts 8:35 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Acts 8:35. Bonus: say Acts 7:60 from last week, or last Term’s verses.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Going on his way</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Go where God sends. Say what Jesus did.<br>Very happy, he went on his way.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for friends — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray out loud together for friends who don’t know Jesus. Short and honest is perfect. You can say, “my friend,” instead of a name. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. You don’t need to name anyone.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is, thinking of Philip’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you guide us to the right people. / God, you care about every traveller on every road.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you guide us, and you care about every person!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God where you’ve ignored a nudge to care about a friend.<br>Prompts: Sorry for not noticing people who are alone. / Thank you that you forgive us and give us fresh starts.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the chances we missed.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for about Jesus.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, for taking our place like a lamb. / Thank you for the people who told us about you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for the good news, and for the people who shared it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for your chariot: one friend, and one chance to be kind.<br>Prompts: Please help my friend find out who Jesus is. / Please show me a chance to be kind or say one thing.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, show us our chariots, and help our friends find Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say it together: “God, guide us to the people you love.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers, and let students stay unnamed.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Saul Becomes Paul (and Ananias Says Yes) — a blinding light on the Damascus road, and a frightened believer who says yes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 4: Saul Becomes Paul (and Ananias Says Yes)":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 9:1–31; Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Changes Lives</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for guidance — Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Saul wasn’t looking for directions, but Jesus gave them anyway: “Get up and go into the city.” Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am,” and then did what Jesus asked. God usually guides us through the Bible, through prayer, through wise people and through a quiet nudge to do something kind, and we can ask for it any day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Light on the Damascus Road</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning who Saul was and why he set out for Damascus.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Light on the Damascus Road → Can people change? → Acts 9:1–9 → The light on the road → Whose voice? → “To me” → The Light on the Road → Sure and wrong → Head · Heart · Hands → The voice from the light → Memory verse peek → Praying for guidance — still and listening</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ananias Is Afraid, but Obeys</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who Ananias was and what Jesus asked him to do.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Ananias Is Afraid, but Obeys → Recap → Acts 9:10–15 → Afraid, but obeying → Who he was, who he will be → Which street? → Not told off → Brave means not scared? → Head · Heart · Hands → Straight Street at dawn → Build the verse → Praying for guidance — a “Here I am” note</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>“Brother Saul”</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning what Ananias said and did when he finally met Saul.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>“Brother Saul” → First words → Acts 9:17–19 → “Brother Saul” → What fell away? → “Brother Saul” → A welcome first → Who is the Ananias? → Head · Heart · Hands → Saul can see → Verse with actions → Praying for guidance — stand up and say it</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Life Changed</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Saul began to tell people about Jesus, and how Barnabas welcomed him.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Life Changed → Who said it? → Acts 9:27 · Galatians 2:20 → A new friend, a new life → Who spoke up? → Standing up for Saul → This week’s big idea → What changed? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Welcome, Saul → Praying for guidance — out loud together</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Light on the Damascus Road":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 9:1–31; Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Light on the Damascus Road</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for guidance — Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Saul wasn’t looking for directions, but Jesus gave them anyway: “Get up and go into the city.” Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am,” and then did what Jesus asked. God usually guides us through the Bible, through prayer, through wise people and through a quiet nudge to do something kind, and we can ask for it any day.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Saul was and why he set out for Damascus.</li><li>We are learning what happened when Jesus stopped Saul on the road.</li><li>I can retell what Jesus said to Saul and explain why it surprised him.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Saul was hunting followers of Jesus, that a bright light and the voice of Jesus stopped him, that Jesus said “why are you so cruel to me”, and that Saul was left blind.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch for the light in the role-play</li><li>Optional: a scarf to tie loosely over Saul’s eyes after the light</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Light on the Damascus Road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week is about a very big change in one man, and about a very ordinary man who helped it happen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Can people change?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Can a person ever really change? Think of someone who stopped a bad habit, or who went from grumpy to kind. What do you think it took?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would it take to change someone who was completely against something?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Philip and the Ethiopian official, and how Philip ran up to the chariot to share the good news about Jesus. Your challenge: ask Jesus who your “chariot” is, pray for one friend who doesn’t know him, and look for a chance to be kind or to say one thing. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students who they prayed for or what kind thing they did, and keep it light; some may not have managed it, and that’s fine. Then the hook: take three or four ideas (a good friend, a hard lesson, time, a big surprise). Land it: ‘Today we meet the most surprising change in the whole book of Acts, and it was not a small one.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 9:1–9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:1</b> Saul kept on threatening to kill the Lord’s followers.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:3</b> When Saul had almost reached Damascus, a bright light from heaven suddenly flashed around him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:4</b> He fell to the ground and heard a voice that said, “Saul! Saul! Why are you so cruel to me?”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:5</b> “Who are you?” Saul asked. “I am Jesus,” the Lord answered. “I am the one you are so cruel to.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:6</b> “Now get up and go into the city, where you will be told what to do.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:9</b> For three days Saul was blind, and he did not eat or drink.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Most Bibles say “Why do you persecute me?” where the CEV says “Why are you so cruel to me?” To persecute means to treat people cruelly because of what they believe. Say it plainly and calmly. The CEV spells “Savior” the American way, but we write Saviour in our own words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The light on the road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Meet Saul. He was clever, well taught and very, very sure of himself. Saul believed he was serving God, and he believed that the followers of Jesus were wrong, because they said that Jesus was God’s Son and that he was alive again.</p><p>You might remember Stephen from earlier in Acts, who gave his life trusting Jesus. Saul had been there, and he had agreed with it. After that, Saul went from house to house in Jerusalem, arresting believers and putting them in prison. Many of them left the city. That is called persecution: treating people cruelly because of what they believe.</p><p>But Saul wasn’t finished. ‘There are believers in Damascus too,’ he said. So he went to the high priest, asked for official letters, and set off on the long road north. It was about a week’s walk, and some men travelled with him.</p><p>It was midday. The sun was high and hot. The road was dusty and quiet. Saul was nearly at Damascus, planning what he would do when he got there.</p><p>And then, a light. Not a lamp, not lightning, but a light from heaven, brighter than the midday sun, flashing all around him. Saul fell flat on the ground. His men stumbled back, frightened.</p><p>Then Saul heard a voice. ‘Saul! Saul! Why are you so cruel to me?’</p><p>Saul’s heart thudded. ‘Who are you?’ he asked.</p><p>And the voice answered, ‘I am Jesus. I am the one you are so cruel to.’</p><p>Jesus! The man Saul had been fighting against wasn’t gone. He was alive, and he was speaking to Saul. Notice that Jesus didn’t say, ‘Why are you cruel to my followers?’ He said, ‘to me’. Jesus is so close to the people who trust him that when they are hurt, he is hurt too.</p><p>But Jesus wasn’t finished. ‘Now get up and go into the city, where you will be told what to do.’</p><p>Saul’s men stood there speechless. They had heard a voice, but they couldn’t see anyone. Slowly Saul stood up. He opened his eyes, and he couldn’t see a thing. The proud man who had marched out of Jerusalem now had to be led into Damascus by the hand, like a small child.</p><p>For three days, Saul was blind. He didn’t eat. He didn’t drink. He waited in the dark for whatever Jesus would say next.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. Saul is sure he is serving God. · 2. A week’s walk to Damascus. · 3. A light from heaven. · 4. “Saul! Saul! Why are you so cruel to me?” · 5. “I am Jesus.” · 6. Led by the hand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Sensitive: Stephen’s death is implied only (“gave his life trusting Jesus”); do not describe how he died. Persecution is explained plainly and calmly. Some students may have family from places where Christians are still persecuted, so keep the tone steady and put the focus on Jesus’ power and mercy, not on the harm. Imagined details: the length of the walk (roughly a week), Saul’s planning, and the heat of noon (Acts 26:13 says the light was brighter than the sun at midday). Acts 9:7 says the men heard the voice but saw no one; Acts 22:9 describes it from another angle, so don’t get tangled up in that.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Whose voice?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Whose voice did Saul hear on the road to Damascus?<br>A) An angel &nbsp; B) Peter &nbsp; C) Jesus ✓ &nbsp; D) The high priest</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: C, Jesus (“I am Jesus”, Acts 9:5). Follow-up: ‘What did Jesus ask Saul?’ (Why are you so cruel to me?) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the men travelling with Saul see and hear?’ (A voice, but they saw no one.) If silent: re-read the fourth scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: “To me”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus said, “Why are you so cruel to me?” He didn’t say, “to my followers.” What does that tell us about how Jesus feels about the people who trust him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus is very close to his people; what hurts them hurts him; he is alive and cares; they are not alone. Follow-up: ‘How do you think Saul felt hearing that?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does it tell us about how Jesus wants us to treat other people?’ If silent: ask how it feels when someone is unkind to your best friend, and whether it feels like it was done to you too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Light on the Road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Light on the Road</b> (Acts 9:1–9). The words of Jesus and the narrator’s lines about the light, the speechless men and the blindness follow Acts 9:3–9 (CEV). The high priest’s words, the guards’ chatter and Saul’s thoughts are imagined. Saul’s question “What should I do, Lord?” is how Saul later told the story in Acts 22:10.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Saul</b> — Clever, certain, and about to be stopped<br><b>High Priest</b> — Gives Saul his letters (imagined)<br><b>Guard 1</b> — Travelling with Saul (imagined)<br><b>Guard 2</b> — Travelling with Saul (imagined)<br><b>Jesus</b> — Heard, never seen — a calm, strong voice from the light<br><b>Everyone</b> — Marching feet, then a flash of light</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: march on the spot, left, right, left, right · Everyone: shield your eyes and gasp when the light flashes<br>Props: Optional: a torch for the light · Optional: a scarf to tie loosely over Saul’s eyes<br>Jesus’ lines are the CEV words of Acts 9:4–6. Jesus is never acted: choose a calm reader who stands at the back of the room, so the voice comes from behind the class. Use the torch for the flash if you have one. Do not play up the violence of the persecution; the point is Jesus stopping Saul. Some students may have family from places where Christians are persecuted today, so keep the mood steady and respectful.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Jerusalem. Saul stands before the high priest, certain that he is doing God’s work.</li><li><b>Saul:</b> Give me letters to the Jewish leaders in Damascus. I will find any followers of Jesus there. <i>(stand tall)</i></li><li><b>High Priest:</b> You have my letters, Saul. Take these guards and go quickly. <i>(hand over a scroll)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Saul set off on the long road north to Damascus. It was about a week’s walk.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Left, right, left, right… <i>(march on the spot)</i></li><li><b>Guard 1:</b> Saul walks fast! My feet are sore already. <i>(limp a little)</i></li><li><b>Guard 2:</b> He won’t even stop for a rest, and it’s nearly midday. <i>(wipe your forehead)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Not far now. Damascus is just ahead. We will stop this new teaching for good. <i>(point down the road)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> It is midday. The sun is high. And then…</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whooosh! <i>(gasp and shield your eyes, a flash of brilliant light)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Aaah! What is that? I can’t… I can’t… <i>(fall to the ground)</i></li><li><b>Guard 1:</b> Did you hear that? Someone is speaking! <i>(spin round, wide-eyed)</i></li><li><b>Guard 2:</b> I can’t see anyone. There’s no one there! <i>(look all around)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Saul! Saul! Why are you so cruel to me? <i>(a calm, strong voice)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Who are you? <i>(tremble)</i></li><li><b>Jesus:</b> I am Jesus. I am the one you are so cruel to.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Saul was sure he knew who Jesus was, and he was wrong. What do you think is going through his head right now? (20s)</li><li><b>Jesus:</b> Now get up and go into the city, where you will be told what to do.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The men with Saul stood speechless. They had heard a voice, but they had not seen anyone.</li><li><b>Saul:</b> I can’t see. I can’t see a thing! Please, someone take my hand. <i>(feel around in front of you)</i></li><li><b>Guard 1:</b> Come on, Saul. I’ve got you. Step by step. <i>(take his hand)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> They led Saul into Damascus. For three days he was blind, and he did not eat or drink.</li><li><b>Saul:</b> What should I do, Lord? <i>(quietly, head bowed)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhh… Listen. <i>(everyone goes completely still)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Jesus say to Saul, and what does it tell us about who Jesus is?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He said ‘Why are you so cruel to me?’ and ‘I am Jesus.’ He is alive, he speaks, and he is so close to his people that he takes what is done to them personally.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to Saul’s eyes, and who led him into the city?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was blind for three days, and his men led him by the hand. The proud man had to be helped.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Sure and wrong</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Saul was completely sure he was serving God, and he was completely wrong. How can someone be very sure and still be wrong? How could he have checked?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: being sure is a feeling and not proof; he didn’t listen to the people he was against; he didn’t check what God had really said or done; pride; only Jesus himself could turn him round. Follow-up: ‘When were you very sure and then found out you were wrong?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps us stay open to being corrected?’ If silent: ask about a sports ruling someone was certain about until they saw the replay.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus is alive, he speaks, and he can stop anyone in their tracks and turn them around.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, if I’m going the wrong way anywhere, please show me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight before you fall asleep, ask Jesus one honest question: “Is there anywhere I’m going the wrong way?” Then wait quietly for ten seconds.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep this gentle. Students are not expected to hear a voice; the point is to ask honestly and listen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The voice from the light</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Saul! Saul! Why are you so cruel to me?”<br>“I am Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Galatians 2:20 (second half). Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Tell them Paul wrote it years later, remembering the day Jesus changed his life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for guidance — still and listening</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Be still for a moment, like Saul on the road, and listen for a gentle nudge. <i>(silent)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit still. Close your eyes or look at the floor. Imagine Saul in the dust of the road, stopped and listening. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about the light on the road, and the voice that stopped Saul in his tracks.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you are brighter than the midday sun. / Jesus, you are alive, and you can stop anyone and turn them around.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus, you are alive, and you are brighter than the sun!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: Saul was sure he was right, and he was wrong. Is there anywhere you were so sure that you stopped listening?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I was cruel or unkind to someone. / Sorry for the times I was so sure I was right that I wouldn’t listen.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, we’re sorry for the times we were unkind, or so sure we were right that we didn’t listen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus that he went after Saul instead of giving up on him.<br>Prompts: Thank you, Jesus, that you don’t give up on people who are going the wrong way. / Thank you that you forgive us and turn us around too.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, that you never give up on us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask Jesus for guidance: which way should I go today?<br>Prompts: Please show me the right way to go today, at school and at home. / Please help me to listen when you speak, through the Bible and through wise people.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, show me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Jesus, show us the way, and help us to listen.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit in silence for ten more seconds and just listen. Then everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model one short sentence first. Be clear that Saul’s meeting with Jesus was a one-off; God usually guides us through the Bible, prayer, wise people and a quiet nudge, not a flash of light. Some students may follow other faiths or have family who do; keep the tone welcoming and invite them to listen quietly.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Ananias, an ordinary believer in Damascus, hears Jesus tell him to visit the very man he is afraid of.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Ananias Is Afraid, but Obeys":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 9:1–31; Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ananias Is Afraid, but Obeys</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for guidance — Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Saul wasn’t looking for directions, but Jesus gave them anyway: “Get up and go into the city.” Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am,” and then did what Jesus asked. God usually guides us through the Bible, through prayer, through wise people and through a quiet nudge to do something kind, and we can ask for it any day.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Ananias was and what Jesus asked him to do.</li><li>We are learning that Ananias was afraid, told Jesus so, and obeyed anyway.</li><li>I can explain why saying yes while you are scared takes real courage.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming Ananias as an ordinary follower of Jesus in Damascus, saying why he was afraid of Saul, and explaining that Jesus listened to his fear and still asked him to go.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Ananias Is Afraid, but Obeys</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday a bright light stopped Saul on the road to Damascus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who spoke to Saul, what did he say, and what happened to Saul’s eyes?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus spoke; he said ‘I am Jesus, the one you are so cruel to’ and told Saul to go into the city; Saul was blind for three days and had to be led by the hand. ‘Today we leave Saul waiting in the dark and meet a man in Damascus who is about to be asked to do something very scary.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 9:10–15</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:10</b> In Damascus there was a follower named Ananias. The Lord spoke to him in a vision, and Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:11</b> “Get up and go to Straight Street. Find the house of Judas and ask for a man named Saul. He is there, praying.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:13</b> “Lord, many people have told me about the terrible things this man has done to your followers in Jerusalem.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:15</b> “Go! I have chosen him to tell foreigners, kings, and the people of Israel about me.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A vision is like a very clear dream while you are awake. The Judas on Straight Street is not Judas Iscariot; it was a common name. Ananias is a different man from the Ananias in Acts 5.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Afraid, but obeying</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s move to Damascus, to a house on Straight Street. It belongs to a man named Judas, and inside, Saul is sitting in the dark. He can’t see. He hasn’t eaten or had a drink for three days. All he can do is pray. And as he prays, God shows him a picture: a man named Ananias walking in and putting his hands on him so that he can see again.</p><p>Now meet Ananias. He wasn’t an apostle. He wasn’t famous. The Bible says only that he was a follower of Jesus who lived in Damascus. Maybe he was a baker or a carpenter or a dad with a family. And one night, as he prayed, the Lord spoke to him in a vision, which is like a very clear dream while you’re awake.</p><p>‘Ananias!’</p><p>‘Lord, here I am,’ said Ananias.</p><p>Then Jesus gave him his instructions. ‘Get up and go to Straight Street. Find the house of Judas, and ask for a man named Saul. He is there, praying.’</p><p>Saul?! Ananias’s stomach must have dropped. Everyone in his church had heard about Saul. Believers had come running from Jerusalem and told their stories. Saul had come to Damascus to arrest people exactly like Ananias.</p><p>And Ananias did something brave. He told Jesus the truth. ‘Lord, many people have told me about the terrible things this man has done to your followers in Jerusalem.’ He was honest about being afraid. And notice what Jesus did: he didn’t tell him off. Jesus listened.</p><p>Then Jesus answered. ‘Go! I have chosen him to tell foreigners, kings, and the people of Israel about me.’ Ananias saw who Saul had been. Jesus saw who Saul was going to be. Jesus also said that Saul would face hard times because of Jesus’ name. Following Jesus isn’t always easy, but Jesus wasn’t going to leave Saul alone.</p><p>What would you have done? Here is what Ananias did. He got up. The Bible doesn’t tell us how his legs felt, but we can guess. We can picture him walking down that long street early in the morning, past shopkeepers opening up, one step at a time, afraid and obeying. Because Ananias knew something: when Jesus gives you a job, he goes with you.</p><p>That is what real courage looks like. It isn’t having no fear. It is saying yes to Jesus while you are afraid. And notice what this shows us about Jesus. He was the only one who could change Saul, and he chose to do it using an ordinary man who said yes.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Saul waits, blind and praying. · 2. The Lord speaks to Ananias. · 3. “But Lord… I’ve heard about this man.” · 4. “Go! I have chosen him.” · 5. Ananias gets up and goes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Imagined: Ananias’s job, his family, the stories the refugees told, the early morning walk and his shaky legs. The Bible says only that Ananias was a follower of Jesus in Damascus, that he had heard about Saul, and that he went. Acts 9:16 says Jesus would show Saul how much he must suffer for his name; keep that calm and brief: following Jesus can be costly, and Jesus is with his people. Ananias is commonly understood to mean “the Lord has been gracious”, which is a lovely thing to say aloud.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who he was, who he will be</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ananias had a worry. Jesus had an answer. Match what Ananias said with how Jesus replied.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Ananias said…</th><th>Jesus said…</th></tr><tr><td>“Many people have told me about the terrible things this man has done.”</td><td>“Go! I have chosen him.”</td></tr><tr><td>“He has come to arrest those who worship in your name.”</td><td>“He is there, praying.”</td></tr><tr><td>Ananias saw who Saul had been</td><td>Jesus saw who Saul would become</td></tr><tr><td>Ananias was afraid, and said so</td><td>Jesus listened, and still asked him to go</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ananias saw who Saul had been. Jesus saw who Saul would be, and asked Ananias to be part of it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Row 2’s left side is Ananias’s worry from Acts 9:14, in our words; it is not quoted directly. After the last row ask: ‘What was the same in Ananias’s heart at the start and at the end?’ (He was afraid at both, but he trusted Jesus enough to go.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which street?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Get up and go to ________ Street. Find the house of Judas and ask for a man named Saul.”<br><i>Answer: “…Straight Street.” (Acts 9:11)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: Straight. Follow-up: ‘What was Saul doing in that house?’ (Praying.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Ananias say when the Lord first called his name?’ (Lord, here I am.) If silent: point to the second scripture line and read it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not told off</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ananias told Jesus he was afraid. Why do you think Jesus didn’t tell him off for it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus knows our hearts already; honesty is better than pretending; Ananias still obeyed; Jesus is kind and patient with scared people. Follow-up: ‘What does that tell us about what we can say to God?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Jesus give Ananias to help him go?’ (A reason: Saul was chosen.) If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Brave means not scared?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If God asks you to do something, you shouldn’t feel afraid.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Ananias was afraid and obeyed, and Jesus didn’t scold him. Courage is going while you’re afraid. Follow-up: ‘What does being brave look like for a kid your age?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who helped Ananias be brave?’ (Jesus: he was sent by the Lord, who goes with him.) If silent: ask for one time they did something scary and it went well.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ananias was afraid, but he said yes to Jesus anyway, and Jesus used him to welcome a man who was going to change the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I get scared sometimes. I’m going to tell you honestly, and I’m still willing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write on a sticky note one thing you’re nervous about this week. Tell Jesus about it, then ask: “Is there anything you want me to do?” Keep the note somewhere you’ll see it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out sticky notes. Students keep their notes private. Help students pick something small: a new group, a hard conversation, a trial or test. This leads into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Straight Street at dawn</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Afraid, and obeying.<br>“Lord, here I am.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Galatians 2:20 (second half) chunk by chunk, like building a house: one chunk at a time. Remind students that Paul wrote it years after Damascus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for guidance — a “Here I am” note</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a “Here I am” note to God, the way Ananias answered him. Say you’re ready to listen, tell him honestly what you’re afraid of, and ask what to do next. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and a small piece of paper. At the top, write: “Lord, here I am.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who Jesus is.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you saw who Saul would become, not only who he had been. / Jesus, you know every street and every person in every city.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, you see who people can become, and you know everyone by name.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: think of a time you decided someone could never change, or hid a fear from God.<br>Prompts: Sorry for deciding someone could never change. / Sorry for hiding my fears from you instead of telling you the truth.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for deciding that people can’t change.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank-you for someone who was kind to you when you were new, or gave you a chance.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who were kind to me when I was new. / Thank you that you listen when I tell you I’m afraid.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you listen to us when we are afraid.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’re nervous about and ask Jesus what he wants you to do.<br>Prompts: Please give me the courage to say yes when you ask me to do something hard. / Please show me what to do next about the thing I’m nervous about.<br>Starter: <b>Please show me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, Jesus, show us what to do, and give us courage to say yes.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your note and keep it in your pocket or bag. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their notes private. If a student writes about a worry that concerns you, check in quietly afterwards. Remind them that guidance usually comes through the Bible, wise people and a quiet nudge, and that they can always check with a trusted adult.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Ananias knocks on the door of the house on Straight Street, and the first words he says to Saul are a welcome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: “Brother Saul”":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 9:1–31; Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Brother Saul”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for guidance — Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Saul wasn’t looking for directions, but Jesus gave them anyway: “Get up and go into the city.” Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am,” and then did what Jesus asked. God usually guides us through the Bible, through prayer, through wise people and through a quiet nudge to do something kind, and we can ask for it any day.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Ananias said and did when he finally met Saul.</li><li>We are learning how Saul’s sight, baptism and new family showed what Jesus can do.</li><li>I can explain why the first words Ananias said to Saul matter.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Ananias laid hands on Saul and called him a friend or brother, that Saul could see again, was baptised and ate, and that Jesus used an ordinary man’s welcome to start Saul’s new life.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl of water for the baptism moment</li><li>Optional: a torch</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: “Brother Saul”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: First words</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine you walk into a room where you’re afraid nobody wants you. What are the first words you’d hope to hear?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would those words do for you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three answers: ‘Come and sit with us’, ‘We’re glad you’re here’, a name. Recap: ‘Yesterday Ananias was afraid but said yes to Jesus and set off down Straight Street. Today he knocks on the door, and we hear the first words he says to the man he feared.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 9:17–19</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:17</b> Ananias went to the house where Saul was staying. He placed his hands on him and said, “Saul, my friend, the Lord Jesus has sent me.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:17</b> “He wants you to be able to see again and to be filled with the Holy Spirit.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:18</b> Suddenly something like fish scales fell from Saul’s eyes, and he could see. He got up and was baptized.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:19</b> Afterwards, he ate and felt much stronger.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The CEV says “Saul, my friend”; many other Bibles say “Brother Saul”. Both show a welcome. The Holy Spirit is God’s own presence, living in and helping everyone who trusts Jesus. Baptism is being washed with water as a sign of belonging to Jesus and having a fresh start.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: “Brother Saul”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ananias is on Straight Street. He finds the house of Judas, takes a deep breath and knocks. A servant opens the door and leads him inside to a back room, and there sits Saul.</p><p>If you had seen Saul a week ago, you would have seen a man who stormed out of Jerusalem with letters and guards. Now he is sitting on the edge of a bed, thin and tired, his eyes closed, his hands still. Three days without food or drink.</p><p>Ananias’s heart is thumping. This is the man he was afraid of. He could have turned around right at the door. But he has said yes to Jesus. So he walks across the room, puts his hands gently on Saul, and says words that nobody expected.</p><p>‘Saul, my friend.’ Many Bibles say, ‘Brother Saul.’ Brother! Not enemy. The first thing Ananias said to the man who had come to arrest people like him was a welcome. Then he said, ‘The Lord Jesus has sent me. He is the one who appeared to you along the road. He wants you to be able to see again, and to be filled with the Holy Spirit.’ The Holy Spirit is God’s own presence, living in and helping everyone who trusts Jesus.</p><p>And suddenly, something like fish scales fell from Saul’s eyes. He blinked. Light poured in. Colours, shapes, and a kind face looking at him. Saul could see!</p><p>But look at what Saul did first. He didn’t rush off to tell everyone. He got up and was baptised. Baptism is being washed with water as a sign that you belong to Jesus and have a fresh start. Then he ate some food, and he felt strong again.</p><p>Think about everything that changed in one week. Saul went from hunting believers to being welcomed by one. From ‘I’m sure I’m right’ to ‘Who are you, Lord?’ From alone to part of a family.</p><p>And think about Ananias. He is in the Bible for only a few sentences. But because he said yes while he was afraid, Saul heard a welcome instead of a warning. Ananias didn’t have to be powerful. He had to be willing. And that is exactly who Jesus asks to do some of his biggest jobs.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A door on Straight Street. · 2. “Saul, my friend…” · 3. Something like fish scales falls away. · 4. Baptised. · 5. He eats. He is strong again.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Imagined: the servant at the door, the thin and tired look, and Ananias’s thumping heart. Acts says only that Ananias went to the house, laid his hands on Saul, spoke, and that Saul could see, was baptised and ate. The fish scales detail is from the text (“something like fish scales”). Keep the baptism simple: a sign of belonging to Jesus, not a magic act. Some students come from families with different baptism customs; leave that for another day.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What fell away?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the Bible say fell from Saul’s eyes?<br>A) Dust from the road &nbsp; B) Something like fish scales ✓ &nbsp; C) Tears of sadness &nbsp; D) A blindfold</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, something like fish scales (Acts 9:18). Follow-up: ‘What did Saul do right after he could see?’ (He got up and was baptised.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he do after that?’ (He ate and felt stronger.) If silent: re-read the third scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: “Brother Saul”</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>“Brother Saul”</b> (Acts 9:17–19). Ananias’s words to Saul and the narrator’s lines about the fish scales, baptism and food follow Acts 9:17–19 (CEV). Judas’s welcome, the neighbour’s worry and Saul’s replies are imagined. “Brother Saul” is how many Bibles translate Ananias’s greeting.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Ananias</b> — Afraid, but willing, and kind<br><b>Saul</b> — Blind, tired and humbled<br><b>Judas</b> — The host whose house is on Straight Street (imagined)<br><b>Neighbour</b> — A worried friend who came along (imagined)<br><b>Everyone</b> — Knocks, gasps and a big welcome</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: knock, knock, knock on your desk · Everyone: gasp and shade your eyes when the light floods in<br>Props: Optional: a scarf to tie loosely over Saul’s eyes · Optional: a small bowl of water<br>Ananias’s speech is the CEV text of Acts 9:17. The pause lets students put themselves in Ananias’s shoes. Keep Saul humble, not grovelling. Judas, the neighbour and their lines are imagined. If you have a bowl of water, keep the baptism moment short and gentle; it is a sign, not a ritual to be copied exactly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Straight Street, Damascus. Ananias stands outside the house of Judas, his heart thumping.</li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> Ananias, are you sure? That’s Saul. He came to arrest people like us. <i>(whisper, wide-eyed)</i></li><li><b>Ananias:</b> I’m scared. I told Jesus so. But he said, ‘Go!’ So I’m going. <i>(deep breath)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Knock, knock, knock. <i>(knock softly on desks)</i></li><li><b>Judas:</b> Hello? If you’re looking for Saul, he’s in the back room. He hasn’t eaten for three days. <i>(open the door a crack)</i></li><li><b>Ananias:</b> I’m Ananias. The Lord sent me. <i>(step inside)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Saul sat on the edge of the bed, blind, his head bowed.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Ananias is standing right in front of the man he feared. What is the first thing you would say? (20s)</li><li><b>Ananias:</b> Saul, my friend. Brother Saul. <i>(lay hands gently on his shoulders)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Who… who is it? Who is there? <i>(turn toward the voice)</i></li><li><b>Ananias:</b> The Lord Jesus has sent me. He is the one who appeared to you along the road.</li><li><b>Ananias:</b> He wants you to be able to see again, and to be filled with the Holy Spirit.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Suddenly something like fish scales fell from Saul’s eyes, and he could see.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ahhh! <i>(gasp and shade your eyes as the light floods in)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> I can see! I can see you! Thank you, Ananias. <i>(blink at your hands, then at him)</i></li><li><b>Ananias:</b> Thank Jesus. He is the one who sent me.</li><li><b>Judas:</b> There is water here, if you want to be baptised. And food too. You must be starving. <i>(bring the bowl)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Yes. I want to belong to Jesus. <i>(stand up slowly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Saul got up and was baptized. Afterwards, he ate and felt much stronger.</li><li><b>Neighbour:</b> I was wrong to doubt. Welcome to the family, Brother Saul. <i>(shake his hand)</i></li><li><b>Saul:</b> Brother. Nobody has called me that in a very long time. Thank you. <i>(quietly, moved)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Welcome, Brother Saul! <i>(arms wide, big smiles)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The man who came to Damascus to arrest believers stayed with them instead.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the first thing Ananias said to Saul, and why does that matter?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He called him a friend, or brother. He welcomed him before he did anything else. A welcome is how Saul’s new life began.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What happened to Saul after the scales fell away?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He could see, he got up and was baptised, he ate and felt stronger, and he stayed with the believers in Damascus.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A welcome first</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">On your whiteboard, write one reason why Ananias’s first words to Saul were so important for Saul’s new life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Saul would have expected anger; the welcome showed Jesus’ forgiveness before Saul had said a word; it made him part of the family; Ananias took a risk. Follow-up: ‘How would Saul have felt if Ananias had started with a lecture?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about how Jesus welcomes us?’ If silent: ask what Saul might have said if he’d been greeted with ‘You’re the one who arrested my friends.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is the Ananias?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus could have fixed Saul by himself. Why do you think he asked an ordinary man to go to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: Jesus works through people; Saul needed a human welcome and a family; Ananias’s yes showed trust; Jesus likes to include us in his work. Follow-up: ‘What did Ananias risk?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who has been an Ananias to you?’ If silent: ask what would have been different if Ananias had stayed home.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus changes people, and he often uses ordinary people who say yes, like Ananias, to welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, make me someone who welcomes, not someone who stays away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, say hello to someone who is often on their own, at lunch or on the field. Your first words can be as simple as “Want to join us?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and safe: a smile, a hello, a seat. Remind students that if someone makes them feel unsafe, they should tell a teacher. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Saul can see</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Saul, my friend, the Lord Jesus has sent me.”<br>Hands on. A welcome first.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Galatians 2:20: chest, hands together, point up, heart, arms wide, point to self. Keep the arms-wide action reverent.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for guidance — stand up and say it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up and pray, “Here I am, Lord”, the way Ananias answered God. Standing shows we are ready to listen and to do whatever he asks. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone stand, feet planted, hands open by your sides. Say it quietly with me: “Here I am, Lord.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look up. Think about Jesus, who spoke to Ananias and sees every person in every city.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you know every person by name and every street in every city. / Jesus, you can turn an enemy into a brother.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, Jesus, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, Jesus, you turn enemies into brothers!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: is there anyone you’ve left out, or been unwelcoming to, at school or on the field?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t welcomed someone new. / Sorry for ignoring a nudge to be kind.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we left someone out.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank Jesus for the Ananias people in your life, the ones who welcomed you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who welcomed me when I felt like an outsider. / Thank you that Saul got a new start, and that we can too.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, Jesus, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, Jesus, for the people who welcome us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask for ears to hear and the courage to say yes.<br>Prompts: Please help me notice when you nudge me to welcome or help someone. / Please make me brave enough to say yes, like Ananias.<br>Starter: <b>Please, Jesus, help me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Here I am, Lord. Please help us notice, and help us say yes.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone together: “Here I am, Lord.” Amen. Then sit down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students who prefer not to stand can sit with open hands; there is no pressure. Model one short “Here I am, Lord” prayer yourself first.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Saul tells everyone about Jesus, escapes over the wall in a basket, and Barnabas welcomes him in Jerusalem.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: A Life Changed":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 9:1–31; Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Life Changed</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for guidance — Praying for guidance means asking God, “Which way should I go?” and then listening. Saul wasn’t looking for directions, but Jesus gave them anyway: “Get up and go into the city.” Ananias answered, “Lord, here I am,” and then did what Jesus asked. God usually guides us through the Bible, through prayer, through wise people and through a quiet nudge to do something kind, and we can ask for it any day.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Saul began to tell people about Jesus, and how Barnabas welcomed him.</li><li>We are learning what this story shows us about God: Jesus changes lives.</li><li>I can choose one small way to be an Ananias this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the week (the light, Ananias’s yes, the welcome, the basket, Barnabas), explain that Jesus changes lives and often uses ordinary people, and say the second half of Galatians 2:20.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Sticky notes from Tuesday</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Life Changed</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Who are you?” — <b>Saul</b><br>“I am Jesus. I am the one you are so cruel to.” — <b>Jesus</b><br>“Lord, here I am.” — <b>Ananias</b><br>“Lord, many people have told me about the terrible things this man has done.” — <b>Ananias</b><br>“Go! I have chosen him to tell foreigners, kings, and the people of Israel about me.” — <b>Jesus</b><br>“Saul, my friend, the Lord Jesus has sent me.” — <b>Ananias</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Which day was that from, Monday, Tuesday or Wednesday?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 9:27 · Galatians 2:20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 9:27</b> Then Barnabas helped him by taking him to the apostles.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Galatians 2:20</b> I have been nailed to the cross with Christ. I am no longer alive, but Christ lives in me.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Galatians 2:20</b> I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Paul wrote Galatians years after Damascus. “Nailed to the cross with Christ” is a picture: his old self-centred life ended with Jesus’ death, and a new life began. Say it simply: Jesus took our place, and he lives in everyone who trusts him. Our memory verse is the second sentence.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A new friend, a new life</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This week we met two people: Saul, who was sure he was right until Jesus stopped him on the road, and Ananias, who was afraid and said yes anyway. Now let’s see what happened next.</p><p>As soon as Saul could see, he went to the Jewish meeting places in Damascus and began telling people that Jesus is the Son of God. Everyone was amazed. ‘Isn’t this the man who was arresting believers?’ they said. And Saul grew braver every day.</p><p>But some people did not like it, and they made plans to stop him. They watched the city gates day and night. So one night Saul’s new friends did something very clever. They put him in a big basket and lowered him through an opening in the city wall.</p><p>Later Saul went back to Jerusalem and tried to join the believers, but they were all afraid of him. Who could blame them? Then along came Barnabas. His name means ‘encourager’, and he lived up to it. He took Saul to the apostles, including Peter, and told them everything that had happened on the road. Barnabas risked his own good name for a man everybody feared, and Saul was welcomed.</p><p>Later on, Saul used his Roman name, Paul. And years later, Paul wrote: ‘I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.’ That is what changed him: Jesus, who loved Saul even when Saul was against him. Jesus changes lives, and he often does it through ordinary people, like Ananias and Barnabas, who say yes.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Saul tells everyone about Jesus. · 2. Down the wall in a basket. · 3. Barnabas says, “Welcome.” · 4. Jesus changes lives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Acts 9:23–25 says some people plotted to kill Saul; keep it calm and brief (“made plans to stop him”). The basket escape is a lovely detail to enjoy. Galatians 1:18–19 says Saul stayed with Peter in Jerusalem for fifteen days; Barnabas’s introduction is from Acts 9:27. Acts 13:9 says Saul was also called Paul; Saul was his Hebrew name and Paul his Roman name. The detail “including Peter” is from Galatians 1:18.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who spoke up?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who took Saul to meet the apostles in Jerusalem when everyone was afraid of him?<br>A) Ananias &nbsp; B) Barnabas ✓ &nbsp; C) Stephen &nbsp; D) Peter</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, Barnabas (Acts 9:27). Follow-up: ‘How did Saul escape from Damascus?’ (In a basket, lowered through an opening in the wall.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why were the believers in Jerusalem afraid of him?’ (He used to arrest them.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Standing up for Saul</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Barnabas stood up for the man everyone was afraid of. What do you think that cost him, and what did it give Saul?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Barnabas risked his own reputation and trust; he might have been wrong; it gave Saul a welcome, a family and a chance to start again. Follow-up: ‘What did Barnabas and Ananias have in common?’ (Both said yes and welcomed Saul.) Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about how Jesus treats people who have done wrong?’ If silent: ask what they’d need if they were the new person.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Jesus is alive, and he can stop anyone and turn their life around, even Saul.</li><li>Ananias was afraid, told Jesus so, and said yes anyway. Ordinary people can do big things for Jesus.</li><li>Barnabas and Ananias both welcomed Saul, and Jesus, who loved him and gave his life for him, did the changing.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus can change anyone, even someone who is fighting against him, and he invites ordinary people like Ananias to say yes and welcome them in.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What changed?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul later wrote, “Christ lives in me.” What do you think changed in Saul: his job, his friends, his heart, or all of them? Which is the biggest change?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: his heart, since he went from hurting people to loving them; his friends, from the people who agreed with him to the believers; his purpose, from stopping Jesus to telling people about him. Follow-up: ‘What stayed the same?’ (His passion, his brain, his courage.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who did the changing?’ (Jesus.) If silent: ask what they noticed about Saul on Monday and Wednesday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be an Ananias. When you feel a small nudge to help or welcome someone this week, say yes, even if you feel a bit nervous.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Use the sticky notes from Tuesday. Ideas: sit with someone who is on their own; carry a bag; say hello to the new student; offer to help a sibling; text a friend who is struggling. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this. Students who feel no nudge can simply ask Jesus each morning, “Is there anyone you want me to help today?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I now live in this body by having faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave his life for me.” — Galatians 2:20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Galatians 2:20 (second half). Say the first sentence once for context, then fade the verse until they can say it from memory.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Welcome, Saul</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Barnabas said, “Welcome.”<br>Jesus changes lives.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for guidance — out loud together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray out loud together. Guidance prayer is asking God to show us the way and then listening. Ask him this week to show you who to welcome or help, then pay attention to the quiet nudge. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing Jesus is or does. Think of the light and the welcome.<br>Prompts: Jesus, you change lives. You changed Saul’s. / Jesus, you loved us and gave your life for us.<br>Starter: <b>Jesus, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Jesus, you change lives, and you love us!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell Jesus about the times you’ve ignored a nudge to be kind.<br>Prompts: Sorry for deciding that someone could never change. / Sorry for ignoring your nudge to help or welcome someone.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for ignoring your nudges to help.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for from this week’s story.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Ananias and Barnabas who say yes and welcome others. / Thank you for loving us, even when we’re going our own way.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who welcome us and for loving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask for help with this week’s challenge.<br>Prompts: Please show me who to welcome or help this week. / Please give me courage to say yes, even when I’m nervous.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, show me…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, show us who to welcome, and help us to say yes.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say Ananias’s prayer together: “Lord, here I am.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Encourage short, honest prayers. Students who feel nervous about praying aloud can pray silently or join in on the closing line.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Midnight Songs in Prison — Paul and Silas sing at midnight in a Philippian jail, and an earthquake changes everything.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 5: Midnight Songs in Prison":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:11–40; Philippians 4:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Present in Hard Times</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in trouble — Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight in a prison, not because it was easy, but because God was with them. This week we practise praying with thanks and joy even when things are hard. Philippians 4:6 (CEV): “Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Lydia by the River</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how God sent Paul and Silas to Philippi and who they met by the river.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Lydia by the River → The new place → Acts 16:14–15 → God sends them across the sea → Where was the prayer meeting? → Not what he expected → Lydia by the River → Who opens hearts? → Head · Heart · Hands → By the river → Memory verse peek → Joy in trouble — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Locked in the Inner Cell</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why Paul and Silas ended up in prison in Philippi.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Locked in the Inner Cell → Recap → Acts 16:17, 24 → Trouble in Philippi → Who wanted what? → Why the anger? → Honest feelings → Does God’s presence mean no trouble? → Head · Heart · Hands → Deep inside the jail → Build the verse → Joy in trouble — write it down</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Earthquake at Midnight</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning what Paul and Silas did at midnight in the prison, and what happened next.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Earthquake at Midnight → What would you do? → Acts 16:25–26, 30–31 → Midnight songs → Midnight activity → Earthquake at Midnight → Songs in the dark → Why didn’t they escape? → Head · Heart · Hands → The jailer’s question → Verse with actions → Joy in trouble — a prayer-song</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Joy in Hard Times</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how the story ended, and what Paul’s surprise showed the Philippian officials.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Joy in Hard Times → Put it in order → Philippians 4:4, 6–7 → Dawn and a surprise → The surprise → Present in the dark → This week’s big idea → Joy or pretending? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Midnight songs → Joy in trouble — standing together</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Lydia by the River":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:11–40; Philippians 4:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Lydia by the River</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in trouble — Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight in a prison, not because it was easy, but because God was with them. This week we practise praying with thanks and joy even when things are hard. Philippians 4:6 (CEV): “Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how God sent Paul and Silas to Philippi and who they met by the river.</li><li>We are learning what happened when the Lord opened Lydia’s heart.</li><li>I can explain what Lydia did when she believed in Jesus (Acts 16:14–15).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Paul and Silas found women praying by a river, that the Lord opened Lydia’s heart, that she and her household were baptised, and that she opened her home to them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a map showing Troas, the sea crossing, and Philippi in Macedonia</li><li>Optional: a piece of purple cloth or ribbon to show Lydia’s trade</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Lydia by the River</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week’s picture is a hint: a prison, a midnight, and singing. Ask students to guess what the story is about, but don’t answer yet.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The new place</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think about the first day in a new place, such as a new school, a new town or a new team, when you didn’t know anyone. What helped most?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would it feel like if someone invited you into their home on your very first day?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome back. Last week we met Saul become Paul, and Ananias, who was scared but said yes to God. Your challenge: say yes to one small nudge to help or welcome someone. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students about one small nudge they said yes to, and how it felt. Then the hook: a smile, someone saving a seat, an invitation. Land it: ‘Today Paul and Silas arrive in a city where they know nobody, and a woman opens her door. God had been planning it all along.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 16:14–15</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:14</b> The Lord opened her heart, and she accepted what Paul was saying.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:15</b> If you think I really do have faith in the Lord, please come and stay in my home.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Lydia is the first person in Europe named in Acts as coming to faith in Jesus. Read the lines slowly. The second line is Lydia speaking. Baptism = being washed in water as a sign that you belong to Jesus and have a fresh start.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: God sends them across the sea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Paul had a puzzle. He and his friend Silas had wanted to take the good news about Jesus to several places, but each time God closed the door. Then one night Paul had a dream. A man from Macedonia, a region across the sea in Greece, was calling, ‘Come over here and help us!’ Paul understood. God was sending them to a new continent. So they sailed at once.</p><p>They arrived in Philippi, a proud Roman city. It was a colony, which means a town run like a little piece of Rome, with Roman soldiers, Roman officials and Roman laws. Paul looked around for the man from his dream. He waited a few days. No man. There wasn’t even a meeting place for God’s people.</p><p>On the Sabbath, the Jewish day of rest and worship, Paul and Silas walked outside the city gate to the river. They hoped to find a place where people prayed. And they did! A small group of women sat in the grass beside the water, praying. It was not at all what Paul had pictured. But he and Silas sat down with them and began to talk about Jesus.</p><p>One of the women was called Lydia. She came from a city called Thyatira, and she sold purple cloth, which was very expensive and worn by rich and important people. She was a successful businesswoman with a big household to look after. She already worshipped the God of Israel. But as she listened to Paul, something happened inside her. The Bible says, ‘The Lord opened her heart,’ and she accepted what Paul was saying. Lydia believed in Jesus!</p><p>She and everyone in her household were baptised, which is a way of showing that you belong to Jesus and have a fresh start. And Lydia didn’t keep her faith on a shelf. Straight away she said, ‘If you think I really do have faith in the Lord, please come and stay in my home.’ And she would not take no for an answer.</p><p>Think about it. God’s plan to bring Jesus’ good news into a whole new part of the world began with a few women praying quietly by a river, and one businesswoman with an open heart and an open door.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. God said, “Go to Macedonia.” · 2. No meeting hall. Just women praying. · 3. The Lord opened her heart. · 4. “Please come and stay in my home.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Acts 16:9–10 gives the dream; the Bible doesn’t say Paul was disappointed, so keep that light: he was surprised. Acts 16:14–15 (Lydia, Thyatira, purple cloth, heart opened, household baptised, hospitality) is Scripture. How long they waited before the Sabbath (“a few days”, Acts 16:12) and the group sitting in a circle are lightly imagined framing. Purple dye was costly, so Lydia was likely well off. Acts 16:11–15 has “we” in it because Luke, the author, was travelling with them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where was the prayer meeting?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did Paul and Silas find Lydia and the other women praying?<br>A) In the market square &nbsp; B) By a river outside the city gate ✓ &nbsp; C) Inside a big temple &nbsp; D) On a ship</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, by a river outside the city gate (Acts 16:13). Follow-up: ‘What did Lydia sell?’ (Expensive purple cloth.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Lydia do after she believed?’ (She was baptised and invited Paul and Silas to stay at her home.) If silent: re-read the story’s third paragraph.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not what he expected</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul dreamed of a man from Macedonia, but he met a group of women praying by a river. Why might God surprise us like that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God’s plans are bigger than our pictures; God cares about everyone, not only important people; God was already at work in Philippi before Paul arrived. Follow-up: ‘When did something turn out different from what you expected, but better?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this say about trusting God’s guidance?’ If silent: ask who was already praying when Paul arrived.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Lydia by the River</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Lydia by the River</b> (Acts 16:11–15). Acts 16:13–15 tells us the women prayed by the river, that Lydia’s heart was opened, that her household was baptised, and that she invited Paul and Silas to stay. Lydia’s invitation is Scripture. The friend, the chat about purple cloth, and most of Paul and Silas’s words are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Paul</b> — Eager traveller — a little surprised<br><b>Silas</b> — Steady and cheerful friend of Paul<br><b>Lydia</b> — Confident businesswoman with an open heart<br><b>Friend</b> — A woman from Lydia’s household (imagined)<br><b>Everyone</b> — The women praying by the river</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: sit in a circle and pretend to pray quietly · Everyone: splash the river with your hands, then cheer for Lydia’s family<br>Props: Optional: a piece of purple cloth for Lydia<br>Imagined retelling around Scripture. Lydia’s invitation (Acts 16:15) and the heart-opening (Acts 16:14) are Scripture. The Friend and the details of the conversation are imagined. Keep the singing and splashing joyful but short, so the pause lands.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The river outside the city gate of Philippi, on the Sabbath. A few women sit in the grass, praying quietly.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Silas, there’s no meeting hall here. I dreamed of a man from Macedonia. Where is he? <i>(look around, puzzled)</i></li><li><b>Silas:</b> God brought us to Philippi, Paul. Look: women praying by the river. Let’s sit and listen. <i>(point to the circle)</i></li><li><b>Lydia:</b> Welcome, travellers. Come and sit. We gather here every Sabbath to pray to the God of Israel. <i>(wave them over)</i></li><li><b>Friend:</b> Lydia sells the finest purple cloth in Philippi. She came all the way from Thyatira. <i>(whisper proudly)</i></li><li><b>Lydia:</b> My friend is too kind. Please, tell us your news. <i>(smile, shake head)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> We have good news. God’s promised King has come. His name is Jesus, and he rose from the dead!</li><li><b>Silas:</b> Jesus forgives everyone who trusts him, and he welcomes us into God’s family.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooooh! Tell us more! <i>(lean in and listen)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> As Lydia listened, the Lord opened her heart, and she accepted what Paul was saying.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Lydia’s heart is opening. What do you think she will say next? How do you think she feels? (20s)</li><li><b>Lydia:</b> I believe in Jesus. I want to belong to him. Will you baptise me? <i>(hands over heart)</i></li><li><b>Friend:</b> And us too, Lydia. We’ll come with you. Our whole household wants to follow Jesus. <i>(step forward)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Lydia and everyone in her household were baptised in the river.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Splash! Hooray! Welcome to God’s family! <i>(splash hands, cheer quietly)</i></li><li><b>Lydia:</b> If you think I really do have faith in the Lord, please come and stay in my home.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Lydia, we couldn’t put you to any trouble. <i>(shake head politely)</i></li><li><b>Lydia:</b> It’s no trouble at all. My door is open. I won’t take no for an answer! <i>(arms folded, grinning)</i></li><li><b>Silas:</b> Paul, God has already given us a friend and a home in Philippi. Let’s say yes. <i>(nudge Paul)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> You’re right. God knew exactly where to send us. It began with a river, a prayer and an open door.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> A church was born in Philippi. And this was only the beginning.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Open hearts! Open doors! <i>(open both hands wide)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the Bible say happened to Lydia’s heart, and what did she do next?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The Lord opened her heart. She believed, was baptised with her household, and invited Paul and Silas to stay in her home.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think Lydia’s first move was to open her home?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Faith shows up in love and kindness. Lydia shared what she had. She was thankful and wanted to look after God’s messengers.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who opens hearts?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Bible says “the Lord opened her heart.” Why do we need God’s help, and not just good words, for someone to believe in Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: only God changes what is inside a person; Paul could speak but God opened her heart; we can share Jesus but we can’t force anyone; this takes pressure off us and gives us a reason to pray. Follow-up: ‘Who might you pray for, asking God to open their heart?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What can we do that still helps?’ If silent: ask what Paul did and what God did.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God sent Paul and Silas across the sea to Philippi. He opened Lydia’s heart, and she opened her home.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that you open hearts. Please open mine to listen to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look for one chance to open a door today: invite someone to sit with you, join your game or share your lunch table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. Lydia’s welcome was ordinary kindness, which God used.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: By the river</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The Lord opened her heart.<br>A few women, a quiet river, and God’s big plan.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Acts 16:31. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Tell them: we’ll hear who asks the question behind this answer on Wednesday.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Joy in trouble — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">All week we practise joy in trouble. Start with open hands, like Lydia’s open heart and open door: open hands show we are ready to listen to God and to give thanks. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably. Hold your hands out in front of you, open, like an open door. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your open hands. God opened Lydia’s heart. He opens hearts and doors.<br>Prompts: God, you led Paul and Silas across the sea to exactly the right place. / God, you opened Lydia’s heart, and you are good at opening doors.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you lead us and you open hearts and doors!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn your hands over. Be honest with God about the doors we close.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I didn’t welcome someone new. / Sorry for the times my heart wasn’t open to listen to you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we haven’t been open and welcoming.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up again. Thank God for the people who opened a door for you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who welcomed me when I was new. / Thank you for Lydia, who said yes to Jesus and gave Paul and Silas a home.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who open their hearts and homes to us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands out again. Ask God to open one heart and one door.<br>Prompts: Please open the heart of someone I know who needs you. / Please help me welcome someone new this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, open…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, open hearts and doors, and help us welcome people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, then open them again. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it gentle and short. Some students may be new to the school or the country; keep the focus on being welcomed and welcoming, without singling anyone out.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: trouble in Philippi. Paul and Silas do a kind thing and end up locked in the inner cell of a prison.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Locked in the Inner Cell":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:11–40; Philippians 4:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Locked in the Inner Cell</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in trouble — Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight in a prison, not because it was easy, but because God was with them. This week we practise praying with thanks and joy even when things are hard. Philippians 4:6 (CEV): “Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Paul and Silas ended up in prison in Philippi.</li><li>We are learning who wanted what in the forum, and how the officials treated Paul and Silas unfairly.</li><li>I can say honestly how hard it is when we do the right thing and things still go wrong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Paul set a slave girl free in Jesus’ name, that her owners were angry because they lost money, that Paul and Silas were put in prison without a fair hearing, and that being in the right place doesn’t guarantee an easy road.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper and pens for the prayer writing time</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Locked in the Inner Cell</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today is the hardest day of the week. Keep your voice calm and steady.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met Lydia by the river, whose heart the Lord opened.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Lydia do after she believed in Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>She was baptised with her household and opened her home to Paul and Silas. Then: ‘Today things get hard. Paul and Silas are doing exactly what God asked, and they still end up in trouble.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 16:17, 24</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:17</b> These men are servants of the Most High God! They are telling you how to be saved.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:24</b> He put them deep inside the jail.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 16:17 is what the slave girl shouted: true words, but Paul didn’t want that kind of advertising. Saved = rescued by God. Acts 16:24 says the jailer put them in the inner cell with their feet fastened; we say it plainly and move on.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Trouble in Philippi</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday Lydia opened her door. Today the story takes a hard turn.</p><p>Paul, Silas and their friends kept going to the place of prayer by the river. One day a slave girl began following them. She had a spirit in her that seemed to let her tell people what would happen in the future, and her owners made a lot of money from her. For days she followed Paul and Silas, shouting, ‘These men are servants of the Most High God! They are telling you how to be saved!’ What she said was true, but Paul didn’t want people to hear Jesus’ message mixed up with that. In the end, he turned and ordered the spirit, in the name of Jesus Christ, to leave her. At once it was gone.</p><p>That was wonderful for the girl. She was set free. But her owners were furious. The way they made their money had just stopped. So they grabbed Paul and Silas and dragged them to the forum, the big open square in the middle of the city, where the city officials, called magistrates, were in charge. ‘These men are Jews, and they’re causing trouble in our city!’ they shouted. ‘They are teaching ways we Romans are not allowed to follow!’ The crowd joined in. Nobody asked Paul and Silas a single question.</p><p>The magistrates gave an order. Paul and Silas were to be punished and then locked up. We won’t go through what happened. It was unfair, and it was hard. The jailer was told to guard them carefully, so he took them to the inner cell, the darkest part of the prison, and fastened their feet in heavy wooden blocks called stocks. Then he locked the door and went away.</p><p>Now stop and think. Paul and Silas had come to Philippi because God sent them. They had done a kind thing. And now here they were, tired and sore, in the dark.</p><p>If you were Paul, what would you be thinking? Maybe, ‘God, why did you send us here?’ Those are honest feelings, and the Bible lets people tell God exactly how they feel. Jesus himself said that his followers would have trouble in this world, but he also promised to be with them.</p><p>Luke doesn’t tell us that Paul and Silas gave up. Tomorrow we’ll find out what they did at midnight.</p><p>For today, remember this: being in the right place, doing the right thing, doesn’t mean nothing hard will ever happen. But it does mean God is with you in the dark.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A slave girl, and angry owners. · 2. Dragged to the forum. · 3. The inner cell. · 4. Right place. Right thing. Still hard.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. SENSITIVE: Acts 16:22–23 describes a harsh public beating. We say only that they were punished, that it was unfair and hard, and we move on. Do not describe the method, pain or injuries. Some students may have family from places where Christians are still treated unfairly. Reassure them calmly: this is a true story, God was with Paul and Silas, and it ends well. The slave girl: the Bible says she had a spirit that let her tell fortunes. Say it plainly and briefly; the key point is that Jesus’ name set her free and her owners cared only about money. Scripture here: Acts 16:16–24. The words of the owners’ accusation are paraphrased from Acts 16:20–21. Jesus’ words about trouble are John 16:33, paraphrased.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who wanted what?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">There were many people in the forum that day. What was each one thinking about?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they wanted</th></tr><tr><td>The slave girl’s owners</td><td>Their money back, and revenge</td></tr><tr><td>The angry crowd</td><td>To join in and not be left out</td></tr><tr><td>The magistrates</td><td>Quiet streets, fast, without asking questions</td></tr><tr><td>Paul and Silas</td><td>To keep telling people about Jesus, and to trust God</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Almost everyone was thinking about themselves. Paul and Silas were thinking about God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Point out how much was unfair: no questions, no fair hearing, no justice. Say it calmly. We’ll find out on Thursday that Paul and Silas were Roman citizens, which makes it even more unfair.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why the anger?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “The slave girl’s owners were furious because they had lost their ________.”<br><i>Answer: “…money.” Their way of making money stopped when the girl was set free.</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: money, or their way of making money. Follow-up: ‘What did Paul do that set the girl free?’ (He ordered the spirit to leave in the name of Jesus Christ.) Follow-up 2: ‘Where did the crowd drag Paul and Silas?’ (To the forum, to the magistrates.) If silent: re-read the story’s third paragraph.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Honest feelings</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul and Silas did what God asked and still ended up in prison. What might they have felt, and is it okay to tell God that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: scared, confused, angry, tired, disappointed; yes, it is okay, because the Bible is full of honest prayers, and God can handle our real feelings. Follow-up: ‘When did something hard happen to you even though you did the right thing?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could you say to God then?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one word.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Does God’s presence mean no trouble?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If God is with you, nothing hard will happen to you.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: God was with Paul and Silas, and they were still locked up. Jesus said his followers would have trouble, and he promised to be with us in it. Follow-up: ‘What does God’s presence do for us in hard times?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who is someone who helped you feel not alone in a hard time?’ If silent: ask what Paul and Silas still had in that cell.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Doing the right thing doesn’t mean things will always go well, but God is with us in hard places.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when things are hard, help me remember you are with me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one hard thing you or someone you know is facing. Fold the paper and put it somewhere safe. Tomorrow we’ll find out what Paul and Silas did with their hard night.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it private; students needn’t share. Be gentle: some students are going through real difficulty. Don’t push anyone to share a specific problem.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Deep inside the jail</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Right place. Right thing. Still hard.<br>But God was there in the dark.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Acts 16:31 chunk by chunk. Tomorrow we hear the question that this verse answers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Joy in trouble — write it down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In a dark prison, Paul and Silas still prayed. Today we write: tell God one honest thing about a hard time, and then one thing to thank him for. Honest first, then thankful. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and paper. At the top, write: “God, you are with me in the dark.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is when things are hard.<br>Prompts: God, you were with Paul and Silas in the inner cell. / God, you see everything, even in the darkest place, and you never leave.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are with us even in the dark.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: are there times you complain without telling God, or turn away from him when things are hard?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I forgot you were with me in a hard time. / Sorry for the times I was unfair to someone else, like the crowd in the forum.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting you are with us when things are hard.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write three things you’re thankful for, even on a hard day.<br>Prompts: Thank you that the slave girl was set free in Jesus’ name. / Thank you for the people who are on my side when things are hard.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the good things you give us, even on hard days.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one hard thing you’re facing, and ask God for help. Then one for someone else.<br>Prompts: Please be with people who are treated unfairly today. / Please give me joy and peace in the hard thing I’m facing.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with people who are treated unfairly, and help us trust you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your page and keep it somewhere safe. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. Remind them that God can handle honest words. Some students may be facing something very hard; stay near and be available afterwards. Persecuted Christians today are prayed for gently, without scary detail.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: midnight in the prison. Two men sing, the ground shakes, and a jailer asks the biggest question of his life.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Earthquake at Midnight":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:11–40; Philippians 4:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Earthquake at Midnight</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in trouble — Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight in a prison, not because it was easy, but because God was with them. This week we practise praying with thanks and joy even when things are hard. Philippians 4:6 (CEV): “Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what Paul and Silas did at midnight in the prison, and what happened next.</li><li>We are learning how the jailer asked the biggest question of his life, and what Paul answered.</li><li>I can explain what it means to “have faith in the Lord Jesus” (Acts 16:31).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight, that an earthquake opened the doors, that no prisoner ran away, that the jailer asked what he must do to be saved, and that Paul told him to have faith in the Lord Jesus.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a dim lamp or torch for the role-play</li><li>Optional: a tray to rattle for earthquake sounds</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Earthquake at Midnight</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Remind students that yesterday ended with two men in the dark.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: What would you do?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine it’s midnight, you’re cold, tired and stuck somewhere you didn’t choose to be. What would you do?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do people usually do when they’re stuck in a bad situation? What would you not expect them to do?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take three answers: complain, cry, sleep, panic, play games, or pray. Then: ‘Yesterday Paul and Silas were locked in the inner cell. Today we find out what they did at midnight, and it is not what anyone expected.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 16:25–26, 30–31</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:25</b> About midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, while the other prisoners listened.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:26</b> Suddenly a strong earthquake shook the jail to its foundations. The doors flew open, and everyone’s chains came loose.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:30</b> What must I do to be saved?</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:31</b> Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 16:31 is our memory verse. The jailer’s question and Paul’s answer are the heart of the lesson. Hymns here were songs of praise to God, probably from the psalms.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Midnight songs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It is midnight in the inner prison at Philippi. The air is cold and heavy. Somewhere a prisoner coughs. Paul and Silas sit in the dark with their feet in the stocks, tired and sore.</p><p>And then something unexpected happens. They begin to pray. And then they begin to sing. Not quietly in their heads, but out loud: hymns, songs of praise to God, in the middle of the night, in a prison. And the Bible tells us something wonderful: ‘the other prisoners listened.’</p><p>Imagine being one of those prisoners. You have heard shouting and crying in this place every night. Now you hear singing.</p><p>Suddenly the ground begins to shake. A strong earthquake shakes the prison right down to its foundations. The doors fly open, and everyone’s chains come loose!</p><p>The jailer jolts awake. He sees the doors wide open and thinks every prisoner has escaped. He knows what that means for him. In those days a Roman jailer who lost his prisoners could pay with his own life. He is in despair, sure that everything is over.</p><p>Then out of the darkness comes a loud voice. It is Paul. ‘Don’t hurt yourself! We are all here!’</p><p>Nobody had run. Think about that. The doors were open, the chains were off, and every single prisoner stayed. Maybe it was because of the songs, or maybe God held them there. The Bible doesn’t say. But the jailer is amazed. He calls for lights, rushes in, trembling all over, and falls on his knees in front of Paul and Silas. Then he leads them outside and asks the biggest question of his life: ‘What must I do to be saved?’</p><p>Saved. The jailer had just been saved from one danger, but he could feel a bigger rescue was what he needed: rescue from being far from God. And Paul gives the shortest, clearest answer in the Bible: ‘Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.’</p><p>Not ‘do ten hard things’. Not ‘be good enough’. Trust Jesus. Paul and Silas told the Lord’s message to the jailer and everyone in his house. And right there, in the middle of the night, the jailer cared for the two men he had locked up, and he and his whole family were baptised.</p><p>Where did all this begin? In the dark, with two tired men singing.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Midnight. Singing. · 2. The ground shakes. · 3. “Don’t hurt yourself! We are all here!” · 4. “What must I do to be saved?” · 5. “Have faith in the Lord Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Acts 16:25–34 is the source. SENSITIVE: in Acts 16:27 the jailer, thinking the prisoners had escaped, was about to take his own life. We say only that he was in despair and afraid, and we do not mention how. If a student raises it or seems affected, stay calm, reassure them, and follow your school’s pastoral procedures. A Roman jailer risked death if prisoners escaped (see Acts 12:19 and 16:27). Why nobody ran is not stated; we say so honestly. “Saved” = rescued, here from sin and from being cut off from God. The jailer may have first meant rescue from danger; Paul’s answer points to the bigger rescue. The shaking and the songs are Scripture; sounds and the smell of the cell are lightly imagined.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Midnight activity</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were Paul and Silas doing at midnight in the prison?<br>A) Sleeping &nbsp; B) Praying and singing hymns ✓ &nbsp; C) Planning an escape &nbsp; D) Arguing with each other</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, praying and singing hymns to God (Acts 16:25). Follow-up: ‘Who was listening?’ (The other prisoners.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened suddenly?’ (A strong earthquake; doors flew open and chains came loose.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line on the screen.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Earthquake at Midnight</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Earthquake at Midnight</b> (Acts 16:25–34). Acts 16:25–34 gives the singing, the earthquake, Paul’s call, the jailer’s question and Paul’s answer, which are Scripture. The prisoner, the exact wording of most lines, the jailer’s apology and the song words are imagined, to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Paul</b> — Calm, tired and trusting<br><b>Silas</b> — Steady friend who loves to sing<br><b>Jailer</b> — Gruff Roman guard, then shaken and humbled<br><b>Prisoner</b> — A listening prisoner in the cell (imagined)<br><b>Everyone</b> — The singing, then the earthquake</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: sing or hum a gentle song softly, like Paul and Silas · Everyone: rumble, stamp and rattle for the earthquake, then freeze<br>Props: Optional: a dim torch as the jailer’s lamp<br>Imagined retelling around Scripture. Paul’s call (“We are all here”), the jailer’s question and Paul’s answer are from Acts 16. The prisoner and the jailer’s lines before and after are imagined. Do not act out the jailer’s despair beyond a worried, frantic look; keep it light. Keep the earthquake short and fun and stop the noise before the pause.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Midnight in the inner cell of the prison at Philippi. Paul and Silas sit in the dark, feet in heavy stocks.</li><li><b>Prisoner:</b> Is anyone else awake? I can’t sleep in this cold, dark place. <i>(whisper, shivering)</i></li><li><b>Silas:</b> I’m awake. It’s been a long, hard day, Paul. Shall we pray?</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Yes. God is here with us, even in this cell. Let’s tell him how great he is.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Praise God! Praise God! Praise God! <i>(sing or hum softly)</i></li><li><b>Prisoner:</b> Singing? In here? I’ve never heard anything like it. I’m listening. <i>(lean closer)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> About midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, while the other prisoners listened.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Rumble! Rattle! Shake! <i>(stamp feet and shake, then freeze)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Suddenly a strong earthquake shook the jail to its foundations. The doors flew open, and everyone’s chains came loose.</li><li><b>Jailer:</b> Wake up! The doors are open! They must all have escaped. This is the end for me! <i>(frantic, hands on head)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> Don’t hurt yourself! We are all here! <i>(call out loudly)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The doors are open and the chains are off. Why do you think nobody ran away? How does the jailer feel? (20s)</li><li><b>Jailer:</b> Bring a light! Is everyone here? Every single one? <i>(hold up lamp, trembling)</i></li><li><b>Prisoner:</b> We’re all here. We heard those songs. We couldn’t leave. <i>(shake head, amazed)</i></li><li><b>Jailer:</b> Sirs, what must I do to be saved? <i>(kneel, shaking)</i></li><li><b>Silas:</b> Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Bring your family. We’ll tell you all about the Lord Jesus, who loves you. <i>(hold out a hand)</i></li><li><b>Jailer:</b> First let me care for you. You were treated badly. I’m so sorry. <i>(bow head)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> That same night the jailer and his whole family were baptised. Then he took them home and gave them a meal.</li><li><b>Jailer:</b> Come in, sit, eat! I have never felt so glad. I have faith in God now! <i>(beaming)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Joy in the dark! Praise the Lord! <i>(hands up, cheer quietly)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Paul and Silas do at midnight, and what happened next?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They prayed and sang hymns while the prisoners listened. Then an earthquake opened the doors and loosened every chain.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think the jailer asked, “What must I do to be saved?”<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He had just been saved from danger, and he saw something about Paul and Silas that he wanted. He knew he needed a bigger rescue, and he asked the right question.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Songs in the dark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul and Silas sang in a prison. What might singing have done for them, and for the people listening?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it reminded them God was with them; it kept fear away; it showed the prisoners a different way to be; it made their trust visible. Follow-up: ‘What song, prayer or verse helps you in a hard time?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who might be listening to you when things are hard?’ If silent: ask what the other prisoners did while they sang.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why didn’t they escape?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The doors were open and the chains were off, but Paul stayed and told the jailer, “We are all here.” Why might Paul have stayed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: to save the jailer’s life; because he trusted God, not running away; because Paul wanted the jailer to hear about Jesus; because love for others mattered more than freedom. Follow-up: ‘What did staying make possible?’ (The jailer’s whole household believed.) Follow-up 2: ‘When has kindness meant staying put?’ If silent: ask what would have happened to the jailer if they had left.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Think · Pair · Share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul and Silas prayed and sang even in the dark, and God used it to bring a whole family to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I trust you, and I want my whole family to know you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Next time something is hard this week, stop and thank God for three things before you do anything else. Say them out loud or in your head.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is Thursday’s challenge, previewed. Keep it small: three real thank-yous, even tiny ones.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: The jailer’s question</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“What must I do to be saved?”<br>“Have faith in the Lord Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Acts 16:31: heart, arms up, point, sweep, pat the ground, roof.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Joy in trouble — a prayer-song</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul and Silas sang at midnight. Praying like a song means saying each line slowly, and then letting it ring. We pray one line at a time, and the class repeats it back. <i>(psalm)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit or stand tall. We’re going to pray like a song, line by line. I’ll say a line, and you say it back, quietly or out loud.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say it back, line by line, like a song in the dark.<br>Prompts: God, you are with us even at midnight. / God, you can shake a prison and set the doors wide open.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God — you are with us in the dark, and you are stronger than any prison!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, tell God about a time you sang or smiled on the outside but didn’t trust him inside, or about a time you forgot about him in a hard moment.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I only talk to you when things are easy. / Sorry for the times I gave up hoping when things got dark.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we forget you in hard times.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God the way Paul and Silas did, even in a hard place.<br>Prompts: Thank you that no one is ever alone in a dark place, because you are there. / Thank you for the jailer’s family, and for everyone who came to know Jesus that night.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are with us when it is dark.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ask God to help someone who is in a dark place right now.<br>Prompts: Please be with people who are in prison or treated unfairly, and bring them peace. / Please give me joy and trust in a hard time, like Paul and Silas.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, be with…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with people in dark places, and give us your joy.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s finish with one soft “Amen”, like the last note of a song.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t worry if the “song” is just spoken lines; the point is slow, shared, repeated words. Acts 16:25 says hymns; Jewish people of the time often sang psalms, though Luke doesn’t say which. Pray for people who are treated unfairly for their faith without scary detail; some students may have family in places where this is real.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a surprise at dawn, a very good meal, and what this story shows us about God in hard times.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Joy in Hard Times":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:11–40; Philippians 4:4–7</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in Hard Times</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Joy in trouble — Paul and Silas prayed and sang at midnight in a prison, not because it was easy, but because God was with them. This week we practise praying with thanks and joy even when things are hard. Philippians 4:6 (CEV): “Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the story ended, and what Paul’s surprise showed the Philippian officials.</li><li>We are learning what this story shows us about God in hard times, and how Paul later wrote about joy.</li><li>I can choose to thank God for three things when something is hard.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the week’s story in order, explain that God was present with Paul and Silas in the prison, say that Paul’s joy came from the Lord and not from easy circumstances, and recite Acts 16:31.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Joy in Hard Times</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Put it in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put this week’s story in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lydia’s heart is opened → The slave girl is set free → Dragged to the forum → Locked in the inner cell → Midnight singing → Earthquake → The jailer asks, “What must I do to be saved?”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next event; click to place it. Ask: ‘Which part happened by a river? Which part happened in the dark?’ Then: ‘Where do you see God in each part?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Philippians 4:4, 6–7</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Philippians 4:4</b> Always be glad because of the Lord! I will say it again: Be glad.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Philippians 4:6</b> Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything. With thankful hearts offer up your prayers and requests to God.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Philippians 4:7</b> Then, because you belong to Christ Jesus, God will bless you with peace that no one can completely understand.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Paul wrote these words in a letter to the church at Philippi, years after this story, and he wrote it while in prison. Being glad because of the Lord is different from pretending to feel happy: it is knowing who is with you.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Dawn and a surprise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s go back to that night. The jailer, who had been the one in charge of the cell, now cared for Paul and Silas. He took them to his own home, gave them a meal, and he and his whole family were very glad, because now they had faith in God.</p><p>When morning came, the magistrates sent police with a message: ‘Let those men go!’ The jailer was delighted. But Paul said something surprising. He and Silas were Roman citizens, and the magistrates had punished them in public without a fair trial. That was against Roman law. ‘Now they want to send us away quietly?’ said Paul. ‘No. Let them come and let us out themselves.’</p><p>When the magistrates heard ‘Roman citizens’, they were frightened. They came, apologised, led Paul and Silas out, and asked them politely to leave the city. Paul wasn’t being proud. He wanted it clear that they had done nothing wrong, so that the new little church in Philippi would be safe.</p><p>Paul and Silas walked out with their heads held high, went to Lydia’s house, met with the believers and encouraged them. Then they left.</p><p>What does this show us about God? He is present in hard times. He didn’t stop the stocks or the dark. But he was in the cell, and he turned a prison into a place of singing and a jailer’s home into a church.</p><p>Years later, Paul was in prison again. From there he wrote to the church in Philippi: ‘Always be glad because of the Lord!’ That is not pretending. It is knowing who is with you. Jesus, who was himself arrested and treated unfairly, gave his life for us, rose again, and promised to be with his followers always.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The jailer’s table. · 2. “Let those men go!” · 3. They walked out with their heads held high. · 4. God is present in hard times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Acts 16:35–40 is Scripture: the order to release them, Paul’s citizenship, the apology, and their visit to Lydia’s house. We have paraphrased Paul’s words. That they were punished without trial and were Roman citizens is documented in Acts 16:37. Philippians was written from prison, Paul says in the letter itself (Philippians 1:7, 13); the location is debated, so we say only “in prison”. Jesus’ promise to be with us is Matthew 28:20 (paraphrased). Keep the cross simple and reverent.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The surprise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What surprising thing did Paul tell the officials the next morning?<br>A) That he was a Roman citizen ✓ &nbsp; B) That he was the king &nbsp; C) That he would sue the jailer &nbsp; D) That he wanted to stay in jail</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, that he and Silas were Roman citizens (Acts 16:37), so punishing them without a trial was against the law. Follow-up: ‘What did the magistrates do when they heard?’ (They were frightened, came and apologised, and led them out.) Follow-up 2: ‘Where did Paul and Silas go next?’ (To Lydia’s house.) If silent: re-read the story’s second paragraph.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Present in the dark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God didn’t stop Paul and Silas from going into prison, but he was with them there. What difference does it make to know God is with you in a hard time?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: you’re not alone; you can pray and sing; hope is possible; God can use hard things for good; peace even when nothing has changed. Follow-up: ‘What did God do through Paul and Silas in the prison?’ (Brought a whole family to faith.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who helped you feel God’s presence in a hard time?’ If silent: ask what the prisoners could see in Paul and Silas.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God sent Paul and Silas to Philippi. He opened Lydia’s heart, and she opened her home.</li><li>In prison, in the dark, Paul and Silas prayed and sang, and God was with them. The jailer and his whole household had faith in Jesus.</li><li>God is present in hard times. We can pray with thankful hearts and be glad because of the Lord.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God is with his people even in the darkest place, so we can pray and sing with joy in hard times, and Jesus is the one who brings rescue.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Have students say the memory verse, Acts 16:31, with the actions.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Joy or pretending?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Being glad in hard times means pretending everything is fine.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Paul and Silas were honest about their situation; their joy came from trusting the Lord, not from pretending. We can be sad and still trust God. Follow-up: ‘What is the difference between joy and pretending?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could thanking God for three things help in a hard moment?’ If silent: ask whether Paul and Silas were sitting in the dark pretending.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When something is hard this week, thank God for three things before you do anything else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Don’t worry about anything, but pray about everything. With thankful hearts offer up your prayers and requests to God.” — Philippians 4:6</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Give out the take-home cards. Help students think of examples: a warm bed, a friend, a meal, a teacher. Some students face real hardship at home; keep the ‘three things’ simple and gentle. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Have faith in the Lord Jesus and you will be saved! This is also true for everyone who lives in your house.” — Acts 16:31 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Acts 16:31. Bonus: say Luke 8:15 or any earlier verse your class still remembers.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Midnight songs</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Always be glad because of the Lord!<br>God is present in hard times.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Joy in trouble — standing together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul and Silas thanked God at midnight, so we practise the same: thanks first, then asking. Stand as we pray, like people with their heads held high, and give thanks for three things. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up tall, like Paul and Silas walking out at dawn. Take a slow breath. We’ll pray out loud, one short sentence at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is, even in hard times.<br>Prompts: God, you are with us in the dark. / God, you are stronger than any prison and any storm.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are with us, and you give us joy even in hard times!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about the times you grumbled and forgot to thank him.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I complain and forget you are with me. / Thank you that you forgive us and give us a new start, like the jailer.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting you, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out three things you’re thankful for, big or small.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Jesus, who stays with us in hard times. / Thank you for Lydia’s open door and the jailer’s family table.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people and gifts you give us every day.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for someone who is in a hard time right now.<br>Prompts: Please be with Christians who are treated badly because they follow Jesus. / Please help me remember to thank you for three things when something is hard.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with people in hard times, and help us thank you first.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together: “Always be glad because of the Lord!” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. If any students would prefer not to stand, they can pray seated. Pray for persecuted Christians today gently and without scary detail.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Ordinary Heroes Who Served. Priscilla, Aquila, Timothy and Barnabas show how ordinary people helped spread the good news.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 6: Ordinary Heroes Who Served":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:1–3; 18:1–4, 24–28; 4:36–37; 2 Timothy 1:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Uses Ordinary People</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for team-mates — This week we practise praying for the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila prayed and worked beside Paul every day. Pick one team-mate (at school, sport, church or home) and pray for them by name: ask God to bless them, help them, and help you work well together.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Tentmakers in Corinth</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning how Priscilla and Aquila welcomed Paul into their home and their workshop in Corinth.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Tentmakers in Corinth → The people behind the scenes → Acts 18:3–4 → Three tentmakers → What was their job? → Work and Jesus together → The Tentmakers → Who gets the credit? → Head · Heart · Hands → A stranger at the stall → Memory verse peek → Praying for team-mates — working hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Timothy and the Kitchen Table</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Timothy’s faith began with his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Timothy and the Kitchen Table → Recap → Acts 16:1–2 · 2 Timothy 1:5 → Timothy’s family → Who did what? → Finish the line → Saying yes when you’re shy → Is faith inherited? → Head · Heart · Hands → “Will you come with me?” → Build the verse → Praying for team-mates — a thank-you list</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Apollos Comes Home for Supper</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Priscilla and Aquila helped Apollos know Jesus more fully.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Apollos Comes Home for Supper → The right way to say it → Acts 18:24–26 → Apollos comes home → Where did they take him? → Supper With Apollos → Humble enough to learn → Kind and honest → Head · Heart · Hands → Supper at Priscilla’s → Verse with actions → Praying for team-mates — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Barnabas the Encourager</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who Barnabas was and how he encouraged others.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Barnabas the Encourager → Our week in order → Acts 4:36–37 · Colossians 3:23 → Barnabas and the God who uses ordinary people → What does Barnabas mean? → God’s kind of hero → This week’s big idea → Does small count? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Ordinary heroes → Praying for team-mates — stand in a circle</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Tentmakers in Corinth":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:1–3; 18:1–4, 24–28; 4:36–37; 2 Timothy 1:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tentmakers in Corinth</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for team-mates — This week we practise praying for the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila prayed and worked beside Paul every day. Pick one team-mate (at school, sport, church or home) and pray for them by name: ask God to bless them, help them, and help you work well together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Priscilla and Aquila welcomed Paul into their home and their workshop in Corinth.</li><li>We are learning how ordinary work and sharing Jesus went together for these three tentmakers.</li><li>I can explain how Priscilla and Aquila served God without being famous (Acts 18:1–4).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Paul met Priscilla and Aquila in Corinth, that all three were tentmakers, that Priscilla and Aquila welcomed Paul to stay and work with them, and that they talked about Jesus while they worked.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a map showing Athens, Corinth and Rome</li><li>Optional: a needle and thick thread with a piece of felt or canvas to show what stitching tent cloth was like</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Tentmakers in Corinth</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Ask: ‘Who do you think the heroes of this week might be?’ Collect a few guesses (kings? preachers? soldiers?) and don’t answer yet. The surprise is that the heroes are tentmakers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The people behind the scenes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think about the people who make things work without being noticed: whoever set up this room, cooked your dinner, washed the sports gear, or helped you with your homework.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who is someone who helps you a lot but hardly ever gets noticed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome back. Last week we heard Midnight Songs in Prison: Paul and Silas praised God in the dark, and the jailer’s family found Jesus. Your challenge: when something is hard, thank God for three things before you do anything else. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students what they thanked God for when something was hard, and whether it changed how they felt. Then the hook: caretaker, cook, bus driver, parent, older sibling. Land it: ‘This week we meet people the Bible calls helpers, tentmakers, grandmothers and encouragers, and God used every one of them.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 18:3–4</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 18:3</b> Paul was also a tent maker, so he stayed with them, and they worked together.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 18:4</b> Every Sabbath, Paul went to the synagogue.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Sabbath = the Jewish day of rest (Saturday). A synagogue is a Jewish meeting place for prayer and reading Scripture. In verse 4 Paul speaks there, trying to convince both Jews and Greeks about Jesus. The same page tells us Aquila and Priscilla (also called Prisca) had recently come from Rome.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Three tentmakers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture the busy port city of Corinth in Greece, about two thousand years ago. Ships unload at the harbour, merchants shout in the market, and the streets are crowded with sailors, traders and workers. Into this city walks a tired traveller with dusty sandals. His name is Paul.</p><p>Paul has been on the road for a long time, telling people about Jesus. Corinth is a hard place to start. He knows nobody, he has very little money, and some people in this city don’t want to hear about Jesus at all. So where does God send him first?</p><p>Not to a palace. Not to a temple. To a workshop.</p><p>In the market there are stalls that make tents. Tents in those days were stitched from tough goat-hair cloth, sewn by hand with thick needles, and the people who did it had strong, sore fingers. Paul had learned the trade when he was young. So he looks around, and he finds two other tentmakers: a husband and wife named Aquila and Priscilla.</p><p>They are Jewish believers in Jesus who have just arrived from Rome. The emperor, Claudius, had ordered Jewish people to leave the city, so they had to pack up their whole lives and start again in a strange place. Maybe that is why they notice a stranger who needs a friend. They don’t just give Paul a job. They invite him to stay with them and work beside them.</p><p>So every day, Paul, Priscilla and Aquila sit side by side, stitching tent cloth. Pull the thread through. Pull it tight. Next stitch. It is ordinary work. Nobody would write a poem about it. But while their hands work, their mouths talk about Jesus: who he is, what he did, and why he is the best news in the world. Customers who come to buy a tent stay to listen. And every Sabbath, Paul goes to the meeting place and speaks to anyone who will hear.</p><p>Here is something to notice. Priscilla and Aquila are not apostles. They are not famous. They never stand on a stage. They are tentmakers who open their door, share their table and share their faith. And God used their workshop and their open door to support Paul’s work in Corinth, where many people came to believe in Jesus.</p><p>That is how God often works. He uses ordinary people doing ordinary things for Jesus. And sometimes the work that looks the smallest is the work he is using the most.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Paul arrives in Corinth. · 2. God sends him to a workshop. · 3. Priscilla and Aquila say, “Stay with us.” · 4. They stitch. They talk about Jesus. · 5. Ordinary work. Extraordinary God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Source: Acts 18:1–4. Documented: Paul met Aquila (and Priscilla), who had come from Italy because Claudius had ordered the Jews to leave Rome; they were tentmakers like Paul; he stayed and worked with them; he spoke in the synagogue each Sabbath. The details of customers listening, thick needles and sore fingers are imagined colour based on how tentmaking worked. Be sensitive if any students have moved countries or been forced to move: Priscilla and Aquila started again in a new place and God was with them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What was their job?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What job did Paul, Priscilla and Aquila all do?<br>A) Fishing &nbsp; B) Making tents ✓ &nbsp; C) Building ships &nbsp; D) Baking bread</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, making tents (Acts 18:3). Follow-up: ‘Where did Paul meet Priscilla and Aquila?’ (In Corinth, in a workshop in the market.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did they offer Paul?’ (A place to stay and work.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Work and Jesus together</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul stitched tents all day and also talked about Jesus. Why might ordinary work be a good place to share about Jesus?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: people meet us there; they see how we treat others; we have time to chat while working; ordinary life is where faith shows. Follow-up: ‘Where do you spend ordinary time with other people?’ (class, sport, home, the bus.) Follow-up 2: ‘What could you do there that shows you follow Jesus?’ If silent: ask what a customer might notice about three happy tentmakers.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Tentmakers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Tentmakers</b> (Acts 18:1–4). Acts 18:1–4 tells us only that Paul met Aquila and Priscilla in Corinth, stayed and worked with them, and spoke in the synagogue each Sabbath. All the conversations here are imagined. The Customer is an invented character. Nothing said by Paul, Priscilla or Aquila is a quote from Scripture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Paul</b> — Tired traveller — then full of energy about Jesus<br><b>Priscilla</b> — Quick-witted and kind — opens her home<br><b>Aquila</b> — Strong, cheerful tentmaker — a welcoming man<br><b>Customer</b> — A curious shopper who needs a tent (imagined)<br><b>Everyone</b> — Stitching, listening and cheering</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: stitch pretend tent cloth with a big needle · Everyone: nod and lean in as a customer listens<br>Props: Optional: a needle and thread with a scrap of felt · Optional: a rolled-up cloth for tent cloth<br>All lines are imagined except the facts in Acts 18:1–4 (tentmakers; stayed and worked together; Sabbath speaking). Let the stitching be fun and rhythmic. When the Customer says “Jesus? Tell me more”, take your time: this is the heart of the scene.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The busy market of Corinth. A dusty traveller named Paul walks up to a tentmaker’s workshop.</li><li><b>Aquila:</b> Welcome, friend! You look worn out. I’m Aquila, and this is my wife, Priscilla. <i>(stand and shake hands)</i></li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> We’ve only just arrived ourselves. Rome sent us away, so we’re starting over here.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> I’m Paul. I’m a tentmaker too! I need a place to stay, and I’d love to work with you. <i>(pick up a needle)</i></li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> Then you’ll stay with us. There’s a spare bed, a warm meal, and a mountain of tent cloth waiting.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Stitch, stitch, stitch! Pull it tight! <i>(stitch the air with a big needle)</i></li><li><b>Aquila:</b> Pass me the big needle, Paul. This goat-hair cloth is tough on the fingers. <i>(wince and shake out a hand)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> Mine are sore too. But it’s good work, and it keeps us together.</li><li><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me, I need a tent for a sea trip. Why are you three smiling at tent cloth? <i>(peer over the bench)</i></li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> Because we follow Jesus, and he is with us even when we’re stitching.</li><li><b>Customer:</b> Jesus? Tell me more. I’ve got time while you finish my tent. <i>(lean on the bench)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> Jesus is God’s Son, the Saviour who died for us and rose again. Keep stitching while I tell you.</li><li><b>Aquila:</b> Come back on the Sabbath. Paul speaks at the meeting place, and everyone is welcome.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Day after day they worked and talked, and more people stopped to listen.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Stitch, stitch, stitch! Talk about Jesus! <i>(stitch and nod)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Priscilla, Aquila and Paul are not famous. They are tentmakers. Why might God want to use ordinary workers like them? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Paul was not alone. Priscilla and Aquila had given him a home, a job and a team.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> I couldn’t do this without you both. Thank you for opening your door. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> We’re just stitching tents, Paul. We’re glad to do it for Jesus.</li><li><b>Aquila:</b> Every stitch for the Lord. Now, who’s ready for the next tent?</li><li><b>Customer:</b> I’ll be back on the Sabbath. I want to hear more. <i>(wave and leave)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Do your work willingly, for the Lord! <i>(stitch once, then point up)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Priscilla and Aquila help Paul, apart from giving him a job?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They gave him a home, a meal, friendship and a team to work with. They welcomed a stranger. Helping behind the scenes is real service.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What were Paul, Priscilla and Aquila doing while they talked about Jesus?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Stitching tent cloth. Their ordinary work was the place where they shared the good news.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who gets the credit?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Priscilla and Aquila are not famous, but Paul’s letters mention them again and again with thanks. Why do you think God uses people who aren’t famous?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God cares about faithfulness, not fame; ordinary people can be where we live and work; God wants every kind of person on his team; the helpers make the big jobs possible. Follow-up: ‘Who in your life is a helper like Priscilla?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would you miss if helpers stopped?’ If silent: ask about the person who set up your room this morning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God uses ordinary people doing ordinary things, like tentmakers sharing their home and their faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that you can use me, even when I feel small.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Do one small helping job today without being asked and without wanting any credit: tidy up, set the table, carry something.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small. The point is serving quietly. Some students may feel they have nothing to give; reassure them that small helpful things count.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A stranger at the stall</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Stay with us.”<br>A workshop in Corinth became a home for Paul.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Colossians 3:23. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Explain: ‘master’ means the person you’re working for.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for team-mates — working hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we practise praying for team-mates: the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila worked beside Paul every day. Hands that work are hands we can offer to God. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold out both hands, like tentmakers with sore, hard-working fingers. Take a slow breath in and out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look at your hands. God uses ordinary hands for his work.<br>Prompts: God, you used two tentmakers to help Paul in Corinth. / God, you see every stitch and every small job done for you.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you use ordinary people to do your work!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn your hands palms down. Tell God about times we have worked badly with others.<br>Prompts: Sorry for grumbling about jobs that seem boring or small. / Sorry for the times I’ve left a team-mate out.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we grumble and leave people out.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up again. Thank God for the people who work beside you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who open their door, like Priscilla and Aquila. / Thank you for team-mates at school and sport who share the work.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who work beside us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose one team-mate and pray for them silently by name.<br>Prompts: Please bless the team-mates I work and play beside, and help us work well together. / Please help me do my work willingly, as if I’m doing it for you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help our teams and help us work willingly for you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, like holding the names of your team-mates. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Team-mates can be anyone: a class group, a sports team, family, church. If a student feels left out of teams, gently invite them to pray for one person who might need a friend.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: a young man named Timothy, and the grandmother and mother who taught him about God long before he met Paul.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Timothy and the Kitchen Table":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:1–3; 18:1–4, 24–28; 4:36–37; 2 Timothy 1:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Timothy and the Kitchen Table</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for team-mates — This week we practise praying for the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila prayed and worked beside Paul every day. Pick one team-mate (at school, sport, church or home) and pray for them by name: ask God to bless them, help them, and help you work well together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Timothy’s faith began with his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice.</li><li>We are learning why Paul invited young Timothy to travel with him.</li><li>I can explain how ordinary people in a family can pass on faith (2 Timothy 1:5).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students naming Lois and Eunice as the ones who first lived and taught faith in Jesus to Timothy, saying that Timothy was young and probably shy, and explaining that Paul invited him to travel and help.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper and pens (for the thank-you list)</li><li>Optional: a toy scroll or rolled paper</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Timothy and the Kitchen Table</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met three tentmakers in Corinth: Priscilla, Aquila and Paul.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Priscilla and Aquila do for Paul, and what did they do while they worked?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>They welcomed him to stay and work with them; they stitched tents and talked about Jesus. ‘Today we meet a young man whose faith started at a kitchen table with the women who loved him: Timothy.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 16:1–2 · 2 Timothy 1:5</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:1</b> There was a follower named Timothy. His mother was also a follower. She was Jewish, and his father was Greek.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 16:2</b> The Lord’s followers in Lystra and Iconium said good things about Timothy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>2 Timothy 1:5</b> I also remember the genuine faith of your mother Eunice. Your grandmother Lois had the same sort of faith, and I am sure you have it as well.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Genuine = real, not pretend. 2 Timothy is a letter Paul wrote to Timothy later in life; it is part of the New Testament. Lystra and Iconium were towns in what is now central Turkey.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Timothy’s family</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today we meet a young man called Timothy. But to understand Timothy, we have to go back to where his story really began: his grandmother’s kitchen.</p><p>Timothy grew up in a town called Lystra, in a hilly part of what is now Turkey. His mother, Eunice, was Jewish and believed in Jesus. His father was Greek. And living with them was Timothy’s grandmother, Lois.</p><p>The Bible doesn’t describe their home, so let’s imagine it. Picture a small stone house, lamplight, and a boy of about eight. Lois unrolls a precious scroll: the holy writings we call the Old Testament. She puts her finger on a line and reads it slowly. Then Eunice explains, ‘This is about God’s promise.’ Night after night, year after year, the boy hears about a God who keeps his promises, and about the Saviour God promised to send. Nobody in that kitchen is famous. Nobody is on a stage. They are just a grandmother and a mum, passing on their faith one lamp-lit evening at a time.</p><p>Years later, Paul comes to Lystra on his travels, and he meets a young follower of Jesus. The believers in Lystra and Iconium all say good things about him. Timothy’s faith is real, and Paul sees it. Later, Paul will write that Timothy’s grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice had the same sort of genuine faith, and that he is sure Timothy has it as well.</p><p>So Paul asks him: ‘Timothy, will you come with me?’</p><p>That is a big question. It means leaving home. It means long roads, strange cities, and sometimes trouble. We don’t think Timothy was loud or bold. In later letters Paul gently tells him not to be afraid, because God’s Spirit gives power, love and self-control. Timothy may have been shy, and a bit nervous. But he says yes.</p><p>Timothy becomes Paul’s helper and travelling partner, and a dear friend, almost like a son. Paul sends him to churches, trusting him with important jobs. And one day Paul writes him a letter, which is in our Bible, that begins by remembering his faith and the women who taught him.</p><p>Here is the surprise. When we read about Paul’s great journeys, we might think the heroes are the people on the road. But a lot of the story started years earlier at a kitchen table, with a grandmother and a mother who simply said, ‘Let me tell you about God.’</p><p>They never travelled far. But their faith travelled all the way to Timothy, and through Timothy, to many others. That’s how God works: through ordinary people, loving and teaching, one lamp-lit evening at a time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Before Timothy meets Paul… · 2. Lois and Eunice tell him about God. · 3. Paul comes to Lystra. · 4. “Will you come with me?” · 5. A faith passed down.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Documented (Acts 16:1–3; 2 Timothy 1:5; 3:15): Timothy’s mother was a Jewish believer, his father Greek; Lois and Eunice had genuine faith; Timothy was well spoken of; Paul wanted him to join them. Imagined: the scroll-reading scene, Lois’s and Eunice’s words, and Timothy’s age at the time. “Shy” is a gentle inference from 2 Timothy 1:7 (about not being timid): say so if asked. Acts 16:3 also says Paul had Timothy circumcised, because of the Jewish people in that area; we leave this out of the story for age reasons, but if students ask, answer it simply and calmly. Be sensitive: some students live in homes where faith is not practised, or have no grandparent nearby. Stress that anyone, a teacher, neighbour, friend or coach, can be a Lois or an Eunice.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Who did what?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith is like a torch passed from hand to hand. Match each person to what they did.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they did</th></tr><tr><td>Lois (grandmother)</td><td>Had genuine faith and shared the Scriptures with Timothy</td></tr><tr><td>Eunice (mother)</td><td>Believed in Jesus and brought Timothy up in that faith</td></tr><tr><td>Timothy</td><td>Said yes to God and to Paul, even if he felt shy</td></tr><tr><td>Paul</td><td>Invited Timothy to join him and trusted him with important jobs</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Every one of them was ordinary, and God used every one of them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Ask after each: ‘Which person do we hear about least, and how important was their job?’ Lois and Eunice only get one verse, yet without them there is no Timothy.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Finish the line</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “Your grandmother Lois had the same sort of ________ faith.”<br><i>Answer: “…genuine faith.” (2 Timothy 1:5)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: genuine, meaning real. Follow-up: ‘Who was Timothy’s mother?’ (Eunice.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who were the first people to teach Timothy about God?’ (Lois and Eunice, his grandmother and mother.) If silent: point to the last scripture line and read it aloud together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Saying yes when you’re shy</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Timothy was young and probably shy, but Paul asked him to come. Why might God choose a shy young person for a big job?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God looks at faith, not confidence; God’s Spirit gives power; shy people may be good listeners and helpers; Paul saw that Timothy’s faith was real. Follow-up: ‘What helps you say yes when you feel nervous?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who believed in you before you believed in yourself?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is faith inherited?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “You only follow Jesus if your family does.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Timothy’s father was Greek, and Paul himself met Jesus on his own. Families can pass on faith, but each person says their own yes. Follow-up: ‘Who else besides family can help someone know Jesus?’ (Friends, teachers, church, coaches.) Follow-up 2: ‘What could you pass on to someone younger?’ If silent: ask who first taught them something important.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faith is often passed on by ordinary people at ordinary tables: grandparents, parents, teachers and friends.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank you, God, for the people who have helped me know you, or who have helped me when I needed it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the name of one person who has taught you something good or helped you grow, and one thing you’re thankful for about them. Say thank you to them this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Give out paper and pens. Students keep their note private. For anyone without family support, encourage naming a teacher, neighbour, coach or friend. Some students may be sad if someone they would write about has died; allow them to write and keep the note.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “Will you come with me?”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Timothy, will you come with me?”<br>A faith that began at a kitchen table travelled the world.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Colossians 3:23 chunk by chunk, like building a tent: one piece at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for team-mates — a thank-you list</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the names of people who teach you or help you, and people you work and play beside. Praying for team-mates starts with knowing their names. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top of your paper write “Thank you for…” and underneath draw a small lamp, like the one at Timothy’s kitchen table.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you use ordinary families to pass on your love. / God, you chose a shy young man and made him brave.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you use ordinary people, and you make us brave!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have you been too shy, or too busy, to say thank you or to help?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the people I forget to thank. / Sorry for the times I say no to you because I’m afraid.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for forgetting to say thank you and for saying no when we’re afraid.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the name of one person who taught you about God or helped you grow.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the grandparent, parent or teacher who taught me. / Thank you for the friend who helps me be brave.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who taught us and helped us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the name of one team-mate and one thing you’d like to ask God for them.<br>Prompts: Please help my team-mate (name) with what is hard for them right now. / Please help me be brave, like Timothy, and say yes when you call me.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help our team-mates, and help us say yes to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your list and keep it safe. Perhaps you’ll give one name a thank-you this week. Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their list private. Be gentle: some may find it hard to name a team-mate or helper; they can write “a teacher” or “someone at church”.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Apollos, a brilliant speaker, and how Priscilla and Aquila help him in a kind and clever way.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Apollos Comes Home for Supper":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:1–3; 18:1–4, 24–28; 4:36–37; 2 Timothy 1:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Apollos Comes Home for Supper</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for team-mates — This week we practise praying for the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila prayed and worked beside Paul every day. Pick one team-mate (at school, sport, church or home) and pray for them by name: ask God to bless them, help them, and help you work well together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Priscilla and Aquila helped Apollos know Jesus more fully.</li><li>We are learning why kindness and humility matter when someone needs to learn more.</li><li>I can explain how Priscilla and Aquila handled a hard moment in a kind way (Acts 18:24–28).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Apollos was a gifted speaker who only knew part of the story, that Priscilla and Aquila invited him home instead of correcting him publicly, and that Apollos listened and learned and then helped others.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a bowl of bread or crackers on a table for the role-play</li><li>Optional: a map showing Alexandria, Ephesus and Corinth</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Apollos Comes Home for Supper</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The right way to say it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine a friend is explaining something and gets part of it wrong, perhaps the rules of a game. How could you help them?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What’s a kind way to help someone get it right?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three ideas: ask a question, show them privately, say what they got right first, offer to explain over lunch, not laugh or correct them in front of others. Recap: ‘Yesterday we met Timothy and his family. Today we meet Apollos, and the tentmakers who helped him with exactly this problem.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 18:24–26</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 18:24</b> He was a very good speaker and knew a lot about the Scriptures.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 18:26</b> Apollos started speaking bravely in the synagogue.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 18:26</b> But when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him to their home and helped him understand God’s Way even better.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Apollos came from Alexandria in Egypt, a city famous for its learning. Acts 18:25 says he taught accurately about Jesus but knew only the baptism of John, the baptism that told people to get ready for the Messiah. ‘God’s Way’ is an early name for following Jesus. Messiah means God’s promised King.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Apollos comes home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Now we travel to Ephesus, a grand city on the coast of what is now Turkey, with a huge theatre and a famous temple. Priscilla and Aquila have moved here to help Paul, and they’ve opened their home to the believers.</p><p>One Sabbath, they go to the synagogue, the Jewish meeting place, and a new visitor stands up to speak. His name is Apollos. He’s from Alexandria in Egypt, a city famous for its libraries and scholars. And Apollos is brilliant. The Bible says he was a very good speaker who knew a lot about the Scriptures. He speaks with fire and energy, and he teaches about Jesus bravely. People lean forward to listen.</p><p>But at the back, Priscilla and Aquila notice something. Apollos is saying true things about Jesus, but he only seems to know part of the story. The Bible says he knew only about the baptism of John, the teaching that got people ready for Jesus. He hasn’t heard everything about what Jesus has done since.</p><p>Now, what would you do? Maybe you would feel too shy to say anything. After all, Apollos is the clever one. Maybe you would be tempted to stand up and correct him in front of everyone, or whisper about him afterwards. Priscilla and Aquila do neither.</p><p>They wait until he finishes. Then they walk up and say, ‘Apollos, that was wonderful. Would you come to our home?’</p><p>So he does. They sit around the table with bread and lamps, and the Bible says they took him to their home and helped him understand God’s Way even better. We don’t know their exact words, so we imagine them speaking kindly and clearly, with a scroll open between them.</p><p>And Apollos? Here is the second beautiful thing. He doesn’t get offended. He doesn’t say, ‘Who are you to teach me?’ He listens. He learns. A great teacher is willing to be taught.</p><p>Later, Apollos wants to go to Greece. The believers in Ephesus encourage him, and write to the followers there to welcome him. And when he arrives, the Bible says he is a great help to the believers, showing from the Scriptures that Jesus is the Messiah, God’s promised King.</p><p>See what happened? Priscilla and Aquila weren’t famous teachers. They were tentmakers with a kitchen table. But because they were brave and kind, a gifted speaker became even more useful to God. That is what happens when ordinary people serve, and when we are humble enough to keep learning.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A brilliant speaker arrives in Ephesus. · 2. Priscilla and Aquila notice. · 3. They don’t shame him. They invite him home. · 4. Apollos listens and learns. · 5. He becomes a great help.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Source: Acts 18:24–28. Documented: Apollos was from Alexandria, a gifted speaker who knew the Scriptures and taught about Jesus, but knew only the baptism of John; Priscilla and Aquila took him home and explained more; the believers encouraged him to go to Achaia (Greece); he was a great help there. Imagined: the exact words and the supper. Acts names Priscilla first here, which suggests she may have been a leader in the teaching; we do not make a point of that, but do not read their roles as less than equal. Be clear that Apollos wasn’t a bad person: he was honest, willing and brilliant, just missing part of the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did they take him?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Priscilla and Aquila do after they heard Apollos speak?<br>A) They argued with him in the synagogue &nbsp; B) They took him to their home ✓ &nbsp; C) They sent him away &nbsp; D) They told everyone his mistake</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, they took him to their home and helped him understand God’s Way even better (Acts 18:26). Follow-up: ‘What did Apollos know well?’ (The Scriptures.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was missing?’ (He knew only John’s baptism.) If silent: re-read the last scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Supper With Apollos</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Supper With Apollos</b> (Acts 18:24–28). Acts 18:24–28 tells us only that Priscilla and Aquila heard Apollos, took him home and explained God’s Way better, and that he later went to Greece and helped the believers. The conversation, the supper, and the Listener in the synagogue are all imagined. Nobody in this scene is quoting Scripture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Apollos</b> — Brilliant, bold and humble enough to learn<br><b>Priscilla</b> — Kind and clever — explains with care<br><b>Aquila</b> — Gentle, steady and welcoming<br><b>Listener</b> — A synagogue visitor, impressed (imagined)<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd, then the cheering friends</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: lean forward and nod as a crowd listening · Everyone: show a lightbulb moment when Apollos understands<br>Props: Optional: a bowl of bread or crackers · Optional: a rolled-up paper as a scroll<br>Imagined, based on Acts 18:24–28. Play Priscilla and Aquila warmly and quietly: the scene is about kindness, not about winning. Apollos is a good man, not a villain. Keep the “whisper” moment light and not gossipy.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A synagogue in Ephesus on the Sabbath. A visitor from Alexandria stands up to speak.</li><li><b>Apollos:</b> Friends, the Scriptures point to Jesus! Listen closely, because this is good news for everyone. <i>(big, bold gestures)</i></li><li><b>Listener:</b> He’s brilliant! I’ve never heard anyone speak the Scriptures like that. <i>(whisper to a neighbour)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Wow! Amazing! What a speaker! <i>(lean forward and nod)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> At the back, Priscilla and Aquila listen carefully. They notice that something is missing.</li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> He’s so bold, and everything he says is true. But he only knows part of the story. <i>(whisper to Aquila)</i></li><li><b>Aquila:</b> We could correct him in front of everyone. Or we could whisper about him. Or… <i>(raise eyebrows)</i></li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> Or we could invite him home for supper and explain the rest, kindly.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Name two unkind ways Priscilla and Aquila could have handled this. Why is inviting him home better? (25s)</li><li><b>Aquila:</b> Apollos! That was wonderful. Would you come to our home for supper tonight? <i>(step forward and smile)</i></li><li><b>Apollos:</b> Me? Thank you, I’d be glad to. I’m always happy to learn more about Jesus.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Around the table, with lamps and bread, Priscilla and Aquila helped him understand God’s Way even better.</li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> Let us tell you everything we know about Jesus, from the beginning right to the end. <i>(open the scroll)</i></li><li><b>Apollos:</b> I didn’t know all of that! Please, tell me more. I want to understand properly. <i>(lean in)</i></li><li><b>Aquila:</b> And when you’ve heard it all, you’ll tell it even better than we can.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ohhh! Now I see! <i>(lightbulb gesture above the head)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Later, Apollos sailed to Greece. The believers wrote to welcome him, and he was a great help.</li><li><b>Apollos:</b> Thank you for not shaming me. You gave me the rest of the story, and you gave it kindly.</li><li><b>Priscilla:</b> That’s what friends do. We’re all on the same team.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ordinary people, kind hearts, and God makes a team! <i>(hands together in the middle)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did Priscilla and Aquila invite Apollos home instead of correcting him in front of everyone?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They wanted to help, not embarrass him. A private, kind conversation protects someone’s dignity and makes it easier to listen.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did Apollos react when they explained more?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He listened and learned. He wasn’t offended. That humility made him even more useful to God.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Humble enough to learn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Apollos was a brilliant speaker, but he listened and learned. Why is it hard to learn from someone who isn’t as famous or clever as we are, and why did it matter?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: pride gets in the way; we want to look clever; he would have missed the full story; his humility helped many others. Follow-up: ‘When has someone helped you learn something you didn’t want to hear?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps us stay teachable?’ If silent: ask them to write one word that describes Apollos’s attitude.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Mini-whiteboards · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Kind and honest</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Priscilla and Aquila told Apollos the truth, but they did it kindly. How can we be both honest and kind to a team-mate?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: talk privately, start with what is good, ask questions, offer to help, choose a good moment, speak gently. Follow-up: ‘When have you been corrected in a kind way, and when in an unkind way? How did each feel?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What would you want someone to do for you?’ If silent: ask how Apollos would have felt if they had corrected him publicly.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Serving others includes helping them gently and kindly, and being humble enough to learn from others.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, make me kind and honest, and teachable when someone helps me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, if someone makes a mistake in front of you, choose to help quietly and kindly, or choose to say thank you when someone helps you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it practical. Examples: a friend who gets a game rule wrong; a sibling who forgets a job; a team-mate who needs help. The aim is a kind word, not a lecture.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Supper at Priscilla’s</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Would you come to our home?”<br>Kindness turned a mistake into a team.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Colossians 3:23: sleeves, stitch, point up, shake head, tap floor, stand tall.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for team-mates — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner and pray for each other as team-mates. Priscilla, Aquila and Apollos were a team, and kind prayer helps teams stay kind. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Take turns to tell each other one thing a team you’re on (class, sport, family) needs this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you gave Apollos the rest of the story through two friends. / God, you use ordinary people to help each other know you.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you use friends to help us know you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have you been unkind, or too proud to listen?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’ve shamed someone instead of helping them. / Sorry for being too proud to learn from others.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for being unkind and too proud to learn.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for someone who has helped you learn.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who explain things kindly. / Thank you for friends who help me when I get it wrong.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who help us learn.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner’s team.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) and their team work well together and be kind. / Please help me be teachable and gentle with my team-mates.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help our teams be kind, gentle and teachable.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for your team. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a student doesn’t feel part of a team, they can pray for “my class” or “my family”.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Barnabas the encourager, what this week shows us about God, and a challenge about encouraging someone by name.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Barnabas the Encourager":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 16:1–3; 18:1–4, 24–28; 4:36–37; 2 Timothy 1:5</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Barnabas the Encourager</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for team-mates — This week we practise praying for the people we work, learn and play beside. Priscilla and Aquila prayed and worked beside Paul every day. Pick one team-mate (at school, sport, church or home) and pray for them by name: ask God to bless them, help them, and help you work well together.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Barnabas was and how he encouraged others.</li><li>We are learning what this week shows us about God, who uses ordinary people.</li><li>I can plan one real, specific way to encourage someone by name (Colossians 3:23).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can name Priscilla, Aquila, Timothy, Apollos and Barnabas; explain that God uses ordinary people; describe what Barnabas’s name means; and recite Colossians 3:23.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Small paper cards or sticky notes for encouragement notes</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Barnabas the Encourager</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Our week in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put our week’s ordinary heroes in order, Monday to Thursday!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul meets Priscilla and Aquila in Corinth → Timothy learns about God from Lois and Eunice → Paul invites Timothy to travel with him → Priscilla and Aquila invite Apollos home → Barnabas sells his field to help others</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next story; click to place it. Ask: ‘Which hero would you be most like?’ Point out that none of them was famous.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 4:36–37 · Colossians 3:23</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:36</b> Joseph was a Levite from Cyprus, and the apostles called him Barnabas, which means “one who encourages others.”</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 4:37</b> Joseph was one of the followers who had sold a piece of property and brought the money to the apostles.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Colossians 3:23</b> Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>A Levite was from the tribe that served in the temple. To encourage someone is to put courage (heart) into them. Colossians 3:23 is our memory verse. Paul wrote it to people at work, where nobody might notice them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Barnabas and the God who uses ordinary people</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does this week show us about God? Look at who he used: a tentmaker couple, a shy young man, a grandmother, and a man named Joseph who got a new nickname.</p><p>Joseph came from the island of Cyprus. When the first believers in Jerusalem were sharing what they had so that nobody went without, Joseph sold a piece of land and brought the money to the apostles. And they gave him a new name: Barnabas, which means ‘one who encourages others’. Imagine being known for that!</p><p>Barnabas lived up to it. When everyone was afraid of Saul, who had once hurt Jesus’ followers, Barnabas took him by the hand and brought him to the apostles. He later stood beside Paul on their first big trip. And when a young man named John Mark had let them down, Barnabas wanted to give him another go. Years later, Paul wrote that Mark was useful to him. Encouragement changed that.</p><p>Here is what it shows about God. He doesn’t only use the famous or the clever. He uses tentmakers and shy teenagers and people who sell their fields and say, ‘Keep going. I believe in you.’ And Jesus himself, the greatest servant of all, said he came not to be served but to serve, and to give his life to rescue many.</p><p>So what can we do? Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself. Imagine a netball court at school today: someone misses a shot, and you jog over, give a high five and say their name and one true thing: ‘Great defence, Mia.’ That’s Barnabas. Small, specific and real.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Joseph gets a new name. · 2. He believed in people. · 3. God uses ordinary people. · 4. Your turn: one real thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Documented: Acts 4:36–37 (Joseph/Barnabas, a Levite from Cyprus, sold a field and gave the money); Acts 9:26–27 (Barnabas brought Saul to the apostles); Acts 13–15 (travelled with Paul; took John Mark); 2 Timothy 4:11 (Paul calls Mark useful). Mark 10:45 is paraphrased. The netball scene and the name Mia are imagined: a modern example only. Be gentle with students who find encouragement hard; remind them it can be a quiet note or a smile as well as words.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What does Barnabas mean?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What does the name Barnabas mean?<br>A) Son of thunder &nbsp; B) One who encourages others ✓ &nbsp; C) A strong builder &nbsp; D) The tentmaker</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, one who encourages others (Acts 4:36). Follow-up: ‘What was Barnabas’s name before the apostles gave him this one?’ (Joseph.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did he do to help the early believers?’ (Sold his field and gave the money.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God’s kind of hero</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Our heroes this week were tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother and an encourager. What does that tell us about the kind of people God uses?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God uses ordinary people; he looks at hearts, not fame; every job matters, even small or hidden ones; anyone can be part of his team. Follow-up: ‘Which hero do you think had the hardest job?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could God do through you where you are?’ If silent: ask which hero they would most like to be, and why.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work.</li><li>Kindness, humility and encouragement are real service, whether anyone sees it or not.</li><li>Whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself, and Jesus, the greatest servant, shows us how.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God uses ordinary people (tentmakers, a shy young man, a grandmother, an encourager) to do his work, and whatever we do, we can do it willingly for the Lord himself.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Does small count?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Small, hidden jobs don’t matter to God.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: Priscilla and Aquila’s stitching, Lois and Eunice’s lamp-lit lessons and Barnabas’s gift were all small and hidden, yet God used them. Follow-up: ‘What small, hidden job do you do?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who is watching when nobody else is?’ (The Lord, Colossians 3:23.) If silent: ask what would happen if nobody did the small jobs.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Encourage one person by name. Say or write one real, specific thing, such as “You were kind to him today” or “Your drawing made me smile”.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Give out paper cards. Examples: a teacher, a team-mate, a sibling, a grandparent, a neighbour. Specific beats generic: ‘You were really patient with the little ones’ beats ‘Good job’. Remind students it must be true. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Do your work willingly, as though you were serving the Lord himself, and not just your earthly master.” — Colossians 3:23 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Colossians 3:23. Bonus: say Acts 16:31 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Ordinary heroes</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ordinary people. A faithful God.<br>Whatever you do, do it willingly for the Lord.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying for team-mates — stand in a circle</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand in a circle, like a team before a game. Praying for team-mates means calling out their names to God, and Barnabas-style, thanking God for what is good in them. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up and make a circle, or just stand where you are. Imagine your team-mates standing beside you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is. Think about the ordinary people he uses.<br>Prompts: God, you use tentmakers, grandmothers and encouragers. / God, you give courage to the shy and strength to the weary.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you use ordinary people, and you give us courage!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about a time you could have encouraged someone and didn’t.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I said nothing when I could have encouraged. / Sorry for putting people down instead of lifting them up.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we haven’t encouraged people, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out the name of someone who has encouraged you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the people who cheer me on, like Barnabas. / Thank you for team-mates who help me and teach me.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who encourage us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for the person you’ll encourage this week, and for your team-mates.<br>Prompts: Please help me find one real thing to say to (name) and give me courage to say it. / Please bless my team-mates, and help us all work willingly for you.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, bless our team-mates and help us encourage one another.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put a hand on the shoulder of the person beside you, or just nod to them. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Standing is optional; students who prefer to sit can. Touching a shoulder is optional too; a nod is enough. Celebrate short, honest prayers.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Storm, Shipwreck and Rome. Paul sails for Rome, a terrible storm hits the ship, and God keeps his promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 7: Storm, Shipwreck and Rome":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 27–28; Philippians 1:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Keeps His Promises</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying in a storm — A storm prayer can be very short. Paul didn’t make a speech in the storm; he trusted God and he gave thanks. This week we practise one-sentence prayers for stormy moments: “God, I trust you.” “Help!” “Thank you, God, you are with me.” Short prayers count, and God hears every one.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Storm and the Angel’s Message</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning why Paul was on a ship in a terrible storm, and what an angel told him there.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Storm and the Angel’s Message → No sun, no stars → Acts 27:20, 24–25 → The storm → The angel’s message → Where did the courage come from? → Take Courage! → Scared and trusting → Head · Heart · Hands → No sun, no stars → Memory verse peek → Praying in a storm — fists and open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Bread Before Dawn</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened on the fourteenth night, before the ship reached land.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Bread Before Dawn → Recap → Acts 27:25, 37, 44 → Bread before dawn → Promise and result → How many? → Thanks in the storm → Saved too → Head · Heart · Hands → Everyone safe → Build the verse → Praying in a storm — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Fire in the Rain</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how the people of Malta welcomed 276 cold, wet strangers.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Fire in the Rain → A warm welcome → Acts 28:2, 5, 8 → Kindness on a beach → The welcome → A Fire in the Rain → God’s kind helpers → Sticks in a storm → Head · Heart · Hands → A snake in the firelight → Memory verse: scramble → Praying in a storm — write it down</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Still Preaching in Rome</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how Paul finally reached Rome, and what he did there while under guard.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Still Preaching in Rome → True or false? → Acts 28:30–31; Philippians 1:12 → Rome at last → Where did Paul end up? → A wilder road → This week’s big idea → Easy road? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Storm, shipwreck and Rome → Praying in a storm — one sentence aloud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Storm and the Angel’s Message":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 27–28; Philippians 1:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Storm and the Angel’s Message</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying in a storm — A storm prayer can be very short. Paul didn’t make a speech in the storm; he trusted God and he gave thanks. This week we practise one-sentence prayers for stormy moments: “God, I trust you.” “Help!” “Thank you, God, you are with me.” Short prayers count, and God hears every one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why Paul was on a ship in a terrible storm, and what an angel told him there.</li><li>We are learning what Paul did when everyone else gave up hope.</li><li>I can explain why Paul said, “Have courage!” (Acts 27:25).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Paul was a prisoner sailing to Rome, that the storm lasted many days with no sun or stars, that an angel promised everyone on board would live, and that Paul trusted God’s word even though the storm hadn’t changed.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a length of rope for the “hold on” moment in the role-play</li><li>Optional: a spray bottle or a tray of water for storm sound effects (use gently)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Storm and the Angel’s Message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. A quiet voice helps here: this is a big storm story, but it is mostly about trust, not fear.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: No sun, no stars</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine you’re on a boat in the middle of the sea. A storm hides the sun and the stars for days, so nobody knows where you are. What would you be thinking?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do people usually do when they feel completely out of control?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Priscilla, Aquila, Timothy and Barnabas, ordinary people who served God in ordinary ways. Your challenge: encourage one person by name by saying or writing one real, specific thing. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students who they encouraged and what they said, and how the person reacted. Then the hook: scared, panicking, wanting to go home, praying, getting angry. Land it: ‘Today Paul is on a ship in exactly that situation. Listen for what he does.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 27:20, 24–25</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:20</b> For several days we could not see either the sun or the stars, and we were being badly tossed around by the storm. We finally gave up all hope of being saved.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:24</b> Paul, don’t be afraid! You will stand trial before the Emperor, and because of you, God will save the lives of everyone on the ship.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:25</b> Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 27:25 is our memory verse. The first line is Luke’s: the book of Acts is written by Luke, who was on this ship (the story says “we”). The angel’s words in verse 24 and Paul’s words in verse 25 are the heart of the story. “Saved” in verse 20 means rescued from the storm.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Paul is a prisoner, and he is on his way to Rome. He asked to have his case heard by the Roman Emperor, so a Roman officer named Julius is taking him there by ship, along with other prisoners, soldiers, sailors and Paul’s friends. One of those friends is Luke, who later wrote this story down. Some time before, the Lord had told Paul, ‘You must tell people about me in Rome too.’ So Paul is sure of one thing: God wants him to get there.</p><p>It is already autumn, the time of year when sailing across the sea becomes dangerous. When the ship stops at a harbour on the island of Crete, Paul speaks up. ‘Friends, if we sail on now, there will be terrible damage and loss.’ But the ship’s captain and owner want to keep going, and so does nearly everyone else. Then a gentle south wind begins to blow. ‘Perfect!’ they think, and off they go.</p><p>It does not last. Soon a wild wind roars down from the island and grabs the ship. The sailors can’t steer. They tie ropes right round the hull to stop it falling apart. They throw the heavy cargo into the sea. The next day, they throw out the ship’s own equipment. Day after day the sun and the stars stay hidden behind the storm, and without them the sailors can’t tell where they are. The waves crash, the timbers groan, and nobody has eaten properly for days. Luke writes, ‘We finally gave up all hope of being saved.’</p><p>And then, in the middle of that terrible time, Paul stands up.</p><p>‘Friends, you should have listened to me,’ he says. ‘But now, please, take courage. Not one of you will die. Only the ship will be lost. Last night an angel of the God I belong to, the God I worship, stood beside me. He said, “Paul, don’t be afraid. You will stand before the Emperor, and God has given you the lives of everyone sailing with you.”’</p><p>Then Paul says the words that are our memory verse this week: ‘Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me. But first we will be shipwrecked on some island.’</p><p>Notice what Paul does not say. He does not say, ‘The storm is nearly over.’ The waves are just as big. Paul is just as wet. But he has heard from God, and he knows God keeps his promises.</p><p>So here is the question for our classroom: when the waves are loud, whose voice will you listen to?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A prisoner on a ship. · 2. A wild wind. · 3. All hope gone. · 4. Then Paul stands up. · 5. He trusts what God said, not what the waves say.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Source: Acts 27:1–26. Scripture: Paul’s warning at Crete (27:10), the wild wind, throwing cargo and gear overboard (27:14–19), no sun or stars (27:20), Paul’s speech and the angel’s message (27:21–26), and the fact that Luke was aboard (the “we” passages). Imagined: tone and small gestures only. Luke’s name is not mentioned in the sea story itself; Christians have long understood him to be the “we” writer, so we say so gently. The Lord’s earlier words to Paul are Acts 23:11, paraphrased. SENSITIVE: keep the storm exciting but not frightening. Canterbury students may carry memories of earthquakes and some may have had scary experiences on boats or in storms; keep the focus on God’s promise, not on danger, and reassure that this story ends safely.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The angel’s message</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the angel promise about the people on the ship?<br>A) God would save the lives of everyone on board ✓ &nbsp; B) Only Paul would live &nbsp; C) The ship would not be damaged &nbsp; D) The storm would end that night</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, God would save the lives of everyone on the ship (Acts 27:24). Follow-up: ‘What did Paul say would happen to the ship?’ (It would be lost; they would be shipwrecked on an island.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who did the angel say Paul would stand before?’ (The Emperor.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did the courage come from?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul was a prisoner on a ship that was being smashed by waves, and he told everyone to take courage. Where do you think his courage came from?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God had spoken to him; he trusted God’s promise; he remembered God’s word to him about Rome; faith, not feelings. Follow-up: ‘Was his courage about the storm getting smaller?’ (No. The storm stayed the same.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is the difference between being brave and trusting God?’ If silent: ask what Paul had that the others didn’t.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Take Courage!</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Take Courage!</b> (Acts 27:13–26). The angel’s words (Acts 27:24) and Paul’s words about courage (27:22–25) are Scripture, kept close to the CEV. Julius the officer is a real person in Acts, but his lines are imagined. The sailor and the soldier are invented characters, and so are their chatter, their fears and Paul’s kind reply about being scared. They are there to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Paul</b> — A prisoner, calm and trusting<br><b>Julius</b> — The Roman officer in charge of the prisoners<br><b>Sailor</b> — An invented sailor, exhausted and scared<br><b>Soldier</b> — An invented soldier who doesn’t believe it yet<br><b>Angel</b> — Heard, not seen — a clear, steady reader<br><b>Everyone</b> — The storm, then the crew holding on together</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the storm, whoosh with your voices and rock side to side · Everyone: grip a pretend rope and hold on tight, then stand tall<br>Props: Optional: a length of rope the Everyone group can hold<br>Scripture-close lines: the angel’s two lines (Acts 27:24) and Paul’s “Have courage…just as his angel told me” (27:25) and “the ship will be lost… shipwrecked on some island” (27:22, 26). Imagined: the sailor, the soldier and their chatter, Julius’s questions, and Paul’s reply about being scared. Let the storm be big and silly, then make the final line quiet and strong. The Angel is a voice only: have a calm reader stand slightly apart and read it clearly. Keep the rope moment gentle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Night on the sea. The storm has raged for days. Nobody has seen the sun or the stars.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Whoooosh! Crash! Splash! <i>(rock side to side, wiggle fingers for rain)</i></li><li><b>Sailor:</b> Another wave! Heave the cargo over the side! Quick, quick! <i>(heave a pretend sack)</i></li><li><b>Soldier:</b> I have never been so wet in my whole life. How long has it been? <i>(wring out a sleeve)</i></li><li><b>Sailor:</b> Days. Without the sun or the stars, I can’t even tell where we are. <i>(shake head)</i></li><li><b>Julius:</b> Keep working. Hold the ropes tight. We must not let this ship break apart. <i>(point firmly)</i></li><li class='dir'>Then, in the middle of all the fear, Paul stands up.</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Friends, please listen. You have not eaten for days. But take courage. Not one of you will die. <i>(stand steady, one hand up)</i></li><li><b>Soldier:</b> Nobody will die? Look at those waves! How can you say that? <i>(wave arm at the storm)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Paul is a prisoner on a ship that is breaking up, and he says, ‘Take courage.’ Would you believe him? Why or why not? (20s)</li><li><b>Paul:</b> Last night an angel of the God I belong to and worship stood beside me. <i>(look up)</i></li><li><b>Angel:</b> Paul, don’t be afraid! You will stand trial before the Emperor.</li><li><b>Angel:</b> And because of you, God will save the lives of everyone on the ship.</li><li><b>Sailor:</b> An angel? Out here? In this? <i>(stare)</i></li><li><b>Julius:</b> You are a prisoner, Paul. Why should I trust this message? <i>(arms folded)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> Because I trust the God who sent it. Men, have courage! It will happen just as his angel told me. <i>(calm and kind)</i></li><li><b>Soldier:</b> I’m still scared, though. <i>(small voice)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> So am I, a little. Let’s be scared and trust God together. <i>(put a hand on his shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> But the ship will be lost. We will run aground on an island. <i>(nod slowly)</i></li><li><b>Sailor:</b> I’ll take that. I would rather lose a ship than a life. <i>(a tired smile)</i></li><li><b>Julius:</b> Then we hold on, all of us, until morning. <i>(grip the rope)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Take courage! Hold on! <i>(grip the pretend rope, then stand up tall)</i></li><li class='dir'>The storm still roared. But now everyone on board had a promise to hold on to.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the angel’s message to Paul?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: ‘Don’t be afraid. You will stand trial before the Emperor, and God will save the lives of everyone on the ship.’ (Acts 27:24.) Follow-up: ‘What would happen to the ship?’ (It would be lost.)</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul still felt scared, but he said ‘take courage’. What was he holding on to?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God’s promise: what God had said, not how the storm looked. Courage is not no fear; it is trusting God while we are afraid.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Scared and trusting</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The angel told Paul, “Don’t be afraid,” but Paul was still on the same ship in the same storm. Can a person feel scared and still trust God? How?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: yes, trust is a choice not a feeling; Paul could be scared and still hold on to God’s word; Jesus’ friends were often afraid and he stayed with them. Follow-up: ‘What helps you trust when you feel scared?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What could you say to God in a scary moment?’ If silent: ask what the soldier in our role-play said, ‘I’m still scared’, and what Paul said back.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God keeps his promises, so we can take courage even in a storm that hasn’t gone away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, when I’m scared, help me remember that you have spoken and I can trust you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When a worry storm hits today or this week (a test, a new place, a fight with a friend, thunder at night), whisper “I trust God” once, take one slow breath, then do the next small thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable. Some students are carrying real worries; don’t push them to say what they are. Model it: whisper ‘I trust God’ together once.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: No sun, no stars</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“We finally gave up all hope of being saved.”<br>“Men, have courage!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Acts 27:25. Echo each chunk with its action: say it, they say it back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying in a storm — fists and open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This term we’re praying like the early church. This week: praying in a storm. A storm prayer can be one short sentence, like “God, I trust you.” Fists show how tightly we hold on when we are scared; open hands show we let God hold us. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Make two tight fists, like holding the ship’s rope in a storm. Take a slow breath. We’ll open our hands bit by bit as we pray.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open your hands a little. God spoke to Paul in the middle of the storm.<br>Prompts: God, you are stronger than any wind or wave. / God, you sent an angel to say “Don’t be afraid,” and you keep your word.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are stronger than every storm, and you keep your promises!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn your hands over, palms down. Tell God about the times we only listened to the loud voice of fear.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I only listened to my fears, not to what you say. / Sorry for the times I ignored good advice, like the sailors ignored Paul’s warning at Crete.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for listening to our fears more than to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up. Thank God that nobody on that ship, and nobody in this room, faces a storm alone.<br>Prompts: Thank you that Paul was not alone on that ship, and that I’m not alone either. / Thank you for the people who say “take courage” to me when I’m scared.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that you are with us in every storm.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hands open wide. Say one sentence about a storm you or someone you know is in.<br>Prompts: Please help me say “I trust God” when I’m scared. / Please be with people today who are in storms: real ones at sea, storms of worry, or storms of sickness.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us trust you in every storm.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say our storm prayer together, with open hands: “God, I trust you.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first: ‘God, I’m scared about ___, but I trust you.’ Students pray silently or aloud. Be gentle: some students face a real storm at home or at school. Short and simple is great.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the fourteenth night, a surprising prayer over bread before dawn, and a shipwreck where God keeps his promise.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Bread Before Dawn":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 27–28; Philippians 1:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bread Before Dawn</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying in a storm — A storm prayer can be very short. Paul didn’t make a speech in the storm; he trusted God and he gave thanks. This week we practise one-sentence prayers for stormy moments: “God, I trust you.” “Help!” “Thank you, God, you are with me.” Short prayers count, and God hears every one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened on the fourteenth night, before the ship reached land.</li><li>We are learning how Paul gave thanks for bread in the middle of the storm.</li><li>I can explain how God kept his promise to save everyone on the ship.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling the fourteenth night, the sailors’ attempt to leave in the lifeboat, Paul giving thanks for bread in front of everyone, the ship running aground, and all 276 people reaching land safely, just as the angel said.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a loaf or a bread roll to break and share (check allergies first)</li><li>Paper and pens for the apply activity</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Bread Before Dawn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. If you brought bread, keep it covered until the story slide.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we were on a ship in a terrible storm, and an angel gave Paul a promise.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the angel promise about the people on the ship?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God would save the lives of everyone on the ship, though the ship itself would be lost. Then: ‘The storm hasn’t stopped. Today we find out what Paul does on the fourteenth night, and how God keeps that promise.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 27:25, 37, 44</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:25</b> Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:37</b> There were 276 of us on the ship.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 27:44</b> This is how everyone made it safely to shore.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts 27:44 is Luke’s summary: the others held on to planks or pieces of the ship, and everyone reached land. Some ancient copies give a different number for the people on board; we use the one in most Bibles, 276.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Bread before dawn</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we left a ship tossed around in a storm, with a promise from God ringing in the night. Today the storm is still going. It is the fourteenth night. For two weeks the ship has been driven across the dark sea, and nobody knows where it will end up.</p><p>About midnight, the sailors sense something. Land is close. They drop a weighted rope into the water and find that the sea is getting shallower. Now they are afraid of a different thing: rocks! So they throw four anchors out from the back of the ship and wait, wishing for daylight.</p><p>Then some of the sailors try something sneaky. They start lowering the little lifeboat, pretending they are putting out more anchors, ready to escape and leave everyone else behind. Paul sees it, and tells Julius and the soldiers, ‘If these men don’t stay on board, you can’t be saved.’ The soldiers cut the ropes and let the lifeboat drift away. Now everyone is in the same boat, in the most real way.</p><p>Just before dawn, Paul urges everyone to eat. ‘You have been so worried that you haven’t eaten for two weeks,’ he says. ‘Please eat something. You need it to stay alive. Not one of you will lose even a hair from your head.’ And then Paul does something wonderful. In front of all of them, soldiers, sailors and prisoners, he takes a loaf of bread, thanks God for it, breaks it, and begins to eat. In the middle of a storm, he says thank you.</p><p>It works. Everyone cheers up, and all 276 people on the ship have a good meal. Then they throw the rest of the wheat into the sea to make the ship lighter.</p><p>When daylight comes, nobody recognises the coast. But they see a bay with a beach, and they decide to try to run the ship onto it. They cut the anchors loose, hoist a small sail, and head for the shore. Then, bang! The front of the ship sticks fast in a sandbank, while the back is battered to pieces by the waves.</p><p>Some soldiers worry that the prisoners will swim away and escape. But Julius, who wants to save Paul, stops them. He orders everyone who can swim to jump in first and head for land, and the rest to follow on planks and pieces of the broken ship.</p><p>One by one, they wade out of the water, soaked, coughing and shaking. They count each other. Nobody is missing. All 276 reach the shore safely, just as God had promised. And the ship is lost, just as God had said.</p><p>God did not miss a single detail.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The fourteenth night. · 2. A thank-you in the storm. · 3. A beach. A sandbar. · 4. Everyone safe.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Source: Acts 27:27–44. Scripture: the fourteenth night, the soundings, the four anchors, the lifeboat and the cut ropes (27:30–32), Paul’s urging to eat and giving thanks for bread (27:33–36), the number 276 (27:37), throwing out the wheat, the sandbar, and everyone reaching land (27:38–44). Imagined: the sailors’ wishes for daylight and the wading. SENSITIVE: Acts 27:42 says the soldiers wanted to kill the prisoners so none could escape, and Julius stopped them. We say only that the soldiers worried about prisoners escaping and Julius said no, with no mention of how. Paul’s bread is not the Lord’s Supper; Luke uses the same words to show Paul giving thanks to God in front of everyone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Promise and result</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God made some very specific promises through the angel and Paul. Match each promise to what happened.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>What God said</th><th>What happened</th></tr><tr><td>“God will save the lives of everyone on the ship”</td><td>All 276 people reached land safely</td></tr><tr><td>“Not one of you will lose even a hair from your head”</td><td>Nobody was hurt badly enough to be lost; they counted and nobody was missing</td></tr><tr><td>“The ship will be lost”</td><td>The ship stuck on a sandbank and broke apart in the waves</td></tr><tr><td>“We will be shipwrecked on some island”</td><td>They landed on an island called Malta</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God kept every word, even the surprising parts. Not one detail was missed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. The last line of the promise in Acts 27:34 is a proverb-like way to say “you will be completely safe”. Point out that God also promised Paul would reach Rome (Acts 27:24); we’ll see that on Thursday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: How many?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “There were ________ people on the ship, and every one of them reached land safely.”<br><i>Answer: “…276.” Luke counted, and God didn’t lose a single person.</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: 276 (Acts 27:37, 44). Follow-up: ‘What did Paul do just before dawn that surprised everyone?’ (He thanked God for bread in front of them all.) Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to the ship?’ (It stuck on a sandbank and broke apart.) If silent: re-read the second scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Thanks in the storm</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul gave thanks for bread in front of everyone while the ship was still in danger. Why do you think he did that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he trusted God to keep his promise; thanking God showed he believed; it encouraged the others; he wanted them to eat and keep up their strength; thankfulness helps with fear. Follow-up: ‘What did the others do after they saw him?’ (They cheered up and ate.) Follow-up 2: ‘When has someone’s calm made you feel braver?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet, then ask for one word.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Saved too</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Even the sailors who tried to sneak off in the lifeboat were saved, because God’s promise was for everyone on the ship. What does that tell you about the kind of promise God made?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God’s promise doesn’t depend on how good people are; it depends on God; God is kind and generous even to people who don’t deserve it; it is a promise of grace. Follow-up: ‘Is that fair?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where else does God show kindness to people who don’t deserve it?’ (Jesus, who died for everyone.) If silent: ask who on the ship had earned being rescued.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God keeps every word he says, so his promises can be trusted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that your word is true, even when I can’t see how.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose one promise of Jesus, like “I will be with you always” (Matthew 28:20), and write it on a sticky note or a piece of paper. Put it where you will see it tomorrow morning, such as inside your bag, on your bedroom door, or in your lunch box.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Matthew 28:20 (CEV): “I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” Help students who struggle to choose by giving them that one. This is a gentle, private activity. Students keep their note.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Everyone safe</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">276 on board. 276 on the shore.<br>God did not miss a single detail.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Acts 27:25 chunk by chunk. Ask: ‘When did Paul say this? Was the storm over yet?’ Tomorrow we meet the kind people who build a fire.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying in a storm — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul said thank you to God right in the middle of the storm. Today we pray in pairs: one sentence for each other. Praying for a friend’s storm is one of the kindest things we can do. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to the person next to you. You’ll take turns. First you’ll say something to God, then you’ll pray one sentence for each other. You can pray very quietly.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell God what he is like, using the bread story.<br>Prompts: God, you keep every promise, down to the last detail. / God, you looked after all 276 people on that ship, and you look after us.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you keep every promise you make!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think of the times we tried to escape in the “lifeboat” instead of trusting God.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I tried to do things my own way instead of trusting you. / Sorry for the times I have been scared and forgot to thank you.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for trying to do things our own way instead of trusting you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for food and for people who encourage you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for bread, and for the food we eat every day. / Thank you for everyone on that ship who made it to the shore, and for the friends who stay with me in my storms.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the food and the friends you give us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tell your partner one thing that feels a bit stormy for you, then pray one sentence for each other.<br>Prompts: Please help my partner trust you in their stormy thing. / Please be with people who are on dangerous seas today, such as sailors and fishers, and keep them safe.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 45s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help each other and keep people safe in every storm.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say together: “God, we trust you.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Partners can say their stormy thing in just a word. Nobody has to share anything private. Stay close to students who may find pairs hard and offer to pair with them. Short and simple is great.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the island of Malta, where kind strangers light a fire in the rain, and Paul gets a very surprising visitor on his hand.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: A Fire in the Rain":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 27–28; Philippians 1:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Fire in the Rain</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying in a storm — A storm prayer can be very short. Paul didn’t make a speech in the storm; he trusted God and he gave thanks. This week we practise one-sentence prayers for stormy moments: “God, I trust you.” “Help!” “Thank you, God, you are with me.” Short prayers count, and God hears every one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the people of Malta welcomed 276 cold, wet strangers.</li><li>We are learning what happened when a snake bit Paul’s hand, and how God cared for him.</li><li>I can describe how kindness from strangers was part of God keeping his promise.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that the islanders built a fire in the rain, that a snake bit Paul’s hand and he was not hurt, that the islanders changed their minds about him twice, and that Publius’ father was healed after Paul prayed. Listen for students noticing that God used kind strangers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a torch or battery tea light as the “fire”</li><li>Optional: blankets or jackets for the “shivering survivors”</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Fire in the Rain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Remind students that yesterday ended with everybody safe on a beach.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: A warm welcome</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday all 276 people reached land. Imagine you’ve just crawled out of the sea, cold, wet and tired, onto a strange beach. What would you most need?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would it mean to you if someone you had never met welcomed you?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take three answers: warmth, food, dry clothes, a hug, to know where they were, someone friendly. Then: ‘Today we meet the people of Malta, and we find out how they welcomed a whole shipload of strangers, and what a surprise was waiting on Paul’s hand.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 28:2, 5, 8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 28:2</b> The people of the island were very friendly, and they welcomed us around a fire that they had built because it was cold and starting to rain.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 28:5</b> But Paul shook the snake off into the fire and wasn’t harmed.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 28:8</b> Paul went to his room and prayed. Then he placed his hands on the man and healed him.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>These are Luke’s words. In verse 8 the sick man is Publius’ father, who had a fever and stomach trouble. The islanders spoke a different language from the sailors and soldiers; Luke simply calls them “the people of the island”. The Bible says Paul prayed and God healed him; the healing came from God, not from Paul.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Kindness on a beach</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The sea is behind them. The shipwrecked people stagger up a rocky beach on an island called Malta, soaked to the bone. It is cold. It is raining. There are 276 of them, and nobody knows what the islanders will do.</p><p>The islanders could have stayed away. Instead, they come running. They build a huge fire and call everyone to come and get warm, because of the rain and the cold. Luke writes that the people of the island were ‘very friendly’. They are strangers who don’t even speak the same language as the sailors, and they give a whole shipload of strangers a fire, a welcome and a place to dry out.</p><p>Paul doesn’t sit back and wait to be served. Even though he’s a prisoner, tired and cold, he picks up an armful of sticks and puts them on the fire.</p><p>And out of the heat slithers a snake. A viper, a poisonous one, and it fastens onto Paul’s hand and hangs there.</p><p>The islanders gasp. ‘This man must be a murderer!’ they whisper to each other. ‘He escaped from the sea, but justice won’t let him live.’ They watch and wait, expecting something terrible.</p><p>Paul simply shakes the snake off into the fire. He is not hurt at all.</p><p>The islanders wait. And wait. Nothing happens. So they change their minds completely. ‘He must be a god!’ they say. Paul never said he was, and he certainly wasn’t. The Bible tells us that God looked after him, and that is all.</p><p>Nearby, a man named Publius, the chief man of the island, owns some land. He welcomes Julius, Paul and their friends into his home for three days, and treats them very kindly. Publius’ father is lying in bed with a fever and a bad stomach. Paul goes in to see him. He prays, puts his hands on him, and God heals him. After that, the other sick people on the island come to Paul too, and they are healed.</p><p>They stay on Malta for three months, and when it is time to go, the islanders honour them and load the ship with everything they need.</p><p>Notice who looked after Paul in this part of the story. Not the Romans. Not his own people. Strangers on an island. God had promised to save everyone on that ship, and part of his plan was a bonfire and some very kind people on a beach.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. A fire in the rain. · 2. Paul gathers sticks. · 3. A snake! · 4. Murderer? A god? · 5. Publius’ father is healed.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Source: Acts 28:1–10. Scripture: the island (Malta), the friendly islanders and the fire, the snake, the islanders’ two guesses, Publius’ welcome, his father’s healing, the other healings, the honour and gifts at the end. Imagined: Paul’s armful of sticks is in Acts 28:3; the whispered words are paraphrased from 28:4; the details of the weather and the viper’s heat come from the text. Paul’s reply “I’m only a man” in the role-play is imagined (Acts doesn’t record his words). SENSITIVE: some students are afraid of snakes (there are no snakes in Aotearoa); keep it matter-of-fact. Some are praying for a sick family member: be honest that God heals in his own way and time, and that this is one story of him doing it; do not promise that every prayer will end this way. Christchurch students may find a shipwreck survivor story familiar; keep it kind.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The welcome</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the islanders welcome the shipwrecked people?<br>A) They built a fire and welcomed everyone ✓ &nbsp; B) They sailed away &nbsp; C) They locked their doors &nbsp; D) They asked everyone for money</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, they built a fire and welcomed everyone around it, because it was cold and starting to rain (Acts 28:2). Follow-up: ‘How does Luke describe the islanders?’ (Very friendly.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Paul do at the fire?’ (He gathered sticks and put them on.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: A Fire in the Rain</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>A Fire in the Rain</b> (Acts 28:1–9). Acts 28:1–9 gives the rain and cold, the fire, the snake, the islanders’ two guesses, Publius’ welcome, and his father’s healing. The islanders’ chatter, Julius’ thank-you and Paul’s words (such as “I’m only a man”) are imagined. The two islanders are invented characters; Publius and Julius are real people in Acts, but their lines are imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Paul</b> — Cold, tired, and still helping<br><b>Julius</b> — The Roman officer, grateful and amazed<br><b>Islander (woman)</b> — An invented islander who takes charge of the fire<br><b>Islander (man)</b> — An invented islander who jumps to conclusions<br><b>Publius</b> — The chief man of the island, welcoming and worried<br><b>Everyone</b> — Shivering survivors, then cheering islanders</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: shiver and chatter your teeth, hug yourself · Everyone: gasp when the snake appears, then cheer when the sick man is well<br>Props: Optional: a torch as the “fire” · Optional: a rubber snake or a rolled-up scarf<br>Scripture-close lines: the cold and the fire (28:2), the snake (28:3), the islanders’ words (28:4, paraphrased), the shaking off (28:5), the second guess (28:6), Publius’ welcome (28:7), and the healing (28:8). Imagined: the chatter, Paul’s kind jokes and “I’m only a man”, Julius’ thank-you, and the islanders’ names (none are given). Keep the snake moment funny, not scary. Some students may be nervous about snakes; the toy is optional. Keep the healing gentle.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A cold, wet beach on the island of Malta. Two hundred and seventy-six shipwrecked people shiver on the sand.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Brrrr! My teeth are chattering! <i>(hug yourself and shiver)</i></li><li><b>Islander (woman):</b> Quick! More wood! These people are freezing! Build up the fire! <i>(wave arms, pile on wood)</i></li><li><b>Islander (man):</b> Where did they all come from? There must be hundreds of them! <i>(stare at the wrecked ship)</i></li><li><b>Julius:</b> A storm. Fourteen nights at sea. We have nothing to give you, but thank you. <i>(bow your head)</i></li><li><b>Islander (woman):</b> Sit by the fire. Take a blanket. We’ll find you something warm to drink. <i>(wrap a blanket round a shoulder)</i></li><li class='dir'>Paul gathers an armful of sticks and puts them on the fire.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ssssss! Hissss! <i>(wiggle a hand like a snake)</i></li><li><b>Islander (man):</b> Look! A snake! It’s fastened onto his hand! <i>(point and gasp)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The islanders think the worst. What do you think they’re whispering to each other? What would you do if you were Paul? (20s)</li><li><b>Islander (woman):</b> He must be a murderer! He escaped the sea, but justice won’t let him live. <i>(step back)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> Off you go, little one. Into the fire. <i>(calmly shake the snake off)</i></li><li class='dir'>They waited. And waited. Paul was not hurt at all.</li><li><b>Islander (man):</b> Not even swollen! Maybe he isn’t a murderer. Maybe he’s a god! <i>(whisper loudly)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> No, no. I’m only a man. It is God who looks after us. <i>(shake head, smiling)</i></li><li><b>Publius:</b> Welcome, strangers. Stay at my home. But my father is very ill with a fever. <i>(hands wide, then worried)</i></li><li><b>Paul:</b> May I see him? <i>(walk to the sick man)</i></li><li class='dir'>Paul prayed. He placed his hands on the man, and God healed him.</li><li><b>Publius:</b> Father! You’re sitting up! The fever is gone! <i>(hug the sick man)</i></li><li><b>Islander (woman):</b> Bring our sick people too! The strangers’ God has helped! <i>(wave people over)</i></li><li><b>Julius:</b> We came out of the storm, and kindness met us on this beach. We won’t forget it. <i>(hand on heart)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Thank you, God! Thank you, Malta! <i>(cheer and stand tall)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">How did the islanders change their minds about Paul, and was either guess right?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: First they thought he was a murderer, then a god. Neither was right: he was a man God was looking after. Follow-up: ‘What did Paul say about himself in our play?’</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who showed God’s kindness in that scene? How?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: The islanders, with fire, blankets and a home; Publius with hospitality; Paul with prayer. God used ordinary kind people to keep his promise of safety.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: God’s kind helpers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God promised to save everyone on the ship, and part of how he kept that promise was through kind strangers. What does that tell you about how God looks after people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God often uses ordinary people to help; kindness can be part of God’s plan; God provides through people; he can work through people who don’t even know him yet. Follow-up: ‘Who has been a kind helper to you?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How could you be that for someone?’ If silent: ask who built the fire, and who was warmed by it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Sticks in a storm</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul was a prisoner, cold and shipwrecked, and he still went to gather sticks for the fire. Why might serving others, even when we feel stormy, help us trust God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it takes our eyes off ourselves; helping reminds us we have something to give; God can use us in a hard moment; trust shows up in what we do. Follow-up: ‘Is it easy to help when we are the ones who feel low?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What small job could you do for someone this week?’ If silent: ask what Paul could have said instead of picking up sticks.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God looks after people through kind strangers and ordinary helpers, and he keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for people who are kind to me. Make me a kind helper for someone else.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be a Malta person this week: find one classmate who looks like they’re having a stormy day and do one kind thing. Share your lunch, save them a spot, or help them carry something.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Make it concrete: playground, sports team, lunch table, bus. Remind students that kindness doesn’t need a big speech; the islanders just built a fire.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A snake in the firelight</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A snake! He must be a murderer!<br>…No, he must be a god!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: scramble</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Scramble: put the chunks back in order. Scramble Acts 27:25. Ask students to unscramble it in pairs, then say it with the actions.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying in a storm — write it down</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today we write our prayers. Writing slows us down and helps us say what we really mean. Paul’s prayers on Malta were short and simple: he prayed over a sick man and God helped. Short prayers are fine. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and paper. At the top, write: “God, I trust you in my storm.” Then write one line for each part of our prayer.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is, using today’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you looked after Paul when the snake bit his hand. / God, you use ordinary, kind people to keep your promises.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you look after your people, and you keep your promises!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: think of a time you could have been kind, like the islanders, and weren’t.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I walked past someone who needed help. / Sorry for the times I judged someone quickly, like the islanders did when they saw the snake.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for judging people and for not helping when we could.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write a thank-you for someone who has been kind to you in a hard time.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the islanders who built a fire in the rain. / Thank you for the people who have welcomed me when I felt like a stranger.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for kind people who help us in our storms.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one sentence for someone you know who is in a stormy time right now.<br>Prompts: Please be with people who are sick, and bring them help and healing. / Please help me be a kind helper to someone who is in a storm this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, look after people in storms and help us be kind helpers.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your page and keep it somewhere safe. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. Some may be praying for a sick relative: stay close and be gentle, and don’t promise any particular outcome. Short is great.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Paul finally reaches Rome, and we see why God’s promises are always kept.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Still Preaching in Rome":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Acts 27–28; Philippians 1:12–14</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Still Preaching in Rome</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying in a storm — A storm prayer can be very short. Paul didn’t make a speech in the storm; he trusted God and he gave thanks. This week we practise one-sentence prayers for stormy moments: “God, I trust you.” “Help!” “Thank you, God, you are with me.” Short prayers count, and God hears every one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Paul finally reached Rome, and what he did there while under guard.</li><li>We are learning what this story shows us about God keeping his promises.</li><li>I can say “I trust God” and pray one short sentence when things feel stormy (Acts 27:25).</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the week’s story in order, explain that God kept every promise he made on the ship, say that Paul kept telling people about Jesus even in chains, and recite Acts 27:25 with the actions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Pens</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Still Preaching in Rome</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: True or false?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">True or false? Stand up for TRUE, sit down for FALSE!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul was on the ship because he was sailing to Rome. — <b>True</b> (He was a prisoner on his way to stand before the Emperor (Acts 27:1, 24).)<br>The angel promised that the ship would be saved. — <b>False</b> (The ship would be lost, but God would save everyone’s lives (Acts 27:22, 24).)<br>Paul gave thanks for bread before dawn, in front of everyone. — <b>True</b> (He thanked God and ate, and everyone cheered up and ate too (Acts 27:35–36).)<br>Some people on the ship did not reach land. — <b>False</b> (All 276 reached land safely, just as God had promised (Acts 27:44).)<br>The islanders of Malta were unfriendly to the shipwrecked people. — <b>False</b> (They were very friendly, and built a fire in the rain (Acts 28:2).)<br>A snake bit Paul’s hand, and he shook it off into the fire. — <b>True</b> (He wasn’t harmed (Acts 28:3–5).)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Read each statement aloud. Students stand for true, sit for false. After each, ask ‘How do you know?’ and let students point to the story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Acts 28:30–31; Philippians 1:12</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 28:30</b> For two years Paul stayed in a rented house and welcomed everyone who came to see him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Acts 28:31</b> He bravely preached about God’s kingdom and taught about the Lord Jesus Christ, and no one tried to stop him.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Philippians 1:12</b> My friends, I want you to know that what has happened to me has actually helped to spread the good news.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Acts ends here, with the good news still spreading. Philippians 1:12–14 is from a letter Paul wrote to the church in Philippi (the church from Week 5) while he was a prisoner. Many Christians think he wrote it from Rome; some think another place; we say “while in prison”. “Good news” = the message about Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Rome at last</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>After three months on Malta, Paul and the others sail on in a new ship, and at last they reach Italy. News of Paul’s arrival races ahead. When he is still a long way from Rome, Christians from the city walk out along the road to meet him. When Paul sees them, he thanks God and, Luke says, he takes courage. After all that, a welcome party!</p><p>And so Paul comes to Rome, the biggest city in the world, where the Emperor lives. Just as God had promised, he is there. But Paul is not free. He is allowed to live in a rented house, but a Roman soldier guards him all the time.</p><p>So what does Paul do? Complain? Give up? No. He invites local Jewish leaders to his house, and from morning till evening he explains the kingdom of God, showing from the Scriptures that Jesus is the promised King. Some believe. Some don’t. And for two whole years, Paul welcomes everyone who comes. Luke ends his book like this: Paul ‘bravely preached about God’s kingdom and taught about the Lord Jesus Christ, and no one tried to stop him.’</p><p>Think about the soldiers who guarded Paul. They changed shifts, but each one had to sit there and listen. Paul wrote in a letter from this time, ‘What has happened to me has actually helped to spread the good news.’ His chains became a way to share Jesus.</p><p>The book of Acts does not tell us what happened to Paul after that. Luke stops there, with the good news still spreading. But we know this: God said Paul would get to Rome, through storm, shipwreck, snake and chains, and he did. God keeps his promises, even when the road is wilder than we thought. And the greatest promise of all, that Jesus would rescue us and be with us always, God has kept too.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A welcome on the road. · 2. A rented house in Rome. · 3. Chains that spread the news. · 4. God keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. Source: Acts 28:11–31 and Philippians 1:12–14. Scripture: the three months on Malta, the believers meeting Paul on the road (28:15), the rented house, the soldier guard, the talk from morning till evening, the two years and Luke’s closing line. Paul being chained to a guard is from Acts 28:16, 20. The soldiers hearing the message is the point of Philippians 1:13 (the guard knew he was in prison for Christ). Philippians is one of the letters Paul wrote while a prisoner; where exactly is debated. If a student asks what happened to Paul after Acts: Acts does not say. Christian writers soon after say he died in Rome; we do not need to go further, but we can say he trusted Jesus to the end (see 2 Timothy 4:7). Keep it calm and short. Some students may have family from places where Christians still face trouble: reassure them that Paul’s story shows God is with his people in hard places.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did Paul end up?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where is Paul at the end of the book of Acts?<br>A) In Rome, under guard, still telling people about Jesus ✓ &nbsp; B) Back home in Tarsus &nbsp; C) Still on Malta &nbsp; D) In Jerusalem</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, in Rome, in a rented house with a soldier guarding him, bravely preaching about Jesus (Acts 28:30–31). Follow-up: ‘How long did he stay there?’ (Two years.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who was allowed to visit him?’ (Everyone who came.) If silent: re-read the first scripture line.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A wilder road</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God promised Paul he would reach Rome. He did, but through a storm, a shipwreck, a snake and chains. What does that tell you about how God keeps his promises?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: God keeps them, but not always in the way we expect; the road can be hard; God is with us on the road; his promises are bigger than our plans. Follow-up: ‘What would Paul have said about God at the end of the road?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Which part of the story do you remember most, and where was God?’ If silent: ask which part of the trip looked least like a promise being kept.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>In a terrible storm, God sent an angel to Paul with a promise: everyone on the ship would live. Paul said, “Men, have courage!”</li><li>God kept every word. All 276 reached land, the ship was lost, and kind islanders on Malta looked after them.</li><li>Paul reached Rome and kept telling people about Jesus, even in chains. God keeps his promises, so we can take courage and trust him.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God keeps his promises: he brought Paul and everyone on the ship safely through the storm, so we can take courage and trust him, and Jesus is the greatest promise God has ever kept.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Have students say the memory verse, Acts 27:25, with the actions.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Easy road?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “If God makes a promise, the road to it will be easy.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: God’s promise to Paul came true, but the road went through a storm, a shipwreck, a snake and chains. God’s promises are sure, but the road isn’t always easy, and God is with us on it. Follow-up: ‘What did Paul do on the hard road?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What promise of Jesus would you hold on to in a storm?’ If silent: ask what helped Paul keep going.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">When you feel stormy, say “I trust God” (Acts 27:25) and pray one sentence, then do the next small thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Give out the take-home cards. Help students think of examples: a test, a game, a family worry, a dark bedroom, a fight with a friend. Remind them: one breath, one sentence, then the next small thing. Some students are in real storms at home; stay available. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Men, have courage! I have faith in God, and I am sure that things will happen just as his angel told me.” — Acts 27:25 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Acts 27:25. Bonus: say Acts 16:31 or Colossians 3:23 from earlier this term if your class still remembers them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Storm, shipwreck and Rome</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Men, have courage!<br>God keeps his promises.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying in a storm — one sentence aloud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Paul prayed and gave thanks in the middle of the storm, and God kept every promise. Today we practise the short “storm prayer”: one sentence aloud, like “God, I trust you.” Short is enough. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably and take a slow breath. We’ll pray out loud, one short sentence at a time. You can say yours quietly, or just say Amen with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is, from this week’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you keep every promise you make. / God, you are stronger than every storm, and you are with us in all of them.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you keep your promises, and you are stronger than any storm!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about the times we ran from him, or didn’t trust him, when we felt stormy.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I tried to escape in my own lifeboat instead of trusting you. / Sorry for the times I only listened to my fears.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for not trusting you, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing you’re thankful for from the story.<br>Prompts: Thank you that all 276 people reached land safely. / Thank you for Jesus, the greatest promise you ever kept.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for keeping your promises, and for Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Say your own storm prayer: one short sentence, aloud or silently.<br>Prompts: Please help me say “I trust God” when I feel stormy this week. / Please be with people today who are in storms, and bring them courage and help.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us trust you in every storm.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together: “God, I trust you.” Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. If any students would rather pray silently, that’s fine. Pray for Christians who are in hard places gently, without scary detail.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: Polycarp, “Eighty-Six Years I Have Served Him”. We step out of the Bible and into church history to meet an old man who stayed faithful to Jesus all his life.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 8: Polycarp: “Eighty-Six Years I Have Served Him”":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Martyrdom of Polycarp (c. AD 155); Revelation 2:8–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faithful</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for the persecuted church — “Persecuted” means treated badly because of what you believe. Polycarp prayed for Jesus’ church “all over the world”. We can do the same: we can’t be in other countries, but we can carry Christians there to God in prayer. Pray calmly and simply: ask God for courage, peace, good friends and safety for them, and thank him that they are not forgotten.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>An Hour to Pray</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning who Polycarp was and what happened when soldiers came to the farmhouse where he was staying.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>An Hour to Pray → The calmest person → Revelation 2:8–10 → The farmhouse at dusk → One small favour → Dinner for the enemy → An Hour to Pray → Careful or afraid? → Head · Heart · Hands → Coming down the stairs → Memory verse peek → Standing to pray for the persecuted church</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>After the Apostles</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning who the church leaders were after the apostles, and what Polycarp left behind.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>After the Apostles → Recap → Revelation 2:9–10 → Why the trouble? → Words to know → The loyalty test → Two Lords → Pretend inside? → Head · Heart · Hands → A loyalty test → Build the verse → Writing for the persecuted church</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>In the Stadium</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened on the road into Smyrna and in the stadium.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>In the Stadium → Just this once → Revelation 2:8, 10 · Martyrdom 9 → Into the stadium → The question again → Eighty-Six Years → A lifetime of faithfulness → A small thing? → Head · Heart · Hands → “I am a Christian” → Verse with actions → Praying in pairs for courage</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Church Remembers</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning how the church in Smyrna remembered Polycarp, and what his story shows about God.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Church Remembers → Who said it? → Revelation 2:8, 10 → What it shows about God → The letter → Worship and love → This week’s big idea → Does praying help? → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Faithful → Praying out loud for the persecuted church</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: An Hour to Pray":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Martyrdom of Polycarp (c. AD 155); Revelation 2:8–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">An Hour to Pray</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for the persecuted church — “Persecuted” means treated badly because of what you believe. Polycarp prayed for Jesus’ church “all over the world”. We can do the same: we can’t be in other countries, but we can carry Christians there to God in prayer. Pray calmly and simply: ask God for courage, peace, good friends and safety for them, and thank him that they are not forgotten.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Polycarp was and what happened when soldiers came to the farmhouse where he was staying.</li><li>We are learning how Polycarp treated the people who came to arrest him.</li><li>I can say what Polycarp asked the soldiers for, and what he did first.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Polycarp was an old church leader in Smyrna, that he chose not to run away, that he gave the soldiers food and drink, and that he asked for an hour to pray and prayed for two.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a world map or globe to find Smyrna (today’s Izmir, Turkey)</li><li>Teacher source: The Martyrdom of Polycarp, chapters 5–8 (a letter from the church in Smyrna, Roberts–Donaldson translation, newadvent.org/fathers/0102.htm)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: An Hour to Pray</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week is church history: a real person who lived about 1,900 years ago, in a city that is in Turkey today. We keep the story calm. Polycarp’s death is never described; we stop before it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: The calmest person</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think of the calmest person you know — someone who stays steady when everyone else is panicking.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What do you think makes a person able to stay calm when things are scary?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome back! Last week we heard Storm, Shipwreck and Rome — Paul on the sinking ship, trusting God’s promise. Your challenge: when you feel stormy, say “I trust God” and pray one sentence, then do the next small thing. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students when they said “I trust God” and what small thing they did next. Then the hook: a parent in an emergency, a coach, a nurse, a teacher in a fire drill. Take two or three ideas (training, knowing what to do, trusting someone). Land it: ‘Today we meet an old man in church history who stayed amazingly calm when soldiers came for him, and we find out why.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Revelation 2:8–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:8</b> I am the first and the last. I died, but now I am alive!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:9</b> I know how much you suffer and how poor you are, but you are rich.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> Don’t worry about what you will suffer.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>These are the risen Jesus’ words to the church in Smyrna, in the last book of the Bible. They were written before Polycarp was an old man, to Christians who were already facing hard times. Smyrna is today the Turkish city of Izmir. “Faithful” = loyal; keeps trusting and keeps going. Verse 10 is our memory verse.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The farmhouse at dusk</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Picture a farmhouse on a hill above the city of Smyrna, in what is now Turkey. The year is about AD 155, and the evening sky is turning orange over the olive trees.</p><p>Upstairs, in a quiet room, an old man is praying. His hair and beard are white, and his back is a little bent. His name is Polycarp, and he is the bishop of the church in Smyrna. A bishop is a church leader who looks after God’s people, a bit like a shepherd looks after sheep. Polycarp has followed Jesus for a very, very long time.</p><p>The city authorities are hunting for Christians. Christians haven’t hurt anyone. But they refuse to call the Roman emperor ‘Lord’, because for them Jesus is Lord, and that has made some people angry. Tomorrow we will look at why. Polycarp’s friends begged him to leave the city, so he moved out to this farm to stay safe.</p><p>Then comes the sound of hooves. Armed men on horses ride up the track. Someone has told them where he is.</p><p>Here is what the church in Smyrna wrote down soon afterwards. Polycarp could have slipped away to another farm. But he said, ‘The will of God be done.’ That means: I trust God, whatever happens. So he walks down the stairs, and he talks with the men. They are amazed. Why is such a gentle old man being hunted?</p><p>And then Polycarp does something nobody expects. He tells his friends to give the men food and drink. The soldiers have been riding for hours, and they are hungry. So the man they came to arrest sets a meal in front of them.</p><p>Then he asks for one small favour. He asks for an hour to pray, alone and quietly. The soldiers agree. And Polycarp stands and prays. One hour becomes two. He prays for everyone he has ever known, important people and ordinary people, and for Jesus’ church all over the world. The soldiers can’t help listening. The letter says that many of them wished they had never come after such a good old man.</p><p>Where does that calm come from? Jesus once sent a message to the church in Smyrna, and it is in the last book of the Bible. He said, ‘I am the first and the last. I died, but now I am alive!’ Polycarp wasn’t calm because he was strong. He was calm because the one who beat death was his King.</p><p>When the prayer is over, it is time to go. Tomorrow we find out why Christians were in trouble, and who taught Polycarp to follow Jesus like this.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. A farmhouse above Smyrna, about AD 155. · 2. He could have slipped away. · 3. He walks down to meet them. · 4. Then he feeds them. · 5. “Give me an hour to pray.” · 6. Why so calm?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. DOCUMENTED (the Martyrdom of Polycarp, chapters 5–8, a letter written by the church in Smyrna soon afterwards): friends persuaded him to leave the city for a country house; the pursuers came with horsemen in the evening; he could have escaped to another farm but said “The will of God be done”; he ordered food and drink for them; he asked for an hour to pray and prayed for two; he remembered everyone he had known and the whole church; many of the pursuers regretted coming. IMAGINED FRAMING: the orange sky, the olive trees, the captain’s puzzlement and ‘the soldiers can’t help listening’ is our paraphrase of the letter’s report. We leave out a detail from the letter (how the police found where he was) and a dream he reported, because they are not needed and are not for young students. Smyrna is Izmir in Turkey today. The date, about AD 155–156, is the usual one; some historians argue for a later date.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: One small favour</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Polycarp ask the soldiers for before he went with them?<br>A) A faster horse &nbsp; B) An hour to pray ✓ &nbsp; C) A map of the city &nbsp; D) A place to hide</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, an hour to pray. Follow-up: ‘What did he do for the soldiers first?’ (He gave them food and drink.) Follow-up 2: ‘How long did he actually pray?’ (Two hours.) If silent: re-read the story beat ‘Give me an hour to pray’.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Dinner for the enemy</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp served a meal to the very men who had come to arrest him. Why do you think he did that?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus taught his followers to love their enemies; Polycarp wanted to show kindness, not anger; he was calm and not afraid; he treated them as people, not just soldiers. Follow-up: ‘How does that feel different from what most people would do?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who is one person you find hard to be kind to?’ If silent: ask what Jesus said about enemies (Matthew 5:44).</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: An Hour to Pray</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>An Hour to Pray</b> (Martyrdom of Polycarp 7 (retold)). DOCUMENTED (Martyrdom of Polycarp 7): the horsemen came; he could have escaped; he said “The will of God be done”; he had food and drink put out; he asked for an hour to pray and prayed for two; he remembered all who had known him and the whole church; many regretted coming. IMAGINED: the Captain, the Young Soldier and the Friend are invented characters, and almost all the spoken words, including Polycarp’s gentle lines, are imagined to bring the story to life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Polycarp</b> — Very old, calm and kind — steady voice<br><b>Friend</b> — A worried believer who owns the farm<br><b>Captain</b> — Leader of the soldiers — gruff but puzzled<br><b>Young Soldier</b> — Hungry, curious and uneasy<br><b>Everyone</b> — Horses, then a hush</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: tap your knees like galloping horses, then stop dead · Everyone: stay perfectly still and quiet during the prayer<br>Props: Optional: a few cups and a plate of pretend bread<br>Words and actions are documented only where the imagined note says. Polycarp’s ‘He died, and he is alive’ echoes Jesus’ words in Revelation 2:8; it is our framing, not a quote from the letter. Keep the tone gentle: nobody is hurt in this role-play. Let the silence after the pause and the final line be real quiet. The role-play stops before anything else happens.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A farmhouse above Smyrna, about AD 155. It is evening. Upstairs, an old man is praying.</li><li><b>Friend:</b> Polycarp! Horsemen are coming up the track. There is still time. Another farm is only a short ride away! <i>(rush in, out of breath)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> The will of God be done. <i>(calm, steady voice)</i></li><li><b>Friend:</b> But sir, they are armed! Please, let me hide you. <i>(wring your hands)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> God is here with me, my friend. Come. We will go down and greet our visitors.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Clip-clop, clip-clop, clip-clop! <i>(tap knees like hooves, then stop suddenly)</i></li><li><b>Captain:</b> Open up! We have orders to find the bishop Polycarp. <i>(knock on an imaginary door)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> Welcome, friends. I am Polycarp. You have ridden a long way. Please come in out of the cold.</li><li><b>Young Soldier:</b> Captain, he is so old. And he is smiling at us. I thought he would be… different. <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Captain:</b> Quiet, boy. Orders are orders. Polycarp, you must come with us to the city.</li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> I will come. But first, you must be hungry. Friend, please bring bread and drink for these men. <i>(gesture to the table)</i></li><li><b>Friend:</b> For the men who came to arrest you? Yes, sir. Of course. <i>(set out pretend cups and bread)</i></li><li><b>Young Soldier:</b> Bread and olives! I haven’t eaten since morning. <i>(sit and eat)</i></li><li><b>Captain:</b> Hmm. This is the strangest arrest I have ever made. <i>(scratch your head)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> May I ask one favour? Please give me an hour to pray, with nobody disturbing me.</li><li><b>Captain:</b> An hour. Very well. We will wait. <i>(wave a hand)</i></li><li class='dir'>Polycarp stands, lifts his hands, and begins to pray. The room goes very quiet.</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Polycarp is praying for everyone he has ever known, and for Jesus’ church all over the world. What do you think the soldiers are thinking as they listen? (20s)</li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> Lord, remember everyone I have known, the great and the small. And remember your church all over the world. <i>(hands lifted, eyes closed)</i></li><li><b>Young Soldier:</b> Captain, I think he is praying for us too. I wish we had never come. <i>(put down the bread)</i></li><li><b>Captain:</b> Hush, boy. Two hours have gone by, and I have not wanted to stop him once. <i>(stare at the floor)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> Thank you for waiting, my friends. I am ready now. <i>(lower your hands)</i></li><li><b>Captain:</b> Polycarp, how can you be so calm?</li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> Because Jesus is my King. He died, and he is alive. He is with me. <i>(gentle smile)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> The will of God be done. <i>(say it quietly and steadily, then hold the silence)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The soldiers came to arrest Polycarp, but something changed in the room. What do you think changed it?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He was kind instead of angry; he fed them; he prayed for others, even for them; his calm showed them something they hadn’t expected. Kindness and calm can change a room.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which of Polycarp’s lines is a real quote from the letter the church in Smyrna wrote?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: “The will of God be done.” The idea of the food, the hour of prayer and his praying for everyone is also documented, but the wording of most lines is imagined.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Careful or afraid?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp let his friends move him to the farm to stay safe, but when the soldiers came he stayed calm. What is the difference between being careful and being afraid?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: being careful is wise and sensible, like moving to the farm; being afraid means letting fear decide what you do; Polycarp was careful, but he would not let fear make him give up Jesus; trusting God is not the same as being reckless. Follow-up: ‘When is it wise to be careful?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What helps you not let fear decide?’ If silent: ask for an example from sport or school.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp was calm and kind to the very people who came for him, because he trusted Jesus, who died and is alive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, I want to trust you so much that I can be calm and kind even when people are unkind to me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Today, be kind to someone who has been unkind to you: say a friendly word, share something, or simply don’t get them back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and doable: one kind word or act. Don’t ask students to name who has hurt them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Coming down the stairs</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The will of God be done.”<br>He didn’t run. He went down to meet them.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Revelation 2:10. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Explain “faithful” = loyal, keeps trusting and keeps going.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Standing to pray for the persecuted church</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The letter says Polycarp stood to pray, and he prayed for Jesus’ church all over the world. Stand up and pray for Christians far away who are treated badly because they follow Jesus. Pray calmly and simply: courage, peace, good friends. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand up, feet on the floor, hands open or by your sides. Take a slow breath. Like Polycarp, we are going to pray for people we have never met.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand tall. Say who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are faithful, and you never let Polycarp down. / Jesus, you are the first and the last. You died, and you are alive.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are faithful, and Jesus, you are alive!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Bow your head a little. Silently: where have you not been kind?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t been kind to someone who was unkind to me. / Sorry for the times I stay quiet about Jesus just to fit in.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we haven’t been kind.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Lift your head. Say what you are thankful for.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Polycarp, who was calm, kind and loyal for a whole lifetime. / Thank you for your church all over the world, and that we are part of it.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Polycarp, and for your church all over the world.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Open your hands. Pray for Christians who are treated badly today.<br>Prompts: Please be with Christians today who are treated unfairly because they follow Jesus. / Please give them courage, peace and good friends.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, be with…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give courage and peace to Christians who are treated unfairly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stay standing for one more breath. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep this prayer calm and general; there is no need for scary detail. Some students may have family from places where Christians are treated badly: don’t ask anyone to share, and be ready to offer a quiet word afterwards. Students of other faiths or none can stand quietly and simply listen.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: who came after the apostles, and why some Roman officials treated Christians as a problem.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: After the Apostles":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Martyrdom of Polycarp (c. AD 155); Revelation 2:8–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">After the Apostles</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for the persecuted church — “Persecuted” means treated badly because of what you believe. Polycarp prayed for Jesus’ church “all over the world”. We can do the same: we can’t be in other countries, but we can carry Christians there to God in prayer. Pray calmly and simply: ask God for courage, peace, good friends and safety for them, and thank him that they are not forgotten.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who the church leaders were after the apostles, and what Polycarp left behind.</li><li>We are learning why some Roman officials treated Christians as a problem.</li><li>I can explain what “Lord Caesar” meant and why Christians could not say it.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Christians were asked to say “Lord Caesar” and honour the emperor like a god, that Christians said “Jesus is Lord”, and that Polycarp’s story is documented while the claim that he knew the apostle John is a tradition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper or sticky notes</li><li>Teacher sources: Martyrdom of Polycarp 8–10; Polycarp’s Letter to the Philippians; Ignatius’ letter to Polycarp; Irenaeus (quoted by Eusebius, Church History 5.20); Pliny’s letter to Trajan (c. AD 112)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: After the Apostles</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s picture shows what a tradition says: a young Polycarp listening to the apostle John. We will explain that this is a tradition, not something we can prove.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday soldiers came to a farmhouse for an old man named Polycarp.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Polycarp do before he went with the soldiers, and what did that show about him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>He gave them food and drink and asked for an hour to pray (and prayed for two). It showed he was calm, kind and trusted God. ‘Today we ask: who was Polycarp, and why were soldiers sent to find a gentle old man?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Revelation 2:9–10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:9</b> I know how much you suffer and how poor you are, but you are rich.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> Don’t worry about what you will suffer. The devil will throw some of you into jail, and you will be tested and made to suffer for ten days.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus’ message to the church in Smyrna. The “ten days” means a short, limited time. Jesus says: don’t be afraid, and he promises a glorious life. Explain “rich” = rich in what matters, in Jesus. Don’t dwell on the devil; point to Jesus’ comfort.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Why the trouble?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday an old man fed his enemies and prayed for two hours. Today we ask: who was Polycarp, and why were soldiers sent to find him?</p><p>The apostles, Jesus’ first friends and helpers, had died. But the good news about Jesus kept spreading, because each generation told the next. Polycarp belonged to that next generation.</p><p>Here is a tradition, which means a story passed down. A man called Irenaeus wrote many years later that, when he was a boy, he listened to Polycarp tell what he had heard from the apostle John and from others who had seen Jesus. We can’t prove it, and some historians doubt the details. But it was believed very early, by people who knew Polycarp. So we say: tradition reports that Polycarp knew John.</p><p>Here is what we know from real letters. Another church leader, a man named Ignatius, wrote a letter to Polycarp to encourage him. And Polycarp himself wrote a letter to the church in Philippi, the same city where Paul and Silas sang in prison. We can still read it today.</p><p>Smyrna was a real city with a real church. Jesus’ message to it is in Revelation chapter 2. He said, ‘I know how much you suffer and how poor you are, but you are rich.’ And he said, ‘Don’t worry about what you will suffer.’ Christians in Smyrna knew hard times long before Polycarp was old.</p><p>Why were they in trouble? Persecution means being treated badly because of what you believe. The Roman Empire had many gods, and most people didn’t mind adding one more. But the emperor was treated like a god too. On special days, people dropped a pinch of incense on a small fire in front of the emperor’s statue and said, ‘Lord Caesar.’ It was a loyalty test.</p><p>Christians were good neighbours. Polycarp taught that Christians should respect the people in charge and pray for them. But the first Christians had a very short statement of faith: ‘Jesus is Lord.’ They could not say ‘Lord Caesar’ and mean it. And because they had no statues or temples, neighbours called them ‘atheists’, people who didn’t believe in the gods, and rumours spread.</p><p>A Roman governor named Pliny wrote to the emperor around AD 112 and described how it worked. He asked people accused of being Christians to bow to the emperor’s statue and to speak against Christ. He wrote that people who truly followed Jesus would not do it.</p><p>So the soldiers weren’t at the farmhouse because Polycarp had done anything wrong. They came because of one question: would he say ‘Lord Caesar’? Tomorrow we find out what an old man said.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. The apostles were gone. · 2. A tradition: Polycarp learned from John. · 3. Real letters, real people. · 4. Smyrna: a church in a hard place. · 5. “Lord Caesar.” · 6. “Jesus is Lord.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. DOCUMENTED: Ignatius of Antioch’s letter to Polycarp; Polycarp’s Letter to the Philippians (the only writing of his that survives); Revelation 2:8–11; the custom of offering incense to the emperor and the question of calling him ‘Lord’ (Martyrdom 8–10); Pliny’s letter to Trajan (c. AD 112). In the Martyrdom, Polycarp says Christians have been taught to honour the authorities God has put in place (chapter 10). TRADITION: that Polycarp knew the apostle John and others who had seen Jesus comes from Irenaeus, who wrote about 190 that he listened to Polycarp as a boy; it is widely repeated, but we cannot confirm the details. Image d2-a shows this tradition. We don’t know whether Polycarp was already a church leader when Revelation was written. Handle persecution calmly; some students may have family from places where Christians are still treated badly, and some students may be of another faith: keep the tone respectful. The word ‘atheist’ is explained simply.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Words to know</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Some words from this story are churchy or old. What does each one mean?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Word</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td>Bishop</td><td>A church leader who looks after God’s people, like a shepherd looks after sheep</td></tr><tr><td>Persecution</td><td>Being treated badly because of what you believe</td></tr><tr><td>Lord</td><td>The one in charge, who gets your deepest loyalty</td></tr><tr><td>Faithful</td><td>Loyal: someone who keeps trusting and keeps going</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Lord Caesar” or “Jesus is Lord”: whose word do you trust most?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. Link ‘Lord’ to the loyalty test: Christians gave Jesus their deepest loyalty, and that is why they couldn’t say “Lord Caesar” and mean it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The loyalty test</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “People were asked to burn incense and say, ‘________ Caesar’.”<br><i>Answer: “…Lord Caesar.” It was a loyalty test (Martyrdom of Polycarp 8).</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: Lord. Follow-up: ‘What did the first Christians say instead?’ (Jesus is Lord.) Follow-up 2: ‘What was the incense for?’ (Showing loyalty to the emperor like a god.) If silent: point to the ‘Lord Caesar’ story beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Two Lords</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why would saying “Lord Caesar” be a problem for someone who says “Jesus is Lord”?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: calling someone Lord means giving them your deepest loyalty; a Christian’s deepest loyalty belongs to Jesus; they couldn’t give it to two. Follow-up: ‘What things in our lives can act like a lord?’ (Fame, popularity, money, being first.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Christians still respect the emperor?’ (Prayed for him, obeyed the laws.) If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Pretend inside?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “It’s fine to say the words and just believe differently inside.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is a real puzzle; let both sides speak. Disagree: Polycarp saw that words show who we belong to, and that loyalty matters. But Peter denied Jesus and was forgiven (John 21), so we don’t say people who were afraid were beyond hope. Follow-up: ‘Why do words matter?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How does Jesus treat people who’ve failed?’ If silent: ask what Peter did after he failed.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp’s story is documented in a letter; Jesus is Lord, and that is where our deepest loyalty belongs.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, you are my Lord. Help me give you my deepest loyalty.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write on a sticky note or paper: “Jesus is Lord.” Then write one small thing you could do this week to show your loyalty to him, such as telling the truth or being kind.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small. This builds towards Thursday’s challenge about being loyal in small things.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A loyalty test</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">A pinch of incense. A few words. “Lord Caesar.”<br>Jesus is Lord.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Revelation 2:10 chunk by chunk: one chunk at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Writing for the persecuted church</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The church in Smyrna wrote a letter to tell other churches about Polycarp. Today we write our own short prayer-note for Christians who are treated badly today. Keep it simple and hopeful. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take a pen and paper. At the top write: “For Christians who are treated badly for following Jesus.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is.<br>Prompts: God, you are faithful: you were with Polycarp for eighty-six years. / Jesus, you are Lord of everything, and nobody is bigger than you.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are faithful, and Jesus, you are Lord!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: when have you gone along with the crowd instead of doing what is right?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I was afraid to be different from my friends. / Sorry for the times I forgot about people who are treated badly.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we went along with the crowd.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write three things you’re thankful for.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the letters and stories that tell us about Polycarp. / Thank you for Christians who keep going in hard places.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who stay faithful to you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one prayer for Christians who are treated unfairly.<br>Prompts: Please be with them and give them courage and peace. / Please bring them good friends and help them to feel they aren’t alone.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with Christians who are treated unfairly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your note. Pray it again tomorrow. Everyone: Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their notes private. Keep the tone calm, and avoid scary detail about what happens to Christians in some countries. Some students may have family from those places.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the stadium. An old man, a Roman governor, and one famous answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: In the Stadium":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Martyrdom of Polycarp (c. AD 155); Revelation 2:8–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">In the Stadium</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for the persecuted church — “Persecuted” means treated badly because of what you believe. Polycarp prayed for Jesus’ church “all over the world”. We can do the same: we can’t be in other countries, but we can carry Christians there to God in prayer. Pray calmly and simply: ask God for courage, peace, good friends and safety for them, and thank him that they are not forgotten.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened on the road into Smyrna and in the stadium.</li><li>We are learning what Polycarp said when he was asked to speak against Jesus.</li><li>I can say Polycarp’s answer in my own words and explain why it was so strong.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Herod and Nicetes urged him to say “Lord Caesar”, that he refused, that the proconsul asked him to speak evil of Christ, and that he answered that Jesus had never done him any wrong in eighty-six years.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Teacher source: The Martyrdom of Polycarp, chapters 8–12 (newadvent.org/fathers/0102.htm). The famous answer in the old translation is “Eighty and six years have I served Him, and He never did me any injury: how then can I blaspheme my King and my Saviour?”</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: In the Stadium</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story is the part of the letter that is best known. We stop before the end: Polycarp’s death is never described.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Just this once</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Has anyone ever asked you to say or do just one tiny thing, “just this once”, that you knew wasn’t right?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What made it hard to say no?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three ideas without asking for details: friends watching, wanting to fit in, it seeming so small, not wanting to be the odd one out. Recap: ‘Yesterday we learned that Christians were asked to say “Lord Caesar”. Today an old man is asked, one more time.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Revelation 2:8, 10 · Martyrdom 9</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV · Martyrdom of Polycarp, modernised)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:8</b> I am the first and the last. I died, but now I am alive!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Martyrdom of Polycarp 9</b> Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has never done me any wrong. How can I speak evil of my King, who saved me?</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The first two lines are Scripture (CEV). The third is from the letter by the church in Smyrna, in a modernised form; the old Roberts–Donaldson translation reads: “Eighty and six years have I served Him, and He never did me any injury: how then can I blaspheme my King and my Saviour?” ‘Blaspheme’ means to speak evil about God. We don’t know if the 86 years means his age or the time since he began to follow Jesus; either way, it is a lifetime.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Into the stadium</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Morning comes in Smyrna. Polycarp has been lifted onto a donkey, and the soldiers lead him towards the city. It is a big day, and the streets are busy.</p><p>Beside the road a carriage rolls up. Inside are the police captain, Herod, and his father, Nicetes. The church’s letter tells us they tried to talk him round. ‘What harm is there in saying, “Lord Caesar”, and offering a little incense, and so being safe?’ At first Polycarp doesn’t answer. They ask again. Then he says, quietly and firmly, ‘I shall not do as you advise me.’ The carriage rolls on, and the old man goes on towards the stadium.</p><p>The stadium is a huge stone bowl with thousands of people in tiers. The roar of the crowd is like the sea. The letter reports that as Polycarp walked in, a voice came from heaven: ‘Be strong, and show yourself a man, O Polycarp!’ No one saw who spoke, but the Christians who were standing nearby said they heard it. We can’t check that, but it shows what the church remembered: that God was with him.</p><p>Now he stands in front of the proconsul, the Roman governor. The proconsul is a little uncomfortable. He says, ‘Have some respect for your age. Swear by the fortune of Caesar, and I will set you free. Just speak evil of Christ.’</p><p>The whole stadium waits. And Polycarp gives the answer people have remembered for nearly two thousand years. ‘Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has never done me any wrong. How can I speak evil of my King, who saved me?’</p><p>The letter doesn’t say whether eighty-six is his age or the years since he first followed Jesus. Either way, it is a whole lifetime. Eighty-six years of Jesus never letting him down.</p><p>The proconsul asks again. ‘Swear by the fortune of Caesar.’ And Polycarp says three simple words: ‘I am a Christian.’</p><p>Three times a herald calls out across the stadium that Polycarp has confessed he is a Christian. The proconsul stares at the old man’s calm face, which the letter says was full of courage and joy.</p><p>This is where our story stops. The church in Smyrna wrote that Polycarp gave his life that day, trusting Jesus. He never changed his answer, not because he was a superhero, but because Jesus had been faithful to him all his life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 6 on-screen beats: 1. A donkey, a carriage and a question. · 2. “I shall not do as you advise me.” · 3. A voice from heaven. · 4. The stadium. The governor. · 5. “Eighty-six years I have served him.” · 6. “I am a Christian.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. DOCUMENTED in the Martyrdom of Polycarp 8–12 (a letter from the church in Smyrna): the donkey, Herod and Nicetes urging him to say “Lord Caesar” and sacrifice to be safe, and his reply “I shall not do as you advise me”; the proconsul’s urging to swear by Caesar’s fortune and to “reproach Christ”; the “eighty and six years” answer; “I am a Christian”; the herald proclaiming it three times; the proconsul’s astonishment at his calm. REPORTED (the church’s own report; we can’t verify it): the voice from heaven. We’ve modernised the wording. LEFT OUT on purpose: injuries on the road, anything about how he died, and the letter’s harsh remarks about parts of the crowd (it also blames Jewish people in ways that have been used wrongly; no group is to blame, so we don’t repeat it). If a student asks how he died: ‘The letter says he gave his life, trusting Jesus. The details are hard, and we don’t need them.’ Some students may have family from places where Christians are still persecuted; keep the focus on faith, calm and Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The question again</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Herod and his father urge Polycarp to say on the road into the city?<br>A) “Lord Caesar” ✓ &nbsp; B) “Goodbye, Smyrna” &nbsp; C) “I forgive you” &nbsp; D) “Long live the bishop”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, “Lord Caesar”, with a little incense, to be safe. Follow-up: ‘What did Polycarp reply?’ (“I shall not do as you advise me.”) Follow-up 2: ‘Who asked him again in the stadium?’ (The proconsul.) If silent: re-read the first story beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Eighty-Six Years</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Eighty-Six Years</b> (Martyrdom of Polycarp 8–12 (retold)). DOCUMENTED (Martyrdom of Polycarp 8–12): Herod and Nicetes urging him to say “Lord Caesar” and be safe; “I shall not do as you advise me”; the voice from heaven (reported by the church); the proconsul urging him to swear by Caesar’s fortune and speak evil of Christ; Polycarp’s “eighty-six years” answer (modernised wording); “I am a Christian”; the herald proclaiming three times. IMAGINED: Nicetes’ extra lines, the proconsul’s final warning, and the Everyone parts. The proconsul and the crowd are not named or judged beyond what the letter says.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Polycarp</b> — Very old, calm and clear — never shouting<br><b>Herod</b> — Police captain — polite but pushy<br><b>Nicetes</b> — Herod’s father — worried and persuasive<br><b>Proconsul</b> — The Roman governor — in charge, but astonished<br><b>A Voice</b> — Heard, never seen<br><b>Everyone</b> — The crowd in the stadium, then the herald</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: make a low crowd rumble, then stop when the Narrator lifts a hand · Everyone: stand quietly at the end<br>Props: Optional: a chair for the proconsul’s platform<br>Imagined retelling that follows the letter closely; see the imagined note for what is documented. The Voice is a ‘voice: true’ character, so no one is drawn or pointed at. Keep the crowd noise low and controlled and stop it quickly when the Narrator lifts a hand. The role-play ends before anything else happens; the Narrator’s last words are all we say about what followed. If any student seems upset, reassure them that Polycarp’s story is told with hope, because Jesus is alive.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Morning in Smyrna. Polycarp rides a donkey towards the city. A carriage rolls up beside him.</li><li><b>Herod:</b> Polycarp, what harm is there in saying “Lord Caesar”? Offer a pinch of incense, and you will be safe. <i>(lean out of the carriage)</i></li><li><b>Nicetes:</b> Yes, old man. A few words. Why throw everything away over something so small? <i>(nod eagerly)</i></li><li class='dir'>Polycarp does not answer at first. They ask him again.</li><li><b>Herod:</b> Come now. Say it, and all this ends. <i>(spread your hands)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> I shall not do as you advise me. <i>(steady and calm)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Roooar! <i>(low crowd rumble that grows, then stops when the Narrator lifts a hand)</i></li><li class='dir'>The stadium. As Polycarp walks in, the church’s letter says a voice came from heaven.</li><li><b>A Voice:</b> Be strong, and show yourself a man, O Polycarp!</li><li class='dir'>No one saw who spoke. But the Christians who were nearby heard it.</li><li><b>Proconsul:</b> Have respect for your old age, Polycarp. Swear by the fortune of Caesar, and I will set you free. <i>(sit up straight)</i></li><li><b>Proconsul:</b> Just speak evil of Christ. That is all I ask. <i>(wave a hand)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The whole stadium is waiting. What would you say if you were Polycarp? What would it cost you? (20s)</li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has never done me any wrong. How can I speak evil of my King, who saved me? <i>(look straight ahead, calm)</i></li><li><b>Proconsul:</b> Swear by the fortune of Caesar. I will ask you one last time. <i>(frown)</i></li><li><b>Polycarp:</b> I am a Christian. <i>(quiet and clear)</i></li><li class='dir'>Three times a herald calls across the stadium.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Polycarp has confessed that he is a Christian! <i>(call it like a herald, three times, a bit softer each time)</i></li><li class='dir'>The proconsul stares. The old man’s face is calm, and full of courage and joy.</li><li class='dir'>This is where our story stops. The church wrote that Polycarp gave his life that day, trusting Jesus.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Jesus is my King. He never lets his people down. <i>(stand quietly and say it together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp could have said a few words to be safe. Why do you think he said no?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Because Jesus had never let him down; because loyalty matters even when it costs; because words show who we belong to; because Jesus was his King. He wasn’t being stubborn but loyal.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was Polycarp’s reason, in his own words?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Eighty-six years of serving Jesus, who had never done him any wrong, and who saved him. His loyalty was a response to Jesus’ faithfulness.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A lifetime of faithfulness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp said Jesus had never done him any wrong in eighty-six years. Why was that such a strong reason to stay loyal?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he had a lifetime of knowing Jesus was good and trustworthy; loyalty grows from a long friendship; you stick with someone who has always been faithful to you; he didn’t want to turn against his best friend. Follow-up: ‘Who has been faithful to you for a long time?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How does knowing Jesus better make loyalty easier?’ If silent: ask who they would never betray, and why.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A small thing?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The proconsul only wanted a few words. Why did Polycarp say no to something that looked so small?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: it wasn’t small to him, because words show who we belong to; small choices build the habit of big loyalty; Polycarp’s loyalty had grown through a lifetime of small choices; the question was whether Jesus or Caesar was Lord. Follow-up: ‘What small choices could build loyalty in us?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What small thing is hard for you to say no to?’ If silent: ask how telling a small lie can grow.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp’s loyalty came from knowing that Jesus had been faithful to him all his life.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus, thank you that you have never let me down. Help me to be loyal to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one small thing today where you can do the right thing even if nobody would notice: tell the truth, pick up litter, include someone, or give back change.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it small and specific. This leads straight into Thursday’s challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: “I am a Christian”</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“How can I speak evil of my King, who saved me?”<br>A single beam of light. A small, calm man.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Revelation 2:10: hands out, heart, walk, point, cup, sweep.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying in pairs for courage</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Ask: “Where is it hard to do the right thing?” Then pray one sentence for each other and for Christians who are treated badly around the world. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Quietly tell them one place where it can be hard to do the right thing, such as with friends, at sport or online.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you are faithful. You never let Polycarp down for eighty-six years. / Jesus, you are a King who saved us, and you are alive.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are faithful, and Jesus, you are our King!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: where have you gone along with something you knew wasn’t right?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I said yes to something small and wrong because of my friends. / Sorry for the times I wasn’t loyal to you, Jesus.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the small wrong things we went along with.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the people who have been faithful to you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for friends and family who never give up on me. / Thank you, Jesus, that you never let your people down.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who stay faithful.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner, and one for Christians far away.<br>Prompts: Please give (partner’s name) courage to do the right thing this week. / Please give courage and peace to Christians who are treated badly today.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, give…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, give us courage, and be with Christians who are treated unfairly.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. If a partner would rather not share, they can say “help with something at school” and leave it there. Keep the prayer for persecuted Christians calm and general.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: how the church remembered Polycarp, what his story shows about God, and how we can pray for Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: The Church Remembers":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Martyrdom of Polycarp (c. AD 155); Revelation 2:8–11</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Church Remembers</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Praying for the persecuted church — “Persecuted” means treated badly because of what you believe. Polycarp prayed for Jesus’ church “all over the world”. We can do the same: we can’t be in other countries, but we can carry Christians there to God in prayer. Pray calmly and simply: ask God for courage, peace, good friends and safety for them, and thank him that they are not forgotten.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how the church in Smyrna remembered Polycarp, and what his story shows about God.</li><li>We are learning why we can pray for Christians who are treated badly today.</li><li>I can choose one small way to be loyal this week and say a prayer for the persecuted church.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the main events of Polycarp’s story, say that God is faithful, explain that Christians still face trouble in some places and that we can pray for them, and recite Revelation 2:10.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Notes from Tuesday’s prayer</li><li>Teacher sources: Martyrdom of Polycarp 17–18, 21–22 (newadvent.org/fathers/0102.htm); en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martyrdom_of_Polycarp; Irenaeus (in Eusebius, Church History 5.20). Optional: a current prayer resource from a trusted Christian organisation that supports persecuted Christians</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Church Remembers</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Who said it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who said it? Shout out your answer!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“The will of God be done.” — <b>Polycarp</b><br>“What harm is there in saying, “Lord Caesar”, and offering a little incense, and so being safe?” — <b>Herod and Nicetes (the police captain and his father)</b><br>“I shall not do as you advise me.” — <b>Polycarp</b><br>“Be strong, and show yourself a man, O Polycarp!” — <b>The voice from heaven (reported)</b><br>“Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has never done me any wrong. How can I speak evil of my King, who saved me?” — <b>Polycarp</b><br>“I am a Christian.” — <b>Polycarp</b></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Click to show each quote; students call out who said it; click again to reveal. Ask: ‘Which parts did the church in Smyrna write down?’ (All of these.) Note that the wording is modernised from an old translation.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Revelation 2:8, 10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:8</b> I am the first and the last. I died, but now I am alive!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> Don’t worry about what you will suffer.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Revelation 2:10</b> But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus’ own words to the church in Smyrna. He is the faithful one: he died, and he is alive. That is why he can promise a glorious life.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: What it shows about God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>So what does Polycarp’s story show us about God? The church in Smyrna wrote a letter to another church to tell what had happened. That letter is how we know Polycarp’s story today. They called him a martyr, a word that means ‘witness’: someone who tells the truth about Jesus even when it costs a lot.</p><p>And they were careful about one thing. They wrote that Jesus they worship, because he is the Son of God, and the martyrs they love, as followers of the Lord. Polycarp is not a superhero to worship. He is a person who followed a faithful Jesus. They also met each year on the day they remembered him, to encourage each other.</p><p>Here is what it shows about God. First, God is faithful. Polycarp said that in eighty-six years Jesus had never done him any wrong. Jesus is the first and the last, the one who died and is alive. He was faithful all the way to the cross for us. That is why we can be loyal to him: he was loyal to us first.</p><p>Second, God is with his people. Polycarp didn’t stay calm because he was strong; he was calm because God was with him.</p><p>Third, we are not alone. Today, in some countries, Christians are still treated unfairly: they might lose jobs, find it hard to meet, or be treated badly at school. We can pray for them, and they are not forgotten.</p><p>Now, how do we live this? Not by doing something dramatic, but with loyalty in small things. Polycarp’s big answer grew from a lifetime of small choices. This week: do one right thing when nobody would notice, and pray for Christians who are treated badly. Small things, one day at a time.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The church writes a letter. · 2. Jesus we worship. The martyrs we love. · 3. God is faithful. · 4. Be loyal in a small thing.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. DOCUMENTED: the Smyrna church’s letter (to the church in Philomelium); their words about worshipping Christ and loving the martyrs (Martyrdom 17, wording modernised); gathering on the day to remember him (chapter 18). We say ‘about AD 155’; some historians date it later (around AD 167). Do not hero-worship: Polycarp points to Jesus. When you pray for persecuted Christians, stay calm and general, and avoid scary detail; some students may have family from such places, and some students may be of another faith or none. If you can, include a prayer item from a trusted Christian organisation that supports persecuted Christians.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The letter</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did the church in Smyrna do to tell Polycarp’s story?<br>A) Wrote a letter to other churches ✓ &nbsp; B) Built a statue of him &nbsp; C) Kept it a secret &nbsp; D) Moved to Rome</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, they wrote a letter to another church. Follow-up: ‘What does “martyr” mean?’ (A witness.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who did they say they worship?’ (Jesus, the Son of God.) If silent: re-read the first story beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Worship and love</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The church said Jesus they worship, and the martyrs they love. Why is that difference important?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Polycarp wasn’t God; he pointed to Jesus; we respect and admire brave people but worship only God; the story is about a faithful God, not a hero. Follow-up: ‘Who do you admire?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can we copy someone without worshipping them?’ If silent: ask who Polycarp himself said was his King.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus all his life, because Jesus had never let him down.</li><li>God is faithful, and Jesus, who died and is alive, gives a glorious life to those who stay loyal to him.</li><li>We can be loyal in small things, and pray for Christians who are treated badly today.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Polycarp stayed loyal to Jesus his whole life because Jesus had never let him down, and the same faithful Jesus helps us to be loyal in small things and to remember Christians who are treated badly today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Does praying help?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Praying for Christians in other countries doesn’t really do anything.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: God hears us; the early church prayed for each other and wrote letters; prayer joins us to God’s family around the world, and God can act where we can’t go. Follow-up: ‘How might they feel if they knew we were praying?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What else could we do besides praying?’ (Write, give, learn.) If silent: ask who has prayed for them.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Be loyal in a small thing: do the right thing once this week when nobody would notice, and pray for Christians who are persecuted.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Ideas: put the shopping trolley back, tell the truth about a mistake, include someone left out, tidy up when no one asked. Some students may have family from places where Christians are persecuted: keep the prayer calm. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“But if you are faithful until you die, I will reward you with a glorious life.” — Revelation 2:10 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Revelation 2:10. Bonus: say Acts 27:25 from Week 7.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Faithful</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Eighty-six years. Jesus never let him down.<br>He is faithful.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Praying out loud for the persecuted church</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray out loud together for Christians who are treated badly. Short, simple, calm prayers are perfect: courage, peace, friends, and for those who hurt them to meet Jesus. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. Short is perfect.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing God is. Think of Polycarp’s King.<br>Prompts: God, you are faithful! / Jesus, you are alive, and you never let your people down.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are faithful, and Jesus, you are our King!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently: tell God about a time you weren’t loyal or kind.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t prayed for people who are treated badly. / Thank you that you forgive us and help us start again.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you that you forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for.<br>Prompts: Thank you for Polycarp, and for the church in Smyrna that told his story. / Thank you that we can meet and pray safely.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for Polycarp, and that we can pray together safely.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for Christians who are treated unfairly, and for our challenge.<br>Prompts: Please give courage, peace and good friends to Christians who are treated badly today. / Please help me be loyal to you in one small thing this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, be with Christians who are treated unfairly, and help us be loyal.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say together: “Jesus is my King. He never lets his people down.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Keep the prayer for persecuted Christians calm and without scary details.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: William Tyndale: The Bible in Our Own Language — a man who worked so that ordinary people could read God’s Word.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 9: William Tyndale: The Bible in Our Own Language":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Church history, 1494–1536; Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Speaks Through His Word</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hungry for the Bible — This week we practise being hungry for God’s word. Jesus said, “People need every word that God has spoken” (Matthew 4:4, CEV): we need God’s words the way we need food. William Tyndale was so hungry for them that he wanted everyone to be able to read them. Pray: “Lord, make me hungry to hear you.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Ploughboy Promise</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning why most people in England about 500 years ago could not read the Bible for themselves.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Ploughboy Promise → A book you can’t read → Psalm 119:105 · Matthew 4:4 → The ploughboy promise → What did he promise? → Why Latin was a problem → The Ploughboy Promise → Who is the Bible for? → Head · Heart · Hands → A boy and a plough → Memory verse peek → Hungry for the Bible — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ink, Candles and the Printing Press</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning why William Tyndale had to leave England to translate the Bible.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Ink, Candles and the Printing Press → Recap → Psalm 119:105 · Isaiah 40:8 → Escape to Europe → Words that came from Tyndale → Which language? → Why print it? → Worth leaving everything? → Head · Heart · Hands → Late-night translating → Build the verse → Hungry for the Bible — write a prayer</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Bibles in Bales of Cloth</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning how Tyndale’s New Testaments were smuggled into England, and which details are reported rather than certain.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Bibles in Bales of Cloth → Carrying something precious → Isaiah 40:8 · Luke 2:10 → Hidden in bales of cloth → Where did the books hide? → The Bale of Cloth → Why the risk? → Burning books → Head · Heart · Hands → Wool on top, pages underneath → Verse with actions → Hungry for the Bible — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>A Lamp in a Cold Cell</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened to William Tyndale at the end of his life and which parts are documented and which are reported.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>A Lamp in a Cold Cell → Tyndale’s journey → Psalm 119:105 · Luke 2:10–11 → A lamp in a cold cell → What did he ask for? → A lamp in the dark → This week’s big idea → A prayer for the king → This Week’s Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → A lamp in the dark → Hungry for the Bible — out loud</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: The Ploughboy Promise":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Church history, 1494–1536; Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Ploughboy Promise</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hungry for the Bible — This week we practise being hungry for God’s word. Jesus said, “People need every word that God has spoken” (Matthew 4:4, CEV): we need God’s words the way we need food. William Tyndale was so hungry for them that he wanted everyone to be able to read them. Pray: “Lord, make me hungry to hear you.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why most people in England about 500 years ago could not read the Bible for themselves.</li><li>We are learning about William Tyndale’s promise, as John Foxe reported it, and which parts of this story are documented and which are reported.</li><li>I can explain why Tyndale wanted even a ploughboy to know the Bible.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that the Bible was in Latin and ordinary people could not understand it, that Tyndale wanted everyone to be able to read God’s word in English, and that Foxe reported the ploughboy words some years later (so we say “reported”, not “proved”).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a Bible in English and, if you can find one, a page in another language to show how it feels not to understand</li><li>Teacher sources: John Foxe, Acts and Monuments (1563/1583), the ploughboy account; Tyndale’s own preface to his Pentateuch (1530), bible-researcher.com/tyndale2.html; Plowboy trope, en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plowboy_trope; William Tyndale, en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Tyndale</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Ploughboy Promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This week we leave the book of Acts and follow the story of God’s word into history: how the Bible came into English.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: A book you can’t read</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Imagine you owned the most important book in the world, but it was written in a language you couldn’t read. How would that feel?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What would you want to do about it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Last week we met Polycarp, the 86-year-old who stayed loyal to Jesus all his life. Your challenge: be loyal in a small thing, do the right thing once when nobody would notice, and pray for Christians who are persecuted (treated badly for following Jesus). How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students about a small right thing they did, or someone they prayed for. Keep it light and private; no one has to share. Then the hook: take two or three feelings (frustrated, left out, curious). Land it: ‘About 500 years ago that was true for almost everyone in England, and the book was the Bible.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Psalm 119:105 · Matthew 4:4</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 119:105</b> Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 4:4</b> Don’t depend on bread alone to live. People need every word that God has spoken.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Psalm 119:105 is our memory verse. Jesus said the Matthew 4:4 words when he was tested in the desert. He was quoting the Old Testament, so he knew God’s word well.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The ploughboy promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let’s travel back about five hundred years to England in the early 1500s. Henry the Eighth is king. There are no phones or TVs, and hardly anyone owns a book. And here is the strange part. On Sunday, the church reads the Bible aloud in Latin, the old language of Rome. But ordinary English people don’t speak Latin. They stand there, hearing words they cannot understand. Church leaders are very nervous about Bibles in English and have made strict rules about them. So God’s own words are locked away in a language most people can’t read.</p><p>Around the year 1494, a boy was born in Gloucestershire, in the west of England, a land of green hills, sheep and ploughed fields. His name was William Tyndale. William was brilliant with languages. He studied at Oxford and then at Cambridge, and he learned Greek. But the more he read the New Testament, the more amazed he was. Here were the words of Jesus. Here was forgiveness, and God’s love. And nearly everyone in England was missing it!</p><p>Around 1521, William became a tutor and chaplain, a kind of church minister, for the children of a rich knight, Sir John Walsh, at a manor house called Little Sodbury. Visitors often came to dinner, and many were churchmen. And William was never shy about opening his Bible and saying what it really taught.</p><p>One of those dinner arguments became famous. We know it from a writer called John Foxe, who wrote down the story about forty years later. So this is what Foxe reported, not something written on the day. Foxe says a learned churchman told William that people were better off without God’s laws than without the Pope’s. And William answered, ‘I defy the Pope and all his laws. If God spares my life, before many years I will make sure a boy who drives the plough knows more of the Scripture than you do!’</p><p>Think about that promise. A ploughboy was a farm boy, who probably couldn’t read, walking behind two horses all day. William was saying that God’s word is not only for bishops and scholars. It is for him too.</p><p>Historians say these exact words may have grown a little in the telling. But here is what we are sure of: William Tyndale spent the rest of his life working to put the Bible into English. That is where our story begins.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. England, five hundred years ago. · 2. A boy named William. · 3. Dinner at Little Sodbury. · 4. The ploughboy promise. · 5. Reported by Foxe. Lived by William.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. DOCUMENTED: Tyndale born c.1494 in Gloucestershire (exact year uncertain); studied at Oxford and Cambridge; about 1521 became chaplain and tutor in the household of Sir John Walsh at Little Sodbury. REPORTED: the dinner argument and the ploughboy words come from John Foxe’s Acts and Monuments (first printed 1563), written about forty years after the event, so we say “Foxe reported”. Some historians think the story may be partly legend; it fits what Tyndale did. The words here are modernised. Say plainly that the church rules about English Bibles were strict; the Pope and church leaders were real people with a real argument, so keep your tone respectful: Tyndale’s quarrel was with those who kept the Bible from ordinary people, not with every Christian in the Catholic Church, and many students’ families belong to different churches. Chaplain = a minister who serves a household or institution.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did he promise?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to John Foxe, what did Tyndale promise that a ploughboy would one day know?<br>A) How to read Latin &nbsp; B) More of the Scripture than the learned man did ✓ &nbsp; C) How to drive a plough faster &nbsp; D) How to write a book</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: B, more of the Scripture than the learned churchman. Follow-up: ‘Who reported these words, and when?’ (John Foxe, about forty years later.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is a ploughboy?’ (A farm boy who drove the plough horses.) If silent: re-read the fourth story caption together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why Latin was a problem</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The church services and the Bible were in Latin, but ordinary people spoke English. Why do you think that mattered so much?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: people couldn’t understand what God said; they had to trust others to tell them; they couldn’t check for themselves; they felt the Bible wasn’t for them. Follow-up: ‘Why does God speak in words we can understand?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How would you feel at church if it was all in a language you didn’t know?’ If silent: ask them to imagine a school assembly entirely in another language.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Ploughboy Promise</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–13:00 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Ploughboy Promise</b> (Foxe’s Acts and Monuments (the ploughboy account)). The two sentences William says in the middle (“I defy the Pope and all his laws…” and the ploughboy promise) are modernised from John Foxe’s report, written about forty years later. Everything else is imagined: the dinner details, Sir John’s and the churchman’s other lines, the boy Tom, and William’s words about translating.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>William Tyndale</b> — A young scholar — calm, clever and determined<br><b>Sir John Walsh</b> — The host — friendly, a bit nervous at his own dinner<br><b>Learned Churchman</b> — A visiting churchman — proud and grumpy<br><b>Tom</b> — An imagined ploughboy — cheerful, can’t read<br><b>Everyone</b> — Church-goers and dinner guests</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: mumble pretend Latin with puzzled faces — “Mm-mm… dominus… mm!” · Everyone: raise an imaginary Bible high at the end<br>Props: Optional: a Bible to hold up at the end<br>Handle the Pope lines respectfully. William’s quarrel was with a refusal to let ordinary people read the Bible, not with every Catholic. Foxe’s words are used in modernised form; the learned churchman’s first line about God’s laws is close to Foxe. Tom is a made-up character for the story. Let the pretend Latin be playful, not mocking.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Little Sodbury Manor, Gloucestershire, about 1522. Dinner is served, and a visiting churchman is in a grumpy mood.</li><li><b>Sir John Walsh:</b> Welcome, everyone! Master Tyndale, our tutor, tell our guests what you have been reading.</li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> The New Testament, in Greek. It is full of good news about Jesus.</li><li><b>Learned Churchman:</b> Ordinary people cannot understand the Bible. They need priests and bishops to explain it to them.</li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> But if they could read it in English, they could hear Jesus for themselves.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Mm-mm… dominus… mm! What does that mean? <i>(mumble pretend Latin, puzzled faces)</i></li><li><b>Learned Churchman:</b> We were better to be without God’s laws than without the Pope’s! <i>(thump the table)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The room goes quiet. William stands. John Foxe reported what he said next.</li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> I defy the Pope and all his laws. <i>(stand tall, calm voice)</i></li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> If God spares my life, before many years I will make a boy who drives the plough know more of the Scripture than you do!</li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! William chose a ploughboy, not a bishop or a king. Why? What is he saying about who the Bible is for? (20s)</li><li><b>Learned Churchman:</b> A ploughboy? Ha! He cannot even read. You will never manage it. <i>(laugh and fold arms)</i></li><li><b>Sir John Walsh:</b> Gentlemen, the soup is getting cold! Let us eat. <i>(wave hands to calm everyone)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Out in the fields, a farm boy leads two horses home. We will call him Tom. He cannot read a word.</li><li><b>Tom:</b> Plough, plough, plough! Church words are all in Latin. I only know ‘Amen’. <i>(cheerful shrug)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> William watches Tom pass the manor gate and thinks hard.</li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> If only Tom could hear God’s own words in plain English. Then he would know how loved he is.</li><li><b>William Tyndale:</b> I will put the Bible into English, whatever it costs me. That is my promise.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Give us the Bible in English! <i>(raise an imaginary Bible high)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Soon William would have to leave Little Sodbury. Tomorrow we find out how he kept his promise.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why did William think a ploughboy mattered as much as a bishop?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: God’s word is for everyone, not only the educated or powerful. God loves all people and speaks to all of them. Jesus often spoke to ordinary people: fishermen, farmers, children.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which words in this script did John Foxe report, and which did we imagine?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Foxe reported the “I defy the Pope…” sentence and the ploughboy promise (about forty years later). The dinner chatter, Sir John, and Tom are imagined.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who is the Bible for?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The learned man said ordinary people might misunderstand the Bible, so only experts should read it. Is that fair? What do you think William would say?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: experts can help us understand; but God wrote it for everyone; ordinary people can understand the main message; we can ask questions and study together. Follow-up: ‘What helps you understand the Bible when it’s hard?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where else in the Bible does God speak to ordinary people?’ If silent: ask whether a ploughboy can understand the story of Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God speaks to everyone through his word, not just to experts, so everyone should be able to read it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I can hear your word in a language I understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, read one Bible verse out loud to someone at home, or to yourself, and say thank you for the chance to read it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it simple: one verse, one thank you. If a student has no Bible at home, point out the class Bible or a free app, and don’t draw attention to it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A boy and a plough</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“If God spares my life…”<br>…a boy who drives the plough would know the Scripture.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Psalm 119:105. Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Hungry for the Bible — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we pray hungry. Open hands show we are ready to receive. Jesus said we need every word God has spoken the way we need bread (Matthew 4:4). Ask God to make you hungry for his word. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Hold your hands out in front of you, open, like a book you are about to read. Take a slow breath.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think about a God who speaks, who wants to be heard, even by a ploughboy.<br>Prompts: God, you are a God who speaks. You wanted people to hear you. / God, your word is a lamp that shows us the way.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you speak, and you want everyone to hear you!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms down. Think about the times we have ignored God’s word when it was right in front of us.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t wanted to listen to your word. / Sorry for taking the Bible for granted when William Tyndale worked so hard to give it to us.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for ignoring your word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up. Thank God for what he has put in your hands.<br>Prompts: Thank you that I can read the Bible in a language I understand. / Thank you for people like William Tyndale who worked so that everyone could hear you.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, that we can read your word in our own language.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for a hungry heart, and for others who can’t read the Bible yet.<br>Prompts: Please make me hungry to hear your word, like I’m hungry for lunch. / Please help people who still don’t have the Bible in a language they understand.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, make us hungry for your word, and help everyone hear it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Close your hands gently, like closing a treasured book. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model a one-sentence prayer first. Some students may not read easily, or may come from homes with no Bible: keep the tone warm and invitational, never a test.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: William leaves England, translates from Greek by candlelight, and prints the first English New Testament.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: Ink, Candles and the Printing Press":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Church history, 1494–1536; Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ink, Candles and the Printing Press</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hungry for the Bible — This week we practise being hungry for God’s word. Jesus said, “People need every word that God has spoken” (Matthew 4:4, CEV): we need God’s words the way we need food. William Tyndale was so hungry for them that he wanted everyone to be able to read them. Pray: “Lord, make me hungry to hear you.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning why William Tyndale had to leave England to translate the Bible.</li><li>We are learning how the first printed English New Testament was made at Worms in 1526.</li><li>I can explain why Tyndale translated from Greek and why printing mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that William could not do the work in England so he went to Germany, that he translated from the Greek New Testament, that the printing press let many copies be made, and that the first printed edition was in 1526.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Sticky notes or small cards</li><li>Pens</li><li>Teacher sources: Tyndale’s own preface to the Pentateuch (1530), bible-researcher.com/tyndale2.html; Tyndale’s Version, bible-researcher.com/tyndale4.html; Tyndale Bible, en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyndale_Bible</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Ink, Candles and the Printing Press</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met William Tyndale and heard the ploughboy promise that John Foxe reported.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What was the problem with the Bible in England, and what did William want to do about it?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The Bible was in Latin, so ordinary people could not understand it; William wanted to translate it into English. Remind them: the ploughboy words are what Foxe reported, but we know for certain William spent his life on translation. ‘Today: what it cost him, and how he did it.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Psalm 119:105 · Isaiah 40:8</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 119:105</b> Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 40:8</b> Grass withers and flowers fade, but what our God says will last forever.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Isaiah wrote this long before Tyndale. The words of God do not fade, even if people try to stop them. Hold it for Wednesday’s story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Escape to Europe</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard William Tyndale’s promise. Today we find out what it cost him.</p><p>William knew he needed two things to translate the Bible into English: a safe place to work, and help from the church leaders. So, around 1523, he went to London and asked the Bishop of London, a famous scholar called Cuthbert Tunstall, for a place to work. The bishop said no. Years later William wrote about it himself. He said that he found there was no room to translate the New Testament in the bishop’s palace, and no place to do it in all of England.</p><p>So in 1524 William made a huge decision. He would leave England. He boarded a small sailing ship with a satchel of books, and he looked back at the white cliffs of his homeland. He didn’t know it, but he would never see England again.</p><p>Across the sea, in the cities of Germany, William found a room, a desk and some friends, and he got to work. He did not translate from the Latin Bible that church leaders used. He worked from the Greek New Testament, the language the first Christians wrote in. A scholar called Erasmus had printed it a few years earlier. Night after night, by candlelight, with ink on his fingers, William wrote the words of Jesus, and Matthew, and Paul in plain, strong English. He wanted words a ploughboy could understand and that sounded good when read aloud.</p><p>Then came printing. The printing press had been invented about seventy-five years earlier. It could make hundreds of copies faster than anyone could copy by hand. In 1525, William began printing his New Testament in the city of Cologne. But an enemy of his work, a writer called John Cochlaeus, found out and told the authorities. The story mostly comes from Cochlaeus himself. William grabbed the printed sheets and escaped by boat up the River Rhine to the city of Worms.</p><p>In Worms, in 1526, the printing was finished. The world had its first printed New Testament in English. It was a small, thick book, small enough to hold in one hand and hide in a pocket. Only a handful of copies survive in libraries today.</p><p>Why would a man give up his home and everything he knew for this? Because William believed the Bible is not just an old book. It is where God speaks to us and tells us the good news about Jesus. And everyone deserves to hear it.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Yesterday’s promise. · 2. No room in England. · 3. Greek, ink and candlelight. · 4. Printing, and a dash up the Rhine. · 5. The first printed English New Testament.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. DOCUMENTED: Tyndale’s own later words about there being no room in the bishop’s house nor in all England (preface to his Pentateuch, 1530; modernised here); he left England in 1524 and never returned; he worked from the Greek New Testament (Erasmus’s edition of 1516, plus other helps); printing began at Cologne in 1525, was interrupted (our main source is John Cochlaeus, an opponent, who tells it himself); the complete English New Testament was printed at Worms in 1526 by Peter Schöffer; only a few copies survive. UNCERTAIN: whether Tyndale also studied at Wittenberg in 1524–25 (we leave it out). The printing press (Gutenberg, mid-1400s) is about seventy-five years old by 1525. Erasmus = a famous Dutch scholar.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Words that came from Tyndale</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Many everyday English phrases that scholars link to Tyndale’s translations are still in our language. Where do these come from in the Bible?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>A phrase we still say</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr><tr><td>“Let there be light”</td><td>Genesis 1:3, in Tyndale’s Old Testament work (1530)</td></tr><tr><td>“The powers that be”</td><td>Romans 13:1, in Tyndale’s New Testament</td></tr><tr><td>“The signs of the times”</td><td>Matthew 16:3, in Tyndale’s New Testament</td></tr><tr><td>“It came to pass”</td><td>Used again and again through the Bible stories</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Words he chose 500 years ago are still in our language today.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Scholars credit Tyndale with first using these English phrases, though some lines have earlier roots (for example in the Wycliffe Bible, c.1382), so we say “scholars link” rather than “he invented”. Reveal row by row. A fun extra: ask students where they have heard “Let there be light”.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which language?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Finish the sentence: “William translated the New Testament mainly from the ________ New Testament, not the Latin one.”<br><i>Answer: “…Greek.”</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: Greek, the language the New Testament was first written in. Follow-up: ‘Why did he go back to Greek?’ (To be closest to the original words.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who printed the Greek New Testament he used?’ (A scholar called Erasmus.) If silent: point back to the story’s third beat.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why print it?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why was the printing press so important for William’s plan?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: copying by hand was slow and costly; a press could make hundreds of copies; the books were cheaper and could spread quickly; more people could read it themselves. Follow-up: ‘What new technology helps spread good news today?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does it say about God that he uses ordinary tools like ink and paper?’ If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Worth leaving everything?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">William left his home, his friends and his country, and never came back. Agree or disagree: “Some things are worth leaving everything for.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Either side can be argued. Agree: things that last forever, such as knowing Jesus, are worth more than comfort. Disagree: caring for family and friends also matters. Follow-up: ‘What would you give up for something you believed in?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What did Jesus say about what matters most?’ If silent: ask what William would say.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God speaks through his word, and William believed everyone should be able to hear it, so he gave up a lot to translate it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, make me hungry for your word, as hungry as William was.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, find your Bible (a book or an app) and put it where you’ll see it tomorrow morning: beside your bed, on your desk or on your device’s home screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Hand out sticky notes: students can write “Read Luke” and stick it where they’ll see it. This leads into Thursday’s challenge. Be sensitive to students who don’t have a Bible at home: a free app or a school Bible is fine.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Late-night translating</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ink on his fingers. Candlelight on the page.<br>Words a ploughboy could understand.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Psalm 119:105 chunk by chunk, like lighting a lamp one step at a time.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Hungry for the Bible — write a prayer</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your prayer like a letter to God. Writing slows us down and helps us say exactly what we mean. Copy out Psalm 119:105 at the top, if you like. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen. At the top of your paper write: “Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is: a God who speaks and keeps his word.<br>Prompts: God, your word lasts forever, even when people try to stop it. / God, you speak in words that we can read and understand.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, your word lasts forever.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently think: when did I leave my Bible shut because I was too busy or didn’t care?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the days I never opened your word. / Sorry for thinking the Bible is boring when you speak through it.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we ignore your word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write two things you’re thankful for about the Bible.<br>Prompts: Thank you for William Tyndale, who left home so we could read your word. / Thank you for the stories of Jesus that I can read in English.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the Bible in our own language.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you’d like God to show you in his word this week.<br>Prompts: Please help me read a bit of your word every day. / Please show me who Jesus is as I read.<br>Starter: <b>Please show me…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, show us who Jesus is as we read your word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fold your prayer and keep it in your Bible or bag. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their prayer private. Reassure those who find writing hard that a few words, or a drawing, is a real prayer.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: how the little books were smuggled into England in bales of cloth, and what the bishops did about it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Bibles in Bales of Cloth":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Church history, 1494–1536; Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Bibles in Bales of Cloth</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hungry for the Bible — This week we practise being hungry for God’s word. Jesus said, “People need every word that God has spoken” (Matthew 4:4, CEV): we need God’s words the way we need food. William Tyndale was so hungry for them that he wanted everyone to be able to read them. Pray: “Lord, make me hungry to hear you.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning how Tyndale’s New Testaments were smuggled into England, and which details are reported rather than certain.</li><li>We are learning what the bishops did, and why more copies kept coming.</li><li>I can explain Isaiah 40:8 using this part of the story.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students explaining that Bibles were smuggled in goods such as cloth, that Bibles were collected and burned in 1526 but more kept coming, and linking it to “what our God says will last forever” (Isaiah 40:8).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a piece of cloth and a small book for the role-play</li><li>Teacher sources: Tyndale’s Version, bible-researcher.com/tyndale4.html; Revisiting William Tyndale, Father of the English Bible, rsc.byu.edu (vol. 12 no. 2, 2011); Tyndale Society Journal 17, “A Tale of Two Packingtons”, tyndale.org</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Bibles in Bales of Cloth</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Carrying something precious</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever had to carry something precious past someone who might take it away? How carefully did you carry it?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Now imagine what you’re carrying is a book that’s against the rules.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three answers: a trophy, a gift, a pet, a secret. Recap: ‘Yesterday we saw the New Testament printed at Worms in 1526. Today we find out how those little books reached England, and what happened next.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Isaiah 40:8 · Luke 2:10</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Isaiah 40:8</b> Grass withers and flowers fade, but what our God says will last forever.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:10</b> But the angel said, “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke 2:10 is what the family reads in today’s role-play. It is CEV wording; Tyndale’s own 1526 English was spelled differently, but the message is the same. This verse comes back next week at Christmas Day 1814.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Hidden in bales of cloth</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The little books were ready in Worms. Now they had to cross the sea. And that was dangerous, because in England the church leaders had decided Tyndale’s New Testament must not be read.</p><p>So how do you smuggle a Bible? Merchants who traded with England found a way. The reports tell us that copies were hidden inside bales of cloth, and tucked in among other goods too. Picture a merchant in a dim warehouse in Antwerp, rolling the small, thick books deep inside a bale of woollen cloth, then roping it up like any ordinary cargo. If an officer poked around, he would feel only wool. Ship by ship, the books slipped into English ports, and then from hand to hand, they travelled to towns and villages.</p><p>Reading one was risky. People read in secret, in kitchens at night, by candlelight, one person reading aloud while the others listened. Imagine hearing the story of Jesus in your own language for the very first time, in your own kitchen. For many, that was how they met Jesus.</p><p>The church leaders soon heard about it. Late in 1526, Bishop Tunstall, the very bishop who had said no to William, preached a sermon at St Paul’s Cross, an open-air pulpit in London. Copies of the New Testament were collected, and they were burned in public. The aim was to stop the Bible from being read.</p><p>But here is a surprising story. A chronicler called Edward Hall reported that a merchant named Augustine Packington told the bishop he could buy up lots of the books for him. The bishop paid, and burned them. The money went to the people who had printed them, and William used it to print a better, corrected edition. Historians can’t be sure that is exactly how it happened. But we do know that, despite the burning, new editions kept coming, and thousands of copies reached England.</p><p>Think about those readers for a moment. Most had never held a Bible in their lives. Some could barely read, so a friend or a child read aloud while the rest listened with their eyes shut. They did not read it to win an argument. They read it because it told them that God loved them, that Jesus came to rescue them, and that they could talk to God themselves. Once people had heard that good news, they could not forget it, and they told their neighbours.</p><p>Isaiah wrote it long ago: grass withers and flowers fade, but what our God says will last forever. They could burn the paper. They could not put out the light.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Books in Worms. Readers in England. · 2. Hidden in bales of cloth. · 3. Quiet nights, candlelight. · 4. The bishop burns copies. · 5. But more keep coming.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. DOCUMENTED: Tyndale’s New Testaments reached England from 1526; Bishop Tunstall condemned them and had copies bought up and burned at St Paul’s Cross in late 1526; further editions were printed and sent in over the next years; Tyndale revised the New Testament in 1534. REPORTED (not certain): that copies were hidden in bales of cloth and other goods (a standard account, details sparse); the Augustine Packington story is told by the chronicler Edward Hall, who wrote some years later; the Tyndale Society has argued over the details, so we say “reported”. Sensitive: only books are burned in this story; some people who owned them were later punished, but we do not go there. If students ask about people being hurt, say calmly that some believers suffered for their faith and that we remember them, and move on. Chronicler = someone who writes down the history of their times.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did the books hide?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">According to the reports, where were Tyndale’s New Testaments hidden when they were smuggled into England?<br>A) In bales of cloth ✓ &nbsp; B) Inside barrels of apples &nbsp; C) Under the king’s bed &nbsp; D) In the bishop’s hat</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, in bales of cloth, among other goods. Say that this is reported, not certain in every detail. Follow-up: ‘Who carried them?’ (Merchants who traded between Europe and England.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did the bishop do with the copies he found?’ (Had them burned in public.) If silent: re-read the second story caption.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: The Bale of Cloth</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>The Bale of Cloth</b> (Tyndale’s New Testaments reach England (c.1526 onwards); Luke 2:10). The merchant, the customs officer, the father, his daughter Meg and the bishop’s man are imagined; their words are imagined too. That Bibles were smuggled in bales of cloth is reported, not certain. Meg reads Luke 2:10 in CEV wording for ease; Tyndale’s own 1526 English was spelled differently.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Merchant</b> — A brave, quiet cloth trader<br><b>Customs Officer</b> — Suspicious, but not very clever<br><b>Father</b> — A poor farm worker who has never held a Bible<br><b>Meg</b> — His daughter, who can read<br><b>Bishop’s Man</b> — Collects books to burn<br><b>Everyone</b> — Smugglers, family and crowd</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: finger on your lips and “Shhh!” when the merchant looks at the door · Everyone: raise an imaginary book high at the end<br>Props: Optional: a piece of cloth and a small book for the bale<br>Imagined scene based on reports of smuggling. Keep the officer funny rather than scary and the tone breathless but safe. Only books are burned, no people. The “Shhh!” moments are the fun part: stay quiet for a second afterwards, then carry on. Luke 2:10 is the verse that Samuel Marsden will preach on in next week’s story.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>A dim warehouse in Antwerp, across the sea. Bales of cloth stand ready for England.</li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Quick, tuck them deep inside. Small books, big risk. Wool on top, pages underneath. <i>(tuck imaginary books into a bale)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Shhh! <i>(finger on lips, look at the door)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The ship sails across the grey sea. At an English port, a customs officer climbs aboard.</li><li><b>Customs Officer:</b> Cloth from Antwerp? Open that bale. I am looking for banned books.</li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Only wool, sir. The best wool. Feel it, so soft, so warm. <i>(smile a bit too widely)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! The officer’s hand is on the bale. If you were the merchant, what are you thinking? Why take such a risk? (20s)</li><li><b>Customs Officer:</b> Hmm. Wool. Nothing but wool. Carry on. <i>(wave them through, yawning)</i></li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Thank you, sir. Thank you, God. <i>(whisper the second part)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Weeks later, on a foggy wharf at night, a bale is cut open.</li><li><b>Merchant:</b> A New Testament, in English. Take it, friend. Read it carefully, and keep it hidden. <i>(hand over a small book)</i></li><li><b>Father:</b> A Bible I can understand? I have never held one. Meg, you are the reader. <i>(hold it like treasure)</i></li><li><b>Meg:</b> By candlelight, Father? Everyone, quietly. This is from the story of Jesus’ birth. <i>(lean toward the lantern)</i></li><li><b>Meg:</b> “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.” <i>(read slowly)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Good news! Read it again! <i>(lean in, eyes wide)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Meanwhile in London, the bishop’s men collect copies at St Paul’s Cross.</li><li><b>Bishop’s Man:</b> Bring every copy you can find! They will be burned in front of the whole city.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But the bishop’s money helped print more copies, and more bales crossed the sea.</li><li><b>Merchant:</b> Another ship tonight. Another hundred books hidden in the wool.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> What our God says will last forever! <i>(raise an imaginary book high)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Why do you think ordinary people risked trouble to read a Bible in secret?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: They wanted to hear God for themselves; it was good news; they had never been able to read it before; the story of Jesus gave them hope. People value most what they’ve been kept from.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Meg read, and how did the family react?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Luke 2:10, the angel’s good news at Jesus’ birth. They leaned in, wanted to hear it again, and held the book like treasure.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Why the risk?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The bishop had Bibles burned to stop people reading them, yet people kept reading. What does that tell us about what God’s word does to people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it gives hope and truth; people want to know God; it’s good news that can’t be kept quiet; God’s word is powerful. Follow-up: ‘What does Isaiah 40:8 say about God’s word?’ (It lasts forever.) Follow-up 2: ‘Why do you think some leaders were afraid of it?’ If silent: ask what Meg’s family felt when they heard Luke 2:10.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Burning books</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The bishop thought he was protecting people from wrong teaching. William thought everyone should be free to read for themselves. Who do you think was right, and how can we tell?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: truth can stand being read and tested; hiding the Bible from people can’t be right when Jesus welcomed everyone; people can still disagree respectfully. Follow-up: ‘What should we do when we disagree about what the Bible says?’ (Read it, ask questions, listen to each other.) Follow-up 2: ‘How did Jesus treat people who asked him questions?’ If silent: ask what Jesus said about little children coming to him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s word gives light and hope, and no one can put it out.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you that I can read your word, and that no one can take it away.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Tonight, ask someone at home, “Which Bible story is your favourite?” or read two minutes of a Gospel (Matthew, Mark, Luke or John) with a family member.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Keep it light. If a student’s home doesn’t share their faith, they can ask a friend, or just read quietly on their own.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Wool on top, pages underneath</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Small books. Big risk.<br>“Only wool, sir.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Actions for Psalm 119:105: book, lamp, give, shine, sweep, walk.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Hungry for the Bible — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray with a partner. Take turns: one person says one sentence, the other says Amen. You’re practising praying aloud for each other. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Sit close and keep your voices low, like people reading in secret by candlelight.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God’s word.<br>Prompts: God, your word lasts forever. Nobody can burn it. / God, you speak good news to ordinary people.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, your word lasts forever!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently think: where do I take it for granted that I can read God’s word whenever I like?<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I’ve not bothered to open your word. / Sorry for thinking God’s word is boring when people risked so much for it.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for taking your word for granted.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for the brave people who carried the Bible across the sea.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the merchants and readers who risked trouble so people could hear you. / Thank you that I can read the Bible in English, safely, at home.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the people who brought us your word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner, and then one for people today who can’t read the Bible freely.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) to read your word this week. / Please help Christians who live where the Bible is banned or hard to get.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help everyone who wants to read your word.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model first with a student. Some students may have family in countries where Bibles are banned or hard to get, so keep the tone calm and hopeful and don’t add frightening detail. Pairs can say “help with something at home” and leave it there.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Tyndale in prison with a lamp and a Hebrew Bible, his prayer for the king, and what his work shows us about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: A Lamp in a Cold Cell":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Church history, 1494–1536; Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">A Lamp in a Cold Cell</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Hungry for the Bible — This week we practise being hungry for God’s word. Jesus said, “People need every word that God has spoken” (Matthew 4:4, CEV): we need God’s words the way we need food. William Tyndale was so hungry for them that he wanted everyone to be able to read them. Pray: “Lord, make me hungry to hear you.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened to William Tyndale at the end of his life and which parts are documented and which are reported.</li><li>We are learning what his story shows us about God, who speaks through his Word.</li><li>I can plan to read a chapter of Luke this week.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the main steps in Tyndale’s story, say that God speaks through his Word and that the Bible leads to Jesus, label which parts are documented (the letter, the dates) and which are reported (Foxe’s words), and recite Psalm 119:105.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Optional: a copy of Luke, a Bible, or a Bible app on a class device</li><li>Teacher sources: Tyndale’s Letter from Prison, bible-researcher.com/tyndale3.html; Tyndale’s prefaces, bible-researcher.com/tyndale2.html; John Foxe, Acts and Monuments (Tyndale’s death); William Tyndale, en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Tyndale; Tyndale’s Version, bible-researcher.com/tyndale4.html</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: A Lamp in a Cold Cell</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Today’s story contains a death; it is implied only. Please read the Thursday teacher notes first.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Tyndale’s journey</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put William Tyndale’s story in order!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The ploughboy promise at Little Sodbury → He leaves England for Europe → Printing begins in Cologne and is interrupted → The first English New Testament is printed at Worms → Copies are smuggled into England in bales of cloth → The bishop burns copies, but more arrive → Prison at Vilvoorde</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next step; click to place it. Point out: promise, leaving, printing, smuggling, burning, and then prison. Ask: ‘Which steps are documented and which are reported?’ (The promise and the bales are reported; the printing and the burning are documented.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Psalm 119:105 · Luke 2:10–11</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Psalm 119:105</b> Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:10</b> But the angel said, “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:11</b> This very day in King David’s home town a Savior was born for you. He is Christ the Lord.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Luke is the Gospel we’ll read for this week’s challenge. Christ = God’s promised King, the Chosen One. Savior = the one who rescues us.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A lamp in a cold cell</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>In May 1535, William was betrayed. A man called Henry Phillips pretended to be a friend, then led officers to arrest him in the city of Antwerp. William was taken to a castle at Vilvoorde, near Brussels, and kept in a cold cell for more than a year.</p><p>We have a letter from that prison, written in Latin, and historians believe it is genuine. William did not ask to be set free. He asked for a warmer cap, because of the cold, a warmer coat, because his was very thin, and a woollen shirt. He asked for a lamp in the evening, because it is dreary to sit alone in the dark. And most of all he asked for his Hebrew Bible, his Hebrew grammar and his dictionary, so that he could spend his time studying. Even in prison, he wanted God’s word. He wrote that he would be patient and wait for God’s will, for the glory of the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ.</p><p>In the autumn of 1536, William died for his work. We do not know every detail, and we will not dwell on them. John Foxe reported that William’s last words were a prayer, spoken in a loud voice: ‘Lord, open the King of England’s eyes.’ William had spent his life giving the Bible to ordinary people, and at the end he prayed for the king who had refused him. He died, trusting God.</p><p>And God’s word went on. Within a few years, English Bibles built on William’s work were allowed in England, and the government ordered that an English Bible be placed in every church. Scholars estimate that most of the New Testament in the King James Bible of 1611 still uses William’s wording.</p><p>Here is the best part: the Bible he worked so hard to give us leads us to Jesus, the Word who became a person. Maybe tonight, in a bedroom somewhere in Canterbury, someone will open Luke and read about him.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 5 on-screen beats: 1. Betrayed, then locked up. · 2. A lamp and a Hebrew Bible. · 3. A prayer for the king. · 4. The Word could not be stopped. · 5. Your turn: open Luke.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes. DOCUMENTED: Henry Phillips’ betrayal and Tyndale’s arrest in Antwerp in May 1535; imprisonment at Vilvoorde for more than a year; a surviving Latin letter from prison asking for warmer clothing, a lamp and his Hebrew Bible, grammar and dictionary (its text is in Mombert’s Tyndale’s Five Books of Moses, 1884; historians generally accept it as genuine); his execution in October 1536 (6 October is the traditional date; some records suggest it may have been a little earlier); English Bibles built on his work were licensed in 1537 (Matthew Bible) and the Great Bible of 1539 was placed in churches. REPORTED: his last words, “Lord, open the King of England’s eyes”, come from John Foxe, not from an eyewitness account we can check. Death is implied only here: do not describe the method, and if students ask, say calmly that he was put to death for translating the Bible and that he died trusting God. Some students may have family from places where Christians are still persecuted; keep the tone calm and hopeful. Do not add detail beyond the script.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: What did he ask for?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In his letter from prison, what did William ask for, besides warm clothes and a lamp?<br>A) His Hebrew Bible, grammar and dictionary ✓ &nbsp; B) A ship home &nbsp; C) A feast &nbsp; D) A new printing press</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, his Hebrew Bible, Hebrew grammar and Hebrew dictionary, so he could keep studying. Follow-up: ‘Why did he ask for a lamp?’ (Because it’s dreary to sit alone in the dark.) Follow-up 2: ‘Is the letter documented or reported?’ (Documented: it still survives.) If silent: re-read the second story caption.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A lamp in the dark</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In a cold, dark cell, William asked for a lamp and his Bible. What does that tell us about what God’s word was to him?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: it was light in a dark place; comfort and company; more precious than comfort; it kept him trusting God. Follow-up: ‘How is that like Psalm 119:105?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this show about God, who speaks through his word?’ (He’s with us even in dark places.) If silent: ask what they’d ask for in a cold cell.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: This week’s big idea</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>God speaks through his Word, and he wants everyone to hear it, a ploughboy as much as a bishop.</li><li>William Tyndale gave his life so ordinary people could read the Bible in English. People tried to burn the books, but God’s word lasts forever (Isaiah 40:8).</li><li>The Bible is a lamp that shows us the way to Jesus, the Word who became a person (John 1).</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God speaks through his Word, so William Tyndale gave his life to put the Bible into plain English, and that Word is a lamp that lights our path to Jesus.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Then the Big Idea stamps in. Say what this story shows about God: he speaks, he is faithful to his Word, and the Bible leads to Jesus.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A prayer for the king</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Foxe reported that William’s last prayer was for the king who had refused his work. Jesus taught us to pray for people who stand against us. Why is that so hard, and what does it show about William’s heart?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: forgiving is hard; William trusted God to change hearts; he cared more about the Bible reaching England than about himself; he was following Jesus’ words (Matthew 5:44). Follow-up: ‘Who do you find hard to pray for?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What happened to England’s Bibles soon after?’ If silent: ask them to write one name they find hard to pray for, privately.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Mini-whiteboards · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: This Week’s Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Read one chapter of Luke this week. Luke tells the story of Jesus’ life, from his birth to the empty tomb. Pick a time and a place, and read it slowly, the way William wanted every ploughboy to read.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Help students choose when and where: before bed, at breakfast, on the bus. Suggest starting at Luke 1 or Luke 2 (the Christmas story, which links to next week). If a student doesn’t have a Bible, point them to a free app or a school copy. Next Monday’s check-in will ask about this.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” — Psalm 119:105 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Psalm 119:105. Bonus: say Matthew 7:24 from Term 3, or Acts 16:31 from Week 5.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A lamp in the dark</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Your word is a lamp.<br>A Bible in our own language.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Hungry for the Bible — out loud</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray out loud, one short sentence each. Short and honest is perfect. You can even pray words from the Bible back to God, such as “Your word is a lamp”. <i>(out-loud)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s pray together. In each part, anyone can call out one short sentence. We’re praying with thanks for the Bible we can read.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out one thing about who God is: a God who speaks.<br>Prompts: God, you speak, and your word lasts forever. / God, you light up dark places with your word.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you speak, and your word is a lamp!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently tell God where you’ve ignored his word, or been unkind to someone who disagrees with you.<br>Prompts: Sorry for ignoring your word. / Sorry for the people I find hard to love or listen to.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 25s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry, and thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out something you’re thankful for about the Bible.<br>Prompts: Thank you for William Tyndale and everyone who risked so much to give us your word. / Thank you for Jesus, the Word who became a person.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for the Bible in our own language.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray for a hungry heart, for this week’s Luke challenge, and for those who still can’t read the Bible.<br>Prompts: Please help me to read one chapter of Luke this week. / Please open our eyes to see wonderful things in your word.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, make us hungry for your word, and open our eyes to see Jesus in it.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Let’s say our verse together once more: “Your word is a lamp that gives light wherever I walk.” Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the class’s voices. Remind students that short prayers count. Next week we meet Ruatara and Samuel Marsden and hear what happened when the Bible came to Aotearoa.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Next week: The Gospel Comes to Aotearoa — Ruatara, Samuel Marsden and Christmas Day 1814 at Oihi Bay.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}},"Week 10: The Gospel Comes to Aotearoa":{"label":"Day","children":{"Week Overview":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samuel Marsden, Ruatara, Christmas Day 1814, Oihi Bay; Luke 2:10; Matthew 28:19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">God is…</th><td class=\"lt-td\">With Us</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer skill</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My own prayer plan — This week you build your own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Monday: choose your time. Tuesday: choose your place. Wednesday: choose the one thing. Thursday: put it together and pray in your own words. Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20), so you can talk to him anywhere.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Source</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Morninglight 2027 — original lessons (CEV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">The week at a glance</div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Monday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Ruatara’s Invitation</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18.5 min<br>We are learning who Ruatara was and how a kindness on a ship, the Ann, began a friendship with Samuel Marsden.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Ruatara’s Invitation → Kindness far from home → Luke 2:10 and Matthew 28:19 → A kindness on the Ann → Where did they meet? → Not walking past → Ruatara’s Invitation → A door opened by kindness → Head · Heart · Hands → A blanket and a cup of water → Memory verse peek → Choose your time — open hands</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Tuesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>The Active and the Preparing</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning about the voyage of the brig Active and who sailed on it.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>The Active and the Preparing → Recap → Matthew 28:18–20 → The voyage and the preparing → Two men, two hopes → Finish the line → A risky welcome → The same hopes? → Head · Heart · Hands → A place made ready → Build the verse → Your place, your time — write it</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Wednesday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Christmas Day 1814</b> · 🎭 role-play · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened at Oihi Bay on Christmas Day 1814.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Christmas Day 1814 → Explaining what you mean → Luke 2:10 and Matthew 28:20 → Christmas Day at Oihi → Which verse? → Ruatara Explains → Standing between two worlds → Good news for everyone → Head · Heart · Hands → Good news of great joy → Verse with actions → Pray for each other — in pairs</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Thursday</th><td class=\"lt-td\"><b>Faithful People, Faithful God</b> · 18 min<br>We are learning what happened after Christmas Day 1814, including how Māori made the Bible and the faith their own.<br><span style='color:#6b6b5f'>Faithful People, Faithful God → Term 4 in order → Matthew 28:18–20 → What happened next → Who invited whom? → Faithful people, faithful God → A year of faithful people → Is the story finished? → Holiday Challenge → Memory verse: fade-out → Sent, and never alone → Our own words — standing together</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Monday: Ruatara’s Invitation":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18.5 minutes (20 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samuel Marsden, Ruatara, Christmas Day 1814, Oihi Bay; Luke 2:10; Matthew 28:19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Ruatara’s Invitation</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My own prayer plan — This week you build your own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Monday: choose your time. Tuesday: choose your place. Wednesday: choose the one thing. Thursday: put it together and pray in your own words. Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20), so you can talk to him anywhere.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning who Ruatara was and how a kindness on a ship, the Ann, began a friendship with Samuel Marsden.</li><li>We are learning about Ruatara’s dream of wheat for his people and his invitation to Marsden.</li><li>I can explain why Marsden’s kindness mattered and what Ruatara asked him to do.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Ruatara was a young Ngāpuhi leader from the Bay of Islands, that Marsden found him sick on the ship Ann and cared for him, that Ruatara learned about wheat and farming at Parramatta, and that he invited Marsden to bring teachers to Rangihoua.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a map of the Pacific showing the Bay of Islands, Sydney and England</li><li>Optional: a stalk of wheat, or a slice of bread</li><li>Teacher: sources used, all checked for this lesson: Te Ara, Dictionary of New Zealand Biography, “Ruatara”; NZ History, “Māori intermediaries: Ruatara”; DOC, Oihi Mission Station 1814–1832 Historic Heritage Assessment and Rangihoua Heritage Park; NZ Geographic, “Marsden’s first Christmas”</li><li>Teacher: this lesson is a starting point. Please check it with your local iwi or hapū, a Māori-medium colleague, or your church’s Māori ministry before or alongside teaching it</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Ruatara’s Invitation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. This is the last week of the year, and the story comes close to home: it happened in Aotearoa, in the Bay of Islands, on Christmas Day 1814. Say: ‘We will tell it with care. Some of it is well documented, some of it is told differently by different people, and some parts are hard.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Kindness far from home</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–2:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Think of a time you were a long way from home, or feeling unwell or very low, and someone was kind to you. What did their kindness do for you?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Can one kind thing change a whole friendship?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Check-in</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Welcome back. Last week we met William Tyndale, who gave his life so ordinary people could read the Bible in their own language. Your challenge: read one chapter of Luke. How did it go?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Check-in first: ask two or three students which chapter of Luke they read and what they noticed about Jesus. Then the hook: a friend who sat with them, a teacher who noticed, a new-kid welcome. Land it: ‘Today’s story starts with a kindness on a ship, between two very different men, and it ends up changing the story of Aotearoa. We will meet Ruatara of Ngāpuhi and Samuel Marsden.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 2:10 and Matthew 28:19</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:00–3:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:10</b> But the angel said, “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:19</b> Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The good news (the gospel) is that Jesus, the Saviour, has come. Luke 2:10 is the angel speaking to the shepherds on the first Christmas; we will hear it again on Christmas Day 1814. In Matthew 28:19 Jesus is speaking to his friends after he rose from the dead. A ‘disciple’ is a follower and learner of Jesus. This week we’ll see where these two verses led on the other side of the world.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: A kindness on the Ann</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–6:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Today our story takes us to the Bay of Islands in the north of Aotearoa, and then far out to sea. It is a true story, and it begins with a young man called Ruatara.</p><p>Ruatara belonged to Ngāpuhi, and he was born around 1787 near Te Puna, close to Rangihoua. He was curious, brave and clever. When he was about eighteen, he joined the crew of a whaling ship, because he wanted to see the world, and even to meet King George, the King of England. But the voyage did not go the way he hoped. For about four years he was passed from ship to ship, cheated and badly treated by sailors who did not respect him. He never got to meet the king.</p><p>By 1809, Ruatara was on a convict ship called the Ann, sailing to Sydney. He was thin, weak and very sick. And on that ship was a clergyman called Samuel Marsden. A clergyman is a church leader. Marsden was the chaplain, the minister, of the new British settlement in New South Wales. When he found Ruatara ill and neglected, he did not walk past. He made sure the young man was fed, given clothes and cared for. Marsden followed Jesus, and Jesus taught that caring for someone who is sick is like caring for him. The two men talked, too. Marsden wrote that Ruatara gave him lessons in the Māori language.</p><p>When the ship reached Sydney, Marsden invited Ruatara to stay at his home in Parramatta, and he stayed about eight months. He learned how Europeans farmed and built, and one idea took hold of him: wheat. Wheat grows into flour, and flour makes bread. It could feed his whānau and bring trade. Marsden gave him tools and seed to take home.</p><p>Getting home was another hard journey. A captain cheated Ruatara and left him stranded on Norfolk Island, and he lost the tools and seed. But he did not give up. By about 1812 he reached Rangihoua. Te Pahi, a leading chief, had died after an attack by European whalers, and Ruatara was now looked to as a senior rangatira, a chief and leader. He began to grow wheat. In 1814 Marsden sent him a hand-turned flour mill, and Ruatara showed other chiefs what wheat could do.</p><p>And Ruatara had one more dream. He asked his friend to send teachers to his people, and he promised to protect them. It was an invitation home.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A young Ngāpuhi leader, far from home. · 2. A sick young man on the ship Ann. · 3. Eight months at Parramatta. · 4. “Come, and bring teachers.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Documented (Te Ara, NZ History, DOC): Ruatara left on the Argo in 1805; Marsden met him sick on the Ann in 1809 and cared for him; Ruatara stayed about eight months at Parramatta; he was left stranded on Norfolk Island and lost his seed and tools; he reached Rangihoua about 1812; Te Pahi had died; Marsden’s mill reached him in 1814; he asked for teachers and offered protection. Marsden wrote that Ruatara taught him Māori. Birth year, parentage and some dates are uncertain, so we say ‘around’. The beating Ruatara suffered is not described: keep it gentle. Te Pahi’s death is mentioned in one line only.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Where did they meet?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Where did Samuel Marsden first meet Ruatara?<br>A) On the convict ship Ann ✓ &nbsp; B) In the Bay of Islands &nbsp; C) In a church in London &nbsp; D) At Parramatta</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, on the convict ship Ann, in 1809. Follow-up: ‘What was wrong with Ruatara?’ (He was sick and badly treated.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Marsden do?’ (Made sure he was fed, clothed and cared for.) If silent: re-read the second story beat together and look at the picture.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Not walking past</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Marsden did not have to help a sick stranger. What do you think his kindness showed about Jesus, the one he followed?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: Jesus cares about people who are hurting; Jesus noticed outsiders; Jesus said that helping someone is like helping him; followers of Jesus copy his kindness. Follow-up: ‘How do you think Ruatara felt?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does this tell us about what God is like?’ (Kind, close to the hurting.) If silent: ask what the Samaritan did in Term 3.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Ruatara’s Invitation</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–13:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Ruatara’s Invitation</b> (Documented history, retold). The setting, the people and the main events are documented history: Marsden cared for Ruatara on the Ann, they exchanged language lessons, Ruatara grew wheat, and he invited the mission. All the spoken words are imagined to bring the story to life, including the Sailor, who is made up, and all the te reo greetings. We are not quoting Marsden or Ruatara.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Marsden</b> — A kind English clergyman, calm and practical<br><b>Ruatara</b> — A young Ngāpuhi leader: proud, clever, weak at first<br><b>Sailor</b> — An imagined crew member who doesn’t see the point of helping<br><b>Everyone</b> — The ship’s crew, then Ruatara’s whānau</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the sailors on the ship, muttering and curious · Everyone: be Ruatara’s whānau, admiring the wheat and welcoming guests<br>Props: Optional: a blanket and a cup for Marsden · Optional: a stalk of wheat or a ruler as a ‘sheaf’<br>Play Ruatara as dignified, intelligent and in charge of his own choices, never as a helper or a servant. The Sailor is imagined and should be a short, light moment, not cruel. Marsden should be warm and practical, not preachy. The wheat scene is set at Rangihoua. Te reo: kia ora (hello and thanks), tino pai (very good), whānau (family), hākari (feast), haere mai (welcome). Check pronunciation with a Māori-medium colleague or a student who knows te reo, and thank them. Documented: Marsden’s care on the Ann, the language lessons, the wheat and the invitation. Everything the characters say is imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>The deck of the convict ship Ann, at sea, 1809. Grey sky, creaking ropes, a very long way from home.</li><li><b>Sailor:</b> Don’t waste your time on him, Reverend. He’s sick, and he’s not our problem. <i>(shrug, walk past)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Samuel Marsden, the ship’s chaplain, stops. By the mast sits a thin, weak young man.</li><li><b>Marsden:</b> Young man, you are very ill. What is your name, and where is your home? <i>(kneel beside him, gentle)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> Ruatara. Ngāpuhi. My home is the Bay of Islands, in Aotearoa. It is a long way. <i>(weak voice, proud eyes)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> I sailed to see King George. They would not let me. <i>(look at the deck)</i></li><li><b>Marsden:</b> That was a hard road. Here is water, and a blanket. Rest now. <i>(hand him a cup)</i></li><li><b>Sailor:</b> Why do you bother, Reverend?</li><li><b>Marsden:</b> Because Jesus cared for people who were hurting, and so should I.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Hmm… strange man, that parson. But a kind one. <i>(mutter, nod slowly)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Day after day Marsden brings food and clothes. Slowly Ruatara grows stronger. They begin to swap words from each other’s languages.</li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> Kia ora. That is how we greet a friend. <i>(smile, hand on chest)</i></li><li><b>Marsden:</b> Kia ora, Ruatara. In English we say ‘thank you’. <i>(carefully)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Marsden had no reason to help a stranger. Why do you think he did? What might Ruatara be thinking about him now? (20s)</li><li class='dir'>Years pass. 1814, Rangihoua, in the Bay of Islands. A small field of green and gold on the hillside.</li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> Look, whānau! This is wheat, from the seed my friend gave me. Look how tall it grows! <i>(hold up a sheaf, proud)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Ooh! Tino pai! Very good! <i>(admire the wheat, touch the grain)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> From this, flour. From flour, bread. One day we will share a hākari of bread from our own fields. <i>(arms wide)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> But Ruatara wants more than wheat. He remembers the friend who cared for him, and the God his friend followed.</li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> Marsden told me about Jesus. I want our people to hear it too, in a way we can trust. <i>(look out to sea)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> I will ask him to send teachers. I will protect them here. This is my invitation. <i>(firm and hopeful)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Across the sea, Marsden hears the invitation. And he says yes.</li><li><b>Marsden:</b> Ruatara has asked us to come. We will bring teachers, and the good news of Jesus. <i>(pack a bag)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Haere mai! Welcome! <i>(arms open, together)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara decided to invite Marsden and the teachers. Why do you think kindness on the Ann mattered so much to that decision?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Marsden had treated him with care when he was at his lowest, so Ruatara trusted him; trust grows from kindness, not force; Ruatara had seen Marsden’s faith lived out. Ruatara was the one who invited; it was his choice.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Ruatara want to grow at home, and what did he want Marsden to send?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He wanted to grow wheat, which makes flour and bread, and he asked for teachers, with a promise that he would protect them.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A door opened by kindness</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–14:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara invited Marsden years after one kindness on a ship. Why can small acts of care open doors that big speeches cannot?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: people trust those who have helped them; kindness shows what we believe better than words; people remember how they were treated; being cared for makes you listen. Follow-up: ‘What would Ruatara have thought if Marsden had walked past?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Where might you open a door with a kind act?’ If silent: ask who was kind to them when they started at a new school.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:30–15:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Jesus cares about people who are hurting, and kindness can open doors for his good news.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for people who were kind to me. Help me notice who needs me.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week, do one kind thing for someone who is sick, new or left out. And start your own prayer plan by choosing a time each day to talk with God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This is day one of building a ‘prayer plan’ this week: a time (today), a place (Tuesday) and one thing to pray (Wednesday). Thursday puts it together as the holiday challenge.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A blanket and a cup of water</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:00 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">He did not walk past.<br>Kia ora, Ruatara.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse peek</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–17:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Echo: say each chunk, class echoes it back. Introduce Matthew 28:19–20 (the two parts we have chosen). Echo each chunk: say it, they say it back. Jesus’ promise, ‘I will be with you always’, is the heart of this week.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Choose your time — open hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">17:00–20:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This week we build our own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Today we choose a time. Use your hands: palms down to let go of things, palms up to receive from God. <i>(hands)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Sit comfortably with your hands resting on your knees. Breathe in slowly, and out. Think of a kind person who once helped you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think of Jesus caring for people who were sick and far from home. Say what God is like.<br>Prompts: God, you notice people who are sick and lonely, like Ruatara on the Ann. / God, you are kind, and you turn small kindnesses into friendships that change the world.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you are kind, and you notice everyone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms down: let go of times you walked past someone who needed help.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I walked past, like the sailors who ignored Ruatara. / Sorry for the times I decided someone wasn’t worth my time.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we’re sorry for the times we walked past. Please forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Palms up: receive, and thank God for those who were kind to you.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people who helped me when I was low. / Thank you that Jesus never walks past us.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who care for us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Decide on a time of day for your prayer plan, and ask God to help you keep it.<br>Prompts: Please help me choose a time each day to talk with you, like after breakfast or before bed. / Please show me one person to be kind to this week.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us find a time to pray every day, and help us be kind.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Don’t ask anyone to share. Help students choose a real time: on the bus, after cleaning teeth, before dinner. Some students may be far from home or have a family member who is unwell; reassure them that God is close to people who are hurting.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: the voyage of the brig Active across the sea, and how Ruatara prepared a place at Oihi Bay for a very special Christmas.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Tuesday: The Active and the Preparing":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samuel Marsden, Ruatara, Christmas Day 1814, Oihi Bay; Luke 2:10; Matthew 28:19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">The Active and the Preparing</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My own prayer plan — This week you build your own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Monday: choose your time. Tuesday: choose your place. Wednesday: choose the one thing. Thursday: put it together and pray in your own words. Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20), so you can talk to him anywhere.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning about the voyage of the brig Active and who sailed on it.</li><li>We are learning how Ruatara prepared a place at Oihi Bay for the first Christmas service.</li><li>I can explain that Ruatara and Marsden had both shared and different hopes, and that Ruatara was the host.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students saying that the Active carried Marsden, three missionary families and Ruatara home, that Ruatara prepared the enclosure, pulpit and seats, and that Marsden and Ruatara had overlapping but different hopes, with Ruatara making his own choices as a rangatira.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Paper and pens</li><li>Optional: a map of the Bay of Islands showing Rangihoua and Oihi</li><li>Teacher: please check how you tell this story with local iwi, hapū or a Māori-medium colleague</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: The Active and the Preparing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Ask: ‘Why do you think the sea voyage was such a big deal in 1814?’ (Sailing ships, no engines, about a month at sea.)</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Recap</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we met Ruatara, a young Ngāpuhi leader, and Samuel Marsden, who cared for him on the ship Ann.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Ruatara ask Marsden to send to his people?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Teachers, and he promised to protect them; he also grew wheat from seed Marsden gave him. ‘Today the invitation is accepted. A ship called the Active sails across the sea, and Ruatara gets ready for a very special day.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 28:18–20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:18</b> Jesus came to them and said: “I have been given all authority in heaven and on earth!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:19</b> Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. Baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit,</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:20</b> and teach them to do everything I have told you. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Jesus said this to his friends after he rose from the dead. ‘Authority’ means the right and power to be in charge. ‘Baptize’ means to wash with water as a sign of belonging to Jesus. This is where the missionaries’ journey started: they believed Jesus had sent them. Notice that Jesus’ promise to be with us comes with the sending.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: The voyage and the preparing</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–6:00 · 3½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Yesterday we heard how Ruatara invited Marsden and his teachers to the Bay of Islands. Today the invitation is accepted.</p><p>On 19 November 1814, a small two-masted ship called the brig Active set sail from Sydney. The Church Missionary Society had sent three men to begin a mission: Thomas Kendall, a schoolteacher, William Hall, a carpenter, and John King, a shoemaker. Their wives and children came too, and so did farm animals. A mission is a group of people sent to share the good news about Jesus. With them sailed Samuel Marsden, who was about forty-nine, and Ruatara himself, together with Hongi Hika and other Ngāpuhi leaders who had been to Sydney. The voyage took about a month. Imagine weeks of waves and salt wind, and wondering what waited on the shore.</p><p>Now, it is important to tell the truth about these two men. They had hopes in common. Both wanted friendship and learning to grow between their people. But they did not want exactly the same things. Marsden believed he was bringing the good news of Jesus. He also wrote, as many Europeans of his day did, that he was bringing ‘civilisation’, as if Māori life needed to be replaced. We can say plainly that this was not respectful.</p><p>Ruatara had his own reasons. He wanted wheat, tools, reading and trade for his people. He wanted the newcomers to settle at Rangihoua, under his protection, so he could look after them and keep his hand on how the friendship went. Historians say he had seen how strangers had been treated in Australia, and he was cautious. Ruatara was no helper in someone else’s story. He was a rangatira, making his own choices and risking his own mana, his standing among his people.</p><p>On 22 December 1814 the Active sailed into the Bay of Islands. Waka paddled out to meet it, and Ruatara was home. Then he got to work. At Oihi Bay, just around the headland from Rangihoua, he fenced off about half an acre. He built a pulpit and a reading desk from planks and part of an old waka, and covered them with black cloth. He set the bottoms of old waka as seats for hundreds of people, and he raised a flagpole on the highest hill. When Marsden saw it, he wrote in his journal: ‘The whole was becoming and had a solemn appearance.’</p><p>Hundreds of people would soon gather, and Ruatara wanted it done well, with care and respect for everyone who came. The place was ready. Christmas was only three days away.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. The brig Active sails from Sydney. · 2. Who was on board? · 3. Two men, shared hopes, and some different ones. · 4. Ruatara prepares the place.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3½ minutes. Documented: the Active sailed from Sydney on 19 November and arrived on 22 December 1814 (DOC); the missionaries Kendall, Hall and King and their families; Ruatara and Hongi Hika aboard; Ruatara’s preparations at Oihi (half an acre fenced, pulpit and reading desk of planks and an old canoe covered in black cloth, canoe bottoms as seats, a flagpole) and Marsden’s quoted journal line (NZ Geographic; Marsden’s journal). Marsden did use the word ‘civilization’ in his journal. Historians such as those writing in the Dictionary of NZ Biography and NZ History describe Ruatara’s strategy of keeping the mission under his protection; ‘he had seen how strangers had been treated in Australia’ is the reported view of historians, not a quote from Ruatara. The flag was British; we mention it once and don’t dwell on it. The Active also carried others; we have named only the main people. Check any details you plan to extend with local sources.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Bible dig: Two men, two hopes</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–8:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Marsden and Ruatara were friends. Did they want exactly the same things? Match the hopes. Note: these are what historians reported, not exact quotes.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><table class='lt-tbl'><tr><th>Marsden</th><th>Ruatara</th></tr><tr><td>To share the good news of Jesus</td><td>To have the mission settle at Rangihoua under his protection</td></tr><tr><td>To teach farming and other skills</td><td>To bring wheat, tools, reading and trade to his people</td></tr><tr><td>Wrote of ‘civilising’, seeing Māori life as needing to change</td><td>Cautious: he had seen how Europeans treated others and wanted to set the terms</td></tr><tr><td>Believed he was bringing something good</td><td>Chose, using his own mana, to welcome the mission and host the day</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Reveal</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Real history has more than one side. Both men made real choices, and Ruatara was the host, not just a helper.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Reveal row by row. This is the honest-history moment in the week. Say: ‘We can admire the care Marsden showed, and still say that some of his words about Māori were not respectful. And we can admire how Ruatara led, thought carefully and welcomed the mission on his own terms.’ Don’t push students to judge anyone harshly. If you have students with Māori whakapapa, invite but don’t expect them to speak for everyone. Check with local iwi about how they tell this story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Finish the line</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:00–9:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara built the pulpit from planks and part of an old ________.<br><i>Answer: …waka (canoe), and he covered it with black cloth.</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: waka, a canoe (NZ Geographic, Marsden’s journal, DOC). Follow-up: ‘What did he use for seats?’ (The bottoms of old canoes, upturned.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who prepared all of this?’ (Ruatara.) If silent: look at the picture of Ruatara and his people with the upturned waka and ask what they can see.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Finish the sentence · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: A risky welcome</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">9:00–10:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara risked his mana, his standing among his own people, by welcoming the mission. Why do you think he took that risk?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he trusted Marsden because of the kindness on the Ann; he wanted wheat, tools and learning for his people; he wanted to protect his people and set the terms; he was curious about the God Marsden followed. Follow-up: ‘What could have gone wrong for him?’ Follow-up 2: ‘What does it tell you about Ruatara?’ (Brave, careful, a leader.) If silent: give 20 seconds of quiet thinking, then ask for one idea.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Silent think, then share · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: The same hopes?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:30–12:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “Marsden and Ruatara wanted exactly the same things.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: they shared friendship and learning, but Marsden also spoke of ‘civilising’ and Ruatara wanted wheat, tools and teachers on his own terms, with protection under his mana. Follow-up: ‘Can two people be friends and want different things?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How do good friends deal with that?’ (Listen, be honest.) If silent: ask students to give one hope each man had.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">12:00–14:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara prepared a place, and he made a real choice. We too can prepare a place to listen to God.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, I want a quiet place where I can talk with you each day.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose a place for your prayer plan: a spot on your bed, a window seat, a corner of the garden, the bus seat. Make a small card: ‘My time ____ My place ____’, and put it where you will see it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ruatara fenced off a place and prepared it. This is a gentle echo: a prayer plan is a little prepared place. Hand out small cards or paper. Today they write the time and place; tomorrow they add what to pray for. Keep it simple and doable.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: A place made ready</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara prepared the place.<br>The whole was becoming and had a solemn appearance.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Build the verse</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Build: each click adds a chunk; read the growing verse together. Build Matthew 28:19–20 chunk by chunk. After the build, ask: ‘Which part is the sending, and which part is the promise?’ (Go and make disciples; I will be with you always.)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Your place, your time — write it</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write your own prayer plan on a small card: a time, a place, and (tomorrow) one thing to pray. Writing it down makes it real, like Ruatara preparing the place at Oihi. <i>(write)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take your pen and a small card. At the top, write: “My prayer plan.” Take a breath, and think of a quiet place.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one line about who God is, from today’s story.<br>Prompts: God, you sail with your people across the sea, and you keep your promise to be with them. / God, you go before us and prepare the way, like Ruatara preparing Oihi.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you go with us wherever we go!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, think of one time you wanted things your own way and didn’t listen to someone else.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I didn’t listen to someone who saw things differently. / Sorry for the times I thought my way was the only way.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we are sorry for not listening. Please forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write one thing you are thankful for about the people who prepared the way for others to hear about Jesus.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Ruatara who were brave enough to welcome new things. / Thank you for places where I can pray and read your Word.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you for…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who prepare the way for others.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Write the place where you will pray each day, and ask God to meet you there.<br>Prompts: Please help me find a quiet place where I can talk with you every day. / Please help me be a person who listens well, like a good host.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 35s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us find our place, and meet us there.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your card somewhere you’ll see it tonight. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students keep their writing private. If a student has no quiet place at home, help them think of one, like the school library, a walk to school, or a moment at bedtime. God hears anywhere.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: Christmas Day 1814 at Oihi Bay. Marsden preaches, Ruatara explains, and a great gathering listens.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Wednesday: Christmas Day 1814":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19.5 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samuel Marsden, Ruatara, Christmas Day 1814, Oihi Bay; Luke 2:10; Matthew 28:19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Christmas Day 1814</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My own prayer plan — This week you build your own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Monday: choose your time. Tuesday: choose your place. Wednesday: choose the one thing. Thursday: put it together and pray in your own words. Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20), so you can talk to him anywhere.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened at Oihi Bay on Christmas Day 1814.</li><li>We are learning how Ruatara explained Marsden’s words to his people.</li><li>I can explain what Luke 2:10 means and why Ruatara’s part in the day mattered.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Listen for students recalling that Marsden preached on Luke 2:10 at Oihi Bay, that Ruatara prepared the place and explained the message to his people, that hundreds of Ngāpuhi gathered, and that the good news is for everyone, including children.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Optional: a Bible open at Luke 2</li><li>Teacher: please check with local iwi, hapū or a Māori-medium colleague before using any haka, waiata or karakia in the lesson; do not imitate a haka without their guidance</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Christmas Day 1814</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Let the splash play. Say: ‘Today we picture the day, and we remember that no Māori account of it was written down that survives. We know it mainly through Marsden and one other visitor, plus what Ngāpuhi and historians say today.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Hook: Explaining what you mean</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–1:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Have you ever tried to explain something important to someone who spoke another language, or didn’t know the story?</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What helped them understand?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Take two or three stories: a new student, a visitor, a grandparent. Recap: ‘Yesterday Ruatara sailed home on the Active and prepared a place at Oihi Bay. Today it is Christmas Day, and Ruatara plays one of the most important parts: the person who explains.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Luke 2:10 and Matthew 28:20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">1:30–2:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Luke 2:10</b> But the angel said, “Don’t be afraid! I have good news for you, which will make everyone happy.</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:20</b> I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Marsden preached from Luke 2:10 in the older English he read: ‘Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy.’ The CEV wording above is the one we use in class. The angel was speaking to the shepherds on the first Christmas night. ‘Everyone’ is the key word: the good news was for all people.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: Christmas Day at Oihi</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">2:30–5:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>It is Sunday morning, the twenty-fifth of December, 1814. In Aotearoa it is summer. The sun is bright on the water at Oihi Bay, and pōhutukawa grow on the slopes above the sand. This is the day Ruatara has been preparing for.</p><p>At ten o’clock, Samuel Marsden and the missionary families come ashore. Ruatara and two other leading rangatira wear red coats that the Governor of New South Wales had given them. Hundreds of Ngāpuhi people have come: men, women and many children. They are arranged around the pulpit in a wide circle, the missionaries on seats beside it, and each group of people in its place. Ruatara has made sure everything is in order.</p><p>Marsden steps up into the pulpit built from planks and part of an old waka. The missionaries sing a well-known hymn, the Old Hundredth, based on Psalm 100. Then Marsden opens his Bible and reads from the Gospel of Luke, chapter two, verse ten. Those are the words the angel spoke to the shepherds on the first Christmas night: ‘Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy.’ That means good news that will make everyone happy.</p><p>But there is a problem. Most of the people listening do not understand English. Marsden wrote afterwards that the people told Ruatara they could not understand what he meant. And listen to what Ruatara does. He tells them to be patient, that they would understand by and by, and that he would explain Marsden’s meaning as far as he could. Ruatara stands between two worlds, and he is the one who carries the message from one to the other.</p><p>When the service ends, Marsden’s party sees hundreds of people performing a loud, strong haka. Marsden called it a war dance. Today many Māori historians say it was more likely a haka of joy and support for Ruatara, honouring him for what he had done.</p><p>That night Marsden wrote in his journal: ‘In this manner, the gospel has been introduced into New Zealand.’ But the story was not only Marsden’s. It belonged to Ruatara, and to the people of Ngāpuhi, who would make their own decisions about it. Remember: no Māori account of that day was written down that survives. This is what we know.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. Sunday 25 December 1814. · 2. Hundreds gather in a wide circle. · 3. “Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy.” · 4. Ruatara explains.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 3 minutes. Documented (Marsden’s journal; NZ Geographic; DOC; Anglican Church bicentenary): Sunday 25 December 1814; service at 10 a.m. at Oihi; Ruatara and two other leading rangatira in regimentals given by Governor Macquarie; the Old Hundredth; the text Luke 2:10 (‘Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy’); Ruatara’s interpreting, including that they would understand by and by and that he would explain as far as he could; the haka afterwards, with 300–400 people (Marsden’s count for the haka; ‘hundreds’ is our wording for the gathering); and Marsden’s journal line. The reading of the haka as a gesture of support for Ruatara is an interpretation from Māori and other historians (e.g. as described in the 2014 commemorations), not certain. Not documented: how Ruatara phrased his explanation, what the people said. No Māori written account survives. Marsden also administered Communion on the Active that evening. We haven’t confirmed a hākari (feast) on Christmas Day, so don’t add one. The flag Marsden mentions is left out of the narration on purpose; say if asked that the British flag flew from a hill and that it is part of the complicated colonial story.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Which verse?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">5:30–6:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Which Bible verse did Marsden read at Oihi Bay on Christmas Day 1814?<br>A) Luke 2:10 ✓ &nbsp; B) Genesis 1:1 &nbsp; C) John 3:16 &nbsp; D) Psalm 23:1</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, Luke 2:10, the angel’s words to the shepherds about good news of great joy. Follow-up: ‘Who first said those words?’ (The angel on the first Christmas night.) Follow-up 2: ‘Who explained them to the crowd?’ (Ruatara.) If silent: look at the first scripture line together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Role-play: Ruatara Explains</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:30–11:30 · 5 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Introduce <b>Ruatara Explains</b> (Documented history, retold). That Marsden read Luke 2:10 (in the older English words, ‘Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy’), that the people said they could not understand, and that Ruatara said they would understand by and by and that he would explain as far as he could, come from Marsden’s journal. Everything else is imagined, including Kuia and Tamaiti, Ruatara’s explanation, the child’s question and the te reo lines.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Cast</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><b>Narrator</b> — Tells the story<br><b>Marsden</b> — An English clergyman, sincere and a little out of his depth<br><b>Ruatara</b> — A confident, patient Ngāpuhi leader<br><b>Kuia</b> — An imagined grandmother: wise, thoughtful, asks good questions<br><b>Tamaiti</b> — An imagined child: curious and brave<br><b>Everyone</b> — The gathering at Oihi Bay</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Everyone: be the crowd at Oihi, listening closely · Everyone: give one loud, joyful cheer together at the end (a cheer, not a haka)<br>Props: Optional: a Bible for Marsden · Optional: a rectangle of dark cloth over a desk as the pulpit<br>Play Ruatara as the person in charge: patient, respected and confident. Play Kuia with dignity, not as a comic. Tamaiti is a brave, curious child. Pronunciation: mokopuna (moh-koh-POO-nah), kuia (KOO-ee-ah), tamaiti (tah-MAH-ee-tee), kia ora (KEE-ah OH-rah). Check with a Māori-medium colleague. The cheer at the end is a cheer, not a haka: please don’t imitate a haka, because it is a taonga; if local whānau or a knowledgeable colleague wishes to lead one, welcome that. Documented lines are Marsden’s Luke 2:10 reading, the people’s confusion, and Ruatara’s ‘by and by’ reply (all from Marsden’s journal, in paraphrase). Everything else is imagined.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Script</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol class='lt-script'><li class='dir'>Oihi Bay, Christmas Day 1814. Warm sun, blue sea, and a pulpit made from planks and an old waka.</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Ruatara has prepared everything. Hundreds of Ngāpuhi gather in a wide circle.</li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Murmur, murmur… <i>(quiet, curious chatter)</i></li><li><b>Tamaiti:</b> Kuia, what is he going to say? <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Kuia:</b> I do not know, mokopuna. Ruatara says it is important. Listen well. <i>(hand on the child’s shoulder)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> The missionaries sing the old hymn. Then Marsden opens his Bible and reads.</li><li><b>Marsden:</b> Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy. <i>(calm, open Bible)</i></li><li><b>Tamaiti:</b> I don’t understand the words, Kuia. <i>(whisper)</i></li><li><b>Kuia:</b> Nor do I. Let us wait for Ruatara. <i>(nod)</i></li><li class='frz'>FREEZE-FRAME: Freeze! Hundreds of people are hearing words they cannot understand. How might that feel? What would help them? (20s)</li><li><b>Narrator:</b> Marsden finishes. The people turn to Ruatara, their rangatira, and say they cannot understand.</li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> Be patient. You will understand by and by. I will explain what he means, as far as I can. <i>(stand tall, open hands)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> He speaks of a child called Jesus, and of angels. He says the news is good, with great joy for everyone. <i>(explaining to the crowd)</i></li><li><b>Tamaiti:</b> For children too? For us? <i>(stand up, brave)</i></li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> For everyone, mokopuna. It is only the beginning. There is time for your questions. <i>(kind smile)</i></li><li><b>Kuia:</b> Then we will listen. And we will ask many questions. <i>(firm nod)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Yes! Yes! Yes! <i>(stand and give one joyful cheer)</i></li><li><b>Marsden:</b> I feel my soul melt within me, to see so great a gathering. <i>(hand on heart, quiet)</i></li><li><b>Narrator:</b> That night, Marsden wrote that the gospel had been introduced into New Zealand. But Ruatara had made the day.</li><li><b>Ruatara:</b> This is the beginning. We will learn from each other, and we will decide for ourselves. <i>(look at the sea)</i></li><li><b>Everyone:</b> Kia ora. Amen. <i>(heads bowed, quiet)</i></li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara explained Marsden’s words to his people. Why was that so important?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: Most people could not understand English, so without Ruatara the message could not be heard properly; he was trusted by his people; he made the words clear and gave them time to ask; the message was for them, not just for Marsden.</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Debrief question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">What did Ruatara say to the people when they said they could not understand?<br><span style='font-weight:400'>Listen for: He said they would understand by and by, and that he would explain Marsden’s meaning as far as he could (from Marsden’s journal).</span></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Standing between two worlds</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Ruatara explained Marsden’s words to his own people. What does that show about the part he played that day?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Good answers: he was the bridge between English and te reo Māori; he was trusted by his people; the message would not have been understood without him; he made choices, he wasn’t just helping; he was the host. Follow-up: ‘What did he risk?’ (His mana.) Follow-up 2: ‘What might it feel like to carry a message between two groups?’ If silent: ask who in the room helps others understand.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Good news for everyone</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:00–14:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Luke 2:10 says the good news will make everyone happy. How can good news be for everyone, and yet people still have the right to listen, question and decide for themselves?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ideas: God invites, he does not force; good news is told with respect and patience; Ruatara said there was time for questions; people can choose. Follow-up: ‘Why did Ruatara say they would understand later?’ Follow-up 2: ‘How can we share Jesus kindly?’ (By listening and not pushing.) If silent: ask how they like to be invited to try something new.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Whole-class discussion · 40-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Head · Heart · Hands: Head · Heart · Hands</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">14:00–15:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Head</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">The good news is for everyone, and God invites us to listen and respond. People help us understand it.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Heart</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God, thank you for people who explain your good news. Help me to listen well.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Hands</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Choose the one thing you will pray about in your prayer plan: a person, a need, or a thank you. Add it to your card.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>This completes the three parts of the plan: a time (Monday), a place (Tuesday), one thing to pray (today). Tomorrow they put it together.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Good news of great joy</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–15:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Behold! I bring you glad tidings of great joy.<br>Ruatara: “You will understand by and by.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Verse with actions</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:30–16:30 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Actions: learn one action per chunk. Do the actions for Matthew 28:19–20: walk on the spot and point out, a wide sweep, an open invitation, pointing to your eyes, a hand on your shoulder, and arms wide.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Pray for each other — in pairs</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:30–19:30 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Share your prayer plan with a partner: your time, your place, and the one thing you will pray about. Then pray one sentence for each other. Praying together is a gift. <i>(pairs)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Turn to a partner. Take a breath. Tell your partner your time and your place in one sentence each.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Take turns finishing the sentence about God.<br>Prompts: God, you bring good news of great joy for everyone. / God, you speak every language, and you understand every heart.<br>Starter: <b>Wow, God, you are…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Wow, God, you bring good news for everyone!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Silently, say sorry for times when people who carried Jesus’ name did not listen well.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times people who followed Jesus did not listen or were unfair to others. / Sorry for the times I haven’t listened to someone who sees things differently.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we are sorry for the times we did not listen. Please forgive us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank God for people who explain what they have learned about him.<br>Prompts: Thank you for people like Ruatara who helped others understand. / Thank you for the church in Aotearoa, and for Māori and Pākehā who follow Jesus together.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for people who help us understand.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray one sentence for your partner, using their name, and for the one thing on your card.<br>Prompts: Please help (partner’s name) keep their prayer time and place. / Please bless our local iwi and our churches, and help us all listen well to each other.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, help us listen to you and to each other.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Thank your partner for praying for you. Everyone: Amen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Model it first with a student. Sorry is silent. If a student doesn’t want to share their plan, they can simply pray ‘Please help us both keep our prayer time’. The Sorry prompt is gentle on purpose: the history includes both good and hurtful things, and we can ask God’s forgiveness for the hurtful without pointing at anyone.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Tomorrow: our year celebration. What happened next in Aotearoa, Jesus’ promise to be with us, and a look back at all the faithful people of Term 4.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},"Thursday: Faithful People, Faithful God":{"content":"<div class=\"lt-group\"><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Duration</th><td class=\"lt-td\">18 minutes (19 with stretch slides)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Passage</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Samuel Marsden, Ruatara, Christmas Day 1814, Oihi Bay; Luke 2:10; Matthew 28:19–20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Topic</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Faithful People, Faithful God</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-gold\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Big Idea</th><td class=\"lt-td\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Memory verse</th><td class=\"lt-td\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-navy\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Prayer focus</th><td class=\"lt-td\">My own prayer plan — This week you build your own prayer plan: a time, a place and one thing to pray. Monday: choose your time. Tuesday: choose your place. Wednesday: choose the one thing. Thursday: put it together and pray in your own words. Jesus promised, ‘I will be with you always’ (Matthew 28:20), so you can talk to him anywhere.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Learning Intentions</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>We are learning what happened after Christmas Day 1814, including how Māori made the Bible and the faith their own.</li><li>We are learning what the story of Ruatara and Marsden shows about God, who is with us.</li><li>I can say Matthew 28:19–20, put together my own prayer plan, and name faithful people from Term 4.</li></ul></div><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-maroon\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Assessment</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Students can retell the week’s story in order, explain that Ruatara invited the mission and explained the message, say that Jesus promised to be with his followers always, recite Matthew 28:19–20, and name at least five of the faithful people from Term 4 and what they showed about God.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-slate\">Resources</div><div class=\"lt-cell-list\"><ul class='lt-list'><li>Screen</li><li>Take-home challenge cards</li><li>Pens</li><li>Optional: the ten ‘God is…’ words from Term 4 on cards</li><li>Optional: certificates or a small treat for the year celebration</li><li>Teacher: key sources: Te Ara DNZB ‘Ruatara’; NZ History ‘Māori intermediaries: Ruatara’; DOC Oihi Mission Station Historic Heritage Assessment and Rangihoua Heritage Park; NZ Geographic ‘Marsden’s first Christmas’; Anglican Church in Aotearoa 2014 bicentenary; The Spinoff, ‘150 years of the Bible in te reo Māori’</li><li>Teacher: this lesson is a starting point. Please check with local iwi or hapū, a Māori-medium colleague or church Māori ministry about how the story is told and what they would add</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"lt-group\"><div class=\"lt-section-head lt-bg-navy\">Teaching Script</div><table class=\"lt-tbl lt-tbl-steps\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">1</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Welcome &amp; title: Faithful People, Faithful God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:00–0:30 · 30 sec</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>The last devotion of the year. Celebrate! Let the splash play. Say: ‘This week we finish our year the way Term 4 began: with faithful people and a faithful God.’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">2</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Review game: Term 4 in order</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">0:30–3:00 · 2½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Put Term 4’s ten faithful stories in order, Week 1 to Week 10. What did each show about God?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Answer</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Peter and John — God Gives Power → Stephen — God is Forgiving → Philip and the Ethiopian — God is Guiding → Saul Becomes Paul — God Changes Lives → Midnight Songs in Prison — God is Present in Hard Times → Ordinary Heroes Who Served — God Uses Ordinary People → Storm, Shipwreck and Rome — God Keeps His Promises → Polycarp — God is Faithful → William Tyndale — God Speaks Through His Word → Ruatara and Marsden — God is With Us</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Students call out the next story; click to place it. For each ask: ‘Who was it about?’ then ‘What did it show about God?’ Keep it quick, about 15 seconds each. Then: ‘Which one will you remember?’</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">3</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Read the Bible: Matthew 28:18–20</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">3:00–4:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Read aloud (CEV)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:18</b> Jesus came to them and said: “I have been given all authority in heaven and on earth!</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:19</b> Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. Baptize them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit,</div><div class=\"lt-verse\"><b>Matthew 28:20</b> and teach them to do everything I have told you. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.”</div></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Our memory verse uses two parts of this: ‘Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples’ and ‘I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.’ Notice the promise at the end: Jesus doesn’t send us out alone. Being sent starts in our own lives: at home, at school, with friends.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">4</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Tell the story: What happened next</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">4:00–6:00 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher talk (read aloud)</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Ruatara did not live long after that Christmas. In February 1815 he fell ill with a fever, and within weeks he died, probably about twenty-eight years old. Sources give slightly different dates, but it was only about two months after Christmas Day. Marsden and the new mission were deeply saddened. But the story did not stop. Without Ruatara, there would have been no mission at Rangihoua, and many Ngāpuhi people had now heard Jesus’ name.</p><p>What came next belonged to Māori. A school was started at Rangihoua in 1816. The first pages of the Bible in te reo Māori were printed in 1827, and the whole New Testament in 1837. The complete Bible, Te Paipera Tapu, was finished in 1868. Māori people learned to read at remarkable speed. Historians say that by 1854 there were hundreds of Māori teachers, far more than European missionaries. People travelled to other villages carrying the Bible and their questions. Māori leaders then used the Bible to shape their own faith, and sometimes to challenge the Crown and the churches themselves. Some made new movements of their own, such as Ringatū and Rātana.</p><p>And we must be honest: the story of Aotearoa after 1814 also includes hurt. Land was lost, promises were broken, and people who carried Jesus’ name did not always treat others with respect. Both the good and the painful are part of the history. That is why we pray, listen and ask the people whose story it is.</p><p>So what does Ruatara’s story show us about God? That he is faithful, and he is With Us. Jesus said, ‘I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.’ He was with Ruatara, he was with Marsden, he was with Ngāpuhi, and he is with us now, wherever we go.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Click through 4 on-screen beats: 1. A short, hard season. · 2. Māori make the faith their own. · 3. The history is complicated. · 4. Jesus is with us.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-slate\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Notes</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>About 2 minutes, not word-checked. Documented: Ruatara fell ill on 13 February 1815 and died within weeks; sources differ on the date (19 February or 3 March) so we say ‘about two months after Christmas’; his age is about 27–28 because his birth year is uncertain. The Rangihoua school opened in 1816; the first Māori scripture was printed in 1827 (Genesis 1–3, John 1, the Ten Commandments, the Lord’s Prayer and hymns); the New Testament in 1837; the whole Bible in 1868 (Bible Society NZ, The Spinoff). The claim of ‘hundreds’ of Māori teachers comes from The Spinoff (about 558 Māori teachers and 23 European missionaries in the Anglican church by 1854). Ringatū and Rātana are Māori movements that drew on the Bible; mention but don’t explain at length. The colonial history includes land loss and broken promises; keep it calm and brief and invite local voices to say more. Some students may have Māori whakapapa or family who feel this history personally. Don’t ask them to speak for others. The Marsden Cross at Oihi marks the place of the 1814 service; Rangihoua Heritage Park is open to visit.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">5</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Who invited whom?</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">6:00–7:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Who invited the mission to come to the Bay of Islands?<br>A) Ruatara ✓ &nbsp; B) Marsden &nbsp; C) The king of England &nbsp; D) The Governor of New South Wales</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Answer: A, Ruatara invited Marsden and the missionaries and promised to protect them. Follow-up: ‘What did Ruatara prepare at Oihi?’ (A fenced space, pulpit and seats.) Follow-up 2: ‘What did Ruatara do during the service?’ (Explained Marsden’s words to his people.) If silent: re-read the first story beat on Monday.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Look question · Quick poll · 20-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">6</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Faithful people, faithful God</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">7:00–8:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pick one faithful person from Term 4. With your partner, say what their story shows us about God, and why it matters to you.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>God is words: Gives Power (Peter and John), Forgiving (Stephen), Guiding (Philip), Changes Lives (Paul), Present in Hard Times (Paul and Silas), Uses Ordinary People (Priscilla, Aquila and friends), Keeps His Promises (the storm), Faithful (Polycarp), Speaks Through His Word (Tyndale), With Us (Ruatara and Marsden). Follow-up: ‘Which one matters most right now?’ Follow-up 2: ‘Who was always faithful behind them all?’ (God.) If silent: give each pair a person.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Think question · Think · Pair · Share · 45-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">7</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Recap &amp; Big Idea: A year of faithful people</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">8:30–10:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Three takeaways</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><ol style='margin:0;padding-left:20px'><li>This year we met God as our Creator (Term 1), a God we can trust (Term 2), and the One Jesus showed us in his stories (Term 3).</li><li>In Term 4 we followed real people who followed Jesus, from Acts to Aotearoa. Their story keeps showing the same thing: faithful people, faithful God.</li><li>Jesus is with us always. He sends us out, and he goes with us, so we can pray, listen, serve and share his good news.</li></ol></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Big Idea</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">God’s good news of great joy is for everyone, and Jesus promises to be with those who follow him, in Aotearoa and everywhere. It was Ruatara’s invitation and leadership that made the first Christmas service at Oihi Bay possible, and Māori went on to shape their own faith.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Optionally name one favourite story from each term: Term 1 creation or the Promise-Maker, Term 2 a faith hero such as Esther, Term 3 the Lost Son. Then the Week 10 Big Idea stamps in. Celebrate: this class has listened, prayed and learned all year.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">8</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Searching question: Is the story finished?</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">skip if short</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">10:00–11:30 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Key question</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Agree or disagree: “The story of Jesus’ followers finished with the people we learned about.”<br>A) Agree &nbsp; B) Disagree</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Disagree: the story goes on in us and in every church today; Jesus promised to be with his followers always, to the end of the world. Follow-up: ‘Who is writing the next chapter?’ (We are.) Follow-up 2: ‘What is one small way you could follow Jesus over the holidays?’ If silent: ask where they could pray or help someone.</p></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Dig question · Vote with your feet · 30-second thinking timer on screen.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">9</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>This week’s challenge: Holiday Challenge</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">11:30–13:30 · 2 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-maroon\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Challenge</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Keep your own prayer plan going through the holidays: pick a time, a place and one thing to pray.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Why</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:20</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Print take-home cards from the challenge slide. Give out the take-home cards and let students finish their prayer-plan cards from Tuesday and Wednesday. There is no Monday check-in until next year. A good moment for certificates or a small treat.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">10</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Memory verse: Memory verse: fade-out</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">13:30–15:00 · 1½ min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Memory verse</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">“Go to the people of all nations and make them my disciples. I will be with you always, even until the end of the world.” — Matthew 28:19–20 (CEV)</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Activity note</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Fade: each click removes more words; can they still say it? Fade out Matthew 28:19–20. Bonus: can anyone say a verse from earlier this year, such as Acts 16:31, Psalm 119:105 or Luke 18:14?</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">11</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Scene: Sent, and never alone</strong><span class=\"lt-flag\">stretch</span> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">15:00–16:00 · 1 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">On screen</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Faithful people. Faithful God.<br>I will be with you always.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-step-head lt-bg-navy\"><th class=\"lt-step-num\">12</th><th colspan=\"2\" class=\"lt-step-title\"><strong>Directed prayer: Our own words — standing together</strong> <span class=\"lt-step-time\">16:00–19:00 · 3 min</span></th></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-gold\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">How to pray</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">This is the final prayer of the year, and it is yours. Use your own words, and your own prayer plan. There are no wrong words: short and honest is perfect. <i>(stand)</i></td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Opening</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Stand in a circle if you can. Take a slow breath. We will finish the year with our own prayers. Anyone can call out one short sentence in each part.</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Wow</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">In your own words, say who God has shown himself to be this year.<br>Prompts: God, you are with us, as you were with Ruatara, Marsden and everyone who followed you. / God, you are faithful, and you keep your promise to be with us always.<br>Starter: <b>God, you are…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “God, you are faithful, and you are with us always!”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Sorry</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Quietly, in your own words, say sorry for something from this year.<br>Prompts: Sorry for the times I haven’t listened to you or to others. / Sorry for the times people who follow you have been unfair, and please help us do better.<br>Starter: <b>God, I’m sorry for…</b> · 30s<br>Pray aloud: “God, we are sorry. Thank you for forgiving us.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Thanks</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Call out anything you are thankful for from this year, in your own words.<br>Prompts: Thank you for the faithful people whose stories we heard this year. / Thank you for the Bible in te reo Māori and in English, and for people who gave their lives so we could read it.<br>Starter: <b>Thank you, God, for…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Thank you, God, for this year and for your faithful people.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Please</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Pray in your own words for the holidays, and for the one thing on your prayer-plan card.<br>Prompts: Please keep us safe these holidays, and help me keep my time, place and one thing for prayer. / Please be with Christians around the world, and with our churches and iwi here in Aotearoa.<br>Starter: <b>Please, God, help…</b> · 40s<br>Pray aloud: “Please, God, keep us safe, keep us close to you, and help us pray every day.”</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-navy\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Closing</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\">Together: “I will be with you always.” Thank you, Jesus. Amen!</td></tr><tr class=\"lt-substep lt-row-green\"><td class=\"lt-pad\"></td><td class=\"lt-substep-label\">Teacher guidance</td><td class=\"lt-substep-val\"><p>Celebrate the whole-class voice. Names and prayers stay in the circle. If any student would prefer not to stand, they can pray seated. Pray for persecuted Christians calmly, and remember that some students may have family from places where Christians are mistreated. Congratulations on a faithful year!</p></td></tr></tbody></table><table class=\"lt-tbl\"><tbody><tr class=\"lt-row lt-row-slate\"><th class=\"lt-th\">Where to next</th><td class=\"lt-td\">Summer holidays — keep your prayer plan going, and see you in 2028!</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}}}}}}}};